DB.DEEP = DB.DEEP || {}; Object.assign(DB.DEEP, {"acarbose": {"origin": "Acarbose is a natural product made by soil bacteria of the genus Actinoplanes. In the 1970s researchers at Bayer in Germany screened microbes for substances that block the enzymes that digest starch, hoping to blunt the rise in blood sugar after meals. Acarbose, a pseudo-sugar that looks like a short chain of glucose units, was the standout. It was launched in Europe as Glucobay and approved by the FDA in 1995 as Precose. It mattered because it offered a way to lower blood sugar that works entirely inside the gut and does not push the pancreas to release more insulin.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Bayer scientists find acarbose while screening Actinoplanes bacteria for carbohydrate-enzyme blockers."], ["1990", "Acarbose is launched in Germany as Glucobay."], ["1995", "The FDA approves acarbose as Precose for type 2 diabetes."]], "cell": "Starch and table sugar cannot be absorbed whole. Pancreatic amylase first breaks starch into small sugars, and then alpha-glucosidase enzymes on the brush border of the small intestine (such as sucrase, maltase and glucoamylase) split them into glucose and other single sugars that can cross the gut wall. Acarbose is shaped like a carbohydrate, so it fits into the active site of these enzymes and competitively blocks them. It also weakly inhibits pancreatic alpha-amylase. Digestion of complex carbohydrates is delayed and spread further down the intestine, so glucose enters the blood more slowly. The result is a smaller blood sugar spike after meals. It does not affect absorption of glucose that is swallowed already as glucose.", "body": [["Small intestine", "Carbohydrate digestion slows and moves further down the bowel, so post-meal glucose rises more gently."], ["Colon", "Undigested carbohydrate reaches the colon, where bacteria ferment it into gas and short-chain fatty acids, causing bloating and flatulence."], ["Blood sugar", "Lowers post-meal glucose and modestly lowers A1c; by itself it does not cause hypoglycemia because it does not raise insulin."], ["Pancreas", "Smaller glucose spikes mean the pancreas needs to release less insulin after meals."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme elevations have been reported, so liver tests are watched in some patients."]], "journey": "Acarbose is taken by mouth with the first bite of a meal, because it must be in the gut at the same time as the food. Very little is absorbed into the blood. Most of it stays in the intestine, where it works, and is broken down by gut bacteria and digestive enzymes. The small fraction that is absorbed is cleared by the kidneys, and the rest leaves in the stool. Its effect is tied to each meal rather than to blood levels.", "sidefx": "Gas, bloating and diarrhea happen because undigested carbohydrate reaches the colon and is fermented by bacteria. These often ease over time and are worse with high-sugar diets. Acarbose alone rarely causes low blood sugar, but it can when combined with insulin or a sulfonylurea. In that case table sugar will not work well, because the drug blocks its breakdown, so hypoglycemia should be treated with pure glucose (dextrose).", "fun": "Because acarbose blocks sucrase, a patient on acarbose with low blood sugar needs glucose tablets or gel rather than orange juice or candy made from table sugar, a favorite exam point.", "src": ["Precose (acarbose) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "acetazolamide": {"origin": "Acetazolamide traces back to the early sulfa antibiotics. In 1940 researchers noticed that sulfanilamide inhibits the enzyme carbonic anhydrase and made patients' urine alkaline and their blood mildly acidic. Chemists at Lederle Laboratories (American Cyanamid) in the US then set out to build a stronger, more targeted carbonic anhydrase inhibitor, and in the early 1950s they produced acetazolamide, sold as Diamox. It was first used as a diuretic, but doctors quickly found it lowered pressure inside the eye, making it an important glaucoma drug. Later it found further uses in altitude sickness, idiopathic intracranial hypertension and some forms of epilepsy.", "timeline": [["1940", "Sulfanilamide is shown to inhibit carbonic anhydrase."], ["1950s", "Lederle chemists develop acetazolamide (Diamox)."], ["1950s", "Acetazolamide becomes a key oral treatment for glaucoma."]], "cell": "Carbonic anhydrase speeds the reaction between carbon dioxide and water to form carbonic acid, which splits into bicarbonate and hydrogen ions. In the proximal tubule of the kidney, the enzyme on the brush border (type IV) and inside the cell (type II) is needed to reclaim filtered bicarbonate. Acetazolamide, a sulfonamide, binds the zinc at the enzyme's active site and blocks it. Filtered bicarbonate stays in the tubule and is lost in the urine, carrying sodium and water with it. The urine becomes alkaline and the blood becomes mildly acidic (a normal anion gap, hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis). The same enzyme is needed in the ciliary body of the eye to make aqueous humor and in the choroid plexus to make cerebrospinal fluid, so blocking it lowers eye pressure and CSF pressure.", "body": [["Kidneys", "Bicarbonate, sodium and potassium are lost in the urine, producing a mild diuresis and alkaline urine. The effect weakens after a few days as acidosis develops."], ["Blood", "Loss of bicarbonate causes a mild metabolic acidosis, which stimulates breathing."], ["Lungs and breathing", "The acidosis drives the breathing center to increase ventilation, which helps people acclimatize to high altitude."], ["Eyes", "Less aqueous humor is made, so intraocular pressure falls in glaucoma."], ["Brain and CSF", "Less CSF is produced, lowering pressure in idiopathic intracranial hypertension; it may also reduce seizures in some patients."], ["Nerves", "Tingling in the fingers, toes and around the mouth is common and linked to the acid-base changes."]], "journey": "Acetazolamide is well absorbed by mouth and can also be given IV. It binds tightly to carbonic anhydrase in red blood cells and kidney tissue. It is not metabolized by the liver. It leaves the body unchanged through the kidneys by filtration and tubular secretion, so kidney disease raises levels. The half-life is several hours, with effects lasting most of a day by mouth.", "sidefx": "Because the drug dumps bicarbonate, it causes metabolic acidosis and low potassium as more sodium reaches the distal tubule and is exchanged for potassium. Alkaline urine and lower urinary citrate allow calcium phosphate stones to form. Tingling and altered taste (carbonated drinks taste flat) are common. As a sulfonamide it can rarely cause severe skin reactions (SJS, TEN) and blood disorders such as aplastic anemia and agranulocytosis. There is no specific antidote; treatment is stopping the drug and correcting electrolytes.", "fun": "Carbonated drinks taste flat or metallic on acetazolamide because carbonic anhydrase on the tongue helps us sense the fizz of carbon dioxide.", "src": ["Acetazolamide FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "activated-charcoal": {"origin": "Charcoal has been used as a remedy since ancient times, with mentions in Egyptian and Greek medical writing. Its power against poisons was shown dramatically in the 1800s. In 1813 the French chemist Michel Bertrand swallowed arsenic mixed with charcoal and survived, and in 1831 the pharmacist Pierre-Fleury Touery swallowed a lethal amount of strychnine with charcoal in front of the French Academy of Medicine and walked away unharmed. In the early 1900s industrial processes were developed to 'activate' charcoal by heating it with steam or chemicals, which creates a huge network of tiny pores. Activated charcoal became the standard tool for gut decontamination in poisonings during the 20th century.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Charcoal is used in Egyptian and Greek medicine."], ["1813", "Michel Bertrand survives swallowing arsenic mixed with charcoal."], ["1831", "Pierre-Fleury Touery survives a lethal strychnine dose with charcoal before the French Academy."], ["1900s", "Industrial activation of charcoal is developed, greatly increasing surface area."]], "cell": "Activated charcoal does not act on receptors or enzymes. It works by adsorption, a physical process where molecules stick to a surface. Activation creates an enormous internal surface area, so that a small amount of charcoal has the surface of a large field. Drug and toxin molecules in the gut bind to this surface through weak chemical forces and are trapped, so they pass out in the stool instead of being absorbed. With repeated doses, charcoal can also pull some drugs back out of the blood. It does this by keeping drug concentration in the gut lumen near zero, so drug diffuses from blood into the gut ('gut dialysis'), and by trapping drugs that the liver secretes into bile, which breaks their enterohepatic recirculation. Small, charged or ionic substances such as alcohols, metals (iron, lithium), acids, alkalis and hydrocarbons bind poorly.", "body": [["Stomach and intestine", "Binds the ingested toxin before it can be absorbed, lowering the amount that reaches the blood."], ["Blood", "With multiple doses it can lower blood levels of certain drugs such as carbamazepine, phenobarbital, dapsone, quinine and theophylline."], ["Lungs", "If vomited and inhaled, charcoal can cause aspiration pneumonitis, which is why a protected airway is required."], ["Bowel movement", "Stools turn black, and constipation or, rarely, blockage can occur, especially with repeated doses."]], "journey": "Activated charcoal is swallowed as a slurry or given by a gastric tube. It is not absorbed at all. It travels the length of the gut, binding drugs along the way, and leaves in the stool, which turns black. Its benefit is greatest when given soon after the ingestion, usually within about an hour, while toxin is still in the gut. Some products contain sorbitol as a cathartic to speed transit.", "sidefx": "Because it is not absorbed, it has no systemic toxicity. Problems come from where it goes. Vomiting is common, and if the patient has a depressed mental status or an unprotected airway, inhaled charcoal can cause severe lung injury. In the gut, especially with repeated doses or poor motility, it can cause constipation, bowel obstruction or perforation. Sorbitol-containing products can cause diarrhea, dehydration and electrolyte problems, so they are not repeated. It also adsorbs oral antidotes and medications, which can reduce their effect.", "fun": "A common memory aid for substances charcoal does not bind well is 'PHAILS': pesticides, hydrocarbons, acids and alkalis, iron and other metals, lithium, and solvents such as alcohols.", "src": ["Activated charcoal product labeling", "AACT/EAPCCT position statement on single-dose activated charcoal", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]}, "acyclovir": {"origin": "Acyclovir was discovered at Burroughs Wellcome in the 1970s, in the lab of Gertrude Elion and George Hitchings, who had spent decades designing drugs that imitate the building blocks of DNA. Chemist Howard Schaeffer synthesized the compound, and Elion's team worked out why it was so selective: only cells infected with herpes viruses could activate it. The work was published in 1977, and acyclovir was approved in the US in 1982, first as an ointment and soon in IV and oral forms. It was the first truly selective, safe antiviral drug and proved that viruses could be attacked without harming the host cell. Elion and Hitchings shared the 1988 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine, with James Black, for their approach to drug design.", "timeline": [["1974", "Acyclovir is synthesized and tested at Burroughs Wellcome."], ["1977", "Elion and colleagues publish acyclovir's selective action against herpes viruses."], ["1982", "Acyclovir is approved in the US."], ["1988", "Gertrude Elion and George Hitchings share the Nobel Prize, with James Black."]], "cell": "Acyclovir is a guanosine analog missing part of the sugar ring. It is inactive until phosphorylated. The first phosphate is added by a thymidine kinase made by herpes simplex and varicella-zoster viruses, an enzyme that human cells barely use on acyclovir. This is the key to its selectivity. Host cell kinases then add two more phosphates to make acyclovir triphosphate. This triphosphate competes with deoxyguanosine triphosphate for the viral DNA polymerase, which it prefers over human polymerase. When incorporated into the growing viral DNA chain, it causes chain termination because it lacks the 3'-hydroxyl group needed to attach the next nucleotide. It also traps the viral polymerase. Viral replication stops, but latent virus in nerve ganglia is not eliminated. Resistance usually comes from loss or mutation of viral thymidine kinase.", "body": [["Skin and mucous membranes", "Shortens healing time and viral shedding in herpes simplex and shingles outbreaks; it does not cure latent infection."], ["Nervous system", "Treats herpes simplex encephalitis when given IV, and can reduce acute shingles pain. High levels can cause confusion, tremor and hallucinations."], ["Kidneys", "Acyclovir is cleared by the kidneys and can crystallize in the tubules, causing acute kidney injury, especially with rapid IV infusion or dehydration."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea can occur with oral dosing."]], "journey": "Oral acyclovir is poorly absorbed, with low bioavailability, which led to the prodrug valacyclovir that is turned into acyclovir after absorption. IV acyclovir is used for serious infections. The drug distributes widely, reaches the cerebrospinal fluid at useful levels and crosses the placenta. Little is metabolized. It is cleared mainly unchanged by the kidneys through filtration and tubular secretion. The half-life is about two to three hours in normal kidney function and much longer in kidney failure, so doses are adjusted.", "sidefx": "Because only infected cells activate it, acyclovir is gentle on normal cells. Most toxicity comes from kidney handling. The drug is poorly soluble in urine, so with IV use and low fluid intake it can form crystals in the renal tubules and cause obstructive kidney injury; good hydration and slower infusion lower the risk. High blood levels, usually in kidney failure or the elderly, can cause neurotoxicity such as confusion, agitation and seizures. IV solution is alkaline and can cause phlebitis if it leaks. There is no specific antidote; hemodialysis removes it.", "fun": "Gertrude Elion never earned a formal PhD, yet she won a Nobel Prize and her lab produced drugs for leukemia, gout, transplant rejection and herpes.", "src": ["Zovirax (acyclovir) FDA label", "Elion GB et al. PNAS, 1977", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "adalimumab": {"origin": "Adalimumab came out of the push to block tumor necrosis factor alpha (TNF-alpha), a cytokine shown in the 1980s and 1990s to drive joint destruction in rheumatoid arthritis. Earlier TNF blockers used mouse-derived antibody parts or a receptor fusion protein. Adalimumab was built to be fully human. It was discovered in the 1990s through a collaboration between BASF and Cambridge Antibody Technology, which used phage display, a method that screens huge libraries of human antibody fragments displayed on viruses. Abbott Laboratories brought it to market as Humira, approved by the FDA at the end of 2002 for rheumatoid arthritis. It was the first fully human monoclonal antibody approved in the US, gained many more indications, and became one of the best-selling drugs in the world. Biosimilars reached the US market in 2023.", "timeline": [["1990s", "BASF and Cambridge Antibody Technology discover adalimumab using phage display."], ["2002", "The FDA approves adalimumab (Humira) for rheumatoid arthritis."], ["2000s", "Approvals expand to psoriatic arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, Crohn disease and psoriasis."], ["2023", "Adalimumab biosimilars launch in the US."]], "cell": "TNF-alpha is made mainly by activated macrophages and T cells. It exists as a membrane-bound form and a soluble form, and it signals through two receptors, TNFR1 (p55) and TNFR2 (p75). Binding activates NF-kappa B and other pathways that switch on genes for IL-1, IL-6, chemokines and adhesion molecules, recruit white cells and activate enzymes that destroy cartilage and bone. Adalimumab is a fully human IgG1 antibody that binds both soluble and membrane-bound TNF-alpha with high affinity and prevents it from engaging either receptor. It can also kill cells that display TNF on their surface through complement and antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity. The inflammatory cascade dampens, and joints, gut and skin become less inflamed. TNF also holds granulomas together, which is why blocking it can let latent TB escape.", "body": [["Joints", "Less synovial inflammation means less pain, swelling and stiffness, and slower cartilage and bone erosion in rheumatoid and psoriatic arthritis."], ["Gut", "Reduces inflammation and promotes mucosal healing in Crohn disease and ulcerative colitis."], ["Skin", "Clears plaques in psoriasis and lesions in hidradenitis suppurativa by quieting TNF-driven inflammation."], ["Immune system", "Weakens defenses against bacteria, fungi and intracellular organisms, raising risk of serious infections and TB or hepatitis B reactivation."], ["Heart", "Can worsen moderate to severe heart failure."], ["Nervous system", "Rarely triggers or unmasks demyelinating disease such as multiple sclerosis or optic neuritis."]], "journey": "Adalimumab is a large protein that would be digested in the gut, so it is injected under the skin. It is absorbed slowly through the lymphatics, peaking several days later. It stays mostly in blood and tissue fluid and, like other IgG antibodies, crosses the placenta, especially late in pregnancy. It is broken down by protein catabolism in the reticuloendothelial system rather than by liver enzymes or the kidneys, and FcRn recycling protects it, giving a half-life of about two weeks. Some patients make anti-drug antibodies that speed clearance and reduce its effect.", "sidefx": "Most serious side effects come straight from blocking TNF, a key part of host defense. Infections, including sepsis, invasive fungal infections such as histoplasmosis, and reactivation of latent TB (often outside the lungs) are the main concerns, so patients are screened for TB and hepatitis B before starting. Lymphoma and other cancers carry a boxed warning, including rare hepatosplenic T-cell lymphoma in young patients. Autoantibodies can form and cause a lupus-like syndrome. Injection site reactions are common. There is no reversal agent; the drug wears off over weeks after stopping.", "fun": "Drug names ending in -mab are monoclonal antibodies, and the '-umab' ending in adalimumab signals a fully human antibody, compared with '-ximab' (chimeric) and '-zumab' (humanized).", "src": ["Humira (adalimumab) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "adenosine": {"origin": "Adenosine is made naturally by every cell in the body, as part of ATP and as a signal released during stress and low oxygen. In 1929 Alan Drury and Albert Szent-Gyorgyi at Cambridge reported that adenosine compounds from heart muscle slowed the heart rate and blocked conduction in animals. For decades this was a lab curiosity. In the 1980s researchers showed that a rapid IV bolus could stop reentrant supraventricular tachycardia within seconds, and adenosine was approved in the US in 1989 as Adenocard. Its extremely short action made it a safe first choice for converting SVT, replacing verapamil for many patients. A related product, Adenoscan, is used for pharmacologic cardiac stress testing.", "timeline": [["1929", "Drury and Szent-Gyorgyi describe adenosine's slowing effects on the heart."], ["1980s", "Clinical studies show a rapid IV bolus terminates SVT."], ["1989", "Adenosine (Adenocard) is approved in the US for PSVT."]], "cell": "Adenosine acts on G protein-coupled adenosine receptors. In the SA and AV nodes it binds A1 receptors, which couple to Gi proteins. The beta-gamma subunits open acetylcholine- and adenosine-sensitive potassium channels (IK-ACh/Ado), so potassium flows out and the cells hyperpolarize. The alpha subunit inhibits adenylyl cyclase, lowering cAMP, which reduces the calcium current that drives nodal action potentials. The AV node briefly stops conducting, and any reentry circuit that uses the AV node is broken, so SVT terminates. In vascular smooth muscle, A2A receptors raise cAMP and cause vasodilation, especially of coronary arteries, which is the basis for stress testing. A2B and A3 receptors on airway cells and mast cells can trigger bronchoconstriction.", "body": [["Heart (AV node)", "Causes a brief, complete AV block that interrupts reentrant SVT; flutter waves or fibrillation may be unmasked if the rhythm is atrial."], ["Heart (SA node)", "Slows the sinus rate, often producing a few seconds of asystole on the monitor before normal rhythm returns."], ["Blood vessels", "Dilates arteries, causing flushing, headache and a short drop in blood pressure; coronary vasodilation is used in stress imaging."], ["Lungs", "Can cause bronchospasm, so it is used with caution in asthma and COPD."], ["Chest and nerves", "Activates sensory nerves, causing chest pressure, shortness of breath and a frightening sense of doom."]], "journey": "Adenosine is given by rapid IV push close to the heart, followed by a fast saline flush, because it disappears almost instantly. Red blood cells and vessel lining cells take it up through nucleoside transporters, where adenosine deaminase and adenosine kinase break it down. The half-life is under ten seconds and effects are gone within a minute or two. It is not cleared by the liver or kidneys, so organ failure does not change its effect. Dipyridamole blocks its uptake and greatly strengthens it, while caffeine and theophylline block its receptors and weaken it.", "sidefx": "Side effects mirror the receptors it activates and fade within a minute. Brief asystole and bradycardia come from A1 effects on the SA and AV nodes. Flushing, headache and low blood pressure come from vasodilation. Chest pressure and dyspnea are common and usually harmless. Bronchospasm can occur in reactive airway disease. By shortening the atrial refractory period, adenosine can trigger atrial fibrillation, which is dangerous in Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome. Theophylline or aminophylline can reverse prolonged effects by blocking adenosine receptors.", "fun": "Patients often describe the few seconds after an adenosine push as feeling like their heart stopped, and on the monitor it nearly did, which is why staff warn patients first.", "src": ["Adenocard and Adenoscan (adenosine) FDA labels", "Drury AN, Szent-Gyorgyi A. J Physiol, 1929", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "alendronate": {"origin": "Bisphosphonates were first made by chemists in the 1800s and used in industry to soften water and prevent scale. In the 1960s Herbert Fleisch and colleagues in Switzerland studied pyrophosphate, a natural compound that stops calcium crystals from forming, and found that bisphosphonates were stable look-alikes that bind bone and slow its breakdown. Early versions like etidronate were weak. Alendronate, a more potent nitrogen-containing bisphosphonate, was developed in the 1980s and brought to market by Merck. The FDA approved it as Fosamax in 1995 for postmenopausal osteoporosis, and large trials showed it lowered spine and hip fractures. It became the most widely used osteoporosis drug.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Herbert Fleisch and colleagues show bisphosphonates inhibit bone mineral breakdown."], ["1980s", "Alendronate is developed as a potent nitrogen-containing bisphosphonate."], ["1995", "The FDA approves alendronate (Fosamax) for postmenopausal osteoporosis."], ["2000s", "Weekly dosing and links to jaw osteonecrosis and atypical femur fractures are recognized."]], "cell": "Alendronate has a phosphorus-carbon-phosphorus backbone that binds tightly to hydroxyapatite crystals, so it concentrates on bone surfaces, especially where bone is being remodeled. When osteoclasts dissolve bone, they take up the drug. Inside the osteoclast, alendronate inhibits farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase, an enzyme in the mevalonate pathway. This blocks prenylation, the attachment of lipid groups to small signaling proteins such as Ras, Rho and Rab. Without prenylation, these proteins cannot organize the cytoskeleton or form the ruffled border the osteoclast needs to resorb bone, and the cells eventually undergo apoptosis. Bone resorption slows, while bone formation continues for a time, so bone mineral density rises and fracture risk falls.", "body": [["Bone", "Slows bone breakdown, raises bone mineral density and lowers vertebral, hip and other fracture risk."], ["Esophagus", "The tablet can irritate and ulcerate the esophagus if it lingers there, which is why patients stay upright after taking it."], ["Stomach", "Heartburn, indigestion and abdominal pain are common."], ["Calcium balance", "Less calcium is released from bone, so serum calcium may dip; low calcium and vitamin D should be corrected first."], ["Jaw", "Rarely, poor healing of jaw bone after dental work leads to osteonecrosis of the jaw."], ["Thigh bone", "Long-term use is linked to rare atypical femur fractures from suppressed bone remodeling."]], "journey": "Oral absorption is very poor, under one percent, and food, coffee, juice, calcium and minerals reduce it further. It must be taken first thing in the morning with plain water on an empty stomach. About half of the absorbed drug quickly binds to bone; the rest is excreted unchanged in the urine within hours. It is not metabolized. The drug stored in bone is released very slowly as bone is remodeled, giving a terminal half-life of more than ten years. It is avoided in severe kidney impairment.", "sidefx": "Esophagitis and ulcers happen because the tablet is directly irritating to the lining of the esophagus. Taking it with a full glass of water and staying upright helps it pass into the stomach. Low calcium can occur because resorption slows. Osteonecrosis of the jaw and atypical femur fractures are thought to come from long-term suppression of bone remodeling, which impairs the repair of small injuries. Severe bone, joint or muscle pain and eye inflammation can also occur. There is no reversal agent, and because it stays in bone, effects persist after stopping, which is the basis for 'drug holidays.'", "fun": "Bisphosphonates were used to keep pipes free of scale long before they were used in bone, because they bind calcium crystals so tightly.", "src": ["Fosamax (alendronate) FDA label", "Fleisch H. Bisphosphonates in Bone Disease", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "allopurinol": {"origin": "Allopurinol came from the Burroughs Wellcome lab of Gertrude Elion and George Hitchings, who designed drugs by imitating purines, the building blocks of DNA. The compound was first synthesized in the mid-1950s. Elion's team was trying to make the leukemia drug 6-mercaptopurine last longer, because the enzyme xanthine oxidase breaks it down. Allopurinol blocked xanthine oxidase very well. Then they realized the same enzyme makes uric acid, the cause of gout. Allopurinol lowered uric acid in patients and was approved by the FDA in 1966. It became the main long-term treatment for gout and is still used to prevent uric acid buildup during cancer chemotherapy.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Allopurinol is first synthesized and studied as a xanthine oxidase inhibitor."], ["1960s", "Elion and Hitchings show it lowers uric acid and boosts 6-mercaptopurine."], ["1966", "The FDA approves allopurinol (Zyloprim) for gout."], ["1988", "Elion and Hitchings share the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine with James Black."]], "cell": "Purines from DNA, RNA and food are broken down to hypoxanthine, which xanthine oxidase converts to xanthine and then to uric acid. Allopurinol is a structural analog of hypoxanthine. It is a substrate and inhibitor of xanthine oxidase, and the enzyme converts it to oxypurinol (alloxanthine), which binds tightly to the reduced molybdenum center of the enzyme and keeps it blocked. Uric acid production falls. The more soluble precursors hypoxanthine and xanthine are excreted in the urine instead, and some hypoxanthine is recycled into nucleotides by the salvage enzyme HGPRT, which also feeds back to reduce new purine synthesis. Lower serum uric acid lets urate crystals in joints and tissues slowly dissolve.", "body": [["Joints", "Lower uric acid levels slowly dissolve urate crystals, so gout attacks become less frequent over months; early on, shifting crystals can trigger flares."], ["Kidneys", "Less uric acid reaches the urine, lowering the risk of uric acid kidney stones and urate nephropathy during tumor lysis."], ["Skin", "Rarely causes severe rashes such as SJS, TEN and DRESS, which are more common in carriers of the HLA-B*58:01 gene."], ["Liver", "Can cause liver enzyme elevations and, rarely, hepatitis."], ["Bone marrow", "It blocks breakdown of azathioprine and 6-mercaptopurine, so combining them can cause severe bone marrow suppression."]], "journey": "Allopurinol is well absorbed by mouth and quickly converted in the liver to oxypurinol, its active metabolite. Allopurinol itself has a half-life of only one to two hours, but oxypurinol lasts much longer, around fifteen hours or more, allowing once daily dosing. Oxypurinol is cleared by the kidneys, so it builds up in kidney disease and doses are adjusted. The full effect on uric acid takes days to weeks.", "sidefx": "Gout flares are common when starting because falling uric acid levels loosen crystals from joint deposits; colchicine or an NSAID is often given at the same time to prevent this. The most serious reaction is an immune-mediated hypersensitivity syndrome with rash, fever, hepatitis, eosinophilia and kidney injury, linked to HLA-B*58:01 and kidney impairment; the drug must be stopped at the first sign of rash. Because allopurinol blocks xanthine oxidase, it greatly raises levels of azathioprine and 6-mercaptopurine, which are inactivated by that enzyme. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Allopurinol was invented to help a cancer drug, not to treat gout. It worked so well on uric acid that its intended use became a footnote.", "src": ["Zyloprim (allopurinol) FDA label", "Elion GB. Nobel Lecture, 1988", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "alprazolam": {"origin": "Benzodiazepines began with chlordiazepoxide (Librium), found by Leo Sternbach at Hoffmann-La Roche in the late 1950s, followed by diazepam (Valium). In the late 1960s scientists at the Upjohn Company in Michigan explored triazolobenzodiazepines, which add a triazole ring to the benzodiazepine core. Alprazolam came out of this work and was approved by the FDA in 1981 as Xanax. It was marketed for anxiety and later became the first drug approved for panic disorder. Alprazolam became one of the most prescribed psychiatric drugs in the US, and its fast onset and short action also made it one of the most misused.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Leo Sternbach discovers the first benzodiazepine at Hoffmann-La Roche."], ["1970s", "Upjohn develops alprazolam, a triazolobenzodiazepine."], ["1981", "The FDA approves alprazolam (Xanax) for anxiety."], ["1990", "Alprazolam is approved for panic disorder."], ["2020", "The FDA adds a boxed warning on abuse, dependence and withdrawal for all benzodiazepines."]], "cell": "GABA-A receptors are chloride channels built from five subunits. The neurotransmitter GABA binds between alpha and beta subunits and opens the channel, letting chloride in and hyperpolarizing the neuron so it is less likely to fire. Alprazolam binds at a separate site between the alpha and gamma subunits, the benzodiazepine site. It is a positive allosteric modulator: it does not open the channel by itself, but it increases how often the channel opens when GABA binds. More chloride flows in, and neurons in the amygdala, cortex and other circuits become less excitable. Receptors containing alpha-1 subunits mediate sedation and amnesia, while alpha-2 and alpha-3 subunits contribute to anxiety relief and muscle relaxation. Because it needs GABA to work, it has a built-in ceiling when used alone, unlike barbiturates.", "body": [["Brain", "Calms anxiety and panic, causes drowsiness and can impair memory for events while the drug is active."], ["Breathing", "Mild effect alone, but combined with opioids, alcohol or other sedatives it can cause dangerous respiratory depression."], ["Muscles and coordination", "Reduces muscle tone and coordination, raising fall risk, especially in older adults."], ["Nervous system adaptation", "With regular use, receptors adapt, causing tolerance and physical dependence; sudden stopping can cause rebound anxiety and seizures."]], "journey": "Alprazolam is well absorbed by mouth, with effects in about fifteen to thirty minutes for the immediate-release form. It is lipid-soluble and crosses the blood-brain barrier and placenta and enters breast milk. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP3A4 into weakly active products, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ketoconazole or ritonavir raise levels sharply. Metabolites leave through the kidneys. Its half-life is about eleven hours, but the felt effect of the immediate-release form is shorter, which encourages repeated dosing. An extended-release form smooths levels.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness, dizziness, slowed reactions and memory problems come from enhanced GABA inhibition in the brain. When combined with opioids or alcohol, depressant effects add up and can stop breathing, which is the basis for the FDA boxed warning. Because the brain adapts by reducing GABA-A function, stopping suddenly unmasks overexcitement, causing anxiety, tremor, insomnia and potentially fatal seizures; doses are tapered. Older adults are more sensitive to confusion and falls. Flumazenil, a benzodiazepine site antagonist, can reverse sedation but may cause seizures in dependent patients, so it is used carefully.", "fun": "The triazole ring that makes alprazolam different from diazepam is also found in the related drugs triazolam and estazolam.", "src": ["Xanax (alprazolam) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "FDA Drug Safety Communication on benzodiazepines, 2020"]}, "alteplase": {"origin": "Tissue plasminogen activator (tPA) is a natural human enzyme made by the cells that line blood vessels to dissolve clots. In the late 1970s and early 1980s Desire Collen and colleagues in Leuven, Belgium, purified tPA from a human melanoma cell line that made large amounts of it, and showed that it worked best on fibrin-bound clots. In 1983 scientists at Genentech, working with Collen, cloned the tPA gene, allowing the protein to be made with recombinant DNA technology. The recombinant version, alteplase (Activase), was approved by the FDA in 1987 for heart attacks, making it one of the first biotechnology drugs. In 1996 it became the first approved treatment for acute ischemic stroke, after the NINDS trial showed better recovery when given within three hours.", "timeline": [["1981", "Rijken and Collen report purified tPA from human melanoma cells."], ["1983", "Genentech scientists clone the human tPA gene."], ["1987", "The FDA approves alteplase for acute myocardial infarction."], ["1995", "The NINDS trial shows benefit of tPA in acute ischemic stroke."], ["1996", "Alteplase is approved for acute ischemic stroke."]], "cell": "Clots are held together by a mesh of fibrin. The body breaks them down with plasmin, an enzyme that cuts fibrin. Plasmin circulates in an inactive form, plasminogen. Alteplase is a serine protease that cleaves plasminogen at a single peptide bond (Arg561-Val562), turning it into plasmin. Alteplase binds fibrin through its finger and kringle domains, and its activity is much higher when bound to fibrin, so it acts mainly on plasminogen already trapped in the clot. This makes it relatively fibrin-specific compared with older agents like streptokinase. Plasmin then digests fibrin into fibrin degradation products and the clot dissolves. Some plasmin escapes into the blood and degrades fibrinogen and factors V and VIII, adding to bleeding risk.", "body": [["Blood clots", "Dissolves fresh clots in coronary arteries, brain arteries, pulmonary arteries or blocked catheters, restoring blood flow."], ["Heart", "Reopening a blocked coronary artery saves heart muscle; restored flow can cause reperfusion arrhythmias such as accelerated idioventricular rhythm."], ["Brain", "In ischemic stroke, early reperfusion can save brain tissue, but it also raises the risk of bleeding into the brain."], ["Blood and bleeding", "Breaks down protective clots at puncture sites and old injuries, so bleeding from IV sites, gums and the GI tract is common."], ["Airway and skin", "Can cause angioedema of the tongue and lips, especially in patients on ACE inhibitors."]], "journey": "Alteplase is a protein, so it must be given IV, usually as a bolus followed by an infusion. It stays mostly in the blood and binds fibrin in clots. The liver clears it rapidly, giving an initial half-life of about five minutes, so the infusion keeps levels up. The clot-dissolving effect and the drop in fibrinogen last for hours after the infusion ends. A small amount is used directly in blocked central lines (Cathflo Activase), where it acts locally.", "sidefx": "Bleeding is the main toxicity and follows directly from the mechanism: plasmin cannot tell a harmful clot from a protective one. The most feared complication is intracranial hemorrhage, which is why strict exclusion criteria are used. Angioedema is thought to involve bradykinin, which plasmin can generate, and it is more common with ACE inhibitors that block bradykinin breakdown. There is no direct antidote. Serious bleeding is managed by stopping the infusion and giving cryoprecipitate or fibrinogen to replace fibrinogen, plus antifibrinolytics such as tranexamic acid or aminocaproic acid.", "fun": "The melanoma cell line used to purify tPA, called the Bowes melanoma line, made so much of the enzyme that it became the source used to clone the gene.", "src": ["Activase (alteplase) FDA label", "NINDS rt-PA Stroke Study Group. N Engl J Med, 1995", "Pennica D et al. Nature, 1983"]}, "amantadine": {"origin": "Amantadine is a cage-shaped molecule developed at DuPont in the early 1960s. It was found to block influenza A and was approved by the FDA in 1966 for flu prevention. Its second career came by chance. In the late 1960s a patient with Parkinson disease told her neurologist, Robert Schwab at Massachusetts General Hospital, that her tremor and stiffness improved while she took amantadine for the flu. Schwab and colleagues confirmed the effect and published it in 1969, and amantadine became an antiparkinson drug. Influenza A later became widely resistant, and the CDC stopped recommending it for flu. An extended-release form (Gocovri) was approved in 2017 for levodopa-induced dyskinesia.", "timeline": [["1960s", "DuPont develops amantadine as an antiviral."], ["1966", "The FDA approves amantadine for influenza A prophylaxis."], ["1969", "Schwab and colleagues report its benefit in Parkinson disease."], ["2006", "The CDC advises against amantadine for flu because of widespread resistance."], ["2017", "Extended-release amantadine (Gocovri) is approved for dyskinesia."]], "cell": "Amantadine has several actions. In Parkinson disease, it is a weak noncompetitive antagonist of NMDA glutamate receptors, blocking the channel. Overactive glutamate signaling in the basal ganglia contributes to levodopa-induced dyskinesias, so NMDA blockade reduces them. It also increases dopamine release from surviving nigrostriatal nerve terminals and inhibits dopamine reuptake, boosting dopamine signaling in the striatum, and it has mild anticholinergic effects. As an antiviral, amantadine blocks the M2 proton channel of influenza A. This stops acidification inside the virus particle, so it cannot uncoat and release its RNA into the host cell. Influenza B lacks this M2 protein, and a single mutation (S31N) makes most current influenza A strains resistant.", "body": [["Brain (movement)", "Modestly improves tremor, rigidity and slowness in Parkinson disease and reduces involuntary dyskinesias caused by levodopa."], ["Brain (mind)", "Can cause confusion, hallucinations, vivid dreams and insomnia, especially in older adults and in kidney disease."], ["Skin", "Causes livedo reticularis, a purple lace-like pattern on the legs, from local blood vessel changes."], ["Legs and fluids", "Ankle swelling (peripheral edema) is common."], ["Blood pressure", "Can cause orthostatic hypotension and dizziness on standing."], ["Gut and bladder", "Mild anticholinergic effects such as dry mouth, constipation and urinary retention."]], "journey": "Amantadine is well absorbed by mouth and distributes widely, crossing the blood-brain barrier easily. It is not significantly metabolized by the liver. It leaves the body mostly unchanged in the urine. The half-life is about sixteen hours in healthy adults but can be much longer in older adults and kidney disease, so doses are reduced. Extended-release forms are taken at bedtime so peak levels occur in the morning.", "sidefx": "Confusion and hallucinations reflect added dopamine activity and NMDA blockade in the brain, worsened by build-up in kidney failure. Anticholinergic effects cause dry mouth and urinary retention. Livedo reticularis and leg edema are thought to come from local release of catecholamines narrowing skin vessels. Overdose can cause seizures, arrhythmias and agitation. Stopping suddenly can trigger a syndrome similar to neuroleptic malignant syndrome, with fever and rigidity, because dopamine signaling falls abruptly; it should be tapered. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Amantadine's structure is based on adamantane, a molecule shaped like a tiny piece of a diamond crystal lattice, which gives the drug its name.", "src": ["Gocovri and amantadine FDA labels", "Schwab RS et al. JAMA, 1969", "CDC influenza antiviral guidance"]}, "amiloride": {"origin": "Amiloride was developed by scientists at Merck Sharp & Dohme in the 1960s, during a search for diuretics that would not drain the body's potassium the way thiazide and loop diuretics do. Instead of blocking aldosterone like spironolactone, amiloride turned out to block a sodium channel directly. It became a standard potassium-sparing diuretic and was combined with hydrochlorothiazide (Moduretic) to balance potassium loss. Later, amiloride became a valuable research tool, used in laboratories to identify and study the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC).", "timeline": [["1960s", "Merck scientists develop amiloride as a potassium-sparing diuretic."], ["1980s", "Amiloride and the HCTZ combination are marketed in the US."], ["1990s", "ENaC is cloned; amiloride is a defining blocker of the channel."]], "cell": "In the late distal tubule and cortical collecting duct, principal cells reabsorb sodium through the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) on the luminal side. Sodium moving into the cell makes the tubule lumen electrically negative, which pulls potassium out through ROMK channels and helps intercalated cells secrete hydrogen ions. Amiloride plugs the ENaC pore from the lumen. Sodium reabsorption at this site drops, so a little more sodium and water stay in the urine. With less lumen-negative voltage, potassium and hydrogen secretion fall, so potassium and acid are retained. This works whether or not aldosterone is present, unlike spironolactone. Amiloride also blocks lithium entry through ENaC, which is why it is used for lithium-induced nephrogenic diabetes insipidus.", "body": [["Kidneys", "Causes mild sodium and water loss while holding on to potassium, hydrogen ions and magnesium."], ["Blood potassium", "Raises serum potassium, which offsets losses from thiazide and loop diuretics but can cause dangerous hyperkalemia."], ["Blood pressure", "Lowers blood pressure modestly, mostly when combined with another diuretic."], ["Acid-base balance", "Reduced acid excretion can cause a mild metabolic acidosis."], ["Lithium handling", "Limits lithium's entry into collecting duct cells, reducing lithium-induced diabetes insipidus."]], "journey": "Amiloride is taken by mouth and is only partly absorbed, with less absorbed when taken with food. It is not metabolized by the liver. It is secreted into the proximal tubule by organic cation transporters and reaches its target from inside the tubule fluid, then leaves unchanged in the urine. Onset is about two hours, with the peak at six to ten hours and effects lasting about a day. Kidney impairment raises levels and the risk of hyperkalemia.", "sidefx": "Hyperkalemia is the main danger and follows directly from blocking ENaC, which reduces the electrical drive for potassium secretion. Risk is highest with kidney disease, diabetes, older age, potassium supplements, ACE inhibitors, ARBs or other potassium-sparing drugs. Severe hyperkalemia can cause fatal arrhythmias. Hyponatremia and metabolic acidosis can also occur. There is no specific antidote; hyperkalemia is treated with calcium to stabilize the heart, insulin with glucose and other potassium-lowering measures.", "fun": "Amiloride has been studied as an inhaled treatment for cystic fibrosis, where overactive ENaC in the airways dries out mucus.", "src": ["Amiloride hydrochloride FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "amitriptyline": {"origin": "The first tricyclic antidepressant, imipramine, was found in the 1950s when Swiss psychiatrist Roland Kuhn noticed that a compound tested as an antipsychotic lifted depression. Drug companies quickly made relatives. Amitriptyline, with a slightly different three-ring structure, was developed by Merck and approved by the FDA in 1961 as Elavil. It became one of the most prescribed antidepressants for decades. As SSRIs replaced tricyclics for depression in the 1990s, amitriptyline found a lasting role at low doses for chronic pain, nerve pain, migraine prevention and insomnia.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Roland Kuhn discovers the antidepressant effect of imipramine, the first tricyclic."], ["1961", "The FDA approves amitriptyline (Elavil) for depression."], ["1990s", "SSRIs largely replace tricyclics for depression; amitriptyline remains common for chronic pain."]], "cell": "Amitriptyline inhibits the serotonin transporter (SERT) and norepinephrine transporter (NET) on presynaptic nerve endings. More serotonin and norepinephrine stay in the synapse. Over several weeks, receptors and downstream signaling adapt, which is thought to underlie the antidepressant effect. In the spinal cord, more serotonin and norepinephrine strengthen descending pain inhibition, which explains its use in neuropathic pain at low doses. The liver converts it to nortriptyline, which is more selective for NET. Amitriptyline also blocks histamine H1 receptors (sedation, weight gain), muscarinic receptors (anticholinergic effects) and alpha-1 receptors (orthostatic hypotension). At high levels it blocks fast sodium channels in the heart, slowing conduction and widening the QRS.", "body": [["Brain (mood)", "Lifts depression gradually over weeks by boosting serotonin and norepinephrine signaling."], ["Pain pathways", "Reduces nerve pain, fibromyalgia pain and migraine frequency by strengthening descending pain-inhibiting pathways."], ["Sleep", "Strong H1 blockade causes sedation, which helps insomnia but causes daytime grogginess."], ["Heart", "Speeds the heart rate, can cause orthostatic hypotension and, in overdose, widens the QRS and causes deadly arrhythmias."], ["Autonomic system", "Muscarinic blockade causes dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention, blurred vision and confusion in older adults."], ["Weight", "Increased appetite and weight gain from H1 and serotonin receptor effects."]], "journey": "Amitriptyline is well absorbed by mouth but has a large first-pass effect. It is very lipid-soluble and protein bound, spreading widely into tissues and crossing the blood-brain barrier and placenta. The liver demethylates it, mainly by CYP2C19, to active nortriptyline, and both are hydroxylated by CYP2D6, so genetic CYP2D6 differences change levels. Metabolites leave through the kidneys. The half-life is roughly ten to twenty-eight hours, allowing once daily bedtime dosing. Mood effects take weeks, while sedation is immediate.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from receptors it blocks besides the transporters: dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention and confusion from muscarinic blockade, sedation and weight gain from H1 blockade, and dizziness on standing from alpha-1 blockade. In overdose, sodium channel blockade in the heart widens the QRS and causes ventricular arrhythmias, while anticholinergic toxicity, seizures and hypotension follow. Overdose is treated with sodium bicarbonate, which raises sodium load and blood pH to overcome the sodium channel block. Like all antidepressants, it carries a boxed warning for suicidal thoughts in young people.", "fun": "A classic memory aid for tricyclic overdose is the 3 Cs: convulsions, coma and cardiotoxicity.", "src": ["Amitriptyline hydrochloride FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]}, "amoxicillin-clavulanate": {"origin": "By the 1970s many bacteria had learned to destroy penicillins with beta-lactamase enzymes. Scientists at Beecham Research Laboratories in England screened soil microbes for natural beta-lactamase blockers and, in the mid-1970s, found clavulanic acid made by the bacterium Streptomyces clavuligerus. On its own it is a weak antibiotic, but it acts as a decoy that disables beta-lactamase. Beecham paired it with its own amoxicillin to protect it. The combination, Augmentin, launched in the UK in 1981 and in the US in 1984. It was the first beta-lactam and beta-lactamase inhibitor combination and restored amoxicillin's power against many resistant strains.", "timeline": [["1972", "Beecham launches amoxicillin."], ["1970s", "Beecham scientists discover clavulanic acid from Streptomyces clavuligerus."], ["1981", "Amoxicillin-clavulanate (Augmentin) launches in the UK."], ["1984", "Augmentin is approved in the US."]], "cell": "Amoxicillin is a beta-lactam that binds and inactivates penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), the transpeptidases that cross-link peptidoglycan in the bacterial cell wall. Without cross-links the wall weakens and autolysins cause the cell to burst. Many bacteria resist by secreting beta-lactamase enzymes that hydrolyze the beta-lactam ring before the drug reaches the PBPs. Clavulanate also has a beta-lactam ring, so beta-lactamase binds it. Once bound, clavulanate's structure rearranges and forms a stable, covalent complex that permanently inactivates the enzyme. It is a 'suicide inhibitor.' With the enzyme disabled, amoxicillin survives to reach its targets. Clavulanate works well against many class A beta-lactamases, such as those of Staphylococcus aureus, Haemophilus influenzae, Moraxella catarrhalis, E. coli, Klebsiella and Bacteroides. It does not help against resistance due to altered PBPs, such as MRSA or penicillin-resistant pneumococcus.", "body": [["Infected tissue", "Treats bite wounds, sinusitis, otitis media, pneumonia, skin and urinary infections where beta-lactamase producers and anaerobes are likely."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is more common than with amoxicillin alone, largely due to clavulanate; C. difficile colitis can occur."], ["Liver", "Can cause cholestatic hepatitis, usually linked to clavulanate and more common in older men and with longer courses; it may appear after the drug is stopped."], ["Skin and immune system", "Rash, hives and, rarely, anaphylaxis or severe skin reactions."], ["Kidneys", "Amoxicillin is cleared by the kidneys, so doses are adjusted in kidney disease."]], "journey": "Both parts are well absorbed by mouth, and taking the dose at the start of a meal improves clavulanate absorption and reduces stomach upset. Both reach peak levels in about one to two hours and spread into most tissues, including middle ear fluid and sinuses. Amoxicillin is excreted mostly unchanged by the kidneys. Clavulanate is metabolized more in the liver, with part excreted in the urine. Both have half-lives of about one hour. Formulations differ in the ratio of amoxicillin to clavulanate, so products are not interchangeable.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea is the most common problem, partly from altered gut flora and partly from clavulanate itself, which stimulates gut motility. Allergic reactions happen because the beta-lactam ring forms antigens bound to body proteins, so penicillin-allergic patients should not take it. Clavulanate is associated with cholestatic liver injury, thought to be immune-mediated, which explains why it can show up weeks after therapy ends. As with other antibiotics, disruption of normal bacteria allows C. difficile and yeast infections. Epinephrine treats anaphylaxis.", "fun": "Clavulanic acid is named after Streptomyces clavuligerus, the bacterium it comes from, whose name means 'club-bearing' for the shape of its spore structures.", "src": ["Augmentin (amoxicillin-clavulanate) FDA label", "Reading C, Cole M. Antimicrob Agents Chemother, 1977", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "amphetamine-dextroamphetamine": {"origin": "Amphetamine was first synthesized in 1887 by the Romanian chemist Lazar Edeleanu in Berlin, but its effects were not studied until the American chemist Gordon Alles made it again in 1929 while looking for a substitute for ephedrine. Smith, Kline & French sold it as the Benzedrine inhaler for nasal congestion starting in the early 1930s. In 1937 psychiatrist Charles Bradley noticed that Benzedrine helped children with behavior problems focus, an early sign of its ADHD use. Amphetamines were widely given to soldiers in World War II and later sold as diet pills. A mixed amphetamine salt diet pill called Obetrol was reformulated and approved in 1996 as Adderall for ADHD, and the extended-release form followed in 2001.", "timeline": [["1887", "Lazar Edeleanu first synthesizes amphetamine."], ["1929", "Gordon Alles resynthesizes amphetamine and describes its effects."], ["1930s", "Smith, Kline & French markets the Benzedrine inhaler."], ["1937", "Charles Bradley reports Benzedrine improves behavior and focus in children."], ["1996", "Mixed amphetamine salts are approved as Adderall for ADHD."], ["2001", "Adderall XR is approved."]], "cell": "Adderall contains a mix of amphetamine salts in a 3 to 1 ratio of dextroamphetamine (d) to levoamphetamine (l). Amphetamine is taken into dopamine and norepinephrine nerve terminals by the dopamine transporter (DAT) and norepinephrine transporter (NET). Inside, it enters synaptic vesicles through VMAT2 and disrupts the proton gradient that stores neurotransmitter, so dopamine and norepinephrine leak into the cytoplasm. Amphetamine also activates the receptor TAAR1, which triggers phosphorylation of DAT and NET and causes them to run in reverse, pumping neurotransmitter out of the cell into the synapse. It also blocks reuptake and weakly inhibits monoamine oxidase. The result is a large release of dopamine and norepinephrine that does not depend on nerve firing. In the prefrontal cortex this improves attention and impulse control; in the reward pathway it causes euphoria and potential for addiction. The d-isomer is more potent centrally.", "body": [["Brain (attention)", "Improves focus, attention and impulse control in ADHD and increases wakefulness in narcolepsy."], ["Brain (reward)", "Dopamine release in the nucleus accumbens can cause euphoria, making misuse and addiction possible."], ["Heart and vessels", "Released norepinephrine raises heart rate and blood pressure and can trigger arrhythmias in vulnerable hearts."], ["Appetite and weight", "Suppresses appetite through hypothalamic effects, often causing weight loss and slowed growth in children."], ["Sleep", "Causes insomnia, especially when doses are taken late in the day."], ["Body temperature", "At high doses it raises body temperature through muscle activity and central effects."]], "journey": "Amphetamine is well absorbed by mouth and starts working in about thirty to sixty minutes. The extended-release form uses two types of beads to give a second release hours later. It crosses the blood-brain barrier easily and crosses the placenta. The liver metabolizes part of it, including by CYP2D6, but much is excreted unchanged in the urine. Urine pH matters: acidic urine traps the charged drug and speeds its removal, while alkaline urine slows it. The half-life is about nine to fourteen hours, with the d-isomer slightly shorter than the l-isomer.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow from extra dopamine and norepinephrine. Norepinephrine raises heart rate and blood pressure, and stimulant misuse can cause arrhythmias, heart attack, stroke and sudden death, especially with structural heart disease. Dopamine and norepinephrine in the brain cause insomnia, anxiety, irritability, tics and at high doses psychosis. Appetite loss can slow growth. Overdose can cause hyperthermia, seizures and rhabdomyolysis. Combining with MAO inhibitors or serotonergic drugs can cause hypertensive crisis or serotonin syndrome. There is no specific antidote; benzodiazepines are used for agitation and seizures, with cooling and supportive care. It carries a boxed warning for abuse and dependence.", "fun": "Adderall was reformulated from an earlier weight-loss product called Obetrol, and it was rebranded for ADHD in the 1990s.", "src": ["Adderall and Adderall XR FDA labels", "Bradley C. Am J Psychiatry, 1937", "Rasmussen N. On Speed: The Many Lives of Amphetamine, 2008"]}, "amphotericin-b": {"origin": "Amphotericin B is a natural product of the soil bacterium Streptomyces nodosus, found in a soil sample collected near the Orinoco River in Venezuela. Scientists at the Squibb Institute for Medical Research isolated it in the mid-1950s; it was named for its amphoteric chemistry, meaning it can act as both acid and base. It reached patients in the late 1950s and for decades was the only reliable treatment for life-threatening fungal infections such as cryptococcal meningitis, histoplasmosis and invasive candidiasis. Its harsh side effects earned it the nickname 'amphoterrible.' Lipid formulations developed in the 1990s, including liposomal amphotericin B (AmBisome, approved in the US in 1997), made it gentler on the kidneys.", "timeline": [["1955", "Squibb scientists isolate amphotericin B from Streptomyces nodosus."], ["1950s", "Amphotericin B deoxycholate enters clinical use for systemic fungal infections."], ["1990s", "Lipid formulations such as Abelcet and AmBisome are developed."], ["1997", "Liposomal amphotericin B (AmBisome) is approved in the US."]], "cell": "Fungal cell membranes use ergosterol instead of cholesterol. Amphotericin B is a polyene with one water-loving side and one fat-loving side. It binds ergosterol in the fungal membrane, and several molecules group together to form pores. Potassium, magnesium and other small ions and molecules leak out of the cell, and the cell dies, so the drug is fungicidal. Recent work suggests it also pulls ergosterol out of the membrane like a sponge, further damaging it. Amphotericin also causes oxidative damage. It binds cholesterol in human cell membranes much more weakly, but enough to injure human cells, particularly in the kidney, which explains much of its toxicity. Resistance is uncommon and usually involves reduced ergosterol.", "body": [["Fungal infections", "Kills a wide range of fungi including Candida, Cryptococcus, Aspergillus, Histoplasma, Blastomyces, Coccidioides and Mucorales, and is used for severe, life-threatening infections."], ["Kidneys", "Constricts kidney arterioles and damages tubule cells, lowering kidney function and causing loss of potassium and magnesium (distal renal tubular acidosis)."], ["Immune response", "Triggers release of cytokines such as TNF and IL-1, causing fever, chills and rigors during infusions."], ["Blood", "Causes anemia by suppressing erythropoietin production in the kidney."], ["Veins", "Irritates veins, causing phlebitis at the infusion site."], ["Heart", "Severe low potassium and magnesium, or overdose, can cause arrhythmias and cardiac arrest."]], "journey": "Amphotericin B is not absorbed by mouth, so systemic infections are treated IV. It is highly protein bound and distributes into tissues such as the liver, spleen, kidneys and lungs, but penetrates cerebrospinal fluid poorly, though it still works in cryptococcal meningitis. Lipid formulations deliver the drug to the reticuloendothelial system and release it at infection sites, reducing kidney exposure. It is not well metabolized and is excreted very slowly by kidneys and bile; the terminal half-life is about fifteen days. Conventional and lipid forms have very different dosing and must never be confused.", "sidefx": "Fever, chills and rigors during infusion come from cytokine release triggered by the drug; premedication with acetaminophen, diphenhydramine or hydrocortisone and meperidine for rigors are often used. Kidney injury comes from both constriction of the afferent arterioles and direct damage to tubule cell membranes containing cholesterol; saline loading before infusion reduces it. Leaky tubules waste potassium and magnesium, so levels are watched closely. Anemia follows from lower erythropoietin. Lipid formulations cause much less kidney toxicity. There is no antidote, and confusing conventional with liposomal doses has caused fatal overdoses.", "fun": "The nickname 'amphoterrible' comes from the shaking chills and fever patients used to experience during the first infusions.", "src": ["Amphotericin B and AmBisome FDA labels", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "ampicillin": {"origin": "Ampicillin was the first broad-spectrum penicillin. Natural penicillin G, from Penicillium mold, worked mainly against gram-positive bacteria. In 1957 scientists at Beecham Research Laboratories in England isolated the core of penicillin, 6-aminopenicillanic acid, which allowed them to attach new side chains by chemistry. Adding an amino group produced ampicillin, which Beecham launched in 1961 as Penbritin. It could enter many gram-negative bacteria such as E. coli and Haemophilus influenzae, which penicillin G could not. Ampicillin remains an important IV antibiotic, especially for Listeria, enterococci and neonatal sepsis.", "timeline": [["1928", "Alexander Fleming discovers penicillin."], ["1957", "Beecham isolates 6-aminopenicillanic acid, the penicillin core."], ["1961", "Beecham launches ampicillin, the first broad-spectrum penicillin."]], "cell": "Ampicillin is a beta-lactam antibiotic. Its beta-lactam ring resembles the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of peptidoglycan precursors. Penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), the transpeptidases that cross-link the bacterial cell wall, react with the ring and become covalently bound and inactivated. The wall cannot be cross-linked, autolysins keep breaking old wall, and the bacterium bursts from osmotic pressure. The effect is bactericidal and time-dependent: what matters most is how long drug levels stay above the minimum inhibitory concentration. The added amino group makes ampicillin more water-soluble so it passes through outer membrane porins of some gram-negative bacteria. Beta-lactamase enzymes destroy it, so resistance is common in staphylococci, many E. coli and some Haemophilus.", "body": [["Infected tissue", "Kills Listeria, enterococci, many streptococci and susceptible E. coli, Proteus and Haemophilus; used for meningitis, endocarditis and neonatal sepsis."], ["Brain and meninges", "Crosses inflamed meninges well enough to treat bacterial meningitis, including Listeria."], ["Gut", "Disrupts normal flora, causing diarrhea and sometimes C. difficile colitis."], ["Skin", "Rashes are common, and a non-allergic rash often occurs in patients with infectious mononucleosis."], ["Immune system", "True penicillin allergy can cause hives, angioedema or anaphylaxis."]], "journey": "Oral ampicillin is poorly absorbed compared with amoxicillin, so it is mainly given IV or IM. It distributes into most tissues and fluids and crosses inflamed meninges and the placenta. Little is metabolized. The kidneys clear it mostly unchanged by filtration and tubular secretion. The half-life is about one hour, so it is given every few hours, and doses are adjusted in kidney failure.", "sidefx": "Allergic reactions arise because the opened beta-lactam ring binds body proteins and forms antigens; anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine. Diarrhea and C. difficile result from killing normal gut bacteria. The rash in mononucleosis is a known non-allergic reaction. Very high levels, especially in kidney failure, can lower the seizure threshold. Rarely, interstitial nephritis and blood count changes occur.", "fun": "Ampicillin plus gentamicin is the classic empiric combination for newborns with suspected sepsis because ampicillin covers Listeria and enterococci, which cephalosporins miss.", "src": ["Ampicillin for injection FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "ampicillin-sulbactam": {"origin": "As beta-lactamase enzymes spread in the 1970s, drug companies searched for molecules that could block them. Scientists at Pfizer designed sulbactam, a synthetic penicillanic acid sulfone that acts as a beta-lactamase inhibitor. Pfizer paired it with ampicillin to make an IV combination, Unasyn, which became available in the US in the 1980s. It extended ampicillin's reach to beta-lactamase-producing Staphylococcus aureus, Haemophilus, Bacteroides and other anaerobes. Sulbactam turned out to have its own activity against Acinetobacter baumannii, which later made it useful against that hospital pathogen.", "timeline": [["1961", "Ampicillin is launched by Beecham."], ["1970s", "Pfizer develops sulbactam, a synthetic beta-lactamase inhibitor."], ["1980s", "Ampicillin-sulbactam (Unasyn) is approved in the US."], ["2023", "Sulbactam-durlobactam is approved for Acinetobacter infections."]], "cell": "Ampicillin binds penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), the transpeptidases that cross-link peptidoglycan. Without cross-linking, the cell wall fails and the bacterium lyses. Many bacteria resist by making beta-lactamases that break the beta-lactam ring. Sulbactam is itself a beta-lactam. Beta-lactamases bind and open its ring, but the result is a stable, covalent enzyme complex that permanently inactivates the enzyme, so sulbactam is a suicide inhibitor. This protects ampicillin from many class A enzymes produced by S. aureus, H. influenzae, Moraxella, E. coli, Klebsiella and Bacteroides. Sulbactam also binds PBP1 and PBP3 of Acinetobacter baumannii and kills it directly. The combination does not overcome resistance due to altered PBPs, such as MRSA, or most AmpC and extended-spectrum beta-lactamases.", "body": [["Infected tissue", "Treats skin and soft tissue, intra-abdominal, gynecologic, bite wound and aspiration infections that involve mixed aerobic and anaerobic bacteria."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and C. difficile colitis can occur from disrupted flora."], ["Veins and muscle", "IV infusion can cause phlebitis and IM injection can be painful."], ["Immune system", "Contains a penicillin, so penicillin-allergic patients can have hives, angioedema or anaphylaxis."], ["Liver and blood", "Mild liver enzyme elevations and changes in blood counts can occur."]], "journey": "Ampicillin-sulbactam is given IV or IM because neither part is absorbed well enough by mouth. Both drugs distribute widely into tissues and fluids, including peritoneal fluid and inflamed meninges, and cross the placenta. Neither is significantly metabolized. Both are cleared mainly unchanged by the kidneys, with half-lives of about one hour, so doses are spaced every several hours and adjusted in kidney impairment.", "sidefx": "The side effects mostly come from ampicillin. Allergic reactions occur because the beta-lactam ring forms protein-bound antigens; cross-reactivity with other penicillins is high. Killing normal gut flora leads to diarrhea and C. difficile infection. Local vein irritation causes phlebitis. Seizures can occur with very high levels in kidney failure. Epinephrine treats anaphylaxis.", "fun": "Sulbactam is one of the few beta-lactamase inhibitors that kills a bacterium on its own, which is why it is the active ingredient targeted against Acinetobacter.", "src": ["Unasyn (ampicillin-sulbactam) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "anastrozole": {"origin": "In postmenopausal women most estrogen comes not from the ovaries but from the conversion of androgens in fat tissue by the enzyme aromatase. Researchers realized that blocking this enzyme could starve estrogen-dependent breast cancers. Early aromatase inhibitors such as aminoglutethimide were not selective and had many side effects. Anastrozole, a selective nonsteroidal aromatase inhibitor, was developed by Zeneca (later AstraZeneca) in the UK and approved by the FDA in 1995 as Arimidex for advanced breast cancer. The large ATAC trial, first reported in the early 2000s, showed it prevented recurrence better than tamoxifen in early breast cancer, and it became a standard of care.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Aminoglutethimide, an early nonselective aromatase inhibitor, is used for breast cancer."], ["1995", "The FDA approves anastrozole (Arimidex) for advanced breast cancer."], ["2002", "First ATAC trial results compare anastrozole with tamoxifen in early breast cancer."], ["2002", "Anastrozole is approved for adjuvant treatment of early breast cancer."]], "cell": "Aromatase is a cytochrome P450 enzyme (CYP19A1) that converts androstenedione to estrone and testosterone to estradiol. After menopause, aromatase in fat, muscle, skin and breast tissue is the main estrogen source. Anastrozole is a triazole that binds reversibly to the heme iron in aromatase's active site, competing with its androgen substrates. Estrogen synthesis falls sharply, lowering blood estradiol by roughly eighty percent or more. In estrogen receptor-positive breast cancer cells, less estrogen means less estrogen receptor activation, less transcription of growth-promoting genes such as cyclin D1, and slower tumor growth. In premenopausal women, falling estrogen would trigger more ovarian stimulation through gonadotropins, so anastrozole alone does not work in women with functioning ovaries.", "body": [["Breast tissue", "Deprives estrogen receptor-positive tumors of estrogen, lowering recurrence and slowing advanced disease."], ["Bone", "Low estrogen speeds bone loss, raising osteoporosis and fracture risk; bone density is monitored."], ["Joints and muscles", "Joint pain and stiffness are common and a frequent reason patients stop the drug."], ["Brain and blood vessels", "Hot flashes, mood changes and fatigue come from estrogen withdrawal."], ["Vagina and urinary tract", "Vaginal dryness and painful intercourse due to low estrogen."], ["Lipids and heart", "Can raise cholesterol, and ischemic heart events have been reported in women with prior heart disease."]], "journey": "Anastrozole is well absorbed by mouth, with peak levels in about two hours, and food does not matter much. It is modestly protein bound. The liver metabolizes it by N-dealkylation, hydroxylation and glucuronidation into inactive products, which leave through the kidneys. Its half-life is about two days, so it reaches steady state in about a week with once daily dosing. Estradiol suppression starts within a day.", "sidefx": "Nearly all side effects come from estrogen deprivation. Hot flashes and mood changes reflect estrogen loss in the brain's temperature control centers. Joint pain is a well-known effect of aromatase inhibitors, likely tied to estrogen's role in joint tissue. Without estrogen's brake on osteoclasts, bone loss accelerates; bisphosphonates or denosumab may be used to protect bone. Unlike tamoxifen, it does not raise the risk of endometrial cancer or blood clots, because it lowers estrogen rather than acting on estrogen receptors. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Anastrozole is sometimes misused by male bodybuilders taking anabolic steroids to block conversion of testosterone to estrogen and prevent breast growth.", "src": ["Arimidex (anastrozole) FDA label", "ATAC Trialists' Group. Lancet, 2002", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "argatroban": {"origin": "Argatroban was designed in Japan in the late 1970s and 1980s by Shosuke Okamoto and colleagues working with Mitsubishi Chemical. They built a small synthetic molecule derived from the amino acid L-arginine to fit the active site of thrombin. It was first used in Japan, and in the US the FDA approved it in 2000 for preventing and treating clots in heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT), a dangerous immune reaction to heparin. Because argatroban is not related to heparin, HIT antibodies do not react with it, which made it a key option for these patients.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Okamoto and colleagues in Japan begin designing arginine-based thrombin inhibitors."], ["2000", "The FDA approves argatroban for heparin-induced thrombocytopenia."]], "cell": "Thrombin (factor IIa) is the central enzyme of clotting. It cleaves fibrinogen into fibrin, activates factors V, VIII, XI and XIII, activates protein C and strongly activates platelets. Argatroban is a small, univalent direct thrombin inhibitor. It binds reversibly to the catalytic active site of thrombin, without needing antithrombin. Because it is small, it inhibits both free thrombin and thrombin already bound to fibrin in clots, which heparin does poorly. Fibrin formation, amplification of the cascade and thrombin-induced platelet aggregation all fall. In HIT, antibodies against heparin-platelet factor 4 complexes activate platelets and cause huge thrombin generation, and argatroban blocks this thrombin without reacting with those antibodies.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Blocks thrombin, preventing new clots and the growth of existing ones in HIT and during PCI."], ["Platelets", "Reduces thrombin-driven platelet activation, helping platelet counts recover in HIT once heparin is stopped."], ["Coagulation tests", "Prolongs aPTT (used for monitoring), ACT, PT and INR, which complicates switching to warfarin."], ["Bleeding sites", "Bleeding from IV sites, gums, GI and urinary tracts can occur."], ["Liver", "Cleared by the liver, so liver disease greatly prolongs its effect."]], "journey": "Argatroban is given IV as a continuous infusion. It works immediately and reaches steady state in one to three hours. It is metabolized in the liver by hydroxylation and aromatization, with CYP3A4/5 involved, and is excreted mainly in bile and stool. Its half-life is under an hour, so clotting function returns within a few hours after stopping in normal liver function. Because it is not cleared by the kidneys, it is useful in kidney failure, but doses must be reduced in liver impairment.", "sidefx": "Bleeding is the main toxicity because thrombin inhibition impairs normal clot formation. Intracranial and major internal bleeding are the most serious risks. Argatroban raises the INR, so when patients transition to warfarin, the INR overstates warfarin's effect and requires special handling. There is no specific reversal agent. Because the half-life is short, stopping the infusion and supportive care are the main treatment for bleeding.", "fun": "Argatroban's name reflects its origin: 'arg' for arginine, the amino acid it is built from, and 'troban' for thrombin inhibitor.", "src": ["Argatroban injection FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "aripiprazole": {"origin": "Aripiprazole was discovered by scientists at Otsuka Pharmaceutical in Japan in the late 1980s, in a program led by Tetsuro Kikuchi and colleagues who were looking for an antipsychotic that would calm overactive dopamine signaling without fully shutting it off. The result was the first dopamine D2 partial agonist used to treat schizophrenia, sometimes called a 'dopamine system stabilizer.' Otsuka developed it with Bristol-Myers Squibb, and the FDA approved it as Abilify in 2002. Later approvals added bipolar mania, adjunctive treatment of depression, irritability in autism and Tourette syndrome. It became one of the top-selling drugs in the US.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Otsuka scientists in Japan discover aripiprazole."], ["2002", "The FDA approves aripiprazole (Abilify) for schizophrenia."], ["2007", "Aripiprazole becomes the first drug approved as an add-on treatment for major depression."], ["2013", "A long-acting injectable form (Abilify Maintena) is approved."]], "cell": "Most antipsychotics are full antagonists that block dopamine D2 receptors. Aripiprazole is a high-affinity partial agonist at D2 receptors. A partial agonist binds the receptor and produces a smaller response than dopamine. In brain areas where dopamine is high, such as the mesolimbic pathway in psychosis, it displaces dopamine and lowers the overall signal, acting like an antagonist and reducing positive symptoms. Where dopamine is low, such as the mesocortical pathway, it provides some stimulation, which may help negative and cognitive symptoms. It is also a partial agonist at serotonin 5-HT1A receptors and an antagonist at 5-HT2A receptors, which may further reduce motor side effects and support mood effects. Because D2 signaling in the pituitary is partly preserved, prolactin usually does not rise and may fall.", "body": [["Brain (psychosis)", "Reduces hallucinations and delusions in schizophrenia and mania by lowering excess dopamine signaling."], ["Brain (mood)", "Used as an add-on to antidepressants in major depression."], ["Movement", "Restlessness (akathisia) is common; other extrapyramidal effects and tardive dyskinesia are less likely than with older drugs but possible."], ["Hormones", "Usually does not raise prolactin, unlike many antipsychotics."], ["Metabolism", "Lower risk of weight gain and diabetes than many second-generation antipsychotics, though changes can still occur."], ["Behavior", "Rarely triggers compulsive gambling, shopping, eating or sexual urges from its dopamine agonist activity."]], "journey": "Aripiprazole is well absorbed by mouth with peak levels in three to five hours. It crosses the blood-brain barrier and is highly protein bound. The liver metabolizes it by CYP2D6 and CYP3A4 into dehydro-aripiprazole, an active metabolite. Strong inhibitors of these enzymes, or being a CYP2D6 poor metabolizer, raise levels, so doses are adjusted. It leaves mostly in stool and some in urine. Its half-life is about seventy-five hours, and the active metabolite lasts even longer. Long-acting injections release drug over weeks to months.", "sidefx": "Akathisia, an inner restlessness, is thought to arise from its effects on dopamine in motor circuits. Because it partly stimulates D2 receptors, it can drive impulse control problems such as pathological gambling. Like all antipsychotics, it carries a boxed warning for increased death in elderly patients with dementia-related psychosis and for suicidal thoughts in young people when used for depression. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome, with fever, rigidity and autonomic instability, is a rare but life-threatening effect of dopamine blockade. There is no antidote; NMS is treated by stopping the drug and supportive care.", "fun": "Aripiprazole is often called a 'third-generation' antipsychotic because its partial agonist action differs from both older and newer full D2 antagonists.", "src": ["Abilify (aripiprazole) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology"]}, "artemether-lumefantrine": {"origin": "Artemether comes from artemisinin, a compound in sweet wormwood (Artemisia annua), a plant used in traditional Chinese medicine for fevers. In 1967 China launched a secret military research program, Project 523, to find new malaria treatments. Pharmacologist Tu Youyou and her team searched ancient texts and found a recipe by Ge Hong from around 340 AD that soaked the herb in cold water. Using a low-temperature extraction, they isolated artemisinin in 1972. Chinese scientists then made derivatives such as artemether, and the Academy of Military Medical Sciences in Beijing developed lumefantrine. Novartis partnered to bring the fixed combination, Coartem, to the world, and in 2009 it became the first artemisinin-based combination approved by the FDA. Tu Youyou received the 2015 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine.", "timeline": [["c. 340", "Ge Hong describes using sweet wormwood steeped in cold water for fevers."], ["1967", "China launches Project 523 to find new antimalarials."], ["1972", "Tu Youyou's team isolates artemisinin from Artemisia annua."], ["2009", "The FDA approves artemether-lumefantrine (Coartem)."], ["2015", "Tu Youyou is awarded the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."]], "cell": "Malaria parasites live inside red blood cells and digest hemoglobin, releasing toxic free heme. They protect themselves by polymerizing heme into inert crystals called hemozoin. Artemether, and its active metabolite dihydroartemisinin, contain an endoperoxide bridge. Inside the parasite, iron in heme cleaves this bridge, generating carbon-centered free radicals that alkylate and damage parasite proteins and membranes and may inhibit the parasite calcium pump PfATP6. This kills blood-stage parasites very rapidly. Artemether is short-lived, so it is paired with lumefantrine, a long-acting aryl-amino alcohol that is thought to block heme detoxification by interfering with hemozoin formation, letting toxic heme build up. Lumefantrine clears the parasites that survive the first strike and reduces the chance of resistance. Neither drug kills the dormant liver forms of P. vivax or P. ovale.", "body": [["Blood", "Rapidly lowers parasite levels in red blood cells, ending fever cycles in uncomplicated falciparum malaria."], ["Heart", "Lumefantrine can prolong the QT interval, so it is avoided with other QT-prolonging drugs."], ["Brain and nerves", "Headache, dizziness and sleep problems are common and partly due to the infection itself."], ["Muscles and joints", "Muscle and joint aches and weakness are common side effects."], ["Gut and appetite", "Loss of appetite, nausea and abdominal pain can occur."], ["Red blood cells", "Delayed hemolysis can appear weeks after artemisinin treatment, especially in severe malaria."]], "journey": "Both drugs are taken by mouth with a fatty meal or milk, which greatly increases absorption of lumefantrine. Artemether is absorbed quickly and converted by CYP3A4 into dihydroartemisinin; both have half-lives of about two hours. Lumefantrine is very fat-soluble and highly protein bound, absorbed more slowly, and has a half-life of several days, so it keeps killing parasites after artemether is gone. Lumefantrine is metabolized by CYP3A4, and both drugs are removed mostly through the liver and stool. Strong CYP3A4 inducers can make treatment fail.", "sidefx": "Most side effects, such as headache, dizziness, weakness and aches, are mild and overlap with malaria symptoms. Lumefantrine affects cardiac repolarization and can prolong the QT interval, which matters with other QT drugs or electrolyte problems. Artemisinins can cause delayed hemolysis because red cells that held parasites are cleared by the spleen. Artemisinin resistance has emerged in Southeast Asia and parts of Africa through mutations in the parasite kelch13 gene. There is no antidote; treatment is supportive.", "fun": "Tu Youyou's breakthrough came from a single line in a 4th century handbook of emergency remedies, which suggested a cold extraction rather than boiling, which would have destroyed the active compound.", "src": ["Coartem (artemether-lumefantrine) FDA label", "Tu Y. Nobel Lecture, 2015", "CDC Malaria Treatment Guidelines"]}, "atenolol": {"origin": "Beta blockers began with James Black at Imperial Chemical Industries (ICI) in England, who developed propranolol in the early 1960s to reduce the heart's oxygen demand in angina. Propranolol blocked beta receptors everywhere, including the lungs. ICI chemists then looked for drugs that preferred the heart's beta-1 receptors, and atenolol was introduced in the 1970s as Tenormin. It was cardioselective and water-soluble, so it entered the brain less and caused fewer sleep disturbances. The FDA approved it in 1981, and it became one of the most prescribed drugs for high blood pressure. Black received the 1988 Nobel Prize for the beta blocker and H2 blocker work.", "timeline": [["1960s", "James Black at ICI develops propranolol, the first widely used beta blocker."], ["1970s", "ICI introduces atenolol (Tenormin), a cardioselective beta-1 blocker."], ["1981", "The FDA approves atenolol."], ["1988", "James Black shares the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."]], "cell": "Norepinephrine and epinephrine bind beta-1 adrenergic receptors in the heart and kidney, which couple to Gs proteins. Gs activates adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP and activating protein kinase A. In the SA node this speeds pacemaker firing; in heart muscle it increases calcium entry and contraction; in the AV node it speeds conduction; and in juxtaglomerular cells it releases renin. Atenolol competitively blocks beta-1 receptors, so cAMP signaling falls. Heart rate, contractility and AV conduction decrease, lowering cardiac output and myocardial oxygen demand. Less renin release reduces angiotensin II and aldosterone. Atenolol is relatively selective for beta-1 over beta-2 receptors, but that selectivity fades at higher doses. It has no intrinsic sympathomimetic activity or membrane-stabilizing action.", "body": [["Heart", "Slows heart rate and reduces the force of contraction, lowering oxygen demand and relieving angina; slows AV conduction to control fast atrial rhythms."], ["Blood pressure", "Lowers blood pressure through lower cardiac output and reduced renin release."], ["Kidneys", "Less renin means less angiotensin II and aldosterone; the kidneys also clear atenolol, so kidney disease raises levels."], ["Lungs", "Relatively heart-selective, but at higher doses it can still block airway beta-2 receptors and trigger bronchospasm."], ["Metabolism", "Can mask tachycardia and tremor that warn of low blood sugar in diabetes."], ["Hands and feet", "Cold extremities from reduced cardiac output and unopposed alpha vasoconstriction."]], "journey": "Atenolol is taken by mouth and about half of a dose is absorbed. It is water-soluble, so it crosses the blood-brain barrier poorly, causing fewer central effects than propranolol. It crosses the placenta. It is barely metabolized by the liver and is excreted mostly unchanged in the urine, with a half-life of about six to seven hours that becomes much longer in kidney failure. Its effect on blood pressure lasts about twenty-four hours. It is removed by hemodialysis.", "sidefx": "Bradycardia, heart block, fatigue and exercise intolerance come directly from beta-1 blockade of the heart. Worsening heart failure can occur when contractility falls in a weak heart. Bronchospasm comes from partial beta-2 blockade, a concern in asthma. In diabetes, beta blockers can hide warning signs of hypoglycemia. Stopping suddenly after long use can cause rebound tachycardia, angina or infarction because receptors have upregulated. In overdose, IV glucagon bypasses the beta receptor to raise cAMP in the heart; atropine, calcium, high-dose insulin and vasopressors are also used.", "fun": "Beta blocker names end in -olol. Drugs from A to M, like atenolol and metoprolol, are mostly beta-1 selective, a handy but imperfect memory aid.", "src": ["Tenormin (atenolol) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Black JW. Nobel Lecture, 1988"]}, "atomoxetine": {"origin": "Atomoxetine was developed at Eli Lilly, the company that also made fluoxetine. It was first studied in the 1980s as an antidepressant under the name tomoxetine, but it did not move forward for depression. In the 1990s researchers led by Lilly and Massachusetts General Hospital tested it for ADHD, since norepinephrine is important for attention. The name was changed to atomoxetine to avoid confusion with the breast cancer drug tamoxifen. The FDA approved it as Strattera in 2002, the first nonstimulant medication approved for ADHD and the first approved for adult ADHD. Because it is not a controlled substance, it offered an option for people at risk of stimulant misuse.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Eli Lilly studies tomoxetine as a possible antidepressant."], ["1990s", "Clinical trials test tomoxetine for ADHD."], ["2002", "The FDA approves atomoxetine (Strattera), the first nonstimulant ADHD drug."], ["2005", "A boxed warning for suicidal thoughts in children and teens is added."]], "cell": "Atomoxetine is a selective inhibitor of the presynaptic norepinephrine transporter (NET). Blocking NET prevents reuptake of norepinephrine, so its levels rise in the synapse. In the prefrontal cortex, where there are few dopamine transporters, NET also clears dopamine, so atomoxetine raises both norepinephrine and dopamine there. More stimulation of alpha-2A adrenergic and D1 receptors in the prefrontal cortex strengthens signaling in networks that control attention, working memory and impulse control. Because it does not raise dopamine in the nucleus accumbens or striatum the way stimulants do, it carries little abuse potential and does not cause tics. The full therapeutic effect takes several weeks as the brain adapts.", "body": [["Brain (attention)", "Improves attention, focus and impulse control over several weeks without stimulant-type euphoria."], ["Heart and vessels", "Raised norepinephrine modestly increases heart rate and blood pressure."], ["Gut and appetite", "Nausea, abdominal pain and loss of appetite are common, especially at the start."], ["Sleep and mood", "Can cause insomnia or sleepiness, irritability and, rarely, suicidal thoughts in young people."], ["Liver", "Rarely causes severe liver injury, so jaundice or dark urine is a reason to stop the drug."], ["Bladder and sexual function", "Urinary retention and erectile problems can occur in adults from increased adrenergic tone."]], "journey": "Atomoxetine is well absorbed by mouth, with peak levels in one to two hours. It is highly protein bound and crosses the blood-brain barrier. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP2D6 to 4-hydroxyatomoxetine, which is glucuronidated and excreted in the urine. CYP2D6 differences matter a lot: in poor metabolizers, the half-life rises from about five hours to around twenty-four hours, and levels are many times higher. Strong CYP2D6 inhibitors such as fluoxetine and paroxetine have a similar effect. Despite the short half-life in most people, once daily dosing works because the brain effect builds over time.", "sidefx": "Most side effects follow from increased norepinephrine: higher heart rate and blood pressure, insomnia, dry mouth, urinary retention and sexual dysfunction. Nausea and loss of appetite are common. Like antidepressants, it carries a boxed warning for suicidal thoughts in children and teens, so mood is monitored. Rare severe liver injury has occurred, and it should be stopped if jaundice develops. It should not be combined with MAO inhibitors, because of the risk of hypertensive crisis. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Atomoxetine started life as 'tomoxetine' and was renamed to avoid prescription mix-ups with tamoxifen.", "src": ["Strattera (atomoxetine) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology"]}, "azathioprine": {"origin": "Azathioprine was developed in the late 1950s by Gertrude Elion and George Hitchings at Burroughs Wellcome. They had already made 6-mercaptopurine (6-MP) for leukemia and created azathioprine as a prodrug of 6-MP. Around 1960 the British surgeon Roy Calne showed that thiopurine drugs prevented kidney rejection in dogs, and working in Joseph Murray's group in Boston he found azathioprine especially effective. In 1962 azathioprine helped make the first successful kidney transplants between unrelated humans possible. It was the main transplant drug until cyclosporine arrived in the 1980s, and it is still used today for autoimmune diseases such as rheumatoid arthritis, lupus and inflammatory bowel disease.", "timeline": [["1957", "Elion and Hitchings make azathioprine as a prodrug of 6-mercaptopurine."], ["1960", "Roy Calne reports that thiopurine drugs prevent kidney graft rejection in dogs."], ["1962", "Azathioprine enables the first successful unrelated donor kidney transplant in humans."], ["1960s", "Azathioprine becomes the standard drug to prevent transplant rejection."], ["1988", "Elion and Hitchings share the Nobel Prize with James Black."]], "cell": "Azathioprine is a prodrug. In red cells and tissues, glutathione splits it nonenzymatically into 6-mercaptopurine. The enzyme HGPRT converts 6-MP into thioinosine monophosphate, which is turned into thioguanine nucleotides. These purine look-alikes block several enzymes in de novo purine synthesis, including the first committed step (PRPP amidotransferase), and are incorporated into DNA and RNA, damaging them. Lymphocytes depend heavily on de novo purine synthesis to divide, so T and B cell proliferation falls, reducing antibody production and cell-mediated immunity. One thioguanine nucleotide also blocks the Rac1 signaling protein in T cells, triggering their apoptosis. 6-MP is inactivated by thiopurine methyltransferase (TPMT) and xanthine oxidase.", "body": [["Immune system", "Reduces T and B cell proliferation, helping prevent transplant rejection and control autoimmune disease over weeks to months."], ["Bone marrow", "Suppresses all blood cell lines, causing leukopenia, anemia and thrombocytopenia, especially with low TPMT or NUDT15 activity."], ["Infections", "Weakened immunity raises the risk of bacterial, viral and fungal infections."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are common at the start; pancreatitis can occur."], ["Liver", "Can cause hepatotoxicity, including cholestasis and veno-occlusive disease."], ["Skin and lymph nodes", "Long-term use raises the risk of skin cancer and lymphoma."]], "journey": "Azathioprine is well absorbed by mouth and quickly converted to 6-mercaptopurine. Its plasma half-life is short, a few hours, but its active nucleotides stay in cells for much longer, so the effect builds over weeks. 6-MP is inactivated by TPMT in tissues and by xanthine oxidase in the liver and gut. Metabolites are excreted in the urine. People with low or absent TPMT activity, or certain NUDT15 variants, build up toxic levels, so genetic testing is recommended before starting. Allopurinol and febuxostat block xanthine oxidase and raise levels sharply.", "sidefx": "Bone marrow suppression comes from the same purine starvation that targets lymphocytes, since blood cell precursors also divide quickly. This is most severe in TPMT or NUDT15 deficiency or when combined with allopurinol. Infection risk follows from immune suppression. Cancers, including skin cancer and lymphoma, are a boxed warning, reflecting reduced immune surveillance and DNA damage. Pancreatitis and liver injury are idiosyncratic effects. Nausea is common early on. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped or reduced, and severe marrow suppression may need growth factors or transfusion.", "fun": "Azathioprine is why allopurinol and azathioprine together are a classic exam trap: allopurinol blocks xanthine oxidase, a major route that inactivates 6-mercaptopurine.", "src": ["Imuran (azathioprine) FDA label", "Calne RY. The rejection of renal homografts. Lancet, 1960", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "aztreonam": {"origin": "In the late 1970s and early 1980s scientists at Squibb in the US and at Takeda in Japan independently found a new type of beta-lactam antibiotic made by soil bacteria. Unlike penicillins and cephalosporins, these molecules had a single beta-lactam ring with no second ring fused to it, so they were named monobactams. The natural compounds were weak, so Squibb chemists designed a synthetic version, aztreonam, with strong activity against gram-negative bacteria. It was approved by the FDA in the mid-1980s as Azactam. An inhaled form, Cayston, was approved in 2010 for Pseudomonas in cystic fibrosis. Aztreonam is especially valued because it is generally safe in patients with penicillin allergy.", "timeline": [["1981", "Squibb and Takeda report the discovery of monobactams from soil bacteria."], ["1980s", "Squibb develops aztreonam, a synthetic monobactam."], ["1986", "The FDA approves aztreonam (Azactam)."], ["2010", "Inhaled aztreonam (Cayston) is approved for cystic fibrosis."]], "cell": "Aztreonam is a monocyclic beta-lactam. Like other beta-lactams, it binds penicillin-binding proteins, the transpeptidases that cross-link peptidoglycan in the bacterial cell wall. It has a strong preference for PBP3 of aerobic gram-negative rods, an enzyme needed for building the septum during cell division. Blocking PBP3 makes the bacteria grow as long filaments that cannot divide and then lyse, so it is bactericidal. Its side chain resists many beta-lactamases, including some that destroy penicillins and cephalosporins and metallo-beta-lactamases, but it is broken down by extended-spectrum beta-lactamases and KPC carbapenemases. It barely binds the PBPs of gram-positive bacteria and anaerobes, so it has no useful activity against them.", "body": [["Infected tissue", "Kills aerobic gram-negative rods such as Pseudomonas aeruginosa, E. coli, Klebsiella and Serratia in lung, urinary, abdominal, skin and blood infections."], ["Lungs in cystic fibrosis", "Inhaled aztreonam reduces Pseudomonas in the airways and improves breathing."], ["Immune system", "Low cross-reactivity with penicillins and most cephalosporins, so it is used in serious penicillin allergy; it shares a side chain with ceftazidime."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and, rarely, C. difficile colitis."], ["Veins and skin", "Can cause phlebitis at the IV site and skin rash."], ["Liver and blood", "Mild liver enzyme rise and low platelets can occur."]], "journey": "Aztreonam is not absorbed by mouth, so it is given IV, IM or inhaled. It distributes widely into tissues and body fluids, including inflamed meninges, and crosses the placenta. It is minimally metabolized. The kidneys excrete most of it unchanged by filtration and tubular secretion, with a half-life of about two hours, so doses are reduced in kidney impairment. Inhaled aztreonam reaches high levels in the airways with little in the blood.", "sidefx": "Aztreonam is generally well tolerated. Allergic reactions are uncommon, and cross-reactivity with other beta-lactams is low because allergic reactions often target the side chain and the second ring, which aztreonam lacks. The exception is ceftazidime, which shares an identical side chain. Diarrhea and C. difficile follow from flora disruption. Phlebitis comes from local irritation. Inhaled aztreonam can cause cough and wheezing. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Aztreonam's single ring is what gave the class its name: 'monobactam' stands for monocyclic bacterially produced beta-lactam.", "src": ["Azactam and Cayston (aztreonam) FDA labels", "Sykes RB et al. Nature, 1981", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "baclofen": {"origin": "Baclofen was synthesized in 1962 by the Swiss chemist Heinrich Keberle at Ciba. It was designed as a version of the inhibitory neurotransmitter GABA that could cross into the brain, with the hope of treating epilepsy. It did not work well for seizures, but doctors noticed it relaxed tight, spastic muscles. It became a treatment for spasticity from multiple sclerosis and spinal cord injury, and was approved in the US in the 1970s as Lioresal. Its target, a then unknown GABA receptor that it activated but classic GABA blockers did not, was identified around 1980 by Norman Bowery and colleagues and named GABA-B. An intrathecal pump form for severe spasticity followed in the 1990s.", "timeline": [["1962", "Heinrich Keberle at Ciba synthesizes baclofen."], ["1970s", "Baclofen is approved in the US for spasticity."], ["1980", "Norman Bowery and colleagues identify the GABA-B receptor using baclofen."], ["1990s", "Intrathecal baclofen pumps are approved for severe spasticity."]], "cell": "Baclofen is a selective agonist at GABA-B receptors, which are G protein-coupled receptors (unlike GABA-A, which is a chloride channel). GABA-B receptors couple to Gi/o proteins. On presynaptic terminals of sensory and excitatory neurons in the spinal cord, activation inhibits voltage-gated calcium channels, so less calcium enters and less excitatory transmitter (glutamate, substance P) is released. On postsynaptic neurons, the G protein beta-gamma subunits open inward-rectifier potassium channels (GIRK), hyperpolarizing the cell. Adenylyl cyclase is also inhibited. Together, these effects dampen monosynaptic and polysynaptic reflexes in the spinal cord. Overactive stretch reflexes that cause spasticity and painful spasms quiet down. It does not act directly on skeletal muscle. GABA-B activation in the brain causes sedation and other central effects.", "body": [["Spinal cord", "Reduces exaggerated reflexes, relieving spasticity, clonus and painful spasms in multiple sclerosis, spinal cord injury and cerebral palsy."], ["Brain", "Causes drowsiness, dizziness and confusion; in overdose it can cause coma and seizures."], ["Muscles", "Muscle weakness and reduced tone can occur, which may affect walking and posture in some patients."], ["Breathing", "High doses or overdose can depress breathing."], ["Heart and vessels", "Can cause low blood pressure and slow heart rate in overdose."]], "journey": "Baclofen is well absorbed by mouth, peaking in a few hours. Because it is a polar molecule, it crosses the blood-brain barrier poorly, which limits how much reaches the spinal cord, so intrathecal pumps deliver tiny amounts directly into the spinal fluid. Very little is metabolized. The kidneys excrete it mostly unchanged, with a half-life of about three to four hours. In kidney failure it builds up quickly, causing toxicity, so it is used with great caution.", "sidefx": "Sedation, dizziness and weakness come from GABA-B activation in the brain and from reduced muscle tone. Accumulation in kidney failure can cause encephalopathy, coma and breathing problems. Abrupt withdrawal, especially from intrathecal pumps when a catheter fails or a refill is missed, is an emergency: loss of GABA-B tone causes rebound spasticity, high fever, mental changes, seizures and rhabdomyolysis, which can be fatal. The drug is tapered rather than stopped suddenly. There is no specific antidote; overdose is managed with supportive care, airway support and hemodialysis in kidney failure.", "fun": "Baclofen's discovery of a then unknown receptor gave the GABA-B receptor its identity; baclofen was the key used to tell GABA-B apart from GABA-A.", "src": ["Baclofen and Lioresal Intrathecal FDA labels", "Bowery NG et al. Nature, 1980", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "benazepril": {"origin": "ACE inhibitors began with a snake. In the 1960s the Brazilian scientist Sergio Ferreira found that the venom of the pit viper Bothrops jararaca contained peptides that enhanced bradykinin and blocked the enzyme that makes angiotensin II. Building on this, Miguel Ondetti and David Cushman at Squibb designed captopril, approved in 1981. Other companies then designed ACE inhibitors that lasted longer and lacked captopril's sulfhydryl group. Benazepril was developed by Ciba-Geigy and approved in the US in the early 1990s as Lotensin. It was later combined with amlodipine as Lotrel.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Sergio Ferreira finds ACE-blocking peptides in Bothrops jararaca venom."], ["1981", "Captopril, the first ACE inhibitor, is approved in the US."], ["1990s", "Benazepril (Lotensin) is approved in the US for hypertension."]], "cell": "In the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE), mostly in the lungs and on endothelial cells, converts angiotensin I into angiotensin II. Angiotensin II binds AT1 receptors and constricts arterioles, triggers aldosterone release from the adrenal cortex, increases sodium reabsorption, constricts the efferent arteriole in the kidney and promotes heart and vessel remodeling. Benazepril is a prodrug. The liver converts it to benazeprilat, which binds the zinc in ACE's active site and blocks it. Angiotensin II falls, so vessels relax, aldosterone falls, sodium and water are lost and potassium is retained. ACE is also kininase II, the enzyme that breaks down bradykinin. Blocking it raises bradykinin, which adds to vasodilation through nitric oxide and prostaglandins but also causes cough and angioedema.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Lowers angiotensin II, relaxing arterioles and reducing blood pressure and afterload."], ["Heart", "Reduces afterload and remodeling, which benefits heart failure and the heart after infarction."], ["Kidneys", "Dilates the efferent arteriole, lowering glomerular pressure and protein loss in diabetic kidney disease, but can reduce filtration and raise creatinine."], ["Adrenal gland and potassium", "Less aldosterone means more sodium loss and potassium retention, so hyperkalemia can develop."], ["Lungs and airway", "Raised bradykinin causes a dry cough and, rarely, angioedema of the face, lips, tongue or throat."], ["Fetus", "Blocking angiotensin in pregnancy damages fetal kidneys and skull development."]], "journey": "Benazepril is taken by mouth and rapidly absorbed, then converted in the liver to active benazeprilat. Peak benazeprilat levels occur within a few hours. It is highly protein bound. Benazeprilat is excreted by the kidneys and partly in bile. The effective half-life of benazeprilat is about ten to eleven hours, with a longer terminal phase, allowing once daily dosing. Kidney impairment raises levels. It crosses the placenta.", "sidefx": "Dry cough comes from bradykinin and substance P that build up because ACE no longer breaks them down. Angioedema, a swelling of the deep skin and airway that can be life threatening, also comes from bradykinin and is more common in Black patients; it is not reversed well by epinephrine or antihistamines, and airway management is the priority. Hyperkalemia results from low aldosterone. Kidney function can drop when blood flow to the kidneys depends on angiotensin II, such as in bilateral renal artery stenosis. Hypotension can occur with the first dose. There is a boxed warning for fetal toxicity.", "fun": "ACE inhibitor names end in -pril, a reminder of their peptide origins in Brazilian snake venom.", "src": ["Lotensin (benazepril) FDA label", "Ferreira SH. Br J Pharmacol, 1965", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "benztropine": {"origin": "Benztropine was created by combining parts of two older drugs. Its tropine portion comes from atropine, the anticholinergic alkaloid of deadly nightshade, and its benzhydryl portion comes from antihistamines such as diphenhydramine. Introduced in the 1950s and sold as Cogentin, it was used to treat Parkinson disease, at a time when anticholinergic drugs were the main treatment before levodopa arrived in the late 1960s. Later it became the go-to drug for the movement side effects of antipsychotics, especially acute dystonia.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Benztropine (Cogentin), combining atropine-like and antihistamine-like structures, is introduced for Parkinson disease."], ["1960s", "Levodopa becomes the main Parkinson drug; benztropine remains for tremor and antipsychotic side effects."]], "cell": "In the striatum, dopamine from the substantia nigra and acetylcholine from local interneurons balance each other to control movement. Dopamine loss in Parkinson disease, or dopamine D2 blockade by antipsychotics, leaves acetylcholine signaling relatively too strong. This drives tremor, rigidity and dystonia. Benztropine is a centrally acting antagonist at muscarinic receptors, mostly M1, on striatal neurons. Blocking these receptors reduces the cholinergic excess and restores balance. It also weakly blocks dopamine reuptake, which can modestly increase dopamine in the synapse, and blocks histamine H1 receptors, adding sedation. Its peripheral muscarinic blockade causes classic anticholinergic side effects.", "body": [["Brain (movement)", "Reduces tremor and rigidity in Parkinson disease and rapidly reverses acute dystonic reactions from antipsychotics."], ["Brain (thinking)", "Can cause confusion, memory problems and delirium, especially in older adults."], ["Mouth and eyes", "Dries the mouth and blurs vision by blocking muscarinic receptors in glands and the eye."], ["Gut and bladder", "Slows gut movement and relaxes the bladder, causing constipation and urinary retention."], ["Skin and temperature", "Reduces sweating, which can lead to overheating and heat stroke in hot weather."], ["Heart", "Increases heart rate by blocking vagal tone."]], "journey": "Benztropine can be given by mouth, IM or IV. Injected forms act within minutes, which is useful for acute dystonia, while oral doses take about an hour. It crosses the blood-brain barrier easily. It is metabolized in the liver and excreted in urine and stool. Its effects last a long time, often a day or more, allowing once or twice daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow from muscarinic blockade outside the striatum: dry mouth, blurred vision, constipation, urinary retention, tachycardia and decreased sweating. Older adults are especially prone to confusion and delirium, which is why it is on the Beers list of drugs to avoid in the elderly. It can worsen narrow-angle glaucoma. It does not improve, and can worsen, tardive dyskinesia. In overdose, the anticholinergic toxidrome with hyperthermia can occur; physostigmine can reverse severe central effects in selected patients.", "fun": "Benztropine's name reflects its two halves: 'benz' from the benzhydryl group of antihistamines and 'tropine' from atropine.", "src": ["Benztropine mesylate FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "American Geriatrics Society Beers Criteria"]}, "betamethasone": {"origin": "After cortisone was introduced in the late 1940s, drug companies raced to make stronger synthetic steroids with less salt retention. Betamethasone was developed by Schering around 1960, by adding a fluorine and a methyl group to the cortisol structure, making it a potent, long-acting glucocorticoid. Its most famous use came from New Zealand. In 1969 the obstetrician Graham Liggins noticed that lambs exposed to steroids before birth had more mature lungs. With pediatrician Ross Howie, he ran a landmark trial published in 1972 showing that betamethasone given to mothers in preterm labor reduced breathing problems and death in their premature babies. Antenatal steroids are now standard care worldwide.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Betamethasone, a potent fluorinated glucocorticoid, enters clinical use."], ["1969", "Graham Liggins observes accelerated lung maturation in lambs exposed to steroids."], ["1972", "Liggins and Howie publish their trial of antenatal betamethasone in preterm labor."], ["1994", "An NIH consensus conference recommends antenatal corticosteroids for preterm birth."]], "cell": "Betamethasone is lipid-soluble and enters cells, where it binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor. The receptor releases its heat shock proteins, moves into the nucleus and binds glucocorticoid response elements on DNA, switching on some genes and off others. It increases anti-inflammatory proteins such as annexin-1 (lipocortin), which inhibits phospholipase A2, and blocks inflammatory transcription factors NF-kappa B and AP-1, reducing cytokines, prostaglandins and leukotrienes. In the fetal lung, the same receptor pathway increases production of surfactant proteins and lipids by type II pneumocytes, speeds thinning of the alveolar walls and increases lung fluid clearance. This lowers the risk of respiratory distress syndrome. It has strong glucocorticoid and almost no mineralocorticoid activity.", "body": [["Fetal lungs", "Speeds lung maturation and surfactant production, lowering respiratory distress syndrome and newborn death in preterm babies."], ["Fetal brain and gut", "Lowers the risk of intraventricular hemorrhage and necrotizing enterocolitis in preterm infants."], ["Immune system", "Suppresses inflammation and immune responses, raising infection risk with longer use."], ["Blood sugar", "Raises maternal blood glucose for several days by increasing gluconeogenesis and insulin resistance."], ["Skin and joints", "Topical and injected forms reduce local inflammation in skin conditions and joints."], ["Adrenal glands", "Repeated or long use suppresses the body's own cortisol production."]], "journey": "For fetal lung maturity, a mixture of betamethasone sodium phosphate (fast-acting, soluble) and betamethasone acetate (slow-release suspension) is given IM to the mother. It crosses the placenta well to reach the fetus, because placental enzymes inactivate it much less than cortisol. It is metabolized in the liver and excreted in the urine. Its plasma half-life is several hours, but its biologic effects last about one and a half to two days. The benefit is greatest from about one day after the first dose to about a week.", "sidefx": "Maternal hyperglycemia results from glucocorticoid effects on liver glucose production and insulin resistance, which matters in diabetes. Short courses rarely cause major side effects. Longer or repeated use causes the typical steroid effects of infection risk, fluid retention, mood changes, muscle wasting, bone loss and adrenal suppression, all from altered gene expression. A transient rise in white blood cell count can confuse the picture of infection. There is no antidote; the effects wear off.", "fun": "Graham Liggins was studying what triggers labor, not lung development, when he noticed that the steroid-exposed premature lambs could breathe surprisingly well.", "src": ["Celestone Soluspan (betamethasone) FDA label", "Liggins GC, Howie RN. Pediatrics, 1972", "ACOG Committee Opinion on Antenatal Corticosteroid Therapy"]}, "bethanechol": {"origin": "Acetylcholine itself is useless as a drug because acetylcholinesterase breaks it down within milliseconds and it stimulates both muscarinic and nicotinic receptors. In the 1930s pharmacologists made synthetic choline esters to fix these problems. Bethanechol (carbamyl-beta-methylcholine) combined two changes: a carbamate group that resists cholinesterase and a methyl group that removes most nicotinic activity. The result was a long-acting, muscarine-like drug that acts on the bladder and gut. It was marketed as Urecholine and became the classic treatment for urinary retention after surgery or childbirth.", "timeline": [["1914", "Henry Dale describes muscarinic and nicotinic actions of acetylcholine."], ["1930s", "Synthetic choline esters, including bethanechol, are developed."]], "cell": "Bethanechol is a direct-acting agonist at muscarinic receptors with almost no nicotinic activity. On bladder detrusor smooth muscle and gut smooth muscle, it activates M3 receptors coupled to Gq proteins. Gq activates phospholipase C, producing IP3 and DAG. IP3 releases calcium from intracellular stores, and calcium-calmodulin activates myosin light chain kinase, causing smooth muscle contraction. The detrusor contracts and the trigone and sphincter relax, emptying the bladder; gut motility and secretions increase. It also acts on M2 receptors in the heart, slowing heart rate. Its carbamate structure resists hydrolysis by acetylcholinesterase and pseudocholinesterase, so it lasts hours. As a quaternary ammonium compound, it is charged and does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so it has little effect on the brain.", "body": [["Bladder", "Contracts the detrusor muscle and helps empty the bladder in nonobstructive urinary retention."], ["Gut", "Increases peristalsis and secretions, which can cause cramping, diarrhea and urgency."], ["Glands", "Increases saliva, sweat and tear production through M3 receptors on glands."], ["Heart and vessels", "Can slow heart rate and lower blood pressure through muscarinic stimulation."], ["Lungs", "Can cause bronchoconstriction and increased secretions, which is dangerous in asthma."], ["Eyes", "Can constrict the pupils and cause blurred vision."]], "journey": "Bethanechol is given by mouth on an empty stomach, as food reduces absorption and can cause nausea. Absorption is poor because it is a charged molecule, but it still works within about thirty to ninety minutes. It does not cross the blood-brain barrier. It is not broken down by cholinesterases, and its exact metabolism and elimination are not well characterized. Effects last up to about six hours.", "sidefx": "Side effects mirror muscarinic stimulation throughout the body: sweating, salivation, flushing, abdominal cramps, diarrhea, urinary urgency, low blood pressure and slow heart rate. In asthma it can cause bronchospasm. It should not be used when the bladder or bowel is mechanically blocked, because forcing contraction against an obstruction can cause rupture. Overdose can produce a cholinergic crisis. Atropine, a muscarinic antagonist, is the antidote.", "fun": "The memory aid SLUDGE (salivation, lacrimation, urination, defecation, GI cramping, emesis) describes bethanechol's effects as well as those of cholinergic poisoning.", "src": ["Bethanechol chloride FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "bevacizumab": {"origin": "In 1971 the surgeon Judah Folkman at Harvard proposed that tumors cannot grow beyond a tiny size without new blood vessels, and that blocking this angiogenesis could starve them. In 1989 Napoleone Ferrara and colleagues at Genentech isolated and cloned vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF), a key signal for blood vessel growth. Ferrara's team showed that an antibody against VEGF slowed tumor growth in mice, and Genentech humanized this antibody to create bevacizumab. In 2004 the FDA approved it as Avastin for metastatic colorectal cancer, the first approved antiangiogenic drug. Its approval for metastatic breast cancer was revoked in 2011 after later trials showed no survival benefit. Related anti-VEGF drugs changed the treatment of wet macular degeneration.", "timeline": [["1971", "Judah Folkman proposes blocking tumor angiogenesis as a cancer therapy."], ["1989", "Napoleone Ferrara and colleagues at Genentech clone VEGF."], ["1993", "Ferrara's group shows anti-VEGF antibodies suppress tumor growth in mice."], ["2004", "The FDA approves bevacizumab (Avastin) for metastatic colorectal cancer."], ["2011", "The FDA revokes the breast cancer indication."]], "cell": "Tumors that outgrow their blood supply become hypoxic. Hypoxia stabilizes the transcription factor HIF-1 alpha, which increases production of VEGF-A. VEGF-A binds VEGFR-1 and VEGFR-2, receptor tyrosine kinases on endothelial cells. VEGFR-2 activation triggers pathways such as PLC-gamma, MAPK and PI3K/Akt that make endothelial cells survive, proliferate, migrate, become leaky and form new vessels. Bevacizumab is a humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody that binds circulating VEGF-A itself, not the receptor, and prevents it from engaging VEGFR-1 and VEGFR-2. New vessel growth slows, some immature tumor vessels regress, and the remaining vessels become more normal and less leaky, which can improve delivery of chemotherapy. Because VEGF also maintains normal vessels in the kidney, brain and elsewhere, side effects appear in those tissues.", "body": [["Tumors", "Slows tumor growth by blocking new blood vessel formation; used with chemotherapy for colorectal, lung, kidney, cervical, ovarian cancers and glioblastoma."], ["Blood pressure", "Hypertension is common, likely from reduced nitric oxide production and loss of small vessels."], ["Kidneys", "Damages glomerular filtration barrier cells that rely on VEGF, causing proteinuria and sometimes nephrotic syndrome."], ["Wounds and gut", "Impairs wound healing and can cause GI perforation or wound dehiscence; surgery is timed around it."], ["Bleeding and clotting", "Causes nosebleeds and serious bleeding, especially pulmonary bleeding in squamous lung cancer, as well as arterial and venous clots."], ["Brain", "Rarely causes posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome (PRES) with headache, seizures and vision changes."]], "journey": "Bevacizumab is a large protein given by IV infusion, and the first infusion is given slowly. It stays mostly in the blood and interstitial fluid. Like other IgG antibodies, it is protected from breakdown by the neonatal Fc receptor and is cleared by protein catabolism in the reticuloendothelial system, not by the liver or kidneys. Its half-life is about twenty days, so it is given every two or three weeks. It crosses the placenta and can harm fetal development. It is also injected directly into the eye for some retinal diseases.", "sidefx": "Most toxicities come straight from blocking VEGF in normal tissue. VEGF keeps blood vessel linings healthy, makes nitric oxide and maintains kidney filtration cells. Losing it causes high blood pressure, protein in the urine, poor wound healing, bleeding and clotting events. GI perforation is thought to involve weakened gut wall blood supply. PRES relates to loss of normal vessel regulation in the brain, often with hypertension. Infusion reactions can occur. There is no reversal agent, and because of its long half-life, effects persist for weeks.", "fun": "Bevacizumab helped inspire ranibizumab, a smaller antibody fragment from the same Genentech program, made for injection into the eye for wet macular degeneration.", "src": ["Avastin (bevacizumab) FDA label", "Folkman J. N Engl J Med, 1971", "Ferrara N et al. Nature, 1993"]}, "bisacodyl": {"origin": "Bisacodyl belongs to the triarylmethane family of laxatives, related to phenolphthalein, an older laxative. Phenolphthalein was famous as a chemistry indicator and was used as a laxative in the early 1900s, after its laxative effect was noticed in Hungary in 1902. Researchers looking for safer relatives developed bisacodyl, which was introduced in the 1950s by Boehringer Ingelheim as Dulcolax. It works only on the colon and became one of the most common laxatives in the world for constipation and bowel preparation. Phenolphthalein was later removed from US over-the-counter products in the late 1990s because of cancer concerns in animal studies, but bisacodyl remained.", "timeline": [["1900s", "Phenolphthalein, a related compound, is used as a laxative."], ["1950s", "Bisacodyl is introduced as Dulcolax."], ["1990s", "Phenolphthalein is removed from US OTC laxatives; bisacodyl remains widely used."]], "cell": "Bisacodyl is a prodrug. Enzymes on the intestinal lining and bacteria in the colon remove its acetyl groups, forming the active metabolite BHPM (bis-(p-hydroxyphenyl)-pyridyl-2-methane). BHPM acts directly on the colonic mucosa. It stimulates sensory nerve endings in the myenteric plexus, which triggers strong propulsive contractions of the colon. It also changes water and electrolyte transport across the colonic lining: it reduces absorption and increases secretion of chloride and water into the lumen, partly through prostaglandin E2 signaling and changes in aquaporin water channels. More water in the colon and stronger contractions together soften stool and push it out.", "body": [["Colon", "Stimulates strong contractions and increases fluid in the colon, causing a bowel movement in about six to twelve hours by mouth or within an hour rectally."], ["Gut lining", "Can cause cramping and abdominal discomfort from strong contractions."], ["Fluids and electrolytes", "Overuse can cause dehydration and loss of potassium and other electrolytes."], ["Rectum", "Suppositories can cause local burning and irritation."]], "journey": "Oral bisacodyl is enteric-coated so it passes the stomach intact; it should not be crushed, chewed or taken with antacids or milk, which dissolve the coating early and irritate the stomach. In the intestine and colon it is converted to its active form, BHPM, which acts locally. Only a small amount is absorbed, and absorbed drug is conjugated in the liver and excreted in the urine and bile. Most leaves in the stool. By mouth it works in about six to twelve hours, so it is often taken at bedtime; as a suppository it works in fifteen to sixty minutes.", "sidefx": "Cramping and diarrhea come directly from strong colon stimulation. Excess fluid loss can cause dehydration and low potassium, especially in older adults or with overuse. Taking tablets with milk or antacids dissolves the coating in the stomach, causing irritation and vomiting. Suppositories can irritate the rectal lining. It should be avoided in suspected bowel obstruction or appendicitis. Long-term heavy use was once thought to damage colon nerves, but this has not been clearly shown with modern stimulant laxatives. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Phenolphthalein, bisacodyl's relative, is the same pink indicator dye used in school chemistry labs.", "src": ["Dulcolax (bisacodyl) OTC labeling", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "bisoprolol": {"origin": "Bisoprolol was developed in the 1970s and 1980s by Merck KGaA (E. Merck) in Darmstadt, Germany, as one of the most beta-1 selective blockers available. It was approved in the US in the early 1990s as Zebeta for high blood pressure, and a low-dose combination with hydrochlorothiazide (Ziac) was approved soon after as a first-line treatment. Its biggest moment came in 1999 when the CIBIS-II trial showed bisoprolol reduced death by about a third in patients with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction. This helped overturn the old belief that beta blockers were dangerous in heart failure. It is now one of three beta blockers proven to improve survival in heart failure.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Merck KGaA in Germany develops bisoprolol."], ["1990s", "Bisoprolol (Zebeta) is approved in the US for hypertension."], ["1999", "The CIBIS-II trial shows bisoprolol reduces death in heart failure."]], "cell": "Bisoprolol is a competitive antagonist with high selectivity for beta-1 adrenergic receptors. Normally, norepinephrine and epinephrine activate beta-1 receptors coupled to Gs proteins, raising cAMP and activating protein kinase A, which increases calcium entry, heart rate, contractility, AV conduction and renin release. Bisoprolol blocks this signaling. Heart rate, contractility and conduction fall, reducing oxygen demand, and less renin lowers angiotensin II and aldosterone. In heart failure, chronic sympathetic overstimulation is toxic to heart cells: it causes calcium overload, arrhythmias, cell death and remodeling. Long-term beta-1 blockade protects the heart from this, allowing receptor upregulation, better calcium handling and gradual improvement in ejection fraction. Bisoprolol has no intrinsic sympathomimetic activity or membrane-stabilizing effect.", "body": [["Heart", "Slows heart rate and reduces oxygen demand; in heart failure, long-term use reduces remodeling, improves ejection fraction and lowers death."], ["Blood pressure", "Lowers blood pressure by reducing cardiac output and renin release."], ["Heart rhythm", "Slows AV conduction to control rate in atrial fibrillation and reduces arrhythmias after infarction."], ["Lungs", "Highly beta-1 selective, so it is better tolerated in COPD and mild asthma, though bronchospasm can still occur."], ["Metabolism", "Can mask tachycardia from low blood sugar in diabetes."], ["Energy level", "Fatigue and exercise intolerance are common early on."]], "journey": "Bisoprolol is well absorbed by mouth, with high bioavailability because first-pass metabolism is small. It is moderately fat-soluble. About half of it is metabolized in the liver to inactive products, and about half is excreted unchanged by the kidneys, so both liver and kidney disease affect levels. Its half-life is about nine to twelve hours, allowing once daily dosing. In heart failure, doses are started low and increased slowly over weeks.", "sidefx": "Bradycardia, heart block, dizziness and fatigue come from beta-1 blockade in the heart. When starting in heart failure, symptoms can temporarily worsen because contractility drops before the long-term benefits appear, which is why doses are increased slowly. Bronchospasm is less common than with nonselective beta blockers but possible at higher doses. Stopping suddenly can cause rebound tachycardia or angina. In overdose, glucagon raises cAMP independently of beta receptors; atropine, calcium, high-dose insulin and vasopressors are also used.", "fun": "The three beta blockers proven to lower death in heart failure are bisoprolol, carvedilol and metoprolol succinate.", "src": ["Zebeta (bisoprolol) FDA label", "CIBIS-II Investigators. Lancet, 1999", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "bivalirudin": {"origin": "Leeches have been used in medicine since ancient times. In 1884 the British physiologist John Berry Haycraft discovered that leech saliva contains a substance that stops blood from clotting, and in the 1950s Fritz Markwardt in Germany purified it and named it hirudin, after the medicinal leech Hirudo medicinalis. Hirudin was a very potent thrombin inhibitor but hard to produce. In the early 1990s John Maraganore and colleagues at Biogen designed bivalirudin, a short synthetic peptide of 20 amino acids built from the key parts of hirudin. The Medicines Company developed it, and the FDA approved it in 2000 as Angiomax for anticoagulation during coronary angioplasty.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Medicinal leeches are used in bloodletting."], ["1884", "John Berry Haycraft discovers an anticoagulant in leech saliva."], ["1950s", "Fritz Markwardt purifies hirudin from the medicinal leech."], ["1990", "Maraganore and colleagues at Biogen describe bivalirudin (Hirulog)."], ["2000", "The FDA approves bivalirudin (Angiomax) for use in angioplasty."]], "cell": "Thrombin (factor IIa) has an active catalytic site and a separate exosite 1, which it uses to grab fibrinogen. Bivalirudin is bivalent: one end binds the active site and the other end binds exosite 1, so it blocks thrombin very specifically. It works without antithrombin and inhibits both free thrombin and thrombin bound to clots, which heparin does poorly. With thrombin blocked, fibrinogen is not converted to fibrin, factors V, VIII and XIII are not activated, and thrombin-driven platelet activation falls. Thrombin slowly cleaves the bond within bivalirudin that sits in its active site, so the inhibition is reversible and short-lived. Bivalirudin does not bind platelet factor 4, so it does not trigger or worsen heparin-induced thrombocytopenia.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Prevents clot formation in coronary arteries and catheters during PCI and in patients with heparin-induced thrombocytopenia."], ["Platelets", "Reduces thrombin-induced platelet activation, helping protect stents and treated arteries."], ["Coagulation tests", "Prolongs ACT, aPTT, PT and INR during infusion."], ["Bleeding sites", "Bleeding at the arterial access site and elsewhere is the main risk, though lower than with heparin plus glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitors."], ["Kidneys", "Partly cleared by the kidneys, so effects last longer in kidney impairment."]], "journey": "Bivalirudin is given IV as a bolus and infusion and works immediately. It stays mainly in the blood and does not bind plasma proteins other than thrombin. About eighty percent is broken down by proteolysis, including by thrombin itself, and the rest is cleared by the kidneys. Its half-life is about twenty-five minutes in normal kidney function, so clotting returns to normal about an hour after stopping. It is partly removed by hemodialysis.", "sidefx": "Bleeding is the main risk because thrombin is central to clotting. Major bleeding, intracranial or retroperitoneal bleeding are the most serious. Because it wears off quickly, there is a small risk of acute stent thrombosis right after PCI if the infusion is stopped too soon. Back pain, nausea, headache and low blood pressure can occur. There is no specific reversal agent. Its short half-life means stopping the infusion is usually enough, with supportive care and blood products for serious bleeding.", "fun": "Medicinal leeches are still FDA-cleared as medical devices and are used after some plastic and reconstructive surgeries to relieve venous congestion.", "src": ["Angiomax (bivalirudin) FDA label", "Maraganore JM et al. Biochemistry, 1990", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "bleomycin": {"origin": "Bleomycin is a natural product of the soil bacterium Streptomyces verticillus. It was discovered in 1966 by the Japanese microbiologist Hamao Umezawa and his team at the Institute of Microbial Chemistry in Tokyo, who were screening microbes for compounds that could kill cancer cells. Umezawa found that bleomycin worked well against squamous cell cancers and lymphomas while sparing the bone marrow, an unusual feature among cancer drugs. It was approved by the FDA in 1973. Bleomycin became a key part of the combination regimens that made testicular cancer and Hodgkin lymphoma highly curable, such as BEP and ABVD.", "timeline": [["1966", "Hamao Umezawa's team discovers bleomycin from Streptomyces verticillus."], ["1973", "The FDA approves bleomycin."], ["1970s", "Bleomycin becomes part of curative regimens for Hodgkin lymphoma (ABVD) and testicular cancer."]], "cell": "Bleomycin is a glycopeptide that binds DNA, sliding part of its structure between base pairs. It also binds ferrous iron (Fe2+). The iron-bleomycin complex reacts with oxygen to form an activated complex that generates superoxide and hydroxyl free radicals right next to the DNA. These radicals attack the deoxyribose sugar backbone, causing single-strand and double-strand DNA breaks. Cells with broken DNA arrest in the G2 phase of the cell cycle and undergo apoptosis, so bleomycin is cell cycle specific for G2 and also acts in M phase. Most tissues contain bleomycin hydrolase, an enzyme that inactivates the drug. The lungs and skin have very little of this enzyme, which explains why toxicity concentrates there. Bone marrow has plenty, which is why bleomycin causes little myelosuppression.", "body": [["Tumors", "Kills rapidly dividing cancer cells in testicular cancer, Hodgkin lymphoma and some squamous cell cancers."], ["Lungs", "Generates free radicals in lung tissue, causing inflammation that can progress to pulmonary fibrosis; risk rises with total lifetime dose, older age, kidney disease and high oxygen."], ["Skin and nails", "Causes hyperpigmentation, including a striking flagellate (whip-like) pattern, skin thickening, nail changes and hair loss."], ["Mouth", "Mucositis and painful mouth sores can occur because the lining of the mouth is also exposed."], ["Immune response", "Fever and chills are common within hours of a dose, and rare idiosyncratic reactions with fever, low blood pressure and confusion can occur, especially in lymphoma."], ["Blood vessels", "Raynaud phenomenon can develop, particularly with combination regimens."]], "journey": "Bleomycin is given IV, IM, subcutaneously or directly into the pleural space to treat malignant effusions. It distributes widely, concentrating in the skin and lungs. It is inactivated in tissues by bleomycin hydrolase. About two-thirds of a dose is excreted unchanged in the urine. The half-life is about two to four hours in normal kidney function but rises sharply in kidney failure, raising the risk of lung toxicity, so doses are adjusted.", "sidefx": "Lung toxicity is the most important risk. It comes from oxygen free radicals damaging lung cells, where bleomycin hydrolase is scarce, triggering inflammation and fibrosis. High concentrations of oxygen given during later surgery can worsen injury, so anesthesiologists need to know about prior bleomycin. Skin changes follow the same logic, since skin also lacks the hydrolase. Fever and chills reflect cytokine release, and a test dose is sometimes given to patients with lymphoma because of severe idiosyncratic reactions. There is no antidote; treatment of lung toxicity includes stopping the drug and corticosteroids.", "fun": "Bleomycin is one of the few chemotherapy drugs that barely suppresses the bone marrow, a favorite exam fact.", "src": ["Bleomycin for injection FDA label", "Umezawa H et al. J Antibiot, 1966", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "bosentan": {"origin": "In 1988 the Japanese researcher Masashi Yanagisawa and colleagues discovered endothelin-1, a peptide made by blood vessel lining cells and one of the most powerful vasoconstrictors known. High levels of endothelin were soon found in pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH), a disease in which the lung arteries narrow and thicken. Scientists at Roche in Switzerland developed bosentan, the first orally active endothelin receptor antagonist, in the early 1990s. A small company, Actelion, was founded by former Roche scientists to develop it. The FDA approved bosentan as Tracleer in 2001, making it the first oral drug for PAH, which until then required continuous IV prostacyclin therapy.", "timeline": [["1988", "Yanagisawa and colleagues discover endothelin-1."], ["1990s", "Roche scientists develop bosentan, an oral endothelin receptor antagonist."], ["1997", "Actelion is founded and takes bosentan forward."], ["2001", "The FDA approves bosentan (Tracleer) for pulmonary arterial hypertension."]], "cell": "Endothelin-1 acts on two G protein-coupled receptors. ETA receptors on vascular smooth muscle couple to Gq; activation releases intracellular calcium and causes strong, long-lasting vasoconstriction, and also stimulates smooth muscle cell growth and fibrosis. ETB receptors on smooth muscle also cause constriction, while ETB receptors on endothelial cells release nitric oxide and prostacyclin and clear endothelin from the blood. In PAH, endothelin production rises and drives both narrowing and remodeling of small pulmonary arteries. Bosentan is a competitive antagonist at both ETA and ETB receptors. Blocking them relaxes pulmonary vascular smooth muscle and slows proliferation, lowering pulmonary vascular resistance and the workload on the right ventricle. This improves exercise capacity and slows clinical worsening.", "body": [["Lung arteries", "Relaxes and slows remodeling of pulmonary arteries, lowering pulmonary artery pressure and resistance."], ["Heart (right ventricle)", "Lower pulmonary resistance reduces strain on the right ventricle and improves cardiac output."], ["Liver", "Causes dose-related liver enzyme rises and, rarely, liver failure, so liver tests are monitored."], ["Blood", "Lowers hemoglobin, partly from fluid shifts, so blood counts are checked."], ["Fetus", "Endothelin signaling is essential for embryo development, so bosentan causes serious birth defects."], ["Vessels and fluids", "Headache, flushing and leg edema come from systemic vasodilation."]], "journey": "Bosentan is taken by mouth and about half is absorbed, with peak levels in three to five hours. It is highly protein bound. The liver metabolizes it by CYP2C9 and CYP3A4, and it also induces those enzymes, so it lowers levels of drugs such as hormonal contraceptives, warfarin and simvastatin. It is excreted in bile. Liver uptake depends on OATP transporters, which is why cyclosporine and glyburide interactions are important. The half-life is about five hours. It takes weeks to show clinical benefit.", "sidefx": "Liver injury is thought to come partly from inhibition of the bile salt export pump, which causes bile acids to build up in liver cells. Monthly liver tests have been part of its safety program. Birth defects follow from blocking endothelin signals needed for development of the head, face and heart, so pregnancy must be excluded and two reliable forms of contraception used, keeping in mind that bosentan makes hormonal contraceptives less effective. Anemia, edema, flushing and headache come from vasodilation and fluid shifts. Bosentan may reduce sperm counts. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Endothelin-1 was discovered and sequenced in a single landmark paper in Nature in 1988, a rare case of a major hormone going from unknown to fully characterized so quickly.", "src": ["Tracleer (bosentan) FDA label", "Yanagisawa M et al. Nature, 1988", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "bromocriptine": {"origin": "Bromocriptine comes from ergot, a fungus (Claviceps purpurea) that infects rye. In the Middle Ages, eating contaminated bread caused ergotism, also called St. Anthony's fire, with burning limbs and hallucinations. In the 20th century the Swiss company Sandoz studied ergot alkaloids systematically, and in the 1960s its scientists made bromocriptine by adding a bromine atom to the natural ergot alkaloid ergocryptine. It turned out to be a strong dopamine agonist that blocked prolactin release. It was approved in the US in the late 1970s as Parlodel for hyperprolactinemia and later for acromegaly and Parkinson disease. A quick-release, morning-dosed version (Cycloset) was approved for type 2 diabetes in 2009.", "timeline": [["9th century", "Outbreaks of ergotism, or St. Anthony's fire, from contaminated rye are recorded in Europe."], ["1960s", "Sandoz scientists synthesize bromocriptine from ergocryptine."], ["1970s", "Bromocriptine (Parlodel) is approved in the US for hyperprolactinemia."], ["1990s", "The US indication for postpartum lactation suppression is withdrawn over safety concerns."], ["2009", "Bromocriptine (Cycloset) is approved for type 2 diabetes."]], "cell": "Bromocriptine is an ergot-derived agonist at dopamine D2 receptors, with partial D1 antagonism and effects at serotonin and adrenergic receptors. In the anterior pituitary, dopamine from the hypothalamus normally holds prolactin secretion down by acting on D2 receptors on lactotroph cells, which couple to Gi proteins and lower cAMP. Bromocriptine mimics dopamine there, so prolactin release falls and prolactinomas often shrink. In acromegaly it paradoxically lowers growth hormone release from tumor cells. In the striatum it stimulates postsynaptic D2 receptors, replacing missing dopamine signaling in Parkinson disease. When taken in the morning for diabetes, it is thought to reset low hypothalamic dopamine tone, which lowers sympathetic output and liver glucose production and improves insulin sensitivity.", "body": [["Pituitary gland", "Lowers prolactin, restoring menstrual cycles and fertility and shrinking prolactinomas; also lowers growth hormone in acromegaly."], ["Brain (movement)", "Improves motor symptoms of Parkinson disease by stimulating striatal dopamine receptors."], ["Brain (mind)", "Can cause hallucinations, confusion, sleep attacks and impulse control problems."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are common because the brainstem chemoreceptor trigger zone is outside the blood-brain barrier and has dopamine receptors."], ["Blood vessels", "Causes orthostatic hypotension, nasal congestion and, as an ergot, digital vasospasm."], ["Heart and lungs", "Long-term use has been linked to fibrosis of the pleura, pericardium, retroperitoneum and heart valves."]], "journey": "Bromocriptine is taken by mouth, and only a small portion reaches the blood because of heavy first-pass metabolism by CYP3A4 in the liver. Taking it with food reduces nausea. It is highly protein bound and crosses the blood-brain barrier. The liver metabolizes it extensively, and over ninety percent is excreted in bile and stool. Its half-life is biphasic, with an early phase of a few hours and a terminal phase up to about fifteen hours. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels.", "sidefx": "Nausea, vomiting and dizziness come from stimulating dopamine receptors in the brainstem vomiting center and from vasodilation, so doses start low and rise slowly. Psychiatric effects such as hallucinations and compulsive behaviors come from stimulating dopamine reward and limbic pathways. As an ergot derivative, it can cause vasospasm and fibrotic reactions, likely related to serotonin 5-HT2B receptor effects. It was used to suppress lactation after delivery, but reports of postpartum strokes, heart attacks and seizures led to withdrawal of that use. Dopamine antagonists can reverse its effects but worsen Parkinson symptoms. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "LSD was also discovered at Sandoz from ergot chemistry, by Albert Hofmann, a colleague in the same research program that produced many ergot medicines.", "src": ["Parlodel and Cycloset (bromocriptine) FDA labels", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "budesonide": {"origin": "Budesonide was developed in the 1970s by scientists at Draco, a subsidiary of the Swedish company Astra in Lund. They were looking for a corticosteroid with strong effects where it is applied, such as the airway or gut lining, but weak effects elsewhere in the body. Budesonide fit the bill: it is very potent at the glucocorticoid receptor, but the liver breaks down most of it on its first pass. It was introduced in Europe in the early 1980s as an inhaled asthma treatment, Pulmicort. Later it was developed as a nasal spray for allergies, a nebulized solution for children with asthma and croup, a combination inhaler with formoterol (Symbicort), and special oral capsules (Entocort, Uceris) that release the drug in the gut for Crohn disease and ulcerative colitis.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Scientists at Draco (Astra) in Sweden develop budesonide."], ["1980s", "Inhaled budesonide (Pulmicort) is introduced in Europe for asthma."], ["2000", "Nebulized budesonide (Pulmicort Respules) is approved in the US for young children with asthma."], ["2006", "Budesonide-formoterol (Symbicort) is approved in the US."]], "cell": "Budesonide is a lipid-soluble glucocorticoid that diffuses into cells and binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor with high affinity. The activated receptor moves into the nucleus. There it switches on anti-inflammatory genes and, more importantly, blocks transcription factors NF-kappa B and AP-1, which drive inflammatory genes. Production of cytokines such as IL-4, IL-5 and IL-13, chemokines, adhesion molecules and inducible enzymes like COX-2 falls. Eosinophils, mast cells and T cells decline in the airway and gut lining, airway hyperresponsiveness lessens, mucus production falls and swelling settles. Steroids also increase beta-2 receptor expression, helping bronchodilators work. Budesonide's ester side chain allows it to form reversible fatty acid conjugates inside airway cells, which keeps it in the tissue longer.", "body": [["Airways", "Reduces airway inflammation and hyperreactivity over days to weeks, preventing asthma attacks and improving COPD symptoms; not a rescue drug."], ["Upper airway in croup", "Nebulized budesonide reduces swelling around the larynx in croup within hours."], ["Nose", "Nasal spray reduces allergic rhinitis symptoms and nasal polyps."], ["Gut", "Oral controlled-release forms reduce inflammation in Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis and microscopic colitis."], ["Mouth and throat", "Inhaled forms can cause thrush and hoarseness from local steroid effects on the mouth and vocal cords."], ["Adrenal glands and bone", "High doses can still reach the blood enough to suppress the adrenal glands, slow growth in children and thin bones."]], "journey": "Inhaled budesonide is deposited in the airways, where it acts locally; the swallowed portion is absorbed from the gut. About ninety percent of what is absorbed from the gut is destroyed by CYP3A4 on first pass through the liver, so systemic exposure is low. Oral capsules are designed to release drug in the ileum and colon. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ritonavir and ketoconazole block that breakdown, which can cause Cushing syndrome. Inactive metabolites leave in the urine. The half-life is about two to three hours, but anti-inflammatory effects last much longer.", "sidefx": "Thrush and hoarseness come from steroid suppression of local immune defenses and effects on vocal cord muscles; rinsing the mouth after each dose reduces this. Systemic effects, such as adrenal suppression, growth slowing in children, bone loss, cataracts and glaucoma, depend on how much drug escapes into the blood, which is low because of the liver's first-pass metabolism but rises with high doses or CYP3A4 inhibitors. Combining with ritonavir has caused Cushing syndrome and adrenal crisis. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Budesonide was one of the first drugs designed on purpose for 'topical' action inside the body, working where it is placed while being largely destroyed after absorption.", "src": ["Pulmicort, Entocort EC and Symbicort FDA labels", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "GINA Global Strategy for Asthma Management"]}, "bumetanide": {"origin": "Loop diuretics were developed in the early 1960s when furosemide and ethacrynic acid showed that drugs acting on the loop of Henle produced much stronger diuresis than thiazides. Bumetanide was developed in the early 1970s by chemists at Leo Pharmaceutical Products in Denmark, who were looking for a more potent sulfonamide-related loop diuretic. It proved roughly forty times as potent as furosemide by weight, with better and more predictable oral absorption. It was approved in the US in the 1980s as Bumex. Today it is used for edema in heart failure, kidney and liver disease, often when furosemide does not work well by mouth.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Furosemide and ethacrynic acid establish the loop diuretic class."], ["1970s", "Leo Pharmaceutical Products in Denmark develops bumetanide."], ["1980s", "Bumetanide (Bumex) is approved in the US."]], "cell": "In the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle, the Na-K-2Cl cotransporter (NKCC2) on the luminal membrane reabsorbs about a quarter of filtered sodium. Potassium recycles back into the lumen through ROMK channels, creating a positive charge in the lumen that drives calcium and magnesium reabsorption between cells. Bumetanide reaches NKCC2 from inside the tubule, after being secreted in the proximal tubule by organic anion transporters, and binds the chloride site of the transporter. Sodium, potassium and chloride stay in the lumen, causing a large diuresis. Because the positive lumen voltage is lost, calcium and magnesium are also lost. More sodium reaching the collecting duct increases potassium and hydrogen secretion, causing hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis. Loop diuretics also block macula densa sensing, which raises renin, and they increase venous capacitance through prostaglandins.", "body": [["Kidneys", "Causes strong diuresis with loss of sodium, chloride, potassium, magnesium and calcium."], ["Lungs and heart", "Removing fluid lowers preload and relieves pulmonary congestion and edema in heart failure."], ["Veins", "IV doses dilate veins, reducing preload even before urine output increases."], ["Electrolytes and acid-base", "Can cause hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, hyponatremia and contraction alkalosis."], ["Ears", "High levels can damage hair cells in the inner ear, causing tinnitus or hearing loss."], ["Uric acid and glucose", "Raises uric acid, which can trigger gout, and can modestly raise glucose."]], "journey": "Bumetanide is well and predictably absorbed by mouth, with bioavailability that is high and less variable than furosemide, so oral and IV effects are similar. By IV it works within minutes; by mouth within about an hour. It is highly protein bound and must be secreted into the tubule to work, so low protein states and kidney disease reduce how much reaches its target. Part is metabolized in the liver and the rest is excreted unchanged in the urine. Its half-life is short, about one to one and a half hours, so its effect lasts several hours.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from its strong effect on salt and water. Volume depletion causes dizziness, low blood pressure and kidney injury from poor perfusion. Blocking NKCC2 and delivering more sodium downstream causes loss of potassium and magnesium, which can trigger arrhythmias, especially with digoxin. Ototoxicity relates to inhibition of a related transporter (NKCC1) in the inner ear that maintains endolymph, and is more likely with high IV doses or other ototoxic drugs such as aminoglycosides. It contains a sulfonamide group, but cross-reactivity with sulfa antibiotics is uncommon. There is no antidote; fluids and electrolytes are replaced.", "fun": "Bumetanide is also widely used in laboratory research to block NKCC1, the chloride transporter that shapes GABA signaling in developing neurons.", "src": ["Bumetanide FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "bupivacaine": {"origin": "Local anesthesia began with cocaine, introduced for eye surgery by Carl Koller in 1884. Its toxicity and addictiveness led chemists to synthesize safer drugs, starting with procaine in 1905 and then the amide anesthetic lidocaine in Sweden in the 1940s. In 1957 Bo af Ekenstam and colleagues at AB Bofors in Sweden synthesized a series of long-acting amide anesthetics, including bupivacaine. It entered clinical practice in the 1960s and became the standard for long-lasting nerve blocks and spinal and epidural anesthesia, including labor epidurals. In 1979 an editorial by George Albright reported cardiac arrests after accidental IV injection, highlighting its cardiac toxicity. Later, lipid emulsion therapy was discovered as a rescue treatment, and a liposomal form (Exparel) was approved in 2011 for longer local pain relief.", "timeline": [["1884", "Carl Koller introduces cocaine as a local anesthetic for eye surgery."], ["1957", "Bo af Ekenstam synthesizes bupivacaine in Sweden."], ["1960s", "Bupivacaine enters clinical use."], ["1979", "Albright reports cardiac arrests linked to bupivacaine."], ["1998", "Guy Weinberg shows lipid emulsion reverses bupivacaine cardiotoxicity in animals."], ["2011", "Liposomal bupivacaine (Exparel) is approved in the US."]], "cell": "Nerve impulses travel when voltage-gated sodium channels open and sodium rushes in. Bupivacaine is an amide local anesthetic. Its uncharged form crosses the nerve membrane, then inside the cell it picks up a proton and the charged form binds a site inside the pore of the sodium channel. It binds preferentially to open and inactivated channels, so actively firing nerves are blocked faster (use-dependent block). With sodium channels blocked, the action potential cannot reach threshold and conduction stops. Small, unmyelinated pain fibers and autonomic fibers are blocked before larger motor fibers. Bupivacaine is very lipid-soluble and highly protein bound, which gives a long duration. It also binds cardiac sodium channels strongly and dissociates slowly, much more than lidocaine, which explains its severe heart toxicity.", "body": [["Peripheral nerves", "Blocks pain signals for hours after injection near nerves, in nerve blocks or infiltration of a wound."], ["Spinal cord", "Spinal and epidural injection blocks sensation and, at higher concentrations, movement for surgery and labor."], ["Blood vessels", "Neuraxial blocks inhibit sympathetic nerves, causing vasodilation and hypotension."], ["Brain", "If blood levels rise, it causes ringing in the ears, metallic taste, perioral numbness, agitation and seizures."], ["Heart", "High levels block cardiac sodium channels, causing wide QRS, bradycardia, ventricular arrhythmias and cardiac arrest."]], "journey": "Bupivacaine is injected at the site where it is needed, and its onset is about five to twenty minutes, slower than lidocaine. Absorption into the blood depends on the site, with more blood flow meaning higher levels; intercostal and caudal blocks give higher levels than subcutaneous injection. Epinephrine can be added to slow absorption. It is highly protein bound and crosses the placenta. The liver metabolizes it through CYP3A4 and other enzymes, and metabolites leave in the urine. Its half-life is about three hours in adults. The liposomal form releases drug slowly over several days.", "sidefx": "Local anesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST) happens when too much reaches the blood, often from accidental injection into a vessel. Sodium channel blockade in the brain first disinhibits circuits, causing seizures, then depresses them. In the heart, bupivacaine's tight binding to sodium channels causes conduction block and arrhythmias that are hard to resuscitate. Lipid emulsion 20% is the antidote: it is thought to draw the drug out of tissues like a sink and support heart metabolism. Neuraxial use can cause hypotension from sympathetic blockade. Allergies are rare with amides.", "fun": "The discovery that lipid emulsion could rescue local anesthetic toxicity grew from Guy Weinberg's experience with a patient with a disorder of fat metabolism who had a cardiac arrest after a small dose of bupivacaine.", "src": ["Marcaine and Exparel (bupivacaine) FDA labels", "Albright GA. Anesthesiology, 1979", "Weinberg GL et al. Anesthesiology, 1998"]}, "buprenorphine": {"origin": "Buprenorphine was developed by the British company Reckitt & Colman, better known for household cleaning products. In the 1960s chemists led by Kenneth Bentley and John Lewis worked on derivatives of thebaine, an opium alkaloid, hoping to find a strong painkiller with less risk of addiction and respiratory depression. Buprenorphine was synthesized in 1966. It was marketed as an injectable painkiller (Buprenex in the US in the early 1980s). Studies in the 1970s and 1980s showed it could also treat opioid addiction. In 2002 the FDA approved sublingual Subutex and Suboxone (with naloxone) for opioid use disorder, allowing doctors to treat addiction in office practice. The special federal waiver needed to prescribe it was removed in 2023.", "timeline": [["1966", "Reckitt & Colman chemists synthesize buprenorphine from thebaine."], ["1981", "Injectable buprenorphine (Buprenex) is approved in the US for pain."], ["2000", "The Drug Addiction Treatment Act allows office-based opioid treatment."], ["2002", "The FDA approves Subutex and Suboxone for opioid use disorder."], ["2023", "The federal X-waiver requirement to prescribe buprenorphine is removed."]], "cell": "Buprenorphine is a partial agonist at mu-opioid receptors with very high affinity and slow dissociation. Mu receptors couple to Gi/o proteins; activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase, opens potassium channels and closes calcium channels, reducing neuron firing and transmitter release in pain pathways and reward circuits. As a partial agonist, buprenorphine gives a smaller maximum effect than full agonists like morphine or fentanyl, so its respiratory depression and euphoria plateau at higher doses, the 'ceiling effect.' Its high affinity lets it displace full agonists from mu receptors. In a person physically dependent on a full agonist, this sudden drop in receptor activation causes precipitated withdrawal. It is also an antagonist at kappa receptors, which may help mood. In Suboxone, naloxone is poorly absorbed under the tongue but blocks opioid effects if the product is injected.", "body": [["Brain (reward and craving)", "Steady partial stimulation of mu receptors relieves withdrawal and cravings and blocks the high from other opioids."], ["Brain (pain)", "Provides strong pain relief, used in injection, patch and buccal film forms."], ["Breathing", "Causes respiratory depression with a ceiling effect alone, but dangerous depression with benzodiazepines, alcohol or in children who swallow it."], ["Gut", "Slows gut movement, causing constipation and nausea."], ["Liver", "Can raise liver enzymes and, rarely, cause hepatitis."], ["Heart", "Some forms, such as the patch and buccal film, can prolong the QT interval at higher doses."]], "journey": "Buprenorphine has very poor oral bioavailability because of first-pass metabolism, so it is given under the tongue, in the cheek, by patch, IV or as long-acting injections under the skin. Sublingual tablets and films work within about an hour. It is very fat-soluble, highly protein bound and crosses the blood-brain barrier and placenta. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP3A4 to norbuprenorphine and by glucuronidation, and it is excreted mainly in stool. Its half-life is long, about one to one and a half days, and its tight receptor binding makes its effect last even longer. Monthly or weekly injections release it slowly.", "sidefx": "Precipitated withdrawal happens when buprenorphine is started too soon after a full agonist, because it pushes the full agonist off the receptor but activates it less. Waiting for moderate withdrawal before the first dose avoids this. Its ceiling effect makes overdose less likely than with full agonists, but combined with benzodiazepines or alcohol it can still stop breathing. Constipation, nausea and sweating are usual opioid effects. Naloxone can reverse it but may need higher or repeated doses because buprenorphine binds the receptor so tightly. Dental problems have been reported with films and tablets dissolved in the mouth.", "fun": "Buprenorphine was discovered by a company better known for mustard and Dettol disinfectant, and its addiction treatment business later became the company Indivior.", "src": ["Suboxone, Sublocade and Buprenex (buprenorphine) FDA labels", "SAMHSA TIP 63: Medications for Opioid Use Disorder", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "bupropion": {"origin": "Bupropion was synthesized in 1969 by Nariman Mehta at Burroughs Wellcome, which was looking for antidepressants unlike the tricyclics. Its structure is related to amphetamine and to cathinone, a stimulant from the khat plant. The FDA approved it as Wellbutrin in 1985, but its launch was pulled in 1986 after seizures were seen in a study of patients with bulimia. It returned in 1989 with lower doses, and sustained-release forms reduced the seizure risk further. In 1997 it was approved as Zyban to help people quit smoking. It is one of the few antidepressants that does not usually cause weight gain or sexual side effects.", "timeline": [["1969", "Nariman Mehta at Burroughs Wellcome synthesizes bupropion."], ["1985", "The FDA approves bupropion (Wellbutrin) for depression."], ["1986", "Launch is withdrawn after reports of seizures."], ["1989", "Bupropion returns with revised dosing."], ["1997", "Bupropion is approved as Zyban for smoking cessation."], ["2003", "Extended-release Wellbutrin XL is approved."]], "cell": "Bupropion and its main active metabolite, hydroxybupropion, inhibit the reuptake of norepinephrine and dopamine by blocking the norepinephrine transporter (NET) and dopamine transporter (DAT). This increases norepinephrine and dopamine in synapses, including in the prefrontal cortex and reward pathways, which is thought to underlie its antidepressant effect and its ability to reduce nicotine craving and withdrawal. It has little effect on serotonin, which explains why it rarely causes sexual side effects. It is also a noncompetitive antagonist of nicotinic acetylcholine receptors, which may reduce the reward from smoking. It does not block histamine, muscarinic or alpha-1 receptors in a meaningful way, so it has few sedative or anticholinergic effects.", "body": [["Brain (mood)", "Improves depression and seasonal affective disorder over several weeks; often energizing."], ["Brain (craving)", "Reduces nicotine craving and withdrawal, roughly doubling quit rates."], ["Brain (seizures)", "Lowers the seizure threshold in a dose-related way."], ["Sleep", "Can cause insomnia and agitation, so doses are often taken in the morning."], ["Heart and vessels", "Can raise blood pressure and heart rate; overdose can cause tachycardia and QRS widening."], ["Weight and sexual function", "Tends to cause mild weight loss and few sexual side effects, unlike SSRIs."]], "journey": "Bupropion is absorbed by mouth and taken in immediate, sustained or extended-release forms to smooth peaks. It crosses the blood-brain barrier and is extensively metabolized in the liver, mainly by CYP2B6, to hydroxybupropion, which is active and present at higher levels than bupropion itself. Other active metabolites are also formed. Bupropion and its metabolites strongly inhibit CYP2D6, raising levels of drugs like metoprolol and some antidepressants. Metabolites leave mainly through the kidneys. The half-life is about twenty-one hours, with active metabolites lasting longer.", "sidefx": "Seizures are the most important risk and rise with higher doses and peak levels, likely from increased catecholamine activity. Risk is higher in people with eating disorders, alcohol or sedative withdrawal, head injury or seizure history, so it is avoided in those settings. Insomnia, agitation, dry mouth and tremor come from its activating catecholamine effects. Like all antidepressants, it carries a boxed warning for suicidal thoughts in young people. In overdose, seizures are common and the QRS can widen. There is no antidote; benzodiazepines treat seizures, and sodium bicarbonate may be used for wide QRS.", "fun": "Bupropion is chemically a cathinone, the same class as the active stimulant in khat leaves, though it has much weaker stimulant effects.", "src": ["Wellbutrin and Zyban (bupropion) FDA labels", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology"]}, "buspirone": {"origin": "Buspirone was synthesized in 1968 by chemists at Mead Johnson, part of Bristol-Myers, who were looking for new antipsychotic drugs. It did not work well as an antipsychotic, but researchers noticed it reduced anxiety in animals without causing sedation or muscle relaxation. It was developed as an anxiolytic and approved by the FDA in 1986 as BuSpar. It was the first of the azapirone class and the first major anxiety drug that did not act through GABA receptors, so it lacked the sedation, dependence and abuse potential of benzodiazepines. It remains a common choice for generalized anxiety disorder and is sometimes added to antidepressants.", "timeline": [["1968", "Mead Johnson chemists synthesize buspirone while looking for antipsychotics."], ["1986", "The FDA approves buspirone (BuSpar) for anxiety."]], "cell": "Buspirone acts mainly at serotonin 5-HT1A receptors, which are Gi-coupled. In the raphe nuclei of the brainstem it acts as a full agonist on presynaptic somatodendritic 5-HT1A autoreceptors. At first, this reduces serotonin neuron firing. Over two to four weeks, these autoreceptors become desensitized and downregulated, so serotonin neurons fire more and release more serotonin in target areas. At postsynaptic 5-HT1A receptors in the hippocampus and cortex, it is a partial agonist. The net result is enhanced serotonergic signaling in circuits that regulate anxiety, which explains the delayed onset. It also has weak dopamine D2 antagonist activity. It does not bind the GABA-A receptor, so it does not cause sedation, muscle relaxation, anticonvulsant effects or physical dependence, and it does not protect against benzodiazepine or alcohol withdrawal.", "body": [["Brain (anxiety)", "Gradually reduces worry and tension in generalized anxiety disorder over two to four weeks."], ["Brain (alertness)", "Causes little sedation and does not impair memory or coordination as benzodiazepines do."], ["Nervous system", "Dizziness, light-headedness, headache and nervousness are common, especially early on."], ["Gut", "Nausea is a common early side effect and usually fades as the body adjusts."], ["Movement", "Weak dopamine blockade can rarely cause restlessness or akathisia."]], "journey": "Buspirone is rapidly absorbed by mouth but has very low bioavailability because of extensive first-pass metabolism by CYP3A4. Taking it with food increases the amount absorbed, so it should be taken consistently with or without food. It crosses the blood-brain barrier. The liver converts it to several metabolites, including one that is weakly active, and they are excreted in urine and stool. Its half-life is only about two to three hours, so it is taken two or three times a day. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors and grapefruit juice raise levels sharply, while inducers such as rifampin lower them.", "sidefx": "Dizziness, nausea, headache and nervousness relate to early changes in serotonin signaling before receptors adapt. Because it does not act on GABA, it does not cause dependence, withdrawal or dangerous respiratory depression with alcohol. Combined with other serotonergic drugs, it can contribute to serotonin syndrome. With MAO inhibitors it can raise blood pressure dangerously, so that combination is avoided. It cannot be used to treat benzodiazepine withdrawal. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Because buspirone takes weeks to work, it is useless 'as needed' for a panic attack, a common teaching point when comparing it with benzodiazepines.", "src": ["Buspirone hydrochloride FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology"]}, "cabergoline": {"origin": "Cabergoline belongs to the ergot family, a group of drugs whose story begins with Claviceps purpurea, a fungus that infects rye. Ergot poisoning caused epidemics of gangrene and convulsions in the Middle Ages, but chemists later learned that ergot alkaloids act on dopamine, serotonin and adrenergic receptors. Cabergoline was made in the 1980s by chemists at the Italian company Farmitalia Carlo Erba as a long-acting synthetic ergoline built to stimulate dopamine D2 receptors. It was approved in the US in 1996 as Dostinex for high prolactin levels. Its very long half-life lets patients take it only once or twice a week, which made it a popular choice over the older drug bromocriptine.", "timeline": [["11th century", "Ergot poisoning from infected rye causes outbreaks known as Saint Anthony's fire."], ["1980s", "Farmitalia Carlo Erba develops cabergoline as a long-acting dopamine agonist."], ["1996", "FDA approves cabergoline (Dostinex) for hyperprolactinemia."], ["2007", "Studies in Parkinson disease link high-dose ergot dopamine agonists to heart valve disease."]], "cell": "In the anterior pituitary, lactotroph cells make prolactin. Their main brake is dopamine released from the hypothalamus, which acts on D2 receptors. Cabergoline is a strong, long-lasting agonist at these D2 receptors. D2 receptors couple to Gi proteins, so binding lowers adenylyl cyclase activity and cAMP, opens potassium channels and reduces calcium entry. The result is less prolactin gene transcription and less prolactin release. Over weeks, the constant dopamine signal also shrinks prolactin-secreting tumor cells. Cabergoline is also an agonist at serotonin 5-HT2B receptors on heart valve cells, which can drive fibrosis.", "body": [["Pituitary gland", "Prolactin falls and prolactinomas often shrink, which can relieve headaches and pressure on the optic chiasm."], ["Reproductive system", "High prolactin suppresses GnRH. Lowering prolactin lets normal FSH and LH return, so ovulation, menstrual cycles, sperm production and libido can recover."], ["Breasts", "Galactorrhea stops because the prolactin signal that drives milk production is removed."], ["Blood vessels", "Dopamine agonism can lower blood pressure and cause orthostatic dizziness, especially early in treatment."], ["Brain", "Dopamine stimulation in reward circuits can cause impulse control problems such as compulsive gambling or shopping, and rarely psychosis."], ["Heart valves", "Long-term 5-HT2B stimulation can thicken and stiffen valve leaflets, causing regurgitation."]], "journey": "Cabergoline is taken by mouth and absorbed from the gut. Prolactin starts to fall within hours, and one dose keeps prolactin suppressed for many days. It is broken down mainly in the liver by hydrolysis, with little role for the CYP enzymes, and leaves mostly in the feces with a smaller share in the urine. Its half-life is very long, around two to three days, which is why it can be given only once or twice a week.", "sidefx": "Nausea, dizziness and orthostatic hypotension come from dopamine stimulation in the vomiting center and in blood vessels. Impulse control disorders reflect overstimulation of dopamine reward pathways. The serious fibrotic problems, including heart valve disease and pleural or retroperitoneal fibrosis, are linked to 5-HT2B agonism, an ergot family trait. This is why echocardiograms are used to watch patients on long-term therapy. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Dopamine is the body's own prolactin-inhibiting factor, so hyperprolactinemia is one of the few conditions where a doctor deliberately mimics dopamine in the pituitary.", "src": ["Dostinex (cabergoline) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "calcium-acetate": {"origin": "Calcium acetate is the calcium salt of acetic acid, the acid in vinegar. Its role in medicine came from kidney failure. When the kidneys fail, they cannot get rid of phosphate, and high phosphate damages bones and blood vessels. In the 1970s and 1980s dialysis patients were given aluminum-based binders, but aluminum built up in the body and caused bone disease and dialysis dementia. Doctors turned to calcium salts as safer binders. Calcium acetate was found to bind more phosphate for each unit of calcium than calcium carbonate, so less calcium is absorbed for the same effect. It became a standard prescription phosphate binder under the brand name PhosLo.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Aluminum-based phosphate binders are linked to bone disease and dialysis dementia."], ["1980s", "Calcium salts replace aluminum as first-line phosphate binders in dialysis."], ["1990s", "Calcium acetate (PhosLo) becomes a common prescription phosphate binder in the US."]], "cell": "Calcium acetate does not act on a receptor. Taken with a meal, it dissolves in the stomach and small intestine and releases calcium ions. These calcium ions combine with phosphate from food to form calcium phosphate, an insoluble salt that cannot cross the gut wall. The bound phosphate passes out in the stool instead of being absorbed. Less phosphate entering the blood lowers serum phosphate. Lower phosphate in turn reduces the stimulus for parathyroid hormone release and slows the cycle of secondary hyperparathyroidism and vascular calcification seen in chronic kidney disease. Some free calcium is still absorbed, which raises serum calcium.", "body": [["Gut", "Binds phosphate in the meal so it leaves in the stool. Can cause nausea and constipation."], ["Blood", "Serum phosphate falls. Serum calcium can rise because some calcium is absorbed, and high calcium is the main safety concern."], ["Parathyroid glands", "Lower phosphate and higher calcium reduce the drive for parathyroid hormone release, which protects bone in kidney disease."], ["Bones", "Better control of phosphate and PTH limits renal osteodystrophy, the bone disease of kidney failure."], ["Heart and vessels", "Too much calcium and phosphate together can deposit in vessel walls. High calcium can also cause dysrhythmias, especially with digoxin."]], "journey": "Calcium acetate is taken by mouth with each meal, because it has to be in the gut at the same time as the food phosphate. Most of it stays in the intestine and works there. The calcium phosphate it forms leaves in the stool. A portion of the calcium is absorbed into the blood and is handled like any dietary calcium, stored in bone or removed by the kidneys, which work poorly in these patients. That is why serum calcium is checked regularly.", "sidefx": "Constipation and nausea come from the salt and its bulk in the gut. Hypercalcemia happens when absorbed calcium builds up in a patient whose kidneys cannot remove it. High calcium causes confusion, weakness and heart rhythm changes and makes digoxin toxicity more likely. A high calcium times phosphate product can lead to calcium deposits in vessels and soft tissue. Compared with calcium carbonate, calcium acetate binds more phosphate per amount of calcium, which is its main advantage. There is no specific antidote. The drug is stopped and calcium is lowered if needed.", "fun": "Calcium acetate binds phosphate best near neutral pH, so unlike calcium carbonate it still works well in patients taking acid-reducing drugs.", "src": ["PhosLo (calcium acetate) FDA label", "KDIGO CKD-Mineral and Bone Disorder Guideline", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "calcium-carbonate": {"origin": "Calcium carbonate is one of the most common minerals on Earth. It makes up chalk, limestone and marble, as well as eggshells, seashells and coral. People have eaten chalk to settle the stomach for centuries. In 1930 a St. Louis pharmacist named James Howe created a chewable calcium carbonate tablet for his wife's indigestion, and it was sold as Tums. Calcium carbonate later became the most widely used calcium supplement because it packs the most calcium by weight of any common calcium salt, about 40 percent elemental calcium. It is also used as a phosphate binder in kidney disease.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Chalk and crushed shells are used as stomach remedies."], ["1930", "Pharmacist James Howe introduces Tums, a chewable calcium carbonate antacid."], ["1980s", "Calcium salts replace aluminum binders for phosphate control in kidney failure."]], "cell": "Calcium carbonate has no receptor. In the stomach, carbonate reacts with hydrochloric acid to form calcium chloride, water and carbon dioxide. This neutralizes acid and raises gastric pH, which relieves heartburn and reduces the activity of pepsin. Some of the freed calcium is absorbed in the small intestine, mainly in the duodenum through active, vitamin D dependent transport and in the rest of the gut through passive diffusion. Absorbed calcium joins body stores and supports bone mineral, nerve signaling, muscle contraction and clotting. Calcium that stays in the gut can bind dietary phosphate into insoluble calcium phosphate, which is why it also works as a phosphate binder.", "body": [["Stomach", "Neutralizes acid within minutes and relieves heartburn. The carbon dioxide made in the reaction causes belching and bloating."], ["Bones", "Supplies calcium for bone mineral, which helps prevent and treat osteoporosis when intake is low."], ["Gut", "Slows bowel movement and often causes constipation. In kidney disease it binds dietary phosphate."], ["Kidneys", "Extra absorbed calcium is filtered by the kidneys and can raise the risk of kidney stones."], ["Blood chemistry", "Very high intake with milk or other absorbable alkali can cause milk-alkali syndrome with high calcium, alkalosis and kidney injury."]], "journey": "Calcium carbonate is taken by mouth. It needs stomach acid to dissolve well, so it is best absorbed when taken with food. People on proton pump inhibitors absorb it less well. Only part of the calcium is absorbed, mostly in the duodenum with the help of vitamin D. The rest leaves in the feces. Absorbed calcium is stored in bone or removed by the kidneys. The antacid effect starts within minutes and lasts as long as the tablet stays in the stomach.", "sidefx": "Constipation, gas and bloating come from the reaction with stomach acid and the slowing effect of calcium on the gut. Acid rebound can follow because the stomach responds to a high pH by making more gastrin and acid. Kidney stones and hypercalcemia happen when too much calcium is absorbed. Milk-alkali syndrome combines high calcium, metabolic alkalosis and kidney injury, and it has become more common as people take large amounts of calcium carbonate supplements. Calcium carbonate also binds many drugs in the gut, such as tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones and levothyroxine. Treatment of overload is stopping the calcium and giving fluids.", "fun": "Milk-alkali syndrome was first described in ulcer patients treated with milk and alkali powders in the early 1900s and is now seen mostly in people taking large amounts of over-the-counter calcium carbonate.", "src": ["Calcium carbonate OTC drug facts label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "calcium-chloride": {"origin": "The idea that the heart needs calcium came from an accident. In the early 1880s the British physiologist Sydney Ringer was studying isolated frog hearts. When his assistant made the salt solution with London tap water, the hearts kept beating. When it was made with distilled water, they stopped. Ringer found that the calcium in the hard tap water was the key, and his work led to Ringer's solution. Calcium chloride became the classic resuscitation form of calcium because it delivers calcium in a small volume. Today it is used for hyperkalemia, calcium channel blocker overdose and low ionized calcium after massive transfusion.", "timeline": [["1882", "Sydney Ringer reports that calcium is needed to keep the isolated frog heart beating."], ["1883", "Ringer publishes further work that leads to Ringer's solution."], ["2010s", "Resuscitation guidelines limit routine calcium in cardiac arrest to specific causes such as hyperkalemia."]], "cell": "Calcium chloride dissolves completely into calcium and chloride ions, so it raises ionized calcium right away. In hyperkalemia, high potassium raises the resting membrane potential of heart cells toward threshold and inactivates sodium channels. Extra extracellular calcium raises the threshold potential, which restores the gap between resting and threshold voltage. This stabilizes the membrane and lowers the risk of dangerous rhythms. It does not lower potassium. In heart and smooth muscle, more extracellular calcium increases calcium entry through L-type channels, which strengthens contraction and raises vascular tone. That is why it helps in calcium channel blocker and magnesium toxicity.", "body": [["Heart", "Stabilizes the cell membrane in hyperkalemia and strengthens contraction. Rapid push can cause slowing of the heart and, rarely, arrest."], ["Blood vessels", "Increases smooth muscle tone, which can raise blood pressure in calcium channel blocker overdose."], ["Blood", "Restores ionized calcium after massive transfusion, where citrate in blood products binds calcium."], ["Nerves and muscle", "Normal calcium steadies nerve membranes. Correcting low calcium stops tetany, spasms and seizures caused by hypocalcemia."], ["Veins and tissue", "Calcium chloride is very irritating. If it leaks out of a vein it can cause tissue necrosis, so a large or central vein is preferred."]], "journey": "Calcium chloride is given IV or IO and works almost immediately because it is already fully ionized. Calcium chloride 10 percent contains about three times as much elemental calcium as the same volume of calcium gluconate 10 percent, which is the key difference between the two salts. The membrane-stabilizing effect in hyperkalemia lasts roughly 30 to 60 minutes. Calcium is then taken up into bone and other stores or removed by the kidneys.", "sidefx": "A sense of heat, tingling and a chalky taste come from a sudden rise in ionized calcium. Rapid injection can cause bradycardia, hypotension from vasodilation and rarely cardiac arrest. In patients on digoxin, high calcium adds to digoxin's effect on heart cells and can trigger arrhythmias. The chloride salt is acidic and highly irritating, so leakage into tissue can cause severe burns and necrosis. Too much calcium causes hypercalcemia. There is no antidote. Treatment is stopping the drug and managing extravasation.", "fun": "Sydney Ringer's famous discovery that calcium keeps the heart beating happened because his lab assistant used tap water instead of distilled water.", "src": ["Calcium chloride injection FDA label", "Ringer S. J Physiol 1883", "American Heart Association ACLS Guidelines"]}, "calcium-gluconate": {"origin": "Calcium gluconate is calcium bound to gluconic acid, a mild acid made from glucose. Scientists knew from Sydney Ringer's work in the 1880s that calcium was vital for the heart and muscles, but early calcium salts were harsh on veins and the gut. In 1929 the Swiss company Sandoz launched Calcium-Sandoz, a calcium gluconate product that could be given more comfortably, and it became one of the company's best-selling drugs. Calcium gluconate remains the preferred calcium salt for peripheral IVs because it is much less irritating to tissue than calcium chloride. It is also used for magnesium toxicity, hyperkalemia and hydrofluoric acid burns.", "timeline": [["1880s", "Sydney Ringer shows that calcium is required for heart contraction."], ["1929", "Sandoz launches Calcium-Sandoz, a calcium gluconate product."]], "cell": "Calcium gluconate releases ionized calcium into the blood. It does not need the liver to release its calcium, a common myth. In hyperkalemia, extra calcium raises the threshold potential of heart cells and restores the normal gap between resting and threshold potential, so the heart is less likely to develop dangerous rhythms. It does not lower potassium. At the neuromuscular junction, magnesium blocks calcium entry into nerve terminals and reduces acetylcholine release. Raising calcium overcomes this competition and restores muscle strength and breathing. In hydrofluoric acid burns, calcium binds fluoride ions into insoluble calcium fluoride and stops tissue damage.", "body": [["Heart", "Stabilizes the heart cell membrane in hyperkalemia and supports contraction. Fast injection in a patient on digoxin can cause arrhythmias."], ["Neuromuscular junction", "Reverses magnesium-induced weakness and breathing depression, for example in magnesium toxicity during preeclampsia treatment."], ["Nerves and muscle", "Corrects hypocalcemia, stopping tetany, carpopedal spasm, laryngospasm and seizures."], ["Skin", "As a gel or injection, it binds fluoride in hydrofluoric acid burns and relieves the deep pain."], ["Veins", "Much less irritating than calcium chloride, so it is the preferred salt for peripheral IV lines."]], "journey": "Calcium gluconate is usually given IV and acts within minutes. Gluconate is a large molecule, so calcium gluconate 10 percent has about one third the elemental calcium of the same volume of calcium chloride 10 percent, and larger volumes are needed for the same calcium. The heart membrane effect lasts about 30 to 60 minutes. The gluconate is metabolized like other sugars. The calcium is stored in bone or removed by the kidneys.", "sidefx": "Tingling, warmth and a chalky taste reflect the quick rise in ionized calcium. Fast injection can cause bradycardia, flushing and hypotension. Calcium adds to the effect of digoxin on heart cells, so arrhythmias are a risk in digitalized patients. In newborns, ceftriaxone must not be given with calcium solutions because they can form deadly precipitates in the lungs and kidneys. Too much calcium causes hypercalcemia. There is no antidote. Treatment is stopping the infusion.", "fun": "Calcium gluconate gel is the classic first aid for hydrofluoric acid burns because it traps fluoride as calcium fluoride before fluoride can pull calcium out of the victim's tissues.", "src": ["Calcium gluconate injection FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "American Heart Association ACLS Guidelines"]}, "carbamazepine": {"origin": "Carbamazepine was made in 1953 by the chemist Walter Schindler at J.R. Geigy in Basel, Switzerland, during work on compounds related to the tricyclic antidepressant imipramine. It turned out to be a strong anticonvulsant. It was first used for trigeminal neuralgia in the early 1960s, then for epilepsy, and was approved in the US in 1968. Later it was found to stabilize mood in bipolar disorder. In 2007 the FDA added a warning that people with the gene HLA-B*1502, common in many Asian populations, have a much higher risk of severe skin reactions, one of the first big examples of pharmacogenomics in practice.", "timeline": [["1953", "Walter Schindler synthesizes carbamazepine at J.R. Geigy."], ["1962", "Carbamazepine is introduced for trigeminal neuralgia."], ["1968", "FDA approves carbamazepine (Tegretol)."], ["2007", "FDA adds HLA-B*1502 genetic screening warning for severe skin reactions."]], "cell": "Carbamazepine binds voltage-gated sodium channels in neurons and stabilizes them in the inactivated state. After a neuron fires, its sodium channels must recover before it can fire again. By slowing this recovery, carbamazepine limits the rapid, repetitive firing that spreads a seizure, while normal low-frequency firing is mostly spared. This use-dependent block also calms the abnormal bursts in the trigeminal nerve that cause neuralgia pain. It has a tricyclic structure, which explains some anticholinergic effects. It also acts on the kidney to increase the effect of ADH, which causes hyponatremia.", "body": [["Brain", "Reduces seizure spread and stabilizes mood. At higher levels it causes dizziness, double vision, unsteady gait and drowsiness."], ["Cranial nerves", "Quiets the abnormal firing of the trigeminal nerve, making it a first-line treatment for trigeminal neuralgia."], ["Kidneys", "Increases kidney sensitivity to ADH, so the body holds water and sodium levels fall (SIADH-like picture)."], ["Bone marrow", "Can lower white cells and rarely cause aplastic anemia or agranulocytosis."], ["Skin", "Can cause rashes from mild to life-threatening SJS and TEN, especially with HLA-B*1502."], ["Liver", "Strongly induces CYP3A4 and other enzymes, speeding the breakdown of many drugs, including itself."]], "journey": "Carbamazepine is taken by mouth and absorbed slowly and somewhat unevenly, with peak levels several hours later. It spreads through the body, crosses the blood-brain barrier and the placenta, and is heavily protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A4 into carbamazepine-10,11-epoxide, an active metabolite. It also induces its own metabolism (autoinduction), so the half-life shortens over the first weeks of therapy. Metabolites leave in the urine.", "sidefx": "Dizziness, double vision and ataxia come from sodium channel block in the cerebellum and brainstem. Hyponatremia comes from its ADH-like effect in the kidney. Skin reactions such as SJS and TEN are immune reactions strongly tied to HLA-B*1502 and HLA-A*3101, so genetic testing is advised for at-risk groups. Bone marrow suppression can cause aplastic anemia or agranulocytosis. As a strong enzyme inducer it lowers levels of oral contraceptives, warfarin and many other drugs. It is a teratogen linked to neural tube defects. Overdose has no antidote. Care is supportive, sometimes with activated charcoal.", "fun": "Carbamazepine is a classic board example of autoinduction: it speeds up the liver enzymes that destroy it, so blood levels fall after the first few weeks.", "src": ["Tegretol (carbamazepine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "carbidopa-levodopa": {"origin": "Levodopa, also called L-DOPA, was first isolated from fava beans in 1913 by the chemist Markus Guggenheim. Its medical value came much later. In 1957 the Swedish scientist Arvid Carlsson showed that L-DOPA reversed the frozen state of animals drained of dopamine by reserpine, proving dopamine is a brain transmitter. In 1960 Oleh Hornykiewicz found that dopamine is depleted in the brains of people with Parkinson disease. In 1967 George Cotzias showed that high oral doses of levodopa dramatically improved patients. Levodopa was approved in the US in 1970. Adding carbidopa, which blocks levodopa breakdown outside the brain, led to Sinemet in 1975. Carlsson shared the 2000 Nobel Prize.", "timeline": [["1913", "Markus Guggenheim isolates L-DOPA from fava beans."], ["1957", "Arvid Carlsson shows L-DOPA reverses dopamine depletion in animals."], ["1960", "Oleh Hornykiewicz finds low dopamine in Parkinson disease brains."], ["1967", "George Cotzias reports dramatic benefit of oral levodopa."], ["1975", "Carbidopa-levodopa (Sinemet) is approved in the US."], ["2000", "Arvid Carlsson shares the Nobel Prize for work on dopamine."]], "cell": "Dopamine itself cannot cross the blood-brain barrier, but levodopa can. It rides the large neutral amino acid transporter into the brain. Inside the remaining dopamine neurons of the substantia nigra, aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (DOPA decarboxylase) turns levodopa into dopamine. The new dopamine is released into the striatum, where it stimulates D1 and D2 receptors and restores the balance of the basal ganglia motor circuits, easing slowness, rigidity and tremor. Carbidopa blocks DOPA decarboxylase but cannot cross the blood-brain barrier. It stops levodopa from turning into dopamine in the gut and blood, so more levodopa reaches the brain and fewer peripheral side effects occur.", "body": [["Basal ganglia", "Restores dopamine signaling, improving bradykinesia and rigidity more than tremor."], ["Brain and mind", "Excess dopamine can cause hallucinations, vivid dreams, confusion and impulse control disorders."], ["Motor system", "Over years, swings in levodopa levels lead to dyskinesias (extra writhing movements) and wearing-off periods."], ["Gut and vomiting center", "Dopamine stimulates the chemoreceptor trigger zone, causing nausea. Carbidopa reduces this by limiting peripheral dopamine."], ["Blood vessels", "Dopamine and the disease itself cause orthostatic hypotension and dizziness."]], "journey": "Carbidopa-levodopa is taken by mouth and levodopa is absorbed in the small intestine by amino acid transporters. Dietary protein competes for these transporters, so high-protein meals can blunt the effect. Levodopa crosses the blood-brain barrier by the same transporter system. Carbidopa stays outside the brain and blocks peripheral breakdown. Levodopa has a short half-life of about 90 minutes, which explains the wearing-off between doses in advanced disease. It is metabolized by decarboxylase and COMT, and metabolites leave in the urine.", "sidefx": "Nausea and orthostatic hypotension come from dopamine made outside the brain. Hallucinations, psychosis and impulse control problems come from too much dopamine in limbic and reward pathways. Dyskinesias reflect pulsatile stimulation of sensitized striatal receptors. Stopping levodopa suddenly can cause a syndrome like neuroleptic malignant syndrome with fever and rigidity, so it should never be stopped abruptly. Pyridoxine (vitamin B6) increases peripheral breakdown of plain levodopa, which carbidopa largely prevents. Nonselective MAO inhibitors can cause a hypertensive crisis with levodopa.", "fun": "Oliver Sacks wrote about awakening patients frozen by encephalitis lethargica with levodopa in his book Awakenings, later made into a film.", "src": ["Sinemet (carbidopa-levodopa) FDA label", "Cotzias GC et al. N Engl J Med 1967", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "carboprost": {"origin": "Prostaglandins were named in the 1930s by Ulf von Euler, who found a substance in seminal fluid that contracted smooth muscle and assumed it came from the prostate. Later work by Sune Bergström, Bengt Samuelsson and John Vane, who shared the 1982 Nobel Prize, showed these were a family of lipid messengers made throughout the body. Natural prostaglandin F2-alpha contracts the uterus but is broken down within minutes. In the 1970s chemists at Upjohn added a methyl group at carbon 15 to make 15-methyl PGF2-alpha, carboprost, which resists breakdown and lasts much longer. Sold as Hemabate, it became a key second-line drug for postpartum hemorrhage caused by uterine atony.", "timeline": [["1935", "Ulf von Euler describes and names prostaglandin."], ["1970s", "Upjohn develops carboprost, a long-acting 15-methyl PGF2-alpha analog."], ["1982", "Bergström, Samuelsson and Vane share the Nobel Prize for prostaglandin research."]], "cell": "Carboprost binds the prostaglandin FP receptor on uterine smooth muscle. This Gq-coupled receptor activates phospholipase C, which makes IP3 and DAG. IP3 releases calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, and calcium binds calmodulin to activate myosin light chain kinase. The result is strong, sustained contraction of the myometrium. A contracted uterus squeezes the blood vessels running through its wall, which stops bleeding after delivery. The methyl group at carbon 15 blocks 15-hydroxyprostaglandin dehydrogenase, the enzyme that normally inactivates PGF2-alpha, so the drug acts much longer. FP receptors in bronchial and gut smooth muscle respond the same way.", "body": [["Uterus", "Produces strong, sustained contractions that clamp the uterine vessels and control postpartum bleeding. It can also be used to end a pregnancy in the second trimester."], ["Lungs", "Contracts bronchial smooth muscle and can cause severe bronchospasm, which is why asthma is a contraindication."], ["Gut", "Increases smooth muscle activity, causing diarrhea, nausea and vomiting in most patients."], ["Blood vessels", "Can raise blood pressure and constrict pulmonary vessels, which may worsen oxygenation."], ["Temperature control", "Prostaglandins act on the hypothalamus and commonly cause fever, chills and flushing."]], "journey": "Carboprost is given by deep IM injection, or directly into the uterine muscle in some settings. It is absorbed quickly, and the uterus responds within minutes. It is broken down mainly by enzymes in the lungs and liver, more slowly than natural prostaglandin because of its methyl group. Metabolites leave in the urine. Its effects last a few hours, and doses can be repeated.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea, vomiting, fever and flushing come from the same FP receptor activation in the gut, blood vessels and hypothalamus. These are so common that antidiarrheals and antiemetics are often given with it. Bronchospasm and hypoxemia come from contraction of airway smooth muscle and pulmonary vasoconstriction, so it is avoided in asthma. Hypertension comes from vasoconstriction. There is no antidote. Bronchospasm is treated with bronchodilators and supportive care.", "fun": "Prostaglandins got their name from the prostate gland, even though nearly every tissue in the body makes them.", "src": ["Hemabate (carboprost tromethamine) FDA label", "ACOG Practice Bulletin: Postpartum Hemorrhage", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "carvedilol": {"origin": "For decades beta blockers were thought to be dangerous in heart failure because they weaken contraction. Carvedilol helped overturn that belief. It was developed in Germany at Boehringer Mannheim in the 1980s as a beta blocker that also blocks alpha-1 receptors and dilates vessels. Marketed in the US by SmithKline Beecham as Coreg, it was approved for hypertension in 1995. After the US Carvedilol Heart Failure Trials showed lower death rates, the FDA approved it for heart failure in 1997, making it the first beta blocker with that indication. Later trials such as COPERNICUS confirmed benefit even in severe heart failure.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Boehringer Mannheim develops carvedilol."], ["1995", "FDA approves carvedilol (Coreg) for hypertension."], ["1996", "US Carvedilol Heart Failure Trials report lower mortality."], ["1997", "Carvedilol becomes the first beta blocker approved for heart failure in the US."], ["2001", "COPERNICUS trial shows benefit in severe heart failure."]], "cell": "Carvedilol is a nonselective competitive antagonist at beta-1 and beta-2 receptors and also blocks alpha-1 receptors. At beta-1 receptors in the heart, it blocks catecholamine activation of Gs, adenylyl cyclase and cAMP, which lowers heart rate, contractility and renin release from the kidney. In heart failure, chronic sympathetic stimulation is toxic to heart muscle, promoting cell death, arrhythmias and remodeling. Blocking it allows the heart to recover and remodel favorably over months. Alpha-1 blockade relaxes vascular smooth muscle by blocking the Gq and IP3 calcium pathway, lowering afterload. It also has antioxidant properties.", "body": [["Heart", "Slows heart rate and reduces oxygen demand. Over months it improves ejection fraction and lowers death rates in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction."], ["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 blockade dilates arteries, lowering blood pressure and afterload, but it can cause dizziness and orthostatic hypotension."], ["Kidneys", "Beta-1 blockade reduces renin release, lowering angiotensin II and aldosterone."], ["Lungs", "Beta-2 blockade can tighten airways, so it is used with caution in asthma."], ["Metabolism", "Can mask the warning signs of low blood sugar, such as tremor and palpitations."]], "journey": "Carvedilol is taken by mouth with food, which slows absorption and lowers the risk of orthostatic hypotension. It has a large first-pass effect, so only part of each dose reaches the blood. It is fat soluble and highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP2D6 and CYP2C9, so poor CYP2D6 metabolizers have higher levels. Metabolites leave mostly through bile in the feces. Its half-life is about 7 to 10 hours.", "sidefx": "Dizziness and hypotension come from alpha-1 blockade plus lower cardiac output. Bradycardia and heart block come from beta-1 blockade of the SA and AV nodes. Heart failure can briefly worsen when therapy is started or increased because the heart loses its sympathetic support, so it is started low and increased slowly. Bronchospasm comes from beta-2 blockade. Weight gain and fluid retention can occur early. Overdose is treated with glucagon, which raises cAMP without using beta receptors, plus high-dose insulin and vasopressors.", "fun": "Carvedilol helped flip a medical rule: beta blockers went from being avoided in heart failure to being one of its core life-saving therapies.", "src": ["Coreg (carvedilol) FDA label", "Packer M et al. N Engl J Med 1996", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "caspofungin": {"origin": "Caspofungin comes from a fungus that makes its own antifungal weapon. Researchers at Merck found that the fungus Glarea lozoyensis produces pneumocandin B0, a lipopeptide that attacks the cell walls of other fungi. Merck chemists modified this natural product to make caspofungin, which is more stable and more soluble. In 2001 the FDA approved it as Cancidas, the first echinocandin. It gave doctors a new class of antifungal that hits a target human cells do not have, the fungal cell wall, and it became a key drug for invasive Candida infections and for Aspergillus infections that do not respond to other drugs.", "timeline": [["1970s", "The first echinocandin natural products are discovered in fungi."], ["1990s", "Merck develops caspofungin from pneumocandin B0 made by Glarea lozoyensis."], ["2001", "FDA approves caspofungin (Cancidas), the first echinocandin."]], "cell": "The fungal cell wall is built partly from long chains of beta-1,3-D-glucan. These are made by the enzyme beta-1,3-D-glucan synthase in the fungal cell membrane, with Fks1 as its catalytic subunit. Caspofungin noncompetitively inhibits this enzyme. Without new glucan, the wall becomes weak and cannot resist the cell's internal osmotic pressure. Growing Candida cells swell and burst, so the drug is fungicidal against Candida. In Aspergillus, it damages the growing tips of the hyphae and is fungistatic. Human cells have no cell wall and no glucan synthase, so the drug has very little direct toxicity to people.", "body": [["Fungal infections", "Kills most Candida species, including many resistant to fluconazole, and slows Aspergillus growth."], ["Liver", "Can raise liver enzymes. Doses are adjusted in moderate liver disease because the liver clears it."], ["Skin and vessels", "Can cause flushing, rash and itching during infusion from histamine release, plus phlebitis at the IV site."], ["Blood chemistry", "Can cause low potassium levels, so electrolytes are checked during therapy."], ["Immune system", "Fever and chills during or after infusion are fairly common."]], "journey": "Caspofungin is a large molecule that is not absorbed by mouth, so it is given by IV infusion. It is highly bound to albumin and spreads into tissues, especially the liver, but penetrates poorly into the brain, eye and urine. It is broken down slowly by hydrolysis and N-acetylation, not by CYP enzymes, so it has fewer drug interactions than azoles. Metabolites leave slowly in the urine and feces. A loading dose is used to reach effective levels quickly, and it is given once daily.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are mild because the drug's target does not exist in human cells. Infusion reactions such as flushing, rash and facial swelling come from histamine release, similar to vancomycin, and slowing the infusion helps. Liver enzyme rises reflect its uptake and processing in the liver. Cyclosporine increases caspofungin levels and the two together raise liver enzymes more. Enzyme inducers such as rifampin lower its levels. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Because poor penetration into urine and the eye limits it, caspofungin is a weak choice for Candida urinary tract infections or endophthalmitis, a frequent exam twist.", "src": ["Cancidas (caspofungin) FDA label", "IDSA Clinical Practice Guideline for Candidiasis", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "cefazolin": {"origin": "The cephalosporin story began in 1945 when the Italian doctor Giuseppe Brotzu collected a mold, Cephalosporium acremonium (now Acremonium chrysogenum), from seawater near a sewage outlet in Sardinia. He noticed it killed typhoid bacteria. In the 1950s, Edward Abraham and Guy Newton at Oxford isolated cephalosporin C and its core, 7-aminocephalosporanic acid, which chemists could modify. Cefazolin was developed by the Japanese company Fujisawa and introduced in the early 1970s. It became the standard first-generation IV cephalosporin and is still the most common antibiotic used to prevent surgical site infections.", "timeline": [["1945", "Giuseppe Brotzu isolates a cephalosporin-producing mold from Sardinian seawater."], ["1950s", "Abraham and Newton at Oxford isolate cephalosporin C."], ["1964", "The first cephalosporin, cephalothin, enters clinical use."], ["1970s", "Cefazolin is introduced as an IV first-generation cephalosporin."]], "cell": "Cefazolin is a beta-lactam antibiotic. Bacteria build their cell walls from peptidoglycan, long sugar chains cross-linked by short peptides. The final cross-linking step is done by transpeptidases called penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs). The beta-lactam ring of cefazolin mimics the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of the peptide chain and binds PBPs, acylating and inactivating them. Without cross-links, the wall is weak. As the bacterium grows, autolysins break down the old wall and the cell bursts. It is bactericidal and works best on dividing bacteria. It is strong against MSSA and streptococci and covers some gram-negatives such as E. coli, Klebsiella and Proteus.", "body": [["Skin and soft tissue", "Kills staph and strep that cause cellulitis and wound infections, and prevents surgical site infections."], ["Bones and joints", "Used for MSSA bone, joint and bloodstream infections."], ["Kidneys", "Cleared by the kidneys, so levels rise in kidney failure and doses are adjusted."], ["Gut", "Can upset normal gut flora, causing diarrhea and sometimes C. difficile colitis."], ["Brain", "Very high levels in kidney failure can irritate neurons and cause seizures."], ["Immune system", "Can cause rash and, rarely, anaphylaxis. Cross-reactivity with penicillin allergy is low."]], "journey": "Cefazolin is given IV or IM because it is not absorbed well by mouth. It reaches high levels in blood, bone, skin and tissues but does not cross the blood-brain barrier well, so it is not used for meningitis. It is not metabolized much and leaves unchanged in the urine. Its half-life is under two hours, so it is given every 8 hours and redosed during long surgeries.", "sidefx": "Rash and allergic reactions come from the immune system recognizing the beta-lactam and its side chains. The risk of cross-reaction with penicillin is low because cefazolin's side chain is unique. Diarrhea and C. difficile come from killing normal gut bacteria. High levels in kidney failure can cause seizures because beta-lactams can block GABA-A receptors. Phlebitis comes from local vein irritation. There is no antidote. Anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine.", "fun": "Cefazolin has a unique side chain shared with no other common beta-lactam, which is why guidelines now allow it for many patients with penicillin allergy before surgery.", "src": ["Cefazolin for injection FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "cefdinir": {"origin": "Cefdinir is a descendant of the cephalosporin mold that Giuseppe Brotzu found in Sardinian seawater in 1945. Like other modern cephalosporins, it was made by chemically modifying the cephalosporin core to change its spectrum and how it is absorbed. Cefdinir was developed by the Japanese company Fujisawa and was approved in the US in 1997 under the brand name Omnicef. It became a popular oral third-generation cephalosporin for children with ear infections and sinusitis because its liquid form tastes good and it is given once or twice a day.", "timeline": [["1945", "Giuseppe Brotzu discovers the cephalosporin-producing mold."], ["1991", "Cefdinir is introduced in Japan by Fujisawa."], ["1997", "FDA approves cefdinir (Omnicef)."]], "cell": "Cefdinir is a beta-lactam that binds penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), the transpeptidases that cross-link peptidoglycan in the bacterial cell wall. By acylating and inactivating these enzymes, it stops new cross-links from forming. The weakened wall cannot hold back the cell's internal pressure, and autolysins break it down, so the bacterium bursts. It is bactericidal. As a third-generation agent it is more stable against many beta-lactamases from Haemophilus influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis than older oral drugs, while keeping good activity against streptococci and MSSA.", "body": [["Ears, sinuses and throat", "Treats otitis media, sinusitis and strep throat caused by common respiratory bacteria."], ["Lungs", "Used for some community-acquired pneumonia and bronchitis flares."], ["Skin", "Treats uncomplicated skin infections from staph and strep."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is the most common side effect. Stools can turn reddish when cefdinir binds iron in the gut."], ["Immune system", "Can cause rash and rarely serious allergic reactions."]], "journey": "Cefdinir is taken by mouth as capsules or a liquid. Only a modest fraction is absorbed. Iron supplements and antacids with aluminum or magnesium bind it in the gut and lower absorption, so they should be separated. It is distributed into tissues such as the middle ear and sinuses. It is barely metabolized and leaves unchanged in the urine with a half-life under two hours, so doses are reduced in kidney disease.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea and C. difficile colitis come from killing normal gut bacteria. Rash and allergic reactions come from immune recognition of the beta-lactam. Red or brick-colored stools happen because cefdinir and its breakdown products bind iron in the gut, and this is harmless. Anaphylaxis is rare and is treated with epinephrine. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The harmless red-tinged stool seen with cefdinir, especially in babies on iron-fortified formula, is a classic call that worries parents.", "src": ["Omnicef (cefdinir) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "cefepime": {"origin": "Cefepime is a fourth-generation cephalosporin, built on the cephalosporin core that came from Giuseppe Brotzu's Sardinian mold in 1945. By the late 1980s, hospital bacteria were producing enzymes that destroyed third-generation cephalosporins. Bristol-Myers Squibb developed cefepime with a positively charged group that gives the molecule both a positive and a negative charge (a zwitterion). This lets it slip through the outer membrane of gram-negative bacteria quickly and makes it more stable against some beta-lactamases. It was approved in the US in 1996 as Maxipime and became a mainstay for hospital infections, including those caused by Pseudomonas, and for fever in neutropenic patients.", "timeline": [["1945", "Giuseppe Brotzu discovers the cephalosporin-producing mold."], ["1996", "FDA approves cefepime (Maxipime)."], ["2012", "FDA warns about cefepime neurotoxicity, including nonconvulsive status epilepticus, in kidney impairment."]], "cell": "Cefepime binds and inactivates penicillin-binding proteins, the transpeptidases that cross-link the bacterial cell wall, so bacteria burst as they grow. It is bactericidal. Its zwitterion structure helps it pass rapidly through porin channels in the outer membrane of gram-negative bacteria and reach PBPs in high amounts. It is a poor substrate for, and is less readily hydrolyzed by, AmpC chromosomal beta-lactamases, which inactivate third-generation cephalosporins in organisms such as Enterobacter. It covers Pseudomonas aeruginosa as well as streptococci and MSSA. It does not cover MRSA, enterococci or most anaerobes.", "body": [["Lungs", "Treats hospital-acquired pneumonia, including Pseudomonas."], ["Blood and immune system", "Used as empiric therapy for fever in neutropenic patients."], ["Urinary tract", "Treats complicated urinary tract infections."], ["Brain", "Can cause confusion, myoclonus and nonconvulsive seizures, especially when kidney function is poor."], ["Kidneys", "Cleared by the kidneys, so doses must be adjusted in kidney disease."], ["Gut", "Can cause diarrhea and C. difficile colitis."]], "journey": "Cefepime is given IV or IM. It distributes widely into body fluids and tissues and reaches the cerebrospinal fluid when the meninges are inflamed. It is barely metabolized and is removed almost entirely unchanged by the kidneys, with a half-life of about two hours in normal kidney function. In kidney failure the half-life becomes much longer, which allows the drug to build up.", "sidefx": "Neurotoxicity is the side effect to remember. Cefepime can cross into the brain and block GABA-A receptors, removing inhibitory signals. When the kidneys cannot clear it, levels rise and patients can develop confusion, myoclonus, encephalopathy and nonconvulsive status epilepticus. This usually improves after stopping the drug, and dialysis can remove it. Diarrhea, C. difficile and allergic reactions are shared with other beta-lactams.", "fun": "Cefepime encephalopathy is easy to miss because a confused, twitching ICU patient may have nonconvulsive seizures that only an EEG reveals.", "src": ["Maxipime (cefepime) FDA label", "FDA Drug Safety Communication on cefepime, 2012", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "ceftaroline": {"origin": "For decades, MRSA was resistant to every beta-lactam because it makes PBP2a, a cell wall enzyme that ignores them. Ceftaroline was designed to break that rule. It was developed from an earlier compound made by the Japanese company Takeda and brought to market in the US by Forest Laboratories through its Cerexa unit. In October 2010 the FDA approved ceftaroline fosamil as Teflaro for skin infections and community-acquired pneumonia. It was the first US cephalosporin with reliable activity against MRSA and is sometimes called a fifth-generation cephalosporin.", "timeline": [["1945", "Giuseppe Brotzu discovers the cephalosporin-producing mold."], ["1961", "MRSA is first reported in the United Kingdom."], ["2010", "FDA approves ceftaroline fosamil (Teflaro), an anti-MRSA cephalosporin."]], "cell": "Ceftaroline fosamil is a prodrug. In the blood, phosphatase enzymes remove its phosphono group to release active ceftaroline. Ceftaroline binds penicillin-binding proteins and blocks the transpeptidation step of cell wall synthesis, so bacteria burst. What sets it apart is high affinity for PBP2a, the altered PBP encoded by the mecA gene that makes MRSA resistant to other beta-lactams. Its side chain reaches into PBP2a's active site, which other beta-lactams cannot do well. It also binds PBP2x, which helps against penicillin-resistant Streptococcus pneumoniae. It does not cover Pseudomonas, ESBL-producing bacteria or most anaerobes.", "body": [["Skin and soft tissue", "Treats acute bacterial skin infections, including those caused by MRSA."], ["Lungs", "Treats community-acquired pneumonia, including resistant pneumococcus. It is not approved for MRSA pneumonia."], ["Blood", "Can cause a positive direct Coombs test and, rarely, hemolytic anemia. Long courses can lower neutrophil counts."], ["Kidneys", "Cleared by the kidneys, so doses are adjusted in kidney disease."], ["Gut", "Can cause diarrhea, nausea and C. difficile colitis."]], "journey": "Ceftaroline fosamil is given IV and converted to active ceftaroline in the plasma within minutes. It distributes into extracellular fluid and has low protein binding. It is mostly removed by the kidneys, with a half-life of about 2.5 hours, which allows dosing every 8 to 12 hours.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea, nausea and rash are typical beta-lactam effects from changes in gut flora and immune recognition. A positive Coombs test happens because the drug can coat red blood cells, and rarely this leads to hemolytic anemia. Long courses can cause neutropenia. Like other beta-lactams it can cause seizures at high levels. Anaphylaxis is rare and is treated with epinephrine. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Ceftaroline broke a long-standing rule that taught students no beta-lactam could treat MRSA.", "src": ["Teflaro (ceftaroline fosamil) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "ceftriaxone": {"origin": "Ceftriaxone is a third-generation cephalosporin, part of the family that grew out of the mold Giuseppe Brotzu found in Sardinian seawater in 1945. It was developed by Hoffmann-La Roche in the late 1970s and approved in the US in 1984 as Rocephin. Its long half-life allowed once-daily dosing, a big change for hospital and outpatient antibiotic therapy. It became one of the most widely used antibiotics in the world for meningitis, pneumonia, gonorrhea and serious infections in the emergency department.", "timeline": [["1945", "Giuseppe Brotzu discovers the cephalosporin-producing mold."], ["1978", "Roche patents ceftriaxone."], ["1984", "FDA approves ceftriaxone (Rocephin)."], ["2007", "FDA warns against giving ceftriaxone with calcium IV solutions in neonates."]], "cell": "Ceftriaxone binds penicillin-binding proteins in the bacterial cell membrane and blocks the final cross-linking of peptidoglycan. Without cross-links the wall weakens, autolysins break it down further, and the bacterium bursts. It is bactericidal. As a third-generation cephalosporin, it is stable against many common beta-lactamases and has strong activity against gram-negative organisms such as Neisseria, Haemophilus and E. coli, while keeping good activity against Streptococcus pneumoniae. It does not cover Pseudomonas, MRSA, enterococci or Listeria.", "body": [["Brain and meninges", "Crosses inflamed meninges and is a first-line drug for bacterial meningitis."], ["Lungs", "Treats community-acquired pneumonia, usually combined with a macrolide or doxycycline for atypical bacteria."], ["Reproductive tract", "A single IM injection is the recommended US treatment for gonorrhea."], ["Gallbladder", "About half is excreted in bile. It can form calcium-ceftriaxone sludge (pseudolithiasis) in the gallbladder."], ["Blood", "Rarely causes immune hemolytic anemia when antibodies form against drug-coated red cells."], ["Gut", "Can cause diarrhea and C. difficile colitis."]], "journey": "Ceftriaxone is given IV or IM. It is highly bound to albumin and has a long half-life of about 6 to 9 hours, which allows once-daily dosing. It reaches the cerebrospinal fluid well when the meninges are inflamed. It is not metabolized much and leaves both in bile and in urine, so it usually needs no dose change in kidney disease alone. In newborns, it can push bilirubin off albumin.", "sidefx": "Biliary sludge forms because high concentrations in bile combine with calcium into a precipitate. It usually clears after stopping the drug. In neonates, ceftriaxone displaces bilirubin from albumin, raising the risk of kernicterus, and it can form fatal calcium precipitates in the lungs and kidneys if given with IV calcium. Diarrhea and C. difficile come from killing gut flora. Hemolysis is an immune reaction. Allergic reactions and anaphylaxis are treated with epinephrine. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Ceftriaxone is often mixed with lidocaine for IM shots because the injection alone is quite painful.", "src": ["Rocephin (ceftriaxone) FDA label", "CDC Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "celecoxib": {"origin": "Aspirin and other NSAIDs block cyclooxygenase (COX), but they often damage the stomach. In the early 1990s scientists discovered a second form of the enzyme, COX-2, which appears mainly at sites of inflammation, while COX-1 protects the stomach and helps platelets work. This suggested a drug that blocks only COX-2 could relieve pain without ulcers. A team at G.D. Searle led by John Talley designed celecoxib, and the FDA approved it as Celebrex in December 1998, the first COX-2 inhibitor in the US. Concerns about heart attacks led to the withdrawal of rofecoxib (Vioxx) in 2004, but celecoxib stayed on the market with a boxed warning.", "timeline": [["1991", "Researchers identify COX-2, an inducible form of cyclooxygenase."], ["1998", "FDA approves celecoxib (Celebrex), the first COX-2 selective inhibitor."], ["2004", "Rofecoxib is withdrawn for cardiovascular risk. Celecoxib remains with warnings."], ["2016", "The PRECISION trial compares celecoxib with ibuprofen and naproxen for heart safety."]], "cell": "Cyclooxygenase turns arachidonic acid into prostaglandin H2, which other enzymes convert into prostaglandins and thromboxane. COX-1 is always present and makes protective prostaglandins in the stomach and thromboxane A2 in platelets. COX-2 is induced by cytokines during inflammation and makes prostaglandins that cause pain, fever and swelling. Celecoxib has a sulfonamide side group that fits a side pocket found in COX-2 but not COX-1, so it reversibly and selectively blocks COX-2. Less PGE2 means less inflammation and pain. Because it spares platelet COX-1, it does not prevent clotting. It also reduces vascular prostacyclin, which is partly COX-2 dependent, tilting the balance toward thromboxane.", "body": [["Joints and tissues", "Reduces inflammatory prostaglandins, easing pain and swelling in arthritis."], ["Stomach", "Causes fewer ulcers than nonselective NSAIDs because protective COX-1 prostaglandins are spared."], ["Heart and vessels", "Lowers prostacyclin while thromboxane continues, which can raise the risk of heart attack and stroke and can raise blood pressure."], ["Kidneys", "Blocks prostaglandins that maintain kidney blood flow and sodium excretion, causing fluid retention, edema and possible acute kidney injury."], ["Platelets", "Has no meaningful antiplatelet effect, so it does not replace aspirin for heart protection."], ["Skin", "As a sulfonamide, it can rarely cause serious skin reactions."]], "journey": "Celecoxib is taken by mouth and peaks in about three hours. It is highly bound to plasma proteins and distributes widely. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP2C9, so poor CYP2C9 metabolizers and patients on CYP2C9 inhibitors such as fluconazole have higher levels. Inactive metabolites leave in feces and urine. Its half-life is about 11 hours.", "sidefx": "The cardiovascular risk comes from reducing protective prostacyclin in blood vessels while platelet thromboxane goes unchecked, favoring clotting. Kidney effects, including edema, high blood potassium and acute kidney injury, come from blocking kidney prostaglandins, which is a COX-2 effect as well. GI bleeding is less common than with older NSAIDs but still possible. Sulfonamide allergy and serious skin reactions such as SJS are rare. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Celecoxib was designed using X-ray crystal structures of the COX enzymes, an early success of structure-based drug design.", "src": ["Celebrex (celecoxib) FDA label", "Nissen SE et al. PRECISION Trial, N Engl J Med 2016", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "cephalexin": {"origin": "Cephalexin traces back to the mold that Giuseppe Brotzu found near a sewage outlet in Sardinia in 1945 and to the work of Edward Abraham and Guy Newton at Oxford, who isolated the cephalosporin core in the 1950s. The first cephalosporins had to be injected. Eli Lilly developed cephalexin as an oral first-generation cephalosporin, and it was introduced in the late 1960s under the name Keflex. It became one of the most prescribed antibiotics for skin infections, strep throat and urinary tract infections.", "timeline": [["1945", "Giuseppe Brotzu discovers the cephalosporin-producing mold."], ["1950s", "Abraham and Newton isolate cephalosporin C at Oxford."], ["1960s", "Eli Lilly introduces oral cephalexin (Keflex)."]], "cell": "Cephalexin binds penicillin-binding proteins, the enzymes that cross-link peptidoglycan strands in the bacterial cell wall. Its beta-lactam ring looks like the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of the peptide side chain, so the enzyme binds it and becomes permanently acylated. Without cross-links, the wall cannot resist osmotic pressure and the bacterium bursts, so it is bactericidal. As a first-generation cephalosporin it works best against gram-positive cocci such as MSSA and streptococci, with modest coverage of E. coli, Klebsiella and Proteus. It does not cover MRSA, enterococci or Pseudomonas.", "body": [["Skin", "Treats cellulitis and impetigo caused by strep and MSSA."], ["Throat", "Treats strep throat, often in patients with mild non-severe penicillin allergy."], ["Urinary tract", "Reaches high levels in urine and treats simple urinary tract infections, including in pregnancy."], ["Bones", "Used for some bone infections as follow-up oral therapy."], ["Gut", "Can cause nausea and diarrhea and rarely C. difficile colitis."]], "journey": "Cephalexin is taken by mouth and absorbed almost completely from the small intestine. It peaks in about an hour and spreads into most tissues but does not enter the cerebrospinal fluid well. It is not metabolized and leaves unchanged in the urine, which is why urine levels are very high. Its half-life is about one hour, so it is given several times a day, and doses are adjusted in kidney disease.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea and C. difficile colitis come from disruption of normal gut bacteria. Rashes and allergic reactions come from immune recognition of the beta-lactam. Cephalexin shares a side chain with ampicillin and amoxicillin, so cross-reaction is more likely in people allergic to those drugs. Anaphylaxis is rare and is treated with epinephrine. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Cephalexin and amoxicillin have nearly identical R1 side chains, which matters more for allergy cross-reactivity than the shared beta-lactam ring.", "src": ["Keflex (cephalexin) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "chloramphenicol": {"origin": "Chloramphenicol came from soil. In 1947, researchers at Parke-Davis led by John Ehrlich isolated it from Streptomyces venezuelae, a bacterium grown from a soil sample collected in Venezuela. Paul Burkholder at Yale worked on the same organism. The drug was tested in a typhus outbreak in Bolivia and against typhoid fever, with striking results. In 1949, chemist Mildred Rebstock at Parke-Davis made it entirely by chemical synthesis, the first antibiotic made this way. It was sold as Chloromycetin and was widely used until reports of fatal aplastic anemia and gray baby syndrome in the 1950s limited its use.", "timeline": [["1947", "Chloramphenicol is isolated from Streptomyces venezuelae."], ["1949", "Mildred Rebstock completes the first total synthesis of an antibiotic, chloramphenicol."], ["1950s", "Reports link chloramphenicol to aplastic anemia."], ["1959", "Gray baby syndrome is described in newborns given chloramphenicol."]], "cell": "Chloramphenicol binds the 50S subunit of the bacterial ribosome at the peptidyltransferase center. It blocks the proper placement of the aminoacyl end of the incoming tRNA, so the ribosome cannot form a new peptide bond. Protein production stops. It is usually bacteriostatic but is bactericidal against Haemophilus influenzae, Neisseria meningitidis and Streptococcus pneumoniae. It also binds human mitochondrial ribosomes, which resemble bacterial ribosomes. This slows protein synthesis in mitochondria of rapidly dividing cells such as bone marrow precursors, which explains its dose-related marrow suppression.", "body": [["Infections", "Covers a broad spectrum: gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria, anaerobes, rickettsiae and typhoid."], ["Brain", "Penetrates the brain and cerebrospinal fluid very well, which made it useful for meningitis."], ["Bone marrow", "Causes dose-dependent anemia, low white cells and low platelets, and rarely irreversible aplastic anemia."], ["Newborns", "Infants cannot clear it, leading to gray baby syndrome with gray skin, low blood pressure and collapse."], ["Liver", "The liver inactivates it by glucuronidation, and it inhibits several CYP enzymes."]], "journey": "Chloramphenicol can be given IV or by mouth in some countries. It distributes widely and enters the brain, eye and cerebrospinal fluid well. It crosses the placenta and enters breast milk. The liver inactivates it by glucuronidation, and the glucuronide is removed by the kidneys. Its half-life is a few hours in adults. In newborns, low glucuronidation and immature kidneys let it build up to toxic levels.", "sidefx": "Dose-related marrow suppression comes from blocking mitochondrial ribosomes in red and white cell precursors, and it usually reverses when the drug is stopped. Aplastic anemia is rare, not dose related and often fatal, and its exact cause is unclear. Gray baby syndrome happens because newborns lack enough UDP-glucuronyl transferase, so the drug builds up and poisons mitochondria in heart and muscle. It also inhibits CYP enzymes and raises levels of warfarin and phenytoin. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Mildred Rebstock's 1949 synthesis of chloramphenicol made it the first antibiotic that could be made entirely in a chemistry lab.", "src": ["Chloramphenicol sodium succinate FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "chloroquine": {"origin": "For centuries the only malaria drug was quinine from the bark of the South American cinchona tree. In 1934 Hans Andersag at Bayer in Germany synthesized a new 4-aminoquinoline called Resochin, now known as chloroquine, but the company judged it too toxic. During World War II, Allied researchers studied a related German compound, and US scientists re-examined chloroquine and found it safe and very effective. It entered wide use in the late 1940s and became the backbone of global malaria control. Resistance in Plasmodium falciparum appeared around 1960 and spread widely, so today it is used mainly for sensitive malaria species and regions.", "timeline": [["1630s", "Cinchona bark from Peru becomes known in Europe as a fever remedy."], ["1934", "Hans Andersag at Bayer synthesizes chloroquine (Resochin)."], ["1940s", "US wartime research confirms chloroquine's safety and effectiveness."], ["c. 1960", "Chloroquine-resistant Plasmodium falciparum is reported in South America and Southeast Asia."]], "cell": "Inside red blood cells, the malaria parasite digests hemoglobin in its acidic food vacuole. This releases heme (ferriprotoporphyrin IX), which is toxic to the parasite. The parasite protects itself by polymerizing heme into an inert crystal called hemozoin. Chloroquine is a weak base that diffuses into the acidic vacuole, becomes protonated and is trapped there at high concentration. It binds heme and blocks its polymerization into hemozoin. Free heme builds up, damages membranes and kills the parasite. It acts on the blood stage only and does not kill dormant liver hypnozoites. Resistance comes from mutations in the PfCRT transporter, which pumps chloroquine out of the vacuole.", "body": [["Blood", "Kills malaria parasites inside red blood cells. In G6PD deficiency it can cause hemolysis."], ["Eyes", "Long-term use can damage the retina, producing a bull's eye maculopathy and permanent vision loss."], ["Heart", "Blocks potassium channels, prolonging the QT interval, and can cause cardiomyopathy with long use."], ["Gut", "Commonly causes nausea, abdominal pain and diarrhea."], ["Skin", "Can cause itching, especially in people with darker skin, and pigment changes."], ["Brain", "Can cause headache, psychiatric symptoms and, in overdose, seizures."]], "journey": "Chloroquine is taken by mouth and absorbed almost completely. It spreads into tissues to a huge degree, binding melanin in the eye and skin and building up in the liver, spleen and kidneys. Because of this tissue storage, its terminal half-life is one to two months. The liver partly converts it by CYP2C8 and CYP3A4 into desethylchloroquine, which is also active. Much of the drug leaves unchanged in the urine. It crosses the placenta.", "sidefx": "Retinal damage comes from binding to melanin in the retinal pigment epithelium over long periods. QT prolongation and torsades come from blocking cardiac potassium channels. Overdose is very dangerous: it can cause seizures, low potassium, wide QRS complexes from sodium channel block and cardiovascular collapse within hours. Overdose is treated with high-dose diazepam, epinephrine and supportive care. Hemolysis in G6PD deficiency reflects oxidant stress on red cells.", "fun": "Chloroquine's bitter cousin quinine is the reason tonic water tastes bitter, a legacy of British colonial efforts to prevent malaria.", "src": ["Chloroquine phosphate FDA label", "CDC Malaria Treatment Guidelines", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "chlorpromazine": {"origin": "Chlorpromazine started a revolution in psychiatry. In December 1950, chemist Paul Charpentier at the French company Rhone-Poulenc synthesized it while working on phenothiazine antihistamines. The surgeon Henri Laborit used it to calm patients before surgery and noticed a striking indifference to stress. In 1952 the psychiatrists Jean Delay and Pierre Deniker in Paris gave it to patients with psychosis and saw dramatic improvement. It was approved in the US in 1954 as Thorazine. It was the first effective antipsychotic, and it helped empty many large psychiatric hospitals in the decades that followed.", "timeline": [["1950", "Paul Charpentier synthesizes chlorpromazine at Rhone-Poulenc."], ["1951", "Henri Laborit uses it in surgical anesthesia."], ["1952", "Jean Delay and Pierre Deniker report its antipsychotic effect."], ["1954", "FDA approves chlorpromazine (Thorazine)."]], "cell": "Chlorpromazine is a low-potency typical antipsychotic. Its main action is blocking dopamine D2 receptors. In the mesolimbic pathway, D2 blockade reduces the overactive dopamine signaling linked to hallucinations and delusions. In the chemoreceptor trigger zone, D2 blockade stops nausea and vomiting. It also blocks histamine H1 receptors (sedation, weight gain), muscarinic receptors (dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention) and alpha-1 adrenergic receptors (vasodilation and orthostatic hypotension). Blocking D2 in the nigrostriatal pathway causes movement side effects, and blocking D2 in the pituitary raises prolactin.", "body": [["Brain", "Reduces psychosis and agitation and causes strong sedation. It can lower the seizure threshold."], ["Movement system", "Can cause dystonia, parkinsonism, akathisia and later tardive dyskinesia from nigrostriatal D2 blockade."], ["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 blockade causes orthostatic hypotension and dizziness."], ["Gut and vomiting center", "Strong antiemetic effect. Anticholinergic effects slow the gut and cause constipation."], ["Hormones", "Raises prolactin, which can cause breast enlargement, milk production and missed periods."], ["Eyes and skin", "Can cause photosensitivity, blue-gray skin pigment and deposits in the lens and cornea with long use."], ["Body temperature", "Impairs temperature control and can contribute to heat illness."]], "journey": "Chlorpromazine can be given by mouth, IM or IV. Oral absorption is variable and first-pass metabolism is large. It is very fat soluble, highly protein bound and spreads widely, including into the brain and across the placenta. The liver breaks it into many metabolites, some active, mainly via CYP2D6. Metabolites leave in urine and bile. Its long half-life of about a day allows once or twice daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Each side effect matches a receptor it blocks. Sedation comes from H1 blockade, orthostatic hypotension from alpha-1 blockade, and dry mouth and constipation from muscarinic blockade. Extrapyramidal effects come from D2 blockade in the basal ganglia and are treated with benztropine or diphenhydramine. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome, with fever, rigidity and high CK, is a dangerous reaction to D2 blockade treated with cooling, dantrolene or bromocriptine. QT prolongation and rare agranulocytosis also occur.", "fun": "Chlorpromazine's success launched modern psychopharmacology and the idea that dopamine plays a central role in psychosis.", "src": ["Chlorpromazine hydrochloride FDA label", "Delay J, Deniker P. 1952 reports", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "chlorthalidone": {"origin": "In the late 1950s the thiazide diuretics, starting with chlorothiazide from Merck, transformed blood pressure care. Chlorthalidone was developed soon after by the Swiss company Geigy and approved in the US around 1960. It is not a true thiazide in structure, but it acts at the same site in the kidney, so it is called thiazide-like. Its key feature is a very long half-life. In 2002 the large ALLHAT trial used chlorthalidone and found it at least as good as newer drugs at preventing heart events, which made it a preferred diuretic in many hypertension guidelines.", "timeline": [["1958", "Chlorothiazide, the first thiazide diuretic, is introduced."], ["1960", "Chlorthalidone is approved in the US."], ["2002", "The ALLHAT trial supports chlorthalidone as first-line therapy for hypertension."]], "cell": "In the distal convoluted tubule, the sodium-chloride cotransporter (NCC) reabsorbs sodium and chloride from the urine. Chlorthalidone reaches the tubule by secretion through organic anion transporters in the proximal tubule and binds NCC from the urine side, blocking it. More sodium and water stay in the urine. Extra sodium reaching the collecting duct is exchanged for potassium and hydrogen, which causes low potassium and alkalosis. In the DCT, less sodium inside the cell increases calcium reabsorption, so urinary calcium falls. Over weeks, blood volume normalizes but peripheral resistance stays lower, sustaining the blood pressure effect.", "body": [["Kidneys", "Increases sodium and water loss. Lowers urine calcium, which helps prevent calcium kidney stones."], ["Blood vessels", "Lowers peripheral vascular resistance over time, which keeps blood pressure down."], ["Blood chemistry", "Can cause low potassium, low sodium, low magnesium, high calcium and high uric acid."], ["Joints", "Higher uric acid from competition for tubular secretion can trigger gout attacks."], ["Metabolism", "Can raise blood sugar and lipids, partly because low potassium reduces insulin release."], ["Bones", "Calcium retention may modestly protect bone density."]], "journey": "Chlorthalidone is taken by mouth and absorbed slowly. It binds tightly to carbonic anhydrase inside red blood cells, which acts as a reservoir. This gives it a very long half-life of about two days and an effect that lasts more than 24 hours. It is mostly removed unchanged by the kidneys. Because it must reach the tubule to work, it loses effect when kidney function is very poor.", "sidefx": "Low potassium and metabolic alkalosis come from extra sodium delivery to the collecting duct, where sodium is traded for potassium and hydrogen. Low potassium can cause weakness and arrhythmias, especially with digoxin. Low sodium happens because the drug impairs urine dilution, and it is more common in older women. High uric acid and gout come from competition with urate for secretion. High calcium comes from increased calcium reabsorption. Its long action makes electrolyte problems more frequent than with hydrochlorothiazide. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Chlorthalidone is about twice as potent as hydrochlorothiazide and lasts much longer, which is why many experts argue it controls blood pressure better through the night.", "src": ["Thalitone (chlorthalidone) FDA label", "ALLHAT Collaborative Research Group, JAMA 2002", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "cholestyramine": {"origin": "Cholestyramine is a plastic-like resin, a relative of the ion-exchange resins used to soften water. Researchers in the 1950s and 1960s found that such resins could grab bile acids in the gut. Because the liver makes bile acids from cholesterol, removing them forces the liver to use up cholesterol. Cholestyramine became one of the first cholesterol-lowering drugs. In 1984 the Lipid Research Clinics Coronary Primary Prevention Trial showed that lowering cholesterol with cholestyramine reduced heart attacks, one of the first major proofs that lowering cholesterol prevents coronary disease.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Cholestyramine, a bile acid-binding resin, enters medical use."], ["1984", "The Lipid Research Clinics trial shows cholestyramine lowers coronary events."], ["1987", "Statins arrive and become the main cholesterol-lowering drugs."]], "cell": "Bile acids made by the liver are secreted into the gut to help absorb fats, and most are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum and return to the liver (enterohepatic circulation). Cholestyramine is a large, positively charged resin that is not absorbed. It trades chloride ions for negatively charged bile acids and holds them in the gut so they leave in the stool. The liver senses fewer returning bile acids and increases cholesterol 7-alpha-hydroxylase, which converts cholesterol into new bile acids. Liver cholesterol falls, so the liver makes more LDL receptors and pulls more LDL out of the blood. HMG-CoA reductase and VLDL production also increase, which can raise triglycerides.", "body": [["Blood lipids", "Lowers LDL cholesterol by increasing LDL receptor uptake in the liver. Can raise triglycerides."], ["Gut", "Commonly causes constipation, bloating and gas because the resin stays in the bowel."], ["Liver and gallbladder", "Increases bile acid production. Lower bile acid in bile can favor cholesterol gallstones."], ["Skin", "Relieves itching from partial biliary obstruction by removing bile acids."], ["Vitamins", "Binds fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K, which can cause deficiency and bleeding from low vitamin K."], ["Drug absorption", "Binds many drugs in the gut, such as warfarin, digoxin, levothyroxine and thiazides."]], "journey": "Cholestyramine is a powder mixed with liquid and taken by mouth. It is not absorbed at all and works only in the gut. It leaves in the feces with the bound bile acids. LDL starts to fall within a week or two, with full effect in a few weeks. Because it binds other drugs, they should be taken at least an hour before or several hours after it.", "sidefx": "Constipation, bloating and gas come from the resin itself sitting in the bowel. Fat-soluble vitamin deficiency comes from reduced fat absorption when bile acids are removed. Rising triglycerides come from increased VLDL production by the liver, so it is avoided when triglycerides are very high. Because chloride is released as bile acids are bound, it can rarely cause hyperchloremic acidosis, especially in children or kidney disease. It also speeds removal of some drugs and toxins, a trick used in leflunomide washout. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Because it breaks the bile acid loop, cholestyramine is used to speed removal of leflunomide and to treat diarrhea caused by bile acids after ileal resection.", "src": ["Questran (cholestyramine) FDA label", "Lipid Research Clinics Program, JAMA 1984", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "cilostazol": {"origin": "Cilostazol was developed by Otsuka Pharmaceutical in Japan and was first approved there in 1988. It was designed to block phosphodiesterase type 3, an enzyme that breaks down cAMP in platelets and blood vessels. In the US it was approved in 1999 as Pletal for intermittent claudication, the leg pain with walking caused by peripheral artery disease. Because another PDE3 inhibitor, milrinone, had increased deaths when given by mouth in heart failure, cilostazol carries a boxed warning against use in heart failure.", "timeline": [["1988", "Cilostazol is approved in Japan."], ["1991", "The PROMISE trial shows oral milrinone, another PDE3 inhibitor, increases deaths in heart failure."], ["1999", "FDA approves cilostazol (Pletal) for intermittent claudication."]], "cell": "Cilostazol selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase 3A (PDE3A). PDE3 normally breaks down cAMP. With PDE3 blocked, cAMP builds up. In platelets, high cAMP activates protein kinase A, which lowers intracellular calcium and blocks the shape change, granule release and aggregation that form clots. This antiplatelet effect is reversible. In vascular smooth muscle, more cAMP activates PKA, which inhibits myosin light chain kinase and relaxes the muscle, causing arterial vasodilation, especially in leg arteries. It also modestly raises HDL and lowers triglycerides.", "body": [["Legs", "Over weeks it increases pain-free walking distance in people with claudication."], ["Platelets", "Reduces platelet aggregation, which adds to bleeding risk with other antiplatelets."], ["Blood vessels", "Dilates arteries, which can cause headache and flushing."], ["Heart", "Raises cAMP in heart cells too, causing palpitations and fast heart rate. It is avoided in heart failure."], ["Gut", "Commonly causes diarrhea and loose stools."], ["Blood lipids", "Modestly raises HDL and lowers triglycerides."]], "journey": "Cilostazol is taken by mouth, usually on an empty stomach, because high-fat meals increase absorption. It is highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A4, with help from CYP2C19, into active metabolites. Drugs that inhibit these enzymes, such as some azoles, macrolides and omeprazole, raise its levels. Metabolites leave mostly in the urine. Its half-life is about 11 to 13 hours, and benefit for walking takes weeks to build.", "sidefx": "Headache is the most common side effect and comes from vasodilation of cranial vessels. Palpitations and tachycardia come from cAMP effects in the heart and from reflex response to vasodilation. The heart failure warning exists because chronic PDE3 inhibition with oral milrinone increased arrhythmic deaths in heart failure patients, and the same risk is assumed for cilostazol. Bleeding risk comes from its antiplatelet effect. Rare agranulocytosis and thrombocytopenia have been reported. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Cilostazol and milrinone share the same target, PDE3, but one is a pill for leg pain while the other is an IV inotrope in the ICU.", "src": ["Pletal (cilostazol) FDA label", "Packer M et al. PROMISE Trial, N Engl J Med 1991", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "ciprofloxacin": {"origin": "The quinolones began by accident. In 1962, George Lesher and colleagues at Sterling Drug found that a byproduct of making the antimalarial chloroquine had antibacterial activity. This became nalidixic acid, used for urinary infections. Adding a fluorine atom and a piperazine ring later created the fluoroquinolones, with much broader activity. Ciprofloxacin was synthesized at Bayer in the early 1980s and approved in the US in 1987. It became famous during the 2001 anthrax letter attacks. In 2008 the FDA added a boxed warning for tendon rupture, and later warnings covered nerve damage, aortic aneurysm and other serious effects.", "timeline": [["1962", "Nalidixic acid, the first quinolone, is discovered as a byproduct of chloroquine synthesis."], ["1981", "Bayer chemists synthesize ciprofloxacin."], ["1987", "FDA approves ciprofloxacin (Cipro)."], ["2001", "Cipro is widely used after the US anthrax letter attacks."], ["2008", "FDA adds a boxed warning for tendinitis and tendon rupture."]], "cell": "Bacteria must constantly cut and reseal their DNA to relieve twisting during copying. DNA gyrase (topoisomerase II) adds negative supercoils and topoisomerase IV separates daughter chromosomes. Ciprofloxacin binds the complex of these enzymes with DNA after the DNA has been cut, and it prevents resealing. The trapped complexes create double-strand breaks, stop DNA replication and trigger cell death, so it is bactericidal and concentration dependent. In gram-negative bacteria gyrase is the main target, and in gram-positive bacteria topoisomerase IV is. Resistance comes from mutations in these enzymes and from efflux pumps.", "body": [["Urinary tract", "Reaches high levels in urine and treats complicated urinary tract infections and prostatitis."], ["Gut", "Treats some infectious diarrhea and is used with other drugs in intra-abdominal infections. Can cause nausea and C. difficile."], ["Tendons", "Can cause tendinitis and rupture, especially of the Achilles tendon, more often in older adults or with steroids."], ["Heart", "Can prolong the QT interval and contribute to torsades de pointes."], ["Brain and nerves", "Can cause dizziness, confusion, seizures and peripheral neuropathy that may be permanent."], ["Blood vessels", "Linked to a higher risk of aortic aneurysm and dissection."], ["Blood sugar", "Can cause dangerous high or low blood sugar, especially with diabetes drugs."]], "journey": "Ciprofloxacin is well absorbed by mouth and can also be given IV. Calcium, magnesium, aluminum, iron and zinc bind it in the gut into chelates, sharply lowering absorption, so it must be separated from antacids, supplements and dairy. It spreads widely into tissues, including the prostate, bone and lung. Part is metabolized in the liver, and it inhibits CYP1A2, raising levels of theophylline and tizanidine. Most leaves in the urine. Its half-life is about 4 hours.", "sidefx": "Tendon damage is thought to come from toxic effects on tendon cells and the collagen matrix, partly through chelation of magnesium. The risk rises with age, steroids and transplant status. QT prolongation comes from blocking cardiac hERG potassium channels. Seizures and confusion reflect GABA-A receptor antagonism in the brain. Peripheral neuropathy and aortic effects are thought to involve damage to connective tissue. Blood sugar swings relate to effects on pancreatic potassium channels and insulin release. It is avoided in children and in pregnancy when alternatives exist because of cartilage damage in young animals. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The quinolone class grew from an impurity found while making chloroquine, a malaria drug.", "src": ["Cipro (ciprofloxacin) FDA label", "FDA Drug Safety Communications on fluoroquinolones", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "cisatracurium": {"origin": "Muscle relaxants trace back to curare, the arrow poison of South American hunters. Atracurium was designed in the 1970s by John Stenlake and colleagues at the University of Strathclyde in Scotland, with Wellcome, to break down on its own in the blood by a chemical reaction called Hofmann elimination, so it would not depend on the liver or kidneys. Atracurium is a mix of ten stereoisomers. Cisatracurium is one of them, the 1R-cis, 1'R-cis isomer, chosen because it is more potent and releases much less histamine. It was approved in the US in 1995 as Nimbex and became a preferred paralytic in the ICU, especially for patients with organ failure.", "timeline": [["1500s", "European explorers describe curare arrow poisons in South America."], ["1942", "Curare is first used in surgery to relax muscles."], ["1970s", "Atracurium is designed to break down by Hofmann elimination."], ["1995", "FDA approves cisatracurium (Nimbex)."]], "cell": "At the neuromuscular junction, acetylcholine released from the nerve binds nicotinic acetylcholine receptors on the muscle end plate. These receptors are ion channels, and when open they let sodium in and depolarize the muscle. Cisatracurium is a nondepolarizing neuromuscular blocker. It competes with acetylcholine for the alpha subunits of the nicotinic receptor without opening the channel, so the end plate cannot depolarize and the muscle stays limp. Because the block is competitive, raising acetylcholine with a cholinesterase inhibitor such as neostigmine can reverse it. It breaks down spontaneously at body pH and temperature through Hofmann elimination into laudanosine and a monoquaternary acrylate.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Produces flaccid paralysis of all skeletal muscles, including the diaphragm, so breathing must be supported."], ["Lungs", "Used in severe ARDS to improve ventilator synchrony, since the patient cannot breathe against the ventilator."], ["Brain", "Has no sedative or pain-relieving effect. Patients must receive sedation and analgesia to avoid awareness."], ["Heart and vessels", "Releases very little histamine, so it rarely causes low blood pressure or flushing."], ["Liver and kidneys", "Breakdown does not depend on these organs, which makes it a good choice in organ failure."]], "journey": "Cisatracurium is given IV as a bolus or infusion. It distributes in the extracellular fluid and does not cross the blood-brain barrier or placenta to a significant degree because it is a charged quaternary compound. It is broken down mainly by Hofmann elimination, a reaction that depends on pH and temperature, and partly by ester hydrolysis. Onset takes a few minutes, and effects last about 35 to 45 minutes after a bolus. Acidosis and low body temperature slow its breakdown.", "sidefx": "Paralysis without sedation can cause awareness, so sedation is required. Prolonged weakness after long ICU use, called ICU-acquired weakness, can occur, especially with steroids. Laudanosine, a breakdown product, can cause seizures in animals at high levels, but cisatracurium produces much less of it than atracurium. Anaphylaxis is rare. Its effect is reversed with neostigmine plus glycopyrrolate. Sugammadex does not reverse it because sugammadex only binds steroid-type blockers such as rocuronium.", "fun": "Hofmann elimination, the reaction that breaks down cisatracurium, was described in the 1800s by the German chemist August Wilhelm von Hofmann.", "src": ["Nimbex (cisatracurium) FDA label", "Miller's Anesthesia", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "cisplatin": {"origin": "Cisplatin was first made in 1845 by the Italian chemist Michele Peyrone, but nobody knew it fought cancer. In 1965 Barnett Rosenberg, a biophysicist at Michigan State University, was passing an electric current through E. coli using platinum electrodes. The bacteria stopped dividing and grew into long filaments. He found that platinum compounds formed in the solution were the cause and then showed they could shrink tumors in mice. The FDA approved cisplatin in 1978. It turned metastatic testicular cancer into a largely curable disease and became a core drug for many solid tumors.", "timeline": [["1845", "Michele Peyrone first synthesizes the compound later called cisplatin."], ["1965", "Barnett Rosenberg finds platinum compounds stop bacterial cell division."], ["1969", "Rosenberg reports platinum compounds shrink tumors in mice."], ["1978", "FDA approves cisplatin."]], "cell": "Cisplatin enters cells partly through the copper transporter CTR1. Inside the cell, where chloride levels are low, its chloride ligands are replaced by water (aquation). The activated platinum binds the N7 position of guanine bases in DNA. It mostly forms intrastrand crosslinks between neighboring guanines, plus some interstrand crosslinks. These crosslinks kink the DNA, block replication and transcription, and are recognized by damage-sensing proteins that trigger p53 and apoptosis. It acts in all phases of the cell cycle. Repair by nucleotide excision repair and efflux contribute to resistance.", "body": [["Tumors", "Kills cancer cells in testicular, ovarian, bladder, lung, head and neck and other cancers."], ["Kidneys", "Damages the proximal tubules, causing acute kidney injury and wasting of magnesium and potassium."], ["Ears", "Damages cochlear hair cells, causing ringing and permanent high-frequency hearing loss."], ["Gut and brain", "Causes some of the most severe nausea and vomiting of any chemotherapy, both acute and delayed."], ["Nerves", "Causes a mainly sensory peripheral neuropathy with numbness and loss of position sense."], ["Bone marrow", "Causes mild to moderate myelosuppression and anemia."]], "journey": "Cisplatin is given IV. In the blood, much of it binds plasma proteins, and protein-bound platinum is inactive. Free drug distributes to tissues, with high levels in the kidney, liver and intestine. It does not cross the blood-brain barrier well. It is not metabolized by enzymes but is activated by aquation inside cells. It is removed mainly by the kidneys. Free drug clears in hours, but platinum can be found in tissues for months or years.", "sidefx": "Kidney damage happens because the proximal tubule takes up cisplatin and is injured by it. Aggressive IV hydration, sometimes with magnesium, reduces this. Hearing loss comes from damage to cochlear outer hair cells. Severe vomiting comes from serotonin release by gut cells and substance P in the brain, so 5-HT3 and NK1 antagonists are given. Neuropathy comes from damage to dorsal root ganglion neurons. Amifostine can protect the kidneys, and sodium thiosulfate is approved to reduce hearing loss in children.", "fun": "Cisplatin was discovered because an experiment on how electric fields affect bacteria accidentally released platinum from the electrodes.", "src": ["Cisplatin injection FDA label", "Rosenberg B et al. Nature 1965 and 1969", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "citalopram": {"origin": "Citalopram was synthesized in 1972 by chemists at the Danish company Lundbeck as part of a search for a highly selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor. It was launched in Denmark in 1989 and approved in the US in 1998 as Celexa, marketed by Forest Laboratories. Citalopram is a mix of two mirror-image forms. Lundbeck later developed the S-form alone as escitalopram (Lexapro), approved in the US in 2002. In 2011 the FDA warned that citalopram can prolong the QT interval at higher doses.", "timeline": [["1972", "Lundbeck chemists synthesize citalopram."], ["1989", "Citalopram is launched in Denmark."], ["1998", "FDA approves citalopram (Celexa)."], ["2002", "Its S-enantiomer, escitalopram, is approved in the US."], ["2011", "FDA warns about dose-related QT prolongation."]], "cell": "After serotonin is released into the synapse, the serotonin transporter (SERT) pumps it back into the presynaptic neuron. Citalopram binds SERT and blocks this reuptake, so serotonin stays in the synapse longer. At first, extra serotonin activates 5-HT1A autoreceptors on the raphe neurons, which slows their firing. Over weeks, these autoreceptors desensitize, firing returns, and serotonin release increases in the cortex and limbic areas. Downstream changes in gene expression, including increased BDNF and neuroplasticity, are thought to explain the mood benefit. It is among the most selective SSRIs and has little effect on norepinephrine or dopamine reuptake.", "body": [["Brain", "Improves depression and anxiety over several weeks. Early on it can increase anxiety or restlessness."], ["Heart", "Can prolong the QT interval in a dose-related way, raising the risk of torsades."], ["Gut", "Serotonin receptors in the gut cause nausea and loose stools, especially early in therapy."], ["Sexual function", "Commonly reduces libido and delays orgasm through serotonin effects in the spinal cord and brain."], ["Blood", "Lowers platelet serotonin, which impairs clotting and raises bleeding risk with NSAIDs or anticoagulants."], ["Kidneys and sodium", "Can cause SIADH and low sodium, especially in older adults."]], "journey": "Citalopram is taken by mouth and absorbed well, peaking in about four hours. It crosses the blood-brain barrier and the placenta and enters breast milk. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP2C19 and CYP3A4, so poor CYP2C19 metabolizers and those on drugs such as omeprazole have higher levels. Metabolites leave in the urine. Its half-life is about 35 hours, so it is given once daily. Mood benefit takes several weeks.", "sidefx": "Nausea, sweating and sexual problems come from serotonin acting at 5-HT2 and 5-HT3 receptors outside the mood circuits. QT prolongation comes from blocking cardiac potassium channels and is greater at high doses and in poor CYP2C19 metabolizers. Low sodium comes from increased ADH release. Bleeding risk comes from platelets losing serotonin. With other serotonergic drugs, it can cause serotonin syndrome, treated with stopping the drugs, benzodiazepines and cyproheptadine. Like other antidepressants it carries a boxed warning about suicidal thoughts in young people. Stopping suddenly can cause withdrawal symptoms.", "fun": "Escitalopram is the active half of citalopram, an example of a company patenting a single mirror-image form of its own drug.", "src": ["Celexa (citalopram) FDA label", "FDA Drug Safety Communication on citalopram, 2011", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "clarithromycin": {"origin": "Clarithromycin is a chemical improvement on erythromycin. Erythromycin was found in 1952 in Saccharopolyspora erythraea, a soil bacterium from the Philippines, and was developed by Eli Lilly. It works well but breaks down in stomach acid and causes stomach cramps. In the early 1980s chemists at Taisho Pharmaceutical in Japan added a methyl group at the 6-hydroxyl position, making 6-O-methylerythromycin. This change makes it stable in acid, better absorbed and longer lasting. It was approved in the US in 1991 as Biaxin, marketed by Abbott. It became a key part of treatment for H. pylori ulcers and for Mycobacterium avium complex in people with HIV.", "timeline": [["1952", "Erythromycin is discovered in a soil bacterium from the Philippines."], ["1980", "Taisho Pharmaceutical develops clarithromycin."], ["1991", "FDA approves clarithromycin (Biaxin)."], ["1990s", "Clarithromycin becomes a standard part of H. pylori treatment."]], "cell": "Clarithromycin binds the 23S ribosomal RNA in the 50S subunit of the bacterial ribosome, inside the exit tunnel where the new protein chain leaves. This blocks translocation, so the ribosome cannot move along the mRNA and the protein chain stops growing. It is usually bacteriostatic. Resistance comes from methylation of the 23S rRNA by erm genes, which prevents binding, or from efflux pumps (mef). It is also a strong inhibitor of CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein, which drives many drug interactions.", "body": [["Lungs and sinuses", "Treats pneumonia, sinusitis and bronchitis, including atypical organisms such as Mycoplasma and Chlamydophila."], ["Stomach", "Kills H. pylori as part of combination therapy, helping ulcers heal and preventing relapse."], ["Immune-compromised hosts", "Treats and prevents Mycobacterium avium complex in advanced HIV."], ["Gut", "Can cause nausea, diarrhea and a metallic or bitter taste. Less cramping than erythromycin."], ["Heart", "Prolongs the QT interval and can cause torsades, especially with other QT drugs or CYP3A4 substrates."], ["Liver", "Can cause hepatitis. It sharply raises levels of drugs cleared by CYP3A4."]], "journey": "Clarithromycin is taken by mouth and is stable in stomach acid. It is converted in the liver into 14-hydroxyclarithromycin, an active metabolite that works especially well against H. influenzae. It reaches high levels in lung tissue and white blood cells. It is cleared by both liver metabolism and the kidneys. Its half-life is about 3 to 7 hours and gets longer at higher doses.", "sidefx": "QT prolongation comes from blocking cardiac hERG potassium channels. Strong CYP3A4 inhibition can raise levels of statins (rhabdomyolysis), colchicine (fatal toxicity), calcium channel blockers (hypotension) and others. Nausea and taste changes come from direct effects in the gut. Like most antibiotics it can cause C. difficile. A large trial suggested a long-term increase in cardiac deaths in patients with heart disease, and the FDA added a warning in 2018. It is generally avoided in pregnancy. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Clarithromycin's power as a CYP3A4 inhibitor is so strong that it is a standard test drug for studying CYP3A4 interactions.", "src": ["Biaxin (clarithromycin) FDA label", "FDA Drug Safety Communication on clarithromycin, 2018", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "clevidipine": {"origin": "Clevidipine was designed for a very specific job: lowering blood pressure in minutes and wearing off in minutes. It was developed by the Swedish company Astra (later AstraZeneca) as a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker with an ester group that blood enzymes cut quickly. Because it does not dissolve in water, it is made as a milky lipid emulsion similar to propofol. The Medicines Company brought it to the US market, and the FDA approved it as Cleviprex in 2008. It is used in hypertensive emergencies, perioperative hypertension and stroke care, where tight, fast blood pressure control matters.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers are first developed."], ["1990s", "Astra develops clevidipine as an ultra-short-acting IV agent."], ["2008", "FDA approves clevidipine (Cleviprex)."]], "cell": "In arterial smooth muscle, depolarization opens L-type voltage-gated calcium channels. Calcium enters, binds calmodulin and activates myosin light chain kinase, which drives contraction. Clevidipine is a dihydropyridine that binds the alpha-1 subunit of L-type channels and holds them in the inactivated state. Less calcium enters, so arterioles relax and systemic vascular resistance falls. Dihydropyridines are much more selective for vascular smooth muscle than for heart muscle, so clevidipine has little effect on venous tone, heart contractility or the AV node. Lower afterload lowers blood pressure, often with a reflex rise in heart rate.", "body": [["Arteries", "Relaxes arterial smooth muscle and lowers systemic vascular resistance within minutes."], ["Heart", "Does not slow the heart. Reflex tachycardia can occur, so a beta blocker is needed first in aortic dissection."], ["Brain", "Allows careful blood pressure lowering in stroke and brain bleeds. Headache can occur from vasodilation."], ["Metabolism", "The lipid emulsion adds fat calories and can raise triglycerides with long infusions."], ["Immune system", "Contains soy and egg components, so it is avoided in people allergic to them."]], "journey": "Clevidipine is given only as a continuous IV infusion. Blood pressure begins to fall within a few minutes. Esterases in the blood and tissues rapidly break its ester bond into inactive metabolites, so it does not depend on the liver or kidneys. Its initial half-life is about one minute, and blood pressure returns toward baseline within about 5 to 15 minutes after stopping. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. Lipid emulsions support bacterial growth, so strict sterile technique is needed.", "sidefx": "Hypotension comes from too much arterial dilation and is fixed quickly by stopping or slowing the infusion because the drug clears fast. Reflex tachycardia comes from the baroreceptor response to falling pressure. Headache and flushing come from vasodilation. Because it is a lipid emulsion, it is avoided in disorders of lipid metabolism and severe hypertriglyceridemia. It is also avoided in severe aortic stenosis, where lowering afterload can drop coronary perfusion. Calcium can partly counter an overdose of calcium channel blockers.", "fun": "Clevidipine's nickname is the milky BP drip because its lipid emulsion looks just like propofol.", "src": ["Cleviprex (clevidipine) FDA label", "AHA/ACC Hypertension Guideline", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "clindamycin": {"origin": "Clindamycin comes from a soil microbe. In the early 1960s scientists at Upjohn isolated lincomycin from Streptomyces lincolnensis, a bacterium found in soil near Lincoln, Nebraska, which gave the drug its name. In 1966 Upjohn chemist Barney Magerlein replaced a hydroxyl group on lincomycin with a chlorine atom. The new drug, clindamycin, was better absorbed and more potent. It was sold as Cleocin and became important for anaerobic infections, skin infections and patients with penicillin allergy. It also became the classic example of an antibiotic linked to C. difficile colitis.", "timeline": [["1962", "Lincomycin is isolated from Streptomyces lincolnensis from Nebraska soil."], ["1966", "Barney Magerlein at Upjohn creates clindamycin from lincomycin."], ["1970s", "Clindamycin use is linked to pseudomembranous colitis, later traced to C. difficile."]], "cell": "Clindamycin is a lincosamide. It binds the 23S rRNA of the 50S ribosomal subunit, near the peptidyltransferase center and overlapping the macrolide binding site. It blocks peptide bond formation and early translocation, stopping bacterial protein synthesis. It is usually bacteriostatic. Because it stops making new protein, it quickly shuts down the production of bacterial toxins such as streptococcal and staphylococcal toxins, which is why it is added in toxic shock and necrotizing fasciitis. Resistance comes from erm methylation of the ribosome, which also confers macrolide resistance. Some staph show inducible resistance, detected by the D-test.", "body": [["Skin and soft tissue", "Treats cellulitis and abscesses, including some community MRSA."], ["Toxin-mediated infections", "Suppresses toxin production in toxic shock syndrome and necrotizing fasciitis."], ["Mouth and lungs", "Covers anaerobes in dental infections, lung abscess and aspiration pneumonia."], ["Bones", "Penetrates bone well and is used in some bone infections."], ["Gut", "Strongly disrupts normal flora, so C. difficile colitis is a major risk."], ["Skin (topical)", "Topical forms are used for acne and bacterial vaginosis."]], "journey": "Clindamycin is well absorbed by mouth and can also be given IV, IM, topically or vaginally. It spreads widely into tissues, including bone and abscesses, but does not enter the cerebrospinal fluid well. The liver breaks it down, and metabolites leave mainly through bile with some in urine. Its half-life is about two to three hours.", "sidefx": "C. difficile colitis is the key toxicity. Clindamycin wipes out many normal anaerobic gut bacteria but does not kill C. difficile, so the organism overgrows and makes toxins that damage the colon. Treatment is stopping clindamycin and giving oral vancomycin or fidaxomicin. Diarrhea, nausea and a metallic taste are common. Rash and rare serious skin reactions can occur. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Clindamycin and lincomycin are named after Lincoln, Nebraska, where the original soil sample was collected.", "src": ["Cleocin (clindamycin) FDA label", "IDSA guidance on C. difficile infection", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "clomiphene": {"origin": "Clomiphene was first synthesized in 1956 by chemists at the William S. Merrell Company in Cincinnati, during work on compounds related to the estrogen drug chlorotrianisene. In 1961 Robert Greenblatt and colleagues reported that it induced ovulation in women who did not ovulate. The FDA approved it in 1967 as Clomid. It became the first widely used oral fertility drug and remains a standard first-line treatment for ovulation induction, especially in polycystic ovary syndrome, along with letrozole.", "timeline": [["1956", "Clomiphene is synthesized at the William S. Merrell Company."], ["1961", "Robert Greenblatt reports clomiphene induces ovulation."], ["1967", "FDA approves clomiphene (Clomid)."]], "cell": "Clomiphene is a selective estrogen receptor modulator. It is a mixture of two isomers, enclomiphene and zuclomiphene. In the hypothalamus it binds estrogen receptors and blocks them for a long time, so the brain no longer senses circulating estrogen. Normally estrogen provides negative feedback that limits GnRH. With that feedback blocked, the hypothalamus increases GnRH pulses, and the pituitary releases more FSH and LH. More FSH drives follicle growth in the ovary, and an LH surge then triggers ovulation. In the endometrium and cervix it acts as an antagonist, which can thin the endometrium and thicken cervical mucus.", "body": [["Hypothalamus and pituitary", "Blocks estrogen feedback, raising GnRH, FSH and LH."], ["Ovaries", "Stimulates follicles to grow and ovulate. Can enlarge the ovaries and cause cysts."], ["Uterus and cervix", "Antiestrogen effects can thin the endometrial lining and make cervical mucus less friendly to sperm."], ["Body temperature control", "Hot flashes are common because the hypothalamus senses low estrogen."], ["Eyes", "Can cause blurred vision, spots or flashes. Treatment is stopped if these occur."], ["Pregnancy outcomes", "Raises the chance of twins and other multiple pregnancies because more than one follicle may ovulate."]], "journey": "Clomiphene is taken by mouth for several days early in the menstrual cycle. It is well absorbed and processed by the liver through CYP2D6 and CYP3A4. It leaves mainly in the feces through bile and undergoes enterohepatic recycling. Its half-life is about five days, and the zuclomiphene isomer can linger for weeks. Ovulation usually occurs about 5 to 10 days after the last pill.", "sidefx": "Hot flashes come from blocking estrogen receptors in the hypothalamus. Ovarian enlargement, cysts and multiple pregnancy come from the stimulation of several follicles at once. Ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome is uncommon with clomiphene but can cause fluid shifts, ascites and clots. Visual symptoms are thought to come from effects on the retina or visual pathways and are a reason to stop. Thin endometrium may lower pregnancy rates despite ovulation. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Clomiphene is also used off-label in men with low testosterone because blocking hypothalamic estrogen feedback raises LH and the testes' own testosterone production.", "src": ["Clomid (clomiphene citrate) FDA label", "Greenblatt RB et al. JAMA 1961", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "clonidine": {"origin": "Clonidine was made in the early 1960s by chemists at the German company Boehringer Ingelheim, who were looking for a nasal decongestant related to imidazoline drugs. When it was tested, it caused sleepiness, a slow heart rate and a large drop in blood pressure. The company changed course and developed it for hypertension. It came into medical use in 1966 as Catapres and was approved in the US in 1974. Later uses grew to include opioid and alcohol withdrawal, ADHD, epidural pain control and hot flashes.", "timeline": [["1961", "Clonidine is patented by Boehringer Ingelheim."], ["1966", "Clonidine enters medical use as Catapres for hypertension."], ["1974", "FDA approves clonidine for hypertension."], ["2010", "FDA approves extended-release clonidine (Kapvay) for ADHD."]], "cell": "Clonidine is a central alpha-2 adrenergic agonist. Alpha-2 receptors are Gi-coupled. In the brainstem, especially the nucleus tractus solitarius and the rostral ventrolateral medulla, clonidine stimulates alpha-2A receptors and imidazoline I1 receptors. This lowers sympathetic outflow from the brain. On nerve terminals, alpha-2 activation lowers cAMP, reduces calcium entry and decreases norepinephrine release. The result is lower heart rate, lower peripheral resistance and lower renin release. In the locus coeruleus, alpha-2 activation calms the noradrenergic overactivity that drives withdrawal symptoms and causes sedation. In the spinal cord, it reduces pain transmission.", "body": [["Brain", "Reduces sympathetic outflow and causes sedation. Calms the noradrenergic surge of opioid and alcohol withdrawal and improves attention in ADHD."], ["Heart", "Slows heart rate and lowers cardiac output."], ["Blood vessels", "Lowers peripheral resistance and blood pressure."], ["Kidneys", "Lowers renin release, reducing angiotensin II."], ["Mouth and gut", "Causes dry mouth and constipation by reducing salivary and gut activity."], ["Spinal cord", "Given epidurally, it reduces pain transmission in cancer pain."]], "journey": "Clonidine is well absorbed by mouth and starts working in 30 to 60 minutes. It is also given as a weekly skin patch, which takes two to three days to reach full effect, and by epidural injection. It is fat soluble and crosses the blood-brain barrier easily. About half is metabolized in the liver, and the rest leaves unchanged in the urine. Its half-life is about 12 to 16 hours and is longer in kidney disease.", "sidefx": "Sedation, dry mouth and dizziness come from central alpha-2 stimulation. Bradycardia and hypotension come from lowered sympathetic tone. Stopping it suddenly causes rebound hypertension because norepinephrine release surges after long suppression, and receptors may have adapted, so it must be tapered. Overdose, common in children who find pills, causes an opioid-like picture with coma, small pupils, slow breathing, bradycardia and hypotension. Naloxone sometimes helps partially, and care is mostly supportive. Very high levels can cause brief hypertension from stimulation of peripheral alpha-2 receptors on vessels.", "fun": "Clonidine was nearly a nasal spray. Its blood pressure effect was discovered when testing showed it made people sleepy and lowered their blood pressure.", "src": ["Catapres (clonidine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "clopidogrel": {"origin": "Clopidogrel came from a search for a safer version of ticlopidine, an antiplatelet thienopyridine developed in France in the 1970s that could cause dangerous blood disorders. Researchers at Sanofi developed clopidogrel as a related compound with fewer of these problems. In 1996 the CAPRIE trial showed it was slightly better than aspirin at preventing heart attack, stroke and vascular death in high-risk patients. The FDA approved it in 1997 as Plavix, marketed by Bristol-Myers Squibb and Sanofi, and it became one of the best-selling drugs in the world. In 2010 the FDA added a boxed warning that people with poor CYP2C19 function get less benefit.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Ticlopidine, the first thienopyridine antiplatelet, is developed in France."], ["1996", "The CAPRIE trial compares clopidogrel with aspirin."], ["1997", "FDA approves clopidogrel (Plavix)."], ["2001", "The CURE trial shows benefit of clopidogrel plus aspirin in acute coronary syndromes."], ["2010", "FDA adds a boxed warning about poor CYP2C19 metabolizers."]], "cell": "When a platelet is activated, it releases ADP, which binds the P2Y12 receptor on nearby platelets. P2Y12 is Gi-coupled. It lowers cAMP and activates PI3 kinase, which amplifies activation of the glycoprotein IIb/IIIa receptor and builds a stable clot. Clopidogrel is a prodrug. In the liver, CYP enzymes, especially CYP2C19, convert it in two steps into an active thiol metabolite. This metabolite forms a disulfide bond with the P2Y12 receptor and blocks it permanently. Because platelets have no nucleus and cannot make new receptors, the effect lasts for the life of the platelet, about 7 to 10 days.", "body": [["Platelets", "Prevents ADP-driven platelet activation and aggregation for the rest of each platelet's life."], ["Heart", "Lowers the risk of heart attack and stent clotting after acute coronary syndromes and stent placement, usually with aspirin."], ["Brain", "Reduces the risk of recurrent ischemic stroke. It also raises the risk of bleeding into the brain."], ["Legs and vessels", "Used in peripheral artery disease to reduce vascular events."], ["Gut", "Can cause GI bleeding, especially combined with aspirin or other anticoagulants."], ["Blood", "Rarely causes thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP)."]], "journey": "Clopidogrel is taken by mouth and absorbed in the gut. About 85 percent is converted by esterases into an inactive carboxylic acid, and only a small fraction is turned into the active metabolite by liver CYP2C19 and other enzymes. A loading dose speeds the effect to within hours. The active metabolite acts quickly and has a half-life of about 30 minutes, but platelet inhibition lasts days. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. Platelet function returns about 5 to 7 days after stopping, as new platelets are made.", "sidefx": "Bleeding is the main risk and comes directly from impaired platelet aggregation. Because the block is irreversible, there is no reversal agent. Platelet transfusion can help in serious bleeding because new platelets are not yet blocked, though remaining active drug can affect them shortly after a dose. Poor CYP2C19 metabolizers make little active drug and are at higher risk of stent thrombosis. Strong CYP2C19 inhibitors such as omeprazole can blunt its effect. TTP is rare and appears to be immune mediated. It is usually held before surgery.", "fun": "Genetic testing for CYP2C19 before choosing clopidogrel is one of the best-known examples of pharmacogenomics in cardiology.", "src": ["Plavix (clopidogrel) FDA label", "CAPRIE Steering Committee, Lancet 1996", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "clozapine": {"origin": "Clozapine was synthesized in 1958 by chemists at Wander AG in Bern, Switzerland. Unlike older antipsychotics, it caused almost no movement side effects, which puzzled researchers who believed those effects were needed for the drug to work. It was launched in Europe in the early 1970s, but in 1975 a cluster of deaths from agranulocytosis in Finland led to its withdrawal in many countries. In 1988 John Kane and colleagues showed that clozapine helped patients with schizophrenia who had failed other drugs. The FDA approved it in 1989 as Clozaril, marketed by Sandoz, with mandatory blood count monitoring. It is still the only antipsychotic proven better for treatment-resistant schizophrenia and the only one approved to reduce suicidal behavior in schizophrenia.", "timeline": [["1958", "Clozapine is synthesized at Wander AG in Switzerland."], ["1975", "Agranulocytosis deaths in Finland lead to withdrawal in many countries."], ["1988", "John Kane's trial shows benefit in treatment-resistant schizophrenia."], ["1989", "FDA approves clozapine (Clozaril) with blood monitoring."], ["2002", "FDA approves clozapine to reduce suicidal behavior in schizophrenia."]], "cell": "Clozapine is an atypical antipsychotic that blocks many receptors. It binds dopamine D2 receptors weakly and loosens off them quickly, while binding D4 receptors more strongly. It strongly blocks serotonin 5-HT2A receptors. 5-HT2A blockade increases dopamine release in the nigrostriatal and prefrontal pathways, which protects against movement side effects and may help negative symptoms. It also blocks histamine H1, muscarinic, and alpha-1 adrenergic receptors. Its main metabolite, norclozapine, is a muscarinic agonist at some subtypes, which may explain the heavy drooling. Its exact advantage in resistant schizophrenia is not fully understood.", "body": [["Brain", "Treats resistant schizophrenia and lowers suicide risk. Causes heavy sedation and lowers the seizure threshold in a dose-related way."], ["Bone marrow", "Can cause severe neutropenia (agranulocytosis), which requires regular absolute neutrophil count monitoring."], ["Heart", "Can cause myocarditis, usually in the first weeks, and cardiomyopathy later. Tachycardia is common."], ["Gut", "Slows gut movement strongly through anticholinergic effects and can cause severe constipation and ileus."], ["Mouth", "Causes drooling (sialorrhea), especially at night."], ["Metabolism", "Causes major weight gain, diabetes and high lipids."], ["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 blockade causes orthostatic hypotension and fainting, especially early in treatment."]], "journey": "Clozapine is taken by mouth and absorbed well, but first-pass metabolism lowers how much reaches the blood. It is highly protein bound and crosses into the brain. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP1A2, with help from CYP3A4 and CYP2D6, into norclozapine. Smoking induces CYP1A2 and lowers clozapine levels, so quitting smoking can cause levels to rise sharply. Fluvoxamine and ciprofloxacin also raise levels. Its half-life is about 12 hours. It is started at a low dose and increased slowly to limit low blood pressure and seizures.", "sidefx": "Agranulocytosis is thought to be immune mediated, possibly linked to a reactive metabolite that damages neutrophils, and it is why counts are checked. Myocarditis is an immune reaction that can present with fever, chest pain and rising troponin. Seizures reflect a lowered seizure threshold at high levels. Constipation and ileus come from strong muscarinic blockade and can be fatal. Sedation and weight gain come from H1 blockade. Orthostatic hypotension comes from alpha-1 blockade. NMS is possible but rare. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Clozapine is a classic example of a drug withdrawn for toxicity and then brought back because no other drug could match its benefit for the sickest patients.", "src": ["Clozaril (clozapine) FDA label", "Kane J et al. Arch Gen Psychiatry 1988", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "colchicine": {"origin": "Colchicine comes from the autumn crocus, Colchicum autumnale, and related plants. The plant was known as a poison in ancient Greece, and in the 6th century the Byzantine physician Alexander of Tralles recommended a related plant extract for joint pain. In 1820 the French chemists Pierre-Joseph Pelletier and Joseph Caventou isolated the active alkaloid. Colchicine was used for gout for nearly two centuries without formal FDA approval. In 2009 the FDA approved Colcrys under its unapproved drugs initiative, which removed older unapproved versions and raised the price sharply. In 2023 a low-dose colchicine product was approved to lower the risk of heart attack and stroke.", "timeline": [["6th century", "Alexander of Tralles recommends a colchicum-type plant for joint pain."], ["1820", "Pelletier and Caventou isolate colchicine."], ["2009", "FDA approves Colcrys for gout and familial Mediterranean fever."], ["2023", "FDA approves low-dose colchicine (Lodoco) to reduce cardiovascular events."]], "cell": "Colchicine binds tubulin, the building block of microtubules, and forms a tubulin-colchicine complex that caps the growing end of microtubules and stops them from growing. Neutrophils need microtubules to move, change shape and release their contents. In gout, uric acid crystals activate the NLRP3 inflammasome in white cells, which releases IL-1 beta and draws in neutrophils. Colchicine reduces neutrophil migration, adhesion and degranulation and blunts inflammasome activation. This calms the joint inflammation. It also arrests dividing cells in mitosis, which explains its toxicity to fast-growing tissues such as the gut lining and bone marrow.", "body": [["Joints", "Reduces inflammation in acute gout and prevents flares when starting uric acid lowering therapy."], ["Heart", "Treats and prevents recurrent pericarditis and lowers cardiovascular events in coronary disease."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea and cramps are common because rapidly dividing gut cells are sensitive to mitotic arrest."], ["Muscles and nerves", "Can cause myopathy and neuropathy, especially in kidney disease or with interacting drugs."], ["Bone marrow", "At toxic levels it can stop blood cell production."], ["Genetic fever syndromes", "Prevents attacks and amyloidosis in familial Mediterranean fever."]], "journey": "Colchicine is taken by mouth and absorbed in the gut, with variable bioavailability. It concentrates inside white blood cells. It is a substrate of CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein, so drugs such as clarithromycin, ketoconazole, ritonavir and cyclosporine can raise levels to dangerous amounts. It is cleared by the liver through bile and by the kidneys. Its half-life is about a day, and it builds up in kidney and liver disease.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea, nausea and vomiting come from blocked cell division in the gut lining. Overdose is very dangerous. It begins with GI symptoms in the first day, followed by multi-organ failure, bone marrow suppression, heart failure and rhabdomyolysis over the next days, with hair loss later in survivors. All of this reflects mitotic arrest in many tissues. There is no approved antidote, and care is supportive. Myopathy and neuropathy are more likely when CYP3A4 or P-gp inhibitors are combined with it or with statins.", "fun": "Plant breeders use colchicine to double the chromosomes of plants, a trick that created many seedless watermelons.", "src": ["Colcrys (colchicine) FDA label", "Lodoco (colchicine) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "cyclobenzaprine": {"origin": "Cyclobenzaprine was developed by Merck and was first studied as a possible antidepressant because it is almost identical in structure to amitriptyline, a tricyclic antidepressant. It differs by just one double bond. Instead it proved useful for relaxing muscle spasm. It was approved in the US in 1977 under the brand name Flexeril. It became one of the most prescribed muscle relaxants for short-term back and neck strain.", "timeline": [["1977", "FDA approves cyclobenzaprine (Flexeril) for muscle spasm."], ["2007", "An extended-release form (Amrix) is approved."]], "cell": "Cyclobenzaprine acts mainly in the brainstem, not directly on muscle. It reduces tonic somatic motor activity by acting on descending serotonergic pathways, largely as an antagonist at 5-HT2 receptors. This lowers the excitability of alpha and gamma motor neurons in the spinal cord, which reduces muscle spasm from a local strain. Because it shares the tricyclic structure, it also blocks muscarinic receptors and histamine H1 receptors and inhibits norepinephrine reuptake. These actions explain its sedation and anticholinergic effects. It does not relax spasticity from brain or spinal cord disease well.", "body": [["Brainstem and spinal cord", "Lowers motor neuron activity and reduces muscle spasm from acute strains."], ["Brain", "Causes drowsiness and dizziness, which may contribute to its benefit at night."], ["Mouth and eyes", "Anticholinergic effects cause dry mouth and blurred vision."], ["Heart", "Can cause tachycardia and arrhythmias, and in overdose it acts like a tricyclic antidepressant."], ["Bladder and gut", "Can cause urinary retention and constipation, especially in older adults."]], "journey": "Cyclobenzaprine is taken by mouth and absorbed well, but first-pass metabolism reduces how much reaches the blood. It is highly protein bound and crosses into the brain. The liver breaks it down through CYP3A4, CYP1A2 and CYP2D6. Metabolites leave mainly in the urine. Its half-life is long, about 18 hours, and longer in older adults and liver disease.", "sidefx": "Sedation comes from H1 blockade, and dry mouth, constipation and urinary retention from muscarinic blockade. It is on the Beers list of drugs to avoid in older adults because of falls and confusion. Its tricyclic structure means overdose can cause seizures, QRS widening and arrhythmias, treated with sodium bicarbonate like TCA overdose. Combined with MAO inhibitors, SSRIs or tramadol, it can contribute to serotonin syndrome. It is avoided after a recent heart attack and in heart block or heart failure.", "fun": "Cyclobenzaprine and amitriptyline differ by just one double bond, yet one is marketed as a muscle relaxant and the other as an antidepressant.", "src": ["Flexeril (cyclobenzaprine) FDA label", "American Geriatrics Society Beers Criteria", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "cyclophosphamide": {"origin": "Cyclophosphamide comes from a war gas. Doctors noticed that soldiers exposed to sulfur mustard gas had low white cell counts. In 1942 Louis Goodman and Alfred Gilman at Yale tested a nitrogen mustard in a patient with lymphoma, starting cancer chemotherapy. Nitrogen mustards were very toxic, so in 1958 Norbert Brock and colleagues at Asta-Werke in Germany designed cyclophosphamide as an inactive prodrug that they hoped tumors would activate. It turned out the liver activates it instead, but the drug worked and was safer to handle. The FDA approved it in 1959. It became one of the most widely used drugs in cancer and in severe autoimmune disease.", "timeline": [["1917", "Sulfur mustard gas is used in World War I."], ["1942", "Goodman and Gilman test a nitrogen mustard in a lymphoma patient at Yale."], ["1958", "Norbert Brock and colleagues at Asta-Werke synthesize cyclophosphamide."], ["1959", "FDA approves cyclophosphamide."], ["1980s", "Mesna is introduced to protect the bladder."]], "cell": "Cyclophosphamide is a prodrug. In the liver, CYP2B6 and other CYP enzymes oxidize it to 4-hydroxycyclophosphamide, which exists in balance with aldophosphamide. Aldophosphamide breaks down into phosphoramide mustard and acrolein. Phosphoramide mustard is the cytotoxic alkylating agent. It forms reactive aziridinium ions that attack the N7 position of guanine and form crosslinks within and between DNA strands. Crosslinked DNA cannot be copied or read, so cells die by apoptosis. It works in any cell cycle phase. Cells with high aldehyde dehydrogenase, such as marrow stem cells, inactivate aldophosphamide and are partly protected. Acrolein, the byproduct, is filtered into urine and injures the bladder lining.", "body": [["Cancer cells", "Kills cancer cells in lymphomas, leukemias, breast cancer and other tumors."], ["Immune system", "Depletes B and T lymphocytes, which is used in lupus nephritis, vasculitis and other autoimmune diseases."], ["Bladder", "Acrolein in urine causes hemorrhagic cystitis and raises bladder cancer risk."], ["Bone marrow", "Lowers white cells, with a nadir about one to two weeks after a dose."], ["Kidneys", "Can cause SIADH with water retention and low sodium, especially with large IV fluid loads."], ["Reproductive organs", "Can cause infertility and early menopause by damaging ovarian and testicular cells."], ["Lungs and heart", "Rarely causes pulmonary fibrosis. Very high doses can cause hemorrhagic myocarditis."]], "journey": "Cyclophosphamide can be given by mouth or IV and is well absorbed. It is inactive until the liver converts it. The active metabolites spread through the body, including the brain to some degree. Aldehyde dehydrogenase inactivates some aldophosphamide into carboxyphosphamide. Metabolites, including acrolein, leave in the urine. The parent drug has a half-life of a few hours. Its peak effect on the marrow comes about 8 to 14 days later.", "sidefx": "Bone marrow suppression, hair loss and mouth sores come from killing fast-dividing cells. Hemorrhagic cystitis comes from acrolein in the urine and is prevented with lots of fluids and mesna, which binds acrolein in the bladder. SIADH causes water retention. Infertility comes from damage to germ cells. Long-term risks include bladder cancer and therapy-related leukemia from DNA damage. Infections are common because of immune suppression. There is no general antidote, but mesna is the specific bladder protector.", "fun": "Cancer chemotherapy began with mustard gas research in World War II, and cyclophosphamide is a direct descendant.", "src": ["Cyclophosphamide injection FDA label", "Goodman LS, Gilman A et al. JAMA 1946", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "cyclosporine": {"origin": "Cyclosporine came from a fungus in a soil sample collected in Norway in 1969 by a Sandoz employee on vacation. The fungus, Tolypocladium inflatum, made a cyclic peptide of 11 amino acids. In the early 1970s Jean-Francois Borel at Sandoz found that it powerfully suppressed T cells without killing bone marrow cells. In 1978 the British surgeon Roy Calne used it in kidney transplants. The FDA approved it in 1983 as Sandimmune. It transformed organ transplantation by greatly improving the survival of transplanted kidneys, hearts and livers.", "timeline": [["1969", "A Sandoz employee collects the soil sample in Norway that contains the fungus."], ["1972", "Jean-Francois Borel identifies its immunosuppressive effect."], ["1978", "Roy Calne reports cyclosporine use in kidney transplantation."], ["1983", "FDA approves cyclosporine (Sandimmune)."], ["2002", "Cyclosporine eye drops (Restasis) are approved for dry eye."]], "cell": "When a T cell receptor recognizes an antigen, intracellular calcium rises and activates calcineurin, a phosphatase. Calcineurin removes phosphates from NFAT (nuclear factor of activated T cells), which then enters the nucleus and switches on genes for IL-2 and other cytokines. IL-2 drives T cell growth and expansion. Cyclosporine enters T cells and binds cyclophilin. The cyclosporine-cyclophilin complex binds calcineurin and blocks it. NFAT stays outside the nucleus, IL-2 production falls, and T cells cannot multiply in response to the transplanted organ. This prevents rejection.", "body": [["Immune system", "Suppresses T cell activation, preventing organ rejection and calming autoimmune diseases such as psoriasis and rheumatoid arthritis."], ["Kidneys", "Constricts the afferent arterioles, lowering kidney blood flow. Causes acute and chronic kidney injury and high potassium."], ["Blood vessels", "Raises blood pressure through vasoconstriction and sodium retention."], ["Brain and nerves", "Causes tremor and headache. Can cause PRES with seizures and vision changes."], ["Gums and hair", "Causes gum overgrowth and increased hair growth (hirsutism)."], ["Infections and cancer", "Raises the risk of infections and skin cancers and lymphomas from weakened immune surveillance."]], "journey": "Cyclosporine is taken by mouth or given IV. Absorption by mouth varies a lot, and the microemulsion form (Neoral) is absorbed more reliably than the older form, so the two are not interchangeable. It is fat soluble and spreads widely. The liver and gut break it down mainly by CYP3A4, and it is a P-glycoprotein substrate. Many drugs and grapefruit juice change its levels. Metabolites leave in bile. Blood levels are monitored to keep it in range.", "sidefx": "Kidney damage comes from calcineurin inhibition in the kidney, which causes afferent arteriole constriction and, over time, scarring. High blood pressure has the same cause. High potassium and low magnesium come from effects on kidney tubules. Tremor and PRES reflect its effect on the brain. Gum overgrowth and hirsutism are unique to cyclosporine and not tacrolimus. Infections and cancer reflect broad immunosuppression. CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels and toxicity, while inducers such as rifampin can cause rejection. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Cyclosporine was found in soil collected by a Sandoz employee on vacation in Norway's Hardangervidda plateau.", "src": ["Sandimmune and Neoral (cyclosporine) FDA labels", "Calne RY et al. Lancet 1978", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "cytarabine": {"origin": "Cytarabine's story begins in the sea. In the early 1950s the chemist Werner Bergmann at Yale isolated unusual nucleosides from a Caribbean sponge, now called Tectitethya crypta. These compounds used the sugar arabinose instead of ribose. They inspired chemists to make arabinose-based drugs. Cytarabine, also called cytosine arabinoside or ara-C, was synthesized in 1959 by Richard Walwick and colleagues at the University of California, Berkeley. The FDA approved it in 1969. It became the backbone of treatment for acute myeloid leukemia and remains so today.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Werner Bergmann isolates arabinose nucleosides from a Caribbean sea sponge."], ["1959", "Cytarabine is synthesized at the University of California, Berkeley."], ["1969", "FDA approves cytarabine."], ["2017", "FDA approves Vyxeos, a liposome with daunorubicin and cytarabine."]], "cell": "Cytarabine looks like cytidine, but with arabinose in place of deoxyribose. It enters cells through nucleoside transporters. Inside, deoxycytidine kinase phosphorylates it, and other kinases finish the job, making ara-CTP. Ara-CTP competes with dCTP and inhibits DNA polymerase. It is also built into the new DNA strand, where the arabinose sugar blocks further chain extension and stalls replication. The damaged DNA triggers apoptosis. It is S-phase specific, so it mainly kills cells that are actively copying DNA. Cytidine deaminase inactivates it to ara-U, and cancer cells with high deaminase or low deoxycytidine kinase are resistant.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Kills leukemia cells but also normal marrow cells, causing deep drops in all blood counts."], ["Brain", "High doses can damage the cerebellum, causing slurred speech, unsteady walking and nystagmus. It can be given intrathecally for leukemia in the brain."], ["Eyes", "High doses cause conjunctivitis, prevented with steroid eye drops."], ["Gut and mouth", "Kills dividing lining cells, causing mouth sores, nausea and diarrhea."], ["Lungs", "Can cause noncardiogenic pulmonary edema."], ["Whole body", "Cytarabine syndrome causes fever, muscle pain, bone pain and rash hours after a dose."]], "journey": "Cytarabine is not reliable by mouth because cytidine deaminase in the gut and liver destroys it, so it is given IV, subcutaneously or intrathecally. It distributes into body tissues and, at high doses, reaches the cerebrospinal fluid. It is inactivated quickly by cytidine deaminase in the liver, blood and tissues. Its half-life is a few hours. The inactive metabolite ara-U leaves in the urine. Its effect on marrow peaks about one to two weeks after treatment.", "sidefx": "Bone marrow suppression comes from killing dividing marrow precursors, leading to infection and bleeding. Cerebellar toxicity is linked to high doses, older age and poor kidney function, and it requires stopping the drug. Conjunctivitis comes from drug secreted into tears. Mucositis and diarrhea reflect killing of dividing gut cells. Tumor lysis syndrome can occur when many leukemia cells die at once. Cytarabine syndrome is an inflammatory reaction. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Cytarabine was one of the first anticancer drugs inspired by a marine natural product, a sea sponge from the Caribbean.", "src": ["Cytarabine injection FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "dabigatran": {"origin": "Direct thrombin inhibitors trace back to hirudin, the anticoagulant in leech saliva. Dabigatran was designed by the German company Boehringer Ingelheim as a small, synthetic molecule that fits thrombin's active site. Because dabigatran itself is poorly absorbed, it is given as the prodrug dabigatran etexilate. In 2009 the RE-LY trial showed it worked at least as well as warfarin for preventing stroke in atrial fibrillation. The FDA approved it in 2010 as Pradaxa, the first oral anticoagulant approved in the US in decades as an alternative to warfarin. In 2015 the FDA approved idarucizumab (Praxbind), an antibody fragment that reverses it.", "timeline": [["1884", "John Haycraft describes an anticoagulant in leech saliva, later named hirudin."], ["2009", "The RE-LY trial compares dabigatran with warfarin in atrial fibrillation."], ["2010", "FDA approves dabigatran (Pradaxa)."], ["2015", "FDA approves idarucizumab (Praxbind) to reverse dabigatran."]], "cell": "Thrombin (factor IIa) is the central enzyme of clotting. It converts fibrinogen to fibrin, activates factors V, VIII, XI and XIII, and strongly activates platelets through PAR receptors. Dabigatran etexilate is a prodrug that esterases convert into dabigatran. Dabigatran binds directly and reversibly to the active site of thrombin. Unlike heparin, it does not need antithrombin, so it blocks both free thrombin and thrombin already bound to fibrin in a clot. With thrombin blocked, less fibrin forms and clots cannot grow or stabilize. This prevents strokes from atrial fibrillation and treats or prevents venous clots.", "body": [["Blood", "Prevents clot formation by blocking thrombin. Raises the risk of bleeding."], ["Brain", "Lowers the risk of stroke in atrial fibrillation, with lower rates of brain bleeding than warfarin."], ["Veins and lungs", "Treats and prevents deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism."], ["Gut", "Causes indigestion and has a higher rate of GI bleeding than warfarin, partly due to drug in the gut."], ["Kidneys", "Most of the drug leaves through the kidneys, so poor kidney function raises levels and bleeding risk."]], "journey": "Dabigatran etexilate is taken by mouth in capsules containing tartaric acid, which helps absorption. Only a small fraction is absorbed. Esterases in the gut, blood and liver convert it to active dabigatran. It reaches peak effect in one to two hours. It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes but is a P-glycoprotein substrate, so P-gp inhibitors and inducers change its levels. About 80 percent is removed unchanged by the kidneys. Its half-life is about 12 to 17 hours and gets much longer in kidney disease. Capsules must not be opened because this greatly increases absorption.", "sidefx": "Bleeding is the main risk because thrombin is blocked. Indigestion is common and is partly due to the tartaric acid in the capsule. Rates of GI bleeding are higher than with warfarin because some active drug reaches the gut lining. Idarucizumab is a humanized antibody fragment that binds dabigatran tightly and reverses its effect within minutes. Dabigatran is also one of the few DOACs that can be removed by hemodialysis. Stopping it early without another anticoagulant raises the risk of stroke and clots.", "fun": "Dabigatran's reversal agent, idarucizumab, binds the drug about 350 times more tightly than thrombin does.", "src": ["Pradaxa (dabigatran etexilate) FDA label", "Connolly SJ et al. RE-LY Trial, N Engl J Med 2009", "Praxbind (idarucizumab) FDA label"]}, "dantrolene": {"origin": "Dantrolene was first made in 1967 by chemists at Norwich Pharmacal in the US, during a search for new muscle relaxants. At first it was used for spasticity. Malignant hyperthermia, a deadly reaction to some anesthetics, was described in 1960 by Michael Denborough in Australia, and it killed most of the people it struck. In 1975 the South African anesthesiologist Gaisford Harrison showed that dantrolene reversed malignant hyperthermia in susceptible pigs. An IV form was approved in the US in 1979, and deaths from malignant hyperthermia fell dramatically. A more concentrated form, Ryanodex, was approved in 2014 so it can be prepared much faster.", "timeline": [["1960", "Michael Denborough describes malignant hyperthermia in a family in Australia."], ["1967", "Dantrolene is synthesized at Norwich Pharmacal."], ["1975", "Gaisford Harrison shows dantrolene treats malignant hyperthermia in pigs."], ["1979", "IV dantrolene is approved in the US for malignant hyperthermia."], ["2014", "FDA approves Ryanodex, a rapid-preparation dantrolene."]], "cell": "In skeletal muscle, a nerve signal depolarizes the muscle membrane and T-tubules. The dihydropyridine receptor senses this and opens the ryanodine receptor RyR1 on the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Calcium floods out, binds troponin and starts contraction. In malignant hyperthermia, mutated RyR1 channels release calcium uncontrollably when exposed to volatile anesthetics or succinylcholine. Muscles contract continuously, burn energy, produce heat, carbon dioxide and lactic acid, and break down. Dantrolene binds RyR1 and reduces calcium release, uncoupling excitation from contraction. Muscle rigidity, heat production and hypermetabolism stop. It has little effect on cardiac or smooth muscle, which use RyR2.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Reverses rigidity and hypermetabolism in malignant hyperthermia and reduces spasticity in chronic use."], ["Body temperature", "Stops excess heat production from contracting muscle, bringing temperature down."], ["Blood chemistry", "Reverses the acidosis, high carbon dioxide and high potassium caused by muscle breakdown."], ["Liver", "Long-term oral use can cause hepatitis and rarely fatal liver failure."], ["Lungs and airway", "Muscle weakness can reduce breathing strength and swallowing after treatment."]], "journey": "For malignant hyperthermia, dantrolene is given IV as fast as possible. It acts within minutes. It is poorly soluble in water, which is why older forms take time to mix and newer forms are more concentrated. The liver metabolizes it to 5-hydroxydantrolene, which is also active. Metabolites leave in urine and bile. Its half-life is several hours, and doses are repeated until signs resolve. Oral forms are used for chronic spasticity.", "sidefx": "Muscle weakness, drowsiness and dizziness come directly from reduced calcium release in skeletal muscle and some CNS effects. Phlebitis comes from the alkaline IV solution. Liver injury with long-term oral use is idiosyncratic and is more likely with high doses, in women and in people over 35, so liver tests are checked. Combining IV dantrolene with verapamil can cause high potassium and heart depression, so calcium channel blockers are avoided. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Before dantrolene, malignant hyperthermia killed most patients who developed it. With dantrolene and modern monitoring, deaths are now rare.", "src": ["Dantrium, Revonto and Ryanodex (dantrolene) FDA labels", "Malignant Hyperthermia Association of the United States guidance", "Harrison GG. Br J Anaesth 1975"]}, "dapagliflozin": {"origin": "The SGLT2 story begins with an apple tree. In 1835 French chemists isolated phlorizin from apple tree bark. Later scientists found it made animals spill glucose into the urine by blocking sodium-glucose cotransporters, but it was poorly absorbed and not selective. Bristol-Myers Squibb, working with AstraZeneca, designed dapagliflozin as a selective, oral SGLT2 inhibitor. The FDA approved it in 2014 as Farxiga for type 2 diabetes. Trials then showed surprising benefits beyond blood sugar. DAPA-HF in 2019 and DAPA-CKD in 2020 showed fewer deaths and hospitalizations in heart failure and chronic kidney disease, even in people without diabetes.", "timeline": [["1835", "Phlorizin, the first known SGLT inhibitor, is isolated from apple tree bark."], ["2014", "FDA approves dapagliflozin (Farxiga) for type 2 diabetes."], ["2019", "DAPA-HF shows benefit in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction."], ["2020", "FDA approves dapagliflozin for heart failure. DAPA-CKD reports kidney benefit."], ["2021", "FDA approves dapagliflozin for chronic kidney disease."]], "cell": "In the early proximal tubule, SGLT2 reabsorbs most of the glucose filtered by the kidney, using the sodium gradient to bring glucose and sodium together into the cell. Dapagliflozin selectively blocks SGLT2. Glucose and sodium stay in the urine, causing glycosuria and mild natriuresis. Blood sugar falls without depending on insulin, so hypoglycemia is uncommon alone. More sodium reaching the macula densa restores tubuloglomerular feedback, constricting the afferent arteriole and lowering glomerular pressure, which protects the kidney. Lower volume and preload, a shift toward ketone use and other effects are thought to help the failing heart.", "body": [["Kidneys", "Causes glucose and sodium loss in urine and lowers pressure inside the glomerulus, slowing kidney disease."], ["Heart", "Lowers hospitalization and death in heart failure, likely through reduced preload, afterload and better heart metabolism."], ["Blood sugar", "Lowers glucose and A1c without causing much hypoglycemia on its own."], ["Blood vessels", "Mildly lowers blood pressure through sodium and water loss."], ["Genitals and urinary tract", "Sugar in the urine feeds yeast and bacteria, raising the risk of genital yeast infections and UTIs."], ["Metabolism", "Causes calorie loss and modest weight loss. Can push the body toward ketone production."]], "journey": "Dapagliflozin is taken by mouth once daily and absorbed well, peaking in about two hours. It is highly protein bound. The liver and kidney break it down mainly by glucuronidation through UGT1A9 into an inactive metabolite, with no meaningful CYP involvement. Metabolites leave mostly in the urine. Its half-life is about 13 hours. Its glucose-lowering effect drops as kidney function declines, but heart and kidney benefits persist.", "sidefx": "Genital yeast infections and UTIs come from glucose in the urine. Dehydration and low blood pressure come from extra sodium and water loss, especially with diuretics. Euglycemic diabetic ketoacidosis can occur because the drug lowers glucose and insulin while raising glucagon and ketone production, so patients can have DKA with near-normal sugar. It is held before surgery and during acute illness. Fournier's gangrene, a rare perineal infection, is a labeled warning. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The gliflozins trace back to phlorizin, a bitter compound in apple tree bark first isolated in 1835.", "src": ["Farxiga (dapagliflozin) FDA label", "McMurray JJV et al. DAPA-HF, N Engl J Med 2019", "Heerspink HJL et al. DAPA-CKD, N Engl J Med 2020"]}, "dapsone": {"origin": "Dapsone was first made in 1908 by the German chemists Emil Fromm and Jakob Wittmann, decades before anyone knew it fought bacteria. In 1937, researchers in England and France found that it was active against streptococci in mice. In 1941 Guy Faget at the US leprosy hospital in Carville, Louisiana, began treating patients with promin, a dapsone derivative, and saw dramatic results. Oral dapsone itself soon replaced promin. It became the backbone of leprosy treatment worldwide and is still part of multidrug therapy. It is also used to prevent Pneumocystis pneumonia and to treat skin diseases driven by neutrophils.", "timeline": [["1908", "Emil Fromm and Jakob Wittmann synthesize dapsone."], ["1937", "Dapsone's antibacterial activity is discovered."], ["1941", "Guy Faget begins treating leprosy with promin at Carville, Louisiana."], ["1981", "WHO recommends multidrug therapy for leprosy including dapsone."]], "cell": "Bacteria must make their own folic acid. Dihydropteroate synthase joins para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA) to a pteridine to make dihydropteroic acid, a folate precursor. Dapsone is a PABA look-alike that competitively inhibits this enzyme, so bacteria cannot make folate, nucleotides or DNA. It is bacteriostatic, like sulfonamides. Human cells take in folate from food and are not affected. In skin disease, dapsone also inhibits myeloperoxidase in neutrophils, reducing the toxic oxidants they release, and it interferes with neutrophil migration. This anti-inflammatory action helps dermatitis herpetiformis and other neutrophil-driven skin diseases.", "body": [["Skin and nerves", "Kills Mycobacterium leprae as part of multidrug therapy for leprosy."], ["Lungs", "Prevents Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia in people who cannot take trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole."], ["Skin", "Calms neutrophil-driven diseases such as dermatitis herpetiformis. A topical gel treats acne."], ["Red blood cells", "Oxidizes hemoglobin, causing methemoglobinemia and mild hemolysis, severe in G6PD deficiency."], ["Bone marrow", "Can rarely cause agranulocytosis, usually in the first few months, so blood counts are checked."], ["Immune system", "Can cause dapsone hypersensitivity syndrome with fever, rash and organ injury."]], "journey": "Dapsone is taken by mouth and absorbed almost completely. It spreads widely into tissues, including skin and nerves. The liver metabolizes it by N-acetylation and by CYP-mediated N-hydroxylation. The hydroxylamine metabolite causes most of its blood toxicity. It undergoes enterohepatic recycling. Metabolites leave in the urine. Its half-life is about 30 hours.", "sidefx": "The hydroxylamine metabolite oxidizes iron in hemoglobin from ferrous to ferric, causing methemoglobinemia and blue-gray skin with low pulse oximetry readings. It is treated with methylene blue, unless the patient has G6PD deficiency. The same oxidant stress causes hemolysis, which is severe in G6PD deficiency, so testing is done before starting. Agranulocytosis and dapsone hypersensitivity syndrome are immune reactions, the latter linked to HLA-B*13:01. Liver injury can occur. Cimetidine can reduce hydroxylamine formation.", "fun": "The Carville, Louisiana leprosarium was the only national leprosy hospital in the continental US, and its sulfone trials changed leprosy treatment worldwide.", "src": ["Dapsone tablets FDA label", "WHO Guidelines for the Diagnosis, Treatment and Prevention of Leprosy", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "daptomycin": {"origin": "Daptomycin comes from Streptomyces roseosporus, a bacterium found in a soil sample from Mount Ararat in Turkey. Eli Lilly discovered it in the early 1980s, but early trials with twice-daily dosing caused muscle toxicity, and Lilly stopped development. In 1997 the small company Cubist Pharmaceuticals licensed it and tested once-daily dosing, which kept the killing power while reducing muscle harm. The FDA approved it in 2003 as Cubicin. It became an important drug for MRSA and vancomycin-resistant enterococcus, especially in bloodstream infections and endocarditis.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Eli Lilly discovers daptomycin from Streptomyces roseosporus."], ["1997", "Cubist Pharmaceuticals licenses daptomycin."], ["2003", "FDA approves daptomycin (Cubicin) for skin infections."], ["2006", "FDA approves daptomycin for S. aureus bacteremia and right-sided endocarditis."]], "cell": "Daptomycin is a cyclic lipopeptide. In the presence of calcium, it changes shape and its fatty tail inserts into the cell membrane of gram-positive bacteria, binding phosphatidylglycerol. The molecules gather together and disrupt the membrane, forming ion leaks. Potassium flows out, the membrane depolarizes, and the cell can no longer make DNA, RNA or protein. The bacterium dies quickly without bursting, so it releases less inflammatory material. It is bactericidal and concentration dependent. Gram-negative bacteria are protected by their outer membrane. Pulmonary surfactant binds and inactivates it, so it does not work for pneumonia.", "body": [["Bloodstream and heart", "Treats S. aureus bacteremia and right-sided endocarditis, including MRSA."], ["Skin and soft tissue", "Treats complicated skin infections from MRSA, streptococci and VRE."], ["Lungs", "Not used for pneumonia because surfactant inactivates it. It can rarely cause eosinophilic pneumonia."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Can raise CPK and cause muscle pain, weakness and rarely rhabdomyolysis."], ["Nerves", "Can rarely cause peripheral neuropathy with numbness or tingling."], ["Kidneys", "Cleared by the kidneys, so dosing intervals are lengthened in kidney disease."]], "journey": "Daptomycin is given IV once daily, either as an infusion or a short push. It is highly protein bound and stays mostly in the blood and extracellular fluid. It penetrates bone and cerebrospinal fluid poorly. It is barely metabolized and is cleared mainly unchanged by the kidneys. Its half-life is about 8 to 9 hours in normal kidney function.", "sidefx": "Muscle toxicity is thought to come from its effects on skeletal muscle cell membranes, similar to its action on bacteria. CPK is checked weekly, and statins are often held because they add to muscle risk. Eosinophilic pneumonia is an immune reaction that presents with fever, cough and lung infiltrates and improves after stopping. Daptomycin can falsely prolong the PT and INR with some lab reagents. DRESS is rare. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Daptomycin was nearly abandoned. It succeeded only after Cubist realized that giving the same daily amount once instead of twice reduced muscle damage.", "src": ["Cubicin (daptomycin) FDA label", "IDSA MRSA Treatment Guideline", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "deferoxamine": {"origin": "Deferoxamine is a molecule bacteria make to steal iron. Many microbes release iron-grabbing compounds called siderophores to scavenge iron from their environment. In the late 1950s and early 1960s, researchers at Ciba in Switzerland, working with chemists at ETH Zurich, isolated ferrioxamines from the bacterium Streptomyces pilosus. Removing the iron from ferrioxamine B produced deferoxamine, a powerful iron chelator. It was introduced in the 1960s as Desferal. It became the standard treatment for iron poisoning and, for decades, the only effective way to remove the iron overload that builds up in people who need lifelong blood transfusions, such as those with thalassemia.", "timeline": [["1960", "Ferrioxamines are isolated from Streptomyces bacteria by Ciba and ETH Zurich researchers."], ["1960s", "Deferoxamine (Desferal) is introduced as an iron chelator."], ["2005", "Deferasirox, an oral chelator, is approved in the US as an alternative."]], "cell": "Deferoxamine is a hexadentate chelator, meaning it wraps around one ferric iron (Fe3+) ion with six binding points. It binds free iron in the blood and iron stored in ferritin and hemosiderin, forming a stable, water-soluble complex called ferrioxamine. Ferrioxamine is excreted in urine and bile. Deferoxamine does not pull iron out of hemoglobin, myoglobin or cytochromes, which hold iron very tightly. In iron poisoning, free iron causes oxidative damage through free radical formation, which injures the gut, liver and mitochondria. Binding free iron stops this chain. It also binds aluminum, which is used in aluminum overload in dialysis patients.", "body": [["Blood", "Binds free iron in iron poisoning, reducing oxidative damage."], ["Urine", "Ferrioxamine turns the urine a reddish orange called vin rose."], ["Liver and heart", "Removes chronic iron overload from transfusions, protecting the liver and heart from iron damage."], ["Blood vessels", "Rapid IV infusion can cause flushing and low blood pressure."], ["Lungs", "Long, high-dose infusions have been linked to ARDS."], ["Eyes and ears", "Long-term use can damage the retina and cause hearing loss."], ["Immune system", "Iron bound to ferrioxamine can feed Yersinia, raising the risk of Yersinia infections."]], "journey": "Deferoxamine is poorly absorbed by mouth, so it is given IV, IM or as a slow subcutaneous infusion over many hours. It works within minutes in the blood. Plasma enzymes break it down. The iron complex ferrioxamine leaves mainly in the urine, with some in bile. Its half-life is short, about 6 hours, which is why chronic overload treatment uses long, slow infusions on most days of the week.", "sidefx": "Hypotension with fast infusion is thought to involve histamine release, so infusion speed is controlled. Vin rose urine is harmless and shows that iron is being removed. ARDS is linked to prolonged high-dose IV use in acute poisoning. Retinal and hearing toxicity relate to long-term use, especially when iron stores are low. Yersinia and mucormycosis infections occur because these organisms can use ferrioxamine as an iron source. Injection site pain is common. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Deferoxamine is a bacterial weapon turned medicine: microbes make siderophores to steal iron, and doctors use one to pull iron out of people.", "src": ["Desferal (deferoxamine) FDA label", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "denosumab": {"origin": "Denosumab grew out of basic bone biology. In 1997 researchers, including a team at Amgen, discovered osteoprotegerin (OPG), a natural protein that protects bone. Soon after, its partner RANK ligand (RANKL) was identified as the key signal that tells osteoclasts to form and break down bone. Amgen designed denosumab, a fully human monoclonal antibody that acts like OPG and blocks RANKL. The 2009 FREEDOM trial showed it cut spine fractures sharply in women with osteoporosis. The FDA approved it in June 2010 as Prolia for osteoporosis and later that year as Xgeva to prevent bone complications from cancer that has spread to bone.", "timeline": [["1997", "Osteoprotegerin, a natural RANKL decoy that protects bone, is discovered."], ["2009", "The FREEDOM trial shows denosumab reduces fractures in postmenopausal osteoporosis."], ["2010", "FDA approves denosumab as Prolia for osteoporosis and Xgeva for bone metastases."], ["2024", "FDA approves the first denosumab biosimilars."]], "cell": "Osteoblasts and stromal cells in bone make RANKL, a protein that binds RANK receptors on osteoclast precursors. RANK signaling, through NF-kB and other pathways, drives these cells to fuse into mature osteoclasts and keeps them active and alive. Osteoclasts dissolve bone mineral and matrix. The body normally limits this with OPG, a decoy receptor that soaks up RANKL. Denosumab is a fully human IgG2 antibody that binds RANKL with high affinity and blocks it from reaching RANK. Osteoclast formation, function and survival drop, so bone resorption slows. Bone density rises in both cortical and trabecular bone, and fracture risk falls.", "body": [["Bones", "Reduces bone breakdown, increases bone density and lowers fracture risk. Prevents fractures and other bone events from bone metastases."], ["Blood calcium", "Less bone resorption releases less calcium, so blood calcium can drop, especially in kidney disease."], ["Jaw", "Rarely causes osteonecrosis of the jaw, usually after dental work or with high-dose cancer use."], ["Thigh bone", "Rarely causes atypical femur fractures with long-term use."], ["Skin and immune system", "Can cause skin infections such as cellulitis, eczema and rashes, possibly because RANKL also plays a role in immune cells."], ["Spine after stopping", "Stopping without follow-up therapy can cause rapid bone loss and multiple vertebral fractures."]], "journey": "Denosumab is given as a subcutaneous injection. It is absorbed slowly into the blood and peaks in about 10 days. As an antibody, it is broken down into amino acids by the reticuloendothelial system, not by the liver or kidneys, so kidney disease does not change its clearance. Bone breakdown markers fall within days. Its effect lasts about six months, which is why Prolia is given twice a year. Xgeva is given more often for cancer.", "sidefx": "Hypocalcemia happens because blocking osteoclasts stops calcium release from bone. It is more severe in advanced kidney disease and is prevented by correcting calcium and vitamin D first. The FDA added a boxed warning about severe hypocalcemia in patients on dialysis. Osteonecrosis of the jaw and atypical femur fractures relate to very low bone turnover. When it is stopped, osteoclast activity rebounds strongly, which can cause multiple spine fractures, so patients are switched to another drug such as a bisphosphonate. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Denosumab copies the action of osteoprotegerin, whose name means protector of bone.", "src": ["Prolia and Xgeva (denosumab) FDA labels", "Cummings SR et al. FREEDOM Trial, N Engl J Med 2009", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "desmopressin": {"origin": "Desmopressin is a redesigned version of vasopressin, the body's antidiuretic hormone. In 1953 Vincent du Vigneaud worked out and synthesized the structure of vasopressin and oxytocin, winning the 1955 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. In 1967 Milan Zaoral and colleagues in Prague made two changes to vasopressin. They removed the amino group from the first amino acid and swapped L-arginine for D-arginine at position 8. The result, 1-deamino-8-D-arginine vasopressin (DDAVP), lasted longer and acted almost only on the kidney, with little effect on blood pressure. In 1977 Pier Mannucci showed it could raise factor VIII and von Willebrand factor in people with mild hemophilia A and von Willebrand disease.", "timeline": [["1953", "Vincent du Vigneaud determines the structure of vasopressin."], ["1955", "Du Vigneaud receives the Nobel Prize in Chemistry."], ["1967", "Milan Zaoral and colleagues synthesize desmopressin in Prague."], ["1977", "Pier Mannucci shows desmopressin raises factor VIII and von Willebrand factor."]], "cell": "Desmopressin is a selective agonist at vasopressin V2 receptors. In the collecting duct of the kidney, V2 receptors are Gs-coupled. Activation raises cAMP and activates protein kinase A, which moves aquaporin-2 water channels into the cell membrane facing the urine. Water is pulled back into the blood, so urine becomes concentrated and output falls. On blood vessel endothelium, V2 activation causes release of stored von Willebrand factor and factor VIII from Weibel-Palade bodies. This improves platelet adhesion and clotting. Because it has little V1 activity, it causes little vasoconstriction.", "body": [["Kidneys", "Reduces urine output and concentrates urine. Treats central diabetes insipidus and nighttime bedwetting."], ["Blood clotting", "Raises von Willebrand factor and factor VIII, helping mild hemophilia A, type 1 von Willebrand disease and uremic platelet dysfunction."], ["Blood sodium", "Water retention dilutes the blood and can cause low sodium."], ["Brain", "Severe low sodium can cause headache, confusion, seizures and coma."], ["Blood vessels", "Can cause facial flushing and mild changes in blood pressure."]], "journey": "Desmopressin can be given IV, subcutaneously, as a nasal spray or by mouth. Oral bioavailability is very low because digestive enzymes break peptides down, so oral doses are much larger than nasal doses. Its antidiuretic effect lasts many hours. It is cleared mainly by the kidneys and has a half-life of about one and a half to three hours. Repeated doses for bleeding lose effect over days (tachyphylaxis) because endothelial stores run out.", "sidefx": "Hyponatremia is the main danger. The kidney holds onto water, and if the person keeps drinking, the blood becomes diluted, causing brain swelling and seizures. This is why fluid intake is limited and sodium is checked, especially in children and older adults. Headache and flushing come from vasodilation. Repeated doses for bleeding work less well as stored factor is used up. It is not used in nephrogenic diabetes insipidus because the kidney does not respond to V2 stimulation. There is no antidote. Treatment of low sodium is fluid restriction and sometimes hypertonic saline.", "fun": "DDAVP stands for 1-deamino-8-D-arginine vasopressin, the two tweaks that made it longer acting and kidney selective.", "src": ["DDAVP (desmopressin) FDA label", "Mannucci PM et al. Lancet 1977", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "desvenlafaxine": {"origin": "Desvenlafaxine is the main active metabolite of venlafaxine (Effexor), an SNRI antidepressant introduced by Wyeth in 1993. Venlafaxine is converted by the liver enzyme CYP2D6 into O-desmethylvenlafaxine, which carries most of the drug's effect. Wyeth developed this metabolite as its own drug, which avoids the variability of CYP2D6 activity between people. The FDA approved it in 2008 as Pristiq for major depressive disorder.", "timeline": [["1993", "FDA approves venlafaxine (Effexor), the parent drug."], ["2008", "FDA approves desvenlafaxine (Pristiq) for major depressive disorder."]], "cell": "Desvenlafaxine blocks the serotonin transporter (SERT) and the norepinephrine transporter (NET) on presynaptic neurons, with greater effect on serotonin reuptake. Both transmitters stay longer in the synapse. Over weeks, presynaptic autoreceptors such as 5-HT1A and alpha-2 desensitize, increasing the release of serotonin and norepinephrine in mood circuits. Downstream changes, including increased BDNF and neuroplasticity, are thought to explain the antidepressant effect. Higher norepinephrine also acts on blood vessels and the heart, which explains the rise in blood pressure. It has little action at muscarinic, histamine or alpha-adrenergic receptors.", "body": [["Brain", "Improves depression over several weeks. Can cause insomnia, dizziness and anxiety early on."], ["Heart and blood vessels", "Higher norepinephrine raises blood pressure and heart rate in a dose-related way."], ["Gut", "Nausea is common from serotonin acting on gut receptors."], ["Skin", "Increased sweating is common, reflecting serotonin and norepinephrine effects on sweat control."], ["Sexual function", "Can reduce libido and delay orgasm through serotonin effects in the brain and spinal cord."], ["Blood", "Reduced platelet serotonin raises bleeding risk with NSAIDs or anticoagulants."]], "journey": "Desvenlafaxine is taken by mouth as an extended-release tablet and is well absorbed, peaking in about 7.5 hours. It has low protein binding. Unlike venlafaxine, it does not need CYP2D6 activation. It is mostly conjugated by UGT enzymes, with a small part metabolized by CYP3A4, so it has few drug interactions. It leaves mainly in the urine, about half unchanged, so doses are reduced in kidney disease. Its half-life is about 11 hours. The empty tablet shell can appear in stool.", "sidefx": "Nausea, sweating and sexual problems come from serotonin effects outside the mood circuits. High blood pressure comes from norepinephrine reuptake blockade, so blood pressure is checked. Low sodium comes from SIADH. Bleeding risk comes from platelet serotonin depletion. Combined with other serotonergic drugs it can cause serotonin syndrome, treated with stopping the drugs, benzodiazepines and cyproheptadine. Stopping suddenly causes discontinuation symptoms such as dizziness, irritability and electric shock sensations because of its short half-life. It carries the antidepressant boxed warning about suicidality in young people.", "fun": "Desvenlafaxine is what venlafaxine becomes in the body, so taking it skips the CYP2D6 step that varies so much from person to person.", "src": ["Pristiq (desvenlafaxine) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "dexamethasone": {"origin": "Dexamethasone grew out of the discovery of cortisone. In 1948 Philip Hench at the Mayo Clinic gave cortisone to a woman with severe rheumatoid arthritis, and she walked again within days. Hench, Edward Kendall and Tadeus Reichstein shared the 1950 Nobel Prize. Cortisone and hydrocortisone also caused salt and water retention. Chemists then added a fluorine atom and a methyl group to the steroid skeleton to make dexamethasone, first synthesized in the late 1950s and approved for medical use in 1958. It is about 25 times more potent than hydrocortisone as an anti-inflammatory and has almost no salt-retaining effect. In 2020 the RECOVERY trial showed that dexamethasone lowered deaths in hospitalized COVID-19 patients who needed oxygen.", "timeline": [["1948", "Philip Hench first gives cortisone to a patient with rheumatoid arthritis."], ["1950", "Hench, Kendall and Reichstein share the Nobel Prize for adrenal hormone work."], ["1958", "Dexamethasone is introduced for medical use."], ["2020", "The RECOVERY trial shows dexamethasone lowers COVID-19 deaths in patients on oxygen."]], "cell": "Dexamethasone is a fat-soluble steroid that crosses the cell membrane and binds the glucocorticoid receptor in the cytoplasm. Binding releases heat shock proteins, and the drug-receptor complex moves into the nucleus. There it binds glucocorticoid response elements in DNA and turns on anti-inflammatory genes such as lipocortin (annexin A1), which inhibits phospholipase A2 and lowers prostaglandin and leukotriene production. The complex also blocks transcription factors NF-kB and AP-1, shutting down genes for cytokines such as IL-1, IL-6 and TNF, COX-2 and adhesion molecules. The result is less inflammation, fewer immune cells moving into tissues, less capillary leak and swelling, and suppression of the immune response.", "body": [["Immune system", "Suppresses inflammation and immune responses, which helps in asthma, allergic reactions, autoimmune disease and some cancers."], ["Airway and brain", "Reduces swelling, such as in croup, airway edema and swelling around brain tumors."], ["Blood sugar", "Raises blood sugar by increasing glucose production in the liver and reducing insulin sensitivity."], ["Stomach", "Raises the risk of ulcers and GI bleeding, especially with NSAIDs."], ["Brain and mood", "Can cause insomnia, mood swings, euphoria and sometimes psychosis."], ["Adrenal glands", "Long-term use suppresses the HPA axis, so the adrenal glands stop making cortisol."], ["Bones and muscles", "Long-term use causes bone loss, muscle weakness and slow wound healing."], ["Gut and vomiting center", "Prevents nausea and vomiting with chemotherapy."]], "journey": "Dexamethasone is well absorbed by mouth and is also given IV, IM, inhaled, in the eye and in joints. It distributes widely, crosses the blood-brain barrier and crosses the placenta, which is why it is used to speed fetal lung maturation. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP3A4, and it can induce CYP3A4 too. Metabolites leave in the urine. Its plasma half-life is a few hours, but because it changes gene expression, its biologic effects last 36 to 54 hours, making it a long-acting steroid.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from glucocorticoid receptor activation in tissues other than the target. High blood sugar comes from increased gluconeogenesis. Infection risk comes from immune suppression. Mood changes and insomnia reflect effects on the brain. Perineal burning or itching can follow fast IV push and is avoided by giving it slowly. Long-term use suppresses the adrenal glands, so stopping suddenly can cause adrenal crisis, and it must be tapered. Osteoporosis, cataracts, muscle wasting and Cushing-like features occur with long use. Because it has almost no mineralocorticoid effect, it causes little salt retention compared with hydrocortisone. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Dexamethasone is the drug used in the dexamethasone suppression test, which helps tell whether high cortisol comes from the pituitary or another source.", "src": ["Dexamethasone tablets and injection FDA labels", "RECOVERY Collaborative Group, N Engl J Med 2021", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "dexmedetomidine": {"origin": "Dexmedetomidine began in veterinary medicine. The Finnish company Orion Pharma developed medetomidine as a sedative for animals. It is a mix of two mirror-image forms, and the active dextro form is dexmedetomidine. Orion and Abbott developed it for people, and the FDA approved it in 1999 as Precedex for sedation of mechanically ventilated ICU patients. Its appeal was that patients could be sedated yet easily woken, with little effect on breathing. In 2022 a sublingual film, Igalmi, was approved for agitation in schizophrenia and bipolar disorder.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Orion Pharma develops medetomidine, a veterinary alpha-2 sedative."], ["1999", "FDA approves dexmedetomidine (Precedex) for ICU sedation."], ["2008", "FDA approves dexmedetomidine for procedural sedation of non-intubated patients."], ["2022", "FDA approves sublingual dexmedetomidine (Igalmi) for agitation."]], "cell": "Dexmedetomidine is a highly selective alpha-2 adrenergic agonist, much more selective than clonidine. Alpha-2 receptors are Gi-coupled. In the locus coeruleus of the brainstem, activation of alpha-2A receptors lowers cAMP, opens potassium channels and closes calcium channels, so neurons fire less and release less norepinephrine. Reduced locus coeruleus activity releases inhibition of sleep-promoting areas, producing a state similar to natural non-REM sleep. In the spinal cord, alpha-2 activation reduces pain transmission. Lower sympathetic outflow slows the heart and lowers blood pressure. It does not act on GABA receptors, so breathing drive is largely preserved.", "body": [["Brain", "Produces a light, arousable sedation and lowers anxiety. Patients can usually follow commands."], ["Breathing", "Causes little respiratory depression, so it can be used in patients who are not intubated."], ["Heart", "Slows the heart rate and can cause sinus arrest or heart block in susceptible patients."], ["Blood vessels", "Usually lowers blood pressure. A fast loading dose can briefly raise it through peripheral alpha-2B vasoconstriction."], ["Pain pathways", "Has mild pain-relieving effects and reduces opioid needs."], ["Mouth", "Causes dry mouth by reducing salivary secretion."]], "journey": "Dexmedetomidine is given IV as an infusion, and its effect starts within minutes. It is also absorbed through the lining of the mouth as a film. Its distribution half-life is about 6 minutes, and its elimination half-life is about two hours. The liver breaks it down almost completely by glucuronidation and by CYP2A6, so doses are lowered in liver disease. Metabolites leave in the urine. Kidney disease does not much change its clearance.", "sidefx": "Bradycardia and hypotension come from lower sympathetic tone and increased vagal activity. Fast loading doses can first raise blood pressure because high levels stimulate alpha-2B receptors in blood vessel muscle. Sinus arrest and heart block can occur, especially in patients with conduction disease or on other heart rate lowering drugs. Long infusions followed by sudden stopping can cause withdrawal with agitation, tachycardia and high blood pressure. Bradycardia is treated with atropine or glycopyrrolate and slowing the infusion. There is no reversal agent.", "fun": "Dexmedetomidine's sedation looks more like natural sleep on EEG than that of most other sedatives, which is why ICU patients on it can often be woken for a neuro exam.", "src": ["Precedex (dexmedetomidine) FDA label", "Igalmi (dexmedetomidine) FDA label", "Miller's Anesthesia"]}, "dexmethylphenidate": {"origin": "Dexmethylphenidate is the active half of methylphenidate. Methylphenidate was synthesized in 1944 by Leandro Panizzon at Ciba in Switzerland. His wife Marguerite, nicknamed Rita, took it to boost her tennis game, and the brand name Ritalin came from her name. Ritalin was approved in the US in 1955 and became the most famous ADHD medicine. Methylphenidate is a mix of mirror-image forms, and researchers found that the d-threo form does almost all the work. That form was developed as dexmethylphenidate, and the FDA approved it in 2001 as Focalin.", "timeline": [["1944", "Leandro Panizzon synthesizes methylphenidate at Ciba."], ["1955", "Methylphenidate (Ritalin) is approved in the US."], ["2001", "FDA approves dexmethylphenidate (Focalin)."], ["2005", "Extended-release dexmethylphenidate (Focalin XR) is approved."]], "cell": "Dexmethylphenidate blocks the dopamine transporter (DAT) and the norepinephrine transporter (NET) on presynaptic neurons. These transporters normally pull dopamine and norepinephrine back into the neuron after release. With reuptake blocked, both transmitters stay longer in the synapse, especially in the prefrontal cortex and striatum. In the prefrontal cortex, increased norepinephrine acting on alpha-2A receptors and dopamine acting on D1 receptors strengthen the signals that support attention, working memory and impulse control. Unlike amphetamine, it does not reverse the transporters or release stored transmitter in large amounts.", "body": [["Brain", "Improves attention and reduces impulsivity and hyperactivity in ADHD. Can cause insomnia, anxiety and irritability."], ["Appetite", "Reduces appetite, which can lead to weight loss and slowed growth in children."], ["Heart", "Raises heart rate and blood pressure through increased norepinephrine."], ["Blood vessels", "Can cause cold fingers and Raynaud-type changes from vasoconstriction."], ["Reward system", "Increased dopamine in reward circuits creates a risk of misuse and dependence, especially if snorted or injected."]], "journey": "Dexmethylphenidate is taken by mouth and absorbed well. The immediate-release form works within about 30 minutes, and the extended-release form uses a mix of beads for longer action. It crosses the blood-brain barrier easily. It is not processed much by CYP enzymes. Instead, carboxylesterase 1 in the liver removes its ester to form inactive ritalinic acid, which leaves in the urine. Its half-life is about two to four and a half hours.", "sidefx": "Insomnia, low appetite, anxiety and headache come from increased catecholamine signaling in the brain. Raised heart rate and blood pressure come from norepinephrine in the heart and vessels, and serious cardiac events are rare but possible in people with heart disease. It is a Schedule II drug with a boxed warning for abuse and dependence because it raises dopamine in reward pathways. It can rarely cause psychosis or mania. With MAO inhibitors it can cause a hypertensive crisis. Overdose is managed with benzodiazepines and supportive care. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Ritalin was named after Rita, the wife of the chemist who first made methylphenidate.", "src": ["Focalin and Focalin XR (dexmethylphenidate) FDA labels", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "diazepam": {"origin": "Diazepam was created by Leo Sternbach, a chemist at Hoffmann-La Roche in New Jersey. In 1955 he made a compound that sat on a shelf until a colleague tested it in 1957 and found it calmed animals without heavy sedation. That compound became chlordiazepoxide (Librium), approved in 1960, the first benzodiazepine. Sternbach then made diazepam, which was more potent, and it was approved in 1963 as Valium. It was the best-selling drug in the US from the late 1960s into the early 1980s. Over time, concerns about dependence grew, and benzodiazepines became controlled substances. Today diazepam remains important for seizures, alcohol withdrawal and muscle spasm.", "timeline": [["1955", "Leo Sternbach makes the compound that becomes chlordiazepoxide."], ["1960", "Chlordiazepoxide (Librium), the first benzodiazepine, is approved."], ["1963", "Diazepam (Valium) is approved in the US."], ["1975", "Benzodiazepines are placed in Schedule IV in the US."], ["2020", "FDA adds a boxed warning on abuse, dependence and withdrawal for benzodiazepines."]], "cell": "GABA is the brain's main inhibitory neurotransmitter. When GABA binds the GABA-A receptor, a chloride channel opens, chloride flows into the neuron and the cell becomes harder to excite. Diazepam binds a separate site on the GABA-A receptor, between the alpha and gamma subunits. This is a positive allosteric modulator site. Diazepam increases how often the channel opens in response to GABA, so more chloride enters and neurons are more inhibited. It does not open the channel without GABA, which is why it is safer alone than barbiturates. Receptors with alpha-1 subunits mediate sedation and amnesia, alpha-2 and alpha-3 mediate anxiety relief and muscle relaxation.", "body": [["Brain", "Stops seizures, reduces anxiety, causes sedation and memory loss for events during its peak effect."], ["Spinal cord and muscle", "Increases presynaptic inhibition in the spinal cord, relaxing muscle spasm."], ["Breathing", "Mild effect alone, but combined with opioids or alcohol it can cause dangerous respiratory depression."], ["Heart and vessels", "Can lower blood pressure, especially with fast IV push or in volume-depleted patients."], ["Withdrawal states", "Calms the overactive nervous system in alcohol withdrawal and prevents withdrawal seizures."]], "journey": "Diazepam can be given IV, by mouth, rectally or as a nasal spray. IM absorption is erratic. It is very fat soluble, so it enters the brain within minutes, which makes it fast for seizures. It then redistributes into fat, so its seizure effect wears off in less than an hour even though the drug stays in the body. The liver breaks it down by CYP3A4 and CYP2C19 into desmethyldiazepam, an active metabolite with a half-life up to about 100 hours. This long tail causes buildup, especially in older adults and in liver disease. Metabolites leave in the urine. It crosses the placenta and enters breast milk.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness, unsteadiness and confusion come from enhanced GABA inhibition in the brain and cerebellum. Respiratory depression is greatest when combined with other CNS depressants, which is the focus of the boxed warning with opioids. Older adults are prone to falls and delirium because of the long-acting metabolite. Long-term use causes tolerance and dependence, and stopping suddenly can cause withdrawal seizures. The IV form contains propylene glycol and can irritate veins. Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepine effects but can trigger seizures in dependent patients, so supportive airway care is the mainstay in overdose.", "fun": "The first benzodiazepine sat untested on a lab shelf for about two years before a coworker of Leo Sternbach tried it during a cleanup.", "src": ["Valium (diazepam) FDA label", "FDA Drug Safety Communication on benzodiazepines, 2020", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "diclofenac": {"origin": "Diclofenac was designed in a lab rather than found in nature. In the 1960s chemists Alfred Sallmann and Rudolf Pfister at the Swiss company Ciba-Geigy set out to build an anti-inflammatory drug from scratch. They studied the shape of existing NSAIDs and reasoned that a molecule with two bulky chlorine atoms that twisted its rings out of line would fit the COX enzyme well. The result was diclofenac, introduced as Voltaren in the 1970s. It became one of the most widely used NSAIDs in the world. In the US the tablets were approved in 1988, and the topical gel later made it possible to treat a sore joint with much less drug reaching the blood.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Sallmann and Pfister at Ciba-Geigy design diclofenac by rational structure study."], ["1970s", "Diclofenac is launched as Voltaren outside the US."], ["1988", "FDA approves Voltaren tablets in the United States."], ["2007", "FDA approves diclofenac gel for joint pain from osteoarthritis."], ["2020", "Diclofenac gel becomes available over the counter in the US."]], "cell": "Diclofenac blocks cyclooxygenase, the enzyme that turns arachidonic acid (released from cell membranes by phospholipase A2) into prostaglandin H2. It inhibits both COX-1 and COX-2, with some preference for COX-2. Without prostaglandin H2, cells make less PGE2 and prostacyclin, which normally sensitize pain nerve endings, dilate vessels and drive swelling at sites of injury. Less PGE2 in the hypothalamus also lowers fever. Blocking COX-1 in the stomach lining reduces protective prostaglandins, and blocking COX-2 in blood vessel walls lowers prostacyclin while platelet thromboxane is less affected, which tilts the balance toward clotting.", "body": [["Joints and soft tissue", "Less prostaglandin at the injured site means less swelling, redness and pain, which is why it helps arthritis and sprains."], ["Nerves", "Pain nerve endings become less sensitive because PGE2 normally lowers their firing threshold."], ["Stomach", "Loss of COX-1 prostaglandins thins the protective mucus and bicarbonate layer and reduces blood flow, so ulcers and bleeding can occur."], ["Kidneys", "Prostaglandins keep the afferent arterioles open when blood flow is low. Blocking them can drop kidney filtration, especially with dehydration, ACE inhibitors or diuretics."], ["Heart and vessels", "Reduced prostacyclin with continued thromboxane raises the risk of clots, heart attack and stroke, and salt retention can raise blood pressure."], ["Liver", "Diclofenac causes liver enzyme rises more often than many other NSAIDs, likely from reactive metabolites made in liver cells."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth, diclofenac is absorbed quickly, but a good part is removed by the liver on its first pass. It is very tightly bound to albumin in the blood and moves into the joint fluid, where levels stay up longer than in plasma. The liver breaks it down, mainly by CYP2C9 hydroxylation and then glucuronidation, and the inactive products leave mostly in urine with some in bile. The plasma half-life is short, about two hours. Gel rubbed on the skin gives much lower blood levels than pills.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come straight from losing prostaglandins. In the stomach, less protective mucus leads to ulcers and bleeding. In the kidney, loss of vessel-opening prostaglandins can cause acute kidney injury and fluid retention. Unbalanced COX-2 blockade favors thromboxane, which explains the boxed warning for heart attack and stroke. Liver injury is thought to come from reactive quinone-imine and acyl-glucuronide metabolites. There is no specific antidote, so care is supportive.", "fun": "Diclofenac given to cattle in South Asia nearly wiped out several vulture species in the 1990s and 2000s. Vultures that ate treated carcasses developed fatal kidney failure, so veterinary diclofenac was banned in India, Pakistan and Nepal.", "src": ["Voltaren (diclofenac) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "dicloxacillin": {"origin": "Dicloxacillin is a grandchild of Alexander Fleming's penicillin, which he noticed in 1928 when a Penicillium mold killed staphylococci on a culture plate. By the 1950s many staph strains made penicillinase, an enzyme that breaks penicillin apart. In 1957 scientists at Beecham Research Laboratories in England isolated 6-aminopenicillanic acid, the bare penicillin core. That let chemists attach new side chains. They first made methicillin, then a family of isoxazolyl penicillins (oxacillin, cloxacillin and dicloxacillin) in the early 1960s. These had bulky side chains that shield the drug from penicillinase and survive stomach acid, giving doctors an oral option for staph skin infections.", "timeline": [["1928", "Alexander Fleming observes penicillin killing staphylococci."], ["1957", "Beecham scientists isolate the penicillin core, 6-aminopenicillanic acid."], ["1960s", "Isoxazolyl penicillins including dicloxacillin are developed and introduced."], ["1961", "The first MRSA strains are reported in England soon after methicillin enters use."]], "cell": "Dicloxacillin is a beta-lactam. Its four-membered beta-lactam ring mimics the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of the peptide chains that bacteria cross-link to build peptidoglycan. Penicillin-binding proteins (transpeptidases) attack the ring and become permanently acylated, so they can no longer cross-link the cell wall. The weakened wall cannot resist the high pressure inside the cell, and autolysin enzymes add to the damage, so the bacterium bursts. The bulky dichlorophenyl isoxazolyl side chain blocks staphylococcal penicillinase from reaching the ring. MRSA escapes because its altered PBP2a, made from the mecA gene, binds beta-lactams poorly.", "body": [["Skin and soft tissue", "Kills methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus and streptococci, which cause most cellulitis, impetigo and wound infections."], ["Bacterial cells", "Only dividing bacteria building new wall are killed; human cells have no peptidoglycan, which is why the drug is so selective."], ["Gut", "Changes in normal gut flora cause nausea and diarrhea and can let Clostridioides difficile overgrow."], ["Kidneys", "Rarely an immune reaction in the kidney tissue causes acute interstitial nephritis with fever, rash and rising creatinine."], ["Immune system", "The beta-lactam ring can bind body proteins and act as an allergen, causing rash or, rarely, anaphylaxis."]], "journey": "Dicloxacillin is acid stable, so it can be swallowed, but food lowers absorption and it is best taken on an empty stomach. It is very highly protein bound, so only a small free fraction acts at any moment. It spreads into skin, bone and soft tissue but not well into the brain. Part is metabolized in the liver and the rest is cleared unchanged by the kidneys and in bile. The half-life is short, under an hour, which is why it is taken several times a day.", "sidefx": "Allergy is the main concern because the beta-lactam ring reacts with human proteins to form immune targets, ranging from rash to anaphylaxis, and cross-reaction with other penicillins is expected. Killing normal gut bacteria explains diarrhea and C. difficile colitis. Interstitial nephritis and liver injury are rare immune-mediated reactions. There is no antidote; epinephrine treats anaphylaxis.", "fun": "Dicloxacillin is a favorite board pick for an outpatient purulent skin infection when MRSA is unlikely, because it is one of the few oral anti-staph penicillins.", "src": ["Dicloxacillin FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "dicyclomine": {"origin": "Dicyclomine (called dicycloverine outside the US) was made in the mid-1940s by chemists at the William S. Merrell Company in Cincinnati. Drug companies were looking for synthetic versions of belladonna alkaloids like atropine that would calm cramping gut muscle with fewer side effects. Dicyclomine was approved in the US around 1950 and sold as Bentyl. It remains a common choice for the cramping pain of irritable bowel syndrome.", "timeline": [["1940s", "Merrell chemists synthesize dicyclomine."], ["1950", "Dicyclomine is approved for medical use in the United States."], ["1956", "Merrell launches Bendectin for morning sickness with dicyclomine as one ingredient."], ["1976", "Dicyclomine is removed from Bendectin as adding no benefit."]], "cell": "Dicyclomine is a competitive antagonist at muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, especially the M1 and M3 subtypes. In gut smooth muscle, acetylcholine from enteric nerves normally binds M3 receptors, activates Gq, raises IP3 and releases calcium to cause contraction. By blocking this signal, dicyclomine reduces the strength and frequency of spasms. It also has a direct relaxing effect on smooth muscle that does not depend on acetylcholine, which adds to its antispasmodic action.", "body": [["Gut", "Calms cramping and spasm of intestinal smooth muscle, easing the colicky pain of irritable bowel syndrome."], ["Mouth and skin", "Blocks M3 receptors on salivary and sweat glands, causing dry mouth and reduced sweating."], ["Eyes", "Relaxes the iris sphincter and the ciliary muscle, causing blurred near vision and light sensitivity."], ["Bladder", "Relaxes the detrusor muscle, which can cause urinary retention, especially with an enlarged prostate."], ["Brain", "Some drug enters the brain and can cause dizziness, drowsiness or confusion, especially in older adults."]], "journey": "Dicyclomine is absorbed well from the gut and reaches its peak in about an hour or two. It can also be given as an intramuscular shot, but never into a vein. It spreads into tissues and some crosses into the brain. It is metabolized in the liver and leaves mostly in the urine. The effect lasts several hours, so it is usually taken a few times a day.", "sidefx": "Nearly all side effects are anticholinergic and follow from blocking muscarinic receptors outside the gut: dry mouth, blurred vision, constipation, urinary retention, fast heart rate and reduced sweating with heat intolerance. In the brain this can cause confusion and delirium in older adults. Overdose produces the classic anticholinergic toxidrome. Physostigmine, a cholinesterase inhibitor that crosses into the brain, can reverse severe central toxicity in selected cases.", "fun": "Dicyclomine is not recommended for infants younger than six months; its label warns of serious breathing problems, seizures and other reactions reported in young infants.", "src": ["Bentyl (dicyclomine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "digoxin": {"origin": "Digoxin comes from the woolly foxglove plant, Digitalis lanata. Foxglove leaf was a folk remedy in England for dropsy, the swelling caused by heart failure. In 1785 the physician William Withering published An Account of the Foxglove, a careful record of how he dosed the leaf and what happened to his patients. It is one of the earliest examples of a drug studied in a systematic way. In 1930 Sydney Smith at Burroughs Wellcome isolated the pure glycoside digoxin from Digitalis lanata. That gave doctors a consistent, measurable drug instead of a leaf powder whose strength varied from batch to batch.", "timeline": [["1785", "William Withering publishes An Account of the Foxglove, describing foxglove for dropsy."], ["1930", "Sydney Smith isolates digoxin from Digitalis lanata at Burroughs Wellcome."], ["1960s", "Blood level testing makes it possible to watch for toxicity."], ["1997", "The DIG trial shows digoxin reduces heart failure hospitalizations without changing overall survival."]], "cell": "Digoxin binds to and inhibits the sodium-potassium ATPase pump on the outside of heart muscle cells. With the pump slowed, sodium builds up inside the cell. That weakens the gradient that drives the sodium-calcium exchanger, so less calcium leaves the cell and more is loaded into the sarcoplasmic reticulum. More calcium released with each beat means a stronger contraction (positive inotropy). Separately, digoxin increases vagal (parasympathetic) tone, which slows the SA node and slows conduction through the AV node (negative chronotropy and dromotropy).", "body": [["Heart muscle", "Each beat squeezes harder because more calcium is available inside the cells, so the heart pumps more blood with each contraction."], ["AV node", "Extra vagal tone slows signals through the AV node, which lowers the ventricular rate in atrial fibrillation."], ["Kidneys", "Better cardiac output improves blood flow to the kidneys. The kidneys are also the main way digoxin leaves the body, so kidney disease raises levels."], ["Eyes and brain", "At high levels it can cause blurred or yellow-green vision, halos, confusion and fatigue."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and loss of appetite are common early signs of too much digoxin."], ["Blood potassium", "In an acute overdose, widespread pump blockade keeps potassium outside cells, so blood potassium rises."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth, digoxin is absorbed in the small intestine and spreads widely into tissues, especially skeletal and heart muscle, so blood levels are only a small part of the total in the body. It works slowly: the full effect builds over several hours. Little is metabolized. It is mostly cleared unchanged by the kidneys, partly through the P-glycoprotein pump, with a long half-life of about one and a half to two days. This is why kidney function and blood levels guide therapy, and why P-glycoprotein blockers such as amiodarone and verapamil raise levels.", "sidefx": "The same pump blockade that strengthens the heart can overload heart cells with calcium and make them too excitable, causing extra beats, atrial tachycardia with block and bidirectional ventricular tachycardia. Low potassium makes this worse because potassium and digoxin compete for the same spot on the pump. The vagal effect explains slow heart rates and heart block. Nausea and vision changes reflect effects on the brain's vomiting center and the retina. Severe toxicity is reversed with digoxin immune fab.", "fun": "The yellow tint some people see during digoxin toxicity is called xanthopsia. Some art historians have speculated it influenced Van Gogh's yellow period, though this is unproven.", "src": ["Lanoxin (digoxin) FDA label", "Withering W. An Account of the Foxglove, 1785", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "digoxin-immune-fab": {"origin": "Digoxin immune fab grew out of work at Massachusetts General Hospital in the late 1960s, where Thomas Smith, Edgar Haber and Vincent Butler learned to raise antibodies against digoxin. Digoxin is too small to trigger antibodies alone, so it was attached to a carrier protein and injected into animals. The team then cut the antibodies with the enzyme papain to keep only the small antigen-binding Fab pieces, which are less likely to cause allergy and are cleared by the kidneys. In 1976 they reported reversing life-threatening digoxin poisoning in a patient. The product, made in sheep, was approved in the US in 1986 as Digibind. The current product is DigiFab.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Smith, Haber and Butler at Massachusetts General Hospital develop antibodies to digoxin."], ["1976", "First reported reversal of severe digoxin toxicity with digoxin-specific Fab fragments."], ["1986", "FDA approves Digibind."], ["2001", "FDA approves DigiFab, the ovine Fab product used today."]], "cell": "Each Fab fragment has one antigen-binding site that grabs digoxin (and digitoxin) with an affinity far higher than digoxin has for its target, the sodium-potassium ATPase. Fab binds free digoxin in the blood, dropping the free level to near zero. This creates a gradient that pulls digoxin off the pumps in heart and other tissues, back into the blood, where it is bound in turn. With the pumps freed, sodium and potassium handling returns to normal, cells become less excitable and potassium moves back into cells. The digoxin-Fab complexes are inactive and are cleared by the kidneys.", "body": [["Heart rhythm", "Toxic rhythms such as ventricular tachycardia and high-grade AV block usually improve within about half an hour to an hour."], ["Blood potassium", "Freed pumps move potassium back into cells, so high potassium falls and can overshoot into low potassium."], ["Heart pumping", "Removing digoxin's inotropic support can unmask or worsen heart failure in patients who depended on it."], ["AV node", "In atrial fibrillation, loss of digoxin's vagal effect can allow a fast ventricular rate."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys clear the bound complexes. In kidney failure complexes linger, and digoxin can be released again later."], ["Immune system", "Because it is made from sheep protein, allergic reactions can occur, especially in people allergic to sheep products or papain."]], "journey": "Digoxin immune fab is given into a vein. It works fast, with rhythm improvement often seen within an hour. It spreads through the blood and the fluid around cells, where it binds digoxin drawn out of tissues. The complexes leave through the kidneys, with a half-life of about fifteen to twenty hours in normal kidney function and much longer in kidney failure. After treatment, standard digoxin blood tests become hard to interpret because they measure bound plus free drug.", "sidefx": "Most problems come from suddenly taking digoxin's effects away. Potassium drops as pumps restart, so potassium must be watched. Heart failure can worsen without the inotropic boost, and rate control in atrial fibrillation can be lost. Allergic reactions reflect the sheep-derived protein. Measured total digoxin levels rise after treatment because bound digoxin is still counted, which does not mean toxicity is worse.", "fun": "Fab stands for fragment antigen-binding. Removing the Fc tail makes the fragment small enough to filter through the kidney and less able to trigger immune reactions.", "src": ["DigiFab (digoxin immune fab) FDA label", "Smith TW et al. N Engl J Med 1976", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]}, "diltiazem": {"origin": "Diltiazem was discovered in Japan by the drug company Tanabe Seiyaku in the late 1960s and 1970s, as researchers searched for new drugs to treat angina. It turned out to belong to a new chemical class, the benzothiazepines, distinct from verapamil and the dihydropyridines like nifedipine. Its action fit the then-new idea, championed by German physiologist Albrecht Fleckenstein, that blocking calcium entry into heart and vessel cells could relieve angina. Diltiazem was approved in the US in 1982 as Cardizem and is now widely used for angina, high blood pressure and rapid atrial fibrillation.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Albrecht Fleckenstein describes calcium antagonism as a drug action."], ["1970s", "Tanabe Seiyaku develops diltiazem in Japan."], ["1982", "Diltiazem is approved in the United States."], ["1990s", "IV diltiazem becomes a common choice for rate control in atrial fibrillation."]], "cell": "Diltiazem blocks L-type (Cav1.2) voltage-gated calcium channels, binding from inside the cell when the channel is open or inactivated. This use-dependence means it works more in tissue that fires often, like the AV node during a fast rhythm. In SA and AV nodal cells, the upstroke of the action potential depends on calcium current, so blocking it slows the SA node rate, slows AV conduction and lengthens AV refractoriness. In heart muscle, less calcium entry means less calcium-induced calcium release and weaker contraction. In arterial smooth muscle, less calcium means less calcium-calmodulin activation of myosin light-chain kinase, so arteries relax. Its effects on heart and vessels are intermediate between verapamil and the dihydropyridines.", "body": [["AV node", "Slows conduction through the AV node, which controls the ventricular rate in atrial fibrillation and flutter and can end AV nodal reentrant tachycardia."], ["SA node", "Slows the sinus rate, which lowers heart rate and oxygen demand."], ["Heart muscle", "Mildly weakens contraction, which helps reduce oxygen demand in angina but can worsen heart failure with reduced ejection fraction."], ["Arteries", "Relaxes peripheral and coronary arteries, lowering blood pressure and relieving coronary spasm."], ["Veins and legs", "Arteriolar dilation without matching venous dilation raises capillary pressure and causes ankle swelling."], ["Gut", "Less calcium in gut smooth muscle can cause constipation."]], "journey": "By mouth, diltiazem is well absorbed but much is removed by the liver on first pass, so only about forty percent reaches the blood. Extended-release forms allow once-daily use. IV diltiazem works within a few minutes. It is metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4, and it also inhibits CYP3A4, raising levels of drugs like some statins. The half-life is about three to four and a half hours for the immediate-release drug. Metabolites leave in urine and feces.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow from calcium channel blockade. Too much AV and SA nodal slowing causes bradycardia and heart block, especially with beta blockers. Weaker contraction can worsen heart failure. Vasodilation causes low blood pressure, headache, flushing and ankle edema. It should not be used for wide-complex tachycardia or atrial fibrillation with an accessory pathway, because slowing the AV node can push impulses down the pathway. Overdose is treated with calcium, vasopressors and high-dose insulin therapy.", "fun": "Diltiazem and verapamil are the two non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers, a board favorite because they are the ones that slow the heart rate.", "src": ["Cardizem (diltiazem) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "dimercaprol": {"origin": "Dimercaprol was invented as a wartime antidote. Before World War II, Britain feared attack with Lewisite, an arsenic-containing blister gas. Around 1940 at Oxford, biochemist Rudolph Peters with Lloyd Stocken and Robert Thompson worked out that arsenic poisons cells by grabbing pairs of sulfur (thiol) groups on enzymes. They designed a small molecule with two neighboring thiol groups that would grab arsenic even more tightly. They called it British Anti-Lewisite, or BAL. Lewisite was never used in the war, but BAL became the first modern chelating antidote for heavy metal poisoning.", "timeline": [["1918", "Lewisite, an arsenical blister agent, is developed as a chemical weapon."], ["1940", "Peters, Stocken and Thompson at Oxford develop British Anti-Lewisite."], ["1940s", "BAL comes into medical use for arsenic, mercury and gold poisoning."]], "cell": "Arsenic and mercury poison cells by binding sulfhydryl (thiol) groups on enzymes. Trivalent arsenic is especially toxic because it bridges two neighboring thiols, such as those of lipoic acid in pyruvate dehydrogenase, shutting down cell energy production. Dimercaprol has two adjacent thiol groups on a small, fat-soluble molecule. It binds the metal to form a stable ring-shaped chelate that the metal prefers over the enzyme. This pulls the metal off tissue proteins, restores enzyme function and turns the metal into a complex that can be excreted in urine and bile. Because the complex can come apart, enough drug must be present to keep the metal bound.", "body": [["Cells and enzymes", "Freeing thiol enzymes such as pyruvate dehydrogenase restores energy production in cells poisoned by arsenic."], ["Kidneys", "Most chelated metal leaves in urine. Keeping urine alkaline helps protect the kidneys from the complex coming apart."], ["Brain", "Because it is fat soluble, dimercaprol reaches the brain. This helps in some poisonings but can move some metals, like methylmercury, into the brain, so it is avoided there."], ["Heart and vessels", "Commonly causes a rise in blood pressure and heart rate shortly after a dose."], ["Blood", "Can cause hemolysis in people with G6PD deficiency."], ["Injection site", "The painful intramuscular shot is given in peanut oil, so it is avoided in people with peanut allergy."]], "journey": "Dimercaprol is an oily liquid and is given only by deep intramuscular injection. It is absorbed within about half an hour and spreads widely, including into the brain and inside cells. It is metabolized quickly and the drug and its metal complexes leave in urine and bile within hours, so it is given in repeated doses. It is often used together with another chelator, such as with calcium EDTA in severe lead poisoning in children.", "sidefx": "Many side effects come from its own reactive thiol groups and its rapid effects on vessels: high blood pressure, fast heart rate, nausea, headache, burning of the lips and mouth, and a feeling of tightness in the chest. Injection pain is common. Hemolysis can occur in G6PD deficiency. It must not be used for iron, cadmium or selenium poisoning, because the complexes it forms with these metals are more toxic, especially to the kidneys.", "fun": "The name BAL, short for British Anti-Lewisite, is still used in hospitals today, decades after the war gas it was made for became a relic.", "src": ["BAL in Oil (dimercaprol) FDA label", "Peters RA, Stocken LA, Thompson RHS. Nature 1945", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]}, "dinoprostone": {"origin": "Dinoprostone is the body's own prostaglandin E2 made as a drug. In the 1930s the Swedish physiologist Ulf von Euler found a substance in semen that made smooth muscle contract and named it prostaglandin, thinking it came from the prostate. In the 1950s and 1960s Sune Bergström and Bengt Samuelsson at the Karolinska Institute purified the prostaglandins and worked out their structures, and they shared the 1982 Nobel Prize with John Vane. Chemists such as E. J. Corey then found ways to make them in the lab. Synthetic PGE2 came into obstetric use in the 1970s to ripen the cervix and start labor.", "timeline": [["1930s", "Ulf von Euler discovers and names prostaglandin."], ["1960s", "Bergström and Samuelsson determine the structures of prostaglandins."], ["1970s", "Dinoprostone comes into obstetric use."], ["1982", "Bergström, Samuelsson and Vane receive the Nobel Prize for prostaglandin research."], ["1995", "The FDA approves the Cervidil vaginal insert."]], "cell": "Dinoprostone is prostaglandin E2. It binds EP receptors, a family of G protein-coupled receptors. In the cervix, signaling through EP2 and EP4 raises cAMP and triggers remodeling of the connective tissue. Collagenase and other enzymes break down collagen, water and glycosaminoglycans increase, and the cervix becomes soft, thin and able to open. In the uterine muscle, EP1 and EP3 signaling raises intracellular calcium and lowers cAMP, which increases contractions and makes the uterus more responsive to oxytocin.", "body": [["Cervix", "Softens, thins and begins to open the cervix, called ripening, which makes induction of labor more likely to succeed."], ["Uterus", "Increases contractions. Too much activity, called tachysystole, can reduce blood flow to the placenta."], ["Fetus", "Very frequent contractions can lower oxygen delivery and change the fetal heart rate pattern."], ["Gut", "Stimulates gut smooth muscle, causing nausea, vomiting and diarrhea."], ["Temperature", "PGE2 acts on the hypothalamus to raise the temperature set point, so fever can occur."]], "journey": "Dinoprostone is placed in the vagina, near the cervix, as a gel, a suppository or a slow-release insert on a string. It acts locally and is absorbed into the blood. The body inactivates prostaglandins very quickly, mostly during passage through the lungs, so the blood half-life is only minutes. The metabolites are cleared by the kidneys. The insert can be pulled out if contractions become too strong, which stops new drug release.", "sidefx": "Side effects mirror the drug's job. Excess uterine stimulation can cause tachysystole, fetal heart rate changes and, rarely, uterine rupture, which is more likely with a prior uterine scar. Its action on gut smooth muscle causes nausea, vomiting and diarrhea, and its hypothalamic effect causes fever. There is no antidote; removing the insert and giving a tocolytic such as terbutaline can calm the uterus.", "fun": "The name prostaglandin is a historical mistake. Most of the prostaglandin in semen comes from the seminal vesicles, not the prostate, but the name stuck.", "src": ["Cervidil (dinoprostone) FDA label", "Prepidil (dinoprostone) FDA label", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1982 materials"]}, "dipyridamole": {"origin": "Dipyridamole was developed by the German company Dr. Karl Thomae, part of Boehringer Ingelheim, and introduced around 1959 as Persantin, a coronary vasodilator for angina. It did not prove very useful for angina, but researchers then found it made platelets less sticky. Later it was paired with aspirin in an extended-release capsule (Aggrenox) to prevent a second stroke, approved in the US in 1999 after the ESPS-2 trial. Its ability to widen healthy coronary arteries also made the IV form useful for nuclear stress testing in people who cannot exercise.", "timeline": [["1959", "Dipyridamole is introduced as Persantin for angina."], ["1980s", "IV dipyridamole is used as a pharmacologic stress agent for heart imaging."], ["1996", "The ESPS-2 trial reports benefit of dipyridamole plus aspirin after stroke."], ["1999", "FDA approves Aggrenox (aspirin and extended-release dipyridamole)."]], "cell": "Dipyridamole raises the level of adenosine outside cells by blocking the equilibrative nucleoside transporter ENT1 on red blood cells and endothelium, which normally takes adenosine back up, and by inhibiting adenosine deaminase. The extra adenosine acts on A2A receptors, which couple to Gs and raise cAMP. In platelets, more cAMP reduces calcium release and blocks activation and aggregation. In coronary smooth muscle, more cAMP causes relaxation and dilation. Dipyridamole also inhibits platelet phosphodiesterases (PDE3 and PDE5), so cAMP and cGMP break down more slowly, adding to both effects.", "body": [["Platelets", "Higher cAMP makes platelets less likely to stick and clump, which lowers the chance of clots forming on damaged vessel walls."], ["Coronary arteries", "Adenosine strongly dilates small coronary arteries. Healthy vessels open wide while narrowed ones cannot, which reveals blockages on stress imaging."], ["Brain vessels", "Combined with aspirin, the antiplatelet effect helps prevent repeat ischemic stroke."], ["Head", "Dilation of vessels in the head causes headache, which is the most common reason people stop the drug."], ["Lungs", "Adenosine can tighten the airways, so IV dipyridamole can cause bronchospasm in people with asthma."], ["Blood pressure", "Widespread vasodilation can cause flushing, dizziness and low blood pressure."]], "journey": "By mouth, dipyridamole absorption varies and depends on stomach acid. The extended-release form in Aggrenox spreads the effect through the day. Given IV for stress testing, it acts within minutes. It is highly bound to plasma proteins. The liver joins it to glucuronic acid, and it leaves mainly in bile and feces. The terminal half-life is about ten hours.", "sidefx": "Headache, flushing and dizziness all come from adenosine-driven vasodilation. Chest pain can happen because adenosine dilates healthy coronary vessels and diverts blood away from narrowed ones, called coronary steal. Bronchospasm occurs because adenosine constricts airways in susceptible people. These effects during stress testing are reversed by aminophylline, which blocks adenosine receptors. Bleeding risk mostly comes from the aspirin partner in Aggrenox.", "fun": "Caffeine and theophylline block adenosine receptors, so patients are told to avoid coffee before a dipyridamole stress test or the test may not work.", "src": ["Persantine (dipyridamole) FDA label", "Aggrenox FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "disulfiram": {"origin": "Disulfiram was used to make rubber long before it was a medicine, and rubber workers were noticed to feel ill after drinking alcohol. Its use for alcoholism came from an accident in Copenhagen in the mid-1940s. Erik Jacobsen and Jens Hald at the drug company Medicinalco were testing it as a treatment for intestinal worms and took it themselves. They then became sick after drinking alcohol at social events. They realized the drug made alcohol unpleasant and published their findings in 1948. Disulfiram, sold as Antabuse, was approved in the US in 1951 and was the first medicine approved to treat alcohol use disorder.", "timeline": [["1900s", "Disulfiram is used in the rubber industry to speed vulcanization."], ["1937", "A US physician reports that rubber workers exposed to disulfiram react badly to alcohol."], ["1948", "Jacobsen and Hald in Denmark publish its use as an alcohol deterrent."], ["1951", "FDA approves disulfiram (Antabuse) for alcoholism."]], "cell": "Ethanol is converted by alcohol dehydrogenase into acetaldehyde, which aldehyde dehydrogenase (mainly ALDH2 in mitochondria) then turns into harmless acetate. Disulfiram and its metabolites, including diethyldithiocarbamate, irreversibly block aldehyde dehydrogenase by reacting with a cysteine thiol in its active site. If the person drinks, acetaldehyde builds up five to ten times higher than normal. Acetaldehyde dilates blood vessels and causes flushing, throbbing headache, nausea, vomiting, fast heart rate and low blood pressure. Disulfiram also inhibits dopamine beta-hydroxylase, which lowers norepinephrine and raises dopamine; this may add to the low blood pressure and to psychiatric effects.", "body": [["Liver enzymes", "Aldehyde dehydrogenase stays blocked until the liver makes new enzyme, which takes up to two weeks after stopping."], ["Skin and vessels", "After alcohol, acetaldehyde causes intense flushing, sweating and vasodilation."], ["Heart", "The reaction causes fast heart rate, palpitations and low blood pressure, and in severe cases chest pain, arrhythmia or shock."], ["Gut", "Acetaldehyde causes nausea and vomiting, which helps make drinking aversive."], ["Brain and nerves", "Can cause drowsiness, a metallic or garlic taste, and with long use peripheral neuropathy or optic neuritis; psychosis may relate to higher dopamine."], ["Liver", "Rarely causes severe hepatitis, so liver tests are checked."]], "journey": "Disulfiram is taken by mouth and absorbed slowly. It is fat soluble and stored in fat tissue. In the blood and liver it is quickly reduced to diethyldithiocarbamate, which is then broken down further, including to carbon disulfide, which is partly breathed out and gives breath an odd smell. Because the enzyme block is irreversible, a reaction to alcohol can occur for up to about two weeks after the last dose.", "sidefx": "The disulfiram-alcohol reaction is the intended effect, but it can be dangerous with heart disease, so even small hidden alcohol in mouthwash, cough syrup or sauces can cause symptoms. Liver injury, neuropathy and optic neuritis are thought to come from toxic metabolites such as carbon disulfide and from thiol reactivity. Blocking dopamine beta-hydroxylase may contribute to psychosis. There is no antidote; the reaction is treated with fluids and supportive care.", "fun": "Metronidazole and some cephalosporins can cause a similar but milder reaction with alcohol, which is why they are called disulfiram-like drugs on exams.", "src": ["Disulfiram FDA label", "Hald J, Jacobsen E. Lancet 1948", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "dobutamine": {"origin": "Dobutamine was designed on purpose by Ronald Tuttle and Jack Mills at Eli Lilly in the 1970s. Doctors wanted a drug to make a failing heart pump harder without the strong vessel squeezing of norepinephrine or the fast rates of isoproterenol. Tuttle and Mills changed the structure of isoproterenol step by step and tested each new molecule on heart strength, heart rate and blood vessels. Dobutamine was the best balance. It was approved in the US in 1978 as Dobutrex and became a standard inotrope for cardiogenic shock and severe heart failure, and later for stress echocardiography.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Ronald Tuttle and Jack Mills design dobutamine at Eli Lilly from isoproterenol."], ["1975", "Tuttle and Mills publish the development of dobutamine."], ["1978", "Dobutamine is approved in the United States."], ["1980s", "Dobutamine stress echocardiography comes into use."]], "cell": "Dobutamine is a racemic mixture of two forms. The (+) form is a strong beta-1 agonist and an alpha-1 antagonist, with some beta-2 agonism. The (-) form is an alpha-1 agonist. The alpha effects largely cancel, so the main result is beta-1 stimulation. Beta-1 receptors couple to Gs, which activates adenylyl cyclase, raises cAMP and activates protein kinase A. PKA phosphorylates L-type calcium channels (more calcium entry) and phospholamban (faster calcium uptake into the sarcoplasmic reticulum). This makes contractions stronger and relaxation faster. Mild beta-2 stimulation dilates vessels, so blood pressure may stay the same or fall.", "body": [["Heart muscle", "Stronger contraction raises stroke volume and cardiac output in a weak heart."], ["Heart rate", "Raises heart rate, usually less than isoproterenol, but more at higher levels."], ["AV node", "Speeds AV conduction, which can increase the ventricular rate in atrial fibrillation."], ["Blood vessels", "Mild beta-2 dilation lowers systemic vascular resistance, so blood pressure may fall, especially in a dry patient."], ["Kidneys and organs", "Better cardiac output improves blood flow to the kidneys and other organs in low-output states."], ["Heart oxygen use", "The heart works harder and uses more oxygen, which can cause ischemia. This is used on purpose in stress echocardiography."]], "journey": "Dobutamine is given only as a continuous IV infusion because it is destroyed in the gut and liver if swallowed. It starts working within a couple of minutes and reaches steady effect in about ten minutes. It is quickly broken down by COMT in the liver and tissues and by conjugation, and its metabolites leave in urine. The half-life is about two minutes, so effects end within minutes after the infusion stops.", "sidefx": "Side effects are what you would expect from beta-1 stimulation: fast heart rate, palpitations, extra beats and ventricular arrhythmias from higher cAMP and calcium in heart cells. Higher oxygen demand can cause angina or ischemia. Beta-2 vasodilation can lower blood pressure. Because the half-life is so short, most effects resolve quickly when the infusion is slowed or stopped. Tolerance can develop after a few days of continuous use.", "fun": "Dobutamine is a classic example of one drug made of two mirror-image molecules with opposite effects on alpha-1 receptors that cancel out.", "src": ["Dobutamine FDA label", "Tuttle RR, Mills J. Circulation Research 1975", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "docusate": {"origin": "Docusate (dioctyl sulfosuccinate) was first made in the 1930s by the American Cyanamid Company as an industrial wetting agent, a detergent-like chemical that helps water soak into things. In the 1950s doctors reported that taking it by mouth made hard stools softer and easier to pass, and it was marketed as a stool softener under names like Colace. It became one of the most common drugs in hospitals, though later studies have questioned how much it actually helps.", "timeline": [["1930s", "American Cyanamid develops dioctyl sulfosuccinate as a wetting agent."], ["1955", "Clinical reports describe docusate as a stool softener."], ["2010", "Docusate is a component of the oil dispersant used after the Deepwater Horizon spill."]], "cell": "Docusate is an anionic surfactant. It has a water-loving charged end and a fat-loving tail, so it sits at the boundary between water and fat in the stool. This lowers surface tension and lets water and fats mix into and soak through the hard fecal mass, making it softer. It does not act on a receptor. Lab studies show it may also increase water and electrolyte secretion by the intestinal lining, possibly through cAMP, but this effect is small in people at normal doses.", "body": [["Colon", "Softer stool passes with less straining, which matters after surgery, heart attack, or with hemorrhoids or anal fissures."], ["Gut lining", "May slightly increase fluid secretion into the gut, which adds to softening."], ["Mouth and throat", "The liquid form tastes bitter and can irritate the throat."], ["Electrolytes", "With heavy overuse, diarrhea can cause fluid and electrolyte loss."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth, docusate acts mostly inside the gut and works slowly, usually over one to three days. Only a small amount is absorbed; that part is processed by the liver and some is excreted in bile. Most of the drug simply leaves in the stool. As a rectal enema it can work within minutes.", "sidefx": "Docusate is mild because it acts locally and is barely absorbed. Cramping and diarrhea come from added water in the stool. Its detergent action can increase absorption of other drugs taken with it, which is why it should not be combined with mineral oil, since it can increase absorption of the oil. There is no antidote and none is usually needed.", "fun": "Docusate is one of the ingredients of Corexit, the dispersant sprayed on oil in the Gulf of Mexico after the 2010 Deepwater Horizon spill, the same detergent action that softens stool.", "src": ["Docusate sodium OTC drug labeling", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "dofetilide": {"origin": "Dofetilide was developed by Pfizer as a pure class III antiarrhythmic, designed to block one potassium channel and nothing else, unlike older drugs such as amiodarone that act on many targets. It was approved in the US in 1999 as Tikosyn to convert atrial fibrillation and flutter and keep the heart in normal rhythm. Because it can cause the dangerous rhythm torsades de pointes, the FDA approved it only with a special program that requires starting the drug in a hospital with ECG monitoring and kidney function checks.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Pfizer develops dofetilide as a selective IKr blocker."], ["1999", "FDA approves Tikosyn with a required in-hospital start program."], ["1990s", "The DIAMOND trials show dofetilide is neutral on survival in heart failure and after heart attack."]], "cell": "Dofetilide selectively blocks the rapid component of the delayed rectifier potassium current, IKr, carried by the hERG (Kv11.1) channel. IKr helps repolarize heart cells during phase 3 of the action potential. Blocking it lengthens the action potential and the effective refractory period in atria and ventricles without slowing conduction or affecting sodium or calcium channels. A longer refractory period stops reentry circuits from re-exciting tissue, which ends and prevents atrial fibrillation and flutter. On the ECG this shows as a longer QT interval. Its effect is stronger at slow heart rates, called reverse use-dependence.", "body": [["Atria", "A longer refractory period makes reentry harder, converting atrial fibrillation or flutter and keeping sinus rhythm."], ["Ventricles", "Prolongs ventricular repolarization, seen as QT lengthening on the ECG."], ["Heart pumping", "Does not weaken contraction, so it can be used in heart failure."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys clear most of the drug, so kidney function sets how much is safe."]], "journey": "Dofetilide is taken by mouth and is almost completely absorbed. It peaks in two to three hours and has a half-life of about ten hours. Most of it is cleared unchanged by the kidneys through filtration and active secretion by cation transporters. A small part is metabolized by CYP3A4. Drugs that block kidney cation secretion, like cimetidine, trimethoprim, dolutegravir and verapamil, can raise levels sharply, which is why several are contraindicated.", "sidefx": "The main danger comes directly from blocking IKr: a long QT interval can trigger early afterdepolarizations and torsades de pointes, which can lead to sudden death. Risk rises with high drug levels from kidney disease or interacting drugs, low potassium or magnesium, slow heart rate and female sex. This is why dosing is based on kidney function and the QT is monitored for the first days in hospital. Torsades is treated with IV magnesium and, if needed, overdrive pacing or defibrillation.", "fun": "Dofetilide is one of the few oral heart drugs that by law must be started in a hospital, with continuous ECG monitoring for at least three days.", "src": ["Tikosyn (dofetilide) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "dolutegravir": {"origin": "Dolutegravir was developed by ViiV Healthcare (a company formed by GlaxoSmithKline and Pfizer) together with the Japanese company Shionogi. It is a second-generation integrase inhibitor, following raltegravir, which was approved in 2007. Researchers wanted an integrase inhibitor that could be taken once a day and that HIV would have a hard time becoming resistant to. Dolutegravir was approved in the US in 2013 as Tivicay. Its strength, few drug interactions and high barrier to resistance made it a backbone of HIV treatment worldwide, including in low-cost combination pills recommended by the WHO.", "timeline": [["2007", "Raltegravir becomes the first approved HIV integrase inhibitor."], ["2013", "FDA approves dolutegravir (Tivicay)."], ["2014", "FDA approves Triumeq, a single pill of dolutegravir, abacavir and lamivudine."], ["2019", "WHO recommends dolutegravir-based therapy as preferred first-line HIV treatment."]], "cell": "After HIV enters a cell, reverse transcriptase copies its RNA into DNA. The viral enzyme integrase then cuts the ends of that DNA (3' processing) and inserts it into the host chromosome (strand transfer). Integrase needs two magnesium ions in its active site. Dolutegravir binds the integrase-DNA complex and grips both magnesium ions with its metal-binding atoms, blocking the strand transfer step. Without integration, viral DNA cannot become a permanent provirus and is degraded, so new virus is not made. Dolutegravir stays bound longer than first-generation drugs, which helps explain its high barrier to resistance.", "body": [["Immune system", "Stopping viral spread lets CD4 T cell counts recover, lowering the risk of opportunistic infections."], ["Blood virus levels", "HIV RNA drops quickly, often faster than with other drug classes."], ["Kidneys", "Blocks the OCT2 transporter that secretes creatinine, so serum creatinine rises slightly without true loss of kidney function."], ["Brain and mood", "Insomnia, headache and, less often, depression or suicidal thoughts have been reported."], ["Body weight", "Weight gain is more common than with some older HIV drugs."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise, especially in people with hepatitis B or C or when hepatitis B therapy is stopped."]], "journey": "Dolutegravir is taken by mouth and peaks in two to three hours. It is very highly protein bound but still reaches the spinal fluid in amounts that fight the virus. It crosses the placenta. The liver breaks it down mainly by UGT1A1, with a smaller role for CYP3A4. Most leaves in feces and some in urine as metabolites. The half-life is about fourteen hours, allowing once-daily use. Antacids, iron and calcium bind it in the gut, so it is taken apart from them.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are mild: insomnia, headache and nausea. Rare hypersensitivity reactions with rash and organ injury require stopping the drug. The small creatinine rise reflects transporter blockade, not kidney damage. Because it binds magnesium in integrase, it also binds metal ions like calcium, iron and aluminum in the gut, which lowers absorption. Weight gain and mood changes are reported, with mechanisms not fully known. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Multivalent cations bind integrase inhibitors for the same reason they block the enzyme: the drugs are built to grip metal ions.", "src": ["Tivicay (dolutegravir) FDA label", "DHHS Guidelines for the Use of Antiretroviral Agents", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "donepezil": {"origin": "Donepezil was discovered at the Japanese company Eisai by a team led by chemist Hachiro Sugimoto, starting in the early 1980s. The idea behind it was the cholinergic hypothesis of Alzheimer disease: memory loss is linked to the death of acetylcholine-making neurons, so slowing the breakdown of acetylcholine might help. The first drug of this type, tacrine, was approved in 1993 but caused liver damage and needed dosing four times a day. Sugimoto's team found a chemical lead by chance in an unrelated project and refined it into donepezil, which was more selective, kinder to the liver and long-acting enough for once-daily use. It was approved in the US in 1996 as Aricept, marketed with Pfizer.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Researchers link Alzheimer memory loss to loss of cholinergic neurons."], ["1983", "Eisai begins the research program that leads to donepezil."], ["1993", "Tacrine becomes the first approved cholinesterase inhibitor for Alzheimer disease."], ["1996", "FDA approves donepezil (Aricept)."], ["2006", "Donepezil is approved for severe Alzheimer disease."]], "cell": "Acetylcholinesterase sits in the synaptic cleft and rapidly breaks acetylcholine into choline and acetate, ending each signal. Donepezil is a reversible, noncompetitive inhibitor that binds this enzyme with high selectivity over butyrylcholinesterase found in plasma. With the enzyme slowed, acetylcholine stays longer in the synapse and stimulates more nicotinic and muscarinic receptors on surviving neurons in the cortex and hippocampus. This partly makes up for lost cholinergic input from the basal forebrain (nucleus basalis of Meynert). It does not stop the underlying amyloid and tau damage, so the benefit is modest and the disease keeps progressing.", "body": [["Brain", "More acetylcholine in memory circuits can modestly improve attention, memory and daily function for a time."], ["Heart", "Extra acetylcholine at the SA and AV nodes increases vagal effects, causing slow heart rate, heart block and fainting."], ["Gut", "More acetylcholine increases gut movement and acid, causing nausea, vomiting, diarrhea and a risk of ulcer bleeding."], ["Sleep", "Cholinergic activity affects REM sleep, which explains insomnia and vivid dreams, so it is often taken at bedtime or in the morning if dreams bother the patient."], ["Muscles", "Can cause muscle cramps from extra acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction."], ["Bladder and lungs", "Cholinergic effects can worsen urinary frequency and narrow the airways in asthma or COPD."]], "journey": "Donepezil is taken by mouth and is fully absorbed. It peaks in three to four hours and is highly protein bound. It crosses the blood-brain barrier easily. The liver metabolizes it by CYP2D6 and CYP3A4, and the products leave mostly in urine. The half-life is about seventy hours, so steady levels take about two weeks to reach, and changes in benefit are judged over weeks to months.", "sidefx": "Nearly all side effects are cholinergic, from extra acetylcholine outside the brain: nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, loss of appetite, weight loss, muscle cramps and slow heart rate. Increased vagal tone on the heart can cause bradycardia, heart block and fainting, which are a cause of falls. Overdose causes a cholinergic crisis; atropine treats the muscarinic effects. It can also prolong the effect of succinylcholine during anesthesia.", "fun": "Galantamine, another drug in the same class, was first taken from snowdrop flowers, while donepezil was built entirely in the lab.", "src": ["Aricept (donepezil) FDA label", "Sugimoto H et al. Current Medicinal Chemistry 2000", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "dopamine": {"origin": "Dopamine was first made in a lab in 1910 by George Barger and James Ewens in London, but for decades it was thought to be only a stepping stone in the body's production of norepinephrine. In the late 1950s the Swedish scientist Arvid Carlsson showed that dopamine is a neurotransmitter in its own right and that losing it causes the movement problems of Parkinson disease. He shared the 2000 Nobel Prize for this work. In the 1960s Leon Goldberg and colleagues studied dopamine as an IV drug and found it could raise blood pressure and cardiac output in shock, which made it a common vasopressor for decades.", "timeline": [["1910", "Barger and Ewens first synthesize dopamine."], ["1958", "Arvid Carlsson shows dopamine is a brain neurotransmitter."], ["1960s", "Leon Goldberg and colleagues develop dopamine as a treatment for shock."], ["2000", "Arvid Carlsson shares the Nobel Prize for discoveries about dopamine signaling."], ["2010", "The SOAP II trial finds more arrhythmias with dopamine than norepinephrine in shock."]], "cell": "Dopamine acts on different receptors as its level rises. At low levels it stimulates D1 receptors on renal, mesenteric and coronary vessels; D1 couples to Gs, raises cAMP and relaxes vascular smooth muscle. At moderate levels it stimulates beta-1 receptors in the heart, raising cAMP, opening L-type calcium channels and increasing contractility and rate. It also enters sympathetic nerve endings and releases stored norepinephrine. At high levels alpha-1 stimulation dominates; alpha-1 couples to Gq, raises IP3 and calcium in vascular smooth muscle and causes vasoconstriction. Given IV, dopamine does not cross the blood-brain barrier.", "body": [["Heart", "Beta-1 stimulation increases contractility, heart rate and cardiac output, which helps cardiogenic and other forms of shock."], ["Blood vessels", "At higher rates alpha-1 vasoconstriction raises systemic vascular resistance and blood pressure."], ["Kidneys", "Low-level D1 effects increase renal blood flow and urine output, but this does not protect the kidneys, so it is no longer used for that."], ["Heart rhythm", "Beta-1 stimulation and norepinephrine release make extra beats and tachyarrhythmias common."], ["Skin and limbs", "Strong vasoconstriction can reduce blood flow to fingers and toes and cause tissue damage."], ["Hormones", "Dopamine lowers prolactin and can blunt other pituitary hormones in critically ill patients."]], "journey": "Dopamine is given only as a continuous IV infusion, preferably through a central line. It is destroyed in the gut and liver if swallowed. It starts within about five minutes. It is broken down quickly by MAO and COMT in the liver, kidney and plasma, and part is converted to norepinephrine in nerve endings. Its half-life is about two minutes, so effects fade within minutes of stopping. Metabolites such as homovanillic acid leave in urine.", "sidefx": "Tachycardia, palpitations and arrhythmias come from beta-1 stimulation and released norepinephrine. Alpha-1 vasoconstriction can cause cold, mottled limbs and gangrene. If the IV leaks into tissue, local vasoconstriction can cause necrosis; phentolamine, an alpha blocker injected into the area, reverses it. Patients on MAO inhibitors are very sensitive to dopamine because it is not broken down normally.", "fun": "Dopamine itself cannot be used for Parkinson disease because it cannot cross the blood-brain barrier. Its precursor, levodopa, can, and is converted to dopamine inside the brain.", "src": ["Dopamine hydrochloride injection FDA label", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 2000 materials", "De Backer D et al. N Engl J Med 2010 (SOAP II)"]}, "doxazosin": {"origin": "Doxazosin was developed by Pfizer as a longer-acting cousin of prazosin, the first selective alpha-1 blocker, which Pfizer had introduced in the 1970s. Prazosin worked for blood pressure but had a short half-life and could cause fainting with the first dose. Pfizer chemists changed the side chain to make a drug that lasts all day. Doxazosin was approved in the US in 1990 as Cardura for high blood pressure and later for urinary symptoms of benign prostatic hyperplasia. In 2000 the large ALLHAT trial stopped its doxazosin arm early because of more heart failure compared with a diuretic, which moved it from first-line blood pressure treatment to an add-on.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Pfizer introduces prazosin, the first selective alpha-1 blocker."], ["1990", "FDA approves doxazosin (Cardura) for hypertension."], ["2000", "ALLHAT stops its doxazosin arm early because of more heart failure than chlorthalidone."], ["2005", "An extended-release form for BPH (Cardura XL) is approved."]], "cell": "Doxazosin is a quinazoline that competitively blocks postsynaptic alpha-1 adrenergic receptors (alpha-1A, alpha-1B and alpha-1D). These receptors normally couple to Gq, activate phospholipase C, raise IP3 and release calcium to contract smooth muscle. In arterioles and veins, blocking them causes relaxation, lower peripheral resistance and lower blood pressure. In the prostate stroma, prostatic capsule and bladder neck, rich in alpha-1A receptors, blocking them relaxes the smooth muscle and widens the urethra. Because it spares presynaptic alpha-2 receptors, norepinephrine release is not increased, so reflex tachycardia is milder than with nonselective alpha blockers.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Arteries and veins relax, lowering blood pressure. Venous pooling explains dizziness on standing."], ["Prostate and bladder neck", "Relaxed smooth muscle widens the urinary outlet, improving urine flow and reducing urgency and nocturia in BPH."], ["Heart", "Lower afterload and only mild reflex tachycardia, but it did less than a diuretic to prevent heart failure in ALLHAT."], ["Blood lipids", "Alpha-1 blockers have a small favorable effect on cholesterol and insulin sensitivity."], ["Eyes", "Blocking alpha-1 receptors in the iris dilator can cause floppy iris syndrome during cataract surgery."], ["Penis", "Rarely, blocked alpha-1 tone can cause priapism."]], "journey": "Doxazosin is taken by mouth and well absorbed, peaking in two to three hours. It is highly protein bound. The liver metabolizes it, mainly by CYP3A4, and most is excreted in the feces through bile. The half-life is about twenty-two hours, which allows once-daily dosing. Treatment is started low and increased slowly to limit first-dose hypotension.", "sidefx": "The key side effect, orthostatic hypotension with dizziness or fainting, comes from blocking alpha-1 receptors on veins, so blood pools in the legs on standing. It is worst with the first dose or a dose increase, which is why the first dose is often taken at bedtime. Fatigue, headache and ankle edema are related to vasodilation. Intraoperative floppy iris syndrome comes from blocking alpha-1A receptors in the iris dilator muscle, so eye surgeons should be told. Severe hypotension is treated with fluids and, if needed, vasopressors.", "fun": "The -azosin drugs (prazosin, terazosin, doxazosin) share a quinazoline core, and prazosin is also used off-label to reduce PTSD nightmares.", "src": ["Cardura (doxazosin) FDA label", "ALLHAT Collaborative Research Group. JAMA 2000", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "doxepin": {"origin": "Doxepin is a tricyclic antidepressant discovered in Germany in 1963, during the era that followed the 1950s discovery of imipramine, the first tricyclic. It was introduced in Europe by Boehringer and marketed in the US by Pfizer as Sinequan, approved in 1969 for depression and anxiety. Doctors noticed that doxepin is one of the most powerful blockers of histamine H1 receptors known, stronger than many antihistamines. This led to a topical cream for itching and, in 2010, a very low-dose tablet (Silenor) approved for insomnia, where it works mostly as an antihistamine.", "timeline": [["1963", "Doxepin is discovered in Germany."], ["1969", "FDA approves doxepin (Sinequan) for depression and anxiety."], ["2010", "FDA approves low-dose doxepin (Silenor) for insomnia."]], "cell": "At the very low doses used for sleep, doxepin acts almost purely as an H1 receptor antagonist. Histamine neurons in the tuberomammillary nucleus keep the cortex awake through H1 receptors, so blocking them helps people stay asleep. At the higher antidepressant doses, doxepin also blocks the serotonin transporter (SERT) and norepinephrine transporter (NET), raising these transmitters in synapses and, over weeks, changing receptor signaling in mood circuits. Like other tricyclics it also blocks muscarinic receptors, alpha-1 receptors and fast sodium channels, which account for most of its side effects and its danger in overdose.", "body": [["Brain (sleep)", "H1 blockade reduces wake-promoting histamine signaling, mainly helping people stay asleep through the night."], ["Brain (mood)", "Higher doses raise serotonin and norepinephrine, treating depression and anxiety over several weeks."], ["Skin", "Strong H1 and H2 blockade reduces itch, which is why a cream is used for eczema itching."], ["Mouth, eyes, bladder and gut", "Muscarinic blockade at antidepressant doses causes dry mouth, blurred vision, urinary retention and constipation."], ["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 blockade causes orthostatic hypotension and dizziness."], ["Heart", "At high levels, sodium channel blockade slows conduction, widens the QRS and can cause deadly arrhythmias."]], "journey": "Doxepin is absorbed from the gut and peaks in a few hours, but much is removed by the liver on first pass. It is fat soluble, spreads widely, crosses into the brain and placenta and enters breast milk. The liver converts it, mainly by CYP2C19 and CYP2D6, to nordoxepin, which is also active. Metabolites leave in urine. The half-life is about fifteen hours for doxepin and longer for nordoxepin, so next-morning drowsiness can occur. A cream used over large areas can be absorbed enough to cause drowsiness.", "sidefx": "At sleep doses the main side effects are drowsiness and next-day sedation from H1 blockade. At antidepressant doses, muscarinic blockade causes dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention and confusion; alpha-1 blockade causes orthostatic dizziness; and weight gain relates to H1 blockade. In overdose, sodium channel blockade widens the QRS and causes arrhythmias, seizures and low blood pressure, the classic tricyclic toxicity. Sodium bicarbonate is the key antidote for the cardiac effects. Like all antidepressants, it carries a boxed warning about suicidal thinking in young people.", "fun": "Doxepin is often cited as one of the strongest H1 antagonists ever measured, binding the receptor more tightly than diphenhydramine.", "src": ["Silenor (doxepin) FDA label", "Sinequan (doxepin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "doxorubicin": {"origin": "Doxorubicin comes from soil bacteria. In the 1950s the Italian company Farmitalia searched soil samples for new antibiotics. A red pigment made by Streptomyces peucetius, found in soil near Castel del Monte in southern Italy, showed strong anticancer activity and became daunorubicin. In the late 1960s Farmitalia scientists including Federico Arcamone used a mutated strain of the bacterium that made a slightly different molecule with one extra hydroxyl group. They named it adriamycin after the Adriatic Sea. It worked against a much wider range of cancers. Doxorubicin was approved in the US in 1974 and remains a core drug for breast cancer, lymphomas, leukemias and sarcomas. In 1995 a liposomal form, Doxil, became the first FDA-approved nanomedicine.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Farmitalia scientists find anticancer pigments from Streptomyces peucetius in Italian soil."], ["1969", "Arcamone and colleagues report adriamycin from a mutant Streptomyces strain."], ["1974", "FDA approves doxorubicin."], ["1995", "FDA approves liposomal doxorubicin (Doxil), the first nanoparticle drug."]], "cell": "Doxorubicin works in several ways. First, its flat ring system slides between DNA base pairs (intercalation), distorting the helix. Second, it traps topoisomerase II after the enzyme has cut both DNA strands, preventing the enzyme from rejoining them. These double-strand breaks pile up during the S and G2 phases of the cell cycle, trigger p53 and other DNA damage pathways, and lead to cell death, though it can kill cells in any phase. Third, its quinone group cycles between oxidized and reduced forms, especially with iron, producing superoxide and hydroxyl radicals that damage membranes, DNA and mitochondria. Heart muscle has low antioxidant defenses, which is why the radicals injure it.", "body": [["Cancer cells", "Rapidly dividing tumor cells collect DNA breaks and die, shrinking many solid tumors and blood cancers."], ["Bone marrow", "Dividing blood stem cells are also killed, lowering white cells, platelets and red cells, with the low point about ten to fourteen days after treatment."], ["Heart", "Free radicals and topoisomerase II-beta damage in heart cells cause a dose-dependent cardiomyopathy and heart failure, sometimes years later."], ["Hair and mouth", "Hair follicle and mucosa cells divide quickly, so hair loss and mouth sores are common."], ["Urine", "The red drug and its metabolites turn urine red-orange for a day or two, which is harmless."], ["Skin and veins", "If it leaks out of the vein it causes severe tissue necrosis, and it can cause a flare at previously radiated skin (radiation recall)."]], "journey": "Doxorubicin is given IV only. It leaves the blood quickly and binds tightly to tissues, including heart, liver and kidney, but does not cross the blood-brain barrier. The liver converts it by aldo-keto reductase to doxorubicinol, an active metabolite that may add to heart toxicity, and further breaks it down. Most is excreted in bile, so liver disease requires lower doses, and only a small part leaves in urine. The terminal half-life is about one to two days. The liposomal form circulates much longer and builds up in tumors with leaky vessels.", "sidefx": "Bone marrow suppression, hair loss and mouth sores come from killing dividing normal cells. The main limiting toxicity is heart damage from free radicals and iron in cardiomyocytes. Risk rises with the total lifetime amount given, chest radiation and other cardiotoxic drugs such as trastuzumab, so heart function is checked by echocardiogram or MUGA. Dexrazoxane, an iron chelator, reduces cardiotoxicity and also treats extravasation. Topoisomerase II damage can cause secondary acute myeloid leukemia years later. The liposomal form causes hand-foot syndrome.", "fun": "The old name adriamycin honors the Adriatic Sea, and the red color of the drug earned it the nickname red devil on cancer wards.", "src": ["Doxorubicin hydrochloride FDA label", "Arcamone F et al. Biotechnology and Bioengineering 1969", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "doxycycline": {"origin": "Doxycycline is part of the tetracycline family, which began in 1948 when Benjamin Duggar at Lederle Laboratories found chlortetracycline (Aureomycin) in the soil bacterium Streptomyces aureofaciens. Pfizer soon found oxytetracycline (Terramycin) in another soil microbe. Chemists at Pfizer then modified the tetracycline structure to make it more stable and longer lasting. The result, doxycycline, was approved in the US in 1967 as Vibramycin. It is better absorbed, lasts longer and is safer for the kidneys than older tetracyclines, and it remains widely used for Lyme disease, rickettsial infections, acne, chlamydia and malaria prevention.", "timeline": [["1948", "Benjamin Duggar reports chlortetracycline from Streptomyces aureofaciens."], ["1950", "Pfizer introduces oxytetracycline (Terramycin)."], ["1967", "FDA approves doxycycline (Vibramycin)."]], "cell": "Doxycycline enters bacteria by passive diffusion through porins and by active transport. Inside, it binds reversibly to the 16S rRNA of the 30S ribosomal subunit and blocks aminoacyl-tRNA from binding the A site of the ribosome-mRNA complex. Without new amino acids being added, protein synthesis stops and the bacteria cannot grow or divide, so the effect is bacteriostatic. Because it accumulates inside cells, it works well against intracellular organisms such as Rickettsia, Chlamydia, Mycoplasma and Ehrlichia. Resistance comes mainly from efflux pumps (tet genes) and ribosomal protection proteins. Doxycycline also inhibits matrix metalloproteinases, which helps in gum disease and rosacea.", "body": [["Infected tissues", "Stops growth of a broad range of bacteria, including atypical and intracellular organisms and tick-borne infections."], ["Skin", "Reduces acne and rosacea through antibacterial and anti-inflammatory effects, but increases sunburn risk because the drug absorbs light in the skin."], ["Teeth and bone", "Binds calcium in developing teeth and bone, which can stain teeth in young children with prolonged use, so it is avoided in pregnancy."], ["Esophagus", "A pill that sticks in the esophagus can cause painful ulcers, so it is taken with water and while upright."], ["Gut", "Nausea and diarrhea come from direct irritation and changes in gut flora."], ["Brain", "Rarely raises intracranial pressure (pseudotumor cerebri), causing headache and vision changes."]], "journey": "Doxycycline is nearly completely absorbed by mouth, and food has less effect on it than on older tetracyclines. Calcium, magnesium, aluminum, iron and zinc bind it in the gut and block absorption, so antacids, dairy and supplements are separated from doses. It is fat soluble, spreads widely into tissues and cells, crosses the placenta and enters breast milk. It is mostly excreted in feces, through bile and the intestinal wall, rather than urine, so it is safe in kidney disease. The half-life is about eighteen to twenty-two hours.", "sidefx": "Photosensitivity happens because the drug absorbs ultraviolet light in the skin and creates reactive oxygen species. Tooth staining and bone effects come from calcium binding. Pill esophagitis comes from the acidic tablet sitting on the esophageal lining. GI upset reflects irritation and flora changes. Intracranial hypertension is rare and its cause is unclear. There is no antidote; stopping the drug is the treatment.", "fun": "Bones from ancient Sudanese Nubia, buried about 1,500 years ago, contain tetracycline, likely from beer brewed with grain contaminated by Streptomyces.", "src": ["Vibramycin (doxycycline) FDA label", "Bassett EJ et al. Science 1980"]}, "droperidol": {"origin": "Droperidol was made in 1961 at Janssen Pharmaceutica in Belgium, the company founded by Paul Janssen, which had just introduced haloperidol. Both belong to the butyrophenone class of dopamine blockers. Droperidol was faster and shorter acting, and it was used with the opioid fentanyl for neuroleptanalgesia, a calm, pain-free state for procedures. It became popular for preventing nausea after surgery and for calming agitated patients in emergency departments. In 2001 the FDA added a boxed warning about QT prolongation, which led to a sharp drop in use, though many emergency physicians still consider it safe and effective for agitation and nausea.", "timeline": [["1958", "Janssen Pharmaceutica creates haloperidol, the first butyrophenone."], ["1961", "Droperidol is discovered at Janssen Pharmaceutica."], ["2001", "FDA adds a boxed warning on QT prolongation and torsades de pointes."]], "cell": "Droperidol is a potent antagonist at dopamine D2 receptors. In the chemoreceptor trigger zone of the area postrema, which lies outside the blood-brain barrier and senses toxins and drugs in the blood, D2 blockade prevents signals to the vomiting center, giving a strong antiemetic effect. In the mesolimbic and other forebrain pathways, D2 blockade produces calm, sedation and indifference to surroundings. It also blocks alpha-1 receptors, causing vasodilation and mild low blood pressure. In heart cells, it blocks the hERG potassium channel (IKr), which slows repolarization and lengthens the QT interval.", "body": [["Vomiting center", "Strongly prevents and treats nausea and vomiting after surgery and from other causes."], ["Brain", "Produces rapid calming and sedation, useful for severe agitation."], ["Movement", "D2 blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway can cause akathisia (restlessness) and acute dystonia."], ["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 blockade lowers blood pressure, especially in a dehydrated patient."], ["Heart", "IKr blockade lengthens the QT interval and in rare cases causes torsades de pointes."], ["Temperature and muscles", "Rarely, profound dopamine blockade triggers neuroleptic malignant syndrome with fever, rigidity and confusion."]], "journey": "Droperidol is given IV or IM. It works within minutes IV and in ten to twenty minutes IM, faster than haloperidol. It is highly protein bound and crosses the blood-brain barrier and the placenta. The liver metabolizes it extensively, and metabolites are excreted in urine and feces. The half-life is about two hours, but the calming effect can last several hours.", "sidefx": "QT prolongation comes from blocking the hERG potassium channel, and risk rises with other QT drugs, low potassium or magnesium, and congenital long QT, which is why ECG monitoring is advised. Akathisia and dystonia come from D2 blockade in the basal ganglia and respond to diphenhydramine or benztropine. Low blood pressure comes from alpha-1 blockade. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is rare and treated by stopping the drug, cooling and supportive care, sometimes with dantrolene or bromocriptine. Torsades is treated with IV magnesium.", "fun": "After the 2001 boxed warning, many experts argued that the QT risk at usual antiemetic doses had been overstated, and the drug made a comeback in many emergency departments.", "src": ["Inapsine (droperidol) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Tintinalli's Emergency Medicine"]}, "dulaglutide": {"origin": "Dulaglutide was developed by Eli Lilly as a once-weekly GLP-1 receptor agonist. It builds on the discovery that the gut hormone GLP-1 boosts insulin after meals, and on exenatide, the first drug in the class, approved in 2005. Natural GLP-1 lasts only minutes in the blood because the enzyme DPP-4 cuts it. Lilly engineered a GLP-1 analog resistant to DPP-4 and fused two copies of it to the Fc part of a human antibody, which makes the molecule too large for the kidneys to filter and lets it last about a week. It was approved in the US in 2014 as Trulicity. The REWIND trial later showed it reduced heart attacks and strokes, adding a heart-protection indication in 2020.", "timeline": [["1980s", "GLP-1 is identified as a gut hormone that boosts insulin secretion."], ["2005", "Exenatide becomes the first approved GLP-1 receptor agonist."], ["2014", "FDA approves dulaglutide (Trulicity)."], ["2019", "The REWIND trial reports fewer major cardiovascular events with dulaglutide."], ["2020", "FDA adds a cardiovascular risk reduction indication."]], "cell": "Dulaglutide binds and activates GLP-1 receptors, which are Gs-coupled receptors. In pancreatic beta cells, higher cAMP activates protein kinase A and Epac2, which increase insulin release, but only when glucose is high. This glucose dependence is why it rarely causes low blood sugar alone. In alpha cells it lowers glucagon release after meals, reducing liver glucose output. In the stomach, vagal pathways slow gastric emptying, so sugar enters the blood more slowly. In the hypothalamus and brainstem it increases satiety and reduces appetite. Its amino acid changes protect it from DPP-4, and the Fc fusion prevents rapid kidney clearance.", "body": [["Pancreas", "More insulin and less glucagon when glucose is high lower blood sugar and A1c."], ["Stomach", "Slower emptying blunts the rise in sugar after meals and adds to fullness, but causes nausea."], ["Brain", "Signals of fullness reduce appetite and food intake, leading to weight loss."], ["Heart and vessels", "Reduces major cardiovascular events in people with type 2 diabetes, likely through effects on weight, blood pressure, lipids and vessel inflammation."], ["Gallbladder", "Weight loss and changes in bile flow increase gallstone risk."], ["Kidneys", "Vomiting and diarrhea can cause dehydration and acute kidney injury; it also slows progression of albuminuria."]], "journey": "Dulaglutide is injected under the skin once a week. It is absorbed slowly, reaching peak levels in one to three days. As a large protein it stays mostly in the blood and does not cross the blood-brain barrier well, though it reaches brain areas that sense hormones. It is not metabolized by liver enzymes; like other proteins it is broken down into amino acids. The half-life is about five days, so steady levels are reached after a few weeks. It slows stomach emptying, which can change absorption of oral drugs.", "sidefx": "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea come from slowed gastric emptying and GLP-1 signaling in the brainstem, and they ease over time. Pancreatitis is a rare but serious risk. Gallbladder disease relates to rapid weight loss and reduced gallbladder emptying. Low blood sugar is uncommon alone but can occur with insulin or sulfonylureas. In rodents, GLP-1 agonists caused thyroid C-cell tumors, so it carries a boxed warning and is avoided in people with a history of medullary thyroid cancer or MEN 2. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Dulaglutide's name ends in -glutide like other GLP-1 drugs, and the du- hints at its dual structure: two GLP-1 chains on one antibody Fc.", "src": ["Trulicity (dulaglutide) FDA label", "Gerstein HC et al. Lancet 2019 (REWIND)", "ADA Standards of Care in Diabetes"]}, "duloxetine": {"origin": "Duloxetine was developed at Eli Lilly, the company that made fluoxetine (Prozac). In the 1980s Lilly scientists sought a drug that would block both serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake, thinking a dual action might help depression more broadly and also ease pain, since both transmitters dampen pain signals in the spinal cord. After a long development, duloxetine was approved in the US in 2004 as Cymbalta for depression and diabetic nerve pain. It was later approved for generalized anxiety disorder, fibromyalgia and chronic musculoskeletal pain, including low back pain and osteoarthritis.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Eli Lilly scientists discover duloxetine as a dual reuptake inhibitor."], ["2004", "FDA approves duloxetine (Cymbalta) for depression and diabetic peripheral neuropathic pain."], ["2007", "Duloxetine is approved for generalized anxiety disorder."], ["2008", "Duloxetine is approved for fibromyalgia."], ["2010", "Duloxetine is approved for chronic musculoskeletal pain."]], "cell": "Duloxetine is a serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor (SNRI). It binds and blocks the serotonin transporter (SERT) and the norepinephrine transporter (NET) on presynaptic neurons, so these transmitters stay longer in the synapse. In mood circuits, the extra serotonin and norepinephrine trigger slow adaptive changes over weeks, including downregulation of some receptors and increased neurotrophic signaling, which relieve depression and anxiety. In the spinal cord, descending pathways from the brainstem use serotonin and norepinephrine to inhibit pain transmission in the dorsal horn. Boosting them strengthens this built-in pain brake, which explains its use in neuropathic and chronic pain. It has little effect on dopamine transporters or histamine and muscarinic receptors.", "body": [["Brain (mood)", "Improves depressed mood and anxiety over several weeks."], ["Spinal cord (pain)", "Strengthens descending pain inhibition, easing nerve pain, fibromyalgia and chronic musculoskeletal pain."], ["Gut", "Serotonin acting on 5-HT3 receptors in the gut and brainstem causes nausea, especially early in treatment."], ["Heart and vessels", "Norepinephrine raises heart rate and blood pressure slightly."], ["Bladder", "Norepinephrine increases urethral sphincter tone, which can cause urinary hesitancy."], ["Liver", "Can raise liver enzymes and rarely cause liver injury, so heavy alcohol use and liver disease are concerns."], ["Blood sodium", "Can cause SIADH and low sodium, especially in older adults."]], "journey": "Duloxetine is taken by mouth in delayed-release capsules because stomach acid breaks it down. Absorption is delayed about two hours, and peak levels come at about six hours. It is highly protein bound and enters the brain. The liver metabolizes it by CYP1A2 and CYP2D6, and it moderately inhibits CYP2D6, raising levels of drugs like metoprolol or tricyclics. Smoking induces CYP1A2 and lowers levels. Inactive metabolites leave mostly in urine. The half-life is about twelve hours.", "sidefx": "Nausea, dry mouth, constipation, sweating and insomnia come from extra serotonin and norepinephrine at receptors outside the target circuits. A small rise in blood pressure comes from norepinephrine. Sexual dysfunction and bleeding risk are shared with SSRIs because serotonin affects sexual response and platelets take up serotonin for clotting. Combining it with other serotonergic drugs can cause serotonin syndrome, treated with stopping the drugs, benzodiazepines and sometimes cyproheptadine. Stopping suddenly can cause discontinuation symptoms such as dizziness and electric shock feelings. It carries a boxed warning about suicidal thinking in young people.", "fun": "Duloxetine and pregabalin were both approved in 2004 as the first drugs specifically labeled for diabetic peripheral neuropathic pain.", "src": ["Cymbalta (duloxetine) FDA label", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "edoxaban": {"origin": "Edoxaban was developed by the Japanese company Daiichi Sankyo as one of the direct oral anticoagulants that began to replace warfarin in the 2010s. These drugs block a single clotting factor directly and do not need routine blood tests. Edoxaban was first approved in Japan in 2011. Its approval in the US in 2015 as Savaysa followed the large ENGAGE AF-TIMI 48 trial in atrial fibrillation and the Hokusai-VTE trial in blood clots. Those trials showed it worked about as well as warfarin with less major bleeding.", "timeline": [["2011", "Edoxaban is first approved in Japan."], ["2013", "ENGAGE AF-TIMI 48 and Hokusai-VTE trial results are published."], ["2015", "FDA approves edoxaban (Savaysa)."]], "cell": "Factor Xa sits at the meeting point of the intrinsic and extrinsic clotting pathways. As part of the prothrombinase complex with factor Va, it converts prothrombin (factor II) into thrombin. Edoxaban binds directly and reversibly to the active site of factor Xa, whether free in the blood or within prothrombinase, and does not need antithrombin to work. This blocks the thrombin burst. Less thrombin means less fibrinogen converted to fibrin, less activation of factors V, VIII and XI, and less thrombin-driven platelet activation, so clots are less likely to form or grow.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Less thrombin generation prevents clots in the left atrial appendage in atrial fibrillation and in leg and lung veins."], ["Brain", "Fewer clots traveling from the heart lowers stroke risk in atrial fibrillation, though bleeding into the brain is a rare but serious risk."], ["Gut", "Bleeding from the stomach and intestines is one of the most common serious bleeds."], ["Kidneys", "About half the drug is cleared by the kidneys, so kidney function determines the right dose."], ["Spine", "Spinal or epidural procedures during treatment risk a spinal hematoma and paralysis."]], "journey": "Edoxaban is taken by mouth once a day and peaks in one to two hours. It is moderately protein bound. Little is metabolized; a small part is changed by hydrolysis and CYP3A4. It is a P-glycoprotein substrate, so strong P-gp inhibitors raise levels. About half is cleared unchanged by the kidneys and the rest is eliminated in feces. The half-life is ten to fourteen hours. Unusually, people with very good kidney function clear it so fast that it works less well for stroke prevention in atrial fibrillation.", "sidefx": "Bleeding is the main side effect and follows directly from blocking factor Xa; anemia can result from slow, hidden blood loss. Stopping it early without another anticoagulant raises clot and stroke risk, which is a boxed warning. Another boxed warning notes lower effectiveness in atrial fibrillation patients with very high creatinine clearance, because blood levels fall too low. No reversal agent is specifically approved for edoxaban in the US; prothrombin complex concentrate may be used for life-threatening bleeding, and the drug is not removed well by dialysis.", "fun": "Edoxaban is the only direct oral anticoagulant with a warning that it works worse when the kidneys work too well.", "src": ["Savaysa (edoxaban) FDA label", "Giugliano RP et al. N Engl J Med 2013 (ENGAGE AF-TIMI 48)", "Hokusai-VTE Investigators. N Engl J Med 2013"]}, "efavirenz": {"origin": "Efavirenz was discovered at Merck in the early 1990s and developed by DuPont Pharmaceuticals. It belongs to the non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTIs), a class first approved in 1996 with nevirapine. Efavirenz was approved in the US in 1998 as Sustiva. It was strong and long-acting enough for once-daily dosing, and in 2006 it was combined with tenofovir and emtricitabine in Atripla, the first complete HIV regimen in a single once-daily pill. For about a decade it was a preferred first-line drug worldwide until better-tolerated integrase inhibitors replaced it.", "timeline": [["1996", "Nevirapine becomes the first approved NNRTI."], ["1998", "FDA approves efavirenz (Sustiva)."], ["2006", "FDA approves Atripla, the first once-daily single-pill complete HIV regimen."], ["2010s", "Integrase inhibitors replace efavirenz as preferred first-line therapy in most guidelines."]], "cell": "HIV-1 reverse transcriptase copies viral RNA into DNA. Efavirenz binds a hydrophobic pocket on the enzyme's p66 subunit, close to but separate from the active site. This allosteric binding changes the enzyme's shape and locks the active site in an inactive position, so it cannot add nucleotides. Unlike nucleoside drugs, efavirenz does not need to be phosphorylated and is not built into DNA. HIV-2 has a differently shaped pocket, so efavirenz does not work against it. A single amino acid change, such as K103N, can block binding, which is why resistance develops easily if it is not combined with other active drugs.", "body": [["Immune system", "Blocking viral replication lowers HIV RNA and allows CD4 T cells to recover."], ["Brain", "Commonly causes vivid dreams, dizziness, insomnia and poor concentration in the first weeks, and less often depression, suicidal thoughts or psychosis."], ["Skin", "Rash is common early and is usually mild, though severe reactions can occur."], ["Liver", "Can raise liver enzymes and rarely cause liver failure."], ["Blood lipids", "Raises total cholesterol and triglycerides, so lipid levels are checked during treatment."], ["Breast tissue", "Can cause breast tissue growth (gynecomastia) in men, which may improve after switching drugs."], ["Fetus", "Animal studies raised concern about neural tube defects, though later human data were reassuring."]], "journey": "Efavirenz is taken by mouth, usually at bedtime and on an empty stomach, because fatty food raises absorption and worsens brain side effects. It is very highly protein bound and reaches the brain. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP2B6, with help from CYP3A4. It induces both enzymes, which speeds its own breakdown and lowers levels of many other drugs, including some hormonal contraceptives. People with slow CYP2B6 gene variants, more common in people of African descent, have higher levels and more side effects. The half-life is long, about two days, and metabolites leave in urine and feces.", "sidefx": "Brain effects are the most typical and are thought to come from efavirenz and its metabolites acting on several brain targets, including serotonin receptors; they are worse with high blood levels. Rash is an immune reaction. Liver injury and lipid changes relate to metabolism and enzyme induction. Its long half-life means that if all drugs are stopped together, efavirenz lingers alone and resistance can develop. Enzyme induction causes many drug interactions. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Efavirenz can cause a false-positive urine drug screen for cannabis on some immunoassays.", "src": ["Sustiva (efavirenz) FDA label", "DHHS Guidelines for the Use of Antiretroviral Agents", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "empagliflozin": {"origin": "Empagliflozin traces back to phlorizin, a bitter compound first isolated from the bark of apple trees in 1835. In the 1880s Joseph von Mering found that phlorizin caused sugar to spill into the urine. Much later scientists learned it blocks sodium-glucose cotransporters in the kidney, but it was poorly absorbed and not selective. Boehringer Ingelheim, partnering with Eli Lilly, developed empagliflozin as a selective SGLT2 inhibitor, approved in the US in 2014 as Jardiance. The 2015 EMPA-REG OUTCOME trial surprised the field by showing fewer cardiovascular deaths. Later trials led to approvals for heart failure and chronic kidney disease, even in people without diabetes.", "timeline": [["1835", "Phlorizin is isolated from apple tree bark."], ["1880s", "Joseph von Mering shows phlorizin causes glucose in the urine."], ["2014", "FDA approves empagliflozin (Jardiance) for type 2 diabetes."], ["2015", "EMPA-REG OUTCOME shows reduced cardiovascular death."], ["2020s", "Empagliflozin is approved for heart failure and chronic kidney disease."]], "cell": "In the early proximal tubule, the SGLT2 transporter reabsorbs most filtered glucose, moving one glucose with one sodium into tubule cells. Empagliflozin selectively blocks SGLT2. Glucose that would have been reabsorbed instead stays in the urine, lowering blood glucose without needing insulin. The sodium that also stays in the tubule reaches the macula densa, which restores tubuloglomerular feedback, constricts the afferent arteriole and lowers pressure inside the glomerulus. This is thought to protect the kidney. The added sodium and glucose in urine cause a mild diuresis, lowering blood volume and pressure, which helps the failing heart. A shift toward burning fat and ketones may also contribute.", "body": [["Kidneys", "Glucose and sodium are lost in the urine, and lower glomerular pressure slows the loss of kidney function."], ["Blood sugar", "Lowers glucose and A1c without causing low blood sugar on its own, because it does not raise insulin."], ["Heart", "Lowers heart failure hospitalizations and cardiovascular death through volume reduction, lower blood pressure and other effects on heart metabolism."], ["Blood pressure and weight", "Mild diuresis and calorie loss in the urine lower blood pressure and body weight."], ["Genital and urinary tract", "Sugar in the urine feeds yeast and bacteria, causing genital yeast infections and urinary tract infections."], ["Metabolism", "Lower insulin and higher glucagon shift the body toward making ketones, which can lead to ketoacidosis with near-normal glucose."]], "journey": "Empagliflozin is taken by mouth once daily and peaks in about one and a half hours. It is highly protein bound. It is not metabolized much by CYP enzymes; instead the liver joins it to glucuronic acid. About half leaves in urine and half in feces. The half-life is about twelve hours. Its glucose-lowering effect weakens as kidney function falls, but its heart and kidney benefits persist at lower kidney function.", "sidefx": "Genital yeast infections and urinary tract infections come from glucose in the urine. Rarely, a severe perineal infection called Fournier gangrene occurs. Dehydration, dizziness and low blood pressure follow the diuretic effect. Euglycemic diabetic ketoacidosis happens because the drug lowers glucose and insulin while raising glucagon and ketone production, so blood sugar can look normal while ketoacids rise, especially with illness, fasting or surgery. A small early drop in kidney filtration reflects reduced glomerular pressure rather than damage. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The SGLT2 story started with the apple tree: phlorizin, the first known SGLT blocker, came from apple bark.", "src": ["Jardiance (empagliflozin) FDA label", "Zinman B et al. N Engl J Med 2015 (EMPA-REG OUTCOME)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "emtricitabine": {"origin": "Emtricitabine was discovered at Emory University in Atlanta in the early 1990s by Dennis Liotta, Raymond Schinazi and Woo-Baeg Choi. They made a cytidine-like molecule with a fluorine atom that HIV reverse transcriptase would mistake for a normal DNA building block. It was developed by Triangle Pharmaceuticals, which Gilead Sciences bought, and approved in the US in 2003 as Emtriva. Combined with tenofovir as Truvada in 2004, it became a backbone of HIV treatment. In 2012 Truvada became the first drug approved to prevent HIV infection (PrEP).", "timeline": [["1990s", "Liotta, Schinazi and Choi discover emtricitabine at Emory University."], ["2003", "FDA approves emtricitabine (Emtriva)."], ["2004", "FDA approves Truvada (emtricitabine and tenofovir disoproxil fumarate)."], ["2012", "Truvada becomes the first drug approved for HIV pre-exposure prophylaxis."], ["2019", "FDA approves Descovy (emtricitabine and tenofovir alafenamide) for PrEP."]], "cell": "Emtricitabine is a nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor, an analog of cytidine. It enters cells and is phosphorylated by host kinases to emtricitabine triphosphate. This active form competes with the natural building block dCTP for HIV reverse transcriptase. When reverse transcriptase adds it to the growing viral DNA chain, the chain cannot grow further, because the drug lacks the 3'-hydroxyl group needed to attach the next nucleotide. This chain termination stops viral DNA synthesis. It also blocks hepatitis B polymerase the same way. The M184V mutation in reverse transcriptase blocks the drug but makes the virus less fit. It has low affinity for human DNA polymerase gamma, so mitochondrial toxicity is uncommon.", "body": [["Immune system", "As part of combination therapy, it lowers HIV levels so CD4 T cells can recover."], ["Prevention", "Taken before exposure with tenofovir, the active drug in cells of the genital and rectal tissues blocks HIV from establishing infection."], ["Liver", "Suppresses hepatitis B. Stopping it in a person with hepatitis B can cause a severe flare of liver inflammation."], ["Skin", "Can cause darkening of the palms and soles, more often in people with darker skin."], ["Kidneys", "Cleared by the kidneys, so dosing intervals are adjusted in kidney disease."], ["Gut and head", "Headache, nausea and diarrhea are usually mild."]], "journey": "Emtricitabine is taken by mouth, absorbed well and peaks in one to two hours. It is barely bound to plasma proteins and is not metabolized by liver CYP enzymes, so it has few drug interactions. Inside cells, the active triphosphate lasts much longer than the drug in blood, which supports once-daily dosing. It crosses the placenta and enters breast milk. It is cleared unchanged by the kidneys by filtration and secretion, with a plasma half-life of about ten hours.", "sidefx": "Emtricitabine is one of the best-tolerated HIV drugs. Mild headache, nausea and diarrhea are common. Skin hyperpigmentation is benign. Lactic acidosis and fatty liver, a class warning for nucleoside drugs due to mitochondrial DNA polymerase inhibition, are rare because it has low affinity for that enzyme. The boxed warning is the risk of hepatitis B flare when the drug is stopped, because the suppressed virus rebounds. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Emtricitabine is almost the same molecule as lamivudine (3TC), differing only by a fluorine atom, and the two drugs are never combined because they compete for the same activation.", "src": ["Emtriva (emtricitabine) FDA label", "Truvada FDA label", "DHHS Guidelines for the Use of Antiretroviral Agents"]}, "enalapril": {"origin": "Enalapril grew out of snake venom research. In the 1960s Brazilian scientist Sergio Ferreira found that venom of the pit viper Bothrops jararaca contained peptides that blocked angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE). Using that clue, David Cushman and Miguel Ondetti at Squibb designed captopril, approved in 1981. Captopril's sulfhydryl group was linked to rash and taste changes, so a Merck team led by Arthur Patchett designed enalapril without it. Enalapril is a prodrug that the liver converts to the active enalaprilat. Approved in the US in 1985 as Vasotec, it became the first ACE inhibitor shown to improve survival in severe heart failure in the CONSENSUS trial in 1987.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Sergio Ferreira finds ACE-blocking peptides in Bothrops jararaca venom."], ["1981", "Captopril, the first ACE inhibitor, is approved."], ["1985", "FDA approves enalapril (Vasotec)."], ["1987", "The CONSENSUS trial shows enalapril improves survival in severe heart failure."], ["1991", "The SOLVD trial shows benefit in milder heart failure."]], "cell": "Enalapril is an inactive ester prodrug. The liver removes the ester group to form enalaprilat, which binds the zinc-containing active site of angiotensin-converting enzyme. ACE normally converts angiotensin I into angiotensin II. With less angiotensin II, AT1 receptors on blood vessels, adrenal glands, kidneys and heart are less stimulated. Vessels relax, the adrenal cortex releases less aldosterone so the kidney keeps less sodium and loses less potassium, and angiotensin-driven heart and vessel remodeling slows. ACE is also kininase II, the enzyme that breaks down bradykinin, so bradykinin builds up. Bradykinin adds to vasodilation through nitric oxide and prostaglandins but also causes cough and angioedema.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Less angiotensin II and more bradykinin relax arteries, lowering blood pressure and the load on the heart."], ["Heart", "Lower afterload and less remodeling improve survival and symptoms in heart failure and after heart attack."], ["Kidneys", "Angiotensin II normally constricts the efferent arteriole. Removing that lowers glomerular pressure, which protects kidneys long term but can raise creatinine, especially with renal artery stenosis."], ["Adrenal glands and potassium", "Less aldosterone means more potassium is kept, which can lead to hyperkalemia."], ["Lungs", "Bradykinin and substance P build up in the airways, causing a dry cough in some people."], ["Pregnancy", "Blocking the fetal renin-angiotensin system harms fetal kidney development, so it is contraindicated in pregnancy."]], "journey": "Enalapril is taken by mouth and is about sixty percent absorbed, unaffected by food. The liver converts it to enalaprilat, which peaks about three to four hours after a dose. Enalaprilat is also available IV for urgent blood pressure control, acting within about fifteen minutes. Enalaprilat does not cross the blood-brain barrier well. It is cleared mainly by the kidneys, with an effective half-life of about eleven hours, so doses are reduced in kidney disease.", "sidefx": "Dry cough comes from bradykinin buildup in the airways. Angioedema, swelling of the lips, tongue and throat, also comes from bradykinin and is more common in Black patients; it can be life threatening and does not respond well to epinephrine. Hyperkalemia comes from lower aldosterone. A rise in creatinine and acute kidney injury come from losing efferent arteriolar constriction, especially with dehydration, NSAIDs or bilateral renal artery stenosis. First-dose low blood pressure is common when the renin system is highly active. There is no antidote; icatibant or fresh frozen plasma have been used for severe angioedema.", "fun": "The whole ACE inhibitor class exists because of a Brazilian pit viper whose venom causes a sudden drop in blood pressure in its prey.", "src": ["Vasotec (enalapril) FDA label", "CONSENSUS Trial Study Group. N Engl J Med 1987", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "enoxaparin": {"origin": "Enoxaparin comes from heparin, which is purified from pig intestinal lining. Heparin was discovered in 1916 by Jay McLean, a medical student working in the lab of William Howell at Johns Hopkins. Standard heparin is a mix of long sugar chains whose effect varies from person to person and needs frequent blood tests. In the 1970s and 1980s researchers found that shorter heparin chains mainly block factor Xa and behave more predictably. The French company Rhône-Poulenc developed enoxaparin by chemically breaking heparin into smaller pieces. It was approved in the US in 1993 as Lovenox and became the most widely used low molecular weight heparin.", "timeline": [["1916", "Jay McLean discovers heparin in William Howell's lab at Johns Hopkins."], ["1930s", "Purified heparin enters clinical use."], ["1970s", "Researchers show short heparin chains inhibit factor Xa more than thrombin."], ["1993", "FDA approves enoxaparin (Lovenox)."], ["1997", "FDA issues warnings about spinal hematoma with neuraxial anesthesia."]], "cell": "Enoxaparin works through antithrombin, a natural clotting inhibitor in the blood. A special five-sugar sequence (pentasaccharide) on enoxaparin binds antithrombin and changes its shape, speeding up its inhibition of factor Xa about a thousandfold. To block thrombin (factor IIa), heparin must also wrap around thrombin itself, which requires a chain at least eighteen sugars long. Most enoxaparin chains are too short for that, so its anti-Xa effect is several times greater than its anti-IIa effect. Blocking Xa reduces conversion of prothrombin to thrombin and so reduces fibrin formation.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Blocks factor Xa and, to a lesser degree, thrombin, preventing new clots and stopping existing clots from growing."], ["Veins", "Prevents and treats deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism, such as after surgery or in hospitalized patients."], ["Heart", "Used in unstable angina and some heart attacks to limit clot growth in the coronary arteries."], ["Platelets", "Less likely than unfractionated heparin to trigger heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, but it can still occur."], ["Spine", "Bleeding near the spinal cord during epidural or spinal procedures can cause a hematoma and paralysis."], ["Kidneys and potassium", "Cleared by the kidneys, so it builds up in kidney failure; heparins can also suppress aldosterone and raise potassium."]], "journey": "Enoxaparin is injected under the skin, usually in the belly, and is almost fully absorbed. It peaks in three to five hours. It stays mostly in the blood, does not cross the placenta, and binds less to plasma proteins and cells than standard heparin, which makes its effect predictable without routine testing. It is partly broken down in the liver to smaller pieces and cleared mainly by the kidneys. The half-life is about four to seven hours, longer in kidney disease. When monitoring is needed, an anti-Xa level is used, not the aPTT.", "sidefx": "Bleeding and bruising come directly from blocking clotting. Spinal or epidural hematoma is a boxed warning because blood in the closed space presses on the spinal cord. Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia happens when antibodies form against heparin bound to platelet factor 4, activating platelets and causing both low platelets and clots. High potassium can occur from aldosterone suppression. Protamine sulfate, a positively charged protein that binds the negatively charged heparin, partly reverses enoxaparin, neutralizing most of its anti-IIa effect but only part of its anti-Xa effect.", "fun": "Jay McLean was looking for a clotting promoter when he found heparin, which turned out to do the opposite. Its name comes from the Greek word for liver, where it was first extracted.", "src": ["Lovenox (enoxaparin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "eplerenone": {"origin": "Eplerenone was designed as a more selective version of spironolactone, the aldosterone blocker that Searle introduced in 1959. Spironolactone also blocks androgen and progesterone receptors, which causes breast growth and sexual side effects. Chemists modified the steroid structure, adding an epoxy bridge, to create a molecule that binds the mineralocorticoid receptor but barely touches sex hormone receptors. It was patented in 1983 and developed by Searle, later part of Pfizer. Eplerenone was approved in the US in 2002 as Inspra for high blood pressure and in 2003 for heart failure after a heart attack, based on the EPHESUS trial.", "timeline": [["1959", "Searle introduces spironolactone, the first aldosterone antagonist."], ["1983", "Eplerenone is patented."], ["2002", "FDA approves eplerenone (Inspra) for hypertension."], ["2003", "The EPHESUS trial shows improved survival in heart failure after heart attack."], ["2011", "The EMPHASIS-HF trial shows benefit in mild heart failure."]], "cell": "Aldosterone normally enters cells and binds the mineralocorticoid receptor, a nuclear receptor. The complex moves to the nucleus and turns on genes, including those for the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC), the sodium-potassium ATPase and SGK1, in principal cells of the collecting duct. This increases sodium reabsorption and potassium excretion. Eplerenone competitively blocks the mineralocorticoid receptor, so these genes are not switched on. The kidney loses more sodium and water and keeps more potassium. In the heart and vessels, blocking the receptor also reduces aldosterone-driven inflammation, fibrosis and remodeling. Its affinity for androgen and progesterone receptors is very low, unlike spironolactone.", "body": [["Kidneys", "More sodium and water are lost and potassium is kept, giving a mild diuretic effect and a tendency toward high potassium."], ["Heart", "Less aldosterone-driven fibrosis and remodeling improves survival in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction and after heart attack."], ["Blood pressure", "Lowers blood pressure, especially in people with high aldosterone, such as resistant hypertension or primary aldosteronism."], ["Hormones", "Unlike spironolactone, it rarely causes breast enlargement or menstrual changes because it spares sex hormone receptors."], ["Blood lipids", "Can raise triglycerides slightly, a minor lab change seen in clinical trials."]], "journey": "Eplerenone is taken by mouth and peaks in about one and a half hours. It is moderately protein bound. The liver metabolizes it by CYP3A4 into inactive products, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ketoconazole are contraindicated and moderate inhibitors require lower doses. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. The half-life is about four to six hours, much shorter than spironolactone, which has long-lived active metabolites. The full blood pressure effect takes a few weeks.", "sidefx": "Hyperkalemia is the main risk because blocking aldosterone stops potassium secretion in the collecting duct. Risk is higher with kidney disease, diabetes, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, potassium supplements and CYP3A4 inhibitors, so potassium and creatinine are checked often. A rise in creatinine and dizziness follow volume loss. There is no antidote; high potassium is treated with standard measures such as calcium, insulin with glucose and potassium binders.", "fun": "Eplerenone's main selling point is what it does not do: it largely avoids the gynecomastia that limits spironolactone in men.", "src": ["Inspra (eplerenone) FDA label", "Pitt B et al. N Engl J Med 2003 (EPHESUS)", "Zannad F et al. N Engl J Med 2011 (EMPHASIS-HF)"]}, "epoetin-alfa": {"origin": "Erythropoietin is the kidney hormone that tells bone marrow to make red blood cells. Its existence was suspected in the early 1900s, but it took decades to isolate. In 1977 Eugene Goldwasser at the University of Chicago, with Takaji Miyake, finally purified a tiny amount of human erythropoietin from huge volumes of urine from patients with aplastic anemia. In 1985 Fu-Kuen Lin at Amgen used that work to clone the human erythropoietin gene, so the hormone could be made in hamster ovary cells. Epoetin alfa was approved in the US in 1989 as Epogen. It transformed care for kidney failure patients, who had needed frequent blood transfusions.", "timeline": [["1906", "Carnot and Deflandre propose a blood-borne factor that controls red cell production."], ["1977", "Goldwasser and Miyake purify human erythropoietin from urine."], ["1985", "Fu-Kuen Lin at Amgen clones the human erythropoietin gene."], ["1989", "FDA approves epoetin alfa (Epogen)."], ["2007", "FDA adds boxed warnings about death, heart attack, stroke and tumor growth at higher hemoglobin targets."]], "cell": "Epoetin alfa is recombinant human erythropoietin with the same amino acid sequence as the natural hormone. It binds the erythropoietin receptor on red cell precursors in the bone marrow, mainly CFU-E and proerythroblasts. Binding brings two receptor chains together and activates the JAK2 kinase, which phosphorylates STAT5 and turns on the PI3K-Akt and MAPK pathways. These signals keep the precursor cells alive by blocking apoptosis, and make them divide and mature into red blood cells. Iron, folate and vitamin B12 must be available for the new cells to make hemoglobin.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "More red cell precursors survive and mature, raising the reticulocyte count in about a week and hemoglobin over several weeks."], ["Blood", "Higher hemoglobin reduces fatigue and the need for transfusions in kidney disease, chemotherapy and some other anemias."], ["Iron stores", "Rapid red cell production uses up iron, so iron deficiency often develops and must be treated."], ["Blood pressure", "Blood pressure often rises, partly from higher blood viscosity and vessel effects, which is worse in kidney disease."], ["Blood vessels and clotting", "Higher red cell mass and platelet effects raise the risk of clots, heart attack, stroke and dialysis access clotting, especially when hemoglobin is pushed high."], ["Tumors", "Some tumors may have erythropoietin receptors, and studies showed faster tumor growth or shorter survival in some cancers."]], "journey": "Epoetin alfa is injected IV or under the skin. Given under the skin, it is absorbed slowly and peaks after many hours. As a large glycoprotein, it stays mostly in the blood and does not cross the placenta well. It is cleared partly by binding to receptors on red cell precursors and being broken down inside them. The half-life is several hours, longer under the skin than IV. Because the marrow takes time to respond, hemoglobin rises gradually over weeks.", "sidefx": "The serious risks follow from raising red cell mass and stimulating the receptor too strongly. Higher hemoglobin targets increase high blood pressure, seizures, clots, heart attack, stroke and death, so the lowest dose that avoids transfusions is used. Possible tumor progression led to restrictions in cancer patients. Rarely, antibodies form against the drug and also block natural erythropoietin, causing pure red cell aplasia. There is no antidote; the drug is held or reduced.", "fun": "Epoetin became infamous in endurance sports. Undetectable doping with it fueled scandals in professional cycling in the 1990s and 2000s.", "src": ["Epogen (epoetin alfa) FDA label", "Miyake T, Kung CK, Goldwasser E. J Biol Chem 1977", "Lin FK et al. PNAS 1985"]}, "epoprostenol": {"origin": "Epoprostenol is synthetic prostacyclin, a substance made by the lining of blood vessels. It was discovered in 1976 by Salvador Moncada, Ryszard Gryglewski, Stuart Bunting and John Vane at the Wellcome Research Laboratories in England. They found that vessel walls turn prostaglandin precursors into a short-lived substance that relaxes vessels and stops platelets from sticking. Vane shared the 1982 Nobel Prize for his prostaglandin work. Because prostacyclin breaks down within minutes, it must be given by continuous infusion. In 1995 the FDA approved it as Flolan, the first drug approved for pulmonary arterial hypertension. It improved survival in severe disease, which until then often meant a lung transplant or death.", "timeline": [["1976", "Moncada, Vane and colleagues discover prostacyclin at Wellcome."], ["1982", "John Vane shares the Nobel Prize for prostaglandin research."], ["1995", "FDA approves epoprostenol (Flolan) as the first drug for pulmonary arterial hypertension."], ["2008", "A more heat-stable formulation (Veletri) is approved."]], "cell": "Epoprostenol binds the prostacyclin (IP) receptor, a Gs-coupled receptor on vascular smooth muscle and platelets. Activated Gs stimulates adenylyl cyclase and raises cAMP. In smooth muscle, cAMP activates protein kinase A, which lowers intracellular calcium and inhibits myosin light-chain kinase, so the muscle relaxes and the vessel widens. In platelets, higher cAMP blocks calcium release and shape change, so platelets do not aggregate. In pulmonary arterial hypertension, vessel cells make too little prostacyclin; replacing it lowers pulmonary vascular resistance and may slow harmful vessel remodeling.", "body": [["Lung vessels", "Widens pulmonary arteries and lowers pulmonary vascular resistance and pressure."], ["Right heart", "Lower resistance lets the strained right ventricle pump more easily, raising cardiac output and exercise ability."], ["Systemic vessels", "Also widens vessels throughout the body, causing flushing, headache and low blood pressure."], ["Platelets", "Blocks platelet aggregation, which raises bleeding risk."], ["Jaw and limbs", "Jaw pain on first bite and leg or foot pain are common class effects of prostacyclins."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are common."]], "journey": "Epoprostenol is given as a continuous IV infusion through a permanent central line using a portable pump. In hospitals it can also be inhaled to dilate lung vessels in ventilated patients. It is very unstable and breaks down quickly in the blood, both by spontaneous hydrolysis and by enzymes, into much weaker products. The half-life is only a few minutes, so effects begin within minutes and also end within minutes. Metabolites are cleared by the kidneys.", "sidefx": "Flushing, headache, jaw pain, low blood pressure and nausea all come from IP receptor activation and vasodilation outside the lungs. Bleeding risk comes from platelet inhibition. The most dangerous risk is sudden interruption, such as a pump failure or a kinked line. Because the half-life is minutes, pulmonary pressures can rebound within minutes and cause collapse or death, so patients keep backup pumps and supplies. The permanent central line also brings a risk of bloodstream infection. There is no antidote; overdose is managed by lowering the infusion rate.", "fun": "Prostacyclin was first called PGX before its structure was known. Its job in the body is the opposite of platelet thromboxane, and the two keep clotting in balance.", "src": ["Flolan (epoprostenol) FDA label", "Moncada S et al. Nature 1976", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "eptifibatide": {"origin": "Eptifibatide was inspired by rattlesnake venom. Around 1990 researchers at COR Therapeutics in California studied barbourin, a protein from the venom of the southeastern pygmy rattlesnake (Sistrurus miliarius barbouri). Many snake venoms prevent blood clotting with proteins containing the amino acid sequence RGD, which blocks several integrins. Barbourin was unusual: it had a KGD sequence that blocked only the platelet glycoprotein IIb/IIIa receptor. Scientists built a small ring-shaped peptide around this idea, and eptifibatide was approved in the US in 1998 as Integrilin for acute coronary syndromes and coronary stenting.", "timeline": [["1991", "Barbourin is reported from pygmy rattlesnake venom as a selective IIb/IIIa blocker."], ["1998", "The PURSUIT trial shows benefit in acute coronary syndromes."], ["1998", "FDA approves eptifibatide (Integrilin)."]], "cell": "When platelets are activated by thrombin, ADP, collagen or thromboxane, their glycoprotein IIb/IIIa receptors (integrin alpha-IIb beta-3) change shape and become able to bind fibrinogen and von Willebrand factor. Because each fibrinogen molecule can bridge two platelets, this is the final common step of platelet aggregation. Eptifibatide is a cyclic heptapeptide that mimics the binding sequence of fibrinogen. It occupies the IIb/IIIa receptor competitively and reversibly, so fibrinogen cannot link platelets together, no matter what activated them. Platelets can still stick to the vessel wall, but they cannot clump into a large plug.", "body": [["Coronary arteries", "Blocking platelet clumping on a ruptured plaque or new stent reduces death and heart attack in acute coronary syndromes and during PCI."], ["Platelets", "Platelet aggregation is nearly fully blocked during the infusion and returns within hours after stopping."], ["Bleeding sites", "Bleeding is common at the arterial access site, in the gut and urinary tract, and rarely in the brain."], ["Platelet count", "Rarely, the platelet count falls sharply, possibly from antibodies against drug-bound receptors."], ["Kidneys", "About half the drug is cleared by the kidneys, so doses are reduced in kidney disease."]], "journey": "Eptifibatide is given IV as a bolus followed by an infusion. Platelet inhibition starts within minutes. It is only lightly protein bound. It undergoes little metabolism, and about half is cleared by the kidneys, partly as the deaminated form. The half-life is about two and a half hours, and platelet function recovers about four to eight hours after the infusion stops. This short, reversible action differs from abciximab, an antibody that binds receptors for much longer.", "sidefx": "Bleeding is the main side effect because blocking the final step of platelet aggregation impairs clot formation everywhere. Risk rises with heparin, other antiplatelet drugs, older age, low body weight and kidney disease. Thrombocytopenia is uncommon but can be profound and sudden. There is no specific antidote; stopping the infusion allows recovery within hours, and because it is a small molecule that is not bound tightly, platelet transfusion is less effective than with abciximab. Dialysis can remove it.", "fun": "The KGD in eptifibatide stands for lysine-glycine-aspartate. Changing one amino acid from the venom RGD motif made the drug specific for platelets.", "src": ["Integrilin (eptifibatide) FDA label", "PURSUIT Trial Investigators. N Engl J Med 1998", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "erythromycin": {"origin": "Erythromycin came from a soil sample in the Philippines. In 1949 the Filipino physician and scientist Abelardo Aguilar, who worked for Eli Lilly, sent soil from Iloilo to the company's research labs in Indianapolis. A Lilly team led by James McGuire isolated a new antibiotic from the soil bacterium then called Streptomyces erythreus (now Saccharopolyspora erythraea). Lilly marketed it in 1952 as Ilotycin, and a later salt was named Ilosone after Iloilo. Erythromycin became the first macrolide antibiotic and an important option for people allergic to penicillin. It was later the parent of azithromycin and clarithromycin.", "timeline": [["1949", "Abelardo Aguilar sends soil samples from Iloilo, Philippines, to Eli Lilly."], ["1952", "McGuire and colleagues report erythromycin, and Lilly brings it to market."], ["1980s", "Erythromycin is recognized as a motilin receptor agonist that speeds gastric emptying."], ["1990s", "Clarithromycin and azithromycin, derived from erythromycin, are approved in the US."]], "cell": "Erythromycin is a macrolide, a large lactone ring with sugars attached. It binds the 23S rRNA in the 50S subunit of the bacterial ribosome, at the entrance of the tunnel through which new proteins exit. This blocks translocation, the step in which the ribosome moves along the mRNA, so the growing peptide chain cannot lengthen. Protein synthesis stops and bacteria stop growing, which is mostly bacteriostatic. Resistance comes from methylation of the 23S rRNA binding site (erm genes) or efflux pumps (mef genes). Separately, erythromycin mimics the gut hormone motilin and activates motilin receptors on gut smooth muscle, which strengthens stomach contractions.", "body": [["Lungs and throat", "Treats atypical pneumonia organisms, pertussis and streptococcal infections in penicillin-allergic patients."], ["Eyes", "Erythromycin eye ointment is given to newborns to prevent gonococcal eye infection."], ["Stomach", "Motilin receptor activation speeds gastric emptying, which is used for gastroparesis and to clear the stomach before endoscopy for bleeding."], ["Gut", "The same motilin effect causes cramping, nausea and diarrhea, the most common reasons people stop it."], ["Heart", "Blocks the hERG potassium channel, lengthening the QT interval and rarely causing torsades de pointes."], ["Liver", "The estolate salt in particular can cause cholestatic hepatitis."], ["Infant stomach", "Use in young infants has been linked to pyloric stenosis."]], "journey": "Erythromycin is destroyed by stomach acid, so oral forms are coated or made as acid-stable salts or esters. It can also be given IV, which is painful in the vein. It spreads into most tissues and inside cells, but not well into the brain. It crosses the placenta and enters breast milk. The liver metabolizes it by CYP3A4, and it strongly inhibits CYP3A4, which raises levels of many drugs. Most is excreted in bile, so little dose change is needed in kidney disease. The half-life is about one and a half to two hours.", "sidefx": "GI cramping and diarrhea are mainly from motilin receptor stimulation, not just changes in gut flora. QT prolongation comes from hERG channel blockade, and the risk rises sharply when CYP3A4 inhibition raises its own level or when combined with other QT drugs. CYP3A4 inhibition also raises levels of drugs like simvastatin (risk of muscle injury), carbamazepine and some calcium channel blockers. Cholestatic hepatitis is thought to be a hypersensitivity reaction. High IV doses can cause reversible hearing loss. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Lilly named the erythromycin estolate brand Ilosone in honor of Iloilo, the Philippine province whose soil gave the world its first macrolide.", "src": ["Erythromycin FDA label", "McGuire JM et al. Antibiotics and Chemotherapy 1952", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "escitalopram": {"origin": "Escitalopram was developed by the Danish company Lundbeck. Lundbeck first made citalopram in 1972, a racemic mixture of two mirror-image molecules. Citalopram was approved in the US in 1998. Researchers found that nearly all the antidepressant activity came from the S-enantiomer, while the R-enantiomer added little and might even work against it. Lundbeck isolated the S-form as escitalopram, a so-called chiral switch. It was approved in the US in 2002 as Lexapro, marketed by Forest Laboratories, for depression and later for generalized anxiety disorder.", "timeline": [["1972", "Lundbeck first synthesizes citalopram."], ["1998", "Citalopram (Celexa) is approved in the United States."], ["2002", "FDA approves escitalopram (Lexapro) for major depression."], ["2003", "Escitalopram is approved for generalized anxiety disorder."], ["2009", "Escitalopram is approved for depression in adolescents."]], "cell": "Escitalopram is a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI) and the most selective of the class. It binds the main site on the serotonin transporter (SERT) and also an allosteric site that slows its own release from the transporter. Blocking SERT stops presynaptic neurons from pulling serotonin back, so serotonin rises in the synapse. Early on, extra serotonin activates 5-HT1A autoreceptors, which reduce firing. Over two to six weeks these autoreceptors desensitize, serotonin release increases, and downstream changes in receptor signaling and brain-derived neurotrophic factor occur. This delay explains why mood improves over weeks rather than days. It has very little effect on norepinephrine, dopamine, histamine or muscarinic receptors.", "body": [["Brain (mood and anxiety)", "Gradually reduces depression, worry and panic as serotonin circuits adapt over weeks."], ["Gut", "Serotonin acts on gut and brainstem 5-HT3 receptors, causing nausea and loose stools, mostly early in treatment."], ["Sexual function", "Serotonin dampens sexual response through 5-HT2 receptors and reduced dopamine, causing low libido, delayed orgasm and erectile problems."], ["Platelets", "Platelets rely on SERT to take up serotonin for clotting. Blocking it depletes their serotonin and raises bleeding risk, especially with NSAIDs or anticoagulants."], ["Kidneys and sodium", "Can cause SIADH and low sodium, especially in older adults."], ["Heart", "Can lengthen the QT interval slightly, more at higher doses."]], "journey": "Escitalopram is taken by mouth, absorbed well and peaks in about five hours. It is about half bound to plasma proteins, crosses into the brain and placenta, and enters breast milk. The liver metabolizes it by CYP2C19, CYP3A4 and CYP2D6 into weaker metabolites, and it barely inhibits CYP enzymes, so it has fewer drug interactions than some SSRIs. Metabolites leave mainly in urine. The half-life is about twenty-seven to thirty-two hours, longer in older adults and poor CYP2C19 metabolizers.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from serotonin acting outside mood circuits: nausea, insomnia or drowsiness, sweating and sexual dysfunction. Bleeding risk comes from platelet serotonin depletion. Low sodium comes from SIADH. Combined with other serotonergic drugs like MAO inhibitors, linezolid or tramadol, it can cause serotonin syndrome, treated by stopping the drugs, benzodiazepines and sometimes cyproheptadine. Stopping suddenly can cause discontinuation symptoms. It carries a boxed warning about suicidal thinking in children and young adults.", "fun": "Escitalopram is a classic example of a chiral switch: taking one mirror-image form of an existing racemic drug and marketing it as a new drug.", "src": ["Lexapro (escitalopram) FDA label", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "esmolol": {"origin": "Esmolol was designed in the early 1980s as an ultra-short-acting beta blocker for critical care and surgery, where doctors needed to slow the heart quickly and stop the effect quickly if blood pressure fell. Researchers built an ester group into the molecule so that enzymes in red blood cells would break it down in minutes. This made it a classic soft drug, one designed to be inactivated predictably. It was patented in 1980 and approved in the US in 1986 as Brevibloc. It is used for rapid atrial fibrillation, perioperative tachycardia and high blood pressure, including in aortic dissection.", "timeline": [["1980", "Esmolol is patented as an ultra-short-acting beta-1 blocker."], ["1986", "Esmolol (Brevibloc) is approved in the United States."]], "cell": "Esmolol is a selective beta-1 adrenergic receptor antagonist without intrinsic sympathomimetic activity. Beta-1 receptors in the heart couple to Gs, raise cAMP and activate protein kinase A, which increases the pacemaker current in the SA node, calcium entry through L-type channels, and conduction through the AV node. By competitively blocking these receptors, esmolol reduces cAMP signaling. The SA node fires more slowly, AV conduction slows and refractoriness lengthens, and contraction weakens. This lowers heart rate, blood pressure and oxygen demand. In aortic dissection, lower heart rate and force of ejection reduce shear stress on the torn aortic wall.", "body": [["SA node", "Slows the heart rate quickly, within minutes."], ["AV node", "Slows conduction, which controls ventricular rate in atrial fibrillation and flutter."], ["Heart muscle", "Weaker contraction and slower rate reduce oxygen demand, helping ischemia."], ["Aorta", "Lower heart rate and force reduce stress on the wall of an aortic dissection."], ["Blood pressure", "Blood pressure falls as cardiac output drops, sometimes more than intended."], ["Lungs", "Beta-1 selectivity limits bronchospasm, but at higher levels it can still block beta-2 receptors in the airways."]], "journey": "Esmolol is given only IV, as a bolus and continuous infusion. It works within one to two minutes. Esterases in red blood cells, not plasma cholinesterase, quickly split its ester bond into an inactive acid metabolite and methanol. The half-life is about nine minutes, so its effects fade within about ten to thirty minutes after stopping. Liver and kidney disease barely affect its breakdown, though the acid metabolite is cleared by the kidneys.", "sidefx": "Low blood pressure is the most common side effect, from reduced cardiac output. Bradycardia and heart block come from too much SA and AV nodal blockade. Heart failure can worsen because contraction weakens. Bronchospasm is possible at higher levels from beta-2 blockade. Infusion site irritation and necrosis can occur with leaking. Its very short half-life is the main safety feature, since stopping the drug reverses effects within minutes. For severe overdose, glucagon raises cAMP by bypassing the beta receptor.", "fun": "Esmolol's name hints at its design: es for the ester that red blood cell enzymes cut, and -olol for beta blocker.", "src": ["Brevibloc (esmolol) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "eszopiclone": {"origin": "Eszopiclone is the active mirror-image form of zopiclone, a sleep drug developed by the French company Rhône-Poulenc and used in Europe since the 1980s. Zopiclone is one of the Z-drugs, non-benzodiazepine sleep medicines that act at the same GABA-A receptor. The US company Sepracor isolated the S-enantiomer and developed it as eszopiclone. It was approved in the US in 2004 as Lunesta and was the first sleep drug approved without a short-term limit on use. In 2019 the FDA added a boxed warning about complex sleep behaviors such as sleepwalking and sleep driving.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Zopiclone is introduced in Europe."], ["2004", "FDA approves eszopiclone (Lunesta)."], ["2014", "FDA lowers the recommended starting dose because of next-morning impairment."], ["2019", "FDA adds a boxed warning about complex sleep behaviors to Z-drugs."]], "cell": "The GABA-A receptor is a chloride channel made of five subunits. Eszopiclone binds at the benzodiazepine site, between alpha and gamma subunits, and acts as a positive allosteric modulator. It does not open the channel itself but makes GABA open it more often. More chloride enters neurons, making them more negative inside (hyperpolarized) and less likely to fire. Reduced firing in arousal circuits brings on sleep. Unlike zolpidem, which prefers alpha-1-containing receptors, eszopiclone acts on receptors with several alpha subunits, which may add mild anxiety-reducing and muscle-relaxing effects. Flumazenil can block this site.", "body": [["Brain (sleep)", "Shortens the time to fall asleep and helps maintain sleep through the night."], ["Brain (memory and coordination)", "Can impair memory, attention and balance, and cause next-morning drowsiness and impaired driving."], ["Complex behaviors", "Some people sleepwalk, drive, cook or make calls while not fully awake and remember nothing."], ["Mouth", "A bitter or metallic taste is common because the drug is secreted into saliva."], ["Breathing", "Alone it has little effect on breathing, but with alcohol, opioids or other sedatives it can depress breathing."]], "journey": "Eszopiclone is taken by mouth right before bed. It is absorbed quickly and peaks in about an hour; a heavy, high-fat meal slows its onset. It is weakly protein bound and crosses into the brain. The liver metabolizes it by CYP3A4 and CYP2E1, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels. Metabolites leave mainly in urine. The half-life is about six hours, longer in older adults, which can cause next-morning impairment.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness, dizziness and next-day impairment follow from enhanced GABA-A inhibition lasting into the morning. Amnesia and complex sleep behaviors happen when parts of the brain are suppressed while others are active. Combining with alcohol or opioids adds CNS and respiratory depression. Long use can cause tolerance and dependence, and stopping suddenly can cause rebound insomnia. The taste problem comes from drug in saliva. Flumazenil, a benzodiazepine-site antagonist, can reverse an overdose but can trigger seizures in dependent people.", "fun": "The bitter taste from eszopiclone is so common that it is one of the most reported side effects in its clinical trials.", "src": ["Lunesta (eszopiclone) FDA label", "FDA Drug Safety Communication 2019 on complex sleep behaviors", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "etanercept": {"origin": "Etanercept grew from the discovery that the immune signal tumor necrosis factor (TNF) drives joint damage in rheumatoid arthritis. In the early 1990s Bruce Beutler and colleagues showed that a molecule built by joining a TNF receptor to part of an antibody could soak up TNF. The Seattle company Immunex developed etanercept, joining two copies of the human p75 TNF receptor to the Fc region of human IgG1. It was approved in the US in 1998 as Enbrel for rheumatoid arthritis, the same year infliximab was approved, launching the era of biologic therapy for autoimmune disease.", "timeline": [["1975", "Tumor necrosis factor is described as a factor that kills tumors in mice."], ["1991", "Beutler and colleagues report a TNF receptor-IgG fusion protein that neutralizes TNF."], ["1998", "FDA approves etanercept (Enbrel) for rheumatoid arthritis."], ["2004", "Etanercept is approved for plaque psoriasis."]], "cell": "TNF-alpha is made by activated macrophages and T cells. It binds TNF receptors on many cells and turns on NF-kappa-B, which drives inflammatory cytokines (IL-1, IL-6), adhesion molecules, and enzymes like matrix metalloproteinases that destroy cartilage and bone. Etanercept is a decoy receptor: a dimer of the ligand-binding part of TNF receptor 2 fused to an IgG1 Fc tail. It binds soluble and membrane TNF-alpha, as well as lymphotoxin-alpha (TNF-beta), so they cannot reach cell receptors. The inflammatory cascade quiets, synovial inflammation falls and joint damage slows. Unlike monoclonal antibodies such as infliximab, it does not cause much killing of TNF-bearing cells, which may explain why it does not work in Crohn disease.", "body": [["Joints", "Less inflammation in the joint lining reduces pain, swelling and stiffness and slows erosion of cartilage and bone."], ["Skin", "Reduces psoriatic plaques by calming TNF-driven inflammation in the skin."], ["Spine", "Improves pain and stiffness in ankylosing spondylitis."], ["Immune defense", "TNF is needed to keep granulomas intact, so blocking it can reactivate latent tuberculosis and fungal infections."], ["Nerves", "Rarely triggers or worsens demyelinating disease such as multiple sclerosis."], ["Blood", "Can rarely cause low blood counts, including aplastic anemia."], ["Injection site", "Redness and swelling at the injection site are very common."]], "journey": "Etanercept is injected under the skin, usually once or twice a week. As a large protein it is absorbed slowly through the lymphatic system and peaks in about two to three days. It stays mainly in the blood and tissue fluid. It is not metabolized by liver enzymes; like other proteins it is broken down into amino acids. The half-life is about four days. Clinical benefit appears over a few weeks and builds over months.", "sidefx": "Serious infections are the main risk because TNF helps the body fight bacteria, fungi and mycobacteria and maintain granulomas around latent TB. This is why patients are screened for TB and hepatitis B before starting, and live vaccines are avoided. Demyelination, lupus-like syndrome, cytopenias and new or worsening heart failure are less common immune-related effects. Lymphoma and other cancers carry a boxed warning, especially in children and young adults. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped and infections are treated.", "fun": "The suffix -cept marks a receptor fusion protein, and etanercept's name tells you it is a TNF receptor attached to an antibody tail.", "src": ["Enbrel (etanercept) FDA label", "Peppel K, Crawford D, Beutler B. J Exp Med 1991", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "ethacrynic-acid": {"origin": "Ethacrynic acid was developed by Merck in the early 1960s as part of a search for powerful new diuretics. Older mercury-based diuretics worked by binding sulfhydryl groups in the kidney, and Merck chemists designed a non-mercury molecule with a reactive group that could do something similar. The result, sold as Edecrin, was one of the first loop diuretics, alongside furosemide. Unlike furosemide, bumetanide and torsemide, ethacrynic acid has no sulfonamide group. That makes it the loop diuretic of choice for people with true sulfa allergy, its main modern use.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Mercury-based diuretics are the strongest diuretics in common use."], ["1960s", "Merck develops ethacrynic acid, an early non-sulfonamide loop diuretic."], ["1960s", "Ethacrynic acid and furosemide establish the loop diuretic class."]], "cell": "In the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle, the Na-K-2Cl cotransporter (NKCC2) on the luminal membrane reabsorbs about a quarter of filtered sodium. Ethacrynic acid, after secretion into the tubule, inhibits NKCC2. Sodium, potassium and chloride stay in the urine, and water follows, causing strong diuresis. Because NKCC2 also generates the positive voltage in the lumen that drives calcium and magnesium reabsorption, more calcium and magnesium are lost too. More sodium reaching the collecting duct increases potassium and hydrogen secretion, causing low potassium and metabolic alkalosis. Blocking a related transporter in the inner ear disturbs endolymph, which explains ototoxicity.", "body": [["Kidneys", "Causes strong, fast diuresis that removes excess fluid in heart failure, cirrhosis and kidney disease."], ["Lungs", "Removing fluid reduces pulmonary congestion and eases breathing in acute pulmonary edema."], ["Electrolytes", "Causes losses of potassium, magnesium, calcium and chloride, with metabolic alkalosis."], ["Ears", "More ototoxic than other loop diuretics; can cause ringing, hearing loss and vertigo, sometimes permanent."], ["Gut", "Causes more GI upset and diarrhea than other loop diuretics and has been linked to GI bleeding."], ["Blood volume", "Too much diuresis causes dehydration, low blood pressure and kidney injury."]], "journey": "Ethacrynic acid is taken by mouth or given IV as its sodium salt. Orally it works within about half an hour and lasts several hours; IV it works within minutes. It is highly protein bound, so it reaches its site of action mainly by active secretion into the proximal tubule. Part is conjugated with cysteine in the liver to an active metabolite. It leaves in bile and urine. The half-life is short, about an hour.", "sidefx": "Electrolyte problems and dehydration follow directly from blocking NKCC2. Ototoxicity is more frequent than with other loop diuretics and is worse with high IV doses, kidney failure and other ototoxic drugs like aminoglycosides; it reflects changes in ion transport in the inner ear. GI irritation is more common and may relate to its reactive structure. Low potassium can increase the risk of digoxin toxicity. There is no antidote; losses are replaced.", "fun": "Ethacrynic acid is the answer to a classic exam question: which loop diuretic can be used in a patient with sulfa allergy.", "src": ["Edecrin (ethacrynic acid) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "ethambutol": {"origin": "Ethambutol was discovered at Lederle Laboratories in the United States. In 1961 a research team reported that a new synthetic compound stopped the growth of tuberculosis bacteria in lab and animal studies. Tuberculosis treatment then relied on streptomycin, isoniazid and para-aminosalicylic acid, which caused many side effects. Ethambutol was easier to take and helped prevent resistance when combined with other drugs. Approved in the US in the 1960s as Myambutol, it became one of the four first-line drugs for tuberculosis (RIPE: rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, ethambutol).", "timeline": [["1944", "Streptomycin becomes the first effective drug against tuberculosis."], ["1961", "Lederle researchers report the antituberculosis activity of ethambutol."], ["1960s", "Ethambutol is approved in the United States."], ["1990s", "Ethambutol becomes part of standard four-drug initial therapy for tuberculosis."]], "cell": "Mycobacteria have a thick, waxy cell wall built on a framework of peptidoglycan linked to arabinogalactan, which in turn anchors mycolic acids. Ethambutol inhibits arabinosyl transferases, enzymes encoded by the embCAB genes, that add arabinose units to build arabinogalactan and lipoarabinomannan. Without arabinogalactan, mycolic acids cannot attach properly, so the wall becomes defective and more permeable. Bacterial growth stops, a bacteriostatic effect. The leakier wall may also help other drugs enter. Mutations in embB are the main cause of resistance.", "body": [["Lungs and infected tissue", "Stops growth of Mycobacterium tuberculosis and Mycobacterium avium complex and helps prevent resistance to the other drugs."], ["Eyes", "Can cause optic neuritis, with blurred vision, reduced visual acuity and loss of red-green color vision."], ["Joints", "Reduces uric acid excretion by the kidney, which can raise uric acid and trigger gout."], ["Gut and head", "Nausea, loss of appetite and headache can occur."], ["Kidneys", "Most of the drug is cleared by the kidneys, so it builds up in kidney disease and increases eye toxicity."]], "journey": "Ethambutol is taken by mouth and about eighty percent is absorbed. It peaks in two to four hours. It spreads into most tissues and into red blood cells, and it reaches the spinal fluid mainly when the meninges are inflamed. A small part is metabolized in the liver. Most is excreted unchanged in urine, with some in feces. The half-life is about three to four hours, longer in kidney disease.", "sidefx": "Optic neuritis is the key toxicity. It is related to dose and duration and is thought to involve zinc and copper chelation and damage to mitochondria in the optic nerve. It usually improves if the drug is stopped early, but can be permanent. This is why visual acuity and red-green color testing are done at baseline and monthly, and why it is used with care in young children who cannot report vision changes. High uric acid comes from reduced renal urate excretion. There is no antidote.", "fun": "A board mnemonic: ethambutol hurts the eyes, so think E for eyes, starting with red-green color blindness.", "src": ["Myambutol (ethambutol) FDA label", "Thomas JP et al. American Review of Respiratory Disease 1961", "CDC/ATS/IDSA Treatment of Drug-Susceptible Tuberculosis guidelines"]}, "ethinyl-estradiol": {"origin": "Ethinyl estradiol was made in 1938 by the chemists Hans Herloff Inhoffen and Walter Hohlweg at Schering AG in Berlin. Natural estradiol was almost useless by mouth because the liver destroyed it so quickly. Adding an ethinyl group at the 17-alpha position blocked that breakdown, creating the first potent oral estrogen. In 1960 the first birth control pill, Enovid, was approved in the US; it contained mestranol, a prodrug the liver converts to ethinyl estradiol. Ethinyl estradiol itself later became the estrogen in most combined oral contraceptives, patches and vaginal rings. Over the years, doses were lowered a great deal to reduce clotting risk.", "timeline": [["1938", "Inhoffen and Hohlweg synthesize ethinyl estradiol at Schering AG."], ["1951", "Carl Djerassi's team synthesizes the progestin norethindrone."], ["1960", "FDA approves Enovid, the first oral contraceptive."], ["1970s", "Lower estrogen pills are introduced to reduce clotting risk."]], "cell": "Ethinyl estradiol binds nuclear estrogen receptors ER-alpha and ER-beta. The hormone-receptor complex dimerizes, binds estrogen response elements on DNA and changes gene transcription. In the hypothalamus and pituitary, constant estrogen exerts negative feedback, lowering GnRH pulses and FSH. Without an FSH rise, no dominant follicle develops. The progestin in combination products suppresses the LH surge, preventing ovulation, and thickens cervical mucus. Estrogen also stabilizes the endometrium, reducing breakthrough bleeding. In the liver, estrogen receptor activation increases production of SHBG, clotting factors (II, VII, IX, X and fibrinogen), angiotensinogen and binding proteins like TBG.", "body": [["Ovaries", "Suppressed FSH and LH prevent follicle growth and ovulation, which is the main contraceptive effect."], ["Uterus", "Stabilizes the endometrium, giving more regular, lighter bleeding and less menstrual pain."], ["Liver", "More clotting factors, SHBG, TBG and angiotensinogen are made. This raises clot risk and blood pressure and changes thyroid and hormone lab values."], ["Blood clotting", "Raises the risk of deep vein thrombosis, pulmonary embolism and stroke, especially in smokers over 35 and people with clotting disorders."], ["Skin", "Higher SHBG lowers free testosterone, which improves acne and unwanted hair growth."], ["Gallbladder and breasts", "Estrogen raises cholesterol in bile, increasing gallstone risk, and can cause breast tenderness and nausea."], ["Bones", "Estrogen slows bone breakdown by osteoclasts."]], "journey": "Ethinyl estradiol is taken by mouth, through a skin patch or with a vaginal ring. By mouth it is well absorbed and peaks in one to two hours, but part is broken down in the gut wall and liver. It is highly bound to albumin and, unlike natural estradiol, does not bind SHBG. The liver metabolizes it by CYP3A4 and by sulfate and glucuronide conjugation. Gut bacteria can split the conjugates and allow reabsorption (enterohepatic recirculation). Drugs that induce CYP3A4, such as rifampin, carbamazepine and St. John's wort, can make it fail. The half-life is about ten to twenty hours.", "sidefx": "The biggest risk is venous blood clots from increased liver production of clotting factors and reduced natural anticoagulants. Smoking adds arterial risk, which is why combined pills carry a boxed warning for women over 35 who smoke. Higher angiotensinogen can raise blood pressure. Nausea, breast tenderness and bloating are direct estrogen effects. Cholestasis, gallstones and rarely hepatic adenomas relate to effects on the liver. Melasma comes from estrogen stimulating skin pigment cells. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped.", "fun": "The 17-alpha ethinyl group that protects ethinyl estradiol from the liver is the same chemical trick used in the early progestin norethindrone.", "src": ["Combined oral contraceptive FDA labeling", "Inhoffen HH, Hohlweg W. Naturwissenschaften 1938", "Speroff's Clinical Gynecologic Endocrinology and Infertility"]}, "ethosuximide": {"origin": "Ethosuximide belongs to the succinimides, a drug family studied in the 1950s at the US company Parke-Davis as researchers searched for safer treatments for absence (petit mal) seizures. The older drug trimethadione worked but caused serious side effects. Ethosuximide was approved in the US in 1960 as Zarontin. Decades later, in 1989, researchers including Douglas Coulter, John Huguenard and David Prince showed that it works by reducing T-type calcium currents in thalamic neurons. It remains a first-line drug for childhood absence epilepsy.", "timeline": [["1940s", "Trimethadione becomes the first drug used for absence seizures."], ["1960", "Ethosuximide (Zarontin) is approved in the United States."], ["1989", "Coulter, Huguenard and Prince show it reduces T-type calcium currents in thalamic neurons."], ["2010", "A large randomized trial finds ethosuximide as effective as valproate and better tolerated for childhood absence epilepsy."]], "cell": "Absence seizures come from an abnormal rhythm between the thalamus and the cortex. Thalamic relay neurons have low-threshold T-type calcium channels (Cav3.1 to Cav3.3). When these neurons are hyperpolarized, the channels recover and then open in a burst, driving rhythmic firing at about three cycles per second. This creates the classic 3 Hz spike-and-wave pattern on EEG. Ethosuximide blocks T-type calcium channels, especially when they are in their inactive state, and reduces these bursts. The thalamocortical circuit loses its ability to sustain the oscillation, so absence seizures stop. It has little effect on the sodium channel and GABA systems involved in other seizure types.", "body": [["Brain (thalamus)", "Stops the 3 Hz spike-and-wave discharges of absence seizures, ending staring spells."], ["Other seizures", "Does not protect against tonic-clonic or focal seizures, so another drug is needed if these also occur."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting, stomach pain and loss of appetite are common, often reduced by taking it with food."], ["Brain (behavior)", "Can cause drowsiness, headache, hiccups and sometimes behavioral or mood changes."], ["Blood", "Rarely lowers white cells or other blood counts, including aplastic anemia."], ["Skin and immune system", "Rarely causes severe rashes such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome or a lupus-like syndrome."]], "journey": "Ethosuximide is taken by mouth as capsules or syrup and is almost completely absorbed, peaking in a few hours. It is barely bound to plasma proteins and spreads evenly in body water, including the brain and spinal fluid. The liver metabolizes it, mainly by CYP3A4, to inactive products that leave in urine. The half-life is long, about thirty hours in children and about sixty hours in adults, so steady levels take about a week.", "sidefx": "GI upset is the most common side effect and is a direct effect of the drug, often dose related. Drowsiness, headache and hiccups reflect its central actions. Rare blood dyscrasias and severe skin reactions are idiosyncratic immune-mediated reactions, so blood counts are checked and rashes taken seriously. A lupus-like syndrome can occur. Strong CYP3A4 inducers lower its levels and inhibitors raise them. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The board phrase is ethosuximide for absence: it is the drug of choice when a child has staring spells with 3 Hz spike-and-wave on EEG and no other seizure types.", "src": ["Zarontin (ethosuximide) FDA label", "Coulter DA, Huguenard JR, Prince DA. Annals of Neurology 1989", "Glauser TA et al. N Engl J Med 2010"]}, "etomidate": {"origin": "Etomidate was developed at Janssen Pharmaceutica in Belgium in 1964, a company that also created fentanyl, haloperidol and droperidol. It is an imidazole compound, chemically unlike other anesthetics. It was introduced as an IV anesthetic in Europe in 1972 and in the US in 1983 as Amidate. Its key advantage is that it causes sleep with very little drop in blood pressure. In 1983 Ian Ledingham and Iain Watt reported higher death rates in ICU trauma patients sedated with continuous etomidate infusions, because the drug suppresses the adrenal glands. Infusions stopped, but single doses remain widely used for rapid sequence intubation.", "timeline": [["1964", "Etomidate is developed at Janssen Pharmaceutica."], ["1972", "Etomidate is introduced as an IV anesthetic in Europe."], ["1983", "Etomidate (Amidate) is introduced in the United States."], ["1983", "Ledingham and Watt link etomidate infusions to higher ICU mortality from adrenal suppression."], ["2009", "A randomized trial finds no mortality difference between etomidate and ketamine for emergency intubation."]], "cell": "Etomidate is a positive allosteric modulator of the GABA-A receptor. It binds at the interface between beta and alpha subunits, particularly receptors with beta-2 or beta-3 subunits, and increases the effect of GABA. At higher levels it can open the chloride channel directly. More chloride enters neurons, hyperpolarizing them and making firing less likely. This rapidly depresses the reticular activating system and cortex, causing unconsciousness. It has no analgesic effect. Separately, its imidazole structure inhibits 11-beta-hydroxylase in the adrenal cortex, blocking the conversion of 11-deoxycortisol to cortisol, and also affects aldosterone synthesis.", "body": [["Brain", "Causes rapid loss of consciousness and lowers brain metabolism, blood flow and intracranial pressure."], ["Heart and vessels", "Has very little effect on heart rate, contractility or blood pressure, making it useful in unstable or trauma patients."], ["Lungs", "Causes less breathing depression than many induction agents, though brief apnea can occur."], ["Adrenal glands", "Even one dose blocks cortisol production for many hours, which is concerning in septic shock."], ["Muscles", "Myoclonus (involuntary jerks) is common after injection."], ["Veins and gut", "Pain on injection and nausea and vomiting after anesthesia are common."]], "journey": "Etomidate is given IV. It is very fat soluble, so it reaches the brain within one arm-to-brain circulation time, causing sleep within a minute. It then redistributes from brain to muscle and fat, so the patient wakes in a few minutes. It is about three-quarters protein bound. Liver and plasma esterases break it down to an inactive acid that leaves in urine. Its elimination half-life is a few hours, but redistribution, not metabolism, ends the clinical effect.", "sidefx": "Adrenal suppression comes from blocking 11-beta-hydroxylase, lowering cortisol and aldosterone. This is why it is not given by continuous infusion and why its use in sepsis is debated. Myoclonus is thought to result from disinhibition of subcortical activity and can be mistaken for a seizure. Pain on injection is related to the drug's solvent. Nausea and vomiting after anesthesia are more common than with propofol. There is no specific antidote; adrenal suppression is temporary.", "fun": "Etomidate's adrenal effect is so strong that a related drug, metomidate, has been studied for imaging adrenal tumors, and etomidate itself has been used to treat severe Cushing syndrome.", "src": ["Amidate (etomidate) FDA label", "Ledingham IM, Watt I. Lancet 1983", "Jabre P et al. Lancet 2009"]}, "etoposide": {"origin": "Etoposide comes from the mayapple, Podophyllum peltatum, a woodland plant of North America, and its Himalayan relative Podophyllum emodi. Native Americans used mayapple root as a strong laxative. Its active compound, podophyllotoxin, kills dividing cells by blocking microtubules but was too toxic to use as a cancer drug. Chemists at Sandoz in Switzerland modified podophyllotoxin and in 1966 made etoposide. Surprisingly, it worked in a completely different way, by poisoning the enzyme topoisomerase II. It was approved in the US in 1983 and is now a key drug for testicular cancer, small cell lung cancer and lymphomas.", "timeline": [["1880", "Podophyllotoxin is first isolated from Podophyllum."], ["1966", "Sandoz chemists synthesize etoposide."], ["1983", "FDA approves etoposide (VePesid)."], ["1980s", "Etoposide is found to act by poisoning topoisomerase II."]], "cell": "Topoisomerase II relieves twisting in DNA by cutting both strands, passing another DNA segment through the gap, and rejoining the strands. Etoposide binds the topoisomerase II-DNA complex after the cut is made and prevents the enzyme from resealing it. The trapped cleavage complexes become permanent double-strand breaks when replication or transcription machinery runs into them. Cells in late S and G2 phase are most sensitive because topoisomerase II is most active then. The DNA damage activates checkpoints, arrests cells in G2 and triggers apoptosis. Unlike podophyllotoxin, etoposide does not bind tubulin.", "body": [["Cancer cells", "Rapidly dividing tumor cells collect DNA breaks and die, which is why it is effective in testicular cancer and small cell lung cancer."], ["Bone marrow", "White cells and platelets drop, with the low point about one to two weeks after treatment."], ["Hair and mouth", "Hair loss and mouth sores occur because these cells divide quickly."], ["Blood pressure", "Fast IV infusion can cause a sharp drop in blood pressure."], ["Immune system", "Allergic-type reactions can occur during infusion."], ["Long-term blood", "Can cause a secondary acute myeloid leukemia years later, often with a characteristic 11q23 (MLL) gene rearrangement."]], "journey": "Etoposide can be given IV or by mouth. Oral absorption is about half and varies between people. It is highly protein bound, so low albumin increases free drug and toxicity. It crosses poorly into the brain. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP3A4, with some glucuronidation. About forty percent leaves unchanged in urine and the rest in feces, so both kidney and liver function matter. The half-life is about four to eleven hours.", "sidefx": "Bone marrow suppression, hair loss and mouth sores come from killing dividing normal cells, because they also depend on topoisomerase II. Hypotension with rapid infusion is related to the infusion rate and its vehicle. Allergic reactions may relate to the solvent polysorbate 80; etoposide phosphate, a water-soluble prodrug, avoids it. Secondary leukemia comes from topoisomerase II-induced breaks that cause chromosome rearrangements in marrow stem cells. There is no antidote; growth factors and transfusions support blood counts.", "fun": "Etoposide is a reminder that tinkering with a natural toxin can create a totally new mechanism: its plant parent blocks microtubules, while etoposide blocks topoisomerase II.", "src": ["Etoposide FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "evolocumab": {"origin": "Evolocumab came from a fast path from human genetics to medicine. In 2003 Nabil Seidah in Montreal discovered the enzyme PCSK9, and that same year a French team led by Catherine Boileau and Marianne Abifadel found that gain-of-function PCSK9 mutations cause familial high cholesterol. In 2005 and 2006 Helen Hobbs and Jonathan Cohen in Dallas showed that people with loss-of-function PCSK9 variants have low LDL cholesterol and far fewer heart attacks, with no clear harm. Amgen developed evolocumab, a fully human antibody against PCSK9. It was approved in the US in 2015 as Repatha. The FOURIER trial in 2017 showed it reduces heart attacks and strokes.", "timeline": [["2003", "Seidah's group discovers PCSK9, and Abifadel and Boileau link PCSK9 mutations to familial hypercholesterolemia."], ["2006", "Cohen and Hobbs show PCSK9 loss-of-function variants lower LDL and coronary heart disease risk."], ["2015", "FDA approves evolocumab (Repatha)."], ["2017", "The FOURIER trial shows fewer heart attacks and strokes with evolocumab."]], "cell": "Liver cells remove LDL from the blood using LDL receptors. After an LDL receptor binds LDL and is taken into the cell, it normally recycles back to the surface many times. PCSK9, secreted by the liver, binds the LDL receptor on the surface. When the complex is taken into the cell, PCSK9 steers the receptor to the lysosome to be destroyed instead of recycled. Evolocumab is a human IgG2 monoclonal antibody that binds circulating PCSK9 and blocks it from binding the LDL receptor. More receptors recycle to the cell surface, the liver clears more LDL from the blood, and LDL cholesterol falls by about sixty percent. It also lowers lipoprotein(a) modestly.", "body": [["Liver", "More LDL receptors on liver cells pull more LDL out of the blood."], ["Blood lipids", "LDL cholesterol drops sharply, with smaller drops in apolipoprotein B, non-HDL cholesterol and lipoprotein(a)."], ["Arteries", "Lower LDL slows plaque growth and can shrink plaque volume, reducing heart attacks and strokes."], ["Injection site", "Redness, pain or bruising at the injection site can occur."], ["Airways", "Cold-like symptoms such as runny nose and sore throat are among the most common reported effects."]], "journey": "Evolocumab is injected under the skin every two weeks or once a month. It is absorbed slowly and peaks in three to four days. LDL starts falling within days and reaches its lowest point in a few weeks. As an antibody, it stays mostly in the blood and does not cross the blood-brain barrier. At low levels it is cleared mainly by binding to PCSK9, and at higher levels by general protein breakdown into amino acids. It is not cleared by the kidneys or liver enzymes, so there are no CYP interactions. The effective half-life is about eleven to seventeen days.", "sidefx": "Evolocumab is generally well tolerated because it acts on one protein in the blood. Injection-site reactions and upper respiratory symptoms are the most common effects. Allergic reactions, including rare angioedema, reflect an immune response to the protein. Large trials did not show an increase in memory problems or new diabetes. Very low LDL levels have not been linked to clear harm. There is no antidote, and none is usually needed.", "fun": "The PCSK9 story is a model for using human genetics to find drug targets: people born with broken PCSK9 genes showed what blocking it would do years before the drug existed.", "src": ["Repatha (evolocumab) FDA label", "Sabatine MS et al. N Engl J Med 2017 (FOURIER)", "Cohen JC et al. N Engl J Med 2006"]}, "exenatide": {"origin": "Exenatide came from the saliva of the Gila monster, a venomous lizard of the southwestern US. In the early 1990s John Eng, an endocrinologist at the Bronx Veterans Affairs Medical Center, studied lizard venoms and found exendin-4, a peptide that acts like the human gut hormone GLP-1 but lasts much longer, because the enzyme DPP-4 cannot easily break it down. Amylin Pharmaceuticals licensed it and developed it with Eli Lilly. A synthetic version, exenatide, was approved in the US in 2005 as Byetta, the first GLP-1 receptor agonist. A once-weekly form, Bydureon, followed in 2012.", "timeline": [["1992", "John Eng reports exendin-4 from Gila monster venom."], ["2005", "FDA approves exenatide (Byetta), the first GLP-1 receptor agonist."], ["2012", "FDA approves once-weekly extended-release exenatide (Bydureon)."], ["2017", "The EXSCEL trial shows extended-release exenatide is safe for the heart."]], "cell": "Exenatide binds and activates the GLP-1 receptor, a Gs-coupled receptor. In pancreatic beta cells, higher cAMP activates protein kinase A and Epac2, which increase insulin release only when glucose is high, so it rarely causes low blood sugar alone. It also suppresses glucagon release from alpha cells when glucose is high, reducing liver glucose output. Through vagal and brainstem pathways it slows gastric emptying, so sugar enters the blood more slowly after meals, and it increases satiety in the hypothalamus. Exendin-4 shares about half its amino acids with human GLP-1 but has a glycine at position 2 instead of alanine, which protects it from DPP-4.", "body": [["Pancreas", "Boosts glucose-dependent insulin release and lowers glucagon, reducing blood sugar after meals."], ["Stomach", "Slows emptying, which blunts sugar spikes after meals and causes fullness and nausea."], ["Brain", "Increases fullness and reduces appetite, leading to weight loss."], ["Kidneys", "Cleared by the kidneys, so it is avoided in severe kidney disease; vomiting can cause dehydration and kidney injury."], ["Pancreas (exocrine)", "Rarely associated with acute pancreatitis."], ["Thyroid", "In rodents, long-acting GLP-1 agonists caused thyroid C-cell tumors, leading to a boxed warning for the weekly form."]], "journey": "Exenatide is injected under the skin. The twice-daily form is given before meals, peaks in about two hours and has a half-life of about two and a half hours. The weekly form uses tiny polymer microspheres that release the drug slowly over weeks. As a peptide, it does not enter cells much and is not metabolized by liver enzymes. It is filtered by the kidneys and broken down there by proteolysis, which is why kidney function matters. Slowed stomach emptying can change absorption of oral drugs.", "sidefx": "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea come from slowed gastric emptying and GLP-1 effects in the brainstem, and they usually ease over weeks. Pancreatitis is rare but serious. Dehydration from vomiting can cause acute kidney injury, especially since the kidneys clear the drug. Low blood sugar mainly occurs when combined with sulfonylureas or insulin. Antibodies to exenatide can form because it is a lizard-derived peptide, and high levels can reduce its effect. The weekly form can cause injection-site nodules. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The Gila monster is one of the few venomous lizards in the world and lives in the deserts of the southwestern US and northwestern Mexico.", "src": ["Byetta (exenatide) FDA label", "Eng J et al. J Biol Chem 1992", "Holman RR et al. N Engl J Med 2017 (EXSCEL)"]}, "ezetimibe": {"origin": "Ezetimibe was discovered by accident at Schering-Plough in the early 1990s. Scientists were looking for drugs to block ACAT, an enzyme that stores cholesterol inside cells. One compound lowered cholesterol in animals even though it barely affected ACAT. A team including Duane Burnett, Stuart Rosenblum and John Clader refined it into ezetimibe, which blocked cholesterol absorption in the gut. It was approved in the US in 2002 as Zetia, before anyone knew its exact target. In 2004 scientists identified that target as the NPC1L1 transporter. The IMPROVE-IT trial in 2015 showed that adding ezetimibe to a statin further reduced heart attacks and strokes.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Schering-Plough scientists discover ezetimibe while searching for ACAT inhibitors."], ["2002", "FDA approves ezetimibe (Zetia)."], ["2004", "NPC1L1 is identified as the target of ezetimibe."], ["2015", "The IMPROVE-IT trial shows added benefit of ezetimibe with simvastatin after acute coronary syndrome."]], "cell": "Cholesterol from food and bile is taken up by enterocytes in the small intestine through the Niemann-Pick C1-Like 1 (NPC1L1) transporter on the brush border. Ezetimibe and its active glucuronide bind NPC1L1 and block cholesterol uptake, reducing intestinal cholesterol absorption by about half. Less cholesterol travels to the liver in chylomicron remnants. The liver senses lower cholesterol, activates SREBP-2 and makes more LDL receptors, which clear more LDL from the blood. The liver also increases its own cholesterol synthesis, which is why combining ezetimibe with a statin works so well. It also blocks absorption of plant sterols.", "body": [["Small intestine", "Less dietary and biliary cholesterol is absorbed into the body."], ["Liver", "More LDL receptors are made, pulling more LDL out of the blood."], ["Blood lipids", "Lowers LDL cholesterol by about a fifth alone and adds further lowering to a statin."], ["Arteries", "Lower LDL with a statin reduced heart attacks and strokes in IMPROVE-IT."], ["Muscles and liver", "Rare muscle pain and liver enzyme rises are reported, mostly when combined with a statin."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is occasionally reported, usually mild and short-lived."]], "journey": "Ezetimibe is taken by mouth and absorbed in the gut, where it is quickly converted to an active glucuronide in the intestinal wall and liver. Both are highly protein bound. They are excreted into bile and return to the gut, where they are reabsorbed again (enterohepatic recirculation). This repeated cycling keeps the drug at its site of action and gives a half-life of about twenty-two hours. It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes, so drug interactions are few. Most leaves in feces. Bile acid sequestrants bind it, so doses are separated.", "sidefx": "Ezetimibe is usually well tolerated because it acts locally in the gut and is not metabolized by CYP enzymes. Upper respiratory symptoms, diarrhea and joint pain are reported. Liver enzyme rises and muscle problems are mostly seen with statins, likely reflecting the statin. Cyclosporine and fibrates can raise its levels. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Ezetimibe was approved and used for two years before scientists knew exactly which protein it blocked.", "src": ["Zetia (ezetimibe) FDA label", "Altmann SW et al. Science 2004", "Cannon CP et al. N Engl J Med 2015 (IMPROVE-IT)"]}, "febuxostat": {"origin": "Febuxostat was discovered in the 1990s by the Japanese company Teijin as the first new type of xanthine oxidase inhibitor since allopurinol, which had been approved in 1966. Unlike allopurinol, febuxostat does not look like a purine, so it does not act as a fake substrate. It was developed for the US market by Takeda and approved in 2009 as Uloric for chronic gout. In 2018 the CARES trial found more cardiovascular deaths with febuxostat than with allopurinol, and in 2019 the FDA added a boxed warning. It is now reserved for people who cannot take allopurinol.", "timeline": [["1966", "Allopurinol is approved, the first xanthine oxidase inhibitor."], ["1990s", "Teijin discovers febuxostat in Japan."], ["2009", "FDA approves febuxostat (Uloric)."], ["2018", "The CARES trial reports higher cardiovascular death with febuxostat than allopurinol."], ["2019", "FDA adds a boxed warning on cardiovascular death."]], "cell": "Xanthine oxidase catalyzes the last two steps of purine breakdown: hypoxanthine to xanthine and xanthine to uric acid. Febuxostat is a non-purine, selective inhibitor that binds tightly in the channel leading to the enzyme's molybdenum-pterin active site, blocking both the oxidized and reduced forms of the enzyme. Uric acid production falls, and blood uric acid drops below the level at which it crystallizes. Over months, existing monosodium urate crystals in joints and tophi dissolve. Hypoxanthine and xanthine build up instead, but they are more soluble and are excreted. Because it is not a purine, it does not affect other enzymes of purine and pyrimidine metabolism.", "body": [["Blood uric acid", "Lowers serum uric acid strongly, often more than standard allopurinol doses."], ["Joints", "Over months, urate crystals dissolve and gout attacks become less frequent, but attacks often increase at first."], ["Skin and tissues", "Tophi slowly shrink as uric acid levels stay low."], ["Kidneys", "Lower uric acid production reduces uric acid kidney stones; it needs little adjustment in mild to moderate kidney disease."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises are common, and rare liver injury has occurred."], ["Heart and vessels", "Associated with higher cardiovascular death than allopurinol in people with gout and heart disease."]], "journey": "Febuxostat is taken by mouth and is well absorbed, peaking in about one to one and a half hours. It is very highly protein bound. The liver metabolizes it mostly by glucuronidation (UGT enzymes) and partly by CYP oxidation. Metabolites leave in both urine and feces, so it can be used in mild to moderate kidney disease without dose change. The half-life is about five to eight hours, but its enzyme block allows once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Gout flares at the start are common because falling uric acid levels pull urate from crystal deposits, which breaks crystals loose and triggers inflammation. That is why colchicine or an NSAID is given for the first months. Liver enzyme rises reflect liver metabolism of the drug. The cause of the higher cardiovascular death rate in CARES is not known. Febuxostat is contraindicated with azathioprine and mercaptopurine, because xanthine oxidase normally breaks these drugs down and blocking it can cause severe bone marrow toxicity. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Like allopurinol, febuxostat can turn the cancer drugs azathioprine and mercaptopurine deadly, a top board interaction.", "src": ["Uloric (febuxostat) FDA label", "White WB et al. N Engl J Med 2018 (CARES)", "ACR Guideline for the Management of Gout"]}, "fenofibrate": {"origin": "Fenofibrate is a fibrate, a drug family that began with clofibrate, discovered by Imperial Chemical Industries in Britain and introduced in the 1960s. Clofibrate lowered triglycerides but a large WHO trial raised concerns about higher death rates. Chemists at the French company Laboratoires Fournier made fenofibrate in 1974 as a more potent derivative, and it came into use in France in the mid-1970s. It later reached the US market in the 1990s. Its target was unknown until 1990, when PPAR-alpha was discovered as a nuclear receptor activated by fibrates and other compounds that cause peroxisome proliferation in rodent livers.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Clofibrate becomes the first widely used fibrate."], ["1974", "Laboratoires Fournier synthesizes fenofibrate in France."], ["1975", "Fenofibrate comes into medical use in France."], ["1990", "Issemann and Green discover PPAR-alpha, later shown to be the fibrate target."], ["2010", "The ACCORD Lipid trial finds no added cardiovascular benefit of fenofibrate with a statin overall."]], "cell": "Fenofibrate is a prodrug. It is hydrolyzed to fenofibric acid, which binds and activates peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor alpha (PPAR-alpha), a nuclear receptor highly expressed in liver, muscle and heart. PPAR-alpha pairs with the retinoid X receptor and binds response elements in DNA. It increases genes for lipoprotein lipase and fatty acid oxidation enzymes, and decreases apolipoprotein C-III, which normally blocks lipoprotein lipase. The result is faster breakdown of triglyceride-rich VLDL and chylomicrons and less VLDL production by the liver. PPAR-alpha also increases apolipoproteins A-I and A-II, raising HDL. Fenofibric acid also increases uric acid excretion by the kidney.", "body": [["Blood lipids", "Lowers triglycerides substantially, raises HDL modestly and causes small changes in LDL."], ["Pancreas", "Lowering very high triglycerides reduces the risk of acute pancreatitis."], ["Liver", "Increases fatty acid burning but can raise liver enzymes; liver disease is a contraindication."], ["Gallbladder", "More cholesterol is secreted into bile, raising gallstone risk."], ["Muscles", "Can cause muscle pain and rarely rhabdomyolysis, especially with statins or kidney disease."], ["Kidneys", "Often raises serum creatinine in a reversible way, and dose is reduced in kidney disease."]], "journey": "Fenofibrate is taken by mouth. Older forms needed food for absorption; newer micronized and nanocrystal forms are absorbed well with or without food. Gut and blood esterases quickly convert it to fenofibric acid, which is almost entirely protein bound and peaks in several hours. Fenofibric acid is joined to glucuronic acid and cleared mainly by the kidneys. The half-life is about twenty hours, allowing once-daily dosing. It displaces warfarin from proteins and can increase its effect.", "sidefx": "Gallstones come from increased cholesterol in bile. Myopathy and rhabdomyolysis are more likely when combined with statins or in kidney disease, though fenofibrate carries less risk with statins than gemfibrozil, which blocks statin metabolism. Creatinine rises are usually reversible. Liver enzyme rises reflect its liver actions. It can increase the effect of warfarin. Pancreatitis has been reported. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Fibrates got their target's name, peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor, because in rodent livers they make peroxisomes multiply, an effect not seen in humans.", "src": ["Tricor (fenofibrate) FDA label", "Issemann I, Green S. Nature 1990", "ACCORD Study Group. N Engl J Med 2010"]}, "fentanyl": {"origin": "Fentanyl was first made in 1960 by Paul Janssen at his company Janssen Pharmaceutica in Belgium. Janssen was searching for opioids more potent and faster than morphine and pethidine (meperidine), and he systematically modified the structure of pethidine. Fentanyl turned out to be about fifty to one hundred times as potent as morphine. It entered medical use in Europe in the 1960s as Sublimaze and was approved in the US in 1968, first combined with droperidol as Innovar. The skin patch, Duragesic, followed in 1990. Fentanyl transformed anesthesia, but illicitly made fentanyl later became the leading driver of overdose deaths in North America.", "timeline": [["1960", "Paul Janssen synthesizes fentanyl."], ["1960s", "Fentanyl is introduced in Europe as an anesthetic."], ["1968", "Fentanyl is approved in the United States."], ["1990", "FDA approves the fentanyl transdermal patch (Duragesic)."], ["2010s", "Illicitly manufactured fentanyl becomes the main cause of opioid overdose deaths in the US."]], "cell": "Fentanyl is a potent full agonist at the mu-opioid receptor, a Gi/o-coupled receptor. When it binds, the G protein inhibits adenylyl cyclase (lowering cAMP), opens G protein-gated inwardly rectifying potassium channels (hyperpolarizing neurons) and closes voltage-gated calcium channels at presynaptic terminals (reducing release of glutamate and substance P). In the spinal dorsal horn, this blocks pain signals from reaching the brain. In the brain it activates descending pain-inhibiting pathways and changes how pain is perceived. In the brainstem it reduces the response of breathing centers to carbon dioxide, causing respiratory depression. Unlike morphine, it releases little histamine.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Produces strong pain relief and sedation within minutes."], ["Breathing", "Depresses the brainstem breathing response to carbon dioxide, slowing and stopping breathing at high levels."], ["Chest wall", "Rapid high doses IV can cause chest wall and vocal cord rigidity, making ventilation difficult."], ["Heart and vessels", "Causes little blood pressure drop compared with morphine because it releases little histamine, though it can slow the heart rate through vagal effects."], ["Eyes", "Stimulates the Edinger-Westphal nucleus, causing pinpoint pupils."], ["Gut", "Slows gut movement, causing constipation and nausea."], ["Skin", "Causes less itching and flushing than morphine because it releases little histamine."]], "journey": "Fentanyl is very fat soluble. It can be given IV, IM, intranasally, through the skin as a patch, through the mouth lining as lozenges or films, and into the spine. IV it reaches the brain within a minute or two. A single IV dose works briefly because the drug redistributes into fat and muscle, but after long infusions it builds up in fat and lasts much longer (context-sensitive half-time). The liver metabolizes it by CYP3A4 to inactive norfentanyl, which leaves in urine. CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ritonavir or clarithromycin can raise levels dangerously. A skin patch takes about half a day to reach full effect and keeps releasing drug from the skin depot after removal. It crosses the placenta.", "sidefx": "Respiratory depression is the major danger and follows directly from mu-receptor action in the brainstem; it is worse with benzodiazepines, alcohol or other sedatives. Chest wall rigidity relates to rapid high doses acting on central pathways controlling muscle tone. Bradycardia comes from vagal stimulation. Constipation and nausea come from mu receptors in the gut and the chemoreceptor trigger zone. Tolerance, dependence and addiction come from long-term changes in mu-receptor signaling and reward circuits. Naloxone, a competitive mu-opioid antagonist, reverses toxicity, though repeated doses may be needed with high-potency exposures.", "fun": "Paul Janssen and his company created an unusually large number of essential medicines, including fentanyl, haloperidol, loperamide and etomidate.", "src": ["Sublimaze (fentanyl citrate) FDA label", "Duragesic (fentanyl transdermal) FDA label", "Stanley TH. The fentanyl story. J Pain 2014"]}, "ferrous-sulfate": {"origin": "Iron has been used as medicine for thousands of years. Ancient Greek stories describe iron rust added to wine as a strengthening tonic. In the 17th century the English physician Thomas Sydenham recommended iron for chlorosis, the green sickness of young women now recognized as iron deficiency anemia. In 1832 the French physician Pierre Blaud introduced pills of ferrous sulfate and potassium carbonate, known as Blaud's pills, which reliably cured chlorosis. In the early 20th century, studies of anemia in infants and pregnant women confirmed that the problem was a lack of iron and that enough iron had to be given to fix it. Ferrous sulfate remains the cheapest, most widely used oral iron supplement.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Greek tradition describes iron rust in wine as a remedy for weakness."], ["17th century", "Thomas Sydenham recommends iron for chlorosis."], ["1832", "Pierre Blaud introduces ferrous sulfate pills for chlorosis."], ["1930s", "Studies show that iron deficiency anemia responds to adequate iron."]], "cell": "Ferrous sulfate supplies iron in the ferrous (Fe2+) form, which is the form the gut can absorb. In the duodenum, the transporter DMT1 on the brush border carries Fe2+ into enterocytes. Iron then either stays in the cell bound to ferritin, to be lost when the cell sheds, or crosses into the blood through ferroportin on the basolateral side. Hephaestin oxidizes it to ferric (Fe3+) iron, which binds transferrin. Transferrin carries iron to the bone marrow, where developing red cells take it up through transferrin receptors and build it into heme for hemoglobin. Extra iron is stored as ferritin and hemosiderin. The liver hormone hepcidin controls the system by degrading ferroportin; inflammation raises hepcidin and blocks absorption.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Supplies iron for new hemoglobin, raising reticulocytes within about a week and hemoglobin over weeks."], ["Blood", "Fixes iron deficiency anemia, restoring oxygen-carrying capacity and reducing fatigue and shortness of breath."], ["Iron stores", "Continued therapy for a few months after hemoglobin normalizes refills ferritin stores."], ["Stomach and gut", "Iron irritates the gut lining, causing nausea, stomach pain, constipation or diarrhea."], ["Stool", "Unabsorbed iron turns stools dark green or black, which is expected and harmless."], ["Teeth", "Liquid iron can stain teeth, which is reduced by using a straw and rinsing."]], "journey": "Ferrous sulfate is taken by mouth and absorbed mainly in the duodenum and upper jejunum. Only a small part of each dose is absorbed, and absorption rises when iron stores are low. An acid stomach and vitamin C help keep iron in the absorbable ferrous form. Food, antacids, proton pump inhibitors, calcium, tea and some antibiotics reduce absorption, and iron in turn reduces absorption of drugs like levothyroxine and tetracyclines. Absorbed iron travels bound to transferrin. The body has no active way to excrete iron; it is lost only in small amounts through shed gut and skin cells, sweat and bleeding.", "sidefx": "GI side effects come from iron's direct irritant and oxidative effects on the gut lining. In overdose, especially in young children who mistake tablets for candy, free iron overwhelms transferrin. It corrodes the gut, causing vomiting and bleeding, and then enters cells, where it disrupts mitochondria and generates free radicals. This causes shock, metabolic acidosis, liver failure and, later, bowel scarring. Deferoxamine, given IV, binds free iron to form ferrioxamine, which the kidneys excrete and which can turn urine reddish-brown (vin rose urine).", "fun": "Iron was once a leading cause of fatal poisoning in young children, which led the FDA in 1997 to require a warning label about accidental overdose on iron-containing supplements.", "src": ["Ferrous sulfate OTC drug labeling", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]}, "fexofenadine": {"origin": "Fexofenadine is the grown-up version of an older allergy pill. In the 1980s terfenadine (Seldane) became a hit as one of the first non-drowsy antihistamines. Doctors then learned that terfenadine itself could block cardiac potassium channels and trigger torsades de pointes when its liver breakdown by CYP3A4 was blocked by drugs like ketoconazole or erythromycin, or by grapefruit. The actual allergy relief came from terfenadine's carboxylic acid metabolite, which did not affect the heart. That metabolite was developed as its own drug, fexofenadine, and approved by the FDA in 1996 as Allegra (Hoechst Marion Roussel). Terfenadine was pulled from the US market in the late 1990s, and fexofenadine became available over the counter in 2011.", "timeline": [["1985", "Terfenadine (Seldane) reaches the US market as a non-sedating antihistamine."], ["1996", "FDA approves fexofenadine (Allegra), the active metabolite of terfenadine."], ["1998", "Terfenadine is withdrawn in the US because of cardiac arrhythmia risk."], ["2011", "Fexofenadine becomes available over the counter in the US."]], "cell": "Fexofenadine acts on the histamine H1 receptor, a Gq-coupled receptor. H1 receptors have some activity even without histamine, and fexofenadine binds and stabilizes the inactive form, so it is technically an inverse agonist rather than a simple blocker. With H1 held inactive, histamine released from mast cells cannot activate the Gq, phospholipase C, IP3 and calcium pathway. That prevents the downstream effects of histamine: leaky capillaries, smooth muscle changes and stimulation of sensory nerves. Fexofenadine is a zwitterion and a substrate of the P-glycoprotein efflux pump, so it is pushed back out of the brain and does not occupy central H1 receptors, which is why it causes little sedation.", "body": [["Nose and eyes", "Less histamine signaling means less sneezing, itching, watery eyes and runny nose in allergic rhinitis. It does little for nasal congestion, which is why it is often combined with a decongestant."], ["Skin", "In hives, blocking H1 on small blood vessels and itch nerves reduces wheals, redness and itching."], ["Brain", "Because P-glycoprotein keeps it out of the brain, it causes much less drowsiness and impaired thinking than first-generation antihistamines."], ["Heart", "Unlike its parent terfenadine, fexofenadine does not meaningfully block the hERG potassium channel, so it is not linked to QT prolongation at usual use."]], "journey": "Fexofenadine is taken by mouth and absorbed in the gut, partly with the help of OATP uptake transporters. Fruit juices such as grapefruit, orange and apple inhibit these transporters and lower absorption, and aluminum or magnesium antacids also reduce absorption. Relief starts within about an hour. Very little is metabolized by the liver. Most is eliminated unchanged in the feces through bile, with a smaller amount in urine. The half-life is about 14 hours, so it lasts through the day.", "sidefx": "Fexofenadine is one of the best tolerated antihistamines. Headache is the most commonly reported effect. Because it stays out of the brain and lacks anticholinergic activity, it rarely causes sedation, dry mouth or urinary retention. It lacks the heart rhythm risk of terfenadine because it does not block cardiac potassium channels. Reduced effect with fruit juice is an absorption problem, not toxicity. There is no specific antidote; overdose care is supportive.", "fun": "Fexofenadine is a classic example of a drug developed by finding the safe, active metabolite of a problem parent drug. Cetirizine and desloratadine have similar stories.", "src": ["Allegra (fexofenadine) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "filgrastim": {"origin": "Filgrastim is a lab-made copy of a natural human hormone, granulocyte colony-stimulating factor (G-CSF), that tells bone marrow to make neutrophils. Colony-stimulating factors were discovered in the 1960s when researchers found substances that made bone marrow cells grow into colonies in a dish. In the mid-1980s scientists at Amgen, led by Lawrence Souza, cloned the human G-CSF gene. Amgen produced the protein in E. coli bacteria and the FDA approved it as Neupogen in 1991 to reduce infections in patients receiving chemotherapy that suppresses the bone marrow. A longer-acting pegylated version, pegfilgrastim (Neulasta), followed in 2002. In 2015 Zarxio, a version of filgrastim, became the first biosimilar approved in the US.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Colony-stimulating factors are discovered in bone marrow culture experiments."], ["1986", "Amgen scientists report cloning of the human G-CSF gene."], ["1991", "FDA approves filgrastim (Neupogen) for chemotherapy-induced neutropenia."], ["2002", "Pegfilgrastim (Neulasta), a long-acting pegylated form, is approved."], ["2015", "Zarxio (filgrastim-sndz) becomes the first US-approved biosimilar."]], "cell": "Filgrastim binds the G-CSF receptor, a cytokine receptor on myeloid progenitor cells and mature neutrophils. Binding causes the receptor to pair up and activates the associated JAK kinases, which phosphorylate STAT3 and other signaling proteins. These signals drive committed neutrophil progenitors to divide, mature faster, and survive longer instead of undergoing apoptosis. G-CSF also loosens the bone marrow niche by lowering CXCL12 signaling, which releases neutrophils and stem cells into the blood. In mature neutrophils it enhances chemotaxis, phagocytosis and killing. Because it is made in E. coli, filgrastim is not glycosylated and carries an extra methionine at the start, but it acts like natural G-CSF.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Neutrophil production speeds up sharply. The marrow expands, which causes the deep bone pain many patients feel, often in the back, hips and sternum."], ["Blood", "Neutrophil counts rise within a day and the low point after chemotherapy is shorter and less deep. Stem cells also move into the blood, which is used to collect cells for transplant."], ["Immune system", "More and better-functioning neutrophils lower the rate of febrile neutropenia and serious bacterial infection."], ["Spleen", "The spleen can enlarge as it fills with blood cell production and storage. Rarely it can rupture, which shows up as left upper abdominal or shoulder tip pain."], ["Lungs", "Activated neutrophils can collect in the lungs, and rarely this leads to acute respiratory distress syndrome."]], "journey": "Filgrastim is a protein, so it would be digested in the gut and must be given by subcutaneous injection or IV infusion. It enters the blood over hours after a subcutaneous injection. It is cleared two ways: by the kidneys and by binding to G-CSF receptors on neutrophils, which take it in and break it down. That second route means clearance rises as neutrophil counts recover. The half-life is short, about three and a half hours, so it is given daily. Pegfilgrastim is too large for kidney filtration and lasts much longer.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from overstimulating the marrow. Bone pain reflects rapid marrow expansion and usually responds to simple analgesics. Very high white counts (leukocytosis) are expected effects of too much stimulation. Splenic enlargement and rare rupture come from extramedullary blood cell production in the spleen. ARDS likely reflects activated neutrophils collecting in the lungs. Sickle cell crises can occur in patients with sickle cell disease. Allergic reactions and capillary leak syndrome are rare. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped and care is supportive.", "fun": "Filgrastim is used not only to treat neutropenia but also to mobilize stem cells so donors can give them from the blood rather than through bone marrow harvest.", "src": ["Neupogen (filgrastim) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Souza LM et al. Science 1986"]}, "finasteride": {"origin": "Finasteride was inspired by a rare genetic condition. In the early 1970s Julianne Imperato-McGinley and colleagues studied a group of children in the Dominican Republic who were born with genetic male (XY) chromosomes but looked female at birth and then developed male features at puberty. They had an inherited lack of 5-alpha reductase type 2, the enzyme that turns testosterone into dihydrotestosterone (DHT). As adults they had small prostates and did not develop male pattern baldness. Researchers at Merck reasoned that blocking this enzyme could shrink an enlarged prostate. The result was finasteride, approved by the FDA in 1992 as Proscar for benign prostatic hyperplasia. A lower-strength version, Propecia, was approved in 1997 for male pattern hair loss.", "timeline": [["1974", "Imperato-McGinley and colleagues describe inherited 5-alpha reductase deficiency in the Dominican Republic."], ["1992", "FDA approves finasteride (Proscar) for benign prostatic hyperplasia."], ["1997", "FDA approves finasteride (Propecia) for male pattern hair loss."], ["2003", "The Prostate Cancer Prevention Trial reports fewer prostate cancers with finasteride."]], "cell": "Testosterone is converted to the more potent androgen dihydrotestosterone (DHT) by 5-alpha reductase. The type 2 isoenzyme is found mainly in the prostate, seminal vesicles, and hair follicles. Finasteride is a 4-azasteroid that acts as a competitive, mechanism-based inhibitor of type 2 5-alpha reductase, forming a tight, slowly reversible complex with the enzyme. DHT levels in blood fall by roughly two thirds and fall even more in the prostate. Less DHT binds the androgen receptor in prostate cells, so androgen-driven gene expression and growth slow. Over months the prostate's glandular tissue shrinks. Testosterone itself is not blocked and may rise slightly, so muscle mass and libido are largely preserved compared with full androgen blockade.", "body": [["Prostate", "Over 6 to 12 months the prostate shrinks by about a fifth, which eases urinary flow and lowers the risk of acute urinary retention and the need for surgery."], ["Blood tests", "PSA drops by about half because PSA production depends on DHT. A PSA value on finasteride must be doubled for interpretation, and a rising PSA on therapy needs evaluation."], ["Hair follicles", "Lower DHT in scalp follicles slows the miniaturization that causes male pattern baldness and can regrow hair in some men."], ["Sexual function", "Some men notice lower libido, erectile dysfunction or reduced ejaculate volume because DHT supports these functions."], ["Fetus", "DHT is needed for normal development of male external genitalia, so exposure of a male fetus can cause malformations. Pregnant people should not handle crushed or broken tablets."]], "journey": "Finasteride is taken by mouth and well absorbed, with good bioavailability. It is highly bound to plasma proteins and does cross into semen in small amounts. The liver breaks it down, mainly by CYP3A4, into weak metabolites that leave in the feces and urine. The plasma half-life is only about 5 to 6 hours, but because it binds the enzyme so tightly, the drop in DHT lasts much longer. Clinical effects on the prostate and hair take months to appear and reverse over months after stopping.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow from lower DHT. Reduced libido, erectile dysfunction and lower ejaculate volume occur because DHT supports sexual function. Breast tenderness and enlargement can happen because more testosterone is available to be converted to estrogen. Mood changes including depression have been reported. Because it lowers PSA, it can hide prostate cancer if PSA is not adjusted, and trials showed a small increase in high-grade tumors. The fetal risk comes from DHT's role in male genital development. There is no antidote; effects fade after stopping.", "fun": "The Dominican children in the original study were locally called 'guevedoces', a slang term meaning roughly 'penis at twelve', because male features appeared at puberty.", "src": ["Proscar (finasteride) FDA label", "Imperato-McGinley J et al. Science 1974", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "flecainide": {"origin": "Flecainide is a synthetic class IC antiarrhythmic developed by the 3M company's pharmaceutical division in the 1970s as a fluorinated local anesthetic-like compound. It was approved by the FDA in 1985 as Tambocor. It was very good at suppressing extra ventricular beats, and many doctors gave it after heart attacks to prevent sudden death. In 1989 the Cardiac Arrhythmia Suppression Trial (CAST) changed everything: patients after a heart attack who took flecainide or encainide to suppress extra beats died more often than patients on placebo. CAST taught medicine that making an ECG look better does not always help patients. Flecainide is now reserved mainly for atrial fibrillation and supraventricular tachycardias in hearts without structural disease.", "timeline": [["1985", "FDA approves flecainide (Tambocor)."], ["1989", "CAST shows higher mortality with flecainide and encainide after myocardial infarction."], ["2000s", "'Pill in the pocket' use for paroxysmal atrial fibrillation in structurally normal hearts becomes established."]], "cell": "Flecainide blocks the fast voltage-gated sodium channel Nav1.5 that produces phase 0 of the action potential in atrial, ventricular and Purkinje cells. It binds mainly to open channels and unbinds very slowly, so block builds up and is strongest at fast heart rates (use dependence). With fewer sodium channels available, the upstroke of phase 0 is slower and smaller, so the impulse travels more slowly through the His-Purkinje system and muscle. This widens the QRS and lengthens the PR interval while having little effect on action potential duration. Slowed conduction can end reentrant circuits. Flecainide also blocks the ryanodine receptor (RyR2) in the sarcoplasmic reticulum, which is why it helps in catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia.", "body": [["Heart conduction", "Impulses move more slowly through the atria, AV node pathway, His-Purkinje system and ventricles, widening the QRS. This can stop reentrant SVT and atrial fibrillation."], ["Heart muscle", "Sodium channel block and lower calcium entry weaken contraction, which can worsen heart failure in a weak heart."], ["Atrial flutter", "Slowing the flutter circuit can let the AV node pass every flutter wave, producing a very fast 1:1 ventricular rate. An AV nodal blocker is usually given with it for this reason."], ["Brain and eyes", "Dizziness, blurred vision and trouble focusing are common because sodium channels also matter in neurons."], ["Scarred heart", "In hearts with old infarcts, slowed conduction creates new reentry loops, which explains the higher death rate seen in CAST."]], "journey": "Flecainide is taken by mouth and absorbed almost completely, with little first-pass loss. It reaches peak levels in about three hours. The liver metabolizes it through CYP2D6, so poor metabolizers and drugs that block CYP2D6 such as fluoxetine or quinidine raise levels. A large part also leaves unchanged in the urine, and alkaline urine slows its excretion. The half-life is long, roughly 20 hours, so steady levels take a few days to reach. Blood levels are sometimes measured.", "sidefx": "The main dangers come from too much sodium channel block. Wide QRS, heart block and new ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation (proarrhythmia) happen because slowed conduction favors reentry, especially in scarred or ischemic hearts. Negative inotropy worsens heart failure. The 1:1 flutter problem comes from slowing the atrial rate without blocking the AV node. Dizziness and visual blurring reflect neuronal sodium channel effects. For toxicity with wide QRS, IV sodium bicarbonate (hypertonic sodium) is used to overcome the block.", "fun": "CAST is one of the most cited trials in cardiology, and it is why flecainide is avoided in patients with coronary artery disease or prior heart attack.", "src": ["Tambocor (flecainide) FDA label", "CAST Investigators. N Engl J Med 1989", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "fluconazole": {"origin": "Fluconazole was designed by medicinal chemists at Pfizer's research center in Sandwich, England, in the late 1970s and early 1980s. Earlier azole antifungals like ketoconazole worked by mouth but were poorly soluble, needed stomach acid to absorb, and caused liver problems and hormone side effects. The Pfizer team, including Ken Richardson, swapped the imidazole ring for triazole rings and added fluorine atoms, which made a small, water-soluble, stable molecule that is absorbed almost completely and spreads into body fluids including the spinal fluid. The FDA approved it as Diflucan in 1990. It became a mainstay for Candida infections and for cryptococcal meningitis, which was common in people with AIDS at that time.", "timeline": [["1981", "Ketoconazole, an earlier oral azole, is approved in the US."], ["1980s", "Pfizer chemists in Sandwich, England, develop the triazole fluconazole."], ["1990", "FDA approves fluconazole (Diflucan)."]], "cell": "Fungal cell membranes use ergosterol instead of cholesterol. Ergosterol is made from lanosterol by a pathway that includes lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase, a fungal cytochrome P450 enzyme called CYP51 (encoded by ERG11). Fluconazole's triazole nitrogen binds the heme iron of CYP51 and blocks it. Ergosterol production falls, and toxic 14-alpha-methylated sterols build up in the membrane. The membrane becomes stiff and leaky and membrane enzymes stop working well, so the fungus cannot grow. Against Candida this is fungistatic. Fluconazole is far more selective for fungal CYP51 than human P450s, but it still inhibits human CYP2C9, CYP2C19 and CYP3A4.", "body": [["Mouth, esophagus and vagina", "Candida growth stops, clearing thrush, esophagitis and vaginal yeast infections."], ["Brain and spinal fluid", "It reaches good levels in cerebrospinal fluid, so it is used for cryptococcal meningitis, usually after initial therapy with amphotericin B and flucytosine."], ["Urinary tract", "Because it is excreted unchanged in urine, high urine levels make it useful for Candida urinary infections."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises are usually mild, but rare serious hepatitis can occur."], ["Heart", "It can prolong the QT interval, especially with other QT-prolonging drugs or low potassium or magnesium."]], "journey": "Fluconazole is absorbed almost completely from the gut, and unlike older azoles it does not need stomach acid, so oral and IV doses give similar levels. It has low protein binding and spreads widely into tissues and fluids, including the spinal fluid, eye, skin, and urine. Very little is metabolized. About 80 percent leaves unchanged in the urine, so doses are adjusted in kidney disease. The half-life is about 30 hours, which allows once-daily or even single-dose treatment.", "sidefx": "Headache, nausea and abdominal pain are the common complaints. Liver injury can range from mild enzyme rises to rare hepatic failure. QT prolongation and torsades come from effects on cardiac potassium channels and from raising levels of other QT-prolonging drugs. Many drug interactions happen because fluconazole inhibits human CYP2C9, CYP2C19 and CYP3A4, raising levels of warfarin, phenytoin, sulfonylureas and some statins. It is less of a hormone blocker than ketoconazole. There is no antidote; hemodialysis removes it.", "fun": "The 'flu' in fluconazole refers to its fluorine atoms, not influenza. The two fluorines on the benzene ring help block its breakdown.", "src": ["Diflucan (fluconazole) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "flucytosine": {"origin": "Flucytosine (5-fluorocytosine) was first made in 1957 by chemists at Hoffmann-La Roche, in the same research effort that produced the cancer drug fluorouracil. It was tested as an anticancer agent but did little to tumors, because human cells cannot convert it into an active form. In the 1960s researchers found that it killed or stopped yeasts such as Candida and Cryptococcus, which do have the enzyme needed to activate it. It was approved in the US in the early 1970s as Ancobon. Because fungi quickly become resistant when it is used alone, it found its lasting role as a partner with amphotericin B for cryptococcal meningitis.", "timeline": [["1957", "Flucytosine is synthesized at Hoffmann-La Roche during fluoropyrimidine research."], ["1960s", "Antifungal activity against Candida and Cryptococcus is discovered."], ["1970s", "Flucytosine (Ancobon) is approved in the US."], ["2010s", "Trials in Africa confirm that flucytosine combinations lower death from cryptococcal meningitis."]], "cell": "Flucytosine is a prodrug. It enters fungal cells through cytosine permease. Inside, the fungal enzyme cytosine deaminase removes an amino group and converts it into 5-fluorouracil (5-FU). 5-FU then takes two paths. It is turned into 5-fluorouridine triphosphate (FUTP), which is built into RNA in place of uracil and disrupts protein synthesis. It is also turned into 5-fluorodeoxyuridine monophosphate (FdUMP), which inhibits thymidylate synthase, so the cell cannot make thymidine for DNA. Human cells lack cytosine deaminase, which is the basis of its selectivity. Fungi become resistant by losing the permease or deaminase, so it is combined with amphotericin B, which damages the membrane and helps flucytosine get in.", "body": [["Brain and spinal fluid", "It crosses into the cerebrospinal fluid very well, and with amphotericin B it clears Cryptococcus from the spinal fluid faster than amphotericin alone."], ["Bone marrow", "Leukopenia and thrombocytopenia occur when blood levels are high, probably because gut bacteria convert some of it to 5-FU, which harms dividing marrow cells."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are common, and severe enterocolitis can occur, again tied to local 5-FU formation."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises and hepatitis can occur, mainly with high levels."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys clear it unchanged, so kidney injury from amphotericin B can raise flucytosine levels and toxicity."]], "journey": "Flucytosine is given by mouth in the US and is absorbed well. It has little protein binding and spreads throughout body water, including the spinal fluid, eye, and urine. Human cells do not break it down, but gut bacteria can convert a small part to 5-FU. About 90 percent leaves unchanged in the urine by filtration. The half-life is a few hours with normal kidneys but becomes much longer in kidney failure, which is why blood levels are checked.", "sidefx": "Bone marrow suppression, gut toxicity and liver injury are the main problems. They are thought to come from conversion of flucytosine to 5-FU by gut microbes, giving the patient a small amount of a cancer drug. Toxicity is strongly tied to high blood levels, which most often happen when amphotericin B has reduced kidney function. Monitoring levels, blood counts and kidney function lowers the risk. There is no specific antidote; it is removed by hemodialysis.", "fun": "Flucytosine is a natural example of a prodrug that relies on an enzyme only the target organism has, the same idea later used in suicide gene cancer therapies that put cytosine deaminase into tumors.", "src": ["Ancobon (flucytosine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "fludrocortisone": {"origin": "Fludrocortisone was a chemistry surprise. In the early 1950s Josef Fried and Emily Sabo at the Squibb Institute were making derivatives of cortisol (hydrocortisone). In 1954 they reported that adding a fluorine atom at the 9-alpha position made the steroid far more potent. The new compound, 9-alpha-fluorohydrocortisone, had much stronger glucocorticoid activity and even more dramatic mineralocorticoid (salt-retaining) activity. It was too salt-retaining to use as an anti-inflammatory, but it was ideal for replacing aldosterone in Addison disease. It is widely considered the first fluorinated drug, and the same fluorine trick later produced potent anti-inflammatory steroids such as dexamethasone and triamcinolone.", "timeline": [["1954", "Fried and Sabo at Squibb report that 9-alpha-fluorination greatly increases corticosteroid potency."], ["1950s", "Fludrocortisone enters use for mineralocorticoid replacement in adrenal insufficiency."], ["1958", "Dexamethasone, another 9-alpha-fluorinated steroid, is introduced."]], "cell": "Fludrocortisone is an agonist at the mineralocorticoid receptor (MR), a nuclear receptor in principal cells of the kidney's late distal tubule and collecting duct. It enters the cell, binds MR in the cytoplasm, and the complex moves to the nucleus to act as a transcription factor. It increases expression of the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) on the luminal side, the Na-K-ATPase pump on the blood side, and the kinase SGK1 that keeps ENaC in the membrane. More sodium is reabsorbed, which makes the tubule lumen more negative and drives potassium and hydrogen secretion. Water follows sodium, so plasma volume expands. Its 9-alpha-fluorine also slows breakdown by 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2, which normally protects MR from cortisol, helping it act on MR so strongly.", "body": [["Kidneys", "Sodium and water are kept while potassium and hydrogen ions are lost in the urine."], ["Blood volume and pressure", "Expanded plasma volume raises blood pressure. This treats the low pressure and salt craving of adrenal insufficiency and is used for orthostatic hypotension."], ["Electrolytes", "Potassium falls, sometimes enough to cause weakness and arrhythmias, and metabolic alkalosis can develop."], ["Heart and lungs", "Too much volume can cause edema, heart failure and pulmonary edema in patients with weak hearts."], ["Whole body", "It also has glucocorticoid activity, but at replacement use the effect is mostly mineralocorticoid, so patients with Addison disease still need hydrocortisone."]], "journey": "Fludrocortisone is taken by mouth and absorbed well from the gut. It is bound to plasma proteins and broken down in the liver, then excreted in the urine. The plasma half-life is only a few hours, but its biological effect lasts one to two days because it works by changing gene expression. Full effects on volume and blood pressure build over several days.", "sidefx": "Side effects are what too much aldosterone would do. Sodium and water retention cause edema, weight gain and hypertension, including high blood pressure lying down. Potassium wasting causes hypokalemia, muscle weakness and arrhythmias. Fluid overload can tip patients into heart failure or pulmonary edema. At higher use its glucocorticoid activity can add steroid effects. There is no antidote; the dose is reduced or stopped and potassium is replaced.", "fun": "Many sources call fludrocortisone the first fluorine-containing drug. Today about a fifth of new drugs contain fluorine.", "src": ["Fludrocortisone acetate FDA label", "Fried J, Sabo EF. J Am Chem Soc 1954", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "flumazenil": {"origin": "Flumazenil came from the benzodiazepine research program at Hoffmann-La Roche, the company that had introduced chlordiazepoxide (Librium) and diazepam (Valium). In the late 1970s and early 1980s Roche scientists, working with the group of Willy Haefely in Basel, found an imidazobenzodiazepine, code-named Ro 15-1788, that bound tightly to the benzodiazepine site on the GABA-A receptor but had almost no effect of its own. That made it a pure antagonist that could block and reverse benzodiazepines. It also became a key research tool that proved benzodiazepines act through a specific receptor site. The FDA approved it as Romazicon in 1991 to reverse benzodiazepine sedation.", "timeline": [["1960", "Roche introduces chlordiazepoxide, the first benzodiazepine."], ["1981", "Roche scientists describe Ro 15-1788 (flumazenil) as a benzodiazepine antagonist."], ["1991", "FDA approves flumazenil (Romazicon)."]], "cell": "The GABA-A receptor is a chloride channel made of five subunits. Benzodiazepines bind a site between the alpha and gamma subunits and make GABA open the channel more often, which increases chloride entry and quiets neurons. Flumazenil binds the same benzodiazepine site with high affinity but does not change channel function in a meaningful way. It competitively pushes benzodiazepines and the related Z-drugs (zolpidem, zaleplon, eszopiclone) off the site. GABA still works normally, but the boost from the benzodiazepine is removed. Flumazenil does not reverse barbiturates, alcohol, propofol or opioids, which act at different sites.", "body": [["Brain", "Sedation, amnesia and drowsiness from benzodiazepines reverse within a few minutes. In a patient dependent on benzodiazepines, the sudden loss of GABA support can cause agitation, anxiety and seizures."], ["Breathing", "It can improve benzodiazepine-induced hypoventilation, though reversal of respiratory depression is less reliable than reversal of sedation."], ["Seizure threshold", "Removing benzodiazepine protection unmasks seizures in patients with epilepsy or those who took seizure-causing drugs like tricyclic antidepressants."], ["Heart", "Arrhythmias have been reported, especially in mixed overdoses with tricyclics, where benzodiazepines were protecting against toxicity."]], "journey": "Flumazenil is given IV because it has large first-pass metabolism by the liver when taken by mouth. It enters the brain quickly, and reversal begins within one to two minutes. The liver breaks it down into inactive metabolites that leave in the urine. Its half-life is about one hour, which is shorter than most benzodiazepines, so sedation can come back (resedation) after it wears off. Patients must be watched and doses can be repeated.", "sidefx": "Most serious effects come from abruptly removing benzodiazepine action. In dependent patients this triggers acute withdrawal with agitation and seizures. In overdoses with tricyclic antidepressants or other seizure-causing drugs, the benzodiazepine was suppressing seizures that then appear. These seizures can be hard to treat because the benzodiazepine site is blocked. Resedation happens because flumazenil is cleared faster than the drug it reverses. For this reason it is used mainly for procedural sedation reversal rather than routine overdose care.", "fun": "Because of the seizure risk, flumazenil is not part of a routine 'coma cocktail' for unknown overdoses, a common board exam point.", "src": ["Flumazenil injection FDA label", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "fluorouracil": {"origin": "Fluorouracil (5-FU) was rationally designed. In the 1950s Charles Heidelberger at the University of Wisconsin noticed reports that rat liver tumors took up and used the nucleobase uracil more than normal tissue did. He reasoned that a fake uracil could jam the tumor's DNA machinery. Swapping a hydrogen for a fluorine atom at position 5 gave a molecule cells would accept as uracil but could not use properly. Robert Duschinsky and colleagues at Hoffmann-La Roche synthesized it, and Heidelberger's group reported its anticancer activity in 1957. The FDA approved it in 1962. It remains a backbone of treatment for colorectal and other gastrointestinal cancers, often paired with leucovorin, and a cream form treats precancerous skin lesions.", "timeline": [["1957", "Heidelberger, Duschinsky and colleagues report the synthesis and tumor activity of 5-fluorouracil."], ["1962", "FDA approves fluorouracil for cancer treatment."], ["1980s", "Leucovorin is shown to enhance 5-FU activity in colorectal cancer."]], "cell": "5-FU is a pyrimidine analog that must be activated inside cells. One path converts it to 5-fluoro-2'-deoxyuridine monophosphate (FdUMP). FdUMP binds thymidylate synthase together with the folate cofactor 5,10-methylene-tetrahydrofolate, forming a stable covalent ternary complex that shuts down the enzyme. Without it, cells cannot make thymidine monophosphate (dTMP), so DNA synthesis stops and cells undergo 'thymineless death'. Leucovorin raises the folate cofactor and makes the complex more stable. A second path makes FUTP, which is built into RNA and disrupts RNA processing, and FdUTP can be built into DNA. Its effect is greatest in the S phase of the cell cycle.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Dividing blood precursors are hit, causing neutropenia, thrombocytopenia and anemia, with the low point about one to two weeks after treatment."], ["Gut", "The fast-renewing lining of the mouth and intestines is damaged, causing mucositis and diarrhea, which can be severe and dehydrating."], ["Skin and hands", "Hand-foot syndrome (painful redness and peeling of palms and soles), darkening of the skin and sun sensitivity are common, especially with continuous infusion."], ["Heart", "It can cause coronary vasospasm with chest pain, ECG changes and rarely heart attack."], ["Brain", "Cerebellar ataxia and confusion can occur, and high ammonia levels can cause encephalopathy."], ["Tumors", "Rapidly dividing tumor cells such as colorectal, gastric and pancreatic cancers are slowed or killed."]], "journey": "5-FU is given IV because oral absorption is unreliable; capecitabine is an oral prodrug that turns into 5-FU. It spreads through body water and enters the spinal fluid. More than 80 percent is broken down by the enzyme dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase (DPD), mainly in the liver, into inactive products that leave as carbon dioxide in breath and as metabolites in urine. The half-life is very short, only minutes, which is why it is often given by continuous infusion.", "sidefx": "Toxicities hit the fastest-dividing normal tissues: marrow, gut lining, skin and hair follicles. People with DPD deficiency cannot break it down and can develop severe or fatal toxicity, which is why DPD testing is discussed before treatment. Coronary vasospasm causes chest pain and heart attacks. Uridine triacetate (Vistogard) is the antidote for overdose or early severe toxicity; it supplies uridine that competes with FUTP for RNA incorporation. Leucovorin does not reverse 5-FU toxicity; it makes it stronger.", "fun": "Leucovorin rescues cells from methotrexate but increases 5-FU toxicity, a classic board exam trap.", "src": ["Fluorouracil injection FDA label", "Heidelberger C et al. Nature 1957", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "fluticasone": {"origin": "Fluticasone propionate was developed by Glaxo in Britain in the late 1970s and early 1980s. Researchers wanted an inhaled or nasal steroid with very strong local anti-inflammatory power but almost no effect on the rest of the body. Their design used a fluorinated steroid with a special ester side chain that makes it very lipophilic so it stays in airway tissue, and makes any swallowed drug almost completely destroyed by the liver on its first pass. The FDA approved the nasal spray Flonase in 1994 and the asthma inhaler Flovent in 1996. In 2000 it was combined with salmeterol as Advair, which became one of the best-selling respiratory medicines. A related ester, fluticasone furoate, is used in Arnuity, Breo and Trelegy.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Glaxo develops fluticasone propionate."], ["1994", "FDA approves Flonase nasal spray."], ["1996", "FDA approves Flovent inhaler for asthma."], ["2000", "Advair (fluticasone with salmeterol) is approved."], ["2014", "Flonase becomes available over the counter in the US."]], "cell": "Fluticasone is a very potent agonist at the glucocorticoid receptor (GR), with high receptor affinity. It diffuses into airway cells and binds GR in the cytoplasm, which releases heat shock proteins and lets the complex enter the nucleus. There it switches on anti-inflammatory genes, such as lipocortin-1 (annexin A1, which inhibits phospholipase A2) and beta-2 receptor genes, and switches off inflammatory genes by blocking transcription factors NF-kB and AP-1. This reduces production of cytokines like IL-4, IL-5 and IL-13, chemokines and adhesion molecules. The result is fewer eosinophils, mast cells and T cells in the airway, less mucus, less swelling, and lower airway hyperresponsiveness. Because it changes gene expression, effects build over days to weeks.", "body": [["Lungs", "Airway inflammation and twitchiness fall, leading to fewer asthma attacks, better lung function, and fewer COPD exacerbations when combined with a bronchodilator. It does not relieve acute bronchospasm."], ["Nose", "Nasal sprays reduce congestion, sneezing, itching and runny nose in allergic rhinitis over several days."], ["Mouth and throat", "Drug deposited in the mouth suppresses local immunity, allowing oral thrush, and can affect vocal cords causing hoarseness."], ["Adrenal glands", "At high inhaled use, enough can reach the blood to suppress the HPA axis, especially with CYP3A4 inhibitors."], ["Lungs in COPD", "Local immune suppression increases the risk of pneumonia in COPD patients."], ["Bones and eyes", "Long-term high exposure may contribute to lower bone density, cataracts and glaucoma, though risk is much lower than with oral steroids."]], "journey": "Inhaled fluticasone lands mostly in the mouth and throat, with a smaller fraction reaching the lungs, where it is absorbed slowly. The swallowed portion is almost completely destroyed by CYP3A4 on first pass through the liver, so oral bioavailability is about one percent. What reaches the blood is highly protein bound and also cleared by CYP3A4. Metabolites leave mainly in the feces. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ritonavir or cobicistat block this clearance and allow large systemic exposure.", "sidefx": "Local effects come from steroid sitting on mucosa: thrush, hoarseness, sore throat, nasal irritation and nosebleeds. Rinsing the mouth and using a spacer reduce these. Systemic effects are uncommon because first-pass metabolism is so efficient, but with high use or with ritonavir, fluticasone can cause Cushing syndrome and adrenal suppression, and stopping suddenly can then cause adrenal crisis. In COPD the steroid's immune suppression raises pneumonia risk. There is no antidote; the dose is reduced and interacting drugs reviewed.", "fun": "Case reports of Cushing syndrome from fluticasone with ritonavir in people with HIV made this one of the best known steroid drug interactions.", "src": ["Flovent HFA (fluticasone propionate) FDA label", "Flonase FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "fluvastatin": {"origin": "The statin story began in Japan in the 1970s, when Akira Endo at Sankyo found compactin, a fungal compound from Penicillium that blocked cholesterol synthesis. Merck's lovastatin, from Aspergillus, became the first marketed statin in 1987. Fluvastatin, developed by Sandoz (now Novartis), took a different route: it was the first statin made entirely by chemical synthesis rather than derived from a fungal product. Its structure includes a fluorophenyl-indole core that mimics the binding of the natural substrate. The FDA approved it as Lescol in 1993, and an extended-release form, Lescol XL, followed. It is a lower-intensity statin today but is notable for being metabolized by CYP2C9 instead of CYP3A4.", "timeline": [["1976", "Akira Endo reports compactin, the first statin, from Penicillium."], ["1987", "Lovastatin becomes the first FDA-approved statin."], ["1993", "FDA approves fluvastatin (Lescol), the first fully synthetic statin."]], "cell": "HMG-CoA reductase is the rate-limiting enzyme in cholesterol synthesis; it converts HMG-CoA into mevalonate in liver cells. Fluvastatin's structure contains a part that mimics the HMG-CoA intermediate, so it binds the active site competitively and blocks the enzyme. Liver cells sense the drop in intracellular cholesterol, which activates SREBP-2, a transcription factor that increases production of LDL receptors. More LDL receptors on the liver surface pull LDL out of the blood. The result is lower LDL cholesterol, a modest drop in triglycerides, and a small rise in HDL. Statins also have so-called pleiotropic effects, such as stabilizing plaques and reducing vascular inflammation, partly from lower isoprenoid production.", "body": [["Liver", "Cholesterol synthesis falls and LDL receptor numbers rise, so the liver clears more LDL from the blood. Mild liver enzyme rises can occur."], ["Blood lipids", "LDL cholesterol falls moderately, by roughly a quarter to a third, with small benefits on triglycerides and HDL."], ["Arteries", "Lower LDL slows atherosclerotic plaque growth and stabilizes plaques, reducing heart attacks and strokes."], ["Muscle", "Some people develop muscle aches; rarely, muscle breakdown (rhabdomyolysis) occurs."], ["Pregnancy", "Cholesterol is essential for fetal development, so statins are generally avoided in pregnancy."]], "journey": "Fluvastatin is taken by mouth and absorbed quickly and almost completely, but the liver takes up much of it on the first pass. This is useful because the liver is where it needs to work. It is highly protein bound in the blood. Unlike most statins, it is broken down mainly by CYP2C9, with smaller roles for CYP3A4 and CYP2C8. Metabolites leave mainly in the bile and feces. The immediate-release half-life is short, a few hours, and the extended-release form lasts longer.", "sidefx": "Muscle symptoms are the most known statin effect. Blocking mevalonate production lowers coenzyme Q10 and other isoprenoids that muscle cells need, which may contribute to myalgia and, rarely, rhabdomyolysis with kidney injury. Risk rises with high exposure, such as with CYP2C9 inhibitors like fluconazole. Liver enzyme rises reflect the drug's concentration in the liver. A small rise in blood sugar and new diabetes is seen with statins. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped and rhabdomyolysis is treated with fluids.", "fun": "Because fluvastatin avoids most CYP3A4 metabolism, it has fewer interactions with drugs like macrolides and azole antifungals than simvastatin or atorvastatin.", "src": ["Lescol (fluvastatin) FDA label", "Endo A. Proc Jpn Acad Ser B 2010 (history of statins)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "fomepizole": {"origin": "Fomepizole (4-methylpyrazole) came from basic enzyme research. In the 1960s Swedish researchers, including Hugo Theorell's group, studied alcohol dehydrogenase (ADH) and found that pyrazole and its derivatives were strong inhibitors. 4-Methylpyrazole was less toxic than plain pyrazole and became a research tool. Toxicologists then realized it could treat poisoning by ethylene glycol (antifreeze) and methanol, because their damage comes from toxic acids made by ADH. Before fomepizole, the main option was IV ethanol, which competes for ADH but causes intoxication and is hard to manage. The FDA approved fomepizole as Antizol in 1997 for ethylene glycol poisoning, and later added methanol poisoning.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Pyrazole compounds are found to inhibit alcohol dehydrogenase."], ["1997", "FDA approves fomepizole (Antizol) for ethylene glycol poisoning."]], "cell": "Ethylene glycol and methanol are not very toxic themselves. Alcohol dehydrogenase in the liver oxidizes ethylene glycol to glycoaldehyde, which is then converted to glycolic acid, glyoxylic acid and oxalic acid. Methanol is oxidized by ADH to formaldehyde, then to formic acid. Fomepizole binds ADH with very high affinity and competitively blocks its active site, so the first and rate-limiting step is stopped. The parent alcohols stay in their less toxic form and are slowly cleared by the kidneys or removed by hemodialysis. This prevents formation of the acids that cause anion gap metabolic acidosis, calcium oxalate crystals in the kidneys, and formic acid damage to the optic nerve.", "body": [["Blood chemistry", "Toxic acid production stops, so the anion gap metabolic acidosis does not develop or stops worsening."], ["Kidneys", "In ethylene glycol poisoning, fewer calcium oxalate crystals form in the tubules, protecting against acute kidney injury."], ["Eyes and brain", "In methanol poisoning, blocking formic acid formation protects the retina and optic nerve from blindness and the basal ganglia from damage."], ["Brain", "Unlike ethanol therapy, fomepizole does not cause intoxication, which makes patients easier to monitor."]], "journey": "Fomepizole is given IV. It distributes throughout body water. It is metabolized by the liver, mainly by CYP2E1, to 4-carboxypyrazole, which is excreted in urine. Its elimination is saturable and changes over time: at higher levels it follows zero-order kinetics, and after about two days it induces its own metabolism (autoinduction), so it clears faster. It is removed by hemodialysis, so dosing is adjusted during dialysis.", "sidefx": "Fomepizole is generally well tolerated. Headache, nausea, dizziness and a metallic taste are the most common complaints. Phlebitis can occur at the IV site. Slow heart rate, mild liver enzyme rises and eosinophilia have been reported. Rare allergic reactions occur. Fomepizole does not remove toxic acids that have already formed, so late presenters may still need hemodialysis, along with cofactors such as folate for methanol and thiamine and pyridoxine for ethylene glycol.", "fun": "Fomepizole was used in veterinary medicine for dogs poisoned by sweet-tasting antifreeze before it was approved for people.", "src": ["Antizol (fomepizole) FDA label", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Brent J et al. N Engl J Med 1999"]}, "fondaparinux": {"origin": "Fondaparinux is heparin boiled down to its single most important piece. Heparin is a mixture of long sugar chains extracted from animal tissue, and in the late 1970s and early 1980s researchers, including Jean Choay and Maurice Petitou in France and groups in Sweden, found that a specific five-sugar sequence (a pentasaccharide) is what binds antithrombin. Chemists at Sanofi and Organon, including Petitou and Constant van Boeckel, then built this pentasaccharide entirely by chemical synthesis, a long multistep process with no animal source. The FDA approved it as Arixtra in 2001 for preventing deep vein thrombosis after hip fracture and joint replacement surgery. It showed that a precisely defined synthetic molecule could do the main anticoagulant job of heparin.", "timeline": [["1916", "Heparin is discovered at Johns Hopkins."], ["1980s", "The antithrombin-binding pentasaccharide sequence in heparin is identified and synthesized."], ["2001", "FDA approves fondaparinux (Arixtra) for thrombosis prevention after orthopedic surgery."]], "cell": "Antithrombin (antithrombin III) is a natural serine protease inhibitor that slowly inactivates clotting factors. Fondaparinux is the exact pentasaccharide sequence in heparin that binds antithrombin. Binding changes the shape of antithrombin and speeds up its inhibition of factor Xa roughly 300-fold. Fondaparinux then lets go and can activate another antithrombin molecule. Inhibiting thrombin (factor IIa) requires a chain long enough to bridge antithrombin and thrombin together, at least 18 sugars, and fondaparinux is only five, so it has essentially no direct anti-thrombin effect. With factor Xa blocked, the prothrombinase complex cannot convert prothrombin to thrombin, so less fibrin forms. It does not bind platelet factor 4, so it rarely causes heparin-induced thrombocytopenia.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Thrombin generation falls, so new clots are less likely to form and existing clots are less likely to grow. Standard aPTT and PT barely change, and anti-Xa levels are used if measurement is needed."], ["Veins and lungs", "Deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism are prevented after surgery and treated once present."], ["Platelets", "It does not directly affect platelet function, and because it does not bind platelet factor 4, HIT is very rare."], ["Spine", "In patients with spinal or epidural anesthesia or punctures, bleeding around the spinal cord can cause a hematoma and paralysis."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys remove it unchanged, so drug builds up with poor kidney function and bleeding risk rises."]], "journey": "Fondaparinux is given by subcutaneous injection and is absorbed completely, peaking in about two to three hours. In the blood it binds mostly to antithrombin rather than other proteins, so it has predictable effects without routine monitoring. It is not metabolized. It leaves unchanged in the urine. Its half-life is long, about 17 to 21 hours, allowing once-daily dosing, and it is longer in older adults and people with kidney disease. It is contraindicated with severe kidney impairment.", "sidefx": "Bleeding is the main risk because factor Xa inhibition reduces the body's ability to form clots. Risk is highest with kidney impairment, low body weight, older age, and use with antiplatelet drugs or NSAIDs. Spinal or epidural hematomas are a boxed warning. Thrombocytopenia can occur, though true HIT is rare. There is no specific reversal agent. Protamine does not work because the molecule is too small to be neutralized, and recombinant factor VIIa has been used in severe bleeding. It is not removed well by dialysis.", "fun": "Making fondaparinux by chemical synthesis takes dozens of steps, which made it one of the most complex fully synthetic sugar-based drugs ever produced at scale.", "src": ["Arixtra (fondaparinux) FDA label", "Petitou M, van Boeckel CA. Angew Chem Int Ed 2004", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "formoterol": {"origin": "Formoterol was created by the Japanese company Yamanouchi Pharmaceutical, which patented it in the early 1970s. It was designed as a beta-2 agonist that was both fast to start and long lasting. Short-acting drugs like albuterol work in minutes but wear off in four to six hours, while salmeterol lasts twelve hours but takes longer to start. Formoterol does both. It was used in Europe and Japan before reaching the US, where the FDA approved Foradil Aerolizer in 2001 as the second long-acting beta-2 agonist after salmeterol. It is now most often used in combinations with inhaled steroids such as budesonide (Symbicort) and mometasone (Dulera), and in triple inhalers.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Yamanouchi in Japan patents formoterol."], ["1990s", "Formoterol enters clinical use in Europe."], ["2001", "FDA approves Foradil Aerolizer for asthma maintenance."], ["2006", "Symbicort (budesonide and formoterol) is approved in the US."], ["2017", "FDA removes the boxed warning for LABA use combined with inhaled corticosteroids."]], "cell": "Formoterol is a selective agonist at beta-2 adrenergic receptors on airway smooth muscle. These are Gs-coupled receptors. Activation turns on adenylyl cyclase, raising cyclic AMP, which activates protein kinase A. PKA phosphorylates and inactivates myosin light chain kinase, lowers intracellular calcium, and opens calcium-activated potassium channels, so the muscle relaxes and the airways widen. Formoterol is moderately lipophilic. It enters the cell membrane, which acts as a depot, and leaks out slowly to keep stimulating the receptor, giving a 12-hour effect. Because it is also water-soluble enough to reach the receptor directly, it starts working within minutes, unlike salmeterol.", "body": [["Lungs", "Airway smooth muscle relaxes within minutes and stays relaxed for about 12 hours, improving airflow in asthma and COPD."], ["Heart", "Some beta-2 activation and spillover onto beta-1 receptors raise heart rate and can cause palpitations and arrhythmias."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Beta-2 stimulation of muscle causes tremor."], ["Potassium", "Beta-2 receptors drive the Na-K-ATPase, shifting potassium into cells and lowering blood potassium."], ["Metabolism", "Glucose can rise slightly because of beta-2 effects on liver glycogen breakdown."]], "journey": "Formoterol is inhaled as a powder, aerosol or nebulized solution. A portion reaches the lungs and acts locally; much of the rest is swallowed and absorbed. In the blood it is moderately protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly by glucuronidation and O-demethylation through several CYP enzymes, including CYP2D6 and CYP2C19. Metabolites and some unchanged drug leave in the urine. Onset is within one to three minutes, and the half-life is about 10 hours, with bronchodilation lasting around 12 hours.", "sidefx": "Side effects come from stimulating beta receptors outside the airways: tremor, tachycardia, palpitations, headache and nervousness. Hypokalemia and hyperglycemia come from beta-2 driven potassium shifts and glycogen breakdown. In asthma, using a LABA alone without an inhaled steroid was linked to more severe asthma events and deaths, so it is always paired with an inhaled corticosteroid for asthma. Paradoxical bronchospasm can occur. Overdose is managed supportively, and a cardioselective beta-blocker can be used with great caution.", "fun": "Because formoterol starts so quickly, budesonide-formoterol can be used as both a daily controller and a rescue inhaler, an approach called SMART (single maintenance and reliever therapy).", "src": ["Perforomist (formoterol) FDA label", "Foradil Aerolizer FDA label", "GINA Global Strategy for Asthma"]}, "foscarnet": {"origin": "Foscarnet (phosphonoformic acid) is a small, simple chemical that was first made in the 1920s, long before anyone knew what it could do. In the 1970s researchers at the Swedish company Astra found that it blocked viral DNA polymerases, including those of herpes viruses, by mimicking pyrophosphate. Its value became clear during the AIDS epidemic, when cytomegalovirus (CMV) retinitis was causing blindness in many patients. The FDA approved foscarnet as Foscavir in 1991 for CMV retinitis in people with AIDS, and later for acyclovir-resistant herpes simplex in immunocompromised patients. Because it does not need viral activation, it still works when the virus has mutated to resist ganciclovir and acyclovir.", "timeline": [["1920s", "Phosphonoformic acid is first synthesized."], ["1978", "Astra researchers report that foscarnet inhibits herpes virus DNA polymerase."], ["1991", "FDA approves foscarnet (Foscavir) for CMV retinitis in AIDS."]], "cell": "When a DNA polymerase adds a nucleotide to a growing DNA strand, it splits off pyrophosphate from the incoming deoxynucleotide triphosphate. Foscarnet is a pyrophosphate analog. It binds directly to the pyrophosphate-binding site of viral DNA polymerase (and HIV reverse transcriptase) and blocks this step, so new nucleotides cannot be added and viral DNA synthesis stops. It does not need to be phosphorylated by viral kinases like thymidine kinase (HSV) or UL97 (CMV). That means it still works against CMV with UL97 mutations and HSV lacking thymidine kinase, the common causes of resistance to ganciclovir and acyclovir. It inhibits viral polymerases much more than human DNA polymerases.", "body": [["Eyes", "CMV replication in the retina is suppressed, which halts progression of CMV retinitis."], ["Kidneys", "Foscarnet is filtered and concentrated in the kidneys, where it can damage tubules and cause acute kidney injury, crystal deposits and nephrogenic diabetes insipidus."], ["Electrolytes", "It chelates divalent cations, lowering ionized calcium and magnesium. Potassium and phosphate can also become abnormal."], ["Nerves and heart", "Low ionized calcium causes tingling around the mouth, muscle spasm, seizures and QT prolongation."], ["Genitals", "Concentrated drug in urine can cause painful genital ulcers, which hydration and good hygiene help prevent."], ["Bones", "Part of the drug is deposited in bone and released slowly over time."]], "journey": "Foscarnet is given only IV because it is poorly absorbed by mouth. It enters tissues including the spinal fluid and eye. It is not metabolized. The kidneys remove most of it unchanged by filtration and secretion, so dosing is adjusted to kidney function and patients receive IV fluids before infusions. The plasma half-life is a few hours, but a portion binds to bone and is released slowly, giving a much longer terminal half-life.", "sidefx": "Kidney toxicity is the main limit. High concentrations in tubules injure cells, and saline loading reduces this. Electrolyte problems come from foscarnet's ability to bind calcium and magnesium: ionized calcium can drop even when total calcium looks normal, causing tingling, tetany, seizures and arrhythmias, made worse by fast infusion. Genital ulcers come from drug in the urine. Anemia, nausea and fever are common. There is no specific antidote; hydration, slow infusion and electrolyte replacement are the key measures.", "fun": "Foscarnet is one of the few antivirals that is not a nucleoside analog, which is why it is the go-to drug for resistant CMV on exams.", "src": ["Foscavir (foscarnet) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "fosphenytoin": {"origin": "Fosphenytoin was invented to fix the problems of IV phenytoin. Phenytoin, discovered as an anticonvulsant by Houston Merritt and Tracy Putnam in 1938, is poorly soluble in water. IV phenytoin must be dissolved in propylene glycol and alcohol at a very alkaline pH, which causes burning pain, vein damage, and the 'purple glove syndrome' of swelling and discoloration of the hand. Fast infusion also causes low blood pressure and arrhythmias. Chemists added a phosphate group to phenytoin, making a water-soluble prodrug that the body's phosphatases quickly turn back into phenytoin. It was developed by Parke-Davis (Warner-Lambert) and approved by the FDA in 1996 as Cerebyx. It can be given faster and can even be given intramuscularly.", "timeline": [["1938", "Merritt and Putnam report phenytoin as an anticonvulsant."], ["1996", "FDA approves fosphenytoin (Cerebyx)."], ["2020", "Sesquient, a fosphenytoin formulation that can be stored at room temperature, is approved."]], "cell": "Fosphenytoin itself has no anticonvulsant activity. Phosphatase enzymes in blood and tissues cleave its phosphate group, releasing phenytoin, phosphate and a small amount of formaldehyde. Phenytoin binds voltage-gated sodium channels in neurons and stabilizes their inactivated state. Each sodium channel then takes longer to recover between firings. This has little effect on normal firing but strongly limits the rapid, repetitive firing that spreads a seizure. Doses are expressed in phenytoin sodium equivalents (PE) so the amount matches phenytoin.", "body": [["Brain", "Rapid, repetitive neuron firing is dampened, which helps stop status epilepticus and prevent seizures after head injury or neurosurgery."], ["Heart", "Phenytoin also blocks cardiac sodium channels, so fast infusion can cause hypotension, bradycardia, heart block and arrhythmias. ECG and blood pressure monitoring are required."], ["Skin", "Itching and burning, often in the groin, are common during infusion and are thought to relate to the phosphate group."], ["Veins", "Because it is water-soluble and less alkaline, it causes much less vein irritation and purple glove syndrome than IV phenytoin."], ["Cerebellum", "Nystagmus, dizziness and unsteady walking are signs of high phenytoin levels."]], "journey": "Fosphenytoin is given IV or IM and is completely available. Phosphatases convert it to phenytoin with a conversion half-life of about 15 minutes. From there it behaves like phenytoin: highly bound to albumin, metabolized in the liver by CYP2C9 and CYP2C19 with saturable (zero-order) kinetics at higher levels, and excreted as metabolites in the urine. In low albumin or kidney failure, more fosphenytoin and phenytoin are free, which increases effect and side effects.", "sidefx": "Cardiovascular toxicity, including hypotension and arrhythmias, comes from phenytoin's sodium channel block in the heart and is related to infusion speed, so it has a boxed warning on rate. Itching and paresthesias during infusion are linked to the phosphate. Phenytoin effects follow: nystagmus, ataxia, sedation, gum overgrowth with long-term use, and severe skin reactions such as SJS/TEN, more common in people with HLA-B*1502. Phosphate load matters in kidney failure. There is no antidote; the infusion is slowed or stopped and care is supportive.", "fun": "Fosphenytoin dosing is written in phenytoin equivalents (PE), and mixing up 'PE' with plain milligrams has caused real medication errors.", "src": ["Cerebyx (fosphenytoin) FDA label", "Merritt HH, Putnam TJ. JAMA 1938", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "galantamine": {"origin": "Galantamine is a natural alkaloid from snowdrops and related plants of the amaryllis family. In the early 1950s Soviet pharmacologists, including Mikhail Mashkovsky and R. P. Kruglikova-Lvova, reported that galantamine from the Caucasian snowdrop (Galanthus woronowii) inhibited acetylcholinesterase. In Bulgaria, Dimitar Paskov developed an industrial process to extract it from the common snowdrop, and it was used in Eastern Europe as Nivalin for nerve and muscle conditions and to reverse neuromuscular blockade. Decades later it was developed for Alzheimer disease, and the FDA approved it in 2001 as Reminyl. The name was changed to Razadyne in 2005 after prescribing mix-ups with the diabetes drug Amaryl. Today it is also made synthetically and extracted from daffodils.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Soviet scientists report galantamine from snowdrops as an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor."], ["1950s", "Bulgaria begins producing galantamine (Nivalin) from Galanthus nivalis."], ["2001", "FDA approves galantamine (Reminyl) for mild to moderate Alzheimer disease."], ["2005", "Reminyl is renamed Razadyne after confusion with Amaryl."]], "cell": "Alzheimer disease causes loss of cholinergic neurons from the nucleus basalis of Meynert, reducing acetylcholine in the cortex and hippocampus. Galantamine is a reversible, competitive inhibitor of acetylcholinesterase, the enzyme that breaks down acetylcholine in the synapse. With the enzyme slowed, acetylcholine lasts longer and reaches higher levels, which helps the remaining cholinergic signaling. Galantamine is also described as an allosteric potentiating ligand at nicotinic acetylcholine receptors, binding a site separate from acetylcholine and making those receptors respond more strongly. It does not stop the underlying loss of neurons, so benefits are modest and symptomatic.", "body": [["Brain", "Memory, attention and daily function improve modestly or decline more slowly for a time in mild to moderate Alzheimer disease."], ["Gut", "More acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors in the gut increases secretions and motility, causing nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, poor appetite and weight loss."], ["Heart", "Increased vagal tone slows the heart and AV conduction, which can cause bradycardia, heart block and fainting."], ["Lungs", "Increased cholinergic tone can worsen bronchospasm in asthma or COPD."], ["Bladder", "Cholinergic stimulation can increase urinary frequency or worsen outflow obstruction."]], "journey": "Galantamine is taken by mouth and absorbed well, with high bioavailability and low protein binding. It crosses the blood-brain barrier easily. The liver metabolizes it by CYP2D6 and CYP3A4, so inhibitors such as paroxetine or ketoconazole raise levels. A portion leaves unchanged in the urine. The half-life is about seven hours. It is started low and increased slowly to lessen gut side effects.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are predictable from more acetylcholine throughout the body. Gut effects (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, weight loss) come from muscarinic stimulation. Bradycardia, heart block and syncope come from vagal effects on the heart, which raises fall risk. Bronchospasm can worsen. Rare serious skin reactions such as SJS have been reported. In overdose, a cholinergic crisis can occur, and atropine is used to block the muscarinic effects.", "fun": "Some historians have suggested the magical herb 'moly' that protected Odysseus from Circe's drugs might have been a snowdrop, since galantamine counters anticholinergic poisons.", "src": ["Razadyne (galantamine) FDA label", "Heinrich M, Teoh HL. J Ethnopharmacol 2004", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "ganciclovir": {"origin": "Ganciclovir was developed in the early 1980s as a cousin of acyclovir, the herpes drug made by Burroughs Wellcome. Acyclovir worked poorly against cytomegalovirus (CMV), which lacks the thymidine kinase acyclovir needs. Researchers at Syntex and elsewhere made a related guanosine analog with an extra hydroxymethyl group that CMV could activate through its own kinase, UL97. With the AIDS epidemic, CMV retinitis was blinding many patients, and ganciclovir became the first drug approved for a CMV disease when the FDA approved Cytovene in 1989 for CMV retinitis in immunocompromised patients. An oral prodrug, valganciclovir (Valcyte), followed in 2001, and ganciclovir is now central to preventing CMV in organ and stem cell transplant recipients.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Ganciclovir is synthesized as an acyclovir-related nucleoside analog active against CMV."], ["1989", "FDA approves ganciclovir (Cytovene) for CMV retinitis, the first approved treatment for CMV disease."], ["2001", "Valganciclovir (Valcyte), an oral prodrug, is approved."]], "cell": "Ganciclovir is a guanosine analog prodrug. In CMV-infected cells, the virus's UL97 protein kinase adds the first phosphate. In HSV-infected cells, viral thymidine kinase does this step. Host cell kinases then add two more phosphates to make ganciclovir triphosphate. This molecule competes with deoxyguanosine triphosphate for the viral DNA polymerase (UL54) and is built into the growing viral DNA chain, slowing and eventually stopping elongation. Because the first phosphorylation needs a viral enzyme, the active form builds up mainly in infected cells. Resistance most often comes from mutations in UL97 and less often from UL54 polymerase mutations, which can also cause cross-resistance to cidofovir and foscarnet.", "body": [["Eyes", "CMV replication in the retina is suppressed, halting progression of retinitis. It is also used as an intraocular implant or injection."], ["Gut and lungs", "It treats CMV colitis, esophagitis and pneumonitis in transplant and HIV patients."], ["Bone marrow", "Some activation occurs in dividing human cells, so the marrow is suppressed, causing neutropenia, thrombocytopenia and anemia."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys clear it, so kidney function guides dosing, and injury can occur."], ["Reproductive organs", "It is toxic to sperm and causes birth defects in animal studies, so it is handled as a hazardous drug."]], "journey": "Ganciclovir itself is poorly absorbed by mouth, so it is given IV, while valganciclovir is an oral prodrug that is well absorbed and converted to ganciclovir by esterases in the gut wall and liver. Ganciclovir distributes widely, including into the eye and spinal fluid. It is not significantly metabolized. The kidneys excrete it unchanged by filtration and tubular secretion. The half-life is about three hours with normal kidney function and much longer in kidney failure.", "sidefx": "Bone marrow suppression is the key toxicity, especially neutropenia, because dividing marrow cells can phosphorylate the drug and take it into their DNA. Combining it with other marrow-suppressing drugs like zidovudine increases this risk. Kidney impairment raises levels and toxicity. Fever, diarrhea, nausea and seizures can occur. It is carcinogenic and teratogenic in animals and can reduce fertility. There is no antidote; doses are reduced or held, and filgrastim can be used for severe neutropenia.", "fun": "A common board pairing: ganciclovir causes leukopenia, while foscarnet and cidofovir mainly cause nephrotoxicity.", "src": ["Cytovene (ganciclovir) FDA label", "Valcyte (valganciclovir) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "gentamicin": {"origin": "Gentamicin came from a soil microbe. In 1963 a team at Schering Corporation in New Jersey, led by Marvin Weinstein, described a new antibiotic from Micromonospora purpurea, a rare type of actinomycete bacterium. Most earlier aminoglycosides, like streptomycin, came from Streptomyces and use the ending '-mycin'. Antibiotics from Micromonospora are spelled with '-micin' to show their different source. Gentamicin had strong activity against gram-negative bacteria, including Pseudomonas aeruginosa, which was a growing problem in burn units and hospitals. It became one of the most used hospital antibiotics and remains widely used, especially in combinations for serious infections and endocarditis.", "timeline": [["1944", "Streptomycin, the first aminoglycoside, is discovered."], ["1963", "Schering scientists describe gentamicin from Micromonospora purpurea."], ["1970s", "Gentamicin becomes a mainstay for serious gram-negative hospital infections."], ["1990s", "Once-daily (extended-interval) dosing becomes widely adopted."]], "cell": "Gentamicin is positively charged. It first binds to negatively charged lipopolysaccharide on gram-negative outer membranes, displacing magnesium and calcium and making the membrane leaky. It then crosses the inner membrane by an oxygen-dependent transport process driven by the membrane potential, which is why it does not work against anaerobes. Inside, it binds the 16S rRNA of the bacterial 30S ribosomal subunit. This causes misreading of mRNA, so wrong amino acids are added and faulty proteins are made. Some of these faulty proteins insert into the membrane and damage it further, letting even more drug in. It also blocks initiation complexes. The result is rapid, concentration-dependent killing with a post-antibiotic effect. Beta-lactams damage the cell wall and help gentamicin enter, explaining synergy for gram-positives like enterococci.", "body": [["Infections", "It rapidly kills aerobic gram-negative bacteria such as E. coli, Klebsiella, Proteus and Pseudomonas, and is used with a beta-lactam for some gram-positive endocarditis."], ["Kidneys", "It is taken up and stored in proximal tubule cells, where it damages lysosomes and mitochondria, causing acute tubular necrosis, rising creatinine and magnesium wasting. This is usually reversible."], ["Inner ear", "It builds up in inner ear fluids and kills hair cells in the cochlea and vestibular system, causing hearing loss, tinnitus, vertigo and imbalance that can be permanent."], ["Neuromuscular junction", "It reduces acetylcholine release and blocks receptors, which can cause weakness and respiratory failure, especially with neuromuscular blockers or myasthenia gravis."], ["Pregnancy", "It crosses the placenta and can cause fetal hearing loss."]], "journey": "Gentamicin is highly polar and is not absorbed from the gut, so it is given IV or IM, and topically for eye and skin infections. It distributes mainly into extracellular fluid and penetrates poorly into the spinal fluid, eye and lung secretions. It is not metabolized. It is filtered by the kidneys and excreted unchanged, so its half-life of two to three hours becomes much longer when kidney function falls. Peak and trough blood levels are monitored to balance killing power and toxicity.", "sidefx": "Nephrotoxicity happens because proximal tubule cells take up gentamicin through the megalin receptor and accumulate it. Ototoxicity comes from accumulation in inner ear hair cells and oxidative damage; certain mitochondrial gene variants increase this risk. Toxicity rises with high trough levels, long courses, older age, dehydration and other toxic drugs like vancomycin, loop diuretics or cisplatin. Extended-interval dosing lowers trough levels. Neuromuscular blockade can be reversed with IV calcium. There is no antidote for kidney or ear damage; hemodialysis removes the drug.", "fun": "The spelling difference between 'gentamicin' (from Micromonospora) and 'tobramycin' (from Streptomyces) is a real naming convention, not a typo.", "src": ["Gentamicin sulfate injection FDA label", "Weinstein MJ et al. J Med Chem 1963", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "glimepiride": {"origin": "Sulfonylureas were discovered by accident. In 1942 in Montpellier, France, Marcel Janbon noticed that an experimental sulfonamide antibiotic used for typhoid caused low blood sugar, and physiologist Auguste Loubatieres showed it worked by stimulating the pancreas to release insulin. First-generation sulfonylureas such as tolbutamide followed in the 1950s. Glimepiride, developed by Hoechst in Germany, is a later, more potent second-generation sulfonylurea that works with once-daily dosing. The FDA approved it as Amaryl in 1995 for type 2 diabetes. It is still widely used because it is inexpensive and effective at lowering glucose.", "timeline": [["1942", "Janbon and Loubatieres observe that a sulfonamide causes hypoglycemia by stimulating insulin release."], ["1950s", "First-generation sulfonylureas like tolbutamide reach the market."], ["1995", "FDA approves glimepiride (Amaryl)."]], "cell": "In pancreatic beta cells, ATP-sensitive potassium (K-ATP) channels keep the cell resting. Each channel is built from Kir6.2 pore subunits and SUR1 regulatory subunits. Normally, when glucose rises, the cell makes more ATP, which closes these channels. Glimepiride binds SUR1 and closes the channel directly, whatever the glucose level. Potassium can no longer leave, so the membrane depolarizes. That opens voltage-gated L-type calcium channels, calcium rushes in, and insulin-containing granules fuse with the membrane and release insulin. Because insulin release is triggered even when glucose is low, hypoglycemia can occur. The drug needs working beta cells, so it is not useful in type 1 diabetes.", "body": [["Pancreas", "Beta cells release more insulin, including between meals and overnight."], ["Liver", "Higher insulin suppresses glucose production and glycogen breakdown."], ["Muscle and fat", "Insulin increases glucose uptake and fat storage, which contributes to weight gain."], ["Blood glucose", "Fasting glucose and HbA1c fall. Low blood sugar can occur, especially with missed meals, alcohol, or kidney disease."], ["Heart", "SUR2 subunits in the heart are much less affected by glimepiride than by older agents, but cardiovascular safety of sulfonylureas has long been debated."]], "journey": "Glimepiride is taken by mouth and absorbed completely. It is highly bound to plasma proteins. The liver metabolizes it by CYP2C9 into a weakly active metabolite and an inactive one, which leave in the urine and feces. The half-life is about five to nine hours, but its effect lasts about 24 hours, so it is taken once daily. CYP2C9 inhibitors such as fluconazole, and poor kidney function, can increase its effect.", "sidefx": "Hypoglycemia is the main risk because insulin is released whether or not glucose is high. Older adults, people with kidney or liver disease, and those who skip meals or drink alcohol are most at risk. Weight gain reflects insulin's storage effects. As a sulfonamide derivative it can cause rash and, rarely, hemolysis in G6PD deficiency. Treatment of hypoglycemia is glucose, and in prolonged or repeated hypoglycemia octreotide is used because it blocks insulin release.", "fun": "Amaryl's name was confused often enough with Reminyl that the Alzheimer drug galantamine was renamed Razadyne.", "src": ["Amaryl (glimepiride) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "glipizide": {"origin": "Glipizide is a second-generation sulfonylurea, a drug family that began in 1942 when French physicians noticed that a sulfonamide antibiotic lowered blood sugar. Second-generation agents were developed in the 1960s and early 1970s to be far more potent and cause fewer drug interactions than first-generation drugs like tolbutamide and chlorpropamide. Glipizide was approved in the US in 1984, marketed by Pfizer as Glucotrol. An extended-release form, Glucotrol XL, was later introduced. Glipizide is popular in older adults and people with kidney disease because its metabolites are inactive and its action is shorter than glyburide's.", "timeline": [["1942", "Sulfonamide-induced hypoglycemia is observed in France, leading to sulfonylureas."], ["1984", "FDA approves glipizide (Glucotrol) and glyburide, the first second-generation sulfonylureas in the US."], ["1990s", "Glucotrol XL, an extended-release form, is introduced."]], "cell": "Glipizide binds the SUR1 subunit of the ATP-sensitive potassium (K-ATP) channel on pancreatic beta cells. Closing these channels stops potassium from leaving the cell, so the membrane depolarizes. Depolarization opens voltage-gated calcium channels, calcium enters, and insulin granules are released by exocytosis. This mimics the normal effect of glucose, which closes the same channels by raising ATP. Because glipizide closes the channels even when glucose is normal or low, it can cause hypoglycemia. It works only if the pancreas still has functioning beta cells.", "body": [["Pancreas", "Insulin release increases, especially around meals since glipizide acts faster than many sulfonylureas."], ["Liver", "Higher insulin reduces hepatic glucose output."], ["Muscle and fat", "Insulin increases glucose uptake and storage, causing modest weight gain."], ["Blood glucose", "Fasting and after-meal glucose and HbA1c fall. Hypoglycemia can occur if meals are missed."]], "journey": "Glipizide is absorbed quickly and nearly completely by mouth. Immediate-release tablets work best when taken about 30 minutes before a meal, because food delays absorption. It is highly protein bound. The liver metabolizes it extensively, mainly by CYP2C9, into inactive metabolites that are excreted mostly in the urine. The half-life is short, about two to five hours, though its effect lasts longer. Because its metabolites are inactive, it is a safer sulfonylurea choice in kidney impairment than glyburide.", "sidefx": "Hypoglycemia occurs because insulin release is triggered regardless of blood sugar. Symptoms include sweating, tremor, confusion and, if severe, seizures and coma. Risk increases with older age, poor food intake, alcohol, and drugs that inhibit CYP2C9 or displace it from proteins. Weight gain comes from insulin's anabolic effects. Rarely, hemolysis occurs in G6PD deficiency. Hypoglycemia is treated with glucose, and octreotide is used for recurrent or refractory sulfonylurea-induced hypoglycemia.", "fun": "The extended-release Glucotrol XL uses an osmotic pump tablet, and the empty tablet shell can appear in stool.", "src": ["Glucotrol (glipizide) FDA label", "Glucotrol XL FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "glucagon": {"origin": "Glucagon was discovered as a contaminant. In 1922 and 1923, soon after insulin was first given to patients, researchers noticed that crude pancreatic extracts sometimes caused a brief rise in blood sugar before it fell. In 1923 C. P. Kimball and John Murlin isolated the substance responsible and named it glucagon, from 'glucose agonist'. Its 29-amino acid structure was worked out in the 1950s by researchers at Eli Lilly. Lilly marketed glucagon extracted from animal pancreases as a rescue drug for severe hypoglycemia. Recombinant human glucagon later replaced the animal product, and new ready-to-use forms arrived, including a nasal powder (Baqsimi) and a liquid prefilled syringe and autoinjector (Gvoke) in 2019.", "timeline": [["1923", "Kimball and Murlin identify and name glucagon from pancreatic extracts."], ["1957", "The amino acid sequence of glucagon is reported by Lilly researchers."], ["1960s", "Glucagon becomes available as a treatment for severe hypoglycemia in the US."], ["2019", "Nasal glucagon (Baqsimi) and ready-to-use liquid glucagon (Gvoke) are approved."]], "cell": "Glucagon binds the glucagon receptor, a Gs-coupled receptor found mainly on liver cells. Gs activates adenylyl cyclase, raising cyclic AMP and activating protein kinase A. In the liver, PKA turns on glycogen phosphorylase (glycogenolysis) and turns off glycogen synthase, releasing glucose from stored glycogen within minutes. It also increases gluconeogenesis and ketogenesis. In heart muscle, glucagon receptors raise cAMP without going through beta-adrenergic receptors, which increases heart rate and contractility even when beta receptors are blocked. In smooth muscle of the gut, glucagon causes relaxation. Glucagon works only if there is liver glycogen to release.", "body": [["Liver", "Glycogen is broken down and glucose is released into the blood, raising blood sugar within about 10 minutes."], ["Heart", "By raising cAMP independently of beta receptors, it increases heart rate and contractility, which is used in beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker overdose."], ["Gut", "Smooth muscle of the stomach, intestines and lower esophagus relaxes. It is used to reduce bowel motion during imaging and has been tried for food bolus impaction."], ["Brain", "Raising blood glucose restores consciousness in severe hypoglycemia."], ["Electrolytes", "It can lower potassium and cause mild hyperglycemia after the dose."], ["Stomach", "It triggers nausea and vomiting, so an unconscious patient should be placed on their side."]], "journey": "Glucagon is a peptide that would be digested if swallowed, so it is given IM, subcutaneously, IV or as a nasal powder absorbed through the nasal lining. IV glucagon works within a minute, IM or subcutaneous within about 10 minutes, and nasal within about 15 minutes. It is broken down quickly by the liver, kidneys and plasma enzymes, with a half-life of only minutes. Its effect lasts an hour or so, so the patient should eat once awake.", "sidefx": "Nausea and vomiting are common and come from glucagon's effects on the gut and brain. Tachycardia and transient hypertension reflect increased cAMP in the heart. Glucagon is not effective when glycogen stores are low, such as in starvation, adrenal insufficiency or chronic hypoglycemia, or after heavy alcohol use. It is contraindicated in pheochromocytoma because it can trigger catecholamine release, and in insulinoma because it can provoke insulin release and rebound hypoglycemia. Allergic reactions are rare.", "fun": "Glucagon's ability to bypass beta receptors makes it a classic antidote question for beta-blocker overdose.", "src": ["GlucaGen and Gvoke FDA labels", "Baqsimi (nasal glucagon) FDA label", "Kimball CP, Murlin JR. J Biol Chem 1923"]}, "glyburide": {"origin": "Glyburide, called glibenclamide outside the US, was developed in the late 1960s by Boehringer Mannheim and Hoechst in Germany as a highly potent second-generation sulfonylurea. It was one of the first two second-generation sulfonylureas approved in the US in 1984, alongside glipizide, marketed as DiaBeta and Micronase. A micronized form (Glynase) and a combination with metformin (Glucovance) followed. Glyburide has been widely used worldwide, but its long-lasting effect and active metabolites make it a higher risk for prolonged hypoglycemia, so it is now avoided in older adults. It is also a classic research tool for studying K-ATP channels.", "timeline": [["1969", "Glibenclamide (glyburide) is introduced in Europe."], ["1984", "FDA approves glyburide in the US."], ["2012", "The Beers Criteria list glyburide as a drug to avoid in older adults because of prolonged hypoglycemia."]], "cell": "Glyburide binds with high affinity to the SUR1 subunit of K-ATP channels on pancreatic beta cells. Closing these channels depolarizes the cell, which opens voltage-gated calcium channels, and the rise in calcium triggers insulin granule release. Glyburide binds SUR1 tightly and is taken up into the beta cell, which helps explain its long effect beyond its plasma half-life. It also has active metabolites that continue to stimulate insulin release. Like all sulfonylureas, it needs functioning beta cells and releases insulin whether or not glucose is high.", "body": [["Pancreas", "Insulin release rises strongly and stays elevated, including overnight."], ["Liver", "Insulin suppresses glucose production by the liver."], ["Muscle and fat", "More glucose uptake and storage, causing weight gain."], ["Blood glucose", "Fasting glucose and HbA1c fall, but the long effect makes prolonged, recurrent hypoglycemia more likely than with glipizide."], ["Kidneys", "Active metabolites rely on the kidneys for clearance, so kidney disease greatly increases hypoglycemia risk."]], "journey": "Glyburide is taken by mouth and well absorbed, and the micronized form is absorbed faster. It is more than 99 percent bound to plasma proteins, so other highly bound drugs can displace it. The liver metabolizes it, mainly by CYP3A4 and CYP2C9, into hydroxy metabolites that are weakly active. These leave in both urine and bile. The half-life is about 10 hours, but the glucose-lowering effect can last up to a day or longer.", "sidefx": "Hypoglycemia is the main danger, and with glyburide it can be prolonged and recur over many hours because of its long action and active metabolites. Older adults and people with kidney disease are at highest risk, and overdose patients often need admission for monitoring. Weight gain comes from insulin's anabolic effects. Rare effects include rash, cholestatic jaundice, and hemolysis in G6PD deficiency. Hypoglycemia is treated with glucose, and octreotide suppresses further insulin release in recurrent cases.", "fun": "Glyburide was once commonly used for gestational diabetes, but current guidelines prefer insulin or metformin because glyburide crosses the placenta and is linked to neonatal hypoglycemia.", "src": ["DiaBeta and Glynase (glyburide) FDA labels", "American Geriatrics Society Beers Criteria", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "glycopyrrolate": {"origin": "Glycopyrrolate is a synthetic relative of atropine, the alkaloid from the deadly nightshade plant (Atropa belladonna). Chemists wanted an anticholinergic that would reduce stomach acid and secretions without the confusion, delirium and other brain effects of atropine and scopolamine. Making the molecule a quaternary ammonium compound, with a permanent positive charge, kept it from crossing the blood-brain barrier. It was approved in the US in 1961 as Robinul to treat peptic ulcer. As better ulcer drugs replaced it, anesthesiologists adopted it to reduce saliva and airway secretions during surgery and to block the slow heart rate caused by neostigmine when reversing muscle relaxants. An oral solution (Cuvposa) was later approved for chronic drooling in children.", "timeline": [["1830s", "Atropine is isolated from belladonna, the model for later anticholinergics."], ["1961", "Glycopyrrolate (Robinul) is approved in the US for peptic ulcer."], ["2010", "An oral solution (Cuvposa) is approved for chronic severe drooling in children."]], "cell": "Glycopyrrolate is a competitive antagonist at muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, mainly M1, M2 and M3. It binds the receptor without activating it and prevents acetylcholine from binding. Blocking M3 on glands reduces Gq-mediated calcium signaling, so salivary, bronchial, sweat and gastric secretions fall. Blocking M3 on smooth muscle relaxes the gut and bladder and widens airways. Blocking M2 on the SA and AV nodes removes vagal braking, so the heart rate rises. Because it is a quaternary ammonium with a permanent charge, it does not cross the blood-brain barrier or placenta well and has very few central effects.", "body": [["Mouth and airway", "Saliva and bronchial secretions drop, which keeps the airway dry during surgery and helps control drooling and death rattle."], ["Heart", "Blocking vagal input raises heart rate, which counters bradycardia during surgery and from neostigmine."], ["Stomach and gut", "Gastric acid secretion and gut motility fall, causing constipation."], ["Bladder", "Relaxing the bladder wall can cause urinary retention, especially with an enlarged prostate."], ["Skin", "Reduced sweating can cause flushing and heat intolerance."], ["Brain", "Very little reaches the brain, so it rarely causes the confusion or delirium seen with atropine or scopolamine."]], "journey": "Glycopyrrolate is given IV, IM or by mouth. Oral absorption is low and variable because the charged molecule crosses gut membranes poorly, and high-fat meals reduce it further. IV doses act within about a minute. It distributes mainly outside the brain and crosses the placenta poorly. Little is metabolized. Most is excreted unchanged in the urine and some in bile. The plasma half-life is about an hour, but antisialagogue effects last several hours.", "sidefx": "Side effects are classic peripheral anticholinergic effects: dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention, blurred vision, flushing, reduced sweating and tachycardia. Heat intolerance and fever can occur because sweating is blocked. It can trigger angle-closure glaucoma in susceptible eyes. Central effects are rare because it does not cross the blood-brain barrier. For severe overdose, the cholinesterase inhibitor neostigmine can reverse peripheral effects; physostigmine is not needed because there are few central effects.", "fun": "Glycopyrrolate is often given together with neostigmine in the operating room so the neostigmine reverses muscle relaxation while glycopyrrolate blocks its heart-slowing and secretion effects.", "src": ["Robinul (glycopyrrolate) FDA label", "Cuvposa FDA label", "Miller's Anesthesia"]}, "haloperidol": {"origin": "Haloperidol was discovered by Paul Janssen and his team at Janssen Pharmaceutica in Beerse, Belgium, in 1958. They had been modifying the opioid pethidine (meperidine) looking for stronger painkillers, and some compounds caused calming and catalepsy in animals, like the recently discovered antipsychotic chlorpromazine. By changing the structure they made a butyrophenone, haloperidol, with powerful antipsychotic effects and almost no opioid activity. It was used in Europe within a year and was approved by the FDA in 1967, marketed as Haldol. It became one of the most widely used antipsychotics, a standard drug for acute agitation and delirium, and is on the WHO list of essential medicines. A long-acting decanoate injection was later developed.", "timeline": [["1952", "Chlorpromazine, the first antipsychotic, is introduced in France."], ["1958", "Paul Janssen's team synthesizes haloperidol in Belgium."], ["1967", "FDA approves haloperidol (Haldol) in the US."], ["1980s", "Haloperidol decanoate, a monthly depot injection, becomes available in the US."]], "cell": "Haloperidol is a potent antagonist at dopamine D2 receptors, which are Gi-coupled receptors. Normally dopamine at D2 lowers cAMP and reduces neuron firing. In the mesolimbic pathway, too much dopamine signaling is linked to hallucinations and delusions, and blocking about 65 to 80 percent of D2 receptors reduces these positive symptoms. In the chemoreceptor trigger zone of the brainstem, D2 blockade prevents nausea and vomiting. Blocking D2 in the nigrostriatal pathway upsets the balance between dopamine and acetylcholine in the basal ganglia, causing extrapyramidal symptoms. Blocking D2 in the tuberoinfundibular pathway removes dopamine's brake on prolactin release. Haloperidol has relatively little anticholinergic, antihistamine or alpha-blocking activity compared with low-potency typical antipsychotics.", "body": [["Brain (mesolimbic)", "Hallucinations, delusions and agitation decrease, often within hours for agitation and over days to weeks for psychosis."], ["Brain (basal ganglia)", "Extrapyramidal symptoms occur: acute dystonia within days, akathisia, drug-induced parkinsonism, and tardive dyskinesia with long-term use."], ["Brainstem", "Blocking the chemoreceptor trigger zone reduces nausea and vomiting."], ["Pituitary", "Higher prolactin can cause breast enlargement, milk production, and menstrual and sexual problems."], ["Heart", "It blocks hERG potassium channels, prolonging the QT interval and occasionally causing torsades de pointes, especially with IV use."], ["Temperature control", "Rarely, neuroleptic malignant syndrome causes high fever, severe rigidity, confusion and unstable blood pressure."]], "journey": "Haloperidol can be given by mouth, IM, or IV (off-label). Oral bioavailability is moderate because of first-pass metabolism. It is lipophilic and highly protein bound, crossing the blood-brain barrier and placenta. The liver metabolizes it by CYP3A4 and CYP2D6, plus glucuronidation and reduction to reduced haloperidol, which can be converted back. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. The half-life is long, about 12 to 36 hours. The decanoate ester is injected into muscle and slowly releases haloperidol over about a month.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow its strong D2 blockade. Extrapyramidal symptoms come from blocking dopamine in the basal ganglia; acute dystonia is treated with diphenhydramine or benztropine, and akathisia with beta-blockers or benzodiazepines. Tardive dyskinesia reflects long-term receptor changes and may be permanent. Hyperprolactinemia comes from pituitary D2 blockade. QT prolongation is from hERG channel block. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is a medical emergency treated with stopping the drug, cooling, fluids, and sometimes dantrolene or bromocriptine. It increases death risk in older adults with dementia-related psychosis.", "fun": "Droperidol, a close butyrophenone relative of haloperidol also developed at Janssen, is used for nausea and agitation and carries a similar QT warning.", "src": ["Haldol (haloperidol) FDA label", "Granger B, Albu S. Ann Clin Psychiatry 2005 (history of haloperidol)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "heparin": {"origin": "Heparin was found by a medical student. In 1916 Jay McLean, working in the lab of physiologist William Howell at Johns Hopkins, was studying clotting substances from dog tissues and found a fat-soluble extract from liver that actually prevented clotting. Howell and his team later developed a water-soluble anticoagulant from liver and named it heparin in 1918, from the Greek 'hepar' for liver. In the 1930s Charles Best and colleagues in Toronto and Erik Jorpes in Sweden purified it so it could be safely used in people, and clinical trials began around 1935 to 1937. Today heparin is mostly extracted from pig intestinal lining. In 2008 contaminated heparin from overseas suppliers caused severe allergic reactions and deaths, leading to tighter testing standards.", "timeline": [["1916", "Jay McLean discovers an anticoagulant in liver extracts at Johns Hopkins."], ["1918", "William Howell names the substance heparin."], ["1930s", "Charles Best in Toronto and Erik Jorpes in Sweden purify heparin and begin clinical use."], ["1980s", "Low molecular weight heparins are developed from unfractionated heparin."], ["2008", "Contamination with oversulfated chondroitin sulfate causes serious reactions and a global recall."]], "cell": "Unfractionated heparin is a mixture of negatively charged sulfated polysaccharide chains of many lengths. A specific pentasaccharide sequence on some chains binds antithrombin III (a natural serine protease inhibitor) and changes its shape, speeding its action about 1,000-fold. Antithrombin then inactivates factor Xa and thrombin (factor IIa), along with factors IXa, XIa and XIIa. To inactivate thrombin, a heparin chain must be long enough (at least 18 sugars) to bind both antithrombin and thrombin and hold them together like a bridge. Most heparin chains are long enough, so unfractionated heparin inhibits thrombin and factor Xa about equally. This stops conversion of fibrinogen to fibrin and prevents new clot formation and growth of existing clots. Heparin does not break down clots that already exist.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Thrombin and factor Xa are blocked, so new clots do not form and existing clots stop growing. The aPTT and anti-Xa levels rise and are used to monitor therapy."], ["Veins and lungs", "It treats and prevents DVT and pulmonary embolism."], ["Heart", "It is used in acute coronary syndromes, during cardiac catheterization, and on bypass to stop clotting in the circuit."], ["Platelets", "In heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT), antibodies form against heparin bound to platelet factor 4, activating platelets and paradoxically causing clots."], ["Potassium", "Heparin suppresses aldosterone production, which can raise potassium."], ["Bones", "Long-term use over months can reduce bone density and raise fracture risk."]], "journey": "Heparin is a large, charged molecule that is not absorbed by mouth. It is given IV, where it works immediately, or subcutaneously, where it starts in about 20 to 30 minutes. It binds many plasma proteins, endothelial cells and macrophages, so its effect varies a lot between people and needs monitoring. It is cleared mainly by binding to endothelial cells and macrophages of the reticuloendothelial system, which break it down, and at higher levels some is excreted by the kidneys. The half-life is short, around one to one and a half hours, and becomes longer at higher doses. It does not cross the placenta, so it is used in pregnancy.", "sidefx": "Bleeding is the main risk because clotting is blocked. HIT happens when the immune system makes antibodies to heparin bound to platelet factor 4; these activate platelets, lowering platelet counts and causing dangerous venous and arterial clots, usually 5 to 10 days after starting. HIT is treated by stopping all heparin and giving a non-heparin anticoagulant like argatroban. Hyperkalemia comes from suppressed aldosterone, and osteoporosis from long-term use. Protamine sulfate reverses heparin; its positive charges bind the negatively charged heparin chains and neutralize them.", "fun": "Jay McLean was a second-year medical student when he made the discovery, and later in life he argued that he had not received enough credit for it.", "src": ["Heparin sodium injection FDA label", "McLean J. Am J Physiol 1916", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "hydralazine": {"origin": "Hydralazine was discovered by accident. In the 1940s scientists at Ciba in Switzerland were looking for drugs to treat malaria. One compound, later named hydralazine, failed against malaria but lowered blood pressure. Ciba filed a patent in 1945, the first reports of its effects appeared in 1950, and the FDA approved it as Apresoline in 1953. It was one of the first oral drugs for high blood pressure. Later, the V-HeFT trials of the 1980s showed hydralazine combined with isosorbide dinitrate helped people with heart failure. The A-HeFT trial found a large benefit in self-identified Black patients, and in 2005 the FDA approved BiDil, a fixed combination, for that population.", "timeline": [["1945", "Ciba files a patent on hydralazine, discovered during antimalarial research."], ["1953", "FDA approves hydralazine (Apresoline)."], ["1986", "V-HeFT I shows hydralazine plus isosorbide dinitrate improves survival in heart failure."], ["2005", "FDA approves BiDil (isosorbide dinitrate and hydralazine) for heart failure in self-identified Black patients."]], "cell": "Hydralazine directly relaxes the smooth muscle of arterioles, with little effect on veins. The exact mechanism is still not fully understood. It appears to interfere with IP3-triggered calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum in smooth muscle cells, reducing the calcium available for contraction. It may also open potassium channels, which hyperpolarizes the cell, and increase nitric oxide and cGMP signaling. It also has antioxidant effects that may protect nitric oxide, which is one reason it is paired with nitrates to prevent nitrate tolerance. The result is lower arteriolar resistance and afterload. The body senses the drop in pressure and triggers reflex sympathetic activation and renin release.", "body": [["Arterioles", "Arterioles widen, lowering systemic vascular resistance and blood pressure. Veins are largely unaffected, so orthostatic hypotension is less common than with some vasodilators."], ["Heart", "Reflex sympathetic activation raises heart rate and contractility, which can worsen angina in coronary disease. A beta-blocker is often added."], ["Kidneys", "Renin release increases, causing sodium and water retention, so a diuretic is often added."], ["Heart failure", "Lower afterload helps a weak heart pump more blood, especially combined with a nitrate that lowers preload."], ["Pregnancy", "It is used for severe hypertension in pregnancy, including preeclampsia, because of long experience with it."], ["Immune system", "With long-term use, especially in slow acetylators, it can trigger drug-induced lupus."]], "journey": "Hydralazine is well absorbed by mouth but undergoes large first-pass metabolism by acetylation in the gut wall and liver through N-acetyltransferase 2 (NAT2). Slow acetylators have higher blood levels and more side effects. It can also be given IV or IM, acting within minutes. It is highly protein bound. Metabolites are excreted in the urine. The plasma half-life is a few hours, but its effect lasts longer because it binds to arterial walls.", "sidefx": "Headache, flushing, palpitations and tachycardia come from vasodilation and the reflex sympathetic response. This reflex can provoke angina or heart attack in people with coronary artery disease. Fluid retention comes from activated renin and aldosterone. Drug-induced lupus, with joint pain, fever, rash and positive antihistone antibodies, is linked to long-term use and slow acetylation. Peripheral neuropathy can occur from pyridoxine interference. There is no antidote; severe hypotension is treated with fluids and positioning.", "fun": "Hydralazine, procainamide and isoniazid are the classic drugs that cause drug-induced lupus, and all three are metabolized by acetylation.", "src": ["Hydralazine hydrochloride FDA label", "Taylor AL et al. N Engl J Med 2004 (A-HeFT)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "hydrocodone": {"origin": "Hydrocodone is a semi-synthetic opioid made from codeine, which itself comes from the opium poppy, Papaver somniferum. It was first synthesized in Germany in 1920 by Carl Mannich and Helene Lowenheim, and the company Knoll marketed it as Dicodid, mainly as a cough suppressant. In the US it was approved in the 1940s and became widely used in combination with acetaminophen, as in Vicodin and Norco. For years it was among the most prescribed medications in the country, and it played a major role in the prescription opioid epidemic. In 2014 the DEA moved hydrocodone combination products from Schedule III to Schedule II. Extended-release single-ingredient forms (Zohydro ER, Hysingla ER) were approved in 2013 and 2014.", "timeline": [["1920", "Mannich and Lowenheim synthesize hydrocodone in Germany."], ["1920s", "Knoll markets hydrocodone as Dicodid."], ["1943", "Hydrocodone is approved in the US."], ["2013", "Zohydro ER, the first single-ingredient extended-release hydrocodone, is approved."], ["2014", "The DEA reschedules hydrocodone combination products to Schedule II."]], "cell": "Hydrocodone is an agonist at mu-opioid receptors, which are Gi/Go protein-coupled receptors. Binding inhibits adenylyl cyclase and lowers cAMP. It opens G protein-gated inwardly rectifying potassium (GIRK) channels, which hyperpolarizes postsynaptic neurons, and closes presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels, which reduces release of glutamate and substance P from pain-transmitting nerves. In the spinal cord dorsal horn, brainstem and thalamus, this blocks pain signals from traveling upward. It also activates descending pain-inhibiting pathways by reducing GABA tone in the periaqueductal gray. The liver converts some hydrocodone by CYP2D6 into hydromorphone, a stronger mu agonist, which adds to its effect.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Pain perception and the emotional distress of pain are reduced. Euphoria and sedation also occur, which contribute to misuse."], ["Breathing", "Mu receptors in the brainstem respiratory centers reduce the response to rising carbon dioxide, slowing breathing. This is the cause of overdose deaths."], ["Cough center", "It suppresses the cough reflex in the medulla, which is why it is used in cough syrups."], ["Gut", "Reduced gut motility and secretions cause constipation that does not improve with time."], ["Eyes", "Pupils constrict (miosis) through stimulation of the Edinger-Westphal nucleus."], ["Liver (combinations)", "Most products contain acetaminophen, which can cause liver failure if too much is taken."]], "journey": "Hydrocodone is taken by mouth and absorbed well, with pain relief starting in about 10 to 30 minutes. It is moderately bound to plasma proteins and crosses the blood-brain barrier and placenta. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP3A4 to norhydrocodone and by CYP2D6 to hydromorphone. Drugs that inhibit CYP3A4 (such as clarithromycin or ketoconazole) raise levels and overdose risk. Metabolites are excreted in the urine. The half-life is about four hours for immediate-release forms.", "sidefx": "Side effects reflect mu-opioid activation throughout the body. Respiratory depression is the deadliest, especially with benzodiazepines, alcohol, or CYP3A4 inhibitors. Constipation comes from gut mu receptors. Nausea comes from stimulation of the chemoreceptor trigger zone. Sedation and dizziness increase fall risk. Long-term use causes tolerance, dependence and addiction. Combination products add the risk of acetaminophen liver injury, which is treated with N-acetylcysteine. Opioid overdose is reversed by naloxone, a competitive mu antagonist.", "fun": "From the late 2000s through the early 2010s, hydrocodone with acetaminophen was the most dispensed prescription drug in the United States.", "src": ["Norco and Hysingla ER (hydrocodone) FDA labels", "DEA Final Rule on Rescheduling Hydrocodone Combination Products, 2014", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "hydrocortisone": {"origin": "Hydrocortisone is the body's own cortisol, made by the adrenal cortex. In the 1930s Edward Kendall at the Mayo Clinic and Tadeus Reichstein in Switzerland isolated a series of steroid hormones from adrenal glands, including the compound later called cortisol. In 1948 Philip Hench at Mayo gave the related hormone cortisone to a patient with severe rheumatoid arthritis, with dramatic improvement. Kendall, Hench and Reichstein shared the 1950 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. Making these hormones was hard until 1952, when Upjohn scientists found that the mold Rhizopus could add a key oxygen to progesterone, making steroid production practical. Hydrocortisone became the standard for adrenal replacement and for adrenal crisis.", "timeline": [["1930s", "Kendall and Reichstein isolate adrenal cortical steroids."], ["1948", "Philip Hench first uses cortisone for rheumatoid arthritis at the Mayo Clinic."], ["1950", "Kendall, Hench and Reichstein receive the Nobel Prize for adrenal hormones."], ["1952", "Upjohn scientists use fungal fermentation to simplify corticosteroid production."]], "cell": "Hydrocortisone is identical to cortisol. It crosses cell membranes and binds the glucocorticoid receptor (GR) in the cytoplasm. This releases heat shock proteins and the complex moves into the nucleus, where it binds glucocorticoid response elements to switch on genes such as annexin A1 (which inhibits phospholipase A2) and IkB (which holds NF-kB inactive). It also turns off inflammatory genes by blocking NF-kB and AP-1, reducing production of cytokines, COX-2 and adhesion molecules. It also binds the mineralocorticoid receptor with high affinity, increasing sodium channel (ENaC) and Na-K-ATPase activity in the kidney. At high levels, it overwhelms the enzyme 11-beta-HSD2 that normally protects these receptors, so sodium retention occurs. It also increases blood vessel sensitivity to catecholamines.", "body": [["Adrenal replacement", "It replaces missing cortisol in Addison disease, congenital adrenal hyperplasia and after adrenal surgery, and in adrenal crisis it restores blood pressure."], ["Blood vessels", "It restores vessel response to norepinephrine and epinephrine, which is why it helps in septic shock that does not respond to fluids and vasopressors."], ["Immune system", "Inflammatory cytokines fall, eosinophils and lymphocytes drop, and neutrophils rise in the blood. Infection risk increases."], ["Metabolism", "Gluconeogenesis increases and insulin sensitivity falls, raising blood glucose. Protein breakdown increases."], ["Kidneys", "Mineralocorticoid action retains sodium and water and loses potassium."], ["Brain", "Mood changes, insomnia and, rarely, psychosis can occur."]], "journey": "Hydrocortisone is available by mouth (well absorbed), as an IV or IM sodium succinate injection, and topically. IV hydrocortisone acts within about an hour. In blood it binds corticosteroid-binding globulin and albumin. The liver metabolizes it, partly by CYP3A4, into inactive metabolites conjugated and excreted in the urine. The plasma half-life is short, about 1.5 to 2 hours, but because it works through gene changes, its biological effect lasts about 8 to 12 hours.", "sidefx": "Side effects reflect excess cortisol action. Hyperglycemia comes from increased glucose production and insulin resistance. Fluid retention and hypokalemia come from mineralocorticoid effects. Immune suppression raises infection risk and masks fever. Mood changes and psychosis reflect brain effects. Long-term use causes Cushing features, bone loss, muscle wasting, cataracts and adrenal suppression, because the pituitary stops making ACTH. Stopping suddenly after long use can cause adrenal crisis, so it is tapered. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Hench's first cortisone patient in 1948, a woman with severe rheumatoid arthritis who could barely walk, was moving with ease within days, a result that stunned the medical world.", "src": ["Solu-Cortef (hydrocortisone sodium succinate) FDA label", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1950", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "hydromorphone": {"origin": "Hydromorphone is a semi-synthetic opioid made by modifying morphine, the main alkaloid of the opium poppy. It was developed in Germany in the early 1920s, and the company Knoll introduced it in 1926 under the name Dilaudid. Hydromorphone is several times more potent than morphine, and it became an important drug for severe pain, especially in hospitals and in patients with kidney problems where morphine's metabolites can build up. An extended-release form, Exalgo, was later approved for opioid-tolerant patients.", "timeline": [["1804", "Friedrich Serturner isolates morphine from opium."], ["1926", "Knoll introduces hydromorphone as Dilaudid."], ["2010", "Exalgo, an extended-release hydromorphone, is approved in the US."]], "cell": "Hydromorphone is a full agonist at mu-opioid receptors with weaker activity at delta receptors. Mu receptors are Gi/Go-coupled. Activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase, opens GIRK potassium channels that hyperpolarize neurons, and closes presynaptic N-type calcium channels, which reduces release of pain transmitters like glutamate and substance P. In the spinal cord dorsal horn, this blocks ascending pain transmission. In the periaqueductal gray and rostral ventromedial medulla, it reduces GABA inhibition of descending pain-control neurons, activating them. Its structure, a ketone at position 6 and a saturated 7,8 bond, makes it more lipophilic and more potent than morphine.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Strong relief of severe pain, along with sedation and euphoria."], ["Breathing", "Brainstem respiratory centers become less sensitive to carbon dioxide, slowing breathing. This is the main danger."], ["Gut", "Slowed motility causes constipation, and nausea and vomiting come from stimulating the chemoreceptor trigger zone."], ["Eyes", "Pupils constrict to pinpoints in overdose."], ["Skin", "Itching occurs, but hydromorphone releases less histamine than morphine."], ["Heart and vessels", "Hypotension and bradycardia can occur, mainly with IV use."]], "journey": "Hydromorphone can be given IV, IM, subcutaneously, by mouth, or rectally. Oral bioavailability is low because of extensive first-pass metabolism, so oral doses are much larger than IV doses. IV hydromorphone works within about five minutes. It crosses the blood-brain barrier and placenta. The liver converts it mainly by glucuronidation to hydromorphone-3-glucuronide (H3G), which has no pain-relieving effect but can excite neurons. H3G is excreted by the kidneys and can build up in kidney failure. The half-life is about two to three hours.", "sidefx": "Respiratory depression, sedation, nausea, constipation and itching come from mu receptor activation in the brain, brainstem and gut. Because it is so potent, confusing hydromorphone with morphine has led to fatal overdoses. Accumulation of H3G in kidney failure can cause myoclonus, agitation and seizures. Long-term use leads to tolerance, dependence and risk of addiction. Naloxone, a competitive mu antagonist, reverses overdose; repeat doses may be needed.", "fun": "Hydromorphone and morphine have been involved in many medication errors because of similar names, and they are on the Institute for Safe Medication Practices high-alert list.", "src": ["Dilaudid (hydromorphone) FDA label", "Exalgo FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "hydroxocobalamin": {"origin": "Hydroxocobalamin is a natural form of vitamin B12. The B12 story began in 1926, when George Minot and William Murphy showed that eating large amounts of liver cured pernicious anemia, earning a Nobel Prize in 1934 with George Whipple. In 1948 teams led by Karl Folkers at Merck and E. Lester Smith at Glaxo isolated the active factor, cyanocobalamin. Dorothy Hodgkin solved its complex structure by X-ray crystallography, published in the mid-1950s, part of the work that earned her the 1964 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. Researchers found that hydroxocobalamin, which has a hydroxyl group on its cobalt, could grab cyanide and become harmless cyanocobalamin. It was used for cyanide poisoning in France, and the FDA approved it as Cyanokit in 2006.", "timeline": [["1926", "Minot and Murphy show that liver cures pernicious anemia."], ["1948", "Vitamin B12 is isolated by Merck and Glaxo researchers."], ["1956", "Dorothy Hodgkin publishes the structure of vitamin B12."], ["2006", "FDA approves hydroxocobalamin (Cyanokit) for cyanide poisoning."]], "cell": "Cyanide binds the ferric iron in cytochrome c oxidase (complex IV) of the mitochondrial electron transport chain. This blocks oxygen use, so cells switch to anaerobic metabolism and produce lactic acid even when oxygen is present. Hydroxocobalamin has a cobalt atom at its center with a hydroxyl group. Cyanide binds cobalt with higher affinity than it binds cytochrome oxidase, so it replaces the hydroxyl group to form cyanocobalamin. This pulls cyanide out of the blood and tissues and frees cytochrome oxidase to restart aerobic respiration. Cyanocobalamin is stable and is excreted in the urine. Hydroxocobalamin also binds nitric oxide, which narrows blood vessels and raises blood pressure.", "body": [["Cells and mitochondria", "Oxidative phosphorylation resumes, ATP production recovers and lactic acid falls."], ["Brain", "Restored oxygen use can improve seizures, confusion and coma from cyanide poisoning."], ["Heart and vessels", "Blood pressure rises because nitric oxide is bound, which helps in cyanide-induced shock. It can also cause hypertension."], ["Skin and urine", "Its deep red color tints skin, mucous membranes and urine red for days to weeks."], ["Lab tests", "The red color interferes with colorimetric blood tests and can falsely trigger hemodialysis machine blood leak alarms."], ["Kidneys", "Oxalate crystals in the kidney and acute kidney injury have been reported after treatment."]], "journey": "Hydroxocobalamin for cyanide poisoning is given IV. It binds cyanide within minutes in the blood and tissues. It also binds plasma proteins. It is not significantly metabolized. Both hydroxocobalamin and the cyanocobalamin it forms are excreted mainly by the kidneys, which is why urine turns red. The half-life is about one day. Color changes in skin can last up to two weeks and in urine up to five weeks.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from its color and its nitric oxide binding. Red skin and urine are expected and harmless. Hypertension comes from nitric oxide scavenging. Headache, nausea and injection site reactions are common. The red color interferes with lab tests for bilirubin, creatinine, liver enzymes and others, so blood should be drawn before giving it when possible. Rare allergic reactions occur. Oxalate kidney injury has been reported. It is considered safer than the older nitrite and thiosulfate kit because it does not cause methemoglobinemia or worsen hypotension.", "fun": "Because it does not form methemoglobin, hydroxocobalamin is preferred for smoke inhalation victims who may have both cyanide and carbon monoxide poisoning.", "src": ["Cyanokit (hydroxocobalamin) FDA label", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1934 and Chemistry 1964"]}, "hydroxychloroquine": {"origin": "Hydroxychloroquine's family tree starts with quinine, from the bark of the South American cinchona tree, used against malaria since the 1600s. In 1934 Hans Andersag and colleagues at Bayer created chloroquine, which became a key antimalarial after World War II. Researchers at Sterling Drug, with Alexander Surrey listed as inventor, made hydroxychloroquine by adding a hydroxyl group to chloroquine, disclosing it in a 1949 patent. It was less toxic than chloroquine, and the US approved it in 1955 as Plaquenil. Doctors noticed that antimalarials helped lupus and rheumatoid arthritis, and hydroxychloroquine became a cornerstone of lupus treatment. In 2020 it received a brief emergency use authorization for COVID-19 that was revoked after trials showed no benefit.", "timeline": [["1630s", "Cinchona bark is used in Europe to treat malaria."], ["1934", "Hans Andersag at Bayer develops chloroquine."], ["1949", "Sterling Drug patents hydroxychloroquine."], ["1955", "Hydroxychloroquine (Plaquenil) is approved in the US."], ["2020", "FDA grants and then revokes an emergency use authorization for COVID-19."]], "cell": "Hydroxychloroquine is a weak base. It enters cells and becomes trapped in acidic compartments like lysosomes and endosomes, where it becomes charged and raises the pH. Higher pH impairs lysosomal enzymes and autophagy and interferes with processing of antigens and loading onto MHC class II molecules, so fewer self-antigens are presented to T cells. It also blocks endosomal Toll-like receptors TLR7 and TLR9, which detect nucleic acids and drive interferon production in lupus. This reduces production of type I interferon, IL-1, IL-6 and TNF. In malaria, it accumulates in the parasite's acidic food vacuole and blocks the polymerization of toxic heme into hemozoin, so free heme kills the parasite.", "body": [["Immune system", "Autoimmune activity and interferon signaling fall, reducing lupus flares, skin disease and joint inflammation. It also lowers clot risk in lupus."], ["Joints", "In rheumatoid arthritis it gives modest relief, often in combination with methotrexate and sulfasalazine."], ["Eyes", "It binds melanin in the retinal pigment epithelium and with long-term use can cause irreversible bull's eye maculopathy, so yearly eye exams are needed after five years."], ["Heart", "It can prolong the QT interval and rarely causes cardiomyopathy with long-term use."], ["Blood glucose", "It can lower blood sugar, sometimes causing hypoglycemia, especially with diabetes medications."], ["Skin", "Rash, itching and bluish-gray hyperpigmentation can occur."]], "journey": "Hydroxychloroquine is taken by mouth and absorbed well. It has an enormous volume of distribution because it binds tissues, especially those rich in melanin like the eye and skin, as well as the liver, spleen and kidneys. The liver partly metabolizes it by CYP2C8, CYP3A4 and CYP2D6 to desethyl metabolites. About half is excreted in the urine. Its half-life is very long, about 40 days, so it takes weeks to reach steady state and months to see full benefit in autoimmune disease.", "sidefx": "Retinal toxicity comes from long-term accumulation in the retina. QT prolongation and torsades come from cardiac potassium channel block, and cardiomyopathy may relate to lysosome dysfunction in heart cells. Hypoglycemia reflects reduced insulin breakdown and increased insulin sensitivity. Gut upset is common early. It can cause hemolysis in G6PD deficiency and worsen psoriasis and porphyria. Overdose is dangerous, causing seizures, hypokalemia and cardiovascular collapse; treatment includes diazepam, epinephrine and potassium repletion with careful monitoring.", "fun": "Bone and tissue binding means hydroxychloroquine can still be detected in the body for months after the last dose.", "src": ["Plaquenil (hydroxychloroquine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "hydroxyurea": {"origin": "Hydroxyurea is one of the oldest drugs in use: it was first synthesized in Germany in 1869 by W. F. C. Dresler and R. Stein. For decades it was only a chemical curiosity. In the 1920s it was noticed to affect blood cells in animals, and in the 1960s it was tested and approved as an anticancer drug, reaching the US market in 1967 as Hydrea. In the 1980s researchers found that it raised fetal hemoglobin in sickle cell disease. The Multicenter Study of Hydroxyurea in Sickle Cell Anemia, stopped early in 1995 because of clear benefit, showed fewer pain crises. The FDA approved it for sickle cell anemia as Droxia in 1998, and Siklos was approved for children in 2017.", "timeline": [["1869", "Hydroxyurea is first synthesized in Germany."], ["1967", "FDA approves hydroxyurea (Hydrea) for cancer."], ["1995", "The Multicenter Study of Hydroxyurea shows fewer sickle cell crises."], ["1998", "FDA approves Droxia for sickle cell anemia in adults."], ["2017", "Siklos is approved for children with sickle cell anemia."]], "cell": "Hydroxyurea inhibits ribonucleotide reductase, the enzyme that converts ribonucleotides into deoxyribonucleotides, the building blocks of DNA. It does this by destroying a tyrosyl free radical in the R2 subunit that the enzyme needs for catalysis. With fewer deoxyribonucleotides, DNA synthesis stops, so cells in S phase die or arrest. This hits rapidly dividing bone marrow cells and malignant myeloid cells. In sickle cell disease, it increases fetal hemoglobin (HbF), partly by stressing red cell production and through nitric oxide that activates soluble guanylyl cyclase and the gamma-globin gene. HbF does not polymerize with sickle hemoglobin, so red cells sickle less. It also lowers neutrophil and reticulocyte counts and reduces adhesion of cells to blood vessel walls.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Production of white cells, platelets and red cells slows. This lowers high counts in myeloproliferative disorders and can cause neutropenia and thrombocytopenia."], ["Red blood cells", "Fetal hemoglobin rises and red cells become larger (macrocytosis). In sickle cell disease, cells sickle less and pain crises, acute chest syndrome and transfusions decrease."], ["Blood vessels", "Fewer neutrophils and less adhesion reduce vaso-occlusion."], ["Skin and nails", "Hyperpigmentation, nail darkening, skin atrophy and painful leg ulcers can occur. Long-term use raises skin cancer risk."], ["Cancer", "It reduces cell counts in chronic myeloid leukemia, polycythemia vera and essential thrombocythemia, and sensitizes some tumors to radiation."]], "journey": "Hydroxyurea is taken by mouth and absorbed well, peaking in one to four hours. It spreads throughout body water, crosses into the brain and the placenta, and enters breast milk. Part of it is metabolized in the liver and part by gut bacteria. About half leaves unchanged in the urine, so doses are adjusted for kidney function. The half-life is two to four hours. Blood counts fall within days, while fetal hemoglobin rises over months.", "sidefx": "Bone marrow suppression comes directly from blocking DNA synthesis in dividing marrow cells, so blood counts are checked regularly. Macrocytosis is expected and is used to check that patients are taking it. Skin and nail changes and leg ulcers reflect effects on dividing skin cells. It can cause birth defects and is avoided in pregnancy. Long-term use may increase risk of secondary leukemia and skin cancer. With didanosine and stavudine in HIV, fatal pancreatitis and liver toxicity have occurred. There is no antidote; the drug is held until counts recover.", "fun": "Hydroxyurea was made in 1869, the same decade as the periodic table, but took nearly a century to become a medicine.", "src": ["Hydrea and Droxia (hydroxyurea) FDA labels", "Charache S et al. N Engl J Med 1995", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "hydroxyzine": {"origin": "Hydroxyzine was first synthesized by the Belgian company Union Chimique Belge (UCB) in 1956, part of a family of piperazine antihistamines. Pfizer received approval to sell it in the US the same year, as Atarax (hydrochloride) and later Vistaril (pamoate). It was promoted not just for allergies but as a tranquilizer for anxiety, at a time when drugs like meprobamate were popular. Its sedating and anti-itch effects kept it in wide use. Decades later, UCB chemists found that hydroxyzine's main metabolite, cetirizine, kept the antihistamine effect with much less sedation, and cetirizine (Zyrtec) became a major second-generation antihistamine.", "timeline": [["1956", "UCB synthesizes hydroxyzine, and Pfizer gains US approval the same year."], ["1987", "Cetirizine, the active metabolite of hydroxyzine, is introduced in Europe."], ["2015", "European regulators restrict hydroxyzine use because of QT prolongation risk."]], "cell": "Hydroxyzine is a first-generation antihistamine. It binds the histamine H1 receptor and stabilizes it in its inactive state (inverse agonism), so histamine cannot activate the Gq, phospholipase C and calcium pathway. Because it is lipophilic and not strongly pushed out by P-glycoprotein, it crosses the blood-brain barrier easily. In the brain, H1 receptors from tuberomammillary nucleus neurons help keep people awake, so blocking them causes sedation and reduces anxiety. It also has some antagonist effects at muscarinic, serotonin 5-HT2A and dopamine D2 receptors, although these are weaker than its H1 effect. At high levels it blocks hERG potassium channels in the heart.", "body": [["Skin", "Itching from hives, eczema and other allergic skin conditions is reduced, and it is a favored antipruritic."], ["Brain", "It causes sedation and reduces anxiety, which is used for short-term anxiety and before procedures. Confusion is more likely in older adults."], ["Anticholinergic effects", "Dry mouth, constipation and urinary retention can occur."], ["Heart", "It can prolong the QT interval and rarely cause torsades, especially with other QT-prolonging drugs or electrolyte problems."], ["Nausea", "Central antihistamine effects help reduce nausea and motion sickness."]], "journey": "Hydroxyzine is taken by mouth or given IM. It is absorbed quickly, with effects in 15 to 30 minutes. It spreads widely, including into the brain. The liver oxidizes it, partly through CYP3A4, into cetirizine, which is an active H1 blocker and contributes to its long antihistamine effect. Metabolites are excreted in the urine. The half-life is about 20 hours in adults and longer in older adults and people with liver disease. IV use is avoided because it can damage tissue.", "sidefx": "Sedation comes from blocking H1 receptors in the brain that promote wakefulness, and it is worsened by alcohol and other sedatives. Anticholinergic effects such as dry mouth and urinary retention come from muscarinic receptor blockade. QT prolongation comes from hERG channel block. Older adults are more prone to confusion and falls, so it is on the Beers list of drugs to avoid. In overdose, sedation, anticholinergic toxicity and seizures can occur. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Cetirizine is literally what the body makes from hydroxyzine, so the two drugs share an antihistamine effect but differ in how much reaches the brain.", "src": ["Vistaril and hydroxyzine hydrochloride FDA labels", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "American Geriatrics Society Beers Criteria"]}, "idarucizumab": {"origin": "Idarucizumab was designed by Boehringer Ingelheim, the company that makes the oral anticoagulant dabigatran (Pradaxa), approved in the US in 2010. When newer oral anticoagulants arrived, a major concern was that there was no specific antidote for life-threatening bleeding or emergency surgery, unlike warfarin. Boehringer researchers developed a humanized monoclonal antibody fragment (Fab) that binds dabigatran tightly. The RE-VERSE AD study showed it rapidly reversed dabigatran's effect in bleeding patients and those needing urgent procedures. In October 2015 the FDA granted accelerated approval to idarucizumab as Praxbind, the first specific reversal agent for a direct oral anticoagulant.", "timeline": [["2010", "FDA approves dabigatran (Pradaxa)."], ["2015", "FDA approves idarucizumab (Praxbind), the first specific reversal agent for a direct oral anticoagulant."], ["2017", "Full results of the RE-VERSE AD study are published."]], "cell": "Dabigatran is a direct thrombin inhibitor that binds the active site of thrombin (factor IIa). Idarucizumab is a humanized antibody fragment (Fab) that binds dabigatran and its active glucuronide metabolites with very high affinity, roughly 300 times stronger than dabigatran binds thrombin. It binds both free dabigatran and dabigatran attached to thrombin, pulling it away. Once bound, the complex cannot inhibit thrombin, so thrombin is freed to convert fibrinogen to fibrin and clotting resumes. Idarucizumab does not bind thrombin or other clotting factors and does not affect other anticoagulants such as rivaroxaban, apixaban or heparin.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Clotting tests such as the dilute thrombin time and ecarin clotting time return to normal within minutes in most patients."], ["Bleeding", "It helps stop major bleeding in the brain, gut and other sites when combined with standard supportive care."], ["Surgery", "It allows emergency surgery or procedures in patients taking dabigatran."], ["Clotting risk", "Removing anticoagulation exposes patients to their underlying clot risk, so anticoagulation is restarted as soon as safely possible."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys clear the drug, and patients with poor kidney function may have more dabigatran to bind."]], "journey": "Idarucizumab is given IV and works immediately. It stays mostly in the bloodstream and binds dabigatran there; dabigatran then moves from tissues into the blood and is also bound. The kidneys filter the drug and its complex, and protein is broken down in the kidney tubules. The initial half-life is short, about 47 minutes, with a terminal half-life of about 10 hours. Rarely, clotting tests rise again after 12 to 24 hours as more dabigatran moves out of tissues.", "sidefx": "Idarucizumab is generally well tolerated. The main risk is thrombosis, because reversing dabigatran removes protection against clots in patients who are on anticoagulation for a reason. Headache is the most common reported side effect. Allergic reactions are possible. Some formulations contain sorbitol, which can be dangerous in people with hereditary fructose intolerance. If bleeding returns with a rebound in dabigatran levels, a second treatment may be considered.", "fun": "Idarucizumab is part of a small group of 'antidote antibodies', alongside digoxin immune Fab (DigiFab) and crotalidae antivenom Fab (CroFab).", "src": ["Praxbind (idarucizumab) FDA label", "Pollack CV et al. N Engl J Med 2017 (RE-VERSE AD)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "imatinib": {"origin": "Imatinib is the model of targeted cancer therapy. In 1960 Peter Nowell and David Hungerford in Philadelphia found an abnormally small chromosome in cells from patients with chronic myeloid leukemia (CML), later called the Philadelphia chromosome. In 1973 Janet Rowley showed it came from a swap between chromosomes 9 and 22, and in the 1980s researchers showed this created the BCR-ABL fusion gene, which makes an always-on tyrosine kinase. In the 1990s chemists at Ciba-Geigy (later Novartis), including Nicholas Lydon and Jurg Zimmermann, designed molecules to block this kinase. Oncologist Brian Druker at Oregon Health and Science University led the clinical trials, which showed dramatic responses. The FDA approved imatinib as Gleevec in May 2001 after an unusually fast review.", "timeline": [["1960", "Nowell and Hungerford describe the Philadelphia chromosome in CML."], ["1973", "Janet Rowley identifies the 9;22 translocation."], ["1990s", "Ciba-Geigy and Novartis scientists develop imatinib (STI571)."], ["2001", "FDA approves imatinib (Gleevec) for CML."], ["2002", "Imatinib is approved for gastrointestinal stromal tumors (GIST)."]], "cell": "The BCR-ABL fusion protein is a tyrosine kinase that is permanently switched on. It phosphorylates many signaling proteins, driving the RAS-MAPK, PI3K-AKT and JAK-STAT pathways, which push myeloid cells to divide and resist apoptosis. Imatinib binds the ATP-binding pocket of the ABL kinase domain when the kinase is in its inactive conformation and locks it there. Without ATP, the kinase cannot phosphorylate its targets, so the growth signals stop and BCR-ABL-driven cells die by apoptosis or stop growing. Imatinib also blocks the related kinases c-KIT (CD117), which drives most GISTs, and PDGFR-alpha and beta. Resistance usually comes from point mutations in the ABL kinase domain, such as T315I, that block imatinib binding.", "body": [["Blood and bone marrow", "Leukemic cell counts fall within weeks, and most patients reach normal blood counts and loss of the Philadelphia chromosome. Normal blood cells can also drop, causing neutropenia and thrombocytopenia."], ["Gut tumors", "In GIST, blocking c-KIT shrinks tumors and reduces recurrence after surgery."], ["Fluid balance", "PDGFR blockade affects fluid handling, causing periorbital edema, ankle swelling and sometimes pleural effusions."], ["Muscle and bone", "Muscle cramps and bone or joint pain are common, especially in the first months of treatment."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise and rare serious liver injury occurs, so liver tests are monitored."], ["Heart", "Rarely, heart failure or worsening of existing heart disease can occur."]], "journey": "Imatinib is taken by mouth with food and is absorbed almost completely, with high bioavailability. It is highly protein bound. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP3A4 into an active N-desmethyl metabolite. CYP3A4 inhibitors like ketoconazole raise levels, while inducers like rifampin and St. John's wort lower them. Most of the drug leaves in the feces, with a smaller amount in the urine. The half-life is about 18 hours, and the active metabolite's is about 40 hours, allowing once-daily use.", "sidefx": "Fluid retention and edema relate to PDGFR blockade affecting interstitial fluid pressure. Nausea comes from local gut irritation, which taking it with food reduces. Muscle cramps and low phosphate may relate to effects on bone and muscle cells. Neutropenia and thrombocytopenia come from blocking signaling in normal blood cell precursors, especially early in treatment when leukemic cells die off. Liver toxicity and rare heart failure occur. It is avoided in pregnancy because it harms the fetus. There is no antidote; doses are held or reduced.", "fun": "Time magazine put imatinib on its cover in May 2001 with the headline describing it as ammunition in the war on cancer, and it turned CML from a fatal disease into a manageable chronic condition for most patients.", "src": ["Gleevec (imatinib) FDA label", "Druker BJ et al. N Engl J Med 2001", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "imipenem-cilastatin": {"origin": "Imipenem came from thienamycin, a natural antibiotic discovered by Merck scientists in 1976 from the soil bacterium Streptomyces cattleya. Thienamycin killed an extraordinary range of bacteria but was chemically unstable and broke down in solution. Merck chemists made a more stable derivative, N-formimidoyl thienamycin, called imipenem, the first carbapenem antibiotic. A new problem then appeared: an enzyme in the kidney tubules, dehydropeptidase I, destroyed imipenem, producing low urine levels and toxic breakdown products. Merck solved this by pairing it with cilastatin, a drug that blocks that enzyme. The FDA approved the combination as Primaxin in 1985. A version adding the beta-lactamase inhibitor relebactam (Recarbrio) was approved in 2019.", "timeline": [["1976", "Merck scientists discover thienamycin from Streptomyces cattleya."], ["1985", "FDA approves imipenem-cilastatin (Primaxin), the first carbapenem."], ["2019", "Imipenem-cilastatin-relebactam (Recarbrio) is approved."]], "cell": "Imipenem is a beta-lactam. It crosses the outer membrane of gram-negative bacteria through porins, especially OprD in Pseudomonas, and binds penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), especially PBP2 and PBP1. PBPs are transpeptidases that cross-link peptidoglycan strands in the cell wall. Imipenem's beta-lactam ring acylates the PBP active site and inactivates it, so new cell wall cannot be properly cross-linked. Autolysins continue to break down the wall, and the bacterium bursts from internal pressure. Its structure resists most beta-lactamases, including ESBLs and AmpC enzymes, but carbapenemases such as KPC, NDM and OXA-48 can destroy it. Cilastatin has no antibacterial activity; it blocks renal dehydropeptidase I in the proximal tubule brush border, preventing breakdown of imipenem in the kidney.", "body": [["Infections", "It kills a very broad range of bacteria, including gram-positive cocci, many gram-negative rods (including Pseudomonas and ESBL producers) and anaerobes. It is reserved for serious or resistant infections."], ["Brain", "Imipenem binds GABA-A receptors and can lower the seizure threshold, especially with high levels, kidney impairment or CNS disease."], ["Kidneys", "Cilastatin protects the kidney tubules from imipenem metabolites and keeps urine levels high. The kidneys clear both drugs, so doses are adjusted for kidney function."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are common, and it can cause C. difficile infection by disrupting normal gut flora."], ["Immune system", "Allergic reactions can occur, and cross-reactivity with penicillin allergy is low but possible."]], "journey": "Imipenem-cilastatin is given IV because it is not absorbed by mouth. Both drugs distribute widely into tissues and body fluids, including lung, bone and peritoneal fluid, and into the spinal fluid when the meninges are inflamed. Without cilastatin, imipenem would be broken down in the kidney tubules. With cilastatin, most imipenem leaves unchanged in the urine. Both have short half-lives of about one hour, so it is given several times a day.", "sidefx": "Seizures are the signature toxicity, linked to imipenem binding GABA-A receptors and blocking inhibitory signaling; risk rises with kidney impairment, high exposure and brain lesions. Imipenem also sharply lowers valproic acid levels, which can cause breakthrough seizures. Nausea and vomiting are often related to infusion speed. C. difficile colitis comes from broad killing of gut bacteria. Allergic reactions follow the beta-lactam pattern. There is no antidote; it is removed by hemodialysis.", "fun": "Meropenem, a later carbapenem, resists renal dehydropeptidase on its own, so it needs no cilastatin partner and carries a lower seizure risk.", "src": ["Primaxin (imipenem and cilastatin) FDA label", "Kahan JS et al. J Antibiot 1979 (thienamycin)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "infliximab": {"origin": "Infliximab came from the discovery that a single cytokine, tumor necrosis factor alpha (TNF-alpha), drives much of the damage in rheumatoid arthritis and Crohn disease. Jan Vilcek and Junming Le at New York University developed a mouse antibody against TNF in the late 1980s, which was made into a chimeric antibody (cA2) with the company Centocor. In the early 1990s Marc Feldmann and Ravinder Maini in London showed that blocking TNF dramatically improved rheumatoid arthritis, and trials also showed benefit in Crohn disease. The FDA approved infliximab as Remicade in 1998 for Crohn disease, followed by rheumatoid arthritis in 1999 and many other diseases. The first US biosimilar version, Inflectra, was approved in 2016.", "timeline": [["1975", "TNF is identified as a tumor-killing factor in serum."], ["1980s", "Vilcek and Le at NYU develop the anti-TNF antibody later called infliximab."], ["1998", "FDA approves infliximab (Remicade) for Crohn disease."], ["1999", "Approval is added for rheumatoid arthritis."], ["2003", "Feldmann and Maini receive the Lasker Award for anti-TNF therapy."], ["2016", "Inflectra becomes the first US-approved infliximab biosimilar."]], "cell": "TNF-alpha is made by macrophages, T cells and other immune cells as a membrane protein that can be cleaved into a soluble form. It binds TNF receptors 1 and 2, activating NF-kB and MAP kinase pathways that increase inflammatory cytokines (IL-1, IL-6), adhesion molecules on blood vessels, and enzymes that damage cartilage and gut lining. Infliximab is a chimeric IgG1 monoclonal antibody, with a mouse variable region and a human constant region. It binds both soluble and membrane-bound TNF-alpha with high affinity, preventing it from reaching its receptors. By binding membrane TNF, it can also cause complement-mediated or antibody-dependent killing of TNF-producing cells and trigger apoptosis of activated T cells in the gut. Granulomas depend on TNF, which is why blocking it can reactivate tuberculosis.", "body": [["Gut", "In Crohn disease and ulcerative colitis, inflammation falls, the gut lining heals and fistulas can close."], ["Joints", "In rheumatoid arthritis, psoriatic arthritis and ankylosing spondylitis, joint swelling, pain and erosion slow or stop."], ["Skin", "Psoriasis plaques clear. Paradoxically, some patients develop new psoriasis."], ["Immune system", "Defense against intracellular pathogens is weakened, increasing risk of serious bacterial infections, TB reactivation, fungal infections and hepatitis B reactivation."], ["Heart", "It can worsen moderate to severe heart failure."], ["Blood and cancer", "Rare lymphomas, including hepatosplenic T-cell lymphoma, have been reported, especially in young men also taking thiopurines."]], "journey": "Infliximab is a large protein given by IV infusion, usually as loading doses followed by maintenance infusions every several weeks. A subcutaneous form (Zymfentra) is also available for maintenance. It stays mainly in the bloodstream and tissues and does not cross the blood-brain barrier well. It is broken down by protein catabolism in the reticuloendothelial system, not by the liver or kidneys. Its half-life is about 8 to 10 days. Antibodies against infliximab can form and speed its clearance, which causes loss of response; methotrexate or azathioprine help prevent this.", "sidefx": "Infection risk comes from blocking TNF, which is essential for macrophage activation and granuloma formation. Latent TB must be tested for and treated before starting, and hepatitis B must be screened for. Infusion reactions such as fever, chills, rash and shortness of breath are common, partly because the mouse portion of the antibody can be seen as foreign. Delayed serum sickness-like reactions, lupus-like syndrome and liver injury can occur. It can worsen heart failure and, rarely, demyelinating disease. There is no antidote; infusion reactions are treated with slowing, antihistamines, steroids and epinephrine when severe.", "fun": "The '-ximab' ending signals a chimeric antibody (part mouse, part human), unlike '-umab' (fully human) and '-zumab' (humanized).", "src": ["Remicade (infliximab) FDA label", "Lasker Award 2003 (Feldmann and Maini)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "insulin-aspart": {"origin": "Insulin was discovered in 1921 and 1922 by Frederick Banting, Charles Best, James Collip and John Macleod in Toronto. For decades, injected insulin acted too slowly to match a meal, because insulin forms clusters of six molecules (hexamers) that must break apart before absorption. Novo Nordisk in Denmark used recombinant DNA technology to make insulin aspart, in which one amino acid at position B28 of the B chain is changed from proline to aspartic acid. This extra negative charge makes the molecules repel each other so they break into monomers faster. The FDA approved it as NovoLog in 2000. A faster version, Fiasp, adding niacinamide to speed absorption further, was approved in the US in 2017.", "timeline": [["1922", "Banting, Best, Collip and Macleod give the first successful insulin injection in Toronto."], ["1982", "Recombinant human insulin (Humulin) is approved."], ["2000", "FDA approves insulin aspart (NovoLog)."], ["2017", "Fiasp, faster-acting insulin aspart with niacinamide, is approved in the US."]], "cell": "Insulin aspart binds the insulin receptor, a receptor tyrosine kinase on liver, muscle and fat cells. Binding causes the receptor beta subunits to phosphorylate each other and then insulin receptor substrate (IRS) proteins. This activates the PI3K-AKT pathway, which moves GLUT4 glucose transporters to the surface of muscle and fat cells, turns on glycogen synthase, and switches off gluconeogenesis in the liver. It also activates the MAPK pathway for growth effects. In addition, insulin activates the Na-K-ATPase, which moves potassium into cells. The change at B28 does not change receptor binding much; it only weakens self-association so the insulin is absorbed faster from under the skin.", "body": [["Blood glucose", "It lowers the spike in blood sugar after meals, starting about 10 to 20 minutes after injection and peaking in one to three hours."], ["Liver", "Glucose release from the liver is suppressed and glycogen storage increases."], ["Muscle and fat", "Glucose uptake through GLUT4 increases, and fat is stored rather than broken down, which suppresses ketone formation."], ["Potassium", "Potassium moves into cells, which can cause hypokalemia, especially with IV use."], ["Skin", "Repeated injections at one spot can cause lipohypertrophy, which changes absorption."]], "journey": "Insulin aspart is injected subcutaneously or used in insulin pumps, and it can be given IV under close monitoring. Because its hexamers break apart quickly, it is absorbed faster than regular human insulin, so it is taken just before or at the start of a meal. Its effects last about three to five hours, shorter than regular insulin, so there is less risk of late low blood sugar. Like all insulins, it is broken down mainly by the liver and kidneys through insulin-degrading enzymes, and kidney failure prolongs its action.", "sidefx": "Hypoglycemia is the main risk and happens when insulin effect exceeds carbohydrate intake or the body's needs. Symptoms include sweating, shaking and fast heart rate from adrenaline release, then confusion and seizures as the brain runs out of glucose. Mild cases are treated with oral glucose, and severe cases with IV dextrose or glucagon. Hypokalemia comes from potassium shifting into cells. Weight gain reflects insulin's storage effects. Injection site reactions and lipohypertrophy can occur.", "fun": "The 'aspart' name comes from the aspartic acid that replaces proline at position B28.", "src": ["NovoLog (insulin aspart) FDA label", "Fiasp FDA label", "Banting FG, Best CH. J Lab Clin Med 1922"]}, "insulin-degludec": {"origin": "Insulin degludec was designed by Novo Nordisk to be an ultra-long-acting basal insulin with a very flat profile. Its design removes the threonine at position B30 and attaches a 16-carbon fatty diacid (hexadecanedioic acid) to lysine B29 through a glutamic acid linker. In the vial, phenol and zinc keep it as dihexamers. After injection, the phenol diffuses away and the dihexamers link into long multi-hexamer chains under the skin, which slowly release single insulin molecules. Degludec was approved in Japan in 2012 and Europe in 2013, but the FDA first asked for more cardiovascular safety data. After the DEVOTE trial program, the FDA approved it as Tresiba in 2015. It is also combined with liraglutide as Xultophy.", "timeline": [["2012", "Insulin degludec is approved in Japan."], ["2013", "Degludec is approved in Europe, while the FDA requests more cardiovascular safety data."], ["2015", "FDA approves insulin degludec (Tresiba)."], ["2017", "The DEVOTE trial shows cardiovascular safety and less severe hypoglycemia than glargine."]], "cell": "Degludec binds the insulin receptor like human insulin and activates the same pathways: receptor autophosphorylation, IRS proteins, PI3K-AKT signaling, GLUT4 movement to the cell surface in muscle and fat, glycogen synthesis, and suppression of liver glucose production. What makes degludec different is its physical behavior. Under the skin it forms multi-hexamer chains that slowly release monomers from the ends. In the blood, its fatty acid side chain also binds albumin, which creates a second reservoir. These two effects together give a smooth, flat action that lasts more than 42 hours.", "body": [["Blood glucose", "It provides steady basal insulin that controls fasting and between-meal glucose without a pronounced peak."], ["Liver", "Overnight and fasting glucose production is suppressed."], ["Fat tissue", "Fat breakdown is suppressed, which helps prevent ketoacidosis between meals."], ["Potassium", "Potassium shifts into cells, though less abruptly than with rapid-acting insulin."], ["Hypoglycemia pattern", "The flat profile and day-to-day consistency are linked to fewer nighttime low blood sugars than with some other basal insulins."]], "journey": "Degludec is injected subcutaneously once daily. It forms a depot of multi-hexamers that release monomers slowly and evenly. Over 99 percent of circulating degludec is bound to albumin. Effects begin within about an hour, but steady state takes about three to four days. Its half-life is about 25 hours, and its effect lasts more than 42 hours, which allows some flexibility in the time of injection. It is broken down like human insulin by insulin-degrading enzymes in the liver and kidneys.", "sidefx": "Hypoglycemia is the main risk because it lowers glucose continuously. Because it lasts so long, a low blood sugar from too much degludec can be prolonged and may recur, so monitoring is needed after an overdose. Dose changes take several days to show their full effect. Weight gain reflects insulin's storage effects. Hypokalemia, injection site reactions and lipodystrophy can occur. Treatment of hypoglycemia is glucose or glucagon. It must never be mixed with other insulins in the same syringe.", "fun": "Because its effect is so long and flat, degludec is one of the few basal insulins whose label allows flexible timing of the daily injection.", "src": ["Tresiba (insulin degludec) FDA label", "Marso SP et al. N Engl J Med 2017 (DEVOTE)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "insulin-detemir": {"origin": "Insulin detemir was developed by Novo Nordisk as a long-acting basal insulin that uses albumin binding to slow its action. It removes the threonine at position B30 and attaches myristic acid, a 14-carbon fatty acid, to lysine B29. This fatty acid makes detemir form hexamers and dihexamers at the injection site and bind reversibly to albumin, both under the skin and in the blood. The FDA approved it as Levemir in 2005. It was notable for less weight gain than other basal insulins and was studied and used in pregnancy. Novo Nordisk discontinued Levemir in the US, with supplies ending around the end of 2024, but it remains an important example of insulin engineering.", "timeline": [["2004", "Insulin detemir is approved in Europe."], ["2005", "FDA approves insulin detemir (Levemir)."], ["2024", "Novo Nordisk discontinues Levemir in the US."]], "cell": "Detemir binds the insulin receptor and triggers the standard insulin pathway: receptor tyrosine kinase activation, IRS phosphorylation, PI3K-AKT signaling, GLUT4 movement to the membrane, glycogen synthesis, and suppression of liver glucose production. Its affinity for the insulin receptor is lower than human insulin, so it is used in molar amounts adjusted for this. Its long action comes from the myristic acid chain, which causes self-association at the injection site and reversible binding to albumin. Only the free fraction is active, and albumin acts as a buffer that smooths out its effects and slows its distribution into peripheral tissues.", "body": [["Blood glucose", "It provides basal glucose control, with a relatively flat effect that can wane before 24 hours, so many patients need it twice daily."], ["Liver", "Detemir may act relatively more on the liver than on peripheral tissues, which is one theory for its smaller weight gain."], ["Weight", "Studies consistently showed less weight gain than NPH or glargine."], ["Fat tissue", "Suppression of fat breakdown helps prevent ketone production."], ["Potassium", "Potassium moves into cells, as with all insulins."]], "journey": "Detemir is injected subcutaneously, once or twice daily. It forms hexamers and binds albumin at the injection site, then is slowly absorbed. In the blood about 98 percent is bound to albumin. Onset is about one to two hours, with a relatively flat effect lasting 12 to 24 hours depending on the dose. Its half-life is about five to seven hours. Like other insulins, it is broken down by insulin-degrading enzymes in the liver and kidneys.", "sidefx": "Hypoglycemia is the main risk and happens when insulin effect exceeds glucose supply. Its flatter profile than NPH lowers the risk of nighttime hypoglycemia. Weight gain occurs but tends to be less than with other basal insulins. Hypokalemia, injection site reactions and lipodystrophy can occur. Hypoglycemia is treated with glucose or glucagon. It should not be mixed with other insulins.", "fun": "Detemir's 14-carbon fatty acid makes it 'ride' on albumin, the same carrier strategy later used in GLP-1 drugs like liraglutide and semaglutide.", "src": ["Levemir (insulin detemir) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "insulin-lispro": {"origin": "Insulin lispro was the first insulin analog ever approved. Eli Lilly, which began mass-producing insulin in partnership with the Toronto discoverers in 1923 and later made the first recombinant human insulin (Humulin) in 1982, wanted an insulin that acted fast enough to be injected right at mealtime. The inspiration came from insulin-like growth factor 1 (IGF-1), which has lysine and proline in the reverse order and does not self-associate. Lilly scientists reversed the proline at B28 and lysine at B29 of human insulin, which weakened hexamer formation. The FDA approved it as Humalog in 1996. Later versions include Admelog, a follow-on product, and Lyumjev (2020), which adds ingredients that speed absorption even more.", "timeline": [["1923", "Eli Lilly begins commercial insulin production."], ["1982", "Lilly's recombinant human insulin (Humulin) is approved."], ["1996", "FDA approves insulin lispro (Humalog), the first insulin analog."], ["2020", "Lyumjev, an ultra-rapid lispro formulation, is approved."]], "cell": "Lispro binds the insulin receptor with about the same affinity as human insulin and activates the same pathways: receptor tyrosine kinase autophosphorylation, IRS proteins, PI3K-AKT signaling, GLUT4 insertion into muscle and fat cell membranes, glycogen synthesis and suppression of gluconeogenesis. It also activates the Na-K-ATPase, moving potassium into cells. The reversal of proline B28 and lysine B29 (giving 'lys-pro') disrupts the contacts that hold insulin dimers together, so after injection, lispro breaks into monomers quickly and absorbs into capillaries faster than regular human insulin.", "body": [["Blood glucose", "Mealtime glucose spikes are blunted because lispro starts working in about 15 minutes and peaks in one to two hours."], ["Liver", "Liver glucose production is suppressed after meals."], ["Muscle and fat", "Glucose uptake into muscle and fat increases, and fat breakdown and ketone production are suppressed."], ["Potassium", "Potassium moves into cells, so low potassium can occur."], ["Pump therapy", "Its fast onset and short duration make it a standard insulin for insulin pumps."]], "journey": "Lispro is injected subcutaneously, used in pumps, or given IV under medical supervision. It is absorbed faster than regular insulin, so it is taken within 15 minutes before or right after a meal. Its effect lasts about three to five hours, which reduces late hypoglycemia between meals. It is broken down by insulin-degrading enzymes in the liver and kidneys. Its half-life after injection is about one hour, but the absorption rate determines its duration. Kidney failure prolongs its effect.", "sidefx": "Hypoglycemia is the main risk, especially if a meal is skipped after injecting. Symptoms include sweating, tremor and palpitations from adrenaline, followed by confusion and seizures if severe. Mild cases are treated with fast-acting carbohydrate and severe cases with IV dextrose or glucagon. Hypokalemia comes from potassium shifts. Injection site reactions, lipohypertrophy and weight gain can occur. Because it is short-acting, missed doses or pump failure can lead to hyperglycemia and ketoacidosis quickly.", "fun": "The name 'lispro' comes from the swapped amino acids: lysine (lys) and proline (pro).", "src": ["Humalog (insulin lispro) FDA label", "Lyumjev FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "insulin-nph": {"origin": "NPH insulin was developed by the Danish physician and scientist Hans Christian Hagedorn at Nordisk. Early insulin had to be injected many times a day. In 1936 Hagedorn found that combining insulin with protamine, a protein from fish sperm, slowed its absorption. Adding zinc made it more stable, and by 1946 his team had developed a neutral crystalline form that could be premixed with regular insulin. It was named NPH, for Neutral Protamine Hagedorn, and launched in 1950. NPH became the world's main intermediate-acting insulin. It is now made as recombinant human insulin (Humulin N, Novolin N), and in the US human NPH vials can be bought without a prescription.", "timeline": [["1922", "Insulin is first used in patients in Toronto."], ["1936", "Hagedorn discovers that protamine prolongs insulin action."], ["1946", "NPH (Neutral Protamine Hagedorn) insulin is developed."], ["1950", "NPH insulin is launched commercially."], ["1982", "Recombinant human insulin, including NPH forms, is approved."]], "cell": "NPH is regular human insulin crystallized with protamine and zinc at neutral pH. Under the skin, enzymes slowly break down the protamine, gradually releasing insulin hexamers that then break into dimers and monomers for absorption. Once absorbed, the insulin binds the insulin receptor tyrosine kinase, which autophosphorylates and phosphorylates IRS proteins. This activates PI3K-AKT signaling, which moves GLUT4 to the membrane in muscle and fat cells, activates glycogen synthase, and suppresses gluconeogenesis and fat breakdown. It also activates the Na-K-ATPase, moving potassium into cells, and promotes protein synthesis.", "body": [["Blood glucose", "It lowers glucose with an onset of one to two hours and a peak at about 4 to 12 hours, lasting 12 to 18 hours. It is usually given twice daily."], ["Liver", "Glucose production is suppressed, especially overnight when given in the evening."], ["Muscle and fat", "Glucose uptake increases and fat breakdown is suppressed."], ["Hypoglycemia pattern", "Its peak can cause unexpected low blood sugar, especially overnight or between meals."], ["Potassium", "Potassium shifts into cells, as with all insulins."], ["Pregnancy and steroids", "It is often used in pregnancy and to match the glucose rise from morning steroid doses."]], "journey": "NPH is a cloudy suspension that must be gently rolled before use to mix the crystals evenly. It is given only subcutaneously, never IV. Absorption is slow and more variable than with modern analogs, which explains its broad peak and day-to-day differences. Once absorbed, insulin is broken down by insulin-degrading enzymes in the liver and kidneys. Its plasma half-life is not meaningful because absorption controls its effect. It can be mixed in the same syringe with regular insulin, drawing the clear insulin first.", "sidefx": "Hypoglycemia is the main risk, and its peak makes low blood sugar more likely at predictable times, such as overnight or before lunch. Weight gain, lipohypertrophy and injection site reactions can occur. Protamine can cause allergies, and patients who have used protamine-containing insulin have a higher risk of reactions to protamine used to reverse heparin. Hypokalemia occurs from potassium shifts. Hypoglycemia is treated with glucose or glucagon.", "fun": "The phrase 'clear before cloudy' for mixing insulins comes from NPH: regular insulin (clear) is drawn up first so cloudy NPH does not contaminate the regular vial.", "src": ["Humulin N and Novolin N FDA labels", "Owens DR. Diabetes Technol Ther 2011 (history of insulin)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "ipratropium": {"origin": "Ipratropium grew from an old remedy: smoking the leaves of plants like Datura stramonium (jimsonweed), which contain atropine-like alkaloids, was used for asthma in India and later in Europe in the 1800s. Atropine itself dilated airways but caused dry mouth, fast heart rate and confusion. Scientists at Boehringer Ingelheim in Germany made ipratropium in the 1970s by adding an isopropyl group to atropine's nitrogen, creating a quaternary ammonium compound that is poorly absorbed and does not enter the brain. Inhaled, it acts mainly in the airways. The FDA approved the Atrovent inhaler in 1986. It is widely used in COPD and in emergency treatment of severe asthma, often combined with albuterol (Combivent, DuoNeb). Its long-acting successor is tiotropium.", "timeline": [["1800s", "Smoking Datura stramonium is used for asthma in Europe, building on Indian practice."], ["1970s", "Boehringer Ingelheim develops ipratropium bromide."], ["1986", "FDA approves the Atrovent inhaler."], ["1996", "Combivent (ipratropium with albuterol) is approved."]], "cell": "Airway smooth muscle tone is controlled mainly by the vagus nerve, which releases acetylcholine onto M3 muscarinic receptors. M3 receptors are Gq-coupled; activation raises IP3 and calcium, causing bronchoconstriction and mucus secretion from glands. Ipratropium is a competitive antagonist at muscarinic receptors. Blocking M3 on smooth muscle and glands prevents vagally mediated bronchoconstriction and reduces mucus secretion. It also blocks M2 autoreceptors on vagal nerve endings, which normally limit acetylcholine release, and this partly offsets its effect. Because it is a quaternary amine with a permanent positive charge, little is absorbed from the lungs or gut, and it does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so systemic anticholinergic effects are minimal.", "body": [["Lungs", "Airways widen and mucus secretion falls, improving airflow in COPD and severe asthma attacks. It is especially effective where vagal tone is high."], ["Mouth", "Dry mouth and a bitter taste are common from local effects."], ["Nose", "As a nasal spray, it reduces watery runny nose from rhinitis and colds."], ["Eyes", "If sprayed into the eyes, for example from a loose nebulizer mask, it can dilate the pupils and trigger acute angle-closure glaucoma."], ["Bladder", "Urinary retention can occur in men with enlarged prostates."], ["Heart", "Little reaches the heart, so heart rate effects are usually minimal."]], "journey": "Ipratropium is inhaled by metered-dose inhaler or nebulizer, or used as a nasal spray. Most of the dose is swallowed, but very little is absorbed from the gut or lungs because it is permanently charged. Bronchodilation begins within 15 to 30 minutes, peaks in one to two hours, and lasts four to six hours. The small absorbed portion is partly metabolized by the liver and excreted in urine; the swallowed portion leaves in the feces. The half-life is about two hours.", "sidefx": "Side effects are local anticholinergic effects: dry mouth, cough, bitter taste and throat irritation. Urinary retention and blurred vision are less common because absorption is low. Eye exposure can cause pupil dilation and angle-closure glaucoma. Paradoxical bronchospasm and allergic reactions are rare. Some older formulations used soy lecithin, a concern in soy or peanut allergy. Systemic overdose is unlikely because so little is absorbed.", "fun": "Datura stramonium 'asthma cigarettes' were sold in pharmacies in the US into the mid-20th century.", "src": ["Atrovent HFA (ipratropium) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "iron-sucrose": {"origin": "Iron deficiency anemia has been treated with iron since ancient times, but some patients cannot absorb or tolerate oral iron. Early injectable iron products, such as high molecular weight iron dextran introduced in the 1950s, worked but carried a risk of severe allergic reactions. Iron sucrose is an iron hydroxide core wrapped in a shell of sucrose, which releases iron slowly and has a low risk of anaphylaxis. It has been used clinically since 1949, mainly in Europe, where it was made by the Swiss company Vifor. In the US, the FDA approved it as Venofer in 2000 for iron deficiency in chronic kidney disease patients on hemodialysis receiving erythropoietin, and its use later expanded to other kidney disease patients.", "timeline": [["1949", "Iron sucrose enters clinical use in Europe."], ["1950s", "Iron dextran, an early injectable iron, is introduced."], ["2000", "FDA approves iron sucrose (Venofer) for iron deficiency in hemodialysis patients."]], "cell": "Iron sucrose is a complex of polynuclear iron(III) hydroxide surrounded by sucrose. After IV injection, macrophages of the reticuloendothelial system in the liver, spleen and bone marrow take up the complex. Inside, the iron is released from the sucrose shell. It can be stored as ferritin or exported through the iron channel ferroportin. In the blood, iron binds transferrin, which delivers it to developing red blood cells in the bone marrow. These cells take it in through transferrin receptors and use it to make heme for hemoglobin. Because the shell slowly releases iron, little free reactive iron appears in the blood. It bypasses the gut, so it works even when hepcidin, which is high in chronic inflammation and kidney disease, blocks gut absorption.", "body": [["Blood", "Hemoglobin and reticulocyte counts rise over weeks as iron becomes available for red cell production. Ferritin and transferrin saturation increase."], ["Bone marrow", "Red cell precursors make more hemoglobin, and patients often need less erythropoietin."], ["Kidney disease", "It corrects iron deficiency in chronic kidney disease, where oral iron is often poorly absorbed."], ["Heart and vessels", "Low blood pressure can occur during or after infusion, especially with fast administration."], ["Muscles", "Muscle cramps are common, especially in dialysis patients."]], "journey": "Iron sucrose is given IV, either as a slow push or as an infusion, often during dialysis. The complex is taken up by macrophages in the liver, spleen and bone marrow. Its half-life in blood is about six hours. Iron released from the complex joins the body's iron stores and is used for hemoglobin production. The body has no active way to excrete iron, so only small amounts leave through cell shedding and blood loss. A small amount of the complex is excreted in urine in the first hours.", "sidefx": "Hypotension is the most common serious reaction and is linked to infusion speed and the release of iron. Muscle cramps, nausea, headache and taste changes occur. Hypersensitivity reactions are much rarer than with older iron dextran. Too much iron over time can cause iron overload, so ferritin and transferrin saturation are monitored. Iron overload is treated with chelators like deferoxamine. Iron can also leak into tissue around the IV and stain the skin brown.", "fun": "Iron sucrose is one of the oldest injectable medicines still in common use, with clinical history going back to 1949.", "src": ["Venofer (iron sucrose) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "isoniazid": {"origin": "Isoniazid (isonicotinic acid hydrazide) was first synthesized in 1912 by Hans Meyer and Josef Mally in Prague as part of a chemistry doctoral project, with no thought of medicine. Forty years later, in 1951 and 1952, three drug companies, Hoffmann-La Roche, Squibb and Bayer, independently found that it was very active against tuberculosis. Trials at Sea View Hospital on Staten Island in 1952 showed dramatic improvement, and newspapers printed photos of recovering patients celebrating. Isoniazid was cheap and taken by mouth, and it became the core drug of tuberculosis treatment. A related drug, iproniazid, was noticed to improve mood in TB patients, which led to the first MAO inhibitor antidepressants.", "timeline": [["1912", "Meyer and Mally synthesize isonicotinic acid hydrazide in Prague."], ["1952", "Roche, Squibb and Bayer report isoniazid's activity against tuberculosis."], ["1952", "Trials at Sea View Hospital, Staten Island, show striking clinical improvement."], ["1950s", "Iproniazid's mood-elevating effect leads to the first MAOI antidepressants."]], "cell": "Isoniazid is a prodrug. It diffuses into Mycobacterium tuberculosis and is activated by the bacterial enzyme KatG (catalase-peroxidase), which converts it into an isonicotinic acyl radical. This radical joins NAD to form an isoniazid-NAD adduct, which tightly binds and inhibits InhA, the enoyl-acyl carrier protein reductase in the fatty acid synthase II system. InhA is needed to make mycolic acids, the very long fatty acids unique to the mycobacterial cell wall. Without mycolic acids, the wall cannot be built and rapidly dividing bacilli die. Resistance most often comes from KatG mutations (high-level resistance) or inhA promoter mutations (low-level resistance). Isoniazid also interferes with pyridoxine (vitamin B6), which causes neuropathy and seizures.", "body": [["Lungs and infection", "It kills actively dividing tubercle bacilli and is used in multidrug therapy for active TB and alone or with rifapentine for latent TB."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes often rise, and a small number of patients develop hepatitis, which can be fatal. Risk increases with age, alcohol use and other liver toxins."], ["Nerves", "By depleting active pyridoxine, it causes peripheral neuropathy with numbness and tingling, which pyridoxine supplements prevent."], ["Brain", "In overdose, pyridoxine depletion lowers GABA production, causing seizures that do not respond well to standard treatment."], ["Immune system", "It can cause drug-induced lupus with positive antinuclear and antihistone antibodies."], ["Drug metabolism", "It inhibits CYP2C19 and CYP3A4, raising levels of drugs like phenytoin and carbamazepine."]], "journey": "Isoniazid is taken by mouth and absorbed well, though food reduces absorption. It spreads throughout the body, including the spinal fluid and caseous TB lesions, crosses the placenta and enters breast milk. The liver metabolizes it mainly by N-acetyltransferase 2 (NAT2). Fast acetylators clear it quickly with a half-life of about one hour, while slow acetylators have a half-life of about three hours and higher levels. Metabolites are excreted in the urine.", "sidefx": "Hepatotoxicity comes from toxic metabolites, especially acetylhydrazine and hydrazine, formed during metabolism. Patients are monitored and told to stop it if they develop symptoms of hepatitis. Peripheral neuropathy is caused by isoniazid binding pyridoxine and increasing its excretion, so pyridoxine is given with it, especially in people with diabetes, malnutrition, alcohol use or pregnancy. In overdose, lack of pyridoxine blocks GABA synthesis, causing seizures, metabolic acidosis and coma; IV pyridoxine is the antidote. Drug-induced lupus is more common in slow acetylators.", "fun": "The 'INH' abbreviation comes from isonicotinic acid hydrazide, and the classic mnemonic for its toxicity is 'INH injures neurons and hepatocytes'.", "src": ["Isoniazid FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]}, "isoproterenol": {"origin": "Isoproterenol (isoprenaline) was one of the first synthetic sympathomimetic amines. It was discovered in 1940 by Heribert Konzett and colleagues at Boehringer Ingelheim in Germany while studying derivatives of adrenaline, by adding an isopropyl group to the amine. It turned out to be a powerful bronchodilator and was approved for medical use in the US in 1947. In 1948, Raymond Ahlquist used isoproterenol, along with epinephrine and norepinephrine, to propose two kinds of adrenergic receptors, alpha and beta, a landmark in pharmacology. In the 1960s, deaths among people with asthma rose in several countries where high-strength isoproterenol inhalers were sold, which pushed the development of more selective beta-2 agonists like albuterol.", "timeline": [["1940", "Isoproterenol is discovered at Boehringer Ingelheim."], ["1947", "Isoproterenol is approved for medical use in the US."], ["1948", "Raymond Ahlquist uses isoproterenol to define alpha and beta adrenergic receptors."], ["1960s", "An epidemic of asthma deaths is linked to high-strength isoproterenol inhalers in several countries."]], "cell": "Isoproterenol is a nonselective agonist at beta-1 and beta-2 adrenergic receptors, with almost no alpha activity. These receptors are Gs-coupled. Activation turns on adenylyl cyclase, raising cyclic AMP and activating protein kinase A. In the heart (beta-1), PKA phosphorylates L-type calcium channels and phospholamban, increasing calcium entry and reuptake, so the heart beats faster (chronotropy), more forcefully (inotropy), and conducts faster through the AV node (dromotropy). In vascular and bronchial smooth muscle (beta-2), cAMP and PKA reduce myosin light chain kinase activity and lower calcium, causing vasodilation and bronchodilation. Its isopropyl group makes it fit beta receptors very well and alpha receptors poorly.", "body": [["Heart rate", "Heart rate rises strongly, which is used to treat bradycardia and heart block until pacing is available, and torsades de pointes when it is pause-dependent."], ["Heart muscle", "Contractility increases, but so does oxygen demand, which can worsen ischemia."], ["Blood vessels", "Beta-2 vasodilation in skeletal muscle lowers diastolic pressure. Systolic pressure may rise from increased output, while mean arterial pressure usually falls."], ["Lungs", "Airways widen strongly, but more selective agents have replaced it for asthma."], ["Electrophysiology lab", "It is used to provoke arrhythmias during electrophysiology studies and in tilt-table testing."], ["Metabolism", "Beta-2 effects raise blood glucose and lower potassium."]], "journey": "Isoproterenol is given by IV infusion for cardiac uses. It acts immediately and its effects fade within minutes of stopping. It is a catecholamine, so it is broken down by catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT) in the liver and tissues; it is a poor substrate for MAO and is not taken back up efficiently by nerve terminals. Metabolites and some unchanged drug are excreted in the urine. The half-life is only a few minutes, so it is given as a continuous, titrated infusion.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from strong beta stimulation. Tachycardia, palpitations and arrhythmias, including ventricular tachycardia and fibrillation, come from beta-1 stimulation of the heart. Increased oxygen demand with lower diastolic pressure (which reduces coronary perfusion) can cause angina and myocardial ischemia. Beta-2 vasodilation causes hypotension, flushing and headache. Tremor, hypokalemia and hyperglycemia reflect beta-2 effects in muscle and liver. Because of its short half-life, stopping the infusion reverses most effects quickly, and beta-blockers can be used for severe toxicity.", "fun": "Ahlquist's 1948 paper describing alpha and beta receptors was initially rejected by a journal, and it later became one of the most cited papers in pharmacology.", "src": ["Isuprel (isoproterenol) FDA label", "Ahlquist RP. Am J Physiol 1948", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "isosorbide-dinitrate": {"origin": "Isosorbide dinitrate belongs to the organic nitrate family, whose story starts with nitroglycerin. In 1879 the London physician William Murrell reported that small amounts of nitroglycerin relieved angina. Nitroglycerin works fast but wears off quickly, so chemists looked for nitrates that lasted longer. Isosorbide dinitrate, a nitrate ester of the sugar alcohol isosorbide, came into clinical use for angina in the mid-twentieth century. Its second life came in heart failure. The V-HeFT trial in 1986 showed that isosorbide dinitrate plus hydralazine lowered death rates in heart failure, and the A-HeFT trial in self-identified Black patients led the FDA to approve the fixed combination BiDil in 2005, the first drug approved with a race-specific indication.", "timeline": [["1879", "William Murrell reports nitroglycerin for angina in The Lancet."], ["1986", "V-HeFT trial shows hydralazine plus isosorbide dinitrate improves survival in heart failure."], ["2004", "A-HeFT trial shows a mortality benefit in Black patients with heart failure."], ["2005", "FDA approves BiDil (isosorbide dinitrate with hydralazine)."]], "cell": "Isosorbide dinitrate is a prodrug of nitric oxide (NO). Inside vascular smooth muscle cells, enzymatic bioactivation releases NO. NO binds the heme group of soluble guanylate cyclase and switches it on, so the enzyme turns GTP into cyclic GMP (cGMP). cGMP activates protein kinase G, which lowers intracellular calcium and activates myosin light chain phosphatase. Myosin light chains are dephosphorylated, and the muscle relaxes. Veins are most sensitive, so venous pooling lowers preload, ventricular wall stress and myocardial oxygen demand. Larger coronary arteries also dilate, which helps blood reach ischemic areas and relieves vasospasm.", "body": [["Veins", "Veins relax and hold more blood, so less returns to the heart. Lower preload means a smaller, less stressed ventricle that needs less oxygen."], ["Heart", "Lower wall stress and better coronary flow reduce angina. In heart failure, combined with hydralazine, it lowers filling pressures and improves survival in selected patients."], ["Coronary arteries", "Epicardial coronary arteries and collateral vessels dilate, and coronary spasm is relieved."], ["Arteries", "At higher levels arterioles also relax, lowering blood pressure and afterload, which can trigger reflex tachycardia."], ["Brain", "Dilation of meningeal vessels causes the classic throbbing nitrate headache."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth or under the tongue, isosorbide dinitrate is absorbed well, but oral doses lose much of their drug to first-pass metabolism in the liver. Sublingual tablets bypass the liver and act within minutes, while swallowed tablets take about an hour. The liver converts it into two active metabolites, isosorbide-2-mononitrate and isosorbide-5-mononitrate, which last longer than the parent drug. The parent half-life is about an hour, but the active mononitrates keep working for several hours. Metabolites leave mainly in the urine. Continuous exposure causes tolerance, so schedules include a daily nitrate-free interval.", "sidefx": "Headache, flushing and dizziness come straight from blood vessel dilation. Venous pooling and arteriolar relaxation lower blood pressure, causing orthostatic hypotension, fainting and reflex tachycardia. Tolerance develops with round-the-clock exposure, likely from reduced bioactivation and oxidative stress, which is why a nitrate-free period is built in. Combining nitrates with PDE-5 inhibitors such as sildenafil is dangerous: nitrates raise cGMP production while PDE-5 inhibitors block cGMP breakdown, and the result can be profound hypotension.", "fun": "Alfred Nobel, who built his fortune on nitroglycerin explosives, was later prescribed nitroglycerin for his own chest pain and wrote about the irony in a letter.", "src": ["Isordil (isosorbide dinitrate) FDA label", "BiDil FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Cohn JN et al. NEJM 1986 (V-HeFT)", "Taylor AL et al. NEJM 2004 (A-HeFT)"]}, "isosorbide-mononitrate": {"origin": "Isosorbide mononitrate began as a metabolite. Researchers studying isosorbide dinitrate found that the liver converts it into mononitrates, and that isosorbide-5-mononitrate does much of the work. Because the mononitrate is not broken down on its first pass through the liver, nearly all of a swallowed dose reaches the blood. That made it more predictable than its parent. Drug companies developed it as a medicine of its own, and immediate- and extended-release products (Ismo, Monoket, Imdur) reached the US market in the early 1990s. The once-daily extended-release tablet made long-acting nitrate therapy simpler and built in the nitrate-free interval that prevents tolerance.", "timeline": [["1879", "Nitroglycerin is first reported for angina, launching the nitrate drug family."], ["1990s", "Isosorbide mononitrate products reach the US market, including once-daily extended-release tablets."]], "cell": "Isosorbide mononitrate is an organic nitrate that releases nitric oxide (NO) inside vascular smooth muscle. NO activates soluble guanylate cyclase by binding its heme group, raising cyclic GMP. cGMP activates protein kinase G, which lowers cytosolic calcium and activates myosin light chain phosphatase. Dephosphorylated myosin cannot cross-bridge with actin, so the muscle relaxes. Veins respond at the lowest concentrations, lowering preload and left ventricular wall tension, which cuts myocardial oxygen demand. Coronary arteries and collaterals also dilate, improving supply to ischemic heart muscle.", "body": [["Veins", "Venodilation pools blood in the veins and lowers preload, the main way it prevents angina."], ["Heart", "A smaller, less stretched ventricle needs less oxygen, so exercise and stress are less likely to cause ischemic chest pain."], ["Coronary arteries", "Large coronary arteries and collaterals widen, and vasospasm is eased."], ["Arteries", "Some arteriolar dilation lowers blood pressure and afterload, which can cause reflex tachycardia."], ["Brain", "Dilation of cranial vessels causes headache, which is most common early in treatment."]], "journey": "Swallowed isosorbide mononitrate is almost completely absorbed and, unlike its parent, avoids first-pass liver breakdown, so bioavailability is close to complete. Effects start in under an hour. Extended-release tablets release the drug slowly over the day and must not be crushed. The half-life is about five hours. The liver converts it to inactive products such as isosorbide and glucuronide conjugates, which the kidneys excrete. Once-daily dosing lets blood levels fall overnight, giving the vessels a nitrate-free period that limits tolerance.", "sidefx": "Headache, dizziness and flushing reflect dilation of cranial and skin vessels. Lower venous return and blood pressure can cause lightheadedness, fainting and reflex tachycardia, especially when standing. Tolerance develops with constant exposure, so the dosing pattern lets levels drop. It is not used to stop an acute angina attack because it acts too slowly. Combining it with PDE-5 inhibitors can cause severe, life-threatening hypotension because both drugs raise cGMP.", "src": ["Imdur (isosorbide mononitrate) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "ivabradine": {"origin": "Ivabradine grew out of the discovery of the funny current. In the late 1970s Dario DiFrancesco and colleagues described an unusual inward current in sinoatrial node cells that is switched on by hyperpolarization rather than depolarization, and they named it the funny current, If. The French company Servier set out to find a drug that would slow the heart by blocking this current alone, without the blood pressure and contractility effects of beta-blockers. Ivabradine was approved in Europe in 2005 for angina. The SHIFT trial, published in 2010, showed fewer heart failure hospitalizations in patients whose heart rates stayed high despite beta-blockers. The FDA approved it as Corlanor in 2015.", "timeline": [["1979", "DiFrancesco and colleagues describe the funny current in sinoatrial node cells."], ["2005", "Ivabradine is approved in Europe for stable angina."], ["2010", "SHIFT trial shows benefit in heart failure with elevated heart rate."], ["2015", "FDA approves ivabradine (Corlanor) for heart failure."]], "cell": "Sinoatrial node cells have no stable resting potential. After each beat, HCN4 channels (hyperpolarization-activated cyclic nucleotide-gated channels) open and carry a mixed sodium and potassium inward current called If. This current drives the slow phase 4 diastolic depolarization that brings the cell to threshold. Ivabradine enters the open HCN4 channel from inside the cell and blocks the pore. Phase 4 becomes flatter, so the cell takes longer to reach threshold and fires less often. The block is use-dependent and works better at higher heart rates. The result is a pure slowing of heart rate, with no change in contractility, AV conduction or ventricular repolarization.", "body": [["SA node", "Slower diastolic depolarization lowers the resting heart rate, which is the only intended cardiac effect."], ["Heart muscle", "A slower rate lengthens diastole, so the ventricles fill more completely and the heart uses less oxygen. Contractility is not reduced."], ["Coronary arteries", "Coronary flow happens mostly in diastole, so a longer diastole improves blood supply to the heart muscle."], ["Blood pressure", "Blood pressure is largely unchanged because the drug does not relax vessels or weaken the heart."], ["Eyes", "Retinal HCN1 channels are similar to HCN4, so partial block causes brief bright visual flashes called phosphenes."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth with food, ivabradine is absorbed quickly but loses part of each dose to first-pass metabolism in the gut and liver. It is broken down almost entirely by CYP3A4, and its main metabolite, an N-desmethyl derivative, is also active. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels sharply and are contraindicated, while inducers lower them. Its plasma half-life is about two hours, with an effective half-life of about six hours. Metabolites leave in both urine and feces. Animal studies show fetal harm, so it is avoided in pregnancy.", "sidefx": "Bradycardia is the expected extension of If blockade and can become symptomatic or worsen heart block in people with conduction disease. Phosphenes, brief luminous areas in the visual field, happen because the drug also blocks the related HCN1 channel (Ih current) in the retina. They usually appear early and often fade. Atrial fibrillation is more common in treated patients. Blood pressure can rise in some patients. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors can cause severe bradycardia by raising drug levels. There is no specific antidote; severe bradycardia is managed with standard measures such as atropine or pacing.", "fun": "The funny current earned its name because it does the opposite of most channels: it opens when the cell becomes more negative.", "src": ["Corlanor (ivabradine) FDA label", "Swedberg K et al. Lancet 2010 (SHIFT)", "DiFrancesco D. Circ Res 2010 review of the funny current"]}, "ivermectin": {"origin": "Ivermectin began in a handful of Japanese soil. In the early 1970s Satoshi Omura at the Kitasato Institute collected soil samples and grew microbes from them, including a new species later named Streptomyces avermitilis, from soil near a golf course on the Izu Peninsula. He sent cultures to Merck, where William Campbell's team found that broths from this microbe killed parasitic worms in mice. The active compounds were named avermectins, and Merck chemists made a safer, more potent derivative, ivermectin. It launched as a veterinary drug in 1981. In 1987 Merck began donating Mectizan to fight river blindness, one of the largest drug donation programs in history. Omura and Campbell shared the 2015 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. The FDA approved oral ivermectin (Stromectol) in 1996.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Satoshi Omura isolates Streptomyces avermitilis from Japanese soil."], ["1979", "Merck scientists report the avermectins and their antiparasitic power."], ["1981", "Ivermectin enters veterinary use."], ["1987", "Merck begins donating Mectizan for river blindness."], ["1996", "FDA approves oral ivermectin (Stromectol) for human use."], ["2015", "Omura and Campbell share the Nobel Prize."]], "cell": "Ivermectin is a macrocyclic lactone. In nematodes and arthropods it binds glutamate-gated chloride channels in nerve and muscle cells, a channel type that mammals do not have. Binding locks the channels open, so chloride flows in and the cell hyperpolarizes. Hyperpolarized neurons and pharyngeal muscle cannot fire, which causes flaccid paralysis and starvation of the parasite. It also enhances GABA-gated chloride channels. In humans, GABA-gated chloride channels are confined to the brain, and P-glycoprotein at the blood-brain barrier pumps ivermectin out, which explains its wide safety margin. In onchocerciasis it kills microfilariae and suppresses their release, but does not reliably kill adult worms.", "body": [["Parasites", "Worms and mites are paralyzed and die, or are cleared by the host. Microfilariae in skin and eyes are killed in river blindness."], ["Skin", "Topical forms kill lice and Demodex mites in rosacea; oral doses treat scabies by killing mites in the skin."], ["Immune system", "Dying microfilariae release antigens and can trigger the Mazzotti reaction, with itching, fever, rash and swollen lymph nodes."], ["Brain", "Normally protected by P-glycoprotein. When the barrier is weak or levels are very high, it can cause confusion, ataxia, seizures and coma."], ["Gut", "Strongyloides larvae in the intestine are killed, which is critical before steroids or immunosuppression."]], "journey": "Oral ivermectin is taken on an empty stomach with water. It is very fat-soluble, so it spreads widely into fat and the liver, and it is highly bound to plasma proteins. P-glycoprotein keeps most of it out of the brain. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP3A4. It is eliminated almost entirely in the feces through bile, with very little in the urine. The half-life is about 18 hours, but parasite effects last much longer, so a single dose or a repeat dose after a set interval is often enough.", "sidefx": "Most side effects in people with heavy infection come from the immune response to dying parasites rather than the drug itself. That explains the Mazzotti reaction in onchocerciasis, with itching, fever and low blood pressure. In patients with heavy Loa loa infection, mass death of microfilariae in the brain can cause a dangerous encephalopathy, so screening matters in endemic areas. Overdose, often from veterinary products, overwhelms P-glycoprotein and allows GABA channel effects in the brain, causing confusion, ataxia, seizures and coma. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Some herding dog breeds such as collies carry an MDR1 gene mutation that disables P-glycoprotein, which is why ivermectin can be toxic to them.", "src": ["Stromectol (ivermectin) FDA label", "Campbell WC. Ivermectin and Abamectin, 1989", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 2015 press release"]}, "ketamine": {"origin": "Ketamine was born from the search for a safer phencyclidine. Phencyclidine (PCP) was a powerful anesthetic, but it caused long, frightening emergence delirium. In 1962 chemist Calvin Stevens at Parke-Davis synthesized ketamine, a shorter-acting relative. In 1964 Edward Domino and Guenter Corssen gave it to human volunteers in Michigan, and Domino's wife suggested the term dissociative anesthesia for its strange effect. The FDA approved it in 1970, and it was soon used for field anesthesia in the Vietnam War because it keeps breathing and blood pressure up. Decades later it found new roles in emergency agitation, pain control and depression, and the FDA approved the nasal S-enantiomer esketamine (Spravato) for treatment-resistant depression in 2019.", "timeline": [["1962", "Calvin Stevens synthesizes ketamine at Parke-Davis."], ["1964", "Domino and Corssen perform the first human studies."], ["1970", "FDA approves ketamine as an anesthetic."], ["2019", "FDA approves esketamine nasal spray for treatment-resistant depression."]], "cell": "Ketamine is a noncompetitive antagonist of the NMDA glutamate receptor. It enters the open channel and binds the phencyclidine site inside the pore, blocking calcium and sodium entry. This cuts excitatory signaling between the thalamus and cortex and the limbic system, so the cortex is functionally cut off from sensory input. That produces dissociation, amnesia and analgesia. NMDA block in the spinal cord also reduces central sensitization (wind-up), which is why low doses help pain and reduce opioid needs. Ketamine also blocks reuptake of norepinephrine and causes central sympathetic stimulation, which raises heart rate and blood pressure. It has weaker effects at opioid and muscarinic receptors. Its rapid antidepressant effect is linked to a burst of glutamate signaling through AMPA receptors and new synapse growth.", "body": [["Brain", "Dissociation, analgesia, amnesia and sedation. Eyes may stay open with nystagmus. Emergence can bring vivid dreams or agitation."], ["Heart and vessels", "Sympathetic stimulation raises heart rate, blood pressure and cardiac output, which helps hypotensive patients but strains those with coronary disease."], ["Lungs", "Breathing and airway reflexes are largely preserved, and bronchial smooth muscle relaxes, making it useful in severe asthma."], ["Airway", "Salivation and airway secretions increase, and laryngospasm can occur, especially with airway stimulation."], ["Spinal cord", "NMDA block reduces pain amplification and opioid tolerance."], ["Bladder", "Long-term recreational use can cause severe ulcerative cystitis."]], "journey": "Ketamine works by many routes: IV, IO, IM, intranasal and by mouth. It is very lipid-soluble, so after an IV dose it reaches the brain within about a minute, and the effect fades in minutes as it redistributes to other tissues. It crosses the placenta. The liver demethylates it through CYP3A4 and CYP2B6 into norketamine, an active metabolite with about one-third the potency, which extends analgesia. Oral doses lose much to first-pass metabolism. The elimination half-life is about two to three hours, and metabolites leave in the urine.", "sidefx": "Emergence reactions, including vivid dreams, hallucinations and agitation, come from NMDA blockade disconnecting the cortex from sensory input. Hypertension and tachycardia come from sympathetic stimulation. Increased salivation can complicate airway management. Laryngospasm is uncommon but serious. Rapid IV pushes can cause brief apnea. Nausea and vomiting are common on recovery. With chronic misuse, bladder damage and liver injury can occur. There is no reversal agent. Care is supportive, and benzodiazepines are often used for severe emergence agitation.", "fun": "Ketamine is on the WHO Model List of Essential Medicines because it can provide anesthesia without ventilators or monitors in low-resource settings.", "src": ["Ketalar (ketamine) FDA label", "Domino EF. Anesthesiology 2010, Taming the ketamine tiger", "Spravato (esketamine) FDA label"]}, "ketorolac": {"origin": "Ketorolac was developed by the California company Syntex, best known for creating naproxen. Syntex chemists made a series of pyrrolo-pyrrole acetic acids in the 1970s and 1980s and found that ketorolac had strong pain-relieving power with only modest anti-inflammatory action. It was approved in the US in 1989 as Toradol, first as an injection. It was one of the first NSAIDs that could be given by injection in the US, giving hospitals a strong non-opioid choice for moderate to severe pain. Reports of bleeding and kidney injury led to strict limits on how long it can be used. A nasal spray form, Sprix, followed in 2010.", "timeline": [["1989", "FDA approves ketorolac injection (Toradol)."], ["1990s", "Label limits added on duration of use after reports of bleeding and kidney injury."], ["2010", "FDA approves intranasal ketorolac (Sprix)."]], "cell": "Ketorolac reversibly inhibits both cyclooxygenase isoforms, COX-1 and COX-2. These enzymes turn arachidonic acid into prostaglandin H2, the precursor of prostaglandins, prostacyclin and thromboxane. Less prostaglandin E2 at injured tissue means pain nerve endings are less sensitized to bradykinin and other mediators. Fewer prostaglandins in the spinal cord also reduce central pain signaling. Blocking COX-1 in platelets lowers thromboxane A2, reducing platelet aggregation for as long as drug is present. Its pain-relieving effect is much stronger than its anti-inflammatory effect.", "body": [["Pain pathways", "Lower prostaglandin levels in tissues and the spinal cord give strong pain relief, often comparable to moderate opioid doses for renal colic and musculoskeletal pain."], ["Stomach", "Loss of protective prostaglandins reduces mucus, bicarbonate and blood flow, raising the risk of ulcers and bleeding."], ["Kidneys", "Prostaglandins keep the afferent arterioles open when blood volume is low. Blocking them can cause acute kidney injury, sodium retention and high potassium."], ["Blood", "Platelet thromboxane falls, so bleeding time is prolonged while the drug is present."], ["Heart and vessels", "Fluid retention and loss of prostacyclin can raise blood pressure and the risk of heart attack and stroke."], ["Ureter", "Lower prostaglandins reduce ureteral spasm and renal pelvic pressure, which helps kidney stone pain."]], "journey": "Ketorolac is absorbed completely after IM injection and well by mouth, and it can also be given IV or by nasal spray. IV pain relief begins within about half an hour and lasts four to six hours. It is very highly bound to plasma proteins and crosses the placenta. The liver conjugates it with glucuronic acid and hydroxylates part of it. About 90 percent leaves in the urine, so kidney disease and older age raise blood levels. The half-life is about five to six hours. Total treatment is limited to a short course across all routes.", "sidefx": "All the major toxicities trace back to COX inhibition. Losing protective prostaglandins in the stomach causes ulcers, bleeding and perforation. Losing renal prostaglandins causes acute kidney injury, especially in dehydrated or elderly patients. Reduced platelet thromboxane causes bleeding, so it is avoided around high-bleeding-risk surgery. Like other NSAIDs it carries boxed warnings for heart attack and stroke. These risks rise with duration, which is why therapy is limited to a few days. There is no antidote; management is supportive.", "src": ["Toradol (ketorolac) FDA label", "Sprix (ketorolac nasal) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's"]}, "labetalol": {"origin": "Labetalol was the first drug designed to block both alpha and beta adrenergic receptors. Beta-blockers such as propranolol, developed by James Black in the 1960s, lowered heart rate but could raise vascular resistance. Scientists at the British company Allen & Hanburys (part of Glaxo) set out to combine beta blockade with alpha-1 blockade, so a single molecule could slow the heart and relax blood vessels at the same time. Labetalol came out of that work in the early 1970s. It reached the US market in the 1980s under the brands Trandate and Normodyne. Because it lowers blood pressure smoothly without dropping cardiac output or uterine blood flow much, it became a mainstay for hypertensive emergencies and for high blood pressure in pregnancy.", "timeline": [["1960s", "James Black develops the first clinically useful beta-blockers."], ["1970s", "Allen & Hanburys scientists develop labetalol, the first combined alpha and beta blocker."], ["1980s", "Labetalol is approved in the US as Trandate and Normodyne."]], "cell": "Labetalol is a competitive antagonist at beta-1, beta-2 and alpha-1 adrenergic receptors. Blocking cardiac beta-1 receptors prevents norepinephrine from activating Gs proteins, so adenylyl cyclase makes less cAMP. Less cAMP means less protein kinase A activity, less calcium entry and a slower, less forceful heartbeat. Beta-1 block in the kidney also reduces renin release. Blocking alpha-1 receptors on arteriolar smooth muscle prevents Gq activation, IP3 formation and calcium release, so the vessels relax. Beta block is stronger than alpha block, about 7 to 1 by IV and 3 to 1 by mouth. Because vasodilation offsets the slower heart, resistance falls while cardiac output is largely preserved.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 block relaxes arterioles and lowers systemic vascular resistance, the main way blood pressure falls."], ["Heart", "Beta-1 block slows heart rate and limits reflex tachycardia from the vasodilation. Contractility drops somewhat."], ["Kidneys", "Less renin release lowers angiotensin II and aldosterone. Renal blood flow is generally maintained."], ["Lungs", "Beta-2 block can narrow airways in people with asthma or COPD."], ["Brain", "Cerebral blood flow is preserved, which makes it popular for blood pressure control in stroke."], ["Uterus and placenta", "Uteroplacental blood flow is generally maintained, so it is a first-line choice for severe hypertension in pregnancy."]], "journey": "Labetalol can be given IV or by mouth. Oral doses are well absorbed but lose much to first-pass metabolism in the liver. IV doses start working in minutes, with peak effect within about 5 to 15 minutes. It distributes widely, crosses the placenta, and only a small amount passes the blood-brain barrier. The liver inactivates it mainly by glucuronide conjugation. Metabolites leave in the urine and feces. The half-life is about five and a half to eight hours.", "sidefx": "Orthostatic hypotension and dizziness come from alpha-1 blockade, which blunts the normal vessel tightening on standing. Bradycardia, heart block and worsened heart failure come from beta-1 blockade. Bronchospasm comes from beta-2 blockade in sensitive airways. Scalp tingling is a distinctive, harmless side effect. Rare liver injury has been reported with long-term use, so liver tests are checked if symptoms appear. For beta-blocker overdose, glucagon is a classic treatment because it raises cAMP through its own receptor, bypassing the blocked beta receptors.", "fun": "Labetalol has two chiral centers, so it is a mix of four stereoisomers, and different isomers do the alpha and beta blocking.", "src": ["Trandate (labetalol) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's"]}, "lacosamide": {"origin": "Lacosamide came from academic chemistry. In the 1980s and 1990s Harold Kohn and his group at the University of Houston studied a class of molecules called functionalized amino acids and found that some of them protected animals from seizures. Lacosamide emerged as one of the most promising. The German company Schwarz Pharma developed it, and UCB acquired Schwarz in 2006. The FDA approved lacosamide (Vimpat) in 2008 as an add-on treatment for partial-onset seizures. It stood out because it works differently from older sodium channel drugs and comes in IV and oral forms with the same dosing, making it easy to switch in the hospital.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Harold Kohn's lab begins studying functionalized amino acids as anticonvulsants."], ["2006", "UCB acquires Schwarz Pharma, the developer of lacosamide."], ["2008", "FDA approves lacosamide (Vimpat) for partial-onset seizures."]], "cell": "Voltage-gated sodium channels can close in two ways: fast inactivation, which happens within milliseconds of each action potential, and slow inactivation, which builds up over seconds when a neuron stays depolarized or fires repeatedly. Older drugs such as phenytoin and carbamazepine mainly enhance fast inactivation. Lacosamide selectively enhances slow inactivation. Neurons that are firing in long, high-frequency bursts, as in a seizure, spend more time depolarized, so more of their channels slip into the slow-inactivated state and become unavailable. Normal neurons firing briefly are less affected. This reduces sustained repetitive firing and stops seizure spread. The same effect on cardiac sodium channels can slow conduction and prolong the PR interval.", "body": [["Brain", "Fewer available sodium channels in overactive neurons reduce seizure spread and frequency."], ["Heart", "Sodium channel effects on cardiac tissue can prolong the PR interval and cause AV block, especially with other PR-prolonging drugs."], ["Balance and vision", "Dizziness, double vision and unsteady gait come from effects on cerebellar and brainstem neurons."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are common, especially early or with fast increases."]], "journey": "Lacosamide is almost completely absorbed by mouth, so IV and oral doses are interchangeable. Food does not affect absorption. It binds very little to plasma proteins and distributes into body water, crossing the blood-brain barrier easily. About 40 percent leaves the body unchanged in the urine, and much of the rest is demethylated by liver enzymes including CYP2C19 into an inactive metabolite that the kidneys also excrete. The half-life is about 13 hours. It has few drug interactions, but severe kidney or liver disease raises levels.", "sidefx": "Dizziness, double vision, nausea and unsteadiness come from sodium channel effects in the brainstem and cerebellum and are dose-related. Because it also affects cardiac sodium channels, it can prolong the PR interval and cause AV block or atrial arrhythmias, so an ECG is checked in people with heart disease. Like all antiseizure drugs it carries a warning about suicidal thoughts. Rare multiorgan hypersensitivity (DRESS) has been reported. There is no antidote; overdose is managed supportively with cardiac monitoring.", "src": ["Vimpat (lacosamide) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Choi D, Stables JP, Kohn H. J Med Chem 1996"]}, "lactulose": {"origin": "Lactulose is a synthetic sugar made from milk sugar. In 1929 the American chemists Edward Montgomery and C. S. Hudson created it by chemically rearranging lactose. It sat on the shelf for decades. In 1957 the Austrian pediatrician Friedrich Petuely found that it promoted the growth of Lactobacillus bifidus in the gut and proposed it as an infant food additive. In 1966 Johannes Bircher and colleagues reported that lactulose helped patients with hepatic encephalopathy, the confusion caused by ammonia build-up in liver failure. That discovery made lactulose a cornerstone of liver care, and it remains a standard treatment today.", "timeline": [["1929", "Montgomery and Hudson synthesize lactulose from lactose."], ["1957", "Petuely reports lactulose promotes Lactobacillus bifidus in the gut."], ["1966", "Bircher and colleagues use lactulose for hepatic encephalopathy."]], "cell": "Lactulose is a disaccharide of galactose and fructose. Humans have no enzyme in the small intestine that can split it, so it reaches the colon intact. There, colonic bacteria ferment it into short-chain fatty acids such as lactic and acetic acid, plus gas. These acids lower the pH of the colon. In acid, ammonia (NH3), which can cross membranes, picks up a hydrogen ion and becomes ammonium (NH4+), which cannot. Ammonium is trapped in the gut and passed in stool, and ammonia also diffuses from blood into the acidic colon. Lactulose also shifts the gut flora toward less ammonia-producing bacteria. The unabsorbed sugar and its acids hold water in the colon by osmosis, softening stool and speeding transit.", "body": [["Colon", "Fermentation lowers pH, traps ammonia as ammonium and draws water into the bowel, causing soft, frequent stools."], ["Brain", "Lower blood ammonia improves confusion and drowsiness in hepatic encephalopathy."], ["Gut bacteria", "Favors acid-tolerant bacteria and reduces bacteria that make ammonia from protein."], ["Fluids and electrolytes", "Heavy diarrhea can cause dehydration, low potassium and high sodium."]], "journey": "Lactulose is taken by mouth or given as a retention enema. Very little is absorbed, and what little is absorbed is excreted unchanged in the urine. Almost all of it travels to the colon, where bacteria break it down. Because it depends on bacterial fermentation, the laxative effect takes about one to two days for constipation. In hepatic encephalopathy, doses are adjusted until the patient has a target number of soft stools each day.", "sidefx": "Gas, bloating and cramping come from bacterial fermentation, which releases hydrogen and other gases. Diarrhea is the intended osmotic effect, but too much causes dehydration and electrolyte problems. Watery stool loses more water than sodium, so excessive use can cause high sodium levels, which can worsen confusion in liver patients. Lactulose syrup contains small amounts of free galactose and lactose, so caution is used in people on a galactose-restricted diet. There is no antidote; stopping or reducing the drug and replacing fluids corrects the problems.", "fun": "Lactulose is so sweet that it is sometimes mixed with juice, and it is used in a breath test because bacterial fermentation releases hydrogen that can be measured in exhaled air.", "src": ["Enulose (lactulose) FDA label", "Bircher J et al. Lancet 1966", "Goodman & Gilman's"]}, "lamotrigine": {"origin": "Lamotrigine came from a theory that turned out to be wrong. In the 1960s and 1970s researchers noticed that some antiseizure drugs lowered folate levels and suspected that folate itself might promote seizures. Scientists at the Wellcome Foundation in the UK, experts in antifolate drugs, began testing phenyltriazine compounds that blocked folate metabolism. Lamotrigine turned out to be a good anticonvulsant, but its power had little to do with folate. It actually works by blocking sodium channels. The FDA approved lamotrigine (Lamictal) for epilepsy in 1994. In 2003 it was also approved for maintenance treatment of bipolar I disorder, where it is especially good at preventing depressive episodes.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Wellcome scientists study phenyltriazines as possible anticonvulsants."], ["1994", "FDA approves lamotrigine (Lamictal) for epilepsy."], ["2003", "FDA approves lamotrigine for bipolar I maintenance."]], "cell": "Lamotrigine binds voltage-gated sodium channels and stabilizes their inactivated state. The block is use-dependent, so neurons that fire rapidly and repetitively, as in a seizure, are blocked more than neurons firing normally. This stabilizes neuronal membranes and reduces sustained high-frequency firing. Because presynaptic sodium channels help trigger release of the excitatory neurotransmitter glutamate, lamotrigine also lowers glutamate release. It may also block some high-voltage-activated calcium channels. Lower glutamate output is thought to contribute to its mood-stabilizing effect.", "body": [["Brain", "Reduced sodium channel firing and glutamate release decreases seizure frequency and spread, including focal and generalized seizures."], ["Mood", "Helps prevent depressive episodes in bipolar disorder, though it is not effective for acute mania."], ["Skin", "Can cause rash, ranging from mild to life-threatening Stevens-Johnson syndrome or toxic epidermal necrolysis."], ["Balance and vision", "Dizziness, double vision and unsteady gait come from effects on cerebellar and brainstem neurons."], ["Immune system", "Rarely triggers an overactive immune response called hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (HLH)."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth, lamotrigine is almost completely absorbed, with peak levels in a few hours. It distributes widely and enters the brain and breast milk. The liver inactivates it mainly by glucuronidation, not by CYP enzymes. Valproate blocks this glucuronidation and roughly doubles lamotrigine's half-life. Enzyme inducers such as carbamazepine and phenytoin, and estrogen-containing contraceptives, speed its clearance and lower levels. Its usual half-life is about 25 to 33 hours. The inactive glucuronide leaves in the urine.", "sidefx": "Dizziness, double vision, headache and unsteadiness come from sodium channel effects in the brain. The most feared problem is a serious rash, including Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis, which is an immune reaction to the drug. The risk is highest in the first two months, in children, with rapid dose increases and when valproate raises levels. This is why doses are increased slowly and any rash prompts urgent evaluation. Rare aseptic meningitis and HLH also reflect immune activation. There is no specific antidote; the drug is stopped and care is supportive.", "fun": "The slow titration of lamotrigine is board-favorite trivia: it is the main way to lower the risk of a life-threatening rash.", "src": ["Lamictal (lamotrigine) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's"]}, "leucovorin": {"origin": "Leucovorin was discovered as a bacterial growth factor. In 1948 Harry Sauberlich and Carl Baumann at the University of Wisconsin found an unknown substance in liver extracts that the bacterium Leuconostoc citrovorum needed to grow. They called it the citrovorum factor. It turned out to be folinic acid, a reduced and active form of folate. The drug name leucovorin comes from the bacterium's name. In the 1970s oncologists learned they could give very high doses of methotrexate and then rescue healthy cells with leucovorin, a strategy called leucovorin rescue. Later, researchers found that leucovorin also strengthens the anticancer effect of fluorouracil, and the pair became a backbone of colorectal cancer treatment.", "timeline": [["1948", "Sauberlich and Baumann describe the citrovorum factor."], ["1970s", "High-dose methotrexate with leucovorin rescue enters cancer treatment."], ["1980s", "Leucovorin is combined with fluorouracil for colorectal cancer."], ["2008", "FDA approves levoleucovorin (Fusilev), the active isomer alone."]], "cell": "Leucovorin is 5-formyltetrahydrofolate, a folate that is already reduced. Normally, dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR) must convert dietary folate and dihydrofolate into tetrahydrofolate before cells can use it. Methotrexate, trimethoprim and pyrimethamine block DHFR. Leucovorin enters cells through the reduced folate carrier and is converted to 5,10-methylene-tetrahydrofolate and other active folates without needing DHFR. This restores the cofactors needed by thymidylate synthase to make dTMP and by the purine pathway to make AMP and GMP, so DNA synthesis resumes in normal cells. With fluorouracil, the extra 5,10-methylene-tetrahydrofolate locks the drug's active metabolite FdUMP onto thymidylate synthase in a stable ternary complex, deepening enzyme inhibition and boosting cell kill.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Restores DNA synthesis in blood-forming cells, preventing or reversing methotrexate-induced low blood counts."], ["Gut lining", "Rescues rapidly dividing mucosal cells, reducing mouth sores and diarrhea after high-dose methotrexate."], ["Tumor cells", "With fluorouracil, enhances thymidylate synthase inhibition and increases tumor cell death."], ["Blood", "Corrects megaloblastic anemia caused by folate deficiency when oral therapy is not possible."]], "journey": "Leucovorin can be given IV, IM or by mouth. Oral absorption is good at lower doses but becomes saturated at higher doses. In the gut wall and liver it is quickly converted to 5-methyltetrahydrofolate, the main circulating active folate. IV doses act within minutes. The active metabolite has a half-life of about six hours. Folate products leave mainly in the urine. Leucovorin is a mixture of two isomers, and only the levo isomer is active, which is why levoleucovorin products are given at half the amount.", "sidefx": "Leucovorin itself is usually well tolerated. Allergic reactions occur rarely. The main danger is that it amplifies fluorouracil: by stabilizing the FdUMP and thymidylate synthase complex, it can worsen fluorouracil toxicity such as diarrhea, mouth sores and low white counts, especially in older patients. It should never be given intrathecally. In methotrexate overdose it must be given early, because rescue works only before damaged cells die. Glucarpidase, which breaks down methotrexate in the blood, is another option when kidney failure delays methotrexate clearance.", "fun": "The name leucovorin is built from Leuconostoc citrovorum, the bacterium that first revealed it.", "src": ["Leucovorin calcium FDA label", "Fusilev (levoleucovorin) FDA label", "Sauberlich HE, Baumann CA. J Biol Chem 1948"]}, "leuprolide": {"origin": "Leuprolide traces back to the discovery of the brain's master reproductive hormone. In 1971 Andrew Schally's team identified the structure of gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH), a peptide of ten amino acids made in the hypothalamus, and Roger Guillemin's group worked on related hypothalamic hormones. Schally and Guillemin shared the 1977 Nobel Prize. Natural GnRH breaks down in minutes, so chemists built longer-lasting analogs. Leuprolide, developed in Japan by Takeda with Abbott, swaps in a D-amino acid and a modified tail that resist enzymes and bind the receptor more tightly. The FDA approved it for advanced prostate cancer in 1985. Monthly and longer depot injections followed, which made medical castration practical without surgery.", "timeline": [["1971", "Schally's group determines the structure of GnRH."], ["1977", "Schally and Guillemin share the Nobel Prize."], ["1985", "FDA approves leuprolide for advanced prostate cancer."], ["1989", "Lupron Depot monthly injection is approved."]], "cell": "Leuprolide is a synthetic GnRH agonist that binds GnRH receptors on gonadotroph cells in the anterior pituitary. These are Gq-coupled receptors that raise IP3 and calcium and trigger release of LH and FSH. Natural GnRH is released in pulses, which keeps the receptors responsive. Leuprolide gives continuous stimulation instead. At first this causes a surge of LH and FSH, which raises testosterone or estrogen for one to two weeks (the flare). Constant stimulation then makes the receptors uncouple and internalize, and the pituitary stops responding. LH and FSH fall, and the testes or ovaries stop making sex hormones, reaching castrate levels within a few weeks.", "body": [["Pituitary", "After an initial surge, LH and FSH release is shut down by receptor desensitization."], ["Testes and prostate", "Testosterone falls to castrate levels, starving hormone-sensitive prostate cancer cells."], ["Ovaries and uterus", "Estrogen falls, shrinking endometriosis lesions and fibroids and halting early puberty."], ["Bones", "Low sex hormones speed bone loss and raise fracture risk with long-term use."], ["Metabolism and heart", "Androgen deprivation can raise blood sugar, weight and body fat, with increased cardiovascular risk."], ["Brain and skin", "Hot flashes, lower libido and mood changes come from sex hormone withdrawal."]], "journey": "Leuprolide is a peptide, so it would be digested if swallowed and is given by injection. Depot forms use microspheres or a polymer gel that release drug slowly over one to six months. The free drug has a short half-life of about three hours, but the depot keeps a steady supply in the blood. Peptidases break it into smaller inactive peptides, which leave in the urine. Testosterone surges within days and reaches castrate levels in about two to four weeks.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from loss of sex hormones: hot flashes, low libido, erectile dysfunction, fatigue, mood changes, bone loss, weight gain and higher blood sugar. The initial flare is a special danger in prostate cancer with spinal or urinary tract involvement, where the brief testosterone surge can worsen bone pain, compress the spinal cord or block urine flow. An antiandrogen is often started first to prevent this. Androgen deprivation can prolong the QT interval. Rarely, pituitary apoplexy occurs soon after a first dose in people with a pituitary tumor. There is no antidote; effects fade as the depot is used up.", "fun": "Leuprolide shows a board-favorite paradox: the same hormone that stimulates the pituitary in pulses shuts it down when given continuously.", "src": ["Lupron Depot (leuprolide) FDA label", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1977", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "levalbuterol": {"origin": "Levalbuterol is the pure R form of albuterol. Albuterol, also called salbutamol, was developed in the 1960s by David Jack and colleagues at Allen & Hanburys in the UK and became the world's best-known rescue inhaler. Albuterol is a racemic mixture of two mirror-image molecules, R-albuterol and S-albuterol. Only the R form activates beta-2 receptors. Sepracor, a US company focused on single-isomer versions of older drugs, developed R-albuterol alone and named it levalbuterol. The FDA approved the nebulizer solution (Xopenex) in 1999 and the HFA inhaler later. The idea was that removing the S form might reduce side effects, although large studies have shown similar real-world effects to albuterol.", "timeline": [["1960s", "David Jack's team at Allen & Hanburys develops albuterol (salbutamol)."], ["1999", "FDA approves levalbuterol (Xopenex) nebulizer solution."], ["2005", "FDA approves levalbuterol HFA inhaler."]], "cell": "Levalbuterol (R-albuterol) is a selective agonist at beta-2 adrenergic receptors on bronchial smooth muscle. These receptors couple to Gs proteins. Activation stimulates adenylyl cyclase, which raises cyclic AMP. cAMP activates protein kinase A, which phosphorylates and inhibits myosin light chain kinase, lowers intracellular calcium and opens calcium-activated potassium channels. The smooth muscle relaxes, and the airway widens. Beta-2 stimulation on mast cells also reduces release of histamine and other mediators. The S isomer found in racemic albuterol has no useful bronchodilator effect.", "body": [["Lungs", "Bronchial smooth muscle relaxes within minutes, relieving wheeze and shortness of breath in asthma and COPD."], ["Heart", "Some drug reaches cardiac beta receptors, and lower blood pressure from vasodilation triggers reflexes, so heart rate rises and palpitations can occur."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Beta-2 receptors on muscle cause a fine tremor."], ["Blood chemistry", "Beta-2 activation drives potassium into cells through the sodium-potassium pump, lowering blood potassium, and raises blood sugar."]], "journey": "Levalbuterol is inhaled by nebulizer or metered-dose inhaler, so most of its effect is in the airways. Bronchodilation begins within about 5 to 15 minutes and lasts several hours. The portion that is swallowed or absorbed into the blood is metabolized mainly by sulfate conjugation in the liver and gut wall. Its half-life is about three to four hours, and drug and metabolites leave in the urine. In the body, R-albuterol does not convert to the S form.", "sidefx": "Side effects are the same as albuterol because they come from beta-2 stimulation outside the lungs. Tremor comes from skeletal muscle beta-2 receptors. Fast heart rate and palpitations come from direct and reflex cardiac effects. Low potassium comes from potassium moving into cells. Nervousness and headache are common. Paradoxical bronchospasm can rarely occur and requires stopping the drug. In overdose, beta-blockers can reverse effects but must be used with great caution in people with asthma.", "src": ["Xopenex (levalbuterol) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "levetiracetam": {"origin": "Levetiracetam came from the Belgian company UCB, which had earlier made piracetam, a drug marketed in Europe for memory problems. Searching for related compounds, UCB scientists found that levetiracetam, the S enantiomer of a piracetam analog, protected animals from seizures in ways older screening tests had missed. The FDA approved it as Keppra in 1999 for partial-onset seizures. Its target stayed a mystery until 2004, when Berkley Lynch and colleagues at UCB showed it binds synaptic vesicle protein 2A (SV2A). With few drug interactions, an IV form and broad effectiveness, it became one of the most prescribed antiseizure drugs and a common choice in emergency seizure care.", "timeline": [["1999", "FDA approves levetiracetam (Keppra) for partial-onset seizures."], ["2004", "UCB researchers identify SV2A as levetiracetam's binding site."], ["2006", "IV levetiracetam is approved in the US."], ["2015", "Spritam, a levetiracetam tablet, becomes the first FDA-approved 3D-printed drug."]], "cell": "Levetiracetam binds synaptic vesicle protein 2A (SV2A), a membrane protein found on synaptic vesicles in nearly all neurons. SV2A helps regulate how vesicles are primed and how much neurotransmitter they release when calcium enters the nerve terminal. Binding to SV2A appears to reduce vesicle release during intense, high-frequency firing, which limits the burst-like release that lets seizures spread. Levetiracetam also partly inhibits N-type calcium channels and reduces the effects of drugs that block GABA and glycine currents. The result is less neuronal hypersynchrony without blocking normal signaling the way sodium channel drugs do.", "body": [["Brain", "Reduces seizure spread and frequency across focal and generalized seizure types."], ["Mood and behavior", "Can cause irritability, aggression, anxiety, depression and rarely psychosis, a well-known effect sometimes called Keppra rage."], ["Energy level", "Drowsiness, fatigue and dizziness are common, especially at the start."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys clear most of the drug unchanged, so kidney function guides dosing."]], "journey": "Levetiracetam is almost completely absorbed by mouth, and IV and oral forms are interchangeable. Levels peak in about an hour. It binds very little to plasma proteins and passes easily into the brain. It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes. A small part is hydrolyzed in the blood to an inactive acid, and about two-thirds leaves unchanged in the urine. Its half-life is about six to eight hours in adults and longer in kidney disease. Dialysis removes it, so supplemental doses are given after sessions.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness, fatigue and dizziness reflect reduced neuronal signaling. Behavioral and mood changes, including irritability, aggression and depression, are its signature side effects, though the exact cause is not known. Like other antiseizure drugs it carries a warning for suicidal thoughts. Rare serious reactions include Stevens-Johnson syndrome, DRESS, anaphylaxis and low blood counts. Stopping it suddenly can trigger seizures. There is no antidote; overdose mainly causes sleepiness and is managed supportively, and dialysis can remove it.", "src": ["Keppra (levetiracetam) FDA label", "Lynch BA et al. PNAS 2004", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "levofloxacin": {"origin": "Levofloxacin descends from an accident. In 1962 George Lesher and colleagues at Sterling-Winthrop found nalidixic acid as a byproduct while making the antimalarial chloroquine. Adding a fluorine atom later produced the fluoroquinolones, which reached far more bacteria. The Japanese company Daiichi Seiyaku developed ofloxacin and then separated its two mirror-image forms. The left-handed form, levofloxacin, carried almost all the antibacterial activity. It was approved in Japan in 1993 and by the FDA in 1996 as Levaquin. Strong activity against Streptococcus pneumoniae made it a respiratory fluoroquinolone. Over time, reports of tendon rupture, nerve damage and other harms led the FDA to add boxed warnings and limit its use for minor infections.", "timeline": [["1962", "Lesher and colleagues discover nalidixic acid, the first quinolone."], ["1993", "Levofloxacin is approved in Japan."], ["1996", "FDA approves levofloxacin (Levaquin)."], ["2008", "FDA adds a boxed warning for tendinitis and tendon rupture."], ["2016", "FDA advises against fluoroquinolones for uncomplicated infections when alternatives exist."]], "cell": "Bacteria must constantly cut and reseal their DNA to manage supercoiling. DNA gyrase (topoisomerase II) adds negative supercoils ahead of the replication fork, and topoisomerase IV separates linked daughter chromosomes. Levofloxacin binds the enzyme-DNA complex just after the enzyme has cut both strands and blocks the resealing step. The trapped complexes become physical roadblocks to the replication machinery, and double-stranded breaks accumulate. This triggers cell death, so the drug is bactericidal with concentration-dependent killing. In gram-negatives gyrase is the main target, and in gram-positives topoisomerase IV is often the main target. Resistance arises from target mutations, efflux pumps and reduced porins.", "body": [["Lungs", "Reaches high levels in lung tissue and covers pneumococcus and atypical organisms such as Legionella, Mycoplasma and Chlamydophila."], ["Urinary tract", "Concentrated in urine, so it treats complicated urinary and kidney infections."], ["Tendons", "Can damage tendon cells and collagen, causing tendinitis and rupture, most often of the Achilles tendon."], ["Heart", "Blocks hERG potassium channels, prolonging the QT interval and raising torsades risk."], ["Nerves and brain", "Can cause peripheral neuropathy, dizziness, insomnia, confusion and seizures by antagonizing GABA-A receptors."], ["Blood sugar", "Can cause both low and high blood sugar, especially in older adults with diabetes."]], "journey": "Levofloxacin is almost completely absorbed by mouth, so oral and IV doses are equivalent. Antacids, iron, zinc, calcium and magnesium bind it in the gut and block absorption, so dosing is separated. It spreads well into lung, prostate, bone and urine and crosses into the brain to a limited degree. It is barely metabolized and leaves the body largely unchanged in the urine. Its half-life of about six to eight hours supports once-daily dosing, and kidney disease requires adjustment.", "sidefx": "Tendon damage, including rupture, is thought to come from toxic effects on tendon cells and changes in collagen and matrix enzymes. Risk rises with age, corticosteroid use and organ transplants. Peripheral neuropathy can be long-lasting. CNS effects and seizures relate to GABA-A receptor antagonism. QT prolongation comes from hERG potassium channel blockade. It can worsen myasthenia gravis. Dysglycemia and an increased risk of aortic aneurysm or dissection are also warned about. Broad killing of gut flora raises C. difficile risk. It is avoided in children when possible because of joint cartilage injury in young animals. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Levofloxacin and ofloxacin have the same chemical formula; levofloxacin is just the active left-handed half of ofloxacin.", "src": ["Levaquin (levofloxacin) FDA label", "Lesher GY et al. J Med Pharm Chem 1962", "FDA Drug Safety Communication 2016 on fluoroquinolones"]}, "lidocaine": {"origin": "Lidocaine was one of the first modern amide local anesthetics. Cocaine had been the first local anesthetic in the 1880s, but it was toxic and addictive, and later ester drugs such as procaine caused allergies. In 1943 Nils Löfgren and his student Bengt Lundqvist at Stockholm University synthesized a new compound, then called xylocaine, from a series of aniline derivatives. Lundqvist tested it by injecting himself. The Swedish company Astra marketed it in 1948 as Xylocaine, and it quickly became the world's most widely used local anesthetic. In the 1950s and 1960s doctors learned that IV lidocaine could suppress ventricular arrhythmias, giving it a second career as a Class IB antiarrhythmic.", "timeline": [["1943", "Nils Löfgren and Bengt Lundqvist synthesize lidocaine in Stockholm."], ["1948", "Astra markets lidocaine as Xylocaine."], ["1960s", "IV lidocaine becomes standard for ventricular arrhythmias after heart attack."], ["1999", "FDA approves the lidocaine patch (Lidoderm) for postherpetic neuralgia."]], "cell": "Lidocaine blocks voltage-gated sodium channels from the inside. Uncharged molecules cross the nerve membrane, then pick up a proton inside the cell, and the charged form binds a site within the channel pore. It binds best to open and inactivated channels, so it blocks rapidly firing nerves and depolarized tissue most. In nerves, blocking sodium entry prevents action potential spread, so pain signals stop at the site of injection. Small fibers carrying pain and temperature are blocked before large motor fibers. In the heart, Class IB action means fast on and off binding. It acts mainly on ischemic or depolarized ventricular tissue, where channels spend more time inactivated. It shortens the action potential and refractory period, and suppresses abnormal automaticity and reentry with little effect on normal tissue.", "body": [["Nerves", "Local injection or topical use blocks pain conduction, numbing skin, mucosa or a nerve region."], ["Heart", "Suppresses ventricular ectopy and some ventricular arrhythmias, especially in ischemic tissue. It has little effect on atrial tissue."], ["Brain", "Rising blood levels first cause numbness of lips and tongue, tinnitus and lightheadedness, then confusion and seizures."], ["Blood vessels", "Most amides cause mild vasodilation, so epinephrine is often added to keep lidocaine at the site longer."], ["Airway", "Topical lidocaine blunts gag and cough reflexes during procedures."]], "journey": "Lidocaine works by many routes: injection, IV, topical and patch. It is not given by mouth for arrhythmias because the liver destroys most of it on first pass. After an IV bolus it acts within a minute and fades in 10 to 20 minutes as it redistributes, which is why infusions follow. It crosses the blood-brain barrier and placenta. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP1A2 and CYP3A4 into active metabolites, including monoethylglycinexylidide. The half-life is about one and a half to two hours and longer in heart failure or liver disease, because liver blood flow drives clearance. Metabolites leave in the urine.", "sidefx": "Toxicity comes from sodium channel blockade spreading beyond its target. In the brain, early inhibitory neurons are blocked first, causing perioral numbness, metallic taste, tinnitus, agitation and seizures. Higher levels depress the whole brain, causing coma and breathing failure. In the heart, high levels slow conduction and depress contractility, causing bradycardia, hypotension and collapse. This picture is called local anesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST). Treatment includes airway support, benzodiazepines for seizures and IV lipid emulsion, which acts as a sink to pull lidocaine out of tissues. Rarely, topical use causes methemoglobinemia.", "fun": "The original name Xylocaine reflects its chemistry: lidocaine is built from 2,6-xylidine, an aniline derivative.", "src": ["Xylocaine (lidocaine) FDA label", "Lidoderm FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's"]}, "linezolid": {"origin": "Linezolid was the first member of a brand new antibiotic class in decades. Chemists at DuPont discovered the oxazolidinones in the late 1970s and 1980s while studying compounds for plant diseases, and found they could kill bacteria, but early versions were too toxic. In the 1990s scientists at Pharmacia & Upjohn redesigned the class and produced linezolid. The FDA approved it as Zyvox in April 2000. It was the first truly new structural class of antibiotics in about 35 years. Linezolid arrived just as MRSA and vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus (VRE) were spreading, and it gave doctors an oral and IV option for these resistant gram-positive infections.", "timeline": [["1980s", "DuPont scientists report antibacterial oxazolidinones."], ["1990s", "Pharmacia & Upjohn develop linezolid."], ["2000", "FDA approves linezolid (Zyvox), the first oxazolidinone antibiotic."]], "cell": "Linezolid binds the 23S ribosomal RNA in the peptidyl transferase center of the bacterial 50S subunit. This prevents the 50S subunit from joining with the 30S subunit, mRNA and initiator formyl-methionine tRNA to form a working 70S initiation complex. Without that complex, protein synthesis cannot start. Because this step differs from where other protein synthesis inhibitors act, cross-resistance is rare. It is bacteriostatic against staphylococci and enterococci and bactericidal against most streptococci. Resistance comes mainly from mutations in 23S rRNA or the cfr methyltransferase gene. Linezolid also weakly and reversibly inhibits human monoamine oxidase, and it inhibits human mitochondrial ribosomes, which resemble bacterial ones.", "body": [["Infection sites", "Treats MRSA pneumonia and skin infections and VRE infections, reaching lung lining fluid, skin and soft tissue well."], ["Bone marrow", "Mitochondrial toxicity suppresses blood cell production, especially platelets, with courses longer than two weeks."], ["Nerves and eyes", "Long courses can cause peripheral neuropathy and optic neuropathy from mitochondrial damage."], ["Brain and serotonin", "Weak MAO inhibition can raise serotonin and, with SSRIs or other serotonergic drugs, cause serotonin syndrome."], ["Metabolism", "Mitochondrial dysfunction can cause lactic acidosis, and low blood sugar has occurred in diabetics."]], "journey": "Linezolid is completely absorbed by mouth, so oral and IV doses are the same. Levels peak in one to two hours. It is only about 30 percent protein bound and spreads well into tissues, including lung lining fluid and bone, and modestly into cerebrospinal fluid. It is broken down by nonenzymatic oxidation of its morpholine ring, not by CYP enzymes, into two inactive acids. These and some unchanged drug leave mainly in the urine. Its half-life is about five hours.", "sidefx": "Myelosuppression, especially low platelets, plus neuropathy, optic damage and lactic acidosis all share one root: human mitochondrial ribosomes look like bacterial ribosomes, and linezolid blocks protein synthesis there too. Risk climbs with longer courses, so blood counts are watched weekly. Its weak MAO inhibition explains serotonin syndrome with serotonergic drugs and blood pressure spikes with tyramine-rich foods or pseudoephedrine. Diarrhea, including C. difficile, comes from disruption of gut flora. There is no antidote; stopping the drug usually reverses marrow effects, though neuropathy may persist.", "src": ["Zyvox (linezolid) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's"]}, "liothyronine": {"origin": "Liothyronine is synthetic T3, the most active thyroid hormone. For decades after thyroxine (T4) was isolated in 1914, scientists thought it was the only thyroid hormone. In 1952 Jack Gross and Rosalind Pitt-Rivers at the National Institute for Medical Research in London identified a second, more potent hormone in blood and thyroid tissue: 3,5,3'-triiodothyronine, or T3. A French team led by Jean Roche reported the same hormone independently that year. Synthetic T3 was soon developed as a drug and sold as Cytomel in the 1950s. An IV form, Triostat, was later approved for myxedema coma. Today levothyroxine is the main treatment for hypothyroidism, and liothyronine is used in special situations where a fast-acting hormone is wanted.", "timeline": [["1914", "Edward Kendall isolates thyroxine (T4)."], ["1952", "Gross and Pitt-Rivers identify triiodothyronine (T3)."], ["1950s", "Synthetic T3 (Cytomel) becomes available."]], "cell": "Liothyronine is T3, the active form of thyroid hormone, so it does not need deiodinase conversion the way T4 does. T3 enters cells through transporters such as MCT8 and moves into the nucleus. There it binds thyroid hormone receptors (TR-alpha and TR-beta), which are already sitting on thyroid response elements in DNA paired with the retinoid X receptor. Binding releases corepressors and recruits coactivators, turning on gene transcription. Targets include Na-K-ATPase, mitochondrial enzymes, cardiac myosin heavy chain alpha, phospholamban and beta-adrenergic receptors. These raise oxygen use, heat production, heart rate and contractility. T3 also feeds back on the pituitary to lower TSH.", "body": [["Metabolism", "Raises metabolic rate, oxygen use and heat production quickly, within hours to days."], ["Heart", "Increases heart rate and contractility; too much can trigger angina, arrhythmias or heart attack."], ["Brain", "Improves slowed thinking and fatigue, and is used in severe hypothyroid states such as myxedema coma."], ["Pituitary", "Suppresses TSH, which is used in some thyroid cancer and diagnostic protocols."], ["Bones", "Excess hormone speeds bone turnover and can cause bone loss over time."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth, liothyronine is almost completely absorbed, and levels peak in a few hours. It is less tightly bound to plasma proteins than T4, which is one reason it acts faster and wears off sooner. The liver and other tissues remove iodine from it and conjugate it, and products leave in urine and bile. Its half-life is about one to two and a half days, much shorter than T4's week. Levels rise and fall more over the day than with levothyroxine, so it is not the usual choice for routine replacement. An IV form is used in myxedema coma.", "sidefx": "Side effects come from too much thyroid hormone action. Extra beta-adrenergic signaling and metabolism cause palpitations, tremor, nervousness, sweating, heat intolerance and weight loss. Because T3 works quickly and peaks sharply, it is more likely than T4 to strain the heart, causing angina, atrial fibrillation or heart attack in older or cardiac patients. Long-term excess can cause bone loss. The label warns against use for weight loss. Treatment of overdose is supportive, and beta-blockers control sympathetic symptoms.", "fun": "Rosalind Pitt-Rivers was one of the few women working at the top of endocrine chemistry in the 1950s and was later elected a Fellow of the Royal Society.", "src": ["Cytomel (liothyronine) FDA label", "Triostat FDA label", "Gross J, Pitt-Rivers R. Lancet 1952"]}, "lisdexamfetamine": {"origin": "Lisdexamfetamine was designed to make an old stimulant harder to misuse. Amphetamine was first synthesized in 1887 by the Romanian chemist Lazar Edeleanu in Berlin and became a medicine in the 1930s. Dextroamphetamine is effective for ADHD but can be misused by snorting or injection. The Virginia company New River Pharmaceuticals attached the amino acid lysine to dextroamphetamine, creating an inactive prodrug that must be cut apart in the body. The FDA approved it as Vyvanse in 2007, and Shire acquired it soon after. In 2015 it became the first drug approved by the FDA for moderate to severe binge eating disorder in adults.", "timeline": [["1887", "Lazar Edeleanu first synthesizes amphetamine."], ["2007", "FDA approves lisdexamfetamine (Vyvanse) for ADHD."], ["2015", "FDA approves Vyvanse for binge eating disorder, the first drug for that condition."]], "cell": "Lisdexamfetamine itself is inactive. After absorption, enzymes in red blood cells cleave the peptide bond between lysine and dextroamphetamine, releasing active d-amphetamine. Dextroamphetamine enters dopamine and norepinephrine nerve terminals through the dopamine transporter (DAT) and norepinephrine transporter (NET). Inside, it disrupts the vesicular monoamine transporter 2 (VMAT2) and the pH gradient of storage vesicles, so neurotransmitter leaks into the cytoplasm. It then makes DAT and NET run in reverse, pumping dopamine and norepinephrine out into the synapse. It also blocks their reuptake and weakly inhibits monoamine oxidase. More dopamine and norepinephrine in the prefrontal cortex and striatum improves attention and impulse control.", "body": [["Brain", "Raised dopamine and norepinephrine in prefrontal circuits improve focus, attention and impulse control, and reduce binge eating."], ["Heart and vessels", "Norepinephrine release raises heart rate and blood pressure."], ["Appetite and weight", "Central effects suppress appetite, which can slow weight gain and growth in children."], ["Sleep", "Increased arousal can cause trouble falling asleep, especially if taken late in the day."], ["Mouth and skin", "Sympathetic activation causes dry mouth and sometimes sweating."]], "journey": "Lisdexamfetamine is taken by mouth and absorbed in the small intestine by peptide transporters. Red blood cells convert it to dextroamphetamine. The prodrug has a half-life under an hour, while dextroamphetamine lasts about 10 to 12 hours. Because conversion happens in the blood and is rate-limited, onset is smooth, effects last most of the day, and snorting or injecting does not produce a faster high. Dextroamphetamine crosses the blood-brain barrier and placenta and leaves mainly in the urine. Acidic urine speeds its excretion, and alkaline urine slows it.", "sidefx": "Side effects reflect increased dopamine and norepinephrine activity. Sympathetic stimulation causes fast heart rate, higher blood pressure and, rarely, arrhythmias or sudden cardiac events in people with heart disease. Central arousal causes insomnia, anxiety and irritability. Appetite suppression causes weight loss and possible growth slowing. High doses can cause psychosis or mania. It carries a boxed warning for misuse and addiction because dopamine release in reward pathways reinforces use, even though its prodrug design lowers abuse potential. Combination with MAO inhibitors is dangerous. There is no specific antidote; overdose is treated supportively with benzodiazepines for agitation.", "fun": "The lis in lisdexamfetamine comes from lysine, the amino acid that is attached to dextroamphetamine to make the prodrug.", "src": ["Vyvanse (lisdexamfetamine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "lithium": {"origin": "Lithium is a simple element, the lightest metal, discovered in 1817 by the Swedish chemist Johan August Arfwedson. In the 1800s lithium salts were promoted for gout and many other ailments, and lithium waters were popular. Its psychiatric story began in 1949, when the Australian psychiatrist John Cade gave lithium salts to guinea pigs and noticed they became calm. He then treated patients with mania, and several improved dramatically. In the 1950s and 1960s the Danish psychiatrist Mogens Schou ran careful studies showing lithium treated mania and prevented relapse. US approval was delayed, partly because lithium chloride sold as a salt substitute had poisoned heart patients in the late 1940s. The FDA approved lithium for mania in 1970.", "timeline": [["1817", "Johan August Arfwedson discovers lithium."], ["1949", "John Cade reports lithium for mania in Australia."], ["1954", "Mogens Schou publishes a controlled trial of lithium in mania."], ["1970", "FDA approves lithium for acute mania."]], "cell": "Lithium is a small cation that the body handles somewhat like sodium, and its exact mood-stabilizing mechanism is still not fully known. Two leading targets are well established. First, it inhibits inositol monophosphatase and other enzymes that recycle inositol. Cells that fire heavily through Gq-coupled receptors use up their inositol and cannot rebuild PIP2, so overactive phosphoinositide signaling is damped (the inositol depletion hypothesis). Second, it inhibits glycogen synthase kinase-3 (GSK-3), an enzyme involved in neuroplasticity, circadian rhythm and cell survival. Lithium also alters G-protein and adenylyl cyclase signaling and has neuroprotective effects. In the kidney it enters collecting duct cells through ENaC channels and blocks ADH-driven aquaporin insertion.", "body": [["Brain", "Stabilizes mood, treats mania, prevents both manic and depressive relapses and lowers suicide risk."], ["Kidneys", "Interferes with ADH signaling in the collecting duct, causing frequent urination, thirst and sometimes nephrogenic diabetes insipidus."], ["Thyroid", "Blocks thyroid hormone release and can cause goiter and hypothyroidism."], ["Parathyroid", "Can raise parathyroid hormone and blood calcium."], ["Heart", "Can flatten T waves and cause sinus node dysfunction or bradycardia."], ["Nerves and muscles", "A fine hand tremor is common; coarse tremor and unsteadiness suggest toxicity."]], "journey": "Lithium is taken by mouth and absorbed almost completely in the gut. It is not bound to proteins and not metabolized at all. It distributes through total body water and enters the brain slowly, which is one reason mood benefits take weeks. The kidneys filter it, and about 80 percent is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule along with sodium. When the body is low on sodium or water, the kidney reabsorbs more lithium, and levels rise. Its half-life is about 18 to 36 hours. Thiazides, ACE inhibitors, ARBs and NSAIDs all reduce lithium clearance.", "sidefx": "Lithium has a narrow therapeutic index, so blood levels are monitored. Polyuria and thirst come from blocked ADH signaling in the collecting duct. Hypothyroidism comes from impaired thyroid hormone release. Tremor, nausea and weight gain are common. Toxicity often follows dehydration or interacting drugs, because the kidney then holds onto lithium. Toxicity causes vomiting, diarrhea, coarse tremor, confusion, ataxia, seizures and arrhythmias. It is teratogenic and linked to Ebstein anomaly. There is no antidote. Treatment is IV fluids, and hemodialysis for severe cases. Activated charcoal does not bind lithium.", "fun": "The soft drink 7 Up started out in 1929 as Bib-Label Lithiated Lemon-Lime Soda and contained lithium citrate until around 1950.", "src": ["Lithium carbonate FDA label", "Cade JFJ. Med J Aust 1949", "Goodman & Gilman's"]}, "lorazepam": {"origin": "Lorazepam belongs to the benzodiazepine family, which began with a lucky find. In 1955 Leo Sternbach at Hoffmann-La Roche in New Jersey made a compound that sat untested on a shelf for two years. When it was finally screened, it calmed animals without heavy sedation. It became chlordiazepoxide (Librium) in 1960, followed by diazepam (Valium) in 1963. Many companies then built their own benzodiazepines. Wyeth developed lorazepam and introduced it in the US as Ativan in the 1970s. Its short metabolic path, without active metabolites, and its reliable IM and IV effects made it a favorite for anxiety, status epilepticus and sedation.", "timeline": [["1955", "Leo Sternbach synthesizes the first benzodiazepine at Hoffmann-La Roche."], ["1960", "Chlordiazepoxide (Librium) is marketed."], ["1970s", "Wyeth introduces lorazepam (Ativan) in the US."]], "cell": "GABA-A receptors are chloride channels built from five subunits. GABA binds between alpha and beta subunits and opens the channel. Lorazepam binds a separate site between an alpha subunit (alpha 1, 2, 3 or 5) and a gamma 2 subunit. It is a positive allosteric modulator. It does not open the channel by itself, but it increases the receptor's affinity for GABA and the frequency of channel opening. More chloride flows in, the neuron hyperpolarizes and becomes harder to fire. Alpha 1 subunits mediate sedation and amnesia, alpha 2 and 3 mediate anxiety relief and muscle relaxation, and the anticonvulsant effect is broad. Because it needs GABA to work, it has a ceiling effect when used alone, unlike barbiturates.", "body": [["Brain", "Calms anxiety, causes sleepiness and amnesia, and stops seizures by boosting inhibition."], ["Seizures", "First-line for status epilepticus because it rapidly enhances GABA inhibition across the cortex."], ["Breathing", "Mild alone, but combined with opioids or alcohol it can cause serious breathing depression."], ["Muscles", "Spinal GABA-A effects relax skeletal muscle."], ["Heart and vessels", "Mild drop in blood pressure, more with IV doses or in volume-depleted patients."]], "journey": "Lorazepam is well absorbed by mouth, by IM injection and under the tongue, and it can be given IV. It is less fat-soluble than diazepam, so it enters the brain a bit more slowly but stays in the brain longer, which gives a longer anticonvulsant effect. It crosses the placenta. The liver conjugates it directly with glucuronic acid, a step that does not use CYP enzymes and is relatively spared in older adults and liver disease. There are no active metabolites. Its half-life is about 10 to 20 hours, and the glucuronide leaves in the urine.", "sidefx": "Sedation, dizziness, weakness and unsteadiness come from enhanced GABA inhibition. Respiratory depression is the main danger, especially with opioids, alcohol or other sedatives, and this combination carries a boxed warning. Paradoxical agitation occurs in some children and older adults. Anterograde amnesia is common. Long-term use causes tolerance and dependence, and abrupt stopping can cause withdrawal seizures. The IV form contains propylene glycol, which in prolonged infusions can cause metabolic acidosis and kidney injury. Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepine effects at the receptor but can trigger seizures in dependent patients.", "src": ["Ativan (lorazepam) FDA label", "Sternbach LH. J Med Chem 1979", "Goodman & Gilman's"]}, "lovastatin": {"origin": "Lovastatin was the first statin approved anywhere, and it came from a fungus. In the early 1970s the Japanese biochemist Akira Endo at Sankyo screened thousands of fungal cultures, reasoning that fungi might make chemicals to block cholesterol synthesis in competing microbes. He found mevastatin (compactin) in Penicillium citrinum, but its development stalled. In 1978 researchers at Merck, led by Alfred Alberts and working under Roy Vagelos, isolated a similar compound from Aspergillus terreus, which became lovastatin. The FDA approved it as Mevacor in 1987. It was the first drug to directly target the rate-limiting step of cholesterol synthesis and launched one of the most important drug classes in cardiology.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Akira Endo discovers mevastatin in Penicillium citrinum at Sankyo."], ["1978", "Merck scientists isolate lovastatin from Aspergillus terreus."], ["1987", "FDA approves lovastatin (Mevacor), the first statin."]], "cell": "Lovastatin is a lactone prodrug. In the body its ring is opened to the active beta-hydroxy acid, which closely resembles the HMG-CoA intermediate. This acid competitively inhibits HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting enzyme that turns HMG-CoA into mevalonate in the cholesterol pathway. With less cholesterol made inside liver cells, the transcription factor SREBP-2 is activated. SREBP-2 increases production of LDL receptors on the liver cell surface. More LDL receptors pull more LDL out of the blood, so LDL cholesterol falls. Lower mevalonate also reduces isoprenoid intermediates, which contributes to pleiotropic effects such as plaque stabilization and reduced inflammation.", "body": [["Liver", "Cholesterol synthesis falls and LDL receptors increase, so the liver clears more LDL from the blood."], ["Blood lipids", "LDL cholesterol falls substantially, triglycerides fall modestly and HDL rises slightly."], ["Arteries", "Lower LDL and less inflammation slow plaque growth and make plaques more stable, lowering heart attack and stroke risk."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Can cause muscle aches and, rarely, muscle breakdown (rhabdomyolysis)."]], "journey": "Lovastatin is taken by mouth, ideally with the evening meal, because food improves absorption and cholesterol synthesis peaks overnight. Less than 5 percent reaches the blood as active drug because the gut wall and liver remove most of it on first pass, mainly through CYP3A4. This targets the drug to the liver, where it works. The lactone is hydrolyzed to its active acid. Both forms are highly protein bound. Elimination is mostly in bile and feces. The half-life is short, about one to two hours, but the effect on LDL lasts because enzyme inhibition and receptor changes persist. Grapefruit juice and strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels.", "sidefx": "Statin myopathy, ranging from aches to rhabdomyolysis, is thought to relate to reduced production of mevalonate products in muscle, such as coenzyme Q10 and prenylated proteins. Risk rises with high blood levels, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as clarithromycin and itraconazole, and drugs like gemfibrozil, are avoided or restricted. Liver enzyme elevation reflects drug effects in liver cells. Statins slightly raise the risk of diabetes. They are avoided in pregnancy because cholesterol is needed for fetal development. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped and fluids are given for rhabdomyolysis.", "fun": "Red yeast rice contains monacolin K, which is chemically identical to lovastatin, so some supplements act like a low-dose statin.", "src": ["Mevacor (lovastatin) FDA label", "Endo A. J Lipid Res 1992 (history of statins)", "Alberts AW et al. PNAS 1980"]}, "magnesium-hydroxide": {"origin": "Magnesium hydroxide is an old mineral remedy. Magnesium-containing salts and earths have been used as purgatives and antacids since at least the 1600s. In the 1870s the American chemist Charles Henry Phillips patented a stable suspension of magnesium hydroxide in water, which he sold as Phillips' Milk of Magnesia. The name came from its milky white color. It became one of the best-known household remedies in the US for constipation and heartburn. Today magnesium hydroxide is still sold alone and is also combined with aluminum hydroxide in antacids such as Maalox and Mylanta, where the two balance each other's effects on the bowels.", "timeline": [["1870s", "Charles Henry Phillips patents and sells Phillips' Milk of Magnesia."], ["20th century", "Magnesium hydroxide is combined with aluminum hydroxide in antacid products."]], "cell": "Magnesium hydroxide works through simple chemistry rather than a receptor. As an antacid, its hydroxide reacts with hydrochloric acid in the stomach to form magnesium chloride and water, raising gastric pH. Less acid means less irritation of the esophagus and stomach lining and less pepsin activity. As a laxative, the magnesium ions that reach the intestine are poorly absorbed. They stay in the lumen and hold water by osmosis, increasing the volume and water content of stool. The swelling of the bowel stretches its wall and stimulates peristalsis. Magnesium may also trigger release of cholecystokinin, which increases intestinal secretion and motility.", "body": [["Stomach", "Neutralizes acid within minutes, relieving heartburn and indigestion."], ["Intestines", "Unabsorbed magnesium draws water into the bowel, causing a watery bowel movement within hours."], ["Kidneys", "The small amount of absorbed magnesium is cleared by the kidneys; kidney failure can let it build up."], ["Heart and nerves", "In severe magnesium build-up, low blood pressure, slow heart rate, weakness and loss of reflexes can occur."]], "journey": "Magnesium hydroxide is taken by mouth. In the stomach it neutralizes acid, and the antacid effect lasts longer when taken after meals. Only a small fraction of the magnesium, up to about 15 to 30 percent, is absorbed in the small intestine. The absorbed magnesium is filtered and excreted by the kidneys. The rest stays in the gut, where it acts as an osmotic laxative and leaves in the feces. The laxative effect usually appears within 30 minutes to six hours.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea and cramping are the direct result of the osmotic effect, and heavy use can cause dehydration and electrolyte loss. Because the kidneys clear absorbed magnesium, people with kidney failure can develop hypermagnesemia. Magnesium blocks calcium channels and acetylcholine release, so high levels cause flushing, low blood pressure, weakness, loss of deep tendon reflexes, breathing depression and cardiac arrest. IV calcium gluconate or chloride counters magnesium toxicity at the heart and neuromuscular junction, and dialysis removes it. Antacids also bind some drugs, such as tetracyclines and fluoroquinolones, reducing their absorption.", "fun": "Antacids pair magnesium hydroxide, which loosens stool, with aluminum hydroxide, which constipates, so the two side effects tend to cancel out.", "src": ["Milk of Magnesia drug facts label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "magnesium-sulfate": {"origin": "Magnesium sulfate is Epsom salt. In the early 1600s, according to local tradition, a farmer in Epsom, England, noticed that his cows refused to drink from a bitter spring. The water was found to soothe skin and act as a strong laxative, and Epsom became a spa town. In 1695 the English physician Nehemiah Grew described the salt and how to extract it. Its use in eclampsia began in the early 1900s, and in the 1920s doctors began giving it IV to stop eclamptic seizures. Large trials settled its place: the Collaborative Eclampsia Trial in 1995 showed it beat phenytoin and diazepam for preventing repeat seizures, and the Magpie Trial in 2002 showed it halved the risk of eclampsia in preeclampsia.", "timeline": [["c. 1618", "Bitter spring water at Epsom, England, becomes known for its effects."], ["1695", "Nehemiah Grew describes Epsom salt."], ["1920s", "IV magnesium sulfate is used to treat eclampsia."], ["1995", "Collaborative Eclampsia Trial shows magnesium is best for eclamptic seizures."], ["2002", "Magpie Trial shows magnesium halves eclampsia risk in preeclampsia."]], "cell": "Magnesium acts as a natural calcium antagonist. At the neuromuscular junction it competes with calcium at presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels, so less acetylcholine is released and the muscle end plate is less sensitive. In smooth muscle it blocks calcium entry and competes for intracellular binding sites, causing relaxation of bronchial, vascular and uterine muscle. In the brain it blocks the NMDA receptor channel in a voltage-dependent way, which raises the seizure threshold. In the heart it is a cofactor for the sodium-potassium ATPase and blocks L-type calcium channels, slowing conduction and suppressing early afterdepolarizations, the trigger for torsades de pointes. It also causes cerebral vasodilation, which may help in eclampsia.", "body": [["Brain", "Prevents and stops eclamptic seizures, likely through NMDA blockade and relief of cerebral vasospasm."], ["Heart", "Suppresses early afterdepolarizations and terminates torsades de pointes; corrects arrhythmias caused by low magnesium."], ["Lungs", "Relaxes bronchial smooth muscle, which helps in severe asthma attacks."], ["Blood vessels", "Vasodilation causes flushing, warmth and a drop in blood pressure."], ["Muscles and reflexes", "Less acetylcholine release weakens muscles; loss of deep tendon reflexes is an early sign of toxic levels."], ["Uterus", "Relaxes uterine muscle; it is used for fetal brain protection before very early delivery."]], "journey": "Magnesium sulfate is given IV or IM for acute care. IV doses act almost immediately, and IM doses act within about an hour. Magnesium distributes through extracellular fluid and bone, and crosses the placenta, so the newborn may be drowsy or weak. It is not metabolized. The kidneys filter and excrete it, so reduced kidney function raises levels and requires close monitoring. Clinical monitoring uses reflexes, breathing rate, urine output and blood levels.", "sidefx": "Flushing, warmth, sweating and low blood pressure come from vasodilation. Rising levels follow a predictable pattern: loss of deep tendon reflexes, then respiratory depression, then heart block and cardiac arrest. This reflects magnesium blocking calcium-dependent nerve, muscle and heart conduction. Kidney failure is the main risk factor because it is cleared by the kidneys. The antidote is IV calcium gluconate or calcium chloride, which directly counters magnesium at the cell membrane. Diuretics or dialysis remove excess magnesium.", "fun": "Checking the patellar reflex is a classic bedside test during magnesium therapy because reflexes disappear before breathing is affected.", "src": ["Magnesium sulfate injection FDA label", "Eclampsia Trial Collaborative Group. Lancet 1995", "Magpie Trial Collaborative Group. Lancet 2002"]}, "mannitol": {"origin": "Mannitol is a sugar alcohol named after manna, the sweet dried sap of the manna ash tree, Fraxinus ornus, which grows around the Mediterranean. In the early 1800s the French chemist Joseph Louis Proust isolated mannitol from manna. It occurs naturally in many plants, seaweeds and fungi. Doctors began using it as a diuretic in the mid-twentieth century, and in the early 1960s neurosurgeons showed that IV mannitol could lower raised pressure inside the skull. It became a standard tool for brain swelling and acute glaucoma. Today it is also used as a sweetener in sugar-free foods and as an inhaled powder to test for asthma.", "timeline": [["1806", "Joseph Louis Proust isolates mannitol from manna."], ["1960s", "IV mannitol is used to lower intracranial pressure."]], "cell": "Mannitol has no receptor. It works by osmosis. Given IV, it stays in the blood and extracellular fluid and does not enter cells or cross an intact blood-brain barrier. This raises plasma osmolality and pulls water out of cells, including brain cells and the eye, into the blood. In the kidney, mannitol is freely filtered at the glomerulus but not reabsorbed. Its presence in the tubular fluid holds water in the proximal tubule and the thin descending limb of the loop of Henle, opposing water reabsorption. More water and some sodium stay in the tubule, so urine output rises sharply. The expanded plasma volume and reduced blood viscosity can also improve blood flow in the brain.", "body": [["Brain", "Draws water out of brain tissue across an intact blood-brain barrier, lowering intracranial pressure within minutes."], ["Eyes", "Pulls fluid out of the eye, lowering intraocular pressure in acute angle-closure glaucoma."], ["Kidneys", "Causes a large-volume osmotic diuresis with water loss greater than sodium loss."], ["Blood volume", "First expands plasma volume as water moves in from cells, which can overload the heart and lungs."], ["Electrolytes", "Early dilution can lower sodium, and later water loss can raise sodium and lower potassium."]], "journey": "Mannitol is given IV because it is poorly absorbed by mouth, where it acts as a laxative. It stays in the extracellular space. Intracranial pressure begins to fall within 15 to 30 minutes, with peak effect in about 30 to 60 minutes. It is barely metabolized. The kidneys filter and excrete it unchanged, with a half-life of about one to two hours in normal kidney function and much longer in kidney failure. Concentrated solutions can crystallize and are given through a filter.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow from fluid shifts. The first rush of water into the blood can cause fluid overload, pulmonary edema and heart failure in patients with weak hearts. Later, the heavy diuresis causes dehydration, low potassium, high sodium and low blood pressure. In kidney failure mannitol builds up, raising osmolality and possibly causing acute kidney injury. If the blood-brain barrier is damaged, mannitol can leak into brain tissue and pull water back in, causing rebound brain swelling. Treatment of complications is supportive, and dialysis removes it.", "fun": "Manna ash trees in Sicily are still cut by hand to collect the sweet sap that gave mannitol its name.", "src": ["Osmitrol (mannitol) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "mebendazole": {"origin": "Mebendazole was created by Janssen Pharmaceutica in Belgium, the same company that made loperamide and haloperidol. The benzimidazole class of anthelmintics began with thiabendazole, introduced by Merck in the early 1960s, mostly for animals. In the early 1970s Janssen scientists developed mebendazole as a broad-spectrum, poorly absorbed drug that acts mainly in the gut. It was introduced as Vermox and became one of the most widely used treatments for pinworm, roundworm, whipworm and hookworm. It is on the WHO Model List of Essential Medicines and is used in mass deworming of schoolchildren. In 2016 the FDA approved a chewable tablet called Emverm.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Thiabendazole introduces the benzimidazole class of anthelmintics."], ["1970s", "Janssen develops mebendazole (Vermox)."], ["2016", "FDA approves mebendazole chewable tablets (Emverm)."]], "cell": "Mebendazole binds beta-tubulin, the building block of microtubules, in the worm's intestinal cells. Binding blocks the polymerization of tubulin into microtubules. Worm intestinal cells depend on microtubules to move secretory vesicles and to take up glucose. Without functioning microtubules, the worm cannot absorb glucose, and it uses up its glycogen stores. ATP production falls, and the worm becomes immobile and dies slowly, then is passed in stool. Mebendazole binds parasite tubulin much more tightly than human tubulin, which explains its selectivity. Eggs are also affected.", "body": [["Intestinal worms", "Kills pinworms, roundworms, whipworms and hookworms by cutting off their glucose supply."], ["Gut", "Dying worms and local effects may cause abdominal pain, diarrhea or gas."], ["Bone marrow", "With long courses at high levels, can rarely suppress white blood cells."], ["Liver", "Long, high-exposure courses can raise liver enzymes, so liver tests are checked."]], "journey": "Mebendazole is taken by mouth and should be chewed. Very little is absorbed because it is poorly soluble and most absorbed drug is removed by the liver on first pass. That keeps most of the drug in the intestine where the worms live. A fatty meal increases absorption. The small absorbed amount is highly protein bound and metabolized in the liver. Its half-life in the blood is about three to six hours. Most of the dose leaves unchanged in the feces, with a small amount of metabolites in the urine.", "sidefx": "Short courses are well tolerated because so little drug is absorbed. Abdominal pain, gas and diarrhea are the most common effects, partly from dying worms. Because the drug targets microtubules, high exposure in long courses, as once used for hydatid disease, can affect rapidly dividing human cells, causing low white counts, liver enzyme elevation and hair loss. Rare serious reactions include agranulocytosis and Stevens-Johnson syndrome or toxic epidermal necrolysis, with reports of the skin reactions when combined with metronidazole. It is generally avoided in early pregnancy because microtubule drugs can harm a developing embryo. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Emverm (mebendazole) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "WHO Model List of Essential Medicines"]}, "medroxyprogesterone": {"origin": "Medroxyprogesterone acetate is a synthetic progestin developed by the Upjohn Company in the 1950s, during a wave of research into hormones that could be given by mouth or injection. Upjohn introduced oral Provera in 1959 for gynecologic problems such as abnormal bleeding. A long-acting injection, Depo-Provera, was used for cancer and later studied as a contraceptive. Its road to approval for birth control in the US was long and controversial, with the FDA declining it in the 1970s over animal safety concerns while it was used in many other countries. The FDA finally approved Depo-Provera for contraception in 1992. A lower-dose subcutaneous version followed in 2004.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Upjohn develops medroxyprogesterone acetate."], ["1959", "Oral Provera is introduced."], ["1992", "FDA approves Depo-Provera as an injectable contraceptive."], ["2004", "FDA approves depo-subQ provera 104 and adds a boxed warning on bone loss."]], "cell": "Medroxyprogesterone acetate is an agonist at progesterone receptors, which are nuclear receptors that act as transcription factors. When the drug binds, the receptor moves to the nucleus, binds progesterone response elements on DNA and changes gene expression. In the hypothalamus, constant progestin slows the pulse frequency of GnRH release. In the pituitary, this blunts the mid-cycle LH surge, so ovulation does not occur. In the cervix, mucus becomes thick and hostile to sperm. In the uterus, it converts the estrogen-primed endometrium to a secretory and then thin, inactive state that does not support implantation. It also binds glucocorticoid receptors and has weak androgenic activity.", "body": [["Ovaries", "Ovulation is blocked because the LH surge is suppressed, providing contraception."], ["Uterus", "The lining becomes thin and inactive, which causes irregular bleeding at first and often no periods after about a year."], ["Cervix", "Cervical mucus becomes thick and scant, which blocks sperm from entering the uterus."], ["Bones", "Low estrogen from ovarian suppression causes bone mineral density loss with long-term use."], ["Metabolism", "Weight gain is common, and glucocorticoid-like activity can affect glucose."], ["Brain", "Headache and mood changes, including depression, can occur."]], "journey": "The contraceptive is given by deep IM or subcutaneous injection every three months. The drug forms a depot in the muscle or fat and is released slowly. Levels peak in about three weeks. The half-life of the IM form is about 50 days. It is highly protein bound, mostly to albumin. The liver metabolizes it through CYP3A4, and conjugates leave mainly in the urine. Because of the slow release, its effect on fertility can last many months after the last injection. Oral Provera has a half-life of about half a day.", "sidefx": "Irregular bleeding and spotting happen because the endometrium becomes thin and unstable. Many people stop having periods after a year. Bone loss happens because the drug suppresses ovarian estrogen, and estrogen protects bone. This led to a boxed warning, and use beyond two years is limited unless other methods are unsuitable. Weight gain and mood changes are common. Return of fertility can be delayed for many months because the depot takes time to clear. It does not protect against sexually transmitted infections. There is no antidote; effects fade as the depot is used up.", "src": ["Depo-Provera CI FDA label", "depo-subQ provera 104 FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's"]}, "meloxicam": {"origin": "Meloxicam belongs to the oxicam family of NSAIDs. The first oxicam, piroxicam, was developed by Pfizer in the 1970s and was notable for its long half-life. In the 1980s, the German company Boehringer Ingelheim developed meloxicam as a related drug. In the early 1990s scientists discovered that there are two cyclooxygenase enzymes, COX-1, which protects the stomach, and COX-2, which drives inflammation. Meloxicam turned out to prefer COX-2 at lower doses, which promised fewer stomach problems. It was launched in Europe in the 1990s and approved by the FDA as Mobic in 2000 for osteoarthritis. Later approvals added rheumatoid arthritis and juvenile arthritis, and an IV form was approved in 2020.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Pfizer develops piroxicam, the first oxicam NSAID."], ["1991", "Researchers identify COX-2 as a separate inducible enzyme."], ["2000", "FDA approves meloxicam (Mobic) for osteoarthritis."], ["2020", "FDA approves IV meloxicam (Anjeso)."]], "cell": "Meloxicam inhibits cyclooxygenase, the enzyme that converts arachidonic acid into prostaglandin H2, the precursor for prostaglandins, prostacyclin and thromboxane. At lower doses it inhibits COX-2 more than COX-1, and this preference shrinks as the dose rises. COX-2 is induced at sites of inflammation by cytokines. Less COX-2 activity means less prostaglandin E2 and prostacyclin, so blood vessels are less dilated and leaky, nerve endings are less sensitized to pain, and the hypothalamus is less driven to raise body temperature. Because COX-1 is partly spared, platelet thromboxane and stomach-protective prostaglandins are reduced less than with nonselective NSAIDs, though not eliminated.", "body": [["Joints", "Less prostaglandin production reduces pain, swelling and stiffness in osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis."], ["Stomach", "Partial sparing of COX-1 lowers, but does not remove, the risk of ulcers and bleeding."], ["Kidneys", "Loss of renal prostaglandins can reduce blood flow, causing fluid retention, high potassium and kidney injury."], ["Heart and vessels", "COX-2 inhibition reduces vessel-protective prostacyclin, raising blood pressure and heart attack and stroke risk."], ["Blood", "Platelet effects are milder than with aspirin or ibuprofen at usual doses."]], "journey": "Meloxicam is absorbed well by mouth, with about 89 percent bioavailability, and levels peak several hours after a dose. It is over 99 percent bound to plasma proteins and enters joint fluid. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP2C9, with a smaller role for CYP3A4, into inactive metabolites. These leave in roughly equal amounts in urine and feces. Its long half-life of about 15 to 20 hours allows once-daily dosing. People who are poor CYP2C9 metabolizers have higher levels.", "sidefx": "Side effects come from blocking prostaglandins that the body uses for protection. Reduced stomach prostaglandins cause dyspepsia, ulcers and bleeding. Reduced kidney prostaglandins cause fluid retention, edema, high blood pressure and acute kidney injury, especially in dehydrated people or with ACE inhibitors and diuretics. COX-2 inhibition tips the balance toward thromboxane in vessels, raising heart attack and stroke risk, a boxed warning for all NSAIDs. It is avoided late in pregnancy because prostaglandins keep the fetal ductus arteriosus open. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "src": ["Mobic (meloxicam) FDA label", "Anjeso (meloxicam injection) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's"]}, "memantine": {"origin": "Memantine has an unusual history. It was first synthesized in 1963 by researchers at Eli Lilly, who were studying it as a possible drug to lower blood sugar, but it did not work for that. The German company Merz later studied it for neurologic diseases and began selling it in Germany in the 1980s for dementia and other conditions. Only in 1989 did researchers show that its main action was blocking NMDA glutamate receptors. That fit a growing theory that too much glutamate activity harms neurons in Alzheimer disease. After larger trials, the FDA approved memantine (Namenda) in 2003 for moderate to severe Alzheimer dementia, the first drug in its class for that use.", "timeline": [["1963", "Memantine is first synthesized at Eli Lilly."], ["1980s", "Merz markets memantine in Germany."], ["1989", "Researchers show memantine blocks NMDA receptors."], ["2003", "FDA approves memantine (Namenda) for moderate to severe Alzheimer disease."], ["2014", "FDA approves Namzaric, memantine with donepezil."]], "cell": "NMDA receptors are glutamate-gated channels that let calcium into neurons. At rest, a magnesium ion plugs the channel and is pushed out only when the neuron depolarizes. In Alzheimer disease, glutamate signaling is thought to be chronically and mildly overactive, allowing constant calcium leak that harms neurons and drowns out real learning signals. Memantine is an uncompetitive, low-affinity, voltage-dependent channel blocker. It enters only open channels and binds near the magnesium site. Because it binds weakly and leaves quickly, it blocks the steady low-level background activation but lets go during strong, brief bursts of glutamate, such as those used in learning. This differs from high-affinity blockers like ketamine and PCP, which block normal signaling and cause dissociation.", "body": [["Brain", "Modestly slows decline in memory, thinking and daily function in moderate to severe Alzheimer disease."], ["Behavior", "May reduce agitation in some patients, though confusion or agitation can occur in others."], ["Gut", "Constipation is a common complaint, especially in older adults."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys clear most of the drug unchanged, so kidney disease raises levels and dosing is adjusted."]], "journey": "Memantine is absorbed almost completely by mouth, and food does not matter. Levels peak in three to seven hours for the immediate-release form. It is about 45 percent protein bound and crosses the blood-brain barrier well. Most of it is not metabolized; the liver makes only minor, inactive metabolites. About half leaves unchanged in the urine, partly through active tubular secretion. Alkaline urine slows its clearance. Its very long half-life of 60 to 80 hours means steady state takes about two weeks.", "sidefx": "Memantine is usually well tolerated because its weak, fast binding spares normal glutamate signaling. Dizziness, headache, confusion and constipation are the most common side effects. Rarely, especially at high levels or in kidney disease, it can cause hallucinations or agitation, reflecting stronger NMDA blockade. Drugs or diets that make urine alkaline can raise levels. Other NMDA blockers such as amantadine, ketamine and dextromethorphan add to its effects. There is no antidote; overdose care is supportive, and acidifying the urine can speed clearance.", "fun": "Memantine is a close chemical relative of amantadine, an antiviral and Parkinson drug that also weakly blocks NMDA receptors.", "src": ["Namenda (memantine) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Bormann J. Eur J Pharmacol 1989"]}, "mercaptopurine": {"origin": "Mercaptopurine came from the lab of Gertrude Elion and George Hitchings at Burroughs Wellcome in New York. Instead of testing random chemicals, they studied how normal and cancer cells used purines and pyrimidines, then built look-alike molecules that would jam the process. In 1951 Elion synthesized 6-mercaptopurine by swapping an oxygen atom in the purine hypoxanthine for sulfur. It produced remissions in children with acute leukemia and was approved by the FDA in 1953. The same research program later produced azathioprine, allopurinol and acyclovir. Elion and Hitchings shared the 1988 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine with James Black for their rational approach to drug design.", "timeline": [["1951", "Gertrude Elion synthesizes 6-mercaptopurine at Burroughs Wellcome."], ["1953", "FDA approves mercaptopurine for acute leukemia."], ["1988", "Elion and Hitchings share the Nobel Prize."], ["2014", "FDA approves Purixan, an oral suspension of mercaptopurine."]], "cell": "Mercaptopurine is a prodrug. Inside cells, the salvage enzyme hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase (HGPRT) converts it to thioinosine monophosphate (TIMP). TIMP and its methylated form inhibit PRPP amidotransferase, the first committed step of de novo purine synthesis, and block conversion of IMP to AMP and GMP. Further metabolism produces thioguanine nucleotides, which are incorporated into DNA and RNA, causing strand damage, mismatch and cell death. The drug acts mainly in S phase. Rapidly dividing cells, including leukemic blasts and activated lymphocytes, are most affected. Two pathways inactivate it: thiopurine methyltransferase (TPMT) methylates it, and xanthine oxidase oxidizes it to 6-thiouric acid.", "body": [["Leukemic cells", "Blocks purine production and damages DNA in rapidly dividing blasts, a core part of maintenance therapy for acute lymphoblastic leukemia."], ["Immune system", "Suppresses proliferating lymphocytes, which is why it helps Crohn disease and ulcerative colitis."], ["Bone marrow", "Suppresses blood cell production, causing low white cells, platelets and red cells."], ["Liver", "Can cause cholestatic hepatitis and, rarely, veno-occlusive disease."], ["Pancreas", "Can cause pancreatitis, more often in inflammatory bowel disease."]], "journey": "Mercaptopurine is taken by mouth, but absorption is incomplete and variable, and much is removed on first pass by xanthine oxidase in the gut and liver. The parent drug has a short half-life of about one to two hours, but its active thioguanine nucleotides stay inside cells for days. It is converted to active forms by HGPRT and inactivated by TPMT and xanthine oxidase. Metabolites and some unchanged drug leave in the urine. Variants in the TPMT and NUDT15 genes greatly increase active metabolite levels, so testing guides dosing.", "sidefx": "Myelosuppression is the main toxicity and follows directly from blocking DNA synthesis in rapidly dividing marrow cells. People with low TPMT or NUDT15 activity build up very high thioguanine nucleotide levels and can develop severe, life-threatening low blood counts. Allopurinol and febuxostat block xanthine oxidase, one of its main breakdown routes, so the dose must be cut sharply when they are used together. Liver injury, pancreatitis, mouth sores and higher infection risk also occur. Long-term immunosuppression raises the risk of certain lymphomas. There is no specific antidote; treatment is stopping the drug and supporting blood counts.", "fun": "Gertrude Elion never earned a formal PhD, yet she received a Nobel Prize and many honorary doctorates.", "src": ["Purixan (mercaptopurine) FDA label", "Elion GB. Science 1989 (Nobel lecture)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "meropenem": {"origin": "Meropenem descends from thienamycin, a natural antibiotic discovered by Merck scientists in 1976 in the soil bacterium Streptomyces cattleya. Thienamycin had remarkable power against many bacteria but was chemically unstable. Merck made the stable derivative imipenem, approved in 1985, but kidney enzymes broke it down, so it had to be paired with cilastatin. Scientists at Sumitomo Pharmaceuticals in Japan added a small methyl group to the carbapenem ring, which protected the drug from the kidney enzyme dehydropeptidase-I. The result was meropenem, co-developed with ICI (later Zeneca, now AstraZeneca). The FDA approved it as Merrem in 1996. It can be given alone, has less seizure risk than imipenem and became a key drug for serious hospital infections.", "timeline": [["1976", "Merck scientists discover thienamycin from Streptomyces cattleya."], ["1985", "FDA approves imipenem with cilastatin, the first carbapenem."], ["1996", "FDA approves meropenem (Merrem)."], ["2017", "FDA approves meropenem with vaborbactam (Vabomere)."]], "cell": "Meropenem is a beta-lactam antibiotic. It crosses the outer membrane of gram-negative bacteria through porin channels and binds penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), the transpeptidase enzymes that cross-link peptidoglycan strands in the cell wall. It has especially high affinity for PBP2 and PBP3 in gram-negatives. The beta-lactam ring acylates the PBP active site, permanently disabling it. Without cross-links, the wall weakens while autolysins keep breaking it down, and the cell bursts from internal pressure. This kills bacteria (bactericidal) in a time-dependent way. Its structure resists most beta-lactamases, including ESBLs and AmpC enzymes, but carbapenemases such as KPC, NDM and OXA-48 can destroy it. A 1-beta-methyl group protects it from renal dehydropeptidase-I.", "body": [["Infection sites", "Very broad bactericidal coverage of gram-positive, gram-negative and anaerobic bacteria, including many resistant gram-negatives."], ["Brain", "Penetrates inflamed meninges well enough to treat bacterial meningitis; its seizure risk is lower than imipenem's."], ["Gut flora", "Broad killing of normal gut bacteria can lead to C. difficile diarrhea and overgrowth of resistant organisms."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys clear most of the drug, so kidney function guides dosing."], ["Seizure threshold", "Lowers valproate levels sharply, which can cause breakthrough seizures in patients on valproate."]], "journey": "Meropenem is given IV because it is not absorbed by mouth. It spreads widely into tissues and body fluids, including lung, bile, peritoneal fluid and cerebrospinal fluid when meninges are inflamed. It binds very little to proteins. Its methyl group protects it from renal dehydropeptidase-I, so it does not need cilastatin. About 70 percent leaves unchanged in the urine, and the rest as an inactive open-ring metabolite. Its half-life is about one hour in normal kidneys and longer in kidney disease. Because killing depends on time above the MIC, extended infusions are often used.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea and C. difficile infection come from wiping out normal gut flora. Seizures are less common than with imipenem but can occur, especially in kidney failure or brain disease, because beta-lactams can block GABA-A receptors at high levels. Meropenem lowers valproate levels sharply, likely by inhibiting the enzyme that recycles valproate glucuronide, so the two should not be combined. Allergic reactions occur, though cross-reactivity with penicillin allergy is low. Overuse drives the spread of carbapenem-resistant organisms. There is no antidote; dialysis removes it.", "src": ["Merrem (meropenem) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's"]}, "mesalamine": {"origin": "Mesalamine's story begins with sulfasalazine. In the late 1930s and 1940s the Swedish physician Nanna Svartz worked with Pharmacia to join an anti-inflammatory salicylate, 5-aminosalicylic acid (5-ASA), to an antibiotic sulfa drug, sulfapyridine, hoping to treat rheumatoid arthritis. The combined drug, sulfasalazine, also helped ulcerative colitis. For decades no one knew which half did the work. In 1977 Azad Khan, Piris and Truelove in Oxford showed that 5-ASA was the active part and that sulfapyridine mainly caused side effects. Companies then developed coated, delayed-release and rectal forms of 5-ASA alone, called mesalamine, which deliver the drug directly to the inflamed colon. Rowasa enema and Asacol tablets were among the first US products.", "timeline": [["1940s", "Nanna Svartz develops sulfasalazine in Sweden."], ["1977", "Azad Khan, Piris and Truelove show 5-ASA is the active moiety."], ["1987", "FDA approves mesalamine enema (Rowasa), followed by oral delayed-release forms."]], "cell": "Mesalamine works topically on the inside surface of the colon, not through the bloodstream. Within the mucosa it inhibits cyclooxygenase and lipoxygenase, lowering prostaglandins and leukotrienes that drive inflammation. It scavenges reactive oxygen species released by activated white blood cells. It inhibits activation of the transcription factor NF-kappa B, which reduces production of inflammatory cytokines such as TNF-alpha and IL-1. It also activates peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma (PPAR-gamma) in colonic epithelial cells, which dampens inflammatory gene expression. Together these actions calm the mucosal inflammation of ulcerative colitis.", "body": [["Colon", "Reduces mucosal inflammation, bleeding and ulceration, inducing and maintaining remission in mild to moderate ulcerative colitis."], ["Rectum", "Suppositories and enemas deliver high local concentrations for proctitis and left-sided colitis."], ["Kidneys", "Rarely causes interstitial nephritis, so kidney function is checked periodically."], ["Heart", "Rarely triggers myocarditis or pericarditis through a hypersensitivity reaction."], ["Gut symptoms", "A small number of patients develop an acute intolerance syndrome that mimics a colitis flare."]], "journey": "Plain 5-ASA would be absorbed in the upper small bowel, so mesalamine products are designed to delay release. pH-dependent coatings dissolve in the terminal ileum and colon, while time-release granules release it along the gut. Rectal forms put it directly on the distal colon. About 20 to 30 percent of an oral dose is absorbed. Absorbed drug is acetylated by N-acetyltransferase in the intestinal wall and liver to acetyl-5-ASA. Absorbed drug and metabolite leave in the urine, and the unabsorbed portion leaves in the feces.", "sidefx": "Because only a fraction is absorbed, side effects are usually mild: headache, nausea, abdominal pain and gas. Removing the sulfapyridine of sulfasalazine avoids most sulfa side effects such as rash, low sperm count and folate problems. Absorbed 5-ASA can cause rare but serious hypersensitivity reactions, including interstitial nephritis, myocarditis, pericarditis, hepatitis and pancreatitis. An acute intolerance syndrome with cramps, bloody diarrhea and fever can look like a flare. It is a salicylate, so it is used with care in people with salicylate allergy. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Lialda (mesalamine) FDA label", "Azad Khan AK, Piris J, Truelove SC. Lancet 1977", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "mesna": {"origin": "Mesna was developed to solve a problem created by chemotherapy. The German company Asta-Werke developed cyclophosphamide in the 1950s and later ifosfamide. Both drugs are broken down into acrolein, which collects in the bladder and causes hemorrhagic cystitis, sometimes with severe bleeding. Asta researchers looked for a protective molecule that would neutralize acrolein in the urine without blocking the anticancer effect in the rest of the body. Mesna, a small sulfur-containing compound, did exactly that and entered use in Europe in the late 1970s and early 1980s. It made high-dose ifosfamide practical. The FDA approved mesna injection (Mesnex) in the late 1980s, and an oral tablet later followed.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Asta-Werke develops cyclophosphamide."], ["1970s", "Asta researchers develop mesna as a bladder protectant."], ["1980s", "Mesna is approved in the US to prevent ifosfamide-induced hemorrhagic cystitis."]], "cell": "Cyclophosphamide and ifosfamide are converted by liver CYP enzymes into active alkylating agents and the byproduct acrolein. Acrolein is a reactive aldehyde that collects in urine and damages the bladder lining, causing hemorrhagic cystitis. Mesna (2-mercaptoethane sulfonate) has a free sulfhydryl (thiol) group. In the blood, it is quickly oxidized to dimesna, an inactive disulfide that does not interfere with the anticancer drug. In the kidney tubules, dimesna is reduced back to free mesna and concentrated in urine. There, the thiol reacts with acrolein and with 4-hydroxy metabolites of ifosfamide, forming stable, nontoxic thioether compounds that are passed in the urine. Because the active thiol appears only in urine, the antitumor effect is preserved.", "body": [["Bladder", "Neutralizes acrolein in urine, preventing bleeding and inflammation of the bladder lining."], ["Kidneys", "Concentrates and regenerates active mesna from dimesna in the tubules, putting the protector right where it is needed."], ["Tumor and rest of body", "Does not neutralize the anticancer effect because it is inactive dimesna while in the blood."], ["Gut and taste", "Can cause nausea, vomiting and an unpleasant taste, especially with oral doses."]], "journey": "Mesna can be given IV or by mouth. After IV dosing it is oxidized in the blood to dimesna within minutes, and mesna's half-life is very short, about 20 minutes, while dimesna's is about an hour. Oral bioavailability is roughly half. In the kidney, dimesna is filtered and reduced back to free mesna, which is excreted in the urine. Because mesna leaves faster than ifosfamide and its metabolites, it is given in several doses or as an infusion to cover the whole period of acrolein exposure. A urine test can check for blood.", "sidefx": "Mesna itself is generally well tolerated. Nausea, vomiting, bad taste, headache and fatigue are common, though some of these are hard to separate from chemotherapy. Hypersensitivity reactions, including rash, flushing, low blood pressure and rarely anaphylaxis, can occur, and these are more common in people with autoimmune disease. Severe skin reactions such as SJS or TEN and DRESS have been reported. The thiol can cause a false-positive urine ketone test. Mesna does not prevent other ifosfamide toxicities such as encephalopathy or kidney damage. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Mesna's name is a contraction of its chemistry: mercaptoethane sulfonate of sodium (Na).", "src": ["Mesnex (mesna) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "methadone": {"origin": "Methadone was created in Germany. In 1937 and 1938, chemists Max Bockmühl and Gustav Ehrhart at the IG Farben (Hoechst) labs were searching for new drugs to relieve spasms and pain. They found a fully synthetic compound unrelated in structure to morphine that turned out to be a powerful opioid. After World War II, details of the drug passed to the Allies, and Eli Lilly brought it to the US as Dolophine in 1947. In 1964 and 1965 the New York physicians Vincent Dole and Marie Nyswander showed that steady daily methadone helped people with heroin addiction stabilize their lives, founding methadone maintenance treatment. It remains a key treatment for opioid use disorder and a long-acting analgesic.", "timeline": [["1937", "Bockmühl and Ehrhart develop methadone at IG Farben in Germany."], ["1947", "Eli Lilly introduces methadone in the US as Dolophine."], ["1965", "Dole and Nyswander report methadone maintenance for heroin addiction."], ["1972", "Federal regulations formally establish methadone treatment programs in the US."]], "cell": "Methadone is a full agonist at mu-opioid receptors, which are Gi/Go-coupled receptors. Activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase, lowers cAMP, opens potassium channels so neurons hyperpolarize, and closes presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels so less neurotransmitter is released. In the spinal cord dorsal horn and brainstem this blocks pain transmission, and in the brainstem it reduces breathing drive. Methadone also blocks NMDA glutamate receptors, which may reduce opioid tolerance and help neuropathic pain. It weakly inhibits reuptake of serotonin and norepinephrine. In addiction treatment, steady occupancy of mu receptors prevents withdrawal and cravings, and cross-tolerance blunts the euphoria of other opioids. It also blocks cardiac hERG potassium channels.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Relieves pain and suppresses opioid withdrawal and cravings with once-daily dosing in opioid use disorder."], ["Breathing", "Reduces brainstem response to carbon dioxide, causing respiratory depression that can peak later than pain relief."], ["Heart", "Blocks hERG potassium channels, prolonging the QT interval and risking torsades de pointes."], ["Gut", "Slows gut movement, causing constipation that does not improve with tolerance."], ["Skin and glands", "Causes heavy sweating and sometimes itching from histamine release and autonomic effects."], ["Hormones", "Long-term use suppresses the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis and can lower sex hormones."]], "journey": "Methadone is absorbed well by mouth, with high bioavailability. It is very fat-soluble and spreads into tissues, where it builds up and then slowly leaks back into the blood. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP3A4 and CYP2B6, with a role for CYP2D6, into inactive products that leave in urine and feces. Its half-life is long and varies widely, from about 8 to nearly 60 hours. Pain relief lasts only about 4 to 8 hours, but breathing effects and drug levels keep rising for days after a dose change. This mismatch is why dose increases are slow. It crosses the placenta, and newborns may have withdrawal.", "sidefx": "Respiratory depression is the deadliest risk and comes from mu receptor action in the brainstem. Because methadone builds up in tissues over several days, overdose often happens during the first weeks of treatment or after dose changes. QT prolongation and torsades come from hERG potassium channel blockade, so ECGs are checked. Constipation, sweating, sedation and low sex hormones are common. Drugs that inhibit CYP3A4 or CYP2B6 raise levels, and inducers can cause withdrawal. Naloxone reverses opioid effects, but its short action means repeated doses or an infusion are often needed for methadone.", "fun": "A persistent myth says Dolophine was named after Adolf Hitler; the name more likely comes from the French douleur, meaning pain, and Latin finis, meaning end.", "src": ["Dolophine (methadone) FDA label", "Dole VP, Nyswander M. JAMA 1965", "Goodman & Gilman's"]}, "methimazole": {"origin": "Methimazole belongs to the thionamide family, discovered by accident in the early 1940s. Researchers studying sulfur compounds, including sulfaguanidine and thiourea, noticed that animals given them developed enlarged thyroid glands. Edwin Astwood at Harvard realized these drugs were blocking thyroid hormone production and, in 1943, published the first use of thiourea and thiouracil to treat hyperthyroidism in people. Chemists then searched for stronger, safer versions. Methimazole was introduced around 1950 by Eli Lilly as Tapazole. It is more potent and longer acting than propylthiouracil, needs fewer daily doses and is now the first-choice antithyroid drug except in early pregnancy and thyroid storm.", "timeline": [["1943", "Edwin Astwood reports thionamides for treating hyperthyroidism."], ["c. 1950", "Methimazole (Tapazole) is introduced."], ["2009", "FDA warns about severe liver injury with propylthiouracil, increasing reliance on methimazole."]], "cell": "Thyroid hormone is made in steps inside the thyroid follicle. The enzyme thyroid peroxidase (TPO) oxidizes iodide, attaches iodine to tyrosine residues on thyroglobulin (organification), and couples the iodinated tyrosines into T4 and T3. Methimazole is concentrated in the thyroid and inhibits TPO by acting as a preferred substrate, diverting oxidized iodine away from thyroglobulin. New hormone synthesis stops. Existing stores of hormone in the gland and the blood are not affected, so symptoms improve only after these stores are used up. Unlike propylthiouracil, methimazole does not block peripheral conversion of T4 to T3.", "body": [["Thyroid", "Stops new T4 and T3 production; the gland may enlarge if TSH rises with overtreatment."], ["Metabolism and heart", "As hormone levels fall over weeks, heart rate, tremor, sweating and weight loss improve."], ["Bone marrow", "Can rarely cause agranulocytosis, a sudden drop in neutrophils that raises infection risk."], ["Liver", "Can cause cholestatic liver injury, which is usually less severe than PTU's hepatocellular injury."], ["Skin and joints", "Rash, itching and joint pain are the most common side effects."]], "journey": "Methimazole is absorbed well by mouth and is concentrated in the thyroid gland, where its effect lasts much longer than its blood half-life of about five to six hours. This allows once-daily dosing. It is not bound to proteins. It crosses the placenta more readily than PTU and is linked to rare birth defects in the first trimester. The liver metabolizes it, and metabolites leave in the urine. Clinical improvement takes weeks because the thyroid's stored hormone must be used up first.", "sidefx": "Rash, itching and joint pain are common and probably immune-mediated. Agranulocytosis, a rare but dangerous drop in neutrophils, is also immune-mediated and usually occurs in the first few months. Patients are told to stop the drug and seek care for fever or sore throat. Liver injury, usually cholestatic, can occur. Overtreatment causes hypothyroidism. In the first trimester it can cause rare birth defects such as aplasia cutis, so PTU is preferred in early pregnancy and methimazole later. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped and complications are treated.", "fun": "Carbimazole, used widely in the UK, is a prodrug that the body converts into methimazole.", "src": ["Tapazole (methimazole) FDA label", "Astwood EB. JAMA 1943", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "methocarbamol": {"origin": "Methocarbamol came from the American company A.H. Robins of Richmond, Virginia. It is a carbamate derivative of guaifenesin, the expectorant ingredient in many cough medicines, which itself was known to have mild muscle-relaxing effects. Robins introduced methocarbamol in the 1950s, when several centrally acting muscle relaxants were being developed after the discovery of mephenesin in the 1940s. It became a widely used treatment for acute, painful muscle spasm and is also given IV, including as part of treatment for tetanus.", "timeline": [["1940s", "Mephenesin, an early centrally acting muscle relaxant, is introduced."], ["1950s", "A.H. Robins introduces methocarbamol (Robaxin)."]], "cell": "Methocarbamol's exact mechanism is not fully known. It does not act directly on skeletal muscle, the neuromuscular junction or the nerve fiber. Instead, it is thought to depress the central nervous system, especially polysynaptic reflex pathways in the spinal cord and brainstem. These reflex arcs, which involve interneurons between sensory input and motor output, help sustain muscle spasm after injury. By dampening transmission through these interneurons, methocarbamol reduces the reflex muscle tightening that causes spasm and pain. Much of its benefit may also come from general sedation.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "General CNS depression and reduced polysynaptic reflexes lower muscle spasm."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Spasm and stiffness ease, though muscle strength is not directly reduced."], ["Alertness", "Drowsiness and dizziness are common and add to alcohol and other sedatives."], ["Heart and vessels", "IV doses can cause low blood pressure, slow heart rate and fainting, especially when given quickly."], ["Urine", "Metabolites may turn urine brown, black or green, which is harmless."]], "journey": "Methocarbamol is absorbed rapidly by mouth and begins working in about 30 minutes. It can also be given IV or IM. The liver breaks it down by dealkylation and hydroxylation, followed by conjugation. Metabolites leave mostly in the urine. Its half-life is short, about one to two hours, so it is taken several times a day. The IV form contains polyethylene glycol, which can be harmful in kidney failure.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness, dizziness and lightheadedness reflect central nervous system depression and are worse with alcohol, opioids or benzodiazepines. Older adults are more prone to falls and confusion, and it is on the Beers list of drugs to avoid in older adults. IV use can cause slow heart rate, low blood pressure, fainting and irritation at the injection site, and the polyethylene glycol vehicle can worsen kidney failure. Harmless discoloration of urine can occur. There is no antidote; overdose causes sedation and is managed supportively.", "src": ["Robaxin (methocarbamol) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "American Geriatrics Society Beers Criteria"]}, "methotrexate": {"origin": "Methotrexate grew out of folate research and a bold experiment. In the 1940s scientists noticed that folic acid seemed to speed the growth of leukemia. Yellapragada SubbaRow and colleagues at Lederle Laboratories made folate look-alikes that blocked the vitamin instead. In 1947 Sidney Farber at Children's Hospital in Boston gave one of them, aminopterin, to children with acute leukemia and saw the first temporary remissions, published in 1948. Lederle chemists then made the closely related, less toxic methotrexate, which the FDA approved in 1953. In 1956 Min Chiu Li and Roy Hertz at the NIH used it to cure metastatic choriocarcinoma, the first solid tumor cured by chemotherapy. Low-dose methotrexate was approved for rheumatoid arthritis in 1988.", "timeline": [["1947", "Sidney Farber treats childhood leukemia with aminopterin."], ["1953", "FDA approves methotrexate."], ["1956", "Li and Hertz cure choriocarcinoma with methotrexate."], ["1988", "FDA approves low-dose methotrexate for rheumatoid arthritis."]], "cell": "Methotrexate is a folic acid analog that binds dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR) about a thousand times more tightly than its natural substrate. It enters cells through the reduced folate carrier and is retained inside as polyglutamates. Blocking DHFR stops regeneration of tetrahydrofolate from dihydrofolate. Without tetrahydrofolate, cells run out of N5,N10-methylene-tetrahydrofolate, the cofactor thymidylate synthase needs to make dTMP for DNA, and they also lose cofactors for purine synthesis. DNA synthesis halts, and rapidly dividing cells die in S phase. At the low doses used in rheumatoid arthritis, polyglutamates also inhibit AICAR transformylase, so AICAR builds up and adenosine is released outside cells. Adenosine acts on A2A receptors to suppress inflammation.", "body": [["Tumor cells", "Blocks DNA synthesis in rapidly dividing cells, treating leukemia, lymphoma, choriocarcinoma, osteosarcoma and other cancers."], ["Immune system", "Suppresses activated lymphocytes and raises anti-inflammatory adenosine, treating rheumatoid arthritis, psoriasis and other autoimmune diseases."], ["Bone marrow", "Suppresses blood cell production, causing low white counts, platelets and megaloblastic anemia."], ["Gut and mouth", "Kills rapidly dividing mucosal cells, causing mouth sores, nausea and diarrhea."], ["Liver", "Long-term use can cause fatty change, fibrosis and cirrhosis, especially with alcohol."], ["Lungs", "Can cause a hypersensitivity pneumonitis with cough, fever and shortness of breath."], ["Pregnancy", "Kills rapidly dividing trophoblast cells, which is why it is used for ectopic pregnancy, and causes birth defects."]], "journey": "Methotrexate can be given by mouth, by injection or intrathecally. Oral absorption is good at low doses but saturates at higher doses. About half is bound to albumin. It enters cells through the reduced folate carrier and is trapped as polyglutamates, so its effects last days to weeks. It collects in fluid spaces such as ascites and pleural effusions and leaks out slowly, prolonging exposure. The liver converts a small part to 7-hydroxymethotrexate. Most of the drug leaves unchanged through the kidneys by filtration and tubular secretion. NSAIDs, penicillins, PPIs and probenecid can slow its excretion.", "sidefx": "Most toxicity comes from the same folate block in healthy fast-dividing cells, causing low blood counts, mouth sores and diarrhea. Folic acid is given with low-dose regimens to reduce these effects. High doses can crystallize in acidic urine and damage the kidneys, so urine is alkalinized and hydrated. Liver fibrosis, pneumonitis and opportunistic infections can occur with long-term use. It is strongly teratogenic and abortifacient. Accidental daily instead of weekly dosing has caused fatal toxicity. The antidote is leucovorin, a reduced folate that bypasses DHFR and rescues normal cells. Glucarpidase breaks down methotrexate in the blood when kidney failure delays clearance.", "fun": "Choriocarcinoma was the first solid cancer cured by chemotherapy, and doctors tracked the cure with the pregnancy hormone hCG as a tumor marker.", "src": ["Methotrexate injection FDA label", "Farber S et al. NEJM 1948", "Li MC, Hertz R, Spencer DB. Proc Soc Exp Biol Med 1956"]}, "methyldopa": {"origin": "Methyldopa was developed by Merck in the late 1950s. Researchers first studied it as an inhibitor of dopa decarboxylase, the enzyme that makes dopamine and norepinephrine, hoping it would lower blood pressure by starving nerves of their transmitter. In 1960 clinical studies, including work by John Oates and Albert Sjoerdsma at the NIH, showed that it did lower blood pressure, and Merck introduced it as Aldomet in the early 1960s. It became one of the most prescribed antihypertensives of its era. Scientists later learned it works not by blocking transmitter synthesis but by becoming a false transmitter that acts in the brainstem. Today it is used mostly for high blood pressure in pregnancy because of its long safety record.", "timeline": [["1960", "Oates, Sjoerdsma and colleagues report methyldopa lowers blood pressure."], ["1960s", "Merck introduces methyldopa as Aldomet."]], "cell": "Methyldopa is a prodrug that enters adrenergic neurons in place of L-dopa. Dopa decarboxylase converts it to alpha-methyldopamine, and dopamine beta-hydroxylase then converts that to alpha-methylnorepinephrine. This molecule is stored in vesicles and released in place of norepinephrine. In the brainstem, especially the nucleus tractus solitarius, alpha-methylnorepinephrine stimulates presynaptic and central alpha-2 adrenergic receptors. These Gi-coupled receptors reduce adenylyl cyclase activity and inhibit sympathetic outflow from the brain. Less sympathetic tone to the vessels lowers peripheral resistance and blood pressure, with little change in heart rate, cardiac output or kidney blood flow.", "body": [["Brainstem", "Central alpha-2 stimulation reduces sympathetic outflow to the heart and blood vessels."], ["Blood vessels", "Peripheral resistance falls, lowering blood pressure without much reflex tachycardia."], ["Kidneys", "Renal blood flow is maintained, a useful feature in kidney disease and pregnancy."], ["Uterus and placenta", "Long safety experience in pregnancy makes it an option for chronic hypertension in pregnancy."], ["Brain", "Reduced central adrenergic activity causes sedation, fatigue and sometimes depression."], ["Blood and immune system", "Can trigger a positive direct Coombs test and, rarely, autoimmune hemolytic anemia."]], "journey": "Methyldopa is taken by mouth, with incomplete and variable absorption. It is carried into the brain by an amino acid transporter. It must be converted to alpha-methylnorepinephrine inside neurons, so blood pressure falls slowly, over several hours, with peak effect at about four to six hours, and lasts 12 to 24 hours. The plasma half-life is short, about two hours. The liver and gut wall conjugate it as a sulfate. Unchanged drug and metabolites leave in the urine, so kidney disease prolongs its action. An IV form, methyldopate, was once used.", "sidefx": "Sedation, fatigue, dry mouth and depression come from reduced central adrenergic activity. Orthostatic hypotension can occur. Its most distinctive effects are immune: up to about 10 to 20 percent of long-term users develop a positive direct Coombs test, and a small number develop autoimmune hemolytic anemia. Liver injury, from mild hepatitis to rare fatal necrosis, and drug fever can occur, so liver tests are monitored early in therapy. Elevated prolactin can cause breast enlargement or milk production. Stopping it rarely causes rebound hypertension. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Methyldopa tablets FDA label", "Oates JA et al. Science 1960", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "methylene-blue": {"origin": "Methylene blue was the first fully synthetic drug used in medicine. In 1876 the German chemist Heinrich Caro at BASF synthesized it as a dye for cotton. Paul Ehrlich used it to stain nerve tissue and bacteria and noticed it was taken up selectively by certain cells. That idea of a chemical that targets specific cells led him, with Paul Guttmann, to treat two patients with malaria in 1891, and both recovered. Later the dye was found to reverse methemoglobinemia, a condition in which hemoglobin cannot carry oxygen. For decades unapproved products were used, until the FDA approved ProvayBlue in 2016, the first approved methylene blue product for acquired methemoglobinemia.", "timeline": [["1876", "Heinrich Caro synthesizes methylene blue at BASF."], ["1891", "Guttmann and Ehrlich use methylene blue to treat malaria."], ["1930s", "Methylene blue is used to treat methemoglobinemia and cyanide poisoning."], ["2016", "FDA approves ProvayBlue for acquired methemoglobinemia."]], "cell": "In methemoglobin, the iron in heme is oxidized from ferrous (Fe2+) to ferric (Fe3+), which cannot carry oxygen and shifts the dissociation curve of remaining heme to the left. Red blood cells normally fix this using NADH-cytochrome b5 reductase. Methylene blue uses a backup pathway. The enzyme NADPH-methemoglobin reductase reduces methylene blue to leucomethylene blue, using NADPH made by the pentose phosphate pathway through glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD). Leucomethylene blue then donates electrons to methemoglobin, turning Fe3+ back into Fe2+ so hemoglobin can carry oxygen again. Methylene blue also inhibits nitric oxide synthase and soluble guanylate cyclase, which raises vascular tone. It is a potent inhibitor of monoamine oxidase A.", "body": [["Blood", "Converts methemoglobin back to normal hemoglobin within about an hour, restoring oxygen delivery."], ["Blood vessels", "Blocking nitric oxide and cGMP signaling raises vascular tone, which is why it is studied in vasoplegic shock."], ["Brain and serotonin", "MAO-A inhibition raises serotonin, so combining it with serotonergic drugs can cause serotonin syndrome."], ["Urine and skin", "The dye turns urine blue-green and can tint the skin, which is harmless."], ["Pulse oximetry", "Its dark blue color absorbs light and can make SpO2 readings falsely low."]], "journey": "For methemoglobinemia, methylene blue is given IV, and methemoglobin levels usually fall within 30 to 60 minutes. In tissues and red blood cells it is reduced to leucomethylene blue, a colorless form. Both forms are distributed through the body, and it crosses into the brain. The parent and its metabolites leave through urine and bile, which is why urine turns blue-green. Its terminal half-life is roughly a day, with wide variation.", "sidefx": "Blue or green urine and skin are expected and harmless. In people with G6PD deficiency, red cells cannot make enough NADPH to reduce methylene blue, so it does not work, and it can act as an oxidant, causing hemolysis and even worse methemoglobinemia. Very high doses can paradoxically cause methemoglobin by oxidizing hemoglobin directly. It is a strong MAO-A inhibitor and carries a boxed warning for serotonin syndrome with SSRIs, SNRIs and similar drugs. Pulse oximetry can read falsely low. When methylene blue fails or is contraindicated, exchange transfusion or hyperbaric oxygen are alternatives.", "fun": "Paul Ehrlich's work with methylene blue stains inspired his idea of the magic bullet, a chemical that could seek out and kill only harmful cells.", "src": ["ProvayBlue (methylene blue) FDA label", "Guttmann P, Ehrlich P. Berl Klin Wochenschr 1891", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]}, "methylergonovine": {"origin": "Methylergonovine comes from ergot, the fungus Claviceps purpurea that grows on rye. For centuries, eating bread from infected rye caused outbreaks of ergotism, known as Saint Anthony's fire, with burning limbs and gangrene from blood vessel constriction. Midwives also used ergot to speed labor and stop bleeding after birth. In 1808 the American doctor John Stearns described its use to hasten childbirth. In 1935 several groups isolated the main uterine-active alkaloid, ergonovine (ergometrine). Chemists at Sandoz in Basel, where Albert Hofmann also worked on ergot alkaloids, made a semisynthetic derivative with an added methyl group, methylergonovine, sold as Methergine. It became a standard treatment for postpartum hemorrhage caused by a relaxed uterus.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Ergot-contaminated grain causes outbreaks of ergotism in Europe."], ["1808", "John Stearns describes ergot to speed childbirth."], ["1935", "Ergonovine is isolated as the uterine-active ergot alkaloid."], ["1930s", "At Sandoz, Albert Hofmann and colleagues synthesize ergonovine and related lysergic acid derivatives."]], "cell": "Methylergonovine is a semisynthetic ergot alkaloid that acts as a partial agonist at several receptors. In uterine smooth muscle it stimulates 5-HT2A serotonin receptors and alpha-1 adrenergic receptors, both Gq-coupled. Activation raises IP3 and intracellular calcium, causing strong, sustained contraction. The uterus becomes firm with frequent, prolonged contractions that squeeze the spiral arteries in the placental bed and stop bleeding. It also acts at dopamine receptors. In vascular smooth muscle, the same alpha-1 and 5-HT2 activation constricts arteries and veins, including coronary arteries, which explains its blood pressure effects.", "body": [["Uterus", "Produces sustained, tetanic contraction that compresses bleeding vessels after delivery of the placenta."], ["Blood vessels", "Constricts peripheral and coronary arteries, which can raise blood pressure and cause chest pain."], ["Heart", "Coronary vasospasm can cause angina or, rarely, heart attack."], ["Brain", "Vasoconstriction and serotonin effects can cause headache and, rarely, stroke or seizures."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are common, partly from serotonin and dopamine receptor activity."]], "journey": "Methylergonovine can be given IM, IV or by mouth. IM doses act within minutes, and IV acts almost immediately but is reserved for emergencies because of the risk of sudden hypertension. The uterine effect lasts several hours. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP3A4. Its half-life is about three and a half hours, and metabolites leave in urine and bile. Small amounts pass into breast milk. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors can cause dangerous buildup.", "sidefx": "Hypertension, headache and chest pain come from alpha-1 and serotonin-driven vasoconstriction. For this reason it is avoided in people with hypertension or preeclampsia. Coronary vasospasm can cause angina or heart attack. With strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as clarithromycin, ritonavir or azole antifungals, levels rise and can cause ergotism, with severe vasospasm, limb ischemia and stroke. It is contraindicated in pregnancy before delivery because tetanic contractions would harm the fetus. Severe vasospasm is treated with vasodilators such as nitroprusside or nitroglycerin.", "fun": "Some historians have suggested that ergot poisoning from rye may have played a role in the Salem witch trials of 1692, though this remains debated.", "src": ["Methergine (methylergonovine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "methylphenidate": {"origin": "Methylphenidate was first synthesized in 1944 by the chemist Leandro Panizzon at Ciba in Basel, Switzerland. His wife, Marguerite, nicknamed Rita, took it before playing tennis because she felt it improved her game. Panizzon named the drug Ritalin in her honor. Ciba patented it in the early 1950s, and it was first marketed for fatigue, depression and lethargy in older adults. The FDA approved it in 1955. Its use for children with hyperactivity grew through the 1960s and later, and it became one of the most widely used treatments for ADHD. Long-acting forms, such as the osmotic-release Concerta tablet in 2000 and the Daytrana skin patch in 2006, made once-daily treatment possible.", "timeline": [["1944", "Leandro Panizzon synthesizes methylphenidate at Ciba."], ["1955", "FDA approves methylphenidate (Ritalin)."], ["1960s", "Methylphenidate is increasingly used for childhood hyperactivity."], ["2000", "FDA approves extended-release Concerta."], ["2006", "FDA approves the Daytrana skin patch."]], "cell": "Methylphenidate blocks the dopamine transporter (DAT) and the norepinephrine transporter (NET) on presynaptic nerve terminals. These transporters normally pull dopamine and norepinephrine back into the neuron after release. By binding them, methylphenidate leaves more transmitter in the synapse for longer. Unlike amphetamine, it does not enter the neuron, reverse the transporters or empty storage vesicles, so it acts mainly as a reuptake inhibitor. Higher dopamine and norepinephrine in the prefrontal cortex and striatum strengthen signal-to-noise in attention circuits, improving focus and impulse control. The d-threo enantiomer, dexmethylphenidate, carries most of the activity.", "body": [["Brain", "Improves attention, focus and impulse control in ADHD, and promotes wakefulness in narcolepsy."], ["Heart and vessels", "More norepinephrine raises heart rate and blood pressure."], ["Appetite", "Central effects reduce appetite, which can slow weight gain and growth in children."], ["Sleep", "Increased arousal can cause insomnia, especially with late doses or long-acting forms."], ["Gut", "Stomachache and nausea are common, especially in children."], ["Skin", "The patch can cause skin irritation and, rarely, permanent loss of skin color at the site."]], "journey": "Methylphenidate is well absorbed by mouth, but first-pass metabolism leaves only about 10 to 30 percent available. Immediate-release forms act within 30 to 60 minutes and last three to five hours. Extended-release capsules and tablets release drug in stages to last most of the day. The patch bypasses the liver. It crosses the blood-brain barrier readily. The main route of breakdown is not CYP but de-esterification by the liver enzyme carboxylesterase 1 to ritalinic acid, which is inactive and leaves in the urine. Its half-life is about two to three hours. Alcohol can form ethylphenidate and raise levels.", "sidefx": "Side effects reflect extra dopamine and norepinephrine. Insomnia, appetite loss, weight loss, headache and stomach pain are common. Sympathetic stimulation raises heart rate and blood pressure and, rarely, causes arrhythmias or sudden cardiac events in people with heart disease. High doses can cause anxiety, psychosis or mania. Growth can slow slightly with long-term use. Raised dopamine in reward pathways creates misuse potential, which is why it is Schedule II and carries a boxed warning. Rare priapism has been reported. There is no antidote; overdose is treated supportively with benzodiazepines for agitation.", "fun": "Ritalin is named after Rita, the nickname of chemist Leandro Panizzon's wife, who used the drug to boost her tennis game.", "src": ["Ritalin (methylphenidate) FDA label", "Concerta FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's"]}, "methylprednisolone": {"origin": "Methylprednisolone came out of the race to improve cortisone. In 1948 Philip Hench at the Mayo Clinic gave cortisone, isolated by Edward Kendall, to a patient with severe rheumatoid arthritis, and she was soon able to walk. Hench, Kendall and Tadeus Reichstein shared the 1950 Nobel Prize. Cortisone caused heavy salt and water retention, so drug companies modified the steroid structure. Prednisolone added a double bond that boosted anti-inflammatory power. Scientists at the Upjohn Company in Kalamazoo then added a methyl group, producing methylprednisolone, which was even more potent and caused less salt retention. Upjohn introduced it as Medrol in 1957, and the injectable Solu-Medrol and Depo-Medrol followed.", "timeline": [["1948", "Philip Hench treats rheumatoid arthritis with cortisone at the Mayo Clinic."], ["1950", "Hench, Kendall and Reichstein share the Nobel Prize."], ["1957", "Upjohn introduces methylprednisolone (Medrol)."]], "cell": "Methylprednisolone is a synthetic glucocorticoid that diffuses into cells and binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor. Binding releases heat shock proteins, and the receptor complex moves into the nucleus. There it binds glucocorticoid response elements on DNA and switches on anti-inflammatory genes, such as lipocortin-1 (annexin A1), which inhibits phospholipase A2 and so lowers prostaglandin and leukotriene production. It also tethers to and blocks the transcription factors NF-kappa B and AP-1, turning off genes for cytokines, adhesion molecules and COX-2. This reduces white blood cell recruitment and capillary leakiness. It also increases beta-2 receptor expression in the airways and induces enzymes for gluconeogenesis. It has much stronger glucocorticoid than mineralocorticoid activity.", "body": [["Immune system", "Suppresses inflammation and lymphocyte activity, treating asthma flares, allergic reactions, autoimmune flares and transplant rejection."], ["Lungs", "Reduces airway inflammation and edema and restores beta-2 receptor response in severe asthma and COPD."], ["Metabolism", "Raises blood sugar by increasing gluconeogenesis and reducing insulin sensitivity."], ["Blood", "Raises neutrophil counts by releasing them from vessel walls, while lowering lymphocytes and eosinophils."], ["Stomach", "Combined with NSAIDs, it raises the risk of ulcers and bleeding."], ["Brain", "Can cause insomnia, mood swings, euphoria and, at high doses, psychosis."], ["Adrenal glands", "Long courses suppress the HPA axis, so the body's own cortisol production falls."]], "journey": "Methylprednisolone can be given by mouth, IV, IM or into joints. The sodium succinate salt (Solu-Medrol) is water-soluble for rapid IV use, while the acetate (Depo-Medrol) is a slow-release suspension for IM and joint injection. Oral absorption is good. It is moderately protein bound and crosses the placenta. The liver metabolizes it, partly through CYP3A4, to inactive products that leave in the urine. Its plasma half-life is about two to three hours, but because it works through gene expression, its biologic effect lasts 12 to 36 hours. Anti-inflammatory effects take several hours to appear.", "sidefx": "Most side effects follow from the same gene effects that calm inflammation. Gluconeogenesis and insulin resistance raise blood sugar. Immune suppression raises the risk of infection and hides its signs. Mild mineralocorticoid action causes some fluid retention and blood pressure rise. Brain effects include insomnia, mood swings and psychosis. Long-term use causes osteoporosis, muscle wasting, thin skin, cataracts, weight gain and Cushing features. Long courses suppress the adrenal glands, so sudden stopping can cause adrenal crisis, which is why long courses are tapered. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Solu-Medrol (methylprednisolone) FDA label", "Hench PS et al. Proc Staff Meet Mayo Clin 1949", "Goodman & Gilman's"]}, "metoclopramide": {"origin": "Metoclopramide was developed in France in the early 1960s. Louis Justin-Besançon and Charles Laville, working with the company Laboratoires Delagrange, were studying derivatives of procainamide, the antiarrhythmic drug, and found that some benzamide compounds had strong antinausea and gut-stimulating effects. Metoclopramide was introduced in Europe in the 1960s. It reached the US market in the late 1970s as Reglan, made by A.H. Robins. It became widely used for diabetic gastroparesis, reflux and nausea. Concern about tardive dyskinesia with long-term use led the FDA to add a boxed warning in 2009. A nasal spray, Gimoti, was approved in 2020.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Justin-Besançon and Laville develop metoclopramide in France."], ["1970s", "Metoclopramide reaches the US market as Reglan."], ["2009", "FDA adds a boxed warning about tardive dyskinesia."], ["2020", "FDA approves metoclopramide nasal spray (Gimoti) for diabetic gastroparesis."]], "cell": "Metoclopramide blocks dopamine D2 receptors. In the chemoreceptor trigger zone of the area postrema, which lies outside the blood-brain barrier, dopamine signals trigger nausea and vomiting, so blocking D2 there is antiemetic. At higher levels it also blocks 5-HT3 receptors, adding to its antiemetic effect. In the gut, dopamine normally slows motility by inhibiting acetylcholine release from enteric neurons. Blocking enteric D2 receptors and stimulating 5-HT4 receptors on these neurons increases acetylcholine release. This speeds gastric emptying, increases lower esophageal sphincter tone and coordinates antral and duodenal contractions, with little effect on the colon. D2 blockade in the basal ganglia and pituitary explains movement and prolactin side effects.", "body": [["Brain (vomiting center)", "D2 blockade in the chemoreceptor trigger zone reduces nausea and vomiting."], ["Stomach", "Speeds gastric emptying and coordinates contractions, helping diabetic gastroparesis."], ["Esophagus", "Increases lower esophageal sphincter tone, reducing reflux."], ["Basal ganglia", "D2 blockade causes restlessness (akathisia), acute dystonia and, with long use, tardive dyskinesia."], ["Pituitary", "D2 blockade removes dopamine's brake on prolactin, causing breast swelling, milk discharge and missed periods."], ["Heart and vessels", "IV doses given too fast can cause anxiety and, rarely, blood pressure changes."]], "journey": "Metoclopramide is absorbed quickly by mouth, but first-pass metabolism lowers and varies its availability. It can also be given IV, IM or as a nasal spray. IV doses act within minutes. It crosses the blood-brain barrier and the placenta and enters breast milk. The liver metabolizes it partly through CYP2D6, and poor metabolizers have higher levels. About 85 percent leaves in the urine, partly unchanged, so kidney disease requires lower doses. Its half-life is about five to six hours.", "sidefx": "Most serious side effects come from blocking D2 receptors in the brain. In the basal ganglia, dopamine block upsets the dopamine and acetylcholine balance, causing akathisia, acute dystonia and parkinsonism, which respond to diphenhydramine or benztropine. Long-term or high-dose use can cause tardive dyskinesia, involuntary movements that may be permanent, so treatment beyond 12 weeks is avoided. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is rare. Raised prolactin causes breast and menstrual changes. Depression and suicidal thoughts have been reported. Fast IV injection can cause sudden anxiety and restlessness. It is avoided in bowel obstruction because it increases gut movement.", "fun": "Metoclopramide was discovered by tinkering with procainamide, a heart rhythm drug, which shows how small chemical changes can redirect a molecule to a new organ.", "src": ["Reglan (metoclopramide) FDA label", "Gimoti FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "metolazone": {"origin": "Metolazone is a thiazide-like diuretic. The thiazide family began in 1957, when Karl Beyer, James Sprague, John Baer and Frederick Novello at Merck developed chlorothiazide, the first safe oral diuretic for everyday use. Chlorothiazide changed the treatment of high blood pressure and heart failure, and chemists then made many related drugs. Metolazone has a quinazoline structure instead of the benzothiadiazine ring of true thiazides, but it acts at the same site in the kidney. It was introduced in the US in the 1970s as Zaroxolyn. It is valued because, unlike most thiazides, it still works when kidney function is poor, and it is used with loop diuretics to overcome diuretic resistance in heart failure.", "timeline": [["1957", "Merck scientists develop chlorothiazide, the first thiazide diuretic."], ["1970s", "Metolazone (Zaroxolyn) is introduced in the US."]], "cell": "Metolazone inhibits the sodium-chloride cotransporter (NCC) on the luminal membrane of cells in the distal convoluted tubule. NCC normally reabsorbs about 5 percent of filtered sodium. Blocking it keeps sodium, chloride and water in the tubule, so urine output rises. It also has some action in the proximal tubule. More sodium reaching the collecting duct leads to more potassium and hydrogen secretion through ENaC and ROMK channels, causing potassium loss and alkalosis. In contrast to loop diuretics, it increases calcium reabsorption in the distal tubule. Combined with a loop diuretic, it blocks the distal tubule's compensatory sodium reabsorption that develops with chronic loop diuretic use, producing sequential nephron blockade and a powerful diuresis.", "body": [["Kidneys", "Increases sodium, chloride and water excretion, and still works at a low glomerular filtration rate."], ["Heart", "Removes excess fluid in heart failure, especially when added to a loop diuretic in diuretic resistance."], ["Blood vessels", "Lowers blood pressure, first through volume loss and later through reduced vascular resistance."], ["Electrolytes", "Causes low potassium, low sodium and low magnesium, which can be severe with loop diuretics."], ["Blood chemistry", "Can raise uric acid, glucose and calcium, like other thiazides."]], "journey": "Metolazone is taken by mouth and absorbed somewhat slowly and incompletely, with diuresis beginning in about an hour. Much of it binds to red blood cells and plasma proteins. It is barely metabolized and is excreted mostly unchanged in the urine. It reaches its tubular site by secretion in the proximal tubule. Its half-life is about 14 hours, and the diuretic effect lasts 12 to 24 hours. Different products are not interchangeable because their absorption differs.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow from blocking sodium and chloride reabsorption. More sodium delivered to the collecting duct drives potassium and hydrogen loss, causing hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis. It impairs the kidney's ability to make dilute urine, which explains hyponatremia, especially in older adults. Magnesium is also lost. Volume depletion causes dizziness and a rise in creatinine. Combined with a loop diuretic, these effects can be dramatic, so electrolytes are watched closely. Like thiazides it can raise uric acid, calcium and blood sugar. It contains a sulfonamide group, so sulfa allergy reactions are possible. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Zaroxolyn (metolazone) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "metronidazole": {"origin": "Metronidazole started with a soil microbe. In the 1950s Japanese researchers isolated azomycin, a 2-nitroimidazole, from soil bacteria, and it showed activity against the protozoan Trichomonas vaginalis. Chemists at the French company Rhône-Poulenc then synthesized many related nitroimidazoles and found that metronidazole, a 5-nitroimidazole, was safe and effective against trichomoniasis. It was introduced as Flagyl around 1960. In 1962 a dentist noticed that a patient taking metronidazole for trichomoniasis had a sudden cure of an anaerobic gum infection. That observation led to its use against anaerobic bacteria. It became a key drug for anaerobic infections, amebiasis, giardiasis and C. difficile infection.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Azomycin, a nitroimidazole, is isolated from soil bacteria in Japan."], ["c. 1960", "Rhône-Poulenc introduces metronidazole (Flagyl) for trichomoniasis."], ["1962", "A dentist reports metronidazole cleared an anaerobic gum infection."], ["1963", "FDA approves metronidazole in the US."]], "cell": "Metronidazole is a prodrug that enters cells by passive diffusion. Inside anaerobic bacteria and protozoa, low-redox electron carriers such as ferredoxin, reduced by pyruvate-ferredoxin oxidoreductase, donate electrons to its nitro group. This reduction creates short-lived, highly reactive nitro radical intermediates. These radicals bind and break DNA strands and destabilize the helix, killing the organism. The reduction also keeps the gradient pulling more drug into the cell. Aerobic human cells lack these low-redox carriers, and oxygen quickly reoxidizes any radicals that do form, which explains its selectivity for anaerobes. It is bactericidal with concentration-dependent killing.", "body": [["Infection sites", "Kills anaerobic bacteria such as Bacteroides and Clostridioides, and protozoa such as Trichomonas, Giardia and Entamoeba."], ["Gut", "Treats C. difficile infection and amebic colitis, but can cause nausea, diarrhea and metallic taste."], ["Brain", "Crosses the blood-brain barrier well, treating brain abscesses but rarely causing encephalopathy or seizures."], ["Nerves", "Long courses can cause peripheral neuropathy, usually in the hands and feet."], ["Alcohol metabolism", "Can cause a disulfiram-like reaction with flushing, nausea and vomiting when alcohol is used."], ["Urine", "A metabolite can turn urine dark red-brown, which is harmless."]], "journey": "Metronidazole is almost completely absorbed by mouth, so oral and IV doses are nearly equivalent. It is also used topically and vaginally. It binds little to plasma proteins and spreads widely, reaching abscesses, bone, cerebrospinal fluid, saliva and breast milk, and it crosses the placenta. The liver metabolizes it by oxidation and glucuronidation, including an active hydroxy metabolite. Metabolites and some unchanged drug leave mostly in the urine. Its half-life is about eight hours, longer in liver disease.", "sidefx": "Nausea and metallic taste are common. The disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol is often attributed to inhibition of aldehyde dehydrogenase, although the mechanism is debated, so alcohol is avoided during and shortly after treatment. Neurotoxicity, including peripheral neuropathy, cerebellar ataxia, encephalopathy and seizures, is linked to longer courses and higher total exposure, likely from reactive metabolites in nerve tissue. It inhibits CYP2C9 and increases the effect of warfarin. It carries a boxed warning based on cancer in rodents. Dark urine from a metabolite is harmless. There is no antidote; neurologic effects usually improve after stopping.", "fun": "Metronidazole's anaerobic power was found by chance in 1962, when a dentist noticed that a patient's acute ulcerative gingivitis cleared while on treatment for trichomoniasis.", "src": ["Flagyl (metronidazole) FDA label", "Shinn DLS. Lancet 1962", "Goodman & Gilman's"]}, "mexiletine": {"origin": "Mexiletine was developed by the German company Boehringer Ingelheim in the 1970s. Doctors already used IV lidocaine for ventricular arrhythmias, but lidocaine cannot be taken by mouth because the liver destroys it on first pass. Mexiletine was designed as a lidocaine-like drug that survives oral dosing. It was approved in the US as Mexitil in 1985 for life-threatening ventricular arrhythmias. After the CAST trial in 1989 showed that some sodium channel blockers increased death rates after heart attack, use of Class I drugs fell. Mexiletine remains useful in selected arrhythmias, such as long QT syndrome type 3, and for muscle stiffness in myotonic disorders. The US brand was later discontinued, but generics remain.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Boehringer Ingelheim develops mexiletine."], ["1985", "FDA approves mexiletine (Mexitil) for ventricular arrhythmias."], ["1989", "CAST trial raises concerns about Class I antiarrhythmics after heart attack."]], "cell": "Mexiletine is a Class IB antiarrhythmic that blocks fast voltage-gated sodium channels (Nav1.5) in heart muscle. Like lidocaine, it binds quickly to channels in the open and inactivated states and lets go quickly. Normal cells at a normal heart rate recover between beats, so they are little affected. Ischemic or depolarized cells, where channels spend more time inactivated, and cells firing very fast are blocked more. This reduces abnormal automaticity and slows conduction in damaged tissue, interrupting reentry circuits. It shortens the action potential duration and refractory period in Purkinje fibers and ventricular muscle. It also blocks the late sodium current, which is why it helps long QT syndrome type 3. In skeletal muscle, sodium channel block reduces repetitive firing and myotonia.", "body": [["Ventricles", "Suppresses ventricular ectopy and ventricular tachycardia, especially in damaged or ischemic tissue."], ["Atria and AV node", "Has little effect on atrial tissue, so it is not used for atrial arrhythmias."], ["QT interval", "Shortens the QT interval in long QT syndrome type 3 by blocking the late sodium current."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Reduces muscle stiffness in myotonic disorders by limiting repetitive firing."], ["Brain", "Causes tremor, dizziness, blurred vision and unsteadiness through sodium channel effects."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and heartburn are common and are reduced by taking it with food."]], "journey": "Mexiletine is absorbed well by mouth, and unlike lidocaine it largely escapes first-pass metabolism, with high bioavailability. It is taken with food to limit stomach upset. It is moderately protein bound and crosses the blood-brain barrier and placenta. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP2D6 and CYP1A2 into inactive metabolites, and about 10 percent leaves unchanged in the urine. Acidic urine speeds its excretion and alkaline urine slows it. Its half-life is about 10 to 12 hours. Drugs that change CYP1A2 activity, such as fluvoxamine and smoking, can alter levels.", "sidefx": "Nausea, vomiting and heartburn are common and come partly from local irritation. Tremor, dizziness, unsteadiness and blurred vision come from sodium channel block in the brain and are dose-related. Like all Class I drugs, it can provoke new or worse arrhythmias, especially in people with structural heart disease, and it carries a boxed warning based on the CAST trial. Rare liver injury, low blood counts and severe skin reactions such as DRESS can occur. In overdose, wide QRS complexes and seizures can occur. Sodium bicarbonate is used for sodium channel toxicity, and IV lipid emulsion is considered in severe cases.", "src": ["Mexiletine hydrochloride capsules FDA label", "CAST Investigators. NEJM 1989", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "micafungin": {"origin": "Micafungin is an echinocandin, a class of antifungals derived from natural compounds made by fungi. The first echinocandins were discovered in the 1970s, but early versions were too toxic or poorly soluble. Scientists at the Japanese company Fujisawa (now Astellas) found a natural lipopeptide called FR901379, produced by the fungus Coleophoma empetri, that blocked fungal cell wall synthesis. They modified its side chain to make it safer and more stable, creating micafungin. The FDA approved it as Mycamine in 2005, after caspofungin in 2001. Echinocandins gave doctors a new way to treat Candida infections, including strains resistant to fluconazole, with fewer drug interactions and kidney problems than older antifungals.", "timeline": [["1970s", "The first echinocandin compounds are discovered."], ["2001", "FDA approves caspofungin, the first echinocandin."], ["2005", "FDA approves micafungin (Mycamine)."]], "cell": "The fungal cell wall is made of chitin, mannoproteins and beta-1,3-D-glucan, a sugar polymer that gives the wall its strength. Micafungin noncompetitively inhibits beta-1,3-D-glucan synthase, an enzyme in the fungal plasma membrane (the Fks1 subunit). Without new glucan, the wall becomes weak and cannot withstand osmotic pressure, so the cell swells and bursts. Human cells have no cell wall and no glucan synthase, which explains the drug's safety. It is fungicidal against Candida species and fungistatic against Aspergillus, where it damages growing hyphal tips. Resistance comes from mutations in the FKS genes. It has no useful activity against Cryptococcus, which has little beta-1,3-glucan in its wall.", "body": [["Infection sites", "Kills Candida in the blood and esophagus, including many fluconazole-resistant strains, and is used to prevent fungal infection in stem cell transplant."], ["Liver", "Can raise liver enzymes and, rarely, cause liver injury."], ["Blood", "Rarely causes hemolysis or hemolytic anemia."], ["Kidneys", "Not cleared by the kidneys, so no dose change is needed in kidney failure, and it does not cause kidney toxicity like amphotericin B."], ["Infusion reactions", "Histamine-like reactions, such as flushing, rash and itching, can occur during the infusion."]], "journey": "Micafungin is a large molecule that is not absorbed by mouth, so it is given IV once daily. It is over 99 percent bound to plasma proteins, mainly albumin, and distributes into tissues such as lung, liver and spleen, but little enters the brain, eye or urine. It is metabolized in the liver by arylsulfatase and catechol-O-methyltransferase, with a minor role for CYP3A, and has few drug interactions. Metabolites leave mainly in the feces. Its half-life is about 14 to 17 hours.", "sidefx": "Because humans have no glucan synthase, micafungin is generally well tolerated. Nausea, headache and fever are common. Liver enzyme rises reflect its liver metabolism and are usually mild, but rare serious liver injury has occurred. Rare hemolysis has also been reported. Infusion-related histamine release can cause flushing, rash and itching, and anaphylaxis is rare. In rat studies, high doses over long periods caused liver tumors, which is noted on the label. There is no antidote, and it is not removed by dialysis because it is highly protein bound.", "fun": "Echinocandins are sometimes called the penicillin of antifungals because, like penicillin, they target a cell wall that human cells do not have.", "src": ["Mycamine (micafungin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "midazolam": {"origin": "Midazolam was made in the lab, not found in nature. In the 1970s chemists at Hoffmann-La Roche, the company behind diazepam (Valium), set out to build a benzodiazepine that would dissolve in water and act and wear off quickly. Armin Walser and Rodney Ian Fryer synthesized midazolam in 1975. Its fused imidazole ring lets it be water soluble in an acidic vial and then turn fat soluble at body pH, so it enters the brain fast without the painful, oily solvent that injected diazepam needs. The FDA approved it as Versed in 1985. Early use for sedation brought reports of deaths from respiratory depression, which led to slower, smaller titration and closer monitoring. It went on to become the standard drug for procedural sedation, and later nasal and intramuscular forms made it a first-line seizure rescue drug outside the hospital.", "timeline": [["1975", "Walser and Fryer at Hoffmann-La Roche synthesize midazolam."], ["1985", "FDA approves midazolam injection as Versed."], ["2018", "An intramuscular form (Seizalam) is approved for status epilepticus in adults."], ["2019", "FDA approves Nayzilam nasal spray for seizure clusters."]], "cell": "The GABA-A receptor is a chloride channel made of five subunits. Benzodiazepines bind a separate site between the alpha and gamma subunits, not the GABA site itself. Midazolam is a positive allosteric modulator: it does nothing alone, but when GABA is bound it increases how often the chloride channel opens. More chloride flows into the neuron, the membrane becomes more negative (hyperpolarized), and the cell is harder to fire. Receptors with the alpha-1 subunit mainly mediate sedation and amnesia, while alpha-2 and alpha-3 containing receptors mediate anxiety relief and muscle relaxation. Widespread inhibition in the cortex stops seizure spread, and inhibition in the brainstem lowers the drive to breathe, especially when combined with opioids or alcohol.", "body": [["Brain", "Calms anxiety, causes drowsiness and sleep, and blocks the forming of new memories (anterograde amnesia), which is why patients often do not remember a procedure."], ["Seizure circuits", "Boosting GABA inhibition stops the runaway firing of a seizure, which is why it is a first-line rescue drug for status epilepticus."], ["Breathing", "Depresses the brainstem response to carbon dioxide and relaxes upper airway muscles, so breathing slows and the airway can obstruct, especially with opioids."], ["Heart and vessels", "Lowers sympathetic tone and relaxes vessels, so blood pressure can drop, most in elderly, hypovolemic or critically ill patients."], ["Muscles", "Spinal and brainstem effects relax skeletal muscle tone."]], "journey": "Midazolam can be given IV, IM, intranasally or by mouth. It is very fat soluble at body pH, so it reaches the brain within minutes and then redistributes into fat and muscle, which ends the effect of a single dose fairly quickly. The liver breaks it down with CYP3A4 into alpha-hydroxymidazolam, which is active and is then glucuronidated and excreted by the kidneys. The half-life is short, a few hours, but it grows longer in the elderly, in obesity, in liver failure and with CYP3A4 inhibitors. Long infusions build up in fat and the active metabolite builds up in kidney failure, so waking can be delayed. It crosses the placenta.", "sidefx": "Sedation, amnesia and slurred speech are the expected brain effects of extra GABA inhibition. The dangerous effect is respiratory depression and apnea, from brainstem inhibition and loss of airway tone; risk climbs sharply with opioids, alcohol and fast pushes. Low blood pressure comes from reduced sympathetic tone. Some patients, especially children and the elderly, become paradoxically agitated. Flumazenil is a competitive antagonist at the benzodiazepine site and can reverse sedation, but it is used rarely because it can trigger seizures in people dependent on benzodiazepines or after mixed overdoses. Airway support is the mainstay.", "fun": "The imidazole ring in midazolam opens and closes with pH, so the same molecule is water soluble in the vial and fat soluble in the bloodstream.", "src": ["Midazolam injection FDA label", "Nayzilam (midazolam nasal spray) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "midodrine": {"origin": "Midodrine is a synthetic drug designed as a prodrug. It was developed in Europe, where it was used for low blood pressure for years before it reached the US. In the United States the FDA approved it in 1996 as ProAmatine for symptomatic orthostatic hypotension. It was one of the first drugs approved under the accelerated approval pathway based on a surrogate marker, a rise in standing blood pressure, with the requirement that the maker prove real clinical benefit later. That follow-up evidence was slow to come, and in 2010 the FDA proposed withdrawing the approval, which drew strong objections from patients who depended on it. The drug stayed on the market. It mattered because people with autonomic failure, such as from Parkinson disease or diabetic neuropathy, had few options to stay upright without fainting.", "timeline": [["1996", "FDA grants accelerated approval to midodrine (ProAmatine) for symptomatic orthostatic hypotension."], ["2010", "FDA proposes withdrawal over missing confirmatory trials; the drug remains available after public response."]], "cell": "Midodrine itself is inactive. After it is absorbed, enzymes in many tissues remove a glycine group (deglycination), producing desglymidodrine. Desglymidodrine is a selective agonist at alpha-1 adrenergic receptors on vascular smooth muscle. Alpha-1 receptors are Gq coupled: activation turns on phospholipase C, which makes IP3 and DAG. IP3 releases calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, and calcium-calmodulin activates myosin light chain kinase, so the muscle contracts. Arterioles narrow, raising peripheral resistance, and veins tighten, pushing more blood back to the heart. Standing blood pressure rises. Desglymidodrine does not cross the blood-brain barrier well and has almost no beta activity, so it does not directly stimulate the heart.", "body": [["Arteries", "Arterioles constrict, raising systemic vascular resistance and blood pressure, which helps keep blood flowing to the brain on standing."], ["Veins", "Venous tone increases, so less blood pools in the legs and more returns to the heart."], ["Heart", "The heart is not stimulated directly. The rise in pressure triggers the baroreceptor reflex, which can slow the heart rate."], ["Skin", "Alpha-1 receptors on the tiny muscles at hair follicles contract, causing goosebumps, and scalp tingling and itch are common."], ["Bladder", "Alpha-1 stimulation tightens the bladder neck and internal sphincter, which can cause urinary hesitancy or retention."]], "journey": "Midodrine is taken by mouth and absorbed quickly. It is then converted in tissues to the active desglymidodrine, which peaks in the blood about one to two hours after a dose. The active metabolite has a half-life of about three to four hours, so the effect lasts only a few hours, which is why it is timed to daytime hours when the patient is upright. It is cleared mostly by the kidneys, so levels rise in kidney disease. Because it barely enters the brain, it does not cause much nervousness or insomnia.", "sidefx": "Every side effect traces to alpha-1 stimulation. Constricted vessels raise pressure even when the patient lies down, so supine hypertension is the key danger and the reason for the boxed warning; the last dose is given well before bedtime and patients sleep with the head raised. Goosebumps, scalp itch and tingling come from follicle and skin receptors. Urinary retention comes from tightening of the bladder outlet. Slow heart rate is a reflex response to the higher pressure. There is no specific antidote; for severe hypertension an alpha blocker such as phentolamine can counter the effect.", "src": ["Midodrine hydrochloride FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "milrinone": {"origin": "Milrinone came from a search in the late 1970s and 1980s for a heart-strengthening drug that did not work through beta receptors. Researchers at Sterling-Winthrop first developed amrinone, a bipyridine that increased contractility but caused frequent low platelet counts. Milrinone is a close chemical relative that turned out to be far more potent with much less effect on platelets. It was approved in the US in the late 1980s as an IV drug, sold as Primacor. An oral version was tested in long-term heart failure, but the PROMISE trial, published in 1991, found that oral milrinone increased death rates. That result taught a lasting lesson: forcing a failing heart to work harder can help for days but harm over months. Today milrinone is used only short term, IV, in hospitals.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Sterling-Winthrop develops amrinone and then the more potent milrinone."], ["1991", "The PROMISE trial shows that long-term oral milrinone increases mortality in heart failure."]], "cell": "Milrinone inhibits phosphodiesterase-3 (PDE3), the enzyme that breaks down cyclic AMP in heart muscle and vascular smooth muscle. In heart cells, cAMP normally rises when beta-1 receptors are stimulated; milrinone raises cAMP downstream of the receptor, so it still works when beta receptors are blocked or downregulated. More cAMP activates protein kinase A, which phosphorylates L-type calcium channels (more calcium entry) and phospholamban (faster calcium reuptake into the sarcoplasmic reticulum). The result is a stronger contraction and faster relaxation. In vascular smooth muscle, more cAMP and PKA activity lower the activity of myosin light chain kinase, so arteries and veins relax. Both systemic and pulmonary vascular resistance fall. The combined effect is often called an inodilator.", "body": [["Heart muscle", "Contractions become stronger and the muscle relaxes faster between beats, so cardiac output rises."], ["Systemic vessels", "Arteries and veins relax, lowering afterload and preload, which eases the work of the failing heart but can drop blood pressure."], ["Lungs", "Pulmonary arteries dilate, lowering pulmonary vascular resistance, which is helpful when the right ventricle is struggling."], ["Heart rhythm", "Higher cAMP and calcium make heart cells more excitable, which can cause atrial and ventricular arrhythmias."], ["Kidneys", "Better cardiac output can improve kidney blood flow, but the kidneys are also its main exit, so kidney failure lets it build up."]], "journey": "Milrinone is given only by IV infusion, sometimes after a loading dose. It acts within minutes. It is not changed much by the liver; most of it leaves unchanged in the urine. The half-life is a little over two hours in healthy kidneys but much longer in kidney failure, so the infusion must be reduced in renal impairment and effects can linger after it is stopped. It is moderately bound to plasma proteins.", "sidefx": "Low blood pressure is the most common problem and follows directly from relaxation of vascular smooth muscle. Arrhythmias, including ventricular ectopy and atrial fibrillation, come from higher cAMP and calcium in heart cells, the same pathway that boosts contraction. That extra calcium handling and oxygen demand over the long term is thought to be why oral PDE3 inhibition raised mortality. Headache follows vasodilation. Low platelet counts can occur but are much less common than with amrinone. There is no antidote; treatment is slowing or stopping the infusion and supporting blood pressure.", "fun": "Milrinone's cousin amrinone was renamed inamrinone in the US because its name was too easily confused with amiodarone.", "src": ["Milrinone lactate injection FDA label", "Packer M et al. Effect of oral milrinone on mortality in severe chronic heart failure (PROMISE). NEJM 1991", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "minoxidil": {"origin": "Minoxidil was developed by the Upjohn Company. It was first studied in the late 1950s and 1960s as a possible treatment for stomach ulcers, but it proved to be a powerful dilator of arteries instead. Upjohn developed it as a pill for severe high blood pressure, and the FDA approved it as Loniten in 1979. Doctors soon noticed an unexpected side effect: many patients grew new hair on their face, arms and scalp. Upjohn turned that side effect into a topical solution for male pattern baldness, approved as Rogaine in 1988, the first FDA-approved drug for hair loss. In 1996 topical minoxidil became available over the counter. It is a classic example of a side effect becoming a new use.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Upjohn researchers begin studying the compound, first for ulcers."], ["1979", "FDA approves oral minoxidil (Loniten) for severe hypertension."], ["1988", "FDA approves topical minoxidil (Rogaine) for hair loss."], ["1996", "Topical minoxidil becomes available over the counter."]], "cell": "Minoxidil is a prodrug. A sulfotransferase enzyme, mainly in the liver, converts it to minoxidil sulfate, the active form. Minoxidil sulfate opens ATP-sensitive potassium channels (KATP) in arteriolar smooth muscle. Potassium flows out, the cell membrane hyperpolarizes, and voltage-gated L-type calcium channels stay closed. With less calcium inside, myosin light chain kinase is less active and the muscle relaxes. The effect is strong on arterioles and minimal on veins, so afterload falls sharply while preload is largely unchanged. In hair follicles, opening KATP channels and increasing local blood flow is thought to prolong the anagen (growth) phase and enlarge shrunken follicles, though the exact hair mechanism is not fully understood.", "body": [["Arterioles", "Powerful relaxation lowers systemic vascular resistance and blood pressure, even in hypertension that has not responded to other drugs."], ["Heart", "The baroreceptor reflex senses the pressure drop and speeds the heart and increases its force, which can cause palpitations and angina; this is why a beta blocker is given with it."], ["Kidneys", "Lower pressure triggers renin release and sodium and water retention, causing edema; a loop diuretic is given with it to counter this."], ["Pericardium", "Fluid can collect around the heart, causing pericardial effusion and, rarely, tamponade."], ["Hair and skin", "Hair growth increases over the face and body (hypertrichosis) with the oral form; topical use stimulates scalp follicles."]], "journey": "Oral minoxidil is almost completely absorbed from the gut and works within about half an hour. The liver turns some of it into the active minoxidil sulfate, and most of the rest is glucuronidated. The drug and its metabolites leave through the kidneys. The blood half-life is about four hours, but the blood pressure effect can last a day or longer, because the active form stays bound in the arteriolar wall. Topical minoxidil is absorbed through the scalp only in small amounts and is converted to the active sulfate by enzymes in the hair follicles.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are the body fighting back against a large drop in blood pressure. Reflex tachycardia and increased heart work can trigger angina. Renin release and kidney sodium retention cause fluid buildup and weight gain. Pericardial effusion can occur, sometimes progressing to tamponade, which is why the oral form carries a boxed warning. Hair growth all over the body follows the follicle effect. ECG T-wave changes are common. There is no antidote; severe hypotension is treated with fluids and, if needed, a pure alpha agonist such as phenylephrine rather than epinephrine, which would worsen the fast heart rate.", "fun": "Rogaine was the first FDA-approved drug to treat baldness, and it exists only because doctors noticed hair growing on patients taking a blood pressure pill.", "src": ["Minoxidil tablets FDA label", "Rogaine (minoxidil topical) labeling", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "mirtazapine": {"origin": "Mirtazapine was developed by the Dutch company Organon. It is a close chemical relative of mianserin, an older Organon antidepressant. Chemists added a nitrogen to the mianserin structure, which changed its receptor profile, giving a drug that blocks alpha-2 receptors and certain serotonin receptors rather than blocking reuptake. It was launched in Europe in the mid 1990s and approved by the FDA as Remeron in 1996. It offered a different mechanism from the SSRIs that dominated at the time, with less sexual dysfunction and nausea but more sleepiness and weight gain. Those same side effects made it a useful choice for depressed patients who cannot sleep or have lost weight.", "timeline": [["1996", "FDA approves mirtazapine (Remeron) for major depression."]], "cell": "Mirtazapine blocks presynaptic alpha-2 adrenergic receptors. These receptors normally act as a brake: when norepinephrine binds them on its own nerve endings (autoreceptors), release slows; when it binds them on serotonin neurons (heteroreceptors), serotonin release slows. Blocking the brake increases release of both norepinephrine and serotonin. Mirtazapine also blocks postsynaptic 5-HT2A, 5-HT2C and 5-HT3 receptors, so the extra serotonin acts mainly through 5-HT1A receptors, which are linked to antidepressant and anti-anxiety effects. Blocking 5-HT2 reduces sexual side effects and insomnia, and blocking 5-HT3 reduces nausea. It is also a potent H1 histamine antagonist, which causes sedation and increased appetite.", "body": [["Brain mood circuits", "More norepinephrine and serotonin signaling, mainly through 5-HT1A receptors, gradually lifts depression over weeks."], ["Sleep centers", "Strong H1 histamine blockade causes drowsiness, which helps patients with insomnia."], ["Appetite and weight", "H1 and 5-HT2C blockade increase appetite and weight, useful in patients who have lost weight but unwanted in others."], ["Gut", "5-HT3 blockade means it causes little nausea, unlike SSRIs."], ["Blood", "Rarely it lowers white blood cells (agranulocytosis), so fever or sore throat needs a blood count."]], "journey": "Mirtazapine is taken by mouth, as a tablet or a tablet that dissolves on the tongue. About half of a dose reaches the bloodstream after first-pass metabolism. It is about 85 percent bound to plasma proteins and crosses into the brain. The liver breaks it down using CYP2D6, CYP1A2 and CYP3A4, and the metabolites leave mostly in the urine. The half-life is long, about 20 to 40 hours, so it is given once daily at bedtime. Clearance is lower in the elderly and in liver or kidney disease.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness and increased appetite with weight gain come from H1 histamine blockade and 5-HT2C blockade. Sedation can be paradoxically stronger at lower doses, when antihistamine effects dominate over noradrenergic effects. Dry mouth and dizziness are common. Cholesterol can rise. Rare agranulocytosis is the most serious blood risk. Like all antidepressants it carries a boxed warning for suicidal thoughts in young people, and combining it with other serotonergic drugs can contribute to serotonin syndrome. It is relatively safe in overdose compared with tricyclic antidepressants. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Remeron (mirtazapine) FDA label", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "misoprostol": {"origin": "Misoprostol is a synthetic copy of prostaglandin E1, one of the lipid messengers studied by Sune Bergstrom, Bengt Samuelsson and John Vane, who shared the 1982 Nobel Prize for prostaglandin research. Natural prostaglandins break down in seconds and cannot be taken as pills. Chemists at G.D. Searle changed the structure, moving a hydroxyl group and adding a methyl group, so the molecule lasts long enough to work when swallowed. The FDA approved it as Cytotec in 1988 to prevent stomach ulcers in people taking NSAIDs, which block the body's own protective prostaglandins. Its strong effect on the uterus was obvious from the start and appeared as a warning on the label. Because it is cheap, heat stable and works by many routes, it became an important drug in obstetrics worldwide, especially where refrigerated oxytocin is not available.", "timeline": [["1982", "Bergstrom, Samuelsson and Vane share the Nobel Prize for prostaglandin research."], ["1988", "FDA approves misoprostol (Cytotec) to prevent NSAID-induced gastric ulcers."], ["2000", "FDA approves mifepristone for use with misoprostol for early pregnancy termination."]], "cell": "After absorption, misoprostol is converted to misoprostol acid, which binds prostaglandin E receptors (EP receptors). In the stomach, it acts on EP3 receptors on parietal cells. EP3 is coupled to Gi, which lowers cyclic AMP and so reduces activity of the proton pump, decreasing acid. It also stimulates surface cells to secrete mucus and bicarbonate and supports mucosal blood flow. In the uterus, binding to EP receptors on myometrial smooth muscle raises intracellular calcium, causing strong contractions. In the cervix, prostaglandin signaling breaks down and rearranges collagen, softening and opening it (ripening). Together these effects explain ulcer protection, labor induction and control of bleeding after birth.", "body": [["Stomach", "Lowers acid secretion and boosts the protective mucus and bicarbonate layer, replacing the prostaglandins that NSAIDs block."], ["Uterus", "Causes firm contractions that can expel uterine contents or clamp down on bleeding vessels after delivery."], ["Cervix", "Softens and dilates the cervix by remodeling its collagen, which helps labor induction."], ["Intestines", "Increases fluid secretion and gut movement, causing cramping and diarrhea, the most common side effect."], ["Temperature control", "Prostaglandins act on the hypothalamus to raise the set point, causing fever and shivering, especially with higher exposure."]], "journey": "Misoprostol can be given by mouth, under the tongue, in the cheek, vaginally or rectally. It is rapidly de-esterified to its active form, misoprostol acid. Under the tongue gives the fastest, highest peak; vaginal and rectal routes give lower but longer-lasting levels, which suit the uterus. The active acid has a short half-life of under an hour. It is broken down by fatty acid oxidation pathways and leaves mainly in the urine. It does not need refrigeration, a major advantage in low-resource settings.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea and abdominal cramping come from prostaglandin stimulation of gut secretion and motility. Fever and shivering come from prostaglandin action on the hypothalamic temperature center. The serious risks are in the uterus: overly strong contractions (hyperstimulation) can stress the fetus, and in a uterus with a prior scar the contractions can cause uterine rupture. For this reason it carries a boxed warning against use for ulcer prevention in pregnancy. There is no antidote; treatment is supportive.", "fun": "Misoprostol is on the WHO Model List of Essential Medicines partly because it can be stored at room temperature, unlike oxytocin.", "src": ["Cytotec (misoprostol) FDA label", "WHO recommendations on uterotonics for postpartum hemorrhage", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "morphine": {"origin": "Morphine comes from the opium poppy, Papaver somniferum. Opium, the dried milky juice of the unripe seed pod, has been used for pain and sleep since ancient times. Around 1804 to 1805 a young German pharmacist's apprentice, Friedrich Serturner, isolated the main active substance from opium. He named it after Morpheus, the Greek god of dreams. It was the first alkaloid ever isolated from a plant, and it showed that a plant's medicinal power could come from a single pure chemical. The Merck company began selling morphine commercially in 1827. The invention of the hypodermic syringe in the 1850s made injected morphine common, which also brought widespread dependence, including among wounded soldiers. Its complex structure was worked out by Robert Robinson in 1925, and it remains the reference opioid against which others are compared.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Opium from the poppy is used for pain and sleep."], ["c. 1805", "Friedrich Serturner isolates morphine from opium."], ["1827", "Merck begins commercial production of morphine."], ["1850s", "The hypodermic syringe makes injected morphine widespread."], ["1925", "Robert Robinson determines the structure of morphine."]], "cell": "Morphine is a full agonist at mu-opioid receptors, which are Gi/Go protein coupled receptors. Binding inhibits adenylyl cyclase, lowering cAMP. On presynaptic nerve endings, it closes voltage-gated calcium channels, so less neurotransmitter, such as glutamate and substance P, is released from pain fibers in the dorsal horn of the spinal cord. On postsynaptic neurons, it opens potassium channels, hyperpolarizing the cell so it fires less. Mu receptors in the periaqueductal gray and rostral ventromedial medulla also activate descending pathways that suppress pain. In the brainstem, mu activation blunts the response to carbon dioxide, causing respiratory depression. Morphine also directly releases histamine from mast cells, which is not opioid receptor mediated.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Blocks pain signals in the spinal cord and changes how pain is perceived, so pain feels less distressing; also causes sedation and euphoria."], ["Breathing", "Lowers the brainstem's response to carbon dioxide, so breathing becomes slow and shallow; this is the cause of death in overdose."], ["Eyes", "Stimulates the Edinger-Westphal nucleus, causing pinpoint pupils."], ["Heart and vessels", "Histamine release and reduced sympathetic tone dilate veins and arteries, lowering preload and blood pressure."], ["Gut", "Reduces propulsive movement and increases sphincter tone, causing constipation; stimulates the chemoreceptor trigger zone, causing nausea."], ["Skin", "Histamine release causes itching, flushing and hives."], ["Bladder", "Increases sphincter tone, which can cause urinary retention."]], "journey": "Morphine can be given IV, IM, under the skin, by mouth, rectally or into the spinal space. By mouth, a large share is destroyed by first-pass metabolism in the liver, so oral doses are higher than IV doses. It is less fat soluble than fentanyl, so it enters the brain more slowly; peak effect after IV is around 20 minutes. The liver glucuronidates it into morphine-3-glucuronide, which is inactive for pain but can cause neuroexcitation, and morphine-6-glucuronide, which is a more potent opioid. Both leave through the kidneys, so in kidney failure they build up and cause prolonged sedation and respiratory depression. The half-life is two to four hours. It crosses the placenta.", "sidefx": "Respiratory depression is the dangerous effect, caused by mu-receptor suppression of the brainstem breathing centers; risk increases with benzodiazepines, alcohol and kidney failure. Constipation comes from mu receptors in the gut and does not fade with tolerance. Nausea and vomiting come from stimulation of the chemoreceptor trigger zone. Itching, flushing and low blood pressure come largely from histamine release. Dependence and tolerance develop with repeated use, and it is a Schedule II controlled substance. Naloxone, a competitive mu antagonist, reverses opioid effects, but it may wear off before morphine does, so patients need continued monitoring.", "fun": "Morphine was the first active compound ever isolated from a plant, which helped launch the field of alkaloid chemistry.", "src": ["Morphine sulfate injection FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "mycophenolate": {"origin": "Mycophenolate comes from a mold. In 1893 the Italian physician Bartolomeo Gosio isolated mycophenolic acid from a Penicillium mold while studying spoiled corn linked to pellagra. It was one of the first antibiotic substances ever purified in crystalline form, decades before penicillin, but it was largely forgotten. In the 1980s scientists at Syntex, including Anthony Allison and Elsie Eugui, recognized that mycophenolic acid blocks a key enzyme that lymphocytes depend on to make DNA. Syntex chemists made a prodrug, mycophenolate mofetil, to improve absorption. The FDA approved it as CellCept in 1995 for preventing kidney transplant rejection. An enteric-coated salt, mycophenolate sodium (Myfortic), followed in 2004. It largely replaced azathioprine in transplant regimens and is also used for lupus nephritis.", "timeline": [["1893", "Bartolomeo Gosio isolates mycophenolic acid from a Penicillium mold."], ["1980s", "Allison and Eugui at Syntex develop it as a lymphocyte-selective immunosuppressant."], ["1995", "FDA approves mycophenolate mofetil (CellCept) for kidney transplant rejection prevention."], ["2004", "FDA approves enteric-coated mycophenolate sodium (Myfortic)."]], "cell": "Mycophenolate mofetil is a prodrug hydrolyzed to mycophenolic acid (MPA). MPA is a reversible, noncompetitive inhibitor of inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase (IMPDH), the rate-limiting enzyme in the de novo synthesis of guanine nucleotides. IMPDH converts IMP to xanthosine monophosphate, which becomes GMP and then GTP and dGTP. Most cells can recycle guanine through the salvage pathway, but activated T and B lymphocytes rely heavily on the de novo pathway, and MPA preferentially inhibits the type II IMPDH isoform that lymphocytes express. Without enough guanine nucleotides, lymphocytes cannot make DNA and stall before the S phase. T-cell and B-cell proliferation and antibody production fall, which prevents rejection and reduces autoimmune damage.", "body": [["Immune system", "Stops activated T and B cells from multiplying, lowering rejection of transplanted organs and reducing antibody production in lupus."], ["Gut", "The gut lining divides quickly and is exposed to high drug levels, causing diarrhea, nausea and abdominal pain."], ["Bone marrow", "Can lower white cells, red cells and platelets, requiring blood count monitoring."], ["Infection defense", "Weakened immunity raises the risk of infections such as CMV, BK virus and, rarely, progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy."], ["Fetus", "Exposure in pregnancy causes miscarriage and birth defects, including ear and facial malformations."]], "journey": "Mycophenolate mofetil is taken by mouth or IV. After oral dosing it is rapidly and almost completely converted to mycophenolic acid. MPA is highly bound to albumin. The liver glucuronidates it to an inactive glucuronide (MPAG), which is excreted in the bile; gut bacteria cleave it back to MPA, which is reabsorbed. This enterohepatic recycling produces a second peak several hours after a dose. The glucuronide leaves mainly through the kidneys. The half-life is about 17 hours. Drugs that interrupt enterohepatic recycling, such as cholestyramine, lower its levels.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea and GI upset happen because gut lining cells divide rapidly and see high local drug concentrations, partly from enterohepatic recycling. Low white blood cell counts reflect effects on marrow precursors. Infections and a higher risk of lymphoma and skin cancer follow from suppressed immune surveillance; these are part of its boxed warning. It is also boxed for embryofetal toxicity, so reliable contraception is required. There is no antidote; serious toxicity is managed by lowering the dose or stopping the drug.", "fun": "Mycophenolic acid was isolated in 1893, making it one of the oldest known antibiotics, yet it took about a century to become a medicine.", "src": ["CellCept (mycophenolate mofetil) FDA label", "Allison AC, Eugui EM. Mycophenolate mofetil and its mechanisms of action. Immunopharmacology 2000", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "n-acetylcysteine": {"origin": "N-acetylcysteine is a modified form of the amino acid cysteine. It was first used in the 1960s as a mucolytic, inhaled to thin thick secretions, and was approved in the US as Mucomyst in 1963. In the early 1970s researchers showed that acetaminophen liver damage is caused by a toxic metabolite that glutathione normally neutralizes, and that liver injury begins when glutathione runs out. That suggested giving a glutathione precursor. In the 1970s Laurie Prescott and colleagues in Edinburgh showed that IV acetylcysteine given early after overdose protected the liver. Oral acetylcysteine was approved for acetaminophen overdose in the US in 1985, and the IV form, Acetadote, was approved in 2004. It turned a once frequently fatal overdose into one that is highly treatable when caught early.", "timeline": [["1963", "Acetylcysteine (Mucomyst) is approved in the US as an inhaled mucolytic."], ["1970s", "Prescott and colleagues show acetylcysteine prevents liver injury after acetaminophen overdose."], ["1985", "Oral acetylcysteine is approved in the US for acetaminophen overdose."], ["2004", "FDA approves IV acetylcysteine (Acetadote)."]], "cell": "Normally most acetaminophen is made safe by glucuronidation and sulfation. A small fraction is oxidized by CYP2E1 and CYP3A4 into NAPQI, a highly reactive molecule. Glutathione binds NAPQI immediately, forming a harmless conjugate. In overdose, the safe pathways saturate, more NAPQI forms, and glutathione stores are used up. Free NAPQI then binds covalently to liver cell proteins, especially in mitochondria, causing oxidative stress and death of cells around the central vein (zone 3). Acetylcysteine is deacetylated to cysteine, the rate-limiting building block for glutathione, so the liver makes more glutathione. It may also bind NAPQI directly and support sulfation. Later, it acts as an antioxidant and improves microcirculation in an injured liver. Inhaled, its free thiol breaks disulfide bonds in mucus glycoproteins, thinning mucus.", "body": [["Liver", "Restores glutathione so NAPQI is neutralized before it damages liver cells; most protective when started within about eight hours of an overdose."], ["Lungs", "Inhaled, it breaks disulfide bonds in mucus, thinning thick secretions, but it can trigger bronchospasm in people with asthma."], ["Kidneys", "Acetaminophen can also injure the kidneys; acetylcysteine supports glutathione there too."], ["Gut", "The oral form tastes and smells strongly of sulfur, often causing nausea and vomiting."], ["Skin and vessels", "IV doses can release histamine, causing flushing, rash and, less often, wheezing or low blood pressure."]], "journey": "Acetylcysteine can be given IV, by mouth or inhaled. Oral absorption is good but bioavailability is low because the liver extracts much of it on the first pass, which is useful because the liver is where it is needed. It is deacetylated in the liver to cysteine, which enters glutathione synthesis. It distributes into extracellular fluid and is partly protein bound. The half-life of IV acetylcysteine is about five to six hours, longer in liver failure. It leaves partly through the kidneys.", "sidefx": "The oral form causes nausea and vomiting because of its strong rotten-egg sulfur smell and taste. IV acetylcysteine can cause an anaphylactoid reaction, a non-IgE release of histamine that produces flushing, rash, itching, wheezing and sometimes low blood pressure, most often during the loading phase. It is usually managed by pausing the infusion, giving antihistamines and restarting more slowly. Inhaled acetylcysteine can irritate the airways and cause bronchospasm, so it is often given with a bronchodilator. In small children, the IV volume can cause fluid overload and low sodium if not adjusted.", "fun": "Acetylcysteine smells like rotten eggs because of its sulfur-containing thiol group, the same chemical feature that lets it rebuild glutathione.", "src": ["Acetadote (acetylcysteine injection) FDA label", "Prescott LF et al. Intravenous N-acetylcysteine: the treatment of choice for paracetamol poisoning. BMJ 1979", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]}, "nafcillin": {"origin": "Nafcillin was born from the problem of penicillin resistance. By the 1950s many Staphylococcus aureus strains made penicillinase, an enzyme that destroys penicillin. In 1957 scientists at Beecham Research Laboratories in England isolated 6-aminopenicillanic acid, the core of the penicillin molecule. That allowed chemists to attach new side chains and design semisynthetic penicillins. Methicillin, the first penicillinase-resistant penicillin, came in 1959 to 1960. Nafcillin was developed shortly afterward at Wyeth and entered use in the early 1960s. Its bulky side chain blocks penicillinase from reaching the beta-lactam ring. Nafcillin and oxacillin are still first-line drugs for serious methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus (MSSA) infections, often working better than vancomycin for MSSA.", "timeline": [["1957", "Beecham scientists isolate 6-aminopenicillanic acid, the penicillin core."], ["1959", "Methicillin, the first penicillinase-resistant penicillin, is introduced."], ["1960s", "Nafcillin enters clinical use for penicillinase-producing staphylococci."]], "cell": "Bacterial cell walls are made of peptidoglycan, long sugar chains cross-linked by short peptides. Penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), which are transpeptidases, form these cross-links. Nafcillin's beta-lactam ring mimics the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of the peptide, so it binds the PBP active site and forms a stable covalent bond, permanently inactivating the enzyme. Without cross-links, the wall weakens, autolysins break it down further, and the bacterium bursts from osmotic pressure. Killing is time dependent. Nafcillin's large ethoxynaphthyl side chain physically blocks staphylococcal penicillinase from reaching and breaking the beta-lactam ring. MRSA resists nafcillin because it carries the mecA gene, which encodes PBP2a, a PBP with low affinity for beta-lactams.", "body": [["Infection sites", "Kills MSSA in bloodstream infections, endocarditis, bone and joint infections and skin infections."], ["Veins", "Very irritating to vein walls, causing phlebitis, and if it leaks out of the vein it can cause severe tissue injury."], ["Kidneys", "Can cause allergic interstitial nephritis, with fever, rash and rising creatinine."], ["Blood", "Prolonged courses can cause neutropenia."], ["Liver", "Mostly cleared through the bile and can raise liver enzymes."], ["Electrolytes", "Acts as a nonabsorbable anion in the kidney tubule, pulling potassium out and causing low potassium."]], "journey": "Nafcillin is given IV because it is poorly and unreliably absorbed by mouth. It is highly protein bound and spreads into most tissues, including bone, and reaches the cerebrospinal fluid when the meninges are inflamed. Unlike most penicillins, it is cleared mainly by the liver into the bile, with only a small share in the urine, so it usually needs no adjustment in kidney failure. The half-life is short, under an hour, so it must be given frequently or by continuous infusion. It induces CYP3A4, which lowers levels of drugs such as warfarin.", "sidefx": "Allergic reactions, from rash to anaphylaxis, occur because the beta-lactam ring binds host proteins and forms antigens. Interstitial nephritis is an immune reaction in the kidney. Phlebitis and tissue necrosis on extravasation reflect the drug's irritating nature; hyaluronidase is used for extravasation. Neutropenia appears with longer courses. Low potassium comes from high delivery of the drug's sodium salt and nonabsorbable anion to the distal tubule. CYP3A4 induction can make warfarin less effective. There is no antidote; reactions are managed by stopping the drug.", "src": ["Nafcillin injection FDA label", "Rolinson GN, Geddes AM. The 50th anniversary of the discovery of 6-aminopenicillanic acid. Int J Antimicrob Agents 2007", "Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases"]}, "naltrexone": {"origin": "Naltrexone is a synthetic relative of naloxone. Scientists at Endo Laboratories developed it in the 1960s by changing the side chain on the nitrogen to a cyclopropylmethyl group, which made the molecule last much longer and work by mouth. The goal was a blocker that could protect a person from relapse to opioids: if opioids cannot activate the receptor, they produce no high. The FDA approved oral naltrexone in 1984 for opioid dependence. In the early 1990s studies showed that it also reduced heavy drinking, and the FDA approved it for alcohol dependence in 1994. A monthly extended-release injection, Vivitrol, was approved for alcohol dependence in 2006 and for opioid dependence in 2010.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Naltrexone is synthesized at Endo Laboratories."], ["1984", "FDA approves oral naltrexone for opioid dependence."], ["1994", "FDA approves naltrexone for alcohol dependence."], ["2006", "FDA approves extended-release injectable naltrexone (Vivitrol) for alcohol dependence."], ["2010", "Vivitrol is approved for preventing relapse to opioid dependence."]], "cell": "Naltrexone is a competitive antagonist at mu-opioid receptors, with lower affinity for kappa and delta receptors. It occupies the receptor without activating it, so opioids taken afterward cannot bind and trigger Gi signaling. The usual opioid effects, including euphoria and respiratory depression, are blocked as long as naltrexone occupancy is high. Alcohol works partly by releasing endorphins, which act on mu receptors in the ventral tegmental area and nucleus accumbens and increase dopamine release in the reward pathway. Blocking those mu receptors blunts the pleasurable reinforcement from drinking, so craving and heavy drinking decrease. Its active metabolite, 6-beta-naltrexol, is also an antagonist and extends the effect.", "body": [["Reward circuits", "Blunts the reward of opioids and alcohol by blocking mu receptors that drive dopamine release, reducing craving and relapse."], ["Breathing and brainstem", "Blocks opioid respiratory depression, but it also blocks opioid pain relief, which matters if surgery or trauma occurs."], ["Liver", "Can raise liver enzymes, so liver function is checked; most risk is with liver disease or other liver stresses."], ["Gut", "Nausea is common, especially early in treatment."], ["Injection site", "The monthly injection can cause painful reactions, lumps and, rarely, tissue death at the site."]], "journey": "Oral naltrexone is well absorbed, but extensive first-pass metabolism leaves only a small portion intact. A liver enzyme (dihydrodiol dehydrogenase, not a CYP enzyme) converts it to 6-beta-naltrexol, an active antagonist that lasts longer than the parent drug. Both are excreted mainly by the kidneys. The parent half-life is about four hours and the metabolite about 13 hours, so oral blockade can last a day or more. The extended-release injection uses biodegradable microspheres in the buttock muscle that release drug steadily for about a month.", "sidefx": "Because it blocks opioid receptors, giving it to someone who still has opioids in the body precipitates sudden withdrawal, so patients must be opioid free for 7 to 10 days first. Nausea, headache and dizziness are common. Liver enzyme elevation can occur. The most serious risk is overdose after stopping: tolerance is lost during treatment, so a previously tolerated amount of opioid can be fatal. Trying to overcome the blockade with large opioid amounts is also dangerous. Opioid pain control is difficult while it is active. The injection can cause injection-site reactions. There is no antidote; blockade fades as the drug is cleared.", "src": ["Revia (naltrexone tablets) FDA label", "Vivitrol (naltrexone extended-release injectable suspension) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "neostigmine": {"origin": "Neostigmine is a synthetic version of physostigmine, the poison from the Calabar bean. In 1931 chemists John Aeschlimann and Marc Reinert at Hoffmann-La Roche made it by adding a quaternary ammonium group, which gives it a permanent positive charge so it stays out of the brain. It was sold as Prostigmin. In 1934 Mary Walker, a physician at St Alfege's Hospital in London, noticed that the weakness of myasthenia gravis resembled curare poisoning, which physostigmine could reverse. She gave physostigmine and saw strength return within minutes, and in 1935 she reported similar results with neostigmine. This was called the miracle at St Alfege's. Neostigmine has been used for decades to reverse muscle paralysis after surgery, and the FDA formally approved a branded injection, Bloxiverz, in 2013.", "timeline": [["1931", "Aeschlimann and Reinert at Hoffmann-La Roche synthesize neostigmine."], ["1934", "Mary Walker reverses myasthenic weakness with physostigmine."], ["1935", "Walker reports benefit of neostigmine in myasthenia gravis."], ["2013", "FDA approves neostigmine injection (Bloxiverz) under a formal NDA."]], "cell": "Acetylcholinesterase sits in the synaptic cleft and rapidly breaks acetylcholine into choline and acetate, ending each signal. Neostigmine is a reversible carbamate inhibitor: it binds the enzyme's active site and transfers its carbamoyl group to a serine residue, which then takes minutes, rather than microseconds, to come off. While the enzyme is tied up, acetylcholine builds up in the synapse. At the neuromuscular junction, more acetylcholine competes with and outnumbers a nondepolarizing blocker such as rocuronium at nicotinic receptors, so muscle contraction returns. Neostigmine also has a small direct agonist effect at the nicotinic receptor. Because its quaternary nitrogen keeps it out of the brain, its effects are peripheral, but acetylcholine also builds up at muscarinic receptors in the heart, gut, glands and airways.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Restores strength by increasing acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction, reversing nondepolarizing paralytics and improving myasthenia gravis."], ["Heart", "Extra acetylcholine at muscarinic M2 receptors slows the heart, which can progress to severe bradycardia or asystole; an antimuscarinic is given with it."], ["Airways", "Muscarinic stimulation constricts bronchi and increases secretions, which can worsen asthma."], ["Gut", "Increases gut movement, which helps ileus but can cause cramps, nausea and diarrhea."], ["Bladder", "Contracts the detrusor muscle, helping urinary retention."], ["Glands", "Extra acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors increases saliva, tears and sweat."]], "journey": "Neostigmine is usually given IV for paralytic reversal; it can also be given IM or under the skin. Because it is charged, it is poorly absorbed by mouth and does not cross the blood-brain barrier or the placenta to a large degree. Its effect peaks around 7 to 10 minutes after IV dosing, which is slower than people often expect. It is broken down by cholinesterases in the blood and metabolized in the liver, and much of it leaves through the kidneys. The half-life is about one hour and is longer in kidney failure.", "sidefx": "Nearly all side effects come from extra acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors: slow heart rate, heart block, bronchospasm, drooling, sweating, nausea, cramps and diarrhea. That is why glycopyrrolate or atropine is given at the same time to block muscarinic effects while leaving the nicotinic effect in muscles. Too much acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction can cause a cholinergic crisis with muscle weakness from constant depolarization, which can look like a myasthenic crisis. Atropine treats muscarinic toxicity. Neostigmine cannot reverse deep paralysis, because there is a ceiling to how much acetylcholine it can add.", "fun": "Mary Walker's 1934 discovery came from noticing that myasthenia gravis looked like curare poisoning, a classic example of reasoning from one disease to another.", "src": ["Bloxiverz (neostigmine methylsulfate) FDA label", "Walker MB. Treatment of myasthenia gravis with physostigmine. Lancet 1934", "Miller's Anesthesia"]}, "niacin": {"origin": "Niacin, or nicotinic acid, is vitamin B3. Its story starts with pellagra, a disease of skin rash, diarrhea, dementia and death that was common in the American South in the early 1900s among people who lived mostly on corn. Joseph Goldberger of the US Public Health Service showed that pellagra was caused by poor diet, not infection. In 1937 Conrad Elvehjem at the University of Wisconsin identified nicotinic acid as the factor that cured black tongue, the dog version of pellagra. In 1955 Rudolf Altschul and colleagues in Canada discovered that large amounts of niacin lowered cholesterol, making it the first lipid-lowering drug. The Coronary Drug Project, reported in 1975, showed fewer heart attacks with niacin. Later trials adding niacin to statins, AIM-HIGH in 2011 and HPS2-THRIVE in 2014, showed no benefit, and its use has declined.", "timeline": [["1910s", "Joseph Goldberger shows that pellagra is a dietary deficiency."], ["1937", "Conrad Elvehjem identifies nicotinic acid as the anti-pellagra factor."], ["1955", "Altschul and colleagues report that niacin lowers cholesterol."], ["1975", "The Coronary Drug Project reports fewer heart attacks with niacin."], ["2011", "AIM-HIGH finds no added cardiovascular benefit of niacin with statins."]], "cell": "In fat cells, niacin activates GPR109A (also called HCA2), a Gi coupled receptor. This lowers cAMP and protein kinase A activity, reducing hormone-sensitive lipase activity, so fewer free fatty acids are released into the blood. With fewer fatty acids reaching the liver, and with direct inhibition of hepatic diacylglycerol acyltransferase-2 (DGAT2), the liver makes less triglyceride and secretes fewer VLDL particles. Less VLDL means less LDL forms from it, and lipoprotein(a) also falls. Niacin raises HDL mainly by slowing the liver's uptake and breakdown of apoA-I, the main HDL protein. The flushing comes from GPR109A on skin Langerhans cells and keratinocytes, which release prostaglandin D2 and E2 that dilate skin blood vessels.", "body": [["Blood lipids", "Lowers triglycerides and LDL and raises HDL, a combination no other single lipid drug matches."], ["Skin", "Prostaglandins released in the skin dilate vessels, causing flushing, warmth and itching, especially on the face and upper body."], ["Liver", "High doses, especially of some sustained-release forms, can injure liver cells and raise liver enzymes."], ["Glucose metabolism", "Increases insulin resistance, which can raise blood sugar in people with or at risk for diabetes."], ["Uric acid", "Competes with uric acid for kidney excretion, raising levels and potentially triggering gout."], ["Stomach", "Can irritate the stomach and reactivate peptic ulcers."]], "journey": "Niacin is taken by mouth and absorbed quickly. Immediate-release forms peak within an hour; extended-release forms release it over several hours. The liver handles it by two routes: a conjugation route that makes nicotinuric acid, which is linked to flushing, and an amidation route that makes nicotinamide and related compounds, which is linked to liver toxicity. Sustained-release products push more drug through the amidation route, which is why they are more hepatotoxic. The half-life is short, under an hour, and metabolites leave through the kidneys.", "sidefx": "Flushing is the most common reason patients stop it. It is caused by prostaglandin release in the skin through GPR109A, which is why taking aspirin before the dose reduces it, and why tolerance develops with steady use. Liver injury is related to the amidation pathway and is most common with sustained-release products or when switching formulations. High blood sugar comes from increased insulin resistance. High uric acid and gout come from competition for kidney excretion. Combining it with statins slightly raises the risk of muscle injury. There is no antidote; toxicity is managed by stopping it.", "fun": "Niacin was once called nicotinic acid, but the name was changed to avoid confusion with nicotine; the word niacin comes from NIcotinic ACid vitamIN.", "src": ["Niaspan (niacin extended-release) FDA label", "Altschul R, Hoffer A, Stephen JD. Influence of nicotinic acid on serum cholesterol in man. Arch Biochem Biophys 1955", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "nicardipine": {"origin": "Nicardipine is a synthetic dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, a cousin of nifedipine. It was developed in Japan by Yamanouchi Pharmaceutical in the 1970s, as companies sought calcium channel blockers that were more selective for blood vessels. The concept of calcium antagonism had been described in the 1960s by the German physiologist Albrecht Fleckenstein. Nicardipine was approved in the US as oral Cardene in the late 1980s, and the IV form was approved soon after. Its value is its use as a smooth, titratable IV infusion that lowers blood pressure without much effect on the heart's pumping or conduction. That made it a standard choice for hypertensive emergencies, blood pressure control in stroke and brain bleeding, and other critical care settings.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Albrecht Fleckenstein describes calcium antagonism as a drug mechanism."], ["1970s", "Yamanouchi Pharmaceutical develops nicardipine in Japan."], ["1980s", "Nicardipine is approved in the US as Cardene."]], "cell": "Smooth muscle in artery walls contracts when calcium enters through voltage-gated L-type (Cav1.2) calcium channels. Calcium binds calmodulin, which activates myosin light chain kinase, allowing myosin to pull on actin. Nicardipine binds the dihydropyridine site on the alpha-1 subunit of the L-type channel and stabilizes it in the inactivated state, so less calcium enters during depolarization. Less calcium means less myosin light chain phosphorylation and relaxation of arteriolar smooth muscle. Peripheral vascular resistance falls, and blood pressure drops. Dihydropyridines strongly favor vascular smooth muscle over heart muscle, partly because vascular cells rest at a more depolarized membrane potential, so nicardipine has little effect on contractility or on the SA and AV nodes. It also dilates coronary and cerebral arteries.", "body": [["Arterioles", "Relaxes arterial smooth muscle, lowering systemic vascular resistance and blood pressure in a controllable way."], ["Heart", "Little direct effect on contraction or conduction, but the baroreceptor reflex often speeds the heart rate."], ["Coronary arteries", "Dilates coronary arteries, improving blood supply to heart muscle."], ["Brain", "Dilates cerebral arteries; vasodilation can cause headache and may raise intracranial pressure in some patients."], ["Veins at the IV site", "The infusion can irritate peripheral veins, causing phlebitis, so sites are changed or a large vein is used."]], "journey": "IV nicardipine starts working within minutes and the dose can be adjusted in steps. When a short infusion stops, its effect fades within about an hour as it redistributes, though after long infusions it lasts longer. It is more than 95 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it extensively with CYP3A4, so CYP3A4 inhibitors raise its levels. Oral nicardipine has low bioavailability because of first-pass metabolism. Metabolites leave in urine and feces.", "sidefx": "Headache, flushing and dizziness come from dilation of blood vessels in the head and skin. Reflex tachycardia happens because the baroreceptors sense falling pressure and boost sympathetic output. Too much effect causes low blood pressure, which can reduce blood flow to the brain or heart. Phlebitis at the IV site comes from direct vein irritation. Ankle swelling with longer use comes from arteriolar dilation that raises capillary pressure. In overdose, calcium, high-dose insulin and vasopressors are used; there is no true antidote.", "src": ["Cardene IV (nicardipine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "nifedipine": {"origin": "Nifedipine was the first dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker. Chemists Friedrich Bossert and Wulf Vater at Bayer in Germany made it in the 1960s while working on coronary dilators. At about the same time, Albrecht Fleckenstein, a physiologist in Freiburg, showed that certain drugs relaxed muscle by blocking calcium entry and named them calcium antagonists. Bayer launched nifedipine as Adalat in 1975, and the FDA approved it in the US as Procardia in 1981, first for angina. Later, short-acting capsules used to drop blood pressure quickly were found to cause strokes and heart attacks from overly rapid falls, so that use was abandoned. Extended-release forms became the standard for long-term hypertension. It is also used in pregnancy for severe high blood pressure and to slow preterm labor.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Bossert and Vater at Bayer synthesize nifedipine; Fleckenstein describes calcium antagonism."], ["1975", "Bayer launches nifedipine as Adalat."], ["1981", "FDA approves nifedipine (Procardia) for angina."], ["1990s", "Rapid blood pressure lowering with short-acting capsules is abandoned because of strokes and heart attacks."]], "cell": "Nifedipine binds the dihydropyridine receptor site on the alpha-1 subunit of L-type (Cav1.2) voltage-gated calcium channels and holds them in the inactivated state. In vascular smooth muscle, less calcium enters during depolarization, so less calcium binds calmodulin and myosin light chain kinase is less active. The muscle relaxes and arterioles widen, lowering peripheral resistance and blood pressure. It also relieves coronary artery spasm, which helps variant angina. In uterine smooth muscle, the same drop in calcium reduces contractions, which is the basis for its use in preterm labor. Dihydropyridines are much more selective for vascular smooth muscle than for cardiac muscle, so they have little direct effect on heart rate or AV conduction at usual exposure.", "body": [["Arteries", "Dilates arterioles throughout the body, lowering blood pressure and the work the heart must pump against."], ["Coronary arteries", "Relaxes coronary arteries and prevents spasm, relieving angina, especially variant angina."], ["Heart", "Little direct effect on contraction or conduction, but the reflex response to falling pressure speeds the heart, especially with short-acting forms."], ["Uterus", "Relaxes uterine muscle, slowing preterm contractions."], ["Legs and ankles", "Arteriolar dilation raises pressure in capillaries, pushing fluid into tissues and causing ankle swelling."], ["Gums", "Can cause gingival overgrowth with long-term use."]], "journey": "Nifedipine is taken by mouth. Immediate-release capsules are absorbed quickly and act within about 20 minutes; extended-release tablets release it slowly over a day and must be swallowed whole. A sizeable share is removed by first-pass metabolism. It is highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down with CYP3A4, so grapefruit juice and CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels and inducers lower them. Inactive metabolites leave mainly in the urine. The half-life is about two to five hours, which is why extended-release forms are used for steady control.", "sidefx": "Headache, flushing and dizziness come from vasodilation. Reflex tachycardia and palpitations come from the baroreceptor response, and are worst when pressure falls quickly. That rapid fall is why immediate-release nifedipine can trigger stroke or heart attack and is not used for quick blood pressure lowering in most adults. Ankle edema results from arteriolar dilation without matching venous dilation, which raises capillary pressure; diuretics help little. Gingival hyperplasia occurs with long use. In overdose, especially of extended-release tablets, shock can be delayed and prolonged; calcium, high-dose insulin and vasopressors are used, but there is no true antidote.", "src": ["Procardia XL (nifedipine) FDA label", "Fleckenstein A. History of calcium antagonists. Circ Res 1983", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "nitrofurantoin": {"origin": "Nitrofurantoin belongs to the nitrofurans, a group of synthetic antibacterials discovered in the 1940s. Researchers at Eaton Laboratories in Norwich, New York, including Matthew Dodd and William Stillman, reported in 1944 that adding a nitro group to the furan ring gave compounds strong antibacterial activity. Nitrofurantoin was introduced in the US in 1953 under the name Furadantin, specifically for urinary tract infections. Later, a macrocrystal form (Macrodantin) was developed to slow absorption and reduce nausea, and a twice-daily form combining macrocrystals and monohydrate (Macrobid) followed. Because bacteria rarely develop resistance to it, and because it reaches high levels only in urine, it remains a first-line treatment for uncomplicated bladder infections more than 70 years after its introduction.", "timeline": [["1944", "Dodd and Stillman report the antibacterial activity of nitrofurans."], ["1953", "Nitrofurantoin (Furadantin) is introduced for urinary tract infections."]], "cell": "Nitrofurantoin is a prodrug activated inside bacteria. Bacterial flavoproteins called nitroreductases reduce its nitro group, producing highly reactive intermediates. These intermediates attack many targets at once: they damage bacterial DNA, bind to ribosomal proteins and stop protein synthesis, and disrupt enzymes in the citric acid cycle and other metabolic pathways. Because it hits so many targets, a single mutation rarely makes bacteria resistant. Bacteria reduce the drug much faster than human cells do, which gives it selectivity. It is bactericidal at the high concentrations found in urine. It is active against E. coli, Staphylococcus saprophyticus, Enterococcus and many Klebsiella, but not Proteus, which is intrinsically resistant, or Pseudomonas.", "body": [["Bladder", "Reaches high concentrations in urine and kills bacteria causing cystitis, without meaningful blood or kidney tissue levels."], ["Lungs", "Can cause an acute allergic pneumonitis with fever, cough and shortness of breath, or, with long-term use, chronic lung fibrosis."], ["Liver", "Rarely causes hepatitis or cholestatic injury, more often with long courses."], ["Nerves", "Can cause peripheral neuropathy, more likely when kidney function is poor and the drug builds up."], ["Red blood cells", "In people with G6PD deficiency, the oxidative intermediates can cause hemolytic anemia."], ["Urine", "Turns urine dark yellow or brown, which is harmless."]], "journey": "Nitrofurantoin is taken by mouth, and food improves its absorption. The macrocrystal form dissolves slowly, which reduces nausea. Once absorbed, it is cleared from the blood so quickly that blood levels stay very low, with a half-life under an hour. About a quarter to a third is filtered and secreted unchanged into the urine, where it concentrates to antibacterial levels; much of the rest is broken down in tissues. Because it depends on kidney excretion to reach the bladder, it works poorly and is more toxic when kidney function is markedly reduced. It does not reach useful levels in kidney tissue, so it is not used for pyelonephritis.", "sidefx": "Nausea and GI upset are the most common effects and are why the macrocrystal form was developed. The lung reactions are serious: the acute form is a hypersensitivity reaction, and the chronic form is thought to involve oxidative injury that leads to fibrosis. Hepatitis and neuropathy are linked to longer courses and to drug buildup in poor kidney function. Hemolysis in G6PD deficiency happens because red cells cannot handle the oxidative stress from its reactive intermediates; it is avoided at term pregnancy and in newborns for this reason. The brown urine is harmless. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Macrobid (nitrofurantoin monohydrate/macrocrystals) FDA label", "Dodd MC, Stillman WB. The in vitro bacteriostatic action of some simple furan derivatives. J Pharmacol Exp Ther 1944", "Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases"]}, "nitroprusside": {"origin": "Sodium nitroprusside was first made in 1849 by the British chemist Lyon Playfair. It was mostly a laboratory reagent for decades. Its ability to lower blood pressure was noted in the 1920s, and in 1955 Irvine Page and colleagues at the Cleveland Clinic reported using it as an IV drug for hypertensive emergencies. Wider use came in the 1970s, once stable commercial preparations made it practical. It became the reference drug for rapid, precise blood pressure control because it acts within seconds and wears off within minutes. Its main limitation, the release of cyanide as it breaks down, led to careful limits on how long and how much it is used and to newer alternatives such as nicardipine and clevidipine.", "timeline": [["1849", "Lyon Playfair first prepares sodium nitroprusside."], ["1955", "Irvine Page and colleagues report its clinical use in hypertensive emergencies."], ["1970s", "Commercial preparations bring widespread use in critical care."]], "cell": "Nitroprusside is a complex of an iron atom bound to five cyanide groups and one nitric oxide group. In the blood it reacts with oxyhemoglobin and sulfhydryl groups and releases nitric oxide without needing enzymatic activation, unlike nitroglycerin. NO diffuses into vascular smooth muscle and activates soluble guanylate cyclase, raising cyclic GMP. Cyclic GMP activates protein kinase G, which lowers intracellular calcium and activates myosin light chain phosphatase, relaxing the muscle. Because no enzyme step is needed, it relaxes both arteries and veins about equally and does not cause the tolerance seen with nitrates. Each molecule also releases five cyanide ions. Cyanide is detoxified by rhodanese in the liver, which uses thiosulfate to convert it to thiocyanate.", "body": [["Arteries", "Arterial dilation lowers systemic vascular resistance and afterload, dropping blood pressure within seconds."], ["Veins", "Venous dilation lowers preload, reducing congestion in heart failure."], ["Heart", "Lower afterload helps a failing heart eject more blood; reflex tachycardia can occur."], ["Brain", "Cerebral vasodilation can raise intracranial pressure, a concern in head injury or stroke."], ["Lungs", "Pulmonary vasodilation can worsen ventilation-perfusion matching and lower oxygen levels."], ["Cell metabolism", "Cyanide released during breakdown can block cellular respiration, causing metabolic acidosis."]], "journey": "Nitroprusside is given only as a continuous IV infusion. It works in under two minutes and its effect ends within minutes of stopping, because the parent drug has a half-life of about two minutes. It breaks down in red blood cells and tissues, releasing nitric oxide and cyanide. The liver enzyme rhodanese converts cyanide to thiocyanate using thiosulfate as a sulfur donor, and thiocyanate is excreted by the kidneys over days, with a half-life of about three days, longer in kidney failure. The solution breaks down in light, so the bag is covered.", "sidefx": "Low blood pressure is an extension of its action and is why arterial line monitoring is preferred. Cyanide toxicity occurs when cyanide release exceeds the body's thiosulfate supply, as with high rates, long infusions or poor nutrition. Cyanide blocks cytochrome oxidase, so cells cannot use oxygen, producing lactic acidosis, confusion and, eventually, cardiovascular collapse. Treatment is stopping the infusion and giving hydroxocobalamin, which binds cyanide to form cyanocobalamin, or sodium thiosulfate, which feeds rhodanese. Thiocyanate builds up in kidney failure and causes confusion, tremor and seizures. Methemoglobinemia can occur. Increased intracranial pressure follows cerebral vasodilation.", "fun": "Each nitroprusside molecule contains five cyanide groups around a single iron atom, which is why cyanide toxicity is a board-favorite topic for this drug.", "src": ["Nitropress (sodium nitroprusside) FDA label", "Page IH, Corcoran AC, Dustan HP, Koppanyi T. Cardiovascular actions of sodium nitroprusside in animals and hypertensive patients. Circulation 1955", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]}, "nitrous-oxide": {"origin": "Nitrous oxide was first made in 1772 by the English chemist Joseph Priestley. In 1799 the young chemist Humphry Davy, working at the Pneumatic Institution in Bristol, inhaled it and described its euphoric effects, calling it laughing gas. In 1800 he even wrote that it might be used to relieve pain during surgery, but the idea was ignored for decades. Laughing gas parties and traveling shows made it popular entertainment. At one such show in 1844, the Connecticut dentist Horace Wells noticed a man injure his leg without feeling pain. The next day Wells had his own tooth pulled while breathing the gas. His public demonstration in Boston in 1845 went poorly, but nitrous oxide later became a mainstay of dental and surgical anesthesia and of prehospital pain relief.", "timeline": [["1772", "Joseph Priestley first prepares nitrous oxide."], ["1799", "Humphry Davy describes its euphoric effects and names it laughing gas."], ["1844", "Horace Wells has a tooth extracted under nitrous oxide."], ["1868", "Edmund Andrews describes mixing nitrous oxide with oxygen."]], "cell": "Nitrous oxide is a noncompetitive antagonist at NMDA glutamate receptors. Glutamate signaling through NMDA receptors amplifies pain transmission in the spinal cord and brain, so blocking these channels reduces pain and produces dissociation and euphoria. Nitrous oxide also releases endogenous opioid peptides in the periaqueductal gray, which activate descending noradrenergic pathways that inhibit pain transmission at the spinal cord; this part of its pain relief can be partly reduced by naloxone in animal studies. It has a minimum alveolar concentration (MAC) above 100 percent, meaning it cannot produce full surgical anesthesia on its own at normal pressure. Separately, it irreversibly oxidizes the cobalt atom in vitamin B12, inactivating methionine synthase, which matters with prolonged exposure.", "body": [["Brain", "Produces pain relief, relaxation, euphoria and mild sedation within minutes, with patients usually remaining responsive."], ["Breathing", "Only mildly depresses breathing on its own, but adds to the effects of opioids and other sedatives."], ["Heart and vessels", "Has mild direct myocardial depression offset by mild sympathetic stimulation, so blood pressure and heart rate usually change little."], ["Air-filled spaces", "It is far more soluble in blood than nitrogen, so it enters closed air spaces faster than nitrogen leaves, expanding pneumothorax, bowel obstruction and middle ear spaces."], ["Nerves and blood", "Prolonged or repeated exposure inactivates vitamin B12, which can cause anemia and spinal cord damage."]], "journey": "Nitrous oxide is inhaled and leaves the same way. It has very low solubility in blood, so the blood and brain quickly reach equilibrium with the gas in the lungs. That is why it works within a few minutes and wears off within a few minutes after stopping. It is essentially not metabolized; almost all of it is exhaled unchanged. A tiny amount leaves through the skin. When it is stopped, it rushes from the blood into the lungs and can dilute alveolar oxygen, so oxygen is given for several minutes afterward. It crosses the placenta.", "sidefx": "Nausea and dizziness are common. Diffusion hypoxia happens when the gas is stopped: large amounts leave the blood into the alveoli and dilute oxygen, which is prevented by giving pure oxygen after use. It expands any trapped gas space, so it is avoided with pneumothorax, bowel obstruction, recent eye surgery with a gas bubble, decompression sickness and some ear conditions. By oxidizing vitamin B12, prolonged or repeated exposure inhibits methionine synthase, causing megaloblastic anemia and subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord, seen especially in recreational misuse. Treatment is stopping exposure and replacing vitamin B12. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Horace Wells's public demonstration in 1845 failed when the patient cried out, and the audience mocked him, but his idea was right.", "src": ["Miller's Anesthesia", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Wood Library-Museum of Anesthesiology historical resources"]}, "norepinephrine": {"origin": "Norepinephrine is the body's own main sympathetic nerve transmitter. It was first synthesized in the early 1900s, around the same time adrenaline was being studied. In 1946 the Swedish physiologist Ulf von Euler showed that the transmitter released by sympathetic nerves was norepinephrine, not epinephrine. He shared the 1970 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine with Julius Axelrod and Bernard Katz for work on how nerve transmitters are stored, released and inactivated. Synthetic levo-norepinephrine was introduced as a drug under the name Levophed around 1950. It became a standard vasopressor and is now the first-choice drug for septic shock in international guidelines because it raises blood pressure reliably with less tachycardia than epinephrine or dopamine.", "timeline": [["1904", "Norepinephrine is first synthesized, shortly after adrenaline."], ["1946", "Ulf von Euler identifies norepinephrine as the sympathetic nerve transmitter."], ["c. 1950", "Norepinephrine is introduced as an IV vasopressor (Levophed)."], ["1970", "Von Euler shares the Nobel Prize with Axelrod and Katz."]], "cell": "Norepinephrine is a catecholamine agonist at alpha-1, alpha-2 and beta-1 adrenergic receptors, with little action at beta-2. At alpha-1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle, which are Gq coupled, it activates phospholipase C, producing IP3 and DAG; IP3 releases calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, and calcium-calmodulin activates myosin light chain kinase, causing strong vasoconstriction. At beta-1 receptors in the heart, which are Gs coupled, it activates adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP and protein kinase A, which phosphorylates L-type calcium channels and phospholamban, increasing contractility and heart rate. Because it lacks beta-2 effect, there is no offsetting vasodilation in muscle beds. The net result is a large rise in systemic vascular resistance and mean arterial pressure, with modest support of cardiac output.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Strong alpha-1 vasoconstriction raises systemic vascular resistance and mean arterial pressure, restoring perfusion pressure in shock."], ["Heart", "Beta-1 stimulation increases contraction force; heart rate may stay the same or slow because the baroreceptor reflex reacts to the higher pressure."], ["Veins", "Venous constriction moves blood from the veins back to the heart, improving preload."], ["Kidneys", "In hypotensive shock, restoring pressure often improves kidney perfusion, though excess constriction can reduce flow."], ["Gut and fingers", "Intense vasoconstriction can reduce blood flow to the bowel, fingers and toes, causing ischemia at high exposure."], ["Skin and tissue", "If it leaks out of the vein, local vasoconstriction can cause tissue death."]], "journey": "Norepinephrine is given only as a continuous IV or IO infusion, because it is destroyed in the gut and liver if swallowed and would cause local tissue injury if injected under the skin. It acts within one to two minutes and its effect ends within a few minutes of stopping, because its half-life is only about two to three minutes. It is taken back into nerve endings and other tissues and broken down by monoamine oxidase (MAO) and catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT). The inactive metabolites, such as vanillylmandelic acid, leave in the urine. It does not cross the blood-brain barrier well.", "sidefx": "Most toxicity comes from too much vasoconstriction. Excess alpha-1 effect can cause severe hypertension, headache and ischemia of the fingers, toes, bowel and kidneys. Beta-1 stimulation can trigger arrhythmias. Reflex bradycardia comes from the baroreceptor response to rising pressure. Extravasation causes intense local vasoconstriction and tissue necrosis; the treatment is local infiltration of phentolamine, an alpha blocker that reverses the constriction. MAO inhibitors and tricyclic antidepressants can exaggerate its effect. Toxicity is managed by lowering or stopping the infusion, which works quickly because of its short half-life.", "fun": "Norepinephrine is also called noradrenaline; the nor comes from the German phrase Nitrogen ohne Radikal, meaning the nitrogen lacks the methyl group that adrenaline has.", "src": ["Levophed (norepinephrine bitartrate) FDA label", "Von Euler US. A specific sympathomimetic ergone in adrenergic nerve fibres. Acta Physiol Scand 1946", "Surviving Sepsis Campaign guidelines"]}, "nortriptyline": {"origin": "Nortriptyline is a tricyclic antidepressant and the main active metabolite of amitriptyline. Tricyclics began with imipramine, which the Swiss psychiatrist Roland Kuhn found in 1957 relieved depression. Amitriptyline followed, and when scientists studied how the body breaks down amitriptyline, they found that removing one methyl group from its side chain produced nortriptyline, which is itself a strong antidepressant. Nortriptyline was marketed in the US in the 1960s, first as Aventyl by Eli Lilly and later also as Pamelor. As a secondary amine, it blocks norepinephrine reuptake more than serotonin and causes fewer anticholinergic and sedating effects than amitriptyline. It is one of the few tricyclics with a well-studied therapeutic blood level range, which makes it useful in older adults and for neuropathic pain.", "timeline": [["1957", "Roland Kuhn reports that imipramine relieves depression, launching the tricyclic class."], ["1960s", "Nortriptyline, the active metabolite of amitriptyline, enters US use."]], "cell": "Nortriptyline inhibits the norepinephrine transporter (NET) more strongly than the serotonin transporter (SERT). Blocking these reuptake pumps on presynaptic nerve endings leaves more norepinephrine and serotonin in the synapse. Over weeks, the extra transmitter leads to adaptive changes, including downregulation of beta-adrenergic and some serotonin receptors and changes in gene expression and neuroplasticity, which track with the antidepressant effect. In the spinal cord, more norepinephrine and serotonin strengthen descending pathways that suppress pain, explaining its benefit in neuropathic pain. Like other tricyclics, it also blocks muscarinic, H1 histamine and alpha-1 adrenergic receptors, though less than amitriptyline. At high levels it blocks fast sodium channels in heart muscle, which is the main cause of toxicity in overdose.", "body": [["Brain", "Gradually improves mood over several weeks by increasing norepinephrine and serotonin signaling."], ["Pain pathways", "Strengthens descending pain-blocking pathways, relieving nerve pain such as diabetic neuropathy and postherpetic neuralgia."], ["Heart", "Sodium channel blockade slows conduction and widens the QRS; in overdose this can cause dangerous arrhythmias."], ["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 blockade can cause orthostatic hypotension, though less than with amitriptyline."], ["Mouth, gut and bladder", "Anticholinergic effects cause dry mouth, constipation and urinary retention, milder than with tertiary-amine tricyclics."]], "journey": "Nortriptyline is taken by mouth and is well absorbed but undergoes first-pass metabolism. It is very fat soluble, highly protein bound and spreads widely into tissues, so the body holds much more drug than the blood shows. It crosses the placenta and enters breast milk. The liver hydroxylates it mainly with CYP2D6, and genetic differences in CYP2D6 cause large differences in blood levels between people. Metabolites leave through the kidneys. The half-life is long, around a day or more, which supports once-daily dosing, often at bedtime. Blood levels are monitored because it works best within a defined range.", "sidefx": "Dry mouth, constipation, blurred vision and urinary retention come from muscarinic blockade. Drowsiness comes from H1 blockade. Dizziness on standing comes from alpha-1 blockade. Weight gain is common. The most serious risk is overdose, where sodium channel blockade in the heart widens the QRS and causes ventricular arrhythmias and low blood pressure, and the drug can also cause seizures. Sodium bicarbonate is the treatment for QRS widening, because raising blood sodium and pH helps overcome the channel block. Like all antidepressants, it carries a boxed warning for suicidal thoughts in young people.", "src": ["Pamelor (nortriptyline) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology"]}, "nystatin": {"origin": "Nystatin was discovered by two women scientists working for the New York State Department of Health: Elizabeth Lee Hazen, a microbiologist in New York City, and Rachel Fuller Brown, a chemist in Albany. Working mostly by mail, they tested soil microbes for antifungal activity. In 1950 they found a Streptomyces species in soil from a farm in Virginia that produced a substance that killed fungi but was safe enough for use. The organism was later named Streptomyces noursei after the farm's owner. They named the drug nystatin after the New York State Health Department. It was the first antifungal antibiotic safe enough for human use, and it was marketed by E.R. Squibb in the mid 1950s. Hazen and Brown donated their royalties to a foundation that funded scientific research.", "timeline": [["1950", "Elizabeth Hazen and Rachel Brown discover nystatin from Streptomyces noursei."], ["1950s", "Nystatin is marketed for fungal infections, the first safe antifungal antibiotic."], ["1994", "Hazen and Brown are inducted into the National Inventors Hall of Fame."]], "cell": "Nystatin is a polyene antifungal. Its large ring has a water-loving side and a fat-loving side. It binds ergosterol, the main sterol in fungal cell membranes. Several nystatin molecules group together with ergosterol to form pores, or channels, through the membrane. Potassium and other small ions leak out, and the cell loses its electrochemical balance and dies. It binds ergosterol much more strongly than cholesterol, the sterol in human cell membranes, which gives it selectivity. It is active against Candida species. It is fungicidal or fungistatic depending on concentration. Resistance is rare because it would require the fungus to change its membrane sterols.", "body": [["Mouth and throat", "Kills Candida on contact in oral thrush when swished and held in the mouth before swallowing."], ["Skin", "As a cream or powder, treats Candida infections in skin folds and diaper areas."], ["Gut", "Passes through the gut without being absorbed, so it acts only on fungi it touches there; large amounts can cause nausea or diarrhea."], ["Rest of the body", "Not absorbed into the blood, so it does not treat systemic or deep fungal infections."]], "journey": "Nystatin is essentially not absorbed through the gut lining or intact skin. When taken by mouth, it stays in the GI tract and is passed in the stool unchanged. That is why it is used only for infections of the skin, mouth and gut lining. It is too toxic to be given IV, because if it reached the blood it would also bind human cell membranes. The oral suspension works only while it is in contact with the infected lining, so it is held in the mouth before swallowing.", "sidefx": "Because it is not absorbed, nystatin is very safe. The most common effects are nausea, vomiting and diarrhea with large oral doses, from local irritation in the gut. Topical forms can cause mild skin irritation or rarely allergic contact reactions. Its close cousin amphotericin B is given IV for serious fungal infections and causes kidney toxicity and infusion reactions, partly because polyenes also bind cholesterol in human cells; nystatin avoids these effects by staying out of the blood. There is no antidote, and none is needed.", "fun": "The name nystatin honors the New York State Department of Health, where it was discovered.", "src": ["Nystatin oral suspension FDA label", "Science History Institute: Elizabeth Lee Hazen and Rachel Fuller Brown", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "octreotide": {"origin": "Octreotide grew out of the discovery of somatostatin, a hormone that shuts off growth hormone release. In 1973 Paul Brazeau, Roger Guillemin and colleagues at the Salk Institute isolated somatostatin from sheep hypothalamus. Guillemin shared the 1977 Nobel Prize for his work on brain hormones. Natural somatostatin lasts only a few minutes in the blood, so it was impractical as a drug. In the late 1970s, a team at Sandoz in Switzerland led by Wilfried Bauer built a shorter, eight amino acid analog that kept the active part of the molecule but resisted breakdown. The FDA approved it as Sandostatin in 1988, first for hormone-secreting tumors. A monthly depot form followed, and it became widely used for variceal bleeding and for sulfonylurea overdose.", "timeline": [["1973", "Brazeau, Guillemin and colleagues isolate somatostatin."], ["1977", "Roger Guillemin shares the Nobel Prize for discoveries about brain peptide hormones."], ["1982", "Sandoz scientists led by Wilfried Bauer report octreotide (SMS 201-995)."], ["1988", "FDA approves octreotide (Sandostatin)."]], "cell": "Octreotide is an agonist at somatostatin receptors, mainly SSTR2 and SSTR5, with some activity at SSTR3. These are Gi coupled receptors. Activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase and lowers cAMP, opens potassium channels and closes calcium channels in hormone-secreting cells, which blocks the calcium-triggered release of secretory granules. This shuts down secretion of growth hormone from the pituitary, insulin and glucagon from the pancreas, and gastrin, VIP, serotonin and other gut hormones. In the splanchnic circulation, it causes vasoconstriction, partly by blocking release of vasodilating gut peptides such as glucagon, which lowers portal blood flow and pressure. Many neuroendocrine tumors express SSTR2, so octreotide reduces their hormone output and can slow their growth.", "body": [["Liver and portal circulation", "Constricts splanchnic vessels and lowers portal pressure, reducing bleeding from esophageal varices."], ["Pancreas", "Blocks insulin release, which is why it prevents recurring low blood sugar after sulfonylurea overdose; it also blocks glucagon."], ["Pituitary", "Lowers growth hormone and IGF-1, treating acromegaly."], ["Gut", "Reduces secretions and slows gut movement, controlling diarrhea from carcinoid and VIP-secreting tumors."], ["Gallbladder", "Reduces gallbladder contraction, so long-term use can cause sludge and gallstones."], ["Heart", "Can slow the heart rate and cause conduction changes."]], "journey": "Octreotide is given IV or under the skin for short-acting use, as a monthly IM depot injection, or as an oral capsule for acromegaly. Under the skin it is absorbed quickly and completely. Its half-life is about one and a half to two hours, far longer than natural somatostatin, and its effects last several hours after a subcutaneous dose. It is cleared partly by the liver and partly by the kidneys. Clearance is reduced in kidney failure and liver cirrhosis.", "sidefx": "Nausea, cramping, gas and loose, fatty stools come from reduced pancreatic enzyme secretion and slowed gut function. Gallstones and sludge come from reduced gallbladder emptying, especially with long-term use. Blood sugar can go low or high because it blocks both insulin and glucagon. Slow heart rate and conduction changes are possible. Long-term use can lower vitamin B12 and thyroid hormone levels. There is no antidote; side effects are managed by adjusting or stopping therapy.", "src": ["Sandostatin (octreotide) FDA label", "Bauer W et al. SMS 201-995: a very potent and selective octapeptide analogue of somatostatin. Life Sci 1982", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "olanzapine": {"origin": "Olanzapine was developed by Eli Lilly in the 1980s and early 1990s. Lilly scientists set out to make a drug with the benefits of clozapine, the first atypical antipsychotic, which works well but can cause agranulocytosis and requires regular blood tests. They modified the clozapine structure, building a thienobenzodiazepine. The FDA approved olanzapine as Zyprexa in 1996 for schizophrenia. Approvals for bipolar mania, an IM form for agitation and a dissolving tablet followed. It became one of the best-selling drugs in the world, but its strong link to weight gain and diabetes led to major lawsuits and to closer metabolic monitoring for all antipsychotics. Today it is also widely used to prevent chemotherapy nausea.", "timeline": [["1996", "FDA approves olanzapine (Zyprexa) for schizophrenia."], ["2000", "FDA approves olanzapine for acute bipolar mania."], ["2009", "FDA approves a long-acting injection (Zyprexa Relprevv) with a monitoring program for post-injection delirium."]], "cell": "Olanzapine blocks dopamine D2 receptors in the mesolimbic pathway, which reduces hallucinations and delusions, the positive symptoms of psychosis. It also strongly blocks serotonin 5-HT2A receptors. 5-HT2A blockade increases dopamine release in the nigrostriatal and mesocortical pathways, which lowers the risk of movement side effects and may improve negative symptoms. Its high ratio of 5-HT2A to D2 blockade is what makes it atypical. It also blocks histamine H1 receptors, causing sedation and increased appetite, 5-HT2C receptors, which also increases appetite, muscarinic receptors, causing anticholinergic effects, and alpha-1 receptors, causing orthostasis. D2 blockade in the chemoreceptor trigger zone explains its antiemetic effect.", "body": [["Brain", "Reduces psychotic symptoms and mania, and causes calming and sedation that make it useful in acute agitation."], ["Metabolism", "H1 and 5-HT2C blockade increase appetite, causing weight gain; it also increases insulin resistance, raising blood sugar and triglycerides."], ["Vomiting center", "Blocks dopamine and serotonin receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone, preventing chemotherapy nausea."], ["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 blockade can cause dizziness on standing."], ["Movement", "Lower risk of stiffness and tremor than older antipsychotics, but tardive dyskinesia and neuroleptic malignant syndrome can still occur."], ["Pituitary", "Can raise prolactin modestly, less than risperidone."]], "journey": "Olanzapine is taken by mouth as a tablet or a dissolving tablet, or given IM. It is well absorbed but about 40 percent is removed on the first pass through the liver. It is highly protein bound and crosses into the brain well. The liver breaks it down mainly with CYP1A2, with some help from glucuronidation and CYP2D6. Smoking induces CYP1A2, so smokers clear it faster and may need more, and quitting smoking can raise levels. The half-life is long, about one to two days, supporting once-daily dosing. Metabolites leave in urine and feces.", "sidefx": "Weight gain, high blood sugar and high lipids are its signature problems, linked to H1 and 5-HT2C blockade and insulin resistance. Sedation comes from H1 blockade, dry mouth and constipation from muscarinic blockade, and orthostatic hypotension from alpha-1 blockade. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome, from D2 blockade, causes fever, rigidity and autonomic instability. The IM form should not be given close to IM benzodiazepines because of reports of severe cardiorespiratory depression. The long-acting injection can cause post-injection delirium sedation syndrome, which is why patients are observed afterward. Like all antipsychotics, it carries a boxed warning for increased mortality in elderly patients with dementia. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Zyprexa (olanzapine) FDA label", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "olmesartan": {"origin": "Olmesartan is a synthetic angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB) developed by the Japanese company Sankyo. The ARB class traces back to the 1980s, when researchers at DuPont found small molecules that blocked the angiotensin II receptor, leading to losartan, the first ARB, approved in 1995. Sankyo designed olmesartan as a potent, long-acting member of the class, given as the prodrug olmesartan medoxomil to improve absorption. The FDA approved it as Benicar in 2002, and combinations with amlodipine and hydrochlorothiazide followed. In 2013 the FDA added a warning that olmesartan can cause a sprue-like enteropathy, a severe chronic diarrhea with weight loss that resolves when the drug is stopped, a reaction reported much more with olmesartan than with other ARBs.", "timeline": [["1995", "Losartan, the first ARB, is approved in the US."], ["2002", "FDA approves olmesartan medoxomil (Benicar)."], ["2013", "FDA warns that olmesartan can cause sprue-like enteropathy."]], "cell": "The renin-angiotensin system produces angiotensin II, which binds AT1 receptors, Gq coupled receptors on vascular smooth muscle, the adrenal cortex, the kidney and the brain. AT1 activation causes vasoconstriction, aldosterone release, sodium reabsorption in the proximal tubule, thirst, sympathetic activation, and growth and fibrosis of heart and vessel tissue. Olmesartan is a selective, insurmountable antagonist of the AT1 receptor; it binds tightly and comes off slowly. Blocking AT1 relaxes arterioles, lowers aldosterone so less sodium is retained and less potassium is excreted, and reduces remodeling. Unlike ACE inhibitors, ARBs do not block the breakdown of bradykinin, so they rarely cause cough. Angiotensin II levels rise and can act on unblocked AT2 receptors.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Blocking angiotensin II relaxes arterioles, lowering systemic vascular resistance and blood pressure."], ["Adrenal glands", "Less aldosterone means less sodium and water retention and less potassium loss, which can raise potassium."], ["Kidneys", "Dilates the efferent arteriole, lowering glomerular pressure and protein loss in diabetic kidney disease, but this can raise creatinine when kidney blood flow depends on angiotensin II."], ["Heart", "Reduces hypertrophy and fibrosis driven by angiotensin II."], ["Gut", "Rarely causes a sprue-like enteropathy with villous atrophy, severe diarrhea and weight loss that resolves after stopping."], ["Fetus", "Blocking the renin-angiotensin system injures the developing fetal kidneys, so it is contraindicated in pregnancy."]], "journey": "Olmesartan medoxomil is an inactive prodrug taken by mouth. Esterases in the gut wall and blood rapidly cut off the medoxomil group, releasing active olmesartan. About a quarter of the dose reaches the bloodstream. It is more than 99 percent protein bound. It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes, which means few drug interactions. It is cleared about half by the kidneys and half in the bile and feces. The half-life is about 13 hours, so it is given once a day. Full blood pressure effect develops over about two weeks.", "sidefx": "Dizziness and low blood pressure come from vasodilation, especially in volume-depleted patients. High potassium comes from reduced aldosterone. Kidney function can worsen when blood flow to the kidneys depends on angiotensin II, as in bilateral renal artery stenosis or dehydration. It carries a boxed warning for fetal toxicity. Angioedema is much less common than with ACE inhibitors but can occur. The sprue-like enteropathy is thought to be an immune-mediated reaction specific to olmesartan. There is no antidote; overdose is treated with fluids and supportive care.", "src": ["Benicar (olmesartan medoxomil) FDA label", "FDA Drug Safety Communication on olmesartan and sprue-like enteropathy, 2013", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "omalizumab": {"origin": "Omalizumab is built on the discovery of IgE. In 1966 the Japanese immunologists Kimishige and Teruko Ishizaka, working in Denver, identified a new antibody class responsible for allergic reactions, which became known as IgE. Decades later, scientists at Genentech and Tanox designed an antibody that would grab free IgE before it could attach to mast cells. They started with a mouse antibody and humanized it so the immune system would tolerate it, leaving only the small binding region from the mouse. Genentech and Novartis brought it to market, and the FDA approved it as Xolair in 2003 for moderate to severe allergic asthma, making it the first biologic approved for asthma. Approvals for chronic hives, nasal polyps and, in 2024, IgE-mediated food allergy followed.", "timeline": [["1966", "Kimishige and Teruko Ishizaka identify IgE as the allergy antibody."], ["2003", "FDA approves omalizumab (Xolair) for allergic asthma."], ["2014", "FDA approves omalizumab for chronic idiopathic urticaria."], ["2024", "FDA approves omalizumab to reduce allergic reactions from accidental food exposure."]], "cell": "In allergy, B cells make IgE against allergens. Free IgE binds through its Fc region to the high-affinity receptor FcepsilonRI on mast cells and basophils. When an allergen cross-links two IgE molecules on the cell, the cell degranulates, releasing histamine, leukotrienes, prostaglandins and cytokines. Omalizumab is a humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody that binds the C epsilon 3 domain of free IgE, the exact site IgE uses to bind FcepsilonRI. Bound IgE cannot attach to mast cells or basophils. Free IgE levels fall sharply. Because mast cells need IgE bound to keep making FcepsilonRI, the receptor count on their surface also falls over weeks, further reducing sensitivity. Omalizumab does not bind IgE already attached to cells, so it cannot cross-link receptors and trigger degranulation itself.", "body": [["Airways", "Fewer IgE-armed mast cells means less allergen-triggered bronchoconstriction and inflammation, reducing asthma attacks and steroid needs."], ["Skin", "Reduces hives and itching in chronic spontaneous urticaria, where mast cell activation is central."], ["Nose and sinuses", "Shrinks nasal polyps and reduces congestion in polyp disease with high IgE activity."], ["Food allergy", "Raises the amount of food needed to trigger a reaction, lowering the danger of accidental exposure."], ["Immune system", "Lowers free IgE, which may reduce defense against some parasitic worm infections."]], "journey": "Omalizumab is given by subcutaneous injection every two or four weeks, with the amount based on body weight and baseline IgE for asthma. It is absorbed slowly from the injection site, peaking in about a week. Like other antibodies, it stays mainly in the blood and extracellular fluid. It is cleared by the reticuloendothelial system: omalizumab-IgE complexes are taken up mainly in the liver and broken down into amino acids, and it is also recycled and degraded like ordinary IgG. The half-life is about 26 days. Free IgE drops within hours, but asthma benefit builds over about 12 to 16 weeks.", "sidefx": "Injection-site reactions, headache and joint pain are common. Anaphylaxis can occur, sometimes after the first dose and sometimes after many, and can be delayed; it carries a boxed warning, and patients are observed after early doses and carry epinephrine. The reason is that omalizumab is a foreign protein and can itself provoke an immune response. Serum sickness-like reactions occur rarely. Blocking IgE may increase risk of parasitic infection in endemic areas. When oral steroids are reduced, eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis can appear, likely unmasked rather than caused. Total IgE lab values rise during therapy because the test measures bound complexes. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The -mab ending marks a monoclonal antibody, and the -zu- in omalizumab marks it as humanized, mostly human with a small mouse binding region.", "src": ["Xolair (omalizumab) FDA label", "Ishizaka K, Ishizaka T. Physicochemical properties of reaginic antibody. J Immunol 1966", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "oseltamivir": {"origin": "Oseltamivir is a product of structure-based drug design. Influenza viruses carry an enzyme on their surface called neuraminidase. In the 1980s, scientists determined its three-dimensional structure, which showed a conserved pocket that could be targeted. Zanamivir, an inhaled neuraminidase inhibitor, came first from Australian research. Chemists at Gilead Sciences in California then designed oseltamivir in the mid 1990s as a pill that could be swallowed, using a molecule that fits the pocket and an ester form that is absorbed from the gut. Gilead licensed it to Roche, and the FDA approved it as Tamiflu in 1999. Its commercial synthesis originally started from shikimic acid, extracted from Chinese star anise, which caused supply worries during the 2005 bird flu scare and the 2009 H1N1 pandemic.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Scientists solve the 3D structure of influenza neuraminidase."], ["1990s", "Gilead Sciences designs oseltamivir and licenses it to Roche."], ["1999", "FDA approves oseltamivir (Tamiflu) for influenza."], ["2009", "Oseltamivir is widely stockpiled and used during the H1N1 pandemic."]], "cell": "Influenza virus binds to sialic acid on the surface of respiratory cells to get in. After the virus copies itself inside the cell, new virus particles bud from the membrane but stay stuck to sialic acid on the cell surface and to each other. Viral neuraminidase cuts sialic acid, freeing the new viruses to spread. Oseltamivir carboxylate, the active form, mimics the transition state of sialic acid in the neuraminidase active site and binds it as a competitive inhibitor. With neuraminidase blocked, new virus particles clump together and stay stuck to the infected cell, so the infection spreads more slowly through the airway. It does not stop virus from entering cells or replicating inside them, which is why it works best started early, within about two days of symptoms. It works against influenza A and B.", "body": [["Airways", "Limits spread of influenza from cell to cell in the respiratory tract, shortening illness by about a day when started early and reducing complications in high-risk patients."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are the most common side effects, reduced by taking it with food."], ["Brain", "Rare reports of delirium, abnormal behavior and self-injury, mostly in children in Japan; influenza itself can also cause these."], ["Prevention", "Taken after exposure, it lowers the chance of developing influenza."]], "journey": "Oseltamivir phosphate is an ethyl ester prodrug taken by mouth. It is well absorbed, and liver esterases convert most of it to oseltamivir carboxylate, the active form. The carboxylate has low protein binding and spreads into the lungs, nasal lining and middle ear. It is not metabolized further and is not handled by CYP enzymes. It leaves almost entirely unchanged in the urine by filtration and tubular secretion, so doses are lowered in kidney impairment. The half-life of the active form is about six to ten hours, supporting twice-daily dosing for treatment.", "sidefx": "Nausea and vomiting are common and are thought to reflect local irritation in the gut; taking it with food helps. Rare neuropsychiatric events such as confusion, abnormal behavior and self-injury have been reported, especially in children, and are on the label, although it is unclear how much is caused by the drug versus influenza encephalopathy. Rare skin reactions such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome have been reported. Resistance can develop through mutations in neuraminidase, such as H275Y in H1N1 strains. It can reduce the effect of the live intranasal flu vaccine. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Early Tamiflu manufacturing relied on shikimic acid from Chinese star anise, the spice used in pho and five-spice powder.", "src": ["Tamiflu (oseltamivir) FDA label", "Kim CU et al. Influenza neuraminidase inhibitors possessing a novel hydrophobic interaction in the enzyme active site. J Am Chem Soc 1997", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "oxybutynin": {"origin": "Oxybutynin is a synthetic antimuscarinic drug. It was developed in the 1960s and approved in the US in the 1970s as Ditropan for bladder instability. For many years it was the main drug for overactive bladder, but dry mouth and constipation caused many people to stop taking it. Extended-release tablets, introduced around 1998, released the drug slowly and reduced peaks that caused side effects. A skin patch, Oxytrol, was approved in 2003; by avoiding first-pass metabolism, it produced less of the metabolite linked to dry mouth. In 2013 the FDA approved the patch for over-the-counter sale for women, the first overactive bladder drug available without a prescription.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Oxybutynin (Ditropan) is approved in the US for bladder instability."], ["2003", "FDA approves the oxybutynin patch (Oxytrol)."], ["2013", "FDA approves the Oxytrol patch for over-the-counter use in women."]], "cell": "The detrusor muscle of the bladder contracts when acetylcholine from parasympathetic nerves binds muscarinic M3 receptors. M3 receptors are Gq coupled: activation turns on phospholipase C, producing IP3, which releases calcium and drives smooth muscle contraction. In overactive bladder, involuntary detrusor contractions cause urgency, frequency and leakage. Oxybutynin is a competitive antagonist at muscarinic receptors, especially M1 and M3. Blocking M3 on the detrusor relaxes the bladder wall, increases bladder capacity and reduces involuntary contractions. It also has a mild direct relaxing effect on smooth muscle and some local anesthetic activity. Because it is a tertiary amine, it crosses the blood-brain barrier and blocks M1 receptors in the brain, which can cause confusion, especially in older adults.", "body": [["Bladder", "Relaxes the detrusor muscle, increasing bladder capacity and reducing urgency, frequency and urge incontinence."], ["Mouth", "Blocks M3 receptors on salivary glands, causing dry mouth, the most common side effect."], ["Gut", "Slows gut movement, causing constipation."], ["Eyes", "Blocks the iris sphincter and ciliary muscle, causing blurred vision and pupil dilation, and can trigger angle-closure glaucoma."], ["Brain", "Crosses the blood-brain barrier and can cause drowsiness, confusion and memory problems, especially in older adults."], ["Skin and temperature", "Reduces sweating, raising the risk of overheating in hot weather."]], "journey": "Oral oxybutynin is absorbed quickly but has low bioavailability because the gut wall and liver metabolize much of it with CYP3A4. The main metabolite, N-desethyloxybutynin, is also active and is thought to cause much of the dry mouth. Extended-release tablets release drug gradually and produce lower peaks of this metabolite. The patch and gel bypass first-pass metabolism, giving lower metabolite levels and fewer side effects. It crosses into the brain. The half-life of immediate-release tablets is about two to three hours. Metabolites leave mainly in the urine. CYP3A4 inhibitors raise its levels.", "sidefx": "Nearly every side effect follows from muscarinic blockade beyond the bladder: dry mouth from salivary glands, constipation from the gut, blurred vision from the eye, fast heart rate from the heart, and reduced sweating with heat intolerance. Urinary retention can occur, especially with an enlarged prostate. Because it enters the brain, it can cause confusion and memory problems and is on the Beers list of drugs to avoid in older adults. Skin reactions occur with the patch. Severe overdose produces an anticholinergic toxidrome; physostigmine, a cholinesterase inhibitor that crosses into the brain, can reverse severe delirium.", "src": ["Ditropan XL (oxybutynin) FDA label", "Oxytrol (oxybutynin transdermal) labeling", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "oxycodone": {"origin": "Oxycodone is a semisynthetic opioid made from thebaine, an alkaloid in the opium poppy that has little pain-relieving effect on its own. In 1916 Martin Freund and Edmund Speyer at the University of Frankfurt in Germany made oxycodone from thebaine, hoping for a drug that kept the pain relief of morphine with fewer drawbacks. It was introduced in Germany in 1917 as Eukodal. In the US it became common in combination products such as Percodan, with aspirin, from 1950, and Percocet, with acetaminophen, from the 1970s. In 1995 the FDA approved OxyContin, an extended-release oxycodone by Purdue Pharma. Aggressive marketing that understated addiction risk helped drive the US opioid epidemic, which led to an abuse-deterrent reformulation in 2010, major lawsuits and criminal guilty pleas by Purdue.", "timeline": [["1916", "Freund and Speyer synthesize oxycodone from thebaine in Frankfurt."], ["1917", "Oxycodone is introduced in Germany as Eukodal."], ["1950", "Percodan (oxycodone with aspirin) is approved in the US."], ["1995", "FDA approves extended-release OxyContin."], ["2010", "OxyContin is reformulated to resist crushing and dissolving."]], "cell": "Oxycodone is a full agonist at mu-opioid receptors, which are Gi/Go coupled receptors, with some activity at kappa receptors. Binding inhibits adenylyl cyclase and lowers cAMP. Presynaptically, it closes voltage-gated calcium channels on pain fibers in the dorsal horn of the spinal cord, reducing release of glutamate and substance P. Postsynaptically, it opens G protein-coupled inwardly rectifying potassium (GIRK) channels, hyperpolarizing neurons so they fire less. In the brainstem and midbrain, mu activation strengthens descending pain inhibition. In the brainstem respiratory centers, the same mechanism blunts the response to carbon dioxide, causing respiratory depression. In the ventral tegmental area, mu activation inhibits GABA interneurons, increasing dopamine release in the nucleus accumbens, which drives euphoria and reinforcement.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Blocks pain transmission and reduces the emotional impact of pain; causes sedation and euphoria."], ["Breathing", "Lowers the brainstem drive to breathe, the cause of death in overdose."], ["Eyes", "Causes pinpoint pupils through the Edinger-Westphal nucleus."], ["Gut", "Slows gut movement and increases sphincter tone, causing constipation, and stimulates the chemoreceptor trigger zone, causing nausea."], ["Bladder", "Increases sphincter tone and can cause urinary retention."], ["Reward circuits", "Increases dopamine in the reward pathway, which underlies dependence and addiction risk."]], "journey": "Oxycodone is taken by mouth and has high oral bioavailability, about 60 to 87 percent, much higher than morphine, because it undergoes less first-pass metabolism. Immediate-release forms act within about 15 to 30 minutes; extended-release tablets release it over about 12 hours. The liver breaks it down mainly with CYP3A4 to noroxycodone, which is weak, and partly with CYP2D6 to oxymorphone, a potent active metabolite present in small amounts. CYP3A4 inhibitors such as some antifungals and macrolides raise its levels and can cause overdose. Metabolites leave mainly in the urine. The half-life is about three to five hours. It crosses the placenta and into breast milk.", "sidefx": "Respiratory depression is the most dangerous effect, from mu-receptor suppression of brainstem respiratory centers, and it is worsened by benzodiazepines, alcohol and CYP3A4 inhibitors. Constipation does not fade with tolerance. Nausea comes from the chemoreceptor trigger zone. Sedation and dizziness raise fall risk. Tolerance and physical dependence develop with repeated use, and it is a Schedule II controlled substance with a boxed warning for addiction, misuse and overdose. Combination products also carry acetaminophen liver risk. Naloxone reverses opioid effects but may wear off before extended-release oxycodone does, so patients need continued monitoring.", "src": ["OxyContin (oxycodone extended-release) FDA label", "Roxicodone (oxycodone) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "oxytocin": {"origin": "Oxytocin is the body's own hormone from the posterior pituitary gland. In 1906 the British physiologist Henry Dale found that extracts of the posterior pituitary made the uterus contract. The name oxytocin comes from Greek words meaning quick birth. Pituitary extracts were used in childbirth for decades, but they varied in strength and contained vasopressin as well. In 1953 the American biochemist Vincent du Vigneaud worked out oxytocin's structure, a small ring of nine amino acids, and then synthesized it, the first polypeptide hormone ever made in the lab. He won the 1955 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. Synthetic oxytocin, sold as Pitocin, became one of the most widely used drugs in obstetrics, both to start or strengthen labor and to prevent and treat bleeding after delivery.", "timeline": [["1906", "Henry Dale shows that posterior pituitary extract makes the uterus contract."], ["1953", "Vincent du Vigneaud determines the structure of oxytocin and synthesizes it."], ["1955", "Du Vigneaud receives the Nobel Prize in Chemistry."]], "cell": "Oxytocin binds the oxytocin receptor, a Gq coupled receptor on uterine smooth muscle (myometrium) and on myoepithelial cells in the breast. Activation turns on phospholipase C, which makes IP3 and DAG. IP3 releases calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, and voltage-gated calcium channels also open. Calcium binds calmodulin, activating myosin light chain kinase, and the muscle contracts in strong, rhythmic waves. Oxytocin also stimulates the uterine lining to release prostaglandins, which add to contractions. Estrogen sharply increases the number of oxytocin receptors late in pregnancy, which is why the term uterus is so sensitive. After delivery, firm contraction compresses the blood vessels that run through the uterine muscle, which controls bleeding. Its structure is close to vasopressin, so at high levels it weakly activates V2 receptors in the kidney, retaining water.", "body": [["Uterus", "Causes rhythmic contractions that progress labor and, after delivery, a firm, contracted uterus that clamps down on bleeding vessels."], ["Breasts", "Contracts the cells around milk ducts, causing milk letdown; breastfeeding releases natural oxytocin for the same reason."], ["Kidneys", "At high levels, its vasopressin-like effect makes the kidneys hold water, which can cause low sodium and water intoxication."], ["Heart and vessels", "A rapid IV bolus relaxes vascular smooth muscle, causing a sudden drop in blood pressure and fast heart rate."], ["Fetus", "Too many or too strong contractions reduce blood flow through the placenta and can cause fetal distress."]], "journey": "Oxytocin is a peptide, so it is destroyed in the gut and is given IV or IM. IV oxytocin acts almost immediately, and IM within a few minutes. Its half-life is very short, a few minutes, so a continuous infusion is used to keep a steady effect, and the effect fades soon after stopping. It is broken down by oxytocinase enzymes in the liver, kidney and, during pregnancy, the placenta. Very little leaves the body unchanged.", "sidefx": "Uterine hyperstimulation, meaning contractions that are too frequent or too long, comes from too much receptor activation and can cause fetal distress, placental abruption and, in a scarred uterus, rupture. Water intoxication comes from its vasopressin-like effect on the kidney, especially with long infusions in large volumes of dilute fluid; it causes low sodium, headache, seizures and coma. A rapid undiluted IV bolus can cause sudden low blood pressure, fast heart rate and, rarely, cardiac arrest, which is why it is diluted and infused. Nausea and vomiting are common. There is no antidote; the infusion is stopped and its short half-life brings rapid recovery.", "fun": "Oxytocin was the first polypeptide hormone ever synthesized, which earned Vincent du Vigneaud the 1955 Nobel Prize in Chemistry.", "src": ["Pitocin (oxytocin injection) FDA label", "Dale HH. On some physiological actions of ergot. J Physiol 1906", "Du Vigneaud V et al. Synthesis of an octapeptide amide with the hormonal activity of oxytocin. J Am Chem Soc 1953"]}, "paclitaxel": {"origin": "Paclitaxel comes from the bark of the Pacific yew tree, Taxus brevifolia. In 1962, as part of a National Cancer Institute program that screened thousands of plants for anticancer activity, the botanist Arthur Barclay collected Pacific yew bark in Washington State. Monroe Wall and Mansukh Wani at the Research Triangle Institute in North Carolina found that the bark extract killed cancer cells, isolated the active compound, named it taxol, and published its structure in 1971. In 1979 Susan Horwitz at the Albert Einstein College of Medicine discovered its unique mechanism: it stabilizes microtubules instead of breaking them down. Supply was a huge problem, because the bark of several trees was needed per patient. Bristol-Myers Squibb developed it, and the FDA approved it as Taxol in 1992 for ovarian cancer. Semisynthesis from needles of the European yew, and later plant cell culture, solved the supply problem.", "timeline": [["1962", "Arthur Barclay collects Pacific yew bark for the NCI plant screening program."], ["1971", "Monroe Wall and Mansukh Wani publish the structure of taxol."], ["1979", "Susan Horwitz shows that taxol stabilizes microtubules."], ["1992", "FDA approves paclitaxel (Taxol) for ovarian cancer."], ["2005", "FDA approves albumin-bound paclitaxel (Abraxane)."]], "cell": "Microtubules are hollow tubes built from alpha and beta tubulin. They constantly grow and shrink, a process called dynamic instability, and this movement is essential for the mitotic spindle to capture chromosomes and pull them apart. Paclitaxel binds the beta-tubulin subunit on the inside of the microtubule and locks the tubulin units together, so microtubules cannot shrink. The cell becomes filled with rigid, nonfunctional microtubule bundles. During mitosis, the spindle cannot work properly, the spindle assembly checkpoint stays active, and the cell is stuck in M phase, between metaphase and anaphase. Prolonged arrest triggers apoptosis, partly through phosphorylation of BCL-2. This is the opposite of vinca alkaloids, which block microtubule assembly. Microtubules are also needed for transport along long nerve axons, which explains its neurotoxicity.", "body": [["Tumors", "Kills rapidly dividing cancer cells by arresting them in mitosis, used for breast, ovarian, lung, pancreatic and other cancers."], ["Bone marrow", "Dividing blood cell precursors are also killed, causing neutropenia, which is usually the dose-limiting toxicity."], ["Nerves", "Disrupts axonal transport in long sensory nerves, causing numbness, tingling and burning in a stocking-glove pattern."], ["Hair", "Hair follicle cells divide quickly, so hair loss is usually complete."], ["Muscles and joints", "Aching muscles and joints are common a few days after each infusion."], ["Heart", "Can cause slow heart rate and, less often, conduction problems during infusion."]], "journey": "Paclitaxel is given only by IV infusion, because it is poorly absorbed by mouth. It is very fat soluble and insoluble in water, so the standard form is dissolved in Cremophor EL, a castor oil-based solvent; Abraxane binds it to albumin nanoparticles instead. It is highly protein bound and spreads widely into tissues, but it does not cross the blood-brain barrier well. The liver metabolizes it mainly with CYP2C8 and also CYP3A4, so drugs that block or induce these enzymes change its levels. It is excreted mainly in the bile and feces, with little in the urine. The terminal half-life ranges from several hours to over two days, depending on the schedule.", "sidefx": "Neutropenia comes from killing dividing marrow cells and raises the risk of serious infection; it carries a boxed warning. Peripheral neuropathy happens because stabilized microtubules cannot carry cargo down long sensory axons, and it can be permanent. Hair loss and mouth sores reflect damage to rapidly dividing cells. Severe hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis, are largely caused by the Cremophor solvent, which activates complement and mast cells; this is why patients receive a steroid and antihistamines beforehand and why the albumin-bound form does not need them. Muscle and joint aches are common. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Early taxol came from stripping Pacific yew bark, and treating a single patient could take the bark of more than one mature tree, which raised real fears of overharvesting.", "src": ["Taxol (paclitaxel) FDA label", "Wani MC et al. Plant antitumor agents VI: the isolation and structure of taxol. J Am Chem Soc 1971", "Schiff PB, Fant J, Horwitz SB. Promotion of microtubule assembly in vitro by taxol. Nature 1979"]}, "pancuronium": {"origin": "Pancuronium was one of the first steroid-based neuromuscular blockers. Curare, the arrow poison of South American hunters, had shown that blocking nicotinic receptors at the neuromuscular junction could relax muscles for surgery, and its active compound tubocurarine was used from the 1940s. Chemists noticed that a natural steroid alkaloid from an African plant had weak muscle-relaxing activity, and they reasoned that a rigid steroid skeleton could hold two quaternary nitrogens at the right distance, mimicking two acetylcholine molecules. In the 1960s, chemists at Organon in Scotland, including William Hewett and David Savage, built pancuronium on this design. It entered clinical use in the late 1960s. Its success led to vecuronium and rocuronium, which have largely replaced it. Pancuronium is also of historical note because it was used in some lethal injection protocols in the US.", "timeline": [["1940s", "Tubocurarine, from curare, enters anesthesia practice."], ["1960s", "Organon chemists design pancuronium on a steroid skeleton."], ["1980s", "Vecuronium, a related steroid blocker without vagal effects, is introduced."]], "cell": "At the neuromuscular junction, acetylcholine released from the motor nerve binds nicotinic NM receptors on the muscle endplate. These are ligand-gated channels; two acetylcholine molecules must bind the two alpha subunits to open the channel, letting sodium in to depolarize the endplate and trigger contraction. Pancuronium is a nondepolarizing, competitive antagonist. Its two quaternary ammonium groups, held apart by the steroid skeleton, occupy the acetylcholine binding sites on the alpha subunits without opening the channel. With receptors blocked, the endplate cannot depolarize, and the muscle becomes flaccid. Because it is competitive, raising acetylcholine with a cholinesterase inhibitor such as neostigmine can overcome the block. Pancuronium also blocks cardiac muscarinic M2 receptors, causing a vagolytic effect, and may increase norepinephrine release, which explains the rise in heart rate and blood pressure.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Causes complete flaccid paralysis, including the diaphragm, so the patient must be ventilated."], ["Brain", "Has no sedative or pain-relieving effect, so patients must be fully sedated to avoid awareness while paralyzed."], ["Heart", "Blocks cardiac muscarinic receptors, removing vagal braking and raising heart rate, with mild increases in blood pressure."], ["Breathing", "Paralysis of respiratory muscles means breathing stops until the drug wears off or is reversed."], ["Kidneys and liver", "Mostly cleared by the kidneys with some liver metabolism, so kidney or liver failure greatly prolongs paralysis."]], "journey": "Pancuronium is given IV. Because it is charged, it does not cross the blood-brain barrier or the placenta much. It acts within a few minutes and lasts more than an hour, longer than vecuronium or rocuronium. Most of it is excreted unchanged by the kidneys, and some is deacetylated in the liver to 3-hydroxypancuronium, which is also active. In kidney failure, both build up and paralysis can last much longer. The half-life is about two hours in normal kidney function. Neostigmine, given with glycopyrrolate, can reverse it once recovery has begun; sugammadex binds it only weakly.", "sidefx": "Prolonged paralysis is the main risk, especially in kidney failure or in the elderly, and can cause residual weakness after surgery with poor breathing and aspiration. Long use in the ICU, especially with steroids, is linked to critical illness myopathy. Tachycardia and mild hypertension come from vagal blockade and increased norepinephrine release. Because it provides no sedation, inadequate sedation causes awareness during paralysis. Reversal uses neostigmine to increase acetylcholine, paired with glycopyrrolate or atropine to block muscarinic effects such as bradycardia.", "src": ["Pancuronium bromide injection FDA label", "Miller's Anesthesia", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "pantoprazole": {"origin": "Pantoprazole was developed by the German company Byk Gulden in Konstanz in the 1980s, following the success of omeprazole as the first proton pump inhibitor. Byk Gulden chemists designed a benzimidazole PPI that was more chemically stable at near-neutral pH, which made it possible to give as an IV injection. It was launched in Germany in 1994. In the US, Wyeth marketed it, and the FDA approved the oral form as Protonix in 2000 and the IV form soon after. The IV form became a standard in hospitals for patients who cannot take pills and for upper GI bleeding, where suppressing acid helps blood clots stay stable on ulcers.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Byk Gulden develops pantoprazole in Germany."], ["1994", "Pantoprazole is launched in Germany."], ["2000", "FDA approves oral pantoprazole (Protonix)."]], "cell": "Pantoprazole is a prodrug and a weak base. After it reaches the parietal cells of the stomach, it concentrates in the highly acidic secretory canaliculi, where acid converts it to an active cyclic sulfenamide. This active form binds covalently to cysteine residues on the H+/K+ ATPase, the proton pump that exchanges hydrogen ions for potassium ions as the final step in acid secretion. Binding is irreversible, so acid production returns only when the cell makes new pumps, which takes about a day or more. Because it acts on active pumps, it works best when pumps are being stimulated by a meal. Less acid also means pepsin is less active, and in GI bleeding a higher stomach pH helps platelets aggregate and clots stay intact.", "body": [["Stomach", "Strongly suppresses acid, allowing ulcers and erosions to heal and helping clots stay stable over bleeding ulcers."], ["Esophagus", "Less acid reflux reduces heartburn and allows erosive esophagitis to heal."], ["Gut and infection", "Low acid lets more swallowed bacteria survive, raising risk of C. difficile infection."], ["Nutrients and minerals", "Long-term use can lower vitamin B12, iron and magnesium absorption and is linked to fractures."], ["Kidneys", "Rarely causes acute interstitial nephritis, an immune reaction in the kidney."]], "journey": "Pantoprazole is given by mouth as an enteric-coated tablet or IV. Oral bioavailability is high, about 77 percent. IV doses begin suppressing acid within about 15 to 30 minutes. The blood half-life is only about one hour, but because it permanently disables pumps, its effect lasts about a day. The liver metabolizes it mainly with CYP2C19 and also CYP3A4, then sulfates it, and the metabolites leave mainly in the urine. It has a lower tendency to inhibit CYP2C19 than omeprazole, so fewer drug interactions. Poor CYP2C19 metabolizers have higher levels.", "sidefx": "Headache, diarrhea and abdominal pain are common short-term effects. Long-term risks come from reduced stomach acid: C. difficile infection, low vitamin B12 and iron, low magnesium, which can cause cramps, seizures and arrhythmias, and modestly increased fracture risk. Acute interstitial nephritis and cutaneous lupus have been reported. Rebound acid can occur when stopped, because gastrin rises during treatment. IV site reactions can occur. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Protonix (pantoprazole) FDA label", "Protonix I.V. FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "paroxetine": {"origin": "Paroxetine was first made in the 1970s by chemists at the Danish company Ferrosan. It was later developed by Beecham, which became SmithKline Beecham, and the FDA approved it as Paxil in 1992 for depression. Approvals for panic disorder, social anxiety disorder, generalized anxiety disorder, obsessive-compulsive disorder and PTSD followed, and it was heavily marketed for social anxiety disorder. It became one of the most widely used SSRIs. In 2004 the FDA added a boxed warning about suicidality in young people for all antidepressants, after a review that included paroxetine data in adolescents. In 2013 a low-dose form, Brisdelle, became the first nonhormonal drug approved for menopausal hot flashes.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Ferrosan chemists synthesize paroxetine in Denmark."], ["1992", "FDA approves paroxetine (Paxil) for depression."], ["2004", "FDA adds a boxed warning on suicidality in young people for antidepressants."], ["2013", "FDA approves low-dose paroxetine (Brisdelle) for menopausal hot flashes."]], "cell": "Paroxetine is a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI). It binds the serotonin transporter (SERT) on presynaptic neurons with very high affinity, blocking reuptake of serotonin from the synapse. Serotonin levels in the synapse rise right away, but the clinical effect takes weeks. Over time, presynaptic 5-HT1A autoreceptors desensitize, so serotonin neurons fire more, and downstream changes in receptors, gene expression and neuroplasticity develop; these track with improved mood and anxiety. Paroxetine has more muscarinic blocking activity than other SSRIs, causing dry mouth and constipation, and it weakly blocks norepinephrine reuptake. It is also a potent inhibitor of CYP2D6, which affects many other drugs.", "body": [["Brain", "Over several weeks, more serotonin signaling improves depression and anxiety disorders, including panic, social anxiety, OCD and PTSD."], ["Temperature control", "Low-dose use reduces hot flashes, likely by stabilizing serotonin signaling in the hypothalamic temperature center."], ["Sexual function", "Higher serotonin commonly lowers libido and delays orgasm and ejaculation."], ["Gut", "Serotonin receptors in the gut cause nausea early on; muscarinic blockade causes constipation."], ["Blood", "Platelets take up serotonin through SERT; blocking it reduces platelet serotonin and raises bleeding risk, especially with NSAIDs."], ["Fetus", "Early pregnancy exposure has been linked to heart defects, and late exposure to newborn adaptation problems."]], "journey": "Paroxetine is taken by mouth and absorbed completely, but much of it is metabolized on its first pass. It is highly protein bound and spreads widely, including into the brain. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP2D6. Because paroxetine itself inhibits CYP2D6, it slows its own breakdown, so levels rise more than expected with higher doses (nonlinear kinetics). Metabolites leave mainly in the urine. The half-life is about one day, shorter than fluoxetine, and it has no long-lived active metabolite.", "sidefx": "Nausea, sweating, sexual dysfunction and drowsiness come from increased serotonin signaling. Dry mouth and constipation come from muscarinic blockade, more than with other SSRIs. Weight gain is more common than with most SSRIs. Because of its short half-life and lack of active metabolites, stopping it suddenly often causes a discontinuation syndrome with dizziness, irritability, flu-like symptoms and electric shock sensations. Low sodium (SIADH) can occur, especially in older adults. Combining it with other serotonergic drugs can cause serotonin syndrome. Strong CYP2D6 inhibition reduces activation of tamoxifen and raises levels of drugs such as metoprolol. It carries a boxed warning for suicidal thoughts in young people. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Paxil (paroxetine) FDA label", "Brisdelle (paroxetine) FDA label", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology"]}, "patiromer": {"origin": "Patiromer is a man-made polymer designed to work entirely inside the gut. For decades, the main oral potassium binder was sodium polystyrene sulfonate (Kayexalate), approved in 1958, which works slowly, adds sodium, and has been linked to bowel injury. Doctors also needed a way to let patients keep taking drugs that protect the heart and kidneys, such as ACE inhibitors, ARBs and spironolactone, which raise potassium. Relypsa, a California biotech company, developed patiromer as a nonabsorbed polymer that swaps calcium for potassium. The FDA approved it as Veltassa in 2015 for hyperkalemia, the first new potassium binder in more than 50 years. Sodium zirconium cyclosilicate followed in 2018.", "timeline": [["1958", "Sodium polystyrene sulfonate (Kayexalate) is approved as a potassium binder."], ["2015", "FDA approves patiromer (Veltassa) for hyperkalemia."]], "cell": "Patiromer is a nonabsorbed, cross-linked polymer of fluoroacrylate, given as a calcium-sorbitol salt. Its carboxylate groups carry a negative charge and are loaded with calcium ions. As it passes through the gut, it releases calcium and binds potassium in exchange. Most of the binding happens in the distal colon, where potassium concentration in the stool is highest because the colon secretes potassium. The bound potassium is passed in the feces. Removing potassium from the gut increases the concentration gradient, so more potassium moves from the blood into the colon, lowering serum potassium. It also binds magnesium, which explains low magnesium levels. Because the polymer exchanges calcium rather than sodium, it does not add a sodium load.", "body": [["Blood potassium", "Gradually lowers serum potassium over hours to days, allowing continued use of ACE inhibitors, ARBs and spironolactone."], ["Colon", "Binds potassium where it is secreted into stool; constipation, diarrhea and gas are common."], ["Magnesium", "Also binds magnesium in the gut, which can lower blood magnesium."], ["Heart", "Keeping potassium in a safe range reduces the risk of potassium-related arrhythmias over time, but it is too slow for emergencies."]], "journey": "Patiromer is taken by mouth as a powder mixed with water. It is not absorbed from the gut and is not metabolized. It passes through the GI tract and is excreted in the feces, carrying bound potassium with it. Its effect on serum potassium begins in about seven hours and continues for about a day after a dose. Because it can bind other oral drugs in the gut, other medicines are separated from it by at least three hours.", "sidefx": "Because it acts in the bowel, constipation, diarrhea, gas and nausea are the most common side effects. Low magnesium happens because the polymer binds magnesium along with potassium, so magnesium levels are checked. Too much effect causes low potassium. It can bind other oral drugs and lower their absorption. It should not be used for life-threatening hyperkalemia, because its onset is too slow; emergency treatment uses calcium, insulin with glucose, beta agonists and dialysis. Unlike sodium polystyrene sulfonate, it does not add sodium. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Veltassa (patiromer) FDA label", "Weir MR et al. Patiromer in patients with kidney disease and hyperkalemia receiving RAAS inhibitors (OPAL-HK). NEJM 2015"]}, "pembrolizumab": {"origin": "Pembrolizumab rests on two discoveries about how the immune system is held in check. In 1992 Tasuku Honjo's lab at Kyoto University discovered PD-1, a receptor on T cells. Later work by Honjo, Gordon Freeman, Arlene Sharpe and others showed that PD-1 acts as a brake: when it binds its partner PD-L1, the T cell shuts down, and many tumors display PD-L1 to protect themselves. Meanwhile James Allison showed that blocking another brake, CTLA-4, could make the immune system attack tumors. Honjo and Allison shared the 2018 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. Pembrolizumab was created as an anti-PD-1 antibody by scientists at Organon, and it passed to Merck through company mergers. The FDA approved it as Keytruda in 2014 for advanced melanoma, the first PD-1 blocker approved in the US. In 2017 it became the first cancer drug approved based on a tumor's genetic feature, MSI-high status, regardless of where the cancer started.", "timeline": [["1992", "Tasuku Honjo's lab discovers PD-1."], ["2014", "FDA approves pembrolizumab (Keytruda) for advanced melanoma."], ["2017", "FDA approves pembrolizumab for any MSI-high solid tumor, the first tissue-agnostic approval."], ["2018", "Honjo and James Allison share the Nobel Prize for cancer immunotherapy."]], "cell": "T cells recognize tumor antigens through their T-cell receptor, but their activity is controlled by checkpoint receptors. PD-1 is expressed on activated T cells. When it binds PD-L1 or PD-L2 on tumor cells or antigen-presenting cells, PD-1 recruits the phosphatase SHP-2, which removes phosphate groups from signaling molecules downstream of the T-cell receptor and CD28. T-cell activation, cytokine production and killing are suppressed, a state called exhaustion. Many tumors raise PD-L1 to escape attack. Pembrolizumab is a humanized IgG4 monoclonal antibody that binds PD-1 and blocks its interaction with PD-L1 and PD-L2. The brake is released, and tumor-specific cytotoxic T cells resume proliferating, making interferon-gamma and killing tumor cells. The IgG4 form was chosen because it is less likely to kill the T cells it binds. Because the same brake protects normal tissues, releasing it can cause immune attack on healthy organs.", "body": [["Tumors", "Reactivated T cells attack cancer cells; responses can be slow but sometimes durable for years, and tumors may briefly appear larger as immune cells enter them."], ["Thyroid", "Immune attack on the thyroid commonly causes thyroiditis, often brief hyperthyroidism followed by permanent hypothyroidism."], ["Lungs", "Can cause pneumonitis with cough and shortness of breath, which can be serious."], ["Gut and liver", "Can cause colitis with diarrhea, and immune hepatitis with rising liver enzymes."], ["Skin", "Rash, itching and vitiligo are common; rarely severe blistering reactions occur."], ["Hormone glands", "Can inflame the pituitary or adrenals, or destroy pancreatic islet cells, causing type 1 diabetes with DKA."], ["Heart", "Rarely causes myocarditis, which can be fatal."]], "journey": "Pembrolizumab is given by IV infusion every three or six weeks. Like other antibodies, it stays mostly in the blood and extracellular fluid. It is not metabolized by liver enzymes; instead it is broken down into peptides and amino acids by protein catabolism throughout the body. It is recycled by the neonatal Fc receptor, which gives it a long half-life of about three to four weeks. Kidney and liver impairment do not meaningfully change its clearance. Immune effects can begin weeks to months after starting and can appear even after it is stopped.", "sidefx": "Side effects are immune-related adverse events: the same release of the PD-1 brake that lets T cells attack tumors also lets them attack normal tissue. This explains thyroiditis, pneumonitis, colitis, hepatitis, nephritis, skin rash, hypophysitis, adrenal insufficiency, type 1 diabetes and myocarditis. Most are managed by pausing the drug and giving corticosteroids to suppress the immune response, with other immunosuppressants for steroid-resistant cases. Hormone deficiencies such as hypothyroidism are often permanent and need lifelong replacement. Infusion reactions can occur. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Former US President Jimmy Carter was treated with pembrolizumab in 2015 for melanoma that had spread to his brain, and his cancer went into remission.", "src": ["Keytruda (pembrolizumab) FDA label", "Ishida Y, Agata Y, Shibahara K, Honjo T. Induced expression of PD-1. EMBO J 1992", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 2018 press release"]}, "penicillamine": {"origin": "Penicillamine is a breakdown product of penicillin. During the intense wartime research on penicillin in the 1940s, chemists including Ernst Chain and colleagues identified it as a fragment produced when the penicillin molecule is split. It is a simple amino acid with a free thiol (sulfhydryl) group that grabs metals. In 1956 the British physician John Walshe, working on Wilson disease, a genetic disorder in which copper builds up in the liver and brain, reported that penicillamine made patients excrete large amounts of copper in the urine. It became the first effective oral treatment for Wilson disease and turned a fatal disease into a manageable one. It was later used for cystinuria and severe rheumatoid arthritis, though safer drugs have largely replaced it for arthritis.", "timeline": [["1940s", "Penicillamine is identified as a degradation product of penicillin."], ["1956", "John Walshe reports penicillamine as an oral copper chelator for Wilson disease."], ["1960s", "Penicillamine is used to dissolve cystine in cystinuria."]], "cell": "Penicillamine is D-penicillamine, a thiol-containing amino acid. Its free sulfhydryl group and nearby amine bind copper tightly, along with lead, mercury and other metals, forming stable, water-soluble complexes that the kidneys excrete in urine. In Wilson disease, this pulls excess copper out of tissues. It may also induce metallothionein, a protein that binds copper in a nontoxic form. In cystinuria, penicillamine reacts with cystine in a thiol-disulfide exchange, forming a mixed disulfide of penicillamine and cysteine that is far more soluble than cystine, so stones are less likely to form and existing stones can dissolve. In rheumatoid arthritis it modulates the immune system, reducing T-cell activity and rheumatoid factor, but this mechanism is less well understood. The thiol group also reacts with pyridoxine, causing vitamin B6 deficiency.", "body": [["Liver and brain", "Removes excess copper from tissues in Wilson disease, slowly improving liver function and neurological symptoms."], ["Kidneys", "Excretes copper-penicillamine complexes in urine; also prevents cystine stones but can cause protein in the urine and nephrotic syndrome."], ["Bone marrow", "Can suppress blood cell production, causing low platelets, low white cells or aplastic anemia."], ["Immune system", "Can trigger autoimmune diseases such as drug-induced lupus, myasthenia gravis and Goodpasture syndrome."], ["Skin", "Rash is common, and long-term use can weaken skin collagen and elastin, causing fragile, wrinkled skin."], ["Taste", "Often causes loss of taste, partly by binding zinc and copper needed for taste."]], "journey": "Penicillamine is taken by mouth on an empty stomach, because food, antacids and iron reduce its absorption. About half of a dose is absorbed. In the blood it binds plasma proteins and forms disulfides with itself and cysteine. The liver metabolizes some of it, but most leaves through the kidneys as disulfides and metal complexes. The blood half-life is a few hours, but it lingers longer in tissues. In Wilson disease, improvement takes months, and neurological symptoms can briefly worsen when treatment starts as copper is mobilized.", "sidefx": "Many side effects come from its reactive thiol group and its effects on the immune system. Rash and fever are early hypersensitivity reactions. Protein in the urine and nephrotic syndrome reflect immune complex damage in the kidney. Bone marrow suppression, including aplastic anemia, can be fatal, which is why blood counts and urine are checked regularly. Autoimmune syndromes include drug-induced lupus, myasthenia gravis, pemphigus and Goodpasture syndrome. Binding of pyridoxine causes vitamin B6 deficiency, so pyridoxine is given with it. Taste loss reflects binding of trace metals. People with penicillin allergy can rarely react to it. There is no antidote; reactions are managed by stopping the drug.", "src": ["Cuprimine (penicillamine) FDA label", "Walshe JM. Penicillamine, a new oral therapy for Wilson's disease. Am J Med 1956", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "penicillin-g": {"origin": "Penicillin G comes from the mold Penicillium. In 1928 Alexander Fleming, at St Mary's Hospital in London, noticed that mold contaminating a staphylococcus culture plate had killed the bacteria around it. He named the active substance penicillin but could not purify it. In 1939 to 1941 at Oxford, Howard Florey, Ernst Chain and Norman Heatley worked out how to extract and purify it, and in 1941 they treated their first patient, a policeman named Albert Alexander, who improved but died when supplies ran out. During World War II, a US government and industry effort led by the Department of Agriculture lab in Peoria, Illinois, developed deep-tank fermentation and found a high-yielding strain on a moldy cantaloupe, enabling mass production by 1944. Fleming, Florey and Chain shared the 1945 Nobel Prize. Long-acting benzathine penicillin G followed in the early 1950s and remains the treatment of choice for syphilis.", "timeline": [["1928", "Alexander Fleming discovers penicillin from Penicillium mold."], ["1941", "Florey, Chain and Heatley treat the first patient with purified penicillin at Oxford."], ["1943", "A high-yielding mold strain is found on a cantaloupe in Peoria, aiding mass production."], ["1945", "Fleming, Florey and Chain share the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."], ["1950s", "Long-acting benzathine penicillin G is introduced."]], "cell": "Bacterial cell walls are made of peptidoglycan, sugar chains cross-linked by short peptides. Penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), which are transpeptidases, form the cross-links by joining the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of one peptide to a neighboring chain. Penicillin G's beta-lactam ring mimics D-ala-D-ala and enters the PBP active site, where it opens and forms a stable covalent bond with an active-site serine, permanently inactivating the enzyme. New peptidoglycan cannot be cross-linked, the wall weakens, and bacterial autolysins further break it down. The bacterium bursts from internal osmotic pressure. Killing is time dependent and works best on actively dividing bacteria. Gram-positive bacteria, with thick exposed peptidoglycan, are most susceptible. Resistance comes from beta-lactamases that break the beta-lactam ring, altered PBPs such as in pneumococci, and reduced entry through gram-negative outer membranes.", "body": [["Infection sites", "Kills susceptible streptococci, Treponema pallidum (syphilis), Neisseria meningitidis, Clostridium and other organisms."], ["Brain and meninges", "Reaches the cerebrospinal fluid in useful amounts when the meninges are inflamed, which is why high-dose IV penicillin treats neurosyphilis and meningitis."], ["Immune system", "Its breakdown products bind body proteins and can trigger allergic reactions, from rash to anaphylaxis."], ["Kidneys", "Rapidly secreted into urine; can cause interstitial nephritis, and potassium salts add potassium in kidney failure."], ["Brain at high levels", "Very high levels, especially with kidney failure, can block GABA-A receptors and cause myoclonus and seizures."], ["Blood", "High doses can cause immune hemolytic anemia with a positive Coombs test."]], "journey": "Penicillin G is destroyed by stomach acid, so it is given IV or IM; penicillin V is the oral relative. Aqueous IV penicillin G reaches high levels quickly. It spreads into most tissues and fluids but poorly into the brain and eye unless they are inflamed. It is not metabolized much. The kidneys clear it rapidly, mainly by tubular secretion through organic anion transporters, giving a short half-life of about 30 minutes; probenecid blocks this secretion and raises levels. Benzathine penicillin G, injected deep IM, dissolves very slowly and releases low levels of drug for two to four weeks. It crosses the placenta, which is how it treats congenital syphilis.", "sidefx": "Allergic reactions are the most important problem. The beta-lactam ring opens and binds to body proteins, forming antigens that can provoke IgE-mediated anaphylaxis, hives or delayed reactions such as rash, serum sickness and interstitial nephritis. Many people labeled allergic are not, and skin testing can clarify. Very high levels block GABA-A receptors in the brain, causing seizures, especially in kidney failure. Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction, with fever and chills after treating syphilis, comes from the release of bacterial products as spirochetes die. Accidental IV injection of benzathine penicillin can cause cardiorespiratory arrest. Anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine. There is no antidote for penicillin itself.", "fun": "The high-yielding Penicillium strain that made mass production possible was found on a moldy cantaloupe in a Peoria, Illinois market.", "src": ["Pfizerpen (penicillin G potassium) FDA label", "Bicillin L-A (penicillin G benzathine) FDA label", "Fleming A. On the antibacterial action of cultures of a penicillium. Br J Exp Pathol 1929"]}, "phenelzine": {"origin": "Phenelzine belongs to the monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs), the first true antidepressants. Their story began with tuberculosis. In the early 1950s, doctors noticed that iproniazid, a drug for tuberculosis, made patients cheerful and energetic. It was found to inhibit monoamine oxidase, and in 1957 Nathan Kline and others reported it as an antidepressant. Iproniazid was soon withdrawn for liver toxicity, and related hydrazine MAOIs were developed. Phenelzine was approved in the US as Nardil in the early 1960s. In 1963 Barry Blackwell, then a young psychiatrist in London, recognized that headaches and dangerous blood pressure spikes in patients on MAOIs were caused by eating aged cheese, which is rich in tyramine. That discovery, the cheese reaction, led to the dietary restrictions that still go with these drugs.", "timeline": [["1952", "Iproniazid, a tuberculosis drug, is noticed to improve mood."], ["1957", "Iproniazid is reported as an antidepressant, launching the MAOI class."], ["1960s", "Phenelzine (Nardil) is approved in the US."], ["1963", "Barry Blackwell identifies the cheese reaction caused by tyramine with MAOIs."]], "cell": "Monoamine oxidase is an enzyme on the outer membrane of mitochondria that breaks down monoamines by oxidative deamination. MAO-A mainly breaks down serotonin and norepinephrine, and MAO-B mainly breaks down dopamine and phenylethylamine. In the gut and liver, MAO-A also destroys dietary tyramine. Phenelzine is a hydrazine that irreversibly inhibits both MAO-A and MAO-B by binding covalently to the enzyme's flavin cofactor. Monoamines that leak from storage vesicles are no longer broken down, so more serotonin, norepinephrine and dopamine accumulate and are released. Over weeks, receptors adapt, which tracks with the antidepressant effect. Recovery requires making new enzyme, which takes about two weeks. Phenelzine also inhibits GABA transaminase, raising brain GABA, which may add to its anti-anxiety effect.", "body": [["Brain", "Raises serotonin, norepinephrine and dopamine, which can lift treatment-resistant and atypical depression and some anxiety disorders after several weeks."], ["Blood vessels and blood pressure", "Commonly causes orthostatic hypotension, partly because false transmitters build up in sympathetic nerves; with tyramine, it can cause a dangerous hypertensive crisis."], ["Gut and liver", "Normal MAO-A in the gut wall and liver protects against dietary tyramine; blocking it lets tyramine reach the blood."], ["Liver", "Hydrazine structure can rarely injure the liver."], ["Nerves", "Binds pyridoxine (vitamin B6), which can cause peripheral neuropathy."], ["Weight and sleep", "Weight gain, insomnia or daytime sleepiness and sexual dysfunction are common."]], "journey": "Phenelzine is taken by mouth and absorbed well. It reaches peak levels in about an hour and has a short blood half-life of about 12 hours, but because it permanently disables the enzyme, its effect lasts about two weeks after the last dose, until new MAO is made. The liver metabolizes it by oxidation and partly by acetylation, and metabolites leave in the urine. The two-week recovery period is why other serotonergic drugs and tyramine-rich foods must be avoided for about two weeks after stopping.", "sidefx": "Hypertensive crisis is the signature danger. Tyramine in aged cheese, cured meats, tap beer, soy sauce and similar foods normally gets broken down by gut and liver MAO-A; with phenelzine it reaches nerve endings and releases large amounts of stored norepinephrine, causing severe headache, very high blood pressure and possible stroke. Phentolamine or another fast vasodilator treats it. Combining it with SSRIs, SNRIs, meperidine, dextromethorphan, linezolid or tramadol can cause serotonin syndrome, because serotonin is both released more and broken down less. Orthostatic hypotension, weight gain, sexual dysfunction and sleep changes are common. Pyridoxine deficiency and, rarely, liver injury occur. Overdose effects can be delayed. It carries a boxed warning for suicidal thoughts in young people.", "fun": "The tyramine reaction with MAOIs is still called the cheese reaction, because aged cheese was the first food clearly linked to it.", "src": ["Nardil (phenelzine) FDA label", "Blackwell B. Hypertensive crisis due to monoamine-oxidase inhibitors. Lancet 1963", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology"]}, "phenobarbital": {"origin": "Phenobarbital is one of the oldest drugs still in regular use. Barbiturates are made from barbituric acid, first synthesized by Adolf von Baeyer in 1864. In 1903 Emil Fischer and Joseph von Mering introduced barbital (Veronal), the first barbiturate sleeping drug. In 1911 chemists at Bayer, including Heinrich Horlein, made phenobarbital, and it was sold as Luminal starting in 1912 as a sedative. That same year, the young German psychiatrist Alfred Hauptmann gave it to his epilepsy patients to help them sleep and noticed that their seizures decreased dramatically. It became the first truly effective anticonvulsant drug. It remains on the WHO list of essential medicines, is used for neonatal seizures, and in 2022 the FDA approved an IV phenobarbital product (Sezaby) specifically for neonatal seizures.", "timeline": [["1864", "Adolf von Baeyer synthesizes barbituric acid."], ["1903", "Fischer and von Mering introduce barbital, the first barbiturate drug."], ["1912", "Bayer markets phenobarbital as Luminal; Alfred Hauptmann reports its anticonvulsant effect."], ["2022", "FDA approves an IV phenobarbital product for neonatal seizures."]], "cell": "The GABA-A receptor is a chloride channel. Phenobarbital binds a barbiturate site on the receptor, separate from the GABA and benzodiazepine sites. At lower concentrations, it is a positive allosteric modulator that increases the duration of each channel opening when GABA binds; benzodiazepines, by contrast, increase how often it opens. More chloride enters the neuron, hyperpolarizing it and making it harder to fire. At higher concentrations, phenobarbital can open the channel directly even without GABA, which is why barbiturates have no ceiling on central nervous system depression and are more dangerous than benzodiazepines. It also blocks AMPA and kainate glutamate receptors, reducing excitation, and at high levels blocks voltage-gated calcium channels. Together these raise the seizure threshold and limit seizure spread.", "body": [["Brain", "Raises the seizure threshold and stops seizure spread; also causes sedation, which is the main limit on its use."], ["Breathing", "Depresses the brainstem respiratory centers, especially after IV loading or with other sedatives."], ["Heart and vessels", "At high levels it depresses heart contraction and relaxes vessels, causing low blood pressure."], ["Liver", "Strongly induces CYP enzymes such as CYP3A4 and CYP2C9, speeding the breakdown of many other drugs, including warfarin and oral contraceptives."], ["Behavior", "In children it can cause hyperactivity and irritability; in adults it can affect mood and concentration."], ["Bone", "Long-term use speeds vitamin D breakdown and can weaken bones."]], "journey": "Phenobarbital can be given IV, IM or by mouth and is absorbed well. It is less fat soluble than many sedatives, so after IV dosing it reaches its peak brain effect more slowly, over about 15 to 30 minutes. It is moderately protein bound. The liver metabolizes it with CYP2C9 and other enzymes, and about a quarter is excreted unchanged in the urine. Because it is a weak acid, alkalinizing the urine increases its excretion, which is used in overdose. The half-life is very long, often two to five days in adults and longer in newborns, so levels take weeks to stabilize and effects linger after stopping. It crosses the placenta.", "sidefx": "Sedation, drowsiness and slowed thinking come from enhanced GABA inhibition throughout the brain. Respiratory depression and low blood pressure are the dangerous effects at high levels, and they are worse with alcohol, opioids and benzodiazepines because barbiturates can directly open GABA-A channels without a ceiling. CYP enzyme induction causes many drug interactions. Tolerance and dependence develop, and sudden withdrawal can cause seizures. Rare serious rashes such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome occur. It can worsen acute porphyria because it induces enzymes that make heme precursors. There is no antidote; overdose is treated with airway support, multiple-dose activated charcoal, urinary alkalinization and, in severe cases, dialysis.", "fun": "Phenobarbital's anticonvulsant effect was discovered by accident when Alfred Hauptmann gave it to his epilepsy patients so they, and he, could get a better night's sleep.", "src": ["Phenobarbital sodium injection FDA labeling", "Sezaby (phenobarbital sodium) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "phenoxybenzamine": {"origin": "Phenoxybenzamine is a synthetic beta-haloalkylamine, a chemical relative of nitrogen mustards. In the 1940s, the pharmacologist Mark Nickerson, working with Louis Goodman at the University of Utah, studied dibenamine, a related compound, and showed that it blocked the effects of epinephrine in a long-lasting, irreversible way. That research helped establish the concept of alpha-adrenergic receptors. Phenoxybenzamine, a more practical oral relative, was developed by Smith, Kline and French and sold as Dibenzyline starting in the 1950s. It became the traditional drug used to prepare patients for surgery to remove a pheochromocytoma, a tumor that releases large amounts of catecholamines, because its long-lasting block protects against blood pressure surges during the operation.", "timeline": [["1940s", "Mark Nickerson and Louis Goodman show that dibenamine blocks adrenergic responses irreversibly."], ["1950s", "Phenoxybenzamine is introduced as Dibenzyline."]], "cell": "Phenoxybenzamine is a nonselective alpha-adrenergic antagonist that acts at both alpha-1 and alpha-2 receptors. Inside the body, it forms a highly reactive ethylenimonium (aziridinium) ion. This reactive intermediate binds covalently to the alpha receptor, permanently blocking it. Because the block is irreversible and noncompetitive, high levels of norepinephrine or epinephrine cannot overcome it, which is exactly why it protects against massive catecholamine release from a pheochromocytoma. Blocking alpha-1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle relaxes arteries and veins, lowering blood pressure. Blocking presynaptic alpha-2 receptors removes their brake on norepinephrine release, so more norepinephrine reaches cardiac beta-1 receptors, contributing to tachycardia. The effect lasts until the cell makes new receptors, which takes days.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Relaxes arteries and veins, lowering blood pressure and preventing surges from catecholamine-releasing tumors."], ["Heart", "Reflex activation and increased norepinephrine release from alpha-2 blockade speed the heart; a beta blocker is added only after alpha blockade is in place."], ["Body fluid volume", "Loosening chronically constricted vessels lets the circulation expand, so patients are given salt and fluids before surgery."], ["Nose", "Dilation of nasal blood vessels causes stuffiness."], ["Eyes", "Alpha-1 blockade of the iris dilator muscle causes small pupils."], ["Reproductive system", "Blocking alpha-1 receptors in the vas deferens and bladder neck can inhibit ejaculation."]], "journey": "Phenoxybenzamine is taken by mouth. Only part of a dose is absorbed. Its effect builds over hours as it covalently binds receptors and accumulates with repeated dosing over days. The blood half-life is about one day, but the effect lasts several days after stopping because new receptors must be made. It is fat soluble and enters many tissues. The liver metabolizes it, and metabolites leave in urine and bile.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come directly from irreversible alpha blockade. Orthostatic hypotension and dizziness happen because alpha-1 receptors are needed to constrict vessels on standing. Reflex tachycardia comes from the baroreceptor response and from more norepinephrine release after alpha-2 blockade. Nasal congestion, small pupils and inhibited ejaculation reflect alpha-1 blockade in the nose, iris and reproductive tract. After pheochromocytoma removal, the lasting block can cause prolonged low blood pressure. Because the block is irreversible, vasopressors that work through alpha receptors may be less effective, and vasopressin may be used. Fatigue is common. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Dibenzyline (phenoxybenzamine) FDA label", "Nickerson M, Goodman LS. Pharmacological properties of a new adrenergic blocking agent: N,N-dibenzyl-beta-chloroethylamine (Dibenamine). J Pharmacol Exp Ther 1947", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "phentolamine": {"origin": "Phentolamine is a synthetic imidazoline alpha blocker developed by the Swiss company Ciba in the late 1940s and early 1950s. It was sold as Regitine. Unlike phenoxybenzamine, its block was reversible and short, which made it useful for quick action. In the 1950s it was used as a diagnostic test for pheochromocytoma: an IV dose that caused a sharp fall in blood pressure suggested that the high pressure was driven by catecholamines. That test was later replaced by measuring catecholamine metabolites in blood and urine. Phentolamine's lasting roles are treating catecholamine-driven hypertensive crises and reversing tissue injury when vasopressors such as norepinephrine leak out of a vein. A dental formulation, OraVerse, was later approved to speed recovery from numbness after dental anesthesia.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Ciba introduces phentolamine (Regitine)."], ["1950s", "Phentolamine is used as a diagnostic test for pheochromocytoma."], ["2008", "FDA approves OraVerse to reverse soft-tissue numbness after dental anesthesia."]], "cell": "Phentolamine is a competitive, reversible antagonist at both alpha-1 and alpha-2 adrenergic receptors. At alpha-1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle, which are Gq coupled, norepinephrine normally activates phospholipase C, producing IP3 and calcium release that cause vasoconstriction. Phentolamine occupies the receptor without activating it, so arteries and veins relax and blood pressure falls. Because the block is competitive, it can be overcome by enough catecholamine, and it wears off within minutes to hours. Blocking presynaptic alpha-2 receptors removes the brake on norepinephrine release from sympathetic nerves, so more norepinephrine reaches cardiac beta-1 receptors, which increases heart rate and contractility. Injected into tissue around a vasopressor leak, it locally reverses alpha-1 vasoconstriction, restoring blood flow and limiting necrosis.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Relaxes arteries and veins, rapidly lowering blood pressure in catecholamine excess such as pheochromocytoma, MAOI-tyramine crises and cocaine toxicity."], ["Skin and tissue", "Injected around a norepinephrine or other vasopressor leak, it restores local blood flow and prevents tissue death."], ["Heart", "Reflex activation plus extra norepinephrine release from alpha-2 blockade speed the heart, which can cause angina or arrhythmias."], ["Nose", "Dilation of nasal vessels causes congestion."], ["Gut", "It can stimulate the gut, causing abdominal pain, nausea and diarrhea."], ["Mouth", "In dentistry, local vasodilation speeds removal of local anesthetic, shortening lip and tongue numbness."]], "journey": "Phentolamine is given IV, IM or by local injection; it is poorly absorbed by mouth. IV doses act within one to two minutes, and the effect lasts about 15 to 30 minutes. IM doses act within about 15 to 20 minutes and last somewhat longer. The half-life is short, about 20 minutes. It is metabolized extensively, mostly in the liver, and some is excreted in the urine unchanged. Local infiltration for extravasation acts right at the injection site.", "sidefx": "Low blood pressure and dizziness follow directly from alpha-1 blockade. Tachycardia, palpitations, angina and arrhythmias come from both the reflex response and increased norepinephrine release after alpha-2 blockade, which is why it is used cautiously in coronary disease. Heart attack and stroke from sudden hypotension have been reported. Nasal congestion and GI upset are common. Excess effect is treated with fluids and norepinephrine; epinephrine is avoided because, with alpha receptors blocked, its beta-2 effect can lower blood pressure further. There is no antidote, but its effect is short.", "src": ["Phentolamine mesylate for injection FDA label", "OraVerse (phentolamine mesylate) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "phenylephrine": {"origin": "Phenylephrine is a synthetic adrenergic agonist designed in the early 20th century as chemists explored relatives of epinephrine. It differs from epinephrine by lacking one hydroxyl group on the ring, which makes it selective for alpha-1 receptors and resistant to COMT breakdown. It was patented in the 1930s and marketed by Winthrop as Neo-Synephrine, first as a nasal decongestant and an injectable vasopressor. In anesthesia it became a common drug for low blood pressure caused by spinal and general anesthesia. Oral phenylephrine became the main over-the-counter decongestant after 2006, when pseudoephedrine was moved behind the pharmacy counter. In 2023 an FDA advisory committee concluded that oral phenylephrine is not effective as a decongestant, and in 2024 the FDA proposed removing it from oral cold medicines.", "timeline": [["1930s", "Phenylephrine is introduced as Neo-Synephrine."], ["2006", "Oral phenylephrine replaces many pseudoephedrine products after sales restrictions."], ["2023", "An FDA advisory committee concludes that oral phenylephrine is ineffective as a decongestant."], ["2024", "FDA proposes removing oral phenylephrine from nonprescription decongestants."]], "cell": "Phenylephrine is a direct agonist at alpha-1 adrenergic receptors with little effect at alpha-2 and essentially no beta activity. Alpha-1 receptors are Gq coupled. Activation turns on phospholipase C, which splits PIP2 into IP3 and DAG. IP3 releases calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, and calcium-calmodulin activates myosin light chain kinase, causing vascular smooth muscle to contract. Arterioles narrow, raising systemic vascular resistance and blood pressure, and veins tighten, increasing venous return. Because it lacks beta-1 activity, it does not directly increase heart rate or contractility. Instead, the rise in pressure activates the baroreceptor reflex, which increases vagal tone and often slows the heart. In the nose, alpha-1 activation constricts swollen blood vessels in the mucosa, reducing congestion. In the eye, it contracts the iris dilator muscle, dilating the pupil.", "body": [["Arteries and veins", "Constricts vessels, raising systemic vascular resistance and blood pressure, useful for hypotension during anesthesia."], ["Heart", "No direct stimulation; the baroreceptor reflex often slows the heart, and higher afterload can lower cardiac output in a weak heart."], ["Nose", "Topically, it shrinks swollen nasal blood vessels; overuse causes rebound congestion."], ["Eyes", "Contracts the iris dilator muscle, dilating the pupil for eye exams, without blocking focusing."], ["Kidneys and gut", "Vasoconstriction can reduce blood flow to the kidneys and gut at high levels."], ["Tissue at IV site", "Leakage out of the vein can cause local vasoconstriction and tissue injury."]], "journey": "IV phenylephrine acts almost immediately, and a single bolus lasts about 15 to 20 minutes. By mouth, very little reaches the bloodstream, because MAO and sulfation in the gut wall break most of it down before it is absorbed, which helps explain why oral decongestant doses are ineffective. It is metabolized by MAO and by sulfate conjugation, mainly in the liver and intestine, and the metabolites leave in the urine. It is not broken down by COMT. Topical nasal and eye forms act locally, though some is absorbed.", "sidefx": "Hypertension and headache come from excess alpha-1 vasoconstriction. Reflex bradycardia is a predictable baroreceptor response. Because it raises afterload without supporting the heart, it can reduce cardiac output in patients with heart failure. Strong vasoconstriction can reduce blood flow to the fingers, kidneys and gut. Extravasation can cause tissue necrosis, treated with phentolamine. Interactions with MAO inhibitors can greatly increase its effect. Topical nasal overuse causes rebound congestion. Severe hypertension can be treated with an alpha blocker such as phentolamine.", "src": ["Phenylephrine hydrochloride injection FDA label", "FDA Nonprescription Drugs Advisory Committee meeting on oral phenylephrine, 2023", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "phenytoin": {"origin": "Phenytoin was first made in 1908 by the German chemist Heinrich Biltz, but for decades no one knew it had any medical use. In the 1930s, Tracy Putnam and H. Houston Merritt at Boston City Hospital set out to find an anticonvulsant that did not cause the heavy sedation of phenobarbital. They built a device to trigger seizures in cats with electric shocks and tested hundreds of compounds supplied by the drug company Parke-Davis. In 1937 they found that diphenylhydantoin prevented seizures without much sedation. Parke-Davis began selling it as Dilantin in 1938. It was one of the first drugs found through systematic animal screening, and it showed that seizure control and sedation could be separated. Fosphenytoin, a water-soluble prodrug that is safer to infuse, was approved in 1996.", "timeline": [["1908", "Heinrich Biltz synthesizes phenytoin in Germany."], ["1937", "Putnam and Merritt discover its anticonvulsant effect using an electroshock seizure model."], ["1938", "Parke-Davis markets phenytoin as Dilantin."], ["1996", "FDA approves fosphenytoin, a water-soluble phenytoin prodrug."]], "cell": "Neurons fire action potentials through voltage-gated sodium channels, which open, then enter an inactivated state before they can reset. Phenytoin binds preferentially to sodium channels in the inactivated state and stabilizes them there, slowing their recovery. This effect is use dependent: neurons firing rapidly and repeatedly, as in a seizure, have more channels in the inactivated state, so they are blocked most. Normal, slower firing is affected less. The result is limitation of sustained high-frequency firing and of seizure spread from a focus to the rest of the brain. Phenytoin also blocks cardiac sodium channels, which is why it has class IB antiarrhythmic activity, and at high levels it affects calcium channels.", "body": [["Brain", "Prevents the spread of seizures, controlling focal and generalized tonic-clonic seizures, with relatively little sedation at therapeutic levels."], ["Cerebellum and eyes", "Rising levels cause nystagmus, then unsteady walking, slurred speech and confusion."], ["Gums", "Long-term use causes gingival hyperplasia, partly by stimulating fibroblasts."], ["Skin and hair", "Causes coarse facial features and excess hair growth; rarely serious rashes such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome."], ["Heart", "Rapid IV infusion can cause low blood pressure and arrhythmias, partly from the solvent propylene glycol."], ["Bone and blood", "Induces liver enzymes that break down vitamin D and folate, leading to bone loss and megaloblastic anemia."]], "journey": "Phenytoin is given by mouth or IV, but it is poorly soluble in water, so the IV solution is highly alkaline and contains propylene glycol. Oral absorption is slow and varies between products. It is about 90 percent bound to albumin, so low albumin or kidney failure raise the active free fraction even when total levels look normal. The liver metabolizes it mainly with CYP2C9 and also CYP2C19. These enzymes saturate within the usual therapeutic range, so the drug follows zero-order kinetics: small dose increases can cause large jumps in blood level, and the half-life lengthens as levels rise. It strongly induces CYP3A4 and other enzymes, causing many drug interactions. Metabolites leave in the urine.", "sidefx": "Nystagmus, ataxia and confusion reflect toxic levels affecting the cerebellum and brainstem. Gingival hyperplasia, hirsutism and coarse facial features occur with long-term use. Severe skin reactions are more likely in people of Asian ancestry with the HLA-B*1502 gene. Enzyme induction speeds breakdown of vitamin D, folate and other drugs, causing bone loss, anemia and interactions. IV infusion carries a boxed warning for hypotension and arrhythmias when given too fast, and the alkaline solution can damage tissue, causing purple glove syndrome. It is teratogenic, linked to fetal hydantoin syndrome. There is no antidote; toxicity is managed by stopping it, supportive care and, sometimes, multiple-dose activated charcoal.", "fun": "Phenytoin follows zero-order kinetics in the usual range, a favorite board fact, so its levels can rise sharply with a small dose change.", "src": ["Dilantin (phenytoin) FDA label", "Merritt HH, Putnam TJ. Sodium diphenyl hydantoinate in the treatment of convulsive disorders. JAMA 1938", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "physostigmine": {"origin": "Physostigmine comes from the Calabar bean, the seed of the West African vine Physostigma venenosum. In the Calabar region of what is now southeastern Nigeria, the bean was used as an ordeal poison: an accused person was made to swallow it, and those who vomited it up and survived were judged innocent. Scottish missionaries and physicians brought the bean to Edinburgh in the 1800s, where Robert Christison tasted it himself and nearly died, and Thomas Fraser studied its effects on the eye and body. In 1864 Julius Jobst and Oswald Hesse isolated the active alkaloid, physostigmine, also called eserine from the local name for the bean. It became one of the first drugs used for glaucoma in the 1870s. In 1935 the American chemist Percy Julian completed its total synthesis, a landmark achievement.", "timeline": [["1800s", "The Calabar bean, used as an ordeal poison in West Africa, reaches Scottish scientists."], ["1864", "Jobst and Hesse isolate physostigmine from the Calabar bean."], ["1870s", "Physostigmine is used to treat glaucoma."], ["1935", "Percy Julian and Josef Pikl complete the total synthesis of physostigmine."]], "cell": "Acetylcholinesterase in synapses rapidly breaks acetylcholine into choline and acetate. Physostigmine is a reversible carbamate inhibitor: it binds the enzyme's active site and carbamylates a serine residue, which takes minutes to regenerate instead of microseconds. While the enzyme is blocked, acetylcholine builds up at muscarinic and nicotinic synapses throughout the body. Unlike neostigmine, physostigmine is a tertiary amine with no permanent charge, so it crosses the blood-brain barrier. That is why it can reverse central anticholinergic delirium. In anticholinergic poisoning, drugs such as diphenhydramine, atropine or scopolamine block muscarinic receptors competitively. The extra acetylcholine produced by physostigmine outcompetes these blockers and restores muscarinic signaling in the brain and body.", "body": [["Brain", "Reverses anticholinergic delirium, agitation and hallucinations, often within minutes, because it crosses the blood-brain barrier."], ["Heart", "Increases acetylcholine at cardiac muscarinic receptors, slowing a fast heart rate; too much can cause bradycardia or asystole."], ["Eyes", "Constricts the pupils, reversing the dilated pupils of anticholinergic poisoning."], ["Gut and bladder", "Restores gut movement and bladder emptying, reversing ileus and urinary retention."], ["Skin and glands", "Restores sweating and secretions, helping the body cool and reversing dry, flushed skin."], ["Airways", "Excess acetylcholine can cause bronchospasm and increased secretions."]], "journey": "Physostigmine is given slowly IV, or sometimes IM. It acts within minutes and its effect peaks in about 15 minutes. It crosses the blood-brain barrier readily. Plasma and tissue cholinesterases break it down by hydrolysis, and very little is excreted unchanged in the urine. The half-life is short, under an hour, and its effects last about 30 to 60 minutes, often shorter than the anticholinergic poison, so delirium can return and repeat doses may be needed.", "sidefx": "Side effects come from too much acetylcholine: nausea, vomiting, drooling, sweating, diarrhea, urination and bronchospasm at muscarinic receptors, and muscle twitching and weakness at nicotinic receptors. Bradycardia and asystole are the most serious risks, especially in tricyclic antidepressant overdose, where conduction is already impaired; it is avoided when the ECG shows QRS or PR prolongation. Seizures can occur, particularly with fast injection. Atropine reverses the muscarinic effects of excess physostigmine and is kept at the bedside. The patient is monitored on a cardiac monitor during and after dosing.", "fun": "The Calabar bean was used in trials by ordeal, and Edinburgh scientists who tested it on themselves helped found the study of how drugs act on the nervous system.", "src": ["Physostigmine salicylate injection labeling", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Julian PL, Pikl J. Studies in the indole series V: the complete synthesis of physostigmine (eserine). J Am Chem Soc 1935"]}, "phytonadione": {"origin": "Phytonadione is vitamin K1, the form of vitamin K made by green plants. In 1929 the Danish biochemist Henrik Dam noticed that chicks fed a fat-free diet developed bleeding. He found that a fat-soluble factor in the diet prevented it and named it vitamin K, for Koagulation in German and Danish. In 1939 the American biochemist Edward Doisy at Saint Louis University isolated and determined the structure of vitamin K1 from alfalfa, and it was synthesized soon after. Dam and Doisy shared the 1943 Nobel Prize. Around the same time, Karl Paul Link at the University of Wisconsin discovered that spoiled sweet clover caused fatal bleeding in cattle because of dicoumarol, a vitamin K antagonist, which led to warfarin. Vitamin K became the antidote to warfarin. Giving vitamin K to every newborn has been recommended in the US since 1961 to prevent bleeding in infants.", "timeline": [["1929", "Henrik Dam discovers vitamin K by studying bleeding chicks."], ["1939", "Edward Doisy isolates and determines the structure of vitamin K1."], ["1943", "Dam and Doisy share the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."], ["1961", "The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends vitamin K for all newborns."]], "cell": "The liver makes clotting factors II, VII, IX and X, plus the anticoagulant proteins C and S, in an inactive form. To work, these proteins need gamma-carboxylation of several glutamic acid residues, which lets them bind calcium and attach to phospholipid surfaces on platelets. The enzyme gamma-glutamyl carboxylase performs this step, using reduced vitamin K (vitamin K hydroquinone) as a cofactor. In the reaction, vitamin K is oxidized to vitamin K epoxide. The enzyme vitamin K epoxide reductase (VKORC1) then recycles it back to the reduced form. Warfarin blocks VKORC1, so reduced vitamin K runs out and the liver releases nonfunctional clotting factors. Giving phytonadione supplies new vitamin K, which can be reduced by a separate warfarin-insensitive pathway, so carboxylation resumes and the liver produces working clotting factors. Because new factors must be made, the effect takes hours.", "body": [["Liver", "Allows the liver to make working clotting factors II, VII, IX and X and proteins C and S, correcting vitamin K deficiency or warfarin effect."], ["Blood clotting", "INR begins to fall within hours, with full correction over about a day; for life-threatening bleeding it is combined with faster products such as prothrombin complex concentrate."], ["Newborns", "Newborns have low vitamin K stores and sterile guts, so a dose at birth prevents vitamin K deficiency bleeding, which can cause brain hemorrhage."], ["Bone", "Vitamin K also activates bone proteins such as osteocalcin."]], "journey": "Phytonadione can be given by mouth, IV, IM or under the skin. Oral absorption needs bile salts and fat, so it is reduced in biliary obstruction and malabsorption. Oral doses begin to lower INR in about 6 to 10 hours, and IV doses in 1 to 2 hours, with full effect over 12 to 24 hours or more. Subcutaneous absorption is unpredictable and is no longer preferred. It is concentrated in the liver, metabolized there to more water-soluble forms and excreted in bile and urine. It crosses the placenta poorly, which is part of why newborns have low stores.", "sidefx": "Oral vitamin K is very safe. The main danger is with IV administration: severe hypersensitivity or anaphylactoid reactions, including shock and cardiac arrest, which are thought to be caused partly by the solvent used to dissolve the fat-soluble vitamin. This is the reason for its boxed warning and for giving it as a slow, diluted infusion. IM injection can cause hematomas in patients with severe coagulopathy. Too much vitamin K given to a patient on warfarin can make it difficult to restore anticoagulation for days. Its onset is slow because the liver must make new clotting factors, so it is not used alone for immediate reversal of severe bleeding.", "fun": "The letter K stands for Koagulation, the Danish and German word for coagulation, from Henrik Dam's original report.", "src": ["Phytonadione injectable emulsion FDA label", "American Academy of Pediatrics policy statement on vitamin K for newborns", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "pilocarpine": {"origin": "Pilocarpine comes from the leaves of jaborandi, Pilocarpus shrubs native to Brazil. Indigenous peoples in Brazil chewed the leaves, which made them sweat and salivate heavily. In the 1870s the Brazilian physician Symphronio Coutinho brought jaborandi leaves to Europe, where doctors were impressed by their power to cause sweating. In 1875 the active alkaloid, pilocarpine, was isolated, independently by Edmund Hardy in France and A.W. Gerrard in England. Within a couple of years, the German ophthalmologist Adolf Weber showed that it constricted the pupil and lowered eye pressure, and it became a mainstay of glaucoma treatment for more than a century. Oral pilocarpine (Salagen) was approved in 1994 for dry mouth after radiation therapy, and in 2021 eye drops (Vuity) were approved for presbyopia, the loss of near vision with age.", "timeline": [["1875", "Pilocarpine is isolated by Hardy in France and Gerrard in England."], ["1870s", "Pilocarpine is used to lower eye pressure in glaucoma."], ["1994", "FDA approves oral pilocarpine (Salagen) for dry mouth after head and neck radiation."], ["2021", "FDA approves pilocarpine eye drops (Vuity) for presbyopia."]], "cell": "Pilocarpine is a direct agonist at muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, mainly M3. M3 receptors are Gq coupled: activation turns on phospholipase C, producing IP3 and DAG, which raise intracellular calcium. In the eye, calcium contracts the iris sphincter muscle, constricting the pupil (miosis), and contracts the ciliary muscle. Ciliary contraction pulls on the scleral spur, opening the trabecular meshwork and increasing outflow of aqueous humor, which lowers intraocular pressure. It also shifts focus to near objects (accommodation), the basis of its use in presbyopia. In salivary and sweat glands, M3 activation increases calcium and fluid secretion, producing saliva and sweat. Sweat glands use sympathetic cholinergic nerves with muscarinic receptors, which is why pilocarpine causes so much sweating. As a tertiary amine, it can enter the brain.", "body": [["Eyes", "Constricts the pupil and contracts the ciliary muscle, opening drainage channels and lowering eye pressure, and improves near focus."], ["Salivary glands", "Stimulates saliva production, relieving dry mouth in Sjogren syndrome and after radiation to the head and neck."], ["Sweat glands", "Strongly increases sweating, the most common side effect of the oral form, and the basis of the sweat chloride test for cystic fibrosis."], ["Heart", "Can slow the heart rate through muscarinic receptors."], ["Airways", "Can constrict bronchi and increase secretions, worsening asthma or COPD."], ["Gut and bladder", "Increases gut movement and bladder contraction, causing nausea, diarrhea and frequent urination."]], "journey": "Pilocarpine is used as eye drops or taken by mouth. Eye drops act within about 10 to 30 minutes and last several hours; pressing on the inner corner of the eye reduces absorption into the body. Oral tablets are absorbed quickly, and saliva increases within about 20 minutes, with effects lasting a few hours. A high-fat meal slows absorption. It is inactivated in plasma and at synapses and partly metabolized by the liver enzyme CYP2A6. It leaves mainly in the urine. The half-life is about one hour.", "sidefx": "Side effects are predictable muscarinic actions beyond the target. Sweating, flushing, runny nose, nausea, diarrhea and frequent urination come from M3 stimulation of glands and smooth muscle. Slow heart rate and bronchospasm are concerns in heart disease and asthma. In the eye, ciliary spasm causes brow ache and blurred distance vision, and small pupils make night vision poor, so driving at night is risky. Rarely, the pull on the retina can contribute to retinal detachment, especially in people with nearsightedness. Severe overdose causes a cholinergic toxidrome, which is treated with atropine, a muscarinic antagonist.", "fun": "The sweat test for cystic fibrosis uses pilocarpine delivered into the skin by a small electric current to make the skin sweat.", "src": ["Salagen (pilocarpine) FDA label", "Vuity (pilocarpine ophthalmic solution) FDA label", "Holmstedt B, Wassen SH, Schultes RE. Jaborandi: an interdisciplinary appraisal. J Ethnopharmacol 1979"]}, "pioglitazone": {"origin": "Pioglitazone is a thiazolidinedione (TZD), a class discovered by the Japanese company Takeda. In the late 1970s and early 1980s, Takeda scientists found that ciglitazone, a molecule studied for lipid effects, lowered blood sugar in insulin-resistant animals. Further chemistry produced pioglitazone. In the mid 1990s, researchers showed that TZDs work by activating PPAR-gamma, a nuclear receptor that controls genes in fat cells. The FDA approved pioglitazone as Actos in 1999, the same year as rosiglitazone. Troglitazone, the first TZD, was withdrawn in 2000 for severe liver failure, and rosiglitazone was restricted for a time over heart attack concerns. Pioglitazone remained in use, though label warnings were added for heart failure in 2007 and for bladder cancer risk in 2011.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Takeda scientists develop thiazolidinediones, leading to pioglitazone."], ["1999", "FDA approves pioglitazone (Actos)."], ["2000", "Troglitazone, the first TZD, is withdrawn for liver toxicity."], ["2007", "FDA adds a boxed warning on heart failure for TZDs."], ["2011", "FDA adds bladder cancer risk to the pioglitazone label."]], "cell": "Pioglitazone is an agonist of peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma (PPAR-gamma), a nuclear receptor found mainly in fat tissue and also in muscle, liver and other cells. When pioglitazone binds, PPAR-gamma pairs with the retinoid X receptor (RXR). This complex binds specific DNA sequences and changes the transcription of many genes. It promotes the formation of new, small, insulin-sensitive fat cells, increases fat storage in subcutaneous fat rather than in the liver and muscle, and raises production of adiponectin, a hormone that improves insulin sensitivity. It also increases GLUT4 glucose transporters and reduces inflammatory signals from fat. As a result, muscle takes up more glucose and the liver makes less glucose. It also weakly activates PPAR-alpha, which helps lower triglycerides. Because it works through gene transcription, its full effect takes weeks.", "body": [["Fat tissue", "Promotes new insulin-sensitive fat cells and shifts fat away from the liver and muscle, which improves insulin action but causes weight gain."], ["Muscle", "Increases glucose uptake by improving insulin sensitivity."], ["Liver", "Reduces glucose production and liver fat, which can help nonalcoholic fatty liver disease."], ["Kidneys and fluid", "Increases sodium and water reabsorption in the collecting duct, causing edema and possibly worsening heart failure."], ["Bone", "Shifts bone marrow stem cells toward fat cells and away from bone-forming cells, increasing fracture risk, especially in women."], ["Bladder", "Long-term use has been associated with a small increase in bladder cancer risk."]], "journey": "Pioglitazone is taken by mouth once a day and is absorbed well, with peak levels in about two hours. It is more than 99 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it mainly with CYP2C8 and partly with CYP3A4, forming several active metabolites with long half-lives of about 16 to 24 hours. Gemfibrozil, a strong CYP2C8 inhibitor, raises its levels. It is excreted mainly in bile and feces. Because it changes gene expression, blood sugar falls gradually over several weeks, with full effect taking a few months.", "sidefx": "Weight gain comes from both new fat storage and fluid retention. Edema and worsening heart failure happen because PPAR-gamma activation in the kidney collecting duct increases sodium reabsorption through ENaC channels; this is the basis of its boxed warning, and it is avoided in symptomatic heart failure. Fractures come from effects on bone marrow stem cells. Bladder cancer risk has been reported with long-term use. Macular edema has been reported. Used alone, it rarely causes low blood sugar because it does not increase insulin secretion. Liver enzymes are checked, because troglitazone caused severe liver failure, though pioglitazone has not shown the same pattern. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Actos (pioglitazone) FDA label", "Lehmann JM et al. An antidiabetic thiazolidinedione is a high affinity ligand for PPAR-gamma. J Biol Chem 1995", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "piperacillin-tazobactam": {"origin": "Piperacillin-tazobactam pairs two synthetic molecules. Piperacillin is an extended-spectrum penicillin of the ureidopenicillin group, developed in the 1970s by the Japanese company Toyama Chemical. Its side chain lets it penetrate gram-negative bacteria and reach Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Bacteria fought back with beta-lactamases, enzymes that break the beta-lactam ring. The solution was to add a beta-lactamase inhibitor. Clavulanic acid, from a Streptomyces bacterium, had been discovered at Beecham in the 1970s, and sulbactam followed. Tazobactam, a sulbactam relative with stronger inhibition, was developed by Taiho Pharmaceutical in Japan in the early 1980s. The FDA approved the combination as Zosyn in 1993. It became one of the most widely used hospital antibiotics for sepsis, pneumonia and intra-abdominal infections.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Toyama Chemical develops piperacillin."], ["1980s", "Taiho Pharmaceutical develops tazobactam, a beta-lactamase inhibitor."], ["1993", "FDA approves piperacillin-tazobactam (Zosyn)."]], "cell": "Piperacillin is a beta-lactam antibiotic. Its beta-lactam ring mimics the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of peptidoglycan precursors and binds penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), especially PBP3 in gram-negative bacteria, forming a covalent bond with an active-site serine. This blocks transpeptidase cross-linking of the cell wall. The weakened wall breaks down with the help of autolysins, and the bacterium bursts. Its polar side chain helps it pass through porin channels in the gram-negative outer membrane, giving it activity against Pseudomonas. Tazobactam has little antibacterial activity of its own. It is a penicillanic acid sulfone that acts as a suicide inhibitor of many class A beta-lactamases: the enzyme attacks tazobactam, which then forms a stable, inactive complex, so the enzyme cannot destroy piperacillin. It does not inhibit AmpC well in many organisms, carbapenemases or metallo-beta-lactamases.", "body": [["Infection sites", "Kills a broad range of gram-positive, gram-negative and anaerobic bacteria, including Pseudomonas, in pneumonia, sepsis, intra-abdominal and diabetic foot infections."], ["Kidneys", "Mostly cleared by the kidneys; combined with vancomycin, it is associated with higher rates of acute kidney injury."], ["Gut", "Kills normal gut bacteria, causing diarrhea and raising the risk of C. difficile infection."], ["Blood", "Prolonged courses can cause low platelets, low white cells and, rarely, bleeding from platelet dysfunction."], ["Immune system", "Can cause allergic reactions from rash to anaphylaxis, like other penicillins."], ["Brain", "Very high levels, especially in kidney failure, can cause seizures."]], "journey": "Piperacillin-tazobactam is given only IV, either as an intermittent infusion over about 30 minutes or as an extended infusion, which keeps levels above the bacteria's minimum inhibitory concentration longer because beta-lactam killing is time dependent. Both drugs spread into most tissues, including lung, bile and abdominal tissue, and reach the cerebrospinal fluid mainly when the meninges are inflamed. They are not metabolized much. Both are cleared mainly by the kidneys through filtration and tubular secretion, so doses are adjusted in kidney impairment. The half-life of each is about one hour.", "sidefx": "Allergic reactions occur because the opened beta-lactam ring binds host proteins and forms antigens. Diarrhea and C. difficile infection come from disruption of normal gut bacteria. Acute kidney injury is more frequent when it is combined with vancomycin, though the cause of this association is still debated. Low platelets and white cells and platelet dysfunction occur with longer courses. Seizures can occur with very high levels, because beta-lactams can block GABA-A receptors in the brain, especially when kidney function is poor. Low potassium can occur from sodium delivery to the kidney. There is no antidote; anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine.", "src": ["Zosyn (piperacillin and tazobactam) FDA label", "Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "polyethylene-glycol": {"origin": "Polyethylene glycol (PEG) is a fully synthetic polymer made by linking many ethylene oxide units into long chains. Industry used it for decades in cosmetics, ointments and as a drug carrier because it is inert and barely absorbed. In 1980, gastroenterologists led by John Fordtran in Dallas described a balanced electrolyte lavage solution built around PEG that could clean the colon before colonoscopy without the big fluid and salt shifts caused by older preps. That solution became GoLYTELY from Braintree Laboratories. Later, PEG 3350 powder without electrolytes was approved as a prescription laxative (MiraLAX) and was switched to over-the-counter sale in 2006. It is now one of the most widely used treatments for constipation in children and adults.", "timeline": [["1980", "Davis, Fordtran and colleagues publish a PEG-electrolyte lavage solution for bowel cleansing."], ["1980s", "PEG-electrolyte bowel prep (GoLYTELY) enters US practice."], ["1999", "PEG 3350 powder is approved as a prescription laxative (MiraLAX)."], ["2006", "MiraLAX becomes available over the counter."]], "cell": "PEG does not act on a receptor or enzyme. It is a large, water-loving polymer that the gut cannot digest or absorb, and colonic bacteria cannot ferment it. Because the molecules stay inside the bowel lumen, they hold water there by osmosis. Each PEG chain binds water through hydrogen bonds, so water that would normally be pulled back into the body stays in the stool. More water means softer, larger stools, which stretch the bowel wall and trigger normal peristalsis. In bowel-prep solutions, added sodium, potassium, bicarbonate and sulfate are balanced so that almost no net salt or water is gained or lost across the gut wall.", "body": [["Colon", "Water held in the lumen softens and enlarges the stool, which makes it easier to pass and triggers more regular bowel movements over one to three days."], ["Fluid and electrolytes", "Because PEG is not absorbed and bowel-prep versions are salt balanced, large volumes cause little change in blood sodium or potassium compared with older saline laxatives."], ["Gut comfort", "Extra fluid and gas in the bowel can cause bloating, cramping and nausea, especially when large prep volumes are drunk quickly."], ["Bowel prep", "In large volumes it washes the colon clear so the lining can be seen well during colonoscopy."]], "journey": "PEG is taken by mouth dissolved in a drink. Almost none of it is absorbed, so it does not travel through the bloodstream, reach the brain or cross the placenta in any meaningful amount. It is not metabolized by the liver or by gut bacteria. It simply passes through the intestines and leaves in the stool. As a daily laxative, the effect usually builds over one to three days. As a bowel prep, it causes watery stools within hours.", "sidefx": "Nearly all side effects come from water and gas inside the bowel: bloating, cramping, fullness and nausea. Too much effect causes diarrhea, and with heavy diarrhea a person can still lose fluid and electrolytes, which matters most in the frail and elderly. Large-volume preps can cause vomiting and, rarely, aspiration in people with weak swallowing. It should not be used when bowel obstruction or perforation is suspected, because adding fluid to a blocked bowel raises pressure. There is no antidote; stopping the drug ends the effect.", "fun": "The 3350 in PEG 3350 is the average molecular weight of the polymer chains, which is why the same chemical family includes other grades like PEG 400 used in ointments.", "src": ["MiraLAX (polyethylene glycol 3350) labeling", "GoLYTELY FDA label", "Davis GR et al. Gastroenterology 1980"]}, "potassium-chloride": {"origin": "Potassium was first isolated as an element in 1807 by the English chemist Humphry Davy, who passed electric current through molten potash and saw shiny metal beads appear. Its key role in the body became clear in the 20th century, when physiologists showed that the balance of potassium inside and outside cells sets the resting voltage of nerves and muscles, and that both low and high blood potassium cause dangerous heart rhythms. As thiazide and loop diuretics spread in the late 1950s and 1960s, many patients lost potassium in their urine, so oral potassium chloride became one of the most prescribed supplements. Early enteric-coated tablets released potassium all at once and caused small bowel ulcers, which led to wax-matrix and microencapsulated forms that release it slowly.", "timeline": [["1807", "Humphry Davy isolates potassium metal by electrolysis of potash."], ["1950s", "Diuretic therapy makes potassium replacement a routine need."], ["1960s", "Small bowel ulcers from enteric-coated potassium tablets are reported, prompting slow-release forms."]], "cell": "Potassium is the main positive ion inside cells. The sodium-potassium ATPase pumps potassium in and sodium out, building a high potassium level inside the cell. Potassium then leaks out through resting potassium channels, and that outward leak creates the negative resting membrane potential. When blood potassium is low, the gradient becomes steeper, the membrane becomes hyperpolarized and repolarization of the heart slows, which raises the risk of ectopic beats and arrhythmias and causes weakness and ileus. Replacing potassium chloride restores the normal gradient. The chloride matters too: in metabolic alkalosis with chloride loss (vomiting, diuretics), the kidney needs chloride to stop wasting potassium and bicarbonate.", "body": [["Heart", "Normal potassium stabilizes repolarization. Low levels cause U waves, flattened T waves and ectopy; correcting them lowers arrhythmia risk, especially in people on digoxin."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Restoring the gradient relieves weakness, cramps and, in severe cases, paralysis or rhabdomyolysis linked to low potassium."], ["Gut", "Smooth muscle of the bowel moves more normally, which helps ileus caused by hypokalemia. Concentrated tablets can irritate the stomach or small bowel lining."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys clear most extra potassium, so kidney disease sharply raises the risk of high levels. Chloride helps correct alkalosis that keeps the kidney wasting potassium."], ["Veins", "Concentrated IV potassium irritates veins, causing burning and phlebitis, and can damage tissue if it leaks out."]], "journey": "By mouth, potassium chloride is absorbed well in the small intestine; slow-release tablets and capsules spread absorption over several hours to protect the gut lining. IV potassium enters the blood at once. From the blood, most of it is pushed into cells by the sodium-potassium pump, helped by insulin and beta-2 stimulation, so blood levels show only a small part of total body potassium. It is not metabolized. The kidneys remove most of it in urine, with a small amount lost in stool. Because cells absorb it gradually, blood levels need to be rechecked after replacement.", "sidefx": "Too much potassium, or potassium given too fast, raises blood levels faster than cells can absorb it. High potassium makes cardiac cells less excitable: peaked T waves, widened QRS, slow rhythms and, at worst, cardiac arrest. This is why IV potassium is never pushed fast. Solid tablets can cause GI ulcers or bleeding where they sit against the gut wall, so they are taken with food and water. IV potassium stings veins. Treatment of dangerous high potassium uses IV calcium to stabilize the heart, insulin with glucose and beta-2 agonists to shift potassium into cells, and binders, diuretics or dialysis to remove it.", "fun": "The symbol K comes from kalium, the Latin name derived from the Arabic word for plant ashes, because potash came from burned wood.", "src": ["Potassium chloride FDA labels", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "pralidoxime": {"origin": "Organophosphate insecticides and military nerve agents were developed in Germany in the 1930s and 1940s, and they kill by locking up acetylcholinesterase. Atropine could block the extra acetylcholine at muscarinic sites, but it did nothing for paralyzed breathing muscles. In the early 1950s, Irwin Wilson and colleagues at Columbia University studied how the phosphorus group sits on the enzyme and reasoned that a strong nucleophile placed in the right spot could pull it off. In 1955, Wilson and Sarah Ginsburg reported pyridine-2-aldoxime methiodide (2-PAM), and British researchers working on nerve gas defense reached the same compound about the same time. 2-PAM became the standard oxime antidote in the United States and is paired with atropine in military and EMS autoinjectors.", "timeline": [["1930s", "German chemists develop organophosphate compounds, including the first nerve agents."], ["1955", "Wilson and Ginsburg report 2-PAM as an acetylcholinesterase reactivator."], ["2000s", "Atropine plus pralidoxime autoinjectors (such as DuoDote) are used for nerve agent and organophosphate poisoning."]], "cell": "Organophosphates bind the serine at the active site of acetylcholinesterase and attach a phosphate group to it. The enzyme can no longer break down acetylcholine, which builds up at muscarinic and nicotinic synapses. Pralidoxime has a positively charged pyridinium ring that docks at the enzyme's anionic site, which lines up its oxime group right next to the phosphorus. The oxime attacks the phosphorus, pulls the organophosphate off the serine and frees the enzyme to work again. This only works before aging, when the bound phosphate loses an alkyl group and the bond becomes permanent. Some nerve agents, such as soman, age within minutes, so oximes must be given early.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Reactivated enzyme clears acetylcholine from the neuromuscular junction, relieving fasciculations, weakness and paralysis. This nicotinic effect is the main reason to give it."], ["Breathing", "Restoring strength to the diaphragm and chest muscles supports ventilation, which is the main cause of death in poisoning."], ["Muscarinic organs", "Some reversal of secretions and bradycardia occurs, but atropine is still needed to block muscarinic effects such as bronchorrhea and wheezing."], ["Brain", "As a charged quaternary compound it crosses the blood-brain barrier poorly, so it does little for seizures or central effects; benzodiazepines treat seizures."], ["Heart and vessels", "Rapid IV doses can raise blood pressure and heart rate."]], "journey": "Pralidoxime is given IM by autoinjector or IV. Absorption from muscle is fast, with effects within minutes. It stays mostly in the blood and extracellular fluid and does not cross into the brain well because of its permanent positive charge. It has little liver metabolism and is cleared mainly unchanged by the kidneys, with a short half-life of a few hours. Because organophosphates can linger in fat and keep re-poisoning the enzyme, repeated doses or an infusion are often needed.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are mild: blurred vision, dizziness, headache and nausea, which can be hard to separate from the poisoning. Giving it too fast IV can cause high blood pressure, fast heart rate, muscle rigidity, laryngospasm and even cardiac arrest, likely from sudden shifts at the neuromuscular junction. At high levels oximes can themselves weakly inhibit acetylcholinesterase. Kidney failure raises levels. It is not useful once the enzyme has aged and is generally not used for carbamate poisoning, where the enzyme recovers by itself.", "fun": "The 2 in 2-PAM refers to the oxime sitting on carbon 2 of the pyridine ring, the exact spot that lets it reach the bound phosphorus.", "src": ["Protopam (pralidoxime chloride) FDA label", "DuoDote FDA label", "Wilson IB, Ginsburg S. Biochim Biophys Acta 1955", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]}, "pramipexole": {"origin": "Pramipexole was developed by the German company Boehringer Ingelheim as a non-ergot dopamine agonist. Older dopamine agonists such as bromocriptine and pergolide were based on ergot alkaloids from a fungus, and they carried risks of fibrosis of the lungs and heart valves. Chemists designed pramipexole as a small synthetic aminobenzothiazole that acts directly on dopamine receptors without the ergot structure. The FDA approved it as Mirapex for Parkinson disease in 1997, and in 2006 it was approved for restless legs syndrome. It gave patients a way to delay or reduce levodopa use in early disease.", "timeline": [["1997", "FDA approves pramipexole (Mirapex) for Parkinson disease."], ["2006", "FDA approves pramipexole for moderate to severe restless legs syndrome."], ["2010", "Once-daily extended-release form (Mirapex ER) is approved."]], "cell": "In Parkinson disease, dopamine neurons from the substantia nigra die, so striatal neurons lack dopamine signals. Pramipexole skips the dying neurons and directly stimulates postsynaptic dopamine receptors of the D2 family, with highest affinity for D3, then D2. These are Gi-coupled receptors: activation lowers cAMP and opens potassium channels in striatal neurons of the indirect pathway, which reduces the indirect pathway's braking effect on movement. The net result is less inhibition of the thalamus and cortex and easier movement. Unlike levodopa it needs no enzyme conversion, so it works even when few dopamine neurons remain. D3 receptors are dense in limbic areas, which is linked to reward-related side effects.", "body": [["Brain motor circuits", "Stimulating striatal D2 receptors reduces slowness, rigidity and tremor in Parkinson disease and eases the urge to move in restless legs syndrome."], ["Brain reward circuits", "D3 stimulation in limbic areas can drive compulsive gambling, shopping, eating or hypersexuality."], ["Sleep and alertness", "It can cause sleepiness and sudden sleep attacks during daily activities, including driving."], ["Blood vessels", "Dopamine agonism lowers sympathetic tone and can cause orthostatic hypotension, especially when starting."], ["Gut", "Stimulation of the chemoreceptor trigger zone, which lies outside the blood-brain barrier, causes nausea."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth, pramipexole is absorbed almost completely and reaches peak levels in about two hours. It crosses the blood-brain barrier easily. It is barely metabolized and has low protein binding. The kidneys clear most of it unchanged in urine, so kidney disease raises levels and needs dose adjustment. Its half-life is roughly eight to twelve hours, longer in older adults. The extended-release form allows once-daily use.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow dopamine receptors in places other than the motor circuit. Nausea comes from the chemoreceptor trigger zone. Orthostatic hypotension comes from reduced sympathetic outflow. Hallucinations and confusion come from dopamine stimulation in cortical and limbic areas, especially in older patients. Impulse control disorders come from D3 activity in reward pathways. Sleep attacks may happen without warning. Stopping suddenly can cause a withdrawal syndrome or a neuroleptic malignant-like state. In restless legs syndrome, long use can lead to augmentation, where symptoms start earlier and spread. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Mirapex (pramipexole) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "prasugrel": {"origin": "Prasugrel is a third-generation thienopyridine, a family that began with ticlopidine and continued with clopidogrel. Clopidogrel had a weakness: it needed two steps of liver activation, much of it by CYP2C19, so many patients with common gene variants got weak platelet blockade. Japanese companies Daiichi Sankyo and Ube Industries discovered prasugrel, and Daiichi Sankyo developed it with Eli Lilly. Its structure lets intestinal esterases do the first step quickly, so more active drug forms faster and more reliably. The TRITON-TIMI 38 trial, published in 2007, showed fewer heart attacks and stent clots than with clopidogrel in acute coronary syndrome treated with PCI, but more major bleeding. The FDA approved it as Effient in 2009 with a boxed warning about bleeding.", "timeline": [["2007", "TRITON-TIMI 38 trial compares prasugrel with clopidogrel in ACS patients undergoing PCI."], ["2009", "FDA approves prasugrel (Effient) with a boxed bleeding warning."]], "cell": "Prasugrel is a prodrug. Esterases in the gut quickly turn it into a thiolactone, and liver CYP enzymes (mainly CYP3A4 and CYP2B6) then convert it to the active metabolite. The active form has a reactive thiol that forms a permanent disulfide bond with the P2Y12 ADP receptor on platelets. P2Y12 normally couples to Gi, lowers cAMP and amplifies GP IIb/IIIa activation when ADP is released. With P2Y12 locked off, cAMP stays higher, GP IIb/IIIa activation is blunted and platelets aggregate much less. Because platelets have no nucleus and cannot make new receptors, the effect lasts for the platelet's life of about 7 to 10 days.", "body": [["Platelets", "Strong, consistent, irreversible blocking of ADP-driven aggregation reduces clot formation on ruptured plaques and inside new coronary stents."], ["Coronary arteries", "Fewer platelet clots means fewer heart attacks and stent thromboses after PCI for acute coronary syndrome."], ["Brain", "The same strong antiplatelet effect raises the risk of bleeding into the brain, which is why it is contraindicated after prior stroke or TIA."], ["Gut", "The GI tract is a common bleeding site, especially with aspirin, NSAIDs or anticoagulants."]], "journey": "Prasugrel is taken by mouth and absorbed quickly. It is rapidly converted by gut esterases and then by liver CYP enzymes into its active metabolite, with platelet inhibition beginning within about 30 minutes after a loading dose. The active metabolite is highly protein bound and has a half-life of about seven hours, but this does not matter much because the receptor is blocked permanently. Inactive metabolites leave mostly in urine and some in feces. Platelet function returns over about a week as new platelets are made, which is why it is stopped about seven days before surgery.", "sidefx": "Bleeding is the main risk because platelet function is blocked for the life of each platelet. Bleeding risk is highest in people 75 and older, those with low body weight, those with prior stroke or TIA, and those needing urgent CABG. There is no specific reversal agent; platelet transfusion can help, though transfused platelets can be blocked by drug still in the blood soon after a dose. A rare but serious reaction is thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP).", "src": ["Effient (prasugrel) FDA label", "Wiviott SD et al. NEJM 2007 (TRITON-TIMI 38)"]}, "pravastatin": {"origin": "The statin story starts with fungi. In the early 1970s, Akira Endo at Sankyo in Japan screened thousands of molds, reasoning that fungi might make chemicals that block cholesterol synthesis to fight competing microbes. In 1973 he found compactin (mevastatin) in Penicillium citrinum. Compactin was never marketed, but Sankyo found that a microbe, Streptomyces carbophilus, could add a hydroxyl group to it, creating pravastatin. The extra hydroxyl made it more water soluble and more selective for the liver. Pravastatin was approved in Japan in 1989 and in the US in 1991, marketed by Bristol-Myers Squibb as Pravachol. In 1995 the WOSCOPS trial showed it prevented heart attacks in men with high cholesterol but no prior heart disease.", "timeline": [["1973", "Akira Endo discovers compactin in Penicillium citrinum at Sankyo."], ["1989", "Pravastatin is approved in Japan."], ["1991", "FDA approves pravastatin (Pravachol)."], ["1995", "WOSCOPS trial shows primary prevention benefit."]], "cell": "Pravastatin competitively inhibits HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting enzyme that converts HMG-CoA to mevalonate in the cholesterol synthesis pathway. Its structure mimics the HMG-CoA intermediate, so it sits in the enzyme's active site. With less cholesterol being made inside liver cells, the cells sense low sterol levels and activate the transcription factor SREBP-2. SREBP-2 turns on the gene for the LDL receptor, so the liver puts more LDL receptors on its surface and pulls more LDL out of the blood. Pravastatin is hydrophilic and is taken into liver cells by the OATP1B1 transporter, which focuses its effect on the liver and limits entry into muscle cells.", "body": [["Blood lipids", "More liver LDL receptors clear LDL from the blood. As a moderate-intensity statin it lowers LDL less than high-intensity statins, and modestly lowers triglycerides."], ["Arteries", "Lower LDL and anti-inflammatory effects slow plaque growth and stabilize plaques, reducing heart attacks and strokes."], ["Liver", "The liver is the main target. Mild rises in liver enzymes can occur; serious liver injury is rare."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Muscle aches can occur, and rarely myopathy or rhabdomyolysis, though risk is lower because little drug enters muscle."]], "journey": "Pravastatin is taken by mouth. Absorption is incomplete and first-pass uptake into the liver is high, so only a small part reaches the general circulation, which is good because the liver is the target. Being hydrophilic, it crosses into the brain and muscle less than fat-soluble statins. Unlike most statins it is not metabolized much by CYP enzymes; it is changed partly by sulfation and other non-CYP routes. It leaves in both bile and urine, and its half-life is short, about one and a half to three hours, though LDL effects last all day.", "sidefx": "Muscle side effects happen when statins reduce production of mevalonate-derived products like coenzyme Q10 and prenylated proteins in muscle cells. Because pravastatin is hydrophilic and not cleared by CYP3A4, it has fewer drug interactions than simvastatin, which makes it a common choice for people on many medicines. Liver enzyme rises reflect its liver action. Statins can slightly raise blood glucose and the chance of diabetes. They are avoided in pregnancy because cholesterol is needed for fetal development. There is no antidote; stopping the drug reverses side effects.", "fun": "Endo screened about 6,000 fungal strains before finding compactin, and he received the Lasker Clinical Medical Research Award in 2008 for starting the statin era.", "src": ["Pravachol (pravastatin) FDA label", "Endo A. Proc Jpn Acad Ser B 2010 (history of statins)", "Shepherd J et al. NEJM 1995 (WOSCOPS)"]}, "praziquantel": {"origin": "Praziquantel came from a joint research program between the German companies Bayer and E. Merck in the 1970s. Chemists made a series of pyrazinoisoquinoline compounds, and when these were screened against parasites, praziquantel proved remarkably active against flatworms, both flukes (trematodes) and tapeworms (cestodes), often after a single day of treatment. Before this, schistosomiasis was treated with toxic antimony compounds. Praziquantel was first used in veterinary medicine and then in humans, and it was approved in the US in the early 1980s as Biltricide. It transformed mass treatment programs for schistosomiasis, which affects hundreds of millions of people, and it is on the WHO list of essential medicines.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Bayer and E. Merck discover praziquantel in a joint research program."], ["1980s", "Praziquantel is approved for human use, including in the US as Biltricide."], ["2000s", "Praziquantel becomes the backbone of WHO mass drug programs for schistosomiasis."]], "cell": "Praziquantel acts on the worm's outer layer (tegument) and muscle. It causes a rapid flood of calcium into the parasite cells. Current evidence points to activation of a worm-specific TRPM channel (TRPM PZQ) and to effects involving the beta subunits of schistosome voltage-gated calcium channels. The sudden calcium entry makes worm muscles contract hard and stay contracted (spastic paralysis), so the worm loses its grip on blood vessel walls or intestinal lining. At the same time, the tegument forms blisters and vacuoles, exposing hidden parasite antigens. The host immune system can then attack and destroy the damaged worms. Human calcium channels are not affected in the same way, which gives selectivity.", "body": [["Parasites", "Flukes and tapeworms are paralyzed within minutes and then cleared by host defenses or passed in stool."], ["Liver and bladder vessels", "Killing schistosome worms stops new eggs, so egg-driven inflammation and scarring in the liver, gut and bladder can slow or partly reverse."], ["Brain", "In neurocysticercosis, dying cysts trigger local inflammation that can cause headache, seizures or raised intracranial pressure, so steroids are often given with it."], ["Gut", "Abdominal pain, nausea and loss of appetite are common, partly from dying worms."], ["Nervous system", "Headache, dizziness and drowsiness can occur, so driving is avoided the day of treatment."]], "journey": "Praziquantel is taken by mouth with food and is absorbed quickly and nearly completely. It then undergoes heavy first-pass metabolism in the liver, mainly by CYP3A4, so only a small part reaches the blood unchanged. It does reach the cerebrospinal fluid, which matters for brain cysts. The parent drug has a short half-life of about one to one and a half hours, and inactive metabolites leave mostly in urine. Strong CYP3A4 inducers such as rifampin can lower levels so much that treatment fails.", "sidefx": "Many side effects come from the dying parasites rather than the drug itself: abdominal pain, fever, hives and malaise as antigens are released. In neurocysticercosis, the inflammation around dying brain cysts explains seizures and raised intracranial pressure. It is contraindicated in eye cysticercosis because destroying a cyst in the eye can cause permanent damage. Dizziness and drowsiness reflect central nervous system effects. Interactions are tied to CYP3A4. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Praziquantel kills both flukes and tapeworms, two very different flatworm groups, which is why one drug can treat schistosomiasis, liver flukes and tapeworm infections.", "src": ["Biltricide (praziquantel) FDA label", "WHO Model List of Essential Medicines", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "prazosin": {"origin": "Prazosin was developed by Pfizer as a quinazoline compound and became the first selective alpha-1 adrenergic blocker. Older alpha blockers such as phenoxybenzamine and phentolamine blocked both alpha-1 and alpha-2 receptors, which caused strong reflex tachycardia. Prazosin's selectivity let it lower blood pressure with much less speeding of the heart. It was approved in the US in 1976 as Minipress. Doctors quickly learned about the first-dose effect, a sudden drop in blood pressure with fainting after the first pill. In the 2000s, psychiatrist Murray Raskind and colleagues in Seattle studied prazosin for PTSD-related nightmares in veterans, which became a widely used off-label application.", "timeline": [["1976", "FDA approves prazosin (Minipress) for hypertension."], ["2003", "Raskind and colleagues publish a controlled trial of prazosin for PTSD nightmares in veterans."]], "cell": "Prazosin competitively blocks postsynaptic alpha-1 adrenergic receptors on vascular smooth muscle. Alpha-1 receptors are Gq-coupled: norepinephrine binding normally activates phospholipase C, which makes IP3 and DAG, releases calcium from stores and causes contraction. By blocking this, prazosin relaxes both arterioles (lowering resistance) and veins (lowering venous return). It does not block presynaptic alpha-2 receptors, which act as a brake on norepinephrine release. With that brake intact, norepinephrine release does not surge, so reflex tachycardia is much smaller than with nonselective blockers. Alpha-1 receptors in the prostate and bladder neck are also blocked. In the brain, blocking alpha-1 receptors reduces noradrenergic overactivity tied to nightmares.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Relaxing arteries and veins lowers blood pressure, but venous pooling can cause dizziness or fainting when standing."], ["Heart", "Because presynaptic alpha-2 receptors are spared, heart rate rises only a little compared with nonselective alpha blockers."], ["Prostate and bladder", "Relaxing smooth muscle at the bladder neck and prostate improves urine flow in benign prostatic hyperplasia."], ["Brain", "Blocking central alpha-1 receptors can reduce trauma-related nightmares and sleep disruption."], ["Lipids", "It may slightly improve cholesterol levels, a minor effect seen with alpha-1 blockers."]], "journey": "Prazosin is taken by mouth and absorbed well, with peak levels in about two to three hours. It has significant first-pass liver metabolism and is highly protein bound. It crosses into the brain enough to affect sleep. The liver breaks it down mostly by demethylation and conjugation, and metabolites leave mainly in bile and feces. The half-life is short, about two to three hours, yet blood pressure effects last longer, which is why it is usually taken two or three times a day.", "sidefx": "The first-dose effect, a sharp fall in blood pressure with fainting about 30 to 90 minutes after the first dose, happens because veins dilate before the body's reflexes adjust. Starting low at bedtime reduces the risk. Dizziness, headache, fatigue and palpitations follow from vasodilation. Priapism can occur because alpha-1 blockade prevents blood from draining from erectile tissue. Alpha-1 blockers can also cause floppy iris syndrome during cataract surgery. There is no antidote; severe hypotension is treated by lying flat, IV fluids and, if needed, vasopressors.", "src": ["Minipress (prazosin) FDA label", "Raskind MA et al. Am J Psychiatry 2003", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "prednisolone": {"origin": "Prednisolone was created at Schering Corporation in New Jersey in the early 1950s by chemist Arthur Nobile and colleagues. Cortisone and hydrocortisone had just been shown to work dramatically in rheumatoid arthritis, but they caused salt and water retention. Nobile used the soil bacterium Corynebacterium simplex (now Arthrobacter simplex) to add a double bond between carbons 1 and 2 of hydrocortisone. The new compound, prednisolone, was several times stronger as an anti-inflammatory with less salt-retaining effect. It reached the market in 1955 along with its sister drug prednisone, made the same way from cortisone. Because prednisolone is already active and does not need the liver to switch it on, it is used in liquid form for children and as eye drops.", "timeline": [["1950", "Nobel Prize honors the discovery of adrenal cortex hormones, including cortisone."], ["1950s", "Arthur Nobile at Schering makes prednisolone by microbial dehydrogenation of hydrocortisone."], ["1955", "Prednisolone and prednisone reach the market."]], "cell": "Prednisolone is fat soluble and diffuses into cells, where it binds the glucocorticoid receptor in the cytoplasm. Binding releases heat shock proteins, and the receptor-drug complex moves into the nucleus. There it works in two main ways. In transactivation, it binds glucocorticoid response elements on DNA and turns on genes such as annexin A1 (lipocortin), which blocks phospholipase A2 and lowers prostaglandin and leukotriene production. In transrepression, it blocks the transcription factors NF-kappaB and AP-1, which shuts down genes for cytokines like IL-1, IL-2 and TNF-alpha, as well as COX-2. Because the effects need gene changes, the anti-inflammatory action builds over hours. The 1,2 double bond increases glucocorticoid activity relative to mineralocorticoid activity.", "body": [["Immune system", "Lower cytokine production, fewer circulating lymphocytes and eosinophils and less white cell movement into tissues reduce inflammation in asthma, croup, allergies and autoimmune disease."], ["Airways", "It reduces airway swelling and mucus and makes beta-2 receptors more responsive, which helps asthma attacks over hours."], ["Metabolism", "It raises blood glucose by boosting liver glucose production and reducing insulin sensitivity, and over time shifts fat to the face and trunk."], ["Adrenal glands", "It suppresses ACTH from the pituitary, and with longer use the adrenal glands shrink and stop making cortisol."], ["Bone and muscle", "Long use lowers bone formation and calcium absorption, leading to osteoporosis, and can cause muscle wasting."], ["Eyes", "As eye drops it calms inflammation inside the eye; long use can raise eye pressure and cause cataracts."]], "journey": "Prednisolone is well absorbed by mouth and is already active, so it does not depend on liver conversion. This makes it a good choice in liver disease and for children, who take it as a liquid. It also comes as eye drops. It binds to transcortin and albumin in the blood, crosses the placenta (partly inactivated there) and enters breast milk in small amounts. The liver metabolizes it, partly through CYP3A4, and the kidneys excrete the metabolites. Its plasma half-life is about two to four hours, but because it changes gene expression, its biological effect lasts 12 to 36 hours.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are glucocorticoid effects that are useful in one place and harmful in another. Blocking immunity raises infection risk and can hide fever. Increased glucose output causes high blood sugar. Brain effects cause mood swings, insomnia and sometimes psychosis. Weaker bone building causes osteoporosis. Long use suppresses the HPA axis, so stopping suddenly can cause adrenal crisis; doses are tapered after longer courses. Oral liquid forms taste bitter and can cause vomiting in children. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The pred in prednisolone and prednisone comes from pregnadiene, describing the steroid backbone with its extra double bond in the A ring.", "src": ["Orapred (prednisolone) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Herzog HL, Nobile A et al. Science 1955"]}, "pregabalin": {"origin": "Pregabalin was first made in 1989 by chemist Richard Silverman at Northwestern University, with postdoctoral researcher Ryszard Andruszkiewicz. Silverman was trying to make compounds that would activate glutamic acid decarboxylase, the enzyme that makes GABA, as a possible seizure treatment. Pregabalin worked well against seizures in animals, but later research showed it did not act on GABA systems as planned. Instead it bound the alpha-2-delta subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels, like its relative gabapentin. Northwestern licensed it to Parke-Davis, later part of Pfizer. The FDA approved it as Lyrica at the end of 2004 for diabetic nerve pain and postherpetic neuralgia, and in 2007 it became the first drug approved for fibromyalgia. Royalties funded a large new building at Northwestern.", "timeline": [["1989", "Richard Silverman and Ryszard Andruszkiewicz synthesize pregabalin at Northwestern University."], ["2004", "FDA approves pregabalin (Lyrica) for diabetic neuropathy and postherpetic neuralgia."], ["2005", "Pregabalin is placed in Schedule V by the DEA."], ["2007", "Pregabalin becomes the first FDA-approved drug for fibromyalgia."]], "cell": "Pregabalin is a GABA analog in shape, but it does not bind GABA-A or GABA-B receptors and does not change GABA levels in a meaningful way. Instead it binds the alpha-2-delta subunit (mainly alpha-2-delta-1) of voltage-gated calcium channels on presynaptic nerve terminals. After nerve injury, these subunits are made in larger amounts and help carry calcium channels to the cell surface. Binding pregabalin reduces this trafficking and lowers calcium entry when the nerve fires. With less calcium, hyperexcited neurons release less glutamate, substance P, norepinephrine and CGRP. The result is calmer pain signaling in the spinal cord dorsal horn and less abnormal firing in seizure circuits.", "body": [["Spinal cord and nerves", "Lower release of excitatory transmitters in the dorsal horn reduces burning, shooting nerve pain and pain from light touch."], ["Brain", "Dampened excitatory signaling reduces partial-onset seizures and anxiety, but also causes dizziness, sleepiness and trouble concentrating."], ["Breathing", "Combined with opioids or other sedatives, it can depress breathing, especially in older adults and people with lung disease."], ["Tissues and weight", "Peripheral edema and weight gain are common; the edema is not due to heart or kidney failure in most cases."], ["Eyes", "Blurred vision and other visual changes can occur, likely from effects on nerve signaling."]], "journey": "Pregabalin is taken by mouth and absorbed fast and nearly completely, about 90 percent, through amino acid transporters that do not saturate the way gabapentin's do. This gives predictable, dose-proportional levels. It peaks in about an hour, is not bound to plasma proteins and crosses the blood-brain barrier by a transporter. It is barely metabolized and is cleared almost entirely unchanged by the kidneys, with a half-life of about six hours. Kidney function directly controls dosing, and it is removed by hemodialysis.", "sidefx": "Dizziness, sleepiness, unsteadiness and blurred vision come from dampened nerve signaling in the brain. Breathing depression risk rises when combined with opioids or other sedatives. Edema and weight gain are common, though the mechanism is not fully understood. Some people feel euphoric, which explains misuse and its Schedule V status. Stopping suddenly can cause insomnia, anxiety, nausea and seizures, so it is tapered. Angioedema and allergic reactions are rare. Kidney impairment causes buildup. There is no antidote; overdose is managed with supportive care, and hemodialysis can remove it.", "fun": "Pregabalin royalties to Northwestern University helped fund the Richard and Barbara Silverman Hall for Molecular Therapeutics and Diagnostics.", "src": ["Lyrica (pregabalin) FDA label", "Silverman RB. Angew Chem Int Ed 2008 (history of pregabalin)"]}, "primaquine": {"origin": "Primaquine is an 8-aminoquinoline, a family that began with pamaquine (plasmochin), made by Bayer chemists in Germany in the 1920s. During and after World War II, the US government ran a huge antimalarial program to find better drugs, and chemist Robert Elderfield's group at Columbia University synthesized primaquine in the mid-1940s. It was the first drug that could reliably kill the dormant liver forms (hypnozoites) of Plasmodium vivax, which cause relapses months after infection. It was used widely in soldiers returning from the Korean War. In 1956, Alving and colleagues working with volunteers at Stateville prison in Illinois found that primaquine-induced hemolysis was caused by red cell deficiency of glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD), one of the first enzyme deficiencies linked to a drug reaction.", "timeline": [["1920s", "Bayer chemists develop pamaquine, the first 8-aminoquinoline antimalarial."], ["1940s", "Primaquine is synthesized in the US wartime antimalarial program."], ["1950s", "Primaquine is used for radical cure of vivax malaria in Korean War veterans."], ["1956", "Carson, Alving and colleagues identify G6PD deficiency as the cause of primaquine hemolysis."]], "cell": "The exact mechanism is not fully known, but primaquine works mainly through its metabolites. In the liver, CYP2D6 converts it to hydroxylated metabolites that cycle into quinone-imines. These redox-active compounds generate reactive oxygen species such as hydrogen peroxide inside the parasite. The oxidative stress damages parasite mitochondria and interferes with electron transport, killing the dormant hypnozoites of P. vivax and P. ovale in liver cells and the gametocytes of P. falciparum in the blood. The same oxidant stress affects human red cells. Normal red cells use G6PD to make NADPH and keep glutathione reduced, which neutralizes the oxidants. G6PD-deficient red cells cannot do this, so hemoglobin is oxidized and the cells break apart.", "body": [["Liver", "Killing dormant hypnozoites gives a radical cure of vivax and ovale malaria, preventing relapse months or years later."], ["Blood parasites", "Killing gametocytes of P. falciparum stops mosquitoes from picking up the parasite, which blocks spread in a community."], ["Red blood cells", "Oxidant stress causes hemolysis in G6PD deficiency, with dark urine, jaundice and anemia. It also oxidizes hemoglobin to methemoglobin, causing cyanosis."], ["Gut", "Abdominal cramps, nausea and stomach upset are common and lessen when taken with food."], ["White blood cells", "Leukopenia and, rarely, agranulocytosis can occur."]], "journey": "Primaquine is taken by mouth and absorbed well, reaching peak levels in about one to three hours. It spreads widely into tissues, including the liver, where it is needed. The liver metabolizes it in two main ways: MAO-A converts much of it to carboxyprimaquine, which is inactive, and CYP2D6 makes the active metabolites. People with poor CYP2D6 function may not activate it well and may relapse. Metabolites leave in urine. The parent drug half-life is about six hours. It crosses the placenta and is avoided in pregnancy because the fetus's G6PD status is unknown.", "sidefx": "The key toxicity is hemolytic anemia in G6PD deficiency, caused by oxidative stress that the red cells cannot handle. Testing G6PD status before use is required. Mild hemolysis may occur even with normal G6PD. Methemoglobinemia comes from oxidation of hemoglobin iron; it can be treated with methylene blue, but methylene blue itself can worsen hemolysis in G6PD deficiency. Stomach upset comes from local irritation. It is avoided in pregnancy and in breastfeeding infants of unknown G6PD status. There is no antidote for hemolysis; care is supportive with transfusion if needed.", "fun": "The 1956 discovery of G6PD deficiency from primaquine reactions is often called one of the founding moments of pharmacogenetics, the study of how genes change drug responses.", "src": ["Primaquine phosphate FDA label", "Carson PE et al. Science 1956", "CDC Yellow Book: Malaria"]}, "probenecid": {"origin": "Probenecid was developed in the late 1940s by Karl Beyer and colleagues at Sharp & Dohme (later Merck Sharp & Dohme). After World War II, penicillin was still expensive, and the kidneys cleared it so fast that much of each dose was lost in the urine within hours. Beyer reasoned that a drug blocking the kidney's acid secretion pumps could keep penicillin in the blood longer. Probenecid did exactly that. As penicillin became cheap, that use faded, but doctors noticed that probenecid also made the kidneys excrete more uric acid. It became one of the main long-term treatments to lower uric acid in gout, marketed as Benemid starting in the early 1950s. It is still used to boost levels of some antibiotics, such as with cefazolin or penicillin for neurosyphilis.", "timeline": [["1940s", "Penicillin shortages drive the search for a way to slow its kidney excretion."], ["1949", "Karl Beyer and colleagues at Sharp & Dohme describe probenecid."], ["1950s", "Probenecid (Benemid) enters use and is adopted as a uricosuric for gout."]], "cell": "Probenecid works on transporters in the proximal tubule of the kidney. Uric acid is filtered by the glomerulus and then mostly reabsorbed from tubular fluid back into blood by URAT1 on the apical membrane. Probenecid inhibits URAT1, so more uric acid stays in the urine and blood uric acid falls. Probenecid also blocks organic anion transporters OAT1 and OAT3 on the basolateral side, which normally pull weak acids such as penicillins, cephalosporins and methotrexate from blood into tubular cells for secretion. Blocking them keeps these drugs in the blood longer and at higher levels. At low doses, it can briefly decrease uric acid excretion by blocking its secretion, which is why low doses can worsen gout.", "body": [["Kidneys", "More uric acid in the urine lowers blood uric acid but raises the risk of uric acid kidney stones; drinking plenty of fluid and alkalinizing urine help."], ["Joints", "Over months, lower uric acid lets urate crystals dissolve, reducing gout attacks and tophi. Early on, shifting crystals can trigger flares."], ["Antibiotic levels", "Blocking tubular secretion keeps penicillins and cephalosporins in the blood longer, which can help treat severe infections."], ["Drug interactions", "It raises levels of methotrexate, some NSAIDs and other acidic drugs, increasing their toxicity."], ["Gut and skin", "GI upset, loss of appetite, headache and rash can occur."]], "journey": "Probenecid is taken by mouth and absorbed well, peaking in about two to four hours. It is highly bound to plasma albumin and stays mostly in the blood and extracellular fluid. The liver metabolizes it by glucuronidation and oxidation, and its half-life varies with dose, roughly three to eight hours. Metabolites leave in the urine. It works poorly when kidney function is reduced, because its effect depends on working kidney tubules.", "sidefx": "Pushing more uric acid into urine is the reason for kidney stone risk, especially in people who already make a lot of uric acid. Gout flares when starting come from crystals mobilizing as blood uric acid drops. Blocking excretion of other acidic drugs causes interactions, most dangerously with methotrexate. Aspirin and salicylates block its uricosuric effect. Rare reactions include hemolysis in G6PD deficiency, nephrotic syndrome, aplastic anemia and anaphylaxis. There is no antidote.", "fun": "During World War II, penicillin was so scarce that it was sometimes recovered from patients' urine and reused, which is the problem probenecid was invented to solve.", "src": ["Probenecid tablets FDA label", "Beyer KH et al. Am J Physiol 1951", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "procainamide": {"origin": "Procainamide grew out of the local anesthetic procaine (Novocain). In the 1930s, researchers noticed that procaine applied to the heart raised the threshold for abnormal beats, but procaine was broken down so quickly by plasma esterases that it was useless as a heart drug and caused central nervous system effects. Chemists changed procaine's ester bond to an amide, which plasma esterases cannot easily cut. The new compound, procainamide, kept the antiarrhythmic effect, lasted longer and could be given by mouth. It was introduced in the early 1950s by Squibb as Pronestyl. Procainamide became a standard drug for ventricular and atrial arrhythmias, and it was also central to the discovery of drug-induced lupus. Today it is used mainly IV for stable wide-complex tachycardia and pre-excited atrial fibrillation.", "timeline": [["1930s", "Procaine is shown to suppress cardiac arrhythmias in animal studies."], ["1950s", "Procainamide (Pronestyl) is introduced as an oral and IV antiarrhythmic."], ["1960s", "Procainamide-induced lupus and the role of slow acetylation are described."]], "cell": "Procainamide is a Class IA antiarrhythmic. It blocks open fast sodium channels (Nav1.5) in heart muscle and the conduction system, with intermediate binding kinetics. Fewer sodium channels open during phase 0, so the upstroke is slower and conduction through the atria, His-Purkinje system and ventricles slows, widening the QRS. It also blocks potassium channels, especially IKr, which slows phase 3 repolarization, lengthens the action potential and refractory period and prolongs the QT. Its liver metabolite, N-acetylprocainamide (NAPA), is a pure potassium channel blocker with Class III effects. Longer refractory periods break reentry circuits, and slowed conduction through accessory pathways makes it useful in pre-excited atrial fibrillation. It also has mild ganglionic blocking effects, which cause vasodilation.", "body": [["Heart conduction", "Slowed conduction and longer refractory periods stop reentrant arrhythmias. The ECG shows widening of the QRS and lengthening of the QT."], ["Accessory pathways", "It slows conduction through bypass tracts in Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome without blocking the AV node first, which is why it is chosen for pre-excited atrial fibrillation."], ["Blood vessels", "Ganglionic blockade and direct effects relax vessels, so rapid IV use can cause hypotension."], ["Immune system", "Long use can trigger antinuclear antibodies and a lupus-like syndrome with joint pain, fever and pleuritis, more often in slow acetylators."], ["Bone marrow", "Rarely it causes agranulocytosis, a dangerous drop in white cells."]], "journey": "Procainamide is now mostly given IV, with effects within minutes. It spreads into tissues and binds little to plasma proteins. The liver acetylates part of it to NAPA through N-acetyltransferase 2; people who are fast acetylators make more NAPA, slow acetylators make less. Both procainamide and NAPA are cleared mainly by the kidneys. The half-life of procainamide is about three to four hours, while NAPA lasts longer, so kidney failure can cause NAPA buildup and QT prolongation. Levels of both are monitored in prolonged therapy.", "sidefx": "Because it blocks sodium channels, too much drug widens the QRS and slows conduction, which can cause heart block. Because it blocks potassium channels, it prolongs the QT and can trigger torsades de pointes. Hypotension comes from vasodilation, especially with fast infusion. These are reasons to stop the infusion when QRS widens by half, hypotension occurs or the arrhythmia stops. Drug-induced lupus reflects immune effects of the drug's aromatic amine, and is more common in slow acetylators. Agranulocytosis is rare but serious. Sodium bicarbonate can help with sodium channel blocker toxicity; magnesium treats torsades.", "fun": "Procainamide and hydralazine are the classic board answers for drug-induced lupus, and both are metabolized by acetylation, which links slow acetylators to higher risk.", "src": ["Procainamide hydrochloride injection FDA label", "AHA Advanced Cardiovascular Life Support guidelines", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "prochlorperazine": {"origin": "Prochlorperazine belongs to the phenothiazines, a drug family that grew out of dye chemistry. In the 1940s and early 1950s, the French company Rhone-Poulenc made phenothiazine antihistamines like promethazine and then chlorpromazine, which changed psychiatry. Chemists kept changing the side chain on the phenothiazine ring, and adding a piperazine group produced drugs such as prochlorperazine that were strong dopamine blockers with less sedation. Smith Kline & French brought it to the US market as Compazine in the 1950s. Although it was approved for psychosis and anxiety, it became best known as an antiemetic and is still used in emergency departments for nausea, vomiting and migraine headache.", "timeline": [["1950", "Rhone-Poulenc chemists synthesize chlorpromazine, launching the phenothiazine era."], ["1950s", "Prochlorperazine is introduced in the US as Compazine."], ["1990s", "Studies support IV dopamine antagonists like prochlorperazine for emergency migraine care."]], "cell": "Prochlorperazine is a piperazine phenothiazine that blocks dopamine D2 receptors. Its antiemetic effect comes from blocking D2 receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (area postrema) in the floor of the fourth ventricle, which lies outside the blood-brain barrier and senses toxins in the blood. Blocked D2 signaling there sends fewer signals to the vomiting center in the medulla. In migraine, D2 blockade likely reduces dopamine-driven nausea and pain pathways. It also blocks D2 receptors in the nigrostriatal pathway, which upsets the dopamine-acetylcholine balance and causes movement side effects, and in the tuberoinfundibular pathway, which raises prolactin. Weaker blockade of H1, muscarinic and alpha-1 receptors adds sedation, dry mouth and low blood pressure.", "body": [["Brainstem", "Blocking D2 receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone reduces nausea and vomiting from many causes."], ["Head pain pathways", "IV use in the emergency department often relieves migraine headache as well as the nausea that comes with it."], ["Motor system", "Nigrostriatal D2 blockade can cause acute dystonia, akathisia (inner restlessness) and parkinsonism; akathisia is common after fast IV pushes."], ["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 blockade can lower blood pressure, especially when standing."], ["Heart", "It can prolong the QT interval, which matters when combined with other QT-prolonging drugs or low potassium."], ["Endocrine", "Blocking dopamine in the pituitary raises prolactin."]], "journey": "Prochlorperazine can be given by mouth, as a rectal suppository, IM or IV. Oral absorption is variable because of first-pass liver metabolism. IV doses work within about ten minutes. It is very fat soluble, highly protein bound and spreads widely, crossing the blood-brain barrier and placenta. The liver metabolizes it extensively, and metabolites leave in urine and bile. The half-life is several hours.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from blocking dopamine in places other than the vomiting center. In the nigrostriatal pathway, the result is dystonia (sudden muscle spasms of the neck, face or eyes), akathisia and parkinsonism; acute dystonia is treated with diphenhydramine or benztropine. Rarely, it causes neuroleptic malignant syndrome with fever, rigidity and autonomic instability, treated by stopping the drug, cooling and sometimes dantrolene or bromocriptine. Alpha-1 blockade causes orthostatic hypotension, and anticholinergic effects cause dry mouth and urinary retention. Children are more prone to dystonic reactions.", "src": ["Prochlorperazine FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "promethazine": {"origin": "Promethazine was made in the 1940s at the French company Rhone-Poulenc, during a search for new antihistamines built on the phenothiazine ring. It proved to be a strong H1 blocker with marked sedation. In the early 1950s, French surgeon Henri Laborit used promethazine in a lytic cocktail to calm patients and reduce surgical shock. Its strong central effects encouraged Rhone-Poulenc chemists to keep changing the molecule, which led to chlorpromazine in 1950, the first modern antipsychotic. Promethazine itself never became an antipsychotic but stayed in use for allergy, nausea, motion sickness and sedation. It was marketed in the US as Phenergan.", "timeline": [["1940s", "Rhone-Poulenc chemists develop promethazine as a phenothiazine antihistamine."], ["1950s", "Henri Laborit uses promethazine in surgical lytic cocktails, helping lead to chlorpromazine."], ["2004", "FDA adds a boxed warning against use in children under 2 years old."], ["2009", "FDA adds a boxed warning about severe tissue injury and gangrene with injection."]], "cell": "Promethazine is a phenothiazine with a short side chain. Its main action is competitive blockade of histamine H1 receptors. In the brain, histamine neurons from the hypothalamus keep people awake, so H1 blockade causes sedation. Blocking H1 and muscarinic receptors in the vestibular system and vomiting centers reduces motion sickness. It also has weak D2 blocking activity in the chemoreceptor trigger zone, which adds antiemetic effect and occasionally causes movement side effects. Its muscarinic blockade causes dry mouth and other anticholinergic effects, and alpha-1 blockade lowers blood pressure. It has local anesthetic-like effects and is caustic to tissue, which matters when it is injected.", "body": [["Brain", "H1 blockade in the brain causes strong sedation, which is used before surgery and for sleep, but adds to the effect of opioids and other sedatives."], ["Vestibular system and gut", "Reduces nausea, vomiting and motion sickness by blocking histamine and acetylcholine signals."], ["Skin and airways", "Blocking peripheral H1 receptors reduces itching, hives and runny nose in allergic reactions."], ["Breathing", "In young children it can cause fatal breathing depression, which is why it is contraindicated under 2 years of age."], ["Blood vessels and tissue", "Alpha-1 blockade causes low blood pressure. Injected drug that leaks or enters an artery can cause severe tissue injury and gangrene."]], "journey": "Promethazine can be given by mouth, rectally, IM or IV. Oral absorption is good, but first-pass liver metabolism lowers bioavailability. IV effects start within minutes, and oral effects within about 20 minutes. It is fat soluble, highly protein bound and crosses the blood-brain barrier and placenta. The liver metabolizes it, largely by CYP2D6, and metabolites leave in urine and bile. The half-life is long, about 10 to 14 hours, which explains next-day grogginess.", "sidefx": "Sedation, confusion and dry mouth are expected from H1 and muscarinic blockade, and older adults are especially sensitive. Breathing depression is dangerous in children under 2, leading to a boxed warning. Injection is caustic; if it leaks out of a vein or enters an artery, it can cause burning, tissue death and gangrene needing amputation, which led to a second boxed warning and preference for deep IM use. Weak D2 blockade can cause dystonia and rarely neuroleptic malignant syndrome. Anticholinergic overdose can cause delirium, which may be treated with physostigmine in selected cases.", "fun": "Promethazine is often called a parent of modern psychiatry drugs because the study of its sedative effects led directly to chlorpromazine.", "src": ["Phenergan (promethazine) FDA label", "Lopez-Munoz F et al. Ann Clin Psychiatry 2005 (history of chlorpromazine)"]}, "propafenone": {"origin": "Propafenone was developed in Germany in the 1970s and became widely used in Europe before reaching the United States, where it was approved in the late 1980s as Rythmol, marketed by Knoll. Its approval came just as the CAST trial showed that the related Class IC drugs flecainide and encainide increased deaths after heart attack. As a result, propafenone, like other Class IC drugs, is avoided in people with structural heart disease. Today it is used mainly to keep normal rhythm in people with paroxysmal atrial fibrillation who have structurally normal hearts, sometimes as a single dose taken at the onset of an episode.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Propafenone is developed and introduced in Germany."], ["1989", "CAST trial shows higher mortality with Class IC drugs after heart attack."], ["2000s", "Extended-release propafenone (Rythmol SR) becomes available in the US."]], "cell": "Propafenone is a Class IC antiarrhythmic. It binds fast sodium channels (Nav1.5) in the open and inactivated state and unbinds very slowly. This slow release means many channels stay blocked even between beats, especially at fast heart rates (use-dependence). The result is a much slower phase 0 upstroke, slowed conduction in the atria, His-Purkinje system and ventricles and widening of the QRS, with little change in action potential duration. This stops reentry circuits that drive atrial fibrillation and supraventricular tachycardia. Propafenone also has a beta-blocking effect, because its structure resembles beta blockers, and weak calcium channel blocking activity. The beta-blocking effect is stronger in people who are CYP2D6 poor metabolizers.", "body": [["Heart conduction", "Slowed conduction in atrial and ventricular tissue stops reentrant atrial fibrillation and supraventricular tachycardias. The ECG often shows wider QRS and a longer PR."], ["Heart rate", "Beta blockade slows the heart and can cause bradycardia or heart block."], ["Heart muscle", "It weakens contraction somewhat, which can worsen heart failure."], ["Lungs", "Beta blocking activity can cause bronchospasm in asthma."], ["Taste and gut", "A metallic or bitter taste, nausea and constipation are common."]], "journey": "Propafenone is taken by mouth and is absorbed well, but first-pass liver metabolism by CYP2D6 is heavy and saturable, so bioavailability rises as doses go up. CYP2D6 makes an active metabolite, 5-hydroxypropafenone. About 1 in 10 white people are CYP2D6 poor metabolizers, whose half-life is much longer (more than 10 hours versus about 2 to 10 hours) and who get more beta blockade. CYP3A4 and CYP1A2 also contribute. It is highly protein bound, and metabolites are excreted in urine and feces. Inhibitors of CYP2D6, such as fluoxetine and quinidine, raise levels.", "sidefx": "The same sodium channel block that stops reentry can create new arrhythmias, especially in scarred or weak hearts, which is why it is avoided after heart attack and in structural heart disease. In atrial flutter, slowing atrial conduction can allow 1 to 1 conduction to the ventricles and a very fast heart rate, so an AV nodal blocker is usually given too. Beta blockade explains bradycardia, fatigue and bronchospasm. The taste change is a classic clue. Sodium bicarbonate can help treat wide-QRS toxicity from sodium channel blockers.", "src": ["Rythmol and Rythmol SR (propafenone) FDA labels", "CAST Investigators. NEJM 1989", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "propofol": {"origin": "Propofol was discovered by Scottish veterinarian and pharmacologist John (Iain) Glen at Imperial Chemical Industries (ICI) in the UK. In the early 1970s, his team screened simple phenol compounds for anesthetic effects, and 2,6-diisopropylphenol stood out because animals went to sleep fast and woke up clear headed. Propofol does not dissolve in water, so the first human trials in 1977 used the solvent Cremophor EL, which caused allergic reactions. ICI reformulated it as a white soybean oil and egg lecithin emulsion, which gave it its milky look. It was launched in the UK in 1986 and approved by the FDA in 1989 as Diprivan. It became the most widely used IV anesthetic in the world, and Glen received the Lasker Award in 2018.", "timeline": [["1973", "John Glen's team at ICI identifies 2,6-diisopropylphenol as a promising anesthetic."], ["1977", "First human studies use a Cremophor formulation."], ["1986", "Propofol in lipid emulsion is launched in the UK."], ["1989", "FDA approves propofol (Diprivan)."], ["2018", "John Glen receives the Lasker-DeBakey Clinical Medical Research Award."]], "cell": "Propofol binds sites on the GABA-A receptor, a chloride channel made of five subunits, at the junction between beta and alpha subunits within the membrane. At low concentrations it increases how long the channel stays open when GABA binds; at higher levels it can open the channel directly. More chloride flowing in hyperpolarizes neurons, making them harder to fire. This depresses activity in the cortex, thalamus and brainstem arousal centers, producing sedation, amnesia and unconsciousness. It also inhibits NMDA glutamate receptors and some sodium channels. It has no analgesic effect. In the vasomotor center and blood vessels it reduces sympathetic tone and directly relaxes vascular smooth muscle, which lowers blood pressure.", "body": [["Brain", "Rapid sedation and loss of consciousness with lower brain oxygen use, lower blood flow and lower intracranial pressure. It also has antiseizure and antiemetic effects."], ["Breathing", "Depresses the breathing center and often causes apnea after a bolus. It also reduces airway reflexes, which helps intubation."], ["Heart and vessels", "Vasodilation and reduced sympathetic tone drop blood pressure, often more than other induction agents, especially in hypovolemic or elderly patients."], ["Metabolism", "The lipid carrier provides calories and can raise triglyceride levels during long infusions, sometimes causing pancreatitis."], ["Injection site", "It often stings when injected into small veins; lidocaine or a larger vein reduces pain."]], "journey": "Propofol is given IV. Because it is very fat soluble, it enters the brain within about 30 seconds, and patients go to sleep within one arm-to-brain circulation time. Quick recovery after a single dose happens because the drug moves out of the brain into muscle and fat (redistribution). The liver and other tissues rapidly conjugate it to inactive glucuronides and sulfates, and the kidneys excrete them. Its clearance is faster than liver blood flow, which suggests other organs such as the lungs and kidneys help. After long infusions, it slowly comes back out of fat, but waking is still fairly quick. It crosses the placenta.", "sidefx": "Hypotension and apnea follow directly from its effects on the vasomotor and breathing centers, so it is given only with airway support and monitoring. Injection pain comes from the free drug irritating the vein. Long, high-dose infusions can cause propofol infusion syndrome (PRIS): metabolic acidosis, rhabdomyolysis, high potassium, kidney failure and heart failure, thought to arise from impaired fatty acid oxidation in mitochondria. The lipid emulsion supports bacterial growth, so strict sterile technique is needed. Rarely it turns urine green. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Its milky look earned propofol the nickname milk of amnesia, and the 2009 death of Michael Jackson brought public attention to its misuse outside hospitals.", "src": ["Diprivan (propofol) FDA label", "Lasker Foundation 2018 award citation", "Miller's Anesthesia"]}, "propranolol": {"origin": "Propranolol was developed by Scottish pharmacologist James Black at Imperial Chemical Industries (ICI) in England. Black reasoned that instead of making a failing heart work harder, doctors should reduce its oxygen demand by blocking adrenaline's effects. Building on Raymond Ahlquist's 1948 idea of alpha and beta receptors, his team first made pronethalol, which caused tumors in mice, and then propranolol in 1964. Propranolol was marketed in the UK in the mid-1960s and reached the US later that decade as Inderal. It was the first widely successful beta blocker and changed treatment of angina, high blood pressure, arrhythmias and more. Black shared the 1988 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine, partly for this work.", "timeline": [["1948", "Raymond Ahlquist proposes separate alpha and beta adrenergic receptors."], ["1964", "James Black's team at ICI describes propranolol."], ["1960s", "Propranolol is marketed in the UK and then the US."], ["1988", "James Black shares the Nobel Prize for drug discovery principles, including beta blockers."], ["2014", "FDA approves an oral propranolol solution (Hemangeol) for infantile hemangioma."]], "cell": "Propranolol is a nonselective competitive antagonist at beta-1 and beta-2 adrenergic receptors. These are Gs-coupled: normally epinephrine or norepinephrine binding activates adenylyl cyclase, raises cAMP and activates protein kinase A. In the heart, PKA phosphorylates L-type calcium channels and other proteins to increase rate, contraction force and conduction. By blocking beta-1, propranolol lowers cAMP in the SA node, AV node and myocardium, so heart rate, contractility and AV conduction fall, and oxygen demand drops. Blocking beta-1 on kidney juxtaglomerular cells lowers renin release. Blocking beta-2 in bronchi, blood vessels and liver removes their relaxing and glucose-releasing effects. At high concentrations it blocks sodium channels (membrane stabilizing). It also reduces conversion of T4 to T3.", "body": [["Heart", "Slower heart rate, weaker contraction and slowed AV conduction reduce angina, control fast heart rates and help after heart attack."], ["Blood pressure", "Lower cardiac output and renin release lower blood pressure over time."], ["Lungs", "Beta-2 blockade narrows airways and can trigger severe bronchospasm in asthma."], ["Brain", "As a fat-soluble drug it enters the brain, which can prevent migraines and reduce physical symptoms of performance anxiety, but can cause vivid dreams, fatigue and depression."], ["Thyroid", "In hyperthyroidism and thyroid storm it calms tremor, sweating and fast heart rate and reduces conversion of T4 to active T3."], ["Metabolism", "Blocking beta-2 in the liver limits glucose release, and blocking beta signaling hides warning signs of low blood sugar such as tremor and racing heart."], ["Portal circulation", "It lowers portal pressure in cirrhosis, reducing variceal bleeding risk."]], "journey": "Propranolol is absorbed almost completely by mouth, but first-pass liver metabolism removes much of it, so bioavailability is low and varies between people. It is very fat soluble, highly protein bound and crosses the blood-brain barrier and placenta. CYP2D6, CYP1A2 and CYP2C19 metabolize it; the 4-hydroxy metabolite is active. Metabolites leave in urine. The half-life is about three to six hours; long-acting capsules allow once-daily use. It can be given IV for some arrhythmias.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow beta blockade where it is not wanted. Beta-2 blockade causes bronchospasm in asthma and COPD and cold hands from reduced blood vessel relaxation. Beta-1 blockade can cause bradycardia, heart block and worsening of acute heart failure. Brain entry causes fatigue, vivid dreams and depression. Masked hypoglycemia is dangerous in diabetics on insulin. Stopping suddenly can cause rebound tachycardia and angina because receptors have increased in number. In overdose, its sodium channel blocking makes it one of the most dangerous beta blockers, with seizures and wide QRS. Overdose is treated with glucagon (which raises cAMP without beta receptors), high-dose insulin and glucose, calcium, vasopressors and sodium bicarbonate for wide QRS.", "fun": "James Black also later discovered the H2 blocker cimetidine, so one scientist created two of the best-selling drug classes of the 20th century.", "src": ["Inderal LA (propranolol) FDA label", "Black JW et al. Lancet 1964", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1988 press release"]}, "propylthiouracil": {"origin": "In the early 1940s, researchers studying sulfur-containing compounds in rats noticed that some of them caused goiters, enlarged thyroid glands. Edwin Astwood, a physician in Boston, realized these compounds were blocking thyroid hormone production, which made the pituitary drive the gland to grow. In 1943 he reported treating patients with hyperthyroidism using thiourea and thiouracil, the first drug therapy for Graves disease. Thiouracil was toxic, so his group and others tested related compounds, and propylthiouracil (PTU) was introduced in the 1940s as a safer choice. Methimazole followed. In 2010 the FDA added a boxed warning about severe liver injury from PTU, so it is now reserved mainly for the first trimester of pregnancy and thyroid storm.", "timeline": [["1943", "Edwin Astwood reports thiourea and thiouracil for hyperthyroidism."], ["1940s", "Propylthiouracil is introduced as a less toxic thionamide."], ["2010", "FDA adds a boxed warning for severe liver injury with PTU."]], "cell": "Thyroid hormone production happens in the thyroid follicle. Thyroid peroxidase (TPO) oxidizes iodide, attaches it to tyrosine residues on thyroglobulin (organification) and couples iodotyrosines into T4 and T3. PTU is a thionamide that serves as a substrate for TPO and competes with tyrosine, blocking both organification and coupling, so new hormone production falls. Hormone already stored in the gland is still released, so the full effect takes weeks. PTU has a second action that methimazole lacks: it inhibits type 1 5'-deiodinase in peripheral tissues, which lowers conversion of T4 to the more active T3. That extra action is one reason it is used in thyroid storm.", "body": [["Thyroid gland", "Blocks new T4 and T3 synthesis, so hormone levels fall gradually as stored hormone is used up over several weeks."], ["Peripheral tissues", "Less T4 is converted to T3, which reduces thyroid effects faster in thyroid storm."], ["Heart and metabolism", "As hormone levels fall, heart rate, tremor, heat intolerance and weight loss improve."], ["Liver", "It can cause severe liver injury, including liver failure needing transplant, which is the reason for its boxed warning."], ["Bone marrow", "It can cause agranulocytosis, a sudden loss of neutrophils, usually in the first months."], ["Pregnancy", "It is preferred in the first trimester because methimazole early in pregnancy is linked to specific birth defects."]], "journey": "PTU is taken by mouth and is absorbed fast, peaking within about one to two hours. It is concentrated in the thyroid gland, so its effect lasts longer than its short plasma half-life of about one to two hours, though it still needs dosing several times a day. It is highly bound to plasma proteins, which limits how much enters breast milk. It does cross the placenta and can affect the fetal thyroid. The liver metabolizes it, mainly by glucuronidation, and the kidneys excrete the metabolites.", "sidefx": "Agranulocytosis is an immune reaction against neutrophils. It usually starts with fever and sore throat, which means the drug should be stopped and a blood count checked. Severe liver injury is less common but can be fatal, more often in children, and its unpredictable nature led to the boxed warning. Rash, itching, joint pain and nausea are common. PTU can also cause ANCA-associated vasculitis. Too much effect causes hypothyroidism. There is no antidote; care is to stop the drug and give supportive treatment.", "src": ["Propylthiouracil tablets FDA label", "Astwood EB. JAMA 1943", "American Thyroid Association hyperthyroidism guidelines"]}, "protamine": {"origin": "Protamine was discovered by Swiss biologist Friedrich Miescher, who also discovered DNA (nuclein). In the 1870s, working with salmon sperm from the Rhine River in Basel, he found a simple, strongly basic protein bound to the DNA in sperm heads and named it protamine. In 1936, Danish researcher Hans Christian Hagedorn added protamine to insulin to make it absorb more slowly, which later led to NPH (neutral protamine Hagedorn) insulin. In the late 1930s, researchers including Erwin Chargaff showed that protamine neutralizes heparin. When heart surgery with cardiopulmonary bypass began in the 1950s, protamine became the standard way to reverse heparin at the end of the case. It is still made from fish sperm today.", "timeline": [["1870s", "Friedrich Miescher discovers protamine in salmon sperm."], ["1936", "Hans Christian Hagedorn combines protamine with insulin to slow absorption."], ["1937", "Chargaff and Olson show that protamine neutralizes heparin."], ["1950s", "Protamine becomes routine for heparin reversal after cardiopulmonary bypass."]], "cell": "Protamine is a small protein rich in the amino acid arginine, which gives it a strong positive charge. Heparin is a long chain of sulfated sugars with a strong negative charge. When protamine meets heparin in the blood, the two bind tightly by charge attraction and form a stable salt complex. In that complex, heparin can no longer bind antithrombin, so antithrombin stops being boosted and thrombin and factor Xa are no longer rapidly inactivated. Clotting returns to normal within minutes. Protamine fully reverses unfractionated heparin but only partly reverses low molecular weight heparin, because the shorter chains bind protamine less completely and its anti-Xa effect is only partly neutralized. Given alone in excess, protamine has its own weak anticoagulant effect.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "It neutralizes heparin within minutes, restoring normal clotting times such as the aPTT and activated clotting time."], ["Blood vessels", "Fast injection can release histamine and cause flushing and a sudden drop in blood pressure."], ["Lungs", "Rarely it causes severe pulmonary vasoconstriction and pulmonary hypertension, which can strain the right heart."], ["Immune system", "People previously exposed to protamine, such as through NPH insulin, or those with fish allergy or prior vasectomy may have antibodies that cause anaphylaxis."], ["Heart", "Bradycardia and cardiovascular collapse can occur in severe reactions."]], "journey": "Protamine is given slowly IV and acts within about five minutes. It binds heparin in the bloodstream right away. The protamine-heparin complex is cleared by the reticuloendothelial system, and protamine itself is broken down by proteases. Its own half-life is short, about seven minutes, which is shorter than unfractionated heparin's. Because of this, heparin can come back into circulation from tissues after the protamine is gone (heparin rebound), especially after cardiac surgery, so clotting tests are rechecked.", "sidefx": "Fast injection causes histamine release and complement activation, leading to flushing, low blood pressure and bradycardia, which is why it is given slowly. Anaphylaxis is more likely in people with antibodies to protamine, including diabetics on NPH or protamine zinc insulin, people with fish allergy and men after vasectomy, who may form antibodies to sperm proteins. A rare catastrophic reaction causes intense pulmonary vasoconstriction and right heart failure. Too much protamine can itself impair clotting by interfering with platelets and clotting factors. There is no antidote; reactions are treated with epinephrine, fluids and supportive care.", "fun": "The scientist who discovered protamine, Friedrich Miescher, also discovered DNA in 1869, and both came from his study of cell nuclei.", "src": ["Protamine sulfate injection FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "pyrazinamide": {"origin": "In 1945, French researcher Vital Chorine noticed that nicotinamide, a form of vitamin B3, had some activity against tuberculosis in animals. This prompted drug companies to test related compounds. Pyrazinamide had been first synthesized in 1936 but had no known use. In 1952, researchers at Lederle Laboratories and Merck independently reported that it had strong activity against TB in mice, even though it barely worked in standard lab cultures. It turned out to work best in acidic conditions, like inside macrophages and inflamed tissue. In the 1970s and 1980s, British Medical Research Council trials showed that adding pyrazinamide to isoniazid and rifampin allowed TB treatment to be shortened from nine months to six. It is now one of the four first-line TB drugs, known as RIPE therapy.", "timeline": [["1936", "Pyrazinamide is first synthesized."], ["1945", "Vital Chorine reports nicotinamide activity against TB."], ["1952", "Lederle and Merck researchers report pyrazinamide activity against TB in mice."], ["1970s", "British MRC trials show pyrazinamide allows short-course six-month TB therapy."]], "cell": "Pyrazinamide is a prodrug. It diffuses into Mycobacterium tuberculosis, where the bacterial enzyme pyrazinamidase (encoded by the pncA gene) converts it to pyrazinoic acid. In acidic environments, such as inside macrophage phagolysosomes and areas of active inflammation, pyrazinoic acid becomes protonated, leaks back into the bacterium and builds up. This disrupts membrane energy (proton motive force) and acidifies the cell. Proposed targets include fatty acid synthase I and the protein PanD in the coenzyme A pathway. The result is killing of slow-growing, semi-dormant bacilli in acidic sites, which other drugs miss. Mutations in pncA that remove pyrazinamidase are the main cause of resistance. M. bovis naturally lacks this enzyme and is resistant.", "body": [["Lungs and macrophages", "Kills dormant TB bacilli hiding in acidic areas such as inside macrophages and inflamed lesions, which is why it shortens therapy."], ["Liver", "It can cause hepatitis, which may be severe, especially with other TB drugs that also stress the liver."], ["Joints and uric acid", "Pyrazinoic acid blocks kidney excretion of uric acid, raising blood uric acid and causing joint pain or gout flares."], ["Skin", "Rash and sensitivity to sunlight can occur."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and loss of appetite are common, and can also be early signs of liver injury."]], "journey": "Pyrazinamide is taken by mouth, absorbed well and reaches peak levels in about two hours. It spreads widely, including into the cerebrospinal fluid, which makes it useful for TB meningitis, and into macrophages. It has low protein binding. The liver converts it to pyrazinoic acid and then to hydroxylated metabolites. These are excreted mainly by the kidneys. The half-life is about nine to ten hours, allowing once-daily or intermittent dosing.", "sidefx": "Hepatotoxicity is the most serious side effect and is related to dose. Liver tests and symptoms like nausea and dark urine are monitored. Hyperuricemia happens because pyrazinoic acid competes for kidney transport and lowers uric acid secretion; this causes arthralgia and sometimes gout. Gout or severe liver disease are reasons to avoid it. Photosensitivity and rash can occur. The four-drug regimen is often taught with the mnemonic RIPE (rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, ethambutol). There is no antidote.", "src": ["Pyrazinamide tablets FDA label", "CDC/ATS/IDSA treatment of drug-susceptible tuberculosis guidelines", "Zhang Y, Mitchison D. Int J Tuberc Lung Dis 2003"]}, "pyridostigmine": {"origin": "In 1934, English physician Mary Walker at St Alfege's Hospital in London noticed that myasthenia gravis looked like curare poisoning and tried physostigmine, which reverses curare. Her patient's weakness improved quickly, a result called the miracle at St Alfege's. This pointed to the neuromuscular junction as the site of the disease. Physostigmine and then neostigmine became the first treatments. Pyridostigmine was synthesized at Hoffmann-La Roche in the 1940s and introduced as Mestinon in the 1950s. It lasted longer than neostigmine with fewer gut side effects, and it became the mainstay for symptom control in myasthenia gravis. The US military also gave it to troops in the 1991 Gulf War as a nerve agent pretreatment, and the FDA approved it for soman pretreatment in 2003.", "timeline": [["1934", "Mary Walker shows physostigmine improves myasthenia gravis."], ["1940s", "Pyridostigmine is synthesized at Hoffmann-La Roche."], ["1950s", "Pyridostigmine (Mestinon) enters use for myasthenia gravis."], ["1991", "US troops take pyridostigmine as nerve agent pretreatment in the Gulf War."], ["2003", "FDA approves pyridostigmine for military pretreatment against soman."]], "cell": "Pyridostigmine is a carbamate that reversibly inhibits acetylcholinesterase. It binds the enzyme's active site and transfers a carbamyl group to the catalytic serine. The carbamylated enzyme cannot break down acetylcholine and takes hours to recover, much longer than the microseconds it normally needs. Acetylcholine therefore stays longer in the synapse and acts on more receptors. In myasthenia gravis, antibodies have destroyed many nicotinic receptors at the neuromuscular junction, and the extra acetylcholine helps the remaining receptors reach threshold, so muscle fibers fire and strength improves. It is a quaternary amine with a permanent positive charge, so it does not cross the blood-brain barrier and has no central effects. As a nerve agent pretreatment, it shields a fraction of enzyme from irreversible binding.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "More acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction improves strength in myasthenia gravis, including eyelid droop, double vision, chewing and swallowing."], ["Gut", "Muscarinic stimulation increases gut movement and secretions, causing cramps, diarrhea and nausea."], ["Glands", "Increased salivation, sweating and tearing come from muscarinic effects."], ["Heart", "Increased vagal tone slows the heart and can cause bradycardia."], ["Lungs", "More bronchial secretions and bronchoconstriction can worsen asthma."], ["Brain", "Because it is charged and cannot enter the brain, it does not cause confusion or seizures at normal doses."]], "journey": "Pyridostigmine is taken by mouth or given IV. Because it is charged, oral absorption is poor and variable, with only a small fraction absorbed. Effects start in about 30 to 60 minutes and last several hours; an extended-release form helps overnight. It stays outside the brain. Cholinesterases in plasma and the liver break down part of it, and much of it is excreted unchanged by the kidneys. The half-life is short, about one and a half to two hours, which is why dosing is spaced through the day. Kidney disease prolongs its action.", "sidefx": "Side effects reflect too much acetylcholine at muscarinic sites: cramping, diarrhea, sweating, salivation and slow heart rate. Glycopyrrolate or atropine can be used to block these. Too much drug causes cholinergic crisis, in which excess acetylcholine depolarizes the muscle end plate and causes weakness that can be confused with a myasthenic crisis, and it can lead to respiratory failure. Fasciculations and muscle cramps come from nicotinic overstimulation. Atropine treats muscarinic toxicity, and ventilation supports breathing.", "fun": "Mary Walker's 1934 insight, based on the resemblance between myasthenia and curare poisoning, came before scientists had proved that acetylcholine carries the nerve-to-muscle signal.", "src": ["Mestinon (pyridostigmine) FDA label", "Pyridostigmine bromide tablets for soman pretreatment FDA label", "Walker MB. Lancet 1934"]}, "quetiapine": {"origin": "Quetiapine was discovered by researchers at ICI Pharmaceuticals in England, later Zeneca and then AstraZeneca, in the 1980s. The goal was a drug that treated psychosis like clozapine but without clozapine's risk of agranulocytosis and with fewer movement problems than older antipsychotics. Chemists built it on a dibenzothiazepine structure related to clozapine. The FDA approved it as Seroquel in 1997 for schizophrenia. Later approvals added bipolar mania, bipolar depression and, for the extended-release form, add-on treatment of major depression. Its low risk of movement side effects makes it a common choice for psychosis in Parkinson disease.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Quetiapine is developed at ICI Pharmaceuticals in England."], ["1997", "FDA approves quetiapine (Seroquel) for schizophrenia."], ["2007", "Extended-release quetiapine (Seroquel XR) is approved."]], "cell": "Quetiapine is a second-generation antipsychotic that blocks serotonin 5-HT2A receptors more strongly than dopamine D2 receptors. Its D2 binding is loose and it comes off the receptor quickly (fast off-rate), so it blocks D2 in the mesolimbic pathway enough to reduce psychosis but leaves the nigrostriatal pathway mostly free, which limits extrapyramidal symptoms and prolactin rise. Blocking 5-HT2A increases dopamine release in the cortex and striatum, further protecting movement. Its active metabolite norquetiapine blocks the norepinephrine transporter and is a partial agonist at 5-HT1A receptors, which may explain its antidepressant effect. Strong histamine H1 blockade causes sedation, and alpha-1 blockade causes orthostatic hypotension.", "body": [["Brain (mood and thought)", "Reduces hallucinations and delusions in schizophrenia, calms mania and improves bipolar depression."], ["Sleep and alertness", "Strong H1 blockade causes marked sedation, especially at lower doses where antihistamine effects dominate."], ["Motor system", "Fast dissociation from D2 receptors means movement side effects such as parkinsonism are uncommon."], ["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 blockade causes dizziness and drops in blood pressure on standing, especially when starting."], ["Metabolism", "Weight gain, high blood sugar and high cholesterol and triglycerides can occur, raising heart risk over time."], ["Heart and eyes", "It can lengthen the QT interval, and cataracts were seen in animal studies, so eye exams are suggested."]], "journey": "Quetiapine is taken by mouth and absorbed well, with peak levels in about one and a half hours for the regular form. First-pass metabolism lowers bioavailability. It crosses the blood-brain barrier and is about 83 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it extensively, mainly by CYP3A4, into norquetiapine and inactive products, and the kidneys excrete most metabolites. The half-life is about six to seven hours. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ketoconazole or ritonavir raise its levels a lot, and inducers such as carbamazepine lower them.", "sidefx": "Sedation and weight gain come mostly from H1 blockade, and orthostatic hypotension from alpha-1 blockade. Metabolic effects such as high blood sugar and lipids are linked to receptor effects on appetite and insulin action. Like other antipsychotics, it carries a boxed warning for higher death rates in elderly people with dementia-related psychosis and for suicidal thoughts in young people. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome, tardive dyskinesia and QT prolongation are rare. Anticholinergic effects such as constipation and dry mouth can occur. There is no antidote; overdose care is supportive.", "src": ["Seroquel (quetiapine) FDA label", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology"]}, "quinidine": {"origin": "Quinidine comes from the bark of the cinchona tree of South America, the same source as quinine. Indigenous people of the Andes and later Jesuit missionaries used the bark for fevers, and by the 1600s it was known in Europe as Jesuit's bark for malaria. In 1820, French chemists Pierre Pelletier and Joseph Caventou isolated quinine, and chemists later isolated its isomer quinidine. In 1914, Dutch cardiologist Karel Wenckebach reported a merchant who stopped his own atrial fibrillation with quinine. In 1918, German physician Walter Frey tested the cinchona alkaloids and found that quinidine worked best. Quinidine became the first widely used antiarrhythmic drug. Today it is used rarely, mostly for special conditions such as Brugada syndrome.", "timeline": [["1600s", "Cinchona bark spreads in Europe as a remedy for malarial fevers."], ["1820", "Pelletier and Caventou isolate quinine from cinchona bark."], ["1914", "Karel Wenckebach reports quinine stopping atrial fibrillation."], ["1918", "Walter Frey shows quinidine is the most effective cinchona alkaloid for atrial fibrillation."], ["2019", "IV quinidine gluconate, long used for severe malaria, stops being made for the US market."]], "cell": "Quinidine is the classic Class IA antiarrhythmic. It blocks fast voltage-gated sodium channels (Nav1.5) in the open state with intermediate kinetics, slowing the phase 0 upstroke and conduction, which widens the QRS. It also blocks several potassium currents, mainly IKr and the transient outward current Ito, so repolarization is slowed, the action potential is longer, the refractory period lengthens and the QT increases. Blocking Ito is why it can help in Brugada syndrome. It has anticholinergic (vagolytic) effects that can speed AV node conduction and alpha-1 blocking effects that cause vasodilation. It also inhibits CYP2D6 and P-glycoprotein. As an antimalarial, it builds up in the parasite's food vacuole and blocks detoxification of heme.", "body": [["Heart conduction", "Slowed conduction and longer refractory periods suppress atrial and ventricular arrhythmias, widening the QRS and lengthening the QT."], ["AV node", "Vagolytic effects can speed AV conduction, so in atrial flutter the ventricular rate can rise unless an AV nodal blocker is also used."], ["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 blockade causes vasodilation and low blood pressure, especially when given IV."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is very common and is a frequent reason people stop the drug."], ["Ears, eyes and brain", "Cinchonism causes ringing in the ears, hearing loss, headache, dizziness and blurred vision."], ["Blood", "Immune reactions can cause thrombocytopenia and, in G6PD deficiency, hemolysis."]], "journey": "Quinidine is taken by mouth as sulfate or gluconate salts. The gluconate form is absorbed more slowly. It is mostly bound to plasma proteins and spreads widely into tissues. The liver metabolizes most of it by CYP3A4, with about 20 percent excreted unchanged by the kidneys. The half-life is about six to eight hours. Because it inhibits CYP2D6 and P-glycoprotein, it raises levels of drugs such as digoxin and blocks activation of codeine to morphine.", "sidefx": "Its potassium channel blockade can trigger torsades de pointes, a dangerous polymorphic ventricular tachycardia, sometimes at low levels, which is called quinidine syncope. Diarrhea is common and can cause low potassium, which raises torsades risk further. Cinchonism reflects toxicity of cinchona alkaloids on the inner ear and nervous system. Immune thrombocytopenia, hepatitis and a lupus-like syndrome can occur. It roughly doubles digoxin levels by lowering its clearance and blocking P-gp. Torsades is treated with IV magnesium, and QRS widening from sodium channel blockade can be treated with sodium bicarbonate.", "fun": "Quinidine is the mirror-image partner (a stereoisomer) of quinine, yet one is mainly a heart drug and the other mainly an antimalarial.", "src": ["Quinidine sulfate and quinidine gluconate FDA labels", "Frey W. Berl Klin Wochenschr 1918", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "racemic-epinephrine": {"origin": "Epinephrine was isolated from adrenal glands in 1901 by Japanese chemist Jokichi Takamine, working in the US, and was synthesized in 1904 by Friedrich Stolz. Natural epinephrine is the L form only, but chemical synthesis produces a racemic mix of L and D forms in equal parts. Inhaled racemic epinephrine was used for asthma, and in the 1970s pediatricians began using nebulized racemic epinephrine for croup, the barking cough illness in young children caused by viral swelling below the vocal cords. It quickly reduced stridor and breathing effort. Later studies showed plain L-epinephrine works equally well, but racemic epinephrine remains widely known in EMS and pediatric care, and an over-the-counter inhaled form is sold for mild asthma.", "timeline": [["1901", "Jokichi Takamine isolates epinephrine from adrenal glands."], ["1904", "Friedrich Stolz synthesizes epinephrine."], ["1970s", "Nebulized racemic epinephrine becomes a standard treatment for croup."], ["1990s", "Studies show L-epinephrine works as well as racemic epinephrine for croup."]], "cell": "Racemic epinephrine is a 50 to 50 mix of L-epinephrine and D-epinephrine. The L form is the active one. It stimulates alpha-1 adrenergic receptors on blood vessels in the airway lining; these Gq receptors raise calcium in vascular smooth muscle and cause vasoconstriction. Narrower mucosal vessels leak less fluid, so swelling in the subglottic airway shrinks. The L form also stimulates beta-2 receptors on bronchial smooth muscle; these Gs receptors raise cAMP, activate protein kinase A and relax the muscle, widening the airways. Beta-1 stimulation in the heart raises rate and contractility. The D form has little activity, so racemic epinephrine acts like about half the amount of pure L-epinephrine.", "body": [["Upper airway", "Shrinking swollen subglottic tissue quickly reduces stridor and work of breathing in croup and post-extubation airway swelling."], ["Lower airways", "Beta-2 stimulation relaxes bronchial smooth muscle, helping wheezing in asthma."], ["Heart", "Some drug is absorbed, so heart rate rises and palpitations or arrhythmias can occur, especially with repeated doses."], ["Blood vessels", "Blood pressure may rise, and the skin can look pale from vasoconstriction."], ["Nervous system", "Tremor, anxiety and restlessness come from adrenergic stimulation."]], "journey": "Racemic epinephrine is inhaled as a nebulized mist, which delivers it straight to the airway lining. Effects begin within minutes and peak within about 10 to 30 minutes. Some of it is absorbed into the blood through the mucosa. It is rapidly broken down by MAO and COMT in tissues and the liver, so its action is short, about one to two hours. Inactive metabolites leave in urine. Because the effect can wear off and swelling can return (rebound), children with croup are usually observed for a few hours after treatment.", "sidefx": "Side effects come from alpha and beta stimulation spreading beyond the airway. Beta-1 effects cause fast heart rate and palpitations. Alpha-1 effects cause pallor and raised blood pressure. Central effects cause tremor and anxiety. The effect on swelling is temporary, so stridor can return as the drug wears off, which is why it is paired with corticosteroids for croup. People with heart disease and those taking MAO inhibitors are at higher risk of arrhythmias and high blood pressure. Treatment of overstimulation is mainly stopping the drug and supportive care.", "src": ["S2 and Asthmanefrin (racepinephrine) labeling", "Westley CR et al. Am J Dis Child 1978", "Waisman Y et al. Pediatrics 1992"]}, "raloxifene": {"origin": "Raloxifene was developed by Eli Lilly. It began in the early 1980s as a compound called keoxifene, tested as a breast cancer treatment similar to tamoxifen. Results were disappointing, and work stopped. Researchers then found that the drug acted like estrogen in bone but blocked estrogen in the breast and uterus. Lilly revived it as a drug to prevent bone loss after menopause. The FDA approved it as Evista in 1997 for osteoporosis prevention, and later for treatment. The STAR trial, published in 2006, compared it with tamoxifen and showed it reduced invasive breast cancer risk with less uterine cancer. In 2007 the FDA approved it for reducing breast cancer risk in postmenopausal women at high risk or with osteoporosis.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Eli Lilly tests the compound, then called keoxifene, for breast cancer."], ["1997", "FDA approves raloxifene (Evista) to prevent postmenopausal osteoporosis."], ["1999", "MORE trial reports fewer vertebral fractures with raloxifene."], ["2006", "STAR trial compares raloxifene and tamoxifen for breast cancer prevention."], ["2007", "FDA approves raloxifene to reduce invasive breast cancer risk in selected postmenopausal women."]], "cell": "Raloxifene is a selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM). It binds estrogen receptors alpha and beta with high affinity. Each tissue has its own mix of coactivator and corepressor proteins, and the shape the receptor takes when raloxifene binds decides which proteins it recruits. In bone, the complex acts as an agonist: it reduces osteoclast formation and activity, partly through lowered RANKL signaling and cytokines, so bone resorption slows. In the liver it acts as an agonist, lowering LDL cholesterol and raising clotting factor production. In breast and uterine tissue it acts as an antagonist, blocking estrogen-driven growth. Unlike tamoxifen, it does not stimulate the endometrium.", "body": [["Bone", "Slowing bone resorption increases bone mineral density and reduces vertebral fractures, though it has not been shown to reduce hip fractures."], ["Breast", "Blocking estrogen receptors lowers the risk of invasive estrogen receptor positive breast cancer."], ["Uterus", "It does not stimulate the endometrium, so it does not raise uterine cancer risk."], ["Liver and lipids", "Agonist action lowers LDL cholesterol, but higher clotting factor production raises the risk of blood clots."], ["Veins", "DVT, pulmonary embolism and retinal vein thrombosis are more common, so it is stopped before long immobility."], ["Brain and vessels", "It does not relieve and may cause hot flashes, and in women with heart disease or risk it was linked to more fatal strokes."]], "journey": "Raloxifene is taken by mouth. About 60 percent is absorbed, but heavy first-pass glucuronidation in the gut and liver leaves only about 2 percent bioavailable. The glucuronides are converted back and forth, and the drug cycles through the liver and gut (enterohepatic recirculation), which gives it a long half-life of about 28 hours. It is highly protein bound. It is excreted mainly in feces. Cholestyramine blocks its recirculation and lowers levels. It is not used in pregnancy.", "sidefx": "The higher clot risk comes from estrogen-like effects on the liver that raise clotting factors, which explains DVT, PE and retinal vein thrombosis and is the basis for its boxed warning. The same warning notes more fatal strokes in women with coronary disease or risk factors. Hot flashes happen because it blocks estrogen in the brain's temperature center. Leg cramps and swelling are also common. It is avoided in women with a history of blood clots. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Evista (raloxifene) FDA label", "Ettinger B et al. JAMA 1999 (MORE)", "Vogel VG et al. JAMA 2006 (STAR)"]}, "ramelteon": {"origin": "Ramelteon was discovered and developed by Takeda Pharmaceutical in Japan. Melatonin, the hormone made by the pineal gland at night, was known to signal darkness to the brain's circadian clock, but melatonin supplements have short action and are not regulated as drugs in the US. Takeda designed a synthetic molecule that binds melatonin receptors more strongly and selectively than melatonin itself. The FDA approved it as Rozerem in 2005 for insomnia with trouble falling asleep. It was the first prescription sleep medicine in the US that was not a controlled substance, because it showed no abuse potential and does not act on GABA receptors.", "timeline": [["1958", "Aaron Lerner and colleagues at Yale isolate melatonin from bovine pineal glands."], ["2005", "FDA approves ramelteon (Rozerem) for sleep-onset insomnia."]], "cell": "Ramelteon is a selective agonist at melatonin MT1 and MT2 receptors, which are Gi-coupled receptors concentrated in the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) of the hypothalamus, the body's master clock. MT1 activation inhibits firing of SCN neurons, which reduces the clock's wake-promoting signal and helps start sleep. MT2 activation shifts the timing of the circadian rhythm. Both lower cAMP in target neurons. Ramelteon binds these receptors with higher affinity than melatonin and has little activity at MT3 or at GABA-A, benzodiazepine, dopamine, opioid or other receptors. That is why it shortens the time to fall asleep without the sedation, memory effects, dependence and withdrawal of GABA-based hypnotics.", "body": [["Brain clock", "MT1 and MT2 stimulation in the suprachiasmatic nucleus quiets the wake signal and helps people fall asleep sooner."], ["Alertness", "It causes mild sleepiness and dizziness, but next-day impairment is generally less than with GABA-based hypnotics."], ["Hormones", "Long use may slightly raise prolactin and lower testosterone in some people."], ["Breathing", "It does not depress breathing meaningfully, which makes it an option for people with mild sleep apnea or COPD."]], "journey": "Ramelteon is taken by mouth and is absorbed quickly, but most of it is removed by first-pass liver metabolism, so only a small amount reaches the blood unchanged. A high-fat meal delays absorption, so it is not taken with or right after a heavy meal; it is taken about 30 minutes before bed. CYP1A2 is the main enzyme that breaks it down, with help from CYP2C and CYP3A4. Its active metabolite, M-II, reaches higher levels and lasts longer than the parent drug. The half-life is short, about one to two and a half hours. Metabolites leave mainly in urine. Fluvoxamine, a strong CYP1A2 inhibitor, raises levels sharply and should not be combined.", "sidefx": "Sleepiness, dizziness and fatigue come from its intended effect on the sleep system. Because it does not touch GABA receptors, it has little risk of abuse or rebound insomnia and is not a controlled substance. Hormone effects such as raised prolactin are thought to come from melatonin receptor activity on the reproductive axis. Complex sleep behaviors and allergic reactions, including angioedema, are rare. Severe liver disease raises levels. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Rozerem (ramelteon) FDA label", "Lerner AB et al. J Am Chem Soc 1958"]}, "ramipril": {"origin": "ACE inhibitors began with snake venom. In the 1960s, Brazilian pharmacologist Sergio Ferreira found that venom of the Brazilian pit viper Bothrops jararaca contained peptides that boosted bradykinin, and these peptides were later shown to block angiotensin-converting enzyme. Squibb scientists Miguel Ondetti and David Cushman used that knowledge to design captopril, approved in 1981. Many companies then made longer-acting ACE inhibitors. Ramipril was developed by the German company Hoechst and approved by the FDA in 1991 as Altace. In 2000, the HOPE trial showed that ramipril cut heart attacks, strokes and cardiovascular deaths in high-risk patients even without heart failure, which made it a model for using ACE inhibitors to protect the heart and vessels.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Sergio Ferreira finds bradykinin-potentiating peptides in Bothrops jararaca venom."], ["1981", "Captopril becomes the first ACE inhibitor approved by the FDA."], ["1991", "FDA approves ramipril (Altace)."], ["2000", "HOPE trial shows ramipril reduces cardiovascular events in high-risk patients."]], "cell": "Ramipril is a prodrug. Liver esterases remove an ethyl group to make ramiprilat, the active form. Ramiprilat binds tightly to the zinc-containing active site of angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE), mostly found on lung and vascular endothelium. ACE normally converts angiotensin I into angiotensin II and also breaks down bradykinin. With ACE blocked, angiotensin II falls, so there is less AT1 receptor stimulation: less vasoconstriction, less aldosterone release from the adrenal cortex, less sodium and water retention and less sympathetic drive and tissue remodeling. Bradykinin builds up and adds vasodilation through nitric oxide and prostaglandins. In the kidney, lower angiotensin II relaxes the efferent arteriole, which lowers glomerular pressure and protein loss.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Less angiotensin II and more bradykinin relax arteries and veins, lowering blood pressure and the heart's workload."], ["Heart", "Lower afterload and less angiotensin-driven remodeling help heart failure and reduce heart attacks and deaths in high-risk patients."], ["Kidneys", "Relaxing the efferent arteriole lowers pressure in the glomerulus and protein leak, protecting kidneys in diabetes, but GFR can drop when kidney blood flow is already low."], ["Adrenal glands", "Less aldosterone means less sodium retention and more potassium retention, which can raise blood potassium."], ["Lungs and airway", "Bradykinin buildup can cause a dry cough and, rarely, angioedema of the lips, tongue or throat."]], "journey": "Ramipril is taken by mouth and absorbed fairly well. The liver converts it to ramiprilat, which reaches peak levels in about three to six hours. Ramiprilat binds tightly to ACE in tissues, so it has a long terminal phase that supports once-daily dosing; its effective half-life is about 13 to 17 hours. Most of the drug and its metabolites leave in urine, with some in feces, so kidney disease raises levels. It crosses the placenta, and ACE inhibitors are contraindicated in pregnancy.", "sidefx": "Dry cough comes from bradykinin and substance P building up in the airways. Angioedema, which can block the airway, also comes from bradykinin and is more common in Black patients; it does not respond well to epinephrine or antihistamines, and bradykinin-targeted treatments are sometimes used. High potassium comes from low aldosterone. A rise in creatinine comes from relaxed efferent arterioles, and in bilateral renal artery stenosis kidney function can fall sharply. First-dose low blood pressure happens in salt-depleted patients. In pregnancy, ACE inhibitors harm fetal kidney development, so they carry a boxed warning.", "fun": "Captopril, the first ACE inhibitor, was built from a peptide in pit viper venom, which is why ACE inhibitors are a classic example of drugs inspired by animal toxins.", "src": ["Altace (ramipril) FDA label", "Yusuf S et al. NEJM 2000 (HOPE)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "ranolazine": {"origin": "Ranolazine was first studied in the 1980s by the pharmaceutical company Syntex, which was interested in its ability to protect heart muscle during ischemia. At first researchers thought it worked by changing how the heart used fuel, shifting it from fatty acids to glucose. Development passed to CV Therapeutics in California, which ran the trials needed for approval. The FDA approved it as Ranexa in 2006 for chronic angina. Later research showed that its main action is blocking the late sodium current in heart cells. It was the first new class of antianginal drug in the US in decades and is useful because it does not lower heart rate or blood pressure.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Ranolazine is first studied by Syntex as an anti-ischemic agent."], ["2006", "FDA approves ranolazine (Ranexa) for chronic angina."], ["2007", "MERLIN-TIMI 36 trial studies ranolazine in acute coronary syndrome."]], "cell": "In heart muscle cells, most sodium channels open briefly during phase 0 and then inactivate. A small number reopen or fail to close during the plateau phase, creating a late sodium current (late INa). In ischemia and heart failure, this late current grows. Extra sodium entry raises intracellular sodium, which makes the sodium-calcium exchanger run in reverse and bring calcium in. Calcium overload keeps muscle fibers from relaxing fully in diastole, raising wall tension, squeezing small coronary vessels and increasing oxygen demand. Ranolazine selectively blocks late INa, so sodium and calcium overload fall, diastolic relaxation improves and oxygen use drops. It also blocks IKr potassium current, which mildly lengthens the QT.", "body": [["Heart muscle", "Better relaxation in diastole lowers wall tension and oxygen demand, reducing angina attacks and the need for nitroglycerin."], ["Coronary blood flow", "Lower diastolic wall tension relieves pressure on small vessels inside the heart wall, improving blood flow to the inner muscle layer."], ["Heart rate and blood pressure", "Unlike beta blockers, calcium channel blockers or nitrates, it barely changes heart rate or blood pressure, so it can be added when those are already low."], ["Heart rhythm", "Mild IKr blockade prolongs the QT interval, usually by a small amount."], ["Kidneys", "Acute kidney injury has been reported in people with existing kidney disease."], ["Blood sugar", "It modestly lowers HbA1c in people with diabetes, likely through effects on pancreatic sodium channels and insulin release."]], "journey": "Ranolazine is taken by mouth as an extended-release tablet, which spreads absorption over the day; it should be swallowed whole. Peak levels come about two to five hours after a dose. It is about 62 percent protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A4, with help from CYP2D6, and it is a substrate for P-glycoprotein. It is a weak inhibitor of CYP3A4, CYP2D6 and P-gp, so it can raise levels of simvastatin, digoxin and metformin. Most metabolites leave in urine. The half-life is about seven hours.", "sidefx": "Dizziness, headache, constipation and nausea are the most common side effects. QT prolongation comes from IKr blockade and is related to drug level, which is why strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ketoconazole and clarithromycin are contraindicated and strong inducers make it ineffective. Liver cirrhosis is a contraindication because levels and QT effects increase. Kidney injury can occur in people with poor kidney function, so kidney tests are monitored. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Ranexa (ranolazine) FDA label", "Morrow DA et al. JAMA 2007 (MERLIN-TIMI 36)"]}, "regular-insulin": {"origin": "In 1921, at the University of Toronto, surgeon Frederick Banting and student Charles Best, working in John Macleod's lab, extracted a substance from dog pancreas that lowered blood sugar in diabetic dogs. Biochemist James Collip purified it enough for human use. In January 1922, 14-year-old Leonard Thompson, who was dying of type 1 diabetes, became the first person treated, and he improved dramatically. Banting and Macleod won the 1923 Nobel Prize, and Eli Lilly began large-scale production from animal pancreas. Frederick Sanger worked out insulin's amino acid sequence in the 1950s. In 1982, Humulin, human insulin made in bacteria using recombinant DNA from Genentech and Lilly, became the first recombinant drug approved by the FDA. Regular insulin is the same short-acting human insulin, used under the skin and IV.", "timeline": [["1921", "Banting and Best extract insulin in Macleod's lab in Toronto."], ["1922", "Leonard Thompson becomes the first person treated with insulin."], ["1923", "Banting and Macleod receive the Nobel Prize; Lilly begins commercial insulin production."], ["1950s", "Frederick Sanger determines insulin's amino acid sequence."], ["1982", "Humulin becomes the first recombinant DNA drug approved by the FDA."]], "cell": "Insulin binds the insulin receptor, a receptor tyrosine kinase made of two alpha and two beta subunits. Binding to the alpha subunits makes the beta subunits phosphorylate each other and then insulin receptor substrate (IRS) proteins. IRS turns on the PI3K-Akt pathway, which moves GLUT4 glucose transporters from storage vesicles to the cell surface in skeletal muscle and fat, so glucose enters these cells. Akt also activates glycogen synthase, increases protein and fat synthesis and blocks glucose production in the liver. Insulin inhibits hormone-sensitive lipase in fat, lowering free fatty acid release and ketone production. Separately, insulin stimulates the sodium-potassium ATPase, which moves potassium into cells and lowers blood potassium. The MAPK branch promotes cell growth.", "body": [["Blood glucose", "Glucose moves into muscle and fat, and the liver stops making new glucose, so blood sugar falls."], ["Fat tissue and ketones", "Blocking fat breakdown shuts down ketone production, which is the main reason IV insulin is used in diabetic ketoacidosis."], ["Potassium", "Driving potassium into cells lowers blood potassium, which treats dangerous hyperkalemia but can cause hypokalemia in DKA if potassium is not replaced."], ["Liver", "The liver stores glucose as glycogen and makes fewer ketones and less glucose."], ["Muscle", "It increases protein building and glycogen storage in muscle."], ["Heart", "At high doses insulin improves heart muscle energy use and contractility, which is used in severe beta blocker and calcium channel blocker overdose."]], "journey": "Regular insulin can be given under the skin or IV. Under the skin, the molecules cluster in groups of six (hexamers) and must break apart before entering the blood, so onset takes about 30 to 60 minutes, with peak effect in two to four hours and duration of five to eight hours. This is why it is taken about 30 minutes before meals. IV, it acts immediately and has a half-life of only about five to fifteen minutes, which allows close control in drips. It does not cross the placenta in meaningful amounts. Insulin is broken down mainly by insulin-degrading enzyme in the liver and kidneys, so kidney failure prolongs its effect.", "sidefx": "Hypoglycemia is the main danger, caused by more insulin than food or body needs. Symptoms include shaking, sweating and fast heart rate (adrenergic warning signs) and confusion, seizures and coma (brain glucose shortage). Treatment is oral glucose if awake, or IV dextrose or glucagon if not. Hypokalemia comes from potassium shifting into cells and can cause arrhythmias. Low phosphate and low magnesium can also occur. Weight gain reflects storage of calories. Repeated injection in one spot causes lipohypertrophy, which makes absorption unpredictable. Concentrated forms (U-500) carry a high risk of dosing errors.", "fun": "Leonard Thompson, the first insulin patient, lived for 13 more years after starting treatment; before insulin, children diagnosed with type 1 diabetes usually died within months.", "src": ["Humulin R and Novolin R FDA labels", "Banting FG, Best CH et al. CMAJ 1922", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1923"]}, "remdesivir": {"origin": "Remdesivir (GS-5734) was developed by Gilead Sciences in the 2010s from a library of nucleoside analogs created to fight RNA viruses, including hepatitis C and respiratory viruses. During the 2014 to 2016 West African Ebola outbreak, researchers working with the US Army Medical Research Institute of Infectious Diseases and the CDC found it active against Ebola. In a 2019 trial in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, however, monoclonal antibodies worked better. When COVID-19 emerged in early 2020, lab studies showed remdesivir blocked SARS-CoV-2. The NIH ACTT-1 trial found it shortened recovery in hospitalized patients. The FDA issued an emergency use authorization in May 2020 and fully approved it as Veklury in October 2020, making it the first approved COVID-19 treatment in the US.", "timeline": [["2010s", "Gilead develops remdesivir from its nucleoside analog program."], ["2016", "Remdesivir is reported to protect monkeys from Ebola virus disease."], ["2020", "ACTT-1 trial shows faster recovery in hospitalized COVID-19 patients."], ["2020", "FDA approves remdesivir (Veklury) in October, the first approved COVID-19 drug."]], "cell": "Remdesivir is a phosphoramidate prodrug of an adenosine nucleotide analog. The prodrug design lets it cross cell membranes and skip the slow first phosphorylation step. Inside cells, esterases and phosphoramidases remove the protecting groups, and cellular kinases add phosphates to form the active triphosphate (GS-443902). This molecule looks like ATP, so the SARS-CoV-2 RNA-dependent RNA polymerase (nsp12) adds it to the growing viral RNA strand. After a few more nucleotides are added, its 1'-cyano group clashes with the polymerase and stalls RNA synthesis (delayed chain termination). The delay also helps it avoid removal by the virus's proofreading exonuclease. With RNA copying blocked, the virus cannot replicate.", "body": [["Lungs and airways", "Less viral replication in airway cells can shorten illness and reduce progression to severe disease when started early."], ["Liver", "Mild to moderate rises in liver enzymes are common, so liver tests are checked."], ["Heart", "Slow heart rate (bradycardia) has been reported during and after infusion."], ["Immune system", "Infusion-related reactions and, rarely, anaphylaxis can occur."], ["Gut", "Nausea is one of the more common side effects."]], "journey": "Remdesivir is given IV because it is poorly absorbed by mouth and heavily broken down by the liver. In the blood it is quickly taken up by cells and converted to its active triphosphate. The parent drug has a half-life of about one hour, but the active form stays inside cells much longer, which supports once-daily dosing. The main circulating metabolite, GS-441524, is cleared by the kidneys with a long half-life. Part of the drug is metabolized by esterases and to a lesser extent by CYP enzymes. The IV formulation contains a sugar-based solubilizer (SBECD) that is also cleared by the kidneys.", "sidefx": "Liver enzyme rises likely reflect direct liver stress from the drug and its metabolites. Bradycardia may relate to the adenosine-like structure of the nucleotide. Infusion reactions, including low blood pressure, nausea and rash, and rarely anaphylaxis, can occur, so patients are monitored. Chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine may reduce its antiviral activity, so they are not combined. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Veklury (remdesivir) FDA label", "Beigel JH et al. NEJM 2020 (ACTT-1)"]}, "reteplase": {"origin": "Natural tissue plasminogen activator (tPA) is made by the inner lining of blood vessels. Alteplase, recombinant tPA from Genentech, was approved in 1987 for heart attacks, but it has a very short half-life and must be given as a long infusion. Researchers at Boehringer Mannheim in Germany engineered a shorter version by removing parts of the protein that the liver uses to clear it. The result, reteplase (r-PA), is made in E. coli bacteria and lasts longer in the blood, so it can be given as two quick IV boluses. The FDA approved it as Retavase in 1996 for acute heart attack. In the GUSTO-III trial, published in 1997, it worked about as well as alteplase. Its bolus dosing made it popular for prehospital and emergency use.", "timeline": [["1987", "Alteplase becomes the first recombinant tPA approved for heart attack."], ["1996", "FDA approves reteplase (Retavase) for acute myocardial infarction."], ["1997", "GUSTO-III trial compares reteplase with alteplase."]], "cell": "Reteplase is a deletion mutant of human tPA. Full tPA has five regions: a finger domain, an epidermal growth factor domain, kringle 1, kringle 2 and the protease domain. Reteplase keeps only kringle 2 and the protease domain. Removing the finger and growth factor domains reduces clearance by liver receptors, which lengthens its half-life. Removing the finger domain also lowers how tightly it binds fibrin, so it is less fibrin specific than alteplase and moves through clots more freely. Its protease domain cuts plasminogen at the arginine 561 to valine 562 bond, making plasmin. Plasmin breaks down the fibrin mesh holding the clot together, which reopens the blocked artery. Because it is made in bacteria, it is not glycosylated.", "body": [["Coronary arteries", "Breaking down fibrin in the clot reopens a blocked coronary artery during STEMI, restoring blood flow to the heart muscle."], ["Heart muscle", "Faster reperfusion saves heart muscle, but sudden restored flow can cause reperfusion arrhythmias such as accelerated idioventricular rhythm."], ["Blood clotting", "Plasmin also breaks down circulating fibrinogen and clotting factors, which raises bleeding risk everywhere in the body."], ["Brain", "Intracranial hemorrhage is the most feared complication, more likely in older patients and those with high blood pressure."], ["Puncture sites", "Bleeding from IV sites, gums and recent wounds is common."]], "journey": "Reteplase is given only IV, as two boluses spaced about 30 minutes apart. It works within minutes, and artery reopening is usually seen within 30 to 90 minutes. Its half-life is about 13 to 16 minutes, several times longer than alteplase, which is why it can be given as boluses. It is cleared mainly by the kidneys and liver. Its clot-dissolving effect on fibrinogen and clotting factors lasts for hours after the drug is gone.", "sidefx": "Bleeding is the main risk because plasmin cannot tell a harmful clot from one that is sealing a blood vessel. This is why recent surgery, stroke, active bleeding, severe uncontrolled high blood pressure and brain tumors are contraindications. Intracranial hemorrhage is the most dangerous outcome. Reperfusion arrhythmias are usually short-lived. There is no specific reversal agent; serious bleeding is treated by stopping anticoagulants and giving cryoprecipitate (to replace fibrinogen), fresh frozen plasma, platelets and antifibrinolytics such as tranexamic acid.", "src": ["Retavase (reteplase) FDA label", "GUSTO-III Investigators. NEJM 1997"]}, "ribavirin": {"origin": "Ribavirin was first synthesized in 1970 by Joseph Witkowski and Roland Robins at ICN Pharmaceuticals in California. In 1972, ICN scientists reported in Science that it was active against a wide range of RNA and DNA viruses in the lab, an unusual broad-spectrum result. ICN marketed it under the name Virazole. In the mid-1980s, the FDA approved inhaled ribavirin for severe RSV infections in hospitalized infants. Also in the 1980s, Joseph McCormick and colleagues showed that early IV ribavirin lowered death rates from Lassa fever in Sierra Leone. Its biggest role came in 1998, when it was approved in combination with interferon alfa for chronic hepatitis C. Direct-acting antivirals have since replaced it for most hepatitis C care.", "timeline": [["1970", "Joseph Witkowski and Roland Robins synthesize ribavirin at ICN Pharmaceuticals."], ["1972", "Broad-spectrum antiviral activity of ribavirin is reported in Science."], ["1980s", "Aerosolized ribavirin is approved for severe RSV in infants."], ["1986", "McCormick and colleagues report ribavirin reduces deaths in Lassa fever."], ["1998", "Ribavirin with interferon alfa is approved for chronic hepatitis C."]], "cell": "Ribavirin is a guanosine analog. Cellular kinases convert it to its monophosphate, diphosphate and triphosphate forms. It acts in several ways. The monophosphate competitively inhibits inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase (IMPDH), the enzyme needed to make guanine nucleotides, which drains the cell's GTP pool so viruses lack building blocks for RNA. The triphosphate competes with GTP at viral RNA-dependent RNA polymerases. When polymerases add ribavirin to new viral RNA, it can pair with either cytosine or uracil, causing many mutations, so the virus population accumulates so many errors that it collapses (lethal mutagenesis). It also interferes with mRNA capping and shifts the immune response toward a Th1 antiviral pattern.", "body": [["Liver", "In hepatitis C, combined with interferon or other antivirals, it raised cure rates and lowered relapse."], ["Lungs", "Inhaled ribavirin is used rarely for severe RSV in high-risk infants and for RSV in some transplant patients."], ["Red blood cells", "Ribavirin triphosphate builds up inside red cells, which lack the enzymes to remove it, causing ATP depletion, oxidative damage and hemolytic anemia."], ["Heart", "Anemia from hemolysis can worsen heart disease and trigger angina or heart attack in people with coronary disease."], ["Pregnancy", "It is strongly teratogenic and embryocidal in animals, so pregnancy must be avoided in patients and their partners."]], "journey": "Ribavirin can be taken by mouth or inhaled as a fine aerosol. Oral absorption is fairly good and is improved by a high-fat meal. It does not bind plasma proteins and spreads widely into tissues. It is taken up and trapped inside red blood cells as phosphates, where it can remain for weeks. The body clears it slowly by phosphorylation, deribosylation and amide hydrolysis, and the kidneys excrete the drug and metabolites. At steady state its half-life is very long, around 12 days, and it can stay in the body for months after stopping, which is why pregnancy must be avoided for months afterward.", "sidefx": "Hemolytic anemia is the main dose-limiting toxicity, caused by ribavirin triphosphate building up in red cells and lowering ATP, which damages their membranes. Fatigue, cough, shortness of breath and insomnia are common, especially with interferon. It is a boxed-warning teratogen; health workers who are pregnant should avoid exposure to the aerosol. The aerosol can cause bronchospasm and can deposit on ventilator valves. There is no antidote; anemia is managed with dose reduction and, if needed, transfusion.", "src": ["Rebetol and Virazole (ribavirin) FDA labels", "Sidwell RW et al. Science 1972", "McCormick JB et al. NEJM 1986"]}, "rifampin": {"origin": "In 1957, researchers Piero Sensi and Pinhas Margalith at Lepetit Pharmaceuticals in Milan, Italy, studied a soil sample from a pine forest on the French Riviera. It contained a bacterium, now called Amycolatopsis rifamycinica (originally Streptomyces mediterranei), that made a group of antibiotics. The team named them rifamycins after the 1955 French heist film Rififi. The natural rifamycins worked poorly by mouth, so chemists modified them, and in 1965 they made rifampicin, which was well absorbed orally and very active against tuberculosis. It was introduced in Italy in 1968 and approved in the US in 1971 as Rifadin. Combined with isoniazid, it allowed TB treatment to be shortened dramatically, and it remains a cornerstone of TB therapy.", "timeline": [["1957", "Sensi and Margalith at Lepetit isolate rifamycins from a soil bacterium."], ["1965", "Rifampicin is synthesized as an orally active rifamycin."], ["1968", "Rifampicin is introduced in Italy."], ["1971", "FDA approves rifampin in the US."]], "cell": "Rifampin binds the beta subunit of bacterial DNA-dependent RNA polymerase, encoded by the rpoB gene. It sits in a pocket next to the enzyme's active site, in the path the new RNA strand must take, and physically blocks the RNA chain once it reaches two or three nucleotides. Transcription cannot proceed, protein synthesis stops and the bacterium dies (bactericidal). Human RNA polymerase has a different structure, so it is not affected at normal levels. A single mutation in rpoB can change the binding pocket and cause resistance, which develops rapidly if rifampin is used alone, so it is always combined with other drugs for active infections. In human liver cells, rifampin activates the pregnane X receptor (PXR), which turns on genes for many CYP enzymes and P-glycoprotein.", "body": [["Lungs and TB infection", "Kills actively dividing and semi-dormant TB bacilli, making it essential for short-course TB therapy."], ["Other infections", "Used with other drugs for staph infections on implanted hardware, to prevent meningococcal disease in close contacts and for leprosy."], ["Liver", "It can cause hepatitis, especially with isoniazid or in people with liver disease."], ["Drug metabolism", "Strong induction of CYP3A4, CYP2C9 and other enzymes speeds breakdown of many drugs, including warfarin, birth control pills, HIV drugs and antiseizure medicines."], ["Body fluids", "It colors urine, sweat, tears and saliva orange-red and can permanently stain soft contact lenses."], ["Blood", "Thrombocytopenia and, with intermittent dosing, a flu-like immune syndrome can occur."]], "journey": "Rifampin is taken by mouth, best on an empty stomach, and is absorbed well. It can also be given IV. It spreads into most tissues and fluids, including the cerebrospinal fluid when the meninges are inflamed, and into macrophages. The liver deacetylates it to an active metabolite. It induces its own metabolism (autoinduction), so its half-life falls from about three to five hours to about two to three hours over the first weeks. Most of it is excreted in bile and feces, with part in urine.", "sidefx": "Hepatotoxicity comes from liver metabolism and is worse with isoniazid. Drug interactions are the most common practical problem: by activating PXR and inducing CYP enzymes and P-gp, rifampin can lower levels of other drugs enough to cause transplant rejection, contraceptive failure, clotting with warfarin underdosing or HIV treatment failure. The orange discoloration is harmless. Intermittent dosing can trigger immune reactions with fever, flu-like symptoms, thrombocytopenia, hemolysis or kidney injury. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The rifamycins were named after Rififi, a 1955 French heist movie that the research team enjoyed.", "src": ["Rifadin (rifampin) FDA label", "Sensi P. Rev Infect Dis 1983 (history of rifamycins)"]}, "risperidone": {"origin": "Risperidone was developed by Janssen Pharmaceutica in Belgium, the company founded by Paul Janssen, who had earlier discovered haloperidol. By the 1980s, clozapine had shown that a drug blocking serotonin 5-HT2A receptors as well as dopamine D2 receptors could treat psychosis with fewer movement side effects, but clozapine caused agranulocytosis. Janssen researchers combined strong 5-HT2A blockade with D2 blockade in a new benzisoxazole molecule. The FDA approved risperidone as Risperdal in 1993, making it one of the first second-generation antipsychotics after clozapine. In 2003, Risperdal Consta became the first long-acting injectable second-generation antipsychotic in the US. Its active metabolite, paliperidone, was later marketed as a separate drug.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Janssen Pharmaceutica develops risperidone in Belgium."], ["1993", "FDA approves risperidone (Risperdal) for schizophrenia."], ["2003", "FDA approves Risperdal Consta, a long-acting injectable form."], ["2006", "Risperidone is approved for irritability in autism in children and teens."]], "cell": "Risperidone blocks serotonin 5-HT2A receptors and dopamine D2 receptors, with higher affinity for 5-HT2A. Blocking D2 receptors in the mesolimbic pathway reduces positive symptoms such as hallucinations and delusions. Blocking 5-HT2A receptors increases dopamine release in the nigrostriatal and prefrontal areas, which offsets some D2 blockade in motor circuits and may help negative symptoms and mood. However, risperidone binds D2 receptors tightly, so at higher doses it acts much like a first-generation drug, with more movement side effects. D2 blockade in the tuberoinfundibular pathway removes dopamine's brake on prolactin, causing high prolactin. It also blocks alpha-1 and H1 receptors, which causes orthostatic hypotension and some sedation, and it has little anticholinergic activity.", "body": [["Brain (thought and mood)", "Reduces hallucinations, delusions and disorganized thinking, calms mania and reduces severe irritability in autism."], ["Pituitary", "Blocking dopamine raises prolactin more than most second-generation drugs, which can cause breast enlargement, milk discharge, missed periods and sexual dysfunction."], ["Motor system", "Higher doses cause more extrapyramidal symptoms such as stiffness, tremor and restlessness."], ["Metabolism", "Weight gain, high blood sugar and abnormal lipids can occur."], ["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 blockade causes dizziness and orthostatic hypotension, especially when starting."]], "journey": "Risperidone is taken by mouth as tablets, dissolving tablets or liquid, or as a long-acting injection. Oral absorption is good, with peak levels in about an hour. The liver converts it mainly by CYP2D6 into 9-hydroxyrisperidone (paliperidone), which is equally active. Fast CYP2D6 metabolizers have more paliperidone and slow ones more risperidone, but the total active level is similar. Combined active half-life is about 20 hours. Paliperidone is cleared mainly by the kidneys, so kidney disease raises levels. Long-acting injections release drug slowly over weeks.", "sidefx": "High prolactin is common because risperidone and paliperidone bind pituitary D2 receptors tightly and paliperidone crosses the blood-brain barrier less, acting strongly on the pituitary, which sits outside it. Extrapyramidal symptoms come from nigrostriatal D2 blockade and increase with dose. Metabolic effects reflect receptor actions on appetite and insulin sensitivity. Orthostatic hypotension comes from alpha-1 blockade. Rarely, neuroleptic malignant syndrome or tardive dyskinesia occurs. Like all antipsychotics, it carries a boxed warning for higher death rates in older adults with dementia-related psychosis. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Risperdal (risperidone) FDA label", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology"]}, "ritonavir": {"origin": "Ritonavir was developed by Abbott Laboratories in the early 1990s as part of the race to block HIV protease, an enzyme the virus needs to mature. The FDA approved it as Norvir in 1996, after a review of only about 72 days, during the period when protease inhibitors made combination HIV therapy possible. In 1998, a new crystal form of ritonavir appeared in Abbott's factories that dissolved poorly, and the capsules had to be pulled and reformulated, a famous case of polymorphism in drug manufacturing. Ritonavir caused many side effects at full doses, but researchers found that small doses strongly blocked CYP3A4, raising levels of other protease inhibitors. This boosting role made it part of lopinavir/ritonavir (Kaletra) and, in 2021, nirmatrelvir/ritonavir (Paxlovid) for COVID-19.", "timeline": [["1996", "FDA approves ritonavir (Norvir) for HIV infection."], ["1998", "A new, less soluble crystal form forces reformulation of ritonavir capsules."], ["2000", "Lopinavir/ritonavir (Kaletra) is approved, using ritonavir as a booster."], ["2021", "Nirmatrelvir with ritonavir (Paxlovid) receives emergency use authorization for COVID-19."], ["2023", "FDA approves Paxlovid for COVID-19 in high-risk adults."]], "cell": "HIV makes long gag-pol polyproteins that must be cut into individual structural proteins and enzymes by HIV-1 protease, an aspartyl protease that works as a dimer. Ritonavir is a peptidomimetic that fits into the protease active site and blocks it. Without cleavage, new virus particles bud from the cell but stay immature and cannot infect other cells. Ritonavir's most important use today relies on a second action: it is a potent mechanism-based inhibitor of CYP3A4. Its thiazole group binds the heme iron of CYP3A4 and inactivates the enzyme. It also inhibits P-glycoprotein. Blocking CYP3A4 in the gut and liver slows breakdown of partner drugs such as darunavir, lopinavir and nirmatrelvir, raising their levels and prolonging their action.", "body": [["Immune system", "As part of HIV therapy, blocking viral maturation lowers viral load and lets CD4 T cells recover."], ["Drug levels", "Strong CYP3A4 inhibition raises levels of many other medicines, including statins, sedatives, antiarrhythmics and some opioids, causing serious toxicity."], ["Gut", "Nausea, diarrhea, vomiting and taste changes are common, especially at higher doses."], ["Metabolism", "Protease inhibitors can raise triglycerides, cholesterol and blood sugar and cause fat redistribution."], ["Liver and pancreas", "Hepatitis and pancreatitis can occur, the latter often linked to very high triglycerides."], ["Nerves", "Tingling around the mouth and in the hands and feet can occur."]], "journey": "Ritonavir is taken by mouth as tablets or liquid, and taking it with food improves tolerance. It is almost completely bound to plasma proteins. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP3A4 and CYP2D6, and it inhibits CYP3A4 while also inducing some other enzymes, such as CYP1A2 and CYP2C9, and glucuronidation. That is why it can lower levels of some drugs, such as ethinyl estradiol. It is excreted mostly in feces. The half-life is about three to five hours, but CYP3A4 inhibition lasts longer because the enzyme must be newly made.", "sidefx": "The most dangerous problem is drug interactions from CYP3A4 inhibition. Combined with simvastatin or lovastatin it can cause rhabdomyolysis; with midazolam or triazolam, prolonged sedation; with amiodarone or other antiarrhythmics, dangerous rhythms; with fentanyl, breathing depression. GI side effects come from direct irritation and are worse at full antiviral doses. Lipid and sugar changes relate to protease inhibitor effects on fat and insulin signaling. It can prolong the PR interval. There is no antidote, so interactions are prevented by checking every medicine before starting it.", "fun": "Ritonavir's 1998 crystal form crisis, when a more stable, less soluble form suddenly appeared and could not be eliminated, is still taught as a classic warning in pharmaceutical chemistry.", "src": ["Norvir (ritonavir) FDA label", "Paxlovid (nirmatrelvir and ritonavir) FDA label", "Bauer J et al. Pharm Res 2001 (ritonavir polymorphism)"]}, "rituximab": {"origin": "Rituximab grew out of research on B-cell lymphoma antibodies by Ronald Levy at Stanford and others in the 1980s. IDEC Pharmaceuticals in San Diego created a chimeric antibody, IDEC-C2B8, by joining the binding regions of a mouse antibody against the B-cell protein CD20 with the constant region of a human IgG1 antibody. This made it less likely to be rejected by the human immune system and better at recruiting human immune cells. IDEC partnered with Genentech, and in 1997 the FDA approved it as Rituxan for relapsed low-grade B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma, the first monoclonal antibody approved to treat cancer. It later gained approvals for chronic lymphocytic leukemia, rheumatoid arthritis, ANCA vasculitis and pemphigus vulgaris.", "timeline": [["1975", "Kohler and Milstein develop hybridoma technology for making monoclonal antibodies."], ["1997", "FDA approves rituximab (Rituxan), the first monoclonal antibody for cancer."], ["2006", "Rituximab is approved for rheumatoid arthritis."], ["2011", "Rituximab is approved for ANCA-associated vasculitis."], ["2018", "Rituximab is approved for pemphigus vulgaris."]], "cell": "CD20 is a membrane protein found on pre-B cells and mature B cells but not on stem cells, pro-B cells or plasma cells. Rituximab is a chimeric mouse-human IgG1 antibody whose variable regions bind CD20. Once it coats a B cell, it kills the cell in three main ways. Its Fc region binds Fc-gamma receptors on natural killer cells and macrophages, which kill the cell by antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity and phagocytosis. It activates the classical complement pathway, punching holes in the membrane (complement-dependent cytotoxicity). It also triggers direct apoptosis signals through CD20 clustering. Because stem cells lack CD20, B cells regrow after treatment, and because plasma cells lack it, existing antibody levels fall only slowly.", "body": [["B cells", "Circulating B cells disappear within days to weeks and usually stay depleted for six to twelve months."], ["Lymphoma and leukemia", "Killing malignant CD20-positive B cells shrinks lymph nodes and tumors, especially when combined with chemotherapy."], ["Autoimmune disease", "Removing B cells lowers autoantibody production, antigen presentation and cytokine release, easing rheumatoid arthritis, vasculitis and pemphigus."], ["Immune defense", "Low antibody levels (hypogammaglobulinemia) and B-cell loss increase infection risk, including reactivation of hepatitis B and rare progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML)."], ["Infusion reactions", "The first infusion often causes fever, chills, rigors and low blood pressure as dying B cells release cytokines."], ["Kidneys and metabolism", "Rapid killing of large tumor masses can cause tumor lysis syndrome with high potassium, phosphate and uric acid."]], "journey": "Rituximab is given as an IV infusion, and a form combined with hyaluronidase can be given under the skin. It stays mainly in the blood and lymphatic tissue and does not enter the brain well. Like other IgG antibodies, it is broken down by protein catabolism throughout the body rather than by liver enzymes or the kidneys. Binding to CD20 also removes it (target-mediated clearance), so people with a large tumor burden clear it faster. Its half-life is about three weeks. B-cell depletion lasts long after the drug is gone. It crosses the placenta, especially later in pregnancy, and can deplete B cells in the newborn.", "sidefx": "Infusion reactions come from cytokine release when the antibody kills many B cells at once, which is why the first infusion is given slowly with premedication such as acetaminophen, antihistamines and steroids. Hepatitis B reactivation happens because B cells and antibodies help keep the virus under control, so hepatitis B screening is required. PML is caused by JC virus reactivation in the brain. Late-onset neutropenia can appear weeks to months after treatment. Severe skin reactions like SJS/TEN are rare. These risks are listed in its boxed warning. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The -ximab ending marks a chimeric antibody, part mouse and part human, while -umab means fully human and -zumab means humanized.", "src": ["Rituxan (rituximab) FDA label", "Maloney DG et al. Blood 1997"]}, "rivaroxaban": {"origin": "For more than 50 years, warfarin was the main oral anticoagulant, but it needed frequent blood tests and had many food and drug interactions. Researchers at Bayer in Germany set out to make a small molecule that would directly block factor Xa, the enzyme where the intrinsic and extrinsic clotting pathways meet. Their compound, rivaroxaban, became the first oral direct factor Xa inhibitor approved in the US. The FDA approved it as Xarelto in 2011, first for preventing blood clots after hip and knee replacement, then later that year for stroke prevention in atrial fibrillation after the ROCKET AF trial. It is marketed in the US by Janssen.", "timeline": [["2008", "Rivaroxaban is first approved in Canada and Europe."], ["2011", "FDA approves rivaroxaban (Xarelto) for clot prevention after joint replacement and for atrial fibrillation."], ["2011", "ROCKET AF trial results are published."]], "cell": "Rivaroxaban binds directly and reversibly to the active site of factor Xa, both free in the blood and bound within the prothrombinase complex on platelet surfaces and in clots. It does not need antithrombin, unlike heparin and fondaparinux. Factor Xa, together with factor Va, converts prothrombin (factor II) into thrombin. By blocking Xa, rivaroxaban sharply reduces the thrombin burst that normally follows tissue injury. Less thrombin means less conversion of fibrinogen to fibrin, less activation of platelets and factors V, VIII and XI, and smaller clots. It does not directly affect platelets.", "body": [["Veins", "Fewer and smaller venous clots prevent and treat deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism."], ["Heart and brain", "In atrial fibrillation, preventing clots in the left atrial appendage lowers stroke risk."], ["Coronary and leg arteries", "At low doses with aspirin, it reduces major cardiovascular events in some people with stable coronary or peripheral artery disease."], ["Gut", "GI bleeding is the most common serious bleeding site."], ["Brain", "Intracranial hemorrhage is less common than with warfarin but can be fatal."], ["Spine", "Epidural or spinal hematomas can occur with neuraxial anesthesia or spinal puncture, leading to a boxed warning."]], "journey": "Rivaroxaban is taken by mouth. Higher doses must be taken with food to be well absorbed. It peaks in about two to four hours, so its effect begins quickly. It is highly protein bound, so dialysis does not remove it. About two thirds is metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4 and CYP2J2 and by non-CYP pathways, and it is a P-glycoprotein substrate. About one third is excreted unchanged by the kidneys, and the rest leaves in urine and feces as metabolites. The half-life is about five to nine hours in younger adults and eleven to thirteen hours in older adults. Strong inhibitors of both CYP3A4 and P-gp raise its levels.", "sidefx": "Bleeding is the main risk because blocking factor Xa limits clot formation everywhere. Kidney and liver problems and interacting drugs raise levels and bleeding risk. Stopping it early without a substitute raises the risk of clots and stroke, which is part of its boxed warning, along with spinal hematoma. Activated charcoal can help if a dose was taken within a few hours. For life-threatening bleeding, four-factor prothrombin complex concentrate (4F-PCC) is the usual reversal in the US; andexanet alfa was withdrawn from the US market in 2025.", "src": ["Xarelto (rivaroxaban) FDA label", "Patel MR et al. NEJM 2011 (ROCKET AF)"]}, "rivastigmine": {"origin": "Rivastigmine was designed by pharmacologist Marta Weinstock-Rosin and colleagues at the Hebrew University of Jerusalem in the 1980s. They started from physostigmine, a natural cholinesterase inhibitor from the Calabar bean of West Africa, and made carbamate derivatives that could enter the brain and last longer. The compound was licensed to Sandoz (later Novartis) and developed as a treatment for Alzheimer disease, where loss of acetylcholine-producing neurons contributes to memory loss. The FDA approved it as Exelon capsules in 2000. In 2006, it became the first drug approved in the US for dementia associated with Parkinson disease, and in 2007, the Exelon patch became the first skin patch approved for dementia.", "timeline": [["1860s", "Physostigmine is isolated from the Calabar bean."], ["1980s", "Marta Weinstock-Rosin's group at Hebrew University develops rivastigmine."], ["2000", "FDA approves rivastigmine (Exelon) for Alzheimer disease."], ["2006", "Rivastigmine is approved for dementia with Parkinson disease."], ["2007", "FDA approves the Exelon transdermal patch."]], "cell": "In Alzheimer disease, cholinergic neurons in the basal forebrain that project to the cortex and hippocampus are lost early, so less acetylcholine is available for memory and attention circuits. Rivastigmine is a carbamate that inhibits both acetylcholinesterase (AChE) and butyrylcholinesterase (BuChE). It binds the enzymes' active site, and the enzyme cleaves it, leaving a carbamyl group attached to the catalytic serine. The carbamylated enzyme recovers only slowly, over hours, which is called pseudo-irreversible inhibition. Its phenolic leaving product is inactive. With the enzymes blocked, acetylcholine lasts longer in brain synapses, boosting signaling through remaining cholinergic pathways. BuChE becomes more important in advanced Alzheimer disease, which may add to its effect.", "body": [["Brain", "More acetylcholine in memory and attention circuits produces modest improvement or slower decline in thinking, daily function and behavior in mild to moderate dementia."], ["Gut", "Increased cholinergic activity in the gut and brainstem vomiting centers causes nausea, vomiting, diarrhea and weight loss, especially with capsules."], ["Heart", "Increased vagal tone can slow the heart and cause fainting, especially with beta blockers."], ["Lungs", "Bronchial constriction and secretions can increase, which matters in asthma or COPD."], ["Bladder", "Cholinergic stimulation can increase urinary frequency or incontinence."], ["Motor system", "It may worsen tremor in Parkinson disease."]], "journey": "Rivastigmine is taken by mouth or delivered through a daily skin patch. Oral absorption is fast and good, with peak levels in about an hour, and taking it with food lowers GI upset. The patch gives steadier levels with lower peaks, which means fewer GI side effects. It crosses the blood-brain barrier easily. It is broken down mainly by the cholinesterases it inhibits, not by liver CYP enzymes, so it has few CYP drug interactions. Metabolites leave mostly in urine. Its plasma half-life is short, about one and a half hours, but enzyme inhibition lasts much longer.", "sidefx": "Side effects reflect extra acetylcholine outside the target circuits. Nausea and vomiting are the most common and are why the dose is raised slowly; a gap in treatment of several days means restarting at a low dose. Bradycardia and syncope come from vagal stimulation. Patch errors, such as forgetting to remove an old patch and applying several at once, have caused severe cholinergic toxicity. Cholinergic crisis with sweating, salivation, vomiting, bradycardia and weakness can be treated with atropine.", "src": ["Exelon (rivastigmine) capsules and patch FDA labels", "Weinstock M. CNS Drugs 1999"]}, "rocuronium": {"origin": "Neuromuscular blockers began with curare, the arrow poison of South American Indigenous peoples, made from plants including Chondrodendron tomentosum. Tubocurarine was first used in anesthesia in 1942 by Harold Griffith in Montreal. Later, chemists at Organon in the Netherlands and Scotland built synthetic aminosteroid blockers, including pancuronium and vecuronium. They needed a non-depolarizing drug with a fast onset that could replace succinylcholine for rapid sequence intubation without its risks of high potassium and malignant hyperthermia. Changing the structure of vecuronium produced rocuronium, which acts faster because it is less potent, so more molecules reach the junction quickly. The FDA approved it as Zemuron in 1994. In 2015, sugammadex, a drug designed to trap rocuronium, was approved.", "timeline": [["1942", "Harold Griffith first uses curare to relax muscles during anesthesia."], ["1960s", "Organon develops pancuronium, the first aminosteroid neuromuscular blocker."], ["1994", "FDA approves rocuronium (Zemuron)."], ["2015", "FDA approves sugammadex for reversal of rocuronium and vecuronium."]], "cell": "At the neuromuscular junction, acetylcholine released from the motor nerve binds nicotinic receptors (Nm type) on the muscle end plate. Each receptor is a ligand-gated cation channel with five subunits; two acetylcholine molecules must bind the alpha subunits to open it. Sodium rushes in, the end plate depolarizes and an action potential spreads, triggering contraction. Rocuronium is a competitive antagonist: it binds the alpha subunits without opening the channel, so acetylcholine cannot bind. With enough receptors blocked, the end plate cannot reach threshold, and the muscle stays relaxed (flaccid paralysis) without fasciculations. Because it is competitive, raising acetylcholine with neostigmine can overcome it. Sugammadex, a ring-shaped cyclodextrin, wraps around rocuronium molecules in the blood and pulls them away from the junction.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Relaxation of all skeletal muscles, including the jaw and vocal cords, makes intubation and surgery easier. Small fast-moving muscles of the eyes and face are affected first, then the limbs and trunk, and the diaphragm last."], ["Breathing", "The diaphragm and chest muscles are paralyzed, so the patient cannot breathe and needs ventilation."], ["Brain", "It has no effect on consciousness or pain, so sedation and analgesia must be given to prevent awareness while paralyzed."], ["Heart", "It has mild vagal blocking activity, which can slightly raise heart rate."], ["Immune system", "It is one of the more common causes of anaphylaxis during anesthesia."]], "journey": "Rocuronium is given IV. Higher intubating doses produce good conditions within about a minute, close to succinylcholine. It is a charged molecule that stays in the extracellular fluid and does not cross the blood-brain barrier or placenta much. It is taken up by the liver and excreted mainly unchanged in bile, with some in urine. Its duration depends on dose, often about 30 to 60 minutes or longer after intubating doses. Liver disease and old age prolong its effect. Reversal is with sugammadex, or with neostigmine plus glycopyrrolate once some recovery has started.", "sidefx": "Its main danger comes from its intended effect: a patient who cannot be intubated or ventilated is paralyzed and cannot breathe, which is why airway planning and reversal agents must be ready. Awareness under paralysis happens when sedation is inadequate. Prolonged weakness can occur in liver disease and with drugs such as aminoglycosides and magnesium, and in ICU use. Anaphylaxis is rare but serious. Sugammadex encapsulates rocuronium for rapid reversal even from deep block.", "fun": "The name rocuronium is often explained as rapid onset curonium, reflecting its design goal of fast onset to replace succinylcholine.", "src": ["Rocuronium bromide injection FDA label", "Bridion (sugammadex) FDA label", "Griffith HR, Johnson GE. Anesthesiology 1942"]}, "ropinirole": {"origin": "Ropinirole was developed by SmithKline Beecham (now GSK) as a non-ergot dopamine agonist. Like pramipexole, it was designed to avoid the ergot structure of bromocriptine and pergolide, which was linked to fibrosis of the lungs and heart valves. It has an indolone structure that mimics part of the dopamine molecule. The FDA approved it as Requip for Parkinson disease in 1997. In 2005, it became the first drug approved by the FDA for restless legs syndrome. A once-daily extended-release form, Requip XL, followed for Parkinson disease.", "timeline": [["1997", "FDA approves ropinirole (Requip) for Parkinson disease."], ["2005", "Ropinirole becomes the first FDA-approved treatment for restless legs syndrome."], ["2008", "Extended-release ropinirole (Requip XL) is approved for Parkinson disease."]], "cell": "Ropinirole is a full agonist at dopamine D2-family receptors, with highest affinity for D3, then D2, and little action at D1 or non-dopamine receptors. In the striatum, D2 receptors sit on medium spiny neurons of the indirect pathway. They are Gi-coupled, so activation lowers cAMP and makes these neurons less active. Less indirect pathway activity reduces inhibition of the thalamus, so motor signals to the cortex flow more easily. Because it acts directly on receptors, it does not need surviving dopamine neurons or conversion by dopa decarboxylase. In restless legs syndrome, it is thought to correct low dopamine signaling in spinal and subcortical circuits. D3 activity in limbic reward circuits explains its impulse-control side effects.", "body": [["Brain motor circuits", "Improves slowness, stiffness and tremor in Parkinson disease, and can delay or reduce levodopa needs in early disease."], ["Legs and sensory circuits", "Relieves the uncomfortable urge to move the legs at night in restless legs syndrome."], ["Brain reward circuits", "Can cause compulsive gambling, shopping, eating or sexual behavior."], ["Sleep and alertness", "Causes sleepiness and sudden sleep attacks, so driving needs caution."], ["Blood vessels", "Lowers sympathetic tone, causing dizziness and orthostatic hypotension when standing."], ["Gut", "Nausea comes from stimulation of the chemoreceptor trigger zone."]], "journey": "Ropinirole is taken by mouth and absorbed quickly, with peak levels in one to two hours. First-pass metabolism leaves about half the dose available. It crosses the blood-brain barrier and is about 40 percent protein bound. Unlike pramipexole, which is cleared by the kidneys, ropinirole is broken down mainly in the liver by CYP1A2 into inactive metabolites that leave in urine. Its half-life is about six hours. CYP1A2 inhibitors such as ciprofloxacin and fluvoxamine raise levels, while smoking induces CYP1A2 and lowers them.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow dopamine stimulation outside the motor system. Nausea comes from the chemoreceptor trigger zone. Orthostatic hypotension comes from reduced sympathetic outflow. Hallucinations and confusion come from cortical and limbic stimulation, more often in older adults. Impulse control disorders come from D3 stimulation in reward pathways. Sleep attacks can occur without warning. Stopping suddenly can cause withdrawal symptoms or a fever and rigidity syndrome similar to neuroleptic malignant syndrome. In restless legs syndrome, long use can cause augmentation. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Requip (ropinirole) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "rosuvastatin": {"origin": "Rosuvastatin was discovered by the Japanese company Shionogi in the 1990s and licensed to AstraZeneca for worldwide development. By then, several statins were on the market, and the goal was a more potent drug with fewer drug interactions. Chemists added a polar methane sulfonamide group that made it hydrophilic and helped it bind tightly to HMG-CoA reductase. The FDA approved it as Crestor in 2003. It is one of the most potent statins and a high-intensity option. The JUPITER trial, published in 2008, showed that it reduced heart attacks and strokes in people with normal LDL but raised C-reactive protein, supporting the idea that statins also act on inflammation.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Shionogi discovers rosuvastatin and licenses it to AstraZeneca."], ["2003", "FDA approves rosuvastatin (Crestor)."], ["2008", "JUPITER trial shows benefit in people with raised C-reactive protein."], ["2016", "Generic rosuvastatin becomes available in the US."]], "cell": "Rosuvastatin competitively inhibits HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting step in cholesterol synthesis that turns HMG-CoA into mevalonate. It forms more binding interactions with the enzyme than older statins, which makes it very potent. Less cholesterol made in liver cells lowers intracellular sterol levels, which activates SREBP-2. SREBP-2 increases LDL receptor production, so liver cells pull more LDL and its precursors out of the blood. Rosuvastatin is hydrophilic and enters liver cells mainly through the OATP1B1 transporter, so its action is concentrated in the liver. Statins also reduce isoprenoid intermediates, which lowers vascular inflammation and may stabilize plaques.", "body": [["Blood lipids", "As a high-intensity statin, it can lower LDL by about half or more, and it also lowers triglycerides and modestly raises HDL."], ["Arteries", "Lower LDL and less inflammation slow plaque growth and reduce heart attacks and strokes."], ["Liver", "The liver is the target; mild liver enzyme rises can occur."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Muscle aches can occur, and rarely myopathy or rhabdomyolysis, especially at high doses or with interacting drugs."], ["Kidneys", "High doses can cause protein in the urine, usually without kidney damage."], ["Blood sugar", "Statins slightly raise blood sugar and the chance of a diabetes diagnosis."]], "journey": "Rosuvastatin is taken by mouth, with about 20 percent bioavailability, and peaks in three to five hours. It can be taken at any time of day because its half-life is long, about 19 hours. It is about 88 percent protein bound. It is barely metabolized; a small part goes through CYP2C9. Most of it leaves unchanged in feces, with some in urine. Because CYP3A4 plays no role, it has fewer interactions than simvastatin or atorvastatin. People of Asian ancestry tend to have about twice the blood levels, linked to differences in transporters such as OATP1B1 and BCRP, so lower starting doses are used.", "sidefx": "Muscle symptoms come from reduced mevalonate products in muscle cells, such as coenzyme Q10 and prenylated proteins. Risk rises with high doses, kidney disease, hypothyroidism, older age and interacting drugs such as cyclosporine and gemfibrozil, which block its transport or clearance. Rhabdomyolysis can cause kidney failure. Proteinuria at high doses may come from reduced uptake of proteins in kidney tubules. Statins are avoided in pregnancy. There is no antidote; stopping the drug reverses side effects.", "src": ["Crestor (rosuvastatin) FDA label", "Ridker PM et al. NEJM 2008 (JUPITER)"]}, "sacubitril-valsartan": {"origin": "Natriuretic peptides such as ANP and BNP are hormones made by the heart that relax vessels and help the kidneys excrete salt. Neprilysin is the enzyme that breaks them down. In the 1990s and 2000s, researchers tried omapatrilat, a single molecule blocking both neprilysin and ACE, but it caused severe angioedema because both enzymes break down bradykinin. Novartis took a different approach: it paired the neprilysin inhibitor sacubitril with the angiotensin receptor blocker valsartan, which does not raise bradykinin as much. The PARADIGM-HF trial, published in 2014, showed this combination lowered cardiovascular death and heart failure hospitalization compared with enalapril in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction. The FDA approved it as Entresto in 2015, the first angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor (ARNI).", "timeline": [["1980s", "Atrial natriuretic peptide is discovered as a heart-made hormone."], ["2000s", "Omapatrilat, a combined ACE and neprilysin inhibitor, is not approved due to angioedema."], ["2014", "PARADIGM-HF trial shows benefit over enalapril in heart failure."], ["2015", "FDA approves sacubitril/valsartan (Entresto)."]], "cell": "Sacubitril is a prodrug that esterases convert to LBQ657, which inhibits neprilysin (neutral endopeptidase), a zinc enzyme on cell surfaces in the kidney, vessels and lungs. Neprilysin normally breaks down ANP, BNP and CNP, along with bradykinin, substance P and angiotensin II. With neprilysin blocked, natriuretic peptides build up and bind NPR-A receptors, which make cGMP. cGMP relaxes vascular smooth muscle, increases sodium and water excretion in the kidney, lowers renin and aldosterone and reduces cardiac fibrosis and hypertrophy. Because neprilysin also breaks down angiotensin II, blocking it alone would raise angiotensin II. Valsartan blocks angiotensin II at the AT1 receptor, preventing vasoconstriction, aldosterone release and remodeling, so both effects add up.", "body": [["Heart", "Less afterload, less wall stress and less fibrosis and remodeling improve symptoms and lower death and hospitalization in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction."], ["Blood vessels", "Vasodilation from natriuretic peptides and AT1 blockade lowers blood pressure, which can cause dizziness or hypotension."], ["Kidneys", "More salt and water excretion reduces fluid overload. Kidney function can drop and creatinine can rise, especially with low blood pressure."], ["Potassium", "Valsartan lowers aldosterone, so potassium can rise."], ["Airway and face", "Higher bradykinin can cause angioedema, so it must not be combined with an ACE inhibitor, which requires a 36-hour washout."], ["Lab tests", "BNP rises because neprilysin breaks down BNP, so NT-proBNP is used to monitor heart failure instead."]], "journey": "Sacubitril/valsartan is taken by mouth as a combination tablet. Sacubitril is absorbed quickly and converted by esterases into LBQ657, which peaks in about two hours. Valsartan peaks in about one and a half hours. Both are highly protein bound. LBQ657 is cleared mainly by the kidneys, with a half-life of about 11 to 12 hours. Valsartan is barely metabolized and leaves mostly in bile and feces, with a half-life of about 10 hours. Twice-daily dosing keeps effects steady. Both parts can harm the fetus, so it is not used in pregnancy.", "sidefx": "Hypotension is the most common problem, from combined vasodilation and natriuresis. Hyperkalemia comes from lower aldosterone. Kidney function changes come from lower glomerular pressure when angiotensin II is blocked. Angioedema comes from bradykinin buildup when neprilysin is blocked, which is why the drug is never given with or within 36 hours of an ACE inhibitor and is avoided in people with prior angioedema. Cough is less common than with ACE inhibitors. Like other drugs that block the renin-angiotensin system, it carries a boxed warning for fetal toxicity. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Entresto (sacubitril/valsartan) FDA label", "McMurray JJV et al. NEJM 2014 (PARADIGM-HF)"]}, "salmeterol": {"origin": "Salmeterol was designed at Glaxo's Allen & Hanburys research labs in England in the 1980s, the same group that developed albuterol (salbutamol). Albuterol relieved asthma quickly but wore off in four to six hours. Chemists added a long, fat-soluble side chain to the albuterol structure, creating a molecule that anchors in the cell membrane near the beta-2 receptor and keeps stimulating it for about 12 hours. The FDA approved it as Serevent in 1994, and the combination inhaler with fluticasone (Advair) followed in 2000. In 2006, the SMART trial found more asthma-related deaths with salmeterol used without an inhaled steroid, leading to a boxed warning. In 2017, after large safety trials, the FDA removed the boxed warning for combinations of LABAs with inhaled corticosteroids.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Allen & Hanburys (Glaxo) designs salmeterol from the albuterol structure."], ["1994", "FDA approves salmeterol (Serevent)."], ["2000", "Fluticasone/salmeterol (Advair) is approved."], ["2006", "SMART trial reports increased asthma deaths with salmeterol alone."], ["2017", "FDA removes the boxed warning for LABA plus inhaled corticosteroid combinations."]], "cell": "Salmeterol is a selective, long-acting agonist at beta-2 adrenergic receptors on airway smooth muscle. Its long lipophilic tail dissolves into the cell membrane and binds a site near the receptor (often called an exosite), which keeps the active head of the molecule close to the binding pocket so it can repeatedly activate the receptor. Beta-2 receptors are Gs-coupled: activation stimulates adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP and activating protein kinase A. PKA lowers intracellular calcium and inactivates myosin light chain kinase, which relaxes airway smooth muscle and opens the airways. Because the drug works its way into the membrane first, onset is slower than albuterol, about 10 to 20 minutes, so it is not a rescue inhaler.", "body": [["Airways", "Keeps airways open for about 12 hours, reducing nighttime asthma symptoms and COPD breathlessness when used regularly."], ["Inflammation", "It does not treat airway inflammation, so in asthma it must be paired with an inhaled corticosteroid."], ["Heart", "Absorbed drug can stimulate cardiac beta receptors, causing palpitations and fast heart rate, especially at high doses."], ["Muscles and nerves", "Beta-2 stimulation in skeletal muscle causes tremor and sometimes cramps."], ["Potassium and glucose", "Beta-2 activation moves potassium into cells and raises blood sugar, which matters at high doses."]], "journey": "Salmeterol is inhaled as a dry powder. Most of the effect comes from drug landing directly on airway walls, and only small amounts reach the blood. Swallowed drug is broken down in the liver. Onset of airway opening is about 10 to 20 minutes, with peak effect in a few hours and duration of about 12 hours. Absorbed salmeterol is highly protein bound and is metabolized by CYP3A4, then excreted mainly in feces. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ritonavir and ketoconazole raise blood levels and the risk of heart effects.", "sidefx": "Tremor, headache and palpitations come from beta-2 and some beta-1 stimulation outside the lungs. Hypokalemia and high blood sugar come from beta-2 effects on cells and the liver. The higher risk of asthma-related death when used alone is thought to come from relieving symptoms while inflammation continues, so attacks become severe before people seek help. This is why LABAs are never used alone in asthma. It should never be used for sudden attacks because of its slow onset. Overdose is treated with supportive care, and a cardioselective beta blocker may be used with caution.", "src": ["Serevent (salmeterol) FDA label", "Nelson HS et al. Chest 2006 (SMART)", "FDA Drug Safety Communication, December 2017"]}, "scopolamine": {"origin": "Scopolamine comes from plants of the nightshade family, including henbane (Hyoscyamus niger), jimsonweed (Datura) and Scopolia, which was named after 18th-century naturalist Giovanni Antonio Scopoli. These plants have been used for centuries as medicines, poisons and ritual drugs. German chemist Albert Ladenburg isolated the alkaloid, which he named hyoscine, around 1880. In the early 1900s, scopolamine combined with morphine was used in childbirth to produce twilight sleep, a state of reduced pain and no memory, which was later abandoned. In 1979, the FDA approved Transderm Scop, a patch worn behind the ear for motion sickness. It was the first transdermal drug patch approved in the US, developed by Alza.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Nightshade plants containing scopolamine are used as medicines and poisons."], ["1880", "Albert Ladenburg isolates hyoscine (scopolamine)."], ["1900s", "Scopolamine with morphine is used for twilight sleep during childbirth."], ["1979", "FDA approves Transderm Scop, the first US transdermal patch."]], "cell": "Scopolamine is a competitive antagonist at all five muscarinic acetylcholine receptor subtypes (M1 to M5). It is a tertiary amine, so it crosses the blood-brain barrier easily and has stronger brain effects than atropine. Motion sickness happens when signals from the inner ear's vestibular system conflict with what the eyes see. These signals travel through cholinergic pathways from the vestibular nuclei to the brainstem vomiting center (the nucleus tractus solitarius and related areas). By blocking muscarinic receptors in the vestibular nuclei and these pathways, scopolamine dampens the mismatch signal and prevents nausea. Blocking M1 receptors in the cortex and hippocampus causes sedation and memory loss. Peripheral muscarinic blockade causes dry mouth, dilated pupils, fast heart rate and urinary retention.", "body": [["Brain and inner ear", "Blocks vestibular signals to the vomiting center, preventing motion sickness and postoperative nausea."], ["Memory and alertness", "Central M1 blockade causes drowsiness and, at higher levels, confusion, memory loss and hallucinations, especially in older adults."], ["Eyes", "Blocking the iris sphincter and ciliary muscle dilates pupils and blurs near vision. Touching the patch and then the eye can dilate one pupil."], ["Glands", "Less saliva, sweat and airway secretions cause dry mouth and can raise body temperature."], ["Heart", "Blocking vagal effects can increase heart rate."], ["Bladder and gut", "It slows bowel movement and can cause urinary retention, especially in men with enlarged prostates."]], "journey": "The patch is applied to hairless skin behind the ear, where the skin is thin and absorption is steady. Drug is released slowly over about three days, and the patch is applied about four hours before travel because it takes time to build up. It can also be given by injection. It crosses the blood-brain barrier and placenta. The liver metabolizes it, and a small amount leaves unchanged in urine. After the patch is removed, drug already in the skin keeps being absorbed, so side effects can last for a day or more.", "sidefx": "All side effects come from muscarinic blockade. Dry mouth is the most common. Drowsiness, confusion and hallucinations come from brain entry, which is why it is used with caution in older adults. Dilated pupils and blurred vision occur, and it can trigger acute angle-closure glaucoma. Urinary retention and constipation follow from smooth muscle relaxation. Overdose causes classic anticholinergic toxicity: hot, dry, flushed skin, dilated pupils, fast heart rate, urinary retention and delirium. Physostigmine, a cholinesterase inhibitor that enters the brain, can reverse severe toxicity in selected cases. Stopping after long use can cause withdrawal nausea and dizziness.", "fun": "Scopolamine and atropine come from the same plant family; atropine's plant, Atropa belladonna, means beautiful lady because its extract was once used to dilate women's pupils.", "src": ["Transderm Scop (scopolamine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "selegiline": {"origin": "Selegiline (also called L-deprenyl) was synthesized in the early 1960s at the Chinoin company in Budapest, Hungary, by chemist Zoltan Ecseri, and was studied by pharmacologist Jozsef Knoll. It was first developed as an antidepressant. Knoll found it selectively blocked MAO-B and, unlike older MAO inhibitors, did not cause the cheese reaction, a dangerous rise in blood pressure from tyramine in food. In 1975, Austrian neurologist Walther Birkmayer reported benefits when it was added to levodopa in Parkinson disease. The DATATOP trial, published in 1989, showed it delayed the need for levodopa in early disease. The FDA approved it as Eldepryl in 1989. In 2006, a selegiline skin patch (Emsam) was approved for major depression.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Zoltan Ecseri and Jozsef Knoll develop selegiline at Chinoin in Hungary."], ["1975", "Walther Birkmayer reports benefits with levodopa in Parkinson disease."], ["1989", "DATATOP trial results are published; FDA approves selegiline (Eldepryl)."], ["2006", "FDA approves the selegiline transdermal patch (Emsam) for depression."]], "cell": "Monoamine oxidase (MAO) is an enzyme on the outer membrane of mitochondria that breaks down monoamines. MAO-A mainly breaks down serotonin, norepinephrine and dietary tyramine in the gut. MAO-B mainly breaks down dopamine in the brain, particularly in glial cells of the striatum. Selegiline contains a propargyl group that forms a permanent covalent bond with the FAD cofactor of MAO-B, inactivating it (a suicide inhibitor). With MAO-B blocked, dopamine lasts longer in the striatum, boosting the effect of the remaining dopamine and of levodopa. Recovery requires new enzyme, which takes days to weeks. At low oral doses it is selective for MAO-B, but at higher doses, such as with the patch for depression, it also blocks MAO-A, which raises serotonin and norepinephrine.", "body": [["Brain motor circuits", "Extending dopamine action modestly improves symptoms in early Parkinson disease and smooths levodopa wearing-off."], ["Mood", "At higher patch doses, combined MAO-A and MAO-B inhibition raises serotonin and norepinephrine, treating depression."], ["Sleep", "Its amphetamine-like metabolites can cause insomnia, so it is taken in the morning."], ["Blood vessels", "It can cause orthostatic hypotension, and at high doses with tyramine-rich foods it can cause hypertensive crisis."], ["Brain (side effects)", "Added to levodopa, it can increase dyskinesias, confusion and hallucinations."]], "journey": "Selegiline is taken by mouth as a tablet or capsule, as a tablet that dissolves on the tongue (Zelapar) or as a skin patch (Emsam). Oral drug undergoes heavy first-pass metabolism; the dissolving tablet and patch bypass much of this, giving more drug to the brain with less effect on gut MAO-A. It crosses the blood-brain barrier easily. The liver metabolizes it through CYP2B6 and other enzymes to desmethylselegiline, L-methamphetamine and L-amphetamine, which leave in urine. The drug's half-life is short, but because the enzyme is destroyed permanently, the effect lasts until new MAO-B is made over days to weeks.", "sidefx": "Insomnia and jitteriness come from amphetamine metabolites. Dyskinesias and hallucinations come from extra dopamine in the brain when combined with levodopa. Serotonin syndrome can occur with SSRIs, SNRIs, tricyclics, meperidine, tramadol, methadone and dextromethorphan, because MAO inhibition plus serotonin boosting overloads serotonin receptors. Hypertensive crisis can occur at higher doses with tyramine-rich foods because gut MAO-A no longer breaks down tyramine, which then releases norepinephrine. Its metabolites can cause positive urine amphetamine screens. There is no antidote; serotonin syndrome is treated with supportive care and cyproheptadine.", "src": ["Eldepryl (selegiline) FDA label", "Emsam (selegiline transdermal system) FDA label", "Parkinson Study Group. NEJM 1989 (DATATOP)"]}, "semaglutide": {"origin": "The story begins in the 1980s, when researchers including Joel Habener, Svetlana Mojsov, Daniel Drucker and Jens Juul Holst identified glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1), a gut hormone that boosts insulin after meals. Natural GLP-1 lasts only about two minutes in the blood because the enzyme DPP-4 cuts it. At Novo Nordisk in Denmark, a team led by Lotte Bjerre Knudsen first developed liraglutide by attaching a fatty acid chain that binds albumin. They then built semaglutide with an amino acid change that resists DPP-4 and a longer fatty acid chain, giving it a week-long half-life. The FDA approved Ozempic for type 2 diabetes in 2017, oral Rybelsus in 2019 and Wegovy for weight management in 2021. Habener, Mojsov and Knudsen received the 2024 Lasker Award.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Researchers identify GLP-1 as an insulin-boosting gut hormone."], ["2017", "FDA approves once-weekly semaglutide (Ozempic) for type 2 diabetes."], ["2019", "Oral semaglutide (Rybelsus) becomes the first oral GLP-1 receptor agonist approved."], ["2021", "FDA approves semaglutide (Wegovy) for chronic weight management."], ["2024", "Habener, Mojsov and Knudsen receive the Lasker Clinical Medical Research Award."]], "cell": "Semaglutide is a GLP-1 analog that is about 94 percent identical to human GLP-1. A change at position 8 protects it from DPP-4, and an attached C-18 fatty acid binds albumin tightly, which slows clearance by the kidney and enzymes. It activates GLP-1 receptors, which are Gs-coupled receptors. In pancreatic beta cells, raised cAMP boosts insulin release, but only when glucose is high, so it causes little hypoglycemia by itself. In alpha cells it lowers glucagon release. In the stomach it slows emptying. In the hypothalamus and brainstem, including the arcuate nucleus and area postrema, it activates satiety neurons (POMC/CART) and reduces hunger signals, which lowers food intake and body weight.", "body": [["Pancreas", "Glucose-dependent insulin release increases and glucagon falls, which lowers blood sugar and HbA1c."], ["Brain appetite centers", "Activating GLP-1 receptors in the hypothalamus and brainstem reduces hunger and food cravings, leading to substantial weight loss."], ["Stomach and gut", "Slower stomach emptying increases fullness but causes nausea, vomiting, diarrhea or constipation, especially when doses are increased."], ["Heart and vessels", "In trials, it lowered major cardiovascular events in people with type 2 diabetes and in people with obesity and heart disease."], ["Gallbladder and pancreas", "Rapid weight loss and changes in gallbladder motility raise the risk of gallstones; pancreatitis is rare but serious."], ["Kidneys", "Vomiting and diarrhea can cause dehydration and acute kidney injury."]], "journey": "Semaglutide is given as a once-weekly injection under the skin or as a daily tablet. The tablet contains an absorption enhancer (SNAC) that protects it from stomach acid and helps it cross the stomach lining; it must be taken on an empty stomach with a small amount of water, and only a small fraction is absorbed. After injection, it is absorbed slowly over one to three days. Over 99 percent binds albumin. It is broken down by proteolysis and beta-oxidation of the fatty acid chain, like natural proteins, and metabolites leave in urine and feces. Its half-life is about one week, so steady levels take four to five weeks.", "sidefx": "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea come from slowed stomach emptying and direct action on brainstem nausea centers, which is why doses are raised slowly. Dehydration from GI losses can cause kidney injury. Gallstones relate to rapid weight loss and reduced gallbladder emptying. Pancreatitis is rare. In rodents, GLP-1 agonists caused thyroid C-cell tumors, so there is a boxed warning and it is not used in people with medullary thyroid cancer or MEN 2. Rapid lowering of blood sugar can worsen diabetic retinopathy. Hypoglycemia mainly happens with insulin or sulfonylureas. Slowed stomach emptying raises aspiration risk during anesthesia. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The first GLP-1 drug, exenatide, came from the saliva of the Gila monster, a venomous lizard, before human-based analogs like semaglutide took over.", "src": ["Ozempic, Wegovy and Rybelsus (semaglutide) FDA labels", "Lasker Foundation 2024 award citation", "Lincoff AM et al. NEJM 2023 (SELECT)"]}, "sennosides": {"origin": "Senna comes from the leaves and pods of the senna plant (Senna alexandrina, formerly Cassia angustifolia and Cassia acutifolia), a shrub that grows in Egypt, Sudan and India. Arab physicians used senna as a laxative more than a thousand years ago, and it spread through medieval Europe through trade from Alexandria. The name senna comes from the Arabic word sana. In the 20th century, chemists identified the active compounds as anthraquinone glycosides called sennosides, mainly sennosides A and B. Standardized senna products allowed predictable dosing. Today senna is one of the most common over-the-counter stimulant laxatives and is often used to prevent opioid-induced constipation.", "timeline": [["9th century", "Arab physicians describe senna as a laxative."], ["1900s", "Chemists identify sennosides A and B as the active anthraquinone glycosides."], ["1950s", "Standardized senna laxative products become widely available."]], "cell": "Sennosides are glycosides, meaning a sugar is attached to the active anthrone part. The sugar keeps them from being absorbed in the stomach and small intestine, so they travel to the colon intact. There, colonic bacteria cut off the sugars with beta-glucosidase enzymes and reduce them to rhein anthrone, the active metabolite. Rhein anthrone acts locally in two ways. It stimulates the myenteric nerve plexus and colonic smooth muscle, increasing propulsive contractions and speeding transit. It also changes salt and water transport across the colonic lining by reducing sodium and water absorption and increasing chloride secretion, partly through prostaglandin E2 release from macrophages and lower aquaporin-3 levels. More water stays in the stool and the stool moves faster.", "body": [["Colon", "Stronger contractions and more water in the stool produce a bowel movement, usually 6 to 12 hours after an oral dose, so it is often taken at bedtime."], ["Opioid constipation", "Senna counteracts slowed gut movement from opioids, so it is commonly paired with opioid therapy."], ["Gut comfort", "Cramping and abdominal pain come from stronger colon contractions."], ["Fluid and electrolytes", "Heavy or long-term use can cause diarrhea, dehydration and low potassium."], ["Urine", "Absorbed metabolites can turn urine yellow-brown, or reddish in alkaline urine, which is harmless."], ["Skin", "Prolonged contact with liquid stool in people with incontinence can irritate perianal skin and cause blistering."]], "journey": "Sennosides are taken by mouth as tablets, liquid or tea. They pass through the stomach and small intestine mostly unabsorbed and are activated by bacteria in the colon, which explains the delay of 6 to 12 hours before the effect. A small amount of rhein anthrone and other metabolites is absorbed, processed by the liver and excreted in urine and bile, and small amounts may enter breast milk. Most of the drug leaves in the stool.", "sidefx": "Cramping comes from strong colonic contractions. Diarrhea and fluid loss come from increased secretion and reduced absorption of water and salt; with heavy use this can cause low potassium, which can itself slow the bowel and, in severe cases, cause muscle weakness or arrhythmias. Long-term use can cause melanosis coli, a harmless brown pigmentation of the colon lining. It should not be used when bowel obstruction or acute abdominal pain of unknown cause is present. Antibiotics that change colonic bacteria can reduce its effect. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Senokot (sennosides) labeling", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "sevelamer": {"origin": "Sevelamer was developed in the 1990s by GelTex Pharmaceuticals in Massachusetts, which designed non-absorbed polymers that could trap substances in the gut. People with kidney failure cannot excrete phosphate, and high phosphate drives bone disease, itching and calcification of blood vessels. Older binders had problems: aluminum caused bone and brain toxicity, and calcium-based binders could add to vascular calcification. Sevelamer was a calcium-free, aluminum-free plastic-like polymer that binds phosphate in the gut. The FDA approved sevelamer hydrochloride as Renagel in 1998. Because the hydrochloride form could worsen metabolic acidosis, sevelamer carbonate (Renvela) was later approved in 2007 and is now the more common form. GelTex was acquired by Genzyme, now part of Sanofi.", "timeline": [["1990s", "GelTex Pharmaceuticals develops sevelamer as a non-absorbed phosphate-binding polymer."], ["1998", "FDA approves sevelamer hydrochloride (Renagel)."], ["2007", "FDA approves sevelamer carbonate (Renvela)."]], "cell": "Sevelamer is a cross-linked polymer of allylamine that is not absorbed from the gut. Many of its amine groups pick up protons in the intestine and become positively charged. Dietary phosphate is negatively charged, so it binds to these sites by ionic and hydrogen bonds. The bound phosphate stays attached to the polymer and leaves in the stool instead of being absorbed. Lower phosphate absorption lowers blood phosphate, which reduces stimulation of parathyroid hormone and FGF23 and lowers the calcium-phosphate product that drives vascular calcification. Sevelamer also binds bile acids, like cholestyramine, which forces the liver to use cholesterol to make new bile acids, lowering LDL cholesterol.", "body": [["Gut", "Binds dietary phosphate so it is passed in the stool, which is why it is taken with meals."], ["Blood phosphate", "Lower phosphate levels reduce secondary hyperparathyroidism and bone disease in chronic kidney disease."], ["Blood vessels", "Because it adds no calcium, it avoids the calcium load linked to vascular calcification from calcium binders."], ["Cholesterol", "Bile acid binding lowers LDL cholesterol by about 15 to 30 percent."], ["Bowel function", "Nausea, constipation, diarrhea and bloating are common; rarely it can cause bowel obstruction or perforation."], ["Vitamins and drugs", "It can bind fat-soluble vitamins and some drugs, such as ciprofloxacin, mycophenolate and levothyroxine, so timing must be separated."]], "journey": "Sevelamer is taken by mouth with meals as tablets or powder. It is not absorbed at all, so it does not enter the blood, reach the brain or cross the placenta, and it is not metabolized. It works entirely inside the gut and leaves in stool with the phosphate and bile acids it binds. Its effect on blood phosphate appears over days to weeks. Because it acts only during meals, it must be taken with each meal to be effective.", "sidefx": "GI side effects come from a bulky polymer in the gut. Constipation and, rarely, obstruction, perforation or bezoars occur, especially in people with slow bowel movement or recent bowel surgery. Tablets swell in water and should be swallowed whole. Binding of fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) and folate can cause deficiencies. Binding of other drugs reduces their absorption. The hydrochloride form can worsen metabolic acidosis by releasing chloride. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Renvela (sevelamer carbonate) FDA label", "KDIGO CKD-Mineral and Bone Disorder Guideline"]}, "sevoflurane": {"origin": "Sevoflurane is a fluorinated methyl isopropyl ether that was first made at Travenol Laboratories in the United States around 1970, and its anesthetic properties were described by Wallin and colleagues in 1975. Development stalled because of concerns about its breakdown in the body to fluoride and its reaction with carbon dioxide absorbents. Maruishi Pharmaceutical in Japan picked it up, and it was approved there in 1990, where it quickly became popular. Abbott Laboratories brought it to the US, and the FDA approved it as Ultane in 1995. Its sweet smell and lack of airway irritation made it the agent of choice for inhaled induction, especially in children, and it became one of the most widely used inhaled anesthetics in the world.", "timeline": [["1846", "William Morton demonstrates ether anesthesia at Massachusetts General Hospital."], ["1975", "Wallin and colleagues describe sevoflurane as a new inhaled anesthetic."], ["1990", "Sevoflurane is approved in Japan."], ["1995", "FDA approves sevoflurane (Ultane)."]], "cell": "Like other volatile anesthetics, sevoflurane acts on several ion channels in the brain and spinal cord. It enhances GABA-A receptors, increasing chloride flow and hyperpolarizing neurons, and it enhances inhibitory glycine receptors in the spinal cord. It inhibits excitatory NMDA glutamate receptors and neuronal nicotinic receptors. It opens two-pore-domain potassium channels (K2P, such as TREK-1), which also hyperpolarizes neurons. Together these actions reduce neuron firing in the cortex, thalamus and brainstem, causing unconsciousness and amnesia, and in the spinal cord, causing immobility in response to pain. Its low blood-gas partition coefficient, about 0.65, means it does not dissolve much in blood, so brain levels rise and fall quickly.", "body": [["Brain", "Produces unconsciousness and amnesia; it lowers brain metabolism but dilates cerebral vessels at higher levels, which can raise intracranial pressure."], ["Airways", "It does not irritate the airway and is a bronchodilator, which makes it ideal for mask induction in children and useful in asthma."], ["Breathing", "It depresses breathing in a dose-dependent way, with faster, shallower breaths and a blunted response to carbon dioxide."], ["Heart and vessels", "It lowers blood pressure mainly by relaxing blood vessels and causes mild heart muscle depression, with little effect on heart rate."], ["Skeletal muscle", "It relaxes muscles and boosts the effect of neuromuscular blockers. In susceptible people, it can trigger malignant hyperthermia."], ["Waking up", "Children can have emergence delirium, a period of agitation and crying as they wake."]], "journey": "Sevoflurane is inhaled through a mask or breathing tube. Because it is not very soluble in blood, its concentration in the lungs, blood and brain rises quickly, so induction takes one to two minutes and waking is fast once it is turned off. Almost all of it leaves the body unchanged through the lungs when the patient breathes out. A small amount, about 3 to 5 percent, is metabolized by the liver enzyme CYP2E1 into hexafluoroisopropanol and inorganic fluoride, which are cleared by the kidneys. It crosses the placenta. In the breathing circuit, it can react with carbon dioxide absorbents to form compound A, which was nephrotoxic in rats.", "sidefx": "Low blood pressure comes from vasodilation and mild myocardial depression. Breathing depression comes from effects on the brainstem breathing center. Nausea and vomiting after surgery are common with volatile anesthetics. Emergence delirium in children may relate to rapid waking in an unfamiliar setting. It can prolong the QT interval. Malignant hyperthermia, a genetic disorder of the RYR1 calcium channel in muscle, causes uncontrolled calcium release, rigidity, high carbon dioxide, fever and rhabdomyolysis; it is treated by stopping the trigger and giving dantrolene, which blocks RYR1. Fluoride and compound A have not caused meaningful kidney injury in clinical use with recommended fresh gas flows.", "src": ["Ultane (sevoflurane) FDA label", "Wallin RF et al. Anesth Analg 1975", "Miller's Anesthesia"]}, "sildenafil": {"origin": "Sildenafil was created at Pfizer's research center in Sandwich, England, in 1989, by a team including chemists Andrew Bell, David Brown and Nicholas Terrett. It was designed to inhibit phosphodiesterase-5 (PDE5) to dilate coronary arteries and treat angina. In early clinical trials in the 1990s, its effect on angina was disappointing, but male volunteers reported erections as a side effect. Pfizer redirected development toward erectile dysfunction, and the FDA approved it as Viagra in 1998, the first oral treatment for erectile dysfunction. That same year, Robert Furchgott, Louis Ignarro and Ferid Murad won the Nobel Prize for discovering nitric oxide signaling, the pathway sildenafil boosts. In 2005, it was approved as Revatio for pulmonary arterial hypertension.", "timeline": [["1989", "Sildenafil is synthesized at Pfizer in Sandwich, England, as a possible angina drug."], ["1990s", "Clinical trials reveal erections as an unexpected effect."], ["1998", "FDA approves sildenafil (Viagra) for erectile dysfunction."], ["1998", "Nobel Prize honors the discovery of nitric oxide as a signaling molecule."], ["2005", "FDA approves sildenafil (Revatio) for pulmonary arterial hypertension."]], "cell": "During sexual arousal, nerves and endothelial cells in the penis release nitric oxide (NO). NO activates soluble guanylyl cyclase in smooth muscle cells, which makes cGMP. cGMP activates protein kinase G, which lowers intracellular calcium and relaxes smooth muscle in the corpus cavernosum and its arteries. Blood flows in, and expanding tissue compresses veins, trapping blood and producing an erection. PDE5 breaks down cGMP and ends the signal. Sildenafil competitively inhibits PDE5, so cGMP lasts longer and builds up. It does not cause an erection without sexual stimulation, because NO release is still needed. In the lungs, PDE5 is abundant in pulmonary artery smooth muscle, so inhibiting it relaxes pulmonary vessels and lowers pulmonary pressure.", "body": [["Penis", "Higher cGMP enhances smooth muscle relaxation and blood flow during sexual stimulation, improving erections."], ["Lungs", "Relaxing pulmonary arteries lowers pulmonary artery pressure and improves exercise capacity in pulmonary arterial hypertension."], ["Blood vessels", "Mild relaxation of systemic vessels slightly lowers blood pressure and causes flushing, headache and nasal congestion."], ["Eyes", "Weak inhibition of PDE6 in the retina causes a temporary blue tint to vision and light sensitivity. Rarely, nonarteritic ischemic optic neuropathy (NAION) causes sudden vision loss."], ["Stomach and esophagus", "Relaxing the lower esophageal sphincter causes heartburn and indigestion."], ["Ears", "Sudden hearing loss has been reported rarely."]], "journey": "Sildenafil is taken by mouth or given IV for pulmonary hypertension. It is absorbed quickly, with about 40 percent bioavailability, reaching peak levels in about an hour; a high-fat meal delays absorption. It is about 96 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP3A4, with help from CYP2C9, into an active N-desmethyl metabolite with about half the potency. Metabolites leave mostly in feces, with some in urine. The half-life is about four hours. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ritonavir raise levels a lot, so doses must be reduced or the combination avoided.", "sidefx": "Headache, flushing and nasal congestion come from vasodilation. Blue-tinted vision comes from cross-inhibition of PDE6 in retinal rods and cones. The most dangerous interaction is with nitrates such as nitroglycerin: both raise cGMP, nitrates by producing NO and sildenafil by blocking its breakdown, so together they can cause severe, sometimes fatal hypotension. Nitrates are avoided for at least 24 hours after sildenafil. Combining with alpha blockers can also lower blood pressure. Priapism, an erection lasting more than four hours, is an emergency. Sudden vision or hearing loss needs urgent evaluation. There is no antidote; hypotension is treated with fluids and vasopressors.", "fun": "Sildenafil's discovery as an erectile dysfunction drug happened only because volunteers in a failed angina trial reported an unexpected side effect.", "src": ["Viagra and Revatio (sildenafil) FDA labels", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1998 press release", "Boolell M et al. Int J Impot Res 1996"]}, "simvastatin": {"origin": "Simvastatin came from the work that produced lovastatin. In the late 1970s, Merck scientists led by Alfred Alberts found lovastatin in the fungus Aspergillus terreus, building on Akira Endo's discovery of compactin. Lovastatin was approved in 1987 as the first statin in the US. Merck chemists then added a single methyl group to the side chain of lovastatin, creating simvastatin, which was more potent. It was first approved in Sweden in 1988 and in the US in 1991 as Zocor. In 1994, the Scandinavian Simvastatin Survival Study (4S) became the first large trial to show that lowering cholesterol with a statin reduced overall deaths in people with heart disease. In 2011, the FDA limited use of its highest dose because of higher rates of muscle injury.", "timeline": [["1978", "Merck scientists discover lovastatin in Aspergillus terreus."], ["1988", "Simvastatin is first approved in Sweden."], ["1991", "FDA approves simvastatin (Zocor)."], ["1994", "The 4S trial shows simvastatin reduces deaths in people with coronary disease."], ["2011", "FDA restricts use of the highest simvastatin dose because of myopathy risk."]], "cell": "Simvastatin is a lactone prodrug. After absorption, it is hydrolyzed to its active open-ring form, simvastatin acid, a beta-hydroxy acid. The active form competitively inhibits HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting enzyme that converts HMG-CoA into mevalonate in cholesterol synthesis. Lower cholesterol production in liver cells activates SREBP-2, which increases the number of LDL receptors on liver cells, so they clear more LDL from the blood. Simvastatin is lipophilic, so it enters many tissues, including muscle, by passive diffusion as well as by OATP1B1 transport into the liver. Reduced production of isoprenoids such as farnesyl and geranylgeranyl groups also has anti-inflammatory and plaque-stabilizing effects.", "body": [["Blood lipids", "As a moderate-intensity statin at usual doses, it lowers LDL by about a third and modestly lowers triglycerides."], ["Arteries", "Lower LDL slows plaque buildup and stabilizes plaques, reducing heart attacks, strokes and deaths."], ["Liver", "The liver is the main site of action; mild liver enzyme rises can occur."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Because it is fat soluble and CYP3A4 dependent, it has a higher risk of muscle pain, myopathy and rhabdomyolysis than some statins, especially with interacting drugs."], ["Blood sugar", "Statins slightly raise blood sugar and the risk of a diabetes diagnosis."]], "journey": "Simvastatin is taken by mouth, usually in the evening, because its short half-life of about two hours means it acts most when the liver makes cholesterol at night. It is well absorbed but has heavy first-pass metabolism in the gut wall and liver, so less than 5 percent reaches the blood. It is about 95 percent protein bound. CYP3A4 metabolizes it, and its metabolites are mainly excreted in bile and feces. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as clarithromycin, itraconazole, ritonavir and large amounts of grapefruit juice raise levels sharply, and people with a SLCO1B1 gene variant have higher levels and more muscle risk.", "sidefx": "Muscle injury is the key concern. Statins reduce coenzyme Q10 and prenylated proteins inside muscle cells, which can damage them; high blood levels make this more likely. Because simvastatin depends on CYP3A4, many drugs, including strong CYP3A4 inhibitors, some calcium channel blockers, amiodarone and gemfibrozil, can raise levels and cause rhabdomyolysis with dark urine and kidney failure. The SLCO1B1 variant reduces liver uptake and raises risk. Liver enzyme rises reflect liver action. Statins are avoided in pregnancy. There is no antidote; stopping the drug and hydration treat rhabdomyolysis.", "fun": "Simvastatin differs from lovastatin by just one extra methyl group, yet that small change made it roughly twice as potent.", "src": ["Zocor (simvastatin) FDA label", "Scandinavian Simvastatin Survival Study Group. Lancet 1994", "Alberts AW et al. PNAS 1980"]}, "sitagliptin": {"origin": "Sitagliptin grew out of research on the incretin hormones GLP-1 and GIP, gut hormones that boost insulin release after a meal. Scientists learned that the enzyme dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4) chops these hormones apart within minutes. If the enzyme could be blocked, the body's own incretins would last longer. A medicinal chemistry team at Merck set out to build a safe, selective DPP-4 blocker by mouth. After an early lead caused toxicity in animals, the team redesigned the molecule for selectivity. The result, sitagliptin, was approved by the FDA in 2006 as Januvia, the first DPP-4 inhibitor. It gave patients a pill that lowers glucose with little risk of low blood sugar and no weight gain.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Researchers describe GLP-1 as a gut hormone that boosts insulin release after meals."], ["1990s", "DPP-4 is shown to rapidly break down GLP-1 and GIP, suggesting a drug target."], ["2006", "FDA approves sitagliptin (Januvia), the first DPP-4 inhibitor."], ["2007", "FDA approves Janumet, sitagliptin combined with metformin."]], "cell": "After a meal, L cells in the intestine release GLP-1 and K cells release GIP. Normally the enzyme DPP-4, found on cell surfaces and in plasma, cleaves the two N-terminal amino acids off these hormones and inactivates them within minutes. Sitagliptin competitively and reversibly inhibits DPP-4. Active GLP-1 and GIP levels rise. They bind Gs-coupled receptors on pancreatic beta cells, raising cAMP and increasing glucose-dependent insulin secretion. GLP-1 also lowers glucagon release from alpha cells. Because the insulin boost only happens when glucose is high, the risk of hypoglycemia is low when sitagliptin is used alone.", "body": [["Pancreas", "Beta cells release more insulin after meals and alpha cells release less glucagon, so blood glucose falls, mainly after eating."], ["Liver", "Less glucagon means the liver makes and releases less glucose, which lowers fasting glucose."], ["Weight and appetite", "The rise in the body's own incretins is modest, so sitagliptin is weight neutral rather than causing weight loss like injected GLP-1 drugs."], ["Pancreatic tissue", "Rare cases of acute pancreatitis have been reported, so new severe abdominal pain needs evaluation."], ["Joints and immune system", "Rare severe joint pain and hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis and angioedema, have been reported."]], "journey": "Sitagliptin is taken by mouth and is well absorbed, with peak levels in a few hours. It is only partly bound to plasma proteins. Very little is metabolized by the liver. Most of the drug leaves the body unchanged in the urine through filtration and active tubular secretion. Its half-life of about 12 hours supports once-daily use. Because the kidneys do the clearing, reduced kidney function raises levels and the label calls for adjustment.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are mild, such as headache and stuffy nose, and fit a drug that does little beyond extending the life of natural hormones. Hypoglycemia is uncommon alone because incretin-driven insulin release depends on glucose, but it becomes more likely when sitagliptin is combined with insulin or a sulfonylurea. Pancreatitis, severe joint pain and allergic reactions are rare. DPP-4 also cleaves other peptides, which may explain some of these rare effects. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "The '-gliptin' suffix marks all DPP-4 inhibitors, so saxagliptin, linagliptin and alogliptin all share sitagliptin's mechanism.", "src": ["Januvia (sitagliptin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "sodium-bicarbonate": {"origin": "Sodium bicarbonate is the same chemical as baking soda. People in the ancient world used natron, a natural salt mix from dry lake beds that contains sodium carbonate and sodium bicarbonate, for cleaning and in Egyptian mummification. Large-scale production came with industrial chemistry in the 1800s, especially the Solvay process of the 1860s, which made the compound cheap and pure. Doctors used it for indigestion and later as an intravenous buffer for severe acidosis. In resuscitation it was once given routinely during cardiac arrest, but guidelines later limited it to specific problems such as hyperkalemia, tricyclic antidepressant overdose and some cases of severe metabolic acidosis.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Egyptians use natron, a natural mix containing sodium bicarbonate, for cleaning and embalming."], ["1860s", "The Solvay process allows cheap industrial production of sodium carbonate and bicarbonate."], ["1990s", "Resuscitation guidelines move away from routine bicarbonate in cardiac arrest."]], "cell": "Bicarbonate is the main extracellular buffer. Given IV, it combines with hydrogen ions to form carbonic acid, which breaks down into carbon dioxide and water. The lungs exhale the carbon dioxide, so blood pH rises. In sodium channel blocker poisoning, such as tricyclic antidepressant overdose, the extra sodium increases the gradient across cardiac sodium channels and the higher pH reduces drug binding to the channel. This narrows a wide QRS. Raising urine pH ionizes weak acids like salicylate in the tubule so they cannot be reabsorbed (ion trapping). Alkalemia also drives potassium into cells in exchange for hydrogen ions, lowering serum potassium.", "body": [["Blood", "pH rises as hydrogen ions are buffered. Potassium shifts into cells and ionized calcium falls because more calcium binds albumin at higher pH."], ["Heart", "In sodium channel blocker overdose the QRS narrows and dangerous wide-complex rhythms may improve."], ["Lungs", "The carbon dioxide made by buffering must be exhaled, so poor ventilation blunts the benefit and can worsen acidosis inside cells."], ["Kidneys", "Urine becomes alkaline, which traps salicylate and some other weak acids in the urine so they are excreted faster."], ["Vessels and tissue", "The solution is hypertonic and can damage tissue if it leaks out of the vein."]], "journey": "Given IV, sodium bicarbonate acts within minutes because it reacts directly with acid in the blood. There is no liver metabolism. The bicarbonate is turned into carbon dioxide and water, and the carbon dioxide leaves through the lungs. Extra bicarbonate and sodium are handled by the kidneys. Taken by mouth as an antacid, it neutralizes stomach acid and releases carbon dioxide gas in the stomach.", "sidefx": "Most problems are too much of the intended effect. Overcorrection causes metabolic alkalosis, and the pH shift pulls potassium into cells (hypokalemia) and lowers ionized calcium, which can cause tetany. The large sodium load can cause hypernatremia, high osmolality and fluid overload. Carbon dioxide made by buffering diffuses into cells faster than bicarbonate, so without good ventilation it can worsen acidosis inside cells. Leaks out of the vein can cause tissue necrosis. There is no antidote; treatment is stopping it and correcting electrolytes.", "fun": "A single ampule of sodium bicarbonate carries a large sodium load, which is why repeated doses can push serum sodium up quickly.", "src": ["Sodium bicarbonate injection FDA label", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "sodium-zirconium-cyclosilicate": {"origin": "Sodium zirconium cyclosilicate is not a plant or microbe product. It is a man-made crystal, a zirconium silicate with a lattice whose pores are sized to fit potassium ions. Older potassium binders such as sodium polystyrene sulfonate were slow, poorly selective and linked to bowel injury. The company ZS Pharma developed this engineered crystal as a more selective option. AstraZeneca acquired ZS Pharma in 2015, and the FDA approved the drug as Lokelma in 2018 for hyperkalemia in adults. It gave clinicians a fast, nonabsorbed binder that works throughout the gut.", "timeline": [["2010s", "ZS Pharma develops a zirconium silicate crystal designed to trap potassium."], ["2015", "AstraZeneca acquires ZS Pharma."], ["2018", "FDA approves sodium zirconium cyclosilicate (Lokelma) for hyperkalemia."]], "cell": "The drug is an insoluble, nonabsorbed crystal with a microporous structure. Its pore openings are about the size of a dehydrated potassium ion, so it selectively captures potassium (and ammonium) over calcium and magnesium. In exchange it releases sodium and hydrogen ions. Binding begins in the upper gut and continues through the colon. Trapped potassium is carried out in the stool. Less potassium is absorbed, and more is pulled from the blood into the gut lumen, so serum potassium falls within hours.", "body": [["Blood", "Serum potassium falls, often starting within about an hour, which lowers the risk of hyperkalemic arrhythmias over time."], ["Heart", "Lower potassium stabilizes resting membrane potential, but the effect is too slow for emergencies, so calcium, insulin and other acute treatments are still needed."], ["Gut", "The crystal stays in the gut and leaves in stool. It is generally well tolerated without the bowel injury seen with older resins."], ["Fluid balance", "Released sodium is partly absorbed, which can cause swelling (edema), especially in heart or kidney disease."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth as a powder mixed in water, the crystal is not absorbed into the blood. It travels the full length of the gut, binding potassium along the way, and leaves in the stool. Serum potassium starts to drop within about an hour and keeps falling over several hours. There is no liver metabolism or kidney clearance. Because it can bind other drugs in the gut and changes stomach pH, other oral drugs are separated in time.", "sidefx": "Edema comes from the sodium the crystal releases in exchange for potassium. Hypokalemia happens when binding removes more potassium than intended, so levels are monitored. It can transiently raise stomach pH, which changes absorption of drugs whose uptake depends on acid. It is radiopaque and may show up on abdominal X-rays. There is no antidote; stopping the drug ends its effect.", "fun": "Because the drug is a crystal sieve, it is essentially a mineral filter for potassium, chosen by pore size rather than by chemical charge alone.", "src": ["Lokelma (sodium zirconium cyclosilicate) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "sofosbuvir": {"origin": "Hepatitis C virus was identified in 1989. For two decades treatment relied on interferon injections plus ribavirin, which caused flu-like illness and cured only some patients. At the biotech company Pharmasset, chemist Michael Sofia led work on a nucleotide prodrug that could slip into liver cells and block the viral polymerase. The compound later named sofosbuvir was made around 2007. Gilead bought Pharmasset in 2012 for about 11 billion dollars, and the FDA approved sofosbuvir as Sovaldi in 2013. Combination pills followed, including ledipasvir-sofosbuvir (Harvoni) and the pangenotypic velpatasvir-sofosbuvir (Epclusa). These all-oral regimens cure most patients in weeks.", "timeline": [["1989", "Hepatitis C virus is identified."], ["2007", "Michael Sofia's team at Pharmasset makes the compound later named sofosbuvir."], ["2012", "Gilead acquires Pharmasset."], ["2013", "FDA approves sofosbuvir (Sovaldi)."], ["2014", "FDA approves ledipasvir-sofosbuvir (Harvoni)."], ["2016", "FDA approves velpatasvir-sofosbuvir (Epclusa); Sofia shares the Lasker clinical research award."]], "cell": "Sofosbuvir is a phosphoramidate prodrug of a uridine nucleotide. The prodrug design carries a phosphate group into hepatocytes, skipping the slow first phosphorylation step. Inside the liver cell, enzymes remove the protecting groups and host kinases add two more phosphates, forming the active triphosphate GS-461203. The HCV NS5B RNA-dependent RNA polymerase mistakes this for a natural uridine nucleotide and incorporates it into the growing viral RNA. A modified sugar then blocks addition of the next nucleotide, so the chain terminates. Because the NS5B active site is highly conserved, sofosbuvir works across genotypes and has a high barrier to resistance.", "body": [["Liver", "Viral replication in hepatocytes stops, viral load drops quickly, and liver inflammation settles. Cure means lower risk of cirrhosis and liver cancer."], ["Heart", "Given with amiodarone, sofosbuvir regimens can cause serious, sometimes fatal, slow heart rhythms. The exact mechanism is not fully known."], ["Immune system and HBV", "In people also infected with hepatitis B, clearing hepatitis C can let HBV flare, sometimes with liver failure, so HBV is screened before treatment."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue, headache, nausea and insomnia are the most common complaints and are usually mild."]], "journey": "Sofosbuvir is taken by mouth and absorbed quickly. The parent drug is short-lived in plasma because it is rapidly taken up and converted in the liver to the active triphosphate, which works inside hepatocytes. The triphosphate is eventually broken down to an inactive nucleoside metabolite, GS-331007, which circulates with a much longer half-life and is cleared mainly by the kidneys. It is a substrate of the P-gp transporter, so strong P-gp inducers such as rifampin lower its levels.", "sidefx": "Sofosbuvir targets a viral enzyme that human cells lack, so it is usually well tolerated. Fatigue and headache are common. Severe bradycardia with amiodarone is a key warning. Hepatitis B reactivation is a class warning for HCV direct-acting antivirals, because removing HCV can release suppression of HBV. P-gp inducers such as rifampin and St. John's wort lower levels and risk treatment failure. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Sofosbuvir drew worldwide attention for its launch price of about 1,000 dollars per pill, sparking a long debate on drug pricing.", "src": ["Sovaldi (sofosbuvir) FDA label", "Sofia MJ et al. J Med Chem 2010", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "somatostatin": {"origin": "Somatostatin is a natural hormone. In 1973 Paul Brazeau, Roger Guillemin and colleagues at the Salk Institute isolated it from sheep hypothalamus while searching for factors that control growth hormone. They found a small peptide that blocked growth hormone release and named it somatostatin. It was later found in the pancreas, gut and many nerves, where it acts as a general 'off switch' for hormone secretion. Guillemin shared the 1977 Nobel Prize for hypothalamic hormone work. Natural somatostatin lasts only minutes in blood, so longer-acting analogs such as octreotide were developed. Somatostatin itself is used outside the US, mainly to lower portal pressure in variceal bleeding.", "timeline": [["1973", "Brazeau, Guillemin and colleagues isolate somatostatin from sheep hypothalamus."], ["1977", "Guillemin shares the Nobel Prize for work on hypothalamic peptide hormones."], ["1988", "FDA approves octreotide, a long-acting somatostatin analog."]], "cell": "Somatostatin binds five G protein-coupled receptors, SSTR1 through SSTR5. These couple to Gi, which inhibits adenylyl cyclase and lowers cAMP. They also open potassium channels and close voltage-gated calcium channels, which hyperpolarizes cells and lowers calcium entry. The net effect is less exocytosis of hormone granules. Growth hormone, TSH, insulin, glucagon, gastrin, secretin, VIP and other gut hormones all fall. In the splanchnic circulation, lower glucagon and other vasodilator peptides plus direct effects lead to mesenteric vasoconstriction, which reduces portal blood flow and pressure.", "body": [["Splanchnic circulation", "Blood flow into the portal system falls, which lowers portal pressure and helps control bleeding from esophageal varices."], ["Pituitary", "Release of growth hormone and TSH is inhibited."], ["Pancreas", "Both insulin and glucagon release fall, so blood glucose may rise or fall; hyperglycemia is a known effect."], ["Gut", "Stomach acid, pancreatic enzymes and gut motility decrease, and secretions from hormone-making tumors fall."], ["Heart", "Slow heart rate and heart block can occur, more often with rapid bolus injection."]], "journey": "Somatostatin is a peptide, so it must be given IV, usually as a bolus followed by continuous infusion. Peptidases in the blood and tissues break it down within one to three minutes. That short half-life is why it must be infused continuously and why long-acting analogs were developed. Fragments are cleared by the kidneys. Its effects stop soon after the infusion ends.", "sidefx": "Side effects reflect broad hormone suppression. Nausea, abdominal cramps and flushing come from changes in gut secretion and motility. Blood glucose may swing because both insulin and glucagon are suppressed. Slow heart rate can occur. Because it acts so briefly, side effects fade fast after stopping. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The name means 'body standstill' (soma for body, statin for stopping), because it was first found as the hormone that stops growth hormone.", "src": ["Brazeau P et al. Science 1973", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "sotalol": {"origin": "Sotalol was made in the 1960s as one of the early beta blockers, following the success of propranolol. In 1970 pharmacologists Bramah Singh and Miles Vaughan Williams at Oxford reported that it also lengthened the cardiac action potential, an effect they called class III antiarrhythmic action. That made sotalol unusual: one molecule with both class II and class III effects. It was approved in the US as Betapace in 1992 for serious ventricular arrhythmias, and later as Betapace AF to maintain normal rhythm in atrial fibrillation and flutter. Its tendency to cause torsades de pointes is why starting it usually involves hospital ECG monitoring.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Sotalol is developed as an early beta blocker."], ["1970", "Singh and Vaughan Williams describe its class III action of prolonging repolarization."], ["1992", "FDA approves sotalol (Betapace) for ventricular arrhythmias."]], "cell": "Sotalol is a racemic mixture. Both forms block the rapid delayed rectifier potassium current (IKr, carried by hERG channels) in heart muscle. Less potassium leaves the cell during phase 3, so repolarization slows, the action potential and refractory period lengthen, and the QT interval grows. Reentry circuits become harder to sustain. The l-isomer also competitively blocks beta-1 and beta-2 receptors, lowering cAMP. This slows the sinus rate and AV nodal conduction. Sotalol has no intrinsic sympathomimetic activity and no membrane-stabilizing effect.", "body": [["Heart rhythm", "Longer refractory periods in atria and ventricles suppress reentrant arrhythmias and help maintain sinus rhythm."], ["Heart rate and AV node", "Beta blockade slows the sinus rate and AV conduction, which can cause bradycardia and heart block."], ["ECG", "The QT interval lengthens in a dose-related way; excessive QT prolongation predicts torsades de pointes."], ["Lungs", "Nonselective beta-2 blockade can tighten airways in asthma."], ["Kidneys", "Sotalol is cleared unchanged by the kidneys, so kidney function strongly affects levels and QT risk."]], "journey": "Sotalol is taken by mouth and almost completely absorbed, with no first-pass metabolism. It is not protein bound and is not metabolized by the liver. It is water soluble, so little enters the brain. It leaves the body unchanged in the urine with a half-life of about 12 hours, longer in kidney disease. Food reduces absorption somewhat. Because of its renal clearance and QT risk, therapy is usually started in a monitored setting with checks of creatinine clearance and QT.", "sidefx": "Blocking IKr is both the benefit and the main danger. Too much repolarization delay lets early afterdepolarizations trigger torsades de pointes, especially with low potassium or magnesium, slow heart rates, kidney failure or other QT drugs. Beta blockade explains fatigue, bradycardia, AV block, worsening heart failure and bronchospasm. There is no specific antidote; torsades is treated with magnesium and correction of electrolytes, and severe beta blockade is managed like beta blocker toxicity.", "fun": "Sotalol is the drug often used to show students that one molecule can sit in two Vaughan Williams classes at once (II and III).", "src": ["Betapace / Betapace AF (sotalol) FDA label", "Singh BN, Vaughan Williams EM. Br J Pharmacol 1970", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "spironolactone": {"origin": "Aldosterone was isolated in 1953 by Sylvia Simpson and James Tait. Soon after, chemists at G.D. Searle in Chicago looked for a steroid that could block it. In 1957 John Cella and colleagues described spirolactone steroids that opposed aldosterone's salt-retaining action. Spironolactone was approved by the FDA in 1960 as Aldactone. For decades it was a modest diuretic used for edema and primary aldosteronism. In 1999 the RALES trial showed that adding it to standard therapy lowered death rates in severe heart failure, turning an old diuretic into a key heart failure drug.", "timeline": [["1953", "Simpson and Tait isolate aldosterone."], ["1957", "Searle chemists describe spirolactones as aldosterone blockers."], ["1960", "FDA approves spironolactone (Aldactone)."], ["1999", "RALES trial shows lower mortality in severe heart failure."]], "cell": "Aldosterone normally enters principal cells of the late distal tubule and collecting duct and binds the cytoplasmic mineralocorticoid receptor. The complex moves to the nucleus and increases transcription of ENaC sodium channels, ROMK potassium channels and the basolateral Na/K ATPase. Spironolactone competitively binds the mineralocorticoid receptor without activating it. Fewer sodium channels and pumps are made, so less sodium is reabsorbed and less potassium and hydrogen are secreted. Because the effect works through gene expression, it builds over days. Spironolactone also blocks androgen receptors and weakly affects progesterone receptors, which explains its antiandrogen effects. In the heart, mineralocorticoid blockade reduces fibrosis and remodeling.", "body": [["Kidneys", "Mild sodium and water loss with potassium retention. Hydrogen ion secretion also falls, which can cause mild acidosis."], ["Heart", "Blocking aldosterone in heart tissue reduces fibrosis and remodeling, which lowers mortality in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction."], ["Blood vessels", "Blood pressure falls, especially in resistant hypertension and primary aldosteronism."], ["Liver and ascites", "In cirrhosis, where aldosterone is high, it is a mainstay for reducing ascites."], ["Endocrine and skin", "Antiandrogen effects cause gynecomastia and breast tenderness in men and can be used to treat acne and hirsutism in women."]], "journey": "Spironolactone is taken by mouth and absorbed well, especially with food. It is highly protein bound. The liver rapidly converts it to active metabolites, including canrenone and 7-alpha-thiomethylspironolactone, which carry most of the effect and last much longer than the parent drug. Metabolites leave in urine and bile. Because it works through gene transcription, the diuretic effect takes days to build and fades slowly after stopping.", "sidefx": "Hyperkalemia is the main danger because blocking aldosterone stops potassium secretion. Risk is highest with kidney disease, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, potassium supplements or salt substitutes. Gynecomastia, breast pain, impotence and menstrual changes come from androgen receptor blockade. Hyponatremia and dehydration can occur. There is no antidote; hyperkalemia is treated with standard measures such as calcium, insulin with glucose and potassium removal.", "fun": "Eplerenone was later designed as a more selective mineralocorticoid blocker to avoid spironolactone's gynecomastia.", "src": ["Aldactone (spironolactone) FDA label", "Pitt B et al. RALES, N Engl J Med 1999", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "succimer": {"origin": "Succimer descends from dimercaprol, also called British anti-Lewisite (BAL), which British scientists developed during World War II as an antidote to the arsenic war gas lewisite. Dimercaprol worked but was oily, painful to inject and toxic. Succimer, meso-2,3-dimercaptosuccinic acid (DMSA), is a water-soluble cousin with the same pair of neighboring sulfur groups. It was studied as a metal antidote from the 1950s onward. The FDA approved it as Chemet in 1991 for lead poisoning in children, making it the first oral chelator approved for that use in the US. It let many children be treated at home instead of with injections.", "timeline": [["1940s", "Dimercaprol (British anti-Lewisite) is developed as an antidote to the arsenic war gas lewisite."], ["1950s", "DMSA is studied as a metal antidote."], ["1991", "FDA approves succimer (Chemet) for lead poisoning in children."]], "cell": "Lead, mercury and arsenic poison cells by binding sulfhydryl groups on enzymes. Lead, for example, blocks delta-aminolevulinic acid dehydratase and ferrochelatase in heme synthesis. Succimer has two vicinal sulfhydryl groups that grab the metal ion tightly and form a stable, water-soluble ring complex (a chelate). The complex is filtered by the kidneys and excreted in urine. Pulling lead out of blood and soft tissues frees enzymes to work again. Succimer binds little of the essential minerals calcium, iron and magnesium, which makes it safer than older chelators.", "body": [["Blood", "Blood lead levels fall during treatment, and heme synthesis enzymes recover. Levels may rebound after treatment as lead moves out of bone."], ["Brain", "Lowering lead is meant to limit further damage, though chelation does not clearly reverse cognitive harm that already happened."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys excrete the lead-succimer complex, so urine lead rises during therapy."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea and a sulfur taste or odor are common."], ["Liver and marrow", "Mild rises in liver enzymes and rare neutropenia can occur, so blood counts and liver tests are watched."]], "journey": "Succimer is taken by mouth; capsules can be opened and sprinkled on food for children. It is absorbed rapidly but variably. In the blood it is largely bound to proteins and is extensively converted to mixed disulfides with cysteine. Both the drug and its metal complexes are cleared mainly by the kidneys, with some in feces. Blood lead begins to fall within days. A course lasts a few weeks, and blood lead is rechecked afterward because bone stores can release more lead.", "sidefx": "Gut upset and the sulfur smell come from its sulfhydryl groups. Rash, rises in liver enzymes and occasional neutropenia are reported, so labs are checked. Removing lead does not fix the source, so ongoing exposure must be stopped. It should not be given with other chelators unless directed. Succimer is itself the antidote here; there is no reversal agent for it.", "fun": "Its parent, dimercaprol, got the name British anti-Lewisite because it was built to counter lewisite, an arsenic blister gas.", "src": ["Chemet (succimer) FDA label", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "succinylcholine": {"origin": "Succinylcholine was first made in 1906 by Reid Hunt and René de M. Taveau, who were studying choline compounds. Their test animals had been given curare, which hid the drug's paralyzing effect, so they missed it. Its neuromuscular blocking action was recognized in 1949 by Daniel Bovet and colleagues in Rome. In 1951 anesthesiologists in Stockholm and Vienna began using it in patients. Its very fast onset and short action made rapid sequence intubation practical. Bovet later received the 1957 Nobel Prize, partly for work on muscle relaxants.", "timeline": [["1906", "Hunt and Taveau synthesize succinylcholine but miss its paralytic effect."], ["1949", "Bovet and colleagues describe its neuromuscular blocking action."], ["1951", "First clinical use in anesthesia in Stockholm and Vienna."], ["1957", "Daniel Bovet receives the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."]], "cell": "Succinylcholine is essentially two acetylcholine molecules joined together. It binds the nicotinic (Nm) acetylcholine receptors on the motor end plate and opens them, depolarizing the membrane. This causes brief, disorganized muscle twitches (fasciculations). Unlike acetylcholine, it is not broken down by acetylcholinesterase at the synapse, so the end plate stays depolarized. Nearby voltage-gated sodium channels become stuck in their inactivated state and cannot reset, so no new action potentials fire and the muscle goes limp. This is phase I block. With prolonged exposure the receptors become desensitized and the block changes to a phase II block that resembles a nondepolarizing block.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Brief fasciculations are followed by flaccid paralysis of all skeletal muscles, including the diaphragm, so breathing must be supported."], ["Blood potassium", "Depolarization lets potassium leave muscle cells, raising serum potassium. In burns, crush injury, denervation or long immobility, extra receptors spread across the muscle and potassium can rise to fatal levels."], ["Heart", "It also stimulates muscarinic receptors in the SA node, which can cause bradycardia, especially in children or with repeat doses."], ["Eye and stomach", "Muscle contraction briefly raises intraocular and intragastric pressure."], ["Muscle metabolism", "In people with a susceptible ryanodine receptor, it can trigger malignant hyperthermia: uncontrolled calcium release, rigidity, high CO2 and fever."]], "journey": "Succinylcholine is given IV (or IM when no IV is available). It reaches the neuromuscular junction within about a minute. Most of the dose is broken down in the plasma by butyrylcholinesterase (pseudocholinesterase) before it even reaches the muscle. The drug at the junction is ended by diffusion back into plasma, where the enzyme destroys it. Function returns within about ten minutes. People with inherited pseudocholinesterase deficiency can stay paralyzed for hours. It is ionized and does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so it provides no sedation.", "sidefx": "Every major side effect follows from it being a depolarizing agonist. Persistent depolarization releases potassium and can cause hyperkalemic cardiac arrest in at-risk patients. Fasciculations cause muscle aches after surgery. Muscarinic stimulation causes bradycardia. It is a trigger for malignant hyperthermia, treated with dantrolene. Prolonged apnea occurs with pseudocholinesterase deficiency; the treatment is sedation and ventilation until it wears off. There is no reversal agent; neostigmine can worsen phase I block and sugammadex does not bind it.", "fun": "Because the 1906 inventors' animals were already paralyzed with curare, one of anesthesia's most important drugs sat unnoticed for over 40 years.", "src": ["Anectine / Quelicin (succinylcholine) FDA label", "Miller's Anesthesia", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "sucralfate": {"origin": "Sucralfate was developed in Japan by Chugai Pharmaceutical in the 1960s and reached the Japanese market in 1968. It is a complex of sucrose octasulfate and aluminum hydroxide. Unlike antacids or acid blockers, it was designed to coat and protect the ulcer itself. It was approved in the US in 1981 as Carafate. It became a common option for duodenal ulcers and is sometimes used for stress ulcer prevention because it does not raise stomach pH.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Chugai Pharmaceutical in Japan develops sucralfate."], ["1968", "Sucralfate is marketed in Japan."], ["1981", "FDA approves sucralfate (Carafate)."]], "cell": "In stomach acid, sucralfate releases aluminum and its sucrose octasulfate part becomes a sticky, negatively charged paste. The paste binds positively charged proteins such as albumin and fibrinogen that are exposed in the ulcer base. This forms a physical barrier that shields the ulcer from acid, pepsin and bile salts. It also binds pepsin and bile acids directly. Sucralfate stimulates local prostaglandin production, mucus and bicarbonate secretion, and binds growth factors such as epidermal growth factor at the ulcer site, which supports healing.", "body": [["Stomach and duodenum", "A protective coat forms over ulcers, letting them heal while being protected from acid and pepsin."], ["Gut motility", "Aluminum slows the bowel, so constipation is the most common side effect."], ["Gut lumen", "In people with slow stomach emptying or tube feeds, the paste can clump into a bezoar."], ["Kidneys and bones", "A small amount of aluminum is absorbed. In kidney failure it can build up and cause bone and brain toxicity."]], "journey": "Sucralfate is taken by mouth, usually before meals and at bedtime. It needs acid to activate, which is why it is given on an empty stomach. Very little is absorbed into the blood; the small sucrose sulfate and aluminum amounts that are absorbed are cleared by the kidneys. Most of the drug stays in the gut and leaves in the stool. Its coating effect lasts around six hours.", "sidefx": "Because it works locally, side effects are mostly in the gut. Aluminum causes constipation. Clumping can form bezoars. Absorbed aluminum can accumulate in kidney failure. The paste binds other drugs, such as fluoroquinolones, levothyroxine, digoxin, phenytoin and warfarin, so they are spaced apart in time. Some forms contain sugar and can raise glucose in diabetes. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Sucralfate's name blends 'sucrose' and 'aluminum sulfate,' the two parts of the complex.", "src": ["Carafate (sucralfate) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "sugammadex": {"origin": "Sugammadex was designed rather than found. In the late 1990s Anton Bom and colleagues at Organon's research lab in Newhouse, Scotland, looked for a molecule that could grab rocuronium out of the blood instead of fighting it at the receptor. They modified a gamma-cyclodextrin, a ring of eight sugar units, adding side chains that make a deep pocket that fits the steroid nucleus of rocuronium. It was approved in Europe in 2008. US approval was delayed by questions about allergic reactions, and the FDA approved it as Bridion in 2015. It made fast, reliable reversal of deep rocuronium block possible.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Anton Bom's team at Organon designs a modified cyclodextrin to bind rocuronium."], ["2008", "Sugammadex is approved in the European Union."], ["2015", "FDA approves sugammadex (Bridion)."]], "cell": "Sugammadex is a modified gamma-cyclodextrin, a doughnut-shaped ring of sugars with a water-friendly outside and a fat-friendly central cavity. The aminosteroid neuromuscular blockers rocuronium and vecuronium fit into this cavity. Each sugammadex molecule binds one blocker molecule in a tight one-to-one complex, rocuronium most strongly. This removes free drug from plasma. A concentration gradient then pulls rocuronium away from the neuromuscular junction back into the plasma, where it too is captured. Nicotinic receptors are freed, acetylcholine can work normally, and muscle strength returns. It does not act on acetylcholinesterase, so it causes none of the muscarinic effects of neostigmine.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Strength returns within minutes, even from deep rocuronium block, so breathing and airway protection recover."], ["Heart", "Bradycardia, sometimes marked and rarely leading to arrest, has been reported soon after giving it."], ["Immune system", "Hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis, can occur."], ["Hormones", "It can bind progestogen, so hormonal contraceptives may be less effective for about a week afterward and backup contraception is advised."], ["Blood clotting", "It can briefly prolong some lab clotting times such as aPTT and PT."]], "journey": "Sugammadex is given IV and works within a few minutes. It stays mostly in the blood and extracellular fluid. It is not metabolized. Both free sugammadex and the sugammadex-rocuronium complex are excreted unchanged by the kidneys, with a half-life of about two hours in normal kidney function. In severe kidney failure clearance is very slow and use is not recommended.", "sidefx": "Bradycardia and hypotension may relate to rapid changes after reversal, but the mechanism is not fully known. Anaphylaxis is an immune reaction to the molecule itself. Lower effect of hormonal contraceptives happens because the cavity binds progestogens. If another dose of rocuronium is needed soon after, sugammadex still in the blood can bind it, so paralysis may be harder to re-establish. Sugammadex does not reverse succinylcholine or benzylisoquinolines like cisatracurium.", "fun": "The name blends 'sugar' and 'gamma-cyclodextrin,' describing the sugar ring at the heart of the molecule.", "src": ["Bridion (sugammadex) FDA label", "Bom A et al. Angew Chem Int Ed 2002", "Miller's Anesthesia"]}, "sulfasalazine": {"origin": "In the late 1930s and early 1940s, Swedish physician Nanna Svartz at the Karolinska Institute in Stockholm thought rheumatoid arthritis might be caused by an infection. Working with the company Pharmacia, she joined a sulfa antibiotic (sulfapyridine) to an anti-inflammatory salicylate (5-aminosalicylic acid). The result, sulfasalazine, helped arthritis and unexpectedly helped ulcerative colitis too. It reached the US market in 1950 as Azulfidine. In 1977 a study by Azad Khan, Piris and Truelove showed that 5-ASA was the active part in colitis, which led to the newer mesalamine drugs.", "timeline": [["1940s", "Nanna Svartz and Pharmacia develop sulfasalazine in Sweden."], ["1950", "Sulfasalazine is approved in the US as Azulfidine."], ["1977", "Study shows 5-ASA is the active part in ulcerative colitis."]], "cell": "Sulfasalazine is a prodrug made of sulfapyridine and 5-aminosalicylic acid (5-ASA) joined by an azo bond. Little is absorbed in the small intestine. In the colon, bacterial azoreductases break the bond and release the two parts. 5-ASA stays mostly in the colon and acts locally: it inhibits cyclooxygenase and lipoxygenase (fewer prostaglandins and leukotrienes), scavenges free radicals, activates PPAR-gamma and dampens NF-kB signaling in the lining. Sulfapyridine is absorbed and acts systemically; it is thought to account for much of the benefit in rheumatoid arthritis and many side effects. Its effects include reducing inflammatory cytokines and increasing adenosine release.", "body": [["Colon", "Inflammation in the lining of the colon falls, which reduces bleeding, diarrhea and flares in ulcerative colitis."], ["Joints", "In rheumatoid arthritis and spondyloarthritis, joint swelling and pain improve over weeks to months."], ["Blood", "It can cause hemolysis in G6PD deficiency, folate deficiency with large red cells and, rarely, agranulocytosis or aplastic anemia."], ["Skin", "Rash, photosensitivity and, rarely, severe reactions like SJS or TEN can occur, reflecting the sulfa component."], ["Reproductive system", "In men it can cause reversible low sperm count."], ["Urine and skin color", "It can turn urine and sometimes skin orange-yellow."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth, only a small part of intact sulfasalazine is absorbed. Most reaches the colon, where bacteria split it. 5-ASA is poorly absorbed and mostly leaves in the stool, with some acetylated and excreted in urine. Sulfapyridine is well absorbed, acetylated in the liver by NAT2 and excreted in urine. Slow acetylators build up more sulfapyridine and have more side effects. Clinical benefit takes weeks for colitis and longer for arthritis.", "sidefx": "Most systemic side effects come from sulfapyridine, a sulfonamide. Nausea, headache and rash are common and dose related, worse in slow acetylators. Hemolysis in G6PD deficiency reflects oxidant stress on red cells. Folate malabsorption causes megaloblastic anemia, so folate is often given. Rare idiosyncratic reactions include agranulocytosis, hepatitis, DRESS and pneumonitis. Orange urine is harmless. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Svartz designed it around a wrong idea (that arthritis was an infection), yet the drug worked and is still used today.", "src": ["Azulfidine (sulfasalazine) FDA label", "Azad Khan AK, Piris J, Truelove SC. Lancet 1977", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "sumatriptan": {"origin": "In the 1970s and 1980s, a team led by pharmacologist Patrick Humphrey at Glaxo in England set out to build a drug that would narrow swollen blood vessels in the head during migraine, copying an effect of serotonin without its many unwanted actions. They targeted a then-new subtype of serotonin receptor, later called 5-HT1B/1D. The result was sumatriptan, the first triptan. It launched in Europe in 1991 and was approved by the FDA in 1992 as Imitrex injection, followed by tablets and a nasal spray. It changed migraine care from general pain relief to a targeted attack treatment.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Patrick Humphrey's team at Glaxo begins searching for a selective serotonin-like migraine drug."], ["1991", "Sumatriptan launches in Europe."], ["1992", "FDA approves sumatriptan injection (Imitrex)."]], "cell": "Sumatriptan is a selective agonist at serotonin 5-HT1B and 5-HT1D receptors, which couple to Gi and lower cAMP. On smooth muscle of dilated meningeal and other intracranial arteries, 5-HT1B activation causes vasoconstriction. On trigeminal nerve endings, 5-HT1D activation blocks the release of CGRP, substance P and neurokinin A, which reduces neurogenic inflammation and vessel dilation. It also dampens pain signal transmission in the trigeminal nucleus. Together these effects stop the migraine attack once it has started.", "body": [["Brain vessels", "Dilated meningeal arteries constrict, easing the throbbing pain of migraine."], ["Trigeminal nerve", "Less release of CGRP and other neuropeptides reduces inflammation and pain signaling, relieving headache, nausea and light sensitivity."], ["Heart", "5-HT1B receptors are also on coronary arteries, so triptans can cause coronary spasm. They are avoided in coronary disease and uncontrolled hypertension."], ["Chest and throat", "Many people feel tightness, pressure or tingling in the chest, throat or neck, usually not from heart ischemia."], ["Skin and nerves", "Flushing, warmth and tingling are common, especially after the injection."]], "journey": "Sumatriptan can be given by subcutaneous injection, nasal spray or tablet. The injection works fastest, within about ten minutes, and is nearly fully absorbed. Tablets have low bioavailability because of first-pass metabolism. It is mainly broken down by monoamine oxidase A (MAO-A) to an inactive metabolite excreted in urine. Its half-life is about two hours, which is why migraine can come back the same day. MAO inhibitors raise its levels and are a contraindication.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow from 5-HT1B vasoconstriction outside the head: rarely coronary spasm, myocardial infarction, stroke or peripheral ischemia. Chest tightness and tingling are common but usually benign. Combining it with SSRIs or SNRIs has been linked with serotonin syndrome warnings. Frequent use can cause medication overuse headache. There is no antidote; treatment is supportive.", "fun": "Sumatriptan gave the whole triptan class its name, and every later drug ending in '-triptan' follows its design.", "src": ["Imitrex (sumatriptan) FDA label", "Humphrey PPA et al. Br J Pharmacol 1988", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "tacrolimus": {"origin": "Tacrolimus comes from a soil bacterium. In 1984 scientists at Fujisawa Pharmaceutical in Japan isolated it from Streptomyces tsukubaensis, found in a soil sample near Mount Tsukuba. Coded FK506, it was a macrolide far more potent than cyclosporine at blocking T cells. Transplant surgeon Thomas Starzl at the University of Pittsburgh led early clinical trials around 1989, showing it could rescue failing liver transplants. The FDA approved it as Prograf in 1994 for liver transplants, and later for kidney and heart. It is now the backbone of most transplant regimens.", "timeline": [["1984", "Fujisawa scientists isolate FK506 from Streptomyces tsukubaensis."], ["1989", "Thomas Starzl's team reports early transplant use in Pittsburgh."], ["1994", "FDA approves tacrolimus (Prograf) for liver transplant."], ["2000", "FDA approves tacrolimus ointment (Protopic) for atopic dermatitis."]], "cell": "When a T cell receptor recognizes an antigen, calcium rises inside the cell and activates calcineurin, a calcium-calmodulin-dependent phosphatase. Calcineurin removes phosphates from NFAT (nuclear factor of activated T cells), letting NFAT enter the nucleus and switch on the gene for interleukin-2 and other cytokines. Tacrolimus enters the T cell and binds FKBP-12 (an immunophilin). The tacrolimus-FKBP12 complex binds and inhibits calcineurin. NFAT stays phosphorylated in the cytoplasm, IL-2 is not made, and T cells cannot multiply in response to the graft. Cyclosporine blocks the same enzyme but binds cyclophilin instead.", "body": [["Immune system", "T cell activation and IL-2 production are blocked, which prevents organ rejection but raises the risk of infections and some cancers, especially skin cancer and lymphoma."], ["Kidneys", "Constriction of the afferent arteriole and chronic tubular injury cause acute and chronic nephrotoxicity, plus hyperkalemia and magnesium loss."], ["Brain and nerves", "Tremor and headache are common. Rarely it causes seizures or posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome (PRES)."], ["Pancreas", "It is toxic to beta cells and can cause new-onset diabetes after transplant, more often than cyclosporine."], ["Blood vessels", "Constriction of blood vessels, including in the kidney, raises blood pressure, so many transplant patients need antihypertensives."]], "journey": "Tacrolimus is taken by mouth (also IV or as an ointment). Absorption is poor and variable. It is a substrate of both CYP3A4 and P-gp in the gut wall and liver. In blood it concentrates in red cells and is highly protein bound. The liver metabolizes it through CYP3A4, and metabolites leave in bile. Its half-life is roughly 12 hours. Because levels swing with diet, genetics (CYP3A5) and drug interactions, whole blood trough levels are monitored.", "sidefx": "Calcineurin is not only in T cells, so blocking it causes harm elsewhere. In the kidney, vasoconstriction and tubular damage cause nephrotoxicity and hyperkalemia. In the brain, tremor, headache and PRES. In beta cells, reduced insulin release causes diabetes. Broad immune suppression raises infection and cancer risk. CYP3A4 inhibitors like azoles and macrolides can push levels into toxicity; inducers like rifampin can trigger rejection. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The name tacrolimus comes from 'Tsukuba macrolide immunosuppressant,' after the mountain where the soil was collected.", "src": ["Prograf (tacrolimus) FDA label", "Starzl TE et al. Lancet 1989", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "tadalafil": {"origin": "Tadalafil came out of the biotech company ICOS, which began working on a phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitor in the early 1990s with Glaxo, then later through a joint venture with Eli Lilly. After sildenafil (Viagra) showed that blocking PDE-5 treats erectile dysfunction, ICOS and Lilly developed tadalafil with a much longer half-life. The FDA approved it as Cialis in 2003. Its long action earned it the nickname 'the weekend pill.' It was later approved for pulmonary arterial hypertension (Adcirca, 2009) and for symptoms of benign prostatic hyperplasia (2011).", "timeline": [["1990s", "ICOS discovers the compound that becomes tadalafil."], ["2003", "FDA approves tadalafil (Cialis) for erectile dysfunction."], ["2009", "FDA approves tadalafil (Adcirca) for pulmonary arterial hypertension."], ["2011", "FDA approves tadalafil for benign prostatic hyperplasia symptoms."]], "cell": "In the corpus cavernosum, sexual stimulation causes nerves and endothelium to release nitric oxide. NO activates soluble guanylyl cyclase, which raises cGMP. cGMP activates protein kinase G, lowering intracellular calcium and relaxing smooth muscle, so blood fills the erectile tissue. PDE-5 normally breaks down cGMP. Tadalafil selectively inhibits PDE-5, so cGMP stays high and relaxation lasts longer. It does not cause an erection without stimulation, because NO must start the process. The same mechanism relaxes pulmonary arteries and smooth muscle in the prostate and bladder neck. It inhibits PDE-6 in the retina less than sildenafil but has some effect on PDE-11.", "body": [["Penis", "Increased cGMP relaxes cavernosal smooth muscle so erections occur more easily in response to sexual stimulation."], ["Lungs", "Pulmonary arteries relax, lowering pulmonary vascular resistance and improving exercise capacity in PAH."], ["Prostate and bladder", "Relaxing smooth muscle in the lower urinary tract eases urinary symptoms of BPH."], ["Blood vessels", "Mild systemic vasodilation causes flushing, headache, nasal congestion and a small fall in blood pressure."], ["Muscles", "Back pain and muscle aches are more common than with other PDE-5 inhibitors, possibly linked to PDE-11."]], "journey": "Tadalafil is taken by mouth and absorbed well; food does not change absorption much. It is highly protein bound. The liver metabolizes it through CYP3A4, and inactive metabolites leave mostly in the feces. Its half-life is about 17.5 hours, so effects can last up to about 36 hours. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels and inducers lower them.", "sidefx": "Headache, flushing and nasal congestion come from vasodilation. Combining it with nitrates is dangerous because both raise cGMP, causing severe hypotension; nitrates are avoided for at least 48 hours after a dose. Alpha blockers add to hypotension. Priapism can happen when relaxation of erectile tissue persists. Sudden vision or hearing loss is rare. There is no antidote; hypotension is treated with fluids and vasopressors.", "fun": "Its long half-life led to the 'weekend pill' nickname, since one dose can work for about a day and a half.", "src": ["Cialis (tadalafil) FDA label", "Adcirca (tadalafil) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "tamoxifen": {"origin": "Tamoxifen started as a failed contraceptive. In 1962 chemist Dora Richardson at Imperial Chemical Industries (ICI) in England made the compound ICI 46,474 for a fertility program led by Arthur Walpole. Instead of preventing pregnancy in women, it actually induced ovulation. Because it blocked estrogen, Walpole pushed to test it in breast cancer, and early trials in Manchester in the early 1970s were promising. Pharmacologist V. Craig Jordan then showed how it worked and argued for long-term use. The FDA approved it in 1977 for advanced breast cancer and in 1998 for lowering breast cancer risk in high-risk women.", "timeline": [["1962", "Dora Richardson at ICI synthesizes ICI 46,474."], ["1971", "Early clinical trial in breast cancer reported from Manchester."], ["1977", "FDA approves tamoxifen (Nolvadex) for metastatic breast cancer."], ["1998", "FDA approves tamoxifen for breast cancer risk reduction."]], "cell": "Tamoxifen is a prodrug. CYP2D6 and CYP3A4 convert it to 4-hydroxytamoxifen and especially endoxifen, which bind the estrogen receptor much more tightly. Estrogen receptors are nuclear transcription factors. In breast tissue, tamoxifen-bound receptors adopt a shape that recruits corepressors instead of coactivators, so estrogen-driven genes for growth (such as cyclin D1) are not turned on. Tumor cells stall in G1 and stop dividing. In other tissues the same complex recruits coactivators, so it acts as an estrogen agonist: in bone it preserves density, in the endometrium it stimulates growth, and in the liver it raises clotting factors and lowers LDL. This mix of blocking and activating is what makes it a selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM).", "body": [["Breast", "Estrogen-receptor-positive cancer cells stop growing, lowering recurrence and death and reducing risk of new breast cancers."], ["Uterus", "Agonist action stimulates the endometrium, raising the risk of hyperplasia, polyps, endometrial cancer and uterine sarcoma."], ["Bone", "In postmenopausal women, it acts like estrogen and helps preserve bone density."], ["Blood and liver", "More clotting factors raise the risk of DVT, pulmonary embolism and stroke. LDL cholesterol falls and fatty liver can develop."], ["Brain and hypothalamus", "Estrogen blockade in the brain causes hot flashes."], ["Eyes", "Cataracts and rarely retinal changes can occur."]], "journey": "Tamoxifen is taken by mouth and well absorbed. It is highly protein bound and widely distributed. The liver converts it via CYP2D6 and CYP3A4 into active metabolites, mainly endoxifen. People with poor CYP2D6 function, or who take strong CYP2D6 inhibitors such as paroxetine or fluoxetine, make less endoxifen. Metabolites go into bile and leave in the feces. The half-life is about a week for tamoxifen and longer for its metabolites, so steady state takes weeks.", "sidefx": "Side effects reflect its two faces. Estrogen blockade in the brain and vagina causes hot flashes and vaginal symptoms. Estrogen-like action in the liver increases clotting factors, raising clot risk. Estrogen-like action in the uterus causes endometrial growth and cancer, so abnormal vaginal bleeding needs evaluation. It is teratogenic and avoided in pregnancy. There is no antidote.", "fun": "A drug meant to prevent pregnancy ended up being used to induce ovulation, and then became one of the most important breast cancer drugs in history.", "src": ["Tamoxifen FDA label", "Jordan VC. Br J Pharmacol 2006", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "tamsulosin": {"origin": "Older alpha-1 blockers like prazosin and terazosin eased urinary symptoms of an enlarged prostate but also lowered blood pressure and caused dizziness. Researchers learned that the prostate is rich in the alpha-1A receptor subtype. Yamanouchi Pharmaceutical in Japan developed tamsulosin to favor that subtype. It launched in Japan in 1993 and was approved by the FDA in 1997 as Flomax. It needs no dose titration for blood pressure and became one of the most used drugs for BPH. In 2005 surgeons Chang and Campbell described intraoperative floppy iris syndrome during cataract surgery in men taking it.", "timeline": [["1993", "Tamsulosin is launched in Japan by Yamanouchi."], ["1997", "FDA approves tamsulosin (Flomax) for BPH."], ["2005", "Intraoperative floppy iris syndrome is described with tamsulosin."]], "cell": "Smooth muscle in the prostate, prostatic urethra and bladder neck contracts when norepinephrine activates alpha-1 receptors, mainly the alpha-1A subtype. These receptors couple to Gq, activating phospholipase C, IP3 and calcium release, which tightens the muscle. Tamsulosin is a competitive antagonist with higher affinity for alpha-1A (and alpha-1D) than for alpha-1B, the subtype that dominates in blood vessels. Blocking alpha-1A relaxes muscle around the urethra, reducing outflow resistance. The bladder empties more easily and urine flows faster. It does not shrink the prostate.", "body": [["Prostate and bladder neck", "Smooth muscle relaxes, improving urine stream and reducing hesitancy, frequency and nighttime urination."], ["Blood vessels", "Less vascular effect than older alpha blockers, but orthostatic hypotension and dizziness can still occur, especially after the first dose."], ["Eyes", "Alpha-1A blockade in the iris dilator muscle can cause intraoperative floppy iris syndrome during cataract surgery, even after stopping."], ["Reproductive system", "Abnormal ejaculation, including reduced or absent ejaculate, is common."], ["Nose", "Rhinitis and nasal stuffiness can occur from vascular effects in the nasal mucosa."]], "journey": "Tamsulosin is taken by mouth as a modified-release capsule, usually about 30 minutes after the same meal each day; food affects absorption. It is highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down through CYP3A4 and CYP2D6. Metabolites leave mostly in the urine. Its half-life is about 9 to 13 hours. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ketoconazole raise levels.", "sidefx": "The side effects follow from alpha-1 blockade in places other than the prostate. Blood vessel relaxation causes dizziness, orthostatic hypotension and fainting. Iris muscle relaxation causes floppy iris during cataract surgery, so eye surgeons should be told. Ejaculatory problems come from relaxation of smooth muscle in the vas deferens and bladder neck. Priapism is rare. There is no antidote; hypotension is managed with fluids and lying the patient flat.", "fun": "Tamsulosin is also widely used off label to help small kidney stones pass, by relaxing the smooth muscle of the lower ureter.", "src": ["Flomax (tamsulosin) FDA label", "Chang DF, Campbell JR. J Cataract Refract Surg 2005", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "temazepam": {"origin": "Temazepam belongs to the benzodiazepine family that began with Leo Sternbach's work at Hoffmann-La Roche in the late 1950s, which produced chlordiazepoxide (Librium, 1960) and diazepam (Valium, 1963). Temazepam is a natural product of that chemistry: it is one of the active metabolites the body makes from diazepam, formed by adding a hydroxyl group. Developed as a drug in its own right, it became a common hypnotic for short-term treatment of insomnia, marketed in the US as Restoril. Because it skips oxidative metabolism and is simply conjugated, it was seen as a cleaner option for older patients than long-acting benzodiazepines.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Leo Sternbach at Hoffmann-La Roche discovers the benzodiazepine class."], ["1963", "Diazepam (Valium), whose metabolites include temazepam, is introduced."], ["1980s", "Temazepam is widely used in the US as the hypnotic Restoril."]], "cell": "GABA-A receptors are chloride channels made of five subunits. GABA binds between alpha and beta subunits and opens the channel. Temazepam binds a separate site between the alpha and gamma subunits (the benzodiazepine site). As a positive allosteric modulator, it increases how often the channel opens when GABA is present. More chloride enters neurons, hyperpolarizing them and making them less likely to fire. It does not open the channel without GABA, which gives benzodiazepines a ceiling on their own. Effects through alpha-1 containing receptors give sedation and sleep; others contribute to anxiety relief, muscle relaxation and anticonvulsant effects.", "body": [["Brain", "Neurons fire less, which shortens the time to fall asleep and reduces awakenings. Memory formation can be impaired (anterograde amnesia)."], ["Breathing", "Alone it causes little respiratory depression, but combined with opioids, alcohol or other sedatives it can cause dangerous respiratory depression."], ["Muscles and balance", "Muscle relaxation and slowed reflexes raise the risk of falls, especially in older adults."], ["Next day", "Residual drug can cause morning drowsiness and impaired driving."], ["Dependence", "Regular use leads to tolerance and physical dependence, and stopping abruptly can cause rebound insomnia, anxiety and rarely seizures."]], "journey": "Temazepam is taken by mouth and absorbed well, with peak levels in about one to two hours. It is highly protein bound and crosses the blood-brain barrier and placenta. The liver conjugates it directly with glucuronic acid, with no active metabolites and little dependence on CYP enzymes. The glucuronide leaves in the urine. Its half-life of about 8 to 15 hours places it in the intermediate-acting group.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness, dizziness and next-day impairment are extensions of increased GABA inhibition. Adding other CNS depressants multiplies the effect, which is the basis of the FDA boxed warning about combining benzodiazepines with opioids. Long-term use causes tolerance and dependence, and abrupt withdrawal removes inhibition and can cause seizures. Complex sleep behaviors can occur. Flumazenil is a competitive antagonist at the benzodiazepine site, but it can trigger seizures in dependent patients, so it is used cautiously.", "fun": "Temazepam, oxazepam and lorazepam are the 'LOT' benzodiazepines, which only need glucuronidation and are often preferred in liver disease.", "src": ["Restoril (temazepam) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "tenecteplase": {"origin": "Tenecteplase is an engineered version of tissue plasminogen activator (tPA), the body's own clot-dissolving enzyme. Genentech cloned human tPA in the early 1980s and marketed it as alteplase in 1987. Alteplase worked but had a very short half-life and had to be given as an infusion. Genentech scientists then changed the protein at three sites to make it last longer, bind fibrin more selectively and resist its natural inhibitor, PAI-1. The FDA approved this variant as TNKase in 2000 for heart attacks. Because it is given as a single bolus, it is useful in prehospital and rural care. In 2025 the FDA also approved it for acute ischemic stroke.", "timeline": [["1983", "Genentech scientists report cloning human tissue plasminogen activator."], ["1987", "FDA approves alteplase, the first recombinant tPA."], ["2000", "FDA approves tenecteplase (TNKase) for acute myocardial infarction."], ["2025", "FDA approves tenecteplase for acute ischemic stroke."]], "cell": "Clots are held together by fibrin. The inactive enzyme plasminogen binds fibrin in the clot. Tenecteplase also binds fibrin and cleaves the bound plasminogen into plasmin, an active protease. Plasmin breaks fibrin into fragments (fibrin degradation products such as D-dimer), dissolving the clot and restoring blood flow. Tenecteplase has three changes from native tPA. These give it slower clearance by the liver, about 14-fold greater fibrin specificity than alteplase, and resistance to plasminogen activator inhibitor-1. Higher fibrin specificity means more action at the clot and less systemic breakdown of fibrinogen, though bleeding risk remains.", "body": [["Coronary arteries", "Clots blocking a coronary artery dissolve, restoring blood flow and limiting heart muscle death in STEMI when PCI is not quickly available."], ["Brain", "In ischemic stroke, dissolving the clot can restore blood flow to threatened brain. Bleeding into the brain is the most feared complication."], ["Blood", "Clotting ability falls everywhere to some degree, so bleeding can occur from IV sites, gums, gut and urinary tract."], ["Heart rhythm", "When blood flow returns, reperfusion arrhythmias such as accelerated idioventricular rhythm can appear."], ["Immune system", "Allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis and angioedema, are rare."]], "journey": "Tenecteplase is given as a single IV bolus. It acts within minutes. It stays in the blood and binds fibrin in clots. The liver clears it, with an initial half-life of about 20 to 24 minutes and a longer terminal phase. That slower clearance compared with alteplase is what allows a single push instead of a long infusion. Its clot-dissolving effects and bleeding risk outlast its plasma levels.", "sidefx": "Bleeding is the main toxicity, because plasmin breaks down fibrin in healthy hemostatic plugs as well as in the target clot. Intracranial hemorrhage is the most dangerous, which is why strict contraindications exist, such as recent stroke, recent surgery, active bleeding and severe uncontrolled hypertension. Arrhythmias reflect reperfusion. There is no true antidote. Serious bleeding is managed by stopping antithrombotics, giving cryoprecipitate to replace fibrinogen, and antifibrinolytics such as tranexamic acid or aminocaproic acid.", "fun": "The letters TNK name the three engineered sites: T (threonine 103), N (asparagine 117) and K (the lysine-histidine-arginine-arginine stretch at 296 to 299).", "src": ["TNKase (tenecteplase) FDA label", "Pennica D et al. Nature 1983", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "tenofovir": {"origin": "Tenofovir came from work on acyclic nucleoside phosphonates by Czech chemist Antonín Holý at the Institute of Organic Chemistry and Biochemistry in Prague and Belgian virologist Erik De Clercq at the Rega Institute in Leuven, starting in the 1980s. Their compounds already carried a phosphonate group, skipping a step that viruses often rely on. Gilead Sciences licensed the work and made an oral prodrug, tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF). The FDA approved it as Viread in 2001 for HIV, and later for hepatitis B. In 2012 TDF with emtricitabine (Truvada) became the first approved HIV pre-exposure prophylaxis. A newer prodrug, tenofovir alafenamide (TAF), followed in 2015 with lower kidney and bone effects.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Holý and De Clercq develop acyclic nucleoside phosphonates."], ["2001", "FDA approves tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (Viread) for HIV."], ["2008", "FDA approves tenofovir for chronic hepatitis B."], ["2012", "FDA approves emtricitabine-TDF (Truvada) for HIV pre-exposure prophylaxis."], ["2015", "First tenofovir alafenamide product approved, in a combination pill."]], "cell": "Tenofovir is an analog of adenosine monophosphate. Because it already has a phosphonate group, host kinases need only add two phosphates to form tenofovir diphosphate. This active form competes with natural dATP for HIV reverse transcriptase and HBV polymerase. When it is built into the growing viral DNA chain, it lacks a 3'-hydroxyl group, so the next nucleotide cannot be added and the chain terminates. The virus cannot complete DNA copies of its genome. TDF and TAF are prodrugs that mask the phosphonate's negative charge so it can be absorbed. TAF is stable in plasma and is mainly activated inside cells by cathepsin A, giving high intracellular levels with lower blood levels.", "body": [["Immune system", "HIV replication falls, viral load drops and CD4 counts recover, which prevents opportunistic infections."], ["Liver", "In hepatitis B, HBV DNA falls and liver inflammation improves. Stopping it suddenly can cause a severe hepatitis flare."], ["Kidneys", "Tenofovir is taken up by proximal tubule cells and can cause tubular injury, Fanconi syndrome and falling kidney function, more with TDF."], ["Bones", "Phosphate wasting and other effects can lower bone mineral density, more with TDF than TAF."], ["Weight and lipids", "TAF regimens are linked with more weight gain and higher lipids than TDF."]], "journey": "TDF and TAF are taken by mouth. TDF is converted to tenofovir in the gut and blood, so plasma tenofovir levels are high. TAF stays intact longer in blood and is converted inside lymphocytes and hepatocytes. Inside cells, tenofovir is phosphorylated to the active diphosphate, which has a very long intracellular half-life. Tenofovir is not metabolized by CYP enzymes. It leaves the body in the urine through glomerular filtration and active tubular secretion via OAT transporters.", "sidefx": "Tenofovir weakly affects human DNA polymerases, so it causes far less mitochondrial toxicity than older NRTIs. Kidney injury comes from high drug concentrations in proximal tubule cells that it enters through OAT transporters. Bone loss relates to phosphate wasting and bone turnover. Stopping it in HBV removes suppression of the virus, causing flares. Lactic acidosis is a rare class effect. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Antonín Holý's royalties from tenofovir and related drugs helped make his Prague institute one of the best-funded research centers in Central Europe.", "src": ["Viread (tenofovir disoproxil fumarate) FDA label", "Vemlidy (tenofovir alafenamide) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "terbinafine": {"origin": "Terbinafine belongs to the allylamines, a class of antifungals discovered at the Sandoz Research Institute in Vienna. In the 1970s Anton Stütz and colleagues found the first allylamine, naftifine, partly by chance during work on other compounds. They then improved it into terbinafine in the early 1980s. Terbinafine was much more potent against dermatophytes, the fungi that infect skin, hair and nails. It was marketed as Lamisil, and the FDA approved the oral tablets in 1996 for fungal nail infection. Because it builds up in keratin, it became a leading oral treatment for onychomycosis.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Sandoz researchers in Vienna discover naftifine, the first allylamine antifungal."], ["1980s", "Terbinafine is developed as a more potent allylamine."], ["1996", "FDA approves oral terbinafine (Lamisil) for onychomycosis."]], "cell": "Fungal cell membranes use ergosterol instead of cholesterol. Ergosterol is made from squalene through several steps. Terbinafine inhibits fungal squalene epoxidase (squalene monooxygenase), an early enzyme that converts squalene to squalene epoxide on the way to lanosterol. Two things happen. Ergosterol runs out, so the membrane becomes leaky and unstable. Squalene builds up inside the cell to toxic levels and damages membranes. This makes terbinafine fungicidal against dermatophytes. It acts earlier in the pathway than azoles, which block lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase. It has much weaker effect on the human version of the enzyme.", "body": [["Nails, skin and hair", "The drug concentrates in keratin and stays there for months, killing dermatophytes as healthy nail grows out."], ["Liver", "Rare but serious liver injury, including liver failure, can occur, so liver tests are checked before starting."], ["Taste and smell", "Taste disturbance or loss, and less often smell loss, can occur and may last a long time after stopping."], ["Skin and immune system", "Rash is common; rarely severe reactions such as SJS, TEN or DRESS develop."], ["Brain and mood", "Depressed mood and anxiety have been reported, so new mood changes during treatment should be reported."]], "journey": "Terbinafine is taken by mouth and well absorbed, though first-pass metabolism reduces bioavailability. It is very lipophilic and highly protein bound, and it spreads into skin, nails, hair and fat. It is metabolized in the liver by several CYP enzymes and is a strong inhibitor of CYP2D6. Metabolites leave mainly in the urine. The effective half-life is about 36 hours, but very slow release from tissues gives a terminal half-life of weeks.", "sidefx": "The mechanism is fungus-selective, so most side effects are mild gut upset, headache and rash. Liver injury is idiosyncratic. Taste loss is not fully explained. Its strong CYP2D6 inhibition raises levels of drugs like tricyclic antidepressants, some beta blockers and dextromethorphan, and reduces activation of prodrugs such as codeine and tamoxifen. There is no antidote.", "fun": "A full course of oral terbinafine for toenails lasts months, yet the cure shows only as the nail slowly grows out, which can take a year.", "src": ["Lamisil (terbinafine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "terbutaline": {"origin": "Terbutaline was developed in the 1960s by the Swedish company Draco, part of Astra, during the search for bronchodilators that would act on beta-2 receptors in the lungs with fewer heart effects than isoproterenol. It was one of the early selective beta-2 agonists, alongside albuterol from Britain. It came into clinical use around 1970 as Bricanyl, available by mouth, inhaler and injection. In the US it was used for asthma and, off label, to stop preterm labor. In 2011 the FDA added a boxed warning against injectable terbutaline for prolonged tocolysis and against oral terbutaline for preventing or treating preterm labor, because of serious maternal heart problems.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Draco (Astra) develops terbutaline in Sweden as a selective beta-2 agonist."], ["1970", "Terbutaline enters clinical use as Bricanyl."], ["2011", "FDA adds a boxed warning against prolonged or oral use for preterm labor."]], "cell": "Terbutaline is a selective beta-2 adrenergic agonist. Beta-2 receptors on bronchial and uterine smooth muscle couple to Gs. Activation turns on adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP. cAMP activates protein kinase A, which phosphorylates and inactivates myosin light chain kinase and lowers intracellular calcium. The smooth muscle relaxes, opening the airways and calming uterine contractions. Beta-2 activation also drives the Na/K ATPase in skeletal muscle (shifting potassium into cells) and increases glycogen breakdown in liver and muscle. At higher levels it also stimulates beta-1 receptors in the heart.", "body": [["Lungs", "Bronchial smooth muscle relaxes, so airflow improves during asthma or bronchospasm."], ["Uterus", "Uterine contractions slow, the basis of its short-term use in preterm labor or uterine hyperstimulation."], ["Heart", "Direct beta-1 effects and reflex responses to vasodilation cause tachycardia and palpitations, and can cause arrhythmias and ischemia."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Tremor is common. Potassium shifts into muscle cells, lowering serum potassium."], ["Metabolism", "Glycogen breakdown raises blood glucose and can cause lactic acid buildup."], ["Lungs (rare)", "In pregnancy, pulmonary edema has been reported, especially with fluids and prolonged use."]], "journey": "Terbutaline can be given subcutaneously, by mouth or by inhalation outside the US. After subcutaneous injection it acts within minutes and peaks in about 30 to 60 minutes. Oral absorption is incomplete with first-pass sulfate conjugation in the gut and liver. It crosses the placenta. It is cleared partly unchanged and partly as conjugates in the urine. The half-life is relatively long for a beta agonist.", "sidefx": "Tachycardia, palpitations and chest pain come from beta-1 stimulation and reflex responses. Tremor comes from beta-2 receptors in skeletal muscle. Hypokalemia and hyperglycemia come from beta-2 effects on potassium shifts and glycogen breakdown. In pregnant women, prolonged use has caused arrhythmias, myocardial ischemia, pulmonary edema and death. A beta blocker can reverse severe toxicity, but nonselective agents can worsen bronchospasm.", "fun": "Terbutaline is still sometimes given by subcutaneous injection for severe asthma when a patient cannot inhale a nebulized drug effectively.", "src": ["Terbutaline sulfate injection FDA label", "FDA Drug Safety Communication on terbutaline, 2011", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "teriparatide": {"origin": "Parathyroid hormone (PTH) was first extracted in the 1920s, including by James Collip in Canada in 1925, and was known for raising blood calcium by breaking down bone. Researchers later found something surprising: brief daily pulses of PTH built bone instead of destroying it. The first 34 amino acids of PTH carry its activity. Eli Lilly developed a recombinant version of this fragment, made in E. coli bacteria, called teriparatide. The FDA approved it in 2002 as Forteo, the first drug that builds new bone for osteoporosis. Early rat studies showed osteosarcoma, leading to a boxed warning that was removed in 2020 after long-term human data.", "timeline": [["1925", "James Collip prepares an active parathyroid extract."], ["2002", "FDA approves teriparatide (Forteo), the first anabolic osteoporosis drug."], ["2020", "FDA removes the boxed warning on osteosarcoma and the lifetime limit on use."]], "cell": "Teriparatide is the 1-34 N-terminal fragment of human PTH. It binds the PTH1 receptor, a Gs and Gq coupled receptor on osteoblasts and kidney tubule cells. In bone, short daily pulses increase osteoblast number and activity, reduce osteoblast apoptosis and lower sclerostin, a Wnt pathway inhibitor. Bone formation rises before resorption catches up, which is called the anabolic window. Continuous high PTH, as in hyperparathyroidism, instead increases RANKL on osteoblasts, activates osteoclasts and causes net bone loss. In the kidney, PTH1 receptor activation increases calcium reabsorption, reduces phosphate reabsorption and stimulates 1-alpha-hydroxylase to make active vitamin D.", "body": [["Bone", "New bone forms on trabecular and cortical surfaces, raising bone density and lowering vertebral and other fractures."], ["Blood calcium", "Serum calcium rises for several hours after each injection, then returns to normal."], ["Kidneys", "More calcium is reabsorbed and more phosphate is excreted. Urinary calcium can rise, so it is used carefully with kidney stones."], ["Blood vessels", "Brief vasodilation can cause orthostatic hypotension, dizziness and fast heart rate soon after injection."], ["Joints and muscles", "Leg cramps and joint aches are common complaints."]], "journey": "Teriparatide is given by subcutaneous injection, usually once a day. It is absorbed quickly and peaks in about 30 minutes. It is cleared by enzymatic breakdown in the liver, followed by removal of fragments through the kidneys. The half-life is about one hour. That short exposure is the key to its bone-building action, because the receptor is stimulated only briefly each day.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow from PTH action. Transient high calcium after each dose explains nausea and, with digoxin, a theoretical risk of toxicity. Orthostatic hypotension reflects PTH's vasodilator effect. High uric acid and urinary calcium come from kidney effects. In rats given high doses for most of their lives, osteosarcoma developed, so it is avoided in people with higher osteosarcoma risk such as Paget disease, open growth plates or prior skeletal radiation. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The same hormone can destroy or build bone depending only on its timing, which makes teriparatide a favorite example of pulsatile versus continuous signaling.", "src": ["Forteo (teriparatide) FDA label", "Neer RM et al. N Engl J Med 2001", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "testosterone": {"origin": "In 1889 the physiologist Charles-Édouard Brown-Séquard reported injecting himself with extracts of animal testicles and claimed renewed vigor, which sparked wide interest in the testis as a hormone gland. The real hormone was isolated in 1935 by Ernst Laqueur and colleagues in Amsterdam, working with the company Organon, from bull testes. Laqueur named it testosterone. The same year, Adolf Butenandt and Leopold Ružička each published ways to synthesize it from cholesterol-like starting materials, and they shared the 1939 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. Synthetic testosterone and its esters soon became treatments for male hypogonadism.", "timeline": [["1889", "Brown-Séquard reports self-injection of testicular extracts."], ["1935", "Laqueur's group isolates and names testosterone; Butenandt and Ružička report its synthesis."], ["1939", "Butenandt and Ružička share the Nobel Prize in Chemistry."], ["1991", "Anabolic steroids, including testosterone, become federally controlled substances in the US."]], "cell": "Testosterone is a steroid that passes through cell membranes. In some tissues it acts directly on the cytoplasmic androgen receptor. In others, such as the prostate, skin and hair follicles, 5-alpha reductase converts it to the more potent dihydrotestosterone (DHT). The hormone-receptor complex moves into the nucleus, binds androgen response elements on DNA and changes transcription of genes for growth, protein synthesis and male traits. Aromatase converts some testosterone to estradiol, which mediates effects on bone closure and can cause gynecomastia. Testosterone also feeds back on the hypothalamus and pituitary, lowering GnRH, LH and FSH, which reduces sperm production and testicular size.", "body": [["Muscle and bone", "Protein synthesis increases, muscle mass and strength rise and bone density improves."], ["Blood", "Testosterone stimulates erythropoietin and red cell production, so hematocrit rises and erythrocytosis can develop."], ["Reproductive system", "Libido and sexual function improve in hypogonadism, but suppressed LH and FSH shrink the testes and lower sperm count, causing infertility."], ["Skin", "Sebaceous glands grow, causing oily skin and acne. Hair growth increases."], ["Prostate", "DHT stimulates prostate growth, which can worsen BPH symptoms."], ["Breathing and heart", "It can worsen sleep apnea, raise blood pressure and cause fluid retention."]], "journey": "Testosterone taken by mouth is mostly destroyed by first-pass liver metabolism, so it is given as injections of esters (cypionate, enanthate, undecanoate), skin gels, patches, nasal gel, pellets or special oral forms. Esters are released slowly from muscle and split to free testosterone. In blood it is bound to sex hormone-binding globulin and albumin. The liver metabolizes it to inactive 17-ketosteroids, and tissues convert some to DHT and estradiol. Metabolites leave mainly in the urine as conjugates.", "sidefx": "Side effects are extra androgen action. Erythrocytosis comes from erythropoietin stimulation and raises clot risk. Gynecomastia comes from aromatization to estradiol. Testicular atrophy and infertility come from feedback suppression of LH and FSH. Gels can transfer to women and children by skin contact, causing virilization, which is a boxed warning. 17-alpha alkylated oral androgens can harm the liver. Undecanoate injection can cause pulmonary oil microembolism. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Testosterone gel's boxed warning is not about the user but about secondary exposure, such as a child who touches skin where the gel was applied.", "src": ["AndroGel (testosterone gel) FDA label", "Depo-Testosterone (testosterone cypionate) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "theophylline": {"origin": "Theophylline is a natural methylxanthine, a cousin of caffeine. In 1888 German biochemist Albrecht Kossel isolated it from tea leaves, and the name means 'divine leaf.' It was synthesized by chemists in the 1890s. It was first used as a diuretic. In the 1920s doctors reported that it relaxed the airways in asthma. Aminophylline, a more soluble salt, became an emergency IV treatment for asthma for decades. As safer inhaled drugs arrived, theophylline faded because of its narrow therapeutic range, though it is still used for some patients with asthma and COPD.", "timeline": [["1888", "Albrecht Kossel isolates theophylline from tea leaves."], ["1890s", "Theophylline is first synthesized."], ["1920s", "Its bronchodilator effect in asthma is described."]], "cell": "Theophylline has two main actions. It inhibits phosphodiesterase enzymes (especially PDE3 and PDE4) that break down cAMP and cGMP. Higher cAMP in airway smooth muscle relaxes it, and in inflammatory cells it dampens activation. It also blocks adenosine A1 and A2 receptors. Adenosine causes bronchoconstriction in asthma, slows the heart, and calms the brain, so blocking it causes airway opening, faster heart rate and CNS stimulation. At low levels it also activates histone deacetylase-2, which may add anti-inflammatory effects. It strengthens diaphragm contraction and increases respiratory drive in the brainstem.", "body": [["Lungs", "Airway smooth muscle relaxes and the diaphragm contracts more strongly, easing breathing in asthma and COPD."], ["Brain", "CNS stimulation raises respiratory drive and alertness, but can also cause insomnia, anxiety, tremor and seizures."], ["Heart", "Heart rate and contractility rise; at high levels, atrial and ventricular arrhythmias occur."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are often the first signs of high levels. It can relax the lower esophageal sphincter."], ["Kidneys", "Adenosine blockade increases urine output (mild diuresis)."], ["Metabolism", "Toxicity can cause hypokalemia, hyperglycemia and metabolic acidosis from catecholamine release."]], "journey": "Theophylline is taken by mouth, often as an extended-release tablet, and is well absorbed. It is distributed widely and crosses the placenta and into breast milk. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP1A2, and metabolism can become saturated at higher levels. Clearance is faster in smokers and slower in older adults, heart failure, liver disease and with CYP1A2 inhibitors such as ciprofloxacin and fluvoxamine. Metabolites leave in the urine. The adult half-life is about 8 hours but varies widely, so blood levels are monitored.", "sidefx": "Its narrow therapeutic index means small changes in levels cause toxicity. Adenosine blockade and PDE inhibition in the heart and brain cause tachycardia, arrhythmias and seizures, which can be hard to control. Nausea and vomiting come from central and gut effects. Drug interactions through CYP1A2 are a common cause of toxicity. There is no antidote. Severe poisoning is treated with seizure control, beta blockers for tachycardia in selected cases, and multiple-dose activated charcoal or hemodialysis.", "fun": "Caffeine, theophylline and theobromine (from chocolate) are all methylxanthines, which is part of why too much coffee can feel a bit like theophylline toxicity.", "src": ["Theo-24 (theophylline) FDA label", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "thiamine": {"origin": "Thiamine's story began with beriberi, a disease of nerve damage and heart failure common where people ate polished white rice. In the 1880s Japanese navy doctor Kanehiro Takaki prevented beriberi in sailors by changing their diet. In the 1890s Christiaan Eijkman in the Dutch East Indies found that chickens fed polished rice developed a beriberi-like illness, which rice bran prevented. He shared the 1929 Nobel Prize. In 1926 Barend Jansen and Willem Donath isolated the active factor, and in 1936 Robert Williams worked out its structure and synthesized it, naming it thiamine. In emergency care it is given to prevent and treat Wernicke encephalopathy.", "timeline": [["1880s", "Kanehiro Takaki prevents beriberi in Japanese sailors through diet."], ["1890s", "Christiaan Eijkman links polished rice to beriberi-like disease in chickens."], ["1926", "Jansen and Donath isolate the anti-beriberi factor."], ["1929", "Eijkman shares the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."], ["1936", "Robert Williams determines the structure and synthesizes thiamine."]], "cell": "Thiamine is converted in cells to thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP). TPP is a coenzyme for four key enzymes of energy metabolism: pyruvate dehydrogenase (pyruvate to acetyl-CoA), alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase (in the citric acid cycle), branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase, and transketolase (in the pentose phosphate pathway). Without TPP, cells cannot move glucose-derived pyruvate into the citric acid cycle efficiently, so ATP production falls and lactate builds up. Neurons and heart cells, which depend heavily on glucose oxidation, are hit first. Giving thiamine restores these enzymes and energy production.", "body": [["Brain", "Restores energy metabolism in vulnerable areas such as the mammillary bodies and thalamus, preventing or treating Wernicke encephalopathy (confusion, ataxia, eye movement problems) and Korsakoff memory loss."], ["Peripheral nerves", "Treats dry beriberi, a symmetric peripheral neuropathy with weakness and numbness."], ["Heart", "Treats wet beriberi, a high-output heart failure from vasodilation and poor heart muscle energy."], ["Metabolism", "Helps clear lactic acid in thiamine-deficient patients by restoring pyruvate dehydrogenase."]], "journey": "Thiamine can be given by mouth, IM or IV. Oral absorption is limited and saturable, which is why IV is used for suspected Wernicke encephalopathy and in alcohol use disorder, where absorption is poor. It is taken up by tissues and turned into thiamine pyrophosphate. Body stores are small and can run out in a few weeks. Excess is excreted in the urine, so toxicity is rare.", "sidefx": "Thiamine is very safe because excess is excreted. Injection site pain can occur. Rare allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, have been reported mainly with IV use. Giving glucose to a thiamine-deficient patient without thiamine can speed glucose metabolism that uses up remaining TPP and may worsen Wernicke encephalopathy, which is why thiamine is given with or before glucose in at-risk patients. There is no antidote, and none is needed.", "fun": "Thiamine was the first B vitamin identified, and Casimir Funk's work on the anti-beriberi factor gave us the word 'vitamine' in 1912.", "src": ["Thiamine hydrochloride injection FDA label", "Carpenter KJ. Beriberi, White Rice, and Vitamin B, 2000", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "ticagrelor": {"origin": "Clopidogrel had shown that blocking the platelet P2Y12 receptor saves lives after heart attacks, but it is a prodrug that some people activate poorly, and it blocks the receptor permanently. AstraZeneca designed ticagrelor as a different kind of P2Y12 blocker: a direct-acting, reversible drug that does not need liver activation. It is a cyclopentyl-triazolopyrimidine, structurally related to the natural signaling molecule ATP. The 2009 PLATO trial showed that ticagrelor reduced cardiovascular death, heart attack and stroke compared with clopidogrel in acute coronary syndrome. The FDA approved it in 2011 as Brilinta.", "timeline": [["2009", "PLATO trial shows ticagrelor beats clopidogrel in acute coronary syndrome."], ["2011", "FDA approves ticagrelor (Brilinta)."], ["2015", "FDA approves long-term use after prior heart attack (PEGASUS-TIMI 54)."]], "cell": "When platelets are activated they release ADP. ADP binds the P2Y12 receptor, a Gi-coupled receptor that lowers cAMP and amplifies activation of the glycoprotein IIb/IIIa receptor. GPIIb/IIIa binds fibrinogen and links platelets together. Ticagrelor binds P2Y12 at a site distinct from ADP and holds the receptor in an inactive state. This is reversible, so platelet function returns as drug levels fall rather than waiting for new platelets. It is active itself and also has an active metabolite. Ticagrelor also inhibits the ENT1 transporter that takes adenosine into cells. Higher adenosine levels may contribute to dyspnea and slow heart rhythms.", "body": [["Platelets", "Platelets clump less, which lowers clot formation in coronary arteries and stents."], ["Heart", "Fewer heart attacks and cardiovascular deaths after ACS. Adenosine effects can cause ventricular pauses and bradyarrhythmias."], ["Lungs", "Shortness of breath is common and usually mild, likely from adenosine-related effects on sensory nerves in the lungs."], ["Blood", "Bleeding risk rises, including GI bleeding and, rarely, bleeding in the brain."], ["Kidneys and uric acid", "It can slightly raise creatinine and uric acid."]], "journey": "Ticagrelor is taken by mouth and absorbed quickly, with platelet inhibition within about 30 minutes and a peak in about two hours. It is highly protein bound. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP3A4 to an active metabolite with similar potency. Both are cleared mostly through bile. The half-life is about 7 hours, so it is taken twice daily. Platelet function recovers over a few days after stopping. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors and inducers are avoided.", "sidefx": "Bleeding comes directly from platelet inhibition. Dyspnea and bradyarrhythmias are linked to higher adenosine levels from ENT1 blockade. With aspirin, higher aspirin doses reduce ticagrelor's benefit, which is why the label limits aspirin to a low dose (a boxed warning). Stopping early raises the risk of stent thrombosis and heart attack. There is no approved specific reversal agent in the US; bentracimab, an antibody fragment that binds ticagrelor, has been studied. Platelet transfusion works poorly while drug is still circulating.", "fun": "Unlike clopidogrel and prasugrel, ticagrelor is not a thienopyridine and not a prodrug, so CYP2C19 genetics do not affect it.", "src": ["Brilinta (ticagrelor) FDA label", "Wallentin L et al. PLATO, N Engl J Med 2009", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "tiotropium": {"origin": "Anticholinergic bronchodilators have plant roots. For centuries people smoked leaves of Datura stramonium and other nightshade plants, which contain atropine-like alkaloids, to ease breathing. Atropine itself was too toxic for routine use. Boehringer Ingelheim developed ipratropium, a quaternary atropine derivative that stays in the lungs, and then tiotropium, a longer-acting relative that binds muscarinic receptors for many hours. Tiotropium was launched in Europe in 2002 and approved by the FDA in 2004 as Spiriva HandiHaler. It became the first once-daily inhaled anticholinergic and a cornerstone of COPD maintenance.", "timeline": [["c. 1800", "Smoking Datura stramonium leaves becomes a popular asthma remedy in Britain."], ["1970s", "Ipratropium, a quaternary atropine derivative, is developed by Boehringer Ingelheim."], ["2002", "Tiotropium is launched in Europe."], ["2004", "FDA approves tiotropium (Spiriva HandiHaler) for COPD."]], "cell": "In the airways, vagal nerves release acetylcholine, which activates M3 muscarinic receptors on bronchial smooth muscle and mucous glands. M3 couples to Gq, raising IP3 and calcium, which contracts smooth muscle and increases mucus. Tiotropium is a competitive antagonist at M1, M2 and M3 receptors. It dissociates very slowly from M3 and M1 but faster from M2. That gives long M3 blockade (about 24 hours) while sparing M2 autoreceptors, which normally limit acetylcholine release. Blocking M3 relaxes airway smooth muscle and reduces mucus secretion. As a quaternary ammonium compound, it is permanently charged and absorbed poorly from the lungs and gut.", "body": [["Lungs", "Airways stay open for about a day, improving lung function, reducing breathlessness and lowering COPD flare rates."], ["Mouth", "Saliva production falls, so dry mouth is the most common side effect."], ["Urinary tract", "Anticholinergic effects can cause urinary retention, especially with BPH."], ["Eyes", "If powder or mist gets into the eyes, it can dilate the pupils and worsen narrow-angle glaucoma."], ["Gut", "Constipation can occur from reduced bowel motility."]], "journey": "Tiotropium is inhaled as a dry powder (HandiHaler) or soft mist (Respimat). Only a small fraction is absorbed into the blood. Because it is a permanently charged quaternary compound, it barely crosses the blood-brain barrier, so it causes little confusion. It is minimally metabolized; most absorbed drug leaves unchanged in the urine. Its long effect comes from slow release from the M3 receptor, and its elimination half-life is several days.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow from muscarinic blockade wherever drug reaches. Dry mouth, constipation and urinary retention reflect the small amount absorbed. Eye effects come from direct contact. Paradoxical bronchospasm after inhalation is rare. Because it is mostly confined to the lungs and does not enter the brain, systemic anticholinergic toxicity is uncommon. It is not a rescue inhaler. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "The British trend of smoking 'asthma cigarettes' made from Datura leaves lasted well into the 20th century, long before tiotropium refined the idea.", "src": ["Spiriva HandiHaler (tiotropium) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "tirzepatide": {"origin": "Tirzepatide builds on decades of work on the incretin hormones GIP and GLP-1. GLP-1 receptor agonists such as exenatide and semaglutide had already lowered glucose and weight. Scientists at Eli Lilly designed a single peptide, based on the GIP sequence, that activates both GIP and GLP-1 receptors. A fatty acid chain lets it bind albumin and last about a week. The FDA approved tirzepatide in 2022 as Mounjaro for type 2 diabetes, the first dual GIP and GLP-1 agonist. It was approved as Zepbound for chronic weight management in 2023 and for obstructive sleep apnea with obesity in 2024. It produced some of the largest weight losses seen with any drug.", "timeline": [["2022", "FDA approves tirzepatide (Mounjaro) for type 2 diabetes."], ["2023", "FDA approves tirzepatide (Zepbound) for chronic weight management."], ["2024", "FDA approves Zepbound for moderate to severe obstructive sleep apnea in adults with obesity."]], "cell": "Tirzepatide is a 39-amino-acid peptide that binds both the GIP receptor and the GLP-1 receptor, both Gs-coupled. In pancreatic beta cells, receptor activation raises cAMP and increases insulin release, but only when glucose is high. GLP-1 receptor activation also lowers glucagon from alpha cells, slows stomach emptying and acts on the hypothalamus and brainstem to reduce appetite. GIP receptor activation adds to insulin release and may influence fat tissue and nausea pathways. It has more potent action at the GIP receptor than at the GLP-1 receptor. A C20 fatty acid side chain binds albumin, protecting it from breakdown and extending its half-life to about five days.", "body": [["Pancreas", "Glucose-dependent insulin release rises and glucagon falls, lowering blood glucose and A1C."], ["Brain", "Appetite and food intake drop, leading to substantial weight loss."], ["Stomach and gut", "Slower stomach emptying causes fullness and is a main cause of nausea, vomiting, diarrhea and constipation."], ["Gallbladder", "Rapid weight loss and changes in bile flow raise the risk of gallstones and gallbladder inflammation."], ["Pancreas (inflammation)", "Pancreatitis has been reported; severe abdominal pain needs evaluation."], ["Airway", "Weight loss reduces airway obstruction in obesity-related sleep apnea."]], "journey": "Tirzepatide is given by subcutaneous injection once a week. It is absorbed slowly, peaking one to three days later. It binds strongly to albumin, which keeps it in the blood and slows its clearance. It is broken down like other proteins, by proteolysis, beta-oxidation of the fatty acid chain and amide hydrolysis. Breakdown products leave in urine and feces. The half-life is about five days. Its slowing of stomach emptying can delay absorption of oral drugs, including oral contraceptives.", "sidefx": "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea come from GLP-1 effects on stomach emptying and the brain. Vomiting and diarrhea can dehydrate and cause acute kidney injury. Gallbladder disease is linked to rapid weight loss. Hypoglycemia is uncommon alone but likely with insulin or sulfonylureas. Like GLP-1 agonists, it has a boxed warning for thyroid C-cell tumors seen in rodents, so it is avoided with a history of medullary thyroid cancer or MEN2. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Tirzepatide is often called a 'twincretin' because one molecule acts on two incretin receptors.", "src": ["Mounjaro (tirzepatide) FDA label", "Zepbound (tirzepatide) FDA label", "Jastreboff AM et al. SURMOUNT-1, N Engl J Med 2022"]}, "tizanidine": {"origin": "Tizanidine is a chemical cousin of clonidine, the alpha-2 agonist blood pressure drug. It was developed by Sandoz in Switzerland as a muscle relaxant that acts in the spinal cord with less blood pressure lowering than clonidine. It was used in Europe before reaching the US, where it was approved as Zanaflex in the 1990s for spasticity from conditions such as multiple sclerosis and spinal cord injury. It became one of the main oral antispastic drugs alongside baclofen.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Tizanidine is approved in the US as Zanaflex for spasticity."]], "cell": "Tizanidine is a central alpha-2 adrenergic agonist. In the spinal cord, it activates presynaptic alpha-2 receptors on interneurons. These receptors couple to Gi, lower cAMP, and reduce calcium entry into nerve terminals. Release of excitatory amino acids such as glutamate and aspartate onto motor neurons falls. This reduces polysynaptic reflex activity and the excessive muscle tone that causes spasticity. Alpha-2 activation in the brainstem (locus coeruleus) and vasomotor center causes sedation and lowers sympathetic outflow, which explains drowsiness and low blood pressure.", "body": [["Spinal cord and muscles", "Muscle tone and spasms decrease, easing stiffness from multiple sclerosis or spinal cord injury."], ["Brain", "Sedation, drowsiness and dizziness are common because alpha-2 activation calms the locus coeruleus."], ["Heart and vessels", "Lower sympathetic tone causes hypotension and bradycardia, especially when standing."], ["Mouth", "Dry mouth is common because alpha-2 activation reduces salivary secretion."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises and rarely liver injury can occur, so liver tests are checked."]], "journey": "Tizanidine is taken by mouth. It is well absorbed but has low bioavailability because of extensive first-pass metabolism by CYP1A2 in the liver. Food and the choice of tablet versus capsule change how much is absorbed. It is cleared mostly as inactive metabolites in the urine. The half-life is short, about two and a half hours, so effects last only a few hours. Strong CYP1A2 inhibitors such as fluvoxamine and ciprofloxacin are contraindicated because they raise levels many times over.", "sidefx": "Sedation, hypotension, bradycardia and dry mouth all follow from alpha-2 agonism. Combining it with CYP1A2 inhibitors or other sedatives magnifies these effects. Liver injury is idiosyncratic. Stopping suddenly after long use can cause rebound hypertension, tachycardia and increased spasticity, like clonidine withdrawal. There is no specific antidote; toxicity is managed supportively.", "fun": "Tizanidine and clonidine share the imidazoline ring design, and both can cause rebound hypertension when stopped suddenly.", "src": ["Zanaflex (tizanidine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "tolvaptan": {"origin": "Vasopressin, also called antidiuretic hormone, was identified in pituitary extracts in the late 1800s, and Vincent du Vigneaud worked out its structure in the 1950s. In conditions such as SIADH and heart failure, too much vasopressin makes the kidneys hold water and lowers sodium. Otsuka Pharmaceutical in Japan developed tolvaptan as an oral drug that blocks the kidney's vasopressin V2 receptor. The FDA approved it as Samsca in 2009 for hypervolemic and euvolemic hyponatremia. In 2018 it was approved as Jynarque to slow kidney function decline in autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease, where vasopressin drives cyst growth.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Vincent du Vigneaud determines the structure of vasopressin."], ["2009", "FDA approves tolvaptan (Samsca) for hyponatremia."], ["2018", "FDA approves tolvaptan (Jynarque) for ADPKD."]], "cell": "Vasopressin binds V2 receptors on the basolateral side of principal cells in the collecting duct. V2 couples to Gs, raising cAMP and activating protein kinase A, which moves aquaporin-2 water channels into the apical membrane. Water then flows from urine back into the body. Tolvaptan is a selective competitive V2 antagonist. With the receptor blocked, cAMP stays low, aquaporin-2 stays inside the cell and the collecting duct becomes less permeable to water. The kidney excretes free water without much sodium or potassium (aquaresis), so serum sodium rises. In ADPKD, lowering cAMP in cyst-lining cells slows cell growth and fluid secretion into cysts.", "body": [["Kidneys", "Large volumes of dilute urine are produced, causing thirst, frequent urination and nighttime urination."], ["Blood sodium", "Serum sodium rises. If it rises too fast, it can cause osmotic demyelination, so treatment starts in a hospital with close sodium checks."], ["Kidney cysts", "In ADPKD, cyst growth slows and the decline in kidney function slows."], ["Liver", "It can cause serious and sometimes fatal liver injury, which limits how long it is used for hyponatremia and requires liver test monitoring."], ["Fluid balance", "Too much water loss can cause dehydration and hypernatremia if a patient cannot drink."]], "journey": "Tolvaptan is taken by mouth and is well absorbed. It is highly protein bound. The liver metabolizes it almost entirely through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors are contraindicated and inducers reduce its effect. Metabolites leave mainly in the feces. The water-excreting effect begins within hours, peaks in about four to eight hours and wanes within a day. The half-life is about 12 hours.", "sidefx": "Thirst, dry mouth and polyuria follow directly from aquaresis. Overly rapid correction of chronic hyponatremia can damage myelin in the brainstem (osmotic demyelination syndrome), a boxed warning. Liver injury is a separate boxed warning; the mechanism may involve bile acid transport inhibition. Dehydration and hypernatremia happen when water loss outpaces drinking. If sodium rises too fast, treatment involves stopping the drug and giving free water or desmopressin to relower it.", "fun": "Tolvaptan causes 'aquaresis,' a word coined to separate pure water loss from the sodium-losing 'diuresis' of standard diuretics.", "src": ["Samsca (tolvaptan) FDA label", "Jynarque (tolvaptan) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "topiramate": {"origin": "Topiramate was discovered by accident. In 1979 chemist Bruce Maryanoff at McNeil Laboratories (part of Johnson & Johnson) was making sugar derivatives from fructose, hoping to find a diabetes drug that blocked an enzyme of glucose production. The compound did not work for diabetes, but screening by Joseph Gardocki showed strong anticonvulsant activity in animals. The FDA approved topiramate as Topamax for epilepsy in 1996 and for migraine prevention in 2004. In 2012 it was approved with phentermine (Qsymia) for weight management.", "timeline": [["1979", "Bruce Maryanoff makes topiramate while seeking a diabetes drug."], ["1996", "FDA approves topiramate (Topamax) for epilepsy."], ["2004", "FDA approves topiramate for migraine prevention."], ["2012", "FDA approves phentermine-topiramate (Qsymia) for weight management."]], "cell": "Topiramate works through several mechanisms. It blocks voltage-gated sodium channels, limiting repetitive firing of neurons. It enhances GABA-A receptor activity at a site distinct from benzodiazepines, increasing inhibitory chloride currents. It blocks AMPA and kainate glutamate receptors, reducing excitatory transmission. It also inhibits carbonic anhydrase, especially isoforms II and IV, which can lower brain bicarbonate and cause mild metabolic acidosis. Together these effects reduce neuronal excitability and seizure spread. In migraine, dampened cortical excitability may lower cortical spreading depression.", "body": [["Brain", "Seizures and migraine attacks become less frequent. Cognitive slowing, trouble finding words and memory problems are common."], ["Nerves", "Paresthesias, tingling in the hands and feet, are common and linked to carbonic anhydrase inhibition."], ["Kidneys", "Carbonic anhydrase inhibition causes loss of bicarbonate, mild metabolic acidosis and alkaline urine, favoring calcium phosphate kidney stones."], ["Eyes", "Rarely it causes acute angle-closure glaucoma with myopia, usually in the first month."], ["Appetite and weight", "Appetite falls and weight loss is common."], ["Skin and temperature", "Decreased sweating (oligohidrosis) can cause hyperthermia, especially in children in hot weather."]], "journey": "Topiramate is taken by mouth and absorbed well, regardless of food. It is not highly protein bound and spreads widely. Only a small fraction is metabolized by the liver; most leaves unchanged in the urine. The half-life is about 21 hours. Enzyme-inducing drugs such as carbamazepine and phenytoin increase its metabolism. At higher doses it can induce CYP3A4 and reduce the effect of oral contraceptives.", "sidefx": "Carbonic anhydrase inhibition explains paresthesias, metabolic acidosis, kidney stones and reduced sweating. Effects on sodium channels, GABA and glutamate explain sedation and cognitive slowing. Angle-closure glaucoma comes from swelling of the ciliary body pushing the lens forward. It is teratogenic, raising cleft lip and palate risk with first-trimester use. Stopping abruptly can trigger seizures. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Topiramate's trouble with word-finding has earned it the student nickname 'Dopamax.'", "src": ["Topamax (topiramate) FDA label", "Maryanoff BE et al. J Med Chem 1987", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "torsemide": {"origin": "Loop diuretics began with furosemide in the 1960s. Torsemide was developed later by Boehringer Mannheim in Germany as a loop diuretic with nearly complete oral absorption and a longer action than furosemide. Chemically it is a pyridine-sulfonylurea rather than a classic sulfonamide-based loop diuretic. It was approved in the US in 1993 as Demadex. Its predictable absorption makes it attractive in heart failure, where gut swelling can make furosemide absorption unreliable.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Furosemide introduces the loop diuretic class."], ["1993", "FDA approves torsemide (Demadex)."]], "cell": "In the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle, the NKCC2 cotransporter on the luminal membrane moves one sodium, one potassium and two chloride ions into the cell. This segment normally reclaims about a quarter of filtered sodium. Torsemide reaches the tubule lumen by secretion through organic anion transporters in the proximal tubule. It then binds the chloride site of NKCC2 and blocks it. Sodium, chloride and water are lost in large amounts. Because the transporter also builds the positive lumen voltage that drives calcium and magnesium reabsorption, those ions are lost too. More sodium reaching the collecting duct increases potassium and hydrogen secretion.", "body": [["Kidneys", "Strong diuresis with loss of sodium, chloride, potassium, magnesium and calcium."], ["Heart and lungs", "Lower blood volume reduces preload and pulmonary congestion, easing breathlessness and edema in heart failure."], ["Blood vessels", "Blood pressure falls modestly from volume loss."], ["Blood chemistry", "Hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, metabolic alkalosis and higher uric acid can develop."], ["Ears", "High levels can damage the inner ear and cause tinnitus or hearing loss, though this is less common than with furosemide."]], "journey": "Torsemide is taken by mouth or given IV. Oral bioavailability is high, about 80 percent or more, and less variable than furosemide. It is highly protein bound, so it must be secreted into the tubule rather than filtered. The liver metabolizes most of it, mainly through CYP2C9, and the rest is excreted in urine. The half-life is about three and a half hours, and the diuretic effect lasts around six to eight hours.", "sidefx": "Side effects reflect NKCC2 blockade. Electrolyte loss explains hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia and alkalosis, which can provoke arrhythmias, especially with digoxin. Volume loss causes dizziness, hypotension and prerenal kidney injury. Ototoxicity relates to a related cotransporter in the inner ear, NKCC1. NSAIDs blunt its effect by reducing renal prostaglandins. There is no antidote; treatment is replacing fluid and electrolytes.", "fun": "Torsemide is built on a sulfonylurea core, the same structural family as some diabetes drugs, though it does not lower blood glucose.", "src": ["Demadex (torsemide) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "tramadol": {"origin": "Tramadol was synthesized in 1962 at the German company Grünenthal. It was marketed in Germany in 1977 under the name Tramal. Its two-part action, a weak opioid effect plus effects on serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake, made it seem a lower-risk opioid. The FDA approved it in 1995 as Ultram, and for years it was not a controlled substance in the US. Reports of misuse, dependence and overdose grew, and in 2014 the DEA placed it in Schedule IV.", "timeline": [["1962", "Grünenthal synthesizes tramadol in Germany."], ["1977", "Tramadol is marketed in Germany as Tramal."], ["1995", "FDA approves tramadol (Ultram)."], ["2014", "DEA places tramadol in Schedule IV."]], "cell": "Tramadol is a racemic mixture with two actions. The parent drug weakly binds mu-opioid receptors. CYP2D6 converts it to O-desmethyltramadol (M1), which binds mu receptors much more strongly and carries most of the opioid effect. Mu receptor activation through Gi lowers cAMP, opens potassium channels and closes calcium channels, reducing release of pain neurotransmitters in the spinal cord and brain. Separately, the (+) enantiomer inhibits serotonin reuptake and the (-) enantiomer inhibits norepinephrine reuptake. More serotonin and norepinephrine in descending pain pathways in the spinal cord adds to pain relief.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Pain is relieved through opioid action and enhanced descending inhibition. Dizziness and drowsiness are common."], ["Breathing", "Opioid action can depress breathing, especially in ultra-rapid CYP2D6 metabolizers, children, and with other sedatives."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and constipation come from opioid effects on the gut and brainstem."], ["Seizure threshold", "It lowers the seizure threshold, causing seizures even at normal doses in some people and more often in overdose."], ["Serotonin system", "With SSRIs, SNRIs, MAO inhibitors or triptans, serotonin syndrome can occur."], ["Blood glucose and sodium", "It can cause hypoglycemia and hyponatremia (SIADH)."]], "journey": "Tramadol is taken by mouth and well absorbed. It is distributed widely and crosses the blood-brain barrier and placenta. The liver converts it by CYP2D6 to the active M1 metabolite and by CYP3A4 and CYP2B6 to inactive N-desmethyltramadol. Poor CYP2D6 metabolizers get less pain relief, and ultra-rapid metabolizers may build up dangerous M1 levels. Parent drug and metabolites leave mainly in the urine. The half-life is about six to seven hours.", "sidefx": "Opioid effects explain nausea, constipation, sedation and respiratory depression. Reuptake inhibition explains serotonin syndrome and contributes to seizures. CYP2D6 genetics explain why some patients get too much or too little effect, leading to an FDA contraindication in children under 12. Naloxone reverses opioid respiratory depression, but it may not fully reverse tramadol toxicity and has been linked to seizures in tramadol overdose; seizures are treated with benzodiazepines.", "fun": "Tramadol leans so heavily on CYP2D6 to make its main active opioid that poor CYP2D6 metabolizers may get noticeably weaker pain relief.", "src": ["Ultram (tramadol) FDA label", "Grond S, Sablotzki A. Clin Pharmacokinet 2004", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "tranexamic-acid": {"origin": "Tranexamic acid was discovered in Japan by the husband and wife team Shosuke and Utako Okamoto. In the 1950s they found that the amino acid analog epsilon-aminocaproic acid could block clot breakdown. Searching for something more potent, they reported tranexamic acid in 1962, about ten times stronger. Utako Okamoto hoped it would save women from bleeding after childbirth. It was approved in the US as IV Cyklokapron in 1986 and as oral Lysteda in 2009 for heavy menstrual bleeding. The CRASH-2 trial in 2010 showed it lowered death from bleeding in trauma, and the WOMAN trial in 2017 showed it lowered death from postpartum hemorrhage, decades after Okamoto's original goal.", "timeline": [["1962", "Shosuke and Utako Okamoto report tranexamic acid in Japan."], ["1986", "FDA approves IV tranexamic acid (Cyklokapron)."], ["2009", "FDA approves oral tranexamic acid (Lysteda) for heavy menstrual bleeding."], ["2010", "CRASH-2 trial shows lower death from bleeding in trauma."], ["2017", "WOMAN trial shows lower death from bleeding after childbirth."]], "cell": "Clots are broken down by plasmin, which forms when tissue plasminogen activator cleaves plasminogen. Plasminogen and plasmin attach to fibrin through lysine-binding sites in their kringle domains. Tranexamic acid is a synthetic analog of lysine. It occupies these lysine-binding sites, so plasminogen cannot bind fibrin and be activated there, and plasmin that forms cannot attach to fibrin to cut it. The fibrin clot is protected from breakdown and stays in place, reducing bleeding. At high concentrations it can also directly inhibit plasmin. It does not make new clots; it stabilizes clots that form normally.", "body": [["Blood", "Clots resist breakdown, so blood loss falls in trauma, surgery, postpartum hemorrhage and heavy menstrual bleeding."], ["Vessels", "Rapid IV injection can cause hypotension, so it is given over several minutes."], ["Brain", "High levels can cause seizures, possibly by blocking inhibitory GABA-A and glycine receptors; this is seen especially with high doses in cardiac surgery or kidney failure."], ["Eyes", "Visual changes, including color vision changes, have been reported."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea can occur, especially with the oral form."]], "journey": "Tranexamic acid can be given IV, by mouth or topically. IV dosing works within minutes. It is distributed through body fluids, crosses the placenta and enters cerebrospinal fluid in small amounts. Very little is metabolized. About 95 percent leaves unchanged in the urine, so kidney failure raises levels and requires adjustment. The half-life is about two to three hours. Benefit in trauma is greatest when given early, within three hours of injury.", "sidefx": "Because it stabilizes clots, it could raise the risk of thrombosis, especially with prior clots or with hormonal contraceptives; large trials in trauma did not show a large increase. Seizures relate to central receptor effects at high levels. Accidental intrathecal injection has caused seizures, arrhythmias and death, so vials are kept apart from spinal anesthetics. Hypotension follows fast IV push. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Utako Okamoto lived to see the WOMAN trial prove her decades-old hope that the drug could save mothers from bleeding after childbirth.", "src": ["Cyklokapron (tranexamic acid) FDA label", "CRASH-2 collaborators. Lancet 2010", "WOMAN Trial Collaborators. Lancet 2017"]}, "trastuzumab": {"origin": "In the early 1980s, Robert Weinberg's lab at MIT found a cancer gene in rat nerve tumors and called it neu. Around the same time, Axel Ullrich's team at Genentech cloned a human version, HER2. In 1987 oncologist Dennis Slamon at UCLA, working with Ullrich, showed that about a quarter to a third of breast cancers have extra copies of HER2 and that these tumors behave aggressively. Genentech scientists then humanized a mouse antibody against HER2, creating trastuzumab. The FDA approved it as Herceptin in 1998, one of the first targeted cancer therapies chosen by testing the tumor. The story was told in the 2008 film Living Proof.", "timeline": [["1984", "The neu oncogene is described in rat tumors by Weinberg's group."], ["1987", "Slamon and Ullrich link HER2 amplification to aggressive breast cancer."], ["1998", "FDA approves trastuzumab (Herceptin) for HER2-positive metastatic breast cancer."], ["2006", "FDA approves trastuzumab for early-stage HER2-positive breast cancer after surgery."]], "cell": "HER2 (ERBB2) is a receptor tyrosine kinase in the EGFR family. It has no known ligand but forms dimers with other HER receptors and, when overexpressed, signals constantly. That drives PI3K/AKT and RAS/MAPK pathways, pushing cells to grow and resist apoptosis. Trastuzumab is a humanized IgG1 antibody that binds domain IV of the HER2 extracellular region. Binding blocks ligand-independent HER2 signaling, prevents cleavage of the extracellular domain that leaves an active fragment, and promotes receptor internalization. Its Fc portion recruits natural killer cells and other immune cells, causing antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity (ADCC) against tumor cells. Cells pause in G1 and some die.", "body": [["Breast tumor", "HER2-positive cancer cells stop growing and are attacked by immune cells, improving survival in early and metastatic disease."], ["Heart", "HER2 helps heart muscle cells survive stress. Blocking it can lower ejection fraction and cause heart failure, usually reversible, and worse with anthracyclines."], ["Lungs", "Rarely, interstitial lung disease, pulmonary infiltrates or ARDS occur, often linked to infusion reactions."], ["Immune system", "First infusions often cause fever, chills and rigors from cytokine release; rarely severe infusion reactions or anaphylaxis."], ["Pregnancy", "Exposure can cause oligohydramnios and fetal harm, likely by affecting fetal kidney function."], ["Stomach", "It is also used for HER2-positive stomach and gastroesophageal cancers."]], "journey": "Trastuzumab is given by IV infusion or as a subcutaneous injection with hyaluronidase. It is a large antibody, so it stays mainly in blood and extracellular fluid and does not cross the blood-brain barrier well, which is why brain metastases can still progress. It is not metabolized by liver enzymes. Like other IgG antibodies, it is broken down by protein catabolism in cells throughout the body, and the neonatal Fc receptor recycles it, giving a half-life of weeks.", "sidefx": "Cardiotoxicity happens because HER2 signaling protects cardiomyocytes, so blocking it impairs their stress response. Unlike anthracycline damage, it is usually reversible after stopping. Cardiac function is monitored with echocardiography or MUGA scans. Infusion reactions reflect immune cell activation. Embryo-fetal toxicity reflects HER2 roles in fetal development. All three are boxed warnings. There is no antidote; reactions and heart failure are managed by pausing therapy and treating symptoms.", "fun": "Trastuzumab only works for tumors that overexpress HER2, so it helped make tumor biomarker testing a standard step before choosing cancer treatment.", "src": ["Herceptin (trastuzumab) FDA label", "Slamon DJ et al. Science 1987", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "trazodone": {"origin": "Trazodone was developed in Italy in the 1960s by the drug company Angelini, as part of research into new antidepressants that worked differently from the tricyclics. It was one of the first antidepressants that was not a tricyclic or MAO inhibitor. It reached the US in 1981 as Desyrel. It was safer in overdose than tricyclics, with fewer anticholinergic and heart effects. Over time its strong sedating effect at low doses made it one of the most prescribed drugs for insomnia, an off-label use, while its antidepressant use declined after SSRIs arrived.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Angelini develops trazodone in Italy."], ["1981", "FDA approves trazodone (Desyrel) for depression."], ["2010", "FDA approves an extended-release form (Oleptro)."]], "cell": "Trazodone is a serotonin antagonist and reuptake inhibitor (SARI). Its strongest action is blocking 5-HT2A receptors, which reduces anxiety, insomnia and the sexual side effects linked to 5-HT2A stimulation. It also blocks 5-HT2C receptors. At higher levels it inhibits the serotonin transporter (SERT), raising synaptic serotonin and adding antidepressant effect. It also blocks histamine H1 receptors (sedation) and alpha-1 adrenergic receptors (orthostatic hypotension and priapism). Its metabolite mCPP is a serotonin receptor agonist. Low doses mostly give the receptor blocking effects that promote sleep, while higher doses add SERT inhibition for depression.", "body": [["Brain", "Mood improves over weeks at antidepressant levels. H1 and 5-HT2A blockade promote sleep and are the reason it is widely used for insomnia."], ["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 blockade relaxes vessels, causing dizziness and orthostatic hypotension, raising fall risk in older adults."], ["Penis and clitoris", "Alpha-1 blockade can trap blood in erectile tissue, causing priapism, a urologic emergency."], ["Heart", "It can prolong the QT interval and rarely cause arrhythmias such as torsades, especially in overdose or with other QT drugs."], ["Sexual function", "Fewer sexual side effects than SSRIs because of 5-HT2A blockade."]], "journey": "Trazodone is taken by mouth and well absorbed; food slows absorption a little. It is highly protein bound and crosses the blood-brain barrier. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP3A4 to the active metabolite mCPP, which is broken down by CYP2D6. Metabolites leave mostly in the urine. The half-life is about seven to ten hours. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise trazodone levels, and inducers like carbamazepine lower them.", "sidefx": "Sedation comes from H1 blockade. Orthostatic hypotension and priapism come from alpha-1 blockade. QT prolongation reflects potassium channel effects. Combining it with other serotonergic drugs can cause serotonin syndrome. Like all antidepressants it carries a boxed warning about suicidal thoughts in young people. There is no antidote; overdose is treated supportively, with attention to QT and blood pressure. Priapism may need urgent treatment with an alpha agonist such as phenylephrine injected into the penis.", "fun": "Trazodone is one of the most prescribed sleep aids in the US, even though it is not FDA approved for insomnia.", "src": ["Desyrel (trazodone) FDA label", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole": {"origin": "This combination joins two separate stories. Sulfonamides were the first widely effective antibacterial drugs. In 1932 Gerhard Domagk at Bayer found that the red dye Prontosil cured streptococcal infections in mice; it was later shown to be broken down in the body to sulfanilamide. Domagk received the 1939 Nobel Prize. Trimethoprim came from George Hitchings and Gertrude Elion at Burroughs Wellcome, who designed drugs to block folate enzymes; it was developed in the late 1950s and early 1960s. They shared the 1988 Nobel Prize. Combining trimethoprim with the sulfonamide sulfamethoxazole blocked two steps in the same bacterial pathway. The pairing was introduced in the late 1960s and reached the US in the early 1970s as Bactrim and Septra.", "timeline": [["1932", "Gerhard Domagk discovers the antibacterial effect of Prontosil."], ["1939", "Domagk is awarded the Nobel Prize for Prontosil."], ["1960s", "Hitchings and Elion's work produces trimethoprim; the combination with sulfamethoxazole is introduced."], ["1970s", "Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is marketed in the US."], ["1988", "Hitchings and Elion share the Nobel Prize."]], "cell": "Bacteria must make their own folate, while humans take it from food. Folate is needed to make thymidine and purines for DNA. Sulfamethoxazole is a structural analog of para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA). It competitively inhibits dihydropteroate synthase, so dihydrofolic acid is not formed. Trimethoprim inhibits bacterial dihydrofolate reductase much more strongly than the human enzyme, blocking conversion of dihydrofolate to tetrahydrofolate. Blocking two sequential steps (sequential blockade) cuts off folate supply, stops DNA synthesis and makes the combination bactericidal, while each drug alone is mostly bacteriostatic. The combination also slows development of resistance.", "body": [["Infection sites", "Covers many urinary, skin (including community MRSA), respiratory and gut infections, and is first line for Pneumocystis pneumonia treatment and prevention."], ["Kidneys and potassium", "Trimethoprim blocks the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) in the collecting duct like amiloride, causing hyperkalemia. It also blocks creatinine secretion, raising creatinine without true kidney injury."], ["Skin", "Rash and photosensitivity are common. Rarely Stevens-Johnson syndrome or toxic epidermal necrolysis occur from the sulfonamide."], ["Blood", "Sulfonamides can cause hemolysis in G6PD deficiency. Folate antagonism and immune reactions can cause anemia, thrombocytopenia, neutropenia or agranulocytosis."], ["Blood glucose", "Sulfamethoxazole can cause hypoglycemia, especially in kidney disease."], ["Newborns", "Sulfonamides displace bilirubin from albumin, which can cause kernicterus in newborns, so it is avoided near term and in young infants."]], "journey": "Both drugs are taken by mouth and are well absorbed; an IV form also exists. They are fixed in a ratio that gives about a 1 to 20 trimethoprim to sulfamethoxazole ratio in blood, which is the most synergistic. Both distribute well into tissues, including cerebrospinal fluid and prostate, and cross the placenta. Sulfamethoxazole is acetylated and glucuronidated in the liver; trimethoprim is partly metabolized. Both are cleared mainly by the kidneys, with half-lives of about 8 to 11 hours, longer in kidney failure.", "sidefx": "Most serious reactions come from the sulfonamide part: rash, severe skin reactions, hemolysis in G6PD deficiency, kernicterus risk, and bone marrow suppression. Hyperkalemia comes from trimethoprim blocking ENaC. Folate deficiency effects are worse in people already low in folate. It raises warfarin effect by inhibiting CYP2C9 and displacing it from albumin. Leucovorin (folinic acid) can treat folate-related marrow toxicity because human cells can use it to bypass the blocked enzyme. There is no other specific antidote.", "fun": "Domagk was forbidden by the Nazi government from accepting his 1939 Nobel Prize and only received the medal in 1947.", "src": ["Bactrim (sulfamethoxazole and trimethoprim) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "valacyclovir": {"origin": "Valacyclovir is a prodrug of acyclovir. Acyclovir was discovered in the 1970s at Burroughs Wellcome by a team including Howard Schaeffer and Gertrude Elion, and it was approved in 1982. It was the first truly selective antiviral, but it is poorly absorbed by mouth, so patients had to take it many times a day. Burroughs Wellcome chemists attached the amino acid L-valine to acyclovir, which lets gut transporters carry it into the body much more efficiently. The FDA approved valacyclovir as Valtrex in 1995. It gave higher blood levels of acyclovir with fewer pills.", "timeline": [["1974", "Acyclovir is discovered at Burroughs Wellcome."], ["1982", "FDA approves acyclovir, the first highly selective antiviral."], ["1988", "Gertrude Elion shares the Nobel Prize, in part for antiviral drug design."], ["1995", "FDA approves valacyclovir (Valtrex)."]], "cell": "After absorption, valacyclovir is quickly converted to acyclovir and valine by valacyclovir hydrolase in the gut wall and liver. Acyclovir is a guanosine analog. In cells infected with herpes simplex or varicella zoster virus, the viral thymidine kinase adds the first phosphate. This step barely happens in uninfected cells, which is the key to selectivity. Host kinases then add two more phosphates to form acyclovir triphosphate. It competes with dGTP for viral DNA polymerase and, once built into viral DNA, ends the chain because it lacks a 3'-hydroxyl group. It also traps the viral polymerase on the DNA. Viruses with thymidine kinase mutations can be resistant.", "body": [["Skin and mucosa", "Herpes lesions (cold sores, genital herpes, shingles) heal faster, with less viral shedding and pain."], ["Nerves", "Early treatment of shingles shortens acute pain. Suppressive therapy reduces outbreaks from latent virus in sensory ganglia."], ["Kidneys", "Acyclovir can crystallize in renal tubules, especially with dehydration or high levels, causing acute kidney injury."], ["Brain", "High levels, mainly in older adults or kidney failure, can cause confusion, hallucinations, agitation or seizures."], ["Blood", "At very high doses in immunocompromised patients, thrombotic microangiopathy (TTP/HUS) has been reported."]], "journey": "Valacyclovir is taken by mouth and absorbed by the intestinal peptide transporter PEPT1. First-pass hydrolysis in the gut and liver releases acyclovir, giving three to five times more acyclovir in the blood than oral acyclovir itself. Acyclovir distributes widely, including into cerebrospinal fluid. It is not much metabolized. The kidneys clear it unchanged by glomerular filtration and tubular secretion, with a half-life of about two and a half to three hours. Kidney disease raises levels, so the label calls for adjustment.", "sidefx": "Because only infected cells activate acyclovir well, side effects are mild in most people, such as headache and nausea. Kidney injury comes from acyclovir crystals in tubules, prevented by good hydration. Neurotoxicity is tied to high acyclovir levels, usually when kidney function is poor and doses are not adjusted. Hemodialysis removes acyclovir in severe toxicity. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Elion and Hitchings' Nobel-winning work produced drugs for gout, leukemia, transplants, malaria and herpes, and acyclovir was one of the most celebrated.", "src": ["Valtrex (valacyclovir) FDA label", "Elion GB. Nobel Lecture 1988", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "valproic-acid": {"origin": "Valproic acid was first made in 1882 by American chemist Beverly Burton as a branched fatty acid. For decades it was used only as a lab solvent. In 1962 French researcher Pierre Eymard and colleagues in Lyon used it to dissolve other compounds they were testing for seizure protection in animals. Every test compound seemed to work, and they realized the solvent itself was the anticonvulsant. Valproate was marketed in France in 1967 and approved by the FDA in 1978. It became a broad-spectrum seizure drug and later a mood stabilizer for bipolar disorder and a migraine preventive.", "timeline": [["1882", "Beverly Burton synthesizes valproic acid."], ["1962", "Eymard and colleagues discover its anticonvulsant effect by accident in Lyon."], ["1967", "Valproate is marketed in France."], ["1978", "FDA approves valproic acid for epilepsy."], ["1995", "FDA approves divalproex for acute mania."]], "cell": "Valproate acts through several mechanisms. It blocks voltage-gated sodium channels, prolonging their inactivated state and limiting high-frequency neuron firing. It blocks T-type calcium channels in thalamic neurons, which underlie absence seizures. It raises brain GABA by inhibiting GABA transaminase and succinic semialdehyde dehydrogenase, which break GABA down, and may increase GABA synthesis. It also inhibits histone deacetylases, changing gene expression, which may contribute to mood effects and to teratogenicity. Together these reduce excitability across many seizure types.", "body": [["Brain", "Controls generalized, absence, myoclonic and focal seizures. Stabilizes mood in mania and reduces migraine frequency. Tremor and sedation are common."], ["Liver", "Can cause serious, sometimes fatal liver failure, especially in children under two and in people with mitochondrial disorders such as POLG mutations. It also raises ammonia."], ["Pancreas", "Rare but life-threatening pancreatitis can occur."], ["Blood", "Dose-related thrombocytopenia and platelet dysfunction raise bleeding risk."], ["Metabolism and hair", "Weight gain and hair thinning are common. In women it is linked to polycystic ovary features."], ["Fetus", "It is highly teratogenic, causing neural tube defects, other malformations and lower IQ, so it is avoided in pregnancy when possible."]], "journey": "Valproate is taken by mouth or given IV. It is well absorbed; delayed-release divalproex peaks in a few hours. It is highly protein bound, but binding becomes saturated at high levels, so free drug rises faster than total levels. It crosses the placenta. The liver metabolizes it mostly by glucuronidation and mitochondrial beta-oxidation, with some CYP metabolism. Metabolites leave in the urine. The half-life is about 9 to 16 hours. It inhibits several liver enzymes and raises levels of lamotrigine, phenobarbital and others.", "sidefx": "Liver toxicity may relate to toxic metabolites such as 4-en-valproate and to disrupted mitochondrial fatty acid metabolism. Hyperammonemia comes from impaired urea cycle function, partly through carnitine depletion. HDAC inhibition and folate effects are thought to contribute to birth defects. All three (liver failure, fetal harm, pancreatitis) are boxed warnings. L-carnitine is used for valproate-induced hyperammonemia and hepatotoxicity. Hemodialysis can remove it in severe overdose.", "fun": "A drug used only to dissolve other test compounds turned out to be the real anticonvulsant, one of the luckiest accidents in neurology.", "src": ["Depakote (divalproex sodium) FDA label", "Meunier H et al. Therapie 1963", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "valsartan": {"origin": "Angiotensin receptor blockers were developed after ACE inhibitors showed the value of blocking the renin-angiotensin system. ACE inhibitors cause cough and rarely angioedema because they also raise bradykinin. Researchers looked for drugs that block angiotensin II right at its AT1 receptor instead. Losartan from DuPont was the first ARB approved, in 1995. Valsartan was developed by Ciba-Geigy, which became part of Novartis, and the FDA approved it in 1996 as Diovan. In 2015 valsartan was paired with the neprilysin inhibitor sacubitril as Entresto after the PARADIGM-HF trial showed it lowered death in heart failure compared with enalapril. In 2018 some generic valsartan was recalled for a nitrosamine impurity.", "timeline": [["1995", "Losartan becomes the first ARB approved by the FDA."], ["1996", "FDA approves valsartan (Diovan)."], ["2014", "PARADIGM-HF trial shows sacubitril-valsartan beats enalapril in heart failure."], ["2015", "FDA approves sacubitril-valsartan (Entresto)."], ["2018", "Some generic valsartan products are recalled for nitrosamine impurities."]], "cell": "Angiotensin II acts on AT1 receptors, which couple to Gq. This raises intracellular calcium, constricts arterioles, stimulates aldosterone release from the adrenal cortex, increases sympathetic activity and ADH release, and drives cardiac and vascular remodeling. Valsartan competitively and selectively blocks AT1 receptors. Angiotensin II levels actually rise, but the extra hormone can act only on AT2 receptors, which may cause mild vasodilation. Because ARBs do not block ACE, bradykinin is not built up, so cough is rare. In Entresto, sacubitril is a prodrug whose active form inhibits neprilysin, raising natriuretic peptides that cause natriuresis and vasodilation; valsartan blocks the angiotensin II that would also rise from neprilysin blockade.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Arterioles relax, lowering blood pressure and the load the heart pumps against."], ["Kidneys", "Efferent arteriole dilation lowers glomerular pressure, which reduces protein in the urine but can raise creatinine, especially with renal artery stenosis or dehydration."], ["Adrenal glands", "Less aldosterone means less sodium retention and more potassium retention, so hyperkalemia can occur."], ["Heart", "Reduced remodeling and fibrosis improve outcomes in heart failure and after myocardial infarction."], ["Fetus", "Blocking angiotensin in the fetus impairs kidney development and can cause death, so it is contraindicated in pregnancy."]], "journey": "Valsartan is taken by mouth. Its bioavailability is low and partly reduced by food, but effects are consistent. It is highly protein bound. Very little is metabolized; most is excreted unchanged in bile and feces, with a small portion in urine. The half-life is about six to nine hours, but blood pressure lowering lasts about a day. The full effect on blood pressure takes a few weeks.", "sidefx": "Hypotension and dizziness come from blocking angiotensin's vasoconstriction. Hyperkalemia comes from lower aldosterone. Kidney function can fall when glomerular filtration depends on angiotensin II constricting the efferent arteriole. Fetal toxicity is a boxed warning. Angioedema is less common than with ACE inhibitors but more common with Entresto because neprilysin also breaks down bradykinin, which is why Entresto needs a washout period after an ACE inhibitor. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Entresto is often called an 'ARNI' (angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor), the first new class to lower heart failure deaths in years.", "src": ["Diovan (valsartan) FDA label", "Entresto (sacubitril/valsartan) FDA label", "McMurray JJV et al. PARADIGM-HF, N Engl J Med 2014"]}, "vancomycin": {"origin": "Vancomycin comes from a soil microbe. In the 1950s Eli Lilly collected soil samples from around the world. A missionary sent one from the jungles of Borneo, and Lilly organic chemist Edmund Kornfeld isolated a compound from the bacterium then called Streptomyces orientalis, now Amycolatopsis orientalis. It killed penicillin-resistant staphylococci, a growing problem. The FDA approved it in 1958. Early batches were brown and impure, nicknamed 'Mississippi mud,' and caused more side effects. It fell out of favor when methicillin arrived, but MRSA brought it back as a key drug. Vancomycin-resistant enterococci were reported in the late 1980s.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Edmund Kornfeld at Eli Lilly isolates vancomycin from a Borneo soil microbe."], ["1958", "FDA approves vancomycin."], ["1980s", "Vancomycin use rises with MRSA; vancomycin-resistant enterococci are first reported."], ["2002", "First US case of vancomycin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is reported."]], "cell": "Bacterial cell walls are built from peptidoglycan, long sugar chains crosslinked by short peptides. New building blocks carry a peptide ending in D-alanyl-D-alanine. Vancomycin is a large glycopeptide that binds tightly to this D-Ala-D-Ala end. The bulky drug blocks transglycosylase from adding the unit to the growing chain and blocks transpeptidases from crosslinking it. The cell wall weakens and the bacterium dies, though killing is slow and time dependent. It is too large to pass the outer membrane of gram-negative bacteria, so it works mainly against gram-positive organisms. Resistant enterococci change the end to D-Ala-D-lactate, which vancomycin binds about a thousand times less well.", "body": [["Infection sites", "Treats serious gram-positive infections such as MRSA bacteremia, endocarditis, osteomyelitis and pneumonia; given orally it treats C. difficile colitis in the gut."], ["Kidneys", "It can injure kidney tubule cells, causing acute kidney injury, especially at high trough levels or with piperacillin-tazobactam, aminoglycosides or contrast."], ["Skin and vessels", "Fast infusion makes mast cells release histamine directly, causing flushing, itch and hypotension, called vancomycin infusion reaction (formerly red man syndrome)."], ["Ears", "Ototoxicity with hearing loss or tinnitus is rare, mostly with very high levels or other ototoxic drugs."], ["Blood", "Neutropenia and thrombocytopenia can occur with longer courses."], ["Skin (rare)", "Severe reactions like DRESS and linear IgA bullous dermatosis are reported."]], "journey": "Given IV, vancomycin spreads through most body fluids and reaches cerebrospinal fluid when the meninges are inflamed. Given by mouth it is not absorbed, so it stays in the gut and is used only for intestinal infections. It is not metabolized. The kidneys excrete it unchanged by glomerular filtration, with a half-life of about four to six hours in normal kidneys and much longer in kidney failure. Dosing is guided by blood levels or by AUC calculations.", "sidefx": "The infusion reaction is not a true allergy. It is direct histamine release from mast cells and is managed by slowing the infusion and giving antihistamines. Kidney injury likely involves oxidative stress in proximal tubule cells and is linked to high exposure. Ototoxicity is rare. There is no antidote; kidney injury is managed by adjusting dosing, and hemodialysis with high-flux membranes can remove some drug.", "fun": "The name vancomycin comes from the word 'vanquish,' reflecting its power against penicillin-resistant staph.", "src": ["Vancomycin hydrochloride for injection FDA label", "Levine DP. Clin Infect Dis 2006", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "varenicline": {"origin": "Varenicline's design started with a plant. Cytisine, an alkaloid from the laburnum tree (golden rain), is a partial agonist at nicotinic receptors and was used in Eastern Europe as a smoking-cessation aid for decades. Chemists at Pfizer, including Jotham Coe, used cytisine as a model to design a more selective partial agonist at the alpha4-beta2 nicotinic receptor, the main receptor behind nicotine addiction. The FDA approved varenicline as Chantix in 2006. A boxed warning about psychiatric events was added in 2009 and removed in 2016 after the large EAGLES trial. In 2021 Pfizer recalled Chantix over a nitrosamine impurity, and generic versions became the main US supply.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Cytisine is marketed in Bulgaria as a smoking-cessation aid."], ["2006", "FDA approves varenicline (Chantix)."], ["2009", "FDA adds a boxed warning on neuropsychiatric events."], ["2016", "FDA removes the boxed warning after the EAGLES trial."], ["2021", "Pfizer recalls Chantix over a nitrosamine impurity."]], "cell": "Nicotine from smoking binds alpha4-beta2 nicotinic acetylcholine receptors on dopamine neurons in the ventral tegmental area. This releases dopamine in the nucleus accumbens, which produces reward and drives addiction. Varenicline is a partial agonist at alpha4-beta2 receptors. It activates them enough to release a moderate amount of dopamine, which eases craving and withdrawal when a person stops smoking. At the same time it occupies the receptors with high affinity, so nicotine from a cigarette cannot bind and produce its full dopamine surge. Smoking becomes less rewarding. It is a full agonist at alpha7 receptors, but the meaning of that is less clear.", "body": [["Brain reward system", "Craving and withdrawal symptoms fall, and cigarettes feel less satisfying, raising quit rates."], ["Gut", "Nausea is the most common side effect, likely from nicotinic receptor stimulation in the gut and brain. It is taken with food and water to reduce this."], ["Sleep", "Insomnia and vivid or abnormal dreams are common."], ["Mood and behavior", "Mood changes, agitation and depressive symptoms have been reported. The EAGLES trial did not show a significant increase in serious neuropsychiatric events compared with nicotine patch."], ["Nervous system", "Seizures are rare. It can lower alcohol tolerance and has been linked to sleepwalking."]], "journey": "Varenicline is taken by mouth and almost fully absorbed. It is not highly protein bound. Very little is metabolized; over 90 percent leaves unchanged in the urine through glomerular filtration and active tubular secretion by OCT2. The half-life is about 24 hours, and steady state takes about four days. Severe kidney disease raises levels. A quit date is usually set about a week after starting.", "sidefx": "Nausea and vivid dreams reflect nicotinic receptor activation. Neuropsychiatric effects are thought to involve changes in dopamine and other pathways, and are monitored even though the boxed warning was removed. Because it is a partial agonist, it can cause some nicotine-like effects but blocks the full effect of nicotine. Hypersensitivity reactions including angioedema and severe skin reactions are rare. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Cytisine, the plant model for varenicline, was given to Soviet and Eastern Bloc smokers for decades before varenicline existed.", "src": ["Chantix (varenicline) FDA label", "Coe JW et al. J Med Chem 2005", "Anthenelli RM et al. EAGLES, Lancet 2016"]}, "vasopressin": {"origin": "In 1895 George Oliver and Edward Schäfer in London found that an extract of the pituitary gland raised blood pressure in animals. That pressor substance was later shown to also reduce urine output, giving it the name antidiuretic hormone. In the early 1950s American biochemist Vincent du Vigneaud worked out the amino acid sequences of vasopressin and oxytocin and synthesized them. He won the 1955 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. Vasopressin was used for decades without formal FDA approval, then in 2014 the FDA approved Vasostrict to raise blood pressure in vasodilatory shock. It is commonly added to norepinephrine in septic shock.", "timeline": [["1895", "Oliver and Schäfer show pituitary extract raises blood pressure."], ["1950s", "Vincent du Vigneaud sequences and synthesizes vasopressin and oxytocin."], ["1955", "Du Vigneaud receives the Nobel Prize in Chemistry."], ["2014", "FDA approves vasopressin (Vasostrict) for vasodilatory shock."]], "cell": "Vasopressin is a nine-amino-acid peptide made in the hypothalamus and released from the posterior pituitary. It acts on three G protein-coupled receptors. V1 (V1a) receptors on vascular smooth muscle couple to Gq, raising IP3 and calcium and causing vasoconstriction. V2 receptors on collecting duct cells couple to Gs, raising cAMP and inserting aquaporin-2 channels, so water is reabsorbed; V2 activation also releases von Willebrand factor and factor VIII from endothelium. V3 (V1b) receptors in the pituitary increase ACTH release. In septic shock, natural vasopressin levels can fall and vessels become less responsive to catecholamines. Giving vasopressin restores tone through a pathway that does not depend on adrenergic receptors.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Strong vasoconstriction raises systemic vascular resistance and blood pressure, often letting norepinephrine be reduced."], ["Kidneys", "V2 activation makes urine more concentrated and reduces urine output; excess water retention can cause hyponatremia."], ["Gut", "Splanchnic vasoconstriction lowers portal pressure but can cause abdominal cramps and mesenteric ischemia."], ["Heart", "Coronary vasoconstriction and higher afterload can lower cardiac output and cause ischemia; reflex bradycardia can occur."], ["Skin and fingers", "Intense peripheral vasoconstriction can cause digital and skin ischemia."], ["Blood clotting", "V2 effects release von Willebrand factor, the basis for using the analog desmopressin in some bleeding disorders."]], "journey": "Vasopressin is a peptide, so it is given IV, usually as a continuous infusion. It acts within minutes. It is broken down by peptidases (vasopressinases) in the liver and kidneys, and some is excreted in urine. Its half-life is about 10 to 20 minutes, so effects fade soon after the infusion stops. In pregnancy, the placenta makes a vasopressinase that speeds its breakdown.", "sidefx": "Side effects are too much vasoconstriction or water retention. V1 effects in the heart, gut and skin cause myocardial ischemia, mesenteric ischemia and digital necrosis. Increased afterload can lower cardiac output. V2 effects cause water retention and hyponatremia. There is no antidote; stopping the infusion reverses effects within minutes because of the short half-life.", "fun": "The longer-acting analog desmopressin (DDAVP) was designed to keep vasopressin's V2 water effect while almost removing its V1 blood pressure effect.", "src": ["Vasostrict (vasopressin) FDA label", "Oliver G, Schäfer EA. J Physiol 1895", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "vecuronium": {"origin": "Muscle relaxants began with curare, a plant-based arrow poison used by Indigenous peoples of South America. Its active ingredient, tubocurarine, entered anesthesia in 1942. In the 1960s chemists at Organon in Scotland designed synthetic aminosteroid relaxants, partly inspired by natural steroidal alkaloids, which led to pancuronium. Pancuronium caused tachycardia because it also blocked cardiac muscarinic receptors. By removing one methyl group to create a monoquaternary relative, the Organon team produced vecuronium, which had almost no cardiovascular effects. It came into clinical use in the early 1980s and was later followed by rocuronium.", "timeline": [["1942", "Tubocurarine, from curare, is first used in surgical anesthesia."], ["1960s", "Organon develops the aminosteroid pancuronium."], ["1980s", "Vecuronium enters clinical use."], ["2015", "FDA approval of sugammadex adds a direct reversal agent for vecuronium."]], "cell": "Vecuronium is a nondepolarizing neuromuscular blocker. At the motor end plate, acetylcholine normally binds the two alpha subunits of the nicotinic (Nm) receptor, opening a cation channel and depolarizing the muscle membrane. Vecuronium binds the same sites competitively without opening the channel. When enough receptors are blocked, the end plate potential cannot reach threshold and the muscle does not contract. Small, fast muscles such as those of the eyes are affected first, then limbs and trunk, and the diaphragm last. Because the block is competitive, raising acetylcholine with an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor such as neostigmine can overcome it. Sugammadex reverses it by encapsulating the drug.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Flaccid paralysis of all skeletal muscles, including the diaphragm, for intubation, surgery or ventilation, with no fasciculations."], ["Breathing", "Breathing stops, so the airway and ventilation must be fully controlled."], ["Brain", "It does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so it gives no sedation or pain relief; patients can be awake and paralyzed without proper sedation."], ["Heart and vessels", "It causes little histamine release and little vagal blockade, so heart rate and blood pressure stay stable."], ["Muscle after ICU use", "Prolonged use, especially with corticosteroids, is linked to ICU-acquired weakness and myopathy."]], "journey": "Vecuronium is given IV and acts within a few minutes. It stays largely in extracellular fluid. The liver takes up and metabolizes much of it, and an active metabolite, 3-desacetylvecuronium, has about half the potency. The drug and its metabolites leave mostly through bile, with some through the kidneys. Duration is intermediate. Liver failure, kidney failure and long infusions prolong its action, partly through buildup of the active metabolite.", "sidefx": "Its intended action, paralysis, is also its main danger: inadequate ventilation, or awareness without sedation. Prolonged block happens with liver or kidney disease, hypothermia, magnesium, aminoglycosides and some anesthetic gases. Anaphylaxis is rare but neuromuscular blockers are a leading cause of anaphylaxis during anesthesia. Reversal can be done with neostigmine (given with glycopyrrolate to block muscarinic effects) once some recovery is present, or with sugammadex, which works even from deeper block.", "fun": "Vecuronium is pancuronium minus one methyl group, a tiny chemical change that removed most of pancuronium's heart-racing effect.", "src": ["Vecuronium bromide for injection FDA label", "Miller's Anesthesia", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "venlafaxine": {"origin": "Venlafaxine was developed by Wyeth in the 1980s as an antidepressant that blocks reuptake of both serotonin and norepinephrine, without the receptor-blocking side effects of tricyclics. The FDA approved it as Effexor in 1993, making it the first widely used serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor (SNRI) in the US. An extended-release form, Effexor XR, was approved in 1997 and later approved for generalized anxiety, social anxiety and panic disorder. Its active metabolite, desvenlafaxine, was later marketed on its own as Pristiq.", "timeline": [["1993", "FDA approves venlafaxine (Effexor) for depression."], ["1997", "FDA approves extended-release venlafaxine (Effexor XR)."], ["2008", "Its active metabolite, desvenlafaxine (Pristiq), is approved."]], "cell": "Venlafaxine inhibits the serotonin transporter (SERT) and, at higher concentrations, the norepinephrine transporter (NET). At lower doses it acts mostly like an SSRI. As doses rise, norepinephrine reuptake blockade adds, and at very high doses there is weak dopamine reuptake inhibition. Blocking reuptake raises synaptic serotonin and norepinephrine. Over weeks, presynaptic autoreceptors such as 5-HT1A desensitize, and downstream changes in gene expression and BDNF signaling develop, which are thought to underlie the delayed antidepressant effect. It has little affinity for muscarinic, histamine or alpha-1 receptors, so it lacks most tricyclic side effects.", "body": [["Brain", "Mood and anxiety improve over weeks. Nausea, insomnia and headache are common early on. It can trigger mania in bipolar disorder."], ["Blood pressure", "Norepinephrine effects raise blood pressure in a dose-related way, so blood pressure is monitored."], ["Skin and sweating", "Increased sweating is common, linked to higher serotonin and norepinephrine activity."], ["Sexual function", "Serotonin effects cause decreased libido, delayed orgasm and erectile problems."], ["Sodium", "SIADH can cause hyponatremia, especially in older adults."], ["Heart in overdose", "Overdose can cause seizures, tachycardia and QRS and QT prolongation, more often than with SSRIs."]], "journey": "Venlafaxine is taken by mouth and well absorbed, usually as an extended-release capsule. It has low protein binding. The liver converts it by CYP2D6 to O-desmethylvenlafaxine (desvenlafaxine), which is equally active. Both are cleared mainly by the kidneys. The half-life is about 5 hours for the parent and 11 hours for the metabolite. Liver or kidney disease raises levels. The short half-life makes withdrawal symptoms likely if doses are missed.", "sidefx": "Nausea, insomnia, sexual problems and sweating come from more serotonin. Higher blood pressure and tachycardia come from norepinephrine. Combining it with MAO inhibitors, linezolid, triptans or tramadol can cause serotonin syndrome. Stopping quickly causes discontinuation syndrome with dizziness, 'brain zaps,' irritability and flu-like symptoms, because of its short half-life. It carries the antidepressant boxed warning about suicidal thoughts in young people. There is no antidote; serotonin syndrome may be treated with cyproheptadine and supportive care.", "fun": "Venlafaxine's withdrawal is notorious for 'brain zaps,' brief electric-shock feelings that can follow even a single missed dose.", "src": ["Effexor XR (venlafaxine) FDA label", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "verapamil": {"origin": "Verapamil was synthesized in 1962 by the German company Knoll AG while searching for coronary vasodilators. It was first thought to act like a beta blocker. In the mid-1960s German physiologist Albrecht Fleckenstein showed that verapamil and related drugs blocked calcium entry into heart and smooth muscle cells, and he coined the term 'calcium antagonist.' Verapamil became the first clinically used calcium channel blocker. It reached the US market in the early 1980s, first as an IV drug for supraventricular tachycardia and then as tablets for angina, hypertension and rate control.", "timeline": [["1962", "Knoll AG synthesizes verapamil in Germany."], ["1960s", "Albrecht Fleckenstein describes calcium antagonism and coins the term."], ["1980s", "Verapamil is approved in the US for arrhythmias, angina and hypertension."]], "cell": "Verapamil is a non-dihydropyridine (phenylalkylamine) calcium channel blocker. It binds the alpha-1 subunit of L-type voltage-gated calcium channels from inside the cell. It is use-dependent: it binds best to channels that open often, which is why it works strongly in rapidly firing nodal tissue. In the SA and AV nodes, depolarization depends on calcium current, so blocking it slows sinus rate and AV conduction and lengthens the AV nodal refractory period. In heart muscle, less calcium entry means less calcium-induced calcium release and weaker contraction. In arteriolar smooth muscle, less calcium lowers contraction and causes vasodilation. Its cardiac effects are stronger than its vascular effects compared with dihydropyridines.", "body": [["AV node", "Slower conduction ends reentrant SVT and controls ventricular rate in atrial fibrillation and flutter."], ["Heart muscle", "Weaker contraction lowers oxygen demand in angina but can worsen heart failure with reduced ejection fraction."], ["Blood vessels", "Arterioles dilate, lowering blood pressure and coronary spasm."], ["Gut", "Calcium channel blockade in intestinal smooth muscle causes constipation, its most common side effect."], ["Head and vessels", "It is also used to prevent cluster headache, a use based on clinical experience."], ["Wide-complex tachycardia", "Giving it for wide-complex tachycardia that is actually ventricular tachycardia, or for WPW with atrial fibrillation, can cause collapse."]], "journey": "Verapamil can be given IV or by mouth. Oral bioavailability is low because of large first-pass metabolism by CYP3A4. IV dosing acts within minutes. It is highly protein bound. The liver converts it to norverapamil, which has some activity. Metabolites leave mainly in urine. The half-life is about three to seven hours, longer in liver disease and with chronic use. It inhibits CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein, raising levels of drugs such as digoxin, statins and some immunosuppressants.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow from calcium channel blockade. Nodal suppression causes bradycardia and AV block. Reduced contractility causes hypotension and heart failure. Gut smooth muscle relaxation causes constipation. Combining it with beta blockers adds to nodal and contractility depression. In overdose, it causes shock with bradycardia and hyperglycemia because insulin release depends on calcium. Treatment includes IV calcium, high-dose insulin with glucose, vasopressors and pacing; there is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Fleckenstein's discovery that verapamil blocks calcium entry launched the entire calcium channel blocker class used today.", "src": ["Calan (verapamil) FDA label", "Fleckenstein A. Annu Rev Pharmacol Toxicol 1977", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "vincristine": {"origin": "Vincristine comes from the Madagascar periwinkle, Catharanthus roseus. Folk healers used periwinkle tea for diabetes. In the 1950s Robert Noble and Charles Beer at the University of Western Ontario tested extracts for blood sugar effects. Instead, the extracts lowered white blood cell counts in rats, which pointed to cancer use. They isolated vinblastine in 1958. Around the same time, scientists at Eli Lilly, including Gordon Svoboda, isolated vincristine and other vinca alkaloids. Vincristine was approved in 1963. It is a core drug for childhood acute lymphoblastic leukemia, which went from almost always fatal to mostly curable.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Noble and Beer find periwinkle extracts lower white blood cell counts."], ["1958", "Vinblastine is isolated from Catharanthus roseus."], ["1961", "Eli Lilly scientists report isolating vincristine."], ["1963", "Vincristine is approved for clinical use."]], "cell": "Vincristine binds beta-tubulin at the vinca binding site, near the growing plus end of microtubules. This blocks the addition of new tubulin dimers, so microtubules cannot form and existing ones break down. Without microtubules, the mitotic spindle cannot form, chromosomes cannot separate and cells arrest in metaphase (M phase). Arrested cells then undergo apoptosis. This makes it cell-cycle phase specific for M phase. Neurons also rely on microtubules to move material along long axons, so vincristine disrupts axonal transport. That is why its main toxicity is neuropathy rather than bone marrow suppression.", "body": [["Cancer cells", "Rapidly dividing cells, as in leukemia, lymphoma and childhood solid tumors, arrest in mitosis and die."], ["Peripheral nerves", "Dose-limiting neuropathy causes tingling, loss of reflexes, foot drop, jaw pain and weakness."], ["Autonomic nerves and gut", "Autonomic neuropathy causes constipation and paralytic ileus, plus urinary retention and orthostatic hypotension."], ["Cranial nerves", "Ptosis, hoarseness and double vision can occur."], ["Kidneys and sodium", "SIADH can cause hyponatremia, so serum sodium is watched during treatment."], ["Bone marrow", "Marrow suppression is mild compared with vinblastine."]], "journey": "Vincristine is given only IV. It distributes rapidly into tissues and binds tightly to platelets and tissues, but poorly enters the brain. The liver metabolizes it by CYP3A4 and CYP3A5, and most is excreted in bile and feces. The terminal half-life is long, around 85 hours. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as azole antifungals raise levels and worsen neuropathy. Liver dysfunction calls for dose reduction.", "sidefx": "Neuropathy follows from microtubule disruption in axons. Constipation and ileus reflect autonomic neuropathy, so bowel regimens are given. Giving it intrathecally by mistake is fatal, causing ascending paralysis; it is labeled 'For intravenous use only, fatal if given by other routes' and is often given in small IV bags to prevent errors. It is a vesicant and severe tissue damage occurs with extravasation; hyaluronidase and warm compresses are used. There is no systemic antidote.", "fun": "Researchers testing a folk diabetes remedy instead found one of the most important cancer drugs for children.", "src": ["Vincristine sulfate injection FDA label", "Noble RL. Biochem Cell Biol 1990", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "voriconazole": {"origin": "Voriconazole was developed by Pfizer as a second-generation triazole antifungal, derived from fluconazole. Fluconazole was safe but weak against molds such as Aspergillus. Pfizer chemists changed the structure to broaden coverage. The FDA approved voriconazole as Vfend in 2002. That same year a major trial by Raoul Herbrecht and colleagues showed it had better survival than amphotericin B for invasive aspergillosis. It became the first-line drug for that deadly infection in immunocompromised patients.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Pfizer develops voriconazole as a broad-spectrum triazole based on fluconazole."], ["2002", "FDA approves voriconazole (Vfend); a key trial shows it beats amphotericin B for invasive aspergillosis."]], "cell": "Fungal membranes use ergosterol. The fungal cytochrome P450 enzyme lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51, encoded by ERG11) converts lanosterol toward ergosterol. Voriconazole's triazole nitrogen binds the heme iron of this enzyme and blocks it. Ergosterol is depleted and toxic 14-alpha-methyl sterols build up. The membrane becomes abnormal, membrane enzymes fail and fungal growth stops. Against Aspergillus and other molds this is fungicidal, while against Candida it is mostly fungistatic. Voriconazole also inhibits human CYP enzymes, especially CYP2C19, CYP2C9 and CYP3A4, which explains many drug interactions.", "body": [["Lungs and other infection sites", "Treats invasive aspergillosis, candidemia and some other molds, improving survival in immunocompromised patients."], ["Eyes", "Transient visual changes, such as blurred vision, altered color vision and seeing bright lights, are very common shortly after doses."], ["Brain", "Hallucinations and confusion can occur, especially at high levels."], ["Liver", "Hepatotoxicity ranges from mild enzyme rises to rare liver failure."], ["Skin", "Photosensitivity is common, and long-term use is linked to squamous cell carcinoma and melanoma."], ["Heart and bone", "It can prolong QT and cause torsades. Long-term use can cause fluorosis and periostitis from fluoride in the molecule."]], "journey": "Voriconazole can be given by mouth or IV, with high oral bioavailability on an empty stomach. It distributes widely, including into the brain and eye. The liver metabolizes it through CYP2C19, CYP2C9 and CYP3A4. Metabolism is nonlinear, so small dose changes can cause large level changes. CYP2C19 genetics strongly affect levels: poor metabolizers have high levels and rapid metabolizers may have too little. Metabolites leave in the urine. The IV form contains a cyclodextrin carrier that can build up in kidney failure.", "sidefx": "Visual changes likely relate to effects on retinal function. Liver injury, QT prolongation and neurotoxicity rise with high levels, so trough levels are often measured. Photosensitivity and skin cancer risk are linked to a phototoxic metabolite. As a strong CYP3A4 inhibitor, it raises levels of tacrolimus, cyclosporine, sirolimus, vincristine and many others, and is contraindicated with some. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Voriconazole's fluoride atoms can cause skeletal fluorosis and painful periostitis with long-term use, an unusual side effect for an antifungal.", "src": ["Vfend (voriconazole) FDA label", "Herbrecht R et al. N Engl J Med 2002", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "zidovudine": {"origin": "Zidovudine, also called AZT, was first made in 1964 by Jerome Horwitz at the Michigan Cancer Foundation as a possible cancer drug. It failed in mice and was set aside. In the early 1980s the AIDS epidemic spread with no treatment. Burroughs Wellcome sent compounds, including AZT, to the National Cancer Institute, where Samuel Broder, Hiroaki Mitsuya and colleagues showed in 1985 that it blocked HIV in cell culture. A trial showed patients lived longer, and the FDA approved it in 1987 as Retrovir, the first HIV drug. In 1994 the ACTG 076 trial showed AZT greatly cut mother-to-child HIV transmission.", "timeline": [["1964", "Jerome Horwitz synthesizes AZT as a potential cancer drug."], ["1985", "NCI researchers show AZT blocks HIV in the lab."], ["1987", "FDA approves zidovudine (Retrovir), the first HIV drug."], ["1994", "ACTG 076 trial shows AZT reduces mother-to-child HIV transmission."]], "cell": "Zidovudine is a thymidine analog in which the 3'-hydroxyl group on the sugar is replaced by an azido group. Host thymidine kinase and other cellular kinases add three phosphates to form zidovudine triphosphate. HIV reverse transcriptase, which copies viral RNA into DNA, accepts it in place of natural thymidine triphosphate (dTTP). Once built into the viral DNA chain, the missing 3'-hydroxyl means no further nucleotide can attach, and the chain terminates. The virus cannot complete DNA copies of its genome to integrate into the host cell. Zidovudine triphosphate also weakly inhibits human mitochondrial DNA polymerase gamma, which explains long-term toxicities.", "body": [["Immune system", "HIV replication falls and CD4 counts rise, though monotherapy leads quickly to resistance, so it is used in combination."], ["Bone marrow", "Marrow suppression causes macrocytic anemia and neutropenia, the main dose-limiting toxicity."], ["Muscle", "Long-term use can cause myopathy from mitochondrial damage."], ["Fat tissue", "Lipoatrophy, loss of fat in the face and limbs, is linked to mitochondrial toxicity."], ["Liver and metabolism", "Rare lactic acidosis with severe fatty liver can occur, a class warning for older NRTIs."], ["Pregnancy and newborns", "It crosses the placenta and is used to prevent mother-to-child transmission, including in newborns."]], "journey": "Zidovudine is taken by mouth or given IV. Oral bioavailability is moderate because of first-pass metabolism. It crosses the blood-brain barrier and placenta. The liver converts most of it by glucuronidation through UGT2B7 to an inactive glucuronide, which leaves in urine. The plasma half-life is short, about one hour, but the active triphosphate lasts longer inside cells. Drugs that cause marrow suppression, such as ganciclovir, add to its toxicity, and stavudine competes with it for activation.", "sidefx": "Marrow suppression comes from effects on dividing marrow cells, causing anemia and neutropenia, which are boxed warnings. Myopathy, lipoatrophy and lactic acidosis come from inhibition of mitochondrial DNA polymerase gamma, which lowers mitochondrial function. Nail hyperpigmentation is common. Anemia may need transfusion or erythropoietin; there is no antidote.", "fun": "A drug abandoned as a cancer treatment in the 1960s sat on a shelf for twenty years before becoming the first approved treatment for HIV.", "src": ["Retrovir (zidovudine) FDA label", "Mitsuya H et al. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA 1985", "Connor EM et al. ACTG 076, N Engl J Med 1994"]}, "ziprasidone": {"origin": "Ziprasidone was developed by Pfizer as a second-generation (atypical) antipsychotic. Its approval was delayed when regulators raised concerns about QT prolongation, and Pfizer carried out a dedicated study comparing QT effects with other antipsychotics. The FDA approved ziprasidone as Geodon in 2001 for schizophrenia, and later for bipolar mania. An intramuscular form was approved in 2002, making it one of the first atypical antipsychotics available as a rapid injection for acute agitation. It became known for having less weight gain and metabolic effect than several other atypicals.", "timeline": [["2001", "FDA approves ziprasidone (Geodon) for schizophrenia."], ["2002", "FDA approves intramuscular ziprasidone for acute agitation in schizophrenia."], ["2004", "FDA approves ziprasidone for acute bipolar mania."]], "cell": "Ziprasidone blocks dopamine D2 receptors in the mesolimbic pathway, which reduces positive symptoms such as hallucinations and delusions. It blocks serotonin 5-HT2A receptors even more strongly. 5-HT2A blockade increases dopamine release in the nigrostriatal and mesocortical pathways, which lowers the risk of extrapyramidal symptoms and may help negative symptoms. It is also a 5-HT1A partial agonist and moderately inhibits serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake, which may add mood effects. It blocks H1 and alpha-1 receptors to a moderate degree, explaining sedation and orthostasis, and has little anticholinergic activity. It also blocks cardiac hERG potassium channels, prolonging the QT interval.", "body": [["Brain", "Psychotic symptoms and manic agitation decrease; sedation and dizziness are common."], ["Heart", "QT prolongation is greater than with many other antipsychotics, so it is avoided with other QT-prolonging drugs or with known long QT."], ["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 blockade can cause orthostatic hypotension."], ["Metabolism", "Weight gain, glucose and lipid changes are smaller than with olanzapine or clozapine."], ["Movement", "Extrapyramidal symptoms, akathisia and rarely neuroleptic malignant syndrome or tardive dyskinesia can occur from D2 blockade."], ["Skin and immune system", "A rare but serious drug reaction, DRESS, has been reported."]], "journey": "Ziprasidone is taken by mouth or given IM. Oral absorption roughly doubles with food, so it must be taken with a meal of adequate calories. It is highly protein bound and crosses the blood-brain barrier. The liver metabolizes it mainly through aldehyde oxidase, with a smaller CYP3A4 role. Metabolites leave in feces and urine. The half-life is about 7 hours by mouth. IM ziprasidone peaks within about an hour.", "sidefx": "D2 blockade in the striatum and pituitary causes movement disorders and, less often, raised prolactin. hERG blockade causes QT prolongation and rare torsades. H1 blockade causes sedation; alpha-1 blockade causes orthostasis. Like all antipsychotics it has a boxed warning for increased death in older adults with dementia-related psychosis. There is no antidote; torsades is treated with magnesium, and dystonia with diphenhydramine or benztropine.", "fun": "Ziprasidone is one of the few medications where skipping food can cut absorption roughly in half.", "src": ["Geodon (ziprasidone) FDA label", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "zolpidem": {"origin": "Zolpidem was developed in France by Synthélabo in the early 1980s. It is an imidazopyridine, chemically different from benzodiazepines but acting at the same receptor complex. It was designed to favor GABA-A receptors with the alpha-1 subunit, which mediate sleep, over those that mediate muscle relaxation and anticonvulsant effects. It was marketed in France in 1988 and approved by the FDA in 1992 as Ambien. It became one of the most prescribed sleep aids in the world. In 2013 the FDA lowered the recommended starting dose for women because of next-morning impairment, and in 2019 added a boxed warning for complex sleep behaviors.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Synthélabo develops zolpidem in France."], ["1988", "Zolpidem is marketed in France."], ["1992", "FDA approves zolpidem (Ambien)."], ["2013", "FDA lowers the recommended dose for women due to next-morning impairment."], ["2019", "FDA adds a boxed warning for complex sleep behaviors."]], "cell": "Zolpidem binds the benzodiazepine site at the interface of the alpha and gamma-2 subunits of the GABA-A receptor. It is a positive allosteric modulator, so it increases how often the chloride channel opens when GABA binds. It has much higher affinity for receptors containing the alpha-1 subunit, which are concentrated in sleep-related brain areas, than for alpha-2, alpha-3 or alpha-5 receptors. More chloride entry hyperpolarizes neurons, which promotes sleep onset. Because of its alpha-1 preference, it has strong sedative effects with weaker anxiolytic, muscle relaxant and anticonvulsant effects than benzodiazepines. Flumazenil can reverse it because it acts at the benzodiazepine site.", "body": [["Brain", "Shortens the time to fall asleep. Short-term memory and alertness are impaired while it acts, and amnesia for events after taking it can occur."], ["Behavior", "Complex sleep behaviors such as sleepwalking, sleep-driving and sleep-eating can happen, sometimes with serious injury."], ["Next morning", "Residual drug, especially with extended release or in women, impairs driving and alertness."], ["Breathing", "It causes little respiratory depression alone, but combined with alcohol or opioids it can be dangerous."], ["Muscles and balance", "Dizziness and impaired coordination raise fall risk, especially in older adults."]], "journey": "Zolpidem is taken by mouth or under the tongue and absorbed quickly, with onset in about 30 minutes. Food delays absorption. It is highly protein bound and crosses the blood-brain barrier. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP3A4 to inactive metabolites, which leave in urine. The half-life is short, about two and a half hours, longer in older adults and liver disease. Women clear it more slowly than men, which is why lower doses are recommended for women.", "sidefx": "Sedation, dizziness and amnesia follow from enhanced GABA-A alpha-1 signaling. Complex sleep behaviors are thought to happen when parts of the brain that control movement are active while awareness and memory are suppressed. Next-day impairment depends on how much drug remains in the blood. Tolerance and dependence can develop. Flumazenil reverses it, though overdose is usually managed with supportive care.", "fun": "Although it is called a 'Z-drug,' zolpidem works at the same receptor site as benzodiazepines, which is why flumazenil reverses it.", "src": ["Ambien (zolpidem) FDA label", "FDA Drug Safety Communication on zolpidem, 2013", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "meclizine": {"origin": "Meclizine is a piperazine antihistamine, one of the first-generation H1 blockers that came out of the great antihistamine boom of the 1940s and 1950s. Doctors had noticed that some sedating antihistamines also calmed motion sickness, and meclizine was found to do this with a longer action and somewhat less drowsiness than older options. It was introduced in the 1950s and became known in the US as Antivert for vertigo and as Bonine and Dramamine Less Drowsy for motion sickness. It remains one of the most prescribed drugs for dizziness, though it treats symptoms rather than the cause of vertigo.", "timeline": [["1940s", "First-generation antihistamines are found to ease motion sickness."], ["1950s", "Meclizine is introduced as a long-acting antihistamine for motion sickness and vertigo."]], "cell": "Meclizine blocks (acts as an inverse agonist at) H1 histamine receptors and also blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors in the brain. Motion sickness and vertigo arise when the vestibular system in the inner ear sends signals that clash with what the eyes and body sense. These signals travel through the vestibular nuclei, which use histamine and acetylcholine, to the vomiting center and the chemoreceptor trigger zone. By blocking H1 and muscarinic receptors along this pathway, meclizine dampens the excitability of the labyrinth and slows conduction from the vestibular nuclei to the cerebellum and vomiting center. Less signal means less spinning, nausea and vomiting.", "body": [["Inner ear and vestibular nuclei", "Calmer vestibular signaling reduces the sense of spinning and the dizziness of motion sickness."], ["Vomiting center", "Blocking histamine and acetylcholine input lowers nausea and vomiting triggered by motion."], ["Brain", "Central H1 blockade causes drowsiness and fatigue, a concern for driving and for older adults."], ["Glands, eyes and bladder", "Muscarinic blockade causes dry mouth, blurred vision and, in some people, trouble passing urine."]], "journey": "Meclizine is swallowed and absorbed from the gut, starting to work in about an hour. Its effect lasts most of a day, which is why it is often taken once for travel. The liver breaks it down, mainly through CYP2D6, so drugs that block CYP2D6 can raise its levels. Metabolites leave in the urine and some unchanged drug leaves in the stool. The half-life is about five to six hours, though the clinical effect lasts longer.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness comes from H1 blockade in the brain, where histamine normally keeps us awake. Dry mouth, blurred vision, constipation and urinary retention come from muscarinic blockade. Older adults are sensitive to these anticholinergic effects, which can cause confusion. Alcohol and other sedatives add to the drowsiness. Used every day for chronic dizziness, it can slow the brain's natural adjustment (vestibular compensation). There is no specific antidote; severe anticholinergic toxicity is treated with supportive care.", "fun": "In the UK and many other countries the same drug is called meclozine, a reminder that one molecule can carry different official names in different places.", "src": ["Antivert (meclizine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "levocetirizine": {"origin": "Levocetirizine is the result of a family tree inside the Belgian company UCB. UCB first developed hydroxyzine, a sedating antihistamine. Its main metabolite, cetirizine, turned out to be a less sedating antihistamine of its own and was approved in the US in 1995 as Zyrtec. Cetirizine is a mix of two mirror-image forms. UCB scientists found that nearly all of the H1-blocking activity lives in the R form, levocetirizine. Isolating that one form allowed the same effect from half the amount of drug. Levocetirizine was approved in Europe in 2001 and by the FDA in 2007 as Xyzal, and it became available over the counter in the US in 2017.", "timeline": [["1956", "UCB introduces hydroxyzine, the parent of cetirizine."], ["1995", "Cetirizine (Zyrtec) is approved in the US."], ["2001", "Levocetirizine is approved in Europe."], ["2007", "FDA approves levocetirizine (Xyzal)."], ["2017", "Levocetirizine becomes available over the counter in the US."]], "cell": "Levocetirizine is a selective inverse agonist at the H1 histamine receptor, a Gq-coupled receptor. When allergen crosslinks IgE on mast cells, histamine is released and binds H1 receptors on blood vessels, sensory nerves and smooth muscle. Through Gq, phospholipase C, IP3 and calcium, histamine causes vessel leak and dilation, itch nerve firing and sneezing reflexes. H1 receptors also have some constant activity even without histamine, and levocetirizine stabilizes them in the inactive state. It binds tightly and comes off slowly, giving a long action. As a charged zwitterion it is a substrate for P-glycoprotein and crosses the blood-brain barrier poorly, so central H1 receptors are less affected than with older antihistamines.", "body": [["Nose", "Less histamine signaling means less sneezing, itching and runny nose in allergic rhinitis, though it helps congestion less."], ["Skin", "Blocking H1 receptors on small vessels and itch nerves reduces wheals and itching in chronic hives."], ["Eyes", "Less itching and watering of the eyes during allergy season."], ["Brain", "Most people stay alert, but some feel sleepy because a small amount still reaches brain H1 receptors."]], "journey": "Levocetirizine is swallowed and absorbed quickly, with effects in about an hour that last a full day. It stays mostly in the blood and extracellular fluid, with a small volume of distribution and high protein binding. Very little is metabolized. Most of the drug is filtered and secreted unchanged by the kidneys, so its half-life of about eight to nine hours lengthens in kidney disease, and the label restricts use in severe kidney failure.", "sidefx": "Sleepiness and fatigue happen in a minority of people because a little drug crosses into the brain, where histamine supports wakefulness. Dry mouth and sore throat are also reported, though it has far less anticholinergic action than first-generation drugs. Because it relies on the kidneys, levels build up in kidney disease and side effects increase. Rare urinary retention and itching after stopping long-term use have been reported. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Levocetirizine is an example of a chiral switch: drug makers isolate the one active mirror-image form of an older mixed drug and develop it as a new product.", "src": ["Xyzal (levocetirizine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "dimenhydrinate": {"origin": "Dimenhydrinate was found by accident at Johns Hopkins. In 1947 allergists Leslie Gay and Paul Carliner were testing a new antihistamine salt for hives. One patient, a woman with lifelong car sickness, reported that her streetcar rides no longer made her ill. Gay and Carliner then ran a famous test, often called Operation Seasickness, on a US Army troop transport crossing the stormy North Atlantic, and the soldiers given the drug had far less seasickness. Searle marketed the drug as Dramamine starting in 1949. Chemically, it is a salt that combines diphenhydramine with 8-chlorotheophylline, a caffeine-like stimulant.", "timeline": [["1947", "Gay and Carliner at Johns Hopkins notice a patient's motion sickness resolve on the drug."], ["1947", "A trial on an Army troopship crossing the Atlantic shows it prevents seasickness."], ["1949", "Searle introduces dimenhydrinate as Dramamine."]], "cell": "Dimenhydrinate splits in the body into diphenhydramine and 8-chlorotheophylline. Diphenhydramine, an ethanolamine first-generation antihistamine, crosses the blood-brain barrier and blocks (acts as an inverse agonist at) H1 receptors and muscarinic acetylcholine receptors. The vestibular nuclei and the pathway to the vomiting center depend on histamine and acetylcholine, so blocking them reduces the mismatched motion signals that cause nausea and dizziness. 8-chlorotheophylline is a methylxanthine that blocks adenosine receptors and was meant to offset some drowsiness, though people still usually feel sleepy. Diphenhydramine also blocks fast sodium channels at high concentration, which matters in overdose.", "body": [["Inner ear pathways", "Less vestibular signaling to the vomiting center prevents and treats motion sickness, nausea and dizziness."], ["Brain", "Central H1 blockade causes strong drowsiness, and children sometimes become excited instead."], ["Glands and eyes", "Muscarinic blockade dries the mouth and blurs vision."], ["Bladder and gut", "Anticholinergic effects can slow the gut and make urination difficult."], ["Heart in overdose", "Sodium channel blockade can widen the QRS complex and cause dangerous rhythms after large ingestions."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth, dimenhydrinate is absorbed and releases diphenhydramine, which starts to work in about 15 to 30 minutes and lasts four to six hours. The injection works almost immediately when given IV. Diphenhydramine spreads widely, including into the brain, and is heavily protein bound. The liver breaks it down, mainly by CYP2D6, and products leave in the urine. Its half-life is roughly four to eight hours, longer in older adults.", "sidefx": "Nearly all of the side effects come from diphenhydramine. Sleepiness reflects H1 blockade in the brain. Dry mouth, blurred vision, constipation, urinary retention and confusion are anticholinergic effects and are stronger in older adults. In overdose, central anticholinergic toxicity causes agitation, hallucinations and seizures, and sodium channel blockade widens the QRS and can cause wide-complex rhythms, which are treated with sodium bicarbonate. Physostigmine can reverse severe central anticholinergic delirium in selected cases under expert guidance.", "fun": "The 1947 troopship test was one of the earliest large controlled drug trials at sea, and the results made Dramamine a household name within a couple of years.", "src": ["Dramamine (dimenhydrinate) OTC Drug Facts label", "Dimenhydrinate injection FDA label", "Gay LN, Carliner PE. Science 1949", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "chlorpheniramine": {"origin": "Chlorpheniramine belongs to the alkylamine family of antihistamines developed at Schering in the late 1940s. Schering chemists first made pheniramine and then found that adding a chlorine atom to one ring greatly raised its potency while keeping drowsiness lower than in many other early antihistamines. Chlorpheniramine was sold as Chlor-Trimeton starting around 1950. It became one of the most widely used allergy drugs in the world and is still found in many multi-symptom cold products, often paired with decongestants and pain relievers.", "timeline": [["1940s", "Schering develops the alkylamine antihistamines, starting with pheniramine."], ["c. 1950", "Chlorpheniramine is introduced as Chlor-Trimeton."]], "cell": "Chlorpheniramine is a first-generation inverse agonist at H1 histamine receptors. In an allergic reaction, mast cells release histamine, which acts on H1 receptors (Gq, phospholipase C, IP3 and calcium) on small blood vessels, sensory nerves and glands to cause leaky vessels, itch, sneezing and watery secretions. Chlorpheniramine competes with histamine and shifts the receptor to its inactive state. Being small and fat-soluble, it crosses the blood-brain barrier and blocks H1 receptors in the brain, where histamine from the tuberomammillary nucleus maintains wakefulness. It also has mild muscarinic blocking activity and some serotonin reuptake inhibition, though less anticholinergic action than ethanolamines like diphenhydramine.", "body": [["Nose and eyes", "Reduced histamine action means less sneezing, itching, runny nose and watery eyes."], ["Skin", "Helps itching and hives by blocking H1 receptors on skin vessels and nerves."], ["Brain", "Crosses into the brain and causes drowsiness and slowed reactions; children may become excited instead."], ["Glands and bladder", "Mild anticholinergic action dries the mouth and can cause urinary retention."]], "journey": "Chlorpheniramine is absorbed well by mouth, starting to work within an hour. Part of it is removed by the liver on its first pass. It spreads widely, including into the brain. The liver breaks it down by CYP2D6 and CYP3A4, and products leave in the urine. Its half-life is long, about a day in many adults and longer in older adults, which is why sedation can carry into the next day even though each dose of the immediate-release form is labeled for only a few hours.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness comes from blocking brain H1 receptors that keep us awake, and it adds to alcohol and other sedatives. Dry mouth, constipation and urinary retention reflect its muscarinic blocking action. Young children can have paradoxical excitement. In overdose, central nervous system toxicity can cause agitation, hallucinations and seizures. Because it is common in cold combinations, overdose often includes other ingredients like acetaminophen or dextromethorphan. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Chlorpheniramine is the antihistamine in the Coricidin HBP line, chosen because those products leave out decongestants for people with high blood pressure.", "src": ["Chlor-Trimeton (chlorpheniramine) OTC Drug Facts label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "brompheniramine": {"origin": "Brompheniramine is a close cousin of chlorpheniramine. Both grew out of the alkylamine antihistamine work of the late 1940s, in which chemists added halogen atoms to the pheniramine molecule. Placing a bromine atom where chlorpheniramine has a chlorine gave a similar, potent H1 blocker. It was introduced in the 1950s and is best known in the US in children's cold and allergy syrups such as Dimetapp, often mixed with a decongestant.", "timeline": [["1940s", "Halogenated alkylamine antihistamines are developed from pheniramine."], ["1950s", "Brompheniramine is introduced and later becomes a staple of Dimetapp syrups."]], "cell": "Brompheniramine is a first-generation alkylamine that acts as an inverse agonist at H1 histamine receptors. It blocks histamine released by mast cells from activating Gq-linked H1 receptors on blood vessels, sensory nerves and mucous glands, which reduces vessel leak, itch and sneezing reflexes. The bromine atom makes the molecule fat-soluble, so it crosses into the brain and blocks central H1 receptors that support wakefulness. Like other first-generation antihistamines it has some muscarinic blocking activity, which helps dry a runny nose but causes anticholinergic side effects.", "body": [["Nose and eyes", "Less sneezing, runny nose and itchy, watery eyes in hay fever and colds."], ["Brain", "Causes drowsiness in many people, while some children become restless or excited."], ["Glands", "Anticholinergic action dries the nose and mouth."], ["Bladder", "Can make urination harder, especially in men with an enlarged prostate."]], "journey": "Brompheniramine is absorbed well from liquids and tablets, working within about an hour. It spreads widely in the body, including the brain. The liver breaks it down and products leave in the urine. Its half-life is long, about a day in adults, so the drug and its sedation can build up when doses are repeated, although labeled dosing intervals are only a few hours.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness reflects blockade of histamine signaling in the brain. Dry mouth, constipation and urinary retention come from muscarinic blockade. Children can show paradoxical excitation and, in overdose, hallucinations and seizures. In 2008 the FDA advised that OTC cough and cold products not be used in children under 2 after reports of serious harm, and the current brompheniramine label says not to use it in children under 6. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Brompheniramine and chlorpheniramine differ by only one atom, a bromine in place of a chlorine, yet each became its own brand-name family of cold medicines.", "src": ["Dimetapp (brompheniramine) OTC Drug Facts label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "FDA public health advisory on OTC cough and cold products in children, 2008"]}, "cyproheptadine": {"origin": "Cyproheptadine was developed at Merck in the late 1950s and introduced in the early 1960s as Periactin. Its three-ring structure resembles the tricyclic antidepressants, and it was designed to block both histamine and serotonin, two mediators of allergic and inflammatory reactions. Doctors soon noticed that patients taking it gained weight and ate more, which led to off-label use as an appetite stimulant. Its serotonin-blocking action also made it the oral antidote used for serotonin syndrome in toxicology practice. The Periactin brand is no longer sold in the US, but generic tablets and syrup remain available.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Merck researchers develop cyproheptadine as a combined antihistamine and antiserotonin drug."], ["1960s", "Cyproheptadine is marketed as Periactin."], ["1990s", "Cyproheptadine becomes the standard oral treatment option for serotonin syndrome in toxicology references."]], "cell": "Cyproheptadine is a first-generation H1 inverse agonist that also blocks serotonin 5-HT2A and 5-HT2C receptors and, to a lesser degree, 5-HT1A receptors, along with muscarinic receptors. Blocking H1 receptors reduces histamine-driven vessel leak and itch. Blocking 5-HT2A receptors in the brainstem and spinal cord counters the excess serotonin stimulation that drives the muscle rigidity, hyperthermia and autonomic instability of serotonin syndrome. Blocking 5-HT2C receptors in the hypothalamus, where serotonin normally signals fullness, is thought to increase appetite. Central H1 and muscarinic blockade cause sedation and anticholinergic effects.", "body": [["Skin, nose and eyes", "H1 blockade reduces itching, hives, sneezing and allergic eye symptoms, and it is labeled for cold urticaria."], ["Hypothalamus", "Blocking serotonin signals of fullness increases appetite and weight gain."], ["Nervous system", "Blocking 5-HT2A receptors is used off-label to calm the overstimulation of serotonin syndrome."], ["Brain", "Strong sedation from H1 blockade, which is also why it is given at night in many uses."], ["Glands and bladder", "Muscarinic blockade causes dry mouth and can cause urinary retention."]], "journey": "Cyproheptadine is absorbed well by mouth and can be crushed and given through a nasogastric tube when a patient cannot swallow. It starts to work in one to two hours. The liver breaks it down, mainly by glucuronidation, and the products leave in the urine. Its half-life in adults is about eight hours, and the clinical effect lasts several hours per dose.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness and slowed thinking come from blocking histamine in the brain. Dry mouth, constipation, blurred vision and urinary retention come from muscarinic blockade. Increased appetite and weight gain come from serotonin 5-HT2C blockade. Children can have paradoxical excitement, and overdose causes anticholinergic toxicity with agitation, hallucinations and seizures. There is no specific antidote; supportive care is the mainstay.", "fun": "Cyproheptadine is used by veterinarians as an appetite stimulant for cats that stop eating.", "src": ["Cyproheptadine hydrochloride FDA label", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "doxylamine": {"origin": "Doxylamine is an ethanolamine antihistamine developed in the late 1940s, in the same family as diphenhydramine. It is one of the most sedating antihistamines, so it became a common over-the-counter sleep aid (Unisom SleepTabs) and the drowsy ingredient in nighttime cold medicines. Its most famous story involves pregnancy. In 1956 Merrell introduced Bendectin, a combination containing doxylamine and pyridoxine, for morning sickness. Lawsuits claiming birth defects followed, and the maker stopped selling it in 1983 even though large studies did not show harm. Decades later the FDA approved the same two ingredients as Diclegis in 2013 and Bonjesta in 2016 for nausea and vomiting of pregnancy.", "timeline": [["1940s", "Doxylamine is developed as an ethanolamine antihistamine."], ["1956", "Bendectin, containing doxylamine and pyridoxine, is introduced for morning sickness."], ["1983", "The maker withdraws Bendectin amid lawsuits, though studies did not confirm birth defects."], ["2013", "FDA approves doxylamine with pyridoxine (Diclegis) for nausea and vomiting of pregnancy."], ["2016", "FDA approves an extended-release combination (Bonjesta)."]], "cell": "Doxylamine is a first-generation inverse agonist at H1 histamine receptors with strong muscarinic blocking activity. Histamine neurons in the tuberomammillary nucleus of the hypothalamus project across the brain and keep us awake through H1 receptors. Doxylamine crosses the blood-brain barrier easily and blocks these receptors, which lowers arousal and causes sleepiness. Its antiemetic effect in pregnancy is thought to come from H1 and muscarinic blockade in the vestibular system and vomiting center. In Diclegis it is paired with pyridoxine, which becomes pyridoxal 5-phosphate, a coenzyme in many reactions; how pyridoxine eases nausea is not fully understood.", "body": [["Brain", "Blocking wakefulness signals from histamine neurons causes drowsiness and helps people fall asleep."], ["Vomiting center", "Reduced histamine and acetylcholine input lowers nausea and vomiting, used in pregnancy with pyridoxine."], ["Nose", "In cold products it dries a runny nose and reduces sneezing."], ["Glands, eyes and bladder", "Anticholinergic action causes dry mouth, blurred vision and urinary retention."], ["Muscle in overdose", "Large overdoses can cause muscle breakdown (rhabdomyolysis)."]], "journey": "Doxylamine is absorbed well by mouth and causes drowsiness within an hour. Diclegis tablets have a delayed-release coating, so effects start several hours after a bedtime dose and help with morning nausea. It spreads widely, including into the brain. The liver breaks it down through CYP2D6, CYP1A2 and CYP2C9, and products leave in the urine. Its half-life is about ten to twelve hours, longer in older adults, which explains next-day grogginess.", "sidefx": "Next-day sleepiness and slowed reactions reflect its long half-life and brain H1 blockade. Dry mouth, constipation, blurred vision, urinary retention and confusion in older adults come from muscarinic blockade. Overdose causes anticholinergic toxicity with agitation, seizures and dysrhythmias, and doxylamine is notable for causing rhabdomyolysis, which can injure the kidneys. There is no specific antidote; supportive care is used, and physostigmine is reserved for selected cases of severe anticholinergic delirium.", "fun": "The Bendectin lawsuits led to the 1993 US Supreme Court case Daubert v. Merrell Dow, which set the standard for how judges screen scientific expert testimony.", "src": ["Diclegis (doxylamine and pyridoxine) FDA label", "Unisom SleepTabs OTC Drug Facts label", "Daubert v. Merrell Dow Pharmaceuticals, 1993", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "desloratadine": {"origin": "Desloratadine is what the body makes from loratadine. Schering-Plough launched loratadine as Claritin, approved in the US in 1993, one of the first widely used non-drowsy antihistamines. Researchers knew that the liver converts loratadine into desloratadine, which is the main active form and lasts longer. Schering-Plough developed the metabolite as its own drug, Clarinex, approved by the FDA in 2001. When Claritin moved to over-the-counter sale in 2002, Clarinex remained a prescription product, and it is still prescription only in the US.", "timeline": [["1993", "FDA approves loratadine (Claritin)."], ["2001", "FDA approves desloratadine (Clarinex), the active metabolite of loratadine."], ["2002", "Loratadine moves over the counter while desloratadine stays prescription only."]], "cell": "Desloratadine is a selective, long-acting inverse agonist at peripheral H1 histamine receptors. It binds the receptor with high affinity and holds it in its inactive state, which prevents histamine from triggering the Gq, phospholipase C and calcium chain that causes vessel leak, itching and sneezing. In laboratory studies it also reduces release of some inflammatory mediators from mast cells and basophils. It is a substrate of P-glycoprotein at the blood-brain barrier and crosses poorly, so brain H1 receptors are largely spared at usual doses. It has little action at muscarinic receptors.", "body": [["Nose and eyes", "Less sneezing, itching, runny nose and watery eyes in seasonal and year-round allergies."], ["Skin", "Reduces the itch and number of hives in chronic idiopathic urticaria."], ["Brain", "Most people do not feel drowsy because little drug enters the brain."], ["Throat and head", "Sore throat, dry mouth, headache and tiredness are the most commonly reported effects."]], "journey": "Desloratadine is absorbed well by mouth and peaks in about three hours. It binds blood proteins moderately and spreads into tissues. The liver hydroxylates it to 3-hydroxydesloratadine and then glucuronidates it; a small share of people are slow metabolizers and reach higher levels. Products leave in urine and stool. Its half-life is long, about 27 hours, which supports once-daily dosing. Liver or kidney impairment raises levels, so the label suggests less frequent dosing.", "sidefx": "Because little drug crosses into the brain, sedation is uncommon. The most common complaints, such as sore throat, dry mouth, fatigue and headache, are mild. Rare reports include fast heart rate and allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis. Slow metabolizers have higher exposure without clearly more side effects. Clarinex-D also contains pseudoephedrine, which adds insomnia, raised blood pressure and fast heart rate. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Every time someone takes Claritin, much of the effect actually comes from desloratadine made in the liver.", "src": ["Clarinex (desloratadine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "clemastine": {"origin": "Clemastine was developed by the Swiss company Sandoz in the 1960s as a long-acting ethanolamine antihistamine. It was sold as Tavist and became available over the counter in the US in the 1990s. Clemastine later drew interest far beyond allergy. In laboratory screens it was found to help oligodendrocyte precursor cells mature and make myelin, and a small clinical trial published in 2017 (ReBUILD) suggested a modest improvement in nerve signal speed in people with multiple sclerosis. That use remains experimental.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Sandoz develops clemastine."], ["1990s", "Clemastine becomes available over the counter in the US as Tavist."], ["2017", "The ReBUILD trial reports possible remyelination effects in multiple sclerosis (experimental)."]], "cell": "Clemastine is a first-generation inverse agonist at H1 histamine receptors with notable muscarinic blocking activity. It stops histamine from activating the Gq, phospholipase C and calcium cascade in blood vessels, sensory nerves and glands, which reduces leaky vessels, itch and sneezing. It crosses the blood-brain barrier and blocks central H1 receptors, which causes drowsiness. Its antimuscarinic action dries secretions. The remyelination effect seen in research is thought to come from blocking the M1 muscarinic receptor on oligodendrocyte precursor cells, which normally restrains their maturation.", "body": [["Nose and eyes", "Less sneezing, runny nose and itchy eyes in allergic rhinitis."], ["Skin", "Reduces hives and the swelling of mild angioedema."], ["Brain", "Drowsiness and dizziness from central H1 blockade."], ["Glands and bladder", "Anticholinergic action dries the mouth and can make urination harder."]], "journey": "Clemastine is absorbed well by mouth, working within an hour, and its effect lasts about half a day or longer. It spreads widely, including into the brain. The liver breaks it down extensively, and the products leave mainly in the urine. Its half-life is long, about 21 hours, so effects can carry over between doses.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness and slowed reactions come from blocking histamine signaling in the brain. Dry mouth, thick secretions, constipation and urinary retention reflect its muscarinic blockade, and these effects are stronger in older adults. Children may have paradoxical excitement. In overdose, anticholinergic toxicity causes agitation, hallucinations and seizures. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Clemastine was picked out of a screen of about a thousand approved drugs tested for their ability to promote myelin formation in the lab.", "src": ["Clemastine fumarate FDA label", "Green AJ et al. Lancet 2017 (ReBUILD trial)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "carbinoxamine": {"origin": "Carbinoxamine is an ethanolamine antihistamine, related to diphenhydramine and doxylamine, that was first approved in the US in the 1950s. For decades it was sold in many cough and cold combinations, a lot of which had never gone through modern FDA approval. In 2006 the FDA acted to remove unapproved carbinoxamine products from the market after reports of deaths in children under 2, and approved carbinoxamine products now state that use in children younger than 2 is contraindicated. Today it is a prescription drug sold as tablets, solution and an extended-release suspension (Karbinal ER).", "timeline": [["1950s", "Carbinoxamine is approved in the US as an antihistamine."], ["2006", "FDA moves to remove unapproved carbinoxamine products after deaths in young children."]], "cell": "Carbinoxamine is a first-generation inverse agonist at H1 histamine receptors. It blocks histamine released from mast cells from activating Gq-linked H1 receptors on small vessels, sensory nerves and glands, which reduces vessel leak, swelling, itch and sneezing. It crosses the blood-brain barrier and blocks H1 receptors on the histamine neurons that maintain wakefulness, producing sedation. It also blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, which dries secretions and adds anticholinergic side effects.", "body": [["Nose and eyes", "Less sneezing, runny nose and itchy, watery eyes in allergic rhinitis and conjunctivitis."], ["Skin", "Reduces itching, hives and dermatographism."], ["Brain", "Sedation and dizziness from central H1 blockade; young children may become excited or have serious breathing depression."], ["Glands and bladder", "Muscarinic blockade causes dry mouth and can cause urinary retention."]], "journey": "Carbinoxamine is absorbed well by mouth. Immediate-release tablets work for a few hours, while the extended-release suspension uses a resin that releases the drug slowly over about half a day. It spreads into tissues, including the brain. The liver breaks it down almost completely, and the products leave in the urine. The half-life is roughly ten to twenty hours.", "sidefx": "Sedation and slowed reactions come from blocking brain histamine. Dry mouth, blurred vision, constipation and urinary retention come from muscarinic blockade. Infants and toddlers are especially vulnerable to central nervous system depression and breathing problems, which is why it is contraindicated under age 2. Overdose causes anticholinergic toxicity and seizures. MAO inhibitors can prolong and intensify its anticholinergic effects. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "src": ["Karbinal ER (carbinoxamine) FDA label", "FDA action on unapproved carbinoxamine products, 2006", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "azelastine": {"origin": "Azelastine is a synthetic phthalazinone antihistamine developed by the German company Asta Medica in the 1980s. Unlike most antihistamines, it was developed mainly for use right where allergy happens, sprayed into the nose or dropped into the eye, so that high local levels act quickly with less drug in the blood. The FDA approved the nasal spray Astelin in 1996, followed by the eye drop Optivar. In 2012 the FDA approved Dymista, which combines azelastine with the steroid fluticasone in one spray, and in 2021 Astepro Allergy became the first antihistamine nasal spray sold over the counter in the US.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Asta Medica in Germany develops azelastine."], ["1996", "FDA approves azelastine nasal spray (Astelin)."], ["2012", "FDA approves azelastine with fluticasone (Dymista)."], ["2021", "Astepro Allergy becomes the first over-the-counter antihistamine nasal spray in the US."]], "cell": "Azelastine is a competitive H1 receptor antagonist (inverse agonist). Applied to the nasal or conjunctival lining, it blocks histamine released by mast cells from activating Gq-linked H1 receptors on blood vessels, sensory nerve endings and glands. That stops the phospholipase C, IP3 and calcium chain that causes vessel leak, swelling, itch and the sneeze reflex. Azelastine also has mast cell stabilizing and anti-inflammatory actions in laboratory studies, reducing release of histamine, leukotrienes and some cytokines. Because it is delivered at high concentration to the tissue, it works within minutes and also helps nonallergic (vasomotor) rhinitis, where nasal nerves and vessels overreact to irritants.", "body": [["Nose", "Less sneezing, itching, runny nose and congestion within about half an hour, in both allergic and vasomotor rhinitis."], ["Eyes", "The eye drop reduces itching from allergic conjunctivitis within minutes."], ["Mouth and taste", "Spray that drips down the throat leaves a bitter taste, the most common complaint."], ["Brain", "Some drug is absorbed, and a minority of people feel sleepy."]], "journey": "After spraying, azelastine acts on the nasal lining within about 30 minutes, and the eye drop works within a few minutes. About 40 percent of a nasal dose reaches the blood, mostly by absorption through the nose and some by swallowing. The liver converts it by CYP enzymes to desmethylazelastine, which is also active. Both have long half-lives, about one to two days for the parent and longer for the metabolite, and they leave mainly in the stool. The nasal effect lasts about twelve hours.", "sidefx": "The bitter taste comes from spray that runs down the back of the throat onto taste buds. Nasal burning, sneezing and nosebleeds reflect local irritation of the lining. Drowsiness happens in some people because enough drug reaches the blood to block a few brain H1 receptors, and alcohol or sedatives can add to it. Serious reactions are rare. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Tilting the head forward while spraying and not sniffing hard helps keep more azelastine in the nose and less of its bitter taste on the tongue.", "src": ["Astepro and Astelin (azelastine) FDA labels", "Dymista FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "olopatadine": {"origin": "Olopatadine was discovered by the Japanese company Kyowa Hakko and developed as an eye drop with the eye care company Alcon. Researchers designed it to have two actions in one drop: blocking H1 receptors for fast itch relief and calming mast cells in the eye to prevent more histamine release. The FDA approved Patanol eye drops in 1996, followed by once-daily Pataday in 2004, a stronger once-daily form, and a nasal spray (Patanase). In 2020 the eye drops moved to over-the-counter sale in the US. A combination nasal spray with mometasone, Ryaltris, was approved in 2022.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Kyowa Hakko discovers olopatadine and partners with Alcon on an eye drop."], ["1996", "FDA approves Patanol eye drops."], ["2004", "FDA approves once-daily Pataday."], ["2020", "Olopatadine eye drops become available over the counter in the US."], ["2022", "FDA approves Ryaltris, olopatadine with mometasone nasal spray."]], "cell": "Olopatadine is a selective H1 receptor antagonist (inverse agonist) that also stabilizes mast cells. In allergic conjunctivitis, allergen binds IgE on conjunctival mast cells, which release histamine and other mediators. Histamine acting on H1 receptors on conjunctival nerves causes itch, and on small vessels causes redness and swelling through Gq and calcium signaling. Olopatadine blocks these receptors and also reduces mast cell degranulation, limiting release of histamine, tryptase and inflammatory cytokines. It has little effect on muscarinic, alpha adrenergic or serotonin receptors, which keeps side effects mostly local.", "body": [["Eyes", "Rapid relief of itching and less redness and swelling from allergic conjunctivitis, often for a whole day with the stronger drops."], ["Nose", "The nasal spray reduces sneezing, itching and runny nose."], ["Mouth and taste", "Nasal spray can leave a bitter taste as it drains into the throat."], ["Head", "Headache is a common minor complaint with the eye drops."]], "journey": "Placed in the eye, olopatadine acts within about 30 minutes, with very little reaching the blood, often too little to measure. The nasal spray gives more absorption, with plasma levels peaking within a couple of hours. Olopatadine is not dependent on CYP enzymes and is only lightly metabolized. Most absorbed drug leaves unchanged in the urine. Its plasma half-life is a few hours by mouth and somewhat longer after nasal use.", "sidefx": "Eye burning, stinging and blurred vision come from the drop itself and its preservative contacting the eye surface. Headache and a bitter taste are common. The nasal spray can irritate the nasal lining, causing nosebleeds and, rarely, ulcers or septal injury, and some people feel sleepy. Because little drug enters the blood, systemic effects are rare. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Pataday and Patanol (olopatadine) FDA labels", "Patanase FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "ketotifen": {"origin": "Ketotifen was developed by the Swiss company Sandoz in the 1970s as an oral drug for asthma and allergy, valued for both blocking histamine and calming mast cells. Oral ketotifen is still used in some countries, but it is not approved in that form in the US. In the US, ketotifen is used as an eye drop. Zaditor eye drops were approved in 1999 and became available over the counter in 2006, sold under names such as Alaway and Zaditor. In 2022 the FDA approved a daily disposable contact lens that releases ketotifen, the first drug-releasing contact lens approved in the US.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Sandoz develops ketotifen as an antiallergic drug."], ["1999", "FDA approves ketotifen eye drops (Zaditor)."], ["2006", "Ketotifen eye drops become available over the counter in the US."], ["2022", "FDA approves a ketotifen-releasing daily contact lens."]], "cell": "Ketotifen is a noncompetitive H1 receptor antagonist that binds tightly and blocks histamine from triggering the Gq and calcium signals that cause itching and vessel leak in the conjunctiva. It also stabilizes mast cells, reducing release of histamine, leukotrienes and other mediators when allergen crosslinks IgE. In addition, it reduces eosinophil chemotaxis and activation, which dampens the late-phase allergic response that keeps eyes red and irritated hours after exposure. These combined actions give quick itch relief that lasts many hours.", "body": [["Eyes", "Rapid relief of itchy eyes from pollen, grass, ragweed and animal dander, lasting up to about half a day."], ["Eye surface", "Some redness, burning or stinging can follow a drop, especially at first."], ["Head", "Headache and a runny nose are reported in some users."]], "journey": "As an eye drop, ketotifen acts on the conjunctiva within minutes. Only tiny amounts reach the blood. When ketotifen is taken by mouth (as in other countries), it is absorbed well, broken down in the liver and excreted mostly in the urine with a half-life of around half a day, but the eye drop gives exposures far below that.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are local: brief burning, redness and stinging from the drop on a sensitive eye surface, and occasionally a feeling of dryness or light sensitivity. Headache may occur. Oral ketotifen, used outside the US, causes drowsiness and weight gain because it reaches brain H1 receptors, but the eye drop delivers too little drug for these effects to be expected. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Zaditor and Alaway (ketotifen) OTC Drug Facts labels", "Acuvue Theravision with Ketotifen FDA approval, 2022", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "cromolyn": {"origin": "Cromolyn grew out of an Egyptian folk remedy and one man's lungs. The khella plant, Ammi visnaga, had long been used in the Middle East as a muscle relaxant, and its compound khellin relaxes smooth muscle. In the 1950s and 1960s Roger Altounyan, a doctor with severe asthma working at the British company Fisons, made and tested hundreds of khellin derivatives by inhaling them himself and then breathing in allergens he was sensitive to. In 1965 he found one compound, disodium cromoglycate, that prevented his asthma attacks. It did not open the airways but stopped the attack from starting. Fisons launched it as Intal in the UK in 1968, and it was approved in the US in the early 1970s. Today it is sold as NasalCrom, eye drops and an oral form for mastocytosis.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Khella (Ammi visnaga) is used in Egypt and the Middle East as a muscle relaxant."], ["1965", "Roger Altounyan at Fisons identifies cromoglycate by testing compounds on himself."], ["1968", "Cromolyn is launched as Intal in the UK."], ["1970s", "Cromolyn is approved in the US."]], "cell": "Cromolyn is a mast cell stabilizer. When allergen crosslinks IgE on the surface of a mast cell, calcium enters the cell and triggers degranulation, releasing histamine and newly made leukotrienes and prostaglandins. Cromolyn is thought to block chloride channels on the mast cell membrane and reduce the calcium influx needed for degranulation, so fewer mediators are released. It also reduces activation of eosinophils and sensory nerve reflexes. It does not block histamine receptors or relax airway smooth muscle directly, so it only works if it is on board before allergen exposure and has no role in treating an attack already in progress.", "body": [["Nose", "Used regularly, it prevents sneezing, itching and runny nose from allergen exposure, with full benefit after one to two weeks."], ["Eyes", "Eye drops prevent itching and redness of allergic conjunctivitis."], ["Lungs", "Inhaled before exercise or allergen exposure, it prevents asthma symptoms, though it is now rarely used for asthma."], ["Gut", "Oral cromolyn stays in the gut and reduces diarrhea, cramping and flushing from mast cell mediators in systemic mastocytosis."]], "journey": "Cromolyn is a highly charged molecule that crosses membranes poorly. Only about one percent of an oral dose is absorbed, so the oral form acts almost entirely on the gut lining. A small share of nasal, eye or inhaled doses is absorbed. Absorbed drug is not metabolized and is cleared quickly and unchanged in urine and bile, with a plasma half-life of about an hour and a half. Because it is cleared fast, it must be used several times a day.", "sidefx": "Cromolyn has very few side effects because so little reaches the blood and it acts on a narrow process. Nasal stinging and sneezing and eye burning reflect local irritation. Inhaled powder can trigger cough or bronchospasm by irritating the airway. Oral use can cause headache and diarrhea. Serious allergic reactions are rare. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Roger Altounyan was also the real-life model for one of the children in Arthur Ransome's Swallows and Amazons books, and he designed the Spinhaler inhaler using ideas from his wartime work on aircraft propellers.", "src": ["NasalCrom and Gastrocrom (cromolyn) labels", "Edwards AM, Howell JB. The chromones: history, chemistry and clinical development. Clin Exp Allergy 2000", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "mometasone": {"origin": "Mometasone furoate was developed by Schering-Plough as a potent synthetic glucocorticoid designed to act strongly on the skin or airway lining while very little survives into the bloodstream. It first appeared as the skin cream Elocon in the late 1980s. The nasal spray Nasonex was approved by the FDA in 1997 for allergic rhinitis and later for nasal polyps, and an inhaled form (Asmanex) followed for asthma. In 2022 a nasal spray version became available over the counter in the US as Nasonex 24HR Allergy.", "timeline": [["1987", "Mometasone furoate cream (Elocon) is approved in the US."], ["1997", "FDA approves mometasone nasal spray (Nasonex)."], ["2022", "Mometasone nasal spray becomes available over the counter in the US."]], "cell": "Mometasone is a highly lipophilic glucocorticoid with high affinity for the glucocorticoid receptor. After diffusing into nasal lining cells, it binds the cytoplasmic receptor, which drops its heat shock proteins and moves into the nucleus. There it switches off inflammatory genes, mainly by blocking the transcription factors NF-kappa B and AP-1 (transrepression), and switches on anti-inflammatory genes such as lipocortin-1 (annexin A1) and MAP kinase phosphatase-1 (transactivation). The result is less production of cytokines such as IL-4, IL-5 and IL-13, fewer eosinophils, mast cells and T cells in the mucosa, and less vessel leak. Because the effect works through gene expression, it builds over days.", "body": [["Nose", "Less congestion, sneezing, itching and runny nose, with some relief within a day and full benefit over one to two weeks."], ["Sinuses and polyps", "Reduced inflammation can shrink nasal polyps and ease sinus blockage."], ["Nasal lining", "Local steroid effect can thin and irritate the lining, causing nosebleeds and, rarely, septal perforation."], ["Eyes", "Allergic eye symptoms often improve too, and long-term steroid use carries a small risk of glaucoma and cataracts."], ["Whole body", "Very little reaches the blood, so effects on growth and adrenal function are minimal at label doses."]], "journey": "Sprayed into the nose, mometasone acts on the lining. Most of the spray is eventually swallowed, but the liver removes nearly all absorbed drug on its first pass using CYP3A4, so systemic bioavailability is under one percent. The small amount in the blood is heavily protein bound and its products leave mainly in the stool. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ritonavir can raise blood levels and cause steroid effects.", "sidefx": "Local side effects come from the steroid suppressing the normal repair and immune activity of the nasal lining: dryness, nosebleeds, and rarely septal perforation or thrush (Candida) in the nose and throat. Headache and sore throat are common. Long-term use may slightly raise the risk of glaucoma and cataracts. Adrenal suppression and slowed growth in children are rare at label doses but can occur with high doses or with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors. There is no antidote; steroid effects fade after stopping.", "src": ["Nasonex (mometasone furoate) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "triamcinolone": {"origin": "Triamcinolone was one of the fluorinated synthetic corticosteroids created in the 1950s, after cortisone's dramatic success in rheumatoid arthritis. Chemists found that adding a fluorine atom and other groups to the cortisol skeleton boosted anti-inflammatory power while reducing salt retention. Lederle introduced triamcinolone in the late 1950s. Its acetonide form, made more lipophilic for local use, went on to be used in creams, joint injections and nasal sprays. In 2013 Nasacort Allergy 24HR became the first steroid nasal spray sold over the counter in the US.", "timeline": [["1948", "Cortisone is first given for rheumatoid arthritis, sparking the steroid era."], ["1950s", "Lederle develops triamcinolone, a fluorinated corticosteroid."], ["2013", "Nasacort Allergy 24HR becomes the first over-the-counter steroid nasal spray in the US."]], "cell": "Triamcinolone acetonide is a glucocorticoid receptor agonist. It diffuses into cells of the nasal lining and binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor, which moves to the nucleus. The activated receptor suppresses inflammatory transcription factors such as NF-kappa B and AP-1, lowering production of cytokines, chemokines and adhesion molecules. It also induces anti-inflammatory proteins. Over several days the nasal lining holds fewer eosinophils, mast cells and T cells, vessels leak less and mucus glands are less active. Because the effect depends on new protein synthesis, it takes days to reach its peak.", "body": [["Nose", "Less congestion, sneezing, itching and runny nose, with some relief on the first day and full effect in about a week."], ["Nasal lining", "Local steroid action can cause dryness, nosebleeds and, rarely, septal perforation or thrush."], ["Eyes", "Long-term steroid exposure carries a small risk of glaucoma and cataracts."], ["Growth in children", "Studies of steroid nasal sprays have shown a small slowing of growth in some children, so the label limits long daily use in children."]], "journey": "After spraying, triamcinolone acts on the nasal lining. Some of the dose is absorbed through the nose and some swallowed, but the liver metabolizes absorbed drug, so blood levels stay low. Its systemic half-life is about three hours, and products leave in the urine and stool. Its anti-inflammatory effect lasts much longer than its time in the blood because it acts through gene expression.", "sidefx": "Side effects come from the steroid acting where it lands. It suppresses immune and repair activity in the nasal lining, which explains dryness, nosebleeds, sore throat and occasional nasal thrush, and rarely septal perforation. Long-term use may modestly raise the risk of cataracts and glaucoma. Absorption is low, so adrenal suppression is rare at label doses, but slowed growth in children has been reported with intranasal steroids. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Nasacort Allergy 24HR (triamcinolone acetonide) OTC Drug Facts label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "oxymetazoline": {"origin": "Oxymetazoline is a synthetic imidazoline developed in Germany in the early 1960s by the company E. Merck, as an improvement on the earlier nasal decongestant xylometazoline. It was designed to give long-lasting relief from a single spray. Sold in the US under names such as Afrin from the 1960s, it became the classic 12-hour nasal spray. Its strong vessel-narrowing effect also made it useful for nosebleeds and before nasal procedures. Newer products use the same molecule elsewhere: Rhofade cream for the facial redness of rosacea (2017) and Upneeq eye drops for mild droopy eyelid (2020).", "timeline": [["1960s", "Oxymetazoline is developed by E. Merck in Germany."], ["1960s", "Oxymetazoline nasal spray enters the US market as a long-acting decongestant."], ["2017", "FDA approves oxymetazoline cream (Rhofade) for persistent facial redness of rosacea."], ["2020", "FDA approves oxymetazoline eye drops (Upneeq) for acquired droopy eyelid."]], "cell": "Oxymetazoline is a direct agonist at alpha-1 (especially alpha-1A) and alpha-2 adrenergic receptors. In the nose, the venous sinusoids of the turbinates swell with blood during a cold or allergy. Alpha-1 receptors on their smooth muscle signal through Gq, phospholipase C and IP3 to release calcium and contract the vessel wall. Alpha-2 receptors, coupled to Gi, lower cAMP and also tighten vessels. The result is fewer engorged vessels, a thinner lining and a wider airway. The same vessel narrowing slows bleeding from a nosebleed. Because it acts directly on receptors rather than by releasing norepinephrine, it works within minutes.", "body": [["Nose", "Rapid shrinking of swollen nasal lining opens the airway for up to about twelve hours."], ["Nosebleeds", "Sprayed before firm pressure, vessel narrowing helps stop anterior bleeding."], ["Nasal lining with overuse", "Use for more than a few days causes rebound swelling and congestion (rhinitis medicamentosa)."], ["Heart and blood pressure", "Absorbed drug can raise blood pressure and cause palpitations in sensitive people."], ["Brain in small children", "Swallowed oxymetazoline acts on central alpha-2 receptors, causing sleepiness, slow heart rate, low breathing drive and even coma."]], "journey": "Sprayed into the nose, oxymetazoline acts within five to ten minutes and lasts up to twelve hours. Some is absorbed through the nasal lining and some is swallowed, but blood levels are normally low. Absorbed drug is only lightly metabolized and leaves in the urine and stool, with a half-life of about five to eight hours.", "sidefx": "Rebound congestion happens because constant alpha stimulation causes the receptors to become less responsive and the lining to swell back more when the drug wears off, which leads to more spraying. That is why labels limit use to three days. Burning, stinging and dryness come from local irritation. Absorbed drug can raise blood pressure and cause palpitations. Swallowed by small children, it acts like clonidine on central alpha-2 receptors, causing drowsiness, slow heart rate, low blood pressure and breathing depression. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive, and naloxone has been tried in imidazoline poisoning with mixed results.", "fun": "Oxymetazoline and clonidine are both imidazolines, which is why a toddler who drinks a bottle of nasal spray can look like a child who swallowed a blood pressure pill.", "src": ["Afrin (oxymetazoline) OTC Drug Facts label", "Rhofade and Upneeq FDA labels", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "zafirlukast": {"origin": "Zafirlukast came from decades of chasing a mystery substance in asthma. In the late 1930s researchers described a slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis (SRS-A) released from allergic lungs that caused long-lasting airway contraction. In 1979 Bengt Samuelsson and colleagues in Sweden showed that SRS-A was a mix of cysteinyl leukotrienes made from arachidonic acid, work that contributed to his 1982 Nobel Prize. Drug makers then raced to block these leukotrienes. Scientists at Zeneca (now AstraZeneca) designed zafirlukast, which in 1996 became the first leukotriene receptor antagonist approved by the FDA, sold as Accolate. Montelukast followed in 1998 and became more widely used because it is taken once daily and has fewer liver concerns.", "timeline": [["1938", "A slow-reacting substance (SRS-A) is described in allergic tissue."], ["1979", "Samuelsson's group identifies SRS-A as cysteinyl leukotrienes."], ["1982", "Bengt Samuelsson shares the Nobel Prize for prostaglandin and leukotriene research."], ["1996", "FDA approves zafirlukast (Accolate), the first leukotriene receptor antagonist."]], "cell": "In asthma, mast cells and eosinophils use 5-lipoxygenase to turn arachidonic acid into LTA4 and then into the cysteinyl leukotrienes LTC4, LTD4 and LTE4. These bind the CysLT1 receptor, a Gq-coupled receptor on airway smooth muscle, mucus glands and vessels. Activation raises intracellular calcium, causing strong, long-lasting bronchoconstriction, mucus secretion, vessel leak with airway swelling and recruitment of eosinophils. Zafirlukast is a selective, competitive CysLT1 receptor antagonist. By keeping leukotrienes off the receptor, it reduces bronchoconstriction and airway inflammation over time. It is a controller, not a rescue drug. It also inhibits CYP2C9 and CYP3A4, which matters for drug interactions.", "body": [["Airways", "Less leukotriene-driven narrowing, swelling and mucus, which lowers day-to-day asthma symptoms and attacks when taken regularly."], ["Immune cells", "Fewer eosinophils are drawn into the airway wall, reducing chronic inflammation."], ["Liver", "Can raise liver enzymes and rarely cause hepatitis or liver failure, so it is contraindicated in liver impairment."], ["Brain and mood", "Like other leukotriene modifiers, it carries warnings for neuropsychiatric effects such as sleep problems, agitation and depression."]], "journey": "Zafirlukast is swallowed and absorbed well on an empty stomach, but food cuts absorption by about 40 percent, so it is taken an hour before or two hours after meals. It peaks in about three hours and is more than 99 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down extensively, mainly by CYP2C9, and products leave mostly in the stool. Its half-life is about ten hours, supporting twice-daily dosing, and full benefit builds over about a week.", "sidefx": "Headache and stomach upset are common. Liver injury, from mild enzyme rises to rare hepatic failure, is thought to involve its liver metabolism, which is why liver function is watched and it is not used with liver disease. Neuropsychiatric reactions are a class warning. Rarely, people with asthma whose oral steroids are tapered develop eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Churg-Strauss syndrome), which may be unmasked as steroids are reduced. Because it inhibits CYP2C9, it can raise warfarin levels and bleeding risk. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Accolate (zafirlukast) FDA label", "Samuelsson B. Leukotrienes. Science 1983", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "liraglutide": {"origin": "Liraglutide grew out of research on incretins, gut hormones that tell the pancreas to release insulin after a meal. Glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) was identified in the 1980s, with key work by Joel Habener, Daniel Drucker, Svetlana Mojsov and Jens Juul Holst, among others. Natural GLP-1 lasts only minutes in the blood because the enzyme DPP-4 cuts it apart. Scientists at Novo Nordisk in Denmark, including Lotte Bjerre Knudsen, solved this by changing one amino acid and attaching a fatty acid chain that lets the molecule ride on albumin. The result, liraglutide, lasts about a day. The FDA approved it as Victoza for type 2 diabetes in 2010 and as Saxenda for weight management in 2014. The LEADER trial (2016) showed fewer heart attacks, strokes and cardiovascular deaths.", "timeline": [["1980s", "GLP-1 is identified and shown to stimulate insulin release."], ["1990s", "Novo Nordisk designs liraglutide, an albumin-binding GLP-1 analog."], ["2010", "FDA approves liraglutide (Victoza) for type 2 diabetes."], ["2014", "FDA approves liraglutide (Saxenda) for chronic weight management."], ["2016", "The LEADER trial shows reduced major cardiovascular events."]], "cell": "Liraglutide is a GLP-1 receptor agonist with 97 percent sequence identity to human GLP-1. The GLP-1 receptor is a Gs-coupled receptor. On pancreatic beta cells, activation raises cAMP and activates protein kinase A and Epac2, which boosts insulin release only when glucose is high; glucose entry and ATP-sensitive potassium channel closure are still needed, so hypoglycemia is uncommon when used alone. In alpha cells it lowers glucagon release when glucose is high. In the stomach it slows emptying through vagal pathways. In the hypothalamus and hindbrain it activates satiety neurons such as POMC neurons and reduces hunger. A C16 fatty acid attached to lysine binds albumin and helps the molecule form clusters at the injection site, which slows absorption and protects it from DPP-4.", "body": [["Pancreas", "More insulin and less glucagon after meals, which lowers blood sugar and A1c."], ["Stomach", "Slower emptying blunts after-meal glucose spikes and adds fullness, but causes nausea and can leave food in the stomach before sedation or surgery."], ["Brain", "Activation of appetite centers lowers hunger and food intake, leading to weight loss."], ["Heart and vessels", "Lowers the risk of major cardiovascular events in adults with type 2 diabetes and heart disease and slightly raises resting heart rate."], ["Gallbladder and pancreas", "Rapid weight loss and changes in bile flow raise the risk of gallstones, and pancreatitis has been reported."], ["Thyroid C cells", "Caused C-cell tumors in rodents, so it carries a boxed warning and is not used with a history of medullary thyroid cancer or MEN 2."]], "journey": "Liraglutide is injected under the skin once daily. It is absorbed slowly, reaching peak levels 8 to 12 hours later. More than 98 percent is bound to albumin, which shields it from enzymes and the kidney. It is broken down like other proteins by DPP-4, neutral endopeptidase and general protein breakdown, and no intact drug is found in urine or stool. Its half-life is about 13 hours. Glucose effects begin within hours, but full A1c and weight effects take weeks.", "sidefx": "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea come from slowed stomach emptying and direct effects on brainstem nausea centers, which is why doses are started low and raised gradually. Vomiting and diarrhea can dehydrate a person and injure the kidneys. Gallstones and pancreatitis are linked to its effects on bile and the pancreas. Hypoglycemia is mainly a risk when it is combined with insulin or sulfonylureas. Food kept in the stomach raises aspiration risk during sedation. The thyroid C-cell tumor warning comes from rodent studies of the GLP-1 receptor on C cells. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Exendin-4, the first GLP-1 drug template, came from the saliva of the Gila monster lizard, but liraglutide was built from the human hormone instead.", "src": ["Victoza and Saxenda (liraglutide) FDA labels", "Marso SP et al. LEADER trial, N Engl J Med 2016", "Drucker DJ. Cell Metabolism 2018 (GLP-1 history)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "abaloparatide": {"origin": "Abaloparatide was modeled on parathyroid hormone-related protein (PTHrP), a hormone discovered in the late 1980s as the cause of high calcium in some cancers (humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy). PTHrP shares the same receptor as parathyroid hormone. Researchers had already learned that daily pulses of parathyroid hormone build bone rather than break it down, leading to teriparatide in 2002. Abaloparatide is a synthetic peptide based on the first 34 amino acids of PTHrP with selected changes, designed to favor bone building with less calcium rise. It was developed by Radius Health, approved by the FDA as Tymlos in 2017 for postmenopausal osteoporosis at high fracture risk, and approved for men with osteoporosis in 2022.", "timeline": [["1987", "PTHrP is identified as the cause of humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy."], ["2002", "Teriparatide, the first PTH-based bone-building drug, is approved."], ["2017", "FDA approves abaloparatide (Tymlos) for postmenopausal osteoporosis."], ["2022", "Abaloparatide is approved for men with osteoporosis at high fracture risk."]], "cell": "Abaloparatide is an agonist at the PTH1 receptor, a Gs-coupled receptor on osteoblasts and kidney cells. Binding raises cAMP and activates protein kinase A, which turns on genes that promote osteoblast survival and bone matrix formation and reduces sclerostin, a natural brake on bone building. PTH1 receptors exist in two shapes: a G-protein-bound form (RG) that gives long signals and a form (R0) that signals briefly. Abaloparatide prefers the RG form, giving a short cAMP burst. When given once daily, this brief, pulsed signal builds new bone more than it triggers the osteoclast activation (through RANKL) that continuous PTH exposure causes. The net result is thicker, denser bone and fewer fractures.", "body": [["Bone", "Daily pulses stimulate osteoblasts to lay down new bone, raising bone mineral density and lowering vertebral and nonvertebral fractures."], ["Kidneys", "PTH1 receptor activation increases calcium reabsorption and can cause high urine calcium and, rarely, kidney stones."], ["Blood calcium", "A small, temporary rise in blood calcium follows each dose."], ["Heart and vessels", "Vessel relaxation can lower blood pressure and speed the heart, causing dizziness or fainting, especially with the first doses."]], "journey": "Abaloparatide is injected under the skin of the lower abdomen once daily. It is absorbed fast, peaking in about half an hour, and is cleared quickly, with a half-life of under two hours, which gives the brief pulse needed for bone building. It is broken down into peptide fragments by normal protein breakdown, and the fragments leave through the kidneys.", "sidefx": "Dizziness, a fast heartbeat and orthostatic hypotension come from PTHrP's natural effect of relaxing blood vessels, so first doses are given sitting or lying down. Raised blood and urine calcium reflect PTH1 receptor action on bone and kidney, which can lead to kidney stones. Injection-site redness and nausea are common. Rats given high doses developed osteosarcoma, so use is limited in people with higher bone cancer risk, such as those with Paget disease, open growth plates or prior skeletal radiation. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Tymlos (abaloparatide) FDA label", "Miller PD et al. ACTIVE trial, JAMA 2016", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "tesamorelin": {"origin": "Tesamorelin traces back to the hunt for the hypothalamic hormone that tells the pituitary to release growth hormone. In 1982 teams led by Roger Guillemin and Wylie Vale isolated growth hormone-releasing hormone (GHRH) from pancreatic tumors that were making it in excess and causing acromegaly. The natural hormone breaks down in minutes. The Canadian company Theratechnologies added a trans-3-hexenoyl group to the start of the full 44-amino-acid GHRH chain, which protects it from the enzyme DPP-4. People living with HIV on antiretroviral therapy can build up deep belly fat (lipodystrophy), and tesamorelin was found to reduce this fat. The FDA approved it as Egrifta in 2010.", "timeline": [["1982", "Guillemin's and Vale's groups isolate GHRH from pancreatic tumors."], ["2000s", "Theratechnologies develops tesamorelin, a stabilized GHRH analog."], ["2010", "FDA approves tesamorelin (Egrifta) for excess abdominal fat in HIV lipodystrophy."]], "cell": "Tesamorelin binds the GHRH receptor on somatotroph cells of the anterior pituitary. This Gs-coupled receptor raises cAMP and activates protein kinase A, which opens calcium channels and triggers growth hormone release, while also increasing growth hormone gene transcription. Because tesamorelin works through the pituitary, normal feedback from IGF-1 and somatostatin still shapes the pattern, so growth hormone is released in pulses. Growth hormone acts on the liver to raise IGF-1 and directly on fat cells to increase lipolysis. Visceral fat cells are especially responsive, so deep abdominal fat shrinks more than fat under the skin.", "body": [["Pituitary", "More growth hormone is released in natural pulses."], ["Liver", "Growth hormone raises IGF-1, which is monitored during treatment."], ["Abdominal fat", "Increased fat breakdown reduces visceral fat in the belly, though fat returns if the drug is stopped."], ["Glucose control", "Growth hormone opposes insulin, so blood sugar can rise and new diabetes can occur."], ["Joints, muscles and nerves", "Fluid retention and tissue swelling cause joint pain, muscle aches, swelling of the hands and feet and carpal tunnel symptoms."]], "journey": "Tesamorelin is injected under the skin of the abdomen once daily. It is absorbed quickly and has a very short half-life of under an hour, broken down like other peptides by protease enzymes. Despite its short time in the blood, the growth hormone pulses it triggers keep IGF-1 raised throughout daily treatment. Its elimination routes are not fully characterized.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from raised growth hormone and IGF-1. Fluid retention explains swelling, joint pain, muscle aches and carpal tunnel syndrome. Growth hormone counters insulin, which can raise blood sugar. Injection-site redness and itching are common, and hypersensitivity reactions can occur. Because IGF-1 promotes cell growth, it is avoided in active cancer, and it is contraindicated in pregnancy, when fat gain is normal and lowering it offers no benefit. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Egrifta SV (tesamorelin) FDA label", "Guillemin R et al. Science 1982", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "bremelanotide": {"origin": "Bremelanotide began with a tanning experiment. In the 1980s and 1990s researchers at the University of Arizona developed melanotan peptides, synthetic versions of alpha-melanocyte stimulating hormone, as a way to darken skin and protect it from sunburn. Volunteers given melanotan II reported unexpected sexual arousal, including erections in men. The company Palatin Technologies developed bremelanotide, a related cyclic peptide that is a metabolite of melanotan II, first as a nasal spray for sexual dysfunction and then as an injection. The FDA approved it as Vyleesi in 2019 for premenopausal women with acquired, generalized hypoactive sexual desire disorder.", "timeline": [["1980s", "University of Arizona researchers develop melanotan peptides for tanning."], ["1990s", "Melanotan II volunteers report sexual arousal as an unexpected effect."], ["2019", "FDA approves bremelanotide (Vyleesi) for hypoactive sexual desire disorder in premenopausal women."]], "cell": "Bremelanotide is a nonselective melanocortin receptor agonist with its main action at the melanocortin 4 receptor (MC4R). MC4R is a Gs-coupled receptor on neurons in the hypothalamus, including the medial preoptic area, which helps regulate sexual desire. Activation is thought to increase dopamine release in these circuits, which shifts the balance toward excitatory signals related to sexual desire. The exact pathway in humans is not fully known. Bremelanotide also activates MC1R on melanocytes, which increases melanin production and explains skin darkening, and its MC4R action on autonomic pathways causes brief changes in blood pressure and heart rate.", "body": [["Brain", "Activation of hypothalamic MC4R pathways is thought to increase sexual desire and reduce related distress."], ["Gut and brainstem", "Nausea is the most common effect, especially with the first doses, and some people vomit."], ["Blood vessels and heart", "Blood pressure rises briefly and heart rate drops slightly after each dose, usually returning to normal within about half a day."], ["Skin", "MC1R activation can cause flushing and darkening of the skin, gums or face, sometimes lasting after stopping."]], "journey": "Bremelanotide is self-injected under the skin of the abdomen or thigh with an autoinjector, at least 45 minutes before expected activity. It is absorbed almost completely, peaks in about an hour and has a half-life of roughly two to three hours. It is broken down by cutting of its peptide bonds, and the products leave mainly in the urine with some in the stool.", "sidefx": "Nausea comes from melanocortin receptor activation in brain areas that control vomiting. The temporary blood pressure rise and heart rate drop reflect MC4R effects on the autonomic system, which is why it is not used in uncontrolled high blood pressure or known heart disease. Flushing and headache are common. Focal skin darkening comes from MC1R stimulation of melanocytes and is more common with frequent use. It slows stomach emptying and can lower levels of oral naltrexone. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Melanotan peptides are still sold illegally online as tanning injections, and they are not FDA approved for that or any cosmetic use.", "src": ["Vyleesi (bremelanotide) FDA label", "Hadley ME, Dorr RT. Peptides 2006 (melanotan history)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "calcitonin-salmon": {"origin": "Calcitonin was discovered in 1962 by the Canadian physiologist Harold Copp at the University of British Columbia. He showed that a hormone, not just parathyroid hormone, helped control blood calcium, and named it calcitonin because it regulated calcium tone. The hormone was later found to come from the C cells of the thyroid gland in mammals and from separate ultimobranchial glands in fish. Salmon calcitonin turned out to be far more potent and longer acting in humans than human calcitonin, so the synthetic salmon version became the medicine. It was approved in the US in the 1970s as an injection (Miacalcin) and later as a nasal spray. Branded nasal sprays have since been discontinued, while the injection and generic nasal spray remain available.", "timeline": [["1962", "Harold Copp discovers calcitonin."], ["1960s", "Calcitonin is found in thyroid C cells and in fish ultimobranchial glands."], ["1970s", "Synthetic salmon calcitonin injection is approved in the US."], ["1995", "Calcitonin salmon nasal spray is approved for postmenopausal osteoporosis."]], "cell": "Calcitonin salmon binds calcitonin receptors, Gs-coupled receptors that are dense on osteoclasts. Rising cAMP within minutes causes osteoclasts to pull back their ruffled border, shrink and stop resorbing bone. With less bone breakdown, less calcium and phosphate are released into the blood. In the kidney, calcitonin receptors in the tubules reduce reabsorption of calcium and phosphate, adding to the calcium drop. Calcitonin also has a pain-relieving effect in acute vertebral fractures that is not fully understood. Over a day or two, osteoclasts reduce their receptor numbers and escape the effect (tachyphylaxis), which limits its usefulness in hypercalcemia.", "body": [["Bone", "Osteoclasts stop breaking down bone, which lowers calcium release and slows the high bone turnover of Paget disease."], ["Blood calcium", "Calcium falls within hours, but the drop is modest and fades within about two days as receptors down-regulate."], ["Kidneys", "More calcium and phosphate are passed in the urine."], ["Gut and skin", "Nausea and facial flushing are common after injection."], ["Nose", "The nasal spray can cause irritation, dryness and nosebleeds."]], "journey": "Calcitonin salmon is a peptide, so it cannot be swallowed. It is given by injection under the skin or into muscle, or as a nasal spray, which delivers only a small share of the dose to the blood. Calcium starts to fall in about two hours after injection. It is broken down quickly in the kidney, blood and tissues by protein enzymes, with a half-life of about an hour after injection and shorter after nasal use. Inactive fragments leave in the urine.", "sidefx": "Nausea and flushing come from calcitonin's effects on the gut and blood vessels. Low calcium, rarely severe enough to cause tetany or seizures, reflects its intended action. Because it is a foreign (fish) protein, it can cause allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis and bronchospasm, and antibodies can form and reduce its effect. Long-term use has been linked to a small increase in cancer risk in pooled studies, so it is reserved for cases where other options do not fit. There is no specific antidote; low calcium is treated with calcium.", "fun": "Salmon calcitonin is roughly 40 to 50 times more potent than human calcitonin because it binds the human receptor more tightly and resists breakdown.", "src": ["Miacalcin (calcitonin salmon) FDA label", "Copp DH et al. Endocrinology 1962", "FDA Drug Safety Communication on calcitonin salmon and cancer risk, 2014", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "pramlintide": {"origin": "Pramlintide is based on amylin, a second hormone made by pancreatic beta cells. In 1987 two research groups, one led by Garth Cooper in the UK and one by Per Westermark in Sweden, identified amylin as the main protein in the amyloid deposits found in the pancreas of people with type 2 diabetes. Amylin is released together with insulin and helps control glucose after meals. Human amylin itself sticks together into insoluble fibrils, so it cannot be used as a drug. Scientists at Amylin Pharmaceuticals in San Diego borrowed from rat amylin, which does not clump, and replaced three amino acids in the human sequence with proline. The resulting soluble analog, pramlintide, was approved by the FDA in 2005 as Symlin, to be used alongside mealtime insulin.", "timeline": [["1987", "Cooper's and Westermark's groups identify amylin from pancreatic amyloid."], ["1990s", "Amylin Pharmaceuticals designs pramlintide with three proline substitutions to prevent clumping."], ["2005", "FDA approves pramlintide (Symlin) for type 1 and type 2 diabetes with insulin."]], "cell": "Pramlintide is an agonist at amylin receptors, which are made of the calcitonin receptor paired with a receptor activity-modifying protein (RAMP1, 2 or 3). These Gs-coupled receptors are concentrated in the area postrema and nearby brainstem regions that sit outside the blood-brain barrier. Activation there sends signals through the vagus nerve that slow stomach emptying, lower the glucagon surge that normally follows a meal and increase fullness. Unlike insulin, pramlintide does not push glucose into cells. It works by slowing glucose arrival from the gut and reducing liver glucose output driven by glucagon, which flattens the after-meal glucose rise.", "body": [["Stomach", "Slower emptying means glucose from a meal enters the blood more gradually."], ["Pancreas and liver", "Lower after-meal glucagon reduces glucose release from the liver."], ["Brain", "Greater fullness leads to smaller meals and modest weight loss."], ["Gut", "Nausea, loss of appetite and vomiting are common, especially when starting."], ["Blood sugar", "Combined with mealtime insulin, it can cause severe low blood sugar, which is why insulin doses are reduced when it is started."]], "journey": "Pramlintide is injected under the skin of the abdomen or thigh right before major meals, in a separate site from insulin and never mixed with it. It is absorbed within about 20 minutes and has a short half-life of under an hour, matching the time course of a meal. The kidneys metabolize it to an active fragment and clear it. Its effect lasts about three hours.", "sidefx": "Nausea and appetite loss come directly from its action on brainstem satiety and vomiting centers and from slowed stomach emptying. The boxed warning for severe hypoglycemia is not caused by pramlintide alone. It happens because pramlintide slows glucose arrival while the full mealtime insulin dose keeps working, so blood sugar can fall within a few hours, especially in type 1 diabetes. Slowed stomach emptying also changes how quickly other oral drugs are absorbed. It is not used in gastroparesis or in people unaware of their low blood sugars. Low blood sugar is treated with glucose or glucagon.", "fun": "Human amylin's tendency to form amyloid is why pramlintide was built with three prolines taken from the rat version of the hormone, since rats do not form islet amyloid.", "src": ["Symlin (pramlintide) FDA label", "Cooper GJ et al. PNAS 1987", "Westermark P et al. PNAS 1987", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "setmelanotide": {"origin": "Setmelanotide came from understanding how the brain senses fat stores. After leptin was discovered in 1994, researchers mapped a hypothalamic pathway in which leptin activates POMC neurons, POMC is cut by the enzyme PCSK1 into alpha-melanocyte stimulating hormone, and this hormone activates the melanocortin 4 receptor (MC4R) to reduce hunger. Rare genetic defects anywhere along this chain cause extreme hunger and obesity starting in early childhood. Setmelanotide, a cyclic peptide MC4R agonist, was originally developed at Ipsen and then by Rhythm Pharmaceuticals to bypass these broken steps. The FDA approved it as Imcivree in 2020 for obesity due to POMC, PCSK1 or LEPR deficiency, and in 2022 for Bardet-Biedl syndrome, with later expansion that includes acquired hypothalamic obesity.", "timeline": [["1994", "Leptin is discovered, opening the hypothalamic appetite pathway to study."], ["1990s", "Mutations in MC4R and POMC are linked to severe early-onset obesity."], ["2020", "FDA approves setmelanotide (Imcivree) for POMC, PCSK1 and LEPR deficiency obesity."], ["2022", "FDA approves setmelanotide for obesity in Bardet-Biedl syndrome."]], "cell": "Setmelanotide is an agonist at the melanocortin 4 receptor, a Gs-coupled receptor on neurons of the paraventricular nucleus of the hypothalamus. Normally leptin signals through its receptor (LEPR) on arcuate POMC neurons, which release alpha-MSH made from POMC by PCSK1. Alpha-MSH activates MC4R, raising cAMP and signaling satiety while increasing energy use. In POMC, PCSK1 and LEPR deficiency, too little alpha-MSH is made, but MC4R itself still works. Setmelanotide activates MC4R directly, replacing the missing signal and reducing hunger. Its partial activity at MC1R on melanocytes increases melanin production, and MC4R signaling in spinal pathways can cause spontaneous erections.", "body": [["Hypothalamus", "Restored MC4R signaling reduces the constant, intense hunger of these genetic and hypothalamic disorders."], ["Body weight", "Lower food intake and some increase in energy expenditure produce weight loss over weeks to months."], ["Skin", "MC1R activation darkens skin and can darken moles, so skin exams are part of follow-up."], ["Mood", "Depression and suicidal thoughts have been reported, consistent with other drugs acting on central appetite pathways."], ["Sexual function", "Spontaneous erections in males and sexual arousal changes in females can occur."]], "journey": "Setmelanotide is injected under the skin once daily at the start of the day. It is absorbed slowly, peaking about eight hours later, and has a half-life of about eleven hours. It is broken down like other peptides into small fragments, and a small share leaves unchanged in the urine. Weight and hunger effects build over weeks. Kidney impairment raises exposure, so the dose is adjusted in moderate to severe kidney disease.", "sidefx": "Skin darkening and new or darker moles come from MC1R stimulation of melanocytes. Spontaneous erections and arousal changes come from MC4R signaling in sexual function pathways. Nausea, vomiting and headache are common early. Depression and suicidal ideation may relate to MC4R effects in mood circuits. Injection-site reactions are frequent. In newborns, benzyl alcohol in the vial is a concern, so it is not used in very young infants. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Imcivree (setmelanotide) FDA label", "Clement K et al. Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol 2020", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "pyridoxine": {"origin": "Vitamin B6 was named in 1934 by the Hungarian-born scientist Paul Gyorgy, who found a factor in food that cured a skin disease in rats on deficient diets. In 1938 several laboratories, including Samuel Lepkovsky's, isolated the pure vitamin, and in 1939 Stanton Harris and Karl Folkers at Merck, along with Richard Kuhn in Germany, worked out its structure and synthesized it. Gyorgy called it pyridoxine because of its pyridine ring. Later, doctors learned that the tuberculosis drug isoniazid, introduced in 1952, depletes vitamin B6 and in overdose causes seizures that resist usual drugs. Large doses of pyridoxine became the specific antidote. It is also part of Diclegis, with doxylamine, for nausea and vomiting of pregnancy.", "timeline": [["1934", "Paul Gyorgy names vitamin B6."], ["1938", "Vitamin B6 is isolated in pure form."], ["1939", "Harris and Folkers at Merck and Kuhn in Germany determine its structure and synthesize it."], ["1950s", "Isoniazid is linked to B6 depletion, neuropathy and seizures."], ["2013", "FDA approves doxylamine with pyridoxine (Diclegis) for nausea and vomiting of pregnancy."]], "cell": "Pyridoxine is converted by pyridoxal kinase and an oxidase into pyridoxal 5-phosphate (PLP), the active coenzyme. PLP is required by more than a hundred enzymes, including transaminases, decarboxylases and delta-aminolevulinic acid synthase, the first step of heme synthesis. A key one is glutamic acid decarboxylase, which turns glutamate into GABA, the brain's main inhibitory neurotransmitter. Isoniazid forms hydrazones with pyridoxine and inhibits pyridoxal kinase, so PLP falls and GABA production drops while excitatory glutamate builds up. The result is seizures that respond poorly to benzodiazepines, because those drugs need GABA to be present to work. Giving pyridoxine restores PLP and GABA synthesis, which stops the seizures.", "body": [["Brain", "Restored GABA production stops isoniazid-induced seizures and lets benzodiazepines work again."], ["Nerves", "Adequate B6 prevents the peripheral neuropathy caused by isoniazid, but very high doses for months can cause sensory neuropathy of their own."], ["Blood", "PLP is needed for heme synthesis, and deficiency can cause a sideroblastic anemia."], ["Skin and mouth", "Deficiency causes cracked lips, a sore tongue and a scaly rash."], ["Metabolism", "Supports amino acid breakdown and the conversion of homocysteine in the transsulfuration pathway."]], "journey": "Pyridoxine is absorbed easily from the small intestine and works within minutes when given IV. The liver converts it to PLP, which binds tightly to albumin in the blood and to enzymes in tissues. Body stores of PLP last weeks. The final breakdown product, 4-pyridoxic acid, and any excess vitamin leave in the urine. In isoniazid overdose, the IV form is used because seizures need rapid correction, and hospital emergency stocks are often too small for a full antidote dose.", "sidefx": "Pyridoxine is very safe in short courses. Injection can cause local pain, and nausea or headache can occur. Months of very high daily intake can damage sensory nerve cells in the dorsal root ganglia, causing numbness and unsteady gait, which usually improves after stopping. Allergic reactions are rare. High doses can reduce the effect of levodopa when taken without carbidopa, because PLP speeds levodopa breakdown outside the brain.", "fun": "Pyridoxine is one of the few vitamins used as a true antidote, reversing seizures from isoniazid and some mushroom poisonings (such as Gyromitra) that block the same B6 pathway.", "src": ["Pyridoxine hydrochloride injection FDA label", "Diclegis FDA label", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements vitamin B6 fact sheet"]}, "folic-acid": {"origin": "The story of folic acid began in Bombay in the late 1920s, where the British doctor Lucy Wills studied a severe megaloblastic anemia in pregnant textile workers. In 1931 she reported that the yeast extract Marmite cured it, and the unknown nutrient was called the Wills factor. In 1941 Herschel Mitchell, Esmond Snell and Roger Williams isolated a growth factor from spinach and named it folic acid, from the Latin folium for leaf. In 1945 a team at Lederle Laboratories led by Yellapragada SubbaRow synthesized it. Decades later, the 1991 UK Medical Research Council Vitamin Study proved that folic acid before and early in pregnancy prevents most neural tube defects, which led the US to require folic acid fortification of enriched grain products starting in 1998.", "timeline": [["1931", "Lucy Wills reports that Marmite cures anemia of pregnancy in India."], ["1941", "Mitchell, Snell and Williams isolate folic acid from spinach."], ["1945", "SubbaRow's team at Lederle synthesizes folic acid."], ["1991", "The MRC Vitamin Study shows folic acid prevents neural tube defects."], ["1998", "US fortification of enriched grain products with folic acid takes effect."]], "cell": "Folic acid is a synthetic, oxidized form of folate. Dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR) reduces it first to dihydrofolate and then to tetrahydrofolate (THF). THF carries single carbon units in several forms. N5,N10-methylene THF gives a methyl group to thymidylate synthase, which turns dUMP into dTMP, a building block of DNA. N10-formyl THF supplies carbons for purine rings. N5-methyl THF gives its methyl group to homocysteine through B12-dependent methionine synthase, making methionine and regenerating THF. Without enough folate, rapidly dividing cells, such as red cell precursors and the cells closing the embryonic neural tube, cannot make DNA fast enough. Nuclei mature slowly while cytoplasm keeps growing, producing large megaloblastic cells.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Restores normal DNA synthesis in red cell precursors, which corrects megaloblastic anemia."], ["Developing embryo", "Adequate folate around conception and early pregnancy supports closure of the neural tube in the first weeks, preventing most spina bifida and anencephaly."], ["Blood vessels and metabolism", "Folate lowers homocysteine by supplying methyl groups for its conversion to methionine."], ["Nervous system", "Folic acid can correct the anemia of B12 deficiency while nerve damage continues, which can mask B12 deficiency."]], "journey": "Folic acid is absorbed well in the upper small intestine and peaks in the blood within an hour. Unlike food folates, which carry chains of glutamates that must be trimmed first, folic acid is absorbed directly. The liver and other tissues reduce it to tetrahydrofolate and its methyl form, and the liver stores some and secretes some into bile for reuse. Excess leaves in the urine. Plasma levels fall within hours, but body stores last a few months.", "sidefx": "Folic acid is very safe. Allergic reactions are rare. The main concern is that it can fix the blood picture of B12 deficiency without fixing B12's role in nerve myelin, so nerve damage can progress unnoticed. That is why B12 is checked in megaloblastic anemia. Folic acid does not reverse the effects of methotrexate or trimethoprim on DHFR, because it needs DHFR to become active; leucovorin (folinic acid), which bypasses DHFR, is the rescue drug for methotrexate toxicity.", "fun": "Lucy Wills chose Marmite because it was a cheap, rich source of yeast nutrients, so a breakfast spread helped uncover a vitamin.", "src": ["Folic acid tablets FDA label", "MRC Vitamin Study Research Group. Lancet 1991", "Wills L. BMJ 1931", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements folate fact sheet"]}, "cyanocobalamin": {"origin": "Vitamin B12 was found while solving pernicious anemia, a disease that used to be fatal. In 1926 George Minot and William Murphy showed that patients recovered if they ate large amounts of raw liver, work that earned them and George Whipple the 1934 Nobel Prize. In 1929 William Castle showed that the stomach makes an intrinsic factor needed to absorb something in food. In 1948 Karl Folkers' group at Merck and E. Lester Smith at Glaxo isolated the red crystalline vitamin. The cyanide group in cyanocobalamin is actually an artifact of the purification process, but it made a stable form for medicine. In 1956 Dorothy Hodgkin used X-ray crystallography to solve its complex structure, a feat cited in her 1964 Nobel Prize in Chemistry.", "timeline": [["1926", "Minot and Murphy treat pernicious anemia with a liver diet."], ["1929", "William Castle describes intrinsic factor from the stomach."], ["1934", "Minot, Murphy and Whipple share the Nobel Prize for liver therapy."], ["1948", "Vitamin B12 is isolated at Merck and at Glaxo."], ["1956", "Dorothy Hodgkin solves the structure of vitamin B12."]], "cell": "Cyanocobalamin is converted in cells to two active coenzymes. Methylcobalamin is the cofactor for methionine synthase, which moves a methyl group from N5-methyl tetrahydrofolate to homocysteine, making methionine and freeing tetrahydrofolate for DNA synthesis. Without B12, folate becomes trapped as N5-methyl THF (the methyl-folate trap), DNA synthesis slows and megaloblastic anemia results. Adenosylcobalamin is the cofactor for methylmalonyl-CoA mutase in mitochondria, which converts methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA. Without it, methylmalonic acid builds up, and abnormal fatty acids and loss of methionine for methylation reactions are thought to damage myelin, causing neuropathy and subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Freed tetrahydrofolate restores DNA synthesis, and new red cells (reticulocytes) rise within about 3 to 5 days."], ["Nerves and spinal cord", "Supports myelin, stopping the progression of numbness, weakness, balance problems and memory changes; long-standing damage may not fully recover."], ["Metabolism", "Lowers homocysteine and methylmalonic acid, which are both raised in deficiency."], ["Blood potassium", "Rapid new red cell production at the start of treatment pulls potassium into cells and can drop blood potassium."]], "journey": "Food B12 is freed by stomach acid, bound to intrinsic factor from the stomach and absorbed in the terminal ileum. In pernicious anemia intrinsic factor is missing, so injection into muscle or deep under the skin bypasses the gut. A tiny share of oral B12 is also absorbed by passive diffusion without intrinsic factor, which is why very high oral doses can work for some people. In the blood it binds transcobalamin, which delivers it to cells. The liver stores large amounts that can last years. Excess beyond what binding proteins can hold is lost in the urine.", "sidefx": "B12 is very safe. Injection pain, mild diarrhea and itching can occur. When severe deficiency is corrected, a burst of new red cell production can lower potassium and, in rare cases, uncover polycythemia vera. Allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, are rare and may relate to the cobalt molecule or preservatives. Cyanocobalamin should not be confused with hydroxocobalamin, the form used for cyanide poisoning that turns urine red. There is no antidote needed.", "fun": "Vitamin B12 is the only vitamin with a metal atom, cobalt, at its center, and only bacteria and archaea can make it.", "src": ["Cyanocobalamin injection FDA label", "Minot GR, Murphy WP. JAMA 1926", "Hodgkin DC et al. Nature 1956", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements vitamin B12 fact sheet"]}, "magnesium-oxide": {"origin": "Magnesia takes its name from Magnesia, a district in ancient Greece where white magnesium-rich minerals were found. In 1755 the Scottish chemist Joseph Black showed that magnesia was different from lime, and in 1808 Humphry Davy isolated the element magnesium. Magnesium compounds became popular stomach remedies in the 1800s; the American chemist Charles Phillips patented his milk of magnesia (magnesium hydroxide) in 1872. Magnesium oxide, the simple oxide of the metal, is a dense, inexpensive tablet that neutralizes stomach acid and provides a large share of magnesium by weight, so it is sold both as an antacid and as a magnesium supplement.", "timeline": [["1755", "Joseph Black distinguishes magnesia from lime."], ["1808", "Humphry Davy isolates the element magnesium."], ["1872", "Charles Phillips patents milk of magnesia, popularizing magnesium antacids."]], "cell": "Magnesium oxide is a base. In the stomach it reacts with hydrochloric acid to form magnesium chloride and water, raising gastric pH and reducing acid irritation of the esophagus and stomach lining. Higher pH also lowers pepsin activity. Most magnesium is not absorbed, and the remaining magnesium ions in the intestine are poorly absorbed and hold water by osmosis. They may also trigger release of cholecystokinin and other signals that increase intestinal secretion and movement. The extra water softens stool and speeds transit, which is a laxative effect at larger amounts. The fraction that is absorbed adds to the body's magnesium, which is needed for hundreds of enzyme reactions, ATP use and nerve and muscle function.", "body": [["Stomach", "Neutralizes acid, easing heartburn, sour stomach and indigestion."], ["Intestines", "Unabsorbed magnesium pulls water into the bowel, which can cause loose stools or diarrhea."], ["Blood magnesium", "The absorbed portion helps correct low magnesium."], ["Kidneys", "Absorbed magnesium is cleared by the kidneys, so it builds up when kidney function is poor."], ["Heart, nerves and muscles", "Very high blood magnesium blocks calcium channels and nerve signals, causing weakness, low blood pressure and slow heart rate."]], "journey": "Magnesium oxide works on stomach acid within minutes. It is not metabolized. Only a fraction of the magnesium, often well under half, is absorbed in the small intestine, and absorption falls at higher amounts. Absorbed magnesium is filtered by the kidneys, which normally adjust excretion very well. The unabsorbed portion leaves in the stool. Magnesium also binds some drugs in the gut, such as tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones and bisphosphonates, reducing their absorption.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea and cramping come from unabsorbed magnesium drawing water into the bowel. The serious risk is hypermagnesemia in kidney failure, when the kidneys cannot clear absorbed magnesium. High magnesium blocks calcium entry at nerve endings and in the heart, causing loss of reflexes, weakness, low blood pressure, breathing depression and, in severe cases, cardiac arrest. IV calcium counteracts these effects, and dialysis removes magnesium. Raising stomach pH and binding drugs in the gut explain its many absorption interactions.", "fun": "Magnesium oxide is the same compound used as a heat-resistant lining in furnaces, because it melts at about 2,800 degrees Celsius.", "src": ["Magnesium oxide OTC Drug Facts label", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements magnesium fact sheet", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "bismuth-subsalicylate": {"origin": "Bismuth compounds have been used for stomach complaints since at least the 1700s. Pepto-Bismol began in the early 1900s, when a New York physician created a pink remedy for infant diarrhea, a deadly problem at the time, which was later sold by the Norwich Pharmacal Company. It took the name Pepto-Bismol around 1919. In the 1970s and 1980s, careful studies showed that bismuth subsalicylate helps prevent and treat travelers' diarrhea. After 1982, when Barry Marshall and Robin Warren identified Helicobacter pylori, bismuth also became a standard part of quadruple therapy for H. pylori infection, work that earned them the 2005 Nobel Prize.", "timeline": [["1700s", "Bismuth salts are used for digestive complaints."], ["1900s", "A pink bismuth remedy for infant diarrhea is created in New York, later called Pepto-Bismol."], ["1970s", "Studies show bismuth subsalicylate prevents and treats travelers' diarrhea."], ["1982", "Marshall and Warren identify Helicobacter pylori; bismuth becomes part of eradication regimens."]], "cell": "In the stomach, bismuth subsalicylate splits into bismuth and salicylate. Salicylate is absorbed and inhibits cyclooxygenase, lowering prostaglandins that drive intestinal fluid and chloride secretion, so less water is lost into the bowel. Bismuth stays mostly in the gut. It binds bacterial toxins, such as those from enterotoxigenic E. coli, coats the lining and has direct antimicrobial effects. Against H. pylori, bismuth disrupts the bacterial cell wall and inhibits enzymes such as urease and ATP synthesis. In the colon, bismuth reacts with hydrogen sulfide made by gut bacteria to form bismuth sulfide, a black compound that darkens stool and tongue.", "body": [["Intestines", "Lower prostaglandin-driven secretion and toxin binding reduce diarrhea and cramping, including travelers' diarrhea."], ["Stomach", "Coats and soothes the lining, easing heartburn, nausea and fullness, and helps kill H. pylori in combination regimens."], ["Tongue and stool", "Bismuth sulfide forms a harmless black color on the tongue and in the stool that can be mistaken for bleeding."], ["Ears and whole body", "Absorbed salicylate can cause ringing in the ears and, with heavy use, salicylate toxicity."], ["Brain", "Long-term overuse of bismuth has rarely caused bismuth encephalopathy, with confusion and muscle jerks."]], "journey": "Bismuth subsalicylate is swallowed as a liquid or chewed tablet and works in the gut within about an hour. In the stomach it separates into salicylate and bismuth. Most of the salicylate is absorbed and handled like aspirin's salicylate, broken down by the liver and excreted by the kidneys, with a half-life of a few hours at usual amounts. Less than one percent of the bismuth is absorbed; the rest leaves in the stool, and the small absorbed amount is cleared slowly in the urine.", "sidefx": "Black tongue and stool come from bismuth sulfide and are harmless. Constipation reflects slowed secretion. Salicylate explains ringing in the ears and salicylate toxicity with heavy use, especially when combined with aspirin. Because of salicylate, it is avoided in children and teens recovering from chickenpox or flu (Reye syndrome risk), in aspirin allergy and with anticoagulants. Bismuth encephalopathy is a rare effect of chronic heavy use. Salicylate toxicity is treated with supportive care, sodium bicarbonate to alkalinize urine and sometimes dialysis.", "fun": "Eight doses of regular-strength liquid bismuth subsalicylate contain an amount of salicylate comparable to several adult aspirin tablets, which is why it can contribute to salicylate poisoning.", "src": ["Pepto-Bismol (bismuth subsalicylate) OTC Drug Facts label", "DuPont HL et al. JAMA 1987 (travelers' diarrhea prevention)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "simethicone": {"origin": "Simethicone is a mix of polydimethylsiloxane (a silicone polymer) and silicon dioxide. Silicones were developed in the mid-1900s, with Frederic Kipping's earlier work and later industrial chemistry at companies such as Dow Corning and General Electric. Silicone antifoam agents became important in industry for breaking up unwanted foam in food processing and manufacturing. Doctors borrowed the same idea for gas trapped as foam in the gut, and simethicone entered medical use in the mid-1900s. It is now a common over-the-counter gas remedy and is also used before endoscopy and some imaging to clear bubbles that hide the view.", "timeline": [["1900s", "Frederic Kipping's research lays the groundwork for silicone chemistry."], ["1940s", "Silicone products, including antifoams, are made on an industrial scale."], ["1950s", "Silicone antifoams are adopted in medicine for intestinal gas."]], "cell": "Simethicone has no receptor or enzyme target. It acts physically. In the stomach and intestine, gas often gets trapped as many small bubbles in mucus-rich foam. Simethicone spreads across the bubble surfaces and lowers their surface tension. The thin films between bubbles become unstable and break, so small bubbles merge into larger pockets. Larger gas pockets are easier to move along and release by belching or passing gas. It does not reduce the amount of gas made by gut bacteria; it only changes its form so it passes more easily.", "body": [["Stomach and intestines", "Merges small gas bubbles into larger ones, which can relieve bloating, pressure and fullness."], ["Endoscopy and imaging", "Clears foam from the lining so the gut wall can be seen clearly during endoscopy or ultrasound."], ["Infants", "Drops are widely used for gas, although studies have not shown clear benefit for infant colic."]], "journey": "Simethicone is taken by mouth as chewable tablets, softgels or drops. It acts within minutes inside the gut. It is not absorbed into the blood and is not metabolized. It passes through the digestive tract and leaves unchanged in the stool.", "sidefx": "Because it is not absorbed and has no drug target, simethicone has almost no side effects. Mild loose stools or nausea are occasionally reported. It can bind some levothyroxine in the gut, so they are usually separated in time. Allergy is rare. There is no toxicity syndrome and no antidote is needed.", "fun": "The same kind of silicone antifoam in gas pills is added to some cooking oils and to industrial vats to keep them from foaming over.", "src": ["Gas-X (simethicone) OTC Drug Facts label", "FDA antiflatulent monograph (21 CFR 332)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "benzocaine": {"origin": "Benzocaine came from the search for a safer local anesthetic than cocaine. In 1884 the Viennese eye doctor Carl Koller showed that cocaine could numb the eye for surgery, but cocaine was toxic and addictive. In 1890 the German chemist Eduard Ritsert synthesized ethyl para-aminobenzoate, a simple ester that numbed tissue on contact. It was sold from 1902 under the name Anasthesin. Because it dissolves poorly in water, it stays where it is applied, which made it ideal for creams, gels, sprays and lozenges for skin, mouth and throat pain. In 2018 the FDA warned against OTC benzocaine teething products for infants and children under 2 because of the risk of methemoglobinemia.", "timeline": [["1884", "Carl Koller introduces cocaine as a local anesthetic for eye surgery."], ["1890", "Eduard Ritsert synthesizes benzocaine."], ["1902", "Benzocaine is marketed as Anasthesin."], ["2018", "FDA warns against OTC benzocaine teething products for children under 2."]], "cell": "Benzocaine is an ester local anesthetic. Most local anesthetics exist in charged and uncharged forms, but benzocaine has no ionizable amine at body pH, so it stays uncharged and passes easily into nerve membranes. It blocks voltage-gated sodium channels on sensory nerve endings, likely by entering the channel from the membrane. Blocked channels cannot open to carry the sodium surge that forms an action potential, so pain signals do not travel from the area to the spinal cord. Small pain fibers, the C and A-delta fibers, are blocked most easily. When it is cleared, the channels recover and feeling returns.", "body": [["Mouth and gums", "Numbs toothache, canker sores and gum or denture pain within a minute."], ["Throat", "Lozenges and sprays numb a sore throat and ease swallowing."], ["Skin", "Sprays and creams numb minor burns, sunburn and insect bites."], ["Blood", "Absorbed drug can oxidize hemoglobin to methemoglobin, which cannot carry oxygen and causes cyanosis."]], "journey": "Benzocaine works on the surface where it is applied, with numbness in under a minute that lasts roughly 15 to 20 minutes. It dissolves poorly in water, so little is absorbed through intact skin, but more crosses broken skin and mucous membranes, especially the throat. Absorbed benzocaine is quickly hydrolyzed by plasma cholinesterase to para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA), and products leave in the urine. Sprays to the throat before procedures can deliver larger amounts than intended.", "sidefx": "The most serious toxicity is methemoglobinemia. Benzocaine or its metabolites oxidize iron in hemoglobin from the ferrous to the ferric state, which cannot carry oxygen and shifts the oxygen curve to the left. Patients turn gray-blue, pulse oximetry reads around 85 percent and does not improve with oxygen, and blood looks chocolate brown. Infants, older adults and people with G6PD deficiency or heart and lung disease are most at risk. The antidote is IV methylene blue, which speeds conversion back to normal hemoglobin; it is avoided in G6PD deficiency. Its PABA metabolite explains allergic contact reactions in some people.", "fun": "Benzocaine's breakdown product, PABA, was once a common sunscreen ingredient, and people allergic to one can react to the other.", "src": ["Orajel and other benzocaine OTC Drug Facts labels", "FDA Drug Safety Communication on benzocaine and methemoglobinemia, 2018", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "clotrimazole": {"origin": "Clotrimazole was one of the first imidazole antifungals. It was discovered at the German company Bayer in the late 1960s, as researchers looked for drugs that could stop fungal infections without the toxicity of older agents. Introduced as Canesten in the early 1970s, it became a widely used topical treatment for athlete's foot, jock itch, ringworm and vaginal yeast infections. In the US it is sold over the counter as Lotrimin AF and Gyne-Lotrimin, and by prescription as Mycelex troches for oral thrush. It is not used by mouth for systemic infections because it is poorly tolerated and broken down too quickly.", "timeline": [["1969", "Bayer researchers report clotrimazole as a broad-spectrum antifungal."], ["1970s", "Clotrimazole is introduced as Canesten."], ["1990s", "Clotrimazole vaginal cream becomes one of the first OTC vaginal yeast treatments in the US."]], "cell": "Clotrimazole inhibits lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51), a fungal cytochrome P450 enzyme. The imidazole nitrogen binds the heme iron in the enzyme, blocking it from removing a methyl group from lanosterol. Without this step, the fungus cannot make ergosterol, the main sterol in fungal cell membranes. Ergosterol levels fall while toxic methylated sterols build up and distort the membrane, which disrupts membrane-bound enzymes and makes the membrane leaky. At usual levels this stops fungal growth (fungistatic), and at high local concentrations it can directly damage the membrane and kill the fungus.", "body": [["Skin of feet, groin and body", "Clears dermatophyte infections such as athlete's foot, jock itch and ringworm over two to four weeks, relieving itching and scaling."], ["Vagina", "Vaginal cream treats Candida yeast infections."], ["Mouth", "Prescription troches dissolved in the mouth treat oral thrush."], ["Skin at the site", "Mild burning, redness or irritation can occur where it is applied."]], "journey": "Applied to the skin, clotrimazole stays mostly in the outer skin layers where the fungus lives. Very little reaches the blood, often too little to measure. A small share of vaginal doses is absorbed. Troches release the drug into saliva, and the swallowed portion is absorbed and broken down by the liver. Any absorbed drug is metabolized by liver enzymes and leaves mainly in bile.", "sidefx": "Side effects are mostly local irritation, such as burning, stinging or redness, from the cream or its ingredients. Allergic contact dermatitis is uncommon. Because so little is absorbed from the skin, systemic effects are not expected. Troches can raise liver enzymes and, as an azole that inhibits human CYP3A4, can raise levels of drugs such as tacrolimus. There is no antidote needed.", "src": ["Lotrimin AF (clotrimazole) OTC Drug Facts label", "Mycelex troche FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "miconazole": {"origin": "Miconazole was created at Janssen Pharmaceutica in Belgium, the company founded by Paul Janssen, and reported in 1969. It was one of the early imidazole antifungals and was developed in the same period as Bayer's clotrimazole. An IV form was once used for serious fungal infections but was dropped because of toxicity from its solvent. Miconazole found its lasting role in creams, powders and sprays for skin infections and in vaginal products such as Monistat, which became available over the counter in the US in the early 1990s. A buccal tablet, Oravig, is used for oral thrush.", "timeline": [["1969", "Janssen Pharmaceutica reports miconazole."], ["1970s", "Miconazole creams and vaginal products enter wide use."], ["1990s", "Miconazole vaginal products (Monistat) become available over the counter in the US."]], "cell": "Miconazole is an imidazole that binds the heme iron of fungal lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51). This blocks conversion of lanosterol to ergosterol, the essential sterol of fungal membranes. Ergosterol runs short and abnormal 14-alpha-methyl sterols accumulate, making the membrane leaky and disturbing membrane enzymes such as chitin synthase. Miconazole also causes buildup of reactive oxygen species inside fungal cells and, at high local levels, directly damages the membrane. The overall effect is mainly fungistatic against dermatophytes and Candida.", "body": [["Skin", "Clears athlete's foot, jock itch and ringworm, easing itching, cracking and scaling."], ["Vagina", "Vaginal creams and suppositories treat Candida yeast infections."], ["Mouth", "A buccal tablet stuck to the gum releases drug for oral thrush."], ["Local tissue", "Burning, itching or irritation can occur where it is applied, especially in the vagina."]], "journey": "On the skin, miconazole stays in the outer layers, and very little reaches the blood. Vaginal use leads to a small amount of absorption. The buccal tablet keeps high levels in saliva for many hours. Any drug that is absorbed is broken down by the liver and excreted mainly in the stool. Even small absorbed amounts can matter because miconazole strongly inhibits CYP2C9 and CYP3A4.", "sidefx": "Local side effects come from irritation of the skin or vaginal lining. Allergic contact reactions are uncommon. The key interaction is with warfarin: absorbed miconazole, even from vaginal or oral gel products, inhibits CYP2C9, which breaks down the more potent S-warfarin. Warfarin levels rise and serious bleeding has been reported. Vaginal products can also weaken latex condoms and diaphragms. There is no specific antidote; warfarin over-anticoagulation is managed with vitamin K.", "src": ["Micatin and Monistat (miconazole) OTC Drug Facts labels", "Oravig FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "triple-antibiotic-ointment": {"origin": "Triple antibiotic ointment combines three antibiotics with three separate origin stories. Bacitracin was found in 1945 at Columbia University by Balbina Johnson and Frank Meleney, from a Bacillus strain growing in the wound of a young girl named Margaret Tracy; the name blends Bacillus and Tracy. Polymyxins were found in 1947 in the soil bacterium now called Paenibacillus polymyxa. Neomycin was isolated in 1949 by Selman Waksman and Hubert Lechevalier at Rutgers from Streptomyces fradiae. Burroughs Wellcome combined them in Neosporin, which became one of the best-known first-aid products in the US. Polysporin, without neomycin, is a separate product for people who react to neomycin.", "timeline": [["1945", "Johnson and Meleney discover bacitracin from a Bacillus in a child's wound."], ["1947", "Polymyxins are discovered in a soil Bacillus."], ["1949", "Waksman and Lechevalier isolate neomycin from Streptomyces fradiae."], ["1950s", "Neosporin combines bacitracin, neomycin and polymyxin B for topical use."]], "cell": "Each antibiotic hits a different target. Bacitracin binds undecaprenyl pyrophosphate, the lipid carrier that shuttles peptidoglycan building blocks across the membrane, and stops it from being recycled. Without the carrier, gram-positive bacteria such as staphylococci and streptococci cannot build their cell wall. Neomycin is an aminoglycoside that binds the 30S ribosomal subunit, causing misreading of messenger RNA and faulty proteins, mainly against gram-negative bacteria and some staph. Polymyxin B is a cationic detergent-like peptide that binds the lipid A portion of lipopolysaccharide in the gram-negative outer membrane, displaces calcium and magnesium, and punches holes in the membrane. Together they cover many surface bacteria.", "body": [["Skin wounds", "Reduces bacteria on small cuts, scrapes and burns, helping prevent superficial infection."], ["Wound surface", "The petrolatum base keeps the wound moist, which itself supports healing."], ["Skin immune system", "Neomycin and bacitracin are common contact allergens, which can cause an itchy, red rash that looks like a wound that is not healing."]], "journey": "The ointment acts on the wound surface. Very little of any of the three antibiotics is absorbed through small wounds or intact skin. Large areas of broken skin or burns can allow more absorption, and neomycin and polymyxin B, if absorbed in quantity, can harm the kidneys and, for neomycin, the hearing nerves. Any absorbed drug leaves through the kidneys.", "sidefx": "The main problem is allergic contact dermatitis, a delayed T-cell reaction. Neomycin is one of the most common contact allergens tested by skin specialists, and bacitracin has rarely caused anaphylaxis when applied to wounds. Mild irritation can occur. Used over large wounds for long periods, absorbed neomycin can cause kidney damage and hearing loss, and polymyxin can injure kidneys and nerves. Overuse may also encourage resistance. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Neomycin was named Allergen of the Year in 2010 by the American Contact Dermatitis Society, and bacitracin earned the same title in 2003.", "src": ["Neosporin (bacitracin, neomycin, polymyxin B) OTC Drug Facts label", "Johnson BA, Anker H, Meleney FL. Science 1945", "Waksman SA, Lechevalier HA. Science 1949", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "terbinafine-topical": {"origin": "Terbinafine belongs to the allylamine antifungals, a class found by chance at the Sandoz Research Institute in Vienna in the 1970s. Researchers there noticed that a compound made for a different purpose, naftifine, had antifungal activity. Building on that lead, Anton Stutz and colleagues designed terbinafine in the early 1980s, a more potent allylamine that kills dermatophytes rather than only slowing them. It was launched as Lamisil, first as a cream and later as tablets for nail infections. Terbinafine 1% cream was approved in the US in the 1990s and moved over the counter as Lamisil AT in 1999. Oral terbinafine remains a separate prescription drug with its own liver risks.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Sandoz researchers in Vienna discover the allylamine antifungal naftifine."], ["1980s", "Anton Stutz and colleagues develop terbinafine."], ["1990s", "Terbinafine cream is approved in the US."], ["1999", "Terbinafine 1% cream becomes available over the counter as Lamisil AT."]], "cell": "Terbinafine inhibits squalene epoxidase, an enzyme early in the fungal ergosterol pathway that converts squalene to squalene epoxide. This step comes before the CYP51 step blocked by azoles. Blocking it has two effects. Ergosterol, the sterol fungal membranes need, runs short, and squalene builds up to toxic levels inside the cell, collecting in lipid droplets and damaging the membrane. The squalene buildup is the main reason terbinafine is fungicidal against dermatophytes, while azoles are mostly fungistatic. Human squalene epoxidase is much less sensitive, which gives terbinafine its selectivity.", "body": [["Skin of the feet", "Kills dermatophytes that cause athlete's foot, often clearing between-the-toes infection in about a week of use."], ["Groin and body", "Treats jock itch and ringworm with short courses."], ["Outer skin layer", "Builds up in the stratum corneum and lingers there after stopping, which helps keep killing fungus."], ["Skin at the site", "Mild burning, itching or dryness can occur where it is applied."]], "journey": "Terbinafine cream, gel or spray is applied to the skin, where the drug is very fat-soluble and concentrates in the outer skin layer and hair follicles. Levels in the skin stay high for days after the last application. Very little reaches the blood. The oral tablets are a different case: they are absorbed, broken down in the liver by several CYP enzymes and can rarely injure the liver, but the topical product gives far too little blood exposure for that to be expected.", "sidefx": "Topical side effects are local and mild: burning, stinging, itching, redness or dryness, mostly from irritation. Allergic contact dermatitis is uncommon. Because topical use gives very low blood levels, the liver injury, taste loss and drug interactions (it inhibits CYP2D6) seen with oral terbinafine are not expected. There is no antidote needed.", "src": ["Lamisil AT (terbinafine) OTC Drug Facts label", "Petranyi G, Ryder NS, Stutz A. Science 1984", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "docosanol": {"origin": "Docosanol is a long, 22-carbon saturated fatty alcohol, also called behenyl alcohol, found naturally in some plant waxes and long used as a thickener in cosmetics. In the 1980s and 1990s researchers at a San Diego company then called Lidak Pharmaceuticals (later Avanir) found that it could block herpes simplex virus from entering cells in laboratory studies. After clinical trials showed a modest shortening of cold sore healing time, the FDA approved docosanol 10% cream in 2000 as Abreva. It was the first over-the-counter cold sore medicine approved by the FDA to shorten healing time.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Researchers find that docosanol inhibits herpes virus entry into cells in lab studies."], ["2000", "FDA approves docosanol 10% cream (Abreva) as an over-the-counter cold sore treatment."]], "cell": "Herpes simplex virus is an enveloped virus that infects cells by fusing its lipid envelope with the host cell membrane. Docosanol does not attack the virus directly or block viral DNA polymerase, as acyclovir does. Instead, it inserts into the host cell membrane and is partly metabolized into membrane lipids. This changes the membrane so the viral envelope cannot fuse with it easily. Viral entry is reduced, so fewer new cells become infected and the spread from cell to cell in the blister slows. Because it acts on healthy cells, it works best when applied at the first tingle, before many cells are infected.", "body": [["Lips and face", "Started at the first tingle, it shortens healing time of cold sores by a modest amount and eases tingling, pain and itching."], ["Skin cells", "Protects uninfected cells around the sore from new viral entry."], ["Skin at the site", "Mild application-site reactions such as dryness or redness can occur."]], "journey": "Docosanol cream is rubbed onto the cold sore area on the lips or face several times a day. It stays in the skin, where it works on the membranes of local cells. Almost none reaches the blood. It is not used inside the mouth or near the eyes.", "sidefx": "Because so little is absorbed, side effects are limited to the skin and include mild irritation, redness or rash where it is applied. Headache has been reported in trials. Allergic reactions are rare. There are no known systemic toxicities and no antidote is needed.", "fun": "The same molecule that is a cold sore drug is used in many lotions and lipsticks simply as a thickener, under the name behenyl alcohol.", "src": ["Abreva (docosanol) OTC Drug Facts label", "Sacks SL et al. J Am Acad Dermatol 2001", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "saline-nasal-spray": {"origin": "Rinsing the nose with salt water is very old. Jala neti, washing the nasal passages with salted water from a small pot, comes from the yoga and Ayurvedic traditions of India. In Western medicine, saline sprays and irrigations became common in the 1900s for dry noses, crusting, sinus problems and recovery after nasal surgery. Today they are sold as simple over-the-counter sprays and as rinse bottles and neti pots. After rare deaths from Naegleria fowleri, a brain-eating amoeba found in tap water, were linked to neti pot use in Louisiana in 2011, the FDA advised using only distilled, sterile or boiled-and-cooled water for nasal rinses.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Jala neti nasal washing with salt water is practiced in India."], ["1900s", "Saline irrigation becomes a standard care measure for nasal and sinus problems."], ["2011", "Fatal Naegleria fowleri infections in Louisiana are linked to neti pots filled with tap water."], ["2012", "FDA warns to use distilled, sterile or boiled-and-cooled water for nasal rinsing devices."]], "cell": "Saline spray has no drug receptor. It acts physically on the nasal lining. The nose is lined with ciliated cells covered by a thin mucus layer that traps dust, pollen and germs, while the cilia beat to sweep the mucus to the throat. When air is dry or mucus is thick, cilia beat poorly. Isotonic saline adds water to the surface layer, thins mucus, washes away allergens and crusts and helps the cilia beat more effectively (better mucociliary clearance). Slightly hypertonic solutions can also draw water out of swollen tissue, though they may sting more.", "body": [["Nose", "Moistens dry, irritated nasal passages and loosens crusts and thick mucus."], ["Sinuses", "Rinses help clear mucus and allergens, which can ease symptoms of chronic sinusitis and allergies."], ["Airway clearance", "Cilia sweep thinner mucus more easily toward the throat."], ["Infants", "Saline drops followed by gentle suction help babies who cannot blow their noses."]], "journey": "Saline spray acts on the surface where it lands and is not absorbed as a drug. The salt and water join the body's own fluids, and the spray is cleared by the cilia and swallowed like normal mucus. Its effect is immediate and short-lived, so it can be used as often as needed.", "sidefx": "Saline is about as safe as a nasal product can be. Mild stinging or sneezing can occur, especially with hypertonic solutions or on irritated tissue. The main real risk comes from rinse devices filled with untreated tap water, which can carry Naegleria fowleri or other organisms into the nose, where the amoeba can travel along the olfactory nerve to the brain. Using sterile, distilled or boiled-and-cooled water and cleaning devices prevents this. Ready-made sprays are sterile.", "src": ["Ocean and other saline nasal spray OTC labels", "FDA consumer update on nasal rinsing devices, 2012", "CDC reports on Naegleria fowleri and neti pots"]}, "cough-drops-menthol": {"origin": "Menthol is the cooling compound in mint plants, especially Japanese mint (Mentha arvensis) and peppermint (Mentha x piperita). Mint has been used as a remedy across Asia and Europe for centuries, and menthol crystals were first described in Europe in the 1700s. Medicated throat lozenges became popular in the 1800s, and menthol drops went on to become one of the best-selling cough remedies in the world. The reason menthol feels cold was not known until 2002, when the labs of David Julius and Ardem Patapoutian independently identified TRPM8, the cold and menthol receptor. Julius and Patapoutian shared the 2021 Nobel Prize for discovering receptors for temperature and touch.", "timeline": [["c. 1700s", "Menthol crystals from mint oil are described in Europe."], ["1800s", "Medicated throat lozenges become popular."], ["2002", "The Julius and Patapoutian labs identify TRPM8 as the cold and menthol receptor."], ["2021", "Julius and Patapoutian share the Nobel Prize for discoveries of temperature and touch receptors."]], "cell": "Menthol activates TRPM8, a cold-sensing cation channel on sensory nerve endings of the trigeminal and vagal nerves in the mouth, throat and airways. Opening TRPM8 lets sodium and calcium in, which fires the nerve and sends a cold signal to the brain even though temperature has not changed. Cold-sensing input is thought to calm the cough reflex arising from irritated airway nerves, and menthol also has a mild local anesthetic effect from weak sodium channel block. It may also reduce activity of TRPA1, an irritant receptor that drives cough. Separately, slowly dissolving a lozenge increases saliva and swallowing, which soothes the throat.", "body": [["Throat and mouth", "A cooling sensation and mild numbing ease throat irritation and soreness."], ["Cough reflex", "Cold-receptor input may raise the threshold for coughing from minor throat irritation."], ["Nose", "Menthol creates a sensation of easier airflow, even though it does not shrink nasal tissue or truly open the airway."], ["Saliva", "Slowly dissolving lozenges increase saliva, which keeps the throat moist."]], "journey": "The lozenge dissolves in the mouth over several minutes. Menthol acts on nerve endings in the mouth and throat, and some is swallowed and absorbed. The liver links it to glucuronic acid, and menthol glucuronide leaves in the urine. Its half-life is about an hour, and the cooling feeling fades soon after the lozenge is gone.", "sidefx": "Menthol drops are very safe at usual use. Overuse can irritate the mouth. Allergic reactions are rare. The main hazard is choking in young children, since a hard candy can block the airway. Very large swallowed amounts of concentrated menthol oil (not lozenges) can cause nausea, drowsiness and, rarely, seizures. Many lozenges are also mostly sugar, which can affect blood sugar and teeth with frequent use. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "The 2021 Nobel Prize for physiology or medicine was partly earned by figuring out why mint feels cold and chili peppers feel hot.", "src": ["Halls (menthol) OTC Drug Facts label", "McKemy DD, Neuhausser WM, Julius D. Nature 2002", "Peier AM et al. Cell 2002", "FDA OTC monograph for cough, cold and oral health care products"]}, "calamine": {"origin": "Calamine is one of the oldest skin remedies still on pharmacy shelves. The name originally referred to zinc ores, and zinc minerals were used on wounds and skin and eye irritations in ancient Egypt, Greece and Rome. Modern calamine is zinc oxide with a small amount of ferric (iron) oxide, which gives it its pink, skin-like color. Calamine lotion became a household remedy in the 1800s and 1900s for itchy, weeping rashes. In the US it is regulated under the FDA's over-the-counter skin protectant rules, and it is best known for drying the oozing blisters of poison ivy, oak and sumac.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Zinc minerals are used on skin and wounds in Egypt, Greece and Rome."], ["1800s", "Calamine lotion becomes a common skin remedy."], ["2003", "FDA's final OTC skin protectant monograph includes calamine and zinc oxide."]], "cell": "Calamine works physically rather than through a receptor. When the lotion is applied and the water evaporates, the cooling of evaporation calms itch nerves for a time. The fine zinc oxide and iron oxide particles left behind form a thin protective film over the skin. Zinc oxide is a mild astringent: it causes surface proteins in weeping skin to clump slightly, tightening the outer layer and reducing oozing of fluid from blisters. Zinc also has mild antiseptic properties. Drier skin with a protective coat is less irritated and itches less.", "body": [["Skin with weeping rashes", "Dries the oozing and weeping of poison ivy, oak and sumac blisters."], ["Itch nerves", "Evaporative cooling and a protective layer reduce the urge to scratch."], ["Skin surface", "The pink film shields irritated skin from rubbing and dirt."]], "journey": "Calamine lotion is shaken and spread on cleansed, dry skin. The water evaporates within minutes, leaving a dry film. The zinc oxide and iron oxide are not absorbed into the body in meaningful amounts. The film is washed off with soap and water.", "sidefx": "Calamine has very few side effects. The drying effect that helps weeping rashes can over-dry skin that is not oozing, causing tightness and mild irritation. Allergic reactions are rare. Some products such as Caladryl add pramoxine or other ingredients, which can cause their own reactions. It is not swallowed and is not used on large open wounds. There is no antidote needed.", "src": ["Calamine lotion OTC Drug Facts label", "FDA OTC skin protectant monograph (21 CFR 347)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "hydrocortisone-cream": {"origin": "Hydrocortisone is the body's own main glucocorticoid, cortisol. In the 1930s Edward Kendall at the Mayo Clinic and Tadeus Reichstein in Switzerland isolated a series of adrenal hormones, including compound F, now called hydrocortisone. In 1948 Philip Hench gave cortisone to a woman with severe rheumatoid arthritis with dramatic results, and in 1950 Kendall, Hench and Reichstein shared the Nobel Prize. In 1952 dermatologists Marion Sulzberger and Victor Witten showed that compound F rubbed on the skin cleared certain rashes, launching the era of topical steroids. Low-strength hydrocortisone cream was allowed over the counter in the US in 1979, and the 1% strength in the early 1990s. This entry covers the skin product, not the oral or IV drug used in hospitals.", "timeline": [["1930s", "Kendall and Reichstein isolate adrenal steroids, including compound F (hydrocortisone)."], ["1950", "Kendall, Hench and Reichstein share the Nobel Prize for adrenal cortex hormones."], ["1952", "Sulzberger and Witten show topical compound F treats skin disease."], ["1979", "Low-strength hydrocortisone cream becomes available over the counter in the US."], ["1990s", "Hydrocortisone 1% becomes available over the counter in the US."]], "cell": "Hydrocortisone diffuses through skin cell membranes and binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor. The receptor complex moves into the nucleus, where it suppresses inflammatory transcription factors such as NF-kappa B and AP-1 and turns on anti-inflammatory genes, including annexin A1, which inhibits phospholipase A2. Less arachidonic acid is released, so fewer prostaglandins and leukotrienes are made. Cytokine production by T cells and other immune cells drops, mast cells and immune cells in the skin calm down, and small vessels constrict, reducing redness and swelling. Because hydrocortisone is low potency, these effects are mild and best suited to minor inflammation.", "body": [["Skin at the site", "Less itching, redness and swelling from bug bites, poison ivy, mild eczema and other minor rashes."], ["Small skin vessels", "Mild narrowing of small skin vessels reduces redness and warmth."], ["Skin structure with long use", "Long or heavy use can thin the skin, cause stretch marks and lead to acne-like bumps or redness around the mouth."], ["Whole body", "Very little reaches the blood through intact skin, so body-wide steroid effects are not expected at OTC use."]], "journey": "Rubbed on as a thin layer, hydrocortisone passes into the outer skin layers, where it acts. Only a small share crosses intact skin into the blood. Absorption rises with thin skin (face, eyelids, groin), broken or inflamed skin, large areas, occlusion under dressings or diapers and in infants with their larger skin surface for their size. Any absorbed hydrocortisone is handled like natural cortisol, metabolized in the liver and excreted in the urine.", "sidefx": "Mild burning or stinging can occur on raw skin. With long or heavy use, the steroid slows the growth of skin cells and collagen, which leads to skin thinning, stretch marks, easy bruising and visible vessels. It can also cause acne-like eruptions and perioral dermatitis. By calming local immunity, it can make fungal or bacterial skin infections worse if used on them. Rarely, allergic contact reactions occur to the steroid or the cream base. Systemic effects such as adrenal suppression are very unlikely with brief OTC use. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Cortizone-10 (hydrocortisone 1%) OTC Drug Facts label", "Sulzberger MB, Witten VH. J Invest Dermatol 1952", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "chlorambucil": {"origin": "Chlorambucil grew out of the first cancer chemotherapy, nitrogen mustard. Mustard compounds were highly reactive and had to be injected. In the early 1950s chemists at the Chester Beatty Research Institute in London, including W. C. J. Ross and colleagues working under Alexander Haddow, attached the mustard group to an aromatic ring. This slowed its reactivity enough to allow an oral, more gently acting drug. Chlorambucil was reported in 1953 and developed by Burroughs Wellcome as Leukeran, approved by the FDA in 1957. For decades it was a mainstay for chronic lymphocytic leukemia, especially in older patients, before newer targeted drugs took over much of that role.", "timeline": [["1940s", "Nitrogen mustard becomes the first cancer chemotherapy."], ["1953", "Chester Beatty Research Institute chemists report chlorambucil."], ["1957", "FDA approves chlorambucil (Leukeran)."]], "cell": "Chlorambucil is an aromatic nitrogen mustard with two chloroethyl arms. Each arm can close into a reactive aziridinium ion, which attacks electron-rich sites on DNA, mainly the N7 position of guanine. Because it has two arms, one molecule can bind two guanines, forming interstrand and intrastrand crosslinks. Crosslinks prevent DNA strands from separating for replication and transcription and trigger DNA damage signaling through p53, leading to apoptosis. It is cell-cycle nonspecific but most harmful to dividing cells. The aromatic ring pulls electrons away from the nitrogen, which slows aziridinium formation and makes it gentler and orally usable. Its liver metabolite, phenylacetic acid mustard, is also active.", "body": [["Lymphocytes", "Kills malignant lymphocytes in CLL and lymphomas, lowering high white counts and shrinking lymph nodes and spleen over weeks."], ["Bone marrow", "Suppresses blood cell production, lowering white cells, platelets and red cells."], ["Brain", "Can lower the seizure threshold, especially in children and in people with a seizure history."], ["Lungs and liver", "Rarely causes lung fibrosis and liver injury."], ["Skin", "Rash is common, and rare severe reactions such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome occur."], ["Reproductive system", "Can cause infertility and is harmful to a developing fetus."]], "journey": "Chlorambucil is swallowed and absorbed quickly, peaking in about an hour. It is almost fully bound to plasma proteins, mainly albumin. The liver converts it to phenylacetic acid mustard, which is also an active alkylator. Both are broken down further by spontaneous chemical reaction and leave the body as metabolites in the urine. Its half-life is short, about one and a half hours, but the DNA damage lasts much longer, so blood counts fall over one to three weeks.", "sidefx": "Myelosuppression happens because marrow stem and progenitor cells divide quickly and are hit by DNA crosslinking. Infections and bleeding follow low white cells and platelets. Seizures relate to central nervous system effects of the alkylator. Lung fibrosis and liver injury reflect direct tissue damage. Because alkylating agents damage DNA permanently, they can cause secondary leukemias and are toxic to sperm and eggs, causing infertility. Severe skin reactions are thought to be immune mediated. There is no antidote; low counts are supported with transfusions and growth factors.", "src": ["Leukeran (chlorambucil) FDA label", "Everett JL, Roberts JJ, Ross WCJ. J Chem Soc 1953", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "lomustine": {"origin": "Lomustine is a nitrosourea, a class developed in the 1960s at Southern Research Institute in Birmingham, Alabama, in a program with the US National Cancer Institute. Chemists including John Montgomery and Thomas Johnston were searching for alkylating drugs that could cross the blood-brain barrier. Nitrosoureas are very fat-soluble, so they reach the brain well. Carmustine (BCNU) came first, and lomustine (CCNU), an oral relative, followed. The FDA approved lomustine in 1976 as CeeNU. It is now sold as Gleostine and is used for brain tumors, often in the PCV regimen with procarbazine and vincristine, and for Hodgkin lymphoma that has come back.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Southern Research Institute and the NCI develop nitrosourea anticancer drugs."], ["1976", "FDA approves lomustine (CeeNU)."], ["2010s", "Lomustine is relaunched in the US under the brand name Gleostine."]], "cell": "Lomustine breaks down without enzymes in the body into two reactive pieces. One is a chloroethyl carbonium (diazonium) ion that alkylates DNA, first at the O6 position of guanine. That chloroethyl group then reacts with the opposite strand to form an interstrand crosslink, which blocks DNA replication and transcription and triggers cell death. The second piece is an isocyanate that carbamoylates lysine residues on proteins, which can disable DNA repair enzymes. Tumor cells with high levels of the repair enzyme MGMT, which removes the O6 alkyl group before a crosslink forms, are more resistant. Its high lipid solubility lets it and its active metabolites reach brain tumors. It is cell-cycle nonspecific.", "body": [["Brain tumors", "Crosses the blood-brain barrier and kills tumor cells in gliomas and other brain tumors."], ["Lymph nodes", "Kills malignant cells in relapsed Hodgkin lymphoma."], ["Bone marrow", "Causes delayed, cumulative suppression of blood counts, with platelets lowest at about four weeks and white cells at about six weeks."], ["Lungs", "Can cause lung infiltrates and fibrosis, sometimes years later and more with higher cumulative doses."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting often start within hours of the dose."], ["Liver and kidneys", "Can raise liver tests and damage the kidneys with long-term use."]], "journey": "Lomustine is taken by mouth as a single dose once every six weeks. It is absorbed rapidly and so fat-soluble that it reaches the brain and cerebrospinal fluid readily. The parent drug disappears from the blood very quickly. The liver converts it to active hydroxylated metabolites, which have half-lives of roughly 16 to 48 hours. Metabolites leave mainly in the urine. The long gap between doses gives the marrow time to recover.", "sidefx": "Marrow suppression is delayed because lomustine damages early stem and progenitor cells, which take weeks to fail, and it builds up with each cycle. That is why the label stresses giving only one dose per cycle; fatal overdoses have happened when patients took it daily by mistake. Lung toxicity reflects alkylation damage in lung tissue, and secondary leukemias reflect permanent DNA damage. Nausea and vomiting come from effects on the brainstem vomiting center. It is harmful to a developing fetus. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "src": ["Gleostine (lomustine) FDA label", "Johnston TP, McCaleb GS, Montgomery JA. J Med Chem 1963", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "temozolomide": {"origin": "Temozolomide was designed in the early 1980s by Malcolm Stevens and his team at Aston University in Birmingham, England, working with the drug company May and Baker. Stevens set out to make an oral prodrug of the reactive compound MTIC, the active form of the older drug dacarbazine, without needing liver activation. Temozolomide turned out to break down on its own at body pH, and it crossed into the brain. It was later developed by Schering-Plough as Temodar and approved by the FDA in 1999 for anaplastic astrocytoma. In 2005 a trial led by Roger Stupp showed that adding temozolomide to radiation improved survival in newly diagnosed glioblastoma, and it became the standard of care.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Malcolm Stevens and colleagues at Aston University synthesize temozolomide."], ["1999", "FDA approves temozolomide (Temodar) for refractory anaplastic astrocytoma."], ["2005", "The Stupp trial shows improved survival with temozolomide plus radiation in glioblastoma."], ["2005", "FDA approves temozolomide for newly diagnosed glioblastoma."]], "cell": "Temozolomide is a prodrug that changes spontaneously, without enzymes, at the slightly alkaline pH of the body into MTIC. MTIC then breaks down into a methyldiazonium ion that methylates DNA, mainly at N7 of guanine, N3 of adenine and O6 of guanine. O6-methylguanine is the key lesion. During replication it pairs with thymine instead of cytosine, and the mismatch repair system repeatedly tries and fails to fix the mismatch, causing DNA strand breaks and apoptosis. The repair enzyme MGMT removes the methyl group from O6-guanine and protects tumor cells. When the MGMT gene promoter is methylated (silenced), tumors make little MGMT and respond better. It is cell-cycle nonspecific.", "body": [["Brain tumors", "Methylates DNA in glioma cells, slowing tumor growth and improving survival in glioblastoma with radiation."], ["Bone marrow", "Lowers platelets and neutrophils, with lowest counts about three to four weeks into a cycle."], ["Immune system", "Lowers lymphocytes, especially CD4 T cells, which raises the risk of Pneumocystis pneumonia during combined radiation therapy."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and constipation are common."], ["Liver", "Can raise liver enzymes and rarely cause severe liver injury."]], "journey": "Temozolomide is swallowed as capsules and absorbed almost completely, peaking in about an hour; an IV form gives similar exposure. It is small and fat-soluble enough to reach the brain and cerebrospinal fluid. It does not depend on liver enzymes: it breaks down chemically at body pH into MTIC and then into its final products. Its half-life is short, about 1.8 hours. Metabolites leave mainly in the urine.", "sidefx": "Marrow suppression and low lymphocyte counts come from methylation damage in rapidly dividing blood cells. Long daily use with radiation lowers CD4 cells enough to allow Pneumocystis pneumonia, so preventive antibiotics are used. Nausea comes from effects on vomiting pathways. Permanent DNA damage explains the small risk of later myelodysplastic syndrome or leukemia. Liver injury can be severe and occur late. It is harmful to a developing fetus. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "src": ["Temodar (temozolomide) FDA label", "Stupp R et al. N Engl J Med 2005", "Hegi ME et al. N Engl J Med 2005 (MGMT)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "procarbazine": {"origin": "Procarbazine was found while Hoffmann-La Roche chemists were studying methylhydrazine compounds in the early 1960s, a family then being explored as monoamine oxidase inhibitors for depression. Screening showed that one compound, procarbazine, had strong anticancer activity. At the US National Cancer Institute, Vincent DeVita and colleagues included it in MOPP, a four-drug combination of mechlorethamine, vincristine (Oncovin), procarbazine and prednisone, which in the mid-1960s made advanced Hodgkin lymphoma curable for many patients for the first time. The FDA approved procarbazine as Matulane in 1969. It is still used in some Hodgkin regimens and in the PCV regimen for certain brain tumors.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Hoffmann-La Roche identifies the anticancer activity of procarbazine."], ["1960s", "DeVita and colleagues at the NCI develop the MOPP regimen for Hodgkin lymphoma."], ["1969", "FDA approves procarbazine (Matulane)."]], "cell": "Procarbazine is a prodrug. In the liver, CYP450 enzymes and monoamine oxidase convert it to azo-procarbazine and then to methylazoxy compounds. These form methyldiazonium ions that methylate DNA, including at O6 of guanine, which leads to DNA strand breaks. Its breakdown also produces hydrogen peroxide and free radicals that damage DNA. Together these effects inhibit DNA, RNA and protein synthesis. It is cell-cycle nonspecific. Procarbazine itself is a weak MAO inhibitor, which explains its interactions with tyramine-rich foods and sympathomimetic drugs. It also inhibits aldehyde dehydrogenase, causing a disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol.", "body": [["Lymph nodes", "Kills malignant cells in Hodgkin lymphoma as part of combination therapy."], ["Brain", "Crosses the blood-brain barrier, useful in some brain tumors, but can cause drowsiness, confusion, nerve tingling and, rarely, seizures."], ["Bone marrow", "Lowers white cells, platelets and red cells, with lowest counts about four weeks into therapy."], ["Blood pressure", "Weak MAO inhibition can cause dangerous blood pressure spikes with tyramine-rich foods or sympathomimetic drugs."], ["Reproductive system", "Commonly causes lasting azoospermia and infertility."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and loss of appetite are common, especially early."]], "journey": "Procarbazine is swallowed and absorbed quickly and nearly completely, peaking in about an hour. It crosses into the brain and cerebrospinal fluid. The parent drug is gone from the blood within minutes because the liver rapidly converts it to active metabolites, which last longer. Most of the drug leaves as metabolites in the urine, much of it within a day.", "sidefx": "Marrow suppression and secondary leukemias reflect DNA methylation and free radical damage in dividing and stem cells. Weak MAO inhibition means tyramine from aged cheeses and cured meats, or drugs such as pseudoephedrine, can trigger excess norepinephrine release and a hypertensive crisis, while combining it with serotonergic drugs raises serotonin syndrome risk. Inhibiting aldehyde dehydrogenase causes flushing, nausea and headache with alcohol. Central nervous system effects include drowsiness, confusion and neuropathy. It is toxic to sperm and to a developing fetus. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "src": ["Matulane (procarbazine) FDA label", "DeVita VT et al. Ann Intern Med 1970 (MOPP)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "mechlorethamine": {"origin": "Mechlorethamine, or nitrogen mustard, was the first modern cancer chemotherapy, and it came out of chemical warfare research. Sulfur mustard gas in World War I was known to destroy bone marrow and lymph tissue. In 1942 Louis Goodman and Alfred Gilman at Yale, with Thomas Dougherty, tested the related nitrogen mustard in mice with lymphoma and then in a man known as J.D. with advanced lymphoma, whose tumors shrank dramatically for a time. The work was secret until after the war and was published in 1946. A 1943 German air raid on Bari, Italy, which released mustard gas from a US ship, also showed low white counts in exposed sailors. The FDA approved mechlorethamine (Mustargen) in 1949, the first approved chemotherapy. The IV form has been discontinued in the US, and the drug is now used as Valchlor gel, approved in 2013 for early cutaneous T-cell lymphoma.", "timeline": [["1917", "Sulfur mustard gas is used in World War I and found to damage marrow and lymph tissue."], ["1942", "Goodman, Gilman and Dougherty at Yale treat a patient with lymphoma using nitrogen mustard."], ["1943", "The Bari air raid exposes sailors to mustard gas, revealing marrow and lymphoid damage."], ["1949", "FDA approves mechlorethamine (Mustargen), the first chemotherapy drug."], ["2013", "FDA approves mechlorethamine gel (Valchlor) for mycosis fungoides."]], "cell": "Mechlorethamine is a bifunctional alkylating agent with two chloroethyl arms on a nitrogen. In water, the nitrogen pushes out a chloride and forms a strained three-membered aziridinium (ethylenimmonium) ring that is highly reactive. This ring attacks the N7 position of guanine in DNA. The second arm then forms another aziridinium ring and binds a second guanine, creating interstrand or intrastrand crosslinks. Crosslinked DNA cannot be separated for replication or transcription, which triggers DNA damage signals and apoptosis. It is cell-cycle nonspecific but most toxic to rapidly dividing cells. On the skin it kills malignant T cells in the epidermis and dermis of mycosis fungoides.", "body": [["Skin lymphoma", "The gel kills malignant T cells in patches and plaques of early mycosis fungoides, with improvement over weeks to months."], ["Skin", "Commonly causes dermatitis with redness, itching and irritation, and can cause darkening, blisters or ulcers."], ["Skin cancer risk", "Long-term topical use is linked to nonmelanoma skin cancers in treated areas."], ["Bone marrow (historic IV)", "IV use caused severe suppression of blood counts."], ["Gut (historic IV)", "IV use caused severe nausea and vomiting."], ["Veins and tissue (historic IV)", "Leaking from the IV caused severe tissue damage, which was treated with sodium thiosulfate."]], "journey": "Mechlorethamine is very unstable in water and reacts with tissues or breaks down within seconds to minutes. The gel is applied as a thin film to affected skin, where it acts locally, and very little reaches the blood. Showering is delayed for hours to let it work, and the gel is flammable until dry. When it was given IV, it disappeared from the blood almost immediately because it reacted with tissues and water, and inactive breakdown products left in the urine.", "sidefx": "Skin side effects come from the drug alkylating skin cells and triggering inflammation. Allergic contact dermatitis is also common because the reactive molecule binds skin proteins and becomes an allergen. Permanent DNA damage in skin cells explains the risk of nonmelanoma skin cancers, and caregivers must avoid skin contact. With the historic IV form, marrow suppression, severe vomiting and tissue necrosis from leakage all followed from its reactivity in dividing cells and local tissue. Sodium thiosulfate neutralizes mechlorethamine and was used for extravasation. It is harmful to a developing fetus.", "fun": "Louis Goodman and Alfred Gilman, who ran the first nitrogen mustard trial, are the same authors behind the textbook Goodman and Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics.", "src": ["Valchlor (mechlorethamine) FDA label", "Goodman LS et al. JAMA 1946", "Gilman A. Am J Surg 1963 (history of nitrogen mustard)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "capecitabine": {"origin": "Capecitabine is an oral prodrug of fluorouracil (5-FU). 5-FU was designed in 1957 by Charles Heidelberger at the University of Wisconsin after the discovery that tumor cells take up the base uracil faster than normal cells. 5-FU worked but had to be given IV, because absorption by mouth was unreliable and toxic to the gut. In the 1990s scientists at the Nippon Roche Research Center in Kamakura, Japan, designed capecitabine to pass through the gut intact and be converted to 5-FU in three enzyme steps, with the last step done by thymidine phosphorylase, an enzyme often higher in tumor tissue. The FDA approved it as Xeloda in 1998 for breast cancer and later for colorectal and other cancers.", "timeline": [["1957", "Charles Heidelberger and colleagues report fluorouracil."], ["1990s", "Nippon Roche scientists in Japan design capecitabine as an oral 5-FU prodrug."], ["1998", "FDA approves capecitabine (Xeloda) for metastatic breast cancer."], ["2001", "Capecitabine is approved for metastatic colorectal cancer."]], "cell": "Capecitabine is converted to 5-FU in three steps: carboxylesterase in the liver makes 5'-deoxy-5-fluorocytidine, cytidine deaminase makes 5'-deoxy-5-fluorouridine, and thymidine phosphorylase, often higher in tumors, releases 5-FU. Inside cells 5-FU becomes FdUMP, which forms a stable complex with thymidylate synthase and the folate cofactor 5,10-methylene tetrahydrofolate. This blocks conversion of dUMP to dTMP, so cells run out of thymidine and cannot copy DNA (thymineless death), making it S-phase specific. Another metabolite, FUTP, is built into RNA and disrupts RNA processing, and FdUTP can enter DNA. The enzyme DPD breaks down most 5-FU, so people with DPD deficiency build up toxic levels.", "body": [["Tumors", "Converted to 5-FU partly within tumor tissue, slowing growth of colorectal, breast, gastric, esophageal and pancreatic cancers."], ["Hands and feet", "Hand-foot syndrome causes redness, swelling, pain and peeling of palms and soles."], ["Gut", "Damage to rapidly dividing lining cells causes diarrhea, which can be severe, and mouth sores."], ["Bone marrow", "Lowers white cells and platelets, especially with other chemotherapy."], ["Heart", "Can cause coronary vasospasm with chest pain and, rarely, heart attack or arrhythmia."], ["Liver", "Raises bilirubin in some patients, which is monitored during treatment."]], "journey": "Capecitabine is swallowed after a meal and absorbed quickly and well, peaking in about an hour and a half. It is converted step by step in the liver and tissues to 5-FU. Both capecitabine and 5-FU have short half-lives, under an hour. DPD in the liver breaks 5-FU down into inactive products, and about 96 percent of the dose leaves in the urine as metabolites. Reduced kidney function raises exposure, so the dose is lowered in moderate kidney impairment.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea, mouth sores and marrow suppression come from 5-FU blocking DNA synthesis in fast-dividing cells of the gut lining and marrow. Hand-foot syndrome is thought to relate to drug and metabolite buildup in the skin of the palms and soles. Coronary vasospasm causes chest pain. People with DPD deficiency cannot clear 5-FU and can have severe or fatal toxicity, so testing may be considered before starting. Capecitabine raises warfarin effect, causing bleeding. Uridine triacetate is the antidote for fluorouracil or capecitabine overdose or early severe toxicity.", "src": ["Xeloda (capecitabine) FDA label", "Heidelberger C et al. Nature 1957", "Vistogard (uridine triacetate) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "decitabine-cedazuridine": {"origin": "This tablet pairs two drugs. Decitabine (5-aza-2'-deoxycytidine) was first synthesized in 1964 by Jiri Pliml and Frantisek Sorm in Czechoslovakia. Decades later researchers learned that at low exposures it reverses abnormal DNA methylation that silences genes in cancer cells, and IV decitabine (Dacogen) was approved by the FDA in 2006 for myelodysplastic syndromes. Oral decitabine does not work on its own because the enzyme cytidine deaminase in the gut and liver destroys it. Astex Pharmaceuticals developed cedazuridine, a cytidine deaminase inhibitor, to protect decitabine. The FDA approved the combination as Inqovi in 2020 for MDS and CMML, the first oral hypomethylating therapy for these diseases, giving exposure similar to the IV drug.", "timeline": [["1964", "Pliml and Sorm synthesize decitabine in Czechoslovakia."], ["1980s", "Decitabine is found to inhibit DNA methylation and reactivate silenced genes."], ["2006", "FDA approves IV decitabine (Dacogen) for myelodysplastic syndromes."], ["2020", "FDA approves oral decitabine with cedazuridine (Inqovi) for MDS and CMML."]], "cell": "Decitabine is a cytidine analog with a nitrogen in place of carbon at position 5 of the ring. Cells take it up and phosphorylate it to the triphosphate, which is built into newly made DNA in S phase. When DNA methyltransferase 1 (DNMT1) tries to copy methyl marks onto the new strand, it forms a covalent bond with the azacytosine and becomes trapped, and the trapped enzyme is then degraded. As cells divide without DNMT1, methyl marks are lost across the genome (hypomethylation), which can turn back on silenced tumor suppressor and differentiation genes, so abnormal marrow cells mature or die. Trapped enzyme-DNA adducts also cause direct damage at higher exposure. Cedazuridine has no anticancer effect itself; it blocks cytidine deaminase so oral decitabine survives first pass.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Allows more normal blood cell production in MDS and CMML, reducing transfusion needs in some patients, though counts often drop first."], ["Blood counts", "Neutropenia, thrombocytopenia and anemia are common, especially in the first cycles."], ["Immune defense", "Low neutrophils raise the risk of fever, pneumonia and sepsis."], ["Gut", "Nausea, constipation, diarrhea and mouth sores are common."], ["Liver", "Can raise liver enzymes, which are checked during treatment."]], "journey": "The tablet is swallowed on an empty stomach once daily for five days of each 28-day cycle. Cedazuridine blocks cytidine deaminase in the gut wall and liver, so decitabine passes the first-pass barrier and reaches exposures close to the IV drug. Decitabine peaks in about an hour and has a half-life of about one and a half hours, cleared mainly by deamination. Cedazuridine has a longer half-life, about seven hours, and is cleared mostly by the kidneys, partly after conversion to an epimer. Food lowers absorption.", "sidefx": "Marrow suppression is the main toxicity because decitabine is built into the DNA of dividing marrow cells, and early cycles often lower counts before they improve. Low neutrophils lead to febrile neutropenia, pneumonia and sepsis, which can be fatal, and low platelets lead to bleeding. Gut effects come from damage to the dividing gut lining. It is harmful to a developing fetus because it disrupts DNA methylation needed for development. There is no antidote; counts are supported with transfusions, antibiotics and treatment delays.", "fun": "Decitabine is an epigenetic drug: it changes which genes are switched on by removing chemical tags on DNA rather than changing the DNA sequence itself.", "src": ["Inqovi (decitabine and cedazuridine) FDA label", "Dacogen (decitabine) FDA label", "Garcia-Manero G et al. Blood 2020 (oral decitabine-cedazuridine phase 2)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "thioguanine": {"origin": "Thioguanine came out of one of the most famous partnerships in drug discovery. At Burroughs Wellcome in New York, George Hitchings and Gertrude Elion reasoned that if cancer cells and microbes needed purines to copy their DNA, a slightly altered purine might jam the machinery. Instead of screening at random, they built look-alike molecules on purpose. In the early 1950s this led to mercaptopurine and its close cousin thioguanine, which swaps an oxygen on guanine for a sulfur atom. Thioguanine was marketed as Tabloid tablets for acute myeloid leukemia. The same rational approach later gave azathioprine, allopurinol and acyclovir, and in 1988 Hitchings and Elion shared the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine with James Black.", "timeline": [["1940s", "Hitchings and Elion begin designing purine look-alikes at Burroughs Wellcome."], ["1950s", "Thioguanine and mercaptopurine are made and tested in leukemia."], ["1966", "Thioguanine is approved in the US as Tabloid tablets."], ["1988", "Hitchings and Elion share the Nobel Prize for rational drug design."]], "cell": "Thioguanine is a prodrug that must be activated inside the cell. The salvage enzyme hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase (HGPRT) attaches a ribose phosphate, making 6-thioguanosine monophosphate. Kinases add more phosphates to form thioguanine nucleotides. These fake nucleotides do two things. They give false feedback that shuts down de novo purine synthesis and block interconversion between purine nucleotides, starving the cell of normal building blocks. They are also built into DNA and RNA in place of guanine. Thioguanine in DNA is mismatched during replication, which triggers mismatch repair, strand breaks and apoptosis, so the drug acts mainly on cells in S phase. Thiopurine S-methyltransferase (TPMT) methylates the drug into an inactive form, and NUDT15 breaks down the active triphosphate. Unlike mercaptopurine, thioguanine is not a major substrate of xanthine oxidase.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Rapidly dividing blood-forming cells take up the fake purine, so white cells, platelets and red cells fall over one to four weeks."], ["Leukemia cells", "Myeloid leukemia blasts copying their DNA incorporate thioguanine and die, which is why it is used in induction and consolidation for AML."], ["Liver", "Long-term use injures the small sinusoidal vessels of the liver, causing veno-occlusive disease, nodular regenerative hyperplasia and portal hypertension."], ["Gut and mouth", "Lining cells of the gut turn over quickly, so nausea, vomiting and mouth sores occur."], ["Kidneys and uric acid", "Killing many leukemia cells releases purines that are turned into uric acid, which can raise blood uric acid levels."]], "journey": "Thioguanine is swallowed as a tablet, and absorption is incomplete and varies a lot between people. Peak levels arrive in about eight hours. The parent drug leaves the blood quickly, with a half-life of roughly 80 minutes, but this is misleading: once HGPRT turns it into thioguanine nucleotides inside cells, those active forms stay much longer. The drug is inactivated by TPMT methylation and by guanase deamination, and its metabolites leave mainly in the urine.", "sidefx": "Marrow suppression is the expected result of a drug that kills dividing cells. People who inherit low or absent TPMT or NUDT15 activity cannot clear the active nucleotides, so they build up and can cause severe, even fatal, marrow failure on standard therapy; genotype or enzyme testing guides use. Liver injury comes from damage to sinusoidal endothelial cells, which is why the label advises against long-term continuous use. Nausea and mouth sores come from injury to fast-dividing lining cells. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Gertrude Elion never earned a formal PhD, yet she received a Nobel Prize and later an honorary doctorate from many universities.", "src": ["Tabloid (thioguanine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Elion GB. Nobel Lecture, 1988"]}, "trifluridine-tipiracil": {"origin": "This tablet combines two old ideas. Trifluridine, a fluorinated thymidine look-alike, was first made in the 1960s by Charles Heidelberger, the same chemist who invented fluorouracil. It was too quickly broken down in the body to work well as an oral cancer drug, so it found a use as antiviral eye drops for herpes keratitis. Decades later, the Japanese company Taiho Pharmaceutical paired it with tipiracil, a drug that blocks the enzyme that destroys trifluridine. The combination, called TAS-102 during development, kept trifluridine levels high enough to attack tumors. After the RECOURSE trial showed longer survival in heavily treated colorectal cancer, the FDA approved it as Lonsurf in 2015.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Charles Heidelberger synthesizes trifluridine."], ["1980", "Trifluridine eye drops are approved in the US for herpes keratitis."], ["2015", "FDA approves trifluridine-tipiracil for refractory metastatic colorectal cancer."], ["2019", "Approval expands to previously treated metastatic gastric cancer."], ["2023", "FDA approves use together with bevacizumab in colorectal cancer."]], "cell": "Trifluridine is a thymidine analog. Inside the cell, thymidine kinase adds a phosphate, and further kinases make trifluridine triphosphate. DNA polymerase mistakes this for normal thymidine triphosphate and builds it into new DNA. DNA containing trifluridine is unstable and malfunctions, causing DNA damage and cell death in cells that are copying DNA (S phase). Its monophosphate also inhibits thymidylate synthase, but this is a smaller effect with the oral schedule. The second part, tipiracil, has no direct anticancer action. It inhibits thymidine phosphorylase, the enzyme in the gut and liver that rapidly splits trifluridine into an inactive base. By blocking this first-pass breakdown, tipiracil raises trifluridine exposure many times over.", "body": [["Tumor cells", "Colorectal and gastric cancer cells that divide build trifluridine into their DNA and die, including tumors that no longer respond to fluorouracil."], ["Bone marrow", "Blood-forming cells are also dividing, so neutrophil counts drop, with a low point in the second half of each cycle; anemia and low platelets also occur."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea and loss of appetite come from injury to the dividing cells of the gut lining."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue is common and partly reflects anemia and the general burden of chemotherapy."]], "journey": "Both drugs are taken by mouth after meals, which reduces peaks of trifluridine. Peak levels arrive in about two hours. Without tipiracil most trifluridine would be destroyed by thymidine phosphorylase on its first pass through the gut and liver. Trifluridine is not handled by CYP enzymes; it is broken down to an inactive metabolite, and tipiracil is cleared largely by the kidneys. Both have short half-lives of about two hours, so dosing is twice daily within a cycle.", "sidefx": "The main danger is neutropenia, because marrow precursors take up trifluridine just as tumor cells do. Low neutrophils open the door to febrile neutropenia and serious infection, which is why blood counts are checked before each cycle and mid-cycle. GI side effects reflect damage to the rapidly renewing gut lining. Because tipiracil is cleared by the kidneys, kidney impairment raises exposure. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Trifluridine eye drops for herpes keratitis were on pharmacy shelves for about 35 years before the same molecule was approved as a cancer pill.", "src": ["Lonsurf (trifluridine and tipiracil) FDA label", "Mayer RJ et al. RECOURSE trial, N Engl J Med 2015", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "omacetaxine": {"origin": "Omacetaxine is the semisynthetic form of homoharringtonine, an alkaloid from the Cephalotaxus trees, known as plum yews, native to East Asia. In the 1970s researchers in China and at the US National Cancer Institute found that extracts of Cephalotaxus bark had anti-leukemia activity, and Chinese hematologists used homoharringtonine for leukemia for years. Harvesting enough bark was hard, so a semisynthetic process was developed that starts from cephalotaxine extracted from the leaves. The drug found a niche when imatinib and later kinase inhibitors left some patients with resistant chronic myeloid leukemia, including those with the T315I mutation. Because omacetaxine does not target the kinase at all, the FDA granted it accelerated approval as Synribo in 2012.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Cephalotaxus alkaloids including homoharringtonine are found to have anti-leukemia activity."], ["1980s", "Homoharringtonine is used for leukemia in China."], ["2012", "FDA grants accelerated approval to omacetaxine for CML after two or more kinase inhibitors."]], "cell": "Omacetaxine targets the ribosome rather than any kinase. It binds the A-site cleft of the large 60S subunit of the 80S human ribosome, the spot where incoming aminoacyl-tRNAs must dock. By blocking correct positioning of the aminoacyl-tRNA, it halts the first steps of elongation, so new proteins cannot be made. All proteins are affected, but those with very short lifetimes vanish first. These include the survival protein Mcl-1, MYC and the BCR-ABL1 protein itself. Losing Mcl-1 removes a brake on apoptosis, so leukemia cells die through the mitochondrial pathway. Because the drug lowers BCR-ABL1 levels rather than binding its ATP pocket, kinase domain mutations such as T315I do not cause resistance.", "body": [["Leukemia cells", "CML cells depend on short-lived survival proteins and on BCR-ABL1, so cutting off protein production drives them into apoptosis."], ["Bone marrow", "Normal blood-forming cells also need constant protein synthesis, so severe drops in platelets, neutrophils and red cells are common."], ["Blood clotting", "Very low platelets can lead to bleeding, including fatal brain and GI hemorrhage."], ["Metabolism", "Blood sugar can rise sharply, sometimes to a hyperosmolar state, especially in people with diabetes."], ["Skin", "Injection site redness and pain occur because it is given under the skin."]], "journey": "Omacetaxine is injected under the skin and absorbed quickly, peaking in about half an hour. It spreads into tissues and is broken down mainly by plasma esterases, which hydrolyze it to 4'-desmethyl homoharringtonine; liver CYP enzymes play only a small role. A minor share leaves unchanged in urine. Its half-life is about six hours, but its effect on blood counts lasts through each cycle of treatment.", "sidefx": "Because the drug stops protein synthesis in any cell that needs to make new proteins quickly, the marrow is hit hardest, which explains the severe thrombocytopenia, neutropenia and anemia. Low platelets plus fragile vessels explain bleeding, and other drugs that impair clotting add to the risk. Hyperglycemia is a known effect listed in the label, and glucose is followed. There is no antidote; care is supportive with transfusion and treatment of infection.", "fun": "Plum yew trees are slow growing, which is one reason the drug is made semisynthetically from leaf extracts instead of harvested bark.", "src": ["Synribo (omacetaxine mepesuccinate) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "tretinoin": {"origin": "Tretinoin is all-trans retinoic acid, the active acid form of vitamin A. It first became known as a skin drug: topical tretinoin was developed for acne in the late 1960s and approved in the US in 1971. Its role in cancer came from China. In the 1980s hematologist Wang Zhen-yi and colleagues in Shanghai gave oral all-trans retinoic acid to patients with acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL), a fast and bleeding-prone leukemia. Instead of killing the cancer cells, the drug made them grow up into mature white cells. Their 1988 report in Blood showed high remission rates, and it became the first widely used differentiation therapy. The FDA approved oral tretinoin (Vesanoid) for APL in 1995. The brand was later discontinued, and generic capsules remain.", "timeline": [["1971", "Topical tretinoin is approved in the US for acne."], ["1988", "Shanghai researchers report remissions in APL with all-trans retinoic acid."], ["1995", "FDA approves oral tretinoin for APL."], ["2000s", "Tretinoin with arsenic trioxide becomes a chemotherapy-free cure for many with APL."]], "cell": "APL is driven by the t(15;17) translocation, which fuses the PML gene to the retinoic acid receptor alpha gene (RARA). The PML-RARalpha fusion protein binds DNA at retinoic acid response elements and recruits corepressors and histone deacetylases far more tightly than normal RARalpha. This locks genes needed for maturation in the off position, so cells stall at the promyelocyte stage. At therapeutic levels, tretinoin binds the RARalpha ligand-binding domain of the fusion protein. This changes its shape, releases the corepressor complex, recruits coactivators and also triggers breakdown of PML-RARalpha by the proteasome. Silenced genes switch back on and the promyelocytes finish maturing into granulocytes, which then die off naturally.", "body": [["Leukemia cells", "APL blasts mature into neutrophil-like cells instead of being killed outright, and the leukemia clone fades over weeks."], ["Blood clotting", "APL cells release procoagulant and fibrinolytic factors; as they mature, the bleeding disorder improves within days."], ["White cell count", "Maturing cells pour into the blood, so the white count can rise sharply in the first weeks."], ["Lungs and circulation", "Activated maturing cells can lodge in lungs and release cytokines, causing differentiation syndrome with fever, breathing trouble, fluid and low blood pressure."], ["Brain", "Raised pressure around the brain (pseudotumor cerebri) can cause headache, especially in children."], ["Skin and lipids", "Dry skin and lips, bone pain and high triglycerides reflect classic vitamin A excess effects."]], "journey": "The capsules are taken by mouth and reach a peak in one to two hours. Tretinoin is more than 95 percent bound to albumin. The liver breaks it down through CYP26 and CYP3A4 enzymes. Tretinoin induces its own breakdown, so blood levels fall over the course of continuous therapy. Its half-life is short, under about two hours, and metabolites leave in urine and feces. Topical tretinoin products are separate dermatologic drugs; very little of a skin dose reaches the blood.", "sidefx": "Differentiation syndrome happens because large numbers of maturing leukemia cells become sticky, release cytokines and migrate into the lungs and other tissues; it can be fatal and is treated with dexamethasone. Leukocytosis is the same maturation seen in a blood count. Headache, pseudotumor cerebri, dry skin and high lipids are effects of high vitamin A signaling. Retinoids are powerful teratogens because RAR signaling patterns the developing embryo, so pregnancy is avoided during treatment.", "fun": "Combining tretinoin with arsenic trioxide, itself an ancient remedy, turned APL from one of the deadliest leukemias into one of the most curable.", "src": ["Tretinoin capsules FDA label", "Huang ME et al. Blood, 1988", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "bexarotene": {"origin": "Bexarotene was designed in the 1990s at Ligand Pharmaceuticals in San Diego as part of a search for drugs that act on single members of the retinoid receptor family. Most retinoids act on RAR receptors, but bexarotene was built to switch on only the retinoid X receptors (RXRs), so it was nicknamed a rexinoid. It showed activity against cutaneous T-cell lymphoma (CTCL), a lymphoma that lives in the skin. The FDA approved the oral capsules as Targretin in 1999 for refractory CTCL, followed by a topical gel in 2000 for early skin lesions.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Ligand Pharmaceuticals designs bexarotene as an RXR-selective retinoid."], ["1999", "FDA approves bexarotene capsules for cutaneous T-cell lymphoma."], ["2000", "FDA approves bexarotene gel for early-stage CTCL skin lesions."]], "cell": "Bexarotene binds and activates the three retinoid X receptor subtypes (RXRalpha, beta and gamma), which are nuclear receptors that act as transcription factors. RXRs are unusual partners: they form heterodimers with many other nuclear receptors, including RAR, the vitamin D receptor, thyroid hormone receptor, PPARs and LXR. When bexarotene binds, these dimers change which genes are switched on. In malignant T cells this promotes differentiation and apoptosis and slows growth. The same RXR activity affects other tissues. In the liver it raises triglyceride production through LXR and related pathways. In the pituitary, RXR activation suppresses the TSH beta gene, lowering TSH and causing central hypothyroidism.", "body": [["Skin lymphoma cells", "Malignant T cells in skin plaques and tumors undergo apoptosis, so lesions flatten over weeks to months."], ["Liver and blood lipids", "Triglycerides, and to a lesser degree cholesterol, rise sharply, often enough to need lipid-lowering drugs."], ["Pancreas", "Very high triglycerides can trigger acute pancreatitis."], ["Thyroid axis", "TSH falls because the pituitary is suppressed, so thyroid hormone drops even though the thyroid gland itself is normal."], ["Blood", "White cells, especially neutrophils, can decrease."], ["Eyes", "Cataracts have been reported with the capsules."]], "journey": "The capsules are taken with food, which improves absorption, and peak in about two hours. Bexarotene is very highly protein bound. The liver oxidizes it through CYP3A4 and conjugates it with glucuronide, and it leaves mostly in bile and feces. Its half-life is about seven hours. The gel is spread on skin lesions, and only small amounts reach the blood, so the systemic lipid and thyroid effects are mainly seen with the capsules.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come straight from turning on RXR in normal tissues. Liver RXR partners increase triglyceride output, which explains hyperlipidemia and the pancreatitis risk. Pituitary RXR suppresses TSH, which explains central hypothyroidism with low TSH and low free T4. Like other retinoids, it can cause severe birth defects, so it is contraindicated in pregnancy. There is no antidote; lipids and thyroid hormone are managed directly.", "fun": "Bexarotene gained attention in 2012 when a mouse study suggested it cleared amyloid from the brain; follow-up work did not confirm a clear benefit.", "src": ["Targretin (bexarotene) capsules and gel FDA labels", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "vorinostat": {"origin": "Vorinostat grew from a curious lab observation. In the 1970s Charlotte Friend and later Paul Marks and Richard Rifkind at Columbia and Memorial Sloan Kettering found that the solvent DMSO made mouse leukemia cells mature. Working with Columbia chemist Ronald Breslow, they designed stronger molecules with the same effect, and one of these was suberoylanilide hydroxamic acid, or SAHA. Only later did they learn that SAHA works by blocking histone deacetylase enzymes. A small company, Aton Pharma, developed it, and Merck acquired Aton in 2004. The FDA approved vorinostat as Zolinza in 2006 for cutaneous T-cell lymphoma, making it the first approved HDAC inhibitor.", "timeline": [["1970s", "DMSO is found to make leukemia cells differentiate in the lab."], ["1990s", "Breslow, Marks and Rifkind design SAHA as a more potent differentiating agent."], ["2004", "Merck acquires Aton Pharma and the SAHA program."], ["2006", "FDA approves vorinostat, the first HDAC inhibitor, for CTCL."]], "cell": "Histone deacetylases (HDACs) remove acetyl groups from lysines on histone tails. Without acetyl groups, histones hold DNA tightly and many genes stay silent. Vorinostat is a hydroxamic acid. Its hydroxamate end slips into the narrow catalytic pocket of class I HDACs (HDAC1, 2 and 3) and class II HDAC6 and grips the zinc ion that the enzyme needs. With HDAC blocked, acetylated histones build up, chromatin opens and silenced genes such as the cell-cycle brake p21 are switched on. Non-histone proteins such as p53, tubulin and HSP90 also stay acetylated, which changes their activity. The result is cell-cycle arrest, differentiation and apoptosis, with malignant T cells especially sensitive.", "body": [["Skin lymphoma", "Malignant T cells in the skin stop growing and die, which can reduce plaques and itching."], ["Blood and marrow", "Platelets and red cells fall, likely because HDACs are needed for normal megakaryocyte maturation."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea, loss of appetite and taste change are very common and can cause dehydration."], ["Blood vessels", "Pulmonary embolism and deep vein clots have occurred."], ["Metabolism", "Blood sugar can rise, and electrolytes such as potassium and magnesium can shift."], ["Heart", "QT prolongation is reported, so electrolytes are kept normal."]], "journey": "Vorinostat is a capsule taken with food; a high-fat meal raises absorption, and levels peak in about four hours. It is moderately protein bound. Its main breakdown is not by CYP enzymes but by glucuronidation and by hydrolysis followed by beta-oxidation, giving inactive metabolites that leave in the urine. The half-life is short, about two hours, so it is given daily.", "sidefx": "HDACs regulate genes in normal cells too, which explains the broad side effects. Thrombocytopenia reflects impaired platelet production. Gut effects lead to fluid loss and dehydration, which can also strain the kidneys, so fluid intake is encouraged. Clots appear to be a class concern. Valproic acid is also an HDAC inhibitor, and combining the two has caused severe low platelets and GI bleeding. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "SAHA was designed to imitate the effect of DMSO, a solvent also known for giving people a garlic-like breath odor.", "src": ["Zolinza (vorinostat) FDA label", "Marks PA, Breslow R. Nature Biotechnology, 2007", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "ixazomib": {"origin": "Ixazomib was created at Millennium Pharmaceuticals, by then part of Takeda, as an oral successor to bortezomib, the first proteasome inhibitor. Bortezomib had transformed multiple myeloma, but it had to be injected and often caused nerve damage. Millennium chemists searched for a boronic acid that would be stable as a pill and dissociate from the proteasome faster, which they hoped would spread drug into tumors more evenly. The result was ixazomib, given as a citrate ester prodrug. After the TOURMALINE-MM1 trial, the FDA approved it as Ninlaro in 2015 with lenalidomide and dexamethasone, making it the first oral proteasome inhibitor.", "timeline": [["2003", "Bortezomib becomes the first proteasome inhibitor approved for myeloma."], ["2000s", "Millennium (Takeda) develops ixazomib as an oral proteasome inhibitor."], ["2015", "FDA approves ixazomib with lenalidomide and dexamethasone for myeloma after one prior therapy."]], "cell": "The 26S proteasome is the cell's protein shredder. Its 20S core contains three active sites, and ixazomib preferentially binds the chymotrypsin-like site on the beta5 subunit. The boron atom forms a reversible bond with the active-site threonine, blocking cleavage. Proteins tagged with ubiquitin then pile up. Myeloma cells are especially vulnerable because they make huge amounts of antibody protein, so misfolded proteins quickly build up in the endoplasmic reticulum and trigger the unfolded protein response, which turns to apoptosis when stress is too great. Proteasome block also stops degradation of IkB, which keeps NF-kB in the cytoplasm and cuts off survival signals. Pro-apoptotic proteins also accumulate.", "body": [["Myeloma cells", "Plasma cells overloaded with unfolded antibody protein die, so monoclonal protein levels fall."], ["Platelets", "Platelets drop in a cyclic pattern, falling during each cycle and recovering in the rest period, because platelet release from megakaryocytes depends on NF-kB signaling."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, constipation, nausea and vomiting are common."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral neuropathy occurs, but less often and less severely than with bortezomib."], ["Skin and tissues", "Rash and leg swelling (edema) occur; rare severe skin reactions have been reported."]], "journey": "Ixazomib citrate is swallowed on an empty stomach and quickly hydrolyzes in the body to active ixazomib, peaking in about one hour. It is highly bound to plasma proteins and spreads widely into tissues, including red blood cells. The liver breaks it down through several CYP enzymes and non-CYP routes, and metabolites leave in urine and feces. Its half-life is very long, about nine and a half days, which is why it is taken just once a week.", "sidefx": "Proteasome inhibition is not limited to cancer cells. Platelet production depends on proteasome-regulated signaling, so platelet counts fall and bleeding can occur. Gut toxicity reflects effects on the rapidly renewing lining, and fluid loss can harm the kidneys. Nerve damage probably comes from protein buildup and mitochondrial stress in sensory neurons. There is no antidote; doses are held or reduced.", "fun": "The name of the proteasome's chymotrypsin-like site comes from its resemblance to chymotrypsin, a digestive enzyme from the pancreas.", "src": ["Ninlaro (ixazomib) FDA label", "Moreau P et al. TOURMALINE-MM1, N Engl J Med 2016", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "selinexor": {"origin": "Selinexor grew from interest in a protein called exportin 1 (XPO1), which ferries other proteins out of the nucleus. A natural product, leptomycin B from Streptomyces bacteria, was known to block XPO1 but was far too toxic in early human testing. Scientists at Karyopharm Therapeutics in Massachusetts set out to make safer, slowly reversible XPO1 blockers, which they called selective inhibitors of nuclear export (SINE). Selinexor was the lead compound. The FDA approved it as Xpovio in 2019 for heavily treated multiple myeloma with dexamethasone, and in 2020 with bortezomib and dexamethasone after one prior therapy. A separate accelerated approval for DLBCL was later removed from the label.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Leptomycin B is identified as a natural inhibitor of exportin 1."], ["2010s", "Karyopharm develops selinexor as an oral SINE compound."], ["2019", "FDA approves selinexor with dexamethasone for heavily pretreated myeloma."], ["2020", "FDA approves selinexor with bortezomib and dexamethasone after one prior therapy."]], "cell": "Exportin 1 (XPO1, also called CRM1) is a transport protein that carries cargo with nuclear export signals out of the nucleus. Many cancers overexpress XPO1, which keeps tumor suppressor proteins such as p53, p21, IkB and FOXO out of the nucleus, where they would normally act. XPO1 also exports mRNAs bound to eIF4E for oncoproteins such as c-Myc and cyclin D1. Selinexor binds covalently, but slowly reversibly, to cysteine 528 in the cargo-binding groove of XPO1. Tumor suppressors are then trapped in the nucleus, where they can halt the cell cycle and trigger apoptosis. Trapped IkB also blocks NF-kB, and translation of oncoproteins falls. Cancer cells with damaged DNA are more sensitive than normal cells.", "body": [["Myeloma cells", "Nuclear retention of tumor suppressors causes cell-cycle arrest and death of malignant plasma cells."], ["Brain and appetite", "Nausea, vomiting, loss of appetite and weight loss are very common, likely from effects on brain centers that control hunger and nausea."], ["Bone marrow", "Platelets fall, partly because XPO1 block impairs megakaryocyte maturation; neutropenia and anemia also occur."], ["Kidneys and salt balance", "Low blood sodium (hyponatremia) is common and can be severe."], ["Nervous system", "Dizziness, confusion, fainting and changes in mental status can occur."], ["Eyes", "New or worsening cataracts are reported."]], "journey": "Selinexor is a tablet taken by mouth once or twice weekly. It peaks in two to four hours and is highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down through CYP3A4, glucuronidation and glutathione conjugation, and it leaves mostly as metabolites. Its half-life is about six to eight hours. Its effect lasts longer than its blood level because XPO1 inhibition persists for a time after the drug is gone.", "sidefx": "XPO1 is needed by normal cells too, so blocking it widely explains the side effects. Appetite loss, nausea and weight loss appear to have a central origin, which is why antiemetics such as 5-HT3 antagonists are given with it. Thrombocytopenia reflects impaired platelet production. Hyponatremia and dehydration add to the confusion and dizziness that can occur. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Leptomycin B, the toxic natural XPO1 blocker, was first studied as an antifungal compound from soil bacteria.", "src": ["Xpovio (selinexor) FDA label", "Chari A et al. STORM trial, N Engl J Med 2019", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "tazemetostat": {"origin": "Tazemetostat came from Epizyme, a Massachusetts company founded to make drugs against epigenetic enzymes that write chemical marks on histones. Researchers found that some lymphomas carry gain-of-function mutations in EZH2, a histone methyltransferase, and that tumors lacking the INI1 (SMARCB1) protein, such as epithelioid sarcoma, depend on EZH2 to survive. Epizyme, working early on with Eisai, developed tazemetostat as a selective EZH2 inhibitor. In 2020 the FDA granted accelerated approval as Tazverik for epithelioid sarcoma in January, its first EZH2 inhibitor approval, and later that year for relapsed follicular lymphoma. Epizyme was acquired by Ipsen in 2022.", "timeline": [["2010", "EZH2 activating mutations are reported in some B-cell lymphomas."], ["2010s", "Epizyme develops tazemetostat as a selective EZH2 inhibitor."], ["2020", "FDA grants accelerated approval for epithelioid sarcoma and then follicular lymphoma."], ["2022", "Ipsen acquires Epizyme."]], "cell": "EZH2 is the catalytic subunit of polycomb repressive complex 2 (PRC2). It uses S-adenosylmethionine (SAM) as a methyl donor to add three methyl groups to lysine 27 on histone H3 (H3K27me3), a mark that packs chromatin and silences genes, including genes that drive cell maturation. Tazemetostat competes with SAM at the EZH2 active site, blocking both normal and mutant EZH2. H3K27me3 levels fall and silenced genes switch back on. In follicular lymphoma with EZH2 gain-of-function mutations, this reverses the block on B-cell differentiation. In INI1-deficient tumors, loss of the SWI/SNF chromatin remodeling complex leaves PRC2 unopposed, so the cells are dependent on EZH2 and stop growing when it is blocked.", "body": [["Tumor cells", "Silenced differentiation genes reactivate, so epithelioid sarcoma and follicular lymphoma cells stop growing and can shrink."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue, pain and decreased appetite are common."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and constipation occur."], ["Blood and marrow", "Anemia and other cytopenias can occur, and new blood cancers such as MDS, AML and T-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma have been reported."]], "journey": "Tazemetostat is a tablet taken twice daily, peaking in one to two hours. It is about 88 percent protein bound. The liver breaks it down through CYP3A into inactive metabolites, so strong CYP3A inhibitors and inducers change its levels a lot. Its half-life is about three hours, and it is eliminated mostly in feces with some in urine.", "sidefx": "EZH2 helps control normal blood cell development, and changing the epigenetic program of blood stem cells is thought to explain the secondary malignancies seen in trials, which is why long-term follow-up is part of the label. Fatigue and GI effects are common nonspecific effects. Like many drugs that alter development genes, it can harm a fetus. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Tazverik (tazemetostat) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "venetoclax": {"origin": "Venetoclax traces back to the discovery of BCL-2. In 1984 researchers including Yoshihide Tsujimoto and Carlo Croce cloned the gene from the t(14;18) translocation of follicular lymphoma, and in 1988 David Vaux and colleagues in Melbourne showed it kept cells alive rather than making them divide, revealing a new way cancer survives. At Abbott Laboratories, scientists used NMR-based fragment screening to build small molecules that mimic BH3 death proteins, first ABT-737 and then navitoclax. Navitoclax also blocked BCL-XL, which platelets need, so it dropped platelet counts. Abbott, and later AbbVie with Genentech, re-engineered it into the BCL-2-selective venetoclax. The FDA approved it as Venclexta in 2016 for CLL with 17p deletion.", "timeline": [["1984", "The BCL2 gene is cloned from the follicular lymphoma t(14;18) translocation."], ["1988", "BCL-2 is shown to block cell death rather than drive division."], ["2005", "Abbott reports ABT-737, a BH3 mimetic built by fragment screening."], ["2016", "FDA approves venetoclax for CLL with 17p deletion."], ["2018", "Venetoclax is approved with hypomethylating agents or low-dose cytarabine for older adults with AML."]], "cell": "Cells decide whether to die through the intrinsic (mitochondrial) apoptosis pathway. BCL-2 sits on the mitochondrial outer membrane and holds BH3-only proteins such as BIM in its BH3-binding groove. BIM, once free, activates BAX and BAK, which punch holes in the mitochondrial outer membrane. Venetoclax is a BH3 mimetic: it fits tightly into the BCL-2 groove and displaces BIM. Freed BIM activates BAX and BAK, cytochrome c leaks into the cytoplasm, the apoptosome forms and caspase-9 then caspase-3 execute the cell. CLL cells are primed for death and depend on BCL-2, so they die within hours. Because venetoclax spares BCL-XL, platelets, which rely on BCL-XL, are less affected. The effect does not depend on the cell cycle.", "body": [["Leukemia cells", "CLL and AML cells that depend on BCL-2 undergo rapid apoptosis, so blood and lymph node disease can shrink within days."], ["Kidneys and electrolytes", "Massive cell death releases potassium, phosphate and uric acid, which can cause tumor lysis syndrome with kidney injury and dangerous heart rhythms."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutrophil precursors also depend partly on BCL-2, so neutropenia and infections are common."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and nausea are frequent, partly from effects on the gut lining."], ["Immune system", "Low neutrophils and the underlying leukemia increase pneumonia and sepsis risk."]], "journey": "Venetoclax is a tablet taken with a meal, because food raises absorption several fold. Peak levels arrive in five to eight hours, and more than 99 percent is protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP3A4 and CYP3A5, so strong CYP3A inhibitors such as posaconazole raise levels sharply. Almost all of it leaves in feces. The half-life is about 26 hours, and in CLL the dose is increased stepwise over several weeks to limit tumor lysis.", "sidefx": "Tumor lysis syndrome is the direct result of the drug working too well: when many leukemia cells die at once, their contents flood the blood, raising potassium, phosphate and uric acid and lowering calcium. This is why the dose is ramped up, with hydration and uric acid-lowering drugs and close lab checks. Neutropenia reflects partial BCL-2 dependence of normal myeloid cells. There is no antidote; tumor lysis is managed with fluids, rasburicase or allopurinol, and electrolyte treatment.", "fun": "Platelets rely on BCL-XL to survive their roughly ten-day life in the blood, which is why the earlier drug navitoclax dropped platelet counts and the BCL-2-selective venetoclax largely does not.", "src": ["Venclexta (venetoclax) FDA label", "Vaux DL et al. Nature, 1988", "Souers AJ et al. Nature Medicine, 2013"]}, "mitotane": {"origin": "Mitotane is a chemical cousin of the insecticide DDT. In the late 1940s researchers studying insecticide toxicity, notably Arthur Nelson and Geoffrey Woodard, reported that DDD, a DDT relative, caused the adrenal cortex of dogs to shrink while sparing other organs. Scientists realized that a chemical that selectively destroys adrenal tissue might treat adrenal cancer. The ortho-para isomer, o,p'-DDD, proved to be the most active form and was named mitotane. It was tried in patients with adrenocortical carcinoma around 1960 and was approved by the FDA as Lysodren in 1970. It remains one of the few drugs aimed at this rare cancer.", "timeline": [["1949", "DDD is reported to cause selective adrenal atrophy in dogs."], ["1959", "Mitotane is first reported in the treatment of adrenocortical carcinoma."], ["1970", "FDA approves mitotane (Lysodren) for inoperable adrenocortical carcinoma."]], "cell": "Mitotane is activated inside adrenal cortical cells, where mitochondrial enzymes convert it to a reactive acyl chloride. This intermediate binds covalently to mitochondrial proteins and lipids, damaging the mitochondria and causing selective necrosis of the zona fasciculata and zona reticularis, the layers that make cortisol and adrenal androgens. Mitotane also inhibits steroid-making enzymes, including cholesterol side-chain cleavage (CYP11A1) and 11-beta-hydroxylase (CYP11B1). It blocks sterol-O-acyltransferase 1 (SOAT1), so free cholesterol builds up in adrenal cells and triggers endoplasmic reticulum stress and cell death. Outside the adrenal gland it changes cortisol metabolism, induces liver CYP3A4 and raises cortisol-binding globulin.", "body": [["Adrenal cortex", "Cortisol-producing tissue and adrenal cancer cells are destroyed, which lowers cortisol and can shrink tumors or control hormone excess."], ["Whole-body cortisol", "Falling cortisol leads to adrenal insufficiency, so glucocorticoid replacement is a routine part of treatment."], ["Brain", "At high blood levels it causes sleepiness, lethargy, dizziness, unsteady walking and confusion."], ["Liver", "Strong CYP3A4 induction speeds the breakdown of many drugs, including the replacement steroids, and blood cholesterol rises."], ["Gut", "Loss of appetite, nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are very common."], ["Hormone tests", "Increased binding globulins alter lab values for cortisol and thyroid hormone, and gynecomastia can occur in men."]], "journey": "Mitotane is taken by mouth, and only part of the dose is absorbed; taking it with fatty food helps. Because it is extremely fat soluble, it is stored in body fat and builds up slowly, so it usually takes months to reach target blood levels. It is metabolized in the liver and adrenal gland, and metabolites leave in urine and bile. Its half-life is very long, often weeks to months, and the drug can stay in the body for a long time after it is stopped.", "sidefx": "Destroying the adrenal cortex is the goal, but it means the body cannot raise cortisol in a crisis. Shock, severe injury or infection can therefore trigger adrenal crisis, which is why the label describes stopping the drug temporarily and giving stress steroids in those settings. Brain effects track with high blood levels, reflecting a lipophilic drug that enters nervous tissue. Gut upset is common. CYP3A4 induction lowers levels of many drugs. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Mitotane was found because of public health worries about insecticides, not cancer research.", "src": ["Lysodren (mitotane) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "thalidomide": {"origin": "Thalidomide was made by the West German company Chemie Grunenthal in the 1950s and sold from 1957 as a sedative, under names such as Contergan, and was given for morning sickness. Within a few years, more than ten thousand babies worldwide were born with severe limb defects such as phocomelia, along with other malformations. Physicians Widukind Lenz in Germany and William McBride in Australia linked the defects to the drug, and it was withdrawn in 1961. In the US, FDA reviewer Frances Kelsey had refused to approve it because the safety data were inadequate, a stand that led to the 1962 Kefauver-Harris drug law reforms. In 1964 Jacob Sheskin in Israel found it relieved erythema nodosum leprosum (ENL), and in 1998 the FDA approved it for ENL under strict controls. Later trials showed activity in multiple myeloma, and it was approved for myeloma in 2006.", "timeline": [["1957", "Thalidomide is launched in West Germany as a sedative."], ["1961", "Thalidomide is withdrawn after being linked to severe birth defects."], ["1962", "Kefauver-Harris amendments strengthen US drug approval rules."], ["1964", "Jacob Sheskin reports benefit in erythema nodosum leprosum."], ["1998", "FDA approves thalidomide for ENL with restricted distribution."], ["2010", "Cereblon is identified as the protein target of thalidomide."]], "cell": "The direct target of thalidomide is cereblon (CRBN), the substrate receptor of the CRL4 E3 ubiquitin ligase complex. Thalidomide works as a molecular glue: when it sits in a pocket on cereblon, it creates a new surface that grabs proteins cereblon would normally ignore. These neosubstrates are tagged with ubiquitin and destroyed by the proteasome. In myeloma cells, loss of the transcription factors Ikaros (IKZF1) and Aiolos (IKZF3) lowers IRF4 and MYC and kills the cells, while in T cells it boosts IL-2 production and immune activity. Degradation of SALL4, a transcription factor needed for limb development, is strongly linked to its birth defects. Thalidomide also reduces TNF-alpha production, which helps in ENL, and inhibits angiogenesis.", "body": [["Myeloma cells", "Destruction of Ikaros and Aiolos in malignant plasma cells leads to their death."], ["Immune system", "T-cell and NK-cell activity rise, while TNF-alpha falls, which calms the inflammatory nodules of ENL."], ["Developing embryo", "Even brief exposure in early pregnancy can cause limb, ear, eye, heart and other malformations."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral neuropathy with numbness and tingling is common with longer use and can be permanent."], ["Blood vessels", "Risk of deep vein clots and pulmonary embolism rises, especially with dexamethasone."], ["Brain and heart", "Drowsiness, dizziness and slowed heart rate occur."]], "journey": "Thalidomide is swallowed as a capsule and absorbed slowly, peaking in two to five hours. Unlike most drugs, it is broken down mainly by nonenzymatic hydrolysis in the blood, with little CYP involvement. The hydrolysis products leave in urine. Its half-life is about five to seven hours. It also crosses the placenta and enters semen, which is why male patients are counseled about contraception and blood and sperm donation are not allowed.", "sidefx": "Birth defects come from cereblon-dependent breakdown of developmental proteins such as SALL4 in the embryo, which is why the drug is available only through a REMS program with pregnancy tests and contraception. Clotting risk is thought to involve effects on endothelium and platelets and rises with dexamethasone, so clot prevention is recommended in myeloma. Neuropathy likely reflects direct toxicity to sensory nerves. Sedation was its original purpose. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Frances Kelsey received the President's Award for Distinguished Federal Civilian Service in 1962 for keeping thalidomide off the US market.", "src": ["Thalomid (thalidomide) FDA label", "Ito T et al. Science, 2010", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "lenalidomide": {"origin": "After thalidomide returned to medicine, the company Celgene set out to make analogs that were more potent against cancer and less sedating. Adding an amino group and removing a carbonyl from the thalidomide structure produced lenalidomide. It was first approved by the FDA as Revlimid in 2005 for anemia from myelodysplastic syndrome with deletion 5q, and in 2006 for multiple myeloma with dexamethasone. It became a backbone of myeloma treatment and one of the best-selling drugs in the world. In 2014 research groups showed that it works by making cereblon destroy Ikaros and Aiolos, and in 2015 they explained its special effect in del(5q) MDS through casein kinase 1-alpha.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Celgene develops thalidomide analogs, including lenalidomide."], ["2005", "FDA approves lenalidomide for del(5q) myelodysplastic syndrome."], ["2006", "FDA approves lenalidomide with dexamethasone for multiple myeloma."], ["2014", "Ikaros and Aiolos are identified as targets degraded through cereblon."], ["2017", "Approval expands to maintenance after autologous stem cell transplant."]], "cell": "Lenalidomide binds cereblon (CRBN), the substrate receptor of the CRL4 E3 ubiquitin ligase, and acts as a molecular glue that recruits new protein targets to the ligase. In myeloma cells it triggers ubiquitination and proteasomal destruction of the lymphoid transcription factors Ikaros (IKZF1) and Aiolos (IKZF3). Their loss shuts down IRF4 and MYC, which the myeloma cells need, causing apoptosis. In T cells, removing Ikaros and Aiolos lifts repression of the IL-2 gene, boosting T-cell and NK-cell activity. In del(5q) MDS, lenalidomide also degrades casein kinase 1-alpha (CK1a). These cells already have only one copy of the CSNK1A1 gene, so further loss is lethal to the abnormal clone while normal cells survive. It also has antiangiogenic effects.", "body": [["Myeloma cells", "Malignant plasma cells lose the transcription factors they depend on and die."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils and platelets fall, most severely in del(5q) MDS, where the abnormal clone is cleared before normal blood cells recover."], ["Red cells in MDS", "As the del(5q) clone is removed, normal red cell production returns and many patients stop needing transfusions."], ["Immune system", "T cells and NK cells become more active, aiding the attack on cancer cells."], ["Blood vessels", "Risk of DVT, pulmonary embolism, heart attack and stroke increases, especially with dexamethasone."], ["Developing embryo", "Like thalidomide, it is expected to cause severe birth defects."]], "journey": "Lenalidomide is a capsule that is absorbed quickly, peaking in about one hour. It is only about 30 percent protein bound and is barely metabolized. Most of the dose leaves unchanged in the urine through the kidneys, so kidney function strongly affects its levels and doses are adjusted in renal impairment. Its half-life is about three to five hours with normal kidneys and longer when kidney function is reduced.", "sidefx": "Neutropenia and thrombocytopenia occur because lenalidomide affects blood-forming cells, and in del(5q) MDS the drug removes much of the marrow's working clone before normal cells refill it. Clotting risk is raised by its effects on endothelium and platelets, so clot prevention is recommended with dexamethasone. Birth defects relate to cereblon-dependent breakdown of developmental proteins, hence the REMS program. Long-term use is linked with second primary cancers. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Revlimid (lenalidomide) FDA label", "Kronke J et al. Science, 2014", "Kronke J et al. Nature, 2015"]}, "pomalidomide": {"origin": "Pomalidomide is the third of the thalidomide family of immunomodulatory drugs developed by Celgene. It keeps thalidomide's ring structure but adds an amino group, which makes it far more potent at activating the immune system and degrading its target proteins. It proved useful in multiple myeloma that no longer responds to lenalidomide and bortezomib. The FDA approved it as Pomalyst in 2013 with dexamethasone for relapsed myeloma. In 2020 it gained approval for Kaposi sarcoma, both AIDS-related and in HIV-negative adults.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Pomalidomide is identified among Celgene's thalidomide analogs."], ["2013", "FDA approves pomalidomide for relapsed and refractory multiple myeloma."], ["2020", "FDA approves pomalidomide for Kaposi sarcoma."]], "cell": "Pomalidomide binds cereblon (CRBN), part of the CRL4 E3 ubiquitin ligase, and reshapes its surface so that it captures the transcription factors Ikaros (IKZF1) and Aiolos (IKZF3). These are then ubiquitinated and destroyed by the proteasome. In myeloma cells, losing Ikaros and Aiolos collapses IRF4 and MYC signals and leads to apoptosis. It binds and degrades these targets more strongly than lenalidomide, which helps explain why it can still work after lenalidomide fails. In T cells and NK cells, the same degradation releases IL-2 and interferon-gamma production and boosts immune attack. It also inhibits angiogenesis, which is relevant to vascular tumors like Kaposi sarcoma.", "body": [["Myeloma cells", "Even lenalidomide-resistant plasma cells can be killed when Ikaros and Aiolos are degraded."], ["Immune system", "Increased T-cell and NK-cell activity helps clear cancer cells."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia is the most common severe effect, with anemia and low platelets as well."], ["Blood vessels", "DVT, pulmonary embolism, heart attack and stroke are risks, so clot prevention is recommended."], ["Brain and nerves", "Dizziness, confusion and neuropathy can occur."], ["Developing embryo", "Severe birth defects are expected, as with other drugs in this class."]], "journey": "Pomalidomide capsules are absorbed well, peaking in two to three hours. Unlike lenalidomide, it is extensively metabolized, mainly through CYP1A2 and CYP3A4 hydroxylation and through hydrolysis. Strong CYP1A2 inhibitors such as fluvoxamine raise its levels. Metabolites leave mostly in urine. The half-life is about seven and a half to nine and a half hours.", "sidefx": "Neutropenia and infections reflect effects on myeloid cells in the marrow. Blood clots occur because, like its relatives, it shifts endothelial and platelet behavior toward clotting. Birth defects come from cereblon-dependent loss of developmental proteins, which is why it is available only through the POMALYST REMS program with pregnancy testing and contraception. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Thalidomide, lenalidomide and pomalidomide differ by only a few atoms, yet each binds cereblon a little differently and each degrades a somewhat different set of proteins.", "src": ["Pomalyst (pomalidomide) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "dasatinib": {"origin": "The story of dasatinib starts with chronic myeloid leukemia (CML). In 1960 Peter Nowell and David Hungerford in Philadelphia found a shortened chromosome in CML cells, and in 1973 Janet Rowley showed it came from a swap between chromosomes 9 and 22, which creates the BCR-ABL1 fusion kinase. Imatinib, approved in 2001, blocked that kinase, but some leukemias became resistant. At Bristol-Myers Squibb, chemists had been making SRC family kinase inhibitors for immune diseases. One of them, dasatinib, also blocked ABL potently and could bind the kinase in more than one shape. The FDA approved it as Sprycel in 2006 for imatinib-resistant CML and Ph-positive ALL, and in 2010 for newly diagnosed CML.", "timeline": [["1960", "Nowell and Hungerford describe the Philadelphia chromosome in CML."], ["1973", "Janet Rowley shows it results from a 9;22 translocation."], ["2001", "Imatinib is approved, the first BCR-ABL1 inhibitor."], ["2006", "FDA approves dasatinib for imatinib-resistant or intolerant CML and Ph+ ALL."], ["2010", "Dasatinib is approved for newly diagnosed chronic phase CML."]], "cell": "In CML, BCR-ABL1 is a kinase that is always switched on. It phosphorylates signaling proteins that drive RAS-MAPK, PI3K-AKT and STAT5 pathways, making cells divide and resist apoptosis. Dasatinib competes with ATP in the ABL kinase pocket. Unlike imatinib, it can bind both the active and the inactive shapes of the kinase, which makes it far more potent and lets it overcome most imatinib-resistance mutations. The T315I gatekeeper mutation still blocks it, because the bulkier isoleucine prevents binding. Dasatinib also inhibits SRC family kinases (SRC, LCK, YES, FYN), c-KIT, EPHA2 and PDGFR-beta. Blocking BCR-ABL1 shuts off survival signals, and the leukemia cells die by apoptosis.", "body": [["Leukemia cells", "BCR-ABL-driven cells lose their growth signal, so blood counts normalize and BCR-ABL levels fall."], ["Bone marrow", "Normal blood cell production is also suppressed, especially early, causing low platelets, neutrophils and red cells."], ["Lungs and pleura", "Fluid can collect around the lungs (pleural effusion), causing cough or breathlessness."], ["Lung blood vessels", "Rarely it causes pulmonary arterial hypertension, which may improve after stopping."], ["Platelets and bleeding", "SRC kinases help platelets work, so bleeding can occur even with near-normal counts."], ["Heart", "QT prolongation and heart failure are reported."]], "journey": "Dasatinib is a tablet taken once daily with or without food. It is absorbed within half an hour to six hours, and absorption falls when stomach acid is suppressed. It is about 96 percent protein bound and spreads widely into tissues. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP3A4, so strong inhibitors and inducers change exposure. Most leaves in feces. Its half-life is short, about three to five hours, but kinase inhibition is enough to work with once daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Marrow suppression reflects blockade of normal ABL and KIT signals in blood-forming cells. Pleural effusion is a distinctive dasatinib effect, thought to relate to SRC family and PDGFR-beta inhibition in immune and vascular cells that control fluid leak. Platelet SRC inhibition explains bleeding despite adequate counts. Pulmonary arterial hypertension is a rare off-target vascular effect. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Dasatinib is widely reported to be named after Jagabandhu Das, a Bristol-Myers Squibb chemist who worked on the program.", "src": ["Sprycel (dasatinib) FDA label", "Rowley JD. Nature, 1973", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "nilotinib": {"origin": "Nilotinib was designed at Novartis by chemists who had made imatinib. Using the crystal structure of imatinib bound to the ABL kinase, they rebuilt parts of the molecule to fit the pocket more snugly, and the result bound BCR-ABL1 many times more tightly than imatinib while keeping a similar binding style. Reported in 2005, it reached patients quickly. The FDA approved it as Tasigna in 2007 for CML resistant to imatinib and in 2010 for newly diagnosed chronic phase CML. Its label carries a boxed warning about QT prolongation and sudden death, and the capsules must be taken on an empty stomach.", "timeline": [["2001", "Imatinib, the first BCR-ABL1 inhibitor, is approved."], ["2005", "Novartis reports nilotinib, a rationally redesigned imatinib relative."], ["2007", "FDA approves nilotinib for imatinib-resistant or intolerant CML."], ["2010", "Nilotinib is approved for newly diagnosed chronic phase CML."]], "cell": "Nilotinib competes with ATP for the ABL kinase domain of BCR-ABL1. Like imatinib, it binds the inactive DFG-out shape of the kinase, but its improved fit gives much higher affinity. This shuts down the constant signals BCR-ABL1 sends through STAT5, RAS-MAPK and PI3K-AKT, so CML cells stop proliferating and undergo apoptosis. It overcomes most imatinib-resistance mutations but not T315I. Nilotinib also inhibits c-KIT and PDGFR. Off target, it inhibits UGT1A1, the liver enzyme that conjugates bilirubin, and it blocks the hERG potassium channel in heart cells, which slows repolarization.", "body": [["Leukemia cells", "BCR-ABL-dependent cells die, so blood counts and BCR-ABL levels return toward normal."], ["Heart", "QT prolongation can lead to torsades de pointes and sudden death, so ECGs and electrolytes are checked."], ["Arteries", "Over time it raises the risk of peripheral artery disease, heart attack and stroke."], ["Liver", "Indirect bilirubin rises from UGT1A1 inhibition, and liver enzymes can increase."], ["Pancreas and metabolism", "Lipase rises, pancreatitis can occur, and blood glucose and cholesterol can increase."], ["Bone marrow", "Low platelets and neutrophils are common, especially early in treatment."]], "journey": "The capsules are taken twice daily without food, because a meal raises absorption and blood levels and so raises QT risk. Peak levels come in about three hours. It is about 98 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors are avoided. Most is eliminated in feces. Its half-life is about 17 hours.", "sidefx": "QT prolongation comes from blockade of the hERG potassium channel, which delays cardiac repolarization; low potassium or magnesium, QT-prolonging drugs, CYP3A4 inhibitors and food all make it worse. Raised indirect bilirubin is from UGT1A1 inhibition, the same pathway affected in Gilbert syndrome. Vascular occlusive events are thought to reflect off-target effects on vascular and metabolic cells. Marrow suppression reflects on-target ABL and KIT effects. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Tasigna (nilotinib) FDA label", "Weisberg E et al. Cancer Cell, 2005", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "bosutinib": {"origin": "Bosutinib was discovered at Wyeth, where chemists were developing quinoline compounds to block SRC family kinases. The molecule, known as SKI-606, also turned out to be a strong inhibitor of ABL, and therefore of BCR-ABL1 in chronic myeloid leukemia. Pfizer took over the program when it acquired Wyeth in 2009. The FDA approved bosutinib as Bosulif in 2012 for CML resistant or intolerant to prior therapy, and in 2017 for newly diagnosed chronic phase CML. It was later approved for children with chronic phase CML as well.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Wyeth develops SKI-606, a dual SRC and ABL inhibitor."], ["2009", "Pfizer acquires Wyeth."], ["2012", "FDA approves bosutinib for previously treated CML."], ["2017", "Bosutinib is approved for newly diagnosed chronic phase CML."]], "cell": "Bosutinib is an ATP-competitive inhibitor of BCR-ABL1 and of SRC family kinases such as SRC, LYN and HCK. By occupying the ATP pocket of the ABL kinase, it stops BCR-ABL1 from phosphorylating its downstream partners, shutting down STAT5, RAS-MAPK and PI3K-AKT signals that keep CML cells alive, so the cells undergo apoptosis. It works against most imatinib-resistance mutations but not T315I or V299L. Compared with imatinib, dasatinib and nilotinib, it has little activity against c-KIT and PDGFR, which may explain less fluid retention and marrow suppression than some others.", "body": [["Leukemia cells", "CML cells driven by BCR-ABL1 lose their growth signal and die."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is very common, usually early, along with nausea, vomiting and abdominal pain."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes often rise in the first months, so liver tests are checked regularly."], ["Bone marrow", "Platelets and other blood counts can drop because normal blood cells also use ABL signals."], ["Kidneys", "Kidney function can decline gradually with long-term use."], ["Fluid balance", "Fluid retention, including effusions, can occur but is less prominent than with dasatinib."]], "journey": "Bosutinib is a tablet taken once daily with food, which improves absorption and tolerance. Peak levels come in four to six hours. It is about 94 percent protein bound. The liver breaks it down through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A inhibitors and inducers are avoided. Nearly all leaves in feces. The half-life is about 22 hours.", "sidefx": "The heavy early diarrhea is likely from kinase effects on gut lining cells and usually settles over weeks. Liver enzyme rises reflect direct hepatocyte stress. Marrow effects come from blocking ABL signals in normal blood cells, though sparing KIT may soften them. Kidney function declines over time. There is no antidote; care uses antidiarrheals, fluids and dose holds.", "src": ["Bosulif (bosutinib) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "ponatinib": {"origin": "By the late 2000s, the T315I mutation was the main weak spot of CML treatment: it blocked imatinib, dasatinib, nilotinib and bosutinib. At ARIAD Pharmaceuticals in Cambridge, Massachusetts, scientists used structure-based design to build a molecule with a slim carbon-carbon triple bond that could slide past the bulky isoleucine at position 315. The result was ponatinib. The FDA granted accelerated approval as Iclusig in December 2012. Within a year, reports of serious artery blockages led the FDA to suspend its marketing in late 2013. It returned weeks later with a narrower indication and stronger warnings, and later trials showed that lowering the dose after a response reduced arterial risk.", "timeline": [["2000s", "ARIAD designs ponatinib to overcome the T315I mutation."], ["2012", "FDA grants accelerated approval to ponatinib for resistant CML and Ph+ ALL."], ["2013", "Marketing is briefly suspended over arterial occlusion events, then resumed with restrictions."], ["2020", "Label updates to response-based dose reduction in chronic phase CML."], ["2024", "Approval expands to newly diagnosed Ph+ ALL with chemotherapy."]], "cell": "Ponatinib is an ATP-competitive pan-BCR-ABL1 inhibitor. Most kinase inhibitors make contact with threonine 315, a gatekeeper residue at the back of the ATP pocket. When this becomes isoleucine (T315I), the larger side chain blocks them and removes a key hydrogen bond. Ponatinib's ethynyl linker, a straight carbon-carbon triple bond, threads past the isoleucine without clashing, so it binds native BCR-ABL1 and single mutants, including T315I. With BCR-ABL1 blocked, STAT5, RAS-MAPK and PI3K-AKT signals stop, and leukemia cells die. Ponatinib is a broad inhibitor: it also hits VEGFR, FGFR, PDGFR, SRC, KIT, RET, TIE2 and FLT3. VEGFR inhibition in particular raises blood pressure and harms vascular endothelium.", "body": [["Leukemia cells", "CML and Ph+ ALL cells, including those with T315I, lose BCR-ABL1 signaling and die."], ["Arteries", "Heart attacks, strokes and narrowing of leg and other arteries can occur, even in younger patients."], ["Blood pressure", "Hypertension is common, reflecting VEGFR inhibition."], ["Heart", "Heart failure and rhythm problems are reported."], ["Liver", "Liver toxicity, including liver failure, can occur."], ["Pancreas", "Lipase rises and pancreatitis can occur."], ["Bone marrow", "Low platelets and neutrophils are common, especially early."]], "journey": "Ponatinib is a tablet taken once daily. Peak levels come in about six hours, and it is more than 99 percent protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP3A4, with help from CYP2C8, CYP2D6, CYP3A5 and esterases, so strong CYP3A inhibitors raise its levels. Most of it leaves in feces. The half-life is about 24 hours.", "sidefx": "The arterial and venous clotting events, hypertension and heart failure tie to its broad kinase inhibition, particularly VEGFR, which damages the inner lining of vessels and promotes narrowing and clotting. The risk rises with dose, which is why the label lowers the dose in chronic phase CML once a good response is reached and stresses control of blood pressure and cholesterol. Liver and pancreas toxicity are additional off-target effects. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Iclusig (ponatinib) FDA label", "O'Hare T et al. Cancer Cell, 2009", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "asciminib": {"origin": "Asciminib took a completely different approach to CML. Normal ABL1 has a pocket where a myristoyl fatty acid tail tucks in and switches the kinase off, but in BCR-ABL1 that regulatory tail is lost. Scientists at Novartis used fragment screening and structure-guided design to find a small molecule that could sit in this empty myristoyl pocket and restore the off switch. They called the approach STAMP, for Specifically Targeting the ABL Myristoyl Pocket. Because it binds far from the ATP site, it works against mutations like T315I. The FDA approved asciminib as Scemblix in 2021 for previously treated CML and for T315I CML, and in 2024 for newly diagnosed chronic phase CML.", "timeline": [["2000s", "The myristoyl pocket of ABL1 is shown to act as a natural off switch."], ["2010s", "Novartis develops asciminib through fragment-based design."], ["2021", "FDA approves asciminib for previously treated CML and CML with T315I."], ["2024", "Asciminib gains accelerated approval for newly diagnosed chronic phase CML."]], "cell": "Asciminib is an allosteric BCR-ABL1 inhibitor, not an ATP-competitive one. In normal ABL1, the N-terminal myristoyl group binds a pocket in the C-lobe of the kinase domain and holds the enzyme in a clamped, inactive shape. The BCR fusion removes this region, so BCR-ABL1 stays switched on. Asciminib binds the empty myristoyl pocket and mimics myristate, bending the C-terminal helix and locking the kinase into the inactive conformation. Downstream signaling through STAT5, RAS-MAPK and PI3K-AKT stops and leukemia cells die. Because it does not use the ATP site, mutations there, including T315I, do not prevent binding. Its selectivity for ABL means fewer off-target kinase effects.", "body": [["Leukemia cells", "BCR-ABL1-driven CML cells, including those resistant to ATP-site drugs, lose their growth signal."], ["Bone marrow", "Platelets and neutrophils can fall, especially early."], ["Pancreas", "Lipase and amylase can rise and pancreatitis can occur."], ["Blood pressure and heart", "Hypertension can develop, and ischemic heart events and heart failure have been reported."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue, musculoskeletal pain, headache and upper respiratory infections are common."]], "journey": "Asciminib is a tablet taken without food, because meals lower absorption. Peak levels come in about two and a half hours, and it is about 97 percent protein bound. It is metabolized by CYP3A4 oxidation and by UGT2B7 and UGT2B17 glucuronidation, and most of the dose leaves in feces, largely unchanged. Its half-life is about five to nine hours, supporting once or twice daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Marrow suppression is an on-target effect of blocking ABL signaling in normal blood cells. Pancreatic enzyme rises and hypertension are recognized class effects of BCR-ABL1 inhibitors. Because it hits few other kinases, many side effects seen with broader drugs are less frequent. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Asciminib can be combined in the lab with ATP-site inhibitors because the two classes bind different parts of the same kinase at the same time.", "src": ["Scemblix (asciminib) FDA label", "Wylie AA et al. Nature, 2017"]}, "erlotinib": {"origin": "The epidermal growth factor (EGF) was discovered by Stanley Cohen in the early 1960s, work that earned a Nobel Prize in 1986, and its receptor EGFR was later found to be overactive in many cancers. Erlotinib was discovered in the 1990s in a collaboration between Oncogene Science (later OSI Pharmaceuticals) and Pfizer, and OSI developed it with Genentech and Roche. The FDA approved it as Tarceva in 2004 for advanced non-small cell lung cancer after chemotherapy, and in 2005 with gemcitabine for pancreatic cancer. In 2004 researchers discovered that lung tumors with activating EGFR mutations respond dramatically, and erlotinib's label later focused on EGFR exon 19 deletion and L858R tumors.", "timeline": [["1962", "Stanley Cohen reports epidermal growth factor."], ["2004", "FDA approves erlotinib for advanced NSCLC after chemotherapy."], ["2004", "Activating EGFR mutations are linked to dramatic responses to EGFR inhibitors."], ["2005", "Erlotinib is approved with gemcitabine for pancreatic cancer."], ["2013", "Erlotinib is approved for first-line EGFR-mutant NSCLC."]], "cell": "EGFR (HER1, ErbB1) is a receptor tyrosine kinase. When ligands such as EGF bind, receptors pair up and phosphorylate each other on tyrosines inside the cell. These phosphotyrosines recruit adaptor proteins that switch on RAS-RAF-MEK-ERK and PI3K-AKT pathways, driving growth and survival. In tumors with exon 19 deletions or the L858R mutation, the kinase is always active and the cancer becomes addicted to it. Erlotinib reversibly competes with ATP at the EGFR kinase site, blocking autophosphorylation and downstream signaling. Mutant EGFR binds erlotinib more tightly than normal EGFR, which helps explain the strong responses. Cells arrest in G1 and undergo apoptosis. The T790M mutation, which raises ATP affinity, is the most common route of resistance.", "body": [["Lung tumor", "EGFR-mutant lung cancers can shrink quickly as the growth signal is cut off."], ["Skin", "Acne-like rash on the face and upper body is common because normal skin cells depend on EGFR."], ["Nails", "Painful inflammation around the nails (paronychia) occurs."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea results from EGFR blockade in the gut lining, which increases chloride secretion."], ["Lungs", "Rarely, interstitial lung disease can develop and can be fatal."], ["Eyes and liver", "Keratitis and eyelash changes can occur, and liver enzymes can rise."]], "journey": "Erlotinib is a tablet taken on an empty stomach, because food sharply raises absorption. Acid-reducing drugs lower absorption. It peaks in about four hours and is about 93 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP3A4, with CYP1A2 and CYP1A1 contributing. Cigarette smoke induces CYP1A2, so smokers have lower levels. Most of it leaves in feces. Its half-life is about 36 hours.", "sidefx": "The rash, dry skin and nail changes occur because skin keratinocytes and hair follicles need EGFR signaling for normal growth and repair; blocking it causes follicle inflammation. Diarrhea comes from EGFR effects on intestinal epithelium. Interstitial lung disease is less common but serious. Erlotinib can raise the INR in people taking warfarin. There is no antidote.", "fun": "In early trials, people whose rash was worse often had better tumor responses, because the rash shows that EGFR is truly being blocked.", "src": ["Tarceva (erlotinib) FDA label", "Lynch TJ et al. N Engl J Med, 2004", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "gefitinib": {"origin": "Gefitinib, first known as ZD1839, was developed by AstraZeneca in the 1990s as one of the first small molecules to block the EGFR tyrosine kinase. It was first approved in Japan in 2002, and the FDA granted it accelerated approval as Iressa in 2003 for advanced lung cancer after chemotherapy. Only about one in ten unselected patients responded, but some responded dramatically. In 2004 groups in Boston showed that these patients' tumors carried activating EGFR mutations. A large survival trial in unselected patients was negative, so US use was restricted in 2005. After the IPASS trial in Asia showed gefitinib worked best in EGFR-mutant tumors, the FDA approved it again in 2015 as first-line treatment for EGFR-mutant NSCLC.", "timeline": [["2002", "Gefitinib is first approved in Japan."], ["2003", "FDA grants accelerated approval for advanced NSCLC after chemotherapy."], ["2004", "Activating EGFR mutations are shown to predict response."], ["2005", "US use is restricted after a negative survival trial."], ["2009", "The IPASS trial shows benefit in EGFR-mutant lung cancer."], ["2015", "FDA approves gefitinib for first-line EGFR-mutant NSCLC."]], "cell": "Gefitinib is a reversible, ATP-competitive inhibitor of the EGFR (HER1, ErbB1) tyrosine kinase domain. Normally, when EGF binds, EGFR dimerizes and autophosphorylates, activating RAS-RAF-MEK-ERK and PI3K-AKT signals for growth and survival. In lung cancers with exon 19 deletions or L858R mutations, the kinase is active without ligand and the cells depend on it. These mutations also change the shape of the ATP pocket so gefitinib binds the mutant kinase more tightly than the normal one. Blocking autophosphorylation shuts down AKT survival signals and ERK growth signals, causing G1 arrest and apoptosis. Resistance often develops through the T790M gatekeeper mutation, which increases the kinase's affinity for ATP and pushes the drug out.", "body": [["Lung tumor", "EGFR-mutant tumors shrink as their main growth signal is turned off."], ["Skin", "An acne-like rash and dry skin develop because skin cells need EGFR signaling."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea and loss of appetite come from EGFR blockade in the gut lining."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises are relatively common and can lead to dose changes."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease is uncommon but can be fatal, and was reported more often in Japan."], ["Eyes", "Keratitis and eye irritation can occur, since EGFR helps maintain the corneal surface."]], "journey": "Gefitinib is a tablet taken once daily with or without food. Absorption is slow, peaking in three to seven hours, and drugs that raise stomach pH reduce it. It is about 90 percent protein bound and spreads widely into tissues. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP3A4, with a contribution from CYP2D6. Most leaves in feces. Its half-life is about 48 hours.", "sidefx": "The rash, dry skin and nail problems reflect the role of EGFR in normal skin and hair follicle renewal. Diarrhea reflects the role of EGFR in gut epithelium. Liver enzyme rises are a direct liver effect. Interstitial lung disease is a rare, serious inflammatory reaction in the lungs; new cough or breathlessness leads to holding the drug. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Gefitinib's first approval, rejection in practice and later comeback helped make biomarker-selected trials the standard for targeted cancer drugs.", "src": ["Iressa (gefitinib) FDA label", "Paez JG et al. Science, 2004", "Mok TS et al. IPASS, N Engl J Med 2009"]}, "afatinib": {"origin": "First-generation EGFR inhibitors bind reversibly, and resistance often appeared within about a year. Boehringer Ingelheim designed afatinib to bind irreversibly, forming a covalent bond with a cysteine in the kinase pocket, and to block the wider ErbB family, including HER2 and HER4. The idea was that permanent, broader blockade would delay resistance. Afatinib did not reliably overcome the T790M mutation, but in the LUX-Lung trials it outperformed chemotherapy in EGFR-mutant lung cancer. The FDA approved it as Gilotrif in 2013. It was later approved for squamous NSCLC after chemotherapy, and its label expanded to some less common EGFR mutations.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Boehringer Ingelheim develops afatinib as an irreversible ErbB family blocker."], ["2013", "FDA approves afatinib for first-line EGFR-mutant NSCLC."], ["2016", "Afatinib is approved for metastatic squamous NSCLC after platinum chemotherapy."], ["2018", "The label expands to tumors with certain uncommon EGFR mutations."]], "cell": "Afatinib binds the ATP pocket of EGFR (ErbB1), HER2 (ErbB2) and HER4 (ErbB4) and then forms a covalent bond with a cysteine residue at the edge of the pocket (Cys797 in EGFR). Once bonded, the kinase stays inhibited until the cell makes new receptor protein. Without autophosphorylation, receptor dimers cannot recruit adaptors, so RAS-RAF-MEK-ERK and PI3K-AKT signaling falls and EGFR-dependent tumor cells stop growing and die. Blocking HER2 also prevents EGFR-HER2 dimers from signaling. Because afatinib also strongly inhibits normal (wild-type) EGFR in skin and gut, it causes more rash and diarrhea than first-generation drugs. It is cytostatic rather than tied to one phase of the cell cycle.", "body": [["Lung tumor", "EGFR-mutant cancers shrink as ErbB signaling is permanently shut off."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is very common and can be severe enough to cause dehydration and kidney injury."], ["Skin", "Acne-like rash, dry skin and itching are frequent because normal skin EGFR is blocked."], ["Nails and mouth", "Paronychia and mouth sores (stomatitis) are common."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease is uncommon but serious."], ["Liver and eyes", "Liver enzymes can rise, and keratitis can occur."]], "journey": "Afatinib is a tablet taken on an empty stomach, because food lowers absorption. It peaks in two to five hours and is about 95 percent protein bound. It is barely metabolized by enzymes; instead, much of it forms covalent bonds with proteins. It is a P-glycoprotein substrate, so P-gp inhibitors raise its levels and inducers lower them. Most leaves in feces. Its half-life is about 37 hours.", "sidefx": "Afatinib's strong grip on wild-type EGFR is the reason for its heavy skin and gut toxicity. In the gut, losing EGFR signals increases fluid secretion and slows lining repair, causing diarrhea, which is why loperamide is started early. In skin, EGFR loss inflames hair follicles and disrupts the barrier. Severe fluid loss can cause acute kidney injury. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Gilotrif (afatinib) FDA label", "Sequist LV et al. LUX-Lung 3, J Clin Oncol 2013", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "osimertinib": {"origin": "Osimertinib was designed by AstraZeneca chemists to solve a specific problem: the T790M mutation, which causes about half of resistance to first-generation EGFR inhibitors. Starting around 2009, the team searched for a molecule that would bind mutant EGFR, including T790M, while largely sparing normal EGFR to reduce rash and diarrhea. The compound, AZD9291, formed a covalent bond with cysteine 797. Development moved unusually fast: it went from first human dosing to FDA accelerated approval as Tagrisso in 2015 in less than three years. In 2018 the FLAURA trial made it a standard first-line drug, and approvals followed for adjuvant use after surgery and for other settings.", "timeline": [["2009", "AstraZeneca begins the program that leads to osimertinib."], ["2015", "FDA grants accelerated approval for T790M-positive NSCLC."], ["2018", "Osimertinib is approved for first-line EGFR-mutant NSCLC."], ["2020", "Approval extends to adjuvant therapy after tumor resection."], ["2024", "Approvals add use with chemotherapy and after chemoradiation in stage III disease."]], "cell": "Osimertinib is a third-generation EGFR inhibitor. It binds the ATP pocket of mutant EGFR and forms an irreversible covalent bond with cysteine 797. It inhibits the sensitizing mutations (exon 19 deletion, L858R) and the T790M gatekeeper mutation. T790M resists older drugs mainly by raising the kinase's affinity for ATP, but covalent binding makes osimertinib's block permanent. It is much less active against wild-type EGFR, so side effects in skin and gut are milder. With EGFR blocked, MAPK and PI3K-AKT signals fall and tumor cells stop dividing and die. It crosses the blood-brain barrier well, which helps control brain metastases. Resistance can come from the C797S mutation, which removes the binding cysteine, or from MET amplification.", "body": [["Lung tumor", "EGFR-mutant cancers, including those with T790M, shrink as the receptor is blocked."], ["Brain", "Good CNS penetration allows responses in brain metastases and lowers the chance of new ones."], ["Skin and nails", "Rash, dry skin and paronychia occur but are usually milder than with earlier EGFR drugs."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and mouth sores are common, usually mild."], ["Heart", "QTc prolongation and reduced heart pumping function (LVEF) can occur."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease or pneumonitis is uncommon but serious."], ["Blood", "Lymphocytes and platelets can decrease, and rare aplastic anemia has been reported."]], "journey": "Osimertinib is a tablet taken once daily with or without food. It is absorbed slowly, peaking in about six hours, and is highly protein bound. It spreads into tissues including the brain. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP3A4 and CYP3A5 into two active metabolites, AZ5104 and AZ7550. Strong CYP3A inducers lower its levels. Most leaves in feces, with a smaller part in urine. Its half-life is about 48 hours.", "sidefx": "Sparing wild-type EGFR is the reason rash and diarrhea are milder, but some normal EGFR is still hit. QTc prolongation comes from effects on cardiac potassium channels, and reduced LVEF may involve off-target effects on heart muscle. Interstitial lung disease is an inflammatory lung reaction seen with the class. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Osimertinib was designed so specifically that resistance often arises from a single change at its binding site, cysteine 797 becoming serine.", "src": ["Tagrisso (osimertinib) FDA label", "Cross DA et al. Cancer Discovery, 2014", "Soria JC et al. FLAURA, N Engl J Med 2018"]}, "dacomitinib": {"origin": "Dacomitinib, first called PF-00299804, was developed by Pfizer as a second-generation, irreversible pan-HER inhibitor. Like afatinib, it was designed to bind EGFR covalently and also block HER2 and HER4, with the goal of stronger and more lasting inhibition than erlotinib or gefitinib. Early trials in unselected patients were disappointing, but the ARCHER 1050 trial compared it directly with gefitinib in EGFR-mutant lung cancer and showed longer progression-free survival. The FDA approved it as Vizimpro in 2018 for first-line treatment of NSCLC with EGFR exon 19 deletions or L858R mutations.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Pfizer develops dacomitinib as an irreversible pan-HER inhibitor."], ["2017", "The ARCHER 1050 trial shows benefit over gefitinib in EGFR-mutant NSCLC."], ["2018", "FDA approves dacomitinib for first-line EGFR-mutant NSCLC."]], "cell": "Dacomitinib enters the ATP-binding site of EGFR (ErbB1), HER2 (ErbB2) and HER4 (ErbB4) and forms a covalent bond with a cysteine near the pocket entrance. This permanently inactivates each receptor molecule it binds. Without autophosphorylation, receptor dimers cannot recruit signaling proteins, so MAPK and PI3K-AKT pathways shut down and EGFR-dependent tumor cells arrest and die. Because it is a strong inhibitor of wild-type EGFR as well, it affects normal skin and gut cells. It is not effective against the T790M mutation at doses people can tolerate.", "body": [["Lung tumor", "EGFR exon 19 deletion and L858R tumors shrink as signaling stops."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is very common and can be severe."], ["Skin", "Rash, dry skin and exfoliative reactions occur from wild-type EGFR blockade."], ["Nails and mouth", "Paronychia and stomatitis are frequent because nail folds and mouth lining depend on EGFR for repair."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease can occur and can be fatal."], ["Metabolism", "Loss of appetite and weight loss are common."]], "journey": "Dacomitinib is a tablet taken once daily with or without food. It is absorbed slowly, peaking in about six hours, and is about 98 percent protein bound. Proton pump inhibitors lower its absorption. The liver breaks it down by oxidation, mainly through CYP2D6 and CYP3A4, and by glutathione conjugation. Most leaves in feces. Its half-life is long, about 70 hours. Dacomitinib itself strongly inhibits CYP2D6, raising levels of drugs cleared by that enzyme.", "sidefx": "Like other pan-HER inhibitors, dacomitinib blocks normal EGFR in skin and gut, which explains the rash, nail problems and diarrhea. Sun protection and moisturizers are used because damaged skin barrier is more sensitive. Severe diarrhea can lead to dehydration. Interstitial lung disease is a class effect. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Vizimpro (dacomitinib) FDA label", "Wu YL et al. ARCHER 1050, Lancet Oncology 2017"]}, "lazertinib": {"origin": "Lazertinib was discovered by Genosco, a US research subsidiary of the Korean company Oscotec, and was developed by the Korean company Yuhan Corporation, which licensed global rights to Janssen in 2018. Like osimertinib, it is a third-generation, mutant-selective EGFR inhibitor that also covers T790M and enters the brain well. It was first approved in South Korea in 2021. Janssen paired it with amivantamab, an antibody that binds both EGFR and MET on the outside of cancer cells. After the MARIPOSA trial, the FDA approved lazertinib as Lazcluze in 2024 in combination with amivantamab for first-line EGFR-mutant NSCLC. In the US it is approved only with amivantamab.", "timeline": [["2018", "Yuhan licenses lazertinib to Janssen."], ["2021", "Lazertinib is first approved in South Korea."], ["2024", "FDA approves lazertinib with amivantamab for first-line EGFR-mutant NSCLC."]], "cell": "Lazertinib is a third-generation EGFR tyrosine kinase inhibitor. It binds the ATP pocket of mutant EGFR and forms a covalent bond with cysteine 797, irreversibly blocking sensitizing mutations (exon 19 deletion, L858R) and T790M while relatively sparing wild-type EGFR. This shuts down intracellular MAPK and PI3K-AKT signals. Its partner, amivantamab, is a bispecific antibody that binds the outside of EGFR and MET, blocks ligand binding, triggers receptor breakdown and recruits immune cells to attack the tumor. Together they block EGFR from outside and inside the cell and also cover MET signaling, a common escape route for resistance.", "body": [["Lung tumor", "EGFR-mutant tumors shrink from combined inside and outside blockade of EGFR and MET."], ["Brain", "Lazertinib reaches the brain and can control brain metastases."], ["Skin and nails", "The combination causes frequent rash, acne-like eruptions and paronychia."], ["Blood vessels", "Blood clots in veins (DVT and pulmonary embolism) are common with the combination, so preventive anticoagulation is used early."], ["Tissues", "Edema and low albumin occur, partly from MET blockade by amivantamab."], ["Lungs and liver", "Pneumonitis and liver enzyme rises can occur."]], "journey": "Lazertinib is a tablet taken once daily, peaking in about two to four hours. It is highly protein bound and enters the brain. It is cleared mainly by glutathione conjugation and by CYP3A4, and leaves mostly in feces. Its half-life is long, about three and a half to four days. Amivantamab is given into a vein and is cleared like other antibodies.", "sidefx": "Most skin and nail effects arise from combined EGFR blockade, as amivantamab blocks normal skin EGFR from the outside. The high rate of venous blood clots with the combination is why the label describes preventive anticoagulation for the first four months. Edema reflects MET blockade. Lung inflammation is a class effect of EGFR inhibitors. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Lazcluze (lazertinib) FDA label", "Cho BC et al. MARIPOSA, N Engl J Med 2024"]}, "sunvozertinib": {"origin": "EGFR exon 20 insertion mutations make up a minority of EGFR-mutant lung cancers, and they resisted most EGFR pills because the insertion changes the shape of the kinase so drugs that fit common mutants do not fit well. Sunvozertinib was designed by the Chinese company Dizal Pharmaceutical as an irreversible EGFR inhibitor that binds exon 20 insertion mutants while sparing normal EGFR. It was first approved in China in 2023. After the WU-KONG1 studies, the FDA granted it accelerated approval as Zegfrovy in 2025 for advanced NSCLC with EGFR exon 20 insertions after platinum chemotherapy, making it an oral option for this group.", "timeline": [["2023", "Sunvozertinib is first approved in China."], ["2025", "FDA grants accelerated approval for EGFR exon 20 insertion NSCLC after platinum chemotherapy."]], "cell": "In most EGFR mutations, such as exon 19 deletions, the kinase is pushed into an active shape that binds drugs well. Exon 20 insertions add a few amino acids right after the C-helix, which wedges the kinase into an active shape but leaves the drug-binding pocket crowded and similar to the normal receptor. Older drugs therefore could not block the mutant without also blocking normal EGFR heavily. Sunvozertinib was designed to fit this altered pocket and to bind irreversibly, forming a covalent bond with the kinase, with selectivity for exon 20 insertion mutants over wild-type EGFR. With the mutant receptor blocked, downstream MAPK and PI3K-AKT signals fall and the tumor cells stop growing and die.", "body": [["Lung tumor", "NSCLC with EGFR exon 20 insertions can shrink as the mutant receptor is blocked."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is the most common side effect, reflecting partial normal EGFR inhibition in the gut lining."], ["Skin and nails", "Rash and paronychia are common, from partial blockade of normal skin EGFR."], ["Mouth", "Stomatitis (mouth sores) occurs because the mouth lining also relies on EGFR to renew itself."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease or pneumonitis can occur."], ["Eyes", "Keratitis and other eye problems are listed in the label."]], "journey": "Sunvozertinib is a tablet taken once daily with food. It reaches peak levels over several hours and is highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP3A, so strong CYP3A inhibitors and inducers change exposure. It is eliminated mostly in feces. Its long half-life supports once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Its selectivity for exon 20 insertion mutants is good but not complete, so some normal EGFR is blocked in skin and gut, causing diarrhea, rash and nail inflammation. Lung inflammation is a shared risk with EGFR inhibitors. There is no antidote; side effects are managed with dose holds and supportive care.", "src": ["Zegfrovy (sunvozertinib) FDA label", "FDA accelerated approval announcement, 2025"]}, "zongertinib": {"origin": "HER2 mutations, distinct from the HER2 amplification seen in breast cancer, occur in a small share of lung cancers. Earlier pan-HER pills also blocked EGFR, causing heavy rash and diarrhea that limited the doses people could take. Boehringer Ingelheim designed zongertinib as an irreversible HER2 inhibitor that largely spares EGFR. After the Beamion LUNG-1 trial, the FDA granted it accelerated approval as Hernexeos in 2025 for previously treated non-squamous NSCLC with HER2 tyrosine kinase domain mutations, making it one of the first oral drugs for this group. In 2026 the indication was expanded to include patients who had not had prior treatment.", "timeline": [["2010s", "Boehringer Ingelheim develops zongertinib as an EGFR-sparing HER2 inhibitor."], ["2025", "FDA grants accelerated approval for previously treated HER2-mutant NSCLC."], ["2026", "The FDA expands the indication to first-line treatment."]], "cell": "HER2 (ERBB2) is a member of the EGFR family. It has no ligand of its own but is the favored partner for other family members. Activating mutations in its tyrosine kinase domain, including exon 20 insertions, switch it on constantly and drive MAPK and PI3K-AKT signaling. Zongertinib binds the HER2 kinase domain and forms an irreversible covalent bond, inhibiting both normal and mutant HER2. Its design gives strong selectivity for HER2 over wild-type EGFR. With HER2 signaling blocked, tumor cells stop proliferating and undergo apoptosis. Sparing EGFR means skin and gut cells keep most of their normal EGFR signaling.", "body": [["Lung tumor", "HER2-mutant non-squamous NSCLC can shrink as HER2 signaling is shut down."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises are among the main toxicities, so liver tests are checked."], ["Heart", "Because HER2 helps heart muscle cope with stress, reduced pumping function (LVEF) can occur."], ["Gut and skin", "Diarrhea and rash occur but are less frequent than with pan-HER drugs that block EGFR."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease or pneumonitis can occur."]], "journey": "Zongertinib is a tablet taken once daily, peaking in about two hours. It is more than 99 percent protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP3A4 and CYP3A5 oxidation, plus conjugation, so strong CYP3A inducers lower its levels. Most leaves in feces. Its effective half-life is about 12 hours.", "sidefx": "HER2 signaling protects heart muscle cells, so blocking it can lower LVEF, as seen with HER2 antibodies. Liver toxicity is a direct effect on liver cells. Because EGFR is largely spared, skin and gut toxicity is milder than with older pan-HER inhibitors. Lung inflammation is a shared risk. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Hernexeos (zongertinib) FDA label", "FDA approval announcements, 2025 and 2026"]}, "sevabertinib": {"origin": "Sevabertinib, developed by Bayer under the code BAY 2927088, is another oral drug aimed at lung cancers driven by HER2 mutations. It was designed to block HER2 tyrosine kinase domain mutations, including exon 20 insertions, and it also has activity against EGFR. Based on response rates and duration of response in the SOHO-01 study, the FDA granted it accelerated approval as Hyrnuo in November 2025 for previously treated patients with HER2-mutant non-squamous NSCLC. It joined zongertinib as one of the first oral targeted drugs for this subgroup.", "timeline": [["2020s", "Bayer develops sevabertinib (BAY 2927088) for HER2-mutant lung cancer."], ["2025", "FDA grants accelerated approval for previously treated HER2-mutant non-squamous NSCLC."]], "cell": "Sevabertinib is a tyrosine kinase inhibitor that binds the kinase domain of HER2 (ERBB2). Activating HER2 mutations, especially exon 20 insertions, lock the kinase in an active state, so it constantly signals through RAS-RAF-MEK-ERK and PI3K-AKT pathways. Sevabertinib blocks this kinase activity, preventing phosphorylation of HER2 and its partners and cutting off downstream growth and survival signals, so the tumor cells stop dividing and die. It also inhibits EGFR, including some EGFR mutants. This EGFR activity is part of why diarrhea, rash and nail inflammation are common.", "body": [["Lung tumor", "HER2-mutant non-squamous NSCLC can shrink as HER2 signaling is blocked."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is very common and can be severe, sometimes with low potassium."], ["Skin and nails", "Rash and paronychia reflect EGFR inhibition in skin."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise, so liver tests are reviewed during treatment."], ["Heart", "Reduced heart pumping function can occur, as with other HER2-targeting drugs."], ["Lungs and eyes", "Interstitial lung disease, keratitis and pancreatic enzyme rises are listed in the label."]], "journey": "Sevabertinib is a tablet taken twice daily with food. It is absorbed over several hours and is highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down through CYP3A, so strong CYP3A inhibitors are avoided or the dose is reduced. It also inhibits CYP3A and P-glycoprotein itself, which affects other drugs. Most is eliminated in feces.", "sidefx": "Because it blocks EGFR along with HER2, sevabertinib affects normal skin and gut lining, which explains diarrhea, rash and nail changes. Severe diarrhea can cause dehydration and low potassium. HER2 blockade can reduce heart function. Lung inflammation and liver injury are shared class risks. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Hyrnuo (sevabertinib) FDA label", "Bayer FDA approval announcement, 2025"]}, "lapatinib": {"origin": "HER2 was linked to aggressive breast cancer in 1987, when Dennis Slamon and colleagues showed that tumors with extra copies of the HER2 gene had worse outcomes. That led to the antibody trastuzumab in 1998. Scientists at GlaxoSmithKline took another approach and built a small molecule that blocks the HER2 and EGFR kinases from inside the cell. Because it acts inside the cell, it could still work when trastuzumab failed. The FDA approved lapatinib as Tykerb in 2007 with capecitabine for HER2-positive metastatic breast cancer after trastuzumab, and in 2010 with letrozole for hormone receptor-positive, HER2-positive disease. It was the first oral HER2-targeted drug.", "timeline": [["1987", "HER2 gene amplification is linked to poor outcomes in breast cancer."], ["1998", "Trastuzumab is approved for HER2-positive breast cancer."], ["2007", "FDA approves lapatinib with capecitabine."], ["2010", "Lapatinib is approved with letrozole for HR-positive, HER2-positive disease."]], "cell": "Lapatinib is a reversible, ATP-competitive inhibitor of the intracellular kinase domains of EGFR (ErbB1) and HER2 (ErbB2). In HER2-amplified breast cancer, huge numbers of HER2 receptors form dimers that signal constantly through MAPK and PI3K-AKT, driving growth. Lapatinib binds the inactive form of the kinase and blocks autophosphorylation, cutting off these signals and causing growth arrest and apoptosis. Because it works inside the cell, it still blocks p95HER2, a truncated HER2 that has lost the outside part where trastuzumab binds. Its EGFR inhibition explains some of its skin and gut effects. As a small molecule, it reaches the brain somewhat, unlike large antibodies.", "body": [["Breast tumor", "HER2-overexpressing breast cancers slow or shrink, including some resistant to trastuzumab."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is very common and can be severe, reflecting EGFR blockade in the gut lining."], ["Skin", "Rash and, with capecitabine, hand-foot syndrome are common."], ["Liver", "Serious liver injury can occur and has caused deaths, so liver tests are checked."], ["Heart", "LVEF can decrease, and QT prolongation can occur."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease is rare but serious."]], "journey": "Lapatinib is taken by mouth on an empty stomach, at least an hour before or after food, because a meal greatly increases absorption. Absorption is variable and peaks in about four hours. It is over 99 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it extensively through CYP3A4 and CYP3A5, so strong inhibitors and inducers change exposure. Most leaves in feces. Its half-life with repeated dosing is about 24 hours.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea and rash come from EGFR blockade in normal gut and skin. Liver injury, the subject of the boxed warning, appears to be partly immune mediated and is linked to certain HLA types. HER2 helps heart muscle tolerate stress, so blocking it can reduce LVEF, though less than with trastuzumab. QT prolongation reflects potassium channel effects. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Tykerb (lapatinib) FDA label", "Slamon DJ et al. Science, 1987", "Geyer CE et al. N Engl J Med, 2006"]}, "neratinib": {"origin": "Neratinib was discovered at Wyeth, under the code HKI-272, as an irreversible inhibitor of HER2 and EGFR. Pfizer acquired Wyeth in 2009 and later licensed the drug to Puma Biotechnology in 2011. Puma tested whether a year of neratinib after standard trastuzumab could lower the chance of breast cancer returning. The ExteNET trial showed a modest reduction in recurrence, and the FDA approved neratinib as Nerlynx in 2017 for extended adjuvant treatment of early-stage HER2-positive breast cancer. In 2020 it was approved with capecitabine for advanced HER2-positive breast cancer after two or more HER2-directed regimens.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Wyeth develops neratinib (HKI-272)."], ["2011", "Pfizer licenses neratinib to Puma Biotechnology."], ["2017", "FDA approves neratinib for extended adjuvant therapy in HER2-positive breast cancer."], ["2020", "Neratinib is approved with capecitabine for metastatic HER2-positive breast cancer."]], "cell": "Neratinib binds the ATP pocket of EGFR (HER1), HER2 and HER4 and forms a covalent bond with a conserved cysteine, permanently inactivating each receptor it binds. In HER2-amplified breast cancer cells, this stops autophosphorylation and the downstream MAPK and PI3K-AKT signals that drive proliferation and survival, so the cells stop dividing and die. Irreversible binding means signaling stays off until new receptor protein is made. Because it also hits EGFR in the intestine, it increases chloride and water secretion into the gut and causes secretory diarrhea. It is not specific to any phase of the cell cycle.", "body": [["Breast cancer cells", "HER2-driven cells lose their growth signals, lowering the chance of recurrence after trastuzumab."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea affects most people, especially in the first month, and can cause dehydration and kidney injury."], ["Electrolytes", "Severe diarrhea leads to low potassium and other electrolyte losses."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise, so liver tests are monitored."], ["Whole body", "Nausea, vomiting, fatigue, abdominal pain and decreased appetite are common."]], "journey": "Neratinib is a tablet taken once daily with food, peaking in about two to eight hours. It is over 98 percent protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors and inducers are avoided. Acid-reducing drugs lower its absorption. Nearly all leaves in feces. Its half-life is about 7 to 17 hours.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea is the defining side effect and comes from EGFR blockade in gut cells, which increases fluid secretion. That is why the label describes loperamide prophylaxis for the first cycles or a gradual dose escalation. Untreated, severe diarrhea leads to dehydration, low potassium and kidney failure. Liver toxicity is a direct effect on hepatocytes. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Nerlynx (neratinib) FDA label", "Chan A et al. ExteNET, Lancet Oncology 2016"]}, "tucatinib": {"origin": "Tucatinib was discovered at Array BioPharma in Colorado as a HER2 inhibitor that hardly touches EGFR, with the aim of avoiding the rash and diarrhea that limited lapatinib and neratinib. It was licensed to Oncothyreon, later renamed Cascadian Therapeutics, and then acquired by Seattle Genetics (Seagen) in 2018. The HER2CLIMB trial added it to trastuzumab and capecitabine in heavily treated HER2-positive breast cancer, including patients with active brain metastases, and showed longer survival. The FDA approved it as Tukysa in 2020. In 2023 it gained accelerated approval with trastuzumab for HER2-positive, RAS wild-type colorectal cancer. Pfizer acquired Seagen in 2023.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Array BioPharma discovers tucatinib as a HER2-selective inhibitor."], ["2018", "Seattle Genetics acquires Cascadian Therapeutics and tucatinib."], ["2020", "FDA approves tucatinib with trastuzumab and capecitabine for metastatic HER2-positive breast cancer."], ["2023", "Accelerated approval is granted for HER2-positive colorectal cancer with trastuzumab."]], "cell": "Tucatinib is a reversible, ATP-competitive inhibitor of the HER2 (ERBB2) tyrosine kinase. In HER2-amplified cancer cells, overexpressed HER2 forms dimers, especially with HER3, that constantly signal through the MAPK and PI3K-AKT pathways. Tucatinib blocks phosphorylation of HER2 and HER3, shutting down ERK and AKT signals, so cells stop dividing and undergo apoptosis. It is highly selective, with far weaker activity against EGFR than lapatinib or neratinib, so normal skin and gut EGFR signaling is largely spared. As a small molecule, it crosses into the brain, which helps treat brain metastases. It works together with trastuzumab, which blocks HER2 from outside and recruits immune cells, giving dual inside and outside blockade.", "body": [["Breast and colorectal tumors", "HER2-driven tumors slow or shrink when tucatinib is combined with trastuzumab, with or without capecitabine."], ["Brain", "Tucatinib enters the brain and can shrink HER2-positive brain metastases."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is common and can be severe, though less tied to EGFR than with other HER2 pills."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes and bilirubin can rise, so liver tests are checked."], ["Kidney lab tests", "Serum creatinine rises because tucatinib blocks the MATE1 and MATE2-K transporters that secrete creatinine, without a true fall in kidney filtration."], ["Skin", "Hand-foot syndrome seen in the regimen mainly comes from capecitabine."]], "journey": "Tucatinib is a tablet taken twice daily with or without food. It peaks in about two hours and is about 97 percent protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP2C8, with some CYP3A, so strong CYP2C8 inhibitors such as gemfibrozil raise its levels. It is itself a strong CYP3A inhibitor, raising levels of drugs cleared by that enzyme. Most leaves in feces. Its half-life is about eight and a half hours.", "sidefx": "Because tucatinib spares EGFR, acne-like rash is uncommon, but diarrhea still occurs and can lead to dehydration and kidney injury. Liver toxicity is a direct effect on hepatocytes. The creatinine rise is a lab effect from blocked tubular secretion rather than true kidney damage, which matters when judging kidney function. There is no antidote.", "fun": "HER2CLIMB was one of the few major breast cancer trials to enroll patients with active, untreated or progressing brain metastases.", "src": ["Tukysa (tucatinib) FDA label", "Murthy RK et al. HER2CLIMB, N Engl J Med 2020"]}, "crizotinib": {"origin": "Crizotinib began as a MET inhibitor. At Pfizer in La Jolla, a team led by medicinal chemist J. Jean Cui used structure-based design to make PF-02341066, which also strongly blocked ALK. ALK had first been linked to cancer in 1994 as the NPM-ALK fusion in anaplastic large cell lymphoma. In 2007 Hiroyuki Mano's group in Japan discovered the EML4-ALK fusion in a subset of lung cancers. Pfizer quickly redirected crizotinib to these patients, and responses were dramatic. The FDA granted accelerated approval as Xalkori in 2011, only about four years after the fusion was found, alongside a companion diagnostic test. ROS1-positive lung cancer was added in 2016.", "timeline": [["1994", "The NPM-ALK fusion is identified in anaplastic large cell lymphoma."], ["2007", "The EML4-ALK fusion is discovered in non-small cell lung cancer."], ["2011", "FDA grants accelerated approval for ALK-positive NSCLC."], ["2016", "Crizotinib is approved for ROS1-positive NSCLC."], ["2021", "Approval extends to ALK-positive ALCL in children and young adults."]], "cell": "In ALK-positive lung cancer, a chromosome inversion fuses part of EML4 to the kinase domain of ALK. The EML4 part makes the fusion protein cluster together, which switches the ALK kinase on permanently. The active kinase drives STAT3, PI3K-AKT and RAS-MAPK signals for growth and survival. Crizotinib competes with ATP in the ALK kinase pocket, blocking autophosphorylation and these downstream pathways, so the tumor cells stop dividing and die. ROS1 has a kinase domain similar to ALK, so ROS1 fusions are also blocked, and crizotinib inhibits MET. Resistance develops through new ALK mutations such as L1196M and G1202R, through other bypass pathways, and often through brain relapse because crizotinib is pumped out of the CNS by P-glycoprotein.", "body": [["Lung tumor", "ALK- and ROS1-rearranged cancers often shrink rapidly once the fusion kinase is blocked."], ["Eyes", "Most people notice visual effects such as flashes, trails of light or trouble adjusting to light, usually starting early and often mild."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises are common and severe hepatotoxicity has been fatal, so liver tests are checked often early on."], ["Heart", "Heart rate slows (bradycardia) and QT can lengthen."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea and constipation are common."], ["Lungs and kidneys", "Pneumonitis can occur, and complex kidney cysts have been reported."]], "journey": "Crizotinib is a capsule taken twice daily with or without food. It peaks in about four to six hours and is about 91 percent protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP3A4 and CYP3A5. It is also a time-dependent CYP3A inhibitor, raising levels of other CYP3A drugs. Most leaves in feces. Its half-life is about 42 hours. P-glycoprotein limits how much reaches the brain.", "sidefx": "The visual disturbances are thought to come from effects on retinal signaling, possibly through ALK or MET in the retina, and they are a known driving hazard in dim light. Bradycardia and QT prolongation reflect effects on cardiac pacemaker and potassium channels. Liver injury is a direct hepatocyte toxicity. Pneumonitis is an inflammatory reaction in the lungs. There is no antidote.", "fun": "J. Jean Cui, who led the design of crizotinib, later founded the company that developed repotrectinib, a next-generation ROS1 and TRK inhibitor.", "src": ["Xalkori (crizotinib) FDA label", "Soda M et al. Nature, 2007", "Kwak EL et al. N Engl J Med, 2010"]}, "ceritinib": {"origin": "Ceritinib was developed by Novartis as a second-generation ALK inhibitor to treat lung cancers that had become resistant to or could not tolerate crizotinib. In lab assays it was much more potent against ALK and worked against several crizotinib-resistance mutations. It moved quickly through trials: the FDA granted accelerated approval as Zykadia in 2014 for ALK-positive NSCLC after crizotinib, a few years after first human testing. In 2017 the approval extended to first-line treatment. A later study showed that taking it with food at a lower dose kept exposure similar while greatly reducing stomach and bowel side effects.", "timeline": [["2010s", "Novartis develops ceritinib as a second-generation ALK inhibitor."], ["2014", "FDA grants accelerated approval for ALK-positive NSCLC after crizotinib."], ["2017", "Ceritinib is approved for first-line ALK-positive NSCLC."]], "cell": "Ceritinib is an ATP-competitive inhibitor of the ALK tyrosine kinase, including the EML4-ALK fusion protein. In enzyme assays it is about 20 times more potent than crizotinib. By blocking ALK autophosphorylation, it shuts down STAT3, PI3K-AKT and MAPK signals, and the cancer cells stop growing and die. It works against several mutations that cause crizotinib resistance, such as L1196M and G1269A, but not G1202R. Ceritinib also inhibits IGF-1R, the insulin receptor and ROS1. Blocking the insulin receptor and IGF-1R interferes with glucose uptake and insulin signaling, which explains its tendency to raise blood sugar. It does not act on a specific phase of the cell cycle.", "body": [["Lung tumor", "ALK-positive cancers shrink, including many that progressed on crizotinib and some brain metastases."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea, vomiting and abdominal pain are very common; taking it with food reduces them."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises are frequent and can be severe."], ["Blood sugar", "Hyperglycemia can occur because insulin receptor and IGF-1R signaling is blocked."], ["Heart", "QT prolongation and slow heart rate can occur."], ["Pancreas and lungs", "Lipase can rise, pancreatitis can occur, and pneumonitis is a rare serious effect."]], "journey": "Ceritinib is taken by mouth once daily with food. It peaks in about four to six hours and is about 97 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP3A, so strong CYP3A inhibitors and inducers change exposure. It also inhibits CYP3A and CYP2C9. Most leaves in feces. The half-life is about 41 hours.", "sidefx": "The heavy GI toxicity seemed tied to high peak levels in the gut, which is why the label moved to a lower dose taken with food. Hyperglycemia comes from off-target blockade of insulin receptor and IGF-1R. QT prolongation and bradycardia reflect effects on cardiac ion channels and pacemaker cells. Liver injury is direct hepatocyte toxicity. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Zykadia (ceritinib) FDA label", "Shaw AT et al. N Engl J Med, 2014"]}, "alectinib": {"origin": "Alectinib was discovered by Chugai Pharmaceutical in Japan, a member of the Roche group. Chugai designed it to be highly selective for ALK and, importantly, not to be a substrate for P-glycoprotein, the pump that pushes many drugs out of the brain. This allowed it to reach brain metastases, a common site of relapse on crizotinib. It was first approved in Japan in 2014, and the FDA approved it as Alecensa in 2015 for ALK-positive NSCLC after crizotinib. The ALEX trial showed it beat crizotinib as initial therapy, leading to first-line approval in 2017. In 2024 it was approved as adjuvant treatment after surgery for ALK-positive lung cancer.", "timeline": [["2014", "Alectinib is first approved in Japan."], ["2015", "FDA approves alectinib for ALK-positive NSCLC after crizotinib."], ["2017", "Alectinib is approved for first-line ALK-positive NSCLC after the ALEX trial."], ["2024", "Approval extends to adjuvant therapy after tumor resection."]], "cell": "Alectinib is a highly selective, ATP-competitive inhibitor of ALK and also inhibits RET. It binds the ALK kinase pocket and stops autophosphorylation of EML4-ALK and other ALK fusions, cutting off STAT3 and PI3K-AKT survival signals so tumor cells die. It works against many crizotinib-resistance mutations, such as L1196M, though not G1202R. Its main metabolite, M4, is also active. Unlike crizotinib, alectinib is not pumped out by P-glycoprotein, so it reaches high levels in the brain and spinal fluid, which is why it controls and prevents brain metastases well.", "body": [["Lung tumor", "ALK-positive cancers shrink and stay controlled for a long time in many patients."], ["Brain", "High CNS levels allow it to treat and help prevent brain metastases."], ["Muscles", "Muscle aches with rising creatine kinase (CPK) can occur, and can be severe."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes and bilirubin often rise, so liver tests are checked every two weeks early on."], ["Heart", "Heart rate often slows (bradycardia), a shared effect of ALK inhibitors."], ["Blood and kidneys", "Anemia, including hemolytic anemia, and kidney impairment can occur."], ["Skin", "Photosensitivity means sunburn happens more easily."]], "journey": "Alectinib is a capsule taken twice daily with food, which improves its absorption. It peaks in about four to six hours. More than 99 percent is protein bound. The liver converts it through CYP3A4 into the active metabolite M4, and both contribute to the effect. Almost all leaves in feces, with very little in urine. The half-life is about 33 hours.", "sidefx": "Alectinib causes less nausea and diarrhea than earlier ALK drugs. Myalgia with CPK rises suggests a direct effect on muscle cells. Bradycardia reflects effects on cardiac pacemaker tissue. Liver injury and hemolytic anemia are direct toxicities, and photosensitivity reflects how the drug interacts with light in the skin. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Alecensa (alectinib) FDA label", "Peters S et al. ALEX, N Engl J Med 2017", "Wu YL et al. ALINA, N Engl J Med 2024"]}, "brigatinib": {"origin": "Brigatinib was designed at ARIAD Pharmaceuticals in Cambridge, Massachusetts, the same company that created ponatinib. Its chemists added an unusual dimethylphosphine oxide group, which improved potency and gave favorable drug properties. Brigatinib blocked ALK and many crizotinib-resistance mutations. The FDA granted accelerated approval as Alunbrig in April 2017 for ALK-positive NSCLC after crizotinib, shortly after Takeda acquired ARIAD. The ALTA-1L trial then showed it worked better than crizotinib as initial therapy, leading to first-line approval in 2020.", "timeline": [["2010s", "ARIAD designs brigatinib with a dimethylphosphine oxide group."], ["2017", "Takeda acquires ARIAD, and the FDA grants accelerated approval for brigatinib after crizotinib."], ["2020", "Brigatinib is approved for first-line ALK-positive NSCLC."]], "cell": "Brigatinib is an ATP-competitive inhibitor of ALK, including many mutant forms that resist crizotinib, such as L1196M and, to some degree, G1202R. It also inhibits ROS1, IGF-1R and some EGFR mutants. By blocking ALK autophosphorylation, it stops STAT3, AKT and ERK signaling, and the tumor cells stop growing and die. The dimethylphosphine oxide group acts as a strong hydrogen-bond acceptor that helps anchor the drug in the kinase pocket. It penetrates the brain well and controls brain metastases. It is not tied to a particular phase of the cell cycle.", "body": [["Lung tumor", "ALK-positive cancers shrink, including many that resisted crizotinib."], ["Brain", "Good CNS activity helps control brain metastases."], ["Lungs", "Early lung reactions such as cough, breathlessness and pneumonitis can occur in the first week, which is why a lower lead-in dose is used."], ["Blood pressure and heart", "Hypertension and slow heart rate can develop."], ["Muscles and pancreas", "Creatine kinase, lipase and amylase can rise."], ["Metabolism and eyes", "Blood sugar can rise, and visual disturbances can occur."]], "journey": "Brigatinib is a tablet taken once daily with or without food. It peaks in about one to four hours and is about 66 percent protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP2C8 and CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A inhibitors and inducers are avoided. Most leaves in feces, with some in urine. Its half-life is about 25 hours.", "sidefx": "The early pulmonary events are distinctive to brigatinib and usually appear within days of starting. Their cause is not fully understood, but they are less common with a lower lead-in week, which suggests they relate to rapid early drug exposure. Hypertension, bradycardia, CPK rises and hyperglycemia reflect off-target kinase effects, including IGF-1R and insulin pathways. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Alunbrig (brigatinib) FDA label", "Camidge DR et al. ALTA-1L, N Engl J Med 2018"]}, "lorlatinib": {"origin": "By the mid-2010s, two problems limited ALK inhibitors: brain relapse and the G1202R mutation, which resisted second-generation drugs. Pfizer chemists designed lorlatinib as a small macrocycle, a molecule whose atoms close into a ring. The ring shape kept it compact, helped it cross the blood-brain barrier and helped it fit the pocket even when G1202R was present. The FDA granted accelerated approval as Lorbrena in 2018 for ALK-positive NSCLC after other ALK inhibitors. The CROWN trial then showed a large benefit over crizotinib as initial therapy, and first-line approval followed in 2021.", "timeline": [["2010s", "Pfizer designs lorlatinib as a brain-penetrant macrocyclic ALK inhibitor."], ["2018", "FDA grants accelerated approval for ALK-positive NSCLC after prior ALK inhibitors."], ["2021", "Lorlatinib is approved for first-line ALK-positive NSCLC."]], "cell": "Lorlatinib is a third-generation, ATP-competitive ALK and ROS1 inhibitor. Its macrocyclic ring holds the molecule in a compact, rigid shape that fits tightly into the ALK ATP pocket. This lets it inhibit nearly all known single ALK resistance mutations, including the solvent-front G1202R mutation that resists alectinib, ceritinib and brigatinib. Blocking ALK autophosphorylation shuts down STAT3, AKT and ERK signals, so tumor cells stop dividing and die. The compact, low-polarity design and poor fit for efflux pumps let it reach high levels in the brain. Some tumors later escape through combinations of two or more ALK mutations.", "body": [["Lung tumor", "ALK-positive cancers shrink, including those resistant to earlier ALK drugs."], ["Brain", "High CNS levels give strong control of brain metastases, but also cause brain side effects such as mood, cognition, speech and sleep changes."], ["Blood lipids", "Cholesterol and triglycerides rise in most people, often enough to need lipid-lowering drugs."], ["Heart", "PR interval lengthening and AV block can occur."], ["Tissues and nerves", "Edema, weight gain and peripheral neuropathy are common."], ["Lungs and metabolism", "Pneumonitis, hypertension and high blood sugar can occur."]], "journey": "Lorlatinib is a tablet taken once daily with or without food. It peaks in about one to two hours and is about 66 percent protein bound. It crosses into the brain well. The liver breaks it down through CYP3A4 and UGT1A4, and it induces its own metabolism over time. Strong CYP3A inducers such as rifampin are contraindicated because they can cause severe liver toxicity. Elimination is through urine and feces. Its half-life is about 24 hours.", "sidefx": "The CNS effects are the flip side of its excellent brain penetration, possibly from action on kinases in neurons. Raised lipids are a striking class effect of lorlatinib whose exact mechanism is not settled. AV block reflects effects on cardiac conduction. Liver injury with strong CYP3A inducers is a dangerous drug interaction. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Lorbrena (lorlatinib) FDA label", "Shaw AT et al. CROWN, N Engl J Med 2020", "Johnson TW et al. J Med Chem, 2014"]}, "ensartinib": {"origin": "Ensartinib was developed by Xcovery Holdings, a company based in Florida, together with Betta Pharmaceuticals of China. It is an aminopyridazine ALK inhibitor that also blocks MET and ROS1. It was first approved in China in 2020. In the eXalt3 trial, it outperformed crizotinib as initial therapy for ALK-positive NSCLC, including control of brain metastases. The FDA approved it as Ensacove in December 2024 for adults with ALK-positive locally advanced or metastatic NSCLC who had not previously received an ALK inhibitor.", "timeline": [["2020", "Ensartinib is first approved in China."], ["2024", "FDA approves ensartinib for ALK-positive NSCLC not previously treated with an ALK inhibitor."]], "cell": "Ensartinib is an ATP-competitive inhibitor of the ALK tyrosine kinase, including the EML4-ALK fusion and several crizotinib-resistance mutations. It also inhibits MET and ROS1. Blocking ALK autophosphorylation shuts off STAT3, PI3K-AKT and MAPK signals that ALK-driven lung cancer depends on, so tumor cells stop growing and undergo apoptosis. It reaches the brain well enough to control many brain metastases. It does not act on a specific phase of the cell cycle.", "body": [["Lung tumor", "ALK-positive cancers shrink and stay controlled longer than with crizotinib."], ["Brain", "It can shrink brain metastases and delay new ones."], ["Skin", "Rash and itching are the most common side effects, and sun protection is advised."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises can occur, so liver tests are monitored."], ["Heart", "Heart rate can slow (bradycardia), as with other ALK inhibitors."], ["Muscles and metabolism", "Creatine kinase, blood sugar and uric acid can rise."]], "journey": "Ensartinib is a capsule taken once daily. It is absorbed over several hours and is highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A inhibitors and inducers change exposure. Most is eliminated in feces. Its long half-life supports once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Rash is more common with ensartinib than with most ALK drugs, and its exact cause is not fully explained; it likely reflects off-target kinase effects in skin. Liver injury, bradycardia, CPK rises and hyperglycemia are shared ALK inhibitor class effects. Pneumonitis is rare but serious. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Ensacove (ensartinib) FDA label", "Horn L et al. eXalt3, JAMA Oncology 2021"]}, "entrectinib": {"origin": "Entrectinib was discovered at Nerviano Medical Sciences, a research center near Milan, Italy, as an inhibitor of TRK, ROS1 and ALK kinases designed to cross into the brain. It was licensed to the San Diego company Ignyta, which ran broad basket trials enrolling patients by gene fusion rather than tumor type. Roche acquired Ignyta in 2018. The FDA approved entrectinib as Rozlytrek in 2019 for ROS1-positive metastatic NSCLC and, under accelerated approval, for solid tumors with NTRK gene fusions regardless of where in the body they began.", "timeline": [["2010s", "Nerviano Medical Sciences discovers entrectinib."], ["2013", "Ignyta licenses entrectinib and begins basket trials."], ["2018", "Roche acquires Ignyta."], ["2019", "FDA approves entrectinib for ROS1-positive NSCLC and NTRK fusion-positive solid tumors."]], "cell": "TRKA, TRKB and TRKC are receptor tyrosine kinases, encoded by NTRK1, NTRK2 and NTRK3, that normally respond to nerve growth factors in the nervous system. In some cancers, an NTRK gene fuses with another gene, creating a TRK fusion protein that is always active. ROS1 and ALK fusions work the same way in lung cancer. Entrectinib competes with ATP at the kinase site of TRKA, B and C, ROS1 and ALK. This blocks constant signaling through MAPK-ERK, PI3K-AKT and PLC-gamma pathways, causing cell-cycle arrest and apoptosis. It was designed to cross the blood-brain barrier and is not a strong P-gp substrate. Its main metabolite, M5, is also active.", "body": [["Tumors with fusions", "Cancers with NTRK or ROS1 fusions can shrink, regardless of the organ they started in."], ["Brain", "It reaches the brain and can shrink brain metastases."], ["Nervous system", "Blocking normal TRK signaling causes dizziness, taste changes, tingling and cognitive or mood changes."], ["Weight and appetite", "Weight gain is common, likely because TRKB helps the brain control appetite."], ["Heart", "Heart failure and QT prolongation can occur."], ["Bones and metabolism", "Fractures and high uric acid levels are reported."]], "journey": "Entrectinib is a capsule taken once daily with or without food. It peaks in about four to six hours and is more than 99 percent protein bound. The liver converts it through CYP3A4 to the active metabolite M5, so CYP3A inhibitors and inducers change exposure. Most leaves in feces with little in urine. The half-life is about 20 hours for entrectinib and about 40 hours for M5.", "sidefx": "Many side effects are on-target: TRK receptors are essential to normal nerve function, appetite and balance, so blocking them causes dizziness, weight gain, taste change and paresthesias. Heart failure is less common and its mechanism is unclear. Fractures may relate to TRK roles in bone. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Rozlytrek (entrectinib) FDA label", "Doebele RC et al. Lancet Oncology, 2020"]}, "larotrectinib": {"origin": "The first TRK cancer gene was found in 1986, when Mariano Barbacid's laboratory identified a fused trk oncogene in a colon cancer. Decades later, larotrectinib was discovered at Array BioPharma as a highly selective TRK inhibitor and was developed by Loxo Oncology, later with Bayer. Trials enrolled patients of any age with any tumor carrying an NTRK fusion, from infant fibrosarcoma to thyroid and lung cancers, and response rates were high. In 2018 the FDA approved larotrectinib as Vitrakvi for NTRK fusion-positive solid tumors, an early tissue-agnostic cancer approval after pembrolizumab in 2017.", "timeline": [["1986", "A trk oncogene fusion is identified in colon cancer."], ["2013", "Loxo Oncology licenses larotrectinib from Array BioPharma."], ["2018", "FDA approves larotrectinib for NTRK fusion-positive solid tumors in adults and children."], ["2019", "Eli Lilly acquires Loxo Oncology, while Bayer holds larotrectinib rights."]], "cell": "TRKA, TRKB and TRKC are receptors for the neurotrophins NGF, BDNF and NT-3 and normally guide nerve growth and survival. In NTRK fusion-positive cancers, a partner gene joins the TRK kinase domain and makes it constantly active without any neurotrophin. Larotrectinib is a highly selective ATP-competitive inhibitor of the three TRK kinases, with very little action on other kinases. It blocks TRK autophosphorylation and downstream MAPK, PI3K-AKT and PLC-gamma signaling, so the tumor cells stop growing and undergo apoptosis. Tumors that are driven by the fusion respond regardless of origin. Resistance can come from TRK solvent-front mutations such as TRKA G595R.", "body": [["Tumors with NTRK fusions", "Cancers in many organs shrink, often dramatically, in both children and adults."], ["Nervous system", "Dizziness, tingling, gait changes and cognitive or mood effects reflect blockade of normal TRK signals."], ["Weight", "Weight gain is common because TRKB helps control appetite."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes often rise, so liver tests are checked regularly."], ["Bones", "Fractures have been reported in adults and children."]], "journey": "Larotrectinib comes as capsules or an oral solution taken twice daily, which helps dosing in infants. It peaks in about one hour and is about 70 percent protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A inhibitors and inducers change its levels. It leaves through feces and urine. Its half-life is short, about three hours.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are on-target. TRK receptors keep normal neurons healthy and regulate balance, pain sensing and appetite, so blocking them causes dizziness, paresthesias and weight gain. Withdrawal of TRK blockade can cause pain in some patients. Liver enzyme rises are direct effects on hepatocytes. There is no antidote.", "fun": "NGF, the natural partner of TRKA, was discovered by Rita Levi-Montalcini, who shared the 1986 Nobel Prize with Stanley Cohen, the discoverer of EGF.", "src": ["Vitrakvi (larotrectinib) FDA label", "Drilon A et al. N Engl J Med, 2018", "Martin-Zanca D et al. Nature, 1986"]}, "repotrectinib": {"origin": "Repotrectinib was designed by J. Jean Cui, the chemist who led the design of crizotinib at Pfizer. In 2013 she co-founded TP Therapeutics, later Turning Point Therapeutics, in San Diego to build compact macrocyclic kinase inhibitors that could fit around resistance mutations that blocked earlier drugs. Repotrectinib was designed to inhibit ROS1 and TRK even with solvent-front mutations. Bristol Myers Squibb acquired Turning Point in 2022. The FDA approved repotrectinib as Augtyro in 2023 for ROS1-positive NSCLC and in 2024 for NTRK fusion-positive solid tumors.", "timeline": [["2013", "J. Jean Cui co-founds the company that becomes Turning Point Therapeutics."], ["2022", "Bristol Myers Squibb acquires Turning Point."], ["2023", "FDA approves repotrectinib for ROS1-positive NSCLC."], ["2024", "Repotrectinib gains accelerated approval for NTRK fusion-positive solid tumors."]], "cell": "Repotrectinib is a small, rigid macrocyclic ATP-competitive inhibitor of ROS1 and the TRK kinases TRKA, TRKB and TRKC; in lab studies it also inhibits ALK, JAK2 and SRC. Many earlier inhibitors stick out into the solvent front of the kinase pocket, so a bulky mutation there, such as ROS1 G2032R or TRKA G595R, blocks them. Repotrectinib's compact ring sits fully inside the pocket, avoiding those clashes. It also tolerates gatekeeper mutations. By blocking the fusion kinases, it shuts down MAPK and PI3K signals, and the tumor cells stop growing and die. It enters the brain well.", "body": [["Tumors with fusions", "ROS1-positive lung cancers and NTRK fusion tumors shrink, including many that resisted earlier inhibitors."], ["Brain", "CNS penetration allows responses in brain metastases."], ["Nervous system", "Dizziness is very common, along with unsteady walking, tingling and cognitive or mood changes from TRK blockade."], ["Taste", "Taste changes (dysgeusia) are frequent, also from loss of TRK signaling."], ["Muscles and bones", "Muscle weakness, CK rises and fractures can occur."], ["Lungs and liver", "Pneumonitis and liver enzyme rises are reported."]], "journey": "Repotrectinib is a capsule taken by mouth, once daily during a lead-in period and then twice daily. It peaks in about two to three hours and is about 95 percent protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP3A4, and it induces its own metabolism, so the half-life drops from about 50 hours after one dose to shorter at steady state. Most leaves in feces. Because it induces CYP3A, hormonal contraceptives can become less effective.", "sidefx": "Dizziness, taste change and paresthesias are on-target effects of blocking TRK in the nervous system, which is why they are more common than with ROS1-selective drugs. Falls can result from dizziness and ataxia. Pneumonitis, liver injury and CK elevation are class effects. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Augtyro (repotrectinib) FDA label", "Drilon A et al. TRIDENT-1, N Engl J Med 2024"]}, "taletrectinib": {"origin": "ROS1 fusions were identified as drivers in a small share of lung cancers in 2007, and crizotinib became the first drug for them. Many tumors later escaped through the G2032R solvent-front mutation or relapsed in the brain. Taletrectinib was first made at Daiichi Sankyo in Japan and was then developed by AnHeart Therapeutics, which was acquired by Nuvation Bio in 2024. It was designed to block ROS1, including G2032R, to reach the brain and to cause fewer TRK-related neurologic effects. After the TRUST-I and TRUST-II trials, the FDA approved it as Ibtrozi in 2025 for adults with advanced ROS1-positive NSCLC, both untreated and previously treated with a ROS1 inhibitor.", "timeline": [["2007", "ROS1 fusions are identified in non-small cell lung cancer."], ["2016", "Crizotinib becomes the first FDA-approved ROS1 inhibitor."], ["2024", "Nuvation Bio acquires AnHeart Therapeutics and taletrectinib."], ["2025", "FDA approves taletrectinib for ROS1-positive NSCLC."]], "cell": "In ROS1-positive lung cancer, a chromosome rearrangement fuses the ROS1 kinase domain to a partner such as CD74, creating a kinase that is always on. It drives MAPK-ERK and PI3K-AKT signals for growth and survival. Taletrectinib is an ATP-competitive inhibitor that binds the ROS1 kinase pocket and blocks autophosphorylation, cutting off these signals so the cells stop dividing and die. It is active against the solvent-front G2032R mutation, a common cause of resistance to crizotinib and entrectinib. It also inhibits TRK kinases, but with relative selectivity for ROS1 over TRKB, which is thought to explain fewer TRK-related brain side effects. It enters the central nervous system well.", "body": [["Lung tumor", "ROS1-positive cancers shrink in both treatment-naive and previously treated patients."], ["Brain", "Good CNS penetration allows responses in brain metastases."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises are very common and can be severe, so liver tests are followed closely."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea and vomiting are common."], ["Heart", "QTc prolongation can occur, so ECG and electrolytes are checked in people at risk."], ["Nervous system", "Dizziness and taste change occur, though less often than with less selective ROS1 and TRK drugs."], ["Lungs, muscles and bones", "Pneumonitis, CK rises with muscle pain, and fractures are reported."]], "journey": "Taletrectinib is a capsule taken once daily. It is absorbed over several hours and is highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP3A, so strong CYP3A inhibitors and inducers change exposure. Most is eliminated in feces. Its long half-life supports once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Liver injury and GI effects are the main problems and appear to be direct effects on liver cells and the gut. QTc prolongation reflects effects on cardiac ion channels. The dizziness and taste changes that do occur come from partial TRK blockade in the nervous system. Fractures and CK rises are shared ROS1 and TRK class effects. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Ibtrozi (taletrectinib) FDA label", "FDA approval announcement, 2025", "Rikova K et al. Cell, 2007"]}, "capmatinib": {"origin": "The MET gene was discovered in 1984 by George Vande Woude's laboratory at the National Cancer Institute, as part of a fusion oncogene found in a chemically treated cell line. Its partner, hepatocyte growth factor, is also called scatter factor because it makes cells spread and move. In 2015 groups showed that some lung cancers have mutations that cause MET exon 14 to be skipped, producing a MET receptor that is not broken down normally. Capmatinib was discovered at Incyte and licensed to Novartis in 2009. After the GEOMETRY mono-1 trial, the FDA granted accelerated approval as Tabrecta in 2020 for metastatic NSCLC with MET exon 14 skipping, the first drug for this group, and converted it to full approval in 2022.", "timeline": [["1984", "The MET oncogene is discovered at the National Cancer Institute."], ["2009", "Incyte licenses capmatinib to Novartis."], ["2015", "MET exon 14 skipping is recognized as a driver in lung cancer."], ["2020", "FDA grants accelerated approval for MET exon 14 skipping NSCLC."], ["2022", "Capmatinib receives full FDA approval."]], "cell": "MET is a receptor tyrosine kinase activated by hepatocyte growth factor (HGF). Exon 14 of the MET gene encodes the juxtamembrane region, including tyrosine 1003, which recruits the ubiquitin ligase Cbl. Cbl tags MET for destruction after it signals. When exon 14 is skipped, the receptor loses this off switch, stays on the cell surface longer and signals constantly. Capmatinib is a highly selective type Ib ATP-competitive inhibitor of MET. It blocks MET autophosphorylation and downstream ERK, AKT and STAT3 signaling, so tumor cells stop growing and die. It also inhibits the kidney transporters MATE1 and MATE2-K, which affects creatinine handling.", "body": [["Lung tumor", "NSCLC with MET exon 14 skipping shrinks, including brain metastases in some patients."], ["Tissues", "Peripheral edema, especially in the legs, is very common with MET inhibitors and can be long lasting."], ["Kidney lab tests", "Serum creatinine rises because tubular secretion of creatinine is blocked, not because filtration falls."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises can occur, so liver tests are checked every two weeks early on."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis is uncommon but can be fatal."], ["Pancreas and skin", "Amylase and lipase can rise, and photosensitivity is described."]], "journey": "Capmatinib is a tablet taken twice daily with or without food. It is absorbed quickly, peaking in one to two hours, and is about 96 percent protein bound. The liver breaks it down through CYP3A4 and aldehyde oxidase. Most leaves in feces, with a small portion in urine. Its half-life is about six and a half hours.", "sidefx": "Edema appears to be a class effect of MET inhibition, perhaps because HGF and MET help maintain blood vessel integrity and fluid balance; low albumin often accompanies it. The creatinine rise is from blocked MATE transporters. Liver and pancreatic enzyme rises are direct effects. Pneumonitis is an inflammatory reaction. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Scatter factor and hepatocyte growth factor were discovered separately and later turned out to be the same protein.", "src": ["Tabrecta (capmatinib) FDA label", "Wolf J et al. GEOMETRY mono-1, N Engl J Med 2020", "Cooper CS et al. Nature, 1984"]}, "tepotinib": {"origin": "Tepotinib was developed by Merck KGaA of Darmstadt, Germany (EMD Serono in the US), as a highly selective MET inhibitor. Like capmatinib, it targets lung cancers whose MET receptor has lost exon 14 and can no longer be switched off normally. Tepotinib was first approved in Japan in 2020, making it the first MET inhibitor approved anywhere for MET exon 14 skipping NSCLC. Based on the VISION trial, the FDA granted accelerated approval as Tepmetko in 2021, which was converted to full approval in 2024.", "timeline": [["2020", "Tepotinib is first approved in Japan for MET exon 14 skipping NSCLC."], ["2021", "FDA grants accelerated approval for MET exon 14 skipping NSCLC."], ["2024", "Tepotinib receives full FDA approval."]], "cell": "Normally, after HGF activates MET, the ubiquitin ligase Cbl binds tyrosine 1003 in the juxtamembrane region encoded by exon 14 and tags MET for breakdown. Mutations that cause exon 14 skipping remove this site, so MET stays active and keeps driving MAPK, PI3K-AKT and STAT3 pathways. Tepotinib is a highly selective type Ib ATP-competitive inhibitor that binds the MET kinase pocket. It blocks MET phosphorylation whether or not HGF is present, so downstream growth and survival signals stop and the tumor cells die. Like other MET inhibitors, it also inhibits kidney tubule transporters that secrete creatinine.", "body": [["Lung tumor", "NSCLC with MET exon 14 skipping shrinks, including some brain metastases."], ["Tissues", "Peripheral edema is the most common side effect and often worsens over months."], ["Blood proteins", "Albumin levels often fall, which can add to swelling."], ["Kidney lab tests", "Creatinine rises from blocked tubular secretion, without true loss of kidney function."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise, so liver tests are checked regularly."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis and pleural effusion can occur."]], "journey": "Tepotinib is a tablet taken once daily with food, which improves absorption. It is absorbed slowly, peaking in about eight hours, and is about 98 percent protein bound. The liver breaks it down through CYP3A4 and CYP2C8. Most leaves in feces. Its half-life is about 32 hours, which supports once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Edema and low albumin are class effects of MET inhibition, thought to reflect the role of HGF-MET signaling in vessel and kidney function. The creatinine rise reflects transporter blockade rather than kidney damage. Liver injury and pneumonitis are direct toxicities. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Tepmetko (tepotinib) FDA label", "Paik PK et al. VISION, N Engl J Med 2020"]}, "selpercatinib": {"origin": "The RET gene was discovered in 1985 by Masahide Takahashi and colleagues in Japan, who named it for being rearranged during transfection. Inherited RET mutations were linked to multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2 in 1993, and RET fusions were later found in thyroid and lung cancers. Older multikinase drugs hit RET weakly while also blocking VEGFR, causing heavy side effects. Loxo Oncology designed selpercatinib (LOXO-292) as a highly selective RET inhibitor, and Eli Lilly acquired Loxo in 2019. In 2020 the FDA approved it as Retevmo, the first selective RET inhibitor, for RET-altered lung and thyroid cancers. In 2022 it gained a tissue-agnostic approval for RET fusion-positive solid tumors.", "timeline": [["1985", "The RET gene is identified in Japan."], ["1993", "RET mutations are linked to multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2."], ["2019", "Eli Lilly acquires Loxo Oncology."], ["2020", "FDA approves selpercatinib for RET-altered lung and thyroid cancers."], ["2022", "Selpercatinib gains accelerated tissue-agnostic approval for RET fusion-positive solid tumors."]], "cell": "RET is a receptor tyrosine kinase used during development of nerves, kidneys and the thyroid's C cells. Cancers activate it in two main ways: gene fusions such as KIF5B-RET or CCDC6-RET, which make the kinase cluster and stay on, and point mutations such as M918T, which drive medullary thyroid cancer. Selpercatinib is a highly selective ATP-competitive inhibitor of wild-type RET, RET fusions and activating mutations, including gatekeeper V804L and V804M. It blocks RET autophosphorylation and downstream MAPK and PI3K signaling, so tumor cells stop growing and die. It has little VEGFR2 activity, so it avoids much of the toxicity of older multikinase drugs. It enters the brain.", "body": [["Lung and thyroid tumors", "RET-driven cancers shrink, and in medullary thyroid cancer the tumor markers calcitonin and CEA fall."], ["Brain", "It can shrink RET-positive brain metastases."], ["Blood pressure", "Hypertension is common and is controlled before treatment."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises are common and can be serious, so liver tests are checked every two weeks early on."], ["Mouth and gut", "Dry mouth, diarrhea, constipation and abdominal pain are frequent."], ["Heart", "QT prolongation can occur, so ECG and electrolytes are checked in people at risk."], ["Growing bones", "In children, the growth plate of the hip can slip (slipped capital femoral epiphysis)."]], "journey": "Selpercatinib is taken by mouth twice daily, as capsules or tablets. It peaks in about two hours and is about 97 percent protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP3A4, so strong inhibitors and inducers are avoided. Acid-reducing drugs lower absorption of the capsules. It leaves mostly in feces with some in urine. Its half-life is about 32 hours.", "sidefx": "Hypertension likely reflects residual effects on vascular kinases and is a shared feature of RET inhibitors. Liver toxicity is a direct effect. QT prolongation reflects ion channel effects. Growth plate problems in children may relate to RET roles in bone growth. Impaired wound healing is why it is held before elective surgery. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Retevmo (selpercatinib) FDA label", "Drilon A et al. N Engl J Med, 2020", "Takahashi M et al. Cell, 1985"]}, "pralsetinib": {"origin": "Pralsetinib, first called BLU-667, was designed by Blueprint Medicines in Cambridge, Massachusetts, as a highly selective RET inhibitor that could also block the gatekeeper mutations that resist older drugs. Blueprint partnered with Roche and Genentech in 2020. The FDA granted accelerated approval as Gavreto in September 2020 for RET fusion-positive NSCLC, followed later that year by approvals in RET-altered thyroid cancers. The US indication for RET-mutant medullary thyroid cancer was later withdrawn, and the current label covers RET fusion-positive NSCLC and RET fusion-positive thyroid cancer.", "timeline": [["1985", "The RET gene is discovered in Japan."], ["2020", "FDA grants accelerated approval to pralsetinib for RET fusion-positive NSCLC."], ["2020", "Approvals follow for RET-altered thyroid cancers."], ["2023", "The medullary thyroid cancer indication is withdrawn in the US."]], "cell": "RET fusions, such as KIF5B-RET in lung cancer and CCDC6-RET in thyroid cancer, join the RET kinase domain to a partner that makes the protein cluster, switching the kinase on permanently. Activating point mutations such as M918T do the same. Pralsetinib is a highly selective ATP-competitive inhibitor of wild-type RET, RET fusions and activating mutations, including gatekeeper V804L and V804M. Blocking RET autophosphorylation stops MAPK and PI3K signaling, so the RET-dependent cells stop dividing and die. It has limited VEGFR2 activity, which reduces the vascular toxicity of older multikinase inhibitors, but some off-target effects remain.", "body": [["Lung and thyroid tumors", "RET fusion-positive cancers shrink as the fusion kinase is blocked."], ["Blood pressure", "Hypertension is common and is controlled before starting."], ["Blood cells", "Lymphocytes, neutrophils and red cells often fall, which adds to infection risk."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis and pneumonia, sometimes fatal, have been reported."], ["Muscles and bones", "Musculoskeletal pain is one of the most common side effects."], ["Liver and minerals", "Liver enzymes rise, and phosphate and calcium can fall."]], "journey": "Pralsetinib is a capsule taken once daily on an empty stomach. It peaks in about two to four hours and is about 97 percent protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A inhibitors and inducers are avoided. Most leaves in feces. Its half-life is about 15 to 22 hours.", "sidefx": "Hypertension and bleeding reflect residual effects on vascular signaling. Cytopenias and infections, including pneumonia, may relate to off-target kinase effects on immune cells. Pneumonitis is an inflammatory lung reaction. Liver injury is a direct effect. Impaired wound healing is why it is held before elective surgery. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Gavreto (pralsetinib) FDA label", "Gainor JF et al. ARROW, Lancet Oncology 2021"]}, "sotorasib": {"origin": "RAS genes were among the first human cancer genes found, in 1982, and KRAS mutations drive many lung, colorectal and pancreatic cancers. For about 40 years KRAS was called undruggable because its surface seemed smooth, with no obvious pocket, and it binds GTP extremely tightly. In 2013 Kevan Shokat's lab at UC San Francisco showed that the G12C mutant, which swaps glycine for cysteine, could be targeted by molecules that bond to the new cysteine in a hidden pocket called switch II. Amgen built on that idea and developed sotorasib (AMG 510). In 2021 the FDA granted it accelerated approval as Lumakras for KRAS G12C-mutated NSCLC, the first KRAS-targeted drug. In 2025 it was approved with panitumumab for KRAS G12C colorectal cancer.", "timeline": [["1982", "Mutant RAS genes are identified as human cancer genes."], ["2013", "Shokat's lab reports covalent molecules targeting KRAS G12C."], ["2019", "Amgen reports sotorasib in Nature."], ["2021", "FDA grants accelerated approval for KRAS G12C NSCLC."], ["2025", "Sotorasib is approved with panitumumab for KRAS G12C colorectal cancer."]], "cell": "KRAS is a small GTPase that acts as an on-off switch. Bound to GTP it is on and activates RAF-MEK-ERK signaling; it turns itself off by hydrolyzing GTP to GDP. The G12C mutation impairs this hydrolysis, so the mutant spends more time in the on state, but it still cycles to the GDP-bound off state. Sotorasib binds a pocket under the switch II region that is open only in the GDP-bound form, and its reactive group forms a permanent covalent bond with cysteine 12. This traps KRAS G12C in the inactive state. MAPK signaling falls, the cells arrest in G1 and undergo apoptosis. Normal KRAS lacks the cysteine, so it is spared. In colorectal cancer, EGFR feedback reactivates the pathway, which is why sotorasib is paired with panitumumab there.", "body": [["Lung and colon tumors", "KRAS G12C-driven cancers shrink as MAPK signaling is cut off."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises and drug-induced liver injury are the main serious toxicities, more common after recent immune checkpoint inhibitor therapy."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and nausea are common, and diarrhea can occasionally be severe."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease or pneumonitis can occur."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue, musculoskeletal pain and cough are common."]], "journey": "Sotorasib is a tablet taken once daily. It peaks in about two hours and is about 89 percent protein bound. It is cleared mainly by nonenzymatic conjugation and by CYP3A, and it induces CYP3A, lowering levels of some other drugs. Acid-reducing drugs lower its absorption. Most leaves in feces. Its half-life is short, about five hours, but covalent binding keeps each KRAS molecule inactive until the cell makes new protein.", "sidefx": "Because normal KRAS is spared, on-target toxicity is limited. Liver injury appears to be partly immune related, which may explain why it is more frequent soon after checkpoint inhibitors. Diarrhea and nausea are common nonspecific effects. Pneumonitis is a shared risk of targeted lung cancer drugs. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The G12C mutation is common in lung cancers from smokers, because tobacco chemicals tend to cause the particular DNA change that creates it.", "src": ["Lumakras (sotorasib) FDA label", "Ostrem JM et al. Nature, 2013", "Canon J et al. Nature, 2019"]}, "adagrasib": {"origin": "Adagrasib (MRTX849) was developed by Mirati Therapeutics in San Diego, building on the discovery that the KRAS G12C mutant can be locked in its off state by molecules that bond to cysteine 12. Mirati tuned the drug to have a long half-life, so that KRAS G12C would stay inhibited around the clock, and to penetrate the brain. The FDA granted accelerated approval as Krazati in December 2022 for previously treated KRAS G12C-mutated NSCLC. In 2024 it gained accelerated approval with cetuximab for KRAS G12C colorectal cancer. Bristol Myers Squibb acquired Mirati in 2024.", "timeline": [["2013", "KRAS G12C is shown to be targetable by covalent switch II pocket inhibitors."], ["2022", "FDA grants accelerated approval for KRAS G12C NSCLC."], ["2024", "Bristol Myers Squibb acquires Mirati Therapeutics."], ["2024", "Adagrasib gains accelerated approval with cetuximab for KRAS G12C colorectal cancer."]], "cell": "KRAS cycles between an active GTP-bound state and an inactive GDP-bound state. The G12C mutation slows the switch-off step but still lets KRAS visit the GDP state. Adagrasib binds the switch II pocket that exists only in GDP-bound KRAS and forms an irreversible covalent bond with cysteine 12, locking the protein off. RAF-MEK-ERK signaling falls, and tumor cells arrest and undergo apoptosis. Normal KRAS has glycine at position 12 and is not bound. Its long half-life keeps inhibition steady as new KRAS protein is made. In colorectal cancer, blocking KRAS releases feedback through EGFR, so cetuximab is added to block that escape route.", "body": [["Lung and colon tumors", "KRAS G12C cancers shrink as MAPK signaling is cut off."], ["Brain", "It enters the brain and can shrink brain metastases."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are very common and can be severe."], ["Heart", "QTc prolongation can occur, so ECGs are checked in people at risk."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise, so liver tests are checked during the first months."], ["Kidneys and lungs", "Creatinine rises, and pneumonitis can occur."]], "journey": "Adagrasib is a tablet taken twice daily with or without food. It is absorbed slowly, peaking in about six hours, and is about 98 percent protein bound. The liver breaks it down through CYP3A4, but adagrasib is also a strong CYP3A4 inhibitor, so it slows its own breakdown and raises levels of other CYP3A drugs. Most leaves in feces. Its half-life is about 23 hours.", "sidefx": "GI toxicity is the most common problem and is usually managed with antiemetics and antidiarrheals. QTc prolongation reflects effects on cardiac potassium channels and is worsened by other QT-prolonging drugs or electrolyte loss. Liver enzyme rises and pneumonitis are shared risks of KRAS G12C inhibitors. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Krazati (adagrasib) FDA label", "Janne PA et al. N Engl J Med, 2022"]}, "dabrafenib": {"origin": "In 2002, scientists at the Wellcome Trust Sanger Institute in England, scanning cancer genomes, found that about half of melanomas carry a mutation in BRAF, most often V600E. This made BRAF one of the clearest targets in cancer. GlaxoSmithKline developed dabrafenib as a potent BRAF V600 inhibitor, and the FDA approved it as Tafinlar in 2013 for BRAF V600E melanoma. In 2014 it was approved together with the MEK inhibitor trametinib, a pairing that worked better and caused fewer skin cancers. Novartis acquired the drugs from GSK in 2015. Later approvals extended the combination to BRAF V600E lung cancer, anaplastic thyroid cancer, other solid tumors and pediatric low-grade glioma.", "timeline": [["2002", "BRAF mutations are reported in about half of melanomas."], ["2013", "FDA approves dabrafenib for BRAF V600E melanoma."], ["2014", "Dabrafenib plus trametinib is approved for BRAF V600 melanoma."], ["2015", "Novartis acquires GSK's oncology drugs, including dabrafenib."], ["2022", "The combination gains accelerated tissue-agnostic approval for BRAF V600E solid tumors."], ["2023", "Approval extends to BRAF V600E pediatric low-grade glioma."]], "cell": "BRAF is a serine-threonine kinase in the RAS-RAF-MEK-ERK (MAPK) pathway. The V600E mutation makes BRAF active as a monomer, without needing RAS, so it constantly phosphorylates MEK, which activates ERK and drives growth. Dabrafenib is an ATP-competitive inhibitor of mutant BRAF V600E, V600K and V600D, and at higher levels of wild-type BRAF and CRAF. Blocking mutant BRAF stops ERK signaling, causing G1 arrest and apoptosis. In cells without the mutation, such as normal skin cells with active RAS, the drug binds one RAF in a dimer and transactivates its partner, paradoxically increasing MAPK signaling. This causes skin growths and squamous cell cancers, and adding a MEK inhibitor blocks the paradoxical signal.", "body": [["Tumor cells", "BRAF V600-mutant melanoma, lung, thyroid and other cancers shrink when MAPK signaling is blocked."], ["Skin", "Paradoxical MAPK activation causes thickened skin, papillomas, keratoacanthomas and cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma, less often with trametinib."], ["Temperature regulation", "Fever is a hallmark, sometimes with chills, low blood pressure and kidney injury, especially with the combination."], ["Eyes", "Uveitis (inflammation inside the eye) can occur and causes pain, redness or blurred vision."], ["Heart", "Reduced pumping function (cardiomyopathy) is reported, mainly with the combination."], ["Blood", "Hemolytic anemia can occur in G6PD deficiency, and bleeding and blood sugar rises are reported."]], "journey": "Dabrafenib is a capsule taken twice daily on an empty stomach; a soluble tablet form exists for children. It peaks in about two hours and is about 99.7 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it through CYP2C8 and CYP3A4 to active hydroxy- and desmethyl-dabrafenib. It also induces CYP3A4 and CYP2C9, which can lower levels of other drugs, including hormonal contraceptives. It leaves mostly in feces. Its half-life is about eight hours, while the metabolites last longer.", "sidefx": "New skin cancers arise from paradoxical MAPK activation in normal skin cells that carry RAS mutations from sun damage. Fever is common and its mechanism is not fully known; it is thought to involve cytokine release. Severe febrile reactions can cause dehydration and kidney injury. Hemolysis in G6PD deficiency reflects the drug's sulfonamide structure. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Tafinlar (dabrafenib) FDA label", "Davies H et al. Nature, 2002", "Long GV et al. N Engl J Med, 2014"]}, "trametinib": {"origin": "Trametinib was discovered by Japan Tobacco, whose researchers found it in a cell-based screen. It turned out to be a potent MEK inhibitor, and GlaxoSmithKline licensed and developed it. MEK sits just downstream of BRAF in the MAPK pathway, so blocking it was a logical partner to BRAF inhibitors. In 2013 the FDA approved trametinib as Mekinist, the first MEK inhibitor, for BRAF V600E or V600K melanoma. In 2014 it was approved with dabrafenib. Novartis acquired it in 2015, and the combination later spread to BRAF V600E lung, thyroid and other solid tumors and pediatric glioma.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Japan Tobacco discovers trametinib, later developed by GlaxoSmithKline."], ["2013", "FDA approves trametinib, the first MEK inhibitor, for BRAF V600 melanoma."], ["2014", "Trametinib is approved with dabrafenib."], ["2018", "The combination is approved as adjuvant therapy for resected melanoma."], ["2022", "The combination gains accelerated tissue-agnostic approval for BRAF V600E solid tumors."]], "cell": "MEK1 and MEK2 are dual-specificity kinases that are activated by RAF and then phosphorylate ERK1 and ERK2. Trametinib is a reversible, allosteric inhibitor: instead of competing with ATP, it binds a pocket next to the ATP site and locks MEK in an inactive shape. This blocks both MEK activation by RAF and MEK's own kinase activity, so ERK is not phosphorylated, the cells arrest in G1 and undergo apoptosis. When combined with dabrafenib, it gives vertical blockade of the same pathway at two points, which deepens inhibition, delays resistance and blocks the paradoxical MAPK activation that BRAF inhibitors cause in normal skin. It is not used for colorectal cancer because it lacks activity there.", "body": [["Tumor cells", "BRAF V600-mutant cancers shrink, especially when trametinib is paired with dabrafenib."], ["Skin", "Acne-like rash, dry skin and paronychia occur because skin cells depend on MAPK signals."], ["Heart", "Reduced LVEF (cardiomyopathy) can occur, so heart function is checked."], ["Eyes", "Fluid can collect under the retina (serous retinopathy), and retinal vein occlusion can occur."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is common, and colitis or GI perforation is rare but serious."], ["Fluid and vessels", "Peripheral edema, hypertension, bleeding and blood clots are reported."]], "journey": "Trametinib is taken by mouth once daily on an empty stomach, as a tablet or, for children, an oral solution. It peaks in about one and a half hours and is about 97 percent protein bound. It is metabolized mainly by deacetylation, which does not depend on CYP enzymes, along with oxidation and glucuronidation. Most leaves in feces. Its half-life is long, about four to five days, so it takes weeks to reach steady state.", "sidefx": "The skin, eye and heart effects are on-target: MEK-ERK signaling helps keep the skin barrier, the retinal pigment epithelium and heart muscle healthy. Blocking it in the retina lets fluid gather under the retina, and in the heart it can weaken contraction. Gut toxicity reflects MAPK roles in the intestinal lining. Fever with dabrafenib is more frequent with the combination. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Mekinist (trametinib) FDA label", "Flaherty KT et al. N Engl J Med, 2012", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "vemurafenib": {"origin": "Vemurafenib was the first BRAF inhibitor approved. It was discovered at Plexxikon, a small company in Berkeley, California, using a fragment-based approach: chemists started with tiny molecules that bound weakly to kinases, solved their crystal structures, and built them up into a potent, selective inhibitor of BRAF V600E, called PLX4032. Roche and Genentech developed it further. In early trials, people with widespread melanoma saw rapid, sometimes dramatic tumor shrinkage. The FDA approved vemurafenib as Zelboraf in 2011 together with a companion test for the BRAF V600E mutation. It was later approved for Erdheim-Chester disease with BRAF V600 mutation and is used with cobimetinib in melanoma.", "timeline": [["2002", "BRAF mutations are found in about half of melanomas."], ["2000s", "Plexxikon discovers PLX4032 using fragment-based design."], ["2011", "FDA approves vemurafenib with a companion diagnostic for BRAF V600E melanoma."], ["2015", "Cobimetinib is approved for use with vemurafenib."], ["2017", "Vemurafenib is approved for BRAF V600 Erdheim-Chester disease."]], "cell": "BRAF V600E is a mutant kinase that signals as a monomer without needing RAS, constantly phosphorylating MEK and driving ERK-dependent growth. Vemurafenib is an ATP-competitive inhibitor that binds the active shape of BRAF V600E and blocks MEK phosphorylation. ERK activity falls, the melanoma cells arrest in G1 and die. In cells with normal BRAF and active RAS, such as sun-damaged skin cells, RAF proteins work as dimers. When vemurafenib binds one partner of a dimer, it transactivates the other, paradoxically increasing MAPK signaling. This causes rapid growth of keratoacanthomas and cutaneous squamous cell carcinomas. Adding a MEK inhibitor such as cobimetinib blocks this paradoxical signal and delays resistance.", "body": [["Melanoma", "BRAF V600E tumors can shrink within days to weeks, though resistance often develops without a MEK inhibitor."], ["Skin", "Paradoxical MAPK activation causes papillomas, thickened skin, keratoacanthomas and squamous cell carcinomas, plus rash and hair loss."], ["Skin and light", "Strong UVA photosensitivity causes sunburn even with short exposure."], ["Joints", "Joint pain is one of the most common side effects."], ["Heart", "QT prolongation can occur, so ECG and electrolytes are checked."], ["Liver, kidneys and eyes", "Liver enzyme rises, kidney injury and uveitis are reported."]], "journey": "Vemurafenib is a tablet taken twice daily. It peaks in about three hours and is over 99 percent protein bound. It is only slightly metabolized, with CYP3A4 playing a minor role. It inhibits CYP1A2 and P-glycoprotein and induces CYP3A4, so it affects many other drugs. Almost all leaves in feces. Its half-life is long, about 57 hours.", "sidefx": "Skin cancers and other skin growths come from paradoxical MAPK activation in cells that already carry RAS mutations from sun exposure. Photosensitivity is driven by UVA, which passes through window glass. QT prolongation reflects potassium channel effects. Arthralgia and Dupuytren's contracture are less well explained. Severe skin reactions such as SJS, TEN and DRESS are immune reactions. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Because vemurafenib's photosensitivity is driven by UVA light, people taking it can sunburn through a car or office window.", "src": ["Zelboraf (vemurafenib) FDA label", "Bollag G et al. Nature, 2010", "Chapman PB et al. BRIM-3, N Engl J Med 2011"]}, "cobimetinib": {"origin": "Cobimetinib was made in a lab, not found in nature. Scientists at the biotech company Exelixis in South San Francisco discovered it as a compound called XL518, built to block MEK, the kinase that sits just below RAF in the MAPK growth pathway. In 2006 Exelixis licensed it to Genentech, part of Roche. The key idea was vertical blockade: BRAF inhibitors alone such as vemurafenib worked fast in BRAF V600 melanoma, but tumors soon found ways to switch MEK back on. Hitting BRAF and MEK together gave longer responses in the coBRIM trial. FDA approved cobimetinib as Cotellic with vemurafenib in 2015. In 2022 it gained a second, single-agent approval for histiocytic neoplasms, rare diseases of immune cells that are often driven by MAPK pathway mutations.", "timeline": [["2006", "Exelixis licenses its MEK inhibitor XL518 to Genentech."], ["2015", "FDA approves Cotellic with vemurafenib for BRAF V600E or V600K melanoma."], ["2022", "FDA approves cobimetinib alone for histiocytic neoplasms."]], "cell": "Normal cells pass growth signals down a relay: RAS activates RAF, RAF phosphorylates MEK1 and MEK2, and MEK phosphorylates ERK, which enters the nucleus and switches on genes for cyclin D and cell division. In BRAF V600 melanoma, mutant BRAF fires all the time. Cobimetinib binds an allosteric pocket next to the ATP site of MEK1 and MEK2, so it does not compete with ATP. Binding locks MEK in a shape that cannot phosphorylate ERK. With ERK silent, cyclin D falls, cells stop in G1 and many undergo apoptosis. Paired with a BRAF inhibitor, the pathway is blocked at two steps, which delays resistance from MEK reactivation and blunts the paradoxical ERK activation that BRAF inhibitors cause in normal skin cells.", "body": [["Melanoma cells", "Tumors driven by mutant BRAF lose their main growth signal and shrink, and response lasts longer than with a BRAF inhibitor alone."], ["Skin", "Keratinocytes rely on MAPK signaling, so acne-like rash appears. Photosensitivity is common, especially with vemurafenib. Fewer new squamous skin cancers appear than with BRAF inhibitors alone."], ["Eyes", "The retinal pigment layer uses MEK-ERK signaling to pump fluid. Blockade lets fluid collect under the retina (serous retinopathy), and retinal vein occlusion can occur."], ["Heart", "ERK signaling supports heart muscle survival, so ejection fraction can fall, which is why heart function is checked before and during treatment."], ["Muscle", "Creatine kinase often rises, and rarely muscle breaks down (rhabdomyolysis)."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea and vomiting are common, linked to MAPK roles in the gut lining."]], "journey": "Cobimetinib is taken by mouth and peaks in a few hours. It binds well to plasma proteins and spreads widely in tissues. The liver clears it mainly by CYP3A oxidation and UGT2B7 glucuronidation, so strong CYP3A inhibitors and inducers change its levels a lot. Most of the drug leaves in the feces, with a smaller share in urine. Its half-life is long, a little under two days, which suits once-daily dosing on a three-weeks-on, one-week-off schedule.", "sidefx": "Most toxicities are on-target MEK effects in normal tissues that also need ERK signals. Rash and acneiform skin changes come from blocked keratinocyte signaling. Serous retinopathy reflects impaired fluid transport in the retinal pigment epithelium. Lower ejection fraction comes from loss of ERK survival signals in heart cells. Bleeding, liver injury and CK elevation are also listed. New skin cancers can still arise from paradoxical MAPK activation by the partner BRAF inhibitor. There is no antidote; care is supportive with dose holds.", "fun": "Adding a MEK inhibitor to a BRAF inhibitor actually lowers the rate of the new skin squamous cell cancers seen with BRAF inhibitors alone, because MEK sits downstream of the paradoxically activated RAF.", "src": ["Cotellic (cobimetinib) FDA label", "Larkin J et al. NEJM 2014 (coBRIM)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "encorafenib": {"origin": "Encorafenib was designed by chemists at Novartis as LGX818, a small molecule built to fit the ATP pocket of the mutant BRAF V600E kinase. BRAF mutations had been found in about half of melanomas in 2002 by researchers at the Wellcome Trust Sanger Institute, which made BRAF a prime target. Novartis later passed the rights to Array BioPharma, which paired encorafenib with its own MEK inhibitor, binimetinib. FDA approved the pair as Braftovi and Mektovi for BRAF V600 melanoma in 2018. Pfizer bought Array in 2019. In 2020 encorafenib plus the antibody cetuximab was approved for BRAF V600E colorectal cancer, and in 2023 the encorafenib and binimetinib pair was approved for BRAF V600E lung cancer.", "timeline": [["2002", "Sanger Institute researchers report BRAF mutations in many melanomas."], ["2018", "FDA approves encorafenib with binimetinib for BRAF V600 melanoma."], ["2019", "Pfizer acquires Array BioPharma."], ["2020", "FDA approves encorafenib with cetuximab for BRAF V600E colorectal cancer."], ["2023", "FDA approves encorafenib with binimetinib for BRAF V600E non-small cell lung cancer."]], "cell": "The V600E change swaps one amino acid in the activation loop of BRAF, so the kinase acts as an always-on monomer that phosphorylates MEK without any signal from RAS. Encorafenib is an ATP-competitive inhibitor that sits in the BRAF ATP pocket and stays bound much longer than older BRAF inhibitors, giving steady suppression of MEK and ERK phosphorylation. Less ERK means less cyclin D, G1 arrest and apoptosis. In BRAF wild-type cells with active RAS, the drug can bind one partner of a RAF dimer and transactivate the other, paradoxically raising ERK signaling. In colon cancer cells, BRAF blockade removes ERK negative feedback on EGFR, and EGFR then restarts the pathway, which is why cetuximab is added.", "body": [["Tumors", "BRAF V600 melanoma, lung and colon cancers lose their main growth driver and shrink, especially when MEK or EGFR is also blocked."], ["Skin", "Paradoxical MAPK activation in normal skin causes thick skin (hyperkeratosis), hand-foot reactions, keratoacanthomas and squamous cell skin cancers."], ["Joints and muscle", "Joint pain and muscle aches are common, and myopathy can occur."], ["Eyes", "Uveitis and other eye inflammation can occur, so new eye symptoms are checked."], ["Heart", "Ejection fraction can fall, and QT prolongation is listed, so heart tests are part of monitoring."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea and belly pain are frequent."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth, encorafenib is absorbed well and peaks in about two hours. It is moderately bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A4, with smaller roles for CYP2C19 and CYP2D6. It also induces CYP3A4, so it lowers levels of some other drugs, including hormonal contraceptives. Metabolites leave in roughly equal amounts in feces and urine. The plasma half-life is short, a few hours, but the drug's slow release from BRAF keeps the target blocked across a once-daily schedule.", "sidefx": "Skin growths and hyperkeratosis are the classic BRAF inhibitor effects caused by paradoxical ERK activation in wild-type keratinocytes that carry RAS signals. The MEK partner reduces this. Fatigue, joint pain and GI upset are common. Eye inflammation, reduced heart function, bleeding, liver injury and QT prolongation are listed warnings. BRAF inhibitors can also speed the growth of BRAF wild-type tumors, which is why a BRAF V600 test is required. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Compared with vemurafenib and dabrafenib, encorafenib causes less fever and sun sensitivity, which helps people stay on full doses.", "src": ["Braftovi (encorafenib) FDA label", "Davies H et al. Nature 2002 (BRAF mutations in cancer)", "Dummer R et al. Lancet Oncol 2018 (COLUMBUS)", "Kopetz S et al. NEJM 2019 (BEACON)"]}, "binimetinib": {"origin": "Binimetinib was discovered at Array BioPharma in Boulder, Colorado, a company known for designing small-molecule kinase inhibitors. Known in trials as MEK162, it was developed for years with Novartis before the rights returned to Array. Array then paired it with encorafenib, the BRAF inhibitor it had also acquired, and the COLUMBUS trial showed the combination worked better than a BRAF inhibitor alone in BRAF V600 melanoma. FDA approved binimetinib as Mektovi in 2018, always used with encorafenib. Pfizer acquired Array in 2019, and in 2023 the pair was also approved for BRAF V600E non-small cell lung cancer.", "timeline": [["2010s", "Array BioPharma and Novartis develop MEK162 in clinical trials."], ["2018", "FDA approves Mektovi with encorafenib for BRAF V600E or V600K melanoma."], ["2019", "Pfizer acquires Array BioPharma."], ["2023", "FDA approves the pair for BRAF V600E lung cancer."]], "cell": "Binimetinib is a reversible allosteric inhibitor of MEK1 and MEK2. It does not compete with ATP. Instead it binds a pocket next to the ATP site and holds MEK in an inactive shape, so MEK cannot phosphorylate its only known substrates, ERK1 and ERK2. In BRAF V600 tumors, where mutant BRAF drives MEK nonstop, this cuts ERK nuclear signaling, lowers cyclin D1, stops cells in G1 and triggers apoptosis. Paired with encorafenib, the pathway is blocked twice in a row. That makes it harder for tumors to escape by reactivating MEK and reduces the paradoxical ERK activation that a BRAF inhibitor causes in normal skin.", "body": [["Tumors", "Dual blockade with encorafenib slows growth of BRAF V600 melanoma and lung cancer longer than BRAF blockade alone."], ["Eyes", "Fluid can collect under the retina (serous retinopathy), and retinal vein occlusion and uveitis can occur, so eye exams are part of care."], ["Heart and vessels", "Ejection fraction can drop, and blood clots such as DVT and pulmonary embolism are listed warnings."], ["Skin", "Acne-like rash reflects loss of MAPK signaling in skin, while new squamous skin cancers are less frequent than with BRAF inhibitors alone."], ["Muscle", "Creatine kinase often rises, and rhabdomyolysis is a rare serious effect."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease is an uncommon but serious warning."]], "journey": "Binimetinib is taken by mouth twice a day and peaks within about two hours. It is highly bound to plasma proteins. The main clearance route is glucuronidation by UGT1A1, with smaller CYP1A2 and CYP2C19 roles, so it has fewer CYP3A drug interactions than many kinase inhibitors. About two thirds leaves in the feces and about one third in urine. The half-life is short, about three and a half hours, which is why it is dosed twice daily.", "sidefx": "Side effects mostly reflect MEK blockade in healthy tissue. The retinal pigment epithelium needs ERK signals to move fluid, so serous retinopathy is common but usually mild. Heart muscle depends on ERK survival signals, so ejection fraction can fall. Skin rash, swelling, diarrhea and CK rise are typical class effects. Clots, lung inflammation, liver injury and bleeding are listed warnings. If encorafenib is stopped for good, binimetinib is stopped too. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Mektovi (binimetinib) FDA label", "Dummer R et al. Lancet Oncol 2018 (COLUMBUS)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "tovorafenib": {"origin": "Tovorafenib is a lab-designed pan-RAF inhibitor that was first studied by Takeda as TAK-580 in adult cancers. In 2019 the newly formed company Day One Biopharmaceuticals licensed it, with the goal of developing it for children. The most common childhood brain tumor, pediatric low-grade glioma, is often driven by a BRAF fusion called KIAA1549-BRAF, and older BRAF inhibitors such as vemurafenib can actually switch the pathway on in fusion tumors. Tovorafenib, a type II RAF inhibitor that blocks RAF dimers and gets into the brain, was tested in the FIREFLY-1 trial. FDA granted accelerated approval as Ojemda in 2024 for relapsed or refractory pediatric low-grade glioma with a BRAF alteration.", "timeline": [["2010s", "The compound is studied by Takeda as TAK-580."], ["2019", "Day One Biopharmaceuticals licenses the drug for pediatric development."], ["2024", "FDA grants accelerated approval of Ojemda for BRAF-altered pediatric low-grade glioma."]], "cell": "RAF kinases (ARAF, BRAF and CRAF) normally work as dimers activated by RAS. A BRAF V600 mutation makes BRAF signal as a single monomer, while a BRAF fusion such as KIAA1549-BRAF loses its regulatory region and signals as a dimer. Type I BRAF inhibitors bind the active shape and block monomers, but in dimers they can transactivate the partner and raise ERK. Tovorafenib is a type II inhibitor: it binds the inactive DFG-out shape of BRAF and CRAF and can occupy both halves of a dimer. This shuts off RAF-MEK-ERK signaling in both fusion and V600 tumors, lowering cyclin D and stopping glioma cells in G1. It crosses the blood-brain barrier, which matters for brain tumors.", "body": [["Brain tumor", "Low-grade gliomas driven by BRAF changes shrink or stop growing, which can protect vision and other functions near the tumor."], ["Hair and skin", "Hair often lightens because MAPK signaling supports melanocyte pigment production. Rash, dry skin, paronychia and photosensitivity are common."], ["Growth", "MAPK signaling in growth plates supports bone growth, so children can grow more slowly during treatment, and height is tracked."], ["Blood and bleeding", "Nosebleeds are common and bleeding inside the tumor or skull can occur. Anemia is frequent."], ["Muscle and liver", "Creatine kinase and liver tests can rise, so blood tests are part of follow-up."]], "journey": "Tovorafenib is taken by mouth once a week, as a tablet or a liquid suspension made from powder. It is absorbed over a few hours and is highly bound to plasma proteins. Because it is designed to cross the blood-brain barrier, it reaches the tumor tissue. The liver breaks it down mainly by aldehyde oxidase and CYP2C8. Most leaves in the feces with some in urine. Its long half-life is what allows once-weekly dosing, a schedule that differs from most daily kinase inhibitors.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from blocking RAF in normal tissues. Hair color change reflects reduced melanin signaling in hair follicles. Skin rash and sun sensitivity come from MAPK roles in keratinocytes. Slower growth reflects MAPK roles in the growth plate. Bleeding, including in the tumor, liver test rises and CK rises are listed. There is no antidote; dose holds and reductions are used.", "fun": "Because tovorafenib is a type II RAF inhibitor, it avoids the paradoxical pathway activation that makes older BRAF drugs a poor fit for BRAF fusion gliomas.", "src": ["Ojemda (tovorafenib) FDA label", "Kilburn LB et al. Nat Med 2024 (FIREFLY-1)"]}, "avutometinib-defactinib": {"origin": "This product joins two drugs with separate histories. Avutometinib was discovered by the Japanese company Chugai Pharmaceutical as a RAF/MEK clamp, a molecule that holds RAF and MEK together in a dead complex. Defactinib began at Pfizer as an inhibitor of focal adhesion kinase (FAK). Verastem Oncology in Massachusetts gained both and tested them together, because lab work showed that tumors under MEK blockade turn on FAK as an escape route. Low-grade serous ovarian cancer is a rare, slow-growing cancer that often carries KRAS mutations and responds poorly to chemotherapy. In 2025 FDA granted accelerated approval to the co-pack (Avmapki Fakzynja) for KRAS-mutated recurrent low-grade serous ovarian cancer, the first FDA-approved treatment aimed specifically at this KRAS-mutated disease.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Chugai discovers the RAF/MEK clamp later named avutometinib; Pfizer develops the FAK inhibitor defactinib."], ["2010s", "Verastem Oncology acquires both compounds and begins combination trials."], ["2025", "FDA grants accelerated approval of the avutometinib and defactinib co-pack for KRAS-mutated low-grade serous ovarian cancer."]], "cell": "Avutometinib binds MEK1 and MEK2 and clamps them to RAF in an inactive complex. Ordinary MEK inhibitors block MEK, but loss of ERK feedback lets RAF phosphorylate MEK again and partly restart the pathway. By holding RAF and MEK together, avutometinib prevents RAF from phosphorylating MEK at all, so ERK signaling stays low even in KRAS-mutant cells. Defactinib is an ATP-competitive inhibitor of focal adhesion kinase (FAK, also called PTK2) and the related kinase PYK2. FAK relays signals from integrins attached to the surrounding matrix and can keep tumor cells alive and dividing when the MAPK pathway is blocked. Blocking both routes deepens and prolongs the growth arrest.", "body": [["Ovarian tumor", "KRAS-driven low-grade serous ovarian cancer shrinks or stops growing when both the RAF-MEK route and the FAK escape route are blocked."], ["Eyes", "Like other MAPK inhibitors, the pair can cause blurred vision, serous retinopathy and retinal pigment layer detachment, so eye exams are part of care."], ["Skin", "Acne-like rash, dermatitis and sun sensitivity come from MAPK blockade in skin cells."], ["Muscle", "Creatine kinase rises and rhabdomyolysis can occur."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes and bilirubin can rise, so liver tests are checked."], ["Gut and fluid", "Nausea, diarrhea and swelling (edema) are common."]], "journey": "Both parts are taken by mouth from one pack on different schedules. Avutometinib has a long half-life that supports dosing twice a week. Defactinib has a short half-life and is taken twice daily. Both are absorbed over hours, highly protein bound and broken down in the liver mainly by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors and inducers change exposure. Both leave mostly in the feces. Treatment follows a three-weeks-on, one-week-off pattern.", "sidefx": "Side effects mostly come from MAPK pathway blockade in normal tissue. The retina needs ERK signals to clear fluid, so vision changes and serous retinopathy occur. Skin cells need ERK, so rash is common. Muscle breakdown, liver test rises and GI upset are listed. Defactinib's FAK blockade adds to fatigue and GI effects. The two different schedules in one pack are a known source of dosing errors. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Avutometinib is called a RAF/MEK clamp because it does not simply block MEK; it glues MEK to RAF so neither can pass the signal on.", "src": ["Avmapki Fakzynja Co-Pack FDA label", "Banerjee S et al. RAMP 201 trial reports", "Verastem Oncology press materials"]}, "selumetinib": {"origin": "Selumetinib was discovered at Array BioPharma in Colorado as ARRY-142886 and licensed to AstraZeneca in 2003. It was first tested in many adult cancers with mixed results. Its breakthrough came in neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1), a genetic disease in which loss of the NF1 gene product, neurofibromin, leaves RAS switched on. Children with NF1 can develop plexiform neurofibromas, large nerve sheath tumors that cause pain, disfigurement and loss of function and are often impossible to remove. A National Cancer Institute team led by pediatric oncologist Brigitte Widemann ran the SPRINT trial, which showed many tumors shrank. In 2020 FDA approved selumetinib as Koselugo, the first drug approved for NF1. The approval was later broadened to younger children and to adults.", "timeline": [["2003", "Array BioPharma licenses the MEK inhibitor ARRY-142886 to AstraZeneca."], ["2010s", "The NCI-led SPRINT trial tests selumetinib in children with NF1 plexiform neurofibromas."], ["2020", "FDA approves Koselugo, the first drug for NF1 plexiform neurofibromas."], ["2020s", "Approval is expanded to younger children and to adults."]], "cell": "Neurofibromin is a RAS GTPase-activating protein. It normally speeds the conversion of active RAS-GTP back to inactive RAS-GDP. When both copies of NF1 are lost in Schwann cells, RAS stays in its active state and drives the RAF-MEK-ERK cascade. Selumetinib is a selective allosteric, non-ATP-competitive inhibitor of MEK1 and MEK2. It binds next to the ATP site and locks MEK so it cannot phosphorylate ERK. Lower ERK activity reduces Schwann cell and tumor-associated cell proliferation, and the tumor volume shrinks over months. Because the drug blocks a step below RAS, it works even though the root problem, missing neurofibromin, is not fixed.", "body": [["Plexiform neurofibromas", "Tumors shrink in many children and adults, which can ease pain and improve movement, breathing or vision affected by the tumor."], ["Skin and nails", "Acne-like rash, dry skin and paronychia (infection around the nails) come from MEK blockade in skin cells."], ["Eyes", "Blurred vision, retinal pigment layer detachment and retinal vein occlusion are possible, so eye exams are done."], ["Heart", "Ejection fraction can fall, so echocardiograms are done at baseline and during treatment."], ["Gut", "Vomiting, diarrhea and belly pain are common; severe diarrhea and colitis can occur."], ["Muscle", "Creatine kinase often rises and rhabdomyolysis is a rare warning."]], "journey": "Selumetinib is taken by mouth twice daily as capsules or as granules mixed with soft food for young children. It peaks in about one to one and a half hours and is almost fully bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down by CYP3A4, CYP2C19, CYP1A2 and glucuronidation, forming an active N-desmethyl metabolite. Most leaves in the feces with some in urine. Its half-life is several hours. The capsules contain vitamin E (TPGS), so extra vitamin E supplements add to exposure.", "sidefx": "Side effects reflect MEK blockade in healthy tissue. Skin rash and paronychia come from loss of ERK signals in keratinocytes. Serous retinopathy comes from impaired fluid pumping by the retinal pigment epithelium. Reduced ejection fraction reflects ERK survival signals in heart muscle. GI upset and CK rise are common. The vitamin E in the product can increase bleeding risk, especially with anticoagulants. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Before selumetinib, the only option for plexiform neurofibromas was surgery, which is often impossible because the tumors wrap around nerves.", "src": ["Koselugo (selumetinib) FDA label", "Gross AM et al. NEJM 2020 (SPRINT)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "mirdametinib": {"origin": "Mirdametinib was discovered at Pfizer's Ann Arbor research site, the former Parke-Davis labs, where some of the first MEK inhibitors were made. Known as PD-0325901, it was tested in adult cancers in the 2000s but was set aside. SpringWorks Therapeutics, a company formed in 2017 to develop promising drugs that larger companies had shelved, licensed it and focused on neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1). The ReNeu trial enrolled both children and adults with symptomatic plexiform neurofibromas. In 2025 FDA approved mirdametinib as Gomekli for adults and for children two years and older, the first NF1 plexiform neurofibroma drug approved for both age groups at once.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Pfizer tests the MEK inhibitor PD-0325901 in adult cancers."], ["2017", "SpringWorks Therapeutics is founded and later licenses mirdametinib."], ["2025", "FDA approves Gomekli for adults and children with NF1 plexiform neurofibromas."]], "cell": "In NF1, both copies of the neurofibromin gene are lost in Schwann cells, so RAS stays active and drives RAF, MEK and ERK. Mirdametinib is a selective allosteric inhibitor of MEK1 and MEK2. It binds a pocket next to the ATP site, not the ATP site itself, and locks MEK in a shape that cannot phosphorylate ERK. With ERK silent, Schwann cell-derived tumor cells stop dividing and the tumor volume shrinks over months. Mirdametinib also crosses the blood-brain barrier well, a property studied for brain tumors.", "body": [["Plexiform neurofibromas", "Tumor volume falls in many patients, and pain and quality of life can improve."], ["Skin", "Acne-like rash, dry skin and nail fold infections are the most common effects, from MEK blockade in skin."], ["Eyes", "Retinal vein occlusion, retinal pigment layer detachment and blurred vision are possible, so eye exams are done."], ["Heart", "Ejection fraction can drop, so heart function is checked."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea and vomiting are common."], ["Blood", "Children more often show a drop in neutrophil counts."]], "journey": "Mirdametinib is taken by mouth twice daily for three weeks out of every four, as capsules or tablets dispersed in water. It is absorbed quickly, peaking in about an hour, and is highly bound to plasma proteins. The liver clears it by glucuronidation and oxidation. It leaves in both feces and urine. Its half-life is about a day.", "sidefx": "Like other MEK inhibitors, its side effects come from blocking ERK signals in normal tissues. Skin cells need ERK, so rash is very common. The retinal pigment epithelium needs ERK to move fluid, so retinal problems can occur. Heart muscle relies on ERK survival signals, so ejection fraction can fall. GI upset and CK rise are frequent. There is no antidote; holds and dose reductions are used.", "src": ["Gomekli (mirdametinib) FDA label", "Moertel CL et al. J Clin Oncol 2025 (ReNeu)"]}, "sorafenib": {"origin": "Sorafenib came out of a joint program between Bayer and the California company Onyx Pharmaceuticals in the 1990s. The team screened a large chemical library for compounds that blocked RAF kinase, which sits just below RAS in a key growth pathway. The hit was optimized into BAY 43-9006, a bis-aryl urea. During testing it turned out to also block VEGF and PDGF receptors, so it attacked both tumor cells and their blood supply. FDA approved it as Nexavar in 2005 for advanced kidney cancer. In 2007 it became the first drug shown to extend survival in advanced liver cancer (the SHARP trial). In 2013 it was approved for thyroid cancer that no longer responds to radioactive iodine.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Bayer and Onyx screen chemicals for RAF kinase inhibitors and find BAY 43-9006."], ["2005", "FDA approves Nexavar for advanced renal cell carcinoma."], ["2007", "FDA approves sorafenib for unresectable hepatocellular carcinoma."], ["2013", "FDA approves sorafenib for radioactive iodine-refractory thyroid cancer."]], "cell": "Sorafenib is a multikinase inhibitor. Inside tumor cells it blocks the serine/threonine kinases CRAF, wild-type BRAF and BRAF V600E, which reduces MEK and ERK signaling and slows proliferation. On blood vessel cells it blocks receptor tyrosine kinases VEGFR-1, -2 and -3 and PDGFR-beta. VEGFR-2 is the main receptor that tells endothelial cells to divide, migrate and form new vessels, and PDGFR-beta supports the pericytes that stabilize them. Blocking these starves the tumor of new blood supply. It also hits KIT, FLT3 and RET. The overall effect is tumor control rather than rapid shrinkage, which is why disease stabilization is a common response.", "body": [["Tumors", "Kidney, liver and thyroid tumors grow more slowly because both tumor cell signaling and new blood vessel growth are cut."], ["Skin", "Hand-foot skin reaction, with painful thick calluses on pressure points, is one of the most common effects. Rash and hair thinning also occur."], ["Blood vessels", "Blocking VEGF lowers nitric oxide production and reduces small vessels, which raises blood pressure."], ["Heart", "Cardiac ischemia and heart attack are listed warnings, and QT prolongation can occur."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is common, and rare bowel perforation reflects poor vessel support in the gut wall."], ["Healing and bleeding", "Wounds heal slowly and bleeding risk rises because VEGF is needed for vessel repair, so the drug is held before surgery."]], "journey": "Sorafenib is taken by mouth on an empty stomach because a high-fat meal lowers absorption. Absorption is incomplete and variable, peaking in about three hours. It is almost entirely bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down by CYP3A4 oxidation and UGT1A9 glucuronidation. Most leaves in the feces, with a smaller part in urine. The half-life is long, about one to two days, and some drug recirculates through the bile.", "sidefx": "Many side effects come from blocking VEGF and PDGF signaling in normal vessels. Less VEGF-driven nitric oxide raises blood pressure. Damaged small vessels in high-pressure skin areas on the palms and soles likely underlie hand-foot skin reaction. Weak vessel repair leads to bleeding, poor wound healing and rare GI perforation. Heart ischemia, QT prolongation and liver injury are listed warnings. There is no antidote; dose holds and reductions are used.", "fun": "Sorafenib was first designed as a RAF inhibitor, but much of its benefit in kidney cancer is thought to come from its accidental VEGF receptor blockade.", "src": ["Nexavar (sorafenib) FDA label", "Llovet JM et al. NEJM 2008 (SHARP)", "Wilhelm S et al. Nat Rev Drug Discov 2006"]}, "sunitinib": {"origin": "Sunitinib was designed at SUGEN, a South San Francisco biotech company that pioneered small molecules aimed at receptor tyrosine kinases. SUGEN was acquired by Pharmacia, which was then bought by Pfizer, and the compound SU11248 was developed by Pfizer. It was built to block several receptors at once, including VEGF and PDGF receptors and KIT. In January 2006 FDA approved it as Sutent on the same day for two cancers: advanced kidney cancer and gastrointestinal stromal tumor (GIST) after imatinib failure. That double approval on a single day was unusual for a new cancer drug. It later gained approval for pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors and for adjuvant use after kidney cancer surgery.", "timeline": [["1990s", "SUGEN designs small-molecule receptor tyrosine kinase inhibitors."], ["2006", "FDA approves Sutent for both advanced RCC and GIST at the same time."], ["2011", "FDA approves sunitinib for pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors."], ["2017", "FDA approves sunitinib as adjuvant therapy for high-risk RCC after nephrectomy."]], "cell": "Sunitinib is an ATP-competitive inhibitor of many receptor tyrosine kinases: VEGFR-1, -2 and -3, PDGFR-alpha and -beta, KIT, FLT3, CSF-1R and RET. In kidney cancer, loss of the VHL gene lets HIF build up, which drives VEGF production; sunitinib blocks the VEGF receptors on endothelial cells, so new vessels stop forming and existing tumor vessels regress. PDGFR blockade removes support from pericytes. In GIST, tumor cells are driven by mutant KIT, and sunitinib blocks some KIT mutations that resist imatinib. The liver converts it to an active metabolite, SU12662, with similar activity.", "body": [["Tumors", "Kidney cancers lose blood supply, and some imatinib-resistant GISTs stop growing."], ["Blood vessels", "Loss of VEGF signaling raises blood pressure and can damage kidney filtration, causing protein in the urine."], ["Thyroid", "Hypothyroidism is common, likely from reduced thyroid blood flow and other effects on the gland."], ["Skin and hair", "The yellow color of the drug can tint the skin, hair can lose pigment from KIT blockade in melanocytes, and hand-foot syndrome can occur."], ["Heart", "Heart failure, reduced ejection fraction and QT prolongation are listed."], ["Liver", "A boxed warning covers severe, sometimes fatal liver injury."], ["Mouth and gut", "Mouth sores, taste change, diarrhea and fatigue are frequent."], ["Blood", "Neutropenia and thrombocytopenia occur, partly from FLT3 and KIT roles in the marrow."]], "journey": "Sunitinib is taken by mouth with or without food and peaks slowly, over 6 to 12 hours. It is highly protein bound and spreads widely in tissues. CYP3A4 in the liver converts it to the active metabolite SU12662, which CYP3A4 then breaks down further. Both leave mostly in the feces. The half-lives are long: about two days for the parent drug and about four days for the metabolite. The classic schedule is four weeks on and two weeks off.", "sidefx": "VEGF blockade explains high blood pressure, protein in urine, bleeding, poor wound healing and thrombotic microangiopathy. KIT blockade in melanocytes explains hair and skin depigmentation. Thyroid underactivity and fatigue are common. Heart failure may reflect kinase roles in heart muscle cell survival. Liver toxicity carries a boxed warning. Low blood sugar and jaw osteonecrosis are listed. There is no antidote; dose holds and reductions are used.", "fun": "Sunitinib is bright yellow, and some people notice their skin taking on a yellow tint during treatment, which is not jaundice.", "src": ["Sutent (sunitinib) FDA label", "Motzer RJ et al. NEJM 2007", "Demetri GD et al. Lancet 2006"]}, "pazopanib": {"origin": "Pazopanib was designed by medicinal chemists at GlaxoSmithKline in the early 2000s as an oral inhibitor of VEGF receptors and related kinases. It was approved by FDA in 2009 as Votrient for advanced kidney cancer. In 2012 it became the first targeted drug approved for several types of advanced soft tissue sarcoma after chemotherapy, based on the PALETTE trial. A large head-to-head trial, COMPARZ, later showed it worked about as well as sunitinib in kidney cancer, with a different side effect profile. In 2015 the drug moved to Novartis as part of an asset swap between the two companies.", "timeline": [["2000s", "GlaxoSmithKline designs pazopanib as a VEGFR inhibitor."], ["2009", "FDA approves Votrient for advanced renal cell carcinoma."], ["2012", "FDA approves pazopanib for advanced soft tissue sarcoma after chemotherapy."], ["2015", "Pazopanib moves to Novartis."]], "cell": "Pazopanib binds the ATP pocket of several receptor tyrosine kinases: VEGFR-1, -2 and -3, PDGFR-alpha and -beta, FGFR-1 and -3, KIT, c-FMS, ITK and LCK. Its main effect is blocking VEGF-induced autophosphorylation of VEGFR-2 on endothelial cells. Without that signal, endothelial cells do not divide, migrate or survive well, so new tumor vessels fail to form. PDGFR blockade weakens the pericytes that stabilize vessels. The result is a tumor starved of oxygen and nutrients, which slows growth. It controls disease more than it directly kills tumor cells.", "body": [["Tumors", "Kidney cancer and soft tissue sarcomas grow more slowly when their blood supply is cut."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises are common, and a boxed warning covers severe, sometimes fatal liver injury, so liver tests are checked often early in treatment."], ["Blood vessels", "VEGF blockade raises blood pressure, causes protein in the urine and raises clot and bleeding risk."], ["Hair", "Hair often turns white or gray because KIT blockade reduces melanocyte pigment production."], ["Heart", "Heart dysfunction and QT prolongation are listed, so ECGs and electrolytes are checked in those at risk."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea and loss of appetite are common; rare bowel perforation and fistula reflect weak vessel repair."]], "journey": "Pazopanib is taken by mouth on an empty stomach because food raises absorption unpredictably, and tablets are not crushed because that speeds absorption. It peaks in a few hours and is almost fully protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A4, with small CYP1A2 and CYP2C8 roles. Nearly all leaves in the feces and very little in urine. The half-life is about 31 hours, which allows once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "VEGF blockade explains high blood pressure, protein in urine, bleeding, clots, poor wound healing and bowel perforation. KIT blockade in hair follicle melanocytes explains hair depigmentation. The liver injury in the boxed warning appears to be idiosyncratic. Studies have linked ALT rises to the HLA-B*57:01 genotype, and UGT1A1 variants (Gilbert syndrome) can raise bilirubin without true liver damage. QT prolongation is listed. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Hair turning white is so common with pazopanib that it is listed among the most frequent side effects in the label.", "src": ["Votrient (pazopanib) FDA label", "Sternberg CN et al. J Clin Oncol 2010", "Motzer RJ et al. NEJM 2013 (COMPARZ)", "van der Graaf WT et al. Lancet 2012 (PALETTE)"]}, "axitinib": {"origin": "Axitinib was designed at Agouron Pharmaceuticals in La Jolla, California, a company known for structure-based drug design, where chemists used crystal structures of kinases to shape molecules that fit the target closely. Agouron became part of Pfizer, which developed the compound, AG-013736. The aim was a more selective VEGF receptor blocker than earlier multikinase drugs. In the AXIS trial it beat sorafenib as second-line therapy for advanced kidney cancer, and FDA approved it as Inlyta in 2012. In 2019 FDA approved axitinib as first-line kidney cancer treatment in combination with the immune checkpoint antibodies pembrolizumab and avelumab, pairing a blood vessel blocker with an immune booster.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Agouron Pharmaceuticals builds a structure-based kinase drug design program."], ["2012", "FDA approves Inlyta for advanced RCC after one prior systemic therapy."], ["2019", "FDA approves axitinib with pembrolizumab and with avelumab for first-line advanced RCC."]], "cell": "Axitinib is a potent, selective ATP-competitive inhibitor of VEGFR-1, VEGFR-2 and VEGFR-3, with much weaker effect on PDGFR and KIT. In clear cell kidney cancer, loss of the VHL tumor suppressor lets HIF accumulate, which drives high VEGF output. VEGF binds VEGFR-2 on endothelial cells, which dimerizes and autophosphorylates to switch on PLC-gamma, MAPK and PI3K-AKT signals for cell division, migration, survival and vessel leakiness. Axitinib blocks that autophosphorylation, so tumor vessels stop sprouting and some regress, and the tumor is starved of oxygen. Less VEGF signaling may also make the tumor environment less immunosuppressive, which supports pairing with checkpoint inhibitors.", "body": [["Kidney tumor", "Tumors lose blood supply and shrink or stop growing, especially when combined with a checkpoint inhibitor."], ["Blood vessels", "Blood pressure rises in a dose-related way because VEGF normally drives nitric oxide release and keeps small vessels open. Hypertensive crisis can occur."], ["Kidneys", "VEGF supports the glomerular filter, so protein can leak into the urine."], ["Thyroid", "Hypothyroidism is common, likely from loss of thyroid capillaries."], ["Voice", "Hoarseness (dysphonia) is a frequent and distinctive effect."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is common; rare bowel perforation or fistula reflects weak vessel repair."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises are more frequent when combined with pembrolizumab."]], "journey": "Axitinib is taken by mouth twice daily, about 12 hours apart. It peaks in a few hours and is almost fully protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A4/5, with small roles for CYP1A2, CYP2C19 and UGT1A1, so strong CYP3A inhibitors and inducers change levels. Metabolites leave in feces and urine. Its half-life is short, a few hours, unlike many other VEGFR inhibitors, so effects wear off quickly when doses are held.", "sidefx": "Most effects are on-target VEGF blockade in healthy vessels: high blood pressure from less nitric oxide and fewer small vessels, protein in urine from glomerular damage, bleeding, clots, poor wound healing and bowel perforation. Thyroid underactivity and hoarseness are common. Heart failure and PRES are rare warnings. With pembrolizumab, immune-mediated side effects and liver enzyme rises are more common. There is no antidote, but the short half-life means toxicity eases soon after stopping.", "fun": "Because blood pressure rise tracks with VEGF blockade, some studies found that people who developed high blood pressure on axitinib tended to have better tumor responses.", "src": ["Inlyta (axitinib) FDA label", "Rini BI et al. Lancet 2011 (AXIS)", "Rini BI et al. NEJM 2019 (KEYNOTE-426)"]}, "cabozantinib": {"origin": "Cabozantinib was discovered at Exelixis in South San Francisco as XL184, a molecule designed to block MET, the receptor for hepatocyte growth factor, along with VEGF receptors. Researchers had noticed that tumors treated with VEGF blockers often escape by switching on MET and AXL, so hitting all of them together seemed smart. FDA first approved it as Cometriq capsules in 2012 for medullary thyroid cancer, which is often driven by RET, another of its targets. A tablet form, Cabometyx, was approved in 2016 for advanced kidney cancer, and later for liver cancer after sorafenib, first-line kidney cancer with nivolumab, differentiated thyroid cancer and neuroendocrine tumors. The capsules and tablets are not interchangeable.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Exelixis discovers XL184, a MET and VEGFR inhibitor."], ["2012", "FDA approves Cometriq capsules for metastatic medullary thyroid cancer."], ["2016", "FDA approves Cabometyx tablets for advanced RCC after prior antiangiogenic therapy."], ["2019", "FDA approves Cabometyx for HCC after sorafenib."], ["2021", "FDA approves cabozantinib with nivolumab for first-line advanced RCC."]], "cell": "Cabozantinib is an ATP-competitive inhibitor of many receptor tyrosine kinases. Its key targets are MET, VEGFR-1, -2 and -3, AXL and RET, plus KIT, FLT3, TIE-2 and others. VEGFR-2 blockade stops endothelial cells from building new tumor vessels. MET, activated by hepatocyte growth factor, drives cell invasion, scattering and metastasis, and its levels rise when tumors are starved of oxygen by VEGF blockade. AXL signaling also helps tumors resist targeted drugs. Blocking MET and AXL along with VEGFR closes these escape routes. RET blockade targets the driver of many medullary thyroid cancers. The drug is not cell-cycle specific.", "body": [["Tumors", "Kidney, liver, thyroid and neuroendocrine tumors grow more slowly as blood supply and invasion signals are cut."], ["Blood vessels", "VEGF blockade raises blood pressure and increases risks of bleeding and clots, including heart attack and stroke."], ["Skin", "Palmar-plantar erythrodysesthesia (painful red hands and feet) is common."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is very common; perforation and fistula are rare serious events from weak vessel repair."], ["Kidneys", "Protein can leak into the urine from damage to the glomerular filter."], ["Mouth and bone", "Mouth sores are common, and osteonecrosis of the jaw is a listed risk."], ["Hormones", "Hypothyroidism, adrenal insufficiency and low calcium are listed."]], "journey": "Cabozantinib is taken by mouth on an empty stomach because food raises its levels. It peaks in a few hours and is almost fully bound to plasma proteins. CYP3A4 in the liver is the main clearance route, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors and inducers matter, and grapefruit is avoided. Metabolites leave in the feces and urine. Its half-life is long, about four days, so it builds up over a couple of weeks and lingers after stopping, which is why it is held weeks before planned surgery.", "sidefx": "VEGF blockade in normal vessels explains high blood pressure, protein in urine, bleeding, clots, poor wound healing and bowel perforation. Hand-foot syndrome and diarrhea are very common and often drive dose cuts. Thyroid and adrenal effects and jaw osteonecrosis are listed. Its long half-life means side effects may take days to fade after a dose hold. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The Cometriq capsules and Cabometyx tablets contain the same drug but differ in absorption, so they cannot be swapped dose for dose.", "src": ["Cabometyx and Cometriq (cabozantinib) FDA labels", "Choueiri TK et al. NEJM 2015 (METEOR)", "Abou-Alfa GK et al. NEJM 2018 (CELESTIAL)"]}, "lenvatinib": {"origin": "Lenvatinib was discovered by Eisai, a Japanese drug company, at its research labs in Tsukuba, Japan, where it was known as E7080. Eisai chemists designed it to block both VEGF receptors and FGF receptors. FGF signaling is a common escape route for tumors treated with VEGF blockers, so hitting both seemed likely to give stronger control. FDA approved it as Lenvima in 2015 for thyroid cancer that no longer responds to radioactive iodine. It went on to gain approvals for kidney cancer (first with everolimus, then with pembrolizumab), unresectable liver cancer, and advanced endometrial cancer with pembrolizumab, making it one of the most widely used partners for checkpoint inhibitors.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Eisai discovers E7080 at its Tsukuba research labs."], ["2015", "FDA approves Lenvima for radioactive iodine-refractory differentiated thyroid cancer."], ["2016", "FDA approves lenvatinib with everolimus for advanced RCC."], ["2018", "FDA approves lenvatinib for first-line unresectable hepatocellular carcinoma."], ["2021", "FDA approves lenvatinib with pembrolizumab for advanced RCC and endometrial cancer."]], "cell": "Lenvatinib binds the ATP pocket of several receptor tyrosine kinases: VEGFR-1, -2 and -3, FGFR-1 to -4, PDGFR-alpha, KIT and RET. VEGFR-2 blockade on endothelial cells stops sprouting of new tumor vessels and makes existing vessels regress. FGFR blockade cuts a parallel pro-angiogenic and tumor growth signal that tumors often use to escape VEGF blockade. RET and KIT blockade directly slows some tumor cells. The net effect is a tumor with less blood supply and fewer growth signals. Lower VEGF also reduces immunosuppressive cells in the tumor, one reason it pairs well with pembrolizumab.", "body": [["Tumors", "Thyroid, kidney, liver and endometrial cancers shrink or stop growing as blood supply and FGF signals are cut."], ["Blood vessels", "High blood pressure is the most common effect and can be severe, because VEGF normally keeps small vessels open through nitric oxide."], ["Kidneys", "Protein in the urine and kidney injury can occur, worsened by fluid loss from diarrhea."], ["Heart", "Heart failure, arterial clots and QT prolongation are listed warnings."], ["Gut and mouth", "Diarrhea, mouth sores, nausea and appetite loss are common; rare GI perforation and fistula reflect weak vessel repair."], ["Thyroid and calcium", "Hypothyroidism and low calcium are common, and TSH suppression is harder to maintain in thyroid cancer."], ["Liver", "Liver injury and liver failure are listed, especially relevant in liver cancer."]], "journey": "Lenvatinib is taken by mouth as capsules, which can be dispersed in a little water or apple juice for those who cannot swallow them. It is absorbed well and peaks in one to four hours. It is highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down by CYP3A4 and aldehyde oxidase, and it leaves mainly in the feces with some in urine. The half-life is about 28 hours, which supports once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Almost all of its main toxicities trace back to VEGF blockade in normal vessels: high blood pressure, protein in urine, bleeding, clots, impaired wound healing and PRES. Tumor bleeding near large vessels can be fatal, especially in thyroid tumors invading the neck. Diarrhea leads to dehydration and kidney injury. Low calcium and thyroid problems are common. With pembrolizumab, immune-mediated side effects are added. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Lenvima (lenvatinib) FDA label", "Schlumberger M et al. NEJM 2015 (SELECT)", "Kudo M et al. Lancet 2018 (REFLECT)", "Motzer R et al. NEJM 2021 (CLEAR)"]}, "regorafenib": {"origin": "Regorafenib was developed by Bayer as a close chemical relative of its earlier drug sorafenib. The two molecules differ by just one fluorine atom added to the central ring, a small change that broadened and strengthened the kinase blockade. Bayer tested it in people whose cancers had already stopped responding to standard treatments. FDA approved it as Stivarga in 2012 for metastatic colorectal cancer after chemotherapy and biologics, in 2013 for GIST after imatinib and sunitinib, and in 2017 for liver cancer after sorafenib. That last approval made it the first second-line drug proven to extend survival in hepatocellular carcinoma.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Bayer develops regorafenib as a fluorinated relative of sorafenib."], ["2012", "FDA approves Stivarga for previously treated metastatic colorectal cancer."], ["2013", "FDA approves regorafenib for GIST after imatinib and sunitinib."], ["2017", "FDA approves regorafenib for hepatocellular carcinoma after sorafenib."]], "cell": "Regorafenib is a multikinase inhibitor that blocks kinases in three areas. For blood vessel growth, it blocks VEGFR-1, -2 and -3 and TIE-2, the receptor for angiopoietins that stabilize vessels. For tumor cell growth, it blocks KIT, RET, RAF-1, BRAF and BRAF V600E. For the tumor environment, it blocks PDGFR, FGFR and CSF1R, which affects stromal cells and tumor-associated macrophages. Together these cut tumor blood supply, slow tumor cell division and reduce support from surrounding tissue. KIT and PDGFR blockade explains its activity in GIST that has escaped imatinib. Its active metabolites, M-2 and M-5, have similar activity.", "body": [["Tumors", "Colorectal, GIST and liver tumors grow more slowly after other treatments stop working."], ["Skin", "Hand-foot skin reaction, with painful red or blistered skin on palms and soles, is common and often appears early. Severe rashes such as SJS are rare."], ["Liver", "A boxed warning covers severe and sometimes fatal liver injury, so liver tests are checked often, especially in the first two months."], ["Blood vessels", "Blood pressure rises and bleeding risk increases from VEGF blockade."], ["Heart", "Cardiac ischemia and heart attack are listed warnings."], ["Gut and mouth", "Diarrhea, mouth sores and appetite loss are common, and voice hoarseness can occur."]], "journey": "Regorafenib is taken by mouth after a low-fat meal, which gives the best balance of parent drug and active metabolites; a high-fat meal lowers metabolite exposure. It peaks in about four hours and is almost fully protein bound. The liver converts it by CYP3A4 to active metabolites M-2 and M-5, and UGT1A9 glucuronidates it. Drug and metabolites go into bile and recycle through the gut, then leave mostly in the feces. Half-lives run from about a day to two days. It is given three weeks on, one week off.", "sidefx": "Hand-foot skin reaction, high blood pressure, bleeding, poor wound healing and GI perforation follow from VEGF and related kinase blockade in normal tissue. The serious liver injury in the boxed warning is thought to be a direct liver cell toxicity and is why liver tests are checked every two weeks at first. Fatigue, voice change and infections are common. There is no antidote; dose holds and reductions are used.", "fun": "Regorafenib and sorafenib differ by a single fluorine atom, yet regorafenib blocks a wider range of kinases.", "src": ["Stivarga (regorafenib) FDA label", "Grothey A et al. Lancet 2013 (CORRECT)", "Bruix J et al. Lancet 2017 (RESORCE)"]}, "vandetanib": {"origin": "Vandetanib was developed by AstraZeneca as ZD6474. It was first designed as a VEGFR-2 inhibitor and also blocked EGFR, and researchers later found it blocked RET too. RET mutations had been linked to inherited medullary thyroid cancer (MEN2) in 1993, and most sporadic medullary thyroid cancers also carry RET mutations. Medullary thyroid cancer arises from calcitonin-making C cells and does not take up radioactive iodine, so options were few. In 2011 FDA approved vandetanib as Caprelsa, the first drug approved for advanced medullary thyroid cancer. Because it prolongs the QT interval, it is only available through a restricted REMS program. Sanofi Genzyme later acquired the rights from AstraZeneca.", "timeline": [["1993", "Researchers link germline RET mutations to MEN2 and inherited medullary thyroid cancer."], ["2000s", "AstraZeneca develops ZD6474, later named vandetanib."], ["2011", "FDA approves Caprelsa, the first drug for advanced medullary thyroid cancer, with a REMS for QT risk."]], "cell": "Vandetanib is an ATP-competitive inhibitor of RET, VEGFR-2, VEGFR-3 and EGFR. In medullary thyroid cancer, activating RET mutations make the receptor signal without its ligand, driving the C cells through MAPK and PI3K-AKT pathways. Blocking RET cuts this driver signal. Blocking VEGFR-2 and VEGFR-3 slows tumor blood and lymph vessel growth, and EGFR blockade removes another growth signal. Tumor shrinkage is tracked by falling calcitonin and CEA levels. Separately, vandetanib blocks the hERG potassium channel in heart cells, which carries the rapid delayed rectifier current that ends each beat, so ventricular repolarization is slowed and the QT interval lengthens.", "body": [["Thyroid tumor", "Medullary thyroid cancer shrinks or stops growing, and calcitonin and CEA levels fall."], ["Heart", "hERG channel blockade lengthens QT and can trigger torsades de pointes and sudden death, which is the reason for the boxed warning and REMS."], ["Skin", "EGFR blockade in skin cells causes acne-like rash, dry skin and sun sensitivity; severe rashes can occur."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is very common and can lower potassium and magnesium, which adds to QT risk."], ["Blood vessels", "VEGF blockade raises blood pressure and adds bleeding and stroke risk."], ["Thyroid hormone", "Hypothyroidism and higher thyroid hormone replacement needs are common after thyroidectomy."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease is a rare serious warning."]], "journey": "Vandetanib is taken by mouth and absorbed slowly, peaking around six hours. It can be dispersed in water for tube use. It is highly protein bound and widely distributed. CYP3A4 makes an active N-desmethyl metabolite, and FMO1 and FMO3 make an N-oxide. It leaves in both feces and urine. Its half-life is very long, about 19 days, so it takes months to reach steady state and weeks to clear. That is why QT effects can linger long after a dose change.", "sidefx": "QT prolongation comes from direct hERG potassium channel blockade, and low potassium, magnesium or calcium make it worse, so electrolytes are corrected and other QT-prolonging drugs are avoided. Rash and sun sensitivity come from EGFR blockade in skin. Diarrhea comes from EGFR effects on gut lining. High blood pressure and bleeding come from VEGF blockade. The very long half-life means side effects fade slowly. There is no antidote; torsades is treated with standard measures such as magnesium.", "fun": "Vandetanib's half-life of nearly three weeks is one of the longest of any kinase inhibitor.", "src": ["Caprelsa (vandetanib) FDA label and REMS", "Wells SA et al. J Clin Oncol 2012 (ZETA)", "Mulligan LM et al. Nature 1993"]}, "tivozanib": {"origin": "Tivozanib was discovered by the Japanese company Kyowa Hakko Kirin as KRN951 and licensed to AVEO Oncology in Massachusetts. It was designed to block all three VEGF receptors very strongly and selectively, with fewer off-target effects than older drugs. Its path to approval was long. In 2013 an FDA advisory committee raised concerns about survival results in the first large trial, TIVO-1, and FDA declined to approve it. AVEO then ran TIVO-3, which compared it with sorafenib in people whose kidney cancer had progressed after two or more prior treatments. In 2021 FDA approved it as Fotivda for that group.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Kyowa Hakko Kirin discovers KRN951 and licenses it to AVEO Oncology."], ["2013", "FDA declines to approve tivozanib after the TIVO-1 trial."], ["2017", "Tivozanib is approved in the European Union for first-line advanced RCC."], ["2021", "FDA approves Fotivda for relapsed or refractory advanced RCC after two or more therapies."]], "cell": "Tivozanib is a highly potent ATP-competitive inhibitor of VEGFR-1, VEGFR-2 and VEGFR-3, working at very low concentrations, with much weaker activity on KIT and PDGFR-beta. In clear cell kidney cancer, VHL loss leads to HIF buildup and VEGF overproduction. VEGF binding to VEGFR-2 on endothelial cells normally triggers receptor autophosphorylation and downstream MAPK and PI3K-AKT signals for division, migration and survival. By blocking this step, tivozanib prevents new tumor vessel growth and causes existing tumor vessels to regress. VEGFR-3 blockade also affects lymphatic vessels. Its selectivity is meant to reduce off-target side effects such as hand-foot reactions.", "body": [["Kidney tumor", "Tumors lose their blood supply and grow more slowly."], ["Blood vessels", "High blood pressure is the most common serious effect, from loss of VEGF-driven nitric oxide and small vessel loss."], ["Heart", "Heart failure, cardiac ischemia and arterial clots are listed warnings."], ["Kidneys", "Protein can leak into the urine from damage to the glomerular filter."], ["Voice and thyroid", "Hoarseness and hypothyroidism are common."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea and appetite loss are frequent."]], "journey": "Tivozanib is taken by mouth as a capsule once daily for three weeks, then one week off. It is absorbed slowly, peaking around ten hours, and is almost fully protein bound. CYP3A4 in the liver is the main route of breakdown, so strong CYP3A4 inducers lower levels. Most leaves in the feces. Its half-life is very long, about four and a half days, so steady state takes about two weeks and the drug is held well before planned surgery.", "sidefx": "The main toxicities are on-target VEGF blockade effects: high blood pressure, protein in urine, bleeding, clots, poor wound healing and PRES. Because it is selective, hand-foot reactions and diarrhea are less frequent than with broader kinase inhibitors. Thyroid underactivity and hoarseness are common. One capsule strength contains the dye tartrazine (FD&C Yellow No. 5), which can cause allergic reactions in sensitive people. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Fotivda (tivozanib) FDA label", "Rini BI et al. Lancet Oncol 2020 (TIVO-3)", "Motzer RJ et al. J Clin Oncol 2013 (TIVO-1)"]}, "fruquintinib": {"origin": "Fruquintinib was discovered in Shanghai by Hutchison MediPharma, now called HUTCHMED, one of the first Chinese companies to invent its own new cancer drugs. It was designed to block VEGF receptors 1, 2 and 3 with high selectivity, avoiding other kinases so that side effects would stay manageable. China approved it in 2018 for metastatic colorectal cancer. HUTCHMED licensed rights outside China to Takeda, and the global FRESCO-2 trial showed it extended survival in heavily pretreated colorectal cancer. FDA approved it as Fruzaqla in 2023 for metastatic colorectal cancer after standard chemotherapy and biologic treatments.", "timeline": [["2010s", "HUTCHMED discovers fruquintinib in Shanghai."], ["2018", "China approves fruquintinib for metastatic colorectal cancer."], ["2023", "FDA approves Fruzaqla for previously treated metastatic colorectal cancer."]], "cell": "Fruquintinib is a highly selective small-molecule inhibitor of VEGFR-1, VEGFR-2 and VEGFR-3 with little activity on other kinases. VEGF released by tumors binds VEGFR-2 on endothelial cells, which normally triggers receptor autophosphorylation and downstream signals for cell division, migration and formation of new vessel tubes. Blocking the receptor kinase stops this process, so tumors cannot grow new vessels and existing ones regress. VEGFR-3 blockade also affects lymphatic vessel growth. Because it hits few other kinases, its side effects are mostly the expected VEGF-blockade effects.", "body": [["Colorectal tumor", "Tumor blood supply is cut, which slows growth and extended survival in the FRESCO-2 trial."], ["Blood vessels", "High blood pressure is common because VEGF normally keeps small vessels open through nitric oxide."], ["Kidneys", "Protein can leak into the urine from damage to the glomerular filter."], ["Skin", "Hand-foot syndrome, with red, sore skin on the palms and soles, is common."], ["Gut and mouth", "Diarrhea, mouth sores and belly pain occur; GI perforation is a rare serious risk."], ["Voice and thyroid", "Hoarseness and hypothyroidism are common."], ["Bleeding", "Bleeding, including GI bleeding, is a listed warning."]], "journey": "Fruquintinib is taken by mouth as a capsule once daily for three weeks, then one week off. It is absorbed well and peaks in about two hours. It is highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down by CYP3A4 and non-CYP pathways, and unlike many kinase inhibitors, more of it leaves in the urine than in the feces. Its half-life is about 42 hours.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from VEGF blockade in normal tissue: high blood pressure, protein in urine, bleeding, poor wound healing, GI perforation, arterial clots and PRES. Hand-foot syndrome and hoarseness are common. Infections and liver test rises are listed. The capsules contain the dyes tartrazine (FD&C Yellow No. 5) and FD&C Yellow No. 6, which can cause allergic reactions. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Fruquintinib was one of the first cancer drugs discovered in mainland China to win FDA approval.", "src": ["Fruzaqla (fruquintinib) FDA label", "Dasari A et al. Lancet 2023 (FRESCO-2)", "Li J et al. JAMA 2018 (FRESCO)"]}, "ibrutinib": {"origin": "Bruton tyrosine kinase is named after Ogden Bruton, a US Army pediatrician who in 1952 described a boy with no antibodies, the first case of what is now called X-linked agammaglobulinemia. In 1993 the gene behind it was found to encode a B-cell kinase, BTK. Ibrutinib was first made at Celera Genomics as a research tool compound that bound BTK permanently. Pharmacyclics bought the program in 2006 and saw that it could kill malignant B cells. With Janssen as partner, it moved fast through trials. FDA approved it as Imbruvica in 2013 for mantle cell lymphoma and in 2014 for chronic lymphocytic leukemia, transforming CLL care by offering an oral pill instead of chemotherapy. AbbVie acquired Pharmacyclics in 2015. The US mantle cell lymphoma indication was later withdrawn.", "timeline": [["1952", "Ogden Bruton describes X-linked agammaglobulinemia."], ["1993", "The BTK gene is identified as the cause of X-linked agammaglobulinemia."], ["2006", "Pharmacyclics acquires the BTK inhibitor program from Celera Genomics."], ["2013", "FDA approves Imbruvica for mantle cell lymphoma."], ["2014", "FDA approves ibrutinib for chronic lymphocytic leukemia."], ["2017", "FDA approves ibrutinib for chronic graft-versus-host disease."]], "cell": "When the B-cell receptor is engaged, the kinases LYN and SYK activate BTK, which phosphorylates PLC-gamma2. That releases calcium and activates NF-kappaB, ERK and AKT signals that tell the B cell to survive, divide and stay anchored in lymph nodes. CLL and other B-cell cancers depend on this signal. Ibrutinib forms a covalent bond with cysteine 481 in the ATP pocket of BTK, blocking it permanently until the cell makes new enzyme. Malignant B cells lose survival signals and die, and chemokine and integrin signals that keep them in lymph nodes weaken, so they spill into the blood. Ibrutinib also hits other kinases with a similar cysteine, such as ITK, TEC and EGFR, and blocks CSK, effects linked to bleeding, atrial fibrillation and rash.", "body": [["Lymph nodes and spleen", "Nodes and spleen shrink as malignant B cells die or leave."], ["Blood", "Lymphocyte counts often rise at first because cancer cells are pushed out of nodes into the blood. This is redistribution, not progression."], ["Platelets", "BTK and TEC help platelets respond to collagen, so bruising and bleeding increase, including rare brain bleeds."], ["Heart", "Atrial fibrillation, high blood pressure and, rarely, ventricular arrhythmias and sudden death can occur."], ["Immune system", "Normal B-cell function is reduced, so infections, including pneumocystis pneumonia, are more likely."], ["Gut, joints and skin", "Diarrhea, joint and muscle pain and rash are common, partly from off-target kinase blockade."]], "journey": "Ibrutinib is taken by mouth and absorbed quickly, peaking in one to two hours, but much is removed by the liver on first pass. It is highly protein bound. CYP3A4 breaks it down, forming the active metabolite PCI-45227, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels greatly. Most leaves in the feces. The plasma half-life is only about four to six hours, but because it binds BTK permanently, BTK stays blocked until new enzyme is made, which supports once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Bleeding comes from BTK and TEC blockade in platelets, which weakens collagen-driven activation, so the drug is held around surgery. Atrial fibrillation has been linked in research to off-target kinase blockade such as CSK in heart cells. High blood pressure is common. Infections follow from reduced B-cell and some T-cell function. Diarrhea and rash may reflect EGFR blockade. There is no antidote; platelet transfusion can help in serious bleeding.", "fun": "Ibrutinib was first made as a lab tool to label BTK, and only later turned out to be a powerful drug.", "src": ["Imbruvica (ibrutinib) FDA label", "Bruton OC. Pediatrics 1952", "Byrd JC et al. NEJM 2013", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "acalabrutinib": {"origin": "Acalabrutinib was developed by Acerta Pharma, a small company based in the Netherlands and California. Its goal was a more selective BTK blocker that hit fewer off-target kinases, in the hope of causing less bleeding, atrial fibrillation and rash. AstraZeneca bought a majority stake in Acerta in 2016. FDA granted accelerated approval to acalabrutinib as Calquence in 2017 for previously treated mantle cell lymphoma and approved it for chronic lymphocytic leukemia in 2019. A head-to-head trial, ELEVATE-RR, later showed similar control of CLL with less atrial fibrillation than ibrutinib. A tablet form approved in 2022 removed a problem with the original capsules, which needed stomach acid to dissolve.", "timeline": [["2016", "AstraZeneca acquires a majority stake in Acerta Pharma."], ["2017", "FDA grants accelerated approval of Calquence for mantle cell lymphoma."], ["2019", "FDA approves acalabrutinib for chronic lymphocytic leukemia."], ["2022", "FDA approves Calquence tablets, which can be taken with acid-reducing drugs."]], "cell": "Acalabrutinib binds covalently to cysteine 481 in the ATP pocket of Bruton tyrosine kinase, blocking it permanently. BTK normally passes signals from the B-cell receptor to PLC-gamma2, calcium release and NF-kappaB, which keep malignant B cells alive and anchored in lymph nodes. With BTK blocked, the cells lose survival and homing signals and die or leave the nodes. Its active metabolite, ACP-5862, also blocks BTK. Compared with ibrutinib, acalabrutinib hits far fewer other kinases such as ITK, TEC and EGFR, which is thought to explain lower rates of some off-target side effects.", "body": [["Lymph nodes and spleen", "Malignant B cells die or leave the nodes, so nodes and spleen shrink and blood counts improve."], ["Platelets", "Bruising and bleeding increase because BTK helps platelets respond to collagen, but serious bleeding is less frequent than with ibrutinib."], ["Heart", "Atrial fibrillation and flutter can occur, though at lower rates than with ibrutinib."], ["Brain", "Headache is very common in the first weeks of treatment and usually fades over time."], ["Immune system", "Reduced normal B-cell function raises infection risk, including PJP, PML and hepatitis B reactivation."], ["Blood", "Neutropenia, anemia and low platelets can occur."]], "journey": "Acalabrutinib is taken by mouth twice daily and absorbed quickly, peaking in under an hour. It is highly protein bound. CYP3A4 breaks it down to the active metabolite ACP-5862, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors and inducers change exposure. Most leaves in the feces with some in urine. The parent drug lasts only about an hour in blood and the metabolite about seven hours, but because BTK binding is permanent, the target stays blocked between doses.", "sidefx": "Bleeding and bruising come from BTK blockade in platelets, so it is held around surgery. Infections follow from reduced normal B-cell function. Atrial fibrillation still occurs but less often, consistent with its greater selectivity. Headache is common early. Second cancers, especially skin cancers, are listed. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The original acalabrutinib capsules needed stomach acid to dissolve, so acid-reducing drugs cut absorption. The newer tablet form removed that interaction.", "src": ["Calquence (acalabrutinib) FDA label", "Byrd JC et al. J Clin Oncol 2021 (ELEVATE-RR)", "Wang M et al. Lancet 2018"]}, "zanubrutinib": {"origin": "Zanubrutinib was discovered by BeiGene, a company founded in Beijing in 2010. Its chemists designed it to bind BTK more selectively than ibrutinib and to keep BTK fully blocked in lymph nodes as well as blood. In 2019 FDA granted accelerated approval to zanubrutinib as Brukinsa for previously treated mantle cell lymphoma, the first cancer drug discovered in China to receive FDA approval. Later approvals followed for Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia, marginal zone lymphoma and chronic lymphocytic leukemia. In the ALPINE trial it controlled relapsed CLL better than ibrutinib, with less atrial fibrillation. In 2024 it was approved with obinutuzumab for follicular lymphoma.", "timeline": [["2010", "BeiGene is founded in Beijing."], ["2019", "FDA grants accelerated approval of Brukinsa for mantle cell lymphoma."], ["2021", "FDA approves zanubrutinib for Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia and marginal zone lymphoma."], ["2023", "FDA approves zanubrutinib for CLL and SLL."], ["2024", "FDA approves zanubrutinib with obinutuzumab for follicular lymphoma."]], "cell": "Zanubrutinib forms a covalent bond with cysteine 481 in the ATP-binding pocket of Bruton tyrosine kinase, permanently disabling it. In B cells, the B-cell receptor activates LYN and SYK, which turn on BTK; BTK then phosphorylates PLC-gamma2, which drives calcium release and NF-kappaB, ERK and AKT signals for survival, growth and homing. Blocking BTK cuts these signals, so malignant B cells undergo apoptosis and lose their hold in lymph nodes and marrow. Zanubrutinib is more selective than ibrutinib, with less blockade of TEC, EGFR and ITK, and it was designed to keep near-complete BTK occupancy across the dosing interval.", "body": [["Lymph nodes and spleen", "Nodes and spleen shrink as malignant B cells die or move out."], ["Blood", "Lymphocyte counts can rise briefly as CLL cells leave nodes. In Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia, the abnormal IgM protein falls."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia and thrombocytopenia are among the most common effects."], ["Platelets and bleeding", "Bruising and bleeding increase because BTK supports platelet activation by collagen."], ["Heart", "Atrial fibrillation and flutter occur, but less often than with ibrutinib; ventricular arrhythmias are rare."], ["Immune system", "Lower normal B-cell function raises the risk of pneumonia and other infections."], ["Skin", "Second skin cancers are more common, so sun protection is commonly advised."]], "journey": "Zanubrutinib is taken by mouth once or twice daily and absorbed quickly, peaking in about two hours. It is highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A, so strong CYP3A inhibitors and inducers change exposure. Most leaves in the feces with little in urine. The plasma half-life is two to four hours, but covalent binding keeps BTK blocked until new enzyme is made.", "sidefx": "Low blood counts and infections follow from effects on normal B cells and marrow. Bleeding and bruising come from BTK blockade in platelets, so it is held around surgery. Atrial arrhythmias occur less often than with ibrutinib, in line with fewer off-target effects. Second skin cancers and liver injury are listed. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Zanubrutinib was the first cancer drug discovered in China to receive FDA approval.", "src": ["Brukinsa (zanubrutinib) FDA label", "Brown JR et al. NEJM 2023 (ALPINE)", "Tam CS et al. Blood 2020 (ASPEN)"]}, "pirtobrutinib": {"origin": "Covalent BTK inhibitors such as ibrutinib changed the care of B-cell cancers, but many tumors eventually escaped by mutating cysteine 481, the exact amino acid those drugs bond to. Scientists at Loxo Oncology, a Connecticut company known for precision cancer drugs, set out to design a BTK blocker that did not need that cysteine at all. The result, LOXO-305, binds BTK reversibly through other contacts in the pocket. Eli Lilly acquired Loxo Oncology in 2019. In the BRUIN trial, pirtobrutinib worked in people whose disease had progressed on covalent BTK inhibitors. FDA granted accelerated approval as Jaypirca in 2023 for relapsed mantle cell lymphoma after a BTK inhibitor, and later that year for CLL and SLL after prior BTK and BCL-2 inhibitor treatment.", "timeline": [["2019", "Eli Lilly acquires Loxo Oncology, developer of LOXO-305."], ["2023", "FDA grants accelerated approval of Jaypirca for relapsed or refractory mantle cell lymphoma after a BTK inhibitor."], ["2023", "FDA grants accelerated approval for CLL and SLL after prior BTK and BCL-2 inhibitors."]], "cell": "BTK relays signals from the B-cell receptor through PLC-gamma2 to calcium release and NF-kappaB, ERK and AKT, which keep malignant B cells alive and anchored in lymph nodes. Ibrutinib, acalabrutinib and zanubrutinib bond covalently to cysteine 481 in BTK. When that cysteine mutates, usually to serine, they can no longer hold on. Pirtobrutinib is a noncovalent, reversible inhibitor that fills the ATP pocket using hydrogen bonds and shape fit rather than a bond to C481. It therefore blocks both normal and C481-mutant BTK. Its long half-life and high selectivity keep BTK inhibited across a once-daily schedule, cutting survival and homing signals so the malignant B cells die or leave the nodes.", "body": [["Lymph nodes and spleen", "Nodes and spleen shrink again in disease that had escaped covalent BTK inhibitors."], ["Blood", "Neutropenia, anemia and thrombocytopenia are common, from effects on normal marrow and the disease itself."], ["Platelets and bleeding", "Bruising and bleeding increase because BTK supports platelet activation by collagen."], ["Heart", "Atrial fibrillation and flutter can occur but appear less often than with ibrutinib."], ["Immune system", "Reduced normal B-cell function raises the risk of serious infections, including pneumonia."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and fatigue are among the most common effects."]], "journey": "Pirtobrutinib is taken by mouth once daily and absorbed well, peaking in about two hours. It is highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down by CYP3A4 and by glucuronidation (UGT1A8 and UGT1A9). Unlike most kinase inhibitors, more of it leaves in urine than in feces. Its half-life is about 19 hours, much longer than covalent BTK inhibitors, which matters because a reversible drug needs steady blood levels to keep BTK blocked.", "sidefx": "Infections come from suppression of normal B cells and low neutrophils. Bleeding comes from BTK blockade in platelets, so it is held around surgery. Atrial arrhythmias and second cancers, mainly skin cancers, are listed. Liver injury has been reported. Because it is selective, rash and diarrhea from off-target kinase blockade are less prominent than with ibrutinib. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Pirtobrutinib still works when BTK has the C481S mutation, the most common way CLL becomes resistant to ibrutinib.", "src": ["Jaypirca (pirtobrutinib) FDA label", "Mato AR et al. Lancet 2021 (BRUIN)", "Mato AR et al. NEJM 2023"]}, "idelalisib": {"origin": "The phosphoinositide 3-kinase (PI3K) family was mapped in the 1980s and 1990s, and the delta isoform, found mostly in white blood cells, was described by Bart Vanhaesebroeck and colleagues in 1997. Because PI3K-delta is concentrated in immune cells, blocking it seemed a way to hit B-cell cancers while sparing other tissues. The Seattle company Calistoga Pharmaceuticals developed CAL-101, and Gilead Sciences acquired Calistoga in 2011. FDA approved idelalisib as Zydelig in 2014, the first PI3K inhibitor, for relapsed CLL with rituximab. Its accelerated approvals for follicular lymphoma and small lymphocytic lymphoma were later withdrawn in the US. It carries a boxed warning for fatal liver, gut, lung and infection toxicities.", "timeline": [["1997", "The PI3K-delta isoform, p110-delta, is described and found mainly in white blood cells."], ["2011", "Gilead acquires Calistoga Pharmaceuticals and its PI3K-delta inhibitor CAL-101."], ["2014", "FDA approves Zydelig, the first PI3K inhibitor, for relapsed CLL with rituximab."], ["2022", "Follicular lymphoma and SLL indications are withdrawn in the US."]], "cell": "Class I PI3Ks phosphorylate the membrane lipid PIP2 to make PIP3, which recruits and activates AKT and other survival kinases. In B cells, PI3K-delta (p110-delta) carries signals from the B-cell receptor, from chemokine receptors CXCR4 and CXCR5 and from integrins. Idelalisib is a selective ATP-competitive inhibitor of p110-delta. Blocking it lowers PIP3 and AKT activity, so CLL cells lose survival signals and undergo apoptosis. Loss of chemokine and integrin signaling loosens their hold in lymph nodes and marrow, so they spill into the blood. PI3K-delta is also needed by regulatory T cells, and losing those cells is thought to unleash the autoimmune-like liver, colon and lung inflammation seen with the drug.", "body": [["Lymph nodes", "Nodes shrink quickly as CLL cells die or are pushed out of their protective niches."], ["Blood", "Lymphocyte counts can rise early from redistribution, which is not progression."], ["Liver", "Immune-mediated hepatitis with transaminase rises is common, usually in the first months, and can be fatal."], ["Colon", "Severe diarrhea and colitis can appear months into therapy, from loss of regulatory T cell control in the gut."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis can occur, as can serious infections such as PJP and CMV, so PJP prophylaxis is used."], ["Skin and immune system", "Severe skin reactions such as SJS, TEN and DRESS and hypersensitivity are listed."]], "journey": "Idelalisib is taken by mouth twice daily, with or without food, and peaks in about an hour and a half. It is highly protein bound. Aldehyde oxidase and CYP3A convert it to its main metabolite, GS-563117, which is inactive against PI3K but is a strong CYP3A inhibitor, so idelalisib raises levels of many other drugs. Most leaves in the feces. Its half-life is about eight hours.", "sidefx": "The serious toxicities are immune-mediated. Blocking PI3K-delta in regulatory T cells weakens immune tolerance, so T cells attack the liver, colon and lungs, causing hepatitis, colitis and pneumonitis. Weakened B-cell and T-cell function raises the risk of PJP and CMV infections. Intestinal perforation and severe skin reactions are listed. These are managed by holding the drug and, for immune toxicity, corticosteroids. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Liver injury from idelalisib was more common in younger, previously untreated patients, likely because their immune systems were more intact.", "src": ["Zydelig (idelalisib) FDA label", "Furman RR et al. NEJM 2014", "Vanhaesebroeck B et al. PNAS 1997"]}, "duvelisib": {"origin": "Duvelisib was discovered at Intellikine, a San Diego company, and developed as IPI-145 by Infinity Pharmaceuticals in Massachusetts. It was designed to block two PI3K isoforms: delta, which drives malignant B cells, and gamma, which drives T cells and macrophages that support the tumor. Verastem Oncology licensed it in 2016. FDA approved duvelisib as Copiktra in 2018 for relapsed or refractory CLL and SLL after at least two prior therapies, and gave accelerated approval for follicular lymphoma, an indication later withdrawn. In 2022 FDA issued a safety communication about a possible increase in deaths in the DUO trial, and the label carries a boxed warning for treatment-related mortality, infections, colitis, skin reactions and pneumonitis.", "timeline": [["2010s", "Intellikine and Infinity Pharmaceuticals develop IPI-145."], ["2016", "Verastem Oncology licenses duvelisib."], ["2018", "FDA approves Copiktra for relapsed or refractory CLL and SLL."], ["2022", "FDA warns of a possible increase in deaths in the DUO trial."]], "cell": "Duvelisib is an inhibitor of two class I PI3K catalytic subunits, p110-delta and p110-gamma. PI3K-delta carries B-cell receptor and chemokine signals in malignant B cells, turning PIP2 into PIP3 and activating AKT for survival and growth. PI3K-gamma acts mainly in T cells and macrophages, carrying chemokine receptor signals that draw these cells into the tumor microenvironment, where they feed CLL cells with growth and survival signals. By blocking both, duvelisib triggers apoptosis of malignant B cells and disrupts their supporting environment. Blocking these isoforms in regulatory T cells also weakens immune tolerance, which underlies its immune-mediated toxicities.", "body": [["Lymph nodes", "Nodes shrink as CLL cells die and lose support from surrounding T cells and macrophages."], ["Colon", "Diarrhea and colitis are very common and can be severe, from immune attack on the gut lining."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis can occur, as well as serious infections such as PJP and CMV, so PJP prophylaxis is required."], ["Skin", "Rash and severe reactions such as SJS, TEN and DRESS can occur."], ["Liver", "Transaminase rises from immune-mediated injury are listed."], ["Blood", "Neutropenia and anemia are common, adding to the infection risk."]], "journey": "Duvelisib is taken by mouth twice daily and absorbed quickly, peaking in one to two hours. It is highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels and strong inducers are avoided. It also moderately inhibits CYP3A4 itself. Most leaves in the feces. The half-life is about five hours.", "sidefx": "Its serious toxicities come from immune dysregulation. Blocking PI3K-delta and gamma in regulatory T cells lets effector T cells attack the colon, lungs, skin and liver. Weakened B and T cell function plus neutropenia explain the high infection risk, including PJP and CMV, so prophylaxis continues until CD4 counts recover. The boxed warning reflects fatal cases of these effects. Steroids are used for immune toxicities. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Copiktra (duvelisib) FDA label", "Flinn IW et al. Blood 2018 (DUO)", "FDA Drug Safety Communication on Copiktra, 2022"]}, "alpelisib": {"origin": "In 2004 Yardena Samuels, Victor Velculescu and colleagues in Bert Vogelstein's lab at Johns Hopkins reported that PIK3CA, the gene for the p110-alpha subunit of PI3K, is mutated in many common cancers. PIK3CA mutations turned out to be present in about 40 percent of hormone receptor-positive breast cancers. Novartis designed alpelisib (BYL719) as the first inhibitor selective for PI3K-alpha. In the SOLAR-1 trial, adding it to fulvestrant improved disease control in PIK3CA-mutated breast cancer, and FDA approved it as Piqray in 2019. In 2022 a separate product, Vijoice, was approved for PIK3CA-Related Overgrowth Spectrum, a rare condition in which a PIK3CA mutation in some body cells causes tissue overgrowth.", "timeline": [["2004", "Johns Hopkins researchers report frequent PIK3CA mutations in human cancers."], ["2019", "FDA approves Piqray with fulvestrant for PIK3CA-mutated HR-positive, HER2-negative breast cancer."], ["2022", "FDA approves Vijoice for PIK3CA-Related Overgrowth Spectrum."]], "cell": "Class I PI3K is a lipid kinase made of a regulatory subunit and a catalytic subunit. Growth factor receptors switch it on so it turns PIP2 into PIP3, which recruits AKT to the membrane, where it is activated. AKT then drives mTOR, protein synthesis, glucose uptake and cell survival. Hotspot PIK3CA mutations, such as E545K and H1047R, keep p110-alpha active without a signal. Alpelisib is a selective ATP-competitive inhibitor of p110-alpha, including these mutants. Lower PIP3 shuts down AKT and mTOR signaling in the tumor, slowing growth. PI3K-alpha also carries the insulin receptor signal in liver, muscle and fat, so blocking it causes insulin resistance.", "body": [["Breast tumor", "PIK3CA-mutated tumors grow more slowly, especially when estrogen signaling is also blocked by fulvestrant."], ["Metabolism", "Insulin cannot signal well in liver, muscle and fat, so blood sugar rises and the pancreas releases more insulin. Severe cases can progress to ketoacidosis."], ["Skin", "Rash is common, usually in the first weeks, and severe reactions such as SJS, TEN and DRESS can occur."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea, mouth sores and appetite loss are common."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis is a listed warning, so new cough or breathlessness is evaluated."], ["Bone", "Osteonecrosis of the jaw is listed, especially with bone-targeted drugs."]], "journey": "Alpelisib is taken by mouth once daily with food, which raises absorption. It peaks in two to four hours and is highly protein bound. Unlike most kinase inhibitors, it is broken down mainly by chemical and enzymatic hydrolysis of its amide bond, with only a small CYP3A4 role. Most leaves in the feces. Its half-life is about eight to nine hours.", "sidefx": "Hyperglycemia is an on-target effect: PI3K-alpha carries the insulin signal, so blocking it creates insulin resistance. Blood sugar is optimized before starting and checked often early on. High insulin levels may in turn feed back and partly reactivate the tumor pathway. Rash and severe skin reactions appear to be immune-mediated. Diarrhea and mouth sores are common. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Piqray (alpelisib) FDA label", "Samuels Y et al. Science 2004", "Andre F et al. NEJM 2019 (SOLAR-1)"]}, "inavolisib": {"origin": "Inavolisib was designed by Genentech as GDC-0077. Earlier PI3K inhibitors blocked the normal enzyme as strongly as the mutant one, so high blood sugar limited how much could be given. Genentech scientists found a molecule that not only blocks PI3K-alpha but also causes the mutant form of the p110-alpha protein to be broken down inside cells, giving more selective action against tumor cells with PIK3CA mutations. In the INAVO120 trial, adding inavolisib to palbociclib and fulvestrant improved outcomes in PIK3CA-mutated, hormone receptor-positive, HER2-negative breast cancer that had come back during or soon after hormone therapy. FDA approved it as Itovebi in 2024.", "timeline": [["2010s", "Genentech discovers GDC-0077, later named inavolisib."], ["2024", "FDA approves Itovebi with palbociclib and fulvestrant for endocrine-resistant PIK3CA-mutated breast cancer."]], "cell": "PIK3CA mutations keep the p110-alpha catalytic subunit of PI3K active, so it keeps making PIP3, which activates AKT and mTOR and drives tumor cell growth and survival. Inavolisib binds the ATP site of p110-alpha and blocks its kinase activity. In cells with mutant PIK3CA, it also triggers degradation of the mutant p110-alpha protein, which lowers pathway signaling more durably. Less AKT and mTOR activity means slower tumor growth. It adds to CDK4/6 inhibition by palbociclib, which stops cells in G1, and estrogen receptor blockade by fulvestrant, attacking three linked growth signals. Because PI3K-alpha also carries the insulin signal, high blood sugar is expected.", "body": [["Breast tumor", "Combined blockade of PI3K, CDK4/6 and the estrogen receptor slows growth of PIK3CA-mutated breast cancer."], ["Metabolism", "Insulin signaling in liver, muscle and fat is reduced, so blood sugar rises; ketoacidosis has occurred."], ["Mouth", "Stomatitis (mouth sores) is very common, so preventive mouthwash has been used."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea and appetite loss are frequent."], ["Blood", "Neutropenia, anemia and low platelets are common, largely from the palbociclib partner."], ["Skin", "Rash can occur but appears less often than with alpelisib."]], "journey": "Inavolisib is taken by mouth once daily with or without food. It peaks in about three hours and, unusually for a kinase inhibitor, is only lightly bound to plasma proteins. It is cleared mainly by hydrolysis with very little CYP metabolism, so CYP drug interactions are few. It leaves in roughly equal amounts in urine and feces. Its half-life is about 15 hours.", "sidefx": "Hyperglycemia is an on-target effect of blocking PI3K-alpha in insulin-responsive tissues, so glucose is optimized before starting and checked often in early weeks. Mouth sores and diarrhea reflect PI3K roles in rapidly renewing lining cells. Low blood counts come mostly from palbociclib. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Inavolisib is described as mutant-selective because it pushes the mutant PI3K-alpha protein toward destruction more than the normal one.", "src": ["Itovebi (inavolisib) FDA label", "Turner NC et al. NEJM 2024 (INAVO120)"]}, "capivasertib": {"origin": "Capivasertib came from a research collaboration between AstraZeneca, the UK company Astex Therapeutics and the Institute of Cancer Research in London, supported by Cancer Research Technology. Using fragment-based and structure-guided design, the team built AZD5363, a molecule that fits the ATP pocket of all three AKT isoforms. AKT sits at the center of the PI3K pathway, so blocking it could help tumors with PIK3CA, AKT1 or PTEN changes. In the CAPItello-291 trial, adding capivasertib to fulvestrant improved disease control in hormone receptor-positive, HER2-negative advanced breast cancer, especially with these alterations. FDA approved it as Truqap in 2023 for breast cancers with one or more of these changes. Its use was later extended to PTEN-deficient prostate cancer with abiraterone.", "timeline": [["2000s", "AstraZeneca, Astex and the Institute of Cancer Research collaborate on AKT inhibitors."], ["2023", "FDA approves Truqap with fulvestrant for HR-positive, HER2-negative breast cancer with PIK3CA, AKT1 or PTEN alterations."]], "cell": "AKT is a serine/threonine kinase activated when PI3K makes PIP3 at the cell membrane. PTEN normally removes that signal by turning PIP3 back into PIP2. When PIK3CA is mutated, PTEN is lost or AKT1 itself is mutated (such as E17K), AKT stays on. Active AKT phosphorylates many targets, such as PRAS40 and GSK3-beta, and activates mTORC1, driving protein synthesis, growth and survival. Capivasertib is an ATP-competitive inhibitor of all three isoforms, AKT1, AKT2 and AKT3. Blocking them cuts these downstream signals and slows tumor growth. AKT2 carries much of the insulin signal in liver and muscle, so blocking it raises blood sugar.", "body": [["Tumors", "Breast and prostate tumors with PI3K/AKT pathway changes grow more slowly when AKT is blocked along with hormone signaling."], ["Metabolism", "AKT2 blockade causes insulin resistance, so blood sugar rises; ketoacidosis has occurred."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is the most common effect and can be severe."], ["Skin", "Rash is common, and severe reactions such as erythema multiforme and DRESS can occur."], ["Mouth", "Mouth sores are frequent, reflecting AKT roles in lining cells."], ["Blood", "Lymphopenia and anemia can occur during treatment."]], "journey": "Capivasertib is taken by mouth twice daily on an intermittent schedule, four days on and three days off each week. The breaks allow insulin signaling to recover. It peaks in one to two hours and is moderately protein bound. The liver clears it by CYP3A4 and UGT2B7 glucuronidation, so strong CYP3A inhibitors and inducers matter. It leaves in about equal amounts in feces and urine. Its half-life is about eight hours.", "sidefx": "High blood sugar comes from blocking AKT2 in insulin signaling, so glucose and HbA1c are checked before and during treatment. Diarrhea and rash are common and appear linked to AKT roles in gut and skin cells. Severe skin reactions are listed. There is no antidote; holds and dose reductions are used.", "fun": "The four-days-on, three-days-off schedule was chosen so that tumor AKT is hit hard while giving the body short breaks to restore normal insulin signaling.", "src": ["Truqap (capivasertib) FDA label", "Turner NC et al. NEJM 2023 (CAPItello-291)", "Davies BR et al. Mol Cancer Ther 2012"]}, "everolimus": {"origin": "Everolimus descends from a soil microbe. In 1964 a Canadian scientific expedition collected soil on Easter Island, known as Rapa Nui. In the early 1970s Suren Sehgal at Ayerst Research Laboratories in Montreal isolated an antifungal compound made by the bacterium Streptomyces hygroscopicus from that soil and named it rapamycin. Rapamycin (sirolimus) turned out to be a strong immune suppressor, and its target was named mTOR, the mechanistic target of rapamycin. Chemists at Sandoz, later Novartis, added a small hydroxyethyl chain to rapamycin to improve its properties, creating everolimus. FDA approved it as Afinitor in 2009 for advanced kidney cancer, and as Zortress in 2010 to prevent kidney transplant rejection. Later approvals covered breast cancer, neuroendocrine tumors and tumors and seizures in tuberous sclerosis complex.", "timeline": [["1964", "A Canadian expedition collects soil on Easter Island (Rapa Nui)."], ["1970s", "Suren Sehgal at Ayerst isolates rapamycin from Streptomyces hygroscopicus."], ["1990s", "Sandoz chemists modify rapamycin to create everolimus."], ["2009", "FDA approves Afinitor for advanced renal cell carcinoma."], ["2010", "FDA approves Zortress for kidney transplant and Afinitor for SEGA in tuberous sclerosis."], ["2012", "FDA approves everolimus with exemestane for HR-positive advanced breast cancer."]], "cell": "Everolimus enters the cell and binds FKBP-12, a small binding protein. The everolimus-FKBP-12 complex attaches to mTOR when it sits in mTOR complex 1 (mTORC1) and blocks it. mTORC1 normally senses growth factors, nutrients and energy and then phosphorylates S6 kinase 1 and 4E-BP1 to switch on protein synthesis. With mTORC1 blocked, cells make less protein and less cyclin D, so they arrest in G1. HIF-1-alpha and VEGF production also fall, reducing blood vessel growth. In tuberous sclerosis, loss of TSC1 or TSC2 removes the natural brake on mTORC1, and everolimus restores that brake. In T cells, mTOR blockade stops IL-2-driven proliferation, which explains its use in transplant.", "body": [["Tumors", "Kidney, breast and neuroendocrine tumors grow more slowly, and tuberous sclerosis tumors such as SEGA and kidney angiomyolipomas shrink."], ["Brain", "In tuberous sclerosis complex, overactive mTOR in brain cells fuels seizures, and everolimus reduces seizure frequency."], ["Mouth", "Stomatitis (mouth ulcers) is the most common effect; steroid mouthwash is often used to prevent it."], ["Lungs", "Noninfectious pneumonitis is a class effect that can be serious."], ["Immune system", "Suppressed T-cell proliferation raises infection risk, including PJP and hepatitis B reactivation."], ["Metabolism", "mTOR helps regulate insulin action and lipid handling, so blood sugar, cholesterol and triglycerides rise."], ["Kidneys and healing", "Kidney injury and protein in urine can occur, and wounds heal slowly."]], "journey": "Everolimus is taken by mouth once daily, consistently with or without food. It is absorbed quickly, peaking in one to two hours, and concentrates in red blood cells. It is a substrate for both CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein, so many drugs and grapefruit change its levels. Most leaves in the feces. Its half-life is about 30 hours. Blood trough levels are monitored for tuberous sclerosis uses and for transplant. Afinitor tablets and Afinitor Disperz are not interchangeable.", "sidefx": "Mouth sores and pneumonitis are classic mTOR inhibitor effects linked to blocked cell renewal and immune effects in lining tissues. Infection risk reflects suppressed T-cell proliferation. High blood sugar and lipids come from mTOR roles in insulin signaling and fat metabolism. Angioedema is more common with ACE inhibitors. Poor wound healing reflects blocked cell growth. There is no antidote; dose holds and steroids for pneumonitis are used.", "fun": "Rapamycin, the parent of everolimus, is named after Rapa Nui, the native name of Easter Island, where the soil sample came from.", "src": ["Afinitor and Zortress (everolimus) FDA labels", "Vezina C, Kudelski A, Sehgal SN. J Antibiot 1975", "Motzer RJ et al. Lancet 2008 (RECORD-1)"]}, "palbociclib": {"origin": "The story starts with the cell cycle. In 1982 Tim Hunt discovered cyclins in sea urchin eggs, work that later shared the 2001 Nobel Prize with Leland Hartwell and Paul Nurse. Cyclin D partners with CDK4 and CDK6 to push cells out of rest and into DNA copying. Chemists at Parke-Davis in Ann Arbor, later part of Pfizer, designed PD-0332991 in the early 2000s as a highly selective CDK4/6 inhibitor. It sat largely unused until Richard Finn and Dennis Slamon at UCLA showed that estrogen receptor-positive breast cancer cell lines were especially sensitive. The PALOMA trials followed, and in 2015 FDA granted accelerated approval to palbociclib as Ibrance, the first CDK4/6 inhibitor, with letrozole for advanced breast cancer.", "timeline": [["1982", "Tim Hunt discovers cyclins in sea urchin eggs."], ["2000s", "Parke-Davis and Pfizer chemists design PD-0332991."], ["2009", "UCLA researchers show ER-positive breast cancer cells are very sensitive to the drug."], ["2015", "FDA approves Ibrance, the first CDK4/6 inhibitor, with letrozole."], ["2016", "FDA approves palbociclib with fulvestrant after endocrine therapy."]], "cell": "In G1 phase, growth signals and estrogen raise cyclin D, which binds and activates CDK4 and CDK6. The cyclin D-CDK4/6 complex phosphorylates the retinoblastoma protein (Rb). Phosphorylated Rb releases E2F transcription factors, which switch on genes for DNA synthesis and push the cell past the G1 restriction point into S phase. Palbociclib is a selective reversible inhibitor of CDK4 and CDK6. With them blocked, Rb stays unphosphorylated, keeps E2F bound and the cell arrests in G1. This is mainly cytostatic. Tumors need intact Rb to respond. In ER-positive breast cancer, estrogen drives cyclin D, so combining palbociclib with endocrine therapy blocks both the signal and its key output.", "body": [["Breast tumor", "HR-positive cancer cells stop dividing, and paired with hormone therapy, disease control lasts much longer."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutrophil precursors depend on CDK6, so neutropenia is very common. It is reversible and recovers in the week off, and febrile neutropenia is uncommon."], ["Blood", "Anemia and low platelets can also occur."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease and pneumonitis are rare but serious warnings."], ["Hair and mouth", "Thinning hair and mouth sores are common and usually mild."], ["Gut", "Nausea and diarrhea occur and are usually mild."]], "journey": "Palbociclib is taken by mouth once daily for three weeks, then one week off. Tablets can be taken with or without food; the older capsules were taken with food. It is absorbed slowly, peaking in six to twelve hours. It spreads widely into tissues. The liver clears it mainly by CYP3A and SULT2A1, so strong CYP3A inhibitors and grapefruit raise levels. Most leaves in the feces. Its half-life is about 29 hours.", "sidefx": "Neutropenia is on-target: blood-forming cells depend on CDK6 to divide, so their output pauses, but because they are arrested rather than killed, counts recover in the week off. This is why febrile neutropenia is less common than with chemotherapy. Fatigue, mouth sores and hair thinning reflect slowed turnover of fast-dividing cells. Pneumonitis is rare. There is no antidote; dose holds and reductions are used.", "fun": "Palbociclib was made around 2001 but sat on the shelf for years until UCLA lab tests pointed to ER-positive breast cancer.", "src": ["Ibrance (palbociclib) FDA label", "Finn RS et al. Breast Cancer Res 2009", "Finn RS et al. Lancet Oncol 2015 (PALOMA-1)", "Evans T et al. Cell 1983"]}, "ribociclib": {"origin": "Ribociclib was discovered through a collaboration between Novartis and Astex Pharmaceuticals and developed by Novartis as LEE011. It followed palbociclib as the second CDK4/6 inhibitor approved. In the MONALEESA-2 trial, adding it to letrozole extended disease control in HR-positive, HER2-negative advanced breast cancer, and FDA approved it as Kisqali in 2017. Later trials showed it extended overall survival, including in premenopausal women in MONALEESA-7. In 2024 FDA approved it as adjuvant therapy with an aromatase inhibitor for a broad group of people with early breast cancer at high risk of recurrence, based on the NATALEE trial. It is also sold in a co-pack with letrozole.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Novartis and Astex discover LEE011."], ["2017", "FDA approves Kisqali with an aromatase inhibitor for HR-positive, HER2-negative advanced breast cancer."], ["2018", "Approval expands to premenopausal women and to use with fulvestrant."], ["2024", "FDA approves ribociclib as adjuvant therapy for high-risk early breast cancer."]], "cell": "In G1, cyclin D pairs with CDK4 and CDK6, and the complex phosphorylates the retinoblastoma protein (Rb). This releases E2F transcription factors, which turn on genes for DNA synthesis and push the cell into S phase. Ribociclib selectively inhibits CDK4 and CDK6, with somewhat more potency for CDK4. Rb stays unphosphorylated and holds E2F, so cells stay in G1. Estrogen drives cyclin D in HR-positive breast cancer, so pairing ribociclib with endocrine therapy blocks the pathway at two levels. Separately, ribociclib prolongs the QT interval in a concentration-dependent way by affecting cardiac potassium currents involved in repolarization.", "body": [["Breast tumor", "Cancer cells stop dividing, and with endocrine therapy this extends disease control and survival."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia is very common because blood cell precursors rely on CDK6, but it is reversible."], ["Heart", "QT prolongation is dose-related, so ECGs are done before and during early treatment; combining with tamoxifen increases this effect."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises can occur, so liver tests are checked every two weeks at first."], ["Lungs and skin", "Pneumonitis and severe skin reactions such as SJS, TEN and DRESS are listed warnings."], ["Gut and hair", "Nausea, diarrhea and hair thinning are common."]], "journey": "Ribociclib is taken by mouth once daily for three weeks then one week off, with or without food. It peaks in one to four hours and is moderately protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A4, and ribociclib itself inhibits CYP3A4, so it raises levels of some other drugs. Grapefruit is avoided. Most leaves in the feces. Its half-life is about 30 to 32 hours.", "sidefx": "Neutropenia is on-target CDK6 blockade in marrow precursors and recovers during the week off. QT prolongation is a distinct effect of ribociclib among CDK4/6 inhibitors, from effects on cardiac repolarization; low potassium or magnesium and other QT drugs add risk. Liver injury is more common than with palbociclib. Pneumonitis is rare. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Kisqali (ribociclib) FDA label", "Hortobagyi GN et al. NEJM 2016 (MONALEESA-2)", "Im SA et al. NEJM 2019 (MONALEESA-7)", "Slamon D et al. NEJM 2024 (NATALEE)"]}, "abemaciclib": {"origin": "Abemaciclib was discovered at Eli Lilly in Indianapolis as LY2835219. It was designed to block CDK4 more strongly than CDK6 and to be taken continuously, without the one-week break needed for other CDK4/6 inhibitors. In the MONARCH 1 trial it shrank tumors as a single drug in heavily treated HR-positive breast cancer, something the other drugs in its class had not shown. FDA approved it as Verzenio in 2017, both with fulvestrant and alone. In 2021, based on the monarchE trial, it became the first CDK4/6 inhibitor approved for early breast cancer at high risk of recurrence, given with endocrine therapy after surgery.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Eli Lilly discovers LY2835219."], ["2017", "FDA approves Verzenio with fulvestrant and as a single agent for advanced HR-positive breast cancer."], ["2018", "FDA approves abemaciclib with an aromatase inhibitor as first-line therapy."], ["2021", "FDA approves abemaciclib as adjuvant therapy for high-risk early breast cancer."]], "cell": "Abemaciclib inhibits cyclin D-CDK4 and cyclin D-CDK6, with greater potency for CDK4. These complexes normally phosphorylate the retinoblastoma protein (Rb), which then releases E2F transcription factors and lets the cell pass the G1 restriction point into S phase. With CDK4/6 blocked, Rb holds on to E2F and the cell stays in G1. At higher levels abemaciclib also inhibits other kinases, which may explain why it can work alone. Its lower effect on CDK6, the more important driver in marrow precursors, allows continuous twice-daily dosing with milder neutropenia. It also inhibits kidney tubule transporters OCT2, MATE1 and MATE2-K, which reduces creatinine secretion.", "body": [["Breast tumor", "Cancer cells arrest in G1, and with endocrine therapy, recurrence risk falls in early disease and control lasts longer in advanced disease."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is the most common effect, often starting in the first week, and can cause dehydration. The label calls for antidiarrheals at the first sign of loose stools."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia occurs but is usually milder than with palbociclib or ribociclib."], ["Kidneys", "Serum creatinine rises because tubular secretion is blocked, without true loss of filtering function."], ["Blood vessels", "Venous clots, including pulmonary embolism, are a listed warning."], ["Liver and lungs", "Liver enzyme rises and pneumonitis can occur."]], "journey": "Abemaciclib is taken by mouth twice daily without a break week, with or without food. It is absorbed slowly, peaking around eight hours, and is highly protein bound. CYP3A4 in the liver converts it into several active metabolites, M2, M18 and M20, which add to its effect, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors and inducers matter and grapefruit is avoided. Most leaves in the feces. Its half-life is about 18 hours. It also reaches the brain in measurable amounts.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea is the signature effect, linked to blockade of kinases in the gut lining, and it causes dehydration if untreated. Neutropenia is milder because CDK4 is targeted more than CDK6. Creatinine rises from blocked tubular transporters, not kidney damage. Clots, liver injury and pneumonitis are listed. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Abemaciclib can raise serum creatinine without harming the kidneys, simply by blocking the transporters that secrete creatinine into urine.", "src": ["Verzenio (abemaciclib) FDA label", "Dickler MN et al. Clin Cancer Res 2017 (MONARCH 1)", "Johnston SRD et al. J Clin Oncol 2020 (monarchE)"]}, "ruxolitinib": {"origin": "In 2005 several research groups, including teams led by William Vainchenker in France, Radek Skoda in Switzerland, Anthony Green in the UK and D. Gary Gilliland in Boston, reported the same discovery: a single mutation, JAK2 V617F, is present in most people with polycythemia vera and in many with essential thrombocythemia and myelofibrosis. That made JAK2 an obvious target. Incyte, a company in Wilmington, Delaware, had already been designing JAK inhibitors and developed INCB018424, a JAK1 and JAK2 blocker. In 2011 FDA approved it as Jakafi, the first drug for myelofibrosis. Approvals followed for polycythemia vera after hydroxyurea in 2014, steroid-refractory acute graft-versus-host disease in 2019 and chronic GVHD in 2021. Novartis markets it outside the US as Jakavi.", "timeline": [["2005", "Several groups independently report the JAK2 V617F mutation in myeloproliferative neoplasms."], ["2011", "FDA approves Jakafi, the first drug for myelofibrosis."], ["2014", "FDA approves ruxolitinib for polycythemia vera after hydroxyurea."], ["2019", "FDA approves ruxolitinib for steroid-refractory acute graft-versus-host disease."], ["2021", "FDA approves ruxolitinib for chronic graft-versus-host disease."]], "cell": "Many cytokine and growth factor receptors, including those for erythropoietin, thrombopoietin, IL-6 and interferons, have no kinase of their own. They rely on attached Janus kinases (JAK1, JAK2, JAK3, TYK2). When the cytokine binds, the JAKs phosphorylate each other and the receptor, which then recruits STAT proteins. The JAKs phosphorylate STATs, which pair up, move to the nucleus and switch on genes for blood cell growth and inflammation. The JAK2 V617F mutation disrupts the inhibitory pseudokinase domain, so JAK2 signals without a cytokine. Ruxolitinib is an ATP-competitive inhibitor of JAK1 and JAK2. It reduces STAT3 and STAT5 phosphorylation, which lowers abnormal blood cell production and cuts inflammatory cytokine signaling. It does not specifically target the mutant enzyme, so normal JAK2 signaling is also reduced.", "body": [["Spleen", "The enlarged spleen of myelofibrosis shrinks, often within weeks, because overactive blood-forming cells there are suppressed."], ["Whole body", "Night sweats, itching, fevers, fatigue and weight loss improve as inflammatory cytokine signaling through JAK1 falls."], ["Blood", "In polycythemia vera, red cell production falls and hematocrit can be controlled. Anemia and thrombocytopenia are dose-related because erythropoietin and thrombopoietin signal through JAK2."], ["Immune system", "JAK1 carries interferon and IL-6 signals, so infection risk rises, including herpes zoster, tuberculosis, hepatitis B reactivation and PML. In GVHD, this immune suppression calms donor T-cell attack."], ["Skin", "Non-melanoma skin cancers are more common."], ["Metabolism", "Weight gain and higher cholesterol are common."]], "journey": "Ruxolitinib is taken by mouth and absorbed almost completely, peaking in about an hour or two. It is highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A4, with some CYP2C9, into active metabolites, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors call for dose changes. Metabolites leave mostly in urine. The half-life is short, about three hours, but the effect on JAK signaling lasts longer. Stopping suddenly can cause symptoms to return quickly, so it is usually tapered.", "sidefx": "Anemia and thrombocytopenia are on-target JAK2 effects because erythropoietin and thrombopoietin need JAK2 to signal. Infections come from loss of JAK1-dependent interferon and cytokine signals. A sudden stop can unleash a rebound of cytokines and splenic growth, called discontinuation syndrome, which is why tapering is described. JAK inhibitor class warnings include major cardiovascular events, clots and cancers. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Ruxolitinib is also made as a cream, Opzelura, for eczema and vitiligo, where it calms local JAK-driven inflammation in the skin.", "src": ["Jakafi (ruxolitinib) FDA label", "Verstovsek S et al. NEJM 2012 (COMFORT-I)", "James C et al. Nature 2005", "Kralovics R et al. NEJM 2005"]}, "fedratinib": {"origin": "Fedratinib was discovered at TargeGen, a San Diego company, as TG101348, a JAK2-selective inhibitor. Sanofi acquired TargeGen in 2010 and took the drug into large trials for myelofibrosis. In 2013 several patients developed suspected Wernicke encephalopathy, a brain disorder caused by thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency. FDA put trials on clinical hold and Sanofi stopped development. Impact Biomedicines acquired the drug in 2016, reviewed the cases and showed that the risk could be managed by checking and supplementing thiamine. Celgene bought Impact in 2018, and FDA approved fedratinib as Inrebic in 2019 for intermediate-2 or high-risk myelofibrosis, with a boxed warning for encephalopathy. It became part of Bristol Myers Squibb after that company acquired Celgene.", "timeline": [["2010", "Sanofi acquires TargeGen and its JAK2 inhibitor TG101348."], ["2013", "Trials are placed on hold after suspected Wernicke encephalopathy cases."], ["2016", "Impact Biomedicines acquires fedratinib."], ["2018", "Celgene acquires Impact Biomedicines."], ["2019", "FDA approves Inrebic for myelofibrosis with a boxed warning for encephalopathy."]], "cell": "In myelofibrosis, JAK2 is often driven by the V617F mutation or by mutations in CALR or MPL that switch on the thrombopoietin receptor-JAK2 pathway. Active JAK2 phosphorylates STAT3 and STAT5, which drive abnormal megakaryocyte and myeloid growth and release of fibrosis-promoting and inflammatory cytokines. Fedratinib is an ATP-competitive inhibitor that is selective for JAK2 over JAK1, JAK3 and TYK2, and it also blocks FLT3. Reducing STAT signaling shrinks the spleen and eases symptoms. Separately, fedratinib inhibits the intestinal thiamine transporter, which lowers thiamine absorption and explains the risk of Wernicke encephalopathy.", "body": [["Spleen", "Enlarged spleens shrink as JAK2-driven blood cell overgrowth is suppressed, including in patients who previously took ruxolitinib."], ["Whole body", "Night sweats, fatigue, belly fullness and other symptoms improve."], ["Brain", "Low thiamine can cause Wernicke encephalopathy, with confusion, eye movement problems and unsteady walking, which can be fatal."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are very common, especially early, and worsen thiamine loss."], ["Blood", "Anemia and thrombocytopenia come from JAK2 blockade of erythropoietin and thrombopoietin signaling."], ["Liver and pancreas", "Liver tests, amylase and lipase can rise."]], "journey": "Fedratinib is taken by mouth once daily as capsules. A high-fat meal can reduce nausea. It peaks in two to four hours and is highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down by CYP3A4, CYP2C19 and flavin-containing monooxygenase, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels. It also inhibits several CYP enzymes itself. Most leaves in the feces. The effective half-life is about 41 hours, which allows once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Encephalopathy comes from thiamine deficiency, which the drug promotes by blocking the intestinal thiamine transporter and by causing vomiting and diarrhea. Thiamine levels are checked before and during treatment, and suspected encephalopathy is treated with intravenous thiamine. Anemia and low platelets are on-target JAK2 effects. GI upset is common. JAK inhibitor class warnings for heart events, clots and cancers apply. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Fedratinib was abandoned once because of a vitamin deficiency risk and then revived after another company showed the problem could be managed with thiamine testing and supplements.", "src": ["Inrebic (fedratinib) FDA label", "Pardanani A et al. JAMA Oncol 2015 (JAKARTA)", "Harrison CN et al. Lancet Haematol 2017 (JAKARTA-2)"]}, "pacritinib": {"origin": "Pacritinib is a macrocyclic molecule, built as a large ring, that was discovered by S*BIO, a drug company in Singapore, as SB1518. CTI BioPharma in Seattle took over development and focused on a hard group: people with myelofibrosis whose platelet counts were already very low, who could not safely take full doses of ruxolitinib. In 2016 FDA placed trials on full clinical hold over concerns about deaths, bleeding and heart events, and the hold was lifted in 2017 after review. In 2022 FDA granted accelerated approval as Vonjo for myelofibrosis with platelet counts below a low threshold, based on the PERSIST-2 trial. The Swedish company Sobi acquired CTI BioPharma in 2023.", "timeline": [["2000s", "S*BIO in Singapore discovers SB1518, later named pacritinib."], ["2016", "FDA places pacritinib trials on full clinical hold."], ["2017", "The clinical hold is lifted."], ["2022", "FDA grants accelerated approval of Vonjo for myelofibrosis with severe thrombocytopenia."], ["2023", "Sobi acquires CTI BioPharma."]], "cell": "Pacritinib is an ATP-competitive inhibitor of JAK2 (normal and V617F) with little effect on JAK1. It also blocks FLT3, IRAK1 and ACVR1 (ALK2). JAK2 blockade reduces STAT3 and STAT5 signaling that drives abnormal myeloid growth and spleen enlargement. IRAK1 relays signals from IL-1 and toll-like receptors toward NF-kappaB, so blocking it lowers inflammatory signaling. ACVR1 blockade reduces hepcidin, which may help iron availability. Because it spares JAK1 and appears to cause less suppression of platelet production than other JAK inhibitors, it can be used when platelets are very low.", "body": [["Spleen", "Spleen size falls in people with low platelet counts who have limited other options."], ["Whole body", "Symptoms such as fatigue, early fullness and night sweats improve."], ["Blood", "Platelet counts tend to stay stable rather than falling as much as with other JAK inhibitors, but thrombocytopenia and anemia still occur."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is the most common effect, usually early and mild to moderate."], ["Bleeding", "Bleeding, including fatal brain and GI bleeds, is a listed warning given the very low platelet counts in this group."], ["Heart", "QT prolongation and cardiac events are listed, so ECGs are done before and during treatment."]], "journey": "Pacritinib is taken by mouth twice daily as capsules. It peaks in about four to five hours and is almost fully protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors and inducers are avoided. Most leaves in the feces. Its half-life is about 28 hours. Doses are held before planned surgery because of bleeding risk.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea and nausea are common GI effects. Bleeding risk is high mainly because of the underlying very low platelet counts, and anticoagulants and antiplatelets are reviewed. QT prolongation and heart events are listed. Infections and JAK class warnings for clots and cancers apply. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Pacritinib's ring-shaped (macrocyclic) structure is unusual among JAK inhibitors.", "src": ["Vonjo (pacritinib) FDA label", "Mascarenhas J et al. JAMA Oncol 2018 (PERSIST-2)"]}, "momelotinib": {"origin": "Momelotinib was discovered by Cytopia, a biotech company in Melbourne, Australia, as CYT387, a JAK1 and JAK2 inhibitor. Cytopia merged into the Canadian company YM Biosciences, which Gilead Sciences acquired in 2013. Early trials noticed something unusual: many patients with myelofibrosis became less dependent on red cell transfusions, the opposite of what other JAK inhibitors did. Researchers traced this to blockade of ACVR1, which lowers hepcidin, the hormone that locks iron away. Sierra Oncology licensed the drug in 2018 and ran the MOMENTUM trial in anemic patients. GSK acquired Sierra in 2022, and FDA approved momelotinib as Ojjaara in 2023 for myelofibrosis in adults with anemia.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Cytopia in Australia discovers CYT387."], ["2013", "Gilead acquires YM Biosciences and momelotinib."], ["2018", "Sierra Oncology licenses momelotinib."], ["2022", "GSK acquires Sierra Oncology."], ["2023", "FDA approves Ojjaara for myelofibrosis with anemia."]], "cell": "Momelotinib is an ATP-competitive inhibitor of JAK1 and JAK2 (including V617F) and of ACVR1, also called ALK2. JAK2 and JAK1 blockade reduces STAT signaling that drives myeloid overgrowth, spleen enlargement and inflammatory cytokine release. In myelofibrosis, inflammation and BMP signaling through ACVR1 raise hepcidin made by the liver. Hepcidin causes the iron exporter ferroportin to be broken down, trapping iron in gut cells and macrophages, so red cell precursors starve for iron and anemia worsens. By blocking ACVR1, momelotinib lowers hepcidin, releases stored iron and supports red blood cell production.", "body": [["Spleen", "The spleen shrinks as JAK-driven overgrowth is reduced."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue, night sweats and other symptoms improve as cytokine signaling falls."], ["Red blood cells", "Lower hepcidin frees iron for red cell production, so hemoglobin often improves and transfusion needs fall."], ["Platelets and white cells", "Thrombocytopenia and neutropenia can occur from JAK2 blockade."], ["Immune system", "Infections, including bacterial infections and hepatitis B reactivation, are more likely."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises can occur, so liver tests are checked."]], "journey": "Momelotinib is taken by mouth once daily, with or without food. It peaks in about two hours and is highly protein bound. Several liver CYP enzymes, including CYP3A4, CYP2C8, CYP2C9, CYP2C19 and CYP1A2, break it down, forming an active metabolite called M21. Most leaves in the feces. Its half-life is about four to eight hours.", "sidefx": "Low platelets and neutrophils come from JAK2 blockade in the marrow. Infections come from JAK1 blockade of interferon and other immune signals. Liver injury and severe skin reactions are listed. JAK inhibitor class warnings for heart events, clots and cancers apply. Symptoms can return when the drug is interrupted, so tapering is described. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Most JAK inhibitors worsen anemia, but momelotinib often improves it by unlocking iron through ACVR1 blockade.", "src": ["Ojjaara (momelotinib) FDA label", "Verstovsek S et al. Lancet 2023 (MOMENTUM)", "Asshoff M et al. Blood 2017"]}, "midostaurin": {"origin": "Midostaurin starts with a soil bacterium. In 1977 Satoshi Omura and colleagues at the Kitasato Institute in Japan isolated staurosporine from a Streptomyces species. Staurosporine blocked almost every protein kinase, which made it a useful lab tool but too unselective to be a drug. In the 1980s chemists at Ciba-Geigy, later Novartis, added a benzoyl group to make a more selective protein kinase C inhibitor, CGP 41251 or PKC412. It was later found to block FLT3, which is mutated in about a third of acute myeloid leukemias, and KIT D816V, the driver of systemic mastocytosis. The large RATIFY trial, led by Richard Stone, showed that adding it to chemotherapy improved survival in FLT3-mutated AML. In 2017 FDA approved midostaurin as Rydapt for both FLT3-mutated AML and advanced systemic mastocytosis.", "timeline": [["1977", "Satoshi Omura's group isolates staurosporine from a Streptomyces species."], ["1980s", "Ciba-Geigy chemists create midostaurin (PKC412) from staurosporine."], ["1996", "FLT3 internal tandem duplications are reported in AML."], ["2017", "FDA approves Rydapt for FLT3-mutated AML with chemotherapy and for advanced systemic mastocytosis."]], "cell": "Midostaurin is a type I ATP-competitive multikinase inhibitor. Its key targets are FLT3, including internal tandem duplication (ITD) and tyrosine kinase domain (TKD) mutants, and KIT, including D816V, plus PDGFR, VEGFR2 and protein kinase C. FLT3 normally signals only when its ligand binds, but ITD and TKD mutations keep it switched on, driving myeloblast survival and growth through STAT5, PI3K-AKT and RAS-ERK. Blocking FLT3 autophosphorylation shuts these down and triggers apoptosis of leukemic cells. In systemic mastocytosis, KIT D816V keeps mast cells growing and releasing histamine and other mediators. Blocking it reduces mast cell numbers and mediator release. Its two active metabolites add to the effect.", "body": [["Leukemia", "Added to induction and consolidation chemotherapy, it improves survival in newly diagnosed FLT3-mutated AML."], ["Mast cells", "In advanced systemic mastocytosis, mast cell burden and tryptase fall, and organ damage from mast cell infiltration can improve."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are very common, so antiemetics are given before doses; diarrhea also occurs."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease and pneumonitis, some fatal, are listed warnings."], ["Heart", "QT prolongation can occur, adding to risk from other QT-prolonging drugs used in leukemia care."], ["Blood", "Myelosuppression and febrile neutropenia add to those from chemotherapy."]], "journey": "Midostaurin is taken by mouth as soft capsules twice daily with food, which helps absorption. It peaks in one to three hours, later with food, and is almost completely protein bound. CYP3A4 in the liver converts it into two active metabolites, CGP62221 and CGP52421. The parent half-life is about 21 hours, CGP62221 about 32 hours, and CGP52421 extremely long, about 20 days. Nearly all leaves in the feces. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels and strong inducers are avoided.", "sidefx": "Nausea and vomiting are frequent, probably from effects on gut and brain kinases, so antiemetic prophylaxis is used. Pulmonary toxicity is a serious warning. Myelosuppression adds to chemotherapy's effects. Its broad kinase profile explains a wide range of side effects. QT prolongation is listed. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Satoshi Omura, whose lab found staurosporine, shared the 2015 Nobel Prize for discovering avermectin, the parent of ivermectin.", "src": ["Rydapt (midostaurin) FDA label", "Stone RM et al. NEJM 2017 (RATIFY)", "Omura S et al. J Antibiot 1977", "Gotlib J et al. NEJM 2016"]}, "gilteritinib": {"origin": "Gilteritinib was discovered by the Japanese company Astellas Pharma in collaboration with Kotobuki Pharmaceutical and developed as ASP2215. It was designed to block FLT3 more potently and selectively than earlier multikinase drugs and, unlike some others, to hit both main types of FLT3 mutation, ITD and TKD. It also blocks AXL, a kinase linked to resistance. In 2018 FDA approved it as Xospata for relapsed or refractory FLT3-mutated AML, the first FLT3 inhibitor approved as a single agent for this setting. The ADMIRAL trial then showed it extended survival compared with salvage chemotherapy.", "timeline": [["2010s", "Astellas and Kotobuki Pharmaceutical discover ASP2215."], ["2018", "FDA approves Xospata for relapsed or refractory FLT3-mutated AML."], ["2019", "The ADMIRAL trial reports longer survival than salvage chemotherapy."]], "cell": "FLT3 is a receptor tyrosine kinase on blood-forming progenitors. In about a third of AML cases, an internal tandem duplication (ITD) in the juxtamembrane region or a point mutation in the tyrosine kinase domain (TKD, often at D835) keeps it active without ligand, driving survival through STAT5, PI3K-AKT and RAS-ERK. Gilteritinib is a type I inhibitor that binds the active conformation of the kinase, so it blocks both ITD and TKD mutants. Blocking FLT3 autophosphorylation shuts down STAT5 signaling, causing apoptosis of leukemic blasts, and some blasts are pushed to mature. It also blocks AXL, which can sustain FLT3 signaling when other inhibitors are used.", "body": [["Leukemia", "FLT3-mutated blasts die or mature, which can bring remission in relapsed or refractory AML."], ["Whole body", "Maturing blasts can trigger differentiation syndrome, with fever, breathing trouble, fluid in the lungs and around the heart, weight gain and low blood pressure."], ["Liver", "Transaminase rises are common and are tracked with blood tests."], ["Heart", "QT prolongation can occur, so ECGs are done in the first cycles."], ["Brain", "Posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome (PRES), with headache, seizures and vision changes, is a rare warning."], ["Pancreas", "Pancreatitis is a listed warning, so belly pain with high lipase is evaluated."], ["Muscle and joints", "Muscle and joint pain are among the most common effects."]], "journey": "Gilteritinib is taken by mouth once daily and absorbed slowly, peaking in four to six hours. It is mainly bound to albumin. CYP3A4 in the liver is the main route of breakdown, so strong CYP3A inhibitors raise levels and combined strong inducers are avoided. Most leaves in the feces. Its half-life is very long, about 113 hours, so steady state takes about two weeks. Response often takes weeks to months.", "sidefx": "Differentiation syndrome happens when large numbers of blasts mature quickly and release cytokines, making capillaries leaky. It carries a boxed warning and is treated with corticosteroids and supportive care. QT prolongation, pancreatitis and PRES are listed. Liver enzyme rises and muscle pain are common. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Xospata (gilteritinib) FDA label", "Perl AE et al. NEJM 2019 (ADMIRAL)"]}, "quizartinib": {"origin": "Quizartinib was discovered at Ambit Biosciences in San Diego as AC220. Ambit used a large kinase binding screen to find a molecule that blocked FLT3 with great potency and few other targets. Daiichi Sankyo acquired Ambit in 2014. Japan approved it for relapsed FLT3-ITD AML in 2019, but FDA declined to approve it for that setting the same year. Daiichi Sankyo then ran the QuANTUM-First trial in newly diagnosed FLT3-ITD-positive AML, adding quizartinib to intensive chemotherapy and then continuing it alone as maintenance. That trial showed longer survival, and FDA approved it as Vanflyta in 2023. Because of serious QT prolongation, it is available only through a restricted REMS program.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Ambit Biosciences discovers AC220 in San Diego."], ["2014", "Daiichi Sankyo acquires Ambit Biosciences."], ["2019", "Japan approves quizartinib for relapsed or refractory FLT3-ITD AML."], ["2023", "FDA approves Vanflyta for newly diagnosed FLT3-ITD-positive AML."]], "cell": "FLT3 internal tandem duplications (ITD) duplicate part of the juxtamembrane region, which normally keeps the kinase turned off, so the receptor signals constantly through STAT5, PI3K-AKT and RAS-ERK. Quizartinib is a type II FLT3 inhibitor: it binds the inactive DFG-out conformation in a pocket next to the ATP site. This gives very high potency against FLT3-ITD. Tyrosine kinase domain mutations such as D835 shift the kinase toward the active shape, which the drug cannot bind well, so it has little activity against TKD mutants, a common escape route. Its active metabolite AC886 has similar activity. Separately, it blocks the slow delayed rectifier potassium current (IKs) in heart cells, prolonging the QT interval.", "body": [["Leukemia", "In newly diagnosed FLT3-ITD-positive AML, adding it to chemotherapy and maintenance extended survival."], ["Heart", "IKs blockade lengthens QT and can cause torsades de pointes and cardiac arrest, which is why ECGs and electrolytes are checked and access is restricted by REMS."], ["Blood", "Myelosuppression and prolonged neutropenia add to chemotherapy effects, raising infection risk."], ["Electrolytes", "Low potassium, magnesium and phosphate are common and add to QT risk."], ["Gut and mouth", "Nausea, diarrhea and mouth sores are common."]], "journey": "Quizartinib is taken by mouth once daily. It peaks in four to six hours and is almost fully protein bound. CYP3A4 converts it to the active metabolite AC886, so strong CYP3A inhibitors require a lower dose. Half-lives are long, about three days for the parent and about five days for AC886, so levels accumulate. Most leaves in the feces.", "sidefx": "QT prolongation is the main safety concern, from IKs potassium channel blockade, and carries a boxed warning. Low potassium and magnesium, other QT drugs and strong CYP3A inhibitors make it worse, so electrolytes are corrected and ECGs are monitored. Myelosuppression and infections come mostly from the combined chemotherapy. There is no antidote; torsades is treated with standard measures.", "fun": "Quizartinib is one of the most potent FLT3-ITD inhibitors known, yet it barely touches FLT3 TKD mutants because they hold the kinase in a shape the drug cannot grip.", "src": ["Vanflyta (quizartinib) FDA label and REMS", "Erba HP et al. Lancet 2023 (QuANTUM-First)", "Zarrinkar PP et al. Blood 2009"]}, "ivosidenib": {"origin": "In 2008 a team at Johns Hopkins sequencing glioblastoma genes found mutations in IDH1, a metabolic enzyme of the citric acid cycle, and in 2009 a Washington University team sequencing a whole AML genome found IDH1 mutations there too. That same year, scientists at Agios Pharmaceuticals in Cambridge, Massachusetts, with academic collaborators, showed that mutant IDH1 gains a new activity: it makes 2-hydroxyglutarate (2-HG), a molecule that builds up and blocks cell maturation, now called an oncometabolite. Agios designed ivosidenib (AG-120) to block mutant IDH1. FDA approved it as Tibsovo in 2018 for relapsed or refractory IDH1-mutated AML. Later approvals covered newly diagnosed AML, cholangiocarcinoma and myelodysplastic syndromes. Servier acquired Agios's oncology business in 2021.", "timeline": [["2008", "IDH1 mutations are reported in glioblastoma."], ["2009", "IDH1 mutations are found in AML, and mutant IDH1 is shown to make 2-hydroxyglutarate."], ["2018", "FDA approves Tibsovo for relapsed or refractory IDH1-mutated AML."], ["2021", "FDA approves ivosidenib for previously treated IDH1-mutated cholangiocarcinoma; Servier acquires the drug."], ["2023", "FDA approves ivosidenib for relapsed or refractory IDH1-mutated MDS."]], "cell": "Normal IDH1 in the cytoplasm converts isocitrate to alpha-ketoglutarate. Cancer-linked mutations at arginine 132 (R132) give the enzyme a new, neomorphic function: it reduces alpha-ketoglutarate to D-2-hydroxyglutarate (2-HG). 2-HG closely resembles alpha-ketoglutarate and competitively blocks enzymes that need it, including TET2, which removes DNA methylation, and Jumonji histone demethylases. The result is hypermethylated DNA and histones that freeze myeloid cells in an immature state. Ivosidenib selectively inhibits mutant IDH1. 2-HG levels fall within days, the epigenetic blocks lift, and leukemic blasts mature into functioning cells. The drug is not directly cytotoxic.", "body": [["Leukemia", "Blasts mature into neutrophils and other cells, so counts improve and some patients reach remission without classic chemotherapy-type marrow wipeout."], ["Whole body", "Rapid maturation can cause differentiation syndrome, with fever, breathing trouble, lung infiltrates, fluid around the lungs, weight gain, low blood pressure and kidney injury."], ["Blood", "White cell counts can rise (leukocytosis) as blasts mature."], ["Heart", "QTc prolongation can occur, so ECGs and electrolytes are checked."], ["Nerves", "Guillain-Barre syndrome is a rare listed warning."], ["Bile ducts", "In cholangiocarcinoma, the drug slows tumor growth; ascites can occur in this group."]], "journey": "Ivosidenib is taken by mouth once daily. High-fat meals are avoided because they raise exposure. It peaks in about three hours and is highly protein bound. CYP3A4 in the liver breaks it down, and ivosidenib also induces CYP3A4, including its own metabolism. Most leaves unchanged in the feces. Its half-life at steady state is about four days.", "sidefx": "Differentiation syndrome comes from large numbers of blasts maturing at once and releasing cytokines, causing capillary leak. It carries a boxed warning for AML and MDS and is treated with corticosteroids. QTc prolongation, worsened by CYP3A4 inhibitors, is listed. Guillain-Barre syndrome is rare. Fatigue, joint pain and diarrhea are common. There is no antidote.", "fun": "2-hydroxyglutarate was one of the first oncometabolites described: a normal-looking small molecule that drives cancer simply by building up.", "src": ["Tibsovo (ivosidenib) FDA label", "Parsons DW et al. Science 2008", "Mardis ER et al. NEJM 2009", "Dang L et al. Nature 2009", "DiNardo CD et al. NEJM 2018"]}, "enasidenib": {"origin": "After IDH1 mutations were found in gliomas and AML, researchers found that the mitochondrial enzyme IDH2 is also mutated in some AMLs, and that mutant IDH2, like IDH1, makes the oncometabolite 2-hydroxyglutarate. Agios Pharmaceuticals in Cambridge, Massachusetts, working with Celgene, designed enasidenib (AG-221) to block mutant IDH2. In 2017 FDA approved it as Idhifa for relapsed or refractory IDH2-mutated AML. It was the first IDH inhibitor approved and showed that switching off an oncometabolite could make leukemia cells mature. The drug later became part of Bristol Myers Squibb after that company acquired Celgene.", "timeline": [["2009", "Mutant IDH enzymes are shown to make 2-hydroxyglutarate."], ["2010", "IDH2 mutations are reported in AML."], ["2017", "FDA approves Idhifa, the first IDH inhibitor, for relapsed or refractory IDH2-mutated AML."]], "cell": "Normal IDH2 sits in the mitochondria and converts isocitrate to alpha-ketoglutarate. Mutations at arginine 140 (R140Q) or arginine 172 (R172K, R172S) give it a new function: reducing alpha-ketoglutarate to D-2-hydroxyglutarate (2-HG). 2-HG blocks alpha-ketoglutarate-dependent enzymes such as TET2 and Jumonji histone demethylases, leaving DNA and histones hypermethylated and myeloid cells stuck as blasts. Enasidenib is an allosteric inhibitor that binds the mutant IDH2 dimer and blocks 2-HG production. As 2-HG falls, methylation patterns normalize and blasts mature into neutrophils. Separately, enasidenib inhibits UGT1A1 and OATP1B1, which handle bilirubin, so indirect bilirubin rises.", "body": [["Leukemia", "Blasts mature into neutrophils, which can restore blood counts and bring remission, often over one to several months."], ["Whole body", "Rapid maturation can cause differentiation syndrome, with fever, breathing trouble, lung infiltrates, fluid buildup, weight gain, swollen lymph nodes, bone pain and kidney injury."], ["Blood", "Noninfectious leukocytosis, a rise in white cells from maturing blasts, is common."], ["Liver", "Indirect bilirubin rises because enasidenib blocks UGT1A1, much like Gilbert syndrome, usually without true liver injury."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea and taste changes are common."], ["Kidneys", "Tumor lysis syndrome can occur as cells turn over."]], "journey": "Enasidenib is taken by mouth once daily and peaks in about four hours. It is highly protein bound. Multiple CYP enzymes and UGTs break it down, forming an active metabolite, AGI-16903. Most leaves in the feces. Its half-life is very long, about five to six days, so levels build over weeks.", "sidefx": "Differentiation syndrome comes from many blasts maturing at once and releasing cytokines, causing capillary leak. It carries a boxed warning and is treated with corticosteroids. Leukocytosis is part of the same process. Raised indirect bilirubin is an off-target effect on UGT1A1 and OATP1B1 rather than liver damage. Tumor lysis syndrome is listed. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Enasidenib often raises bilirubin in the same way as Gilbert syndrome, by blocking the enzyme that conjugates bilirubin.", "src": ["Idhifa (enasidenib) FDA label", "Stein EM et al. Blood 2017", "Ward PS et al. Cancer Cell 2010"]}, "olutasidenib": {"origin": "Olutasidenib was discovered by Forma Therapeutics in Massachusetts as FT-2102. It was designed as a selective allosteric inhibitor of mutant IDH1, binding a site different from the one used by ivosidenib. In 2022 Rigel Pharmaceuticals licensed the drug, and later that year Forma was acquired by Novo Nordisk. FDA approved olutasidenib as Rezlidhia in 2022 for relapsed or refractory AML with a susceptible IDH1 mutation. In its pivotal trial, many responses were durable, lasting more than two years in some patients.", "timeline": [["2010s", "Forma Therapeutics discovers FT-2102."], ["2022", "Rigel Pharmaceuticals licenses olutasidenib."], ["2022", "FDA approves Rezlidhia for relapsed or refractory IDH1-mutated AML."]], "cell": "Cancer-linked IDH1 mutations at arginine 132 give the enzyme a new ability to reduce alpha-ketoglutarate to D-2-hydroxyglutarate (2-HG). 2-HG blocks alpha-ketoglutarate-dependent enzymes such as TET2 and histone demethylases, so DNA and histones become hypermethylated and myeloid cells cannot mature. Olutasidenib binds an allosteric pocket at the dimer interface of mutant IDH1, away from the active site, and locks the enzyme in an inactive shape. 2-HG levels fall, methylation patterns normalize, and leukemic blasts mature into neutrophils and other cells. It is not directly cytotoxic.", "body": [["Leukemia", "Blasts mature, blood counts recover and some patients achieve lasting remission."], ["Whole body", "Differentiation syndrome can occur, with fever, breathing trouble, lung infiltrates, fluid buildup, weight gain, low blood pressure and kidney injury."], ["Liver", "Transaminase and bilirubin rises, including drug-induced liver injury, are listed, so liver tests are checked often early on."], ["Blood", "Leukocytosis from maturing blasts is common."], ["Gut and joints", "Nausea, constipation, diarrhea and joint pain are common."]], "journey": "Olutasidenib is taken by mouth twice daily on an empty stomach because food raises its levels. It peaks after several hours and is highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A4, so strong inducers are avoided. Most leaves in the feces. Its half-life is about 67 hours, so levels accumulate.", "sidefx": "Differentiation syndrome comes from rapid blast maturation and cytokine release, causing capillary leak, and carries a boxed warning; corticosteroids are used. Hepatotoxicity is more prominent than with ivosidenib and is the reason for frequent liver tests. Leukocytosis is part of the maturation process. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Rezlidhia (olutasidenib) FDA label", "de Botton S et al. Blood Adv 2023"]}, "vorasidenib": {"origin": "IDH1 and IDH2 mutations were found in 2008 and 2009 to be present in most grade 2 and grade 3 gliomas, slow-growing brain tumors that mainly strike young adults. Earlier IDH inhibitors did not reach the brain well. Agios Pharmaceuticals designed vorasidenib (AG-881) to block both mutant IDH1 and mutant IDH2 and to cross the blood-brain barrier. Servier acquired the drug with Agios's oncology business in 2021. In the INDIGO trial, people with grade 2 IDH-mutant astrocytoma or oligodendroglioma who had had surgery but did not yet need radiation or chemotherapy took vorasidenib or placebo. It more than doubled the time before tumors grew. In 2024 FDA approved it as Voranigo, the first targeted drug for these gliomas.", "timeline": [["2008", "IDH1 mutations are reported in gliomas."], ["2021", "Servier acquires vorasidenib with Agios's oncology business."], ["2023", "The INDIGO trial reports longer progression-free survival than placebo."], ["2024", "FDA approves Voranigo for grade 2 IDH-mutant astrocytoma or oligodendroglioma."]], "cell": "Mutant IDH1 (at arginine 132, in the cytoplasm) and mutant IDH2 (at arginine 140 or 172, in the mitochondria) gain a new activity: reducing alpha-ketoglutarate to D-2-hydroxyglutarate (2-HG). 2-HG accumulates to very high levels in glioma cells and blocks alpha-ketoglutarate-dependent enzymes, such as TET DNA demethylases and Jumonji histone demethylases. This creates a hypermethylated state (called G-CIMP in gliomas) that locks cells in an immature, proliferative program. Vorasidenib inhibits both mutant enzymes and readily enters the brain. Intratumoral 2-HG falls, the epigenetic block eases, and tumor growth slows.", "body": [["Brain tumor", "Grade 2 IDH-mutant gliomas grow much more slowly, which can delay the need for radiation and chemotherapy and their effects on memory and thinking."], ["Liver", "Transaminase rises are the main serious side effect and can be severe, so liver tests are checked every two weeks at first and then monthly."], ["Nervous system", "Headache, fatigue and seizures occur, though seizures are also part of the disease itself."], ["Gut", "Nausea and diarrhea are common and usually mild."], ["Hormones", "It may make hormonal contraceptives less effective, so the label points to non-hormonal contraception."]], "journey": "Vorasidenib is taken by mouth once daily and peaks in about two hours. It is highly protein bound and crosses the blood-brain barrier well, reaching high levels in tumor tissue. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP1A2, with smaller roles for CYP2C19, CYP2D6 and CYP3A4, so strong CYP1A2 inhibitors such as fluvoxamine and inducers such as smoking change its levels. Most leaves in the feces. Its half-life is very long, about ten days, so steady state takes weeks.", "sidefx": "Hepatotoxicity is the main warning and is thought to be a direct drug effect on liver cells. Fatigue, headache and GI upset are common. Because the drug acts by slowly reversing an epigenetic block rather than killing cells, marrow suppression and hair loss are not typical. Embryo-fetal toxicity is listed. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Vorasidenib is unusual among cancer drugs because it is used to delay radiation and chemotherapy rather than to follow them.", "src": ["Voranigo (vorasidenib) FDA label", "Mellinghoff IK et al. NEJM 2023 (INDIGO)", "Yan H et al. NEJM 2009"]}, "revumenib": {"origin": "Menin is a protein made by the MEN1 gene, best known for causing multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 when lost. In leukemia, research in the 2000s showed that menin acts the opposite way: it binds the KMT2A protein (also called MLL) and its fusion partners and is needed to turn on leukemia-driving genes. Academic labs and companies then set out to design small molecules that block the menin-KMT2A contact. Revumenib was developed from a program at Vitae Pharmaceuticals and licensed to Syndax Pharmaceuticals in 2017. In 2024 FDA approved it as Revuforj for relapsed or refractory acute leukemia with a KMT2A translocation in adults and children one year and older, the first menin inhibitor approved. In 2025 approval expanded to relapsed or refractory AML with an NPM1 mutation.", "timeline": [["2005", "Researchers show menin is an essential partner of MLL fusion proteins in leukemia."], ["2017", "Syndax licenses the menin inhibitor program from Vitae Pharmaceuticals."], ["2024", "FDA approves Revuforj, the first menin inhibitor, for KMT2A-translocated acute leukemia."], ["2025", "FDA approves revumenib for relapsed or refractory NPM1-mutated AML."]], "cell": "KMT2A (MLL) normally helps regulate HOX genes during blood cell development. In KMT2A-rearranged leukemia, the N-terminal part of KMT2A is fused to one of many partner proteins. The fusion still binds menin, and menin tethers the complex to chromatin at HOXA9 and MEIS1, keeping these genes switched on and the cells stuck as self-renewing blasts. In NPM1-mutated AML, the same menin-KMT2A complex keeps HOXA and MEIS1 on. Revumenib fits into the pocket on menin where KMT2A binds, blocking the protein-protein interaction. The complex falls off chromatin, HOXA9 and MEIS1 expression drops, and blasts differentiate into mature myeloid cells.", "body": [["Leukemia", "Blasts mature and blast counts fall, and some patients reach remission, which can allow a stem cell transplant."], ["Whole body", "Differentiation syndrome can occur, with fever, breathing trouble, lung infiltrates, fluid buildup, weight gain, low blood pressure and kidney injury."], ["Heart", "QTc prolongation is common and can lead to torsades de pointes, so ECGs are done often."], ["Blood", "Febrile neutropenia, infections and bleeding are frequent in this heavily treated group."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are common."], ["Liver", "Transaminase rises can occur and are followed with blood tests."]], "journey": "Revumenib is taken by mouth twice daily, fasted or with a low-fat meal, and dosing in smaller children is based on body size. It is absorbed within a few hours. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as azole antifungals, which are common in leukemia care, require a lower dose. Its half-life is short, a few hours. It is mainly cleared by liver metabolism with elimination in the feces.", "sidefx": "Differentiation syndrome comes from many blasts maturing at once and releasing cytokines, causing capillary leak. It carries a boxed warning and is treated with corticosteroids and supportive care. QTc prolongation is also in the boxed warning; low potassium and magnesium and other QT-prolonging drugs add to the risk. Infections and cytopenias reflect the disease and prior therapy. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Menin acts as a tumor suppressor in endocrine glands, where its loss causes MEN1 tumors, yet it acts as a cancer helper in KMT2A-rearranged leukemia.", "src": ["Revuforj (revumenib) FDA label", "Issa GC et al. Nature 2023", "Yokoyama A et al. Cell 2005"]}, "ziftomenib": {"origin": "Ziftomenib is a menin inhibitor developed by Kura Oncology in San Diego as KO-539. It builds on academic research, including work at the University of Michigan, that showed small molecules could block the binding of menin to KMT2A (MLL). Kura focused on AML with NPM1 mutations, one of the most common genetic types of AML, in which the menin-KMT2A complex keeps leukemia-driving genes switched on. In the KOMET-001 trial, ziftomenib produced remissions in relapsed or refractory NPM1-mutated AML. In 2025 FDA approved it as Komzifti for adults with relapsed or refractory AML with a susceptible NPM1 mutation, given once daily.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Researchers show menin is required for KMT2A-driven leukemia gene expression."], ["2010s", "Kura Oncology develops the menin inhibitor KO-539."], ["2025", "FDA approves Komzifti for relapsed or refractory NPM1-mutated AML."]], "cell": "In NPM1-mutated AML, the mutant NPM1 protein moves into the cytoplasm, and leukemic cells keep the HOXA gene cluster and MEIS1 switched on, genes that normally turn off as blood cells mature. This program depends on a complex in which menin tethers wild-type KMT2A to chromatin at these genes. Ziftomenib binds the KMT2A-binding pocket of menin and blocks this protein-protein interaction. The complex leaves chromatin, HOXA and MEIS1 expression falls, the differentiation block is released and leukemic blasts mature into myeloid cells. The drug acts by differentiation rather than by direct cell killing or a specific cell-cycle phase.", "body": [["Leukemia", "Blasts mature and blast counts fall, which can lead to remission in relapsed or refractory NPM1-mutated AML."], ["Whole body", "Differentiation syndrome can occur, with fever, breathing trouble, lung infiltrates, fluid around the lungs or heart, weight gain, low blood pressure and kidney injury."], ["Blood", "White cell counts can rise as blasts mature, so treatment is not started until the count is below a set limit, often with hydroxyurea to lower it."], ["Heart", "QTc prolongation can occur, so ECGs and electrolytes are monitored."], ["Immune system", "Infections and febrile neutropenia are common in this group."], ["Gut and liver", "Nausea, diarrhea and liver test rises are frequent."]], "journey": "Ziftomenib is taken by mouth once daily as capsules on an empty stomach. It is absorbed over several hours after a dose. The liver breaks it down largely by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors and inducers affect levels. Its half-life is long enough to support once-daily dosing. Most leaves in the feces. Clinical responses develop over weeks as blasts mature.", "sidefx": "Differentiation syndrome comes from rapid maturation of many blasts and release of cytokines, which makes capillaries leaky. It carries a boxed warning and is treated with corticosteroids and close monitoring. Leukocytosis is part of the same process. QTc prolongation is listed. Infections and bleeding reflect the underlying leukemia. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Komzifti (ziftomenib) FDA label", "KOMET-001 trial reports", "Kura Oncology press materials"]}, "erdafitinib": {"origin": "Erdafitinib was discovered through a collaboration between Janssen, part of Johnson & Johnson, and Astex Therapeutics in Cambridge, UK, which used fragment-based drug design. It blocks all four fibroblast growth factor receptors. About one in five advanced bladder (urothelial) cancers carry activating FGFR3 mutations or fusions. In 2019 FDA granted accelerated approval to erdafitinib as Balversa, the first FGFR inhibitor approved for any cancer and the first targeted therapy for metastatic bladder cancer. In 2024, based on the THOR trial, it gained full approval for adults with FGFR3-altered urothelial cancer after prior systemic therapy, with a companion genetic test.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Janssen and Astex collaborate on FGFR inhibitor design."], ["2019", "FDA grants accelerated approval of Balversa, the first FGFR inhibitor."], ["2024", "FDA grants full approval for FGFR3-altered urothelial carcinoma after prior therapy."]], "cell": "FGFRs are receptor tyrosine kinases. Normally, binding of FGF with heparan sulfate dimerizes the receptor, which autophosphorylates and activates the MAPK-ERK and PI3K-AKT pathways and PLC-gamma. In some bladder cancers, point mutations in FGFR3 (such as S249C) or fusions such as FGFR3-TACC3 keep the receptor active all the time. Erdafitinib is an ATP-competitive inhibitor of FGFR1, 2, 3 and 4. It blocks receptor phosphorylation and downstream signaling, slowing tumor cell growth and causing cell death. In the kidney, the hormone FGF23 acts through FGFR1 with its co-receptor Klotho to reduce phosphate reabsorption, so blocking FGFR raises serum phosphate, which is used to guide dose increases.", "body": [["Bladder tumor", "FGFR3-driven urothelial cancers shrink in many patients."], ["Kidneys and minerals", "Blocking FGF23 signaling makes the kidneys keep phosphate, so serum phosphate rises. Very high levels can cause calcium-phosphate deposits in soft tissue."], ["Eyes", "Fluid can collect under the retina (central serous retinopathy and retinal pigment epithelial detachment), and dry eye is common, so regular eye exams with OCT are done."], ["Nails and skin", "Nails can lift from the nail bed or get infected, and hand-foot skin reaction and hair loss can occur."], ["Mouth", "Mouth sores, dry mouth and taste changes are frequent."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and appetite loss are common during treatment."]], "journey": "Erdafitinib is taken by mouth once daily and peaks in about two and a half hours. It is very highly bound, mainly to alpha-1 acid glycoprotein. The liver breaks it down by CYP2C9 and CYP3A4, so people who are CYP2C9*3/*3 poor metabolizers have higher levels. Most leaves in the feces with some in urine. Its half-life is about 59 hours. Serum phosphate checked two to three weeks after starting guides whether the dose is increased.", "sidefx": "Hyperphosphatemia is an on-target effect of blocking FGF23 signaling in the kidney, managed with diet and phosphate binders. Eye toxicity is thought to come from FGFR roles in the retinal pigment epithelium. Nail, skin and mouth effects reflect FGFR roles in renewing epithelial tissues. There is no antidote; holds and dose changes are used.", "fun": "Rising phosphate on erdafitinib is used as a sign that the drug is hitting its target, so it helps decide whether to raise the dose.", "src": ["Balversa (erdafitinib) FDA label", "Loriot Y et al. NEJM 2019", "Loriot Y et al. NEJM 2023 (THOR)"]}, "pemigatinib": {"origin": "Pemigatinib was discovered by Incyte in Wilmington, Delaware, as INCB054828, a selective inhibitor of FGFR1, 2 and 3. Cholangiocarcinoma, cancer of the bile ducts, has few treatment options, but about one in seven intrahepatic cholangiocarcinomas carry an FGFR2 fusion that drives the tumor. In the FIGHT-202 trial, pemigatinib shrank many of these tumors. In 2020 FDA granted accelerated approval to pemigatinib as Pemazyre, the first targeted treatment for cholangiocarcinoma. In 2022 it was approved for relapsed or refractory myeloid/lymphoid neoplasms with FGFR1 rearrangement, rare blood cancers, becoming the first targeted therapy for that disease.", "timeline": [["2010s", "Incyte discovers INCB054828."], ["2020", "FDA approves Pemazyre for previously treated FGFR2 fusion-positive cholangiocarcinoma."], ["2022", "FDA approves pemigatinib for myeloid/lymphoid neoplasms with FGFR1 rearrangement."]], "cell": "In cholangiocarcinoma, an FGFR2 gene fusion joins the FGFR2 kinase to a partner protein that makes it dimerize on its own. The fused receptor autophosphorylates without ligand and drives MAPK and PI3K-AKT signals for tumor cell growth. In FGFR1-rearranged myeloid/lymphoid neoplasms, a similar fusion activates FGFR1 in blood stem cells. Pemigatinib is a selective, reversible ATP-competitive inhibitor of FGFR1, 2 and 3, with weaker FGFR4 activity. It blocks receptor phosphorylation and downstream signaling, slowing growth and causing cell death. Blocking FGF23 signaling through FGFR1 in the kidney increases phosphate reabsorption and raises serum phosphate.", "body": [["Bile duct tumor", "FGFR2 fusion-positive cholangiocarcinomas shrink or stop growing in many patients."], ["Blood cancers", "In FGFR1-rearranged neoplasms, abnormal blood cell production can come under control."], ["Kidneys and minerals", "Serum phosphate rises because FGF23 signaling is blocked; it is managed with diet and phosphate-lowering drugs. Later in treatment phosphate can also fall."], ["Eyes", "Retinal pigment epithelial detachment and dry eye can occur, so eye exams with OCT are done."], ["Nails, skin and hair", "Nail problems, hair loss and hand-foot skin reaction are common."], ["Mouth and gut", "Mouth sores, dry mouth, taste change and diarrhea are frequent."]], "journey": "Pemigatinib is taken by mouth once daily, two weeks on and one week off for cholangiocarcinoma or continuously for FGFR1 neoplasms. It peaks in one to two hours and is highly protein bound. CYP3A4 in the liver is the main route of breakdown, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors and grapefruit raise levels and inducers are avoided. Most leaves in the feces. Its half-life is about 15 hours.", "sidefx": "Hyperphosphatemia is an on-target effect of blocking FGF23 signaling in the kidney. Retinal detachment and dry eye reflect FGFR roles in the eye. Nail, hair, skin and mouth changes come from FGFR roles in renewing epithelia. Most effects are manageable with dose holds. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Pemazyre (pemigatinib) FDA label", "Abou-Alfa GK et al. Lancet Oncol 2020 (FIGHT-202)"]}, "futibatinib": {"origin": "Futibatinib was discovered by Taiho Pharmaceutical in Japan as TAS-120. Unlike earlier FGFR drugs, which bind reversibly, it was designed to form a permanent covalent bond with the FGFR kinase. The idea was that a covalent drug could keep working when tumors develop mutations in the kinase that push reversible inhibitors out. In the FOENIX-CCA2 trial, futibatinib shrank tumors in many people with previously treated intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma carrying FGFR2 fusions or rearrangements. FDA granted accelerated approval to futibatinib as Lytgobi in 2022.", "timeline": [["2010s", "Taiho Pharmaceutical discovers TAS-120, a covalent FGFR inhibitor."], ["2022", "FDA approves Lytgobi for previously treated FGFR2-rearranged intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma."]], "cell": "In intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, FGFR2 fusions create receptors that dimerize and signal constantly through MAPK and PI3K-AKT. Futibatinib binds the ATP pocket of FGFR1, 2, 3 and 4 and then forms a covalent bond with a cysteine in the P-loop of the kinase, permanently disabling the receptor. Signaling stays off until the cell makes new receptor. Tumors treated with reversible FGFR inhibitors often develop kinase domain mutations, such as gatekeeper and molecular brake mutations, that weaken drug binding. Covalent binding keeps futibatinib active against some of these. Blocking FGF23 signaling in the kidney raises serum phosphate, as with other FGFR inhibitors.", "body": [["Bile duct tumor", "FGFR2-rearranged intrahepatic cholangiocarcinomas shrink or stop growing in many patients, including some that had escaped reversible FGFR inhibitors."], ["Kidneys and minerals", "Phosphate rises because FGF23 signaling is blocked, so phosphate is checked and lowered with diet and binders when needed."], ["Eyes", "Retinal pigment epithelial detachment and dry eye can occur, so eye exams with OCT are done regularly."], ["Nails and skin", "Nail toxicity, dry skin, hair loss and hand-foot skin reaction are common."], ["Mouth and gut", "Dry mouth, mouth sores, constipation, diarrhea and belly pain are frequent."], ["Liver", "Liver test rises can occur and are followed with blood tests."]], "journey": "Futibatinib is taken by mouth once daily, with or without food, and peaks in one to two hours. It is highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down by CYP3A and by glutathione conjugation, so strong CYP3A inhibitors and inducers are avoided. Most leaves in the feces. The plasma half-life is short, about three hours, but because it binds FGFR permanently, the effect lasts longer than blood levels suggest.", "sidefx": "Hyperphosphatemia is an on-target FGF23 effect. Retinal pigment epithelial detachment reflects FGFR roles in the retina and is usually reversible. Nail, skin, hair and mouth changes come from FGFR roles in renewing epithelia. There is no antidote; dose holds and phosphate control are used.", "fun": "Futibatinib's permanent bond means a pill with a three-hour half-life can keep its target blocked all day.", "src": ["Lytgobi (futibatinib) FDA label", "Goyal L et al. NEJM 2023 (FOENIX-CCA2)"]}, "avapritinib": {"origin": "In 1998 Seiichi Hirota and colleagues in Japan showed that gastrointestinal stromal tumors (GIST) are driven by activating KIT mutations, and in 2003 other GISTs were found to carry PDGFRA mutations. Imatinib transformed GIST care, but the PDGFRA D842V mutation in exon 18 made tumors completely resistant. In systemic mastocytosis, the KIT D816V mutation drives abnormal mast cells and also resists imatinib. Blueprint Medicines in Cambridge, Massachusetts, designed avapritinib (BLU-285) to bind these activation-loop mutants tightly. FDA approved it as Ayvakit in 2020 for PDGFRA exon 18-mutant GIST, the first drug for that group. Approvals followed for advanced systemic mastocytosis in 2021 and indolent systemic mastocytosis in 2023. Sanofi acquired Blueprint in 2025.", "timeline": [["1998", "Hirota and colleagues report KIT mutations in GIST."], ["2003", "PDGFRA mutations are found in GISTs without KIT mutations."], ["2020", "FDA approves Ayvakit for PDGFRA exon 18-mutant GIST."], ["2021", "FDA approves avapritinib for advanced systemic mastocytosis."], ["2023", "FDA approves avapritinib for indolent systemic mastocytosis."]], "cell": "KIT and PDGFRA are receptor tyrosine kinases. Activation-loop mutations, such as PDGFRA D842V in exon 18 and KIT D816V in exon 17, lock the kinase in its active conformation. Imatinib and similar type II drugs bind only the inactive shape, so they fail against these mutants. Avapritinib is a type I inhibitor that binds the active conformation, so it potently blocks these mutants. Blocking KIT and PDGFRA shuts down downstream PI3K-AKT, MAPK and STAT signals. In GIST, this slows tumor cell growth. In systemic mastocytosis, KIT D816V-driven mast cells lose their growth and survival signal, so mast cell numbers, serum tryptase and mediator-related symptoms fall.", "body": [["GIST", "PDGFRA exon 18-mutant tumors, which do not respond to imatinib, shrink in most patients."], ["Mast cells", "In systemic mastocytosis, mast cell infiltration of marrow, skin, liver and spleen falls, tryptase drops, and symptoms such as flushing, itching and diarrhea ease."], ["Brain", "Memory problems, confusion and other cognitive effects are common and dose-related. Intracranial bleeding can occur, especially with low platelets."], ["Fluid", "Swelling around the eyes and in the legs is very common."], ["Blood", "Anemia, low platelets and neutropenia can occur."], ["Hair and skin", "Hair color can change, and photosensitivity can occur."]], "journey": "Avapritinib is taken by mouth once daily on an empty stomach, because food raises its levels. It peaks in two to four hours and is highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down by CYP3A4 and CYP2C9, so strong CYP3A inhibitors and inducers are avoided. Most leaves in the feces. Its half-life is long, about one and a half to two and a half days. Doses differ widely between GIST, advanced mastocytosis and indolent mastocytosis.", "sidefx": "Cognitive effects may reflect KIT or PDGFR signaling roles in the brain and are dose-related. Intracranial bleeding is more likely when platelets are low, so platelet counts are checked in advanced mastocytosis. Edema likely reflects PDGFR roles in fluid balance. Hair color change comes from KIT blockade in melanocytes. There is no antidote; dose holds and reductions are used.", "fun": "Avapritinib was the first drug to work against the PDGFRA D842V mutation, which made GISTs resistant to every earlier kinase inhibitor.", "src": ["Ayvakit (avapritinib) FDA label", "Hirota S et al. Science 1998", "Heinrich MC et al. Lancet Oncol 2020 (NAVIGATOR)", "Gotlib J et al. Nat Med 2021"]}, "ripretinib": {"origin": "Ripretinib was designed at Deciphera Pharmaceuticals in Kansas and Massachusetts as DCC-2618. Deciphera's founding science centered on the switch pocket, a region of a kinase that controls whether it flips between its active and inactive shapes. GISTs treated with imatinib, sunitinib and regorafenib develop new KIT mutations that keep the kinase active in many different ways. Ripretinib was built to hold KIT and PDGFRA in the off position no matter which mutation drove them. In the INVICTUS trial in people who had already received at least three kinase inhibitors, it extended disease control and survival compared with placebo. FDA approved it as Qinlock in 2020 for advanced GIST after three or more prior kinase inhibitors. Ono Pharmaceutical acquired Deciphera in 2024.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Deciphera develops switch-control kinase inhibitor chemistry."], ["2020", "FDA approves Qinlock for advanced GIST after three or more kinase inhibitors."], ["2024", "Ono Pharmaceutical acquires Deciphera."]], "cell": "In GIST, a primary KIT mutation (often exon 11 or 9) starts the disease, and under treatment, secondary mutations appear in the ATP-binding region (exons 13 and 14) or the activation loop (exons 17 and 18). Different inhibitors miss different mutations. Ripretinib is a switch-control inhibitor with a dual mechanism: it binds the switch pocket and also interacts with the activation loop, locking KIT and PDGFRA in their inactive conformation. Because it controls the switch that all these mutants use, it blocks a broad range of primary and secondary mutations. With KIT and PDGFRA off, downstream PI3K-AKT and MAPK signals stop and tumor cell growth slows.", "body": [["GIST", "Heavily pretreated tumors stop growing for longer, and survival improved compared with placebo."], ["Hair and skin", "Hair loss is very common. Hand-foot skin reaction occurs, and new skin cancers, including squamous cell carcinoma and melanoma, can appear, so skin exams are done."], ["Blood vessels", "Blood pressure can rise, so it is controlled before starting."], ["Heart", "Cardiac dysfunction, including heart failure, is a listed warning, so ejection fraction is assessed."], ["Muscle and gut", "Muscle aches, fatigue, nausea, belly pain and constipation are common."], ["Healing", "Wounds heal slowly, so the drug is held before surgery."]], "journey": "Ripretinib is taken by mouth once daily, with or without food. It peaks in about four hours and is almost fully protein bound. CYP3A4 in the liver converts it into an active metabolite, DP-5439, which adds to the effect, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors and inducers matter. Most leaves in the feces. Half-lives are about 15 hours for the parent and 18 hours for the metabolite.", "sidefx": "Hair loss and hand-foot skin reaction likely reflect KIT and other kinase roles in hair follicles and skin. New skin cancers may arise from pathway effects in skin cells, similar to those seen with other kinase inhibitors. High blood pressure and poor wound healing suggest some VEGFR-related effects. Heart dysfunction is listed. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Qinlock (ripretinib) FDA label", "Blay JY et al. Lancet Oncol 2020 (INVICTUS)", "Smith BD et al. Cancer Cell 2019"]}, "olaparib": {"origin": "Poly(ADP-ribose) polymerase activity was first described in 1963 by Paul Mandel's group in Strasbourg, France. Decades later, in 2005, two groups, one led by Alan Ashworth in London and one by Thomas Helleday, published in Nature that cells missing BRCA1 or BRCA2 are extremely sensitive to PARP inhibitors. This is synthetic lethality: losing either repair system alone is survivable, but losing both kills the cell. The Cambridge, UK, company KuDOS Pharmaceuticals, founded by Stephen Jackson, had made the PARP inhibitor that became olaparib, and AstraZeneca bought KuDOS in 2005. In 2014 FDA approved olaparib as Lynparza, the first PARP inhibitor, for BRCA-mutated ovarian cancer. Approvals followed for breast, pancreatic and prostate cancers.", "timeline": [["1963", "Paul Mandel's group in Strasbourg describes poly(ADP-ribose) synthesis."], ["2005", "Two groups report synthetic lethality of PARP inhibitors in BRCA-deficient cells; AstraZeneca acquires KuDOS Pharmaceuticals."], ["2014", "FDA approves Lynparza, the first PARP inhibitor, for BRCA-mutated ovarian cancer."], ["2018", "FDA approves olaparib for germline BRCA-mutated HER2-negative metastatic breast cancer."], ["2019", "FDA approves olaparib for germline BRCA-mutated metastatic pancreatic cancer."], ["2020", "FDA approves olaparib for HRR gene-mutated metastatic prostate cancer."]], "cell": "PARP1 and PARP2 detect single-strand DNA breaks, bind them and build chains of poly(ADP-ribose) that recruit repair proteins. Then PARP releases itself. Olaparib blocks the NAD+ binding site of PARP1, 2 and 3, which stops this repair and also traps PARP on the DNA. When a replication fork runs into an unrepaired break or a trapped PARP, the fork collapses into a double-strand break. Normal cells fix these by homologous recombination, which needs BRCA1, BRCA2, PALB2 and related proteins. Tumor cells lacking homologous recombination cannot repair them accurately, so they rely on error-prone repair, pile up chromosome damage and die. This synthetic lethality explains why the drug works best in BRCA-mutated or HRD-positive cancers.", "body": [["Tumors", "Ovarian, breast, pancreatic and prostate cancers with BRCA or other repair defects shrink or stay controlled longer, especially as maintenance after platinum response."], ["Bone marrow", "Blood-forming cells divide quickly and rely on PARP for repair, so anemia is common and can need transfusion; neutropenia and thrombocytopenia also occur."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting, taste change and appetite loss are very common, usually early."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue is one of the most frequent complaints."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis is a rare but serious listed warning."], ["Blood vessels", "Venous clots, including pulmonary embolism, are listed, especially in prostate cancer with hormone therapy."]], "journey": "Olaparib is taken by mouth twice daily as tablets, which are not interchangeable with the older capsules. It peaks in about an hour and a half and is moderately protein bound. CYP3A4/5 in the liver is the main route of breakdown, so strong or moderate CYP3A inhibitors require lower doses and grapefruit is avoided. It leaves in both urine and feces. Its half-life is about 15 hours.", "sidefx": "Marrow suppression, especially anemia, reflects the dependence of rapidly dividing blood precursors on DNA repair. Long-term DNA damage in marrow stem cells is linked to the rare but serious risk of myelodysplastic syndrome and AML, so prolonged low counts are investigated. Nausea and fatigue are common. Pneumonitis and clots are listed. Creatinine can rise from blocked tubular transporters without true kidney injury. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Synthetic lethality was first described in fruit fly genetics decades before olaparib turned it into a cancer treatment.", "src": ["Lynparza (olaparib) FDA label", "Farmer H et al. Nature 2005", "Bryant HE et al. Nature 2005", "Chambon P, Weill JD, Mandel P. Biochem Biophys Res Commun 1963"]}, "niraparib": {"origin": "Niraparib was discovered at Merck as MK-4827, an oral PARP1 and PARP2 inhibitor. In 2012 Merck licensed it to Tesaro, a young oncology company in Massachusetts. In the NOVA trial, niraparib extended disease control as maintenance in recurrent ovarian cancer after response to platinum chemotherapy, both in women with and without BRCA mutations. In 2017 FDA approved it as Zejula, the first PARP inhibitor approved for maintenance without requiring a BRCA mutation. GSK acquired Tesaro in 2019. It was later approved as first-line maintenance in advanced ovarian cancer, and in 2023 a combination tablet of niraparib with abiraterone (Akeega) was approved for BRCA-mutated metastatic prostate cancer.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Merck discovers MK-4827."], ["2012", "Tesaro licenses niraparib from Merck."], ["2017", "FDA approves Zejula for maintenance in recurrent ovarian cancer regardless of BRCA status."], ["2019", "GSK acquires Tesaro."], ["2020", "FDA approves niraparib for first-line maintenance in advanced ovarian cancer."]], "cell": "PARP1 and PARP2 sense single-strand DNA breaks and build poly(ADP-ribose) chains that call in repair proteins. Niraparib binds the NAD+ site of PARP1 and PARP2, blocking this activity and trapping PARP on DNA. At replication forks, these trapped complexes and unrepaired breaks turn into double-strand breaks. Cells with working homologous recombination repair can fix them, but cells lacking BRCA1, BRCA2 or other HR genes (HRD-positive) cannot, so they die by synthetic lethality. Some HR-proficient tumors also respond, though less. Separately, niraparib inhibits dopamine, norepinephrine and serotonin transporters, which raises catecholamine signaling and explains its effects on blood pressure and heart rate.", "body": [["Ovarian tumor", "As maintenance after platinum response, it delays the return of ovarian, fallopian tube and primary peritoneal cancers, most in BRCA-mutated or HRD-positive tumors."], ["Platelets", "Thrombocytopenia is the most common serious effect, usually in the first months, so starting dose is set by weight and platelet count."], ["Red and white cells", "Anemia and neutropenia are frequent, so counts are checked weekly at first."], ["Heart and vessels", "Blocking norepinephrine and dopamine reuptake raises blood pressure and heart rate and can cause palpitations; hypertensive crisis is rare."], ["Brain", "Insomnia and headache are common, likely from the same transporter effects. PRES is a rare warning."], ["Gut", "Nausea, constipation and vomiting are common; bedtime dosing may lessen nausea."]], "journey": "Niraparib is taken by mouth once daily, with or without food, and peaks in about three hours. It is well absorbed and spreads widely, including into the brain. Unlike many cancer drugs, it is not broken down mainly by CYP enzymes: carboxylesterases convert it to an inactive metabolite, which is then glucuronidated. So CYP drug interactions are few. It leaves in both urine and feces. Its half-life is about 36 hours.", "sidefx": "Low platelets, red cells and neutrophils come from PARP blockade in rapidly dividing marrow precursors that need DNA repair. Thrombocytopenia is more frequent than with olaparib. Rare myelodysplastic syndrome and AML are linked to long-term marrow DNA damage. High blood pressure, fast heart rate and insomnia come from monoamine transporter blockade, so blood pressure and heart rate are checked often in the first year. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Niraparib's off-target blockade of monoamine transporters is similar to how some antidepressants work, which explains its unusual blood pressure and sleep effects.", "src": ["Zejula (niraparib) FDA label", "Mirza MR et al. NEJM 2016 (NOVA)", "Gonzalez-Martin A et al. NEJM 2019 (PRIMA)"]}, "rucaparib": {"origin": "Rucaparib grew out of a long British research effort on the PARP enzymes, which help cells patch single-strand breaks in DNA. Chemists at Newcastle University worked with the US company Agouron (later part of Pfizer) to design potent PARP-blocking molecules, and the compound that became rucaparib was among the first PARP inhibitors given to cancer patients in the early 2000s. In 2005 two groups showed in Nature that cells lacking BRCA1 or BRCA2 are extremely sensitive to PARP blockade, an idea called synthetic lethality. Clovis Oncology took rucaparib through late trials, and the FDA granted approval as Rubraca in 2016 for BRCA-mutated ovarian cancer. Later approvals added ovarian cancer maintenance after a platinum response and BRCA-mutated metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Newcastle University and Agouron/Pfizer scientists bring an early PARP inhibitor into cancer trials."], ["2005", "Nature papers show BRCA-deficient cells are killed by PARP inhibition (synthetic lethality)."], ["2016", "FDA approves rucaparib (Rubraca) for BRCA-mutated ovarian cancer."], ["2018", "Approval expands to ovarian cancer maintenance after response to platinum chemotherapy."], ["2020", "FDA approves rucaparib for BRCA-mutated metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer."]], "cell": "PARP-1, PARP-2 and PARP-3 sense single-strand DNA breaks and build chains of poly(ADP-ribose) that call in repair proteins. Rucaparib sits in the NAD+ binding pocket of these enzymes and blocks that catalytic step. It also traps PARP on the DNA, so the enzyme becomes a physical roadblock. When a replication fork reaches an unrepaired nick or a trapped PARP complex during S phase, the fork collapses and a double-strand break forms. Normal cells fix these breaks by homologous recombination, which needs BRCA1 and BRCA2. Tumor cells with BRCA mutations cannot use this pathway, so breaks pile up, chromosomes become unstable and the cells undergo apoptosis. Healthy cells with one working BRCA copy survive, which gives the drug its tumor selectivity.", "body": [["Tumor cells", "BRCA-mutated ovarian and prostate cancer cells cannot repair the double-strand breaks that form when PARP is blocked, so they die while most normal tissue survives."], ["Bone marrow", "Rapidly dividing blood precursors depend on PARP repair during constant replication, so anemia, low platelets and low neutrophils occur, and a small number of patients develop MDS or AML."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting, constipation, diarrhea and taste change are common and are among the most frequent reasons for dose changes."], ["Liver", "Reversible rises in ALT and AST are common early in therapy."], ["Kidneys", "Serum creatinine can rise because rucaparib blocks transporters that secrete creatinine, which is usually not true kidney injury."], ["Skin", "Photosensitivity causes sunburn-like reactions, so sun protection is part of routine care."]], "journey": "Rucaparib is taken by mouth as a tablet twice a day and is moderately absorbed, reaching a peak in about two hours. It is moderately bound to plasma proteins and spreads widely into tissues. The liver breaks it down mainly with CYP2D6, with smaller roles for CYP1A2 and CYP3A4. Rucaparib itself inhibits several CYP enzymes, so levels of drugs such as warfarin can rise. The metabolites leave in feces and urine. The half-life of roughly a day supports steady levels with twice-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "The marrow suppression comes from the same DNA repair blockade that kills tumor cells, since blood precursors divide constantly. Prolonged DNA damage in marrow stem cells is thought to explain the rare but serious MDS and AML seen with all PARP inhibitors. Gastrointestinal upset reflects effects on the fast-turning gut lining and the brain's vomiting centers. The creatinine rise reflects blocked tubular transporters rather than true loss of filtration in most patients. Photosensitivity is a drug-specific skin effect. Rucaparib can harm a developing fetus. There is no antidote; care is supportive with dose holds and transfusions.", "fun": "Synthetic lethality was first described in fruit fly genetics: two gene defects that are harmless alone become deadly together. PARP inhibitors made this old genetics idea into a cancer treatment.", "src": ["Rubraca (rucaparib) FDA label", "Farmer H et al. Nature 2005", "Bryant HE et al. Nature 2005", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "talazoparib": {"origin": "Talazoparib was discovered in the late 2000s at a small California company, LEAD Therapeutics, and was known in early studies as BMN 673 after BioMarin acquired it. Lab work showed it was by far the strongest PARP trapper among the PARP inhibitors, locking the enzyme onto DNA far more tightly than olaparib. Medivation bought the rights, and Pfizer then acquired Medivation in 2016. The EMBRACA trial in germline BRCA-mutated, HER2-negative advanced breast cancer led to FDA approval as Talzenna in 2018. In 2023 the FDA approved it with enzalutamide for metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer with homologous recombination repair gene mutations, based on the TALAPRO-2 trial.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Talazoparib is discovered at LEAD Therapeutics and developed as BMN 673 by BioMarin."], ["2016", "Pfizer acquires Medivation and with it talazoparib."], ["2018", "FDA approves talazoparib (Talzenna) for germline BRCA-mutated HER2-negative breast cancer."], ["2023", "FDA approves talazoparib plus enzalutamide for HRR gene-mutated metastatic prostate cancer."]], "cell": "Talazoparib binds the NAD+ site of PARP-1 and PARP-2 and stops them from building poly(ADP-ribose) chains at single-strand DNA breaks. Its main strength is trapping: it freezes PARP onto the damaged DNA, creating a bulky protein-DNA complex. During S phase, replication forks run into these trapped complexes and collapse into double-strand breaks. Cells with working BRCA1, BRCA2 and other homologous recombination genes can repair these breaks accurately. Tumor cells with germline BRCA mutations or other HRR gene defects cannot, so damage accumulates and apoptosis follows. Because trapping is more toxic than simple catalytic blockade, talazoparib is active at very low drug concentrations. In prostate cancer, androgen receptor blockade with enzalutamide also lowers expression of some repair genes, which helps explain why the two drugs work together.", "body": [["Tumor cells", "Breast and prostate cancer cells with HRR defects cannot fix the breaks caused by trapped PARP, so tumors shrink or stop growing."], ["Bone marrow", "Anemia is the leading problem and often needs transfusion; neutropenia and low platelets are also common because blood precursors replicate constantly."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and reduced appetite occur but are usually milder than marrow effects."], ["Hair and skin", "Hair thinning (alopecia) is more noticeable than with some other PARP inhibitors."], ["Nervous system", "Fatigue and headache are common complaints."]], "journey": "Talazoparib is swallowed as a capsule once daily and is absorbed quickly, peaking in one to two hours. It spreads into tissues and is moderately protein bound. Unlike most PARP inhibitors it is barely metabolized by the liver. Most of the drug leaves the body unchanged through the kidneys, so reduced kidney function raises exposure. It is a substrate of P-glycoprotein and BCRP, so strong P-gp inhibitors can raise levels. The half-life is long, about 90 hours, so steady state takes a few weeks to reach.", "sidefx": "Because talazoparib traps PARP so strongly, it is especially toxic to dividing marrow cells, which explains the high rate of anemia and the need for blood count checks. Neutropenia raises infection risk and thrombocytopenia raises bleeding risk. Persistent DNA damage in marrow stem cells explains the rare MDS and AML warning shared by the class. Kidney clearance means kidney impairment increases these effects. It can harm a developing fetus because embryonic cells divide rapidly. No antidote exists; care is dose interruption, dose reduction and supportive transfusion.", "fun": "In lab studies talazoparib trapped PARP on DNA roughly 100 times more strongly than olaparib, which is why it is the most potent drug of its class.", "src": ["Talzenna (talazoparib) FDA label", "Litton JK et al. NEJM 2018 (EMBRACA)", "Murai J et al. Mol Cancer Ther 2014 (PARP trapping)"]}, "glasdegib": {"origin": "The Hedgehog pathway is named after a fruit fly mutation found in the famous 1980 Drosophila screen by Christiane Nusslein-Volhard and Eric Wieschaus; affected larvae were covered in spiky bristles like a hedgehog. Later researchers learned that this pathway controls embryo patterning and can be switched back on in cancers. In acute myeloid leukemia, Hedgehog signaling appears to help leukemic stem cells survive and resist chemotherapy. Pfizer scientists designed glasdegib (PF-04449913) as an oral Smoothened blocker. In the BRIGHT AML 1003 trial, adding glasdegib to low-dose cytarabine improved survival in older or less fit adults with newly diagnosed AML, and the FDA approved it as Daurismo in 2018.", "timeline": [["1980", "Nusslein-Volhard and Wieschaus describe the hedgehog mutant in fruit flies."], ["1995", "Nusslein-Volhard and Wieschaus share the Nobel Prize for embryo development genetics."], ["2010s", "Pfizer develops glasdegib as an oral Smoothened inhibitor."], ["2018", "FDA approves glasdegib (Daurismo) with low-dose cytarabine for newly diagnosed AML in older or unfit adults."]], "cell": "In the resting Hedgehog pathway, the receptor Patched (PTCH1) holds back a seven-pass membrane protein called Smoothened (SMO). When Hedgehog ligand binds Patched, SMO is released, moves into the primary cilium and stops the processing of GLI proteins into repressors. Active GLI transcription factors then turn on genes for growth and stem cell self-renewal. Glasdegib binds SMO directly and locks it in an inactive state, so GLI stays off even if Hedgehog ligand is present. In AML, this is thought to reduce the survival and dormancy of leukemic stem cells, making them more vulnerable to cytarabine, an S-phase antimetabolite that blocks DNA synthesis. Together the drugs lower blast counts and prolong survival compared with cytarabine alone.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Leukemic blasts decrease, but anemia, low platelets and febrile neutropenia are common from both the leukemia and the combined treatment."], ["Heart", "Glasdegib can prolong the QTc interval, so ECGs and electrolytes are watched and torsades de pointes is a rare risk."], ["Muscles", "Hedgehog signaling helps maintain muscle, so blockade causes muscle cramps and musculoskeletal pain."], ["Taste and appetite", "Taste changes and reduced appetite reflect Hedgehog roles in taste bud renewal."], ["Embryo", "Hedgehog is essential to forming the face, brain and limbs, so the drug can cause severe birth defects or fetal death."]], "journey": "Glasdegib is taken by mouth once daily and is well absorbed, peaking in under two hours. It is highly bound to plasma proteins. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels and strong inducers lower them. Metabolites and some unchanged drug leave in urine and feces. The half-life of about 17 hours supports once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Most side effects follow from shutting down Hedgehog signaling in tissues that still need it in adults: muscle cramps, taste loss and hair thinning. QT prolongation is a direct effect on cardiac repolarization, which is why ECG checks are standard. Low blood counts, infection and bleeding reflect AML itself plus cytarabine marrow suppression. The boxed warning for embryo-fetal toxicity comes from the central role of Hedgehog in embryo development. There is no antidote.", "fun": "A related Hedgehog gene in mammals was named Sonic hedgehog, after the video game character.", "src": ["Daurismo (glasdegib) FDA label", "Cortes JE et al. Leukemia 2019 (BRIGHT AML 1003)", "Nusslein-Volhard C, Wieschaus E. Nature 1980"]}, "sonidegib": {"origin": "Sonidegib comes from Novartis, where it was developed under the code LDE225 as an oral Smoothened inhibitor. Its roots go back to the discovery that people with basal cell nevus syndrome (Gorlin syndrome), who develop many basal cell carcinomas, carry mutations in PTCH1, the gene that normally keeps the Hedgehog pathway switched off. That showed nearly all basal cell carcinomas are driven by runaway Hedgehog signaling. Sonidegib followed vismodegib as the second Hedgehog inhibitor approved for skin cancer. After the BOLT trial, the FDA approved it as Odomzo in 2015 for locally advanced basal cell carcinoma that has come back after surgery or radiation, or that cannot be treated with them. The product later moved to Sun Pharma.", "timeline": [["1996", "PTCH1 mutations are linked to basal cell nevus syndrome and basal cell carcinoma."], ["2000s", "Novartis develops sonidegib as LDE225."], ["2015", "FDA approves sonidegib (Odomzo) for locally advanced basal cell carcinoma."]], "cell": "Basal cell carcinoma cells usually have a broken PTCH1 gene or an activating SMO mutation. Without functioning Patched, the membrane protein Smoothened is never held in check, so it moves into the primary cilium and continuously activates GLI transcription factors. GLI1 and GLI2 then drive genes for proliferation and survival. Sonidegib binds the drug-binding pocket in the transmembrane domain of SMO and holds it inactive. GLI activity falls, tumor cell division slows and tumors shrink. Some tumors resist through SMO mutations that change the binding pocket or through changes downstream of SMO, which Smoothened blockers cannot reach.", "body": [["Skin tumors", "Locally advanced basal cell carcinomas shrink as Hedgehog-driven growth is shut off, over weeks to months."], ["Muscles", "Muscle spasms and pain are very common; creatine kinase can rise, and rhabdomyolysis can occur rarely."], ["Hair follicles", "Hedgehog drives the hair growth cycle, so hair loss is common."], ["Taste", "Taste buds need Hedgehog signaling to renew, so taste changes or loss develop and can reduce eating and weight."], ["Reproductive system", "Amenorrhea can occur, and the drug can cause severe birth defects or fetal death."]], "journey": "Sonidegib is swallowed once daily on an empty stomach, because a high-fat meal raises absorption greatly. Only a small fraction of the dose is absorbed. It is extremely protein bound and spreads widely into tissues, including skin. The liver breaks it down mainly with CYP3A. Its half-life is very long, about 28 days, so steady state takes about four months and the drug lingers for months after stopping. Elimination is mostly in feces.", "sidefx": "The classic triad of muscle spasms, hair loss and taste loss comes from blocking normal Hedgehog signaling in muscle, hair follicles and taste buds. Muscle injury can raise creatine kinase and rarely progress to rhabdomyolysis, so CK is checked. Weight loss follows taste loss and poor appetite. The boxed warning for embryo-fetal toxicity reflects Hedgehog's role in fetal development, and the long half-life means contraception is continued for months after stopping. Blood donation is avoided during and after treatment for the same reason. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Because of its roughly four-week half-life, sonidegib can still be measured in the body for months after the last dose.", "src": ["Odomzo (sonidegib) FDA label", "Migden MR et al. Lancet Oncol 2015 (BOLT)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "vismodegib": {"origin": "The story of vismodegib begins with sheep. In the 1950s, ranchers in Idaho saw lambs born with a single central eye. Scientists traced the defect to pregnant ewes eating corn lily (Veratrum californicum), and the toxic alkaloid was named cyclopamine. Decades later, researchers showed that cyclopamine works by blocking Smoothened in the Hedgehog pathway, the same pathway that is overactive in basal cell carcinoma. Curis and Genentech then screened for better drug-like Smoothened blockers and found the compound GDC-0449. As vismodegib (Erivedge), it became the first Hedgehog pathway inhibitor approved by the FDA, in 2012, for metastatic or locally advanced basal cell carcinoma not suited to surgery or radiation.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Cyclopic lambs in Idaho are linked to ewes eating corn lily, source of cyclopamine."], ["1998", "Cyclopamine is shown to block Hedgehog signaling."], ["2000s", "Curis and Genentech develop GDC-0449 (vismodegib)."], ["2012", "FDA approves vismodegib (Erivedge), the first Hedgehog pathway inhibitor."]], "cell": "In most basal cell carcinomas, PTCH1 is lost, so Smoothened (SMO) is never restrained. SMO accumulates in the primary cilium and keeps GLI1 and GLI2 in their active form, turning on growth genes. Vismodegib binds SMO in the same region where cyclopamine binds and holds it inactive. GLI proteins are then processed back into repressors, Hedgehog target gene expression falls and tumor cell proliferation stops. Tumors with SMO mutations that alter the binding pocket, or with changes downstream such as SUFU loss or GLI amplification, can escape.", "body": [["Skin tumors", "Advanced basal cell carcinomas shrink or heal as the Hedgehog drive is removed."], ["Muscles", "Muscle spasms are the most common side effect, since Hedgehog helps maintain muscle function."], ["Hair follicles", "Hair loss is common because the hair cycle depends on Hedgehog signaling."], ["Taste and weight", "Taste loss or change is common, which reduces appetite and leads to weight loss."], ["Growing bones", "In children, Hedgehog controls growth plates, so premature fusion of growth plates has been reported."], ["Embryo", "Hedgehog builds the face and brain, so vismodegib can cause severe birth defects, just as cyclopamine did in lambs."]], "journey": "Vismodegib is taken by mouth once daily, with or without food. It is absorbed slowly and binds very tightly to albumin and alpha-1-acid glycoprotein, which keeps free drug low but steady. It undergoes little metabolism, and most of the drug leaves in feces, partly unchanged. The half-life after one dose is about 12 days, and about 4 days with continuous dosing, so steady state takes one to three weeks.", "sidefx": "Muscle spasms, hair loss and taste loss all come from blocking normal Hedgehog signaling in adult tissues that renew. Weight loss follows the taste change. Amenorrhea may reflect effects on ovarian function. The boxed warning for embryo-fetal toxicity reflects the same mechanism that caused cyclopic lambs: Hedgehog is essential for forming midline structures. Contraception continues after the last dose, and blood donation is avoided. Rare severe skin reactions have been reported. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Vismodegib is a modern descendant of a sheep poison: the corn lily alkaloid cyclopamine that caused one-eyed lambs pointed researchers to Smoothened as a drug target.", "src": ["Erivedge (vismodegib) FDA label", "Sekulic A et al. NEJM 2012", "Cooper MK et al. Science 1998"]}, "pexidartinib": {"origin": "Tenosynovial giant cell tumor is a rare, usually non-spreading tumor of the joint lining that can destroy joints. In the 2000s, researchers found that many of these tumors carry a translocation that causes a small group of cells to overproduce CSF1. That signal recruits large numbers of CSF1R-bearing macrophages, which form most of the tumor. Plexxikon, a California company known for structure-guided kinase drug design, created pexidartinib (PLX3397) to block CSF1R. Daiichi Sankyo developed it further. After the ENLIVEN trial, the FDA approved it as Turalio in 2019, the first systemic therapy for symptomatic TGCT not well suited to surgery. Because of serious liver injury, it is available only through a restricted REMS program.", "timeline": [["2006", "Researchers report CSF1 translocations in tenosynovial giant cell tumor."], ["2010s", "Plexxikon and Daiichi Sankyo develop pexidartinib (PLX3397)."], ["2019", "FDA approves pexidartinib (Turalio), the first drug for TGCT, with a REMS for liver toxicity."]], "cell": "In TGCT, a few neoplastic cells overexpress colony stimulating factor 1 (CSF1). CSF1 binds CSF1R, a receptor tyrosine kinase on macrophages and monocytes, triggering receptor dimerization, autophosphorylation and survival and proliferation signals through PI3K-AKT and ERK. Pexidartinib binds the CSF1R kinase in its inactive, autoinhibited form and blocks ATP-driven phosphorylation. Without CSF1R signaling, macrophages are no longer recruited or kept alive, and the tumor mass, which is mostly these reactive cells, shrinks. Pexidartinib also inhibits KIT and FLT3-ITD, and KIT blockade in melanocytes explains hair lightening.", "body": [["Joints", "Tumor volume falls and range of motion, stiffness and pain improve in many patients."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes rise in many patients; rare cases of cholestatic injury and vanishing bile duct syndrome have needed transplant or caused death."], ["Hair and skin", "Hair loses color because KIT signaling is needed by melanocytes; rash and itching also occur."], ["Eyes and face", "Periorbital swelling is common, likely linked to changes in tissue macrophages."], ["Blood tests", "LDH, cholesterol and some enzymes rise partly because tissue macrophages that normally clear them are reduced."]], "journey": "Pexidartinib is taken by mouth, and food, especially a high-fat meal, greatly increases how much is absorbed, so the label sets how it is taken with meals. It peaks in about two and a half hours and is more than 99 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver metabolizes it through CYP3A4 oxidation and UGT1A4 glucuronidation. Most drug leaves in feces, with some in urine. The half-life of about 27 hours supports twice-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "The boxed warning for hepatotoxicity reflects a mixed or cholestatic injury pattern, possibly involving liver macrophages (Kupffer cells) that depend on CSF1R, though the exact cause is not fully known. Liver tests are checked often and the drug is stopped for serious injury. Hair depigmentation comes from KIT blockade in melanocytes. Edema and enzyme changes reflect reduced macrophage function in tissues. There is no antidote; management is stopping the drug and supportive care.", "fun": "Most of a tenosynovial giant cell tumor is not cancer cells at all but recruited macrophages, so pexidartinib shrinks the tumor by cutting off the signal that brings them in.", "src": ["Turalio (pexidartinib) FDA label", "Tap WD et al. Lancet 2019 (ENLIVEN)", "West RB et al. PNAS 2006"]}, "vimseltinib": {"origin": "Vimseltinib was designed by Deciphera Pharmaceuticals, a company that built its drugs around the switch-control region of kinases, the part of the enzyme that flips it between active and inactive shapes. The goal was a CSF1R blocker that spared related kinases such as KIT, FLT3 and PDGFR, hoping to avoid off-target effects like the hair lightening and serious liver injury seen with earlier drugs. In the MOTION phase 3 trial, vimseltinib shrank tenosynovial giant cell tumors and improved movement and pain. The FDA approved it as Romvimza in 2025 for symptomatic TGCT when surgery would worsen function or cause severe harm. Deciphera became part of Ono Pharmaceutical in 2024.", "timeline": [["2010s", "Deciphera designs vimseltinib as a highly selective switch-control CSF1R inhibitor."], ["2024", "Ono Pharmaceutical acquires Deciphera."], ["2025", "FDA approves vimseltinib (Romvimza) for symptomatic tenosynovial giant cell tumor."]], "cell": "TGCT cells overproduce CSF1, which binds CSF1R on macrophages and drives their recruitment, survival and growth. CSF1R is a receptor tyrosine kinase whose switch-control pocket determines whether the activation loop can take its active shape. Vimseltinib binds this switch pocket and locks CSF1R in an inactive conformation, so it cannot autophosphorylate or pass signals through PI3K-AKT and ERK. Macrophages that make up most of the tumor lose their survival signal, and the tumor shrinks. Its high selectivity means little action on KIT, FLT3 or PDGFR, which limits effects on hair color and blood counts. Tissue macrophages also clear certain enzymes from the blood, so fewer macrophages leads to higher measured AST, CPK and amylase.", "body": [["Joints", "Tumor size, stiffness and pain decrease and range of motion improves in many patients."], ["Liver tests", "AST and other enzymes rise mostly because tissue macrophages clear them more slowly, not usually from true liver injury."], ["Face and limbs", "Periorbital, facial and peripheral swelling are common, likely from altered tissue macrophage function."], ["Skin", "Rash and itching occur in some patients."], ["Lipids", "Total cholesterol can rise during treatment, a change also seen with other CSF1R inhibitors."]], "journey": "Vimseltinib is swallowed as a capsule and is absorbed over several hours. It has a long half-life of about six days, which is why it can be given only twice a week rather than daily. The liver metabolizes it, and its products leave in feces and urine. It is highly protein bound. Because of its long half-life, effects build gradually and fade slowly after stopping.", "sidefx": "Enzyme rises in the blood (AST, CPK, amylase, lipase) are largely a lab effect: tissue macrophages that normally remove these enzymes depend on CSF1R, so their loss lets enzymes linger. Edema likely has a similar macrophage-related cause. Serious liver injury was not seen in trials, but liver injury is a concern for the CSF1R class, so liver tests are monitored. The drug can harm a developing fetus. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Romvimza (vimseltinib) FDA label", "Gelderblom H et al. Lancet 2024 (MOTION)"]}, "belzutifan": {"origin": "Belzutifan comes from research on how cells sense oxygen, work that earned William Kaelin, Peter Ratcliffe and Gregg Semenza the 2019 Nobel Prize. They showed that the VHL protein tags hypoxia-inducible factors (HIFs) for destruction when oxygen is normal. In von Hippel-Lindau (VHL) disease and most clear cell kidney cancers, VHL is lost, so HIF-2 alpha builds up and drives tumor growth. Transcription factors were long thought undruggable, but scientists at UT Southwestern, including Richard Bruick and Kevin Gardner, found a small internal cavity in HIF-2 alpha where a drug could bind. Peloton Therapeutics developed belzutifan from that discovery, and Merck acquired Peloton in 2019. The FDA approved belzutifan (Welireg) in 2021 for VHL disease-associated tumors, with later approvals for advanced kidney cancer and for pheochromocytoma and paraganglioma.", "timeline": [["2009", "UT Southwestern researchers describe a druggable pocket inside HIF-2 alpha."], ["2019", "Kaelin, Ratcliffe and Semenza win the Nobel Prize for oxygen sensing; Merck acquires Peloton."], ["2021", "FDA approves belzutifan (Welireg) for VHL disease-associated tumors."], ["2023", "FDA approves belzutifan for advanced clear cell renal cell carcinoma after prior therapies."]], "cell": "With normal oxygen, the VHL protein marks HIF-alpha subunits for ubiquitination and proteasome breakdown. When VHL is mutated, HIF-2 alpha escapes destruction, enters the nucleus and pairs with HIF-1 beta (ARNT). The pair binds hypoxia response elements in DNA and turns on genes such as VEGF (new blood vessels), cyclin D1 (cell division), GLUT1 (glucose uptake) and erythropoietin. Belzutifan binds the cavity in the PAS-B domain of HIF-2 alpha and changes its shape so it cannot pair with ARNT. Target genes switch off, tumor blood supply and growth fall, and tumors shrink. Erythropoietin, also a HIF-2 target in the kidney, falls too, which explains anemia.", "body": [["Kidney and other tumors", "VHL-related kidney cancers, brain and spinal cord hemangioblastomas and pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors shrink as HIF-2 driven growth stops."], ["Blood", "Anemia is very common because the kidney makes less erythropoietin when HIF-2 is blocked."], ["Lungs and oxygen", "Hypoxia can occur, possibly because HIF-2 helps regulate breathing and lung vessel responses to low oxygen."], ["Nervous system", "Fatigue, headache and dizziness are common."], ["Embryo", "Animal studies show fetal harm, and the drug can make hormonal contraception less effective."]], "journey": "Belzutifan is taken by mouth once daily and peaks in about one to two hours. It is moderately protein bound. The liver clears it mainly through UGT2B17 and CYP2C19. People who are poor metabolizers through both of these enzymes have much higher exposure and more anemia and hypoxia. Belzutifan also induces CYP3A4, which can lower levels of some other drugs, including hormonal contraceptives. Metabolites leave in feces and urine, and the half-life is about 14 hours.", "sidefx": "Anemia comes straight from the mechanism: erythropoietin is one of the genes HIF-2 turns on, so blocking HIF-2 lowers red cell production. Hypoxia is linked to HIF-2's role in oxygen sensing in the lungs and carotid body. Both are key label warnings, and both are worse in poor metabolizers. Embryo-fetal toxicity is a boxed warning. There is no antidote; anemia may be managed with dose changes or transfusion.", "fun": "HIF-2 alpha is a transcription factor, a protein type long called undruggable, and belzutifan was one of the first drugs to block a transcription factor by binding inside it.", "src": ["Welireg (belzutifan) FDA label", "Jonasch E et al. NEJM 2021", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 2019 press release"]}, "nirogacestat": {"origin": "Gamma-secretase inhibitors were first created in the hope of treating Alzheimer's disease, because gamma-secretase helps cut amyloid precursor protein into amyloid beta. Those Alzheimer's efforts failed, but the enzyme also cleaves the Notch receptor, a growth signal active in some tumors. Nirogacestat was first made at Pfizer (as PF-03084014) and was later licensed to SpringWorks Therapeutics in 2017, which focused on desmoid tumors. Desmoid tumors are locally aggressive soft tissue growths that do not spread but can cause pain and loss of function. In the DeFi phase 3 trial, nirogacestat lowered the risk of tumor progression. The FDA approved it as Ogsiveo in 2023, the first approved drug for desmoid tumors.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Gamma-secretase is identified as a target in Alzheimer's disease research."], ["2000s", "Pfizer develops nirogacestat as PF-03084014."], ["2017", "SpringWorks Therapeutics licenses nirogacestat from Pfizer."], ["2023", "FDA approves nirogacestat (Ogsiveo), the first drug for desmoid tumors."]], "cell": "Gamma-secretase is a protease complex built around presenilin that cuts proteins inside the cell membrane. When a Notch receptor is activated by a ligand on a neighboring cell, gamma-secretase makes the final cut that frees the Notch intracellular domain (NICD). NICD travels to the nucleus, partners with CSL (RBPJ) and turns on genes that promote growth and block differentiation. Nirogacestat reversibly blocks gamma-secretase, so NICD is not released and Notch target genes stay quiet. In desmoid tumors, which are often driven by beta-catenin, Notch signaling supports tumor growth, so its loss slows or shrinks the tumor. Gamma-secretase also sheds B-cell maturation antigen (BCMA) from myeloma cells, which is why nirogacestat is studied alongside BCMA-directed therapies.", "body": [["Desmoid tumors", "Tumor growth slows or tumors shrink, with less pain and better function."], ["Gut", "Notch keeps intestinal stem cells dividing rather than turning into secretory goblet cells; blocking it causes diarrhea, which can be severe."], ["Ovaries", "Ovarian toxicity is very common, causing irregular or absent periods and menopause-like symptoms that often reverse after stopping."], ["Skin", "Rash and hair loss occur, and new basal or squamous cell skin cancers have been reported because Notch acts as a tumor suppressor in skin."], ["Liver and electrolytes", "Liver enzymes can rise, and low phosphate and low potassium are common."]], "journey": "Nirogacestat is taken by mouth twice daily and peaks in about one and a half hours. It is almost entirely bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors and inducers, and grapefruit, are avoided. Most drug leaves in feces with a small fraction in urine. The half-life of about 23 hours supports steady exposure with twice-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea and electrolyte loss reflect blocked Notch in the gut lining, where it controls cell fate. Ovarian toxicity likely reflects Notch roles in follicle development. Skin cancers relate to Notch's tumor-suppressing role in skin keratinocytes, so skin checks are part of care. Liver enzyme rises are monitored. The drug harms a developing fetus. No antidote exists; side effects are managed by holding or reducing the drug.", "fun": "Nirogacestat belongs to a drug class first built to fight Alzheimer's disease, and found its first approval in a rare soft tissue tumor instead.", "src": ["Ogsiveo (nirogacestat) FDA label", "Gounder M et al. NEJM 2023 (DeFi)"]}, "dordaviprone": {"origin": "Dordaviprone, long known as ONC201, came from a search for small molecules that could switch on the cancer-killing TRAIL pathway. The laboratory of Wafik El-Deiry, then at Penn State, described it in 2013 as a TRAIL-inducing compound, and the company Oncoceutics developed it. Later work showed its main targets are the mitochondrial protease ClpP and the dopamine D2 receptor. Doctors noticed unusual, lasting responses in young patients with diffuse midline gliomas carrying the H3 K27M histone mutation, brain tumors with very poor survival and few options. Chimerix acquired the drug in 2021. The FDA granted accelerated approval to dordaviprone (Modeyso) in 2025 for progressive H3 K27M-mutant diffuse midline glioma in adults and children one year and older. It was the first drug approved specifically for this tumor type.", "timeline": [["2013", "El-Deiry lab reports the compound later called ONC201 as a TRAIL-inducing small molecule."], ["2010s", "Oncoceutics runs early trials, with responses seen in H3 K27M-mutant glioma."], ["2021", "Chimerix acquires ONC201."], ["2025", "FDA grants accelerated approval to dordaviprone (Modeyso) for H3 K27M-mutant diffuse midline glioma."]], "cell": "Dordaviprone is an imipridone that works through two main targets. It binds and hyperactivates the mitochondrial caseinolytic protease P (ClpP), which then chews up mitochondrial proteins, including parts of the electron transport chain. Energy production fails and the cell experiences mitochondrial stress. This activates the integrated stress response, raising ATF4 and CHOP, which push cells toward apoptosis. It also blocks the dopamine D2 receptor, which some glioma cells use for growth signaling. In H3 K27M-mutant glioma cells, the mutant histone suppresses the normal repressive mark H3 K27 trimethylation, and dordaviprone treatment partly restores this mark, shifting cells away from a tumor-promoting state. Together these effects slow or shrink the tumor in some patients.", "body": [["Brain tumor", "Some diffuse midline gliomas shrink or stop growing, with response lasting months in a subset of patients."], ["Nervous system", "Fatigue and headache are common, partly from the tumor and partly from the drug."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are common and can reduce appetite and fluid intake."], ["Heart", "The QTc interval can lengthen, so ECG checks are done in at-risk patients."], ["Immune and blood", "Lymphocyte counts can fall, and serious allergic reactions such as anaphylaxis or angioedema can occur."]], "journey": "Dordaviprone is taken by mouth as capsules once a week. It is absorbed over hours and can cross the blood-brain barrier, which matters for tumors in the brainstem and thalamus. The liver metabolizes it with involvement of CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors and inducers change its levels. Many of its detailed kinetic values are still being described in the literature.", "sidefx": "Fatigue, nausea and liver enzyme rises reflect stress on mitochondria and metabolism. D2 receptor blockade is a possible contributor to nausea and other neurologic effects. QT prolongation is listed as a warning and may cause arrhythmia in people with other risk factors. Hypersensitivity reactions are listed as a warning. The drug can harm a developing fetus. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Diffuse midline glioma with the H3 K27M mutation is a tumor defined by a single change in a histone protein, the spool that DNA wraps around.", "src": ["Modeyso (dordaviprone) FDA label", "Allen JE et al. Sci Transl Med 2013", "Arrillaga-Romany I et al. J Clin Oncol 2024"]}, "ofatumumab": {"origin": "Ofatumumab was created by the Danish biotech company Genmab using transgenic mice whose antibody genes had been replaced with human ones, a platform developed by Medarex. Immunizing these mice with human CD20 produced fully human antibodies, so the final drug has no mouse protein parts. Genmab partnered with GlaxoSmithKline, and the FDA approved ofatumumab as Arzerra in 2009 for chronic lymphocytic leukemia that no longer responded to other drugs. Rights later passed to Novartis, which developed a much lower-dose monthly injection given under the skin for multiple sclerosis. That product, Kesimpta, was approved in 2020 for relapsing forms of MS and can be self-injected at home. Arzerra is no longer commercially marketed in the US.", "timeline": [["1997", "Rituximab, the first anti-CD20 antibody, is approved, proving CD20 as a target."], ["2009", "FDA approves ofatumumab (Arzerra) for refractory chronic lymphocytic leukemia."], ["2020", "FDA approves ofatumumab (Kesimpta) for relapsing forms of multiple sclerosis."]], "cell": "CD20 is a membrane protein found on B cells from the pre-B stage through mature and memory B cells, but not on stem cells or most plasma cells. Ofatumumab, a fully human IgG1 antibody, binds a membrane-close epitope that includes both the small and large extracellular loops of CD20. Because it binds so close to the membrane, it is very good at activating complement: C1q binds the antibody Fc regions, triggering the cascade and forming membrane attack complexes that punch holes in the B cell (complement-dependent cytotoxicity). The Fc region also engages Fc gamma receptors on natural killer cells and macrophages, causing antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity and phagocytosis. In MS, removing B cells reduces their roles as antigen-presenting cells and cytokine producers that drive inflammatory attacks on myelin.", "body": [["Immune system", "Circulating B cells drop to very low levels within weeks and stay low with monthly dosing, lowering defense against some infections."], ["Brain and spinal cord", "In MS, fewer B cells means fewer relapses and fewer new inflammatory lesions on MRI."], ["Antibody levels", "Over time IgM and sometimes IgG levels can fall, raising infection risk."], ["Liver", "Hidden hepatitis B can reactivate when B cells are removed, which can cause severe or fatal hepatitis."], ["Injection site and whole body", "The first injection often causes fever, chills, headache and muscle aches from cytokine release as B cells are destroyed, plus local redness."]], "journey": "For MS, ofatumumab is injected under the skin with a prefilled pen. As a large antibody it is absorbed slowly through the lymphatic system, peaking several days later. It stays mainly in the blood and tissue fluid. It is not cleared by the liver or kidneys. It is broken down like other proteins, and some is removed when it binds CD20 on B cells (target-mediated clearance). The half-life at steady state is about 16 days. B cells come back over weeks to months after stopping.", "sidefx": "Infections follow from B-cell depletion and lower antibody levels. Hepatitis B reactivation happens because B cells and antibodies help keep the virus in check, so HBV testing comes first. Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML), caused by JC virus, is a rare risk with anti-CD20 drugs. First-dose systemic reactions reflect cytokines released as B cells die. Live vaccines are avoided during treatment, and other vaccines work less well. There is no reversal agent; effects fade as B cells regenerate.", "src": ["Kesimpta (ofatumumab) FDA label", "Arzerra (ofatumumab) FDA label", "Hauser SL et al. NEJM 2020 (ASCLEPIOS)"]}, "toremifene": {"origin": "Toremifene was developed in Finland in the 1980s by the drug company Farmos, which later became part of Orion. Chemists added a chlorine atom to the side chain of tamoxifen, the first widely used selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM). The aim was a drug with similar breast cancer activity but a different safety profile, including less DNA damage in rat liver. Clinical trials found toremifene about as effective as tamoxifen in advanced breast cancer. The FDA approved it as Fareston in 1997 for metastatic breast cancer in postmenopausal women with estrogen receptor-positive or unknown tumors. It later received a boxed warning for QT prolongation.", "timeline": [["1977", "Tamoxifen is approved in the US, setting the stage for SERMs."], ["1980s", "Farmos in Finland develops toremifene, a chlorinated tamoxifen analog."], ["1997", "FDA approves toremifene (Fareston) for metastatic breast cancer in postmenopausal women."]], "cell": "Toremifene binds estrogen receptor alpha and beta in competition with estradiol. The shape the receptor takes when a SERM binds depends on the tissue. In breast cancer cells, the bulky side chain displaces helix 12 of the receptor so coactivators cannot bind, and estrogen-driven genes such as those for cyclin D1 are not turned on. Cells stall in G1 and stop dividing. In bone, liver and the uterine lining, the drug-bound receptor can still recruit some coactivators, giving partial estrogen-like effects. Separately, toremifene blocks hERG potassium channels in the heart, delaying repolarization and lengthening the QT interval.", "body": [["Breast tumor", "ER-positive tumor cells stop growing because estrogen signaling is blocked."], ["Heart", "hERG channel blockade lengthens the QTc interval, which can lead to torsades de pointes in at-risk patients."], ["Bone", "Partial estrogen action can help preserve bone, but tumor flare or bone metastases can release calcium and cause hypercalcemia early in treatment."], ["Uterus", "Estrogen-like action on the endometrium can cause thickening (hyperplasia) and rarely cancer."], ["Blood vessels and clotting", "Estrogen-like action on the liver raises clotting factors, increasing the risk of blood clots."], ["Temperature control", "Hot flashes and sweating reflect blocked estrogen signaling in the hypothalamus."]], "journey": "Toremifene is taken by mouth once daily and is well absorbed, peaking in about three hours. It is very tightly bound to albumin. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP3A4 into N-demethyltoremifene, which is also active. The drug and its metabolites go through enterohepatic recirculation and leave mainly in feces. The elimination half-life is about five days, so steady state takes several weeks.", "sidefx": "QT prolongation comes from direct hERG potassium channel blockade, and the boxed warning advises avoiding it in congenital long QT, low potassium or magnesium, and with other QT-prolonging drugs or strong CYP3A4 inhibitors. Hot flashes reflect estrogen blockade in the brain. Clots and endometrial changes reflect partial estrogen agonism in liver and uterus. Hypercalcemia and tumor flare can happen early in patients with bone metastases. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Fareston (toremifene) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "elacestrant": {"origin": "For years the only selective estrogen receptor degrader (SERD) was fulvestrant, approved in 2002, which must be given as large intramuscular injections. Researchers wanted an oral SERD that could also act on tumors with ESR1 mutations, changes in the estrogen receptor gene that often arise after aromatase inhibitor treatment and let the receptor stay switched on without estrogen. Elacestrant (once called RAD1901) was developed by Radius Health and later licensed to the Menarini Group and its US arm, Stemline Therapeutics. In the EMERALD trial, it improved progression-free survival over standard endocrine therapy in ESR1-mutated, ER-positive, HER2-negative advanced breast cancer. The FDA approved it as Orserdu in 2023, the first oral SERD.", "timeline": [["2002", "FDA approves fulvestrant, the first SERD, as an injection."], ["2010s", "Radius Health develops elacestrant (RAD1901) as an oral SERD."], ["2023", "FDA approves elacestrant (Orserdu), the first oral SERD, for ESR1-mutated advanced breast cancer."]], "cell": "Estrogen receptor alpha is a nuclear receptor that, when bound by estradiol, dimerizes, binds estrogen response elements in DNA and turns on growth genes. ESR1 mutations such as Y537S and D538G in the ligand-binding domain lock the receptor in an active shape even without estrogen, so aromatase inhibitors stop working. Elacestrant binds the ligand-binding domain of both normal and mutant receptors and acts as a pure antagonist. Its binding also changes the receptor's shape so it is tagged with ubiquitin and destroyed by the proteasome. With less receptor protein and no active signaling, ER-driven transcription and tumor cell proliferation fall.", "body": [["Breast tumor", "ER-positive cells, including those with ESR1 mutations, lose estrogen receptor signaling and grow more slowly."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting, indigestion and reduced appetite are common, often early in treatment."], ["Musculoskeletal system", "Muscle, joint and bone pain are among the most common complaints."], ["Lipids", "Cholesterol and triglycerides can rise, so lipid levels are checked."], ["Temperature control", "Hot flashes occur because estrogen signaling in the hypothalamus is blocked."]], "journey": "Elacestrant is taken by mouth once daily with food, which reduces nausea. It has modest oral bioavailability and peaks one to four hours after a dose. It is more than 99 percent bound to plasma proteins and spreads widely into tissues. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP3A4, so strong inhibitors and inducers of CYP3A4 are avoided. Most of the drug leaves in feces. The half-life of about 30 to 50 hours supports once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Hot flashes and musculoskeletal pain reflect loss of estrogen receptor signaling in the brain, joints and bone. Nausea and vomiting are common and are partly related to the drug itself. Raised lipids reflect effects on liver lipid metabolism. Liver enzyme rises can occur. The drug can harm a developing fetus. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Orserdu (elacestrant) FDA label", "Bidard FC et al. J Clin Oncol 2022 (EMERALD)"]}, "imlunestrant": {"origin": "Imlunestrant was designed by Eli Lilly as an oral selective estrogen receptor degrader (SERD) that would block the estrogen receptor around the clock and reach the brain, where breast cancer can spread. Like elacestrant, it was built to work against tumors with ESR1 mutations, which commonly arise after aromatase inhibitor treatment and keep the receptor active without estrogen. In the EMBER-3 phase 3 trial, imlunestrant improved progression-free survival compared with standard endocrine therapy in patients with ESR1-mutated, ER-positive, HER2-negative advanced breast cancer. The FDA approved it as Inluriyo in 2025, making it the second oral SERD.", "timeline": [["2023", "FDA approves elacestrant, the first oral SERD."], ["2024", "EMBER-3 trial results for imlunestrant are presented and published."], ["2025", "FDA approves imlunestrant (Inluriyo) for ESR1-mutated advanced breast cancer."]], "cell": "Estrogen receptor alpha drives breast cancer growth by binding estradiol, pairing up, binding DNA and switching on genes for division. ESR1 mutations in the ligand-binding domain hold the receptor in its active shape without estrogen. Imlunestrant binds the ligand-binding domain of both normal and mutant receptors as a pure antagonist, so coactivators cannot attach. It also destabilizes the receptor, which is then marked for proteasome breakdown, lowering total receptor protein. With less receptor and no active signaling, ER-dependent transcription stops and tumor cells stop proliferating. It was designed to give sustained receptor suppression and to cross the blood-brain barrier.", "body": [["Breast tumor", "ER-positive cancer cells, including ESR1-mutant ones, lose estrogen signaling and grow more slowly."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and nausea are the most common digestive effects."], ["Musculoskeletal system", "Muscle and joint pain occur from loss of estrogen signaling in these tissues."], ["Liver and lipids", "AST, ALT and triglycerides can rise, so labs are checked."], ["Blood", "Mild decreases in neutrophils and hemoglobin can occur."]], "journey": "Imlunestrant is taken by mouth once daily. It is absorbed over several hours and is highly protein bound. It distributes widely, including into the brain. The liver metabolizes it through CYP3A and conjugation pathways, and most of it leaves in feces. Its long half-life supports once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Fatigue and musculoskeletal pain reflect estrogen blockade in tissues. Gut upset is common but usually mild. Liver enzyme and lipid changes reflect effects on liver metabolism. Mild drops in blood counts can occur. The drug can harm a developing fetus. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Inluriyo (imlunestrant) FDA label", "Jhaveri KL et al. NEJM 2025 (EMBER-3)"]}, "letrozole": {"origin": "Letrozole was created at Ciba-Geigy in Switzerland in the 1980s by a team led by chemist Ajay Bhatnagar. Earlier aromatase blockers such as aminoglutethimide also shut down adrenal steroid production, which meant patients needed steroid replacement. The Ciba-Geigy team designed a triazole molecule that fits aromatase very tightly and leaves other steroid enzymes alone. The FDA approved letrozole as Femara in 1997 for advanced breast cancer in postmenopausal women. Large trials such as BIG 1-98 and MA.17 then made it a standard treatment after surgery for early hormone receptor-positive breast cancer. Outside its labeled cancer uses, it is also studied and used off-label to induce ovulation.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Ajay Bhatnagar and colleagues at Ciba-Geigy develop letrozole."], ["1997", "FDA approves letrozole (Femara) for advanced breast cancer."], ["2003", "MA.17 trial shows benefit of letrozole after five years of tamoxifen."], ["2005", "BIG 1-98 trial reports letrozole superior to tamoxifen as initial adjuvant therapy."]], "cell": "After menopause, most estrogen is made outside the ovaries by aromatase (CYP19A1), a cytochrome P450 enzyme in fat, muscle, skin and breast tissue. Aromatase turns androstenedione into estrone and testosterone into estradiol. Letrozole is a nonsteroidal triazole whose nitrogen binds the heme iron in aromatase's active site, competitively and reversibly blocking the enzyme. Blood estradiol drops sharply. ER-positive breast cancer cells, which depend on estrogen binding to their receptors, lose their growth signal and stop dividing. In premenopausal women, lower estrogen removes negative feedback on the pituitary, so FSH rises, which explains the off-label ovulation use and why it does not reliably suppress estrogen when the ovaries are active.", "body": [["Breast tumor", "ER-positive cancer cells starved of estrogen grow more slowly or shrink, lowering recurrence risk after surgery."], ["Bone", "Low estrogen speeds bone breakdown, causing bone density loss and more fractures, so bone density is monitored."], ["Joints and muscles", "Joint pain and stiffness are common, likely from estrogen loss in joint tissues."], ["Temperature control", "Hot flashes and night sweats come from low estrogen acting on the hypothalamus."], ["Lipids", "Cholesterol can rise modestly with long-term use."], ["Ovaries", "In premenopausal women, higher FSH can stimulate the ovaries and lead to ovulation."]], "journey": "Letrozole is taken by mouth once daily and is almost completely absorbed, with or without food. It is moderately bound to plasma proteins and spreads into tissues. The liver converts it, through CYP3A4 and CYP2A6, into an inactive carbinol metabolite, which is glucuronidated and leaves in urine. The half-life is about two days, so steady state takes a few weeks, though estradiol levels fall within two to three days.", "sidefx": "Nearly all side effects come from deep estrogen deprivation. Bone loss happens because estrogen normally restrains osteoclasts. Joint pain, hot flashes and vaginal dryness reflect estrogen loss in those tissues. Rises in cholesterol reflect estrogen's role in liver lipid handling. Fatigue and dizziness can occur. It is contraindicated in pregnancy. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Femara (letrozole) FDA label", "Goss PE et al. NEJM 2003 (MA.17)", "BIG 1-98 Collaborative Group. NEJM 2005"]}, "exemestane": {"origin": "Exemestane was developed at the Italian company Farmitalia Carlo Erba in the 1980s, which later became part of Pharmacia and then Pfizer. Unlike letrozole and anastrozole, it is a steroid, built to look like androstenedione, aromatase's natural substrate. That design lets it enter the enzyme as a false substrate and then bind permanently, so it is called an aromatase inactivator or suicide inhibitor. The FDA approved exemestane as Aromasin in 1999 for advanced breast cancer after tamoxifen. The Intergroup Exemestane Study later showed that switching from tamoxifen to exemestane after two to three years improved outcomes in early breast cancer.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Farmitalia Carlo Erba develops exemestane as a steroidal aromatase inactivator."], ["1999", "FDA approves exemestane (Aromasin) for advanced breast cancer after tamoxifen."], ["2004", "Intergroup Exemestane Study shows benefit of switching from tamoxifen to exemestane."]], "cell": "Aromatase (CYP19A1) converts androgens into estrogens in fat, muscle and breast tissue after menopause. Exemestane is a steroid shaped like androstenedione, so aromatase binds it as if it were the real substrate. When the enzyme starts its normal reaction, exemestane is turned into a reactive intermediate that bonds permanently to the enzyme. This mechanism-based (suicide) inactivation means the cell must make new aromatase protein to restore estrogen production. Circulating estrogens fall, ER-positive breast cancer cells lose their growth stimulus and proliferation stops. It does not meaningfully affect cortisol or aldosterone production. Its metabolite 17-hydroexemestane has weak androgenic activity.", "body": [["Breast tumor", "ER-positive tumor cells lose estrogen and grow more slowly, lowering recurrence risk."], ["Bone", "Low estrogen increases bone breakdown, so bone density falls and fractures are more likely."], ["Joints and muscles", "Joint pain and stiffness are common with estrogen loss."], ["Temperature control", "Hot flashes and sweating are common from low estrogen acting on the hypothalamus."], ["Sleep and mood", "Insomnia, fatigue and headache are frequently reported."], ["Skin and hair", "Mild androgenic effects from its metabolite may cause acne or hair changes in some people."]], "journey": "Exemestane is taken by mouth once daily after a meal, because food increases absorption. It peaks in one to two hours and is highly protein bound. The liver metabolizes it through CYP3A4 and aldoketoreductases, forming the active 17-hydroexemestane. Metabolites leave in urine and feces in about equal amounts. The half-life is about a day, but because the enzyme is permanently inactivated, estrogen suppression lasts longer than drug levels.", "sidefx": "As with other aromatase inhibitors, the main side effects come from estrogen deprivation: bone loss, joint pain, hot flashes and fatigue. Strong CYP3A4 inducers can lower exposure. Exemestane is not used in premenopausal women unless ovarian function is suppressed, because the ovaries would compensate. It is contraindicated in pregnancy. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Because exemestane destroys the enzyme it binds, it is often taught as a classic example of a suicide inhibitor.", "src": ["Aromasin (exemestane) FDA label", "Coombes RC et al. NEJM 2004 (IES)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "megestrol": {"origin": "Megestrol acetate is a synthetic progestin first made in the late 1950s, during the era when chemists were modifying progesterone to create oral hormones, including early birth control pills. It was studied as a contraceptive, but its main US use became palliative treatment of advanced breast and endometrial cancer, sold as Megace tablets. Doctors noticed that patients taking it gained appetite and weight. That observation led to studies in people with AIDS-related wasting, and in 1993 the FDA approved megestrol oral suspension for anorexia, cachexia or unexplained weight loss in patients with AIDS. A concentrated form, Megace ES, came later.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Megestrol acetate is synthesized as an oral progestin."], ["1970s", "Megace tablets are used in the US for advanced breast and endometrial cancer."], ["1993", "FDA approves megestrol oral suspension for AIDS-related anorexia and weight loss."], ["2005", "A concentrated suspension, Megace ES, is approved."]], "cell": "Megestrol acetate is a 17-alpha-acetoxyprogesterone derivative that activates progesterone receptors, nuclear receptors that change gene transcription. In endometrial cancer, progesterone receptor activation opposes estrogen-driven growth and promotes differentiation of the endometrial lining. In breast cancer, megestrol suppresses pituitary LH release, lowering hormone production, and has direct antiestrogenic effects on tumor cells. Megestrol also binds the glucocorticoid receptor with meaningful strength, which explains Cushing-like effects, raised blood sugar and suppression of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis. The appetite effect is not fully understood but is thought to involve neuropeptide Y in the hypothalamus and lower levels of inflammatory cytokines that drive cachexia.", "body": [["Appetite and weight", "Appetite increases and weight rises over weeks, though much of the gain is fat and fluid rather than muscle."], ["Endometrium and breast", "Progestin action slows growth of hormone-sensitive tumors in the uterus and breast."], ["Adrenal glands", "Glucocorticoid activity suppresses ACTH, so the adrenal glands make less cortisol, and stopping suddenly can cause adrenal insufficiency."], ["Blood clotting", "Risk of deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism increases."], ["Metabolism", "Blood sugar can rise and new diabetes can develop from glucocorticoid-like effects."], ["Reproductive system", "Breakthrough uterine bleeding and impotence can occur from hormonal effects."]], "journey": "Megestrol is taken by mouth as tablets or a liquid suspension. The concentrated suspension absorbs better than the original suspension, especially without food. It peaks in one to three hours and is mostly bound to albumin. The liver hydroxylates and conjugates it, and most of it leaves in urine. The half-life is about 34 hours on average but varies widely between people.", "sidefx": "Clots likely relate to progestin and glucocorticoid effects on clotting factors and vessels. Fluid retention, high blood sugar and Cushingoid features come from glucocorticoid receptor activation. Adrenal suppression means stress, infection or sudden stopping can trigger adrenal crisis, which needs steroid replacement. Weight gain is mostly fat and fluid. It is contraindicated in pregnancy. There is no specific antidote; adrenal insufficiency is treated with glucocorticoids.", "src": ["Megace (megestrol acetate) FDA labels", "Megace ES FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "relugolix": {"origin": "Gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) was isolated and sequenced in 1971 by the labs of Andrew Schally and Roger Guillemin, who shared the 1977 Nobel Prize. For decades, prostate cancer hormone therapy relied on injected GnRH agonists such as leuprolide, which first cause a testosterone surge before shutting the pituitary down, and later on injected peptide antagonists such as degarelix. Takeda scientists in Japan set out to make a small, non-peptide molecule that blocks the GnRH receptor and can be swallowed. The result was relugolix (TAK-385), developed further by Myovant Sciences. After the HERO trial, the FDA approved it as Orgovyx in 2020, the first oral GnRH receptor antagonist for advanced prostate cancer. A combination with estradiol and norethindrone (Myfembree) was approved in 2021 for uterine fibroid bleeding.", "timeline": [["1971", "Schally and Guillemin labs determine the structure of GnRH."], ["1977", "Schally and Guillemin share the Nobel Prize for hypothalamic hormones."], ["2020", "FDA approves relugolix (Orgovyx), the first oral GnRH antagonist for advanced prostate cancer."], ["2021", "Relugolix combination (Myfembree) is approved for heavy menstrual bleeding with fibroids."]], "cell": "The hypothalamus releases GnRH in pulses, which binds GnRH receptors, Gq-coupled receptors on pituitary gonadotroph cells. This raises calcium and protein kinase C activity and triggers release of LH and FSH. LH tells the testicular Leydig cells to make testosterone. Relugolix is a nonpeptide competitive antagonist that occupies the GnRH receptor without activating it. LH and FSH fall within hours, and testosterone drops to castrate levels within days. Because it blocks rather than overstimulates the receptor, there is no initial testosterone surge or tumor flare. Prostate cancer cells that depend on androgen receptor signaling lose their growth stimulus, and PSA falls. When the drug stops, testosterone recovers relatively quickly.", "body": [["Prostate tumor", "Androgen-dependent cancer cells stop growing as testosterone falls to castrate levels."], ["Pituitary", "LH and FSH release is blocked immediately, without the surge seen with GnRH agonists."], ["Temperature control", "Hot flashes are very common because sex hormones normally stabilize the hypothalamic thermostat."], ["Bone", "Low testosterone, and the estrogen made from it, speeds bone loss and raises fracture risk."], ["Heart", "Androgen deprivation can lengthen the QT interval and is linked to cardiovascular risk, though relugolix had fewer major cardiovascular events than leuprolide in HERO."], ["Metabolism", "Blood sugar and triglycerides can rise, and fatigue and weight changes are common."]], "journey": "Relugolix is taken by mouth once daily. Oral bioavailability is low, partly because it is a P-glycoprotein substrate in the gut, so strong P-gp inhibitors raise its levels and are avoided. It peaks in about two hours. It is metabolized mainly by CYP3A with a small role for CYP2C8, and about 80 percent leaves in feces. The effective half-life is about 25 hours, with a longer terminal phase.", "sidefx": "Hot flashes, fatigue, bone loss and metabolic changes are effects of testosterone deprivation itself. QT prolongation is a class effect of androgen deprivation because androgens shorten cardiac repolarization. Missing doses lets testosterone recover quickly, which matters for disease control. Diarrhea and constipation are drug-related. There is no antidote; effects reverse after stopping as testosterone recovers.", "src": ["Orgovyx (relugolix) FDA label", "Shore ND et al. NEJM 2020 (HERO)", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1977"]}, "bicalutamide": {"origin": "The idea of treating prostate cancer by removing androgens goes back to Charles Huggins, who showed in 1941 that castration or estrogen made prostate cancer regress, earning a Nobel Prize in 1966. Chemists then looked for drugs that block the androgen receptor directly. Bicalutamide was discovered in the 1980s by researchers at Imperial Chemical Industries (ICI) in England, whose pharmaceutical arm became Zeneca and later AstraZeneca. It was designed to be more potent, longer acting and less toxic to the liver than flutamide. The FDA approved it as Casodex in 1995 for use with a GnRH agonist in metastatic prostate cancer. It became the most widely used first-generation antiandrogen.", "timeline": [["1941", "Charles Huggins shows prostate cancer depends on androgens."], ["1966", "Huggins receives the Nobel Prize for hormonal treatment of prostate cancer."], ["1980s", "ICI researchers in England discover bicalutamide."], ["1995", "FDA approves bicalutamide (Casodex) for metastatic prostate cancer with a GnRH agonist."]], "cell": "Testosterone and its more potent form dihydrotestosterone (DHT) bind the androgen receptor in prostate cells. The bound receptor sheds heat shock proteins, moves to the nucleus, binds androgen response elements and drives genes for growth and PSA. Bicalutamide is a nonsteroidal competitive antagonist that sits in the ligand-binding domain and keeps androgens out, so the receptor cannot activate these genes. Nearly all activity comes from the R-enantiomer. When used alone, it also blocks androgen feedback in the hypothalamus and pituitary, so LH and testosterone rise, and more testosterone is converted to estrogen. Paired with a GnRH agonist, it blocks the surge-related flare and also adrenal androgens. Over time, receptor mutations can turn bicalutamide into a weak agonist, which explains why some tumors respond to stopping it (antiandrogen withdrawal).", "body": [["Prostate tumor", "Blocking the androgen receptor slows growth of androgen-dependent prostate cancer and lowers PSA."], ["Breasts", "Gynecomastia and breast pain are common, especially when used alone, because higher testosterone is converted to estrogen while androgen action is blocked."], ["Temperature control", "Hot flashes come from hormonal effects on the hypothalamus."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise, and rare severe liver injury has been reported, so liver tests are checked."], ["Gut", "Constipation and diarrhea are common and are usually mild."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease is a rare reported effect."]], "journey": "Bicalutamide is taken by mouth once daily and is well absorbed but slow to peak, about 31 hours for the active R-enantiomer. It is about 96 percent protein bound. The liver oxidizes the R-enantiomer through CYP3A4 and then glucuronidates it, while the inactive S-enantiomer is glucuronidated directly. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. The half-life of the active form is about six days, so steady state takes about a month.", "sidefx": "Gynecomastia, breast pain and hot flashes follow from blocking androgen action while estrogen is still present. Liver injury is idiosyncratic and less common than with flutamide. Bicalutamide can increase the effect of warfarin by displacing it from proteins, so INR is watched. There is no antidote; side effects resolve after stopping.", "src": ["Casodex (bicalutamide) FDA label", "Huggins C, Hodges CV. Cancer Res 1941", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "flutamide": {"origin": "Flutamide was developed at the Schering Corporation (later Schering-Plough) in the US in the late 1960s and early 1970s as one of the first nonsteroidal androgen receptor blockers. Earlier antiandrogens such as cyproterone were steroids with hormonal side effects. Flutamide's role grew in the 1980s when Canadian researcher Fernand Labrie promoted combined androgen blockade: pairing a GnRH agonist or castration with an antiandrogen to also block adrenal androgens and the flare when GnRH agonists are started. The FDA approved flutamide as Eulexin in 1989. Because of liver toxicity and three-times-daily dosing, it has largely been replaced by bicalutamide and newer agents.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Schering Corporation develops flutamide, an early nonsteroidal antiandrogen."], ["1980s", "Fernand Labrie popularizes combined androgen blockade."], ["1989", "FDA approves flutamide (Eulexin) for prostate cancer with LHRH agonists."]], "cell": "Flutamide itself is a prodrug. The liver quickly turns it into 2-hydroxyflutamide, the active antiandrogen. This metabolite competes with testosterone and dihydrotestosterone for the ligand-binding domain of the androgen receptor in prostate cells. With the receptor blocked, it cannot move to the nucleus and switch on androgen-driven genes for growth and PSA. Combined with a GnRH agonist, it blunts the early testosterone surge (flare) and blocks adrenal androgens that remain after castration. Used alone, it would raise LH and testosterone because pituitary feedback is blocked.", "body": [["Prostate tumor", "Androgen receptor blockade slows prostate cancer growth and lowers PSA when combined with testosterone suppression."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise, and severe or fatal liver failure has occurred, so liver tests are monitored closely."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is a common and sometimes dose-limiting effect."], ["Breasts", "Gynecomastia and breast tenderness can occur from altered androgen-estrogen balance."], ["Blood", "Methemoglobinemia and hemolytic anemia are rare reported effects."], ["Urine", "The urine can turn amber or yellow-green from the drug and its metabolites, which is harmless."]], "journey": "Flutamide is taken by mouth and absorbed well, but most of it is converted on its first pass through the liver, mainly by CYP1A2, into active 2-hydroxyflutamide. That metabolite peaks in about two hours and is highly protein bound. Its half-life is about six hours, longer in older adults, which is why flutamide needs dosing three times daily. Metabolites leave mainly in urine.", "sidefx": "The boxed warning for hepatotoxicity reflects reactive metabolites formed in the liver that can damage liver cells, sometimes leading to liver failure, transplant or death. Diarrhea is a frequent intestinal effect. Gynecomastia and hot flashes come from androgen blockade. Methemoglobinemia relates to oxidizing metabolites acting on hemoglobin. There is no specific antidote; stopping the drug is the key step if liver injury appears.", "src": ["Eulexin (flutamide) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "nilutamide": {"origin": "Nilutamide was developed by the French company Roussel Uclaf as a nonsteroidal androgen receptor blocker, a close chemical relative of flutamide. It was designed to be used after surgical castration (orchiectomy) to block the androgens still made by the adrenal glands, an approach called combined or complete androgen blockade. The FDA approved it as Nilandron in 1996 for metastatic prostate cancer in combination with orchiectomy. Its long half-life allowed once-daily dosing, an advantage over flutamide. Unusual side effects, including trouble adapting to darkness and lung inflammation, limited its use. The Nilandron brand has been discontinued, and generic tablets remain.", "timeline": [["1941", "Charles Huggins shows prostate cancer depends on androgens."], ["1980s", "Roussel Uclaf develops nilutamide for combined androgen blockade."], ["1996", "FDA approves nilutamide (Nilandron) for use with orchiectomy in metastatic prostate cancer."]], "cell": "After orchiectomy, the testes no longer make testosterone, but the adrenal glands still produce weaker androgens that can be converted to testosterone and dihydrotestosterone in prostate tissue. Nilutamide competitively binds the ligand-binding domain of the androgen receptor and keeps these androgens out. The receptor cannot move into the nucleus and switch on androgen-dependent genes that drive prostate cancer growth and PSA production. Combined with castration, this gives more complete blockade of androgen signaling. Its unusual effects on dark adaptation and alcohol tolerance are not explained by androgen blockade and are thought to be drug-specific.", "body": [["Prostate tumor", "Blocking residual adrenal androgens after orchiectomy slows growth of androgen-dependent cancer and lowers PSA."], ["Eyes", "Many patients have delayed adaptation when moving from light to dark, which can make night driving or tunnels hazardous."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial pneumonitis, with cough, shortness of breath and fever, can occur, more often in Japanese patients in studies."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise and hepatitis can occur, so liver tests are checked."], ["Alcohol tolerance", "Some patients have flushing and feel unwell after drinking alcohol, a disulfiram-like reaction."], ["Temperature control", "Hot flashes come from androgen blockade acting on the hypothalamus."]], "journey": "Nilutamide is taken by mouth and is rapidly and completely absorbed. It is moderately protein bound. The liver metabolizes it extensively, with CYP2C19 involvement, into several metabolites, some with weak activity. Metabolites leave mainly in urine. Its half-life is long, about two days on average, which supports once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "The boxed warning for interstitial pneumonitis reflects lung inflammation that can progress to fibrosis; a baseline chest X-ray is recommended and the drug is stopped if symptoms appear. Delayed dark adaptation is a distinctive retinal effect. The alcohol intolerance resembles a disulfiram reaction. Liver injury is idiosyncratic. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Nilandron (nilutamide) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "enzalutamide": {"origin": "Enzalutamide came from an academic lab. In the mid-2000s, Charles Sawyers at UCLA learned that castration-resistant prostate cancer often still depends on the androgen receptor, which tumors overproduce. Older antiandrogens like bicalutamide can even act as weak activators when the receptor is overexpressed. Sawyers teamed with UCLA chemist Michael Jung to design molecules that bind the receptor more tightly and block it at several steps. Their lead, MDV3100, was licensed to Medivation, which developed it with Astellas. The 2009 Science paper by Tran and colleagues described the drug. After the AFFIRM trial, the FDA approved enzalutamide as Xtandi in 2012 for metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer after docetaxel. Later approvals moved it earlier in the disease. Pfizer acquired Medivation in 2016.", "timeline": [["2004", "Sawyers lab shows increased androgen receptor drives castration resistance."], ["2009", "Tran, Jung, Sawyers and colleagues publish MDV3100 in Science."], ["2012", "FDA approves enzalutamide (Xtandi) for metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer."], ["2016", "Pfizer acquires Medivation."]], "cell": "Enzalutamide is a pure androgen receptor antagonist that blocks receptor signaling at three steps. First, it binds the ligand-binding domain with much higher affinity than bicalutamide, keeping testosterone and dihydrotestosterone out. Second, it prevents the receptor from moving from the cytoplasm into the nucleus. Third, it impairs the receptor's binding to DNA and recruitment of coactivators. Unlike first-generation drugs, it has no agonist activity even when the receptor is overexpressed. Androgen-driven genes such as PSA turn off, prostate cancer cells stop dividing and many undergo apoptosis. Its active metabolite, N-desmethyl enzalutamide, has similar potency. Enzalutamide also crosses into the brain and weakly blocks the GABA-A receptor, which lowers the seizure threshold.", "body": [["Prostate tumor", "Androgen receptor signaling shuts down even in castration-resistant cancer, lowering PSA and slowing growth."], ["Brain", "Fatigue is very common, and GABA-A blockade can rarely cause seizures; posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome has been reported."], ["Bones and falls", "Androgen deprivation weakens bone, and falls and fractures are more common."], ["Heart and vessels", "Blood pressure can rise, and ischemic heart disease occurred more often than with placebo in trials."], ["Temperature control", "Hot flashes come from androgen blockade in the hypothalamus."], ["Liver enzymes", "As a strong CYP3A4 inducer, it speeds the breakdown of many other drugs."]], "journey": "Enzalutamide is taken by mouth once daily and absorbed well, peaking in about one hour. It is highly protein bound. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP2C8 into the active N-desmethyl metabolite, with a smaller CYP3A4 role. Because it strongly induces CYP3A4 and moderately induces CYP2C9 and CYP2C19, it lowers levels of many co-medications. Metabolites leave mainly in urine. The half-life is about six days, and the active metabolite lasts even longer, so steady state takes about a month.", "sidefx": "Fatigue, hot flashes, bone loss and falls reflect deeper androgen deprivation. Seizures come from off-target blockade of GABA-A chloride channels in the brain. Hypertension and cardiovascular events are linked to androgen deprivation and drug effects. Strong enzyme induction can cause loss of effect of anticoagulants, immunosuppressants and other drugs. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Xtandi (enzalutamide) FDA label", "Tran C et al. Science 2009", "Scher HI et al. NEJM 2012 (AFFIRM)"]}, "apalutamide": {"origin": "Apalutamide is a close cousin of enzalutamide that came from the same UCLA research program of Charles Sawyers and Michael Jung. Their team made a series of thiohydantoin compounds, and one, later called ARN-509, was developed by Aragon Pharmaceuticals. Johnson & Johnson's Janssen acquired Aragon in 2013. The SPARTAN trial in men with castration-resistant prostate cancer that had not yet spread, but whose PSA was rising quickly, showed a large delay in metastasis. In 2018 the FDA approved apalutamide as Erleada, the first drug approved for non-metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer. It was later approved for metastatic castration-sensitive disease based on the TITAN trial.", "timeline": [["2009", "UCLA researchers publish the thiohydantoin antiandrogen series."], ["2013", "Johnson & Johnson acquires Aragon and ARN-509."], ["2018", "FDA approves apalutamide (Erleada), the first drug for non-metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer."], ["2019", "Approval expands to metastatic castration-sensitive prostate cancer."]], "cell": "Apalutamide binds the ligand-binding domain of the androgen receptor and prevents testosterone and dihydrotestosterone from binding. It also blocks the receptor's movement into the nucleus, its binding to DNA and androgen-driven transcription. It has no agonist activity even when the receptor is overexpressed. As a result, PSA and other androgen-dependent genes switch off, and prostate cancer cells proliferate less and die more. Its active metabolite, N-desmethyl apalutamide, also contributes. Like enzalutamide, it can cross into the brain and weakly block GABA-A receptors. Androgen receptor blockade and drug effects also change thyroid hormone handling, which explains hypothyroidism.", "body": [["Prostate tumor", "Androgen receptor signaling is blocked, delaying spread and lowering PSA."], ["Skin", "Rash is common, sometimes widespread, and severe reactions such as SJS/TEN and DRESS are rare but reported."], ["Thyroid", "Hypothyroidism develops in some patients, partly from faster breakdown of thyroid hormone, so TSH is checked."], ["Bones and falls", "Falls and fractures are more common from androgen deprivation and fatigue."], ["Brain", "Seizures can occur rarely because of GABA-A blockade."], ["Heart and vessels", "Ischemic heart disease and stroke occurred more often than with placebo in trials."]], "journey": "Apalutamide is taken by mouth once daily and is almost completely absorbed, peaking in about two hours. It is highly protein bound. The liver metabolizes it through CYP2C8 and CYP3A4 into N-desmethyl apalutamide. Like enzalutamide, it is a strong CYP3A4 and CYP2C19 inducer and a weak CYP2C9 inducer, so it lowers levels of many other drugs. Elimination is mainly in urine with some in feces. The half-life is about three days at steady state.", "sidefx": "Fatigue, hot flashes and bone loss reflect androgen deprivation. Rash is a drug-specific immune reaction. Hypothyroidism can occur from increased thyroid hormone clearance. Seizures relate to GABA-A blockade. Enzyme induction can make warfarin, some anticoagulants and other drugs less effective. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Erleada (apalutamide) FDA label", "Smith MR et al. NEJM 2018 (SPARTAN)", "Chi KN et al. NEJM 2019 (TITAN)"]}, "darolutamide": {"origin": "Darolutamide was discovered by the Finnish company Orion and developed with Bayer. Its chemical structure is quite different from enzalutamide and apalutamide: it is a larger, more polar molecule. That design limits how much drug crosses the blood-brain barrier, with the goal of fewer central nervous system side effects such as seizures and fatigue. In the ARAMIS trial, it delayed metastasis in non-metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer, and the FDA approved it as Nubeqa in 2019. It was later approved with docetaxel for metastatic hormone-sensitive prostate cancer based on the ARASENS trial, and then for that setting without chemotherapy.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Orion discovers darolutamide and develops it with Bayer."], ["2019", "FDA approves darolutamide (Nubeqa) for non-metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer."], ["2022", "Approval expands to metastatic hormone-sensitive prostate cancer with docetaxel."]], "cell": "Darolutamide is a competitive androgen receptor antagonist. It binds the ligand-binding domain and blocks testosterone and dihydrotestosterone binding, receptor movement into the nucleus and receptor-driven transcription. Its active metabolite keto-darolutamide is similarly potent. Its structure also lets it block some mutated receptors that can be activated by other antiandrogens. With androgen signaling stopped, PSA falls and prostate cancer cell growth slows. Because it is polar and a substrate of efflux pumps at the blood-brain barrier, brain levels are low, so it has less GABA-A related seizure risk than enzalutamide in studies.", "body": [["Prostate tumor", "Androgen receptor signaling is blocked, delaying metastasis and prolonging survival in trials."], ["Brain", "Low brain penetration means fatigue and seizures are less common than with some other androgen receptor inhibitors, though seizures can still occur."], ["Heart and vessels", "Ischemic heart disease and heart failure occurred somewhat more often than with placebo."], ["Limbs and skin", "Pain in the arms or legs and rash are among the reported effects."], ["Liver", "AST and bilirubin can rise, so liver tests are monitored."], ["Blood", "Neutrophil counts can fall, usually without causing infection."]], "journey": "Darolutamide is taken by mouth twice daily with food, which greatly improves absorption. It peaks in about four hours. It is highly protein bound, and its metabolite keto-darolutamide is even more tightly bound. The liver metabolizes it through CYP3A4 oxidation and UGT1A9 and UGT1A1 glucuronidation. About 63 percent leaves in urine and 32 percent in feces. The half-life is about 20 hours. It is not a strong enzyme inducer, so it has fewer drug interactions than enzalutamide or apalutamide.", "sidefx": "Fatigue, hot flashes and bone effects come from androgen deprivation. Cardiovascular events relate to androgen deprivation and underlying risk. The lower rate of central nervous system effects reflects its low brain penetration. Strong CYP3A4 inducers can lower its levels. It can harm a developing fetus through a partner, so contraception is used. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Nubeqa (darolutamide) FDA label", "Fizazi K et al. NEJM 2019 (ARAMIS)", "Smith MR et al. NEJM 2022 (ARASENS)"]}, "abiraterone": {"origin": "Abiraterone was designed at the Institute of Cancer Research in London in the early 1990s by a team including chemist Gerry Potter. They knew that the antifungal ketoconazole lowers androgens by blocking the steroid enzyme CYP17 but is too weak and toxic to use long-term. Potter designed a steroid-based molecule that fits CYP17 tightly. The drug sat unused for years until Cougar Biotechnology licensed it, and Johnson & Johnson acquired Cougar in 2009. Trials led by Johann de Bono and others in castration-resistant prostate cancer showed that tumors still depend on androgens made in the adrenal glands and in the tumor itself. The FDA approved abiraterone acetate as Zytiga in 2011, given with prednisone.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Gerry Potter and colleagues at the Institute of Cancer Research design abiraterone."], ["2009", "Johnson & Johnson acquires Cougar Biotechnology and abiraterone."], ["2011", "FDA approves abiraterone acetate (Zytiga) with prednisone for metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer."], ["2018", "Approval expands to high-risk metastatic castration-sensitive prostate cancer."]], "cell": "Abiraterone acetate is a prodrug that is converted to abiraterone, which irreversibly inhibits CYP17A1. This enzyme has two activities, 17-alpha-hydroxylase and C17,20-lyase, needed to turn pregnenolone and progesterone into DHEA and androstenedione, the precursors of testosterone. Blocking CYP17A1 shuts down androgen production in the testes, adrenal glands and prostate tumor tissue, driving testosterone even lower than castration alone. Without cortisol production through the same enzyme, the pituitary raises ACTH. High ACTH pushes steroid precursors toward mineralocorticoids such as deoxycorticosterone, which cause salt retention, high blood pressure and low potassium. Low-dose prednisone replaces cortisol, lowers ACTH and controls this mineralocorticoid excess.", "body": [["Prostate tumor", "Androgen production by all sources drops to very low levels, so castration-resistant cancer that still relies on androgens slows."], ["Adrenal glands", "Cortisol production falls and ACTH rises, so prednisone is given with it and stress can unmask adrenal insufficiency."], ["Kidneys and electrolytes", "Extra mineralocorticoids cause sodium and fluid retention and potassium loss, leading to edema and hypokalemia."], ["Heart and vessels", "Hypertension, fluid retention and low potassium can strain the heart and cause arrhythmias, especially in heart disease."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises are common, and severe hepatotoxicity can occur, so liver tests are monitored."], ["Joints", "Joint swelling and discomfort are commonly reported."]], "journey": "Abiraterone acetate is taken by mouth and is converted to abiraterone in the body. Absorption rises dramatically with food, by several fold with a high-fat meal, so Zytiga is taken on an empty stomach; Yonsa is a finer formulation with different instructions. It peaks in about two hours and is more than 99 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it through CYP3A4 and SULT2A1, and most is eliminated in feces. The half-life is about 12 hours.", "sidefx": "Hypokalemia, hypertension and edema are direct results of mineralocorticoid excess when cortisol feedback is lost, which is why prednisone is given and potassium and blood pressure are monitored. Adrenal insufficiency can appear during stress or when prednisone is stopped. Liver injury is idiosyncratic and is monitored with liver tests. Abiraterone inhibits CYP2D6, raising levels of some drugs. There is no antidote; adrenal crisis is treated with glucocorticoids.", "src": ["Zytiga (abiraterone acetate) FDA label", "de Bono JS et al. NEJM 2011", "Potter GA et al. J Med Chem 1995"]}, "pegfilgrastim": {"origin": "In the 1960s, Australian scientist Donald Metcalf at the Walter and Eliza Hall Institute in Melbourne found that certain proteins, which he called colony-stimulating factors, made blood stem cells grow into white cell colonies in the lab. Granulocyte colony-stimulating factor (G-CSF) was cloned in the mid-1980s, including by Lawrence Souza and colleagues at Amgen, who produced it in bacteria. That product, filgrastim (Neupogen), was approved in 1991 to shorten neutropenia after chemotherapy, but it needed daily shots. Amgen then attached a polyethylene glycol (PEG) chain to filgrastim, making it too large for the kidneys to clear. The resulting pegfilgrastim (Neulasta) was approved in 2002 and needs only one injection per chemotherapy cycle. An on-body injector and several biosimilars followed.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Donald Metcalf discovers colony-stimulating factors in Melbourne."], ["1986", "Human G-CSF is cloned, including by Lawrence Souza's group at Amgen."], ["1991", "FDA approves filgrastim (Neupogen)."], ["2002", "FDA approves pegfilgrastim (Neulasta) for once-per-cycle dosing."], ["2014", "Neulasta Onpro on-body injector is approved."], ["2018", "First pegfilgrastim biosimilar (Fulphila) is approved."]], "cell": "Pegfilgrastim is filgrastim, a recombinant human G-CSF made in E. coli, with a 20 kDa PEG chain attached. It binds the G-CSF receptor (CSF3R) on myeloid progenitor cells and mature neutrophils. Binding causes the receptor to pair up and activate JAK kinases, which phosphorylate STAT3 and STAT5 and also switch on PI3K-AKT and MAPK pathways. These signals make neutrophil precursors divide, mature faster and survive longer. G-CSF also loosens the retention of neutrophils in marrow by lowering CXCL12 signaling and activating proteases, releasing neutrophils into the blood. The PEG chain blocks kidney filtration, so the main way the drug is cleared is by binding and internalization into neutrophils. When neutrophil counts recover, clearance rises, making the drug self-regulating.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Production of neutrophils speeds up, shortening the time patients spend with dangerously low counts after chemotherapy."], ["Blood", "Neutrophil counts rise within a day; too much stimulation can cause leukocytosis."], ["Bones", "Bone pain, often in the back, hips and sternum, comes from marrow expansion and inflammatory signals."], ["Spleen", "The spleen can enlarge from extra blood cell production and, rarely, rupture, which causes left upper abdominal or shoulder pain."], ["Lungs", "Neutrophils can gather in the lungs, rarely causing acute respiratory distress syndrome."], ["Blood vessels", "Rare capillary leak syndrome and aortitis have been reported."]], "journey": "Pegfilgrastim is injected under the skin, by syringe or by an on-body injector that delivers it about a day after chemotherapy. It is absorbed slowly and peaks after one to four days. The PEG chain prevents kidney clearance, so it stays in the blood until neutrophils bind and remove it. Its half-life is about 15 to 80 hours and is shortest when neutrophil counts are high. It is broken down inside cells like other proteins.", "sidefx": "Bone pain reflects marrow expansion and cytokine release. Splenic enlargement and rare rupture come from increased cell production in the spleen. ARDS may follow neutrophils moving into the lungs. In sickle cell disease, more white cells and inflammation can trigger severe crises. Allergic reactions can be serious. It is not given within a short window before chemotherapy because rapidly dividing precursors would be more sensitive to chemotherapy. There is no antidote; effects fade as the drug clears.", "fun": "Pegfilgrastim clears itself: the more neutrophils it creates, the faster those neutrophils bind and remove the drug.", "src": ["Neulasta (pegfilgrastim) FDA label", "Metcalf D. Blood 2008 (history of CSFs)", "Souza LM et al. Science 1986"]}, "aprepitant": {"origin": "Substance P was discovered in 1931 by Ulf von Euler and John Gaddum, who found a gut and brain extract that made intestinal muscle contract. Decades later it was identified as a peptide that acts on neurokinin-1 (NK1) receptors. Merck scientists developed NK1 blockers in the 1990s, first hoping they would treat depression. Those studies were disappointing, but animal work showed that NK1 antagonists blocked vomiting triggered by many causes, including cisplatin. In trials, adding aprepitant to a 5-HT3 blocker and dexamethasone prevented nausea and vomiting in the days after chemotherapy far better. The FDA approved aprepitant capsules as Emend in 2003. An IV prodrug, fosaprepitant, and later IV aprepitant emulsions followed.", "timeline": [["1931", "Ulf von Euler and John Gaddum discover substance P."], ["1990s", "Merck develops NK1 receptor antagonists, first tested for depression."], ["2003", "FDA approves aprepitant (Emend), the first NK1 antagonist for chemotherapy nausea."], ["2008", "IV prodrug fosaprepitant is approved."]], "cell": "Chemotherapy, especially cisplatin, triggers vomiting in two phases. The acute phase in the first day is driven mainly by serotonin from gut enterochromaffin cells acting on 5-HT3 receptors. The delayed phase over the next several days is driven largely by substance P. Substance P binds NK1 receptors, Gq-coupled receptors on neurons in the nucleus tractus solitarius and area postrema in the brainstem, which coordinate the vomiting reflex. Aprepitant crosses the blood-brain barrier and occupies these central NK1 receptors with high affinity, blocking substance P signaling. It adds to 5-HT3 blockers and dexamethasone and is especially effective against delayed-phase vomiting.", "body": [["Brainstem", "Blocking NK1 receptors in the vomiting center prevents nausea and vomiting, especially in the delayed phase after chemotherapy."], ["Gut", "Hiccups, indigestion, diarrhea and reduced appetite are reported."], ["Energy level", "Fatigue and weakness are common, although they are hard to separate from chemotherapy effects."], ["Liver", "Mild, usually temporary rises in ALT can occur."], ["Drug-metabolizing enzymes", "Aprepitant inhibits and then induces CYP3A4 and induces CYP2C9, changing levels of dexamethasone, warfarin and hormonal contraceptives."]], "journey": "Aprepitant capsules are absorbed from the gut and peak in about four hours. Fosaprepitant and IV aprepitant are given into a vein; fosaprepitant is quickly converted to aprepitant. More than 95 percent is protein bound and it crosses into the brain. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP3A4, with minor CYP1A2 and CYP2C19 roles. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. The half-life is about 9 to 13 hours, but its antiemetic effect covers the days of the delayed phase.", "sidefx": "Aprepitant is generally well tolerated. Its drug interactions are the main concern: as a moderate CYP3A4 inhibitor it raises dexamethasone levels, so the dexamethasone dose in regimens is reduced, and it can raise levels of other CYP3A4 substrates. Its later CYP2C9 induction can lower INR on warfarin and reduce the effect of hormonal contraceptives for weeks. Rare serious allergic and skin reactions have been reported. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Emend (aprepitant) FDA label", "von Euler US, Gaddum JH. J Physiol 1931", "Hesketh PJ et al. J Clin Oncol 2003"]}, "granisetron": {"origin": "In the 1980s researchers learned that cisplatin and other chemotherapies make enterochromaffin cells in the gut release large amounts of serotonin, which acts on 5-HT3 receptors to trigger vomiting. Blocking these receptors in animals stopped chemotherapy-induced vomiting. Ondansetron, the first 5-HT3 blocker, was approved in 1991. Granisetron was developed by the British company Beecham (later SmithKline Beecham) and approved in the US in the early 1990s as Kytril, first as an injection and then as tablets. New delivery forms followed: a skin patch (Sancuso) approved in 2008 and an extended-release injection under the skin (Sustol) approved in 2016, which provides protection over several days.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Researchers show serotonin and 5-HT3 receptors drive chemotherapy-induced vomiting."], ["1990s", "Granisetron (Kytril) is approved in the US as an injection and tablets."], ["2008", "Granisetron transdermal patch (Sancuso) is approved."], ["2016", "Extended-release granisetron injection (Sustol) is approved."]], "cell": "Chemotherapy damages enterochromaffin cells in the small intestine, causing them to release serotonin. Serotonin binds 5-HT3 receptors, ligand-gated cation channels on vagal afferent nerve endings in the gut wall. Opening these channels depolarizes the nerves, and signals travel to the nucleus tractus solitarius and the chemoreceptor trigger zone in the area postrema, which also has 5-HT3 receptors. These centers trigger nausea and vomiting. Granisetron is a selective competitive antagonist at 5-HT3 receptors in both the gut and brainstem, blocking the acute vomiting signal. It has little affinity for dopamine, histamine or muscarinic receptors, so it avoids sedation and movement side effects of older antiemetics.", "body": [["Brainstem and gut nerves", "Blocking 5-HT3 receptors on vagal nerves and in the area postrema prevents the acute nausea and vomiting after chemotherapy and radiation."], ["Gut motility", "Constipation is common because serotonin normally helps drive intestinal movement through 5-HT3 receptors."], ["Head", "Headache is the most common side effect, possibly from serotonin-related effects on blood vessels."], ["Heart", "Like other 5-HT3 antagonists, it can lengthen the QT interval."], ["Skin", "The patch can cause local skin reactions, and the extended-release injection can cause injection-site bruising or nodules."]], "journey": "Granisetron can be given IV, by mouth, through a skin patch or as an extended-release injection under the skin. IV doses act within minutes. Oral granisetron is well absorbed, though some is lost to first-pass metabolism. The patch delivers drug slowly through the skin over several days. The liver metabolizes it through N-demethylation and ring oxidation, involving CYP1A1 and CYP3A4. A small part leaves unchanged in urine, and the rest leaves as metabolites in urine and feces. The half-life is about nine hours in cancer patients, with wide variation.", "sidefx": "Constipation follows from blocking serotonin's role in gut motility. Headache is common. QT prolongation reflects effects on cardiac ion channels at higher exposures. Combined with other serotonergic drugs, serotonin syndrome is possible. The patch area is shielded from direct sunlight or heat lamps, which can change skin absorption and cause skin reactions. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "src": ["Granisetron injection FDA label", "Sancuso (granisetron transdermal) FDA label", "Sustol (granisetron extended-release) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "netupitant-palonosetron": {"origin": "This combination comes from Helsinn, a Swiss company that developed palonosetron, a second-generation 5-HT3 antagonist with a much longer half-life and higher receptor affinity than older drugs. Palonosetron was approved in the US as Aloxi in 2003. Helsinn then paired it with netupitant, a long-acting NK1 receptor antagonist, so that one capsule could block both of the main pathways behind chemotherapy-induced vomiting over several days. The FDA approved the oral combination as Akynzeo in 2014, the first fixed combination of an NK1 and a 5-HT3 antagonist. An IV form, using the prodrug fosnetupitant with palonosetron, was approved later.", "timeline": [["2003", "FDA approves palonosetron (Aloxi), a long-acting 5-HT3 antagonist."], ["2014", "FDA approves netupitant-palonosetron (Akynzeo) capsules."], ["2018", "An IV form with fosnetupitant and palonosetron is approved."]], "cell": "Netupitant and palonosetron block two different vomiting pathways. Palonosetron is a highly selective 5-HT3 receptor antagonist with very high affinity. It blocks serotonin released from gut enterochromaffin cells after chemotherapy from activating vagal afferents and the chemoreceptor trigger zone, preventing acute vomiting. It also appears to cause receptor internalization and to interfere with crosstalk between 5-HT3 and NK1 receptors, which may explain some effect in the delayed phase. Netupitant crosses the blood-brain barrier and blocks substance P at NK1 receptors in the nucleus tractus solitarius and area postrema, preventing the delayed phase driven by substance P. Both components have long half-lives, so one dose covers about five days.", "body": [["Brainstem vomiting centers", "Combined NK1 and 5-HT3 blockade prevents acute and delayed nausea and vomiting after highly emetogenic chemotherapy."], ["Gut", "Constipation and indigestion are common from 5-HT3 blockade slowing gut motility."], ["Head", "Headache is common, as with other 5-HT3 antagonists."], ["Energy level", "Weakness and fatigue are reported, though these overlap with chemotherapy effects."], ["Drug-metabolizing enzymes", "Netupitant is a moderate CYP3A4 inhibitor, raising levels of dexamethasone and other CYP3A4 substrates for days."]], "journey": "The capsule is taken by mouth about an hour before chemotherapy. Both drugs peak in about five hours. Netupitant is more than 99 percent protein bound, is metabolized by CYP3A4 and leaves mainly in feces through bile. Its half-life is about 80 hours. Palonosetron is about 62 percent bound, is metabolized mainly by CYP2D6 with smaller CYP3A4 and CYP1A2 roles and leaves mainly in urine. Its half-life is about 48 hours.", "sidefx": "Headache and constipation come from 5-HT3 blockade. Netupitant's CYP3A4 inhibition raises dexamethasone and some chemotherapy drug levels, which is accounted for in regimens. Serotonin syndrome can occur when combined with other serotonergic drugs. Hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis, are rare. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Akynzeo (netupitant and palonosetron) FDA label", "Aloxi (palonosetron) FDA label", "Hesketh PJ et al. Ann Oncol 2014"]}, "fecal-microbiota-live-jslm": {"origin": "Using stool to treat gut illness is old: a fourth-century Chinese physician, Ge Hong, described a fecal preparation for severe diarrhea. In 1958 surgeon Ben Eiseman and colleagues in Denver reported fecal enemas for severe pseudomembranous colitis. Interest exploded after a 2013 Dutch trial in the NEJM showed donor stool infusions cured recurrent Clostridioides difficile infection far better than vancomycin. For years, fecal transplant was done with lightly regulated material. Rebiotix, later part of Ferring Pharmaceuticals, developed a standardized product made from carefully screened donors. In 2022 the FDA approved fecal microbiota, live-jslm (Rebyota), the first FDA-licensed fecal microbiota product, given rectally to adults to prevent recurrence of C. difficile infection after antibiotic treatment.", "timeline": [["4th century", "Ge Hong in China describes a fecal preparation for severe diarrhea."], ["1958", "Eiseman and colleagues report fecal enemas for pseudomembranous colitis."], ["2013", "Dutch randomized trial shows donor stool infusion cures recurrent C. difficile."], ["2022", "FDA approves Rebyota, the first licensed fecal microbiota product."]], "cell": "A healthy colon contains trillions of bacteria that resist colonization by C. difficile. Antibiotics wipe out many of these commensals. One key change involves bile acids: the liver releases primary bile acids such as taurocholate, which strongly stimulate C. difficile spores to germinate. Normal gut bacteria, including bile acid 7-alpha-dehydroxylating Clostridia, convert primary bile acids into secondary bile acids such as deoxycholate and lithocholate, which inhibit C. difficile germination and growth. Rebyota delivers a screened donor microbial community into the colon. These organisms repopulate the gut, restore secondary bile acid production, compete for nutrients such as sialic acid and simple sugars and fill mucosal niches. This rebuilt colonization resistance lowers the chance that remaining spores grow and cause recurrence.", "body": [["Colon", "Donor bacteria restore a more diverse microbial community that resists C. difficile regrowth, lowering recurrence."], ["Bile acid metabolism", "Restored bacteria convert primary bile acids to secondary bile acids that inhibit C. difficile spore germination."], ["Gut comfort", "Abdominal pain, bloating, gas, diarrhea and nausea are the most common effects as the new community settles in."], ["Immune system", "Allergic reactions to food allergens possibly present in donor stool are a theoretical concern."]], "journey": "Rebyota is given as a single dose into the rectum, usually 24 to 72 hours after finishing antibiotics for C. difficile, so the antibiotics do not kill the new bacteria. It is not absorbed into the blood. The bacteria travel through and settle in the colon, where they grow and form a new community. They leave the body in stool. There are no blood levels, metabolism or half-life in the usual sense; benefit is measured by recurrence over the following weeks.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are mild and come from changes in the gut community: bloating, gas, cramping and loose stools. Because it is made from human stool, it may carry infectious agents or food allergens despite screening and testing. Antibiotics given afterward may reduce its effect. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Ge Hong's fourth-century fecal preparation is sometimes called yellow soup in modern retellings.", "src": ["Rebyota (fecal microbiota, live-jslm) FDA prescribing information", "van Nood E et al. NEJM 2013", "Eiseman B et al. Surgery 1958"]}, "fecal-microbiota-spores-live-brpk": {"origin": "After a 2013 Dutch trial showed that donor stool infusions could cure recurrent Clostridioides difficile infection, researchers wanted a safer, more standardized product that could be swallowed. Seres Therapeutics in Cambridge, Massachusetts, focused on the bacterial spores of the Firmicutes group, which survive harsh conditions. They treat donor stool with ethanol, which kills most vegetative bacteria, viruses and fungi, then purify the surviving spores into capsules. The product, once called SER-109, reduced recurrence in the ECOSPOR III trial. In 2023 the FDA approved it as Vowst, the first orally taken fecal microbiota product, for adults to prevent recurrence of C. difficile infection after antibiotic treatment.", "timeline": [["2013", "Dutch randomized trial shows donor stool infusion cures recurrent C. difficile."], ["2010s", "Seres Therapeutics develops purified spore product SER-109."], ["2022", "ECOSPOR III results are published in the NEJM."], ["2023", "FDA approves Vowst, the first oral fecal microbiota product."]], "cell": "Antibiotics strip the colon of many commensal bacteria, leaving room for C. difficile spores to germinate. Primary bile acids from the liver, such as taurocholate, act as germination signals for those spores. Many Firmicutes, especially some Clostridia, convert primary bile acids into secondary bile acids such as deoxycholate, which inhibit C. difficile germination and growth. Vowst capsules contain purified Firmicutes spores that resist stomach acid. In the intestine they germinate into active bacteria, restore secondary bile acid metabolism and compete with C. difficile for nutrients and space. The ethanol treatment and purification are meant to reduce the risk of transmitting other organisms compared with whole stool.", "body": [["Colon", "Restored Firmicutes communities resist C. difficile regrowth, lowering recurrence over the following weeks."], ["Bile acid metabolism", "Germinated bacteria convert primary bile acids into secondary ones that block C. difficile spore germination."], ["Gut comfort", "Abdominal bloating, constipation and diarrhea are the most common effects as the gut community shifts."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue and chills have been reported in trials."]], "journey": "Vowst is taken by mouth as capsules on an empty stomach for three consecutive days, after finishing antibiotics for C. difficile and after a bowel preparation with magnesium citrate to clear remaining antibiotic. The spores pass through the stomach intact and germinate in the intestine. They are not absorbed into the blood. The bacteria settle in the colon and leave in stool. Drug levels and half-life do not apply in the usual sense.", "sidefx": "Side effects are mostly mild and related to shifts in the gut community: bloating, constipation, diarrhea and fatigue. Because it is made from human stool, it may carry infectious agents or food allergens despite processing and testing. Antibiotics taken afterward can reduce its benefit. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The ethanol step in making Vowst exploits the toughness of bacterial spores: alcohol kills most other microbes but spore-forming Firmicutes survive it.", "src": ["Vowst (fecal microbiota spores, live-brpk) FDA prescribing information", "Feuerstadt P et al. NEJM 2022 (ECOSPOR III)"]}, "captopril": {"origin": "Captopril began with snake venom. In the 1960s Brazilian pharmacologist Sergio Ferreira found that venom from the jararaca pit viper (Bothrops jararaca) contained peptides that strengthened the effects of bradykinin and lowered blood pressure. Working with John Vane in London, researchers showed these peptides block angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE). One peptide, teprotide, lowered blood pressure in people but could not be taken by mouth. At Squibb, Miguel Ondetti and David Cushman studied the enzyme's likely active site and designed a small molecule that fits it, adding a sulfhydryl group to grip the zinc atom at the center. The result, captopril, was approved by the FDA in 1981 as Capoten, the first ACE inhibitor and an early triumph of structure-based drug design. The Capoten brand is discontinued, and captopril is available as a generic.", "timeline": [["1965", "Sergio Ferreira reports bradykinin-potentiating factor in jararaca viper venom."], ["1970s", "Teprotide, a venom peptide, is shown to block ACE and lower blood pressure."], ["1977", "Ondetti and Cushman at Squibb report captopril."], ["1981", "FDA approves captopril (Capoten), the first ACE inhibitor."]], "cell": "Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE), also called kininase II, is a zinc metalloprotease on vascular endothelium, especially in the lungs. It cuts angiotensin I into angiotensin II and also breaks down bradykinin. Captopril competitively binds the ACE active site, with its sulfhydryl group binding the zinc ion. Angiotensin II levels fall, so there is less AT1 receptor-driven vasoconstriction, less aldosterone secretion from the adrenal cortex, less sympathetic activity and less sodium retention. In the kidney, less angiotensin II relaxes the efferent arteriole, lowering glomerular pressure and protein leak. Bradykinin builds up, adding vasodilation through nitric oxide and prostaglandins. Without angiotensin II feedback, renin and angiotensin I rise. Captopril is active as given and is not a prodrug.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Lower angiotensin II and higher bradykinin dilate arteries and veins, lowering blood pressure, afterload and preload."], ["Heart", "Reduced afterload and less angiotensin-driven remodeling help in heart failure and after a heart attack."], ["Kidneys", "Efferent arteriole relaxation lowers glomerular pressure and proteinuria, protecting diabetic kidneys, but GFR can drop when kidney blood flow depends on angiotensin II."], ["Adrenal glands", "Less aldosterone means more sodium loss and potassium retention, which can cause hyperkalemia."], ["Airways", "Bradykinin and substance P build up in the airways, causing a dry cough."], ["Mouth and skin", "Taste changes and rash are more common with captopril, likely linked to its sulfhydryl group."]], "journey": "Captopril is taken by mouth on an empty stomach because food reduces absorption. It starts working within about 15 to 60 minutes and peaks in about an hour. It is only modestly bound to plasma proteins. Part of it forms disulfide conjugates, and about half leaves unchanged in urine. The half-life is short, about two hours, so it is dosed two or three times a day, and it builds up in kidney impairment.", "sidefx": "Cough and angioedema come from bradykinin buildup because ACE is the enzyme that breaks bradykinin down; angioedema can block the airway. Hyperkalemia follows reduced aldosterone. Acute kidney injury can occur in bilateral renal artery stenosis or volume depletion, where GFR depends on angiotensin II tightening the efferent arteriole. Rash, taste loss, neutropenia and proteinuria are linked to the sulfhydryl group, especially in kidney disease or autoimmune disease. ACE inhibitors cause fetal kidney damage, so they are contraindicated in pregnancy. There is no specific antidote; angioedema is managed with airway support.", "fun": "Captopril is one of the first drugs designed by modeling an enzyme's active site, and it came from studying the venom of a Brazilian pit viper.", "src": ["Captopril FDA label", "Ondetti MA, Rubin B, Cushman DW. Science 1977", "Ferreira SH. Br J Pharmacol 1965"]}, "quinapril": {"origin": "Quinapril was developed by Parke-Davis, then part of Warner-Lambert, in the 1980s, after captopril and enalapril had proven that blocking ACE helps hypertension and heart failure. Like enalapril, quinapril is an ester prodrug, designed without captopril's sulfhydryl group to reduce taste loss and rash and to last long enough for once-daily dosing. Its active form, quinaprilat, binds strongly to ACE found in tissues such as blood vessel walls. The FDA approved quinapril as Accupril in 1991 for high blood pressure, and it was later approved as an add-on for heart failure. A combination with hydrochlorothiazide is sold as Accuretic.", "timeline": [["1981", "Captopril becomes the first approved ACE inhibitor."], ["1980s", "Parke-Davis develops quinapril as a nonsulfhydryl ester prodrug."], ["1991", "FDA approves quinapril (Accupril) for hypertension."]], "cell": "Quinapril has little activity until liver esterases remove its ester group, creating quinaprilat. Quinaprilat competitively inhibits angiotensin-converting enzyme (kininase II) in plasma and in tissues, including the vascular endothelium. This lowers angiotensin II, so AT1 receptor-mediated vasoconstriction, aldosterone release, sympathetic activation and cardiac and vascular remodeling all decrease. Bradykinin breakdown is reduced, adding nitric oxide and prostaglandin-mediated vasodilation. In the kidney, the efferent arteriole relaxes, which lowers glomerular pressure and proteinuria. Loss of angiotensin II feedback raises renin and angiotensin I.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Arteries and veins relax, lowering blood pressure, afterload and preload."], ["Heart", "Lower afterload and less angiotensin-driven remodeling help symptoms in heart failure."], ["Kidneys", "Lower glomerular pressure reduces proteinuria, but GFR can fall when the kidney depends on angiotensin II."], ["Adrenal glands", "Reduced aldosterone retains potassium and can cause hyperkalemia."], ["Airways", "Bradykinin buildup can cause a persistent dry cough."]], "journey": "Quinapril is taken by mouth and is well absorbed, though a high-fat meal can lower absorption. The liver quickly converts it to quinaprilat, which peaks in about two hours. Quinaprilat is highly protein bound. Its effective half-life is about two hours, but it binds tissue ACE tightly and has a long terminal phase, so blood pressure lowering lasts about a day. It leaves mainly through the kidneys, so it builds up in kidney impairment.", "sidefx": "Cough and angioedema follow from bradykinin accumulation when ACE cannot degrade it. Hyperkalemia follows reduced aldosterone. Low blood pressure, especially with the first dose in volume-depleted people, reflects sudden loss of angiotensin II tone. Kidney function can worsen in renal artery stenosis. ACE inhibitors cause fetal harm, so they are contraindicated in pregnancy. Rare liver injury has been reported with ACE inhibitors. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Accupril (quinapril) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "perindopril": {"origin": "Perindopril was developed by the French company Servier in the early 1980s as a long-acting ester prodrug ACE inhibitor. It became widely used in Europe and Australia and was approved in the US in the 1990s as Aceon. Large international trials made it well known: PROGRESS (2001) studied it after stroke, and EUROPA (2003) showed that it lowered cardiovascular events in people with stable coronary artery disease. The Aceon brand has since been discontinued in the US. A fixed combination with amlodipine, Prestalia, is available.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Servier develops perindopril in France."], ["1990s", "Perindopril is approved in the US as Aceon."], ["2001", "PROGRESS trial reports benefit in people with prior stroke or TIA."], ["2003", "EUROPA trial shows fewer cardiovascular events in stable coronary disease."]], "cell": "Perindopril is an ester prodrug. Liver esterases convert it to perindoprilat, which competitively inhibits angiotensin-converting enzyme (kininase II) in plasma and in tissues. With less angiotensin II, AT1 receptors trigger less vasoconstriction, less aldosterone release, less sympathetic activation and less fibrosis and remodeling in the heart and vessels. Bradykinin breakdown is reduced, adding vasodilation through nitric oxide and prostaglandins and possibly endothelial protective effects. In the kidney, efferent arteriole relaxation lowers glomerular pressure. Loss of angiotensin II feedback raises renin.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Arterial resistance falls, lowering blood pressure, and endothelial function may improve over time."], ["Heart", "Less angiotensin II reduces cardiac remodeling and workload, which helped lower cardiovascular events in stable coronary disease in EUROPA."], ["Kidneys", "Lower glomerular pressure protects against proteinuria, but GFR can drop when it depends on angiotensin II."], ["Adrenal glands", "Lower aldosterone causes potassium retention and possible hyperkalemia."], ["Airways", "Bradykinin buildup can cause a persistent dry cough."]], "journey": "Perindopril erbumine is taken by mouth and well absorbed, but food reduces how much is converted to the active perindoprilat. The liver hydrolyzes it to perindoprilat, which peaks about three to seven hours after a dose. Perindoprilat is only modestly bound to proteins. It has an effective half-life of several hours but binds ACE tightly, with a very long terminal phase, so effects last about a day. It leaves mainly through the kidneys, so it accumulates in kidney impairment.", "sidefx": "Cough and angioedema come from bradykinin buildup. Hyperkalemia comes from reduced aldosterone. First-dose hypotension can occur in volume-depleted patients. Kidney function can worsen in renal artery stenosis or dehydration. ACE inhibitors harm the fetal kidney and are contraindicated in pregnancy. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Aceon (perindopril erbumine) FDA label", "EUROPA Investigators. Lancet 2003", "PROGRESS Collaborative Group. Lancet 2001"]}, "irbesartan": {"origin": "Angiotensin receptor blockers grew from the observation that ACE inhibitors cause cough by letting bradykinin build up. Blocking the angiotensin II type 1 (AT1) receptor directly would stop angiotensin II action without touching bradykinin. After losartan was approved in 1995, Sanofi (then Sanofi-Synthelabo) and Bristol-Myers Squibb developed irbesartan. The FDA approved it as Avapro in 1997 for high blood pressure. In 2001 the IDNT and IRMA 2 trials showed it slowed kidney disease in people with type 2 diabetes and protein in the urine, leading to approval for diabetic nephropathy. A combination with hydrochlorothiazide is sold as Avalide.", "timeline": [["1995", "Losartan becomes the first approved angiotensin receptor blocker."], ["1997", "FDA approves irbesartan (Avapro) for hypertension."], ["2001", "IDNT and IRMA 2 trials show kidney protection in type 2 diabetes."], ["2002", "Irbesartan is approved for diabetic nephropathy."]], "cell": "Angiotensin II acts mainly through the AT1 receptor, a Gq-coupled receptor on vascular smooth muscle, adrenal cortex, kidney tubules and the brain. AT1 activation raises intracellular calcium and causes vasoconstriction, aldosterone release, sodium reabsorption, thirst and cell growth. Irbesartan binds the AT1 receptor selectively and in an insurmountable way, meaning extra angiotensin II cannot easily displace it. Vasoconstriction and aldosterone secretion fall, lowering blood pressure. Unlike ACE inhibitors, irbesartan does not affect bradykinin breakdown. Because AT1 feedback is lost, renin and angiotensin II levels rise, and the extra angiotensin II can stimulate the AT2 receptor, which may cause vasodilation.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "AT1 blockade relaxes arterioles and lowers blood pressure."], ["Kidneys", "Efferent arteriole relaxation lowers glomerular pressure and proteinuria, slowing diabetic kidney disease, but GFR can drop when kidney flow depends on angiotensin II."], ["Adrenal glands", "Less aldosterone means potassium retention and possible hyperkalemia."], ["Heart", "Lower afterload and less angiotensin-driven growth signals reduce strain on the heart."], ["Airways", "Cough is much less common than with ACE inhibitors because bradykinin is not affected."]], "journey": "Irbesartan is taken by mouth and is well absorbed, with good bioavailability not affected by food. It is not a prodrug. It peaks in about two hours and is about 90 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it by glucuronidation and CYP2C9 oxidation. About 80 percent leaves through bile and feces and about 20 percent in urine. The half-life of about 11 to 15 hours allows once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Hyperkalemia comes from reduced aldosterone. Dizziness and low blood pressure come from loss of angiotensin II vascular tone, especially with volume depletion. Kidney function can worsen in bilateral renal artery stenosis. Angioedema is rare because bradykinin is not increased, though it can still happen. Drugs acting on the renin-angiotensin system cause fetal harm and are contraindicated in pregnancy. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Avapro (irbesartan) FDA label", "Lewis EJ et al. NEJM 2001 (IDNT)", "Parving HH et al. NEJM 2001 (IRMA 2)"]}, "candesartan": {"origin": "Candesartan was developed by Takeda in Japan, building on earlier Takeda research that produced some of the first non-peptide molecules able to block the angiotensin II receptor. Those early compounds were weak, but scientists elsewhere refined them into losartan, and Takeda continued its own program. Candesartan is given as candesartan cilexetil, an ester prodrug that is activated in the gut wall during absorption. Developed with AstraZeneca, it was approved by the FDA as Atacand in 1998 for hypertension. The CHARM trials, published in 2003, showed benefits in heart failure, leading to approval for heart failure with reduced ejection fraction.", "timeline": [["1982", "Takeda patents early non-peptide angiotensin II receptor antagonists."], ["1998", "FDA approves candesartan (Atacand) for hypertension."], ["2003", "CHARM trials show benefit in heart failure."]], "cell": "Candesartan cilexetil has no activity of its own. During absorption, esterases in the intestinal wall cleave it to candesartan. Candesartan binds the AT1 receptor very tightly and dissociates slowly, so it acts as an insurmountable antagonist. This blocks angiotensin II-driven vasoconstriction, aldosterone release, sodium reabsorption, sympathetic activation and growth and fibrosis signals in the heart and vessels. It does not block ACE, so bradykinin is not increased. Renin and angiotensin II rise from lost feedback. In heart failure, blocking AT1 reduces afterload, fluid retention and harmful remodeling of the left ventricle.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Arterioles relax, lowering systemic vascular resistance and blood pressure."], ["Heart", "Less angiotensin-driven remodeling and lower afterload reduce hospitalizations and deaths in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction."], ["Kidneys", "Glomerular pressure falls, but GFR and creatinine can worsen in volume depletion or renal artery stenosis."], ["Adrenal glands", "Lower aldosterone causes potassium retention and possible hyperkalemia."], ["Brain", "Dizziness can occur when blood pressure falls, especially at the start."]], "journey": "Candesartan cilexetil is taken by mouth and is converted to active candesartan in the intestinal wall during absorption. Bioavailability is modest, and food does not matter much. It peaks in about three to four hours and is more than 99 percent protein bound. There is little further metabolism. About a third leaves in urine and two-thirds in bile and feces, mostly unchanged. The half-life is about nine hours, but tight receptor binding allows once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Hyperkalemia follows lower aldosterone, and the risk is higher with potassium supplements, spironolactone or kidney disease. Hypotension and kidney function decline occur when angiotensin II is needed to support blood pressure or glomerular filtration. Angioedema is rare. As with other renin-angiotensin drugs, it causes fetal harm and is contraindicated in pregnancy. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Atacand (candesartan cilexetil) FDA label", "Pfeffer MA et al. Lancet 2003 (CHARM-Overall)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "telmisartan": {"origin": "Telmisartan was developed by the German company Boehringer Ingelheim in the early 1990s. Chemists designed it as a non-prodrug angiotensin receptor blocker with high fat solubility and the longest half-life in its class, giving steady 24-hour blood pressure control. The FDA approved it as Micardis in 1998 for hypertension. The large ONTARGET trial, published in 2008, found telmisartan about as effective as ramipril in preventing cardiovascular events in high-risk people, with less cough, and showed that combining the two raised harm without benefit. That led to US approval for cardiovascular risk reduction in people who cannot take ACE inhibitors.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Boehringer Ingelheim develops telmisartan."], ["1998", "FDA approves telmisartan (Micardis) for hypertension."], ["2008", "ONTARGET trial compares telmisartan, ramipril and their combination."]], "cell": "Telmisartan binds the AT1 receptor with high affinity and dissociates slowly, acting as an insurmountable antagonist. Blocking AT1 prevents angiotensin II from triggering Gq-coupled calcium signaling in vascular smooth muscle, aldosterone release from the adrenal cortex, sodium reabsorption in kidney tubules and growth signals in the heart and vessels. It does not affect ACE or bradykinin. Renin and angiotensin II rise from lost feedback. In lab studies, telmisartan also partly activates PPAR-gamma, a nuclear receptor involved in insulin sensitivity, though the clinical importance of this is uncertain.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Arterioles relax and blood pressure falls, with smooth control over the full day and early morning."], ["Heart and brain", "Lower blood pressure and less angiotensin-driven vessel damage reduce heart attack and stroke risk in high-risk people."], ["Kidneys", "Glomerular pressure and proteinuria fall, but kidney function can worsen in volume depletion or renal artery stenosis."], ["Adrenal glands", "Less aldosterone means potassium retention and possible hyperkalemia."], ["Metabolism", "Partial PPAR-gamma activity has been suggested to modestly affect insulin sensitivity, though this is not an approved use."]], "journey": "Telmisartan is taken by mouth and is absorbed quickly, peaking within about an hour. It is very fat soluble and more than 99.5 percent protein bound, and it spreads widely into tissues. It is not a prodrug and is not metabolized by CYP enzymes; instead the liver glucuronidates it. More than 97 percent leaves through bile in feces, so liver or bile duct disease raises levels. The half-life of about 24 hours is the longest among ARBs.", "sidefx": "Hyperkalemia comes from lower aldosterone. Hypotension and dizziness reflect loss of angiotensin II vascular tone. Kidney function can decline when GFR depends on angiotensin II. Combining it with an ACE inhibitor or aliskiren increases kidney injury, hyperkalemia and hypotension without benefit. Telmisartan can raise digoxin levels. It causes fetal harm and is contraindicated in pregnancy. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Micardis (telmisartan) FDA label", "ONTARGET Investigators. NEJM 2008", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "aliskiren": {"origin": "Renin was discovered in 1898 by Finnish physiologist Robert Tigerstedt and his student Per Bergman, who found that kidney extracts raised blood pressure in rabbits. Because renin starts the whole renin-angiotensin cascade, blocking it was a goal for decades, but early renin inhibitors were peptides that could not be absorbed well. Using X-ray crystal structures of renin, scientists at Ciba-Geigy (later Novartis) designed a small non-peptide molecule that fits the enzyme's active site. Aliskiren was developed further with the Swiss company Speedel. The FDA approved it as Tekturna in 2007, the first direct renin inhibitor. The ALTITUDE trial, stopped in 2011, found more harm when aliskiren was added to ACE inhibitors or ARBs in people with diabetes, so that combination is contraindicated.", "timeline": [["1898", "Tigerstedt and Bergman discover renin in kidney extracts."], ["1990s", "Ciba-Geigy and Novartis use crystal structures to design aliskiren."], ["2007", "FDA approves aliskiren (Tekturna), the first direct renin inhibitor."], ["2012", "After ALTITUDE, aliskiren with ACE inhibitors or ARBs becomes contraindicated in diabetes."]], "cell": "Renin is an aspartyl protease released by juxtaglomerular cells of the kidney when blood pressure, sodium delivery or sympathetic tone signals the need. It cuts angiotensinogen into angiotensin I, the rate-limiting first step of the cascade. Aliskiren binds the active site of renin, including the S3 pocket, and blocks this cleavage. Plasma renin activity, angiotensin I, angiotensin II and aldosterone all fall. Without angiotensin II feedback, the kidney releases more renin protein, so plasma renin concentration rises, but that renin cannot work while aliskiren is bound. Lower angiotensin II leads to vasodilation, less aldosterone, more sodium excretion and potassium retention.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Lower angiotensin II relaxes arterioles and lowers blood pressure."], ["Kidneys", "Renin release rises but its activity is blocked; GFR can fall when kidney flow depends on angiotensin II."], ["Adrenal glands", "Aldosterone falls, retaining potassium and causing possible hyperkalemia."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is a common, dose-related effect, possibly because much of the drug stays in the gut."], ["Skin and airway", "Angioedema can occur, and rash and allergic reactions are reported."]], "journey": "Aliskiren is taken by mouth, but very little is absorbed, and high-fat meals reduce absorption further, so it is taken consistently with respect to meals. It is a P-glycoprotein substrate, so cyclosporine, itraconazole and other strong P-gp inhibitors raise levels sharply and are avoided. It peaks in one to three hours. It is barely metabolized, with minor CYP3A4 involvement, and mostly leaves unchanged in bile and feces. The half-life is about 24 to 40 hours, and full blood pressure lowering takes about two weeks.", "sidefx": "Hyperkalemia and kidney function decline reflect reduced angiotensin II and aldosterone, and these risks rose sharply in ALTITUDE when aliskiren was combined with ACE inhibitors or ARBs in diabetes. Angioedema can occur. Diarrhea is related to the large amount of drug left in the intestine. It causes fetal harm and is contraindicated in pregnancy. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Tekturna (aliskiren) FDA label", "Parving HH et al. NEJM 2012 (ALTITUDE)", "Tigerstedt R, Bergman PG. Skand Arch Physiol 1898"]}, "nebivolol": {"origin": "Beta blockers date to the 1960s, when James Black developed propranolol, work that contributed to his 1988 Nobel Prize. Later drugs became more selective for the heart's beta-1 receptors. Nebivolol was discovered in the 1980s by Janssen Pharmaceutica in Belgium. It stood out because, in addition to being highly beta-1 selective, it dilates blood vessels by triggering nitric oxide release from the endothelium. It was approved in Europe in the 1990s and, in 2007, the FDA approved it as Bystolic for hypertension. A combination with valsartan, Byvalson, was approved in 2016 but later discontinued.", "timeline": [["1960s", "James Black develops propranolol, the first clinically successful beta blocker."], ["1980s", "Janssen Pharmaceutica discovers nebivolol."], ["1988", "James Black shares the Nobel Prize, in part for beta blockers."], ["2007", "FDA approves nebivolol (Bystolic) for hypertension."]], "cell": "Nebivolol competitively blocks beta-1 adrenergic receptors on heart muscle and pacemaker cells. Beta-1 receptors are Gs-coupled: when activated by norepinephrine, they raise cAMP, which increases heart rate, contraction strength and AV conduction. Blocking them slows the heart, lowers contractility and reduces renin release from the kidney. At usual doses in most people, nebivolol is highly selective for beta-1. It also stimulates endothelial nitric oxide synthase, likely through beta-3 adrenergic receptor agonism, increasing nitric oxide in vessel walls. Nitric oxide activates guanylyl cyclase in smooth muscle, raising cGMP and causing vasodilation. It has no intrinsic sympathomimetic activity. At higher doses or in CYP2D6 poor metabolizers, beta-1 selectivity is lost.", "body": [["Heart", "Heart rate and contractility fall, lowering oxygen demand and blood pressure; slow heart rate and AV block can occur."], ["Blood vessels", "Nitric oxide release dilates arteries, lowering resistance rather than raising it as some older beta blockers do."], ["Kidneys", "Less beta-1 stimulation of juxtaglomerular cells lowers renin release."], ["Lungs", "High beta-1 selectivity reduces, but does not remove, the risk of bronchospasm in asthma or COPD."], ["Nervous system", "Headache, fatigue and dizziness are the most common complaints."]], "journey": "Nebivolol is taken by mouth once daily. Its bioavailability varies widely because it is metabolized by CYP2D6, which differs between people. It peaks in about one and a half to four hours and is about 98 percent protein bound. The liver makes active hydroxylated metabolites that contribute to its effect. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. The half-life is about 12 hours in extensive metabolizers and about 19 hours in poor metabolizers, with active metabolites lasting longer.", "sidefx": "Bradycardia, heart block and worsening heart failure come from beta-1 blockade of the heart's rate and force. Bronchospasm can still occur because selectivity is not absolute. Beta blockade can hide the rapid heartbeat of low blood sugar. Stopping suddenly can cause rebound angina or heart attack because receptors upregulated during treatment become exposed to catecholamines. In overdose, glucagon raises cAMP independently of beta receptors and is a recognized treatment, along with atropine, fluids and vasopressors.", "src": ["Bystolic (nebivolol) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "nadolol": {"origin": "Nadolol followed the trail opened by James Black's propranolol in the 1960s. Researchers at E. R. Squibb & Sons developed it as a nonselective beta blocker with a very different personality: it is water soluble rather than fat soluble, is not metabolized by the liver and has a long half-life. That meant once-daily dosing and little entry into the brain. The FDA approved nadolol as Corgard in the late 1970s for angina and hypertension. A combination with bendroflumethiazide is sold as Corzide. Beyond its labeled uses, nadolol is known for its off-label role in lowering portal pressure in cirrhosis.", "timeline": [["1960s", "James Black develops propranolol, launching beta blocker therapy."], ["1970s", "Squibb develops nadolol, a long-acting water-soluble beta blocker."], ["1979", "FDA approves nadolol (Corgard) for angina and hypertension."]], "cell": "Nadolol is a competitive antagonist at both beta-1 and beta-2 adrenergic receptors, with no intrinsic sympathomimetic or membrane-stabilizing activity. At beta-1 receptors in the heart, blocking norepinephrine binding lowers Gs-coupled cAMP production, which slows the sinoatrial node, weakens contraction and slows AV conduction, lowering myocardial oxygen demand. At beta-1 receptors in the kidney, it lowers renin release. At beta-2 receptors, it blocks smooth muscle relaxation in airways and splanchnic blood vessels. Splanchnic vasoconstriction lowers blood flow into the portal vein, which reduces portal pressure in cirrhosis. Because it is hydrophilic, little crosses into the brain.", "body": [["Heart", "Lower heart rate and contractility reduce oxygen demand, which prevents angina, but can cause bradycardia and heart block."], ["Blood vessels and kidneys", "Lower renin and cardiac output reduce blood pressure; cold hands and feet can occur from beta-2 blockade."], ["Gut circulation", "Splanchnic vasoconstriction lowers portal pressure, reducing variceal bleeding risk in cirrhosis."], ["Lungs", "Beta-2 blockade narrows airways, so it can trigger bronchospasm in asthma or COPD."], ["Metabolism", "Beta-2 blockade can blunt glycogen breakdown and hide the warning tremor and tachycardia of low blood sugar."]], "journey": "Nadolol is taken by mouth and is incompletely absorbed. It peaks in about three to four hours and is only about 30 percent protein bound. It is not metabolized at all. It leaves the body unchanged through the kidneys, so kidney impairment prolongs its half-life and doses are spaced out. The normal half-life of about 20 to 24 hours allows once-daily dosing. Because it is water soluble, it crosses into the brain poorly.", "sidefx": "Bradycardia, heart block and worsening heart failure come from beta-1 blockade. Bronchospasm comes from beta-2 blockade in airways, which is why nonselective drugs are avoided in asthma. Cold extremities reflect beta-2 vascular effects. Masked hypoglycemia follows blockade of adrenergic warning signs. Abrupt withdrawal can cause rebound angina or heart attack. In overdose, glucagon is a recognized treatment because it raises cAMP through its own receptor, along with atropine and supportive care; nadolol can also be removed by dialysis.", "src": ["Corgard (nadolol) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "timolol": {"origin": "Timolol was developed by Merck in the late 1960s and 1970s as a potent nonselective beta blocker. It made history twice. In 1978 the FDA approved timolol eye drops (Timoptic), the first beta blocker used for glaucoma, and they quickly became a mainstay because they lower eye pressure without shrinking the pupil or blurring vision as older drops did. Then in 1981, the Norwegian Multicenter Study Group reported in the NEJM that oral timolol given after a heart attack lowered deaths and repeat heart attacks, one of the landmark trials that made beta blockers standard care after myocardial infarction. Oral timolol tablets (Blocadren) were approved for hypertension and post-MI use; that brand has been discontinued, but generic tablets and many eye drop products remain.", "timeline": [["1960s", "James Black develops propranolol, launching beta blocker therapy."], ["1978", "FDA approves timolol eye drops (Timoptic), the first beta blocker for glaucoma."], ["1981", "Norwegian Multicenter Study shows oral timolol lowers deaths after heart attack."]], "cell": "Timolol competitively blocks beta-1 and beta-2 adrenergic receptors without meaningful intrinsic sympathomimetic or membrane-stabilizing activity. In the heart, beta-1 blockade lowers Gs-driven cAMP, slowing the sinus rate, reducing contractility and slowing AV conduction, which lowers oxygen demand and the risk of catecholamine-driven arrhythmias after a heart attack. In the kidney it lowers renin release. In the eye, the ciliary body epithelium uses beta-2 receptors and cAMP to produce aqueous humor; blocking them reduces aqueous production and lowers intraocular pressure. Beta-2 blockade in airways and blood vessels explains bronchospasm and cold extremities.", "body": [["Eyes", "Less aqueous humor production lowers intraocular pressure in open-angle glaucoma and ocular hypertension."], ["Heart", "Slower heart rate and weaker contraction lower oxygen demand and reduce reinfarction and death after a heart attack."], ["Blood pressure", "Lower cardiac output and renin release reduce blood pressure."], ["Lungs", "Beta-2 blockade can narrow airways and trigger severe bronchospasm, even from eye drops."], ["Brain", "Fatigue, dizziness and, with eye drops, rare depression or confusion can occur; it is also labeled for migraine prevention."]], "journey": "Oral timolol is well absorbed, though first-pass liver metabolism removes a fair amount. It peaks in one to two hours and is only lightly protein bound. The liver metabolizes most of it through CYP2D6, and about 20 percent leaves unchanged in urine. The half-life is about four hours. Eye drops act locally, but some drug drains through the tear duct into the nose, where it is absorbed directly into the blood without passing through the liver, so drops can cause body-wide beta blockade. Pressing on the inner corner of the eye after a drop reduces this.", "sidefx": "Bradycardia, heart block and heart failure come from beta-1 blockade. Bronchospasm comes from beta-2 blockade and is the reason timolol, including eye drops, is avoided in asthma. Systemic effects from eye drops happen because absorption through the nasal lining skips first-pass metabolism, and CYP2D6 inhibitors can add to this. Masked hypoglycemia and cold extremities reflect beta-2 effects. Abrupt withdrawal of tablets can cause rebound angina or MI. In overdose, glucagon raises cAMP through its own receptor and is a recognized treatment, with atropine and supportive care.", "fun": "Timolol eye drops can slow the heart enough to cause fainting, a reminder that drops reaching the nose behave much like an injection.", "src": ["Timolol maleate tablets FDA label", "Timoptic (timolol ophthalmic) FDA label", "Norwegian Multicenter Study Group. NEJM 1981"]}, "felodipine": {"origin": "Felodipine was developed by the Swedish company Astra, through its research unit Hassle, as a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker highly selective for blood vessels rather than the heart. The FDA approved it as an extended-release tablet, Plendil, in 1991 for hypertension. Felodipine became famous for an accidental discovery. Around 1989, Canadian researcher David Bailey and colleagues used grapefruit juice to mask the taste of alcohol in a study of felodipine and alcohol, and found that felodipine levels were several times higher than expected. That led to the discovery that grapefruit inhibits CYP3A4 in the gut wall, now known to affect many drugs. The Plendil brand is discontinued in the US, but generic extended-release felodipine remains.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Astra in Sweden develops felodipine."], ["1989", "David Bailey's group finds grapefruit juice sharply raises felodipine levels."], ["1991", "FDA approves felodipine extended-release (Plendil) for hypertension."]], "cell": "Vascular smooth muscle contracts when calcium enters through voltage-gated L-type calcium channels (Cav1.2, alpha-1C subunit), binds calmodulin and activates myosin light chain kinase. Felodipine binds the dihydropyridine site on the L-type channel, favoring its inactivated state, which is more common in the relatively depolarized smooth muscle of arterioles. Less calcium enters, myosin light chain phosphorylation falls and arterioles relax. Peripheral resistance and blood pressure fall. Because it is so vascular selective, it has little direct effect on heart contraction or AV node conduction at usual doses. Arteriolar dilation without matching venous dilation raises capillary pressure in the legs, which explains ankle swelling.", "body": [["Arteries", "Arteriolar dilation lowers peripheral resistance and blood pressure."], ["Heart", "A quick drop in pressure can trigger reflex tachycardia, which can worsen angina in coronary disease."], ["Legs and ankles", "Precapillary dilation without venous dilation raises capillary pressure and causes dependent edema that diuretics do not fix well."], ["Head and skin", "Headache and flushing come from dilation of vessels in the head and skin."], ["Gums", "Gingival overgrowth can occur, as with other dihydropyridines."]], "journey": "Felodipine is taken by mouth as an extended-release tablet. It is nearly completely absorbed, but extensive first-pass metabolism by CYP3A4 in the gut wall and liver leaves only about 15 to 20 percent available. The extended-release tablet produces a slow rise to a peak several hours later. It is more than 99 percent protein bound. Inactive metabolites leave mainly in urine. The half-life is about 11 to 16 hours, allowing once-daily dosing. Grapefruit juice and strong CYP3A4 inhibitors can raise levels several fold.", "sidefx": "Edema, headache, flushing and dizziness are direct results of arterial dilation. Reflex tachycardia comes from baroreceptor activation when blood pressure drops. Severe hypotension can occur with CYP3A4 inhibitors or grapefruit, which block first-pass metabolism. Gingival hyperplasia is a class effect. Overdose is treated with supportive care, IV calcium, vasopressors and, in severe cases, high-dose insulin therapy.", "fun": "The grapefruit juice drug interaction was discovered by accident with felodipine, when juice was chosen only to hide the taste of alcohol in a study.", "src": ["Felodipine extended-release FDA label", "Bailey DG et al. Lancet 1991", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "guanfacine": {"origin": "Guanfacine was developed in the 1970s as a centrally acting blood pressure drug related to clonidine but more selective for the alpha-2A adrenergic receptor and longer acting. In the US it was approved as Tenex, marketed by A. H. Robins, in 1986 for hypertension. As newer blood pressure drugs took over, guanfacine found a second life. Research by Amy Arnsten and colleagues at Yale showed that alpha-2A receptors in the prefrontal cortex strengthen working memory and attention. That work supported an extended-release form, Intuniv, approved by the FDA in 2009 for ADHD in children and adolescents, as a nonstimulant option. The Tenex brand is discontinued, and generic immediate-release guanfacine remains.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Guanfacine is developed as a centrally acting antihypertensive."], ["1986", "FDA approves guanfacine (Tenex) for hypertension."], ["1990s", "Yale research links prefrontal alpha-2A receptors to working memory."], ["2009", "FDA approves extended-release guanfacine (Intuniv) for ADHD."]], "cell": "Guanfacine is a selective agonist at alpha-2A adrenergic receptors, which are Gi-coupled. In the brainstem, especially the nucleus tractus solitarius and the vasomotor center, activation lowers cAMP and reduces sympathetic outflow to the heart and blood vessels. Peripheral resistance, heart rate and blood pressure fall. In the prefrontal cortex, alpha-2A receptors sit on dendritic spines of pyramidal neurons. Activating them lowers cAMP and closes HCN channels, which strengthens the network connections that hold information in working memory and improves attention and impulse control. It is more selective for alpha-2A than clonidine, which may explain somewhat less sedation.", "body": [["Brainstem", "Lower sympathetic outflow reduces blood pressure and heart rate."], ["Prefrontal cortex", "Stronger network signaling improves attention, working memory and impulse control in ADHD."], ["Heart", "Slower heart rate and, rarely, symptomatic bradycardia or fainting can occur."], ["Brain arousal", "Drowsiness and fatigue are common, especially at the start, from reduced noradrenergic activity in arousal centers."], ["Salivary glands", "Dry mouth comes from reduced sympathetic and parasympathetic drive to the glands."]], "journey": "Guanfacine is taken by mouth and is well absorbed. Immediate-release tablets peak in one to four hours, while the extended-release form gives a slower, flatter profile and the two are not interchangeable one-for-one. It is about 70 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels and inducers lower them. About half leaves unchanged in urine. The half-life is about 17 hours.", "sidefx": "Sedation, dry mouth and fatigue come from lower central noradrenergic activity. Bradycardia, hypotension and fainting reflect reduced sympathetic tone, made worse by dehydration or overheating. Stopping suddenly can cause rebound high blood pressure because sympathetic outflow returns, although this is less abrupt than with clonidine due to the longer half-life. Overdose causes sedation, bradycardia and hypotension and is managed with supportive care.", "src": ["Intuniv (guanfacine extended-release) FDA label", "Tenex (guanfacine) FDA label", "Arnsten AFT. Biol Psychiatry 2011"]}, "indapamide": {"origin": "Indapamide was developed by the French company Servier in the 1970s. Unlike classic thiazides such as hydrochlorothiazide, it has an indoline ring instead of the benzothiadiazine structure, so it is called a thiazide-like diuretic. Servier promoted its blood pressure lowering, which was thought to come partly from direct vessel effects. It was approved in the US in the 1980s as Lozol. Indapamide gained fame from large trials: PROGRESS (2001) used it with perindopril after stroke, and HYVET (2008) showed that treating hypertension in people 80 and older with indapamide-based therapy lowered stroke, heart failure and death. The Lozol brand is discontinued, and generic indapamide remains.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Servier develops indapamide in France."], ["1980s", "Indapamide is approved in the US as Lozol."], ["2001", "PROGRESS trial uses indapamide with perindopril after stroke."], ["2008", "HYVET trial shows benefit of treating hypertension in people aged 80 and older."]], "cell": "Indapamide inhibits the sodium-chloride cotransporter (NCC, SLC12A3) on the luminal side of distal convoluted tubule cells, which normally reabsorbs a small but important share of filtered sodium. Blocking NCC leaves more sodium, chloride and water in the urine, lowering plasma volume at first. More sodium reaching the collecting duct increases sodium reabsorption there through ENaC, which drives potassium and hydrogen secretion and causes hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis. Lower sodium inside DCT cells increases calcium reabsorption, reducing urinary calcium. Over weeks, blood volume partly returns, and lower peripheral resistance, possibly with some direct vasodilating effect, maintains the blood pressure reduction.", "body": [["Kidneys", "More sodium and water are excreted, while potassium is lost and calcium is retained."], ["Blood vessels", "Peripheral resistance falls over time, keeping blood pressure lower."], ["Electrolytes", "Low potassium, low sodium and low magnesium can develop; severe hyponatremia occurs mostly in older women."], ["Metabolism", "Uric acid can rise, triggering gout, and blood sugar can rise modestly."], ["Brain and stroke risk", "Lower blood pressure reduces stroke risk, shown in trials in older adults and after stroke."]], "journey": "Indapamide is taken by mouth once daily and is rapidly and completely absorbed, peaking in about two hours. It is moderately protein bound and also binds to red blood cells. The liver metabolizes it extensively. About 70 percent leaves in urine, mostly as metabolites, and the rest in feces. The half-life is about 14 to 18 hours, and effects can last up to about a day and a half.", "sidefx": "Hypokalemia follows increased sodium delivery to the collecting duct, which drives potassium secretion, and it raises the risk of arrhythmias, especially with digoxin. Hyponatremia occurs because the diluting segment is impaired. Hyperuricemia comes from competition with uric acid for tubular secretion and from volume contraction. Hyperglycemia is linked to low potassium reducing insulin release. As a sulfonamide, it may cause reactions in people with sulfonamide allergy. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Indapamide FDA label", "Beckett NS et al. NEJM 2008 (HYVET)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "triamterene": {"origin": "Triamterene was developed by Smith Kline & French in the early 1960s. It is a pteridine, chemically related to folic acid, and it was found to cause sodium loss in the urine while holding on to potassium, without blocking aldosterone. The FDA approved it as Dyrenium in the 1960s. Its greatest success came from pairing it with hydrochlorothiazide in Dyazide, which became one of the most prescribed blood pressure products in the US, and later in Maxzide. The pairing makes sense because the thiazide causes potassium loss and triamterene offsets it.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Smith Kline & French develops triamterene, a pteridine diuretic."], ["1960s", "Triamterene (Dyrenium) and the triamterene-hydrochlorothiazide combination Dyazide reach the US market."], ["1980s", "Maxzide, another triamterene-hydrochlorothiazide combination, is introduced."]], "cell": "In the late distal tubule and collecting duct, principal cells reabsorb sodium through epithelial sodium channels (ENaC) on the luminal membrane. Sodium entry makes the lumen negatively charged, which pulls potassium out through ROMK channels and, indirectly, hydrogen ions from intercalated cells. Triamterene directly blocks ENaC from the lumen side. Sodium reabsorption falls a little, causing mild sodium loss, and the lumen becomes less negative, so potassium and hydrogen secretion drop. This works whether or not aldosterone is present, unlike spironolactone. Triamterene also weakly inhibits dihydrofolate reductase, which can matter in people with low folate.", "body": [["Kidneys", "A mild increase in sodium excretion is paired with potassium retention, which balances potassium loss from thiazides."], ["Potassium balance", "Potassium can rise dangerously, especially with kidney disease, ACE inhibitors, ARBs or potassium supplements."], ["Urinary tract", "Triamterene can crystallize in urine and form kidney stones."], ["Blood", "Weak folate antagonism can cause megaloblastic anemia in people with low folate stores, such as those with alcohol use disorder."], ["Skin", "Photosensitivity can occur, making sunburn more likely."]], "journey": "Triamterene is taken by mouth and is fairly well absorbed. It begins working in a few hours and peaks in several hours. The liver hydroxylates it and adds a sulfate group, forming an active metabolite. The drug and metabolite reach the tubule lumen and are excreted in urine. The half-life is short, about two hours, but its effect lasts several hours. Kidney or liver impairment raises levels.", "sidefx": "Hyperkalemia is the main danger and is a boxed warning; it comes directly from ENaC blockade lowering potassium secretion, and it can cause fatal arrhythmias. The risk climbs with kidney impairment, diabetes, older age and other potassium-raising drugs. Kidney stones form from drug crystals. Megaloblastic anemia relates to weak dihydrofolate reductase inhibition. Severe hyperkalemia is treated with IV calcium to protect the heart, insulin with glucose to shift potassium into cells, and measures to remove potassium.", "fun": "Triamterene can give urine a pale blue fluorescence under some lighting, a harmless effect of the drug's pteridine structure.", "src": ["Dyrenium (triamterene) FDA label", "Dyazide FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "finerenone": {"origin": "Spironolactone and eplerenone block the mineralocorticoid receptor, but both are steroids. Spironolactone also blocks androgen and progesterone receptors, causing gynecomastia, and both can raise potassium. Bayer scientists screened large chemical libraries and found a non-steroidal structure, then optimized it into finerenone (BAY 94-8862) with high selectivity for the mineralocorticoid receptor. The FIDELIO-DKD and FIGARO-DKD trials showed that it slowed kidney decline and lowered cardiovascular events in people with chronic kidney disease and type 2 diabetes. The FDA approved it as Kerendia in 2021. In 2025, after the FINEARTS-HF trial, its label expanded to heart failure with left ventricular ejection fraction of 40 percent or higher.", "timeline": [["1959", "Spironolactone, the first mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist, is introduced."], ["2000s", "Bayer discovers finerenone through high-throughput screening."], ["2021", "FDA approves finerenone (Kerendia) for CKD with type 2 diabetes."], ["2025", "FDA approves finerenone for heart failure with ejection fraction of 40 percent or higher."]], "cell": "The mineralocorticoid receptor is a nuclear receptor activated by aldosterone and, in some tissues, cortisol. In the kidney collecting duct it increases ENaC and Na/K-ATPase activity, retaining sodium and losing potassium. In the heart, vessels, kidney glomeruli and immune cells, overactivation drives inflammation, fibrosis and tissue damage. Finerenone binds the receptor as a bulky non-steroidal antagonist. It blocks the receptor's ability to recruit transcriptional coactivators, shutting down pro-inflammatory and pro-fibrotic gene programs. It distributes evenly between heart and kidney, and it has almost no activity at androgen or progesterone receptors. Less inflammation and fibrosis slows loss of kidney function and reduces albuminuria and heart failure events.", "body": [["Kidneys", "Less inflammation and fibrosis reduces albuminuria and slows the decline in eGFR in diabetic kidney disease; eGFR can dip slightly at first."], ["Heart", "Reduced fibrosis and remodeling lower hospitalizations for heart failure."], ["Potassium balance", "Blocking aldosterone in the collecting duct reduces potassium secretion and can cause hyperkalemia, so potassium is checked."], ["Blood pressure", "Blood pressure falls modestly, and hypotension can occur."], ["Hormone balance", "Because it does not block androgen receptors, gynecomastia is much less common than with spironolactone."]], "journey": "Finerenone is taken by mouth once daily and absorbed quickly, peaking within about an hour. First-pass metabolism leaves moderate bioavailability. It is about 92 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it almost completely, mostly through CYP3A4 and partly through CYP2C8, into inactive metabolites, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors are contraindicated and grapefruit is avoided. Metabolites leave mainly in urine. The half-life is only two to three hours, but effects on gene expression last long enough for once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Hyperkalemia is the main risk and comes from blocking aldosterone's effect on potassium secretion; it is more likely with low eGFR, high starting potassium and strong CYP3A4 inhibitors. Hypotension and hyponatremia reflect sodium loss. An early small eGFR drop reflects lower glomerular pressure. Hormonal side effects are rare because of its receptor selectivity. Severe hyperkalemia is treated with IV calcium, insulin with glucose and potassium-lowering measures.", "src": ["Kerendia (finerenone) FDA label", "Bakris GL et al. NEJM 2020 (FIDELIO-DKD)", "Solomon SD et al. NEJM 2024 (FINEARTS-HF)"]}, "sotagliflozin": {"origin": "The story of SGLT inhibitors begins with phlorizin, a bitter compound isolated from apple tree bark by French chemists in 1835. In the 1880s, scientists found that phlorizin made animals pass sugar in the urine. Much later, phlorizin was shown to block both sodium-glucose cotransporters, SGLT1 in the intestine and SGLT2 in the kidney. Most modern drugs, such as empagliflozin, target SGLT2 selectively. Lexicon Pharmaceuticals designed sotagliflozin to block both transporters. The FDA declined to approve it for type 1 diabetes in 2019. The SOLOIST-WHF and SCORED trials then showed fewer cardiovascular deaths and heart failure events, and the FDA approved it as Inpefa in 2023 to reduce cardiovascular death and heart failure events in heart failure and in people with type 2 diabetes, CKD and other cardiovascular risk factors.", "timeline": [["1835", "French chemists isolate phlorizin from apple tree bark."], ["1880s", "Phlorizin is found to cause sugar in the urine."], ["2019", "FDA declines to approve sotagliflozin for type 1 diabetes."], ["2020", "SOLOIST-WHF and SCORED trial results are first presented."], ["2023", "FDA approves sotagliflozin (Inpefa) for heart failure risk reduction."]], "cell": "In the kidney's proximal tubule, SGLT2 reabsorbs most filtered glucose with sodium. Sotagliflozin blocks SGLT2, so glucose and sodium stay in the urine. This causes glycosuria, mild osmotic diuresis and natriuresis. More sodium reaching the macula densa restores tubuloglomerular feedback, constricting the afferent arteriole and lowering glomerular pressure. Unlike selective SGLT2 inhibitors, sotagliflozin also blocks SGLT1 in the small intestine, which absorbs dietary glucose and galactose. Less glucose is absorbed early in the gut, more reaches the lower bowel and stimulates L cells to release GLP-1 and PYY. Lower glucose and insulin levels shift metabolism toward fat burning and ketone production. Reduced preload, afterload and congestion likely explain much of the heart failure benefit.", "body": [["Kidneys", "Glucose and sodium are lost in urine, causing diuresis and lowering glomerular pressure."], ["Heart and circulation", "Less fluid and lower blood pressure reduce strain on the heart and cut heart failure hospitalizations and urgent visits."], ["Intestine", "SGLT1 blockade slows glucose absorption and can cause diarrhea from sugar reaching the lower bowel."], ["Genital and urinary tract", "Sugar in the urine feeds yeast and bacteria, causing genital yeast infections and urinary infections."], ["Metabolism", "Lower insulin and a shift toward fat use can lead to ketoacidosis, sometimes with near-normal blood sugar."]], "journey": "Sotagliflozin is taken by mouth once daily before the first meal of the day. It is absorbed and peaks in about one to three hours, and it is about 98 percent protein bound. It is broken down mainly by glucuronidation through UGT1A9 into an inactive metabolite, with a minor CYP3A4 role. Metabolites leave mainly in urine, with some in feces. The half-life is about 21 to 35 hours. Because some drug acts in the gut lumen before absorption, it can block intestinal SGLT1 directly.", "sidefx": "Genital yeast and urinary infections come from glucose in the urine. Volume depletion and low blood pressure come from osmotic diuresis. Diarrhea comes from SGLT1 blockade leaving sugar in the gut. Euglycemic diabetic ketoacidosis happens because lower glucose and insulin push the body toward ketone production, especially with illness, surgery or reduced insulin. Fournier gangrene is a rare serious infection reported with this class. Hypoglycemia can occur with insulin or sulfonylureas. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The chemical ancestor of every SGLT inhibitor, phlorizin, came from the bark of apple trees and blocks both SGLT1 and SGLT2, just as sotagliflozin does.", "src": ["Inpefa (sotagliflozin) FDA label", "Bhatt DL et al. NEJM 2021 (SOLOIST-WHF)", "Bhatt DL et al. NEJM 2021 (SCORED)"]}, "colesevelam": {"origin": "Bile acid sequestrants have been used since the 1960s, starting with cholestyramine, and the 1984 Lipid Research Clinics trial showed that lowering LDL with cholestyramine reduced heart attacks. But older resins were gritty, caused constipation and bound many other drugs. GelTex Pharmaceuticals in Massachusetts designed colesevelam as a newer polymer with a higher, more specific binding capacity for bile acids that could be made into tablets. The FDA approved it as Welchol in 2000 for lowering LDL cholesterol. Doctors then noticed it lowered blood sugar, and in 2008 it became the first lipid drug also approved to improve blood sugar control in type 2 diabetes.", "timeline": [["1984", "Lipid Research Clinics trial shows cholestyramine lowers heart attacks."], ["1990s", "GelTex Pharmaceuticals designs colesevelam."], ["2000", "FDA approves colesevelam (Welchol) for lowering LDL."], ["2008", "Colesevelam is approved for blood sugar control in type 2 diabetes."]], "cell": "Bile acids are made from cholesterol in the liver, released into the intestine to help absorb fat, and then about 95 percent are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum and returned to the liver. Colesevelam is a large, nonabsorbed polymer with positively charged groups that bind negatively charged bile acids in the intestine. The bound bile acids leave in stool. The liver senses a shortage and turns up cholesterol 7-alpha-hydroxylase (CYP7A1) to make more bile acids from cholesterol. Lower liver cholesterol activates SREBP-2, increasing LDL receptors on liver cells, which pull more LDL out of the blood. The liver also increases cholesterol synthesis and VLDL output, so triglycerides can rise. The blood sugar effect is not fully understood but may involve bile acid signaling through FXR and TGR5 and increased GLP-1 release.", "body": [["Liver", "More LDL receptors pull LDL out of the blood, lowering LDL cholesterol."], ["Intestine", "Bile acids bound in the gut leave in stool, and constipation, indigestion and nausea are common."], ["Blood lipids", "Triglycerides can rise because the liver makes more VLDL, so it is avoided with very high triglycerides."], ["Blood sugar", "A1C falls modestly in type 2 diabetes, and low blood sugar can occur with insulin or sulfonylureas."], ["Vitamin absorption", "Less bile acid can reduce absorption of fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K."]], "journey": "Colesevelam is taken by mouth as tablets or as a powder for suspension, with meals. It is not absorbed into the blood at all. It stays in the intestine, binds bile acids and leaves the body in stool. It is not metabolized and has no half-life in the usual sense. LDL lowering is seen within about two weeks. Because it can bind other drugs, some medicines such as levothyroxine, oral contraceptives and glyburide are taken at least four hours before it.", "sidefx": "Constipation and bloating come from a bulky polymer acting in the gut and from changes in bile acids. Rarely, bowel obstruction can occur in people with gut narrowing. Rising triglycerides reflect increased liver VLDL output, and very high triglycerides can cause pancreatitis. Reduced absorption of fat-soluble vitamins and some drugs comes from binding in the gut. There is no antidote, since the drug is not absorbed.", "src": ["Welchol (colesevelam) FDA label", "Lipid Research Clinics Program. JAMA 1984", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "colestipol": {"origin": "Colestipol was developed by the Upjohn Company as one of the first-generation bile acid sequestrants, alongside cholestyramine. It is an insoluble copolymer made from diethylenetriamine and epichlorohydrin that works as an anion exchange resin. The FDA approved it as Colestid granules in the 1970s, and tablets were added later. In the era before statins, bile acid resins were among the few effective LDL-lowering drugs. They remain useful because they are not absorbed into the blood, which makes them an option in people who cannot use other agents.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Cholestyramine, the first bile acid sequestrant, comes into use."], ["1970s", "FDA approves colestipol (Colestid) granules."], ["1984", "Lipid Research Clinics trial proves LDL lowering with a resin reduces heart attacks."]], "cell": "Colestipol is a large, insoluble polymer with many positively charged amine groups. In the intestine it exchanges chloride ions for negatively charged bile acids and binds them, preventing their reabsorption in the terminal ileum. Bile acids that would normally return to the liver leave in stool. The liver responds by upregulating cholesterol 7-alpha-hydroxylase (CYP7A1), converting more cholesterol into new bile acids. Falling liver cholesterol activates SREBP-2, which increases LDL receptors on liver cells, pulling LDL out of the blood. The liver also increases cholesterol synthesis and VLDL output, so triglycerides can rise. Because it releases chloride, large amounts can contribute to hyperchloremic acidosis.", "body": [["Liver", "More LDL receptors increase LDL clearance, lowering LDL cholesterol."], ["Intestine", "Bound bile acids leave in stool, and constipation, bloating, gas and nausea are common; fecal impaction can occur."], ["Blood lipids", "Triglycerides can rise as the liver makes more VLDL."], ["Vitamins and clotting", "Lower fat-soluble vitamin absorption can lead to vitamin K deficiency and bleeding."], ["Acid-base balance", "Chloride released by the resin can cause hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis, especially in children or kidney disease."]], "journey": "Colestipol is taken by mouth as granules mixed in liquid or as tablets swallowed whole. It is not absorbed into the blood. It stays in the gut, binds bile acids and leaves in stool. It is not metabolized and has no half-life in the usual sense. LDL lowering appears within about a month. Because it binds many other drugs, such as digoxin, warfarin, thyroid hormone and thiazides, other medicines are usually taken at least an hour before or four hours after it.", "sidefx": "Constipation and bloating come from the bulky resin and changes in bile flow. Rising triglycerides come from increased VLDL production. Low fat-soluble vitamins, especially vitamin K, can lead to bleeding, which is treated with vitamin K. Drug binding in the gut can make other medicines less effective. Hyperchloremic acidosis comes from chloride exchange. There is no antidote, as it is not absorbed.", "src": ["Colestid (colestipol) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Lipid Research Clinics Program. JAMA 1984"]}, "pitavastatin": {"origin": "The statin story begins in Japan. In 1973 Akira Endo at Sankyo found mevastatin, a compound from the mold Penicillium citrinum that blocks HMG-CoA reductase, the key enzyme in cholesterol production. That discovery led to lovastatin, approved in the US in 1987, and the statin era. Pitavastatin was also developed in Japan, by Nissan Chemical Industries with Kowa Company, as a fully synthetic statin. It was approved in Japan in 2003, and the FDA approved it as Livalo in 2009. It is notable for being barely metabolized by CYP enzymes. In 2023 the REPRIEVE trial showed that pitavastatin lowered major cardiovascular events in people with HIV.", "timeline": [["1973", "Akira Endo discovers mevastatin from Penicillium citrinum."], ["1987", "Lovastatin becomes the first statin approved in the US."], ["2003", "Pitavastatin is approved in Japan."], ["2009", "FDA approves pitavastatin (Livalo)."], ["2023", "REPRIEVE trial shows pitavastatin lowers cardiovascular events in people with HIV."]], "cell": "HMG-CoA reductase converts HMG-CoA to mevalonate, the rate-limiting step in cholesterol synthesis. Pitavastatin competitively binds the enzyme's active site, mimicking HMG-CoA. Liver cholesterol production falls. Low cholesterol in the endoplasmic reticulum activates SREBP-2, which moves to the nucleus and increases transcription of the LDL receptor gene. More LDL receptors on liver cells pull LDL out of the blood. Lower VLDL production modestly reduces triglycerides, and HDL rises slightly. Blocking mevalonate also lowers isoprenoids used to modify signaling proteins, which may contribute to anti-inflammatory effects on blood vessels.", "body": [["Liver", "Lower cholesterol synthesis and more LDL receptors lower LDL cholesterol."], ["Blood vessels", "Lower LDL and possible anti-inflammatory effects slow plaque buildup and reduce heart attack and stroke risk."], ["Muscles", "Muscle aches can occur, and rarely myopathy or rhabdomyolysis, possibly from reduced coenzyme Q10 and other mevalonate products in muscle cells."], ["Liver tests", "Liver enzymes can rise, usually without serious liver injury."], ["Blood sugar", "A small increase in blood sugar and new diabetes risk is seen with statins as a class."]], "journey": "Pitavastatin is taken by mouth once daily, at any time of day. It is well absorbed and peaks in about an hour. It is taken up into liver cells by the OATP1B1 transporter, so cyclosporine, which blocks OATP1B1, raises levels sharply and is contraindicated. It is more than 99 percent protein bound. It is mainly glucuronidated through UGT1A3 and UGT2B7, with only minimal CYP2C9 and CYP2C8 metabolism, so it has fewer CYP3A4 drug interactions than many statins. It leaves mainly in bile and feces. The half-life is about 12 hours.", "sidefx": "Muscle symptoms and rare rhabdomyolysis are thought to come from depleting mevalonate-derived products needed by muscle cells, and the risk increases with high drug levels from OATP1B1 inhibitors, gemfibrozil, kidney disease or older age. Liver enzyme rises reflect effects on liver cells. The modest rise in diabetes risk is a class effect. Statins are not used in pregnancy because cholesterol is needed for fetal development. There is no antidote; rhabdomyolysis is treated by stopping the drug and giving IV fluids.", "src": ["Livalo (pitavastatin) FDA label", "Endo A. Proc Jpn Acad Ser B 2010 (statin history)", "Grinspoon SK et al. NEJM 2023 (REPRIEVE)"]}, "pentoxifylline": {"origin": "Pentoxifylline is a cousin of caffeine. It belongs to the methylxanthine family, the same chemical group as caffeine in coffee, theophylline in tea and theobromine in cocoa. Chemists at the German company Hoechst modified the xanthine ring in the 1960s and 1970s, looking for a compound that acted on blood flow rather than on the brain or airways. The drug they found did not open arteries very much. Instead it changed the blood itself, making red cells more flexible and the blood less thick. It was used in Europe first and was approved by the FDA as Trental in 1984, becoming the first drug in the US approved specifically for intermittent claudication, the cramping leg pain of peripheral artery disease. The Trental brand is now discontinued, and generic extended-release tablets remain.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Hoechst develops pentoxifylline from the methylxanthine family in Germany."], ["1984", "FDA approves Trental for intermittent claudication."], ["1999", "Cilostazol is approved, giving claudication patients a second drug option."]], "cell": "Pentoxifylline is a nonselective phosphodiesterase inhibitor. Phosphodiesterases break down cyclic AMP. When they are blocked, cAMP builds up inside red blood cells, platelets and white blood cells. In red cells, higher cAMP and better ATP handling make the membrane more deformable, so the cells can bend and squeeze through tiny capillaries. The drug also lowers plasma fibrinogen and reduces red cell clumping, which lowers whole blood viscosity. In platelets, higher cAMP reduces aggregation. In white cells, it reduces production of tumor necrosis factor alpha and makes the cells less sticky. Like other methylxanthines it also weakly blocks adenosine receptors. The sum is better flow through the microcirculation and more oxygen delivered to working leg muscle.", "body": [["Blood", "Thinner, less viscous blood with more flexible red cells moves more easily through narrowed vessels."], ["Leg muscles", "Better microcirculatory flow brings more oxygen to calf muscles, so people may walk farther before pain starts. The benefit builds over weeks."], ["Platelets", "A mild antiplatelet effect adds to bleeding risk, especially with warfarin or antiplatelet drugs."], ["Heart", "In people with coronary disease, angina, palpitations and arrhythmias have been reported, likely from methylxanthine stimulation."], ["Gut and brain", "Nausea, indigestion, dizziness and headache are the most common effects, which is why the tablet is taken with meals."]], "journey": "Pentoxifylline is swallowed as an extended-release tablet that slowly releases drug in the gut. It is absorbed well but undergoes heavy first-pass metabolism in the liver and in red blood cells, which turn it into several active metabolites, including lisofylline. The parent drug has a very short half-life of under an hour, and the metabolites last only slightly longer, which is why the slow-release tablet is taken several times a day. Almost all of it leaves in the urine as metabolites, so kidney disease raises levels.", "sidefx": "Gut upset and headache come from methylxanthine effects on the stomach and the brain, similar to too much caffeine. Bleeding risk comes from the antiplatelet action and from lower fibrinogen, so it adds to warfarin and other blood thinners. Chest pain and rhythm problems in heart patients reflect cAMP-driven stimulation of heart tissue. People who do not tolerate caffeine or theophylline may not tolerate this drug. There is no specific antidote; overdose care is supportive.", "fun": "Researchers have studied pentoxifylline in severe alcoholic hepatitis because it lowers TNF-alpha, though later trials did not show a clear survival benefit.", "src": ["Pentoxifylline extended-release tablets FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "dalteparin": {"origin": "Dalteparin starts with heparin, which was discovered in 1916 at Johns Hopkins when medical student Jay McLean, working in William Howell's lab, found an anticoagulant in liver extracts. Heparin is harvested from pig intestinal lining. By the 1970s and 1980s researchers learned that heparin is a mix of chains of many lengths and that shorter chains still inhibited factor Xa but had less effect on thrombin. Swedish scientists at Kabi (later part of Pharmacia) broke porcine heparin into shorter pieces using nitrous acid. The result was dalteparin, a low molecular weight heparin with a more predictable effect that could be injected under the skin without routine lab monitoring. The FDA approved it as Fragmin in 1994, and in 2007 it became the first drug approved in the US for extended treatment of blood clots in people with cancer.", "timeline": [["1916", "Jay McLean finds an anticoagulant in liver extracts at Johns Hopkins."], ["1930s", "Purified heparin enters clinical use."], ["1980s", "Low molecular weight heparins are developed in Europe."], ["1994", "FDA approves Fragmin (dalteparin)."], ["2007", "Dalteparin is approved for extended treatment of cancer-associated clots."]], "cell": "Dalteparin works through antithrombin, a natural protein in blood that switches off clotting enzymes. A special five-sugar sequence (the pentasaccharide) on dalteparin chains binds antithrombin and changes its shape, speeding its action on factor Xa by a huge amount. To inhibit thrombin (factor IIa), a heparin chain must be long enough to wrap around both antithrombin and thrombin at the same time. Most dalteparin chains are too short to do that, so it inhibits factor Xa more than thrombin. With less factor Xa, the prothrombinase complex makes less thrombin, so less fibrin forms and existing clots stop growing while the body's own clot-dissolving system breaks them down.", "body": [["Veins", "Fewer new clots form in the deep veins of the legs and pelvis, and existing clots stop growing."], ["Lungs", "Preventing clot growth in the legs lowers the chance of a pulmonary embolism."], ["Heart arteries", "In unstable angina, reducing thrombin generation helps limit clot on a ruptured plaque."], ["Platelets", "Heparin can trigger antibodies against platelet factor 4 complexes, causing heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, though less often than unfractionated heparin."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys clear dalteparin, so poor kidney function lets it build up and raises bleeding risk."], ["Spine", "Bleeding around the spinal cord after epidural or spinal procedures can compress the cord, which is the boxed warning."]], "journey": "Dalteparin is injected under the skin and is absorbed almost completely, reaching peak anti-Xa activity in about four hours. Because its chains are shorter, it binds less to plasma proteins and cells than regular heparin, which makes its effect predictable without aPTT checks. It is partly broken down in the liver and the reticuloendothelial system, and the rest is cleared by the kidneys. Its half-life is a few hours and becomes longer in kidney disease, where anti-Xa levels may be measured.", "sidefx": "Bleeding is the expected result of turning down the clotting cascade. Spinal or epidural hematoma happens when bleeding occurs in a closed space around the spinal cord. Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia comes from antibodies against heparin and platelet factor 4 that activate platelets, causing low platelets and, paradoxically, new clots. High potassium comes from heparin suppressing aldosterone production. Protamine sulfate partly reverses dalteparin: it fully neutralizes the anti-thrombin activity but only part of the anti-Xa activity.", "fun": "Heparin got its name from the Greek word for liver, hepar, because it was first found in liver tissue.", "src": ["Fragmin (dalteparin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "isosorbide-dinitrate-hydralazine": {"origin": "This tablet combines two old drugs. Organic nitrates trace back to nitroglycerin, used for angina since the 1870s, and isosorbide dinitrate followed in the 20th century. Hydralazine was developed at Ciba in the late 1940s and early 1950s as one of the first oral blood pressure drugs. In the 1980s cardiologist Jay Cohn led the V-HeFT trials, which showed that the two drugs together helped people with heart failure live longer. Later analysis suggested the benefit was strongest in Black patients. The A-HeFT trial, published in 2004, enrolled only self-identified Black patients with advanced heart failure and stopped early because death rates fell so clearly with the combination. In 2005 the FDA approved BiDil, the first drug approved for a specific racial group, which started a large debate about race in medicine.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Hydralazine enters use as an oral antihypertensive."], ["1986", "The V-HeFT I trial reports a survival benefit with hydralazine plus isosorbide dinitrate in heart failure."], ["2004", "The A-HeFT trial in Black patients stops early due to lower mortality."], ["2005", "FDA approves BiDil as a fixed-dose combination."]], "cell": "Isosorbide dinitrate is a nitric oxide donor. Inside vascular smooth muscle it is converted to nitric oxide, which activates soluble guanylate cyclase. This enzyme raises cyclic GMP, which activates protein kinase G. PKG lowers intracellular calcium and activates myosin light chain phosphatase, so the muscle relaxes. Veins are most sensitive, so venous pooling lowers preload. Hydralazine acts directly on arteriolar smooth muscle. Its exact mechanism is not fully known, but it appears to block IP3-triggered calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, relaxing arterioles and lowering afterload. Hydralazine also has antioxidant effects that may reduce nitrate tolerance, which helps the combination keep working. Together they unload the failing heart from both sides and may improve nitric oxide availability.", "body": [["Veins", "Nitrate-driven venous dilation holds more blood in the veins, which lowers filling pressure and eases lung congestion."], ["Arterioles", "Hydralazine relaxes resistance arteries, so the weak heart pumps against less pressure and cardiac output rises."], ["Heart", "Lower preload and afterload reduce wall stress and oxygen demand. Reflex tachycardia from hydralazine can trigger angina in some people."], ["Brain", "Headache is common early because nitric oxide dilates meningeal vessels; it often fades with continued use."], ["Immune system", "Hydralazine can cause a drug-induced lupus-like syndrome, more often in slow acetylators."], ["Nerves", "Hydralazine binds pyridoxine (vitamin B6), which can cause peripheral neuritis."]], "journey": "Both drugs are absorbed from the gut. Isosorbide dinitrate goes through heavy first-pass metabolism in the liver, where it is denitrated to active 2- and 5-mononitrate metabolites that last longer than the parent drug. Hydralazine is acetylated in the liver by N-acetyltransferase; slow acetylators keep higher levels and have more lupus risk. Hydralazine binds strongly to plasma proteins. Effects begin within about an hour, and the metabolites leave mainly in the urine. The tablet is taken several times a day because the effect lasts only several hours.", "sidefx": "Headache, dizziness and low blood pressure come directly from widened blood vessels. Fainting can happen on standing. Severe, dangerous hypotension occurs with PDE5 inhibitors such as sildenafil because both drug types raise cGMP. Reflex rise in heart rate from arteriolar dilation can cause angina. Lupus-like symptoms reflect hydralazine's immune effects, and numbness or tingling reflects its binding of vitamin B6. No antidote exists; low blood pressure is treated with fluids and supportive care.", "fun": "BiDil's approval was the first time the FDA approved a drug for a single racial group, and it is still widely discussed in debates about race-based medicine.", "src": ["BiDil (isosorbide dinitrate and hydralazine) FDA label", "Taylor AL et al. A-HeFT, N Engl J Med 2004", "Cohn JN et al. V-HeFT, N Engl J Med 1986"]}, "droxidopa": {"origin": "Droxidopa was born from research on how the body makes norepinephrine. Its chemical name is L-threo-dihydroxyphenylserine, often shortened to L-DOPS. It was synthesized in the mid 20th century, and researchers in Japan later developed it as a medicine for people whose nerves could not make enough norepinephrine. It was approved in Japan in 1989 by Sumitomo Pharmaceuticals for conditions including familial amyloid polyneuropathy and Parkinson disease related freezing. In the US, Chelsea Therapeutics developed it for neurogenic orthostatic hypotension, the severe drop in blood pressure on standing seen in Parkinson disease, multiple system atrophy and pure autonomic failure. The FDA granted accelerated approval to Northera in 2014. It was notable as one of the few drugs that directly replace a missing neurotransmitter by giving its immediate precursor.", "timeline": [["1989", "Droxidopa is approved in Japan."], ["2014", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Northera for neurogenic orthostatic hypotension."]], "cell": "Droxidopa is a prodrug. It is an amino acid that looks like norepinephrine with an extra carboxyl group. The enzyme aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (dopa decarboxylase), found in nerve endings and many other tissues, removes that carboxyl group and turns droxidopa directly into norepinephrine. This skips dopamine beta-hydroxylase, the step that is missing in some autonomic disorders. The newly made norepinephrine activates alpha-1 adrenergic receptors on blood vessels, which are Gq coupled. Gq activates phospholipase C, IP3 and calcium release, so the smooth muscle contracts. Arteries and veins tighten, raising blood pressure when the person stands. Norepinephrine also stimulates beta-1 receptors in the heart. Drugs that block dopa decarboxylase, such as carbidopa, can reduce the conversion.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Norepinephrine tightens arterioles and veins, which raises blood pressure and keeps blood from pooling in the legs when standing."], ["Brain", "Better standing blood pressure keeps blood flowing to the brain, reducing dizziness, lightheadedness and fainting."], ["Heart", "Beta-1 stimulation can raise heart rate and force; it can worsen ischemic heart disease, arrhythmias and heart failure."], ["Blood pressure lying down", "The same vasoconstriction persists when lying flat, causing supine hypertension, which raises stroke risk. This is why the head of the bed is raised and the last dose is taken well before bedtime."], ["Temperature control", "Rarely, a syndrome with high fever and confusion similar to neuroleptic malignant syndrome has been reported, especially around dose changes or with Parkinson medicines."]], "journey": "Droxidopa is taken by mouth as a capsule and absorbed from the gut, reaching peak levels in about one to four hours. A high-fat meal slows absorption, so it is taken the same way each time. It is converted to norepinephrine by dopa decarboxylase throughout the body. Norepinephrine and droxidopa are broken down further by COMT and other enzymes. The half-life is about two and a half hours, so it is taken several times through the day, and the metabolites leave in the urine.", "sidefx": "Supine hypertension is the main danger and comes straight from norepinephrine constricting vessels even when the person lies down. Headache and a pounding feeling reflect higher blood pressure. Rhythm problems and chest pain come from beta-1 stimulation of the heart. The NMS-like hyperpyrexia syndrome is thought to relate to shifts in dopamine and norepinephrine signaling. There is no specific antidote; very high blood pressure is managed by sitting up, stopping the drug and supportive care.", "fun": "Droxidopa's name comes from its chemistry: it is like DOPA with an extra hydroxyl group on its side chain, which lets one enzyme step turn it into norepinephrine instead of dopamine.", "src": ["Northera (droxidopa) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "repaglinide": {"origin": "Repaglinide belongs to the meglitinide family. The name comes from meglitinide, the benzamide part of the glyburide molecule that does not contain the sulfonylurea group. Researchers in Germany noticed that this fragment alone could still trigger insulin release, and they built new benzoic acid derivatives around it. Repaglinide came out of that work and was developed by Novo Nordisk. The FDA approved it as Prandin in 1997, making it the first meglitinide in the US. Its selling point was a fast, short burst of insulin taken with each meal, so the dose could follow the meal rather than covering the whole day. The Prandin brand is now discontinued, and generics remain.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Sulfonylureas show that drugs can trigger insulin release from the pancreas."], ["1997", "FDA approves Prandin (repaglinide), the first meglitinide in the US."]], "cell": "Pancreatic beta cells sense glucose through metabolism. When glucose rises, ATP rises and closes ATP-sensitive potassium (K-ATP) channels, built from Kir6.2 pore units and SUR1 regulatory units. Repaglinide binds SUR1 at a site different from the sulfonylurea site and closes these channels directly. With less potassium leaving, the cell membrane depolarizes. Voltage-gated L-type calcium channels open, calcium rushes in, and insulin granules fuse with the cell membrane and release insulin. Repaglinide binds and leaves its site quickly, so the insulin burst is fast and short, timed to cover the rise in glucose after a meal. Because it works on the channel itself, it still releases insulin even when glucose is not high.", "body": [["Pancreas", "Beta cells release a quick pulse of insulin within about 30 minutes of a dose."], ["Liver", "The insulin pulse reduces liver glucose output after meals."], ["Muscle and fat", "Insulin moves glucose into these tissues through GLUT4, lowering after-meal blood sugar."], ["Body weight", "More insulin promotes fat storage, so modest weight gain can occur."], ["Brain", "If a meal is skipped after a dose, low blood sugar can cause shakiness, sweating, confusion and in severe cases seizures."]], "journey": "Repaglinide is absorbed quickly from the gut and peaks in about an hour. It is highly bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down, mainly through CYP2C8 and CYP3A4, into inactive metabolites. It is also taken into the liver by the OATP1B1 transporter. About 90 percent leaves in the bile and feces, with little in the urine. Its half-life is about one hour, which matches its role as a mealtime drug. Strong CYP2C8 inhibitors such as gemfibrozil can raise its levels many times over.", "sidefx": "Low blood sugar is the main risk and comes directly from releasing insulin regardless of glucose level. It is more likely with skipped meals, alcohol, exercise or drugs that block CYP2C8 or OATP1B1. Weight gain is a result of more insulin. Treatment of low blood sugar is glucose by mouth if awake or IV dextrose or glucagon if not.", "fun": "Because repaglinide leaves mostly in bile, it was one of the few insulin secretagogues commonly considered in people with reduced kidney function.", "src": ["Repaglinide FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "nateglinide": {"origin": "Nateglinide came from an unusual source: Ajinomoto, the Japanese company famous for making monosodium glutamate and amino acids. Its scientists explored amino acid derivatives for drug activity and found that a derivative of D-phenylalanine could trigger very fast, short insulin release. Novartis developed it for world markets, and the FDA approved it as Starlix in 2000. It became the second meglitinide-type drug in the US, after repaglinide. It is chemically different from repaglinide but shares its quick on and off action. The Starlix brand is now discontinued, and generics remain.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Ajinomoto researchers study amino acid derivatives and identify nateglinide."], ["2000", "FDA approves Starlix (nateglinide)."]], "cell": "Nateglinide binds SUR1, the regulatory subunit of ATP-sensitive potassium channels on pancreatic beta cells. Binding closes the channel, so potassium can no longer leak out. The membrane depolarizes, voltage-gated calcium channels open, and the rise in calcium inside the cell triggers insulin granule release. Nateglinide binds and unbinds SUR1 very quickly, even faster than repaglinide, giving a sharp early-phase insulin spike. Its effect is greatest when glucose is already rising, which makes it somewhat glucose dependent and gives it a lower rate of low blood sugar than sulfonylureas.", "body": [["Pancreas", "Beta cells release a rapid early burst of insulin, restoring some of the first-phase insulin response that is lost early in type 2 diabetes."], ["Blood sugar after meals", "The insulin spike blunts the glucose rise after eating, with less effect on fasting glucose."], ["Liver", "Insulin briefly suppresses liver glucose release after meals."], ["Brain", "Low blood sugar symptoms such as sweating, tremor and confusion can occur if a dose is taken without a meal."]], "journey": "Nateglinide is absorbed quickly after swallowing and peaks within about an hour. It is tightly bound to plasma proteins, mainly albumin. The liver breaks it down, mostly by CYP2C9 with some CYP3A4. The metabolites leave mainly in the urine. The half-life is about one and a half hours, which is why it is taken just before each meal and skipped when a meal is skipped.", "sidefx": "Low blood sugar is the main concern because it forces insulin release by closing beta cell potassium channels. Risk is lower than with sulfonylureas due to its short action but rises with skipped meals, alcohol, hard exercise and liver or kidney disease. Mild weight gain can occur from added insulin. Low blood sugar is treated with oral glucose, IV dextrose or glucagon.", "fun": "Nateglinide is one of the rare medicines whose discoverer was a food seasoning company.", "src": ["Nateglinide FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "glyburide-metformin": {"origin": "This pill joins two very different stories. Metformin traces back to Galega officinalis, a plant called goat's rue or French lilac, used in medieval Europe for symptoms now linked to diabetes. The plant contains guanidine compounds, and in 1957 French physician Jean Sterne reported metformin as a diabetes treatment, calling it Glucophage, meaning glucose eater. It reached the US in 1995. Glyburide came from the sulfonylurea line, which began when researchers noticed in the 1940s that some sulfa antibiotics caused low blood sugar. Glyburide (glibenclamide) was developed in Germany by Boehringer Mannheim and Hoechst and introduced in the late 1960s, reaching the US in 1984. Bristol-Myers Squibb combined them as Glucovance, approved by the FDA in 2000. That brand is discontinued, and generics remain.", "timeline": [["1957", "Jean Sterne reports metformin for diabetes in France."], ["1969", "Glyburide is introduced in Europe."], ["1984", "Glyburide is approved in the US."], ["1995", "Metformin becomes available in the US."], ["2000", "FDA approves Glucovance (glyburide and metformin)."]], "cell": "Glyburide binds the SUR1 subunit of ATP-sensitive potassium channels on pancreatic beta cells. Closing these channels depolarizes the cell, opens voltage-gated calcium channels and triggers insulin release. Glyburide binds tightly and has active metabolites, so its effect lasts many hours. Metformin works mainly in the liver. It is carried into liver cells by the OCT1 transporter and acts on mitochondria, inhibiting complex I and mitochondrial glycerophosphate dehydrogenase. This shifts the cell's energy state, raising AMP and activating AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK). The result is less gluconeogenesis, so the liver releases less glucose. Metformin also improves insulin sensitivity in muscle and increases glucose use in the gut. The two drugs together attack high glucose from the supply side (liver) and the insulin side (pancreas).", "body": [["Pancreas", "Glyburide makes beta cells release more insulin throughout the day."], ["Liver", "Metformin lowers the liver's overnight and fasting glucose output."], ["Muscle", "Insulin from glyburide and better sensitivity from metformin move more glucose into muscle."], ["Gut", "Metformin often causes diarrhea, nausea and abdominal discomfort, partly because high drug levels in the intestine change glucose handling and bile acids. Long-term use can lower vitamin B12 absorption."], ["Blood", "Glyburide can cause hemolytic anemia in G6PD deficiency."], ["Brain", "Glyburide-related low blood sugar can be prolonged and cause confusion, seizures or coma."]], "journey": "Both parts are absorbed from the gut. Glyburide is nearly all bound to plasma proteins and is broken down in the liver by CYP2C9 and CYP3A4 to weakly active metabolites that leave by both kidney and bile. Its glucose effect lasts up to a day. Metformin is not bound to proteins and is not metabolized at all. It is cleared unchanged by the kidneys through active tubular secretion, with a half-life of several hours. Because metformin depends on the kidneys, kidney function is checked before and during therapy.", "sidefx": "Glyburide's tight, long-lasting channel closure explains its risk of prolonged low blood sugar, especially in older adults and in kidney disease where metabolites build up. Metformin's mitochondrial effect can push cells toward anaerobic metabolism, and if the drug accumulates (kidney failure, low oxygen, contrast dye, heavy alcohol) lactate can build up, causing rare but serious lactic acidosis, the boxed warning. Lactic acidosis is treated supportively and hemodialysis removes metformin. Low blood sugar is treated with glucose or glucagon, and octreotide can be used for sulfonylurea-induced recurrent hypoglycemia.", "fun": "Metformin's plant ancestor, goat's rue, was once a pest weed in the US because it poisoned grazing sheep.", "src": ["Glucovance (glyburide and metformin) FDA label", "Bailey CJ. Metformin: historical overview, Diabetologia 2017", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "saxagliptin": {"origin": "Saxagliptin grew out of incretin research. In the 20th century scientists learned that glucose taken by mouth triggers more insulin than the same glucose given by vein. Gut hormones called incretins, GLP-1 and GIP, explain this. In the 1990s researchers found that the enzyme dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4) destroys these hormones within minutes. Blocking DPP-4 became a drug target, and sitagliptin was the first DPP-4 inhibitor approved in the US in 2006. Saxagliptin was designed by chemists at Bristol-Myers Squibb and developed with AstraZeneca. The FDA approved it as Onglyza in 2009. The SAVOR-TIMI 53 trial in 2013 found no increase in heart attacks or strokes but did find more heart failure hospitalizations, which is now in its label.", "timeline": [["1990s", "DPP-4 is shown to rapidly inactivate GLP-1."], ["2006", "Sitagliptin becomes the first DPP-4 inhibitor approved in the US."], ["2009", "FDA approves Onglyza (saxagliptin)."], ["2013", "SAVOR-TIMI 53 reports more heart failure hospitalizations with saxagliptin."]], "cell": "DPP-4 is a serine protease on cell surfaces and in plasma. It clips two amino acids off the front of GLP-1 and GIP, inactivating them. Saxagliptin binds the active site of DPP-4 and forms a slowly reversible covalent bond with the catalytic serine, blocking the enzyme. Active GLP-1 and GIP levels rise after meals. These hormones bind G protein coupled receptors on beta cells that raise cAMP and amplify glucose-triggered insulin release. GLP-1 also lowers glucagon release from alpha cells. Because incretins only boost insulin when glucose is elevated, the risk of low blood sugar is low when the drug is used alone. Its main metabolite, 5-hydroxy saxagliptin, also inhibits DPP-4.", "body": [["Pancreas", "More insulin is released after meals and less glucagon is released, lowering both after-meal and fasting glucose."], ["Liver", "Lower glucagon means the liver releases less glucose."], ["Heart", "Heart failure hospitalizations were higher in the SAVOR trial; the reason is not known."], ["Immune system", "DPP-4 is also called CD26 on immune cells; infections such as colds and urinary tract infections are slightly more common, and rare skin blistering (bullous pemphigoid) has been reported."], ["Pancreas (exocrine)", "Rare cases of acute pancreatitis have been reported with DPP-4 inhibitors."], ["Joints", "Severe joint pain has been reported and usually resolves after stopping."]], "journey": "Saxagliptin is absorbed well by mouth and peaks within about two hours. It is barely bound to proteins. The liver converts it through CYP3A4/5 into 5-hydroxy saxagliptin, an active metabolite about half as potent. Both are cleared through the kidneys and liver. The half-lives are a few hours, but DPP-4 inhibition lasts about 24 hours. The dose is lowered with reduced kidney function or with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors.", "sidefx": "Because the drug only enhances glucose-dependent insulin release, low blood sugar is uncommon unless it is combined with a sulfonylurea or insulin. Pancreatitis, joint pain and bullous pemphigoid are class effects that may relate to DPP-4's roles outside of incretins, such as in immune cell signaling and other peptides. Angioedema can occur, perhaps because DPP-4 also breaks down substance P and bradykinin-like peptides. Heart failure risk was seen in trials without a clear mechanism. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The incretin effect explains why a sugary drink causes more insulin release than the same amount of sugar given directly into a vein.", "src": ["Onglyza (saxagliptin) FDA label", "Scirica BM et al. SAVOR-TIMI 53, N Engl J Med 2013", "Drucker DJ, Nauck MA. Incretin system, Lancet 2006"]}, "linagliptin": {"origin": "Linagliptin was developed by Boehringer Ingelheim and is chemically different from the other gliptins. Its core is a xanthine ring, the same basic structure found in caffeine, which the company's chemists modified until it bound DPP-4 extremely tightly. The FDA approved it as Tradjenta in 2011, with Eli Lilly as a partner. What set linagliptin apart was its route out of the body. Unlike most other gliptins, it is not cleared mainly by the kidneys, so no dose change is needed for kidney disease. The CARMELINA trial, reported in 2018, found no increase in cardiovascular or kidney events in people with type 2 diabetes at high risk.", "timeline": [["2006", "The first DPP-4 inhibitor, sitagliptin, reaches the US market."], ["2011", "FDA approves Tradjenta (linagliptin)."], ["2018", "CARMELINA shows cardiovascular and kidney safety of linagliptin."]], "cell": "Linagliptin competitively and reversibly binds dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (CD26), the serine protease that cuts and inactivates the incretin hormones GLP-1 and GIP. It binds with very high affinity and comes off slowly. With DPP-4 blocked, active GLP-1 and GIP levels after meals roughly double. Each hormone binds its Gs coupled receptor on beta cells, raising cAMP and activating protein kinase A and Epac2. This makes insulin granules more ready to release when glucose rises. GLP-1 also signals alpha cells to make less glucagon. The effect depends on glucose, so it does not cause low blood sugar alone.", "body": [["Pancreas", "Beta cells release more insulin after meals, while alpha cells release less glucagon, together lowering HbA1c modestly."], ["Liver", "Less glucagon reduces glucose production by the liver."], ["Kidneys", "Because so little is cleared by the kidneys, the same dose is used at any level of kidney function."], ["Weight", "It is weight neutral, unlike sulfonylureas and insulin."], ["Skin and joints", "Rare bullous pemphigoid and severe joint pain are class effects."], ["Pancreas (exocrine)", "Acute pancreatitis has been reported and stops treatment."]], "journey": "Linagliptin is swallowed and absorbed with a peak in about one and a half hours. Its oral bioavailability is only about 30 percent. In blood it binds tightly to DPP-4 itself, both in plasma and in tissues, which is why protein binding depends on concentration. This tight, saturable binding gives it a very long terminal half-life of over 100 hours, though the effective half-life for steady state is about 12 hours. It is barely metabolized. Most leaves unchanged in bile and feces, and only about 5 percent leaves in urine.", "sidefx": "Low blood sugar is rare alone but becomes more likely with insulin or sulfonylureas, which drive insulin release regardless of glucose. Pancreatitis, hypersensitivity reactions such as angioedema, severe joint pain and bullous pemphigoid are uncommon class effects likely related to DPP-4's activity on other peptides and on immune cells. Nasopharyngitis and cough are common mild effects. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Linagliptin shares a xanthine backbone with caffeine but has none of caffeine's stimulant effects.", "src": ["Tradjenta (linagliptin) FDA label", "Rosenstock J et al. CARMELINA, JAMA 2019"]}, "alogliptin": {"origin": "Alogliptin was discovered by Syrrx, a San Diego company that used X-ray crystal structures of the DPP-4 enzyme to design inhibitors that fit its active site. The Japanese company Takeda bought Syrrx in 2005 and developed the drug. Alogliptin was approved in Japan in 2010. In the US, the FDA asked for more heart safety data, and the drug was approved as Nesina in 2013, along with combination tablets with metformin (Kazano) and pioglitazone (Oseni). The EXAMINE trial, also in 2013, studied alogliptin in people who had just had a heart attack and found no increase in major cardiovascular events, though the label now notes heart failure risk.", "timeline": [["2005", "Takeda acquires Syrrx, the company that discovered alogliptin."], ["2010", "Alogliptin is approved in Japan."], ["2013", "FDA approves Nesina (alogliptin) and its combinations."], ["2013", "The EXAMINE trial reports cardiovascular outcomes after recent heart attack."]], "cell": "Alogliptin is a highly selective, competitive inhibitor of DPP-4. It fits the enzyme's active site and blocks it from cleaving the first two amino acids from GLP-1 and GIP. It has very little effect on related enzymes such as DPP-8 and DPP-9. With incretins protected, their levels after meals stay higher. GLP-1 and GIP bind their receptors on beta cells, which raise cAMP and enhance insulin release only when glucose is elevated. GLP-1 also reduces glucagon release from alpha cells. The net effect is lower fasting and after-meal glucose with low risk of hypoglycemia.", "body": [["Pancreas", "Increased glucose-dependent insulin release and reduced glucagon release lower blood sugar."], ["Liver", "Less glucagon lowers liver glucose output. Rare cases of serious liver injury have been reported, so liver tests are checked before starting."], ["Heart", "Heart failure hospitalization was numerically higher in EXAMINE, so the label carries a heart failure warning."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys clear most of the drug unchanged, so the dose depends on kidney function."], ["Skin and joints", "Rare bullous pemphigoid and severe joint pain are class effects."]], "journey": "Alogliptin is absorbed well, with oral bioavailability close to 100 percent and a peak in one to two hours. Only about 20 percent binds plasma proteins. It undergoes very little metabolism, with minor CYP2D6 and CYP3A4 involvement. About 60 to 70 percent leaves unchanged in the urine. The half-life is about 21 hours, which supports once-daily dosing and means kidney disease raises its levels.", "sidefx": "Alone it rarely causes low blood sugar because incretins only act when glucose is high, but combining it with insulin or sulfonylureas raises the risk. Pancreatitis, angioedema, joint pain and bullous pemphigoid are uncommon class effects. Liver injury reports led to baseline liver testing. Heart failure risk was seen in a trial without a clear mechanism. No antidote exists.", "fun": "Alogliptin was one of the early drugs designed with the help of high-throughput crystallography, where many crystal structures of a target enzyme guide chemists.", "src": ["Nesina (alogliptin) FDA label", "White WB et al. EXAMINE, N Engl J Med 2013"]}, "canagliflozin": {"origin": "Canagliflozin descends from phlorizin, a bitter compound found in the root bark of apple trees. French chemists isolated phlorizin in 1835, and later researchers found that it made animals spill sugar into their urine. Phlorizin was useless as a drug because it was poorly absorbed and blocked transporters throughout the body. In the late 20th century scientists identified the kidney's sodium-glucose cotransporters, and SGLT2 emerged as the main transporter that reclaims filtered glucose. Chemists then built stable, selective C-glucoside versions of phlorizin. Canagliflozin was discovered by Mitsubishi Tanabe Pharma in Japan and developed by Janssen. In 2013 it became the first SGLT2 inhibitor approved by the FDA, as Invokana. The CANVAS program in 2017 and CREDENCE trial in 2019 showed heart and kidney benefits, and the boxed warning for leg amputation added in 2017 was removed in 2020.", "timeline": [["1835", "French chemists isolate phlorizin from apple tree root bark."], ["1990s", "SGLT2 is identified as the main kidney glucose reabsorption transporter."], ["2013", "FDA approves Invokana, the first SGLT2 inhibitor in the US."], ["2017", "CANVAS shows fewer cardiovascular events but more amputations."], ["2019", "CREDENCE shows kidney protection in diabetic kidney disease."], ["2020", "The amputation boxed warning is removed."]], "cell": "The kidneys filter a large amount of glucose every day and normally reclaim almost all of it. About 90 percent is taken back by SGLT2, a transporter in the early (S1) segment of the proximal tubule that moves one sodium ion and one glucose molecule together into the tubule cell. Canagliflozin binds SGLT2 from the tubule lumen and blocks it. This lowers the kidney's threshold for glucose, so glucose spills into the urine, taking calories and water with it. Because sodium reabsorption also falls, more sodium reaches the macula densa. That restores tubuloglomerular feedback, constricts the afferent arteriole and lowers pressure inside the glomerulus, a key reason for kidney protection. Canagliflozin also weakly blocks SGLT1 in the gut at higher exposures, slightly delaying glucose absorption.", "body": [["Kidneys", "Glucose and sodium are lost in the urine, and pressure inside the glomeruli falls, which slows kidney damage over time."], ["Blood sugar", "Glucose falls independent of insulin, so the drug works even when beta cells are failing."], ["Heart and vessels", "Mild diuresis lowers blood volume and blood pressure, which reduces heart failure hospitalizations."], ["Weight", "Losing glucose in the urine removes calories, causing modest weight loss."], ["Genitals and bladder", "Sugar in urine feeds yeast and bacteria, so genital fungal infections and urinary infections are more common."], ["Bones and limbs", "Higher fracture rates and leg and foot amputations were seen in CANVAS, possibly related to volume loss and blood flow in people with vascular disease."]], "journey": "Canagliflozin is taken by mouth before the first meal of the day, reaching a peak in one to two hours with oral bioavailability of about 65 percent. It is about 99 percent bound to albumin. It is not mainly a CYP drug; instead it is conjugated in the liver by the glucuronidation enzymes UGT1A9 and UGT2B4 into inactive metabolites. About half leaves in the feces and a third in the urine, mostly as glucuronides. The half-life is about 11 to 13 hours, so one dose lasts all day. Enzyme inducers such as rifampin lower its levels.", "sidefx": "Each main side effect follows from sugar and sodium loss. Sugar in the urine promotes yeast infections, urinary infections and rarely Fournier gangrene. Fluid loss can cause dizziness and low blood pressure, especially with diuretics. By lowering glucose and insulin needs while glucagon rises, SGLT2 inhibitors shift the body toward fat burning and ketone production, so ketoacidosis can occur even with near-normal glucose (euglycemic DKA), especially around surgery or illness, which is why therapy is paused before surgery. There is no antidote; DKA is treated with fluids, insulin and dextrose.", "fun": "Phlorizin was used for over a century as a lab tool to make animals temporarily diabetic, long before anyone thought of turning its action into a treatment.", "src": ["Invokana (canagliflozin) FDA label", "Neal B et al. CANVAS, N Engl J Med 2017", "Perkovic V et al. CREDENCE, N Engl J Med 2019"]}, "ertugliflozin": {"origin": "Ertugliflozin is the fourth SGLT2 inhibitor approved in the US. Like the others it traces back to phlorizin, the apple bark compound isolated in 1835 that makes the kidneys spill sugar. Pfizer chemists designed ertugliflozin with a distinctive bicyclic ketal ring attached to the sugar portion, which made the molecule highly selective for SGLT2. Pfizer developed it together with Merck, and the FDA approved it as Steglatro in December 2017, along with combinations with metformin (Segluromet) and sitagliptin (Steglujan). The VERTIS CV trial, reported in 2020, found that it did not raise cardiovascular risk but, unlike some others in its class, did not clearly lower major cardiovascular events.", "timeline": [["1835", "Phlorizin is isolated from apple tree bark."], ["2017", "FDA approves Steglatro (ertugliflozin) and its combinations."], ["2020", "VERTIS CV reports cardiovascular safety without a significant reduction in major events."]], "cell": "Ertugliflozin selectively blocks sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 in the proximal convoluted tubule. SGLT2 normally uses the sodium gradient to pull filtered glucose back into the blood. With SGLT2 blocked, the renal threshold for glucose drops, and glucose that would have been reabsorbed stays in the tubule and leaves in the urine. Water follows the glucose, causing mild osmotic diuresis, and sodium loss adds a small natriuretic effect. Ertugliflozin is far more selective for SGLT2 than for SGLT1, which is mainly in the gut, so it has little effect on intestinal glucose absorption. Its glucose lowering does not depend on insulin, but it weakens as kidney function falls because less glucose is filtered.", "body": [["Kidneys", "More glucose and some sodium leave in urine. As kidney filtration falls, the glucose-lowering effect becomes smaller."], ["Blood sugar", "Lower fasting and after-meal glucose without needing more insulin."], ["Blood pressure", "Mild fluid and salt loss lowers blood pressure a few points."], ["Weight", "Calories lost as urine glucose lead to modest weight loss."], ["Genital and urinary tract", "Glucose-rich urine encourages yeast infections and urinary tract infections."]], "journey": "Ertugliflozin is absorbed almost completely by mouth, with high bioavailability, and peaks in about an hour. It is about 94 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver mainly glucuronidates it through UGT1A9 and UGT2B7, with only minor CYP oxidation. About half leaves in the urine and about 40 percent in the feces, mostly as inactive metabolites. Its half-life is about 17 hours, the longest among the common SGLT2 inhibitors, and it is taken once in the morning.", "sidefx": "Genital yeast infections and urinary infections come from sugar in the urine. Volume depletion and low blood pressure come from osmotic diuresis. Euglycemic ketoacidosis can occur because lower glucose and insulin plus higher glucagon push the liver toward ketone production; this is why the label describes stopping it at least four days before scheduled surgery. Fournier gangrene and lower limb amputation are rare class warnings. Low blood sugar is mainly a concern with insulin or sulfonylureas. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Ertugliflozin's unusual dioxa-bicyclo ring is a cage-like structure that helps lock the sugar part of the molecule into a shape SGLT2 prefers.", "src": ["Steglatro (ertugliflozin) FDA label", "Cannon CP et al. VERTIS CV, N Engl J Med 2020"]}, "rosiglitazone": {"origin": "The thiazolidinedione (glitazone) family began at Takeda in Japan around 1980 with ciglitazone, which lowered glucose in animals but was not developed further. The first glitazone sold in the US, troglitazone, was withdrawn in 2000 because of severe liver injury. Rosiglitazone was developed by SmithKline Beecham (later GlaxoSmithKline) and approved by the FDA as Avandia in 1999. In the 1990s scientists discovered that glitazones work by activating PPAR-gamma, a nuclear receptor that controls fat cell genes. In 2007 a meta-analysis by Steven Nissen raised concern about heart attacks. The FDA sharply restricted rosiglitazone in 2010 through a safety program. After the RECORD trial was independently readjudicated, the FDA lifted the restrictions in 2013 and removed the program in 2015. The heart failure boxed warning remains.", "timeline": [["c. 1980", "Takeda develops ciglitazone, the first thiazolidinedione."], ["1999", "FDA approves Avandia (rosiglitazone)."], ["2000", "Troglitazone is withdrawn for liver toxicity."], ["2007", "A meta-analysis raises concern about heart attack risk."], ["2010", "FDA restricts rosiglitazone access."], ["2013", "FDA lifts restrictions after RECORD readjudication."]], "cell": "Rosiglitazone is a potent agonist of peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma (PPAR-gamma), a nuclear receptor found mostly in fat tissue but also in muscle, liver, kidney and blood vessels. When rosiglitazone binds, PPAR-gamma pairs with the retinoid X receptor, attaches to response elements in DNA and changes gene transcription. In fat tissue this drives formation of new small fat cells that store fatty acids safely, increases GLUT4 and raises adiponectin. Lower free fatty acids and more adiponectin reduce insulin resistance in muscle and liver, so insulin works better. Because it changes gene expression, the full glucose effect takes weeks. In the kidney collecting duct, PPAR-gamma activation increases sodium reabsorption through ENaC, which causes fluid retention.", "body": [["Fat tissue", "New small fat cells store fat under the skin, which improves insulin sensitivity but adds weight."], ["Muscle", "Muscle cells take up glucose more readily in response to insulin."], ["Liver", "The liver responds better to insulin and releases less glucose."], ["Kidneys", "Increased sodium reabsorption in the collecting duct retains salt and water, causing edema."], ["Heart", "Fluid retention can cause or worsen heart failure, which is the boxed warning."], ["Bones", "Fracture risk rises, especially in women, likely because PPAR-gamma pushes marrow stem cells toward fat instead of bone-forming cells."], ["Eyes", "Macular edema has been reported, likely related to fluid retention."], ["Ovaries", "Better insulin sensitivity can restart ovulation in some premenopausal women who were not ovulating."]], "journey": "Rosiglitazone is almost completely absorbed by mouth, with a peak in about an hour. It is extremely highly bound to albumin. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP2C8, with a minor role for CYP2C9, into inactive metabolites. About two thirds leave in the urine and a quarter in the feces. Its half-life is only three to four hours, but because it works through gene expression, its effects on glucose build over weeks and persist after stopping.", "sidefx": "Weight gain comes from both new fat storage and fluid. Edema and heart failure come from PPAR-gamma driven salt and water retention in the kidney. Fractures come from PPAR-gamma shifting bone marrow cells toward fat. Anemia is partly from blood dilution. Low blood sugar is rare alone but occurs with insulin or sulfonylureas. Strong CYP2C8 inhibitors such as gemfibrozil raise levels. No antidote exists.", "fun": "Glitazones were the first diabetes drugs found to work by turning genes on, which made PPAR-gamma a famous example of drugging a nuclear receptor.", "src": ["Avandia (rosiglitazone) FDA label", "Nissen SE, Wolski K. N Engl J Med 2007", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "lixisenatide": {"origin": "Lixisenatide comes from a lizard. In 1992 endocrinologist John Eng at the Bronx VA Medical Center isolated exendin-4 from the venom of the Gila monster, a lizard of the American Southwest. Exendin-4 acts like human GLP-1 but resists breakdown by DPP-4. It became the drug exenatide in 2005. Danish company Zealand Pharma modified exendin-4 further by removing one proline and adding a tail of six lysines, creating lixisenatide. Sanofi licensed and developed it. It was approved in Europe in 2013, and the FDA approved Adlyxin in 2016 and the insulin glargine combination Soliqua the same year. The standalone Adlyxin product was discontinued in the US by the manufacturer in 2023, while Soliqua remains.", "timeline": [["1992", "John Eng isolates exendin-4 from Gila monster venom."], ["2005", "Exenatide, the first GLP-1 receptor agonist, is approved in the US."], ["2013", "Lixisenatide is approved in Europe."], ["2016", "FDA approves Adlyxin and the combination Soliqua."], ["2023", "Standalone Adlyxin is discontinued in the US."]], "cell": "Lixisenatide is a peptide agonist of the GLP-1 receptor, a Gs protein coupled receptor. On pancreatic beta cells, binding activates adenylyl cyclase, raises cAMP, and activates protein kinase A and Epac2. This makes beta cells release more insulin, but only when glucose is high, because the trigger for release is still glucose-driven closure of K-ATP channels. GLP-1 receptor signaling also lowers glucagon release from alpha cells. In the brainstem and hypothalamus it increases fullness. As a short-acting agonist, its levels rise and fall each day, and this on-off pattern keeps the vagal pathway that slows stomach emptying strongly active. Slow gastric emptying is a major reason lixisenatide especially lowers glucose after the first meal following injection.", "body": [["Stomach", "Gastric emptying slows markedly, so glucose from the meal enters the blood more slowly, flattening the after-meal spike."], ["Pancreas", "Insulin release rises and glucagon falls when glucose is elevated."], ["Brain", "Appetite falls and fullness rises, which can cause modest weight loss."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are common, especially early."], ["Kidneys", "Vomiting and diarrhea can cause dehydration and acute kidney injury."], ["Immune system", "Antibodies can form against the peptide, and rare serious allergic reactions have occurred."]], "journey": "Lixisenatide is injected under the skin within an hour before the first meal of the day. It reaches peak levels in one to a few hours. As a peptide, it is not handled by liver enzymes. It is filtered by the kidneys, reabsorbed in the tubules and then broken down into small peptides and amino acids. Its half-life is about three hours, which makes it a short-acting GLP-1 agonist, unlike weekly agents. It is not recommended in end-stage kidney disease.", "sidefx": "Nausea and vomiting come from GLP-1 action on the brainstem and slowed stomach emptying. Slow emptying can also delay absorption of other pills taken around the same time. Dehydration from vomiting can harm the kidneys. Pancreatitis is a rare class warning. Low blood sugar is mostly seen with sulfonylureas or basal insulin. Serious allergic reactions can occur because it is a foreign peptide. Unlike long-acting GLP-1 agonists, it does not carry a thyroid C-cell tumor boxed warning. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The Gila monster eats only a few large meals a year, and the exendin-4 in its saliva helps it handle sudden large loads of food.", "src": ["Adlyxin (lixisenatide) FDA label", "Eng J et al. J Biol Chem 1992"]}, "insulin-glulisine": {"origin": "Insulin was discovered in 1921 and 1922 at the University of Toronto by Frederick Banting, Charles Best, James Collip and John Macleod, who turned a fatal disease into a treatable one. Early insulins came from cow and pig pancreas. In 1982 human insulin made by recombinant DNA became the first biotech drug approved, made by Genentech and Eli Lilly. Natural insulin forms clusters of six molecules (hexamers) under the skin, which slows absorption. Scientists began altering the amino acid sequence so the molecules would separate faster. Insulin lispro (1996) and insulin aspart came first. Aventis (later Sanofi) developed insulin glulisine by swapping two amino acids, and the FDA approved it as Apidra in 2004. It is also made without zinc, which helps it stay in single units.", "timeline": [["1921", "Banting and Best extract insulin in Toronto."], ["1922", "Insulin is first given to a patient with diabetes."], ["1982", "Recombinant human insulin is approved."], ["1996", "Insulin lispro becomes the first rapid-acting analog."], ["2004", "FDA approves Apidra (insulin glulisine)."]], "cell": "Insulin glulisine differs from human insulin in two spots on the B chain: asparagine at B3 is replaced with lysine, and lysine at B29 is replaced with glutamic acid. These changes, plus a zinc-free formula, make the molecules separate quickly into monomers after injection, so they enter capillaries fast. Glulisine binds the insulin receptor, a tyrosine kinase on cell surfaces. The receptor autophosphorylates and phosphorylates insulin receptor substrate proteins, activating the PI3K-Akt pathway. In muscle and fat, Akt drives GLUT4 transporters to the cell surface so glucose enters. In the liver, insulin activates glycogen synthesis and shuts down gluconeogenesis and glycogen breakdown. Insulin also stops fat breakdown and ketone production. It activates the sodium-potassium ATPase, moving potassium into cells.", "body": [["Muscle", "GLUT4 moves to the cell surface and glucose is stored as glycogen, lowering blood sugar after meals."], ["Liver", "The liver stops releasing glucose and stores it as glycogen instead."], ["Fat tissue", "Fat breakdown stops and glucose is taken up for storage, which also suppresses ketone formation."], ["Potassium balance", "Potassium shifts into cells, so blood potassium can fall."], ["Skin", "Repeated injections in one spot can cause lipodystrophy or localized cutaneous amyloidosis, lumps that absorb insulin unpredictably."], ["Brain", "The brain depends on glucose, so too much insulin quickly causes confusion, sweating, seizures or coma."]], "journey": "Glulisine is injected under the skin just before or right after starting a meal, or given by insulin pump. It is absorbed quickly because it does not form hexamers, beginning to work in about 15 minutes and peaking in about an hour. Its action lasts about three to five hours. It can be given by vein under close supervision. Like natural insulin, it is broken down mainly by insulin-degrading enzyme in the liver and kidneys. The elimination half-life after injection is under an hour.", "sidefx": "Low blood sugar is the main risk and is simply too much insulin action for the food eaten or activity done. Low potassium comes from insulin pushing potassium into cells. With a pump, any blockage stops insulin delivery, and because only rapid insulin is used, ketoacidosis can develop within hours. Lipodystrophy and amyloid lumps come from repeated injections in the same site. Low blood sugar is reversed with oral glucose, IV dextrose or glucagon.", "fun": "Glulisine was formulated without zinc, which most other insulins use to stabilize their hexamers, and that change alone helps it act quickly.", "src": ["Apidra (insulin glulisine) FDA label", "Banting FG, Best CH. J Lab Clin Med 1922", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "insulin-nph-regular-70-30": {"origin": "This premixed insulin combines two inventions. Regular insulin is the original soluble insulin discovered in Toronto in 1921 and 1922. NPH stands for Neutral Protamine Hagedorn, named for Danish researcher Hans Christian Hagedorn at Nordisk. In 1936 Hagedorn showed that adding protamine, a protein from fish sperm, made insulin dissolve slowly under the skin. NPH, a neutral and stable form with zinc, was introduced around 1950 and allowed many people to manage with fewer injections. In 1982 recombinant human insulin became the first biotech drug. Premixed human insulin with 70 percent NPH and 30 percent regular later became a common way to cover both meals and background needs in one shot. Humulin 70/30 and Novolin 70/30 are sold in the US, and vials can be bought without a prescription.", "timeline": [["1922", "Insulin is first used in a patient in Toronto."], ["1936", "Hagedorn shows protamine prolongs insulin action."], ["1950", "NPH insulin is introduced."], ["1982", "Recombinant human insulin is approved."]], "cell": "Both parts are human insulin made by recombinant DNA, so they act identically once in the blood. The difference is how fast they leave the injection site. The regular portion forms hexamers that slowly break into monomers, so it begins acting in about 30 minutes. The NPH portion is insulin bound into crystals with protamine and zinc. These crystals dissolve gradually as tissue enzymes break down the protamine, giving a slower, broader action lasting much of a day. In the blood, insulin binds the insulin receptor tyrosine kinase, activates insulin receptor substrates and the PI3K-Akt pathway, moves GLUT4 to the surface of muscle and fat cells, stimulates glycogen synthesis, and suppresses gluconeogenesis, lipolysis and ketogenesis. It also drives potassium into cells by activating the sodium-potassium ATPase.", "body": [["Muscle and fat", "Glucose uptake rises as GLUT4 moves to the cell surface, lowering blood sugar."], ["Liver", "Glucose output falls; NPH keeps it down for many hours, including overnight."], ["Fat tissue", "Fat breakdown and ketone formation are suppressed."], ["Potassium balance", "Potassium moves into cells, which can lower blood potassium."], ["Brain", "Low blood sugar can be delayed and occur at night because of the NPH peak, causing sweating, nightmares, confusion or seizures."], ["Skin", "Repeated injections at one site can cause lipodystrophy or amyloid lumps."]], "journey": "The cloudy suspension is rolled to mix, then injected under the skin about 30 to 45 minutes before a meal. The regular part is absorbed first and peaks in a few hours, while the NPH part peaks later and lasts up to about a day, giving a two-humped curve. Absorption varies with injection site, exercise and heat. Once absorbed, insulin is broken down by insulin-degrading enzyme in the liver and kidneys. It is not for IV use or insulin pumps because the NPH is a suspension.", "sidefx": "Low blood sugar is the main risk, and the NPH peak can cause it hours after the dose, including overnight or if a meal is late. Weight gain reflects insulin's storage effects. Low potassium comes from potassium moving into cells. Because the ratio is fixed, the meal and background portions cannot be adjusted separately. Protamine can rarely trigger allergic reactions. Low blood sugar is treated with glucose or glucagon.", "fun": "Hagedorn's protamine came from the sperm of trout and other fish, a surprising ingredient that made once- or twice-daily insulin possible.", "src": ["Humulin 70/30 FDA label", "Novolin 70/30 FDA label", "Hagedorn HC et al. JAMA 1936"]}, "inhaled-insulin": {"origin": "The idea of breathing in insulin dates back to the 1920s, soon after insulin was discovered, but early attempts gave unreliable results. The lungs offer a huge, thin surface for absorption. The first approved inhaled insulin, Exubera by Pfizer, came out in 2006 but was pulled from the market in 2007 because of poor sales and its bulky inhaler. MannKind Corporation, founded by entrepreneur Alfred Mann, developed a different approach called Technosphere. Insulin is attached to tiny particles of fumaryl diketopiperazine (FDKP) that dissolve at the neutral pH of the lung. The FDA approved Afrezza in 2014. Sanofi marketed it at first, then returned the rights to MannKind in 2016.", "timeline": [["1920s", "Researchers first try inhaling insulin."], ["2006", "Exubera becomes the first approved inhaled insulin."], ["2007", "Exubera is withdrawn for commercial reasons."], ["2014", "FDA approves Afrezza."], ["2016", "MannKind takes back Afrezza from Sanofi."]], "cell": "Afrezza contains regular human insulin adsorbed onto FDKP microparticles. When inhaled, the particles reach the deep lung. At the neutral pH of the alveolar lining fluid, FDKP dissolves and releases insulin as monomers, which cross the very thin alveolar wall into capillaries within minutes. Once in the blood, insulin acts like natural insulin. It binds the insulin receptor tyrosine kinase, triggering IRS proteins and PI3K-Akt signaling. This moves GLUT4 to the surface of muscle and fat cells, stimulates glycogen synthesis, and quickly suppresses the liver's glucose output. The fast rise and fall mimic the early insulin spike that a healthy pancreas makes at the start of a meal.", "body": [["Lungs", "Insulin is absorbed through the alveoli. It can trigger bronchospasm in people with asthma or COPD and causes a small drop in FEV1, so spirometry is checked before and during use."], ["Airway", "Cough and throat irritation are common because a dry powder is inhaled."], ["Muscle and liver", "Glucose uptake rises and liver glucose output falls quickly, covering the meal."], ["Potassium balance", "Potassium moves into cells, which can lower blood potassium."], ["Brain", "Low blood sugar can cause shakiness, sweating and confusion, though the short action helps limit late lows."]], "journey": "Afrezza is breathed in at the start of a meal from a small inhaler. Serum insulin peaks in about 12 to 20 minutes, faster than injected rapid insulins, and its glucose effect peaks within about an hour. The action is short, about one and a half to three hours. Only a portion of the inhaled dose reaches the blood. Insulin is broken down by insulin-degrading enzyme in the liver and kidneys like natural insulin. The FDKP carrier is not metabolized and leaves unchanged in the urine.", "sidefx": "Bronchospasm happens because inhaled powder can irritate twitchy airways, which is why it is contraindicated in chronic lung disease and carries a boxed warning. Cough is common for the same reason. A small decline in lung function was seen in trials. Lung cancer cases were seen in trials, though too few to know if they were related. Low blood sugar and low potassium come from insulin itself. Low blood sugar is treated with glucose or glucagon.", "fun": "Alfred Mann, who founded MannKind, also built companies that made cochlear implants and insulin pumps.", "src": ["Afrezza (insulin human) inhalation powder FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "teplizumab": {"origin": "Teplizumab descends from OKT3 (muromonab-CD3), a mouse antibody against T cells that in 1986 became the first monoclonal antibody approved by the FDA, used to treat organ transplant rejection. OKT3 caused severe cytokine storms. Immunologist Jeffrey Bluestone and colleagues engineered a humanized version with Fc mutations so it would bind T cells without strongly activating them. Kevan Herold and colleagues then tested it in type 1 diabetes, where T cells destroy insulin-making beta cells. The landmark TN-10 trial in 2019, run by the TrialNet network, showed that one 14-day course delayed the diagnosis of clinical type 1 diabetes by about two years in high-risk relatives. Provention Bio brought it to the FDA, which approved Tzield in 2022 as the first drug to delay type 1 diabetes. Sanofi acquired Provention Bio in 2023.", "timeline": [["1986", "OKT3 becomes the first FDA-approved monoclonal antibody."], ["2002", "A small trial reports that teplizumab preserves insulin production in new-onset type 1 diabetes."], ["2019", "The TN-10 trial shows delay of clinical type 1 diabetes."], ["2022", "FDA approves Tzield (teplizumab)."]], "cell": "Teplizumab is a humanized IgG1 antibody that binds the epsilon chain of CD3, part of the T-cell receptor complex on all T cells. Two amino acid changes in its Fc region (alanine at positions 234 and 235) reduce binding to Fc receptors on other immune cells. This means it engages CD3 without the strong cross-linking that made OKT3 trigger massive T-cell activation. Instead it delivers a partial, weak signal. Over the 14-day course, autoreactive CD8 T cells become exhausted or anergic, some T cells are temporarily removed from the blood, and regulatory T-cell populations expand. The immune attack on pancreatic beta cells slows, so beta cells survive longer and the person keeps making their own insulin for longer before reaching Stage 3 type 1 diabetes.", "body": [["Immune system", "T cells become less aggressive toward beta cells. Lymphocyte counts drop sharply during treatment, reaching a low around day five, then recover."], ["Pancreas", "Beta cells are attacked more slowly, so insulin production (measured by C-peptide) is preserved longer and diagnosis is delayed."], ["Whole body", "Cytokine release can cause fever, fatigue, headache, nausea and muscle aches, especially in the first days."], ["Skin", "Rash is common during the course and usually settles after the infusions end."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise temporarily, so liver tests are checked before and during the course."], ["Infection defense", "Lower T-cell numbers raise the risk of infections, and live vaccines are avoided around treatment."]], "journey": "Teplizumab is given as an IV infusion once daily for 14 days. As an antibody, it is not broken down by liver enzymes but by normal protein catabolism into peptides and amino acids. Its half-life is about four days. The immune effects begin within days, but the benefit of delaying diabetes lasts months to years after the short course ends.", "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome happens because even weak CD3 signaling activates some T cells, which release cytokines; premedication with an NSAID or acetaminophen, an antihistamine and an antiemetic option is used. Lymphopenia comes from T cells being redistributed and depleted. Serious infections follow from lower T-cell defense. Rash, serum sickness and other hypersensitivity reactions reflect immune responses to the antibody. There is no specific antidote; reactions are managed with supportive care and pausing infusions.", "fun": "Teplizumab was the first drug approved to delay an autoimmune disease before it causes symptoms rather than to treat it after diagnosis.", "src": ["Tzield (teplizumab-mzwv) FDA label", "Herold KC et al. TN-10, N Engl J Med 2019"]}, "dasiglucagon": {"origin": "Glucagon was discovered in 1923 by Charles Kimball and John Murlin, who found a substance in pancreatic extracts that raised blood sugar, the opposite of insulin. It was named glucagon for glucose agonist. Natural glucagon is unstable in liquid. It forms fibrils and must be mixed from powder right before use, which is hard during an emergency. Zealand Pharma in Denmark designed dasiglucagon by changing seven amino acids in the glucagon sequence so it stays stable as a ready-to-use liquid. The FDA approved it as Zegalogue in 2021 for severe low blood sugar in people with diabetes aged six and older.", "timeline": [["1923", "Kimball and Murlin discover glucagon in pancreatic extracts."], ["2019", "Nasal glucagon offers a powder-free option."], ["2021", "FDA approves Zegalogue (dasiglucagon)."]], "cell": "Dasiglucagon binds the glucagon receptor, a Gs protein coupled receptor on liver cells. This activates adenylyl cyclase and raises cAMP, which activates protein kinase A. PKA activates phosphorylase kinase, which activates glycogen phosphorylase, so stored glycogen is broken down into glucose-1-phosphate and then free glucose released into the blood (glycogenolysis). PKA also switches on gluconeogenesis and lowers fructose-2,6-bisphosphate, which slows glycolysis, so the liver makes and exports glucose instead of burning it. The rise in blood glucose depends on having glycogen stored in the liver. The amino acid changes keep the molecule soluble without affecting receptor activation.", "body": [["Liver", "Glycogen breaks down rapidly and glucose pours into the blood, raising sugar within about ten minutes in trials."], ["Brain", "Restored glucose supply reverses confusion, seizures or unconsciousness from low blood sugar."], ["Gut", "Glucagon relaxes gut smooth muscle and often causes nausea and vomiting, which is why the person is placed on their side."], ["Heart", "Glucagon can raise heart rate and force through cAMP in heart cells."], ["Adrenal glands", "In pheochromocytoma, glucagon can trigger catecholamine release and dangerous high blood pressure."]], "journey": "Dasiglucagon is injected under the skin from an autoinjector or prefilled syringe with no mixing. It reaches peak levels in about 35 minutes, and the glucose rise begins within minutes. Like natural glucagon, it is broken down by proteolytic enzymes in the blood, liver and kidneys. Its half-life is about 30 minutes, and the effect lasts about an hour and a half, so the person needs food once awake and able to swallow.", "sidefx": "Nausea and vomiting come from glucagon's effects on the gut and brainstem. It does not work well when liver glycogen is depleted, such as in starvation, adrenal insufficiency or long-lasting low blood sugar. In insulinoma, the initial glucose rise can trigger more insulin release and rebound low blood sugar. In pheochromocytoma, it can release catecholamines and cause a hypertensive crisis. Allergic reactions are rare.", "fun": "Glucagon is also used by radiologists to temporarily relax the gut during imaging studies, a use of its smooth muscle effect.", "src": ["Zegalogue (dasiglucagon) FDA label", "Kimball CP, Murlin JR. J Biol Chem 1923"]}, "estradiol": {"origin": "Estradiol is the main estrogen made by the human ovary. In the 1920s Edgar Allen and Edward Doisy identified estrogenic activity in ovarian follicle fluid. Doisy and, separately, Adolf Butenandt isolated estrone from urine in 1929. In 1935 Doisy's group at Saint Louis University isolated estradiol from pig ovaries, using huge amounts of tissue to collect tiny amounts of hormone. Natural estradiol is poorly absorbed by mouth in its normal form, so early therapies relied on estrogen mixtures or synthetic estrogens. Micronized estradiol tablets (Estrace) made oral estradiol practical, and the first estradiol skin patch was approved in the US in 1986. The Women's Health Initiative in 2002 reshaped hormone therapy by showing increased risks of clots, stroke and breast cancer in older women, which led to current boxed warnings.", "timeline": [["1929", "Doisy and Butenandt independently isolate estrone."], ["1935", "Doisy's group isolates estradiol from sow ovaries."], ["1986", "The first estradiol skin patch is approved in the US."], ["2002", "The Women's Health Initiative reports risks of estrogen plus progestin therapy."]], "cell": "Estradiol is a lipid-soluble steroid that crosses cell membranes and binds estrogen receptors alpha and beta inside the cell. The bound receptor forms a dimer, moves to the nucleus and binds estrogen response elements in DNA, recruiting coactivators that turn specific genes on or off. In the hypothalamus, estradiol stabilizes the thermoregulatory zone, which stops hot flashes, and provides negative feedback that lowers GnRH, FSH and LH. In bone, it reduces RANKL signaling and shortens osteoclast life, slowing bone loss. In the vagina it thickens and moisturizes the epithelium. In the liver, especially with oral use, it increases production of clotting factors, SHBG, thyroid binding globulin, triglycerides and HDL. In the uterus it makes the lining grow, which is why a progestin is added in women with a uterus.", "body": [["Brain", "The hypothalamic temperature center becomes stable again, so hot flashes and night sweats ease."], ["Bone", "Osteoclast activity falls, slowing bone loss and lowering fracture risk."], ["Vagina and bladder", "The lining thickens and becomes moist, easing dryness and painful intercourse."], ["Uterus", "Estrogen stimulates endometrial growth. Without a progestin this raises the risk of hyperplasia and cancer."], ["Liver", "Oral estrogen raises clotting factors, SHBG and triglycerides; transdermal forms have less first-pass liver effect."], ["Breast", "Breast tissue grows, causing tenderness; long-term combined therapy raises breast cancer risk."], ["Blood vessels", "Clotting changes raise the risk of DVT, pulmonary embolism and stroke."]], "journey": "Estradiol can be taken by mouth, through the skin as a patch, gel or spray, or in the vagina. Oral estradiol is absorbed but heavily converted in the gut wall and liver to estrone and estrone sulfate, which act as a reservoir and can convert back. Patches and gels skip this first pass and give steadier levels. In blood it binds SHBG and albumin. The liver metabolizes it via CYP3A4 to estrone and estriol and then sulfates and glucuronidates it. Conjugates go into bile, are partly reabsorbed through enterohepatic recycling and finally leave in the urine.", "sidefx": "Most risks come from estrogen's normal actions in the wrong tissue or at the wrong age. Unopposed endometrial growth leads to endometrial cancer, which is why a progestin is added. Increased liver production of clotting factors explains DVT, PE and stroke. Breast tenderness and bloating reflect hormone effects on breast tissue and fluid. Higher bile cholesterol raises gallstone risk. High triglycerides from liver effects can rarely cause pancreatitis. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped.", "fun": "The name estradiol describes its chemistry: an estrane steroid carrying two alcohol groups, a diol.", "src": ["Estrace (estradiol) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Writing Group for the WHI, JAMA 2002"]}, "conjugated-estrogens": {"origin": "Conjugated estrogens come from horses. In the 1930s scientists learned that the urine of pregnant mares contains large amounts of water-soluble estrogens. The Canadian company Ayerst, McKenna and Harrison developed a purified mixture and named it Premarin, from PREgnant MARes' urINe. It was introduced in Canada in 1941, and the FDA approved it in 1942. It became one of the most prescribed drugs in the US for decades. The mixture contains estrone sulfate, equilin sulfate and other estrogens, some of which, such as equilin, are unique to horses. The Women's Health Initiative, published in 2002 and 2004, studied Premarin with and without medroxyprogesterone and found increased risks of stroke and clots, which led to boxed warnings and much lower use. Premarin is now made by Pfizer.", "timeline": [["1930s", "Pregnant mare urine is found to be rich in estrogens."], ["1942", "FDA approves Premarin."], ["1995", "Prempro combines conjugated estrogens with medroxyprogesterone."], ["2002", "The WHI estrogen plus progestin arm is stopped early."], ["2004", "The WHI estrogen-alone arm reports increased stroke risk."], ["2013", "Duavee combines conjugated estrogens with bazedoxifene."]], "cell": "The mixture contains sulfate conjugates of several estrogens, mainly estrone sulfate and equilin sulfate. These conjugates are not active themselves. Sulfatase enzymes in tissues and the gut remove the sulfate group, and 17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenases interconvert the estrones into more potent estradiol-type molecules, such as 17-beta-estradiol and 17-beta-dihydroequilin. These bind nuclear estrogen receptors alpha and beta. The receptor complexes bind estrogen response elements in DNA and switch genes on or off. In the hypothalamus this calms the temperature center and lowers FSH and LH. In bone, osteoclast activity falls. In the vagina, the epithelium thickens. In the liver, synthesis of clotting factors, SHBG, thyroid binding globulin and triglycerides rises. In the uterus the lining proliferates, so a progestin is added in women with a uterus.", "body": [["Brain", "Hot flashes and night sweats ease as the thermoregulatory center stabilizes."], ["Bone", "Bone resorption slows, helping prevent postmenopausal osteoporosis."], ["Vagina", "The vaginal cream restores a thicker, moist lining and relieves atrophy symptoms."], ["Uterus", "The IV form stops abnormal uterine bleeding by rapidly regrowing the endometrium. Without a progestin, long-term use raises endometrial cancer risk."], ["Liver", "Oral use raises clotting factors, SHBG and triglycerides because the liver sees high levels on the first pass."], ["Blood vessels", "Higher clotting factor levels raise the risk of DVT, PE and stroke."], ["Breast", "Tenderness is common; combined estrogen-progestin therapy raised breast cancer risk in the WHI."]], "journey": "Tablets release the conjugated estrogens, which are absorbed in the gut, and some are deconjugated and reconjugated in the gut wall and liver. Peak levels vary by component, from a few hours to about ten. The estrogens circulate bound to SHBG and albumin. The liver metabolizes them through CYP3A4 and conjugation, and much of the drug is recycled through bile and reabsorbed (enterohepatic circulation). Half-lives range from about 10 to over 20 hours depending on the component. Conjugates leave mainly in the urine. The IV form acts within hours for bleeding.", "sidefx": "The side effects reflect estrogen acting on many tissues. Endometrial cancer results from unopposed growth of the uterine lining. Stroke, DVT, PE and heart attack relate to higher clotting factor production and other vascular effects, which were greater when therapy was started in older women in the WHI. Breast cancer risk rose with combined therapy. Gallstones follow from higher bile cholesterol. Angioedema can be triggered or worsened in hereditary angioedema. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Equilin, one of Premarin's components, is found in horses but not naturally in humans.", "src": ["Premarin (conjugated estrogens) FDA label", "Writing Group for the WHI, JAMA 2002", "WHI Steering Committee, JAMA 2004"]}, "progesterone": {"origin": "Progesterone is the hormone of the corpus luteum, the gland that forms in the ovary after ovulation. In 1929 George Corner and Willard Allen at the University of Rochester showed that corpus luteum extracts maintained pregnancy in rabbits. In 1934 several groups, including Adolf Butenandt's, isolated the pure crystalline hormone and named it progesterone, meaning pro-gestation. Early supplies were very expensive. In the 1940s chemist Russell Marker found a way to make progesterone from diosgenin, a compound in wild Mexican yams, which led to the founding of Syntex and made steroid hormones affordable. Natural progesterone is poorly absorbed by mouth, but micronizing it into tiny particles in oil improved absorption. The FDA approved micronized progesterone capsules as Prometrium in 1998.", "timeline": [["1929", "Corner and Allen show corpus luteum extracts maintain pregnancy."], ["1934", "Pure progesterone is isolated and named."], ["1940s", "Russell Marker makes progesterone from Mexican yam diosgenin."], ["1998", "FDA approves Prometrium (micronized progesterone)."]], "cell": "Progesterone is a steroid that enters cells and binds nuclear progesterone receptors A and B, which then bind progesterone response elements in DNA and change gene transcription. Estrogen first increases progesterone receptors in the uterus. Progesterone then converts the growing (proliferative) endometrium into a secretory lining: glands secrete glycogen, the stroma becomes ready for implantation, and cells stop dividing. It also reduces estrogen receptors and induces 17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2, which converts estradiol into the weaker estrone. Together these effects protect the lining from estrogen-driven overgrowth. In the hypothalamus progesterone slows GnRH pulses and raises body temperature. Some of its metabolites, such as allopregnanolone, enhance GABA-A receptor activity in the brain, which explains drowsiness.", "body": [["Uterus", "The lining turns secretory and stops proliferating, which protects against endometrial hyperplasia during estrogen therapy and supports early pregnancy."], ["Brain", "Neurosteroid metabolites act on GABA-A receptors, causing sleepiness and dizziness, which is why oral capsules are taken at bedtime."], ["Hypothalamus", "Body temperature rises slightly and LH pulses slow."], ["Breast", "Progesterone causes breast tenderness and, with estrogen, raised breast cancer risk in the WHI."], ["Blood vessels", "With estrogen, combined therapy was linked to more clots, stroke and heart attack in the WHI trials."], ["Mood", "Mood changes and depression can occur in some people."]], "journey": "Oral micronized progesterone is absorbed but heavily metabolized on its first pass through the liver, so only a small part reaches the blood unchanged, and many active metabolites form. Food increases absorption. Vaginal gels and inserts deliver progesterone directly to the uterus, a so-called first uterine pass. IM progesterone in oil gives high, steady levels. In blood it binds albumin and corticosteroid-binding globulin. The liver metabolizes it via CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 to pregnanediol and allopregnanolone. Conjugates leave in urine and bile. The parent half-life is short.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness and dizziness come from GABA-A acting metabolites. Breast tenderness, bloating and mood changes reflect progesterone receptor effects on breast, fluid balance and brain. When combined with estrogen, the WHI found increased heart attacks, stroke, clots, breast cancer and probable dementia in women 65 and older, which is the boxed warning. Prometrium capsules contain peanut oil, so people with peanut allergy can react. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Russell Marker's yam discovery is sometimes called the start of the steroid industry, and it helped make the birth control pill possible.", "src": ["Prometrium (progesterone) FDA label", "Corner GW, Allen WM. Am J Physiol 1929", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "norethindrone": {"origin": "Norethindrone was created in Mexico City on October 15, 1951, by chemist Luis Miramontes working with Carl Djerassi and George Rosenkranz at the company Syntex. They were trying to build a progestin that worked by mouth. By removing one carbon (the 19-methyl group) and adding an ethinyl group to a testosterone-like steroid, they made a compound that was a strong oral progestin. Norethindrone became one of the key molecules behind the first birth control pills, and Miramontes is listed on the patent as its inventor. Pills with norethindrone alone and no estrogen, called progestin-only pills or mini-pills, came onto the US market in the early 1970s and are still used by people who should avoid estrogen.", "timeline": [["1951", "Luis Miramontes synthesizes norethindrone at Syntex in Mexico City."], ["1960", "The first oral contraceptive, Enovid, is approved in the US."], ["1970s", "Norethindrone-only mini-pills become available in the US."]], "cell": "Norethindrone is a 19-nortestosterone progestin that binds the progesterone receptor and also has weak androgen receptor activity. At the low dose used in the mini-pill, its main contraceptive action is local. It makes cervical mucus thick, scant and sticky, so sperm cannot get through. It keeps the endometrium thin and less receptive to implantation and changes tubal movement. It suppresses the LH surge and ovulation in only about half of cycles, which is why it depends on very regular timing. Because the cervical mucus effect fades within about a day after the last pill, a late pill can quickly lower protection.", "body": [["Cervix", "Mucus becomes thick and hostile to sperm within about 48 hours of starting."], ["Uterus", "The endometrium becomes thin, which causes irregular bleeding, spotting or no periods."], ["Ovaries", "Ovulation is blocked in about half of cycles; functional ovarian cysts can form when follicles grow but do not release."], ["Skin", "Weak androgenic activity can cause acne in some people."], ["Fallopian tubes", "Changes in tubal movement mean that if a pregnancy does occur, it is relatively more likely to be ectopic."], ["Breast", "Breast tenderness is common in the first months and usually fades."]], "journey": "Norethindrone is absorbed quickly by mouth and peaks within one to two hours. Some is lost to first-pass metabolism, and about 97 percent of what reaches the blood binds albumin and SHBG. The liver reduces and conjugates it, with CYP3A4 involved. Metabolites leave in both urine and feces. Its half-life is about eight hours, and the protective cervical mucus effect lasts about 24 hours, which is why each pill is taken at the same time and a pill more than three hours late counts as missed.", "sidefx": "Irregular bleeding is the most common problem and comes from a thin endometrium without the steady estrogen of combined pills. Ovarian cysts come from follicles that grow without ovulating. Acne and other androgenic effects reflect weak androgen receptor binding. Enzyme inducers such as rifampin and some anticonvulsants speed CYP3A4 metabolism and lower effectiveness. Because it lacks estrogen, it does not carry the combined pill's clot and smoking boxed warning. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Luis Miramontes was a 26-year-old chemistry student when he first made norethindrone.", "src": ["Norethindrone 0.35 tablets FDA label", "Djerassi C et al. J Am Chem Soc 1954", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "levonorgestrel": {"origin": "Levonorgestrel is the active half of norgestrel, a progestin first made by total chemical synthesis in the early 1960s by Herchel Smith and colleagues working with Wyeth. Unlike many progestins, it was built in the lab rather than from plant steroids. Norgestrel is a mix of two mirror-image forms, and only one form, levonorgestrel, is active. In the 1970s Canadian physician Albert Yuzpe described emergency contraception using combined pills. Later, the World Health Organization showed that levonorgestrel alone worked better and caused less nausea. The FDA approved Plan B in 1999, allowed over-the-counter sale for adults in 2006, approved the single-tablet Plan B One-Step in 2009 and removed all age limits in 2013. Levonorgestrel is also the hormone in several IUDs, which are separate products.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Herchel Smith and colleagues synthesize norgestrel."], ["1970s", "Albert Yuzpe describes hormonal emergency contraception."], ["1999", "FDA approves Plan B."], ["2006", "Plan B becomes available over the counter for adults."], ["2013", "Levonorgestrel emergency contraception becomes available OTC with no age limit."]], "cell": "Levonorgestrel is a potent progestin that binds the progesterone receptor and has some androgen receptor activity. As emergency contraception it acts in the hypothalamus and pituitary. Taken before the LH surge, it blunts or delays the surge, so the dominant follicle does not rupture and ovulation is postponed. Sperm can survive in the reproductive tract for several days, but if ovulation is delayed past their lifespan, fertilization does not happen. Once the LH surge has started, levonorgestrel has little effect, which is why it works best the sooner it is taken. It does not disrupt an implanted pregnancy.", "body": [["Hypothalamus and pituitary", "The LH surge is blunted or delayed, so ovulation is postponed."], ["Ovaries", "The follicle does not release an egg on time, which prevents fertilization."], ["Uterus", "The next period can come earlier or later than expected, and spotting can occur."], ["Gut", "Nausea, abdominal pain and less commonly vomiting can occur, though much less than with older estrogen-containing methods."], ["Breast", "Breast tenderness can occur for a few days."]], "journey": "Levonorgestrel is almost completely absorbed by mouth, with no significant first-pass loss, and peaks within one to two hours. It binds strongly to SHBG and albumin. The liver metabolizes it by reduction, hydroxylation (CYP3A4) and conjugation. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. Its half-life is about one day or more. Strong CYP3A4 inducers such as rifampin lower its levels and can reduce effectiveness.", "sidefx": "Nausea, headache and fatigue reflect a sudden large progestin exposure. Menstrual timing changes because ovulation and the luteal phase are shifted. If the method fails, an ectopic pregnancy is a consideration with severe lower abdominal pain several weeks later, since progestins can alter tubal movement. It does not protect against sexually transmitted infections. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Levonorgestrel emergency contraception is sometimes called the morning-after pill, but it can be taken up to several days after sex, with effectiveness falling over time.", "src": ["Plan B One-Step (levonorgestrel) FDA label", "Task Force on Postovulatory Methods, WHO, Lancet 1998"]}, "ulipristal": {"origin": "Ulipristal acetate is a selective progesterone receptor modulator. It was first made in the 1990s as part of research supported by the US National Institutes of Health, which was looking for safer compounds that act on the progesterone receptor. The French company HRA Pharma developed it for emergency contraception. It was approved in Europe as ellaOne in 2009, and the FDA approved it as ella in 2010. Its advantage was that it still delays ovulation after the LH surge has begun, when levonorgestrel no longer works, and that it stays effective for up to five days after unprotected sex.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Ulipristal is developed through NIH-supported research."], ["2009", "Ulipristal is approved in Europe as ellaOne."], ["2010", "FDA approves ella for emergency contraception."]], "cell": "Ulipristal binds the progesterone receptor with high affinity and acts as a mix of partial agonist and antagonist. In the ovary, progesterone receptor signaling is needed for the final events of ovulation, including rupture of the follicle wall. By blocking this, ulipristal postpones follicular rupture and delays ovulation by several days. Importantly, it works even when given after the LH surge has started, as long as it is before the LH peak, a window where levonorgestrel has little effect. It may also change the endometrium. At higher exposures it can also bind glucocorticoid receptors.", "body": [["Ovaries", "Follicle rupture is delayed, so no egg is available while sperm are still alive."], ["Uterus", "The next period may come a few days earlier or later, and bleeding may be heavier or lighter."], ["Gut", "Nausea and abdominal pain are common mild effects."], ["Brain", "Headache, fatigue and dizziness can occur."], ["Contraception afterward", "Progestin-containing contraception can blunt ulipristal's effect, so hormonal methods are started no sooner than five days later with a barrier method until the next period."]], "journey": "Ulipristal is taken by mouth as a single tablet and peaks in about an hour. It is highly bound to plasma proteins. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP3A4 into an active mono-N-demethylated metabolite and other products. Most is eliminated in the feces. Its half-life is about 32 hours. Strong CYP3A4 inducers reduce its levels and effectiveness.", "sidefx": "Headache, nausea, abdominal pain and menstrual pain reflect hormonal shifts. Menstrual delay comes from moving ovulation later. Because it blocks the progesterone receptor, a progestin taken soon afterward competes with it and may reduce its effect. If pregnancy occurs, ectopic pregnancy is considered. Repeated use within one cycle is not well studied. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Ulipristal is also approved in some countries for uterine fibroids, but this use was restricted in Europe because of rare serious liver injury.", "src": ["ella (ulipristal acetate) FDA label", "Glasier AF et al. Lancet 2010"]}, "drospirenone-ethinyl-estradiol": {"origin": "This pill pairs two hormones with different histories. Ethinyl estradiol was synthesized in 1938 by Hans Herloff Inhoffen and Walter Hohlweg at Schering AG in Berlin; adding an ethinyl group to estradiol made it resistant to liver breakdown, so it worked by mouth. It became the estrogen in almost all combined pills after the first pill, Enovid, was approved in 1960. Drospirenone was developed by Schering AG as a progestin that resembles spironolactone, the potassium-sparing diuretic, rather than testosterone. The goal was a pill that caused less water retention and fewer androgenic effects. The FDA approved Yasmin in 2001 and Yaz, with a shorter hormone-free interval, in 2006. Yaz is also approved for PMDD and acne. Later studies suggested a somewhat higher clot risk than with levonorgestrel pills.", "timeline": [["1938", "Inhoffen and Hohlweg synthesize ethinyl estradiol at Schering."], ["1960", "Enovid becomes the first oral contraceptive approved in the US."], ["2001", "FDA approves Yasmin (drospirenone and ethinyl estradiol)."], ["2006", "FDA approves Yaz with a 24 active, 4 inert tablet design."], ["2012", "FDA updates labels about higher clot risk reported with drospirenone pills."]], "cell": "Ethinyl estradiol and drospirenone together suppress the hypothalamic-pituitary axis. Steady hormone levels provide negative feedback that lowers GnRH pulses and blunts FSH and LH release. Without FSH, follicles do not mature, and without an LH surge, ovulation does not occur. The progestin also thickens cervical mucus and thins the endometrium. Drospirenone is a 17-alpha-spironolactone analog. Besides activating progesterone receptors, it blocks mineralocorticoid receptors, causing mild sodium and water loss and potassium retention, and it blocks androgen receptors, which helps acne. Ethinyl estradiol binds estrogen receptors, stabilizes the endometrium to limit breakthrough bleeding, and in the liver raises SHBG and clotting factors.", "body": [["Ovaries", "Follicle growth and ovulation are blocked, which prevents pregnancy."], ["Cervix and uterus", "Mucus thickens and the lining thins, adding protection and making periods lighter."], ["Kidneys", "Antimineralocorticoid action causes mild sodium loss and potassium retention, so potassium can rise with ACE inhibitors, ARBs or potassium-sparing diuretics."], ["Skin", "Antiandrogen effects and higher SHBG reduce free testosterone, which helps acne."], ["Mood", "Yaz reduces symptoms of premenstrual dysphoric disorder in some women."], ["Blood vessels", "Ethinyl estradiol raises clotting factors in the liver, increasing the risk of DVT, PE, stroke and heart attack, more so in smokers over 35."]], "journey": "Both hormones are absorbed well by mouth. Drospirenone peaks in one to two hours, is highly protein bound (to albumin, not SHBG) and is broken down mostly without CYP enzymes, with a minor CYP3A4 role. Its half-life is about 30 hours. Ethinyl estradiol is absorbed quickly but undergoes first-pass sulfation in the gut wall and CYP3A4 metabolism in the liver, plus glucuronidation and enterohepatic recycling. Its half-life is about a day. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. Strong CYP3A4 inducers lower levels.", "sidefx": "Blood clots come mainly from estrogen increasing liver production of clotting factors, and smoking adds vascular damage, which is the boxed warning. High potassium comes from drospirenone blocking aldosterone receptors. Nausea, breast tenderness and headache reflect estrogen effects. Irregular bleeding is common in the first months while the lining adjusts. Gallbladder disease and rare liver adenomas relate to estrogen effects on the liver. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Drospirenone's resemblance to spironolactone means it acts a little like a mild diuretic, which was a marketing point for less bloating.", "src": ["Yaz (drospirenone and ethinyl estradiol) FDA label", "Yasmin FDA label", "FDA Drug Safety Communication on drospirenone, 2012"]}, "fezolinetant": {"origin": "Fezolinetant came from research into why hot flashes happen. In the 2000s and 2010s scientists found that a group of hypothalamic neurons called KNDy neurons (for kisspeptin, neurokinin B and dynorphin) become overactive when estrogen falls after menopause. Studies in people found that giving neurokinin B could trigger hot flashes, and blocking its NK3 receptor reduced them. Fezolinetant was discovered by the Belgian company Euroscreen (later Ogeda). Astellas Pharma acquired Ogeda in 2017 and developed the drug. In 2023 the FDA approved Veozah as the first nonhormonal drug that targets the brain's temperature control pathway for menopausal hot flashes. In 2024 a boxed warning for liver injury was added after postmarketing reports.", "timeline": [["2010s", "Research links neurokinin B and NK3 receptors to menopausal hot flashes."], ["2017", "Astellas acquires Ogeda, developer of fezolinetant."], ["2023", "FDA approves Veozah (fezolinetant)."], ["2024", "A boxed warning for hepatotoxicity is added."]], "cell": "In the infundibular (arcuate) nucleus of the hypothalamus, KNDy neurons release neurokinin B, which acts on NK3 receptors. Estrogen normally keeps these neurons in check. After menopause, low estrogen lets them grow larger and fire more, sending excess signals to the nearby thermoregulatory center in the preoptic area. This narrows the comfortable temperature range, so small rises in core temperature trigger heat loss responses: flushing, sweating and vasodilation. Fezolinetant is a selective NK3 receptor antagonist. By blocking neurokinin B binding, it calms KNDy neuron signaling to the temperature center, restoring a more normal thermoregulatory zone. It does not act on estrogen receptors.", "body": [["Hypothalamus", "Calming KNDy neuron signaling widens the comfortable temperature zone, reducing the number and severity of hot flashes within about a week."], ["Skin and blood vessels", "Fewer heat dissipation episodes mean less flushing and sweating, including night sweats."], ["Sleep", "Fewer night sweats can improve sleep, although insomnia is also listed as a side effect."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme and bilirubin rises can occur, and rare serious liver injury has been reported."], ["Uterus and breast", "Because it does not act on estrogen receptors, it does not stimulate the endometrium or breast tissue."]], "journey": "Fezolinetant is taken by mouth once daily and peaks in about an hour and a half. About half is bound to plasma proteins. It is metabolized mainly by CYP1A2 in the liver to an inactive metabolite, ES259564. Most leaves in the urine as metabolite. Its half-life is about ten hours. Drugs that strongly or moderately inhibit CYP1A2, such as fluvoxamine, raise its levels and are contraindicated.", "sidefx": "Liver injury is the main concern; the mechanism is not fully known, which is why liver tests are checked before starting and during the first months, and therapy is stopped at signs of liver injury. Abdominal pain, diarrhea, back pain and insomnia are mild common effects. Because it blocks part of the reproductive hormone pathway, it is not used in pregnancy. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Fezolinetant was the first approved drug to treat hot flashes by blocking a specific brain receptor rather than replacing hormones.", "src": ["Veozah (fezolinetant) FDA label", "Lederman S et al. SKYLIGHT 1, Lancet 2023"]}, "mifepristone": {"origin": "Mifepristone, first called RU-486, was synthesized in 1980 at the French company Roussel Uclaf by a team that included chemist Georges Teutsch. The team was searching for glucocorticoid receptor blockers. Endocrinologist Etienne-Emile Baulieu, a consultant to the company, recognized that the compound also blocked the progesterone receptor and could end early pregnancy. It was approved in France in 1988, then briefly pulled and restored by the French government within days. The FDA approved it as Mifeprex in 2000 for use with misoprostol, under a restricted distribution program that later became a REMS. In 2016 the labeling was updated to extend use to 70 days of gestation. In 2012 the FDA approved a higher-strength form, Korlym, to control high blood sugar in Cushing syndrome by blocking cortisol.", "timeline": [["1980", "Mifepristone (RU-486) is synthesized at Roussel Uclaf."], ["1988", "Mifepristone is approved in France."], ["2000", "FDA approves Mifeprex."], ["2012", "FDA approves Korlym for Cushing syndrome."], ["2016", "Labeling is updated to extend use through 70 days of gestation."]], "cell": "Mifepristone is a competitive antagonist at the progesterone receptor. Early pregnancy depends on progesterone to keep the decidua (the pregnancy-adapted uterine lining) intact, keep the cervix firm and keep the uterine muscle quiet. When mifepristone blocks the receptor, the decidua breaks down and the pregnancy detaches. The cervix softens and opens. The uterine muscle becomes more contractile and much more sensitive to prostaglandins. Misoprostol, a prostaglandin E1 analog taken one to two days later, then causes strong contractions that expel the pregnancy. At higher doses mifepristone also blocks the glucocorticoid receptor (type II), which is the basis for Korlym in Cushing syndrome, where blocking cortisol action improves high blood sugar.", "body": [["Uterus", "The lining breaks down and the uterus becomes primed to contract in response to misoprostol, leading to cramping and bleeding."], ["Cervix", "Softening and opening of the cervix allows the pregnancy to pass."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are common, especially after misoprostol."], ["Blood", "Heavy or prolonged bleeding can occur and rarely requires a transfusion or a procedure."], ["Infection defense", "Rare, serious infections, including Clostridium sordellii sepsis that can occur without fever, are a boxed warning."], ["Adrenal axis (Korlym)", "Blocking glucocorticoid receptors lowers cortisol effects and improves glucose in Cushing syndrome, but can cause adrenal insufficiency symptoms and low potassium."]], "journey": "Mifepristone is absorbed after swallowing and peaks in about an hour and a half. It is highly bound to alpha-1 acid glycoprotein and albumin. The liver metabolizes it through CYP3A4 by N-demethylation and hydroxylation, and some metabolites also bind the progesterone receptor. It is eliminated mainly in the feces. The terminal half-life is about 18 hours, and its tissue effects last longer. Because it inhibits CYP3A4 itself, it can raise levels of other drugs, a key issue with Korlym's daily use.", "sidefx": "Cramping and bleeding are the expected effects of decidual breakdown and prostaglandin-driven contractions. Heavy bleeding happens when expulsion is incomplete or prolonged. Rare serious infections may relate to bacteria entering the uterus during expulsion. An ectopic pregnancy is not treated by mifepristone and must be ruled out, because its symptoms can be mistaken for the expected cramping. With Korlym, glucocorticoid blockade can cause adrenal insufficiency symptoms, and the compensating rise in cortisol can act on mineralocorticoid receptors and lower potassium.", "fun": "Mifepristone was discovered while researchers were looking for a cortisol blocker, and that original cortisol-blocking effect later became an approved use of its own.", "src": ["Mifeprex (mifepristone) FDA label", "Korlym (mifepristone) FDA label", "Baulieu EE. Science 1989"]}, "somatropin": {"origin": "Growth hormone was first given to a child in 1958 by Maurice Raben in Boston, using hormone extracted from human pituitary glands taken at autopsy. Supply was very limited. In 1985 several people who had received pituitary-derived growth hormone developed Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease, a fatal prion illness, and the US stopped distributing it. That same year the FDA approved Protropin (somatrem) from Genentech, a recombinant growth hormone made in bacteria that had one extra amino acid. In 1987 Eli Lilly's Humatrope became the first recombinant somatropin with exactly the same 191-amino-acid sequence as natural human growth hormone. Many brands followed, and approved uses expanded from childhood growth hormone deficiency to Turner syndrome, Prader-Willi syndrome, chronic kidney disease and adult growth hormone deficiency.", "timeline": [["1958", "Maurice Raben first treats a child with pituitary-derived human growth hormone."], ["1985", "Pituitary-derived growth hormone is stopped in the US after Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease cases."], ["1985", "FDA approves the first recombinant growth hormone, Protropin."], ["1987", "Humatrope becomes the first recombinant somatropin identical to natural GH."]], "cell": "Somatropin binds the growth hormone receptor, a class I cytokine receptor. One hormone molecule binds two receptor units, bringing them together. This activates JAK2 kinases attached to the receptor, which phosphorylate the receptor and STAT5 transcription factors. STAT5 moves to the nucleus and turns on genes, most importantly IGF-1 in the liver and in local tissues. IGF-1 acts on its own tyrosine kinase receptor to drive chondrocyte proliferation at open growth plates, which lengthens bones. GH and IGF-1 together increase protein synthesis and lean mass. GH directly increases lipolysis in fat and reduces insulin action, raising blood glucose, which is called its diabetogenic effect. It also causes salt and water retention.", "body": [["Bones", "Growth plate chondrocytes multiply, increasing height in children whose plates are still open."], ["Muscle", "Protein synthesis rises and lean body mass increases."], ["Fat tissue", "Lipolysis increases and body fat decreases, especially around the abdomen."], ["Glucose metabolism", "GH opposes insulin, so blood glucose can rise and diabetes can be unmasked."], ["Fluid balance", "Salt and water retention cause edema and can cause carpal tunnel symptoms and joint pain, especially in adults."], ["Brain", "Fluid shifts can raise intracranial pressure, causing headache and papilledema, so eye exams are done."], ["Hips and spine", "Fast growth can contribute to slipped capital femoral epiphysis and worsen scoliosis in children."]], "journey": "Somatropin is a protein, so it is injected under the skin. It is absorbed slowly from the site, peaking in several hours, which makes its effective half-life longer than the short half-life of hormone given by vein. It is not significantly bound to proteins in a way that matters clinically. It is broken down by proteolysis in the liver and kidneys, and its fragments are filtered and reabsorbed by the kidney. IGF-1 levels rise over days and are used to monitor therapy.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from GH's normal actions in excess. Insulin resistance raises blood glucose. Fluid retention causes edema, joint pain and carpal tunnel symptoms. Raised intracranial pressure causes headache. Rapid bone growth can stress the hip growth plate and the spine. Because GH and IGF-1 promote cell growth, there are warnings about tumor recurrence. In Prader-Willi syndrome, sudden deaths have occurred in children with severe obesity or breathing problems, and in critically ill adults higher mortality was seen. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The 1985 crisis with pituitary-derived growth hormone made somatropin one of the earliest examples of genetic engineering solving a real drug safety problem.", "src": ["Humatrope (somatropin) FDA label", "Genotropin (somatropin) FDA label", "Raben MS. J Clin Endocrinol Metab 1958"]}, "pegvisomant": {"origin": "Pegvisomant was invented at Ohio University in the late 1980s by molecular biologist John Kopchick and his student Wen Chen. They were studying how growth hormone binds its receptor. They found that changing a single amino acid in GH, glycine at position 120 to arginine, created a molecule that bound the receptor but did not activate it. Later changes increased its receptor binding, and polyethylene glycol (PEG) chains were attached to make it last longer and to reduce antibody formation. The drug was developed by Sensus Drug Development and later Pharmacia, which became part of Pfizer. The FDA approved it as Somavert in 2003, the first growth hormone receptor antagonist, for acromegaly.", "timeline": [["1980s", "John Kopchick and Wen Chen discover a growth hormone antagonist at Ohio University."], ["1990", "Their research describing the antagonist is published."], ["2003", "FDA approves Somavert (pegvisomant) for acromegaly."]], "cell": "Growth hormone activates its receptor by binding two receptor units in sequence: first at site 1 with high affinity and then at site 2, which brings the receptors into an active dimer. Pegvisomant is a modified GH with several amino acid changes. Changes at site 1 make it bind even more tightly than natural GH, so it outcompetes the excess hormone. The change at site 2, glycine 120 to arginine, prevents the functional dimer from forming. The receptor is occupied but cannot activate JAK2-STAT5 signaling. As a result, the liver makes much less IGF-1, and the signs of acromegaly improve. It does not act on the pituitary tumor itself, so GH levels in the blood actually rise.", "body": [["Liver", "IGF-1 production falls, normalizing IGF-1 in most patients. Liver enzymes can rise, so liver tests are checked."], ["Soft tissue and joints", "Swelling of hands, feet and face improves and joint pain eases as IGF-1 falls."], ["Glucose metabolism", "Removing GH's anti-insulin effect improves insulin sensitivity, which can cause low blood sugar in people on diabetes drugs."], ["Pituitary", "The tumor is not treated, so it is followed with MRI for growth."], ["Skin", "Lipohypertrophy can form at repeated injection sites."]], "journey": "Pegvisomant is injected under the skin daily. The PEG chains slow its absorption and its clearance, so it takes several days to reach peak levels and has a half-life of about six days. It is broken down by proteolysis, and very little leaves through the kidneys. Because it interferes with GH lab tests and GH rises anyway, IGF-1 levels are used to guide therapy.", "sidefx": "Liver enzyme rises are the main concern and are monitored early in treatment, though the exact mechanism is not clear. Low blood sugar in people with diabetes comes from improved insulin sensitivity once GH action is blocked. Tumor growth is possible because the drug does not suppress the pituitary tumor. Injection site lipohypertrophy and allergic reactions can occur. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Pegvisomant is a rare example of a drug made by turning a hormone into its own blocker with a tiny change in its structure.", "src": ["Somavert (pegvisomant) FDA label", "Kopchick JJ et al. Growth hormone receptor antagonists, Endocr Rev 2002"]}, "ketoconazole": {"origin": "Ketoconazole was made at Janssen Pharmaceutica in Belgium, the company founded by Paul Janssen, which had built a series of imidazole antifungals such as miconazole in the late 1960s. Those early azoles worked on the skin but were poorly absorbed by mouth. Ketoconazole, synthesized in the mid 1970s, was the first azole that worked well as a pill against deep fungal infections. The FDA approved oral ketoconazole (Nizoral) in 1981, and for several years it was a key oral antifungal. Doctors soon noticed it also lowered testosterone and cortisol, which led to off-label use in Cushing syndrome and prostate cancer. Safer triazoles such as fluconazole and itraconazole largely replaced it. In 2013 the FDA added a boxed warning for liver injury and limited oral use to serious fungal infections when nothing else is suitable. The oral Nizoral brand is discontinued; topical creams and shampoos remain common.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Janssen develops early imidazole antifungals such as miconazole."], ["1970s", "Ketoconazole is synthesized at Janssen."], ["1981", "FDA approves oral ketoconazole (Nizoral)."], ["1990", "Fluconazole arrives and begins replacing ketoconazole for many uses."], ["2013", "FDA restricts oral ketoconazole and adds a hepatotoxicity boxed warning."]], "cell": "Ketoconazole's imidazole ring binds the heme iron of cytochrome P450 enzymes. In fungi it inhibits lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51), the enzyme that turns lanosterol into ergosterol, the main sterol of fungal membranes. Ergosterol falls and toxic 14-alpha-methylated sterols build up, so the membrane becomes leaky and membrane enzymes stop working, which halts fungal growth (fungistatic). Ketoconazole is not very selective. It also inhibits human P450 enzymes in steroid synthesis: side-chain cleavage (CYP11A1), 17-alpha-hydroxylase and 17,20-lyase (CYP17), and 11-beta-hydroxylase (CYP11B1). This lowers cortisol, testosterone and other androgens. It is also a strong inhibitor of liver CYP3A4, which causes many drug interactions.", "body": [["Fungal infections", "Fungal membranes lose ergosterol, so growth stops in systemic infections such as blastomycosis and histoplasmosis."], ["Adrenal glands", "Cortisol synthesis falls; this is used off-label for Cushing syndrome but can cause adrenal insufficiency."], ["Testes", "Lower testosterone production can cause gynecomastia, low libido and impotence."], ["Liver", "Serious liver injury, including fatal cases and transplants, is the boxed warning."], ["Heart", "QT prolongation occurs, especially when CYP3A4 blockade raises levels of other QT-prolonging drugs."], ["Drug handling", "Strong CYP3A4 inhibition raises levels of many drugs, including some statins, so many combinations are contraindicated."]], "journey": "Ketoconazole needs stomach acid to dissolve. Antacids, H2 blockers and PPIs reduce absorption, and an acidic drink helps in low-acid states. It peaks in one to two hours and is about 99 percent protein bound. It penetrates the brain poorly, so it is not used for fungal meningitis. The liver metabolizes it extensively, and the inactive products leave mostly in bile and feces. The half-life is biphasic, with an initial phase of about two hours and a terminal phase of about eight hours.", "sidefx": "Liver injury is thought to come from direct toxicity of the drug or its metabolites in liver cells and can be severe. Hormonal effects follow from blocking human steroid-making P450 enzymes: low cortisol can cause adrenal insufficiency, and low testosterone causes gynecomastia and sexual side effects. QT prolongation and torsades result from its own effect and from raising levels of other drugs through CYP3A4 inhibition. Nausea and vomiting are common. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped.", "fun": "Ketoconazole shampoo is a common treatment for dandruff, which is linked to the skin yeast Malassezia.", "src": ["Ketoconazole tablets FDA label", "FDA Drug Safety Communication on oral ketoconazole, 2013", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "cinacalcet": {"origin": "Cinacalcet came from the discovery of the calcium-sensing receptor. In 1993 Edward Brown, Steven Hebert and colleagues at Brigham and Women's Hospital and Harvard cloned this receptor from bovine parathyroid tissue. It showed that parathyroid cells sense blood calcium through a G protein coupled receptor. The biotechnology company NPS Pharmaceuticals in Utah then searched for small molecules that made this receptor more sensitive. These were called calcimimetics because they mimic the effect of calcium. NPS licensed the work to Amgen, and the FDA approved cinacalcet as Sensipar in 2004, the first calcimimetic. It gave dialysis patients with secondary hyperparathyroidism an option besides parathyroid surgery.", "timeline": [["1993", "Brown, Hebert and colleagues clone the calcium-sensing receptor."], ["2004", "FDA approves Sensipar (cinacalcet), the first calcimimetic."], ["2017", "Etelcalcetide, an IV calcimimetic, is approved for dialysis patients."]], "cell": "Parathyroid chief cells release PTH when calcium is low. They sense calcium through the calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR), a G protein coupled receptor on the cell surface. When calcium binds, CaSR signals through Gq and Gi, which lowers cAMP and raises intracellular calcium, and this suppresses PTH secretion and production. Cinacalcet is a positive allosteric modulator. It binds the receptor's transmembrane region, a site separate from where calcium binds, and makes the receptor respond to lower calcium concentrations. The gland now behaves as if calcium were higher than it really is, so PTH release falls within hours. Lower PTH means less calcium released from bone and less calcium reabsorbed by the kidney, so blood calcium falls.", "body": [["Parathyroid glands", "PTH secretion drops within hours, controlling secondary hyperparathyroidism in dialysis patients."], ["Blood calcium", "Calcium falls, which is the goal in parathyroid carcinoma but can cause hypocalcemia in dialysis patients."], ["Bone", "Lower PTH slows bone turnover; if PTH is pushed too low, adynamic bone disease can result."], ["Nerves and muscle", "Low calcium makes nerves more excitable, causing tingling, cramps, tetany and seizures."], ["Heart", "Hypocalcemia prolongs the QT interval and can worsen heart failure."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are very common, partly because CaSR is also expressed in the gut."]], "journey": "Cinacalcet is taken by mouth with food, which increases absorption. It peaks in two to six hours and is highly protein bound. The liver metabolizes it through CYP3A4, CYP2D6 and CYP1A2. It is also a strong CYP2D6 inhibitor, which raises levels of drugs such as some tricyclic antidepressants and flecainide. About 80 percent leaves in the urine as metabolites and the rest in feces. Its half-life is about 30 to 40 hours. PTH reaches its lowest point two to six hours after each dose.", "sidefx": "Hypocalcemia is the main danger, and it follows directly from lowering PTH; it can cause paresthesias, cramps, seizures and QT prolongation. Too much PTH suppression slows bone remodeling and causes adynamic bone disease. Nausea and vomiting are common. Upper GI bleeding has been reported. It is not used in CKD patients not on dialysis because hypocalcemia is more likely. Hypocalcemia is treated with calcium and vitamin D and by holding the drug.", "fun": "The calcium-sensing receptor is unusual because its main signal is a simple ion, calcium, rather than a hormone or neurotransmitter.", "src": ["Sensipar (cinacalcet) FDA label", "Brown EM et al. Nature 1993"]}, "calcitriol": {"origin": "Calcitriol is the active hormone made from vitamin D. In the early 1900s cod liver oil and sunlight were found to prevent rickets, and in 1922 Elmer McCollum named the anti-rickets factor vitamin D. Later it became clear that vitamin D itself is not active. It is first converted in the liver to 25-hydroxyvitamin D and then in the kidney to 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D. In 1971 the laboratory of Hector DeLuca at the University of Wisconsin, along with other groups such as those of Egon Kodicek and Anthony Norman, identified this active form. Synthetic calcitriol was developed and approved in the US as Rocaltrol in the late 1970s. It mattered most for people with kidney failure, whose damaged kidneys can no longer make the active hormone.", "timeline": [["1922", "Elmer McCollum names vitamin D as the anti-rickets factor."], ["1971", "The active form, 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, is identified."], ["1970s", "Synthetic calcitriol (Rocaltrol) is approved in the US."]], "cell": "Calcitriol is 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol, the fully activated form of vitamin D3. It enters cells and binds the vitamin D receptor (VDR), a nuclear receptor. VDR pairs with the retinoid X receptor and binds vitamin D response elements in DNA, changing gene expression. In intestinal cells it increases the apical calcium channel TRPV6, the carrier protein calbindin and the basolateral calcium pump, so more dietary calcium and phosphate are absorbed. In the kidney it increases calcium reabsorption. In bone it works with PTH to release calcium when needed. In the parathyroid glands it directly suppresses transcription of the PTH gene. Because it is already 1-alpha-hydroxylated, it does not need the kidney enzyme 1-alpha-hydroxylase, which is lost in kidney failure.", "body": [["Intestine", "Calcium and phosphate absorption rise, raising blood calcium."], ["Parathyroid glands", "PTH production falls, treating secondary hyperparathyroidism in kidney disease."], ["Bone", "Better calcium supply supports mineralization, and lower PTH reduces bone resorption."], ["Kidneys", "More calcium is reabsorbed, but high calcium in the urine can cause kidney stones and nephrocalcinosis."], ["Blood vessels and soft tissue", "If calcium and phosphate are both high, calcium phosphate can deposit in vessels and tissues."], ["Heart", "High calcium can cause arrhythmias, and it makes digoxin toxicity more likely."]], "journey": "Calcitriol is given as capsules, an oral solution or by IV at dialysis. Oral calcitriol is absorbed in the small intestine within hours and binds tightly to vitamin D binding protein in the blood. Its half-life is about five to eight hours, much shorter than stored vitamin D, which makes it easier to adjust. It is inactivated by 24-hydroxylation in the kidney and other tissues to calcitroic acid and is eliminated mainly in bile and feces. Its effect on calcium lasts a few days after stopping.", "sidefx": "High calcium is the main problem and comes from too much intestinal absorption. It causes weakness, headache, nausea, constipation and confusion, and in severe cases arrhythmias, especially with digoxin. High urine calcium can cause stones. When phosphate is also high, soft tissue and vascular calcification can occur. Oversuppressing PTH causes adynamic bone disease. Hypercalcemia is managed by stopping the drug, stopping calcium supplements and giving fluids.", "fun": "Because calcitriol acts through a nuclear receptor and controls gene expression, it is better described as a steroid hormone than a vitamin.", "src": ["Rocaltrol (calcitriol) FDA label", "Holick MF et al. Biochemistry 1971", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "burosumab": {"origin": "Burosumab grew out of research on rare rickets. In 2000 an international consortium found that mutations in the gene for fibroblast growth factor 23 (FGF23) cause autosomal dominant hypophosphatemic rickets. Researchers soon showed that FGF23 is a hormone made by bone that tells the kidneys to dump phosphate and to make less active vitamin D. In X-linked hypophosphatemia (XLH), the most common inherited form of rickets, PHEX gene mutations cause excess FGF23. In tumor-induced osteomalacia, small tumors secrete FGF23. Kyowa Kirin in Japan developed burosumab, a fully human antibody against FGF23, and partnered with Ultragenyx. The FDA approved Crysvita in 2018 for XLH, the first treatment targeting the cause of the disease, and in 2020 for tumor-induced osteomalacia.", "timeline": [["1995", "PHEX gene mutations are linked to X-linked hypophosphatemia."], ["2000", "FGF23 mutations are identified in autosomal dominant hypophosphatemic rickets."], ["2018", "FDA approves Crysvita (burosumab) for XLH."], ["2020", "Burosumab is approved for tumor-induced osteomalacia."]], "cell": "FGF23 is a hormone made by osteocytes. It binds FGF receptors together with the coreceptor klotho in the kidney. There it reduces the sodium-phosphate cotransporters NaPi-2a and NaPi-2c in the proximal tubule, so phosphate is lost in the urine. It also lowers 1-alpha-hydroxylase and raises 24-hydroxylase, so active vitamin D falls. In XLH and tumor-induced osteomalacia, too much FGF23 causes chronic low phosphate and poor bone mineralization. Burosumab is a fully human IgG1 antibody that binds FGF23 and stops it from activating its receptor. The kidneys keep more phosphate, and calcitriol production rises, which increases gut phosphate absorption. Higher phosphate allows bone to mineralize, healing rickets in children and osteomalacia in adults.", "body": [["Kidneys", "More phosphate is reabsorbed and more active vitamin D is made."], ["Bones", "With enough phosphate, bone matrix mineralizes properly, so rickets heals in children and osteomalacia improves in adults, reducing fractures and pain."], ["Growth", "Children's legs straighten and growth can improve as rickets heals."], ["Muscle", "Low phosphate weakens muscles, so correcting it can improve strength and stiffness."], ["Teeth", "Dental abscesses and toothache are common in XLH and were reported during treatment."], ["Nerves", "Restless legs syndrome can appear or worsen."]], "journey": "Burosumab is injected under the skin by a healthcare provider every few weeks. Being a large antibody, it is absorbed slowly through the lymphatics and peaks in about five to ten days. It stays mostly in the blood and is broken down like other antibodies into peptides and amino acids, not by liver enzymes or the kidneys. Its half-life is about 19 days. Fasting phosphate is checked before doses to guide therapy.", "sidefx": "Hyperphosphatemia can occur if FGF23 is blocked too strongly, raising the risk of calcium phosphate deposits in the kidney (nephrocalcinosis). That is why oral phosphate and active vitamin D are stopped before starting. Injection site reactions and allergic reactions reflect giving an antibody. Restless legs may be linked to changes in phosphate or vitamin D. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Before burosumab, XLH was treated with frequent doses of oral phosphate and calcitriol many times a day, which did not fix the cause.", "src": ["Crysvita (burosumab-twza) FDA label", "ADHR Consortium. Nat Genet 2000"]}, "romosozumab": {"origin": "Romosozumab came from studying families with unusually strong bones. Sclerosteosis, a rare disease seen especially in Afrikaner families in South Africa, causes very dense, overgrown bone. In 2001 researchers found that it is caused by loss of the SOST gene, which makes a protein called sclerostin. A related condition, van Buchem disease, involves the same pathway. People with these conditions rarely break bones, which suggested that blocking sclerostin could build bone. Amgen and UCB developed romosozumab, a humanized antibody against sclerostin. It was approved in Japan in early 2019 and by the FDA as Evenity in April 2019. In the ARCH trial, it reduced fractures more than alendronate but showed more heart attacks and strokes, which led to a boxed warning.", "timeline": [["2001", "Loss of the SOST gene is identified as the cause of sclerosteosis."], ["2017", "The ARCH trial reports fewer fractures than alendronate but more cardiovascular events."], ["2019", "FDA approves Evenity (romosozumab)."]], "cell": "Osteocytes deep in bone release sclerostin. Sclerostin binds LRP5 and LRP6, coreceptors needed for Wnt signaling in osteoblasts. When Wnt binds its Frizzled receptor with LRP5/6, beta-catenin escapes destruction and enters the nucleus to turn on bone-forming genes. Sclerostin blocks this, acting as a brake on bone formation. Romosozumab is a humanized IgG2 antibody that binds sclerostin and removes the brake. Wnt/beta-catenin signaling increases, osteoblasts multiply and lay down new bone, especially in the spine. At the same time osteoblasts make more osteoprotegerin and less RANKL, which reduces osteoclast activity and bone resorption. This dual effect, more formation and less breakdown, increases bone density quickly. The formation effect fades after about a year, so treatment lasts 12 monthly doses and is followed by an antiresorptive drug.", "body": [["Bone", "Bone density rises rapidly, especially at the spine, and vertebral and other fractures decrease."], ["Bone remodeling", "Formation markers rise early while resorption markers fall."], ["Heart and vessels", "Higher rates of heart attack, stroke and cardiovascular death were seen in one trial, which is the boxed warning; it is not started within a year of a heart attack or stroke."], ["Calcium balance", "Rapid new bone formation pulls calcium into bone, so blood calcium can fall, especially in severe kidney disease."], ["Jaw", "Rare osteonecrosis of the jaw can occur, as with other drugs that strongly reduce bone turnover."], ["Joints", "Joint pain, muscle spasms and headache are common side effects during the year of treatment."]], "journey": "Romosozumab is given as two injections under the skin once a month by a healthcare provider. As a large antibody it is absorbed slowly and peaks in about five days. It is broken down by normal protein catabolism, not by liver enzymes or kidneys. Its half-life is about 13 days. Part of its clearance depends on binding to sclerostin, the target itself.", "sidefx": "The cardiovascular signal may relate to sclerostin's presence in blood vessel walls, where it may help limit vascular calcification, but the mechanism is not proven. Low calcium follows from rapid bone building. Osteonecrosis of the jaw and atypical femur fractures relate to profound changes in bone turnover. Injection site reactions and hypersensitivity reflect giving an antibody. Calcium and vitamin D are given during therapy. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Romosozumab is an example of a drug inspired by people with a rare disease that gave them nearly unbreakable bones.", "src": ["Evenity (romosozumab-aqqg) FDA label", "Saag KG et al. ARCH, N Engl J Med 2017", "Balemans W et al. Hum Mol Genet 2001"]}, "ibandronate": {"origin": "Bisphosphonates were first made in Germany in the 1800s and were used in industry to soften water and prevent scale. In the 1960s Herbert Fleisch and colleagues in Switzerland studied pyrophosphate, a natural compound that stops calcium crystals from forming, and found that bisphosphonates, more stable lookalikes of pyrophosphate, also slowed bone resorption. Early drugs such as etidronate came first. Later nitrogen-containing versions were far more potent. Ibandronate was developed by Boehringer Mannheim, later part of Roche. The FDA approved daily Boniva tablets in 2003, a once-monthly tablet in 2005 and an every-three-month IV injection in 2006. Its main appeal was less frequent dosing. The Boniva brand is discontinued in the US, and generic tablets and injection remain.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Herbert Fleisch's group shows bisphosphonates slow bone resorption."], ["2003", "FDA approves Boniva (ibandronate) daily tablets."], ["2005", "A once-monthly ibandronate tablet is approved."], ["2006", "An every-three-month IV form is approved."]], "cell": "Ibandronate is a nitrogen-containing bisphosphonate. Its P-C-P backbone resembles pyrophosphate and binds strongly to hydroxyapatite, especially where bone is being actively resorbed. When osteoclasts dissolve bone, they release the drug and take it into the cell. Inside, it inhibits farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase in the mevalonate pathway. Without farnesyl and geranylgeranyl groups, small GTPases such as Ras, Rho and Rab cannot be attached to membranes. These proteins are needed for the osteoclast's ruffled border, cytoskeleton and vesicle traffic. The osteoclast stops resorbing and undergoes apoptosis. Bone formation continues for a while, so bone density increases.", "body": [["Bone", "Resorption slows and bone density rises, reducing vertebral fractures in postmenopausal osteoporosis."], ["Esophagus and stomach", "Tablets can irritate the esophagus and stomach, causing heartburn, esophagitis or ulcers, which is why the person stays upright and takes it with plain water."], ["Calcium balance", "Less bone breakdown can lower blood calcium, so low calcium is corrected before starting."], ["Muscles and joints", "Severe bone, joint or muscle pain can occur at any time during treatment."], ["Immune response", "The IV form can cause a short flu-like acute phase reaction, from activation of gamma-delta T cells."], ["Jaw and femur", "Rare osteonecrosis of the jaw and atypical femur fractures relate to long-term suppression of bone turnover."]], "journey": "Oral ibandronate is very poorly absorbed, less than 1 percent, and food, coffee, calcium and minerals reduce absorption further. That is why it is taken with plain water at least 60 minutes before anything else. About 40 to 50 percent of the absorbed drug quickly binds to bone, and the rest leaves unchanged in the urine. It is not metabolized. Unabsorbed drug leaves in the feces. Once in bone, it can stay for years and is slowly released during remodeling. The IV form skips the gut entirely.", "sidefx": "Esophageal irritation happens when the tablet sits in the esophagus and damages its lining. Hypocalcemia follows from reduced calcium release from bone. The acute phase reaction after IV doses comes from isoprenoid buildup that activates immune cells. Osteonecrosis of the jaw and atypical femur fractures are thought to relate to very low bone turnover, which limits repair of microdamage. IV doses can harm the kidneys if given too fast. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Bisphosphonates were used to remove scale from pipes and boilers before anyone knew they could protect bone.", "src": ["Boniva (ibandronate) FDA label", "Fleisch H et al. Science 1969", "Russell RGG. Bone 2011"]}, "risedronate": {"origin": "Risedronate is a nitrogen-containing bisphosphonate developed by Procter and Gamble, which had worked on bisphosphonates since the 1960s, partly because of their use in toothpaste and detergents to control calcium deposits. The company earlier introduced etidronate. Risedronate has a pyridine ring that makes it far more potent. The FDA approved Actonel in 1998 for Paget disease of bone and in 2000 for postmenopausal and glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis. Weekly and monthly versions followed, and in 2010 the FDA approved Atelvia, a delayed-release form taken right after breakfast. In large trials, risedronate lowered vertebral, nonvertebral and hip fractures.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Procter and Gamble begins bisphosphonate research."], ["1998", "FDA approves Actonel (risedronate) for Paget disease."], ["2000", "Risedronate is approved for osteoporosis."], ["2010", "FDA approves Atelvia, a delayed-release form taken after breakfast."]], "cell": "Risedronate binds hydroxyapatite on the bone surface, especially at active remodeling sites. Osteoclasts dissolving bone release and engulf the drug. Inside the osteoclast, its nitrogen-containing pyridinyl group lets it potently inhibit farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase in the mevalonate pathway. This prevents prenylation of small GTPases, including Ras, Rho, Rac and Rab, which osteoclasts need to form the ruffled border, organize their cytoskeleton and move vesicles. The osteoclasts stop resorbing bone and many undergo apoptosis. Bone remodeling slows, more bone is preserved, and density gradually rises. In Paget disease, the disordered, overactive bone turnover is calmed.", "body": [["Bone", "Resorption slows, bone density increases and vertebral, nonvertebral and hip fractures decrease."], ["Paget disease", "Overactive bone turnover quiets, alkaline phosphatase falls, and pain and deformity progression improve."], ["Esophagus and stomach", "Tablets can irritate the upper GI tract, causing heartburn, esophagitis or ulcers if not taken upright with water."], ["Calcium balance", "Less bone breakdown can lower blood calcium, so low calcium is corrected before starting."], ["Muscles and joints", "Back pain, joint pain and occasionally severe musculoskeletal pain can occur."], ["Jaw and femur", "Rare osteonecrosis of the jaw and atypical femur fractures relate to long-term suppression of bone turnover."]], "journey": "Risedronate is poorly absorbed, less than 1 percent of an oral dose, and food, calcium, iron and other minerals lower absorption further, so the immediate-release form is taken with plain water 30 minutes before food. Atelvia is designed to release lower in the gut and includes a chelating agent so it can be taken after breakfast. About half the absorbed drug quickly binds to bone; the rest leaves unchanged in the urine. It is not metabolized. Its terminal half-life is very long, hundreds of hours, because it is slowly released from bone.", "sidefx": "Upper GI irritation comes from direct contact of the tablet with the esophageal and stomach lining. Low calcium follows from blocked bone resorption. Bone, joint and muscle pain can occur for unclear reasons. Osteonecrosis of the jaw and atypical femur fractures are linked to suppressed remodeling that limits repair of tiny cracks. The drug is not used in severe kidney impairment. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Procter and Gamble's interest in bisphosphonates started with fighting tartar on teeth and scale in washing machines.", "src": ["Actonel (risedronate) FDA label", "Atelvia (risedronate delayed-release) FDA label", "Russell RGG. Bone 2011"]}, "teprotumumab": {"origin": "Teprotumumab started life as a cancer drug. It was created as a fully human antibody against the insulin-like growth factor-1 receptor (IGF-1R) by Genmab, and Roche tested it in tumors in the 2000s, but those trials were stopped. Meanwhile, endocrinologist Terry Smith, later at the University of Michigan, showed that IGF-1R works together with the TSH receptor on orbital fibroblasts in thyroid eye disease, the condition where the tissue behind the eyes swells and pushes them forward. River Vision Development took the antibody into eye disease trials, and Horizon Therapeutics later completed development. The FDA approved Tepezza in January 2020 as the first drug for thyroid eye disease. Horizon was acquired by Amgen in 2023. The same year, the label was updated with a warning about hearing loss, some of it permanent.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Teprotumumab is tested by Roche as a cancer treatment."], ["2010s", "Research shows IGF-1R and TSH receptor crosstalk drives thyroid eye disease."], ["2017", "A phase 2 trial shows large reductions in eye bulging."], ["2020", "FDA approves Tepezza, the first drug for thyroid eye disease."], ["2023", "Labeling adds a warning about hearing impairment."]], "cell": "In thyroid eye disease, autoantibodies activate the TSH receptor on fibroblasts behind the eye. These fibroblasts also express IGF-1R, and the two receptors physically interact and amplify each other's signals. Activated fibroblasts produce large amounts of hyaluronan, which draws water, and some turn into fat cells, so orbital fat and eye muscles swell. Teprotumumab is a fully human IgG1 antibody that binds IGF-1R, blocks IGF-1 and IGF-2 from binding, and causes the receptor to be internalized and degraded. This blocks IGF-1R signaling and the IGF-1R and TSH receptor crosstalk. Fibroblasts make less hyaluronan and fewer fat cells, so swelling behind the eyes decreases, the eyes move back and double vision improves.", "body": [["Eyes and orbit", "Swelling of orbital fat and muscles decreases, reducing proptosis, double vision and inflammation."], ["Glucose metabolism", "Blocking IGF-1R throughout the body causes insulin resistance and high blood sugar, especially in people with diabetes."], ["Ears", "IGF-1 signaling helps maintain the inner ear, so blocking it can cause hearing loss, tinnitus or ear fullness, sometimes permanent."], ["Muscles", "Muscle spasms are common, possibly because IGF-1 supports muscle."], ["Gut", "Inflammatory bowel disease can flare, and diarrhea and nausea are common."], ["Hair and skin", "Hair loss and dry skin can occur, likely because IGF-1 signaling supports hair follicles and skin."], ["Pregnancy", "IGF-1R is essential for fetal growth, so the drug can harm a fetus and contraception is used during and after treatment."]], "journey": "Teprotumumab is given by IV infusion every three weeks for eight doses. As a large antibody it stays mostly in the blood and extracellular fluid. It is broken down by normal protein catabolism in the reticuloendothelial system, not by liver enzymes or kidneys. Its half-life is about 20 days. Improvement in eye bulging is usually seen within weeks, and some benefit persists after the course ends, though some people relapse.", "sidefx": "High blood sugar comes from blocking IGF-1R, which shares signaling with the insulin receptor and helps tissues take up glucose. Hearing problems likely arise because IGF-1 supports cochlear hair cells. Muscle spasms, hair loss and taste changes reflect IGF-1R roles in many tissues. IBD flares and infusion reactions can occur. Fetal harm follows from IGF-1R's role in growth. There is no antidote; reactions are managed by stopping or slowing the infusion.", "fun": "Teprotumumab failed as a cancer drug but became the first approved treatment for thyroid eye disease, a striking case of drug repurposing.", "src": ["Tepezza (teprotumumab-trbw) FDA label", "Smith TJ et al. N Engl J Med 2017", "Douglas RS et al. OPTIC, N Engl J Med 2020"]}, "esomeprazole": {"origin": "Esomeprazole is the S-isomer of omeprazole. Omeprazole was discovered at the Swedish company Astra (Hassle) in the late 1970s, after scientists learned that the H+/K+ ATPase, the proton pump, was the final step in acid secretion. Omeprazole was launched in Europe in 1988 and in the US in 1989 as Losec, later Prilosec, and became one of the best-selling drugs in the world. Omeprazole is a mix of two mirror-image forms. AstraZeneca found that the S form is cleared more slowly by CYP2C19 in the liver, so it reaches higher, more consistent levels. The FDA approved esomeprazole as Nexium in 2001, marketed as the purple pill. An IV form followed, and Nexium 24HR became available over the counter in 2014.", "timeline": [["1979", "Omeprazole is synthesized at Astra Hassle in Sweden."], ["1989", "Omeprazole is approved in the US."], ["2001", "FDA approves Nexium (esomeprazole)."], ["2014", "Esomeprazole becomes available over the counter in the US."]], "cell": "Parietal cells in the stomach secrete acid using the H+/K+ ATPase, which pumps hydrogen ions into the stomach in exchange for potassium. This is the final common step for all acid stimuli: histamine, acetylcholine and gastrin. Esomeprazole is a prodrug and a weak base. It is absorbed into the blood and concentrates in the very acidic secretory canaliculi of active parietal cells. There, acid converts it into a reactive sulfenamide. The sulfenamide forms covalent disulfide bonds with cysteine residues on the outside of the pump, permanently blocking it. Acid secretion returns only when the cell makes new pumps, which takes about a day or more. Because only active pumps are hit, it works best when taken before a meal, and full effect builds over several days.", "body": [["Stomach", "Acid production drops sharply, allowing ulcers and erosive esophagitis to heal and relieving heartburn."], ["Esophagus", "Less acid refluxes, so inflamed lining heals."], ["Gastrin and stomach lining", "Low acid raises gastrin, which can cause fundic gland polyps and rebound acid when stopped."], ["Gut bacteria", "Less acid lets more bacteria survive, which raises the risk of C. difficile diarrhea."], ["Nutrient absorption", "Low acid reduces B12 release from food and can reduce magnesium absorption with long-term use."], ["Bones", "Long-term, high-dose use is linked to more hip, wrist and spine fractures."], ["Kidneys", "Acute interstitial nephritis is a rare immune reaction."]], "journey": "Esomeprazole is acid sensitive, so it is given as enteric-coated granules that dissolve in the small intestine. It is absorbed quickly and peaks in about one and a half hours. It is about 97 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it through CYP2C19 and CYP3A4; the S form relies less on CYP2C19 than omeprazole does, so levels are more predictable. About 80 percent leaves in the urine as inactive metabolites. Its plasma half-life is only about one to one and a half hours, but because it binds the pump permanently, acid suppression lasts about a day.", "sidefx": "Most long-term effects come from low stomach acid: more C. difficile infection because acid normally kills swallowed bacteria, less B12 absorption, and low magnesium from reduced gut magnesium uptake. Fractures may relate to reduced calcium absorption. Raised gastrin causes polyps. Acute interstitial nephritis and lupus are immune reactions. It inhibits CYP2C19, which can reduce activation of clopidogrel. Headache and diarrhea are common. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Nexium's patent was a classic example of a chiral switch, in which a company sells a single isomer of an existing drug that was a mix.", "src": ["Nexium (esomeprazole) FDA label", "Olbe L et al. Nat Rev Drug Discov 2003", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "lansoprazole": {"origin": "Lansoprazole was developed by Takeda Chemical Industries in Japan in the 1980s, as the second proton pump inhibitor after omeprazole. Takeda chemists modified the benzimidazole structure by adding a trifluoroethoxy group on the pyridine ring, which changed its potency and stability. In the US it was developed by TAP Pharmaceuticals, a joint venture between Takeda and Abbott. The FDA approved Prevacid in 1995. It later became available as an orally disintegrating tablet, Prevacid SoluTab, useful for people who have trouble swallowing and for tube feeding. Prevacid 24HR became available over the counter in 2009.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Takeda develops lansoprazole in Japan."], ["1995", "FDA approves Prevacid (lansoprazole)."], ["2009", "Lansoprazole becomes available over the counter in the US."]], "cell": "Lansoprazole is a substituted benzimidazole prodrug. It is absorbed into the blood and, as a weak base, concentrates in the acidic canaliculi of active parietal cells. Acid converts it into a reactive sulfenamide that forms covalent disulfide bonds with cysteine residues of the H+/K+ ATPase. This permanently shuts down the pump, the final step in acid secretion whether the stimulus is histamine, acetylcholine or gastrin. New pumps must be made for acid production to return. Because only active pumps are hit, it works best before a meal when pumps are being switched on, and full effect builds over a few days of daily use.", "body": [["Stomach", "Acid secretion drops sharply, helping ulcers and erosive esophagitis heal and relieving heartburn."], ["H. pylori", "Raising stomach pH makes antibiotics such as amoxicillin and clarithromycin work better against H. pylori."], ["Gastrin", "Low acid increases gastrin, which can cause fundic gland polyps and rebound acid after stopping."], ["Gut bacteria", "Less acid lets more bacteria survive, raising C. difficile diarrhea risk."], ["Nutrients", "Long-term use can reduce B12 and magnesium absorption."], ["Bones", "Long-term high-dose use is linked to fractures."]], "journey": "Lansoprazole is protected from stomach acid by enteric-coated granules, either in capsules or in the orally disintegrating tablet, which can be dispersed for an oral syringe or tube. Food lowers absorption, so it is taken before eating. It peaks in under two hours and is about 97 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 into inactive metabolites, which leave in bile and urine. The plasma half-life is under two hours, but because the pump block is permanent, acid suppression lasts longer than a day.", "sidefx": "Low acid explains most long-term effects: C. difficile infection, B12 deficiency, low magnesium and possibly fractures. Raised gastrin causes fundic gland polyps. Acute interstitial nephritis and lupus are rare immune reactions. The SoluTab contains phenylalanine, which matters in phenylketonuria. Diarrhea, abdominal pain and headache are common. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Lansoprazole has been studied as a possible treatment for tuberculosis because, after metabolism, it has activity against Mycobacterium tuberculosis in the lab.", "src": ["Prevacid (lansoprazole) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "vonoprazan": {"origin": "Vonoprazan belongs to a newer class called potassium-competitive acid blockers, or P-CABs. Scientists had known since the 1980s that the stomach's proton pump could be blocked reversibly at its potassium site, but early P-CABs had problems such as liver toxicity. Takeda in Japan discovered vonoprazan, a pyrrole derivative, and it was approved in Japan in late 2014, where it became widely used for ulcers, reflux and H. pylori treatment. Phathom Pharmaceuticals licensed it for the US. The FDA approved Voquezna Triple Pak and Dual Pak for H. pylori in 2022, and vonoprazan tablets for healing and maintaining erosive esophagitis in 2023, after a delay caused by testing for nitrosamine impurities. Approval for heartburn from non-erosive reflux followed in 2024.", "timeline": [["2014", "Vonoprazan is approved in Japan."], ["2022", "FDA approves Voquezna Triple Pak and Dual Pak for H. pylori."], ["2023", "FDA approves vonoprazan for erosive esophagitis."], ["2024", "Vonoprazan is approved for heartburn in non-erosive reflux disease."]], "cell": "Like PPIs, vonoprazan targets the H+/K+ ATPase, the final pump that secretes acid from parietal cells. But it works differently. It binds ionically and reversibly at or near the pump's potassium binding site, competing with potassium, so the pump cannot complete its cycle of exchanging potassium for hydrogen ions. It does not need acid to activate it, and it can block pumps that are resting as well as active ones. Its high pKa means it becomes protonated and trapped in the acidic secretory canaliculi, where it builds up to high concentrations and stays a long time. This gives fast, strong acid suppression from the first dose that does not depend on meal timing and lasts through the night.", "body": [["Stomach", "Acid falls quickly and stays low for about a day, helping erosive esophagitis heal and relieving heartburn."], ["H. pylori", "Very low acid helps amoxicillin and clarithromycin kill H. pylori more reliably."], ["Gastrin", "Low acid raises gastrin, often more than with PPIs; long-term effects of this are less studied."], ["Gut bacteria", "Less acid lets more bacteria survive, raising C. difficile diarrhea risk."], ["Nutrients and bones", "As with PPIs, long-term use may reduce B12 and magnesium absorption and is linked to fractures."], ["Kidneys", "Acute interstitial nephritis is a rare immune reaction."]], "journey": "Vonoprazan is taken by mouth with or without food and is acid stable, so it does not need an enteric coating. It peaks in about one and a half to three hours and is about 85 to 88 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP3A4, with help from CYP2B6, CYP2C19, CYP2D6 and SULT2A1. Unlike PPIs, its levels depend little on CYP2C19 genetics. Metabolites leave mainly in the urine. Its half-life is about seven hours, much longer than PPIs, and it stays in parietal cells even longer.", "sidefx": "Most risks mirror PPIs because they come from low stomach acid: C. difficile infection, B12 and magnesium deficiency, fundic gland polyps and possibly fractures. Interstitial nephritis and severe skin reactions are immune reactions. When given in the H. pylori packs, the clarithromycin adds QT prolongation and many CYP3A4 drug interactions. Common effects are gastritis, diarrhea, bloating and nausea. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Because vonoprazan does not need acid activation, it is one of the few acid blockers that works the same whether taken before or after eating.", "src": ["Voquezna (vonoprazan) FDA label", "Laine L et al. Gastroenterology 2023"]}, "cimetidine": {"origin": "Cimetidine is a famous example of rational drug design. In the 1960s pharmacologist James Black, working at Smith Kline and French in Welwyn, England, reasoned that histamine must act through a second receptor type in the stomach, because classic antihistamines did not stop acid. His team, including chemists C. Robin Ganellin and Graham Durant, started with the histamine molecule and changed it step by step. They made burimamide, which proved the H2 receptor existed in 1972, then metiamide, which caused low white cell counts, and finally cimetidine. It was launched in the UK in 1976 and approved by the FDA in 1977 as Tagamet. It transformed ulcer treatment, reducing the need for surgery, and became one of the first drugs to reach a billion dollars in yearly sales. James Black shared the 1988 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine.", "timeline": [["1972", "Black and colleagues define the H2 receptor using burimamide."], ["1976", "Cimetidine is launched in the UK."], ["1977", "FDA approves Tagamet (cimetidine)."], ["1988", "James Black shares the Nobel Prize for his work on drug design."], ["1995", "Cimetidine becomes available over the counter in the US."]], "cell": "Histamine released from enterochromaffin-like cells binds H2 receptors on parietal cells. H2 receptors are Gs coupled, so activation raises cAMP and activates protein kinase A, which helps move proton pumps to the cell surface and switch them on. Cimetidine competitively and reversibly blocks the H2 receptor. With less cAMP, the proton pump is less active, and basal, nocturnal and food-stimulated acid secretion fall. It is especially good at reducing nighttime acid, which is driven mostly by histamine. Its imidazole ring, borrowed from histamine, also binds the heme iron of several cytochrome P450 enzymes, including CYP1A2, CYP2D6 and CYP3A4, inhibiting them. It also blocks androgen receptors and competes with creatinine for secretion in the kidney tubules.", "body": [["Stomach", "Acid production falls, which helps duodenal and gastric ulcers heal and relieves heartburn."], ["Drug metabolism", "Inhibition of liver P450 enzymes raises levels of drugs such as warfarin, phenytoin, theophylline and lidocaine."], ["Hormones", "Androgen receptor blockade and higher prolactin can cause gynecomastia and impotence, especially at high doses."], ["Brain", "Confusion, agitation and hallucinations can occur in older or severely ill people and in kidney failure because the drug enters the brain."], ["Kidneys", "Blocking creatinine secretion raises serum creatinine slightly without true kidney damage."], ["Blood", "Rarely, it can cause low white cell counts or aplastic anemia."]], "journey": "Cimetidine is absorbed well after swallowing and peaks within about 45 to 90 minutes. Protein binding is low. Part of it is metabolized in the liver to a sulfoxide, but much is excreted unchanged in the urine. The half-life is about two hours and gets longer in kidney disease, which is why doses are reduced and why confusion is more common when kidney function is poor.", "sidefx": "Drug interactions come from its imidazole ring blocking P450 enzymes, which slows breakdown of many drugs. Gynecomastia and impotence come from androgen receptor blockade and raised prolactin. Confusion reflects H2 or other effects in the brain when levels are high. A small rise in creatinine is from blocked tubular secretion. Rapid IV doses were historically linked to bradycardia and low blood pressure, possibly through cardiac H2 receptors. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Cimetidine's discovery helped establish the idea that a drug can be designed by changing a natural signal molecule until it blocks its own receptor.", "src": ["Tagamet (cimetidine) FDA label", "Black JW et al. Nature 1972", "Nobel Lecture, J. W. Black, 1988"]}, "dronabinol": {"origin": "Dronabinol is synthetic delta-9-tetrahydrocannabinol (THC), the main psychoactive compound in cannabis. Cannabis has been used for thousands of years, but its active compound was not identified until 1964, when Raphael Mechoulam and Yehiel Gaoni at the Weizmann Institute in Israel isolated THC and worked out its structure. In the 1970s and 1980s cancer patients reported that cannabis eased chemotherapy nausea. The FDA approved dronabinol capsules in sesame oil as Marinol in 1985 for chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting, and in 1992 for weight loss and poor appetite in AIDS. Marinol was moved from Schedule II to Schedule III in 1999. An oral solution, Syndros, was approved in 2016 and is Schedule II. Research on how THC works led to the discovery of the cannabinoid receptors and the body's own endocannabinoids.", "timeline": [["1964", "Mechoulam and Gaoni isolate and identify THC."], ["1985", "FDA approves Marinol for chemotherapy nausea and vomiting."], ["1990", "The CB1 cannabinoid receptor is cloned."], ["1992", "Dronabinol is approved for AIDS-related anorexia; anandamide, an endocannabinoid, is discovered."], ["1999", "Marinol is moved to Schedule III."], ["2016", "FDA approves Syndros oral solution."]], "cell": "Dronabinol is a partial agonist at cannabinoid CB1 and CB2 receptors, which are Gi/o protein coupled receptors. CB1 is abundant in the brain, including the hippocampus, basal ganglia, cerebellum, hypothalamus and the brainstem vomiting centers. Activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase, opens potassium channels and closes calcium channels on nerve terminals, which reduces release of neurotransmitters. In the brainstem's dorsal vagal complex, this reduces nausea and vomiting signals. In the hypothalamus and reward circuits, it increases appetite and makes food more rewarding. In the hippocampus and cortex, it alters memory, attention and perception, causing the high. CB2 receptors are mainly on immune cells and mediate some anti-inflammatory effects.", "body": [["Brainstem", "Reduced activity in vomiting centers lowers chemotherapy nausea and vomiting."], ["Hypothalamus", "Appetite increases, helping people with AIDS wasting gain weight."], ["Mind and mood", "Euphoria, relaxation, anxiety, paranoia and in some people psychosis can occur."], ["Memory and coordination", "Short-term memory, attention and coordination are impaired, so driving is affected."], ["Heart and vessels", "Heart rate can rise and blood pressure can drop on standing, causing dizziness or fainting."], ["Gut", "Paradoxically, long-term heavy cannabinoid use can cause cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome."]], "journey": "Dronabinol is absorbed well by mouth, but heavy first-pass metabolism in the liver means only 10 to 20 percent reaches the blood. Effects begin in 30 minutes to an hour and peak in two to four hours. It is very fat soluble, so it spreads into fat tissue and is released slowly. The liver converts it through CYP2C9 and CYP3A4 to 11-hydroxy-THC, which is also active, then to inactive products. These leave mostly through bile and feces with some in urine, and enterohepatic recycling prolongs its stay. The terminal half-life is more than a day.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from CB1 activation in the brain: dizziness, sleepiness, euphoria, anxiety, paranoia and impaired thinking. Older adults are more sensitive and more likely to fall. Fast heart rate and blood pressure changes come from effects on the autonomic system. Seizures can occur in people with seizure disorders. Abuse potential reflects reward circuit activation. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "The word anandamide, the body's own cannabinoid, comes from the Sanskrit word ananda, meaning bliss.", "src": ["Marinol (dronabinol) FDA label", "Gaoni Y, Mechoulam R. J Am Chem Soc 1964", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "linaclotide": {"origin": "Linaclotide was inspired by a toxin that causes travelers' diarrhea. Some strains of E. coli make a heat-stable enterotoxin (STa) that binds guanylate cyclase-C on intestinal cells and causes them to pour fluid into the gut. In 1992 researchers discovered guanylin, a natural hormone that uses the same receptor, and later uroguanylin. Scientists at Microbia, a Massachusetts company that later became Ironwood Pharmaceuticals, designed a small, stable peptide based on the toxin. The goal was to cause mild fluid secretion in people with constipation. Ironwood developed it with Forest Laboratories, and the FDA approved Linzess in 2012 for IBS with constipation and chronic idiopathic constipation. In 2023 it was approved for functional constipation in children aged six and older.", "timeline": [["1992", "Guanylin, a natural hormone acting on guanylate cyclase-C, is discovered."], ["2012", "FDA approves Linzess (linaclotide)."], ["2023", "Linaclotide is approved for pediatric functional constipation."]], "cell": "Linaclotide is a 14-amino-acid peptide with three disulfide bonds that keep it stable in the gut. It binds guanylate cyclase-C on the luminal surface of intestinal epithelial cells. This activates the enzyme, which converts GTP into cyclic GMP. Rising cGMP inside the cell activates protein kinase G II, which phosphorylates and opens the CFTR chloride channel. Chloride and bicarbonate flow into the gut lumen, and sodium and water follow. More fluid softens the stool and speeds transit. Some cGMP is pumped out of the cell into the tissue below, where it reduces the firing of pain-sensing nerves in the colon, which helps abdominal pain in IBS. It also inhibits the sodium-hydrogen exchanger, reducing sodium absorption.", "body": [["Small and large intestine", "More fluid in the bowel makes stools softer and more frequent, often within the first week."], ["Colon nerves", "Less sensitivity of pain-sensing nerves reduces abdominal pain and bloating in IBS-C."], ["Fluid balance", "Too much secretion causes diarrhea, which can lead to dehydration, especially in young children."], ["Gut gas", "Flatulence and bloating can occur as stool transit changes."]], "journey": "Linaclotide is taken by mouth on an empty stomach and acts inside the gut. It is barely absorbed, so plasma levels are usually too low to measure. In the small intestine it is trimmed into an active metabolite, and both are then broken down into smaller peptides and amino acids within the gut. What remains leaves in the feces. Because almost none reaches the blood, it has no meaningful systemic metabolism or drug interactions.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea is the main side effect and is the same fluid secretion that treats constipation, just too much of it. Severe diarrhea can lead to dehydration and electrolyte loss. The boxed warning against use in children under two comes from deaths from dehydration in very young mice, which have more guanylate cyclase-C in their intestine. There is no antidote; diarrhea is managed by stopping the drug and rehydration.", "fun": "Linaclotide is a rare drug whose design was based directly on a bacterial toxin that causes diarrhea.", "src": ["Linzess (linaclotide) FDA label", "Currie MG et al. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA 1992"]}, "lubiprostone": {"origin": "Lubiprostone was developed by Sucampo Pharmaceuticals, founded in 1996 by Ryuji Ueno and Sachiko Kuno, a married pair of Japanese scientists. They studied prostones, compounds made from prostaglandin metabolites. Lubiprostone is a bicyclic fatty acid derived from prostaglandin E1. They found it caused fluid secretion in the gut without the strong muscle and blood vessel effects of natural prostaglandins. Sucampo partnered with Takeda, and the FDA approved Amitiza in 2006 for chronic idiopathic constipation. It became the first approved chloride channel activator. Approval for IBS with constipation in women came in 2008, and for opioid-induced constipation in 2013.", "timeline": [["1996", "Ryuji Ueno and Sachiko Kuno found Sucampo Pharmaceuticals."], ["2006", "FDA approves Amitiza for chronic idiopathic constipation."], ["2008", "Lubiprostone is approved for IBS-C in women."], ["2013", "Lubiprostone is approved for opioid-induced constipation."]], "cell": "Lubiprostone acts on the luminal surface of intestinal epithelial cells. It activates ClC-2 chloride channels in the apical membrane, which lets chloride move from the cell into the gut lumen. Sodium follows through the spaces between cells to keep charge balanced, and water follows the salt. The result is a chloride-rich fluid that softens stool, adds bulk and speeds transit. This effect does not depend on protein kinase A. Because it acts on the lining rather than on gut muscles or nerves, its effect on opioid-induced constipation does not need opioid receptor blockade. It may also help restore the barrier function of the intestinal lining.", "body": [["Intestine", "More fluid in the bowel makes stools softer and more frequent, often within one to two days."], ["Stomach", "Nausea is common, likely related to its prostaglandin-like origin, and is reduced by taking it with food."], ["Lungs", "Some people feel short of breath or chest tightness within an hour of a dose, usually short-lived."], ["Blood pressure", "Rarely, syncope and hypotension occur soon after a dose."], ["Pregnancy", "Fetal loss in animal studies means pregnancy testing and contraception are discussed."]], "journey": "Lubiprostone is taken by mouth with food. Very little of the parent drug reaches the blood because it is quickly reduced by carbonyl reductase in the stomach and jejunum into its main metabolite, M3, which is used to measure exposure. M3 peaks in about an hour and has a half-life of about an hour. The process does not use CYP450 enzymes, so liver enzyme interactions are minimal. Metabolites leave in urine and feces.", "sidefx": "Nausea is the most common problem and reflects its prostaglandin-derived chemistry. Diarrhea is excess fluid secretion. Shortness of breath, chest discomfort, syncope and low blood pressure soon after a dose are not fully explained but may reflect short-lived prostaglandin-like actions on smooth muscle. Headache and bloating can occur. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The prostone in lubiprostone points to its origin: it is built from a prostaglandin E1 metabolite rather than from a classic laxative.", "src": ["Amitiza (lubiprostone) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "prucalopride": {"origin": "Prucalopride came after earlier serotonin 5-HT4 drugs ran into heart safety problems. Cisapride, a 5-HT4 agonist from Janssen, was widely used for reflux and gut motility but was removed from the US market in 2000 because it blocked hERG potassium channels in the heart and caused fatal arrhythmias. Tegaserod was suspended in 2007 over cardiovascular concerns. Janssen researchers in Belgium designed prucalopride, a dihydrobenzofurancarboxamide, to be highly selective for 5-HT4 receptors with very low affinity for hERG channels and other serotonin receptors. It was approved in Europe in 2009 as Resolor. The FDA approved it as Motegrity in 2018 for chronic idiopathic constipation in adults.", "timeline": [["2000", "Cisapride is removed from the US market over cardiac arrhythmias."], ["2007", "Tegaserod is suspended in the US over cardiovascular concerns."], ["2009", "Prucalopride is approved in Europe."], ["2018", "FDA approves Motegrity (prucalopride)."]], "cell": "Prucalopride is a highly selective agonist at 5-HT4 receptors, which are Gs coupled receptors found on neurons of the enteric nervous system. Activation raises cAMP in these neurons and increases release of acetylcholine at their terminals. Acetylcholine then acts on muscarinic receptors on gut smooth muscle. This strengthens the peristaltic reflex and triggers high-amplitude propagating contractions in the colon, the strong waves that move stool toward the rectum. Colonic transit becomes faster and spontaneous bowel movements increase. Its low affinity for hERG channels and 5-HT1B receptors is what separates it from older drugs that caused arrhythmias or vascular effects.", "body": [["Colon", "Stronger propagating contractions move stool faster, so bowel movements become more frequent, often starting on the first day."], ["Upper gut", "It can also speed stomach and small intestine motility, which may cause nausea or abdominal cramps."], ["Brain", "Headache and dizziness are common early effects that tend to fade."], ["Mood", "Suicidal thoughts and worsening depression have been reported, so mood changes are watched during treatment."], ["Heart", "Designed to avoid hERG channel block, it did not show the arrhythmia risk seen with cisapride in studies."]], "journey": "Prucalopride is absorbed well by mouth, with bioavailability over 90 percent, and peaks in two to three hours. Food does not affect it. Only about 30 percent is bound to plasma proteins. It undergoes little metabolism, with a minor role for CYP3A4. About 60 percent leaves unchanged in the urine through filtration and active secretion, and it is a P-glycoprotein substrate. Its half-life is about one day, supporting once-daily use, and the dose is lowered in severe kidney disease.", "sidefx": "Headache, abdominal pain, nausea and diarrhea come from increased cholinergic activity in the gut and from 5-HT4 effects. They are most common on the first day and tend to fade. The label warns about suicidal ideation and worsening depression, though no clear mechanism has been shown. There is no antidote.", "fun": "5-HT4 receptors are just one of more than a dozen serotonin receptor subtypes, and most of the body's serotonin is in the gut, not the brain.", "src": ["Motegrity (prucalopride) FDA label", "Camilleri M et al. N Engl J Med 2008"]}, "methylnaltrexone": {"origin": "Methylnaltrexone came from a question asked in the late 1970s at the University of Chicago. Pharmacologist Leon Goldberg wondered whether an opioid blocker that could not enter the brain might relieve opioid constipation without blocking pain relief. Goldberg synthesized methylnaltrexone by adding a methyl group to naltrexone, which gave it a permanent positive charge. After his death, anesthesiologist Jonathan Moss and colleagues continued the research for decades. Progenics Pharmaceuticals developed it with Wyeth, and the FDA approved the injection as Relistor in 2008 for opioid-induced constipation in people with advanced illness. Approval for chronic non-cancer pain came in 2014, and tablets followed in 2016. It was the first peripherally acting mu-opioid receptor antagonist, or PAMORA, approved in the US.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Leon Goldberg synthesizes methylnaltrexone at the University of Chicago."], ["2008", "FDA approves Relistor injection for advanced illness."], ["2014", "Methylnaltrexone is approved for opioid-induced constipation in chronic non-cancer pain."], ["2016", "Methylnaltrexone tablets are approved."]], "cell": "Opioids slow the gut by activating mu-opioid receptors on enteric neurons. This reduces acetylcholine release and propulsive movements, increases segmenting contractions and sphincter tone, and reduces intestinal fluid secretion, so stool dries and stalls. Methylnaltrexone is a competitive antagonist at these mu receptors. It is a quaternary amine with a permanent positive charge and low fat solubility, so it crosses the blood-brain barrier very poorly. It blocks opioid effects on the gut wall while leaving opioid receptors in the brain and spinal cord free, so pain relief continues and withdrawal is usually avoided. With the brake released, motility and secretion return and bowel movements occur, sometimes within 30 minutes of an injection.", "body": [["Gut wall", "Blocking mu receptors on enteric nerves restores propulsive movements and fluid secretion, so bowel movements return quickly."], ["Abdomen", "Cramping and abdominal pain are common as motility returns suddenly."], ["Brain and spinal cord", "Because it barely crosses the blood-brain barrier, pain relief from opioids is generally kept."], ["Skin", "Sweating is a common effect, possibly from peripheral opioid receptor blockade."], ["Gut lining", "In people with weakened bowel walls, strong motility can contribute to perforation."]], "journey": "Methylnaltrexone can be injected under the skin or taken as tablets on an empty stomach. After injection it is absorbed quickly and peaks in about 30 minutes. Oral absorption is much lower. Only 11 to 15 percent is bound to plasma proteins. It undergoes limited metabolism to methyl-6-naltrexol isomers and a sulfate. About half leaves unchanged in the urine, with the rest in feces. The half-life after injection is about eight hours.", "sidefx": "Abdominal pain, nausea, diarrhea and gas reflect the sudden return of bowel motility. GI perforation can happen in people with conditions that weaken the bowel wall, such as tumors, ulcers or diverticulitis, because of increased motility and pressure. Opioid withdrawal symptoms can occur if the blood-brain barrier is damaged and some drug reaches the brain. There is no antidote; it is stopped if severe pain or diarrhea develops.", "fun": "Methylnaltrexone shows how one small methyl group can lock a drug out of the brain by giving it a permanent electric charge.", "src": ["Relistor (methylnaltrexone bromide) FDA label", "Thomas J et al. N Engl J Med 2008", "Moss J, Rosow CE. Mayo Clin Proc 2008"]}, "naloxegol": {"origin": "Naloxegol is built from naloxone, the opioid overdose reversal drug patented in 1961 by Jack Fishman and Mozes Lewenstein. Naloxone blocks opioid receptors everywhere, including in the brain, so it would reverse pain relief. Nektar Therapeutics, a company that specializes in attaching polyethylene glycol (PEG) chains to drugs, linked a short PEG chain to naloxone. The PEG chain makes the molecule larger and a substrate for P-glycoprotein, which pumps it out of the brain. Nektar licensed naloxegol to AstraZeneca, and the FDA approved it as Movantik in 2014, the first oral PAMORA approved for opioid-induced constipation in adults with chronic non-cancer pain.", "timeline": [["1961", "Naloxone is patented by Jack Fishman and Mozes Lewenstein."], ["1971", "Naloxone is approved by the FDA."], ["2014", "FDA approves Movantik (naloxegol)."]], "cell": "Opioids bind mu-opioid receptors on neurons in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses. This reduces acetylcholine release and propulsive peristalsis, increases sphincter tone and lowers fluid secretion, causing hard, infrequent stools. Naloxegol is a PEGylated naloxone that antagonizes these peripheral mu receptors. The PEG chain lowers its ability to cross the blood-brain barrier passively, and naloxegol is also a P-glycoprotein substrate, so any drug that enters the brain is pumped back out. As a result it blocks opioid action in the gut while leaving central pain relief largely intact. Gut motility and secretion recover, and spontaneous bowel movements increase.", "body": [["Gut wall", "Blocking mu receptors on enteric nerves restores peristalsis and secretion, so spontaneous bowel movements increase."], ["Abdomen", "Abdominal pain, cramping, diarrhea and gas are common as motility returns."], ["Brain", "Very little enters the brain, so opioid pain relief is preserved, though withdrawal-like symptoms can occur in some people."], ["Gut lining", "In people with weakened bowel walls, perforation is a rare risk."]], "journey": "Naloxegol is taken by mouth on an empty stomach. It is absorbed quickly and peaks in under two hours. Protein binding is very low, about 4 percent. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as clarithromycin and ketoconazole are contraindicated because they raise levels, and strong inducers lower them. Most is eliminated in the feces with a small portion in the urine. Its half-life is about 6 to 11 hours.", "sidefx": "Abdominal pain and diarrhea reflect the release of the opioid brake on the gut. Opioid withdrawal symptoms, such as sweating, chills, anxiety and yawning, can occur, especially if the blood-brain barrier is disrupted or when strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels. GI perforation is rare and occurs mainly in people with weakened bowel walls. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Naloxegol's name combines naloxone with the PEG (polyethylene glycol) chain attached to it.", "src": ["Movantik (naloxegol) FDA label", "Chey WD et al. N Engl J Med 2014"]}, "rifaximin": {"origin": "Rifaximin is part of the rifamycin family. In 1957 scientists at Lepetit Pharmaceuticals in Milan, led by Piero Sensi, isolated rifamycins from a soil bacterium, now called Amycolatopsis rifamycinica, found in a sample from a pine forest on the French Riviera. They named the drugs after Rififi, a popular French crime film of the time. Rifampin, the famous tuberculosis drug, came from this family. Rifaximin was developed by the Italian company Alfa Wassermann, which added a ring structure that makes it barely absorbed from the gut. It was approved in Italy in 1987. Salix Pharmaceuticals brought it to the US, where the FDA approved Xifaxan in 2004 for travelers' diarrhea, in 2010 for reducing recurrent hepatic encephalopathy and in 2015 for IBS with diarrhea.", "timeline": [["1957", "Rifamycins are discovered at Lepetit in Milan."], ["1987", "Rifaximin is approved in Italy."], ["2004", "FDA approves Xifaxan for travelers' diarrhea."], ["2010", "Rifaximin is approved for reducing recurrent hepatic encephalopathy."], ["2015", "Rifaximin is approved for IBS with diarrhea."]], "cell": "Rifaximin binds the beta subunit of bacterial DNA-dependent RNA polymerase, encoded by the rpoB gene. This blocks the enzyme from starting RNA chains, so bacteria cannot make messenger RNA and proteins, which stops growth. Human RNA polymerase is not affected. Its key feature is that less than 0.4 percent is absorbed, so it reaches very high levels inside the gut lumen and very low levels in the blood. In hepatic encephalopathy, it suppresses ammonia-producing gut bacteria, so less ammonia and other toxins reach the brain through the portal blood. In travelers' diarrhea, it acts against noninvasive E. coli. In IBS-D, it is thought to change the gut microbiota and reduce bacterial products that drive symptoms.", "body": [["Gut", "Bacterial populations in the intestine change, reducing ammonia production and certain diarrhea-causing bacteria."], ["Brain", "In liver disease, lower gut ammonia means less reaches the brain, reducing episodes of hepatic encephalopathy."], ["Bowel habits", "In IBS-D, bloating and loose stools improve in some people."], ["Blood", "Very little reaches the blood, so systemic effects and resistance in other body sites are limited."], ["Fluid balance", "Peripheral edema and ascites were reported in hepatic encephalopathy trials, likely reflecting the underlying liver disease."]], "journey": "Rifaximin is swallowed and stays almost entirely in the gut, where it acts. Less than 0.4 percent is absorbed in healthy people. The small absorbed fraction is metabolized by CYP3A4 in the liver. About 97 percent of the dose leaves in the feces, mostly unchanged. In severe liver impairment, absorption rises because the liver cannot clear what is absorbed. P-glycoprotein inhibitors such as cyclosporine greatly increase blood levels.", "sidefx": "Because it stays in the gut, most side effects are mild, such as nausea and dizziness. Changing gut bacteria can let C. difficile overgrow and cause colitis. It does not work for diarrhea with fever or blood or for invasive pathogens because it does not reach tissues. Allergic reactions, including rare severe ones, can occur. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The rifamycins were named after Rififi, a 1955 French crime film that one of the scientists enjoyed.", "src": ["Xifaxan (rifaximin) FDA label", "Bass NM et al. N Engl J Med 2010", "Sensi P. Rev Infect Dis 1983"]}, "diphenoxylate-atropine": {"origin": "Diphenoxylate was synthesized in 1956 by Paul Janssen's team in Belgium while they were exploring compounds related to the opioid meperidine (pethidine). It strongly slowed the gut but had little pain-relieving effect at normal doses and was poorly soluble, which limited misuse. G. D. Searle and Company marketed it in the US as Lomotil around 1960. To discourage people from taking large amounts for a high, a very small amount of atropine was added. At normal doses the atropine does almost nothing, but at high doses it causes unpleasant anticholinergic effects. Janssen's later research produced loperamide, which became the main over-the-counter antidiarrheal, while Lomotil remains prescription only and Schedule V.", "timeline": [["1956", "Paul Janssen's team synthesizes diphenoxylate."], ["1960", "Lomotil (diphenoxylate and atropine) is introduced in the US."], ["1969", "Janssen develops loperamide, a related antidiarrheal."]], "cell": "Diphenoxylate is a meperidine relative that activates mu-opioid receptors on neurons of the enteric nervous system. These receptors are Gi/o coupled. Activation reduces cAMP, opens potassium channels and closes calcium channels, so the neurons release less acetylcholine. Propulsive peristalsis slows, segmenting contractions increase, and intestinal secretion falls, while more water is absorbed from the slower-moving stool. The result is fewer, firmer stools. Diphenoxylate is quickly converted to diphenoxylic acid (difenoxin), which is also active. The small amount of atropine is a muscarinic antagonist added as an abuse deterrent; at normal doses it is below the level that has real anticholinergic effects.", "body": [["Intestine", "Slower movement and more water absorption make stools less frequent and firmer."], ["Brain", "At normal doses little central opioid effect occurs, but high doses cause drowsiness, euphoria and respiratory depression."], ["Atropine effects", "At high doses atropine causes dry mouth, flushing, fast heart rate, urinary retention and hot, dry skin."], ["Colon in colitis", "Slowing the gut in acute ulcerative colitis or infectious colitis can contribute to toxic megacolon."], ["Fluid balance", "Fluid can collect in a slowed bowel, hiding how much fluid has been lost, so dehydration can be underestimated, especially in children."]], "journey": "Diphenoxylate is absorbed well by mouth and acts within about 45 minutes to an hour. In the liver it is rapidly hydrolyzed by esterases to diphenoxylic acid, the main active form, which peaks in about two hours. The parent drug has a half-life of about two and a half hours, while diphenoxylic acid lasts longer, about 12 to 14 hours. Metabolites leave in the feces and urine. The atropine portion is absorbed and cleared in the usual way.", "sidefx": "Constipation, abdominal discomfort and ileus come from excess mu-opioid slowing of the gut. In children, both the opioid and atropine parts can cause serious toxicity, including respiratory depression, coma, and anticholinergic signs such as flushing and high temperature; these effects can be delayed. In acute colitis, slowing the bowel can lead to toxic megacolon. Naloxone reverses the opioid effects, and repeated doses may be needed because diphenoxylic acid lasts longer than naloxone.", "fun": "The atropine in Lomotil is there as a deterrent, not for any treatment benefit.", "src": ["Lomotil (diphenoxylate hydrochloride and atropine sulfate) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "ursodiol": {"origin": "Ursodiol is ursodeoxycholic acid, a bile acid that is found in large amounts in bear bile. Dried bear bile was used in traditional Chinese medicine for liver and eye complaints for well over a thousand years. Western chemists identified the acid in the early 1900s, and Japanese researchers named and crystallized it from bear bile in the 1920s. Chemical synthesis in the 1950s meant it no longer had to come from animals, and it was sold in Japan for liver and gallbladder complaints. In the 1970s studies showed it could slowly dissolve cholesterol gallstones with fewer side effects than the earlier bile acid chenodiol. In the US, Actigall was approved for gallstone dissolution in 1987, and Urso tablets were later approved for primary biliary cholangitis, where it became the standard first-line drug.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Dried bear bile is used in traditional Chinese medicine."], ["1920s", "Japanese researchers crystallize and name ursodeoxycholic acid from bear bile."], ["1950s", "Chemical synthesis frees the drug from animal sources."], ["1987", "FDA approves Actigall for dissolving cholesterol gallstones."], ["1990s", "Urso tablets are approved in the US for primary biliary cholangitis."]], "cell": "Ursodiol is a hydrophilic (water-loving) bile acid. Taken daily, it becomes a large share of the bile acid pool and pushes out the more hydrophobic, detergent-like bile acids such as chenodeoxycholic and deoxycholic acid. Hydrophobic bile acids damage cell membranes and mitochondria, so replacing them protects hepatocytes and the cholangiocytes that line the bile ducts. Ursodiol also reduces the liver's secretion of cholesterol into bile and lowers intestinal cholesterol absorption. Bile becomes less saturated with cholesterol, so cholesterol slowly leaves the surface of gallstones and forms liquid crystal layers that carry it away. In primary biliary cholangitis it stimulates bile flow (choleresis), helps cholangiocytes secrete bicarbonate, and reduces bile acid triggered apoptosis of liver cells.", "body": [["Gallbladder", "Bile that is less saturated with cholesterol can slowly dissolve small, noncalcified cholesterol stones over months. It does not dissolve calcified or pigment stones."], ["Liver", "Liver cells are shielded from toxic bile acids, which lowers alkaline phosphatase and bilirubin and slows disease progression in primary biliary cholangitis."], ["Bile ducts", "Increased bile flow and a protective bicarbonate layer help the small ducts that are injured in cholestatic disease."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and abdominal discomfort can occur because bile acids draw water into the colon."]], "journey": "Ursodiol is taken by mouth and absorbed in the small intestine. The liver captures most of it on the first pass and conjugates it with glycine or taurine, then secretes it into bile. From there it cycles between gut and liver many times (enterohepatic recirculation), which is why it builds up in the bile acid pool rather than in blood. Some is converted by gut bacteria to lithocholic acid and other bile acids. It leaves mainly in feces. Stone dissolution takes months, and the biologic half-life of the pool is several days.", "sidefx": "Ursodiol is gentle because it is a natural, less toxic bile acid. Diarrhea and cramping come from bile acids reaching the colon, where they draw in water. Liver enzymes can rise, and in people with advanced cirrhosis rare worsening of liver function has been reported, so liver tests are watched. Bile acid binders and aluminum antacids bind it in the gut and block its effect. There is no antidote, and overdose mainly causes diarrhea.", "fun": "Because synthetic ursodiol is identical to the bear bile compound, it is often cited as a reason that bear bile farming is not needed for medicine.", "src": ["Actigall and Urso (ursodiol) FDA labels", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "pancrelipase": {"origin": "Pancrelipase is a mix of digestive enzymes taken from pig pancreas glands, a by-product of the meat industry. Doctors in the 1800s already tried pancreas extracts (pancreatin) for people with fatty, bulky stools. For decades these products were sold without modern FDA approval, and their strength varied widely from batch to batch. In 2004 the FDA announced that all pancreatic enzyme products would need full approval, and the deadline arrived in 2010. Creon was the first product approved under the new rules in 2009, followed by others such as Zenpep and Pancreaze. Steady enzyme dosing changed life for people with cystic fibrosis, whose blocked pancreatic ducts keep enzymes from reaching the gut.", "timeline": [["1800s", "Pancreas extracts (pancreatin) are first used for digestive problems."], ["1980s", "Enteric-coated microspheres protect the enzymes from stomach acid."], ["2004", "FDA requires full approval for pancreatic enzyme products."], ["2009", "Creon becomes the first pancrelipase product approved under the new rules."], ["2010", "Unapproved pancreatic enzyme products leave the US market."]], "cell": "Pancrelipase is not a receptor drug. It replaces three groups of enzymes that the pancreas normally pours into the duodenum. Lipase splits dietary triglycerides into free fatty acids and monoglycerides, which bile salts gather into micelles for absorption. Protease enzymes (such as trypsin and chymotrypsin) cut proteins into peptides and amino acids. Amylase breaks starch into dextrins and sugars. Lipase is the most fragile and is destroyed by stomach acid, so most capsules hold enteric-coated beads that stay closed in the acid stomach and open at the higher pH of the duodenum, mixing with the meal at the place where digestion should happen.", "body": [["Small intestine", "Fats, proteins and starches are broken down so they can be absorbed, which reduces greasy, foul stools (steatorrhea) and bloating."], ["Nutrition and growth", "Better fat absorption improves weight gain, growth in children with cystic fibrosis, and uptake of fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K."], ["Mouth", "Beads that are chewed or crushed release enzymes early and can irritate or ulcerate the mouth."], ["Colon", "Very high long-term enzyme exposure has been linked to fibrosing colonopathy, a scarring and narrowing of the colon."], ["Blood sugar", "Better digestion of carbohydrates can change blood glucose levels in people with diabetes from pancreatic disease."]], "journey": "The capsule is swallowed with a meal. In the stomach the coated beads stay intact. In the duodenum, where bile and bicarbonate raise the pH, the coating dissolves and the enzymes go to work on the food. The enzymes act only inside the gut and are not meaningfully absorbed into the blood. Like any dietary protein, they are eventually digested themselves by gut proteases and leave in the stool. Their effect lasts only as long as the meal, which is why they are taken with every meal and snack.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from the enzymes acting in the wrong place or in excess. Chewed beads digest the lining of the mouth. Very high doses over years are linked to fibrosing colonopathy, likely from enzyme injury to the colon wall. The pig material contains purines, so high intake can raise uric acid. Because it is made from pig tissue, there is a theoretical risk of viral transmission and allergy in people sensitive to pork proteins. There is no antidote, and care is supportive.", "fun": "Enzyme strength is measured in lipase units, not milligrams, because what matters is how much fat the enzyme can digest.", "src": ["Creon (pancrelipase) FDA label", "FDA guidance on exocrine pancreatic insufficiency drug products", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "balsalazide": {"origin": "Balsalazide grew out of the story of sulfasalazine. In the 1940s the Swedish physician Nanna Svartz developed sulfasalazine, a sulfa drug linked to a salicylate, for rheumatoid arthritis, and it turned out to help ulcerative colitis. In 1977 researchers showed that the active part in the colon was 5-aminosalicylic acid (mesalamine), and that the sulfapyridine half caused most of the side effects. Drug makers then looked for new carriers that would hold mesalamine through the stomach and small intestine and let colon bacteria release it. Balsalazide links mesalamine to an inert carrier through an azo bond. It was developed in the United Kingdom in the 1980s and approved in the US as Colazal in 2000.", "timeline": [["1940s", "Nanna Svartz develops sulfasalazine in Sweden."], ["1977", "Mesalamine is shown to be the active part of sulfasalazine in colitis."], ["1980s", "Balsalazide is developed in the UK as a sulfa-free mesalamine carrier."], ["2000", "FDA approves Colazal for ulcerative colitis."]], "cell": "Balsalazide is a prodrug. Its azo (N=N) bond joins mesalamine to the carrier 4-aminobenzoyl-beta-alanine. Human enzymes cannot easily split this bond, so the intact drug passes through the small intestine. In the colon, bacterial azoreductase enzymes cut the bond and release mesalamine right at the inflamed lining. Mesalamine works topically on colon epithelial cells. It inhibits cyclooxygenase and lipoxygenase, lowering prostaglandins and leukotrienes that recruit inflammatory cells. It scavenges reactive oxygen species made by neutrophils, interferes with NF-kappa-B signaling, and activates the nuclear receptor PPAR-gamma, which turns down inflammatory gene expression. The carrier is largely inert and leaves in the stool.", "body": [["Colon", "Mesalamine released at the lining calms inflammation, so bleeding, urgency and diarrhea from ulcerative colitis improve over days to weeks."], ["Kidneys", "A small amount of mesalamine is absorbed, and rare interstitial nephritis can occur, so kidney function is checked."], ["Heart", "Rare hypersensitivity reactions can inflame the pericardium or heart muscle."], ["Gut symptoms", "Some people have an acute intolerance reaction with cramping, bloody diarrhea and fever that can look like a colitis flare."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth, very little intact balsalazide is absorbed in the upper gut. It travels to the colon, where bacteria split it into mesalamine and the carrier. Part of the released mesalamine is taken up by colon cells and acetylated to N-acetyl-mesalamine. Most of the drug and its pieces leave in the feces, with a small part of the absorbed mesalamine cleared by the kidneys. Because it depends on colon bacteria, it acts mainly in the colon and not in the small bowel.", "sidefx": "Because the sulfapyridine carrier of sulfasalazine is gone, sulfa-type reactions are much less common. Headache and abdominal pain are frequent. The small absorbed fraction of mesalamine is a salicylate, which explains rare kidney inflammation and, in overdose, salicylate-like effects. The acute intolerance syndrome and rare heart inflammation are immune reactions to mesalamine. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "The drug only works because of the bacteria living in the colon, making gut microbes a built-in delivery system.", "src": ["Colazal (balsalazide) FDA label", "Azad Khan AK et al. Lancet 1977", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "vedolizumab": {"origin": "Vedolizumab traces back to a mouse antibody called Act-1, made in the 1980s by researchers studying how white blood cells find their way into tissues. Later work showed that Act-1 recognized alpha-4 beta-7 integrin, a homing molecule used by lymphocytes that travel to the gut. The idea of blocking gut traffic gained urgency after natalizumab, which blocks a broader alpha-4 target, was linked to the brain infection PML. A gut-selective blocker could, in theory, avoid that risk. Millennium Pharmaceuticals developed a humanized version, and Takeda brought it to market. The FDA approved vedolizumab (Entyvio) in 2014 for ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease, and a subcutaneous form was approved later.", "timeline": [["1980s", "The mouse antibody Act-1 is developed and later found to bind alpha-4 beta-7 integrin."], ["2005", "PML cases with natalizumab highlight the need for gut-selective trafficking blockers."], ["2014", "FDA approves vedolizumab (Entyvio) for ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease."], ["2023", "A subcutaneous form is approved for maintenance in ulcerative colitis."]], "cell": "Lymphocytes that home to the gut carry the integrin alpha-4 beta-7 on their surface. Blood vessels in the gut wall display a matching adhesion molecule called MAdCAM-1. When the two bind, a circulating lymphocyte rolls, sticks firmly and then squeezes through the vessel wall into the intestinal tissue. Vedolizumab is a humanized IgG1 antibody that binds only the alpha-4 beta-7 combination and blocks its contact with MAdCAM-1. Memory T cells can no longer leave the blood into the gut, so fewer inflammatory cells reach the lining. It does not block alpha-4 beta-1 (VLA-4), which T cells use to enter the brain through VCAM-1, so brain immune surveillance is largely preserved.", "body": [["Intestine", "Fewer lymphocytes enter the gut wall, so inflammation, ulcers, bleeding and diarrhea slowly improve over weeks."], ["Immune system", "Effects are mostly limited to gut-homing cells, so whole-body immune suppression is mild compared with TNF blockers."], ["Brain", "Because brain trafficking through VLA-4 is not blocked, PML has been only rarely reported."], ["Liver", "Rare liver injury has been reported, so symptoms of hepatitis are watched for."], ["Infusion site and airway", "Infusion reactions and upper respiratory infections such as nasopharyngitis are among the most common effects."]], "journey": "Vedolizumab is a large protein, so it is given by IV infusion at first and later by IV or subcutaneous injection. It stays mostly in the blood and the fluid between cells. Like other antibodies, it is not broken down by liver enzymes. Cells take it up and break it into amino acids. The FcRn receptor protects it from breakdown, which gives it a long half-life of about 25 days. It is not cleared by the kidneys. Full benefit builds over several weeks.", "sidefx": "Most side effects reflect immune modulation and its protein nature. Colds and sinus infections are common because some mucosal immune traffic is reduced. Infusion reactions and rare anaphylaxis are reactions to a foreign protein. The PML risk appears far lower than with natalizumab because the brain homing pathway is spared. There is no reversal agent, and drug levels fall slowly over months after stopping.", "fun": "Vedolizumab is often called gut-selective because its target is used mainly by lymphocytes headed to the intestines.", "src": ["Entyvio (vedolizumab) FDA label", "Feagan BG et al. GEMINI 1, N Engl J Med 2013", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "ustekinumab": {"origin": "Ustekinumab came from basic immunology work in the 1990s and early 2000s showing that two cytokines, IL-12 and IL-23, share a common p40 protein chain. Since both were thought to drive inflammation in psoriasis and Crohn's disease, one antibody against p40 could block both. Centocor (later Janssen, part of Johnson & Johnson) developed a fully human IgG1 antibody against p40. The FDA approved it as Stelara for plaque psoriasis in 2009, then for psoriatic arthritis, Crohn's disease in 2016 and ulcerative colitis in 2019. Its dosing every few months was a big change from older injectable drugs. Several biosimilar versions were approved starting in 2023.", "timeline": [["2000", "IL-23 is described and found to share the p40 chain with IL-12."], ["2009", "FDA approves Stelara for plaque psoriasis."], ["2016", "Approval extends to Crohn's disease."], ["2019", "Approval extends to ulcerative colitis."], ["2023", "The first ustekinumab biosimilar is approved in the US."]], "cell": "IL-12 and IL-23 are made by dendritic cells and macrophages. Each is a pair of protein chains, and both share the p40 chain. Ustekinumab binds p40 and prevents both cytokines from attaching to the IL-12 receptor beta-1 chain on T cells and natural killer cells. Without IL-12 signaling, naive T cells are less driven to become Th1 cells that make interferon-gamma. Without IL-23 signaling (through JAK2, TYK2 and STAT3), Th17 cells are not maintained and make less IL-17 and IL-22. These cytokines normally drive skin cell overgrowth in psoriasis and recruit neutrophils and other cells to the gut wall in IBD. Dampening both pathways lowers inflammation in skin, joints and intestine.", "body": [["Skin", "Psoriasis plaques thin and clear because IL-17 and IL-22 no longer push keratinocytes to divide quickly."], ["Intestine", "Less Th1 and Th17 inflammation lets ulcers heal in Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis over weeks."], ["Joints", "Joint swelling and pain in psoriatic arthritis decrease."], ["Immune defense", "Th1 and Th17 cells help fight intracellular bacteria such as mycobacteria and Salmonella, so these infections are a concern."], ["Brain", "Rare cases of posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome (PRES) have been reported."]], "journey": "For IBD the first dose is given by IV infusion, then maintenance doses are injected under the skin. From the skin it is absorbed slowly through lymphatics, reaching peak levels about a week or more later. It stays mostly in blood and tissue fluid. Like other antibodies it is broken down by cells into amino acids, not by liver enzymes, and FcRn recycling gives it a half-life of about three weeks. That long half-life allows dosing every 8 to 12 weeks.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow from blocking two pathways that protect against certain germs. Upper respiratory infections are common. Serious infections, especially from mycobacteria and Salmonella, reflect loss of IL-12 driven Th1 immunity, so people are screened for tuberculosis before starting. Injection site reactions and rare anaphylaxis come from the protein itself. Rare noninfectious pneumonia and PRES are reported. There is no reversal agent.", "src": ["Stelara (ustekinumab) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Feagan BG et al. UNITI trials, N Engl J Med 2016"]}, "risankizumab": {"origin": "After ustekinumab showed that blocking the shared p40 chain worked, scientists learned that IL-23, not IL-12, was the main driver of psoriasis and much of IBD. That led to antibodies against p19, the chain found only in IL-23. Risankizumab was discovered at Boehringer Ingelheim and later developed with AbbVie. The FDA approved it as Skyrizi for plaque psoriasis in 2019. Approvals for psoriatic arthritis and Crohn's disease followed in 2022, and ulcerative colitis in 2024. In psoriasis head-to-head trials, it cleared skin better than ustekinumab and adalimumab, which helped confirm that targeting IL-23 alone was enough.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Research shows IL-23 is a key driver of Th17 inflammation."], ["2016", "AbbVie partners with Boehringer Ingelheim on risankizumab."], ["2019", "FDA approves Skyrizi for plaque psoriasis."], ["2022", "Approval extends to psoriatic arthritis and Crohn's disease."], ["2024", "Approval extends to ulcerative colitis."]], "cell": "IL-23 is built from a p19 chain and the p40 chain it shares with IL-12. Risankizumab is a humanized IgG1 antibody that binds p19, so it blocks only IL-23 and leaves IL-12 alone. IL-23 normally binds a receptor made of IL-23R and IL-12R beta-1, activating JAK2 and TYK2, which phosphorylate STAT3. STAT3 keeps Th17 cells and innate lymphoid cells alive and active, making IL-17A, IL-17F and IL-22. With IL-23 blocked, that cytokine stream drops. In skin, keratinocytes stop overgrowing. In the gut, fewer neutrophils are drawn into the wall and the lining can heal. Because IL-12 driven Th1 immunity is preserved, defense against some intracellular germs is less affected than with p40 blockers.", "body": [["Skin", "Psoriasis plaques clear, often almost completely, because the IL-23/IL-17 loop that drives keratinocyte overgrowth is cut."], ["Intestine", "Inflammation and ulcers in Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis settle over weeks."], ["Joints", "Swelling and pain in psoriatic arthritis decrease."], ["Liver", "Liver injury has been seen during IV induction for Crohn's disease, so liver tests are checked."], ["Skin and airway infections", "Colds and fungal skin infections such as tinea are more common, reflecting reduced IL-17 defense at body surfaces."]], "journey": "For IBD, induction is given by IV infusion, then maintenance is given by injection under the skin, including with an on-body injector. Absorption from the skin is slow, with peaks several days later. As a protein, it is broken down into amino acids by cells throughout the body, not by liver enzymes or the kidneys. Its half-life of about four weeks allows maintenance doses every 8 weeks or more.", "sidefx": "IL-17 and IL-22 help defend skin and mucous membranes against fungi and bacteria, so blocking IL-23 increases upper respiratory infections and tinea. Serious infections are possible, so tuberculosis screening happens first. Liver enzyme rises during Crohn's induction appear to be drug related. Injection site reactions and rare anaphylaxis reflect the protein itself. There is no reversal agent.", "src": ["Skyrizi (risankizumab-rzaa) FDA label", "Gordon KB et al. UltIMMa trials, Lancet 2018", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "guselkumab": {"origin": "Guselkumab is a fully human antibody that came from a phage display library, a method that lets scientists pick human antibodies from billions of candidates in the lab. It was discovered using the antibody library of the German company MorphoSys and developed by Janssen. In 2017 the FDA approved guselkumab (Tremfya) for plaque psoriasis, making it the first approved antibody that targets the p19 chain of IL-23. Approval for psoriatic arthritis followed in 2020. In 2024 it was approved for ulcerative colitis, and in 2025 for Crohn's disease, with both IV and subcutaneous induction options in IBD.", "timeline": [["2000s", "MorphoSys phage display technology is used to find a human antibody against IL-23 p19."], ["2017", "FDA approves Tremfya, the first IL-23 p19 blocker, for plaque psoriasis."], ["2020", "Approval extends to psoriatic arthritis."], ["2024", "Approval extends to ulcerative colitis."], ["2025", "Approval extends to Crohn's disease."]], "cell": "Guselkumab is a human IgG1 lambda antibody that binds the p19 subunit of IL-23. Because p19 belongs only to IL-23, IL-12 is untouched. Blocked IL-23 cannot reach its receptor (IL-23R paired with IL-12R beta-1) on Th17 cells, gamma-delta T cells and innate lymphoid cells. That stops JAK2 and TYK2 from phosphorylating STAT3, so these cells make less IL-17A, IL-17F and IL-22. In psoriasis that breaks the loop in which IL-17 drives keratinocytes to divide and to release more inflammatory signals. Guselkumab also has an Fc region that can bind CD64 on myeloid cells that make IL-23, which may help capture IL-23 at its source in inflamed tissue.", "body": [["Skin", "Plaques become thinner and clear because IL-17 driven keratinocyte overgrowth is shut down."], ["Joints", "Swelling, pain and enthesitis in psoriatic arthritis improve."], ["Intestine", "Less Th17 inflammation lets the colon and small bowel lining heal in ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease."], ["Skin and mucosal defense", "Infections such as colds, tinea and herpes simplex are somewhat more common."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises have been reported, mainly with IBD induction dosing."]], "journey": "Guselkumab is injected under the skin, and for IBD the first doses can be given by IV infusion. After a skin injection it is absorbed slowly through the lymphatics, peaking in about five to six days. It stays in the blood and tissue fluid and is broken down by cells into amino acids, not by liver enzymes. FcRn recycling gives a half-life of about two to three weeks, allowing maintenance doses every 4 to 8 weeks.", "sidefx": "The main risks come from turning down IL-17 and IL-22, which defend skin and mucous membranes. That explains more upper respiratory infections, fungal skin infections and herpes simplex. Serious infections are possible, so tuberculosis is screened for first. Rare hypersensitivity, including anaphylaxis, reflects the protein. Liver enzyme rises are monitored. There is no reversal agent.", "src": ["Tremfya (guselkumab) FDA label", "Blauvelt A et al. VOYAGE 1, J Am Acad Dermatol 2017", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "tofacitinib": {"origin": "Tofacitinib began with basic research on Janus kinases (JAKs), enzymes that sit inside cells and carry signals from cytokine receptors. In the 1990s researchers, including John O'Shea's group at the US National Institutes of Health, showed that JAK3 is essential for immune cell signaling, because children lacking it have severe combined immunodeficiency. This suggested that a JAK3 blocker could calm the immune system. Pfizer, working with the NIH, screened compounds and found tofacitinib. The FDA approved it as Xeljanz for rheumatoid arthritis in 2012, the first JAK inhibitor for that disease. Approval for ulcerative colitis came in 2018. In 2021 a large safety study led to boxed warnings about heart events, cancer, clots and death.", "timeline": [["1990s", "JAK3 is shown to be essential for lymphocyte cytokine signaling."], ["2012", "FDA approves Xeljanz for rheumatoid arthritis."], ["2017", "Approval extends to psoriatic arthritis."], ["2018", "Approval extends to ulcerative colitis."], ["2021", "The ORAL Surveillance study leads to expanded boxed warnings for JAK inhibitors."]], "cell": "Many inflammatory cytokines signal through receptors that have no enzyme activity of their own. Instead they rely on paired JAK enzymes attached inside the cell. When a cytokine binds, the JAKs phosphorylate each other and the receptor, creating docking sites for STAT proteins. The STATs are phosphorylated, pair up, move to the nucleus and switch on inflammatory genes. Tofacitinib is a small molecule that competes with ATP in the JAK enzyme pocket, blocking JAK1 and JAK3 most strongly and JAK2 less. This shuts down signals from common gamma-chain cytokines (IL-2, IL-4, IL-7, IL-9, IL-15, IL-21) that need JAK1/JAK3, and from IL-6 and interferons that use JAK1. Lymphocyte activation, proliferation and cytokine release drop, lowering inflammation in joints and gut.", "body": [["Joints", "Less cytokine signaling reduces synovial inflammation, so joint pain, swelling and stiffness improve in rheumatoid and psoriatic arthritis."], ["Colon", "Inflammation in ulcerative colitis settles, often within weeks, because many cytokines driving the disease use JAK pathways."], ["Immune system", "Blocking IL-15 and other signals lowers NK cell and lymphocyte counts and raises infection risk, including shingles."], ["Blood vessels and clotting", "Blood clots in the legs and lungs are more frequent, especially at higher doses and in older people with risk factors."], ["Heart", "Major heart events were more common than with TNF blockers in older patients with cardiovascular risk factors."], ["Lipids", "LDL and HDL cholesterol rise in a dose-related way, partly from reduced IL-6 signaling."]], "journey": "Tofacitinib is taken by mouth and absorbed quickly, peaking within about an hour for the immediate-release tablet. It is moderately bound to plasma proteins. About 70 percent is metabolized in the liver, mainly by CYP3A4 with some CYP2C19, and about 30 percent is cleared unchanged by the kidneys. The half-life is short, about three hours, but the immune effects last longer because cells need time to recover. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels and inducers such as rifampin lower them.", "sidefx": "The immune effects that help arthritis and colitis also weaken defense. Loss of JAK1/JAK3 signaling for IL-15 and interferons explains herpes zoster reactivation, tuberculosis and other serious infections. Effects on JAK2 can lower blood counts. The rise in lipids reflects blocked IL-6 signaling. Clots, heart events and cancer are labeled risks from a large safety trial. Bowel perforation is rare. There is no antidote, but the drug clears within about a day.", "fun": "The name Janus kinase comes from Janus, the two-faced Roman god, because JAKs have two kinase-like domains facing in different directions.", "src": ["Xeljanz (tofacitinib) FDA label", "Ytterberg SR et al. ORAL Surveillance, N Engl J Med 2022", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "upadacitinib": {"origin": "After tofacitinib proved that pills blocking JAK enzymes could treat autoimmune disease, AbbVie set out to make a more selective drug that would hit JAK1 harder than JAK2, aiming to keep strong anti-inflammatory effects while sparing the JAK2 pathways used for making red blood cells and platelets. The result was upadacitinib, an extended-release tablet taken once daily. The FDA approved it as Rinvoq for rheumatoid arthritis in 2019. Approvals followed for psoriatic arthritis, atopic dermatitis, ankylosing spondylitis, ulcerative colitis in 2022 and Crohn's disease in 2023. Like other JAK inhibitors, it carries boxed warnings based on the tofacitinib safety study.", "timeline": [["2010s", "AbbVie develops upadacitinib as a JAK1-selective inhibitor."], ["2019", "FDA approves Rinvoq for rheumatoid arthritis."], ["2022", "Approval extends to ulcerative colitis."], ["2023", "Approval extends to Crohn's disease."]], "cell": "Upadacitinib competes with ATP for the kinase pocket of JAK1, with selectivity for JAK1 over JAK2, JAK3 and TYK2 in cell assays. JAK1 pairs with other JAKs at many cytokine receptors, including those for IL-6, interferon-gamma and the common gamma-chain cytokines such as IL-2, IL-7 and IL-15. When JAK1 cannot phosphorylate the receptor, STAT proteins are not recruited or phosphorylated, so they do not move to the nucleus to switch on inflammatory genes. T cells, B cells and innate immune cells become less activated, cytokine loops weaken, and inflammation in joints, skin and gut falls. Its JAK1 preference is relative, so some effects on other JAKs remain at higher doses.", "body": [["Joints and spine", "Synovial and spinal inflammation falls, easing pain, swelling and stiffness in arthritis and axial spondyloarthritis."], ["Intestine", "Inflammation in ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease decreases, often with rapid symptom relief during induction."], ["Skin", "Itch and eczema lesions in atopic dermatitis improve because IL-4, IL-13 and IL-31 signals rely on JAK1. Acne is a common side effect."], ["Immune system", "Infections, including shingles and pneumonia, are more frequent, and white cell counts can fall."], ["Blood vessels and heart", "Blood clots and major heart events are labeled risks shared across the JAK inhibitor class."], ["Muscle and lipids", "CPK levels and LDL cholesterol rise in a dose-related way."]], "journey": "Upadacitinib is swallowed as an extended-release tablet that releases the drug over many hours, giving a peak a few hours after the dose and allowing once-daily use. About half is bound to plasma proteins. It is metabolized in the liver mainly by CYP3A4, with a minor role for CYP2D6, and much of it leaves unchanged in feces and urine. The half-life with the extended-release form is about 8 to 14 hours. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise exposure and strong inducers lower it.", "sidefx": "Shutting down JAK1 cytokine signals weakens antiviral and antibacterial defense, which explains shingles, pneumonia and opportunistic infections, so tuberculosis is screened for first. Blocked IL-6 signaling raises lipids. Neutropenia and lymphopenia reflect effects on blood cell signaling. Clots, heart attack, stroke, cancer and GI perforation are boxed or labeled risks. Acne is common for reasons not fully understood. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Rinvoq (upadacitinib) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Danese S et al. U-ACHIEVE and U-ACCOMPLISH, Lancet 2022"]}, "ozanimod": {"origin": "Ozanimod descends from a fungus. In the 1990s Japanese researchers studied myriocin, an immune-suppressing compound from the insect-attacking fungus Isaria sinclairii, and simplified it into fingolimod, the first S1P receptor modulator, approved for multiple sclerosis in 2010. Fingolimod hit several S1P receptor types and caused notable first-dose slowing of the heart. Scientists at the Scripps Research Institute, including Hugh Rosen and Ed Roberts, designed more selective molecules that act on S1P1 and S1P5. One of them became ozanimod, developed by Receptos, which was bought by Celgene in 2015, and Celgene was later acquired by Bristol Myers Squibb. The FDA approved ozanimod (Zeposia) for relapsing MS in 2020 and for ulcerative colitis in 2021.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Myriocin from the fungus Isaria sinclairii is simplified into fingolimod."], ["2010", "Fingolimod becomes the first S1P receptor modulator approved for MS."], ["2000s", "Scripps scientists design selective S1P1 and S1P5 modulators, leading to ozanimod."], ["2020", "FDA approves Zeposia for relapsing multiple sclerosis."], ["2021", "Approval extends to ulcerative colitis."]], "cell": "Lymphocytes leave lymph nodes by following a gradient of sphingosine 1-phosphate (S1P), which is high in blood and lymph and low inside the node. They sense it through the S1P1 receptor, a G protein-coupled receptor. Ozanimod and its active metabolites bind S1P1 strongly. At first this activates the receptor, but then the receptor is pulled inside the cell and degraded, so the lymphocyte becomes blind to the S1P signal. This is called functional antagonism. Naive and central memory T cells and B cells stay trapped in lymph nodes, and the number of circulating lymphocytes falls. Fewer of them reach the brain or colon to cause inflammation. Activation of S1P1 on atrial heart cells opens potassium channels (GIRK), which briefly slows heart rate at the start.", "body": [["Immune system", "Circulating lymphocyte counts drop substantially but cells are not destroyed; they are held in lymph nodes and return after stopping."], ["Brain and spinal cord", "Fewer lymphocytes enter the CNS, so MS relapses and new lesions decrease."], ["Colon", "Fewer lymphocytes reach the gut lining, lowering inflammation in ulcerative colitis over weeks."], ["Heart", "A brief slowing of heart rate and AV conduction can occur at the start, which is why the dose is increased gradually over the first week."], ["Eyes", "Macular edema (fluid in the central retina) can develop, more often in people with diabetes or uveitis."], ["Liver and blood pressure", "Liver enzymes can rise and blood pressure can increase modestly with long-term use."]], "journey": "Ozanimod is taken by mouth and absorbed slowly, peaking after several hours. It is turned into several active metabolites by many enzymes, including ALDH, ADH, CYP3A4 and MAO-B. One major metabolite has a half-life of about 11 days and makes up most of the active drug in blood. Because of this, steady levels build over weeks, and lymphocyte counts usually take about a month, and up to three months, to recover after stopping. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. MAO inhibitors are avoided because MAO-B forms the active metabolite.", "sidefx": "Trapping lymphocytes raises the risk of infections, including herpes viruses and rarely PML or cryptococcal meningitis. The first-dose heart rate drop comes from S1P1 activation on atrial cells before the receptor is internalized, so the dose is titrated. Macular edema may reflect S1P effects on retinal blood vessel barriers. Stopping can cause a rebound of disease activity as lymphocytes flood back. There is no antidote, and effects fade only as the long-lived metabolite clears.", "src": ["Zeposia (ozanimod) FDA label", "Sandborn WJ et al. True North, N Engl J Med 2021", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "etrasimod": {"origin": "Etrasimod is a newer member of the S1P receptor modulator family, which began with fingolimod. It was discovered and developed by Arena Pharmaceuticals in San Diego, with the goal of a selective, once-daily tablet that could start at a full dose without the slow first-week titration that other S1P drugs need. Pfizer acquired Arena in 2022. The FDA approved etrasimod as Velsipity in October 2023 for moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis in adults. It acts on S1P1 along with S1P4 and S1P5 and avoids S1P2 and S1P3.", "timeline": [["2010", "Fingolimod becomes the first approved S1P receptor modulator."], ["2010s", "Arena Pharmaceuticals develops etrasimod as a selective S1P modulator."], ["2022", "Pfizer acquires Arena Pharmaceuticals."], ["2023", "FDA approves Velsipity for ulcerative colitis."]], "cell": "Lymphocytes use the S1P1 receptor to sense the high level of sphingosine 1-phosphate in lymph and blood, which pulls them out of lymph nodes. Etrasimod binds S1P1 and, after brief activation, causes the receptor to be internalized and degraded. Without surface S1P1, the cells cannot follow the S1P signal out of the node. They stay there, and the number of lymphocytes in the blood falls. Fewer gut-homing lymphocytes reach the colon wall, so inflammation in ulcerative colitis falls. Etrasimod also acts on S1P4 and S1P5, but not on S1P2 or S1P3, which are linked to some vascular and lung effects. Activating S1P1 on atrial muscle cells at the start opens potassium channels and briefly slows the heart.", "body": [["Colon", "Fewer lymphocytes reach the colon lining, so bleeding, urgency and diarrhea in ulcerative colitis decrease over weeks."], ["Immune system", "Circulating lymphocytes fall but recover within about one to two weeks after stopping, faster than with ozanimod."], ["Heart", "Heart rate and AV conduction slow briefly after the first dose, so an ECG is checked before starting."], ["Eyes", "Macular edema can occur, so eye exams are recommended for people at higher risk."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise, so liver tests are checked."]], "journey": "Etrasimod is taken once daily by mouth and peaks after about four to five hours. It is highly bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down by oxidation using several CYP enzymes, including CYP2C8, CYP2C9 and CYP3A4. Most of it leaves in feces through bile. The half-life is about 30 hours, so steady levels build within about a week. It does not rely on long-lived active metabolites, which helps lymphocyte counts recover quickly after stopping.", "sidefx": "Holding lymphocytes in lymph nodes increases infection risk, including herpes viruses and rare opportunistic infections such as PML. The early heart rate drop and AV block come from direct S1P1 effects on heart pacemaker tissue before the receptors are internalized. Macular edema likely reflects S1P effects on retinal vessels. Headache, dizziness and liver enzyme rises are common. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Velsipity (etrasimod) FDA label", "Sandborn WJ et al. ELEVATE UC trials, Lancet 2023"]}, "certolizumab-pegol": {"origin": "Certolizumab pegol was developed by the British biotech company Celltech, which UCB of Belgium bought in 2004. By then, TNF-alpha blockers such as infliximab had transformed Crohn's disease and rheumatoid arthritis. Celltech took a different design: instead of a full antibody, it used only the Fab' fragment that binds TNF and attached a large polyethylene glycol (PEG) chain to make it last longer in the body. The FDA approved it as Cimzia for Crohn's disease in 2008, followed by rheumatoid arthritis and later psoriatic arthritis, axial spondyloarthritis and plaque psoriasis. Because it has no Fc portion, very little crosses the placenta, which made it notable for use in pregnancy.", "timeline": [["1990s", "TNF-alpha blockers begin to transform treatment of inflammatory diseases."], ["2004", "UCB acquires Celltech, the developer of certolizumab pegol."], ["2008", "FDA approves Cimzia for Crohn's disease."], ["2009", "Approval extends to rheumatoid arthritis."], ["2018", "Label updated with data showing minimal placental transfer and breast milk transfer."]], "cell": "TNF-alpha is a master inflammatory cytokine made mostly by macrophages and T cells. It exists in a membrane-bound form and a soluble form, and it signals through TNF receptors 1 and 2 to activate NF-kappa-B, which turns on adhesion molecules, other cytokines and enzymes that damage tissue. Certolizumab is a humanized antibody Fab' fragment that binds both forms of TNF-alpha and blocks them from reaching their receptors. It is joined to a 40 kDa PEG chain that slows its clearance. Because it lacks an Fc region, it does not activate complement or antibody-dependent cell killing, and it is not carried across the placenta by FcRn. TNF also holds granulomas together, so blocking it can let latent tuberculosis wake up.", "body": [["Intestine", "Lower TNF signaling reduces gut wall inflammation, helping Crohn's disease symptoms and healing."], ["Joints and spine", "Synovial and spinal inflammation falls, easing pain and slowing joint damage in arthritis."], ["Skin", "Psoriasis plaques improve as TNF-driven inflammation drops."], ["Immune defense", "Serious infections, including TB reactivation and fungal infections, are more likely because TNF helps contain them."], ["Heart", "Heart failure can worsen, a known TNF blocker class effect."], ["Pregnancy", "Very little reaches the fetus because the fragment has no Fc region to ride the placental FcRn transporter."]], "journey": "Certolizumab is injected under the skin of the abdomen or thigh. It is absorbed slowly, peaking several days later. The PEG chain makes the molecule large and slows its removal, giving a half-life of about two weeks. Because it lacks an Fc region, it does not use FcRn recycling the way full antibodies do; PEGylation does that job instead. The protein part is broken down into amino acids, and the PEG part is thought to leave mainly through the kidneys.", "sidefx": "Blocking TNF weakens the body's ability to wall off and kill intracellular germs, which explains TB reactivation, invasive fungal infections and hepatitis B reactivation, so screening is done first. Lymphoma and other cancers are a labeled risk for the class. Worsening heart failure and lupus-like syndromes are TNF blocker class effects. Injection site reactions come from the injection itself. There is no reversal agent.", "src": ["Cimzia (certolizumab pegol) FDA label", "Mariette X et al. CRIB study, Ann Rheum Dis 2018", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "natalizumab": {"origin": "Natalizumab grew from a 1992 study in Nature by Ted Yednock and colleagues, who showed that an antibody against alpha-4 integrin stopped white blood cells from entering the brain in an animal model of multiple sclerosis. Elan and Biogen developed a humanized version. The FDA approved it as Tysabri in November 2004 for relapsing MS. Only three months later, in February 2005, it was pulled from the market after cases of progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML), a brain infection caused by the JC virus. After review, it returned in 2006 under a strict risk program called TOUCH. It was approved for Crohn's disease in 2008. A biosimilar, Tyruko, was approved in 2023.", "timeline": [["1992", "Yednock and colleagues show anti-alpha-4 integrin antibody blocks brain inflammation in animals."], ["2004", "FDA approves Tysabri for relapsing multiple sclerosis."], ["2005", "Tysabri is withdrawn after PML cases."], ["2006", "Tysabri returns under the TOUCH risk management program."], ["2008", "Approval extends to Crohn's disease."], ["2023", "The biosimilar Tyruko is approved."]], "cell": "White blood cells cross blood vessel walls in steps: rolling, firm adhesion and squeezing through. Firm adhesion depends on integrins. Natalizumab is a humanized IgG4 antibody that binds the alpha-4 subunit shared by two integrins. It blocks alpha-4 beta-1 (VLA-4) from binding VCAM-1 on inflamed brain blood vessels, and it blocks alpha-4 beta-7 from binding MAdCAM-1 on gut vessels. Lymphocytes and monocytes cannot stick and enter, so fewer inflammatory cells reach the brain, spinal cord or intestinal wall. As a result, more lymphocytes stay in the blood. The cost is reduced immune surveillance of the brain, which lets the JC virus multiply in some people and cause PML.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Far fewer immune cells enter the CNS, sharply cutting MS relapses and new MRI lesions."], ["Intestine", "Blocking gut entry lowers inflammation in Crohn's disease."], ["Blood", "Lymphocyte counts in the blood rise because cells cannot leave into tissues."], ["Brain infection", "Reduced brain immune surveillance allows JC virus to cause PML, especially with longer use, prior immunosuppressants and positive JC virus antibodies."], ["Liver and eyes", "Liver injury, herpes encephalitis and acute retinal necrosis from herpes viruses have been reported."]], "journey": "Natalizumab is given by IV infusion over about an hour, every four weeks. It stays mostly in the blood and keeps alpha-4 integrins on white cells nearly saturated through the dosing interval. As a protein it is broken down by the reticuloendothelial system into amino acids, not by liver enzymes or the kidneys. Its half-life is about 11 days. In PML, plasma exchange can be used to remove it faster.", "sidefx": "The key danger, PML, follows directly from blocking immune cell entry into the brain, where JC virus can then grow unchecked. Risk is stratified by JC virus antibody status, prior immunosuppressants and treatment length, which is why it is prescribed through a restricted program. Herpes infections reflect the same reduced surveillance. Infusion reactions and anaphylaxis are reactions to the protein, and some people form antibodies that reduce its effect. Plasma exchange can speed removal, but there is no true antidote.", "fun": "Natalizumab was one of the first drugs to return to the market after being withdrawn for safety, thanks to a risk program built around monitoring.", "src": ["Tysabri (natalizumab) FDA label", "Yednock TA et al. Nature 1992", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "lactase": {"origin": "Most mammals lose much of their intestinal lactase after weaning, and the same is true of most of the world's adults. Only some populations carry genetic changes that keep lactase active into adulthood. Lactase supplements turned this into a simple fix. The enzyme beta-galactosidase is made by fermenting fungi and yeasts such as Aspergillus oryzae and Kluyveromyces lactis. In the 1970s Lactaid drops that could be added to milk came to market in the US, and later came tablets taken with dairy foods. Lactase products are sold over the counter as dietary supplements and are not FDA-approved drugs.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Genetic changes for lifelong lactase activity spread among early dairying peoples."], ["1970s", "Lactase drops for treating milk come to market in the US."]], "cell": "Lactose is a sugar made of glucose and galactose joined by a beta-1,4 bond. Normally the brush-border enzyme lactase-phlorizin hydrolase on the tips of small intestinal cells splits it so the two simple sugars can be absorbed by the SGLT1 and GLUT transporters. When lactase is low, lactose stays in the gut. It draws water into the bowel by osmosis and travels to the colon, where bacteria ferment it into gas (hydrogen, carbon dioxide, methane) and short-chain fatty acids. Supplemental beta-galactosidase works in the gut lumen, splitting lactose before it reaches the colon. Less lactose means less osmotic water pull and less bacterial fermentation.", "body": [["Small intestine", "Lactose is split into glucose and galactose, which are absorbed normally."], ["Colon", "Less lactose reaches the colon, so less gas, bloating, cramping and loose stool develop after dairy."], ["Whole body", "The enzyme is not absorbed as an active protein, so it has no systemic effects."]], "journey": "Lactase is taken with the first bite or sip of dairy, or the drops are added to milk ahead of time. It works in the stomach and small intestine, mixing with the food. Stomach acid and gut proteases gradually inactivate and digest it like any other protein, so its action lasts roughly the length of a meal. It is not absorbed as active enzyme and leaves as digested protein pieces.", "sidefx": "Because the enzyme is a protein that is simply digested, side effects are rare. Mild stomach upset can occur. Rare allergic reactions can happen, especially in people sensitive to the source molds or yeasts. It works only on lactose eaten at the same time, so symptoms can still occur if the timing is off. There is no antidote, and none is needed.", "fun": "Adult lactase persistence is one of the clearest examples of recent human evolution, tied to the spread of dairy farming.", "src": ["Lactaid product labeling", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Itan Y et al. PLoS Comput Biol 2009"]}, "orlistat": {"origin": "Orlistat came from soil bacteria. In 1987 researchers at Hoffmann-La Roche reported lipstatin, a natural compound from Streptomyces toxytricini that strongly blocked pancreatic lipase, the main fat-digesting enzyme. Lipstatin was chemically reduced to a more stable version, tetrahydrolipstatin, which became orlistat. Instead of acting on the brain like older diet pills, it worked only in the gut. The FDA approved it as Xenical in 1999 for obesity. In 2007 a lower-strength version, Alli, became the first FDA-approved over-the-counter weight-loss drug in the US.", "timeline": [["1987", "Roche scientists report lipstatin from Streptomyces toxytricini."], ["1999", "FDA approves Xenical for obesity management."], ["2007", "Alli becomes the first FDA-approved OTC weight-loss drug."], ["2010", "FDA adds rare severe liver injury reports to the label."]], "cell": "Dietary fat is mostly triglyceride, which is too large to be absorbed. Gastric and pancreatic lipases split it into free fatty acids and monoglycerides that bile salts gather into micelles. Orlistat has a reactive beta-lactone ring that forms a covalent bond with the serine in the lipase active site. The enzyme is inactivated and cannot split triglycerides. Undigested fat passes through the gut and out in the stool, so roughly a third of the fat in a meal is not absorbed. Fewer absorbed calories lead to modest weight loss. The block is reversible in practice because new enzyme is secreted with each meal, and very little orlistat enters the blood.", "body": [["Gut", "Unabsorbed fat causes oily spotting, gas with discharge, urgency and fatty stools, especially after high-fat meals."], ["Body weight", "Lower calorie absorption produces modest weight loss when combined with diet."], ["Lipids", "LDL cholesterol falls slightly because less dietary fat and cholesterol are absorbed."], ["Vitamins", "Fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K and beta-carotene are absorbed less well, so a multivitamin is taken at a different time."], ["Kidneys", "Unabsorbed fat binds calcium in the gut, leaving more oxalate free to be absorbed, which can cause kidney stones or oxalate nephropathy."]], "journey": "Orlistat is taken with fat-containing meals and works inside the stomach and small intestine. Almost none of it is absorbed into the blood. The small amount that is taken up is metabolized in the gut wall. About 97 percent leaves in the feces, mostly unchanged. Its effect on fat in stool appears within a day or two and fades within two to three days after stopping.", "sidefx": "Nearly all side effects come from fat staying in the bowel. Oily stools, urgency and incontinence are worse with high-fat meals. Reduced uptake of fat-soluble vitamins follows from poor fat absorption. Free fat binds calcium, so dietary oxalate is absorbed more and can form kidney stones. It can also lower absorption of levothyroxine and cyclosporine. Rare severe liver injury has been reported. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Lipstatin, the parent compound, was found by screening microbes for natural products that could block a human digestive enzyme.", "src": ["Xenical (orlistat) FDA label", "Weibel EK et al. J Antibiot 1987", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "phentermine": {"origin": "Phentermine is a close chemical cousin of amphetamine, made by adding an extra methyl group to the carbon next to the amine. In the 1950s, drug makers looked for appetite suppressants that kept amphetamine's hunger-dulling effect with less euphoria and abuse. Phentermine was approved by the FDA in 1959 for short-term weight loss and became one of the most prescribed diet drugs in the US. In the 1990s it was paired with fenfluramine as fen-phen. In 1997 fenfluramine was withdrawn after reports of heart valve disease, but phentermine itself stayed on the market. It is a Schedule IV controlled substance.", "timeline": [["1959", "FDA approves phentermine for short-term weight loss."], ["1992", "A long-term study popularizes the fen-phen combination."], ["1997", "Fenfluramine is withdrawn after heart valve reports; phentermine stays on the market."], ["2012", "Phentermine is approved in a combination with topiramate (Qsymia)."], ["2016", "A lower-strength tablet (Lomaira) is approved."]], "cell": "Phentermine is an indirect sympathomimetic. It enters nerve terminals through the norepinephrine and dopamine transporters (NET and DAT). Inside, it disrupts storage vesicles through VMAT2 and activates TAAR1, causing the transporters to run in reverse. Norepinephrine, and to a lesser degree dopamine and serotonin, spill out into the synapse. In the hypothalamus, more norepinephrine acting on appetite circuits lowers hunger and increases satiety. In the periphery, extra norepinephrine stimulates alpha and beta adrenergic receptors on the heart and blood vessels, raising heart rate and blood pressure. Dopamine release in reward pathways is milder than with amphetamine, which lowers but does not remove abuse potential.", "body": [["Brain (hypothalamus)", "Increased norepinephrine signaling reduces appetite, which helps people eat less during a weight loss program."], ["Brain (arousal)", "Stimulation causes restlessness, insomnia and sometimes anxiety, which is why it is taken in the morning."], ["Heart", "Beta-1 stimulation raises heart rate and can cause palpitations."], ["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 stimulation narrows vessels and can raise blood pressure."], ["Mouth and gut", "Sympathetic tone causes dry mouth, constipation and an unpleasant taste."]], "journey": "Phentermine is taken by mouth and absorbed well, peaking in about three to four hours. It crosses into the brain easily. The liver metabolizes only a small part of it. Most of the drug, roughly 70 to 80 percent, leaves unchanged in urine. Acidic urine traps the drug in its charged form and speeds its removal, while alkaline urine slows it. The half-life is about 20 hours, so a morning dose acts through much of the day.", "sidefx": "Side effects are those of extra norepinephrine: fast heart rate, high blood pressure, insomnia, jitteriness and dry mouth. In overdose it causes a sympathomimetic toxidrome with agitation, hyperthermia, arrhythmias and seizures, treated with supportive care and benzodiazepines. Rare pulmonary hypertension and valve disease have been reported, mostly with fenfluramine combinations. Combining it with MAO inhibitors can cause a hypertensive crisis because norepinephrine breakdown is blocked. Tolerance to the appetite effect develops, and there is potential for misuse. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "The name phentermine comes from phenyl-tertiary-butylamine, which describes its chemical structure.", "src": ["Adipex-P (phentermine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "phentermine-topiramate": {"origin": "This combination pairs two old drugs. Phentermine was approved in 1959 as a short-term appetite suppressant. Topiramate was discovered in 1979 by chemist Bruce Maryanoff at McNeil, a Johnson & Johnson company, while looking for an antidiabetic drug. It turned out to stop seizures and was approved for epilepsy in 1996. Doctors noticed that people taking topiramate often lost weight. The company Vivus combined low-dose phentermine with extended-release topiramate in one capsule. After an initial FDA rejection over safety, Qsymia was approved in July 2012 for chronic weight management in adults, and in 2022 for adolescents 12 and older.", "timeline": [["1959", "FDA approves phentermine for short-term weight loss."], ["1979", "Bruce Maryanoff synthesizes topiramate at McNeil."], ["1996", "Topiramate is approved for epilepsy."], ["2012", "FDA approves Qsymia for chronic weight management."], ["2022", "Approval extends to adolescents 12 and older."]], "cell": "Phentermine enters nerve terminals through norepinephrine and dopamine transporters, disrupts vesicle storage and reverses the transporters, so norepinephrine (and some dopamine) floods the synapse. In the hypothalamus this reduces appetite. Topiramate works on several targets: it blocks voltage-gated sodium channels, enhances GABA-A receptor chloride currents, blocks AMPA and kainate glutamate receptors, and weakly inhibits carbonic anhydrase. Exactly how it reduces appetite is not fully known, but calming excitatory signaling in appetite and reward circuits likely increases satiety and reduces cravings. Using both lets each drug be given at a lower dose than it would need alone, with two different routes to eating less.", "body": [["Brain (appetite)", "Phentermine lowers hunger and topiramate increases fullness, giving greater weight loss than either drug alone."], ["Brain (thinking and mood)", "Topiramate can slow thinking, cause word-finding trouble, and contribute to depression or anxiety."], ["Nerves", "Tingling in the hands and feet (paresthesia) is common and linked to carbonic anhydrase inhibition."], ["Kidneys", "Carbonic anhydrase inhibition causes bicarbonate loss, metabolic acidosis and calcium phosphate kidney stones."], ["Heart", "Phentermine raises heart rate through beta-1 stimulation, and palpitations can occur."], ["Eyes", "Topiramate can rarely cause sudden angle-closure glaucoma by shifting the lens and iris forward."], ["Pregnancy", "Topiramate raises the risk of oral clefts in the fetus, so pregnancy testing and contraception are required by the label program."]], "journey": "The once-daily capsule holds immediate-release phentermine and extended-release topiramate beads. Phentermine peaks in about six hours and topiramate later. Both are taken in the morning to limit insomnia. Phentermine has a half-life of about 20 hours and topiramate about 65 hours. Both are cleared mainly by the kidneys, largely unchanged, so kidney function affects levels. The higher strength is tapered when stopped because stopping topiramate suddenly can provoke seizures.", "sidefx": "Each part brings its own effects. Phentermine causes insomnia, dry mouth and fast heart rate through norepinephrine release. Topiramate's carbonic anhydrase inhibition causes paresthesia, altered taste of carbonated drinks, metabolic acidosis and kidney stones. Its effects on brain excitability explain cognitive slowing and mood changes. Decreased sweating can lead to overheating. Birth defects and angle-closure glaucoma are serious labeled risks. Hemodialysis can remove topiramate in overdose, but there is no antidote.", "src": ["Qsymia (phentermine and topiramate extended-release) FDA label", "Gadde KM et al. CONQUER trial, Lancet 2011", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "naltrexone-bupropion": {"origin": "Naltrexone was approved in 1984 to block opioid effects and later used for alcohol use disorder. Bupropion was approved as an antidepressant in the 1980s and later for smoking cessation, and doctors noted it caused mild weight loss instead of the weight gain seen with many antidepressants. Researchers studying the brain's appetite circuits found that bupropion stimulates POMC neurons in the hypothalamus but that this effect is limited by a natural opioid brake. Adding naltrexone removes the brake. The San Diego company Orexigen Therapeutics developed the extended-release combination. The FDA approved it as Contrave in September 2014 for chronic weight management in adults.", "timeline": [["1984", "FDA approves naltrexone for opioid dependence."], ["1980s", "Bupropion is approved as an antidepressant."], ["1997", "Bupropion is approved for smoking cessation."], ["2014", "FDA approves Contrave for chronic weight management."]], "cell": "In the arcuate nucleus of the hypothalamus, POMC neurons release alpha-MSH, which activates melanocortin-4 receptors to reduce food intake and increase energy use. Bupropion blocks reuptake of norepinephrine and dopamine (through NET and DAT), and this stimulates POMC neurons to fire. But POMC neurons also release beta-endorphin, which acts back on mu-opioid receptors on the same cells and slows them down, a built-in brake. Naltrexone is a mu-opioid receptor antagonist that blocks this autoinhibition, so POMC firing is stronger and lasts longer. Together the two drugs also act on the mesolimbic dopamine reward system, which reduces food craving and the pleasure-driven urge to eat.", "body": [["Brain (appetite)", "Stronger POMC and melanocortin signaling reduces hunger and helps people eat less."], ["Brain (reward)", "Changes in dopamine and opioid reward pathways reduce cravings for highly palatable food."], ["Brain (seizure threshold)", "Bupropion lowers the seizure threshold in a dose-related way."], ["Gut", "Nausea is the most common side effect, along with constipation and vomiting, especially early on."], ["Heart and vessels", "Norepinephrine effects can raise blood pressure and heart rate."], ["Mood", "Bupropion carries a boxed warning for suicidal thoughts in young people, as do other antidepressants."]], "journey": "The extended-release tablet is taken by mouth and released slowly. Bupropion is metabolized in the liver mainly by CYP2B6 to hydroxybupropion, an active metabolite that contributes much of its effect. Naltrexone is converted by dihydrodiol dehydrogenase to 6-beta-naltrexol, also active. Naltrexone's half-life is about five hours and bupropion's about 21 hours. Metabolites leave mainly in urine. High-fat meals greatly increase absorption of both drugs, which is why it is not taken with them. Bupropion also strongly inhibits CYP2D6, which affects other drugs.", "sidefx": "Nausea likely reflects naltrexone and bupropion acting on brain and gut pathways. Bupropion's stimulant-like effects explain insomnia, dry mouth, higher blood pressure and the dose-related seizure risk. Because naltrexone blocks opioid receptors, it can trigger withdrawal in people on opioids, and attempts to overcome the block with large opioid doses can cause fatal overdose. Mood changes and suicidality are labeled warnings. In overdose, bupropion causes seizures and QRS widening, treated with benzodiazepines and sodium bicarbonate. There is no single antidote.", "src": ["Contrave (naltrexone and bupropion) FDA label", "Greenway FL et al. COR-I trial, Lancet 2010", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "resmetirom": {"origin": "Thyroid hormone has long been known to burn fat and lower cholesterol, but using it as a drug causes fast heart rate and bone loss through the thyroid receptor alpha. Scientists noticed that the liver mostly uses a different receptor, thyroid hormone receptor-beta. Resmetirom was designed as a beta-selective agonist that is taken up mainly by the liver. It was discovered at Roche and licensed to Madrigal Pharmaceuticals in 2008. In the MAESTRO-NASH trial it reduced liver fat, resolved steatohepatitis and improved fibrosis in many patients. In March 2024 the FDA granted accelerated approval to Rezdiffra, the first drug approved for MASH (formerly called NASH) with moderate to advanced fibrosis.", "timeline": [["2008", "Madrigal licenses the THR-beta agonist from Roche."], ["2010s", "Clinical trials show reduced liver fat with resmetirom."], ["2024", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Rezdiffra, the first drug for MASH."]], "cell": "Thyroid hormone works through nuclear receptors that act as gene switches. The alpha form dominates in the heart and bone, while the beta form dominates in the liver. Resmetirom is about 28 times more selective for receptor-beta than alpha, and it is taken into liver cells by the transporter OATP1B1, so its effects concentrate in the liver. Inside hepatocytes it binds THR-beta, which pairs with the retinoid X receptor on DNA and turns on genes for mitochondrial fatty acid beta-oxidation, fat breakdown inside lysosomes (lipophagy) and LDL receptor expression. It also lowers new fat synthesis. Less fat buildup means less lipotoxic stress, so inflammation, hepatocyte ballooning and fibrosis can improve. Blood LDL, triglycerides and apolipoprotein B also fall.", "body": [["Liver", "Liver fat falls within months, and in many patients inflammation resolves and scarring stops progressing or improves."], ["Blood lipids", "LDL cholesterol, triglycerides and apolipoprotein B fall because the liver clears more lipoproteins and makes less fat."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and nausea are the most common side effects, usually early and mild."], ["Gallbladder", "Gallstones and gallbladder inflammation occur more often, likely from changes in bile composition."], ["Heart and bone", "Because THR-alpha is largely spared, heart rate and bone effects seen with thyroid hormone are minimal."]], "journey": "Resmetirom is taken by mouth once daily and peaks in about four hours. It is more than 99 percent bound to plasma proteins. Liver uptake through OATP1B1 concentrates it at its site of action. It is metabolized mainly by CYP2C8 to a less active metabolite. The half-life is about four and a half hours, and it is eliminated mostly through bile. Strong CYP2C8 inhibitors such as gemfibrozil raise exposure, and it can raise levels of some statins.", "sidefx": "GI upset, mainly diarrhea and nausea, is common and the reason is not fully known. Liver injury is a labeled risk, so liver tests are checked. Gallbladder problems likely relate to changes in bile acids and cholesterol handling in the liver. Because it acts on a thyroid hormone receptor, thyroid levels can shift slightly. Its beta selectivity is meant to avoid the heart rhythm and bone loss problems of thyroid hormone excess. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Rezdiffra (resmetirom) FDA label", "Harrison SA et al. MAESTRO-NASH, N Engl J Med 2024"]}, "ferric-citrate": {"origin": "People with kidney failure cannot remove enough phosphate, and high phosphate causes bone disease and vascular calcification. Older phosphate binders used aluminum, which caused toxicity, or calcium, which added to calcium overload. Researchers looked at iron, which also binds phosphate tightly in the gut. Ferric citrate, a simple iron salt long known in chemistry, was developed as a phosphate binder and brought to the US by Keryx Biopharmaceuticals. The FDA approved it as Auryxia in 2014 to control phosphate in dialysis patients. In 2017 it was also approved to treat iron deficiency anemia in chronic kidney disease not on dialysis, making it a two-in-one drug.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Aluminum phosphate binders are linked to aluminum toxicity in dialysis patients."], ["2014", "FDA approves Auryxia as a phosphate binder for dialysis patients."], ["2017", "Approval extends to iron deficiency anemia in CKD not on dialysis."]], "cell": "Ferric citrate dissolves in the gut and releases ferric (Fe3+) iron. In the gut lumen, ferric iron binds dietary phosphate to form ferric phosphate, which is insoluble and cannot cross the gut wall. This complex leaves in the stool, so less phosphate reaches the blood. Some ferric iron is reduced to ferrous (Fe2+) iron by duodenal cytochrome b reductase on intestinal cells and enters through DMT1. Inside the cell, iron leaves through ferroportin into blood, where it binds transferrin. This refills iron stores, raises ferritin and transferrin saturation, and supports hemoglobin production. The citrate part is absorbed and metabolized, and it can increase absorption of aluminum, so aluminum products are avoided.", "body": [["Gut", "Phosphate from food is trapped as ferric phosphate and leaves in stool. Stools turn dark from iron."], ["Blood phosphate", "Serum phosphorus falls within one to two weeks."], ["Blood and iron stores", "Absorbed iron raises ferritin, transferrin saturation and hemoglobin, which can reduce the need for IV iron or anemia drugs."], ["Bones and vessels", "Lower phosphate helps limit secondary hyperparathyroidism and vascular calcification in kidney disease."], ["Gut symptoms", "Diarrhea, constipation, nausea and abdominal pain are common."]], "journey": "Ferric citrate is taken by mouth with meals so it meets dietary phosphate. Most of the iron stays in the gut and leaves as ferric phosphate in feces. A portion is absorbed through the normal iron pathway in the duodenum and then handled like dietary iron, bound to transferrin and stored as ferritin. The body has no active way to excrete iron, so absorbed iron is lost only slowly through shedding of cells and blood loss. Iron levels are monitored to avoid overload.", "sidefx": "GI effects and dark stools come from iron in the gut. Because some iron is absorbed and the body cannot excrete excess iron, iron overload is possible, so ferritin and transferrin saturation are checked. Accidental ingestion by young children can cause serious iron poisoning, with vomiting, GI bleeding, shock and liver injury. Deferoxamine is the antidote for serious systemic iron toxicity. Iron also binds some antibiotics such as doxycycline and ciprofloxacin, lowering their absorption.", "src": ["Auryxia (ferric citrate) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "penicillin-v": {"origin": "Penicillin was discovered by Alexander Fleming in 1928 and made into a usable drug by Howard Florey and Ernst Chain's team at Oxford in the early 1940s. The first form, penicillin G, was destroyed by stomach acid, so it had to be injected. In 1948 chemists at Eli Lilly made phenoxymethylpenicillin by feeding a different precursor chemical to the Penicillium mold. In 1951 Ernst Brandl and Hans Margreiter at the Biochemie company in Kundl, Austria, noticed that this form survived acid. It could be swallowed as a tablet, and it became penicillin V. It made oral treatment of strep throat and prevention of rheumatic fever simple, and it remains a first-choice drug for strep throat today.", "timeline": [["1928", "Alexander Fleming discovers penicillin from Penicillium mold."], ["1940s", "Florey and Chain's Oxford team develop penicillin as a drug."], ["1948", "Eli Lilly chemists make phenoxymethylpenicillin by precursor feeding."], ["1951", "Brandl and Margreiter in Kundl, Austria, recognize its acid stability for oral use."]], "cell": "Penicillin V is a beta-lactam. Bacteria build their cell wall from peptidoglycan, long sugar chains linked by short peptides. Penicillin-binding proteins (transpeptidases) form those links by grabbing the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of the peptides. The beta-lactam ring looks like that end, so the PBP attacks it and becomes permanently acylated and inactive. The wall can no longer be cross-linked as the bacterium grows. Autolysin enzymes keep cutting the wall, and the cell bursts from internal pressure. Killing is time-dependent, so keeping levels above the MIC matters more than high peaks. The phenoxymethyl side chain resists acid breakdown, but the drug is still destroyed by bacterial beta-lactamase (penicillinase).", "body": [["Throat", "Kills Streptococcus pyogenes in strep throat, which also prevents rheumatic fever."], ["Heart", "Long-term use prevents strep reinfection and new damage to heart valves in people who have had rheumatic fever."], ["Mouth and teeth", "Active against many oral streptococci and anaerobes involved in dental infections."], ["Gut", "Nausea and diarrhea occur, and disrupting gut flora can allow C. difficile overgrowth."], ["Skin and immune system", "Allergic reactions range from rash to hives to rare anaphylaxis."]], "journey": "Penicillin V is taken by mouth and absorbed in the upper small intestine, with blood levels a bit higher on an empty stomach. It peaks within about an hour and spreads into most tissues, but it reaches the brain poorly unless the meninges are inflamed. A part is broken down in the liver to penicilloic acid. Most is removed quickly by the kidneys through tubular secretion, giving a short half-life of about 30 to 60 minutes. Probenecid blocks this secretion and keeps levels higher.", "sidefx": "Most serious reactions are allergic. The beta-lactam ring opens and binds body proteins, creating new antigens that the immune system can react to, causing rash, hives, serum sickness or anaphylaxis. Epinephrine treats anaphylaxis. GI upset and C. difficile diarrhea come from killing normal gut bacteria. Very high levels in kidney failure can cause seizures because penicillins can block GABA-A receptors. Hemodialysis removes it.", "fun": "Penicillin V's acid stability was spotted in a small Austrian village, and the Kundl factory is still a major penicillin producer today.", "src": ["Penicillin V potassium FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "cefuroxime": {"origin": "Cephalosporins began in 1945 when Italian scientist Giuseppe Brotzu found a mold, now called Acremonium, near a sewage outlet in Sardinia that made antibacterial compounds. Oxford researchers led by Edward Abraham and Guy Newton isolated cephalosporin C in the 1950s. Its core could be modified to make many new drugs. Scientists at Glaxo in Britain developed cefuroxime in the 1970s, adding a methoxyimino side chain that shields it from many beta-lactamases. It was launched as an injection in the late 1970s and became a classic second-generation cephalosporin. An oral prodrug, cefuroxime axetil (Ceftin), was approved in the US in the 1980s.", "timeline": [["1945", "Giuseppe Brotzu finds a cephalosporin-producing mold in Sardinia."], ["1950s", "Abraham and Newton at Oxford isolate cephalosporin C."], ["1970s", "Glaxo develops cefuroxime with a beta-lactamase-resistant side chain."], ["1980s", "Oral cefuroxime axetil is approved in the US."]], "cell": "Cefuroxime is a beta-lactam. Its ring mimics the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of peptidoglycan building blocks, so penicillin-binding proteins attack it and become permanently acylated. They can no longer cross-link the cell wall, and autolysins weaken the wall until the bacterium bursts. Killing is time-dependent. The methoxyimino group on its side chain blocks many common beta-lactamases, including those made by Haemophilus influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis, which is why it covers these respiratory pathogens better than first-generation drugs. It does not cover Pseudomonas, enterococci or MRSA, whose PBP2a binds it poorly.", "body": [["Lungs and sinuses", "Kills common respiratory pathogens such as Streptococcus pneumoniae, H. influenzae and Moraxella, so it treats sinusitis, bronchitis and pneumonia."], ["Skin and bone", "Covers MSSA and streptococci in skin and some bone infections."], ["Urinary tract", "High urine levels treat infections from E. coli, Klebsiella and Proteus."], ["Joints and nerves (Lyme)", "The oral form treats early Lyme disease, and killing spirochetes can cause a Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction."], ["Surgical sites", "Given before surgery, it lowers infection risk from skin bacteria."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is common and C. difficile can overgrow when normal flora are killed."]], "journey": "Cefuroxime sodium is given IV or IM and reaches blood quickly. The oral form, cefuroxime axetil, is an ester prodrug that is absorbed better with food and then split in the gut wall and blood to free cefuroxime. It spreads to most tissues and, at high IV levels, can enter inflamed meninges. It is not metabolized and is removed unchanged by the kidneys, with a half-life of about 80 minutes. Oral and IV forms are not interchangeable by amount because of the difference in absorption.", "sidefx": "Allergic reactions arise when the opened beta-lactam ring binds proteins and triggers immune responses, ranging from rash to anaphylaxis. Cross-reactivity with penicillin allergy is low but possible. Diarrhea and C. difficile follow disruption of gut flora. IV use can irritate veins. Very high levels in kidney failure can cause seizures and encephalopathy, and hemodialysis removes it. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Zinacef (cefuroxime) FDA label", "Ceftin (cefuroxime axetil) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "cefpodoxime": {"origin": "Cefpodoxime belongs to the third-generation cephalosporins, drugs built on the cephalosporin core first isolated from an Acremonium mold found in Sardinia in 1945. Most third-generation drugs had to be injected because they were poorly absorbed. Cefpodoxime was developed by the Japanese company Sankyo as an ester prodrug, cefpodoxime proxetil, that could be absorbed by mouth and then turned into the active drug. It was approved in the US in the early 1990s and sold as Vantin, which is now discontinued. Generic versions remain available, giving an oral option with broader gram-negative coverage than older oral cephalosporins.", "timeline": [["1945", "Giuseppe Brotzu discovers the cephalosporin-producing mold."], ["1980s", "Sankyo develops cefpodoxime proxetil as an oral prodrug."], ["1990s", "Cefpodoxime is approved in the US as Vantin."]], "cell": "Cefpodoxime proxetil is inactive. Esterases in the intestinal wall cut off the proxetil group, releasing cefpodoxime into the blood. The active drug binds penicillin-binding proteins, especially PBP3, and acylates them through its beta-lactam ring, so bacteria cannot cross-link peptidoglycan. Gram-negative rods exposed to PBP3 blockade form long filaments and then lyse. Its side chain makes it stable against many common beta-lactamases, so it covers H. influenzae, Moraxella, E. coli, Klebsiella, Proteus and Neisseria gonorrhoeae, plus streptococci. It does not cover Pseudomonas, enterococci or MRSA. Killing is time-dependent.", "body": [["Lungs and sinuses", "Treats community-acquired pneumonia, sinusitis and ear infections caused by pneumococci, H. influenzae and Moraxella."], ["Throat", "Kills group A Streptococcus pyogenes in strep throat (pharyngitis)."], ["Urinary tract", "Covers common gram-negative bladder pathogens, reaching high urine levels."], ["Genital tract", "Has activity against Neisseria gonorrhoeae."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and C. difficile infection follow disruption of normal flora."]], "journey": "Cefpodoxime proxetil is taken by mouth, and food improves absorption of the tablet. Stomach acid helps it dissolve, so antacids and H2 blockers lower absorption. Esterases in the gut wall release active cefpodoxime, which peaks in two to three hours. It spreads into lung, tonsil and other tissues. It is barely metabolized further and leaves unchanged in urine, with a half-life of two to three hours that lengthens in kidney disease.", "sidefx": "Like other beta-lactams, allergic reactions come from the opened ring forming protein complexes that the immune system targets, from rash to anaphylaxis. GI upset and vaginal yeast infections reflect disturbed normal flora, and C. difficile diarrhea is possible. Accumulation in kidney failure can cause seizures. Hemodialysis removes it. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Cefpodoxime proxetil FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "moxifloxacin": {"origin": "The quinolone story began by accident in 1962, when George Lesher and colleagues at Sterling-Winthrop found nalidixic acid as a by-product while making the antimalarial chloroquine. Adding a fluorine atom in the 1970s and 1980s created the fluoroquinolones, which were stronger and broader. Moxifloxacin was developed by Bayer in the 1990s with a methoxy group at the C-8 position that improved activity against Streptococcus pneumoniae and anaerobes. The FDA approved it as Avelox in 1999. It became known as a respiratory fluoroquinolone. Eye drops (Vigamox) followed. FDA boxed warnings about tendon rupture and other disabling effects were added to the class in 2008 and expanded in 2016.", "timeline": [["1962", "George Lesher's team discovers nalidixic acid, the first quinolone."], ["1980s", "Fluorinated quinolones broaden the class."], ["1999", "FDA approves moxifloxacin (Avelox)."], ["2008", "FDA adds a boxed warning on tendon rupture to fluoroquinolones."], ["2016", "FDA expands warnings and limits use for uncomplicated infections."]], "cell": "Bacteria must unwind and untangle their DNA to copy it. DNA gyrase (topoisomerase II) adds negative supercoils ahead of the replication fork, and topoisomerase IV separates linked daughter chromosomes. Both enzymes cut both DNA strands, pass DNA through and reseal the break. Moxifloxacin binds the enzyme-DNA complex, with the help of a magnesium bridge, and freezes it after the cut but before resealing. These trapped complexes block replication forks and turn into permanent double-strand breaks, which kill the bacterium. Killing is concentration-dependent. The C-8 methoxy group strengthens binding to gram-positive topoisomerase IV and gyrase, which improves pneumococcal coverage and reduces resistance selection.", "body": [["Lungs", "Kills pneumococci (including penicillin-resistant strains), H. influenzae and atypical bacteria such as Legionella and Mycoplasma in pneumonia."], ["Sinuses and airways", "Treats sinusitis and bronchitis flares, though it is reserved when other options fit."], ["Abdomen and skin", "Covers many anaerobes, so it treats some abdominal and skin infections."], ["Heart", "Blocks hERG potassium channels, prolonging the QT interval and raising torsades risk."], ["Tendons and nerves", "Can damage tendons, especially the Achilles, and cause peripheral neuropathy."], ["Blood vessels and glucose", "Linked to aortic aneurysm and dissection, and to both low and high blood sugar."]], "journey": "Moxifloxacin is well absorbed by mouth, nearly as well as IV, peaking in one to three hours. Calcium, magnesium, aluminum, iron and zinc bind it in the gut, so these are separated from doses. It penetrates lung tissue and many other tissues well. Unlike many fluoroquinolones, it is metabolized by glucuronidation and sulfation in the liver rather than by CYP enzymes, and it leaves in both feces and urine. Its half-life of about 12 hours allows once-daily dosing, and it does not need kidney dose changes.", "sidefx": "Fluoroquinolones chelate metals and may damage connective tissue by disrupting collagen and matrix in tendons and the aortic wall, explaining tendon rupture and aortic dissection. Blocking the cardiac hERG potassium channel prolongs QT. Effects on GABA-A receptors and the brain cause dizziness, confusion and seizures. Glucose swings may reflect effects on pancreatic potassium channels. Killing gut flora can lead to C. difficile. There is no antidote, and dialysis removes little.", "src": ["Avelox (moxifloxacin) FDA label", "FDA Drug Safety Communication on fluoroquinolones, 2016", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "ofloxacin": {"origin": "Ofloxacin was developed by the Japanese company Daiichi Seiyaku in the early 1980s, during the wave of fluorinated quinolones that followed the 1962 discovery of nalidixic acid. It was approved in the US around 1990 as Floxin, a brand that is now discontinued. Ofloxacin is a racemic mixture, meaning it contains two mirror-image forms. Daiichi scientists found that nearly all the antibacterial activity came from one form, the S-enantiomer. That single form was developed separately and became levofloxacin, approved in the US in 1996. Ofloxacin remains in use mainly as eye and ear drops, with oral tablets still available.", "timeline": [["1962", "Nalidixic acid, the first quinolone, is discovered."], ["1980s", "Daiichi Seiyaku develops ofloxacin in Japan."], ["1990", "Ofloxacin is approved in the US as Floxin around this time."], ["1996", "Levofloxacin, the active S-form of ofloxacin, is approved in the US."]], "cell": "Ofloxacin blocks two bacterial type II topoisomerases. DNA gyrase is the main target in gram-negative bacteria, and topoisomerase IV is a key target in gram-positive bacteria. These enzymes cut both strands of DNA to relieve supercoiling or separate daughter chromosomes. Ofloxacin binds the cut enzyme-DNA complex, linked through magnesium, and prevents resealing. Stalled complexes collide with replication machinery and become lethal double-strand breaks. Killing is concentration-dependent. The S-enantiomer (levofloxacin) binds the target far better than the R-enantiomer, so ofloxacin is roughly half as potent by weight as levofloxacin.", "body": [["Urinary tract and prostate", "Reaches high urine and prostate levels, treating gram-negative bladder and prostate infections."], ["Genital tract", "Active against Chlamydia trachomatis and some other sexually transmitted organisms."], ["Eyes and ears", "Drops treat bacterial conjunctivitis, corneal ulcers and outer or middle ear infections with very little reaching the blood."], ["Heart", "Can prolong the QT interval by blocking cardiac potassium channels."], ["Tendons and nerves", "Shares class risks of tendon rupture and peripheral neuropathy."], ["Brain", "Insomnia, dizziness and rare seizures reflect CNS effects of fluoroquinolones."]], "journey": "Oral ofloxacin is almost completely absorbed, peaking within one to two hours. Metal ions in antacids and supplements bind it and lower absorption. It spreads widely into tissues, including the prostate. Very little is metabolized; about 70 to 80 percent leaves unchanged in urine, so doses depend on kidney function. The half-life is about four to eight hours. Eye and ear drops act locally and give only tiny blood levels.", "sidefx": "Side effects match the fluoroquinolone class. Metal chelation and toxicity to connective tissue cells explain tendon injury. Blocking GABA-A receptors and other brain effects cause insomnia, dizziness and rare seizures. hERG potassium channel block prolongs QT. Blood sugar swings and photosensitivity are class effects. Killing normal flora can cause C. difficile and vaginitis. There is no antidote, and dialysis removes little.", "src": ["Ofloxacin tablets FDA label", "Ocuflox (ofloxacin ophthalmic) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "minocycline": {"origin": "Tetracyclines began with soil bacteria. In 1948 Benjamin Duggar at Lederle Laboratories described chlortetracycline (Aureomycin) from Streptomyces aureofaciens. Chemists then modified the four-ring tetracycline core to make new versions. Minocycline was made at Lederle in the 1960s by semi-synthesis and entered US use in the early 1970s as Minocin. It was more fat-soluble than older tetracyclines, so it reached skin, brain and other tissues well and worked against some resistant staph. It became a mainstay for acne. Later came extended-release tablets for acne and a topical foam, and IV minocycline regained interest for multidrug-resistant Acinetobacter.", "timeline": [["1948", "Benjamin Duggar describes chlortetracycline from Streptomyces aureofaciens."], ["1960s", "Lederle chemists create minocycline by semi-synthesis."], ["1970s", "Minocycline enters US use as Minocin."], ["2006", "Extended-release minocycline is approved for acne."], ["2019", "A topical minocycline foam is approved for acne."]], "cell": "Minocycline enters bacteria by passive diffusion and through porins, then binds the 16S rRNA of the 30S ribosomal subunit. This blocks aminoacyl-tRNA from docking at the ribosome's A site, so new amino acids cannot be added to growing proteins. Bacterial growth stops, making it bacteriostatic. Its high fat solubility lets it cross membranes and reach high levels in skin, sebaceous glands and the CNS. It is less affected by some tetracycline efflux pumps, so it keeps activity against many staph including MRSA. Separately, it has anti-inflammatory effects: it inhibits matrix metalloproteinases, reduces neutrophil chemotaxis and lowers inflammatory cytokines, which helps in acne and rosacea.", "body": [["Skin", "Reduces Cutibacterium acnes and calms inflammation in acne. Long-term use can cause blue-gray pigmentation of skin and scars."], ["Inner ear and brain", "Dizziness and vertigo are more common than with other tetracyclines because it crosses into the CNS easily."], ["Brain pressure", "Can cause pseudotumor cerebri (raised intracranial pressure) with headache and vision changes."], ["Teeth and bones", "Binds calcium in growing teeth and bones, staining teeth in young children."], ["Immune system and liver", "Can trigger drug-induced lupus and autoimmune hepatitis with long-term use."], ["Skin and sun", "Photosensitivity can occur, though less than with doxycycline."]], "journey": "Minocycline is absorbed well by mouth, with less loss from food than older tetracyclines, though calcium, iron, magnesium and aluminum still bind it. It peaks in one to four hours and spreads widely, including into skin, sebaceous glands, saliva, tears and CSF. It is partly metabolized in the liver. Compared with other tetracyclines, less leaves in the urine and more leaves in the feces. The half-life is about 15 to 20 hours.", "sidefx": "Its lipid solubility, which helps it reach tissues, also explains vestibular side effects like dizziness and vertigo. Pigmentation comes from drug or its breakdown products depositing with iron or melanin in tissues. Tooth staining reflects chelation of calcium in developing teeth. Immune reactions such as drug-induced lupus, autoimmune hepatitis and DRESS are linked to minocycline specifically. GI upset and C. difficile come from disturbing gut flora. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Minocycline has been studied for neuroprotective effects in stroke and other brain diseases because it enters the brain well and has anti-inflammatory activity.", "src": ["Minocin (minocycline) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Duggar BM. Ann N Y Acad Sci 1948"]}, "fidaxomicin": {"origin": "Fidaxomicin is a natural product from soil actinomycete bacteria. Related compounds called lipiarmycins were described in Italy in the 1970s, and tiacumicins were later isolated from Dactylosporangium aurantiacum. Fidaxomicin (tiacumicin B) had a narrow spectrum: strong against Clostridioides difficile but weak against most normal gut bacteria. The California company Optimer Pharmaceuticals developed it for C. difficile infection, and in trials it cured as many patients as vancomycin with fewer recurrences. The FDA approved it as Dificid in May 2011, the first new C. difficile drug in decades. Merck later acquired it, and a pediatric suspension was approved in 2020.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Related lipiarmycin antibiotics are described from soil actinomycetes."], ["1980s", "Tiacumicins are isolated from Dactylosporangium aurantiacum."], ["2011", "FDA approves Dificid for C. difficile-associated diarrhea."], ["2020", "Approval extends to children with an oral suspension."]], "cell": "Fidaxomicin binds bacterial DNA-dependent RNA polymerase at the switch region, a hinge that must move for the enzyme to open DNA and start transcription. By locking this hinge, fidaxomicin prevents the strands from separating, so transcription cannot begin. This site is different from where rifamycins bind, so there is no cross-resistance. Without RNA synthesis, C. difficile cannot make proteins and dies, making it bactericidal against this organism. It also suppresses spore formation and toxin production. Its narrow spectrum spares Bacteroides and other protective anaerobes, so the normal microbiome recovers sooner and C. difficile has less room to return.", "body": [["Colon", "Kills C. difficile and lowers toxin output, so diarrhea and colitis resolve."], ["Gut microbiome", "Normal anaerobes like Bacteroides are largely spared, which lowers the chance of a recurrence after treatment."], ["Blood and bone marrow", "Anemia and neutropenia were reported in trials, though a cause and effect link is uncertain."], ["Immune system", "Rare hypersensitivity reactions such as rash, angioedema or anaphylaxis can occur."]], "journey": "Fidaxomicin is taken by mouth and is barely absorbed, so very high levels stay inside the gut where C. difficile lives. A small amount is hydrolyzed to the active metabolite OP-1118. Blood levels are very low, in the nanogram range. More than 92 percent leaves in the feces as the parent drug and metabolite. The half-life in plasma is about 8 to 10 hours, but that has little clinical meaning because the drug acts in the bowel.", "sidefx": "Because so little is absorbed, systemic side effects are uncommon. Nausea, vomiting and abdominal pain reflect local gut effects. GI bleeding and low blood counts were reported in trials but may relate to the illness itself. Hypersensitivity can occur, especially in people with macrolide allergy. Because it does not reach the blood, it does not treat infections outside the gut. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Dificid (fidaxomicin) FDA label", "Louie TJ et al. N Engl J Med 2011", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "fosfomycin": {"origin": "Fosfomycin was found in 1969 by a joint team from Merck in the US and CEPA in Spain, who isolated it from Streptomyces fradiae and related soil bacteria collected in Spain. The team described it in the journal Science. It had a new chemical structure, a small phosphonic acid with an epoxide ring, unlike any other antibiotic. In the US an oral salt, fosfomycin tromethamine, was approved as Monurol in 1996 as a single-dose granule for bladder infections. Interest has grown again because it still works against many drug-resistant E. coli strains, including those making extended-spectrum beta-lactamases.", "timeline": [["1969", "Merck and CEPA scientists report fosfomycin from Streptomyces in Science."], ["1996", "FDA approves Monurol for uncomplicated cystitis."], ["2010s", "Renewed interest as fosfomycin keeps activity against many ESBL-producing E. coli."]], "cell": "Fosfomycin enters bacteria through transporters meant for nutrients: the glycerol-3-phosphate transporter (GlpT) and the hexose phosphate transporter (UhpT). Inside, it resembles phosphoenolpyruvate. It binds the enzyme MurA and forms a covalent bond with a cysteine in its active site, permanently inactivating it. MurA performs the first committed step in peptidoglycan synthesis, adding an enolpyruvyl group to UDP-N-acetylglucosamine to begin making N-acetylmuramic acid. Without this step, no new cell wall precursors are made, the wall weakens and the bacterium dies. Because this target is earlier than beta-lactam targets, there is little cross-resistance. Resistance usually comes from mutations in the uptake transporters.", "body": [["Bladder", "High urine levels kill E. coli and Enterococcus faecalis, curing uncomplicated cystitis with a single dose."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and nausea can occur, and C. difficile is possible."], ["Genital tract", "Vaginitis can follow changes in normal vaginal flora."], ["Whole body", "Blood levels are modest, so the oral form is not used for kidney or bloodstream infections."]], "journey": "The granules are dissolved in water and swallowed in one dose. Part of the dose is absorbed and peaks in blood in about two hours. Fosfomycin is not bound to proteins and is not metabolized. The kidneys filter it out unchanged, building very high urine levels that stay above killing concentrations for one to two days. Some unabsorbed drug leaves in feces. The serum half-life is about six hours.", "sidefx": "Fosfomycin's target, MurA, does not exist in human cells, so side effects are few. Diarrhea and nausea come from local gut effects and changes in flora, and C. difficile is possible. Headache and dizziness can occur. Rare allergic reactions including angioedema have been reported. There is no antidote, and hydration helps urinary elimination.", "src": ["Monurol (fosfomycin tromethamine) FDA label", "Hendlin D et al. Science 1969"]}, "methenamine": {"origin": "Methenamine (hexamethylenetetramine) was first made in 1859 by the Russian chemist Aleksandr Butlerov, who combined formaldehyde and ammonia. In 1895 the German physician Arthur Nicolaier showed that it released formaldehyde in urine and could act as a urinary antiseptic. It was sold as Urotropin and became one of the earliest synthetic drugs for urinary infections, decades before antibiotics. Today it is given as methenamine hippurate or mandelate, salts whose acids help keep urine acidic. With growing antibiotic resistance, it has gained new attention as a non-antibiotic way to prevent recurrent bladder infections.", "timeline": [["1859", "Aleksandr Butlerov synthesizes hexamethylenetetramine."], ["1895", "Arthur Nicolaier shows it works as a urinary antiseptic."], ["1890s", "Methenamine is marketed as Urotropin."], ["2022", "The ALTAR trial supports methenamine as an alternative to antibiotics for preventing recurrent UTIs."]], "cell": "Methenamine itself has no antibacterial action. It is a prodrug that breaks apart in acid. In urine with a pH of about 5.5 or lower, it hydrolyzes into formaldehyde and ammonia. Formaldehyde is a nonspecific antiseptic. It reacts with amino and other groups on bacterial proteins and nucleic acids, forming cross-links that denature them and kill the cell. Because the attack is chemical and broad, bacteria do not develop resistance in the usual way. Hydrolysis takes time and needs acid, so it works in the bladder where urine sits, not in blood or alkaline urine. Hippuric or mandelic acid in the salt helps keep urine acidic.", "body": [["Bladder", "Formaldehyde forms in stored acidic urine and kills bacteria, preventing repeat infections."], ["Kidneys", "Little formaldehyde forms in the upper urinary tract because urine moves through quickly, so it does not treat kidney infections."], ["Stomach", "Some breakdown in stomach acid releases formaldehyde, causing nausea and upset stomach."], ["Urinary lining", "High formaldehyde levels can irritate the bladder, causing painful urination or blood in the urine."]], "journey": "Methenamine is taken by mouth and absorbed from the gut, though some is broken down by stomach acid first, which is why enteric-coated forms exist. In the near-neutral pH of blood it stays intact. The kidneys filter it into urine. In acidic bladder urine it slowly hydrolyzes to formaldehyde over about one to two hours, so the longer urine stays in the bladder, the more formaldehyde forms. Its half-life is about four hours. Alkalinizing agents such as antacids and citrates block its effect.", "sidefx": "Side effects come from formaldehyde made in the wrong place or in excess. Formaldehyde made in the stomach upsets it. Excess formaldehyde in the bladder irritates the lining, causing dysuria and hematuria. Rash and liver enzyme rises can occur. It is avoided with sulfonamides because formaldehyde can form insoluble precipitates with them in urine. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Hexamethylenetetramine is also used in solid camping fuel tablets and was one of the first compounds whose crystal structure was solved by X-ray crystallography.", "src": ["Hiprex (methenamine hippurate) FDA label", "Harding C et al. ALTAR trial, BMJ 2022", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "trimethoprim": {"origin": "Trimethoprim came from the lab of George Hitchings and Gertrude Elion at Burroughs Wellcome, who pioneered rational drug design by studying how cells use nucleic acid building blocks. They looked for chemicals that would block the enzyme dihydrofolate reductase in bacteria far more than in humans. Trimethoprim, made in the late 1950s and early 1960s, did just that. In 1968 it was launched in Britain combined with sulfamethoxazole, and the combination reached the US in the 1970s. Trimethoprim alone is used mainly for bladder infections. Hitchings and Elion shared the 1988 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for this approach to drug discovery.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Hitchings and Elion's lab designs dihydrofolate reductase inhibitors."], ["1968", "Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is launched in Britain."], ["1970s", "The combination is introduced in the US."], ["1988", "Hitchings and Elion share the Nobel Prize for rational drug design."]], "cell": "Bacteria must make their own folate. Dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR) converts dihydrofolate to tetrahydrofolate, the carrier of one-carbon units needed to make thymidine, purines and some amino acids. Trimethoprim competitively blocks bacterial DHFR and binds it thousands of times more tightly than the human enzyme, which is why it is selective. Without tetrahydrofolate, DNA synthesis stalls and growth stops, so alone it is bacteriostatic. Sulfamethoxazole blocks an earlier step (dihydropteroate synthase), so the combination produces a sequential blockade that is often bactericidal. In the kidney, trimethoprim also blocks the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) in the distal nephron like amiloride, and it inhibits tubular secretion of creatinine.", "body": [["Urinary tract", "Concentrates in urine and kills common bladder pathogens such as E. coli and Proteus."], ["Kidneys (potassium)", "Blocking ENaC reduces potassium excretion, which can cause hyperkalemia, especially at high doses or with ACE inhibitors."], ["Kidneys (creatinine)", "Serum creatinine can rise without true kidney damage because tubular creatinine secretion is blocked."], ["Bone marrow", "In people with low folate, it can cause megaloblastic anemia and low white cells or platelets."], ["Skin", "Rash and itching are common, and rare severe reactions such as SJS and TEN can occur."]], "journey": "Trimethoprim is taken by mouth and absorbed almost completely, peaking in one to four hours. It spreads widely, including into prostate fluid and vaginal secretions. About 10 to 20 percent is metabolized in the liver, and about half or more leaves unchanged in urine within a day, giving high urinary levels. The half-life is about 8 to 10 hours and lengthens with kidney disease.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from its two known actions. Mild inhibition of human DHFR can cause megaloblastic anemia and low blood counts, especially in people with folate deficiency, and leucovorin (folinic acid) bypasses the block. Blocking ENaC in the collecting duct causes potassium retention and hyperkalemia. Blocking creatinine secretion raises creatinine without real injury. Rash and severe skin reactions are immune reactions. Hemodialysis partially removes it.", "src": ["Trimethoprim tablets FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1988 press release"]}, "nitazoxanide": {"origin": "Nitazoxanide was first synthesized in the 1970s by Jean-Francois Rossignol and colleagues, initially studied as a drug against tapeworms. It belongs to a family called thiazolides. Rossignol later co-founded Romark Laboratories in Florida, which developed the drug for protozoa that cause diarrhea. In 2002 the FDA approved nitazoxanide as Alinia for children with diarrhea caused by Giardia or Cryptosporidium, and approval for adults followed in 2004. It was the first FDA-approved treatment for Cryptosporidium diarrhea, a parasite that spreads through contaminated water and swimming pools and is resistant to chlorine.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Nitazoxanide is first synthesized and studied against tapeworms."], ["2002", "FDA approves Alinia for children with Giardia or Cryptosporidium diarrhea."], ["2004", "Approval extends to adults."]], "cell": "After absorption, nitazoxanide is quickly turned into its active form, tizoxanide. Tizoxanide blocks pyruvate:ferredoxin oxidoreductase (PFOR), an enzyme that Giardia, Cryptosporidium and some anaerobic bacteria use instead of mitochondrial pyruvate dehydrogenase. PFOR moves electrons from pyruvate to ferredoxin while making acetyl-CoA, a core step of energy metabolism in these organisms. The drug interferes with the cofactor thiamine pyrophosphate in this reaction rather than binding the enzyme directly. Without this step, the parasites cannot make enough energy and die or stop multiplying. Human cells use a different enzyme, which helps explain selectivity.", "body": [["Intestine", "Giardia and Cryptosporidium are cleared, so diarrhea and cramping resolve within days."], ["Urine", "Yellow-colored metabolites can tint the urine, which is harmless."], ["Gut", "Abdominal pain, nausea and headache are the main side effects."], ["Whole body", "It is generally well tolerated because human cells do not rely on PFOR."]], "journey": "Nitazoxanide is taken by mouth with food, which improves absorption. In the blood it is quickly hydrolyzed to tizoxanide, so the parent drug is barely detectable. Tizoxanide is more than 99 percent bound to plasma proteins and is conjugated in the liver to tizoxanide glucuronide. Both forms leave in urine, bile and feces. The half-life of tizoxanide is short, about one to one and a half hours, but the drug also acts directly on parasites in the gut lumen.", "sidefx": "Because its target is absent in human cells, side effects are mild. GI symptoms likely come from local gut effects. Yellow urine is from colored metabolites. Rare allergic reactions occur. Its high protein binding means it could displace other highly bound drugs such as warfarin, so these combinations are watched. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Alinia (nitazoxanide) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "rifabutin": {"origin": "The rifamycin family began in 1957 at the Lepetit laboratories in Milan, where Piero Sensi and colleagues isolated rifamycins from a soil bacterium, now called Amycolatopsis rifamycinica, found in a pine forest soil sample from the French Riviera. Rifampin, developed from this family, became a cornerstone of tuberculosis treatment in the late 1960s. Rifabutin was made in the 1970s at Farmitalia Carlo Erba in Italy as a modified rifamycin. During the AIDS epidemic it found its main role: preventing disseminated Mycobacterium avium complex in people with advanced HIV. The FDA approved it as Mycobutin in 1992. Because it induces liver enzymes less than rifampin, it is also used for TB in people taking HIV protease inhibitors.", "timeline": [["1957", "Rifamycins are isolated at Lepetit in Milan from a soil bacterium."], ["1960s", "Rifampin is developed and becomes key for tuberculosis."], ["1970s", "Rifabutin is synthesized at Farmitalia Carlo Erba."], ["1992", "FDA approves Mycobutin for preventing MAC in advanced HIV."]], "cell": "Rifabutin binds the beta subunit of bacterial DNA-dependent RNA polymerase, encoded by the rpoB gene. It sits in a pocket along the path that new RNA takes as it leaves the enzyme and physically blocks the growing chain after only a few nucleotides. Transcription stops, so no new proteins can be made, and the bacterium dies. It does not bind human RNA polymerase, which explains its selectivity. Mutations in rpoB cause resistance and usually cause cross-resistance with rifampin. Rifabutin is highly fat-soluble and enters cells and tissues well, which helps it reach mycobacteria living inside macrophages. It induces CYP3A4, but less strongly than rifampin.", "body": [["Lungs and blood", "Prevents or treats MAC and TB, which can infect lungs, blood and organs in advanced HIV."], ["Body fluids", "Turns urine, sweat, tears and saliva orange-brown, which can stain soft contact lenses."], ["Eyes", "Can cause uveitis, a painful red eye, especially at higher levels or with drugs that raise its levels such as clarithromycin."], ["Bone marrow", "Can lower neutrophil and platelet counts."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises and hepatitis can occur."], ["Drug interactions", "Induces CYP3A4, lowering levels of many drugs, though less than rifampin."]], "journey": "Rifabutin is taken by mouth, and about 20 percent of the dose reaches the blood. It is very fat-soluble and spreads widely into tissues, with tissue levels much higher than plasma. It is metabolized in the liver mainly by CYP3A4, forming an active 25-O-desacetyl metabolite. It also induces its own metabolism. About half leaves in urine as metabolites and about a third in feces. The half-life is long, about 45 hours on average.", "sidefx": "Orange body fluids come from the drug's own reddish color. Uveitis is dose-related and becomes more likely when CYP3A4 inhibitors such as clarithromycin or protease inhibitors raise rifabutin levels. Bone marrow suppression and liver injury are direct toxicities. CYP3A4 induction lowers levels of other drugs, while CYP3A4 inhibitors raise rifabutin. GI upset and C. difficile can occur. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Mycobutin (rifabutin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Sensi P. Rev Infect Dis 1983"]}, "itraconazole": {"origin": "Itraconazole came from Janssen Pharmaceutica in Belgium, the company founded by Paul Janssen, which made a long series of azole antifungals. Its earlier drug ketoconazole was the first useful oral azole, but it caused liver injury and blocked human steroid hormone production. Janssen chemists built a larger triazole, itraconazole, first made around 1980, which was more selective for the fungal enzyme. The FDA approved it as Sporanox capsules in 1992, followed by an oral solution. It became a key drug for endemic fungal infections such as blastomycosis and histoplasmosis and for toenail fungus. Its potent inhibition of human CYP3A4 makes it one of the most interaction-prone drugs in use.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Janssen develops ketoconazole, the first major oral azole antifungal."], ["1980", "Janssen chemists synthesize itraconazole."], ["1992", "FDA approves Sporanox capsules."], ["2001", "FDA adds warnings about heart failure risk."], ["2018", "A better-absorbed SUBA capsule form is approved as Tolsura."]], "cell": "Fungal cell membranes use ergosterol instead of the cholesterol in human membranes. Ergosterol is made from lanosterol in several steps, one of which is removal of a methyl group by lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase, a cytochrome P450 enzyme called CYP51 (encoded by ERG11). The triazole nitrogen of itraconazole binds the heme iron of CYP51 and blocks the enzyme. Ergosterol runs short, and toxic 14-alpha-methylated sterols build up in the membrane. The membrane becomes leaky and disordered, and membrane enzymes such as chitin synthase work poorly, so fungal growth stops. It is mostly fungistatic. Its long side chain lets it act on molds such as Aspergillus. It also binds human CYP3A4 and blocks P-glycoprotein.", "body": [["Lungs and organs", "Treats endemic fungal infections such as blastomycosis and histoplasmosis that start in the lungs and can spread."], ["Nails", "Builds up in nail keratin and stays for months, clearing fungal nail infections as the nail grows out."], ["Heart", "Has a negative inotropic effect, so it can worsen or cause heart failure, and it is avoided in people with heart failure."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise, and rare liver failure has occurred."], ["Drug interactions", "Strong CYP3A4 inhibition raises levels of many drugs, some to dangerous levels that can prolong QT."], ["Fluids and electrolytes", "Edema, hypertension and low potassium can occur."]], "journey": "Capsules need food and stomach acid to dissolve, so they are taken after a full meal and absorb poorly with acid-lowering drugs. The oral solution uses a cyclodextrin carrier and absorbs better on an empty stomach. It is 99.8 percent bound to plasma proteins and is very fat-soluble, concentrating in skin, nails and fatty tissue while reaching little of the CSF. The liver metabolizes it through CYP3A4 to hydroxy-itraconazole, which is also active. Metabolites leave in feces and urine. Its half-life rises from about a day to more than two days with repeated dosing.", "sidefx": "Azoles are selective for the fungal enzyme but also bind human P450 enzymes, which explains strong CYP3A4-based drug interactions, including dangerous QT prolongation with some drugs. The negative effect on heart muscle contraction underlies the boxed warning for heart failure. Liver injury is a class effect. Edema, high blood pressure and low potassium may relate to effects on steroid hormone pathways. There is no antidote, and it is not removed by dialysis.", "src": ["Sporanox (itraconazole) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "posaconazole": {"origin": "Posaconazole was developed by Schering-Plough as a structural relative of itraconazole, with changes that extended its reach to more molds, including many Mucorales, which cause the deadly infection mucormycosis. The FDA approved the oral suspension as Noxafil in 2006 to prevent invasive fungal infections in people with blood cancers and stem cell transplants. The suspension absorbed unevenly, so delayed-release tablets were approved in 2013, giving more reliable levels. An IV form followed in 2014. Merck acquired Schering-Plough in 2009. In 2021 posaconazole was approved to treat invasive aspergillosis.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Schering-Plough develops posaconazole from the itraconazole structure."], ["2006", "FDA approves Noxafil oral suspension for antifungal prophylaxis."], ["2013", "Delayed-release tablets are approved for more reliable absorption."], ["2014", "An IV form is approved."], ["2021", "Approval extends to treatment of invasive aspergillosis."]], "cell": "Posaconazole is a triazole that blocks fungal lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51). Its triazole nitrogen binds the enzyme's heme iron, while its long side chain makes extra contacts deep in the enzyme channel. This tighter fit broadens activity to Aspergillus and many Mucorales, which shorter azoles such as fluconazole and voriconazole miss. With the enzyme blocked, ergosterol is depleted and toxic methylated sterols build up, damaging membrane structure and function. The result is fungistatic against yeasts and often fungicidal against some molds. It also strongly inhibits human CYP3A4, which raises levels of drugs like tacrolimus, cyclosporine and sirolimus.", "body": [["Lungs and sinuses", "Prevents and treats invasive mold infections like aspergillosis and mucormycosis in people with weak immune systems."], ["Blood and bone marrow (patients)", "Lowers the risk of fungal infection during chemotherapy-induced neutropenia and after stem cell transplant."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise, and rare liver failure has been reported."], ["Heart", "Can prolong the QT interval, especially with low potassium or other QT drugs."], ["Adrenal and blood pressure", "Can cause pseudoaldosteronism with high blood pressure and low potassium by blocking enzymes that make or inactivate steroids."], ["Drug interactions", "Strong CYP3A4 inhibition raises levels of many drugs, including immunosuppressants and some chemotherapy."]], "journey": "The delayed-release tablet is taken with food and releases drug in the small intestine, giving steady absorption. The suspension needs a full, fatty meal. The IV form goes through a central line because its carrier irritates veins. Posaconazole is highly bound to plasma proteins and spreads widely into tissues. Unlike itraconazole, it is not mainly broken down by CYP enzymes; it is glucuronidated by UGT1A4 and mostly leaves unchanged in feces. The half-life is about 26 to 31 hours, so steady levels take about a week.", "sidefx": "Binding to human CYP3A4 explains its many drug interactions. QT prolongation reflects effects on cardiac ion channels and interactions that raise other QT drugs. Blocking 11-beta-hydroxylase and 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase pathways can cause mineralocorticoid excess, high blood pressure and low potassium. Liver injury is an azole class effect. GI upset is common. There is no antidote, and dialysis does not remove it.", "src": ["Noxafil (posaconazole) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Cornely OA et al. N Engl J Med 2007"]}, "griseofulvin": {"origin": "Griseofulvin was isolated in 1939 by Harold Raistrick and colleagues in Britain from the mold Penicillium griseofulvum. In the 1940s scientists noted that it made fungal threads curl and stop growing, earning it the name curling factor. It was first used against fungal diseases of plants. In 1958 James Gentles in Glasgow showed that oral griseofulvin cured ringworm in guinea pigs, and human use followed quickly. It reached the US market around 1959 and was the first oral drug for dermatophyte infections of skin, hair and nails. It is still used for scalp ringworm (tinea capitis) in children.", "timeline": [["1939", "Raistrick and colleagues isolate griseofulvin from Penicillium griseofulvum."], ["1940s", "It is described as the curling factor for its effect on fungal threads."], ["1958", "James Gentles shows oral griseofulvin cures ringworm in animals."], ["1959", "Griseofulvin reaches US clinical use for human dermatophyte infections."]], "cell": "Griseofulvin enters dermatophyte fungi and binds tubulin, interfering with microtubule function. Rather than breaking down microtubules completely, it disrupts their dynamics, so the mitotic spindle cannot work properly and cell division stops in metaphase. Fungal hyphae become twisted and curled. It is fungistatic. Its other key feature is where it goes in the body: it is deposited in the keratin precursor cells of skin, hair and nails. As these cells mature, the new keratin contains griseofulvin and resists fungal invasion. Infected keratin is slowly shed and replaced by protected keratin, which is why treatment takes weeks to months. It does not work against Candida or bacteria.", "body": [["Scalp and hair", "Cures tinea capitis in children as new, drug-containing hair grows out."], ["Skin", "Treats ringworm of the body, groin, feet and beard caused by dermatophytes."], ["Nails", "Can treat nail infections, though it takes many months and newer drugs work better."], ["Liver", "Liver injury can occur, and it induces CYP3A4, lowering levels of drugs like warfarin and oral contraceptives."], ["Skin and immune system", "Photosensitivity, rash and lupus flares can occur."], ["Brain", "Headache is common, and confusion or tingling can occur."]], "journey": "Griseofulvin is poorly soluble in water, so absorption depends on particle size and food. Microsize and ultramicrosize forms were made to improve absorption, and a fatty meal helps further. It peaks in about four hours. It concentrates in the stratum corneum, hair and nails, where it stays for weeks. The liver metabolizes it mainly to 6-desmethylgriseofulvin and glucuronides, which leave in urine, with some drug lost in feces and sweat. The half-life is about 9 to 24 hours.", "sidefx": "Interference with tubulin can also affect human dividing cells, which is why it is avoided in pregnancy and may cause blood count changes. Liver enzyme induction (including CYP3A4) explains interactions that lower contraceptive and warfarin effects. It can trigger porphyria attacks and is avoided in people with porphyria or liver failure. Photosensitivity and lupus-like reactions are immune or light-related effects. Headache is common. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Gris-PEG (griseofulvin ultramicrosize) FDA label", "Gentles JC. Nature 1958", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "atovaquone": {"origin": "Atovaquone comes from a family of chemicals called hydroxynaphthoquinones, which were studied as antimalarials during and after World War II because they resemble ubiquinone (coenzyme Q). Early versions were broken down too quickly in people. In the 1980s scientists at the Wellcome Research Laboratories in Britain designed atovaquone to resist breakdown. It was approved by the FDA in 1992 as Mepron for Pneumocystis pneumonia in people with AIDS who could not tolerate trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole. Later the yellow oral suspension replaced the tablets because it was better absorbed. Atovaquone also became half of the antimalarial Malarone.", "timeline": [["1940s", "Hydroxynaphthoquinones are studied as possible antimalarials."], ["1980s", "Wellcome scientists develop atovaquone."], ["1992", "FDA approves Mepron for Pneumocystis pneumonia."], ["1990s", "A better-absorbed oral suspension replaces the original tablets."]], "cell": "Atovaquone is shaped like ubiquinone, the electron carrier of the mitochondrial respiratory chain. It binds the ubiquinol oxidation (Qo) site of cytochrome bc1, also called complex III, in Pneumocystis, Plasmodium, Toxoplasma and Babesia. This blocks electron flow to cytochrome c, and the mitochondrial membrane potential collapses. Many of these organisms depend on the respiratory chain to recycle ubiquinone for dihydroorotate dehydrogenase, an enzyme needed to make pyrimidines. With that blocked, they cannot make DNA and RNA building blocks and die. The parasite enzymes are much more sensitive than human complex III, which accounts for selectivity. A single mutation in the cytochrome b gene can cause resistance.", "body": [["Lungs", "Prevents and treats mild to moderate Pneumocystis pneumonia when TMP-SMX cannot be used."], ["Brain", "Has activity against Toxoplasma gondii and has been used in toxoplasmosis."], ["Blood", "Active against Babesia, which infects red blood cells, and against malaria parasites."], ["Bone marrow", "Does not cause the low blood counts seen with TMP-SMX or dapsone."], ["Gut and skin", "Diarrhea, nausea and rash are the most common side effects."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise, and rare hepatitis has been reported."]], "journey": "Atovaquone is very fat-soluble and poorly soluble in water, so it is absorbed much better with food, especially fatty food. Blood levels can show a second peak from enterohepatic recycling, as drug secreted in bile is reabsorbed. It is more than 99.9 percent bound to plasma proteins. It is barely metabolized and leaves the body mostly unchanged in feces. Its half-life is long, about two to three days.", "sidefx": "Because human mitochondria are far less sensitive, atovaquone is well tolerated and does not suppress the bone marrow. GI upset and rash are common. Low absorption without food can cause treatment failure, which is a practical rather than toxic problem. Liver enzyme rises can occur. Rifampin and rifabutin lower its levels. There is no antidote, and it is not removed by dialysis.", "src": ["Mepron (atovaquone) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "atovaquone-proguanil": {"origin": "This antimalarial joins two drugs from different eras. Proguanil was discovered in 1945 by chemists at Imperial Chemical Industries in Britain, including Francis Curd and Frank Rose, as part of wartime antimalarial research. Atovaquone was developed at Wellcome in the 1980s. Alone, atovaquone allowed malaria parasites to develop resistance quickly. Researchers found that proguanil strongly enhanced atovaquone's effect on the parasite mitochondria, and resistance to the pair was much slower. Glaxo Wellcome combined them as Malarone, approved by the FDA in 2000 for preventing and treating falciparum malaria. Because it acts on parasites in the liver, travelers take it for only a week after leaving a malaria area.", "timeline": [["1945", "ICI chemists in Britain discover proguanil."], ["1980s", "Atovaquone is developed at Wellcome."], ["1990s", "Studies show strong synergy between atovaquone and proguanil."], ["2000", "FDA approves Malarone for malaria prevention and treatment."]], "cell": "Atovaquone binds the Qo site of the Plasmodium cytochrome bc1 complex (complex III). This stops electron transport and collapses the parasite's mitochondrial membrane potential. The parasite needs this chain to regenerate ubiquinone for dihydroorotate dehydrogenase, so pyrimidine synthesis fails. Proguanil acts in two ways. CYP2C19 in the human liver converts it to cycloguanil, which inhibits plasmodial dihydrofolate reductase and blocks folate-dependent DNA synthesis. Proguanil itself, not cycloguanil, makes the parasite mitochondria more sensitive to atovaquone, lowering the dose of atovaquone needed to collapse the membrane potential. Both drugs act on liver-stage and blood-stage parasites, making the combination a causal prophylactic.", "body": [["Liver", "Kills parasites developing in liver cells before they reach the blood, which is why post-travel dosing lasts only seven days."], ["Blood", "Kills parasites in red blood cells, treating uncomplicated falciparum malaria, including chloroquine-resistant strains."], ["Gut", "Abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting are the most common side effects; taking it with food helps."], ["Mouth", "Mouth ulcers can occur, possibly from proguanil's antifolate effect."], ["Skin and liver", "Rash and liver enzyme rises can occur, with rare severe reactions."]], "journey": "Atovaquone is fat-soluble and needs food for good absorption; proguanil is absorbed well regardless. Atovaquone is highly protein bound, barely metabolized, and leaves unchanged in feces with a half-life of two to three days. Proguanil is converted by CYP2C19 to cycloguanil and leaves mainly in urine, with a half-life of about 12 to 21 hours. People who are poor CYP2C19 metabolizers make less cycloguanil, but the combination still works because proguanil's direct boosting effect remains.", "sidefx": "GI upset is the main problem and likely reflects local effects of both drugs. Mouth ulcers and rare blood effects may reflect proguanil's antifolate action. Rash and liver enzyme rises can occur. Rifampin lowers atovaquone levels, and proguanil can enhance warfarin's effect. Severe kidney disease raises proguanil and cycloguanil levels, so it is avoided for prophylaxis in that setting. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Malarone (atovaquone and proguanil) FDA label", "CDC Yellow Book: Malaria", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "mefloquine": {"origin": "Mefloquine came from the US Army's search for new antimalarials during the Vietnam War, when chloroquine-resistant falciparum malaria was sickening soldiers. Scientists at the Walter Reed Army Institute of Research screened thousands of compounds in the late 1960s and 1970s and selected mefloquine, a quinoline methanol related to quinine. It was developed further with the World Health Organization and Hoffmann-La Roche. The FDA approved it as Lariam in 1989. Its once-weekly dosing made it popular with travelers. Reports of psychiatric and balance problems grew over time, and in 2013 the FDA added a boxed warning that these neurologic effects can last long after stopping. The Lariam brand has since been discontinued.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Chloroquine-resistant malaria spreads during the Vietnam War."], ["1970s", "Walter Reed scientists identify mefloquine in a large screening program."], ["1989", "FDA approves Lariam."], ["2013", "FDA adds a boxed warning for lasting neurologic and psychiatric effects."]], "cell": "Mefloquine kills malaria parasites inside red blood cells. Its exact mechanism is still not fully settled. Like quinine and chloroquine, it is thought to interfere with how the parasite detoxifies heme. The parasite digests hemoglobin in its food vacuole, releasing toxic free heme, which it normally packs into harmless hemozoin crystals. Blocking this process lets toxic heme build up and damage parasite membranes. Research has also shown that mefloquine binds the parasite's 80S ribosome and blocks protein synthesis. It acts only on blood stages, not liver stages or gametocytes, so it does not prevent relapse of P. vivax or P. ovale. It also affects human brain targets, including gap junctions and cholinesterase activity, which may relate to its neuropsychiatric effects.", "body": [["Blood", "Kills blood-stage malaria parasites, including chloroquine-resistant P. falciparum."], ["Brain (mood and thought)", "Can cause vivid dreams, anxiety, depression, paranoia and rarely psychosis or suicidal thoughts."], ["Inner ear and balance", "Dizziness, vertigo, tinnitus and loss of balance can occur and sometimes persist for months or years."], ["Heart", "Can slow the heart rate and prolong the QT interval, especially with other QT drugs."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are common."], ["Brain (seizures)", "Can lower the seizure threshold, so it is avoided in people with seizure disorders."]], "journey": "Mefloquine is taken by mouth with food and water, and food improves absorption. It is absorbed slowly, peaking in about 6 to 24 hours. It is highly bound to proteins and spreads widely, including into red blood cells and the brain. The liver metabolizes it through CYP3A4 to an inactive carboxylic acid metabolite. It leaves mainly through bile and feces. Its half-life is very long, about two to four weeks, which allows weekly dosing but means side effects can last a long time after the last dose.", "sidefx": "Neuropsychiatric effects are the main concern. Mefloquine reaches the brain well and acts on neuronal targets such as gap junctions, which may explain anxiety, nightmares, psychosis and lasting dizziness or vertigo. Its quinine-like effects on the heart can slow conduction and prolong QT, so it is not combined with quinine or quinidine without care. GI upset is common. The very long half-life means effects can persist after stopping. There is no antidote, and it is not removed by dialysis.", "src": ["Mefloquine hydrochloride FDA label", "FDA Drug Safety Communication on mefloquine, 2013", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "albendazole": {"origin": "Albendazole was developed in the 1970s by scientists at SmithKline Animal Health, including Robert Gyurik and Vassilios Theodorides, as a broad-spectrum dewormer for livestock. It belongs to the benzimidazole family, which began with thiabendazole in the early 1960s. Its activity against many worms, including tapeworm cysts, soon led to human use. In the US it was approved as Albenza in 1996 for neurocysticercosis (pork tapeworm cysts in the brain) and hydatid disease (Echinococcus cysts in the liver and lungs). Since 1998 its maker has donated large amounts to global programs to eliminate lymphatic filariasis and control intestinal worms. The Albenza brand is now discontinued, and generics are available.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Thiabendazole, the first benzimidazole dewormer, is introduced."], ["1970s", "SmithKline scientists develop albendazole for livestock."], ["1996", "FDA approves albendazole (Albenza) for neurocysticercosis and hydatid disease."], ["1998", "A large donation program for lymphatic filariasis elimination begins."]], "cell": "Albendazole is quickly converted in the liver to albendazole sulfoxide, the main active form. This binds beta-tubulin in worm cells at the colchicine-sensitive site and prevents tubulin dimers from polymerizing into microtubules. Worm intestinal and tegumental cells depend on microtubules to move secretory vesicles and take up glucose. Without them, glucose uptake fails, glycogen stores run out and ATP production falls. The worms become immobile and die over days. It also kills larvae and eggs of some species. Selectivity comes from much higher affinity for helminth beta-tubulin than for mammalian tubulin.", "body": [["Brain", "Kills pork tapeworm cysts in neurocysticercosis; dying cysts can trigger inflammation, seizures or raised pressure, so steroids and anticonvulsants are often given."], ["Liver and lungs", "Shrinks or kills Echinococcus hydatid cysts, often alongside surgery or drainage."], ["Intestine", "Clears roundworm, hookworm, whipworm and pinworm infections."], ["Bone marrow", "Can lower white cell counts and rarely cause severe marrow suppression with long courses."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes commonly rise during long treatment courses."], ["Hair", "Reversible hair thinning can occur with prolonged use."]], "journey": "Albendazole is poorly absorbed by mouth, but a fatty meal increases absorption several fold, which matters for tissue infections. Once absorbed, it is almost completely converted by liver first-pass metabolism to albendazole sulfoxide, which spreads into tissues, including cyst fluid and CSF. The sulfoxide is further oxidized to an inactive sulfone. Metabolites leave mainly in bile, with a small part in urine. The sulfoxide half-life is about 8 to 12 hours. For intestinal worms, poor absorption is fine because the drug works in the gut.", "sidefx": "Effects on tubulin in rapidly dividing human cells explain bone marrow suppression and hair thinning during long courses, so blood counts and liver tests are checked. Liver enzyme rises reflect hepatic metabolism and injury. In neurocysticercosis, many symptoms come from the immune reaction to dying cysts, not the drug itself. It may harm a developing fetus based on animal data, so pregnancy is avoided. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Albenza (albendazole) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "WHO lymphatic filariasis elimination program"]}, "permethrin": {"origin": "Permethrin is a synthetic version of pyrethrins, natural insecticides from the flowers of the pyrethrum daisy (Tanacetum cinerariifolium), which have been used against insects for centuries. Natural pyrethrins break down quickly in light and air. In 1973 Michael Elliott and his team at Rothamsted Experimental Station in England made permethrin, one of the first light-stable synthetic pyrethroids. It became widely used in agriculture and on clothing and bed nets. In the US, a 1% creme rinse for head lice (Nix) and a 5% cream for scabies (Elimite) were approved in the late 1980s. It is a first-line treatment for scabies today.", "timeline": [["1800s", "Pyrethrum flower powder is widely used as an insecticide."], ["1973", "Michael Elliott's team at Rothamsted synthesizes permethrin."], ["1980s", "US approval of permethrin products for head lice and scabies."]], "cell": "Permethrin acts on voltage-gated sodium channels in the nerve cell membranes of mites and lice. Normally these channels open briefly to start an action potential and then quickly inactivate. Permethrin binds the channel and slows both inactivation and closing, so sodium keeps flowing in. The nerve membrane stays partly depolarized and fires repeatedly, then becomes unable to fire at all. The insect or mite has uncontrolled nerve activity, then paralysis, then death. It kills adults, nymphs and, to a variable degree, eggs. Mammals are protected because very little crosses the skin, mammalian sodium channels are less sensitive, body temperature is higher (pyrethroids work better at cooler temperatures), and liver and blood esterases quickly break it down.", "body": [["Skin (scabies)", "Kills Sarcoptes scabiei mites in their skin burrows, though itch can persist for weeks as the allergic reaction fades."], ["Scalp (lice)", "Kills head lice and many eggs, and residue on hair can keep killing newly hatched lice for days."], ["Skin irritation", "Burning, stinging, redness and itching can occur where it is applied."], ["Whole body", "Less than 2 percent is absorbed, so effects elsewhere in the body are rare."]], "journey": "Permethrin is applied to the skin or hair and left on for hours. Less than 2 percent passes through the skin into the blood. What is absorbed is quickly hydrolyzed by esterases in blood and liver into inactive acid and alcohol products, which are then conjugated and excreted in urine. The systemic half-life is very short, minutes to hours. Most of the drug stays on the skin and hair and is washed off.", "sidefx": "Local burning and stinging come from irritation and mild nerve stimulation in the skin. Itching after treatment is often from the immune reaction to dead mites rather than the drug. Allergic contact dermatitis can occur. Large ingestions can cause nausea and, rarely, seizures from excess sodium channel activity in the brain. There is no antidote. Cats lack some of the enzymes needed to break down pyrethroids, so permethrin is very toxic to them.", "fun": "Permethrin-treated clothing is used by soldiers and hikers to repel ticks and mosquitoes, and the treatment can survive dozens of washes.", "src": ["Elimite (permethrin 5% cream) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Elliott M et al. Nature 1973"]}, "famciclovir": {"origin": "Famciclovir was created by scientists at Beecham Pharmaceuticals in Britain in the 1980s as an oral form of penciclovir. Penciclovir, a guanosine analog like acyclovir, was active against herpes viruses but poorly absorbed by mouth. Beecham chemists added two acetyl groups and removed an oxygen to make a prodrug that the body absorbs well and then converts to penciclovir. It followed the success of acyclovir, developed at Burroughs Wellcome by Gertrude Elion's team in the 1970s. The FDA approved famciclovir as Famvir in 1994 for shingles. Later approvals covered genital herpes and cold sores. The Famvir brand has been discontinued, but generics are available.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Acyclovir is developed, showing that herpes viruses can be targeted selectively."], ["1980s", "Beecham scientists develop famciclovir as a prodrug of penciclovir."], ["1994", "FDA approves Famvir for herpes zoster."], ["1990s", "Approvals extend to genital herpes and recurrent cold sores."]], "cell": "Famciclovir is a prodrug. After absorption it is deacetylated and then oxidized by aldehyde oxidase in the liver to penciclovir. Inside an infected cell, the herpes virus's own thymidine kinase adds the first phosphate to penciclovir. Human cellular kinases then add two more phosphates, making penciclovir triphosphate. This molecule competes with deoxyguanosine triphosphate (dGTP) for the viral DNA polymerase and inhibits viral DNA synthesis. Because the first phosphorylation step needs viral thymidine kinase, the active drug is made mostly in infected cells, which explains its selectivity. Penciclovir triphosphate lasts a long time inside infected cells. Viruses that lack or alter thymidine kinase are resistant, and these are usually resistant to acyclovir too.", "body": [["Skin and nerves (shingles)", "Speeds healing of shingles rash and shortens viral shedding when started within 72 hours."], ["Genital tract", "Shortens genital herpes outbreaks and, taken daily, reduces recurrences."], ["Lips", "A single-day course shortens cold sore episodes when started at the first sign."], ["Brain", "Confusion or hallucinations can occur, mostly in older adults or people with kidney disease."], ["Kidneys", "Penciclovir builds up when kidney function is low, so dosing depends on kidney function."]], "journey": "Famciclovir is absorbed well by mouth and is quickly converted in the gut wall and liver to penciclovir, so famciclovir itself is barely seen in the blood. About three-quarters of the dose becomes available as penciclovir. Penciclovir is weakly bound to plasma proteins. It leaves through the kidneys by filtration and tubular secretion. Its plasma half-life is two to three hours, but the active triphosphate stays in infected cells much longer.", "sidefx": "Because it is activated mainly in infected cells, famciclovir is generally well tolerated. Headache and nausea are common. Confusion and hallucinations reflect high penciclovir levels in the brain, usually when kidney function is reduced. Acute kidney injury has occurred when doses were not adjusted for kidney disease. Hemodialysis removes penciclovir. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Famvir (famciclovir) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "valganciclovir": {"origin": "Ganciclovir was developed in the early 1980s, building on acyclovir, and was approved in the US in 1989 as the first drug for cytomegalovirus (CMV) disease. It quickly became essential in AIDS patients with CMV retinitis, which could cause blindness. But ganciclovir capsules were poorly absorbed, so serious infections required IV infusion through a long-term line. Roche scientists attached the amino acid L-valine to make valganciclovir, an ester prodrug that intestinal transporters absorb well. It was approved as Valcyte in 2001. Oral valganciclovir gives blood levels similar to IV ganciclovir, which made CMV prevention after organ transplant much easier.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Ganciclovir is developed as an antiviral against CMV."], ["1989", "FDA approves ganciclovir, the first CMV drug."], ["2001", "FDA approves Valcyte for CMV retinitis in AIDS."], ["2003", "Approval extends to CMV prevention after solid organ transplant."]], "cell": "Valganciclovir is the L-valyl ester of ganciclovir. Esterases in the gut wall and liver remove the valine, freeing ganciclovir. In CMV-infected cells, the viral protein kinase UL97 adds the first phosphate. In herpes simplex-infected cells, viral thymidine kinase does this step. Human kinases then add two more phosphates, making ganciclovir triphosphate. This molecule competes with dGTP for the viral DNA polymerase (UL54) and is built into the growing viral DNA, slowing and eventually stopping chain elongation. Because the first step needs a viral enzyme, the active drug builds up mainly in infected cells. Ganciclovir triphosphate can still reach human dividing cells, especially in bone marrow, which explains its toxicity. Mutations in UL97 or UL54 cause resistance.", "body": [["Eyes", "Halts CMV retinitis in people with AIDS, protecting vision."], ["Transplanted organs", "Prevents CMV disease after kidney, heart and kidney-pancreas transplants, a major cause of illness and graft problems."], ["Bone marrow", "Suppresses dividing marrow cells, causing neutropenia, anemia and low platelets."], ["Kidneys", "Ganciclovir builds up when kidney function is low, so doses depend on kidney function."], ["Reproductive system", "Animal studies show it can harm fertility and cause birth defects and cancer, so it is handled as a hazardous drug."]], "journey": "Valganciclovir is taken by mouth with food, and intestinal peptide transporters absorb it well. It is quickly converted to ganciclovir in the gut wall and liver, giving about 60 percent bioavailability as ganciclovir, roughly ten times better than old ganciclovir capsules. Ganciclovir barely binds plasma proteins and is not metabolized. The kidneys clear it unchanged by filtration and secretion. The half-life is about four hours in normal kidney function and much longer in kidney disease.", "sidefx": "Ganciclovir triphosphate can enter DNA of rapidly dividing human cells, which explains the main toxicity: bone marrow suppression with neutropenia, anemia and thrombocytopenia. Blood counts are checked often, and growth factors can support the marrow. The same DNA effects explain concern about birth defects, infertility and cancer. Seizures and kidney injury can occur with high levels. Hemodialysis removes ganciclovir, but there is no antidote.", "src": ["Valcyte (valganciclovir) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "baloxavir": {"origin": "Baloxavir was discovered by the Japanese company Shionogi, which studied how influenza viruses steal the caps of human messenger RNA to make their own. Researchers looked for compounds that block the cap-snatching endonuclease, a target no previous flu drug had hit. Baloxavir marboxil was the result. It was approved in Japan in February 2018 and by the FDA in October 2018 as Xofluza, with Genentech handling US marketing. It was the first flu antiviral with a new mechanism in nearly 20 years, and it needs only a single dose. In 2020 it was also approved to prevent flu after exposure.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Shionogi scientists target the influenza cap-dependent endonuclease."], ["2018", "Baloxavir is approved in Japan in February."], ["2018", "FDA approves Xofluza in October for acute uncomplicated influenza."], ["2020", "Approval extends to post-exposure prevention."]], "cell": "Influenza viruses cannot make the 5-prime cap that human cells use to start translating messenger RNA. Instead they steal it. The viral polymerase is made of three subunits: PB1, PB2 and PA. PB2 grabs the cap on a host pre-mRNA, and the PA subunit's endonuclease cuts a short piece off, which then primes viral mRNA synthesis. Baloxavir marboxil is a prodrug that is hydrolyzed to baloxavir acid. The acid binds the PA endonuclease active site, chelating its two metal ions, and blocks the cut. Without capped primers, the virus cannot make mRNA or viral proteins, and replication drops sharply. It acts on influenza A and B. The PA mutation I38T reduces susceptibility and can emerge during treatment.", "body": [["Airways", "Viral replication and shedding fall quickly, shortening flu symptoms by about a day when started within 48 hours."], ["Household contacts", "A single dose after exposure lowers the chance of developing flu."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and nausea are the most common side effects."], ["Lungs", "Bronchitis and sinusitis were reported, and secondary bacterial infections can still occur with flu."], ["Immune system", "Rare hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis, have been reported."]], "journey": "Baloxavir marboxil is taken by mouth as a single dose. It is hydrolyzed in the gut, blood and liver by arylacetamide deacetylase to baloxavir acid, the active form, which peaks in about four hours. Polyvalent cations in dairy, calcium-fortified drinks, antacids and supplements bind it and lower absorption. Baloxavir acid is highly bound to plasma proteins. It is metabolized mainly by UGT1A3 glucuronidation with minor CYP3A4 oxidation and leaves mostly in feces. Its half-life is about 79 hours, so one dose covers the whole illness.", "sidefx": "Because the target exists only in influenza viruses, baloxavir is well tolerated. GI symptoms are the most common. Hypersensitivity reactions can occur. Resistance from PA mutations can develop during treatment, especially in children. Metal ions bind the drug for the same reason it binds the metals in its enzyme target, which explains the interaction with dairy and supplements. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Xofluza (baloxavir marboxil) FDA label", "Hayden FG et al. N Engl J Med 2018"]}, "nirmatrelvir-ritonavir": {"origin": "Nirmatrelvir grew out of Pfizer's work on an earlier coronavirus. After the 2003 SARS outbreak, Pfizer scientists made a protease inhibitor called PF-00835231, which had to be given IV. When COVID-19 emerged in 2020, they redesigned it into an oral drug, nirmatrelvir, in a matter of months. Because the liver broke it down quickly, it was paired with a low dose of ritonavir, an old HIV drug, to slow its breakdown. In the EPIC-HR trial in high-risk unvaccinated adults, it cut hospitalization or death by about 89 percent when started early. The FDA granted emergency use authorization for Paxlovid in December 2021 and full approval for adults in May 2023.", "timeline": [["2003", "Pfizer develops an IV protease inhibitor after the SARS outbreak."], ["2020", "Pfizer designs oral nirmatrelvir against SARS-CoV-2."], ["2021", "FDA grants emergency use authorization for Paxlovid in December."], ["2023", "FDA grants full approval for adults at high risk."]], "cell": "SARS-CoV-2 makes two large polyproteins, pp1a and pp1ab, from its RNA. These must be cut into separate nonstructural proteins, including the RNA polymerase, before the virus can copy itself. The main protease (Mpro, also called 3CL protease or nsp5) makes most of these cuts. Nirmatrelvir is a peptidomimetic that fits the protease's active site. Its nitrile group forms a reversible covalent bond with the catalytic cysteine (Cys145). With the protease blocked, the polyproteins stay uncut and the replication machinery cannot form, so the virus stops multiplying. Humans have no close equivalent of this protease, which explains selectivity. Ritonavir has no activity against SARS-CoV-2; it strongly inhibits CYP3A4, slowing nirmatrelvir's breakdown.", "body": [["Airways and lungs", "Lower viral replication reduces the chance that mild COVID-19 progresses to severe pneumonia, hospitalization or death."], ["Mouth", "A bitter or metallic taste (dysgeusia) is common."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and nausea can occur during the five-day course."], ["Blood pressure and muscles", "Higher blood pressure and muscle aches can occur."], ["Drug interactions", "Ritonavir's CYP3A4 blockade can push other drugs, such as some statins, antiarrhythmics and sedatives, to toxic levels."]], "journey": "Both tablets are swallowed together twice daily for five days. Nirmatrelvir alone would be cleared quickly by CYP3A4 in the liver. Ritonavir blocks that enzyme, so nirmatrelvir levels stay high and it is mostly cleared unchanged by the kidneys, which is why kidney function affects the dose. Nirmatrelvir peaks in about three hours with a half-life of about six hours when boosted. Ritonavir is metabolized by CYP3A and CYP2D6 and leaves mainly in feces. CYP3A inhibition lasts about two to three days after the last dose.", "sidefx": "Most serious problems are drug interactions, because ritonavir strongly inhibits CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein. Drugs that depend on CYP3A4 can reach dangerous levels, causing bleeding, sedation, rhabdomyolysis or arrhythmias. Strong CYP3A inducers can make Paxlovid fail. Taste changes and diarrhea are common. Liver enzyme rises and hypersensitivity, including rare severe skin reactions, can occur. People with untreated HIV risk HIV resistance to protease inhibitors. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The bitter taste is so common that people often describe it as Paxlovid mouth.", "src": ["Paxlovid (nirmatrelvir and ritonavir) FDA label", "Hammond J et al. EPIC-HR, N Engl J Med 2022", "Owen DR et al. Science 2021"]}, "bictegravir-emtricitabine-tenofovir-alafenamide": {"origin": "This single tablet combines three drugs with separate stories. Tenofovir came from the work of Czech chemist Antonin Holy and Belgian virologist Erik De Clercq on nucleotide analogs, and Gilead developed it; tenofovir disoproxil fumarate was approved in 2001, and the newer tenofovir alafenamide (TAF), which concentrates in cells and lowers blood levels, was approved in 2015. Emtricitabine was discovered at Emory University by Dennis Liotta, Raymond Schinazi and Woo-Baeg Choi and approved in 2003. Bictegravir is an integrase inhibitor designed by Gilead to be potent, once daily and free of a booster. The FDA approved the combination as Biktarvy in February 2018. It became one of the most prescribed HIV regimens in the US.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Emtricitabine is discovered at Emory University."], ["2001", "Tenofovir disoproxil fumarate is approved for HIV."], ["2003", "Emtricitabine is approved for HIV."], ["2015", "Tenofovir alafenamide is first approved as part of a combination tablet."], ["2018", "FDA approves Biktarvy as a complete once-daily HIV regimen."]], "cell": "HIV copies its RNA into DNA using reverse transcriptase, then uses integrase to insert that DNA into human chromosomes. Emtricitabine is a cytidine analog. Cellular kinases convert it to emtricitabine triphosphate, which reverse transcriptase builds into the new DNA. It lacks a 3-prime hydroxyl group, so the chain ends. Tenofovir alafenamide enters cells and is hydrolyzed by cathepsin A to tenofovir, which kinases convert to tenofovir diphosphate, an adenosine nucleotide analog that also terminates the chain. Bictegravir binds the integrase active site and chelates the two magnesium ions needed for the strand transfer step. Viral DNA cannot be joined to host DNA, so a new infected cell cannot be established. Blocking two steps with three drugs suppresses replication and makes resistance hard to develop.", "body": [["Immune system", "Viral load falls to undetectable levels in most people, and CD4 cells recover, preventing AIDS-related infections."], ["Liver", "Emtricitabine and tenofovir also suppress hepatitis B, so stopping them can cause a severe hepatitis B flare."], ["Kidneys", "TAF gives much lower tenofovir blood levels than the older form, so kidney and bone effects are smaller, but kidney function is still monitored."], ["Bones", "Bone density loss is less than with tenofovir disoproxil because less tenofovir reaches bone."], ["Gut and brain", "Diarrhea, nausea, headache and abnormal dreams are the most common side effects."], ["Body weight", "Weight gain has been seen with integrase inhibitor and TAF regimens."]], "journey": "The tablet is taken once daily with or without food. Bictegravir is absorbed in a few hours, is more than 99 percent protein bound, and is metabolized by CYP3A and UGT1A1, with a half-life of about 17 hours. Emtricitabine is well absorbed, barely metabolized and cleared by the kidneys, with a plasma half-life of about 10 hours. TAF is stable in blood and enters cells, where it is converted to tenofovir diphosphate, which lasts days inside cells. Antacids with aluminum or magnesium and some supplements bind bictegravir, so timing rules apply.", "sidefx": "The NRTIs can weakly affect human mitochondrial DNA polymerase gamma, which underlies rare lactic acidosis. Tenofovir can injure the proximal kidney tubule, though TAF lowers this risk by keeping plasma tenofovir low. Bictegravir blocks the kidney transporter OCT2 and the MATE1 pump, which slightly raises serum creatinine without true kidney injury. Stopping the drugs can let hepatitis B rebound sharply. Immune reconstitution syndrome reflects the recovering immune system reacting to hidden infections. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Biktarvy (bictegravir, emtricitabine and tenofovir alafenamide) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Gallant J et al. Lancet 2017"]}, "cabotegravir": {"origin": "Cabotegravir was discovered through a partnership between ViiV Healthcare and the Japanese company Shionogi, which had also produced dolutegravir. Cabotegravir is a close relative of dolutegravir with a long half-life and very low water solubility, which made it suitable for a long-acting injectable nanosuspension. In 2020 the HPTN 083 and HPTN 084 trials showed that injections every two months prevented HIV better than daily oral PrEP pills, largely because injections did not depend on taking a pill every day. In December 2021 the FDA approved Apretude as the first long-acting injectable for HIV pre-exposure prophylaxis.", "timeline": [["2010s", "ViiV Healthcare and Shionogi develop cabotegravir, a relative of dolutegravir."], ["2020", "HPTN 083 and 084 trials show injections outperform daily oral PrEP."], ["2021", "FDA approves Cabenuva, which contains cabotegravir, for HIV treatment in January."], ["2021", "FDA approves Apretude in December for HIV PrEP."]], "cell": "After HIV enters a cell, reverse transcriptase copies its RNA into DNA. Integrase then trims the ends of this viral DNA and joins it into the host chromosome in a step called strand transfer. Integration is required for a lasting infection. Cabotegravir binds the integrase active site on the viral DNA-integrase complex (the intasome) and chelates the two magnesium ions that the enzyme needs. This blocks strand transfer, so viral DNA cannot be inserted and is lost. When enough drug is present at the time of exposure, the virus cannot establish infection, which is how PrEP works. If someone is already infected, single-drug exposure can select for integrase resistance mutations.", "body": [["Genital and rectal tissue", "Drug levels in tissues and blood block HIV from establishing infection after sexual exposure."], ["Buttock muscle", "The injection forms a depot that slowly releases drug, often causing pain, nodules or swelling at the site."], ["Liver", "Liver injury can occur, so liver function is watched."], ["Brain and mood", "Depressive disorders, headache and sleep problems have been reported."], ["Whole body", "Fever and fatigue can occur after injections."]], "journey": "Cabotegravir is injected deep into the gluteal muscle as a nanosuspension of tiny drug particles. The particles dissolve slowly, releasing drug over weeks to months. Blood levels peak about a week after injection. In the blood it is more than 99 percent protein bound. It is metabolized by UGT1A1 with a minor role for UGT1A9 and leaves mainly in feces as unchanged drug and in urine as the glucuronide. The apparent half-life after injection is about 6 to 12 weeks, and low levels can remain for a year or more. An optional oral lead-in can test tolerance first.", "sidefx": "Injection site reactions come from the depot sitting in muscle. Because levels fall slowly over many months after the last shot, there is a long tail when levels are too low to prevent infection but still present, which can select for resistant virus if HIV is acquired. That is why HIV testing is done before each injection. Liver injury, hypersensitivity and depression are labeled risks. The depot cannot be removed, so there is no way to reverse it quickly.", "src": ["Apretude (cabotegravir extended-release injectable suspension) FDA label", "Landovitz RJ et al. HPTN 083, N Engl J Med 2021"]}, "cabotegravir-rilpivirine": {"origin": "This was the first complete long-acting injectable HIV treatment. It pairs cabotegravir, an integrase inhibitor from ViiV Healthcare and Shionogi, with rilpivirine, an NNRTI from Janssen. Both drugs are poorly water-soluble and potent, which let them be made into slow-release nanosuspensions. The ATLAS and FLAIR trials showed that monthly injections kept HIV suppressed as well as daily pills, and the ATLAS-2M trial showed every-two-month dosing also worked. The FDA approved Cabenuva in January 2021 for monthly dosing and in 2022 for every two months, and later for adolescents. Many people preferred not having to take a daily pill.", "timeline": [["2011", "Rilpivirine is approved as an oral NNRTI."], ["2019", "ATLAS and FLAIR trials show monthly injections match daily oral therapy."], ["2021", "FDA approves Cabenuva as the first complete long-acting HIV regimen."], ["2022", "Every-two-month dosing and use in adolescents are approved."]], "cell": "The two drugs block two different steps of HIV replication. Rilpivirine is a diarylpyrimidine NNRTI. It binds a hydrophobic pocket on the p66 subunit of reverse transcriptase near, but not in, the active site. This changes the enzyme's shape and stops it from copying viral RNA into DNA. Its flexible structure lets it bend to fit the pocket even when some mutations change it. Cabotegravir binds the integrase active site, chelating two magnesium ions, and blocks the strand transfer step that inserts viral DNA into the host chromosome. Using two drugs with separate targets keeps the virus suppressed. If levels fall too low, resistance to both drug classes can develop.", "body": [["Immune system", "Keeps HIV suppressed in people who are already undetectable, preserving CD4 cells."], ["Buttock muscle", "Injection site pain, nodules and swelling are very common but usually ease with time."], ["Brain and mood", "Depression, sleep problems, headache and dizziness can occur."], ["Whole body", "Fever, fatigue and muscle aches can follow injections."], ["Heart", "High rilpivirine levels can prolong the QT interval."], ["Post-injection reaction", "Rarely, rilpivirine enters a blood vessel and causes sweating, abdominal cramping, anxiety and a warm feeling within minutes, so people are watched after the shot."]], "journey": "The two drugs are given as separate gluteal injections at the same visit. Each forms a depot of nanoparticles that dissolve slowly. Cabotegravir peaks in about a week and rilpivirine in a few days. Cabotegravir is cleared by UGT1A1 and rilpivirine by CYP3A, so strong inducers such as rifampin can drop levels and are not allowed. Both are more than 99 percent protein bound. Their apparent half-lives after injection are long, about 6 to 12 weeks for cabotegravir and months for rilpivirine, and both can be detected for a year or more after the last dose.", "sidefx": "Injection site reactions come from the depots in muscle. The post-injection reaction is thought to happen when some rilpivirine enters the blood too fast. QT prolongation reflects rilpivirine's effect on cardiac repolarization at high levels. Depression is linked to both drugs. Missed injections let levels fall slowly, giving virus a chance to develop resistance to both classes, so if injections stop, a full oral regimen is started on time. The depots cannot be removed, and there is no antidote.", "src": ["Cabenuva (cabotegravir and rilpivirine) FDA label", "Swindells S et al. ATLAS, N Engl J Med 2020", "Orkin C et al. FLAIR, N Engl J Med 2020"]}, "darunavir": {"origin": "Darunavir was designed to beat HIV resistance. Earlier protease inhibitors lost activity when the virus mutated its protease. Arun Ghosh, a chemist then at the University of Illinois at Chicago, designed molecules with a bis-tetrahydrofuran group that formed strong hydrogen bonds with the backbone of the protease, the part the virus cannot easily change. The Belgian company Tibotec, part of Johnson & Johnson, developed darunavir from this work. The FDA approved it as Prezista in 2006 for people whose HIV had become resistant to other drugs. Once-daily use followed, and combinations with cobicistat (Prezcobix) and in a single tablet (Symtuza) came later. It is known for a high genetic barrier to resistance.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Arun Ghosh designs protease inhibitors that bind the protease backbone."], ["2006", "FDA approves Prezista for treatment-experienced HIV."], ["2008", "Approval extends to treatment-naive adults."], ["2015", "A darunavir-cobicistat combination (Prezcobix) is approved."], ["2018", "Symtuza, a single-tablet regimen containing darunavir, is approved."]], "cell": "HIV makes its structural proteins and enzymes as long chains called Gag and Gag-Pol polyproteins. After new virus particles bud from the cell, HIV protease, an aspartyl protease made of two identical halves, cuts these chains into working pieces. This step, called maturation, turns an immature particle into an infectious one. Darunavir is a nonpeptide inhibitor that fits the protease active site. Its bis-THF group forms hydrogen bonds with the main-chain atoms of aspartates 29 and 30, which stay in place even when side chains mutate. It also may block protease dimerization. With protease blocked, virions stay immature and cannot infect new cells. Ritonavir or cobicistat blocks CYP3A4 so darunavir levels stay high.", "body": [["Immune system", "Suppresses HIV, allowing CD4 cells to recover, even in many people with drug-resistant virus."], ["Liver", "Liver injury can occur, especially in people with hepatitis B or C."], ["Skin", "Rash is common, and darunavir contains a sulfonamide group, so caution is used in sulfa allergy. Rare severe reactions occur."], ["Metabolism", "Protease inhibitors can raise blood sugar and lipids."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and nausea are common, partly from the booster."]], "journey": "Darunavir is taken by mouth with food, which raises absorption, and with a booster. Without a booster, the liver and gut quickly break it down by CYP3A4. With ritonavir or cobicistat, levels rise many times and the half-life is about 15 hours. It is about 95 percent bound to plasma proteins, mostly alpha-1 acid glycoprotein. It leaves mainly in feces, with a small part in urine. Because the booster blocks CYP3A4, many other drugs interact.", "sidefx": "The booster drives many drug interactions by blocking CYP3A4. Rash and rare severe skin reactions such as SJS reflect immune reactions, and the sulfonamide part of the molecule raises concern in sulfa allergy. Liver injury is a protease inhibitor class effect. Changes in blood sugar and lipids are linked to protease inhibitor effects on insulin signaling and lipid handling. GI upset is common. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Prezista (darunavir) FDA label", "Ghosh AK et al. J Med Chem 2008", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "atazanavir": {"origin": "Atazanavir is an azapeptide protease inhibitor that was first discovered at Ciba-Geigy (later Novartis) and licensed to Bristol-Myers Squibb, which developed it. Earlier protease inhibitors often required several pills a day and caused high cholesterol and triglycerides. Atazanavir was designed for once-daily dosing and had a gentler effect on lipids. The FDA approved it as Reyataz in June 2003, the first once-daily protease inhibitor. It quickly became known for a harmless side effect: yellow eyes from raised bilirubin. It was later combined with cobicistat as Evotaz. Use has declined as newer regimens arrived, but it remains an option.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Atazanavir is discovered at Ciba-Geigy and licensed to Bristol-Myers Squibb."], ["2003", "FDA approves Reyataz, the first once-daily HIV protease inhibitor."], ["2015", "A combination with cobicistat (Evotaz) is approved."]], "cell": "Atazanavir blocks HIV protease, the viral aspartyl protease that cuts Gag and Gag-Pol polyproteins into mature structural proteins and enzymes after new particles bud. Its azapeptide structure mimics the transition state of the cleavage reaction and fits tightly in the active site. With protease blocked, new virions stay immature and cannot infect other cells. Atazanavir also inhibits the human liver enzyme UGT1A1, which normally conjugates bilirubin. This causes a buildup of unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin, similar to Gilbert syndrome, without liver damage. It needs stomach acid to dissolve, so acid-lowering drugs reduce its absorption. Ritonavir or cobicistat is often added to block CYP3A4 and raise its levels.", "body": [["Immune system", "Suppresses HIV replication and allows CD4 recovery."], ["Liver and eyes", "UGT1A1 inhibition raises indirect bilirubin, causing yellow eyes or skin that is harmless but can bother people."], ["Heart", "Prolongs the PR interval and can rarely cause AV block."], ["Kidneys", "Atazanavir can crystallize in urine, causing kidney stones and, less often, chronic kidney disease."], ["Gallbladder", "Gallstones containing atazanavir have been reported."], ["Lipids", "Has smaller effects on cholesterol and triglycerides than many older protease inhibitors."]], "journey": "Atazanavir is taken by mouth with food, which improves absorption. It needs an acidic stomach to dissolve, so proton pump inhibitors, H2 blockers and antacids can lower levels and are timed or avoided. It is about 86 percent protein bound. It is metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4, which ritonavir or cobicistat can block to raise levels. It leaves mainly in feces, with some unchanged drug in urine. The half-life is about seven hours unboosted and longer when boosted.", "sidefx": "Jaundice comes from UGT1A1 inhibition, not liver injury, and is reversible. PR prolongation reflects effects on cardiac conduction, so it is used carefully with other drugs that slow AV conduction. Poor solubility at normal urine pH lets the drug crystallize, causing kidney stones and gallstones. Liver injury and severe skin reactions are less common. CYP3A4 inhibition, especially with a booster, drives many drug interactions. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Reyataz (atazanavir) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "rilpivirine": {"origin": "Rilpivirine came from a research program at Janssen and its spin-off Tibotec in Belgium, led in its early years by Paul Janssen, who worked on HIV drugs until his death in 2003. The team designed diarylpyrimidine NNRTIs, flexible molecules that could twist to fit the NNRTI binding pocket even after the virus mutated it. Etravirine came first, then rilpivirine. The FDA approved rilpivirine as Edurant in May 2011, and in the same year as part of the single-tablet regimen Complera. It later became part of Odefsey and Juluca, and a long-acting injectable form is combined with cabotegravir in Cabenuva. A pediatric dispersible tablet followed later.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Janssen and Tibotec scientists begin designing flexible diarylpyrimidine NNRTIs."], ["2003", "Paul Janssen dies while still working on HIV drug research."], ["2011", "FDA approves Edurant and the single-tablet regimen Complera."], ["2021", "Long-acting injectable rilpivirine is approved as part of Cabenuva."]], "cell": "Rilpivirine is a non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor. It does not compete with nucleotides. Instead it binds a hydrophobic pocket on the p66 subunit of HIV-1 reverse transcriptase, about 10 angstroms from the polymerase active site. Binding locks the enzyme into an inactive shape, distorting the catalytic site and the thumb domain, so it cannot copy viral RNA into DNA or make the second DNA strand. Its flexible diarylpyrimidine structure lets it wiggle and reposition within the pocket, keeping activity against some mutations that defeat older NNRTIs like efavirenz. It does not inhibit human DNA polymerases and has no activity against HIV-2, whose pocket is different. A single key mutation such as E138K can reduce its activity.", "body": [["Immune system", "Suppresses HIV replication and allows CD4 recovery, especially in people starting with lower viral loads."], ["Brain and mood", "Depression, insomnia and headache can occur, but less often than with efavirenz."], ["Skin", "Rash can occur, and rare severe reactions such as DRESS have been reported."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise, especially with hepatitis B or C."], ["Heart", "High levels can prolong the QT interval."]], "journey": "Oral rilpivirine needs a meal and stomach acid for good absorption. A protein drink alone is not enough, and proton pump inhibitors are contraindicated because they block absorption. It peaks in about four to five hours. It is 99.7 percent bound to albumin. The liver metabolizes it by CYP3A4, so strong inducers such as rifampin and some anticonvulsants lower levels and are not allowed. It leaves mainly in feces. The half-life is about 50 hours, which supports once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "CNS effects such as depression and insomnia appear to be a class effect of NNRTIs, though rilpivirine causes fewer than efavirenz. QT prolongation occurs at high exposures from effects on cardiac repolarization. Rash and DRESS are immune reactions. Dependence on acid and food means low levels and treatment failure can happen if taken incorrectly, which can lead to resistance. CYP3A4-based interactions are common. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Edurant (rilpivirine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Janssen PA et al. J Med Chem 2005"]}, "abacavir": {"origin": "Abacavir grew out of nucleoside chemistry at the University of Minnesota. In the 1980s Robert Vince and colleagues built carbocyclic nucleosides, where a carbon ring replaces the sugar's oxygen, and found that one of them, carbovir, blocked HIV in the lab. Carbovir itself was poorly absorbed, so Burroughs Wellcome (later Glaxo Wellcome) designed abacavir, a better absorbed relative that cells turn into the same active carbovir triphosphate. The FDA approved it as Ziagen in 1998. Its story changed in the 2000s when researchers linked its dangerous hypersensitivity reaction to the HLA-B*5701 gene. The PREDICT-1 trial showed that screening for this gene before treatment almost eliminated true reactions, making abacavir a textbook example of pharmacogenomics in daily practice.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Robert Vince's group at the University of Minnesota makes carbovir, a carbocyclic nucleoside active against HIV."], ["1998", "FDA approves abacavir as Ziagen."], ["2004", "FDA approves Epzicom, abacavir with lamivudine in one tablet."], ["2008", "The PREDICT-1 trial shows HLA-B*5701 screening prevents hypersensitivity reactions."], ["2014", "FDA approves Triumeq, abacavir with dolutegravir and lamivudine."]], "cell": "Abacavir is a carbocyclic guanosine analog and a prodrug. Inside the cell it is phosphorylated and converted to carbovir triphosphate, which looks like the natural building block deoxyguanosine triphosphate (dGTP). HIV-1 reverse transcriptase copies the viral RNA genome into DNA, and it mistakes carbovir triphosphate for dGTP and adds it to the growing DNA chain. Because the carbocyclic ring has no 3'-hydroxyl group, the next nucleotide cannot be attached and the chain stops (chain termination). Carbovir triphosphate also competes directly with dGTP for the enzyme's active site. Without a full DNA copy the virus cannot integrate into the host genome, so new rounds of infection are blocked and the viral load falls. Human DNA polymerases are much less sensitive, which gives the drug its selectivity. The hypersensitivity reaction is a separate immune process: abacavir fits into the peptide-binding groove of the HLA-B*57:01 molecule and changes which self peptides are shown to T cells, so CD8 T cells react as if the body were foreign.", "body": [["Immune system", "Lowers HIV replication, so CD4 T-cell counts can recover over months. In people carrying HLA-B*5701, the drug can trigger a T-cell driven multiorgan hypersensitivity reaction."], ["Skin", "A rash is one part of the hypersensitivity reaction, usually together with fever, gut or breathing symptoms, within the first 6 weeks."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are common early side effects and also appear as part of a hypersensitivity reaction."], ["Liver", "Like other nucleoside analogs, it can rarely cause lactic acidosis with an enlarged, fatty liver from mitochondrial stress."], ["Heart and vessels", "Some studies have linked abacavir to a higher chance of heart attack, so it is often avoided in people with high cardiovascular risk."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth, abacavir is absorbed well, and food has no meaningful effect. Blood levels peak in about an hour. It spreads into body fluids and reaches the brain and spinal fluid. The liver breaks it down mainly by alcohol dehydrogenase and glucuronyl transferase, not by CYP enzymes, and the inactive products leave in urine. The plasma half-life is short, about 1.5 hours, but the active carbovir triphosphate lasts about 20 hours inside cells, which is why once-daily dosing works. Alcohol uses the same alcohol dehydrogenase pathway and raises abacavir levels.", "sidefx": "The hypersensitivity reaction is the key danger. It is an immune reaction tied to the HLA-B*5701 gene, where abacavir changes the peptides displayed by that HLA molecule and T cells attack. Symptoms build with each dose, and restarting the drug after a reaction can cause a fast, fatal reaction, which is why screening comes first and rechallenge is contraindicated. Lactic acidosis and fatty liver come from nucleoside analog stress on mitochondria. There is no antidote; care is to stop the drug and support the patient.", "fun": "Abacavir was one of the first drugs where a simple genetic test became routine before the first dose, turning a feared reaction into a largely preventable one.", "src": ["Ziagen (abacavir) FDA label", "Mallal S et al. HLA-B*5701 screening for hypersensitivity to abacavir. N Engl J Med 2008", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "lamivudine": {"origin": "Lamivudine, also called 3TC, was discovered in Montreal. In the late 1980s chemist Bernard Belleau and colleagues at the Canadian company BioChem Pharma (with McGill University) made a new nucleoside analog in which a sulfur atom sits in the sugar ring. One mirror-image form of the molecule was active against HIV and much less toxic to human cells. Glaxo licensed it, and the FDA approved it as Epivir in 1995. Lamivudine soon became one of the backbone drugs of combination HIV therapy, first paired with zidovudine in Combivir. It also proved active against hepatitis B virus, which uses its own reverse transcriptase, and a lower-strength product, Epivir-HBV, was approved for chronic hepatitis B.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Bernard Belleau's team at BioChem Pharma in Montreal synthesizes lamivudine."], ["1995", "FDA approves lamivudine as Epivir for HIV."], ["1997", "Combivir, lamivudine with zidovudine, is approved."], ["1998", "Epivir-HBV is approved for chronic hepatitis B."], ["2019", "Dovato, dolutegravir with lamivudine, is approved as a two-drug regimen."]], "cell": "Lamivudine is a cytidine analog. Cellular kinases add three phosphates to make lamivudine triphosphate. This molecule competes with the natural building block deoxycytidine triphosphate (dCTP) at the active site of HIV-1 reverse transcriptase and of the hepatitis B virus DNA polymerase, which also works by reverse transcription. Once added to the growing viral DNA strand, it stops the chain because its oxathiolane ring has no 3'-hydroxyl group for the next nucleotide to attach. The virus cannot finish its DNA copy, so it cannot integrate (HIV) or refill the pool of viral DNA in the liver (HBV). Human DNA polymerases, especially mitochondrial polymerase gamma, bind it weakly, so toxicity is low. A single change in reverse transcriptase, M184V, lets the enzyme reject lamivudine and causes high-level resistance, but this mutant enzyme copies DNA less efficiently, so the virus is less fit.", "body": [["Immune system", "Suppresses HIV replication so CD4 T cells can recover, always as part of a combination regimen."], ["Liver", "Lowers hepatitis B virus DNA in liver cells and calms liver inflammation. Stopping it can let HBV rebound and cause a severe hepatitis flare."], ["Pancreas", "Pancreatitis has been reported, mainly in children with advanced HIV who were taking other nucleosides."], ["Nerves and blood", "Peripheral neuropathy and low neutrophil counts are uncommon but reported."], ["General", "Headache, nausea, fatigue and diarrhea are the usual mild side effects, and most people tolerate it well."]], "journey": "Lamivudine is taken by mouth and absorbed very well, with high bioavailability, and food has little effect. Levels peak in about an hour. It is weakly bound to proteins and spreads widely. Very little is metabolized; a small amount becomes a trans-sulfoxide. Most of the dose leaves the body unchanged in urine through filtration and active secretion by organic cation transporters, so kidney disease raises levels and the dose is adjusted. The plasma half-life is about 5 to 7 hours, but the active triphosphate lasts much longer inside cells, which supports once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Lamivudine is one of the gentlest nucleoside analogs because it barely touches mitochondrial DNA polymerase gamma, so lactic acidosis and fatty liver are rare. The biggest danger is in people with hepatitis B: the drug holds the virus down, and stopping it lets HBV replicate quickly again, which can cause a severe liver flare. That is why people are tested for HBV before starting and watched after stopping. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Only one of the two mirror-image forms of the molecule is used. Choosing the minus form gave strong antiviral activity with much less toxicity to human cells.", "src": ["Epivir (lamivudine) FDA label", "Epivir-HBV FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "glecaprevir-pibrentasvir": {"origin": "Hepatitis C virus was identified in 1989 by Michael Houghton, Qui-Lim Choo and George Kuo at Chiron, building on the work of Harvey Alter, and the discovery later earned the 2020 Nobel Prize with Charles Rice. For years treatment relied on interferon injections with poor results. Mapping the virus's proteins showed drug targets such as the NS3/4A protease and the NS5A protein. AbbVie developed glecaprevir, a protease inhibitor discovered with Enanta Pharmaceuticals, and pibrentasvir, an NS5A inhibitor, both designed to work across all major HCV genotypes. The FDA approved the combination as Mavyret in 2017. It offered an 8-week, all-oral cure for many patients, including those with kidney disease or on dialysis, because neither drug depends on the kidneys for removal.", "timeline": [["1989", "Hepatitis C virus is identified by scientists at Chiron."], ["2017", "FDA approves Mavyret (glecaprevir and pibrentasvir) for all six main genotypes."], ["2019", "Approval is expanded to children 12 years and older."], ["2020", "Nobel Prize awarded for the discovery of hepatitis C virus."]], "cell": "HCV is an RNA virus that makes one long polyprotein, which must be cut into working pieces. Glecaprevir binds the active site of the NS3/4A serine protease without forming a covalent bond and blocks it, so the polyprotein cannot be cut into the mature nonstructural proteins (NS3, NS4A, NS4B, NS5A and NS5B) that build the replication machinery. NS3/4A also normally cuts the host signaling proteins MAVS and TRIF to switch off the cell's interferon alarm, so blocking it may help the infected cell fight back. Pibrentasvir binds NS5A, a phosphoprotein with no enzyme activity that is needed to build the membrane-bound replication complex and to assemble new virus particles. Blocking NS5A stops both RNA copying and virion assembly, which is why viral RNA falls very fast. Hitting two separate steps at once makes it hard for the virus to escape with a single mutation, giving a high barrier to resistance across genotypes 1 to 6. With no new virus being made, infected liver cells are cleared over time and the infection is cured.", "body": [["Liver", "Viral RNA drops within days and is cured in about 95 to 99 percent of patients. Liver inflammation calms, and fibrosis can slowly improve."], ["Blood sugar", "As the virus clears, insulin sensitivity improves, so people with diabetes can have low blood sugar on their usual medicines."], ["Bilirubin", "Glecaprevir blocks the OATP transporters that move bilirubin into liver cells, so blood bilirubin can rise mildly without liver damage."], ["Clotting", "Better liver function can change INR in people taking warfarin, so monitoring is needed."], ["Hepatitis B", "Clearing HCV can release hepatitis B from suppression in coinfected people, causing reactivation and sometimes fulminant hepatitis."], ["General", "Headache, fatigue and nausea are the most common complaints."]], "journey": "Both drugs are taken together by mouth with food, which improves absorption. Levels peak in about 5 hours. Both are very highly bound to plasma proteins. Glecaprevir has some CYP3A metabolism, while pibrentasvir is essentially not metabolized. Both leave mainly through bile into the feces, with almost nothing in urine, so they can be used in severe kidney disease and dialysis. Half-lives are about 6 hours for glecaprevir and 13 hours for pibrentasvir, which fits once-daily dosing. Because glecaprevir is a substrate of OATP1B1 and 1B3, drugs that block those transporters raise its levels.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are mild, such as headache and fatigue. Bilirubin rises come from glecaprevir blocking OATP and bilirubin transporters. Hypoglycemia and INR changes follow from the liver getting healthier, not from direct toxicity. The boxed warning is hepatitis B reactivation: HCV can suppress HBV in coinfected people, and when HCV clears, HBV can flare. Protease inhibitors can also cause decompensation in advanced cirrhosis, so the product is not used in moderate or severe liver impairment. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Within about 30 years, hepatitis C went from an unnamed cause of transfusion hepatitis to an infection that can usually be cured in 8 weeks of pills.", "src": ["Mavyret (glecaprevir and pibrentasvir) FDA label", "AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 2020 press release"]}, "sofosbuvir-velpatasvir": {"origin": "Sofosbuvir came from Pharmasset, a small company where chemist Michael Sofia led a team that designed a nucleotide prodrug able to deliver its active form straight into liver cells. Gilead bought Pharmasset in 2012 for about 11 billion dollars, and sofosbuvir alone was approved as Sovaldi in 2013. It became famous both for curing hepatitis C and for its high price. Gilead then paired sofosbuvir with velpatasvir, an NS5A inhibitor built to work across all genotypes. The FDA approved the combination as Epclusa in 2016, the first single tablet able to treat all six main HCV genotypes. It made simple, test-and-treat programs possible because genotype testing was no longer essential before starting.", "timeline": [["1989", "Hepatitis C virus is identified."], ["2012", "Gilead acquires Pharmasset, the developer of sofosbuvir."], ["2013", "FDA approves sofosbuvir as Sovaldi."], ["2016", "FDA approves Epclusa (sofosbuvir and velpatasvir), the first pangenotypic single-tablet regimen."], ["2021", "Approval is extended to children 3 years and older."]], "cell": "Sofosbuvir is a phosphoramidate prodrug of a uridine nucleotide. The prodrug design hides the first phosphate so the molecule can enter liver cells. Inside the hepatocyte, cathepsin A and carboxylesterase 1 remove the protecting groups, and then cellular kinases add more phosphates to make the active triphosphate GS-461203. The HCV NS5B RNA-dependent RNA polymerase copies the viral genome and mistakes GS-461203 for uridine triphosphate. After it is added, its modified sugar (a 2'-fluoro, 2'-methyl group) blocks the next nucleotide from joining, so RNA synthesis stops. Human polymerases do not use it, which keeps toxicity low. The NS5B active site is highly conserved, so the drug works across genotypes and resistance is rare. Velpatasvir binds NS5A, a phosphoprotein needed to build the membrane replication complex and to assemble new virions. Blocking NS5A shuts down both RNA replication and virus assembly. Together the two drugs stop viral production quickly, and infected liver cells are cleared over time, leading to cure.", "body": [["Liver", "HCV RNA falls quickly and is cured in about 95 to 99 percent of patients, including those with cirrhosis. Inflammation settles and scarring can slowly regress."], ["Heart", "Combined with amiodarone, sofosbuvir-containing regimens can cause severe slow heart rhythms, including cardiac arrest, by a mechanism that is not fully understood."], ["Blood sugar", "Clearing the virus improves glucose handling, so people with diabetes can develop low blood sugar."], ["Clotting", "Improved liver function can change INR in people taking warfarin."], ["Hepatitis B", "In coinfected people, clearing HCV can let hepatitis B reactivate and cause severe hepatitis."], ["Blood", "When ribavirin is added in decompensated cirrhosis, anemia from ribavirin hemolysis becomes a main concern."]], "journey": "The tablet is taken by mouth with or without food. Sofosbuvir is absorbed and peaks within an hour, then is taken up by liver cells where it is activated to the triphosphate. Its main circulating product is GS-331007, an inactive metabolite with a half-life of about 25 hours that is cleared by the kidneys. Velpatasvir peaks in about 3 hours, is highly protein bound and is partly broken down by CYP2B6, CYP2C8 and CYP3A4, leaving mostly in bile and feces. Its half-life is about 15 hours. Velpatasvir needs stomach acid to dissolve, so acid-reducing drugs can lower its absorption.", "sidefx": "Headache and fatigue are the common complaints. The serious slow heart rhythm with amiodarone is a known drug combination danger, so amiodarone is generally avoided or monitored closely. Hypoglycemia and INR shifts reflect the liver recovering. HBV reactivation is a boxed warning because HCV can hold HBV in check, and clearing HCV removes that brake. Strong P-gp or CYP inducers, such as rifampin and St. John's wort, lower levels and can cause treatment failure. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The start of the generic name sofosbuvir is widely reported to honor Michael Sofia, the chemist who led its discovery.", "src": ["Epclusa (sofosbuvir and velpatasvir) FDA label", "Sovaldi (sofosbuvir) FDA label", "AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance"]}, "entecavir": {"origin": "Entecavir was developed by Bristol-Myers Squibb, whose chemists made it as a carbocyclic analog of the nucleoside 2'-deoxyguanosine. It was first studied as an antiviral against herpes-family viruses, but lab tests showed it was far more potent against hepatitis B virus. In clinical trials it lowered HBV DNA more than lamivudine and was less likely to lead to resistance in people who had never been treated. The FDA approved it as Baraclude in 2005. Along with tenofovir, it became one of the preferred first-line oral drugs for chronic hepatitis B worldwide, helping reduce progression to cirrhosis and liver cancer.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Bristol-Myers Squibb develops entecavir and finds strong activity against hepatitis B virus."], ["2005", "FDA approves entecavir as Baraclude for chronic hepatitis B."], ["2014", "Approval is extended to children 2 years and older."]], "cell": "Hepatitis B virus copies its genome through an RNA middle step, using a viral polymerase that acts as a reverse transcriptase. Entecavir enters liver cells, where cellular kinases convert it to entecavir triphosphate. This triphosphate competes with the natural building block deoxyguanosine triphosphate (dGTP). It blocks all three jobs of the HBV polymerase: base priming (the polymerase starting its own DNA chain), reverse transcription of the negative DNA strand from pregenomic RNA, and synthesis of the positive DNA strand. When added to DNA it acts as a delayed chain terminator, stopping the chain a few bases later. New viral DNA and new virions fall sharply. Entecavir does not remove the covalently closed circular DNA (cccDNA) template inside the nucleus, so the virus is suppressed rather than cured, and treatment is usually long term. Prior lamivudine resistance mutations (such as M204V/I) lower its activity, so resistance is more common in lamivudine-experienced patients.", "body": [["Liver", "Strongly suppresses HBV DNA, lowers ALT and improves liver inflammation and scarring over time, which reduces cirrhosis and liver cancer risk."], ["Kidneys", "The drug is cleared by the kidneys, so poor kidney function raises levels and calls for dose changes."], ["Metabolism", "Lactic acidosis with an enlarged fatty liver is rare but reported, more often in people with decompensated cirrhosis."], ["General", "Headache, fatigue, dizziness and nausea are the usual mild side effects."], ["HIV", "In people with untreated HIV, entecavir has some activity against HIV and can select the M184V resistance mutation, so HIV testing comes first."]], "journey": "Entecavir is taken by mouth on an empty stomach because food lowers its absorption. Levels peak within about an hour. It is not much bound to proteins and spreads widely. It is not a CYP substrate and is barely metabolized, so drug interactions are few. It leaves mainly unchanged in urine by filtration and tubular secretion. The active triphosphate stays in cells for about 15 hours, and the terminal half-life in blood is very long, about 128 to 149 hours, supporting once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Entecavir is well tolerated. The most important danger comes from stopping it: the virus can rebound quickly, causing a severe hepatitis B flare, so liver tests are watched for months after stopping. Like other nucleoside analogs it carries a warning for lactic acidosis and fatty liver, linked to mitochondrial stress. In people with HIV who are not on full HIV treatment, it can select HIV resistance. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Entecavir is so potent against hepatitis B that it is active at very low blood levels, and it was originally studied for a completely different group of viruses.", "src": ["Baraclude (entecavir) FDA label", "AASLD Hepatitis B Guidance", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "palivizumab": {"origin": "Respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) was first isolated in 1956 from chimpanzees and soon recognized as a major cause of severe lung infection in babies. An early vaccine trial in the 1960s backfired, making later infections worse, so researchers looked for safer ways to protect high-risk infants. MedImmune developed palivizumab, a humanized monoclonal antibody against the RSV fusion (F) protein. The IMpact-RSV trial showed it reduced RSV hospitalization in premature infants and those with lung disease, and the FDA approved it as Synagis in 1998. It was the first monoclonal antibody approved to prevent an infectious disease. Its use is now limited because longer-acting antibodies and maternal vaccination cover more babies with fewer shots.", "timeline": [["1956", "RSV is first isolated, from chimpanzees with colds."], ["1960s", "A formalin-inactivated RSV vaccine worsens later disease, stalling vaccine work for decades."], ["1998", "IMpact-RSV trial results published; FDA approves palivizumab as Synagis."], ["2023", "Nirsevimab is approved, and palivizumab moves to a limited backup role."]], "cell": "RSV enters airway cells with the help of its F (fusion) glycoprotein, which changes shape to pull the viral envelope and the host cell membrane together until they fuse. Palivizumab is a humanized IgG1 kappa monoclonal antibody that binds antigenic site II (also called site A) on the F protein. Binding this site prevents the F protein from completing its fusion shape change. The virus cannot fuse with the cell membrane, so it cannot deliver its genome, and infected cells cannot fuse with neighbors to form the syncytia (giant merged cells) that give RSV its name. Site II is shared by both RSV A and B subtypes, so the antibody neutralizes both. This is passive immunity: the baby receives ready-made antibody and does not make its own, so protection fades as the antibody is broken down and a new dose is needed each month of the season. It does not treat an established infection.", "body": [["Lungs and airways", "Neutralizing antibody in the blood and lung lining fluid prevents RSV from spreading deep into the small airways, lowering the risk of severe bronchiolitis and pneumonia."], ["Immune system", "Provides borrowed antibody only. The baby's own immune memory to RSV is not built by the drug."], ["Skin", "Injection site redness, swelling and rash can occur."], ["Blood", "Rare cases of low platelet counts have been reported."], ["Whole body", "Serious allergic reactions including anaphylaxis are rare but possible, as with any foreign protein."]], "journey": "Palivizumab is given as an injection into the thigh muscle. It is slowly absorbed from the muscle into the blood, peaking in about 5 days. As an antibody it stays mostly in the blood and extracellular fluid and reaches the lung lining fluid. It is not broken down by the liver or kidneys like small drugs. Instead it is slowly digested into amino acids like natural IgG. Its half-life in infants is about 20 days, so it is given monthly through the RSV season to keep protective levels.", "sidefx": "Because palivizumab targets a viral protein and not a human one, it causes few side effects. Fever, rash and injection site reactions are the most common. Allergic reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, come from the immune system reacting to the antibody protein itself. Low platelets have been reported rarely. There is no antidote; reactions are treated with usual allergy care.", "fun": "RSV got its name from the way infected cells merge into large many-nucleated cells called syncytia, the exact process palivizumab blocks.", "src": ["Synagis (palivizumab) FDA label", "IMpact-RSV Study Group. Pediatrics 1998", "American Academy of Pediatrics Red Book"]}, "nirsevimab": {"origin": "Nirsevimab came from a breakthrough in structural biology. In 2013 Jason McLellan, Barney Graham and colleagues at the NIH Vaccine Research Center solved the structure of the RSV F protein in its prefusion shape, the form it has before it attacks a cell. They found a site on top, called site zero, that is only present in that shape and is a target for very powerful neutralizing antibodies. MedImmune (part of AstraZeneca) developed nirsevimab, an antibody aimed at site zero, and engineered its tail to last for months. Developed with Sanofi, it was approved by the FDA as Beyfortus in 2023. Unlike palivizumab, one injection can protect a healthy infant through an entire RSV season, so it is recommended broadly rather than only for the highest-risk babies.", "timeline": [["2013", "NIH scientists describe the prefusion RSV F structure and its site zero."], ["2023", "FDA approves nirsevimab as Beyfortus, and CDC recommends it for infants."], ["2023", "Demand outpaces supply in its first season, leading to temporary prioritization."]], "cell": "The RSV F protein starts in a tense prefusion shape and then snaps into a stable postfusion shape that pulls the virus and the host cell together. Nirsevimab is a recombinant human IgG1 kappa monoclonal antibody that binds site zero, an area at the top of the prefusion F protein. Binding locks F in its prefusion form so it cannot refold, and the virus cannot fuse with the airway cell membrane or with neighboring cells. Because prefusion-specific antibodies are much more potent than those against shared sites, small amounts neutralize both RSV A and B. The antibody's Fc tail carries three amino acid changes called YTE (M252Y, S254T and T256E). These raise its binding to the neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn) at acidic pH inside cells, so more of the antibody is rescued from breakdown and recycled back to the blood. That stretches the half-life to about 2 to 3 months, so one dose covers a 5-month season. It is passive immunity, not a vaccine.", "body": [["Lungs and airways", "Antibody in blood and lung fluid blocks RSV entry, reducing medically attended RSV lower respiratory infection in infants by roughly three quarters and lowering hospital admissions."], ["Immune system", "Gives ready-made protection without needing the infant's immune system to respond, which is ideal in the first months of life."], ["Skin", "Rash and injection site reactions can occur but are uncommon."], ["Whole body", "Hypersensitivity reactions are rare. Serious reactions such as anaphylaxis have been reported very rarely."]], "journey": "Nirsevimab is given as a single injection into the thigh muscle. It is absorbed slowly, reaching peak levels in about 6 days. Like other IgG antibodies it stays mostly in blood and extracellular fluid. It is broken down slowly by normal protein catabolism, not by the liver or kidneys. The YTE change gives it a half-life of about 69 to 71 days, which keeps protective levels for roughly 5 months. Larger infants and children in their second season need a larger amount to reach the same levels.", "sidefx": "Since the target is a viral protein, nirsevimab has few side effects. Rash and injection site reactions are the most common. As with any protein product, the immune system can rarely react to the antibody itself, causing allergic reactions. Because the antibody lasts for months, any reaction cannot be removed, so care is supportive. It does not interfere with routine infant vaccines.", "fun": "The YTE tail mutation that lets one shot last a full season was a general engineering trick, and it is used in other long-acting antibodies too.", "src": ["Beyfortus (nirsevimab-alip) FDA label", "McLellan JS et al. Science 2013", "CDC ACIP RSV recommendations"]}, "tecovirimat": {"origin": "Smallpox was declared eradicated in 1980, but the virus still exists in two official labs, and fears of bioterrorism after 2001 pushed the US government to fund smallpox treatments. SIGA Technologies discovered tecovirimat (first called ST-246) in the early 2000s by screening for compounds that block orthopoxvirus spread. Because testing in people with smallpox is impossible, it was approved in 2018 under the FDA Animal Rule, based on survival in monkeys infected with monkeypox and rabbits infected with rabbitpox, plus safety studies in healthy volunteers. TPOXX became the first drug approved to treat smallpox and is stocked in the Strategic National Stockpile. It was widely used under CDC protocols during the 2022 mpox outbreak, but later randomized trials in mpox found it did not speed lesion healing.", "timeline": [["1980", "World Health Organization declares smallpox eradicated."], ["2000s", "SIGA Technologies discovers ST-246 (tecovirimat)."], ["2018", "FDA approves oral TPOXX under the Animal Rule for smallpox."], ["2022", "IV formulation is approved, and tecovirimat is used under CDC protocol during the mpox outbreak."], ["2024", "Randomized mpox trials report no faster lesion resolution with tecovirimat."]], "cell": "Orthopoxviruses such as variola (smallpox), mpox and vaccinia make several forms of virus particles. Intracellular mature virions are infectious but stay inside the cell. To spread efficiently, they must be wrapped in extra membranes from the Golgi or endosomes, travel to the cell surface and leave as enveloped virions. This wrapping needs VP37, a conserved peripheral membrane protein encoded by the F13L gene. Tecovirimat binds VP37 and blocks its interaction with the cell's membrane trafficking machinery (including Rab9 and TIP47), so the virions cannot be wrapped. Without wrapping, enveloped virions are not formed, and both cell-to-cell spread and long-distance spread through the body are blocked. The drug does not kill the virus or stop viral DNA replication, so the immune system still has to clear the infection. Because VP37 is nearly identical across orthopoxviruses, the drug works against the whole genus. A single amino acid change in F13L can cause resistance, which has been seen in some mpox patients after long treatment.", "body": [["Skin", "By blocking spread from cell to cell, it is meant to limit new pox lesions. Randomized mpox trials did not show faster healing."], ["Whole body", "Stops spread of virus through the blood and lymph to other organs in animal models, which improved survival."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and abdominal pain are among the most common side effects."], ["Nervous system", "Headache is the most common side effect."], ["Blood sugar", "Can lower the levels of repaglinide and similar drugs or raise them, and low blood sugar has been reported with repaglinide."], ["Injection site", "The IV form can cause pain and swelling at the infusion site."]], "journey": "Oral tecovirimat is taken with a fatty meal, which helps absorption. Levels peak about 4 to 6 hours later. It is highly protein bound. It is broken down by hydrolysis and by UGT1A1 and UGT1A4 glucuronidation, and its products leave mainly in urine. The half-life is about 19 hours. It is a weak CYP3A inducer and inhibits CYP2C8 and CYP2C19, which explains its drug interactions. The IV form contains a cyclodextrin carrier that is cleared by the kidneys, so it is used with caution in severe kidney disease.", "sidefx": "Because VP37 has no human counterpart, tecovirimat causes few target-related side effects. Headache and gut upset are the most common. Its effects on CYP enzymes can change levels of drugs such as repaglinide and midazolam. With the IV form, the hydroxypropyl beta-cyclodextrin carrier can build up in severe kidney failure. Resistance can arise from F13L mutations, especially with long courses in immunocompromised patients. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Tecovirimat is one of a handful of drugs approved without any efficacy trial in patients, because testing it against real smallpox in people would be impossible and unethical.", "src": ["TPOXX (tecovirimat) FDA label", "CDC tecovirimat information for clinicians", "FDA Animal Rule guidance"]}, "oxcarbazepine": {"origin": "Oxcarbazepine was designed at the Swiss company Geigy, which became Ciba-Geigy and later Novartis. Carbamazepine, which Geigy had introduced in the 1960s, worked well for seizures and trigeminal neuralgia, but it was turned into a 10,11-epoxide that caused side effects and it strongly induced liver enzymes. Chemists added a ketone group at the 10 position to make oxcarbazepine, which is reduced in the body to a different active metabolite and does not form the epoxide. It was introduced in Denmark in 1990 and spread across Europe. The FDA approved it as Trileptal in 2000 for partial-onset seizures. It offered similar seizure control to carbamazepine with fewer drug interactions, though low sodium turned out to be more common.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Geigy introduces carbamazepine, the parent compound."], ["1990", "Oxcarbazepine is first marketed, in Denmark."], ["2000", "FDA approves oxcarbazepine as Trileptal."], ["2012", "FDA approves Oxtellar XR, a once-daily extended-release form."]], "cell": "Oxcarbazepine is a prodrug. A cytosolic arylketone reductase in the liver quickly reduces it to 10-monohydroxy derivative (MHD, also called licarbazepine), which carries most of the effect. MHD binds voltage-gated sodium channels in neurons and prefers the inactivated state of the channel. During a seizure, neurons fire again and again, so their sodium channels spend more time inactivated. MHD binds those channels and slows their return to the resting state, so the cell cannot fire the next action potential as quickly. This use-dependent block stops high-frequency repetitive firing and the spread of seizure activity, while normal slower firing is spared. MHD may also increase potassium conductance and modulate high-voltage activated calcium channels, which further damps excitability. Unlike carbamazepine, it is not converted to an epoxide. Its antidiuretic-like effect, possibly from greater kidney sensitivity to ADH, lowers blood sodium.", "body": [["Brain", "Reduces repetitive neuronal firing and spread of focal seizures. It can also cause dizziness, sleepiness and slowed thinking."], ["Eyes and balance", "Double vision and unsteady walking reflect sodium channel effects in the brainstem and cerebellum, especially as the dose is raised."], ["Kidneys and sodium", "Promotes water retention and can cause hyponatremia, which may show up as confusion, headache or even seizures."], ["Skin", "Rash can occur, and rarely severe reactions such as SJS/TEN. HLA-B*1502 carriers, mostly of Asian ancestry, are at higher risk."], ["Liver enzymes", "Weakly induces CYP3A4, which can lower levels of hormonal contraceptives and make them less reliable."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are common early side effects."]], "journey": "Oxcarbazepine is taken by mouth and absorbed almost completely. It is rapidly converted to MHD, so the parent drug has a half-life of only about 2 hours while MHD lasts about 9 hours and peaks around 4 to 6 hours. MHD is only moderately protein bound. It is glucuronidated in the liver and leaves almost entirely through the kidneys, so kidney disease raises levels. It weakly induces CYP3A4 and inhibits CYP2C19, giving fewer interactions than carbamazepine.", "sidefx": "Dizziness, double vision and ataxia come from too much sodium channel blockade in balance pathways. Hyponatremia is more common than with carbamazepine and is linked to an ADH-like effect on the kidney, so sodium levels are checked. Skin reactions are immune mediated and more likely with HLA-B*1502, and some people who reacted to carbamazepine also react to oxcarbazepine. Stopping suddenly can trigger seizures. There is no antidote; overdose care is supportive.", "fun": "Oxcarbazepine differs from carbamazepine by just one oxygen atom, yet that small change avoids the epoxide metabolite that drives many of carbamazepine's problems.", "src": ["Trileptal (oxcarbazepine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "eslicarbazepine": {"origin": "Eslicarbazepine acetate was developed by BIAL, a Portuguese pharmaceutical company, as the third step in the carbamazepine family. Oxcarbazepine is turned in the body into a mix of two mirror-image forms of licarbazepine, called S and R. BIAL scientists designed a prodrug that delivers mainly the S form, eslicarbazepine, which is the more active one. It was approved in Europe in 2009 as Zebinix and was BIAL's first new drug. The FDA approved it as Aptiom in 2013 for partial-onset seizures, marketed in the US by Sunovion. Its long half-life allows once-daily dosing.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Carbamazepine is introduced."], ["1990", "Oxcarbazepine is first marketed."], ["2009", "Eslicarbazepine acetate is approved in Europe as Zebinix."], ["2013", "FDA approves eslicarbazepine acetate as Aptiom."], ["2015", "US approval is expanded to monotherapy."]], "cell": "Eslicarbazepine acetate is a prodrug that is quickly hydrolyzed during first pass to eslicarbazepine (S-licarbazepine). Eslicarbazepine binds voltage-gated sodium channels in neurons. Unlike carbamazepine, which acts mostly on fast inactivation, it preferentially binds and stabilizes the slow-inactivated state of the channel. Slow inactivation builds up when neurons fire for a long time, as during a seizure, so the drug targets overactive cells and spares neurons firing at normal rates. Stabilizing slow inactivation lowers the number of channels available to open, so repetitive action potentials fail and seizure spread is limited. Blocking sodium channels in the heart's conduction system can lengthen the PR interval. Like oxcarbazepine, it has an ADH-like effect on the kidney that can lower blood sodium.", "body": [["Brain", "Reduces repetitive firing and spread of focal seizures. Dizziness, sleepiness, headache and tremor are common."], ["Eyes and balance", "Double vision, blurred vision and unsteady walking reflect sodium channel effects in the brainstem and cerebellum."], ["Kidneys and sodium", "Can cause hyponatremia through water retention, which may appear as confusion, fatigue or worsening seizures."], ["Heart", "Can lengthen the PR interval, so caution is needed in people with heart conduction problems."], ["Skin", "Rash and, rarely, SJS/TEN or DRESS can occur, with higher risk in HLA-B*1502 carriers."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are common, especially early."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth, eslicarbazepine acetate is almost completely converted to eslicarbazepine on its first pass, so little prodrug reaches the blood. Levels peak in 1 to 4 hours. It is less than 40 percent protein bound. Some is glucuronidated, and both the unchanged drug and the glucuronide leave in urine, so kidney function affects dosing. Its half-life of 13 to 20 hours supports once-daily dosing. It inhibits CYP2C19 and induces CYP3A4, which can lower hormonal contraceptive levels.", "sidefx": "Dizziness, double vision and ataxia come from sodium channel blockade in balance and eye-movement pathways. Hyponatremia comes from an ADH-like effect on the kidney. PR prolongation reflects sodium channel effects on cardiac conduction. Serious skin reactions are immune mediated. It is generally not combined with oxcarbazepine because both make the same active compound. Abrupt stopping can trigger seizures. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Eslicarbazepine, licarbazepine and oxcarbazepine are so closely linked that eslicarbazepine is actually the main active product formed in the body after oxcarbazepine.", "src": ["Aptiom (eslicarbazepine acetate) FDA label", "Zebinix EMA product information", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "clobazam": {"origin": "Clobazam belongs to the benzodiazepine family that began in 1955 when Leo Sternbach at Hoffmann-La Roche made chlordiazepoxide. Most benzodiazepines have nitrogen atoms at positions 1 and 4 of their seven-membered ring. Clobazam, first made in the 1960s, moved one nitrogen to position 5, creating a 1,5-benzodiazepine. It was marketed in Europe and elsewhere from the 1970s as an anxiety drug, and doctors noticed it also helped seizures with somewhat less sedation than classic benzodiazepines. In the US it arrived much later: the FDA approved it as Onfi in 2011 for seizures of Lennox-Gastaut syndrome, a severe childhood epilepsy. An oral film, Sympazan, followed in 2018.", "timeline": [["1955", "Leo Sternbach makes the first benzodiazepine, chlordiazepoxide, at Roche."], ["1960s", "Clobazam, a 1,5-benzodiazepine, is synthesized."], ["1970s", "Clobazam is marketed outside the US, first as an anxiety drug."], ["2011", "FDA approves clobazam as Onfi for Lennox-Gastaut syndrome."], ["2018", "FDA approves Sympazan oral film."]], "cell": "GABA is the brain's main inhibitory neurotransmitter. Its GABA-A receptor is a ligand-gated chloride channel made of five subunits. Clobazam binds the benzodiazepine site at the junction of an alpha subunit and the gamma2 subunit. This is an allosteric site, separate from where GABA binds. Clobazam does not open the channel on its own. Instead it raises the receptor's affinity for GABA and increases how often the channel opens when GABA is present. More chloride flows into the neuron, the membrane becomes more negative (hyperpolarized), and the cell is harder to fire. In seizure circuits this limits the spread of abnormal discharges. Because its ring structure differs, clobazam has relatively more activity at alpha2-containing receptors (linked to anxiety relief and anticonvulsant action) than at alpha1-containing receptors (linked to sedation), which may explain why it is often less sedating than 1,4-benzodiazepines. Its main metabolite, N-desmethylclobazam, is active, circulates at higher levels and lasts longer, so it provides most of the effect.", "body": [["Brain", "Reduces seizure spread, including drop attacks in Lennox-Gastaut syndrome, and lowers anxiety. Sleepiness, lethargy and ataxia are common."], ["Behavior", "Irritability, aggression and sometimes paradoxical agitation can occur, especially in children with developmental disabilities."], ["Breathing", "Alone it has modest effect on breathing, but with opioids or other depressants it can cause serious respiratory depression."], ["Mouth and gut", "Drooling and constipation are common in children, partly from reduced swallowing and muscle tone."], ["Skin", "Rarely causes Stevens-Johnson syndrome or toxic epidermal necrolysis, most often in the first weeks."], ["Dependence", "The brain adapts to long-term use, so stopping suddenly can cause withdrawal and rebound seizures."]], "journey": "Clobazam is taken by mouth as tablets, liquid or film and is almost completely absorbed, peaking within 0.5 to 4 hours. It is fairly highly protein bound and enters the brain readily. CYP3A4 removes a methyl group to make the active N-desmethylclobazam, which is then broken down mainly by CYP2C19. People who are poor CYP2C19 metabolizers build up much more of the active metabolite. The parent half-life is about 36 to 42 hours and the metabolite about 71 to 82 hours, so it takes 1 to 2 weeks to reach steady state. Metabolites leave in urine.", "sidefx": "Sleepiness, lethargy and poor coordination come from extra GABA-A inhibition in the cortex and cerebellum. Respiratory depression comes from inhibition in brainstem breathing centers, and opioids add to it through a separate pathway, which is why the combination carries a boxed warning. Long-term use changes GABA-A receptors, so dependence develops and abrupt stopping can cause seizures. Flumazenil can reverse benzodiazepine effects, but it may trigger seizures in people with epilepsy or dependence, so it is used with great caution.", "fun": "Clobazam was used in many countries for decades before US approval, and the 2011 FDA approval was granted for a rare epilepsy, Lennox-Gastaut syndrome, under orphan drug status.", "src": ["Onfi (clobazam) FDA label", "Sympazan FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "clonazepam": {"origin": "Clonazepam came out of Leo Sternbach's benzodiazepine program at Hoffmann-La Roche. After chlordiazepoxide (Librium, 1960) and diazepam (Valium, 1963), Roche chemists made many related compounds. Adding a nitro group at position 7 and a chlorine on the side ring produced clonazepam, a very potent benzodiazepine with strong anticonvulsant effects. It was patented in the early 1960s and approved by the FDA as Klonopin in 1975 for seizure disorders. Years later it was also approved for panic disorder, and it became one of the most prescribed benzodiazepines in the US.", "timeline": [["1955", "Leo Sternbach synthesizes the first benzodiazepine at Roche."], ["1960s", "Roche develops clonazepam, a high-potency 7-nitro benzodiazepine."], ["1975", "FDA approves clonazepam as Klonopin for seizure disorders."], ["1997", "Clonazepam is approved for panic disorder."], ["2020", "FDA adds a boxed warning on abuse, dependence and withdrawal to all benzodiazepines."]], "cell": "Clonazepam binds the benzodiazepine site on GABA-A receptors, located between an alpha subunit (alpha1, 2, 3 or 5) and the gamma2 subunit. It is a positive allosteric modulator: it does not open the chloride channel by itself but makes GABA bind more tightly and increases how often the channel opens. More chloride enters the neuron, which hyperpolarizes the membrane and makes action potentials less likely. In the cortex and thalamus this damps the spread of seizure discharges, including the thalamocortical rhythms behind absence and myoclonic seizures. In the amygdala and limbic system it lowers the fear response that drives panic. Because GABA must be present for the drug to work, benzodiazepines have a ceiling effect and are safer alone than barbiturates, which can open the channel directly at high levels. Clonazepam has high affinity for the receptor, which makes it potent at low concentrations.", "body": [["Brain", "Calms anxiety and panic, suppresses seizures, and causes sleepiness, slowed thinking and memory problems."], ["Cerebellum and muscles", "Relaxes muscles and can cause ataxia and falls, especially in older adults."], ["Breathing", "Alone it mildly lowers breathing drive; with opioids or alcohol it can cause dangerous respiratory depression."], ["Mood", "Can cause or worsen depression, and paradoxical agitation can occur, especially in children and older adults."], ["Mouth", "Increased saliva and drooling can occur, particularly in children."], ["Dependence", "Long-term use leads to tolerance and physical dependence, so stopping suddenly can cause withdrawal and seizures."]], "journey": "Clonazepam is taken by mouth as tablets or orally disintegrating tablets. It is well absorbed, with high bioavailability, and peaks in 1 to 4 hours. It is about 85 percent protein bound and fat soluble, so it enters the brain easily. The liver reduces its nitro group to 7-amino-clonazepam, mainly by CYP3A4, and then acetylates it. These inactive metabolites leave in urine. The half-life is long, about 30 to 40 hours, so effects build over several days and can carry over to the next day. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors can raise levels.", "sidefx": "Sedation, poor coordination and memory loss all come from increased GABA inhibition in the cortex, cerebellum and hippocampus. Combined with opioids or alcohol, which depress breathing through other pathways, the effects add up and can stop breathing, the reason for the boxed warning. Over time GABA-A receptors adapt, so tolerance and dependence develop and abrupt stopping can cause rebound anxiety, insomnia and seizures. Flumazenil, a benzodiazepine site antagonist, can reverse the effect but can trigger seizures in dependent patients or those with epilepsy, so it is used rarely and carefully.", "fun": "Clonazepam's long half-life is part of why it is used for panic disorder: levels stay fairly steady, so there is less of the between-dose rebound seen with shorter benzodiazepines.", "src": ["Klonopin (clonazepam) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "FDA Drug Safety Communication on benzodiazepines, 2020"]}, "brivaracetam": {"origin": "Brivaracetam came from the Belgian company UCB, which had made levetiracetam (Keppra, FDA approved 1999). For years nobody knew how levetiracetam worked. In 2004 UCB scientists reported that it binds synaptic vesicle protein 2A (SV2A), a protein in the membranes of the small sacs that store neurotransmitters. With that target known, UCB screened many related compounds for stronger SV2A binding and picked brivaracetam, which binds roughly 15 to 30 times more tightly. The FDA approved it as Briviact in 2016 for partial-onset seizures, in tablet, liquid and IV forms. It is a Schedule V controlled substance.", "timeline": [["1999", "FDA approves levetiracetam, the parent drug, made by UCB."], ["2004", "UCB scientists identify SV2A as the binding site of levetiracetam."], ["2016", "FDA approves brivaracetam as Briviact."], ["2021", "Approval is extended to children 1 month and older."]], "cell": "Neurons store neurotransmitters such as glutamate in synaptic vesicles. When an action potential arrives, calcium enters the nerve terminal and vesicles fuse with the membrane to release their contents. SV2A is a glycoprotein in the vesicle membrane that helps keep vesicles ready for this release, likely by helping regulate the calcium sensor synaptotagmin and the size of the releasable pool. Brivaracetam binds SV2A with high affinity and selectivity. This modulates vesicle exocytosis, especially during rapid, repeated firing, so less excitatory transmitter is released when neurons fire in bursts as they do in seizures. The net effect is reduced hyperexcitability and less seizure spread. Unlike levetiracetam, brivaracetam has little effect on N-type calcium channels or AMPA receptors, and it enters the brain faster because it is more fat soluble.", "body": [["Brain", "Reduces focal seizure frequency and spread. Sleepiness, dizziness and fatigue are the common central effects."], ["Mood and behavior", "Irritability, anxiety, depression and rarely psychosis or suicidal thoughts can occur, though behavioral problems may be less frequent than with levetiracetam."], ["Balance", "Ataxia and dizziness can occur, especially when the dose is raised."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are mild and fairly common."], ["Airways", "Rare bronchospasm and angioedema have been reported."]], "journey": "Brivaracetam is taken by mouth or given IV. By mouth it is almost completely absorbed and peaks in about an hour. It is weakly protein bound and, being lipophilic, crosses into the brain within minutes, faster than levetiracetam. It is broken down mainly by an amidase that turns it into a carboxylic acid, with some CYP2C19 hydroxylation. Over 95 percent leaves in urine as inactive metabolites. Its half-life is about 9 hours, so it is given twice a day. Rifampin lowers its levels, and CYP2C19 poor metabolizers have higher levels.", "sidefx": "Sleepiness and dizziness reflect reduced excitatory signaling in arousal and balance circuits. The psychiatric effects are thought to relate to SV2A modulation in mood circuits, as with levetiracetam. Because the brain adapts to the drug, abrupt stopping can trigger seizures. Hypersensitivity reactions such as bronchospasm and angioedema are rare immune responses. There is no antidote; overdose care is supportive.", "fun": "Brivaracetam is a rare case of a drug designed after a drug target was found by working backward from an older medicine whose mechanism had been a mystery.", "src": ["Briviact (brivaracetam) FDA label", "Lynch BA et al. The synaptic vesicle protein SV2A is the binding site for the antiepileptic drug levetiracetam. PNAS 2004", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "cenobamate": {"origin": "Cenobamate was discovered and developed by SK Biopharmaceuticals of South Korea and its US arm, SK Life Science. Researchers were looking for a new type of carbamate anticonvulsant that could help people whose focal seizures did not respond to other drugs. In clinical trials a notable share of patients with drug-resistant epilepsy became seizure free, which is unusual for an add-on drug. The FDA approved it as Xcopri in 2019 for partial-onset seizures in adults. Early testing showed DRESS, a serious drug reaction, when the dose was raised quickly, so the approved plan uses a slow titration over several weeks.", "timeline": [["2000s", "SK Biopharmaceuticals develops cenobamate in South Korea."], ["2019", "FDA approves cenobamate as Xcopri for partial-onset seizures in adults."], ["2020", "Cenobamate is placed in Schedule V."], ["2021", "Approved in Europe as Ontozry."]], "cell": "Cenobamate works in two ways. First, it blocks voltage-gated sodium channels, but with a preference for the persistent (late) sodium current over the fast transient current. The persistent current is a small leak of sodium that continues after the main spike and helps neurons fire again and again, which drives seizures. By damping this current, cenobamate reduces repetitive firing while affecting normal single action potentials less. Second, it acts as a positive allosteric modulator of GABA-A receptors at a site different from the benzodiazepine site, increasing chloride flow and hyperpolarizing neurons. This adds inhibitory tone, including the tonic inhibition that steadies neurons between signals. Together these actions lower neuronal excitability and stop seizure spread. In the heart, cutting late sodium current shortens repolarization, which is why the QT interval shortens in a dose-related way.", "body": [["Brain", "Reduces focal seizures, with high seizure-freedom rates in trials. Sleepiness, dizziness, fatigue and headache are common."], ["Eyes and balance", "Double vision and balance problems come from sodium channel and GABA effects in the cerebellum and brainstem."], ["Heart", "Shortens the QT interval and is contraindicated in familial short QT syndrome."], ["Skin and immune system", "Rapid dose increases were linked to DRESS, a multiorgan hypersensitivity reaction, so slow titration is used."], ["Liver enzymes", "Inhibits CYP2C19 and induces CYP3A4, raising phenytoin and phenobarbital levels and lowering hormonal contraceptive levels."]], "journey": "Cenobamate is taken by mouth and absorbed well, with bioavailability near 90 percent, peaking in 1 to 4 hours. It is about 60 percent protein bound and enters the brain. It is broken down by glucuronidation, mainly by UGT2B7, and by several CYP enzymes, and the products leave mostly in urine. Its half-life is long, about 50 to 60 hours, and it shows nonlinear kinetics at higher levels, so levels rise more than expected as the dose goes up. Steady state takes about two weeks.", "sidefx": "Dizziness, sleepiness and double vision reflect its dual action on sodium channels and GABA-A receptors in balance and arousal circuits. QT shortening comes from reduced late sodium current in heart muscle. DRESS is an immune reaction that appeared with fast titration and has not been seen with the slow schedule. Its enzyme effects cause clinically important drug interactions. Abrupt stopping can trigger seizures. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Cenobamate is one of the few seizure drugs known to shorten rather than lengthen the QT interval.", "src": ["Xcopri (cenobamate) FDA label", "Krauss GL et al. Lancet Neurology 2020", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "primidone": {"origin": "Primidone was made in 1949 by J. Yule Bogue and H. C. Carrington at Imperial Chemical Industries in England. They removed one oxygen from the barbiturate ring of phenobarbital, hoping to keep its seizure-stopping effect with less sedation. The new compound, sold as Mysoline, reached the market in the early 1950s, including in the US. Later research showed that much of its effect comes from being turned into phenobarbital in the body. In the 1980s neurologists found by chance that primidone eases essential tremor, and that became one of its most common uses.", "timeline": [["1912", "Phenobarbital is introduced as an anticonvulsant."], ["1949", "Bogue and Carrington at ICI synthesize primidone."], ["1950s", "Primidone is marketed as Mysoline in the UK and US."], ["1980s", "Primidone is reported to reduce essential tremor."]], "cell": "Primidone is a deoxybarbiturate that acts through itself and two active metabolites. The parent drug reduces repetitive neuronal firing, likely by slowing recovery of voltage-gated sodium channels. In the liver it is oxidized to phenobarbital and cleaved to phenylethylmalonamide (PEMA). Phenobarbital binds the barbiturate site on the GABA-A receptor, a site different from the benzodiazepine site. It increases the duration of each chloride channel opening when GABA binds, and at high levels it can open the channel directly. More chloride enters, the membrane hyperpolarizes and neurons are harder to fire. Phenobarbital also blocks AMPA glutamate receptors, further lowering excitation. PEMA has weak anticonvulsant activity and may add to the others. In essential tremor, the parent drug appears to matter most, since primidone helps tremor before phenobarbital levels build up. Phenobarbital strongly induces CYP enzymes, which explains many drug interactions.", "body": [["Brain", "Reduces generalized and focal seizures, and calms essential tremor. Sedation, dizziness and fatigue are common."], ["Balance", "Ataxia and vertigo reflect effects on cerebellar and vestibular pathways."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are common, especially at the start."], ["Liver", "Phenobarbital strongly induces liver CYP enzymes, lowering levels of many other drugs including warfarin and hormonal contraceptives."], ["Blood", "Can cause megaloblastic anemia by interfering with folate, which responds to folic acid."], ["Breathing", "In overdose, barbiturate effects can depress breathing and cause coma."]], "journey": "Primidone is taken by mouth and absorbed well, peaking in about 3 to 4 hours. It is only lightly protein bound. The liver converts part of it to phenobarbital and PEMA. Primidone's own half-life is about 10 to 15 hours, but phenobarbital lasts 75 to 120 hours, so it builds up slowly over weeks. Unchanged drug and metabolites leave in urine. Because phenobarbital induces liver enzymes, it also speeds the breakdown of primidone and other medicines over time.", "sidefx": "Many new users have an acute toxic reaction with sleepiness, dizziness and nausea, because primidone itself acts before the body's enzymes adapt, so it is started very slowly. Sedation and ataxia come from GABA-A enhancement by phenobarbital and sodium channel effects. Megaloblastic anemia comes from folate disruption. Abrupt stopping can cause withdrawal seizures and status epilepticus, because the brain adapts to barbiturate effects. There is no specific antidote; overdose is treated with supportive care and sometimes urinary alkalinization to speed phenobarbital removal.", "fun": "Primidone is essentially phenobarbital minus one oxygen atom, and the body puts much of that missing piece back.", "src": ["Mysoline (primidone) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "rizatriptan": {"origin": "Triptans grew from research at Glaxo in the 1970s and 1980s, where Patrick Humphrey and colleagues searched for a drug that would mimic serotonin's effect on cranial blood vessels without its many other effects. Their work produced sumatriptan, approved in the US in 1992. Other companies followed with second-generation triptans that were better absorbed by mouth. Merck developed rizatriptan, which the FDA approved as Maxalt in 1998, along with a melt-in-the-mouth tablet, Maxalt-MLT. It became known for working quickly. In 2011 it became the first triptan approved for children 6 to 17 years old.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Glaxo scientists begin searching for selective serotonin agonists for migraine."], ["1992", "FDA approves sumatriptan, the first triptan."], ["1998", "FDA approves rizatriptan as Maxalt and Maxalt-MLT."], ["2011", "Rizatriptan is approved for migraine in children 6 to 17 years."]], "cell": "Migraine pain involves the trigeminovascular system: trigeminal nerve fibers that wrap around meningeal blood vessels. When activated, these fibers release CGRP, substance P and other peptides that dilate vessels and cause inflammation, and they send pain signals to the brainstem. Rizatriptan is a selective agonist at serotonin 5-HT1B and 5-HT1D receptors, which are Gi/o-coupled receptors that lower cAMP. On smooth muscle of dilated intracranial vessels, 5-HT1B activation causes constriction. On trigeminal nerve terminals, 5-HT1D activation reduces the release of CGRP and substance P, calming neurogenic inflammation. Activation of these receptors in the trigeminal nucleus caudalis also blocks pain signals moving toward the thalamus. Together these effects stop the migraine attack. 5-HT1B receptors are also found on coronary arteries, which is why triptans can cause coronary narrowing and are avoided in heart disease.", "body": [["Head and meninges", "Constricts dilated cranial vessels and calms inflammation around trigeminal nerves, relieving headache, nausea and light sensitivity."], ["Nerves", "Blocks pain signal transmission in the brainstem. Tingling, warmth and paresthesias are common short-lived effects."], ["Heart", "Can narrow coronary arteries, causing chest tightness and rarely heart attack, so it is avoided in coronary or vascular disease."], ["Chest and throat", "Feelings of pressure or tightness in the chest, throat or jaw are common and usually not cardiac, but must be distinguished from heart pain."], ["Brain", "Dizziness, sleepiness and fatigue are common."], ["Blood pressure", "Can raise blood pressure, and it is avoided in uncontrolled hypertension."]], "journey": "Rizatriptan is taken by mouth as a tablet or an orally disintegrating tablet that melts on the tongue but is still absorbed in the gut. Bioavailability is about 45 percent. Tablets peak in about 1 to 1.5 hours and the dissolving tablet a little later. It is lightly protein bound. Monoamine oxidase A breaks it down by oxidative deamination, so MAO inhibitors raise levels and are contraindicated. Propranolol also raises levels. It leaves mainly in urine as metabolites, with a short half-life of 2 to 3 hours.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from 5-HT1B and 5-HT1D activation outside the migraine circuit. Coronary 5-HT1B receptors explain chest tightness and the rare heart attack. Peripheral nerve and vessel effects cause tingling, warmth and flushing. Combining with other serotonin drugs can rarely contribute to serotonin syndrome. Using triptans too often can cause medication overuse headache. Another triptan or an ergot is not taken within 24 hours. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The orally disintegrating tablet melts on the tongue, but it is still absorbed in the gut after swallowing, so it does not work faster than the regular tablet.", "src": ["Maxalt (rizatriptan) FDA label", "Humphrey PPA. The discovery and development of the triptans. Headache 2008", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "zolmitriptan": {"origin": "Zolmitriptan was one of the second-generation triptans made after sumatriptan. It was discovered by scientists at the Wellcome Foundation in the UK in the early 1990s, who aimed for a triptan that was better absorbed by mouth and could reach the brainstem. After Glaxo and Wellcome merged, rights went to Zeneca (later AstraZeneca). The FDA approved it as Zomig in 1997, with a dissolving tablet and a nasal spray following. The nasal spray gives faster relief and is also used for cluster headache attacks.", "timeline": [["1992", "FDA approves sumatriptan, the first triptan."], ["1990s", "Wellcome scientists discover zolmitriptan."], ["1997", "FDA approves zolmitriptan as Zomig tablets."], ["2003", "Zomig nasal spray is approved."]], "cell": "Zolmitriptan is a selective agonist at serotonin 5-HT1B and 5-HT1D receptors, which are Gi/o-coupled receptors that lower cAMP. On dilated cranial blood vessels, 5-HT1B activation causes the smooth muscle to contract and the vessels to narrow. On trigeminal nerve terminals around the meninges, 5-HT1D activation reduces release of CGRP and substance P, which normally dilate vessels and cause neurogenic inflammation. Zolmitriptan is more lipophilic than sumatriptan and crosses into the brainstem, where it can also act on receptors in the trigeminal nucleus caudalis and block pain signals traveling toward the thalamus. In the liver, CYP1A2 converts it to N-desmethylzolmitriptan, an active metabolite that is 2 to 6 times more potent at these receptors and adds to the effect. 5-HT1B receptors on coronary arteries explain why it can narrow heart vessels.", "body": [["Head and meninges", "Narrows dilated cranial vessels and calms inflammation, relieving headache, nausea and sensitivity to light and sound."], ["Brainstem", "Blocks pain signals in the trigeminal nucleus, which helps both migraine and cluster headache."], ["Heart", "Can narrow coronary arteries and rarely cause heart attack, so it is avoided in coronary disease."], ["Chest and throat", "Pressure or tightness in the chest, throat or jaw is common and usually harmless."], ["Nerves", "Tingling, warmth and heaviness are common short-lived sensations."], ["Nose and mouth", "The nasal spray often causes an unusual taste."]], "journey": "Zolmitriptan tablets are absorbed well, with bioavailability around 40 percent, and peak in about 1.5 to 2 hours. The nasal spray is partly absorbed through the nasal lining and works faster. It is lightly protein bound. CYP1A2 makes the active N-desmethyl metabolite, and MAO-A then breaks it down. Cimetidine and MAO inhibitors raise levels. About two thirds of the dose leaves in urine and the rest in feces. The half-life is about 3 hours.", "sidefx": "Side effects reflect 5-HT1B and 5-HT1D activation in places other than the migraine circuit. Coronary vessel narrowing explains chest pressure and rare cardiac events. Activation of receptors on peripheral nerves and vessels causes tingling and warmth. Using it with other serotonin drugs can rarely contribute to serotonin syndrome. Frequent use can cause medication overuse headache. Other triptans and ergots are not taken within 24 hours. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Zolmitriptan's active metabolite is actually stronger at the target receptors than zolmitriptan itself.", "src": ["Zomig (zolmitriptan) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "dihydroergotamine": {"origin": "Dihydroergotamine (DHE) comes from ergot, a fungus called Claviceps purpurea that infects rye. In the Middle Ages ergot-contaminated bread caused outbreaks of ergotism, known as St. Anthony's fire, with burning limbs from severe vessel narrowing and gangrene. In 1918 Arthur Stoll at Sandoz in Basel isolated ergotamine, which was later used for migraine. In 1943 Stoll and Albert Hofmann (who also discovered LSD) added hydrogen to ergotamine to make dihydroergotamine, which narrowed arteries less and caused less vomiting. The FDA approved it as D.H.E. 45 injection in 1946. In 1986 Neil Raskin described repeated IV DHE for hard-to-treat headaches, and a nasal spray, Migranal, was approved in 1997.", "timeline": [["9th century", "Recorded epidemics of ergotism from contaminated rye begin in Europe, later called St. Anthony's fire."], ["1918", "Arthur Stoll at Sandoz isolates ergotamine from ergot."], ["1943", "Stoll and Albert Hofmann create dihydroergotamine."], ["1946", "FDA approves D.H.E. 45 injection."], ["1986", "Neil Raskin describes the repetitive IV DHE protocol for intractable headache."], ["1997", "FDA approves Migranal nasal spray."]], "cell": "DHE is an ergot alkaloid with a structure similar to serotonin, dopamine and norepinephrine, so it binds many receptors. Its migraine effect comes mainly from agonism at serotonin 5-HT1B and 5-HT1D receptors. On meningeal and cranial vessels, 5-HT1B activation narrows dilated arteries and reduces arteriovenous shunting. On trigeminal nerve endings, 5-HT1D activation blocks release of CGRP, substance P and other peptides, stopping neurogenic inflammation. It also acts in the brainstem to inhibit pain signaling in the trigeminal nucleus. DHE also binds 5-HT1A, 5-HT2A, alpha1 and alpha2 adrenergic receptors and dopamine D2 receptors. D2 activation in the chemoreceptor trigger zone causes nausea. Alpha-adrenergic and 5-HT2A effects narrow peripheral arteries and especially veins. DHE comes off its receptors slowly, which gives a long effect and a low rate of headache coming back.", "body": [["Head and meninges", "Narrows dilated cranial vessels and blocks trigeminal inflammation, ending migraine and cluster attacks with a low recurrence rate."], ["Gut and brain", "D2 stimulation in the vomiting center causes nausea and vomiting, so an antiemetic is often given first with IV use."], ["Heart", "Can narrow coronary arteries and cause chest pain, heart attack or arrhythmia, so it is avoided in heart disease."], ["Arms and legs", "Constriction of peripheral arteries can cause cold, numb, pale hands and feet, muscle cramps and, rarely, gangrene."], ["Veins", "Strong venoconstriction is part of its profile, more than with triptans."], ["Pregnancy", "Ergots can trigger uterine contractions and reduce blood flow to the uterus, so DHE is contraindicated in pregnancy."]], "journey": "DHE is poorly absorbed by mouth, so it is given by IV, IM or subcutaneous injection or as a nasal spray. IV works within minutes, IM within about 15 to 30 minutes, and nasal spray within about 30 minutes. It is highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down extensively by CYP3A4, making an active 8-hydroxy metabolite. It leaves mainly in bile and feces. The terminal half-life is about 9 to 10 hours, and the effect often lasts longer because of slow receptor release.", "sidefx": "Nausea comes from D2 stimulation in the brain's vomiting center. Narrowing of coronary and limb arteries, through 5-HT1B, 5-HT2A and alpha-adrenergic effects, explains chest pain, cold extremities and rare ischemia. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as clarithromycin, ritonavir and ketoconazole can raise DHE levels to the point of severe vasospasm and gangrene, which is a boxed warning and why these combinations are contraindicated. DHE is not used within 24 hours of a triptan. There is no specific antidote; severe vasospasm is treated with vasodilators and supportive care.", "fun": "Albert Hofmann, who helped create DHE, is better known for discovering LSD, another ergot derivative, while working at the same Sandoz lab.", "src": ["D.H.E. 45 (dihydroergotamine mesylate) FDA label", "Migranal FDA label", "Raskin NH. Neurology 1986", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "lasmiditan": {"origin": "Triptans work well for migraine but narrow blood vessels, which rules them out for many people with heart disease. Researchers at Eli Lilly noticed that the serotonin 5-HT1F receptor sits on trigeminal nerves but is not found on vessel smooth muscle in a way that causes narrowing. Lilly developed lasmiditan as a selective 5-HT1F agonist, licensed it to the smaller company CoLucid Pharmaceuticals for development, and bought CoLucid back in 2017. The FDA approved it as Reyvow in 2019, the first drug in a new class called ditans. Because it caused dizziness and euphoria in some people, it was placed in Schedule V in 2020.", "timeline": [["1990s", "The 5-HT1F receptor is identified on trigeminal neurons."], ["2017", "Eli Lilly acquires CoLucid Pharmaceuticals and regains lasmiditan."], ["2019", "FDA approves lasmiditan as Reyvow, the first ditan."], ["2020", "DEA places lasmiditan in Schedule V."]], "cell": "Lasmiditan is a highly selective agonist at the serotonin 5-HT1F receptor, a Gi/o-coupled receptor. 5-HT1F receptors are found on trigeminal nerve terminals around meningeal vessels and on neurons in the trigeminal nucleus caudalis and other central pain pathways. Activating them lowers cAMP and reduces release of CGRP and glutamate from trigeminal neurons. Less CGRP means less vasodilation and less neurogenic inflammation in the meninges. Less glutamate in the brainstem means pain signals are not passed on as strongly to higher centers. Lasmiditan has very low affinity for 5-HT1B, the receptor that triptans use to constrict blood vessels, so it does not narrow coronary or cranial arteries in a meaningful way. Its chemical structure is not an indole like the triptans, and it is fat soluble enough to cross the blood-brain barrier, which explains its central nervous system side effects such as dizziness and sleepiness.", "body": [["Head and meninges", "Calms trigeminal nerve inflammation and relieves migraine pain, nausea and light or sound sensitivity without narrowing vessels."], ["Brain", "Dizziness, sleepiness, tingling and fatigue are common, and some people feel euphoria or hallucinations at higher doses."], ["Driving", "Can impair driving for hours, so labeling says not to drive for at least 8 hours after a dose."], ["Heart", "Does not constrict coronary arteries, but can slightly lower heart rate and raise blood pressure."], ["Muscles", "A feeling of muscle weakness or heaviness can occur."]], "journey": "Lasmiditan is taken by mouth and absorbed fairly quickly, peaking in about 1.8 hours. It is moderately protein bound and crosses into the brain. It is broken down mainly by ketone reductases in the liver and other tissues, not by CYP enzymes, and leaves mainly as metabolites in urine. The half-life is about 5.7 hours. It can raise levels of drugs that depend on P-gp or BCRP and can add to the heart-rate-lowering effect of drugs like beta-blockers.", "sidefx": "Because lasmiditan enters the brain readily, 5-HT1F activation and other central effects cause dizziness, sleepiness, tingling and impaired driving. Euphoria and abuse potential led to its Schedule V status. Combining it with other serotonin drugs can rarely contribute to serotonin syndrome. Frequent use can cause medication overuse headache. Its lack of vasoconstriction is what makes it an option for people with cardiovascular risk. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The word ditan was coined for this class to set it apart from the triptans, even though both act on serotonin receptors.", "src": ["Reyvow (lasmiditan) FDA label", "Kuca B et al. Neurology 2018 (SAMURAI trial)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "rimegepant": {"origin": "Calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP) was discovered in 1982 when researchers found that the calcitonin gene could be spliced to make a second, different peptide. In the 1980s and 1990s, studies showed CGRP rises in blood during migraine and that infusing it can trigger migraine. Early small-molecule CGRP blockers, called gepants, worked but had problems; telcagepant from Merck was stopped because of liver toxicity. Bristol-Myers Squibb discovered rimegepant, and Biohaven Pharmaceuticals developed it. The FDA approved it as Nurtec ODT in 2020 for acute migraine and in 2021 for prevention of episodic migraine, making it the first drug approved for both. Pfizer acquired Biohaven's migraine drugs in 2022.", "timeline": [["1982", "CGRP is discovered from alternative splicing of the calcitonin gene."], ["1990s", "CGRP is linked to migraine attacks."], ["2020", "FDA approves rimegepant as Nurtec ODT for acute migraine."], ["2021", "Rimegepant is approved for preventive treatment of episodic migraine."], ["2022", "Pfizer acquires Biohaven's migraine portfolio."]], "cell": "CGRP is a 37-amino-acid neuropeptide released from trigeminal nerve endings during a migraine attack. It acts on the CGRP receptor, a complex of the calcitonin receptor-like receptor (CLR, a G protein-coupled receptor) and receptor activity-modifying protein 1 (RAMP1). When CGRP binds, Gs proteins raise cAMP, which relaxes vascular smooth muscle and dilates meningeal vessels. It also sensitizes trigeminal neurons and helps transmit pain signals into the brainstem, and it promotes neurogenic inflammation. Rimegepant is a small-molecule competitive antagonist that binds the CLR/RAMP1 receptor and prevents CGRP from activating it. This blocks CGRP-driven vasodilation, trigeminal sensitization and pain signaling. Because it blocks a vasodilator rather than activating a vasoconstrictor receptor, it does not narrow blood vessels, which sets it apart from triptans. With regular preventive use, sustained receptor blockade lowers how often attacks occur.", "body": [["Head and meninges", "Blocks CGRP-driven vessel dilation and trigeminal pain signaling, relieving acute migraine and reducing attack frequency with regular use."], ["Heart and vessels", "Does not constrict blood vessels, so it is an option for people who cannot take triptans. Blood pressure rises have been reported."], ["Gut", "Nausea, abdominal pain and indigestion are the most common side effects."], ["Immune system", "Hypersensitivity reactions, including shortness of breath and rash, can occur and may be delayed by days."]], "journey": "Rimegepant comes as an orally disintegrating tablet placed on or under the tongue; it is swallowed and absorbed in the gut, with bioavailability about 64 percent. It peaks in about 1.5 hours, and a high-fat meal delays absorption. It is highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A4, with some CYP2C9, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors or inducers are avoided. Most of the dose leaves in feces, some in urine. The half-life is about 11 hours.", "sidefx": "Because rimegepant blocks CGRP rather than stimulating vessel receptors, it avoids the chest tightness and vasoconstriction of triptans. Nausea and indigestion are the usual complaints. CGRP also helps keep blood vessels open, so blocking it may contribute to blood pressure increases. Hypersensitivity reactions are immune reactions to the drug. Unlike earlier gepants, it has not shown a liver toxicity signal. There is no antidote; it has not been linked to medication overuse headache in trials.", "fun": "Rimegepant was the first migraine drug approved both to stop an attack in progress and to prevent future attacks.", "src": ["Nurtec ODT (rimegepant) FDA label", "Amara SG et al. Nature 1982", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "atogepant": {"origin": "Atogepant is a second-wave gepant, a small-molecule blocker of the CGRP receptor. The first gepants, such as telcagepant from Merck, showed that blocking CGRP could stop migraine, but liver enzyme rises with daily use halted their development. Researchers then designed new gepants with structures that did not carry the same liver signal. Atogepant was discovered at Merck and later developed by Allergan, which became part of AbbVie. Unlike rimegepant and ubrogepant, it was built as a daily pill purely for prevention. The FDA approved it as Qulipta in 2021 for preventing episodic migraine, and in 2023 for chronic migraine.", "timeline": [["1982", "CGRP is discovered."], ["2011", "Merck stops development of telcagepant because of liver safety concerns."], ["2021", "FDA approves atogepant as Qulipta for prevention of episodic migraine."], ["2023", "Approval is expanded to prevention of chronic migraine."]], "cell": "During migraine, trigeminal nerve fibers around meningeal blood vessels release CGRP. CGRP binds its receptor, a complex of calcitonin receptor-like receptor (CLR) and receptor activity-modifying protein 1 (RAMP1), which couples to Gs and raises cAMP. This dilates meningeal arteries, promotes neurogenic inflammation, sensitizes trigeminal neurons and helps pass pain signals into the brainstem. Atogepant is an oral competitive antagonist of the CLR/RAMP1 receptor. Taken every day, it keeps the receptor blocked so CGRP released during the early phase of an attack cannot activate it. Over weeks this lowers trigeminovascular activation and the number of monthly migraine days. Because it blocks a vasodilator pathway and does not stimulate vasoconstrictor receptors, it does not narrow blood vessels. CGRP also slows gut transit, and blocking it may help explain constipation.", "body": [["Head and meninges", "Ongoing CGRP receptor blockade lowers the number of migraine days each month."], ["Gut", "Nausea and constipation are the most common side effects, likely linked to CGRP's role in gut motility."], ["Appetite and weight", "Decreased appetite and modest weight loss have been reported."], ["Liver", "Uncommon liver enzyme rises were seen in trials, though without the clear toxicity seen with older gepants."], ["Heart and vessels", "Does not cause vasoconstriction, so it is an option for prevention in people with vascular risk."]], "journey": "Atogepant is taken by mouth once daily and absorbed quickly, peaking in about 1 to 2 hours. It is highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels and strong inducers lower them, and dose limits apply. It is also a substrate of OATP transporters. Most of the dose leaves in feces and some in urine. The half-life is about 11 hours, which fits once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Because atogepant blocks the CGRP receptor rather than activating vessel receptors, it avoids triptan-type vasoconstriction. CGRP helps move contents through the gut, so blocking it can cause constipation and nausea. Appetite loss and weight loss are less well explained. Hypersensitivity reactions are immune responses to the drug. Liver enzyme monitoring is not routinely required, but liver enzyme rises were noted. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The lessons from failed early gepants paid off: newer gepants were redesigned to avoid the liver problem that stopped the first ones.", "src": ["Qulipta (atogepant) FDA label", "Ailani J et al. N Engl J Med 2021 (ADVANCE trial)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "erenumab": {"origin": "After small-molecule gepants proved that blocking CGRP could treat migraine, companies turned to monoclonal antibodies, which last weeks and are not handled by the liver. Amgen developed erenumab, a fully human antibody, in partnership with Novartis. Unlike the other CGRP antibodies, which grab the CGRP peptide itself, erenumab was designed to block the receptor. The FDA approved it as Aimovig in May 2018, the first drug made specifically to prevent migraine and the first CGRP-pathway antibody. Its approval opened a new era of targeted migraine prevention.", "timeline": [["1982", "CGRP is discovered."], ["2018", "FDA approves erenumab as Aimovig, the first CGRP-pathway monoclonal antibody."]], "cell": "CGRP released from trigeminal nerve endings binds the CGRP receptor, made of calcitonin receptor-like receptor (CLR) and RAMP1, on vascular smooth muscle, trigeminal neurons and other cells. Gs coupling raises cAMP, dilating meningeal vessels and sensitizing pain pathways. Erenumab is a fully human IgG2 monoclonal antibody that binds the extracellular domain of the CLR/RAMP1 complex and blocks CGRP from binding. It is the only CGRP antibody that targets the receptor rather than the peptide. Because it is a large protein, little of it crosses the blood-brain barrier, so it acts mostly on peripheral sites such as the trigeminal ganglion and meningeal vessels, which lie outside the barrier. Blocking CGRP signaling reduces trigeminal activation and the frequency of migraine attacks. The receptor is also present in the gut and in blood vessels throughout the body, which explains constipation and blood pressure effects.", "body": [["Head and meninges", "Reduces the number of migraine days per month by blocking CGRP signaling in the trigeminovascular system."], ["Gut", "Constipation is common, and rarely severe constipation with complications has occurred, because CGRP helps gut motility."], ["Blood pressure", "New or worsened high blood pressure has been reported, consistent with CGRP's role as a vasodilator."], ["Skin", "Injection site pain, redness and itching are common."], ["Muscles", "Muscle spasms and cramps have been reported."], ["Immune system", "Hypersensitivity reactions, including rash and angioedema, can occur."]], "journey": "Erenumab is injected under the skin monthly with a prefilled pen or syringe. It is absorbed slowly through the lymph and peaks in about 6 days. It stays mostly in blood and extracellular fluid. At lower levels it is cleared by binding to the CGRP receptor and being taken into cells (target-mediated clearance), and at higher levels by normal protein breakdown. It is not removed by the liver or kidneys, so drug interactions are few. The effective half-life is about 28 days.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow CGRP's normal job as a vasodilator and gut regulator. Blocking it slows gut transit, causing constipation that is rarely severe. Removing a vasodilating signal may raise blood pressure. Injection site reactions come from the injection itself. There is no antidote, and the effect fades over weeks after the last dose.", "fun": "Erenumab is the only CGRP antibody that blocks the receptor; the others neutralize the CGRP peptide itself.", "src": ["Aimovig (erenumab-aooe) FDA label", "Goadsby PJ et al. N Engl J Med 2017 (STRIVE trial)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "galcanezumab": {"origin": "Galcanezumab was developed by Eli Lilly as one of the first wave of CGRP monoclonal antibodies. Unlike erenumab, which blocks the receptor, it was designed to bind the CGRP peptide itself and pull it out of action. The FDA approved it as Emgality in September 2018 for migraine prevention. In 2019 it became the first FDA-approved treatment to reduce the frequency of episodic cluster headache attacks, a painful condition with few options.", "timeline": [["1982", "CGRP is discovered."], ["2018", "FDA approves galcanezumab as Emgality for migraine prevention."], ["2019", "Galcanezumab is approved for episodic cluster headache."]], "cell": "CGRP exists in two forms, alpha-CGRP (made mostly in sensory neurons) and beta-CGRP (made mostly in gut nerves). During migraine and cluster attacks, trigeminal nerves release CGRP, which binds the CLR/RAMP1 receptor on meningeal vessels and nerve cells, raising cAMP and causing vasodilation, neurogenic inflammation and sensitization of pain pathways. Galcanezumab is a humanized IgG4 monoclonal antibody that binds both alpha and beta CGRP in the circulation and tissue fluids. Once bound, CGRP cannot reach and activate its receptor. Because it is a large antibody, it acts mostly outside the blood-brain barrier, at the trigeminal ganglion and meningeal vessels. Blocking the peptide lowers trigeminovascular activation, so migraine days and cluster attacks become less frequent. The IgG4 backbone was chosen because it causes little immune effector activity, so the antibody neutralizes CGRP without attacking cells.", "body": [["Head and meninges", "Lowers the number of migraine days and the weekly frequency of episodic cluster headache attacks."], ["Skin", "Injection site pain, redness and itching are the most common side effects."], ["Blood pressure", "Hypertension has been reported after approval, consistent with blocking a vasodilator."], ["Immune system", "Hypersensitivity reactions such as rash, hives and rarely anaphylaxis can occur, sometimes days after injection."]], "journey": "Galcanezumab is injected under the skin, starting with a loading dose and then monthly. It is absorbed slowly and peaks in about 5 days. It stays mainly in the blood and extracellular fluid. Like natural IgG, it is broken down by protein catabolism into peptides and amino acids, not by the liver or kidneys, so it has few drug interactions. Its half-life is about 27 days.", "sidefx": "Because it neutralizes CGRP, galcanezumab removes a vasodilator and gut-motility signal, which may explain blood pressure rises. Injection site reactions are common and mild. Hypersensitivity reactions come from the immune system responding to the antibody. There is no antidote, and its effects fade over months after stopping.", "fun": "Galcanezumab gave people with episodic cluster headache, sometimes called suicide headache because of its severity, their first FDA-approved preventive drug.", "src": ["Emgality (galcanezumab-gnlm) FDA label", "Goadsby PJ et al. N Engl J Med 2019 (cluster headache trial)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "onabotulinumtoxina": {"origin": "Botulinum toxin is made by the bacterium Clostridium botulinum. In the 1820s German physician Justinus Kerner described sausage poisoning and suggested the toxin might one day be used as a medicine. In 1895 Emile van Ermengem in Belgium identified the bacterium after a food poisoning outbreak. Edward Schantz later purified the toxin, and in the late 1970s San Francisco ophthalmologist Alan Scott injected tiny amounts to weaken eye muscles in people with strabismus. His product, Oculinum, was approved by the FDA in 1989 for strabismus and blepharospasm. Allergan acquired it and renamed it Botox. Approvals followed for many conditions, including frown lines in 2002 and chronic migraine prevention in 2010.", "timeline": [["1820s", "Justinus Kerner describes sausage poisoning (botulism) and imagines medical use of the toxin."], ["1895", "Emile van Ermengem identifies Clostridium botulinum."], ["1970s", "Alan Scott tests botulinum toxin injections for strabismus."], ["1989", "FDA approves the toxin (as Oculinum) for strabismus and blepharospasm."], ["2002", "Botox Cosmetic is approved for frown lines."], ["2010", "Approved for chronic migraine prevention."]], "cell": "Botulinum neurotoxin type A has a heavy chain and a light chain joined by a disulfide bond. The heavy chain binds gangliosides and the synaptic vesicle protein SV2 on cholinergic nerve terminals, and the toxin is taken in by endocytosis. In the acidic vesicle, the heavy chain forms a channel that lets the light chain escape into the cytosol. The light chain is a zinc-dependent protease that cuts SNAP-25, one of the SNARE proteins needed for vesicles to fuse with the nerve membrane. Without working SNAP-25, acetylcholine-filled vesicles cannot release their contents. At the neuromuscular junction this causes local flaccid weakness, and at autonomic nerves it reduces sweating, saliva and bladder contraction. In chronic migraine, the toxin is thought to block release of CGRP, substance P and glutamate from peripheral sensory nerves, which reduces sensitization. The effect fades over about 3 months as nerve terminals sprout new connections and SNAP-25 is replaced.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Injected muscles become weaker for about 3 months, which eases spasticity, dystonia, eyelid spasm and wrinkles."], ["Head and neck", "Injections around the head and neck reduce chronic migraine headache days over several treatment cycles, though neck pain and eyelid drooping can occur."], ["Bladder", "Relaxes the overactive detrusor muscle, reducing urgency and leaks, but can cause urinary retention and infections."], ["Sweat and salivary glands", "Blocks acetylcholine at sweat and salivary glands, reducing heavy sweating and drooling."], ["Throat and breathing", "If toxin spreads beyond the injection site, it can weaken swallowing and breathing muscles."], ["Eyes", "Eyelid drooping or reduced blinking can occur, which can expose the cornea."]], "journey": "OnabotulinumtoxinA is injected directly into muscles, the skin or the bladder wall. It binds nerve terminals near the injection site and is taken inside them, so at labeled amounts it cannot be measured in the blood. Muscle effects start within 1 to 3 days and peak in 1 to 2 weeks, while migraine benefit builds over weeks. Inside the nerve terminal the toxin is slowly degraded. The effect wears off over about 3 months as new nerve sprouts and SNAP-25 restore transmission, so injections are repeated. Units are specific to each botulinum product and cannot be swapped between brands.", "sidefx": "Side effects mostly come from blocking acetylcholine release in the wrong place. Weakness in nearby muscles causes eyelid drooping, neck weakness or trouble swallowing. The boxed warning covers distant spread of toxin effect, where toxin travels beyond the injection site and causes generalized weakness, swallowing and breathing problems, which can be fatal. Bladder injection can cause retention because the detrusor cannot contract well. Antibodies can form and reduce response over time. Botulism antitoxin can bind toxin that is still outside nerve cells but cannot reverse toxin already inside them.", "fun": "Botulinum toxin is among the most potent toxins known, yet carefully measured tiny amounts became one of the most widely used treatments in medicine.", "src": ["Botox (onabotulinumtoxinA) FDA label", "Scott AB. Ophthalmology 1980", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "rasagiline": {"origin": "Rasagiline was discovered at the Technion Israel Institute of Technology in Haifa by Moussa Youdim and John Finberg. They studied MAO-B inhibitors like selegiline, which was useful in Parkinson's disease but broke down into amphetamine-like products. In the 1970s and 1980s they developed rasagiline, a propargylamine that blocks MAO-B but is metabolized to aminoindan instead of amphetamines. Teva Pharmaceuticals developed it, and the FDA approved it as Azilect in 2006 for Parkinson's disease, both alone and with levodopa.", "timeline": [["1968", "Two forms of monoamine oxidase are described, later named MAO-A and MAO-B."], ["1970s", "Youdim and Finberg at the Technion begin work that leads to rasagiline."], ["2006", "FDA approves rasagiline as Azilect for Parkinson's disease."]], "cell": "In Parkinson's disease, dopamine-making neurons of the substantia nigra die, so less dopamine reaches the striatum. Dopamine that is released is broken down by monoamine oxidase type B (MAO-B), located on the outer mitochondrial membrane of glial cells (astrocytes) and some neurons. Rasagiline contains a propargyl group that forms a covalent bond with the FAD cofactor of MAO-B, permanently inactivating the enzyme. With MAO-B blocked, dopamine is broken down more slowly, so more stays in the synapse and keeps stimulating striatal receptors. This gives a modest improvement in movement on its own and lengthens the benefit of each levodopa dose. At labeled doses it is selective for MAO-B over MAO-A. MAO-A in the gut and liver still breaks down dietary tyramine, so serious tyramine reactions are unlikely, but selectivity can be lost at higher doses. Because inhibition is irreversible, the effect lasts until the body makes new enzyme, which takes weeks.", "body": [["Brain (movement)", "More dopamine in the striatum modestly improves slowness and stiffness and reduces off time when combined with levodopa."], ["Brain (mind)", "Can cause hallucinations, confusion, sudden sleep episodes and impulse control behaviors such as gambling, like other dopaminergic drugs."], ["Movement", "With levodopa, it can worsen dyskinesia because more dopamine is available."], ["Blood pressure", "Orthostatic hypotension can occur, and high blood pressure can occur with interacting drugs or large amounts of tyramine."], ["Joints and gut", "Headache, joint pain, indigestion and flu-like symptoms are common."], ["Skin", "Melanoma has been seen more often in Parkinson's disease, so skin checks are suggested."]], "journey": "Rasagiline is taken by mouth once daily and absorbed quickly, peaking in about an hour, with bioavailability around 36 percent. It crosses into the brain. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP1A2 to 1-aminoindan, which is not an amphetamine. Ciprofloxacin and other CYP1A2 inhibitors raise levels, and smoking can lower them. Metabolites leave mainly in urine. The plasma half-life is only about 3 hours, but the effect lasts for weeks because the enzyme is permanently disabled.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from extra dopamine, which can cause dyskinesia, hallucinations and sleep attacks. Serotonin syndrome can occur when it is combined with drugs that also raise serotonin, such as meperidine, tramadol, methadone, dextromethorphan or some antidepressants, because MAO also helps break down serotonin. Losing MAO-B selectivity at high levels lets tyramine build up, which can cause a hypertensive crisis. There is no antidote; the effect wears off only as new enzyme is made.", "fun": "Unlike selegiline, rasagiline is not broken down into methamphetamine-like metabolites, so it does not cause false positive amphetamine levels.", "src": ["Azilect (rasagiline) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "entacapone": {"origin": "Entacapone was developed by Orion Pharma in Finland in the 1980s. Researchers knew that even with carbidopa blocking one route, much of each levodopa dose was wasted when the enzyme COMT turned it into 3-O-methyldopa before it reached the brain. They designed nitrocatechol compounds that block COMT, and entacapone emerged as a selective, reversible option that works mainly outside the brain. It was approved in Europe in 1998 and by the FDA as Comtan in 1999. In 2003 the FDA approved Stalevo, which combines carbidopa, levodopa and entacapone in one tablet.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Levodopa is introduced for Parkinson's disease."], ["1980s", "Orion Pharma develops entacapone in Finland."], ["1999", "FDA approves entacapone as Comtan."], ["2003", "FDA approves Stalevo, combining carbidopa, levodopa and entacapone."]], "cell": "Levodopa is the precursor of dopamine and crosses the blood-brain barrier. In the body it is broken down by two enzymes: dopa decarboxylase, which carbidopa blocks outside the brain, and catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT), which adds a methyl group to make 3-O-methyldopa. Once decarboxylase is blocked, COMT becomes the main route of peripheral levodopa breakdown. Entacapone is a selective, reversible inhibitor of COMT that acts mainly in the gut wall and liver, since it crosses the blood-brain barrier poorly. With COMT blocked, levodopa lasts longer in the blood, its half-life and area under the curve increase, and more of it enters the brain. Inside the brain it is converted to dopamine, giving steadier stimulation of striatal dopamine receptors. Less 3-O-methyldopa also means less competition with levodopa for transport into the brain. Entacapone has no antiparkinson effect when taken without levodopa.", "body": [["Brain (movement)", "Longer levodopa action means more on time and less end-of-dose wearing off."], ["Movement", "More dopamine can cause or worsen dyskinesia, often requiring a lower levodopa dose."], ["Brain (mind)", "Hallucinations, confusion and sudden sleep episodes can occur from increased dopamine."], ["Gut", "Nausea, diarrhea and abdominal pain are common, and diarrhea can sometimes be severe or due to colitis."], ["Urine", "Urine often turns brownish-orange from the yellow drug and its metabolites, which is harmless."], ["Blood pressure", "Orthostatic hypotension can occur from increased dopaminergic activity."]], "journey": "Entacapone is taken by mouth with each levodopa dose and is absorbed quickly, peaking in about an hour, with bioavailability around 35 percent. It is highly bound to albumin and does not enter the brain much. The liver changes it into its cis-isomer and attaches glucuronic acid. About 90 percent leaves in feces through bile and the rest in urine. It has a short half-life, with an early phase under an hour and a terminal phase of about 2.4 hours, which is why it is taken with each levodopa dose.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from extra levodopa effect: dyskinesia, hallucinations, nausea and low blood pressure. Diarrhea is a separate COMT-inhibitor effect and can be due to colitis. The color change in urine comes from the yellow nitrocatechol molecule. Stopping it abruptly can cause a sudden drop in dopamine effect, with fever, rigidity and confusion resembling neuroleptic malignant syndrome. Unlike the related drug tolcapone, entacapone is not linked to serious liver injury. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Entacapone's sister drug tolcapone was nearly abandoned because of rare fatal liver injury, while entacapone, which works mostly outside the brain, avoided that problem.", "src": ["Comtan (entacapone) FDA label", "Stalevo FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "trihexyphenidyl": {"origin": "Anticholinergic treatment for Parkinson's disease goes back to the 1860s, when Jean-Martin Charcot and his student Leopold Ordenstein used belladonna alkaloids from the deadly nightshade plant to ease tremor and drooling. These natural alkaloids had many side effects, so in the 1940s chemists made synthetic central anticholinergics. Trihexyphenidyl was developed by Lederle Laboratories and introduced as Artane in 1949. It was one of the first synthetic drugs for parkinsonism and remained a main treatment until levodopa arrived in the late 1960s. Today it is used mainly for tremor in younger patients, for dystonia and for drug-induced movement problems.", "timeline": [["1867", "Ordenstein, a student of Charcot, describes belladonna alkaloids for Parkinson's disease."], ["1949", "Trihexyphenidyl is introduced as Artane."], ["1960s", "Levodopa becomes the main Parkinson's treatment, and anticholinergics take a supporting role."]], "cell": "In the striatum, dopamine from the substantia nigra and acetylcholine from local cholinergic interneurons normally balance each other. In Parkinson's disease, or when antipsychotics block dopamine D2 receptors, dopamine signaling drops and acetylcholine becomes relatively dominant. This cholinergic excess, acting on muscarinic receptors (mainly M1 and M4) on striatal projection neurons, contributes to tremor, rigidity and dystonia. Trihexyphenidyl is a competitive antagonist at muscarinic receptors, with relative preference for M1. It crosses the blood-brain barrier and blocks acetylcholine at striatal muscarinic receptors, restoring a better balance with the remaining dopamine. This reduces tremor and rigidity and relieves acute dystonia and drug-induced parkinsonism. It does little for slowness of movement. Blocking muscarinic receptors in the rest of the body causes the classic anticholinergic effects, and blocking central M1 receptors in the cortex and hippocampus causes memory problems and confusion.", "body": [["Brain (movement)", "Reduces tremor, rigidity and dystonia by lowering cholinergic activity in the striatum."], ["Brain (mind)", "Can cause confusion, memory loss, hallucinations and delirium, especially in older adults."], ["Eyes", "Dilates pupils and blurs near vision, and can trigger angle-closure glaucoma in people with narrow angles."], ["Mouth and skin", "Dries the mouth and reduces sweating, which can lead to overheating in hot weather."], ["Heart", "Blocks vagal slowing, raising heart rate."], ["Gut and bladder", "Slows the gut and relaxes the bladder, causing constipation and urinary retention, especially with an enlarged prostate."]], "journey": "Trihexyphenidyl is taken by mouth and absorbed well, peaking in about 1 to 1.5 hours. It crosses into the brain. It is broken down in the liver, mainly by hydroxylation, and leaves in urine. The half-life is about 3 to 4 hours, though a longer terminal phase has been reported, and effects last about 6 to 12 hours. Its full pharmacokinetics are not well characterized.", "sidefx": "All the main side effects come from blocking muscarinic receptors. In the brain this causes confusion, memory loss and delirium. In the eye it dilates the pupil and blocks focusing. Dry mouth, reduced sweating, fast heart rate, constipation and urinary retention are all peripheral muscarinic blockade. Reduced sweating can cause heat stroke. Overdose causes anticholinergic toxicity: hot, dry, flushed skin, dilated pupils, delirium and urinary retention. Physostigmine, a cholinesterase inhibitor that crosses into the brain, can reverse severe central anticholinergic toxicity in selected cases. Stopping suddenly can cause rebound worsening of symptoms.", "fun": "Some of the earliest Parkinson's treatments came from belladonna, the deadly nightshade plant whose name means beautiful lady because it was once used to dilate pupils for appearance.", "src": ["Trihexyphenidyl hydrochloride FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "apomorphine": {"origin": "Apomorphine was first made in 1869 by Augustus Matthiessen and C. R. A. Wright in London, who heated morphine with strong hydrochloric acid. The new compound had no opioid effect but caused powerful vomiting, and for decades it was used as an emetic. Late in the 19th century it was noted to ease movement problems, and in 1951 Robert Schwab reported it helped Parkinson's disease. It acts on dopamine receptors, and its short action made it a rescue drug for sudden off periods. The FDA approved it as Apokyn, an injectable pen, in 2004. A sublingual film, Kynmobi, was approved in 2020 but later discontinued, and a continuous subcutaneous infusion, Onapgo, was approved in 2025.", "timeline": [["1869", "Matthiessen and Wright make apomorphine by heating morphine with acid."], ["1951", "Robert Schwab reports apomorphine's benefit in Parkinson's disease."], ["2004", "FDA approves Apokyn injection for off episodes."], ["2020", "Kynmobi sublingual film is approved; it has since been discontinued."], ["2025", "FDA approves Onapgo continuous subcutaneous infusion."]], "cell": "Despite its name, apomorphine does not bind opioid receptors. Its rigid aporphine ring structure resembles dopamine, and it is a non-ergoline agonist at both D2-like (D2, D3, D4) and D1-like (D1, D5) dopamine receptors. In the caudate and putamen, D1 activation stimulates the direct pathway (Gs, more cAMP) and D2 activation inhibits the indirect pathway (Gi, less cAMP). Both effects release the brakes on the thalamus and cortex, allowing smooth voluntary movement. Because it acts directly on receptors, it does not need surviving dopamine neurons to convert it, so it works even when levodopa has worn off. Its effect is fast and powerful, similar to levodopa. Stimulating D2 receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone, which lies outside the blood-brain barrier, causes strong nausea and vomiting. It also has alpha-adrenergic and serotonergic activity and can lengthen the QT interval at higher levels.", "body": [["Brain (movement)", "Reverses off episodes within about 10 to 20 minutes, easing slowness, stiffness and tremor."], ["Vomiting center", "Strong D2 stimulation in the chemoreceptor trigger zone causes nausea and vomiting, so an antiemetic such as trimethobenzamide is often started first."], ["Blood pressure", "Lowers blood pressure and can cause dizziness and fainting, especially on standing."], ["Brain (mind)", "Can cause sleepiness, sudden sleep episodes, hallucinations and impulse control behaviors."], ["Heart", "Can prolong the QT interval at higher doses, raising risk of dangerous rhythms."], ["Skin", "Repeated injections can cause bruising and nodules at injection sites."]], "journey": "Apomorphine is poorly absorbed by mouth because the liver destroys most of it on first pass, so it is injected under the skin or given as a continuous infusion. After injection it is absorbed quickly, peaking in about 10 to 60 minutes and entering the brain readily. It is about 90 percent protein bound. It is broken down by sulfation, glucuronidation, N-demethylation and auto-oxidation, and the products leave in urine. Its half-life is short, about 40 minutes, so each dose lasts about 60 to 90 minutes.", "sidefx": "Nausea and vomiting come from D2 stimulation in the chemoreceptor trigger zone. Low blood pressure and fainting reflect dopamine and adrenergic effects on blood vessels and heart. Hallucinations, sleep attacks and impulse control problems are shared by dopamine agonists. Combining it with 5-HT3 antagonists such as ondansetron caused profound hypotension and loss of consciousness, so that combination is contraindicated. QT prolongation can lead to torsades de pointes. Dopamine antagonists blunt its effect but also worsen Parkinson's symptoms. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Apomorphine is made from morphine, but it has no opioid effect at all, a classic example of how a small chemical change can produce a completely different drug.", "src": ["Apokyn (apomorphine hydrochloride) FDA label", "Onapgo FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "pimavanserin": {"origin": "Up to half of people with Parkinson's disease eventually develop hallucinations or delusions. Most antipsychotics block dopamine D2 receptors, which worsens Parkinson's movement problems. Acadia Pharmaceuticals in San Diego set out to treat psychosis without touching dopamine receptors, by targeting the serotonin 5-HT2A receptor, which is linked to hallucinations. They developed pimavanserin, a selective 5-HT2A inverse agonist. The FDA approved it as Nuplazid in 2016, the first drug approved specifically for hallucinations and delusions in Parkinson's disease psychosis. It carries the same boxed warning as other antipsychotics about increased death in elderly people with dementia-related psychosis.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Acadia Pharmaceuticals develops pimavanserin as a selective 5-HT2A inverse agonist."], ["2014", "Pimavanserin receives FDA breakthrough therapy designation for Parkinson's disease psychosis."], ["2016", "FDA approves pimavanserin as Nuplazid."]], "cell": "Serotonin 5-HT2A receptors are Gq-coupled receptors found on cortical pyramidal neurons. Activating them raises phospholipase C activity, inositol triphosphate and calcium, increasing cortical excitability. Hallucinogens such as LSD and psilocybin act as 5-HT2A agonists, linking this receptor to visual hallucinations. In Parkinson's disease, changes in serotonin and 5-HT2A signaling in visual and limbic cortex appear to drive psychosis. Pimavanserin is an inverse agonist and antagonist at 5-HT2A receptors, with lower activity at 5-HT2C. As an inverse agonist, it not only blocks serotonin but also reduces the receptor's spontaneous background activity. This lowers abnormal cortical signaling and reduces hallucinations and delusions. It has no meaningful binding to dopamine D2, histamine, muscarinic or adrenergic receptors, so it does not worsen Parkinson's movement symptoms and causes little sedation or anticholinergic effect.", "body": [["Brain (perception)", "Over 2 to 6 weeks it reduces hallucinations and delusions in Parkinson's disease psychosis."], ["Brain (movement)", "Does not block dopamine, so movement symptoms are not made worse, unlike typical antipsychotics."], ["Heart", "Prolongs the QT interval and is avoided with other QT-prolonging drugs or in people with arrhythmia risk."], ["Legs and fluid", "Peripheral edema is one of the more common side effects."], ["Mind and balance", "Confusion and gait disturbance can occur."], ["Gut", "Nausea and constipation can occur and add to the gut slowing already common in Parkinson's disease."]], "journey": "Pimavanserin is taken by mouth once daily and absorbed well, peaking in about 6 hours. It is highly protein bound and enters the brain. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A4 and CYP3A5 to an active N-desmethyl metabolite. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels and inducers lower them. The parent half-life is about 57 hours and the active metabolite about 200 hours, so it takes weeks to reach steady state and to clear. Metabolites leave in feces and urine.", "sidefx": "QT prolongation comes from effects on cardiac potassium channels, which can lead to torsades de pointes, especially with other QT-prolonging drugs. Edema and confusion are common in this frail population. The boxed warning describes increased mortality in elderly people with dementia-related psychosis, shared by all antipsychotics. Because it avoids D2 receptors, it does not cause the parkinsonism or neuroleptic malignant syndrome risk of dopamine blockers. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Pimavanserin works on the same receptor that classic psychedelics stimulate, but it does the opposite, quieting that receptor to reduce hallucinations.", "src": ["Nuplazid (pimavanserin) FDA label", "Cummings J et al. Lancet 2014", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "tetrabenazine": {"origin": "Tetrabenazine was made at Hoffmann-La Roche in the 1950s as a possible antipsychotic, during the era when reserpine, a plant alkaloid that depletes monoamines, was being used for psychosis and high blood pressure. Tetrabenazine depleted monoamines like reserpine but worked faster and for a shorter time. It was not adopted for psychosis but was used outside the US for decades to treat abnormal movements. The FDA approved it as Xenazine in 2008 for chorea in Huntington's disease, the first drug approved in the US for that condition. It carries a boxed warning for depression and suicidality.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Roche develops tetrabenazine during research on reserpine-like drugs."], ["1970s", "Tetrabenazine is used outside the US for hyperkinetic movement disorders."], ["2008", "FDA approves tetrabenazine as Xenazine for Huntington's chorea."]], "cell": "Monoamines such as dopamine, serotonin, norepinephrine and histamine are made in the cytoplasm of neurons and then loaded into synaptic vesicles by vesicular monoamine transporter 2 (VMAT2), which uses a proton gradient. Tetrabenazine is a reversible VMAT2 inhibitor. Its active metabolites, alpha- and beta-dihydrotetrabenazine, carry most of the effect. With VMAT2 blocked, monoamines stay in the cytoplasm, where monoamine oxidase breaks them down. Vesicles hold less transmitter, so less is released with each nerve impulse. The strongest effect is on dopamine in the striatum. In Huntington's disease, loss of indirect pathway neurons makes the remaining dopamine signaling excessive, causing chorea. Lowering dopamine release reduces these involuntary movements. Unlike reserpine, tetrabenazine binds reversibly and is selective for VMAT2 in the brain over VMAT1 in peripheral tissues, so it causes less low blood pressure. Depleting serotonin and norepinephrine in mood circuits explains depression.", "body": [["Brain (movement)", "Reduces chorea in Huntington's disease and other hyperkinetic movements by lowering dopamine release in the striatum."], ["Brain (mood)", "Depletes serotonin and norepinephrine as well as dopamine, which can cause depression and suicidal thoughts."], ["Brain (arousal)", "Sedation, fatigue and insomnia are common."], ["Movement (too little)", "Too much dopamine loss can cause parkinsonism, akathisia and trouble swallowing."], ["Heart", "Can prolong the QT interval, which raises arrhythmia risk with other QT-prolonging drugs or in CYP2D6 poor metabolizers."], ["Blood pressure", "Can cause orthostatic hypotension and dizziness on standing, adding to fall risk."]], "journey": "Tetrabenazine is taken by mouth and absorbed well, but the liver quickly converts most of it on first pass by carbonyl reductase to alpha- and beta-dihydrotetrabenazine, which peak in about 1 to 1.5 hours. These active metabolites are then broken down by CYP2D6. Poor CYP2D6 metabolizers have much higher levels, so genotyping is required before higher doses, and CYP2D6 inhibitors such as fluoxetine and paroxetine raise levels. Metabolites leave mainly in urine. The active metabolites have half-lives of about 4 to 8 hours, so it is taken several times a day.", "sidefx": "Depression and suicidality come from depleting serotonin and norepinephrine and are the subject of a boxed warning. Too much dopamine depletion causes parkinsonism, akathisia and swallowing trouble, and rarely neuroleptic malignant syndrome. Sedation reflects reduced monoamine and histamine signaling. QT prolongation limits its use with other QT drugs. It is contraindicated with MAO inhibitors and reserpine. There is no antidote; side effects usually improve with dose reduction because the inhibition is reversible.", "fun": "Tetrabenazine was invented as an antipsychotic in the 1950s, yet it took about half a century to be approved in the US for a completely different purpose.", "src": ["Xenazine (tetrabenazine) FDA label", "Huntington Study Group. Neurology 2006", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "deutetrabenazine": {"origin": "Deutetrabenazine is tetrabenazine with a twist: some of its hydrogen atoms are replaced by deuterium, a heavier, stable form of hydrogen. A carbon-deuterium bond is harder to break than a carbon-hydrogen bond, so the enzymes that break down a drug work more slowly. Auspex Pharmaceuticals in California applied this idea to tetrabenazine, whose short half-life caused sharp peaks and troughs. Teva bought Auspex in 2015, and the FDA approved deutetrabenazine as Austedo in April 2017 for Huntington's chorea. It was the first deuterated drug approved by the FDA. Later that year it was also approved for tardive dyskinesia, and a once-daily extended-release form, Austedo XR, followed in 2023.", "timeline": [["2008", "Tetrabenazine, the parent drug, is approved in the US."], ["2015", "Teva acquires Auspex Pharmaceuticals, developer of deutetrabenazine."], ["2017", "FDA approves deutetrabenazine as Austedo for Huntington's chorea, the first deuterated drug."], ["2017", "Approval is added for tardive dyskinesia."], ["2023", "Austedo XR once-daily tablets are approved."]], "cell": "Deutetrabenazine is converted by carbonyl reductase to deuterated alpha- and beta-dihydrotetrabenazine, which reversibly inhibit vesicular monoamine transporter 2 (VMAT2) in central neurons. VMAT2 normally loads dopamine, serotonin, norepinephrine and histamine into synaptic vesicles. With it blocked, these monoamines remain in the cytoplasm and are broken down by monoamine oxidase, so less is released with each nerve impulse. The largest effect is on striatal dopamine. In Huntington's disease, loss of indirect pathway neurons leaves dopamine relatively too strong, causing chorea. In tardive dyskinesia, long-term dopamine D2 blockade by antipsychotics makes D2 receptors more numerous and sensitive, so normal dopamine release causes involuntary movements. Lowering dopamine release reduces both. The key difference from tetrabenazine is the deuterium on its two methoxy groups. CYP2D6 normally removes these groups, and deuterium slows that step, so the active metabolites last longer, peak lower and fluctuate less. That allows lower, less frequent dosing with fewer peak-related side effects.", "body": [["Brain (movement)", "Reduces chorea in Huntington's disease and involuntary movements of tardive dyskinesia by lowering striatal dopamine release."], ["Brain (mood)", "Depletion of serotonin and norepinephrine can cause depression and suicidal thoughts, especially in Huntington's disease."], ["Brain (arousal)", "Somnolence and fatigue are common, though often milder than with tetrabenazine."], ["Movement (too little)", "Too much dopamine depletion can cause parkinsonism and akathisia."], ["Heart", "Can prolong the QT interval, especially in CYP2D6 poor metabolizers or with strong CYP2D6 inhibitors."], ["Gut and mouth", "Diarrhea and dry mouth are among the common side effects."]], "journey": "Deutetrabenazine is taken by mouth with food. It is absorbed and quickly reduced to its deuterated active metabolites, which peak in about 3 to 4 hours. Deuterium slows their breakdown by CYP2D6, giving half-lives of about 9 to 10 hours, roughly double those of tetrabenazine's metabolites. Minor routes include CYP1A2 and CYP3A4. Metabolites leave in urine. The immediate-release form is taken twice daily and the extended-release form once daily. Strong CYP2D6 inhibitors and poor metabolizer status raise levels.", "sidefx": "Because it works the same way as tetrabenazine, it shares the boxed warning for depression and suicidality in Huntington's disease, caused by depletion of mood-related monoamines. Too much dopamine loss leads to parkinsonism, akathisia and rarely neuroleptic malignant syndrome. QT prolongation is a concern with other QT drugs. The smoother levels from deuteration tend to reduce peak-related sedation compared with tetrabenazine. It is contraindicated with MAO inhibitors, reserpine and other VMAT2 inhibitors. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Deutetrabenazine was the first FDA-approved drug built on the deuterium switch, swapping hydrogen for its heavier twin to slow metabolism without changing how the drug fits its target.", "src": ["Austedo (deutetrabenazine) FDA label", "Huntington Study Group. JAMA 2016 (First-HD trial)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "valbenazine": {"origin": "Valbenazine was developed by Neurocrine Biosciences in San Diego. Researchers knew that tetrabenazine is turned into four related dihydro metabolites, and that one of them, (+)-alpha-dihydrotetrabenazine, is the most potent and selective VMAT2 blocker. They attached the amino acid valine to that one metabolite to make a prodrug that is absorbed well and releases the active form slowly. In April 2017 the FDA approved it as Ingrezza, the first drug approved for tardive dyskinesia, a movement disorder caused by long-term use of antipsychotics and some other dopamine blockers. In 2023 it was also approved for chorea in Huntington's disease.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Neurocrine Biosciences develops valbenazine."], ["2017", "FDA approves valbenazine as Ingrezza, the first drug for tardive dyskinesia."], ["2023", "Valbenazine is approved for Huntington's chorea."], ["2024", "Ingrezza Sprinkle capsules are approved."]], "cell": "Valbenazine is the valine ester of (+)-alpha-dihydrotetrabenazine ([+]-alpha-HTBZ). After absorption, the ester is hydrolyzed and the active metabolite is released. [+]-alpha-HTBZ is a highly selective, reversible inhibitor of vesicular monoamine transporter 2 (VMAT2), with almost no binding to VMAT1 or to dopamine, serotonin or other receptors. VMAT2 normally pumps dopamine and other monoamines into synaptic vesicles. Blocking it means less dopamine is packaged and released from presynaptic terminals in the striatum. In tardive dyskinesia, years of D2 receptor blockade have increased the number and sensitivity of postsynaptic D2 receptors, so ordinary dopamine release triggers abnormal movements. Lowering dopamine release reduces these movements without directly blocking D2 receptors. The same principle reduces chorea in Huntington's disease. Using only the single most selective isomer, rather than the mixture made from tetrabenazine, allows once-daily dosing and avoids some off-target effects.", "body": [["Brain (movement)", "Reduces the involuntary face, tongue and limb movements of tardive dyskinesia and the chorea of Huntington's disease over a few weeks."], ["Brain (arousal)", "Sleepiness and fatigue are the most common side effects."], ["Heart", "Can prolong the QT interval, especially at higher exposure in CYP2D6 poor metabolizers or with strong CYP3A4 or CYP2D6 inhibitors."], ["Movement (too little)", "Parkinsonism and balance problems with falls can occur from reduced dopamine."], ["Mood", "In Huntington's disease it carries a boxed warning for depression and suicidality."], ["Skin and airways", "Rare angioedema and other hypersensitivity reactions have been reported."]], "journey": "Valbenazine is taken by mouth once daily and absorbed quickly, peaking in about 0.5 to 1 hour. It is then hydrolyzed to [+]-alpha-HTBZ, which peaks in about 4 to 8 hours. Valbenazine is over 99 percent protein bound, and the active metabolite about 64 percent. Both are broken down by CYP3A4/5 and CYP2D6 and leave as metabolites in urine and feces. Half-lives are about 15 to 22 hours, which supports once-daily dosing. Strong CYP3A4 inducers are avoided because they lower levels.", "sidefx": "Sleepiness reflects reduced monoamine signaling in arousal circuits. Lowering dopamine release can cause parkinsonism. QT prolongation becomes important when levels are high, such as in CYP2D6 poor metabolizers. Depression and suicidality are a particular risk in Huntington's disease, where they are already common. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is rare. It is not combined with MAO inhibitors or other VMAT2 inhibitors. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Of the four dihydro forms the body makes from tetrabenazine, valbenazine delivers only the single most selective one.", "src": ["Ingrezza (valbenazine) FDA label", "Hauser RA et al. Am J Psychiatry 2017 (KINECT 3)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "riluzole": {"origin": "Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS), described by Jean-Martin Charcot in 1869 and widely known in the US as Lou Gehrig's disease, had no treatment for over a century. In the 1980s researchers proposed that too much glutamate might help kill motor neurons. Riluzole, a benzothiazole developed by the French company Rhone-Poulenc (later Rhone-Poulenc Rorer), was known to reduce glutamate signaling. A 1994 trial led by Vincent Meininger and Gilbert Bensimon in France showed it modestly prolonged survival. The FDA approved it as Rilutek in 1995, making it the first drug approved for ALS. Liquid and film forms were later added for people who have trouble swallowing.", "timeline": [["1869", "Jean-Martin Charcot describes amyotrophic lateral sclerosis."], ["1994", "Bensimon and colleagues report that riluzole prolongs survival in ALS."], ["1995", "FDA approves riluzole as Rilutek, the first ALS drug."], ["2018", "Tiglutik oral suspension is approved."], ["2019", "Exservan oral film is approved."]], "cell": "In ALS, motor neurons in the brain and spinal cord degenerate. One leading theory is excitotoxicity: too much glutamate overstimulates receptors on motor neurons, letting in excess calcium that damages mitochondria and triggers cell death. Riluzole reduces glutamate signaling in several ways. It inactivates voltage-gated sodium channels, stabilizing them in the inactive state, which lowers the nerve firing that triggers glutamate release from presynaptic terminals. It also inhibits glutamate release more directly and noncompetitively blocks some postsynaptic excitatory amino acid receptor responses, including NMDA-receptor-mediated effects. Together these actions lower the glutamate load on motor neurons and are thought to slow their loss. The exact mechanism in ALS is not known, and the benefit is modest, extending survival or time to tracheostomy by about 2 to 3 months on average.", "body": [["Motor neurons", "Modestly slows the course of ALS, extending survival and time to tracheostomy by a few months on average, without restoring lost strength."], ["General", "Weakness and fatigue (asthenia) are among the most common side effects."], ["Liver", "Raises liver enzymes in many patients and rarely causes hepatitis, so liver tests are monitored."], ["Blood", "Can rarely cause neutropenia, which raises infection risk."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease has been reported rarely."], ["Gut and brain", "Nausea, abdominal pain and dizziness are common."]], "journey": "Riluzole is taken by mouth on an empty stomach, because a high-fat meal lowers absorption. It is well absorbed, with bioavailability about 60 percent, and peaks in about 1 to 1.5 hours. It is highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP1A2, then by glucuronidation. Smoking (which induces CYP1A2) lowers levels, while CYP1A2 inhibitors such as fluvoxamine raise them. Metabolites leave mainly in urine. The half-life is about 12 hours, so it is taken twice daily.", "sidefx": "Riluzole is generally tolerated, but the liver is the main concern because it is processed heavily by liver enzymes; enzyme rises are common and severe injury is rare. Neutropenia is rare and probably immune or idiosyncratic. Asthenia and dizziness may reflect its effects on nerve excitability. Interstitial lung disease is rare. There is no antidote; care is to stop the drug and support the patient.", "fun": "For over two decades riluzole was the only FDA-approved drug for ALS, until edaravone was approved in 2017.", "src": ["Rilutek (riluzole) FDA label", "Bensimon G et al. A controlled trial of riluzole in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis. N Engl J Med 1994", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "edaravone": {"origin": "Edaravone was developed in Japan by Mitsubishi Tanabe Pharma as a free radical scavenger to protect the brain after stroke. Oxygen free radicals form when blood flow returns to injured tissue, and edaravone was designed to neutralize them. It was approved in Japan in 2001 for acute ischemic stroke. Because oxidative stress is also thought to play a role in ALS, the company tested it in that disease. A Japanese trial in a selected group of patients with early ALS showed slower decline, and the FDA approved it as Radicava in 2017, the first new ALS drug since riluzole. An oral suspension, Radicava ORS, followed in 2022. Its benefit in broader groups of ALS patients has been debated.", "timeline": [["2001", "Edaravone is approved in Japan for acute ischemic stroke."], ["2015", "Edaravone is approved in Japan for ALS."], ["2017", "FDA approves edaravone IV as Radicava for ALS."], ["2022", "FDA approves Radicava ORS oral suspension."]], "cell": "Free radicals are molecules with an unpaired electron, such as the hydroxyl radical, peroxyl radicals and peroxynitrite. They steal electrons from lipids, proteins and DNA, causing chain reactions of damage. Cell membranes are especially vulnerable through lipid peroxidation. Motor neurons in ALS show signs of oxidative stress, and in some inherited forms, mutations in superoxide dismutase 1 (SOD1), an antioxidant enzyme, cause disease. Edaravone is a small pyrazolone molecule that gives up an electron to neutralize hydroxyl and peroxyl radicals and peroxynitrite, stopping lipid peroxidation chain reactions. It is both water and fat soluble, so it can protect both the inside and the membranes of cells. Reducing oxidative damage is thought to protect motor neurons and slow functional decline. The exact mechanism of benefit in ALS is not known.", "body": [["Motor neurons", "In selected patients with early ALS, it slowed the decline on the ALS functional rating scale (ALSFRS-R) over 6 months."], ["Skin", "Bruising (contusion), dermatitis and eczema are among the common side effects."], ["Gait", "Gait disturbance and headache were reported more often than with placebo."], ["Immune system", "Hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis, can occur."], ["Airways", "The IV and oral products contain sodium bisulfite, which can trigger sulfite reactions and asthma flares in sensitive people."], ["Kidneys", "Glycosuria (sugar in the urine) has been reported."]], "journey": "Edaravone IV is given as an infusion in cycles of daily doses followed by drug-free periods, and peaks at the end of infusion. The oral suspension is absorbed quickly, peaking in about 0.3 to 0.5 hours, and is taken on an empty stomach because food lowers absorption. It is about 92 percent bound to albumin. It is broken down mainly by glucuronidation and sulfation to inactive conjugates, not by CYP enzymes. Most of the dose leaves in urine as the glucuronide. The half-life is about 4.5 to 6 hours.", "sidefx": "Edaravone has few target-related side effects because it acts on free radicals rather than receptors. Bruising and gait disturbance were the most common in trials. Hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis, are immune responses to the drug. Sulfite reactions come from the sodium bisulfite in the formulation, which can cause wheezing and allergic-type symptoms, especially in people with asthma. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Edaravone was a stroke drug in Japan for more than a decade before it was approved for ALS in the US.", "src": ["Radicava and Radicava ORS (edaravone) FDA label", "Writing Group, Edaravone (MCI-186) ALS 19 Study Group. Lancet Neurology 2017", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "lecanemab": {"origin": "In 1906 German psychiatrist Alois Alzheimer described plaques and tangles in the brain of a woman with dementia. The plaques were later found to be made of amyloid beta. In 2001 Lars Lannfelt and colleagues in Uppsala, Sweden, studied a family with the Arctic mutation in the amyloid precursor protein and found that it made amyloid beta form soluble protofibrils, which are especially toxic. This led to an antibody aimed at protofibrils, developed by BioArctic and then by Eisai with Biogen. After the CLARITY AD trial showed slower decline, the FDA granted accelerated approval to lecanemab as Leqembi in January 2023 and traditional approval in July 2023. It was among the first drugs shown to slow Alzheimer's disease in a large trial. A subcutaneous autoinjector was approved in 2025.", "timeline": [["1906", "Alois Alzheimer describes plaques and tangles in a patient's brain."], ["2001", "Lannfelt's group describes the Arctic mutation that promotes amyloid protofibrils."], ["2022", "The CLARITY AD trial reports slower clinical decline with lecanemab."], ["2023", "FDA grants accelerated approval in January and traditional approval in July."], ["2025", "Subcutaneous Leqembi Iqlik is approved for maintenance dosing."]], "cell": "Amyloid beta is cut from amyloid precursor protein by beta- and gamma-secretase. In Alzheimer's disease it builds up and clumps, passing through soluble oligomers and protofibrils before forming insoluble fibrils that make up plaques. Soluble protofibrils are thought to be especially harmful to synapses. Lecanemab is a humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody that binds soluble amyloid beta protofibrils with high affinity and also binds insoluble fibrils. A small fraction of the antibody crosses the blood-brain barrier. Once bound, its Fc region engages Fc gamma receptors on microglia, the brain's immune cells, which take up and clear the amyloid. Over months, amyloid PET scans show plaque levels falling. Removing amyloid slowed decline on the CDR-SB scale by about 27 percent over 18 months in CLARITY AD. When the antibody and microglia attack amyloid in blood vessel walls (cerebral amyloid angiopathy), vessels can leak fluid or blood, causing amyloid-related imaging abnormalities (ARIA).", "body": [["Brain (cognition)", "Slows, but does not stop or reverse, decline in memory and daily function in early Alzheimer's disease."], ["Brain (imaging)", "Clears amyloid plaque on PET scans over months."], ["Brain blood vessels", "ARIA-E (swelling) and ARIA-H (microbleeds or superficial siderosis) can occur, usually early, and are more common in APOE4 carriers, especially homozygotes."], ["Brain (bleeding)", "Rarely, larger brain hemorrhages occur, and the risk is higher with anticoagulants or clot-busting drugs."], ["Infusion", "Infusion reactions with fever, chills, nausea or blood pressure changes are common, mainly with early doses."], ["Head", "Headache is common and can be a sign of ARIA."]], "journey": "Lecanemab is given by IV infusion every 2 weeks and, for maintenance, every 4 weeks or as a weekly subcutaneous injection. Levels peak at the end of the infusion. As a large antibody, it stays mostly in the blood, and only a small fraction enters the brain. It is broken down by normal protein catabolism, not by the liver or kidneys, so it has few drug interactions. The half-life is about 5 to 7 days. Plaque clearance builds over months.", "sidefx": "ARIA is the key risk. As the antibody pulls amyloid out of brain vessel walls, the vessels become leaky, causing swelling (ARIA-E) or small bleeds (ARIA-H). Most cases have no symptoms and are found on MRI, but some cause headache, confusion, visual changes or seizures. APOE4 carriers, especially those with two copies, have higher risk, which is why genetic testing is discussed. Anticoagulants and thrombolytics raise the risk of serious bleeding. Infusion reactions are immune responses to the antibody. There is no antidote; treatment is pausing the drug and supportive care, sometimes with steroids for severe ARIA.", "fun": "Lecanemab traces back to a single Swedish family whose rare Arctic mutation showed scientists which form of amyloid might be most toxic.", "src": ["Leqembi (lecanemab-irmb) FDA label", "van Dyck CH et al. Lecanemab in early Alzheimer's disease. N Engl J Med 2023", "Nilsberth C et al. Nat Neurosci 2001"]}, "donanemab": {"origin": "Donanemab was developed by Eli Lilly. Rather than targeting all forms of amyloid beta, Lilly scientists chose a modified form found only in established plaques: amyloid beta that has lost its first two amino acids and has a pyroglutamate ring at position 3, called N3pG. Targeting it lets the antibody focus on deposited plaque. In the TRAILBLAZER-ALZ 2 trial, it slowed decline in early Alzheimer's disease, and many patients cleared enough plaque to stop treatment. After an FDA advisory committee review, it was approved as Kisunla in July 2024. Its label allows stopping once amyloid PET scans show plaque clearance.", "timeline": [["1906", "Alois Alzheimer describes plaques and tangles."], ["2021", "TRAILBLAZER-ALZ phase 2 results are published."], ["2023", "TRAILBLAZER-ALZ 2 reports slower decline with donanemab."], ["2024", "FDA approves donanemab as Kisunla."], ["2025", "Label updated with a slower titration schedule to lower ARIA risk."]], "cell": "Amyloid beta deposited in plaques undergoes changes over time. One is the loss of its first two amino acids followed by cyclization of glutamate at position 3 into pyroglutamate, catalyzed by glutaminyl cyclase. This N-terminal pyroglutamate amyloid beta (N3pG) is found in deposited plaques and makes them more stable and prone to aggregate, but it is not found in soluble amyloid or the precursor protein. Donanemab is a humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody that binds the N3pG epitope. A small amount crosses into the brain, binds plaques, and its Fc region recruits microglia through Fc gamma receptors to engulf and clear the plaque. Plaque falls quickly on amyloid PET, and many patients reach amyloid-negative levels within a year. Lower amyloid is associated with slower decline on the iADRS and CDR-SB scales. As with other anti-amyloid antibodies, clearing amyloid from vessel walls can make vessels leak, causing ARIA.", "body": [["Brain (cognition)", "Slows, but does not stop, decline in memory and daily function in early Alzheimer's disease, with larger benefit in those with lower tau levels."], ["Brain (imaging)", "Rapidly clears amyloid plaque, often allowing treatment to stop once PET scans show clearance."], ["Brain blood vessels", "ARIA-E and ARIA-H are common, usually early, and more frequent in APOE4 carriers."], ["Brain (bleeding)", "Rarely causes large brain hemorrhages, with higher risk on blood thinners."], ["Infusion", "Infusion reactions and rarely anaphylaxis can occur."], ["Head", "Headache is common and can be an early sign of symptomatic ARIA, which is checked with MRI."]], "journey": "Donanemab is given by IV infusion every 4 weeks, starting with a gradual titration. Levels peak at the end of the infusion. As an antibody it stays mostly in blood, and only a small fraction reaches the brain. It is broken down by normal protein catabolism. The half-life is about 12 days. Because the target is deposited plaque, treatment can stop once plaque is cleared, and plaque returns only slowly afterward.", "sidefx": "ARIA is the main risk. When microglia attack amyloid in the walls of brain blood vessels, the vessels leak fluid (ARIA-E) or blood (ARIA-H). Most cases are found on MRI without symptoms, but some cause headache, confusion, seizures or rarely death. APOE4 homozygotes have the highest risk. A slower titration schedule was later shown to reduce ARIA-E. Anticoagulants raise bleeding risk. Infusion reactions and anaphylaxis are immune responses. There is no antidote; care is pausing the drug and supportive treatment.", "fun": "Donanemab was one of the first Alzheimer's drugs designed with a built-in finish line: stop when the plaque is gone.", "src": ["Kisunla (donanemab-azbt) FDA label", "Sims JR et al. Donanemab in early symptomatic Alzheimer disease. JAMA 2023", "Mintun MA et al. N Engl J Med 2021"]}, "interferon-beta-1a": {"origin": "Interferons were discovered in 1957 by Alick Isaacs and Jean Lindenmann in London, who found that virus-infected cells release a substance that interferes with viral growth in other cells. In the 1980s scientists tried interferon in multiple sclerosis. Interferon gamma made MS worse, but beta interferon reduced relapses. Interferon beta-1b (Betaseron), made in bacteria, became the first MS disease-modifying drug in 1993. Biogen then developed interferon beta-1a, made in Chinese hamster ovary cells so it is glycosylated and has the same amino acid sequence as natural human interferon beta. The FDA approved it as Avonex in 1996. Serono's Rebif followed in 2002, and Biogen's pegylated form, Plegridy, in 2014.", "timeline": [["1957", "Isaacs and Lindenmann discover interferon."], ["1993", "Interferon beta-1b becomes the first FDA-approved MS disease-modifying therapy."], ["1996", "FDA approves interferon beta-1a as Avonex."], ["2002", "Rebif (interferon beta-1a) is approved in the US."], ["2014", "Plegridy (peginterferon beta-1a) is approved."]], "cell": "In relapsing MS, autoreactive T cells and B cells cross the blood-brain barrier and attack myelin, causing inflammation, demyelination and nerve damage. Interferon beta-1a binds the type I interferon receptor, made of IFNAR1 and IFNAR2 subunits, on immune and other cells. This activates the kinases JAK1 and TYK2, which phosphorylate STAT1 and STAT2. These join IRF9 to form ISGF3, which enters the nucleus and switches on hundreds of interferon-stimulated genes. In MS, the net effects are immunomodulating. Antigen-presenting cells show less MHC class II and costimulatory molecules, so T-cell activation is weaker. Cytokines shift away from inflammatory Th1 and Th17 responses toward anti-inflammatory signals such as IL-10. Matrix metalloproteinase activity falls and adhesion molecules change, so fewer lymphocytes cross the blood-brain barrier. The result is fewer relapses and new MRI lesions and slower disability progression. The same signaling explains flu-like symptoms, since natural interferons cause the aches and fever of viral illness.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Reduces relapses and new MRI lesions and slows disability progression in relapsing MS."], ["Whole body", "Flu-like symptoms, including fever, chills, muscle aches and fatigue, are very common, especially after early injections."], ["Mood", "Can cause or worsen depression, including suicidal thoughts."], ["Liver", "Raises liver enzymes and rarely causes severe liver injury."], ["Blood", "Can lower white cells, red cells and platelets, so blood counts are monitored."], ["Thyroid and skin", "Can cause thyroid dysfunction. Injection site reactions are common, and rarely the skin breaks down (necrosis)."], ["Kidneys and heart", "Rarely causes thrombotic microangiopathy, and heart failure can worsen in people with existing heart disease."]], "journey": "Interferon beta-1a is given by injection: weekly into muscle (Avonex), three times a week under the skin (Rebif), or every 2 weeks as a pegylated form (Plegridy). After IM injection levels peak in about 3 to 15 hours, with a half-life of about 10 hours, while biologic effects last much longer. It is broken down mainly in the liver and kidneys by protein catabolism. Pegylation slows clearance and lets doses be spaced further apart. Some patients make neutralizing antibodies that lower its effect.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from the same type I interferon signaling the body uses during viral infections. Flu-like symptoms reflect cytokine and prostaglandin release, which is why starting low and taking acetaminophen or an NSAID helps. Depression may reflect interferon effects on brain serotonin and inflammatory pathways. Liver injury, cytopenias and thyroid problems are immune and direct effects of interferon signaling. Rare thrombotic microangiopathy involves endothelial injury. There is no antidote; effects resolve after stopping.", "fun": "Interferon was named because it interferes with viral replication, decades before it was used to calm the immune system in MS.", "src": ["Avonex (interferon beta-1a) FDA label", "Rebif FDA label", "Isaacs A, Lindenmann J. Virus interference. Proc R Soc Lond B 1957"]}, "glatiramer": {"origin": "Glatiramer acetate was discovered by accident at the Weizmann Institute of Science in Israel in the late 1960s. Michael Sela, Ruth Arnon and Dvora Teitelbaum made random polymers of four amino acids, alanine, glutamic acid, lysine and tyrosine, to mimic myelin basic protein. They expected the polymers to cause experimental autoimmune encephalomyelitis (EAE), the animal model of MS. Instead, one of them, called Copolymer 1, suppressed the disease. After years of development, Teva Pharmaceuticals brought it to market, and the FDA approved it as Copaxone in 1996. In 2015 Glatopa became the first generic version approved.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Sela, Arnon and Teitelbaum at the Weizmann Institute make Copolymer 1."], ["1971", "Copolymer 1 is reported to suppress experimental autoimmune encephalomyelitis."], ["1996", "FDA approves glatiramer acetate as Copaxone."], ["2014", "A three-times-weekly formulation is approved."], ["2015", "Glatopa is approved as the first generic glatiramer."]], "cell": "Glatiramer acetate is a mixture of random polypeptides made of four amino acids in fixed ratios, resembling myelin basic protein, a target of the immune attack in MS. After injection under the skin, it is taken up by antigen-presenting cells and binds MHC class II molecules. There it competes with myelin antigens for presentation to T cells, so fewer myelin-reactive T cells are activated. It also acts as an altered peptide ligand, steering T cells that recognize it toward a Th2 and regulatory phenotype rather than inflammatory Th1 and Th17. These glatiramer-specific T cells enter the central nervous system. When they meet myelin antigens that cross-react with glatiramer, they release anti-inflammatory cytokines such as IL-4, IL-10 and TGF-beta, quieting nearby inflammation (bystander suppression). Glatiramer also shifts monocytes toward an anti-inflammatory type and may promote neurotrophic factors such as BDNF. It does not broadly suppress the immune system, so infection risk is not raised. Its exact mechanism is not fully defined.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Reduces relapses and new gadolinium-enhancing MRI lesions in relapsing MS."], ["Immune system", "Shifts immune responses to a less inflammatory pattern without broad immunosuppression, so serious infections are not increased."], ["Skin", "Injection site redness, pain, hardening and itching are common, and lipoatrophy (loss of fat under the skin) can cause permanent dents; skin necrosis is rare."], ["Heart and chest", "An immediate post-injection reaction with flushing, chest tightness, palpitations, anxiety and shortness of breath can occur, usually lasting minutes."], ["Liver", "Rare cases of liver injury, including hepatitis, have been reported."], ["Immune reactions", "Anaphylaxis can occur, even years after starting treatment."]], "journey": "Glatiramer is injected under the skin, either daily or three times a week. Much of the dose is broken down into smaller peptides and amino acids at the injection site, and only a small part reaches the lymph and blood. Because of this, its pharmacokinetics in people are not well characterized. Its effect does not depend on steady blood levels but on immune changes that build over months.", "sidefx": "Injection site reactions and lipoatrophy come from local inflammation where the polymer is injected. The immediate post-injection reaction is thought to involve mast cell activation or vasodilation, and though frightening it usually resolves without treatment. Anaphylaxis, which can be life threatening, has been added as a warning and can occur at any time during treatment. Because glatiramer does not broadly suppress immunity, it does not raise infection risk like many other MS drugs. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Glatiramer was invented to cause an MS-like disease in animals, and it turned out to do the opposite.", "src": ["Copaxone (glatiramer acetate) FDA label", "Teitelbaum D et al. Eur J Immunol 1971", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "dimethyl-fumarate": {"origin": "Fumaric acid is a natural compound found in the citric acid cycle and in some plants. In 1959 Walter Schweckendiek, a German chemist who had psoriasis, tried fumaric acid esters on himself, believing psoriasis came from a metabolic problem. His idea grew into a German psoriasis treatment, and the mixture Fumaderm was approved in Germany in 1994. Doctors noted that it changed immune responses, and Biogen developed a purified form of one ester, dimethyl fumarate (first called BG-12), for multiple sclerosis. After two large trials, DEFINE and CONFIRM, the FDA approved it as Tecfidera in 2013. It became a popular oral MS drug, and generics became available in 2020.", "timeline": [["1959", "Walter Schweckendiek treats his own psoriasis with fumaric acid esters."], ["1994", "Fumaderm, a fumaric acid ester mixture, is approved for psoriasis in Germany."], ["2012", "The DEFINE and CONFIRM trials in MS are published."], ["2013", "FDA approves dimethyl fumarate as Tecfidera for relapsing MS."], ["2020", "Generic dimethyl fumarate becomes available in the US."]], "cell": "Dimethyl fumarate is a prodrug. Esterases in the gut, blood and tissues quickly convert it to monomethyl fumarate (MMF), the active form. MMF and dimethyl fumarate react with cysteine residues on KEAP1, a protein that normally holds the transcription factor Nrf2 in the cytoplasm and sends it for degradation. With KEAP1 modified, Nrf2 accumulates, moves to the nucleus and binds antioxidant response elements, turning on genes such as heme oxygenase-1 and NQO1 that protect cells against oxidative stress. MMF is also an agonist at the hydroxycarboxylic acid receptor 2 (HCA2, also called GPR109A), the same receptor niacin uses. HCA2 activation on immune cells shifts cytokines toward an anti-inflammatory profile and reduces immune cell entry into the central nervous system. Together these actions reduce inflammatory T-cell subsets and lower lymphocyte counts, which lowers relapse rates in MS. HCA2 activation on skin cells releases prostaglandins that cause flushing.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Reduces relapses and new MRI lesions in relapsing MS."], ["Skin", "Flushing with warmth, redness and itching is very common, especially early, and is caused by prostaglandin release through HCA2."], ["Gut", "Abdominal pain, diarrhea and nausea are common in the first weeks."], ["Blood", "Lowers lymphocyte counts on average by about 30 percent, and some people develop prolonged, severe lymphopenia."], ["Brain (infection)", "Severe, prolonged lymphopenia has been linked to rare progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML)."], ["Liver", "Can raise liver enzymes, and liver injury has been reported."]], "journey": "Dimethyl fumarate is taken by mouth twice a day as delayed-release capsules. It is broken down so quickly by esterases that it is barely detectable in blood; MMF is what circulates, peaking about 2 to 2.5 hours after a dose. MMF is not broken down by CYP enzymes. It enters the citric acid cycle and is breathed out as carbon dioxide, which accounts for most elimination, with small amounts in urine and feces. MMF's half-life is about an hour. Food reduces flushing a little but does not change total exposure.", "sidefx": "Flushing comes from HCA2 activation releasing prostaglandin D2 in skin, the same mechanism as niacin flushing, and taking it with food or aspirin beforehand can lessen it. Gut upset may come from local irritation by fumarate. Lymphopenia reflects the drug's immune action, and when it is severe and prolonged, it raises risk of opportunistic infections such as PML and herpes zoster, so blood counts are monitored. Anaphylaxis and angioedema are immune reactions. There is no antidote; lymphocytes recover slowly after stopping.", "fun": "This MS drug traces back to a chemist experimenting on his own psoriasis in 1950s Germany.", "src": ["Tecfidera (dimethyl fumarate) FDA label", "Gold R et al. N Engl J Med 2012 (DEFINE trial)", "Fox RJ et al. N Engl J Med 2012 (CONFIRM trial)"]}, "fingolimod": {"origin": "Fingolimod came from a fungus used in traditional Chinese medicine. Isaria sinclairii, related to the cordyceps fungi that grow on insects, produces myriocin, a potent immunosuppressant. In the 1990s Tetsuro Fujita at Kyoto University, working with Yoshitomi Pharmaceutical and Taito, modified myriocin's structure to make a simpler compound, FTY720, with less toxicity. Novartis developed it, first for kidney transplant rejection and then for multiple sclerosis. Researchers learned that it worked through sphingosine 1-phosphate receptors, trapping lymphocytes in lymph nodes. The FDA approved it as Gilenya in 2010, the first oral disease-modifying drug for MS. In 2018 it became the first MS drug approved for children 10 and older.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Fujita's group creates FTY720 by simplifying the fungal compound myriocin."], ["2002", "FTY720 is shown to act through sphingosine 1-phosphate receptors."], ["2010", "FDA approves fingolimod as Gilenya, the first oral MS disease-modifying therapy."], ["2018", "Fingolimod is approved for pediatric MS in patients 10 and older."]], "cell": "Lymphocytes leave lymph nodes by following a gradient of sphingosine 1-phosphate (S1P), which is high in blood and lymph and low inside nodes. They sense it through the S1P1 receptor, a G protein-coupled receptor. Fingolimod is a prodrug that is phosphorylated by sphingosine kinase 2 to fingolimod-phosphate, which mimics S1P and binds S1P1, S1P3, S1P4 and S1P5. On lymphocytes, it first activates S1P1 and then causes the receptor to be pulled inside the cell and degraded. Without S1P1 on their surface, naive and central memory T cells cannot sense the S1P exit signal and stay trapped in lymph nodes. This is called functional antagonism. Fewer autoreactive lymphocytes circulate and reach the brain, so there are fewer relapses and lesions. Effector memory T cells, which do not need to recirculate through nodes, are relatively spared, helping preserve some immune surveillance. On atrial heart cells, S1P1 and S1P3 activation opens GIRK potassium channels, which slows the heart rate and AV conduction after the first dose. S1P receptors in the retina and lungs explain macular edema and reduced lung function.", "body": [["Immune system", "Traps lymphocytes in lymph nodes, lowering blood lymphocyte counts to about 20 to 30 percent of baseline and reducing MS relapses."], ["Heart", "The first dose slows the heart rate and can cause AV block, so heart monitoring is done for the first several hours."], ["Eyes", "Macular edema can occur, usually in the first months, especially in people with diabetes or uveitis."], ["Infections", "Raises risk of herpes zoster, disseminated herpes, cryptococcal infection and rarely PML."], ["Lungs and vessels", "Can mildly lower lung function (FEV1) and raise blood pressure."], ["Brain (rebound)", "Stopping can cause severe rebound of MS activity as lymphocytes return."]], "journey": "Fingolimod is taken by mouth once daily and absorbed slowly, peaking in 12 to 16 hours, with high bioavailability. Sphingosine kinase phosphorylates it to the active form. It is over 99 percent protein bound and spreads widely into tissues, including the brain. CYP4F2 breaks it down, and inactive metabolites leave mainly in urine. The half-life is long, about 6 to 9 days, so it takes 1 to 2 months to reach steady state, and lymphocyte counts take 1 to 2 months to recover after stopping.", "sidefx": "Most side effects follow from where S1P receptors live. S1P1 and S1P3 activation on atrial cells opens GIRK channels, slowing heart rate and conduction after the first dose. Trapping lymphocytes in nodes lowers immune surveillance, raising risk of viral, fungal and opportunistic infections. S1P receptor effects on retinal vessels cause macular edema, and effects on airway smooth muscle reduce lung function. PRES has been reported rarely. Severe rebound disability can follow stopping. There is no antidote; accelerated removal is not practical, so effects fade over weeks.", "fun": "Fingolimod's ancestor, myriocin, comes from a fungus that grows on insect larvae, related to the cordyceps fungi made famous in nature documentaries.", "src": ["Gilenya (fingolimod) FDA label", "Brinkmann V et al. Nat Rev Drug Discov 2010", "Kappos L et al. N Engl J Med 2010 (FREEDOMS trial)"]}, "siponimod": {"origin": "Siponimod was developed by Novartis as a more selective successor to fingolimod. Fingolimod hits four S1P receptor subtypes, including S1P3, which was linked to some of its heart effects in animals, and it lasts for days, so effects fade slowly after stopping. Novartis chemists designed siponimod to bind only S1P1 and S1P5, to work without being phosphorylated, and to clear in about a week. In the EXPAND trial it slowed disability progression in secondary progressive MS. The FDA approved it as Mayzent in 2019 for relapsing forms of MS, including active secondary progressive disease. It was one of the first drugs whose label requires a genetic test of a drug-metabolizing enzyme, CYP2C9, before starting.", "timeline": [["2010", "Fingolimod, the first S1P receptor modulator, is approved."], ["2018", "EXPAND trial results in secondary progressive MS are published."], ["2019", "FDA approves siponimod as Mayzent."]], "cell": "Lymphocytes rely on the S1P1 receptor to sense the high sphingosine 1-phosphate (S1P) levels in lymph and blood and to leave lymph nodes. Siponimod is a selective modulator of S1P1 and S1P5. Unlike fingolimod, it is active as given and does not need phosphorylation. When it binds S1P1 on lymphocytes, the receptor is internalized and degraded, so the cells can no longer follow the S1P exit signal and remain in lymph nodes (functional antagonism). Fewer autoreactive T and B cells circulate and enter the central nervous system. S1P5 is found on oligodendrocytes, the myelin-making cells, and on some other brain cells. Because siponimod crosses into the brain, it may act directly there, and animal studies suggest possible effects on remyelination and microglia, though this is not proven in people. On atrial heart cells, S1P1 activation opens GIRK potassium channels and slows heart rate, which is why the first doses are titrated slowly. Its selectivity avoids S1P3 and S1P4.", "body": [["Immune system", "Keeps lymphocytes in lymph nodes, lowering blood lymphocyte counts and reducing MS activity."], ["Brain and spinal cord", "Slowed disability progression in active secondary progressive MS and reduces relapses and new lesions."], ["Heart", "First doses can lower heart rate and slow AV conduction, which a gradual titration helps reduce."], ["Eyes", "Macular edema can occur, especially in people with diabetes or uveitis."], ["Infections", "Raises risk of herpes zoster and other infections, and rarely PML or cryptococcal infection."], ["Blood pressure", "Can raise blood pressure over time, so blood pressure is checked during treatment."]], "journey": "Siponimod is taken by mouth once daily after a 5-day titration. It is well absorbed, with bioavailability about 84 percent, and peaks in about 4 hours. It is very highly protein bound and spreads into tissues, including the brain. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP2C9, with some CYP3A4. People with the CYP2C9*3/*3 genotype clear it very poorly, so it is contraindicated for them, and *1/*3 or *2/*3 carriers take a lower amount. Metabolites leave in feces. The half-life is about 30 hours, so lymphocytes recover within about 10 days of stopping in most people.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow from S1P receptor biology. S1P1 activation on atrial cells slows heart rate after the first doses. Trapping lymphocytes raises infection risk, including herpes zoster, and vaccination against varicella is checked before starting. Macular edema comes from S1P effects on retinal vessels. Hypertension and PRES are rare vascular effects. Strong rebound of MS activity can occur after stopping. Because CYP2C9 does most of the work, genetic poor metabolizers or drugs that block both CYP2C9 and CYP3A4 can cause high levels. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Siponimod's label requires CYP2C9 genotyping before the first dose, a rare example of routine genetic testing for a non-cancer drug.", "src": ["Mayzent (siponimod) FDA label", "Kappos L et al. Lancet 2018 (EXPAND trial)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "teriflunomide": {"origin": "Teriflunomide is the active metabolite of leflunomide, a rheumatoid arthritis drug developed by Hoechst (later Aventis) and approved by the FDA as Arava in 1998. Researchers learned that leflunomide is almost completely converted to teriflunomide in the body, and that teriflunomide blocks pyrimidine synthesis in activated lymphocytes. Sanofi, which absorbed Aventis, developed teriflunomide itself for multiple sclerosis. After the TEMSO trial showed fewer relapses, the FDA approved it as Aubagio in 2012 as an oral, once-daily MS therapy. Generics became available in 2023.", "timeline": [["1998", "FDA approves leflunomide (Arava), the parent drug, for rheumatoid arthritis."], ["2011", "TEMSO trial results in relapsing MS are published."], ["2012", "FDA approves teriflunomide as Aubagio for relapsing MS."], ["2023", "Generic teriflunomide becomes available in the US."]], "cell": "Cells make pyrimidines (the building blocks C, T and U of DNA and RNA) in two ways: de novo synthesis from scratch, and a salvage pathway that recycles existing bases. Resting cells rely mostly on salvage, but activated T and B lymphocytes that are rapidly dividing need large amounts and depend on de novo synthesis. Dihydroorotate dehydrogenase (DHODH), an enzyme on the inner mitochondrial membrane, catalyzes the fourth step of de novo synthesis, turning dihydroorotate into orotate. Teriflunomide reversibly inhibits DHODH. Activated lymphocytes run short of pyrimidines and stop in the G1 phase of the cell cycle, so their expansion is reduced. This is cytostatic rather than killing cells, and resting or memory cells using salvage are largely spared. Fewer activated autoreactive lymphocytes are available to attack myelin, so relapses and new MRI lesions decrease. Because rapidly dividing fetal cells also need de novo synthesis, the drug is highly teratogenic.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Reduces relapses and new MRI lesions and modestly slows disability progression in relapsing MS."], ["Immune system", "Limits expansion of activated lymphocytes and modestly lowers neutrophil and lymphocyte counts without broad immune depletion."], ["Liver", "Raises liver enzymes and can rarely cause severe liver injury, a boxed warning."], ["Pregnancy", "Causes birth defects in animal studies and is contraindicated in pregnancy, a boxed warning."], ["Hair and skin", "Hair thinning is common, usually in the first months, and serious skin reactions such as SJS/TEN or DRESS are rare."], ["Nerves and blood pressure", "Peripheral neuropathy and higher blood pressure can occur."]], "journey": "Teriflunomide is taken by mouth once daily and is well absorbed, peaking in 1 to 4 hours. It is over 99 percent protein bound. It is mainly broken down by hydrolysis, with minor oxidation, and goes through enterohepatic recirculation, being secreted in bile and reabsorbed from the gut. That recycling gives a very long half-life of about 18 to 19 days, and levels can persist for up to 2 years after stopping. It leaves in feces through bile and some in urine. An accelerated elimination procedure using cholestyramine or activated charcoal binds the drug in the gut and breaks the recycling loop, clearing it in about 11 days.", "sidefx": "Liver injury and teratogenicity are the two boxed warnings. Liver effects may come from drug processing in liver cells, and fetal harm comes from blocking pyrimidine synthesis needed by rapidly dividing embryonic cells. Modest neutropenia and lymphopenia reflect the effect on dividing blood cells. Hair thinning may reflect effects on rapidly dividing hair follicle cells. Because levels stay high for months, the accelerated elimination procedure with cholestyramine or activated charcoal serves as the reversal method for toxicity or planned pregnancy.", "fun": "Because teriflunomide can linger for up to 2 years, it is one of the few drugs with a standard procedure to wash it out of the body using a cholesterol-binding resin.", "src": ["Aubagio (teriflunomide) FDA label", "O'Connor P et al. N Engl J Med 2011 (TEMSO trial)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "ocrelizumab": {"origin": "For decades MS was viewed mainly as a T-cell disease. That changed after trials of rituximab, an anti-CD20 antibody developed for lymphoma, showed in 2008 that depleting B cells sharply reduced new MS lesions. Genentech and Roche then developed ocrelizumab, a humanized anti-CD20 antibody designed for MS. In 2017 the FDA approved it as Ocrevus for relapsing MS and for primary progressive MS, making it the first drug approved for the primary progressive form, based on the ORATORIO trial. A subcutaneous form combined with hyaluronidase, Ocrevus Zunovo, was approved in 2024.", "timeline": [["2008", "A phase 2 trial shows rituximab, a B-cell depleting antibody, reduces MS lesions."], ["2017", "OPERA and ORATORIO results are published, and FDA approves ocrelizumab as Ocrevus, the first drug for primary progressive MS."], ["2024", "Ocrevus Zunovo subcutaneous injection is approved."]], "cell": "CD20 is a membrane protein found on B cells from the pre-B stage through mature and memory B cells, but not on stem cells or most antibody-secreting plasma cells. Ocrelizumab is a humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody that binds an epitope on CD20. Once bound, it marks B cells for destruction mainly through antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity (natural killer cells and macrophages attack antibody-coated cells), with some complement-dependent cytotoxicity and antibody-dependent phagocytosis. Circulating B cells drop to very low levels within two weeks. In MS, B cells present myelin antigens to T cells, release inflammatory cytokines such as IL-6 and GM-CSF, and help form inflammatory aggregates in the meninges. Removing them reduces T-cell activation and inflammation in the central nervous system, lowering relapses and new lesions and slowing progression. Because plasma cells and stem cells are spared, existing antibody levels fall only gradually, and B cells can return from stem cells after treatment stops.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Reduces relapses and new MRI lesions in relapsing MS, and modestly slows disability progression in primary progressive MS."], ["Immune system", "Depletes circulating B cells for months, and immunoglobulin levels can fall over years of treatment."], ["Infusion", "Infusion reactions with itching, rash, throat irritation and flushing are common, mainly with the first infusion, so premedication is given."], ["Infections", "Raises risk of respiratory infections, skin infections and herpes, and rarely PML. Hepatitis B can reactivate."], ["Vaccines", "B-cell depletion blunts response to vaccines, so live vaccines are not given during treatment and others are ideally given before starting."], ["Gut", "Immune-mediated colitis has been reported."]], "journey": "Ocrelizumab is given by IV infusion, starting with two half infusions 2 weeks apart and then every 6 months, or by subcutaneous injection with hyaluronidase, which breaks down tissue matrix so the volume can be absorbed. Levels peak at the end of the IV infusion. As an antibody it stays mostly in blood. It is cleared by normal protein catabolism, not by the liver or kidneys. The half-life is about 26 days, but B-cell depletion lasts much longer, with median recovery about 72 weeks after the last infusion.", "sidefx": "Infusion reactions come from cytokine release as B cells are destroyed and from reactions to the antibody itself. Infections increase because B cells and, over time, antibody levels are reduced. Hepatitis B reactivation happens because B cells help keep HBV in check, so HBV testing comes before treatment. Hypogammaglobulinemia builds up with long treatment. There is no antidote; effects fade only as B cells regrow.", "fun": "Ocrelizumab helped change how MS is understood, showing that B cells, not only T cells, drive much of the disease.", "src": ["Ocrevus (ocrelizumab) FDA label", "Hauser SL et al. N Engl J Med 2017 (OPERA)", "Montalban X et al. N Engl J Med 2017 (ORATORIO)"]}, "dalfampridine": {"origin": "Dalfampridine is the chemical 4-aminopyridine, a simple compound that was long used as a bird repellent because it causes birds to behave erratically and alarm their flock. In the 1970s and 1980s scientists found that it blocks potassium channels and could restore conduction in demyelinated nerves in lab studies, and small studies in people with MS showed improved function. Acorda Therapeutics developed an extended-release form to keep levels steady and reduce seizure risk. The FDA approved it as Ampyra in 2010 to improve walking in people with MS. It does not change the course of the disease.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Studies show 4-aminopyridine can improve nerve conduction in MS."], ["2010", "FDA approves extended-release dalfampridine as Ampyra."], ["2018", "Generic dalfampridine becomes available in the US."]], "cell": "In healthy myelinated axons, voltage-gated potassium channels of the Kv1 family are clustered under the myelin near the nodes of Ranvier, out of the way. When myelin is lost in MS, these channels are exposed along the bare axon. During an action potential, potassium flows out through them and cuts the signal short, so the current leaks away and conduction fails before it reaches the next node. Dalfampridine blocks these exposed Kv channels from inside the axon. With less potassium leaving, the action potential lasts longer and is strong enough to travel across the demyelinated stretch, restoring conduction. It also prolongs action potentials at nerve terminals, which increases calcium entry and neurotransmitter release, including at the neuromuscular junction. This improves walking speed in about a third of people with MS. It does not repair myelin or change the disease course. Because it increases neuronal excitability, high levels can cause seizures.", "body": [["Nerves and walking", "Improves walking speed in responders by restoring conduction along demyelinated nerve fibers."], ["Brain", "Raises neuronal excitability and can cause seizures, especially at higher levels or in kidney impairment."], ["Bladder", "Urinary tract infections are among the most common side effects reported in trials."], ["Sleep and mood", "Insomnia, dizziness and headache are common."], ["Balance", "Balance problems and dizziness can occur."], ["Sensation", "Tingling (paresthesia) can occur from increased nerve excitability."]], "journey": "Dalfampridine is taken by mouth as an extended-release tablet twice daily, about 12 hours apart. It is almost completely absorbed and peaks in about 3 to 4 hours. It is barely protein bound and crosses into the brain. It is minimally metabolized and leaves mostly unchanged in urine, about 96 percent of a dose, so kidney function strongly affects levels. It is contraindicated in moderate to severe kidney impairment. The half-life is about 5 to 6.5 hours.", "sidefx": "Seizures are the main risk because blocking potassium channels makes neurons more excitable throughout the brain, and risk rises with higher levels. Since the kidneys clear the drug, kidney impairment raises levels and seizure risk. Insomnia, dizziness and tingling also come from increased nerve excitability. It is contraindicated in people with a history of seizures. There is no antidote; seizures are treated with standard antiseizure drugs and supportive care.", "fun": "Before it was an MS drug, 4-aminopyridine was used to scare birds away from crops.", "src": ["Ampyra (dalfampridine) FDA label", "Goodman AD et al. Lancet 2009", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "eculizumab": {"origin": "Eculizumab came from Alexion Pharmaceuticals, founded in 1992 by cardiologist Leonard Bell and Stephen Squinto, who wanted to block the complement system, a part of innate immunity that can damage the body's own cells. They designed a humanized antibody against complement protein C5. Its first success was in paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH), a rare disease in which red cells lack protective proteins and are destroyed by complement. The FDA approved it as Soliris in 2007. Approvals followed for atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome in 2011, generalized myasthenia gravis in 2017 and neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder in 2019. It was long one of the most expensive drugs in the world. Biosimilars were approved in 2024.", "timeline": [["1992", "Alexion Pharmaceuticals is founded."], ["2007", "FDA approves eculizumab as Soliris for PNH."], ["2011", "Approved for atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome."], ["2017", "Approved for AChR antibody-positive generalized myasthenia gravis."], ["2019", "Approved for AQP4 antibody-positive NMOSD."], ["2024", "First eculizumab biosimilars are approved."]], "cell": "The complement system is a cascade of plasma proteins. Classical, lectin and alternative pathways all converge on C3 and then C5. C5 convertase cuts C5 into C5a, a potent inflammatory signal (anaphylatoxin), and C5b, which joins C6, C7, C8 and many copies of C9 to form the membrane attack complex (C5b-9), a pore that punches holes in cell membranes. Eculizumab is a humanized IgG2/4 monoclonal antibody that binds C5 tightly and blocks its cleavage. No C5a or C5b is made, so the membrane attack complex cannot form, while earlier steps such as C3b opsonization remain. In PNH, red cells lack CD55 and CD59, which normally block complement, so blocking C5 stops intravascular hemolysis. In aHUS, it stops complement attack on vessel walls that causes thrombotic microangiopathy. In myasthenia gravis, it prevents antibody-triggered complement damage at the neuromuscular junction, and in NMOSD it blocks complement attack on astrocytes. Its hybrid IgG2/4 backbone reduces its ability to activate complement or immune cells itself.", "body": [["Blood", "Stops red cell destruction in PNH, reducing anemia, transfusions and dark urine, and lowers clotting risk."], ["Kidneys and vessels", "Halts thrombotic microangiopathy in aHUS, protecting the kidneys and other organs."], ["Muscles", "Reduces muscle weakness in AChR antibody-positive generalized myasthenia gravis by protecting the neuromuscular junction."], ["Optic nerve and spinal cord", "Prevents relapses in AQP4 antibody-positive NMOSD."], ["Infections", "Greatly raises risk of meningococcal and other Neisseria infections because the membrane attack complex is key to killing them."], ["General", "Headache, upper respiratory infections and infusion reactions are common."]], "journey": "Eculizumab is given by IV infusion, weekly at first and then every 2 weeks for maintenance. Levels peak at the end of infusion. It stays mostly in blood, where C5 circulates. It is broken down by protein catabolism and not cleared by the liver or kidneys. Plasma exchange and IVIG can remove or dilute it, so extra doses are given after those procedures. The half-life is about 11 days, and complement blockade lasts as long as levels stay above the threshold needed to bind all C5.", "sidefx": "Blocking the membrane attack complex removes the body's main defense against Neisseria bacteria, which is why the boxed warning covers life-threatening meningococcal infection. Meningococcal vaccination is given at least 2 weeks before starting unless urgent, and access is controlled by a REMS program. Disseminated gonococcal infection is also more likely. Stopping treatment can lead to rapid hemolysis in PNH or TMA in aHUS as complement activity returns. Infusion reactions are immune responses to the antibody. There is no antidote.", "fun": "People born without terminal complement proteins (C5 to C9) also get repeated Neisseria infections, which predicted eculizumab's main risk before it was ever given.", "src": ["Soliris (eculizumab) FDA label", "Hillmen P et al. N Engl J Med 2006 (TRIUMPH trial)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "nusinersen": {"origin": "Spinal muscular atrophy (SMA) is caused by loss of the SMN1 gene. People still have a backup gene, SMN2, but most of its messages skip exon 7 and make a short, unstable protein. In 2006 Ravindra Singh and colleagues at the University of Massachusetts found a silencer sequence in intron 7 of SMN2, called ISS-N1, that causes the skipping. Adrian Krainer at Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory, working with Isis Pharmaceuticals (now Ionis), designed antisense oligonucleotides to block this site. Biogen joined development, and dramatic results in infants led the FDA to approve nusinersen as Spinraza in December 2016, the first drug for SMA.", "timeline": [["1995", "Loss of SMN1 is identified as the cause of SMA."], ["2006", "Ravindra Singh's group identifies the ISS-N1 splicing silencer in SMN2."], ["2016", "FDA approves nusinersen as Spinraza, the first SMA treatment."], ["2017", "ENDEAR trial results in infants are published."]], "cell": "SMN protein (survival motor neuron) is essential for assembling the small nuclear ribonucleoproteins that make up the spliceosome, and motor neurons are especially sensitive to its loss. SMA results from deletion or mutation of SMN1. SMN2 is almost identical, but a single C-to-T change in exon 7 weakens its splicing, and a silencer sequence in intron 7, ISS-N1, recruits hnRNP proteins that cause exon 7 to be skipped. Most SMN2 messages therefore make a truncated protein that is quickly degraded. Nusinersen is an 18-base antisense oligonucleotide with 2'-O-(2-methoxyethyl) sugars and a phosphorothioate backbone, which resist breakdown and help it enter cells. It binds by base pairing to ISS-N1 in SMN2 pre-mRNA and blocks the hnRNP proteins from binding. With the silencer masked, exon 7 is included, and more full-length, functional SMN protein is made. More SMN protein supports survival of lower motor neurons in the spinal cord and brainstem, improving motor function and survival.", "body": [["Motor neurons", "More SMN protein helps lower motor neurons survive, leading to better motor milestones, such as head control and sitting, and longer survival."], ["Muscles and breathing", "Better motor nerve function improves muscle strength and can reduce the need for permanent ventilation in infants."], ["Lower back", "Repeated lumbar punctures can cause headache, back pain and vomiting."], ["Blood", "Thrombocytopenia and clotting abnormalities can occur, raising bleeding risk after lumbar puncture."], ["Kidneys", "Proteinuria and kidney toxicity, including glomerulonephritis, have been seen with antisense oligonucleotides, so urine protein is checked."], ["Lungs", "Lower respiratory infections are common in infants with SMA."]], "journey": "Nusinersen is injected into the spinal fluid by lumbar puncture, because it cannot cross the blood-brain barrier. It spreads through the cerebrospinal fluid into the spinal cord and brain tissue. Four loading doses are given over about two months, then maintenance every 4 months. Some moves from the CSF into the blood, peaking in plasma in about 2 to 6 hours. It is broken down slowly by exonucleases into shorter fragments, not by CYP enzymes, and these leave in urine. Its CSF half-life is very long, about 135 to 177 days.", "sidefx": "Many side effects come from the lumbar puncture itself, such as headache and back pain. Antisense oligonucleotides with phosphorothioate backbones can bind platelets and proteins, which may explain low platelets and clotting changes. They also build up in kidney tubule cells, where they can cause proteinuria and kidney injury. Hydrocephalus has been reported. Platelets, clotting tests and urine protein are checked before each dose. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Nusinersen works by covering up just 18 letters of a backup gene's message, turning a mostly broken gene into a working one.", "src": ["Spinraza (nusinersen) FDA label", "Finkel RS et al. N Engl J Med 2017 (ENDEAR)", "Singh NK et al. Mol Cell Biol 2006"]}, "risdiplam": {"origin": "After nusinersen showed that correcting SMN2 splicing could treat spinal muscular atrophy, researchers sought a pill that could do the same thing throughout the body without spinal injections. PTC Therapeutics and Roche, with support from the SMA Foundation, screened large libraries of small molecules for ones that boosted SMN2 exon 7 inclusion. They refined a hit into risdiplam. In trials it improved motor function in infants and older patients. The FDA approved it as Evrysdi in 2020, the first oral treatment for SMA. It is taken daily by mouth or feeding tube.", "timeline": [["1995", "SMN1 loss is identified as the cause of SMA."], ["2016", "Nusinersen becomes the first approved SMA drug."], ["2020", "FDA approves risdiplam as Evrysdi, the first oral SMA treatment."], ["2022", "Approval is extended to infants under 2 months."]], "cell": "In SMA, the SMN1 gene is lost, and the backup gene SMN2 mostly makes messages that skip exon 7, producing a short, unstable SMN protein. Risdiplam is a small molecule that binds SMN2 pre-mRNA at two sites: the 5' splice site at the end of exon 7 and an exonic splicing enhancer within exon 7. At the 5' splice site, it stabilizes the weak pairing between the pre-mRNA and the U1 small nuclear ribonucleoprotein, part of the spliceosome that recognizes where an exon ends. This makes exon 7 easier to recognize, so it is included in the mature message. More full-length SMN mRNA is made, and SMN protein roughly doubles. Unlike nusinersen, which stays in the central nervous system, risdiplam is taken by mouth and spreads throughout the body, raising SMN in motor neurons as well as muscle and other tissues. Higher SMN supports lower motor neuron survival and improves motor function. It is selective for SMN2 over most other genes, though at high levels it can affect splicing of some other genes, including FOXM1, linked to cell-cycle effects in animal studies.", "body": [["Motor neurons", "Raises SMN protein in motor neurons, improving motor milestones such as sitting without support, and survival without permanent ventilation."], ["Muscles and other tissues", "Raises SMN in muscle and other organs because it spreads through the whole body."], ["Skin and mouth", "Rash and mouth ulcers are common side effects."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is one of the most common side effects reported in people with later-onset SMA."], ["Infections", "Fever and upper respiratory infections or pneumonia are common, especially in infantile-onset SMA."], ["Reproduction", "Animal studies showed effects on male fertility and harm to the fetus, so contraception is used and fertility effects are discussed."]], "journey": "Risdiplam is taken by mouth once daily as a liquid (or tablet), or through a feeding tube. It is absorbed and peaks in 1 to 4 hours. It is about 89 percent bound to albumin and spreads widely into the brain, spinal cord, muscles and other tissues. The liver breaks it down mainly by flavin monooxygenases 1 and 3 and CYP3A. About half leaves in feces and about a quarter in urine. Its half-life is about 50 hours. It can inhibit MATE transporters, raising levels of drugs such as metformin.", "sidefx": "Most common side effects, like fever, diarrhea, rash and mouth ulcers, do not have a fully explained mechanism. Off-target splicing effects at high exposure are thought to underlie effects seen in animal studies on the retina, skin, gut lining and male fertility, and the risk of fetal harm. Infections reflect the underlying weakness of SMA. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Risdiplam was one of the first approved small-molecule drugs designed to bind messenger RNA and change how it is spliced.", "src": ["Evrysdi (risdiplam) FDA label", "Baranello G et al. N Engl J Med 2021 (FIREFISH)", "Ratni H et al. J Med Chem 2018"]}, "modafinil": {"origin": "Modafinil came from Lafon Laboratories in France. In the 1970s Lafon chemists made adrafinil, a compound that kept animals awake, and found that it worked through its metabolite, modafinil. French neuroscientist Michel Jouvet studied modafinil in the 1980s and found it promoted wakefulness without the jitteriness of amphetamines. It was approved in France and then licensed to Cephalon in the US. The FDA approved it as Provigil in 1998 for narcolepsy, and later for obstructive sleep apnea and shift work disorder. Its R-enantiomer, armodafinil (Nuvigil), was approved in 2007. Modafinil is Schedule IV.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Lafon Laboratories develops adrafinil, the parent of modafinil."], ["1980s", "Michel Jouvet studies modafinil as a wakefulness-promoting agent."], ["1998", "FDA approves modafinil as Provigil for narcolepsy."], ["2004", "Approval extends to obstructive sleep apnea and shift work disorder."], ["2007", "Armodafinil, the R-enantiomer, is approved as Nuvigil."]], "cell": "Modafinil's main target appears to be the dopamine transporter (DAT), the protein that pulls dopamine back into presynaptic neurons. Modafinil binds DAT and blocks reuptake, raising extracellular dopamine in the striatum and other areas. It is a weak inhibitor compared with cocaine and does not cause the large monoamine release that amphetamines do. Higher dopamine signaling promotes wakefulness, and DAT knockout mice do not respond to modafinil, supporting this mechanism. Downstream, modafinil increases activity of orexin (hypocretin) neurons in the lateral hypothalamus and histamine neurons in the tuberomammillary nucleus, both key wake-promoting systems. It also raises norepinephrine, serotonin and glutamate signaling in some regions and lowers GABA release. Together these shifts boost alertness and reduce excessive sleepiness. It is a racemic mixture, and the R-enantiomer (armodafinil) lasts longer.", "body": [["Brain (wakefulness)", "Promotes alertness and reduces excessive daytime sleepiness in narcolepsy, sleep apnea and shift work disorder."], ["Brain (mood and sleep)", "Can cause anxiety, nervousness, insomnia and, rarely, psychiatric symptoms such as mania or psychosis."], ["Heart and vessels", "Can mildly raise heart rate and blood pressure, and can cause palpitations and chest pain."], ["Head", "Headache is the most common side effect."], ["Skin", "Rarely causes serious rashes such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome or DRESS, more often in children."], ["Liver enzymes", "Induces CYP3A4, lowering levels of hormonal contraceptives, and inhibits CYP2C19, raising levels of drugs like some antidepressants and phenytoin."]], "journey": "Modafinil is taken by mouth and absorbed well, peaking in 2 to 4 hours. Food can delay the peak but not the total amount absorbed. It is about 60 percent protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly by amide hydrolysis, with some CYP3A4 metabolism, and less than 10 percent leaves unchanged in urine. The half-life is about 15 hours, so the effect lasts through the day. It induces CYP3A4 over time and inhibits CYP2C19, which explains many interactions.", "sidefx": "Headache, nervousness and insomnia come from its wake-promoting effect on dopamine, orexin and histamine systems. Mild heart rate and blood pressure rises reflect increased catecholamine signaling, so it is used carefully in heart disease. Serious rashes are immune reactions. Its effect on DAT gives it some abuse potential, though less than amphetamines, hence Schedule IV. CYP3A4 induction can make hormonal contraceptives fail. There is no antidote; overdose care is supportive.", "fun": "Modafinil was studied by the French military and others as a way to keep people alert during long missions.", "src": ["Provigil (modafinil) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "suvorexant": {"origin": "In 1998 two research teams independently discovered a pair of neuropeptides made in the lateral hypothalamus. Luis de Lecea and J. Gregor Sutcliffe called them hypocretins, and Takeshi Sakurai and Masashi Yanagisawa called them orexins. In 1999, studies showed that mice lacking orexin and narcoleptic dogs with a broken orexin receptor gene had narcolepsy, proving orexin is a key wake signal. Merck reasoned that blocking orexin receptors could help people fall and stay asleep without the broad brain depression of GABA drugs. It developed suvorexant, which the FDA approved as Belsomra in 2014, the first dual orexin receptor antagonist (DORA).", "timeline": [["1998", "Orexins (hypocretins) are discovered by two independent research groups."], ["1999", "Loss of orexin signaling is linked to narcolepsy in mice and dogs."], ["2014", "FDA approves suvorexant as Belsomra, the first orexin receptor antagonist."], ["2020", "Suvorexant label adds data in insomnia with mild to moderate Alzheimer's disease."]], "cell": "Orexin-producing neurons in the lateral hypothalamus fire mainly during wakefulness. Orexin A and orexin B act on two G protein-coupled receptors, OX1R and OX2R, located on neurons of arousal systems: noradrenergic neurons of the locus coeruleus, histaminergic neurons of the tuberomammillary nucleus, serotonergic raphe neurons, dopaminergic neurons and cholinergic basal forebrain neurons. Orexin stimulation keeps these systems active and stabilizes wakefulness. Suvorexant is a reversible competitive antagonist at both OX1R and OX2R. By blocking orexin, it turns down the wake-promoting drive from these arousal centers, making it easier to fall asleep and stay asleep. This approach differs from benzodiazepines and Z-drugs, which enhance GABA-A inhibition throughout the brain. Because suvorexant only removes a wake signal, it causes less effect on breathing and fewer memory and balance problems, though next-day sleepiness can still occur. Turning down orexin too far can mimic narcolepsy features, such as sleep paralysis and hypnagogic hallucinations.", "body": [["Brain (sleep)", "Shortens time to fall asleep and reduces waking during the night by blocking the orexin wake signal."], ["Brain (next day)", "Can cause next-day drowsiness and impaired driving, especially at higher doses or after less than a full night's sleep."], ["Brain (narcolepsy-like)", "Sleep paralysis, hypnagogic hallucinations and rarely mild cataplexy-like weakness can occur because orexin is reduced."], ["Behavior", "Complex sleep behaviors, such as sleep driving, and worsening depression or suicidal thoughts are rare."], ["Breathing", "In studies of mild to moderate sleep apnea or COPD, it had little effect on breathing."], ["General", "Headache, dizziness, abnormal dreams and dry mouth can occur."]], "journey": "Suvorexant is taken by mouth within 30 minutes of bedtime and peaks in about 2 hours, but a high-fat meal delays the peak and slows onset. It is over 99 percent protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A4, with a little CYP2C19, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors are avoided and inducers reduce its effect. Metabolites leave mainly in feces and some in urine. The half-life is about 12 hours, which explains the possibility of next-morning effects.", "sidefx": "Next-day drowsiness comes from orexin blockade lasting into the morning because of its 12-hour half-life. Sleep paralysis and hallucinations at sleep onset reflect reduced orexin, mirroring what happens in narcolepsy. That is also why suvorexant is contraindicated in narcolepsy. Complex sleep behaviors and depression are rare. It is Schedule IV, with low but present abuse potential. There is no specific antidote; overdose care is supportive.", "fun": "The two names for the same peptides, orexin and hypocretin, reflect a tie: two labs discovered them independently in the same year.", "src": ["Belsomra (suvorexant) FDA label", "Sakurai T et al. Cell 1998", "de Lecea L et al. PNAS 1998"]}, "triazolam": {"origin": "Triazolam was developed by Upjohn as part of the triazolobenzodiazepines, which add a triazole ring to the benzodiazepine structure; the same family includes alprazolam. The triazole ring made it very potent and very short acting. The FDA approved it as Halcion in 1982 for insomnia, and it became one of the most prescribed sleeping pills. Within a decade it drew controversy over reports of amnesia, confusion and behavioral changes. It was withdrawn in the United Kingdom in 1991 and in some other countries, while the FDA kept it on the market with stronger warnings and an emphasis on short courses.", "timeline": [["1955", "Leo Sternbach makes the first benzodiazepine at Roche."], ["1970s", "Upjohn develops triazolobenzodiazepines including triazolam."], ["1982", "FDA approves triazolam as Halcion for insomnia."], ["1991", "Triazolam is withdrawn in the United Kingdom over safety concerns."], ["2020", "FDA adds a boxed warning on abuse, dependence and withdrawal to all benzodiazepines."]], "cell": "Triazolam binds the benzodiazepine site on GABA-A receptors, located between an alpha subunit and the gamma2 subunit. It is a positive allosteric modulator: it does not open the chloride channel itself but increases GABA's affinity for its site and raises how often the channel opens. More chloride flows into neurons, making them more negative and harder to fire. Receptors containing the alpha1 subunit, which are plentiful in the cortex and thalamus, mediate the sedative and hypnotic effect, helping people fall asleep quickly. Alpha1 effects in the hippocampus also cause anterograde amnesia, where new memories are not formed while the drug is active. Alpha2 and alpha3 receptors mediate anxiety relief and muscle relaxation. Because GABA must be present for it to work, it is much safer alone than barbiturates, but combining it with alcohol or opioids, which depress the brain and breathing through other pathways, can be dangerous.", "body": [["Brain (sleep)", "Shortens the time to fall asleep. Its short half-life limits morning hangover but can cause early-morning awakening and rebound insomnia."], ["Brain (memory)", "Causes anterograde amnesia, so people may not remember events after taking it, including complex sleep behaviors."], ["Brain (behavior)", "Paradoxical agitation, confusion and complex sleep behaviors, such as sleepwalking and sleep driving, can occur."], ["Balance", "Dizziness and ataxia increase the risk of falls, especially in older adults."], ["Breathing", "Alone it mildly depresses breathing; with opioids or alcohol it can cause life-threatening respiratory depression."], ["Dependence", "Regular use leads to tolerance and dependence, and abrupt stopping can cause withdrawal, including seizures."]], "journey": "Triazolam is taken by mouth at bedtime and absorbed quickly, with sleep onset in about 15 to 30 minutes and a peak in about 2 hours. It is about 89 percent protein bound. The liver breaks it down by CYP3A4 to alpha-hydroxytriazolam and other products, which are glucuronidated and leave in urine. Its half-life is short, about 1.5 to 5.5 hours. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors, such as ketoconazole, itraconazole and some HIV protease inhibitors, greatly raise levels and are contraindicated. Grapefruit juice also raises levels.", "sidefx": "Sedation, amnesia and poor coordination come from enhanced GABA-A inhibition in the cortex, hippocampus and cerebellum. Its short action means drug levels fall during the night, which can cause rebound insomnia and next-day anxiety. Complex sleep behaviors happen because the drug causes amnesia while parts of the brain remain active. Combining it with opioids or alcohol can stop breathing, the reason for the boxed warning. Long-term use causes dependence. Flumazenil can reverse benzodiazepine sedation but may cause seizures in dependent patients, so it is used with caution.", "fun": "Triazolam is so short acting that it was also used by some travelers to sleep on long flights, though this is not an approved use.", "src": ["Halcion (triazolam) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "FDA Drug Safety Communication on benzodiazepines, 2020"]}, "carisoprodol": {"origin": "Carisoprodol grew out of the 1950s search for calming drugs. At Wallace Laboratories in New Jersey, the pharmacologist Frank Berger and chemist B. J. Ludwig had already created meprobamate (Miltown), one of the first blockbuster tranquilizers. They then modified the meprobamate molecule by adding an isopropyl group, hoping for a drug that relaxed muscles with less sedation. The result was carisoprodol, introduced as Soma in 1959. It turned out that the liver converts much of carisoprodol back into meprobamate, which explains its sedative and habit-forming effects. Misuse grew over the following decades, and the DEA placed carisoprodol in federal Schedule IV in 2012.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Frank Berger and colleagues at Wallace Laboratories develop meprobamate."], ["1959", "Carisoprodol is introduced in the US as Soma."], ["2012", "The DEA places carisoprodol in federal Schedule IV."]], "cell": "Carisoprodol does not act on muscle fibers. Its exact mechanism is not fully defined, but both carisoprodol and its metabolite meprobamate act on GABA-A receptors in a barbiturate-like way. They bind a site separate from the benzodiazepine site, increase how long the chloride channel stays open when GABA binds, and at higher levels can open the channel directly. More chloride entering neurons hyperpolarizes them. In the spinal cord this dampens polysynaptic reflex pathways through interneurons, and in the brainstem reticular formation it reduces arousal. The felt muscle relaxation is closely tied to this general sedation rather than any direct effect on muscle.", "body": [["Brain", "Enhanced GABA-A inhibition causes drowsiness, dizziness and a relaxed, sometimes euphoric feeling, which is the basis of its misuse potential."], ["Spinal cord", "Polysynaptic reflex arcs that maintain muscle spasm are dampened, so a strained back can feel less tight and painful."], ["Breathing", "Alone it rarely stops breathing, but combined with opioids, benzodiazepines or alcohol the added GABA-A depression of the brainstem can cause dangerous respiratory depression."], ["Heart and vessels", "Tachycardia and postural hypotension can occur, partly from central depressant effects on vascular tone."], ["Whole person", "Rare first-dose idiosyncratic reactions include profound weakness, transient quadriplegia, ataxia and confusion, which usually resolve within hours."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth, carisoprodol is absorbed quickly and starts working in about half an hour. In the liver, CYP2C19 removes part of the molecule to make meprobamate. Carisoprodol itself has a short half-life of about two hours, but meprobamate lasts about ten hours, so the sedative effect outlasts the parent drug. People who are CYP2C19 poor metabolizers have more carisoprodol and less meprobamate. Metabolites leave mainly in the urine.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness and impaired driving come directly from enhanced GABA-A inhibition. Because meprobamate acts like an older barbiturate-type sedative, regular use can cause tolerance and physical dependence, and stopping suddenly after heavy use can cause withdrawal with anxiety, tremor and seizures. In overdose, CNS and respiratory depression are deepest when other depressants are on board, which is why the mix with an opioid and a benzodiazepine is called the holy trinity. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Carisoprodol shares its brand name, Soma, with the fictional happiness drug in Aldous Huxley's 1932 novel Brave New World.", "src": ["Soma (carisoprodol) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "DEA Federal Register final rule placing carisoprodol in Schedule IV, 2011"]}, "metaxalone": {"origin": "Metaxalone is a synthetic oxazolidinone, a small ring-shaped molecule made by chemists rather than taken from nature. It was developed by the US company A. H. Robins and approved in the United States in 1962 as Skelaxin, at a time when several centrally acting muscle relaxants were being introduced for back and muscle pain. Unlike carisoprodol, it is not turned into a habit-forming sedative and is not a controlled substance. It has remained in use for decades largely because it tends to cause less drowsiness than other drugs in its class, even though its exact mechanism has never been worked out.", "timeline": [["1962", "Metaxalone is approved in the US as Skelaxin."], ["2000s", "Generic metaxalone tablets become available."]], "cell": "The mechanism of metaxalone has not been established. It has no direct effect on skeletal muscle fibers, the motor end plate or nerve fibers, so it does not work like dantrolene or neuromuscular blockers. Its effects are attributed to general depression of the central nervous system. In animal studies this shows up as reduced polysynaptic reflex activity in the spinal cord, the multi-neuron loops through interneurons that keep a muscle in spasm. Less firing in these loops means lower motor neuron drive to the injured muscle, along with mild sedation that also changes how pain and tightness are felt.", "body": [["Brain", "Mild CNS depression causes drowsiness, dizziness, headache or nervousness, usually less than other muscle relaxants."], ["Spinal cord", "Polysynaptic reflexes that maintain spasm are dampened, easing muscle tightness after strains and sprains."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and stomach upset are common, and taking it with food increases how much is absorbed."], ["Blood", "Rarely, it has been linked to hemolytic anemia and leukopenia, so unexplained infection or anemia raises concern."], ["Liver", "Jaundice has been reported, and the label advises caution and periodic liver tests in people with liver damage."]], "journey": "Metaxalone is taken by mouth. Absorption is variable and rises substantially when it is taken with food, especially a high-fat meal. Peak levels come about three hours after a dose. It is broken down in the liver by several cytochrome P450 enzymes, including CYP1A2, CYP2D6, CYP2E1 and CYP3A4, and the metabolites are excreted in the urine. The half-life is about nine hours but varies widely between people.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness, dizziness and impaired driving reflect its general CNS depressant action, and these add to alcohol and other sedatives. Rare serotonin syndrome has been reported when it is combined with serotonergic drugs. Hemolytic anemia, leukopenia and jaundice are rare idiosyncratic reactions, which is why liver and blood monitoring are mentioned in the label for people at risk. Hypersensitivity and anaphylactoid reactions are rare. There is no antidote; overdose care is supportive.", "src": ["Skelaxin (metaxalone) FDA label", "LiverTox: Metaxalone (NIH)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "orphenadrine": {"origin": "Orphenadrine is a close chemical cousin of diphenhydramine, the antihistamine created in the 1940s. Adding a single methyl group to one of diphenhydramine's rings produced orphenadrine, which kept the antihistamine and strong anticholinergic properties. In the 1950s it was marketed in Europe as Disipal for Parkinson disease, where its anticholinergic effect helped reduce tremor and rigidity, similar to other drugs of that era. It was later sold in the US as Norflex for painful muscle spasm, both as tablets and as an injection. The Norflex brand has since been discontinued, but generic orphenadrine citrate remains available.", "timeline": [["1940s", "Diphenhydramine, the parent structure of orphenadrine, is developed in the US."], ["1950s", "Orphenadrine is introduced, first used mainly for Parkinson disease."], ["1960s", "Orphenadrine is used in the US as Norflex for muscle spasm."]], "cell": "Orphenadrine has several actions and its muscle-relaxing mechanism is not fully established. It is a competitive antagonist at muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, an H1 histamine receptor antagonist of the ethanolamine type like diphenhydramine, and an NMDA glutamate receptor antagonist. It does not relax skeletal muscle directly. Its effect is thought to come from actions in the brainstem and higher centers that reduce motor drive and pain perception. NMDA blockade may dampen central pain sensitization. Muscarinic blockade in the basal ganglia explains its old use in parkinsonism, and blockade of peripheral muscarinic receptors explains dry mouth, fast heart rate and urinary retention.", "body": [["Brain", "H1 and muscarinic blockade cause drowsiness and dizziness; in older adults the anticholinergic effect can cause confusion or hallucinations."], ["Muscles and pain", "Central effects reduce the discomfort of acute muscle spasm, often as an add-on to rest and physical therapy."], ["Heart", "Blocking vagal M2 receptors on the sinus node speeds the heart, causing tachycardia and palpitations."], ["Eyes", "Blocking M3 receptors on the iris and ciliary muscle dilates the pupil and blurs near vision, which can worsen narrow-angle glaucoma."], ["Bladder and gut", "Less muscarinic tone on bladder and gut muscle causes urinary hesitancy, retention and constipation."], ["Mouth", "Saliva production falls, causing dry mouth."]], "journey": "Orphenadrine is well absorbed from the gut, and an extended-release tablet spreads the dose over about twelve hours. It can also be given by injection. The liver breaks it down mainly by N-demethylation into several metabolites, and these leave mostly in the urine, with a small amount unchanged. The half-life is long, roughly fourteen hours, so effects and side effects can build with repeated doses.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are classic anticholinergic effects from muscarinic blockade: dry mouth, blurred vision, fast pulse, constipation and urinary retention, plus confusion in older adults. H1 blockade adds drowsiness. In overdose, a full anticholinergic toxidrome can develop with delirium, hyperthermia, seizures and arrhythmias. Physostigmine can reverse severe central anticholinergic toxicity in selected cases under expert care. Anaphylaxis and aplastic anemia are very rare.", "src": ["Orphenadrine citrate extended-release FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "fluvoxamine": {"origin": "Fluvoxamine was one of the first selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors to reach patients. It was developed in the 1970s by the Dutch company Duphar, later part of Solvay, as researchers looked for antidepressants that blocked serotonin reuptake without the heart and anticholinergic problems of tricyclics. It was launched in Switzerland in 1983, several years before fluoxetine reached the US. In the United States, Solvay instead sought approval for obsessive-compulsive disorder, and the FDA approved it as Luvox in 1994. The Luvox brands have since been discontinued, but generic fluvoxamine remains in use, especially for OCD.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Fluvoxamine is developed at Duphar in the Netherlands."], ["1983", "Fluvoxamine is launched in Switzerland, among the first SSRIs marketed."], ["1994", "FDA approves Luvox for obsessive-compulsive disorder."], ["2020", "Fluvoxamine is studied as a possible COVID-19 treatment because of its sigma-1 activity."]], "cell": "Fluvoxamine binds the serotonin transporter (SERT, SLC6A4) on presynaptic serotonin neurons and blocks the reuptake of serotonin from the synapse. Serotonin therefore stays longer in the synaptic cleft and activates postsynaptic receptors more. At first, extra serotonin also stimulates 5-HT1A autoreceptors on the raphe cell bodies, which slows neuron firing. Over several weeks these autoreceptors desensitize and other 5-HT receptors downregulate, so net serotonin signaling rises in circuits linking the orbitofrontal cortex, striatum and thalamus that are overactive in OCD. Fluvoxamine is also a potent agonist at the sigma-1 receptor, an endoplasmic reticulum chaperone protein, and has little affinity for muscarinic, histamine or adrenergic receptors.", "body": [["Brain", "Over weeks, stronger serotonin signaling reduces obsessions and compulsions and can improve mood and anxiety."], ["Gut", "Gut cells hold most of the body's serotonin; more serotonin acting on 5-HT3 and 5-HT4 receptors causes nausea, diarrhea and stomach upset, especially early."], ["Sleep", "Fluvoxamine can cause either sleepiness or insomnia, reflecting serotonin's role in sleep and arousal circuits."], ["Blood", "Platelets need SERT to take up serotonin for clotting; blocking it lowers platelet serotonin and raises bleeding risk, especially with NSAIDs or anticoagulants."], ["Sexual function", "Serotonin dampens sexual response, so delayed orgasm and reduced libido are common."], ["Kidneys and sodium", "SSRIs can trigger excess ADH release (SIADH), causing low sodium, especially in older adults."]], "journey": "Fluvoxamine is well absorbed by mouth and peaks within several hours. It is about three quarters bound to plasma proteins. The liver metabolizes it extensively by oxidative demethylation, with CYP2D6 involved, into inactive products excreted in the urine. Its kinetics are nonlinear, so levels rise more than expected as the dose goes up. The half-life is about fifteen hours. Fluvoxamine is itself a potent inhibitor of CYP1A2 and CYP2C19 and also inhibits CYP3A4 and CYP2C9, which is why it raises levels of many other drugs.", "sidefx": "Nausea, sleep changes and sexual dysfunction come from more serotonin acting in the gut, sleep centers and spinal reflexes. Bleeding risk reflects lower platelet serotonin. Combining it with other serotonergic drugs or MAOIs can cause serotonin syndrome. Its strong enzyme inhibition causes dangerous interactions: it raises tizanidine (severe low blood pressure), theophylline (seizures), and thioridazine or pimozide (QT prolongation). Stopping abruptly can cause discontinuation symptoms. There is no specific antidote; serotonin syndrome is treated supportively, sometimes with cyproheptadine.", "fun": "In 2020 a small trial found fluvoxamine might help keep COVID-19 from worsening, possibly through sigma-1 effects on inflammation, but later large trials gave mixed results and it was never authorized for COVID-19.", "src": ["Fluvoxamine maleate FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology"]}, "vilazodone": {"origin": "Vilazodone was discovered at the German company Merck KGaA in Darmstadt, where chemists set out to combine two proven ideas in one molecule: blocking serotonin reuptake like an SSRI and partially stimulating 5-HT1A receptors like buspirone. The thinking was that direct 5-HT1A stimulation might speed up or strengthen the antidepressant effect. The drug was later licensed and developed by the US company Clinical Data, and the FDA approved it as Viibryd for major depressive disorder in January 2011. Whether it actually works faster than SSRIs has not been clearly shown.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Vilazodone is discovered at Merck KGaA in Germany."], ["2011", "FDA approves Viibryd for major depressive disorder in adults."]], "cell": "Vilazodone has two actions. First, it binds and blocks the serotonin transporter (SERT), so serotonin released into the synapse is cleared more slowly and acts longer on postsynaptic receptors. Second, it is a partial agonist at serotonin 5-HT1A receptors. With an SSRI alone, extra serotonin first stimulates 5-HT1A autoreceptors on raphe neurons, which slows their firing until the autoreceptors desensitize over weeks. A 5-HT1A partial agonist may help push that desensitization along while also stimulating postsynaptic 5-HT1A receptors in the hippocampus and cortex, which are linked to mood and anxiety regulation. The combined effect raises serotonin signaling, and the antidepressant benefit appears over weeks.", "body": [["Brain", "Stronger serotonin signaling and 5-HT1A stimulation gradually improve depressed mood, sleep, appetite and anxiety."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea and vomiting are the most common side effects, from serotonin acting on gut receptors."], ["Sexual function", "Rates of sexual side effects were lower than some SSRIs in trials, but they still occur."], ["Blood", "Blocking platelet serotonin uptake reduces platelet aggregation and can increase bleeding."], ["Sodium balance", "Low sodium from SIADH can occur, especially in older adults or with diuretics."]], "journey": "Vilazodone must be taken with food. Taken fasting, about half as much is absorbed, so the label directs dosing with a meal. Peak levels come about four to five hours after a dose. It is highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels and inducers lower them. Only a small amount leaves the body unchanged in urine and feces. The half-life is about twenty-five hours, which supports once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Gut side effects, especially diarrhea, reflect serotonin receptors throughout the intestine. Insomnia and dizziness come from serotonergic effects on arousal circuits. Like all serotonergic antidepressants it carries a boxed warning for suicidal thoughts in young people, and it can trigger mania in bipolar disorder. Combined with MAOIs or other serotonergic drugs it can cause serotonin syndrome. Bleeding risk and hyponatremia follow the same logic as SSRIs. Abrupt stopping can cause discontinuation symptoms. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Viibryd (vilazodone) FDA label", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "vortioxetine": {"origin": "Vortioxetine was discovered by the Danish company Lundbeck, which has a long history in antidepressants, and was developed together with Takeda. Lundbeck chemists aimed for a multimodal drug: one that blocks serotonin reuptake and also acts directly on several serotonin receptor subtypes, rather than a pure SSRI. The FDA approved it in 2013 as Brintellix. The name was often confused with the blood thinner Brilinta (ticagrelor), causing prescribing errors, so in 2016 it was renamed Trintellix in the US.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Lundbeck researchers discover vortioxetine."], ["2013", "FDA approves Brintellix for major depressive disorder."], ["2016", "The US brand is renamed Trintellix to avoid confusion with Brilinta."]], "cell": "Vortioxetine blocks the serotonin transporter (SERT), raising synaptic serotonin. It also acts directly at several receptors: it is an antagonist at 5-HT3, 5-HT7 and 5-HT1D receptors, a partial agonist at 5-HT1B receptors and an agonist at 5-HT1A receptors. 5-HT3 receptors sit on GABA interneurons that restrain other neurons; blocking them removes that brake, which in animal studies increases release of norepinephrine, dopamine, acetylcholine and histamine in the prefrontal cortex and hippocampus. 5-HT1A agonism can speed desensitization of raphe autoreceptors. The net result is broad modulation of several neurotransmitter systems, with an antidepressant effect over weeks and possible benefit on some measures of thinking speed.", "body": [["Brain", "Over weeks mood improves; trials suggested benefits on processing speed in depression, linked to its receptor actions in cortex."], ["Gut", "Nausea is the most common side effect, from extra serotonin, while 5-HT3 blockade may partly limit it; constipation and vomiting also occur."], ["Sexual function", "Sexual dysfunction occurs and becomes more common at higher doses."], ["Skin", "Itching (pruritus) is reported more often than with many antidepressants."], ["Blood", "Like SSRIs, it reduces platelet serotonin and can increase bleeding risk."], ["Sodium balance", "Low sodium from SIADH can occur, especially in older adults."]], "journey": "Vortioxetine is well absorbed by mouth, with or without food, and peaks seven to eleven hours after a dose. It is about ninety-eight percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver oxidizes it, mainly by CYP2D6, then conjugates it with glucuronic acid, producing inactive metabolites that leave in urine and feces. Its half-life is very long, about sixty-six hours, so steady state takes about two weeks. CYP2D6 poor metabolizers and people taking strong CYP2D6 inhibitors such as bupropion reach higher levels.", "sidefx": "Nausea comes from serotonin acting on gut and brainstem receptors and tends to fade over the first weeks. Sexual dysfunction and bleeding risk follow from raised serotonin, as with SSRIs. It carries the antidepressant boxed warning for suicidal thoughts in young people and can activate mania. Combined with MAOIs or other serotonergic drugs it can cause serotonin syndrome. Its long half-life makes discontinuation symptoms milder than with short-acting drugs. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Trintellix (vortioxetine) FDA label", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology", "FDA drug safety communication on Brintellix and Brilinta name confusion, 2016"]}, "milnacipran": {"origin": "Milnacipran was developed by the French company Pierre Fabre and has been used as an antidepressant in France and many other countries since the 1990s. In the United States it took a different path. Researchers were interested in drugs that boost norepinephrine because noradrenergic pathways descending from the brainstem help suppress pain signals in the spinal cord. Milnacipran, which leans toward norepinephrine reuptake, was tested in fibromyalgia, and in 2009 the FDA approved it as Savella for that condition. It is not approved for depression in the US.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Milnacipran is marketed as an antidepressant in France and elsewhere."], ["2009", "FDA approves Savella for the management of fibromyalgia."]], "cell": "Milnacipran inhibits both the norepinephrine transporter (NET) and the serotonin transporter (SERT), with about threefold greater potency at NET in laboratory tests. Blocking these transporters keeps norepinephrine and serotonin in synapses longer. In the spinal cord dorsal horn, descending fibers from the locus coeruleus and raphe nuclei release norepinephrine and serotonin. Norepinephrine acts on alpha-2 receptors on pain neurons, reducing release of excitatory transmitters such as glutamate and substance P. Strengthening this descending inhibitory system lowers the central amplification of pain thought to underlie fibromyalgia. Milnacipran has no meaningful binding to histamine, muscarinic or adrenergic receptors.", "body": [["Spinal cord and pain", "Stronger descending noradrenergic and serotonergic inhibition reduces widespread pain and tenderness in fibromyalgia."], ["Heart and vessels", "Extra norepinephrine at sympathetic nerve endings raises heart rate and blood pressure, so both are checked before and during treatment."], ["Gut", "Nausea and constipation are common, from serotonergic effects and sympathetic slowing of the gut."], ["Skin", "Hot flushes and heavy sweating come from noradrenergic stimulation of sweat glands and skin vessels."], ["Bladder and sexual organs", "Norepinephrine tightens the bladder neck, causing urinary hesitancy, and men can have testicular pain or ejaculation problems."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises and rare severe liver injury have been reported."]], "journey": "Milnacipran is well absorbed by mouth, with high bioavailability, and peaks within two to four hours. It is only lightly bound to plasma proteins and is not heavily processed by cytochrome P450 enzymes, so it has fewer enzyme-based drug interactions. Some is conjugated with glucuronic acid, but about half leaves the body unchanged in the urine. The half-life is six to eight hours, which is why it is taken twice daily and why kidney function matters.", "sidefx": "Many side effects are noradrenergic: faster heart rate, higher blood pressure, sweating, flushing, palpitations and urinary hesitancy. Nausea and headache reflect serotonin effects. Like antidepressants, it carries a boxed warning for suicidal thoughts in young people, even though it is not approved for depression or children. Combination with MAOIs or other serotonergic drugs can cause serotonin syndrome. Abrupt stopping can cause discontinuation symptoms. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Savella (milnacipran) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology"]}, "imipramine": {"origin": "Imipramine was the first tricyclic antidepressant, and its discovery was partly an accident. In the early 1950s, after chlorpromazine transformed psychiatry, the Swiss company Geigy looked for similar drugs among its iminodibenzyl compounds. Geigy sent one of them, G 22355, to the psychiatrist Roland Kuhn at Münsterlingen hospital in Switzerland. It did little for schizophrenia, but in 1956 Kuhn noticed that depressed patients improved markedly. He reported this in 1957, and Geigy launched it as Tofranil in 1958. Imipramine became the model for a whole class of antidepressants, and studies of how it blocks amine reuptake helped shape the monoamine theory of depression.", "timeline": [["1956", "Roland Kuhn observes the antidepressant effect of Geigy compound G 22355 in Switzerland."], ["1957", "Kuhn publishes his findings on imipramine in depression."], ["1958", "Geigy launches imipramine as Tofranil."], ["1960s", "Julius Axelrod and others show that tricyclics block norepinephrine reuptake."]], "cell": "Imipramine blocks the presynaptic reuptake transporters for serotonin (SERT) and norepinephrine (NET). As a tertiary amine it is relatively stronger at SERT, but the liver demethylates it (via CYP2C19 and CYP1A2) to desipramine, a secondary amine that is a more selective NET inhibitor, so the active mix affects both systems. Raised synaptic monoamines lead over weeks to receptor adaptations such as downregulation of beta-adrenergic and 5-HT2 receptors, which track with the antidepressant effect. Imipramine also antagonizes muscarinic M1, histamine H1 and alpha-1 adrenergic receptors. At toxic levels it blocks fast voltage-gated sodium channels in the heart, slowing phase 0 depolarization.", "body": [["Brain", "More serotonin and norepinephrine signaling improves mood over two to four weeks; H1 blockade causes sedation within hours."], ["Bladder", "In children with bedwetting, anticholinergic effects on the bladder and changes in sleep and arousal reduce nighttime wetting, though the effect often fades after stopping."], ["Heart", "Muscarinic blockade speeds the heart, and sodium channel blockade at high levels widens the QRS complex and can cause ventricular arrhythmias."], ["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 blockade relaxes vessels, causing orthostatic hypotension and falls, especially in older adults."], ["Mouth, eyes and gut", "Anticholinergic effects cause dry mouth, blurred vision and constipation."], ["Metabolism", "H1 and 5-HT2C effects increase appetite and weight."]], "journey": "Imipramine is well absorbed by mouth but goes through significant first-pass metabolism in the liver. It is highly lipid soluble and about ninety percent protein bound, spreading widely into tissues. CYP2C19 and CYP1A2 demethylate it to active desipramine, and CYP2D6 hydroxylates both compounds into less active products that are excreted in the urine. The half-life of imipramine is roughly six to eighteen hours, and desipramine lasts longer. CYP2D6 poor metabolizers and people on strong CYP2D6 inhibitors can reach toxic levels.", "sidefx": "Anticholinergic, antihistamine and alpha-1 blockade explain dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention, sedation, weight gain and orthostatic hypotension. The dangerous toxicity is cardiac sodium channel blockade, which widens the QRS and causes wide-complex tachycardia, plus seizures and coma in overdose. Sodium bicarbonate is the key treatment for TCA cardiotoxicity, raising serum sodium and pH to overcome the channel block. It carries the antidepressant boxed warning for suicidality in young people, and its narrow safety margin makes overdose especially dangerous.", "fun": "Roland Kuhn reportedly first gave imipramine to patients with schizophrenia; the drug failed there, and it was only when he tried it in patients with severe depression that its true use was found.", "src": ["Tofranil (imipramine) FDA label", "Kuhn R. Am J Psychiatry, 1958", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "tranylcypromine": {"origin": "Tranylcypromine began as a chemical cousin of amphetamine. It was first synthesized in 1948 by Alfred Burger and William Yost, who made it by closing amphetamine's side chain into a small three-carbon ring. It was first looked at as a possible stimulant and decongestant. In the late 1950s researchers found that it strongly inhibits monoamine oxidase, the enzyme that breaks down serotonin, norepinephrine and dopamine. Smith Kline & French introduced it as Parnate in 1961. Soon after, reports of sudden severe headaches and strokes appeared, and in 1963 the pharmacist Barry Blackwell linked these attacks to aged cheese, which is rich in tyramine. After a brief withdrawal, it returned with dietary warnings. The Parnate brand is now discontinued, but generic tablets remain.", "timeline": [["1948", "Alfred Burger and William Yost synthesize tranylcypromine as an amphetamine analog."], ["1961", "Smith Kline & French introduces tranylcypromine as Parnate."], ["1963", "Barry Blackwell links MAOI hypertensive crises to tyramine in cheese."], ["1964", "Parnate is briefly withdrawn, then returns with dietary restrictions."]], "cell": "Tranylcypromine irreversibly inhibits both forms of monoamine oxidase. MAO-A, on the outer mitochondrial membrane of neurons, gut and liver cells, mainly breaks down serotonin, norepinephrine and tyramine; MAO-B mainly breaks down dopamine and phenylethylamine. With the enzyme disabled, monoamines that leak from storage vesicles into the cytoplasm are no longer destroyed, so more is packaged and released into synapses. Over weeks this raises signaling and leads to receptor adaptations tied to the antidepressant effect. Because inhibition is irreversible, activity returns only as new enzyme is made over about two weeks. Its amphetamine-like structure may add mild stimulant and releasing effects.", "body": [["Brain", "More serotonin, norepinephrine and dopamine improve depression over two to three weeks, sometimes in people who did not respond to other drugs; insomnia and stimulation are common."], ["Gut and liver", "MAO-A in the gut wall and liver normally destroys dietary tyramine. With it blocked, tyramine from aged cheese, cured meats and other foods reaches the blood."], ["Blood vessels", "Absorbed tyramine pushes large amounts of norepinephrine out of sympathetic nerve endings, causing severe hypertension, pounding headache and risk of brain hemorrhage."], ["Blood pressure at rest", "Paradoxically, orthostatic hypotension is common, possibly from false transmitters such as octopamine building up in sympathetic nerves."], ["Blood sugar", "MAOIs can enhance the effect of insulin and other antidiabetic drugs, causing low blood sugar."]], "journey": "Tranylcypromine is absorbed quickly by mouth and peaks within one to two hours. It is metabolized in the liver and excreted in the urine. The drug itself has a short half-life of only two to three hours, but that does not matter much because the enzyme it destroys must be rebuilt. MAO inhibition therefore lasts one to two weeks after the last dose, which is why washout periods are needed before starting other serotonergic or sympathomimetic drugs.", "sidefx": "The hallmark danger is the cheese reaction: tyramine or sympathomimetic drugs cause massive norepinephrine release and a hypertensive crisis. Combining it with SSRIs, SNRIs, meperidine, dextromethorphan, linezolid or similar drugs can cause severe serotonin syndrome with hyperthermia. Orthostatic hypotension, insomnia and weight change are common. Its amphetamine-like structure gives some abuse and dependence potential. There is no antidote that restores MAO; hypertensive crises are treated with fast-acting vasodilators or alpha blockers such as phentolamine, and serotonin syndrome with supportive care and sometimes cyproheptadine.", "fun": "Barry Blackwell was a young pharmacist when he linked MAOI crises to cheese; at first some experts doubted the idea, but the tyramine explanation proved correct and he went on to a career as a psychiatrist.", "src": ["Tranylcypromine sulfate FDA label", "Blackwell B. Lancet, 1963", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "zuranolone": {"origin": "Zuranolone came from research into neuroactive steroids, hormones made in the brain that strongly calm neural activity. One of them, allopregnanolone, is made from progesterone and rises sharply during pregnancy, then falls quickly after birth. Researchers proposed that this sudden drop helps trigger postpartum depression. Sage Therapeutics first developed brexanolone, an intravenous form of allopregnanolone, approved in 2019 as a sixty-hour infusion. Sage then designed zuranolone, a synthetic relative that can be taken by mouth. Developed with Biogen, it was approved by the FDA in August 2023 as Zurzuvae, the first oral pill specifically for postpartum depression, taken as a two-week course. The FDA did not approve it for major depressive disorder.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Neurosteroids such as allopregnanolone are shown to strongly enhance GABA-A receptors."], ["2019", "FDA approves brexanolone, an intravenous allopregnanolone, for postpartum depression."], ["2023", "FDA approves zuranolone (Zurzuvae) as the first oral drug for postpartum depression."]], "cell": "Zuranolone is a positive allosteric modulator of GABA-A receptors, the chloride channels that mediate fast inhibition in the brain. It binds a neurosteroid site in the transmembrane part of the receptor, separate from the benzodiazepine site. It acts on both synaptic receptors, which carry quick phasic inhibition, and extrasynaptic receptors containing delta subunits, which carry steady tonic inhibition and are insensitive to benzodiazepines. Enhancing GABA increases chloride flow, hyperpolarizes neurons and calms overactive circuits. In postpartum depression, it is thought to restore GABA-A signaling that was tuned to high pregnancy allopregnanolone levels and disrupted when those levels fell, giving a rapid antidepressant effect within days.", "body": [["Brain mood circuits", "Rebalanced GABA-A inhibition improves depressive symptoms within days, much faster than standard antidepressants, after a fourteen-day course."], ["Alertness", "Strong GABA-A enhancement causes somnolence, dizziness and confusion; the boxed warning says not to drive or operate machinery for at least twelve hours after each dose."], ["Breathing and consciousness", "Combined with alcohol, opioids or other depressants, the added CNS depression can cause loss of consciousness or respiratory depression."], ["Pregnancy", "Animal studies showed fetal harm, so effective contraception is advised during treatment and for a period afterward."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is among the common side effects in trials."]], "journey": "Zuranolone is taken by mouth in the evening with fat-containing food, which increases absorption. Peak levels come five to six hours later. It is more than ninety-nine percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels and inducers lower them. Metabolites leave mostly in the feces and urine. Its half-life of about twenty to twenty-four hours supports once-daily dosing during the two-week course.", "sidefx": "Sleepiness, dizziness and confusion come straight from enhancing GABA-A inhibition, the same reason it impairs driving the next day. Effects add to other sedatives. It is Schedule IV because GABA-A enhancers can be misused. Suicidal thoughts are monitored as with all antidepressants. Animal data showing fetal harm explain the contraception advice. There is no specific antidote; flumazenil does not reverse neurosteroid effects because it acts only at the benzodiazepine site, so overdose care is supportive.", "fun": "Zuranolone was the first postpartum depression treatment designed to be taken for just fourteen days and then stopped, rather than continued for months like standard antidepressants.", "src": ["Zurzuvae (zuranolone) FDA label", "Zulresso (brexanolone) FDA label", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology"]}, "dextromethorphan-bupropion": {"origin": "This combination pairs two old drugs in a new way. Dextromethorphan was developed in the 1950s as a non-addictive cough suppressant to replace codeine and has been sold over the counter for decades. Bupropion was created at Burroughs Wellcome and first approved as an antidepressant in the 1980s. Interest in fast-acting antidepressants grew after ketamine, another NMDA receptor blocker, was shown to lift depression within hours. Dextromethorphan also blocks NMDA receptors, but the liver normally destroys it within hours through CYP2D6. The US company Axsome Therapeutics realized that bupropion strongly blocks CYP2D6, so pairing them keeps dextromethorphan levels high while bupropion adds its own antidepressant effect. The FDA approved the combination as Auvelity in August 2022, and later labeling added agitation associated with Alzheimer dementia.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Dextromethorphan is developed as a non-opioid cough suppressant."], ["1980s", "Bupropion is first approved as an antidepressant in the US."], ["2000s", "Ketamine research shows that NMDA receptor blockade can relieve depression quickly."], ["2022", "FDA approves Auvelity for major depressive disorder in adults."]], "cell": "Dextromethorphan is an uncompetitive antagonist of the NMDA glutamate receptor: it enters the open channel and blocks calcium and sodium flow. In models of rapid antidepressant action, NMDA blockade on inhibitory interneurons leads to a burst of glutamate that activates AMPA receptors, BDNF release and mTOR signaling, which rebuild synaptic connections in the prefrontal cortex. Dextromethorphan is also a sigma-1 receptor agonist and inhibits the serotonin and norepinephrine transporters. Bupropion inhibits norepinephrine (NET) and dopamine (DAT) reuptake, adding its own antidepressant effect, and it is a strong inhibitor of CYP2D6. That inhibition is the key to the pairing: it blocks conversion of dextromethorphan to dextrorphan, raising dextromethorphan exposure and lengthening its half-life.", "body": [["Brain mood circuits", "NMDA blockade plus norepinephrine and dopamine effects improved depression as early as the first week in trials, faster than many standard antidepressants."], ["Seizure threshold", "Bupropion lowers the seizure threshold in a dose-related way, so seizure disorders and eating disorders are contraindications."], ["Balance and alertness", "Dextromethorphan causes dizziness and somnolence, which can lead to falls, especially in older adults."], ["Heart and vessels", "Noradrenergic effects from bupropion can raise blood pressure, which is checked before and during treatment."], ["Mouth and skin", "Dry mouth and sweating reflect noradrenergic and other receptor effects."], ["Mood switching", "Like other antidepressants it can trigger mania in bipolar disorder, so screening is part of starting it."]], "journey": "Both drugs are in one extended-release tablet taken by mouth. Bupropion peaks in about two hours and is converted by CYP2B6 to hydroxybupropion, an active metabolite that also inhibits CYP2D6. Dextromethorphan peaks in about three hours. Normally CYP2D6 would rapidly convert it to dextrorphan, but with bupropion on board, dextromethorphan levels rise sharply and its half-life stretches to about twenty-two hours. Bupropion's half-life is about fifteen hours. Metabolites leave mostly in the urine.", "sidefx": "Seizures come from bupropion lowering the seizure threshold. Dizziness and somnolence reflect dextromethorphan's NMDA and sigma effects. Raised blood pressure comes from extra norepinephrine. Because dextromethorphan blocks serotonin reuptake, combining it with SSRIs, SNRIs or MAOIs can cause serotonin syndrome, and MAOIs are contraindicated. Like all antidepressants it carries a boxed warning for suicidal thoughts in young people. Other drugs metabolized by CYP2D6 can reach higher levels. There is no single antidote; seizures are treated with benzodiazepines.", "src": ["Auvelity (dextromethorphan-bupropion) FDA label", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "lurasidone": {"origin": "Lurasidone was discovered by the Japanese company Dainippon Sumitomo Pharma, now Sumitomo Pharma, during a search for antipsychotics with less weight gain and sedation than earlier atypicals. Its chemists built a benzisothiazol structure that strongly blocks dopamine and serotonin receptors but barely touches histamine H1 and muscarinic receptors, the receptors most linked to weight gain and anticholinergic effects. The FDA approved it as Latuda for schizophrenia in October 2010. In 2013 it was approved for depressive episodes of bipolar I disorder, one of only a few antipsychotics with that approval.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Dainippon Sumitomo Pharma develops lurasidone in Japan."], ["2010", "FDA approves Latuda for schizophrenia."], ["2013", "FDA approves lurasidone for bipolar I depression, alone or with lithium or valproate."]], "cell": "Lurasidone is an antagonist at dopamine D2 receptors and serotonin 5-HT2A receptors. Blocking D2 in the mesolimbic pathway reduces the excess dopamine signaling linked to hallucinations and delusions. Strong 5-HT2A blockade on cortical neurons and on dopamine pathways increases dopamine release in the striatum and prefrontal cortex, which softens motor side effects and may help negative symptoms. Lurasidone is also a high-affinity 5-HT7 antagonist and a 5-HT1A partial agonist; both actions are linked in animal models to antidepressant and cognitive effects, which may help explain its benefit in bipolar depression. It has minimal H1 and muscarinic binding, so weight gain and anticholinergic effects are low.", "body": [["Brain dopamine pathways", "D2 blockade in mesolimbic pathways reduces psychotic symptoms over one to two weeks."], ["Mood", "5-HT7 antagonism and 5-HT1A partial agonism may contribute to improvement in bipolar depression."], ["Motor system", "D2 blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway causes akathisia (restlessness) and parkinsonism, which are among its most common side effects."], ["Metabolism", "Low H1 and 5-HT2C activity means less weight gain and smaller changes in glucose and lipids than many atypicals."], ["Pituitary", "D2 blockade in the tuberoinfundibular pathway can mildly raise prolactin."]], "journey": "Lurasidone must be taken with a meal of at least about 350 calories; absorption is two to three times higher with food than fasting. It peaks within one to three hours and is about ninety-nine percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ketoconazole and strong inducers such as rifampin are contraindicated. About eighty percent leaves in the feces. The half-life is about eighteen hours, supporting once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Akathisia and parkinsonism come from blocking D2 in motor circuits, and long-term exposure risks tardive dyskinesia. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome, with rigidity, high fever and unstable vital signs, is a rare emergency of dopamine blockade. Like all antipsychotics it carries a boxed warning for increased death in elderly people with dementia-related psychosis, and because it treats depression it also carries the antidepressant suicidality warning. Leukopenia and seizures are rare. There is no antidote; acute dystonia responds to anticholinergics such as diphenhydramine or benztropine.", "src": ["Latuda (lurasidone) FDA label", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "asenapine": {"origin": "Asenapine was developed by the Dutch company Organon, which had earlier made the antidepressant mianserin; asenapine's four-ring structure grew out of that chemistry. When the swallowed drug was tested, the liver destroyed almost all of it on the first pass, so developers turned to a sublingual tablet that is absorbed through the lining of the mouth. After Organon became part of Schering-Plough, the FDA approved asenapine as Saphris in 2009 for schizophrenia and bipolar mania. A once-daily skin patch, Secuado, was approved in 2019, the first transdermal antipsychotic in the US.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Organon develops asenapine in the Netherlands."], ["2009", "FDA approves Saphris sublingual tablets for schizophrenia and bipolar I mania."], ["2019", "FDA approves Secuado, an asenapine skin patch, for schizophrenia."]], "cell": "Asenapine is an antagonist at many receptors. It binds 5-HT2A receptors with very high affinity and also blocks dopamine D2, serotonin 5-HT2C and 5-HT7, adrenergic alpha-1 and alpha-2, and histamine H1 receptors, while having almost no muscarinic activity. D2 blockade in the mesolimbic pathway reduces psychosis and mania. The very strong 5-HT2A blockade relative to D2 increases dopamine release in the striatum and cortex, lowering motor side effects. H1 blockade produces sedation, which helps in acute mania, and alpha-1 blockade relaxes blood vessels. The lack of muscarinic blockade means few anticholinergic effects.", "body": [["Brain", "D2 and 5-HT2A blockade reduce hallucinations, delusions and manic symptoms; H1 blockade causes sleepiness within hours."], ["Mouth", "Local action of the dissolving tablet causes numbness (oral hypoesthesia) and an odd taste, and rarely blisters or ulcers."], ["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 blockade lowers blood pressure on standing, causing dizziness and sometimes fainting."], ["Heart", "Asenapine can modestly prolong the QT interval."], ["Metabolism", "H1 and 5-HT2C blockade promote appetite, weight gain and changes in glucose and lipids."], ["Motor system", "Akathisia and other extrapyramidal symptoms can occur from D2 blockade."]], "journey": "Swallowed asenapine is almost entirely destroyed by the liver on the first pass, with oral bioavailability under two percent. Under the tongue it is absorbed through the mouth lining into the bloodstream, reaching peak levels within about an hour; eating or drinking within ten minutes washes drug into the stomach and lowers exposure. The patch delivers drug steadily through the skin over a day. The liver clears it by direct glucuronidation through UGT1A4 and oxidation by CYP1A2. Metabolites leave in urine and feces, and the half-life is about a day.", "sidefx": "Sedation and weight gain come from H1 and 5-HT2C blockade, and dizziness on standing from alpha-1 blockade. Extrapyramidal symptoms and long-term tardive dyskinesia come from D2 blockade, and neuroleptic malignant syndrome is a rare emergency. Serious allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis and angioedema, have been reported, some after the first dose. Oral numbness and ulcers are local effects of the sublingual tablet. It carries the class boxed warning for increased mortality in elderly dementia patients. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Saphris (asenapine) FDA label", "Secuado (asenapine transdermal) FDA label", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology"]}, "paliperidone": {"origin": "Paliperidone is the main active metabolite of risperidone, which was developed at Janssen Pharmaceutica in Belgium, the company founded by Paul Janssen. When the liver hydroxylates risperidone through CYP2D6, it makes 9-hydroxyrisperidone, which is just as active against dopamine and serotonin receptors. Janssen developed this metabolite as its own drug, with an advantage: it does not depend on CYP2D6. The FDA approved oral paliperidone as Invega in 2006, using an osmotic OROS tablet that releases drug slowly. Long-acting injections followed: monthly Invega Sustenna in 2009, three-monthly Invega Trinza in 2015 and six-monthly Invega Hafyera in 2021, giving options for people who struggle with daily pills.", "timeline": [["1993", "Risperidone, the parent drug, is approved in the US."], ["2006", "FDA approves Invega extended-release paliperidone tablets."], ["2009", "Monthly paliperidone palmitate injection (Invega Sustenna) is approved."], ["2015", "Three-month injection Invega Trinza is approved."], ["2021", "Six-month injection Invega Hafyera is approved."]], "cell": "Paliperidone is an antagonist at dopamine D2 and serotonin 5-HT2A receptors. Blocking D2 receptors in the mesolimbic pathway lowers the excess dopamine signaling behind positive symptoms such as hallucinations and delusions. Blocking 5-HT2A receptors on cortical pyramidal neurons and on dopamine neurons increases dopamine release in the striatum and prefrontal cortex, which offsets some motor effects of D2 blockade. It also blocks alpha-1, alpha-2 and H1 receptors, which explain orthostatic hypotension and some sedation, and it has no meaningful muscarinic activity. Paliperidone binds D2 tightly, and blockade of D2 in the pituitary leads to high prolactin, which is one of its characteristic effects.", "body": [["Brain dopamine pathways", "D2 blockade reduces positive symptoms of schizophrenia and schizoaffective disorder over days to weeks."], ["Pituitary and hormones", "Dopamine normally holds back prolactin. Blocking pituitary D2 raises prolactin, causing breast discharge, missed periods, gynecomastia and sexual dysfunction."], ["Motor system", "Akathisia, parkinsonism and dystonia increase at higher doses, and long-term use risks tardive dyskinesia."], ["Heart", "Paliperidone can prolong the QT interval and cause tachycardia."], ["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 blockade causes dizziness on standing."], ["Metabolism", "Weight gain and rises in glucose and lipids can occur."]], "journey": "The OROS tablet works like a tiny pump: water enters through a membrane and pushes drug out through a laser-drilled hole over a day, and the empty shell can pass in the stool. Peak levels come about a day after a dose. The long-acting injections contain paliperidone palmitate, a poorly soluble ester that dissolves slowly at the injection site in muscle and is hydrolyzed to paliperidone, giving months of exposure. Paliperidone is only lightly metabolized and does not depend on CYP2D6. About sixty percent leaves unchanged in the urine, so kidney function guides dosing. The oral half-life is about a day.", "sidefx": "High prolactin comes from D2 blockade in the pituitary. Extrapyramidal symptoms come from D2 blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway, and neuroleptic malignant syndrome is a rare emergency. QT prolongation reflects effects on cardiac potassium channels. Orthostatic hypotension comes from alpha-1 blockade. The OROS tablet can rarely cause obstruction in people with narrowed bowels. It carries the class boxed warning for increased mortality in elderly patients with dementia-related psychosis. There is no antidote, and injected drug cannot be removed once given.", "src": ["Invega (paliperidone) FDA label", "Invega Sustenna, Trinza and Hafyera FDA labels", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology"]}, "brexpiprazole": {"origin": "Brexpiprazole was discovered by the Japanese company Otsuka, which had earlier created aripiprazole, the first dopamine partial agonist antipsychotic. Otsuka chemists aimed for a successor with less intrinsic activity at D2 receptors and stronger serotonin effects, hoping to reduce the restlessness (akathisia) and activation that some people feel on aripiprazole. Developed with the Danish company Lundbeck, it was approved by the FDA in July 2015 as Rexulti for schizophrenia and as an add-on for major depressive disorder. In 2023 it became the first FDA-approved treatment for agitation associated with dementia due to Alzheimer disease.", "timeline": [["2002", "Otsuka's earlier partial agonist, aripiprazole, is approved in the US."], ["2015", "FDA approves Rexulti for schizophrenia and adjunctive treatment of depression."], ["2023", "FDA approves brexpiprazole for agitation associated with Alzheimer dementia."]], "cell": "Brexpiprazole is a partial agonist at dopamine D2 and D3 receptors. A partial agonist binds the receptor and produces a smaller response than dopamine. Where dopamine is high, as in mesolimbic pathways in psychosis, it competes with dopamine and lowers the signal; where dopamine is low it provides some baseline stimulation. Its intrinsic D2 activity is lower than aripiprazole's, so it behaves more like an antagonist. It is also a partial agonist at serotonin 5-HT1A receptors and a potent antagonist at 5-HT2A, alpha-1B and alpha-2C adrenergic receptors. The 5-HT1A and 5-HT2A actions are linked to antidepressant and anti-agitation effects and to fewer motor side effects.", "body": [["Brain dopamine pathways", "D2 partial agonism stabilizes dopamine signaling, reducing psychotic symptoms in schizophrenia."], ["Mood and agitation", "Added to an antidepressant, 5-HT1A partial agonism and 5-HT2A and alpha blockade improve depressive symptoms; in Alzheimer dementia it reduces agitation."], ["Motor system", "Akathisia and restlessness occur but were less common than expected for the class; tardive dyskinesia is a long-term risk."], ["Metabolism", "Weight gain is one of the more common side effects, along with rises in glucose and lipids."], ["Reward circuits", "Dopamine agonist activity in reward pathways has been linked to rare compulsive behaviors such as gambling, binge eating or shopping."], ["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 blockade can cause dizziness on standing and falls."]], "journey": "Brexpiprazole is taken by mouth once daily, with high bioavailability and peak levels at about four hours. It is more than ninety-nine percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it through CYP3A4 and CYP2D6, so doses are adjusted for CYP2D6 poor metabolizers and for strong inhibitors of either enzyme. It leaves in the feces and urine. Its half-life is long, about ninety-one hours, so it takes over two weeks to reach steady state and lingers after stopping.", "sidefx": "Weight gain and metabolic changes reflect serotonin and histamine receptor effects. Akathisia and tardive dyskinesia arise from altering D2 signaling in motor circuits, and neuroleptic malignant syndrome is a rare emergency. Compulsive urges come from partial agonist stimulation of reward pathways. It carries boxed warnings for increased mortality in elderly dementia patients and for suicidal thoughts with antidepressant use in young people. Orthostatic hypotension and falls come from alpha blockade. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Rexulti (brexpiprazole) FDA label", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "cariprazine": {"origin": "Cariprazine was discovered by the Hungarian company Gedeon Richter, which sought an antipsychotic that preferred the dopamine D3 receptor. D3 receptors are concentrated in limbic brain regions tied to mood, motivation and reward, and researchers thought targeting them might help negative symptoms and depression. Developed with Forest Laboratories, later part of Allergan and then AbbVie, it was approved by the FDA in September 2015 as Vraylar for schizophrenia and bipolar mania. Approvals for bipolar I depression followed in 2019 and for add-on treatment of major depressive disorder in 2022.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Gedeon Richter discovers cariprazine in Hungary."], ["2015", "FDA approves Vraylar for schizophrenia and manic or mixed episodes of bipolar I disorder."], ["2019", "FDA approves cariprazine for bipolar I depression."], ["2022", "FDA approves cariprazine as add-on treatment for major depressive disorder."]], "cell": "Cariprazine is a partial agonist at dopamine D3 and D2 receptors, with roughly tenfold higher affinity for D3. As a partial agonist it lowers signaling where dopamine is excessive, such as mesolimbic pathways in psychosis and mania, while providing some stimulation where dopamine is low. Its strong D3 occupancy in limbic areas is thought to contribute to effects on negative symptoms and bipolar depression. It is also a partial agonist at serotonin 5-HT1A receptors and an antagonist at 5-HT2B and 5-HT2A receptors. Its two active metabolites, desmethyl-cariprazine (DCAR) and didesmethyl-cariprazine (DDCAR), share this receptor profile and, because they last so long, provide most of the steady effect.", "body": [["Brain dopamine pathways", "D2 and D3 partial agonism reduces psychotic and manic symptoms over days to weeks."], ["Mood and motivation", "D3 and 5-HT1A actions in limbic regions are linked to improvement in bipolar depression and, as an add-on, major depression."], ["Motor system", "Akathisia and restlessness are among the most common side effects, plus parkinsonism; tardive dyskinesia is a long-term risk."], ["Metabolism", "Weight gain and rises in glucose and lipids are possible, though often modest."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and indigestion are common early side effects."], ["Blood", "Leukopenia and rarely agranulocytosis occur with antipsychotics, including cariprazine."]], "journey": "Cariprazine is taken by mouth as a capsule and peaks in three to six hours. The liver, mainly through CYP3A4 with some CYP2D6, converts it to DCAR and then to DDCAR, both active. Cariprazine lasts about two to four days, but DDCAR has a half-life of one to three weeks. As a result, levels build slowly over several weeks, and drug activity continues for weeks after stopping. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise exposure. Metabolites leave in the feces and urine.", "sidefx": "Akathisia and parkinsonism come from changing D2 signaling in the nigrostriatal pathway. Because DDCAR lingers, side effects can appear or persist weeks after a dose change, which the label calls late-occurring adverse reactions. Metabolic changes, low white counts and neuroleptic malignant syndrome are class effects of antipsychotics. It carries boxed warnings for increased mortality in elderly dementia patients and for suicidal thoughts in young people using antidepressant treatments. There is no antidote, and drug cannot be quickly removed.", "src": ["Vraylar (cariprazine) FDA label", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "lumateperone": {"origin": "Lumateperone came from the laboratory of Paul Greengard at Rockefeller University, who shared the 2000 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for discovering how dopamine signals inside neurons. Greengard co-founded Intra-Cellular Therapies, which built on this work to design a drug that acts on dopamine, serotonin and glutamate signaling at the same time. Lumateperone blocks serotonin 5-HT2A receptors far more strongly than dopamine D2 receptors, which allows low D2 occupancy at its labeled dose. The FDA approved it as Caplyta for schizophrenia in December 2019, and in December 2021 for depressive episodes of bipolar I or II disorder, alone or with lithium or valproate.", "timeline": [["2000", "Paul Greengard shares the Nobel Prize for work on dopamine signaling in the brain."], ["2019", "FDA approves Caplyta for schizophrenia."], ["2021", "FDA approves lumateperone for bipolar I and II depression."]], "cell": "Lumateperone is a potent serotonin 5-HT2A antagonist, with about sixty-fold higher affinity for 5-HT2A than for D2. At dopamine D2 receptors it acts as a partial agonist at presynaptic receptors, which reduces dopamine release, and as an antagonist at postsynaptic receptors. Together these lower mesolimbic dopamine signaling while occupying only a modest share of striatal D2 receptors, so motor and prolactin effects are uncommon. It also moderately inhibits the serotonin transporter, which may help depressive symptoms. Through D1 receptor-dependent signaling it indirectly enhances NMDA and AMPA glutamate receptor activity in the prefrontal cortex, which may support mood and cognitive effects.", "body": [["Brain dopamine pathways", "Presynaptic and postsynaptic D2 actions reduce psychotic symptoms over days to weeks."], ["Mood", "5-HT2A blockade, serotonin reuptake inhibition and glutamate effects contribute to improvement in bipolar depression."], ["Alertness", "Somnolence and sedation are the most common side effects, from strong 5-HT2A and some H1 effects."], ["Motor system", "Low D2 occupancy makes extrapyramidal symptoms uncommon compared with many antipsychotics."], ["Metabolism", "In trials, weight and metabolic changes were similar to placebo, though monitoring is still advised."], ["Blood vessels", "Orthostatic hypotension and dizziness can occur."]], "journey": "Lumateperone is taken by mouth once daily as a capsule and peaks within one to two hours. Only a small fraction of the swallowed dose reaches the blood unchanged because of extensive metabolism. It is about ninety-seven percent protein bound. It is broken down by many enzymes, including CYP3A4, several UGTs and aldoketoreductase, forming many metabolites that leave mainly in the urine. Strong CYP3A4 inducers greatly lower levels and strong inhibitors raise them. The half-life averages about eighteen hours.", "sidefx": "Sleepiness reflects serotonin and histamine receptor effects. Motor side effects and high prolactin are uncommon because D2 occupancy is low, but tardive dyskinesia and neuroleptic malignant syndrome remain class risks. Mania or hypomania can be triggered in bipolar patients. It carries boxed warnings for increased mortality in elderly patients with dementia-related psychosis and for suicidal thoughts in young people. Low white counts, orthostatic hypotension and falls are class effects. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Caplyta (lumateperone) FDA label", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 2000 press release"]}, "xanomeline-trospium": {"origin": "Xanomeline was developed in the 1990s by Eli Lilly with Novo Nordisk as a muscarinic agonist to improve memory in Alzheimer disease. In a 1997 trial it did something unexpected: it reduced hallucinations, delusions and agitation in patients with Alzheimer disease. A later small trial suggested benefit in schizophrenia. But stimulating muscarinic receptors in the body caused heavy nausea, vomiting, sweating and drooling, and development stopped. Years later, the startup Karuna Therapeutics paired xanomeline with trospium, an older bladder drug that blocks muscarinic receptors but cannot enter the brain. The trospium cancels many body-wide effects while leaving brain effects intact. The FDA approved the combination as Cobenfy in September 2024, the first antipsychotic in decades that does not work by blocking dopamine receptors.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Eli Lilly and Novo Nordisk develop xanomeline for Alzheimer disease."], ["1997", "An Alzheimer trial shows that xanomeline reduces psychotic symptoms."], ["2008", "A small trial suggests xanomeline helps schizophrenia, but side effects limit use."], ["2024", "FDA approves Cobenfy (xanomeline-trospium) for schizophrenia."]], "cell": "Xanomeline is an agonist that preferentially activates M1 and M4 muscarinic acetylcholine receptors in the brain. M4 receptors in the striatum and on neurons that drive midbrain dopamine cells reduce dopamine release when activated, lowering the excess striatal dopamine linked to psychosis without blocking D2 receptors directly. M1 receptors in the cortex and hippocampus modulate glutamate and cognitive circuits. Trospium chloride is a quaternary ammonium compound with a permanent positive charge, so it barely crosses the blood-brain barrier. It blocks muscarinic receptors in the gut, salivary glands, sweat glands and bladder, offsetting xanomeline's peripheral cholinergic effects.", "body": [["Brain", "M4 and M1 stimulation reduces positive and negative symptoms of schizophrenia over weeks, without D2 blockade."], ["Motor system and hormones", "Because it does not block D2, it does not cause the usual extrapyramidal effects or raise prolactin."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and indigestion come from residual xanomeline stimulation, while trospium slows gut motility, causing constipation and rarely severe hypomotility."], ["Bladder", "Trospium's antimuscarinic effect can cause urinary retention, especially in men with prostate enlargement."], ["Heart and vessels", "Heart rate can rise and blood pressure can increase."], ["Liver and bile", "Liver enzymes can rise, and gallstones and biliary problems have been reported."]], "journey": "Both drugs are taken by mouth twice daily, at least an hour before or a couple of hours after a meal, because food greatly lowers trospium absorption. Xanomeline peaks in about two hours and is extensively metabolized in the liver by CYP2D6, CYP2B6 and other enzymes, with metabolites leaving in the urine; liver impairment raises its levels. Trospium peaks in about an hour, is poorly absorbed, and is cleared largely unchanged by active tubular secretion in the kidneys. Both have half-lives of about five to six hours.", "sidefx": "Nausea, vomiting and dyspepsia reflect muscarinic stimulation in the gut that trospium does not fully block. Constipation, urinary retention and dry mouth come from trospium's antimuscarinic action, and severe gastric or urinary retention is a warning. Liver enzyme rises reflect heavy hepatic metabolism of xanomeline. Angioedema has been reported with trospium. It does not carry the class boxed warning about elderly dementia-related psychosis. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The idea for Cobenfy came from a failed Alzheimer drug: xanomeline's antipsychotic effect was noticed in elderly patients before anyone tried it seriously in schizophrenia.", "src": ["Cobenfy (xanomeline and trospium chloride) FDA label", "Bodick NC et al. Arch Neurol, 1997", "Brannan SK et al. N Engl J Med, 2021"]}, "fluphenazine": {"origin": "Fluphenazine belongs to the phenothiazines, the family that started modern antipsychotic treatment. In 1950 chemists at Rhône-Poulenc in France made chlorpromazine, and in 1952 the psychiatrists Jean Delay and Pierre Deniker showed it calmed psychosis. Drug companies then modified the phenothiazine structure to make stronger versions. Adding a piperazine side chain and a trifluoromethyl group created fluphenazine, a very high-potency drug introduced in the US by Squibb as Prolixin in the late 1950s. In the 1960s a long-acting decanoate ester was developed that slowly releases fluphenazine from muscle for weeks, one of the first depot antipsychotics. The Prolixin brand is discontinued, but generics remain.", "timeline": [["1950", "Chlorpromazine, the first phenothiazine antipsychotic, is synthesized at Rhône-Poulenc."], ["1952", "Delay and Deniker report chlorpromazine's antipsychotic effect."], ["1950s", "Fluphenazine is introduced as a high-potency phenothiazine."], ["1960s", "Fluphenazine decanoate becomes one of the first long-acting depot antipsychotics."]], "cell": "Fluphenazine is a potent competitive antagonist of dopamine D2 receptors. D2 receptors are Gi-coupled; blocking them prevents dopamine from lowering cAMP and opening potassium channels in target neurons. In the mesolimbic pathway this reduces the overactive dopamine signaling behind hallucinations and delusions. The same blockade occurs everywhere else: in the nigrostriatal pathway it unbalances dopamine and acetylcholine in the basal ganglia, causing extrapyramidal symptoms; in the tuberoinfundibular pathway it removes dopamine's brake on prolactin; and in the chemoreceptor trigger zone it suppresses nausea. As a high-potency drug, it has only weak muscarinic, H1 and alpha-1 blockade compared with chlorpromazine.", "body": [["Brain", "D2 blockade reduces positive psychotic symptoms over weeks; some drowsiness occurs."], ["Motor system", "High potency means frequent extrapyramidal symptoms: acute dystonia within days, akathisia, parkinsonism within weeks and tardive dyskinesia after long exposure."], ["Pituitary", "D2 blockade raises prolactin, causing breast discharge, menstrual changes and sexual dysfunction."], ["Temperature control", "Dopamine blockade in the hypothalamus impairs heat regulation, raising risk of heat illness."], ["Liver", "Cholestatic jaundice is a known phenothiazine reaction."], ["Blood", "Leukopenia and rarely agranulocytosis can occur."]], "journey": "Oral fluphenazine is absorbed from the gut but much is removed on the first pass through the liver. It is highly lipid soluble and over ninety percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it extensively, partly through CYP2D6. The oral half-life is about fifteen hours. The decanoate is injected into muscle, where the oily ester slowly diffuses out and is hydrolyzed to fluphenazine, giving an effective half-life of about a week or more and allowing dosing every few weeks.", "sidefx": "Nearly all its serious effects come from D2 blockade outside the target pathway. Acute dystonia, including dangerous laryngeal dystonia, comes from sudden dopamine loss in the striatum and responds to anticholinergics such as diphenhydramine or benztropine. Tardive dyskinesia is thought to reflect D2 receptor supersensitivity after long blockade. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome, with rigidity, fever and autonomic instability, is a rare emergency treated by stopping the drug and supportive cooling, sometimes with dantrolene or bromocriptine. It carries the class boxed warning for elderly dementia patients.", "src": ["Fluphenazine hydrochloride and fluphenazine decanoate FDA labels", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Lopez-Munoz F et al. Ann Clin Psychiatry, 2005 (history of chlorpromazine)"]}, "perphenazine": {"origin": "Perphenazine is a piperazine phenothiazine developed in the 1950s by Schering Corporation, part of the rush of chlorpromazine relatives that followed the discovery of antipsychotic drugs in France. It was marketed as Trilafon for psychosis and also for severe nausea and vomiting, because blocking dopamine in the brain's vomiting center turned out to be a powerful antiemetic effect. Perphenazine had a second life in 2005, when the large US government-funded CATIE trial found that this older drug worked about as well as several newer atypical antipsychotics for chronic schizophrenia. The Trilafon brand is discontinued, but generic tablets and a combination with amitriptyline remain.", "timeline": [["1952", "Chlorpromazine's antipsychotic effect is reported in France, launching the phenothiazine era."], ["1950s", "Schering introduces perphenazine as Trilafon."], ["2005", "The CATIE trial finds perphenazine broadly comparable to several atypical antipsychotics."]], "cell": "Perphenazine is a competitive antagonist of dopamine D2 receptors. In the mesolimbic pathway this reduces the overactive dopamine signaling linked to psychosis. In the chemoreceptor trigger zone of the area postrema, which sits outside the blood-brain barrier and senses toxins in the blood, D2 blockade suppresses signals to the vomiting center, explaining its antiemetic use. D2 blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway causes extrapyramidal symptoms, and in the tuberoinfundibular pathway it raises prolactin. As a mid-potency drug, it also has moderate alpha-1, histamine H1 and muscarinic blockade, giving some sedation, orthostasis and anticholinergic effects.", "body": [["Brain", "D2 blockade reduces hallucinations and delusions over weeks; H1 blockade causes sedation."], ["Vomiting center", "D2 blockade in the chemoreceptor trigger zone controls severe nausea and vomiting within about an hour."], ["Motor system", "Extrapyramidal symptoms such as dystonia, akathisia and parkinsonism are common, and long use risks tardive dyskinesia."], ["Pituitary", "Raised prolactin can cause breast discharge and menstrual changes."], ["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 blockade causes orthostatic hypotension."], ["Mouth, eyes and gut", "Moderate anticholinergic effects cause dry mouth, blurred vision and constipation."]], "journey": "Perphenazine is taken by mouth and peaks within about one to three hours, but extensive first-pass metabolism in the liver means only a modest part reaches the blood. It is highly protein bound and spreads widely into tissues. CYP2D6 is the main enzyme that breaks it down, and because CYP2D6 varies between people, poor metabolizers can reach much higher levels. Metabolites leave in the urine and feces. The half-life is about nine to twelve hours.", "sidefx": "Extrapyramidal symptoms, tardive dyskinesia and high prolactin come from D2 blockade outside the target pathways. Acute dystonia responds to anticholinergics such as diphenhydramine or benztropine. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is a rare emergency. Sedation, orthostasis and dry mouth reflect H1, alpha-1 and muscarinic blockade. Its strong antiemetic effect can mask vomiting from bowel obstruction, brain lesions or overdose of other drugs. Leukopenia and seizures are rare. It carries the class boxed warning for elderly dementia patients. There is no antidote.", "fun": "In the CATIE trial, perphenazine, one of the cheapest antipsychotics, held its own against much newer and costlier atypical drugs, which surprised many psychiatrists.", "src": ["Perphenazine tablets FDA label", "Lieberman JA et al. N Engl J Med, 2005 (CATIE)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "thioridazine": {"origin": "Thioridazine is a piperidine phenothiazine developed by the Swiss company Sandoz in the 1950s, one of the many chlorpromazine relatives made after phenothiazines transformed psychiatry. Marketed as Mellaril from the late 1950s, it became very widely used because it caused fewer movement side effects than high-potency drugs. Over time, however, evidence grew that it prolongs the QT interval more than most antipsychotics and can trigger the deadly rhythm torsades de pointes. In 2000 the FDA added a boxed warning and restricted it to schizophrenia that has not responded to other drugs. The Mellaril brand was withdrawn, and only generic tablets remain.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Sandoz develops thioridazine and markets it as Mellaril."], ["2000", "FDA adds a boxed warning for QTc prolongation and restricts thioridazine to refractory schizophrenia."], ["2005", "The Mellaril brand is discontinued; generics remain."]], "cell": "Thioridazine is a dopamine D2 receptor antagonist. Blocking D2 in mesolimbic pathways reduces psychotic symptoms. It has relatively weak effects in the nigrostriatal pathway, partly because its strong muscarinic blockade balances the loss of dopamine there, so extrapyramidal symptoms are less common. It also strongly blocks histamine H1 and alpha-1 adrenergic receptors. Importantly, thioridazine and its active metabolite mesoridazine block the cardiac hERG potassium channel that carries the rapid delayed rectifier current (IKr). Less potassium leaving heart cells during phase 3 slows repolarization, prolonging the action potential and the QTc interval in a dose-related way, which sets the stage for early afterdepolarizations and torsades de pointes.", "body": [["Brain", "D2 blockade reduces psychosis over weeks; H1 blockade causes marked sedation."], ["Heart", "hERG blockade prolongs the QTc and can cause torsades de pointes and sudden death, especially with low potassium or CYP2D6 inhibitors."], ["Eyes", "At high doses it can cause pigmentary retinopathy, a buildup of pigment in the retina that can permanently reduce vision; anticholinergic effects also blur vision."], ["Blood vessels", "Strong alpha-1 blockade causes orthostatic hypotension and dizziness."], ["Sexual function", "Alpha-1 blockade can cause retrograde ejaculation, where semen enters the bladder."], ["Gut and bladder", "Muscarinic blockade causes dry mouth, constipation and urinary retention."]], "journey": "Thioridazine is taken by mouth and peaks within one to four hours. It is highly lipid soluble and over ninety-five percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP2D6 to active metabolites, including mesoridazine and sulforidazine, which add to both effect and cardiac risk. Because CYP2D6 is polymorphic, poor metabolizers and people taking CYP2D6 inhibitors such as fluoxetine or paroxetine reach high levels, and these combinations are contraindicated. Metabolites leave in the urine and feces. The half-life is about a day.", "sidefx": "The key danger is QTc prolongation from hERG potassium channel blockade, leading to torsades de pointes and sudden death; this is why baseline ECG and potassium checks are part of use and why it is restricted. Torsades is treated with intravenous magnesium and correction of potassium. Pigmentary retinopathy is linked to high doses. Sedation, orthostatic hypotension and anticholinergic effects come from H1, alpha-1 and muscarinic blockade. Tardive dyskinesia, neuroleptic malignant syndrome and agranulocytosis are class effects. It also carries the boxed warning for elderly dementia patients.", "src": ["Thioridazine hydrochloride tablets FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "loxapine": {"origin": "Loxapine is a dibenzoxazepine antipsychotic developed in the 1960s by Lederle Laboratories, part of American Cyanamid. Its three-ring structure resembles both tricyclic antidepressants and clozapine, and in fact its metabolite amoxapine was later marketed as an antidepressant. Loxapine was approved in the US in the 1970s as Loxitane capsules. Decades later, the company Alexza developed a single-use inhaler that heats a thin film of loxapine into an aerosol that is breathed deep into the lungs, reaching the brain within minutes. The FDA approved this inhaled form, Adasuve, in 2012 for acute agitation, with strict safety rules because it can cause bronchospasm.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Lederle Laboratories develops loxapine."], ["1970s", "Loxapine capsules are approved in the US as Loxitane."], ["2012", "FDA approves Adasuve, an inhaled loxapine, for acute agitation, under a REMS."]], "cell": "Loxapine is an antagonist at dopamine D2 receptors, which reduces mesolimbic dopamine signaling in psychosis and calms agitation. It also has substantial serotonin 5-HT2A antagonism, giving it some features of atypical antipsychotics, especially at lower doses. D2 blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway causes extrapyramidal symptoms, and in the pituitary it raises prolactin. Loxapine also blocks histamine H1, alpha-1 adrenergic and, to a lesser degree, muscarinic receptors, explaining sedation, orthostasis and dry mouth. Its N-desmethyl metabolite, amoxapine, inhibits norepinephrine reuptake like a tricyclic antidepressant.", "body": [["Brain", "D2 and 5-HT2A blockade reduce psychotic symptoms; with the inhaler, agitation can settle within about ten minutes."], ["Alertness", "H1 blockade causes sedation, which contributes to its calming effect."], ["Lungs", "Inhaled loxapine can irritate the airways and cause bronchospasm, especially in asthma or COPD, so it is given only in enrolled facilities with rescue equipment."], ["Motor system", "Akathisia, parkinsonism and dystonia occur, and long-term oral use risks tardive dyskinesia."], ["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 blockade can cause orthostatic hypotension."], ["Mouth", "The inhaled form commonly causes an odd taste."]], "journey": "Oral loxapine is absorbed in one to three hours. The inhaler works differently: a heating element vaporizes the drug, which condenses into tiny particles that reach the alveoli, so blood levels peak in about two minutes, much like an intravenous dose. The liver metabolizes it through CYP1A2, CYP3A4 and CYP2D6 to amoxapine and hydroxylated metabolites, some active. These leave in urine and feces. The parent half-life is a few hours, with metabolites lasting longer.", "sidefx": "Bronchospasm with the inhaled form comes from direct airway irritation; this is the basis of its boxed warning and REMS, and inhaled bronchodilators are kept ready. Extrapyramidal symptoms come from D2 blockade and acute dystonia responds to anticholinergics. Sedation and orthostasis come from H1 and alpha-1 blockade. Seizures, tardive dyskinesia, neuroleptic malignant syndrome and low white counts are class risks. It carries the boxed warning for increased mortality in elderly patients with dementia-related psychosis. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Loxapine capsules FDA label", "Adasuve (loxapine inhalation powder) FDA label", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology"]}, "chlordiazepoxide": {"origin": "Chlordiazepoxide was the first benzodiazepine, and it was almost thrown away. In 1955 the chemist Leo Sternbach at Hoffmann-La Roche in Nutley, New Jersey, made a series of compounds while looking for new tranquilizers. Most were inactive, and the project was dropped. In 1957, during a lab cleanup, a colleague found one leftover compound that had never been tested. Sternbach sent it for screening, and the pharmacologist Lowell Randall found it calmed animals without heavy sedation. It was marketed as Librium in 1960, followed by diazepam (Valium) in 1963. Benzodiazepines soon replaced barbiturates for anxiety because they were much safer in overdose when taken alone.", "timeline": [["1955", "Leo Sternbach synthesizes the compound that becomes chlordiazepoxide at Hoffmann-La Roche."], ["1957", "The forgotten compound is tested and shows calming effects in animals."], ["1960", "Chlordiazepoxide is marketed as Librium, the first benzodiazepine."], ["1963", "Diazepam (Valium) follows from the same research program."]], "cell": "Chlordiazepoxide binds the benzodiazepine site of the GABA-A receptor, located at the interface between an alpha subunit and the gamma subunit. GABA-A is a pentameric chloride channel. Benzodiazepines do not open it on their own; they are positive allosteric modulators that increase the frequency of channel opening when GABA binds. More chloride flows in, hyperpolarizing the neuron and making it harder to fire. Receptors containing alpha-1 subunits mediate sedation and amnesia, while alpha-2 and alpha-3 receptors are linked to anxiety relief and muscle relaxation. In alcohol withdrawal, chronic alcohol has downregulated GABA-A function, and chlordiazepoxide substitutes for alcohol's GABA effect, calming hyperexcitability and preventing seizures.", "body": [["Brain", "Enhanced GABA inhibition relieves anxiety, causes drowsiness and can impair memory and coordination."], ["Alcohol withdrawal", "Cross-tolerance with alcohol lets it calm tremor, agitation, high heart rate and seizure risk during withdrawal; its long-acting metabolites give a smooth self-tapering effect."], ["Muscles", "Spinal GABA-A effects reduce muscle tone, adding to ataxia and fall risk."], ["Breathing", "Alone it has a wide safety margin, but with opioids or alcohol the added depression of brainstem breathing centers can be fatal."], ["Older adults", "Accumulation of long-acting metabolites causes prolonged sedation, confusion and falls."]], "journey": "Chlordiazepoxide is absorbed well by mouth and peaks within a few hours. It is about ninety-six percent protein bound. The liver, mainly through CYP3A4 oxidation, converts it to a chain of active metabolites: desmethylchlordiazepoxide, demoxepam and desmethyldiazepam (nordiazepam), which then forms oxazepam. The parent half-life is up to about a day, but desmethyldiazepam can last forty to over one hundred hours, so active drug builds over days and declines slowly. Metabolites are conjugated and leave in the urine. Liver disease and old age slow clearance.", "sidefx": "Sedation, ataxia and confusion come from enhanced GABA-A inhibition. With opioids or alcohol, combined CNS depression can cause profound sedation, respiratory depression, coma and death, the basis of the boxed warning, which also covers abuse, dependence and withdrawal. Long-term use causes GABA-A adaptation, so abrupt stopping can cause rebound anxiety and seizures. Paradoxical agitation can occur. The antidote is flumazenil, a benzodiazepine-site antagonist, but it can trigger seizures in dependent patients and is used with great caution.", "fun": "Chlordiazepoxide's discovery is often told as a story of luck: the compound sat on a shelf for about two years before a cleanup brought it back to testing.", "src": ["Chlordiazepoxide hydrochloride capsules FDA label", "Sternbach LH. J Med Chem, 1979 (The benzodiazepine story)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "oxazepam": {"origin": "Oxazepam is both a drug and a natural endpoint. When the body breaks down diazepam and chlordiazepoxide, one of the last active products is oxazepam. Researchers at Wyeth recognized that giving this metabolite directly would provide a benzodiazepine without the long chain of active metabolites. Wyeth developed it in the early 1960s and introduced it in the US as Serax in the mid-1960s. Because it is cleared by simple glucuronidation, which is relatively preserved in older age and liver disease, it became a favored choice for anxiety and alcohol withdrawal in those groups. The Serax brand is discontinued, but generic capsules remain.", "timeline": [["1960", "Chlordiazepoxide, whose breakdown chain ends in oxazepam, is introduced."], ["1960s", "Wyeth develops oxazepam and markets it as Serax."]], "cell": "Oxazepam binds the benzodiazepine site at the alpha-gamma subunit interface of the GABA-A receptor, a ligand-gated chloride channel. As a positive allosteric modulator it increases the frequency of channel opening when GABA is bound, letting more chloride enter and hyperpolarizing the neuron. This strengthens inhibitory signaling across the brain. Effects on alpha-2 and alpha-3 containing receptors in limbic circuits ease anxiety, while alpha-1 receptors mediate sedation. In alcohol withdrawal, it replaces the GABA-enhancing effect that alcohol provided, reducing the glutamate-driven hyperexcitability that causes tremor, agitation and seizures. Oxazepam produces no active metabolites.", "body": [["Brain", "Strengthened GABA inhibition relieves anxiety and tension and causes drowsiness."], ["Alcohol withdrawal", "Cross-tolerance with alcohol eases tremor, sweating, agitation and seizure risk."], ["Coordination", "Cerebellar and spinal GABA effects cause unsteadiness and raise fall risk."], ["Memory", "High doses can impair forming new memories."], ["Breathing", "Combined with opioids or alcohol, depression of brainstem breathing centers can be dangerous."]], "journey": "Oxazepam is absorbed slowly from the gut, so it takes longer to start working than diazepam, which makes it less useful when rapid relief is needed. Peak levels come in about two to four hours. It is highly protein bound. Instead of being oxidized by cytochrome P450 enzymes, it is directly joined to glucuronic acid in the liver to form an inactive glucuronide that leaves in the urine. The half-life is about five to fifteen hours, averaging about eight, and does not lengthen much in older adults or mild to moderate liver disease.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness, dizziness and ataxia come from enhanced GABA-A inhibition. Combined use with opioids or alcohol can cause profound sedation, respiratory depression, coma and death, as in the class boxed warning, which also covers abuse, dependence and withdrawal. Regular use leads to receptor adaptation, so abrupt stopping can cause withdrawal and seizures. Paradoxical excitement can occur. Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepine effects but can precipitate seizures in dependent patients.", "src": ["Oxazepam capsules FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "viloxazine": {"origin": "Viloxazine was first developed in the 1970s by the British company Imperial Chemical Industries as an antidepressant. It was sold in Europe for many years under names such as Vivalan, then was withdrawn from those markets for commercial reasons in the early 2000s. It was never sold in the US. The US company Supernus Pharmaceuticals noticed that its main action, blocking norepinephrine reuptake, resembled atomoxetine, a nonstimulant ADHD drug. Supernus developed an extended-release capsule, and the FDA approved it as Qelbree in 2021 for ADHD in children six and older, and in 2022 for adults. It is not a controlled substance.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Imperial Chemical Industries develops viloxazine as an antidepressant in the UK."], ["2000s", "Viloxazine is withdrawn from European markets for commercial reasons."], ["2021", "FDA approves Qelbree for ADHD in children 6 to 17 years old."], ["2022", "FDA extends approval to adults with ADHD."]], "cell": "Viloxazine inhibits the norepinephrine transporter (NET). In the prefrontal cortex, NET also clears dopamine because dopamine transporters are sparse there, so NET blockade raises both norepinephrine and dopamine in this region, which supports attention and impulse control. Unlike stimulants, it does not raise dopamine in the nucleus accumbens reward center, which is why abuse potential is low. Viloxazine also acts on serotonin receptors: it is a 5-HT2B antagonist and a 5-HT2C agonist, and animal studies show increased prefrontal serotonin. These serotonin effects may contribute to its clinical profile, though their exact role is not clear.", "body": [["Brain", "Raised prefrontal norepinephrine and dopamine improve attention, hyperactivity and impulsivity over one to two weeks."], ["Heart and vessels", "Extra norepinephrine at sympathetic nerve endings raises heart rate and blood pressure, which are checked before starting and after dose changes."], ["Alertness", "Somnolence and fatigue are among the most common side effects, while some people instead have insomnia or irritability."], ["Appetite and gut", "Decreased appetite, nausea and vomiting are common."], ["Mood", "Like antidepressants, it carries a warning for suicidal thoughts and can trigger mania in bipolar disorder."]], "journey": "Viloxazine is taken by mouth once daily as an extended-release capsule that can also be opened and sprinkled on applesauce. Peak levels come about five hours after a dose. It is metabolized in the liver mainly by CYP2D6 hydroxylation, followed by glucuronidation through UGT1A9 and UGT2B15, and about ninety percent leaves in the urine. The half-life is about seven hours. Viloxazine is a strong inhibitor of CYP1A2, which raises levels of drugs such as tizanidine and theophylline.", "sidefx": "Increased heart rate and blood pressure come from enhanced norepinephrine signaling. Sleepiness, decreased appetite and nausea are common. It carries a boxed warning for suicidal thoughts and behaviors in children and young adults, similar to antidepressants, and it can trigger mania, so bipolar screening is part of starting it. Its strong CYP1A2 inhibition makes combination with sensitive CYP1A2 substrates such as tizanidine dangerous. Combination with MAOIs is contraindicated because of the risk of hypertensive crisis. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Qelbree (viloxazine extended-release) FDA label", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology"]}, "dextroamphetamine": {"origin": "Amphetamine was first synthesized in 1887 by the Romanian chemist Lazar Edeleanu in Berlin, but its effects went unstudied for decades. In the late 1920s the American chemist Gordon Alles resynthesized it while searching for an ephedrine substitute and tested it on himself, noticing its stimulant effect. Smith, Kline & French introduced racemic amphetamine as the Benzedrine inhaler for nasal congestion in 1933, and later as tablets for narcolepsy. In 1937 the physician Charles Bradley reported that Benzedrine improved behavior and school performance in children, an early step toward ADHD treatment. Scientists found the dextro isomer was the more potent central stimulant, and SKF marketed it as Dexedrine. In 1971 amphetamines were moved to Schedule II because of widespread misuse.", "timeline": [["1887", "Lazar Edeleanu first synthesizes amphetamine in Berlin."], ["1920s", "Gordon Alles resynthesizes amphetamine and describes its stimulant effects."], ["1933", "Smith, Kline & French introduces the Benzedrine inhaler."], ["1937", "Charles Bradley reports that Benzedrine improves behavior in children."], ["1971", "Amphetamines are placed in Schedule II under the Controlled Substances Act."]], "cell": "Dextroamphetamine is a releaser, not just a reuptake blocker. It enters dopamine and norepinephrine nerve terminals through the dopamine transporter (DAT) and norepinephrine transporter (NET). Inside, it enters synaptic vesicles through VMAT2 and collapses their pH gradient, so stored dopamine and norepinephrine leak into the cytoplasm. Activation of the intracellular receptor TAAR1 and other signaling causes DAT and NET to reverse direction and pump transmitter out into the synapse. It also weakly inhibits monoamine oxidase. The result is a large rise in synaptic dopamine and norepinephrine. In the prefrontal cortex this improves attention and executive control; in the nucleus accumbens it produces reward, which drives misuse.", "body": [["Brain", "More prefrontal dopamine and norepinephrine improve attention, focus and impulse control in ADHD, and promote wakefulness in narcolepsy."], ["Reward circuits", "Dopamine surges in the nucleus accumbens produce euphoria, which underlies misuse and addiction."], ["Heart and vessels", "Released norepinephrine raises heart rate and blood pressure and narrows peripheral vessels, rarely causing Raynaud phenomenon."], ["Appetite and growth", "Hypothalamic effects reduce appetite, causing weight loss, and long-term use can modestly slow growth in children."], ["Sleep", "Increased arousal causes insomnia, especially with late doses."], ["Mental state", "High doses or vulnerability can trigger psychosis or mania from excess dopamine."]], "journey": "Dextroamphetamine is well absorbed by mouth and starts working in thirty to sixty minutes. Immediate-release tablets peak in about three hours; extended-release capsules spread the dose over the day. The patch delivers it through the skin. It is lightly protein bound and enters the brain easily. Part is metabolized in the liver, partly by CYP2D6, through hydroxylation and deamination. Much leaves unchanged in the urine, and because it is a weak base, acidic urine speeds its excretion while alkaline urine slows it. The half-life is about ten to twelve hours.", "sidefx": "Insomnia, appetite loss, rapid heart rate and high blood pressure come from released norepinephrine and dopamine. In people with heart disease this can lead to stroke, heart attack or sudden death. Excess dopamine can cause psychosis, mania or tics. The boxed warning covers abuse, misuse and addiction. Combination with MAOIs is contraindicated, and with other serotonergic drugs it can contribute to serotonin syndrome. Overdose causes agitation, hyperthermia, arrhythmias and seizures. There is no antidote; benzodiazepines are used for agitation and seizures, with cooling and supportive care.", "fun": "During World War II, amphetamine tablets were widely given to soldiers and pilots on several sides to fight fatigue.", "src": ["Zenzedi and Dexedrine (dextroamphetamine) FDA labels", "Rasmussen N. On Speed: The Many Lives of Amphetamine, 2008", "Bradley C. Am J Psychiatry, 1937"]}, "acamprosate": {"origin": "Acamprosate (calcium acetylhomotaurinate) is a synthetic molecule built to resemble two natural brain chemicals, the amino acid taurine and the inhibitory transmitter GABA. It was developed by the French company Lipha and introduced in France in 1989 to help people stay sober after alcohol withdrawal. It was used across Europe for over a decade before the FDA approved it in the US in 2004 as Campral. Its arrival gave US prescribers a third option for alcohol use disorder after disulfiram and naltrexone. The Campral brand has been discontinued, but generic delayed-release tablets remain available.", "timeline": [["1989", "Acamprosate is introduced in France by Lipha."], ["1990s", "European trials support its use for maintaining abstinence."], ["2004", "FDA approves acamprosate as Campral."]], "cell": "Acamprosate's exact mechanism is not fully established. Long-term heavy drinking causes the brain to adapt: alcohol dampens NMDA glutamate receptors and enhances GABA-A receptors, so the brain responds by increasing NMDA activity and reducing GABA tone. When drinking stops, this leaves a state of glutamate hyperexcitability that can last for months and is thought to drive craving and relapse. Acamprosate appears to restore balance by modulating NMDA receptors in a functional antagonist manner, and possibly by acting on metabotropic glutamate receptor 5 and calcium channels. It does not act on opioid receptors, does not block alcohol's effects and does not cause a reaction if alcohol is consumed.", "body": [["Brain", "Calming excess glutamate activity after withdrawal reduces craving and the discomfort that pushes people to drink, helping maintain abstinence."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is the most common side effect, along with nausea and gas."], ["Kidneys", "It is cleared entirely by the kidneys, so poor kidney function raises levels, and severe kidney impairment is a contraindication."], ["Mood", "Depression and suicidal thoughts were reported more often than with placebo in trials, so mood is watched."], ["Liver", "It is not metabolized by the liver, making it usable in people with alcohol-related liver disease."]], "journey": "Acamprosate comes as a delayed-release tablet that passes the stomach before releasing drug. Absorption from the gut is poor, with bioavailability of only about eleven percent, and food lowers it further. Peak levels come several hours after a dose, and steady state takes about five days of regular dosing. It is not bound to plasma proteins and is not metabolized at all. It leaves the body unchanged in the urine, with a half-life of roughly twenty to thirty hours after oral dosing.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea is likely related to poorly absorbed drug acting in the gut. Because it is cleared only by the kidneys, kidney impairment causes accumulation. Mood problems and suicidal thoughts were seen more often than placebo, and alcohol use disorder itself carries high suicide risk. Acamprosate does not prevent or treat alcohol withdrawal symptoms, which require separate care. It does not interact with alcohol. There is no antidote; overdose has mainly caused diarrhea.", "src": ["Campral (acamprosate calcium) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "nalmefene": {"origin": "Nalmefene was developed in the 1970s as a chemical cousin of naltrexone, in which a methylene group replaces the ketone at the 6 position of the molecule. This small change gave a strong opioid antagonist that lasts much longer than naloxone. An injectable form, Revex, was approved in the US in 1995 to reverse opioid effects, but it sold poorly and was discontinued in 2008. In Europe, nalmefene tablets were approved in 2013 to help reduce alcohol consumption. The rise of fentanyl and other potent synthetic opioids renewed US interest in long-acting reversal agents. The FDA approved an injection again in 2022, the Opvee nasal spray in 2023 and the Zurnai auto-injector in 2024.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Nalmefene is developed as a 6-methylene analog of naltrexone."], ["1995", "FDA approves nalmefene injection as Revex."], ["2008", "Revex is discontinued in the US."], ["2023", "FDA approves Opvee nalmefene nasal spray for opioid overdose."], ["2024", "FDA approves the Zurnai nalmefene auto-injector."]], "cell": "Nalmefene is a competitive antagonist at mu and delta opioid receptors, with partial agonist activity at kappa receptors. It binds mu receptors with very high affinity and pushes opioid agonists such as fentanyl, heroin or morphine off the receptor. With the receptor no longer activated, Gi signaling stops: adenylyl cyclase is no longer inhibited, potassium channels close and calcium channels in nerve terminals work normally again. In the brainstem respiratory centers this restores the breathing drive to carbon dioxide, and in the brain it reverses sedation and pinpoint pupils. Its long half-life means the receptor block lasts several hours, longer than naloxone.", "body": [["Breathing", "Displacing opioids from mu receptors in the brainstem restores breathing within minutes."], ["Brain", "Sedation and coma lift as opioid signaling stops."], ["Eyes", "Pinpoint pupils return toward normal size."], ["Whole body in opioid dependence", "Sudden loss of opioid effect causes precipitated withdrawal: vomiting, sweating, agitation, pain, yawning and diarrhea, which can last longer than with naloxone."], ["Heart and lungs", "The surge of sympathetic activity during abrupt reversal can rarely cause hypertension, arrhythmias or pulmonary edema."]], "journey": "Nasal nalmefene is absorbed through the nasal lining and reaches peak levels in about fifteen minutes, with effect within minutes. The injection works even faster. It is metabolized in the liver mainly by glucuronidation to inactive conjugates that are excreted in the urine. Its half-life is about eleven hours, much longer than naloxone's roughly one to two hours, which is why its reversal lasts longer.", "sidefx": "Withdrawal symptoms are the direct result of suddenly blocking opioid receptors in someone physically dependent; because nalmefene lasts longer, precipitated withdrawal can be more prolonged than with naloxone. Abrupt reversal releases a burst of catecholamines that can rarely cause pulmonary edema, arrhythmias or cardiac arrest. Very long-acting opioids can still outlast it, and it is less effective against buprenorphine because buprenorphine binds the receptor so tightly. Emergency care is still sought after use. Newborns of opioid-dependent mothers can develop life-threatening withdrawal.", "src": ["Opvee (nalmefene nasal spray) FDA label", "Zurnai (nalmefene injection) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "buprenorphine-naloxone": {"origin": "Buprenorphine was created in 1966 at the British company Reckitt & Colman in Hull, England, where a team led by John Lewis was working on chemicals derived from thebaine, an opium alkaloid. They wanted a strong pain reliever with less abuse potential. Buprenorphine turned out to be a partial agonist with a ceiling on its breathing effects. It was approved in the US for pain as the injection Buprenex in 1981. Researchers then showed it could treat opioid addiction. After the Drug Addiction Treatment Act of 2000 allowed office-based treatment, the FDA approved Subutex (buprenorphine) and Suboxone (buprenorphine with naloxone) in 2002. Naloxone was added to discourage injecting the product.", "timeline": [["1961", "Naloxone is synthesized by Jack Fishman and Mozes Lewenstein."], ["1966", "Buprenorphine is synthesized at Reckitt & Colman in England."], ["1981", "Buprenex injection is approved in the US for pain."], ["2000", "The Drug Addiction Treatment Act allows office-based opioid addiction treatment."], ["2002", "FDA approves Suboxone and Subutex for opioid dependence."], ["2010", "A Suboxone sublingual film is approved."]], "cell": "Buprenorphine is a high-affinity partial agonist at the mu opioid receptor. It binds more tightly than most opioids and dissociates slowly, but activates the receptor's Gi signaling only partly. This gives enough effect to prevent withdrawal and craving, with a ceiling on respiratory depression when used alone. Its tight binding also blocks other opioids from producing euphoria. If given to someone with full agonists on their receptors, it displaces them and gives less activation, which can precipitate withdrawal. It is also a kappa receptor antagonist. Naloxone is a competitive mu antagonist. Under the tongue very little naloxone is absorbed, so it has little effect, but if the product is injected, naloxone becomes active and causes withdrawal.", "body": [["Brain reward and craving", "Steady partial mu activation prevents withdrawal and reduces craving, helping stabilize recovery."], ["Breathing", "The partial agonist ceiling limits respiratory depression when used alone, but combining it with benzodiazepines, alcohol or other depressants can still cause fatal respiratory depression."], ["Gut", "Mu activation in the gut slows motility, causing constipation and nausea."], ["Liver", "Hepatitis and liver injury have been reported, especially in people with hepatitis C or who inject the drug."], ["Teeth", "Dental problems including decay and tooth loss have been reported with medicines that dissolve in the mouth."], ["Hormones", "Long-term opioid use can rarely cause adrenal insufficiency and low sex hormones."]], "journey": "The film or tablet dissolves under the tongue or inside the cheek. Buprenorphine is absorbed through the mouth lining because swallowed drug is mostly destroyed by first-pass liver metabolism. Peak levels come in one to two and a half hours. It is highly protein bound and lipid soluble. CYP3A4 converts it to norbuprenorphine, and both are glucuronidated, mainly leaving in the feces. Its half-life is about one to one and a half days, and tight receptor binding extends its effect further. Naloxone under the tongue has very low bioavailability and is glucuronidated with a short half-life.", "sidefx": "Constipation, nausea and sweating come from mu receptor activation. Precipitated withdrawal happens when it is started too soon after full agonists, because it displaces them but activates the receptor less, which is why treatment begins after withdrawal signs appear. Respiratory depression is most dangerous with benzodiazepines or alcohol. Accidental ingestion by children can be fatal. Naloxone can reverse respiratory depression from buprenorphine, but higher or repeated doses and longer infusions may be needed because buprenorphine binds the receptor so tightly.", "src": ["Suboxone (buprenorphine and naloxone) sublingual film FDA label", "Campbell ND, Lovell AM. Ann N Y Acad Sci, 2012 (history of buprenorphine)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "beclomethasone": {"origin": "Beclomethasone dipropionate is a synthetic corticosteroid made by modifying the structure of cortisol so it is very strong on contact but less active once absorbed. In the late 1960s scientists at Glaxo in Britain saw that it could be delivered straight to the airways with a pressurized inhaler. In 1972 a Lancet report showed that inhaled beclomethasone controlled asthma in patients who previously needed oral steroids, and Glaxo launched it as Becotide that year. It was the first widely used inhaled corticosteroid and changed asthma care from treating attacks toward preventing inflammation. In the US it was approved in the 1970s, and the CFC-free QVAR inhaler followed in 2000.", "timeline": [["1972", "Brown and colleagues report in the Lancet that inhaled beclomethasone controls asthma."], ["1972", "Glaxo launches beclomethasone inhaler as Becotide in the UK."], ["1970s", "Beclomethasone inhalers are approved in the US."], ["2000", "QVAR, a CFC-free HFA beclomethasone inhaler, is approved in the US."]], "cell": "Beclomethasone dipropionate is a prodrug. Esterase enzymes in the lung remove one ester to form beclomethasone 17-monopropionate, which binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor with high affinity. The bound receptor sheds its heat shock proteins, moves into the nucleus and changes gene transcription. It switches off inflammatory genes by blocking the transcription factors NF-kB and AP-1, partly by recruiting histone deacetylase 2. This lowers production of cytokines such as IL-4, IL-5 and IL-13, chemokines and adhesion molecules. Fewer eosinophils, mast cells and T cells collect in the airway, mucus production falls and airway hyperresponsiveness decreases. It also increases beta-2 receptor expression, helping bronchodilators work.", "body": [["Airways", "Over days to weeks, lower inflammation reduces swelling, mucus and twitchiness, so asthma symptoms and attacks become less frequent."], ["Mouth and throat", "Steroid deposited in the mouth suppresses local immunity, allowing oral thrush, and can affect the vocal cords, causing hoarseness."], ["Adrenal glands", "At high doses, absorbed steroid can suppress the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis."], ["Growth", "In children, inhaled steroids can slightly reduce growth velocity, so growth is monitored."], ["Eyes", "Long-term use is linked to a higher risk of cataracts and glaucoma."], ["Immune system", "Systemic absorption can make infections such as chickenpox or measles more serious in unexposed people."]], "journey": "From the inhaler, part of the dose deposits in the lungs and part in the mouth and throat, where it can be swallowed. In the lung, esterases convert it to the active 17-monopropionate, which acts locally and then is absorbed into the blood. The swallowed portion is largely inactivated by the liver on first pass, so it contributes little. The liver, partly through CYP3A4, breaks the active form down, and metabolites leave mostly in the feces. The active metabolite's half-life is about three hours, but the anti-inflammatory effect lasts longer because it works through gene changes.", "sidefx": "Oral thrush and hoarseness come from steroid landing in the mouth and throat; rinsing and spitting after use reduces this. Systemic effects such as adrenal suppression, slowed growth, cataracts and glaucoma come from the small amount absorbed, and increase with higher doses. When switching from oral steroids, the adrenal glands may not yet be ready, so the transfer is gradual. It does not relieve acute bronchospasm. Rarely, the inhaler triggers paradoxical bronchospasm. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Inhaled beclomethasone was one of the first treatments to frame asthma as an inflammatory disease to be prevented, not just an airway spasm to be opened.", "src": ["QVAR RediHaler (beclomethasone dipropionate) FDA label", "Brown HM et al. Lancet, 1972", "Barnes PJ. Br J Pharmacol, 2006 (corticosteroid mechanisms)"]}, "umeclidinium": {"origin": "Anticholinergic treatment for breathing problems is very old. In the 1800s, smoking leaves of Datura stramonium (jimsonweed), which contain atropine-like alkaloids, was a popular remedy for asthma, a practice brought to Britain from India. Modern inhaled anticholinergics began with ipratropium in the 1970s and the once-daily tiotropium, approved in the US in 2004. GlaxoSmithKline designed umeclidinium as a long-acting muscarinic antagonist for its Ellipta dry powder inhaler. It was first approved in December 2013 combined with vilanterol as Anoro Ellipta, and on its own as Incruse Ellipta in 2014. It is also part of the triple inhaler Trelegy Ellipta.", "timeline": [["1800s", "Smoking Datura stramonium, which contains atropine-like alkaloids, is used for asthma."], ["1970s", "Ipratropium, a short-acting inhaled anticholinergic, is developed."], ["2013", "Umeclidinium with vilanterol is approved as Anoro Ellipta."], ["2014", "Umeclidinium alone is approved as Incruse Ellipta for COPD."], ["2017", "Trelegy Ellipta combines umeclidinium with fluticasone furoate and vilanterol."]], "cell": "In the airways, vagus nerve fibers release acetylcholine, which acts on M3 muscarinic receptors on bronchial smooth muscle and submucosal glands. M3 receptors couple to Gq, which activates phospholipase C, makes IP3 and releases calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, causing contraction and mucus secretion. In COPD, this cholinergic tone is a major reversible cause of narrowed airways. Umeclidinium is a competitive antagonist at muscarinic receptors M1 through M5. It dissociates slowly from M3, so a single inhalation blocks the receptors for about twenty-four hours. With M3 blocked, airway muscle relaxes, the airways widen and mucus secretion falls.", "body": [["Airways", "Blocking M3 receptors relaxes bronchial muscle, improving airflow and breathlessness in COPD over the full day."], ["Lungs over time", "Steady bronchodilation reduces hyperinflation and COPD flare-ups."], ["Mouth", "Small amounts of absorbed drug reduce saliva, causing dry mouth."], ["Eyes", "Powder reaching the eyes or absorbed drug can dilate pupils and worsen narrow-angle glaucoma."], ["Bladder", "Muscarinic blockade of bladder muscle can worsen urinary retention, especially with prostate enlargement."], ["Heart", "Blocking cardiac muscarinic receptors can modestly speed heart rate."]], "journey": "Umeclidinium is inhaled as a dry powder. Most of the effect happens locally in the airways. Absorbed drug reaches peak plasma levels within five to fifteen minutes, and systemic exposure is low because swallowed drug is poorly absorbed from the gut. It is about eighty-nine percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes absorbed drug mainly by CYP2D6 oxidation, followed by glucuronidation, and it leaves mostly in the feces. The plasma half-life is about eleven hours, but the bronchodilator effect lasts about twenty-four hours because of slow release from M3 receptors.", "sidefx": "Side effects reflect muscarinic blockade beyond the airways: dry mouth, urinary retention and worsening narrow-angle glaucoma, though systemic exposure is low. Nasopharyngitis and cough are common in trials. Paradoxical bronchospasm can rarely occur right after inhalation. Hypersensitivity reactions are possible, and the label lists severe milk protein hypersensitivity as a contraindication because the powder contains lactose. It is not a rescue inhaler and is not indicated for asthma. There is no antidote; severe anticholinergic toxicity from overdose would be treated supportively.", "src": ["Incruse Ellipta (umeclidinium) FDA label", "Barnes PJ. Br J Pharmacol, 2008 (anticholinergics in COPD)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "fluticasone-salmeterol": {"origin": "Both parts of this inhaler were designed by Glaxo in Britain. In the 1980s, chemists at Glaxo's Allen & Hanburys unit wanted a bronchodilator that lasted longer than albuterol, which they had also invented. They attached a long fatty side chain to the albuterol core, creating salmeterol, which anchors near the beta-2 receptor and keeps activating it for about twelve hours. Salmeterol was approved in the US as Serevent in 1994. Glaxo also developed fluticasone propionate, a potent inhaled corticosteroid that is almost completely inactivated if swallowed. Studies showed that adding a long-acting bronchodilator to an inhaled steroid controlled asthma better than raising the steroid dose. The combination was approved in the US in 2000 as Advair Diskus.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Glaxo chemists design salmeterol by adding a long side chain to the albuterol structure."], ["1994", "Salmeterol (Serevent) is approved in the US."], ["1990s", "Fluticasone propionate inhalers are introduced."], ["2000", "FDA approves Advair Diskus (fluticasone-salmeterol)."], ["2017", "FDA removes the asthma-death boxed warning from ICS/LABA combinations after large safety trials."]], "cell": "Fluticasone propionate is a potent glucocorticoid receptor agonist. Inside airway cells, the activated receptor moves to the nucleus and turns off inflammatory genes by blocking NF-kB and AP-1, lowering cytokines, chemokines and the number of eosinophils, mast cells and T cells in the airway. Salmeterol is a selective beta-2 adrenergic agonist. Its long lipophilic tail anchors in the cell membrane near the receptor, so the active head can keep binding the receptor. Beta-2 receptors couple to Gs, which activates adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP and protein kinase A; this lowers intracellular calcium and inactivates myosin light chain kinase, relaxing airway muscle for about twelve hours. The two drugs help each other: steroids increase beta-2 receptor production, and beta-2 agonists help activate the glucocorticoid receptor.", "body": [["Airways", "Fluticasone reduces inflammation, mucus and airway twitchiness over one to two weeks, and salmeterol keeps airways open day and night."], ["Asthma control", "The combination reduces symptoms and attacks better than a higher steroid dose alone in many patients."], ["COPD lungs", "In COPD it reduces flare-ups, but the inhaled steroid increases the risk of pneumonia."], ["Mouth and throat", "Steroid deposited in the mouth can cause thrush and hoarseness."], ["Heart and muscles", "Absorbed salmeterol can stimulate cardiac and skeletal muscle beta receptors, causing palpitations and tremor."], ["Metabolism", "Beta-2 stimulation shifts potassium into cells and raises blood sugar, and long-term steroid exposure can lower bone density."]], "journey": "Both drugs are inhaled as a dry powder or aerosol, act locally and then are absorbed from the lungs. Swallowed fluticasone is almost fully destroyed on first pass through the liver, so nearly all systemic exposure comes from lung absorption. Both are metabolized by CYP3A4 in the liver, and metabolites leave mostly in the feces. Fluticasone is about ninety-nine percent protein bound with a half-life of about eight hours; salmeterol's half-life is about five and a half hours. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors, especially ritonavir, can raise fluticasone levels enough to cause Cushing syndrome.", "sidefx": "Thrush and hoarseness come from local steroid in the mouth; rinsing and spitting reduces this. Pneumonia in COPD is linked to local immune suppression in the lungs. Adrenal suppression, bone loss, cataracts and glaucoma come from absorbed steroid. Tremor, palpitations, low potassium and high blood sugar come from beta-2 stimulation. LABA alone in asthma increased serious events, which is why salmeterol is used with an inhaled steroid. It is not a rescue inhaler. There is no antidote; beta-agonist overdose is treated supportively, sometimes with a cardioselective beta blocker with caution.", "src": ["Advair Diskus (fluticasone propionate and salmeterol) FDA label", "Barnes PJ. Br J Pharmacol, 2006", "FDA drug safety communication on LABA boxed warning removal, 2017"]}, "budesonide-formoterol": {"origin": "Budesonide was developed by the Swedish company Astra Draco in Lund in the 1970s as an inhaled corticosteroid that is very active in the airways but largely destroyed by the liver once swallowed. Formoterol was created in Japan by Yamanouchi Pharmaceutical as a long-acting beta-2 agonist with an unusual property: it starts working within minutes, about as fast as albuterol. AstraZeneca combined them in one inhaler, Symbicort, which was approved in Europe around 2000 and in the US in 2006 for asthma and in 2009 for COPD. Because formoterol acts quickly, the combination can also be used as both daily controller and reliever in some international guidelines, an approach called MART or SMART.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Astra Draco develops budesonide in Sweden."], ["1970s", "Yamanouchi develops formoterol in Japan."], ["2000", "Budesonide-formoterol is launched in Europe as Symbicort."], ["2006", "FDA approves Symbicort for asthma."], ["2009", "FDA approves Symbicort for COPD."]], "cell": "Budesonide binds the glucocorticoid receptor in airway cells. The receptor complex enters the nucleus and suppresses inflammatory gene transcription by interfering with NF-kB and AP-1, while also turning on anti-inflammatory genes. This lowers cytokines such as IL-4, IL-5 and IL-13, reduces eosinophils and mast cells in the airway wall and lowers mucus production and hyperresponsiveness. Formoterol is a selective beta-2 adrenergic agonist. Beta-2 receptors are Gs-coupled, so binding activates adenylyl cyclase, raises cAMP and activates protein kinase A, which lowers calcium and relaxes bronchial smooth muscle. Formoterol is moderately lipophilic: it reaches the receptor quickly from the watery surface while a reservoir in the membrane keeps acting for about twelve hours.", "body": [["Airways", "Formoterol opens airways within about one to three minutes and keeps them open for about twelve hours, while budesonide reduces inflammation over one to two weeks."], ["Asthma control", "Lower airway inflammation reduces symptoms, nighttime waking and attacks."], ["COPD lungs", "It improves airflow and reduces flare-ups, but inhaled steroid raises pneumonia risk in COPD."], ["Mouth and throat", "Local steroid can cause thrush and hoarseness."], ["Heart and muscles", "Absorbed formoterol can cause palpitations, tachycardia and tremor."], ["Metabolism", "Beta-2 stimulation can lower potassium and raise blood sugar."]], "journey": "The metered-dose inhaler delivers both drugs to the airways, where they act locally and are absorbed. Budesonide reaches peak plasma levels about twenty minutes after inhalation. Swallowed budesonide is about ninety percent removed by CYP3A4 in the liver on its first pass, so little of it reaches the blood. Its half-life is about five hours. Formoterol peaks in about ten minutes and is cleared by glucuronidation and O-demethylation, with a half-life of about eight to ten hours. Metabolites leave in urine and feces.", "sidefx": "Thrush and hoarseness come from steroid in the mouth and throat; rinsing without swallowing helps. Absorbed steroid at high doses can cause adrenal suppression, bone loss, cataracts and glaucoma. Pneumonia risk in COPD reflects local immune suppression. Palpitations, tremor, low potassium and high blood sugar come from beta-2 stimulation. LABA alone in asthma increased serious events, so formoterol is paired with an inhaled steroid. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors can raise budesonide levels. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Since 2019, the Global Initiative for Asthma has preferred as-needed low-dose budesonide-formoterol over albuterol alone for mild asthma in adults and adolescents, a major shift from decades of reliever-only care.", "src": ["Symbicort (budesonide and formoterol) FDA label", "Global Initiative for Asthma (GINA) report, 2019", "Barnes PJ. Br J Pharmacol, 2006"]}, "ensifentrine": {"origin": "Ensifentrine was developed by the British company Verona Pharma, which built on earlier academic work on molecules that block two phosphodiesterase enzymes at once. The idea was to combine the airway-relaxing effect of PDE3 inhibition with the anti-inflammatory effect of PDE4 inhibition in a single inhaled drug, delivering it straight to the lungs to limit the body-wide side effects of oral PDE inhibitors. Originally called RPL554, it was tested as a nebulized solution in large phase 3 trials in COPD. The FDA approved it as Ohtuvayre in June 2024, the first new inhaled mechanism for COPD maintenance in over twenty years.", "timeline": [["2010s", "Verona Pharma develops ensifentrine, then called RPL554, as a dual PDE3 and PDE4 inhibitor."], ["2024", "FDA approves Ohtuvayre for maintenance treatment of COPD in adults."]], "cell": "Ensifentrine selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase 3 and phosphodiesterase 4, enzymes that break down cyclic AMP. PDE3 is abundant in airway smooth muscle. Blocking it lets cAMP build up, which activates protein kinase A, lowers intracellular calcium and relaxes the muscle, opening the airways. PDE4 is the main cAMP-degrading enzyme in inflammatory cells such as neutrophils, eosinophils, macrophages and T cells. Higher cAMP in these cells reduces release of cytokines, chemokines and other inflammatory mediators. Raised cAMP in airway epithelium may also activate CFTR and improve ciliary beating, which could help mucus clearance. Ensifentrine thus combines a bronchodilator and an anti-inflammatory action in one molecule.", "body": [["Airways", "PDE3 inhibition relaxes airway smooth muscle, improving lung function within about an hour and over a twelve-hour dosing interval."], ["Airway inflammation", "PDE4 inhibition in inflammatory cells reduces neutrophil and eosinophil activity, which may lower COPD flare-ups."], ["Mucus", "Raised cAMP in epithelial cells may improve mucociliary clearance."], ["Whole body", "Because it is inhaled, blood levels are low, so the nausea, weight loss and diarrhea typical of oral PDE4 inhibitors are uncommon."], ["Mood", "PDE4 inhibitors as a class carry a warning for psychiatric effects, including suicidality, so mood changes are watched."]], "journey": "Ensifentrine is given as a suspension through a standard jet nebulizer and mouthpiece, twice daily. It acts directly in the airways, and a small amount is absorbed into the blood, peaking about an hour after a dose. Absorbed drug is broken down in the liver by oxidative metabolism and leaves mostly in the feces. The plasma half-life is about seven to ten hours.", "sidefx": "Because only a small amount reaches the blood, side effects are mostly mild: back pain, high blood pressure, urinary tract infection and diarrhea were reported in trials. Like any inhaled drug it can rarely trigger paradoxical bronchospasm. Psychiatric effects including suicidality are a warning for PDE4 inhibitors as a class. It is not a rescue treatment for sudden breathing trouble. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Ohtuvayre (ensifentrine) FDA label", "Anzueto A et al. Am J Respir Crit Care Med, 2023 (ENHANCE trials)"]}, "roflumilast": {"origin": "Roflumilast was discovered by the German company Byk Gulden, later part of Altana and then Nycomed, during research into phosphodiesterase 4 inhibitors. Older drugs such as theophylline weakly block several phosphodiesterases, and researchers hoped a selective PDE4 inhibitor would give anti-inflammatory benefit in COPD. Several early PDE4 inhibitors failed because of severe nausea. Roflumilast was tolerable enough at its effective dose, and trials showed it reduced flare-ups in severe COPD with chronic bronchitis. It was approved in Europe in 2010 and by the FDA in 2011 as Daliresp. A cream and foam form, Zoryve, was later approved for skin conditions such as psoriasis.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Byk Gulden researchers develop roflumilast in Germany."], ["2010", "Roflumilast is approved in the European Union."], ["2011", "FDA approves Daliresp to reduce COPD exacerbations."], ["2022", "Topical roflumilast (Zoryve) is approved for plaque psoriasis."]], "cell": "Roflumilast and its active metabolite, roflumilast N-oxide, selectively inhibit phosphodiesterase 4, the main enzyme that breaks down cyclic AMP in neutrophils, eosinophils, macrophages, T cells and airway epithelial cells. Blocking PDE4 raises intracellular cAMP, which activates protein kinase A and other effectors. In inflammatory cells this reduces release of TNF-alpha, IL-8, leukotriene B4 and reactive oxygen species, and slows neutrophil recruitment. In COPD with chronic bronchitis, neutrophil-driven inflammation drives mucus overproduction and flare-ups, so dampening it lowers exacerbation risk. Roflumilast is not a bronchodilator and does not relieve acute symptoms.", "body": [["Airway inflammation", "Lower neutrophil and cytokine activity reduces COPD flare-ups over weeks to months."], ["Gut", "PDE4 inhibition in the gut and in the brainstem vomiting center causes diarrhea, nausea and decreased appetite, especially early."], ["Weight", "Weight loss is common, from reduced appetite and possibly metabolic effects of raised cAMP."], ["Brain", "Headache and insomnia are common, and depression, anxiety and suicidal thoughts have been reported."], ["Skin", "As a cream, the same anti-inflammatory action reduces psoriasis plaques with little systemic exposure."]], "journey": "Roflumilast is well absorbed by mouth, with about eighty percent bioavailability, and peaks in about an hour. The liver converts it through CYP3A4 and CYP1A2 to roflumilast N-oxide, which is also active and provides most of the total PDE4 inhibition. The N-oxide peaks around eight hours and lasts much longer: its half-life is about thirty hours, compared with about seventeen for the parent. Both are highly protein bound. About seventy percent leaves in the urine as metabolites. Strong CYP inducers such as rifampin lower levels, and inhibitors raise them.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea, nausea, appetite loss and weight loss come from PDE4 inhibition in the gut and in brain areas that control nausea and appetite. Insomnia, anxiety, depression and suicidal thoughts reflect PDE4 effects on brain signaling and are a labeled warning. It is not a bronchodilator and not for acute bronchospasm. A lower starting dose for a few weeks can reduce early side effects, though it is not therapeutic. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Daliresp (roflumilast) FDA label", "Calverley PM et al. Lancet, 2009", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "mepolizumab": {"origin": "Mepolizumab was developed by GlaxoSmithKline as a humanized antibody against interleukin-5, the cytokine that controls eosinophils. Its first asthma study, published in 2000, was disappointing: it nearly removed eosinophils from the blood but did not improve asthma in a general group of patients. Researchers then realized that asthma has subtypes. In patients chosen because they had high eosinophils and frequent attacks, later trials such as DREAM in 2012 showed fewer exacerbations. The FDA approved it as Nucala in November 2015 as the first anti-IL-5 drug for severe eosinophilic asthma. Approvals followed for eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis, hypereosinophilic syndrome, nasal polyps and eosinophilic COPD.", "timeline": [["2000", "An early trial shows mepolizumab lowers eosinophils but not overall asthma symptoms."], ["2012", "The DREAM trial shows fewer attacks in patients selected for eosinophilic asthma."], ["2015", "FDA approves Nucala for severe eosinophilic asthma."], ["2017", "Mepolizumab becomes the first approved treatment for EGPA."]], "cell": "Interleukin-5 is made mainly by Th2 cells and type 2 innate lymphoid cells. It binds the IL-5 receptor, made of an alpha chain (IL-5Ralpha, CD125) and a shared beta chain, on eosinophils and their precursors in the bone marrow. IL-5 signaling through JAK2 and STAT5 drives eosinophil production, maturation, activation and survival. Mepolizumab is a humanized IgG1 kappa antibody that binds free IL-5 with high affinity and prevents it from attaching to IL-5Ralpha. Without IL-5, fewer eosinophils are made and existing ones undergo apoptosis, so blood and sputum eosinophil counts fall. Fewer eosinophils in the airways means less release of toxic granule proteins and leukotrienes that damage tissue and trigger attacks.", "body": [["Blood", "Blood eosinophil counts fall within days and stay low with regular dosing."], ["Airways", "Fewer eosinophils in the airway wall reduce inflammation, leading to fewer asthma attacks and less need for oral steroids."], ["Nose and sinuses", "Lower eosinophilic inflammation shrinks nasal polyps in chronic rhinosinusitis."], ["Blood vessels", "In EGPA, reducing eosinophils helps control the vasculitis and allows lower steroid doses."], ["Immune system", "Eosinophils help fight parasites, so worm infections are treated before starting; herpes zoster has been reported."], ["Injection site", "Local pain, redness and swelling can occur after the injection."]], "journey": "Mepolizumab is injected under the skin, usually every four weeks. As a large protein it is absorbed slowly through the lymphatic system, reaching peak blood levels about four to eight days after a dose. It stays mostly in the blood and extracellular fluid. Like other antibodies, it is broken down by proteolysis into amino acids throughout the body rather than by liver enzymes or the kidneys. Its half-life is about sixteen to twenty-two days.", "sidefx": "Headache, back pain, fatigue and injection site reactions are common. Hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis, angioedema and rash, can occur hours to days after a dose because it is a foreign protein. Herpes zoster has been reported, possibly linked to immune changes. Because eosinophils defend against parasites, worm infections may worsen. Mepolizumab does not relieve acute bronchospasm, and corticosteroids are tapered gradually rather than stopped abruptly. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Nucala (mepolizumab) FDA label", "Pavord ID et al. Lancet, 2012 (DREAM)", "Leckie MJ et al. Lancet, 2000"]}, "benralizumab": {"origin": "Benralizumab was developed by MedImmune, part of AstraZeneca, using an antibody design trick from the Japanese company Kyowa Hakko Kirin. Antibodies normally carry a sugar called fucose on their Fc region. Removing it, a process called afucosylation, makes the antibody bind natural killer cells much more strongly. Instead of just blocking IL-5 like mepolizumab, benralizumab targets the IL-5 receptor on eosinophils and flags them for destruction, nearly emptying the blood of eosinophils within a day. The FDA approved it as Fasenra in November 2017 for severe eosinophilic asthma, and in 2024 for eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Kyowa Hakko Kirin develops afucosylation technology to strengthen antibody cell killing."], ["2017", "FDA approves Fasenra for severe eosinophilic asthma."], ["2024", "FDA approves benralizumab for eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis."]], "cell": "Benralizumab is a humanized afucosylated IgG1 kappa antibody that binds the alpha subunit of the IL-5 receptor (IL-5Ralpha, CD125) on eosinophils and basophils. This blocks IL-5 signaling, but its main power comes from the Fc region. Without fucose, the Fc binds the activating receptor FcgammaRIIIa on natural killer cells with very high affinity. The NK cell then releases perforin and granzymes onto the antibody-coated eosinophil, killing it by antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity (ADCC). This depletes eosinophils in the blood, bone marrow and airway tissue, rather than just slowing their production, so fewer cells remain to release cytotoxic granule proteins and drive airway inflammation.", "body": [["Blood", "Eosinophils drop to near zero within about twenty-four hours of the first dose."], ["Airways", "Removing eosinophils from the airway wall reduces attacks, improves lung function and lowers oral steroid needs in severe eosinophilic asthma."], ["Blood vessels", "In EGPA, eosinophil depletion helps control vasculitis and allows steroid reduction."], ["Immune system", "Because eosinophils help fight parasites, worm infections are treated before therapy."], ["Injection site", "Pain, redness or swelling can follow the injection; headache and sore throat are also common."]], "journey": "Benralizumab is injected under the skin. After the first few doses every four weeks, it is given every eight weeks. As a large protein it is absorbed slowly through lymphatics, with peak levels about four to seven days after an injection. It is cleared by proteolytic breakdown like other IgG antibodies, not by the liver or kidneys. Its half-life is about fifteen days, but its effect lasts longer because depleted eosinophils take time to regrow.", "sidefx": "Headache, sore throat and injection site reactions are common. Hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis, angioedema and rash, can occur because it is a foreign protein, usually within hours but sometimes later. Parasitic infections may be harder to fight without eosinophils. It does not treat acute bronchospasm or status asthmaticus, and steroid doses are lowered gradually. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Benralizumab can lower blood eosinophil counts to undetectable levels within a day, one of the most dramatic cell-depleting effects of any asthma drug.", "src": ["Fasenra (benralizumab) FDA label", "Bleecker ER et al. Lancet, 2016 (SIROCCO)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "dupilumab": {"origin": "Dupilumab was developed by the US company Regeneron with Sanofi. Regeneron made it using VelocImmune mice, which are engineered so their immune systems make fully human antibodies. Researchers chose to block IL-4 receptor alpha, a receptor part shared by the cytokines IL-4 and IL-13, which both drive type 2 or allergic inflammation. Blocking one shared target shut down both signals at once. The FDA approved it as Dupixent in March 2017 for moderate to severe atopic dermatitis, the first biologic for eczema. Approvals followed for asthma in 2018, nasal polyps in 2019, eosinophilic esophagitis and prurigo nodularis in 2022, eosinophilic COPD in 2024 and chronic spontaneous urticaria.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Regeneron develops VelocImmune mice that produce fully human antibodies."], ["2017", "FDA approves Dupixent for moderate to severe atopic dermatitis."], ["2018", "Dupilumab is approved for moderate to severe asthma."], ["2022", "Dupilumab is approved for eosinophilic esophagitis and prurigo nodularis."], ["2024", "Dupilumab becomes the first biologic approved for COPD with type 2 inflammation."]], "cell": "IL-4 and IL-13 are key type 2 cytokines. IL-4 signals through two receptors: the type I receptor, made of IL-4Ralpha with the common gamma chain, found mostly on immune cells, and the type II receptor, made of IL-4Ralpha with IL-13Ralpha1, found on structural cells such as epithelium and smooth muscle. IL-13 uses only the type II receptor. Dupilumab is a fully human IgG4 antibody that binds IL-4Ralpha, so it blocks both receptor types and thus both cytokines. This stops JAK and STAT6 signaling. Downstream, B cells make less IgE, Th2 cells expand less, epithelial cells make less eotaxin, mucus and periostin, nitric oxide in exhaled breath (FeNO) falls, and the skin barrier recovers.", "body": [["Skin", "Blocking IL-4 and IL-13 restores the skin barrier and reduces itch and inflammation in eczema, often within weeks."], ["Airways", "Lower type 2 inflammation reduces asthma attacks, mucus and FeNO and allows lower steroid doses."], ["Nose and sinuses", "Nasal polyps shrink and sense of smell can return."], ["Esophagus", "Reduced eosinophilic inflammation eases swallowing problems in eosinophilic esophagitis."], ["Eyes", "Conjunctivitis and keratitis are common, especially in atopic dermatitis, possibly from changes in eye-surface mucus and goblet cells."], ["Blood", "Blood eosinophils can rise temporarily because their movement into tissues is blocked."]], "journey": "Dupilumab is injected under the skin, usually every two weeks after a loading dose. It is absorbed slowly through lymphatics, reaching peak levels in about a week. Like other antibodies it is broken down by proteolysis. Part of its clearance happens through binding to its target, so elimination is nonlinear; at lower levels it is cleared faster. After the last dose, levels fall below detection in about ten to thirteen weeks.", "sidefx": "Injection site reactions and conjunctivitis or keratitis are the most common problems; eye effects may relate to changes in eye-surface goblet cells when IL-13 is blocked. Blood eosinophilia can occur because eosinophils stay in the blood instead of entering tissue. Rarely, eosinophilic pneumonia or vasculitis appears as oral steroids are tapered, which may unmask EGPA. Hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis and serum sickness-like reactions, can occur. Live vaccines are avoided. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Dupixent (dupilumab) FDA label", "Simpson EL et al. N Engl J Med, 2016", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "pirfenidone": {"origin": "Pirfenidone is a small synthetic pyridone molecule first made in the 1970s and studied as an anti-inflammatory and pain drug. Researchers later found it reduced scarring (fibrosis) in animal models of lung, liver and kidney disease. It was first approved for idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis in Japan in 2008. The US company InterMune ran large trials, and after mixed early results the ASCEND trial confirmed that it slowed lung function decline. The FDA approved it as Esbriet on October 15, 2014, the same day it approved nintedanib, giving people with IPF their first two approved drugs. Roche bought InterMune in 2014.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Pirfenidone is first synthesized and studied as an anti-inflammatory compound."], ["2008", "Pirfenidone is approved for IPF in Japan."], ["2011", "Pirfenidone is approved in the European Union."], ["2014", "FDA approves Esbriet for IPF on the same day as nintedanib."]], "cell": "The exact mechanism of pirfenidone is not fully known. In idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, repeated injury to alveolar epithelium leads to release of transforming growth factor beta 1 (TGF-beta1) and other signals that activate fibroblasts. These fibroblasts multiply, turn into myofibroblasts and lay down excess collagen and extracellular matrix, stiffening the lungs. Pirfenidone lowers TGF-beta1 production and its downstream SMAD signaling, reduces TNF-alpha and other inflammatory and profibrotic cytokines, and inhibits fibroblast proliferation and differentiation into myofibroblasts. It also has antioxidant effects. The result is less new collagen deposition and slower scarring, though it does not reverse existing fibrosis.", "body": [["Lungs", "Slower scarring means forced vital capacity (FVC) declines more slowly over months, preserving lung function longer."], ["Gut", "Nausea, indigestion, poor appetite and weight loss are common; taking it with food reduces these."], ["Skin", "Photosensitivity and rash are common, so sun protection is advised."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise and rare severe liver injury has occurred, so liver tests are monitored."], ["Energy", "Fatigue and dizziness are reported, adding to the tiredness many people with IPF already feel."]], "journey": "Pirfenidone is taken by mouth three times daily with food. Fasting, it peaks in about half an hour to an hour; food delays the peak to about three hours and lowers peak levels, which improves tolerance. About half is bound to plasma proteins. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP1A2 to 5-carboxy-pirfenidone, which leaves in the urine. The half-life is short, about two and a half to three hours. Smoking induces CYP1A2 and lowers levels, while strong CYP1A2 inhibitors such as fluvoxamine raise them sharply.", "sidefx": "Gut side effects relate to direct irritation and central effects and are lessened by food. Photosensitivity likely reflects the drug or metabolites absorbing light in the skin and causing phototoxic reactions. Liver enzyme rises and rare fatal liver injury explain the monitoring schedule. Severe skin reactions such as SJS, TEN and DRESS are rare. Strong CYP1A2 inhibitors can greatly raise exposure. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Esbriet (pirfenidone) FDA label", "King TE Jr et al. N Engl J Med, 2014 (ASCEND)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "nintedanib": {"origin": "Nintedanib was developed by the German company Boehringer Ingelheim, first as a cancer drug. It was designed as BIBF 1120 to block blood vessel growth signals that tumors use. Researchers noticed that the same growth factor receptors (for VEGF, FGF and PDGF) also drive fibroblasts in lung scarring. Trials in idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, the INPULSIS trials, showed it slowed the loss of lung function. The FDA approved it as Ofev on October 15, 2014, the same day as pirfenidone. Approvals followed for systemic sclerosis-associated interstitial lung disease in 2019 and for progressive fibrosing interstitial lung diseases in 2020. In Europe it is also used with chemotherapy for some lung cancers under the name Vargatef.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Boehringer Ingelheim develops nintedanib as an angiogenesis inhibitor for cancer."], ["2014", "FDA approves Ofev for IPF on the same day as pirfenidone."], ["2014", "Nintedanib (Vargatef) is approved in Europe for some lung cancers."], ["2019", "FDA approves nintedanib for systemic sclerosis-associated ILD."], ["2020", "FDA approves nintedanib for progressive fibrosing ILDs."]], "cell": "Nintedanib is a small molecule that enters cells and competes with ATP at the kinase pocket of several receptor tyrosine kinases: VEGFR 1 to 3, FGFR 1 to 3, and PDGFR alpha and beta. It also inhibits non-receptor kinases such as Src, Lck and Lyn, and FLT3. Normally, growth factor binding causes these receptors to pair up and phosphorylate themselves, activating downstream pathways such as MAPK and PI3K-Akt. By blocking this, nintedanib reduces fibroblast proliferation, migration and transformation into collagen-producing myofibroblasts, slowing scar formation in the lung. Blocking VEGF signaling in blood vessels explains its effects on bleeding and wound healing.", "body": [["Lungs", "Slower fibroblast activity slows the decline of forced vital capacity in IPF and other progressive fibrosing lung diseases."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is very common, along with nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain and appetite loss."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise, so liver tests are checked before and during treatment."], ["Blood vessels", "VEGF blockade raises the risk of bleeding, arterial clots such as heart attack, and impaired wound healing."], ["Gut wall", "Rarely, gastrointestinal perforation can occur."], ["Pregnancy", "It can harm a developing fetus, so pregnancy testing and contraception are advised."]], "journey": "Nintedanib is taken by mouth twice daily with food as a soft capsule. Only a small fraction reaches the blood, about five percent, because of first-pass metabolism and P-glycoprotein efflux in the gut. Peak levels come in two to four hours. It is about ninety-eight percent protein bound. Esterases cleave it to the free acid BIBF 1202, which is then glucuronidated by UGT1A enzymes; CYP3A4 plays only a minor role. About ninety-three percent leaves through bile and feces. The half-life is about ten to fifteen hours.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea, the most common side effect, likely reflects kinase inhibition in the intestinal lining and is managed with fluids and loperamide. Liver enzyme rises are a known effect, which is why liver tests are monitored. VEGF blockade explains bleeding, arterial thromboembolic events, impaired wound healing and rare bowel perforation. Growth factor signaling is essential in fetal development, which explains embryo-fetal toxicity. Strong P-gp and CYP3A4 inhibitors can raise exposure. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Ofev (nintedanib) FDA label", "Richeldi L et al. N Engl J Med, 2014 (INPULSIS)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "ivacaftor": {"origin": "Ivacaftor was the first drug to treat the underlying cause of cystic fibrosis rather than its symptoms. The story begins in 1989, when teams led by Lap-Chee Tsui, John Riordan and Francis Collins identified the CFTR gene. In the late 1990s the Cystic Fibrosis Foundation funded the biotech company Aurora Biosciences, later bought by Vertex Pharmaceuticals, to screen hundreds of thousands of compounds for ones that could fix faulty CFTR channels. This early venture philanthropy model was unusual for a patient charity. Vertex found ivacaftor, which helps channels that reach the cell surface but fail to open. The FDA approved it as Kalydeco in January 2012 for patients with the G551D mutation, and later for many other responsive mutations.", "timeline": [["1989", "Tsui, Riordan and Collins identify the CFTR gene."], ["1990s", "The Cystic Fibrosis Foundation begins funding Aurora Biosciences (later Vertex) to search for CFTR modulators."], ["2011", "The STRIVE trial shows major lung function gains with ivacaftor in G551D patients."], ["2012", "FDA approves Kalydeco for cystic fibrosis with the G551D mutation."]], "cell": "CFTR is a chloride and bicarbonate channel in the apical membrane of epithelial cells in the airways, pancreas, gut and sweat glands. Its opening is controlled by phosphorylation of its regulatory domain by protein kinase A and by ATP binding at its nucleotide-binding domains. Class III gating mutations such as G551D make a protein that reaches the cell surface but rarely opens. Ivacaftor is a CFTR potentiator: it binds the CFTR protein in the membrane and increases the channel's open probability, letting more chloride and bicarbonate pass. More chloride secretion draws sodium and water into the airway surface liquid, so mucus becomes thinner and easier to clear. Ivacaftor does not fix misfolded protein such as F508del, which needs correctors.", "body": [["Lungs", "Better hydrated airway mucus improves clearance, raising FEV1 and lowering pulmonary exacerbations."], ["Sweat glands", "Restored CFTR function lets sweat ducts reabsorb chloride again, so sweat chloride falls sharply within two weeks."], ["Nutrition", "Improved gut and pancreatic secretions help weight gain and nutrient absorption."], ["Liver", "Transaminase levels can rise, so liver tests are monitored."], ["Eyes", "Non-congenital lens opacities (cataracts) have been seen in children, so eye exams are done."], ["Nose and airways", "Headache, upper respiratory infection and nasal congestion are common side effects."]], "journey": "Ivacaftor is taken by mouth every twelve hours with fat-containing food, which increases absorption about two to four times. Peak levels come about four hours after a dose. It is about ninety-nine percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it extensively through CYP3A to an active metabolite, M1, and an inactive one, M6. Most leaves in the feces. The half-life is about twelve hours. Strong CYP3A inducers such as rifampin greatly lower levels and are avoided, and strong inhibitors raise levels, requiring dose changes.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are mild: headache, upper respiratory infections, abdominal pain and rash. Transaminase elevation reflects hepatic stress during heavy liver metabolism, which is why liver tests are checked. Cataracts in children have been reported, though the mechanism is unclear. Strong CYP3A inducers can make it ineffective. Hypersensitivity reactions are rare. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The Cystic Fibrosis Foundation's investment in the research behind ivacaftor later earned it billions of dollars in royalties, which it put back into further CF research.", "src": ["Kalydeco (ivacaftor) FDA label", "Ramsey BW et al. N Engl J Med, 2011", "Riordan JR et al. Science, 1989"]}, "elexacaftor-tezacaftor-ivacaftor": {"origin": "This triple combination grew from the same Cystic Fibrosis Foundation and Vertex partnership that produced ivacaftor. About nine in ten people with cystic fibrosis carry at least one copy of F508del, a mutation that makes CFTR protein fold wrongly so it is destroyed before reaching the cell surface. A potentiator alone cannot help a channel that never arrives. Vertex developed correctors that help the protein fold: lumacaftor, then tezacaftor, and finally the next-generation corrector elexacaftor. Combining two correctors at different sites with ivacaftor rescued F508del protein much better than any earlier option. The FDA approved it as Trikafta in October 2019, months ahead of schedule, making effective modulator therapy available to most people with CF.", "timeline": [["1989", "The CFTR gene and the F508del mutation are identified."], ["2012", "Ivacaftor, the potentiator part of the combination, is approved."], ["2018", "Tezacaftor-ivacaftor is approved."], ["2019", "FDA approves Trikafta for patients with at least one F508del mutation."]], "cell": "In F508del cystic fibrosis, the CFTR protein misfolds in the endoplasmic reticulum, is recognized by quality control and is sent for degradation, so little reaches the apical membrane. Elexacaftor and tezacaftor are CFTR correctors that bind different sites on the protein and stabilize its folding and domain assembly. Working together, they allow much more CFTR to complete processing and travel to the cell surface. Ivacaftor, the potentiator, then binds the channel at the membrane and increases its open probability. With more channels present and each opening more often, chloride and bicarbonate secretion rises, the airway surface liquid rehydrates and mucus becomes easier to clear.", "body": [["Lungs", "Improved CFTR function raises FEV1 substantially and reduces pulmonary exacerbations."], ["Sweat glands", "Sweat chloride falls sharply within weeks, a direct marker of restored CFTR function."], ["Nutrition", "Weight and body mass index improve, and some people have better pancreatic and gut function."], ["Sinuses", "Sinus disease and sense of smell can improve."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes and bilirubin can rise, and rare cases of serious liver injury and liver failure have occurred."], ["Eyes", "Cataracts have been seen in children taking CFTR modulators."]], "journey": "The combination is taken by mouth as tablets, with ivacaftor alone taken twelve hours later, all with fat-containing food to improve absorption. Elexacaftor peaks in about four to six hours, tezacaftor and ivacaftor in about three to four hours. All three are more than ninety-nine percent protein bound. They are metabolized in the liver mainly by CYP3A4 and CYP3A5, and leave mostly in the feces. Half-lives are about twenty-seven hours for elexacaftor, twenty-five for tezacaftor and fifteen for ivacaftor. Strong CYP3A inducers are avoided because they lower levels sharply.", "sidefx": "Headache, upper respiratory infections, abdominal pain, diarrhea and rash are common. Liver injury is the most serious risk: a boxed warning describes drug-induced liver injury and liver failure, including cases needing transplant, so liver tests are checked often, especially early. Creatine kinase can rise. Cataracts have been reported in children. CYP3A inducers can make it ineffective, and strong inhibitors require dose changes. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Trikafta (elexacaftor, tezacaftor and ivacaftor) FDA label", "Middleton PG et al. N Engl J Med, 2019", "Heijerman HGM et al. Lancet, 2019"]}, "dornase-alfa": {"origin": "Thick cystic fibrosis mucus is loaded with DNA released by dying neutrophils, which makes it sticky and hard to cough up. In the 1950s doctors tried enzymes from cow pancreas to break this DNA down, but allergic reactions limited them. In 1990 a Genentech team led by Steven Shak cloned human deoxyribonuclease I and showed that the recombinant enzyme quickly thinned CF sputum in the test tube. Genentech developed it for inhalation, and the FDA approved it as Pulmozyme in December 1993. It was one of the first biotech drugs made specifically for cystic fibrosis and remains a standard daily therapy.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Bovine pancreatic DNase is tried to thin sputum but causes reactions."], ["1990", "Steven Shak and colleagues at Genentech clone human DNase I and show it thins CF sputum."], ["1993", "FDA approves Pulmozyme (dornase alfa) for cystic fibrosis."]], "cell": "In cystic fibrosis airways, chronic infection draws in large numbers of neutrophils. As they die, they release long strands of DNA, sometimes in netlike structures, which tangle with mucins and make sputum highly viscous and elastic. Dornase alfa is recombinant human deoxyribonuclease I, an enzyme identical to the natural human one. It hydrolyzes the phosphodiester bonds of extracellular DNA, cutting the long strands into small fragments. This lowers sputum viscosity and elasticity, so cilia and coughing can clear mucus more easily. Clearer airways reduce infection and inflammation over time. It acts outside cells and does not affect DNA inside living cells.", "body": [["Airways and lungs", "Thinner mucus is easier to clear, which improves FEV1 and reduces respiratory infections needing IV antibiotics."], ["Voice and throat", "Voice changes, hoarseness, sore throat and laryngitis are the most common side effects, from local effects of the aerosol."], ["Eyes and skin", "Conjunctivitis and rash have been reported."], ["Chest", "Chest pain and temporary breathlessness or lower lung function can occur early in treatment."]], "journey": "Dornase alfa is inhaled through a labeled jet nebulizer and compressor system or the eRapid nebulizer system. It deposits on airway surfaces and works within the mucus. Sputum enzyme levels peak about fifteen minutes after inhalation and decline over hours. Very little reaches the bloodstream, and any absorbed enzyme is broken down by proteolysis like other proteins. Benefit builds with regular daily use.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are local to the airway: voice change, pharyngitis, laryngitis and chest discomfort. These are thought to be irritation from the aerosol and the loosening of mucus, not systemic effects, because little drug is absorbed. Rash and conjunctivitis occur. Because it is a human protein, serious allergic reactions are uncommon but possible. It is not mixed with other drugs in the nebulizer because they can inactivate it. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Pulmozyme (dornase alfa) FDA label", "Shak S et al. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA, 1990", "Fuchs HJ et al. N Engl J Med, 1994"]}, "sotatercept": {"origin": "Sotatercept was first developed by the US biotech Acceleron Pharma to treat bone loss and anemia. While testing it in healthy volunteers and patients, researchers noticed it raised red blood cell counts, which led to its use as a blood drug. Scientists also knew that pulmonary arterial hypertension is linked to mutations in the BMPR2 gene, which normally restrains cell growth in lung artery walls. Sotatercept traps activin and related signals that push the other way, so it was tested in PAH. Merck acquired Acceleron in 2021, and after the STELLAR trial showed improved exercise capacity and fewer worsening events, the FDA approved it as Winrevair in March 2024, the first activin signaling inhibitor for PAH.", "timeline": [["2000", "Mutations in BMPR2 are linked to heritable pulmonary arterial hypertension."], ["2000s", "Acceleron Pharma develops sotatercept, first for bone and blood disorders."], ["2021", "Merck acquires Acceleron."], ["2024", "FDA approves Winrevair for pulmonary arterial hypertension."]], "cell": "In pulmonary arterial hypertension, the walls of small lung arteries thicken as endothelial cells, smooth muscle and fibroblasts overgrow, narrowing the vessels. Growth in these cells is balanced by two arms of the TGF-beta superfamily. BMPR2 signaling through SMAD1, SMAD5 and SMAD8 restrains growth, while activin and growth differentiation factors signaling through activin receptors and SMAD2 and SMAD3 promote it. In PAH, the BMPR2 arm is weakened. Sotatercept is a fusion protein of the outer part of the activin receptor type IIA linked to an IgG1 Fc. It acts as a ligand trap, binding activin A, GDF8, GDF11 and related ligands before they reach their receptors. This shifts the balance back toward growth restraint, reducing vascular remodeling and lowering pulmonary vascular resistance.", "body": [["Lung arteries", "Less vessel wall overgrowth lowers pulmonary vascular resistance and pressure."], ["Heart", "Lower pressure in the lung circulation eases the strain on the right ventricle."], ["Exercise capacity", "Six-minute walk distance and functional class improve over weeks to months."], ["Blood", "Trapping these ligands also boosts red blood cell production, raising hemoglobin, and it can lower platelet counts."], ["Bleeding", "Serious bleeding, including gastrointestinal and brain hemorrhage, has occurred, especially with low platelets or other risks."], ["Skin and nose", "Telangiectasias (small dilated blood vessels) and nosebleeds are common."]], "journey": "Sotatercept is injected under the skin every three weeks. As a large protein it is absorbed slowly, with peak levels about a week after a dose. It is broken down by proteolytic catabolism like other proteins, not by liver enzymes or the kidneys. Its half-life is about three weeks, which supports the every three week schedule.", "sidefx": "Activin and GDF11 also regulate red cell maturation, so trapping them raises hemoglobin, sometimes too much (erythrocytosis). Platelet counts can fall, and together with vessel changes this raises bleeding risk. Telangiectasias and nosebleeds may reflect changes in small vessel growth. Because these ligands matter in development and reproduction, it may harm a fetus and impair fertility. Hemoglobin and platelets are checked before doses, and doses are delayed or reduced as needed. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Winrevair (sotatercept-csrk) FDA label", "Hoeper MM et al. N Engl J Med, 2023 (STELLAR)", "Lane KB et al. Nat Genet, 2000 (BMPR2)"]}, "benzonatate": {"origin": "Benzonatate is a synthetic molecule built on the structure of the local anesthetics procaine and tetracaine, with a long polyethylene glycol chain attached. Developed in the 1950s, it was designed to numb the stretch receptors in the lungs that trigger coughing, giving a cough suppressant that is not an opioid. It was approved by the FDA in 1958 and is sold as soft gelatin capsules known as Tessalon Perles. In 2010 the FDA warned that accidental ingestion by young children could be fatal, because the shiny round capsules can look like candy.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Benzonatate is developed as a non-opioid cough suppressant."], ["1958", "FDA approves benzonatate."], ["2010", "FDA warns about fatal accidental ingestion in children under 10."]], "cell": "Benzonatate is an ester-type local anesthetic related to tetracaine. The cough reflex begins in vagal afferent nerve endings in the airways, lungs and pleura, including stretch receptors that sense lung inflation and irritation. Benzonatate is thought to act on these receptors, blocking voltage-gated sodium channels in the nerve membrane. With the sodium channels blocked, the nerves cannot generate action potentials, so fewer cough signals travel up the vagus nerve to the cough center in the medulla. It may also have some central antitussive effect. Released in the mouth by chewing, the same sodium channel blockade numbs the mouth and throat.", "body": [["Airways and lungs", "Numbing stretch receptors reduces the cough reflex, starting within about fifteen to twenty minutes."], ["Mouth and throat", "If the capsule is chewed or sucked, the local anesthetic numbs the mouth and throat, which can cause choking, laryngospasm or bronchospasm."], ["Brain", "Sedation, headache and dizziness can occur, and rarely confusion or visual hallucinations."], ["Heart and nervous system in overdose", "Sodium channel blockade in heart and brain can cause seizures, arrhythmias and cardiac arrest, as with other local anesthetic toxicity."], ["Gut and skin", "Nausea, constipation and rash are reported."]], "journey": "Benzonatate capsules are swallowed whole and release the drug in the gut, where it is absorbed. Effects start in fifteen to twenty minutes and last three to eight hours. Like other ester local anesthetics, it is hydrolyzed by plasma pseudocholinesterase, mainly to para-aminobutylbenzoic acid, which is excreted in the urine. Data on its half-life are limited, but it appears short.", "sidefx": "Local anesthetic action explains most risks. Chewing or sucking the capsule releases drug in the mouth and throat, causing numbness, choking and laryngospasm or bronchospasm. In overdose, especially in young children, sodium channel blockade in the brain and heart causes restlessness, tremor, seizures, arrhythmias and cardiac arrest, which can occur within an hour. Lipid emulsion has been used for local anesthetic systemic toxicity, along with seizure control and supportive care. Sedation and rare confusion reflect CNS effects.", "fun": "Benzonatate is a chemical cousin of tetracaine, the anesthetic used to numb eyes and spinal nerves, which is why chewing a capsule numbs the mouth.", "src": ["Tessalon (benzonatate) FDA label", "FDA Drug Safety Communication on benzonatate accidental ingestion, 2010", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "codeine": {"origin": "Codeine comes from the opium poppy, Papaver somniferum, whose dried latex has been used for pain and cough for thousands of years. In 1832 the French chemist Pierre Jean Robiquet isolated codeine from opium, about a quarter century after morphine was isolated by Friedrich Sertürner. Codeine became one of the most widely used opioids for mild pain and cough. Much later, scientists learned that codeine is mostly a prodrug: its pain relief depends on conversion to morphine by the liver enzyme CYP2D6. After deaths in children who were ultra-rapid metabolizers, the FDA added a boxed warning in 2013 and in 2017 contraindicated codeine in children under twelve.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Opium poppy latex is used for pain and cough."], ["1804", "Friedrich Sertürner isolates morphine from opium."], ["1832", "Pierre Jean Robiquet isolates codeine from opium."], ["2013", "FDA adds a boxed warning after child deaths following tonsillectomy in ultra-rapid metabolizers."], ["2017", "FDA contraindicates codeine in children under 12 years."]], "cell": "Codeine itself binds mu opioid receptors only weakly. About five to ten percent of a dose is O-demethylated by CYP2D6 in the liver to morphine, a full mu receptor agonist. Mu receptors are Gi/o-coupled. When morphine binds, adenylyl cyclase is inhibited and cAMP falls, potassium channels open on postsynaptic neurons causing hyperpolarization, and voltage-gated calcium channels close in presynaptic terminals, reducing release of substance P and glutamate from pain fibers. This dampens pain transmission in the spinal cord dorsal horn and alters pain perception in the brain. Codeine also suppresses the cough center in the medulla. Most of each dose is instead converted by CYP3A4 to norcodeine or glucuronidated.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Mu activation reduces pain transmission and changes how pain is perceived, giving mild to moderate pain relief."], ["Cough center", "Suppression of the medullary cough center reduces cough."], ["Breathing", "Mu activation in the brainstem blunts the response to carbon dioxide, which can cause life-threatening respiratory depression, especially in ultra-rapid metabolizers."], ["Gut", "Mu receptors in the gut slow motility, causing constipation, and stimulation of the chemoreceptor trigger zone causes nausea."], ["Skin", "Histamine release from mast cells causes itching and flushing."], ["Eyes", "Mu activation in the Edinger-Westphal nucleus causes pinpoint pupils."]], "journey": "Codeine is well absorbed by mouth and starts working in thirty to sixty minutes, peaking in about an hour. The liver handles it by three routes: CYP2D6 converts a small part to morphine, CYP3A4 makes norcodeine, and UGT2B7 makes codeine-6-glucuronide, the largest share. People who are CYP2D6 poor metabolizers get little pain relief, while ultra-rapid metabolizers make much more morphine. About ninety percent leaves in the urine, mostly as metabolites. The half-life is about three hours. Codeine and morphine pass into breast milk.", "sidefx": "Constipation, nausea, sedation and itching come from mu activation and histamine release. The gravest danger is respiratory depression, which is exaggerated in CYP2D6 ultra-rapid metabolizers who make excess morphine; children and breastfed infants have died this way. The boxed warning also covers addiction, misuse and danger with benzodiazepines. CYP3A4 inhibitors can shift metabolism toward CYP2D6 and raise morphine. Naloxone reverses opioid respiratory depression, and repeated doses may be needed.", "fun": "The name codeine comes from the Greek word kodeia, meaning poppy head.", "src": ["Codeine sulfate tablets FDA label", "FDA Drug Safety Communications on codeine in children, 2013 and 2017", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "hydrocodone-homatropine": {"origin": "Hydrocodone was first synthesized from codeine in 1920 by the German chemists Carl Mannich and Helene Löwenheim. It proved to be a stronger cough suppressant and pain reliever than codeine. In the US it was introduced for cough in the 1940s as Hycodan, combined with a small amount of homatropine methylbromide. Homatropine was added at a dose too low to do much, so that anyone taking large amounts of the syrup would get unpleasant anticholinergic effects, which was meant to discourage misuse. In 2014 the DEA moved all hydrocodone combination products to Schedule II, and in 2018 the FDA limited opioid cough medicines to adults.", "timeline": [["1920", "Carl Mannich and Helene Löwenheim synthesize hydrocodone in Germany."], ["1940s", "Hydrocodone with homatropine is introduced in the US as Hycodan."], ["2014", "The DEA moves hydrocodone combination products to Schedule II."], ["2018", "FDA limits prescription opioid cough and cold medicines to adults 18 and older."]], "cell": "Hydrocodone is an agonist at the mu opioid receptor, a Gi/o-coupled receptor. In the medulla it suppresses the cough center, reducing the cough reflex. The same mu activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase, opens potassium channels and closes presynaptic calcium channels throughout the brain and spinal cord, which causes sedation, pain relief and respiratory depression. The liver converts some hydrocodone by CYP2D6 to hydromorphone, a more potent mu agonist, and by CYP3A4 to norhydrocodone. Homatropine methylbromide is a quaternary ammonium muscarinic antagonist. It carries a permanent charge, so it enters the brain poorly, and at the low amount in the product it has little effect unless large quantities are taken.", "body": [["Cough center", "Mu activation in the medulla reduces the urge to cough."], ["Brain", "Sedation, dizziness and impaired thinking come from mu receptor activation throughout the brain."], ["Breathing", "Brainstem mu activation lowers the response to carbon dioxide and can cause life-threatening respiratory depression, especially with benzodiazepines or alcohol."], ["Gut", "Slowed gut motility causes constipation and nausea."], ["Anticholinergic effects", "Homatropine can cause dry mouth, blurred vision and urinary retention, and is a concern in narrow-angle glaucoma."], ["Eyes", "Hydrocodone causes small pupils, while large homatropine amounts can dilate them."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth as a tablet or solution, hydrocodone is absorbed and starts working in thirty to sixty minutes, peaking in about one and a half hours. The liver metabolizes it through CYP3A4 to norhydrocodone and CYP2D6 to hydromorphone, and metabolites leave in the urine. The half-life is about four hours. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise hydrocodone levels and can cause dangerous sedation. Homatropine methylbromide is poorly absorbed from the gut because of its charge.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness, constipation and nausea come from mu activation. Respiratory depression is the main danger, especially with benzodiazepines, alcohol or CYP3A4 inhibitors, and accidental ingestion by children can be fatal. The boxed warnings also cover addiction, misuse and dosing errors with the liquid. Homatropine adds anticholinergic effects such as dry mouth and urinary retention. Naloxone reverses opioid respiratory depression and may need repeating because hydrocodone can outlast it.", "src": ["Hycodan and hydrocodone bitartrate and homatropine methylbromide FDA labels", "DEA Federal Register final rule rescheduling hydrocodone combination products, 2014", "FDA Drug Safety Communication on opioid cough and cold medicines, 2018"]}, "hypertonic-saline-inhaled": {"origin": "Hypertonic saline is simply salt water stronger than body fluids, and its use in cystic fibrosis began with an observation from the beach. Doctors in Australia noticed that some people with CF who surfed regularly seemed to have clearer lungs, and they wondered whether inhaling salty sea spray was helping. Researchers tested nebulized hypertonic saline, and in 2006 two studies in the New England Journal of Medicine, the Australian National Hypertonic Saline Trial led by Mark Elkins and a mechanism study led by Scott Donaldson in the US, showed it improved mucus clearance and reduced lung flare-ups. It is now a common, low-cost part of CF airway clearance.", "timeline": [["2006", "Two New England Journal of Medicine studies show benefit of inhaled hypertonic saline in cystic fibrosis."]], "cell": "Hypertonic saline works by osmosis, not by binding a receptor. Airway epithelium is covered by a thin airway surface liquid with two layers: a watery periciliary layer where cilia beat and a mucus layer on top. In cystic fibrosis, defective CFTR reduces chloride secretion while the sodium channel ENaC absorbs too much sodium, so water leaves the surface and the periciliary layer collapses. Mucus becomes dehydrated, thick and stuck. Inhaled hypertonic saline raises the salt concentration on the airway surface, which draws water out of epithelial cells and into the airway surface liquid. The rehydrated layer lets cilia beat again and makes mucus less viscous, improving mucociliary clearance. It also stimulates cough, helping move the loosened mucus out.", "body": [["Airways", "Rehydrated airway surface liquid makes mucus thinner and easier to clear, reducing pulmonary exacerbations in CF."], ["Cough reflex", "The salty aerosol stimulates productive coughing, which helps clear secretions and is used to collect induced sputum samples."], ["Bronchial muscle", "The hyperosmolar aerosol can trigger bronchospasm and chest tightness, especially in reactive airways, so a bronchodilator is commonly given first."], ["Throat and mouth", "Throat irritation and a salty taste are common."]], "journey": "Hypertonic saline is inhaled through a nebulizer and works within minutes on the airway surface. The extra salt and water are absorbed through the airway lining and handled by the kidneys like any other sodium and water. It is not metabolized. The effect on mucus clearance lasts a few hours, which is why it is often used once or twice daily with airway clearance techniques.", "sidefx": "Bronchospasm and chest tightness happen because the hyperosmolar aerosol irritates the airways and can trigger mast cell and nerve reflexes that narrow them, especially in asthma or reactive airways; giving a short-acting bronchodilator first reduces this, and the first dose is often observed. Cough, throat irritation and salty taste are local effects. Rare hemoptysis can occur in damaged airways. Contaminated nebulizers can spread infection, so cleaning matters. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The idea for hypertonic saline in CF is often traced to surfers in Australia, whose lung health seemed to improve with time in the salty ocean spray.", "src": ["Elkins MR et al. N Engl J Med, 2006", "Donaldson SH et al. N Engl J Med, 2006", "Cystic Fibrosis Foundation pulmonary care guidelines"]}, "alpha-1-proteinase-inhibitor": {"origin": "Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency was discovered in 1963 in Malmö, Sweden, by Carl-Bertil Laurell and Sten Eriksson. While reviewing serum protein electrophoresis strips, Laurell noticed several samples missing the alpha-1 protein band. Eriksson then found that many of these patients had early, severe emphysema. Researchers later showed that alpha-1 antitrypsin protects the lungs from neutrophil elastase, an enzyme that digests elastin. The idea of replacement followed: purify the protein from donated human plasma and give it by infusion. The first product, Prolastin, was approved by the FDA in 1987. Other plasma-derived products, including Aralast NP, Zemaira and Glassia, followed.", "timeline": [["1963", "Laurell and Eriksson describe alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency and its link to emphysema."], ["1980s", "The SERPINA1 gene and the common Z mutation are characterized."], ["1987", "FDA approves Prolastin, the first alpha-1 proteinase inhibitor augmentation therapy."]], "cell": "Alpha-1 antitrypsin is a serine protease inhibitor (serpin) made mainly by liver cells. Its main job is to inactivate neutrophil elastase, an enzyme released by neutrophils that breaks down elastin and other lung structural proteins. The serpin works like a mousetrap: elastase cleaves a reactive loop on the inhibitor, which then snaps into a new shape and locks the enzyme in a covalent, inactive complex, a suicide-substrate mechanism. In the common PiZZ deficiency, a Glu342Lys change in SERPINA1 makes Z protein misfold and polymerize inside liver cells, so little reaches the blood. Unopposed elastase slowly destroys alveolar walls, causing lower lobe panacinar emphysema. Augmentation therapy raises serum and lung lining fluid levels of the inhibitor, restoring protection.", "body": [["Lungs", "Restoring elastase inhibition in lung lining fluid aims to slow the loss of lung tissue, measured by lung density on CT scans."], ["Blood", "Weekly infusions keep blood levels near or above the protective threshold."], ["Liver", "Augmentation does not treat the liver disease of AAT deficiency, which comes from misfolded protein trapped inside liver cells."], ["Immune system", "Allergic reactions can occur, and people with IgA deficiency and anti-IgA antibodies have higher risk because products contain trace IgA."], ["General", "Headache, dizziness, nausea and chills can occur around infusions."]], "journey": "Alpha-1 proteinase inhibitor is given by intravenous infusion, usually weekly, so blood levels rise immediately and peak at the end of the infusion. The protein spreads from the blood into the lung's lining fluid where it does its job. It is broken down like the body's own proteins, by catabolism, and has a half-life of about four and a half to five days, so weekly dosing keeps trough levels near the protective threshold.", "sidefx": "Hypersensitivity reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, occur because it is a protein product; IgA-deficient patients with anti-IgA antibodies are at greater risk. Because it is made from pooled human plasma, there is a theoretical risk of transmitting infectious agents, although screening and viral inactivation steps make this very unlikely; hepatitis A and B vaccination is commonly recommended. Flare-ups of COPD can occur during therapy. There is no antidote; infusions are slowed or stopped for reactions.", "src": ["Prolastin-C Liquid (alpha1-proteinase inhibitor, human) FDA label", "Laurell CB, Eriksson S. Scand J Clin Lab Invest, 1963", "American Thoracic Society/European Respiratory Society statement on AAT deficiency, 2003"]}, "leflunomide": {"origin": "Leflunomide was developed by the German company Hoechst, later Aventis, as an immune-modulating drug for rheumatoid arthritis. Scientists learned that it is a prodrug, rapidly converted in the body to its active form, teriflunomide (A77 1726), which blocks the enzyme dihydroorotate dehydrogenase. Activated lymphocytes depend heavily on this enzyme to make the pyrimidine building blocks of DNA. The FDA approved leflunomide as Arava in September 1998, giving an oral alternative to methotrexate. Its active metabolite was later approved on its own in 2012 as Aubagio for multiple sclerosis.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Hoechst researchers develop leflunomide."], ["1998", "FDA approves Arava for rheumatoid arthritis."], ["2012", "Its active metabolite, teriflunomide, is approved as Aubagio for multiple sclerosis."]], "cell": "Leflunomide is converted to teriflunomide, which reversibly inhibits dihydroorotate dehydrogenase (DHODH), a mitochondrial enzyme in the de novo pyrimidine synthesis pathway. DHODH converts dihydroorotate to orotate, a step needed to make uridine monophosphate and then the pyrimidine nucleotides used in DNA and RNA. Resting cells can recycle pyrimidines through salvage pathways, but activated T and B lymphocytes, which must expand rapidly, rely on de novo synthesis. Without enough pyrimidines, these cells arrest in the G1 phase of the cell cycle and cannot proliferate. Fewer activated lymphocytes means less inflammation in the joint synovium, less cytokine release and slower joint damage. At higher levels, teriflunomide may also inhibit some tyrosine kinases.", "body": [["Joints", "Reduced lymphocyte activity eases joint pain, swelling and stiffness over four to eight weeks and slows structural damage."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises are common, and severe or fatal liver injury has occurred, the basis of a boxed warning."], ["Immune system", "Lower lymphocyte proliferation increases the risk of serious infections, including reactivation of tuberculosis."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea and abdominal pain are common."], ["Skin and hair", "Rash and hair thinning are common; rare severe skin reactions such as SJS can occur."], ["Lungs and nerves", "Interstitial lung disease and peripheral neuropathy are rare but serious effects."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth, leflunomide is converted almost completely and quickly in the gut wall and liver to teriflunomide. Teriflunomide peaks six to twelve hours after a dose and is more than ninety-nine percent bound to albumin. It is excreted in bile, then reabsorbed from the gut, a cycle called enterohepatic recirculation that greatly prolongs its stay. Its half-life is about two weeks, and without a washout it can remain detectable for up to two years. Elimination is in feces and urine. Cholestyramine or activated charcoal binds teriflunomide in the gut and breaks the recirculation, speeding removal.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea, hair loss and rash reflect effects on rapidly dividing cells and direct drug effects. Infections and low blood counts come from suppressed lymphocyte proliferation. Liver injury explains the hepatotoxicity boxed warning. Because pyrimidine synthesis is essential for fetal development, it can cause birth defects, so pregnancy is excluded before starting and it is contraindicated in pregnancy. The accelerated elimination procedure with cholestyramine or activated charcoal is used to lower levels quickly, for example before pregnancy or after serious toxicity.", "src": ["Arava (leflunomide) FDA label", "Fox RI et al. Clin Immunol, 1999", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "abatacept": {"origin": "Abatacept grew out of basic research on how T cells decide to switch on. In 1987 a French team led by Pierre Golstein described a new T-cell molecule they named CTLA-4. Scientists later learned that CTLA-4 acts as a natural brake: it grabs the B7 molecules (CD80 and CD86) on antigen-presenting cells more tightly than the activating receptor CD28 does. In the early 1990s Peter Linsley and colleagues at Bristol-Myers Squibb fused the outer part of CTLA-4 to the tail of a human antibody, making a soluble decoy called CTLA-4-Ig. This lab tool proved that blocking the second signal could quiet unwanted immune responses. Bristol-Myers Squibb developed it as abatacept (Orencia), and the FDA approved it for rheumatoid arthritis in 2005. It was the first approved drug to work by blocking T-cell costimulation.", "timeline": [["1987", "Golstein's group in France describes the T-cell molecule CTLA-4."], ["1991", "Linsley and colleagues at Bristol-Myers Squibb report the CTLA-4-Ig fusion protein."], ["2005", "FDA approves abatacept (Orencia) for rheumatoid arthritis."], ["2011", "A self-injected subcutaneous form is approved."], ["2021", "FDA approves abatacept to help prevent acute graft-versus-host disease."]], "cell": "A naive T cell needs two signals to become fully active. Signal 1 is its T-cell receptor recognizing an antigen held in an MHC molecule. Signal 2 is costimulation: CD28 on the T cell binding CD80 or CD86 (B7-1 and B7-2) on the antigen-presenting cell. Abatacept is the outer domain of human CTLA-4 joined to a modified IgG1 Fc. It binds CD80 and CD86 with high affinity and covers them, so CD28 has nothing to bind. A T cell that gets signal 1 without signal 2 does not make IL-2, does not multiply well and can become unresponsive (anergic). Fewer activated T cells means less help for B cells, fewer autoantibodies, and less downstream TNF and IL-6 from macrophages in the joint lining. The result is slowly falling joint inflammation over weeks to months.", "body": [["Immune system", "T-cell activation is damped at its starting point, so the whole cascade of cytokines and B-cell help that drives rheumatoid arthritis winds down."], ["Joints", "Less inflammatory signaling in the synovium leads to less swelling, pain and erosion of cartilage and bone over months."], ["Lungs", "Infections such as pneumonia are more common, and people with COPD had more respiratory adverse events in trials."], ["Blood glucose testing", "The IV product contains maltose, which some glucose meters read as glucose, giving falsely high readings on infusion day."]], "journey": "Abatacept is a large protein, so it cannot be taken by mouth. It is given as an IV infusion or as a weekly injection under the skin, where it is slowly absorbed through the lymphatics over several days. It stays mostly in the blood and extracellular fluid. Like other antibody-type proteins it is broken down by protein catabolism into peptides and amino acids rather than by liver enzymes. Its half-life is about two weeks, which allows monthly IV dosing after the start.", "sidefx": "Because abatacept turns down T-cell activation, the body is slower to fight infections, so pneumonia, cellulitis and urinary infections are the main risks. Latent tuberculosis and hepatitis B can reactivate when T-cell control weakens. Combining it with a TNF blocker adds immune suppression without added benefit, so serious infections rise. Infusion and allergic reactions come from the immune system reacting to the foreign protein. There is no antidote; care is supportive and the drug wears off over weeks.", "fun": "The same CTLA-4 brake works in the opposite direction in cancer: ipilimumab blocks CTLA-4 to release the brake and boost T cells against tumors, research that shared the 2018 Nobel Prize.", "src": ["Orencia (abatacept) FDA label", "Linsley PS et al. J Exp Med 1991", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "tocilizumab": {"origin": "Tocilizumab traces back to Japanese immunology. In the 1980s Tadamitsu Kishimoto and Toshio Hirano at Osaka University studied a factor that made B cells turn into antibody-making cells. They cloned its gene in 1986, and the molecule became known as interleukin-6 (IL-6). The team noticed that IL-6 levels were very high in rheumatoid arthritis and in Castleman disease. Working with the Japanese company Chugai, Kishimoto's group developed a humanized antibody against the IL-6 receptor. It was first approved in Japan in 2005 for Castleman disease. The FDA approved it as Actemra for rheumatoid arthritis in 2010. In 2017 it became the first drug approved for cytokine release syndrome after CAR T-cell therapy, and it was later approved for hospitalized COVID-19.", "timeline": [["1986", "Kishimoto and Hirano's team at Osaka University clone the IL-6 gene."], ["2005", "Tocilizumab is approved in Japan for Castleman disease."], ["2010", "FDA approves tocilizumab (Actemra) for rheumatoid arthritis."], ["2017", "FDA approves it for CAR T-cell cytokine release syndrome and giant cell arteritis."], ["2022", "FDA approves tocilizumab for hospitalized adults with COVID-19."]], "cell": "IL-6 signals in two ways. In classic signaling it binds the membrane IL-6 receptor (IL-6R, CD126) on liver cells and some white cells. In trans-signaling it binds soluble IL-6R, and that complex can activate almost any cell that has the shared signal protein gp130. Either way, gp130 pairs up and activates JAK kinases, which phosphorylate STAT3; STAT3 then moves to the nucleus and turns on inflammatory genes. Tocilizumab is a humanized IgG1 antibody that binds both soluble and membrane IL-6R, so IL-6 cannot dock. In the liver this stops the making of acute-phase proteins such as CRP, fibrinogen and hepcidin. In the immune system it reduces Th17 development and B-cell maturation, and in bone it lowers osteoclast activation. Clinically, fever and CRP fall within days and joint inflammation settles over weeks.", "body": [["Liver", "CRP and other acute-phase proteins drop quickly because IL-6 is their main driver; liver enzymes can rise and need monitoring."], ["Immune system", "Fever and the systemic inflammatory response calm down, which is why it helps cytokine release syndrome and severe COVID-19."], ["Blood", "Anemia of inflammation improves as hepcidin falls and iron becomes available, while neutrophil and platelet counts can drop."], ["Lipids", "Cholesterol and triglycerides rise, likely because inflammation had been holding them down."], ["Gut", "GI perforation can occur, especially in people with diverticulitis, and the usual pain and fever warning signs may be blunted."], ["Drug metabolism", "IL-6 suppresses CYP enzymes, so blocking it restores CYP3A4 activity and can lower levels of drugs such as warfarin or cyclosporine."]], "journey": "Tocilizumab is given by IV infusion or as an injection under the skin, where it is absorbed slowly over a few days. It stays mainly in the blood and tissue fluid. Clearance happens two ways: general protein breakdown and binding to IL-6 receptors, which pulls the antibody out of circulation. Because that receptor route can be saturated, the half-life depends on concentration and becomes longer at steady state, up to about one to two weeks.", "sidefx": "Blocking IL-6 weakens defense against bacteria, fungi and TB, so serious infections carry a boxed warning. Since IL-6 makes fever and CRP, infections can be present with few of the usual signs. Neutropenia reflects IL-6's role in neutrophil production and release. The lipid rise and liver enzyme changes come from altered liver signaling. Restored CYP activity explains drug interactions after starting. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped and infections are treated.", "fun": "Tocilizumab is one of the few biologics whose story runs from discovery of the target cytokine to the finished drug largely within one university team and one partner company.", "src": ["Actemra (tocilizumab) FDA label", "Hirano T, Kishimoto T et al. Nature 1986", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "anakinra": {"origin": "In the 1980s researchers studying fever and inflammation, including Charles Dinarello and William Arend, found that the body makes its own blocker of interleukin-1. This natural IL-1 receptor antagonist (IL-1Ra) was found in the urine of people with fever and in the fluid from white cell cultures, and its gene was cloned around 1990. The Colorado biotech company Synergen made a recombinant version and tested it in sepsis, where large trials failed. Amgen later acquired the program and developed it for rheumatoid arthritis. The FDA approved anakinra as Kineret in 2001. Its greatest success came in rare autoinflammatory diseases driven by too much IL-1, and it was approved for NOMID in 2012 and for DIRA, an inherited lack of IL-1Ra, in 2020.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Researchers identify a natural inhibitor of IL-1 in human fluids."], ["1990", "The IL-1 receptor antagonist gene is cloned."], ["2001", "FDA approves anakinra (Kineret) for rheumatoid arthritis."], ["2012", "FDA approves anakinra for neonatal-onset multisystem inflammatory disease (NOMID)."], ["2020", "FDA approves anakinra for deficiency of IL-1 receptor antagonist (DIRA)."]], "cell": "IL-1 alpha and IL-1 beta are master fever and inflammation cytokines. They bind the type I IL-1 receptor (IL-1R1), which then recruits the IL-1 receptor accessory protein. That complex activates MyD88, IRAK kinases and finally NF-kB and MAP kinase pathways, switching on COX-2, IL-6, adhesion molecules and cartilage-degrading enzymes. Anakinra is a recombinant copy of human IL-1Ra with one extra methionine at its start. It sits in the same pocket on IL-1R1 but does not recruit the accessory protein, so no signal is sent. Because it competes with IL-1, it must be present at high levels, which is why it is injected daily. In autoinflammatory syndromes caused by an overactive NLRP3 inflammasome, blocking IL-1 removes the main driver of fever, rash and organ inflammation.", "body": [["Immune system", "Fever, rash and systemic inflammation driven by IL-1 calm down, often dramatically in inherited autoinflammatory syndromes."], ["Joints", "Less IL-1 means less prostaglandin and enzyme release in the joint, reducing pain and slowing cartilage and bone damage."], ["Liver", "Acute-phase proteins such as CRP fall because IL-1 helps drive their production."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutrophil counts can fall, so blood counts are checked during the first year."], ["Skin at injection site", "Redness, itching and swelling at the injection site are very common in the first weeks and usually fade."]], "journey": "Anakinra is a small protein given as a daily injection under the skin. It is absorbed over a few hours and reaches its peak in about three to seven hours. Its half-life is short, only about four to six hours, which is why daily dosing is needed. The kidneys filter and break it down, so severe kidney disease raises levels and dosing is spaced out.", "sidefx": "Injection site reactions come from local inflammation to the protein and are the most common problem. Serious infections rise because IL-1 is part of the early alarm system against bacteria, and the risk climbs further with TNF blockers. Neutropenia reflects IL-1's role in neutrophil production. Allergic reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, are reactions to the recombinant protein. No antidote exists, but its short half-life means effects fade within days of stopping.", "fun": "Anakinra's main blocking trick is simple: it is almost the same shape as IL-1 itself, so it fills the receptor's docking site without turning the receptor on.", "src": ["Kineret (anakinra) FDA label", "Dinarello CA. Blood 2011 review of IL-1", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "baricitinib": {"origin": "Janus kinases (JAKs) were discovered in the early 1990s. The name refers to Janus, the two-faced Roman god, because each enzyme has two kinase-like domains. Researchers soon realized that many cytokines send their signals through JAKs, making them attractive drug targets. Baricitinib was discovered by the US company Incyte and licensed to Eli Lilly in 2009. The FDA approved it as Olumiant for rheumatoid arthritis in 2018. In early 2020 it was flagged as a possible COVID-19 treatment because it calms cytokine signaling, and trials led to emergency use in 2020 and full approval for hospitalized adults in 2022. That same year it became the first FDA-approved systemic treatment for severe alopecia areata.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Janus kinases are discovered and linked to cytokine signaling."], ["2009", "Incyte licenses baricitinib to Eli Lilly."], ["2018", "FDA approves baricitinib (Olumiant) for rheumatoid arthritis."], ["2020", "Baricitinib receives emergency use authorization for COVID-19."], ["2022", "FDA approves it for hospitalized COVID-19 and for severe alopecia areata."]], "cell": "Many cytokine receptors have no enzyme activity of their own. Instead they are paired with Janus kinases inside the cell. When a cytokine such as IL-6 or an interferon binds, the receptor chains move together and their JAKs phosphorylate each other and the receptor tail. STAT proteins dock there, get phosphorylated, pair up and travel to the nucleus to switch on inflammatory genes. Baricitinib is a reversible, ATP-competitive inhibitor that prefers JAK1 and JAK2, with less effect on TYK2 and JAK3. By filling the ATP pocket it stops phosphorylation, so STATs stay inactive. Signals from IL-6, interferons, GM-CSF and other cytokines are damped at once. Because it works inside the cell on many pathways, it lowers joint inflammation, calms the cytokine storm of severe COVID-19 and stops immune attack on hair follicles.", "body": [["Joints", "Less cytokine signaling in the synovium reduces swelling, pain and stiffness within weeks."], ["Lungs", "In severe COVID-19, damping the cytokine response helps reduce lung inflammation and progression to ventilation."], ["Hair follicles", "Blocking interferon-gamma and IL-15 signaling stops T cells from attacking follicles, allowing regrowth in alopecia areata."], ["Blood", "Neutrophil and lymphocyte counts can fall, and JAK2 blockade can lower hemoglobin because erythropoietin also signals through JAK2."], ["Lipids and vessels", "LDL and HDL cholesterol rise, and the JAK inhibitor class carries warnings for blood clots, heart attack and stroke."], ["Kidneys", "Creatinine rises slightly, and since the kidneys clear most of the drug, reduced kidney function raises exposure."]], "journey": "Baricitinib is a pill absorbed quickly, reaching peak levels in about an hour, with or without food. About half is bound to plasma proteins. Only a small part is broken down by CYP3A4 in the liver. Most of the drug leaves unchanged in the urine through filtration and active secretion by the transporter OAT3. That is why probenecid, an OAT3 blocker, raises levels and why dosing depends on kidney function. Its half-life is about 12 hours.", "sidefx": "Blocking JAK signaling weakens antiviral and antibacterial defense, so herpes zoster, other infections and TB reactivation are more common. Lower lymphocyte and neutrophil counts reflect blocked growth signals in the marrow. The class boxed warning for clots, heart attack, stroke, cancer and higher death rates comes from a large safety trial of the related drug tofacitinib in older people with heart risk factors. Lipid and creatine kinase rises are known class effects. There is no antidote; the drug wears off within about a day after stopping.", "fun": "Baricitinib was one of the first drugs proposed for COVID-19 by computer screening of existing medicines, before its benefit was confirmed in large randomized trials.", "src": ["Olumiant (baricitinib) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "secukinumab": {"origin": "Secukinumab came from a shift in how scientists understood psoriasis. For years it was thought of mainly as a Th1 disease. In 2005 researchers described a separate group of helper T cells, Th17 cells, that make interleukin-17 and drive tissue inflammation. Psoriatic skin turned out to be full of IL-17. Novartis developed a fully human antibody against IL-17A, and in 2015 the FDA approved it as Cosentyx for plaque psoriasis. It was the first IL-17A inhibitor approved in the US. Approvals for psoriatic arthritis and ankylosing spondylitis followed in 2016, and later for hidradenitis suppurativa in 2023.", "timeline": [["2005", "Researchers describe Th17 cells as a distinct IL-17-producing T-cell lineage."], ["2015", "FDA approves secukinumab (Cosentyx), the first IL-17A inhibitor, for plaque psoriasis."], ["2016", "Approvals expand to psoriatic arthritis and ankylosing spondylitis."], ["2023", "FDA approves secukinumab for hidradenitis suppurativa and an IV form for arthritis."]], "cell": "In psoriasis, dendritic cells in the skin release IL-23, which keeps Th17 cells and other immune cells producing IL-17A. IL-17A binds a receptor made of IL-17RA and IL-17RC on keratinocytes, fibroblasts and synovial cells. The receptor recruits the adaptor Act1 and turns on NF-kB and other pathways. Keratinocytes then grow faster and release chemokines that call in neutrophils, plus antimicrobial peptides and more cytokines, forming a self-feeding loop. Secukinumab is a human IgG1 kappa antibody that binds IL-17A and prevents it from reaching its receptor. Breaking the loop lets keratinocytes return toward normal turnover, so plaques thin within weeks. In the spine and tendon attachment sites, less IL-17A means less enthesitis and new bone formation signals.", "body": [["Skin", "Plaques become thinner, less red and less scaly as keratinocyte overgrowth and neutrophil recruitment slow, with clear improvement by about three to four weeks."], ["Joints and spine", "Less IL-17A at tendon and ligament attachment sites reduces enthesitis, back pain and stiffness in spondyloarthritis."], ["Mouth and skin defense", "IL-17 is key to defense against Candida, so oral thrush and other yeast and fungal skin infections are more common."], ["Gut", "IL-17 helps protect the intestinal lining, so blocking it can trigger or worsen Crohn disease and ulcerative colitis."]], "journey": "Secukinumab is usually injected under the skin and is absorbed slowly through the lymphatics, peaking about six days later. An IV form is also available for some arthritis types. Like other IgG antibodies, it is recycled by the neonatal Fc receptor, which protects it from breakdown and gives it a long half-life of roughly three to four weeks. It is cleared by protein catabolism into peptides and amino acids, not by liver enzymes.", "sidefx": "IL-17 is a front-line defender of skin and mucous membranes against Candida and Staphylococcus, which explains thrush and skin infections. Its protective role in the gut lining explains new or worse inflammatory bowel disease. TB screening is done because any loss of cytokine defense can let latent TB reactivate. Eczema-like rashes may reflect a shift toward Th2 inflammation once IL-17 is removed. Allergic reactions are reactions to the antibody. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Interleukin-17 was first cloned from a mouse T-cell hybridoma in 1993 and was originally called CTLA-8 before its role in inflammation was understood.", "src": ["Cosentyx (secukinumab) FDA label", "Harrington LE et al. Nat Immunol 2005", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "apremilast": {"origin": "Apremilast came from Celgene, the company that brought thalidomide back for leprosy complications and myeloma. While studying thalidomide's ability to lower the inflammatory cytokine TNF-alpha, Celgene chemists built a family of related molecules. Some of them turned out to work by blocking the enzyme phosphodiesterase 4 (PDE4). Apremilast is one of these, designed to calm immune cells without thalidomide's other effects. The FDA approved it as Otezla in 2014, first for psoriatic arthritis and then for plaque psoriasis. It was notable as a pill for psoriasis that does not need routine blood test monitoring. It was approved for oral ulcers of Behcet disease in 2019, and the brand later passed to Amgen.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Celgene explores thalidomide-related molecules that lower TNF-alpha."], ["2014", "FDA approves apremilast (Otezla) for psoriatic arthritis and then plaque psoriasis."], ["2019", "FDA approves apremilast for oral ulcers of Behcet disease."], ["2019", "Amgen acquires Otezla."]], "cell": "Inside immune cells, cyclic AMP (cAMP) is a brake on inflammation. PDE4 is the main enzyme that breaks down cAMP in monocytes, T cells, dendritic cells and keratinocytes. Apremilast blocks PDE4, so cAMP builds up. Higher cAMP activates protein kinase A, which phosphorylates the transcription factor CREB and reduces NF-kB activity. The net effect is a reset of cytokine output: less TNF-alpha, IL-23, IL-17 and interferon-gamma, and more of the calming cytokine IL-10. With fewer inflammatory signals, psoriatic plaques thin, joint swelling eases and mouth ulcers heal. The effect is a modulation rather than strong immune suppression.", "body": [["Skin", "Lower IL-23 and IL-17 output reduces keratinocyte overgrowth, so plaques and itching improve over weeks."], ["Joints", "Less TNF-alpha and IL-17 in the joint lining reduces swelling and tenderness in psoriatic arthritis."], ["Mouth", "Oral ulcers of Behcet disease heal faster and recur less."], ["Gut", "PDE4 is also active in the gut and brain, so nausea and diarrhea are common, mostly in the first weeks, which is why the dose is built up slowly."], ["Brain and mood", "Depression and, rarely, suicidal thoughts have been reported, and mood is watched during treatment."], ["Weight", "Some people lose weight, possibly from reduced appetite and GI effects."]], "journey": "Apremilast is taken by mouth and is well absorbed, peaking in about two and a half hours. It is moderately bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A4, with some CYP1A2 and CYP2A6 and some non-enzyme hydrolysis. Strong CYP3A4 inducers such as rifampin lower its levels enough to make it ineffective. Metabolites leave in both urine and feces. The half-life is about six to nine hours, which suits twice-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea come from PDE4 blockade in the gut and in the brain's vomiting center, which is why older PDE4 inhibitors were limited by nausea. Slow dose titration lets the body adjust. Weight loss follows from appetite changes and GI effects. The mood effects are not fully explained but PDE4 is present in brain areas that control mood. Because it modulates rather than shuts down immunity, serious infections are not a major feature. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Roflumilast, another PDE4 inhibitor, is used for COPD; both drugs share the same main side effects of nausea, diarrhea and weight loss.", "src": ["Otezla (apremilast) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "deucravacitinib": {"origin": "Deucravacitinib was designed by Bristol Myers Squibb to solve a problem with JAK inhibitors. Older JAK drugs fill the ATP pocket, which looks almost the same in all four family members, so they hit several JAKs at once. BMS scientists instead targeted the regulatory pseudokinase (JH2) domain of TYK2, which differs from JAK to JAK. Human genetics also supported TYK2 as a target, since people with certain TYK2 variants have lower psoriasis risk. The molecule contains deuterium, a heavy form of hydrogen, in one methyl group, which slows a breakdown step and gives the drug its 'deu' prefix. The FDA approved it as Sotyktu for moderate to severe plaque psoriasis in 2022, making it the first approved TYK2 inhibitor.", "timeline": [["2010s", "Bristol Myers Squibb scientists design an allosteric TYK2 inhibitor that binds the JH2 domain."], ["2022", "FDA approves deucravacitinib (Sotyktu), the first TYK2 inhibitor, for plaque psoriasis."]], "cell": "TYK2 is a member of the Janus kinase family. It pairs with JAK2 to carry signals from the IL-23 and IL-12 receptors, and with JAK1 for type I interferons. Each JAK has an active kinase domain (JH1) and a regulatory pseudokinase domain (JH2) that keeps the kinase in check. Deucravacitinib binds the JH2 domain of TYK2 and locks the enzyme in an inactive shape, so it cannot phosphorylate STAT proteins when the cytokine binds. Because the JH2 pocket differs among family members, it has little effect on JAK1, JAK2 or JAK3 at clinical exposures. Blocking IL-23 signaling reduces Th17 cell survival and IL-17 output, and blocking IL-12 and interferon signaling reduces Th1 inflammation. Keratinocyte overgrowth slows and plaques thin.", "body": [["Skin", "Lower IL-23 and IL-17 drive leads to thinner, less red and less scaly plaques over weeks to months."], ["Immune defense", "Upper respiratory infections, herpes simplex and zoster are somewhat more common because interferon and IL-12 help fight viruses."], ["Skin follicles", "Acne and folliculitis are common side effects, for reasons that are not fully understood."], ["Muscle", "Creatine kinase can rise, and rare rhabdomyolysis has been reported."], ["Lipids and liver", "Triglycerides and liver enzymes can rise modestly."]], "journey": "Deucravacitinib is a pill that is well absorbed, peaking in two to three hours, with or without food. It is moderately to highly bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down through several routes, mainly CYP1A2, plus CYP2B6, CYP2D6, carboxylesterase 2 and UGT1A9. The deuterium in its structure slows formation of one metabolite that is less selective. Metabolites and some parent drug leave in urine and feces. The half-life is about ten hours, and once-daily dosing keeps levels steady.", "sidefx": "Infections, especially viral ones, reflect reduced interferon and IL-12 signaling. Because it spares JAK1, JAK2 and JAK3 at usual exposure, it does not carry the JAK class boxed warning, and effects on blood counts, lipids and clotting seen with broad JAK drugs are less prominent, though warnings for infection, TB and malignancy still apply. The cause of acne and CK rises is not fully known. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Deucravacitinib was the first drug designed from the start with deuterium built in, rather than being a deuterated copy of an existing medicine.", "src": ["Sotyktu (deucravacitinib) FDA label", "Wrobleski ST et al. J Med Chem 2019"]}, "belimumab": {"origin": "Belimumab grew out of the genomics boom of the late 1990s. Scientists at Human Genome Sciences searched gene databases for new members of the TNF family and in 1999 described B-lymphocyte stimulator (BLyS), also called BAFF, a protein that keeps B cells alive. Mice with too much BLyS developed a lupus-like disease, and people with lupus were found to have high BLyS levels. Human Genome Sciences and GlaxoSmithKline developed a fully human antibody against it. When the FDA approved belimumab (Benlysta) in 2011, it was the first new drug approved for lupus in more than 50 years. It was later approved for children and, in 2020, for lupus nephritis.", "timeline": [["1999", "Human Genome Sciences scientists describe BLyS (BAFF) as a B-cell survival factor."], ["2011", "FDA approves belimumab (Benlysta), the first new lupus drug in over 50 years."], ["2017", "A weekly self-injected form is approved."], ["2019", "Belimumab is approved for children with lupus."], ["2020", "FDA approves belimumab for lupus nephritis."]], "cell": "B cells need constant survival signals to keep living after they leave the bone marrow. A key one is soluble BLyS, made by myeloid cells and stromal cells. BLyS binds three receptors on B cells: BAFF-R, TACI and BCMA. BAFF-R signaling activates the alternative NF-kB pathway and raises anti-apoptotic proteins such as Bcl-2, letting transitional and naive B cells mature. Autoreactive B cells are especially dependent on extra BLyS. Belimumab is a human IgG1 lambda antibody that binds soluble BLyS and stops it from reaching these receptors. Without the signal, many B cells, including autoreactive ones, undergo apoptosis instead of maturing into antibody-making plasma cells. Over months, anti-dsDNA antibody levels fall, complement levels rise and lupus flares become less frequent.", "body": [["Immune system", "Circulating naive and activated B cells decline, so fewer autoantibodies are made, while memory B cells are less affected."], ["Kidneys", "In lupus nephritis, fewer immune complexes reach the glomeruli, so proteinuria falls and kidney function is better protected."], ["Skin and joints", "Lupus rash, arthritis and fatigue tend to flare less often over months of therapy."], ["Brain and mood", "Depression and suicidal thoughts have been reported, so mood is watched during treatment."], ["Infection defense", "Serious infections are more common, and rare cases of progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML) have been reported."]], "journey": "Belimumab is given as an IV infusion or a weekly injection under the skin, where it is absorbed over several days. It stays mainly in the blood and tissue fluid and, like other IgG antibodies, is protected from breakdown by the neonatal Fc receptor. It is cleared by protein catabolism into peptides and amino acids. Its half-life is about 18 to 19 days, and full clinical effect takes weeks to months to build.", "sidefx": "Because B cells and the antibodies they make are part of infection defense, serious infections and rare PML can occur. Infusion and allergic reactions, including delayed ones, are immune reactions to the infused protein. Live vaccines are avoided because the response to them may be blunted and less controlled. Depression has been reported, though the cause is not well understood. There is no antidote; effects fade over weeks after stopping.", "fun": "BLyS was found by searching computer databases of gene sequences, an early example of a drug target found through genomics rather than through a disease sample.", "src": ["Benlysta (belimumab) FDA label", "Moore PA et al. Science 1999", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "avacopan": {"origin": "Avacopan came from research on how the complement system recruits neutrophils in ANCA-associated vasculitis. Studies in mice in the 2000s showed that the complement fragment C5a, acting on its receptor C5aR1, was needed for ANCA to trigger severe blood vessel and kidney damage. The California company ChemoCentryx designed avacopan as a small-molecule pill that blocks C5aR1. In the ADVOCATE trial, avacopan was compared with a standard steroid taper on top of rituximab or cyclophosphamide. The FDA approved it as Tavneos in 2021 as add-on therapy for granulomatosis with polyangiitis and microscopic polyangiitis. Amgen acquired ChemoCentryx in 2022.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Mouse studies show that C5a and its receptor drive ANCA-induced vasculitis."], ["2021", "The ADVOCATE trial is published, and FDA approves avacopan (Tavneos)."], ["2022", "Amgen acquires ChemoCentryx."]], "cell": "In ANCA vasculitis, autoantibodies against neutrophil proteins (myeloperoxidase or proteinase 3) prime and activate neutrophils. Activated neutrophils release factors that turn on the alternative complement pathway, producing C5a. C5a binds C5aR1 (CD88), a G protein-coupled receptor on neutrophils and monocytes. This draws more neutrophils to small vessels, primes them to respond to ANCA and makes them release oxygen radicals and enzymes, creating a vicious loop that destroys vessel walls and glomeruli. Avacopan is a selective antagonist of C5aR1. It blocks C5a from binding, breaking the loop. It does not block C5 cleavage, so the membrane attack complex still forms, which preserves defense against Neisseria better than C5 blockers. Less neutrophil activation means less vessel inflammation and allows lower steroid exposure.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Fewer neutrophils are recruited and activated in small vessel walls, reducing the necrotizing inflammation of vasculitis."], ["Kidneys", "Kidney function and proteinuria improved faster in trials as glomerular inflammation settled."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes and bilirubin can rise, so liver tests are checked regularly, especially in the first six months."], ["Steroid exposure", "Using avacopan allowed much lower total glucocorticoid exposure in trials, which reduces steroid side effects."], ["GI tract", "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are among the more common side effects."]], "journey": "Avacopan is a capsule taken with food, which increases absorption. It peaks in about two hours and is more than 99 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inducers are avoided and strong inhibitors require a dose change. It leaves mostly in the feces as metabolites. Although twice-daily dosing gives steady levels, its terminal half-life is very long, around 500 hours, reflecting slow release from tissues.", "sidefx": "Liver injury is the main labeled concern; the mechanism is not fully known but likely involves liver handling of the drug. Hepatitis B can reactivate in the setting of combined immunosuppression. Infections occur partly from the other drugs it is used with. Serious allergic reactions, including angioedema, have been reported. Because it leaves the membrane attack complex intact, it does not carry the meningococcal boxed warning of C5 blockers. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Avacopan is one of very few complement-targeting drugs taken as a pill; most others are antibodies or peptides given by injection.", "src": ["Tavneos (avacopan) FDA label", "Jayne DRW et al. N Engl J Med 2021 (ADVOCATE)"]}, "pegloticase": {"origin": "Most mammals have an enzyme called uricase that breaks uric acid into allantoin, which dissolves easily in urine. Humans and other great apes lost working uricase through mutations millions of years ago, which is one reason people get gout. At Duke University, Michael Hershfield and Susan Kelly, working with Mountain View Pharmaceuticals, built a recombinant uricase based on pig and baboon sequences and coated it with polyethylene glycol (PEG) to make it last longer and be less likely to provoke the immune system. Savient Pharmaceuticals developed it, and the FDA approved pegloticase (Krystexxa) in 2010 for gout that does not respond to standard therapy. In 2022 the label was updated to include giving it with methotrexate, which lowers antibody formation.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Duke researchers Hershfield and Kelly develop a PEGylated recombinant uricase."], ["2010", "FDA approves pegloticase (Krystexxa) for refractory chronic gout."], ["2022", "FDA approves co-administration with methotrexate to improve response."]], "cell": "Pegloticase is a recombinant uricase (urate oxidase) enzyme with many PEG chains attached. In the blood it catalyzes the oxidation of uric acid to 5-hydroxyisourate, which breaks down to allantoin, along with hydrogen peroxide and carbon dioxide. Allantoin is about five to ten times more water-soluble than uric acid and is easily cleared by the kidneys. As blood urate drops very low, urate stored in tophi and joints dissolves back into the blood and is also destroyed. The PEG coating shields the enzyme from rapid clearance. However, many people make antibodies against PEG, which speed clearance and cause loss of effect; rising urate before an infusion is the sign of this. Methotrexate suppresses these antibody responses.", "body": [["Blood", "Serum urate falls within a day, often to very low levels, as long as the enzyme remains active."], ["Joints and tophi", "Tophi shrink and dissolve over months, while flares are common early because shifting urate crystals stir up inflammation."], ["Red blood cells", "Hydrogen peroxide made by the reaction can cause hemolysis and methemoglobinemia in people with G6PD deficiency, who cannot handle oxidant stress."], ["Immune system", "Antibodies against PEG can form, leading to loss of response and to infusion reactions or anaphylaxis."], ["Heart", "Heart failure can worsen, so people with heart failure are watched closely."]], "journey": "Pegloticase is a large protein given only by slow IV infusion. It stays in the blood, where it does its work on circulating urate. The PEG coating slows its removal so the half-life is about two weeks, which matches dosing every two weeks. It is cleared by protein breakdown. Anti-PEG antibodies, when they form, can clear it much faster. Urate levels before each infusion show whether the enzyme is still working.", "sidefx": "Anaphylaxis and infusion reactions come from the immune response to the foreign enzyme and to PEG, which is why the drug carries a boxed warning, premedication is used and urate is checked before each infusion. Oral urate-lowering drugs are stopped because they can hide the rising urate that warns of antibody formation. G6PD deficiency is a contraindication because the hydrogen peroxide byproduct can cause hemolysis and methemoglobinemia. Early gout flares come from crystal mobilization. There is no antidote; reactions are treated with standard anaphylaxis care.", "fun": "Scientists have proposed that losing uricase may have helped early apes, because uric acid is an antioxidant and may help hold blood pressure up on a low-salt diet.", "src": ["Krystexxa (pegloticase) FDA label", "Sundy JS et al. JAMA 2011", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "probenecid-colchicine": {"origin": "This tablet joins two very different stories. Colchicine comes from the autumn crocus, Colchicum autumnale, a plant used for joint pain since ancient times; the pure alkaloid was isolated in 1820 by the French chemists Pelletier and Caventou. Probenecid was designed in the 1940s by Karl Beyer and colleagues at Sharp & Dohme (later Merck) during the penicillin shortage of World War II. Penicillin was scarce and the kidneys removed it quickly, so they built a molecule that blocks the kidney's acid transporters to keep penicillin in the blood longer. Doctors then noticed that probenecid made the kidneys dump more uric acid, and it became a gout drug. Merck combined the two as ColBenemid. The brand is gone, but generic probenecid-colchicine tablets remain.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Autumn crocus preparations are used for joint pain."], ["1820", "Pelletier and Caventou isolate colchicine from the autumn crocus."], ["1940s", "Karl Beyer's team at Sharp & Dohme develops probenecid to prolong penicillin levels."], ["1950s", "Probenecid is recognized and used as a uricosuric gout drug."]], "cell": "Probenecid: In the proximal tubule, filtered urate is reabsorbed back into the blood mainly by the transporter URAT1 on the luminal side. Probenecid competitively blocks URAT1 and related organic anion transporters, so more urate stays in the urine and serum urate falls. It also blocks OAT1 and OAT3 on the blood side of tubule cells, which normally secrete weak acids such as penicillin and methotrexate into urine; that is the basis of its penicillin-boosting effect and many interactions. Colchicine: it binds tubulin and prevents microtubules from assembling. Neutrophils need microtubules to move toward urate crystals, release granules and assemble the NLRP3 inflammasome that makes IL-1 beta. Low-dose colchicine therefore blunts the inflammatory reaction to crystals, reducing flares while probenecid slowly lowers the urate pool.", "body": [["Kidneys", "Probenecid raises uric acid in the urine. This can form uric acid stones, which is why plenty of fluid and sometimes urine alkalinization are used."], ["Joints", "Over months lower urate shrinks crystal deposits, and colchicine reduces the neutrophil response, so flares become less frequent."], ["GI tract", "Colchicine blocks cell division in the fast-turning gut lining, causing nausea, vomiting and diarrhea, the usual first signs of too much."], ["Muscles and nerves", "Colchicine can cause myopathy and neuropathy, especially with kidney disease, statins or drugs that raise its levels."], ["Bone marrow", "Rarely, colchicine's block of cell division causes low white cells or aplastic anemia."], ["Other drugs", "Probenecid raises blood levels of penicillins, cephalosporins, methotrexate and ketorolac by blocking their kidney secretion."]], "journey": "Both drugs are taken by mouth. Probenecid is well absorbed, highly bound to plasma proteins and broken down in the liver by glucuronidation and oxidation; its metabolites leave in the urine, with a dose-dependent half-life of several hours. Colchicine is absorbed in the gut, where intestinal P-glycoprotein pumps some back out. It spreads widely into tissues and especially into white cells. It is broken down by CYP3A4 and removed in bile and urine with help from P-glycoprotein, with a half-life of about a day. Strong CYP3A4 or P-gp inhibitors can raise colchicine to toxic levels.", "sidefx": "Starting a urate-lowering drug can trigger flares because crystals shift as urate levels change, and probenecid's extra urinary urate can form stones. Colchicine's toxicity comes from its core action: blocking microtubules in any rapidly dividing or long cell, which explains diarrhea, low blood counts, muscle damage and nerve injury. Clarithromycin, ketoconazole, ritonavir and cyclosporine block colchicine's removal and have caused fatal toxicity. Salicylates blunt probenecid's uricosuric effect. There is no widely available colchicine antidote; overdose care is supportive.", "fun": "Colchicine's ability to stop cells in the middle of division made it a key tool in genetics labs for counting chromosomes.", "src": ["Probenecid and colchicine tablets FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "mycophenolic-acid": {"origin": "Mycophenolic acid is one of the oldest known antibiotics. In 1893 the Italian physician Bartolomeo Gosio isolated it from a Penicillium mold growing on spoiled corn while studying pellagra, and it is often called the first antibiotic obtained in pure crystalline form. It was rediscovered and named in the early 1900s, but it was too weak as an antibacterial to be useful. In the 1970s and 1980s Anthony Allison and Elsie Eugui at Syntex realized it targeted a pathway that lymphocytes depend on. Syntex made a prodrug, mycophenolate mofetil (CellCept), approved in 1995. Novartis later developed an enteric-coated mycophenolate sodium tablet, Myfortic, approved in 2004, which releases mycophenolic acid in the small intestine.", "timeline": [["1893", "Bartolomeo Gosio isolates mycophenolic acid from a Penicillium mold."], ["1980s", "Allison and Eugui at Syntex show it selectively blocks lymphocyte growth."], ["1995", "FDA approves mycophenolate mofetil (CellCept) for kidney transplant."], ["2004", "FDA approves delayed-release mycophenolate sodium (Myfortic)."]], "cell": "Cells can make guanine nucleotides in two ways: the de novo pathway, built from scratch, and the salvage pathway, recycling purines. Most cells can use salvage, but activated T and B lymphocytes rely heavily on the de novo route. A key step there is inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase (IMPDH), which turns IMP into xanthosine monophosphate on the way to GMP. Mycophenolic acid is a reversible, uncompetitive inhibitor of IMPDH and is more potent at the type II isoform that activated lymphocytes express. GTP and dGTP levels fall in lymphocytes, so they cannot copy DNA and stall before S phase. Clonal expansion of T cells and antibody production by B cells drop. Less guanosine also reduces glycosylation of adhesion molecules, limiting lymphocyte homing to the graft.", "body": [["Immune system", "Lymphocyte multiplication and antibody production are suppressed, which helps prevent rejection of the transplanted kidney."], ["GI tract", "Diarrhea, nausea and abdominal pain are common, partly because gut lining cells also use the de novo pathway and partly from local drug and metabolite exposure."], ["Bone marrow", "Anemia, low white cells and rare pure red cell aplasia can occur."], ["Infection defense", "CMV, BK virus kidney disease and rare PML are risks of reduced T-cell control."], ["Pregnancy", "Mycophenolate causes first-trimester pregnancy loss and birth defects, so it carries a boxed warning and a REMS program."], ["Skin", "Long-term immune suppression raises the risk of skin cancer and lymphoma."]], "journey": "Myfortic tablets have an enteric coating, so the drug passes the stomach and releases mycophenolic acid in the small intestine, peaking around two hours after a dose. Mycophenolic acid is more than 98 percent bound to albumin. The liver and gut glucuronidate it to inactive MPAG, which is excreted in bile and then split back into active drug by gut bacteria. This enterohepatic recirculation gives a second bump in levels. MPAG leaves mainly in the urine, and the half-life of mycophenolic acid is roughly 8 to 16 hours.", "sidefx": "Most toxicity follows from blocking guanine production. Rapidly dividing gut cells and marrow cells are affected, causing diarrhea and cytopenias. Infection risk rises because T- and B-cell responses are held back. The fetal damage comes from interference with rapidly dividing embryonic tissues. Magnesium or aluminum antacids, cholestyramine and sevelamer reduce absorption or block recirculation. There is no antidote; doses are reduced or the drug is stopped.", "fun": "Myfortic and CellCept are not interchangeable by weight, because the sodium salt and the mofetil ester deliver different amounts of mycophenolic acid.", "src": ["Myfortic (mycophenolic acid) FDA label", "Allison AC, Eugui EM. Immunopharmacology 2000", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "sirolimus": {"origin": "Sirolimus, also called rapamycin, came from a soil sample collected on Easter Island, known locally as Rapa Nui, during a Canadian scientific expedition in 1964. In the early 1970s Suren Sehgal and colleagues at Ayerst Research Laboratories in Montreal isolated a compound from the bacterium Streptomyces hygroscopicus in that soil and named it after the island. It was first studied as an antifungal, but its strong immune-suppressing effect stood out. Sehgal kept the cultures alive when the project was shelved, and it was later revived. The FDA approved sirolimus (Rapamune) in 1999 to prevent kidney transplant rejection. Work on how it acts led to the discovery of TOR in yeast in 1991 and mTOR in mammals in 1994, a central growth regulator. In 2015 it became the first approved drug for lymphangioleiomyomatosis (LAM).", "timeline": [["1964", "A soil sample is collected on Easter Island (Rapa Nui)."], ["1972", "Suren Sehgal's team at Ayerst isolates rapamycin from Streptomyces hygroscopicus."], ["1994", "Several labs identify mTOR, the mammalian target of rapamycin."], ["1999", "FDA approves sirolimus (Rapamune) for kidney transplant."], ["2015", "FDA approves sirolimus for lymphangioleiomyomatosis."]], "cell": "Sirolimus enters cells and binds the immunophilin FKBP-12, the same binding protein used by tacrolimus. Unlike tacrolimus, the sirolimus-FKBP-12 complex does not block calcineurin. Instead it binds the FRB domain of mTOR and inhibits mTOR complex 1 (mTORC1). mTORC1 normally receives growth signals, such as IL-2 receptor signaling through PI3K and Akt, and responds by phosphorylating S6 kinase and 4E-BP1 to boost protein synthesis and cell cycle progression. With mTORC1 blocked, T cells still make IL-2 but cannot respond to it, so they arrest in the G1 phase and do not move into S phase. Clonal expansion of alloreactive T cells fails. In LAM, abnormal smooth muscle-like cells have overactive mTORC1 because of TSC gene mutations, so blocking mTOR slows their growth and helps stabilize lung function.", "body": [["Immune system", "T-cell proliferation in response to IL-2 is blocked, preventing rejection without the direct kidney toxicity of calcineurin inhibitors."], ["Lungs", "In LAM, mTOR blockade slows the growth of abnormal cells and stabilizes lung function; rarely it causes interstitial pneumonitis."], ["Lipids", "Triglycerides and cholesterol rise often, because mTOR regulates lipid handling in the liver and fat tissue."], ["Wounds and lymph", "Fibroblast and blood vessel growth slow, so wound healing is impaired and lymphoceles can form after surgery."], ["Blood", "Platelets and red cells can fall from reduced growth signaling in the marrow."], ["Mouth and skin", "Mouth ulcers (stomatitis) and acne are common, reflecting slowed renewal of fast-growing lining and skin cells."]], "journey": "Sirolimus is taken by mouth as tablets or liquid. Absorption is low and variable because intestinal CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein remove much of it before it reaches the blood, and high-fat meals change absorption, so it is taken the same way each day. It concentrates heavily inside red blood cells, so whole-blood levels are measured. The liver breaks it down by CYP3A4, and almost all leaves in the feces. The half-life is long, about 62 hours, so a loading dose is often used and levels take days to settle.", "sidefx": "Infection and lymphoma risks come from suppressed T-cell control. Because mTOR also runs growth in fibroblasts, endothelium and the marrow, sirolimus slows wound healing, causes lymphoceles and lowers platelets. Lipid rises reflect mTOR's role in metabolism. In liver and lung transplants it was linked to hepatic artery thrombosis and bronchial anastomosis breakdown. Angioedema is more likely with ACE inhibitors. Strong CYP3A4 or P-gp inhibitors and inducers change levels greatly. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Rapamycin extends lifespan in yeast, worms, flies and mice, which has made it one of the most studied compounds in aging research.", "src": ["Rapamune (sirolimus) FDA label", "Vezina C, Kudelski A, Sehgal SN. J Antibiot 1975", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "belatacept": {"origin": "Belatacept is a second-generation version of abatacept. Abatacept (CTLA-4-Ig) blocked costimulation well enough for rheumatoid arthritis but was not strong enough to prevent transplant rejection, partly because it binds CD86 less tightly than CD80. Scientists at Bristol-Myers Squibb, working with transplant researchers including groups at Emory University, changed two amino acids in the CTLA-4 part of the molecule. This variant, first called LEA29Y, binds CD80 about twice as well and CD86 about four times as well. In primate kidney transplant studies it prevented rejection. The FDA approved belatacept (Nulojix) in 2011 for kidney transplant in adults who already carry Epstein-Barr virus immunity. It offered a way to avoid long-term calcineurin inhibitor damage to the new kidney.", "timeline": [["2005", "LEA29Y (belatacept) is reported to prevent rejection in primate kidney transplants."], ["2011", "FDA approves belatacept (Nulojix) for kidney transplant rejection prophylaxis."], ["2016", "Seven-year BENEFIT trial results show better kidney function and graft survival than cyclosporine."]], "cell": "T cells attacking a transplanted kidney need signal 1, recognition of donor antigen through the T-cell receptor, and signal 2, costimulation through CD28 binding CD80 and CD86 (B7-1 and B7-2) on antigen-presenting cells. Belatacept is the extracellular domain of human CTLA-4 with two amino acid substitutions (L104E and A29Y), fused to a modified IgG1 Fc. It binds CD80 and especially CD86 more tightly than abatacept, coating them so CD28 cannot engage. Without signal 2, T cells do not make IL-2, do not multiply and may become anergic. Unlike calcineurin inhibitors, it acts on the cell surface and does not affect calcineurin in kidney blood vessels, so it avoids their nephrotoxicity, high blood pressure and diabetes.", "body": [["Immune system", "Donor-reactive T cells fail to fully activate, preventing rejection; early acute rejection was somewhat more frequent than with cyclosporine in trials."], ["Kidney graft", "The new kidney is spared calcineurin inhibitor vessel constriction and scarring, so long-term filtration was better in trials."], ["Metabolism", "Blood pressure, lipids and new-onset diabetes are less of a problem than with calcineurin inhibitors."], ["Lymphatic system and brain", "Post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder (PTLD), often in the brain, is the key boxed risk, especially in people without prior EBV immunity."], ["Infection defense", "CMV, BK virus, tuberculosis and rare PML can occur from reduced T-cell control."]], "journey": "Belatacept is a protein given only by IV infusion, more often at first and then every four weeks. It stays in the blood and tissue fluid and is cleared by general protein breakdown, not by the liver or kidneys. Its half-life is about eight to ten days. Because levels do not depend on CYP enzymes, it avoids the many drug interactions and blood level checks needed with calcineurin inhibitors.", "sidefx": "The boxed PTLD warning reflects loss of T-cell control over EBV-infected B cells, which can grow into lymphoma; risk is much higher in people who have never been infected with EBV, so it is limited to EBV-positive recipients. Brain involvement is common in these cases. Infections and PML arise from broad suppression of T-cell responses. It is not recommended in liver transplant because of graft loss and deaths in a trial. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Belatacept differs from abatacept by only two amino acids, yet those small changes made it strong enough for organ transplantation.", "src": ["Nulojix (belatacept) FDA label", "Larsen CP et al. Am J Transplant 2005", "Vincenti F et al. N Engl J Med 2016 (BENEFIT)"]}, "ketoprofen": {"origin": "Ketoprofen was made by chemists at the French company Rhone-Poulenc in the late 1960s as part of the search for better propionic acid NSAIDs, the family that includes ibuprofen and naproxen. It was introduced in Europe in the 1970s and became available in the US in the 1980s as Orudis. Ketoprofen was later sold over the counter in the US for a time, though those products are no longer common. Outside the US it is widely used as gels and patches. Like ibuprofen, it is a chiral molecule, and its activity comes mainly from the S-enantiomer, sold separately in some countries as dexketoprofen.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Rhone-Poulenc chemists synthesize ketoprofen."], ["1970s", "Ketoprofen is introduced in Europe."], ["1980s", "Ketoprofen (Orudis) becomes available in the US."]], "cell": "Cell injury activates phospholipase A2, which frees arachidonic acid from membrane phospholipids. Cyclooxygenase (COX) turns arachidonic acid into prostaglandin H2, the parent of PGE2, prostacyclin and thromboxane A2. Ketoprofen reversibly blocks both COX-1 and COX-2 by competing for the arachidonic acid channel. Less PGE2 at injured tissue means less vessel dilation and swelling and less sensitization of pain nerve endings, so pain from bradykinin and other mediators is felt less. Less PGE2 in the hypothalamus lowers the raised temperature set point in fever. In the uterus, lower prostaglandins reduce the strong contractions of menstrual cramps. Lab studies suggest it may also affect lipoxygenase and bradykinin, but COX blockade is the main mechanism.", "body": [["Joints and soft tissue", "Less prostaglandin at sites of inflammation reduces swelling, stiffness and pain."], ["Uterus", "Lower prostaglandin levels reduce cramping in primary dysmenorrhea."], ["Stomach", "Loss of protective COX-1 prostaglandins thins the mucus and bicarbonate barrier, so ulcers and bleeding can occur."], ["Kidneys", "Prostaglandins keep kidney blood flow up when volume is low; blocking them can cause acute kidney injury and fluid retention."], ["Heart and vessels", "Like all NSAIDs it carries a boxed warning for heart attack and stroke, and it can raise blood pressure."], ["Platelets", "Reversible COX-1 blockade reduces thromboxane, so platelets clump less while drug is present."]], "journey": "Ketoprofen is well absorbed from the gut and reaches peak levels in about half an hour to two hours with the regular capsule; food slows but does not reduce absorption. It is about 99 percent bound to albumin. The liver mainly glucuronidates it, and the glucuronide leaves in the urine. The plasma half-life of the regular form is short, about two to four hours, so extended-release capsules are used for once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "The main side effects are the price of losing prostaglandins. In the stomach this causes dyspepsia, ulcers and bleeding. In the kidneys it can cut filtration, especially with diuretics plus ACE inhibitors or ARBs. Unbalanced COX-2 blockade in vessel walls lowers protective prostacyclin, raising clot risk. Photosensitivity is notable with ketoprofen, especially with topical forms used abroad, because its structure absorbs ultraviolet light. There is no specific antidote; overdose care is supportive.", "fun": "Topical ketoprofen used in Europe is a well-known cause of photoallergic skin reactions, and the structure behind this also links it to reactions with some sunscreen ingredients.", "src": ["Ketoprofen capsules FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "etodolac": {"origin": "Etodolac was developed in the 1970s by chemists at Ayerst Laboratories, part of American Home Products, while searching for new anti-inflammatory compounds related to indole acetic acids. It has a pyranoindole ring system that made it different from older NSAIDs. Later lab studies found that it blocks COX-2 somewhat more than COX-1, which may explain why some studies found less stomach injury than with older NSAIDs. The FDA approved it as Lodine in 1991. The Lodine brand has since been discontinued, but generic capsules, tablets and extended-release tablets remain in use.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Ayerst Laboratories chemists develop etodolac."], ["1991", "FDA approves etodolac (Lodine) in the US."]], "cell": "Etodolac reversibly inhibits cyclooxygenase, the enzyme that converts arachidonic acid into prostaglandin H2 and from there into PGE2, prostacyclin and thromboxane. In lab assays it has a moderate preference for COX-2, the inducible form made at sites of inflammation, over COX-1, the housekeeping form in the stomach and platelets. It is a racemic mixture, and the S-enantiomer carries the COX-blocking activity. Lower PGE2 in inflamed tissue reduces vessel leakage and swelling and lowers the sensitivity of pain nerve endings. In the brain it reduces fever by lowering hypothalamic PGE2. Its COX-2 preference is only partial, so it still affects COX-1 at higher doses.", "body": [["Joints", "Reduced prostaglandins in the synovium ease pain, swelling and stiffness of osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis."], ["Pain nerves", "Analgesia begins in about 30 minutes for acute pain as nerve endings become less sensitive."], ["Stomach", "Ulcers and bleeding can occur, though some studies found less stomach injury than with older NSAIDs."], ["Kidneys", "Blocking kidney prostaglandins can reduce blood flow and cause acute kidney injury or fluid retention."], ["Heart and vessels", "It carries the NSAID boxed warning for heart attack and stroke."], ["Lab tests", "Phenolic metabolites can give a false-positive urine bilirubin test."]], "journey": "Etodolac is well absorbed by mouth and peaks in one to two hours; food delays but does not reduce absorption. It is more than 99 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down by hydroxylation and glucuronidation, and the metabolites leave mainly in the urine, with some in bile. The half-life is about six to seven hours, so immediate-release forms are taken several times a day and extended-release forms once daily.", "sidefx": "The side effects follow from blocking prostaglandins in tissues that need them. Stomach lining protection drops, causing dyspepsia, ulcers and bleeding. Kidney blood flow falls in people who depend on prostaglandins, such as those who are dehydrated or on diuretics plus ACE inhibitors. Reduced prostacyclin raises clot risk. Liver enzyme rises and rare serious skin reactions can occur. There is no antidote; overdose care is supportive.", "src": ["Etodolac FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "nabumetone": {"origin": "Nabumetone was developed by the British company Beecham in the 1970s with a specific goal: an NSAID that would be gentler on the stomach. Most NSAIDs are acids, and part of their stomach damage comes from direct contact with the stomach lining. Beecham chemists designed nabumetone as a non-acidic prodrug that does little to COX until the liver converts it into its active acid, 6-MNA, which is closely related to naproxen. The FDA approved it as Relafen in 1991. Studies showed lower rates of endoscopic ulcers than with some older NSAIDs, though it still carries the class warnings.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Beecham chemists design nabumetone as a non-acidic NSAID prodrug."], ["1991", "FDA approves nabumetone (Relafen) in the US."]], "cell": "Nabumetone itself is a weak COX inhibitor. After absorption, the liver rapidly converts it to 6-methoxy-2-naphthylacetic acid (6-MNA). 6-MNA reversibly blocks COX-1 and COX-2, with some preference for COX-2, by occupying the channel where arachidonic acid would bind. Less prostaglandin H2 means less PGE2 and prostacyclin in inflamed joints, so swelling and pain fall. Because the parent drug is not an acid, it does not get trapped in stomach lining cells the way acidic NSAIDs do, which reduces direct topical injury. However, once 6-MNA circulates it still lowers protective prostaglandins throughout the body, including the stomach and kidneys.", "body": [["Joints", "Prostaglandin-driven inflammation in osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis settles, with full effect over several days."], ["Stomach", "Direct contact injury is lower than with acidic NSAIDs, but systemic COX-1 blockade can still cause ulcers and bleeding."], ["Kidneys", "Kidney prostaglandins are reduced, so acute kidney injury and fluid retention are possible, and doses are adjusted for poor kidney function."], ["Heart and vessels", "It carries the NSAID boxed warning for heart attack and stroke and can raise blood pressure."], ["Platelets", "At usual doses it has only a small effect on platelet function."], ["Skin", "Photosensitivity and rash can occur, so some people burn more easily in sunlight."]], "journey": "Nabumetone is absorbed from the gut and then extensively converted on its first pass through the liver into active 6-MNA, so very little parent drug reaches the blood. Food increases the rate of absorption. 6-MNA is more than 99 percent bound to albumin and has a long half-life, about 24 hours, which allows once-daily dosing. The liver later O-demethylates and conjugates 6-MNA, and the metabolites leave in the urine. Kidney disease slows removal of metabolites, so lower doses are used.", "sidefx": "Because the active form is a COX inhibitor, nabumetone shares all NSAID risks: ulcers and bleeding from lost stomach protection, kidney injury from reduced kidney prostaglandins, and heart attack and stroke from reduced prostacyclin. Its non-acidic prodrug design lowers but does not remove stomach risk. Diarrhea and dyspepsia are common. There is no antidote; overdose care is supportive.", "fun": "The active metabolite of nabumetone differs from naproxen by only a small part of its structure.", "src": ["Relafen (nabumetone) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "sulindac": {"origin": "Sulindac was developed at Merck in the late 1960s and early 1970s as a relative of indomethacin, Merck's powerful but harsh NSAID. Chemists replaced indomethacin's indole ring with an indene ring and added a sulfinyl group, creating an inactive prodrug that the body converts into an active sulfide. The aim was a drug that would cause less stomach and nerve side effects. The FDA approved it as Clinoril in 1978. The brand has been discontinued, but generic sulindac remains available. In the 1990s researchers also found that sulindac can shrink colon polyps in familial adenomatous polyposis, which spurred research into NSAIDs and cancer prevention.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Merck chemists begin work on indomethacin relatives that lead to sulindac."], ["1978", "FDA approves sulindac (Clinoril)."], ["1993", "A trial shows sulindac reduces colon polyps in familial adenomatous polyposis."]], "cell": "Sulindac is a sulfoxide prodrug with little activity itself. In the liver it is reversibly reduced to sulindac sulfide, the active form, and irreversibly oxidized to an inactive sulfone. The sulfide blocks both COX-1 and COX-2 by occupying the arachidonic acid channel, which reduces prostaglandin H2 and its products, PGE2, prostacyclin and thromboxane. Less PGE2 in joints reduces swelling and pain, and in the hypothalamus it lowers fever. A proposed reason for its reputation for gentler kidney effects is that kidney tissue can oxidize the active sulfide back to the inactive sulfoxide, limiting local prostaglandin blockade, though kidney injury still occurs. Both the prodrug and sulfide undergo enterohepatic recirculation, which prolongs its action.", "body": [["Joints and shoulder", "Reduced prostaglandins ease pain and swelling in arthritis, acute painful shoulder and gout flares."], ["Stomach", "Systemic COX-1 blockade can still cause ulcers and bleeding, even though the prodrug is less irritating on contact."], ["Kidneys", "It may spare kidney prostaglandins somewhat, but acute kidney injury and kidney stones made of sulindac metabolites can occur."], ["Liver and pancreas", "Hypersensitivity hepatitis with fever and rash, and pancreatitis, are rare but described with sulindac."], ["Heart and vessels", "It carries the NSAID boxed warning for heart attack and stroke."], ["Colon", "It can shrink adenomatous polyps, an effect studied in familial polyposis."]], "journey": "Sulindac is well absorbed by mouth. The liver reduces it to the active sulfide and oxidizes it to the inactive sulfone. Both the parent and sulfide are excreted into bile and reabsorbed from the gut, which keeps levels up and gives the sulfide a half-life of about 16 hours, longer than the parent's roughly 8 hours. It is highly protein bound. Metabolites leave mostly in the urine as the sulfone and conjugates, with some in the feces.", "sidefx": "Prostaglandin loss explains ulcers, bleeding, fluid retention and kidney injury. Sulindac stands out for a hypersensitivity syndrome with fever, rash and liver injury, and for pancreatitis, which are thought to be immune reactions rather than COX effects. Its metabolites can crystallize as kidney stones. Like other NSAIDs it raises heart attack and stroke risk. DMSO reduces conversion to the active sulfide. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Sulindac's ability to shrink colon polyps helped launch decades of research into whether NSAIDs and aspirin can prevent colorectal cancer.", "src": ["Sulindac FDA label", "Giardiello FM et al. N Engl J Med 1993", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "piroxicam": {"origin": "Piroxicam was created by Joseph Lombardino and colleagues at Pfizer's research center in Groton, Connecticut, in the late 1960s and 1970s. They set out to make an anti-inflammatory drug that was not a carboxylic acid like most NSAIDs, and they arrived at a new chemical class, the oxicams. Its very long half-life allowed once-daily dosing, which was unusual at the time. The FDA approved it as Feldene in 1982, and it became one of the best-selling NSAIDs in the world during the 1980s. Concerns about stomach bleeding, especially in older adults, later led European regulators to restrict its use.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Joseph Lombardino's team at Pfizer begins work leading to the oxicam class."], ["1982", "FDA approves piroxicam (Feldene)."], ["2007", "European regulators restrict piroxicam because of GI and skin risks."]], "cell": "Piroxicam is an enolic acid (oxicam) that reversibly inhibits COX-1 and COX-2 without much preference. By blocking cyclooxygenase, it prevents arachidonic acid from becoming prostaglandin H2, which lowers PGE2, prostacyclin and thromboxane. Less PGE2 in inflamed joints means less vessel leakage, swelling and pain nerve sensitization. It also reduces neutrophil activation and the release of some inflammatory enzymes. Its long-lasting blockade of COX-1 in the stomach is part of why its GI risk is higher than many other NSAIDs. Because levels build slowly, the full anti-inflammatory effect takes one to two weeks.", "body": [["Joints", "Pain, swelling and morning stiffness in osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis ease, with full effect after one to two weeks."], ["Stomach", "Long-lasting COX-1 blockade causes a higher rate of ulcers and bleeding than many other NSAIDs."], ["Kidneys", "Blocking kidney prostaglandins can reduce filtration and cause fluid retention or acute kidney injury."], ["Heart and vessels", "It carries the NSAID boxed warning for heart attack and stroke and can raise blood pressure."], ["Skin", "Serious skin reactions such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome and photosensitivity are reported more often than with some NSAIDs."], ["Platelets", "Platelet function is reduced for as long as drug is present, which is many days because of its long half-life."]], "journey": "Piroxicam is well absorbed by mouth and peaks in three to five hours. It is about 99 percent protein bound. The liver slowly hydroxylates it by CYP2C9 and then glucuronidates it, and it also recirculates through bile. These features give it a very long half-life of about 50 hours, so it takes about a week or more to reach steady levels. People who are CYP2C9 poor metabolizers have much higher levels. Metabolites leave in urine and feces.", "sidefx": "Every major side effect traces to prostaglandin loss: ulcers and GI bleeding from lost mucosal protection, kidney injury and edema from reduced renal blood flow, and clotting events from reduced prostacyclin. Its long half-life means these effects persist for days after a dose, which partly explains its higher GI risk. Skin reactions are thought to be immune-mediated. There is no antidote; overdose care is supportive.", "fun": "The name oxicam was coined for the new class that piroxicam started; meloxicam is a later member of the same family.", "src": ["Feldene (piroxicam) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "isotretinoin": {"origin": "Isotretinoin is a form of vitamin A acid, 13-cis-retinoic acid. Vitamin A derivatives were studied for skin disease from the mid-1900s, and Hoffmann-La Roche made isotretinoin and tested it as a possible cancer-prevention drug. In the late 1970s dermatologist Gary Peck and colleagues at the US National Cancer Institute gave it to patients with severe, scarring nodular acne and saw remarkable, lasting clearance; they reported the results in 1979. The FDA approved it as Accutane in 1982. It quickly became clear that it causes severe birth defects, and strict pregnancy prevention programs followed, leading to the iPLEDGE program in 2006. The Accutane brand was discontinued in 2009, but several generic and branded capsules remain.", "timeline": [["1979", "Peck and colleagues report dramatic results in severe nodular acne."], ["1982", "FDA approves isotretinoin (Accutane)."], ["2006", "The iPLEDGE REMS program begins."], ["2009", "The Accutane brand is discontinued; other brands and generics remain."]], "cell": "Isotretinoin enters cells and is partly converted to all-trans-retinoic acid (tretinoin) and other isomers. These bind nuclear retinoic acid receptors (RAR), which pair with retinoid X receptors (RXR) on DNA and change which genes are read. In sebaceous glands the main effect is apoptosis and shrinkage of sebocytes, which cuts sebum production by up to about 90 percent. In the hair follicle lining it normalizes how keratinocytes mature and shed, preventing the plugs that form comedones. With less oil, Cutibacterium acnes loses its food source and numbers fall, and inflammation settles. It is the only acne drug that addresses all four main causes of acne: excess oil, follicle plugging, bacteria and inflammation.", "body": [["Skin and oil glands", "Sebaceous glands shrink and oil output drops sharply, so nodules and cysts clear, often with lasting remission after one course."], ["Lips, eyes and nose", "Drying of mucous membranes causes chapped lips, dry eyes and nosebleeds in nearly everyone."], ["Developing fetus", "Retinoic acid signaling guides embryo development, so exposure causes severe birth defects of the face, heart, brain and thymus."], ["Brain", "Depression, mood changes and rare suicidal thoughts are reported, and raised intracranial pressure can occur, especially with tetracyclines."], ["Lipids and liver", "Triglycerides can rise sharply and liver enzymes can increase; very high triglycerides can cause pancreatitis."], ["Muscles and bones", "Muscle and joint aches are common, and bone density effects and early growth plate closure have been described."]], "journey": "Isotretinoin is a fat-soluble capsule. Generic forms are absorbed much better with a fatty meal, while one brand uses a formulation designed for consistent absorption with or without food. Levels peak in two to five hours, and almost all of it is bound to albumin. The liver breaks it down using CYP2C8, CYP2C9, CYP3A4 and CYP2B6, mainly into 4-oxo-isotretinoin, and it also interconverts with tretinoin. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. The half-life is about 21 hours, so it clears within weeks after stopping, unlike acitretin.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are retinoid effects on cells that respond to retinoic acid. Drying of skin and mucous membranes comes from reduced gland output and altered skin cell maturation. Birth defects arise because retinoic acid gradients pattern the embryo, which is why pregnancy prevention under iPLEDGE is required. Raised intracranial pressure may relate to effects on fluid absorption in the brain, and the risk rises with tetracyclines. Lipid rises reflect altered liver fat handling. There is no antidote; most effects reverse after stopping.", "fun": "Before isotretinoin, there was no medicine that could produce long-lasting remission of severe nodular acne after a single course.", "src": ["Absorica (isotretinoin) FDA label", "Peck GL et al. N Engl J Med 1979", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "acitretin": {"origin": "Acitretin is part of the second generation of synthetic retinoids made by Hoffmann-La Roche. Roche first developed etretinate, an aromatic retinoid that worked well for severe psoriasis and was approved in the US as Tegison in 1986. Etretinate was very fat-soluble and stayed in body fat for years, which was a serious problem because retinoids cause birth defects. Scientists knew the body turns etretinate into an active acid, acitretin, which leaves the body much faster. Roche developed acitretin itself as Soriatane, approved by the FDA in 1996, and etretinate was later withdrawn. The catch, discovered later, is that alcohol can turn acitretin back into etretinate, which is why strict alcohol and pregnancy rules apply.", "timeline": [["1986", "FDA approves etretinate (Tegison), the parent aromatic retinoid, for psoriasis."], ["1996", "FDA approves acitretin (Soriatane) for severe psoriasis."], ["1990s", "Researchers find that alcohol converts acitretin back into long-lasting etretinate."]], "cell": "Acitretin enters skin cells and binds nuclear retinoic acid receptors (RAR alpha, beta and gamma), which work with retinoid X receptors (RXR) to control gene transcription. In psoriasis, keratinocytes multiply far too fast and do not mature properly, building thick, scaly plaques. Acitretin slows keratinocyte proliferation and restores normal differentiation and shedding. It also reduces the movement of neutrophils into the epidermis and dampens inflammatory signaling, which is why it works especially well for pustular psoriasis. Unlike methotrexate or biologics, it is not an immunosuppressant. Its effects build gradually over two to three months.", "body": [["Skin", "Plaques thin and scale less as keratinocyte growth normalizes; pustular and erythrodermic psoriasis respond well."], ["Lips, eyes and nose", "Dry lips, dry eyes, nosebleeds and rhinitis are common because retinoids reduce gland secretion."], ["Hair and nails", "Hair thinning and fragile nails can occur because retinoids affect fast-growing keratin structures."], ["Liver and lipids", "Liver enzymes and triglycerides can rise; hepatitis and pancreatitis are rare but serious."], ["Developing fetus", "Retinoids disrupt embryo patterning, causing severe birth defects, so pregnancy must be avoided during and for at least three years after treatment."], ["Bones and brain", "Bone spurs (hyperostosis) and raised intracranial pressure can occur, especially with tetracyclines."]], "journey": "Acitretin is taken with the main meal, because fat improves absorption. It peaks in two to five hours and is more than 99.9 percent bound to albumin. The liver converts it partly to cis-acitretin, and metabolites leave in feces and urine. Its own half-life is about two days. With alcohol, however, it is re-esterified into etretinate, which is stored in fat and has a half-life of about 120 days, so traces can remain for years.", "sidefx": "The drying of skin and mucous membranes, hair loss and nail changes are direct retinoid effects on how epithelial cells grow and secrete. Birth defects come from retinoic acid's role in shaping the embryo, and the long-lived etretinate formed with alcohol is why pregnancy must be avoided for three years after stopping. Liver toxicity and very high triglycerides reflect retinoid effects on liver metabolism, which is why combining it with methotrexate is contraindicated. Tetracyclines add to intracranial pressure risk. There is no antidote.", "fun": "People taking acitretin are asked not to donate blood during treatment and for three years after, so that a pregnant recipient is never exposed.", "src": ["Soriatane (acitretin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "adapalene": {"origin": "Adapalene was designed in the 1980s by scientists at the CIRD Galderma research center in Sophia Antipolis, France. Tretinoin, the first topical retinoid, worked for acne but was irritating, broke down in light and was inactivated by benzoyl peroxide. Galderma chemists built a new, rigid naphthoic acid retinoid that targets only certain retinoic acid receptors and is stable in light and alongside benzoyl peroxide. The FDA approved Differin gel in 1996. In 2016 the 0.1 percent gel became available over the counter, the first new active ingredient for over-the-counter acne products in the US since the 1980s. It is also combined with benzoyl peroxide in Epiduo.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Galderma scientists in France design adapalene as a stable, selective retinoid."], ["1996", "FDA approves adapalene (Differin) gel for acne."], ["2008", "FDA approves adapalene with benzoyl peroxide (Epiduo)."], ["2016", "Adapalene 0.1 percent gel becomes available over the counter."]], "cell": "Adapalene enters keratinocytes and binds nuclear retinoic acid receptors, selectively RAR-beta and RAR-gamma, the main RAR in skin; it does not bind RXR. The receptor complex changes gene transcription to normalize how follicle lining cells mature and detach. In acne, these cells stick together and pile up, forming the microcomedone, the first lesion of acne. By making them shed normally, adapalene prevents new plugs and loosens existing ones, so it is both comedolytic and anti-comedogenic. It also reduces AP-1 driven inflammation and the movement of white cells, which helps inflammatory pimples. Because its rigid structure resists light and oxidation, it stays active when used with benzoyl peroxide.", "body": [["Hair follicles", "Follicle lining cells shed normally, so microcomedones, blackheads and whiteheads clear over weeks."], ["Skin inflammation", "Inflammatory papules and pustules decrease as AP-1 signaling and white cell recruitment fall."], ["Skin surface", "Dryness, redness, scaling and stinging are common in the first weeks while skin adjusts."], ["Sun sensitivity", "Thinner outer skin layers make sunburn more likely, so sunscreen is typically recommended."]], "journey": "Adapalene is applied as a thin layer to the face or other acne areas. It concentrates in the follicles and upper skin layers. Very little crosses into the blood; levels are usually too low to measure. The tiny absorbed fraction is thought to leave mainly through bile. Early improvement appears in two to four weeks, with full effect around 8 to 12 weeks.", "sidefx": "Irritation comes from the same action that clears acne: speeding up and normalizing skin cell turnover thins the outer barrier, causing dryness, peeling and stinging, especially in the first month. Acne may seem to flare early as existing microcomedones come to the surface. Thinner outer layers explain sunburn sensitivity. Because almost none reaches the blood, whole-body side effects are not expected. There is no antidote; stopping or using less often lets the skin recover.", "fun": "When adapalene went over the counter in 2016, it became the first retinoid sold for acne in the US without a prescription.", "src": ["Differin (adapalene) gel Drug Facts and FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "benzoyl-peroxide": {"origin": "Benzoyl peroxide is a simple organic peroxide first described by the German chemist Justus von Liebig in the 1850s. In the early 1900s it was used to bleach flour and as a chemical starter for plastics. Doctors also used it on wounds and skin ulcers. Its use for acne grew in the mid-1900s, and it became one of the most common acne treatments sold without a prescription. It is now found in washes, gels and creams and in combination products with clindamycin or adapalene. In 2025 some over-the-counter products were recalled after testing found benzene, a contaminant that can form when the product breaks down.", "timeline": [["1850s", "Justus von Liebig describes benzoyl peroxide."], ["1900s", "Benzoyl peroxide is used to bleach flour and in industry."], ["1960s", "Benzoyl peroxide becomes widely used for acne."], ["2025", "Some OTC products are recalled for benzene contamination."]], "cell": "Benzoyl peroxide is fat-soluble, so it slips into the oily environment of the hair follicle. There it breaks down into benzoic acid and reactive oxygen species. These free radicals oxidize proteins and lipids in Cutibacterium acnes, killing the bacteria quickly. Because the attack is broad chemical oxidation rather than a single target, bacteria do not develop resistance. It also loosens the sticky keratinocytes that plug the follicle (a mild keratolytic and comedolytic effect) and reduces the free fatty acids that bacteria make from sebum. This combination reduces inflammatory pimples and helps prevent new ones. Pairing it with clindamycin or erythromycin lowers the chance of antibiotic-resistant bacteria.", "body": [["Hair follicles", "C. acnes numbers drop within days, so inflammatory papules and pustules fade over a few weeks."], ["Skin surface", "Mild peeling helps unplug pores, but also causes dryness, redness and stinging, especially at higher strengths."], ["Immune reaction", "A small number of people develop allergic contact dermatitis, and rare serious allergic reactions have been reported."], ["Hair and fabric", "As an oxidizer it bleaches hair, towels, pillowcases and clothing."]], "journey": "Benzoyl peroxide is applied to the skin and works locally in the follicles. In the skin it is converted to benzoic acid. A small amount of benzoic acid reaches the blood and is handled the same way as benzoic acid from food: the liver joins it to glycine to make hippuric acid, which leaves in the urine. Blood exposure is not clinically important. Antibacterial effects begin within days, with visible improvement over four to eight weeks.", "sidefx": "Dryness, peeling and burning come from its oxidizing action on the outer skin layer. Allergic contact dermatitis is an immune response to the chemical. Bleaching of fabrics and hair is the same oxidation that kills bacteria. Applying it at the same time as tretinoin can oxidize and inactivate tretinoin, while adapalene is stable with it. Topical dapsone used with it can turn skin temporarily orange-brown. There is no antidote; irritation settles after stopping.", "fun": "Benzoyl peroxide is still used industrially to bleach flour and some cheese, the same oxidizing property that bleaches towels.", "src": ["Benzoyl peroxide OTC Drug Facts", "FDA safety communication on topical acne products", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "clindamycin-topical": {"origin": "Clindamycin descends from lincomycin, an antibiotic found in the early 1960s by Upjohn scientists in a Streptomyces lincolnensis culture from soil near Lincoln, Nebraska. In 1966 Upjohn chemist Barney Magerlein swapped one hydroxyl group on lincomycin for a chlorine atom, creating clindamycin, which was better absorbed and more potent. Oral clindamycin was approved in 1970. Doctors later found it worked well for acne when applied to the skin, and Upjohn developed topical solutions, followed by gels, lotions and foams. Today it is most often used together with benzoyl peroxide, in combination products that help prevent resistance.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Upjohn scientists discover lincomycin from Streptomyces lincolnensis."], ["1966", "Barney Magerlein creates clindamycin by chemically modifying lincomycin."], ["1970", "FDA approves oral clindamycin."], ["2000s", "Fixed combinations of clindamycin and benzoyl peroxide become common for acne."]], "cell": "Clindamycin phosphate is a prodrug that skin enzymes split into active clindamycin. Clindamycin enters Cutibacterium acnes and binds the 23S rRNA of the bacterial 50S ribosomal subunit, close to the peptidyl transferase center. This blocks peptide bond formation and translocation, so the bacteria cannot make proteins and stop growing (a bacteriostatic effect). With fewer bacteria, less sebum is broken down into irritating free fatty acids, and fewer bacterial signals attract neutrophils. Clindamycin also reduces neutrophil chemotaxis directly. Resistance develops through erm genes that methylate the 23S binding site, giving cross-resistance to macrolides, which is why it is paired with benzoyl peroxide.", "body": [["Hair follicles", "Lower C. acnes numbers and less inflammation reduce red papules and pustules over 6 to 12 weeks."], ["Skin surface", "Dryness, redness, burning and peeling can occur, partly from the alcohol in solutions and foams."], ["Gut", "Rare cases of diarrhea and C. difficile colitis have been reported even with topical use, because a small amount is absorbed."], ["Bacterial ecology", "Used alone, it promotes clindamycin-resistant C. acnes and staph on the skin."]], "journey": "Clindamycin phosphate is applied to the skin, where it is converted to active clindamycin in the follicles. Only a very small fraction is absorbed into the blood. That small amount is broken down in the liver by CYP3A4 and leaves in the urine and bile. Blood levels after normal topical use are very low. Improvement appears over one to two weeks, with full effect over 6 to 12 weeks.", "sidefx": "Local irritation comes from the vehicle and from changes in the skin surface. Resistance reflects the selection pressure of an antibiotic on skin bacteria, which is why it is combined with benzoyl peroxide. The rare reports of colitis show that even a small absorbed amount can upset gut bacteria and allow C. difficile to overgrow. Topical erythromycin competes for the same ribosome site and is not used with it. There is no antidote; colitis is treated by stopping the drug and treating C. difficile.", "fun": "Clindamycin is a lincosamide, a name that honors Lincoln, Nebraska, where the parent drug's soil organism was found.", "src": ["Cleocin T (clindamycin phosphate) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "dapsone-topical": {"origin": "Dapsone was first made in 1908 by the German chemists Emil Fromm and Jakob Wittmann as part of dye chemistry, long before anyone knew it was a medicine. In the late 1930s it was found to have antibacterial activity, and in the 1940s sulfone drugs transformed leprosy treatment, starting with work at the US leprosarium in Carville, Louisiana. Dapsone itself became the backbone of leprosy therapy. Dermatologists also noticed it calmed neutrophil-driven skin diseases such as dermatitis herpetiformis. Taking it by mouth can cause methemoglobinemia and hemolysis, so a gel was developed to treat acne with far lower blood levels. The FDA approved dapsone 5 percent gel (Aczone) in 2005 and a once-daily 7.5 percent gel in 2016.", "timeline": [["1908", "Emil Fromm and Jakob Wittmann first synthesize dapsone."], ["1940s", "Sulfone drugs transform leprosy treatment at Carville, Louisiana."], ["2005", "FDA approves dapsone 5 percent gel (Aczone) for acne."], ["2016", "FDA approves dapsone 7.5 percent gel for once-daily use."]], "cell": "Dapsone has two actions. As an antibacterial, it is a sulfone that competes with para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA) at dihydropteroate synthase, blocking bacterial folate synthesis. In acne, its anti-inflammatory action matters more. Neutrophils drawn into inflamed follicles use the enzyme myeloperoxidase to make hypochlorous acid and other reactive oxygen species that damage tissue. Dapsone inhibits myeloperoxidase and reduces neutrophil chemotaxis and adherence. It also interferes with signaling that recruits neutrophils, such as responses to the chemoattractant fMLP. Less neutrophil activity in the follicle means fewer red, swollen papules and pustules, with some effect on comedones as well.", "body": [["Hair follicles", "Neutrophil-driven inflammation falls, so inflammatory acne lesions decrease over about 12 weeks."], ["Skin surface", "Dryness, itching and redness at the application site are the most common effects."], ["Blood", "Methemoglobinemia, and hemolysis in G6PD deficiency, have been reported but are rare at topical exposure levels."], ["Skin color with benzoyl peroxide", "Using it with benzoyl peroxide can turn skin and facial hair temporarily yellow or orange-brown."]], "journey": "The gel is rubbed onto the face and works in the skin and follicles. Only a small amount enters the blood; levels are far lower, roughly a hundred times lower, than with oral dapsone. The absorbed drug is processed in the liver by N-acetylation and by CYP enzymes that make a hydroxylamine metabolite, and metabolites leave in the urine. Oral dapsone has a half-life of about 30 hours.", "sidefx": "Local dryness and itching come from the vehicle and skin effects. The serious oral dapsone toxicities come from its hydroxylamine metabolite, which oxidizes hemoglobin iron to the ferric state, making methemoglobin that cannot carry oxygen, and stresses red cells. People with G6PD deficiency cannot handle that oxidant stress, so their red cells break. With the gel these effects are rare because so little is absorbed. Methemoglobinemia, when significant, is treated with methylene blue, except in G6PD deficiency.", "fun": "Dapsone was made about 30 years before anyone tested whether it could treat infections.", "src": ["Aczone (dapsone) gel FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "azelaic-acid": {"origin": "Azelaic acid is a natural nine-carbon acid found in grains such as wheat and rye and made by Malassezia yeast on the skin. In the 1970s Italian researchers including Marcella Nazzaro-Porro and Siro Passi were studying why tinea versicolor, a Malassezia infection, leaves pale patches. They found that azelaic acid made by the yeast blocks pigment production. Testing it as a skin treatment, they found it helped melasma and acne. A 20 percent cream (Azelex) was approved in the US for acne in the mid-1990s, and a 15 percent gel (Finacea) was approved for rosacea in 2002, followed by a foam.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Italian researchers link Malassezia-made azelaic acid to pale skin patches in tinea versicolor."], ["1990s", "Azelaic acid 20 percent cream (Azelex) is approved in the US for acne."], ["2002", "FDA approves azelaic acid 15 percent gel (Finacea) for rosacea."], ["2015", "FDA approves Finacea foam for rosacea."]], "cell": "Azelaic acid works on several targets at once. In bacteria such as Cutibacterium acnes and Staphylococcus epidermidis it inhibits protein synthesis and disturbs energy metabolism, reducing their numbers. In the follicle lining it slows keratinocyte overgrowth and normalizes how cells mature, reducing comedones. It scavenges and reduces reactive oxygen species released by neutrophils, which helps calm inflammation. In rosacea it lowers kallikrein 5 and the antimicrobial peptide cathelicidin (LL-37), which are overactive in rosacea skin and drive redness and bumps. In melanocytes it competitively inhibits tyrosinase, the key enzyme of melanin production, and it is more active in overactive pigment cells than in normal ones. This is why it can lighten dark spots left after acne.", "body": [["Hair follicles", "Fewer bacteria and normalized cell shedding reduce inflammatory acne lesions over about four weeks and more."], ["Face in rosacea", "Inflammatory papules, pustules and redness decrease as cathelicidin and oxidative inflammation calm down."], ["Pigment cells", "Tyrosinase inhibition fades dark marks after acne and melasma, but can cause unwanted lightening in darker skin."], ["Skin surface", "Burning, stinging and tingling are common at first, especially on sensitive rosacea skin."], ["Airways", "Worsening of asthma has been reported after use, although the cause is unclear."]], "journey": "Applied to the skin, azelaic acid works mainly in the upper skin layers. A small portion, generally a few percent, reaches the blood. Because it is a natural dietary acid, the body handles the absorbed amount easily: some is partly broken down by beta-oxidation like a fatty acid, and much of it leaves unchanged in the urine. The half-life of the absorbed portion is about 12 hours.", "sidefx": "Burning and stinging come from an acid acting on sensitive or inflamed skin, and usually ease as the skin adjusts. Lightening of skin in people with darker skin follows from its tyrosinase-blocking action on pigment cells. Contact dermatitis is uncommon. There are no known significant drug interactions, and whole-body effects are not expected because absorption is low. There is no antidote; irritation resolves after stopping.", "fun": "The pale patches of tinea versicolor led to the discovery of a skin-lightening and acne medicine made by the very yeast that causes them.", "src": ["Azelex (azelaic acid) FDA label", "Finacea (azelaic acid) FDA label", "Nazzaro-Porro M, Passi S. J Invest Dermatol 1978"]}, "mupirocin": {"origin": "Mupirocin is made by the soil and water bacterium Pseudomonas fluorescens. In the early 1970s scientists at Beecham Research Laboratories in England isolated a mix of compounds from it called pseudomonic acids, and pseudomonic acid A became mupirocin. It had an unusual target that no other antibiotic shared, so it showed no cross-resistance with existing drugs. It is broken down too quickly in the body to be useful as a pill or injection, so it was developed as a skin ointment. The FDA approved Bactroban in 1987. A nasal ointment followed for clearing Staphylococcus aureus, including MRSA, from the nose to reduce infections in hospitals.", "timeline": [["1971", "Beecham scientists report pseudomonic acid from Pseudomonas fluorescens."], ["1987", "FDA approves mupirocin ointment (Bactroban) in the US."], ["1990s", "Mupirocin nasal ointment is used to clear MRSA from the nose."]], "cell": "To build proteins, bacteria must load each amino acid onto its matching transfer RNA. Isoleucyl-tRNA synthetase is the enzyme that attaches isoleucine to its tRNA. Mupirocin's structure mimics isoleucyl-adenylate, the activated intermediate in that reaction, so it binds the enzyme's active site and blocks it. Without charged isoleucyl-tRNA, ribosomes stall, and protein and RNA synthesis stop. At lower concentrations this halts growth, and at the high concentrations reached on the skin it kills staphylococci and streptococci. Human isoleucyl-tRNA synthetase is different enough that mupirocin barely affects it. High-level resistance comes from a plasmid gene, mupA (ileS2), that makes a different, resistant synthetase.", "body": [["Skin", "Impetigo and infected minor wounds clear as Staphylococcus aureus and Streptococcus pyogenes are killed, usually with response in three to five days."], ["Nose", "Nasal ointment clears S. aureus, including MRSA, from the nostrils, which lowers the risk of the person spreading or developing staph infection."], ["Application site", "Burning, stinging and itching are common, and the nasal form can cause a runny nose."], ["Kidneys with large wounds", "The polyethylene glycol base can be absorbed from large damaged skin areas, which could harm the kidneys."]], "journey": "Mupirocin is applied to the skin or inside the nose and acts locally. Very little passes through intact skin. Any mupirocin that reaches the blood is rapidly broken down, with a half-life of well under an hour, into inactive monic acid, which leaves in the urine. This fast breakdown is why it only works as a topical drug.", "sidefx": "Local burning and itching are reactions to the ointment and to inflamed skin. Prolonged or repeated use selects for resistant MRSA carrying the mupA gene and lets fungi and other organisms overgrow. Allergic reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, are immune reactions. The PEG ointment base is the concern on large burns or wounds, not mupirocin itself. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Mupirocin's target, isoleucyl-tRNA synthetase, was not hit by any other antibiotic when it was introduced, so even multidrug-resistant staph were initially susceptible.", "src": ["Bactroban (mupirocin) FDA label", "Fuller AT et al. Nature 1971"]}, "bacitracin": {"origin": "Bacitracin was discovered at Columbia University by bacteriologist Balbina Johnson and surgeon Frank Meleney. In 1943 they cultured a bacterium from the contaminated leg wound of a young girl named Margaret Tracy and found it killed other bacteria. They named the substance bacitracin, joining Bacillus with her last name, and published their findings in 1945. The organism was described as a Bacillus subtilis strain, later classified with Bacillus licheniformis. Bacitracin was approved by the FDA in 1948. It was once given by injection, but because of kidney damage that use was withdrawn in the US in 2020. Today it is used mainly as an over-the-counter first aid ointment and an eye ointment.", "timeline": [["1943", "Johnson and Meleney find an antibiotic-producing Bacillus in a wound."], ["1945", "Bacitracin is described in the journal Science."], ["1948", "FDA approves bacitracin."], ["2020", "Bacitracin for injection is withdrawn in the US because of kidney toxicity."]], "cell": "Bacteria build their peptidoglycan wall by making precursor units inside the cell and ferrying them across the membrane on a lipid carrier called bactoprenol (undecaprenyl, C55-isoprenyl) phosphate. After each unit is delivered, the carrier is left as a pyrophosphate and must be dephosphorylated to be used again. Bacitracin, a cyclic polypeptide, binds bactoprenol pyrophosphate with the help of a metal ion such as zinc and prevents this dephosphorylation. The carrier gets stuck, new wall material cannot be moved out, and cell wall synthesis stops. Growing bacteria then burst. It acts mainly on gram-positive organisms such as staphylococci and streptococci, because gram-negative outer membranes keep it out.", "body": [["Skin wounds", "Gram-positive bacteria on minor cuts, scrapes and burns are killed, helping to prevent infection."], ["Eyes", "As an eye ointment it treats or prevents surface infections caused by gram-positive bacteria."], ["Immune reaction", "Allergic contact dermatitis is common; bacitracin was named Allergen of the Year by the American Contact Dermatitis Society in 2003."], ["Kidneys", "When absorbed in large amounts, as with the old injection, it damages kidney tubules."]], "journey": "Bacitracin ointment stays on the skin and is not significantly absorbed through intact skin. Some can be absorbed through large open wounds or burns. The absorbed amount is filtered by the kidneys and leaves in the urine. It is a large peptide that is not absorbed from the gut, so it is not used by mouth for systemic infection.", "sidefx": "The common problem is allergic contact dermatitis, an immune reaction to the peptide that can look like a worsening wound. Rare anaphylaxis has occurred even after topical use. Kidney injury comes from direct toxicity to kidney tubules when blood levels are high, which is why injection use ended. Combining it with neomycin, as in triple antibiotic ointment, adds another common allergen. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Bacitracin is one of the few drugs named after a patient: Margaret Tracy, whose wound grew the bacterium that makes it.", "src": ["Bacitracin ointment OTC Drug Facts", "Johnson BA, Anker H, Meleney FL. Science 1945", "FDA notice on bacitracin for injection, 2020"]}, "clobetasol": {"origin": "Clobetasol stands on the shoulders of cortisone, which Philip Hench and Edward Kendall showed could reverse rheumatoid arthritis in 1949. In 1952 Marion Sulzberger and Victor Witten showed that hydrocortisone applied to the skin cleared eczema, opening the era of topical steroids. Chemists then added halogens and other groups to make steroids stronger in the skin. In the late 1960s and early 1970s scientists at Glaxo in Britain developed clobetasol propionate, which was launched in the UK as Dermovate and later in the US as Temovate. It became the benchmark super-high potency (class I) topical steroid.", "timeline": [["1949", "Hench and Kendall show cortisone's dramatic effect in rheumatoid arthritis."], ["1952", "Sulzberger and Witten report topical hydrocortisone for skin disease."], ["1970s", "Glaxo introduces clobetasol propionate (Dermovate) in the UK."], ["1980s", "Clobetasol (Temovate) becomes available in the US."]], "cell": "Clobetasol is a halogenated synthetic glucocorticoid that crosses the cell membrane and binds the glucocorticoid receptor in the cytoplasm. The receptor drops its chaperone proteins, enters the nucleus and acts in two ways. It turns on anti-inflammatory genes, such as annexin A1 (lipocortin), which blocks phospholipase A2 and so cuts production of prostaglandins and leukotrienes. It also binds and blocks NF-kB and AP-1, shutting down genes for cytokines, adhesion molecules and inflammatory enzymes. In the skin this reduces T-cell and mast cell activity, narrows small blood vessels and slows keratinocyte and fibroblast growth. The vessel-narrowing effect is used to rank steroid potency, and clobetasol is at the top.", "body": [["Skin inflammation", "Redness, swelling and itching fall quickly in psoriasis, eczema and other steroid-responsive rashes."], ["Psoriasis plaques", "Slower keratinocyte growth flattens thick plaques and reduces scale."], ["Skin structure", "With prolonged use, slower fibroblast and collagen production thins the skin, causing atrophy, stretch marks and visible small vessels."], ["Adrenal glands", "Enough can be absorbed from large areas or under occlusion to suppress the HPA axis, lowering the body's own cortisol."], ["Eyes", "Use near the eyes can raise eye pressure and cause cataracts."], ["Skin infection", "Local immune suppression can let fungal or bacterial infections spread."]], "journey": "Clobetasol is applied to the skin and stored in the outer layer (stratum corneum), which acts as a reservoir and releases drug for hours. How much is absorbed depends on skin thickness and condition, the vehicle, the area covered and whether it is covered. Thin skin, inflamed skin and occlusion increase absorption greatly. The absorbed fraction is processed in the liver and leaves in urine and bile.", "sidefx": "Local side effects come from the same actions that make it work: slowing fibroblast and collagen production thins the skin, and constricting then weakening vessels leads to telangiectasia and easy bruising. Perioral dermatitis and steroid rosacea can occur on the face. Systemic absorption can suppress the adrenal glands and cause Cushing features or high blood sugar, which is why courses are limited to about two weeks and a weekly amount. Stopping after long use can cause a rebound flare or adrenal insufficiency.", "fun": "Topical steroid potency is ranked partly by how strongly a steroid blanches the skin, a test called the vasoconstrictor assay.", "src": ["Temovate (clobetasol propionate) FDA label", "Sulzberger MB, Witten VH. J Invest Dermatol 1952", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "tacrolimus-topical": {"origin": "Tacrolimus was discovered in 1984 by scientists at the Japanese company Fujisawa in a soil sample from near Tsukuba, Japan, which contained the bacterium Streptomyces tsukubaensis. The compound, code-named FK506, was a powerful immunosuppressant. Thomas Starzl's team at the University of Pittsburgh used it in liver transplant patients starting in 1989, and the FDA approved it for transplants as Prograf in 1994. Researchers then worked on a form for the skin that could calm eczema without the skin thinning seen with steroids. The FDA approved tacrolimus ointment (Protopic) in 2000, the first topical calcineurin inhibitor. In 2006 a boxed warning was added about rare reports of cancer, though a causal link has not been established.", "timeline": [["1984", "Fujisawa scientists discover tacrolimus (FK506) in Streptomyces tsukubaensis."], ["1989", "Thomas Starzl's team begins using tacrolimus in liver transplant."], ["1994", "FDA approves oral and IV tacrolimus (Prograf) for transplantation."], ["2000", "FDA approves tacrolimus ointment (Protopic) for atopic dermatitis."], ["2006", "A boxed warning on rare malignancy reports is added to topical calcineurin inhibitors."]], "cell": "Tacrolimus enters T cells and binds the immunophilin FKBP-12. The tacrolimus-FKBP-12 complex binds and inhibits calcineurin, a calcium-activated phosphatase. When a T cell is stimulated, calcineurin normally removes phosphate groups from the transcription factor NFAT, allowing NFAT to enter the nucleus and switch on genes for IL-2, IL-4, interferon-gamma and other cytokines. With calcineurin blocked, NFAT stays in the cytoplasm and cytokine production falls, so skin T cells do not expand or drive inflammation. Tacrolimus also reduces mediator release from mast cells and basophils and affects Langerhans cells. Unlike corticosteroids, it does not slow fibroblasts or collagen production, so it does not thin the skin.", "body": [["Skin inflammation", "Redness, itching and thickened skin in eczema improve as T-cell cytokines and mast cell mediators fall."], ["Face and eyelids", "Because it does not cause atrophy, it is useful on thin skin where strong steroids are risky."], ["Application site", "Burning, warmth and stinging are common in the first days, thought to come partly from release of the nerve mediator substance P."], ["Skin infection", "Local immune suppression can allow herpes simplex (eczema herpeticum), varicella zoster or folliculitis."], ["Alcohol reaction", "Some people get facial flushing after drinking alcohol while using it."]], "journey": "The ointment is applied to affected skin and works in the epidermis and dermis. Because tacrolimus is a large molecule, little crosses intact skin, and blood levels are usually low or not measurable. Absorption increases with large areas of damaged or very inflamed skin. Any absorbed drug is broken down in the liver by CYP3A4 and leaves mainly in the feces. After IV use the systemic half-life is long, but this matters little with topical exposure.", "sidefx": "The burning sensation is thought to come from activation and then depletion of substance P in skin nerves, which is why it fades with continued use. Skin infections occur because local T-cell defense is reduced. The boxed warning describes rare reports of skin cancer and lymphoma; a causal link has not been shown, but it is used for short or intermittent courses and not under occlusion or with phototherapy. The 0.1 percent strength is not labeled for children. There is no antidote.", "fun": "FK506 was named after its code number at Fujisawa, and the 'tsukubaensis' in its source organism honors the Tsukuba area of Japan where the soil was collected.", "src": ["Protopic (tacrolimus) ointment FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Starzl TE et al. Lancet 1989"]}, "pimecrolimus": {"origin": "Pimecrolimus was developed by Novartis as a skin-focused cousin of tacrolimus. Its parent compound, ascomycin, is a natural macrolide made by the bacterium Streptomyces hygroscopicus var. ascomyceticus and is closely related to tacrolimus. Novartis chemists modified ascomycin to make a molecule that is very fat-soluble and stays in the skin, with less tendency to reach the blood or suppress immunity throughout the body. The FDA approved pimecrolimus 1 percent cream (Elidel) in 2001 for mild to moderate atopic dermatitis, about a year after tacrolimus ointment. In 2006 it received the same boxed warning as tacrolimus ointment about rare reports of cancer, though a causal link has not been established.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Novartis scientists develop pimecrolimus from the natural macrolide ascomycin."], ["2001", "FDA approves pimecrolimus cream (Elidel) for atopic dermatitis."], ["2006", "A boxed warning on rare malignancy reports is added to topical calcineurin inhibitors."]], "cell": "Pimecrolimus enters T cells and mast cells in the skin and binds the immunophilin FKBP-12 (also called macrophilin-12). The pimecrolimus-FKBP-12 complex binds calcineurin and blocks its phosphatase activity. Calcineurin normally dephosphorylates NFAT after T-cell activation so NFAT can move into the nucleus and switch on cytokine genes. With calcineurin blocked, T cells make much less IL-2, interferon-gamma and the Th2 cytokines IL-4 and IL-10 that drive eczema. In mast cells it reduces the release of histamine and other mediators after allergen exposure. It is more selective for skin inflammatory cells than tacrolimus and has little effect on Langerhans cells or on fibroblasts, so it does not thin the skin.", "body": [["Skin inflammation", "Itching and redness of mild to moderate eczema improve within days to a couple of weeks."], ["Face and eyelids", "It can be used on the face and eyelids, where steroids risk thinning and other effects."], ["Application site", "Warmth or burning is common at first and usually fades within a week."], ["Skin infection", "Herpes simplex, eczema herpeticum, molluscum and warts can appear because local antiviral defense is reduced."], ["Lymph nodes", "Swollen lymph nodes have been reported and are checked to rule out infection or other causes."]], "journey": "The cream is applied to affected skin, where pimecrolimus binds strongly to skin tissue. Because it is large and very fat-soluble, very little passes into the blood, and levels are usually below detection even in children treated over large areas. Any absorbed drug is broken down in the liver by CYP3A4 and leaves mainly in the feces.", "sidefx": "Local warmth and burning are thought to come from effects on skin sensory nerves and usually fade with use. Skin infections, especially viral ones, reflect reduced local T-cell defense. The boxed warning reflects rare reports of skin cancer and lymphoma; a causal link has not been shown, and it is used for short or intermittent treatment, not in children under 2 and not with phototherapy. Systemic immune effects are not expected because of minimal absorption. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Pimecrolimus was designed to have a higher affinity for skin than tacrolimus, so it tends to stay where it is applied.", "src": ["Elidel (pimecrolimus) cream FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "ruxolitinib-cream": {"origin": "Ruxolitinib was discovered by Incyte in Wilmington, Delaware, as an oral JAK1 and JAK2 inhibitor. In 2011 it became the first JAK inhibitor approved by the FDA, as the pill Jakafi for myelofibrosis. Because JAK enzymes carry signals for many skin cytokines, Incyte developed a cream that delivers the drug to the skin with low blood levels. The FDA approved ruxolitinib 1.5 percent cream (Opzelura) in 2021 for mild to moderate atopic dermatitis, making it the first topical JAK inhibitor approved in the US. In 2022 it became the first FDA-approved treatment to restore pigment in nonsegmental vitiligo.", "timeline": [["2011", "FDA approves oral ruxolitinib (Jakafi), the first JAK inhibitor, for myelofibrosis."], ["2021", "FDA approves ruxolitinib cream (Opzelura) for atopic dermatitis."], ["2022", "FDA approves ruxolitinib cream as the first repigmentation treatment for vitiligo."]], "cell": "Many skin cytokines signal through receptor-linked Janus kinases. When IL-4, IL-13, IL-31, IL-22, TSLP or interferon-gamma bind their receptors, the attached JAKs phosphorylate each other and the receptor, creating docking sites for STAT proteins. STATs are phosphorylated, pair up and move to the nucleus to switch on inflammatory and itch genes. Ruxolitinib is a selective ATP-competitive inhibitor of JAK1 and JAK2. Applied to skin, it blocks this chain in keratinocytes, immune cells and itch-sensing nerve endings. In eczema it reduces Th2 inflammation, and blocking IL-31 and IL-4 signaling in sensory nerves reduces itch within days. In vitiligo, interferon-gamma from T cells drives the chemokine CXCL10 through JAK1 and JAK2, which recruits more T cells to destroy melanocytes. Blocking that loop lets surviving melanocytes repopulate the skin over months.", "body": [["Skin inflammation", "Eczema redness and thickening improve over two to eight weeks as Th2 cytokine signaling falls."], ["Itch nerves", "Itch often eases within days because IL-31 and IL-4 signaling in sensory nerves is blocked."], ["Pigment cells", "In vitiligo, the T-cell attack on melanocytes slows, so pigment slowly returns, often taking six months or more."], ["Application site", "Acne-like bumps, redness and irritation can occur where the cream is applied."], ["Whole-body effects", "Because some drug is absorbed, it carries the JAK class boxed warning, and use is limited by area and amount."]], "journey": "The cream is applied to affected skin, where ruxolitinib acts locally. A small amount reaches the blood, more when large areas are treated, which is why the label limits body surface area and tube amounts. Blood levels are generally far below those from the pill. Absorbed drug is about 97 percent protein bound, broken down in the liver mainly by CYP3A4, and its metabolites leave in urine and feces. Based on oral data the systemic half-life is about three hours.", "sidefx": "Local acne-like bumps and irritation reflect JAK blockade in follicles and changes in the skin's immune balance. Blocking JAK2 in the bone marrow, if enough is absorbed, can lower platelets, red cells and neutrophils, because growth factors such as erythropoietin and thrombopoietin signal through JAK2. The boxed warning for serious infection, death, cancer, heart events and clots is a class warning based on oral JAK inhibitors. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors can raise levels when large areas are treated. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Vitiligo research showed that a single interferon-gamma loop drives melanocyte loss, which is why a JAK inhibitor cream can bring pigment back.", "src": ["Opzelura (ruxolitinib) cream FDA label", "Jakafi (ruxolitinib) FDA label"]}, "calcipotriene": {"origin": "The link between vitamin D and psoriasis was found by chance. In 1985 Japanese researchers reported that a patient taking an oral vitamin D compound for osteoporosis had a striking improvement in psoriasis. Oral active vitamin D strongly raises blood calcium, so scientists looked for a version that would act on skin with much weaker effects on calcium. Chemists at the Danish company Leo Pharma designed calcipotriol, known in the US as calcipotriene, which binds the vitamin D receptor well but is cleared rapidly, giving about 100 to 200 times less effect on calcium than calcitriol. It was approved in the US as Dovonex in the 1990s, and later foam and combination products with betamethasone followed.", "timeline": [["1985", "Japanese researchers report psoriasis clearing in a patient taking oral vitamin D for osteoporosis."], ["1980s", "Leo Pharma chemists design calcipotriol (calcipotriene)."], ["1990s", "Calcipotriene (Dovonex) is approved in the US for psoriasis."], ["2006", "A calcipotriene and betamethasone combination ointment is approved in the US."]], "cell": "Calcipotriene is a synthetic analog of calcitriol, the active form of vitamin D3. It enters keratinocytes and binds the vitamin D receptor (VDR), a nuclear receptor that pairs with the retinoid X receptor (RXR) and binds vitamin D response elements on DNA. This changes transcription of genes that control growth and maturation. Keratinocyte proliferation slows, and cells regain normal differentiation, making proteins such as involucrin and transglutaminase that form a normal outer layer. It also affects immune cells, reducing Th17 and Th1 cytokines such as IL-17 and promoting regulatory responses. Together these effects thin plaques and reduce scaling. Because it is metabolized very quickly once absorbed, it has much less effect on calcium absorption and bone than calcitriol.", "body": [["Skin", "Thick, scaly psoriasis plaques flatten over weeks as keratinocyte overgrowth slows and maturation normalizes."], ["Skin immune cells", "IL-17 and other inflammatory cytokines in plaques decrease, reducing redness."], ["Application site", "Burning, itching and redness are common, especially on the face, which is avoided."], ["Calcium balance", "With excessive use over large areas, enough can be absorbed to raise blood and urine calcium."]], "journey": "Calcipotriene is applied to plaques and acts in the skin. Only a small portion of the applied amount is absorbed through psoriatic skin. Any drug that reaches the blood is rapidly broken down in the liver into inactive metabolites, with clearance in minutes to hours, and it leaves mainly through bile into the feces. This rapid metabolism is why it affects calcium much less than active vitamin D taken by mouth.", "sidefx": "Local irritation reflects its effects on skin cell growth and the barrier, and is why it is often combined with a steroid. Hypercalcemia occurs only when large amounts are absorbed, because it still acts on the vitamin D receptor in the gut, bone and kidney. Thiazide diuretics and calcium or vitamin D supplements add to that risk. The foam is flammable. There is no antidote; stopping and treating high calcium if it occurs is the approach.", "fun": "Calcipotriene and calcipotriol are the same molecule; the first is the US name and the second is used in most other countries.", "src": ["Sorilux and Dovonex (calcipotriene) FDA labels", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "imiquimod": {"origin": "Imiquimod was discovered by scientists at 3M Pharmaceuticals in Minnesota during a search for antiviral drugs in the 1980s. It did not kill viruses directly in lab dishes, yet animals treated with it resisted viral infection. The team found that it made immune cells release interferon and other cytokines, so it worked by switching on the body's own defenses. The FDA approved imiquimod 5 percent cream (Aldara) in 1997 for external genital warts. In 2004 approvals followed for actinic keratosis and superficial basal cell carcinoma. Its exact receptor, toll-like receptor 7 (TLR7), was identified in 2002. Lower strength Zyclara creams for actinic keratosis came later.", "timeline": [["1980s", "3M scientists discover imiquimod while searching for antiviral drugs."], ["1997", "FDA approves imiquimod cream (Aldara) for external genital warts."], ["2002", "Researchers identify toll-like receptor 7 as imiquimod's target."], ["2004", "FDA approves imiquimod for actinic keratosis and superficial basal cell carcinoma."], ["2010", "Lower-strength imiquimod (Zyclara) is approved for actinic keratosis."]], "cell": "Imiquimod is a small imidazoquinoline molecule that mimics viral single-stranded RNA. It enters plasmacytoid dendritic cells, macrophages and other immune cells and binds toll-like receptor 7 (TLR7) inside endosomes. TLR7 signals through the adaptor MyD88, activating NF-kB and IRF7. The cells then release interferon-alpha, TNF-alpha, IL-6, IL-12 and other cytokines. These signals mature dendritic cells, help them carry antigens to lymph nodes and push T cells toward a Th1 cytotoxic response. Cytotoxic T cells and natural killer cells then attack wart cells infected with HPV and abnormal precancerous or superficial cancer cells. Imiquimod also induces apoptosis in some tumor cells. It has no direct antiviral activity; all of its benefit depends on the immune response it starts.", "body": [["Treated skin", "A strong local inflammatory reaction with redness, swelling, crusting and erosion develops, followed by clearance of the lesions."], ["Warts", "HPV-infected cells are recognized and destroyed by activated T cells and natural killer cells."], ["Sun-damaged skin", "Actinic keratoses and superficial basal cell carcinomas, including spots not yet visible, are cleared as the immune response sweeps the field."], ["Whole body", "Cytokines can reach the blood, causing flu-like fatigue, fever, headache and muscle aches."], ["Lymph nodes", "Nearby lymph nodes can swell as the immune response builds."], ["Genital area", "Use on the genitals can cause swelling, pain with urination and, rarely, urinary retention."]], "journey": "Imiquimod cream is applied at bedtime and washed off after about eight hours. It stays mostly in the skin, and only a small amount is absorbed into the blood. The absorbed fraction is processed in the liver and leaves in urine and feces, with a half-life of about one day. The immune response it triggers lasts beyond each application, and lesions clear over weeks.", "sidefx": "Nearly all side effects are expected results of the immune activation it is designed to cause. Local redness, crusting and erosion show that the immune system is attacking abnormal cells, and severe reactions may need a rest period. Flu-like symptoms come from cytokines such as interferon-alpha entering the circulation. Because it stimulates immunity, it can worsen autoimmune or inflammatory conditions such as psoriasis. Sun exposure adds to skin inflammation. There is no antidote; reactions settle when treatment is paused.", "fun": "Imiquimod helped show that toll-like receptors, the immune system's pattern sensors, could be turned on with small synthetic drugs.", "src": ["Aldara and Zyclara (imiquimod) FDA labels", "Hemmi H et al. Nat Immunol 2002"]}, "fluorouracil-topical": {"origin": "Fluorouracil was one of the first anticancer drugs designed on purpose. In 1954 researchers noticed that rat liver tumors took up the RNA base uracil more than normal tissue. Charles Heidelberger at the University of Wisconsin reasoned that a fake uracil could jam tumor cells. Working with chemists at Hoffmann-La Roche, including Robert Duschinsky, he made 5-fluorouracil, reported in 1957. It became an IV cancer drug. In the 1960s dermatologists found that applying it to sun-damaged skin selectively inflamed and destroyed precancerous actinic keratoses while normal skin recovered. Topical creams were approved in the US around 1970 (Efudex), and later forms included Carac and Fluoroplex.", "timeline": [["1957", "Heidelberger and colleagues report 5-fluorouracil as an anticancer agent."], ["1960s", "Dermatologists show topical 5-FU clears actinic keratoses."], ["1970", "Topical fluorouracil (Efudex) is approved in the US."], ["2000", "A lower strength 0.5 percent cream (Carac) is approved."]], "cell": "Fluorouracil is a pyrimidine analog that cells take up like uracil. Inside the cell it is converted into several active nucleotides. The key one, FdUMP, binds thymidylate synthase together with the folate cofactor methylene tetrahydrofolate, forming a stable ternary complex that inactivates the enzyme. Without thymidylate synthase, cells cannot make thymidine for DNA, so DNA synthesis stops and cells die (an S-phase specific effect sometimes called thymineless death). Other metabolites, FUTP and FdUTP, are built into RNA and DNA and cause further damage. Precancerous keratinocytes in actinic keratoses divide faster and take up more drug than normal skin cells, so they are destroyed preferentially, which shows up as a brisk inflammatory reaction in the abnormal areas.", "body": [["Sun-damaged skin", "Abnormal keratinocytes are destroyed, producing redness, crusting and erosion in one to two weeks, then healing over one to two months."], ["Hidden lesions", "Inflammation often appears in spots that looked normal, revealing subclinical precancerous areas being treated."], ["Normal skin", "Healthy skin cells take up less drug and recover, so the final result is clearer skin."], ["Sun sensitivity", "Treated skin burns easily, so sun exposure is minimized during treatment."], ["Eyes and mouth", "Contact with eyes and mucous membranes causes irritation and is avoided."]], "journey": "The cream or solution is applied to the affected skin, where it acts locally. Only a small part, about 6 percent of the applied amount, is absorbed into the blood. Any absorbed drug is broken down quickly by the enzyme dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase (DPD) in the liver and tissues, with a half-life of only minutes. Its breakdown products leave in the urine and as carbon dioxide in the breath.", "sidefx": "The intense local reaction is the expected sign that dividing abnormal cells are dying. Pain, burning and itching come from that inflammation. Rarely, people with DPD deficiency cannot clear even the small absorbed amount and develop systemic 5-FU toxicity, such as mucositis, diarrhea and low white cells. Pets, especially dogs, can die from licking the cream or chewing tubes. Uridine triacetate is an antidote for serious systemic 5-FU overdose or early severe toxicity.", "fun": "Fluorouracil cream acts like a map: it inflames only the sun-damaged cells, revealing precancerous spots invisible to the eye.", "src": ["Efudex (fluorouracil) cream FDA label", "Heidelberger C et al. Nature 1957", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "minoxidil-topical": {"origin": "Minoxidil was made by chemists at the Upjohn Company in the late 1950s and 1960s and was found to be a powerful vasodilator. It was approved as an oral pill (Loniten) for severe high blood pressure in 1979. Doctors quickly noticed a striking side effect: many patients grew extra hair on the face, arms and back, and some balding men regrew scalp hair. Upjohn then developed a solution to apply directly to the scalp. The FDA approved topical minoxidil (Rogaine) in 1988, the first drug approved for male pattern hair loss. A version for women followed in 1991, and in 1996 Rogaine became available over the counter, followed by a stronger solution and foam.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Upjohn chemists discover minoxidil's strong blood-vessel-relaxing effect."], ["1979", "FDA approves oral minoxidil (Loniten) for severe hypertension."], ["1988", "FDA approves topical minoxidil (Rogaine) for hair loss."], ["1991", "Topical minoxidil is approved for women."], ["1996", "Topical minoxidil becomes available over the counter."]], "cell": "Minoxidil is a prodrug. In the scalp, the enzyme sulfotransferase SULT1A1 in the outer root sheath of hair follicles converts it to minoxidil sulfate, the active form. Minoxidil sulfate opens ATP-sensitive potassium channels (K-ATP) in vascular smooth muscle and likely in hair follicle cells. In vessels, potassium efflux hyperpolarizes the cell, closing voltage-gated calcium channels, so the muscle relaxes and blood flow rises. In the follicle, the exact pathway is not fully understood, but minoxidil shortens the resting (telogen) phase, prolongs the growth (anagen) phase and enlarges miniaturized follicles. It also appears to increase growth factors such as VEGF in the dermal papilla. People with more follicular sulfotransferase activity tend to respond better.", "body": [["Scalp hair follicles", "Shrunken follicles enlarge and stay in the growth phase longer, producing thicker hair over two to four months."], ["Hair cycle", "Temporary shedding in the first weeks is common as resting hairs are pushed out by new growing hairs."], ["Scalp skin", "Itching, dryness and flaking are common, often from the propylene glycol in the solution."], ["Face and body hair", "Drug that spreads beyond the scalp can cause unwanted hair growth on the forehead or face."], ["Heart and vessels", "Rarely, enough is absorbed to cause dizziness, fast heartbeat, chest pain or fluid retention."]], "journey": "Topical minoxidil is applied to a dry scalp, and only a small portion, roughly 1 to 2 percent, is absorbed into the blood. In the follicles it is activated by sulfation. The absorbed drug is mostly glucuronidated in the liver and leaves in the urine, with a systemic half-life of about four hours. Its action in the scalp lasts longer. Hair regrowth takes months, and the effect is lost within months of stopping.", "sidefx": "Scalp irritation and contact dermatitis usually come from the solvent rather than minoxidil itself, which is why the foam often helps. Unwanted facial hair reflects its growth effect on any follicle it reaches. The rare heart symptoms reflect its blood-vessel-relaxing action if enough reaches the circulation. It is highly toxic to cats, which cannot handle it well, and even small exposures can be fatal. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Minoxidil is a rare case of a drug's annoying side effect, extra hair growth, becoming its best-known use.", "src": ["Rogaine (minoxidil) OTC Drug Facts", "Loniten (minoxidil) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "metronidazole-topical": {"origin": "Metronidazole was developed in the 1950s by chemists at the French company Rhone-Poulenc. They started from azomycin, a nitroimidazole antibiotic found in a Streptomyces culture in Japan, and made versions that could treat the parasite Trichomonas vaginalis. Metronidazole was introduced in 1959 and approved in the US as Flagyl in 1963. Its activity against anaerobic bacteria was discovered later. Dermatologists noticed that it also calmed rosacea, even though rosacea is not a simple infection. Metronidazole gel (MetroGel) was approved in the US for rosacea in the late 1980s, and creams and lotions followed, including a once-daily 1 percent cream.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Rhone-Poulenc chemists develop metronidazole from the natural nitroimidazole azomycin."], ["1959", "Metronidazole is introduced for trichomoniasis."], ["1963", "FDA approves oral metronidazole (Flagyl)."], ["1980s", "Topical metronidazole gel is approved for rosacea."]], "cell": "In anaerobic microbes, metronidazole enters the cell and its nitro group is reduced by low-potential electron carriers such as ferredoxin. This forms short-lived reactive intermediates and free radicals that break DNA strands, killing the organism. In rosacea, the benefit is not mainly antibacterial, and its exact mechanism is unknown. The leading idea is an anti-inflammatory and antioxidant effect: metronidazole reduces the production of reactive oxygen species by neutrophils and may scavenge those already formed. Less oxidative injury in the skin means less damage to vessels and tissue and less inflammation. It may also affect inflammation linked to Demodex mites, though it does not reliably kill them.", "body": [["Face in rosacea", "Inflammatory papules and pustules decrease within about three weeks, with fuller effect over about nine weeks."], ["Facial redness", "Redness linked to inflammation can lessen, though background flushing and visible vessels change less."], ["Application site", "Mild burning, stinging, dryness or itching can occur."], ["Eyes", "Applying it near the eyes can cause tearing, so the eyes are avoided."]], "journey": "Topical metronidazole is applied to the face, where it acts in the skin. Blood levels are very low, roughly a hundred times lower than with oral doses. The small absorbed amount is oxidized and glucuronidated in the liver and leaves in the urine, with a half-life of about eight hours. Because exposure is so low, the drug interactions and disulfiram-like reaction seen with oral metronidazole are not expected.", "sidefx": "Local irritation is the main side effect and reflects the vehicle and sensitive rosacea skin. Oral metronidazole can cause neuropathy, seizures, a metallic taste and a reaction with alcohol, because of its effects on nerves and on alcohol metabolism, but these are not expected at topical exposure levels. Allergic reactions are rare. There is no antidote; irritation resolves after stopping.", "fun": "Topical metronidazole for rosacea is a case where an antimicrobial drug seems to help mainly by calming inflammation rather than killing microbes.", "src": ["MetroGel and Noritate (metronidazole) FDA labels", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "lidocaine-prilocaine": {"origin": "This cream builds on two Swedish anesthetics. Lidocaine was made in 1943 by Nils Lofgren and Bengt Lundqvist at Stockholm University and sold by the company Astra. Prilocaine was developed later in Sweden and introduced around 1960. Normally, local anesthetics cannot get through intact skin well. In the late 1970s researchers at Astra found that mixing lidocaine and prilocaine bases in equal amounts lowers their melting point below room temperature, so they form an oil. This eutectic mixture contains a high concentration of drug that can soak into skin. The cream was named EMLA, for eutectic mixture of local anesthetics. It was approved in the US in the 1990s and is widely used before IV starts, blood draws and vaccinations, especially in children.", "timeline": [["1943", "Nils Lofgren and Bengt Lundqvist synthesize lidocaine in Stockholm."], ["1960", "Prilocaine is introduced as a local anesthetic."], ["1980s", "Astra introduces EMLA cream in Europe."], ["1990s", "EMLA cream is approved in the US."]], "cell": "When the two drugs are mixed as bases, they form a eutectic oil that is mostly un-ionized drug at a high concentration, which lets them cross the stratum corneum. Under an occlusive dressing, they diffuse down to the dermis, where free nerve endings sense pain. Both are amide local anesthetics. In the nerve, the un-ionized form crosses the membrane, then becomes ionized inside and binds the inner pore of voltage-gated sodium channels. They bind best to open and inactivated channels and hold them in the inactivated state. Sodium cannot rush in, so action potentials cannot start or travel. Small pain fibers (A-delta and C fibers) are blocked first, giving numbness of the skin for procedures.", "body": [["Skin nerves", "Nerve endings in the dermis are blocked, so a needle stick or minor skin procedure is felt much less."], ["Skin vessels", "The skin often blanches at first and then may redden, from local effects on small blood vessels."], ["Blood", "The prilocaine metabolite o-toluidine oxidizes hemoglobin to methemoglobin, which cannot carry oxygen; infants are most at risk."], ["Brain and heart", "If too much is absorbed, systemic local anesthetic toxicity can cause seizures and arrhythmias."], ["Eyes", "Contact with the eye numbs the cornea and can lead to injury, so the eyes are avoided."]], "journey": "The cream is applied thickly to intact skin and covered with an occlusive dressing. Numbness develops after about an hour and is deeper after two hours. Blood levels stay low with correct use, but absorption rises with larger areas, longer application, damaged skin and thinner infant skin. Absorbed lidocaine and prilocaine are amides broken down in the liver; prilocaine is also metabolized to o-toluidine. Metabolites leave in the urine. Numbness lasts one to two hours after removal.", "sidefx": "Skin blanching, redness and swelling come from local effects on vessels. Methemoglobinemia is caused by o-toluidine from prilocaine; infants have less of the enzyme that reverses it, and sulfonamides, dapsone, nitrates and acetaminophen add to the risk. Methylene blue treats significant methemoglobinemia. Systemic toxicity from too much absorption shows as numb lips, ringing in the ears, seizures and heart rhythm problems; lipid emulsion is used for severe local anesthetic toxicity.", "fun": "A eutectic mixture melts at a lower temperature than either ingredient alone, which is why two powders become a skin-penetrating oil at room temperature.", "src": ["EMLA (lidocaine and prilocaine) cream FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "diclofenac-topical": {"origin": "Diclofenac was designed in the 1960s by Alfred Sallmann and Rudolf Pfister at the Swiss company Ciba-Geigy, who shaped the molecule to fit the COX enzyme. Oral diclofenac became one of the world's most used NSAIDs. Because oral NSAIDs cause stomach, kidney and heart risks, researchers developed ways to deliver diclofenac through the skin to nearby joints. A 3 percent gel (Solaraze) for actinic keratosis was approved in the US in 2000. In 2007 the FDA approved diclofenac 1 percent gel (Voltaren Gel) for osteoarthritis pain of joints near the skin, as well as a diclofenac patch for acute pain. A topical solution followed in 2009, and in 2020 the 1 percent gel became available over the counter.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Sallmann and Pfister at Ciba-Geigy design diclofenac."], ["2000", "Diclofenac 3 percent gel (Solaraze) is approved for actinic keratosis."], ["2007", "FDA approves diclofenac 1 percent gel and a diclofenac patch."], ["2009", "Diclofenac topical solution (Pennsaid) is approved for knee osteoarthritis."], ["2020", "Diclofenac 1 percent gel becomes available over the counter."]], "cell": "Diclofenac blocks cyclooxygenase (COX-1 and COX-2, with relative preference for COX-2), the enzyme that turns arachidonic acid into prostaglandin H2. When the gel is rubbed onto the skin over a joint, diclofenac diffuses through the skin into nearby tissue, joint capsule and synovial fluid. There it lowers local PGE2 and other prostaglandins, which normally dilate vessels, cause swelling and sensitize pain nerve endings to bradykinin and other signals. With less prostaglandin, nerve endings fire less and swelling settles. The drug reaches tissue levels high enough to act locally while blood levels stay at only a small fraction of those seen with pills, which reduces whole-body prostaglandin blockade.", "body": [["Superficial joints", "Pain from osteoarthritis of the hands, wrists, elbows, knees, ankles and feet eases over days, with full effect in about a week."], ["Skin at site", "Dryness, itching, redness and rash at the application site are the most common effects."], ["Stomach", "Because blood levels are low, ulcer and bleeding risk is much lower than with pills, but not zero."], ["Kidneys and heart", "The NSAID warnings for kidney injury, heart attack and stroke still apply, though systemic exposure is small."], ["Sun-damaged skin", "The 3 percent gel is used for actinic keratosis, where prostaglandin pathways contribute to abnormal cell growth."]], "journey": "Diclofenac gel is rubbed onto the skin, where the drug builds up in the skin and moves slowly into the tissues below. Blood levels peak many hours after application, around 10 to 14 hours, and stay at about 5 to 10 percent of the levels from oral dosing. Absorbed drug is more than 99 percent bound to albumin. The liver breaks it down by CYP2C9 hydroxylation and glucuronidation, and metabolites leave in the urine and bile. The systemic half-life is about two hours.", "sidefx": "Local skin reactions come from irritation and, less often, contact allergy. The systemic NSAID risks, such as stomach bleeding, kidney injury, heart attack and stroke, all come from prostaglandin blockade and are much less likely with topical use because blood levels are low, but they remain listed. Using it with oral NSAIDs raises total exposure without added benefit. Heat, bandages and occlusion increase absorption. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Diclofenac gel concentrates in the tissues under the skin, so joint fluid levels can be meaningful even when blood levels are very low.", "src": ["Voltaren Arthritis Pain (diclofenac) gel Drug Facts", "Pennsaid and Solaraze FDA labels", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "latanoprost": {"origin": "In the 1970s, prostaglandins were thought to raise eye pressure, because high doses caused eye inflammation. Laszlo Bito, a researcher at Columbia University, found the opposite: low doses of prostaglandin F2-alpha applied to the eyes of rabbits and monkeys lowered pressure for many hours. The natural prostaglandin caused too much redness, so chemists at the Swedish company Pharmacia, led by Johan Stjernschantz with chemist Bahram Resul, modified it into latanoprost, an ester prodrug that was more selective and better tolerated. The FDA approved latanoprost (Xalatan) in 1996. Its once-daily dosing and strong effect made prostaglandin analogs the most common first treatment for open-angle glaucoma.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Laszlo Bito at Columbia University shows low-dose prostaglandins lower eye pressure."], ["1980s", "Pharmacia scientists Stjernschantz and Resul develop latanoprost."], ["1996", "FDA approves latanoprost (Xalatan) for glaucoma."], ["2011", "Latanoprost becomes available as a generic in the US."]], "cell": "Latanoprost is an isopropyl ester prodrug. Esterases in the cornea hydrolyze it into latanoprost acid, the active form. Latanoprost acid is a selective agonist at the prostanoid FP receptor, a Gq-coupled receptor found on ciliary muscle cells. FP activation raises intracellular calcium and turns on signaling that increases production of matrix metalloproteinases (MMP-1, MMP-3, MMP-9). These enzymes break down collagen and other extracellular matrix between ciliary muscle bundles, widening the spaces through which aqueous humor leaves the eye by the uveoscleral route. More outflow means lower intraocular pressure, typically by about a quarter to a third. FP stimulation in iris melanocytes also increases melanin production, and in eyelash follicles it prolongs the growth phase.", "body": [["Eye pressure", "Uveoscleral outflow of aqueous humor increases, lowering eye pressure for at least 24 hours after one evening drop."], ["Optic nerve", "Lower pressure reduces ongoing damage to the optic nerve in glaucoma."], ["Conjunctiva", "Eye redness from dilated surface vessels is common, especially at first."], ["Iris", "Brown pigment in the iris can increase over months, especially in mixed-color eyes, and is likely permanent."], ["Eyelids and lashes", "Eyelid skin can darken, and lashes often grow longer, thicker and darker."], ["Inner eye", "Rarely it causes eye inflammation or macular swelling, especially after cataract surgery."]], "journey": "One drop in the evening is absorbed through the cornea, where esterases convert the prodrug into active latanoprost acid. Pressure begins to fall in three to four hours and is lowest at 8 to 12 hours. The small amount that reaches the blood is cleared very quickly, with a plasma half-life of about 17 minutes. The liver breaks it down by fatty acid beta-oxidation, and metabolites leave in the urine. Systemic effects are minimal.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from FP receptor activation in nearby tissues. Redness reflects vessel dilation in the conjunctiva. Iris and eyelid darkening come from increased melanin production in melanocytes, and eyelash growth from longer follicle growth phases. Inflammation and macular edema likely relate to prostaglandin effects on the blood-eye barrier. Using more than once daily can weaken the pressure-lowering effect. There is no antidote; systemic toxicity is very rare.", "fun": "The eyelash-growing effect of prostaglandin analogs was noticed in glaucoma patients and led to the cosmetic eyelash product based on bimatoprost.", "src": ["Xalatan (latanoprost) FDA label", "Bito LZ. Surv Ophthalmol 1997", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "bimatoprost": {"origin": "Bimatoprost was developed by Allergan in the 1990s, following the discovery that prostaglandin F2-alpha analogs lower eye pressure. Instead of an ester like latanoprost, Allergan scientists, including pharmacologist David Woodward, made an ethyl amide version of the molecule. They described it as a prostamide and proposed that it might act at receptors distinct from the classic FP receptor, an idea that is still debated. The FDA approved bimatoprost (Lumigan) for glaucoma in 2001. Patients and doctors noticed that eyelashes grew longer and darker, and in 2008 the FDA approved a cosmetic product, Latisse, applied to the upper eyelid margin for thin eyelashes. In 2020 a tiny dissolving implant placed inside the eye (Durysta) was approved, the first such implant for lowering eye pressure.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Allergan scientists develop bimatoprost as a prostamide analog."], ["2001", "FDA approves bimatoprost (Lumigan) for glaucoma and ocular hypertension."], ["2008", "FDA approves bimatoprost (Latisse) for thin eyelashes."], ["2010", "A lower strength 0.01 percent Lumigan with less redness is approved."], ["2020", "FDA approves an intracameral bimatoprost implant (Durysta)."]], "cell": "Bimatoprost is a synthetic prostamide related to prostaglandin F2-alpha. Corneal amidases convert part of it to bimatoprost free acid, a potent FP receptor agonist, while the intact amide may also act at proposed prostamide-sensitive receptors. FP receptors are Gq-coupled; activation raises intracellular calcium and turns on genes for matrix metalloproteinases in the ciliary muscle. These enzymes remodel the extracellular matrix and widen the spaces of the uveoscleral pathway, so aqueous humor drains more easily. Bimatoprost also increases outflow through the trabecular meshwork, the pressure-sensitive conventional route. Together these lower intraocular pressure. In eyelash follicles, the same signaling lengthens the growth (anagen) phase and enlarges follicles, producing longer, thicker and darker lashes. In iris melanocytes it increases melanin production.", "body": [["Eye pressure", "Aqueous drainage through both uveoscleral and trabecular routes increases, lowering pressure for at least 24 hours."], ["Optic nerve", "Lower pressure slows glaucoma damage to the optic nerve."], ["Conjunctiva", "Eye redness is common and is a frequent reason people switch to another drop."], ["Eyelashes", "Lashes grow longer, thicker and darker, which is the basis of the cosmetic Latisse product."], ["Iris and eyelid skin", "Brown iris pigmentation can increase permanently, and eyelid skin can darken."], ["Fat around the eye", "Long-term use can shrink orbital fat, causing a sunken upper eyelid appearance, called prostaglandin-associated periorbitopathy."]], "journey": "A drop in the evening is absorbed through the cornea and sclera, where amidases convert part of the dose to the free acid. Pressure begins to fall in about four hours and is lowest at 8 to 12 hours. Only a small amount reaches the blood, and it is cleared quickly. Absorbed drug is oxidized, N-deethylated and glucuronidated, partly through CYP3A4, and leaves in urine and feces. With the implant, the drug is released slowly inside the eye over months.", "sidefx": "Redness comes from relaxation of conjunctival blood vessels. Iris and eyelid darkening reflect stimulated melanin production. Eyelash growth reflects prolonged follicle growth. Periorbital fat loss is thought to come from prostaglandin effects that block fat cell formation in orbital tissue. Inflammation and macular edema can occur through effects on the blood-eye barrier. Dosing more than once daily can blunt the pressure-lowering effect. There is no antidote; systemic effects are very rare.", "fun": "Bimatoprost is one of the few drugs approved for both a medical use and a purely cosmetic use under different brand names.", "src": ["Lumigan (bimatoprost) FDA label", "Latisse and Durysta FDA labels", "Woodward DF et al. J Pharmacol Exp Ther 2003"]}, "timolol-ophthalmic": {"origin": "In 1967 doctors reported that the beta blocker propranolol, developed by James Black's team in Britain, lowered eye pressure. Propranolol was not suitable as an eye drop because it numbed the cornea. Merck had developed timolol, a nonselective beta blocker with no local anesthetic effect, in the late 1960s and early 1970s. Testing showed that timolol drops lowered eye pressure well with few local side effects. The FDA approved timolol eye drops (Timoptic) in 1978, the first beta blocker eye drop for glaucoma. For two decades it was the standard first glaucoma drop. A gel-forming solution for once-daily use came later, and timolol is also combined with dorzolamide (Cosopt) and brimonidine (Combigan).", "timeline": [["1967", "Propranolol is reported to lower intraocular pressure."], ["1978", "FDA approves timolol eye drops (Timoptic), the first ophthalmic beta blocker."], ["1990s", "A gel-forming timolol solution for once-daily dosing is introduced."], ["1998", "Timolol is combined with dorzolamide as Cosopt."]], "cell": "Aqueous humor is made by the nonpigmented epithelium of the ciliary body. Beta-2 adrenergic receptors on these cells are coupled to Gs, which activates adenylyl cyclase and raises cAMP; cAMP supports active transport of ions and water into the eye. Timolol is a nonselective beta-1 and beta-2 antagonist with no intrinsic sympathomimetic activity and minimal membrane-stabilizing (local anesthetic) effect. By blocking beta-2 receptors on the ciliary epithelium, it lowers cAMP, so less aqueous humor is produced. Less inflow means lower intraocular pressure. It does not change the pupil size or focusing. Because drops drain through the nasolacrimal duct into the nose, timolol is absorbed into the blood without first passing through the liver, so it can block beta receptors in the heart and lungs.", "body": [["Eye pressure", "Aqueous production falls, so intraocular pressure drops within about 30 minutes and stays down for about a day."], ["Eye surface", "Burning, stinging and dry eye can occur."], ["Heart", "Absorbed drug can slow the heart rate, lower blood pressure, worsen heart failure or cause heart block, especially with oral beta blockers or verapamil."], ["Lungs", "Blocking beta-2 receptors in airways can cause bronchospasm; deaths have occurred in people with asthma."], ["Metabolism", "Beta blockade can hide the fast heartbeat that warns of low blood sugar or an overactive thyroid."], ["Brain and muscle", "Fatigue and depression can occur, and muscle weakness can worsen in myasthenia gravis."]], "journey": "After a drop, timolol enters the eye through the cornea and acts on the ciliary body. Much of the drop drains through the tear duct into the nose, where the mucosa absorbs it straight into the bloodstream, bypassing the liver. Absorbed timolol is broken down in the liver mainly by CYP2D6 and leaves in the urine, with a systemic half-life of about four hours. Pressing on the inner corner of the eye or closing the eyes after a drop reduces this absorption.", "sidefx": "Nearly all serious side effects come from systemic beta blockade: slow heart rate and heart block from beta-1 blockade in the heart, and bronchospasm from beta-2 blockade in the airways. Because it skips liver first-pass metabolism, an eye drop can give meaningful blood levels. CYP2D6 inhibitors raise levels. Combined with oral beta blockers, verapamil or diltiazem, bradycardia becomes more likely. Severe beta blocker toxicity is treated with atropine, glucagon and other supportive measures.", "fun": "Timolol eye drops can lower the pulse enough that some people with asthma or slow heart rates cannot use them, a reminder that eye drops are not purely local.", "src": ["Timoptic (timolol maleate ophthalmic solution) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "dorzolamide": {"origin": "Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor pills, such as acetazolamide, were shown in 1954 by ophthalmologist Bernard Becker to lower eye pressure, but they caused tingling, fatigue, kidney stones and acid-base problems. For decades researchers tried to make an eye drop version, but most compounds could not get through the cornea well enough. Scientists at Merck used the three-dimensional structure of carbonic anhydrase II to design a molecule that would fit tightly and also penetrate the eye. The result, dorzolamide, is often cited as one of the first marketed drugs to come from structure-based drug design. The FDA approved it as Trusopt in 1994. In 1998 it was combined with timolol as Cosopt.", "timeline": [["1954", "Bernard Becker shows that oral acetazolamide lowers eye pressure."], ["1980s", "Merck scientists use the carbonic anhydrase II structure to design a topical inhibitor."], ["1994", "FDA approves dorzolamide (Trusopt)."], ["1998", "FDA approves dorzolamide with timolol (Cosopt)."]], "cell": "In the ciliary body epithelium, carbonic anhydrase II speeds the reaction of carbon dioxide and water into bicarbonate and a hydrogen ion. Bicarbonate is moved into the posterior chamber, and sodium and water follow, forming aqueous humor. Dorzolamide is a sulfonamide that binds the zinc ion in the active site of carbonic anhydrase II and blocks it. Less bicarbonate formation means less sodium and fluid transport, so aqueous production falls and eye pressure drops. Because it is applied directly, it reaches enough ciliary body concentration to work while blood levels stay too low to cause the acid-base and kidney effects of oral carbonic anhydrase inhibitors. Some drug enters the blood and binds tightly to carbonic anhydrase in red blood cells.", "body": [["Eye pressure", "Aqueous humor production falls, lowering pressure within one to two hours."], ["Eye surface", "Burning and stinging on instillation are common, along with allergic reactions of the eyelids and conjunctiva."], ["Taste", "A bitter taste is common because the drop drains into the nose and throat."], ["Cornea", "In corneas with weak endothelial cells, carbonic anhydrase inhibition can cause corneal swelling or permanent decompensation."], ["Whole body", "As a sulfonamide, rare serious reactions such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome and blood disorders are possible."]], "journey": "After a drop, dorzolamide crosses the cornea and sclera to reach the ciliary body. Some reaches the blood, where it binds carbonic anhydrase II in red blood cells and stays there for months, giving a very long red cell half-life of about four months. Plasma levels of free drug stay very low. It is partly converted to an N-desethyl metabolite that also inhibits carbonic anhydrase. Drug and metabolite leave in the urine.", "sidefx": "Stinging reflects its acidic formulation, and the bitter taste comes from drainage into the throat. Allergic eye reactions are common with long use. Corneal swelling can occur because corneal endothelial cells use carbonic anhydrase to pump fluid out of the cornea. Systemic sulfonamide reactions are rare but possible because the drug is absorbed. Oral carbonic anhydrase inhibitors are not combined with it because effects add. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Dorzolamide's design team used X-ray pictures of the enzyme to shape the drug, an approach that later became routine in drug discovery.", "src": ["Trusopt (dorzolamide) FDA label", "Becker B. Am J Ophthalmol 1954", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "brimonidine": {"origin": "Clonidine, an alpha-2 agonist blood pressure drug, was known to lower eye pressure, but it also dropped blood pressure. Researchers looked for a more eye-friendly alpha-2 agonist. Apraclonidine was developed first for short-term use around laser surgery. Allergan then developed brimonidine, which is very selective for alpha-2 over alpha-1 receptors, causing less eye whitening and rebound redness. The FDA approved brimonidine (Alphagan) for glaucoma in 1996, and a version with a gentler preservative (Alphagan P) followed in 2001. Its strong vessel-narrowing effect later led to other uses: a topical gel for rosacea redness (Mirvaso, 2013) and a low-dose over-the-counter drop for red eyes (Lumify, 2017).", "timeline": [["1980s", "Apraclonidine, an earlier alpha-2 agonist, is developed for eye pressure spikes."], ["1996", "FDA approves brimonidine (Alphagan) for glaucoma."], ["2001", "Alphagan P with a Purite preservative is approved."], ["2013", "Brimonidine gel (Mirvaso) is approved for facial redness of rosacea."], ["2017", "Low-dose brimonidine (Lumify) is approved as an OTC redness reliever."]], "cell": "Brimonidine is a highly selective alpha-2 adrenergic agonist, about 1000 times more selective for alpha-2 than for alpha-1 receptors. In the ciliary body, alpha-2 receptors are coupled to Gi proteins. Activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase and lowers cAMP, which reduces aqueous humor production. Over time it also increases outflow through the uveoscleral route, possibly through prostaglandin release. Together these lower eye pressure. In conjunctival and facial skin vessels, alpha-2 activation narrows small blood vessels, which whitens red eyes and reduces facial redness. Because it is fat-soluble and crosses the blood-brain barrier, absorbed drug can activate central alpha-2 receptors in the brainstem, reducing sympathetic output, which causes sedation, low blood pressure and slow heart rate.", "body": [["Eye pressure", "Aqueous production falls and uveoscleral outflow rises, lowering intraocular pressure."], ["Eye surface vessels", "Conjunctival vessels narrow, whitening the eye, which is the basis of the low-dose redness reliever."], ["Eye allergy", "Allergic follicular conjunctivitis with itching and redness is common after months of use."], ["Brain", "Sleepiness and fatigue are common, and young children can become severely sedated."], ["Heart and vessels", "Absorbed drug can lower blood pressure and heart rate through central alpha-2 effects."], ["Mouth", "Dry mouth is common because alpha-2 activation reduces salivary secretion."]], "journey": "After a drop, brimonidine enters the eye through the cornea. Some drains into the nose and is absorbed into the blood, peaking in about two hours. It is moderately bound to plasma proteins and is extensively broken down in the liver, mainly by aldehyde oxidase. Metabolites leave in the urine. The systemic half-life is about two to three hours, and the pressure-lowering effect lasts about 8 to 12 hours, which is why it is used up to three times daily.", "sidefx": "Sedation, fatigue, low blood pressure and slow heart rate come from central alpha-2 activation, the same mechanism as clonidine. Infants and small children have an immature blood-brain barrier and small body size, so they can develop apnea, low body temperature and coma, which is why it is contraindicated under age 2. Allergic conjunctivitis is an immune reaction that develops with long use. MAO inhibitors and CNS depressants add to its effects. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "The same molecule is used at different strengths to treat glaucoma, whiten red eyes and calm facial flushing in rosacea.", "src": ["Alphagan P (brimonidine) FDA label", "Lumify Drug Facts", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "atropine-ophthalmic": {"origin": "Atropine comes from deadly nightshade, Atropa belladonna, and related plants in the nightshade family. The genus was named after Atropos, the Greek Fate who cut the thread of life, reflecting its poison. The species name belladonna means 'beautiful lady' in Italian, a reference to the story that women used the plant's juice to dilate their pupils for a striking look. In 1831 the German pharmacist Heinrich Mein isolated pure atropine, and in 1833 Geiger and Hesse also reported it. Doctors soon used atropine drops to widen the pupil for eye examinations and to rest the eye in painful inflammation. Atropine eye drops remain a long-acting dilating and cycloplegic drop.", "timeline": [["Antiquity", "Belladonna plants are known as poisons and cosmetics."], ["1831", "Heinrich Mein isolates pure atropine."], ["1833", "Geiger and Hesse report atropine from belladonna."], ["1800s", "Atropine drops become a standard way to dilate the pupil."]], "cell": "The iris has two muscles: the sphincter, which narrows the pupil, and the dilator. The sphincter and the ciliary muscle, which changes the lens shape for near focus, are driven by acetylcholine acting on M3 muscarinic receptors. M3 receptors are Gq-coupled, raising intracellular calcium and causing contraction. Atropine is a competitive antagonist at muscarinic receptors. By blocking M3 receptors, it lets the sphincter relax, so the dilator muscle wins and the pupil widens (mydriasis). Blocking the ciliary muscle prevents near focusing (cycloplegia). In anterior uveitis, cycloplegia relieves painful ciliary spasm and keeps the dilated pupil from sticking to the lens (posterior synechiae). Atropine binds tightly and is cleared slowly from the eye, so these effects last a week or more.", "body": [["Pupil", "The pupil dilates within about 40 minutes and can stay dilated for 7 to 10 days, causing light sensitivity."], ["Focusing muscle", "Near focus is lost for days, which also blurs the stronger eye during lazy eye (amblyopia) treatment."], ["Inflamed eye", "Ciliary spasm pain in uveitis eases, and synechiae are prevented."], ["Eye pressure", "In eyes with narrow angles, a dilated pupil can block drainage and trigger acute angle-closure glaucoma."], ["Whole body", "Absorbed drug can cause dry mouth, flushing, fast heart rate and fever, especially in small children."]], "journey": "After a drop, atropine penetrates the cornea and binds muscarinic receptors in the iris and ciliary body, where it lingers and binds to pigment, so the effect lasts days. Some drains through the tear duct into the nose and is absorbed into the blood, bypassing the liver. Absorbed atropine is partly hydrolyzed in the liver, and a large share leaves unchanged in the urine. The systemic half-life is about two to three hours. Pressing on the inner corner of the eye after a drop reduces absorption.", "sidefx": "Blurred vision and light sensitivity are direct results of blocked pupil and focusing muscles. Angle closure can occur because the dilated iris bunches up and blocks drainage in a narrow angle. Systemic anticholinergic toxicity, with hot dry skin, fever, flushing, fast heart rate and delirium, comes from blocking muscarinic receptors throughout the body and is most dangerous in infants. Physostigmine can reverse severe anticholinergic toxicity, under expert care.", "fun": "Anticholinergic toxicity is remembered as 'hot as a hare, dry as a bone, red as a beet, blind as a bat and mad as a hatter', and 'blind as a bat' refers to the dilated pupils.", "src": ["Atropine sulfate ophthalmic solution FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "prednisolone-acetate-ophthalmic": {"origin": "After cortisone was shown to treat rheumatoid arthritis in 1949, doctors quickly tried steroids in the eye, and early reports in the 1950s showed they calmed eye inflammation dramatically. In the early 1950s Arthur Nobile and colleagues at Schering found that a bacterium could add a double bond to cortisone and hydrocortisone, producing prednisone and prednisolone, which were more potent anti-inflammatory steroids with less salt-retaining effect. They were marketed in 1955. Making prednisolone into an acetate ester produced a fat-soluble suspension that penetrates the cornea well. Prednisolone acetate 1 percent (Pred Forte) became the most widely used steroid eye drop after eye surgery and for uveitis.", "timeline": [["1949", "Cortisone is shown to treat rheumatoid arthritis."], ["1950s", "Steroids are shown to reduce eye inflammation."], ["1955", "Prednisone and prednisolone, developed at Schering, are introduced."]], "cell": "Prednisolone acetate is a lipophilic ester that crosses the cornea well and is hydrolyzed to prednisolone inside the eye. Prednisolone binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor. The receptor complex moves into the nucleus and changes gene transcription in two main ways. It turns on anti-inflammatory genes such as annexin A1 (lipocortin), which inhibits phospholipase A2 and so cuts prostaglandin and leukotriene production. It also blocks NF-kB and AP-1, reducing cytokines, chemokines and adhesion molecules. As a result, fewer white cells move into the eye, small vessels leak less, swelling falls, and fibroblast growth and scarring are reduced. These same actions also weaken local defense against infection and slow wound healing.", "body": [["Front of the eye", "Inflammation after cataract or other eye surgery and in anterior uveitis settles, with fewer cells and less flare in the anterior chamber."], ["Cornea and conjunctiva", "Redness, swelling and scarring from allergic or inflammatory conditions are reduced."], ["Eye pressure", "In some people, called steroid responders, outflow through the trabecular meshwork falls and eye pressure rises, which can lead to glaucoma."], ["Lens", "Long-term use can cause posterior subcapsular cataract."], ["Infection defense", "Local immune suppression can let herpes simplex, fungal and bacterial infections worsen."], ["Healing", "Corneal and scleral healing slows, and thin areas can perforate."]], "journey": "The suspension is shaken before use so each drop contains drug particles. The acetate ester penetrates the cornea and is converted to prednisolone in the eye, reaching the aqueous humor within hours. Some drains into the nose and is absorbed into the blood, where prednisolone has a half-life of about two to three hours and is broken down in the liver, with metabolites leaving in the urine. Systemic effects are small with normal use.", "sidefx": "Raised eye pressure is thought to come from steroid effects on trabecular meshwork cells, which build up extracellular material and reduce outflow. Cataracts form from steroid effects on lens proteins and cells. Infection and slow healing reflect suppressed local immune and fibroblast activity, which is why steroids are avoided in active herpes simplex keratitis. Pressure is checked if use lasts more than about ten days, and long courses are tapered. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Prednisolone was one of the early wins of using microbes to do chemistry: a bacterium performed a step that was hard for chemists to do.", "src": ["Pred Forte (prednisolone acetate ophthalmic suspension) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "moxifloxacin-ophthalmic": {"origin": "The quinolone story began in 1962, when George Lesher and colleagues at Sterling-Winthrop found nalidixic acid as a byproduct of making the antimalarial chloroquine. Adding a fluorine atom later created the fluoroquinolones, which were much more potent. Bayer developed moxifloxacin in the 1990s as a fourth-generation fluoroquinolone with an 8-methoxy group that improved activity against gram-positive bacteria such as Streptococcus pneumoniae and Staphylococcus aureus. The oral and IV form (Avelox) was approved in 1999. Alcon developed a 0.5 percent eye drop, Vigamox, approved in 2003 for bacterial conjunctivitis, which became widely used by eye surgeons.", "timeline": [["1962", "George Lesher's team discovers nalidixic acid, the first quinolone."], ["1999", "FDA approves oral and IV moxifloxacin (Avelox)."], ["2003", "FDA approves moxifloxacin eye drops (Vigamox) for bacterial conjunctivitis."], ["2010", "A twice-daily formulation (Moxeza) is approved."]], "cell": "Bacteria must constantly manage the twisting of their DNA. DNA gyrase (topoisomerase II) adds negative supercoils and relieves strain ahead of the replication fork, and topoisomerase IV separates linked daughter chromosomes after replication. Both enzymes cut both DNA strands, pass DNA through the break and reseal it. Moxifloxacin binds the enzyme-DNA complex at the moment the DNA is cut and traps it, preventing resealing. The trapped complexes block replication forks and turn into permanent double-strand breaks, which kills the bacteria. Moxifloxacin targets both enzymes well, which helps against gram-positive bacteria and slows resistance. In the eye, high concentrations in tears give rapid, concentration-dependent killing of common conjunctivitis bacteria.", "body": [["Eye surface", "Bacteria causing conjunctivitis are killed quickly, and symptoms usually improve in one to two days."], ["Tears and cornea", "High drug levels in tears and good corneal penetration give broad coverage of gram-positive and gram-negative organisms."], ["Eye comfort", "Mild irritation, redness, tearing and dry eye can occur."], ["Microbial balance", "Long use can let fungi and resistant bacteria overgrow."]], "journey": "Drops are placed in the eye, where moxifloxacin reaches very high levels in tears within minutes and penetrates the cornea. The drops are preservative-free in the Vigamox formulation. Only tiny amounts reach the blood, far below levels from pills. The absorbed drug is conjugated to glucuronide and sulfate and leaves in urine and feces, with a systemic half-life of about 13 hours.", "sidefx": "Eye irritation and redness are local reactions. Overgrowth of fungi and resistant bacteria follows from killing susceptible bacteria for a long time. Allergic reactions are rare but possible, as with all quinolones. The serious systemic quinolone effects, such as tendon rupture, QT prolongation and nerve problems, are not expected because blood levels after eye drops are extremely low. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The first quinolone, nalidixic acid, was found as an impurity during the manufacture of an antimalarial drug.", "src": ["Vigamox (moxifloxacin ophthalmic solution) FDA label", "Lesher GY et al. J Med Pharm Chem 1962", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "tobramycin-ophthalmic": {"origin": "Aminoglycoside antibiotics began with streptomycin, found in 1943 by Albert Schatz in Selman Waksman's lab at Rutgers. Tobramycin was discovered in the late 1960s by scientists at Eli Lilly as one component of the nebramycin complex made by the soil bacterium Streptomyces tenebrarius. It was especially strong against Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Injectable tobramycin was approved in the 1970s, and Alcon later developed tobramycin 0.3 percent eye drops and ointment (Tobrex) for surface eye infections. Tobramycin is also combined with dexamethasone in TobraDex for infected inflamed eyes.", "timeline": [["1943", "Streptomycin, the first aminoglycoside, is discovered at Rutgers."], ["1960s", "Eli Lilly scientists isolate tobramycin from Streptomyces tenebrarius."], ["1970s", "Injectable tobramycin is approved in the US."], ["1980s", "Tobramycin eye drops and ointment become widely used for eye infections."]], "cell": "Tobramycin is a positively charged aminoglycoside. It binds to the negatively charged outer membrane of gram-negative bacteria and displaces magnesium, which makes the membrane leaky. It then crosses the inner membrane by an oxygen-dependent transport process, which is why aminoglycosides do not work against anaerobes. Inside, it binds the 16S rRNA of the 30S ribosomal subunit at the decoding site. This blocks formation of the initiation complex and causes misreading of messenger RNA, so faulty proteins are made. Some of these misfolded proteins insert into the membrane and make it even leakier, letting more drug in. The result is rapid, concentration-dependent bacterial killing, with strong activity against Pseudomonas aeruginosa and staphylococci.", "body": [["Eye surface", "Bacteria causing conjunctivitis, blepharitis and other outer eye infections are killed, especially gram-negative organisms."], ["Eyelids", "Allergic reactions with lid itching, swelling and redness can occur, especially with longer use."], ["Microbial balance", "Long use can let fungi and resistant bacteria overgrow."], ["Cross-allergy", "Allergy to tobramycin can cross-react with neomycin and gentamicin."]], "journey": "Drops or ointment are applied to the eye, where tobramycin reaches high levels in tears within minutes. It penetrates the cornea poorly unless the surface is damaged. Very little reaches the blood. Tobramycin is not metabolized; any absorbed drug is filtered unchanged by the kidneys. The systemic half-life is about two to three hours.", "sidefx": "Lid itching and swelling are signs of allergy to the drug. Overgrowth of resistant organisms follows prolonged antibiotic pressure. The kidney and inner ear toxicity of injected aminoglycosides comes from drug building up in kidney tubule and inner ear hair cells, but this is not expected with eye drops because blood levels are so low; caution applies only when systemic aminoglycosides are also used. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Aminoglycosides from Streptomyces end in '-mycin', like tobramycin, while those from Micromonospora end in '-micin', like gentamicin.", "src": ["Tobrex (tobramycin ophthalmic) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "erythromycin-ophthalmic": {"origin": "Erythromycin's story starts in the Philippines. In 1949 the Filipino scientist Abelardo Aguilar, working for Eli Lilly, sent soil samples from Iloilo to the company. In 1952 a Lilly team led by J. M. McGuire isolated erythromycin from the bacterium in that soil, then called Streptomyces erythreus and now Saccharopolyspora erythraea. It became a key option for people allergic to penicillin. Separately, in 1881 the German obstetrician Carl Crede showed that silver nitrate drops in newborns' eyes prevented gonococcal blindness. Silver nitrate was irritating and did not cover chlamydia, so erythromycin eye ointment became the standard newborn eye prophylaxis in the US. The original brand, Ilotycin, has been discontinued, but generic ointment is widely used.", "timeline": [["1881", "Carl Crede introduces silver nitrate eye drops to prevent newborn gonococcal eye infection."], ["1949", "Abelardo Aguilar sends Philippine soil samples to Eli Lilly."], ["1952", "Lilly scientists report erythromycin from the Iloilo soil organism."], ["1980s", "Erythromycin ointment increasingly replaces silver nitrate for newborn eye prophylaxis in the US."]], "cell": "Erythromycin is a 14-membered macrolide. It enters susceptible bacteria and binds reversibly to the 23S rRNA of the 50S ribosomal subunit, in the exit tunnel through which new protein chains leave the ribosome. This blocks translocation and elongation of the growing peptide chain, so protein synthesis stops. The effect is mainly bacteriostatic. It is active against gram-positive cocci such as streptococci and many staphylococci, against Neisseria gonorrhoeae and against Chlamydia trachomatis. Resistance develops through methylation of the 23S binding site (erm genes) or efflux pumps (mef genes). In the eye, the ointment keeps drug in contact with the surface for hours.", "body": [["Newborn eyes", "A single application after birth helps prevent gonococcal ophthalmia neonatorum, which can cause blindness."], ["Eye surface", "Surface infections such as conjunctivitis and blepharitis from susceptible bacteria improve."], ["Vision", "The ointment blurs vision for a short time after it is applied."], ["Eye comfort", "Mild irritation and redness of the eye surface can occur, usually brief."]], "journey": "The ointment is placed in the lower conjunctival sac, where it melts and spreads over the eye surface. It stays in contact for hours, which is why ointment suits newborn prophylaxis. Almost none is absorbed into the blood. Any drug that is absorbed would be handled by the liver through CYP3A4 and excreted in bile.", "sidefx": "Side effects are mainly local. Blurred vision comes from the ointment base. Mild irritation or redness can occur. Prolonged use can let fungi or resistant bacteria overgrow. Newborn prophylaxis does not reliably prevent chlamydial pneumonia, because the infection can spread beyond the eye. The systemic macrolide effects, such as QT prolongation and CYP3A4 interactions, are not expected because absorption is negligible.", "fun": "Erythromycin was named after its source organism, Streptomyces erythreus, from the Greek word for red, describing the color of the colonies.", "src": ["Erythromycin ophthalmic ointment FDA label", "McGuire JM et al. Antibiot Chemother 1952", "CDC Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines"]}, "cyclosporine-ophthalmic": {"origin": "Cyclosporine came from a fungus in a Norwegian soil sample. In 1969 Hans Peter Frey, a Sandoz biologist on vacation, collected soil on the Hardangervidda plateau in Norway, as Sandoz employees were encouraged to do. The fungus Tolypocladium inflatum in that soil produced cyclosporine. In 1972 Jean-Francois Borel and colleagues at Sandoz found that it suppressed immune responses without killing bone marrow cells. It transformed organ transplantation after FDA approval as Sandimmune in 1983. Eye researchers later found that inflammation of the tear glands and eye surface drives chronic dry eye. Allergan developed a low-strength emulsion, Restasis, approved in 2002 to increase tear production. Other formulations followed, including Cequa in 2018 and Vevye in 2023.", "timeline": [["1969", "Hans Peter Frey collects the Norwegian soil sample containing the cyclosporine-producing fungus."], ["1972", "Jean-Francois Borel discovers cyclosporine's immunosuppressive effect at Sandoz."], ["1983", "FDA approves cyclosporine (Sandimmune) for organ transplantation."], ["2002", "FDA approves cyclosporine eye emulsion (Restasis) for dry eye."], ["2018", "A nanomicellar cyclosporine solution (Cequa) is approved."]], "cell": "In dry eye disease, stress on the eye surface triggers an immune response. T cells in the conjunctiva and lacrimal gland release cytokines such as IL-2, interferon-gamma and IL-17, which damage tear-producing tissue and goblet cells, further reducing tears in a self-feeding cycle. Cyclosporine enters these T cells and binds the immunophilin cyclophilin. The cyclosporine-cyclophilin complex binds and inhibits calcineurin, so the transcription factor NFAT is not dephosphorylated and cannot enter the nucleus. IL-2 and other cytokine genes are not switched on, and T-cell activation slows. With less inflammation, lacrimal gland function and conjunctival goblet cell numbers can recover, so the eye makes more of its own tears. It also reduces apoptosis of surface cells. Because it repairs the gland over time, benefit builds slowly over months.", "body": [["Lacrimal glands", "Less T-cell inflammation lets the tear glands recover, increasing natural tear production over about three to six months."], ["Conjunctiva", "Goblet cell numbers can increase, improving the mucus layer of the tear film."], ["Eye surface", "Burning on instillation is the most common side effect, along with redness and discharge."], ["Vision", "The milky emulsion can briefly blur vision after a drop."]], "journey": "Drops are placed on the eye surface, where the oily emulsion or other vehicle carries cyclosporine into the conjunctiva, cornea and nearby tissue. It concentrates in these tissues, and blood levels are typically below the limit of detection. Any absorbed drug would be broken down in the liver by CYP3A4 and excreted in bile. Effects build with continued twice-daily use over weeks to months.", "sidefx": "Burning and stinging come from the drug and vehicle on an already irritated eye surface, and often ease with continued use. The serious effects of systemic cyclosporine, such as kidney damage, high blood pressure and broad immune suppression, are not expected because blood levels are essentially undetectable. Touching the vial tip to the eye can injure the eye or contaminate the drop. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Sandoz asked employees to bring back soil samples from their travels, and one vacation sample from Norway led to a drug that changed organ transplantation and later dry eye treatment.", "src": ["Restasis (cyclosporine ophthalmic emulsion) FDA label", "Borel JF et al. Agents Actions 1976", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "ranibizumab": {"origin": "Ranibizumab grew out of the hunt for the signal that makes new blood vessels grow. In 1989 Napoleone Ferrara and his team at Genentech isolated and cloned vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF). Genentech made a mouse antibody against VEGF, then humanized it into the full-size cancer drug bevacizumab. For the eye, the company took a different path: it kept only the antigen-binding Fab fragment and improved its grip on VEGF through affinity maturation. A smaller fragment was expected to spread through the retina better after injection into the eye. In 2006 the FDA approved ranibizumab as Lucentis for wet age-related macular degeneration. It was one of the first treatments that let many patients with wet AMD keep or regain vision instead of slowly losing it. Biosimilars (Byooviz, Cimerli) and the Susvimo refillable implant came later.", "timeline": [["1989", "Napoleone Ferrara's Genentech team isolates and clones VEGF."], ["2006", "FDA approves Lucentis for wet age-related macular degeneration."], ["2010s", "Approvals expand to retinal vein occlusion and diabetic macular edema."], ["2021", "FDA approves Byooviz, the first ophthalmology biosimilar, and the Susvimo implant."]], "cell": "In wet AMD and diabetic eye disease, oxygen-starved retinal cells release VEGF-A. VEGF-A binds VEGFR-2 (and VEGFR-1) on endothelial cells. These are receptor tyrosine kinases, so binding triggers receptor dimerization and autophosphorylation, then signaling through PLC-gamma, PKC, MAPK and PI3K-Akt. The results are endothelial proliferation, migration, survival and leaky junctions, so fragile new vessels grow and fluid seeps into the macula. Ranibizumab is a humanized IgG1 Fab fragment that binds the receptor-binding site of all active VEGF-A isoforms. Bound VEGF-A cannot reach its receptors, so the kinase cascade stays off. Leakage stops, the macula dries, and abnormal choroidal vessels regress. Because it has no Fc region, it cannot be recycled by the neonatal Fc receptor and clears quickly from the blood.", "body": [["Retina and macula", "Less VEGF signaling means less leakage and swelling in the macula, so central vision often stabilizes or improves over weeks."], ["Choroidal vessels", "Abnormal new vessels under the retina stop growing and can shrink, lowering the risk of bleeding and scarring."], ["Eye pressure", "The added volume injected into the eye briefly raises intraocular pressure, which is checked after each injection."], ["Ocular surface", "The needle passing through the conjunctiva often causes a small red spot of bleeding that clears on its own."], ["Whole body", "Very little reaches the blood and it clears fast there, so body-wide VEGF effects are minimal, though arterial clots remain a theoretical class risk."]], "journey": "Ranibizumab is injected directly into the vitreous gel of the eye, so the full amount starts right where it is needed. From the vitreous it diffuses into the retina and choroid. It stays in the eye for days, with a vitreous half-life of about nine days, which is why monthly injections are typical. A small amount escapes into the bloodstream, where levels are very low and fall within hours because the fragment lacks an Fc region. It is broken down like any protein into peptides and amino acids, with no liver enzyme or kidney pathway involved.", "sidefx": "Most serious problems come from the injection itself, not the drug. A needle through the eye wall can carry bacteria inside, causing endophthalmitis, a painful infection that threatens sight. The needle can also tug on the retina and lead to a retinal detachment. The injected volume raises eye pressure for a short time. Floaters and a small surface bleed are common. Because VEGF also helps keep normal vessels healthy, blocking it raises a theoretical concern about strokes and heart attacks, although very little drug reaches the blood. There is no antidote; infection is treated by eye specialists.", "fun": "Ranibizumab and bevacizumab come from the same parent mouse antibody. Bevacizumab is a full antibody built for cancer, while ranibizumab is just one arm of it tuned for the eye.", "src": ["Lucentis (ranibizumab) FDA label", "Ferrara N. VEGF and the quest for tumor angiogenesis factors, Nat Rev Cancer 2002", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "aflibercept": {"origin": "Aflibercept was built by Regeneron Pharmaceuticals in Tarrytown, New York, by a team led by George Yancopoulos. Instead of making an antibody, they borrowed the body's own VEGF receptors. They fused the key binding pieces of VEGFR-1 and VEGFR-2 to the Fc tail of a human antibody, creating a soluble decoy they called the VEGF Trap. The trap binds VEGF very tightly and also captures placental growth factor. The FDA approved it for the eye as Eylea in 2011 for wet age-related macular degeneration. A version given by vein for colon cancer, ziv-aflibercept (Zaltrap), followed in 2012. Its strong binding allowed longer gaps between eye injections than earlier drugs, and a higher-strength Eylea HD was approved in 2023.", "timeline": [["2002", "Regeneron scientists describe the VEGF Trap decoy receptor."], ["2011", "FDA approves Eylea for wet age-related macular degeneration."], ["2012", "Ziv-aflibercept (Zaltrap) is approved for metastatic colorectal cancer."], ["2023", "FDA approves Eylea HD and approves Eylea for retinopathy of prematurity."]], "cell": "Aflibercept is a recombinant fusion protein: domain 2 of VEGFR-1 and domain 3 of VEGFR-2 joined to the Fc portion of human IgG1. It acts as a soluble decoy receptor. Free VEGF-A, VEGF-B and placental growth factor (PlGF) bind to the trap instead of to VEGFR-1 and VEGFR-2 on retinal endothelial cells. Its affinity for VEGF-A is higher than that of the native receptors. With the ligands captured, the receptor tyrosine kinases do not dimerize or autophosphorylate, so the downstream PLC-gamma, MAPK and PI3K-Akt signals that drive vessel growth and leakiness stay quiet. Blocking PlGF as well removes a second signal that recruits inflammatory cells and supports abnormal vessels. Clinically, fluid in the macula dries up and choroidal neovascular membranes regress.", "body": [["Retina and macula", "Capturing VEGF stops fluid leaking into the macula, so swelling falls and reading vision often improves."], ["Choroidal vessels", "Abnormal new vessels stop growing and can regress, which lowers bleeding and scarring under the retina."], ["Eye pressure", "The injected volume briefly raises intraocular pressure, so pressure and optic nerve blood flow are checked after the injection."], ["Lens", "Cataract is listed among common reactions in the eye, reflecting repeated injections in an older population."], ["Whole body", "Small amounts reach the blood bound to VEGF; systemic VEGF blockade is low, but arterial clots are a potential class risk."]], "journey": "Aflibercept is injected into the vitreous gel of the eye by an ophthalmologist. It spreads into the retina and choroid and binds VEGF there. Because it binds so tightly, its effect on fluid can last longer than its presence would suggest, which allows injections every one to two months in maintenance. Some drug enters the blood, where it binds circulating VEGF into inactive complexes. Free drug in the blood has a half-life of about five to six days. Like other proteins, it is broken down into peptides and amino acids, and some is cleared by binding to its target.", "sidefx": "The serious risks come mainly from putting a needle into the eye. Bacteria carried inside can cause endophthalmitis, and traction on the vitreous can tear or detach the retina. Extra fluid volume causes a short rise in eye pressure. Inflammation inside the eye and, rarely, retinal vasculitis have been reported. Because VEGF helps maintain healthy vessels throughout the body, blocking it carries a potential risk of arterial clots such as stroke. There is no antidote; eye complications are managed by retina specialists.", "fun": "The name VEGF Trap describes exactly how it works: it is a piece of the receptor floating free in the eye that grabs the growth signal before the real receptor can.", "src": ["Eylea (aflibercept) FDA label", "Holash J et al. VEGF-Trap, PNAS 2002", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "tolterodine": {"origin": "Tolterodine was developed by the Swedish company Pharmacia in the late 1980s and 1990s. Older bladder drugs such as oxybutynin worked, but they dried the mouth so much that many people stopped taking them. Pharmacia scientists screened for a muscarinic blocker that acted more on the bladder than on the salivary glands in animal tests. Tolterodine was not more selective for any one muscarinic subtype on paper, yet it showed this functional bladder preference. The FDA approved it as Detrol in 1998, and a once-daily extended-release capsule, Detrol LA, followed. It became one of the most prescribed overactive bladder drugs and later gave rise to fesoterodine, which turns into the same active metabolite.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Pharmacia develops tolterodine as a bladder-preferring antimuscarinic."], ["1998", "FDA approves Detrol tablets for overactive bladder."], ["2000s", "Detrol LA extended-release capsules allow once-daily dosing."]], "cell": "Bladder emptying is driven by parasympathetic nerves that release acetylcholine onto the detrusor muscle. Acetylcholine binds M3 receptors, which couple to Gq, activate phospholipase C and produce IP3, releasing calcium and causing contraction. M2 receptors, which are more numerous, couple to Gi and reduce cAMP, which removes a relaxing brake. In overactive bladder the detrusor contracts during filling. Tolterodine competitively blocks M2 and M3 receptors on the detrusor. Its 5-hydroxymethyl metabolite, made by CYP2D6, is equally potent, so both parent and metabolite contribute. With fewer involuntary contractions, the bladder holds more urine and urgency eases. Blockade of muscarinic receptors in salivary glands, gut and eye explains the side effects.", "body": [["Bladder", "The detrusor relaxes during filling, so involuntary contractions fall, capacity rises and urgency and leaking episodes decrease."], ["Mouth", "Blocking salivary gland muscarinic receptors reduces saliva, causing dry mouth, though less than with oxybutynin."], ["Gut", "Slower gut movement from reduced acetylcholine signaling causes constipation and sometimes abdominal pain."], ["Eyes", "Blocking the muscles of the pupil and lens can blur vision and dry the eyes."], ["Brain", "Some drug crosses into the brain, which can cause dizziness, sleepiness or confusion, especially in older adults."], ["Heart", "At high exposure, such as in poor CYP2D6 metabolizers on CYP3A4 inhibitors, the QT interval can lengthen."]], "journey": "Tolterodine is taken by mouth and absorbed well, peaking in one to two hours for the immediate-release tablet and later for the extended-release capsule. In most people the liver enzyme CYP2D6 quickly converts it into the active 5-hydroxymethyl metabolite. About 7 percent of white people are poor CYP2D6 metabolizers; in them CYP3A4 handles the drug, parent levels run higher and the half-life stretches from about two to three hours to about ten. Because the parent and metabolite act together, the overall effect is similar. Most of the drug leaves in the urine as metabolites.", "sidefx": "Nearly every side effect is the predictable result of blocking acetylcholine outside the bladder. Less saliva means dry mouth. Slower gut contractions mean constipation. Blocked eye muscles mean blurred vision. In the brain, antimuscarinic effects cause dizziness, sleepiness and confusion, which matter most in older adults. In a person whose bladder outlet is already narrowed, as with an enlarged prostate, relaxing the detrusor can tip them into urinary retention. Angioedema has been reported. In overdose a full anticholinergic toxidrome can appear; physostigmine is considered for severe central toxicity.", "src": ["Detrol LA (tolterodine) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "solifenacin": {"origin": "Solifenacin was discovered by the Japanese company Yamanouchi Pharmaceutical, which merged with Fujisawa in 2005 to form Astellas Pharma. The goal was an antimuscarinic with more preference for the M3 receptor, the subtype that drives bladder contraction, and a long half-life that would give steady effect with once-daily dosing. The FDA approved solifenacin as Vesicare in 2004 for overactive bladder. A liquid version, Vesicare LS, was later approved for neurogenic detrusor overactivity in children. Solifenacin also became the partner drug in studies combining an antimuscarinic with the beta-3 agonist mirabegron.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Yamanouchi Pharmaceutical in Japan discovers solifenacin."], ["2004", "FDA approves Vesicare for overactive bladder."], ["2005", "Yamanouchi and Fujisawa merge to form Astellas Pharma."], ["2020", "Vesicare LS oral suspension is approved for neurogenic detrusor overactivity in children."]], "cell": "Solifenacin is a competitive muscarinic antagonist with relative selectivity for the M3 subtype. On the detrusor muscle, acetylcholine from parasympathetic nerves normally binds M3 receptors, which couple to Gq and phospholipase C. IP3 then releases stored calcium, and diacylglycerol and Rho kinase sensitize the contractile machinery, so the muscle squeezes. Solifenacin occupies the M3 site without activating it, so acetylcholine cannot start this chain during bladder filling. It also blocks M2 receptors, which otherwise oppose beta-adrenergic relaxation by lowering cAMP. With involuntary contractions damped, the bladder fills to a larger volume before urgency, and leaking episodes fall. M3 receptors in salivary glands, gut smooth muscle and the eye are blocked too, which produces its side effects.", "body": [["Bladder", "Fewer involuntary detrusor contractions let the bladder hold more urine, so urgency, frequency and urge leaks decrease over a few weeks."], ["Mouth", "M3 blockade in salivary glands reduces saliva and causes dry mouth."], ["Gut", "Reduced gut muscle contraction slows transit, causing constipation and, rarely, fecal impaction."], ["Eyes", "Blocking the ciliary and pupil muscles can blur near vision."], ["Brain", "Some people, especially older adults, feel sleepy or confused from central muscarinic blockade."], ["Heart", "At doses above the usual range, the QT interval can lengthen."]], "journey": "Solifenacin is taken by mouth and is absorbed well, with about 90 percent bioavailability. It peaks slowly, three to eight hours after a dose, and spreads widely into tissues. It is about 98 percent protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise its levels. Its half-life is long, about two to three days, which gives smooth once-daily effect and means it takes over a week to reach steady state. Metabolites leave mostly in the urine with some in the stool.", "sidefx": "The side effects follow directly from muscarinic blockade outside the bladder: dry mouth from the salivary glands, constipation from the gut, blurred vision from the eye, and drowsiness or confusion from the brain. If the bladder outlet is blocked, relaxing the detrusor can cause urinary retention. Angioedema and anaphylaxis are rare immune reactions. In overdose an anticholinergic toxidrome appears and can last a long time because of the long half-life. Physostigmine is considered for severe central anticholinergic toxicity.", "src": ["Vesicare (solifenacin) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "fesoterodine": {"origin": "Fesoterodine was designed by Schwarz Pharma in Germany as a smarter way to deliver the active metabolite of tolterodine. Tolterodine depends on the liver enzyme CYP2D6 to make its active 5-hydroxymethyl form, and people vary widely in that enzyme. Schwarz chemists built an ester prodrug that ordinary blood esterases convert straight into 5-hydroxymethyl tolterodine, skipping CYP2D6 for activation. Pfizer, which already sold tolterodine, gained the rights, and the FDA approved fesoterodine as Toviaz in 2008. A pediatric indication for neurogenic detrusor overactivity was added later.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Schwarz Pharma develops fesoterodine as a prodrug of tolterodine's active metabolite."], ["2008", "FDA approves Toviaz for overactive bladder in adults."], ["2021", "Approval extends to neurogenic detrusor overactivity in children."]], "cell": "Fesoterodine itself is inactive. After absorption, nonspecific plasma esterases rapidly hydrolyze it to 5-hydroxymethyl tolterodine (5-HMT). 5-HMT is a competitive antagonist at muscarinic M2 and M3 receptors on the detrusor. Normally acetylcholine at M3 activates Gq, phospholipase C and IP3-driven calcium release, causing contraction, while M2 activation lowers cAMP and blunts relaxation. By blocking both, 5-HMT reduces involuntary contractions during filling and increases bladder capacity. Because activation uses esterases rather than CYP2D6, active drug forms reliably in everyone. CYP2D6 and CYP3A4 still matter later, for breaking down 5-HMT.", "body": [["Bladder", "Detrusor overactivity decreases, so the bladder holds more and urgency and urge incontinence fall within one to two weeks."], ["Mouth", "Salivary muscarinic blockade causes dry mouth, the most common complaint."], ["Gut", "Slower gut motility causes constipation and indigestion."], ["Eyes", "Dry eyes and blurred vision come from blocking tear glands and eye muscles."], ["Skin and temperature", "Reduced sweating can lead to heat intolerance and heat prostration in hot environments."], ["Brain", "Dizziness and sleepiness can occur, especially in older adults."]], "journey": "Fesoterodine is taken by mouth as an extended-release tablet. As it is absorbed, blood esterases convert it almost completely to 5-HMT, so the parent drug is barely detectable in plasma. 5-HMT peaks about five hours after a dose. The liver then breaks down 5-HMT through CYP2D6 and CYP3A4 into inactive products. The half-life is about seven hours for the extended-release form, which supports once-daily dosing. About 70 percent leaves in the urine and the rest in the stool. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise exposure.", "sidefx": "Side effects come from muscarinic blockade beyond the bladder. Dry mouth, constipation and dry eyes reflect reduced secretions and smooth muscle activity. Reduced sweating, also a cholinergic function, can cause dangerous overheating in hot weather. In people with a blocked bladder outlet, relaxing the detrusor can cause urinary retention. Central effects include dizziness and drowsiness. Angioedema is a rare immune reaction. Overdose causes an anticholinergic toxidrome, and physostigmine is considered for severe central toxicity.", "src": ["Toviaz (fesoterodine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "mirabegron": {"origin": "For decades, the only drug class for overactive bladder was the antimuscarinics, which many people stopped because of dry mouth and constipation. Researchers learned that the human detrusor muscle carries beta-3 adrenergic receptors that help it relax during filling. Astellas Pharma in Japan developed mirabegron as a selective beta-3 agonist to use that pathway. The FDA approved it as Myrbetriq in 2012, making it the first drug of a new class for overactive bladder. Later approvals added use together with solifenacin and use in children with neurogenic detrusor overactivity.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Beta-3 adrenergic receptors are identified in human bladder muscle."], ["2012", "FDA approves Myrbetriq, the first beta-3 agonist for overactive bladder."], ["2018", "Approval for use in combination with solifenacin."], ["2021", "Approval for neurogenic detrusor overactivity in children."]], "cell": "During the storage phase, sympathetic nerves release norepinephrine onto beta-3 receptors on the detrusor. Mirabegron is a selective agonist at these receptors. Binding activates the Gs protein, which stimulates adenylyl cyclase and raises cAMP. cAMP activates protein kinase A, which lowers intracellular calcium and opens potassium channels, so the smooth muscle relaxes. This increases bladder capacity and reduces urge sensations without blocking the acetylcholine signal needed for normal voiding. At higher exposures it can also stimulate beta-1 receptors, which helps explain small rises in heart rate and blood pressure. Mirabegron is a moderate inhibitor of CYP2D6.", "body": [["Bladder", "A relaxed detrusor stores more urine during filling, so urgency and leaking fall over several weeks while normal emptying is preserved."], ["Blood pressure", "Small rises in blood pressure occur, likely from beta-receptor stimulation in vessels and heart, so blood pressure is reviewed periodically."], ["Heart", "Heart rate can rise slightly, and palpitations can occur, especially in overdose."], ["Mouth and gut", "Because it does not block acetylcholine, dry mouth and constipation are much less common than with antimuscarinics."], ["Drug handling", "Inhibiting CYP2D6 raises levels of drugs such as metoprolol, desipramine and some antiarrhythmics."]], "journey": "Mirabegron is taken by mouth as an extended-release tablet or granules. It is absorbed over a few hours, peaking about three and a half hours after a dose, and food lowers absorption somewhat. It spreads widely into tissues. The body clears it through several routes, including CYP3A4, CYP2D6, esterases and glucuronidation, so no single pathway controls it. The half-life is long, about 50 hours, which supports once-daily dosing. About half leaves in the urine and the rest in the stool. Full bladder benefit builds over four to eight weeks.", "sidefx": "Side effects reflect beta-adrenergic stimulation outside the bladder. Stimulation of vessels and the heart raises blood pressure and heart rate a little, so it is not recommended in severe uncontrolled hypertension. In someone with a blocked bladder outlet, or also on an antimuscarinic, the extra relaxation can lead to urinary retention. Angioedema of the face, lips or airway is a rare reaction. Because it inhibits CYP2D6, it can raise levels of other drugs. Overdose causes tachycardia and high blood pressure and is treated with supportive care.", "src": ["Myrbetriq (mirabegron) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "vibegron": {"origin": "Vibegron was discovered by scientists at Merck as part of a search for highly selective beta-3 adrenergic agonists. Merck later licensed it, and it was first approved in Japan in 2018 through Kyorin and Kissei Pharmaceutical. Urovant Sciences developed it in the United States, and the FDA approved it as Gemtesa in December 2020. It was the second beta-3 agonist for overactive bladder after mirabegron. It was designed to be very selective for beta-3 over beta-1 and beta-2, and it does not meaningfully inhibit CYP2D6. A later approval covered men with overactive bladder who were also being treated for an enlarged prostate.", "timeline": [["2010s", "Merck scientists discover vibegron as a selective beta-3 agonist."], ["2018", "Vibegron is first approved in Japan."], ["2020", "FDA approves Gemtesa for overactive bladder."], ["2024", "Approval extends to men with overactive bladder on BPH treatment."]], "cell": "Vibegron is a selective agonist of the beta-3 adrenergic receptor on detrusor smooth muscle. When it binds, the receptor activates the Gs protein, which turns on adenylyl cyclase and raises cAMP. Higher cAMP activates protein kinase A, which reduces calcium available to the contractile proteins and promotes potassium channel opening, so the muscle relaxes during the storage phase. The bladder can fill to a larger volume with fewer urgency signals. Because the parasympathetic acetylcholine pathway is untouched, the voiding contraction stays largely intact. Its high selectivity for beta-3 over beta-1 and beta-2 receptors helps explain why its effect on blood pressure is small.", "body": [["Bladder", "Relaxing the detrusor during filling increases capacity and cuts urgency and urge leaks, with benefit building over weeks."], ["Mouth and gut", "Without muscarinic blockade, dry mouth and constipation are uncommon; some people have diarrhea or nausea."], ["Blood pressure", "Changes in blood pressure are small compared with mirabegron."], ["Urinary outlet", "In men with prostate obstruction, or with antimuscarinic use, extra relaxation can lead to retention."], ["Drug handling", "Vibegron can raise digoxin levels, likely through effects on the P-glycoprotein transporter."]], "journey": "Vibegron is a once-daily tablet taken by mouth. It is absorbed within one to three hours. Unlike mirabegron, it is not broken down much: the liver handles a small portion through CYP3A4, and most of the drug leaves the body unchanged, about 59 percent in the stool and 20 percent in the urine. The half-life is long, about 30 hours, which gives steady levels with daily dosing. Because it barely touches CYP2D6, it causes fewer drug interactions of that kind.", "sidefx": "Headache, nasal and throat irritation, diarrhea and nausea are the common complaints. Urinary retention happens when the bladder relaxes in someone whose outlet is already narrowed, such as with an enlarged prostate, or when an antimuscarinic is added. Hot flush is reported. Angioedema of the face, lips, tongue or larynx is rare but can affect the airway. It can raise digoxin levels. There is no specific antidote; overdose care is supportive.", "src": ["Gemtesa (vibegron) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "alfuzosin": {"origin": "Alfuzosin was developed by the French company Synthelabo and introduced in Europe in the late 1980s for benign prostatic hyperplasia. It belongs to the quinazoline family of alpha-1 blockers, like prazosin, but it was chosen because in animal and human studies it relaxed the prostate and bladder neck at doses that caused relatively little drop in blood pressure. This is called functional uroselectivity, because the drug itself is not selective for one alpha-1 subtype. An extended-release form made once-daily use practical. The FDA approved it as Uroxatral in 2003.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Synthelabo in France develops alfuzosin and launches it in Europe."], ["2003", "FDA approves Uroxatral extended-release tablets for BPH."]], "cell": "In BPH, urine flow is limited partly by the bulk of the enlarged gland and partly by smooth muscle tone in the prostate, its capsule and the bladder neck. That tone is set by norepinephrine acting on alpha-1 receptors, which couple to Gq, activate phospholipase C, generate IP3 and release calcium for contraction. Alfuzosin competitively blocks alpha-1A, 1B and 1D receptors. In the prostate this lowers smooth muscle tone and the dynamic resistance to urine flow, so the stream improves and straining eases. Alfuzosin concentrates in prostate tissue, which gives it a functional preference for the urinary tract. Blockade of vascular alpha-1 receptors still occurs and explains orthostatic hypotension. It also has a modest effect on the cardiac QT interval.", "body": [["Prostate and bladder neck", "Smooth muscle relaxes, lowering outflow resistance, so the urine stream strengthens and frequency and night urination improve over days to weeks."], ["Blood vessels", "Blocking alpha-1 receptors in arteries and veins can drop blood pressure on standing, causing dizziness or fainting."], ["Heart", "A small QT prolongation effect is described, which matters with other QT-prolonging drugs."], ["Eye", "Relaxation of the iris dilator muscle can cause floppy iris syndrome during cataract surgery."], ["Penis", "Rarely, alpha blockade causes priapism, a prolonged painful erection."]], "journey": "Alfuzosin is taken by mouth as an extended-release tablet right after the same meal each day. Food is important because absorption drops by about half when it is taken fasting. Levels peak about eight hours after a dose. It is highly bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down extensively through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ketoconazole sharply raise levels and are avoided. The half-life is about ten hours. Most metabolites leave in the stool, with about a quarter in the urine.", "sidefx": "The main side effects come from blocking alpha-1 receptors outside the prostate. In blood vessels, loss of sympathetic tone causes orthostatic hypotension, dizziness and sometimes fainting. In the iris, relaxation of the dilator muscle causes intraoperative floppy iris syndrome during cataract surgery. Priapism is rare. It has a modest QT effect. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors can raise levels enough to cause severe hypotension. Overdose is treated by lying the person flat, giving IV fluids and, if needed, vasopressors.", "src": ["Uroxatral (alfuzosin) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "silodosin": {"origin": "Silodosin was discovered by Kissei Pharmaceutical in Japan. Researchers had learned that the alpha-1A subtype is the dominant alpha receptor in prostate smooth muscle, while alpha-1B receptors are important in blood vessels. Kissei set out to make a blocker that strongly preferred alpha-1A, aiming to relieve urinary symptoms with less low blood pressure. Silodosin was approved in Japan in 2006, and the FDA approved it as Rapaflo in 2008 for the urinary symptoms of benign prostatic hyperplasia.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Alpha-1A receptors are identified as the main subtype in human prostate smooth muscle."], ["2006", "Silodosin is approved in Japan."], ["2008", "FDA approves Rapaflo for BPH symptoms."]], "cell": "Silodosin is a highly selective competitive antagonist of the alpha-1A adrenergic receptor, with roughly 160-fold preference over alpha-1B. In the prostate, prostatic urethra and bladder neck, norepinephrine acting on alpha-1A receptors activates Gq, phospholipase C and IP3, releasing calcium and tightening the smooth muscle. Blocking this lowers the dynamic part of outflow resistance, so urine flows more easily. Because vascular alpha-1B receptors are largely spared, blood pressure falls less than with nonselective blockers. Alpha-1A receptors also drive contraction of the vas deferens, seminal vesicles and bladder neck during ejaculation, so blocking them reduces seminal emission.", "body": [["Prostate and bladder neck", "Relaxed smooth muscle eases urine flow, so weak stream, hesitancy and night urination improve within days."], ["Ejaculation", "Blocking alpha-1A receptors in the vas deferens and seminal vesicles reduces emission, causing absent or retrograde ejaculation in about a quarter of users."], ["Blood vessels", "Orthostatic hypotension is less common than with older alpha blockers but can still cause dizziness or fainting."], ["Nose", "Relaxed vessels in the nasal lining can cause stuffiness."], ["Eye", "Iris dilator relaxation can cause floppy iris syndrome during cataract surgery."]], "journey": "Silodosin is taken by mouth once daily with a meal. It is absorbed within a few hours, peaking about two and a half hours after a dose, and is about 97 percent protein bound. The liver processes it in several ways: UGT2B7 glucuronidation makes an active glucuronide that lasts about a day, CYP3A4 oxidizes it, and alcohol and aldehyde dehydrogenases also play a part. The parent half-life is about 13 hours. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels and are avoided. More than half leaves in the stool and about a third in the urine.", "sidefx": "Its high alpha-1A selectivity shifts the side effect profile. The most common effect is reduced or absent ejaculation, because alpha-1A receptors control the smooth muscle that moves semen. It is harmless but bothersome. Dizziness and orthostatic hypotension are less frequent than with nonselective blockers because vascular alpha-1B receptors are mostly spared. Nasal congestion and diarrhea occur. Floppy iris syndrome can complicate cataract surgery. Priapism is rare. Overdose causes hypotension and is managed with lying flat, IV fluids and vasopressors if needed.", "src": ["Rapaflo (silodosin) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "terazosin": {"origin": "Terazosin is a member of the quinazoline alpha blocker family that began with prazosin, developed by Pfizer and introduced in the 1970s for high blood pressure. Prazosin worked but had a short half-life. Abbott Laboratories developed terazosin as a close relative that lasted long enough for once-daily dosing. The FDA approved it as Hytrin for hypertension in 1987. In the early 1990s it gained approval for benign prostatic hyperplasia, as doctors recognized that relaxing prostate smooth muscle could ease urinary symptoms. The Hytrin brand was later discontinued, but generics and an oral solution remain.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Prazosin, the first quinazoline alpha-1 blocker, enters use for hypertension."], ["1987", "FDA approves Hytrin (terazosin) for hypertension."], ["1990s", "Terazosin gains FDA approval for benign prostatic hyperplasia."]], "cell": "Terazosin is a competitive antagonist at postsynaptic alpha-1 adrenergic receptors (alpha-1A, 1B and 1D) with no meaningful alpha-2 effect. In arterioles and veins, norepinephrine normally binds alpha-1 receptors, which couple to Gq, activate phospholipase C, generate IP3 and raise intracellular calcium for contraction. Blocking this relaxes both arteries and veins, lowering peripheral resistance and venous return, so blood pressure falls. In the prostate and bladder neck the same blockade lowers smooth muscle tone and outflow resistance, improving urine flow. Because presynaptic alpha-2 receptors are not blocked, norepinephrine release stays under feedback control, so reflex tachycardia is smaller than with nonselective alpha blockers.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Arteries and veins relax, so blood pressure falls; venous pooling on standing can cause dizziness."], ["Prostate and bladder neck", "Relaxed smooth muscle reduces outflow resistance, improving stream and easing frequency."], ["Brain and balance", "The first dose, a dose increase or restarting after a break can cause fainting, the first-dose effect."], ["Heart", "Mild palpitations can occur as the body responds to lower pressure."], ["Fluid and tissues", "Peripheral edema, nasal congestion and tiredness are common."], ["Eye", "Iris dilator relaxation can cause floppy iris syndrome during cataract surgery."]], "journey": "Terazosin is taken by mouth and absorbed almost completely, with little first-pass loss. It starts acting within about 15 minutes and peaks around one hour. It is 90 to 94 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it, and about 60 percent leaves in the stool and 40 percent in the urine. The half-life is about 12 hours, which allows once-daily dosing. The first dose is usually taken at bedtime because blood pressure can drop sharply in the first hours.", "sidefx": "The first-dose effect is the signature problem: when alpha-1 receptors are first blocked, veins and arteries relax before the body adapts, so standing up can drop blood pressure enough to cause fainting. This also happens after a dose increase or after restarting the drug. Dizziness, weakness, nasal congestion and edema reflect the same vessel relaxation. Floppy iris syndrome can complicate cataract surgery. Priapism is rare. Overdose causes hypotension and shock and is treated by lying flat, IV fluids and vasopressors if needed.", "src": ["Terazosin FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "dutasteride": {"origin": "The idea behind dutasteride came from a remote village. In 1974 Julianne Imperato-McGinley and colleagues described a group of children in the Dominican Republic born with an inherited lack of the enzyme 5-alpha reductase type 2. They had very low DHT, and as adults their prostates stayed small. That showed DHT, not testosterone itself, drives prostate growth. Merck turned this into finasteride, which blocks the type 2 enzyme. Glaxo Wellcome (later GlaxoSmithKline) then developed dutasteride, a 4-azasteroid that blocks both type 1 and type 2 enzymes and suppresses DHT more completely. The FDA approved it as Avodart in 2001 for BPH, and a capsule combining it with tamsulosin, Jalyn, followed in 2010.", "timeline": [["1974", "Imperato-McGinley describes inherited 5-alpha reductase deficiency in the Dominican Republic."], ["1992", "Finasteride, the first 5-alpha reductase inhibitor, is approved for BPH."], ["2001", "FDA approves Avodart (dutasteride) for BPH."], ["2010", "Jalyn (dutasteride with tamsulosin) is approved."]], "cell": "Testosterone enters prostate stromal and epithelial cells, where 5-alpha reductase reduces it to dihydrotestosterone (DHT). DHT binds the androgen receptor with higher affinity than testosterone, and the receptor complex moves to the nucleus and turns on growth factor genes that drive prostate cell growth. Type 2 enzyme dominates in the prostate, while type 1 is found mainly in skin and liver. Dutasteride is a competitive, mechanism-based inhibitor of both isoenzymes, forming a stable complex with them. Serum DHT falls by about 90 percent or more. With less androgen receptor signaling, epithelial cells undergo involution and the prostate shrinks by about a quarter over six months or more. This reduces the static, bulk part of outflow obstruction. PSA production also falls by about half.", "body": [["Prostate", "Less DHT makes the gland shrink slowly, lowering the risk of acute urinary retention and surgery; symptoms improve over three to six months."], ["PSA levels", "PSA falls by about half, so lab values are interpreted with this in mind when screening for cancer."], ["Sexual function", "Lower DHT can reduce libido, cause erectile problems and lower ejaculate volume."], ["Breasts", "A shift in the androgen to estrogen balance can cause breast tenderness or enlargement."], ["Hair and skin", "Type 1 enzyme blockade in skin lowers scalp DHT, which can slow male-pattern hair loss, though that is not a US indication."], ["Developing fetus", "DHT is needed to form male external genitalia, so a male fetus exposed to the drug could be harmed; pregnant people do not handle leaking capsules."]], "journey": "Dutasteride is a soft gelatin capsule swallowed whole. It is absorbed within two to three hours, with bioavailability of about 60 percent. It is over 99 percent protein bound and spreads into tissues and semen. The liver breaks it down through CYP3A4 and CYP3A5, and metabolites leave mostly in the stool. Its half-life is remarkably long, about five weeks at steady state. DHT stays suppressed for weeks to months after the last capsule, which is why men do not donate blood until at least six months after stopping.", "sidefx": "Most side effects follow from lowering DHT. Reduced androgen signaling in sexual tissues and the brain explains lower libido, erectile dysfunction and smaller ejaculate volume. A relative increase in estrogen action explains breast tenderness and enlargement. In large prevention trials, 5-alpha reductase inhibitors lowered overall prostate cancer detection but showed more high-grade tumors, so it is not approved for cancer prevention. Because DHT shapes the male fetus, women who are or may become pregnant avoid handling broken capsules. Overdose has no specific toxic syndrome.", "fun": "The Dominican children who inspired this drug class were nicknamed guevedoces, roughly meaning eggs at twelve, because their male anatomy became obvious at puberty when testosterone surged.", "src": ["Avodart (dutasteride) FDA label", "Imperato-McGinley J et al. Science 1974", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "phenazopyridine": {"origin": "Phenazopyridine is an azo dye, a member of the brightly colored chemical family that also produced the first sulfa drug. It came into medical use in the early twentieth century, when chemists hoped some dyes would kill bacteria in the urine. It turned out to have no useful antibacterial action, but it did soothe the burning of a urinary tract infection. It has been sold for many decades under names such as Pyridium. Because it was on the market before modern drug approval rules, prescription phenazopyridine is listed by the FDA as a marketed unapproved drug. Lower-strength versions are sold over the counter for a few days of relief while a UTI is treated.", "timeline": [["20th century", "Phenazopyridine enters use as a urinary analgesic, sold as Pyridium among other names."], ["2000s", "Over-the-counter versions such as AZO Urinary Pain Relief are widely sold for short-term use."]], "cell": "Phenazopyridine is absorbed and much of it is excreted into the urine, where it bathes the lining of the bladder and urethra. Its exact mechanism is not well understood. The leading idea is a local analgesic or anesthetic effect on the urothelium, possibly by inhibiting the sensory nerve fibers in the bladder wall that fire when the lining is inflamed. That would explain why burning, urgency and frequency ease. It does not kill bacteria or treat the infection. A small amount is metabolized to aniline-like compounds. These can oxidize the iron in hemoglobin from Fe2+ to Fe3+, forming methemoglobin, and can stress red cells that lack G6PD, which is why the drug can cause methemoglobinemia and hemolysis.", "body": [["Bladder and urethra", "A local numbing effect on the urinary lining relieves burning, urgency and frequency within about an hour."], ["Urine and tears", "The dye turns urine orange-red and can color tears, which can stain clothing and soft contact lenses."], ["Blood", "Aniline-like metabolites can oxidize hemoglobin into methemoglobin and break down red cells, especially in G6PD deficiency."], ["Kidneys", "Accumulation, especially with kidney impairment, can cause acute kidney injury and yellowing of the skin and eyes."], ["Lab tests", "The color interferes with urine dipsticks and other color-based tests."]], "journey": "Phenazopyridine is taken by mouth after meals. It is absorbed from the gut and begins working within about an hour. The liver metabolizes part of it, including to aniline derivatives, and the kidneys excrete much of it unchanged in the urine, where it does its work. Its half-life is not well characterized, but each dose lasts about six to eight hours. When the kidneys work poorly, the drug builds up in the blood instead of reaching the urine, which raises toxicity.", "sidefx": "Orange-red urine is expected and harmless. The serious toxicities come from aniline-type metabolites. These oxidize hemoglobin iron to the ferric state, forming methemoglobin, which cannot carry oxygen, so the person looks blue and pulse oximetry stays low despite oxygen. The same oxidative stress can break down red cells, especially in G6PD deficiency. Buildup with poor kidney function can damage the kidneys and liver and tint the skin yellow. It masks UTI symptoms but does not treat the infection. Methylene blue is the antidote for symptomatic methemoglobinemia.", "src": ["Phenazopyridine DailyMed labeling (marketed unapproved drug)", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]}, "potassium-citrate": {"origin": "Citrate is a natural part of the body's energy cycle and is found in citrus fruits. Kidney stone researchers noticed that many people who form calcium stones pass too little citrate in their urine, and that citrate in urine binds calcium and blocks crystal growth. In the 1980s Charles Y. C. Pak and colleagues at the University of Texas Southwestern Medical Center in Dallas studied potassium citrate as a treatment. Their work led to Urocit-K, an extended-release wax-matrix tablet, which the FDA approved in the mid-1980s to prevent calcium oxalate and uric acid stones. The potassium salt was chosen because sodium can raise urinary calcium.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Charles Pak's group at UT Southwestern studies potassium citrate for kidney stones."], ["1985", "FDA approves Urocit-K extended-release tablets."]], "cell": "After absorption, citrate is metabolized, mainly in the liver, through the citric acid cycle. Each citrate consumes hydrogen ions and generates bicarbonate, giving a mild systemic alkali load. The kidney responds in two ways. First, more bicarbonate is filtered and the urine pH rises. A higher pH keeps uric acid in its more soluble urate form, so uric acid stones are less likely and can dissolve. Second, the less acidic environment in proximal tubule cells reduces citrate reabsorption by the sodium-dicarboxylate cotransporter, so more citrate stays in the urine. Urinary citrate binds calcium into a soluble complex and coats crystal surfaces, inhibiting calcium oxalate and calcium phosphate crystal nucleation, growth and clumping.", "body": [["Kidneys and urine", "Urine becomes less acidic and richer in citrate, so calcium oxalate and uric acid stones form less often."], ["Blood chemistry", "The potassium load raises serum potassium, which can be dangerous with kidney failure or potassium-sparing drugs."], ["Acid-base balance", "Bicarbonate generated from citrate corrects the metabolic acidosis of renal tubular acidosis."], ["Stomach and gut", "Concentrated potassium released from the tablet can irritate the gut lining, causing upset, ulceration or, rarely, bleeding or blockage."], ["Bones", "Correcting chronic acidosis reduces the release of calcium from bone as a buffer."]], "journey": "Potassium citrate is swallowed as an extended-release wax-matrix tablet with meals. The tablet slowly releases potassium citrate as it moves through the gut, and the empty wax shell can show up in stool. Citrate is absorbed and rapidly metabolized to bicarbonate, mainly by the liver. Potassium and the alkali load are handled by the kidneys. Urine pH rises within hours, and the effect is kept steady by dosing with meals through the day.", "sidefx": "The two major risks come from the potassium and from the tablet itself. Too much potassium, especially with kidney impairment, ACE inhibitors, ARBs or potassium-sparing diuretics, can cause hyperkalemia, which depolarizes heart cells and can cause dangerous rhythms and cardiac arrest. Concentrated potassium in contact with the gut lining can cause ulcers, bleeding or obstruction, which is why it is avoided with delayed gastric emptying. There is no specific antidote; hyperkalemia is treated with calcium, insulin with dextrose, potassium binders or dialysis.", "src": ["Urocit-K (potassium citrate) FDA label", "Pak CYC. Citrate and renal calculi, Miner Electrolyte Metab 1987"]}, "iron-dextran": {"origin": "Iron dextran was one of the first ways to give iron by injection safely enough for regular use. Early injected iron salts released free iron that caused severe reactions. In the 1950s researchers in Britain found that wrapping an iron oxyhydroxide core in dextran, a sugar polymer, made a stable complex that could be injected into muscle or vein. A product called Imferon became widely used. Over time, serious allergic reactions linked to the dextran shell, especially with high molecular weight forms, became a concern. The high molecular weight product was discontinued in the US. Today the low molecular weight form, INFeD, remains available and carries a boxed warning.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Iron dextran (Imferon) is introduced in Britain as an injectable iron."], ["1990s", "Low molecular weight iron dextran (INFeD) is used in the US."], ["2000s", "Newer non-dextran IV irons with lower anaphylaxis risk become available."]], "cell": "Iron dextran is a ferric oxyhydroxide core surrounded by a dextran shell. After injection the intact complex is engulfed by macrophages of the reticuloendothelial system in the liver, spleen and bone marrow. Inside lysosomes the macrophage strips the dextran away and frees the iron. The iron leaves the macrophage through ferroportin and is oxidized and loaded onto transferrin. Transferrin carries it to developing red cells in the marrow, which take it up through transferrin receptors and use it to build heme and hemoglobin. Iron not needed right away is stored as ferritin and hemosiderin. The dextran itself can be recognized by preexisting antibodies, which is thought to underlie its anaphylactic-type reactions.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "New iron supply lets red cell precursors make hemoglobin, so reticulocytes rise and hemoglobin climbs over one to two weeks and beyond."], ["Liver and spleen", "Macrophages in these organs take up the complex and store iron as ferritin and hemosiderin."], ["Immune system", "Antibodies against dextran can trigger anaphylactic reactions, including fatal ones, which is why a test dose is given first."], ["Joints and muscles", "A delayed reaction with joint pain, muscle aches and fever can appear one to two days later."], ["Skin (IM)", "Intramuscular injection can stain the skin brown, which is why Z-track technique is used."]], "journey": "Iron dextran is given by slow IV injection or deep IM injection. From muscle it is absorbed slowly through lymphatics into the blood over days to weeks. In the blood the intact complex circulates with a half-life of about 5 to 20 hours until macrophages remove it. Iron is then released to transferrin over days to weeks. The body has no active way to excrete iron, so almost all of it is kept and reused, with only tiny losses through shed cells and bleeding.", "sidefx": "The most feared problem is anaphylaxis, thought to involve antibodies against the dextran shell. It can cause collapse and death, which is the reason for the boxed warning, the test dose and having epinephrine and resuscitation gear on hand. A delayed flu-like reaction with fever, joint and muscle pain reflects inflammation as macrophages process the iron. IM injection can stain the skin. Because the body cannot excrete iron, repeated doses beyond need cause iron overload and organ deposition; chelation with deferoxamine treats overload.", "src": ["INFeD (iron dextran) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "ferric-carboxymaltose": {"origin": "Ferric carboxymaltose was developed by Vifor Pharma in Switzerland in the early 2000s to fix the main limit of older IV irons. Iron dextran could cause anaphylaxis, and iron sucrose had to be given in many small doses. Vifor designed an iron hydroxide core held in a carboxymaltose shell that binds the iron tightly, so large amounts can be given in a short time with little free iron. It was approved in Europe as Ferinject in 2007, and the FDA approved it as Injectafer in 2013. In 2023 the FDA added treatment of iron deficiency in adults with heart failure to improve exercise capacity.", "timeline": [["2007", "Ferric carboxymaltose is approved in Europe as Ferinject."], ["2013", "FDA approves Injectafer for iron deficiency anemia."], ["2023", "FDA approves use for iron deficiency in heart failure."]], "cell": "The complex has a polynuclear iron(III) hydroxide core stabilized by carboxymaltose. It is stable in the blood, so little labile iron is released. Macrophages in the liver, spleen and marrow take it up and break it down in lysosomes. The freed iron is exported through ferroportin, bound to transferrin and delivered to erythroid precursors, which use it to make heme, or stored as ferritin. A distinctive extra effect: ferric carboxymaltose raises levels of intact FGF23, a hormone made by bone cells, likely by reducing its normal cleavage. FGF23 acts on the kidney to cut phosphate reabsorption and lower active vitamin D. This explains the drug's notable risk of low phosphate.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Iron reaches red cell precursors, so hemoglobin, ferritin and transferrin saturation rise over weeks."], ["Heart", "In heart failure with iron deficiency, restoring iron improves exercise capacity, likely because heart and skeletal muscle mitochondria need iron."], ["Kidneys and phosphate", "Raised FGF23 makes the kidneys waste phosphate, which can cause low phosphate, fatigue and, with repeat courses, softening of bone."], ["Blood pressure", "Transient high blood pressure with flushing can occur right after dosing."], ["Skin", "Leakage outside the vein can leave a long-lasting brown stain."]], "journey": "Ferric carboxymaltose is given by slow IV push or short IV infusion, often as two doses about a week apart. Plasma iron peaks within hours. The intact complex is cleared from the blood with a half-life of about 7 to 12 hours as macrophages take it up. The iron is released to transferrin over days and used by the marrow or stored. The kidneys excrete almost no iron, so it stays in the body. Serum iron tests are unreliable for about a day after a dose because they measure iron still in the complex.", "sidefx": "Hypophosphatemia is the signature side effect. The drug raises intact FGF23, which tells the kidney to dump phosphate and lowers active vitamin D. Usually this is silent, but it can cause weakness and, with repeated courses, osteomalacia and fractures. Hypersensitivity and anaphylaxis can occur with any IV iron, so people are watched for at least 30 minutes. Transient hypertension and flushing occur. Leakage outside the vein causes brown staining. Iron overload is possible with doses beyond need and is treated with chelation such as deferoxamine.", "src": ["Injectafer (ferric carboxymaltose) FDA label", "Wolf M et al. JAMA 2020 (hypophosphatemia trials)"]}, "ferric-derisomaltose": {"origin": "Ferric derisomaltose, once called iron isomaltoside 1000, was developed by Pharmacosmos, a Danish company with a long history of making iron dextran. Its scientists designed a carbohydrate made of short, linear isomaltose chains woven into a matrix with iron. The goal was a complex stable enough to replace a person's whole iron deficit in a single infusion, with less of the antibody-related risk of branched dextran. It was approved in Europe as Monofer around 2010, and the FDA approved it as Monoferric in 2020 for adults with iron deficiency anemia who cannot take or do not respond to oral iron, or who have chronic kidney disease not on dialysis.", "timeline": [["2010", "Iron isomaltoside (Monofer) is approved in Europe."], ["2020", "FDA approves Monoferric (ferric derisomaltose)."]], "cell": "Ferric derisomaltose is an iron(III) hydroxide matrix tightly bound with linear derisomaltose carbohydrate. The tight structure releases little labile iron into the blood, which allows a large single dose. Macrophages of the reticuloendothelial system take up the complex and break it down in lysosomes. Iron leaves the macrophage through ferroportin, is loaded onto transferrin, and travels to erythroid precursors in the marrow, which take it in through transferrin receptor 1 and incorporate it into heme. Iron not used is stored as ferritin. The short linear chains are less immunogenic than classic branched dextran. It raises FGF23 much less than ferric carboxymaltose, so low phosphate is less frequent.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Delivered iron lets the marrow make hemoglobin, so hemoglobin begins to rise within one to two weeks."], ["Iron stores", "Ferritin and transferrin saturation rise as liver and marrow macrophages store the extra iron."], ["Immune system", "Hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis, can occur with any IV iron, so observation follows the infusion."], ["Blood pressure", "Both low and high blood pressure have been reported around the infusion."], ["Phosphate", "Low phosphate can occur but is less frequent than with ferric carboxymaltose."]], "journey": "Ferric derisomaltose is given as a single IV infusion over at least 20 minutes. Plasma iron peaks within hours. Because the complex is so stable, it stays in the blood longer than many IV irons, with a dose-dependent half-life of about one to four days. Macrophages gradually clear it and release iron to transferrin. Very little iron is excreted, so nearly all of it is kept and reused. Serum iron tests can read falsely high for a while after the infusion because they detect iron still in the complex.", "sidefx": "Hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis, are the main serious risk and are why people are watched for at least 30 minutes. Infusion reactions with flushing, joint or chest discomfort can also occur and are often mild. Low and high blood pressure have been seen. Hypophosphatemia occurs less often than with ferric carboxymaltose because FGF23 rises less. Nausea and rash are common. Giving more iron than needed can cause iron overload, which is treated with chelation such as deferoxamine.", "src": ["Monoferric (ferric derisomaltose) FDA label", "Wolf M et al. JAMA 2020 (PHOSPHARE trials)"]}, "darbepoetin-alfa": {"origin": "The story starts with erythropoietin (EPO), the kidney hormone that tells the marrow to make red cells. In 1977 Eugene Goldwasser at the University of Chicago purified human EPO from large volumes of urine. At Amgen, Fu-Kuen Lin cloned the human EPO gene in the early 1980s, and recombinant epoetin alfa (Epogen) was approved in 1989, transforming care for anemia in kidney failure. Amgen scientists, including Joan Egrie and Jeffrey Browne, noticed that EPO forms carrying more sialic acid lasted longer in the blood. They engineered a version with two extra sugar chains. This hyperglycosylated molecule, darbepoetin alfa, lasts about three times longer than epoetin. The FDA approved it as Aranesp in 2001.", "timeline": [["1977", "Eugene Goldwasser purifies human erythropoietin from urine."], ["1985", "Fu-Kuen Lin's team at Amgen reports cloning the human EPO gene."], ["1989", "FDA approves epoetin alfa (Epogen)."], ["2001", "FDA approves darbepoetin alfa (Aranesp)."]], "cell": "Darbepoetin binds the erythropoietin receptor (EpoR) on erythroid progenitors in the bone marrow, especially CFU-E cells and early erythroblasts. EpoR is a cytokine receptor without its own kinase activity. Binding brings two receptor chains together, which activates the attached JAK2 kinases. JAK2 phosphorylates the receptor and the transcription factor STAT5, and also turns on PI3K-Akt and MAPK pathways. These signals block apoptosis of progenitors and drive their proliferation and differentiation into reticulocytes. Its two additional N-linked carbohydrate chains add sialic acid, which slows clearance and gives a longer half-life, though receptor affinity is somewhat lower than native EPO.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Progenitors survive and mature, so reticulocytes rise within days and hemoglobin climbs over two to six weeks."], ["Blood", "More red cells raise oxygen-carrying capacity but also blood viscosity, which raises clot risk if hemoglobin goes too high."], ["Blood vessels", "Blood pressure often rises, possibly from higher viscosity and effects on vessel tone, so it is controlled before and during therapy."], ["Heart and brain", "Targeting high hemoglobin raises risks of heart attack, stroke and death, which is the core of the boxed warning."], ["Iron stores", "Rapid red cell production uses up iron, so iron status is checked and iron is often given alongside."], ["Tumors", "Some cancers carry EPO receptors, and ESAs have shortened survival or sped tumor growth in some studies."]], "journey": "Darbepoetin is injected under the skin or into a vein, often through the dialysis circuit. After subcutaneous injection it is absorbed slowly, peaking about one to three days later. It stays mostly in the blood. Its half-life is about 21 hours IV and about 49 hours subcutaneously in kidney disease, roughly three times that of epoetin alfa, which allows dosing every one to three weeks. It is cleared like other glycoproteins, partly by uptake through its receptor, with little kidney excretion.", "sidefx": "The serious risks come from pushing red cell mass too high or too fast. Thicker blood and vascular effects raise the risk of heart attack, stroke, venous clots and dialysis access clotting, and studies showed more deaths when hemoglobin was pushed to higher targets. Hypertension and, rarely, seizures from hypertensive encephalopathy occur. In some cancers, stimulating EPO receptors may promote tumor growth. Rarely, antibodies against the drug also block natural EPO and cause pure red cell aplasia. There is no antidote; therapy is withheld and phlebotomy is used for severe polycythemia.", "src": ["Aranesp (darbepoetin alfa) FDA label", "Egrie JC, Browne JK. Br J Cancer 2001", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "romiplostim": {"origin": "Thrombopoietin (TPO), the main hormone that controls platelet production, was cloned in 1994 by several groups after a long search. Early recombinant TPO drugs were tested, but one, a pegylated form called PEG-MGDF, caused some volunteers to make antibodies that also blocked their own TPO, leading to severe low platelets. Amgen scientists took a different path. They found a short peptide that activated the TPO receptor but looked nothing like TPO, then joined copies of it to an antibody Fc fragment to make it last. This peptibody, romiplostim, was approved by the FDA as Nplate in 2008 for chronic immune thrombocytopenia (ITP). In 2021 it was also approved for the blood-forming injury of acute radiation syndrome.", "timeline": [["1994", "Several research groups clone thrombopoietin, the ligand for the c-Mpl receptor."], ["1990s", "Antibody problems with PEG-MGDF halt early recombinant TPO development."], ["2008", "FDA approves Nplate (romiplostim) for chronic ITP."], ["2021", "Approval for the hematopoietic syndrome of acute radiation syndrome."]], "cell": "Romiplostim is an Fc-peptide fusion protein. Its peptide domains bind the thrombopoietin receptor (c-Mpl) on hematopoietic stem cells and megakaryocyte progenitors at the same site as natural TPO. Binding brings receptor chains together, activating JAK2, which phosphorylates STAT5 and turns on MAPK and PI3K-Akt pathways. These signals promote survival, proliferation and maturation of megakaryocytes, which grow larger, become polyploid and shed more platelets. In ITP, platelets are destroyed by antibodies and production is relatively low, so boosting production raises the count. Because the peptide has no sequence similarity to TPO, antibodies against it are unlikely to cross-react with the body's own TPO. Platelets themselves carry c-Mpl and help clear the drug.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Megakaryocytes multiply and mature, raising platelet output; platelets begin to rise in about four to nine days."], ["Blood clotting", "More platelets reduce bleeding in ITP, but very high counts raise the risk of clots."], ["Marrow structure", "Long-term stimulation can increase reticulin fibers in the marrow."], ["Muscles and joints", "Joint and muscle aches, headache and dizziness are common complaints."], ["After stopping", "Platelets can fall below the starting level after the drug is stopped, raising bleeding risk."]], "journey": "Romiplostim is injected under the skin once a week. It is absorbed slowly and then cleared mostly by binding to TPO receptors on platelets and megakaryocytes, along with general protein breakdown. This target-mediated clearance means its half-life depends on the platelet count, with a median around three and a half days. Platelets peak about 12 to 16 days after a dose and drift back toward baseline over about two weeks. The kidneys play little role.", "sidefx": "Side effects mostly reflect the drug doing its job too well or for too long. Excess platelets can cause arterial or venous clots. Constant stimulation of megakaryocytes can increase reticulin fibers in the marrow. Stopping suddenly removes the stimulus while antibody-mediated destruction continues, so platelets can drop below baseline and bleeding can occur. In myelodysplastic syndromes, stimulation of abnormal clones has been linked to progression to AML, so it is not used for MDS. There is no antidote; dosing is adjusted to the platelet count.", "src": ["Nplate (romiplostim) FDA label", "Kuter DJ. Annu Rev Med 2009"]}, "eltrombopag": {"origin": "After the antibody problems with early recombinant thrombopoietin, researchers looked for small molecules that could switch on the TPO receptor from a different spot. Scientists at SmithKline Beecham (later GlaxoSmithKline), working with Ligand Pharmaceuticals, discovered eltrombopag, a pill that activates the receptor through its transmembrane region. The FDA approved it as Promacta in 2008 for chronic ITP, the first oral thrombopoietin receptor agonist. It was later approved to raise platelets in hepatitis C so interferon could be given, and for severe aplastic anemia, where it helped revive stem cell output. Novartis later acquired it, and a choline salt, Alvaiz, was approved in 2023.", "timeline": [["1990s", "SmithKline Beecham and Ligand begin a search for small-molecule TPO receptor agonists."], ["2008", "FDA approves Promacta, the first oral TPO receptor agonist, for chronic ITP."], ["2014", "Approval for severe aplastic anemia after poor response to immunosuppression."], ["2018", "Approval for first-line use with immunosuppression in severe aplastic anemia."], ["2023", "Alvaiz (eltrombopag choline) is approved."]], "cell": "Eltrombopag binds the transmembrane domain of the thrombopoietin receptor (c-Mpl), a site different from where natural TPO binds. Its effect therefore adds to, rather than competes with, the body's own TPO. Binding activates JAK2, which phosphorylates STAT3 and STAT5, and also MAPK signaling. These pathways drive proliferation and differentiation of megakaryocytes from marrow progenitors, so platelet production rises. Because c-Mpl is also present on hematopoietic stem cells, eltrombopag can support recovery of multiple blood lines in aplastic anemia. Separately, its chemical structure chelates polyvalent cations such as iron, calcium, magnesium and aluminum, which affects absorption and may lower iron in cells.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Megakaryocytes increase and platelets rise within one to two weeks; in aplastic anemia, red and white cells may also recover."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes and bilirubin can rise, and rare severe liver injury occurs; in hepatitis C with interferon, liver decompensation is a boxed risk."], ["Blood clotting", "Higher platelet counts reduce bleeding but raise clot risk, including portal vein thrombosis in liver disease."], ["Eyes", "Cataracts were seen in animal studies and in some patients, so eye exams are part of monitoring."], ["Gut", "Nausea and diarrhea are common, and the drug binds dietary calcium and iron in the gut."]], "journey": "Eltrombopag is taken by mouth once daily. Because it binds calcium, iron, magnesium and aluminum, food high in calcium, dairy, antacids and mineral supplements sharply cut its absorption, so it is spaced away from them. It peaks two to six hours after a dose and is over 99 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it extensively through CYP1A2, CYP2C8, UGT1A1 and UGT1A3. Its half-life is about 21 to 32 hours in healthy adults and longer in ITP. People of East Asian ancestry have higher levels. Most leaves in the stool, with about a third in the urine as metabolites.", "sidefx": "Liver toxicity is the most important risk and is likely related to how the liver handles the drug, so liver tests are checked regularly. In hepatitis C, adding it to interferon and ribavirin raised the risk of liver failure. Raising platelets in people with liver disease, where portal blood flow is already slow, can cause portal vein thrombosis. Marrow reticulin fibers can increase with chronic stimulation. Stopping can cause rebound low platelets. Cataracts have been reported. There is no antidote; oral calcium or magnesium can bind unabsorbed drug in overdose.", "src": ["Promacta (eltrombopag) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "avatrombopag": {"origin": "Avatrombopag is a small-molecule thrombopoietin receptor agonist that was first discovered by researchers in Japan and later developed by Dova Pharmaceuticals in the United States. Dova focused on a specific problem: people with chronic liver disease often have low platelets because the damaged liver makes less thrombopoietin, and they frequently need procedures that require platelet transfusions. The FDA approved avatrombopag as Doptelet in 2018 to raise platelets before a scheduled procedure in adults with chronic liver disease, and in 2019 for chronic immune thrombocytopenia. It does not have the liver toxicity warning or the food and mineral binding problem of eltrombopag.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Avatrombopag is discovered as an oral TPO receptor agonist by researchers in Japan."], ["2018", "FDA approves Doptelet for low platelets in chronic liver disease before a procedure."], ["2019", "Approval extends to chronic immune thrombocytopenia."]], "cell": "Avatrombopag binds the transmembrane domain of the thrombopoietin receptor (c-Mpl), a site separate from natural TPO's binding site, so its effect adds to the body's own TPO. Binding triggers JAK2 activation and downstream STAT and MAPK signaling, which drives proliferation and differentiation of megakaryocytes from marrow progenitors. More mature megakaryocytes release more platelets. In chronic liver disease, the liver makes less TPO, so supplying receptor stimulation from outside corrects the main cause of low platelet production. Unlike eltrombopag, avatrombopag does not chelate polyvalent cations, so it can be taken with food and it lacks a hepatotoxicity signal.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Megakaryocyte growth raises platelet counts starting around three to five days, peaking about 10 to 13 days after starting."], ["Blood clotting", "Higher platelets reduce bleeding risk during procedures but raise clot risk."], ["Portal circulation", "In chronic liver disease, slow portal blood flow plus more platelets can lead to portal vein thrombosis."], ["Whole body", "Fever, abdominal pain, nausea, headache and fatigue are common in trials."], ["Liver", "It has not shown the liver injury signal seen with eltrombopag, which suits people who already have liver disease."]], "journey": "Avatrombopag is taken by mouth with food, which makes absorption more consistent. It peaks within several hours and is more than 96 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it through CYP2C9 and CYP3A4, so strong inducers or inhibitors of those enzymes change its levels. The half-life is about 19 hours. About 88 percent leaves in the stool and a small amount in the urine. For the procedure indication, it is started several days ahead so platelets are up by the procedure day, and they drift back toward baseline over weeks.", "sidefx": "The main concern is blood clots. More platelets in people whose clotting balance is already altered, as in cirrhosis with slow portal flow, can cause portal vein thrombosis, pulmonary embolism or stroke. It is not given to normalize platelet counts in liver disease, only to raise them enough for a procedure. Headache, fatigue, bruising and nosebleeds are reported in ITP. There is no antidote; overdose is managed by watching the platelet count.", "src": ["Doptelet (avatrombopag) FDA label", "Terrault N et al. Gastroenterology 2018 (ADAPT trials)"]}, "caplacizumab": {"origin": "Caplacizumab comes from camels. In 1993 a team at the Vrije Universiteit Brussel led by Raymond Hamers reported in Nature that camels and llamas make unusual antibodies built from heavy chains only. The single binding domain of these antibodies, later called a nanobody, is tiny, stable and easy to engineer. The Belgian company Ablynx, founded in Ghent, built a drug platform around nanobodies. Caplacizumab joins two humanized nanobodies that grab von Willebrand factor. It targets acquired thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP), a disease Eli Moschcowitz first described in 1924. The FDA approved it as Cablivi in 2019, the first nanobody medicine approved in the US, shortly after Sanofi acquired Ablynx.", "timeline": [["1924", "Eli Moschcowitz describes the disease later named TTP."], ["1993", "Hamers' Brussels team reports heavy-chain-only antibodies in camels."], ["2001", "ADAMTS13 deficiency is identified as the cause of TTP."], ["2019", "FDA approves Cablivi, the first nanobody drug in the US."]], "cell": "Von Willebrand factor (vWF) is released as huge multimers that are normally trimmed by the enzyme ADAMTS13. In acquired TTP, autoantibodies block ADAMTS13, so ultralarge vWF strings stay on vessel walls. Under the high shear of small vessels, their A1 domains grab platelet glycoprotein Ib-IX-V, and platelets pile up into microthrombi. These clots block blood flow to the brain, heart and kidneys and consume platelets. Caplacizumab is a bivalent nanobody that binds the A1 domain of vWF and blocks its interaction with GPIb. Platelets can no longer stick to the vWF strings, so new microthrombi stop forming and platelet counts recover. It does not restore ADAMTS13 or remove the autoantibody, which is why plasma exchange and immunosuppression continue alongside it.", "body": [["Small blood vessels", "Blocking vWF-platelet binding stops new microclots, protecting organs from ischemia."], ["Platelets", "With less consumption, the platelet count rises within days, faster than with plasma exchange alone."], ["Brain, heart and kidneys", "Fewer microthrombi lower the risk of stroke, heart injury and kidney damage, and a pivotal trial showed a lower combined rate of TTP-related death, recurrence and major clots."], ["Bleeding", "Because vWF also anchors platelets at injury sites, bleeding such as nosebleeds and gum bleeding is common."], ["Skin", "Injection site reactions and hives can occur."]], "journey": "The first dose is given by IV bolus before a plasma exchange, then daily injections under the skin follow. After subcutaneous injection it peaks in about six to seven hours. Caplacizumab is cleared mainly by binding vWF; the drug-vWF complex is removed by the liver, and some unbound nanobody is filtered by the kidneys. This target-mediated disposition means its levels depend on how much vWF is present. Its effect on vWF lasts about a week after the last dose.", "sidefx": "Bleeding is the expected side effect because vWF normally bridges platelets to injured vessel walls. Nosebleeds, gum bleeding and, more seriously, gastrointestinal, gynecologic or rarely brain bleeding can occur, especially with other anticoagulants or bleeding disorders. The effect lasts about seven days, so it is held for a week before planned surgery. Headache, fatigue and injection site reactions are common. For urgent bleeding, von Willebrand factor concentrate can correct hemostasis.", "fun": "A nanobody is about one tenth the size of a regular antibody, small enough that many can be strung together like beads.", "src": ["Cablivi (caplacizumab) FDA label", "Hamers-Casterman C et al. Nature 1993", "Scully M et al. NEJM 2019 (HERCULES trial)"]}, "l-glutamine": {"origin": "Glutamine is the most abundant amino acid in the blood and is found in many foods. Its use in sickle cell disease came from research by Yutaka Niihara and colleagues at Harbor-UCLA Medical Center in the 1990s. They found that sickle red cells are under heavy oxidative stress and have a reduced NADH redox state, and that oral glutamine raised NAD levels in these cells. Niihara's company, Emmaus Medical, ran the clinical trials. The FDA approved pharmaceutical-grade L-glutamine oral powder as Endari in 2017 to reduce acute complications of sickle cell disease. It was the first new sickle cell drug in nearly 20 years, after hydroxyurea, and the first approved for children with the disease.", "timeline": [["1910", "James Herrick describes sickle-shaped red cells in a patient."], ["1990s", "Yutaka Niihara's group studies glutamine and red cell NAD in sickle cell disease."], ["1998", "Hydroxyurea is approved for adults with sickle cell disease."], ["2017", "FDA approves Endari (L-glutamine) for sickle cell disease."]], "cell": "The exact mechanism is unknown. The leading idea centers on redox balance in sickle red cells. Sickle hemoglobin is unstable and generates reactive oxygen species, which damage membranes, make cells stiff and sticky and shorten their life. Red cells defend themselves with NADH, NADPH and glutathione. Glutamine is a precursor for NAD synthesis and supplies glutamate for glutathione. Supplementation is thought to raise the NAD and NADH pool and the NADH redox potential, so the cells resist oxidative damage better. Less oxidized, less sticky red cells may adhere less to the vessel lining, which could explain fewer vaso-occlusive pain crises. The evidence comes from clinical trials showing fewer crises rather than from a defined receptor target.", "body": [["Red blood cells", "Better antioxidant capacity is thought to make sickle cells less damaged and less adhesive."], ["Small blood vessels", "Fewer sticky cells may mean fewer vaso-occlusive events, the cause of sickle pain crises."], ["Lungs", "The pivotal trial found fewer episodes of acute chest syndrome."], ["Hospital use", "Patients in the trial had fewer hospitalizations and shorter stays."], ["Gut", "Constipation, nausea and abdominal pain are the most common complaints."]], "journey": "L-glutamine is a powder mixed into a cold or room-temperature drink or soft food and taken twice daily. It is absorbed quickly from the small intestine and peaks in the blood in about 30 minutes. Much of it is used by gut cells as fuel before reaching the blood. What enters the circulation joins the body's own glutamine pool and is used to make proteins, nucleotides, NAD and glutathione, or is broken down into glutamate and ammonia for normal nitrogen handling. Its half-life is about an hour. Clinical benefit builds over weeks to months.", "sidefx": "Because glutamine is a normal nutrient, side effects are mostly mild and digestive: constipation, nausea, abdominal pain and headache. Pain in the chest, back and extremities was also reported in trials, partly overlapping with sickle cell symptoms. Glutamine metabolism produces ammonia and glutamate, so it has not been studied in significant kidney or liver impairment. Allergic reactions are rare. There is no antidote, and overdose mainly causes stomach upset.", "src": ["Endari (L-glutamine oral powder) FDA label", "Niihara Y et al. NEJM 2018"]}, "deferasirox": {"origin": "People with thalassemia and sickle cell disease who receive regular transfusions build up iron, because the body has no way to excrete it. For decades the main treatment was deferoxamine, a chelator from the bacterium Streptomyces pilosus that had to be infused under the skin for many hours most nights. Scientists at Novartis in Basel screened compounds for an iron chelator that could be taken by mouth once a day and found deferasirox, a tridentate ligand. The FDA approved it as Exjade, a tablet dispersed in liquid, in 2005. A film-coated tablet that can be swallowed, Jadenu, followed in 2015, along with sprinkle granules.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Deferoxamine, from Streptomyces pilosus, becomes the first iron chelator."], ["2005", "FDA approves Exjade (deferasirox) for transfusional iron overload."], ["2013", "Approval extends to iron overload in non-transfusion-dependent thalassemia."], ["2015", "FDA approves the swallowable Jadenu tablet."]], "cell": "Deferasirox is a tridentate chelator: each molecule grips iron at three points. Two molecules wrap one ferric iron (Fe3+) atom, forming a stable 2:1 complex. It is lipophilic, so it enters cells, where it binds iron from the labile iron pool and from ferritin as it is broken down. The complex is carried to the liver and secreted into bile, then leaves in the stool. Removing labile iron lowers the formation of hydroxyl radicals through the Fenton reaction, which otherwise damages lipids, proteins and DNA in the liver, heart and endocrine glands. Over months, serum ferritin and liver iron fall. Its affinity for zinc and copper is low.", "body": [["Liver", "Liver iron falls over months, reducing the risk of fibrosis, but the drug can also raise liver enzymes and rarely cause liver failure."], ["Heart", "Reducing iron lowers the risk of iron-related heart failure and rhythm problems."], ["Kidneys", "Deferasirox can raise creatinine and damage the proximal tubule, causing Fanconi syndrome or acute kidney injury."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea, vomiting and abdominal pain are common; ulcers and bleeding can occur, especially in older or very ill patients."], ["Ears and eyes", "Hearing loss and cataracts or retinal changes have been reported, so hearing and eye exams are part of care."], ["Skin", "Rash is common; severe reactions are rare."]], "journey": "Deferasirox is taken by mouth once daily. It is absorbed within about one and a half to four hours. It is about 99 percent bound to albumin. The liver conjugates it through glucuronidation by UGT1A1 and UGT1A3, with only minor CYP450 involvement. The drug and its iron complex are secreted into bile, so about 84 percent of the dose leaves in the stool and only about 8 percent in the urine. It undergoes enterohepatic recycling. The half-life is about 8 to 16 hours, enough for once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "The serious risks include kidney injury, liver injury and gut bleeding, which together form the boxed warning. Kidney effects may involve changes in renal blood flow and direct tubular toxicity, which causes losses of glucose, phosphate and bicarbonate (Fanconi syndrome). The liver handles the drug heavily, which may explain liver enzyme rises. Gut irritation explains diarrhea, ulcers and bleeding. Chelating too much iron when stores are low raises toxicity, so dosing follows ferritin. Bone marrow suppression, hearing and vision changes and severe skin reactions are less common. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Exjade and Jadenu (deferasirox) FDA labels", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "deferiprone": {"origin": "Deferiprone was developed in Britain in the early 1980s by researchers seeking a cheap iron chelator that could be taken by mouth. Robert Hider and colleagues designed a family of small 3-hydroxypyridin-4-one molecules that bind iron, and George Kontoghiorghes, working in London, reported the first use of deferiprone in patients in the late 1980s. It was approved in Europe in 1999 and became widely used in thalassemia worldwide. The FDA granted it accelerated approval as Ferriprox in 2011 for transfusional iron overload in thalassemia when other chelation is inadequate. Later approvals extended it to sickle cell disease and other anemias.", "timeline": [["1980s", "British researchers develop hydroxypyridinone iron chelators, including deferiprone."], ["1999", "Deferiprone is approved in the European Union."], ["2011", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Ferriprox."], ["2021", "Approval extends to iron overload in sickle cell disease and other anemias."]], "cell": "Deferiprone is a bidentate chelator: each molecule binds iron at two points, so three molecules wrap one ferric iron (Fe3+) to form a neutral 3:1 complex. Because it is small and neutral, it moves easily across cell membranes, including into heart muscle cells. There it captures labile iron that would otherwise drive the Fenton reaction, which makes hydroxyl radicals that damage membranes and mitochondria. The iron complex leaves the cell, and the kidneys excrete it, turning urine red-brown. Its strength at reaching the heart is the reason it is valued for cardiac iron overload, seen on MRI as an improved T2* signal. Its effect on the marrow that causes agranulocytosis is not fully understood.", "body": [["Heart", "It removes iron from heart muscle well, improving cardiac MRI iron measures and lowering the risk of iron-related heart failure."], ["Bone marrow", "It can cause neutropenia and agranulocytosis, which can lead to fatal infections, so the neutrophil count is checked regularly."], ["Urine", "The iron complex turns urine red-brown, which is harmless."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise, and liver iron falls more slowly than heart iron."], ["Joints", "Joint pain and swelling are common complaints, and the reason is not well understood."], ["Trace minerals", "Zinc can be chelated along with iron, which can cause zinc deficiency."]], "journey": "Deferiprone is taken by mouth two or three times a day. It is absorbed quickly, peaking in about an hour, and food can lessen nausea. Less than 10 percent binds to plasma proteins. The liver converts most of it by UGT1A6 glucuronidation into an inactive metabolite that cannot bind iron. The kidneys excrete 75 to 90 percent of the dose, mainly as the glucuronide, along with the iron complex. The half-life is short, about two hours, which is why it is dosed several times a day.", "sidefx": "Agranulocytosis is the most serious risk and carries a boxed warning. The mechanism is not fully known, but the drug appears to injure neutrophil precursors in the marrow, and neutropenia often comes first. Without neutrophils, infections can become life threatening, so blood counts are checked often and therapy is stopped with infection. Nausea, vomiting and abdominal pain come from gut irritation. Joint pain is common. Chelation of zinc can cause deficiency. High doses in children have caused neurologic effects. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Ferriprox (deferiprone) FDA label", "Kontoghiorghes GJ et al. BMJ 1987"]}, "factor-viii-recombinant": {"origin": "Hemophilia A has been recognized for centuries as a bleeding disease passed through mothers to sons. Treatment began with whole plasma, then improved in 1964 when Judith Graham Pool discovered that cryoprecipitate, a frozen-then-thawed plasma fraction, is rich in factor VIII. Concentrates made from pooled plasma followed, but in the late 1970s and 1980s many people with hemophilia were infected with HIV and hepatitis C from contaminated products. In 1984 teams at Genentech and Genetics Institute cloned the large factor VIII gene. The FDA approved the first recombinant factor VIII products in the early 1990s. Later products such as Advate, approved in 2003, were made without added human or animal proteins, and extended half-life versions came in the 2010s.", "timeline": [["1964", "Judith Graham Pool discovers factor VIII-rich cryoprecipitate."], ["1984", "Genentech and Genetics Institute clone the human factor VIII gene."], ["1992", "FDA approves the first recombinant factor VIII product."], ["2003", "Advate is approved, made without added human or animal plasma proteins."], ["2014", "The first extended half-life factor VIII (Eloctate) is approved."]], "cell": "Factor VIII is a cofactor, not an enzyme. In plasma it circulates bound to von Willebrand factor, which protects it from early clearance. At an injury, thrombin cleaves factor VIII, releasing it from vWF and turning it into active factor VIIIa. On the phospholipid surface of activated platelets, factor VIIIa binds factor IXa and calcium to form the intrinsic tenase complex. This complex activates factor X to Xa roughly 100,000 times faster than factor IXa alone. Factor Xa then forms prothrombinase with factor Va, producing the burst of thrombin that converts fibrinogen to fibrin. In hemophilia A, too little factor VIII means too little thrombin, so clots are weak and bleeding continues into joints and muscles. Recombinant factor VIII, made in mammalian cell lines, replaces the missing cofactor.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Replaced factor VIII restores tenase activity and thrombin generation, so the aPTT corrects and bleeding stops."], ["Joints", "Regular prophylaxis prevents repeated bleeding into knees, ankles and elbows, which otherwise destroys cartilage over time."], ["Muscles and brain", "Rapid replacement is lifesaving for deep muscle bleeds and bleeding in the head."], ["Immune system", "Some patients, especially previously untreated children, form neutralizing antibodies (inhibitors) that make the factor ineffective."], ["Whole body", "Headache, fever and joint aches are reported, and allergic reactions can occur."]], "journey": "Recombinant factor VIII is given by slow IV injection, often at home by patients or families. It works immediately, with peak levels at the end of the infusion. In the blood it binds von Willebrand factor, which shields it from clearance. It is used up in clotting and cleared by receptors such as LRP in the liver, then broken down like normal proteins. The half-life is about 12 hours in adults and shorter in children, which is why standard products are given about every other day for prophylaxis. Extended half-life versions use Fc fusion or pegylation to last longer.", "sidefx": "The most important problem is inhibitor formation. The immune system, especially in people with severe gene defects who make no factor VIII at all, can see the infused protein as foreign and make neutralizing antibodies, so bleeding no longer responds. Allergic reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, can occur. Because it simply replaces a missing protein at normal levels, clotting problems are rare. Recombinant products do not carry the virus risk of old plasma concentrates. Bleeding with inhibitors is treated with bypassing agents.", "fun": "Factor VIII was one of the largest proteins ever produced by recombinant DNA technology when it was first cloned, with a gene spanning about 186,000 DNA base pairs.", "src": ["Advate (antihemophilic factor, recombinant) FDA label", "Gitschier J et al. Nature 1984", "Pool JG, Shannon AE. NEJM 1965"]}, "factor-ix-recombinant": {"origin": "Hemophilia B was recognized as a separate disease in 1952, when Rosemary Biggs and colleagues in Oxford found that blood from a young patient named Stephen Christmas could correct the clotting of other hemophilia patients. The disorder became known as Christmas disease, and the missing protein as Christmas factor, now factor IX. A 2009 DNA study of remains of the Russian royal family showed that the royal disease passed down from Queen Victoria was hemophilia B. The factor IX gene was cloned in 1982. After plasma-derived concentrates spread HIV and hepatitis in the 1980s, Genetics Institute developed recombinant factor IX, and the FDA approved it as BeneFIX in 1997. Extended half-life versions and a gene therapy came later.", "timeline": [["1952", "Biggs and colleagues describe Christmas disease (hemophilia B)."], ["1982", "The human factor IX gene is cloned."], ["1997", "FDA approves BeneFIX, the first recombinant factor IX."], ["2009", "DNA analysis shows the royal disease of Queen Victoria's descendants was hemophilia B."], ["2014", "The first extended half-life factor IX (Alprolix) is approved."]], "cell": "Factor IX is a vitamin K-dependent serine protease zymogen. Its gamma-carboxyglutamate (Gla) domain binds calcium and anchors it to negatively charged phospholipids on activated platelets. At an injury, factor IX is activated to IXa by factor XIa or by the tissue factor-factor VIIa complex. Factor IXa then joins factor VIIIa and calcium on the platelet surface to form the intrinsic tenase complex, which activates factor X to Xa very efficiently. Factor Xa drives prothrombinase and the thrombin burst that builds a stable fibrin clot. In hemophilia B, missing factor IX means weak thrombin generation and prolonged bleeding. Recombinant factor IX, produced in Chinese hamster ovary cells, replaces the enzyme.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Restored factor IX lets tenase work, correcting the aPTT and stopping bleeding."], ["Joints", "Regular prophylaxis prevents bleeding into joints and the arthritis it causes."], ["Tissues outside the blood", "Factor IX moves into tissues and binds to vessel wall collagen, which is part of why measured recovery in blood is lower than with factor VIII."], ["Immune system", "Inhibitors are less common than in hemophilia A, but when they form they often come with allergic reactions or anaphylaxis."], ["Kidneys", "Nephrotic syndrome has occurred during immune tolerance therapy in patients with inhibitors."]], "journey": "Recombinant factor IX is given by slow IV injection, often at home. It acts immediately, peaking at the end of the infusion. Unlike factor VIII, it spreads beyond the blood into tissues and binds to collagen IV in vessel walls, so less stays in plasma, and recombinant factor IX shows lower recovery than plasma-derived factor IX. It is used up in clotting and broken down like other proteins. The half-life is about 18 to 24 hours, allowing standard products to be given once or twice weekly for prophylaxis, and Fc or albumin fusion versions last longer still.", "sidefx": "Allergic reactions and anaphylaxis are the most serious immediate risks, and in hemophilia B they often appear around the time inhibitors form. Inhibitors are neutralizing antibodies against the infused protein, more likely in people with large gene deletions who make no factor IX. Immune tolerance therapy in these patients can cause nephrotic syndrome. High factor IX levels can rarely cause clots. Headache, nausea and altered taste are reported. There is no antidote; bleeding with inhibitors needs bypassing agents.", "fun": "The Christmas name has nothing to do with the holiday. It honors the first patient studied, Stephen Christmas, and the paper appeared in the Christmas issue of the British Medical Journal.", "src": ["BeneFIX (coagulation factor IX, recombinant) FDA label", "Biggs R et al. BMJ 1952", "Rogaev EI et al. Science 2009"]}, "iptacopan": {"origin": "Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH) is a rare disease in which red cells lack the surface proteins CD55 and CD59 that protect them from complement. The first targeted treatment, the antibody eculizumab, blocked complement protein C5 and stopped red cells from bursting in the blood. But many patients stayed anemic because complement fragments still coated their red cells, which were then removed in the spleen and liver. Novartis scientists designed iptacopan, a small molecule that blocks factor B much earlier in the alternative pathway. The FDA approved it as Fabhalta in December 2023 for adults with PNH, the first oral monotherapy for the disease. Approvals for IgA nephropathy and C3 glomerulopathy followed.", "timeline": [["2007", "Eculizumab becomes the first complement inhibitor approved for PNH."], ["2023", "FDA approves Fabhalta (iptacopan) for PNH as an oral monotherapy."], ["2024", "Accelerated approval to reduce proteinuria in IgA nephropathy."], ["2025", "Approval for C3 glomerulopathy."]], "cell": "The alternative complement pathway is always ticking over. C3b deposits on surfaces and binds factor B. Factor D cleaves the bound factor B into Bb, forming the C3 convertase C3bBb, which cleaves more C3 into C3b in a fast amplification loop. Iptacopan binds factor B and blocks this convertase. As a result, less C3b coats cells, so fewer red cells are tagged for removal by macrophages (extravascular hemolysis). Less C3b also means less C5 convertase and less membrane attack complex, so fewer red cells burst in the vessels (intravascular hemolysis). In IgA nephropathy and C3 glomerulopathy, the same block reduces complement-driven injury in the glomeruli, lowering protein in the urine.", "body": [["Red blood cells", "Both intravascular and extravascular hemolysis fall, so hemoglobin rises, LDH falls and transfusions are needed less often."], ["Kidneys", "Less complement activation in the glomeruli reduces inflammation and protein leakage in IgA nephropathy and C3 glomerulopathy."], ["Immune defense", "Complement is key for killing encapsulated bacteria, so infections with meningococcus, pneumococcus and Haemophilus become a serious risk."], ["Blood lipids", "Cholesterol and triglycerides can rise during treatment."], ["After stopping", "In PNH, stopping can cause a rapid return of hemolysis."]], "journey": "Iptacopan is a capsule taken by mouth twice daily. It is absorbed quickly, peaking in about two hours. Protein binding depends on concentration, because it binds factor B in the blood. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP2C8, with glucuronidation as well, so strong CYP2C8 inhibitors and inducers alter levels. The half-life is about 25 hours. Most leaves in the stool, with some in the urine. LDH begins to fall within days to weeks.", "sidefx": "The most serious risk follows directly from the mechanism. The alternative pathway helps the body coat and kill bacteria with polysaccharide capsules. Blocking it makes infections with Neisseria meningitidis, Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae type b far more dangerous, which is the boxed warning. Vaccination is completed before starting, and the drug is available only through a REMS program. Headache, diarrhea and raised lipids are common. In PNH, missed doses or stopping can let complement attack the unprotected red cells again. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Fabhalta (iptacopan) FDA label", "Peffault de Latour R et al. NEJM 2024 (APPLY-PNH)"]}, "trientine": {"origin": "Wilson disease, an inherited copper overload of the liver and brain, was described in 1912 by the neurologist S. A. Kinnier Wilson. In 1956 John Walshe at Cambridge introduced penicillamine, the first oral copper chelator, which turned a fatal disease into a treatable one. Some patients could not tolerate penicillamine, so in 1969 Walshe reported using trientine (triethylenetetramine), an industrial chemical with strong copper binding, as an alternative. The FDA approved it as Syprine in 1985 for Wilson disease in people intolerant of penicillamine. A different salt, trientine tetrahydrochloride (Cuvrior), was approved in 2022 for stable patients.", "timeline": [["1912", "S. A. Kinnier Wilson describes the disease that bears his name."], ["1956", "John Walshe introduces penicillamine for Wilson disease."], ["1969", "Walshe reports trientine for patients intolerant of penicillamine."], ["1985", "FDA approves Syprine (trientine hydrochloride)."], ["2022", "FDA approves Cuvrior (trientine tetrahydrochloride)."]], "cell": "In Wilson disease, a defect in the ATP7B copper transporter stops liver cells from loading copper into ceruloplasmin and excreting it into bile. Free copper builds up in the liver, spills into the blood as non-ceruloplasmin-bound copper and deposits in the brain, cornea and kidneys, where it drives oxidative injury. Trientine is a polyamine whose four nitrogen atoms wrap a copper ion into a stable, water-soluble complex. The complex is filtered by the kidneys, so urinary copper rises. Trientine may also reduce copper absorption from the gut. Unlike penicillamine, it has no sulfhydryl group, which may explain why it causes fewer immune reactions. Over months, free copper falls and copper is pulled from tissues.", "body": [["Liver", "Removing copper lets inflammation settle, and liver tests improve over months."], ["Brain", "Neurologic symptoms such as tremor and speech problems improve slowly, though they can worsen early in chelation as copper shifts."], ["Kidneys and urine", "Urinary copper rises within days as the copper complex is excreted."], ["Blood", "Trientine can also bind iron, leading to iron deficiency anemia."], ["Gut", "Nausea, abdominal pain and, rarely, colitis or duodenitis occur."], ["Skin", "Contact with capsule contents can cause dermatitis."]], "journey": "Trientine is taken by mouth on an empty stomach, apart from food, milk and other drugs, because these can bind it. Absorption is low and variable, with peak levels about half an hour to two hours after a dose. In the body it is acetylated into N1-acetyltriethylenetetramine and a diacetyl metabolite. The parent drug, metabolites and the chelated copper are excreted in the urine. The half-life is about two to four hours, so it is taken more than once daily. Clinical improvement takes months.", "sidefx": "Most side effects follow from its chelating power. Trientine also binds iron, so iron deficiency anemia can develop; iron supplements are separated by at least two hours. Early in therapy, moving copper out of the liver can raise free copper briefly and worsen neurologic symptoms. Too much chelation can cause copper deficiency with anemia, low neutrophils and nerve damage. Colitis, duodenitis and hypersensitivity reactions, including lupus-like reactions, are reported. Capsule contents can irritate the skin. There is no antidote; overtreatment is handled by stopping the drug.", "src": ["Syprine (trientine hydrochloride) FDA label", "Walshe JM. Lancet 1969", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "meperidine": {"origin": "Meperidine, called pethidine outside the US, was the first fully synthetic opioid. In 1939 chemist Otto Eisleb at IG Farben in Germany made it while looking for new anticholinergic drugs to relax smooth muscle. His colleague Otto Schaumann noticed that it relieved pain in animals, and it was marketed as Dolantin. It came to the US as Demerol and was widely used for decades, especially in labor and after surgery, partly because it was wrongly believed to cause less spasm of the bile ducts than morphine. Over time its toxic metabolite normeperidine and its dangerous interaction with MAO inhibitors made it far less favored. It is now on the Beers list of drugs avoided in older adults.", "timeline": [["1939", "Otto Eisleb and Otto Schaumann at IG Farben discover meperidine's pain-relieving effect."], ["1940s", "Meperidine is introduced in the US as Demerol."], ["1984", "The death of Libby Zion, linked to meperidine with phenelzine, draws attention to serotonin toxicity."], ["2000s", "Pain guidelines move away from meperidine because of normeperidine toxicity."]], "cell": "Meperidine is a phenylpiperidine agonist at the mu-opioid receptor, a Gi/o-coupled receptor. Activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase, opens G protein-coupled inward-rectifying potassium channels and closes presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels. Neurons hyperpolarize, and release of glutamate and substance P from pain fibers falls, which blocks pain signaling in the spinal cord and brain. It also has some kappa receptor activity. Unlike morphine, it blocks the serotonin transporter, has atropine-like antimuscarinic effects and acts as a local anesthetic by blocking sodium channels. The liver converts it to normeperidine, which is a weaker analgesic but excites the CNS and can trigger seizures.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Mu-receptor activation relieves pain and causes sedation and euphoria."], ["Breathing", "Mu activation in the brainstem blunts the response to carbon dioxide, slowing and stopping breathing in overdose."], ["Heart and vessels", "Its anticholinergic effect can speed the heart, unlike most opioids, and histamine release can lower blood pressure."], ["Muscles and nerves", "Normeperidine buildup causes tremor, twitching (myoclonus) and seizures, especially with kidney impairment."], ["Shivering", "It is effective at stopping postoperative shivering, an effect linked to kappa and alpha-2 actions."], ["Pupils", "Pupils may be small or, from its anticholinergic effect, normal or dilated."]], "journey": "Meperidine can be given IV, IM, under the skin or by mouth. By mouth, much is lost to first-pass liver metabolism. IV it acts within about five minutes and lasts two to four hours. The liver breaks it down by CYP3A4 and CYP2B6 N-demethylation to normeperidine, and by hydrolysis to meperidinic acid. Meperidine's half-life is three to five hours, but normeperidine lasts 15 to 30 hours and depends on the kidneys for removal. With repeated doses or kidney failure, normeperidine builds up long after pain relief has faded.", "sidefx": "Like other mu agonists it causes respiratory depression, sedation, nausea and constipation, and it carries boxed warnings for addiction, respiratory depression and use with benzodiazepines. Its distinctive dangers are different. Normeperidine excites the CNS and, as it builds up, causes tremor, myoclonus and seizures that naloxone does not reverse. Because meperidine blocks serotonin reuptake, taking it with an MAO inhibitor can cause severe serotonin syndrome with fever, rigidity and death. Naloxone reverses the opioid effects; benzodiazepines treat normeperidine seizures.", "fun": "In 1982 young people in California developed sudden permanent parkinsonism after injecting a homemade meperidine-like drug contaminated with MPTP. The discovery gave scientists a key tool for studying Parkinson disease.", "src": ["Demerol (meperidine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "butorphanol": {"origin": "Butorphanol came from a 1960s and 1970s push to find strong painkillers with less abuse and less risk of stopping breathing than morphine. Chemists at Bristol Laboratories in Syracuse, New York, built it on the morphinan skeleton, the same core found in levorphanol and dextromethorphan. It turned out to be a mixed agonist-antagonist: strong at kappa receptors and only partial at mu receptors. The FDA approved the injection as Stadol in 1978. A nasal spray followed in the early 1990s and became popular for migraine. Reports of misuse of the spray led to butorphanol being placed in Schedule IV in 1997. The Stadol brand is discontinued, but generics remain.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Bristol Laboratories develops butorphanol, a morphinan agonist-antagonist."], ["1978", "FDA approves Stadol injection."], ["1990s", "The nasal spray form is approved."], ["1997", "Butorphanol is placed in Schedule IV after reports of misuse."]], "cell": "Butorphanol is an agonist at kappa-opioid receptors and a partial agonist-antagonist at mu-opioid receptors. Both are Gi/o-coupled, so activation lowers cAMP, opens potassium channels and closes presynaptic calcium channels, reducing release of pain transmitters in the spinal cord and brain. Kappa activation provides much of the analgesia and sedation but can cause dysphoria and hallucinations. At mu receptors it produces a limited effect, so respiratory depression reaches a ceiling as the dose rises. In someone dependent on a full mu agonist, its partial action displaces the stronger drug and can trigger withdrawal. It also raises pulmonary artery pressure and the work of the heart.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Kappa and partial mu activation relieve moderate to severe pain and cause marked sedation."], ["Breathing", "Respiratory depression occurs but tends to plateau, a ceiling effect, though it can still be dangerous with other depressants."], ["Heart and lungs", "It raises pulmonary artery pressure, pulmonary vascular resistance and cardiac workload, which matters in heart attack or heart failure."], ["Mood", "Kappa activation can cause dysphoria, unpleasant dreams or hallucinations."], ["Opioid-dependent patients", "Partial mu activity can push out full agonists and set off sudden withdrawal."], ["Nose", "The spray can cause nasal congestion and irritation."]], "journey": "Butorphanol is given IV, IM or as a nasal spray. By mouth it would be largely destroyed by first-pass metabolism, which is why there is no pill. IV it acts within minutes and peaks around four to five minutes; IM it acts in 10 to 30 minutes. The nasal spray is absorbed through the nasal lining and works within about 15 minutes. The liver breaks it down by hydroxylation and N-dealkylation, and the kidneys excrete the metabolites. The half-life is about four and a half to five and a half hours, with pain relief lasting three to four hours.", "sidefx": "Sedation, dizziness and nausea are common. Kappa activity causes dysphoria and hallucinations, an effect less typical of pure mu agonists. Respiratory depression has a ceiling but can be severe with alcohol, benzodiazepines or other depressants. The rise in pulmonary pressure and cardiac workload is a concern in heart disease. In people dependent on opioids, the partial mu action can precipitate withdrawal. Misuse and dependence occur, especially with the spray. Naloxone reverses its effects.", "src": ["Butorphanol tartrate injection and nasal spray FDA labels", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "tapentadol": {"origin": "Tapentadol was developed by Grunenthal, the German company that earlier developed tramadol. Tramadol works partly as a weak opioid and partly by blocking reuptake of serotonin and norepinephrine, but much of its opioid effect depends on a CYP2D6 metabolite, and its serotonin action raises the risk of serotonin syndrome and seizures. Grunenthal chemists set out to put both pain-relieving actions into one active molecule: mu-opioid agonism plus norepinephrine reuptake inhibition, with little serotonin effect and no need for metabolic activation. Developed with Johnson & Johnson, it was approved by the FDA as Nucynta in 2008 for acute pain. An extended-release form followed in 2011 and was later approved for painful diabetic peripheral neuropathy.", "timeline": [["1977", "Grunenthal launches tramadol in Germany, the forerunner of tapentadol."], ["2008", "FDA approves Nucynta (tapentadol) for acute pain."], ["2011", "Nucynta ER is approved for chronic pain."], ["2012", "Nucynta ER gains approval for painful diabetic peripheral neuropathy."]], "cell": "Tapentadol acts in two complementary ways. First, it is an agonist at the mu-opioid receptor, with lower affinity than morphine. Mu activation through Gi/o lowers cAMP, opens potassium channels and closes presynaptic calcium channels, so pain fibers in the dorsal horn release less glutamate and substance P. Second, it blocks the norepinephrine transporter (NET). More norepinephrine in the spinal cord strengthens the descending inhibitory pathway from the brainstem, acting on alpha-2 adrenergic receptors that further damp pain transmission. The two actions add up, so meaningful pain relief comes with relatively less mu activation. Serotonin reuptake inhibition is minimal, and the parent drug is the active form, with no active metabolites.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Mu activation and boosted descending noradrenergic inhibition relieve acute pain and some nerve pain."], ["Breathing", "Mu activation in the brainstem can slow or stop breathing, especially with other depressants."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are common; constipation occurs but trials showed less than with oxycodone at similar pain relief."], ["Nervous system", "Norepinephrine effects can contribute to dizziness, and seizures have been reported."], ["Serotonin system", "Combined with serotonergic drugs, serotonin syndrome can occur despite weak direct serotonin action."], ["Hormones", "Long opioid use can cause adrenal insufficiency and lower sex hormones."]], "journey": "Tapentadol is taken by mouth as an immediate-release or extended-release tablet. Absorption is good, but first-pass metabolism leaves oral bioavailability at about 32 percent. Immediate-release tablets act in about 30 minutes and peak in a little over an hour. Only about 20 percent is protein bound. The liver metabolizes about 97 percent of it, mainly by glucuronidation, with minor CYP2C9, CYP2C19 and CYP2D6 pathways, and none of the metabolites are active. Nearly all leaves in the urine, mostly as conjugates. The half-life is about four hours.", "sidefx": "The mu-opioid action brings the class risks: sedation, nausea, constipation, respiratory depression, misuse and addiction, carried as boxed warnings. Combining it with benzodiazepines or alcohol multiplies breathing suppression. The norepinephrine reuptake action adds risks: seizures in susceptible people and serotonin syndrome when combined with SSRIs, SNRIs or MAO inhibitors. Crushing or chewing the extended-release tablet can release a fatal amount at once. Stopping suddenly after regular use causes withdrawal. Naloxone reverses the opioid effects.", "src": ["Nucynta and Nucynta ER (tapentadol) FDA labels", "Tzschentke TM et al. J Pharmacol Exp Ther 2007"]}, "oxymorphone": {"origin": "Oxymorphone is a semisynthetic opioid made by chemically modifying thebaine, an alkaloid from the opium poppy, Papaver somniferum. It is closely related to oxycodone; in the body, oxycodone is partly converted to oxymorphone by CYP2D6. Endo Laboratories introduced oxymorphone in the US in 1959 under the name Numorphan, used as an injection, suppository and later tablets. Endo returned with Opana tablets and Opana ER in 2006. In 2017 the FDA asked Endo to remove its reformulated Opana ER from the market because injection misuse of the tablets had caused an HIV and hepatitis C outbreak, the first time the FDA took that step with an opioid over abuse consequences. Generic immediate-release and extended-release tablets remain.", "timeline": [["1959", "Endo Laboratories introduces oxymorphone in the US as Numorphan."], ["2006", "FDA approves Opana and Opana ER tablets."], ["2015", "An HIV outbreak in Indiana is linked to injection of Opana ER tablets."], ["2017", "At FDA request, the reformulated Opana ER is withdrawn from the market."]], "cell": "Oxymorphone is a full agonist at the mu-opioid receptor. The receptor couples to Gi/o proteins. Activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase and lowers cAMP, opens G protein-gated inward-rectifying potassium channels so neurons hyperpolarize, and closes presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels so less glutamate and substance P are released from pain fibers. In the dorsal horn, periaqueductal gray and other brain regions, this blocks the transmission and perception of pain. Mu activation also inhibits GABA neurons in the reward pathway, increasing dopamine release, which explains euphoria and addiction. By injection it is roughly ten times as potent as morphine.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Strong mu activation relieves severe pain and causes sedation and euphoria."], ["Breathing", "Brainstem mu receptors lose sensitivity to carbon dioxide, so breathing slows and can stop in overdose."], ["Pupils", "Mu activation of the Edinger-Westphal nucleus produces pinpoint pupils."], ["Gut", "Reduced gut motility and secretion cause constipation, and nausea comes from the vomiting center."], ["Hormones", "Long-term use can cause adrenal insufficiency and low sex hormones."], ["Heart and vessels", "Low blood pressure and slow heart rate can occur, especially in overdose."]], "journey": "Oxymorphone is taken by mouth on an empty stomach, because food raises levels. Oral bioavailability is only about 10 percent because the liver removes most of it on the first pass. Immediate-release tablets act in 30 to 45 minutes and last four to six hours. Protein binding is low, about 10 to 12 percent. Unlike oxycodone, it relies little on CYP enzymes: the liver mainly glucuronidates it to oxymorphone-3-glucuronide and also forms 6-hydroxy-oxymorphone. Conjugates leave in the urine. The half-life is about seven to nine hours. Alcohol can cause dose dumping from extended-release tablets.", "sidefx": "Every major risk comes from mu-receptor activation. In the brainstem it suppresses breathing, which can be fatal, especially with alcohol or benzodiazepines, or in children who swallow even a small amount. Reward pathway activation drives misuse and addiction, and the boxed warnings cover these risks. Constipation, nausea, sedation and itching are common. Long-term use can cause adrenal insufficiency and opioid-induced hyperalgesia, and stopping suddenly causes withdrawal. Naloxone, a mu antagonist, reverses toxicity, though repeat doses may be needed with extended-release tablets.", "src": ["Oxymorphone hydrochloride FDA label", "FDA statement on Opana ER removal, 2017", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "scopolamine-transdermal": {"origin": "Scopolamine, also called hyoscine, is a natural alkaloid found in plants of the nightshade family, including henbane, Scopolia, Datura and Duboisia. These plants were used for centuries as medicines, poisons and intoxicants. The German chemist Albert Ladenburg isolated hyoscine in 1880. In the early twentieth century it was combined with morphine to produce twilight sleep during childbirth, and in World War II it was used against seasickness. Taken by mouth, its short action and side effects limited it. The company Alza developed a skin patch that releases a steady small amount over three days. Approved by the FDA in 1979 as Transderm Scop, it was the first transdermal drug patch in the United States.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Nightshade plants containing scopolamine are used as medicines and poisons."], ["1880", "Albert Ladenburg isolates hyoscine (scopolamine)."], ["1940s", "Scopolamine is used to prevent motion sickness in military forces."], ["1979", "FDA approves Transderm Scop, the first US transdermal patch."]], "cell": "Motion sickness arises when signals from the inner ear's vestibular system conflict with what the eyes see. Vestibular nerve signals travel to the vestibular nuclei and cerebellum, and on to the nucleus tractus solitarius and the vomiting center in the brainstem. Much of this relay uses acetylcholine acting on muscarinic receptors, especially M1 and other subtypes. Scopolamine is a competitive antagonist at all muscarinic receptors (M1 to M5). By blocking cholinergic transmission from the vestibular nuclei to higher centers and the reticular formation, it stops the mismatch signal from triggering nausea and vomiting. It crosses the blood-brain barrier easily, which makes it effective but also explains its sedation and confusion. Peripheral muscarinic blockade causes dry mouth and dilated pupils.", "body": [["Inner ear and brainstem", "Blocking cholinergic vestibular signals prevents motion sickness and postoperative nausea and vomiting."], ["Mouth and skin", "Reduced saliva and sweat cause dry mouth and can impair heat loss, raising the risk of overheating."], ["Eyes", "Pupils dilate and focusing weakens, causing blurred near vision; touching the eye after handling the patch can dilate one pupil."], ["Brain", "Drowsiness is common, and older adults can become confused, agitated or have hallucinations."], ["Bladder", "Detrusor relaxation can cause urinary retention, especially with an enlarged prostate."], ["Heart", "Blocking vagal tone can raise the heart rate."]], "journey": "The patch sits on hairless skin behind the ear, where the skin is thin and well supplied with blood. A built-in rate-controlling membrane releases scopolamine steadily over three days, with a small priming dose in the adhesive. Antiemetic effect begins in about four hours, and steady blood levels arrive in about 24 hours. Scopolamine spreads into the brain easily. The liver metabolizes it, partly through CYP3A4, and a small fraction leaves unchanged in the urine. After the patch is removed, levels fall with a half-life of about nine and a half hours.", "sidefx": "Every side effect comes from blocking acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors. Dry mouth, blurred vision, dilated pupils and drowsiness are common. In the brain, especially in older adults and children, it can cause confusion, agitation and hallucinations. A dilated pupil can trigger acute angle-closure glaucoma in a narrow-angle eye. Reduced sweating raises the risk of heat illness. Urinary retention occurs with outlet obstruction. Dizziness, nausea and headache can follow removal. The patch has metal and can burn the skin during MRI. Severe anticholinergic toxicity can be reversed by physostigmine in selected cases.", "fun": "The patch was a landmark in drug delivery: it showed that a tiny, steady dose through the skin could replace pills that caused peaks and troughs.", "src": ["Transderm Scop (scopolamine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "norethindrone-acetate": {"origin": "Norethindrone was first synthesized in 1951 by Luis Miramontes, working with Carl Djerassi and George Rosenkranz at the company Syntex in Mexico City. Syntex built its steroid chemistry on diosgenin from Mexican wild yams. Removing one carbon (the 19-methyl group) from a testosterone-like molecule and adding an ethinyl group produced a powerful progestin that worked by mouth, unlike natural progesterone. Norethindrone became one of the hormones in the first generation of birth control pills. Its acetate ester, norethindrone acetate, is sold as Aygestin and generics. At the strengths used, it is not a contraceptive; it treats missing periods, abnormal uterine bleeding and endometriosis.", "timeline": [["1940s", "Russell Marker develops a way to make steroids from Mexican yam diosgenin."], ["1951", "Miramontes, Djerassi and Rosenkranz at Syntex synthesize norethindrone."], ["1960s", "Norethindrone becomes a component of early oral contraceptives."]], "cell": "Norethindrone acetate is rapidly converted to norethindrone, which binds the intracellular progesterone receptor. The hormone-receptor complex dimerizes, moves to the nucleus and binds progesterone response elements on DNA, changing gene transcription. In an endometrium already primed by estrogen, this halts proliferation and transforms the lining into a stable secretory state with glandular secretions and stromal changes. When the drug is stopped, the falling progestin signal causes organized shedding, a withdrawal bleed. With continuous use it suppresses pituitary LH and FSH, lowering ovarian estrogen, and causes decidualization and then atrophy of endometrial tissue, including endometriosis implants outside the uterus. As a 19-nortestosterone derivative it also has weak androgen receptor activity.", "body": [["Uterus", "The lining becomes stable and secretory, and stopping the drug produces a predictable withdrawal bleed, usually within a week."], ["Endometriosis implants", "Continuous use shrinks implants and reduces pelvic pain by causing decidualization and atrophy."], ["Brain and pituitary", "Suppressed LH and FSH reduce ovarian hormone output during continuous use."], ["Skin and hair", "Weak androgenic activity can cause acne or oily skin."], ["Breasts and fluid", "Breast tenderness, bloating and fluid retention are common."], ["Mood", "Some people notice mood changes or depression."]], "journey": "Norethindrone acetate is taken by mouth and absorbed well. It is quickly deacetylated to norethindrone, which peaks in about two hours. In the blood it is about 97 percent bound to albumin and sex hormone-binding globulin. The liver reduces and conjugates it into sulfates and glucuronides. Metabolites leave in the urine and stool. The half-life is about eight to nine hours, which suits once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Most effects follow from progesterone receptor activity. Irregular bleeding and spotting occur as the lining adjusts. Breast tenderness, bloating and mood changes reflect hormone action in those tissues. Weak androgenic action can cause acne. Progestins can slightly reduce glucose tolerance. Like other sex steroids, it carries a warning for blood clots, including retinal vessel clots that cause sudden vision loss. Because it can stop bleeding, organic causes of abnormal bleeding are excluded first. There is no antidote; overdose usually causes only nausea and bleeding.", "src": ["Aygestin (norethindrone acetate) FDA label", "Djerassi C. Steroid research at Syntex, Steroids 1992"]}, "copper-iud": {"origin": "People have tried to prevent pregnancy with objects in the uterus for a long time, but modern IUDs date to the 1920s, when Ernst Grafenberg in Berlin used silver and silk rings. In the late 1960s the Chilean physician Jaime Zipper discovered that copper in the uterus has a strong contraceptive effect. Howard Tatum, working with Zipper, designed the T shape to fit the uterine cavity, and adding copper wire to the T greatly improved effectiveness. The Population Council developed the TCu380A. The FDA approved it in 1984, and it was marketed in the US as ParaGard from 1988. It remains the only non-hormonal IUD in the US and lasts up to ten years.", "timeline": [["1920s", "Ernst Grafenberg develops silver and silk ring IUDs in Germany."], ["1960s", "Jaime Zipper discovers copper's contraceptive effect and Howard Tatum designs the T shape."], ["1984", "FDA approves the Copper T 380A."], ["1988", "The device is marketed in the US as ParaGard."]], "cell": "The copper IUD works without hormones. The copper wire slowly releases copper ions into the uterine and tubal fluid. Copper is toxic to sperm: it impairs their motility, viability and capacitation (the final changes needed to fertilize an egg). The device also creates a sterile foreign-body inflammatory reaction in the endometrium. White blood cells, prostaglandins and inflammatory cytokines in the uterine fluid add to sperm destruction. As a result, very few sperm reach the fallopian tubes, and fertilization usually does not happen. Changes in the endometrium may also make implantation unlikely as a secondary effect. It does not stop ovulation, so natural cycles continue.", "body": [["Uterus", "The local inflammatory and copper environment prevents fertilization for up to ten years with typical failure under 1 percent per year."], ["Menstruation", "Increased prostaglandins and local inflammation make periods heavier, longer and more painful, especially in the first months."], ["Blood", "Heavier bleeding can lead to iron deficiency anemia."], ["Ovaries", "Ovulation and natural hormone cycles continue because no hormones are released."], ["Emergency contraception", "Placed soon after unprotected sex, it is highly effective at preventing pregnancy."]], "journey": "The copper IUD is placed in the uterus by a trained clinician through the cervix using a sterile inserter. It begins working immediately. Copper ions are released locally into the uterine fluid at a slow rate over years. The amount that reaches the bloodstream does not meaningfully change normal copper levels. Nothing is metabolized. When the device is removed by pulling gently on its threads, fertility returns quickly.", "sidefx": "The most common problems, heavier and more painful periods, come from the same inflammation and prostaglandin release that make the device work. Heavier bleeding can cause anemia. The device can be expelled, especially soon after childbirth, or embed in or perforate the uterine wall during insertion. Pelvic infection is mostly a risk in the first 20 days, from bacteria carried in at insertion. If pregnancy happens, a higher share is ectopic, and pregnancy with the device in place raises risks of miscarriage, septic abortion and preterm birth. There is no antidote; the remedy is removal.", "src": ["Paragard (intrauterine copper contraceptive) FDA label", "Speroff's Clinical Gynecologic Endocrinology and Infertility"]}, "levonorgestrel-iud": {"origin": "The levonorgestrel IUD was developed in Finland. In the 1970s and 1980s the physician Tapani Luukkainen worked with the Finnish company Leiras to combine a T-shaped plastic frame with a small reservoir that slowly releases the progestin levonorgestrel. The idea was to put a tiny amount of hormone right where it was needed. The device, Mirena, was approved in Finland in 1990 and by the FDA in 2000. Doctors soon found that it also greatly reduced heavy menstrual bleeding, which became a second approved use. Smaller, lower-dose versions followed: Skyla in 2013 and Kyleena in 2016, and Liletta, developed with a nonprofit to lower cost, in 2015.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Tapani Luukkainen and Leiras in Finland begin developing a hormone-releasing IUD."], ["1990", "Mirena is first approved in Finland."], ["2000", "FDA approves Mirena."], ["2009", "Mirena is approved in the US for heavy menstrual bleeding."], ["2013", "Skyla, a smaller lower-dose device, is approved."]], "cell": "Levonorgestrel is a 19-nortestosterone-derived progestin. Released inside the uterus, it reaches high local concentrations while blood levels stay low. It binds progesterone receptors in the endometrium and cervix, which act as ligand-activated transcription factors. In the cervix, it makes mucus thick and scant, forming a barrier that sperm cannot easily cross. In the uterus and tubes, it impairs sperm motility and function. In the endometrium, it suppresses estrogen-driven growth and causes decidualization and then thinning (atrophy) of the lining, which makes implantation unlikely and greatly reduces menstrual bleeding. Ovulation is suppressed only in some cycles, because blood levels are too low to reliably block the LH surge.", "body": [["Cervix", "Thick cervical mucus blocks sperm from entering the uterus."], ["Uterine lining", "The lining thins, so periods become lighter, and many users eventually stop bleeding altogether."], ["Pelvic pain", "Lighter periods and less prostaglandin release reduce menstrual cramps."], ["Ovaries", "Most cycles still ovulate; some users develop harmless functional ovarian cysts."], ["Skin and breasts", "Low systemic progestin can cause acne, breast tenderness or headache in some users."]], "journey": "The device is placed in the uterus by a trained clinician. Its silicone reservoir releases levonorgestrel at a slowly falling rate over several years, up to eight years for the larger devices. Most of the hormone acts locally in the endometrium. A small amount is absorbed into the blood, where levels peak within weeks of insertion and then settle at low levels. In the blood it binds sex hormone-binding globulin and albumin. The liver metabolizes absorbed drug through CYP3A4, and metabolites leave in urine and stool.", "sidefx": "Irregular bleeding and spotting in the first months happen as the lining adjusts to high local progestin; later, many people stop bleeding because the lining is thin. Functional ovarian cysts form because ovulation often continues. Low systemic levels can cause acne, headache and breast tenderness. Insertion risks include expulsion, embedment, perforation and infection, including rare severe sepsis. If pregnancy occurs, a higher share is ectopic. There is no antidote; removal ends the effect.", "src": ["Mirena (levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine system) FDA label", "Luukkainen T. The levonorgestrel intrauterine system, Ann Med 1993"]}, "prasterone": {"origin": "Prasterone is the medical name for dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA), a steroid made mainly by the adrenal glands. It was first isolated in 1934 by Adolf Butenandt and Hans Dannenbaum from urine. Blood DHEA levels fall steadily with age. The Quebec endocrinologist Fernand Labrie developed the idea of intracrinology: many tissues take in DHEA and make their own estrogens and androgens inside the cell, using them locally with little spillover into the blood. His company, EndoCeutics, applied this to the vagina, where menopause causes thinning and dryness. The FDA approved prasterone vaginal inserts as Intrarosa in 2016 for painful intercourse due to menopause. Oral DHEA sold as a supplement is a different, unregulated product.", "timeline": [["1934", "DHEA is isolated from urine by Adolf Butenandt's group."], ["1980s", "Fernand Labrie develops the concept of intracrinology."], ["2016", "FDA approves Intrarosa (prasterone) vaginal inserts."]], "cell": "Prasterone is an inactive precursor. In vaginal epithelial and stromal cells it is converted by local enzymes: 3-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase, 17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase, 5-alpha reductase and aromatase. These produce small amounts of androgens (testosterone and DHT) and estrogens (estradiol) inside the cell. The new hormones bind local estrogen and androgen receptors, which act as transcription factors. Estrogen receptor signaling thickens the epithelium, increases superficial cells and glycogen, and lowers vaginal pH. Androgen receptor signaling may support collagen, muscle and nerve tissue in the vaginal wall. The active hormones are then inactivated in the same cells, so serum estradiol barely changes.", "body": [["Vaginal lining", "More superficial cells, more glycogen and a lower pH make the lining thicker and more elastic, so sex becomes less painful over weeks."], ["Vaginal wall", "Local androgen action may support the deeper tissue layers, including collagen and smooth muscle."], ["Vaginal secretions", "Lubrication improves, and the melted insert base causes expected discharge."], ["Whole body", "Serum sex hormones stay within normal postmenopausal ranges, so whole-body hormone effects are minimal."], ["Cervix", "Abnormal Pap results, mostly low-grade changes, were reported in trials."]], "journey": "Prasterone is placed in the vagina as a small insert at bedtime. The insert melts, and DHEA enters the cells of the vaginal lining. There it is converted into active hormones and then inactivated within the same cells, a process called intracrinology. Serum DHEA rises within hours of each dose but stays near the normal postmenopausal range, and estradiol barely changes. Metabolites that reach the blood bind albumin and SHBG and leave in the urine. Benefits build over weeks.", "sidefx": "Side effects are mostly local. The melted insert base causes vaginal discharge. Abnormal Pap smears were reported in trials, mostly low-grade changes. Because a small amount of estrogen is made locally, abnormal genital bleeding is evaluated. It has not been studied in women with a history of breast cancer. Unlike vaginal estrogen products, its label has no boxed warning, because serum hormone levels stay low. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Intrarosa (prasterone) FDA label", "Labrie F et al. Menopause 2016"]}, "estradiol-vaginal": {"origin": "Estradiol, the main estrogen of the reproductive years, was isolated in 1935 by Edward Doisy and colleagues at Saint Louis University, who processed huge amounts of sow ovaries to collect a tiny amount. Doctors soon learned that the vaginal lining thins and dries without estrogen after menopause, and that estrogen applied in the vagina can restore it with much less reaching the blood than pills. Estrogen vaginal creams such as Estrace cream became widely used. In the 1990s low-dose options arrived, including a slow-release ring (Estring) and small vaginal tablets (Vagifem). Lower-strength tablets and the Imvexxy softgel insert were approved later, aiming to treat the vagina while keeping blood levels near postmenopausal ranges.", "timeline": [["1935", "Edward Doisy's team isolates estradiol from sow ovaries."], ["1990s", "Low-dose vaginal estradiol ring and tablets are approved in the US."], ["2018", "FDA approves Imvexxy softgel vaginal inserts."]], "cell": "17-beta-estradiol diffuses into cells of the vaginal, vulvar and urethral tissue and binds nuclear estrogen receptors alpha and beta. The hormone-receptor complex dimerizes and binds estrogen response elements on DNA, switching on genes for cell growth and maturation. The epithelium thickens from a few cell layers back toward many layers, superficial cells return and glycogen increases. Resident lactobacilli ferment that glycogen into lactic acid, so vaginal pH falls, which discourages harmful bacteria and reduces infections. Estrogen also improves blood flow, elasticity and lubrication. In the urethra and bladder base, similar changes can reduce urgency and recurrent urinary infections. Low vaginal doses give only small rises in blood estradiol.", "body": [["Vaginal lining", "A thicker, moister and more elastic lining relieves dryness, burning and painful sex over days to weeks."], ["Vaginal flora", "More glycogen feeds lactobacilli, lowering pH and restoring a protective environment."], ["Urinary tract", "Restored urethral and bladder tissue can ease urgency and lower recurrent urinary infections."], ["Uterus", "Small amounts reaching the uterus could stimulate the lining, so unexpected bleeding is evaluated."], ["Breasts", "Breast tenderness is uncommon at low doses but can occur."]], "journey": "Vaginal estradiol is placed as a tablet insert, softgel, cream or ring. The vaginal lining absorbs estradiol well, and absorption is highest when the lining is thin, then falls as it heals. With low-dose products, blood estradiol peaks within hours but stays near the postmenopausal range. Absorbed estradiol avoids first-pass liver metabolism. The liver later converts it through CYP3A4 to estrone and estriol, and to sulfate and glucuronide conjugates that recycle through the gut. Conjugates leave in the urine. The ring releases a steady amount for 90 days.", "sidefx": "Local effects such as discharge and itching are common. The boxed warning shared by all estrogens, covering endometrial cancer, stroke, blood clots, breast cancer and probable dementia, is based on studies of higher-dose systemic estrogen. With low vaginal doses blood levels stay low, so these risks are thought to be much smaller, but the class labeling still applies. Unopposed estrogen reaching the uterus can thicken the lining, which is why persistent or unexpected bleeding is evaluated. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Vagifem (estradiol vaginal inserts) FDA label", "Estring (estradiol vaginal ring) FDA label", "Doisy EA et al. J Biol Chem 1935"]}, "clotrimazole-vaginal": {"origin": "Clotrimazole was discovered at Bayer in Germany in the late 1960s and was one of the first imidazole antifungals, a new class that worked against a broad range of fungi. It was too toxic to the liver to use widely as a body-wide drug, but it worked very well on skin and mucous membranes where little is absorbed. Vaginal clotrimazole became a standard treatment for yeast infections. In 1990 the FDA allowed clotrimazole vaginal cream (Gyne-Lotrimin) to switch from prescription to over the counter, making it one of the first vaginal antifungals people could buy without a prescription for a recognized recurrence of a yeast infection.", "timeline": [["1969", "Bayer researchers report clotrimazole as a broad-spectrum imidazole antifungal."], ["1970s", "Clotrimazole topical and vaginal products enter use."], ["1990", "FDA approves clotrimazole vaginal cream for over-the-counter sale."]], "cell": "Clotrimazole is an imidazole that inhibits fungal lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51), a cytochrome P450 enzyme. Its imidazole nitrogen binds the heme iron of the enzyme, blocking the step that removes a methyl group from lanosterol on the way to ergosterol. Ergosterol is the main sterol in the fungal cell membrane, playing the role cholesterol plays in human cells. Without enough ergosterol, and with toxic 14-methylated sterols building up, the Candida membrane becomes leaky and its membrane enzymes stop working properly. Growth stops (fungistatic), and at the high concentrations reached locally in the vagina it can kill the yeast. Itching, burning and discharge then resolve.", "body": [["Vagina", "Candida stops growing and is cleared, so discharge, itching and burning improve over one to three days."], ["Vulva", "External cream relieves itching and irritation of the outer skin."], ["Local tissues", "Mild burning or irritation can occur where the cream is applied."], ["Latex products", "The oily base can weaken latex condoms and diaphragms during treatment."], ["Whole body", "Very little is absorbed, so body-wide side effects and drug interactions are minimal."]], "journey": "Clotrimazole cream is inserted into the vagina with an applicator, usually at bedtime, for three or seven days, and can also be applied to the vulva. It stays mainly in the vaginal lining and secretions, where it reaches high concentrations. Only a very small amount is absorbed into the blood. What little is absorbed is metabolized by the liver and leaves in the stool. Because blood levels are so low, it does not cause the drug interactions seen with oral azoles.", "sidefx": "Because it acts locally with minimal absorption, side effects are mild and mostly local: burning, itching and irritation, which can overlap with the infection's own symptoms. Some people have abdominal cramping. Allergic reactions are rare. Its oily base can weaken latex condoms and diaphragms. If symptoms do not improve, another diagnosis such as bacterial vaginosis, trichomoniasis or a sexually transmitted infection may be the cause, which an azole will not treat. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Clotrimazole vaginal cream OTC Drug Facts label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "terconazole": {"origin": "Terconazole was developed by Janssen Pharmaceutica in Belgium, the company founded by Paul Janssen, which also discovered miconazole and ketoconazole. It was one of the first triazole antifungals. Triazoles bind more selectively to the fungal enzyme than many older imidazoles and often reach a broader range of Candida species. Terconazole was designed for vaginal use only. It was approved in the US in the late 1980s under the brand Terazol as a vaginal cream and suppositories. The brand has since been discontinued, but generic terconazole cream and suppositories remain available by prescription.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Janssen Pharmaceutica develops terconazole, an early triazole, and Terazol vaginal products are approved in the US."]], "cell": "Terconazole is a triazole that inhibits fungal lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51). A nitrogen in its triazole ring binds the heme iron in the enzyme's active site, blocking the removal of the 14-methyl group from lanosterol. This shuts down ergosterol synthesis. Ergosterol keeps the fungal membrane fluid and supports membrane enzymes and transporters. Its loss, plus the buildup of toxic 14-methylated sterols, makes the Candida membrane leaky and dysfunctional, so the yeast stops growing. Its triazole structure gives broader activity against some non-albicans species, such as Candida glabrata and Candida krusei, than some imidazoles. Clearance of yeast relieves itching, burning and discharge.", "body": [["Vagina", "Yeast growth stops and the infection clears over several days, relieving discharge, itching and burning."], ["Local tissues", "Vulvovaginal burning and itching can occur from the drug itself."], ["Whole body", "A small absorbed fraction can cause headache, and rarely fever, chills or flu-like symptoms."], ["Latex products", "The suppository base can weaken latex condoms and diaphragms."], ["Menstrual cycle", "Treatment continues during menses, and some users report painful periods during treatment."]], "journey": "Terconazole is placed in the vagina as a cream with an applicator or as a suppository, usually at bedtime, for three or seven days. Most of the drug stays in the vaginal tissue and secretions. A small fraction is absorbed into the blood, where it is about 95 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes the absorbed drug, and the products leave in urine and stool. The half-life of the absorbed fraction is about seven hours.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are local, such as burning and itching, because the drug works on the vaginal lining. Headache is the most common body-wide complaint. A small number of users have flu-like reactions with fever and chills, likely a hypersensitivity response to the absorbed fraction, and anaphylaxis is rare. Painful periods and abdominal pain are reported. The suppository base can weaken latex barrier methods. Persistent symptoms can mean a different diagnosis. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Terconazole vaginal cream and suppository FDA labels", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "boric-acid-vaginal": {"origin": "Boric acid is a simple inorganic acid of boron that occurs naturally in minerals such as borax and in volcanic hot springs. It was used as a mild antiseptic in the nineteenth century, including in wound dressings and eyewashes. Over time its use as a general antiseptic faded once its toxicity when swallowed or absorbed through broken skin, especially in infants, became clear. Since the 1970s and 1980s, clinicians have used boric acid packed into gelatin capsules and placed in the vagina for yeast infections that do not respond to azoles, particularly those caused by Candida glabrata. No boric acid vaginal product is FDA approved. Products are sold over the counter or compounded and are not FDA reviewed for safety, purity or dose, though major US guidelines mention it for resistant infections.", "timeline": [["19th century", "Boric acid is used as a mild antiseptic in wound care and eyewashes."], ["1970s", "Reports describe vaginal boric acid capsules for yeast infections."], ["2000s", "Guidelines describe vaginal boric acid for azole-resistant Candida glabrata infections."]], "cell": "Boric acid has no single receptor or enzyme target, and its mechanism is not fully defined. The proposed actions come mainly from laboratory studies. It is fungistatic and, at high local concentrations, fungicidal. It appears to impair fungal oxidative metabolism and cell wall integrity. It blocks Candida from switching into the hyphal form, the invasive shape that penetrates tissue, and it interferes with biofilm formation, which helps yeast resist treatment. Because it does not act on the ergosterol pathway, it keeps activity against Candida glabrata and other species that resist azoles. It also has weak antibacterial effects, which is why it is sometimes used in regimens for recurrent bacterial vaginosis. Clinical evidence comes from case series and small studies rather than large trials.", "body": [["Vagina", "Local antifungal and antiseptic action clears yeast, including azole-resistant species, over days."], ["Vaginal lining", "Burning, watery discharge and irritation are common, and erosions can occur with longer use."], ["Sexual partners", "Partners can have irritation if intercourse occurs during use."], ["Gut and whole body (if swallowed)", "Swallowed boric acid is a systemic poison causing vomiting, diarrhea, a red peeling rash, seizures and kidney injury."], ["Kidneys", "Absorbed boric acid is excreted unchanged by the kidneys, which are injured in serious poisoning."]], "journey": "Boric acid capsules or suppositories are placed in the vagina, usually at bedtime. The capsule dissolves and the powder acts on the vaginal surface. Absorption through intact vaginal lining is thought to be low, and blood levels from vaginal use are not well established. Boric acid that does reach the blood is not metabolized. It spreads through body water and leaves unchanged in the urine, with a half-life of roughly a day reported from poisoning cases. Absorption is much higher if it is swallowed or applied to broken skin.", "sidefx": "Local burning and watery discharge come from its acidic, irritating action on the vaginal lining. The main danger is ingestion. Capsules look like oral supplements and are a known source of accidental poisoning, especially in young children. Swallowed boric acid causes vomiting and diarrhea that can look blue-green, then a bright red rash that peels (the boiled lobster appearance), lethargy, seizures, kidney failure and sometimes death. Because products are not FDA reviewed, strength and purity can vary. There is no antidote; care is supportive, with hemodialysis in severe poisoning.", "src": ["CDC Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines, 2021", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Iavazzo C et al. J Womens Health 2011 (review of boric acid for vaginal infections)"]}, "metronidazole-vaginal": {"origin": "Metronidazole traces back to azomycin, a nitroimidazole antibiotic found in a Streptomyces soil bacterium in Japan in the 1950s. Chemists at Rhone-Poulenc in France made related compounds while looking for a drug against Trichomonas vaginalis and synthesized metronidazole in the late 1950s. It was launched as Flagyl around 1960 for trichomoniasis. In 1962 a dentist noticed by chance that a patient's gum infection cleared while she was taking it, which led to the discovery of its strong activity against anaerobic bacteria. That activity made it a mainstay for bacterial vaginosis. A vaginal gel, MetroGel-Vaginal, was approved in the 1990s to treat the infection with far less drug in the blood, and a higher-strength single-dose gel, Nuvessa, followed.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Azomycin, a nitroimidazole, is isolated from a Streptomyces species in Japan."], ["1960", "Rhone-Poulenc introduces metronidazole (Flagyl) for trichomoniasis."], ["1962", "A chance observation reveals metronidazole's activity against anaerobic bacteria."], ["1990s", "Metronidazole vaginal gel is approved for bacterial vaginosis."]], "cell": "Metronidazole is a prodrug. It enters microbial cells by passive diffusion. In anaerobic bacteria and protozoa, low-redox-potential electron carriers such as ferredoxin, fed by pyruvate-ferredoxin oxidoreductase, transfer electrons to its nitro group. This reduction creates short-lived, highly reactive nitro radical intermediates. These bind to and break DNA strands and destabilize the helix, causing cell death. Reduction also keeps the concentration of unchanged drug inside the cell low, which pulls more drug in. Aerobic human cells lack these low-potential electron carriers, so they do not activate it much. In bacterial vaginosis, killing anaerobes such as Gardnerella-associated and other anaerobic species lets lactobacilli recover.", "body": [["Vagina", "Killing the overgrown anaerobes reduces the fishy odor and thin gray discharge of bacterial vaginosis within days."], ["Vaginal flora", "Removing anaerobes can let yeast overgrow, so vaginal candidiasis may follow treatment."], ["Taste and gut", "A small absorbed amount can cause a metallic taste, nausea or abdominal discomfort."], ["Nervous system", "Rarely, nitroimidazoles cause numbness or seizures, though this is uncommon with low vaginal absorption."], ["Alcohol interaction", "Alcohol during and shortly after treatment can cause a disulfiram-like reaction with flushing and vomiting."]], "journey": "The gel is placed in the vagina with an applicator, usually at bedtime. Most of the drug stays in the vagina, where it reaches high local levels. A small amount is absorbed, giving blood levels far below those after oral metronidazole, peaking about six to eight hours after a dose. Absorbed drug is metabolized by the liver through oxidation, including to an active hydroxy metabolite, and through glucuronidation. The kidneys excrete most of it, with some in stool. The half-life is about eight hours.", "sidefx": "Most side effects follow from shifting the vaginal flora: killing anaerobes can let Candida overgrow, causing a yeast infection. Discharge, pelvic discomfort, headache, nausea and a metallic taste can come from the small absorbed fraction. Metronidazole interferes with alcohol metabolism, so drinking can cause a disulfiram-like reaction. Seizures and peripheral neuropathy, which are linked to nitro radicals and higher systemic exposure, are rare with the gel. Severe hypersensitivity is rare. There is no antidote.", "src": ["MetroGel-Vaginal (metronidazole vaginal gel) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Shinn DLS. Lancet 1962"]}, "clindamycin-vaginal": {"origin": "Clindamycin comes from lincomycin, an antibiotic made by the soil bacterium Streptomyces lincolnensis, first found in a soil sample from near Lincoln, Nebraska, and developed by Upjohn in the early 1960s. In 1966 Upjohn chemist Barney Magerlein replaced one hydroxyl group of lincomycin with a chlorine atom, creating clindamycin, which was absorbed better and was more potent, especially against anaerobes. It was approved by the FDA in 1970. Its strong anaerobic activity made it useful for bacterial vaginosis, and vaginal forms were developed to treat the infection locally with low blood levels. Cleocin vaginal cream and ovules were approved in the 1990s, and a single-dose cream, Clindesse, came later.", "timeline": [["1962", "Upjohn scientists report lincomycin from Streptomyces lincolnensis."], ["1966", "Barney Magerlein synthesizes clindamycin from lincomycin."], ["1970", "FDA approves clindamycin."], ["1990s", "Clindamycin vaginal cream and ovules are approved for bacterial vaginosis."]], "cell": "Clindamycin phosphate is an inactive ester. In the vagina, phosphatases hydrolyze it to active clindamycin. Clindamycin enters bacteria and binds the 23S rRNA of the 50S ribosomal subunit, near the peptidyl transferase center. This blocks peptide bond formation and translocation, so the growing protein chain cannot extend and the bacteria stop making proteins. The effect is mainly bacteriostatic. In bacterial vaginosis, anaerobes such as Gardnerella, Prevotella, Mobiluncus and others overgrow and replace lactobacilli. Clindamycin suppresses these organisms, which lets the normal flora gradually recover. Because it binds near the same site as macrolides, resistance can develop through methylation of the 23S rRNA (erm genes).", "body": [["Vagina", "Suppressing the anaerobic overgrowth clears the discharge and odor of bacterial vaginosis within days."], ["Vaginal flora", "Disturbing the normal flora can allow yeast to overgrow, causing vulvovaginal candidiasis."], ["Colon", "Even with vaginal use, absorbed clindamycin can disrupt gut flora and rarely allow C. difficile colitis."], ["Latex products", "Mineral oil in the cream weakens latex condoms and diaphragms for about 72 hours after the last dose."], ["Pregnancy", "The cream can be used as a seven-day course in pregnancy, with low systemic exposure."]], "journey": "Clindamycin cream or ovules are placed in the vagina, usually at bedtime. Phosphatases in the vagina release the active drug. About 4 percent of the cream dose is absorbed, with higher and more variable absorption from ovules, and blood levels peak many hours after a dose. Absorbed drug is highly protein bound and metabolized by liver CYP3A4. Metabolites leave in urine and stool. The systemic half-life is about one and a half to three hours.", "sidefx": "The main local side effect is yeast overgrowth, because clearing bacteria disturbs the vaginal flora. Irritation and discharge are common. The small absorbed amount can disrupt gut bacteria, and C. difficile diarrhea and colitis have been reported even with vaginal use. The cream's mineral oil base weakens latex condoms and diaphragms, so they are unreliable for 72 hours after treatment. Anaphylaxis is rare. There is no antidote; C. difficile colitis is treated with specific antibiotics.", "src": ["Cleocin Vaginal Cream and Ovules (clindamycin phosphate) FDA labels", "Magerlein BJ et al. J Med Chem 1967", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "influenza-vaccine": {"origin": "The influenza virus was first isolated from humans in 1933 by Wilson Smith, Christopher Andrewes and Patrick Laidlaw in London. Scientists then learned to grow it in fertilized chicken eggs. In the late 1930s and 1940s Thomas Francis Jr. and his young colleague Jonas Salk at the University of Michigan developed an inactivated vaccine, first tested in the US Army during World War II. It was licensed for civilians in 1945. Because the virus changes its surface proteins every year (antigenic drift), the vaccine is updated each season based on global surveillance. Split-virus vaccines, which break the virus apart with detergents, reduced side effects. Newer types include cell-grown vaccine (2012), recombinant hemagglutinin vaccine made without eggs or influenza virus (2013), high-dose and adjuvanted versions for older adults.", "timeline": [["1933", "Smith, Andrewes and Laidlaw isolate human influenza virus in London."], ["1945", "Inactivated influenza vaccine developed by Thomas Francis Jr. and Jonas Salk is licensed in the US."], ["1960s", "Split-virus vaccines reduce reactions."], ["2012", "FDA approves Flucelvax, the first cell culture-based flu vaccine in the US."], ["2013", "FDA approves Flublok, a recombinant hemagglutinin vaccine."]], "cell": "The vaccine contains killed, split influenza virus or purified antigens, mainly hemagglutinin (HA), with some neuraminidase, from the A (H1N1), A (H3N2) and B strains chosen for the season. No live virus is present. After injection into muscle, dendritic cells and macrophages take up the antigens and carry them to lymph nodes, where they present HA peptides on MHC class II to helper T cells. B cells whose receptors recognize HA are activated with T cell help, enter germinal centers, switch to IgG and mature into plasma cells and memory B cells. Antibodies against the HA head block the virus from binding sialic acid receptors on respiratory epithelial cells, so infection is prevented or reduced. Protection is strain specific and wanes, which is why vaccination is yearly.", "body": [["Immune system", "Antibodies against the season's hemagglutinins reach protective levels about two weeks after the shot."], ["Lungs and airways", "Fewer infections and milder disease mean fewer cases of pneumonia, hospitalization and death."], ["Injection site", "Local inflammation from antigen and immune cells causes soreness, redness and swelling."], ["Whole body", "Cytokines released during the immune response can cause aches, headache, tiredness and low fever for a day or two."], ["Nervous system", "Guillain-Barre syndrome is a rare possible risk, about one or two extra cases per million doses in some seasons."]], "journey": "The vaccine is injected into a muscle, the deltoid in adults or the thigh in infants. Antigen-presenting cells pick it up at the site and carry it to nearby lymph nodes, where the immune response is built. Antibody levels rise over about two weeks and peak at two to four weeks. The antigens themselves are broken down by immune cells like any other protein. Protection fades over about six months, and next season's strains are often different, so a new shot is given each year.", "sidefx": "Most reactions are signs of the immune system responding: soreness and swelling at the site from local inflammation, and aches, headache and low fever from circulating cytokines. Fainting can occur after any injection. Febrile seizures in young children are rare. Guillain-Barre syndrome, an autoimmune nerve disorder, is a rare possible risk. Anaphylaxis is very rare, so treatment is available at vaccination sites. Egg allergy alone no longer calls for extra precautions per ACIP. Because no live virus is present, the vaccine cannot cause influenza.", "fun": "Each year the World Health Organization picks the strains for the next season months in advance, because egg-based production takes about six months.", "src": ["Fluzone and Flublok FDA labels", "CDC ACIP influenza vaccine recommendations", "Plotkin's Vaccines"]}, "covid-19-vaccine": {"origin": "mRNA vaccines rest on decades of basic research. In 2005 Katalin Kariko and Drew Weissman at the University of Pennsylvania found that swapping uridine in messenger RNA for modified nucleosides such as pseudouridine kept the RNA from setting off strong inflammatory alarms, so cells could make protein from it. Lipid nanoparticles, developed partly for RNA drugs, were used to carry the mRNA into cells. At the NIH Vaccine Research Center, Barney Graham and Jason McLellan learned how to lock coronavirus spike proteins in their prefusion shape with two proline changes. When the SARS-CoV-2 sequence was released in January 2020, Moderna and Pfizer-BioNTech built vaccines within weeks. The FDA authorized the Pfizer-BioNTech vaccine for emergency use in December 2020 and fully approved it as Comirnaty in August 2021. Kariko and Weissman received the 2023 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. A protein subunit vaccine, Nuvaxovid, is also available.", "timeline": [["2005", "Kariko and Weissman show that modified nucleosides make mRNA less inflammatory."], ["2017", "Graham, McLellan and colleagues describe prefusion-stabilized coronavirus spike proteins."], ["2020", "FDA authorizes the first COVID-19 mRNA vaccines for emergency use in December."], ["2021", "FDA fully approves Comirnaty."], ["2023", "Kariko and Weissman receive the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."]], "cell": "The vaccine contains nucleoside-modified (N1-methylpseudouridine) mRNA encoding the SARS-CoV-2 spike protein, held in its prefusion shape, packaged in lipid nanoparticles. After injection, the particles are taken up by muscle cells and by dendritic cells at the site and in draining lymph nodes. Inside the cell, the mRNA is released into the cytoplasm and read by ribosomes; it never enters the nucleus and cannot change DNA. The cell makes spike protein, which appears on its surface and is shed. Peptides from it are shown on MHC class I to CD8 T cells and on MHC class II to CD4 helper T cells. Helper T cells drive B cells to make neutralizing IgG against the receptor-binding domain, which blocks spike from binding the ACE2 receptor on human cells. The mRNA is degraded within days.", "body": [["Immune system", "Neutralizing antibodies and T cells develop over one to two weeks, strongly reducing the risk of severe COVID-19."], ["Lungs", "Protection against severe disease lowers the risk of viral pneumonia, hospitalization and death, especially in older and high-risk adults."], ["Injection site and lymph nodes", "Local inflammation causes a sore arm, and immune activity can make armpit lymph nodes swell."], ["Whole body", "Cytokines from the immune response cause fatigue, headache, muscle aches, chills and fever, usually for a day or two."], ["Heart", "Rarely, myocarditis or pericarditis occurs, most often in adolescent and young adult males within a few days of a dose."]], "journey": "The vaccine is injected into the deltoid muscle. Lipid nanoparticles carry the mRNA into cells at the site and travel to nearby lymph nodes. The cells make spike protein for a short time. Cellular enzymes break down the mRNA and lipids within days, and the spike protein is processed by the immune system. Antibodies peak about two to four weeks after vaccination. Protection against infection wanes over months, while protection against severe disease lasts longer, and updated formulas match newer variants.", "sidefx": "Most reactions reflect the immune response: sore arm, swollen lymph nodes, fatigue, headache, aches, chills and fever, caused by inflammatory signaling as the body responds to the antigen and the lipid particles. Fainting can occur after any injection. Myocarditis and pericarditis are rare and are most common in young males, usually mild, within days of vaccination; the exact mechanism is not settled. Anaphylaxis is rare and may relate to lipid nanoparticle components such as polyethylene glycol, so treatment is available at vaccination sites.", "src": ["Comirnaty (COVID-19 vaccine, mRNA) FDA label", "Kariko K et al. Immunity 2005", "Pallesen J et al. PNAS 2017"]}, "rsv-vaccine": {"origin": "RSV was first isolated in 1956 from chimpanzees with colds and soon found to be a leading cause of infant lung infections. In the 1960s an early vaccine made from formalin-inactivated whole virus backfired: vaccinated infants who later caught RSV became sicker, and two died. For decades this made scientists cautious. The breakthrough came in 2013, when Jason McLellan, Barney Graham and colleagues at the NIH solved the structure of the RSV fusion (F) protein in its prefusion shape and showed this form is the main target of the most powerful neutralizing antibodies. Stabilizing prefusion F made new vaccines possible. In May 2023 the FDA approved GSK's Arexvy, with an adjuvant, and Pfizer's Abrysvo for older adults. Abrysvo was also approved in pregnancy to protect infants, and an mRNA RSV vaccine, mResvia, followed in 2024.", "timeline": [["1956", "RSV is first isolated from chimpanzees."], ["1960s", "A formalin-inactivated RSV vaccine causes enhanced disease in infants."], ["2013", "McLellan and Graham describe the structure of prefusion RSV F protein."], ["2023", "FDA approves Arexvy and Abrysvo, the first RSV vaccines, for older adults."], ["2024", "FDA approves mResvia, an mRNA RSV vaccine."]], "cell": "RSV enters lung cells using its fusion (F) protein. F starts in an unstable prefusion shape and snaps into a postfusion shape as it fuses the viral envelope with the cell membrane. Antibodies that recognize the prefusion form, especially at its apex sites, neutralize the virus much more powerfully. Arexvy contains recombinant RSV F protein locked in the prefusion shape (RSVPreF3) plus the AS01E adjuvant. AS01E combines MPL, a detoxified lipid that activates Toll-like receptor 4, and QS-21, a saponin from the soapbark tree. Together they activate innate immune cells at the injection site, boosting antigen presentation. Helper T cells then drive B cells to make high levels of neutralizing IgG against prefusion F, which block fusion of RSV with respiratory epithelial cells. Older adults, whose immune responses are weaker, get a strong response from the adjuvant.", "body": [["Immune system", "Neutralizing antibodies and CD4 T cells against prefusion F rise within about two weeks and persist across at least two RSV seasons."], ["Lungs", "Protection lowers the risk of RSV pneumonia and lower respiratory tract disease in older and high-risk adults."], ["Injection site", "The adjuvant drives strong local inflammation, so pain, redness and swelling are common."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue, muscle aches, headache and joint pain reflect innate immune activation for a day or two."], ["Nervous system", "An increased risk of Guillain-Barre syndrome in the 42 days after vaccination was seen in an observational study, a labeled warning."]], "journey": "The freeze-dried antigen is mixed with the adjuvant suspension and injected into the deltoid muscle. At the site, the adjuvant recruits and activates immune cells, which carry the F protein to nearby lymph nodes. Antibody responses build over about two weeks and peak around one month. The protein and adjuvant components are broken down by immune cells. ACIP describes it as a single dose rather than an annual shot.", "sidefx": "Most reactions reflect the immune activation the adjuvant is designed to produce: injection site pain and swelling, fatigue, muscle aches, headache and joint pain. Fainting can occur after any injection. Guillain-Barre syndrome, an autoimmune nerve disorder, appeared slightly more often in the six weeks after vaccination in an observational study, so it is a labeled warning. Anaphylaxis is rare. Arexvy is not labeled for pregnancy; maternal protection of infants uses Abrysvo.", "fun": "The failed 1960s vaccine taught a key lesson: the shape of a viral protein matters. Stabilizing just one shape of the F protein turned decades of failure into success.", "src": ["Arexvy (RSV vaccine, adjuvanted) FDA label", "McLellan JS et al. Science 2013", "CDC ACIP RSV vaccine recommendations"]}, "pneumococcal-conjugate-vaccine": {"origin": "Streptococcus pneumoniae was first isolated in 1881 by Louis Pasteur in France and George Sternberg in the US. Its thick sugar capsule protects it from being eaten by white blood cells, and more than 90 capsule types exist. Early vaccines made from pure capsule sugars worked in adults but failed in infants, whose immune systems respond poorly to sugars alone. In 1929 Oswald Avery and Walther Goebel showed that linking a sugar to a protein made it far more immunogenic. That idea led to conjugate vaccines. The FDA approved the first pneumococcal conjugate vaccine, Prevnar (7 types), in 2000, and invasive disease in children fell sharply, with adults protected too through herd effects. Broader versions followed: PCV13 in 2010, PCV15 and PCV20 in 2021 and the adult PCV21 in 2024.", "timeline": [["1881", "Pasteur and Sternberg independently isolate the pneumococcus."], ["1929", "Avery and Goebel show that linking sugars to proteins boosts immunity."], ["2000", "FDA approves Prevnar, the first pneumococcal conjugate vaccine."], ["2010", "Prevnar 13 is approved."], ["2021", "Prevnar 20 and Vaxneuvance are approved for adults."], ["2024", "Capvaxive (PCV21) is approved for adults."]], "cell": "Pure polysaccharides are T cell-independent antigens: they cross-link B cell receptors but cannot be shown to T cells, so they give a short-lived IgM-heavy response with little memory, and infants under two barely respond. In the conjugate vaccine, each serotype's capsular polysaccharide is covalently linked to CRM197, a nontoxic mutant of diphtheria toxin. A B cell that recognizes the sugar takes in the whole conjugate, breaks down the protein and presents its peptides on MHC class II. Helper T cells recognize those peptides and provide help to that same B cell. This converts the response to a T cell-dependent one, with germinal centers, class switching to IgG, affinity maturation and long-lived memory B cells. Opsonizing IgG coats the capsule so neutrophils and macrophages can engulf and kill the bacteria.", "body": [["Immune system", "Serotype-specific IgG and memory B cells develop within about a month, giving long-lasting protection that boosts on re-exposure."], ["Blood and brain", "Protection against invasive disease means far fewer cases of bacteremia and meningitis."], ["Lungs", "Fewer pneumococcal pneumonias occur, including in older adults."], ["Nose and throat", "Conjugate vaccines reduce carriage of vaccine types in the nose, which cuts spread and protects unvaccinated people."], ["Injection site and whole body", "Soreness, swelling, fatigue and muscle aches are common, and infants may have fever and irritability."]], "journey": "The vaccine is injected into muscle, the thigh in infants or the deltoid in adults. Antigen-presenting cells and B cells take up the conjugate at the site and in draining lymph nodes, where the T cell-dependent response develops. Antibody levels rise over about a month. The polysaccharides and carrier protein are broken down by immune cells. Memory B cells persist, giving long-lasting immunity.", "sidefx": "Most reactions are expected signs of immune activation: local soreness, redness and swelling, and in infants fever and irritability from cytokines. Febrile seizures are rare in young children. Premature infants can have apnea after intramuscular vaccines, so they are monitored. Fainting can follow any injection. Anaphylaxis is rare. Because it protects only against the serotypes it contains, non-vaccine types can become more common over time, a process called serotype replacement.", "src": ["Prevnar 20 (pneumococcal 20-valent conjugate vaccine) FDA label", "Avery OT, Goebel WF. J Exp Med 1929", "CDC ACIP pneumococcal vaccine recommendations"]}, "tdap-vaccine": {"origin": "Tdap combines three bacterial defenses. In 1890 Emil von Behring and Kitasato Shibasaburo in Berlin showed that serum from animals immunized against tetanus or diphtheria toxin could neutralize the toxin, earning Behring the first Nobel Prize in Medicine in 1901. In the 1920s Gaston Ramon at the Pasteur Institute found that formaldehyde turns these toxins into harmless toxoids that still trigger immunity. In the 1930s Pearl Kendrick and Grace Eldering in Michigan developed an effective whole-cell pertussis vaccine. Whole-cell pertussis caused frequent fevers and reactions, so in 1981 Japanese researchers led by Yuji Sato introduced an acellular vaccine made of purified proteins. In 2005 the FDA licensed two Tdap vaccines, Boostrix and Adacel, to boost pertussis immunity in adolescents and adults, whose fading protection was fueling outbreaks.", "timeline": [["1890", "Behring and Kitasato describe antitoxin immunity to tetanus and diphtheria."], ["1920s", "Gaston Ramon develops formaldehyde-treated toxoids."], ["1930s", "Kendrick and Eldering develop a whole-cell pertussis vaccine."], ["1981", "Japan introduces acellular pertussis vaccine."], ["2005", "FDA licenses Boostrix and Adacel, the first Tdap vaccines."], ["2012", "ACIP recommends Tdap during every pregnancy."]], "cell": "Tdap contains tetanus toxoid, a reduced amount of diphtheria toxoid (the small d) and acellular pertussis antigens, adsorbed to aluminum salts that hold antigen at the site and activate innate immunity. Pertussis components include inactivated pertussis toxin, filamentous hemagglutinin and pertactin. Antigen-presenting cells show peptides to helper T cells, which drive B cells to make IgG. Antitetanus IgG binds tetanospasmin before it enters nerves; otherwise the toxin travels to inhibitory interneurons, cleaves synaptobrevin and blocks GABA and glycine release, causing rigid spasms. Antidiphtheria IgG blocks toxin entry into cells, where it would ADP-ribosylate elongation factor 2 and stop protein synthesis. Pertussis antibodies neutralize pertussis toxin and block bacterial attachment to airway cilia. As a booster, it recalls memory from childhood vaccination.", "body": [["Immune system", "Antitoxin and pertussis antibodies rise within one to two weeks as memory cells from childhood shots are recalled."], ["Nervous system", "Antitetanus IgG prevents the toxin from reaching nerves, protecting against lockjaw and spasms."], ["Throat and heart", "Antidiphtheria IgG blocks the toxin that damages the throat, heart and nerves."], ["Airways", "Pertussis antibodies reduce whooping cough; maternal IgG crosses the placenta to protect newborns in their first months."], ["Injection site", "Pain, redness and swelling are common, and extensive limb swelling can occur."]], "journey": "Tdap is injected into the deltoid muscle. The aluminum adjuvant keeps antigen near the site, where immune cells pick it up and carry it to lymph nodes. Because most people were vaccinated as children, memory B cells respond quickly, and antibodies rise within one to two weeks and peak around a month. In pregnancy, maternal IgG is actively transported across the placenta to the fetus. Tetanus protection lasts about ten years, while pertussis protection fades within a few years. Antigens are broken down by immune cells.", "sidefx": "Most reactions reflect local inflammation from the antigens and aluminum adjuvant: pain, redness, swelling, fatigue and headache. Very frequent tetanus toxoid doses can cause an Arthus reaction, a painful local swelling from antibody-antigen complexes that activate complement in vessel walls. Fainting can occur after injection. Guillain-Barre syndrome after an earlier tetanus toxoid dose is a precaution. Anaphylaxis is rare. Because toxoids are inactivated, the vaccine cannot cause the diseases.", "src": ["Boostrix and Adacel (Tdap) FDA labels", "CDC ACIP pertussis vaccine recommendations", "Plotkin's Vaccines"]}, "td-vaccine": {"origin": "Td grew from the same discoveries as other toxoid vaccines. In 1890 Emil von Behring and Kitasato Shibasaburo showed that antibodies could neutralize tetanus and diphtheria toxins. In the 1920s Gaston Ramon at the Pasteur Institute treated the toxins with formaldehyde to make toxoids, which trigger immunity without causing disease. Tetanus toxoid was given widely to Allied soldiers in World War II, and tetanus became rare among them compared with earlier wars. As children grew up vaccinated, doctors found that full-strength diphtheria toxoid caused more reactions in older children and adults. A booster with a reduced amount of diphtheria toxoid, written as a small d, became the standard for people seven and older. Td remains used for boosters and wound care when pertussis protection is not needed.", "timeline": [["1890", "Behring and Kitasato describe antitoxin immunity to tetanus and diphtheria."], ["1920s", "Gaston Ramon develops formaldehyde-inactivated toxoids."], ["1940s", "Tetanus toxoid is widely used in the military during World War II."]], "cell": "Td contains formaldehyde-detoxified tetanus toxoid and a reduced amount of diphtheria toxoid, adsorbed to aluminum salts. The aluminum holds antigen at the site and activates innate immune cells. Antigen-presenting cells take up the toxoids and present peptides to helper T cells, which drive B cells to class-switch and make IgG antitoxins and to form memory cells. Antitetanus IgG binds tetanospasmin before it attaches to motor nerve terminals. Without that, the toxin travels backward along the nerve to inhibitory interneurons in the spinal cord, where it cleaves synaptobrevin and blocks release of GABA and glycine, leaving muscles in uncontrolled spasm. Antidiphtheria IgG blocks the toxin's binding and entry into cells, where it would ADP-ribosylate elongation factor 2 and stop protein synthesis.", "body": [["Immune system", "Memory B cells respond to the booster, raising antitoxin levels within one to two weeks."], ["Nervous system", "Antitetanus antibodies prevent tetanus, protecting the inhibitory nerves that keep muscles from locking."], ["Throat, heart and nerves", "Antidiphtheria antibodies block the toxin that causes throat membranes, myocarditis and neuropathy."], ["Wounds", "A booster after a dirty wound renews protection when the last dose was long ago."], ["Injection site", "Pain, redness and swelling from local inflammation are common."]], "journey": "Td is injected into the deltoid muscle. Aluminum adjuvant keeps antigen at the site, where immune cells pick it up and carry it to lymph nodes. In someone previously vaccinated, memory cells produce a rapid rise in antitoxin within about one to two weeks, peaking around a month. Toxoid proteins are broken down by immune cells. Protection lasts about ten years, which is why boosters are spaced that way.", "sidefx": "Local pain, redness and swelling reflect inflammation from toxoid and aluminum. Giving tetanus toxoid too often can cause an Arthus reaction: when antibody levels are already high, injected antigen forms immune complexes in small vessel walls, which activate complement and cause painful swelling. Headache and tiredness are common. Fainting can follow an injection. Guillain-Barre syndrome after a prior tetanus toxoid dose is a precaution. Anaphylaxis is rare. The toxoids cannot cause disease.", "fun": "Tetanus spores live in soil everywhere, and tetanus cannot be eradicated, so lifelong boosters remain the only protection.", "src": ["Tenivac (Td vaccine) FDA label", "CDC ACIP tetanus and diphtheria recommendations", "Plotkin's Vaccines"]}, "dtap-vaccine": {"origin": "Whooping cough is caused by Bordetella pertussis, first grown in 1906 by Jules Bordet and Octave Gengou in Brussels. In the 1930s Pearl Kendrick and Grace Eldering in Grand Rapids, Michigan, developed an effective whole-cell pertussis vaccine. In the 1940s it was combined with diphtheria and tetanus toxoids into DTP, and childhood pertussis deaths fell dramatically. Whole-cell vaccine, though, often caused fever, crying and other reactions, and public fears in the 1970s led to drops in vaccination and outbreaks in several countries. Japanese researchers led by Yuji Sato developed an acellular vaccine made of purified pertussis proteins, used in Japan from 1981. In the US, DTaP was licensed in 1991 for the later doses and in 1996 for the full infant series, replacing whole-cell DTP.", "timeline": [["1906", "Bordet and Gengou isolate Bordetella pertussis."], ["1930s", "Kendrick and Eldering develop a whole-cell pertussis vaccine."], ["1940s", "Diphtheria, tetanus and pertussis vaccines are combined as DTP."], ["1981", "Acellular pertussis vaccine is introduced in Japan."], ["1991", "DTaP is first licensed in the US for later childhood doses."], ["1996", "DTaP is licensed for the full infant series."]], "cell": "DTaP contains full-strength diphtheria toxoid (the capital D), tetanus toxoid and acellular pertussis antigens such as detoxified pertussis toxin, filamentous hemagglutinin, pertactin and, in some brands, fimbriae types 2 and 3, adsorbed to aluminum salts. Antigen-presenting cells take up these proteins and present peptides to helper T cells, which drive B cells to produce IgG and memory cells. Antitoxin IgG neutralizes tetanospasmin, which otherwise blocks GABA and glycine release from inhibitory interneurons, and diphtheria toxin, which otherwise ADP-ribosylates elongation factor 2. Antibodies against pertussis toxin blunt its effects on airway cells and white blood cells, and antibodies against adhesins block the bacteria from attaching to ciliated airway cells. Several doses are needed in infancy to build lasting immunity.", "body": [["Immune system", "A series of doses builds protective antitoxin and pertussis antibodies and immune memory in young children."], ["Airways", "Protection against pertussis prevents the severe coughing spells, apnea and pneumonia that are most dangerous in infants."], ["Nervous system", "Antitetanus antibodies prevent the spasms of tetanus."], ["Throat and heart", "Antidiphtheria antibodies prevent the throat membrane and heart damage of diphtheria."], ["Injection site", "Redness, swelling and tenderness are common, and the whole limb may swell after the fourth or fifth dose."], ["Whole body", "Fever, fussiness, drowsiness and poor appetite reflect the immune response for a day or two."]], "journey": "DTaP is injected into the thigh muscle in infants and the deltoid in older children. The aluminum adjuvant holds antigen at the site, where immune cells pick it up and carry it to lymph nodes. Each dose builds on the previous one, so antibody levels rise in steps over the series, peaking about a month after each dose. Antigens are broken down by immune cells. Pertussis protection wanes after the fifth dose, which is why a Tdap booster is recommended at 11 to 12 years.", "sidefx": "Most reactions are local or general signs of immune activation: redness, swelling, tenderness, fever and fussiness. Extensive limb swelling after later doses likely reflects strong local antibody and inflammatory responses to repeated antigen. Febrile seizures can follow fever. Hypotonic-hyporesponsive episodes and prolonged crying occur but are much rarer than with whole-cell vaccine. Premature infants can have apnea after intramuscular vaccines. Anaphylaxis is rare. DTaP is not used at age seven and older, when Tdap or Td is used.", "src": ["Daptacel and Infanrix (DTaP) FDA labels", "CDC ACIP pertussis vaccine recommendations", "Plotkin's Vaccines"]}, "mmr-vaccine": {"origin": "The MMR vaccine joins three stories. In 1954 John Enders and Thomas Peebles grew measles virus from the throat of a sick Boston boy named David Edmonston, and passing that virus many times through cell cultures weakened it into the first measles vaccine, licensed in the US in 1963. In 1963 Maurice Hilleman of Merck took mumps virus from his daughter Jeryl Lynn when she fell ill, and the weakened Jeryl Lynn strain became a vaccine in 1967. Stanley Plotkin at the Wistar Institute developed the RA 27/3 rubella strain in the 1960s after a rubella epidemic left thousands of babies with birth defects. Merck combined the viruses into one shot in 1971, and M-M-R II with the RA 27/3 rubella strain followed in 1978. GSK's Priorix was approved in the US in 2022. Two doses made measles elimination in the US possible, declared in 2000.", "timeline": [["1954", "Enders and Peebles isolate measles virus from David Edmonston."], ["1963", "First live measles vaccine is licensed in the US."], ["1967", "Hilleman's Jeryl Lynn mumps vaccine is licensed."], ["1971", "Merck licenses the combined MMR vaccine."], ["1978", "M-M-R II with the RA 27/3 rubella strain replaces the earlier version."], ["2000", "Measles is declared eliminated from the United States."]], "cell": "The vaccine holds three live, weakened (attenuated) viruses. After the shot they infect a small number of cells near the site and in local lymph nodes and copy themselves slowly, far less than wild virus. Dendritic cells take up viral proteins (measles hemagglutinin and fusion protein, mumps hemagglutinin-neuraminidase, rubella E1 and E2) and carry them to lymph nodes. There they present peptides on MHC class II to helper CD4 T cells, and because the viruses actually infect cells, peptides also appear on MHC class I, priming cytotoxic CD8 T cells. Helper T cells drive B cells to switch to IgG and form memory cells. The result is neutralizing antibody that blocks the viruses from attaching to cells, plus T-cell memory, much like natural infection but without disease.", "body": [["Immune system", "A short, mostly silent infection builds neutralizing antibodies and memory B and T cells against all three viruses, often for life after two doses."], ["Skin", "A mild rash can appear about 7 to 12 days after the shot as the weakened measles virus copies itself and immune cells respond."], ["Brain (temperature control)", "Fever in the second week comes from cytokines released during the immune response; in young children a fast rise can trigger a febrile seizure."], ["Joints", "Adult women sometimes get short-lived joint pain from the rubella part, a milder version of the arthritis that natural rubella causes."], ["Blood", "Rarely, platelets fall for a few weeks because antibodies made during the response coat platelets and the spleen removes them."], ["Unborn babies (population)", "By stopping rubella spread, the vaccine prevents congenital rubella syndrome, which causes deafness, heart defects and cataracts."]], "journey": "The vaccine is given as a shot into the fat or muscle. The weakened viruses copy themselves locally and in nearby lymph nodes for about one to two weeks, which is when mild fever or rash may appear. Antibodies appear about two to three weeks after the shot and peak at about four to six weeks. The vaccine viruses are cleared by the immune system rather than by the liver or kidneys. Immunity to measles and rubella is very long lasting; mumps immunity can fade over years.", "sidefx": "Most reactions come from the brief, controlled infection: fever, mild rash and swollen glands appear one to two weeks after the shot, when viral copying peaks. Febrile seizures follow fast fever spikes in young children and do not cause lasting harm. Joint pain reflects rubella's natural tendency to inflame joints. Low platelets are immune mediated and usually resolve. Because the viruses are live, people with severe immune suppression cannot control them, so the vaccine can spread through the body; that is why it is avoided in severe immunodeficiency and pregnancy. Anaphylaxis is rare and is usually linked to vaccine components such as gelatin or neomycin.", "fun": "Maurice Hilleman developed more than 40 vaccines in his career, and the mumps strain in MMR is still named after his daughter Jeryl Lynn, who was five when she had mumps.", "src": ["M-M-R II FDA label", "Priorix FDA label", "CDC Pink Book: Epidemiology and Prevention of Vaccine-Preventable Diseases", "Plotkin's Vaccines"]}, "varicella-vaccine": {"origin": "The chickenpox vaccine came from Japan. In the early 1970s virologist Michiaki Takahashi at Osaka University took varicella-zoster virus from a boy with chickenpox whose family name was Oka. He weakened it by passing it through human and guinea pig cells at low temperature, and he published results of the Oka strain vaccine in 1974. Merck refined the Oka strain into Varivax, which the FDA approved in 1995 as the first chickenpox vaccine in the US. In 2005 the FDA approved ProQuad, which combines varicella with MMR. Before the vaccine, nearly every US child caught chickenpox, and about 100 people died of it each year.", "timeline": [["1974", "Michiaki Takahashi reports the live attenuated Oka strain varicella vaccine."], ["1995", "FDA approves Varivax for chickenpox prevention."], ["2005", "FDA approves ProQuad (MMR plus varicella)."], ["2006", "A routine second dose is recommended for children in the US."]], "cell": "Varivax contains live, weakened Oka/Merck strain varicella-zoster virus (VZV). After the shot it infects a few skin and immune cells near the site and copies itself slowly. Dendritic cells pick up viral glycoproteins such as gE, gB and gH and present them to CD4 helper T cells in lymph nodes. Infected cells also show viral peptides on MHC class I, which primes cytotoxic CD8 T cells. Helper T cells help B cells make neutralizing IgG against the glycoproteins. For VZV, T-cell immunity matters most for controlling the virus, and the vaccine builds both arms. Like wild virus, the vaccine strain can settle quietly in sensory nerve ganglia, but it reactivates as shingles less often than wild virus.", "body": [["Immune system", "Builds antibodies and VZV-specific T cells that prevent chickenpox or make breakthrough cases mild, with very few blisters and little fever."], ["Skin", "A few chickenpox-like spots can appear near the shot or elsewhere within weeks as the weakened virus copies itself."], ["Sensory nerves", "The vaccine strain can become latent in nerve ganglia, so shingles from the vaccine virus is possible but less common than after natural chickenpox."], ["Brain (temperature control)", "Fever can follow the shot as cytokines rise during the immune response; febrile seizures are uncommon, and slightly more frequent after the combined MMRV shot in toddlers."], ["Community", "Fewer cases mean less spread to newborns, pregnant women and people with weak immune systems, who get the most severe chickenpox."]], "journey": "The vaccine is injected under the skin or into muscle. The live virus copies itself locally for a short time and is then cleared by the immune response. Antibodies develop over about two to six weeks. The virus is handled entirely by the immune system, not by the liver or kidneys. Varivax is a frozen product because the live virus loses strength quickly when warm. Two doses give long-lasting protection.", "sidefx": "Soreness and redness come from local inflammation at the shot. A mild rash with a few lesions reflects the vaccine virus copying itself; rarely, the virus in those spots can spread to close contacts, so people with a rash avoid contact with highly vulnerable people. Fever reflects normal immune signaling. Because the virus is live, people with severe immune suppression may not contain it and can develop widespread infection, which is why the vaccine is avoided in that setting and in pregnancy. Shingles can occur later because the vaccine virus can hide in nerves. Antiviral drugs such as acyclovir act on vaccine-strain virus if serious infection occurs.", "fun": "The Oka strain is named after the family of the Japanese boy whose chickenpox provided the original virus.", "src": ["Varivax FDA label", "ProQuad FDA label", "Takahashi M et al. Lancet 1974", "CDC Pink Book"]}, "zoster-recombinant-vaccine": {"origin": "Shingles happens when varicella-zoster virus left in nerves after childhood chickenpox wakes up, usually as T-cell immunity weakens with age. The first shingles vaccine, Zostavax (approved 2006), was a strong dose of live Oka virus, but it worked less well in older adults and faded over time. GSK took a different path: it used just one viral protein, glycoprotein E, and paired it with AS01B, an adjuvant system it built from a bacterial lipid (monophosphoryl lipid A) and a saponin called QS-21 from the bark of the Chilean soapbark tree, Quillaja saponaria. Large trials showed very high protection even in people over 70. The FDA approved Shingrix in 2017, and Zostavax was discontinued in the US in 2020.", "timeline": [["2006", "Live Zostavax becomes the first US shingles vaccine."], ["2015", "ZOE-50 trial reports about 97% efficacy for the recombinant vaccine in adults 50 and older."], ["2017", "FDA approves Shingrix."], ["2020", "Zostavax is discontinued in the United States."], ["2021", "Shingrix is approved for adults 18 and older at increased risk from immunosuppression."]], "cell": "Shingrix is not live. It contains recombinant glycoprotein E (gE), the most abundant protein on VZV and on infected cells. The AS01B adjuvant does the heavy lifting. Monophosphoryl lipid A binds Toll-like receptor 4 on innate immune cells, and QS-21 activates the inflammasome and helps antigen get into antigen-presenting cells. Together they trigger a burst of local cytokines and interferon-gamma, and strongly activate dendritic cells. These cells present gE peptides on MHC class II, producing a very large number of gE-specific CD4 T cells and high antibody levels. Those CD4 T cells patrol the body and help hold latent VZV in the dorsal root and cranial nerve ganglia in check, preventing reactivation as shingles.", "body": [["Immune system", "Restores strong VZV-specific CD4 T-cell immunity that tends to decline with age or immune suppression, preventing reactivation."], ["Sensory nerves", "By blocking reactivation in nerve ganglia, it prevents the painful band-shaped rash and lowers the risk of postherpetic neuralgia, long-term nerve pain after shingles."], ["Injection site", "The adjuvant causes strong local inflammation, so pain, redness and swelling of the arm are common for a few days."], ["Whole body", "Cytokines spread from the injection site cause fatigue, muscle aches, headache, chills and fever for one to three days in many people."], ["Eyes", "Preventing shingles also prevents herpes zoster ophthalmicus, which can damage the eye."]], "journey": "Shingrix is given into the deltoid muscle as a two-dose series. The protein and adjuvant stay near the injection site and in draining lymph nodes, where immune cells take them up. Nothing replicates. The immune response builds over about a month after each dose and peaks about a month after the second. Protection stays high for at least seven to ten years in follow-up studies. The antigen is broken down by immune cells into peptides and amino acids.", "sidefx": "Shingrix causes more short-term reactions than many vaccines, and that is a direct result of the adjuvant. Strong activation of innate immunity at the injection site releases cytokines that cause arm pain and swelling, and the same cytokines in the blood cause fatigue, muscle aches, shivering and fever for a day or two. Because it is not live, it is safe for people with weak immune systems, who cannot take live vaccines. A small increased risk of Guillain-Barre syndrome in the weeks after vaccination was noted in post-marketing data. Fainting can follow any injection. Anaphylaxis is rare.", "fun": "QS-21, one of the adjuvant's two ingredients, is purified from the inner bark of the Chilean soapbark tree, which has been used for centuries to make soap.", "src": ["Shingrix FDA label", "Lal H et al. N Engl J Med 2015 (ZOE-50)", "CDC Pink Book", "Plotkin's Vaccines"]}, "hepatitis-a-vaccine": {"origin": "Hepatitis A virus spreads through food and water contaminated with stool. It was hard to make a vaccine because the virus grows very slowly in the lab. In 1979 Philip Provost and Maurice Hilleman at Merck reported growing hepatitis A virus in cell culture, opening the door to a vaccine. Scientists then grew the virus in human fibroblast cells, purified it and killed it with formalin. SmithKline Beecham's Havrix became the first hepatitis A vaccine approved in the US in 1995, and Merck's Vaqta followed in 1996. Twinrix, which combines hepatitis A and B vaccines, was approved in 2001. Routine childhood vaccination in the US, recommended for all children in 2006, sharply cut hepatitis A cases.", "timeline": [["1973", "Hepatitis A virus is seen in stool by electron microscopy."], ["1979", "Provost and Hilleman grow hepatitis A virus in cell culture."], ["1995", "FDA approves Havrix, the first US hepatitis A vaccine."], ["1996", "FDA approves Vaqta."], ["2006", "Hepatitis A vaccine is recommended for all US children from age 1."]], "cell": "The vaccine contains whole hepatitis A virus that has been killed with formalin, so it cannot copy itself. The virus particles are stuck to aluminum hydroxide, an adjuvant that holds antigen at the site and activates innate immune signals such as the NLRP3 inflammasome. Antigen-presenting cells take up the viral capsid, process it and show peptides on MHC class II to helper T cells. Those T cells help B cells that recognize the capsid switch to IgG and become plasma cells and memory cells. The resulting anti-HAV IgG circulates in the blood and neutralizes virus that has been swallowed and absorbed before it can infect liver cells.", "body": [["Immune system", "Makes neutralizing anti-HAV IgG in more than 90% of people within a few weeks of the first dose; a second dose locks in long-term memory."], ["Liver", "Blocks the virus from infecting hepatocytes, preventing the jaundice, fatigue and rare liver failure of hepatitis A."], ["Injection site", "Aluminum adjuvant causes mild local inflammation, so soreness and redness are common."], ["Whole body", "Headache, tiredness, low fever and poor appetite for a day or two reflect cytokines from the immune response."]], "journey": "The vaccine is a cloudy suspension given into the deltoid (or thigh in small children). The antigen stays near the injection site and in nearby lymph nodes, where immune cells process it. Antibody appears within about two to four weeks. The killed virus and aluminum are slowly cleared; the protein is broken down by immune cells. After two doses, protection likely lasts 20 years or more.", "sidefx": "Because the vaccine is inactivated, it cannot cause hepatitis A. Soreness, redness and swelling come from the aluminum adjuvant and local immune activation. Headache, fatigue and low fever are brief effects of immune cytokines. Fainting can happen after any shot, especially in teens. Anaphylaxis is rare and is usually tied to a component such as neomycin or latex in some packaging.", "fun": "Hepatitis A vaccine works so quickly that one dose given soon after an exposure can still prevent illness, because the virus incubates for about four weeks.", "src": ["Havrix FDA label", "Vaqta FDA label", "CDC Pink Book", "Provost PJ, Hilleman MR. Proc Soc Exp Biol Med 1979"]}, "hepatitis-b-vaccine": {"origin": "In 1965 Baruch Blumberg found an unusual protein in the blood of an Australian Aboriginal man and called it Australia antigen. It turned out to be the surface protein of hepatitis B virus, and Blumberg won the 1976 Nobel Prize. The first vaccine, Merck's Heptavax-B (1981), was made by purifying that surface protein from the blood of chronically infected people. Fear of blood-borne infections pushed researchers to make the protein a safer way. Scientists inserted the gene for hepatitis B surface antigen into baker's yeast, and Merck's Recombivax HB, approved in 1986, became the first recombinant vaccine in the world. Engerix-B followed in 1989, and Heplisav-B, with a CpG adjuvant, in 2017. Because hepatitis B causes liver cancer, it is often called the first anti-cancer vaccine.", "timeline": [["1965", "Baruch Blumberg discovers the Australia antigen."], ["1976", "Blumberg shares the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."], ["1981", "Plasma-derived Heptavax-B is licensed."], ["1986", "Recombivax HB becomes the first recombinant vaccine approved."], ["1991", "Universal infant hepatitis B vaccination is recommended in the US."], ["2017", "FDA approves Heplisav-B, a two-dose adjuvanted vaccine for adults."]], "cell": "The vaccine contains hepatitis B surface antigen (HBsAg) made by yeast cells carrying the viral gene. The protein self-assembles into small particles that look like the outer coat of the virus but contain no viral DNA, so they cannot cause infection. Aluminum adjuvant (or the CpG 1018 adjuvant in Heplisav-B, which activates Toll-like receptor 9) keeps the antigen in place and alerts innate immune cells. Dendritic cells present HBsAg peptides on MHC class II to helper T cells, which help B cells make high-affinity anti-HBs IgG and memory cells. Anti-HBs binds the surface protein on incoming virus and stops it from entering liver cells. A level of at least 10 mIU/mL after the series is the accepted marker of protection.", "body": [["Immune system", "Builds anti-HBs antibody and B-cell memory; even when antibody levels fall over years, memory cells respond fast to exposure."], ["Liver", "Preventing infection prevents acute hepatitis, chronic hepatitis B, cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma."], ["Newborns", "A birth dose, together with hepatitis B immune globulin for babies of infected mothers, blocks most mother-to-baby transmission."], ["Injection site", "Soreness and redness reflect local inflammation from the adjuvant."], ["Whole body", "Brief tiredness, headache or low fever reflect immune cytokines."]], "journey": "The vaccine is injected into the deltoid in adults or the thigh in infants. Gluteal injection is avoided because fat there gives weaker responses. The protein particles stay in the muscle and draining lymph nodes, where immune cells process them. Antibody builds after the second and third doses and peaks one to two months after the last dose. Nothing is metabolized by the liver or kidneys. Responses are weaker with older age, obesity, smoking, diabetes, dialysis and immune suppression.", "sidefx": "The vaccine cannot cause hepatitis B because it contains only a surface protein, no virus. Arm soreness comes from the adjuvant's local inflammatory signal. Fatigue, headache and low fever are short-lived cytokine effects. Fainting can occur after any injection. Anaphylaxis is rare; some products are made in yeast, so severe yeast allergy is a contraindication.", "fun": "Recombivax HB was the first vaccine made with genetic engineering, years before recombinant insulin became the standard for diabetes.", "src": ["Engerix-B FDA label", "Recombivax HB FDA label", "Heplisav-B FDA label", "Blumberg BS et al. JAMA 1965", "CDC Pink Book"]}, "hpv-vaccine": {"origin": "In the early 1980s Harald zur Hausen in Germany found HPV types 16 and 18 in cervical cancer tissue, proving that a virus causes most cervical cancer; he won the 2008 Nobel Prize. The next challenge was a vaccine that did not contain the cancer-causing viral DNA. In the early 1990s researchers learned that the HPV L1 shell protein, made alone in cells, assembles into empty virus-like particles that look like the real virus. Ian Frazer and Jian Zhou at the University of Queensland and teams at the US National Cancer Institute, led by Douglas Lowy and John Schiller, were key to this work. Merck's Gardasil, covering four types, was approved in 2006, and Gardasil 9, covering nine types, in 2014. Countries with high uptake have seen large drops in cervical precancer and cancer.", "timeline": [["1983", "Zur Hausen's group identifies HPV 16 in cervical cancer, followed by HPV 18."], ["1991", "Frazer and Zhou report HPV virus-like particles."], ["2006", "FDA approves Gardasil, the first HPV vaccine."], ["2008", "Zur Hausen shares the Nobel Prize."], ["2014", "FDA approves Gardasil 9."], ["2020", "Gardasil 9 indication adds prevention of oropharyngeal and other head and neck cancers."]], "cell": "Gardasil 9 contains the L1 capsid protein from nine HPV types, made in yeast. Each L1 protein self-assembles into an empty virus-like particle (VLP) with the same repeating surface shape as a real virion but no viral DNA. The densely repeated surface strongly cross-links B-cell receptors, which makes the VLPs very immunogenic. With help from CD4 T cells and an aluminum adjuvant, B cells make high levels of type-specific neutralizing IgG. HPV infects basal cells of the epithelium through tiny breaks. Serum IgG leaks (transudates) onto the cervical and genital mucosa and also reaches sites of microtrauma, where it binds virus and blocks infection before it starts.", "body": [["Immune system", "Produces neutralizing antibodies to nine HPV types at levels higher than natural infection gives, with long-lasting memory."], ["Cervix and genital tract", "Prevents persistent infection with high-risk types, so precancerous lesions and cervical, vulvar, vaginal and anal cancers are prevented."], ["Throat", "Prevents oral infection with high-risk types linked to oropharyngeal cancer."], ["Skin of genital area", "Types 6 and 11 cause most genital warts, and the vaccine prevents them."], ["Nervous system (fainting)", "Teens can faint after the shot from a vasovagal reaction to the injection, not from the vaccine contents."]], "journey": "Gardasil 9 is injected into the deltoid or thigh. The VLPs stay at the injection site and travel to draining lymph nodes, where they are taken up by immune cells. Nothing replicates. Antibodies peak about a month after the final dose and stay well above protective levels for at least ten years in follow-up. The protein is broken down by immune cells. The vaccine works best before any exposure to HPV, which is why it is given in early adolescence.", "sidefx": "Arm pain, swelling and redness come from local immune activation by the adjuvant and protein. Headache and dizziness are common after the shot. Fainting is a reflex reaction to the injection in adolescents, which is why people sit or lie down for 15 minutes afterward. Because the vaccine has no viral DNA, it cannot cause HPV infection or cancer. It also does not treat existing infections or lesions. Anaphylaxis is rare; severe yeast allergy is a contraindication.", "fun": "HPV VLPs are so immunogenic that even a single dose produced lasting protection in several studies, which led the WHO to allow one-dose schedules in 2022.", "src": ["Gardasil 9 FDA label", "zur Hausen H. Nobel Lecture 2008", "Zhou J, Frazer IH et al. Virology 1991", "CDC Pink Book"]}, "meningococcal-acwy-vaccine": {"origin": "Neisseria meningitidis can kill a healthy teenager within a day. Early vaccines in the 1970s were made of plain capsular sugar (polysaccharide), work pioneered at Walter Reed by Emil Gotschlich, Irving Goldschneider and Malcolm Artenstein for military recruits. Plain sugars, however, work poorly in infants and do not build lasting memory. The fix was conjugation, linking the sugar to a protein carrier, an idea proven with Hib vaccines. The first US conjugate meningococcal ACWY vaccine, Menactra, was approved in 2005. Menveo (CRM197 carrier) followed in 2010 and MenQuadfi (tetanus toxoid carrier) in 2020. Combined MenABCWY vaccines, Penbraya and Penmenvy, were approved in 2023 and 2025.", "timeline": [["1969", "Walter Reed researchers report a serogroup C polysaccharide vaccine."], ["1978", "Polysaccharide meningococcal vaccines are licensed in the US."], ["2005", "Menactra becomes the first US conjugate ACWY vaccine."], ["2010", "FDA approves Menveo."], ["2020", "FDA approves MenQuadfi."], ["2023", "FDA approves Penbraya, the first MenABCWY vaccine."]], "cell": "Meningococci hide inside a sugar capsule that resists phagocytes. Pure capsule sugars activate B cells directly, without T-cell help, so the response is weak in young children, mostly IgM and short-lived. In conjugate vaccines each capsular polysaccharide (A, C, W and Y) is chemically joined to a carrier protein, such as tetanus toxoid or CRM197 (a nontoxic diphtheria toxin mutant). A B cell that binds the sugar takes in the whole conjugate, breaks down the protein and presents its peptides on MHC class II. Carrier-specific helper T cells then give that B cell help, which drives class switching to IgG, affinity maturation and memory. The IgG binds the capsule and activates complement, which punches holes in the bacteria (serum bactericidal activity). Antibody on the nasal lining also reduces carriage.", "body": [["Immune system", "Builds IgG that triggers complement-mediated killing of serogroups A, C, W and Y; protection wanes over about five years, so teens get a booster."], ["Brain and meninges", "Prevents meningococcal meningitis, which can cause death, hearing loss and brain injury."], ["Blood and blood vessels", "Prevents meningococcemia, where bacterial endotoxin damages vessels and causes shock, clotting and limb loss."], ["Nose and throat", "Lowers carriage of the bacteria, which reduces spread in dorms and barracks."], ["Injection site", "Pain and redness come from local inflammation."]], "journey": "The vaccine is injected into the deltoid or thigh. The conjugate stays at the site and drains to lymph nodes, where B and T cells meet it. Bactericidal antibody appears within about one to two weeks and peaks around a month. The protein and sugar are broken down by immune cells. Antibody levels fall over three to five years, which is why boosters are timed for later adolescence and for ongoing risk.", "sidefx": "Arm pain, headache, muscle aches and tiredness reflect local and body-wide immune signaling for a day or two. Fainting can follow injection, mostly in teens. People with complement deficiency or on complement inhibitors such as eculizumab stay at high risk even after vaccination, because the antibody kills meningococci mainly by activating complement. Anaphylaxis is rare. Rare reports of Guillain-Barre syndrome were investigated without a clear causal link for current vaccines.", "fun": "People taking complement inhibitor drugs for rare blood diseases have up to a 1,000-fold or more higher risk of meningococcal disease, because these drugs shut down the very pathway the vaccine's antibodies use.", "src": ["MenQuadfi FDA label", "Menveo FDA label", "Goldschneider I, Gotschlich EC, Artenstein MS. J Exp Med 1969", "CDC Pink Book"]}, "meningococcal-b-vaccine": {"origin": "Serogroup B meningococcus defeated the usual vaccine approach. Its sugar capsule is chemically similar to a sugar on human nerve cells, so the body barely responds to it, and using it in a vaccine raised concerns about autoimmunity. In the late 1990s Rino Rappuoli and colleagues at Chiron in Italy read the whole genome of a group B strain and searched it by computer for surface proteins, an approach called reverse vaccinology, published in 2000. They found factor H binding protein, NadA and NHBA, and added outer membrane vesicles from a New Zealand outbreak strain. This became Bexsero, approved in the US in 2015 after use in US college outbreaks. Pfizer's Trumenba, built on two forms of factor H binding protein, was approved in 2014.", "timeline": [["2000", "Pizza, Rappuoli and colleagues report reverse vaccinology for group B meningococcus."], ["2004", "New Zealand uses an outer membrane vesicle vaccine (MeNZB) to fight a long outbreak."], ["2013", "Bexsero is used under special FDA permission during US college outbreaks."], ["2014", "FDA approves Trumenba."], ["2015", "FDA approves Bexsero."]], "cell": "Because the group B capsule (alpha 2,8 polysialic acid) mimics human neural cell adhesion molecule, the vaccine targets proteins below the capsule. Bexsero contains factor H binding protein (fHbp), Neisserial adhesin A (NadA), Neisserial heparin binding antigen (NHBA) and outer membrane vesicles carrying PorA. Antigen-presenting cells process these proteins and activate helper T cells, which help B cells make IgG. The antibodies work in two ways. They activate the classical complement pathway on the bacterial surface, killing the bacteria. Antibody against fHbp also blocks the bacterium from grabbing human factor H, a protein it uses to switch off complement, so the bacterium loses its shield. Strains differ in how much of each protein they make, so coverage is broad but not complete.", "body": [["Immune system", "Builds bactericidal antibodies against surface proteins of many group B strains; levels fall within one to two years."], ["Brain and meninges", "Lowers the risk of group B meningitis, the main cause of meningococcal disease in US adolescents and young adults."], ["Blood", "Helps prevent meningococcemia and septic shock."], ["Injection site", "Pain is often marked, with swelling and firmness, from strong local immune activation by the proteins and vesicles."], ["Whole body", "Muscle aches, tiredness, headache, nausea and fever are common for a day or two."]], "journey": "MenB vaccine is injected into the deltoid as a series. The proteins and vesicles stay in the muscle and drain to lymph nodes. Bactericidal antibody is measured about a month after the series. Nothing replicates, and immune cells break the proteins down. Protection wanes over one to two years, which is why boosters are given during outbreaks or ongoing risk. Bexsero and Trumenba are not interchangeable, so a series stays with one product.", "sidefx": "MenB vaccines cause more arm pain than many vaccines. The outer membrane vesicles contain bacterial components that strongly activate innate immunity, and the cytokines released cause local pain plus fever, fatigue and aches. Fainting can follow injection in teens. As with MenACWY, people with complement deficiency or on complement inhibitors remain at risk because killing depends on complement. Anaphylaxis is rare.", "fun": "Bexsero was one of the first vaccines designed by reading a bacterial genome first and growing the bacteria second, the idea behind the name reverse vaccinology.", "src": ["Bexsero FDA label", "Trumenba FDA label", "Pizza M et al. Science 2000", "CDC Pink Book"]}, "haemophilus-b-vaccine": {"origin": "Before vaccines, Haemophilus influenzae type b (Hib) was the leading cause of bacterial meningitis in US children under five. In the 1970s Porter Anderson and David Smith in Rochester, and John Robbins and Rachel Schneerson at the NIH, worked on vaccines from the Hib capsule sugar, polyribosylribitol phosphate (PRP). A plain PRP vaccine licensed in 1985 did not work in babies under two, who need protection most. Robbins and Schneerson showed in 1980 that linking PRP to a protein made it work in infants. The first conjugate Hib vaccine was licensed in 1987, and use in infants began in 1990. Hib disease in young children then fell by more than 99%. ActHIB, with a tetanus toxoid carrier, was approved in 1993.", "timeline": [["1980", "Schneerson and Robbins report a Hib polysaccharide-protein conjugate."], ["1985", "A plain PRP polysaccharide Hib vaccine is licensed."], ["1987", "The first conjugate Hib vaccine is licensed in the US."], ["1990", "Conjugate Hib vaccines are approved for infants."], ["1993", "FDA approves ActHIB."]], "cell": "Hib is protected by a sugar capsule of PRP that blocks phagocytes. Infants under about two years cannot mount a good T-cell-independent response to sugars, because the needed marginal zone B cells in the spleen are immature. Conjugate vaccines link PRP to a carrier protein, such as tetanus toxoid. A PRP-specific B cell swallows the conjugate, breaks down the carrier and shows its peptides on MHC class II. Carrier-specific helper T cells then help that B cell, which drives IgG class switching, affinity maturation and memory. Anti-PRP IgG coats the bacteria so complement and phagocytes can destroy them. Antibody on mucosa also reduces nasal carriage, which gives herd protection.", "body": [["Immune system", "Gives infants T-cell-dependent anti-PRP IgG and memory; a booster in the second year extends protection."], ["Brain and meninges", "Prevents Hib meningitis, which once caused deafness and brain damage in many survivors."], ["Airway", "Prevents epiglottitis, a rapidly swelling throat infection that can close the airway."], ["Blood and lungs", "Prevents bacteremia, pneumonia, septic arthritis and cellulitis caused by Hib."], ["Injection site", "Redness, pain and swelling reflect local immune activation."]], "journey": "Hib vaccine is injected into the thigh in infants or deltoid in older children, often as part of combination shots such as Pentacel or Vaxelis. The conjugate stays in the muscle and drains to lymph nodes. Antibody rises one to two weeks after each dose in the series and is high a month after the primary series. Immune cells break down the sugar and protein. Long-term protection follows the toddler booster.", "sidefx": "Because the vaccine contains only a sugar joined to a protein, it cannot cause Hib infection. Local redness and pain, fussiness and low fever are short immune responses. Allergic reactions are rare. The carrier protein can boost tetanus antibody, but the vaccine does not replace tetanus immunization.", "fun": "Epiglottitis, once a common pediatric emergency, has become so rare in vaccinated countries that many emergency physicians train for years without seeing a child with it.", "src": ["ActHIB FDA label", "Schneerson R, Robbins JB et al. J Exp Med 1980", "CDC Pink Book", "Plotkin's Vaccines"]}, "polio-vaccine-inactivated": {"origin": "In 1949 John Enders, Thomas Weller and Frederick Robbins grew poliovirus in non-nerve human cells, which made large-scale virus production possible and earned them the 1954 Nobel Prize. Jonas Salk at the University of Pittsburgh then grew all three poliovirus types and killed them with formaldehyde. A huge field trial in 1954 led by Thomas Francis involved over a million children, and on April 12, 1955 the vaccine was declared safe and effective. Albert Sabin's live oral vaccine later replaced it in many countries, but it can rarely revert and cause paralysis. An enhanced-potency inactivated vaccine came in 1987, and in 2000 the US switched fully to inactivated vaccine. IPOL is now the only polio vaccine used in the US.", "timeline": [["1949", "Enders, Weller and Robbins grow poliovirus in tissue culture."], ["1954", "The Francis field trial tests the Salk vaccine."], ["1955", "The Salk inactivated polio vaccine is licensed."], ["1987", "Enhanced-potency IPV is licensed in the US."], ["2000", "The US uses inactivated polio vaccine exclusively."]], "cell": "IPV contains poliovirus types 1, 2 and 3 grown in Vero monkey kidney cells and inactivated with formaldehyde, so the capsid shape is kept but the virus cannot replicate. After injection, antigen-presenting cells process capsid proteins and present them to helper T cells, which help B cells make neutralizing IgG against each type. Poliovirus normally enters through the gut, multiplies in intestinal lymphoid tissue, enters the blood, and then can invade motor neurons in the spinal cord's anterior horn. Serum IgG neutralizes the virus in the blood, preventing that spread to the nervous system. Because the shot does not infect the gut, it makes less intestinal IgA than oral live vaccine, so it protects the person very well but blocks gut infection less.", "body": [["Immune system", "Gives neutralizing antibodies to all three types in about 99% of people after the series."], ["Spinal cord", "Stopping the virus in the blood prevents destruction of anterior horn motor neurons, which causes paralysis."], ["Gut", "Intestinal immunity is weaker than with live oral vaccine, so a vaccinated person can still carry the virus briefly in the gut."], ["Injection site", "Redness, tenderness and swelling from local inflammation are the most common effects."]], "journey": "IPV is given as a shot into muscle or under the skin, usually in combination vaccines for infants. The inactivated virus stays near the site and drains to lymph nodes, where immune cells process it. Antibodies rise within weeks after the second dose and are high about a month after the series. Immune cells break the proteins down. Protection after a full series is likely lifelong.", "sidefx": "Because nothing in IPV can replicate, it cannot cause vaccine-associated paralytic polio, the rare problem with live oral vaccine that drove the US switch. Side effects are local redness and soreness and occasional fever or fussiness. The vaccine may contain trace neomycin, streptomycin and polymyxin B, so severe allergy to these is a reason for caution. Anaphylaxis is rare.", "fun": "When asked who owned the patent on his vaccine, Jonas Salk famously answered that there was no patent, comparing it to patenting the sun.", "src": ["IPOL FDA label", "Francis T et al. Am J Public Health 1955", "CDC Pink Book", "Plotkin's Vaccines"]}, "rotavirus-vaccine": {"origin": "Rotavirus was discovered in 1973 by Ruth Bishop and colleagues in Melbourne, who saw it in intestinal biopsies from children with diarrhea. It became clear that it was the leading cause of severe diarrhea in infants worldwide. The first US vaccine, RotaShield, was approved in 1998 but withdrawn in 1999 because it raised the risk of intussusception, a bowel blockage. RotaTeq grew from work by H. Fred Clark, Paul Offit and Stanley Plotkin at the Children's Hospital of Philadelphia, who combined a cow rotavirus with human surface genes; Merck brought it to approval in 2006. Rotarix, based on a human strain isolated in Cincinnati by Richard Ward and David Bernstein, was approved in 2008. US rotavirus hospitalizations dropped sharply after routine use.", "timeline": [["1973", "Ruth Bishop's team discovers rotavirus in Melbourne."], ["1998", "RotaShield is approved in the US."], ["1999", "RotaShield is withdrawn for intussusception risk."], ["2006", "FDA approves RotaTeq after a trial of over 70,000 infants."], ["2008", "FDA approves Rotarix."]], "cell": "Rotavirus infects mature enterocytes at the tips of small intestinal villi. RotaTeq contains five live reassortant viruses built on a bovine (WC3) backbone that grows poorly in humans, each carrying a human outer capsid gene: VP7 (G1, G2, G3, G4) or VP4 (P1A[8]). Rotarix contains one live attenuated human G1P[8] strain. After the drops are swallowed, vaccine virus infects some enterocytes and replicates limitedly. Gut dendritic cells and M cells over Peyer's patches deliver antigen to lymphoid tissue, where helper T cells drive B cells to make secretory IgA and serum IgG against VP7 and VP4. Secretory IgA in the gut neutralizes wild virus at the surface, before it can damage villi.", "body": [["Small intestine", "Mucosal IgA protects villi from wild rotavirus, preventing the severe watery diarrhea that comes from damaged absorbing cells and the viral toxin NSP4."], ["Whole body (fluid balance)", "Preventing severe diarrhea and vomiting prevents dehydration, the main cause of rotavirus hospitalization and death."], ["Gut (side effects)", "Mild irritability, diarrhea or vomiting can follow a dose as vaccine virus copies itself in the gut."], ["Bowel (rare)", "A small rise in intussusception risk, mostly in the week after the first dose, likely relates to swelling of gut lymphoid tissue that can act as a lead point."], ["Stool", "Vaccine virus is shed in stool for days to weeks, so careful hand washing after diaper changes protects people with weak immunity."]], "journey": "The vaccine is given as liquid drops into the mouth; there is no injection. Vaccine virus passes through the stomach (buffers protect it from acid) and replicates in the small intestine. It is shed in stool, most in the first week after the first dose. Immunity builds over two to four weeks after the series. The virus is cleared by the immune system, not by the liver or kidneys. The series must start and finish early in infancy, partly because intussusception naturally becomes more common in older infants.", "sidefx": "Mild diarrhea, vomiting and fussiness come from the vaccine virus infecting gut cells. Intussusception is the key rare risk: inflamed lymphoid tissue in the bowel wall may let one segment of bowel telescope into the next, so signs such as severe crying, drawing up of the legs, vomiting or bloody stool need urgent care. Infants with severe combined immunodeficiency cannot clear the live virus and may develop prolonged severe diarrhea, which is why SCID is a contraindication.", "fun": "Rotavirus got its name from the Latin rota, wheel, because the particles look like tiny wheels under the electron microscope.", "src": ["RotaTeq FDA label", "Rotarix FDA label", "Bishop RF et al. Lancet 1973", "Vesikari T et al. N Engl J Med 2006", "CDC Pink Book"]}, "rabies-vaccine": {"origin": "Rabies vaccine is one of the oldest in medicine. In 1885 Louis Pasteur treated nine-year-old Joseph Meister, who had been bitten by a rabid dog, with a series of injections of dried rabid rabbit spinal cord. The boy survived, and post-exposure vaccination was born. Early nerve tissue vaccines, however, could cause serious brain inflammation. In the 1960s and 1970s Hilary Koprowski, Tadeusz Wiktor and Stanley Plotkin at the Wistar Institute grew rabies virus in human diploid cells, producing a purer, safer vaccine. The human diploid cell vaccine (HDCV, later Imovax) was licensed in the US in 1980. The purified chick embryo cell vaccine, RabAvert, followed in 1997.", "timeline": [["1885", "Louis Pasteur vaccinates Joseph Meister after a rabid dog bite."], ["1960s", "Wistar Institute scientists grow rabies virus in human diploid cells."], ["1980", "Human diploid cell rabies vaccine is licensed in the US."], ["1997", "RabAvert (PCECV) is licensed in the US."], ["2009", "ACIP reduces the post-exposure series for immunocompetent people to four doses."]], "cell": "The vaccine contains rabies virus grown in cell culture and killed with beta-propiolactone. Its key antigen is the G glycoprotein that forms spikes on the virus. Antigen-presenting cells process G protein and present it to helper T cells, which help B cells make neutralizing IgG. After a bite, rabies virus first replicates in muscle near the wound, then binds nicotinic acetylcholine receptors and other receptors at the neuromuscular junction, enters nerve endings and travels slowly up the axons to the brain. Neutralizing antibody must bind the virus before it enters nerves, because once inside neurons it is hidden from antibody. Since antibody takes about 7 to 14 days to appear, rabies immune globulin is infiltrated into the wound to cover that gap in people never vaccinated before.", "body": [["Immune system", "Makes neutralizing antibody to rabies G protein within about two weeks, plus memory for rapid boosting after future exposures."], ["Nervous system", "Blocks the virus before it climbs nerves to the brain, where rabies causes encephalitis that is almost always fatal."], ["Injection site", "Pain, redness and swelling in the deltoid reflect local immune activation."], ["Whole body", "Headache, nausea, muscle aches and dizziness are common and brief."], ["Immune complexes (boosters)", "A serum sickness-like reaction with hives, joint pain and fever can occur days after HDCV boosters, from immune complexes forming with altered albumin in the vaccine."]], "journey": "The vaccine is injected into the deltoid in adults or thigh in young children, never the buttock, because gluteal fat gives weaker responses and has been linked to vaccine failures. The killed virus stays at the site and drains to lymph nodes. Antibody begins about a week after the first dose and is clearly present by about day 14. Nothing replicates, and immune cells break the protein down. Rabies immune globulin, when needed, is given at a different site with a different syringe so it does not neutralize the vaccine.", "sidefx": "Arm pain, swelling and redness are local immune responses to the killed virus. Headache, nausea and aches are brief cytokine effects. Serum sickness-like reactions after HDCV boosters come from immune complexes. Guillain-Barre syndrome has been reported rarely. There is no reversal agent, and none is needed. Older nerve-tissue vaccines caused autoimmune brain inflammation because they contained myelin; modern cell-culture vaccines removed that problem.", "fun": "Joseph Meister later worked as a caretaker at the Pasteur Institute in Paris for many years.", "src": ["Imovax Rabies FDA label", "RabAvert FDA label", "CDC ACIP Rabies Recommendations 2010", "Plotkin's Vaccines"]}, "mpox-vaccine": {"origin": "Jynneos traces back to the smallpox eradication era. In the 1950s to 1970s Anton Mayr in Germany passed a vaccinia strain called Ankara more than 570 times through chicken embryo cells. The resulting modified vaccinia Ankara (MVA) lost a large part of its genome and could no longer complete its life cycle in human cells, and it was given to over 100,000 people in Germany in the 1970s as a safer pre-vaccine before standard smallpox vaccination. The Danish company Bavarian Nordic developed the MVA-BN strain with US government support for biodefense. The FDA approved Jynneos in 2019 for smallpox and monkeypox (mpox) prevention. It became central to the response during the 2022 global mpox outbreak.", "timeline": [["1796", "Edward Jenner uses cowpox to protect against smallpox."], ["1970s", "Anton Mayr's modified vaccinia Ankara is used in Germany."], ["1980", "The WHO declares smallpox eradicated."], ["2019", "FDA approves Jynneos for smallpox and monkeypox prevention."], ["2022", "Emergency use authorization allows intradermal dose sparing during the mpox outbreak."]], "cell": "MVA-BN is a live vaccinia virus that has lost the genes it needs to finish replicating in human cells. It still enters cells and expresses its early and late proteins, but new infectious virus is not assembled. Infected cells and dendritic cells present these proteins on MHC class I and II, so both CD8 cytotoxic T cells and CD4 helper T cells are activated, and B cells make neutralizing antibodies. Orthopoxviruses, including vaccinia, variola (smallpox) and monkeypox, share many surface and core proteins. Antibodies and T cells made against vaccinia proteins therefore cross-react with mpox and smallpox viruses, neutralizing virus and killing infected cells.", "body": [["Immune system", "Builds antibodies and T cells that cross-protect against mpox and smallpox, with peak protection about two weeks after the second dose."], ["Skin", "Unlike older smallpox vaccines, it does not create a spreading pustule at the site, because the virus cannot complete replication."], ["Injection site", "Pain, redness, swelling and a firm lump are common local reactions."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue, headache, muscle aches and nausea reflect immune signaling for a few days."]], "journey": "Jynneos is a frozen suspension that is thawed and injected under the skin, usually in the upper arm, as two doses about four weeks apart. The virus enters cells near the site and in lymph nodes but does not produce new infectious virus. Immunity builds over weeks. The virus and its proteins are cleared by the immune system. How long protection lasts is still being studied.", "sidefx": "Local pain and firmness come from vaccinia proteins activating the immune system at the site. Body aches and fatigue are cytokine effects. Because MVA-BN cannot replicate in humans, it lacks the serious risks of older replicating vaccinia vaccines such as ACAM2000, which include spread of virus to other body sites or contacts, myocarditis and severe skin infection in people with eczema. Allergic reactions are rare; the vaccine may contain traces of chicken protein, gentamicin and ciprofloxacin.", "fun": "Modified vaccinia Ankara lost about 15% of its original genome during its hundreds of passages through chicken cells, which is why it cannot complete its life cycle in human cells.", "src": ["Jynneos FDA label", "Mayr A et al. Infection 1975", "CDC mpox vaccine guidance", "Plotkin's Vaccines"]}, "tuberculin-ppd": {"origin": "In 1890 Robert Koch, who had found the tuberculosis bacterium in 1882, announced tuberculin, a glycerin extract of TB cultures, as a cure. It failed as a treatment, but doctors noticed that infected people reacted strongly to it. In 1907 Clemens von Pirquet used it as a skin test, and in 1908 Charles Mantoux introduced injection into the skin, the method still used. Crude tuberculin varied from batch to batch. In the 1930s biochemist Florence Seibert purified the active proteins into a purified protein derivative (PPD). Her batch PPD-S became the US standard and later an international standard. Tubersol and Aplisol are today's US PPD products.", "timeline": [["1882", "Robert Koch identifies Mycobacterium tuberculosis."], ["1890", "Koch introduces tuberculin as a proposed treatment."], ["1907", "Clemens von Pirquet uses tuberculin as a skin test."], ["1908", "Charles Mantoux describes the intradermal test."], ["1930s", "Florence Seibert develops purified protein derivative (PPD)."], ["1952", "Seibert's PPD-S is adopted as the international standard."]], "cell": "PPD is a mix of small proteins from M. tuberculosis. Injected into the dermis, it is taken up by skin dendritic cells, which present peptides on MHC class II. In a person previously exposed to mycobacteria, memory Th1 CD4 T cells recognize these peptides. They release interferon-gamma, TNF and chemokines, which bring in and activate macrophages and more lymphocytes. This cell build-up and fluid leak produce a firm raised area (induration) that peaks 48 to 72 hours later, a classic type IV delayed-type hypersensitivity reaction. Without prior sensitization, there are no memory T cells, so little happens. The test shows immune memory, not whether TB is active.", "body": [["Skin", "A firm raised bump forms at 48 to 72 hours in sensitized people; the width of the induration, not the redness, is measured."], ["Immune system", "Reveals T-cell memory from TB infection, BCG vaccination or some environmental mycobacteria, which can cause false-positive results."], ["Immune system (false negatives)", "Severe immune suppression, HIV, recent live-virus vaccines, overwhelming TB or very recent infection can blunt the reaction (anergy)."], ["Skin (strong reactions)", "Very sensitized people can get blistering, ulceration or tissue breakdown at the site from intense T-cell inflammation."]], "journey": "A tiny amount is injected just under the top layer of skin on the inner forearm with a bevel-up needle, raising a pale wheal. It is not given into muscle or vein. The proteins stay in the skin, where immune cells process them. Nothing meaningful reaches the blood. Induration develops over 24 to 48 hours, peaks at 48 to 72 hours, and fades over days. The protein is broken down locally by immune cells.", "sidefx": "PPD is not a treatment and has no effect on TB. Local redness and itching reflect the immune reaction itself. Blistering or ulceration happen in very sensitized people because many activated T cells and macrophages gather at the site. The test cannot give TB because it contains no living bacteria. Anaphylaxis is rare. The main practical problems are false positives (BCG, non-tuberculous mycobacteria) and false negatives (anergy), which interferon-gamma blood tests help address.", "fun": "Florence Seibert also solved a different problem early in her career: she found that fevers after IV infusions came from bacterial contamination of distilled water and developed a way to prevent it.", "src": ["Tubersol FDA label", "Aplisol FDA label", "CDC Core Curriculum on Tuberculosis", "Mantoux C. 1908"]}, "eteplirsen": {"origin": "Duchenne muscular dystrophy (DMD) is caused by mutations in the huge dystrophin gene, often deletions that throw the genetic reading frame off so no working protein is made. Boys with Becker dystrophy have in-frame deletions and make a shorter, partly working protein, with much milder disease. That observation led researchers in the 1990s and 2000s to the idea of exon skipping: use a short antisense molecule to hide one exon so the cell splices around it and restores the reading frame. Eteplirsen uses the phosphorodiamidate morpholino oligomer (PMO) chemistry first developed by James Summerton, whose company later became Sarepta Therapeutics. The FDA granted accelerated approval in 2016 based on a small rise in muscle dystrophin. The decision was controversial because clinical benefit had not been shown, and confirmatory trials are required.", "timeline": [["1986", "The dystrophin gene is identified as the cause of DMD."], ["1980s", "James Summerton begins developing morpholino antisense chemistry."], ["2016", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Exondys 51, the first DMD exon-skipping drug."]], "cell": "Eteplirsen is a 30-base PMO, an antisense strand whose backbone uses morpholine rings and phosphorodiamidate links instead of sugar and phosphate. This makes it uncharged and resistant to nucleases. Inside the nucleus of muscle cells, it binds by base pairing to a sequence in exon 51 of dystrophin pre-mRNA. Binding blocks the splicing machinery from recognizing that exon, so the spliceosome joins exon 50 (or the exon before the deletion) directly to exon 52. In patients whose deletion makes exon 51 skipping restore the reading frame (about 13% of DMD), the mRNA can now be translated into an internally shortened dystrophin, similar to that seen in Becker dystrophy. Unlike some antisense drugs, a PMO does not trigger RNase H, so the RNA is redirected, not destroyed.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "A small amount of shortened dystrophin is made, which in theory helps link the muscle cytoskeleton to the cell membrane and protects fibers from damage during contraction."], ["Heart muscle", "Dystrophin is also missing in the heart in DMD, but uptake of eteplirsen into heart muscle is low and cardiac benefit has not been shown."], ["Nervous system", "Balance problems and headache have been reported in trials."], ["Skin", "Contact dermatitis at the infusion site area has been reported."], ["Immune system", "Hypersensitivity reactions such as rash, fever, flushing and bronchospasm can occur during infusion."]], "journey": "Eteplirsen is given as a weekly IV infusion. It does not enter cells easily because it is large and uncharged, so only a small fraction reaches muscle nuclei; damaged dystrophic muscle membranes may let more in. It is only weakly bound to plasma proteins and is not broken down by CYP enzymes or much at all. It leaves mostly unchanged in urine, with a plasma half-life of about three to four hours. Exon skipping and dystrophin build up slowly over months of weekly dosing.", "sidefx": "Because the PMO backbone is uncharged and not recognized by many protein-binding systems, eteplirsen has fewer of the platelet and kidney effects seen with some charged antisense drugs. The main reported effects are balance disorder, vomiting, upper respiratory infections and contact dermatitis. Hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis, can happen during infusion because the immune system may react to the infused product. Related exon-skipping PMOs (golodirsen, viltolarsen) carry kidney toxicity warnings seen in animals, but that is not a labeled warning for eteplirsen. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "The amount of dystrophin produced in the eteplirsen studies was under 1% of normal on average, which is why the approval became one of the most debated FDA decisions of the decade.", "src": ["Exondys 51 (eteplirsen) FDA label", "FDA Summary Review for Exondys 51, 2016", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "deflazacort": {"origin": "Deflazacort is a synthetic steroid made by modifying prednisolone, adding an oxazoline ring to the steroid's D-ring. It was developed in Italy in the late 1960s and 1970s and was used in Europe and elsewhere for many years for inflammatory conditions. Doctors treating Duchenne muscular dystrophy noticed it seemed to preserve strength like prednisone, with possibly less weight gain. In the US it had never been approved until Marathon Pharmaceuticals ran trials in DMD, and the FDA approved Emflaza in 2017 for boys five and older, later extended to age two. PTC Therapeutics acquired the drug soon after. Its high US price, for an old drug that some families had imported cheaply, drew public criticism.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Deflazacort is first synthesized in Italy as a prednisolone derivative."], ["1980s", "Deflazacort is used in Europe for inflammatory diseases."], ["2017", "FDA approves Emflaza for Duchenne muscular dystrophy."], ["2019", "Approval is extended to children 2 years and older."]], "cell": "Deflazacort is a prodrug. Esterases rapidly remove its acetyl group to form 21-desacetyl deflazacort, the active compound. This molecule diffuses into cells and binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor, which releases its heat shock protein partners and moves to the nucleus. There the receptor binds glucocorticoid response elements to switch on anti-inflammatory genes (such as annexin-1 and IkB-alpha) and tethers to NF-kB and AP-1 to switch off inflammatory genes. In dystrophic muscle, where fibers tear and leak, this reduces cytokine release, immune cell infiltration and the cycle of inflammation and fibrosis, slowing the loss of muscle. Other effects, such as membrane stabilization and improved regeneration, are proposed but less certain.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Less inflammation in damaged muscle slows loss of strength and delays loss of walking in DMD."], ["Metabolism", "Raises blood sugar by increasing liver glucose production and reducing insulin sensitivity; also increases appetite and fat in the trunk and face (cushingoid look)."], ["Bones", "Reduces bone formation and calcium absorption, raising the risk of osteoporosis and fractures, a big concern in DMD."], ["Growth", "Suppresses growth hormone signaling and growth plates, so children grow more slowly."], ["Adrenal glands", "Suppresses the HPA axis; stopping suddenly can cause adrenal crisis."], ["Eyes", "Long-term use can cause cataracts and raise eye pressure."], ["Brain and behavior", "Mood swings and behavior changes come from glucocorticoid effects on the brain."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth as tablets or liquid, deflazacort is quickly absorbed and converted by esterases to its active metabolite, which peaks in about an hour. The active form is about 40% protein bound. It is further broken down by CYP3A4 in the liver, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels and inducers lower them. Metabolites are mainly excreted in urine. The active metabolite half-life is short, about one to two hours, but gene-level effects last much longer, so once-daily dosing works.", "sidefx": "Nearly all of deflazacort's side effects are standard glucocorticoid receptor effects throughout the body. Gene changes in liver, fat and muscle cause weight gain, high blood sugar and the cushingoid look. Reduced osteoblast activity causes bone loss and fractures. Immune suppression raises infection risk and can mask infection. HPA axis suppression means the body cannot make enough cortisol during stress or after sudden withdrawal, which can cause adrenal crisis. Serious skin reactions such as toxic epidermal necrolysis have been reported. There is no antidote; doses are tapered when stopping.", "fun": "Some families in the US had imported deflazacort from abroad for years before it was approved, which made the later high US price especially controversial.", "src": ["Emflaza (deflazacort) FDA label", "Griggs RC et al. Neurology 2016", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "omaveloxolone": {"origin": "Omaveloxolone comes from a family of synthetic triterpenoids built on oleanolic acid, a natural compound found in plants such as olive leaves. In the 1990s Michael Sporn and chemist Gordon Gribble at Dartmouth modified oleanolic acid and created far more potent molecules, such as CDDO, that strongly switch on the Nrf2 antioxidant pathway. Reata Pharmaceuticals developed omaveloxolone from this family and tested it in Friedreich ataxia, a genetic disease in which low frataxin protein damages mitochondria and boosts oxidative stress in nerves and heart. After the MOXIe trial, the FDA approved Skyclarys in 2023 as the first treatment for Friedreich ataxia. Biogen acquired Reata later that year.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Sporn and Gribble at Dartmouth develop synthetic oleanane triterpenoids."], ["1996", "The frataxin gene defect behind Friedreich ataxia is identified."], ["2021", "MOXIe trial results are published."], ["2023", "FDA approves Skyclarys, the first drug for Friedreich ataxia."]], "cell": "Under normal conditions the transcription factor Nrf2 is held in the cytoplasm by Keap1, which tags it with ubiquitin for destruction by the proteasome, so Nrf2 levels stay low. Omaveloxolone reacts with specific cysteine residues on Keap1, changing its shape so it can no longer target Nrf2. Nrf2 then builds up, moves into the nucleus and binds antioxidant response elements. This switches on many protective genes, including those for glutathione synthesis, NQO1, heme oxygenase-1 and other enzymes that neutralize reactive oxygen species and support mitochondrial function. In Friedreich ataxia, Nrf2 signaling is impaired, so restoring it is thought to protect neurons and improve energy production. The exact mechanism of clinical benefit is unknown per the label.", "body": [["Nervous system", "Slows the decline in coordination, speech, swallowing and walking measured on the Friedreich Ataxia Rating Scale."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes often rise, possibly from Nrf2-driven changes in liver metabolism; they usually return toward normal over time."], ["Heart", "BNP can rise and fluid overload or heart failure have been reported; caution is needed because many Friedreich ataxia patients have cardiomyopathy."], ["Lipids", "LDL cholesterol can rise and HDL can fall during treatment."], ["Gut and weight", "Nausea, abdominal pain, diarrhea, lower appetite and weight loss are common."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth on an empty stomach, omaveloxolone is absorbed with a slow peak at about 7 to 14 hours. Food, especially high fat, increases absorption, which is why it is taken fasting. It is about 97% protein bound and is broken down mostly by CYP3A4 in the liver, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors and inducers are avoided or adjusted. It is eliminated mainly in feces. The half-life is long, about 57 hours, which supports once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Liver enzyme rises likely relate to strong activation of Nrf2 target genes in the liver, so enzymes are checked before and during treatment. BNP rises and fluid problems may reflect effects on the heart and fluid handling in people who often already have Friedreich cardiomyopathy. Changes in cholesterol are thought to relate to altered liver lipid metabolism. Nausea, diarrhea and weight loss are common. Because it is metabolized by CYP3A4, interacting drugs can raise or lower levels. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Friedreich ataxia was described by German physician Nikolaus Friedreich in the 1860s, but no approved treatment existed for over 150 years until omaveloxolone.", "src": ["Skyclarys (omaveloxolone) FDA label", "Lynch DR et al. Ann Neurol 2021 (MOXIe)", "Liby KT, Sporn MB. Pharmacol Rev 2012"]}, "trofinetide": {"origin": "Rett syndrome was described by Austrian pediatrician Andreas Rett in 1966. It mostly affects girls, who develop normally for months and then lose speech and hand skills. In 1999 Huda Zoghbi's lab linked it to mutations in the MECP2 gene. Trofinetide traces back to research on insulin-like growth factor 1 (IGF-1). IGF-1 is cut to release a small three-amino-acid piece, glycine-proline-glutamate (GPE), which showed brain-protective effects in animal studies. The New Zealand company Neuren Pharmaceuticals made trofinetide, a modified GPE that lasts longer in the body. Acadia Pharmaceuticals ran the LAVENDER trial, and the FDA approved Daybue in 2023 as the first drug for Rett syndrome.", "timeline": [["1966", "Andreas Rett describes the syndrome."], ["1999", "MECP2 mutations are identified as the cause of Rett syndrome."], ["2023", "FDA approves Daybue (trofinetide), the first Rett syndrome treatment."]], "cell": "Trofinetide is a synthetic analog of glycine-proline-glutamate, with a methyl group added to the proline that protects it from rapid breakdown. Its exact mechanism in Rett syndrome is unknown. In animal and cell studies, GPE-like peptides reduce activation of microglia and the release of inflammatory cytokines, regulate IGF-1 signaling (including the balance of free IGF-1 and its binding proteins), and support synapse maturation and dendritic spine density. In MeCP2-deficient mice, IGF-1 pathway treatments improved synaptic structure and function. Trofinetide is thought to act through these pathways to improve communication between neurons, but this is proposed rather than proven in humans.", "body": [["Brain", "Modest improvements in caregiver-rated behavior, communication and overall clinical impression were seen in the trial, thought to reflect better synaptic function."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is very common, affecting most patients; vomiting is also frequent."], ["Body fluids", "Ongoing diarrhea and vomiting can cause dehydration."], ["Weight", "Weight loss can happen from diarrhea, vomiting and decreased appetite."]], "journey": "Trofinetide is given by mouth or by gastrostomy tube as a liquid. It is absorbed well, peaking in about two to three hours. It barely binds plasma proteins and is not much metabolized by the liver. It leaves mostly unchanged in the urine, so kidney function affects levels. The half-life is short, about an hour and a half, which is why it is given twice daily. Benefits appear over weeks.", "sidefx": "The main side effect is diarrhea, which is thought to come from the large amount of drug and its liquid vehicle drawing water into the gut, though the exact cause is not fully defined. Vomiting can lead to aspiration in children with swallowing problems. Both can cause dehydration and weight loss. Seizures and fever were also reported, although seizures are common in Rett syndrome itself. Antidiarrheal and fluid strategies are used per the label. There is no antidote.", "fun": "In mice, restoring the MECP2 gene after symptoms began reversed many Rett-like problems, a 2007 finding showing the brain in Rett syndrome is not permanently damaged.", "src": ["Daybue (trofinetide) FDA label", "Neul JL et al. Nat Med 2023 (LAVENDER)", "Amir RE, Zoghbi HY et al. Nat Genet 1999"]}, "epinephrine-nasal": {"origin": "Epinephrine was one of the first hormones ever purified. In 1901 Jokichi Takamine and his assistant Keizo Uenaka isolated it from adrenal glands, after earlier work by John Jacob Abel, and it was sold as Adrenalin. It became the main treatment for anaphylaxis, and auto-injectors such as the EpiPen, approved in 1987, put it in patients' pockets. Fear of needles made many people delay using it. ARS Pharmaceuticals developed neffy, a nasal spray that uses dodecyl maltoside, an absorption enhancer, to help epinephrine pass through the nasal lining. The FDA approved neffy in 2024 as the first needle-free epinephrine product for anaphylaxis.", "timeline": [["1901", "Jokichi Takamine isolates epinephrine from adrenal glands."], ["1987", "FDA approves the EpiPen auto-injector."], ["2023", "FDA initially asks for more data on neffy."], ["2024", "FDA approves neffy, the first nasal epinephrine for anaphylaxis."], ["2025", "A lower-strength neffy is approved for younger children."]], "cell": "Epinephrine is a nonselective agonist at alpha-1, alpha-2, beta-1 and beta-2 adrenergic receptors, all G protein-coupled receptors. At alpha-1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle, Gq activation raises IP3 and intracellular calcium, causing vasoconstriction that raises blood pressure and shrinks swelling. At beta-1 receptors in the heart, Gs activation raises cAMP and protein kinase A activity, opening calcium channels to increase heart rate and contractility. At beta-2 receptors, Gs and cAMP relax bronchial smooth muscle and dilate some vessels. Raised cAMP in mast cells and basophils also reduces release of histamine and leukotrienes, slowing the allergic cascade. In the nose, local alpha-1 vasoconstriction might limit absorption, which is why the spray includes an absorption enhancer.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 constriction raises blood pressure and reduces leaky-vessel swelling of the lips, tongue and throat."], ["Heart", "Beta-1 stimulation increases heart rate and force, improving circulation in anaphylactic shock."], ["Lungs", "Beta-2 relaxation of airway smooth muscle reverses wheezing and bronchospasm."], ["Immune cells", "Raised cAMP in mast cells reduces further release of histamine and other mediators."], ["Skin", "Hives and flushing fade as vessels constrict and mediator release falls."], ["Nose", "Local nasal irritation, discomfort and runny nose are common after the spray."]], "journey": "The spray is delivered into one nostril and absorbed through the nasal lining into the blood, helped by the enhancer dodecyl maltoside. Blood levels rise within minutes and peak in roughly 20 to 30 minutes, broadly comparable to injected epinephrine in studies. In the body, epinephrine is quickly broken down by COMT and MAO in the liver and other tissues, with a half-life of only a few minutes in the blood. Metabolites such as metanephrine and vanillylmandelic acid leave in urine. Effects fade over about one to two hours, so a second dose may be needed.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from adrenergic stimulation. Beta-1 effects cause fast heart rate and palpitations. Alpha-1 effects raise blood pressure. Beta-2 effects on skeletal muscle cause tremor. Central and sympathetic activation produces anxiety, jitteriness and headache. In people with heart disease, the extra oxygen demand can rarely cause arrhythmias or ischemia, but the benefit in anaphylaxis is life saving. Nasal effects such as throat irritation and paresthesia come from the spray itself. There is no reversal agent, and effects end quickly because of rapid breakdown.", "fun": "Takamine's patent and trade name Adrenalin are why epinephrine is still called adrenaline in much of the world, while US references use epinephrine, the name Abel used.", "src": ["Neffy (epinephrine nasal spray) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Takamine J. Am J Pharm 1901"]}, "azelastine-fluticasone": {"origin": "This nasal spray joins two drugs with separate histories. Azelastine, a phthalazinone antihistamine, was developed by the German company Asta Medica and approved as a nasal spray (Astelin) in the US in 1996. Fluticasone propionate was developed by Glaxo in England in the 1980s as a steroid that works strongly on the nasal lining but is mostly destroyed by the liver when swallowed; Flonase was approved in 1994. The company Meda combined both in a single spray, Dymista, which the FDA approved in 2012. Trials showed the pair relieved allergy symptoms better than either drug alone.", "timeline": [["1994", "FDA approves Flonase (fluticasone propionate nasal spray)."], ["1996", "FDA approves Astelin (azelastine nasal spray)."], ["2012", "FDA approves Dymista (azelastine and fluticasone)."]], "cell": "Azelastine is an H1 receptor inverse agonist. Histamine released from mast cells normally activates Gq-coupled H1 receptors on nerve endings, blood vessels and glands, causing itch, sneezing, leaky vessels and runny nose. Azelastine stabilizes the inactive H1 receptor, blocking those effects within about 30 minutes. It also reduces mast cell mediator release and some inflammatory signals. Fluticasone propionate enters nasal lining cells and binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor. The complex moves into the nucleus, turning on anti-inflammatory genes and blocking NF-kB and AP-1 driven production of cytokines such as IL-4, IL-5 and IL-13. Over days this reduces eosinophils, mast cells and T cells in the lining and calms the late-phase allergic reaction, which is the main driver of congestion.", "body": [["Nose", "Azelastine quickly reduces sneezing, itch and runny nose, while fluticasone reduces swelling and congestion over days."], ["Eyes", "Less nasal inflammation can also reduce some allergic eye symptoms through the nasal-ocular reflex."], ["Mouth (taste)", "A bitter taste is common, because azelastine drips down the back of the throat."], ["Nasal lining (local)", "Nosebleeds, irritation and rarely ulcers or septal perforation can occur from the steroid thinning the lining."], ["Brain", "A little azelastine reaches the blood and can cause drowsiness."], ["Adrenal glands", "Steroid absorption is very low, but strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ritonavir can raise fluticasone levels enough to cause Cushing syndrome and adrenal suppression."]], "journey": "The spray coats the nasal lining, where both drugs act locally. Some is swallowed. Azelastine absorbed from the nose and gut is broken down by CYP3A4, 2D6 and 1A2 to active desmethylazelastine, which has a long half-life of about two days; the parent half-life is about 25 hours. Swallowed fluticasone is almost entirely destroyed by CYP3A4 on its first pass through the liver, so very little reaches the circulation. Both are cleared mainly in feces. Azelastine works within about 30 minutes; the steroid's full effect builds over days.", "sidefx": "Bitter taste comes from azelastine, which is very bitter, draining into the throat. Drowsiness comes from azelastine reaching the brain, which is more noticeable with alcohol or sedatives. Nosebleeds, irritation and rare septal perforation reflect the steroid's effects on the nasal lining, which also slows healing. Nasal Candida infection can occur because local immune defenses are reduced. Systemic steroid effects are rare unless fluticasone breakdown is blocked by strong CYP3A4 inhibitors. Long-term steroid use can rarely raise eye pressure or cause cataracts.", "fun": "Fluticasone's first-pass liver breakdown is so complete that less than 1% of a swallowed dose reaches the blood, the key design trick that made intranasal steroids safe for long-term use.", "src": ["Dymista FDA label", "Flonase FDA label", "Astelin FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "olopatadine-mometasone": {"origin": "Olopatadine was developed by the Japanese company Kyowa Hakko from the tricyclic dibenzoxepin family and licensed to Alcon, which launched it as Patanol eye drops in the US in 1996 and as Patanase nasal spray in 2008. Mometasone furoate was developed by Schering-Plough as a potent topical steroid, first as a skin cream and then as Nasonex nasal spray, approved in 1997. The Indian company Glenmark combined the two in Ryaltris, which the FDA approved in 2022 for seasonal allergic rhinitis in people 12 and older. It became the second US nasal spray to pair an antihistamine with a steroid, after Dymista.", "timeline": [["1996", "FDA approves olopatadine eye drops (Patanol)."], ["1997", "FDA approves Nasonex (mometasone furoate nasal spray)."], ["2008", "FDA approves Patanase (olopatadine nasal spray)."], ["2022", "FDA approves Ryaltris (olopatadine and mometasone)."]], "cell": "Olopatadine is a selective H1 receptor antagonist (inverse agonist). By holding H1 receptors in their inactive state, it blocks histamine from activating the Gq pathway that causes itching, sneezing, vessel leak and mucus production. It also stabilizes mast cells, reducing histamine and other mediator release. Mometasone furoate is a highly potent glucocorticoid receptor agonist. Inside nasal lining cells, it binds the receptor, which moves to the nucleus to turn on anti-inflammatory genes and suppress NF-kB and AP-1 driven cytokines and adhesion molecules. This reduces recruitment of eosinophils, mast cells and Th2 lymphocytes over days and calms the late-phase response that causes congestion.", "body": [["Nose", "Olopatadine relieves itch, sneezing and runny nose within about 15 to 30 minutes, while mometasone reduces swelling and congestion over days."], ["Mouth (taste)", "A bitter or altered taste can occur when spray drains down the throat."], ["Nasal lining (local)", "Nosebleeds and nasal discomfort are common; rare ulcers or septal perforation reflect the steroid's effect on tissue healing."], ["Brain", "Drowsiness is possible from the antihistamine, though it is less sedating than older antihistamines."], ["Adrenal glands", "Mometasone has very low systemic availability, so adrenal suppression is rare."]], "journey": "The spray acts directly on the nasal lining. Some is absorbed and some is swallowed. Absorbed olopatadine is barely metabolized and is cleared mainly unchanged in urine, with a half-life of about 8 to 12 hours. Mometasone that reaches the gut or blood is extensively broken down by CYP3A4 in the liver, so systemic levels are very low; it is cleared mainly in feces. Olopatadine starts working within minutes; mometasone's full effect builds over several days.", "sidefx": "Bitter taste reflects the antihistamine's taste after drip into the throat. Drowsiness comes from olopatadine reaching the brain, though it crosses less than older antihistamines. Nosebleeds and irritation come from the steroid reducing tissue repair and the spray stream hitting the septum. Nasal Candida can appear because local immunity is reduced. Systemic steroid effects are rare because mometasone is largely destroyed by liver metabolism, but they can occur with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors. Long-term steroid use can rarely raise eye pressure or cause cataracts.", "fun": "Olopatadine is also one of the most widely used allergy eye drops, and the same molecule went from the eye to the nose to this combination spray.", "src": ["Ryaltris FDA label", "Nasonex FDA label", "Patanase FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "sodium-polystyrene-sulfonate": {"origin": "Sodium polystyrene sulfonate is a cation-exchange resin, a type of plastic bead first developed for industry to soften water. In the 1950s doctors began using exchange resins to pull potassium out of the body through the gut in patients with kidney failure, who could not get rid of it otherwise. Kayexalate was approved in the US in 1958, before modern rules required strong efficacy trials. For decades it was often mixed with sorbitol to prevent constipation, but reports of intestinal necrosis led the FDA in 2009 to warn against that mix. The Kayexalate brand is no longer sold in the US, and generic SPS products continue. Newer binders, patiromer (2015) and sodium zirconium cyclosilicate (2018), offer alternatives.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Ion-exchange resins are first used to remove potassium in kidney failure."], ["1958", "FDA approves Kayexalate."], ["2009", "FDA warns against giving SPS with sorbitol because of intestinal necrosis."], ["2015", "Patiromer is approved as a newer potassium binder."], ["2018", "Sodium zirconium cyclosilicate is approved."]], "cell": "SPS is a large, insoluble polymer that is not absorbed. It carries negatively charged sulfonate groups, each loosely holding a sodium ion. In the gut, ions with a positive charge swap places with the sodium according to their concentration and charge. Potassium, which is secreted into the colon in large amounts, binds to the resin as sodium is released. The potassium-loaded resin then leaves in stool, removing potassium from the body. The resin is not selective, so it also binds calcium, magnesium and ammonium, and some oral drugs such as lithium and levothyroxine. In practice each gram removes less potassium than its laboratory capacity because other ions compete.", "body": [["Colon", "Potassium secreted by the colon is captured by the resin, which slowly lowers blood potassium over hours."], ["Heart", "Lower potassium reduces the risk of dangerous rhythms from hyperkalemia, but SPS acts too slowly to be the main emergency treatment."], ["Blood electrolytes", "Calcium and magnesium can fall because the resin binds them too, and potassium can fall too low."], ["Fluid balance", "Sodium released from the resin is absorbed, which can worsen fluid overload, edema and blood pressure in heart or kidney failure."], ["Gut", "Constipation, impaction, nausea and vomiting are common; rarely the bowel lining can die and perforate."]], "journey": "SPS is given by mouth or as a retention enema. It is not absorbed at all and is not metabolized. It travels through the gut, picking up potassium mainly in the colon, and leaves in the feces with the bound potassium. Onset is slow and variable, from about two to six hours or longer. Because it binds other drugs in the gut, other oral medicines are separated by several hours.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow from a nonabsorbed resin that swaps ions. Swapping sodium for potassium explains sodium retention, edema and low potassium. Binding of calcium and magnesium explains hypocalcemia and hypomagnesemia. The resin is bulky and can form hard masses, causing constipation and impaction. Intestinal necrosis is rare but serious; resin crystals have been found in damaged bowel walls, and risk is higher with sorbitol, after surgery, with poor gut movement or in kidney failure. Aspiration of resin can cause pneumonitis. There is no reversal agent; stopping the resin and supportive care are used.", "fun": "Under a microscope, SPS crystals in bowel tissue look like purple fish scales on standard staining, a pattern pathologists use to link them to bowel injury.", "src": ["Sodium polystyrene sulfonate FDA label", "FDA Drug Safety Information 2009 (SPS with sorbitol)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "magnesium-citrate": {"origin": "Saline laxatives have a long history. In 1618 a farmer near Epsom, England, is said to have noticed his cows would not drink from a bitter spring, and the water's magnesium sulfate became famous as Epsom salt. Magnesium salts were then widely used as purges. Magnesium citrate, made by reacting magnesium carbonate with citric acid, was sold as a fizzy lemon drink called citrate of magnesia from the 1800s, because it tasted better than Epsom salt. It is now an over-the-counter laxative sold under the FDA's OTC laxative rules, and it is sometimes used in bowel preparation for colonoscopy.", "timeline": [["1618", "The bitter Epsom spring in England becomes known, leading to Epsom salt (magnesium sulfate)."], ["1800s", "Citrate of magnesia becomes a popular, better-tasting saline laxative."]], "cell": "Magnesium and citrate ions are poorly absorbed from the gut. Staying in the lumen, they keep the intestinal contents more concentrated than the blood, so water is held in the gut and drawn in from the body (an osmotic effect). The extra fluid distends the bowel, stretch receptors trigger the enteric nervous system to increase peristalsis, and stool becomes soft and watery. Magnesium also stimulates release of cholecystokinin from the small intestine, which increases intestinal fluid secretion and motility. Some magnesium may also affect nitric oxide signaling in the gut wall, adding to secretion. The result is a bowel movement usually within 30 minutes to six hours.", "body": [["Intestines", "Water held in the bowel softens stool and stretches the gut wall, triggering strong peristalsis and a bowel movement."], ["Fluid balance", "Large fluid loss into the gut can cause dehydration, especially without enough fluid intake."], ["Kidneys", "The small amount of magnesium absorbed is cleared by the kidneys; in kidney failure it can build up."], ["Heart and nerves", "High magnesium levels block calcium channels and acetylcholine release, causing low blood pressure, slow heart rate, weakness and in severe cases breathing problems."], ["Electrolytes", "Diarrhea can lower potassium and change sodium levels."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth, often chilled, magnesium citrate moves through the stomach into the small intestine and colon. Most magnesium stays in the gut and leaves in the stool. A portion is absorbed and filtered out by healthy kidneys in the urine. Citrate that is absorbed is metabolized to bicarbonate. The laxative effect starts within 30 minutes to six hours and lasts several hours.", "sidefx": "Cramping, bloating and diarrhea are direct results of the osmotic water pull and strong bowel contractions. Dehydration and electrolyte loss happen because a lot of water and salt leave in the stool. In kidney failure, absorbed magnesium cannot be cleared, so blood levels rise and cause hypotension, bradycardia, loss of reflexes and respiratory depression; IV calcium counteracts the effects of high magnesium on heart and nerves, and dialysis can remove it. Magnesium can also bind some oral drugs such as tetracyclines and fluoroquinolones, reducing their absorption.", "fun": "Epsom salt and magnesium citrate work by the same osmotic principle, but the citrate version was popular because its fizzy lemon flavor hid the bitter taste of magnesium.", "src": ["FDA OTC Laxative Drug Products monograph", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "bowel-prep-peg-electrolyte": {"origin": "Before colonoscopy became routine, cleaning the colon was hard and risky. Some early methods used large volumes of salt water, which caused fluid overload, or mannitol, which bacteria can ferment into explosive gas. In 1980 a team at Baylor University Medical Center in Dallas, including Gary Davis, Carol Santa Ana, Stephen Morawski and John Fordtran, designed a lavage solution of polyethylene glycol with balanced electrolytes. PEG is a large molecule the gut cannot absorb, and the salt mix was tuned so the body neither absorbed nor lost sodium and water. GoLYTELY was approved in the 1980s and became the standard prep. NuLYTELY, with no sulfate and better taste, followed. The same idea is used for whole bowel irrigation in some poisonings.", "timeline": [["1980", "Davis, Fordtran and colleagues describe PEG-electrolyte lavage in Gastroenterology."], ["1980s", "GoLYTELY is approved and becomes a standard colonoscopy prep."], ["1990s", "Sulfate-free, better-tasting versions such as NuLYTELY become available."]], "cell": "Polyethylene glycol 3350 is a long polymer that the gut cannot absorb or break down, and bacteria cannot ferment it. Each molecule holds water through hydrogen bonds and keeps the bowel contents osmotically active, so the water swallowed with it stays in the gut. The added electrolytes (sodium sulfate, sodium bicarbonate, sodium chloride and potassium chloride) make the solution roughly equal in salt to body fluids. Sodium sulfate is poorly absorbed, which reduces sodium uptake. This means water and sodium are neither absorbed nor secreted in large amounts. The result is a large volume of fluid moving through the colon, producing watery diarrhea that washes it clean, with little change in blood electrolytes.", "body": [["Colon", "A large volume of nonabsorbed fluid flushes stool out so the lining can be seen clearly during colonoscopy."], ["Stomach", "Drinking a lot of fluid fast causes fullness, nausea, bloating and sometimes vomiting."], ["Electrolytes", "The balanced formula limits salt and water shifts, but vomiting or kidney and heart disease can still lead to imbalances."], ["Heart", "Electrolyte changes or fluid shifts can rarely trigger arrhythmias, especially in people with heart disease or on QT-prolonging drugs."], ["Anus", "Frequent watery stools cause local irritation."]], "journey": "The solution is taken by mouth in repeated glasses, or by nasogastric tube in people who cannot drink. PEG 3350 is not absorbed and is not metabolized. It travels through the small intestine and colon with its water and leaves entirely in the stool. The first bowel movement usually comes about an hour after starting. Diarrhea continues during the prep and ends a few hours after the last glass.", "sidefx": "Nausea, fullness and bloating come from rapidly filling the stomach and gut with fluid. Vomiting can lead to aspiration in people who cannot protect their airway, and forceful vomiting can cause a Mallory-Weiss tear. Although the solution is balanced, dehydration and electrolyte problems can still occur with vomiting, diuretics or kidney disease, and rarely lead to arrhythmias or seizures. Rare allergic reactions to PEG occur. It is avoided in bowel obstruction or perforation, where pushing fluid through could cause rupture. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The name GoLYTELY is a play on go lightly, chosen to make a large-volume prep sound easier than it often feels to patients.", "src": ["GoLYTELY FDA label", "Davis GR, Santa Ana CA, Morawski SG, Fordtran JS. Gastroenterology 1980", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "oral-glucose-gel": {"origin": "Glucose is the main sugar in blood and was first isolated from raisins by German chemist Andreas Marggraf in 1747. The name glucose was coined in 1838 by French chemists. After insulin was discovered in 1921 and used to treat diabetes, insulin-induced low blood sugar became a common emergency, and simple sugars were the obvious fix. Concentrated glucose gels in squeeze tubes were later developed so a person, family member or EMS crew could give a measured amount of sugar quickly to someone awake but confused. Products such as Glutose and Insta-Glucose are sold over the counter.", "timeline": [["1747", "Andreas Marggraf isolates glucose from raisins."], ["1838", "Jean-Baptiste Dumas and colleagues name the sugar glucose."], ["1922", "Insulin is first given to a patient, making treatment of hypoglycemia a common need."]], "cell": "Glucose gel delivers D-glucose, a simple sugar that needs no digestion. In the small intestine, the sodium-glucose cotransporter SGLT1 on the brush border of enterocytes pulls glucose into the cell along with sodium. GLUT2 on the other side of the cell moves it into the blood, and at high concentrations GLUT2 can also move to the brush border to boost uptake. In the blood, glucose enters brain cells mainly through GLUT1 and GLUT3, which do not need insulin, so the brain quickly gets fuel. That rapidly relieves confusion and other neuroglycopenic symptoms. Rising glucose also triggers insulin release from pancreatic beta cells in people who still make insulin. Very little glucose is absorbed through the cheek lining, so swallowing is key.", "body": [["Brain", "The brain runs almost entirely on glucose, so restoring blood sugar quickly reverses confusion, slurred speech and drowsiness."], ["Sympathetic nervous system", "As glucose rises, the adrenaline response to low sugar settles, so sweating, shaking and fast heartbeat ease."], ["Pancreas", "Rising glucose stimulates insulin release in people with working beta cells."], ["Gut", "A concentrated sugar load can cause nausea in some people."], ["Airway", "In people too drowsy to swallow safely, gel in the mouth can be inhaled, which is why it is reserved for alert patients."]], "journey": "The gel is squeezed into the mouth in small amounts and swallowed. It moves to the small intestine, where it is absorbed by SGLT1 and GLUT2. Blood glucose starts to rise within about 10 to 15 minutes and peaks around 15 to 30 minutes. Cells use the glucose for energy or store it as glycogen. It is fully metabolized to carbon dioxide and water. The effect lasts only about one to two hours, shorter than many long-acting insulins or sulfonylureas, so low sugar can return.", "sidefx": "Oral glucose has few side effects because it is the body's normal fuel. Nausea or vomiting can happen with a concentrated sugar load or in someone who is unwell. The main danger is aspiration if the person cannot swallow or protect the airway. In that case IV dextrose or glucagon is used instead. Hypoglycemia from long-acting insulin or sulfonylureas can recur after the gel wears off.", "fun": "The brain makes up about 2% of body weight but uses about 20% of the body's glucose at rest, which explains why low sugar shows up first as confusion.", "src": ["Glutose 15 product labeling", "American Diabetes Association Standards of Care", "Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology"]}, "fosinopril": {"origin": "All ACE inhibitors trace back to snake venom. In the 1960s Brazilian pharmacologist Sergio Ferreira found that venom of the pit viper Bothrops jararaca contained peptides that boosted bradykinin; these peptides were later shown to block angiotensin-converting enzyme. At Squibb, Miguel Ondetti and David Cushman used those peptides to design captopril, approved in 1981. Captopril's sulfhydryl group was linked to rash and taste changes, so chemists searched for other zinc-binding groups. Squibb scientists built fosinopril around a phosphinic acid group that grips the zinc in ACE's active site. Bristol-Myers Squibb's Monopril was approved in the US in 1991. The brand has since been discontinued, and fosinopril is available as a generic.", "timeline": [["1965", "Sergio Ferreira reports a bradykinin-potentiating factor in Bothrops jararaca venom."], ["1981", "Captopril, the first ACE inhibitor, is approved."], ["1991", "FDA approves Monopril (fosinopril)."]], "cell": "Fosinopril is an ester prodrug. After absorption, esterases in the gut wall and liver remove the ester to form fosinoprilat. Fosinoprilat's phosphinate group binds the zinc ion in the active site of angiotensin-converting enzyme (kininase II), competitively blocking it. ACE normally cuts angiotensin I into angiotensin II, which acts at AT1 receptors to constrict arterioles, release aldosterone, increase sympathetic tone and promote cardiac and vascular remodeling. With less angiotensin II, arterioles relax, aldosterone falls (so less sodium is kept and more potassium is retained) and the kidney's efferent arteriole dilates, lowering glomerular pressure. ACE also breaks down bradykinin, so bradykinin rises, adding vasodilation through nitric oxide and prostaglandins. Loss of angiotensin II feedback raises renin.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Less angiotensin II and more bradykinin relax arterioles, lowering blood pressure and the load on the heart."], ["Heart", "Lower afterload and less angiotensin and aldosterone signaling ease the work of a failing heart and reduce remodeling."], ["Kidneys", "Dilating the efferent arteriole lowers filtration pressure, protecting against proteinuria but sometimes causing a rise in creatinine, especially with renal artery stenosis or dehydration."], ["Adrenal glands", "Less aldosterone means less sodium retention and more potassium retention, which can cause hyperkalemia."], ["Lungs and airway", "Bradykinin and substance P build up in the airway, causing a dry cough; rarely bradykinin causes angioedema."], ["Unborn baby", "Blocking the fetal renin-angiotensin system can cause kidney failure and skull and lung problems, so it is avoided in pregnancy."]], "journey": "Fosinopril is taken by mouth and absorbed slowly, then converted by esterases in the gut wall and liver to fosinoprilat, which peaks about three hours later. Fosinoprilat is very highly protein bound. Unlike most ACE inhibitors, it is cleared by two routes, about half through the kidneys and half through the liver into bile. The liver can partly make up for kidney failure, so levels rise less in kidney disease. The half-life is about 12 hours, and the effect lasts about a day.", "sidefx": "Most side effects follow from the mechanism. Dry cough and angioedema come from bradykinin that ACE would normally break down; angioedema is more common in Black patients and with neprilysin inhibitors. Hyperkalemia results from lower aldosterone. Low blood pressure follows vasodilation, especially with dehydration or diuretics. Kidney function can fall when filtration depends on angiotensin II to constrict the efferent arteriole. Rare cholestatic hepatitis can occur. Angioedema of the airway is an emergency; ACE inhibitor angioedema is driven by bradykinin, so it often responds poorly to antihistamines and steroids, and airway support comes first. Antacids reduce absorption if taken together.", "fun": "Fosinopril is the only marketed ACE inhibitor that uses a phosphinate group to grab ACE's zinc, which is also why it is the one most cleared by the liver.", "src": ["Fosinopril sodium FDA label", "Ondetti MA, Rubin B, Cushman DW. Science 1977", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "trandolapril": {"origin": "Trandolapril belongs to the ACE inhibitor family that began with captopril, a drug designed in the 1970s from peptides in Brazilian pit viper venom. Trandolapril was developed in Europe in the 1980s as a non-sulfhydryl ester prodrug with a very lipophilic active form, designed to reach ACE in tissues as well as in blood and to last long enough for once-daily use. The FDA approved it as Mavik in 1996. The TRACE trial in Denmark showed it lowered death in patients with weak heart function after a heart attack. It is also combined with verapamil in the tablet Tarka.", "timeline": [["1981", "Captopril, the first ACE inhibitor, is approved in the US."], ["1995", "TRACE trial shows benefit after heart attack with left ventricular dysfunction."], ["1996", "FDA approves Mavik (trandolapril)."]], "cell": "Trandolapril is an ethyl ester prodrug. The liver hydrolyzes it to trandolaprilat, which is about eight times more potent. Trandolaprilat binds tightly to the zinc site of angiotensin-converting enzyme (kininase II) in plasma and in tissues such as vessel walls, heart and kidney, where its high lipid solubility helps it penetrate. Blocking ACE stops the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II. Less angiotensin II at AT1 receptors means arteriolar relaxation, less aldosterone (less sodium retention, more potassium retention), less sympathetic drive and less signaling for heart and vessel remodeling. ACE also breaks down bradykinin, so bradykinin rises, adding nitric oxide-mediated vasodilation. Slow dissociation from ACE gives a long effect.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Arterioles relax as angiotensin II falls and bradykinin rises, lowering blood pressure."], ["Heart", "After a heart attack with a weak left ventricle, lower afterload and less angiotensin and aldosterone signaling reduce harmful remodeling and improve survival."], ["Kidneys", "Efferent arteriole dilation lowers glomerular pressure; creatinine can rise when filtration depends on angiotensin II."], ["Adrenal glands", "Lower aldosterone raises potassium levels, sometimes causing hyperkalemia."], ["Airway", "Built-up bradykinin causes dry cough and, rarely, angioedema."], ["Heart rhythm (with verapamil)", "In the Tarka combination, verapamil adds slowing of the AV node, so bradycardia and heart block are possible."]], "journey": "Trandolapril is taken by mouth and absorbed, then hydrolyzed mainly in the liver to trandolaprilat, which peaks about 4 to 10 hours after a dose. Trandolaprilat binds ACE tightly, so it leaves slowly; its elimination half-life is about 10 hours but its effective accumulation half-life is close to a day. About a third is cleared in urine and two thirds in feces. Its long hold on ACE allows a 24-hour effect from one daily dose.", "sidefx": "Side effects reflect ACE inhibition. Bradykinin buildup causes dry cough and rarely angioedema, which can block the airway and responds poorly to antihistamines. Less aldosterone causes hyperkalemia. Vasodilation can cause dizziness and hypotension, especially with diuretics or dehydration. Kidney function can drop in renal artery stenosis or volume loss. Rare liver injury has been reported. Like all ACE inhibitors, it can harm a developing fetus by blocking the fetal renin-angiotensin system, so it is avoided in pregnancy.", "fun": "The TRACE trial screened thousands of heart attack patients by echocardiogram to find those with weak hearts, an early example of using imaging to select patients for a drug trial.", "src": ["Mavik (trandolapril) FDA label", "Kober L et al. N Engl J Med 1995 (TRACE)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "moexipril": {"origin": "Moexipril is a later member of the ACE inhibitor family, which began with captopril, designed at Squibb in the 1970s from peptides in Bothrops jararaca snake venom. Moexipril was developed as a non-sulfhydryl ester prodrug, in the same chemical style as enalapril and quinapril. It was approved in the US as Univasc in 1995, and a combination with hydrochlorothiazide (Uniretic) followed. Both brands have since been discontinued, and moexipril is available as a generic. It is notable mainly for being strongly affected by food, so it is taken an hour before meals.", "timeline": [["1981", "Captopril, the first ACE inhibitor, is approved in the US."], ["1995", "FDA approves Univasc (moexipril)."]], "cell": "Moexipril is an ester prodrug. After absorption it is hydrolyzed, mainly in the liver and gut, to moexiprilat, which is far more potent. Moexiprilat binds the zinc in the active site of angiotensin-converting enzyme (kininase II) and blocks it competitively. With less conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II, AT1 receptor activation drops: arterioles relax, aldosterone secretion falls (so sodium is lost and potassium kept), and the efferent arteriole in the kidney dilates. Bradykinin, normally broken down by ACE, builds up and adds nitric oxide and prostaglandin-mediated vasodilation. Loss of negative feedback raises plasma renin and angiotensin I.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Arteriolar relaxation from less angiotensin II and more bradykinin lowers blood pressure; flushing can occur."], ["Kidneys", "Lower glomerular pressure from efferent dilation can raise creatinine, especially with dehydration or renal artery stenosis."], ["Adrenal glands", "Less aldosterone leads to potassium retention and possible hyperkalemia."], ["Airway", "Bradykinin and substance P buildup causes dry cough and rarely angioedema."], ["Unborn baby", "Blocking the fetal renin-angiotensin system can damage fetal kidneys and development, so it is avoided in pregnancy."]], "journey": "Moexipril is taken by mouth, but only a small fraction becomes active moexiprilat in the blood, and food lowers absorption further, which is why doses are taken an hour before meals. Conversion happens in the gut wall and liver, and moexiprilat peaks in about 1.5 hours. It is about half bound to plasma proteins. Moexiprilat is cleared mostly in feces, with a smaller share in urine. Its half-life is about 2 to 9 hours, though binding to ACE lets the effect last about a day.", "sidefx": "The same mechanism explains the side effects. Dry cough and angioedema come from built-up bradykinin; airway angioedema is an emergency that responds poorly to antihistamines. Hyperkalemia comes from lower aldosterone. Hypotension and dizziness follow vasodilation, especially with diuretics. Kidney function can drop when filtration depends on angiotensin II. Rare liver injury is reported. Fetal harm is a boxed warning for all drugs acting on the renin-angiotensin system.", "fun": "Moexipril's absorption can drop sharply when taken with food, one of the largest food effects among the ACE inhibitors.", "src": ["Moexipril hydrochloride FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "azilsartan": {"origin": "Angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs) came from work in the 1980s at DuPont, where chemists built on small imidazole compounds discovered by Takeda in Japan to make losartan, the first ARB, approved in 1995. Takeda then went on to make its own ARBs. Candesartan was one, and azilsartan was a later design in which an oxadiazolone ring replaced the usual tetrazole ring. Takeda gave it as azilsartan medoxomil, a prodrug, to improve absorption. The FDA approved Edarbi in 2011, and Edarbyclor, combined with chlorthalidone, later that year. In trials it lowered blood pressure somewhat more than olmesartan and valsartan at their top doses.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Takeda imidazole compounds lead DuPont to design losartan."], ["1995", "Losartan becomes the first ARB approved in the US."], ["2011", "FDA approves Edarbi (azilsartan medoxomil)."], ["2011", "FDA approves Edarbyclor (azilsartan and chlorthalidone)."]], "cell": "Azilsartan medoxomil is hydrolyzed in the gut to azilsartan, the active drug. Azilsartan binds the angiotensin II type 1 (AT1) receptor, a Gq-coupled receptor, with more than 10,000-fold selectivity over the AT2 receptor. It dissociates very slowly, so it acts as an insurmountable blocker. Without AT1 activation, vascular smooth muscle cells do not raise IP3 and calcium, so arterioles relax. AT1 blockade in the adrenal cortex lowers aldosterone, reducing sodium retention and raising potassium. In the kidney it relaxes the efferent arteriole. Because ACE is not blocked, bradykinin does not build up. With AT1 receptors blocked, renin and angiotensin II levels rise, and the extra angiotensin II can act on AT2 receptors, which may add vasodilation.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Blocking AT1 receptors relaxes arterioles and lowers blood pressure."], ["Adrenal glands", "Less aldosterone reduces salt and water retention but raises potassium levels."], ["Kidneys", "Efferent arteriole dilation reduces filtration pressure and proteinuria; creatinine can rise in dehydration or renal artery stenosis."], ["Heart", "Less angiotensin II signaling reduces hypertrophy and remodeling over time."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is one of the more common side effects in trials."], ["Unborn baby", "Blocking the fetal renin-angiotensin system can injure the fetus, so it is avoided in pregnancy."]], "journey": "Azilsartan medoxomil is taken by mouth and hydrolyzed to azilsartan during absorption, so the prodrug is not found in plasma. Peak levels come in about 1.5 to 3 hours, with bioavailability around 60%. It is more than 99% protein bound. CYP2C9 in the liver converts it to inactive metabolites. About 55% leaves in feces and 42% in urine. The half-life is about 11 hours, and tight receptor binding gives a 24-hour effect.", "sidefx": "Because ARBs block the receptor instead of the ACE enzyme, bradykinin does not rise, so cough is uncommon and angioedema is rare. Lower aldosterone explains hyperkalemia. Vasodilation can cause dizziness and hypotension, especially with volume depletion. Kidney function can fall when glomerular filtration depends on angiotensin II constricting the efferent arteriole. Fetal toxicity is a boxed warning for the whole class. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Azilsartan's oxadiazolone ring is one of the few ARB designs that breaks away from the classic tetrazole ring of losartan and valsartan.", "src": ["Edarbi (azilsartan medoxomil) FDA label", "Edarbyclor FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "eprosartan": {"origin": "Eprosartan came from SmithKline Beecham in the late 1980s and 1990s. Most early ARBs, such as losartan and valsartan, share a biphenyl-tetrazole structure. SmithKline chemists took a different path and designed eprosartan as a thienylacrylic acid, built to mimic the C-terminal end of angiotensin II. The FDA approved it as Teveten in 1997, and Teveten HCT with hydrochlorothiazide followed. Rights later passed to other companies, and US marketing of eprosartan products has been discontinued.", "timeline": [["1995", "Losartan becomes the first ARB approved in the US."], ["1997", "FDA approves Teveten (eprosartan)."], ["2000s", "Teveten HCT (eprosartan and hydrochlorothiazide) is marketed."]], "cell": "Eprosartan is a selective, competitive antagonist of the angiotensin II type 1 (AT1) receptor. By occupying the receptor, it prevents angiotensin II from activating the Gq pathway in vascular smooth muscle, so IP3 and intracellular calcium do not rise and arterioles relax. It also blocks AT1 receptors in the adrenal cortex, lowering aldosterone, and in the kidney's efferent arteriole. Unlike most ARBs, eprosartan was shown to block presynaptic AT1 receptors on sympathetic nerve endings, where angiotensin II normally increases norepinephrine release, so it may also reduce sympathetic tone. It does not block ACE, so bradykinin is not raised. Renin and angiotensin II levels rise because feedback is removed.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "AT1 blockade relaxes arterioles and lowers blood pressure."], ["Sympathetic nerves", "Blocking presynaptic AT1 receptors reduces norepinephrine release, which may add to the blood pressure effect."], ["Adrenal glands", "Less aldosterone means less sodium retention and higher potassium."], ["Kidneys", "Lower glomerular pressure can raise creatinine in renin-dependent states such as renal artery stenosis or dehydration."], ["Unborn baby", "Blocking the fetal renin-angiotensin system can injure the fetus, so it is avoided in pregnancy."]], "journey": "Eprosartan is taken by mouth but absorbed poorly, with oral bioavailability of only about 13%, and food slows absorption slightly. Peak levels come one to two hours after a fasting dose. It is about 98% protein bound. It is not significantly broken down by CYP enzymes, with only a little glucuronidation, so CYP drug interactions are minimal. It is cleared mostly in bile and feces, with only about 7% in urine. The half-life is about 5 to 9 hours.", "sidefx": "Because eprosartan blocks the receptor rather than ACE, cough from bradykinin is uncommon. Lower aldosterone raises potassium. Kidney function can decline in people whose filtration depends on angiotensin II. Dizziness and hypotension follow vasodilation. Angioedema is rare. Fetal toxicity is a class warning. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Eprosartan is the only US-approved ARB without a biphenyl ring, a structure built to mimic part of angiotensin II itself.", "src": ["Teveten (eprosartan) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "pindolol": {"origin": "Beta blockers began with James Black, who designed propranolol at ICI in the early 1960s and later won a Nobel Prize. Researchers soon learned that some beta blockers also weakly stimulate the receptor they block, a property called intrinsic sympathomimetic activity (ISA). Pindolol, developed by Sandoz in Switzerland in the late 1960s, has the strongest ISA of the group. The hope was a beta blocker that would lower blood pressure without slowing the resting heart too much or causing cold hands. Pindolol was approved in the US as Visken in the early 1980s. The brand has been discontinued, and generics remain. Because it also blocks 5-HT1A receptors, it was studied as a way to speed up SSRI antidepressants, with mixed results.", "timeline": [["1964", "James Black's propranolol reaches clinical use."], ["1960s", "Sandoz develops pindolol."], ["1980s", "Pindolol (Visken) becomes available in the US."], ["1994", "Pindolol is first reported as an add-on to speed up SSRI response."]], "cell": "Pindolol binds beta-1 and beta-2 adrenergic receptors nonselectively. It is a partial agonist: when it sits in the receptor it switches on a small amount of Gs signaling and cAMP, but much less than epinephrine or norepinephrine. When sympathetic tone is high, as in exercise, it competes off those stronger agonists and the net effect is blockade. At rest, when sympathetic tone is low, its own weak stimulation keeps some receptor activity, so resting heart rate and cardiac output fall only a little. Blood pressure falls mainly because vascular resistance drops. Pindolol also has weak membrane-stabilizing (sodium channel) activity and blocks presynaptic 5-HT1A autoreceptors.", "body": [["Heart", "Resting heart rate and output change little because of ISA, but the rise in heart rate with exercise or stress is blunted."], ["Blood vessels", "Partial beta-2 stimulation may keep vessels more relaxed than full blockers, lowering peripheral resistance."], ["Lungs", "Nonselective beta-2 blockade can tighten airways in asthma or COPD."], ["Metabolism", "Beta blockade can mask the fast heartbeat of low blood sugar and blunt recovery from it."], ["Brain", "Being moderately lipid soluble, it can enter the brain and cause vivid dreams, insomnia or fatigue."], ["Lipids", "Beta blockers with ISA tend to have less effect on triglycerides and HDL than those without."]], "journey": "Pindolol is taken by mouth and absorbed well, with high bioavailability because the liver removes little on first pass. Levels peak in about an hour. About 40% is protein bound. About 60% is metabolized in the liver and the rest is excreted unchanged in urine, so both liver and kidney disease affect levels. The half-life is about three to four hours, but the blood pressure effect lasts longer.", "sidefx": "Pindolol shares the beta blocker side effects of slowing heart conduction and blocking beta-2 receptors. Heart failure can worsen because sympathetic support of contraction is reduced. Bronchospasm comes from beta-2 blockade in the airways. Bradycardia is less frequent than with other beta blockers because of ISA. Muscle aches and cramps are reported more with ISA drugs. Stopping suddenly can trigger rebound tachycardia or angina because receptors become more sensitive during treatment. In overdose, beta blocker toxicity causes bradycardia, AV block and shock; glucagon, which raises cAMP through its own receptor, and high-dose insulin therapy are used, along with standard resuscitation.", "fun": "Pindolol has the strongest intrinsic sympathomimetic activity of any marketed beta blocker, so in some people at rest it barely slows the heart.", "src": ["Pindolol FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "acebutolol": {"origin": "Acebutolol was developed in the late 1960s by the British company May & Baker, part of the wave of beta blockers that followed James Black's propranolol. It was designed to be cardioselective, acting mostly on beta-1 receptors in the heart, with a mild partial agonist effect. It turned out that much of its activity in people comes from an active metabolite, diacetolol, formed in the liver. Acebutolol was approved in the US as Sectral in the 1980s for high blood pressure and ventricular premature beats. It is one of the few beta blockers with US labeling for ventricular ectopy.", "timeline": [["1964", "Propranolol enters clinical use, launching the beta blocker era."], ["1960s", "May & Baker develops acebutolol in the United Kingdom."], ["1980s", "FDA approves Sectral (acebutolol) for hypertension and ventricular premature beats."]], "cell": "Acebutolol and its active metabolite diacetolol competitively block beta-1 adrenergic receptors, mostly in the heart. Norepinephrine and epinephrine normally activate beta-1 receptors, which couple to Gs, raise cAMP and activate protein kinase A. PKA opens L-type calcium channels and increases the funny current (If) in pacemaker cells. Blocking this lowers heart rate, force of contraction, AV node conduction speed and renin release from the kidney. Acebutolol is a weak partial agonist (mild ISA), so resting heart rate falls less than with pure blockers. It also has membrane-stabilizing activity, a local anesthetic-like block of sodium channels. Together these effects suppress ventricular extra beats (class II antiarrhythmic action) and lower blood pressure. Selectivity for beta-1 fades at higher doses.", "body": [["Heart rate and rhythm", "Slower sinus rate and AV conduction, with suppression of ventricular premature beats."], ["Heart muscle", "Less force of contraction lowers oxygen demand; a weak heart may decompensate."], ["Kidneys", "Less renin release from juxtaglomerular cells lowers angiotensin II and adds to blood pressure lowering."], ["Lungs", "Less effect on airways than nonselective blockers, but bronchospasm can still occur at higher doses."], ["Immune system", "Many patients develop positive antinuclear antibodies, and a few develop a lupus-like syndrome."]], "journey": "Acebutolol is taken by mouth and is well absorbed but undergoes extensive first-pass metabolism in the liver, which converts it to diacetolol, an active metabolite with similar beta-1 blocking power. Bioavailability of the parent is about 40%. The parent drug has a half-life of three to four hours, while diacetolol lasts 8 to 13 hours, so the effect lasts a full day. Diacetolol is cleared mainly by the kidneys and the parent partly in bile, so kidney failure raises diacetolol levels. Protein binding is low, about 26%.", "sidefx": "Side effects reflect beta-1 blockade: bradycardia, AV block, fatigue and worsening heart failure, because sympathetic support of the heart is reduced. At higher doses beta-1 selectivity is lost and bronchospasm can occur. Positive ANA and rare lupus-like illness are an immune quirk of this drug. Stopping abruptly can cause rebound angina or tachycardia. In overdose, its sodium channel blocking effect can widen the QRS and cause dangerous rhythms, which may respond to sodium bicarbonate, in addition to beta blocker care such as glucagon and high-dose insulin.", "fun": "Acebutolol is one of the beta blockers most likely to widen the QRS in overdose, a feature it shares with propranolol because of its sodium channel effects.", "src": ["Sectral (acebutolol) FDA label", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "betaxolol": {"origin": "Betaxolol was developed in France by the company Synthelabo in the 1970s as a highly beta-1 selective blocker with a long half-life. It found two very different uses. As eye drops, it was approved in the US in 1985 (Betoptic) to lower eye pressure in glaucoma, and it was attractive because it was less likely than timolol to tighten airways. As a tablet, Kerlone was approved in the US for high blood pressure in the late 1980s. The oral brand has been discontinued, but generic tablets and the eye drop Betoptic S remain.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Synthelabo develops betaxolol in France."], ["1985", "FDA approves betaxolol eye drops for glaucoma."], ["1980s", "FDA approves oral betaxolol (Kerlone) for hypertension."]], "cell": "Betaxolol is a highly selective competitive antagonist of beta-1 adrenergic receptors with no intrinsic sympathomimetic activity. In the heart, blocking beta-1 stops the Gs, cAMP and protein kinase A chain that norepinephrine uses to speed the sinus node, strengthen contraction and speed AV conduction. Heart rate, force and output fall. In the kidney, beta-1 blockade reduces renin release from juxtaglomerular cells, lowering angiotensin II and aldosterone. Over time vascular resistance falls too. In the eye, beta receptors on the ciliary epithelium drive aqueous humor production through cAMP; blocking them lowers fluid production and eye pressure. It has weak membrane-stabilizing activity.", "body": [["Heart", "Slower rate, less forceful contraction and slower AV conduction reduce oxygen demand and blood pressure."], ["Kidneys", "Lower renin release reduces the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system."], ["Lungs", "High beta-1 selectivity means less airway tightening at usual doses, though caution remains in asthma."], ["Eyes", "As eye drops, it lowers aqueous humor production and eye pressure in open-angle glaucoma."], ["Metabolism", "It can mask the fast heartbeat that warns of low blood sugar."]], "journey": "Oral betaxolol is almost completely absorbed with little first-pass loss, so bioavailability is high, around 90%. Peak levels come in about two to four hours. About half is protein bound. The liver breaks most of it down, and metabolites plus about 15% unchanged drug leave in urine. Its half-life is long, about 14 to 22 hours, allowing once-daily dosing. Eye drops act locally, but some drug drains through the tear duct into the nose and can reach the blood.", "sidefx": "Bradycardia, fatigue and heart block come from blocking beta-1 in the heart's conduction system. Heart failure can worsen because sympathetic support of contraction is reduced. Bronchospasm is less likely than with nonselective blockers but can occur. Stopping suddenly can cause rebound tachycardia and angina because receptors are upregulated. Eye drops can cause stinging and, through systemic absorption, slow heart rate. In overdose, glucagon and high-dose insulin therapy are used for severe beta blocker toxicity.", "fun": "Betaxolol eye drops were favored for glaucoma patients with mild lung disease because the drug spares beta-2 receptors in the airways better than timolol.", "src": ["Betaxolol hydrochloride tablets FDA label", "Betoptic S FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "isradipine": {"origin": "Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers started with nifedipine from Bayer in Germany in the 1960s and 1970s. Albrecht Fleckenstein in Freiburg had shown that these drugs block calcium entry into muscle cells. Sandoz developed isradipine in the 1980s as a dihydropyridine with strong selectivity for blood vessels over the heart. The FDA approved it as DynaCirc in 1990, with a controlled-release form later. The brands are discontinued and generic capsules remain. Isradipine later drew attention in Parkinson disease research because it blocks Cav1.3 channels in dopamine neurons, but a large phase 3 trial (STEADY-PD III) did not show slowing of the disease.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Albrecht Fleckenstein describes calcium antagonism by drugs."], ["1990", "FDA approves DynaCirc (isradipine)."], ["2020", "The STEADY-PD III trial finds isradipine does not slow early Parkinson disease."]], "cell": "Isradipine binds the alpha-1 subunit of L-type voltage-gated calcium channels (Cav1.2), especially in the inactivated state. Vascular smooth muscle cells rest at a more depolarized potential, so more of their channels are inactivated, which is one reason dihydropyridines favor vessels over heart. Blocking calcium entry means less calcium binds calmodulin, myosin light chain kinase is less active, and arterial smooth muscle relaxes. Arterioles widen and systemic vascular resistance falls, lowering blood pressure. Veins are affected little. At usual doses there is little direct depression of heart contraction or AV node conduction, though the fall in pressure can trigger reflex sympathetic activation.", "body": [["Arteries and arterioles", "Calcium channel blockade relaxes arterial muscle, lowering resistance and blood pressure."], ["Heart", "Little direct effect, but the drop in pressure can trigger a reflex rise in heart rate and palpitations."], ["Legs and ankles", "Precapillary arterioles dilate while veins do not, so capillary pressure rises and fluid leaks out, causing ankle edema."], ["Head and skin", "Dilated vessels cause headache and flushing."]], "journey": "Isradipine is taken by mouth and well absorbed, but extensive first-pass CYP3A4 metabolism in the gut and liver leaves only about 15 to 24% in the blood. Peak levels come in about 1.5 hours. It is about 95% protein bound. Metabolites are cleared about 60 to 65% in urine and the rest in feces. The half-life is about eight hours, so immediate-release capsules are given twice a day. CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels.", "sidefx": "Side effects come from vasodilation. Headache, dizziness and flushing reflect widened vessels in the head and skin. Ankle swelling reflects precapillary dilation without matching venous dilation, which pushes fluid into tissues; diuretics do not fix it well, but adding an ACE inhibitor or ARB helps by dilating the venous side. Reflex tachycardia and palpitations come from the baroreceptor response. In overdose, profound vasodilation causes shock; treatment includes IV calcium, vasopressors and high-dose insulin therapy.", "fun": "Isradipine was tested in Parkinson disease because dopamine neurons use L-type calcium channels to drive their pacemaking, which was thought to stress their mitochondria.", "src": ["Isradipine capsules FDA label", "Parkinson Study Group STEADY-PD III. Ann Intern Med 2020", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "nisoldipine": {"origin": "Nisoldipine was made by Bayer in Germany, the company that created nifedipine, as part of its dihydropyridine research in the 1970s. It is one of the most vessel-selective drugs in the class. Because it is broken down so heavily when swallowed, it was developed as a special extended-release tablet with a coat-core design that releases drug slowly through the day. The FDA approved it as Sular in 1995. Its low and food-sensitive bioavailability is a classic example of how formulation shapes a drug.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Bayer develops nisoldipine in its dihydropyridine program."], ["1995", "FDA approves Sular (nisoldipine extended-release)."]], "cell": "Nisoldipine binds the alpha-1 subunit of L-type (Cav1.2) voltage-gated calcium channels and holds them in the inactivated state. It is highly selective for vascular smooth muscle, where cells are more depolarized and channels are more often inactivated. With less calcium entering, less calcium-calmodulin activates myosin light chain kinase, so arterial smooth muscle relaxes. Arterioles widen and systemic vascular resistance falls. At usual doses, there is minimal direct effect on heart contractility or AV node conduction, but the drop in blood pressure can trigger reflex sympathetic activation.", "body": [["Arteries and arterioles", "Relaxation of arterial smooth muscle lowers resistance and blood pressure."], ["Heart", "A reflex increase in heart rate can occur; rarely, angina can worsen when starting or raising the dose."], ["Legs and ankles", "Peripheral edema is the most common side effect because precapillary arterioles dilate and fluid leaks into tissues."], ["Head and skin", "Headache and flushing reflect dilated vessels."]], "journey": "Nisoldipine is swallowed whole as an extended-release tablet. It is well absorbed but very heavily metabolized by CYP3A4 in the gut wall and liver, so only about 5% reaches the circulation. A high-fat meal causes a sudden spike in release and level, so it is taken on an empty stomach, and grapefruit, which blocks gut CYP3A4, is avoided. Peak levels come six to twelve hours after a dose. It is more than 99% protein bound. Metabolites leave mainly in urine. The half-life is about 7 to 12 hours, and the tablet gives a 24-hour effect.", "sidefx": "Vasodilation explains the main side effects: edema, headache, flushing and dizziness. Reflex tachycardia from the baroreceptor response can cause palpitations and, rarely, worsening angina. Because of its very low bioavailability, anything that blocks CYP3A4, such as grapefruit or strong inhibitors, can sharply raise levels and cause hypotension. Crushing or chewing the tablet releases the dose all at once. In overdose, severe vasodilation and shock are treated with IV calcium, vasopressors and high-dose insulin.", "fun": "Grapefruit juice can raise nisoldipine levels several-fold, making it one of the strongest grapefruit interactions among calcium channel blockers.", "src": ["Sular (nisoldipine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "gemfibrozil": {"origin": "Fibrates began with clofibrate, introduced in the 1960s after ICI researchers found that certain fibric acid compounds lowered blood lipids. Parke-Davis chemists developed gemfibrozil in the 1970s, and the FDA approved Lopid in 1981. The Helsinki Heart Study, published in 1987, showed that gemfibrozil lowered heart attacks in middle-aged men with abnormal lipids, and the VA-HIT trial in 1999 showed benefit in men with low HDL. In 1990 scientists discovered PPAR-alpha, the nuclear receptor that explains how fibrates work. Gemfibrozil also gained a reputation for dangerous interactions with statins: cerivastatin was withdrawn in 2001 after deaths from muscle breakdown, many in patients also taking gemfibrozil.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Clofibrate, the first fibrate, is introduced."], ["1981", "FDA approves Lopid (gemfibrozil)."], ["1987", "Helsinki Heart Study shows fewer coronary events with gemfibrozil."], ["1990", "PPAR-alpha is discovered as the fibrate target receptor."], ["1999", "VA-HIT trial shows benefit in men with low HDL."], ["2001", "Cerivastatin is withdrawn, partly due to rhabdomyolysis with gemfibrozil."]], "cell": "Gemfibrozil activates peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor alpha (PPAR-alpha), a nuclear receptor found mainly in liver, heart, kidney and muscle. PPAR-alpha pairs with the retinoid X receptor and binds PPAR response elements in DNA. This turns on genes for lipoprotein lipase and fatty acid uptake and beta-oxidation, and turns down apolipoprotein C-III, which normally blocks lipoprotein lipase. The net result is faster breakdown of triglyceride-rich VLDL and chylomicrons in the blood, less fatty acid available for the liver to make triglycerides, and less VLDL secretion. PPAR-alpha also raises apolipoprotein A-I and A-II, raising HDL. Separately, gemfibrozil and its glucuronide inhibit CYP2C8, the liver uptake transporter OATP1B1 and statin glucuronidation, which explains its drug interactions.", "body": [["Blood lipids", "Triglycerides fall sharply and HDL rises modestly; LDL may rise in people with very high triglycerides as VLDL is converted."], ["Pancreas", "Lowering very high triglycerides reduces the risk of acute pancreatitis."], ["Gallbladder", "More cholesterol is sent into bile, which raises the risk of gallstones."], ["Muscles", "Myopathy can occur, and the risk of rhabdomyolysis is much higher when combined with statins."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise, so liver tests are checked during treatment."], ["Gut", "Indigestion, abdominal pain and diarrhea are common."]], "journey": "Gemfibrozil is taken by mouth 30 minutes before morning and evening meals and is well absorbed, peaking in one to two hours. It is about 97% bound to albumin, which can displace warfarin. The liver oxidizes it and forms glucuronide conjugates, including a glucuronide that strongly inhibits CYP2C8. About 70% leaves in urine, mostly as conjugates. The half-life is about 1.5 hours, which is why it is given twice daily.", "sidefx": "Gallstones come from PPAR-alpha-driven increases in bile cholesterol. Muscle injury reflects a direct effect of fibrates on muscle plus, with statins, gemfibrozil's block of OATP1B1 and statin glucuronidation, which raises statin blood levels and causes rhabdomyolysis with kidney injury. That is why gemfibrozil is contraindicated with simvastatin and avoided with most statins, while fenofibrate is preferred when a fibrate is combined. It boosts warfarin effect and bleeding risk. CYP2C8 inhibition greatly raises repaglinide levels, causing low blood sugar. Mild anemia and low white cells can occur. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Gemfibrozil blocks CYP2C8 so strongly that it is used in drug studies as a model inhibitor to test whether a new drug depends on that enzyme.", "src": ["Lopid (gemfibrozil) FDA label", "Frick MH et al. N Engl J Med 1987 (Helsinki Heart Study)", "Issemann I, Green S. Nature 1990", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "lomitapide": {"origin": "The idea for lomitapide came from a rare disease. People with abetalipoproteinemia have very low cholesterol because they lack working microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP), and in 1992 researchers including John Wetterau linked the disease to MTP. Bristol-Myers Squibb developed MTP inhibitors in the 1990s, including BMS-201038, but stopped work because of liver fat and gut side effects. Daniel Rader at the University of Pennsylvania saw a use for it in homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia (HoFH), where LDL receptors barely work and standard drugs fail. Aegerion Pharmaceuticals licensed it, and the FDA approved lomitapide as Juxtapid in 2012, with a boxed warning for liver toxicity and a restricted program.", "timeline": [["1992", "Loss of MTP is shown to cause abetalipoproteinemia."], ["1990s", "Bristol-Myers Squibb develops BMS-201038, later lomitapide."], ["2007", "Cuchel, Rader and colleagues report lomitapide in HoFH."], ["2012", "FDA approves Juxtapid for homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia."]], "cell": "MTP sits in the endoplasmic reticulum of liver cells and gut cells. As apolipoprotein B is made, MTP transfers triglycerides, cholesterol esters and phospholipids onto it, letting it fold into a lipoprotein particle. In the liver this makes VLDL (with apoB-100), and in the intestine chylomicrons (with apoB-48). Lomitapide binds MTP and blocks this lipid loading, so apoB is broken down instead of secreted. Less VLDL leaves the liver, so less LDL, its end product, forms in the blood. Because this works upstream of LDL receptors, it lowers LDL even when receptors are missing, as in HoFH. The downside is that fat that cannot be packaged stays inside the cells, causing liver and gut fat buildup.", "body": [["Blood lipids", "LDL cholesterol falls by about 40 to 50% in HoFH, along with apoB, non-HDL cholesterol and triglycerides."], ["Liver", "Unexported fat builds up in hepatocytes, causing raised liver enzymes and hepatic steatosis that could progress to steatohepatitis and fibrosis."], ["Intestine", "Fat that cannot be packaged into chylomicrons stays in enterocytes or passes into stool, causing diarrhea, nausea, cramping and fatty stools, worse with high-fat meals."], ["Nutrition", "Absorption of fat-soluble vitamins (especially vitamin E) and essential fatty acids falls, so supplements are taken."], ["Weight", "Gut effects and fat malabsorption can lead to weight loss."]], "journey": "Lomitapide is taken by mouth on an empty stomach, at least two hours after the evening meal, to reduce gut effects. Oral bioavailability is very low, about 7%, and levels peak about six hours after a dose. It is about 99.8% protein bound. CYP3A4 in the liver breaks it down extensively, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors are contraindicated and weaker ones need dose limits. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. The half-life is long, about 40 hours. It also raises levels of warfarin, simvastatin and lovastatin.", "sidefx": "The main toxicities come straight from blocking MTP. In the liver, fat that cannot be exported as VLDL builds up, raising liver enzymes and causing steatosis, the reason for the boxed warning and monitoring under a REMS program. In the gut, blocked chylomicron formation causes diarrhea, nausea, vomiting and abdominal pain, made worse by dietary fat, so a low-fat diet is required. Reduced fat absorption lowers vitamin E and essential fatty acids. CYP3A4 inhibitors can push levels high enough to raise liver injury risk. Lomitapide can harm a fetus. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Lomitapide is a rare case of a drug abandoned for one purpose and rescued by an academic researcher who saw that its main problem was acceptable in a severe rare disease.", "src": ["Juxtapid (lomitapide) FDA label", "Cuchel M et al. N Engl J Med 2007", "Wetterau JR et al. Science 1992"]}, "evinacumab": {"origin": "Evinacumab grew out of human genetics. In 2010 researchers including Sekar Kathiresan and Kiran Musunuru found that people born with loss-of-function mutations in ANGPTL3 have very low LDL, triglycerides and HDL, a condition called familial combined hypolipidemia, and appear healthy with less heart disease. ANGPTL3 is a liver protein that inhibits lipoprotein lipase. Regeneron used its VelocImmune mice, engineered to make human antibodies, to create a fully human antibody against ANGPTL3. The ELIPSE HoFH trial showed it nearly halved LDL even in patients with no working LDL receptors. The FDA approved Evkeeza in 2021 for homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia, with the age range later extended to younger children.", "timeline": [["2010", "ANGPTL3 loss-of-function mutations are linked to familial combined hypolipidemia."], ["2017", "Studies link ANGPTL3 deficiency to lower coronary artery disease risk."], ["2020", "ELIPSE HoFH trial results are published."], ["2021", "FDA approves Evkeeza (evinacumab-dgnb)."]], "cell": "ANGPTL3 is made by the liver and released into the blood, where it inhibits lipoprotein lipase and endothelial lipase. Lipoprotein lipase on the inner wall of capillaries breaks triglycerides in VLDL and chylomicrons into fatty acids; endothelial lipase breaks down phospholipids in HDL and other particles. Evinacumab is a human IgG4 monoclonal antibody that binds ANGPTL3 and neutralizes it, releasing the brake on both enzymes. VLDL particles are then stripped of triglyceride faster and their remnants are cleared, so fewer become LDL. This works without the LDL receptor, which is why it lowers LDL in people with null LDL receptor mutations, where statins and PCSK9 inhibitors do little. HDL also falls because endothelial lipase is more active.", "body": [["Blood lipids", "LDL cholesterol falls by about half in HoFH, triglycerides drop, and HDL also decreases."], ["Blood vessels", "Lower LDL is expected to reduce cholesterol buildup in artery walls, the main danger in HoFH."], ["Immune system", "Infusion reactions and rare anaphylaxis can occur as the body reacts to the antibody."], ["Respiratory tract", "Nasopharyngitis and flu-like illness were among the most common side effects."], ["Unborn baby", "It may harm a fetus based on animal studies, so pregnancy is avoided during treatment."]], "journey": "Evinacumab is given as an IV infusion once every four weeks. As a large protein, it stays mainly in the blood and extracellular fluid. It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes; like other antibodies it is broken down into peptides and amino acids by cells throughout the body. Some clearance also happens when it binds its target (target-mediated clearance), so the half-life depends on the concentration and is long. LDL reduction is seen within about two weeks.", "sidefx": "Because evinacumab is a foreign protein given IV, infusion reactions such as itching, rash and flushing can occur, and rarely anaphylaxis; slowing or stopping the infusion and standard allergy treatment are used. Nasopharyngitis, flu-like illness, dizziness and nausea were common in trials, though their link to mechanism is less clear. Lower HDL follows from releasing endothelial lipase. Animal studies suggested fetal harm. There is no reversal agent.", "fun": "People born without working ANGPTL3 have been found living healthy lives with unusually low cholesterol, which gave scientists confidence that blocking it would be safe.", "src": ["Evkeeza (evinacumab-dgnb) FDA label", "Raal FJ et al. N Engl J Med 2020 (ELIPSE HoFH)", "Musunuru K et al. N Engl J Med 2010"]}, "vorapaxar": {"origin": "Vorapaxar began with a tree. Himbacine is an alkaloid from the bark of an Australian magnolia, Galbulimima belgraveana, first studied as a muscarinic blocker. In 1991 Shaun Coughlin's lab in San Francisco cloned the thrombin receptor on platelets, protease-activated receptor-1 (PAR-1), revealing how thrombin, the strongest platelet activator, switches platelets on. Chemists at Schering-Plough used himbacine as a starting shape and built vorapaxar, a potent PAR-1 blocker. The large TRA 2P-TIMI 50 trial, published in 2012, found fewer heart attacks and strokes in people with prior heart attack or peripheral artery disease, but more bleeding, including brain bleeding in those with prior stroke. Merck, which had acquired Schering-Plough, gained FDA approval for Zontivity in 2014.", "timeline": [["1991", "Shaun Coughlin's lab clones the thrombin receptor PAR-1."], ["2000s", "Schering-Plough designs vorapaxar from the natural product himbacine."], ["2012", "TRA 2P-TIMI 50 trial results are published."], ["2014", "FDA approves Zontivity (vorapaxar)."]], "cell": "Thrombin activates platelets by cutting the N-terminal end of PAR-1, a G protein-coupled receptor. The newly exposed end folds back and acts as a tethered ligand that activates the receptor itself. Activated PAR-1 couples to Gq (raising calcium and driving granule release and shape change), G12/13 (Rho kinase, shape change) and Gi (lowering cAMP). Together these switch on the GPIIb/IIIa integrin, which binds fibrinogen and links platelets into a clot. Vorapaxar binds PAR-1 at a site that blocks activation by the tethered ligand (and by the test peptide TRAP). It is reversible in principle but dissociates so slowly that it acts as if irreversible for the life of the platelet. It does not affect ADP (P2Y12), collagen or thromboxane pathways, or clotting tests, which is why it is added to aspirin or clopidogrel.", "body": [["Platelets", "Thrombin-driven platelet activation is blocked for weeks, adding a third layer of antiplatelet effect on top of aspirin and P2Y12 blockers."], ["Heart and arteries", "Fewer platelet clots on unstable plaques lowers the risk of heart attack, stroke and cardiovascular death in people with prior MI or peripheral artery disease."], ["Blood clotting system", "The coagulation cascade itself is not affected, so PT and aPTT stay normal."], ["Brain", "Brain bleeding is more likely, especially in people with a past stroke, TIA or brain bleed, who should not take it."], ["Skin and mucosa", "Bruising, nosebleeds and gum bleeding are common."], ["Gut", "GI bleeding risk rises, especially with other antithrombotics."]], "journey": "Vorapaxar is taken by mouth with nearly complete absorption, peaking in about an hour. It is about 99% protein bound and spreads into tissues. CYP3A4 and CYP2J2 in the liver break it down, forming an active metabolite (M20), so strong CYP3A inhibitors and inducers are avoided. It leaves mainly in feces. Its effective half-life is three to four days and its terminal half-life about eight days. High-level platelet inhibition builds over about a week, and significant inhibition can last four weeks or more after stopping.", "sidefx": "Bleeding is the main side effect and follows directly from blocking thrombin's main platelet receptor on top of other antiplatelet drugs. Brain bleeding risk is why it is contraindicated after stroke, TIA or intracranial hemorrhage. Because the drug stays bound so long, its effect cannot be quickly stopped. There is no known reversal agent, and platelet transfusion may not work well because the drug remaining in the blood can bind new platelets. Anemia and rash are also reported.", "fun": "Vorapaxar's story starts with an alkaloid from the bark of an Australian rainforest tree whose shape gave chemists a starting point for a heart drug.", "src": ["Zontivity (vorapaxar) FDA label", "Morrow DA et al. N Engl J Med 2012 (TRA 2P-TIMI 50)", "Vu TK, Coughlin SR et al. Cell 1991"]}, "metyrosine": {"origin": "Metyrosine is alpha-methyl-para-tyrosine, a close copy of the amino acid tyrosine. In the 1960s Sidney Spector, Albert Sjoerdsma and Sidney Udenfriend at the US National Institutes of Health helped show that tyrosine hydroxylase is the rate-limiting step in making catecholamines and that alpha-methyl-tyrosine blocks it in animals and people. Because pheochromocytoma tumors pour out norepinephrine and epinephrine, the drug offered a way to cut production at the source. Merck developed it as Demser, approved in the US in the late 1970s. It is still used to prepare some patients for surgery, usually alongside alpha blockers. It also became a research tool for studying catecholamines in the brain.", "timeline": [["1964", "Tyrosine hydroxylase is identified as the enzyme that turns tyrosine into DOPA."], ["1965", "Spector, Sjoerdsma and Udenfriend report blockade with alpha-methyl-tyrosine."], ["1970s", "FDA approves Demser (metyrosine) for pheochromocytoma."]], "cell": "Catecholamines are made in steps: tyrosine to DOPA (by tyrosine hydroxylase), DOPA to dopamine (by aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase), dopamine to norepinephrine (by dopamine beta-hydroxylase), and norepinephrine to epinephrine (by PNMT). Tyrosine hydroxylase is the slowest step, so it controls the rate of the whole chain. Metyrosine resembles tyrosine and competitively inhibits tyrosine hydroxylase. Less DOPA is made, so less dopamine, norepinephrine and epinephrine are produced. In pheochromocytoma, total catecholamine synthesis drops by about 35 to 80%, measured by falling urine metanephrines and VMA. Fewer catecholamines mean fewer alpha-1-driven surges in blood pressure and beta-driven rapid heart rhythms. In the brain, dopamine is depleted too, which explains its central side effects.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Less norepinephrine means fewer hypertensive attacks and lower overall blood pressure."], ["Heart", "Fewer catecholamine surges reduce palpitations and arrhythmias."], ["Brain", "Dopamine depletion causes sedation in most patients, plus anxiety, depression, confusion or hallucinations in some."], ["Movement", "Low brain dopamine can cause drooling, tremor, stiffness and other extrapyramidal signs, similar to drug-induced parkinsonism."], ["Kidneys and urine", "Metyrosine is poorly soluble and can form crystals and stones in the urine, so generous fluid intake is used."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is common and can be severe enough to limit treatment."]], "journey": "Metyrosine is taken by mouth and well absorbed, with peak levels in one to three hours. It is barely metabolized; only small amounts of catechol metabolites form. Most of the drug leaves unchanged in the urine. Its half-life is about three and a half hours, which is why it is taken several times a day. The biochemical effect peaks in two to three days, and catecholamine production returns to baseline three to four days after stopping.", "sidefx": "Sedation, anxiety, depression and confusion come from lower dopamine and norepinephrine in the brain. Extrapyramidal signs such as tremor and drooling come from dopamine depletion in the basal ganglia, which is why drugs that block dopamine, such as haloperidol or metoclopramide, can make them worse. Crystalluria and stones occur because unchanged drug is concentrated in urine. Diarrhea is common. Sudden stopping can cause rebound and psychiatric symptoms. Even with metyrosine, handling the tumor at surgery can release stored catecholamines, so blood pressure surges and arrhythmias remain possible, managed with alpha blockade and short-acting IV drugs. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Metyrosine is used in research to empty catecholamines from the brain, helping scientists show the role of dopamine and norepinephrine in mood and movement.", "src": ["Demser (metyrosine) FDA label", "Spector S, Sjoerdsma A, Udenfriend S. J Pharmacol Exp Ther 1965", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "sparsentan": {"origin": "Endothelin-1, the most powerful vessel-constricting peptide known, was discovered in 1988 by Masashi Yanagisawa and Tomoh Masaki in Japan. Both endothelin and angiotensin II damage the kidney's filters in protein-leaking diseases. Scientists at Bristol-Myers Squibb designed a single molecule that blocks both the endothelin type A receptor and the angiotensin AT1 receptor. The program passed through Ligand Pharmaceuticals to Retrophin, later renamed Travere Therapeutics. The PROTECT trial in IgA nephropathy showed larger drops in proteinuria than irbesartan, and the FDA granted accelerated approval to Filspari in 2023, with full approval in 2024. Its use has also been studied in focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS).", "timeline": [["1988", "Yanagisawa and Masaki discover endothelin-1."], ["2000s", "Bristol-Myers Squibb discovers sparsentan as a dual ETA and AT1 receptor blocker."], ["2023", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Filspari for IgA nephropathy."], ["2024", "Filspari receives full FDA approval in IgA nephropathy."]], "cell": "Sparsentan blocks two receptors with one molecule. At the AT1 receptor, it prevents angiotensin II from constricting the efferent arteriole, lowering pressure inside the glomerulus; it also blocks angiotensin effects on mesangial cells, podocytes and fibrosis signaling (such as TGF-beta). At the endothelin type A (ETA) receptor, it blocks endothelin-1, which is overproduced in diseased kidneys and drives vasoconstriction, mesangial cell growth, inflammation, podocyte injury and scarring. Both are Gq-coupled receptors that raise intracellular calcium. Blocking both lowers glomerular hypertension, protects podocytes (the cells that form the filtration barrier) and reduces leakage of protein into the urine, which slows the decline in kidney function.", "body": [["Kidneys", "Lower glomerular pressure and less podocyte and mesangial injury cut proteinuria and slow the fall in eGFR."], ["Blood vessels", "Blocking two constricting systems lowers blood pressure, sometimes causing dizziness or hypotension."], ["Fluid balance", "Endothelin receptor blockade causes fluid retention and peripheral edema."], ["Blood", "Hemoglobin falls slightly, mostly from fluid dilution."], ["Potassium", "AT1 blockade lowers aldosterone, which can raise potassium."], ["Liver", "Endothelin receptor antagonists can injure liver cells, so liver tests are monitored."]], "journey": "Sparsentan is taken by mouth once daily and peaks in about three hours. It is more than 99% protein bound. CYP3A in the liver breaks it down, so strong CYP3A inhibitors and inducers are avoided. It is mostly cleared in feces, with very little in urine. The half-life is about 9 to 10 hours. Lower proteinuria shows up over weeks.", "sidefx": "Hyperkalemia and acute kidney injury come from AT1 blockade, which lowers aldosterone and reduces filtration pressure. Hypotension follows from blocking two vessel-tightening systems. Edema reflects endothelin receptor effects on sodium and water handling. Liver injury is a known risk of endothelin receptor antagonists and is a boxed warning, with monitoring. Endothelin is needed for normal fetal development, so blocking it can cause serious birth defects, also a boxed warning. It is not combined with ACE inhibitors, ARBs or other endothelin blockers. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Endothelin-1 was described in 1988 as the most potent vasoconstrictor then known, and sparsentan became the first US-approved drug to block it in order to protect the kidney.", "src": ["Filspari (sparsentan) FDA label", "Heerspink HJL et al. Lancet 2023 (PROTECT)", "Yanagisawa M et al. Nature 1988"]}, "aprocitentan": {"origin": "Aprocitentan comes from the family of endothelin receptor antagonists built at the Swiss company Actelion, founded by Jean-Paul and Martine Clozel. Actelion's bosentan (2001) and macitentan (2013) were approved for pulmonary arterial hypertension. Scientists noticed that macitentan's main active metabolite was a dual endothelin blocker in its own right, with a long half-life. After Actelion was sold, the Clozels founded Idorsia in 2017 and developed that metabolite, aprocitentan, for resistant high blood pressure, a condition in which endothelin activity is thought to be high. The PRECISION trial, published in 2022, showed added blood pressure lowering on top of three other drugs. The FDA approved Tryvio in 2024.", "timeline": [["1988", "Endothelin-1 is discovered."], ["2001", "Bosentan becomes the first approved endothelin receptor antagonist."], ["2013", "Macitentan, the parent of aprocitentan, is approved."], ["2022", "PRECISION trial results are published."], ["2024", "FDA approves Tryvio (aprocitentan) for resistant hypertension."]], "cell": "Endothelin-1 is released by vessel lining cells and acts on two G protein-coupled receptors. ETA receptors on vascular smooth muscle couple to Gq, raising calcium and causing strong, long-lasting vasoconstriction plus cell growth and fibrosis. ETB receptors on smooth muscle also constrict; ETB on endothelium releases nitric oxide and clears endothelin from the blood, and in the kidney's collecting duct ETB promotes sodium excretion. Aprocitentan blocks both ETA and ETB. Blocking ETA relaxes vessels and reduces remodeling. Blocking ETB on smooth muscle adds to vasodilation. The overall effect lowers blood pressure in people whose pressure stays high despite other drugs, a group thought to rely more on the endothelin system, especially with salt sensitivity, obesity or kidney disease.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Blocking endothelin relaxes vessels and lowers systolic and diastolic pressure on top of other antihypertensives."], ["Kidneys and fluid", "Endothelin blockade causes sodium and water retention, so edema and fluid overload are common, which is why diuretics are usually continued."], ["Blood", "Hemoglobin falls, mostly from dilution as plasma volume expands."], ["Heart", "Fluid retention can worsen heart failure in susceptible people."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises can occur, as with other endothelin blockers."], ["Unborn baby", "Endothelin signaling is needed for fetal development of the face, heart and great vessels, so blocking it causes birth defects."]], "journey": "Aprocitentan is taken by mouth once a day and is well absorbed, peaking about four to five hours later. It is more than 99% protein bound. Unlike many drugs, it is not dependent on CYP enzymes; it is broken down by glucuronidation (UGT1A1 and UGT2B7) and by non-enzymatic hydrolysis, so CYP interactions are few. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. The half-life is long, about 41 to 44 hours, which smooths levels over the day. The blood pressure effect builds over two to four weeks.", "sidefx": "Edema and fluid retention come from blocking endothelin's effects on kidney sodium handling and vessel permeability; this can worsen heart failure. Lower hemoglobin is mostly a dilution effect of extra plasma volume. Liver injury is a class effect of endothelin receptor blockers, so liver tests are checked. Fetal harm is a boxed warning because endothelin is essential for fetal development, and pregnancy is excluded before and during use. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Aprocitentan was hiding inside an older drug: it is the main active metabolite of macitentan, which had already been used for years for pulmonary arterial hypertension.", "src": ["Tryvio (aprocitentan) FDA label", "Schlaich MP et al. Lancet 2022 (PRECISION)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "bempedoic-acid-ezetimibe": {"origin": "This tablet pairs two drugs that lower LDL by different routes. Ezetimibe was found at Schering-Plough in the 1990s while chemists were searching for blockers of a different cholesterol enzyme; they noticed a compound that stopped cholesterol absorption from the gut. It was approved as Zetia in 2002, and in 2004 its target was identified as the transporter NPC1L1. Bempedoic acid came from Esperion Therapeutics, a company co-founded by Roger Newton, who helped discover atorvastatin at Parke-Davis. It blocks ATP citrate lyase, an enzyme in cholesterol production upstream of the statin target, and is activated only in the liver, not in muscle. The FDA approved Nexletol and the combination Nexlizet in 2020. The CLEAR Outcomes trial (2023) showed fewer cardiovascular events with bempedoic acid in people unable to take statins.", "timeline": [["2002", "FDA approves ezetimibe (Zetia)."], ["2004", "NPC1L1 is identified as ezetimibe's target."], ["2020", "FDA approves Nexletol (bempedoic acid) and Nexlizet (bempedoic acid and ezetimibe)."], ["2023", "CLEAR Outcomes trial shows fewer cardiovascular events with bempedoic acid."], ["2024", "Labeling adds reduction of cardiovascular event risk."]], "cell": "Bempedoic acid is a prodrug. In liver cells, the enzyme very long-chain acyl-CoA synthetase 1 (ACSVL1) converts it to bempedoyl-CoA, which inhibits ATP citrate lyase (ACL). ACL makes acetyl-CoA in the cytoplasm, the raw material for cholesterol synthesis, two steps before HMG-CoA reductase. With less cholesterol made, the liver cell activates SREBP2, which increases LDL receptors on the cell surface, pulling more LDL out of the blood. Because skeletal muscle lacks ACSVL1, the drug is not activated there, which may explain fewer muscle complaints than statins. Ezetimibe and its active glucuronide bind Niemann-Pick C1-Like 1 (NPC1L1) on the brush border of the small intestine, blocking uptake of dietary and biliary cholesterol. Less cholesterol reaches the liver in chylomicrons, so the liver also raises LDL receptors.", "body": [["Blood lipids", "Together they lower LDL cholesterol by about 35 to 38% versus placebo, with non-HDL cholesterol and apoB also falling."], ["Liver", "Less internal cholesterol production and less gut cholesterol supply lead to more LDL receptors and faster LDL clearance."], ["Intestine", "Ezetimibe reduces absorption of dietary and bile cholesterol; diarrhea can occur."], ["Kidneys and uric acid", "Bempedoic acid competes with uric acid for kidney transporter OAT2, raising uric acid and gout risk."], ["Tendons", "Tendon rupture, especially of the rotator cuff, biceps or Achilles, was seen more often with bempedoic acid; the cause is not known."], ["Muscles", "Muscle spasms can occur, but serious muscle injury is less common than with statins because the drug is not activated in muscle."]], "journey": "The combination tablet is taken once a day by mouth. Bempedoic acid is absorbed and peaks in about three and a half hours; it is activated in the liver and also broken down by glucuronidation, and its metabolites leave mainly in urine, with a half-life of about 21 hours. Ezetimibe is absorbed and quickly glucuronidated in the gut wall and liver; the active glucuronide is excreted in bile, reabsorbed and recycled (enterohepatic circulation), which gives a half-life of about 22 hours, and it leaves mainly in feces. Bile acid sequestrants bind ezetimibe, so dosing is separated.", "sidefx": "Raised uric acid and gout come from bempedoic acid competing for kidney uric acid transporters. Tendon rupture risk is a labeled warning with an unclear mechanism, higher in older people and those on steroids or fluoroquinolones. Bempedoic acid raises simvastatin and pravastatin levels, so their doses are limited. Mild anemia and small liver enzyme rises can occur. Ezetimibe alone is usually well tolerated; diarrhea and upper respiratory infections are reported. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Bempedoic acid works on the same cholesterol production line as statins but needs an enzyme that muscle cells lack, a built-in way to spare muscle.", "src": ["Nexlizet FDA label", "Nissen SE et al. N Engl J Med 2023 (CLEAR Outcomes)", "Altmann SW et al. Science 2004", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "omega-3-acid-ethyl-esters": {"origin": "In the 1970s Danish researchers Hans Olaf Bang and Jorn Dyerberg studied Inuit people in Greenland, who ate a diet very rich in fish and seal and seemed to have low rates of heart attack. They found high blood levels of the omega-3 fatty acids EPA and DHA and low triglycerides. That launched decades of fish oil research. The Norwegian company Pronova developed a concentrated, purified prescription form of EPA and DHA ethyl esters. The FDA approved it as Omacor in 2004 for very high triglycerides, and it was renamed Lovaza in 2007 to avoid confusion with another drug name. It is different from icosapent ethyl, a pure EPA product, and from dietary supplement fish oils.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Bang and Dyerberg study omega-3 fatty acids in Greenland Inuit."], ["2004", "FDA approves Omacor (omega-3-acid ethyl esters)."], ["2007", "Omacor is renamed Lovaza in the US."]], "cell": "The exact mechanism is not fully understood. EPA and DHA are taken up by liver cells, where they reduce triglyceride production and secretion. They are poor substrates for diacylglycerol acyltransferase (DGAT), the final enzyme that builds triglycerides, and may inhibit it. They activate PPAR-alpha and increase mitochondrial and peroxisomal beta-oxidation, so fatty acids are burned instead of stored. They suppress SREBP-1c, a transcription factor that drives fat production (lipogenesis). With less triglyceride available, the liver makes fewer VLDL particles. In the blood, omega-3s increase lipoprotein lipase activity and speed removal of triglycerides from VLDL and chylomicrons. Some VLDL is converted to LDL faster, which is why LDL can rise. EPA and DHA also enter cell membranes and change eicosanoid production, which slightly reduces platelet aggregation.", "body": [["Blood lipids", "Triglycerides fall by about 45% in people with severe hypertriglyceridemia; LDL can rise and HDL may rise a little."], ["Pancreas", "Lowering very high triglycerides reduces the risk of acute pancreatitis."], ["Platelets", "EPA replaces arachidonic acid in platelet membranes, so less thromboxane A2 forms and bleeding time can be slightly prolonged."], ["Heart rhythm", "Omega-3 products have been linked with a higher risk of atrial fibrillation, especially in people with a history of it."], ["Gut", "Fishy burps, indigestion and taste changes are common because the oils float in the stomach."], ["Liver", "ALT can rise mildly in some patients, so liver tests are monitored."]], "journey": "The capsules are swallowed whole, ideally with meals. In the gut, the ethyl esters are broken down by pancreatic lipase to free EPA and DHA, which are absorbed and packed into chylomicrons. Fat in the meal improves absorption. In the body, EPA and DHA are built into cell membrane phospholipids and other lipids, and are burned for energy through beta-oxidation like dietary fats, ending as carbon dioxide and water. Plasma levels peak about 5 to 10 hours after a dose. Triglyceride lowering builds over two to four weeks.", "sidefx": "Fishy burps and taste come from the oil itself floating in the stomach. Indigestion is common. Rises in LDL reflect faster conversion of VLDL to LDL. Mild bleeding prolongation comes from lower thromboxane production in platelets, so it is noted with anticoagulants. The link to atrial fibrillation may relate to effects on heart cell membranes and ion channels, though the mechanism is not clear. People with fish or shellfish allergy may react, although the product is highly purified. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Bang and Dyerberg also noted that the Greenland Inuit in their studies tended to bleed longer from small cuts, an early clue to the platelet effects of omega-3 fatty acids.", "src": ["Lovaza (omega-3-acid ethyl esters) FDA label", "Bang HO, Dyerberg J. Lancet 1971", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "amlodipine-benazepril": {"origin": "This capsule joins two drugs from different companies. Amlodipine was developed by Pfizer in the United Kingdom in the 1980s as a long-acting dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker and approved as Norvasc in 1992. Benazepril, an ACE inhibitor, was developed by Ciba-Geigy and approved as Lotensin in 1991. Ciba-Geigy (Novartis from 1996) combined them as Lotrel, approved in the US in 1995. Pairing a calcium blocker with an ACE inhibitor made sense because each blunts a weakness of the other. The ACCOMPLISH trial, published in 2008, found this combination reduced cardiovascular events more than benazepril with hydrochlorothiazide.", "timeline": [["1991", "FDA approves Lotensin (benazepril)."], ["1992", "FDA approves Norvasc (amlodipine)."], ["1995", "FDA approves Lotrel (amlodipine and benazepril)."], ["2008", "ACCOMPLISH trial favors benazepril plus amlodipine over benazepril plus hydrochlorothiazide."]], "cell": "Amlodipine binds L-type voltage-gated calcium channels (Cav1.2), preferring those in vascular smooth muscle. Less calcium entry means less calcium-calmodulin activation of myosin light chain kinase, so arterial muscle relaxes and arterioles widen. Benazepril is an ester prodrug hydrolyzed in the liver to benazeprilat, which binds the zinc site of angiotensin-converting enzyme (kininase II). That lowers angiotensin II production and slows bradykinin breakdown. Less angiotensin II at AT1 receptors relaxes arterioles, reduces aldosterone (less sodium retention, more potassium retention) and dilates the kidney's efferent arteriole. The two work together: amlodipine's vasodilation can activate the renin system, which benazepril blocks, and benazepril dilates veins and postcapillary vessels, which reduces amlodipine's ankle swelling.", "body": [["Arteries", "Two separate vessel-relaxing actions combine to lower blood pressure more than either drug alone."], ["Legs and ankles", "Amlodipine dilates arterioles more than veins, so fluid leaks into tissues; benazepril's venous dilation reduces this edema."], ["Kidneys", "Benazeprilat lowers glomerular pressure and proteinuria; creatinine can rise with dehydration or renal artery stenosis."], ["Adrenal glands", "Less aldosterone means less sodium retention and higher potassium levels."], ["Airway", "Bradykinin buildup from ACE inhibition causes dry cough and, rarely, angioedema."], ["Head and skin", "Headache and flushing reflect vasodilation."]], "journey": "The capsule is swallowed once daily. Benazepril is absorbed quickly and converted in the liver to benazeprilat, which peaks in one to two hours and has an effective half-life of about 10 to 11 hours; it is cleared mainly by the kidneys with some biliary excretion. Amlodipine is absorbed slowly, peaks six to twelve hours after a dose and has a long half-life of 30 to 50 hours, so its full effect builds over days. It is metabolized by CYP3A4 in the liver, and inactive metabolites leave mainly in urine.", "sidefx": "Each part brings its own side effects. From amlodipine, vasodilation causes headache, flushing, dizziness and ankle edema, though edema is less than with amlodipine alone. From benazepril, built-up bradykinin causes dry cough and rare angioedema, which can be life threatening and responds poorly to antihistamines; lower aldosterone causes hyperkalemia; and kidney function can drop in renin-dependent states. Combined vasodilation can cause hypotension. Agranulocytosis is a rare ACE inhibitor effect. Fetal toxicity is a boxed warning. In amlodipine overdose, IV calcium, vasopressors and high-dose insulin are used.", "fun": "The ACCOMPLISH trial was stopped early because the amlodipine-benazepril combination was clearly ahead of the comparison combination with a diuretic.", "src": ["Lotrel FDA label", "Jamerson K et al. N Engl J Med 2008 (ACCOMPLISH)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "amlodipine-valsartan": {"origin": "Amlodipine was created by Pfizer in the 1980s as a long-acting calcium channel blocker and approved as Norvasc in 1992. Valsartan was developed by Ciba-Geigy in the late 1980s and early 1990s as an angiotensin receptor blocker and approved as Diovan in 1996. Novartis combined them in Exforge, which the FDA approved in 2007, allowing many patients to take one tablet instead of two. Pairing a calcium blocker with an ARB adds two different ways of relaxing vessels, and the ARB reduces amlodipine's ankle swelling.", "timeline": [["1992", "FDA approves Norvasc (amlodipine)."], ["1996", "FDA approves Diovan (valsartan)."], ["2007", "FDA approves Exforge (amlodipine and valsartan)."], ["2018", "Many valsartan products are recalled worldwide for a nitrosamine impurity."]], "cell": "Amlodipine blocks L-type voltage-gated calcium channels (Cav1.2) in vascular smooth muscle. With less calcium coming in, less calcium-calmodulin forms, myosin light chain kinase is less active, and arterial muscle relaxes. Valsartan selectively and competitively blocks the angiotensin II type 1 (AT1) receptor, a Gq-coupled receptor. This stops angiotensin II from constricting vessels, releasing aldosterone, boosting sympathetic activity and driving remodeling. Because ACE is not blocked, bradykinin does not rise. Amlodipine's vasodilation tends to switch on the renin-angiotensin system, and valsartan blocks that response. Valsartan also relaxes the venous side of capillary beds, which offsets the fluid leak caused by amlodipine's arteriolar dilation.", "body": [["Arteries", "Combined calcium channel and AT1 blockade lowers vascular resistance and blood pressure more than either drug alone."], ["Legs and ankles", "Edema from amlodipine is less common because valsartan dilates the venous side and balances capillary pressure."], ["Adrenal glands", "Less aldosterone signaling raises potassium."], ["Kidneys", "Efferent arteriole dilation lowers filtration pressure; creatinine can rise in dehydration or renal artery stenosis."], ["Head and skin", "Headache, dizziness and flushing reflect vasodilation."], ["Unborn baby", "Blocking the fetal renin-angiotensin system can injure the fetus, so it is avoided in pregnancy."]], "journey": "The tablet is taken once a day. Valsartan is absorbed fairly quickly, peaking in two to four hours, is about 95% protein bound, is barely metabolized and leaves mainly in bile and feces, with a half-life of about six hours. Amlodipine is absorbed slowly, peaks in six to twelve hours, is about 93% protein bound, is metabolized by CYP3A4 in the liver and leaves mainly in urine as inactive metabolites. Its half-life is 30 to 50 hours, so its effect builds over a week or so and fades slowly.", "sidefx": "Amlodipine's vasodilation causes headache, flushing, dizziness and some ankle edema. Valsartan's AT1 blockade can raise potassium and lower kidney filtration in renin-dependent states. Together they can cause symptomatic hypotension, especially with dehydration or diuretics. Cough is uncommon because bradykinin is not affected, and angioedema is rare. Fetal toxicity is a boxed warning. Calcium channel blocker overdose is treated with IV calcium, vasopressors and high-dose insulin.", "fun": "In 2018 regulators found a probable carcinogen, NDMA, in some valsartan made by one supplier, leading to a global recall and new testing rules for nitrosamines in many medicines.", "src": ["Exforge FDA label", "Diovan FDA label", "FDA statements on valsartan recalls 2018", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "amlodipine-olmesartan": {"origin": "Olmesartan medoxomil was developed by the Japanese company Sankyo and approved in the US as Benicar in 2002. Amlodipine came from Pfizer and was approved as Norvasc in 1992. Daiichi Sankyo combined the two as Azor, which the FDA approved in 2007. In 2013 the FDA added a warning to all olmesartan products after doctors at the Mayo Clinic described a rare sprue-like enteropathy, a celiac-like gut illness with severe diarrhea and weight loss, that improved when olmesartan was stopped.", "timeline": [["1992", "FDA approves Norvasc (amlodipine)."], ["2002", "FDA approves Benicar (olmesartan medoxomil)."], ["2007", "FDA approves Azor (amlodipine and olmesartan)."], ["2013", "FDA warns of sprue-like enteropathy with olmesartan."]], "cell": "Amlodipine blocks L-type voltage-gated calcium channels (Cav1.2) in arterial smooth muscle, reducing calcium entry, calcium-calmodulin activation of myosin light chain kinase and contraction, so arterioles widen. Olmesartan medoxomil is a prodrug de-esterified in the gut wall to olmesartan, which binds the angiotensin II type 1 (AT1) receptor tightly and selectively. Blocking AT1 stops angiotensin II-driven vasoconstriction, aldosterone release and sympathetic activation, and relaxes the kidney's efferent arteriole. ACE is untouched, so bradykinin does not rise. The two drugs complement each other: the ARB blocks the renin-angiotensin activation that vasodilation can trigger, and it dilates the venous side of capillaries, reducing amlodipine's ankle edema.", "body": [["Arteries", "Two vessel-relaxing actions lower vascular resistance and blood pressure."], ["Legs and ankles", "Edema from amlodipine is reduced by the ARB's balancing effect on capillary pressure."], ["Adrenal glands", "Less aldosterone signaling can raise potassium."], ["Kidneys", "Lower glomerular pressure can raise creatinine in dehydration or renal artery stenosis."], ["Small intestine", "Rarely, olmesartan causes a sprue-like enteropathy with villous atrophy, chronic diarrhea and weight loss, months to years after starting."], ["Unborn baby", "Blocking the fetal renin-angiotensin system can harm the fetus, so it is avoided in pregnancy."]], "journey": "The tablet is taken once a day. Olmesartan medoxomil is converted to olmesartan during absorption, with about 26% bioavailability, peaking in one to two hours. Olmesartan is about 99% protein bound, is not metabolized by CYP enzymes and is cleared about 35 to 50% in urine and the rest in bile and feces, with a half-life of about 13 hours. Amlodipine is absorbed slowly, peaks in six to twelve hours, is metabolized by CYP3A4 and has a 30 to 50 hour half-life, so its effect builds over days.", "sidefx": "Vasodilation from amlodipine causes headache, flushing, dizziness and some ankle edema. AT1 blockade raises potassium and can lower filtration in renin-dependent kidneys. Together they can cause symptomatic hypotension. The sprue-like enteropathy is unique to olmesartan among ARBs; it is thought to be an immune-mediated injury to the small intestine and resolves after stopping the drug. Cough is uncommon because bradykinin is not affected. Fetal toxicity is a boxed warning. Severe amlodipine overdose is treated with IV calcium, vasopressors and high-dose insulin.", "fun": "Patients with olmesartan enteropathy were sometimes first diagnosed with celiac disease that did not respond to a gluten-free diet, until the drug was identified as the cause.", "src": ["Azor FDA label", "Rubio-Tapia A et al. Mayo Clin Proc 2012", "FDA Drug Safety Communication 2013 (olmesartan)"]}, "lisinopril-hydrochlorothiazide": {"origin": "Hydrochlorothiazide grew out of 1950s work at Merck, where Karl Beyer, James Sprague, Frederick Novello and John Baer made chlorothiazide, the first thiazide diuretic, approved in 1958. Hydrochlorothiazide, a more potent cousin, followed in 1959. Lisinopril was developed at Merck in the late 1970s and 1980s as a lysine-containing relative of enalaprilat that works without needing activation. It was approved in 1987 and sold by Merck as Prinivil and by Zeneca (then ICI) as Zestril. Combination tablets such as Zestoretic followed, giving two complementary drugs in one pill. Lisinopril is now one of the most prescribed drugs in the US.", "timeline": [["1958", "Chlorothiazide, the first thiazide diuretic, is approved."], ["1959", "Hydrochlorothiazide is introduced."], ["1987", "FDA approves lisinopril (Prinivil and Zestril)."], ["2002", "ALLHAT reports that thiazide-type diuretics are as good as or better than other first-line drugs for many patients."]], "cell": "Lisinopril is an active drug, not a prodrug. It binds the zinc site of angiotensin-converting enzyme (kininase II), blocking conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II and slowing bradykinin breakdown. Less angiotensin II at AT1 receptors relaxes arterioles, lowers aldosterone and dilates the efferent arteriole. Hydrochlorothiazide is secreted into the tubule by organic anion transporters and blocks the sodium-chloride cotransporter (NCC, SLC12A3) on the luminal side of distal convoluted tubule cells. More sodium, chloride and water leave in urine, lowering plasma volume at first; over time vascular resistance also falls. Thiazides increase calcium reabsorption and, by delivering more sodium to the collecting duct, increase potassium loss. The partners balance each other: the thiazide raises renin, which lisinopril blocks, and lisinopril's lower aldosterone partly offsets thiazide potassium loss.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Less angiotensin II and more bradykinin relax arterioles, while volume loss and later vascular relaxation from the thiazide add to the blood pressure drop."], ["Kidneys", "More sodium and water are excreted; potassium, magnesium and sodium levels can shift, and uric acid can rise because thiazides compete for its secretion."], ["Electrolytes", "Thiazide potassium loss and ACE inhibitor potassium retention partly cancel, but either low or high potassium can occur; low sodium is a known thiazide risk."], ["Metabolism", "Thiazides can raise blood sugar and cholesterol slightly, partly through low potassium reducing insulin release."], ["Airway", "Bradykinin buildup can cause dry cough and, rarely, angioedema."], ["Eyes", "Hydrochlorothiazide rarely causes sudden angle-closure glaucoma or choroidal effusion."]], "journey": "Both drugs are taken by mouth once daily, often in the morning to avoid nighttime urination. Lisinopril is absorbed slowly and incompletely, peaks in about seven hours, is not bound to plasma proteins, is not metabolized and leaves unchanged in urine with a half-life of about 12 hours. Hydrochlorothiazide is absorbed well, peaks in about four hours, is not metabolized and is excreted unchanged in urine with a half-life of about 6 to 15 hours. Because both rely on the kidneys, kidney disease raises their levels.", "sidefx": "From lisinopril: bradykinin causes cough and rare angioedema, lower aldosterone causes hyperkalemia, and kidney function can drop when filtration depends on angiotensin II. From hydrochlorothiazide: blocking NCC causes low sodium, low potassium and low magnesium; competition for urate secretion raises uric acid and can trigger gout; and changes in insulin release can raise glucose. Thiazides are sulfonamide-related and rarely cause photosensitivity, pancreatitis or acute angle-closure glaucoma. Both cause dizziness and orthostatic hypotension. Fetal toxicity is a boxed warning for lisinopril. Lithium levels rise with both drugs.", "fun": "Lisinopril is one of the few ACE inhibitors that is not a prodrug, so it works even when liver function is poor.", "src": ["Zestoretic FDA label", "ALLHAT Officers. JAMA 2002", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "losartan-hydrochlorothiazide": {"origin": "Losartan was the first angiotensin receptor blocker. In the 1980s chemists at DuPont, led by researchers including David Carini and John Duncia, built on small imidazole compounds from Takeda and designed a potent, orally active AT1 blocker. Merck, partnering with DuPont, brought it to market as Cozaar, and the FDA approved it and its combination with hydrochlorothiazide, Hyzaar, in 1995. Hydrochlorothiazide itself had come from Merck's diuretic research in the late 1950s. The LIFE trial, published in 2002, found losartan-based treatment lowered stroke more than atenolol-based treatment in people with high blood pressure and left ventricular hypertrophy, which led to a stroke-reduction indication.", "timeline": [["1959", "Hydrochlorothiazide is introduced."], ["1980s", "DuPont chemists design losartan, building on Takeda imidazole compounds."], ["1995", "FDA approves Cozaar (losartan) and Hyzaar (losartan and hydrochlorothiazide)."], ["2002", "LIFE trial shows fewer strokes with losartan-based therapy in hypertension with LVH."]], "cell": "Losartan blocks the angiotensin II type 1 (AT1) receptor competitively. In the liver, CYP2C9 and CYP3A4 convert about 14% of it to E-3174, a carboxylic acid metabolite that is 10 to 40 times more potent and blocks noncompetitively, providing most of the effect. AT1 blockade relaxes arterioles, lowers aldosterone, dilates the efferent arteriole and reduces sympathetic and remodeling signals, without raising bradykinin. Losartan itself also inhibits URAT1, the urate transporter in the proximal tubule, so more uric acid is excreted. Hydrochlorothiazide blocks the NCC transporter in the distal convoluted tubule, increasing sodium, chloride and water excretion and later lowering vascular resistance. The pairing balances side effects: losartan blunts the thiazide-driven renin rise and potassium loss, and its uricosuric effect offsets part of the thiazide's rise in uric acid.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "AT1 blockade and thiazide volume loss together lower vascular resistance and blood pressure."], ["Heart", "Less angiotensin II signaling reduces left ventricular hypertrophy over time."], ["Brain (stroke)", "In hypertension with LVH, losartan-based therapy lowered stroke more than atenolol-based therapy."], ["Kidneys", "More sodium and water loss; uric acid excretion increases from losartan, partly balancing the thiazide's opposite effect."], ["Electrolytes", "Low sodium, low potassium or occasionally high potassium can occur."], ["Metabolism", "The thiazide can raise blood sugar and lipids slightly."]], "journey": "The tablet is taken once a day. Losartan is well absorbed but has a big first-pass effect, with about 33% bioavailability. It peaks in about an hour, and E-3174 peaks in three to four hours. Both are highly protein bound. The half-lives are about two hours for losartan and six to nine hours for E-3174. They are cleared in bile and urine. Hydrochlorothiazide is absorbed, not metabolized and excreted unchanged in urine with a half-life of about 6 to 15 hours. Drugs that strongly change CYP2C9 can affect E-3174 levels.", "sidefx": "From losartan: AT1 blockade can raise potassium and lower kidney filtration in renin-dependent kidneys; cough is uncommon because bradykinin is not affected; angioedema is rare. From hydrochlorothiazide: blocked sodium-chloride reabsorption causes hyponatremia, hypokalemia and volume loss, competition for urate secretion can trigger gout, and glucose can rise. Rare thiazide effects include photosensitivity and acute angle-closure glaucoma. Dizziness and orthostatic hypotension come from both. Fetal toxicity is a boxed warning. Lithium levels can rise.", "fun": "Losartan is the only common ARB with a meaningful uric acid-lowering effect, which partly cancels the uric acid rise from its thiazide partner.", "src": ["Hyzaar FDA label", "Dahlof B et al. Lancet 2002 (LIFE)", "Timmermans PB et al. Pharmacol Rev 1993", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "valsartan-hydrochlorothiazide": {"origin": "Valsartan was designed at Ciba-Geigy in Switzerland in the late 1980s and early 1990s, following DuPont's losartan. Chemists replaced losartan's imidazole ring with an amino acid (valine) based side chain, which is where the name comes from. It was approved as Diovan in 1996. Novartis, formed from Ciba-Geigy and Sandoz in 1996, added hydrochlorothiazide, a diuretic from Merck's 1950s research, to create Diovan HCT, approved in the late 1990s. Valsartan itself later gained heart failure and post-heart attack indications. In 2018 many generic valsartan products were recalled for a nitrosamine impurity, which led regulators to test many other medicines.", "timeline": [["1959", "Hydrochlorothiazide is introduced."], ["1996", "FDA approves Diovan (valsartan)."], ["1990s", "Diovan HCT (valsartan and hydrochlorothiazide) is approved."], ["2018", "Many valsartan products are recalled worldwide for NDMA contamination."]], "cell": "Valsartan selectively and competitively blocks the angiotensin II type 1 (AT1) receptor. Without AT1 activation, vascular smooth muscle does not raise calcium through the Gq pathway, so arterioles relax; aldosterone release from the adrenal cortex falls; the efferent arteriole dilates; and angiotensin-driven sympathetic and remodeling signals drop. ACE is not blocked, so bradykinin does not rise. Hydrochlorothiazide reaches the tubule lumen by secretion and blocks the sodium-chloride cotransporter (NCC) in the distal convoluted tubule, increasing sodium, chloride and water excretion and later reducing vascular resistance. The thiazide's volume loss activates renin, which valsartan's AT1 blockade neutralizes, and lower aldosterone signaling partly offsets thiazide potassium loss.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Blocking angiotensin II and lowering plasma volume together reduce resistance and blood pressure."], ["Kidneys", "More sodium and water are excreted; uric acid can rise and gout flare because the thiazide competes with urate secretion."], ["Electrolytes", "Low sodium, low potassium and low magnesium can occur, though the ARB partly protects potassium."], ["Metabolism", "Thiazide-related low potassium can lower insulin release, raising blood sugar slightly."], ["Eyes", "Hydrochlorothiazide can rarely cause acute angle-closure glaucoma."], ["Unborn baby", "Blocking the fetal renin-angiotensin system can injure the fetus, so it is avoided in pregnancy."]], "journey": "The tablet is taken once a day, often in the morning. Valsartan is absorbed with about 25% bioavailability, peaks in two to four hours, is about 95% protein bound, is minimally metabolized and leaves mainly in bile and feces, with a half-life of about six hours. Hydrochlorothiazide is absorbed well, peaks in about four hours, is not metabolized and leaves unchanged in urine with a half-life of about 6 to 15 hours. The combined effect lasts about 24 hours.", "sidefx": "Valsartan side effects come from AT1 blockade: higher potassium, a rise in creatinine in renin-dependent kidneys and low blood pressure. Cough is uncommon because bradykinin is not affected; angioedema is rare. Hydrochlorothiazide side effects come from NCC blockade and its effects on the kidney: low sodium, low potassium, high uric acid and gout, and higher glucose. As a sulfonamide-related drug it rarely causes photosensitivity and acute angle-closure glaucoma. Dizziness and orthostatic hypotension can come from both. Fetal toxicity is a boxed warning. Lithium levels can rise.", "fun": "The name valsartan reflects its valine-based side chain, a design that set it apart from the imidazole ring of losartan.", "src": ["Diovan HCT FDA label", "Diovan FDA label", "FDA statements on valsartan recalls 2018", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "bisoprolol-hydrochlorothiazide": {"origin": "This tablet joins two older ideas. Hydrochlorothiazide grew out of the sulfonamide antibiotic era: in 1957 chemists Frederick Novello and James Sprague at Merck Sharp & Dohme made chlorothiazide, the first thiazide diuretic, and hydrochlorothiazide followed within about two years as a more potent cousin. Bisoprolol came later. It was developed by the German company E. Merck in Darmstadt as a highly beta-1 selective blocker in the beta-blocker family that James Black had opened with propranolol in the 1960s. Bisoprolol alone reached the US market as Zebeta in the early 1990s, and Ziac followed in 1993. Ziac was notable because it used a very low dose of the diuretic, and the lowest strength became the first combination tablet labeled for starting high blood pressure treatment rather than only for patients already on both drugs.", "timeline": [["1957", "Novello and Sprague at Merck Sharp & Dohme make chlorothiazide, the first thiazide."], ["1959", "Hydrochlorothiazide enters US practice."], ["1980s", "E. Merck in Darmstadt develops bisoprolol as a highly beta-1 selective blocker."], ["1993", "FDA approves Ziac, a low-dose bisoprolol and hydrochlorothiazide tablet."]], "cell": "Bisoprolol competitively blocks beta-1 adrenergic receptors. Normally norepinephrine binds these Gs-coupled receptors, raises cAMP and activates protein kinase A, which opens L-type calcium channels in the heart and speeds the SA node. With the receptor blocked, heart rate and contractility fall. Beta-1 receptors on the juxtaglomerular cells of the kidney also drive renin release, so blockade lowers renin, angiotensin II and aldosterone. Bisoprolol is highly beta-1 selective and has no intrinsic sympathomimetic or membrane-stabilizing activity. Hydrochlorothiazide inhibits the thiazide-sensitive Na-Cl cotransporter (NCC) on the luminal side of the distal convoluted tubule. Less sodium and chloride are reabsorbed, so more salt and water leave in the urine and plasma volume falls. Over weeks, vascular resistance also drops. The extra sodium reaching the collecting duct is swapped for potassium and hydrogen, which explains potassium loss.", "body": [["Heart", "Beta-1 blockade slows the heart rate and softens each contraction, lowering cardiac output and the heart's oxygen demand."], ["Kidney (renin)", "Less sympathetic drive on juxtaglomerular cells means less renin, which reduces angiotensin II and aldosterone and adds to blood pressure lowering."], ["Kidney (tubule)", "Blocking NCC in the distal tubule causes a mild, steady loss of sodium and water and lowers blood volume."], ["Blood vessels", "With lower volume and less angiotensin II, arterial resistance falls over time, so blood pressure drops."], ["Electrolytes", "More sodium delivered to the collecting duct boosts potassium and hydrogen secretion, so potassium can fall; thiazides also raise calcium and uric acid."], ["Metabolism", "Thiazide-related low potassium can reduce insulin release and raise glucose, and beta-blockade can blunt the warning signs of low blood sugar."]], "journey": "Both parts are taken by mouth and absorbed well. Bisoprolol has high bioavailability because little is lost on first pass. About half is broken down in the liver, partly by CYP3A4, and about half leaves unchanged in the urine, a balanced route that makes it fairly forgiving in mild liver or kidney disease. Its half-life of about 9 to 12 hours supports once-daily use. Hydrochlorothiazide is not metabolized. It is pumped into the tubule by organic anion transporters, which is how it reaches its site of action, and leaves unchanged in urine with a half-life of roughly 6 to 15 hours. Diuresis starts within about two hours, but the full blood pressure effect builds over one to two weeks.", "sidefx": "Fatigue, cold hands and a slow pulse come from reduced sympathetic drive to the heart and blood vessels. In people whose heart depends on sympathetic support, blockade can cause heart block or worsen decompensated heart failure. At higher levels selectivity fades, so beta-2 blockade can tighten the airways. Stopping suddenly can trigger rebound tachycardia and angina because receptors upregulate during therapy. The diuretic side explains low potassium, low sodium, gout from uric acid retention (thiazides compete with urate for secretion) and higher glucose. Hydrochlorothiazide has also been linked to acute myopia with angle-closure glaucoma. For beta-blocker overdose, glucagon and high-dose insulin therapy are used because they raise cardiac cAMP or support the heart by other routes.", "fun": "The lowest Ziac strength uses a smaller dose of hydrochlorothiazide than most single-drug tablets, an example of using two drugs at low doses to get the effect with fewer side effects.", "src": ["Ziac (bisoprolol fumarate and hydrochlorothiazide) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "atenolol-chlorthalidone": {"origin": "Atenolol was made at Imperial Chemical Industries (ICI) in England, the same company where James Black had developed propranolol in the early 1960s. ICI chemists set out to build a beta-blocker that stayed mostly in the blood and out of the brain. By making the molecule very water soluble, they produced atenolol, a beta-1 selective blocker that crosses into the central nervous system poorly. It was introduced as Tenormin in the 1970s and approved in the US in 1981. Chlorthalidone is older. It was developed by the Swiss company Geigy and approved in the US around 1960 as Hygroton. It is a thiazide-like diuretic with a different ring structure and a very long half-life. Pairing the two in Tenoretic, approved in the 1980s, gave a once-daily tablet. Later trials such as ALLHAT drew new attention to chlorthalidone as a strong outcome-proven diuretic.", "timeline": [["1960", "Chlorthalidone (Hygroton) reaches the US market from Geigy."], ["1970s", "ICI develops atenolol as a water-soluble, beta-1 selective blocker."], ["1981", "FDA approves atenolol as Tenormin."], ["1980s", "The atenolol and chlorthalidone tablet Tenoretic is approved."], ["2002", "The ALLHAT trial reports that chlorthalidone performs as well as newer drugs for outcomes."]], "cell": "Atenolol is a competitive antagonist at beta-1 adrenergic receptors. Blocking these Gs-coupled receptors prevents norepinephrine from raising cAMP in heart cells, so fewer L-type calcium channels open, the SA node fires more slowly and AV conduction slows. Contractility drops along with heart rate. On juxtaglomerular cells, the same blockade lowers renin release and therefore angiotensin II and aldosterone. Atenolol has no intrinsic sympathomimetic or membrane-stabilizing activity. Chlorthalidone is a phthalimidine diuretic that inhibits the Na-Cl cotransporter (NCC) in the distal convoluted tubule, causing natriuresis and a fall in plasma volume, and later a fall in peripheral resistance. It also inhibits carbonic anhydrase. Its tight binding to carbonic anhydrase inside red blood cells creates a reservoir that explains its very long action.", "body": [["Heart", "Lower heart rate and contractility reduce cardiac output and oxygen demand, which also helps angina."], ["Kidney (renin)", "Less renin release dampens the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, adding to the blood pressure effect."], ["Kidney (tubule)", "Chlorthalidone blocks salt reabsorption in the distal tubule, giving a mild, very long-lasting diuresis."], ["Blood vessels", "Peripheral resistance falls over weeks as volume and angiotensin II drop."], ["Brain", "Atenolol is water soluble and enters the brain poorly, so vivid dreams and sedation are less common than with lipid-soluble blockers like propranolol."], ["Electrolytes and urate", "More sodium at the collecting duct drives potassium loss, and the diuretic competes with uric acid for secretion, which can raise urate and trigger gout."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth, atenolol is only about half absorbed, and it is barely metabolized by the liver. It leaves mainly unchanged in the urine, so its half-life of about 6 to 7 hours lengthens a great deal in kidney failure. Chlorthalidone is absorbed slowly and binds strongly to carbonic anhydrase in red blood cells, which acts like a storage depot. It is also excreted largely unchanged by the kidney. Its half-life of roughly 40 to 60 hours is far longer than that of hydrochlorothiazide, so its effect carries over well beyond 24 hours. Beta-blockade begins within about an hour.", "sidefx": "A slow pulse, fatigue, cold extremities and dizziness come from reduced sympathetic drive. Heart block and cardiogenic shock can occur in people with conduction disease or failing hearts. Selectivity is relative, so higher levels can block beta-2 receptors in the lungs and cause bronchospasm. Sudden withdrawal can cause rebound tachycardia and angina because beta receptors increase in number during long treatment. Chlorthalidone's long action makes low potassium and low sodium somewhat more likely than with hydrochlorothiazide. Uric acid retention can lead to gout, and higher glucose can appear. Because atenolol depends on the kidneys, it accumulates in kidney disease. For beta-blocker overdose, glucagon and high-dose insulin therapy are used, and atenolol can be removed by hemodialysis.", "fun": "Chlorthalidone's long half-life comes partly from hiding inside red blood cells, where it sticks to carbonic anhydrase and leaks out slowly.", "src": ["Tenoretic (atenolol and chlorthalidone) FDA label", "ALLHAT Collaborative Research Group, JAMA 2002", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "ezetimibe-simvastatin": {"origin": "The two halves of Vytorin have very different stories. Simvastatin descends from the fungus-derived statins. In the 1970s Akira Endo in Japan found mevastatin in Penicillium mold, and Merck scientists then isolated lovastatin from Aspergillus terreus. Merck chemically modified lovastatin by adding one methyl group to make simvastatin, approved in the US as Zocor in 1991. The 4S trial in 1994 showed it reduced deaths in people with heart disease. Ezetimibe was found at Schering-Plough in the 1990s almost by accident: chemists searching for ACAT inhibitors noticed that one compound lowered cholesterol in animals by blocking absorption instead. Refining it gave ezetimibe, approved as Zetia in 2002. The pair was approved as Vytorin in 2004. The IMPROVE-IT trial, reported in 2014, showed that adding ezetimibe to simvastatin after acute coronary syndrome gave a modest extra reduction in cardiovascular events.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Akira Endo discovers the first statin in a Penicillium mold."], ["1991", "FDA approves simvastatin (Zocor), a modified version of lovastatin."], ["1994", "The 4S trial shows simvastatin lowers mortality in coronary disease."], ["2002", "FDA approves ezetimibe (Zetia), the first NPC1L1 absorption blocker."], ["2004", "FDA approves Vytorin, combining ezetimibe and simvastatin."], ["2014", "IMPROVE-IT shows added benefit of ezetimibe on top of simvastatin."]], "cell": "Two steps of cholesterol supply are blocked at once. Ezetimibe binds Niemann-Pick C1-Like 1 (NPC1L1), a sterol transporter on the brush border of jejunal enterocytes. It stops cholesterol and plant sterols from being pulled into the cell, so less dietary and biliary cholesterol is packed into chylomicrons and delivered to the liver. Simvastatin is an inactive lactone prodrug. In the liver it opens into its beta-hydroxy acid form, which competitively inhibits HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting enzyme that turns HMG-CoA into mevalonate. With both supply lines reduced, the hepatocyte's cholesterol content falls. This activates SREBP-2, which increases transcription of the LDL receptor gene. More LDL receptors on the liver surface pull LDL particles from the blood. Blocking one pathway alone tends to make the other compensate, so the combination gives a larger LDL drop than either drug alone.", "body": [["Small intestine", "Ezetimibe blocks cholesterol uptake at the brush border, so more cholesterol passes out in the stool."], ["Liver", "Simvastatin lowers cholesterol synthesis, and the cholesterol-poor liver makes more LDL receptors to clear LDL from the blood."], ["Blood lipids", "LDL cholesterol and apolipoprotein B fall substantially, with a modest drop in triglycerides and small rise in HDL."], ["Arteries", "Less LDL entering artery walls slows plaque growth and lowers the risk of heart attack and stroke over years."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Statins can injure muscle cells, causing aches and, rarely, breakdown of muscle (rhabdomyolysis)."]], "journey": "Both are taken by mouth. Ezetimibe is quickly glucuronidated in the gut wall and liver to an active glucuronide, which is excreted in bile, reabsorbed and returned to the gut again and again. This enterohepatic recycling keeps it near its intestinal target and gives a half-life of about 22 hours. It leaves mostly in feces. Simvastatin undergoes heavy first-pass metabolism by CYP3A4 in the gut wall and liver, so only a small fraction reaches the blood, which suits a liver-targeted drug. The active acid has a short half-life of a few hours and is cleared mainly in bile. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors can raise simvastatin levels many times. LDL falls within about two weeks.", "sidefx": "Muscle pain and weakness come mainly from the statin. Blocking HMG-CoA reductase also lowers other mevalonate products, such as coenzyme Q10 and prenylated proteins, which may impair muscle cell energy and repair. Risk rises sharply when CYP3A4 inhibitors, gemfibrozil or high statin exposure push simvastatin levels up, and severe cases cause rhabdomyolysis with kidney injury from myoglobin. Because of this risk, FDA restricted the highest simvastatin strength in 2011. A rare immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy involves antibodies against HMG-CoA reductase and can persist after stopping. Mild liver enzyme rises and a small increase in diabetes risk are also seen. Ezetimibe alone is usually well tolerated. There is no antidote; stopping the drug and supportive care are used.", "fun": "Simvastatin differs from its parent lovastatin by a single extra methyl group, a tiny chemical change that made it more potent.", "src": ["Vytorin (ezetimibe and simvastatin) FDA label", "Cannon CP et al. IMPROVE-IT, N Engl J Med 2015", "Endo A. The discovery and development of HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors, J Lipid Res 1992"]}, "amlodipine-atorvastatin": {"origin": "Caduet came from Pfizer, which owned both of its parts. Amlodipine was developed by Pfizer scientists at Sandwich, England, in the 1980s as a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker with an unusually long half-life, and it was approved in the US as Norvasc in 1992. Atorvastatin was first made in 1985 by Bruce Roth at Parke-Davis in Michigan. It was a fully synthetic statin, unlike the earlier mold-derived drugs. Parke-Davis partnered with Pfizer to launch it as Lipitor in 1996, and it became one of the best-selling medicines in history. Pfizer combined the two in Caduet, approved in 2004, the first single pill to treat high blood pressure or angina together with high cholesterol. The idea was to make it easier for people to take treatments for two major heart risk factors.", "timeline": [["1985", "Bruce Roth at Parke-Davis synthesizes atorvastatin."], ["1992", "FDA approves amlodipine (Norvasc) from Pfizer."], ["1996", "FDA approves atorvastatin (Lipitor)."], ["2004", "FDA approves Caduet, combining amlodipine and atorvastatin."]], "cell": "Amlodipine blocks L-type voltage-gated calcium channels (Cav1.2). In arteriolar smooth muscle, calcium entering through these channels binds calmodulin, which activates myosin light-chain kinase and causes contraction. Amlodipine binds the channel more strongly when the membrane is depolarized, as it is in vascular smooth muscle, so it acts on arteries much more than on heart muscle. Less calcium entry means relaxation of arterioles, lower afterload and lower blood pressure. Coronary arteries also dilate, which helps angina. Atorvastatin competitively inhibits HMG-CoA reductase in hepatocytes, blocking the step that makes mevalonate. Lower hepatocyte cholesterol activates SREBP-2, which raises LDL receptor production. More receptors clear LDL and its precursors from the blood. Atorvastatin and its active metabolites also lower triglycerides.", "body": [["Arteries", "Arteriolar smooth muscle relaxes, so resistance and blood pressure fall; veins are affected little."], ["Heart", "Lower afterload and dilated coronary arteries reduce oxygen demand and improve supply, easing angina; there is little effect on heart rate or contractility at usual levels."], ["Liver", "Atorvastatin lowers cholesterol synthesis and increases LDL receptor numbers."], ["Blood lipids", "LDL cholesterol, apolipoprotein B and triglycerides fall, lowering long-term heart attack and stroke risk."], ["Legs and ankles", "Arteriolar dilation without matching venous dilation raises capillary pressure, causing ankle swelling."], ["Skeletal muscle", "The statin can cause muscle aches and, rarely, muscle breakdown."]], "journey": "Amlodipine is absorbed slowly from the gut, reaches peak levels 6 to 12 hours after a dose and has high bioavailability. It is broken down by CYP3A4 in the liver and its inactive metabolites leave mainly in urine. Its half-life of 30 to 50 hours gives a smooth, gentle effect and makes a missed dose less of a problem. Atorvastatin is absorbed quickly but undergoes heavy first-pass metabolism by CYP3A4, which produces active hydroxylated metabolites. These metabolites extend the LDL-lowering action, so its effective activity lasts much longer than its parent half-life of about 14 hours. It is cleared mainly in bile. Blood pressure and LDL effects both build over one to two weeks.", "sidefx": "Ankle swelling is the classic amlodipine effect. It happens because arterioles open while veins do not, so fluid is pushed out of capillaries; diuretics do little for it. Flushing, headache and dizziness also come from vasodilation, and too much can cause low blood pressure with reflex tachycardia. Atorvastatin can cause muscle aches. Reduced mevalonate products such as coenzyme Q10 may affect muscle energy, and severe cases cause rhabdomyolysis, more likely with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors that raise statin levels. Liver enzyme rises and a small increase in diabetes risk occur. For calcium channel blocker overdose, treatment includes calcium, vasopressors and high-dose insulin therapy.", "fun": "Atorvastatin was the fifth statin to reach the market, and some executives doubted it was needed, yet it went on to become the world's top-selling drug for years.", "src": ["Caduet (amlodipine and atorvastatin) FDA label", "Roth BD. The discovery and development of atorvastatin, Prog Med Chem 2002", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "aspirin-dipyridamole": {"origin": "Aspirin traces back to willow bark, used for pain and fever since ancient times. In 1897 Felix Hoffmann at Bayer made a stable acetylated form of salicylic acid, sold as Aspirin from 1899. Its effect on platelets was only understood in 1971, when John Vane showed that aspirin blocks prostaglandin synthesis, work that shared the 1982 Nobel Prize. Dipyridamole was developed by Boehringer Ingelheim in Germany and introduced around 1959 as Persantine, first used as a coronary vasodilator for angina. Researchers later found it reduced platelet stickiness. Boehringer created an extended-release capsule pairing dipyridamole with low-dose aspirin, and after the European Stroke Prevention Study 2 (ESPS-2) in 1996 showed fewer strokes than with aspirin alone, FDA approved Aggrenox in 1999 to reduce the risk of stroke after a transient ischemic attack or ischemic stroke.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Willow bark is used for pain and fever."], ["1897", "Felix Hoffmann at Bayer makes acetylsalicylic acid."], ["1959", "Boehringer Ingelheim introduces dipyridamole (Persantine)."], ["1971", "John Vane shows aspirin blocks prostaglandin synthesis."], ["1996", "The ESPS-2 trial reports fewer strokes with aspirin plus dipyridamole."], ["1999", "FDA approves Aggrenox for secondary stroke prevention."]], "cell": "The two parts work on separate platelet signals. Aspirin transfers its acetyl group to serine 529 of cyclooxygenase-1 (COX-1), permanently blocking the enzyme. Platelets have no nucleus and cannot make new COX-1, so their production of thromboxane A2, a strong activator and vasoconstrictor, is lost for the platelet's 7 to 10 day life. Dipyridamole blocks the equilibrative nucleoside transporter ENT1 on red cells and endothelium, so adenosine stays outside cells longer. Adenosine activates A2A receptors on platelets, which are Gs coupled and raise cAMP. Dipyridamole also inhibits platelet phosphodiesterases (PDE3 and PDE5), slowing breakdown of cAMP and cGMP. Higher cAMP and cGMP lower free calcium inside platelets, reducing shape change, granule release and glycoprotein IIb/IIIa activation. Adenosine also dilates blood vessels.", "body": [["Platelets", "Thromboxane loss plus higher cAMP and cGMP make platelets less likely to stick and clump, lowering clot formation in brain arteries."], ["Brain circulation", "Fewer platelet clots means fewer recurrent ischemic strokes in people who have already had a stroke or TIA."], ["Blood vessels", "Extra adenosine relaxes vascular smooth muscle, which can widen vessels in the head and cause headache."], ["Stomach", "Loss of COX-1 prostaglandins weakens the protective lining, raising the risk of ulcers and bleeding."], ["Heart", "Adenosine-driven vasodilation can redirect blood away from narrowed coronary arteries (coronary steal), sometimes worsening chest pain."]], "journey": "The capsule contains immediate-release aspirin and extended-release dipyridamole pellets. Aspirin is absorbed quickly and hydrolyzed within minutes to salicylate, but because platelet COX-1 is acetylated in the portal blood during absorption, the effect outlasts the drug. Salicylate is cleared by the kidneys. The dipyridamole pellets release drug gradually in the gut. Dipyridamole is very highly protein bound, is glucuronidated in the liver and leaves mainly in bile, with a half-life of about 13 hours. The aspirin effect on platelets lasts the life of each platelet, about a week to ten days.", "sidefx": "Headache is very common at the start of treatment. It comes from adenosine-mediated dilation of blood vessels in the head and usually fades over the first weeks. Dizziness and low blood pressure arise the same way. Dyspepsia, abdominal pain and GI bleeding reflect aspirin's loss of protective stomach prostaglandins plus its antiplatelet effect. Bleeding anywhere, including inside the skull, is the major serious risk. Chest pain in people with coronary disease may come from coronary steal. There is no specific reversal for either drug; platelet transfusion can replace inhibited platelets in serious bleeding. Dipyridamole's adenosine effect can be blocked by caffeine or aminophylline, which antagonize adenosine receptors.", "fun": "Dipyridamole's ability to raise adenosine is also used in cardiology: given IV, it serves as a pharmacologic stress agent for heart imaging.", "src": ["Aggrenox (aspirin and extended-release dipyridamole) FDA label", "Diener HC et al. ESPS-2, J Neurol Sci 1996", "Vane JR. Nature New Biology 1971"]}, "spironolactone-hydrochlorothiazide": {"origin": "Spironolactone was designed at G. D. Searle and Company in Chicago in the 1950s, shortly after aldosterone itself was identified in 1953. Searle chemists, including John Cella, built steroid molecules that looked like aldosterone but would block its receptor. Spironolactone was approved in the US as Aldactone around 1960, the first drug made to oppose aldosterone. Hydrochlorothiazide came from the thiazide work at Merck Sharp & Dohme in the late 1950s. Searle combined the two as Aldactazide in the early 1960s. The logic was simple: the thiazide wastes potassium, and spironolactone saves it, so together they lower blood pressure and remove fluid with less change in potassium. The brand Aldactazide is no longer marketed, but generic versions remain available.", "timeline": [["1953", "Aldosterone is isolated and identified."], ["1957", "Searle scientists synthesize spironolactone."], ["1959", "Hydrochlorothiazide enters US practice."], ["1960", "Spironolactone (Aldactone) reaches the US market."], ["1960s", "Searle introduces Aldactazide, combining spironolactone and hydrochlorothiazide."]], "cell": "Hydrochlorothiazide inhibits the Na-Cl cotransporter (NCC) in the distal convoluted tubule, so more sodium and chloride stay in the tubule. When that extra sodium reaches the collecting duct, principal cells reabsorb it through the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC), which makes the lumen more negative and drives potassium out through ROMK channels. Spironolactone blocks this second step. It competitively antagonizes the mineralocorticoid receptor inside principal cells. Normally aldosterone binds this receptor, the complex moves to the nucleus and increases production of ENaC, Na-K-ATPase and other proteins. Without that signal, fewer channels and pumps are made, so less sodium is reabsorbed and less potassium and hydrogen are secreted. Because it works through gene expression, its effect builds over days. Spironolactone also blocks androgen receptors and weakly affects progesterone receptors.", "body": [["Kidney (distal tubule)", "The thiazide part blocks salt reabsorption, causing mild natriuresis and volume loss."], ["Kidney (collecting duct)", "Spironolactone reduces sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion, so potassium is conserved while sodium leaves."], ["Blood pressure", "Lower volume and, over time, lower vascular resistance reduce blood pressure."], ["Fluid and edema", "In edema from cirrhosis or nephrotic syndrome, blocking high aldosterone helps remove extra fluid."], ["Hormone tissues", "Androgen receptor blockade can cause breast enlargement in men and menstrual changes in women."], ["Metabolism", "The thiazide can raise uric acid and glucose; spironolactone partly offsets thiazide potassium loss."]], "journey": "Both are taken by mouth. Spironolactone is absorbed well, especially with food, and is quickly and extensively metabolized in the liver to active metabolites such as canrenone and 7-alpha-thiomethylspironolactone. The parent drug lasts only about an hour or two in plasma, but the metabolites last 13 to 24 hours, and because the drug acts through gene transcription, its full effect takes two to three days to build and lingers for days after stopping. Metabolites leave in urine and bile. Hydrochlorothiazide is not metabolized; it is secreted into the proximal tubule by organic anion transporters and leaves unchanged in urine with a half-life of about 6 to 15 hours.", "sidefx": "The main danger is high potassium. Spironolactone reduces potassium secretion, and in kidney disease or with ACE inhibitors, ARBs, potassium supplements or NSAIDs, potassium can rise high enough to cause dangerous heart rhythms. The thiazide part can cause low sodium, dehydration, kidney injury, higher uric acid and higher glucose. Breast enlargement and tenderness in men, decreased libido and menstrual irregularity come from spironolactone's blockade of androgen receptors and effects on progesterone receptors. In advanced cirrhosis, fluid and electrolyte shifts can trigger hepatic encephalopathy. Severe hyperkalemia is treated with IV calcium to protect the heart, insulin with glucose and other measures to shift or remove potassium.", "fun": "Spironolactone's anti-androgen effect is put to use on purpose: it is widely used off label for acne and excess hair growth in women.", "src": ["Spironolactone and hydrochlorothiazide tablets FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "triamterene-hydrochlorothiazide": {"origin": "Triamterene was developed in the early 1960s at Smith Kline & French Laboratories. It is a pteridine, chemically related to folic acid, and it was found to cause sodium loss while keeping potassium, without depending on aldosterone. SK&F paired it with hydrochlorothiazide, the Merck thiazide of the late 1950s, and the capsule Dyazide was approved in 1965. Dyazide became one of the most prescribed blood pressure medicines in the US in the 1970s and 1980s. Tablet versions such as Maxzide followed in the 1980s. The point of the pairing was to get the blood pressure and fluid benefits of a thiazide while limiting the potassium loss that thiazides cause on their own.", "timeline": [["1959", "Hydrochlorothiazide enters US practice."], ["1960s", "Smith Kline & French develops triamterene, a potassium-sparing pteridine."], ["1965", "FDA approves Dyazide capsules (triamterene and hydrochlorothiazide)."], ["1980s", "Tablet forms such as Maxzide reach the market."]], "cell": "Hydrochlorothiazide blocks the Na-Cl cotransporter (NCC) in the distal convoluted tubule, so more sodium reaches the collecting duct. There, principal cells normally take up sodium through the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) on their luminal surface. That sodium entry makes the lumen negative, which pulls potassium out through ROMK channels and helps intercalated cells secrete hydrogen. Triamterene plugs ENaC directly from the luminal side. This works regardless of aldosterone levels, unlike spironolactone, which acts on the hormone receptor. With ENaC blocked, sodium reabsorption falls a little more, and the electrical drive for potassium and hydrogen secretion is lost, so potassium is conserved. The two effects largely cancel each other on potassium while adding up on sodium loss.", "body": [["Kidney (distal tubule)", "The thiazide blocks salt reabsorption, producing diuresis and lower blood volume."], ["Kidney (collecting duct)", "Triamterene blocks ENaC, which saves potassium and hydrogen that the thiazide would otherwise make the kidney waste."], ["Blood pressure", "Lower volume and later lower vascular resistance bring blood pressure down."], ["Electrolytes", "Potassium tends to stay stable, but can rise in kidney disease or with other potassium-raising drugs."], ["Kidneys (stones)", "Triamterene is poorly soluble and can crystallize in urine, contributing to kidney stones."], ["Blood cells", "As a weak folic acid antagonist, triamterene can rarely contribute to megaloblastic anemia in people with low folate."]], "journey": "Both drugs are taken by mouth and absorbed fairly well. Triamterene is hydroxylated and then sulfated in the liver to an active sulfate conjugate that also blocks ENaC. Both parent and metabolite are cleared by the kidneys, and their half-lives are short, about one and a half to two and a half hours, so kidney function strongly affects levels. Hydrochlorothiazide is not metabolized and is secreted into the tubule then excreted unchanged in urine. Diuresis begins within two to four hours. Capsule and tablet products are not equivalent in how much drug is absorbed, so they are not swapped mg for mg.", "sidefx": "High potassium is the key risk because triamterene blocks potassium secretion. It is more likely with kidney disease, diabetes, older age, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, potassium supplements or NSAIDs. NSAIDs are a special concern because the combination with triamterene can cause acute kidney injury. The thiazide part causes low sodium, dehydration, higher uric acid and higher glucose. Kidney stones occur because triamterene can crystallize in concentrated urine. Triamterene may turn urine a bluish fluorescent color, which is harmless. Photosensitivity is linked to both parts. Severe hyperkalemia is treated with IV calcium to stabilize the heart, then insulin with glucose and potassium removal.", "fun": "Triamterene glows: under ultraviolet light, urine of people taking it can show a blue fluorescence.", "src": ["Dyazide (triamterene and hydrochlorothiazide) FDA label", "Maxzide FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "plozasiran": {"origin": "Plozasiran grew from the RNA interference discovery of Andrew Fire and Craig Mello, who showed in 1998 that double-stranded RNA silences matching genes, work that won the 2006 Nobel Prize. Turning this into medicines required ways to deliver small interfering RNA (siRNA) to the right tissue. Attaching the sugar N-acetylgalactosamine (GalNAc) sends siRNA to liver cells. Arrowhead Pharmaceuticals in Pasadena used this approach to design plozasiran against the APOC3 gene, which makes apolipoprotein C-III, a protein that slows triglyceride clearance. Genetic studies had shown that people born with APOC3 loss-of-function variants have low triglycerides and lower heart risk. After the PALISADE trial in familial chylomicronemia syndrome (FCS), FDA approved plozasiran as Redemplo in November 2025 to reduce triglycerides in adults with FCS, a rare disease with very high triglycerides and pancreatitis risk.", "timeline": [["1998", "Fire and Mello describe RNA interference."], ["2006", "Fire and Mello receive the Nobel Prize for RNA interference."], ["2008", "Population genetic studies link APOC3 loss-of-function variants to low triglycerides."], ["2025", "FDA approves plozasiran (Redemplo) for familial chylomicronemia syndrome."]], "cell": "Plozasiran is a double-stranded siRNA with chemically modified nucleotides that resist nucleases, attached to a GalNAc cluster. After injection, GalNAc binds the asialoglycoprotein receptor, which is found in large numbers almost only on hepatocytes, and the siRNA is taken in by endocytosis. A small fraction escapes the endosome into the cytoplasm, where the guide strand loads into the RNA-induced silencing complex (RISC). RISC uses the guide strand to find APOC3 messenger RNA by base pairing, and its Argonaute-2 enzyme cuts it. The cut mRNA is degraded, so less apolipoprotein C-III is made. ApoC-III normally inhibits lipoprotein lipase and blocks hepatic uptake of triglyceride-rich remnants. With less apoC-III, triglyceride-rich particles are broken down and cleared faster. Because the loaded RISC can cut many mRNA copies and persists for weeks, effects last far longer than the drug stays in plasma. This also works in FCS where lipoprotein lipase activity is very low, likely through the liver uptake pathway.", "body": [["Liver", "Hepatocytes make much less apolipoprotein C-III, which is the direct effect of silencing the APOC3 gene."], ["Blood lipids", "Triglycerides and chylomicrons fall sharply because their clearance speeds up."], ["Pancreas", "Very high triglycerides can trigger acute pancreatitis; lowering them reduces this risk, though pancreatitis can still occur from the underlying disease."], ["Glucose metabolism", "Rises in blood glucose and HbA1c were seen in trials, so glucose is followed during treatment."], ["Skin (injection site)", "The injection can cause redness, pain or swelling where it is given."]], "journey": "Plozasiran is injected under the skin. It is absorbed into the blood and taken up rapidly by the liver through the asialoglycoprotein receptor, so plasma levels peak within hours and fall to near zero within a day or two. Its plasma half-life is only a few hours. Inside hepatocytes, however, the siRNA and RISC complexes persist for weeks, which is why it is given only every three months. It is not broken down by CYP enzymes. Nucleases gradually cut it into shorter pieces, and a small amount leaves unchanged in urine. Triglycerides begin to fall within weeks.", "sidefx": "Hyperglycemia and higher HbA1c were the most notable laboratory effects in trials; the exact cause is not fully established. Headache, nausea and injection site reactions are common and mostly mild. Injection reactions reflect local immune responses to the oligonucleotide. Pancreatitis can still happen because FCS itself carries a high baseline risk. GalNAc delivery to the liver limits exposure of other tissues, which is why off-target effects appear modest. There is no specific antidote, and because effects last months, they cannot be rapidly reversed.", "fun": "People born with a broken copy of APOC3, studied in groups such as the Amish of Lancaster County, have naturally low triglycerides, which helped convince researchers that silencing this gene was safe to try.", "src": ["Redemplo (plozasiran) FDA label", "Fire A, Mello CC et al. Nature 1998", "Watts GF et al. PALISADE, N Engl J Med 2025"]}, "atrasentan": {"origin": "Endothelin-1, the most powerful vessel-constricting peptide known, was discovered in 1988 by Masashi Yanagisawa and colleagues in Japan. Drug companies then raced to build endothelin receptor blockers. Atrasentan was developed by Abbott Laboratories in the 1990s as a selective endothelin A receptor antagonist and was first studied for prostate cancer, where it did not show enough benefit in large trials. It was later tested in diabetic kidney disease. Chinook Therapeutics, later acquired by Novartis, took it forward for IgA nephropathy, the most common primary glomerulonephritis worldwide. Based on reduced proteinuria in the ALIGN trial, FDA granted accelerated approval for Vanrafia in April 2025 to reduce proteinuria in adults with primary IgA nephropathy at risk of disease progression. Continued approval may depend on confirming a benefit on kidney function.", "timeline": [["1988", "Yanagisawa and colleagues discover endothelin-1."], ["1990s", "Abbott develops atrasentan as a selective ETA antagonist."], ["2000s", "Atrasentan fails to show enough benefit in prostate cancer trials."], ["2025", "FDA grants accelerated approval for atrasentan (Vanrafia) in IgA nephropathy."]], "cell": "Endothelin-1 acts through two G protein-coupled receptors. The endothelin type A (ETA) receptor is Gq coupled. When activated, it raises intracellular calcium and turns on protein kinase C and MAP kinase signals that constrict vessels and promote cell growth, inflammation and scar formation. The type B (ETB) receptor on endothelium mainly releases nitric oxide and clears endothelin from the blood. Atrasentan is a competitive, highly selective ETA antagonist, so it blocks the harmful signals while leaving ETB clearance mostly intact. In IgA nephropathy, endothelin-1 is overproduced in the kidney. Blocking ETA on mesangial cells reduces their proliferation and matrix production. On podocytes, it reduces cytoskeleton injury and loss of the filtration barrier proteins. ETA blockade also relaxes efferent and afferent arterioles and lowers pressure inside the glomerulus. Together these effects lower protein leaking into the urine.", "body": [["Glomeruli", "Less endothelin signaling protects podocytes and mesangial cells and lowers glomerular pressure, so less protein leaks into urine."], ["Kidney tissue", "Reduced inflammation and fibrosis signals are expected to slow scarring over time."], ["Blood vessels", "Mild vasodilation lowers blood pressure slightly."], ["Fluid balance", "Endothelin blockade promotes sodium and water retention in the collecting duct, which can cause edema."], ["Blood", "Fluid retention dilutes red blood cells, so hemoglobin can fall (hemodilution)."], ["Reproductive system", "ETA blockade is toxic to the developing embryo, and endothelin blockers can lower sperm counts."]], "journey": "Atrasentan is taken by mouth and absorbed quickly, peaking about half an hour to an hour and a half after a dose. More than 99 percent is bound to plasma proteins. It is taken up into the liver by the OATP1B1 and OATP1B3 transporters and metabolized mainly by CYP3A4, then excreted mostly in feces. Its long half-life of about 41 hours supports once-daily dosing and means levels build over several days. Drugs that block OATP1B or strongly affect CYP3A can change its levels. Proteinuria falls gradually over weeks.", "sidefx": "The endothelin family has a known pattern of harms tied to its mechanism. Endothelin signaling is essential for the development of the head, face, heart and great vessels in the embryo, so ETA blockade causes serious birth defects; this is why pregnancy is excluded before and during treatment and the label carries a boxed warning. Edema occurs because blocking endothelin in the collecting duct promotes sodium and water retention, and it can worsen heart failure. Anemia mainly reflects this extra fluid diluting the blood. Liver enzyme rises have been seen with endothelin antagonists as a class. Reduced sperm counts are also a class effect. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Endothelin-1 was found in the culture medium of pig aortic endothelial cells, and within months of its discovery it was recognized as the most potent vasoconstrictor ever identified.", "src": ["Vanrafia (atrasentan) FDA label", "Yanagisawa M et al. Nature 1988", "Heerspink HJL et al. ALIGN, N Engl J Med 2025"]}, "amlodipine-perindopril": {"origin": "Perindopril was developed by the French company Servier in the 1980s, one of the ACE inhibitors that followed captopril. The ACE inhibitor story began with the venom of the Brazilian pit viper Bothrops jararaca. Sergio Ferreira found peptides in it that boosted bradykinin, and scientists at Squibb, led by David Cushman and Miguel Ondetti, used them to design captopril in the 1970s. Perindopril was later tested in large trials such as EUROPA and, with amlodipine, ASCOT, which compared an amlodipine-based regimen (with perindopril added as needed) against an older beta-blocker approach. Amlodipine itself was developed by Pfizer in the 1980s. The fixed-dose pill of perindopril arginine and amlodipine was approved in the US as Prestalia in 2015 for high blood pressure.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Squibb scientists design captopril from pit viper venom peptides."], ["1980s", "Servier develops perindopril."], ["1992", "FDA approves amlodipine (Norvasc)."], ["2005", "The ASCOT trial reports results for an amlodipine-based regimen with perindopril."], ["2015", "FDA approves Prestalia (perindopril arginine and amlodipine)."]], "cell": "Perindopril is a prodrug. After absorption it is hydrolyzed by liver esterases to perindoprilat, which binds the zinc-containing active site of angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE). ACE normally cuts angiotensin I into angiotensin II, which constricts arterioles through AT1 receptors and stimulates aldosterone release. Blocking ACE lowers angiotensin II and aldosterone, so vessels relax and the kidney keeps less sodium and loses less potassium. ACE is the same enzyme as kininase II, which breaks down bradykinin. Higher bradykinin adds vasodilation through nitric oxide and prostaglandins. Amlodipine blocks L-type voltage-gated calcium channels (Cav1.2) in arteriolar smooth muscle. Less calcium entry means less calmodulin and myosin light-chain kinase activity, so the muscle relaxes and afterload falls. The two drugs lower blood pressure through different routes, and the ACE inhibitor also dilates veins, which helps limit amlodipine ankle swelling.", "body": [["Arteries", "Amlodipine relaxes arteriolar muscle while lower angiotensin II removes a constricting signal, lowering resistance and blood pressure."], ["Kidney", "Less aldosterone means mild sodium loss and potassium retention; efferent arteriole dilation lowers pressure inside the glomerulus."], ["Heart", "Lower afterload reduces workload; over time ACE inhibition limits harmful remodeling of the heart muscle."], ["Lungs and airway", "Bradykinin build-up can irritate airway nerves and cause a dry cough."], ["Legs and ankles", "Amlodipine can cause ankle edema; the ACE inhibitor's venous dilation may partly lessen it."]], "journey": "Both parts are taken by mouth. Perindopril arginine is absorbed and converted in the liver to active perindoprilat, which peaks several hours later. Perindoprilat is cleared mainly by the kidneys. It has an effective half-life of several hours but a long terminal phase because it binds tightly to ACE, giving a full day of action. Kidney disease raises its levels. Amlodipine is absorbed slowly, peaking 6 to 12 hours after the dose. It is broken down by CYP3A4 in the liver to inactive products that leave in urine, with a long half-life of 30 to 50 hours.", "sidefx": "Dry cough comes from bradykinin and substance P that build up when ACE cannot break them down. Angioedema, rare but dangerous, is also bradykinin driven and is more common in Black patients; it can occur even after years of use. High potassium and kidney injury happen because less aldosterone reduces potassium secretion and because, when kidneys depend on angiotensin II to keep filtration up (for example with renal artery stenosis or dehydration), filtration falls. ACE inhibitors cause severe birth defects in the second and third trimesters, which is a boxed warning. Amlodipine causes ankle edema, flushing and headache from arteriolar dilation. ACE inhibitor angioedema does not respond well to epinephrine and antihistamines alone because it is bradykinin mediated; airway protection is the priority.", "fun": "The first ACE inhibitors were inspired by a snake: peptides from Bothrops jararaca venom made prey collapse by dropping their blood pressure.", "src": ["Prestalia (perindopril arginine and amlodipine) FDA label", "Dahlof B et al. ASCOT-BPLA, Lancet 2005", "Cushman DW, Ondetti MA. History of the design of captopril, Hypertension 1991"]}, "azilsartan-chlorthalidone": {"origin": "Angiotensin II receptor blockers (ARBs) began with weak imidazole compounds found at Takeda in Japan in the early 1980s. DuPont chemists built on them to make losartan, the first ARB, approved in 1995. Takeda later designed its own ARBs, first candesartan and then azilsartan. Azilsartan is given as the prodrug azilsartan medoxomil, and it was approved as Edarbi in 2011. Chlorthalidone, a long-acting thiazide-like diuretic from the Swiss company Geigy, had been in use since about 1960 and had strong outcome data from the ALLHAT trial. Takeda paired the two in Edarbyclor, approved in December 2011. It was one of the first fixed-dose ARB combinations to use chlorthalidone rather than hydrochlorothiazide.", "timeline": [["1960", "Chlorthalidone reaches the US market from Geigy."], ["1982", "Takeda reports early imidazole angiotensin II antagonists."], ["1995", "FDA approves losartan, the first ARB."], ["2011", "FDA approves azilsartan medoxomil (Edarbi) and later Edarbyclor with chlorthalidone."]], "cell": "Azilsartan medoxomil is hydrolyzed in the gut to azilsartan, which binds the angiotensin II type 1 (AT1) receptor very tightly and dissociates slowly. AT1 is a Gq-coupled receptor. Angiotensin II normally activates it to raise calcium in vascular smooth muscle (vasoconstriction), stimulate aldosterone release from the adrenal zona glomerulosa, increase sodium reabsorption in the proximal tubule, and promote thirst and sympathetic activity. Blocking AT1 reverses all of these. Unlike ACE inhibitors, ARBs do not block bradykinin breakdown, so cough is uncommon. Chlorthalidone inhibits the Na-Cl cotransporter (NCC) in the distal convoluted tubule, causing natriuresis and volume loss. Diuretic volume loss activates renin, but the ARB blocks the resulting angiotensin II at its receptor, so the two effects add. The ARB's lower aldosterone also partly offsets the potassium loss caused by the diuretic.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "AT1 blockade relaxes arterioles and lowers vascular resistance, bringing blood pressure down."], ["Adrenal glands", "Less aldosterone release means less sodium retention and less potassium loss."], ["Kidney (tubule)", "Chlorthalidone blocks salt reabsorption in the distal tubule for a long-lasting diuresis."], ["Kidney (glomerulus)", "Blocking angiotensin II dilates the efferent arteriole, lowering glomerular pressure; creatinine can rise slightly at the start."], ["Electrolytes", "Sodium and potassium can drop from the diuretic, and uric acid can rise; the ARB partly protects potassium."]], "journey": "Azilsartan medoxomil is converted to active azilsartan by hydrolysis in the gut during absorption, so the prodrug is not found in plasma. Azilsartan peaks in one and a half to three hours, is more than 99 percent protein bound and is metabolized mainly by CYP2C9 to inactive products, then excreted mostly in feces. Its half-life is about 11 hours. Chlorthalidone is absorbed slowly, stored in red blood cells bound to carbonic anhydrase, and excreted unchanged in urine with a half-life of about 45 hours. Together they give blood pressure control well beyond 24 hours.", "sidefx": "Dizziness and low blood pressure come from the combined fall in vascular resistance and volume, worse in people who are already dehydrated. Creatinine may rise, especially early, because angiotensin II normally keeps the efferent arteriole tight to hold filtration up; in renal artery stenosis or severe volume loss this can cause acute kidney injury. Chlorthalidone causes low potassium, low sodium and higher uric acid. Like all drugs acting on the renin-angiotensin system, the ARB causes fetal kidney damage and other harm in the second and third trimesters, a boxed warning. Angioedema is rare. There is no antidote; overdose care is supportive with fluids and blood pressure support.", "fun": "Azilsartan has a five-membered oxadiazolone ring where most other ARBs have a tetrazole, a design choice that helps it cling to the AT1 receptor.", "src": ["Edarbyclor (azilsartan medoxomil and chlorthalidone) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "olmesartan-hydrochlorothiazide": {"origin": "Olmesartan was developed by the Japanese company Sankyo (now Daiichi Sankyo) in the 1990s, part of the wave of ARBs that followed DuPont's losartan. It was approved in the US as Benicar in 2002, and the combination with hydrochlorothiazide, Benicar HCT, followed in 2003. Hydrochlorothiazide was the classic thiazide diuretic from Merck in the late 1950s. In 2013, FDA added a warning to olmesartan products after case reports, many from the Mayo Clinic, linked olmesartan to a rare sprue-like enteropathy with severe chronic diarrhea and weight loss, which improves when the drug is stopped.", "timeline": [["1959", "Hydrochlorothiazide enters US practice."], ["1995", "FDA approves losartan, the first ARB."], ["2002", "FDA approves olmesartan medoxomil (Benicar)."], ["2003", "FDA approves Benicar HCT."], ["2013", "FDA warns about sprue-like enteropathy with olmesartan."]], "cell": "Olmesartan medoxomil is a prodrug that is hydrolyzed in the gut wall to olmesartan. Olmesartan is a selective, insurmountable antagonist of the angiotensin II type 1 (AT1) receptor. With AT1 blocked, angiotensin II cannot activate Gq signaling in vascular smooth muscle, so calcium stays low and arterioles relax. It also cannot stimulate aldosterone release, proximal tubule sodium reabsorption or sympathetic activation. Angiotensin II levels rise because feedback on renin is lost, but the blocked receptor cannot respond. Hydrochlorothiazide inhibits the Na-Cl cotransporter (NCC) in the distal convoluted tubule, causing mild natriuresis and loss of volume. The diuretic raises renin, which would normally limit its effect, but the ARB blocks the result, so the combination lowers blood pressure more than either alone. Lower aldosterone partly offsets thiazide potassium loss.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Blocking AT1 relaxes arterioles and lowers resistance, reducing blood pressure."], ["Adrenal glands", "Less aldosterone signaling reduces sodium retention and potassium secretion."], ["Kidney (tubule)", "Hydrochlorothiazide blocks salt reabsorption in the distal tubule, lowering blood volume."], ["Kidney (glomerulus)", "Efferent arteriole relaxation lowers glomerular pressure; creatinine may rise a little early on."], ["Small intestine", "Rarely, olmesartan causes villous atrophy similar to celiac disease, leading to severe diarrhea and weight loss."], ["Electrolytes and urate", "The thiazide can lower potassium and sodium and raise uric acid and glucose."]], "journey": "Olmesartan medoxomil is fully converted to olmesartan during absorption, with bioavailability of about a quarter. Olmesartan peaks in one to two hours, is highly protein bound and is not metabolized by CYP enzymes, so CYP-related drug interactions are minimal. It is cleared in both urine and bile with a half-life of about 13 hours. Hydrochlorothiazide is secreted into the proximal tubule and excreted unchanged in urine with a half-life of about 6 to 15 hours. Once-daily dosing covers the day.", "sidefx": "Dizziness and low blood pressure reflect the combined effects on vessels and volume. Kidney injury can occur when the kidney depends on angiotensin II to maintain filtration, as with dehydration, heart failure or renal artery stenosis. Thiazide effects include low potassium, low sodium, higher uric acid with gout and higher glucose. ARBs are harmful to the fetus in the second and third trimesters (boxed warning). Olmesartan's sprue-like enteropathy is thought to be immune mediated in the gut lining, though the exact mechanism is unclear; it resolves after stopping. Hydrochlorothiazide can cause acute myopia with angle-closure glaucoma, and long-term use is linked to higher risk of nonmelanoma skin cancer. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Olmesartan-associated enteropathy can look so much like celiac disease on biopsy that some people were first misdiagnosed, yet it does not improve with a gluten-free diet.", "src": ["Benicar HCT (olmesartan medoxomil and hydrochlorothiazide) FDA label", "Rubio-Tapia A et al. Mayo Clin Proc 2012", "FDA Drug Safety Communication, July 2013"]}, "telmisartan-amlodipine": {"origin": "Telmisartan was developed by Boehringer Ingelheim in Germany and approved in the US as Micardis in 1998. It has the longest half-life of the common ARBs and, unusually, also partly activates PPAR-gamma, the receptor targeted by the glitazone diabetes drugs. The large ONTARGET trial, published in 2008, showed telmisartan was about as effective as the ACE inhibitor ramipril for reducing cardiovascular events in high-risk people. Amlodipine is Pfizer's long-acting calcium channel blocker, approved in 1992. Boehringer combined the two as Twynsta, approved in 2009, pairing two drugs with long half-lives and different mechanisms. Generic telmisartan and amlodipine tablets are now available.", "timeline": [["1992", "FDA approves amlodipine (Norvasc)."], ["1998", "FDA approves telmisartan (Micardis) from Boehringer Ingelheim."], ["2008", "The ONTARGET trial compares telmisartan with ramipril."], ["2009", "FDA approves Twynsta (telmisartan and amlodipine)."]], "cell": "Telmisartan is an active drug, not a prodrug, and a selective antagonist of the angiotensin II type 1 (AT1) receptor with slow dissociation. Blocking AT1 prevents angiotensin II from triggering Gq-mediated calcium rise in vascular smooth muscle, aldosterone release, sodium reabsorption and sympathetic activation, so vessels relax and blood pressure falls. Telmisartan is also a partial agonist of PPAR-gamma, a nuclear receptor that regulates insulin-sensitivity genes; the clinical importance of this is uncertain. Amlodipine binds L-type voltage-gated calcium channels (Cav1.2) in arteriolar smooth muscle, especially in the depolarized state, reducing calcium entry, calmodulin activation and myosin light-chain phosphorylation. Arterioles relax and afterload drops. Amlodipine dilation can activate renin, and the ARB blocks the result, so the two agents complement each other.", "body": [["Arteries", "Two separate relaxing signals (less calcium entry and less angiotensin II action) lower vascular resistance and blood pressure."], ["Adrenal glands", "AT1 blockade lowers aldosterone, reducing sodium retention and potassium loss."], ["Heart", "Lower afterload reduces cardiac work, and amlodipine dilates coronary arteries."], ["Kidney", "Lower glomerular pressure from efferent dilation; creatinine and potassium can rise in vulnerable people."], ["Legs and ankles", "Amlodipine causes ankle edema by dilating arterioles more than veins; the ARB may lessen this a little."]], "journey": "Telmisartan is absorbed quickly, peaks within about an hour, and is the most lipid soluble ARB, so it spreads widely into tissues. It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes; instead it is glucuronidated and excreted almost entirely in bile and feces, with a half-life of about 24 hours. Very little leaves in urine, so liver disease raises its levels more than kidney disease. Amlodipine is absorbed slowly, peaks 6 to 12 hours later, is metabolized by CYP3A4 and leaves as inactive metabolites in urine, with a half-life of 30 to 50 hours.", "sidefx": "Dizziness and low blood pressure come from the combined vasodilation. Amlodipine's ankle swelling is the most common bothersome effect, caused by higher capillary pressure from arteriolar dilation. High potassium and acute kidney injury can occur because the ARB lowers aldosterone and removes angiotensin II support of filtration, especially with dehydration, NSAIDs or kidney artery narrowing. Like all renin-angiotensin drugs, telmisartan is harmful to the fetus in the second and third trimesters (boxed warning). Back pain was reported in trials. Angioedema is rare. For overdose, calcium, vasopressors and high-dose insulin therapy are used for the calcium channel blocker part.", "fun": "Telmisartan's long half-life means its blood pressure effect is still present in the early morning hours before the next dose, when strokes and heart attacks most often happen.", "src": ["Twynsta (telmisartan and amlodipine) FDA label", "ONTARGET Investigators, N Engl J Med 2008", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "candesartan-hydrochlorothiazide": {"origin": "Candesartan was discovered by Takeda in Japan, the company whose early imidazole compounds had inspired the first ARBs in the 1980s, and it was co-developed with Astra (later AstraZeneca). It was approved in the US as Atacand in 1998 for high blood pressure and later also for heart failure, supported by the CHARM trials. The combination with hydrochlorothiazide, Atacand HCT, followed a few years later. Hydrochlorothiazide is the classic thiazide diuretic developed by Merck chemists in the late 1950s. Candesartan is notable for binding the AT1 receptor so tightly that higher angiotensin II levels cannot easily overcome it.", "timeline": [["1959", "Hydrochlorothiazide enters US practice."], ["1980s", "Takeda discovers imidazole angiotensin II antagonists that lead to the ARB class."], ["1998", "FDA approves candesartan cilexetil (Atacand)."], ["2003", "The CHARM program reports candesartan benefits in heart failure."]], "cell": "Candesartan cilexetil is an ester prodrug that is fully hydrolyzed to candesartan in the intestinal wall during absorption. Candesartan binds the angiotensin II type 1 (AT1) receptor with very high affinity and dissociates slowly, so its blockade is insurmountable: even high angiotensin II levels cannot displace it. Without AT1 signaling, Gq-coupled calcium rise in vascular smooth muscle is prevented, arterioles relax, aldosterone release falls, proximal sodium reabsorption decreases and sympathetic drive is reduced. Hydrochlorothiazide inhibits the Na-Cl cotransporter (NCC) in the distal convoluted tubule, lowering salt reabsorption and plasma volume. Volume loss triggers renin and angiotensin II, but the ARB keeps that from raising blood pressure. Lower aldosterone partly counters thiazide-induced potassium loss.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Insurmountable AT1 blockade keeps arterioles relaxed, lowering resistance and blood pressure."], ["Adrenal glands", "Less aldosterone means less sodium retention and partial protection against potassium loss."], ["Kidney (tubule)", "The thiazide part blocks salt reabsorption, producing mild diuresis and lower volume."], ["Kidney (glomerulus)", "Efferent arteriole dilation lowers glomerular pressure; creatinine may rise slightly."], ["Heart", "Lower afterload and less angiotensin II-driven remodeling help in heart failure (candesartan alone is used for this)."]], "journey": "Candesartan cilexetil is converted to candesartan in the gut wall, so only candesartan reaches the blood. It peaks in three to four hours, is more than 99 percent protein bound and undergoes only minor liver metabolism by CYP2C9. It is excreted unchanged in urine and in feces through bile, with a half-life of about 9 hours, although its tight receptor binding gives a full day of effect. Hydrochlorothiazide is not metabolized and is excreted unchanged in urine with a half-life of about 6 to 15 hours. The blood pressure effect starts within about two hours.", "sidefx": "Dizziness and low blood pressure follow the combined vasodilation and volume loss. Acute kidney injury and high potassium can occur when the kidneys depend on angiotensin II, as in heart failure, dehydration or renal artery stenosis, although the thiazide tends to lower potassium. The diuretic causes low sodium, low potassium, higher uric acid and higher glucose. All renin-angiotensin drugs damage the fetus in the second and third trimesters, a boxed warning. Hydrochlorothiazide can cause acute myopia and angle-closure glaucoma, and photosensitivity. Angioedema is rare with ARBs. There is no antidote; overdose care is supportive.", "fun": "Candesartan grips its receptor so tightly that in lab studies it lowers the maximum response to angiotensin II, not just shifting the dose-response curve, which pharmacologists call insurmountable antagonism.", "src": ["Atacand HCT (candesartan cilexetil and hydrochlorothiazide) FDA label", "Pfeffer MA et al. CHARM-Overall, Lancet 2003"]}, "irbesartan-hydrochlorothiazide": {"origin": "Irbesartan was discovered by the French company Sanofi in the early 1990s and co-developed with Bristol-Myers Squibb. It was approved in the US as Avapro in 1997, and the combination with hydrochlorothiazide, Avalide, followed soon after. Irbesartan is the active drug itself rather than a prodrug and has good oral bioavailability. In 2001 two major trials, IDNT and IRMA-2, showed irbesartan slowed kidney disease progression in people with type 2 diabetes and hypertension, which led to its labeling for diabetic nephropathy. Hydrochlorothiazide is the long-established thiazide from Merck's late-1950s work.", "timeline": [["1959", "Hydrochlorothiazide enters US practice."], ["1990s", "Sanofi discovers irbesartan and partners with Bristol-Myers Squibb."], ["1997", "FDA approves irbesartan (Avapro)."], ["2001", "IDNT and IRMA-2 show irbesartan protects kidneys in type 2 diabetes."]], "cell": "Irbesartan binds the angiotensin II type 1 (AT1) receptor selectively and blocks angiotensin II from activating it. AT1 is Gq coupled; its blockade prevents phospholipase C activation and the calcium rise that contracts arteriolar smooth muscle. It also prevents aldosterone release, proximal tubule sodium reabsorption, sympathetic activation and growth signals in the heart and kidney. Irbesartan has no meaningful effect on the AT2 receptor and does not change bradykinin breakdown. Hydrochlorothiazide inhibits the Na-Cl cotransporter (NCC) in the distal convoluted tubule, causing natriuresis and lower plasma volume. The thiazide-triggered rise in renin cannot raise blood pressure because AT1 is blocked, so the two effects add. Lower aldosterone partly limits thiazide potassium loss.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "AT1 blockade relaxes arterioles, lowering resistance and blood pressure."], ["Adrenal glands", "Less aldosterone reduces sodium retention and potassium secretion."], ["Kidney (tubule)", "The thiazide part blocks distal salt reabsorption and reduces blood volume."], ["Kidney (glomerulus)", "Lower intraglomerular pressure reduces protein leakage, which slows diabetic kidney disease."], ["Electrolytes and urate", "Thiazide effects include lower potassium and sodium and higher uric acid and glucose."]], "journey": "Irbesartan does not need activation. It is absorbed well, with bioavailability around 60 to 80 percent, and peaks in about one and a half to two hours. It is about 90 percent protein bound, less than most ARBs. The liver clears it by glucuronidation and oxidation, mainly by CYP2C9, and it leaves in bile and urine. Its half-life of 11 to 15 hours supports once-daily dosing. Hydrochlorothiazide is excreted unchanged by the kidneys, with a half-life of about 6 to 15 hours.", "sidefx": "Dizziness and low blood pressure come from vessel relaxation plus volume loss. Kidney injury and potassium shifts can occur when the kidneys rely on angiotensin II for filtration. The thiazide causes low potassium, low sodium, higher uric acid and gout, and higher glucose. ARBs harm the developing fetus in the second and third trimesters (boxed warning). Hydrochlorothiazide can cause acute myopia with angle-closure glaucoma and is linked with nonmelanoma skin cancer with long use. Angioedema is rare. There is no antidote; care for overdose is supportive.", "fun": "Irbesartan was one of the first ARBs shown in large trials to protect the kidneys in diabetes beyond what its blood pressure lowering alone would explain.", "src": ["Avalide (irbesartan and hydrochlorothiazide) FDA label", "Lewis EJ et al. IDNT, N Engl J Med 2001", "Parving HH et al. IRMA-2, N Engl J Med 2001"]}, "bexagliflozin": {"origin": "The SGLT2 inhibitor story begins with phlorizin, a bitter compound isolated from apple tree root bark by French chemists in 1835. In the 1880s Joseph von Mering found that phlorizin made animals pass glucose in their urine. Phlorizin was too poorly absorbed and unselective to be a medicine, but chemists used its structure to design selective, stable SGLT2 inhibitors in the 2000s. Bexagliflozin was developed by the Boston company TheracosBio. It was approved by FDA as Brenzavvy in January 2023 for adults with type 2 diabetes, joining canagliflozin, dapagliflozin, empagliflozin and ertugliflozin. Unlike several of those drugs, it does not carry heart failure or kidney indications.", "timeline": [["1835", "French chemists isolate phlorizin from apple tree root bark."], ["1880s", "Joseph von Mering shows phlorizin causes glucose in the urine."], ["2013", "FDA approves canagliflozin, the first US SGLT2 inhibitor."], ["2023", "FDA approves bexagliflozin (Brenzavvy) for type 2 diabetes."]], "cell": "In the kidney, glucose is filtered freely and normally almost all of it is reabsorbed. About 90 percent is taken back by sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 (SGLT2) in the S1 segment of the proximal convoluted tubule, which couples glucose uptake to sodium moving down its gradient. Bexagliflozin selectively and competitively blocks SGLT2. This lowers the renal threshold for glucose, so glucose spills into the urine even at modestly high blood levels. The lost glucose carries water with it (osmotic diuresis), and less sodium is reabsorbed, so more salt reaches the macula densa. Each day the body loses glucose calories in urine, which lowers weight slightly. Because the effect depends on filtered glucose and does not require insulin, hypoglycemia is uncommon unless insulin or sulfonylureas are also used. Lower insulin and higher glucagon also shift metabolism toward fat burning and ketone production.", "body": [["Kidney", "Blocking SGLT2 makes the kidney excrete glucose and some sodium, with a mild diuresis."], ["Blood glucose", "Fasting and after-meal glucose and HbA1c fall without stimulating insulin."], ["Body weight", "Loss of glucose calories in urine causes modest weight loss."], ["Blood pressure", "Sodium and water loss slightly lower blood pressure."], ["Genital and urinary tract", "Sugar in urine feeds yeast and bacteria, so genital fungal infections and urinary infections increase."], ["Metabolism (ketones)", "A shift toward fat use can raise ketones, and rarely ketoacidosis develops with near-normal glucose."]], "journey": "Bexagliflozin is taken by mouth and peaks in two to four hours. It is about 93 percent protein bound. It is metabolized in the liver mainly by UGT1A9 glucuronidation, with a smaller role for CYP3A, and its products leave in feces and urine. Its half-life of about 12 hours supports once-daily dosing. Because the drug works on glucose that the kidneys filter, its glucose-lowering effect falls as kidney function declines. Effects on urine glucose start within hours.", "sidefx": "Genital yeast infections and urinary tract infections happen because glucose in the urine is a food source for microbes. Volume depletion, dizziness and low blood pressure come from the osmotic and sodium diuresis, especially in older people or those on loop diuretics, and can lead to acute kidney injury. Euglycemic diabetic ketoacidosis can occur because the drug lowers insulin needs and raises glucagon, pushing the liver to make ketones while glucose stays only modestly high, so the diagnosis can be missed; surgery, fasting and acute illness raise the risk. Fournier gangrene, a rare severe infection of the perineum, has been reported with the class. Serious urinary infections, including urosepsis and pyelonephritis, also occur. There is no antidote; DKA is treated with fluids, insulin and glucose.", "fun": "Phlorizin, the ancestor of all SGLT2 inhibitors, was first tested as a fever and malaria remedy before scientists noticed its sweet effect on urine.", "src": ["Brenzavvy (bexagliflozin) FDA label", "Ehrenkranz JR et al. Phlorizin: a review, Diabetes Metab Res Rev 2005"]}, "miglitol": {"origin": "Miglitol is a sugar mimic built from nature's own alpha-glucosidase inhibitors. In the 1960s and 1970s researchers found that 1-deoxynojirimycin, an iminosugar made by mulberry leaves and by certain bacteria, blocks the enzymes that break down starch and sugars in the gut. Bayer, the company that also developed acarbose from Actinoplanes bacteria, chemically modified deoxynojirimycin by adding a hydroxyethyl group to make miglitol. It was approved in the US as Glyset in 1996 for type 2 diabetes. Unlike acarbose, which stays in the gut, miglitol is absorbed into the blood, though it still works only in the intestine.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Nojirimycin-type iminosugars are found in microbes and mulberry plants."], ["1970s", "Bayer researchers study alpha-glucosidase inhibitors for diabetes."], ["1995", "FDA approves acarbose, the first US alpha-glucosidase inhibitor."], ["1996", "FDA approves miglitol (Glyset)."]], "cell": "Dietary starch is first cut by salivary and pancreatic amylase into smaller sugars, such as maltose, isomaltose and other oligosaccharides, along with sucrose from food. These must be split into single sugars like glucose by alpha-glucosidase enzymes on the brush border of small intestinal cells, including sucrase, maltase, isomaltase and glucoamylase, before they can be absorbed. Miglitol resembles glucose closely because its ring contains a nitrogen in place of oxygen. It binds the active sites of these enzymes reversibly and competitively, slowing carbohydrate breakdown. Sugars are therefore absorbed later and further down the intestine, so blood glucose rises more slowly and less sharply after a meal. Miglitol does not meaningfully inhibit lactase, so lactose digestion is largely spared. It does not stimulate insulin, so by itself it does not cause hypoglycemia.", "body": [["Small intestine", "Carbohydrate digestion is slowed, so glucose is absorbed more gradually along the length of the intestine."], ["Blood glucose", "After-meal glucose spikes are blunted, and HbA1c falls modestly."], ["Colon", "Undigested carbohydrate reaches the colon, where bacteria ferment it into gas and short-chain fatty acids."], ["Gut hormones", "More carbohydrate reaching the lower gut may boost release of GLP-1, adding a small effect."], ["Iron levels", "Low serum iron has been reported, of uncertain clinical meaning."]], "journey": "Miglitol is taken at the start of each meal so it is present when food arrives. Unlike acarbose, it is almost completely absorbed from the upper small intestine at usual doses, with absorption that becomes saturated at higher amounts. It does not need to enter the blood to work, since its target is in the gut wall, but it does. It is not metabolized, binds little to plasma proteins and is excreted unchanged by the kidneys with a half-life of about two hours. Because it is cleared by the kidneys, levels rise in kidney impairment.", "sidefx": "Gas, bloating, abdominal pain and diarrhea are very common and come directly from the mechanism: carbohydrate that is not broken down in the small intestine passes to the colon, where bacteria ferment it, producing gas and drawing water. These effects often fade over weeks as the gut adapts. Miglitol alone does not cause low blood sugar. If hypoglycemia occurs when it is combined with insulin or a sulfonylurea, plain glucose (dextrose) is used to treat it, because sucrose (table sugar) needs the blocked enzymes to be split and is absorbed too slowly. Skin rash is uncommon.", "fun": "Mulberry leaves, the food of silkworms, naturally contain 1-deoxynojirimycin, the parent compound of miglitol.", "src": ["Glyset (miglitol) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "semaglutide-oral": {"origin": "GLP-1 drugs trace back to the venom of the Gila monster. In 1992 John Eng, a researcher at the Bronx VA, isolated exendin-4 from it, a GLP-1-like peptide that resisted breakdown, which became exenatide. Meanwhile, Novo Nordisk in Denmark designed long-acting human GLP-1 analogs. A team including Lotte Bjerre Knudsen developed liraglutide and then semaglutide, which adds an amino acid substitution against DPP-4 and a fatty acid chain that binds albumin. Injectable semaglutide was approved as Ozempic in 2017. Peptides are normally destroyed in the stomach, so Novo Nordisk paired semaglutide with an absorption enhancer called SNAC, licensed from Emisphere Technologies. FDA approved this tablet as Rybelsus in 2019, the first oral GLP-1 receptor agonist. The DrugBug monograph notes that oral semaglutide tablets are not interchangeable mg for mg with other semaglutide products.", "timeline": [["1992", "John Eng isolates exendin-4 from Gila monster venom."], ["2010", "FDA approves liraglutide, Novo Nordisk's first daily GLP-1 analog."], ["2017", "FDA approves injectable semaglutide (Ozempic)."], ["2019", "FDA approves Rybelsus, the first oral GLP-1 receptor agonist."]], "cell": "Semaglutide is a GLP-1 analog with about 94 percent sequence similarity to human GLP-1. Position 8 has alpha-aminoisobutyric acid (Aib) in place of alanine, which blocks cutting by the enzyme DPP-4, and a C18 fatty diacid side chain binds albumin and slows kidney clearance. It activates the GLP-1 receptor, a Gs-coupled receptor. On pancreatic beta cells, the rise in cAMP activates protein kinase A and Epac2, which amplify insulin release only when glucose is high, so the effect is glucose dependent. It lowers glucagon release from alpha cells, slows gastric emptying and acts on GLP-1 receptors in the hypothalamus and brainstem to increase fullness and reduce appetite. In the tablet, SNAC (sodium N-[8-(2-hydroxybenzoyl)amino]caprylate) raises the local pH around the tablet in the stomach, protecting semaglutide from pepsin, and helps it cross the stomach lining by a transcellular route.", "body": [["Pancreas", "Glucose-dependent insulin release rises and glucagon falls, lowering both fasting and after-meal glucose."], ["Stomach", "Gastric emptying slows, which flattens after-meal glucose spikes and adds to fullness but causes nausea."], ["Brain", "Activation of appetite centers reduces hunger and food intake, leading to weight loss."], ["Liver", "Lower glucagon reduces liver glucose output."], ["Gallbladder", "Rapid weight loss and reduced gallbladder motility raise the risk of gallstones."], ["Eyes", "Rapid improvement of glucose has been associated with temporary worsening of diabetic retinopathy."]], "journey": "Oral semaglutide is absorbed mainly in the stomach, close to where the tablet dissolves, so it is taken on an empty stomach with only a small sip of water, then food and other medicines are delayed for a set time. Even so, only about one percent of the dose is absorbed. Once in the blood it binds albumin very tightly, which protects it from breakdown and kidney filtration, giving a half-life of about one week. Steady levels take several weeks to build, which is why the dose is increased gradually. It is broken down by proteolysis of the peptide chain and beta-oxidation of the fatty acid side chain and leaves in urine and feces.", "sidefx": "Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea and constipation come from slowed gastric emptying and GLP-1 receptor activation in brain areas that control nausea; they are worst during dose increases. Vomiting and diarrhea can dehydrate a person enough to cause acute kidney injury. Pancreatitis has been reported, although a causal link is debated. Gallbladder disease relates to rapid weight loss and reduced gallbladder emptying. Hypoglycemia is uncommon alone but likely with insulin or sulfonylureas. The label carries a boxed warning for thyroid C-cell tumors seen in rodents, which have more GLP-1 receptors on C cells than humans; it is avoided with a history of medullary thyroid carcinoma or MEN2. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Only about one percent of an oral semaglutide dose reaches the blood, which is why the tablet contains much more drug than a weekly injection.", "src": ["Rybelsus (semaglutide tablets) FDA label", "Eng J et al. J Biol Chem 1992", "Buckley ST et al. Transcellular stomach absorption of a derivatized GLP-1 receptor agonist, Sci Transl Med 2018"]}, "sitagliptin-metformin": {"origin": "Metformin comes from Galega officinalis (goat's rue or French lilac), a medieval herbal remedy for frequent urination now known to contain guanidine compounds that lower blood sugar. French physician Jean Sterne reported metformin for diabetes in 1957, but it was not approved in the US until 1994, as Glucophage. Sitagliptin came from a new idea: the gut hormone GLP-1 boosts insulin but is destroyed within minutes by the enzyme DPP-4. Blocking DPP-4 could keep natural incretins active. A Merck team, including Ann Weber, designed sitagliptin in the early 2000s, learning from earlier compounds that had caused toxicity by hitting related enzymes, and made it highly selective. It was approved as Januvia in 2006, the first DPP-4 inhibitor in the US, and the combination Janumet followed in 2007.", "timeline": [["1957", "Jean Sterne reports metformin for treating diabetes."], ["1994", "FDA approves metformin (Glucophage)."], ["2006", "FDA approves sitagliptin (Januvia), the first DPP-4 inhibitor."], ["2007", "FDA approves Janumet (sitagliptin and metformin)."]], "cell": "Sitagliptin competitively and selectively inhibits dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4), a serine protease on cell surfaces and in plasma that cuts two amino acids off the front of GLP-1 and GIP, inactivating them within minutes. With DPP-4 blocked, levels of active GLP-1 and GIP rise two- to threefold after meals. These incretins act on Gs-coupled receptors on beta cells, raising cAMP and amplifying insulin release only when glucose is elevated, and GLP-1 also reduces glucagon from alpha cells. Metformin works mainly in the liver. It is taken up by the OCT1 transporter and mildly inhibits mitochondrial complex I and mitochondrial glycerophosphate dehydrogenase. This lowers cellular energy charge and changes the redox state, which reduces gluconeogenesis, and the higher AMP level activates AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK). Metformin also increases glucose uptake in muscle and acts in the gut, raising GLP-1 release, which may make it pair naturally with a DPP-4 inhibitor.", "body": [["Pancreas", "More active incretin boosts glucose-dependent insulin release and lowers glucagon."], ["Liver", "Metformin reduces glucose output from gluconeogenesis, lowering fasting glucose."], ["Muscle", "Insulin sensitivity improves, so muscle takes up more glucose."], ["Gut", "Metformin raises gut GLP-1 and changes the microbiome; it also causes most of the GI side effects."], ["Body weight", "The combination is weight neutral or causes slight weight loss, unlike insulin or sulfonylureas."], ["Blood (B12)", "Long-term metformin reduces vitamin B12 absorption in the ileum."]], "journey": "Both parts are taken by mouth with meals. Sitagliptin is absorbed well, with about 87 percent bioavailability, peaks in one to four hours and is only lightly metabolized. About 79 percent leaves unchanged in urine through filtration and active secretion, with a half-life of about 12 hours. Metformin has about 50 to 60 percent bioavailability, is not bound to plasma proteins and is not metabolized. It is concentrated in the gut wall and liver by transporters, then secreted by the kidney tubules unchanged, with a plasma half-life of about six hours. Both depend on the kidneys, so doses are adjusted to kidney function.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea, nausea, bloating and a metallic taste are mostly from metformin's high concentration in the gut and its effects on gut cells and bacteria; they are reduced by taking it with food and with extended release. Long-term metformin lowers vitamin B12 absorption and can cause neuropathy or anemia. The rare but serious lactic acidosis occurs when metformin builds up (kidney failure) or when lactate is overproduced (shock, hypoxia, liver failure), because blocking gluconeogenesis also blocks the liver's use of lactate; it carries a boxed warning and is treated with supportive care and often hemodialysis. Sitagliptin has been linked to pancreatitis, severe joint pain and bullous pemphigoid, a blistering skin disease that may relate to DPP-4's role in immune and skin cells. Neither drug commonly causes hypoglycemia alone.", "fun": "Goat's rue, the plant behind metformin, is so toxic to livestock in large amounts that it is listed as a noxious weed in parts of the US.", "src": ["Janumet (sitagliptin and metformin) FDA label", "Bailey CJ. Metformin: historical overview, Diabetologia 2017", "Kim D et al. Discovery of sitagliptin, J Med Chem 2005"]}, "empagliflozin-linagliptin": {"origin": "Glyxambi combines two drugs from the Boehringer Ingelheim and Eli Lilly diabetes alliance. Linagliptin, a DPP-4 inhibitor discovered by Boehringer Ingelheim, was approved as Tradjenta in 2011; it is unusual in its class because it is cleared mostly through bile rather than the kidneys. Empagliflozin, an SGLT2 inhibitor descended from the apple bark compound phlorizin, was approved as Jardiance in 2014. In 2015 the EMPA-REG OUTCOME trial showed empagliflozin reduced cardiovascular death in people with type 2 diabetes and heart disease, a landmark result that changed how diabetes drugs were viewed. FDA approved Glyxambi in 2015 as the first combination of an SGLT2 inhibitor and a DPP-4 inhibitor.", "timeline": [["1835", "French chemists isolate phlorizin from apple tree bark."], ["2011", "FDA approves linagliptin (Tradjenta)."], ["2014", "FDA approves empagliflozin (Jardiance)."], ["2015", "FDA approves Glyxambi, and EMPA-REG OUTCOME reports lower cardiovascular death with empagliflozin."]], "cell": "Empagliflozin is highly selective for sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 (SGLT2) in the S1 segment of the proximal tubule. Blocking it prevents reabsorption of filtered glucose and some sodium, causing glucosuria and mild natriuresis. More sodium reaching the macula densa restores tubuloglomerular feedback, which constricts the afferent arteriole and lowers harmful glomerular hyperfiltration. Linagliptin is a xanthine-based, competitive DPP-4 inhibitor that binds the enzyme very tightly. By blocking DPP-4, it keeps active GLP-1 and GIP higher after meals, increasing glucose-dependent insulin secretion through Gs-coupled incretin receptors on beta cells and lowering glucagon. The two mechanisms complement each other. SGLT2 inhibition tends to raise glucagon and liver glucose output, and the DPP-4 inhibitor partly counters that rise.", "body": [["Kidney", "Glucose and sodium are lost in urine, and glomerular hyperfiltration is reduced, which helps protect the kidney long term."], ["Pancreas", "Higher active incretins boost insulin release when glucose is high and reduce glucagon."], ["Blood glucose", "Fasting and after-meal glucose and HbA1c fall more than with either drug alone."], ["Heart and vessels", "Empagliflozin's mild diuresis and other effects lower heart failure risk and cardiovascular death in high-risk people."], ["Body weight and blood pressure", "Calorie loss in urine and sodium loss cause modest weight and blood pressure reduction."], ["Genital and urinary tract", "Sugar in the urine raises the risk of yeast infections and urinary tract infections."]], "journey": "Both parts are absorbed by mouth and peak at about one and a half hours. Empagliflozin is glucuronidated by several UGT enzymes and leaves in urine and feces, with a half-life of about 12 hours. Its glucose-lowering effect shrinks as kidney function falls. Linagliptin has an unusual profile: it binds tightly to DPP-4 in tissues and plasma, which gives a terminal half-life over 100 hours but an effective accumulation half-life of about 12 hours. It is barely metabolized and is excreted mostly in feces through bile, with only about five percent in urine, so no kidney dose adjustment is needed for linagliptin itself.", "sidefx": "Genital yeast infections and urinary infections occur because glucose in urine feeds microbes. Volume depletion, dizziness and kidney injury come from the diuretic effect, especially with loop diuretics or in older adults. Euglycemic ketoacidosis can develop because SGLT2 inhibition lowers insulin and raises glucagon, favoring ketone production while glucose stays only modestly elevated; fasting, surgery and acute illness raise risk. Fournier gangrene is a rare serious infection reported with SGLT2 inhibitors. Linagliptin is linked to pancreatitis, joint pain and bullous pemphigoid. Hypoglycemia is uncommon unless insulin or a sulfonylurea is added. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "EMPA-REG OUTCOME surprised the field: the drop in cardiovascular death appeared within months, too fast to be explained by glucose lowering alone.", "src": ["Glyxambi (empagliflozin and linagliptin) FDA label", "Zinman B et al. EMPA-REG OUTCOME, N Engl J Med 2015"]}, "dapagliflozin-metformin": {"origin": "Dapagliflozin was discovered at Bristol-Myers Squibb in the 2000s, one of the first C-glucoside SGLT2 inhibitors designed from the apple bark compound phlorizin. The C-glucoside bond made the molecule resistant to the gut enzymes that broke down phlorizin. BMS developed it with AstraZeneca. FDA declined to approve it in 2012, asking for more safety data, then approved it as Farxiga in January 2014. The extended-release combination with metformin, Xigduo XR, followed later in 2014. Metformin itself came from goat's rue (Galega officinalis) and was introduced for diabetes by French physician Jean Sterne in 1957, reaching the US in 1994. Dapagliflozin later gained heart failure and chronic kidney disease indications after the DAPA-HF and DAPA-CKD trials, though those indications belong to dapagliflozin alone.", "timeline": [["1957", "Jean Sterne reports metformin for diabetes."], ["1994", "FDA approves metformin (Glucophage)."], ["2014", "FDA approves dapagliflozin (Farxiga) and later Xigduo XR."], ["2019", "DAPA-HF shows dapagliflozin lowers heart failure events."]], "cell": "Dapagliflozin selectively blocks sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 (SGLT2) in the proximal convoluted tubule, which normally reclaims most filtered glucose. Glucose and some sodium are lost in the urine. Extra sodium delivered to the macula densa triggers tubuloglomerular feedback, constricting the afferent arteriole and reducing glomerular hyperfiltration. Metformin enters liver cells through the OCT1 transporter and mildly inhibits mitochondrial complex I and mitochondrial glycerophosphate dehydrogenase. This lowers ATP relative to AMP and shifts the cytosolic redox state, which slows gluconeogenesis from lactate and glycerol. The rise in AMP also activates AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK) and inhibits adenylate cyclase signaling by glucagon. Metformin also increases glucose uptake into muscle and acts in the gut. The two drugs lower glucose without stimulating insulin release, so neither causes hypoglycemia on its own.", "body": [["Kidney", "Dapagliflozin makes the kidney excrete glucose and sodium and reduces glomerular hyperfiltration."], ["Liver", "Metformin lowers glucose production, especially overnight, which reduces fasting glucose."], ["Muscle", "Insulin sensitivity improves and more glucose is taken up."], ["Heart and circulation", "Mild diuresis and lower blood pressure reduce strain on the heart."], ["Body weight", "Lost glucose calories and lack of insulin stimulation give modest weight loss."], ["Gut", "Metformin's concentration in the intestine causes diarrhea and nausea in some people and reduces B12 absorption over years."]], "journey": "Dapagliflozin is absorbed quickly, with high bioavailability, and peaks in one to two hours. It is converted by UGT1A9 in the liver and kidney to an inactive 3-O-glucuronide that leaves in urine, with a half-life of about 13 hours. The metformin in Xigduo XR is extended release, so it peaks later, around seven to eight hours, and is given once daily, usually with the evening meal. Metformin is not metabolized and is secreted by the kidney tubules unchanged. Because both depend on kidney function for effect or clearance, kidney function is checked before and during treatment.", "sidefx": "From the SGLT2 side, glucose in urine feeds yeast and bacteria, leading to genital fungal infections and urinary tract infections, and the diuretic effect can cause dizziness, low blood pressure and kidney injury when volume is low. Ketoacidosis, including euglycemic DKA, can occur because lower insulin and higher glucagon favor ketone production; risk rises with fasting, surgery or acute illness. Fournier gangrene is a rare, serious infection reported with the class. From the metformin side, GI upset is common and long use lowers vitamin B12. Lactic acidosis is rare but serious; it happens when metformin builds up or lactate production rises in shock or hypoxia, because metformin blocks the liver's conversion of lactate to glucose. It carries a boxed warning and is treated supportively, often with hemodialysis, which also removes metformin.", "fun": "SGLT2 inhibitors can make the kidneys pass roughly 60 to 80 grams of glucose a day in urine, a loss of a few hundred calories daily.", "src": ["Xigduo XR (dapagliflozin and metformin HCl extended-release) FDA label", "McMurray JJV et al. DAPA-HF, N Engl J Med 2019"]}, "insulin-glargine-lixisenatide": {"origin": "This injection pairs two separate lines of discovery. Insulin was discovered in 1921 by Frederick Banting and Charles Best in the Toronto lab of John Macleod, with James Collip purifying it. Decades later, Hoechst (later Sanofi) used recombinant DNA to change the insulin molecule, adding two arginines to the B chain and swapping one A-chain amino acid, which made it precipitate under the skin and release slowly. This was insulin glargine, approved as Lantus in 2000. Lixisenatide was developed by Zealand Pharma in Denmark from exendin-4, the Gila monster venom peptide, and licensed to Sanofi. Sanofi combined the two in a fixed-ratio pen, approved as Soliqua in November 2016. The idea was to cover fasting glucose with basal insulin while the GLP-1 agonist controlled after-meal spikes and limited weight gain. Single-ingredient lixisenatide (Adlyxin) was later discontinued in the US, but Soliqua remains available.", "timeline": [["1921", "Banting and Best discover insulin in Toronto."], ["1992", "Exendin-4 is isolated from Gila monster venom."], ["2000", "FDA approves insulin glargine (Lantus)."], ["2016", "FDA approves lixisenatide (Adlyxin) and the fixed-ratio Soliqua."]], "cell": "Insulin glargine has glycine at position A21 and two extra arginines at the end of the B chain. These changes shift its isoelectric point toward neutral, so the clear acidic solution forms microprecipitates when it meets the neutral pH of subcutaneous tissue. Small amounts dissolve slowly and enter the blood, giving flat, roughly 24-hour coverage. Insulin binds the insulin receptor, a receptor tyrosine kinase. Autophosphorylation recruits IRS proteins and activates PI3K and Akt, which moves GLUT4 transporters to the surface of muscle and fat cells, stimulates glycogen and fat storage and suppresses liver glucose output. Lixisenatide is a short-acting exendin-4-based GLP-1 receptor agonist. Through Gs-coupled GLP-1 receptors it raises cAMP, boosts glucose-dependent insulin secretion and lowers glucagon. Its strongest effect is slowing gastric emptying, which flattens the glucose rise after the meal following the injection.", "body": [["Liver", "Glargine suppresses overnight and fasting glucose production; less glucagon adds to this."], ["Muscle and fat", "Insulin moves GLUT4 to the cell surface so glucose is taken up and stored."], ["Stomach", "Lixisenatide slows gastric emptying, blunting the glucose spike after the meal that follows the dose."], ["Pancreas", "GLP-1 receptor activation boosts insulin release when glucose is high and lowers glucagon."], ["Brain", "GLP-1 signals increase fullness, which can offset some of insulin's weight gain."], ["Potassium", "Insulin drives potassium into cells, which can lower blood potassium."]], "journey": "The pen is injected under the skin once daily, within an hour before the first meal. The fixed ratio means both drugs rise and fall together when the dose is changed. Glargine forms a depot and is absorbed slowly with no pronounced peak. In the tissue and blood it is converted at the B chain to active metabolites M1 and M2, which carry most of its effect. Insulin is cleared by insulin receptors in the liver and kidney. Lixisenatide is absorbed within a few hours and has a half-life of about three hours. It is cleared by kidney filtration and then broken down into peptides and amino acids, so severe kidney disease raises its levels.", "sidefx": "Hypoglycemia is the main risk and comes from the insulin, which lowers glucose regardless of how much is needed at that moment. It is more likely with missed meals, exercise, kidney disease or added sulfonylureas, and is treated with glucose or glucagon. Low potassium occurs because insulin activates Na-K-ATPase and moves potassium into cells. Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea come from slowed gastric emptying and GLP-1 action in the brain's nausea centers; dehydration from vomiting can cause kidney injury. Pancreatitis has been reported with GLP-1 agonists. Weight gain from insulin's storage effects is partly offset. Injection site reactions and lipodystrophy can occur, so sites are rotated.", "fun": "Glargine is crystal clear in the pen yet turns into a fine solid deposit the moment it reaches the neutral pH under the skin, which is the whole trick behind its slow release.", "src": ["Soliqua 100/33 (insulin glargine and lixisenatide) FDA label", "Banting FG, Best CH. J Lab Clin Med 1922", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "insulin-degludec-liraglutide": {"origin": "Both parts of Xultophy were designed by Novo Nordisk in Denmark using the same trick: attaching a fatty acid to a peptide hormone so it binds albumin and lasts longer. Liraglutide, a GLP-1 analog developed by a team including Lotte Bjerre Knudsen, was approved as Victoza in 2010. Insulin degludec has its last B-chain amino acid removed and a hexadecanedioic acid chain attached to lysine B29, which lets it form long chains of hexamers under the skin. FDA first declined degludec in 2013 pending cardiovascular safety data, then approved it as Tresiba in 2015 after further study. The fixed-ratio combination was approved as Xultophy in November 2016. It builds on the basal insulin line that began with the discovery of insulin by Banting and Best in 1921.", "timeline": [["1921", "Banting and Best discover insulin."], ["2010", "FDA approves liraglutide (Victoza)."], ["2015", "FDA approves insulin degludec (Tresiba)."], ["2016", "FDA approves Xultophy, combining degludec and liraglutide."]], "cell": "Insulin degludec is stored in the pen as dihexamers. After injection, phenol diffuses away and the hexamers link into long, soluble multihexamer chains held together by zinc and the fatty acid side chains. Zinc slowly diffuses from the ends, releasing monomers that enter the blood and bind albumin through the fatty acid. This produces a flat, peakless profile lasting beyond 24 hours. Degludec activates the insulin receptor tyrosine kinase, which via IRS, PI3K and Akt moves GLUT4 to the membrane of muscle and fat cells, promotes glycogen, fat and protein storage and suppresses liver glucose output. Liraglutide is a GLP-1 analog with 97 percent similarity to human GLP-1 and a C16 fatty acid on lysine 26. It binds albumin and resists DPP-4. It activates Gs-coupled GLP-1 receptors, increasing glucose-dependent insulin secretion, lowering glucagon, slowing gastric emptying and reducing appetite through hypothalamic and brainstem receptors.", "body": [["Liver", "Basal insulin and lower glucagon reduce glucose production, keeping fasting glucose down."], ["Muscle and fat", "Insulin drives glucose uptake and storage."], ["Pancreas", "Liraglutide boosts insulin release only when glucose is high and lowers glucagon."], ["Stomach", "Gastric emptying slows, reducing after-meal glucose spikes and causing early fullness."], ["Brain", "Appetite drops, which limits the weight gain usually seen with insulin."], ["Gallbladder", "GLP-1 agonists are linked to gallstones and gallbladder inflammation."]], "journey": "The pen is injected under the skin once daily, at any time of day but around the same time. Degludec forms its multihexamer depot and releases monomers slowly; its half-life is about 25 hours, and steady state takes three to four days, which buffers changes in injection time. Liraglutide is absorbed slowly, peaking 11 to 13 hours after the dose, and binds albumin, giving a half-life of about 13 hours. Both peptides are broken down by proteolysis, and liraglutide's fatty acid chain is beta-oxidized, so no single organ is responsible for elimination. The dose is set in insulin units, and the liraglutide amount follows the fixed ratio.", "sidefx": "Hypoglycemia comes from insulin action and is treated with glucose or glucagon; degludec's very long action means a low can recur, though its flat profile tends to reduce night-time lows. Low potassium occurs because insulin pushes potassium into cells. Nausea, diarrhea and vomiting come from slowed gastric emptying and GLP-1 effects in the brain; fluid loss can cause kidney injury. Pancreatitis and gallbladder disease are reported with GLP-1 agonists, and elevated lipase is common. Liraglutide carries a boxed warning about thyroid C-cell tumors seen in rodents; it is avoided with a history of medullary thyroid carcinoma or MEN2. Injection site reactions and lipodystrophy can occur.", "fun": "Under the skin, degludec builds chains of hexamers so long that researchers compare them to strings of pearls slowly dropping beads into the blood.", "src": ["Xultophy 100/3.6 (insulin degludec and liraglutide) FDA label", "Jonassen I et al. Design of the novel protraction mechanism of insulin degludec, Pharm Res 2012"]}, "glucagon-nasal": {"origin": "Glucagon was discovered in 1923, soon after insulin, when C. P. Kimball and John Murlin at the University of Rochester noticed that some pancreatic extracts raised blood sugar instead of lowering it. Its amino acid sequence was worked out in the 1950s. For decades, emergency glucagon came as a powder that had to be mixed with liquid and injected, which bystanders found hard to do during a crisis. The Canadian company Locemia Solutions developed a dry nasal powder that could be sprayed into one nostril without mixing. Eli Lilly acquired it and FDA approved it as Baqsimi in July 2019, the first glucagon for severe hypoglycemia that needs no injection. The product was later sold to Amphastar Pharmaceuticals.", "timeline": [["1923", "Kimball and Murlin describe glucagon at the University of Rochester."], ["1950s", "The amino acid sequence of glucagon is determined."], ["1960s", "Injectable glucagon becomes available for severe hypoglycemia."], ["2019", "FDA approves Baqsimi, the first nasal glucagon."]], "cell": "Glucagon is a 29-amino-acid peptide that binds the glucagon receptor, a Gs-coupled receptor found mainly on hepatocytes. Binding activates adenylate cyclase, raising cAMP and activating protein kinase A. PKA phosphorylates phosphorylase kinase, which activates glycogen phosphorylase and breaks liver glycogen into glucose-1-phosphate, and it inactivates glycogen synthase. It also lowers fructose-2,6-bisphosphate, favoring gluconeogenesis over glycolysis, and boosts transcription of gluconeogenic enzymes. Free glucose leaves the liver cell and blood glucose rises within minutes. The effect depends on liver glycogen stores. Glucagon receptors on heart and gut smooth muscle explain its effects on heart rate and gut motility. The nasal powder contains beta-cyclodextrin and dodecylphosphocholine, which help the peptide cross the nasal lining passively.", "body": [["Liver", "Rapid breakdown of glycogen releases glucose into the blood, reversing severe low blood sugar."], ["Brain", "Restored glucose supply lets consciousness and normal function return."], ["Nose and eyes", "The powder irritates the nasal lining, causing congestion, sneezing and watery eyes."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are common, partly from the glucagon effect on gut smooth muscle and partly from the low sugar episode itself."], ["Heart", "Higher cAMP in heart cells can briefly raise heart rate and blood pressure."]], "journey": "The single-use device sprays dry powder into one nostril. Absorption happens passively across the nasal mucosa, so the person does not need to inhale and it works in someone who is unconscious, and nasal congestion from a cold does not appear to block it much. Plasma glucagon peaks about 15 minutes later and blood glucose begins to rise within about 5 to 15 minutes. Glucagon is broken down by proteases in the liver, kidneys and plasma, with a half-life of about 35 minutes. Once the person can swallow, carbohydrates are given to restore liver glycogen and prevent recurrence.", "sidefx": "Nausea, vomiting and headache are common after glucagon. Nasal and eye irritation come from the powder and its absorption enhancers. A brief rise in heart rate and blood pressure reflects cAMP increases in the heart. Glucagon works poorly when liver glycogen is depleted, as with starvation, chronic hypoglycemia or adrenal insufficiency. In pheochromocytoma, glucagon can trigger catecholamine release and a dangerous rise in blood pressure. In insulinoma, the glucose rise can stimulate the tumor to release more insulin, causing rebound low sugar. Severe allergic reactions are rare.", "fun": "Injected glucagon has a second life in emergency medicine: its Gs-coupled action on the heart bypasses beta receptors, so it is used for beta-blocker overdose.", "src": ["Baqsimi (glucagon) nasal powder FDA label", "Kimball CP, Murlin JR. J Biol Chem 1923"]}, "glipizide-metformin": {"origin": "Sulfonylureas were discovered by accident. In 1942 Marcel Janbon in Montpellier, France, saw that a sulfonamide antibiotic used for typhoid fever caused severe hypoglycemia in patients, and his colleague Auguste Loubatieres showed that these drugs work by stimulating the pancreas to release insulin. Tolbutamide appeared in the 1950s, and more potent second-generation sulfonylureas followed. Glipizide was developed in Europe and approved in the US as Glucotrol in 1984. Metformin, derived from guanidine compounds in goat's rue, was introduced by Jean Sterne in 1957 and reached the US in 1994. Bristol-Myers Squibb combined the two as Metaglip, approved in 2002. The Metaglip brand has been discontinued, but generic glipizide and metformin tablets remain available.", "timeline": [["1942", "Marcel Janbon notes hypoglycemia from a sulfonamide antibiotic in Montpellier."], ["1957", "Jean Sterne reports metformin for diabetes."], ["1984", "FDA approves glipizide (Glucotrol)."], ["1994", "FDA approves metformin (Glucophage)."], ["2002", "FDA approves Metaglip (glipizide and metformin)."]], "cell": "Glipizide binds the sulfonylurea receptor 1 (SUR1) subunit of the ATP-sensitive potassium channel (K-ATP, Kir6.2/SUR1) on pancreatic beta cells. Normally, when glucose enters the beta cell and raises ATP, these channels close, the membrane depolarizes, voltage-gated calcium channels open, and calcium triggers insulin granule release. Glipizide closes the K-ATP channel directly, so insulin is released even when glucose is not high, which explains its hypoglycemia risk. Metformin acts mainly in the liver, entering through the OCT1 transporter and mildly inhibiting mitochondrial complex I and glycerophosphate dehydrogenase. Lower energy charge and a shifted redox state slow gluconeogenesis, and higher AMP activates AMPK. Metformin also improves muscle glucose uptake. The pairing combines more insulin (glipizide) with better use of insulin and less liver glucose output (metformin).", "body": [["Pancreas", "Glipizide forces beta cells to release insulin regardless of the glucose level."], ["Liver", "Metformin reduces glucose production, lowering fasting glucose."], ["Muscle and fat", "More insulin plus improved sensitivity increases glucose uptake."], ["Body weight", "Glipizide tends to cause weight gain through more insulin, while metformin is weight neutral."], ["Gut", "Metformin causes diarrhea, nausea and bloating in some people and lowers B12 absorption over time."]], "journey": "Both are taken by mouth with meals. Glipizide is absorbed well, starts acting within about 30 minutes and peaks in one to three hours. It is about 98 to 99 percent bound to plasma proteins and is broken down in the liver, mainly by CYP2C9, to inactive metabolites that leave in urine, with a short half-life of about two to five hours. Because its metabolites are inactive, it is less likely to accumulate in kidney disease than glyburide. Metformin is not metabolized and is secreted unchanged by the kidney tubules, with a half-life of about six hours.", "sidefx": "Hypoglycemia is the main glipizide risk because it releases insulin regardless of blood glucose. It is more likely with skipped meals, alcohol, exercise, older age or liver disease, and can be prolonged. For sulfonylurea-induced hypoglycemia that recurs despite dextrose, octreotide is used because it blocks insulin release from beta cells. Weight gain comes from insulin's storage effects. Sulfonylureas can cause hemolytic anemia in G6PD deficiency. Metformin causes GI upset from high concentrations in the gut and long-term B12 deficiency. Lactic acidosis, rare but serious, is a boxed warning; it occurs when metformin accumulates (kidney failure) or lactate production rises (shock, hypoxia), and is treated supportively, often with hemodialysis.", "fun": "Sulfonylureas were found because a sulfa antibiotic for typhoid caused seizures and coma from low blood sugar in patients during World War II.", "src": ["Glipizide and metformin HCl tablets FDA label", "Loubatieres A. The discovery of hypoglycemic sulfonamides, Ann N Y Acad Sci 1957", "Bailey CJ. Metformin: historical overview, Diabetologia 2017"]}, "pioglitazone-metformin": {"origin": "Thiazolidinediones were discovered at Takeda in Japan. In the late 1970s and early 1980s, Takeda chemists screening for lipid-lowering compounds found ciglitazone, which lowered blood glucose in insulin-resistant animals. Ciglitazone itself was not good enough to be a medicine, but it led to pioglitazone. Only later, in 1995, did researchers learn that these drugs act on the nuclear receptor PPAR-gamma. Pioglitazone was approved in the US as Actos in 1999. Its sister drug troglitazone was withdrawn in 2000 for liver failure, and rosiglitazone later faced cardiovascular concerns, which put the class under close scrutiny. Takeda combined pioglitazone with metformin, the goat's rue derivative introduced by Jean Sterne in 1957, in Actoplus Met, approved in 2005.", "timeline": [["1957", "Jean Sterne reports metformin for diabetes."], ["1980s", "Takeda discovers ciglitazone, the first thiazolidinedione."], ["1995", "Thiazolidinediones are shown to act on PPAR-gamma."], ["1999", "FDA approves pioglitazone (Actos)."], ["2005", "FDA approves Actoplus Met (pioglitazone and metformin)."]], "cell": "Pioglitazone is an agonist of peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma (PPAR-gamma), a nuclear receptor found mostly in fat tissue. Binding causes PPAR-gamma to pair with the retinoid X receptor and attach to response elements in DNA, changing transcription of many genes. Fat cells differentiate into smaller, more insulin-sensitive cells, take up fatty acids and store them, and release more adiponectin and less inflammatory signals. Lower circulating fatty acids reduce fat build-up in muscle and liver, which improves insulin signaling there. GLUT4 expression rises. The result is better insulin sensitivity in muscle, fat and liver. Because it works through gene transcription, its full effect takes weeks to months. It also has weak PPAR-alpha activity that helps lower triglycerides. Metformin acts mainly in the liver by mildly inhibiting mitochondrial complex I and glycerophosphate dehydrogenase, slowing gluconeogenesis and activating AMPK.", "body": [["Fat tissue", "Pioglitazone reshapes fat storage into more insulin-sensitive cells and moves fat from organs to under the skin."], ["Muscle", "Better insulin signaling increases glucose uptake."], ["Liver", "Metformin lowers glucose production, and pioglitazone reduces liver fat."], ["Kidney and fluid", "PPAR-gamma activation in the collecting duct increases sodium and water reabsorption, causing edema."], ["Heart", "Fluid retention can cause or worsen heart failure, a boxed warning for thiazolidinediones."], ["Bone", "PPAR-gamma steers bone marrow stem cells toward fat instead of bone, raising fracture risk, especially in women."]], "journey": "Both parts are taken by mouth with meals. Pioglitazone is well absorbed, peaks in about two hours and is more than 99 percent protein bound. It is metabolized in the liver by CYP2C8 and CYP3A4 to active metabolites with half-lives of 16 to 24 hours, so it works over a full day. Strong CYP2C8 inhibitors such as gemfibrozil raise its levels. Its metabolites leave in bile and urine. Metformin is not metabolized and is excreted unchanged by the kidneys. Pioglitazone's full glucose-lowering effect takes two to three months to appear.", "sidefx": "Edema and weight gain come from PPAR-gamma-driven sodium and water retention in the kidney and from increased fat storage. That fluid can push a weak heart into failure, so the label has a boxed warning and the drug is not used in symptomatic advanced heart failure. Bone fractures relate to PPAR-gamma diverting marrow stem cells away from bone formation. Macular edema is a rare fluid effect in the eye. Pioglitazone has been studied for a possible bladder cancer signal. Anemia reflects plasma volume expansion. Metformin adds GI upset, B12 deficiency and the rare boxed warning of lactic acidosis, which occurs with metformin build-up or excess lactate production. Neither drug causes hypoglycemia alone.", "fun": "Thiazolidinediones were used in patients for years before anyone knew their target; PPAR-gamma was identified as their receptor only in 1995.", "src": ["Actoplus Met (pioglitazone and metformin) FDA label", "Lehmann JM et al. J Biol Chem 1995", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "alogliptin-pioglitazone": {"origin": "Alogliptin was discovered by Syrrx, a San Diego company that used X-ray crystal structures of the DPP-4 enzyme to design inhibitors. Takeda bought Syrrx in 2005 and developed alogliptin. Pioglitazone was Takeda's own thiazolidinedione, descended from the company's 1980s discovery of ciglitazone and approved as Actos in 1999. In January 2013, FDA approved alogliptin alone (Nesina), alogliptin with metformin (Kazano) and alogliptin with pioglitazone (Oseni) on the same day. The EXAMINE trial showed that alogliptin did not increase major cardiovascular events after acute coronary syndrome, although heart failure risk with DPP-4 inhibitors has been watched closely.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Takeda discovers the first thiazolidinedione, ciglitazone."], ["1999", "FDA approves pioglitazone (Actos)."], ["2005", "Takeda acquires Syrrx, the discoverer of alogliptin."], ["2013", "FDA approves Nesina, Kazano and Oseni on the same day."], ["2013", "The EXAMINE trial reports cardiovascular safety for alogliptin."]], "cell": "Alogliptin is a highly selective, competitive inhibitor of dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4), with little effect on the related DPP-8 and DPP-9 enzymes. Blocking DPP-4 slows the inactivation of GLP-1 and GIP released from the gut after meals. These incretins bind Gs-coupled receptors on beta cells, raising cAMP and amplifying insulin release only when glucose is high, and GLP-1 also lowers glucagon. Pioglitazone activates PPAR-gamma, a nuclear receptor that pairs with the retinoid X receptor and changes transcription of insulin-responsive genes. In fat tissue it promotes the formation of small, insulin-sensitive fat cells, increases adiponectin and lowers free fatty acids, which improves insulin action in muscle and liver. The pairing addresses two core problems of type 2 diabetes at once: weak insulin secretion (alogliptin) and insulin resistance (pioglitazone).", "body": [["Pancreas", "Alogliptin raises glucose-dependent insulin release and lowers glucagon."], ["Fat tissue", "Pioglitazone remodels fat to improve insulin sensitivity and reduce free fatty acids."], ["Muscle and liver", "Insulin works better, so muscle takes up more glucose and the liver releases less."], ["Kidney and fluid", "Pioglitazone increases sodium and water reabsorption, causing edema and weight gain."], ["Heart", "Fluid retention can cause or worsen heart failure; both drug classes have heart failure warnings."], ["Bone", "Pioglitazone raises fracture risk by shifting marrow cells toward fat rather than bone."]], "journey": "Both are taken by mouth once daily. Alogliptin is absorbed well, peaks in one to two hours and is only about 20 percent protein bound. It is barely metabolized and is excreted mostly unchanged in urine, with a half-life of about 21 hours, so doses are reduced in kidney impairment. Pioglitazone peaks in about two hours, is more than 99 percent protein bound and is metabolized by CYP2C8 and CYP3A4 to active metabolites that last 16 to 24 hours. Alogliptin acts within hours, while pioglitazone's full effect takes weeks to months because it works through gene transcription.", "sidefx": "Pioglitazone's PPAR-gamma activation in the kidney collecting duct increases sodium and water retention, which causes edema and weight gain and can precipitate heart failure (boxed warning). It also increases fracture risk and can rarely cause macular edema. Liver injury has been reported with both drugs, so liver tests are checked. Alogliptin is associated with pancreatitis, severe joint pain, bullous pemphigoid and hypersensitivity reactions including angioedema; DPP-4 also acts on immune and skin cells, which may explain some of these. Neither drug typically causes hypoglycemia alone. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Alogliptin was one of the early drugs designed with the help of high-throughput X-ray crystallography, which let chemists see how each candidate sat in the DPP-4 pocket.", "src": ["Oseni (alogliptin and pioglitazone) FDA label", "White WB et al. EXAMINE, N Engl J Med 2013"]}, "saxagliptin-dapagliflozin": {"origin": "Both drugs in Qtern were discovered at Bristol-Myers Squibb and later developed with AstraZeneca. Saxagliptin, a DPP-4 inhibitor with a reactive nitrile group that binds the enzyme covalently but reversibly, was approved as Onglyza in 2009. The SAVOR-TIMI 53 trial in 2013 found no increase in major cardiovascular events but an unexpected rise in hospitalization for heart failure, which led to a label warning. Dapagliflozin, an SGLT2 inhibitor modeled on the apple bark compound phlorizin, was approved as Farxiga in 2014. AstraZeneca combined them as Qtern, approved in February 2017, the second SGLT2 plus DPP-4 inhibitor combination in the US.", "timeline": [["2009", "FDA approves saxagliptin (Onglyza)."], ["2013", "SAVOR-TIMI 53 reports a heart failure hospitalization signal with saxagliptin."], ["2014", "FDA approves dapagliflozin (Farxiga)."], ["2017", "FDA approves Qtern (dapagliflozin and saxagliptin)."]], "cell": "Dapagliflozin blocks sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 (SGLT2) in the proximal convoluted tubule, which normally reabsorbs about 90 percent of filtered glucose. Glucose spills into the urine, carrying water and some sodium, so blood glucose falls without any need for insulin. Saxagliptin inhibits dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4) by forming a reversible covalent bond between its nitrile group and the enzyme's catalytic serine. It is converted by CYP3A4/5 to an active 5-hydroxy metabolite that is about half as potent. With DPP-4 blocked, active GLP-1 and GIP stay higher after meals and stimulate Gs-coupled receptors on beta cells, increasing glucose-dependent insulin release, while GLP-1 lowers glucagon. SGLT2 inhibition alone tends to raise glucagon and liver glucose output, and the DPP-4 inhibitor partly counters that rise, which is part of the rationale for combining them.", "body": [["Kidney", "Glucose and some sodium are excreted in urine, with a mild osmotic diuresis."], ["Pancreas", "Higher active incretins increase insulin when glucose is high and reduce glucagon."], ["Blood glucose", "Fasting and after-meal glucose and HbA1c fall without much hypoglycemia risk."], ["Weight and blood pressure", "Lost glucose calories and sodium give modest weight and blood pressure reductions."], ["Heart", "Saxagliptin has a label warning for heart failure based on SAVOR-TIMI 53."], ["Genital and urinary tract", "Glucose in urine raises the risk of yeast and urinary infections."]], "journey": "The tablet is taken once daily in the morning. Dapagliflozin is absorbed quickly, peaks in about two hours, and is glucuronidated by UGT1A9 to an inactive metabolite excreted in urine, with a half-life of about 13 hours. Saxagliptin also peaks in about two hours and is metabolized by CYP3A4/5 to its active 5-hydroxy metabolite; strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise its levels. Saxagliptin and its metabolite leave mainly in urine, with short half-lives of about two and a half to three hours, but DPP-4 inhibition lasts long enough for once-daily use. Both depend on kidney function.", "sidefx": "SGLT2-related effects include genital yeast infections and urinary infections, because glucose in urine feeds microbes, and volume depletion with dizziness or kidney injury from the diuresis. Euglycemic ketoacidosis can occur when lower insulin and higher glucagon favor ketone production; fasting, surgery and acute illness raise risk. Fournier gangrene and urosepsis are rare serious infections. Saxagliptin-related risks include pancreatitis, joint pain, bullous pemphigoid, hypersensitivity reactions and a heart failure signal whose mechanism is not established. Hypoglycemia is uncommon unless insulin or a sulfonylurea is added. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Saxagliptin works by briefly bonding to its enzyme like a hook that slowly lets go, which gives it a longer action than its short plasma half-life suggests.", "src": ["Qtern (dapagliflozin and saxagliptin) FDA label", "Scirica BM et al. SAVOR-TIMI 53, N Engl J Med 2013"]}, "ertugliflozin-sitagliptin": {"origin": "Ertugliflozin was discovered by Pfizer as an SGLT2 inhibitor with a distinctive bicyclic sugar-like structure, part of the family of drugs inspired by the apple bark compound phlorizin. Pfizer co-developed it with Merck, which already owned sitagliptin, the first US DPP-4 inhibitor, approved as Januvia in 2006. In December 2017, FDA approved ertugliflozin alone (Steglatro), with metformin (Segluromet) and with sitagliptin (Steglujan). The VERTIS CV trial, reported in 2020, showed ertugliflozin did not increase major cardiovascular events, though it did not show the reduction in cardiovascular death seen with some other SGLT2 inhibitors.", "timeline": [["2006", "FDA approves sitagliptin (Januvia), the first DPP-4 inhibitor."], ["2017", "FDA approves ertugliflozin (Steglatro) and the combination Steglujan."], ["2020", "VERTIS CV reports cardiovascular safety results for ertugliflozin."]], "cell": "Ertugliflozin is a selective inhibitor of sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 (SGLT2) in the proximal tubule. Blocking SGLT2 lowers the renal threshold for glucose, so filtered glucose is excreted along with some sodium and water. This lowers blood glucose independently of insulin. Sitagliptin is a selective, competitive inhibitor of dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4). By slowing breakdown of GLP-1 and GIP, it raises their active levels after meals. They stimulate Gs-coupled receptors on beta cells, increasing cAMP and amplifying insulin release only when glucose is elevated; GLP-1 also suppresses glucagon from alpha cells. The SGLT2 inhibitor tends to raise glucagon and endogenous glucose production, while the DPP-4 inhibitor lowers glucagon, so the two mechanisms complement each other and the drop in HbA1c is larger than with either alone.", "body": [["Kidney", "Glucose and sodium are lost in urine, with a mild diuresis."], ["Pancreas", "Sitagliptin boosts glucose-dependent insulin release and lowers glucagon."], ["Blood glucose", "Fasting and after-meal glucose fall, and HbA1c drops."], ["Weight and blood pressure", "Calorie and sodium loss cause modest weight and blood pressure reductions."], ["Genital and urinary tract", "Glucose in urine encourages yeast and urinary infections."], ["Legs and feet", "Lower limb amputations were reported more often with ertugliflozin in VERTIS CV, a signal also seen with canagliflozin."]], "journey": "The tablet is taken once daily in the morning. Ertugliflozin is absorbed quickly, peaking in about one hour, with nearly complete bioavailability. It is about 94 percent protein bound and is glucuronidated mainly by UGT1A9 and UGT2B7, then excreted in urine and feces, with a half-life of about 17 hours. Sitagliptin peaks in one to four hours, is only lightly metabolized and leaves mostly unchanged in urine, with a half-life of about 12 hours. Because sitagliptin is cleared by the kidneys and ertugliflozin's effect depends on kidney filtration, kidney function guides use.", "sidefx": "Genital yeast infections and urinary infections occur because the urine contains glucose. Volume depletion, low blood pressure and kidney injury come from the diuretic effect, especially in older adults or with loop diuretics. Ketoacidosis can develop, sometimes with near-normal glucose, because insulin is lower and glucagon higher; fasting, surgery and acute illness raise risk. Fournier gangrene and urosepsis are rare serious infections. A lower limb amputation signal was seen, with an unclear mechanism, possibly related to volume depletion and poor perfusion. Sitagliptin is linked to pancreatitis, severe joint pain, bullous pemphigoid and hypersensitivity reactions. Hypoglycemia is uncommon alone. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Ertugliflozin's sugar-like core is locked into a rigid bicyclic ring, a structure chemists call a dioxa-bicyclo-octane, which sets it apart from other gliflozins.", "src": ["Steglujan (ertugliflozin and sitagliptin) FDA label", "Cannon CP et al. VERTIS CV, N Engl J Med 2020"]}, "linagliptin-metformin": {"origin": "Linagliptin was discovered by Boehringer Ingelheim in Germany. Its chemists started from a xanthine scaffold, the same ring system found in caffeine and theophylline, and built a DPP-4 inhibitor that binds the enzyme very tightly. Unlike other DPP-4 inhibitors, it is removed mainly through bile and feces rather than the kidneys. It was approved as Tradjenta in 2011 and developed in an alliance with Eli Lilly. Combined with metformin, the goat's rue derivative introduced by Jean Sterne in 1957, it was approved as Jentadueto in 2012, with an extended-release version, Jentadueto XR, later. The CARMELINA trial, reported in 2018, showed linagliptin did not raise cardiovascular or kidney events in high-risk people with type 2 diabetes.", "timeline": [["1957", "Jean Sterne reports metformin for diabetes."], ["1994", "FDA approves metformin (Glucophage)."], ["2011", "FDA approves linagliptin (Tradjenta)."], ["2012", "FDA approves Jentadueto (linagliptin and metformin)."], ["2018", "CARMELINA reports cardiovascular and kidney safety for linagliptin."]], "cell": "Linagliptin is a competitive, reversible inhibitor of dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4) that binds the enzyme with very high affinity. By slowing inactivation of GLP-1 and GIP, it raises active incretin levels after meals. They act on Gs-coupled receptors on beta cells, raising cAMP and amplifying insulin release only when glucose is high, and GLP-1 lowers glucagon from alpha cells. Metformin is taken up into liver cells through OCT1 and mildly inhibits mitochondrial complex I and mitochondrial glycerophosphate dehydrogenase. This lowers the ATP-to-AMP ratio and shifts the redox state so the liver makes less glucose from lactate and glycerol. Higher AMP activates AMPK and dampens glucagon's cAMP signaling. Metformin also lowers intestinal glucose absorption, increases gut GLP-1 release and improves insulin sensitivity in muscle. Together, more insulin when it is needed plus less liver glucose output lower both fasting and after-meal glucose.", "body": [["Pancreas", "More active incretin boosts insulin release when glucose is high and lowers glucagon."], ["Liver", "Metformin lowers glucose production, mainly reducing fasting glucose."], ["Muscle", "Insulin sensitivity improves, increasing glucose uptake."], ["Gut", "Metformin raises GLP-1 release and causes most of the GI upset; it also lowers B12 absorption over years."], ["Body weight", "The combination is weight neutral, unlike insulin or sulfonylureas."]], "journey": "The tablets are taken with meals. Linagliptin has low oral bioavailability, about 30 percent, but because it binds DPP-4 so tightly, even small amounts saturate the enzyme. Its binding to DPP-4 in plasma and tissues gives a very long terminal half-life of over 100 hours, while its effective half-life is about 12 hours. It is barely metabolized and is excreted mostly unchanged in feces, with only about 5 percent in urine, so linagliptin itself needs no kidney dose change. Metformin is not metabolized and is secreted unchanged into urine by the kidney tubules, which is why kidney function limits the combination.", "sidefx": "Metformin causes diarrhea, nausea and abdominal discomfort from high concentrations in the gut, and lowers vitamin B12 absorption over time, which can lead to neuropathy or anemia. Lactic acidosis, rare but serious, carries a boxed warning; it occurs when metformin accumulates, as in kidney failure, or when lactate production rises in shock or hypoxia, and is treated supportively with hemodialysis often used. Linagliptin is linked to pancreatitis, severe joint pain, bullous pemphigoid and hypersensitivity reactions such as angioedema. Hypoglycemia is uncommon unless a sulfonylurea or insulin is added. There is no antidote for linagliptin.", "fun": "Linagliptin's core is a xanthine, the same chemical family as the caffeine in a morning coffee.", "src": ["Jentadueto (linagliptin and metformin) FDA label", "Rosenstock J et al. CARMELINA, JAMA 2019"]}, "empagliflozin-metformin": {"origin": "Empagliflozin was discovered by Boehringer Ingelheim and developed with Eli Lilly. Like all gliflozins, it descends from phlorizin, a compound French chemists isolated from apple tree root bark in 1835 that makes the kidneys spill glucose. Empagliflozin was designed to be highly selective for SGLT2 over SGLT1 and was approved as Jardiance in 2014. In 2015 the EMPA-REG OUTCOME trial showed a striking drop in cardiovascular death in people with type 2 diabetes and heart disease. The combination with metformin, the goat's rue derivative that Jean Sterne introduced in 1957, was approved as Synjardy in 2015, followed by an extended-release version, Synjardy XR. Empagliflozin alone later gained heart failure and chronic kidney disease indications.", "timeline": [["1835", "French chemists isolate phlorizin from apple tree bark."], ["1957", "Jean Sterne reports metformin for diabetes."], ["2014", "FDA approves empagliflozin (Jardiance)."], ["2015", "EMPA-REG OUTCOME reports lower cardiovascular death, and FDA approves Synjardy."]], "cell": "Empagliflozin is a highly selective SGLT2 inhibitor, thousands of times more selective for SGLT2 than for SGLT1, the transporter that absorbs glucose in the gut. Blocking SGLT2 in the S1 segment of the proximal tubule prevents reabsorption of filtered glucose and some sodium. The result is glucosuria, natriuresis and a mild osmotic diuresis. More sodium at the macula densa restores tubuloglomerular feedback, constricting the afferent arteriole and lowering glomerular hyperfiltration. The body also shifts toward burning fat and ketones. Metformin enters liver cells through OCT1, mildly inhibits mitochondrial complex I and glycerophosphate dehydrogenase, and lowers gluconeogenesis; the resulting rise in AMP activates AMPK. Metformin also reduces intestinal glucose absorption, raises gut GLP-1 and improves muscle insulin sensitivity. The two mechanisms are independent of insulin secretion, so hypoglycemia is uncommon.", "body": [["Kidney", "Glucose and sodium leave in the urine, and glomerular hyperfiltration is reduced."], ["Liver", "Metformin cuts glucose production, lowering fasting glucose."], ["Heart and circulation", "Mild diuresis and other effects of empagliflozin lower heart failure risk and cardiovascular death in high-risk people."], ["Muscle", "Insulin works better, so more glucose is taken up."], ["Body weight", "Calorie loss in urine and no insulin stimulation give modest weight loss."], ["Gut", "Metformin can cause diarrhea, nausea and bloating, and lowers B12 absorption over time."]], "journey": "Both parts are taken by mouth with meals. Empagliflozin peaks in about one and a half hours, is about 86 percent protein bound and is glucuronidated by several UGT enzymes, with roughly half of the dose leaving in urine and the rest in feces. Its half-life is about 12 hours. Metformin, in immediate or extended release, is not metabolized and is secreted unchanged into the urine by the kidney tubules, with a half-life of about six hours. Empagliflozin's glucose-lowering effect falls as kidney function declines, and metformin clearance falls too, so kidney function guides use.", "sidefx": "SGLT2 effects include genital yeast and urinary infections from glucose in urine, and volume depletion with dizziness, low blood pressure or kidney injury from the diuresis. Ketoacidosis can occur with near-normal glucose because lower insulin and higher glucagon favor ketone production; risk rises with fasting, surgery and acute illness. Fournier gangrene and urosepsis are rare. Metformin causes GI upset and lowers B12. Its rare lactic acidosis, a boxed warning, occurs when the drug builds up in kidney failure or when lactate production surges in shock or hypoxia, because metformin blocks the liver's use of lactate for glucose. It is treated supportively, often with hemodialysis. Hypoglycemia is uncommon unless insulin or a sulfonylurea is added.", "fun": "Empagliflozin was the first diabetes drug shown in a dedicated outcome trial to reduce cardiovascular death, a result that pushed regulators and doctors to judge diabetes drugs by heart outcomes, not just glucose.", "src": ["Synjardy (empagliflozin and metformin) FDA label", "Zinman B et al. EMPA-REG OUTCOME, N Engl J Med 2015"]}, "pioglitazone-glimepiride": {"origin": "Duetact combines two drugs that attack type 2 diabetes from opposite sides. Pioglitazone came from Takeda's thiazolidinedione program, which began with ciglitazone in the early 1980s; it was approved as Actos in 1999 and was later shown to act on the nuclear receptor PPAR-gamma. Glimepiride is a sulfonylurea developed by Hoechst in Germany and approved as Amaryl in 1995. Sulfonylureas trace to 1942, when Marcel Janbon in Montpellier noticed that a sulfa antibiotic caused low blood sugar. Takeda combined pioglitazone with glimepiride as Duetact, approved by FDA in 2006, pairing an insulin sensitizer with an insulin secretagogue.", "timeline": [["1942", "Marcel Janbon observes hypoglycemia from a sulfonamide in Montpellier."], ["1980s", "Takeda discovers the first thiazolidinedione, ciglitazone."], ["1995", "FDA approves glimepiride (Amaryl)."], ["1999", "FDA approves pioglitazone (Actos)."], ["2006", "FDA approves Duetact (pioglitazone and glimepiride)."]], "cell": "Pioglitazone activates PPAR-gamma, a nuclear receptor that pairs with the retinoid X receptor and switches on genes in fat cells, muscle and liver. Fat cells become smaller and more insulin sensitive, take up and store more fatty acids, release more adiponectin and fewer inflammatory cytokines. Lower free fatty acids reduce fat in muscle and liver, which improves insulin signaling and lowers liver glucose output. Effects build over weeks because they depend on new protein synthesis. Glimepiride binds the SUR1 subunit of the ATP-sensitive potassium channel (Kir6.2/SUR1) on pancreatic beta cells and closes it. The beta cell depolarizes, voltage-gated calcium channels open, and the calcium rise triggers insulin granule release. This happens regardless of the glucose level. Better insulin sensitivity plus more insulin gives additive glucose lowering, but also adds the weight gain and fluid effects of each.", "body": [["Pancreas", "Glimepiride makes beta cells release more insulin, even when glucose is normal."], ["Fat tissue", "Pioglitazone remodels fat into more insulin-sensitive cells and moves fat out of the liver and muscle."], ["Muscle and liver", "Insulin works better, increasing glucose uptake and lowering liver glucose output."], ["Kidney and fluid", "PPAR-gamma in the collecting duct increases sodium and water retention, causing edema."], ["Heart", "Fluid retention can bring on or worsen heart failure (boxed warning)."], ["Body weight", "Both drugs cause weight gain, through fat storage, fluid and higher insulin."]], "journey": "The tablet is taken once daily with the first main meal. Pioglitazone peaks in about two hours and is more than 99 percent protein bound; it is metabolized by CYP2C8 and CYP3A4 to active metabolites that last 16 to 24 hours and are excreted mostly in bile. Glimepiride is fully absorbed, peaks in two to three hours and is broken down by CYP2C9 to metabolites excreted in urine and feces, with a half-life of about five to nine hours. Variants in CYP2C9 that slow its metabolism raise hypoglycemia risk. Pioglitazone's full effect takes weeks, while glimepiride works within hours.", "sidefx": "Hypoglycemia comes from glimepiride releasing insulin regardless of glucose. It is more likely in older adults, with kidney disease, skipped meals or alcohol, and can be prolonged. Recurrent sulfonylurea hypoglycemia is treated with dextrose and, if needed, octreotide, which blocks further insulin release. Edema and weight gain come from pioglitazone's sodium and water retention and fat storage, and from insulin's storage effects. Heart failure can result from the fluid load. Fractures, especially in women, relate to PPAR-gamma shifting marrow stem cells toward fat. Macular edema and liver enzyme rises are uncommon. Sulfonylureas can cause hemolytic anemia in G6PD deficiency.", "fun": "Pioglitazone can shift fat from the belly organs to under the skin, so some people gain weight on it while their insulin resistance improves.", "src": ["Duetact (pioglitazone and glimepiride) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "methyltestosterone": {"origin": "Testosterone was isolated from bull testes in 1935 by Ernst Laqueur's group in Amsterdam, and the same year Adolf Butenandt and Leopold Ruzicka independently made it synthetically; the two chemists shared the 1939 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for their work on sex hormones. Natural testosterone is destroyed by the liver when swallowed. Ruzicka's group in Zurich found that adding a methyl group at the 17-alpha position slowed that breakdown, giving methyltestosterone, one of the first orally active androgens, also in the mid-1930s. It was used for decades to treat low testosterone in men and some other conditions. Its use declined as its liver toxicity became clear and safer injectable, gel and patch forms of testosterone became available. In 1990 the Anabolic Steroids Control Act placed anabolic steroids, including methyltestosterone, in Schedule III in the US.", "timeline": [["1935", "Testosterone is isolated and synthesized; methyltestosterone is made in Ruzicka's lab."], ["1939", "Butenandt and Ruzicka share the Nobel Prize in Chemistry."], ["1990", "The Anabolic Steroids Control Act places anabolic steroids in Schedule III."]], "cell": "Methyltestosterone is a synthetic androgen that enters cells and binds the intracellular androgen receptor, a nuclear receptor. Some is converted by 5-alpha reductase to a more potent 5-alpha-dihydro form, much as testosterone becomes dihydrotestosterone. Hormone binding releases heat-shock proteins from the receptor, and the hormone-receptor complex dimerizes, moves into the nucleus and binds androgen response elements on DNA. This changes transcription of androgen-responsive genes. In muscle, it increases protein synthesis and nitrogen retention. In the bone marrow and kidney, it raises erythropoietin and red cell production. In the hypothalamus and pituitary, it provides negative feedback, lowering LH and FSH, which suppresses the testes' own testosterone and sperm production. The 17-alpha methyl group blocks oxidation of the 17-hydroxyl group, which is why the drug survives first pass, and this same group is linked to its liver toxicity.", "body": [["Reproductive organs", "Androgen signaling supports male secondary sex characteristics and libido in men with low testosterone."], ["Muscle", "Protein synthesis and nitrogen retention rise, increasing muscle mass."], ["Bone marrow", "Red cell production increases, which can raise hematocrit too high."], ["Pituitary and testes", "Negative feedback lowers LH and FSH, which shrinks the testes and lowers sperm counts."], ["Liver", "17-alpha alkylated steroids cause cholestasis and, rarely, blood-filled cysts (peliosis hepatis) and liver tumors."], ["Skin and hair", "Androgen stimulation of oil glands causes acne; in women, it causes virilization such as deep voice and facial hair."]], "journey": "Methyltestosterone is taken by mouth or as buccal tablets. The 17-alpha methyl group protects it from rapid liver breakdown, so a useful amount reaches the blood. It peaks in about two hours, is about 98 percent bound to sex hormone-binding globulin and albumin, and has a short half-life of about three hours. It is metabolized in the liver more slowly than testosterone and leaves in urine as conjugated metabolites. Clinical effects on symptoms and body tissues build over days to weeks.", "sidefx": "Liver problems are the defining risk. The 17-alpha alkyl group interferes with bile secretion, causing cholestatic jaundice, and long use is linked to peliosis hepatis and liver tumors, both benign and malignant. Polycythemia comes from androgen-driven erythropoietin release and raises clot risk. Acne, edema and gynecomastia follow androgen effects on skin, salt retention and conversion of some androgen to estrogen-like signals. Suppressed LH and FSH reduce sperm production. HDL cholesterol falls. In women and children, androgens cause virilization, and in children they can close growth plates early. Methyltestosterone is a Schedule III controlled substance because of its misuse potential for muscle building. There is no antidote; it is stopped.", "fun": "The 17-alpha methyl trick that made methyltestosterone work by mouth was later used in many misused anabolic steroids, which is why liver damage is a common theme in steroid abuse cases.", "src": ["Methitest (methyltestosterone) FDA label", "Nieschlag E, Nieschlag S. The history of testosterone, Eur J Endocrinol 2019", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "etonogestrel-implant": {"origin": "Etonogestrel is the active form of desogestrel, a progestin developed by the Dutch company Organon in the 1970s. The idea of a contraceptive under the skin came from work at the Population Council, which produced Norplant, a set of six levonorgestrel rods approved in the US in 1990 but withdrawn in 2002. Organon designed a single, flexible rod about the size of a matchstick that releases etonogestrel slowly. It was approved in the US as Implanon in 2006. In 2011 it was replaced by Nexplanon, which adds barium sulfate so it shows on X-ray and comes with an improved preloaded applicator. In January 2026, FDA approved an extension of its labeled use from three to five years.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Organon develops desogestrel, the parent of etonogestrel."], ["1990", "FDA approves Norplant, the first US contraceptive implant."], ["2006", "FDA approves Implanon, the single-rod etonogestrel implant."], ["2011", "Nexplanon, a radiopaque version, replaces Implanon."], ["2026", "FDA extends Nexplanon's labeled duration of use to five years."]], "cell": "Etonogestrel is a progestin that binds and activates the progesterone receptor, a nuclear receptor that changes gene transcription. In the hypothalamus and pituitary, steady progestin exposure dampens the pulse pattern of GnRH and prevents the mid-cycle surge of LH, so the dominant follicle does not rupture and ovulation does not occur. This is the main contraceptive effect. In the cervix, progestin makes mucus thick, scant and sticky, which blocks sperm from passing into the uterus. In the endometrium, progestin causes thinning and makes the lining less suitable for implantation. Because the hormone is released continuously and there is no estrogen, the endometrium becomes unstable in some people, which explains the unpredictable bleeding pattern.", "body": [["Ovaries", "Without an LH surge, ovulation is blocked, though some follicle growth continues."], ["Cervix", "Cervical mucus becomes thick and scant, forming a barrier that sperm cannot easily cross."], ["Uterus", "The lining thins, and bleeding becomes irregular, lighter, more frequent or absent."], ["Skin", "Progestin androgenic activity and hormonal shifts can cause acne in some users."], ["Brain and mood", "Headaches and mood changes, including depression, are reported."], ["Arm (insertion site)", "The rod sits just under the skin of the inner upper arm; bruising, pain or, rarely, nerve injury and migration can occur."]], "journey": "The rod is placed just under the skin of the inner, non-dominant upper arm by a trained clinician. It contains a polymer core that releases etonogestrel directly into the surrounding tissue and blood at a slowly declining rate over years. Levels peak within the first weeks, then decline gradually. Because it skips the gut and liver first pass, bioavailability is near complete. Etonogestrel is bound to albumin and sex hormone-binding globulin and is metabolized by CYP3A4 in the liver, with a half-life of about 25 hours; strong CYP3A4 inducers can lower its levels enough to reduce effectiveness. Fertility returns quickly after removal.", "sidefx": "Irregular bleeding is the most common reason people have the implant removed; continuous progestin without estrogen leaves the endometrium thin and fragile, so it bleeds unpredictably. Headache, breast pain, acne and weight change relate to hormonal effects. Ovarian cysts can form because follicles still grow but do not rupture. If pregnancy occurs, a larger share are ectopic, because progestins slow tubal transport. Insertion and removal risks include deep placement, nerve or vessel injury and, rarely, migration of the rod into blood vessels, including to the pulmonary artery. Because of these risks, FDA requires trained clinicians and a REMS program. Clot risk is lower than with estrogen-containing methods but is still listed. Strong CYP3A4 inducers such as rifampin and certain antiseizure drugs can reduce its effect.", "fun": "Nexplanon contains barium sulfate, the same compound used for barium swallow X-rays, so a lost implant can be found on imaging.", "src": ["Nexplanon (etonogestrel implant) FDA label", "Organon sNDA approval announcement, January 2026"]}, "elagolix": {"origin": "Gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) was isolated and sequenced in 1971 by Andrew Schally's group, work that helped earn Schally and Roger Guillemin a share of the 1977 Nobel Prize. The first GnRH drugs were injectable peptide agonists, such as leuprolide, which work by overstimulating the receptor until it shuts down, causing an initial hormone flare. Researchers at Neurocrine Biosciences in San Diego set out to make a small, non-peptide pill that simply blocks the receptor. That work produced elagolix, developed with AbbVie. FDA approved it as Orilissa in July 2018 for endometriosis pain, the first oral GnRH antagonist. A version with add-back estradiol and norethindrone acetate, Oriahnn, was approved in 2020 for heavy menstrual bleeding from uterine fibroids.", "timeline": [["1971", "Schally's group determines the structure of GnRH."], ["1977", "Schally and Guillemin share the Nobel Prize for hypothalamic hormones."], ["2018", "FDA approves elagolix (Orilissa), the first oral GnRH antagonist."], ["2020", "FDA approves Oriahnn (elagolix with estradiol and norethindrone acetate) for fibroid bleeding."]], "cell": "Elagolix is a small, non-peptide molecule that competitively binds the GnRH receptor on gonadotroph cells of the anterior pituitary. The GnRH receptor is Gq coupled; when GnRH binds, phospholipase C raises calcium and activates protein kinase C, driving LH and FSH release. Blocking the receptor stops this signal at once. LH and FSH fall within hours, so the ovaries make less estradiol and progesterone. Unlike GnRH agonists, there is no initial surge or flare, because the receptor is blocked rather than overstimulated. The degree of estrogen suppression depends on the dose, which lets estradiol be lowered partly or nearly completely. Endometriosis lesions depend on estrogen, so lower estradiol shrinks their activity and reduces inflammation and pain.", "body": [["Pituitary", "LH and FSH release falls quickly because the GnRH receptor is blocked."], ["Ovaries", "Less stimulation lowers estradiol and progesterone and suppresses ovulation at higher exposure."], ["Endometriosis lesions", "Estrogen-dependent tissue outside the uterus becomes less active, easing pelvic pain and painful periods."], ["Uterus", "The lining thins and periods become lighter or stop."], ["Bone", "Low estrogen speeds bone breakdown, so bone density falls with longer use."], ["Brain and vessels", "Low estrogen causes hot flushes, sleep problems and mood changes."]], "journey": "Elagolix is taken by mouth and absorbed quickly, peaking in about an hour. It is about 80 percent protein bound. It is taken up into the liver by OATP1B1 and metabolized mainly by CYP3A, with smaller roles for other pathways, and is excreted mostly in feces. Its half-life is short, about four to six hours, so hormone suppression wears off quickly when it is stopped and ovulation returns soon. Strong CYP3A inhibitors or OATP1B1 inhibitors raise its levels, and elagolix itself is a weak to moderate CYP3A inducer. Estradiol falls within hours, while pain relief builds over weeks.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come directly from low estrogen. Hot flushes and night sweats reflect estrogen loss in the brain's temperature center. Bone loss happens because estrogen normally restrains osteoclasts; this is why duration of use is limited and add-back therapy is used in Oriahnn. Headache, insomnia, nausea, joint pain and mood changes are common. Depression and suicidal thoughts have been reported, so mood is monitored. Liver enzyme rises can occur, especially at higher exposure. Amenorrhea can make pregnancy harder to recognize, and elagolix may increase the risk of early pregnancy loss. There is no antidote; its short half-life means effects fade quickly after stopping.", "fun": "Because elagolix blocks the receptor rather than overstimulating it, its effect is dialable: different doses give partial or near-complete estrogen suppression, something long-acting agonists cannot do.", "src": ["Orilissa (elagolix) FDA label", "Taylor HS et al. Elaris EM-I and EM-II, N Engl J Med 2017", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1977 announcement"]}, "relugolix-combination": {"origin": "Relugolix was discovered by Takeda in Japan as an oral, non-peptide GnRH receptor antagonist. Myovant Sciences licensed it and developed it in two directions. Alone, as Orgovyx, it was approved in December 2020 for advanced prostate cancer. For women, blocking GnRH fully would lower estrogen to menopausal levels and cause bone loss, so Myovant combined relugolix with low doses of estradiol and norethindrone acetate as add-back therapy. This triple tablet, Myfembree, was approved in May 2021 for heavy menstrual bleeding from uterine fibroids in premenopausal women, and in 2022 for moderate to severe endometriosis pain. The concept of add-back therapy, giving back just enough hormone to protect bone while keeping estrogen-dependent tissue quiet, had been developed during decades of GnRH agonist use.", "timeline": [["1971", "The structure of GnRH is determined by Schally's group."], ["2020", "FDA approves relugolix alone (Orgovyx) for advanced prostate cancer."], ["2021", "FDA approves Myfembree for heavy bleeding from uterine fibroids."], ["2022", "FDA approves Myfembree for endometriosis pain."]], "cell": "Relugolix competitively blocks the GnRH receptor on pituitary gonadotrophs. Without Gq-mediated GnRH signaling, LH and FSH release falls within hours, so ovarian estradiol and progesterone fall and ovulation stops, with no initial flare. Fibroids and endometriosis lesions grow under estrogen and progesterone, so they shrink in activity and bleeding and pain decrease. The estradiol component activates estrogen receptors alpha and beta to replace a small amount of estrogen. This is enough to reduce hot flushes and limit bone resorption by osteoclasts, but not enough to stimulate fibroids or lesions strongly. The norethindrone acetate component, a progestin that is converted to norethindrone, activates progesterone receptors in the endometrium, preventing estrogen-driven endometrial overgrowth. The design rests on the estrogen threshold idea: different tissues respond at different estrogen levels.", "body": [["Pituitary", "Relugolix blocks GnRH receptors and quickly lowers LH and FSH."], ["Ovaries", "Estradiol and progesterone production fall and ovulation is suppressed, so the product also works as contraception after a month of use."], ["Uterus and fibroids", "Less hormone stimulation reduces heavy menstrual bleeding from fibroids."], ["Endometriosis lesions", "Lower estrogen exposure reduces lesion activity and pelvic pain."], ["Bone", "Add-back estradiol limits bone loss, though some loss can still occur over time."], ["Blood and vessels", "The estrogen component brings a risk of blood clots and stroke, a boxed warning."]], "journey": "The single tablet is taken once daily. Relugolix has low oral bioavailability, about 12 percent, and is a P-glycoprotein substrate, so P-gp inhibitors can raise its levels. It peaks in about two hours, is about 70 percent protein bound and is metabolized mainly by CYP3A, then excreted mostly in feces. Its effective half-life is about 25 hours, with a longer terminal phase. Estradiol is metabolized in the liver to estrone and estriol and conjugated, and norethindrone acetate is rapidly converted to norethindrone; both have shorter half-lives and leave mainly in urine as metabolites.", "sidefx": "Hot flushes, decreased libido and hair thinning reflect the overall drop in estrogen despite add-back. Bone loss can still occur because add-back is low; use is limited to a set total duration. Irregular bleeding comes from changing hormone levels in the endometrium. The estrogen and progestin carry a boxed warning for blood clots, stroke and heart attack, because estrogen increases liver production of clotting factors; it is avoided in people with high clot risk, including women over 35 who smoke. Depression and mood changes, gallbladder disease, hypertension and liver enzyme rises are also reported. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The add-back strategy is based on the estrogen threshold hypothesis: bone and the brain need less estrogen to stay healthy than fibroids and endometriosis need to grow.", "src": ["Myfembree (relugolix, estradiol and norethindrone acetate) FDA label", "Al-Hendy A et al. LIBERTY 1 and 2, N Engl J Med 2021"]}, "ospemifene": {"origin": "Ospemifene belongs to the triphenylethylene family of selective estrogen receptor modulators (SERMs), the same chemical family as tamoxifen and toremifene. It is a metabolite of toremifene and was developed by the Finnish company Hormos Medical, later partnered with Shionogi. Researchers looked for a SERM that would act like estrogen in the vaginal lining while avoiding estrogen-like stimulation of breast tissue. FDA approved it as Osphena in February 2013 for moderate to severe painful intercourse caused by vulvar and vaginal atrophy after menopause, the first non-estrogen oral treatment for this condition. Its labeling was later expanded to include vaginal dryness from menopause.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Triphenylethylene compounds such as tamoxifen are developed."], ["1990s", "Hormos Medical in Finland develops ospemifene, a toremifene metabolite."], ["2013", "FDA approves ospemifene (Osphena) for dyspareunia due to menopause."], ["2019", "FDA expands the Osphena label to include vaginal dryness from menopause."]], "cell": "Ospemifene binds estrogen receptors alpha and beta. A SERM's effect depends on the shape the receptor takes after binding and on which coactivator or corepressor proteins are present in each tissue. In vaginal epithelium, ospemifene acts as an agonist: it increases the proportion of superficial and intermediate cells, thickens the lining, increases glycogen and lowers vaginal pH, which reverses atrophy and reduces pain with intercourse. In the endometrium, it has weak agonist activity, which can thicken the lining. In breast tissue it acts mostly as an antagonist in laboratory studies. In bone, it acts as an agonist and reduces bone turnover markers, although it is not approved for osteoporosis. In the brain's temperature center, it does not relieve and can cause hot flushes, much like other SERMs.", "body": [["Vagina", "Estrogen-like action restores thickness, moisture and lower pH of the vaginal lining, easing dryness and painful intercourse."], ["Uterus", "Mild estrogen-like stimulation of the endometrium can cause thickening, so abnormal bleeding is evaluated."], ["Breast", "It acts mainly as an estrogen blocker in breast tissue in lab models; it has not been studied as a breast cancer treatment."], ["Bone", "Agonist effects reduce bone turnover markers."], ["Blood clotting", "Like estrogens and other SERMs, it increases the risk of clots and stroke."], ["Temperature regulation", "Hot flushes and sweating are common, reflecting antagonist effects in the hypothalamus."]], "journey": "Ospemifene is taken by mouth once daily with food, which roughly doubles its absorption. It peaks about two hours later and is more than 99 percent protein bound. It is metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4, CYP2C9 and CYP2C19, mainly to 4-hydroxyospemifene, which is also active. It leaves mostly in feces, with little in urine. Its half-life of about 26 hours supports once-daily dosing. Strong inhibitors or inducers of these enzymes change its levels. Symptom improvement builds over about 12 weeks.", "sidefx": "Hot flushes and sweating reflect estrogen receptor blockade in the brain's thermoregulatory center. Vaginal discharge reflects the restored, more active vaginal lining. Muscle spasms are common, with an unclear mechanism. The boxed warning covers endometrial cancer and cardiovascular disorders: weak estrogen-like stimulation of the endometrium can cause hyperplasia, and estrogen-like effects on the liver raise clotting factor production, increasing the risk of stroke and deep vein thrombosis. It is not used in women with known or suspected breast cancer because its effects in that setting are not established. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Ospemifene is essentially a metabolite of toremifene, a breast cancer drug, repurposed to act like estrogen in a completely different tissue.", "src": ["Osphena (ospemifene) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "bazedoxifene-conjugated-estrogens": {"origin": "Conjugated estrogens date to the early 1940s, when the Canadian company Ayerst extracted a mix of estrogens from the urine of pregnant mares and sold it as Premarin, approved in the US in 1942. For decades it was paired with a progestin to protect the uterus in women with a uterus, because estrogen alone overstimulates the endometrium. Wyeth, which later merged into Pfizer, took a different approach. It developed bazedoxifene, a selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM) that blocks estrogen in the endometrium and breast while acting like estrogen in bone. Pairing bazedoxifene with conjugated estrogens created the first tissue-selective estrogen complex (TSEC). FDA approved it as Duavee in October 2013 for hot flashes and prevention of postmenopausal osteoporosis in women with a uterus, without the need for a progestin. Bazedoxifene is not sold alone in the US.", "timeline": [["1942", "FDA approves Premarin, conjugated estrogens from pregnant mare urine."], ["2002", "The Women's Health Initiative reports risks of estrogen plus progestin."], ["2013", "FDA approves Duavee (conjugated estrogens and bazedoxifene)."]], "cell": "Conjugated estrogens are a mixture, mainly estrone sulfate and equilin sulfate, that are converted to active estrogens and bind estrogen receptors alpha and beta. These nuclear receptors change gene transcription, reducing hot flashes by stabilizing the hypothalamic temperature center, maintaining vaginal tissue and slowing bone resorption by osteoclasts. Alone, estrogen would also make endometrial cells proliferate, raising the risk of hyperplasia and cancer. Bazedoxifene binds the same receptors, but the receptor shape it induces recruits corepressors in the endometrium and breast, so it blocks estrogen's growth signal there. In bone, it acts as a partial agonist and adds to protection against bone loss. The combination keeps estrogen's benefits in the brain and bone while bazedoxifene shields the uterus, replacing the need for a progestin.", "body": [["Brain (temperature center)", "Estrogens reduce the frequency and severity of hot flashes."], ["Bone", "Both components slow bone resorption, helping prevent postmenopausal osteoporosis."], ["Uterus", "Bazedoxifene blocks estrogen-driven endometrial growth, lowering hyperplasia risk without a progestin."], ["Breast", "Bazedoxifene opposes estrogen action in breast tissue; mammographic breast density did not rise compared with placebo in trials."], ["Liver and blood", "Oral estrogen increases liver production of clotting factors, raising clot and stroke risk."], ["Gallbladder", "Estrogen raises cholesterol saturation of bile, increasing gallbladder disease risk."]], "journey": "The tablet is taken by mouth once daily. Bazedoxifene peaks in about two hours, has low bioavailability and is highly protein bound. It is metabolized mostly by UGT glucuronidation with little CYP involvement and leaves mainly in feces, with a half-life of about 30 hours. Conjugated estrogens are hydrolyzed in the gut and absorbed, peaking several hours later; they are interconverted (estrone and estradiol), sulfated and metabolized by CYP3A4 in the liver, undergo enterohepatic recycling, and leave mainly in urine as conjugates. Estrone's half-life is about 17 hours. Relief of hot flashes builds over days to weeks.", "sidefx": "The estrogen component carries a boxed warning for stroke, blood clots, dementia in older women and, in general, for endometrial cancer if estrogens are used without protection. Oral estrogen raises clot risk because it increases hepatic production of clotting factors. Gallbladder disease and higher triglycerides also follow estrogen's effects on the liver. Bazedoxifene adds its own clot risk, as SERMs do. Muscle spasms, nausea, diarrhea and neck pain are common. It is not combined with other estrogens, progestins or SERMs. There is no antidote; stopping the drug is the treatment for adverse effects.", "fun": "The name Premarin comes from its source: PREgnant MARes' urINe.", "src": ["Duavee (conjugated estrogens and bazedoxifene) FDA label", "Pinkerton JV et al. SMART trials, Menopause 2014", "Writing Group for the Women's Health Initiative, JAMA 2002"]}, "pasireotide": {"origin": "Somatostatin, a hypothalamic hormone that shuts off growth hormone release, was discovered in 1973 by Paul Brazeau, Roger Guillemin and colleagues at the Salk Institute. Natural somatostatin lasts only minutes in the blood, so Sandoz (later Novartis) built the stable analog octreotide, approved in the US in 1988. Octreotide binds mostly somatostatin receptor 2 (SSTR2), which works well for acromegaly but poorly for the pituitary tumors that cause Cushing's disease, because these tumors carry mostly SSTR5. Novartis designed pasireotide as a multireceptor somatostatin analog with strong SSTR5 binding. FDA approved the twice-daily injection as Signifor in December 2012, the first drug aimed at the pituitary tumor in Cushing's disease. The monthly long-acting form, Signifor LAR, was approved for acromegaly in 2014 and later for Cushing's disease. The products are now marketed by Recordati.", "timeline": [["1973", "Brazeau, Guillemin and colleagues discover somatostatin."], ["1988", "FDA approves octreotide, the first long-acting somatostatin analog."], ["2012", "FDA approves pasireotide (Signifor) for Cushing's disease."], ["2014", "FDA approves Signifor LAR for acromegaly."], ["2020", "FDA approves Signifor LAR for Cushing's disease."]], "cell": "Pasireotide is a cyclic hexapeptide that activates somatostatin receptors, which are Gi-coupled GPCRs. It binds SSTR5 with about 40 times the affinity of octreotide, and also SSTR1, SSTR2 and SSTR3. Receptor activation inhibits adenylate cyclase, lowers cAMP, opens potassium channels and reduces calcium entry, which together block hormone secretion. Corticotroph adenomas in Cushing's disease express mainly SSTR5, so pasireotide lowers ACTH secretion and therefore adrenal cortisol. In somatotroph adenomas it lowers growth hormone and IGF-1. SSTR5 is also abundant on pancreatic beta cells and on intestinal L and K cells, so pasireotide strongly reduces insulin, GLP-1 and GIP secretion while lowering glucagon much less. That imbalance causes the high rate of hyperglycemia seen with this drug.", "body": [["Pituitary", "ACTH and growth hormone release from pituitary tumors falls."], ["Adrenal glands", "Less ACTH means less cortisol, improving Cushing's features such as high blood pressure and weight gain."], ["Pancreas and gut", "Insulin and incretin release fall more than glucagon, so blood glucose rises; many people develop or worsen diabetes."], ["Gallbladder", "Reduced cholecystokinin release and gallbladder contraction allow bile to sit and form gallstones."], ["Gut", "Inhibition of digestive secretions and motility causes diarrhea, nausea and abdominal pain."], ["Heart", "Pasireotide slows heart rate and can prolong the QT interval."]], "journey": "The short-acting form is injected under the skin twice daily. It is absorbed quickly, peaking within half an hour, and has a half-life of about 12 hours. The LAR form is a deep intramuscular injection of microspheres that release drug over weeks, given monthly. Pasireotide is about 88 percent protein bound and is metabolically very stable; it is cleared mostly unchanged through the liver and bile into feces, with little in urine. Liver impairment raises its levels, so doses are adjusted. ACTH falls within hours of a dose.", "sidefx": "Hyperglycemia is the hallmark side effect and can be severe. It comes from SSTR5 activation on beta cells and gut L and K cells, which cuts insulin and incretin release, while glucagon is less affected. Glucose is monitored closely, and metformin, DPP-4 inhibitors or GLP-1 drugs are often used because they help restore the incretin effect. Gallstones form because somatostatin signaling reduces gallbladder contraction. Diarrhea, nausea and abdominal pain come from inhibited gut secretion and motility. Bradycardia and QT prolongation reflect effects on cardiac conduction. Too much cortisol lowering causes adrenal insufficiency, treated with glucocorticoids. Liver enzyme rises can occur.", "fun": "Pasireotide's design was guided by the receptor map of Cushing's tumors: because they carry mostly SSTR5, a drug that hit SSTR5 hard could reach a target octreotide largely missed.", "src": ["Signifor (pasireotide) injection FDA label", "Colao A et al. N Engl J Med 2012", "Brazeau P et al. Science 1973"]}, "osilodrostat": {"origin": "Osilodrostat was first developed by Novartis under the code LCI699 as an aldosterone synthase inhibitor for high blood pressure. In early studies, researchers noticed that it also strongly blocked 11-beta-hydroxylase, the last enzyme in cortisol synthesis, which limited its use for hypertension but made it a strong candidate for Cushing's disease, where the body makes too much cortisol. After the LINC trials showed rapid and sustained control of cortisol, FDA approved it as Isturisa in March 2020 for adults with Cushing's disease when pituitary surgery is not an option or has not been curative. The drug is now marketed by Recordati Rare Diseases.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Novartis develops LCI699 as an aldosterone synthase inhibitor for hypertension."], ["2010s", "LCI699 is redirected to Cushing's disease because of its strong cortisol-blocking effect."], ["2020", "FDA approves osilodrostat (Isturisa) for Cushing's disease."]], "cell": "Cortisol is made in the adrenal zona fasciculata from cholesterol through several cytochrome P450 steps. The last step, conversion of 11-deoxycortisol to cortisol, is done by 11-beta-hydroxylase (CYP11B1) in the mitochondria. Osilodrostat potently inhibits CYP11B1, so cortisol production falls quickly. It also inhibits aldosterone synthase (CYP11B2), the closely related enzyme that converts 11-deoxycorticosterone to corticosterone and then aldosterone. With the final steps blocked, precursors pile up: 11-deoxycortisol and 11-deoxycorticosterone. 11-deoxycorticosterone has mineralocorticoid activity, which can cause salt retention and low potassium. Lower cortisol removes negative feedback on the pituitary, so ACTH rises, which pushes more precursors into the adrenal androgen pathway and can raise testosterone in women.", "body": [["Adrenal glands", "Cortisol synthesis is blocked at the final step, lowering urinary free cortisol toward normal."], ["Metabolism", "Lower cortisol improves features of Cushing's such as high glucose, central weight gain and high blood pressure."], ["Pituitary", "Loss of cortisol feedback raises ACTH, which can stimulate the pituitary tumor and adrenal precursors."], ["Kidney and electrolytes", "Built-up 11-deoxycorticosterone acts as a mineralocorticoid, causing low potassium, edema and hypertension."], ["Skin and hair", "Extra adrenal androgens can cause acne and excess hair growth in women."], ["Heart", "Osilodrostat can prolong the QT interval, especially with low potassium or magnesium."]], "journey": "Osilodrostat is taken by mouth twice daily. It is absorbed quickly, peaking in about an hour, and is only about 37 percent protein bound. It is metabolized in the liver by several CYP enzymes and UGTs, with no single dominant pathway, and the metabolites leave mostly in urine. Its half-life is about four hours. Liver impairment raises its levels. Cortisol starts falling within hours, and the dose is adjusted gradually based on cortisol measurements.", "sidefx": "Because the drug lowers cortisol powerfully, the main risk is hypocortisolism or adrenal insufficiency, with fatigue, nausea, vomiting, low blood pressure and, in severe cases, adrenal crisis, which is treated with glucocorticoid replacement. Low potassium, edema and high blood pressure come from build-up of 11-deoxycorticosterone, a mineralocorticoid precursor. Acne and hirsutism come from ACTH-driven adrenal androgen excess. QT prolongation can lead to torsades de pointes, so potassium and magnesium are corrected and ECGs are checked. Headache and joint pain are common, and steroid withdrawal symptoms can occur as cortisol falls even into the normal range.", "fun": "Osilodrostat was meant to be a blood pressure pill; its failure in that role, because it blocked cortisol too, became its success in Cushing's disease.", "src": ["Isturisa (osilodrostat) FDA label", "Pivonello R et al. LINC 3, Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol 2020"]}, "metyrapone": {"origin": "Metyrapone was developed in the late 1950s by the Swiss company Ciba, where it was known by the code SU-4885, during a search for compounds that block adrenal steroid production. In 1959 the endocrinologist George Liddle and colleagues at Vanderbilt showed that it could test the pituitary's ability to make ACTH: when the drug blocks the last step of cortisol synthesis, a healthy pituitary responds by pouring out ACTH. This became the metyrapone test, used for decades to evaluate adrenal and pituitary function. It is marketed in the US as Metopirone, FDA approved as a diagnostic agent. Outside that labeled use, it has long been used to lower cortisol in Cushing's syndrome, and it is approved for treatment of Cushing's syndrome in some other countries.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Ciba develops metyrapone (SU-4885) as an adrenal enzyme blocker."], ["1959", "Liddle and colleagues describe the metyrapone test of pituitary ACTH reserve."]], "cell": "In the adrenal zona fasciculata, cortisol is made from cholesterol through a chain of cytochrome P450 enzymes. The final step, adding a hydroxyl group at carbon 11 to turn 11-deoxycortisol into cortisol, is done by 11-beta-hydroxylase (CYP11B1). Metyrapone binds the heme iron of CYP11B1 and blocks it, so cortisol production drops and 11-deoxycortisol builds up. Lower cortisol removes negative feedback on the hypothalamus and pituitary. If the pituitary is healthy, it raises CRH-driven ACTH secretion, and 11-deoxycortisol rises sharply in the blood. A blunted rise in ACTH or 11-deoxycortisol after metyrapone means pituitary ACTH reserve is impaired. 11-deoxycorticosterone also accumulates, which has mineralocorticoid activity, and higher ACTH drives more adrenal androgen production. Metyrapone also partly inhibits aldosterone synthesis.", "body": [["Adrenal glands", "The final step of cortisol production is blocked, so cortisol falls and its precursor 11-deoxycortisol rises."], ["Pituitary", "With less cortisol feedback, a normal pituitary releases more ACTH, which is what the diagnostic test measures."], ["Metabolism", "In Cushing's syndrome, lowering cortisol eases high glucose, high blood pressure and other cortisol effects."], ["Kidney and electrolytes", "Built-up 11-deoxycorticosterone acts like a mineralocorticoid and can cause salt retention, hypertension and low potassium."], ["Skin and hair", "Extra adrenal androgens driven by high ACTH can cause acne and hirsutism with longer use."], ["Brain", "Dizziness, sedation and headache are common."]], "journey": "Metyrapone is taken by mouth as capsules, usually with milk or food to lessen nausea. It is absorbed quickly and peaks in about an hour. The liver reduces it to metyrapol, an active metabolite that also blocks 11-beta-hydroxylase and lasts longer than the parent, which has a half-life of about two hours. Both are conjugated and excreted in urine. For the overnight test, the drug is taken late at night and blood is drawn the next morning to measure 11-deoxycortisol and cortisol. Its effect on steroid synthesis is seen within hours.", "sidefx": "The main risk follows from the mechanism: blocking cortisol synthesis can cause adrenal insufficiency, especially in people whose adrenal or pituitary reserve is already low. Symptoms include nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, dizziness and low blood pressure, and severe cases become adrenal crisis, which is treated with glucocorticoid replacement. With longer use, 11-deoxycorticosterone build-up causes hypertension and low potassium, and ACTH-driven androgens cause acne and hirsutism in women. Sedation and dizziness are common. Many drugs, including phenytoin, speed its breakdown and can produce a falsely abnormal test result.", "fun": "The metyrapone test turns a drug's side effect into a measurement: by briefly causing a cortisol shortage, it reveals whether the pituitary can respond.", "src": ["Metopirone (metyrapone) FDA label", "Liddle GW et al. J Clin Endocrinol Metab 1959", "Williams Textbook of Endocrinology"]}, "levoketoconazole": {"origin": "Levoketoconazole comes from the antifungal ketoconazole, developed by Janssen Pharmaceutica in Belgium in the 1970s and approved in the US in 1981. Doctors soon noticed that ketoconazole also blocked adrenal and gonadal steroid production, and it was used off label to lower cortisol in Cushing's syndrome. Ketoconazole is a mixture of two mirror-image forms. Researchers found that the 2S,4R enantiomer accounts for more of the cortisol-blocking activity. The Swedish company Cortendo, later Strongbridge Biopharma, developed this single enantiomer as levoketoconazole. After the SONICS and LOGICS trials, FDA approved it as Recorlev in December 2021 for cortisol excess in adults with endogenous Cushing's syndrome when surgery is not an option or has not been curative. In 2013, FDA had restricted oral ketoconazole for fungal infections because of liver injury, a risk that levoketoconazole shares and carries in a boxed warning.", "timeline": [["1981", "FDA approves oral ketoconazole as an antifungal."], ["1980s", "Ketoconazole is found to block adrenal steroid synthesis and used for Cushing's syndrome."], ["2013", "FDA restricts oral ketoconazole because of liver toxicity."], ["2021", "FDA approves levoketoconazole (Recorlev) for Cushing's syndrome."]], "cell": "Levoketoconazole is an imidazole that binds the heme iron of several cytochrome P450 enzymes in the adrenal steroid pathway. It inhibits CYP11A1 (cholesterol side-chain cleavage, the first step), CYP17A1 (17-alpha-hydroxylase and 17,20-lyase, needed for cortisol and androgens) and CYP11B1 (11-beta-hydroxylase, the last step of cortisol synthesis). Blocking several steps lowers cortisol and also reduces adrenal androgen production, so unlike metyrapone or osilodrostat, androgen excess is less of a problem. The same imidazole binding makes it a strong inhibitor of hepatic CYP3A4, which causes many drug interactions. The single 2S,4R enantiomer is more potent on cortisol synthesis enzymes than the racemic mixture.", "body": [["Adrenal glands", "Several steroid-making enzymes are blocked, lowering cortisol and adrenal androgens."], ["Metabolism", "Lower cortisol improves glucose control, blood pressure and other Cushing's features."], ["Gonads", "CYP17 inhibition can also lower testosterone, causing reduced libido or gynecomastia in men."], ["Liver", "Hepatocellular injury can occur, from mild enzyme rises to severe liver failure."], ["Heart", "The drug prolongs the QT interval, increasing the risk of dangerous arrhythmias."], ["Drug interactions", "Strong CYP3A4 inhibition raises levels of many other drugs."]], "journey": "Levoketoconazole is taken by mouth twice daily. It peaks in about one and a half to two hours and is more than 99 percent bound to plasma proteins. Absorption depends on stomach acid, so acid-reducing drugs can lower it. It is metabolized in the liver, mainly by CYP3A4, and excreted mostly in feces. Its half-life is about three to four hours after one dose and becomes longer with repeated dosing, partly because it inhibits its own metabolism. Cortisol levels begin to fall within hours, and the dose is adjusted based on urinary free cortisol.", "sidefx": "Liver injury is the most serious risk and carries a boxed warning. It is thought to arise from toxic effects of the imidazole drug and its metabolites on hepatocytes, and it can be severe or fatal, so liver tests are checked often. QT prolongation, also boxed, comes from blockade of cardiac potassium channels and from drug interactions that raise QT-prolonging drugs through CYP3A4 inhibition; potassium and magnesium are corrected before starting. Too much cortisol suppression causes adrenal insufficiency, treated with glucocorticoids. Hypokalemia, nausea, headache and hypertension are common. Because it strongly inhibits CYP3A4, many drugs such as certain statins and antiarrhythmics are avoided.", "fun": "Levoketoconazole is an example of a chiral switch: isolating one mirror-image form of an older drug to keep the effect wanted and drop part of the rest.", "src": ["Recorlev (levoketoconazole) FDA label", "Fleseriu M et al. SONICS, Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol 2019", "FDA Drug Safety Communication on oral ketoconazole, July 2013"]}, "etelcalcetide": {"origin": "The calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR), the sensor that lets parathyroid cells track blood calcium, was cloned in 1993 by Edward Brown, Steven Hebert and colleagues at Brigham and Women's Hospital in Boston. That discovery led to calcimimetics, drugs that make the receptor more sensitive to calcium. The first, oral cinacalcet, was approved in 2004. Etelcalcetide was developed by KAI Pharmaceuticals as a small peptide that could be given into the dialysis line, avoiding a daily pill for people with kidney failure who already take many tablets. Amgen acquired KAI in 2012 and FDA approved etelcalcetide as Parsabiv in February 2017 for secondary hyperparathyroidism in adults on hemodialysis. It was the first intravenous calcimimetic.", "timeline": [["1993", "Brown, Hebert and colleagues clone the calcium-sensing receptor."], ["2004", "FDA approves cinacalcet, the first calcimimetic."], ["2012", "Amgen acquires KAI Pharmaceuticals, developer of etelcalcetide."], ["2017", "FDA approves etelcalcetide (Parsabiv) for dialysis patients."]], "cell": "In kidney failure, low calcitriol, high phosphate and FGF23 changes push parathyroid glands to make too much PTH, a condition called secondary hyperparathyroidism. PTH secretion is normally controlled by the calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR), a Gq/11 and Gi coupled GPCR on parathyroid chief cells. When calcium binds CaSR, intracellular signaling suppresses PTH release, PTH gene expression and parathyroid cell growth. Etelcalcetide is a synthetic peptide made of D-amino acids, linked to an L-cysteine. It binds the extracellular part of CaSR by forming a disulfide bond with cysteine 482, acting as an allosteric agonist. It activates the receptor directly and makes it more responsive to calcium, so PTH falls at any given calcium level. Lower PTH reduces calcium and phosphate release from bone, so blood calcium and phosphorus also fall.", "body": [["Parathyroid glands", "PTH secretion falls because CaSR is activated, which also limits gland overgrowth."], ["Bone", "Less PTH slows excessive bone turnover that causes renal bone disease."], ["Blood calcium", "Calcium falls, sometimes too low, causing muscle spasms and tingling."], ["Heart", "Low calcium prolongs the QT interval and can worsen heart failure by weakening contraction."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are common; GI bleeding has been reported."]], "journey": "Etelcalcetide is injected into the venous line of the dialysis circuit at the end of each hemodialysis session, three times a week. Because the kidneys are not working, it is cleared mainly by dialysis rather than by urine, which keeps levels steady between sessions. In the blood it forms reversible disulfide bonds with serum albumin and other proteins, creating a reservoir. It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes; instead it is transformed by disulfide exchange with free thiols. Its half-life in dialysis patients is about three to four days, much longer than in people with normal kidneys. PTH falls within hours of each dose.", "sidefx": "Low calcium is the most common and important effect, and comes straight from the mechanism: activating CaSR lowers PTH, so calcium is no longer pulled from bone. This causes muscle spasms, tingling and, if severe, seizures. Low calcium also prolongs the QT interval and can trigger arrhythmias. It can reduce heart muscle contraction and worsen heart failure. Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea likely relate to CaSR in the gut. GI bleeding has been reported. Over-suppressing PTH can lead to adynamic bone disease. Calcium is checked before starting and during treatment, and low calcium is treated with calcium and vitamin D. It is not combined with oral cinacalcet.", "fun": "Etelcalcetide is made mostly of D-amino acids, the mirror images of the amino acids in human proteins, which makes it resist normal protein-cutting enzymes.", "src": ["Parsabiv (etelcalcetide) FDA label", "Brown EM et al. Nature 1993"]}, "paricalcitol": {"origin": "Vitamin D was identified in the early 1920s by Elmer McCollum and colleagues as a cure for rickets. In the 1960s and 1970s, Hector DeLuca at the University of Wisconsin and others showed that vitamin D must be activated twice, first in the liver and then in the kidney, to become calcitriol. In kidney failure the second step is lost, so the parathyroid glands overwork. Calcitriol itself helps, but it also raises calcium and phosphate absorption from the gut, which can cause hypercalcemia. DeLuca's group designed 19-nor vitamin D analogs, which lack one carbon in the A ring, aiming to keep PTH suppression while affecting the gut less. One of these, paricalcitol, was developed by Abbott and approved as Zemplar injection in 1998, with capsules following in 2005, for secondary hyperparathyroidism in chronic kidney disease.", "timeline": [["1922", "McCollum and colleagues identify vitamin D."], ["1970s", "Researchers show vitamin D is activated in the liver and kidney to calcitriol."], ["1990s", "19-nor vitamin D analogs are developed in Hector DeLuca's laboratory."], ["1998", "FDA approves paricalcitol injection (Zemplar)."], ["2005", "FDA approves paricalcitol capsules."]], "cell": "Paricalcitol is 19-nor-1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D2, a synthetic active vitamin D. It already has the 1-alpha hydroxyl group that the kidney normally adds, so it does not need renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase, which is reduced in chronic kidney disease. It enters cells and binds the vitamin D receptor (VDR), a nuclear receptor. The VDR pairs with the retinoid X receptor and binds vitamin D response elements on DNA. In parathyroid cells this represses transcription of the PTH gene and slows parathyroid cell growth. It also increases CaSR and VDR expression in the gland, making it more responsive to calcium and vitamin D. In the intestine, VDR activation increases calcium and phosphate absorption, but paricalcitol does this less than calcitriol at similar levels of PTH suppression, which lowers the risk of high calcium and phosphate.", "body": [["Parathyroid glands", "PTH gene transcription falls, lowering PTH and slowing gland overgrowth."], ["Bone", "Lower PTH reduces excessive bone turnover in renal bone disease."], ["Intestine", "Calcium and phosphate absorption increase somewhat, which can raise blood levels."], ["Blood minerals", "Calcium and phosphorus can rise above normal, increasing the risk of blood vessel calcification."], ["Heart", "High calcium can cause arrhythmias and makes the heart more sensitive to digoxin toxicity."]], "journey": "Paricalcitol is given as an IV bolus during dialysis or as capsules by mouth. Oral absorption is good. It is more than 99 percent protein bound and carried by vitamin D binding protein. It is metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4, CYP24 (the enzyme that breaks down active vitamin D) and UGT1A4, and excreted mainly in feces through bile. Its half-life in chronic kidney disease is about 14 to 15 hours. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ketoconazole raise its levels. PTH suppression develops over days.", "sidefx": "The main problems come from too much vitamin D activity. Increased intestinal calcium and phosphate absorption plus reduced PTH can push calcium and phosphorus too high, which can calcify blood vessels and soft tissues. High calcium causes nausea, vomiting, constipation, confusion and arrhythmias, and it makes digoxin more toxic. Over-suppression of PTH can lead to adynamic bone disease, where bone turnover is too low. Treatment of hypercalcemia includes stopping the drug, reducing calcium intake and adjusting the dialysate. Edema and hypersensitivity can occur. Calcium, phosphorus and PTH are monitored regularly.", "fun": "The 19-nor in paricalcitol's name means the molecule is missing carbon 19, a small change to the vitamin D scaffold that shifts its balance between the parathyroid and the gut.", "src": ["Zemplar (paricalcitol) FDA label", "DeLuca HF. Overview of general physiologic features and functions of vitamin D, Am J Clin Nutr 2004"]}, "doxercalciferol": {"origin": "Doxercalciferol (1-alpha-hydroxyvitamin D2) is a synthetic vitamin D2 prohormone first made in the 1970s, after researchers learned that vitamin D is activated in two steps, in the liver and then the kidney. In chronic kidney disease the kidney step (1-alpha-hydroxylation) is lost, which leads to low active vitamin D and secondary hyperparathyroidism. Doxercalciferol already carries the 1-alpha hydroxyl group, so it only needs the liver step to become active. It was developed by Bone Care International, a Wisconsin company, and approved by FDA as Hectorol capsules in 1999, with an injectable form following soon after. It is used to lower PTH in people with chronic kidney disease, including those on dialysis.", "timeline": [["1922", "McCollum and colleagues identify vitamin D."], ["1970s", "Researchers show vitamin D needs liver and kidney activation; 1-alpha-hydroxyvitamin D2 is synthesized."], ["1999", "FDA approves doxercalciferol capsules (Hectorol)."], ["2000", "An injectable form of doxercalciferol becomes available."]], "cell": "Doxercalciferol is a prohormone. In the liver, the enzyme CYP27A1 adds a hydroxyl group at carbon 25, converting it to 1-alpha,25-dihydroxyvitamin D2, the active hormone. Because it starts with the 1-alpha hydroxyl in place, it bypasses the kidney's 1-alpha-hydroxylase, which is lost in chronic kidney disease. The active form binds the vitamin D receptor (VDR) in target cells. The VDR pairs with the retinoid X receptor and binds vitamin D response elements on DNA. In parathyroid chief cells this suppresses PTH gene transcription and slows gland growth. In the intestine, VDR activation increases calcium and phosphate absorption, and in bone it modulates turnover. The result is lower PTH, with some rise in calcium and phosphorus.", "body": [["Liver", "The liver activates doxercalciferol by adding a 25-hydroxyl group."], ["Parathyroid glands", "PTH production falls as VDR signaling represses the PTH gene."], ["Bone", "Lower PTH reduces excessive bone turnover from renal bone disease."], ["Intestine", "Calcium and phosphate absorption increase, which can raise blood levels."], ["Blood vessels", "High calcium and phosphate together can lead to vascular calcification."]], "journey": "Doxercalciferol is taken by mouth as capsules or given IV during dialysis. After absorption it travels to the liver, where CYP27A1 converts it to the active hormone; peak levels of the active form appear about 8 to 11 hours after an oral dose. The active form is bound to vitamin D binding protein and has a long half-life of about one and a half days, up to several days. It is cleared mainly in bile and feces. Drugs that induce or inhibit liver enzymes can change how much active hormone is formed. PTH falls over days.", "sidefx": "Side effects come from too much vitamin D activity. More gut absorption of calcium and phosphate can cause hypercalcemia and hyperphosphatemia, leading to nausea, vomiting, weakness, confusion and, at worst, arrhythmias. High calcium also raises digoxin toxicity. A high calcium-phosphate product can calcify blood vessels and soft tissues. If PTH is suppressed too far, adynamic bone disease can develop. Hypercalcemia is managed by stopping the drug, reducing calcium intake and adjusting dialysis. Edema, headache and malaise are also reported.", "fun": "Doxercalciferol is essentially half-finished vitamin D: the chemists do the kidney's job in the lab, and the patient's liver finishes the job.", "src": ["Hectorol (doxercalciferol) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "corticotropin": {"origin": "Adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) was isolated from animal pituitary glands in 1943 by two groups, one led by Choh Hao Li and Herbert Evans at Berkeley and another at Yale. In 1949, as cortisone was making headlines for rheumatoid arthritis, Philip Hench and colleagues at the Mayo Clinic showed that ACTH could produce similar benefits by making the patient's own adrenals produce more cortisol. Armour Laboratories made a purified pituitary ACTH in a gelatin gel that released slowly after injection, approved by FDA in 1952 as Acthar Gel. It became the main FDA-approved treatment for infantile spasms in 2010 and drew wide attention for its high price. A second repository product, Purified Cortrophin Gel from ANI Pharmaceuticals, returned to the US market in the 2020s.", "timeline": [["1943", "ACTH is isolated from animal pituitary glands."], ["1949", "Hench and colleagues report ACTH benefits in rheumatoid arthritis."], ["1952", "FDA approves repository corticotropin (Acthar Gel)."], ["2010", "FDA approves Acthar Gel for infantile spasms."]], "cell": "ACTH is a 39-amino-acid peptide cut from the precursor pro-opiomelanocortin (POMC) in the pituitary. Acthar Gel is a purified porcine ACTH preparation in a gelatin vehicle that slows absorption. ACTH binds the melanocortin 2 receptor (MC2R), a Gs-coupled receptor on adrenal cortex cells that needs the accessory protein MRAP to work. Higher cAMP activates protein kinase A, which increases cholesterol delivery into mitochondria through the StAR protein and turns on steroid-making enzymes. The adrenals then make more cortisol and also some corticosterone and adrenal androgens. Most clinical effects come from the extra cortisol, which acts on glucocorticoid receptors to suppress inflammation and immune activity. ACTH also binds other melanocortin receptors (MC1R, MC3R, MC4R and MC5R) on immune cells and in the brain, and these are proposed to contribute steroid-independent effects, for example in infantile spasms, though this is not proven.", "body": [["Adrenal glands", "MC2R stimulation drives the adrenal cortex to make more cortisol and some androgens; over time the glands enlarge."], ["Immune system", "Higher cortisol suppresses inflammation and immune activity, which helps in flares of autoimmune conditions."], ["Brain", "In infantile spasms, ACTH reduces seizures, through cortisol and possibly through brain melanocortin receptors; mood changes and irritability are common."], ["Metabolism", "Cortisol raises glucose, increases appetite and causes fluid retention and weight gain."], ["Blood pressure", "Cortisol and other adrenal steroids promote sodium retention and raise blood pressure."], ["Skin", "Darkening of the skin can occur because ACTH also activates MC1R on melanocytes."]], "journey": "Repository corticotropin is injected into muscle or under the skin. The gelatin vehicle slows release so ACTH levels rise and fall over hours rather than minutes. Free ACTH in plasma has a very short half-life, around 15 minutes, and is broken down by proteases in the blood and tissues. Its effect on the adrenals and the resulting cortisol rise last longer, up to about three days. Fragments are cleared by the kidneys. The product is not given intravenously.", "sidefx": "Most side effects reflect raised cortisol: fluid retention, high blood pressure, high glucose, weight gain, Cushing-like features, infections from immune suppression, mood changes, GI bleeding and, with long use, cataracts and bone loss. Because the adrenal glands and pituitary adapt, stopping suddenly after long use can cause adrenal insufficiency. In infants, ACTH can cause hypertension, irritability, infections and a reversible thickening of the heart muscle (hypertrophic cardiomyopathy). Antibodies to the porcine peptide can rarely cause allergic reactions. There is no antidote; effects are managed by tapering and supportive care.", "fun": "When Philip Hench shared the 1950 Nobel Prize for work on adrenal hormones, both cortisone and ACTH were being hailed as near-miracle drugs for arthritis.", "src": ["Acthar Gel (repository corticotropin injection) FDA label", "Hench PS et al. Proc Staff Meet Mayo Clin 1949", "Li CH, Evans HM, Simpson ME. J Biol Chem 1943"]}, "thyrotropin-alfa": {"origin": "After thyroid cancer surgery, doctors look for leftover thyroid cells with radioactive iodine scans and thyroglobulin blood tests. Both work best when TSH is high. For decades the only way to raise TSH was to stop thyroid hormone for several weeks, leaving patients severely hypothyroid, tired and unwell. Bovine TSH was tried but caused allergic reactions and antibodies. Genzyme produced recombinant human TSH in Chinese hamster ovary cells, with the same amino acid sequence as human TSH. FDA approved it as Thyrogen in 1998 as a diagnostic aid, and in 2007 for use with radioiodine to destroy remaining thyroid tissue after surgery in certain patients with well-differentiated thyroid cancer. It let patients avoid the weeks of hypothyroidism that hormone withdrawal had required.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Genzyme produces recombinant human TSH in hamster ovary cells."], ["1998", "FDA approves thyrotropin alfa (Thyrogen) as a diagnostic aid in thyroid cancer."], ["2007", "FDA approves Thyrogen for use with radioiodine remnant ablation."]], "cell": "Thyrotropin alfa is a glycoprotein made of an alpha subunit (shared with LH, FSH and hCG) and a TSH-specific beta subunit, with sugar chains added by the hamster cells. It binds the TSH receptor, a Gs-coupled GPCR on thyroid follicular cells, including well-differentiated thyroid cancer cells. Higher cAMP activates protein kinase A, which increases expression and activity of the sodium-iodide symporter (NIS) at the base of the cell, so more iodine, including radioactive iodine, is taken up. It also stimulates thyroglobulin synthesis and release. That makes thyroglobulin a more sensitive marker of remaining tissue, and makes radioactive iodine scans and ablation work better. Because patients keep taking thyroid hormone, they stay euthyroid during the process.", "body": [["Thyroid tissue and tumor", "Remaining thyroid cells take up more iodine and release more thyroglobulin, which improves detection and radioiodine treatment."], ["Tumor sites", "Brief swelling of thyroid tissue or tumor can occur; in the neck, brain or spinal cord this can cause pressure symptoms."], ["Whole body", "Patients avoid hypothyroid symptoms such as fatigue, depression and cold intolerance from thyroid hormone withdrawal."], ["Gut and brain", "Nausea and headache are the most common short-term effects."], ["Thyroid hormone levels", "In people with significant remaining thyroid tissue, a short rise in thyroid hormone release can occur."]], "journey": "Thyrotropin alfa is given as an intramuscular injection into the buttock, usually once daily on two consecutive days. It is absorbed slowly and peaks about 10 hours after injection, with a wide range. Its half-life is about 25 hours. It is thought to be cleared by proteolysis in the liver and kidney. Radioiodine is given 24 hours after the second injection, and thyroglobulin is measured a few days later, when the TSH-driven rise peaks.", "sidefx": "Nausea and headache are common and short lived. Fatigue, dizziness, vomiting and tingling can occur. The most important risk follows directly from the mechanism: TSH stimulates thyroid tissue, so remaining thyroid tissue or metastases can swell for a short time. In the brain, spinal cord or near the airway this can cause headache, neurologic deficits or breathing problems, so steroids are sometimes given first in those situations. Stroke has been reported, mostly in older women, with an unclear link. Hypersensitivity is uncommon because the protein is human in sequence. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Before Thyrogen, many patients had to feel deeply hypothyroid for weeks before each cancer surveillance scan; recombinant TSH made the same test possible while they kept taking their thyroid pills.", "src": ["Thyrogen (thyrotropin alfa) FDA label", "Haugen BR et al. 2015 ATA thyroid cancer guidelines, Thyroid 2016"]}, "palopegteriparatide": {"origin": "Parathyroid hormone was first extracted in 1925 by James Collip, the Canadian chemist who had helped purify insulin. For nearly a century, hypoparathyroidism, the lack of PTH most often after neck surgery, was treated with calcium and active vitamin D rather than with the missing hormone. Short-acting PTH injections gave brief spikes and did not match the body's steady levels. Recombinant PTH(1-84) (Natpara) was approved in 2015 but was later discontinued. The Danish company Ascendis Pharma used its TransCon technology to attach PTH(1-34) to a carrier that releases it steadily over a day. FDA approved palopegteriparatide as Yorvipath in August 2024 for hypoparathyroidism in adults, after the PaTHway trial showed many patients could control calcium without regular active vitamin D and high calcium doses.", "timeline": [["1925", "James Collip extracts parathyroid hormone."], ["2015", "FDA approves recombinant PTH(1-84) (Natpara) for hypoparathyroidism."], ["2024", "FDA approves palopegteriparatide (Yorvipath) for adult hypoparathyroidism."]], "cell": "Palopegteriparatide is a prodrug: PTH(1-34), the active front part of parathyroid hormone, is attached through a self-cleaving TransCon linker to a branched methoxypolyethylene glycol carrier. The carrier keeps the peptide inactive and slows its clearance. At body pH and temperature, the linker cleaves itself at a steady rate, releasing free PTH(1-34) and keeping blood levels in a near-physiologic range around the clock. PTH(1-34) activates the PTH1 receptor, a Gs and Gq coupled GPCR. In the distal tubule of the kidney, it increases calcium reabsorption. In the proximal tubule, it reduces phosphate reabsorption by internalizing NaPi-IIa transporters and stimulates 1-alpha-hydroxylase, increasing calcitriol, which in turn raises gut calcium absorption. In bone, it promotes turnover that releases calcium. Together these raise blood calcium and lower phosphate and urinary calcium.", "body": [["Kidney", "More calcium is reabsorbed, more phosphate is excreted and more calcitriol is made, which brings calcium up and phosphate down."], ["Intestine", "Higher calcitriol increases calcium absorption from food."], ["Bone", "PTH activity restores more normal bone turnover in a condition where turnover is very low."], ["Blood calcium", "Steady PTH helps hold calcium in range, reducing the need for active vitamin D and high-dose calcium."], ["Blood vessels", "Vasodilation from PTH can cause dizziness or orthostatic low blood pressure."]], "journey": "Palopegteriparatide is injected under the skin once daily with a prefilled pen. The carrier slowly releases active PTH(1-34) into the blood, giving fairly constant levels over 24 hours with an effective half-life of about 60 hours for the released hormone. Steady state is reached within about one to two weeks. Free PTH(1-34) is broken down by proteases, and the inert carrier leaves mainly through the kidneys. Serum calcium begins rising within hours, and calcium and vitamin D doses are reduced as PTH takes over.", "sidefx": "Hypercalcemia occurs when PTH action plus remaining calcium or active vitamin D supplements push calcium too high, causing nausea, fatigue and confusion. Hypocalcemia can occur during dose changes or if doses are missed or stopped suddenly, because the body has no PTH of its own, which can lead to tingling, tetany, laryngospasm or seizures. Orthostatic hypotension reflects PTH's vasodilating effect. Injection site reactions and headache are common. Rat studies of PTH analogs showed osteosarcoma at high exposure; this risk has not been seen in humans but is listed in labeling. Low calcium is treated with calcium, and high calcium by reducing the dose and supplements.", "fun": "Hypoparathyroidism was one of the last classic hormone deficiencies to be treated by replacing the missing hormone itself, nearly 100 years after PTH was first extracted.", "src": ["Yorvipath (palopegteriparatide) FDA label", "Khan AA et al. PaTHway trial, J Bone Miner Res 2023", "Collip JB. J Biol Chem 1925"]}, "crinecerfont": {"origin": "Corticotropin-releasing factor (CRF), the hypothalamic hormone that drives ACTH release, was isolated and sequenced in 1981 by Wylie Vale and colleagues at the Salk Institute. Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH), most often from 21-hydroxylase deficiency, leaves the adrenals unable to make enough cortisol. The pituitary responds with constant high ACTH, which drives overproduction of adrenal androgens. For decades the only treatment was glucocorticoids at doses high enough to suppress ACTH, which often caused weight gain, bone loss and other steroid harms. Neurocrine Biosciences developed crinecerfont, a small-molecule CRF1 receptor blocker, to cut ACTH at its source. After the CAHtalyst trials in adults and children, FDA approved it as Crenessity in December 2024, the first new treatment approved for CAH in decades.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Glucocorticoid therapy becomes standard treatment for congenital adrenal hyperplasia."], ["1981", "Wylie Vale and colleagues isolate corticotropin-releasing factor."], ["2024", "FDA approves crinecerfont (Crenessity) for classic congenital adrenal hyperplasia."]], "cell": "In 21-hydroxylase deficiency, the adrenal gland cannot efficiently convert 17-hydroxyprogesterone to 11-deoxycortisol, so cortisol output is low. Low cortisol removes negative feedback, so the hypothalamus releases CRF, which binds CRF type 1 receptors (CRF1, a Gs-coupled GPCR) on pituitary corticotrophs. Rising cAMP increases POMC transcription and ACTH release. High ACTH pushes the adrenal to make more steroid precursors, which back up and spill into the androgen pathway, raising 17-hydroxyprogesterone and androstenedione. Crinecerfont is a selective, non-peptide CRF1 receptor antagonist. By blocking CRF1, it lowers ACTH, which reduces adrenal precursor and androgen overproduction. This allows glucocorticoid doses to be lowered toward the amount needed only to replace cortisol, rather than the higher amounts previously needed to suppress ACTH. Crinecerfont does not replace cortisol, so glucocorticoids are still needed.", "body": [["Pituitary", "Blocking CRF1 lowers ACTH release from corticotroph cells."], ["Adrenal glands", "Less ACTH stimulation lowers 17-hydroxyprogesterone and adrenal androgens."], ["Reproductive and skin effects", "Lower androgens may reduce features like early puberty, acne, hirsutism and menstrual problems."], ["Whole body", "Lower glucocorticoid needs may lessen steroid harms such as weight gain, high glucose and bone loss."], ["Brain", "Headache, fatigue and dizziness are common."]], "journey": "Crinecerfont is taken by mouth twice daily with meals, which improve absorption. It peaks about four hours after a dose and is more than 99 percent protein bound. It is metabolized in the liver, mainly by CYP3A4, and excreted mostly in feces. Its half-life is about 14 hours. Strong CYP3A4 inducers lower its levels, so doses are adjusted when they are used. ACTH suppression begins within hours, while lower androgen levels and glucocorticoid reductions are seen over weeks.", "sidefx": "Fatigue, headache, dizziness, joint pain, back pain and decreased appetite are the most common effects. The key safety point follows from the disease and mechanism: crinecerfont lowers ACTH but does not make cortisol, and people with CAH cannot raise cortisol during stress. If glucocorticoids are reduced too far or not increased during illness or surgery, adrenal crisis can result, which is treated with stress-dose glucocorticoids. Hypersensitivity reactions, including angioedema, have been reported. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Crinecerfont works one step above the pituitary: instead of overwhelming the system with extra steroid, it quiets the signal that tells the adrenal glands to overwork.", "src": ["Crenessity (crinecerfont) FDA label", "Vale W et al. Science 1981", "Auchus RJ et al. CAHtalyst, N Engl J Med 2024"]}, "dexamethasone-oral": {"origin": "The steroid era began in 1948, when Philip Hench at the Mayo Clinic gave cortisone, made by Edward Kendall and Merck chemists, to a woman with severe rheumatoid arthritis, who improved dramatically. Hench, Kendall and Tadeus Reichstein shared the 1950 Nobel Prize. Cortisone and hydrocortisone caused salt retention, so chemists tried changing the molecule. Adding a fluorine at position 9 and a double bond in ring A increased potency, and adding a 16-alpha methyl group removed nearly all salt-retaining activity. Dexamethasone was reported in 1958 by Glen Arth and colleagues at Merck, and marketed that year as Decadron. It became one of the most potent and widely used glucocorticoids. In 2020 the RECOVERY trial showed it lowered death in hospitalized COVID-19 patients needing oxygen.", "timeline": [["1948", "Philip Hench first treats rheumatoid arthritis with cortisone."], ["1950", "Hench, Kendall and Reichstein share the Nobel Prize for adrenal hormones."], ["1958", "Merck chemists report dexamethasone, marketed as Decadron."], ["2020", "The RECOVERY trial shows dexamethasone lowers COVID-19 deaths in patients on oxygen."]], "cell": "Dexamethasone diffuses into cells and binds the cytosolic glucocorticoid receptor (NR3C1), releasing heat-shock proteins. The hormone-receptor complex moves into the nucleus. There it switches on some genes (transactivation), such as annexin A1 (lipocortin-1), which inhibits phospholipase A2 and reduces arachidonic acid supply for prostaglandins and leukotrienes, and IkB-alpha, which holds NF-kB in check. It also switches off inflammatory genes (transrepression) by binding NF-kB and AP-1, cutting production of cytokines such as IL-1, IL-6 and TNF-alpha, COX-2 and adhesion molecules. The result is less inflammation, fewer circulating lymphocytes and eosinophils and less capillary leak. The 9-alpha fluorine raises receptor binding, and the 16-alpha methyl group nearly abolishes mineralocorticoid effects, so dexamethasone has strong glucocorticoid and minimal salt-retaining activity. It also strongly suppresses pituitary ACTH, the basis of the dexamethasone suppression test.", "body": [["Immune system", "Lymphocytes and eosinophils fall, cytokine production drops and immune responses are suppressed."], ["Inflammation", "Less prostaglandin, leukotriene and cytokine signaling reduces swelling, pain and capillary leak."], ["Brain", "It reduces swelling around brain tumors, and it also causes insomnia, mood swings and sometimes psychosis."], ["Metabolism", "It raises glucose by increasing liver gluconeogenesis and reducing muscle glucose uptake, and increases appetite."], ["Pituitary and adrenals", "Strong ACTH suppression means long use shuts down the adrenal glands' own cortisol production."], ["Bone and muscle", "Long use reduces bone formation and breaks down muscle protein, causing osteoporosis and myopathy."], ["Gut", "It helps prevent chemotherapy nausea, but it can irritate the stomach, and GI bleeding risk rises when it is combined with NSAIDs."]], "journey": "Dexamethasone is well absorbed by mouth, with high bioavailability, and peaks in one to two hours. About 70 percent is bound to plasma proteins, mostly albumin, because it binds corticosteroid-binding globulin poorly. It is metabolized in the liver mainly by CYP3A4 and its metabolites leave in urine. Its plasma half-life is only about three to four hours, but because it works through gene transcription, its biologic effect lasts 36 to 72 hours, making it a long-acting steroid. CYP3A4 inducers such as phenytoin and rifampin lower its levels, and dexamethasone itself can induce CYP3A4.", "sidefx": "Nearly all side effects come from the same glucocorticoid receptor signaling that controls inflammation. High glucose results from increased liver gluconeogenesis and insulin resistance. Infections are more likely because immune cells and cytokines are suppressed. Mood changes, insomnia and psychosis reflect glucocorticoid receptors in the brain. Long use causes bone loss, avascular necrosis, myopathy, skin thinning, cataracts and Cushing-like features. Because dexamethasone strongly suppresses ACTH, the adrenal glands shrink over time, and stopping suddenly after long use can cause adrenal crisis; long courses are tapered, and adrenal crisis is treated with stress-dose hydrocortisone. It has very little salt-retaining effect, so it is not used alone to replace aldosterone.", "fun": "Dexamethasone is about 25 to 30 times more potent than hydrocortisone as an anti-inflammatory, largely because of one fluorine atom and one methyl group added to the steroid core.", "src": ["Dexamethasone tablets FDA label", "Arth GE et al. J Am Chem Soc 1958", "RECOVERY Collaborative Group, N Engl J Med 2021"]}, "octreotide-lar": {"origin": "Octreotide LAR starts with somatostatin, a small brain hormone found in 1973 by Roger Guillemin's group at the Salk Institute while they were searching for factors that control growth hormone. Natural somatostatin was a poor drug because it lasts only a few minutes in the blood. Chemists at Sandoz in Switzerland, led by Wilfried Bauer, trimmed it to an eight amino acid ring and swapped in a D-amino acid to resist enzymes. The result, octreotide, lasted far longer and was approved in the US as Sandostatin in 1988. Patients still needed injections several times a day, so Sandoz (later Novartis) packed octreotide into tiny biodegradable polymer beads. This monthly depot, Sandostatin LAR, was approved in 1998 and made long-term control of acromegaly and carcinoid symptoms much easier.", "timeline": [["1973", "Guillemin's lab at the Salk Institute discovers somatostatin."], ["1977", "Roger Guillemin shares the Nobel Prize for work on brain peptide hormones."], ["1982", "Sandoz scientists report the octapeptide octreotide."], ["1988", "FDA approves Sandostatin injection."], ["1998", "FDA approves the monthly Sandostatin LAR Depot."]], "cell": "Octreotide is an agonist at somatostatin receptors, with high affinity for SSTR2 and lower affinity for SSTR5 and SSTR3. These are Gi-coupled G protein receptors. Binding inhibits adenylyl cyclase, so cAMP falls. The receptor also opens potassium channels, which hyperpolarizes the cell, and reduces calcium entry through voltage-gated channels. Less calcium means fewer secretory granules fuse with the membrane. In pituitary somatotrophs this cuts growth hormone release, and the liver then makes less IGF-1. In gut neuroendocrine tumor cells it blocks release of serotonin, VIP and other peptides that cause flushing and watery diarrhea. The same signal also lowers insulin, glucagon, gastrin and gallbladder contraction. In the LAR product the drug sits inside poly(lactide-co-glycolide) microspheres that slowly break down in muscle.", "body": [["Pituitary", "Less growth hormone is released, which lowers IGF-1 and slowly eases the soft tissue swelling, sweating and headaches of acromegaly."], ["Gut tumors", "Neuroendocrine tumor cells release less serotonin and VIP, so flushing and secretory diarrhea from carcinoid tumors and VIPomas decrease."], ["Gallbladder", "Reduced cholecystokinin release and weaker gallbladder squeezing leave bile sitting still, which allows sludge and gallstones to form."], ["Pancreas", "Insulin and glucagon both fall, so blood sugar can drift high or low depending on which effect wins in a given person."], ["Heart", "Slowed heart rate and conduction changes can occur, which matters in people with existing conduction disease."], ["Intestine", "Less pancreatic enzyme secretion and slower transit can cause fatty stools, gas and reduced absorption of some oral drugs."]], "journey": "The depot is injected deep into the gluteal muscle. A small burst of drug washes off the bead surfaces in the first hour. After that, levels dip and then climb as the polymer beads hydrolyze into lactic and glycolic acid, freeing octreotide steadily for about four weeks. A plateau is reached roughly two to three weeks after a shot. Once released, each octreotide molecule behaves like the short-acting drug: a half-life of under two hours, breakdown in the liver and excretion in urine and feces. Because steady levels build slowly, people are started on short-acting injections first.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are the same hormone brake applied in places it is not wanted. Blocking cholecystokinin and gallbladder contraction leads to gallstones, sometimes with cholecystitis or pancreatitis. Suppressing both insulin and glucagon explains swings toward high or low blood sugar. Reduced TSH can lower thyroid function. Slowed pancreatic enzyme output causes fatty stools and gas, and less intrinsic factor and acid can affect vitamin B12. Effects on cardiac pacemaker tissue explain bradycardia. There is no antidote; effects fade as the depot runs out.", "fun": "Octreotide is also used short term to lower blood flow to the gut in bleeding esophageal varices, a use that comes from the same splanchnic blood flow effect.", "src": ["Sandostatin LAR Depot (octreotide) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Bauer W et al. Life Sci 1982"]}, "testosterone-cypionate": {"origin": "Testosterone was first isolated in 1935 by Ernst Laqueur's team in Amsterdam, who extracted it from bull testes. In the same year Adolf Butenandt and Leopold Ruzicka each found ways to make it from cholesterol, work that earned them the 1939 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. Plain testosterone is broken down fast, so chemists attached fatty acid chains (esters) to it. An ester dissolved in oil and injected into muscle leaks out slowly, then body esterases cut the chain off. The cypionate ester, made with cyclopentylpropionic acid, gave a longer action than the earlier propionate ester. Upjohn introduced it as Depo-Testosterone in the 1950s, and it became the standard injectable testosterone in the United States.", "timeline": [["1935", "Laqueur's group isolates testosterone; Butenandt and Ruzicka synthesize it."], ["1939", "Butenandt and Ruzicka share the Nobel Prize in Chemistry."], ["1950s", "Upjohn introduces testosterone cypionate in oil as Depo-Testosterone."], ["1990", "US Anabolic Steroids Control Act places testosterone in Schedule III."]], "cell": "After injection, esterases in blood and tissue remove the cypionate chain and free testosterone. Testosterone crosses cell membranes and binds the androgen receptor, a nuclear receptor that sits in the cytoplasm bound to heat shock proteins. Binding releases those proteins, the receptor pairs up, moves into the nucleus and attaches to androgen response elements on DNA, switching genes on or off. In skin, prostate and hair follicles, 5-alpha-reductase converts testosterone to dihydrotestosterone, which binds the same receptor more tightly. Aromatase in fat and other tissues converts part of it to estradiol. Rising testosterone also signals the hypothalamus and pituitary to cut GnRH, LH and FSH, so the testes make less of their own hormone and less sperm.", "body": [["Muscle", "Androgen receptor signaling increases protein synthesis and muscle fiber size, raising lean mass and strength."], ["Bone marrow", "Testosterone raises erythropoietin and iron availability, so red cell mass and hematocrit go up."], ["Testes", "Feedback suppression of LH and FSH shrinks the testes and lowers sperm production, which can cause infertility."], ["Prostate", "Dihydrotestosterone drives prostate growth, so benign enlargement and PSA can increase."], ["Skin and hair", "Oil glands enlarge, causing acne, and body and facial hair grow."], ["Breast", "Conversion to estradiol by aromatase can cause breast tissue growth in men."], ["Brain", "Libido, energy and mood change, and very high levels can bring irritability or aggression."]], "journey": "The drug is dissolved in cottonseed oil and injected deep into muscle, where it forms a small oily pool. The ester leaves that pool slowly and is cut by esterases to release testosterone. Levels peak a few days after a shot and then fall over the next two weeks or more, which explains the up and down feeling some people notice between injections. In blood, testosterone rides mostly on sex hormone binding globulin and albumin. The liver breaks it down to 17-ketosteroids and other metabolites, which are conjugated and leave mostly in the urine.", "sidefx": "Extra androgen signaling in the marrow raises hematocrit, which thickens blood and adds clot and stroke risk. Prostate tissue grows under dihydrotestosterone, worsening urinary symptoms. Aromatization to estradiol explains breast growth. Suppressed LH and FSH shrink the testes and reduce fertility. Fluid retention and higher blood pressure come partly from sodium retention. Rarely, tiny oil droplets reach the lungs right after injection and cause cough or breathlessness (pulmonary oil microembolism). High-dose misuse adds heart, liver and psychiatric harms. There is no antidote; levels fall as the depot clears.", "fun": "Ruzicka and Butenandt shared the 1939 Nobel Prize, but Butenandt was forced by the Nazi government to decline it at the time and accepted it only after the war.", "src": ["Depo-Testosterone (testosterone cypionate) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "testosterone-gel": {"origin": "Testosterone was isolated and synthesized in 1935, but for decades men who needed it had mainly two choices: oily injections with big peaks and valleys, or oral forms that stressed the liver. In the 1990s skin patches arrived, and they often irritated the skin. A clear alcohol gel was the next step. Unimed Pharmaceuticals, later part of Solvay, developed AndroGel, which the FDA approved in 2000. Testim followed in 2002, and a more concentrated AndroGel formulation came in 2011. Gels gave steady daily levels that look more like natural hormone output. After reports of young children becoming virilized from touching a parent's skin, the FDA added a boxed warning on secondary exposure in 2009.", "timeline": [["1935", "Testosterone is isolated and synthesized."], ["2000", "FDA approves AndroGel, the first US testosterone gel."], ["2002", "Testim gel is approved."], ["2009", "FDA adds a boxed warning about virilization of children from secondary exposure."], ["2011", "FDA approves a more concentrated AndroGel formulation."]], "cell": "Testosterone from the gel passes into the outer skin layer, builds up there and leaks steadily into the blood. Inside target cells it binds the cytoplasmic androgen receptor. The receptor drops its heat shock proteins, forms a dimer, enters the nucleus and binds androgen response elements, changing gene transcription. In skin, prostate and hair follicles, 5-alpha-reductase turns testosterone into dihydrotestosterone, a stronger agonist at the same receptor. Aromatase converts some testosterone to estradiol, which acts on estrogen receptors in bone, brain and breast. Steady testosterone levels feed back on the hypothalamus and pituitary, lowering GnRH, LH and FSH, so the testes make less testosterone and sperm of their own.", "body": [["Muscle and bone", "Androgen signaling builds protein and lean mass; estradiol made from testosterone helps maintain bone density."], ["Bone marrow", "Higher erythropoietin drive raises hemoglobin and hematocrit."], ["Prostate", "Dihydrotestosterone stimulates prostate tissue, so PSA and urinary symptoms can rise."], ["Testes", "Lower LH and FSH reduce sperm production and testicular size."], ["Skin", "Application sites can become red or itchy, and oil glands enlarge, causing acne."], ["Brain", "Libido, mood and energy often improve when levels return to the normal range."]], "journey": "The gel is rubbed onto clean, dry skin of the shoulders, upper arms or, for some products, the abdomen or thighs. Alcohol evaporates and leaves testosterone in the stratum corneum, which acts as a reservoir. Only a modest fraction of what is applied is absorbed, but it enters the blood steadily over the day, so levels reach steady state within about two days of daily use. In blood it binds sex hormone binding globulin and albumin. Free testosterone has a short half-life, but the skin reservoir keeps refilling the blood. The liver metabolizes it to estradiol, dihydrotestosterone and 17-ketosteroids, which leave in the urine. Testosterone left on the skin surface can transfer to anyone who touches the site.", "sidefx": "Because it is the body's own hormone, side effects follow from too much androgen action. Marrow stimulation raises hematocrit and clot risk. Prostate growth from dihydrotestosterone raises PSA and can worsen urinary flow. Aromatization explains breast tenderness or growth. Feedback suppression of LH and FSH lowers fertility. The unique hazard is transfer: residue on the skin can rub onto children or women, causing early puberty signs, genital enlargement or hair growth, which is the reason for the boxed warning. The alcohol base is also flammable until dry. There is no antidote; stopping the gel lets levels fall within days.", "fun": "Different testosterone gels are not interchangeable, because brands differ in concentration and in how much hormone actually crosses the skin.", "src": ["AndroGel (testosterone gel) FDA label", "Testim FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "somapacitan": {"origin": "Human growth hormone was first given to children in 1958, when Maurice Raben in Boston treated a child with hormone extracted from human pituitary glands taken at autopsy. In 1985 that supply ended after some recipients developed Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease, and recombinant growth hormone made in bacteria replaced it. Recombinant somatropin works well but has a short half-life, so children needed shots every day for years. The Danish company Novo Nordisk used a trick it also used in semaglutide: it attached a fatty acid side chain that grabs onto albumin in the blood, slowing clearance. It also made one amino acid swap in the hormone to give the side chain a place to attach. The weekly product, somapacitan (Sogroya), was approved for adults with growth hormone deficiency in 2020 and for children in 2023.", "timeline": [["1958", "Maurice Raben first treats a child with human pituitary growth hormone."], ["1985", "Pituitary-derived hormone is withdrawn in the US after Creutzfeldt-Jakob cases; recombinant hormone takes over."], ["2020", "FDA approves Sogroya for adults with growth hormone deficiency."], ["2023", "FDA approves Sogroya for children with growth hormone deficiency."]], "cell": "Somapacitan is human growth hormone with a single amino acid substitution that carries a fatty acid side chain. The side chain binds albumin reversibly, which keeps the molecule from being filtered and broken down quickly. When free, the hormone binds the growth hormone receptor, a cytokine-type receptor on liver, cartilage, fat and muscle cells. One hormone molecule brings two receptor units together, which activates the attached JAK2 tyrosine kinase. JAK2 phosphorylates STAT5, which moves to the nucleus and switches on genes, most importantly IGF-1 in the liver. IGF-1 then drives chondrocyte division at open growth plates. Growth hormone also has direct effects: it breaks down fat, promotes protein synthesis and opposes insulin action on glucose uptake.", "body": [["Growth plates", "IGF-1 and direct hormone action make chondrocytes divide, so long bones lengthen while the plates are still open."], ["Liver", "The liver raises IGF-1 output, which is the blood marker used to judge the response."], ["Fat tissue", "Fat breakdown increases, especially around the abdomen, so body composition shifts toward lean mass."], ["Muscle", "Protein synthesis increases and nitrogen is retained."], ["Glucose control", "Growth hormone blunts insulin action, so blood sugar can rise and diabetes risk increases."], ["Fluid balance", "Sodium and water retention can cause swelling and joint stiffness, especially early in adults."]], "journey": "Somapacitan is injected under the skin once a week. It is absorbed slowly and, once in the blood, more than 99% is bound to albumin, which acts as a circulating reservoir. The albumin binding hides it from kidney filtration and slows uptake by tissues. IGF-1 levels rise and peak a few days after each dose, then drift down before the next one. The half-life is around two to three days. The protein is broken down by proteolysis like other proteins, and the fatty acid side chain is metabolized separately, with fragments excreted in urine and feces.", "sidefx": "Side effects reflect strong growth hormone and IGF-1 signaling. Insulin antagonism raises blood sugar. Sodium and water retention cause swelling, joint pain and sometimes raised pressure inside the skull with headache and papilledema. Fast growth can stress the hip growth plate, leading to slipped capital femoral epiphysis, and can worsen scoliosis. Because IGF-1 is a growth signal, there is concern about stimulating tumors in people with active cancer. Growth hormone can also speed conversion of T4 and cortisol, unmasking hypothyroidism or adrenal insufficiency. There is no antidote; levels fall over days after stopping.", "fun": "Somapacitan and the diabetes drug semaglutide use the same basic albumin-binding approach from Novo Nordisk to turn short-lived peptides into weekly drugs.", "src": ["Sogroya (somapacitan-beco) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "lonapegsomatropin": {"origin": "For decades children with growth hormone deficiency needed a shot of recombinant somatropin every day. Several companies chased a weekly version. The Danish company Ascendis Pharma took a different route from changing the hormone itself. It built a platform called TransCon, in which an unmodified drug is tied to an inert carrier by a linker that falls apart on its own at body pH and temperature. For growth hormone, the carrier is a large methoxypolyethylene glycol molecule that keeps the hormone from being cleared and also blocks it from acting until it is released. The FDA approved lonapegsomatropin (Skytrofa) for children with growth hormone deficiency in 2021, making it the first weekly growth hormone approved for children in the US.", "timeline": [["1985", "Recombinant human growth hormone replaces pituitary-derived hormone in the US."], ["2000s", "Ascendis Pharma develops the TransCon prodrug carrier technology."], ["2021", "FDA approves Skytrofa for children with growth hormone deficiency."]], "cell": "Lonapegsomatropin is a prodrug. Inside it is ordinary somatropin, chemically the same as natural growth hormone, attached through a self-cleaving TransCon linker to a methoxypolyethylene glycol carrier. The bulky carrier blocks receptor binding and slows clearance. At normal pH and temperature the linker undergoes a slow intramolecular reaction that cuts itself, releasing unmodified somatropin at a steady rate through the week. The freed hormone binds the growth hormone receptor, bringing two receptor units together and activating JAK2. JAK2 phosphorylates STAT5, which enters the nucleus and turns on genes such as IGF-1 in the liver. IGF-1 drives chondrocyte proliferation at the growth plates, while growth hormone directly promotes fat breakdown and protein synthesis and opposes insulin.", "body": [["Growth plates", "Released somatropin and IGF-1 stimulate growth plate cartilage, increasing height velocity in children."], ["Liver", "Hepatic IGF-1 production rises and peaks about two days after each weekly dose."], ["Fat and muscle", "Fat breakdown rises and protein synthesis increases, shifting body composition toward lean tissue."], ["Glucose control", "Growth hormone opposes insulin, so blood sugar can rise."], ["Thyroid and adrenal", "Faster conversion of T4 to T3 and of cortisol to cortisone can unmask hidden hypothyroidism or adrenal insufficiency."]], "journey": "The prodrug is injected under the skin once a week. It enters the blood slowly and circulates while still attached to its carrier. The linker cleaves steadily, so free somatropin appears in the blood across the week rather than as one big spike. The released hormone has an apparent half-life of roughly a day because it is constantly being replaced from the prodrug pool. Somatropin is broken down by protein-cutting enzymes in the liver and kidney. The inert polyethylene glycol carrier is not metabolized and is excreted mainly by the kidneys.", "sidefx": "Because the active part is natural growth hormone, side effects match daily somatropin. Insulin antagonism can raise blood sugar. Fluid retention can raise pressure inside the skull, causing headache and papilledema. Rapid growth can stress the hip growth plate and worsen scoliosis. IGF-1 is a growth signal, so active cancer is a concern. Speeding the turnover of thyroid hormone and cortisol can reveal hypothyroidism or adrenal insufficiency. Pancreatitis has been reported with growth hormone products. There is no antidote; levels decline over days after the last dose.", "fun": "TransCon means transient conjugation: the carrier is meant to fall off, which is different from permanently PEGylated drugs whose polymer stays attached.", "src": ["Skytrofa (lonapegsomatropin-tcgd) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "histrelin": {"origin": "Histrelin is one of many drugs built on gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH), the 10 amino acid brain hormone whose structure was worked out in 1971 by Andrew Schally's group, with Roger Guillemin's lab close behind. Both shared the 1977 Nobel Prize. Chemists soon found that swapping certain amino acids made GnRH far more potent and long lasting. They then learned a surprise: constant exposure to these agonists shuts the pituitary down rather than stimulating it. Histrelin was one such modified peptide, first given as daily injections. The key advance was a small flexible hydrogel implant placed under the skin of the upper arm that releases histrelin steadily for a year. Vantas, for prostate cancer, was approved in 2004 and later discontinued. Supprelin LA, for central precocious puberty in children, was approved in 2007.", "timeline": [["1971", "Andrew Schally's group reports the structure of GnRH."], ["1977", "Schally and Guillemin share the Nobel Prize for hypothalamic hormones."], ["2004", "FDA approves the Vantas histrelin implant for advanced prostate cancer (later discontinued)."], ["2007", "FDA approves Supprelin LA for central precocious puberty."]], "cell": "Histrelin is a GnRH analog with a modified amino acid at position 6 and a changed C-terminus, which makes it resist peptidases and bind the GnRH receptor much more tightly than native GnRH. The GnRH receptor on pituitary gonadotroph cells is a Gq-coupled receptor. Normally GnRH arrives in pulses, and each pulse triggers phospholipase C, IP3, calcium release and LH and FSH secretion. When histrelin is present constantly, there is first a burst of LH and FSH (the flare). Within a few weeks the receptors are internalized and uncoupled from their signaling machinery. LH and FSH output then falls, and the ovaries or testes make little estradiol or testosterone. In children this returns sex hormones to prepubertal levels.", "body": [["Pituitary", "After an early surge, LH and FSH secretion is suppressed for as long as the implant releases drug."], ["Ovaries and testes", "Without LH and FSH stimulation, estradiol and testosterone fall to prepubertal levels."], ["Bones", "Lower sex hormones slow bone age advance, protecting final adult height in precocious puberty."], ["Breast and genitals", "Breast development and genital changes stop progressing or regress."], ["Uterus", "Some girls have light vaginal bleeding during the initial flare as estrogen first rises then falls."], ["Brain and mood", "Emotional lability, irritability or aggression have been reported, and rarely seizures."]], "journey": "The implant is a small cylinder of hydrogel polymer placed under the skin of the inner upper arm through a tiny incision. Water enters the hydrogel and histrelin diffuses out at a steady rate for about 12 months. Blood levels stay fairly constant. Each released molecule has a half-life of a few hours and is broken down by peptide hydrolysis, with fragments cleared by the kidneys and liver. Suppression of LH and FSH is established within about a month. When the implant is removed, the pituitary recovers and puberty resumes.", "sidefx": "The early flare reflects the drug briefly acting like extra GnRH before receptors shut down, so pubertal signs can worsen for a few weeks. Low sex hormone levels explain hot flashes in adults and are the intended effect in children. Implant site pain, bruising and swelling come from the minor surgery and the foreign body. Implants can break during removal or work their way out. Psychiatric events, seizures and raised intracranial pressure have been reported with GnRH agonists. There is no antidote; removing the implant ends the effect.", "fun": "One small implant releases drug for a whole year, replacing hundreds of daily injections that earlier histrelin treatment required.", "src": ["Supprelin LA (histrelin) FDA label", "Schally AV et al. Biochem Biophys Res Commun 1971", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "nafarelin": {"origin": "After GnRH's structure was solved in 1971, chemists raced to make stronger versions. At Syntex in Palo Alto, California, John Nestor and colleagues found that putting a bulky synthetic amino acid, D-2-naphthylalanine, at position 6 made a GnRH analog far more potent and resistant to breakdown. That molecule became nafarelin. Because it is a peptide, it would be destroyed in the stomach, so Syntex developed it as a nasal spray. Synarel was approved by the FDA in 1990 for endometriosis and later for central precocious puberty. It offered a needle-free way to create a reversible, temporary menopause-like state.", "timeline": [["1971", "The structure of GnRH is reported by Schally's group."], ["1980s", "Syntex chemists including John Nestor develop nafarelin."], ["1990", "FDA approves Synarel nasal spray for endometriosis."], ["1990s", "Synarel is approved for central precocious puberty."]], "cell": "Nafarelin is a GnRH decapeptide with D-2-naphthylalanine at position 6. That change protects it from peptidase cleavage and greatly increases its binding to the GnRH receptor on pituitary gonadotrophs. The receptor is Gq-coupled and normally responds to pulses of GnRH with phospholipase C activation, calcium release and LH and FSH secretion. Nafarelin first causes a flare of LH and FSH. With continued twice-daily dosing the stimulus is no longer pulsatile, so receptors are internalized and desensitized. LH and FSH fall, ovarian follicles stop developing, and estradiol drops to postmenopausal levels. Endometriosis implants, which depend on estrogen, shrink. In children with precocious puberty the same shutdown returns sex steroids to prepubertal levels.", "body": [["Pituitary", "An initial burst of LH and FSH is followed by suppression as the receptors desensitize."], ["Ovaries", "Follicle growth and ovulation stop, and estradiol falls, a state sometimes called medical oophorectomy."], ["Endometriosis tissue", "Estrogen-dependent implants outside the uterus shrink, easing pelvic pain."], ["Bone", "Low estrogen speeds bone turnover and reduces bone mineral density, only partly regained after stopping."], ["Vasomotor center", "Estrogen withdrawal causes hot flashes and night sweats."], ["Nose", "Local irritation and runny nose can occur at the site of absorption."]], "journey": "Nafarelin is sprayed into the nose and absorbed across the nasal lining, reaching a peak in the blood within about 10 to 40 minutes. Only a small share of each spray is absorbed, but the peptide is potent enough for that to work. About 80% is bound to plasma proteins. It is broken down by peptidases into inactive fragments that leave in urine and feces. Its half-life is about three hours, which is why it is dosed twice daily. A cold or nasal decongestant use can change how much is absorbed.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from the low-estrogen state the drug is designed to create: hot flashes, vaginal dryness, decreased libido, mood changes and loss of bone density. Bone loss is the reason treatment courses are limited. The initial flare can briefly worsen symptoms or cause ovarian cysts. Depression and suicidal thoughts, convulsions and raised intracranial pressure in children have been reported with GnRH agonists. Nasal irritation comes from the spray itself. There is no antidote; the pituitary recovers after stopping.", "fun": "Nafarelin was one of the first peptide hormone drugs delivered by nasal spray, an approach later used for other peptides such as desmopressin and calcitonin.", "src": ["Synarel (nafarelin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "mecasermin": {"origin": "In 1957 William Daughaday and William Salmon showed that growth hormone did not make cartilage grow directly in the lab; a factor in serum made in response to growth hormone did. That factor, later called somatomedin and then insulin-like growth factor 1 (IGF-1), was sequenced in 1978 by Rudolf Humbel and Erich Rinderknecht in Zurich, who noticed how much it looked like insulin. Meanwhile, in 1966 the Israeli pediatric endocrinologist Zvi Laron described children who were very short despite high growth hormone levels. Their growth hormone receptors did not work, so extra growth hormone could not help. Recombinant IGF-1 bypasses the broken step. Tercica developed mecasermin as Increlex, approved by the FDA in 2005 for severe primary IGF-1 deficiency.", "timeline": [["1957", "Salmon and Daughaday describe a growth hormone-dependent serum sulfation factor."], ["1966", "Zvi Laron describes growth hormone insensitivity (Laron syndrome)."], ["1978", "Rinderknecht and Humbel sequence IGF-1."], ["2005", "FDA approves Increlex (mecasermin)."]], "cell": "Mecasermin is recombinant human IGF-1 made in E. coli. It binds the IGF-1 receptor, a transmembrane receptor tyrosine kinase built from two alpha and two beta chains. Binding triggers autophosphorylation of the beta chains, which then recruit insulin receptor substrate 1 and Shc. These start two main cascades: PI3K and Akt, which promote cell survival, glucose uptake and protein synthesis, and Ras and MAPK, which drive cell division. At the epiphyseal growth plate this makes chondrocytes proliferate and mature, lengthening bone. Because IGF-1 resembles insulin, it also binds the insulin receptor weakly and increases glucose uptake into muscle and fat. It acts downstream of the growth hormone receptor, so it works when growth hormone signaling is defective.", "body": [["Growth plates", "Chondrocytes divide and enlarge, so height velocity increases in children with primary IGF-1 deficiency."], ["Glucose control", "Insulin-like action lowers blood sugar, which is the reason doses are linked to meals."], ["Lymphoid tissue", "Tonsils and adenoids grow, which can cause snoring, sleep apnea and ear fluid."], ["Brain and eyes", "Fluid shifts can raise intracranial pressure, causing headache and swelling of the optic disc."], ["Hips and spine", "Fast growth can contribute to slipped capital femoral epiphysis and worsen scoliosis."]], "journey": "Mecasermin is injected under the skin, usually twice a day around meals. In the blood most of it joins IGF binding protein 3 and a protein called the acid-labile subunit, forming a large complex that acts as a reservoir and slows clearance. Peak levels come a few hours after a dose. The half-life in children is about six hours. IGF-1 is broken down by proteolysis in the liver and kidneys. Children with Laron syndrome have low binding protein levels, so more of the drug is free and active.", "sidefx": "Hypoglycemia is the most common serious effect because IGF-1 acts on insulin receptors and boosts glucose uptake; it is why mecasermin is given with food and skipped when a child cannot eat. Treatment of low blood sugar follows usual hypoglycemia care with fast-acting glucose. Growth of lymphoid tissue explains tonsil and adenoid enlargement and sleep apnea. Raised intracranial pressure, slipped capital femoral epiphysis and scoliosis progression reflect rapid growth and fluid shifts. Because IGF-1 is a growth and survival signal, benign and malignant tumors have been reported, so active cancer rules out its use.", "fun": "People with Laron syndrome, studied in Israel and in a large related group in Ecuador, have very low rates of cancer and diabetes, which made IGF-1 a major topic in aging research.", "src": ["Increlex (mecasermin) FDA label", "Salmon WD, Daughaday WH. J Lab Clin Med 1957", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "thyroid-desiccated": {"origin": "Desiccated thyroid is one of the oldest hormone treatments in medicine. In 1891 the English physician George Redmayne Murray injected an extract of sheep thyroid into a woman with severe myxedema, and she recovered and lived for decades more on treatment. Within a year doctors found that eating thyroid gland or taking it by mouth also worked. Dried, powdered pig or cow thyroid tablets became standard therapy in the early 1900s. The meatpacking company Armour, which had animal glands on hand, began selling Armour Thyroid in the early twentieth century. Edward Kendall isolated thyroxine (T4) in 1914, and in 1952 Jack Gross and Rosalind Pitt-Rivers identified triiodothyronine (T3). Synthetic levothyroxine later became the preferred product because its dose is precise. Desiccated thyroid predates modern drug approval rules and has never gone through a standard FDA new drug approval.", "timeline": [["1891", "George Redmayne Murray treats myxedema with sheep thyroid extract."], ["1892", "Oral thyroid gland is shown to work."], ["1914", "Edward Kendall isolates thyroxine at the Mayo Clinic."], ["1952", "Gross and Pitt-Rivers identify triiodothyronine (T3)."]], "cell": "Desiccated thyroid supplies both T4 and T3. Both enter cells through transporters such as MCT8. Inside the cell, 5'-deiodinase enzymes remove one iodine from T4 to make T3, the more active hormone. T3 binds thyroid hormone receptors (TR-alpha and TR-beta), which are nuclear receptors already sitting on thyroid hormone response elements in DNA, usually paired with the retinoid X receptor. Without hormone they repress genes; with T3 bound they recruit coactivators and switch genes on. Targets include Na/K-ATPase, enzymes of fat and carbohydrate metabolism, cardiac alpha-myosin heavy chain, SERCA calcium pumps and beta-adrenergic receptors. The result is a higher metabolic rate, more heat production, faster heart rate and normal growth and brain function. Because the product is rich in T3 compared with human thyroid output, it causes sharper hormone peaks than levothyroxine.", "body": [["Metabolism", "Oxygen use, heat production and calorie burning rise toward normal, relieving cold intolerance and fatigue."], ["Heart", "More beta receptors and faster calcium cycling raise heart rate and contractility; too much can cause palpitations or atrial fibrillation."], ["Brain", "Thinking speed, mood and energy improve; excess causes anxiety and insomnia."], ["Pituitary", "Thyroid hormone feeds back to lower TSH, which is the main lab value used to judge replacement."], ["Bone", "Excess hormone speeds bone turnover and can thin bone over years."], ["Gut and skin", "Gut motility and skin texture return toward normal as myxedema resolves."]], "journey": "The tablets are made from dried, powdered pig thyroid. T4 and T3 are absorbed from the small intestine, and absorption is reduced by food, calcium, iron and bile acid binders. T3 reaches a peak in the blood within a few hours, which can cause a short burst of effect after each dose. Both hormones bind tightly to thyroxine-binding globulin, transthyretin and albumin. T4 has a half-life of about a week, while T3 lasts about one day. Deiodinases in the liver, kidney and other tissues break them down, and the iodine and metabolites leave in urine, with some bile excretion.", "sidefx": "Side effects are signs of too much thyroid hormone. Increased beta receptor sensitivity and faster heart muscle calcium handling cause palpitations, tremor and atrial fibrillation, and can trigger angina in people with heart disease. Higher metabolic rate causes heat intolerance, sweating and weight loss. Long-term excess speeds bone loss. The T3 content gives post-dose peaks that can provoke symptoms even when the daily average is right, and potency can vary more than with synthetic hormone. Thyroid hormone is not effective for weight loss in people with normal thyroid function. Overdose is treated supportively, often with beta blockers.", "fun": "Murray's first patient kept taking thyroid extract and lived about 28 more years, until 1919, a remarkable outcome for a disease that had been progressive.", "src": ["Armour Thyroid prescribing information", "Murray GR. BMJ 1891", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "hydrocortisone-granules": {"origin": "Hydrocortisone is cortisol, the body's main stress hormone. Edward Kendall at the Mayo Clinic and Tadeus Reichstein in Switzerland isolated it, among other adrenal steroids, in the 1930s, and it became available as a medicine in the early 1950s. For babies and young children with adrenal insufficiency, such as those with congenital adrenal hyperplasia, there was a long-standing problem: hydrocortisone came only as adult-strength tablets. Parents had to crush or split them, which gave uneven doses. The British company Diurnal developed tiny taste-masked granules in capsules that can be opened and sprinkled on soft food. Sold as Alkindi Sprinkle, it was approved by the FDA in 2020 as a hydrocortisone form designed for children.", "timeline": [["1930s", "Kendall and Reichstein isolate adrenal cortical steroids including cortisol."], ["1950", "Kendall, Reichstein and Hench share the Nobel Prize for adrenal hormones."], ["1950s", "Hydrocortisone becomes available as a medicine."], ["2020", "FDA approves Alkindi Sprinkle hydrocortisone granules for children with adrenal insufficiency."]], "cell": "Hydrocortisone diffuses into cells and binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor. The receptor releases its heat shock proteins, moves into the nucleus and binds glucocorticoid response elements in DNA, switching on genes for gluconeogenic enzymes and anti-inflammatory proteins such as annexin A1. It also binds and blocks transcription factors NF-kB and AP-1, turning down cytokine and inflammatory genes. Hydrocortisone also activates the mineralocorticoid receptor in the kidney, promoting sodium retention and potassium loss. In congenital adrenal hyperplasia from 21-hydroxylase deficiency, the adrenal gland cannot make cortisol, so ACTH rises and drives excess androgen production. Replacing cortisol restores feedback on the hypothalamus and pituitary, lowering ACTH and the androgen excess.", "body": [["Metabolism", "Cortisol supports blood sugar by driving liver gluconeogenesis, which protects against low sugar during illness."], ["Blood vessels", "Cortisol keeps vessels responsive to adrenaline and noradrenaline, maintaining blood pressure."], ["Kidneys", "Mineralocorticoid activity holds sodium and water, supporting blood volume."], ["Pituitary and adrenals", "Replacement lowers ACTH, reducing the excess adrenal androgens seen in congenital adrenal hyperplasia."], ["Growth", "Correct replacement allows normal growth; too much slows growth by blocking growth plate activity."], ["Immune system", "Too much steroid dampens immune responses and raises infection risk."]], "journey": "The capsule is opened and the granules are sprinkled onto a spoonful of soft food or put directly in the mouth, then swallowed without chewing. A taste-masking coat hides the bitter drug. Granules should not be mixed with liquid because the coating can dissolve and release bitter drug. Hydrocortisone is absorbed well from the gut, with a peak at about one hour. In blood it binds corticosteroid-binding globulin and albumin. The liver inactivates it through 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase and CYP3A4, and metabolites are excreted in the urine. The plasma half-life is under two hours, but gene effects last longer, so doses are spread through the day.", "sidefx": "Too little cortisol is the big danger: a child who misses doses, vomits or has a serious illness can slip into adrenal crisis with low blood sugar, low blood pressure and shock, because the body cannot raise its own cortisol under stress. That is why stress dosing and injectable hydrocortisone are part of the plan. Switching from crushed tablets to granules can change the real dose received. Too much cortisol causes Cushing-like effects such as weight gain and slowed growth, from glucocorticoid action on fat, bone and growth plates, plus higher infection risk.", "fun": "The granules are so small that the full contents of a capsule look like a pinch of sand, which lets very small doses be given accurately to newborns.", "src": ["Alkindi Sprinkle (hydrocortisone granules) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "cortisone": {"origin": "Cortisone has one of the most dramatic debuts in drug history. In the 1930s Edward Kendall at the Mayo Clinic isolated a series of steroids from cattle adrenal glands and labeled them by letters; cortisone was Compound E. Only tiny amounts could be extracted. In 1944 Lewis Sarett at Merck synthesized it from bile acid in dozens of steps. Mayo rheumatologist Philip Hench had long suspected an adrenal factor because arthritis eased during pregnancy and jaundice. In September 1948 he gave Compound E to a woman bedridden with rheumatoid arthritis, and within days she could walk. Merck sold it as Cortone. Kendall, Hench and the Swiss chemist Tadeus Reichstein shared the 1950 Nobel Prize, only two years after that first patient.", "timeline": [["1930s", "Kendall isolates Compound E from adrenal glands; Reichstein works in parallel."], ["1944", "Lewis Sarett at Merck completes a synthesis of cortisone."], ["1948", "Philip Hench gives cortisone to a patient with rheumatoid arthritis at Mayo Clinic."], ["1950", "Kendall, Hench and Reichstein share the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."]], "cell": "Cortisone itself is inactive because it carries a keto group at carbon 11. In the liver, 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 1 reduces that keto group to a hydroxyl, turning cortisone into cortisol (hydrocortisone). Cortisol enters cells and binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor. The activated receptor moves to the nucleus, binds glucocorticoid response elements to switch on genes such as annexin A1 (which blocks phospholipase A2) and gluconeogenic enzymes, and ties up NF-kB and AP-1 to turn off cytokine, COX-2 and adhesion molecule genes. Cortisol also activates mineralocorticoid receptors in the kidney, causing sodium retention. Cortisone is slightly less potent than hydrocortisone and depends on a working liver for activation.", "body": [["Immune system", "Fewer cytokines and less white cell movement reduce inflammation, but also weaken defenses against infection."], ["Joints", "Synovial inflammation calms, which was the first striking effect seen in rheumatoid arthritis."], ["Metabolism", "Liver glucose output rises and insulin action falls, raising blood sugar."], ["Kidneys", "Mineralocorticoid effects retain sodium and water and lose potassium, which can raise blood pressure."], ["Bone and muscle", "Long-term use slows bone formation and breaks down muscle protein."], ["Adrenal axis", "Feedback suppresses ACTH, so the adrenal glands shrink with long courses."]], "journey": "Cortisone acetate is taken by mouth and absorbed from the gut. In the liver, 11-beta-HSD1 converts it into hydrocortisone, which does the work. Cortisone itself lasts only about half an hour in plasma, but the resulting cortisol acts on genes for 8 to 12 hours. Cortisol binds corticosteroid-binding globulin and albumin in blood. It is further inactivated in the liver and kidney, and metabolites are excreted in urine. Severe liver disease can blunt conversion, which makes hydrocortisone the more reliable choice in that setting.", "sidefx": "Side effects come from cortisol acting everywhere at once. Immune suppression raises infection risk. Gluconeogenesis and insulin resistance cause high blood sugar. Mineralocorticoid action causes fluid retention, hypertension and low potassium. Reduced bone formation leads to osteoporosis, and effects on the brain cause insomnia, mood changes or psychosis. Long use suppresses the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis, so stopping suddenly can cause adrenal crisis; that is why long courses are tapered. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Hench's first patient was given her cortisone dose on September 21, 1948, and the Nobel Prize followed in 1950, one of the fastest gaps between discovery and prize.", "src": ["Cortisone acetate prescribing information", "Hench PS et al. Proc Staff Meet Mayo Clin 1949", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "triamcinolone-injection": {"origin": "After cortisone's 1948 debut, chemists spent the 1950s tweaking the steroid skeleton to get stronger anti-inflammatory action with less salt retention. Adding a fluorine atom at carbon 9 boosted potency, and adding a hydroxyl group at carbon 16 removed most of the salt-retaining effect. Triamcinolone, built this way, was introduced in the late 1950s. Linking the 16 and 17 hydroxyl groups with acetone formed triamcinolone acetonide, which is less water soluble. Suspended as tiny crystals, it dissolves slowly after injection, giving weeks of effect from one shot. Squibb sold it as Kenalog. Newer forms include Zilretta, an extended-release joint injection in polymer microspheres approved in 2017, and eye products such as Triesence and Xipere.", "timeline": [["1948", "Cortisone is first used for rheumatoid arthritis, launching the steroid era."], ["1950s", "Fluorinated corticosteroids including triamcinolone are developed."], ["1960s", "Triamcinolone acetonide suspension (Kenalog) becomes a standard depot injection."], ["2017", "FDA approves Zilretta, an extended-release intra-articular triamcinolone."]], "cell": "Triamcinolone acetonide is a glucocorticoid receptor agonist with about five times the anti-inflammatory potency of hydrocortisone and almost no mineralocorticoid activity, thanks to its 9-fluoro and 16-hydroxy groups. It enters cells, binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor and releases heat shock proteins. The receptor complex enters the nucleus. There it switches on anti-inflammatory genes such as annexin A1, which inhibits phospholipase A2 and so lowers both prostaglandin and leukotriene production. It also blocks NF-kB and AP-1, turning off genes for cytokines (IL-1, IL-6, TNF), COX-2 and adhesion molecules. In a joint or tissue this calms swelling, white cell influx and pain. The microcrystalline suspension acts as a depot that slowly dissolves.", "body": [["Joints", "Injected into a joint, it calms synovial inflammation and swelling for weeks."], ["Skin", "Injected into keloids or inflamed skin lesions, it reduces inflammation and collagen growth; superficial injection can thin fat and lighten skin."], ["Whole body (IM)", "A deep muscle injection gives a systemic steroid effect lasting weeks, used for allergic and inflammatory conditions."], ["Metabolism", "Blood sugar rises for days after injection, especially noticeable in people with diabetes."], ["Adrenal axis", "Absorbed steroid suppresses ACTH and the adrenal glands for weeks."], ["Eyes", "Ophthalmic forms reduce inflammation and swelling inside the eye; steroids can raise eye pressure and speed cataracts."]], "journey": "Kenalog is a suspension of tiny crystals that is shaken before use and injected into muscle, joints, bursae or lesions. The crystals dissolve slowly, so drug trickles out over weeks. From a joint, some drug reaches the bloodstream, which is why blood sugar can rise after a joint shot. Free triamcinolone in plasma has a half-life of a few hours and is metabolized in the liver into inactive products that leave in urine. Zilretta places the drug in polymer microspheres that release it in the joint over about three months and keep blood levels lower.", "sidefx": "Local effects come from strong glucocorticoid action at the site: fat atrophy and skin depigmentation when drug sits in superficial tissue, and a short post-injection flare when crystals irritate the joint. Systemic absorption causes high blood sugar, flushing, insomnia and suppression of the adrenal axis. Immune suppression raises infection risk, including septic arthritis if a joint becomes contaminated. The crystals are particles, so the suspension is not for intravenous use; accidental entry into arteries during epidural injection has been linked to stroke and spinal cord injury. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The same triamcinolone acetonide molecule is used in nasal sprays, skin creams, dental pastes and joint injections; the formulation decides where it acts and for how long.", "src": ["Kenalog-40 (triamcinolone acetonide) FDA label", "Zilretta FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "estradiol-progesterone": {"origin": "Estradiol was isolated from sow ovaries in 1935 by Edward Doisy's team in St. Louis, and progesterone was purified from the corpus luteum in 1934 by several groups, including Adolf Butenandt's. Early menopausal hormone therapy used estrogen alone, but by the 1970s it was clear that unopposed estrogen raised endometrial cancer risk, so a progestogen was added for women with a uterus. For decades the combination products used synthetic progestins. TherapeuticsMD developed a single capsule that contains estradiol and progesterone chemically identical to the body's own hormones, using a special oil formulation to dissolve both. Bijuva was approved by the FDA in 2018 as the first approved combination of these two bioidentical hormones in one pill.", "timeline": [["1934", "Progesterone is isolated and crystallized from corpus luteum."], ["1935", "Doisy's group isolates estradiol from sow ovaries."], ["1970s", "Unopposed estrogen is linked to endometrial cancer, prompting combination therapy."], ["2002", "Women's Health Initiative results reshape menopausal hormone therapy."], ["2018", "FDA approves Bijuva estradiol and progesterone capsules."]], "cell": "Estradiol enters cells and binds nuclear estrogen receptors ER-alpha and ER-beta. The receptor dimerizes, binds estrogen response elements and changes gene transcription. In the hypothalamus this restores the narrow comfort zone of the thermoregulatory center, which widens to cause hot flashes when estrogen falls. Estrogen also acts on bone, vaginal tissue and liver, where it raises sex hormone binding globulin and clotting factors. In the uterus estrogen makes the lining grow. Progesterone binds progesterone receptors in the endometrium and changes it from proliferative to secretory. It also lowers estrogen receptor levels and increases enzymes that inactivate estradiol in endometrial cells. That opposes estrogen-driven hyperplasia, which is the reason the progesterone is there.", "body": [["Hypothalamus", "Restored estrogen signaling stabilizes temperature control, reducing hot flashes and night sweats."], ["Uterus", "Progesterone keeps the lining from overgrowing, lowering the endometrial cancer risk that estrogen alone would carry."], ["Liver", "Oral estrogen raises clotting factors, SHBG and triglycerides through first-pass liver effects."], ["Blood clotting", "Higher clotting factor levels raise the risk of DVT, pulmonary embolism and stroke."], ["Breast", "Breast tenderness is common, and combined estrogen plus progestin therapy has been linked with breast cancer risk."], ["Brain", "Progesterone metabolites act on GABA-A receptors, which can cause drowsiness."]], "journey": "The capsule is taken by mouth with food in the evening. Estradiol and progesterone are dissolved in an oil-based fill that helps both be absorbed. In the liver, much estradiol is converted to estrone and conjugated; estrone sulfate then acts as a circulating reservoir that can be turned back into estradiol. Progesterone undergoes heavy first-pass metabolism into pregnane metabolites, including neuroactive ones. Both peak in a few hours. Estradiol is mostly bound to SHBG and albumin and is metabolized by CYP3A4 and conjugation, then excreted in urine. Progesterone's half-life is about 10 hours.", "sidefx": "Oral estrogen passes through the liver first and increases clotting factor production, which explains the higher risk of DVT, pulmonary embolism and stroke. Estrogen plus progestin therapy in the Women's Health Initiative was associated with heart attack, breast cancer and probable dementia in older women. Estrogen raises bile cholesterol, raising gallbladder disease risk, and can raise triglycerides. Progesterone metabolites that act on GABA-A receptors explain drowsiness. Abnormal vaginal bleeding is evaluated because endometrial cancer can still occur. There is no antidote; effects fade after stopping.", "fun": "Progesterone taken by mouth was long considered impractical because the liver destroys most of it; modern micronized and oil-based formulations made oral progesterone workable.", "src": ["Bijuva (estradiol and progesterone) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Women's Health Initiative, JAMA 2002"]}, "conjugated-estrogens-vaginal": {"origin": "Conjugated estrogens come from the urine of pregnant mares, which is rich in water-soluble estrogen sulfates. The Canadian company Ayerst launched the mixture as Premarin in 1941 in Canada, and it reached the US soon after; the name comes from PREgnant MARes' urINe. It became one of the most prescribed drugs in America. A vaginal cream form was developed to treat vaginal and urinary symptoms of menopause more directly. In 2002 the Women's Health Initiative reported risks with systemic estrogen plus progestin, and boxed warnings were added to estrogen labels, including the cream. In 2025 the FDA announced that boxed warnings on menopausal hormone therapy labels would be removed, and labels are being updated.", "timeline": [["1941", "Ayerst introduces Premarin, conjugated estrogens from pregnant mares' urine, in Canada."], ["1942", "Premarin is approved in the US."], ["2002", "Women's Health Initiative results lead to boxed warnings on estrogen products."], ["2025", "FDA announces removal of boxed warnings from menopausal hormone therapy labels."]], "cell": "Conjugated estrogens are a mixture, mainly sodium estrone sulfate and sodium equilin sulfate, plus smaller amounts of other estrogens. In tissue and blood, sulfatase enzymes remove the sulfate to release estrone and equilin, and some estrone is converted to estradiol. These bind nuclear estrogen receptors ER-alpha and ER-beta. The receptors dimerize, bind estrogen response elements in DNA and switch on genes that make the vaginal epithelium thicken and mature into many cell layers. Mature cells store glycogen, which lactobacilli ferment to lactic acid, lowering vaginal pH. Estrogen also increases blood flow and secretions. Equilin is a horse estrogen not made by humans.", "body": [["Vagina", "The thin, dry lining of menopause thickens and becomes moist and elastic again, reducing painful intercourse and itching."], ["Vaginal flora", "Glycogen-rich cells feed lactobacilli, which restore an acidic pH that helps resist infection."], ["Urinary tract", "Estrogen receptors in the urethra and bladder base respond, which can ease urgency and recurrent urinary infections."], ["Uterus", "Absorbed estrogen can stimulate the uterine lining, so unexpected bleeding needs evaluation."], ["Whole body", "Some estrogen is absorbed, which is why class warnings about clots, stroke and breast cancer apply."]], "journey": "The cream is placed inside the vagina with an applicator. Estrogens cross the vaginal lining easily, especially when it is thin at the start of treatment, so some reaches the blood within hours. As the epithelium thickens, absorption tends to fall. Once in the blood, the estrogens are bound to SHBG and albumin, metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4 and conjugation, and recycled through bile and gut before being excreted in urine as sulfates and glucuronides. Estrone has a half-life of many hours because of this recycling.", "sidefx": "Local side effects such as discharge and irritation come from the cream and the changing epithelium. Because some estrogen is absorbed, systemic effects can occur: breast tenderness, stimulation of the uterine lining with possible hyperplasia and endometrial cancer if estrogen is unopposed, and the clotting, stroke and breast cancer risks described for systemic estrogen. Estrogen raises clotting factors and bile cholesterol, linking it to thrombosis and gallbladder disease. The oil-based cream can weaken latex condoms and diaphragms. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Premarin was once among the top-selling drugs in the US, and its production depended on farms that collected urine from pregnant mares.", "src": ["Premarin Vaginal Cream (conjugated estrogens) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Women's Health Initiative, JAMA 2002"]}, "elinzanetant": {"origin": "Elinzanetant comes from a line of research on why hot flashes happen. In the 2000s and 2010s scientists found that a group of hypothalamic neurons, called KNDy neurons because they make kisspeptin, neurokinin B and dynorphin, become overactive when estrogen falls. Neurokinin B from these cells acts on NK3 receptors and pushes the body's thermostat toward heat loss. Blocking NK3 offered a hormone-free treatment. Elinzanetant began as a neurokinin receptor antagonist in GSK research and was developed for hot flashes by the small UK company KaNDy Therapeutics, and was acquired by Bayer in 2020. Unlike fezolinetant, which blocks only NK3, elinzanetant blocks both NK1 and NK3. The FDA approved it as Lynkuet in 2025 for moderate to severe vasomotor symptoms of menopause.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Research links hypothalamic KNDy neurons and neurokinin B to hot flashes."], ["2020", "Bayer acquires KaNDy Therapeutics and elinzanetant."], ["2023", "Fezolinetant becomes the first FDA-approved NK3 antagonist for hot flashes."], ["2025", "FDA approves Lynkuet (elinzanetant), a dual NK1 and NK3 antagonist."]], "cell": "In the arcuate nucleus of the hypothalamus, KNDy neurons are normally restrained by estrogen. After menopause they enlarge and fire more, releasing neurokinin B, which acts on NK3 receptors on these neurons and on nearby thermoregulatory neurons in the preoptic area. NK3 is a Gq-coupled receptor, so activation raises intracellular calcium and excitability. The result is a sudden heat-loss response: skin vessels dilate and sweating starts, felt as a hot flash. Elinzanetant competitively blocks NK3 receptors, damping this signal. It also blocks NK1 receptors, the receptor for substance P, which is thought to contribute to skin vasodilation and to sleep disruption. It does not bind estrogen receptors and does not change hormone levels.", "body": [["Hypothalamus", "Calmer KNDy neuron signaling narrows the thermostat swings that set off hot flashes."], ["Skin vessels", "Fewer sudden heat-loss episodes mean less flushing and sweating."], ["Sleep", "Fewer night sweats and NK1 blockade can reduce night awakenings."], ["Brain alertness", "Sleepiness and fatigue are common, which is why it is taken at bedtime."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises have been reported with the NK3 antagonist class."]], "journey": "Elinzanetant is taken by mouth once daily at bedtime. It is absorbed within about an hour and is highly protein bound. It crosses the blood-brain barrier to reach the hypothalamus. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP3A4, so strong inhibitors raise its levels and inducers lower them. Its half-life is about 15 hours. Metabolites are excreted mainly in the feces. Hot flash frequency falls within the first weeks.", "sidefx": "Somnolence and fatigue reflect central nervous system effects of neurokinin receptor blockade, adding to other sedatives and alcohol. Headache and dizziness are also reported. The NK3 antagonist class has been associated with liver enzyme elevations. Because it is cleared by CYP3A4, drugs that inhibit that enzyme can raise exposure and side effects. It does not carry estrogen risks such as endometrial stimulation. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Neurokinin antagonists for hot flashes are among the first menopause treatments designed to act directly on the brain's temperature circuit instead of replacing estrogen.", "src": ["Lynkuet (elinzanetant) FDA label", "Rance NE et al. Front Neuroendocrinol 2013"]}, "norgestrel-otc": {"origin": "Norgestrel was the product of fully synthetic steroid chemistry. In the early 1960s the British chemist Herchel Smith, working with Wyeth, devised a total synthesis that produced norgestrel, a racemic mix of two mirror-image molecules; only the levo form, levonorgestrel, is active. Wyeth used norgestrel in birth control pills, and in 1973 the FDA approved Ovrette, a progestin-only pill containing it. Progestin-only pills, sometimes called the minipill, contain no estrogen. Decades later, HRA Pharma (now part of Perrigo) asked the FDA to move the product over the counter. In July 2023 the FDA approved Opill, making it the first daily oral contraceptive available in the US without a prescription.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Herchel Smith at Wyeth develops a total synthesis of norgestrel."], ["1973", "FDA approves Ovrette, a norgestrel progestin-only pill, by prescription."], ["2023", "FDA approves Opill for over-the-counter sale, the first US nonprescription daily birth control pill."], ["2024", "Opill becomes available in US stores."]], "cell": "Norgestrel contains equal amounts of levonorgestrel and its inactive mirror image. Levonorgestrel is a 19-nortestosterone (gonane) progestin that binds and activates progesterone receptors, nuclear receptors that change gene transcription. In the cervix, progesterone receptor signaling makes mucus thick and scant, so sperm cannot get through. In the endometrium it thins the lining and makes it less receptive to implantation. At the hypothalamus and pituitary it blunts the mid-cycle LH surge, preventing ovulation in some cycles but not all. Because there is no estrogen, the main protective effect is the cervical mucus change, which wears off within about a day after a pill.", "body": [["Cervix", "Thick, sticky mucus blocks sperm from entering the uterus."], ["Endometrium", "A thinner lining is less likely to support implantation and is more prone to unpredictable spotting."], ["Ovaries", "Ovulation is suppressed in some cycles; when follicles grow without ovulating, functional ovarian cysts can form."], ["Menstrual pattern", "Bleeding becomes irregular because no estrogen is given to stabilize the lining."], ["Fallopian tubes", "Progestin slows tubal movement, which is why a higher share of the rare pregnancies that do occur are ectopic."]], "journey": "The pill is taken by mouth at the same time every day. Levonorgestrel is absorbed well, without much first-pass loss, and peaks in one to two hours. In blood it binds SHBG and albumin. The liver metabolizes it, partly through CYP3A4, and metabolites leave in urine and feces. Its half-life is about a day, but the cervical mucus effect fades after about 24 hours, so a pill taken more than three hours late reduces protection. Enzyme-inducing drugs such as rifampin and some antiseizure drugs speed its breakdown.", "sidefx": "Irregular bleeding and spotting are the most common effects, because a progestin alone thins and destabilizes the endometrium without estrogen to keep it steady. Headache, breast tenderness and nausea reflect hormone receptor effects. Partial ovulation suppression lets follicles grow without releasing an egg, which can cause ovarian cysts. Effectiveness drops when pills are late or when liver enzyme inducers lower levels. It contains no estrogen, so it avoids estrogen's clotting effects. It does not protect against HIV or other sexually transmitted infections. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Opill uses the same active ingredient and strength that was sold by prescription as Ovrette for decades before it moved over the counter.", "src": ["Opill (norgestrel) Drug Facts label", "FDA news release July 2023", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "ethinyl-estradiol-levonorgestrel": {"origin": "This pill joins two lab-made hormones. Ethinyl estradiol was synthesized in 1938 by Hans Herloff Inhoffen and Walter Hohlweg at Schering AG in Berlin; adding an ethinyl group to estradiol let it survive the liver when taken by mouth. Levonorgestrel is the active half of norgestrel, a progestin made by total synthesis in the 1960s by Herchel Smith at Wyeth. After the first US birth control pill, Enovid, was approved in 1960, companies worked to lower hormone doses and improve bleeding control. Levonorgestrel combinations became some of the most widely used pills in the world, and the WHO lists them as essential medicines. Later the same pair was used for extended-cycle pills, starting with Seasonale in 2003, and a continuous pill in 2007.", "timeline": [["1938", "Inhoffen and Hohlweg synthesize ethinyl estradiol at Schering AG."], ["1960", "FDA approves Enovid, the first birth control pill."], ["1960s", "Herchel Smith develops norgestrel, the source of levonorgestrel."], ["2003", "FDA approves Seasonale, an extended-cycle levonorgestrel and ethinyl estradiol pill."], ["2007", "FDA approves the first continuous, no-break combined pill using this pair."]], "cell": "Levonorgestrel activates progesterone receptors and ethinyl estradiol activates estrogen receptors. Together they give steady negative feedback to the hypothalamus and anterior pituitary. Fewer GnRH pulses mean less FSH, so a dominant follicle does not develop, and no mid-cycle LH surge, so ovulation does not occur. The progestin also thickens cervical mucus and makes the endometrium thin and unreceptive. The estrogen stabilizes the endometrium so bleeding is more predictable, and it adds to FSH suppression. Ethinyl estradiol resists liver breakdown far better than estradiol, so it has strong effects on liver protein synthesis, raising SHBG and clotting factors. Levonorgestrel is a second-generation progestin with some androgenic activity.", "body": [["Ovaries", "Follicle growth and ovulation are suppressed, which is the main way the pill prevents pregnancy."], ["Cervix and uterus", "Thick cervical mucus blocks sperm, and a thin lining reduces bleeding and menstrual cramps."], ["Liver", "Ethinyl estradiol raises clotting factors, SHBG, angiotensinogen and triglycerides."], ["Blood vessels", "More clotting factors raise DVT, pulmonary embolism and stroke risk, much more so in smokers over 35."], ["Skin", "Higher SHBG lowers free testosterone, which often improves acne, though levonorgestrel's androgenic effect works the other way somewhat."], ["Blood pressure", "More angiotensinogen from the liver can raise blood pressure."]], "journey": "Both hormones are absorbed well by mouth. Levonorgestrel has nearly complete bioavailability and peaks in one to two hours; ethinyl estradiol is partly sulfated in the gut wall and liver on its first pass. Both are highly protein bound: levonorgestrel to SHBG and albumin, ethinyl estradiol mainly to albumin. Ethinyl estradiol is broken down by CYP3A4 hydroxylation and by conjugation, and its conjugates are recycled through the bile and gut. Levonorgestrel is reduced and conjugated in the liver. Both have half-lives around a day, and metabolites leave in urine and feces. CYP3A4 inducers such as rifampin reduce levels of both.", "sidefx": "Clotting risk comes from ethinyl estradiol's strong effect on the liver, which makes more clotting factors and less natural anticoagulant activity; smoking adds vessel damage, hence the boxed warning. Higher angiotensinogen raises blood pressure. Estrogen increases bile cholesterol, raising gallbladder disease risk, and can raise triglycerides. Liver adenomas are rare but linked to long use. Nausea and breast tenderness are direct estrogen effects. Breakthrough bleeding reflects an endometrium that is not fully stable on low doses. Levonorgestrel's androgenic effect can lower HDL slightly. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Pills built from levonorgestrel and ethinyl estradiol are considered by many studies to have among the lowest clot risks of combined pills.", "src": ["Levora (levonorgestrel and ethinyl estradiol) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "ethinyl-estradiol-norethindrone": {"origin": "Norethindrone was first synthesized on October 15, 1951, at the small company Syntex in Mexico City by chemist Luis Miramontes, working with Carl Djerassi and George Rosenkranz. It was one of the first highly active progestins that worked by mouth, built on a steroid skeleton from Mexican yams. Combined with an estrogen, it became the basis of early birth control pills such as Ortho-Novum. Ethinyl estradiol had been made in 1938 at Schering AG in Berlin. Over time pill makers lowered estrogen doses to reduce clot risk. Loestrin, a low-estrogen combination of norethindrone acetate and ethinyl estradiol, was introduced in the 1970s, and Lo Loestrin Fe, with an even lower estrogen dose, was approved in 2010.", "timeline": [["1938", "Ethinyl estradiol is synthesized at Schering AG."], ["1951", "Miramontes, Djerassi and Rosenkranz synthesize norethindrone at Syntex in Mexico City."], ["1960s", "Norethindrone combination pills such as Ortho-Novum reach the US market."], ["1970s", "Low-estrogen Loestrin is introduced."], ["2010", "FDA approves Lo Loestrin Fe."]], "cell": "Norethindrone acetate is quickly deacetylated to norethindrone, a 19-nortestosterone (estrane) progestin that activates the progesterone receptor. It also has weak androgenic activity and a small part of it is converted to ethinyl estradiol in the body. Ethinyl estradiol activates estrogen receptors ER-alpha and ER-beta. Together the hormones give constant negative feedback to the hypothalamus and pituitary, lowering GnRH pulse frequency, FSH and LH. Without FSH, follicles do not mature, and without the LH surge, ovulation does not happen. The progestin also thickens cervical mucus and thins the endometrium. The estrogen supports the lining so bleeding is more regular and boosts the liver's production of SHBG and clotting factors.", "body": [["Ovaries", "Ovulation is suppressed by loss of the mid-cycle LH surge."], ["Cervix and uterus", "Thick mucus blocks sperm, and a thin lining means lighter periods; with very low estrogen pills, periods may become very light or absent."], ["Liver", "Ethinyl estradiol increases clotting factors, SHBG and angiotensinogen."], ["Blood vessels", "Clot risk rises, especially with smoking and age over 35."], ["Breast and mood", "Breast tenderness and mood changes can occur from hormone receptor effects."], ["Blood counts", "Fe versions include iron tablets in the placebo week, which add iron without hormone."]], "journey": "Norethindrone acetate is absorbed by mouth and converted to norethindrone during first pass. Norethindrone peaks in one to two hours, has a half-life of roughly 8 to 13 hours, and is bound to albumin and SHBG. The liver reduces it and conjugates it to glucuronides and sulfates. Ethinyl estradiol peaks in about two hours, is metabolized by CYP3A4 and conjugation, and is recycled through bile. Both leave in urine and feces as metabolites. Drugs that induce CYP3A4 lower levels and can reduce effectiveness.", "sidefx": "Ethinyl estradiol's push on liver protein synthesis raises clotting factors, explaining venous and arterial clot risk, which is magnified by smoking. It also raises angiotensinogen and blood pressure, bile cholesterol and gallbladder disease, and triglycerides. Very low estrogen pills give less endometrial support, so breakthrough bleeding is more common, and some users stop having periods. Norethindrone's weak androgen activity can affect lipids and skin. Nausea and breast tenderness are direct hormone effects. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Norethindrone's synthesis began with diosgenin, a chemical from wild Mexican yams, which Syntex turned into a cheap starting point for steroid hormones.", "src": ["Loestrin (norethindrone acetate and ethinyl estradiol) FDA label", "Djerassi C et al. J Am Chem Soc 1954", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "desogestrel-ethinyl-estradiol": {"origin": "By the 1970s pill makers wanted progestins that prevented ovulation strongly but had less androgen activity than older ones such as levonorgestrel, which can worsen acne and lower HDL cholesterol. The Dutch company Organon developed desogestrel, a prodrug of etonogestrel, and launched it in Europe with ethinyl estradiol as Marvelon in 1981. It reached the US in 1992 as Desogen and Ortho-Cept. These so-called third-generation pills soon faced a scare: in 1995 UK regulators warned that studies suggested a higher risk of venous blood clots than with levonorgestrel pills. Later research supported a modestly higher clot risk, and FDA labels describe it. Desogestrel pills remain widely used.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Organon develops desogestrel."], ["1981", "Marvelon (desogestrel and ethinyl estradiol) launches in Europe."], ["1992", "FDA approves Desogen and Ortho-Cept in the US."], ["1995", "UK warning on venous clot risk with third-generation pills triggers the pill scare."]], "cell": "Desogestrel itself is inactive. In the gut wall and liver it is converted to etonogestrel (3-keto-desogestrel), a very selective progesterone receptor agonist with low affinity for the androgen receptor. Ethinyl estradiol activates estrogen receptors. Together they suppress hypothalamic GnRH and pituitary FSH and LH, so follicles do not mature and the LH surge does not occur. The progestin thickens cervical mucus and thins the endometrium. Because etonogestrel opposes estrogen's effects on the liver less than levonorgestrel does, the net effect is higher SHBG and stronger estrogenic liver effects, including clotting factor changes. That same higher SHBG lowers free testosterone, which helps acne.", "body": [["Ovaries", "Ovulation is reliably blocked by suppressed FSH and LH."], ["Cervix and uterus", "Thick cervical mucus and a thin lining add to contraceptive effect and lighten periods."], ["Skin", "Low androgenic activity and higher SHBG often improve acne and oily skin."], ["Lipids", "HDL tends to rise rather than fall, because the progestin does not oppose estrogen's HDL effect much."], ["Blood clotting", "Venous clot risk is somewhat higher than with levonorgestrel pills in epidemiologic studies."], ["Blood pressure", "Estrogen-driven angiotensinogen can raise blood pressure."]], "journey": "Desogestrel is absorbed by mouth and converted to etonogestrel by CYP2C9 and CYP3A4 during first pass. Etonogestrel peaks in about one and a half hours, is mostly bound to albumin and SHBG, and has a half-life of over a day. It is then metabolized by CYP3A4. Ethinyl estradiol peaks in one to two hours, is broken down by CYP3A4 and conjugation and recycled through bile. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. Enzyme inducers lower levels of both hormones. Etonogestrel is the same hormone used in the contraceptive implant and vaginal ring.", "sidefx": "Clot risk comes from ethinyl estradiol's liver effects on clotting proteins; because etonogestrel opposes these effects less than levonorgestrel, the net estrogenic push is greater, which is the proposed reason for the higher venous clot rate. Smoking adds arterial risk, the basis of the boxed warning. Blood pressure rise, gallbladder disease and higher triglycerides also come from estrogen's liver effects. Breakthrough bleeding, nausea, breast tenderness and mood changes are hormone receptor effects. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Etonogestrel, the active form of desogestrel, is also the hormone inside the arm implant Nexplanon and the vaginal ring NuvaRing.", "src": ["Apri (desogestrel and ethinyl estradiol) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "testosterone-nasal": {"origin": "Testosterone has been available since the 1930s, but every delivery route has drawbacks: injections cause peaks and troughs, and skin gels can rub off onto children and partners. Researchers looked at the nose, whose thin, blood-rich lining absorbs some drugs quickly. The Canadian company Trimel Pharmaceuticals (later Acerus) developed a thick testosterone gel delivered into each nostril with a metered pump. The FDA approved it as Natesto in 2014. Because the gel is applied inside the nose, there is no skin site for others to touch, and it does not carry the secondary exposure boxed warning found on topical skin gels. Its short action means it is used three times a day.", "timeline": [["1935", "Testosterone is isolated and synthesized."], ["2000", "Testosterone skin gels arrive in the US."], ["2014", "FDA approves Natesto, the first nasal testosterone gel."]], "cell": "Testosterone absorbed through the nasal lining binds the androgen receptor, a ligand-activated transcription factor. In the cytoplasm the receptor sheds its heat shock proteins, dimerizes and moves to the nucleus, where it binds androgen response elements and changes gene expression. In tissues with 5-alpha-reductase, such as prostate, skin and hair follicles, testosterone becomes dihydrotestosterone, which binds the receptor more strongly. Aromatase converts some testosterone to estradiol, which acts on estrogen receptors in bone and brain. These signals drive muscle protein synthesis, red cell production, libido and male secondary sex traits. Testosterone also feeds back on the hypothalamus and pituitary, lowering LH and FSH.", "body": [["Nose", "Local irritation, runny nose, nosebleeds and scabbing can occur where the gel sits."], ["Muscle", "Androgen signaling builds lean mass and strength."], ["Bone marrow", "Red cell production rises, raising hematocrit."], ["Prostate", "Dihydrotestosterone can raise PSA and worsen urinary symptoms of prostate enlargement."], ["Brain", "Libido and energy may improve as levels return to normal."], ["Testes", "LH and FSH suppression can reduce sperm production, though short daily peaks may suppress less than long-acting forms."]], "journey": "A pump places a measured amount of gel inside each nostril. Testosterone crosses the nasal mucosa into the blood, peaking in about 40 minutes, then falls back toward baseline within a few hours. That short profile is why dosing is three times daily. In the blood about 98% is bound to SHBG and albumin. The liver oxidizes and conjugates testosterone, and some is converted to dihydrotestosterone and estradiol. Conjugated metabolites leave mostly in urine. Nose blowing or sniffing right after a dose can reduce absorption.", "sidefx": "General testosterone effects follow from androgen receptor activation: higher hematocrit from marrow stimulation, with clot risk; prostate growth and higher PSA from dihydrotestosterone; fluid retention and higher blood pressure; worsening sleep apnea; and possible misuse. Nasal effects such as runny nose, nosebleeds, scabbing and altered smell come from the gel sitting on delicate nasal tissue. Because no gel is left on the skin, transfer to children is not a concern the way it is with topical gels. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Natesto's short-acting peaks mimic the body's own hormone rhythm more than long-acting injections, and studies have explored whether this better preserves sperm production.", "src": ["Natesto (testosterone nasal gel) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "danazol": {"origin": "Danazol was synthesized in the early 1960s by chemists at Sterling-Winthrop Research Institute in New York, who were modifying ethisterone, an early synthetic steroid. They fused an isoxazole ring onto the steroid and got a compound that strongly suppressed the pituitary's gonadotropins while having only mild androgen effects. The FDA approved it as Danocrine in 1971 for endometriosis, because it created a low-estrogen state that shrank endometrial implants. In 1976 Jeffrey Gelfand and colleagues at the NIH reported that danazol prevented attacks in hereditary angioedema by raising levels of C1 esterase inhibitor. The Danocrine brand was later discontinued, but generic capsules remain.", "timeline": [["1963", "Sterling-Winthrop chemists report danazol, an isoxazole derivative of ethisterone."], ["1971", "FDA approves Danocrine for endometriosis."], ["1976", "Gelfand and colleagues show danazol prevents hereditary angioedema attacks."]], "cell": "Danazol acts at several points. At the hypothalamus and pituitary it blunts the mid-cycle surge of LH and FSH. In the ovary it directly inhibits several steroid-making enzymes, lowering estrogen output. It binds androgen, progesterone and glucocorticoid receptors, and acts as a weak androgen. It also displaces testosterone from SHBG and lowers SHBG production, so free testosterone rises. The combined result is a low-estrogen, no-ovulation state with mild androgen excess, which makes endometrial tissue, both inside and outside the uterus, shrink. In the liver it increases production of C1 esterase inhibitor, the protein missing in hereditary angioedema, and raises complement C4, reducing bradykinin-driven swelling attacks.", "body": [["Endometriosis tissue", "Low estrogen and androgen effects shrink implants and relieve pelvic pain."], ["Ovaries", "Ovulation stops and periods usually stop during treatment."], ["Liver", "More C1 esterase inhibitor is made, reducing hereditary angioedema attacks; liver injury and tumors can also occur."], ["Skin and voice", "Androgen effects cause acne, oily skin, hair growth and voice deepening, which may be permanent."], ["Lipids", "Androgen action on liver lipase lowers HDL cholesterol, and LDL may rise."], ["Blood", "Clotting risk rises, and danazol is also used for some low platelet disorders."]], "journey": "Danazol is taken by mouth and absorbed modestly, with better absorption when taken with fat-containing food. It peaks in about two hours, is highly protein bound and is metabolized extensively in the liver, largely by CYP3A4, into products such as 2-hydroxymethyl danazol and ethisterone. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. Its half-life lengthens with repeated dosing. Danazol inhibits CYP3A4, which raises levels of drugs such as carbamazepine, cyclosporine and some statins.", "sidefx": "Androgen receptor activation and higher free testosterone explain acne, hair growth, weight gain and voice deepening, which can be irreversible. Low estrogen causes hot flashes and vaginal dryness. Liver toxicity, peliosis hepatis (blood-filled liver cysts) and adenomas are linked to its 17-alpha-alkylated structure. Lower HDL comes from androgen effects on hepatic lipase. Thrombosis, including stroke, has been reported. Benign intracranial hypertension can occur. Androgen exposure can masculinize a female fetus, which is why pregnancy is excluded. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Danazol was one of the first drugs shown to correct a genetic deficiency by making the liver produce more of a missing protein, in hereditary angioedema.", "src": ["Danazol capsules FDA label", "Gelfand JA et al. N Engl J Med 1976", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "oxymetholone": {"origin": "Oxymetholone was created at Syntex in the late 1950s, during a burst of research into anabolic steroids that would build tissue with less masculinizing effect than testosterone. Chemists started from dihydrotestosterone and added a methyl group at carbon 17 alpha, which lets the drug survive the liver when swallowed, plus a hydroxymethylene group at carbon 2. Syntex reported it in 1959, and it was introduced in the early 1960s. In the US it was sold as Anadrol and later Anadrol-50. Its approved use is anemia caused by deficient red cell production, such as aplastic anemia and myelofibrosis. It became widely misused by bodybuilders, and anabolic steroids were placed in Schedule III in 1990.", "timeline": [["1959", "Syntex chemists report oxymetholone."], ["1960s", "Oxymetholone is introduced as Anadrol for anemias."], ["1990", "US Anabolic Steroids Control Act places anabolic steroids in Schedule III."]], "cell": "Oxymetholone is a 17-alpha-methylated derivative of dihydrotestosterone that binds and activates the androgen receptor. The activated receptor moves into the nucleus and changes gene expression. In the kidney, androgen signaling increases erythropoietin production. In the bone marrow, androgens also act directly on erythroid progenitor cells and stem cells, making them more responsive to erythropoietin. Together these raise red cell output over months. Androgen receptor activation in muscle increases protein synthesis and nitrogen retention. The 17-alpha-methyl group blocks the liver's normal oxidation of the 17-hydroxyl group, allowing oral use, but this same feature stresses liver cells and impairs bile flow.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Red cell production rises over three to six months in anemias of deficient production."], ["Kidneys", "More erythropoietin is released, adding to the marrow effect."], ["Liver", "Bile flow can slow, causing jaundice, and long use is linked to peliosis hepatis and liver tumors."], ["Muscle", "Protein synthesis and nitrogen retention rise, increasing lean mass."], ["Lipids", "HDL falls and LDL rises, worsening cardiovascular risk."], ["Skin and sex traits", "Acne, hair growth and voice deepening in women; breast growth in men from hormonal imbalance."]], "journey": "Oxymetholone is taken by mouth. The 17-alpha-methyl group protects it from first-pass breakdown, so it reaches the blood intact. Its detailed pharmacokinetics are not well characterized, but its half-life is short, measured in hours. It is metabolized in the liver, and metabolites are excreted in the urine. The blood count response builds slowly over months and fades after stopping.", "sidefx": "The 17-alpha-alkyl structure that allows oral use is also the source of liver toxicity: impaired bile secretion causes cholestatic jaundice, and long use is linked to peliosis hepatis (blood-filled liver cysts) and liver tumors that can bleed, which is the basis of the boxed warning. Androgen action on hepatic lipase lowers HDL and raises LDL. Androgen receptor activation causes virilization in women, acne and early closing of growth plates in children. Fluid retention and edema come from sodium retention. There is no antidote; stopping the drug is the main step.", "fun": "Oxymetholone was one of the first anabolic steroids detected in Olympic drug testing in the 1970s and 1980s.", "src": ["Anadrol-50 (oxymetholone) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "rabeprazole": {"origin": "The proton pump inhibitor story began in Sweden, where Astra scientists found that substituted benzimidazoles block the stomach's acid pump; their omeprazole reached the US in 1989. Other companies soon built their own versions. The Japanese company Eisai developed rabeprazole in the 1980s and 1990s, giving it a pyridine ring that makes it activate faster and over a wider pH range than earlier PPIs. It was approved in Japan in 1997 and by the FDA in 1999 as Aciphex, co-marketed with Janssen. A sprinkle capsule for children followed in 2013.", "timeline": [["1979", "Astra chemists synthesize omeprazole, the first proton pump inhibitor."], ["1989", "Omeprazole, the first PPI, is approved in the US."], ["1997", "Rabeprazole is approved in Japan."], ["1999", "FDA approves Aciphex (rabeprazole)."], ["2013", "FDA approves Aciphex Sprinkle for children."]], "cell": "Rabeprazole is a prodrug and a weak base. It diffuses into gastric parietal cells and becomes trapped and protonated in the very acidic secretory canaliculi. There it rearranges into a reactive sulfenamide, which forms covalent disulfide bonds with cysteine residues on the outside face of the H+/K+ ATPase, the proton pump that swaps potassium for hydrogen ions as the final step of acid secretion. A bound pump is permanently disabled, so acid output only recovers as the cell makes new pumps, which takes about a day or more. Because the drug needs acid to activate and works on active pumps, PPIs work best taken before a meal. Rabeprazole has a higher pKa than other PPIs, so it activates faster and at less acidic pH.", "body": [["Stomach", "Acid secretion falls sharply, letting ulcers and erosive esophagitis heal and easing heartburn."], ["Esophagus", "Less acid reflux reduces inflammation and pain in GERD."], ["H. pylori treatment", "Higher gastric pH makes the antibiotics in eradication regimens work better."], ["Gastrin cells", "Low acid removes feedback on G cells, so gastrin rises and can cause fundic gland polyps."], ["Gut flora", "Less acid lets more swallowed bacteria survive, raising C. difficile and other enteric infection risk."], ["Minerals and vitamins", "Low acid reduces B12 release from food and can lower magnesium with long use."]], "journey": "Rabeprazole is acid sensitive, so it is given as an enteric-coated tablet that dissolves in the small intestine. It is absorbed there, peaking two to five hours after a dose, and is about 96% protein bound. Its plasma half-life is only one to two hours, yet acid suppression lasts more than a day because the pumps it binds are destroyed. Unlike most PPIs, rabeprazole is mainly cleared by non-enzymatic reduction to a thioether, with smaller roles for CYP2C19 and CYP3A4, so genetic differences in CYP2C19 matter less. Metabolites leave mainly in urine.", "sidefx": "Most long-term risks come from low stomach acid. Bacteria survive the stomach more easily, raising C. difficile diarrhea risk. Acid helps free vitamin B12 from food, so deficiency can develop. Long use lowers magnesium absorption in the gut, which can cause cramps, arrhythmias or seizures. Fracture risk may relate to reduced calcium absorption. High gastrin from lost feedback explains fundic gland polyps. Acute interstitial nephritis and cutaneous lupus are immune reactions. Symptom relief can hide stomach cancer. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Because PPIs destroy active pumps, they work best when taken before the first meal of the day, when the most pumps are being switched on.", "src": ["Aciphex (rabeprazole) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "dexlansoprazole": {"origin": "Lansoprazole, the second PPI to reach the US, was developed by the Japanese company Takeda and approved in 1995 as Prevacid. Like most drugs of its era it was a racemic mix of two mirror-image forms. Takeda found that the R-enantiomer is cleared more slowly. It paired that single enantiomer with a new capsule containing two kinds of granules that dissolve at different pH levels in the small intestine, giving two release peaks. The FDA approved it in 2009 as Kapidex. Within about a year it was renamed Dexilant because pharmacists were confusing Kapidex with the cancer drug Casodex and the opioid Kadian.", "timeline": [["1995", "Takeda's lansoprazole is approved in the US as Prevacid."], ["2009", "FDA approves dexlansoprazole as Kapidex."], ["2010", "The brand is renamed Dexilant after name-confusion errors."]], "cell": "Dexlansoprazole is the R-enantiomer of lansoprazole, a substituted benzimidazole prodrug. It accumulates in the acidic secretory canaliculi of parietal cells, where acid converts it into an active sulfenamide. The sulfenamide forms covalent disulfide bonds with cysteine residues of the H+/K+ ATPase, permanently disabling the pump that performs the last step of acid secretion. Acid output returns only as new pumps are made. Because PPIs act on pumps that are active, more drug present over more hours means more pumps caught. The dual delayed-release design keeps drug in the blood longer, covering pumps switched on later in the day.", "body": [["Stomach", "Strong, long-lasting acid suppression heals erosive esophagitis and relieves heartburn."], ["Esophagus", "Less acid reflux allows inflamed esophageal lining to heal."], ["Gastrin cells", "Loss of acid feedback raises gastrin, which can cause benign fundic gland polyps."], ["Gut flora", "Higher pH lets more bacteria survive, adding to C. difficile infection risk."], ["Minerals and vitamins", "Long use can lower magnesium and vitamin B12."]], "journey": "The capsule holds two granule types. One type dissolves at the pH of the upper small intestine and gives a first peak at one to two hours. The other dissolves further down and gives a second peak at four to five hours. Because of this, the capsule can be taken without regard to meals. The drug is highly protein bound and is metabolized in the liver by CYP2C19 (hydroxylation) and CYP3A4 (oxidation to a sulfone). Each molecule has a plasma half-life of only one to two hours, but pump inhibition lasts about a day. Metabolites leave roughly half in urine and half in feces.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow the PPI class and are tied to low acid. Bacteria survive more easily, raising the risk of C. difficile diarrhea. Vitamin B12 is absorbed less well from food. Magnesium absorption falls with long use, which can cause arrhythmias or seizures. Bone fractures have been linked to long-term use. Interstitial nephritis and lupus-like skin reactions are immune responses. High gastrin explains fundic gland polyps. Relief of symptoms can mask gastric cancer. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Dexilant is one of several drugs whose brand name was changed after approval because of look-alike, sound-alike errors in pharmacies.", "src": ["Dexilant (dexlansoprazole) FDA label", "FDA drug safety communication on Kapidex name change", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "nizatidine": {"origin": "Nizatidine is part of the H2 blocker family that began with James Black at Smith, Kline and French in England. In the 1960s and 1970s his team reasoned that histamine must act on a second receptor in the stomach, and they built molecules to block it, leading to cimetidine, approved in the US in 1977. Black later shared the 1988 Nobel Prize. Other companies built improved versions. Eli Lilly developed nizatidine, which uses a thiazole ring instead of cimetidine's imidazole, and it was approved in 1988 as Axid. The brand and the over-the-counter form were later discontinued. In 2020 some nizatidine lots were recalled during a wider investigation of NDMA impurities in H2 blockers.", "timeline": [["1972", "James Black's team describes H2 receptors and the first H2 blocker, burimamide."], ["1977", "Cimetidine is approved in the US."], ["1988", "FDA approves Lilly's nizatidine as Axid; Black wins the Nobel Prize."], ["2020", "Some nizatidine lots are recalled for NDMA impurity."]], "cell": "Nizatidine is a competitive, reversible antagonist of the histamine H2 receptor on the basolateral side of gastric parietal cells. Histamine from nearby enterochromaffin-like cells normally binds H2 receptors, which are Gs-coupled, so adenylyl cyclase makes cAMP. cAMP activates protein kinase A, which moves H+/K+ ATPase pumps to the cell surface and switches them on. By blocking histamine's signal, nizatidine lowers cAMP and pump activity. Because histamine amplifies the effects of gastrin and acetylcholine, blocking it also reduces acid output in response to food. It is especially good at reducing nighttime acid, which is driven mostly by histamine. Unlike cimetidine, it does not meaningfully inhibit cytochrome P450 or bind androgen receptors.", "body": [["Stomach", "Acid volume and concentration fall, helping duodenal and gastric ulcers heal."], ["Esophagus", "Less acid reflux reduces heartburn and esophagitis."], ["Night acid", "Blocking histamine is particularly effective against nocturnal acid secretion."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys clear most of the drug unchanged, so kidney disease raises levels."], ["Brain", "Rarely confusion occurs, mostly in older adults or people with kidney impairment."]], "journey": "Nizatidine is taken by mouth and is absorbed well, with bioavailability above 70%, peaking within about three hours. It is only about 35% protein bound. The liver makes small amounts of metabolites such as N2-monodesmethylnizatidine, but about 60% of a dose leaves the body unchanged in the urine, cleared by filtration and tubular secretion. Its half-life is one to two hours, and acid suppression lasts up to about 12 hours. Kidney function guides how often it can be given.", "sidefx": "Nizatidine is generally well tolerated because H2 receptors outside the stomach play limited roles. Headache and dizziness are common, minor effects. In people with poor kidney function, levels build up and confusion can occur. Rare liver injury has been reported. Because it does not inhibit cytochrome P450 or bind androgen receptors, it lacks cimetidine's drug interactions and gynecomastia. Relief of symptoms can mask gastric cancer. It can cause a false positive urine protein dipstick test. There is no antidote.", "fun": "James Black also invented propranolol, so the same scientist created both the first beta blocker and the first H2 blocker.", "src": ["Axid (nizatidine) FDA label", "Black JW et al. Nature 1972", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "trimethobenzamide": {"origin": "Trimethobenzamide is an older antiemetic introduced by Hoffmann-La Roche as Tigan in the late 1950s, a period when many new drugs for nausea and vomiting came from chemistry related to antihistamines and phenothiazines. It was designed as a benzamide with a trimethoxy group, and it reduces nausea without the strong sedation of many older drugs. Its exact mechanism has never been fully defined. In 2007 the FDA ordered unapproved trimethobenzamide suppositories off the market because their effectiveness had never been shown. Capsules and the intramuscular injection remain available.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Hoffmann-La Roche introduces trimethobenzamide as Tigan."], ["2007", "FDA orders unapproved trimethobenzamide suppositories removed from the market."]], "cell": "The mechanism of trimethobenzamide is not fully understood. It is thought to act at the chemoreceptor trigger zone in the area postrema of the medulla. This region lies outside the blood-brain barrier and samples the blood for toxins and drugs. Its neurons carry dopamine D2 receptors, and stimulation of these receptors sends signals to the vomiting center in the nucleus tractus solitarius. Trimethobenzamide is believed to block these D2 receptors, damping the signal that triggers nausea and vomiting. It has weak antihistamine activity and does not meaningfully block 5-HT3 receptors, which is why it is less effective against chemotherapy-induced vomiting.", "body": [["Chemoreceptor trigger zone", "Reduced dopamine signaling lowers the drive to vomit from drugs, toxins and gastroenteritis."], ["Brain", "Drowsiness and dizziness occur, adding to other sedatives and alcohol."], ["Movement control", "D2 blockade in the basal ganglia can cause extrapyramidal reactions such as muscle spasms, especially in children."], ["Blood pressure", "The injection can cause a drop in blood pressure."], ["Muscle", "Injection into muscle can be painful at the site."]], "journey": "Trimethobenzamide is given by mouth as capsules or by deep intramuscular injection. Oral doses begin working in about 10 to 40 minutes and IM doses in about 15 to 35 minutes. Effects last several hours. The liver oxidizes it mainly to trimethobenzamide N-oxide, and roughly 30% to 50% of a dose leaves unchanged in the urine. The half-life is about 7 to 9 hours. Older adults and people with kidney disease clear it more slowly.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness comes from its central effects. Blocking D2 receptors in movement pathways can cause extrapyramidal symptoms such as dystonia and opisthotonos, and seizures and coma have been reported, particularly in children with fever or viral illness. Because vomiting can be a sign of Reye syndrome or encephalopathy, controlling it can delay diagnosis. Intramuscular injection can drop blood pressure and causes local pain. Acute dystonic reactions from D2 blockers are usually treated with diphenhydramine or benztropine; there is no specific antidote.", "fun": "The 2007 FDA action removed trimethobenzamide suppositories that had been sold for years without proof that they worked in that form.", "src": ["Tigan (trimethobenzamide) FDA label", "FDA news release on unapproved trimethobenzamide suppositories, 2007"]}, "dolasetron": {"origin": "In the 1980s researchers learned that chemotherapy damages enterochromaffin cells in the gut lining, which release serotonin that fires 5-HT3 receptors on vagal nerves and triggers vomiting. Blocking these receptors transformed cancer care, starting with ondansetron in 1991. Dolasetron was developed by Merrell Dow, which became part of Hoechst Marion Roussel, and approved in 1997 as Anzemet in tablet and injection forms. In 2010 the FDA said the injection should no longer be used for chemotherapy nausea because it caused dose-dependent QT prolongation. The tablets remain available.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Serotonin release from gut cells is linked to chemotherapy-induced vomiting."], ["1991", "Ondansetron, the first 5-HT3 antagonist, is approved in the US."], ["1997", "FDA approves Anzemet (dolasetron)."], ["2010", "FDA states the injection should no longer be used for chemotherapy nausea because of QT risk."]], "cell": "Dolasetron is a prodrug. Carbonyl reductase rapidly converts it to hydrodolasetron, the active form. Hydrodolasetron competitively blocks 5-HT3 receptors, which are ligand-gated cation channels. They sit on vagal afferent nerve endings in the gut wall and on neurons in the area postrema and nucleus tractus solitarius. When chemotherapy damages enterochromaffin cells, they release serotonin, which opens these channels, depolarizes the nerves and sends an emetic signal to the brainstem. Blocking the channels stops that signal, which is most effective for acute vomiting in the first day. Hydrodolasetron also blocks cardiac sodium channels, which slows conduction, and it affects ventricular repolarization, so the PR, QRS and QT intervals all lengthen in a dose-dependent way.", "body": [["Gut vagal nerves", "Blocking serotonin's effect on vagal endings stops the main signal for acute chemotherapy vomiting."], ["Brainstem", "Blocking 5-HT3 receptors in the area postrema and solitary nucleus reduces nausea."], ["Heart", "Cardiac ion channel effects prolong PR, QRS and QT, raising heart block and torsades risk."], ["Colon", "Reduced 5-HT3 signaling slows bowel movement and can cause constipation, though diarrhea is also reported."], ["Head", "Headache is common, possibly from serotonin effects on cranial vessels."]], "journey": "Dolasetron tablets are absorbed quickly, and the parent drug is almost undetectable because carbonyl reductase converts it so fast. Hydrodolasetron peaks in about an hour. It is then metabolized by CYP2D6 and CYP3A4 and partly excreted unchanged. About two thirds leaves in urine and one third in feces. Its half-life is about eight hours. Drugs that inhibit these enzymes or that also prolong conduction add to cardiac risk.", "sidefx": "The main safety issue comes from off-target ion channel blockade in the heart. Sodium channel block slows conduction, causing PR and QRS widening and possible AV block, and delayed repolarization prolongs QT, raising torsades risk. Low potassium or magnesium makes this worse, which is why electrolytes are corrected first. Headache, fatigue and dizziness are common. Combining it with other serotonergic drugs can contribute to serotonin syndrome. There is no antidote; cardiac effects are managed supportively.", "fun": "Serotonin is far more abundant in the gut than in the brain; most of the body's serotonin is made by enterochromaffin cells in the intestinal lining.", "src": ["Anzemet (dolasetron) FDA label", "FDA drug safety communication on Anzemet injection, 2010", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "rolapitant": {"origin": "Substance P was discovered in 1931 by Ulf von Euler and John Gaddum, who found it in extracts of horse brain and gut. Decades later researchers showed that substance P acting on NK1 receptors in the brainstem helps drive the delayed vomiting that comes days after chemotherapy. Aprepitant, the first NK1 antagonist, was approved in 2003. Rolapitant was discovered at Schering-Plough and later developed by Tesaro. The FDA approved the tablets as Varubi in 2015. An intravenous emulsion followed in 2017, but its distribution was halted in 2018 after reports of anaphylaxis, leaving only the tablets.", "timeline": [["1931", "Von Euler and Gaddum discover substance P."], ["2003", "Aprepitant becomes the first FDA-approved NK1 antagonist."], ["2015", "FDA approves Varubi (rolapitant) tablets."], ["2018", "Distribution of the IV emulsion is halted after anaphylaxis reports."]], "cell": "Rolapitant is a selective, competitive antagonist of the neurokinin-1 receptor, the main receptor for substance P. NK1 receptors are Gq-coupled and are dense in the nucleus tractus solitarius and area postrema, the brainstem centers that coordinate vomiting. After chemotherapy, substance P release in these regions sustains nausea and vomiting, especially in the delayed phase from about day 2 to day 5. Rolapitant crosses into the brain and blocks NK1 receptors, preventing substance P from activating phospholipase C and calcium signaling in these neurons. It works with 5-HT3 antagonists, which cover the acute phase, and dexamethasone. Unlike aprepitant, it does not inhibit or induce CYP3A4, but it inhibits CYP2D6 for weeks.", "body": [["Brainstem", "Blocking NK1 receptors reduces the delayed nausea and vomiting that occurs days after chemotherapy."], ["Gut", "Hiccups, abdominal pain and decreased appetite are reported."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia is reported in trials, largely reflecting the chemotherapy it is given with."], ["Drug handling", "Long-lasting CYP2D6 inhibition raises levels of drugs such as thioridazine and pimozide."]], "journey": "Rolapitant is taken by mouth before chemotherapy and absorbed slowly, peaking around four hours. It is about 99.8% bound to plasma proteins and spreads widely, including into the brain. The liver metabolizes it through CYP3A4 to an active metabolite, M19. Its half-life is very long, about a week, so a single dose covers the whole delayed phase. It is excreted mainly through bile into the feces. Its inhibition of CYP2D6 lasts at least 28 days after one dose.", "sidefx": "Most of rolapitant's important problems are interactions. It inhibits CYP2D6 for weeks, so drugs cleared by that enzyme, such as thioridazine and pimozide, can build up and prolong QT. It inhibits the transporters BCRP and P-gp, raising levels of drugs such as methotrexate, irinotecan, topotecan and digoxin. Hiccups and dizziness may reflect brainstem NK1 effects. The IV emulsion caused anaphylaxis, likely related to its formulation, which led to its removal. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Because rolapitant does not affect CYP3A4, dexamethasone does not need the dose adjustment that is used with aprepitant.", "src": ["Varubi (rolapitant) FDA label", "von Euler US, Gaddum JH. J Physiol 1931", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "plecanatide": {"origin": "Plecanatide's story begins with traveler's diarrhea. Certain E. coli strains make a heat-stable toxin that binds guanylate cyclase-C on intestinal cells and floods the gut with fluid. In 1992 and 1993 researchers discovered the body's own hormones for that receptor: guanylin, and uroguanylin, which was first found in urine. Scientists realized that a controlled version of this signal could treat constipation. Linaclotide, based on the bacterial toxin, came first in 2012. Synergy Pharmaceuticals designed plecanatide as a near copy of human uroguanylin with one amino acid change. The FDA approved it as Trulance in 2017 for chronic idiopathic constipation and in 2018 for IBS with constipation.", "timeline": [["1992", "Guanylin is discovered as a natural ligand of guanylate cyclase-C; uroguanylin follows in 1993."], ["2012", "Linaclotide becomes the first GC-C agonist approved in the US."], ["2017", "FDA approves Trulance (plecanatide) for chronic idiopathic constipation."], ["2018", "Trulance is approved for IBS with constipation."]], "cell": "Plecanatide is a 16 amino acid peptide that differs from uroguanylin by one amino acid. It binds guanylate cyclase-C, a receptor enzyme on the luminal surface of intestinal epithelial cells. Like uroguanylin, it binds best at the slightly acidic pH of the proximal small intestine. Binding activates the enzyme, which converts GTP to cyclic GMP. Rising cGMP activates protein kinase G II, which phosphorylates and opens the CFTR chloride channel. Chloride and bicarbonate flow into the lumen, sodium and water follow, and NHE3-mediated sodium absorption is inhibited. The extra fluid softens stool and speeds transit. Some cGMP leaves the cell and is thought to quiet nearby pain nerve fibers, which may ease abdominal pain.", "body": [["Small intestine", "More chloride and water secretion softens the stool and makes it easier to pass."], ["Colon", "Faster transit means more frequent bowel movements and less straining."], ["Pain nerves", "Extracellular cGMP may reduce firing of visceral pain fibers, easing IBS-C pain."], ["Body fluids", "Severe diarrhea can lead to dehydration, a particular danger in young children."]], "journey": "Plecanatide is taken by mouth and acts on the inner surface of the gut. It is barely absorbed, so blood levels are usually unmeasurable. In the gut it is trimmed to an active metabolite and then broken down by digestive enzymes into amino acids. Nothing meaningful reaches the bloodstream, so liver and kidney function do not change its effects. Bowel movements often increase within a day.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea is the main side effect and is simply the drug's mechanism going too far: too much CFTR-driven chloride and water secretion. It can be severe and cause dehydration. In juvenile mice, GC-C agonists caused deaths from dehydration, likely because young animals have more GC-C receptors, which is the basis of the boxed warning for children. Gas and bloating reflect changes in gut contents. Because it is not absorbed, systemic side effects are minimal. There is no antidote; stopping the drug and rehydration manage diarrhea.", "fun": "The same receptor plecanatide uses is the target of the E. coli heat-stable toxin, so the drug is a tamed version of a traveler's diarrhea signal.", "src": ["Trulance (plecanatide) FDA label", "Hamra FK et al. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA 1993", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "tenapanor": {"origin": "Tenapanor was designed by the California company Ardelyx, which set out to make drugs that work on the gut lining without entering the bloodstream. It targeted NHE3, the main transporter that pulls sodium out of the intestine. Blocking it keeps sodium and water in the gut, which softens stool. The team then found an unexpected second effect: blocking NHE3 also tightens the junctions between gut cells, so less phosphate slips through. This gave the drug two uses. The FDA approved it as Ibsrela for IBS with constipation in 2019 and as Xphozah to lower phosphate in adults on dialysis in 2023.", "timeline": [["2010s", "Ardelyx develops tenapanor as a minimally absorbed NHE3 inhibitor."], ["2019", "FDA approves Ibsrela for IBS with constipation."], ["2023", "FDA approves Xphozah to reduce serum phosphorus in adults on dialysis."]], "cell": "Tenapanor inhibits the sodium/hydrogen exchanger isoform 3 (NHE3, gene SLC9A3), located on the apical membrane of enterocytes in the small intestine and colon. NHE3 normally swaps luminal sodium for intracellular hydrogen ions, which is a major route for sodium absorption, with water following. Blocking it leaves more sodium in the lumen, so more water stays there too, which softens stool and speeds transit. The resulting slight acidification inside the cells changes tight junction proteins, which reduces paracellular permeability. Since most dietary phosphate is absorbed through this paracellular route, phosphate absorption falls. Animal studies also suggest it lowers visceral pain sensitivity.", "body": [["Small intestine", "More sodium and water stay in the gut lumen, softening stool."], ["Colon", "Faster transit and softer stools relieve constipation and bloating in IBS-C."], ["Pain nerves", "Animal data suggest reduced visceral hypersensitivity, which may ease abdominal pain."], ["Phosphate balance", "Tighter cell junctions reduce phosphate absorption, lowering serum phosphorus in dialysis patients."], ["Body fluids", "Diarrhea can lead to dehydration, especially in young children."]], "journey": "Tenapanor is taken by mouth before meals and acts on the luminal surface of the gut. Very little is absorbed, so blood levels are usually below detection. The small amount that is absorbed is metabolized by CYP3A4. Almost all of the drug passes out in the feces. Because it stays in the gut, kidney and liver function have little effect on it, which suits patients on dialysis.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea is the expected result of keeping sodium and water in the bowel and can be severe, leading to dehydration. Juvenile rats given tenapanor died of dehydration, which is the reason for the boxed warning in children. Abdominal distension and gas reflect more fluid in the gut. Because almost none enters the blood, systemic side effects are minimal. There is no antidote; stopping the drug and rehydration manage diarrhea.", "fun": "Tenapanor is one of the first phosphate-lowering drugs that works by blocking an absorption pathway rather than binding phosphate like a sponge.", "src": ["Ibsrela (tenapanor) FDA label", "Xphozah (tenapanor) FDA label"]}, "naldemedine": {"origin": "Opioids slow the gut by acting on mu receptors in the enteric nervous system, and constipation is one of their most common and lasting side effects. Classic antagonists like naloxone reverse that, but they also reverse pain relief. The solution was to build antagonists that cannot enter the brain, a class called peripherally acting mu-opioid receptor antagonists (PAMORAs). The Japanese company Shionogi modified naltrexone, adding a large side chain that makes the molecule bigger and more polar and a substrate for P-glycoprotein efflux. The FDA approved naldemedine as Symproic in 2017 for opioid-induced constipation in adults with chronic non-cancer pain.", "timeline": [["2008", "Methylnaltrexone becomes the first US-approved PAMORA."], ["2014", "Naloxegol is approved as an oral PAMORA."], ["2017", "FDA approves Symproic (naldemedine) from Shionogi."]], "cell": "Naldemedine is a derivative of naltrexone that antagonizes mu, delta and kappa opioid receptors. In the gut wall, opioid receptors on enteric neurons are Gi-coupled; when activated by opioids, they reduce acetylcholine release, inhibit coordinated peristalsis, increase non-propulsive contractions and reduce fluid secretion, leading to hard stools and slow transit. Naldemedine binds these receptors and blocks the opioid, restoring normal neural control of motility and secretion. Its added side chain raises its molecular weight and polar surface area, and it is pumped out by P-glycoprotein at the blood-brain barrier. As a result very little reaches the central nervous system, so pain relief from the opioid is preserved.", "body": [["Intestine", "Restored peristalsis and secretion increase bowel movement frequency in people on opioids."], ["Brain", "Little drug enters, so pain control from opioids is maintained."], ["Gut wall", "In people with weakened bowel walls, sudden return of motility may contribute to perforation."], ["Withdrawal", "Some people develop opioid withdrawal symptoms such as sweating, chills and abdominal cramps."]], "journey": "Naldemedine is taken by mouth once daily and absorbed quickly, peaking in under an hour. It is about 93% protein bound. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP3A4 to nor-naldemedine, with some glucuronidation. Its half-life is about 11 hours. About half leaves in urine and about a third in feces. P-glycoprotein keeps it out of the brain. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise its levels.", "sidefx": "Abdominal pain, diarrhea and nausea come from the sudden return of gut activity once opioid braking is removed. Rarely, the bowel can perforate, especially when the wall is already weak from ulcers, diverticular disease, cancer or Ogilvie syndrome. Some people, especially those whose blood-brain barrier is disrupted, develop withdrawal symptoms because a little drug reaches central opioid receptors. In pregnancy it may cross the placenta and cause fetal withdrawal. There is no antidote; stopping it reverses effects.", "fun": "Naldemedine is a chemical cousin of naltrexone, but the extra side chain keeps it mostly in the gut rather than the brain.", "src": ["Symproic (naldemedine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "eluxadoline": {"origin": "Opioid drugs have been used against diarrhea for centuries, from opium tinctures to loperamide. Eluxadoline was designed to act more finely on the gut's opioid receptors. It was discovered at Janssen, part of Johnson and Johnson, as a mixed ligand: an agonist at mu and kappa receptors and an antagonist at delta receptors. Development was taken over by Furiex Pharmaceuticals, which was bought by Forest Laboratories, later part of Allergan and AbbVie. The FDA approved it as Viberzi in 2015 for IBS with diarrhea in adults. In 2017 the FDA warned of serious pancreatitis, mostly in patients without a gallbladder, which then became a contraindication.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Janssen scientists discover eluxadoline."], ["2015", "FDA approves Viberzi (eluxadoline) for IBS with diarrhea."], ["2017", "FDA warns of serious pancreatitis in patients without a gallbladder."]], "cell": "Eluxadoline acts mainly in the enteric nervous system. At mu-opioid receptors, which are Gi-coupled, it reduces acetylcholine release from myenteric neurons, slowing propulsive contractions, and reduces secretion from submucosal neurons. Kappa receptor activation adds to reduced pain signaling from the gut. At delta-opioid receptors it acts as an antagonist; delta blockade is thought to limit the strong constipating effect of pure mu agonism and may enhance analgesia. Because oral absorption is low, most of the drug stays in the gut lumen and wall. Mu agonism also increases tone in the sphincter of Oddi, which explains the pancreatitis risk.", "body": [["Intestine", "Slower transit and less secretion firm the stool and reduce urgency in IBS-D."], ["Visceral nerves", "Combined mu and kappa effects reduce abdominal pain."], ["Sphincter of Oddi", "Increased sphincter tone can block bile and pancreatic flow, causing spasm and pancreatitis."], ["Brain", "Little drug enters, but it has low abuse potential and is Schedule IV."], ["Colon", "Constipation can occur and occasionally becomes severe."]], "journey": "Eluxadoline is taken by mouth with food. Its oral bioavailability is low, so most of the dose stays in the gut and acts locally, then passes out in the feces, with under 1% in urine. The small absorbed portion peaks in about 1.5 hours and has a half-life of about 4 to 6 hours. It is a substrate of the liver uptake transporter OATP1B1, so inhibitors of that transporter, such as cyclosporine, raise blood levels.", "sidefx": "Constipation is the direct result of mu-opioid slowing of the bowel and can occasionally lead to obstruction. Nausea and abdominal pain are common. The most serious risk is pancreatitis, linked to sphincter of Oddi spasm from mu agonism. People without a gallbladder lack a pressure reservoir for bile, so sphincter spasm affects them more, and most fatal cases occurred in this group. Alcohol use adds to pancreatitis risk. Hypersensitivity reactions including anaphylaxis have been reported. Naloxone can reverse opioid effects in overdose.", "fun": "Eluxadoline was designed so that its delta receptor blockade would soften the constipating punch of its mu receptor activity, an unusual mixed design.", "src": ["Viberzi (eluxadoline) FDA label", "FDA drug safety communication 2017", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "alosetron": {"origin": "After 5-HT3 antagonists proved their value for chemotherapy nausea, researchers noticed they also slowed the colon. Glaxo Wellcome developed alosetron, a potent 5-HT3 antagonist, specifically for women with diarrhea-predominant IBS. The FDA approved it as Lotronex in February 2000. Within months, reports of ischemic colitis and severe constipation, some fatal, led to its withdrawal in November 2000. Patient groups argued that it had helped people whose lives were ruled by diarrhea. In 2002 the FDA allowed it back for women with severe IBS-D under a restricted program with a boxed warning. It is one of the few drugs to return after a safety withdrawal.", "timeline": [["2000", "FDA approves Lotronex in February."], ["2000", "Lotronex is withdrawn in November after ischemic colitis reports."], ["2002", "Alosetron returns under a restricted program for women with severe IBS-D."]], "cell": "Alosetron is a potent, selective antagonist of 5-HT3 receptors, ligand-gated cation channels on enteric neurons and on visceral sensory afferents. In the gut, serotonin from enterochromaffin cells activates 5-HT3 receptors to trigger peristaltic reflexes, secretion and pain signaling. Blocking these channels slows colonic transit, reduces fluid secretion and blunts the gut's sensitivity to stretching. The result is firmer stools, less urgency and less abdominal pain in IBS-D. The same slowing, when excessive, can cause severe constipation, and changes in colonic blood flow are thought to contribute to ischemic colitis, though that mechanism is not fully established.", "body": [["Colon", "Slower transit and less secretion make stools firmer and less frequent."], ["Visceral sensory nerves", "Reduced pain signaling eases abdominal discomfort and urgency."], ["Colon blood supply", "Rarely, ischemic colitis develops, presenting with rectal bleeding and sudden abdominal pain."], ["Bowel function", "Constipation is common and can become severe with obstruction or perforation."]], "journey": "Alosetron is taken by mouth and absorbed quickly, with a peak at about one hour and bioavailability around 50% to 60%. It is about 82% protein bound. The liver metabolizes it extensively, mainly by CYP1A2 with contributions from CYP3A4 and CYP2C9, so fluvoxamine, a strong CYP1A2 inhibitor, raises levels sharply. Its plasma half-life is about 1.5 hours, though effects on the gut last longer. Metabolites leave mostly in urine.", "sidefx": "Constipation is the expected effect of strong 5-HT3 blockade on colonic motility, and when severe it can cause impaction, obstruction or perforation. Ischemic colitis is the most feared effect; its exact cause is unclear, but it appears linked to the drug's action on the colon and possibly on local blood flow. These risks are the basis of the boxed warning and the restriction to severe IBS-D in women. Rectal bleeding, bloody diarrhea or new abdominal pain are signs that lead to stopping the drug. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Alosetron's 2002 return was influenced by public testimony from patients, an unusual example of patient advocacy reversing a drug withdrawal.", "src": ["Lotronex (alosetron) FDA label", "FDA public advisory committee records 2002", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "obeticholic-acid": {"origin": "In 1999 three research groups found that bile acids are hormones as well as detergents, acting on the nuclear farnesoid X receptor (FXR). The most active natural bile acid at FXR was chenodeoxycholic acid. At the University of Perugia in Italy, chemist Roberto Pellicciari and colleagues added an ethyl group to it, making a compound about 100 times more potent, which they reported in 2002. Intercept Pharmaceuticals developed it as obeticholic acid. The FDA granted accelerated approval as Ocaliva in 2016 for primary biliary cholangitis. Reports of liver failure in patients with advanced cirrhosis led to new restrictions in 2021, and the drug was withdrawn from the US market in 2025.", "timeline": [["1999", "Bile acids are identified as natural ligands of FXR."], ["2002", "Pellicciari's group reports 6-ethyl chenodeoxycholic acid (obeticholic acid)."], ["2016", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Ocaliva for primary biliary cholangitis."], ["2021", "Contraindication added for PBC with advanced cirrhosis."], ["2025", "Ocaliva is withdrawn from the US market."]], "cell": "Obeticholic acid is a semisynthetic bile acid and a potent agonist of the farnesoid X receptor, a nuclear receptor found in liver and intestinal cells. When bound, FXR pairs with the retinoid X receptor and changes gene transcription. In hepatocytes it induces small heterodimer partner (SHP), which represses CYP7A1, the rate-limiting enzyme for making bile acids from cholesterol. In the intestine it induces FGF19, a hormone that travels to the liver and also represses CYP7A1. FXR activation increases expression of the bile salt export pump (BSEP) and other transporters that move bile acids out of liver cells. Less bile acid inside hepatocytes means less toxic injury in cholestatic disease, reflected by falling alkaline phosphatase.", "body": [["Liver cells", "Less bile acid synthesis and more export lower toxic bile acid levels inside hepatocytes."], ["Bile ducts", "Reduced cholestatic stress lowers alkaline phosphatase and bilirubin, markers of disease activity."], ["Skin", "Itching often worsens, a common and sometimes dose-limiting effect."], ["Lipids", "HDL cholesterol falls through FXR effects on lipid metabolism."], ["Liver in cirrhosis", "In advanced disease, the liver can decompensate, with ascites, jaundice and bleeding."]], "journey": "Obeticholic acid is taken by mouth and absorbed in the gut. In the liver it is conjugated with glycine or taurine, just like natural bile acids, and secreted into bile. These conjugates are reabsorbed in the ileum and returned to the liver, repeating the enterohepatic cycle many times. That recycling gives it a long effective half-life of about four days. It is mainly excreted in feces, with very little in urine. Liver impairment greatly increases exposure, a key reason for its liver safety problems.", "sidefx": "Pruritus is common; it is linked to FXR activation and changes in bile acid signaling, though the precise pathway is not fully understood. Lower HDL reflects FXR effects on cholesterol transport. The most serious harm was hepatic decompensation and liver failure in patients with cirrhosis, where impaired clearance raised drug exposure and the liver could not tolerate it. That led to a boxed warning and ultimately to withdrawal. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The parent bile acid, chenodeoxycholic acid, was once used to dissolve gallstones; adding a single ethyl group turned it into a far stronger FXR activator.", "src": ["Ocaliva (obeticholic acid) FDA label", "Pellicciari R et al. J Med Chem 2002", "Makishima M et al. Science 1999"]}, "elafibranor": {"origin": "Peroxisome proliferator-activated receptors (PPARs) are nuclear receptors first described in the early 1990s. The PPAR-alpha agonists called fibrates had long been used for high triglycerides, and doctors noticed that some patients with primary biliary cholangitis improved when given them. The French company Genfit developed elafibranor as a dual PPAR-alpha and PPAR-delta agonist. It was first tested for fatty liver disease (NASH), but a large trial reported in 2020 did not meet its goal. Partnered with Ipsen, elafibranor was then studied in primary biliary cholangitis, where it lowered alkaline phosphatase. The FDA granted accelerated approval as Iqirvo in 2024 for PBC in patients who do not respond enough to or cannot tolerate ursodiol.", "timeline": [["1990", "PPAR-alpha is discovered as the receptor for peroxisome proliferators."], ["2020", "Elafibranor fails a major phase 3 trial in NASH."], ["2024", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Iqirvo (elafibranor) for primary biliary cholangitis."]], "cell": "Elafibranor and its active metabolite GFT1007 bind and activate PPAR-alpha and PPAR-delta, nuclear receptors that pair with the retinoid X receptor and bind PPAR response elements on DNA. In hepatocytes, activation represses CYP7A1, the rate-limiting enzyme of bile acid synthesis, so less bile acid is made. PPAR-alpha activation also increases bile acid detoxifying enzymes and canalicular transporters such as MDR3, which adds phospholipid to bile and makes it less damaging to bile duct cells. PPAR-delta and PPAR-alpha signaling dampen inflammatory pathways in liver cells and reduce fibrosis-promoting signals. The net effect is less cholestatic injury, shown by falling alkaline phosphatase.", "body": [["Liver", "Lower bile acid synthesis and better bile composition reduce injury to bile ducts."], ["Bile ducts", "Alkaline phosphatase falls, the main marker used for approval."], ["Muscle", "Muscle pain and CK elevation can occur, more so with statins, reflecting PPAR effects on muscle."], ["Bone", "Fractures have been reported in treated patients."], ["Gut", "Abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are common."], ["Hormonal contraception", "It lowers exposure to hormonal contraceptives, reducing their reliability."]], "journey": "Elafibranor is taken by mouth once daily and absorbed quickly, peaking in about 1.25 hours. It is extensively metabolized to the active metabolite GFT1007 and to an inactive acyl glucuronide. Both forms are highly bound to albumin. Elafibranor's half-life is about 70 hours, while GFT1007 lasts about 15 hours. Most of the dose leaves in the feces, with about a fifth in urine. Alkaline phosphatase falls over weeks.", "sidefx": "Muscle pain, myopathy and rarely rhabdomyolysis are linked to PPAR activation in muscle and are more likely with statins, which have similar effects. Liver injury and biliary obstruction warnings reflect that it acts in an already damaged liver. Fractures were seen more in treated patients. Animal studies showed harm to the fetus, so pregnancy is excluded, and it lowers hormonal contraceptive levels. Gut symptoms are common. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Elafibranor's path from a failed fatty liver drug to an approved PBC treatment is a good example of drug repurposing within the same organ.", "src": ["Iqirvo (elafibranor) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "seladelpar": {"origin": "Seladelpar grew from research into PPAR-delta, the least known of the three PPAR nuclear receptors. It was developed by CymaBay Therapeutics, a California company formerly called Metabolex, first for lipid and metabolic disease and then for primary biliary cholangitis. In PBC trials it lowered alkaline phosphatase and also reduced itching, which is a major burden for patients. The FDA granted accelerated approval as Livdelzi in 2024 for PBC in patients who respond poorly to or cannot tolerate ursodiol. Gilead acquired CymaBay that same year.", "timeline": [["1990s", "PPAR-delta is identified as a member of the PPAR family."], ["2024", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Livdelzi (seladelpar) for PBC."], ["2024", "Gilead acquires CymaBay Therapeutics."]], "cell": "Seladelpar is a selective agonist of PPAR-delta, a nuclear receptor that pairs with the retinoid X receptor to control gene transcription. In hepatocytes, PPAR-delta activation induces fibroblast growth factor 21 (FGF21). FGF21 signals through a JNK-dependent pathway to repress CYP7A1, the rate-limiting enzyme that turns cholesterol into bile acids, so bile acid production falls. PPAR-delta is also present on cholangiocytes, Kupffer cells and stellate cells, where activation reduces inflammatory and fibrotic signaling. In trials, levels of IL-31, an itch-related cytokine, fell along with pruritus. Unlike elafibranor, it has little PPAR-alpha activity.", "body": [["Liver", "Lower bile acid synthesis reduces toxic exposure of liver cells and bile ducts."], ["Bile ducts", "Alkaline phosphatase falls, the main marker used for approval."], ["Skin", "Many patients report less itching, linked to lower IL-31 and bile acid levels."], ["Bone", "Fractures have been reported in treated patients."], ["Gut", "Abdominal pain, nausea and bloating are reported."]], "journey": "Seladelpar is taken by mouth once daily and absorbed quickly, peaking in about 1.5 hours. It is more than 99% bound to plasma proteins. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP2C9, with smaller roles for CYP2C8 and CYP3A4, into inactive metabolites. Its half-life is roughly 4 to 7 hours. Most of the dose leaves in urine as metabolites, with about a fifth in feces. Strong CYP2C9 inhibitors and probenecid raise its levels.", "sidefx": "Seladelpar is generally well tolerated. Headache and gut symptoms are common. Fractures have been reported. Liver test abnormalities and biliary obstruction warnings reflect the underlying disease and the fact that it acts in the liver. Unlike PPAR-alpha drugs, it has not been strongly linked to muscle injury, consistent with its selectivity for PPAR-delta. Drug interactions through CYP2C9 and transporters can raise exposure. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Seladelpar is one of the few PBC drugs whose trials showed meaningful relief of itching, rather than worsening it as obeticholic acid often did.", "src": ["Livdelzi (seladelpar) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "budesonide-oral": {"origin": "Budesonide was created in the early 1970s at AB Draco, a Swedish subsidiary of Astra in Lund, as part of a search for corticosteroids that would act strongly where applied but be broken down quickly once absorbed. It was first used as an inhaled asthma drug, Pulmicort, in the 1980s. Researchers then realized that the same rapid liver breakdown could help in the gut: a capsule that releases budesonide in the ileum and colon would treat inflammation there while the liver destroyed most of what was absorbed. Entocort EC was approved in the US in 2001 for Crohn's disease. Uceris extended-release tablets, using a multi-matrix system to spread the drug through the colon, were approved for ulcerative colitis in 2013.", "timeline": [["1970s", "AB Draco in Lund, Sweden, synthesizes budesonide."], ["1980s", "Inhaled budesonide (Pulmicort) is introduced for asthma."], ["2001", "FDA approves Entocort EC for Crohn's disease."], ["2013", "FDA approves Uceris tablets for ulcerative colitis."]], "cell": "Budesonide is a potent glucocorticoid receptor agonist. In epithelial and immune cells of the gut wall it binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor, which sheds its chaperone proteins and moves to the nucleus. There it binds glucocorticoid response elements to switch on anti-inflammatory genes such as annexin A1 and IkB-alpha, and it ties up NF-kB and AP-1 to switch off genes for cytokines like TNF, IL-1 and IL-6, chemokines and adhesion molecules. Fewer inflammatory cells are recruited and the mucosa heals. The key feature is not the receptor action, which is like other steroids, but its fate: CYP3A4 in the gut wall and liver inactivates about 80% to 90% of absorbed drug on first pass, so systemic steroid exposure is much lower than with prednisone.", "body": [["Ileum and right colon", "Released at these sites, it calms Crohn's inflammation where the disease is most common."], ["Whole colon", "The MMX tablet spreads drug along the colon for ulcerative colitis."], ["Liver", "Most absorbed drug is destroyed on first pass, limiting whole-body steroid effects."], ["Adrenal axis", "Some suppression still occurs, so stopping suddenly after long use or switching from systemic steroids can cause withdrawal."], ["Immune system", "Infection risk rises, including serious chickenpox and measles in people who are not immune."], ["Metabolism and skin", "Cushingoid effects such as acne, moon face and bruising are less common than with prednisone but can occur."]], "journey": "Entocort EC capsules contain granules with a pH-sensitive coating that dissolves above pH 5.5 in the ileum, and then the drug is released over time. Uceris tablets resist dissolution until about pH 7 and then release drug through a matrix across the colon. Budesonide is absorbed from the gut lining and carried by the portal vein to the liver, where CYP3A4 breaks down most of it into weakly active metabolites. Systemic bioavailability is only about 10% to 20%. The plasma half-life is two to four hours, and metabolites leave in urine and feces. Grapefruit juice and strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ketoconazole block this breakdown and raise blood levels several fold.", "sidefx": "Because the liver removes most of the drug, systemic steroid effects are milder than with prednisone, but they are not absent. Absorbed steroid can suppress the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis, cause Cushingoid features, raise blood sugar and lower potassium. Immune suppression raises infection risk, including severe varicella and measles. Patients moving from systemic steroids to budesonide can feel withdrawal symptoms as their total steroid exposure falls. Anything that blocks CYP3A4 removes the protective first-pass effect and increases all these risks. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The same budesonide molecule is used as an asthma inhaler, a nasal spray and a gut-targeted capsule; only the delivery system changes where it works.", "src": ["Entocort EC (budesonide) FDA label", "Uceris (budesonide) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "olsalazine": {"origin": "Olsalazine traces back to sulfasalazine, created in the late 1930s and early 1940s by Swedish physician Nanna Svartz, who linked the sulfa antibiotic sulfapyridine to 5-aminosalicylic acid (5-ASA) for rheumatoid arthritis and found it also helped ulcerative colitis. In 1977 Azad Khan, Piris and Truelove in Oxford showed that 5-ASA was the active part and that sulfapyridine mainly caused side effects. That opened the door to delivering 5-ASA without sulfa. The Swedish company Pharmacia joined two 5-ASA molecules by the same azo bond used in sulfasalazine, so colonic bacteria would split the pair. Olsalazine was approved by the FDA in 1990 as Dipentum for keeping ulcerative colitis in remission in people who cannot take sulfasalazine.", "timeline": [["1940s", "Nanna Svartz introduces sulfasalazine."], ["1977", "Azad Khan, Piris and Truelove show 5-ASA is the active part of sulfasalazine."], ["1990", "FDA approves Dipentum (olsalazine) for ulcerative colitis maintenance."]], "cell": "Olsalazine is two molecules of 5-ASA linked by an azo bond. It is poorly absorbed in the small intestine. In the colon, bacterial azoreductase enzymes cut the azo bond and release two molecules of 5-ASA right where they are needed. 5-ASA acts topically on the colonic lining. It inhibits cyclooxygenase and lipoxygenase, reducing prostaglandins and leukotrienes. It scavenges reactive oxygen species made by inflamed tissue. It inhibits activation of NF-kB, which lowers inflammatory cytokine production, and it activates PPAR-gamma, a nuclear receptor that dampens inflammation in colonic epithelial cells. Olsalazine itself also stimulates fluid secretion in the small intestine, which explains its typical watery diarrhea.", "body": [["Colon", "Released 5-ASA calms mucosal inflammation and helps keep ulcerative colitis in remission."], ["Small intestine", "Olsalazine stimulates ileal fluid secretion, causing loose stools or diarrhea in some people."], ["Kidneys", "Rarely, interstitial nephritis or kidney injury develops, so kidney function is checked."], ["Heart", "Rare hypersensitivity myocarditis or pericarditis can occur with 5-ASA drugs."], ["Skin", "Rash and photosensitivity can occur as hypersensitivity reactions to 5-ASA."], ["Gut reaction", "A rare intolerance syndrome with cramping, bloody diarrhea and fever can mimic a colitis flare."]], "journey": "Olsalazine is taken by mouth with food. Only about 2% is absorbed in the small intestine. The rest reaches the colon, where bacteria split it into 5-ASA. Most 5-ASA stays in the colon and passes out in the stool, either unchanged or N-acetylated by colonic cells. The small absorbed portion of 5-ASA is acetylated in the liver and gut wall and excreted in urine. A little absorbed olsalazine is sulfated in the liver into a long-lived metabolite. Plasma olsalazine has a half-life of about an hour.", "sidefx": "Watery diarrhea is the most distinct side effect, caused by olsalazine itself stimulating secretion in the small bowel before it is split. Taking it with food lessens this. Kidney injury such as interstitial nephritis is a recognized 5-ASA effect, thought to be immune or direct tubular toxicity. Hypersensitivity can affect the heart, liver, lungs or skin. A paradoxical acute intolerance syndrome can look like a flare. Because no sulfapyridine is present, sulfa-related effects such as low sperm count and folate problems are avoided. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Olsalazine and sulfasalazine both rely on gut bacteria to cut an azo bond, so the drug is activated by the patient's own microbiome.", "src": ["Dipentum (olsalazine) FDA label", "Azad Khan AK, Piris J, Truelove SC. Lancet 1977", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "mirikizumab": {"origin": "Interleukin-23 was discovered in 2000 by researchers at DNAX in California, who found it was a pair of protein subunits: p40, shared with IL-12, and a unique p19. Later work showed IL-23 keeps Th17 cells active, and those cells drive chronic inflammation in the gut, skin and joints. Early drugs such as ustekinumab blocked p40 and so hit both IL-12 and IL-23. Eli Lilly developed mirikizumab, a humanized antibody that targets only p19, to block IL-23 while leaving IL-12 alone. The FDA approved it as Omvoh in 2023 for moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis, the first IL-23 p19 antibody for that disease, and in 2025 for Crohn's disease.", "timeline": [["2000", "Researchers at DNAX discover IL-23."], ["2009", "Ustekinumab, which blocks the shared p40 subunit, is approved for psoriasis."], ["2023", "FDA approves Omvoh (mirikizumab) for ulcerative colitis."], ["2025", "Omvoh is approved for Crohn's disease."]], "cell": "Mirikizumab is a humanized IgG4 monoclonal antibody that binds the p19 subunit of IL-23. IL-23 is made by dendritic cells and macrophages in inflamed gut tissue. Normally it binds a receptor made of IL-23R and IL-12R-beta1 on Th17 cells and innate lymphoid cells, activating JAK2 and TYK2 and then STAT3. STAT3 keeps Th17 cells alive and producing IL-17A, IL-17F, IL-22 and GM-CSF, which recruit neutrophils and sustain mucosal damage. By binding p19, mirikizumab stops IL-23 from engaging its receptor. Because it does not bind p40, it does not block IL-12, which supports Th1 immunity against some infections. Over weeks the inflammatory Th17 activity in the colon falls and the mucosa heals.", "body": [["Colon", "Mucosal inflammation, bleeding and ulceration decrease, and many patients reach mucosal healing."], ["Bowel urgency", "Urgency, a burdensome symptom in ulcerative colitis, improves in trials."], ["Immune system", "Reduced Th17 activity raises infection risk, including upper respiratory and herpes infections."], ["Liver", "Drug-induced liver injury has been reported, especially during Crohn's induction, so liver tests are monitored."], ["Injection sites", "Local redness and pain can occur after subcutaneous doses."]], "journey": "Treatment starts with intravenous infusions to reach high levels quickly, followed by subcutaneous injections every four weeks for maintenance. After a subcutaneous dose, the antibody is absorbed through the lymphatics and peaks in about five days. Like other IgG antibodies, it is protected from breakdown by the neonatal Fc receptor and is cleared slowly by protein catabolism throughout the body, not by the liver's CYP enzymes or the kidneys. Its half-life is about nine days.", "sidefx": "Blocking IL-23 weakens Th17 defenses at mucosal surfaces, which explains a higher rate of respiratory infections and herpes viral infections. Latent tuberculosis can reactivate, so TB screening is done first. Liver injury has been seen, possibly immune mediated, leading to liver test monitoring. Hypersensitivity reactions including anaphylaxis can occur with any protein drug. Live vaccines are avoided because immune responses may be blunted. There is no antidote; effects fade over weeks after stopping.", "fun": "The p19 subunit was found by searching gene databases for proteins resembling IL-6 and IL-12, a computer search that led to a new drug target.", "src": ["Omvoh (mirikizumab-mrkz) FDA label", "Oppmann B et al. Immunity 2000"]}, "golimumab": {"origin": "Tumor necrosis factor was named in 1975 when Lloyd Old's group found a serum factor that caused tumors to die in mice. It turned out to be a central driver of chronic inflammation, and the first TNF blockers were approved in 1998. Centocor (later Janssen) developed golimumab as a fully human antibody, made using transgenic mice whose immune genes had been replaced with human ones, so that the antibody would look less foreign to the immune system than older mouse-human chimeric antibodies. The FDA approved golimumab as Simponi in 2009 for rheumatoid arthritis, psoriatic arthritis and ankylosing spondylitis, as a once-monthly injection. An intravenous form, Simponi Aria, and approval for ulcerative colitis followed in 2013.", "timeline": [["1975", "Lloyd Old's group describes tumor necrosis factor."], ["1998", "The first TNF inhibitors are approved in the US."], ["2009", "FDA approves Simponi (golimumab)."], ["2013", "Simponi Aria IV form and ulcerative colitis approval follow."]], "cell": "Golimumab is a fully human IgG1 kappa monoclonal antibody that binds TNF-alpha with high affinity. It neutralizes both soluble TNF and the transmembrane form on activated macrophages and T cells. By blocking TNF from binding its receptors, TNFR1 (p55) and TNFR2 (p75), it prevents activation of NF-kB and MAP kinase pathways that drive production of IL-1, IL-6, chemokines, adhesion molecules and matrix metalloproteinases. In joints this lowers synovial inflammation, cartilage breakdown and bone erosion. In the colon it reduces mucosal inflammation. CRP and IL-6 levels fall. TNF is also needed to keep granulomas intact around dormant infections like tuberculosis, which explains why blocking it can let those infections reactivate.", "body": [["Joints", "Less synovial inflammation means less pain, swelling and joint damage in RA, psoriatic arthritis and ankylosing spondylitis."], ["Colon", "Mucosal inflammation in ulcerative colitis decreases."], ["Immune defense", "Weaker granuloma maintenance raises the risk of TB reactivation and serious fungal and bacterial infections."], ["Liver", "Hepatitis B can reactivate in carriers, and liver enzymes can rise."], ["Heart", "Heart failure can worsen, as seen with TNF blockers."], ["Nervous system", "Rare demyelinating disease can develop or worsen."]], "journey": "Golimumab is injected under the skin once a month, or given intravenously as Simponi Aria. After subcutaneous injection it is absorbed through the lymphatics, peaking in two to six days. As an IgG antibody it is protected by the neonatal Fc receptor and cleared slowly by protein catabolism, with a half-life of about two weeks. It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes or cleared by the kidneys. Methotrexate lowers formation of anti-drug antibodies and increases golimumab levels.", "sidefx": "Most serious effects come from losing TNF's role in host defense. Granulomas that wall off tuberculosis and fungi can break down, so latent TB and invasive fungal infections can flare; this is the basis of the boxed warning. Hepatitis B can reactivate. Lymphoma and other cancers are reported with TNF blockers, possibly from reduced tumor surveillance. TNF blockade can worsen heart failure, trigger demyelination and produce autoantibodies with a lupus-like syndrome. Combining with other biologics such as abatacept or anakinra adds infection risk. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Golimumab was among the first therapeutic antibodies made with mice engineered to produce fully human antibodies.", "src": ["Simponi (golimumab) FDA label", "Carswell EA et al. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA 1975", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "teduglutide": {"origin": "Glucagon-like peptide-2 (GLP-2) is cut from the same proglucagon precursor as glucagon and GLP-1, in L cells of the intestine. In 1996 Daniel Drucker and colleagues in Toronto showed that GLP-2 makes the intestinal lining grow, with taller villi and deeper crypts. That suggested a treatment for short bowel syndrome, in which people who have lost much of their intestine cannot absorb enough food and fluid. Natural GLP-2 lasts only minutes because DPP-4 cuts it. NPS Pharmaceuticals developed teduglutide, with one amino acid swap that resists DPP-4. The FDA approved it as Gattex in 2012 for adults with short bowel syndrome who depend on IV nutrition, and for children in 2019.", "timeline": [["1996", "Drucker's group shows GLP-2 is intestinotrophic."], ["2012", "FDA approves Gattex (teduglutide) for adults with short bowel syndrome."], ["2019", "Gattex is approved for children with short bowel syndrome."]], "cell": "Teduglutide is a 33 amino acid analog of GLP-2 in which alanine at position 2 is replaced by glycine. That change protects it from DPP-4, which normally removes the first two amino acids and inactivates GLP-2 within minutes. Teduglutide binds GLP-2 receptors, Gs-coupled receptors found on enteroendocrine cells, subepithelial myofibroblasts and enteric neurons rather than on the absorptive cells themselves. These cells respond by releasing mediators such as IGF-1, nitric oxide and keratinocyte growth factor, which act on nearby epithelium. The result is more crypt cell proliferation and less apoptosis, so villi grow taller and the absorptive surface increases. GLP-2 signaling also slows gastric emptying, reduces gastric acid secretion and increases intestinal blood flow.", "body": [["Small intestine", "Taller villi and deeper crypts increase absorptive surface, so more fluid and nutrients are absorbed."], ["Nutrition needs", "Many patients need less IV nutrition and fluid volume over months."], ["Stomach", "Slower gastric emptying and lower acid secretion give more time for absorption."], ["Colon", "Growth signals can also affect the colon, so polyps are screened for before and during treatment."], ["Gallbladder and pancreas", "Changes in bile flow and pancreatic secretion can cause gallbladder, bile duct or pancreas problems."], ["Fluid balance", "Better fluid absorption can cause overload, especially in people with heart disease."]], "journey": "Teduglutide is injected under the skin once daily. It is absorbed with high bioavailability and peaks three to five hours later. Its half-life is about two hours in healthy people, shorter in short bowel syndrome, but the growth effects on the gut lining build over weeks. Like other peptides it is broken down into small peptides and amino acids, and clearance is mainly through the kidneys, so kidney impairment raises exposure. By improving absorption, it can also increase uptake of other oral drugs.", "sidefx": "Because teduglutide is a growth signal for the gut lining, it could speed growth of existing polyps or cancers, which is why colonoscopy and polyp removal are done before starting. Overgrowth of mucosa and slower transit can contribute to intestinal obstruction, often near a surgical join. Gallbladder, bile duct and pancreatic disease reflect changes in bile and pancreatic flow. Improved fluid absorption can cause fluid overload and heart failure. Better absorption of narrow-margin oral drugs can raise their levels. Abdominal pain, bloating and injection site reactions are common. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Teduglutide differs from natural human GLP-2 by just one amino acid, yet that single change stretches its life in the blood from minutes to hours.", "src": ["Gattex (teduglutide) FDA label", "Drucker DJ et al. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA 1996"]}, "maralixibat": {"origin": "About 95% of bile acids are reabsorbed in the last part of the small intestine by a single transporter, the apical sodium-dependent bile acid transporter (ASBT, also called IBAT). Blocking it was first explored as a way to lower cholesterol, because the liver would have to make new bile acids from cholesterol. Maralixibat was one of those early compounds. Lumena Pharmaceuticals, later part of Shire, and then Mirum Pharmaceuticals repurposed it for children with cholestatic liver diseases, where bile acids build up and cause severe itching. The FDA approved it as Livmarli in 2021 for cholestatic pruritus in Alagille syndrome, a genetic disorder with too few bile ducts, and in 2024 for progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis (PFIC).", "timeline": [["1990s", "IBAT inhibitors are explored as cholesterol-lowering drugs."], ["2018", "Mirum Pharmaceuticals licenses maralixibat to develop it for cholestatic liver disease."], ["2021", "FDA approves Livmarli for cholestatic pruritus in Alagille syndrome."], ["2024", "Livmarli is approved for pruritus in PFIC."]], "cell": "Maralixibat is a minimally absorbed, reversible inhibitor of the ileal bile acid transporter (IBAT, also called ASBT, gene SLC10A2) on the apical membrane of terminal ileal enterocytes. IBAT normally uses the sodium gradient to pull conjugated bile acids into the cell so they can return to the liver through the portal vein, the enterohepatic circulation. Blocking it lets more bile acids stay in the gut and pass into the colon and stool. The liver gets fewer bile acids back, so serum bile acid levels fall. In cholestatic diseases, high bile acids are thought to contribute to itching, and lower serum bile acids correlate with less pruritus. More bile acids reaching the colon stimulate fluid secretion and motility, which explains diarrhea.", "body": [["Ileum", "Bile acid reabsorption is blocked, interrupting the enterohepatic cycle."], ["Liver and blood", "Serum bile acid levels fall, easing the load on a liver with poor bile drainage."], ["Skin", "Severe cholestatic itching often improves, which helps sleep and quality of life."], ["Colon", "Extra bile acids reaching the colon trigger secretion, causing diarrhea and abdominal pain."], ["Vitamin absorption", "Less bile acid recycling can reduce absorption of fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K."]], "journey": "Maralixibat is taken by mouth as a liquid and acts on the inside surface of the ileum. Very little is absorbed, and blood levels are usually below the limit of measurement. It is barely metabolized and leaves the body almost entirely unchanged in the stool. Because it stays in the gut, effects on itching build over weeks as serum bile acids fall.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea and abdominal pain come straight from the mechanism: extra bile acids in the colon act like a laxative by stimulating chloride and water secretion. Vomiting and dehydration can follow. Reduced bile acid recycling means less bile for fat digestion, so fat-soluble vitamins can become deficient, with bleeding risk from low vitamin K and bone fracture risk from low vitamin D. Liver test rises and drug-induced liver injury have been reported, so liver function is monitored. There is no antidote.", "fun": "A drug first studied for lowering cholesterol in adults became a treatment for itching in young children with rare liver diseases.", "src": ["Livmarli (maralixibat) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "odevixibat": {"origin": "Odevixibat was developed by Albireo Pharma, a company with roots in Sweden, specifically for rare cholestatic liver diseases of childhood. In progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis (PFIC), gene defects in bile transport cause bile acids to build up in the liver and blood, leading to intense itching and liver damage. Surgeons had long used biliary diversion, an operation that drains bile out of the body, to relieve this. Albireo's idea was a drug that does something similar by blocking bile acid reabsorption in the gut. The FDA approved odevixibat as Bylvay in 2021 for pruritus in PFIC, the first drug approved for that disease, and in 2023 for cholestatic pruritus in Alagille syndrome. Ipsen acquired Albireo in 2023.", "timeline": [["2021", "FDA approves Bylvay (odevixibat), the first drug for PFIC pruritus."], ["2023", "Bylvay is approved for cholestatic pruritus in Alagille syndrome."], ["2023", "Ipsen acquires Albireo Pharma."]], "cell": "Odevixibat is a potent, reversible inhibitor of the ileal bile acid transporter (IBAT, also called ASBT, gene SLC10A2) on terminal ileal enterocytes. IBAT uses the sodium gradient to take up conjugated bile acids from the gut lumen, returning about 95% of them to the liver through the portal vein. By blocking this uptake, odevixibat interrupts the enterohepatic circulation. More bile acids pass into the colon and leave in the stool, and the bile acid pool and serum levels fall. Lower bile acid levels are associated with less pruritus and may reduce bile acid injury to the liver. It works like a medical version of surgical biliary diversion.", "body": [["Ileum", "Bile acid uptake is blocked, so more bile acids leave in stool."], ["Liver and blood", "Serum bile acids fall, relieving a liver that cannot drain bile normally."], ["Skin", "Pruritus often improves, allowing better sleep for children and families."], ["Colon", "More bile acids in the colon cause diarrhea and abdominal pain."], ["Vitamin absorption", "Fat-soluble vitamin absorption can decline, raising risks of bleeding and fractures."]], "journey": "Odevixibat is taken by mouth as capsules or pellets sprinkled on soft food. It acts in the ileum and very little is absorbed; plasma levels are low. The small amount absorbed is highly protein bound, is metabolized slightly by monohydroxylation and has a half-life of about two hours. Almost all of the drug passes out unchanged in the feces. Effects on itching build over weeks.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea is the most common effect and follows directly from extra bile acids reaching the colon, where they stimulate fluid secretion. Dehydration can result. Less bile acid returning to the liver reduces bile available for absorbing fats, so vitamins A, D, E and K can drop, raising fracture and bleeding risk. Liver test elevations and liver injury have been reported, so liver function is monitored. In some PFIC types where the bile salt export pump is completely absent, the drug is not expected to work because few bile acids reach the gut. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Odevixibat was the first medicine approved for PFIC, a disease that before had relied on surgery or liver transplant for severe itching.", "src": ["Bylvay (odevixibat) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "sucroferric-oxyhydroxide": {"origin": "People on dialysis cannot clear phosphate from the diet, and high phosphate leads to bone disease and blood vessel calcification. Early binders used aluminum, which caused brain and bone toxicity, and calcium binders added calcium that could also calcify vessels. Iron was a natural alternative because iron oxides bind phosphate strongly. The challenge was making an iron compound that grabs phosphate but releases very little iron. Vifor, a Swiss company, developed sucroferric oxyhydroxide, a polynuclear iron(III) oxyhydroxide core held stable by sucrose and starches, through its joint venture with Fresenius Medical Care. The FDA approved it as Velphoro in 2013 as a chewable phosphate binder.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Aluminum-based phosphate binders are linked to dialysis encephalopathy and bone disease."], ["2013", "FDA approves Velphoro (sucroferric oxyhydroxide)."]], "cell": "Sucroferric oxyhydroxide does not act on any receptor. It works by chemistry in the gut lumen. Its core is polynuclear iron(III) oxyhydroxide, covered with hydroxyl and water groups. When food releases phosphate in the stomach and intestine, phosphate ions swap places with these surface groups through ligand exchange and bind tightly to the iron. The resulting iron-phosphate complex is insoluble and cannot be absorbed, so it is excreted in the stool. The sucrose and starch shell keeps the core from breaking down, which limits how much free iron is released and absorbed. Less dietary phosphate absorbed means lower serum phosphorus, without adding calcium or aluminum.", "body": [["Gut", "Phosphate from meals is bound and carried out in the stool instead of being absorbed."], ["Blood phosphate", "Serum phosphorus falls over one to two weeks, reducing the drive for secondary hyperparathyroidism."], ["Bones and vessels", "Better phosphate control is aimed at protecting bone and reducing vascular calcification."], ["Stool", "Iron makes the stool black, which can hide signs of bleeding."], ["Iron stores", "Only a small amount of iron is absorbed, but people with iron-storage disorders need care."]], "journey": "The tablets are chewed or crushed and taken with meals, so the binder is mixed with food when phosphate is released. The iron core stays in the gut lumen. A small amount of iron is released and absorbed, but this is low compared with the total iron in each tablet. The bound phosphate and remaining iron compound pass out in the stool. It is not metabolized, so kidney and liver function do not affect it.", "sidefx": "Black or discolored stool is from the iron and is harmless, but it can mask bleeding from the gut. Diarrhea and nausea are common gut effects of the large amount of material passing through. Because a little iron is absorbed, people with hemochromatosis or other iron-overload conditions are at risk of more iron buildup. Binders can also bind some oral drugs in the gut, so certain medications, such as alendronate and doxycycline, are separated in time. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Sucroferric oxyhydroxide is related chemically to iron sucrose, an IV iron, but it is designed to do the opposite: stay in the gut and release as little iron as possible.", "src": ["Velphoro (sucroferric oxyhydroxide) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "lanthanum-carbonate": {"origin": "Lanthanum is a rare earth metal discovered in 1839 by the Swedish chemist Carl Gustaf Mosander, who found it hidden in a cerium mineral; its name comes from a Greek word meaning to lie hidden. Like other trivalent metals, lanthanum binds phosphate very tightly, and very little of it is absorbed from the gut. That made it attractive for dialysis patients who need phosphate binders without the calcium load of calcium salts or the toxicity of aluminum. The British company Shire developed lanthanum carbonate as a chewable tablet, and the FDA approved it as Fosrenol in 2004. An oral powder form was later added.", "timeline": [["1839", "Carl Gustaf Mosander discovers lanthanum."], ["2004", "FDA approves Fosrenol (lanthanum carbonate) for dialysis patients."]], "cell": "Lanthanum carbonate does not act on receptors. In the acidic stomach it dissolves into trivalent lanthanum ions (La3+). These ions bind phosphate released from food with very high affinity, forming lanthanum phosphate, which is insoluble across the pH range of the gut. The insoluble complex cannot be absorbed and passes out in the stool. Less phosphate absorbed means lower serum phosphorus and a lower calcium-phosphate product. Because it adds neither calcium nor aluminum, it avoids calcium loading and aluminum toxicity. Lanthanum is dense to x-rays, so it shows up on abdominal films.", "body": [["Gut", "Dietary phosphate is bound and removed in the stool."], ["Blood phosphate", "Serum phosphorus falls over weeks, which helps control secondary hyperparathyroidism."], ["Bowel", "Unchewed tablets can form a mass, and serious obstruction, ileus and perforation have been reported."], ["Imaging", "The drug appears bright on abdominal x-rays, which can look like a contrast agent."], ["Calcium", "Hypocalcemia can occur as phosphate and parathyroid balance shift."]], "journey": "The tablets are chewed thoroughly or crushed and taken with or right after meals. Lanthanum ions are released in the stomach and bind phosphate there and in the intestine. Less than about 0.002% of a dose is absorbed. The tiny absorbed fraction is more than 99% protein bound and is cleared through bile rather than the kidneys, with a long plasma half-life of about two days. Some lanthanum is deposited in tissues such as bone and liver over time. The rest passes out in the feces.", "sidefx": "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea or constipation reflect the large amount of insoluble material in the gut. Tablets that are not chewed can clump and cause blockage or perforation, especially in people with slow bowels or prior bowel surgery, which is why chewing is emphasized. Lanthanum can bind certain oral drugs such as fluoroquinolones and levothyroxine, so they are separated in time. Its radio-opacity can be mistaken for something else on x-ray. Small amounts deposit in bone and liver, though no clear harm has been shown. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Lanthanum is the first element of the lanthanide series, and its name comes from the Greek lanthanein, to lie hidden.", "src": ["Fosrenol (lanthanum carbonate) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "hyoscyamine": {"origin": "Hyoscyamine comes from plants of the nightshade family: henbane (Hyoscyamus niger), deadly nightshade (Atropa belladonna) and jimsonweed (Datura). These plants were used since ancient times as poisons, pain relievers and, in belladonna's case, as cosmetics to widen the pupils. In 1833 the German chemists Philipp Lorenz Geiger and Germain Hesse isolated hyoscyamine, while atropine was purified around the same time. Chemists later learned that atropine is a racemic mix that forms when the natural levorotatory hyoscyamine is altered during extraction. Hyoscyamine, the pure levo form, carries essentially all the activity. Many hyoscyamine products are older drugs sold for decades without going through modern FDA new drug approval.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Henbane and belladonna are used as poisons and medicines."], ["1833", "Geiger and Hesse isolate hyoscyamine."], ["1800s", "Chemists show atropine is the racemic form of hyoscyamine."]], "cell": "Hyoscyamine is a competitive antagonist of acetylcholine at all five muscarinic receptor subtypes (M1 to M5). It binds the receptor without activating it, blocking acetylcholine released by parasympathetic nerves. On smooth muscle of the gut and bladder, M3 receptors normally drive contraction through Gq and calcium, so blocking them relaxes spasm and slows motility. On salivary, sweat and bronchial glands, blockade reduces secretions. On the heart's SA node, M2 blockade removes vagal slowing, so heart rate rises. In the eye it dilates the pupil and paralyzes focusing. As a tertiary amine it crosses the blood-brain barrier and can cause confusion. Being the active levo isomer, it is about twice as potent as racemic atropine.", "body": [["Gut", "Smooth muscle relaxes, easing cramping in IBS and other spasm, and motility slows."], ["Salivary and bronchial glands", "Saliva and airway secretions decrease, causing dry mouth and thicker mucus."], ["Heart", "Blocking vagal tone speeds the heart rate."], ["Bladder", "Detrusor relaxation can cause urinary hesitancy or retention."], ["Eyes", "Pupils dilate and focusing weakens, blurring near vision."], ["Skin and temperature", "Reduced sweating limits heat loss, raising the risk of heat illness."], ["Brain", "Confusion, drowsiness or agitation can occur, especially in older adults."]], "journey": "Hyoscyamine is given by mouth, under the tongue, as an orally disintegrating tablet, as drops, or by injection. Oral doses work in about 20 to 30 minutes, and sublingual doses faster. Extended-release forms spread the effect over about 12 hours. It is about half bound to plasma proteins and crosses into the brain and the placenta. It is partly metabolized in the liver, and most of a dose leaves unchanged in the urine. The half-life is about three to four hours.", "sidefx": "Side effects are the predictable result of blocking muscarinic receptors throughout the body: dry mouth, blurred vision, constipation, urinary retention, fast heart rate and reduced sweating. Hot weather can cause heat prostration. Because it enters the brain, older adults can become confused or delirious, which is why it is on the Beers list. Overdose causes the classic anticholinergic toxidrome: hot, dry, flushed, dilated pupils and delirium. Physostigmine, a cholinesterase inhibitor that enters the brain, can reverse severe central toxicity under expert care.", "fun": "Belladonna means beautiful lady in Italian, a name from the practice of using the plant's juice to widen women's pupils.", "src": ["Levsin (hyoscyamine) prescribing information", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "glycopyrrolate-oral": {"origin": "Glycopyrrolate was made in the late 1950s as a synthetic antimuscarinic with a permanent positive charge, a quaternary ammonium group, so that it would block acetylcholine in the body without entering the brain. The A. H. Robins Company of Richmond, Virginia, introduced it as Robinul in the early 1960s to reduce stomach acid in peptic ulcer disease. Anesthesiologists soon adopted the injection to dry secretions and protect the heart from slowing during surgery. Oral forms found new uses later. In 2010 the FDA approved Cuvposa oral solution to reduce chronic severe drooling in children with neurologic conditions such as cerebral palsy.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Glycopyrrolate is synthesized as a quaternary antimuscarinic."], ["1960s", "A. H. Robins introduces Robinul for peptic ulcer disease."], ["2010", "FDA approves Cuvposa oral solution for chronic severe drooling in children."]], "cell": "Glycopyrrolate is a competitive antagonist of acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors, mainly M1 to M3 in the periphery. In salivary glands it blocks M3 receptors that drive saliva secretion through Gq, phospholipase C and calcium, so saliva output drops. In the stomach it blocks M1 and M3 receptors involved in acid secretion. In gut smooth muscle it reduces cholinergic contraction, slowing motility. On the heart, M2 blockade reduces vagal slowing. Its quaternary ammonium nitrogen is permanently charged, so it crosses lipid membranes like the blood-brain barrier poorly. That keeps central effects such as confusion low compared with atropine or hyoscyamine.", "body": [["Salivary glands", "Less saliva is produced, which reduces drooling."], ["Stomach", "Acid and pepsin secretion fall, the basis of its old use for peptic ulcer."], ["Gut", "Motility slows, causing constipation and, rarely, pseudo-obstruction."], ["Bladder", "Detrusor relaxation can cause urinary retention."], ["Heart", "Heart rate can rise from vagal blockade."], ["Skin and temperature", "Less sweating can cause overheating in hot weather."], ["Brain", "Little drug enters, so confusion is less common than with tertiary antimuscarinics."]], "journey": "Glycopyrrolate is poorly absorbed from the gut because of its permanent charge; oral bioavailability is low and falls further with food, especially high-fat meals, so oral doses are timed apart from meals. It peaks in one to two hours and lasts about six to eight hours for drying secretions. Very little is metabolized. It leaves mainly unchanged in the urine, so kidney impairment raises levels. It crosses the placenta and blood-brain barrier poorly.", "sidefx": "All the common side effects come from peripheral muscarinic blockade: dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention, blurred vision, flushing, fast heart rate and decreased sweating with risk of heat prostration. Severe constipation can progress to intestinal pseudo-obstruction in children who cannot report symptoms. Central effects are uncommon because it does not cross the blood-brain barrier well. Overdose causes peripheral anticholinergic toxicity; neostigmine, a cholinesterase inhibitor that does not enter the brain, can counter peripheral effects under expert care.", "fun": "Glycopyrrolate's charged structure is the same trick used in methscopolamine and ipratropium: a permanent positive charge keeps a drug out of the brain.", "src": ["Robinul (glycopyrrolate) FDA label", "Cuvposa (glycopyrrolate) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "aluminum-hydroxide-magnesium-hydroxide": {"origin": "Neutralizing stomach acid with alkaline powders is an old idea; chalk and baking soda were used for indigestion for centuries. In the early twentieth century physicians used aluminum and magnesium salts separately, but aluminum tends to cause constipation and magnesium tends to cause diarrhea. Combining them balanced those effects. The Philadelphia company William H. Rorer introduced Maalox in 1949; its name comes from MAgnesium, ALuminum and hydrOXide. Mylanta and many generic suspensions followed, and many modern versions add simethicone for gas. These antacids remain sold over the counter.", "timeline": [["1800s", "Alkaline powders such as sodium bicarbonate are widely used for indigestion."], ["1949", "William H. Rorer introduces Maalox, an aluminum and magnesium hydroxide antacid."], ["1970s", "Aluminum accumulation is linked to brain and bone disease in kidney failure."]], "cell": "These antacids do not act on receptors; they work by simple acid-base chemistry. In the stomach, aluminum hydroxide reacts with hydrochloric acid to form aluminum chloride and water, and magnesium hydroxide reacts to form magnesium chloride and water. Removing hydrogen ions raises gastric pH. Above a pH of about 4, the digestive enzyme pepsin becomes much less active, so the lining is less irritated and refluxed contents are less corrosive. Magnesium ions draw water into the intestine osmotically, which loosens stool, while aluminum ions relax intestinal smooth muscle and bind phosphate, which tends to constipate. Together the two partly cancel each other's bowel effects.", "body": [["Stomach", "Acid is neutralized within minutes, easing heartburn and indigestion."], ["Esophagus", "Refluxed stomach contents are less acidic and less irritating."], ["Intestines", "Magnesium causes looser stools and aluminum firmer stools; the mix aims for balance."], ["Phosphate", "Aluminum binds dietary phosphate, which can lower blood phosphate with heavy use."], ["Kidneys", "Absorbed magnesium and aluminum are cleared by the kidneys, so kidney failure lets them build up."]], "journey": "The suspension or chewable tablet coats the stomach and reacts with acid immediately. On an empty stomach it is emptied quickly, so the effect lasts only about 30 minutes; taken after a meal, food keeps it in the stomach and the effect can last up to about three hours. Most of the aluminum and magnesium is not absorbed and leaves in the stool, partly as insoluble salts such as aluminum phosphate. A small fraction of magnesium and aluminum is absorbed and excreted by healthy kidneys.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea from magnesium is an osmotic effect, and constipation from aluminum comes from its effect on gut muscle and from forming insoluble salts. In kidney failure, absorbed magnesium can build up and cause weakness, low blood pressure and slowed breathing, while aluminum can collect in brain and bone, causing encephalopathy and osteomalacia. Aluminum's phosphate binding can lower blood phosphate. By changing gastric pH and binding metal-sensitive drugs, antacids reduce absorption of tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones, iron and levothyroxine. Calcium can be given for severe magnesium toxicity.", "fun": "Aluminum hydroxide was once also prescribed as a phosphate binder in kidney failure, until its accumulation in the brain was linked to dialysis dementia.", "src": ["Mag-Al antacid suspension Drug Facts label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "crofelemer": {"origin": "Crofelemer comes from the dark red sap of Croton lechleri, a tree of the Amazon rainforest whose latex is called sangre de drago, or dragon's blood. Indigenous peoples and local healers in South America used the sap for wounds and diarrhea. In the 1990s the California company Shaman Pharmaceuticals, founded by Lisa Conte, studied the sap and isolated its active proanthocyanidin oligomers. After Shaman closed, Conte founded Napo Pharmaceuticals to continue the work. The FDA approved crofelemer in 2012 as Fulyzaq, later renamed Mytesi, for non-infectious diarrhea in adults with HIV on antiretroviral therapy. It was the first oral botanical drug approved by the FDA.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Croton lechleri sap is used as a traditional remedy in the Amazon."], ["1990s", "Shaman Pharmaceuticals studies the sap and isolates crofelemer."], ["2012", "FDA approves crofelemer (Fulyzaq), the first oral botanical drug."]], "cell": "Crofelemer is a large mixture of proanthocyanidin oligomers that stays on the luminal side of the gut. In secretory diarrhea, intestinal cells pump chloride into the lumen through two main channels: CFTR, which is opened by cAMP and protein kinase A, and calcium-activated chloride channels such as TMEM16A. Sodium and water follow the chloride, producing watery stool. Crofelemer binds and inhibits both channels from the outside of the cell. With less chloride secreted, less water enters the gut, and stools become more formed. It does not slow motility the way opioids do, so it does not cause typical constipation. HIV-associated diarrhea is thought to involve antiretroviral drug effects and HIV enteropathy that drive secretion.", "body": [["Small intestine and colon", "Less chloride and water secretion means fewer watery stools in HIV-associated diarrhea."], ["Gut motility", "Transit is not slowed much, so constipation is uncommon."], ["Respiratory tract", "Upper respiratory infections and bronchitis were reported more often in trials."], ["Liver tests", "Small bilirubin increases have been reported."], ["Gas", "Flatulence is a common gut effect of the drug acting in the bowel."]], "journey": "Crofelemer is taken by mouth as delayed-release tablets, which are swallowed whole. Its large molecules are barely absorbed, so blood levels are usually not detectable. It acts on the gut lining from the inside and then passes out in the stool. Because almost none reaches the bloodstream, there are few drug interactions and no meaningful liver or kidney clearance. Stool consistency improves over days to weeks.", "sidefx": "Because crofelemer stays in the gut, systemic side effects are few. Gas and mild abdominal effects relate to local action. Respiratory infections and bronchitis occurred somewhat more often in trials, without a clear mechanism. The main warning is not a toxicity but a diagnostic one: if diarrhea is caused by an infection, an antisecretory drug may mask it, so infectious causes are excluded first. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Before crofelemer, the only FDA-approved botanical drug was a topical green tea extract ointment for genital warts.", "src": ["Mytesi (crofelemer) FDA label", "FDA news release December 2012"]}, "methscopolamine": {"origin": "Scopolamine, also called hyoscine, comes from nightshade family plants such as henbane, Datura and Scopolia. It was isolated in the late nineteenth century by the German chemist Albert Ladenburg, who did much of the early chemistry of these tropane alkaloids. Scopolamine enters the brain easily, causing drowsiness and confusion. In the twentieth century chemists added a methyl group to its nitrogen, giving it a permanent positive charge. The resulting quaternary compound, methscopolamine, blocks muscarinic receptors in the body but crosses into the brain poorly. Upjohn introduced methscopolamine bromide as Pamine in the 1950s, mainly for peptic ulcer disease, and it later appeared in some prescription cold products.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Henbane and related plants are used as medicines and poisons."], ["1880s", "Albert Ladenburg isolates hyoscine (scopolamine) and studies the tropane alkaloids."], ["1950s", "Upjohn introduces methscopolamine bromide as Pamine."]], "cell": "Methscopolamine is a competitive antagonist of acetylcholine at peripheral muscarinic receptors. In the stomach it blocks M1 and M3 receptors that help drive acid and pepsin secretion. In salivary and bronchial glands it blocks M3 receptors, reducing secretions. In gut smooth muscle it blocks M3-mediated contraction, slowing motility and reducing spasm. On the heart it blocks M2 receptors and can speed the heart rate. The methylated nitrogen carries a permanent positive charge, which makes the molecule poorly lipid soluble. As a result it is poorly absorbed from the gut and crosses the blood-brain barrier poorly, so the sedation and confusion typical of scopolamine are much less common.", "body": [["Stomach", "Acid and pepsin secretion fall, its historical use in peptic ulcer disease, though it has not been shown to heal ulcers."], ["Salivary glands", "Saliva decreases, causing dry mouth, a property used in some cold products to dry secretions."], ["Gut", "Motility slows and cramps ease, but constipation can follow."], ["Bladder", "Urinary hesitancy or retention can occur, especially with prostate enlargement."], ["Heart", "Heart rate can increase from vagal blockade."], ["Skin and temperature", "Reduced sweating raises the risk of heat prostration in hot weather."]], "journey": "Methscopolamine is taken by mouth. Because of its permanent charge, only a small fraction is absorbed. Effects begin in about an hour and last four to six hours. Little enters the brain or crosses the placenta. It is partly metabolized in the liver, and the absorbed drug and its metabolites leave in the urine, while unabsorbed drug passes in the feces.", "sidefx": "Side effects reflect peripheral muscarinic blockade: dry mouth, blurred vision, constipation, urinary hesitancy, fast heart rate and decreased sweating with heat prostration risk. Diarrhea can be an early sign of a partial bowel blockage, in which case an antimuscarinic could worsen the problem. Older adults are more sensitive to anticholinergic effects. Central effects are much less common than with scopolamine because of its charge. Severe peripheral toxicity can be countered by a cholinesterase inhibitor under expert care.", "fun": "Methscopolamine and the scopolamine motion sickness patch share a parent molecule, but one extra methyl group keeps methscopolamine mostly out of the brain.", "src": ["Pamine (methscopolamine bromide) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "doxylamine-pyridoxine": {"origin": "This pairing has one of the most dramatic histories in pregnancy medicine. Doxylamine is an old ethanolamine antihistamine from the 1940s era of sleep and allergy drugs, and pyridoxine is vitamin B6, which doctors had tried for morning sickness since the 1940s. The William S. Merrell Company combined them as Bendectin, approved in the US in 1956, and millions of pregnant women took it. In the late 1970s and early 1980s lawsuits claimed it caused birth defects. Large studies did not support that claim, but the legal costs were so high that Merrell Dow stopped making it in 1983. Rates of hospital admission for severe nausea in pregnancy rose afterward. The Canadian company Duchesnay kept a similar product, Diclectin, on the market in Canada, and later brought the combination back to the US as Diclegis, approved by the FDA in 2013, with an extended-release version, Bonjesta, in 2016.", "timeline": [["1956", "Bendectin (doxylamine, pyridoxine, and at first dicyclomine) is approved in the US."], ["1983", "Merrell Dow stops making Bendectin because of lawsuit costs, not proven harm."], ["2013", "FDA approves Diclegis delayed-release tablets for nausea and vomiting of pregnancy."], ["2016", "FDA approves Bonjesta, an extended-release version."]], "cell": "Doxylamine is a first-generation H1 antihistamine that acts as an inverse agonist, stabilizing the H1 receptor in its inactive state. It crosses the blood-brain barrier. Blocking H1 receptors in the vestibular system and in brainstem nausea pathways, including inputs to the vomiting center in the medulla, reduces nausea signaling. Doxylamine also blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, which adds to its antiemetic and sedating effect. Pyridoxine is converted in the liver and tissues to pyridoxal 5'-phosphate, a coenzyme for many enzymes, including those that make neurotransmitters such as serotonin, dopamine and GABA. How B6 eases pregnancy nausea is not fully understood. The tablet has an enteric (delayed-release) coating so that a bedtime dose gives its strongest effect the next morning, when nausea is often worst.", "body": [["Brain", "Central H1 blockade causes drowsiness and calms nausea pathways, which is why sleepiness is the most common effect."], ["Inner ear and balance system", "Blocking H1 and muscarinic signaling from the vestibular system reduces motion-related queasiness."], ["Mouth and gut", "Anticholinergic action lowers saliva and slows the bowel, causing dry mouth and constipation."], ["Bladder", "Anticholinergic effects can make it harder to empty the bladder, a concern with urinary retention."], ["Pregnancy", "Large studies of the combination have not shown an increased risk of birth defects, which is why it is a first-line choice for nausea and vomiting of pregnancy."]], "journey": "The tablet passes through the stomach intact because of its coating, then dissolves in the intestine, so absorption is delayed by several hours. That timing is the point of bedtime dosing. Doxylamine is broken down in the liver, mostly by N-dealkylation, and leaves in the urine, with a half-life of roughly 12 hours. Pyridoxine is a water-soluble vitamin. It is quickly converted to its active coenzyme form, pyridoxal 5'-phosphate, and the extra is excreted in the urine. Food further delays absorption.", "sidefx": "Sleepiness comes from blocking histamine, which keeps the brain awake. Because of that, falls and accidents are a real risk, and alcohol or other sedatives add to it. Dry mouth, constipation and urinary retention come from muscarinic blockade, so it is used carefully with narrow-angle glaucoma or bladder outlet obstruction. In overdose, doxylamine can cause an anticholinergic toxidrome with agitation, seizures and rhabdomyolysis. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Bendectin became a famous case in US law. The Supreme Court's 1993 Daubert decision, which set rules for scientific expert testimony in court, grew out of a Bendectin lawsuit.", "src": ["Diclegis (doxylamine and pyridoxine) FDA label", "Bonjesta FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "sodium-sulfate-potassium-sulfate-magnesium-sulfate": {"origin": "Sulfate salts are some of the oldest laxatives in medicine. Magnesium sulfate is Epsom salt, named for the bitter spring water at Epsom in England that people noticed in the 1600s acted as a purge. Sodium sulfate is Glauber's salt, described by the German chemist Johann Rudolf Glauber in the 1600s. Modern colonoscopy needed a cleaner, better tolerated way to empty the colon than old castor oil or large volumes of salt water. Braintree Laboratories developed a low-volume oral sulfate solution that mixes sodium, potassium and magnesium sulfates, and the FDA approved it as Suprep in 2010. A tablet version of a related sulfate formula, Sutab, followed in 2020.", "timeline": [["1600s", "Epsom salt (magnesium sulfate) is recognized as a purgative from Epsom spring water."], ["1600s", "Johann Rudolf Glauber describes sodium sulfate, later called Glauber's salt."], ["2010", "FDA approves Suprep, a sodium, potassium and magnesium sulfate bowel prep."], ["2020", "FDA approves Sutab, a related sulfate-based tablet prep."]], "cell": "This product has no receptor target. It works by simple physics. Sulfate is a large, charged anion that the intestinal lining absorbs poorly. When a concentrated sulfate solution reaches the small intestine and colon, the trapped sulfate pulls water into the lumen by osmosis and holds it there. The bowel fills with fluid, stretches, and peristalsis speeds up, producing large-volume watery diarrhea that flushes stool out. The sodium, potassium and magnesium that come with the sulfate partly balance the salts lost in the diarrhea, and the large volume of clear fluid the patient drinks with it replaces water. Some sulfate and magnesium is still absorbed, which explains the small transient rises in blood levels.", "body": [["Small intestine and colon", "Trapped sulfate holds water in the bowel, so stool turns liquid and is flushed out, leaving the colon wall visible for colonoscopy."], ["Abdomen", "The sudden load of fluid stretches the bowel and causes bloating, cramps and nausea; vomiting can occur if it is taken too fast."], ["Blood and electrolytes", "Fluid shifts can lower sodium, potassium or magnesium and raise serum osmolality and uric acid for a short time."], ["Kidneys", "Dehydration from the diarrhea can reduce kidney blood flow, which is why kidney disease and certain blood pressure drugs raise the risk of acute kidney injury."], ["Heart and brain", "Electrolyte shifts, especially low sodium or potassium, can rarely trigger arrhythmias or seizures."]], "journey": "The solution is swallowed and moves quickly into the small intestine. Most of the sulfate is never absorbed and leaves in the stool along with the water it pulls in. Bowel movements usually start within about one to two hours and continue for several hours after each part of the split prep. The small amount of sulfate that is absorbed raises blood sulfate briefly and is then filtered out by the kidneys into the urine. Nothing is metabolized by the liver.", "sidefx": "Almost every side effect comes from moving a lot of water into the gut. Bloating, cramps and nausea reflect a suddenly stretched bowel. Large fluid losses can cause dehydration and shifts in sodium, potassium and magnesium, which can lead to kidney injury, arrhythmias or seizures in vulnerable people. The sudden flow and high osmolality can cause small surface ulcers in the colon lining that can look like inflammatory bowel disease on colonoscopy. Vomiting can lead to aspiration. There is no antidote; care means fluids and electrolyte correction.", "fun": "Epsom salt from the famous spring became such a popular purgative in the 1600s that Epsom grew into a well known spa town.", "src": ["Suprep Bowel Prep Kit FDA label", "Sutab FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "rifamycin": {"origin": "The rifamycins were discovered in 1957 at Lepetit Pharmaceuticals in Milan, Italy, by a team led by Piero Sensi with Maria Teresa Timbal. They found them in a soil bacterium from near the French Riviera, now called Amycolatopsis rifamycinica. The team named the compounds after the French crime film Rififi. From this family came rifampin and other tuberculosis drugs. Rifamycin SV itself was used for decades in some countries as an injection or topical antibiotic. The modern US product, Aemcolo, came from Cosmo Pharmaceuticals, which packed rifamycin into an enteric-coated, delayed-release tablet using its MMX technology so the drug would be released in the lower small bowel and colon. The FDA approved it in 2018 for travelers' diarrhea caused by noninvasive strains of E. coli.", "timeline": [["1957", "Piero Sensi's team at Lepetit in Milan discovers the rifamycins in a soil bacterium."], ["1960s", "Rifamycin SV comes into use in Europe; rifampin is made from the family."], ["2018", "FDA approves Aemcolo delayed-release rifamycin tablets for travelers' diarrhea."]], "cell": "Rifamycin binds the beta subunit (encoded by rpoB) of bacterial DNA-dependent RNA polymerase. It sits in a pocket in the channel where the new RNA chain leaves the enzyme. Once it is bound, the polymerase can start a transcript but cannot extend it beyond a few nucleotides, so the bacterium cannot make messenger RNA and therefore cannot make new proteins. Human RNA polymerase has a different structure and is not affected. The special tablet coating holds the drug until the pH and conditions of the distal ileum and colon release it, so a high concentration sits right where the diarrhea-causing E. coli live. Resistance arises from point mutations in rpoB that change the binding pocket.", "body": [["Gut (distal ileum and colon)", "High local drug levels suppress noninvasive E. coli, which shortens the time until the last unformed stool."], ["Gut microbiome", "Like any antibiotic it disturbs normal gut bacteria, which can rarely allow C. difficile to overgrow."], ["Whole body", "Blood levels are very low, so it does not treat infections outside the gut and is not used for diarrhea with fever or blood in the stool."]], "journey": "The tablet is swallowed and its coating protects it through the stomach and upper small bowel. It releases the drug in the distal ileum and colon. Very little crosses the gut wall, so plasma levels stay extremely low. Almost all of the dose passes out in the feces unchanged. Because of this, the usual rifamycin drug interactions and orange body fluids seen with rifampin are not a major feature.", "sidefx": "Because so little reaches the blood, side effects are mild, mostly headache and constipation. Killing normal gut bacteria can let C. difficile grow and make toxins, which can cause new diarrhea or colitis. Allergic reactions can occur as with any rifamycin. It is not used when diarrhea comes with fever or bloody stools, because invasive bacteria in the bowel wall would not be reached. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The name rifamycin comes from Rififi, a 1955 French heist film that the Lepetit researchers enjoyed.", "src": ["Aemcolo (rifamycin) FDA label", "Sensi P. History of the development of rifampin. Rev Infect Dis, 1983", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "vonoprazan-amoxicillin": {"origin": "The story starts with Barry Marshall and Robin Warren in Australia, who showed in the early 1980s that Helicobacter pylori causes most stomach ulcers, work that earned them a Nobel Prize in 2005. Curing the infection needs an antibiotic plus strong acid suppression. Amoxicillin was developed by Beecham in England and launched in 1972. Vonoprazan was discovered by Takeda in Japan as the first marketed potassium-competitive acid blocker (P-CAB), a new way to block the proton pump that does not need acid to activate it. It was approved in Japan in 2014. Phathom Pharmaceuticals licensed it for the US, and the FDA approved Voquezna Dual Pak (vonoprazan plus amoxicillin) and Triple Pak (adding clarithromycin) in 2022 for H. pylori infection.", "timeline": [["1972", "Beecham launches amoxicillin."], ["1982", "Marshall and Warren culture H. pylori from stomach biopsies."], ["2014", "Vonoprazan is approved in Japan."], ["2022", "FDA approves Voquezna Dual Pak and Triple Pak for H. pylori."]], "cell": "Vonoprazan blocks the H+/K+-ATPase, the proton pump on the secretory membrane of stomach parietal cells. Unlike proton pump inhibitors, it is not a prodrug and needs no acid activation. It binds reversibly near the potassium binding site and competes with potassium, so the pump cannot exchange potassium for hydrogen ions. It is a weak base that concentrates strongly in the acidic parietal cell canaliculus, so it acts fast and lasts long. Amoxicillin is a beta-lactam that binds penicillin-binding proteins and blocks the transpeptidase step that cross-links peptidoglycan in the H. pylori cell wall, so the bacterium bursts. H. pylori grows and divides faster when the stomach pH is higher, which makes it more vulnerable to amoxicillin. Amoxicillin is also more stable at a higher pH. That is why deep acid suppression lets a two-drug regimen work.", "body": [["Stomach", "Strong, steady acid suppression raises gastric pH within hours, helping ulcers heal and making H. pylori easier to kill."], ["H. pylori infection", "Amoxicillin destroys the bacterial cell wall, and clearing the infection lowers the chance that ulcers come back."], ["Gut microbiome", "Killing normal gut bacteria can cause diarrhea and rarely C. difficile infection."], ["Skin and immune system", "Amoxicillin can trigger allergic rashes, and rarely severe reactions such as anaphylaxis or Stevens-Johnson syndrome."]], "journey": "Both drugs are taken by mouth. Vonoprazan reaches peak levels in about one and a half to two hours and is broken down in the liver, mainly by CYP3A4, with a half-life of about seven hours. Acid suppression lasts around a full day because the drug stays trapped in the parietal cells. Its metabolites leave in urine and feces. Amoxicillin is well absorbed, reaches the stomach lining through the blood and lumen, is hardly metabolized, and leaves mostly unchanged in the urine with a half-life of about one hour.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea and abdominal pain come from antibiotic disruption of gut bacteria, which can also allow C. difficile overgrowth. Amoxicillin is a penicillin, so people with penicillin allergy can have rashes or anaphylaxis. Vonoprazan is processed by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors or inducers change its levels. Long-term deep acid suppression raises gastrin, but the H. pylori regimen is short. There is no specific antidote; epinephrine treats anaphylaxis.", "fun": "Barry Marshall drank a culture of H. pylori in 1984 to prove it could cause stomach inflammation, and he developed gastritis soon after.", "src": ["Voquezna Dual Pak and Triple Pak FDA label", "Marshall BJ, Warren JR. Lancet, 1984", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "bismuth-metronidazole-tetracycline": {"origin": "Each part of this capsule has its own history. Bismuth salts have been used for stomach upset since the 1700s and 1800s. Metronidazole came from a French search at Rhone-Poulenc in the 1950s for drugs against Trichomonas, based on the natural nitroimidazole azomycin, and it reached the US as Flagyl in 1963. Tetracycline was made at Pfizer in the early 1950s by Lloyd Conover, who chemically modified chlortetracycline, the first tetracycline from soil bacteria. After H. pylori was linked to ulcers in the 1980s, doctors found that bismuth combined with metronidazole and tetracycline cured many infections, especially when clarithromycin resistance spread. Pylera packs all three into one capsule, given with a proton pump inhibitor as bismuth quadruple therapy. The FDA approved it in 2006.", "timeline": [["1948", "Chlortetracycline, the first tetracycline, is introduced from Streptomyces aureofaciens."], ["1953", "Lloyd Conover at Pfizer makes tetracycline from chlortetracycline."], ["1963", "Metronidazole (Flagyl) is approved in the US."], ["2006", "FDA approves Pylera capsules for H. pylori with omeprazole."]], "cell": "Bismuth subcitrate potassium has direct toxic effects on H. pylori. Bismuth ions bind bacterial proteins and enzymes, including urease and enzymes of energy metabolism, damage the cell wall, and reduce the ability of the bacteria to stick to the stomach lining. It also coats ulcer craters. Metronidazole is a prodrug. It enters the bacterium and is reduced by low-redox nitroreductases, forming short-lived nitro radicals that break DNA strands and kill the cell. Tetracycline binds the 30S ribosomal subunit and blocks aminoacyl-tRNA from binding at the A site, stopping protein synthesis. Three different targets make resistance less likely, and bismuth works even against metronidazole-resistant strains to some degree.", "body": [["Stomach", "The combination, with acid suppression, clears H. pylori and lets ulcers heal with fewer recurrences."], ["Gut and stool", "Bismuth reacts with sulfide in the gut to form bismuth sulfide, turning stools black, which is harmless but can be mistaken for bleeding."], ["Tongue and taste", "Metronidazole often causes a metallic taste."], ["Skin", "Tetracycline can make skin more sensitive to sunlight, causing exaggerated sunburn."], ["Nerves and brain", "Metronidazole can rarely cause numbness or tingling in the hands and feet, and very rarely seizures or encephalopathy."], ["Teeth and bones", "Tetracycline binds calcium in growing teeth and bone, so it is avoided in pregnancy and young children."]], "journey": "The capsules are swallowed with meals. Very little bismuth is absorbed, so most passes into the stool, though the small absorbed part has a long terminal half-life. Metronidazole is almost completely absorbed, spreads widely into tissues including the brain, is oxidized in the liver to a hydroxy metabolite, and leaves mainly in the urine with a half-life of about eight hours. Tetracycline absorption is reduced by calcium, magnesium, iron and dairy, which bind it. What is absorbed leaves largely unchanged in urine and feces.", "sidefx": "Black stools and a dark tongue come from harmless bismuth sulfide. Metronidazole's radicals can harm nerve tissue with longer use, explaining neuropathy, and it can cause a disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol by blocking aldehyde dehydrogenase. Tetracycline photosensitivity comes from the drug absorbing UV light in the skin and making reactive oxygen species, and its calcium binding stains developing teeth. Severe liver injury has been reported with metronidazole in people with Cockayne syndrome. Antibiotic disruption of gut flora can lead to C. difficile. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Pink bismuth stomach remedies date back to the early 1900s, when a doctor in New York reportedly created one for babies with severe diarrhea.", "src": ["Pylera FDA label", "Flagyl (metronidazole) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "omeprazole-amoxicillin-rifabutin": {"origin": "Talicia combines three older drugs in a new way. Omeprazole was discovered at Astra Hassle in Sweden and became the first proton pump inhibitor, launched in Europe in 1988 and in the US in 1989. Amoxicillin was launched by Beecham in 1972. Rifabutin is a semisynthetic rifamycin, derived from the rifamycin family first found at Lepetit in Italy in 1957; it was approved in the US in 1992 to prevent Mycobacterium avium complex infection in people with HIV. As clarithromycin resistance in H. pylori grew, the Israeli company RedHill Biopharma tested a rifabutin-based triple therapy because H. pylori rarely resists rifabutin. The FDA approved Talicia, an all-in-one capsule, in 2019.", "timeline": [["1957", "The rifamycin antibiotic family is discovered at Lepetit in Milan."], ["1972", "Amoxicillin is launched by Beecham."], ["1989", "Omeprazole is approved in the US as the first proton pump inhibitor."], ["1992", "Rifabutin is approved in the US for MAC prevention."], ["2019", "FDA approves Talicia for H. pylori infection."]], "cell": "Omeprazole is a prodrug. It collects in the acidic canaliculus of parietal cells, where acid turns it into a reactive sulfenamide that forms a covalent disulfide bond with cysteines on the H+/K+-ATPase, shutting the pump off until new pumps are made. Amoxicillin binds penicillin-binding proteins in the H. pylori cell wall and blocks peptidoglycan cross-linking, so the bacteria lyse. Rifabutin binds the beta subunit (rpoB) of bacterial DNA-dependent RNA polymerase and blocks RNA chain elongation, so the bacteria cannot make proteins. Higher gastric pH from omeprazole keeps amoxicillin stable and pushes H. pylori into an active growth state, where the antibiotics kill it best. Existing H. pylori resistance to rifabutin is rare, which is the main reason for this regimen.", "body": [["Stomach", "Acid falls sharply, ulcers can heal, and H. pylori is cleared by the two antibiotics."], ["Gut microbiome", "Antibiotic disruption causes diarrhea and can rarely allow C. difficile infection."], ["Body fluids", "Rifabutin can turn urine, sweat, tears and saliva orange-brown and can stain soft contact lenses."], ["Liver and drug interactions", "Rifabutin induces CYP3A4, which can lower levels of many other drugs, including hormonal contraceptives."], ["Blood and eyes", "Rifabutin can lower white blood cell counts and, rarely, cause uveitis."]], "journey": "The capsule is taken by mouth with food. Omeprazole is absorbed after its delayed-release coating dissolves, is cleared by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 in the liver, and has a plasma half-life of about one hour, though pump blockade lasts much longer. Amoxicillin is absorbed well and leaves unchanged in the urine. Rifabutin is fat-loving and spreads widely into tissues, is metabolized by CYP3A4, and has a long half-life of about 45 hours. Its metabolites leave in urine and feces.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea and nausea come from antibiotic effects on gut bacteria, which can also lead to C. difficile. Amoxicillin can cause allergic rashes or anaphylaxis in people with penicillin allergy. Rifabutin's color explains orange body fluids, and its strong induction of liver enzymes explains drug interactions. Rifabutin can also suppress white blood cells and inflame the eye. There is no antidote; epinephrine treats anaphylaxis.", "fun": "Rifabutin is far less used for H. pylori than for mycobacteria, which may be one reason H. pylori resistance to it has stayed low.", "src": ["Talicia FDA label", "Mycobutin (rifabutin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "lansoprazole-amoxicillin-clarithromycin": {"origin": "This kit packages the classic triple therapy for H. pylori. Lansoprazole was developed by Takeda in Japan as the second proton pump inhibitor and was approved in the US in 1995. Amoxicillin came from Beecham in 1972. Clarithromycin was created by Taisho Pharmaceutical in Japan by adding a methyl group to erythromycin, which made it far more stable in stomach acid; it reached the US in 1991. TAP Pharmaceuticals combined the three in one daily card as Prevpac, approved by the FDA in 1997, to make the two-week regimen easier to follow. The Prevpac brand has since been discontinued, but generic co-packaged kits remain. Because clarithromycin resistance has risen in many regions, this regimen is now used more selectively.", "timeline": [["1972", "Amoxicillin is launched by Beecham."], ["1991", "Clarithromycin is approved in the US."], ["1995", "Lansoprazole is approved in the US."], ["1997", "FDA approves Prevpac, a co-packaged lansoprazole, amoxicillin and clarithromycin kit."]], "cell": "Lansoprazole is a prodrug that concentrates in the acidic canaliculus of the parietal cell, is converted by acid into a reactive sulfenamide, and covalently binds cysteines on the H+/K+-ATPase, irreversibly blocking acid secretion until new pumps are made. Amoxicillin inhibits penicillin-binding proteins and peptidoglycan cross-linking, so the H. pylori cell wall fails. Clarithromycin binds the 23S rRNA in the 50S ribosomal subunit near the peptide exit tunnel and blocks translocation, stopping protein synthesis. Raising the gastric pH keeps the antibiotics stable and pushes H. pylori into active growth, where they kill it best. Resistance to clarithromycin usually comes from point mutations in the 23S rRNA gene, which is why eradication rates fall where resistance is common.", "body": [["Stomach", "Acid suppression helps ulcers heal, and the antibiotics clear H. pylori, lowering the chance of ulcer recurrence."], ["Taste", "Clarithromycin often leaves a bitter or metallic taste."], ["Heart", "Clarithromycin can lengthen the QT interval, especially with other QT drugs or low potassium, raising the risk of torsades de pointes."], ["Liver and drug interactions", "Clarithromycin is a strong CYP3A4 inhibitor, so it can raise levels of statins, some calcium channel blockers and many other drugs."], ["Gut microbiome", "Antibiotic disruption causes diarrhea and can allow C. difficile infection."]], "journey": "All three are taken by mouth. Lansoprazole is absorbed after its enteric coating dissolves, is broken down by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4, and has a short plasma half-life, though acid suppression lasts about a day. Amoxicillin is absorbed well and leaves unchanged in the urine. Clarithromycin is acid-stable, is metabolized by CYP3A4 to an active 14-hydroxy metabolite, and leaves through the kidneys and liver, with a half-life of a few hours.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea comes from antibiotic effects on gut bacteria, and macrolides also stimulate motilin receptors in the gut, increasing motility. Taste changes are typical of clarithromycin. QT prolongation happens because clarithromycin blocks cardiac hERG potassium channels. Its CYP3A4 inhibition causes serious drug interactions, such as statin muscle injury. Penicillin allergy can cause rash or anaphylaxis from amoxicillin. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Clarithromycin was made by adding a single methyl group to erythromycin, a tiny change that stopped the molecule from falling apart in stomach acid.", "src": ["Prevpac FDA label", "Biaxin (clarithromycin) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "cefadroxil": {"origin": "All cephalosporins trace back to Giuseppe Brotzu, an Italian professor who in 1945 found that a mold from near a sewage outlet in Cagliari, Sardinia, made a substance that killed bacteria. The mold, now called Acremonium, was studied at Oxford, where Edward Abraham and Guy Newton isolated cephalosporin C in the 1950s. Chemists then learned to make the cephalosporin core (7-ACA) and attach new side chains. Cephalexin, an oral drug, appeared in the late 1960s. Cefadroxil was made by adding a hydroxyl group to the cephalexin structure, which slowed its elimination and allowed less frequent dosing. It was developed by Bristol Laboratories and approved in the US in the late 1970s as Duricef. The brand is discontinued, but generics remain in wide use for skin and throat infections.", "timeline": [["1945", "Giuseppe Brotzu finds an antibacterial mold in Sardinia."], ["1950s", "Abraham and Newton at Oxford isolate cephalosporin C."], ["1960s", "The cephalosporin core is used to build oral drugs like cephalexin."], ["1970s", "Cefadroxil is approved in the US as Duricef."]], "cell": "Cefadroxil is a beta-lactam. Its four-membered beta-lactam ring looks like the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of the peptide chains that bacteria use to build their peptidoglycan cell wall. Penicillin-binding proteins, the transpeptidase enzymes that cross-link those chains, attack the beta-lactam ring and become permanently acylated. With cross-linking blocked, the wall weakens while autolysins keep cutting, and the bacterium bursts under its own osmotic pressure. Killing is time-dependent, meaning what matters most is how long drug levels stay above the minimum inhibitory concentration. As a first-generation agent it is strongest against streptococci and methicillin-susceptible S. aureus, with some activity against E. coli, Proteus mirabilis and Klebsiella. It does not cover MRSA, because MRSA's PBP2a has low affinity for it, or enterococci.", "body": [["Skin and soft tissue", "It kills strep and staph in skin infections such as cellulitis and impetigo."], ["Throat", "It treats strep throat as an alternative to penicillin."], ["Urinary tract", "High urine levels help it treat some bladder infections from susceptible gram-negative rods."], ["Gut", "Altered gut bacteria can cause diarrhea, and rarely C. difficile colitis."], ["Immune system", "Allergic rashes can occur, and rarely anaphylaxis, especially in people with severe penicillin allergy."]], "journey": "Cefadroxil is taken by mouth and is almost completely absorbed, with or without food. It reaches peak levels in about one and a half to two hours and spreads into most body tissues and fluids. It is not metabolized. More than 90 percent leaves unchanged in the urine within a day. Its half-life is about one and a half hours, longer than cephalexin's, which supports once- or twice-daily dosing. Kidney failure prolongs its half-life.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea and nausea come from the drug killing normal gut bacteria, which can also let C. difficile overgrow. Allergic reactions happen because the beta-lactam can bind body proteins and act as an antigen; cross-reactivity with penicillins is mainly due to similar side chains, and cefadroxil's side chain matches that of amoxicillin. Rarely it causes antibody-coated red cells (Coombs-positive hemolysis). There is no antidote; epinephrine treats anaphylaxis.", "fun": "The first cephalosporin mold was found in sea water near a sewage outfall, because Brotzu reasoned that bacteria-killing microbes might live where the water cleaned itself.", "src": ["Cefadroxil FDA label", "Abraham EP. Cephalosporins 1945-1986. Drugs, 1987", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "cefprozil": {"origin": "Cefprozil belongs to the cephalosporin family that began with Giuseppe Brotzu's antibacterial mold in Sardinia in 1945 and the isolation of cephalosporin C at Oxford in the 1950s. Second-generation cephalosporins were designed in the 1970s and 1980s to add coverage of Haemophilus influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis, common causes of ear, sinus and chest infections. Cefprozil was developed by Bristol-Myers Squibb as an oral second-generation agent with a pleasant-tasting liquid for children, and the FDA approved it as Cefzil in 1991. The brand has been discontinued, but generic cefprozil is still used for respiratory and skin infections.", "timeline": [["1945", "Giuseppe Brotzu discovers the cephalosporin-producing mold."], ["1950s", "Cephalosporin C is isolated at Oxford."], ["1991", "FDA approves cefprozil as Cefzil."]], "cell": "Cefprozil is a beta-lactam that binds penicillin-binding proteins, the transpeptidases that cross-link peptidoglycan strands in the bacterial cell wall. The beta-lactam ring mimics the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of the peptide chain, so the enzyme becomes acylated and stuck. Without cross-links, the wall cannot hold the high internal pressure, autolysins keep breaking it down, and the cell lyses. Its side chain makes it more stable to some beta-lactamases than first-generation drugs, which adds coverage of H. influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis, including beta-lactamase producers, while keeping activity against streptococci and methicillin-susceptible S. aureus. It has no activity against MRSA, enterococci or Pseudomonas.", "body": [["Ears and sinuses", "It treats otitis media and sinusitis from S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae and Moraxella."], ["Throat and lungs", "It treats strep throat and some bronchitis flare-ups."], ["Skin", "It kills strep and staph in uncomplicated skin infections."], ["Gut", "Gut flora changes cause diarrhea, and in children diaper rash can occur from yeast overgrowth or loose stools."], ["Liver", "Mild, reversible rises in liver enzymes occur occasionally."]], "journey": "Cefprozil is taken by mouth and is well absorbed, with or without food. It reaches peak levels in about one and a half hours and spreads into tissues such as the middle ear fluid and tonsils. It is barely metabolized. About 60 percent leaves unchanged in the urine. Its half-life is a little over an hour, longer in kidney failure.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from killing normal bacteria: diarrhea, rarely C. difficile colitis, and yeast overgrowth. Allergic reactions arise when the beta-lactam binds proteins and triggers an immune response. Dizziness is reported but is uncommon. There is no antidote; anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine.", "fun": "Cefprozil exists as a mix of two geometric forms, cis and trans, with the cis form making up about 90 percent and doing most of the work.", "src": ["Cefprozil FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "cefaclor": {"origin": "Cefaclor is part of the cephalosporin family that began with Giuseppe Brotzu's mold in Sardinia in 1945 and Oxford's work on cephalosporin C in the 1950s. Eli Lilly, which had already made cephalothin and cephalexin, created cefaclor in the 1970s by modifying the cephalexin structure, swapping a methyl group for a chlorine atom. The result had better activity against Haemophilus influenzae. The FDA approved it as Ceclor in 1979, and it became one of the most prescribed antibiotics for children's ear infections in the 1980s. It also became known for causing serum sickness-like reactions in children more often than other beta-lactams. The brand is discontinued, but generics and an extended-release tablet remain.", "timeline": [["1945", "Giuseppe Brotzu discovers the cephalosporin-producing mold."], ["1970s", "Eli Lilly develops cefaclor from the cephalexin structure."], ["1979", "FDA approves cefaclor as Ceclor."]], "cell": "Cefaclor is a beta-lactam. It binds and acylates penicillin-binding proteins, the transpeptidases that cross-link peptidoglycan in the bacterial cell wall, because its beta-lactam ring mimics the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of the wall-building peptide. With cross-linking blocked, the wall weakens, autolysins keep cutting, and the bacterium bursts. The chlorine at the 3 position improves activity against H. influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis, but cefaclor is more easily broken down by some beta-lactamases than other second-generation drugs. It covers streptococci, methicillin-susceptible S. aureus and some gram-negative rods, but not MRSA, enterococci or Pseudomonas.", "body": [["Ears and sinuses", "It treats otitis media and sinusitis from common respiratory bacteria."], ["Throat and lungs", "It treats strep throat and some lower respiratory infections."], ["Skin and urinary tract", "It treats some skin infections and bladder infections."], ["Immune system", "It is linked to serum sickness-like reactions in children, with rash, joint pain and fever about a week into treatment."], ["Gut", "Altered gut bacteria cause diarrhea, and rarely C. difficile infection."]], "journey": "Cefaclor is taken by mouth and absorbed quickly, reaching peak levels within about half an hour to an hour. It spreads into most tissues. It is not significantly metabolized, and most of it leaves unchanged in the urine. Its half-life is short, under one hour, which is why the immediate-release form is given several times a day. The extended-release tablet slows absorption to stretch the effect.", "sidefx": "The serum sickness-like reaction is thought to come from the way some people's liver metabolizes cefaclor into reactive products that injure tissues and trigger inflammation, rather than a classic antibody-complex reaction. True allergic reactions come from beta-lactam binding to proteins. Diarrhea and C. difficile reflect disruption of gut bacteria. Symptoms of serum sickness-like reactions fade after the drug is stopped. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Cefaclor is the cephalosporin most often blamed for serum sickness-like reactions, which is why many pediatric references single it out.", "src": ["Cefaclor FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "cefixime": {"origin": "Cefixime was discovered by the Japanese company Fujisawa Pharmaceutical in the early 1980s, as part of a push to create oral third-generation cephalosporins with strong gram-negative activity. Like all cephalosporins, it descends from the mold Giuseppe Brotzu found in Sardinia in 1945. The FDA approved cefixime as Suprax in 1989. It became important for gonorrhea, since a single oral dose could cure it. As Neisseria gonorrhoeae grew less susceptible, the CDC stopped recommending cefixime as a first-line gonorrhea treatment in 2012, preferring injected ceftriaxone. Cefixime is still used for urinary and ear infections and as an oral step-down option.", "timeline": [["1945", "Giuseppe Brotzu discovers the cephalosporin-producing mold."], ["1980s", "Fujisawa in Japan develops cefixime."], ["1989", "FDA approves cefixime as Suprax."], ["2012", "CDC stops recommending cefixime as first-line gonorrhea therapy."]], "cell": "Cefixime is a beta-lactam that binds penicillin-binding proteins, especially PBP3 in gram-negative rods, which is needed to build the cross wall during cell division. Its beta-lactam ring mimics the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of the peptidoglycan precursor, so the transpeptidase becomes acylated and cannot cross-link the wall. The weakened wall and continued autolysin activity cause the cell to lyse. Its aminothiazole oxime side chain makes it stable against many common plasmid beta-lactamases, but not against extended-spectrum beta-lactamases or AmpC enzymes. It is strong against E. coli, Proteus mirabilis, H. influenzae, Moraxella, Streptococcus pyogenes and N. gonorrhoeae, but weak against S. aureus and inactive against Pseudomonas and enterococci.", "body": [["Urinary tract", "It treats uncomplicated urinary infections from E. coli and Proteus."], ["Ears, throat and lungs", "It treats otitis media, pharyngitis and bronchitis from H. influenzae, Moraxella and strep."], ["Genital tract", "It kills N. gonorrhoeae, though rising resistance has limited this use."], ["Gut", "Because a good part stays in the gut and leaves in bile, diarrhea and loose stools are common, and C. difficile can follow."], ["Skin and immune system", "Rash and, rarely, serum sickness-like reactions or severe skin reactions can occur."]], "journey": "Cefixime is taken by mouth, but only about 40 to 50 percent is absorbed, slowly, peaking several hours later. It is not metabolized. About half of the absorbed drug leaves unchanged in the urine, and much of the rest goes out in bile. The half-life is about three to four hours, longer than many oral beta-lactams, which allows once-daily dosing. Kidney failure lengthens it.", "sidefx": "The unabsorbed drug and biliary excretion put high levels in the bowel, which explains frequent diarrhea, gas and abdominal pain, and the risk of C. difficile. Allergic reactions come from beta-lactam-protein complexes triggering the immune system. Rare severe skin reactions, such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome, are immune-mediated. There is no antidote.", "fun": "For years a single swallowed dose of cefixime was a standard gonorrhea cure, until the bacteria's resistance trends pushed guidelines back to injections.", "src": ["Suprax (cefixime) FDA label", "CDC Update to Sexually Transmitted Diseases Treatment Guidelines, MMWR 2012", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "neomycin": {"origin": "Neomycin was discovered in 1949 by Selman Waksman and his student Hubert Lechevalier at Rutgers University. Waksman's lab had already found streptomycin, the first drug for tuberculosis, by systematically screening soil microbes. Neomycin came from Streptomyces fradiae, a soil actinomycete. It proved too toxic to the kidneys and ears for routine injection, but because it is so poorly absorbed from the gut and skin it found lasting use in topical antibiotic creams, in bowel preparation before colon surgery, and in lowering ammonia-producing gut bacteria in hepatic encephalopathy. Waksman received the Nobel Prize in 1952 for streptomycin.", "timeline": [["1949", "Waksman and Lechevalier at Rutgers discover neomycin in Streptomyces fradiae."], ["1952", "Selman Waksman receives the Nobel Prize for streptomycin."], ["1950s", "Neomycin becomes widely used in topical and oral products."]], "cell": "Neomycin is an aminoglycoside. Its positive charges let it bind the negatively charged outer membrane of gram-negative bacteria and then move inside through an oxygen-dependent transport process, which is why it does not work well against anaerobes. Inside, it binds the 16S rRNA of the 30S ribosomal subunit near the decoding A site. This makes the ribosome misread the messenger RNA, so faulty proteins are made. Some of these misfolded proteins insert into the cell membrane and make it leaky, letting even more drug in, and the bacterium dies. When given orally, the drug stays in the gut, where it reduces urease-producing aerobic bacteria that make ammonia, a toxin that contributes to hepatic encephalopathy.", "body": [["Gut", "Local killing of aerobic gut bacteria lowers ammonia production in the colon and reduces bacterial load before bowel surgery."], ["Brain (liver disease)", "Less ammonia reaching the blood can improve confusion from hepatic encephalopathy."], ["Intestinal lining", "Neomycin can damage the intestinal lining and bind bile acids, causing malabsorption of fats and some drugs."], ["Ears", "Absorbed drug can damage cochlear hair cells, causing permanent hearing loss and tinnitus."], ["Kidneys", "Absorbed drug can injure proximal tubule cells, causing kidney damage."], ["Muscles and breathing", "High levels can block acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction, leading to weakness or respiratory paralysis."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth, only about 3 percent of neomycin is absorbed, so almost all of it stays in the gut and leaves in the feces unchanged. The small absorbed amount is not metabolized and is cleared by the kidneys with a half-life of a few hours. Absorption rises when the gut lining is inflamed or ulcerated, and kidney failure lets the absorbed drug build up, which raises the risk of toxicity.", "sidefx": "In the gut, killing normal bacteria causes diarrhea and can allow C. difficile or other superinfections, and damage to the intestinal lining causes malabsorption. The serious toxicities come from absorbed drug. Aminoglycosides concentrate in inner ear hair cells and kidney tubule cells, where they generate reactive oxygen species and cause cell death, so ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity are boxed warnings. They also block presynaptic calcium entry at the neuromuscular junction; calcium can partly reverse this blockade.", "fun": "Waksman coined the modern use of the word antibiotic to describe chemicals made by microbes that kill other microbes.", "src": ["Neomycin sulfate tablets FDA label", "Waksman SA, Lechevalier HA. Science, 1949", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "delafloxacin": {"origin": "Fluoroquinolones grew from nalidixic acid, a byproduct found in 1962 by George Lesher and colleagues at Sterling-Winthrop while making the antimalarial chloroquine. Adding fluorine created drugs like ciprofloxacin and levofloxacin. Delafloxacin was designed later to fix a weakness: most fluoroquinolones lose power in acidic places such as abscesses, where MRSA often lives. It was first made in Japan and later developed by several companies before Melinta Therapeutics brought it to approval. The FDA approved it as Baxdela in 2017 for acute bacterial skin and skin structure infections, and in 2019 for community-acquired bacterial pneumonia.", "timeline": [["1962", "Nalidixic acid, the first quinolone, is discovered at Sterling-Winthrop."], ["1980s", "Fluorinated quinolones such as ciprofloxacin enter practice."], ["2017", "FDA approves delafloxacin (Baxdela) for skin infections."], ["2019", "FDA adds community-acquired bacterial pneumonia to the label."]], "cell": "Delafloxacin inhibits two bacterial type II topoisomerases: DNA gyrase (topoisomerase II), which relieves supercoiling ahead of the replication fork, and topoisomerase IV, which separates daughter chromosomes after replication. The drug traps each enzyme on DNA after it has cut both strands, forming a stable drug-enzyme-DNA complex. These stalled complexes block replication and become permanent double-strand breaks, so the bacterium dies. Delafloxacin hits both enzymes about equally, which makes it harder for a single mutation to cause resistance. Unlike other fluoroquinolones, which are zwitterions, it has no basic group at the C-7 position, so it is anionic. In an acidic environment it becomes uncharged, passes into bacteria more easily, and builds up, which boosts its killing of S. aureus, including MRSA, in abscesses.", "body": [["Skin and soft tissue", "It kills MRSA, streptococci and gram-negative bacteria in cellulitis, wounds and abscesses."], ["Lungs", "It treats community-acquired pneumonia from S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae and atypical organisms."], ["Tendons", "Like all fluoroquinolones, it can inflame or rupture tendons, especially the Achilles, in older adults or those on steroids."], ["Nerves and brain", "It can cause peripheral neuropathy, which may be permanent, and central nervous system effects such as dizziness or confusion."], ["Blood sugar", "It can cause high or low blood sugar, especially in people with diabetes."], ["Gut", "Nausea and diarrhea are common, and C. difficile infection can occur."]], "journey": "Delafloxacin can be given IV or by mouth, with oral bioavailability of about 60 percent. Peak levels come about an hour after a tablet. It spreads into skin and lung tissue. It is mainly broken down by glucuronidation (UGT1A1, UGT1A3 and UGT2B15), with little CYP involvement, so it has fewer CYP drug interactions. Elimination is through the kidneys and feces, with a half-life of roughly four to eight hours. Like other quinolones, its oral absorption drops with antacids, iron or calcium.", "sidefx": "Fluoroquinolone toxicities share a class pattern. Tendon damage is thought to come from chelation of magnesium and direct toxicity to tendon cells and matrix. Neuropathy and central nervous system effects involve blockade of GABA-A receptors and nerve injury. Dysglycemia relates to effects on pancreatic beta-cell potassium channels. Aortic aneurysm risk may come from collagen breakdown in vessel walls. Unlike several older quinolones, delafloxacin has not shown meaningful QT prolongation in studies. The boxed warning covers tendinitis, neuropathy, CNS effects and myasthenia gravis worsening. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Delafloxacin's lack of a positive charge makes it one of the few fluoroquinolones that work better, not worse, in acidic infections.", "src": ["Baxdela (delafloxacin) FDA label", "Lesher GY et al. J Med Pharm Chem, 1962", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "tetracycline": {"origin": "The tetracyclines came out of the great soil-screening hunts of the 1940s. In 1948 Benjamin Duggar at Lederle Laboratories reported chlortetracycline (Aureomycin) from a golden-colored soil bacterium, Streptomyces aureofaciens. Pfizer soon found oxytetracycline (Terramycin). In the early 1950s Lloyd Conover at Pfizer removed the chlorine from chlortetracycline by catalytic hydrogenation and found that the resulting molecule, tetracycline, was itself a strong antibiotic. This was a turning point: it showed that natural antibiotics could be chemically modified into better drugs, and tetracycline was patented in 1955. Tetracycline became one of the most prescribed broad-spectrum antibiotics in the world. The Sumycin brand is gone, but generics remain.", "timeline": [["1948", "Benjamin Duggar at Lederle reports chlortetracycline from Streptomyces aureofaciens."], ["1950", "Pfizer introduces oxytetracycline (Terramycin)."], ["1953", "Lloyd Conover at Pfizer makes tetracycline by modifying chlortetracycline."], ["1955", "Tetracycline is patented and widely marketed."]], "cell": "Tetracycline enters gram-negative bacteria through porin channels and then crosses the inner membrane by an energy-dependent transport system; gram-positive bacteria take it up by active transport too. Human cells do not concentrate it, which gives it selective toxicity. Inside, it binds the 16S rRNA of the 30S ribosomal subunit and blocks aminoacyl-tRNA from docking at the A site of the mRNA-ribosome complex. Without new amino acids being added, the protein chain stops growing. The effect is reversible and bacteriostatic. Resistance mainly comes from tet efflux pumps, such as Tet(A) and Tet(K), that push the drug out, and from ribosomal protection proteins, such as Tet(M), that knock the drug off the ribosome. Tetracyclines also have anti-inflammatory effects, including lower neutrophil migration and matrix metalloproteinase activity.", "body": [["Infections inside cells", "It reaches organisms that live inside cells, such as rickettsiae, chlamydiae and mycoplasma, which explains its use for Rocky Mountain spotted fever and atypical infections."], ["Skin", "It lowers Cutibacterium acnes and calms inflammation in acne, but it also causes photosensitivity with exaggerated sunburn."], ["Teeth and bones", "It binds calcium in growing teeth and bones, causing permanent gray-brown staining of teeth in children under about 8 and in fetuses."], ["Esophagus", "A pill that sticks in the esophagus can cause painful erosions, especially when taken without enough water or before lying down."], ["Kidneys", "It has an antianabolic effect that can raise blood urea, a problem in kidney disease, and outdated tetracycline once caused a Fanconi-like kidney injury."], ["Brain", "It can raise intracranial pressure (pseudotumor cerebri), causing headache and vision changes."]], "journey": "Tetracycline is taken by mouth on an empty stomach and is moderately absorbed in the upper small intestine. Calcium, magnesium, aluminum, iron and dairy bind it into insoluble chelates and greatly lower absorption. It spreads widely into tissues and crosses the placenta. It is not metabolized much. It leaves mainly unchanged in urine and also in feces, with a half-life of about six to eleven hours, longer in kidney failure.", "sidefx": "The calcium-chelating chemistry that blocks absorption also explains tooth staining and deposits in growing bone. Photosensitivity happens when drug in the skin absorbs UV light and creates reactive oxygen species. Gut upset reflects direct irritation and changes in gut flora, and C. difficile can follow. Esophageal ulcers come from the acidic tablet dissolving against the lining. High IV doses in pregnancy historically caused fatal fatty liver. Degraded old tablets formed toxic products that injured the kidneys. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Bones of people from ancient Sudanese Nubia show fluorescent tetracycline deposits, likely from beer made with grain contaminated by Streptomyces.", "src": ["Tetracycline hydrochloride FDA label", "Duggar BM. Ann N Y Acad Sci, 1948", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "omadacycline": {"origin": "Omadacycline was created to beat the two main ways bacteria resist tetracyclines: pumping the drug out and shielding the ribosome. It was discovered at Paratek Pharmaceuticals, a Boston company cofounded by Stuart Levy of Tufts University, a leading researcher on tetracycline resistance. Chemists started from minocycline and added an aminomethyl group at the C-9 position, creating a new subclass called aminomethylcyclines. After years of development, the FDA approved omadacycline as Nuzyra in 2018, in both IV and oral forms, for community-acquired bacterial pneumonia and acute bacterial skin and skin structure infections.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Minocycline, the parent of omadacycline, is developed."], ["1996", "Paratek Pharmaceuticals is founded to study tetracycline resistance."], ["2018", "FDA approves omadacycline (Nuzyra) IV and oral for pneumonia and skin infections."]], "cell": "Omadacycline binds the 16S rRNA of the bacterial 30S ribosomal subunit and blocks aminoacyl-tRNA from entering the A site, so protein chains cannot grow. Its C-9 aminomethyl side chain lets it keep binding even when bacteria carry tet(M) ribosomal protection proteins, which normally knock tetracyclines off the ribosome. The same change makes it a poor substrate for common efflux pumps such as tet(K) and tet(A). This restores activity against many tetracycline-resistant organisms. It is generally bacteriostatic. It covers MRSA, penicillin-resistant S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae, many Enterobacterales, and atypical bacteria such as Legionella, Mycoplasma and Chlamydophila, but not Pseudomonas or Proteus.", "body": [["Lungs", "It treats community-acquired pneumonia, including from penicillin-resistant pneumococcus and atypical bacteria."], ["Skin and soft tissue", "It treats skin infections from MRSA and streptococci."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are common, especially with the oral loading dose, and C. difficile can occur."], ["Teeth and bones", "Like other tetracyclines, it can stain developing teeth and slow bone growth in the second half of pregnancy and in young children."], ["Skin", "It can cause photosensitivity, so sun exposure can lead to an exaggerated sunburn."], ["Blood vessels", "Raised blood pressure has been reported in trials."]], "journey": "It can be given IV or by mouth. Oral bioavailability is only about one third and drops with food, dairy, antacids and multivalent cations, so tablets are taken fasting. It spreads widely into tissues, including lung fluid. It is not metabolized significantly. Most of an oral dose leaves unchanged in the feces, with a smaller part in urine. Its half-life is about 16 hours, which supports once-daily dosing and means no dose change for kidney or liver impairment.", "sidefx": "Nausea and vomiting reflect direct gut irritation and are a class effect of newer tetracyclines. Tooth staining and bone effects come from calcium chelation. Photosensitivity comes from drug in the skin reacting with UV light. In the pneumonia trial, more deaths occurred with omadacycline than with moxifloxacin, though the cause was not clear, and this is listed as a warning. Disruption of gut flora can lead to C. difficile. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Stuart Levy, whose research inspired the company behind omadacycline, also founded the Alliance for the Prudent Use of Antibiotics.", "src": ["Nuzyra (omadacycline) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "sarecycline": {"origin": "Sarecycline was designed as a narrow-spectrum tetracycline for acne. Doctors had used doxycycline and minocycline for decades, but these broad-spectrum drugs also disturb gut bacteria and can encourage resistance. Paratek Pharmaceuticals discovered sarecycline by attaching a large side chain to the C-7 position of the tetracycline core. Allergan licensed it for acne, and the FDA approved it as Seysara in 2018 for inflammatory lesions of non-nodular moderate to severe acne in people 9 years and older. It was the first new tetracycline approved specifically for acne. Almirall later acquired the US rights.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Tetracyclines first come into use for acne."], ["2018", "FDA approves sarecycline (Seysara) for moderate to severe acne."]], "cell": "Sarecycline binds the 30S ribosomal subunit and blocks aminoacyl-tRNA from docking at the A site, stopping protein synthesis in bacteria. Its long C-7 side chain reaches into the messenger RNA channel of the ribosome, where it adds a second interference with the mRNA and tRNA, which strengthens binding in gram-positive bacteria like Cutibacterium acnes. It is much less active against many gram-negative gut bacteria, so it causes less disruption of the gut microbiome than older tetracyclines. Like all tetracyclines, it also has anti-inflammatory actions: it reduces neutrophil chemotaxis and matrix metalloproteinase activity, which lowers redness and swelling in acne lesions.", "body": [["Skin (acne)", "Fewer C. acnes bacteria and less inflammation mean fewer red papules and pustules over several weeks."], ["Skin (sun)", "Photosensitivity can cause easy sunburn."], ["Gut", "Nausea can occur, but its narrow spectrum is meant to spare more of the normal gut bacteria."], ["Vagina", "Changes in normal flora can allow yeast overgrowth (vulvovaginal candidiasis)."], ["Brain", "Like other tetracyclines, it can rarely cause raised intracranial pressure with headache and vision changes."], ["Teeth and bones", "It can stain developing teeth and is avoided in pregnancy."]], "journey": "Sarecycline is taken by mouth once daily, with or without food. It reaches peak levels in about one and a half to two hours. It is moderately protein bound and reaches the skin and sebaceous areas. It undergoes little metabolism. It leaves largely unchanged, mostly in feces and some in urine. Its half-life is long, about 21 to 22 hours. Dairy, antacids and iron can reduce its absorption as with other tetracyclines.", "sidefx": "Photosensitivity occurs because drug in the skin absorbs UV light and makes reactive oxygen species. Calcium chelation explains effects on developing teeth and bone. Killing normal bacteria lets Candida overgrow, causing yeast infections. Idiopathic intracranial hypertension is a class effect, thought to involve changes in fluid absorption in the brain. C. difficile can occur. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Sarecycline's side chain is the longest of any approved tetracycline at the C-7 position, and it was the first tetracycline shown to reach into the ribosome's mRNA channel.", "src": ["Seysara (sarecycline) FDA label", "Batool Z et al. Nat Commun, 2020"]}, "bezlotoxumab": {"origin": "Clostridioides difficile harms the colon through two toxins, toxin A and toxin B. Researchers at MassBiologics of the University of Massachusetts Medical School, working with Medarex, used mice carrying human antibody genes to make fully human antibodies against these toxins. Merck licensed the antibodies and tested them in the large MODIFY trials. The antibody against toxin A (actoxumab) added no benefit, but the antibody against toxin B (bezlotoxumab) lowered the rate of C. difficile coming back. The FDA approved bezlotoxumab as Zinplava in 2016 to reduce recurrence in adults receiving antibiotic treatment for C. difficile infection who are at high risk of recurrence. Merck discontinued US marketing of Zinplava in 2025, citing business reasons rather than a safety concern.", "timeline": [["1978", "C. difficile is identified as a cause of antibiotic-associated colitis."], ["2016", "FDA approves bezlotoxumab (Zinplava) to reduce C. difficile recurrence."], ["2025", "Merck discontinues US marketing of Zinplava."]], "cell": "C. difficile toxin B (TcdB) is a large glucosyltransferase. It binds receptors on colon epithelial cells, is taken in by endocytosis, and then moves into the cytoplasm, where it attaches glucose to Rho family GTPases such as Rho, Rac and Cdc42. This inactivates them, collapses the actin cytoskeleton, opens tight junctions, kills cells and triggers intense inflammation. Bezlotoxumab is a fully human IgG1 monoclonal antibody that binds the combined repetitive oligopeptides (CROPs) domain of toxin B at two sites. This blocks the toxin from binding colon cells, so it cannot enter them. It neutralizes toxin but does not kill bacteria, does not bind toxin A, and does not treat the current episode; it is given alongside antibiotics.", "body": [["Colon", "Neutralizing toxin B while gut bacteria recover lowers the chance of a new C. difficile episode over the following weeks."], ["Gut microbiome", "It gives the normal flora time to recover after antibiotics, since it does not disturb gut bacteria itself."], ["Heart", "In trials, heart failure was reported more often in people with existing heart failure, so it is used with caution in that group."], ["Whole body", "Infusion-related reactions such as nausea, fever and headache can occur."]], "journey": "Bezlotoxumab is given once as an IV infusion during antibiotic treatment for C. difficile. It stays mostly in the blood and extracellular fluid. Some reaches the gut lining and lumen, where it meets the toxin. Like other IgG antibodies, it is broken down slowly by protein catabolism into amino acids, and it is protected from rapid breakdown by the neonatal Fc receptor. Its half-life is about 19 days, long enough to cover the roughly 12-week window when recurrence is most likely.", "sidefx": "Infusion reactions come from the body's response to a large protein given into a vein. The higher rate of heart failure events in people who already had heart failure is not fully explained. Because the antibody only neutralizes toxin, it does not cause the gut flora disruption seen with antibiotics. There is no reversal agent.", "fun": "Bezlotoxumab was one of the first monoclonal antibodies aimed at a bacterial toxin rather than at a human target or a live microbe.", "src": ["Zinplava (bezlotoxumab) FDA label", "Wilcox MH et al. MODIFY I and II. N Engl J Med, 2017"]}, "oritavancin": {"origin": "Oritavancin grew out of vancomycin chemistry. In the 1990s scientists at Eli Lilly modified chloroeremomycin, a natural glycopeptide related to vancomycin, by adding a large fat-loving chlorobiphenyl side chain. This created a lipoglycopeptide that clung to bacterial membranes and lasted very long in the body. The compound was then developed by several other companies, and The Medicines Company won FDA approval for it as Orbactiv in 2014 for acute bacterial skin and skin structure infections. In 2021 a reformulated version, Kimyrsa, was approved with a shorter one-hour infusion. Its main appeal is that a single infusion can treat an entire skin infection.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Vancomycin is discovered at Eli Lilly from a soil organism."], ["1990s", "Lilly scientists create oritavancin from chloroeremomycin."], ["2014", "FDA approves oritavancin (Orbactiv) for skin infections."], ["2021", "FDA approves Kimyrsa, a one-hour infusion formulation."]], "cell": "Oritavancin attacks the gram-positive cell wall in three ways. First, like vancomycin, it binds the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of lipid II, the peptidoglycan building block, and blocks transglycosylation (chain building). Second, it binds the pentaglycyl bridge of peptidoglycan, blocking transpeptidation (cross-linking); this second binding site helps it keep activity against some vancomycin-resistant enterococci. Third, its lipophilic side chain anchors in the bacterial membrane, disrupts it, and causes rapid depolarization and leakage. Together these give rapid, concentration-dependent killing of MRSA, streptococci and enterococci. It cannot penetrate the outer membrane of gram-negative bacteria.", "body": [["Skin and soft tissue", "One infusion kills MRSA, streptococci and enterococci in cellulitis, abscesses and wound infections."], ["Bone", "Osteomyelitis was reported more often than with vancomycin in trials, so new bone infection is a warning."], ["Skin (infusion)", "Rapid infusion can trigger flushing, itching and hives, similar to the red man reaction of vancomycin."], ["Blood tests", "It binds the phospholipid reagents in some clotting tests, falsely prolonging aPTT and PT/INR for days, without changing real clotting."], ["Gut", "Nausea and diarrhea occur, and C. difficile can follow."]], "journey": "Oritavancin is given only by IV infusion. It is highly protein bound and spreads widely into tissues, where it is taken up and slowly released. It is not metabolized. Elimination is extremely slow, with tiny amounts appearing in urine and feces over weeks, and a terminal half-life of about 245 hours (about 10 days). This long tail lets one dose cover a full course. It is a weak inhibitor or inducer of several CYP enzymes, which can matter for warfarin.", "sidefx": "Infusion reactions likely come from histamine release by mast cells, similar to vancomycin. False lab results happen because the drug sticks to the phospholipid reagents used in coagulation tests, so heparin monitoring by aPTT is unreliable for days after a dose. Hypersensitivity can occur and may cross-react with other glycopeptides. Because its effect lasts so long, adverse effects cannot be stopped quickly. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Oritavancin's terminal half-life is so long that a single infusion is still measurable in the body weeks later.", "src": ["Orbactiv (oritavancin) FDA label", "Kimyrsa (oritavancin) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "pretomanid": {"origin": "Pretomanid began as PA-824, one of a series of nitroimidazooxazines made in the 1990s by PathoGenesis Corporation, building on earlier nitroimidazole compounds that had shown activity against tuberculosis. The nonprofit TB Alliance (Global Alliance for TB Drug Development) took over development in 2002, aiming to shorten and simplify treatment for drug-resistant TB. In the Nix-TB trial, a six-month, all-oral combination of bedaquiline, pretomanid and linezolid (BPaL) cured most people with highly drug-resistant TB, who previously faced years of toxic injections. The FDA approved pretomanid in 2019 as part of BPaL, making it only the third new TB drug approved in the US in over 40 years and the first developed and registered by a nonprofit. Viatris (formerly Mylan) makes it.", "timeline": [["1990s", "PathoGenesis Corporation creates PA-824 (pretomanid)."], ["2002", "TB Alliance takes over development."], ["2019", "FDA approves pretomanid as part of the BPaL regimen for highly drug-resistant TB."]], "cell": "Pretomanid is a prodrug. Inside Mycobacterium tuberculosis it is activated by Ddn, a deazaflavin (F420)-dependent nitroreductase that does not exist in human cells. Activation works through two paths. In actively dividing bacteria, its products block the synthesis of ketomycolates, a type of mycolic acid needed for the waxy mycobacterial cell wall, so the wall cannot be built. In low-oxygen, nondividing bacteria, activation releases reactive nitrogen species including nitric oxide, which poisons the respiratory chain and depletes energy. That dual action lets it kill both replicating and dormant bacilli, which matters because dormant TB is hard to sterilize. Resistance arises from mutations in ddn or in genes needed to make F420.", "body": [["Lungs", "As part of BPaL it kills both active and dormant TB bacilli, allowing a much shorter all-oral course for drug-resistant TB."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral neuropathy is common in the regimen, largely driven by linezolid, which pretomanid accompanies."], ["Eyes", "Optic neuropathy can occur, mainly from linezolid in the regimen."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises and hepatotoxicity occur, so liver tests are monitored."], ["Blood", "Anemia and low blood counts reflect linezolid's bone marrow effects within the regimen."], ["Heart", "QT prolongation can occur, especially combined with bedaquiline."]], "journey": "Pretomanid is taken by mouth once daily with food, which improves absorption. Peak levels come in about four to five hours. It is highly protein bound and spreads into tissues. It is broken down by several reductive and oxidative pathways, with CYP3A4 handling about 20 percent, so strong CYP3A4 inducers like rifampin lower its levels. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. The half-life is about 17 hours.", "sidefx": "Many side effects in BPaL come from the combination. Neuropathy, optic neuropathy, anemia and low platelets come mainly from linezolid inhibiting mitochondrial protein synthesis in human cells. Liver injury is seen with pretomanid and bedaquiline and can be serious, so alcohol and other liver toxins are avoided. QT prolongation adds up with bedaquiline. Lactic acidosis can occur from linezolid's mitochondrial toxicity. Animal studies showed testicular toxicity. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Before BPaL, treatment for extensively drug-resistant TB often took 18 months or more and included daily injections; BPaL cut that to six months of pills.", "src": ["Pretomanid Tablets FDA label", "Conradie F et al. Nix-TB. N Engl J Med, 2020", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "bedaquiline": {"origin": "Bedaquiline was found by a team led by Koen Andries at Janssen, the pharmaceutical arm of Johnson & Johnson, in Belgium. They screened compounds directly against live mycobacteria and found a new chemical class, the diarylquinolines. In 2005 they reported in Science that the lead compound, then called TMC207, killed TB by blocking a target no TB drug had used before, ATP synthase. The FDA granted accelerated approval to bedaquiline as Sirturo in December 2012 for multidrug-resistant TB, the first new TB drug with a new mechanism in about 40 years. It later became the backbone of short all-oral regimens such as BPaL, replacing injectable drugs in WHO guidance.", "timeline": [["2005", "Andries and colleagues at Janssen report TMC207 and its ATP synthase target in Science."], ["2012", "FDA grants accelerated approval to bedaquiline (Sirturo) for multidrug-resistant TB."], ["2019", "Bedaquiline is approved as part of the BPaL regimen with pretomanid and linezolid."]], "cell": "Bedaquiline binds the c subunit (AtpE) of the F0 rotor ring of mycobacterial ATP synthase. ATP synthase works like a turbine: protons flow through the membrane portion and turn the c-ring, which drives ATP production in the F1 head. Bedaquiline jams the c-ring so it cannot rotate, stopping proton flow and ATP synthesis. Without ATP, the bacteria run out of energy and die, including dormant, nonreplicating bacilli that still need ATP to survive. It also appears to uncouple the membrane, adding to energy collapse. It has much lower affinity for human mitochondrial ATP synthase, which gives it selectivity. Resistance comes from atpE mutations or from mutations in Rv0678 that increase the MmpL5 efflux pump, which also lowers clofazimine activity.", "body": [["Lungs", "It kills both active and dormant TB bacilli, which speeds culture conversion and allows shorter, all-oral regimens."], ["Heart", "It prolongs the QT interval, and the effect adds with other QT drugs such as clofazimine, moxifloxacin and pretomanid."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises can occur, and liver tests are monitored."], ["Joints and head", "Joint pain and headache are common side effects."], ["Pancreas", "Pancreatitis has been reported, so severe abdominal pain during treatment is taken seriously."]], "journey": "Bedaquiline is taken by mouth with food, which roughly doubles absorption. Peak levels come in about five hours. It is extremely protein bound and very fat-soluble, so it builds up in tissues. CYP3A4 converts it to an N-monodesmethyl metabolite (M2), which is less active but contributes to QT effects. It leaves very slowly, mostly in the feces, with a terminal half-life of about five and a half months as it seeps back out of tissues. That long tail means levels persist long after stopping.", "sidefx": "QT prolongation comes from blockade of cardiac hERG potassium channels by bedaquiline and its M2 metabolite, so ECGs and potassium, magnesium and calcium are monitored. Strong CYP3A4 inducers like rifampin lower its levels, and inhibitors raise them. Liver injury occurs, especially with alcohol or other liver toxins. In the early trial, more deaths occurred in the bedaquiline group, a finding that was never explained. Because it lingers for months, side effects may not resolve quickly. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Bedaquiline was found by testing compounds on whole Mycobacterium smegmatis cells, a fast-growing cousin of TB, rather than on a purified enzyme.", "src": ["Sirturo (bedaquiline) FDA label", "Andries K et al. Science, 2005", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "rifapentine": {"origin": "Rifapentine is a member of the rifamycin family discovered at Lepetit in Milan in 1957. Chemists added a cyclopentyl ring to the rifampin structure, which made the molecule more fat-soluble and gave it a much longer half-life. The FDA approved it as Priftin in 1998 for pulmonary TB. Its long half-life made weekly dosing possible. In 2011 a large CDC-led trial showed that 12 once-weekly doses of rifapentine plus isoniazid (3HP) worked as well as nine months of daily isoniazid to prevent TB in people with latent infection, with better completion. The FDA approved this use in 2014. In 2021 another trial showed a four-month daily regimen with rifapentine and moxifloxacin could treat active drug-susceptible TB, shorter than the standard six months.", "timeline": [["1957", "The rifamycin family is discovered at Lepetit in Milan."], ["1998", "FDA approves rifapentine (Priftin) for pulmonary TB."], ["2011", "A CDC-led trial shows weekly rifapentine plus isoniazid prevents TB."], ["2014", "FDA approves rifapentine with isoniazid for latent TB infection."], ["2021", "A trial shows a four-month rifapentine and moxifloxacin regimen treats active TB."]], "cell": "Rifapentine binds the beta subunit (rpoB) of bacterial DNA-dependent RNA polymerase. It sits in the channel where the growing RNA chain exits and physically blocks elongation after the first few nucleotides, so the bacterium cannot make messenger RNA and therefore cannot make proteins. This kills M. tuberculosis, including slowly metabolizing bacilli. Human RNA polymerase is not affected. Its cyclopentyl ring increases fat solubility, cell penetration and half-life. Esterases convert it to 25-desacetyl rifapentine, which is also active. Resistance arises from point mutations in rpoB, usually in an 81-base-pair hotspot, and gives cross-resistance with rifampin. Like rifampin, it strongly induces liver enzymes through the pregnane X receptor.", "body": [["Lungs and latent infection", "It kills TB bacilli, which allows weekly treatment of latent TB and shorter treatment of active TB in combination regimens."], ["Body fluids", "Its red-orange color stains urine, sweat, tears and saliva, and can permanently stain soft contact lenses."], ["Liver and drug interactions", "It induces CYP3A4, CYP2C8/9 and P-glycoprotein, lowering levels of many drugs, including hormonal contraceptives, warfarin and many HIV drugs."], ["Liver", "It can cause liver enzyme rises and, rarely, serious liver injury, especially combined with isoniazid."], ["Immune system", "Weekly dosing can cause a flu-like illness or hypersensitivity with low blood pressure or fainting."], ["Blood", "It can lower white blood cell counts, especially neutrophils, during treatment."]], "journey": "Rifapentine is taken by mouth, and a meal improves absorption. Peak levels come in about five to six hours. It is highly protein bound and spreads widely into tissues and inside cells. Esterases convert it to the active 25-desacetyl metabolite. Both forms leave mainly in the feces, with a small amount in urine. Its half-life is about 13 to 17 hours, several times longer than rifampin's. It induces its own metabolism to some degree.", "sidefx": "Orange body fluids come from the drug's natural color. Drug interactions come from activation of the pregnane X receptor, which turns on CYP3A4 and other enzymes and transporters, so many co-drugs drop to ineffective levels. The flu-like and hypersensitivity reactions are thought to be immune reactions tied to intermittent dosing. Liver toxicity is a rifamycin class effect and adds with isoniazid. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The 3HP regimen means latent TB can be treated with just 12 doses over three months, compared with 270 daily doses of isoniazid.", "src": ["Priftin (rifapentine) FDA label", "Sterling TR et al. N Engl J Med, 2011", "Dorman SE et al. N Engl J Med, 2021"]}, "isavuconazonium": {"origin": "Isavuconazole, the active drug, came out of Roche research and was then developed by the Swiss company Basilea Pharmaceutica, which later partnered with Astellas. The challenge with azole antifungals for very sick patients was that the IV forms of voriconazole and posaconazole needed a cyclodextrin carrier that builds up in kidney failure. Basilea solved this by making isavuconazonium sulfate, a water-soluble prodrug that does not need cyclodextrin. The FDA approved it as Cresemba in 2015 for invasive aspergillosis and invasive mucormycosis, in both IV and oral forms. It was the first azole approved specifically for mucormycosis. Pediatric use was added in 2023.", "timeline": [["2000", "Basilea Pharmaceutica is founded as a Roche spin-off and takes over isavuconazole."], ["2015", "FDA approves isavuconazonium sulfate (Cresemba) for invasive aspergillosis and mucormycosis."], ["2023", "FDA extends approval to children."]], "cell": "Isavuconazonium has no antifungal activity itself. In the blood, esterases (mainly butylcholinesterase) quickly cleave it to isavuconazole. Isavuconazole is a triazole that binds the heme iron of fungal lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51, the ERG11 gene product). This enzyme removes a methyl group from lanosterol on the way to ergosterol, the main sterol in fungal cell membranes. Blocking it depletes ergosterol and builds up toxic 14-alpha-methylated sterols, which make the membrane stiff and leaky and disrupt membrane enzymes. The result is fungistatic or fungicidal depending on the organism. It covers Aspergillus, many Mucorales, Candida and Cryptococcus. It moderately inhibits human CYP3A4, which causes drug interactions.", "body": [["Lungs and sinuses", "It treats invasive Aspergillus and Mucorales infections, which often attack the lungs and sinuses of immunocompromised people."], ["Heart", "Unlike most azoles, it shortens the QT interval, so it is avoided in familial short QT syndrome."], ["Liver", "It can raise liver enzymes and, rarely, cause serious liver injury."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are common."], ["Blood chemistry", "Low potassium and peripheral swelling can occur."], ["Drug interactions", "Moderate CYP3A4 inhibition raises levels of tacrolimus, sirolimus and other CYP3A4 drugs."]], "journey": "It can be given IV or by mouth, and oral bioavailability is about 98 percent, so the two are interchangeable. Esterases convert the prodrug to isavuconazole within minutes. Isavuconazole is over 99 percent protein bound and spreads widely into tissues, including the brain. It is metabolized by CYP3A4, CYP3A5 and UGT enzymes, and leaves mainly in the feces. The half-life is very long, about 130 hours, so loading doses are used to reach steady state faster.", "sidefx": "Liver toxicity is an azole class effect from hepatocyte stress. QT shortening likely reflects effects on cardiac ion channels, the opposite of voriconazole and posaconazole, which prolong QT. Drug interactions come from CYP3A4 inhibition and from the fact that strong CYP3A4 inducers like rifampin drop its levels too low. Infusion reactions with low blood pressure and chills can occur. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Isavuconazole was the first azole approved in the US specifically for mucormycosis, a fast-moving mold infection seen in uncontrolled diabetes and severe immune suppression.", "src": ["Cresemba (isavuconazonium sulfate) FDA label", "Maertens JA et al. SECURE trial. Lancet, 2016", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "ibrexafungerp": {"origin": "Ibrexafungerp is a semisynthetic version of enfumafungin, a natural triterpene glycoside that Merck researchers reported around 2000 in a fungus, Hormonema carpetanum, isolated from juniper leaves in Spain. Enfumafungin blocked fungal glucan synthase, the same target as the echinocandins, but echinocandins can only be given IV. SCYNEXIS licensed the chemistry and modified it into an oral drug, creating a new class called triterpenoids or fungerps. The FDA approved it as Brexafemme in 2021 for vulvovaginal candidiasis, the first new antifungal class approved in over 20 years, and in 2022 to reduce recurrent infection. In 2023 the tablets were voluntarily recalled because of a cross-contamination concern at a manufacturing supplier, and availability has been affected since.", "timeline": [["2000", "Merck scientists report enfumafungin from the fungus Hormonema carpetanum."], ["2021", "FDA approves ibrexafungerp (Brexafemme) for vulvovaginal candidiasis."], ["2022", "FDA approves it to reduce recurrent vulvovaginal candidiasis."], ["2023", "The tablets are voluntarily recalled over a manufacturing contamination concern."]], "cell": "Ibrexafungerp blocks beta-(1,3)-D-glucan synthase, the enzyme complex in the fungal cell membrane that builds beta-(1,3)-glucan, the main structural polymer of the Candida cell wall. Its catalytic subunit is encoded by the FKS genes. Ibrexafungerp binds at a site that overlaps with, but is not the same as, the echinocandin site, so some echinocandin-resistant FKS mutants stay susceptible. Without new glucan, the wall weakens and the fungal cell cannot resist osmotic pressure, so it lyses. This is fungicidal against Candida, including many azole-resistant strains such as C. glabrata. Human cells have no cell wall and no glucan synthase, which gives it selectivity.", "body": [["Vagina", "It kills Candida in the vaginal tissue, clearing itching, discharge and irritation of a yeast infection."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea and abdominal pain are the most common side effects."], ["Nervous system", "Dizziness can occur, usually mild and short-lived."], ["Pregnancy", "Animal studies showed fetal harm, so it is contraindicated in pregnancy and pregnancy is ruled out before use."]], "journey": "Ibrexafungerp is taken by mouth and is absorbed well, peaking in about four to six hours, and food improves absorption. It is over 99 percent protein bound and distributes into tissues, including vaginal tissue, at higher levels than in plasma. It is broken down by CYP3A4 hydroxylation followed by glucuronidation and sulfation, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise its levels and strong inducers lower them. It leaves mainly in the feces. Its half-life is about 20 hours.", "sidefx": "Gut side effects likely come from direct exposure in the bowel and are usually mild. The embryo-fetal toxicity warning comes from malformations seen in animal studies, which is why effective contraception is advised. Because glucan synthase does not exist in human cells, organ toxicity is otherwise limited. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Ibrexafungerp's natural parent was found in a fungus living on juniper leaves, so one fungus supplied the template to kill another.", "src": ["Brexafemme (ibrexafungerp) FDA label", "Pelaez F et al. Syst Appl Microbiol, 2000"]}, "oteseconazole": {"origin": "Oteseconazole was designed by Viamet Pharmaceuticals, a North Carolina company, to make an azole antifungal that hits fungal CYP51 without hitting human CYP enzymes. Older azoles use a triazole or imidazole ring whose nitrogen binds tightly to the heme iron of many CYP enzymes, which causes drug interactions and side effects. Viamet replaced it with a tetrazole metal-binding group that binds fungal CYP51 strongly but human enzymes weakly. Mycovia Pharmaceuticals completed development, and the FDA approved it as Vivjoa in 2022 for recurrent vulvovaginal candidiasis. It is limited to people who are not of reproductive potential because of its very long persistence and animal reproductive findings.", "timeline": [["2010s", "Viamet Pharmaceuticals designs oteseconazole (VT-1161) as a selective CYP51 inhibitor."], ["2022", "FDA approves oteseconazole (Vivjoa) for recurrent vulvovaginal candidiasis."]], "cell": "Oteseconazole binds fungal lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51), the enzyme that removes a methyl group from lanosterol on the way to ergosterol, the main sterol in fungal membranes. Its tetrazole group binds the heme iron in the active site, and the rest of the molecule fits the shape of the fungal enzyme closely. Blocking CYP51 depletes ergosterol and builds up toxic 14-alpha-methylated sterols, so the membrane becomes leaky and membrane-bound enzymes fail. Because the tetrazole binds human CYP enzymes much less tightly than older azoles do, it causes fewer CYP drug interactions. It is active against many fluconazole-resistant Candida, including some C. glabrata and C. krusei strains.", "body": [["Vagina", "It clears Candida and, by staying in tissue for months, keeps recurrent yeast infections from coming back."], ["Head and gut", "Headache and nausea are the most common side effects."], ["Pregnancy", "It stays in the body for years at low levels, and animal studies showed eye abnormalities in offspring, so it is not used in people who could become pregnant."]], "journey": "Oteseconazole is taken by mouth with food. It peaks in about five to ten hours. It is over 99 percent protein bound and very fat-soluble, so it builds up in tissues, including vaginal tissue. It undergoes very little metabolism and leaves mainly unchanged in the feces. Its median terminal half-life is extremely long, about 138 days, so exposure lasts for years after the last dose, which is the reason for the reproductive restriction.", "sidefx": "Headache and nausea are mild and common. The main risk is embryo-fetal toxicity: in animal studies, exposure caused eye abnormalities in offspring, and because the drug persists so long, it could reach a future pregnancy. Its selectivity for fungal CYP51 is why liver toxicity and drug interactions seem lower than with older azoles. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Oteseconazole lingers so long that its label describes a drug exposure window of about 2 years after the last dose.", "src": ["Vivjoa (oteseconazole) FDA label", "Hoekstra WJ et al. Bioorg Med Chem Lett, 2014"]}, "quinine": {"origin": "Quinine comes from the bark of the cinchona tree of the Andes. Indigenous Peruvians used the bark for fevers and shivering, and Jesuit missionaries brought it to Europe in the 1600s, where Jesuit's bark became famous for curing malaria. In 1820 the French pharmacists Pierre Joseph Pelletier and Joseph Bienaime Caventou isolated pure quinine from the bark. Britain and the Netherlands later grew cinchona in India and Java, and quinine helped European expansion into malaria regions. In 1944 Robert Burns Woodward and William Doering reported a formal synthesis. In the US, Qualaquin was approved in 2005 for uncomplicated falciparum malaria, and in 2006 the FDA ordered unapproved quinine products off the market, warning against its use for leg cramps because of serious blood reactions.", "timeline": [["1600s", "Jesuit missionaries bring cinchona bark from Peru to Europe for fevers."], ["1820", "Pelletier and Caventou isolate pure quinine."], ["1944", "Woodward and Doering report a formal synthesis of quinine."], ["2005", "FDA approves Qualaquin for uncomplicated falciparum malaria."], ["2006", "FDA orders unapproved quinine products removed, warning against use for leg cramps."]], "cell": "Malaria parasites in red blood cells digest hemoglobin in their food vacuole. This releases heme, which is toxic, so the parasite links it into an inert crystal called hemozoin. Quinine is a weak base that concentrates in the acidic food vacuole and is thought to bind heme and block its polymerization into hemozoin. Free heme then builds up, damages membranes and kills the parasite. This acts only on blood-stage parasites (blood schizonticide); it does not kill liver hypnozoites. In the host, quinine blocks cardiac sodium channels (a class IA-like effect) and potassium channels, which prolongs the QT interval. It also stimulates insulin release from pancreatic beta cells by blocking ATP-sensitive potassium channels, causing low blood sugar.", "body": [["Blood (parasites)", "It kills malaria parasites inside red blood cells, clearing fever and parasitemia."], ["Ears, eyes and head", "Cinchonism causes ringing in the ears, hearing loss, headache, nausea and blurred vision, and at high levels blindness."], ["Heart", "Sodium and potassium channel blockade widens the QRS and prolongs QT, which can lead to torsades de pointes."], ["Pancreas and blood sugar", "Extra insulin release can drop blood sugar, especially in pregnancy and severe malaria."], ["Blood (cells)", "It can trigger drug-dependent antibodies that destroy platelets, cause hemolysis, or cause HUS/TTP and DIC."], ["Muscles", "It reduces muscle excitability, which is why it was once used, now discouraged, for leg cramps."]], "journey": "Quinine is taken by mouth and is well absorbed, peaking in about two to four hours. It is highly bound to alpha-1 acid glycoprotein, which rises in malaria, so binding goes up in sick patients. It spreads widely and crosses the placenta. It is mostly metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4, and about 20 percent leaves unchanged in the urine. Its half-life is about 10 to 20 hours and longer in severe malaria.", "sidefx": "Cinchonism comes from quinine's effects on the inner ear and nerves and appears at typical blood levels. QT prolongation and arrhythmias come from cardiac ion channel blockade. Hypoglycemia comes from insulin release. Thrombocytopenia, HUS/TTP and hemolysis are immune reactions in which quinine helps antibodies bind blood cells; these can be fatal, which is why the FDA warned against using it for leg cramps. In G6PD deficiency, it can cause hemolysis. Overdose is treated supportively; octreotide can counter its insulin-releasing effect.", "fun": "Tonic water was originally a way to take quinine for malaria, and British officers in India reportedly mixed it with gin to cover the bitter taste.", "src": ["Qualaquin (quinine sulfate) FDA label", "Achan J et al. Quinine, an old anti-malarial drug in a modern world. Malar J, 2011", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "tafenoquine": {"origin": "Tafenoquine was synthesized in 1978 at the Walter Reed Army Institute of Research as part of a US military search for a better version of primaquine, the 8-aminoquinoline used since the 1950s to kill dormant malaria parasites in the liver. Primaquine must be taken daily for 14 days, and many people do not finish. Tafenoquine's main advantage is a half-life of about two weeks. After decades of work, GlaxoSmithKline and Medicines for Malaria Venture won FDA approval for Krintafel in July 2018 as a single-dose radical cure for Plasmodium vivax, given with a blood-stage drug. In August 2018, the FDA approved Arakoda, from 60 Degrees Pharmaceuticals with the US Army, for malaria prevention in travelers.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Primaquine becomes the standard drug for P. vivax relapse."], ["1978", "Tafenoquine is synthesized at the Walter Reed Army Institute of Research."], ["2018", "FDA approves Krintafel for single-dose radical cure of P. vivax."], ["2018", "FDA approves Arakoda for malaria prophylaxis."]], "cell": "P. vivax and P. ovale can hide in liver cells as dormant hypnozoites that wake weeks or months later and cause relapse. Most antimalarials do not touch them. Tafenoquine is an 8-aminoquinoline like primaquine. Its exact mechanism is not fully known, but it is thought to be metabolized, likely by CYP2D6, into products that redox cycle, generating reactive oxygen species such as hydrogen peroxide that damage parasite mitochondria and other structures. This kills hypnozoites (tissue schizonticide) and also blood-stage parasites. The same oxidative stress damages human red blood cells that lack G6PD, because those cells cannot make enough NADPH to neutralize the oxidants, so they hemolyze.", "body": [["Liver", "It kills dormant P. vivax hypnozoites, preventing malaria relapses weeks to months later."], ["Blood (prevention)", "As Arakoda it also kills developing blood-stage parasites, preventing malaria in travelers."], ["Red blood cells", "In G6PD deficiency, oxidative stress causes hemolytic anemia, which can be severe and long-lasting because the drug lingers."], ["Blood oxygen", "It can oxidize hemoglobin to methemoglobin, which carries oxygen poorly."], ["Brain", "Insomnia, abnormal dreams, anxiety and rarely psychosis can occur, so it is avoided in people with a history of psychosis."], ["Eyes", "Mild corneal deposits (vortex keratopathy) can occur and do not usually affect vision."]], "journey": "Tafenoquine is taken by mouth with food, which improves absorption. It is absorbed slowly, peaking about 12 to 15 hours later. It is very highly protein bound and spreads widely into tissues, including the liver. It undergoes little metabolism in the blood, though activation in the liver probably uses CYP2D6. It leaves slowly, mainly in the feces, with a half-life of about 15 days, which is what allows a single dose to cure liver-stage infection.", "sidefx": "Hemolysis comes from oxidative stress in red cells that lack G6PD, so G6PD testing is required before use, and because tafenoquine lasts weeks the hemolysis cannot be stopped by stopping the drug. Breastfeeding a G6PD-deficient infant is also a concern. Methemoglobinemia comes from oxidation of hemoglobin iron; methylene blue is used for symptomatic methemoglobinemia, though it can worsen hemolysis in G6PD deficiency. Psychiatric effects are not fully explained. There is no specific antidote for hemolysis; care is transfusion and support.", "fun": "Tafenoquine took 40 years to get from a Walter Reed lab to FDA approval.", "src": ["Krintafel (tafenoquine) FDA label", "Arakoda (tafenoquine) FDA label", "Lacerda MVG et al. N Engl J Med, 2019"]}, "pyrimethamine": {"origin": "Pyrimethamine was designed by George Hitchings and Gertrude Elion at the Burroughs Wellcome labs in New York. Starting in the 1940s, they built molecules that imitated the building blocks of DNA, reasoning that rapidly dividing cells and microbes could be starved of what they need to copy DNA. Their diaminopyrimidines blocked dihydrofolate reductase, and pyrimethamine proved especially strong against the malaria parasite's version of the enzyme. It was introduced as Daraprim in 1953. It later became the core treatment for toxoplasmosis, given with a sulfonamide and leucovorin. Hitchings and Elion shared the 1988 Nobel Prize for this rational approach to drug design. In 2015 the drug drew national attention when its US price was raised more than 50-fold overnight.", "timeline": [["1940s", "Hitchings and Elion begin designing antimetabolites at Burroughs Wellcome."], ["1953", "Pyrimethamine (Daraprim) is introduced."], ["1988", "Hitchings and Elion share the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."], ["2015", "A sudden price increase for Daraprim draws national attention to drug pricing."]], "cell": "Cells need tetrahydrofolate to carry one-carbon units for making thymidine and purines, the building blocks of DNA. Dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR) regenerates tetrahydrofolate from dihydrofolate. Pyrimethamine binds the DHFR of Toxoplasma gondii and Plasmodium far more tightly than human DHFR, so parasite folate recycling stops, thymidine synthesis fails, and the parasite cannot replicate its DNA. Sulfadiazine blocks an earlier step in the same pathway, dihydropteroate synthase, which human cells do not have. Hitting two steps in one pathway is strongly synergistic. At the higher exposures used for toxoplasmosis, pyrimethamine also blocks some human DHFR, causing folate deficiency in the bone marrow. Leucovorin (folinic acid) is given because it is already reduced, so it bypasses DHFR and rescues human cells, while Toxoplasma cannot use it well.", "body": [["Brain and eyes", "With a sulfonamide it stops Toxoplasma tachyzoites from multiplying, treating toxoplasmic encephalitis and eye infections."], ["Bone marrow", "Human DHFR blockade causes megaloblastic anemia, low white cells and low platelets, which leucovorin helps prevent."], ["Mouth and gut", "Folate deficiency in fast-dividing lining cells causes a sore smooth tongue (atrophic glossitis), loss of appetite and vomiting."], ["Skin", "With sulfadoxine or sulfadiazine, severe skin reactions such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome can occur, mostly from the sulfa part."], ["Pregnancy", "As a folate antagonist it is used carefully in early pregnancy because folate is vital for fetal development."]], "journey": "Pyrimethamine is taken by mouth and is well absorbed, peaking in about two to six hours. It is about 87 percent protein bound and spreads widely, including into the brain, which matters for toxoplasmic encephalitis. It is metabolized in the liver, and the metabolites leave in the urine. Its half-life is very long, about 85 to 100 hours, so a loading dose is used at the start.", "sidefx": "The bone marrow effects come straight from its mechanism. At high exposures it blocks human DHFR as well as parasite DHFR, so marrow cells cannot make DNA fast enough, causing megaloblastic anemia and low blood counts. Leucovorin is the reversal, because it supplies reduced folate beyond the blocked step. Glossitis and gut upset reflect the same folate shortage in rapidly dividing lining cells. Severe skin reactions mostly come from the sulfonamide partner. Overdose can cause seizures.", "fun": "Gertrude Elion never earned a doctoral degree, yet she shared a Nobel Prize and helped create drugs for malaria, leukemia, gout, herpes and organ transplant rejection.", "src": ["Daraprim (pyrimethamine) FDA label", "Hitchings GH. Nobel Lecture, 1988", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "pyrantel": {"origin": "Pyrantel was discovered by Pfizer scientists in the mid-1960s while searching for new drugs against worms in farm animals. It belongs to the tetrahydropyrimidines, a group that also includes morantel and oxantel. Pyrantel tartrate was first used in veterinary medicine, and the poorly soluble pamoate salt was developed for people because it stays in the gut where the worms live. It became a standard treatment for pinworm (Enterobius vermicularis), the most common worm infection in US children, and in the US it is sold over the counter, for example as Pin-X and Reese's Pinworm Medicine. It is also still used widely in dogs, cats and horses.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Pfizer scientists discover pyrantel while screening for veterinary anthelmintics."], ["1970s", "Pyrantel pamoate comes into human use for pinworm and roundworm."]], "cell": "Worms move through the gut using body wall muscles that are controlled by acetylcholine acting on nicotinic receptors. Pyrantel is a selective agonist at nematode nicotinic acetylcholine receptors, particularly the L-type (levamisole-sensitive) subtype on worm muscle. It opens these ligand-gated cation channels and depolarizes the muscle, like an overdose of acetylcholine. The muscle contracts strongly and then cannot relax, causing spastic paralysis. The paralyzed worms lose their grip on the intestinal wall and are swept out by normal peristalsis. It does not kill eggs, so a repeat treatment is often used to catch newly hatched worms. Human nicotinic receptors are much less sensitive, and very little drug is absorbed.", "body": [["Intestine", "Paralyzed pinworms and roundworms are pushed out with stool, ending itching and other symptoms of infection."], ["Gut", "Mild nausea, cramps and diarrhea can occur, partly from the drug and partly from dying worms."], ["Nervous system", "Headache and dizziness are occasional and usually mild."], ["Liver", "Mild, temporary rises in liver enzymes have been reported."]], "journey": "Pyrantel pamoate is taken by mouth as a single dose. The pamoate salt dissolves poorly, so most of the drug stays in the gut, where it acts. Only a small amount is absorbed and reaches low plasma levels within a few hours. The absorbed part is partly metabolized and leaves in the urine. Most of the dose leaves unchanged in the stool.", "sidefx": "Because so little reaches the blood, side effects are mild and mostly in the gut. Nausea and cramps reflect local effects and worm expulsion. Pyrantel works against piperazine, another anthelmintic, because piperazine relaxes worm muscle while pyrantel contracts it, so they are not combined. Serious reactions are rare. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Pinworm eggs are so light they can float in the air and land on bedding and toys, which is why the whole household is often treated together.", "src": ["Pyrantel pamoate OTC drug facts", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "moxidectin": {"origin": "Moxidectin is a milbemycin, a macrocyclic lactone related to ivermectin. Scientists at American Cyanamid made it in the 1980s by chemically modifying nemadectin, a natural product of the soil bacterium Streptomyces cyanogriseus. It became a widely used veterinary drug for heartworm and parasites in livestock. Because it lasts much longer in the body than ivermectin, the WHO Special Programme for Research and Training in Tropical Diseases tested it for onchocerciasis (river blindness), a worm disease spread by black flies in Africa. The nonprofit Medicines Development for Global Health completed development, and the FDA approved moxidectin in 2018 for river blindness in people 12 years and older.", "timeline": [["1980s", "American Cyanamid scientists create moxidectin from nemadectin."], ["1990s", "Moxidectin is introduced as a veterinary antiparasitic."], ["2018", "FDA approves moxidectin for onchocerciasis in people 12 and older."]], "cell": "Moxidectin binds glutamate-gated chloride channels on nerve and muscle cells of nematodes. These channels exist in invertebrates but not in mammals. Binding locks them open, so chloride flows into the cells and hyperpolarizes them. The worm's pharyngeal muscles, body wall muscles and nerves can no longer fire normally, causing flaccid paralysis and death of Onchocerca volvulus microfilariae, the tiny larval worms in the skin. It may also suppress release of new microfilariae from adult females for months, but it does not kill adult worms. It can also act on GABA-gated chloride channels. In mammals, P-glycoprotein at the blood-brain barrier normally keeps it out of the brain, which protects the host.", "body": [["Skin", "It clears microfilariae from the skin quickly and keeps numbers low for months, reducing itching and skin disease."], ["Eyes", "Lower microfilarial load in the eye reduces the inflammation that leads to river blindness, though dying larvae can cause eye pain."], ["Immune system", "Dying microfilariae release antigens, causing the Mazzotti reaction with itching, rash, fever, swollen lymph nodes and joint pain."], ["Blood vessels", "The Mazzotti reaction can include fast heart rate and a drop in blood pressure on standing."], ["Blood", "Eosinophils rise as the immune system responds to dying parasites."]], "journey": "Moxidectin is taken by mouth as a single dose and peaks in about four hours. It is very fat-soluble and spreads widely, storing in fat tissue. It undergoes very little metabolism and leaves slowly, mainly in the feces. Its half-life is very long, about 23 days, which is why one dose suppresses skin microfilariae for so long. It is a P-glycoprotein substrate, which limits its entry into the brain.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are not direct drug toxicity but the body's inflammatory reaction to dying microfilariae (the Mazzotti reaction): itching, rash, fever, joint pain, swollen lymph nodes and low blood pressure on standing. People heavily infected with Loa loa, a different worm, can develop a dangerous encephalopathy when large numbers of Loa microfilariae die in the brain's blood vessels, so moxidectin has not been studied in them. Treatment of reactions is supportive.", "fun": "In head-to-head trials in Africa, a single dose of moxidectin kept skin microfilariae lower for longer than a single dose of ivermectin.", "src": ["Moxidectin Tablets FDA label", "Opoku NO et al. Lancet, 2018"]}, "benznidazole": {"origin": "Chagas disease was described in 1909 by the Brazilian physician Carlos Chagas, who identified the parasite Trypanosoma cruzi, its insect vector (the kissing bug), and the disease in a single remarkable study. For decades there was no treatment. In the late 1960s scientists at Roche developed benznidazole, a nitroimidazole, and it was introduced in Latin America around 1971 as Rochagan and Radanil. It became the first-line drug for Chagas disease worldwide. Roche later transferred the technology to the Brazilian public lab LAFEPE, and the nonprofit DNDi worked on a child-friendly form. In 2017 the FDA approved benznidazole from Chemo Research (Exeltis) for children ages 2 to 12, the first treatment for Chagas disease approved in the US.", "timeline": [["1909", "Carlos Chagas describes Trypanosoma cruzi and Chagas disease."], ["1971", "Roche introduces benznidazole in Latin America."], ["2017", "FDA approves benznidazole for children 2 to 12 with Chagas disease."]], "cell": "Benznidazole is a prodrug. Inside T. cruzi, a parasite NADH-dependent type I nitroreductase reduces its nitro group in two-electron steps. This creates reactive intermediates, including hydroxylamine derivatives that break down to glyoxal, which form covalent adducts with parasite DNA, proteins and lipids, and also lead to DNA strand breaks. Mammalian cells lack this type I nitroreductase, which gives the drug selectivity. It kills both the trypomastigotes circulating in the blood and the amastigotes hiding inside heart and gut muscle cells. Resistance can come from loss of the nitroreductase. It works best in early (acute) infection and in children with chronic infection.", "body": [["Blood and tissues", "It kills T. cruzi in the blood and inside cells, which lowers parasite load and, in many children, leads to negative serology."], ["Heart and gut (long term)", "Treating children early aims to prevent the later heart and digestive damage of chronic Chagas disease."], ["Skin", "Allergic dermatitis is very common, often a week or two into treatment, and rarely severe reactions such as DRESS or Stevens-Johnson syndrome occur."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral neuropathy can develop, usually later in the course, and is a reason to stop the drug."], ["Bone marrow", "It can lower neutrophils and platelets, and rarely causes agranulocytosis."], ["Gut and weight", "Abdominal pain, nausea, loss of appetite and weight loss are common."]], "journey": "Benznidazole is taken by mouth and is well absorbed, peaking in about three hours. It is moderately protein bound and spreads into tissues. It is metabolized in part by nitroreduction and limited liver metabolism, and the metabolites and some unchanged drug leave mainly in the urine. Its half-life is about 13 hours, which supports twice-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Many side effects come from the same reactive nitro intermediates that kill the parasite, which can also stress human cells. Rash is the most common and is thought to be immune-mediated; severe cutaneous reactions can occur. Neuropathy is dose- and time-dependent. Bone marrow suppression shows up as low white cells or platelets. Because it is genotoxic in animals and can harm a fetus, it is avoided in pregnancy. Children generally tolerate it better than adults. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Carlos Chagas is one of very few scientists to describe a new disease's parasite, vector and clinical picture all in one study.", "src": ["Benznidazole Tablets FDA label", "Chagas C. Mem Inst Oswaldo Cruz, 1909", "CDC Chagas disease clinical guidance"]}, "nifurtimox": {"origin": "Nifurtimox is a nitrofuran developed by Bayer in the 1960s as one of the first effective drugs for Chagas disease, caused by Trypanosoma cruzi. It was introduced in Latin America as Lampit around 1970 and, with benznidazole, became one of only two drugs for the disease. It also found a second life against African sleeping sickness: in 2009 the WHO added nifurtimox-eflornithine combination therapy (NECT), developed with the nonprofit DNDi and Medecins Sans Frontieres, for late-stage Trypanosoma brucei gambiense infection. In 2020 the FDA approved Lampit for children under 18 with Chagas disease, making it the second Chagas drug approved in the US.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Bayer develops nifurtimox."], ["1970", "Nifurtimox (Lampit) comes into use for Chagas disease in Latin America."], ["2009", "WHO adds nifurtimox-eflornithine combination therapy for sleeping sickness."], ["2020", "FDA approves Lampit for children under 18 with Chagas disease."]], "cell": "Nifurtimox is a prodrug. In T. cruzi, the parasite's type I nitroreductase reduces its nitrofuran group in two-electron steps to reactive products, including an unsaturated open-chain nitrile, that damage parasite DNA, proteins and membranes. It can also undergo one-electron reduction to a nitro anion radical, which passes its electron to oxygen and forms superoxide and other reactive oxygen species. T. cruzi has weak defenses against oxidative stress compared with human cells, so the parasite is killed. Mammalian cells lack the type I nitroreductase, which gives selectivity. Loss of the nitroreductase causes cross-resistance with benznidazole.", "body": [["Blood and tissues", "It kills T. cruzi in the blood and inside cells, and many treated children show a serologic response."], ["Gut and appetite", "Nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain and loss of appetite are common and can cause weight loss, so growth is watched in children."], ["Brain and mood", "It can cause anxiety, behavior change, dizziness and sleep problems, and rarely seizures."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral neuropathy can occur with longer courses."], ["Skin and immune system", "Rash and fever can occur, and severe hypersensitivity is rare."]], "journey": "Nifurtimox is taken by mouth with food, which improves absorption, and peaks in about four hours. It is extensively metabolized, mostly by nitroreduction and other non-CYP pathways, and very little unchanged drug appears in the urine. Its half-life is short, about three hours, so it is given three times a day. Tablets can be dispersed in water for children.", "sidefx": "The reactive metabolites and oxidative stress that kill the parasite can also affect human tissues, especially nerves and the brain, which explains neuropathy, anxiety, behavior change and seizure risk. Loss of appetite and gut upset are common and explain weight loss. Alcohol can worsen side effects. It is genotoxic in some tests and can harm a fetus, so it is avoided in pregnancy. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Nifurtimox is one of the few drugs used against two different trypanosome diseases on two different continents.", "src": ["Lampit (nifurtimox) FDA label", "Priotto G et al. NECT trial. Lancet, 2009", "CDC Chagas disease clinical guidance"]}, "miltefosine": {"origin": "Miltefosine was first developed as an anticancer drug. In the 1980s German researchers, including Hansjorg Eibl at the Max Planck Institute in Gottingen and Clemens Unger, studied alkylphosphocholines, fat-like molecules that insert into cell membranes, and found hexadecylphosphocholine (miltefosine) could kill tumor cells. Around the same time, researchers including Simon Croft in London showed it killed Leishmania parasites. It was approved in India in 2002 as the first oral drug for visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar), a disease that had required painful injections. The FDA approved it as Impavido in 2014 for visceral, cutaneous and mucosal leishmaniasis. The CDC also makes it available for rare infections with free-living amoebae such as Naegleria fowleri.", "timeline": [["1980s", "German researchers study miltefosine as an anticancer agent."], ["1987", "Antileishmanial activity of alkylphosphocholines is reported."], ["2002", "Miltefosine is approved in India as the first oral drug for visceral leishmaniasis."], ["2014", "FDA approves miltefosine (Impavido) for leishmaniasis."]], "cell": "Miltefosine is a phospholipid analog: it has a long fatty tail and a phosphocholine head, like the lipids in cell membranes. It inserts into the parasite's membranes and acts on several targets at once. It blocks phosphatidylcholine synthesis and disturbs membrane lipid and sterol metabolism. It interferes with membrane signaling, including the Akt survival pathway. It inhibits mitochondrial cytochrome c oxidase, lowering ATP production. It also disrupts calcium handling inside the parasite, partly by opening a plasma membrane calcium channel and disrupting acidocalcisomes. Together these trigger apoptosis-like death of Leishmania. Because there are several targets, resistance develops slowly, though it can arise from loss of a parasite lipid transporter that brings the drug into the cell.", "body": [["Liver, spleen and bone marrow", "It kills Leishmania in macrophages of these organs, treating visceral leishmaniasis with fever, weight loss and enlarged spleen."], ["Skin and mucosa", "It heals ulcers from cutaneous and mucosal leishmaniasis."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are very common and can lead to dehydration."], ["Kidneys", "Dehydration from vomiting and direct effects can raise creatinine and cause acute kidney injury."], ["Reproductive organs", "It is toxic to the fetus in animals, so it is contraindicated in pregnancy, and it can cause testicular pain and impaired fertility."], ["Blood", "Low platelet counts can occur, so blood counts are checked during treatment."]], "journey": "Miltefosine is taken by mouth with food. It is absorbed slowly and builds up in the body over weeks, reaching steady state after about three weeks. It is highly protein bound and spreads widely. It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes; instead phospholipase D cleaves it into choline and lipid fragments that join normal lipid metabolism. Very little is excreted in the urine. It leaves very slowly, with a terminal half-life of about a month, so it stays in the body for months after the last dose.", "sidefx": "Gut side effects likely come from direct membrane disruption in the gut lining, and the resulting fluid loss explains kidney injury. Its membrane-active, cell-killing action also harms fast-dividing cells in the embryo, which is why it is a boxed warning for embryo-fetal toxicity and effective contraception is needed during treatment and for months after. Vomiting and diarrhea can also reduce the effect of oral contraceptives. Testicular effects were seen in animals. Stevens-Johnson syndrome is rare. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Miltefosine has been used to treat a handful of survivors of Naegleria fowleri, the so-called brain-eating amoeba, an infection that is almost always fatal.", "src": ["Impavido (miltefosine) FDA label", "Sundar S et al. N Engl J Med, 2002", "Dorlo TPC et al. J Antimicrob Chemother, 2012"]}, "spinosad": {"origin": "Spinosad comes from a soil bacterium. In 1982 a scientist vacationing in the Caribbean collected soil from the grounds of an old rum distillery in the Virgin Islands and sent it to Eli Lilly for screening. Lilly scientists isolated a new actinomycete, Saccharopolyspora spinosa, that made insect-killing compounds called spinosyns. The mixture of spinosyn A and spinosyn D became spinosad, developed by DowElanco (later Dow AgroSciences) as a crop insecticide and first registered by the EPA in 1997. Because it was very toxic to insects but much less toxic to mammals, it was later developed for head lice. The FDA approved spinosad topical suspension as Natroba in 2011 for head lice, and later added scabies in 2021.", "timeline": [["1982", "Soil from a Caribbean rum distillery yields Saccharopolyspora spinosa."], ["1997", "Spinosad is registered by the EPA as a crop insecticide."], ["2011", "FDA approves spinosad topical suspension (Natroba) for head lice."], ["2021", "FDA approves spinosad for scabies."]], "cell": "Spinosad attacks the insect nervous system. Spinosyns bind an allosteric site on insect nicotinic acetylcholine receptors that contain the alpha6 subunit, a site different from where nicotine or neonicotinoids bind. This activates the receptors and causes persistent excitation of neurons. Spinosad also acts on GABA-gated chloride channels, which adds to the loss of normal nerve control. The result is involuntary muscle contractions and tremors, followed by paralysis and death of lice, and it also kills lice eggs (ovicidal), so nit combing is optional per the label. It kills scabies mites by a similar nerve effect. Mammalian receptors are far less sensitive, and the drug is not detectably absorbed through intact skin.", "body": [["Scalp and hair", "It kills live lice and most eggs in a single application, clearing infestation."], ["Skin (scabies)", "It kills scabies mites in the skin, though itching can continue for weeks as the skin reaction settles."], ["Skin (irritation)", "Some people get redness or irritation where it was applied."], ["Eyes", "Contact with the eyes causes irritation, so the eyes are covered during use."]], "journey": "Spinosad is applied as a suspension to dry scalp and hair, left on for a short time, then rinsed off. Studies did not find measurable drug in the blood after use, so almost none reaches the body. What is not rinsed off is removed with normal washing. The inactive ingredient benzyl alcohol helps the product work by blocking the lice's breathing openings.", "sidefx": "Because so little is absorbed, side effects are local: skin redness and irritation and eye irritation. The product contains benzyl alcohol, which in large amounts can cause gasping syndrome in premature or very young infants, which is why it is not used in infants under 6 months. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The soil sample that gave the world spinosad came from the grounds of an abandoned rum still, which is how the bacterium got the sugar-related genus name Saccharopolyspora.", "src": ["Natroba (spinosad) FDA label", "Mertz FP, Yao RC. Int J Syst Bacteriol, 1990"]}, "malathion": {"origin": "Malathion is an organophosphate insecticide developed by American Cyanamid around 1950. Organophosphates as a group trace back to German research in the 1930s and 1940s, which produced both insecticides and nerve agents. Malathion stood out because mammals break it down quickly, while insects activate it into a strong poison. It became one of the most widely used insecticides in farming and mosquito control. A lotion with malathion in isopropyl alcohol was approved in the US as Ovide for head lice in 1982. It was later taken off the market for a few years and then reintroduced, and it is valued as an option when lice resist pyrethroids such as permethrin.", "timeline": [["1930s", "German chemists develop organophosphate compounds."], ["1950", "American Cyanamid introduces malathion as an insecticide."], ["1982", "FDA approves malathion lotion (Ovide) for head lice."]], "cell": "Malathion itself is a weak cholinesterase inhibitor. In insects, oxidative enzymes convert its P=S group to P=O, forming malaoxon. Malaoxon phosphorylates a serine in the active site of acetylcholinesterase and inactivates it essentially irreversibly. Without the enzyme, acetylcholine builds up at insect nerve synapses, nerves fire nonstop, and the louse suffers spastic paralysis and dies. It also kills many eggs. Humans are protected mainly because our carboxylesterases rapidly hydrolyze malathion into inactive acids before much can be activated, and very little crosses intact skin. Ingestion overwhelms this defense and causes cholinergic poisoning.", "body": [["Scalp and hair", "It kills lice and many eggs, and some drug stays on the hair, which may give a residual effect."], ["Scalp skin", "The alcohol vehicle can sting and irritate, especially on scratched skin, and can cause chemical burns."], ["Eyes", "Contact with the eyes causes irritation and can cause conjunctivitis."], ["Whole body (if swallowed)", "Swallowing it causes cholinergic toxicity: salivation, sweating, vomiting, diarrhea, slow heart rate, bronchospasm, muscle twitching and weakness."]], "journey": "The lotion is applied to dry hair and scalp, left to air dry, and washed out several hours later. Only a small fraction is absorbed through the scalp. What does enter the blood is rapidly broken down by carboxylesterases into inactive malathion acids, which leave in the urine. Very little is ever converted to malaoxon in humans, so systemic effects after proper topical use are minimal.", "sidefx": "The lotion's isopropyl alcohol makes it highly flammable, so contact with flames, hair dryers, curling irons or cigarettes can cause burns. Alcohol also explains stinging and irritation. If swallowed, malathion and malaoxon block human acetylcholinesterase, causing a cholinergic crisis. Atropine blocks the muscarinic effects, and pralidoxime can reactivate the enzyme if given before aging of the bond. Seizures are treated with benzodiazepines.", "fun": "Malathion has been sprayed over cities, including parts of California in the 1980s and 1990s, to fight Mediterranean fruit fly outbreaks.", "src": ["Ovide (malathion) lotion FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]}, "letermovir": {"origin": "Cytomegalovirus (CMV) is a major threat after stem cell and organ transplants, but the older antivirals ganciclovir, foscarnet and cidofovir can damage the bone marrow or kidneys. Letermovir was discovered at Bayer and then developed by AiCuris, a German company spun out of Bayer's anti-infective research in 2006. It belonged to a new class that targets the CMV terminase, a viral enzyme with no human counterpart. Merck licensed it in 2012, and the FDA approved it as Prevymis in 2017 to prevent CMV infection and disease in adult CMV-seropositive recipients of allogeneic stem cell transplants. In 2023 the FDA added prevention of CMV disease in high-risk kidney transplant recipients.", "timeline": [["2006", "AiCuris is formed from Bayer anti-infective research and develops letermovir."], ["2012", "Merck licenses letermovir from AiCuris."], ["2017", "FDA approves letermovir (Prevymis) for CMV prophylaxis after stem cell transplant."], ["2023", "FDA adds CMV prophylaxis in high-risk kidney transplant recipients."]], "cell": "When CMV copies its DNA in the cell nucleus, it makes long concatemers, many genomes joined end to end. The viral terminase complex, made of pUL56, pUL89 and pUL51, recognizes packaging signals, cuts these concatemers into single genome lengths and feeds each one into an empty capsid. Letermovir inhibits this terminase complex, with resistance mapping mainly to UL56. Viral DNA is still made, but it cannot be cut and packaged, so no infectious virions form. Because it acts on the terminase and not the DNA polymerase, it does not need activation by viral kinases, is not cross-resistant with ganciclovir, and does not damage the bone marrow. It is specific to CMV and has no activity against other herpesviruses, so acyclovir is still needed for HSV and VZV.", "body": [["Immune system after transplant", "It keeps CMV from reactivating in the early months after transplant, when the immune system is weakest."], ["Bone marrow", "Unlike ganciclovir, it does not suppress blood counts, which helps protect the new graft."], ["Gut", "Nausea, diarrhea and vomiting are the most common side effects."], ["Legs and fluid", "Peripheral swelling occurs in some patients."], ["Heart", "Fast heart rate and atrial fibrillation are uncommon."], ["Drug interactions", "It raises levels of cyclosporine, tacrolimus and other drugs through OATP1B1/3 and CYP3A inhibition."]], "journey": "Letermovir can be given by mouth or IV and peaks within a few hours of a tablet. It is about 99 percent protein bound. The liver takes it up through OATP1B1/3 transporters. It is metabolized only a little, by UGT1A1 and UGT1A3 glucuronidation, and most leaves unchanged in bile and feces. Its half-life is about 12 hours, which supports once-daily dosing. Cyclosporine raises its levels by blocking OATP uptake.", "sidefx": "Its specific target explains why it lacks the marrow and kidney toxicity of polymerase inhibitors. Gut side effects and swelling are common but usually mild. Drug interactions come from inhibition of CYP3A and OATP1B1/3, which can raise immunosuppressant and statin levels. The main clinical risk is CMV breakthrough after prophylaxis ends, because it does not build immunity. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Letermovir was the first new anti-CMV drug approved in the US in about 15 years and the first to target the viral terminase.", "src": ["Prevymis (letermovir) FDA label", "Marty FM et al. N Engl J Med, 2017"]}, "maribavir": {"origin": "Maribavir came from research at the University of Michigan in the 1990s, where chemists including Leroy Townsend and virologist John Drach made benzimidazole ribosides with strong activity against CMV. Glaxo Wellcome licensed and developed maribavir, and then several companies, including ViroPharma and Shire, took it forward. An early trial in stem cell transplant prophylaxis was disappointing, but later studies at higher exposures showed it could treat CMV that had failed other drugs. Takeda, which acquired Shire, won FDA approval for maribavir as Livtencity in 2021 for adults and children 12 and older with post-transplant CMV infection that is refractory to treatment, with or without resistance to ganciclovir, valganciclovir, cidofovir or foscarnet.", "timeline": [["1990s", "University of Michigan researchers create benzimidazole ribosides, including maribavir."], ["2000s", "Maribavir moves through clinical development under several companies."], ["2021", "FDA approves maribavir (Livtencity) for refractory post-transplant CMV."]], "cell": "Maribavir competitively blocks ATP binding to the CMV pUL97 protein kinase. pUL97 phosphorylates viral and host proteins needed at several steps: viral DNA replication, packaging of DNA into capsids, and nuclear egress, when the capsid breaks through the nuclear lamina to leave the nucleus. With pUL97 blocked, those steps stall and fewer infectious virions are made. Because the target is the kinase and not the DNA polymerase, maribavir works against many strains resistant to ganciclovir, cidofovir and foscarnet. However, ganciclovir needs pUL97 to add its first phosphate, so maribavir blocks ganciclovir's activation; the two are antagonistic and not given together. Resistance arises from UL97 mutations such as T409M or H411Y, and some UL27 mutations.", "body": [["Immune system after transplant", "It lowers CMV levels in the blood in people whose infection did not respond to other antivirals."], ["Taste", "Taste disturbance (dysgeusia) is the most common side effect."], ["Gut", "Nausea, diarrhea and vomiting occur but are usually mild."], ["Drug interactions", "It raises tacrolimus, cyclosporine, sirolimus and everolimus levels, so those are monitored closely."], ["Bone marrow and kidneys", "It does not carry the marrow or kidney toxicity seen with ganciclovir, foscarnet or cidofovir."]], "journey": "Maribavir is taken by mouth and is well absorbed, peaking in about one to three hours. It is about 98 percent protein bound and does not get into the brain or eye well, so it is not used for CMV of the brain or retina. It is mainly metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4, with minor CYP1A2, and only a small amount leaves unchanged in urine. Its half-life is about four hours, so it is taken twice daily. Strong CYP3A4 inducers lower its levels.", "sidefx": "Taste disturbance is very common and is thought to relate to the drug itself being present in saliva. Gut side effects are usually mild. It inhibits P-glycoprotein and BCRP, which raises levels of immunosuppressants, so levels are checked. Virologic failure can happen if resistance develops in UL97. Its lack of marrow and kidney toxicity reflects that its target is a viral enzyme. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Maribavir took roughly two decades and several company changes to go from a university lab to FDA approval.", "src": ["Livtencity (maribavir) FDA label", "Avery RK et al. SOLSTICE trial. Clin Infect Dis, 2022"]}, "zanamivir": {"origin": "Zanamivir was one of the first drugs made by structure-based design. In 1983 Peter Colman and colleagues at CSIRO in Australia solved the crystal structure of influenza neuraminidase, showing that its active site was the same in all flu A and B strains. Mark von Itzstein and his team at Monash University, working with the Australian company Biota, used computer modeling to build a sialic acid analog that fit the active site much better than the natural substrate. Biota licensed the drug to Glaxo Wellcome, and the FDA approved it as Relenza in 1999, the first neuraminidase inhibitor. Oral oseltamivir followed the same year. Zanamivir is inhaled because it is too polar to be absorbed from the gut.", "timeline": [["1983", "Peter Colman's team at CSIRO solves the influenza neuraminidase crystal structure."], ["1993", "Von Itzstein and colleagues report zanamivir's rational design in Nature."], ["1999", "FDA approves zanamivir (Relenza) inhaled powder."]], "cell": "Influenza virus binds to sialic acid on the surface of airway cells to get in. When new virions bud out, they are still stuck to sialic acid on the cell and on each other. Neuraminidase, an enzyme on the viral surface, cuts sialic acid to free the new virions and also helps them move through the sialic acid-rich mucus of the airway. Zanamivir is a sialic acid analog shaped like the transition state of this reaction, with a guanidino group that fits a pocket in the active site. It binds neuraminidase very tightly and competitively. Newly made virions stay clumped and attached to the cell, so they cannot spread. It works on influenza A and B but does nothing to virus already inside cells, so it must be started early.", "body": [["Airways", "It reduces viral spread in the respiratory tract, shortening flu symptoms by about a day when started within two days of onset."], ["Lungs (airway disease)", "Inhaled powder can trigger bronchospasm, which can be serious in asthma or COPD."], ["Nose and throat", "Local irritation, cough and sinus symptoms can occur from the inhaled powder."], ["Prevention", "Used after exposure, it lowers the chance of catching the flu in household contacts."], ["Brain", "Neuropsychiatric events such as delirium have been reported, mostly in children with influenza, though flu itself can cause them."]], "journey": "Zanamivir is inhaled as a dry powder through a Diskhaler. Most of it lands in the mouth and throat, and a smaller part reaches the lungs, where it acts on the airway lining. Only about 4 to 17 percent is absorbed into the blood. It is not metabolized and leaves unchanged in the urine with a half-life of about two and a half to five hours. Because so little is in the blood, it has very few drug interactions.", "sidefx": "Bronchospasm is the main serious risk. The inhaled lactose powder can irritate sensitive airways and trigger a sharp drop in lung function in people with asthma or COPD, so it is not recommended for them. A fast-acting bronchodilator is used if bronchospasm occurs. The powder must not be dissolved for nebulizers or ventilators, since the lactose can clog equipment. Allergic reactions are rare. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Zanamivir's design was built around a guanidino group placed exactly where a pocket in the neuraminidase active site was seen on the crystal structure.", "src": ["Relenza (zanamivir) FDA label", "von Itzstein M et al. Nature, 1993", "Varghese JN, Laver WG, Colman PM. Nature, 1983"]}, "lenacapavir": {"origin": "For decades, the HIV capsid, the cone-shaped protein shell around the viral genome, was seen as a hard drug target. Gilead Sciences scientists spent years building small molecules that bind between capsid subunits, using structural biology and medicinal chemistry. The result was lenacapavir, the first capsid inhibitor. It was extremely potent and so slowly released from an injection under the skin that a single dose could last six months. The FDA approved it as Sunlenca in 2022 for heavily treatment-experienced adults with multidrug-resistant HIV. In 2024 the PURPOSE 1 trial in young women in Africa found no HIV infections among those receiving twice-yearly lenacapavir, and Science named it the breakthrough of the year. The FDA approved it for pre-exposure prophylaxis as Yeztugo in 2025.", "timeline": [["2022", "FDA approves lenacapavir (Sunlenca) for multidrug-resistant HIV-1."], ["2024", "The PURPOSE 1 trial shows twice-yearly lenacapavir prevents HIV in women."], ["2025", "FDA approves lenacapavir for HIV prevention as Yeztugo."]], "cell": "The HIV-1 capsid is built from about 1,500 copies of the p24 capsid protein arranged mostly in hexamers. Lenacapavir binds a pocket between neighboring capsid subunits within the hexamer, the same site the host proteins CPSF6 and Nup153 use. This has effects at several steps. Early in infection, it overstabilizes the capsid and blocks its interaction with host import factors, so the viral core cannot pass through the nuclear pore to deliver its DNA. Late in infection, it disrupts the assembly of Gag polyproteins and the formation of a normal cone-shaped core, so new virions are malformed and noninfectious. Because the target is different from reverse transcriptase, protease and integrase, there is no cross-resistance with other classes.", "body": [["Immune system", "It suppresses HIV-1 replication, letting CD4 T cells recover, even in virus resistant to other drug classes."], ["HIV prevention", "Steady drug levels in the blood and tissues block infection from taking hold after exposure."], ["Skin (injection site)", "Swelling, pain, redness and firm lumps under the skin are common, and nodules can last months."], ["Gut", "Nausea can occur, especially with the oral starting tablets."], ["Immune reconstitution", "As the immune system recovers, it can attack hidden infections and cause an inflammatory flare."]], "journey": "Treatment starts with tablets by mouth or a first injection to build levels, then a subcutaneous injection into the abdomen every six months. The drug forms a depot under the skin and is released very slowly, with peak levels about 11 to 12 weeks after injection. It is over 99.8 percent protein bound. It is only lightly metabolized by UGT1A1 and CYP3A, and most of it leaves unchanged in the feces. The half-life after injection is about 8 to 12 weeks, so drug can be detected for a year or more after a single injection.", "sidefx": "Injection site reactions and nodules come from the large depot of drug sitting under the skin. Because levels fade slowly over many months, missed doses can expose the virus to low drug levels for a long time, which can select resistance. Lenacapavir moderately inhibits CYP3A, raising levels of some drugs, and strong CYP3A inducers lower its levels. Immune reconstitution syndrome reflects the recovering immune system. There is no reversal agent, and the drug cannot be removed once injected.", "fun": "Lenacapavir is the first HIV drug that can be given just twice a year.", "src": ["Sunlenca (lenacapavir) FDA label", "Yeztugo (lenacapavir) FDA label", "Bekker LG et al. PURPOSE 1. N Engl J Med, 2024"]}, "raltegravir": {"origin": "HIV integrase, the enzyme that splices viral DNA into human chromosomes, was long considered a promising but hard target. In the 1990s and early 2000s, a Merck team including Daria Hazuda showed that diketo acid compounds blocked the strand transfer step of integration specifically, which proved the target could be drugged. Merck chemists in Italy and the US refined this chemistry into raltegravir. The FDA approved it as Isentress in October 2007, making it the first integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI). It was first used in treatment-experienced patients and later for first-line therapy. A once-daily high-dose tablet, Isentress HD, followed in 2017. Integrase inhibitors went on to become the backbone of most modern HIV regimens.", "timeline": [["2000", "Merck scientists report diketo acids that block HIV integrase strand transfer."], ["2007", "FDA approves raltegravir (Isentress), the first integrase inhibitor."], ["2009", "Raltegravir is approved for treatment-naive adults."], ["2017", "FDA approves once-daily Isentress HD."]], "cell": "After HIV reverse transcriptase makes a DNA copy of the viral RNA, integrase does two jobs. First, in 3' processing, it trims two nucleotides from each end of the viral DNA. Then, in strand transfer, it inserts those ends into the host chromosome. Raltegravir binds the integrase-viral DNA complex (the intasome) and chelates the two magnesium ions in the active site, while its halobenzyl group displaces the terminal viral DNA base. This blocks strand transfer, so the viral DNA cannot be joined to host DNA. Unintegrated viral DNA forms dead-end circles that cannot make new virus. Viral load falls quickly because integration is an early step. Resistance arises from integrase mutations such as N155H, Q148 changes and Y143 changes, and raltegravir has a lower genetic barrier than dolutegravir.", "body": [["Immune system", "Blocking integration stops new cells from being infected, so viral load drops quickly and CD4 counts rise."], ["Brain and sleep", "Insomnia, headache, dizziness and, less often, depression can occur."], ["Muscles", "It can raise creatine kinase and rarely cause myopathy or rhabdomyolysis, especially with other drugs that affect muscle."], ["Skin and immune system", "Rash can occur, and rarely severe reactions such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome or DRESS."], ["Gut", "Nausea can occur early in treatment and is usually mild."]], "journey": "Raltegravir is taken by mouth, with or without food, and peaks in about three hours. It is about 83 percent protein bound. It is metabolized mainly by UGT1A1 glucuronidation and does not use CYP enzymes, so it has fewer drug interactions than boosted regimens. Strong UGT1A1 inducers like rifampin lower its levels. Its terminal half-life is about nine hours, so the standard tablet is taken twice daily. Antacids containing aluminum or magnesium bind it and reduce absorption.", "sidefx": "Raltegravir is generally well tolerated. Muscle problems are thought to come from direct effects on muscle and are more likely with statins or other myotoxic drugs. Multivalent cations such as aluminum, magnesium, calcium and iron bind integrase inhibitors through the same metal-chelating groups that act on integrase, which lowers absorption. Severe skin and hypersensitivity reactions are immune-mediated. Immune reconstitution syndrome reflects immune recovery. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The same metal-chelating design that lets integrase inhibitors grab magnesium in the enzyme is why antacids can block their absorption.", "src": ["Isentress (raltegravir) FDA label", "Hazuda DJ et al. Science, 2000", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "lopinavir-ritonavir": {"origin": "Abbott Laboratories developed ritonavir as a protease inhibitor and it was approved in 1996, but at full doses it caused many side effects. Abbott researchers noticed that ritonavir was a powerful inhibitor of CYP3A4, the liver enzyme that breaks down most protease inhibitors. They designed lopinavir, a protease inhibitor that was very potent but broken down so quickly it was useless on its own, and combined it with a low dose of ritonavir to block its breakdown. The FDA approved this fixed combination as Kaletra in 2000. It was the first coformulated boosted protease inhibitor and introduced the idea of pharmacokinetic boosting, now used with many other drugs. In 2020 it was tested for COVID-19, and large trials found no benefit.", "timeline": [["1996", "FDA approves ritonavir as a protease inhibitor."], ["2000", "FDA approves lopinavir and ritonavir (Kaletra)."], ["2005", "A heat-stable tablet form is approved, removing the need for refrigeration."], ["2020", "Trials including RECOVERY find no benefit for COVID-19."]], "cell": "HIV makes its proteins as long Gag and Gag-Pol polyproteins. HIV protease, an aspartyl protease that works as a dimer, must cut these into mature pieces, such as capsid, reverse transcriptase and integrase, for new virions to become infectious. Lopinavir is a peptidomimetic that imitates the transition state of the peptide bond the protease cuts. It binds the active site tightly and blocks cleavage. Virions still bud from the cell, but they are immature and noninfectious. Ritonavir at low dose has little antiviral effect itself. It inhibits CYP3A4 in the liver and gut and also inhibits P-glycoprotein, which keeps lopinavir levels high enough to work and to stay above resistant-virus thresholds.", "body": [["Immune system", "Blocking protease means new virions cannot mature, so HIV load falls and CD4 counts rise."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea and vomiting are very common, especially early."], ["Lipids and blood sugar", "It raises triglycerides and cholesterol and can cause insulin resistance and high blood sugar."], ["Pancreas", "Very high triglycerides can cause pancreatitis."], ["Liver", "It can raise liver enzymes and worsen hepatitis, especially with hepatitis B or C coinfection."], ["Heart", "It can prolong the PR and QT intervals, raising the risk of heart block and torsades de pointes."]], "journey": "The tablets are taken by mouth, and lopinavir peaks in about four hours. Lopinavir is about 98 to 99 percent protein bound. Without ritonavir, CYP3A4 in the gut and liver would destroy most of it; with ritonavir blocking CYP3A4, levels stay high and its half-life is about five to six hours. Both are metabolized mainly by CYP3A4 and leave mostly in the feces. The oral solution contains alcohol and propylene glycol.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea is thought to come from effects on intestinal cells and chloride secretion. Metabolic effects, including high lipids and insulin resistance, are linked to protease inhibitors interfering with lipid-handling proteins and glucose transporter (GLUT4) function. Ritonavir's strong CYP3A4 inhibition causes many serious drug interactions, for example raising levels of some statins, sedatives, antiarrhythmics and inhaled steroids. Heart effects come from ion channel blockade. The oral solution's propylene glycol and alcohol can be toxic to preterm newborns. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Ritonavir's role changed from a full-dose antiviral to a low-dose booster, and that boosting trick was later used in other drugs, including the COVID-19 drug Paxlovid.", "src": ["Kaletra (lopinavir and ritonavir) FDA label", "Sham HL et al. Antimicrob Agents Chemother, 1998", "RECOVERY Collaborative Group. Lancet, 2020"]}, "doravirine": {"origin": "Non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTIs) began in the 1990s with nevirapine, delavirdine and efavirenz, but they were limited by rash, liver toxicity, brain side effects and easy resistance. Merck designed doravirine as a next-generation NNRTI that keeps working against common resistance mutations such as K103N and Y181C, and with fewer central nervous system effects and less impact on lipids than efavirenz. The FDA approved it in 2018 both as a single tablet, Pifeltro, and in a complete once-daily regimen with lamivudine and tenofovir disoproxil fumarate, Delstrigo.", "timeline": [["1996", "Nevirapine becomes the first approved NNRTI."], ["2018", "FDA approves doravirine as Pifeltro and in Delstrigo."]], "cell": "HIV reverse transcriptase copies the viral RNA into DNA. NNRTIs do not compete with nucleotides. Instead, doravirine binds a hydrophobic pocket in the p66 subunit about 10 angstroms from the polymerase active site. Binding changes the shape of the enzyme and locks the active site in an inactive form, so it cannot extend DNA. This is noncompetitive inhibition, and no phosphorylation is needed. Doravirine's structure makes flexible contacts in the pocket, so it still binds when common mutations such as K103N, Y181C and G190A change the pocket shape. Resistance can still develop, often through V106 or F227 changes. It does not work against HIV-2, whose reverse transcriptase lacks the pocket.", "body": [["Immune system", "It suppresses HIV replication, lowering viral load and allowing CD4 recovery."], ["Brain and sleep", "Dizziness, abnormal dreams and sleep problems can occur, but less often than with efavirenz."], ["Lipids", "It has little effect on cholesterol and triglycerides, and in trials it lowered LDL compared with efavirenz or boosted darunavir."], ["Gut", "Nausea and diarrhea can occur and are usually mild."], ["Skin", "Rash can occur but is less common than with older NNRTIs."]], "journey": "Doravirine is taken by mouth once daily, with or without food, and peaks within one to four hours. It is about 76 percent protein bound. It is metabolized mainly by CYP3A in the liver and leaves mostly in the feces. Its half-life is about 15 hours. Strong CYP3A inducers such as rifampin, carbamazepine and St. John's wort sharply lower its levels and are not used with it. It does not itself induce or inhibit CYP enzymes much.", "sidefx": "Doravirine was designed to avoid the problems of older NNRTIs. Brain side effects, including dizziness and odd dreams, appear related to central nervous system exposure but are less frequent than with efavirenz. Rash and liver injury are uncommon. The main practical risk is loss of effect when CYP3A inducers drive levels down, which can let resistance develop. Immune reconstitution syndrome can occur. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Doravirine has a neutral effect on lipids, which is one reason it is chosen when switching people off regimens that raised their cholesterol.", "src": ["Pifeltro (doravirine) FDA label", "Delstrigo FDA label", "Orkin C et al. DRIVE-AHEAD. Clin Infect Dis, 2019"]}, "nevirapine": {"origin": "Nevirapine was discovered by Boehringer Ingelheim scientists in Ridgefield, Connecticut, in the late 1980s while screening compounds against HIV reverse transcriptase. It belonged to a new group, the non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors, which bind outside the active site. The FDA approved it as Viramune in 1996, the first NNRTI. In 1999 the HIVNET 012 trial in Uganda showed that a single dose to the mother in labor and one to the newborn cut mother-to-child HIV transmission by nearly half, which made nevirapine a key tool in low-income countries. Its use has declined because of serious liver and skin reactions and the arrival of safer drugs.", "timeline": [["1990", "Boehringer Ingelheim scientists report nevirapine as an HIV-1 reverse transcriptase inhibitor."], ["1996", "FDA approves nevirapine (Viramune), the first NNRTI."], ["1999", "The HIVNET 012 trial shows single-dose nevirapine reduces mother-to-child transmission."], ["2011", "FDA approves extended-release Viramune XR."]], "cell": "Nevirapine binds the hydrophobic NNRTI pocket in the p66 subunit of HIV-1 reverse transcriptase, close to but separate from the polymerase active site. Binding distorts the enzyme and locks the catalytic site in an inactive shape, so the enzyme cannot add nucleotides to the growing DNA chain. It is a noncompetitive inhibitor and needs no activation by phosphorylation. HIV-2 and group O HIV-1 have a different pocket, so it does not work against them. A single mutation, such as K103N or Y181C, can cause high-level resistance, which is why single-dose use in pregnancy sometimes led to resistance in later treatment. Nevirapine also induces its own metabolism by activating CYP3A and CYP2B6.", "body": [["Immune system", "It suppresses HIV-1 replication as part of combination therapy."], ["Liver", "It can cause severe, sometimes fatal, hepatitis, especially in the first 18 weeks and in women with higher CD4 counts."], ["Skin", "Rash is common in the first weeks and can progress to Stevens-Johnson syndrome or toxic epidermal necrolysis."], ["Immune system (hypersensitivity)", "A hypersensitivity syndrome with fever, rash and organ involvement (DRESS) can occur."], ["Drug interactions", "Induction of CYP3A and CYP2B6 lowers levels of many drugs, including hormonal contraceptives and methadone."]], "journey": "Nevirapine is taken by mouth and is over 90 percent absorbed, peaking in about four hours. It is about 60 percent protein bound and crosses the placenta and into breast milk easily, which is why it worked for preventing transmission to newborns. It is metabolized by CYP3A and CYP2B6 and induces both, so its own half-life falls from about 45 hours after one dose to about 25 to 30 hours with ongoing use. A lead-in period at the start of therapy allows this induction to settle and lowers the rash risk. Metabolites leave mainly in the urine as glucuronides.", "sidefx": "The liver and skin reactions are thought to be immune-mediated, involving drug-specific T cells and certain HLA types, which is why risk is highest early, and higher in women with CD4 counts above 250 and men above 400 cells per microliter. These reactions can be fatal, and the drug is not restarted after a serious reaction. Its CYP induction explains many interactions. Steroids do not prevent the rash. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Because nevirapine crosses the placenta so easily, a single dose to the mother in labor reached protective levels in the newborn.", "src": ["Viramune (nevirapine) FDA label", "Guay LA et al. HIVNET 012. Lancet, 1999", "Merluzzi VJ et al. Science, 1990"]}, "fostemsavir": {"origin": "Fostemsavir came from Bristol-Myers Squibb research on small molecules that block HIV from attaching to CD4 T cells. BMS scientists discovered temsavir, which binds the HIV envelope protein gp120, but its poor solubility limited absorption. They solved this by attaching a phosphonooxymethyl group, creating the prodrug fostemsavir, which gut enzymes convert back to temsavir. The program moved to ViiV Healthcare, and the FDA approved fostemsavir as Rukobia in 2020 for heavily treatment-experienced adults with multidrug-resistant HIV-1 who cannot form a working regimen otherwise. It was the first attachment inhibitor.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Bristol-Myers Squibb discovers temsavir and develops the prodrug fostemsavir."], ["2016", "ViiV Healthcare acquires the program."], ["2020", "FDA approves fostemsavir (Rukobia), the first HIV attachment inhibitor."]], "cell": "HIV enters a T cell in steps. First, gp120 on the viral surface binds the CD4 receptor, which changes gp120's shape and exposes the site that then binds the co-receptor (CCR5 or CXCR4), and finally gp41 drives membrane fusion. Temsavir binds gp120 in a pocket near the CD4 binding site, under the beta-20/21 loop, and locks gp120 in its closed, prefusion shape. gp120 then cannot bind CD4, so the very first step of entry fails. Because it acts on gp120 before co-receptor binding, it works against both CCR5- and CXCR4-tropic virus. It has no cross-resistance with other classes, though some strains carry natural gp120 polymorphisms (for example at S375, M426 or M434) that reduce susceptibility.", "body": [["Immune system", "It blocks new cell infection in people with few remaining drug options, lowering viral load and raising CD4 counts."], ["Heart", "At high exposures it can prolong the QT interval, so it is used carefully with other QT drugs or heart disease."], ["Gut", "Nausea, diarrhea and abdominal pain are common."], ["Immune reconstitution", "As immunity returns, hidden infections can flare."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises can occur, especially in hepatitis B or C coinfection."]], "journey": "Fostemsavir is taken by mouth as an extended-release tablet. Alkaline phosphatase on the surface of the gut lining cleaves it to temsavir, which is absorbed; little fostemsavir reaches the blood. Temsavir peaks in about two hours and is about 88 percent protein bound. It is broken down by esterase hydrolysis and by CYP3A4, and its metabolites leave mainly in the urine, with some in feces. Its half-life is about 11 hours, so it is taken twice daily. Strong CYP3A4 inducers such as rifampin are contraindicated.", "sidefx": "Fostemsavir is generally well tolerated. QT prolongation was seen at higher than standard exposures and is the reason for caution with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors and other QT drugs. Gut side effects are common and usually mild. Strong CYP3A inducers can drop temsavir levels so low that the virus escapes. It raises levels of some statins and estrogen, so those are adjusted. Immune reconstitution syndrome reflects immune recovery. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Fostemsavir works at the very first moment of infection, before the virus even touches its co-receptor.", "src": ["Rukobia (fostemsavir) FDA label", "Kozal M et al. BRIGHTE trial. N Engl J Med, 2020"]}, "ibalizumab": {"origin": "Ibalizumab began as a mouse antibody against the human CD4 receptor, known as 5A8, and was humanized in the 1990s. It was developed under the name TNX-355 by the biotech company Tanox, then taken forward by TaiMed Biologics in Taiwan, with Theratechnologies handling US marketing. The FDA approved it as Trogarzo in 2018 for heavily treatment-experienced adults with multidrug-resistant HIV-1 failing their current regimen. It was the first monoclonal antibody approved for HIV and the first antiretroviral with a new mechanism in about a decade. A faster IV push version was later approved.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Humanized anti-CD4 antibody (later named ibalizumab) is developed from the mouse antibody 5A8."], ["2000s", "Tanox develops it as TNX-355; TaiMed Biologics continues development."], ["2018", "FDA approves ibalizumab (Trogarzo) for multidrug-resistant HIV-1."]], "cell": "Ibalizumab is a humanized IgG4 monoclonal antibody that binds domain 2 of the CD4 receptor on T cells, on the side opposite from where MHC class II molecules bind (domain 1). Because it does not block the MHC class II site, it does not interfere with normal CD4 immune signaling and does not cause immune suppression. HIV gp120 can still bind CD4 domain 1, but the antibody sitting on domain 2 adds bulk and rigidity that prevent the post-attachment shape changes in the CD4-gp120 complex. gp120 therefore cannot engage the co-receptor (CCR5 or CXCR4), and fusion does not happen. It works against both CCR5- and CXCR4-tropic virus, and there is no cross-resistance with other classes. Resistance can come from loss of a glycosylation site in the gp120 V5 loop.", "body": [["Immune system", "It blocks HIV entry into CD4 T cells without depleting them, lowering viral load in people with few other options."], ["Infusion", "Infusion-related reactions can occur, though they are uncommon."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and nausea are the most common side effects."], ["Skin", "Rash can occur, though it is usually mild and short-lived."], ["Immune reconstitution", "As immunity recovers, it can unmask hidden infections."]], "journey": "Ibalizumab is given as an IV infusion or IV push, starting with a loading dose and then every two weeks. It stays mostly in the blood and on CD4 cells. Its clearance depends on how much CD4 it is bound to, so its kinetics are nonlinear, with a half-life of about three to three and a half days at maintenance dosing. Like other IgG antibodies, it is broken down into amino acids by protein catabolism and is not processed by CYP enzymes, so it has very few drug interactions.", "sidefx": "Because it binds a part of CD4 not used for immune signaling, it does not suppress the immune system or lower CD4 counts. Infusion reactions come from the body's response to a large protein. Gut side effects and rash are usually mild. It is always combined with other active drugs because the virus can escape a single agent. Immune reconstitution syndrome reflects immune recovery. There is no reversal agent.", "fun": "Ibalizumab blocks HIV by binding the human receptor, not the virus, which makes it unusual among antiretrovirals.", "src": ["Trogarzo (ibalizumab-uiyk) FDA label", "Emu B et al. N Engl J Med, 2018"]}, "maraviroc": {"origin": "In 1996 several research groups discovered that HIV needs a second receptor, the chemokine receptor CCR5, to infect cells, and that people born with two copies of a CCR5 gene deletion (CCR5-delta32) were highly resistant to HIV infection. This made CCR5 an attractive human target. Pfizer scientists in Sandwich, England, screened for small molecules that block CCR5 and optimized a lead into maraviroc. The FDA approved it as Selzentry in 2007, the first CCR5 antagonist and the first HIV drug that targets a human protein rather than the virus. A tropism test is required first, because it only works against virus that uses CCR5.", "timeline": [["1996", "CCR5 is identified as an HIV co-receptor, and the CCR5-delta32 deletion is linked to resistance."], ["2007", "FDA approves maraviroc (Selzentry), the first CCR5 antagonist."], ["2009", "Maraviroc is approved for treatment-naive adults with CCR5-tropic HIV."]], "cell": "After HIV gp120 binds CD4, it changes shape and must then bind a co-receptor, CCR5 or CXCR4, to trigger gp41-driven membrane fusion. Maraviroc binds a hydrophobic pocket inside the transmembrane helices of CCR5. It is a slowly reversible negative allosteric modulator: binding changes the shape of the receptor's outside loops so that gp120 can no longer recognize it. The virus cannot complete entry. It is inactive against CXCR4-tropic or dual/mixed-tropic virus, which can use the other co-receptor, so tropism testing is done first. Resistance can come from the virus switching to CXCR4 or from gp120 changes that let it use the maraviroc-bound form of CCR5. Because CCR5 is a human chemokine receptor, it also dampens some normal chemokine signaling.", "body": [["Immune system", "It blocks CCR5-tropic HIV from entering cells, lowering viral load and raising CD4 counts."], ["Lungs and upper airway", "Upper respiratory infections and cough are reported, possibly because CCR5 helps immune cells reach infection sites."], ["Liver", "It carries a boxed warning for hepatotoxicity, which can start with a rash or allergic features."], ["Blood pressure", "It can cause postural hypotension, especially at high exposures or with kidney disease."], ["Skin", "Rash occurs, and rarely severe reactions such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome."]], "journey": "Maraviroc is taken by mouth twice daily and peaks in about half an hour to four hours, with modest bioavailability. It is about 76 percent protein bound. It is metabolized by CYP3A in the liver and is a P-glycoprotein substrate, so its dose depends on what other drugs are taken: CYP3A inhibitors raise its levels and inducers lower them. It leaves mainly in the feces, with some in urine. Its half-life is about 14 to 18 hours.", "sidefx": "Liver injury can be part of a systemic allergic reaction, and stopping the drug is required if signs of hepatitis appear. Postural hypotension may come from effects on blood vessel tone at higher levels. Because CCR5 helps recruit immune cells, infections and possible cancer risk were studied; no clear excess was found in trials, but concern remains theoretical. Drug interactions come from its CYP3A metabolism. Immune reconstitution syndrome can occur. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The Berlin patient, Timothy Ray Brown, was cured of HIV after a stem cell transplant from a donor with two copies of the CCR5-delta32 deletion, the same receptor maraviroc blocks.", "src": ["Selzentry (maraviroc) FDA label", "Dorr P et al. Antimicrob Agents Chemother, 2005", "Hutter G et al. N Engl J Med, 2009"]}, "enfuvirtide": {"origin": "Enfuvirtide was discovered at Duke University in the early 1990s by Thomas Matthews, Dani Bolognesi and colleagues, who found that synthetic peptides copied from part of the HIV gp41 protein could block the virus from fusing with cells. They founded the company Trimeris to develop the peptide, then called T-20, and partnered with Roche. Making a 36-amino-acid peptide on a commercial scale was a huge chemical challenge, requiring over 100 manufacturing steps. The FDA approved enfuvirtide as Fuzeon in 2003, the first HIV fusion inhibitor. It is injected twice daily under the skin and is now used rarely, in people with highly resistant virus who have few other options.", "timeline": [["1994", "Duke researchers report that gp41-derived peptides block HIV fusion."], ["2003", "FDA approves enfuvirtide (Fuzeon), the first HIV fusion inhibitor."]], "cell": "After HIV gp120 binds CD4 and a co-receptor, the gp41 protein springs open and inserts its fusion peptide into the host cell membrane. Each gp41 then folds back on itself: its heptad repeat 2 (HR2) region zips onto the heptad repeat 1 (HR1) region, forming a six-helix bundle that pulls the viral and cell membranes together so they fuse. Enfuvirtide is a synthetic peptide that copies the HR2 sequence. It binds the HR1 groove in this short-lived intermediate state and blocks the six-helix bundle from forming, so the membranes cannot fuse and the virus cannot enter. It acts outside the cell, before entry. Resistance comes from mutations in the HR1 region (amino acids 36 to 45) that lower peptide binding.", "body": [["Immune system", "Combined with other active drugs, it lowers viral load and raises CD4 counts in people with highly resistant HIV."], ["Skin (injection site)", "Almost everyone gets injection site reactions such as pain, redness, hard nodules and itching, which can cause cysts or infection."], ["Lungs", "Bacterial pneumonia was more common in people taking it in trials."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral neuropathy and insomnia have been reported."], ["Immune system (hypersensitivity)", "Rare systemic reactions include rash, fever, low blood pressure and kidney or liver involvement."]], "journey": "Enfuvirtide is injected under the skin twice daily and is absorbed slowly, peaking about four to eight hours later, with bioavailability of about 84 percent. It is about 92 percent protein bound and stays mostly in the blood. Like other peptides, it is broken down by proteases into its amino acids, which the body recycles. It is not a CYP substrate. Its half-life is about four hours.", "sidefx": "Injection site reactions come from local inflammation in response to the peptide depot under the skin and are the main reason people stop. The higher rate of bacterial pneumonia is not fully explained. Systemic hypersensitivity is immune-mediated and means the drug is not restarted. Because it is a peptide broken down by proteases, it has almost no CYP drug interactions. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Manufacturing enfuvirtide required one of the largest-scale peptide syntheses ever attempted for a medicine at the time.", "src": ["Fuzeon (enfuvirtide) FDA label", "Wild CT et al. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA, 1994", "Lalezari JP et al. TORO 1. N Engl J Med, 2003"]}, "ledipasvir-sofosbuvir": {"origin": "Hepatitis C virus was identified in 1989 by Michael Houghton and colleagues, building on the work of Harvey Alter and later Charles Rice, and the three shared the 2020 Nobel Prize. For years treatment relied on interferon injections that cured about half of patients and caused severe side effects. Sofosbuvir was discovered at Pharmasset by a team led by Michael Sofia, and its name honors him. Gilead bought Pharmasset in 2011 for about 11 billion dollars, and the FDA approved sofosbuvir as Sovaldi in 2013. Gilead also developed ledipasvir, an NS5A inhibitor, and the FDA approved the combination as Harvoni in 2014. It was the first single-tablet, interferon-free, ribavirin-free regimen for genotype 1 HCV, the most common type in the US, and cured most patients in 8 to 12 weeks.", "timeline": [["1989", "Michael Houghton and colleagues identify the hepatitis C virus."], ["2011", "Gilead acquires Pharmasset, the developer of sofosbuvir."], ["2013", "FDA approves sofosbuvir (Sovaldi)."], ["2014", "FDA approves ledipasvir and sofosbuvir (Harvoni) as a single tablet."], ["2020", "Alter, Houghton and Rice receive the Nobel Prize for the discovery of HCV."]], "cell": "HCV is an RNA virus that copies itself in membrane-bound replication complexes in liver cells. Sofosbuvir is a phosphoramidate prodrug of a uridine nucleotide. Inside liver cells, cathepsin A or carboxylesterase 1 and then HINT1 remove the protecting groups, and cell kinases add phosphates to make the active triphosphate (GS-461203). The HCV NS5B RNA-dependent RNA polymerase adds this to the growing RNA chain, and its 2'-fluoro-2'-C-methyl sugar blocks the next nucleotide, so the chain stops. Because it targets the conserved active site, it works across genotypes and has a high barrier to resistance. Ledipasvir inhibits NS5A, a phosphoprotein with no enzyme activity that is needed both to build the replication complex and to assemble new virions. Blocking two different steps prevents resistant virus from escaping.", "body": [["Liver", "Clearing HCV stops ongoing inflammation, which can halt fibrosis and lower the risk of cirrhosis, liver failure and liver cancer."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue, headache and nausea are the most common side effects and are usually mild."], ["Heart", "With amiodarone, severe symptomatic bradycardia and even cardiac arrest have occurred."], ["Liver (hepatitis B)", "Clearing HCV can let hepatitis B reactivate in coinfected people, sometimes causing fulminant hepatitis."], ["Drug interactions", "Acid reducers lower ledipasvir absorption, and P-gp inducers such as rifampin and St. John's wort lower both drugs."]], "journey": "The tablet is taken by mouth once daily. Ledipasvir needs stomach acid to dissolve, so acid suppressants reduce its absorption. It is over 99.8 percent protein bound, undergoes little metabolism, and leaves mainly in bile and feces, with a half-life of about 47 hours. Sofosbuvir is absorbed quickly and taken up by the liver, where it is activated. In the blood it is rapidly converted to the inactive metabolite GS-331007, which leaves through the kidneys with a half-life of about 27 hours. Both are P-glycoprotein substrates.", "sidefx": "Most patients feel few side effects because the targets are viral enzymes. The bradycardia with amiodarone is not fully understood but likely involves sofosbuvir affecting calcium handling in heart pacemaker cells combined with amiodarone's own effects. HBV reactivation happens because HCV had been suppressing HBV, and removing HCV releases it, which is a boxed warning; HBV status is checked before treatment. Acid reducers and P-gp inducers can cause treatment failure. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The name sofosbuvir comes from Michael Sofia, the chemist who led the team that discovered it.", "src": ["Harvoni (ledipasvir and sofosbuvir) FDA label", "Sofia MJ et al. J Med Chem, 2010", "Choo QL et al. Science, 1989"]}, "brincidofovir": {"origin": "Cidofovir is a nucleotide analog that works against many DNA viruses, including smallpox-family poxviruses, but it must be given IV and can badly damage the kidneys. Karl Hostetler at the University of California, San Diego, developed a strategy of attaching a lipid tail to antivirals so they would be absorbed by mouth and enter cells like natural fats. A hexadecyloxypropyl-cidofovir conjugate, later named brincidofovir (CMX001), was developed by Chimerix. Because smallpox no longer exists in nature, it could not be tested in people with the disease. The FDA approved it as Tembexa in 2021 under the Animal Rule for smallpox, based on animal studies and human safety data, as part of US biodefense preparedness. It was also made available under emergency protocols during the 2022 mpox outbreak.", "timeline": [["1980", "Smallpox is declared eradicated by the World Health Organization."], ["1996", "Cidofovir is approved in the US for CMV retinitis."], ["2000s", "Chimerix develops brincidofovir (CMX001), a lipid conjugate of cidofovir."], ["2021", "FDA approves brincidofovir (Tembexa) for smallpox under the Animal Rule."]], "cell": "Brincidofovir is cidofovir attached to a lipid chain. The lipid lets it cross the gut and enter cells through lipid uptake pathways, rather than relying on the organic anion transporters that concentrate cidofovir in kidney tubule cells. Inside cells, a phospholipase cleaves off the lipid, releasing cidofovir. Cell kinases add two phosphates to make cidofovir diphosphate, the active form. It competes with deoxycytidine triphosphate (dCTP) for the orthopoxvirus DNA polymerase and is built into the viral DNA, which slows and then stops DNA synthesis. Cidofovir does not need a viral kinase to be activated, which helps it act against many DNA viruses. Cidofovir diphosphate stays inside cells for a long time, so effect persists.", "body": [["Infected tissues", "It blocks poxvirus DNA replication, which in animals reduced deaths from smallpox-like infections."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea, vomiting and abdominal pain are common, and the gut is the main site of toxicity."], ["Liver", "It can raise ALT, AST and bilirubin, so liver tests are checked."], ["Kidneys", "Its lipid design limits uptake by kidney tubule cells, giving much less kidney toxicity than IV cidofovir."], ["Reproduction and cancer risk", "Animal studies suggested embryo-fetal toxicity, carcinogenicity and harm to male fertility."]], "journey": "Brincidofovir is taken by mouth as tablets or a suspension as a short course, and peaks in about three hours. It is over 99.9 percent protein bound in the blood. Cells take it up, cleave it to cidofovir and phosphorylate it. Some is oxidized, partly by CYP4F2. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. Brincidofovir has a half-life of about 19 hours, but intracellular cidofovir diphosphate lasts much longer, which is why the course is so short.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea and gut injury are thought to come from high drug levels in gut lining cells, where it is absorbed. Liver enzyme rises reflect liver uptake. The boxed warning about increased mortality with longer use comes from a trial in stem cell transplant recipients given it for CMV prevention, where more people died with brincidofovir than placebo, which is why its smallpox course is short. Kidney toxicity is low because it avoids the transporters that concentrate cidofovir in tubules. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Tembexa is one of only a few drugs approved in the US to treat a disease that no longer occurs naturally anywhere on Earth.", "src": ["Tembexa (brincidofovir) FDA label", "Hostetler KY. Antiviral Res, 2009", "FDA Animal Rule guidance"]}, "emtricitabine-tenofovir-disoproxil": {"origin": "Tenofovir was born from a collaboration between Czech chemist Antonin Holy at the Institute of Organic Chemistry and Biochemistry in Prague and Belgian virologist Erik De Clercq at the Rega Institute in Leuven, who in the 1980s designed acyclic nucleoside phosphonates. Gilead developed the oral prodrug tenofovir disoproxil fumarate, approved as Viread in 2001. Emtricitabine was discovered at Emory University by Dennis Liotta, Raymond Schinazi and Woo-Baeg Choi, and approved as Emtriva in 2003. Gilead combined them as Truvada, approved in 2004 for HIV treatment. In 2012, after the iPrEx trial showed that daily Truvada protected HIV-negative people from infection, the FDA approved it as the first medicine for pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP).", "timeline": [["1980s", "Holy and De Clercq design acyclic nucleoside phosphonates, leading to tenofovir."], ["2001", "FDA approves tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (Viread)."], ["2003", "FDA approves emtricitabine (Emtriva)."], ["2004", "FDA approves emtricitabine and tenofovir disoproxil (Truvada)."], ["2012", "FDA approves Truvada as the first HIV pre-exposure prophylaxis."]], "cell": "Both drugs are reverse transcriptase inhibitors that act as chain terminators. Emtricitabine is a cytidine analog. Cell kinases add three phosphates to make emtricitabine triphosphate, which competes with dCTP. Tenofovir disoproxil is a prodrug: esterases remove the disoproxil groups to release tenofovir, an acyclic adenosine monophosphate analog that already carries one phosphonate, so cells only need to add two phosphates to make tenofovir diphosphate, which competes with dATP. HIV reverse transcriptase builds these into the viral DNA, but they lack a normal 3'-hydroxyl group, so no further nucleotide can be added and the DNA chain stops. Both also inhibit hepatitis B polymerase. In PrEP, enough active drug in cells before exposure means the virus cannot complete reverse transcription and infection does not take hold.", "body": [["Immune system", "In treatment it suppresses HIV replication, and in PrEP it prevents infection when taken consistently."], ["Kidneys", "Tenofovir can injure proximal tubule cells, causing Fanconi syndrome, phosphate wasting and acute kidney injury."], ["Bones", "Tenofovir can lower bone mineral density, partly through phosphate loss from the kidney."], ["Liver (hepatitis B)", "Both drugs suppress HBV, so stopping them in coinfected people can trigger a severe hepatitis flare."], ["Skin", "Emtricitabine can darken the skin of the palms and soles."], ["Gut", "Nausea and diarrhea are common early and usually fade."]], "journey": "The tablet is taken by mouth once daily. Emtricitabine is well absorbed and peaks in one to two hours. Tenofovir disoproxil is absorbed and quickly hydrolyzed to tenofovir, which peaks in about an hour. Neither is a CYP substrate, and both are barely protein bound. Inside cells, the active phosphorylated forms last much longer than the plasma half-lives of about 10 hours for emtricitabine and 17 hours for tenofovir. Both leave unchanged through the kidneys by filtration and active tubular secretion, so kidney function guides use.", "sidefx": "Kidney injury happens because tenofovir is pumped into proximal tubule cells by organic anion transporters OAT1 and OAT3, where it can damage mitochondria, causing Fanconi syndrome. Bone loss is linked to this phosphate wasting and to direct bone effects. Lactic acidosis is a class risk from nucleoside analogs affecting mitochondrial DNA polymerase gamma, though it is rare with these drugs. Severe HBV flares after stopping happen because HBV is no longer suppressed, which is a boxed warning. For PrEP, HIV infection must be ruled out first to avoid resistance. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Antonin Holy's work on tenofovir and related drugs brought so much licensing income to his Prague institute that it funded a major part of its research for years.", "src": ["Truvada (emtricitabine and tenofovir disoproxil fumarate) FDA label", "Grant RM et al. iPrEx. N Engl J Med, 2010", "De Clercq E, Holy A. Nat Rev Drug Discov, 2005"]}, "dolutegravir-lamivudine": {"origin": "Dovato combines two drugs from different eras. Lamivudine (3TC) was discovered by Bernard Belleau and colleagues at BioChem Pharma in Montreal, licensed to Glaxo, and approved in the US in 1995; it became a cornerstone of HIV and hepatitis B therapy. Dolutegravir was discovered through a partnership between Shionogi in Japan and ViiV Healthcare and approved as Tivicay in 2013. It had a high barrier to resistance, which raised the idea that two drugs might be enough instead of the traditional three. The GEMINI 1 and 2 trials showed dolutegravir plus lamivudine worked as well as a three-drug regimen in people starting treatment. The FDA approved Dovato in 2019 as the first complete, once-daily, two-drug single-tablet regimen for treatment-naive adults.", "timeline": [["1995", "FDA approves lamivudine (Epivir)."], ["2013", "FDA approves dolutegravir (Tivicay)."], ["2019", "FDA approves dolutegravir and lamivudine (Dovato) as a two-drug complete regimen."], ["2020", "FDA approves Dovato for switching in people already virally suppressed."]], "cell": "Dolutegravir is an integrase strand transfer inhibitor. It binds the integrase-viral DNA complex (intasome), chelates the two magnesium ions in the active site and displaces the terminal viral DNA base, so the viral DNA cannot be joined into host chromosomes. It binds tightly and leaves the complex slowly, which gives it a high barrier to resistance compared with raltegravir. Lamivudine is a cytidine analog. Cell kinases convert it to lamivudine triphosphate, which competes with dCTP for HIV reverse transcriptase and is built into viral DNA, but it lacks a 3'-hydroxyl group, so the chain stops. Lamivudine also inhibits hepatitis B polymerase. Resistance to lamivudine comes from the M184V/I mutation, which also makes the virus less fit.", "body": [["Immune system", "Two drugs acting at different steps suppress HIV replication, lowering viral load and raising CD4 counts."], ["Kidneys (lab value)", "Dolutegravir blocks the OCT2 transporter that secretes creatinine, so serum creatinine rises slightly without true kidney damage."], ["Brain and sleep", "Insomnia, headache and, less often, depression or suicidal thoughts can occur."], ["Weight", "Integrase inhibitors, including dolutegravir, are linked to more weight gain than some older drugs."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises can occur, and hepatitis B can flare severely after stopping in coinfected people."], ["Immune system (hypersensitivity)", "Rare hypersensitivity reactions with rash and organ involvement can occur."]], "journey": "Dovato is taken by mouth once daily, with or without food. Dolutegravir peaks in two to three hours, is over 98.9 percent protein bound, is metabolized mainly by UGT1A1 with a minor CYP3A role, and leaves mainly in the feces, with a half-life of about 14 hours. Lamivudine peaks in about an hour, is barely protein bound, is hardly metabolized and leaves unchanged in the urine. Its active triphosphate lasts about 18 hours inside cells. Antacids, calcium and iron bind dolutegravir and reduce absorption.", "sidefx": "The small creatinine rise is a transporter effect, not true kidney injury. Sleep and mood effects are linked to integrase inhibitors and are not fully explained. Hypersensitivity is immune-mediated and requires stopping the drug. Severe HBV flares after stopping occur because lamivudine had been suppressing HBV; and because Dovato has only one drug active against HBV, it is not used in people with hepatitis B. Lactic acidosis is a rare nucleoside class effect from mitochondrial toxicity. Polyvalent cations bind dolutegravir through its metal-chelating core. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Lamivudine's molecule has an unusual shape: it is the unnatural mirror-image (L) form of a nucleoside, which helps explain its low toxicity to human cells.", "src": ["Dovato (dolutegravir and lamivudine) FDA label", "Cahn P et al. GEMINI 1 and 2. Lancet, 2019", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "luliconazole": {"origin": "Luliconazole is a fully synthetic imidazole antifungal created in Japan by the agrochemical and pharmaceutical company Nihon Nohyaku. Chemists there built on decades of azole work and added a ketene dithioacetal structure that made the molecule unusually potent against dermatophytes, the fungi that live on skin, hair and nails. It was first approved in Japan in 2005. In the United States the FDA approved it as Luzu cream in 2013. Its selling point was speed: because it binds so strongly and lingers in the outer skin layer, it allowed much shorter treatment courses for athlete's foot, jock itch and ringworm than older creams.", "timeline": [["2005", "Luliconazole is approved in Japan after development by Nihon Nohyaku."], ["2013", "FDA approves Luzu (luliconazole) cream for tinea pedis, cruris and corporis."]], "cell": "Fungal cell membranes use ergosterol instead of cholesterol. Ergosterol is made through a chain of enzymes, and one key step is removal of a methyl group from lanosterol by lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase, a fungal cytochrome P450 enzyme (CYP51). The imidazole nitrogen of luliconazole binds the heme iron inside CYP51 and blocks the enzyme. Ergosterol runs low while methylated sterol precursors pile up and get built into the membrane. The membrane becomes leaky and stiff, and membrane-bound enzymes such as chitin synthase work poorly. Dermatophytes such as Trichophyton rubrum stop growing and are shed with the outer skin as the skin renews itself.", "body": [["Skin (stratum corneum)", "The drug concentrates in the dead outer layer of skin where dermatophytes live, so fungal growth stops right where the infection is."], ["Itch and redness", "As fungal load falls, the inflammatory response to fungal proteins settles, so itching, scaling and redness fade over days to weeks."], ["Whole body", "Very little enters the bloodstream, so effects on the liver or other organs are not expected with normal skin use."]], "journey": "Luliconazole is rubbed onto the skin as a cream. It sinks into the stratum corneum and binds there, building a reservoir that stays for days after the last application. Only trace amounts pass through living skin into the blood. The small absorbed fraction is highly protein bound, broken down by the liver and cleared in urine and feces. Because almost nothing reaches the circulation, a systemic half-life is not clinically important.", "sidefx": "Side effects are rare and local. Burning or irritation can come from the cream base or from the drug touching inflamed, broken skin. Contact dermatitis is an allergic T-cell reaction to the drug or vehicle. Because so little is absorbed, the drug interactions seen with oral azoles, which block human CYP enzymes, are not expected. There is no antidote, and none is needed.", "fun": "Luliconazole is a single enantiomer (the R form). The mirror-image version is far less active, a reminder that enzyme pockets are shaped like a glove for one hand.", "src": ["Luzu (luliconazole) cream FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "tolnaftate": {"origin": "Tolnaftate is a synthetic thiocarbamate antifungal developed in Japan in the early 1960s. It reached American pharmacies in the mid-1960s under the brand name Tinactin and became one of the first effective antifungals people could buy without a prescription. For decades it was the standard over-the-counter treatment for athlete's foot and ringworm. Its mechanism was not understood until much later, when researchers showed that it blocks the same enzyme as terbinafine, even though the two drugs look different chemically.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Tolnaftate is developed in Japan and introduced in the US as Tinactin."], ["1990s", "Studies show it inhibits fungal squalene epoxidase, like the allylamines."]], "cell": "Tolnaftate inhibits squalene epoxidase, an early enzyme in the fungal ergosterol pathway that turns squalene into squalene epoxide. With this step blocked, two things happen. First, ergosterol supply drops, so the fungal cell membrane cannot be built or repaired properly. Second, squalene builds up inside the cell, and high squalene levels are thought to disrupt membranes and kill the cell. Dermatophytes such as Trichophyton and Epidermophyton are sensitive. Candida yeasts are not, which is why tolnaftate is not used for yeast infections.", "body": [["Skin of feet, groin and body", "Fungal growth in the outer skin stops, and as the skin sheds over several weeks the infected layer is replaced with healthy skin."], ["Itch and burning", "Symptoms ease within days as fungal activity and the inflammation it triggers settle."], ["Prevention", "Powders and sprays keep a layer of drug on skin that tends to get damp, which helps prevent athlete's foot from coming back."]], "journey": "Tolnaftate is applied to clean, dry skin as a cream, powder, liquid or spray. It stays in the outer layers of the skin and works locally. Absorption into the blood is negligible, so it is not meaningfully metabolized or excreted by the body. It leaves mostly as the treated skin flakes off, which is why twice-daily use is needed to keep levels up.", "sidefx": "Mild stinging comes from applying the product, especially liquids with alcohol, to cracked or raw skin. Rare contact dermatitis is an immune reaction to the drug or the vehicle. Because the drug is not absorbed, it causes no systemic side effects or drug interactions. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Tolnaftate does not work on Candida, so it can fail for a rash that looks like jock itch but is really a yeast infection.", "src": ["Tinactin (tolnaftate) Drug Facts label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "peginterferon-alfa-2a": {"origin": "Interferon was discovered in 1957 by Alick Isaacs and Jean Lindenmann in London, who saw that virus-infected cells released a substance that 'interfered' with infection of other cells. Recombinant DNA technology made it possible to produce interferon alfa in bacteria in the early 1980s, and it became a treatment for hepatitis. The problem was that plain interferon left the body within hours and had to be injected several times a week. Hoffmann-La Roche attached a large branched polyethylene glycol (PEG) chain to interferon alfa-2a, which slowed its clearance enough for once-weekly shots. The FDA approved Pegasys for hepatitis C in 2002 and for hepatitis B later. For years, peginterferon plus ribavirin was the backbone of hepatitis C cure. Oral direct-acting antivirals have since largely replaced it for hepatitis C.", "timeline": [["1957", "Isaacs and Lindenmann discover interferon."], ["1980s", "Recombinant interferon alfa is produced and used clinically."], ["2002", "FDA approves Pegasys (peginterferon alfa-2a) for chronic hepatitis C."], ["2010s", "Direct-acting oral antivirals replace interferon for most hepatitis C."]], "cell": "Peginterferon alfa-2a binds the type I interferon receptor, made of IFNAR1 and IFNAR2 chains. Binding brings together the kinases JAK1 and TYK2, which phosphorylate STAT1 and STAT2. These join IRF9 to form ISGF3, which moves into the nucleus and switches on hundreds of interferon-stimulated genes. Key products include 2',5'-oligoadenylate synthetase, which activates RNase L to chop up viral RNA; protein kinase R, which shuts down protein translation in infected cells; and Mx proteins that interfere with viral replication. Interferon also raises MHC class I on liver cells and activates NK cells and cytotoxic T cells, helping the immune system find and clear infected hepatocytes. The PEG chain does not change the action; it slows kidney clearance and breakdown so levels stay steady for a week.", "body": [["Liver", "Infected hepatocytes enter an antiviral state and become more visible to T cells, lowering hepatitis B or C virus levels. Liver enzymes may flare as immune cells attack infected cells."], ["Whole body (flu-like)", "Interferon triggers the same fever, chills, aches and fatigue seen in viral illness, because the body uses interferon as its own alarm signal."], ["Bone marrow", "Interferon slows production of white cells and platelets, which lowers neutrophil and platelet counts."], ["Brain and mood", "Interferon alters serotonin and other brain signaling, leading to depression, irritability and insomnia; suicide is a boxed warning."], ["Thyroid and immune system", "Immune activation can unmask or worsen autoimmune disease, especially thyroiditis with high or low thyroid levels."], ["Eyes", "Retinal changes such as cotton wool spots and hemorrhages can occur."]], "journey": "Peginterferon alfa-2a is a large protein injected under the skin once a week. It is absorbed slowly into the blood, peaking about three to four days after a dose. The bulky 40 kDa PEG chain keeps it mostly in the blood and liver and slows its filtration by the kidney. It is broken down like other proteins rather than by CYP enzymes. Its half-life is several days, far longer than plain interferon, which keeps levels fairly steady between weekly doses. Severe kidney disease slows its clearance.", "sidefx": "Nearly every side effect is the drug doing its job too broadly. Interferon is the body's own alarm signal, so flu-like symptoms are expected. The same signaling suppresses the bone marrow, causing neutropenia and low platelets. Immune activation explains autoimmune thyroid disease, colitis and flares of other autoimmune disorders. Effects on brain chemistry cause depression and, rarely, suicide. In advanced cirrhosis, the immune attack on the liver can cause decompensation. There is no antidote; the drug is held or stopped.", "fun": "The name 'interferon' came from its ability to interfere with viral infection; it was found long before scientists knew it was a protein made by our own cells.", "src": ["Pegasys (peginterferon alfa-2a) FDA label", "Isaacs A, Lindenmann J. Virus interference. Proc R Soc Lond B, 1957", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "elbasvir-grazoprevir": {"origin": "Hepatitis C virus was identified in 1989 by a team led by Michael Houghton at the Chiron Corporation, work later honored with the 2020 Nobel Prize along with Harvey Alter and Charles Rice. Once the virus could be grown and studied, drug makers searched for small molecules that block its own enzymes. Merck developed grazoprevir, a macrocyclic NS3/4A protease inhibitor, and elbasvir, an NS5A inhibitor, and paired them in one tablet. The FDA approved Zepatier in 2016 for chronic hepatitis C genotypes 1 and 4. It became one of several all-oral, interferon-free regimens that cure most patients in about three months.", "timeline": [["1989", "Hepatitis C virus is identified by Houghton and colleagues at Chiron."], ["2016", "FDA approves Zepatier (elbasvir and grazoprevir) for genotypes 1 and 4."], ["2020", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine honors the discovery of hepatitis C virus."]], "cell": "Hepatitis C is an RNA virus whose genome is translated into one long polyprotein that must be cut into working parts. Grazoprevir binds the active site of the NS3/4A serine protease, the viral enzyme that cleaves the nonstructural section of this polyprotein. Without cleavage, the virus cannot make mature NS3, NS4A, NS4B, NS5A and NS5B proteins. Elbasvir binds NS5A, a protein with no enzyme activity that organizes the membrane-bound replication complex and helps assemble new virus particles. Blocking NS5A stops both RNA copying and virion assembly. Hitting two separate targets makes resistance much less likely than with either drug alone. Since HCV has no DNA stage and no latent reservoir, stopping replication long enough lets infected liver cells turn over and the virus is cured.", "body": [["Liver", "Viral load falls within days, liver inflammation settles and liver enzymes normalize. Cure lowers the long-term risk of cirrhosis and liver cancer."], ["Blood", "Sustained absence of HCV RNA 12 weeks after treatment defines cure (sustained virologic response)."], ["Kidneys", "Neither drug depends on the kidneys for clearance, which made this combination useful in patients with advanced kidney disease and on dialysis."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue, headache and nausea are the most common complaints and are usually mild."]], "journey": "Both drugs are taken as one tablet by mouth once daily. They are absorbed in a few hours and are very highly protein bound. Grazoprevir needs the OATP1B1 and OATP1B3 transporters to enter liver cells, which is where it works and why OATP-blocking drugs raise its levels sharply. Both are partly broken down by CYP3A and pumped by P-glycoprotein, then leave almost entirely in the feces. Half-lives are about a day, supporting once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Late liver enzyme rises are linked to high grazoprevir levels inside liver cells, which is why drugs that block OATP1B or strongly inhibit CYP3A are avoided and why it is not used in moderate to severe cirrhosis. Hepatitis B can reactivate when hepatitis C is cleared quickly, because the HCV infection had been suppressing HBV; this is a boxed warning and patients are screened. With ribavirin, anemia comes from ribavirin breaking red cells. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Zepatier (elbasvir and grazoprevir) FDA label", "Choo QL et al. Science, 1989", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "sofosbuvir-velpatasvir-voxilaprevir": {"origin": "Sofosbuvir was discovered at Pharmasset, a small company in New Jersey, by a team led by chemist Michael Sofia. They designed a uridine nucleotide prodrug that liver cells could turn into an active chain terminator. Gilead bought Pharmasset in 2012, and sofosbuvir (Sovaldi) was approved in 2013, transforming hepatitis C care. Gilead then added velpatasvir, a pangenotypic NS5A inhibitor, to make Epclusa in 2016. Some patients still failed treatment, often because of NS5A resistance. To rescue them, Gilead added a third drug, the protease inhibitor voxilaprevir. The FDA approved Vosevi in 2017 as a three-drug, once-daily retreatment regimen for people whose earlier direct-acting antiviral treatment did not cure them.", "timeline": [["2013", "FDA approves sofosbuvir (Sovaldi), developed from Pharmasset research."], ["2016", "Sofosbuvir-velpatasvir (Epclusa) is approved for all genotypes."], ["2017", "FDA approves Vosevi for retreatment after prior direct-acting antiviral failure."]], "cell": "This tablet blocks three separate steps in hepatitis C replication. Sofosbuvir is a prodrug: inside liver cells, enzymes remove its protective groups and add phosphates to make the active uridine triphosphate analog GS-461203. The viral NS5B RNA-dependent RNA polymerase mistakes it for a natural nucleotide and adds it to the growing RNA strand, which then cannot be extended (chain termination). Because it targets the conserved active site, resistance is rare. Velpatasvir binds NS5A, which organizes the replication complex and virion assembly. Voxilaprevir binds the NS3/4A protease and blocks cutting of the viral polyprotein into working proteins. Hitting three targets gives a high barrier to resistance even in virus that already survived earlier drugs.", "body": [["Liver", "HCV RNA falls within days and liver inflammation settles; cure lowers future risk of cirrhosis and liver cancer."], ["Heart", "With amiodarone, sofosbuvir-containing regimens can cause serious slow heart rhythms, through a mechanism not fully understood."], ["Bilirubin", "Voxilaprevir blocks the OATP1B transporters and bilirubin handling, so mild bilirubin rises can occur."], ["Gut and whole body", "Headache, fatigue, diarrhea and nausea are common and usually mild."]], "journey": "The tablet is taken once daily with food. Sofosbuvir is absorbed quickly and converted in the liver to its active triphosphate; its main inactive metabolite GS-331007 circulates for about a day and leaves in the urine. Velpatasvir and voxilaprevir are absorbed over a few hours, are highly protein bound, are partly broken down by CYP enzymes and leave mainly through bile in the feces. Voxilaprevir is cleared by the liver, which is why it is not used in moderate to severe cirrhosis.", "sidefx": "Severe bradycardia with amiodarone is a known interaction of sofosbuvir-based regimens and can be fatal, so the combination is avoided or monitored closely. Hepatitis B reactivation is a boxed warning: removing HCV can release HBV from suppression. In advanced cirrhosis, protease inhibitors like voxilaprevir build up and can harm the liver. Drugs that strongly induce P-gp or CYP enzymes, such as rifampin, lower drug levels and risk treatment failure. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Sofosbuvir was so effective and so expensive at launch that it sparked a national debate about drug pricing in the United States.", "src": ["Vosevi (sofosbuvir, velpatasvir and voxilaprevir) FDA label", "Sofia MJ et al. J Med Chem, 2010", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "etravirine": {"origin": "Etravirine came from a structure-based design program at Janssen and its HIV-focused unit Tibotec, guided by Paul Janssen and working with structural biologist Eddy Arnold. Older NNRTIs like nevirapine and efavirenz fit their pocket in reverse transcriptase rigidly, so a single mutation such as K103N could knock them out. The team designed diarylpyrimidine (DAPY) molecules that are flexible, able to twist and reposition themselves to keep binding when the pocket changes shape. Etravirine was the first DAPY approved. The FDA approved Intelence in 2008 for treatment-experienced adults whose virus resisted older NNRTIs.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Janssen and Tibotec scientists begin designing flexible diarylpyrimidine NNRTIs."], ["2008", "FDA approves Intelence (etravirine) for treatment-experienced HIV-1 infection."]], "cell": "HIV-1 reverse transcriptase copies the viral RNA genome into DNA. Etravirine does not compete with nucleotides at the active site. Instead it binds a hydrophobic pocket near the active site, which forms only when the drug is present. Binding stiffens the enzyme and distorts the catalytic site, so it cannot add nucleotides (noncompetitive inhibition). Etravirine's flexible structure lets it rotate and shift within the pocket, so it still binds when mutations such as K103N or Y181C change the pocket's shape. Several mutations are needed together to cause high-level resistance. HIV-2 reverse transcriptase has a different pocket, so etravirine does not work against HIV-2.", "body": [["Immune system", "Lower viral replication allows CD4 T-cell counts to recover, lowering the risk of opportunistic infections."], ["Skin", "Rash is the most common side effect, usually mild and in the first weeks, but rarely severe reactions such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome or DRESS occur."], ["Liver", "Hypersensitivity reactions can include hepatitis, and liver enzymes may rise."], ["Gut", "Nausea and diarrhea are common early on."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral neuropathy has been reported."]], "journey": "Etravirine is taken by mouth twice daily after a meal, since fasting lowers absorption substantially. It is almost completely bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down through CYP3A4, CYP2C9 and CYP2C19, and it also induces CYP3A4 and inhibits CYP2C9 and CYP2C19, creating many drug interactions. Most of the drug leaves in the feces, with very little in urine. Its half-life is about 40 hours.", "sidefx": "Rash and hypersensitivity reflect an immune reaction to the drug, likely involving drug-specific T cells; severe forms such as SJS, TEN and DRESS require stopping the drug for good. Liver injury can be part of the hypersensitivity syndrome. Drug interactions are a major concern because etravirine both changes and depends on CYP enzymes. Immune reconstitution syndrome can occur as the recovering immune system attacks hidden infections. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The design team called etravirine's ability to keep binding despite mutations 'wiggling and jiggling', describing how the molecule shifts inside its pocket.", "src": ["Intelence (etravirine) FDA label", "Das K et al. J Med Chem, 2004", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "efavirenz-emtricitabine-tenofovir": {"origin": "Each part of this pill has its own story. Tenofovir grew out of work by Czech chemist Antonin Holy and Belgian virologist Erik De Clercq on acyclic nucleoside phosphonates; Gilead brought tenofovir disoproxil fumarate to market as Viread in 2001. Emtricitabine was discovered at Emory University by Dennis Liotta, Raymond Schinazi and colleagues and approved as Emtriva in 2003. Efavirenz was developed by Merck and DuPont Pharma and approved as Sustiva in 1998. In 2006 Bristol-Myers Squibb and Gilead formed an unusual joint venture to combine the three into Atripla, the first complete HIV regimen in a single once-daily tablet. It changed HIV care from handfuls of pills to one pill a day. The brand has since been discontinued in the US, but generic versions remain.", "timeline": [["1998", "Efavirenz is approved as Sustiva."], ["2001", "Tenofovir disoproxil fumarate is approved as Viread."], ["2003", "Emtricitabine is approved as Emtriva."], ["2006", "FDA approves Atripla, the first once-daily single-tablet complete HIV regimen."]], "cell": "All three drugs block HIV-1 reverse transcriptase, the enzyme that copies viral RNA into DNA, but in two different ways. Efavirenz is a non-nucleoside inhibitor (NNRTI): it binds an allosteric hydrophobic pocket next to the active site and locks the enzyme in an inactive shape. Emtricitabine, a cytidine analog, is phosphorylated by cellular kinases to its triphosphate. Tenofovir, an adenosine monophosphate analog, is released from its disoproxil prodrug and phosphorylated to tenofovir diphosphate. Reverse transcriptase builds these false nucleotides into viral DNA, and because they lack a 3'-hydroxyl group, the next nucleotide cannot attach, so the chain stops. Emtricitabine and tenofovir also block hepatitis B polymerase.", "body": [["Immune system", "Suppressing HIV replication lets CD4 T cells recover and lowers the risk of AIDS-defining illness."], ["Brain", "Efavirenz causes dizziness, vivid dreams, insomnia and sometimes depression or psychosis, which is why the pill is usually taken at bedtime."], ["Kidneys", "Tenofovir disoproxil gives high plasma tenofovir, which is taken up by proximal tubule cells and can injure them, causing Fanconi syndrome or kidney injury."], ["Bones", "Tenofovir disoproxil is linked to a small loss of bone mineral density."], ["Liver", "Efavirenz can raise liver enzymes. Stopping the pill can cause a hepatitis B flare in co-infected people."], ["Skin", "Efavirenz commonly causes an early rash, usually mild."]], "journey": "The tablet is taken once daily on an empty stomach, since food raises efavirenz levels and its nervous system side effects. Efavirenz is absorbed slowly, is highly protein bound, and is metabolized by CYP2B6 and CYP3A4; people with slow CYP2B6 genes have much higher levels. Efavirenz also induces CYP3A4, speeding the breakdown of many drugs. Tenofovir disoproxil is hydrolyzed in the gut and blood to tenofovir, which with emtricitabine is cleared by the kidneys. Efavirenz has a long half-life of about two days.", "sidefx": "Efavirenz nervous system effects likely relate to its actions on serotonin receptors and other brain targets, and are worse with high blood levels. Tenofovir kidney toxicity arises because proximal tubule cells concentrate the drug through organic anion transporters, which can damage their mitochondria. Bone loss likely follows tubular phosphate wasting. Hepatitis B flare after stopping happens because the HBV-suppressing drugs are removed. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Efavirenz, emtricitabine and tenofovir disoproxil fumarate FDA label (Atripla)", "De Clercq E, Holy A. Nat Rev Drug Discov, 2005", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "darunavir-cobicistat-emtricitabine-tenofovir-alafenamide": {"origin": "Darunavir was designed by chemist Arun Ghosh, who created a bis-tetrahydrofuran group that forms strong hydrogen bonds with the backbone of HIV protease. Because mutations rarely change the backbone, darunavir kept working against many resistant strains. Tibotec developed it and it was approved as Prezista in 2006. Cobicistat, a Gilead booster with no anti-HIV activity, was approved in combination products starting in 2012. Tenofovir alafenamide (TAF) is a newer Gilead prodrug of tenofovir designed to load lymphocytes with less tenofovir in the blood, first approved in 2015. Janssen combined these with emtricitabine to create Symtuza, approved by the FDA in 2018 as the first complete darunavir-based HIV regimen in a single tablet.", "timeline": [["2006", "Darunavir is approved as Prezista."], ["2012", "Cobicistat is first approved as part of a combination HIV tablet."], ["2015", "Tenofovir alafenamide is first approved in a combination product."], ["2018", "FDA approves Symtuza, the first single-tablet regimen built on darunavir."]], "cell": "HIV protease cuts the Gag and Gag-Pol polyproteins into working structural proteins and enzymes as new virions bud from the cell. Darunavir binds tightly in the protease active site, so the polyproteins are not cut and the released virus particles stay immature and noninfectious. Cobicistat is a mechanism-based inhibitor of CYP3A that slows darunavir breakdown, keeping its levels high. Emtricitabine (a cytidine analog) and tenofovir (an adenosine nucleotide analog) are phosphorylated inside cells to their active forms, which reverse transcriptase incorporates into viral DNA; lacking a 3'-hydroxyl group, they stop chain growth. TAF is converted to tenofovir mainly inside cells by cathepsin A, so cells get high tenofovir diphosphate levels while plasma tenofovir stays low.", "body": [["Immune system", "Viral suppression lets CD4 cells recover. The protease inhibitor component gives a high barrier to resistance."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea, gas and abdominal discomfort are common, mostly from darunavir."], ["Skin", "Darunavir contains a sulfonamide group, and rash is common; rarely severe reactions such as SJS or DRESS occur."], ["Liver", "Darunavir can cause hepatitis, especially with existing liver disease."], ["Metabolism", "Boosted protease inhibitors can raise cholesterol, triglycerides and blood sugar."], ["Kidneys", "Cobicistat blocks creatinine secretion, raising serum creatinine without true loss of filtration; TAF is gentler on the kidney than tenofovir disoproxil."]], "journey": "The tablet is taken once daily with food, which improves darunavir absorption. Darunavir and cobicistat are highly protein bound and are broken down by CYP3A in the liver; cobicistat's CYP3A blockade boosts darunavir and also raises levels of many other CYP3A drugs. Emtricitabine is cleared mostly unchanged by the kidneys. TAF has a plasma half-life of under an hour, but its active tenofovir diphosphate lasts a long time inside cells. Darunavir leaves mainly in the feces.", "sidefx": "Gut upset and rash come mainly from darunavir; its sulfonamide structure explains caution in sulfa allergy. Metabolic changes are a known effect of boosted protease inhibitors. Drug interactions are the biggest practical risk, because cobicistat strongly inhibits CYP3A and can raise levels of statins, sedatives, steroids and other drugs to toxic levels. A creatinine rise from cobicistat reflects blocked tubular secretion, not kidney damage. Stopping the pill can trigger a hepatitis B flare. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Symtuza (darunavir, cobicistat, emtricitabine, tenofovir alafenamide) FDA label", "Ghosh AK et al. Acc Chem Res, 2008", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "dolutegravir-abacavir-lamivudine": {"origin": "Lamivudine came from Bernard Belleau's group at BioChem Pharma in Montreal and was approved in 1995. Abacavir grew from carbocyclic nucleoside work by Robert Vince at the University of Minnesota and was approved as Ziagen in 1998. Dolutegravir was discovered through a collaboration between Shionogi and GlaxoSmithKline, later part of ViiV Healthcare, and approved as Tivicay in 2013. ViiV combined all three into Triumeq, approved by the FDA in 2014 as a single-tablet regimen built on an integrase inhibitor. A key safety advance came with abacavir: researchers found that its dangerous hypersensitivity reaction is strongly tied to the gene HLA-B*5701, so screening before treatment became standard and nearly eliminated the reaction.", "timeline": [["1995", "Lamivudine is approved."], ["1998", "Abacavir is approved as Ziagen."], ["2008", "The PREDICT-1 trial shows HLA-B*5701 screening prevents abacavir hypersensitivity."], ["2013", "Dolutegravir is approved as Tivicay."], ["2014", "FDA approves Triumeq."]], "cell": "HIV integrase inserts viral DNA into the host chromosome. Its active site holds two magnesium ions. Dolutegravir binds this site and chelates both magnesium ions while stacking against the viral DNA end, blocking the strand transfer step so viral DNA cannot integrate. Its long residence time on the enzyme helps give a high barrier to resistance. Abacavir is converted inside cells to carbovir triphosphate, a guanosine analog, and lamivudine to lamivudine triphosphate, a cytidine analog. Reverse transcriptase incorporates these into viral DNA, and since they lack a 3'-hydroxyl group, DNA synthesis stops. Lamivudine also blocks hepatitis B polymerase.", "body": [["Immune system", "Rapid viral suppression lets CD4 cells recover. Integrase inhibitors drop viral load faster than many other classes."], ["Immune hypersensitivity", "In people with HLA-B*5701, abacavir triggers a T-cell reaction with fever, rash, gut and breathing symptoms that can be fatal on rechallenge."], ["Brain", "Dolutegravir commonly causes insomnia, headache and abnormal dreams; depression has been reported."], ["Kidneys", "Dolutegravir blocks the OCT2 transporter that secretes creatinine, raising serum creatinine slightly without harming filtration."], ["Weight and metabolism", "Integrase inhibitor regimens are associated with weight gain."], ["Liver", "Hepatotoxicity can occur, and stopping lamivudine can cause a hepatitis B flare in co-infected people."]], "journey": "The tablet is taken once daily with or without food. Dolutegravir is highly protein bound and is cleared mainly by UGT1A1 glucuronidation with some CYP3A, leaving mostly in feces; its half-life is about 14 hours. Abacavir is broken down by alcohol dehydrogenase and glucuronidation, but its active carbovir triphosphate lasts much longer inside cells than in plasma. Lamivudine is mostly cleared unchanged by the kidneys. Antacids, iron and calcium bind dolutegravir in the gut and must be separated from doses.", "sidefx": "Abacavir hypersensitivity happens because the drug fits into the HLA-B*5701 molecule and changes which self peptides it shows to T cells, which then attack the body. That is why genetic screening is required and why rechallenge after a reaction is never done. Dolutegravir's nervous system effects are not fully explained. Polyvalent cations chelate dolutegravir in the gut, mirroring how it binds magnesium in integrase. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Triumeq (abacavir, dolutegravir, lamivudine) FDA label", "Mallal S et al. N Engl J Med, 2008 (PREDICT-1)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "doravirine-lamivudine-tenofovir": {"origin": "Doravirine was developed by Merck as a newer NNRTI designed to avoid the problems of efavirenz: nervous system side effects, cholesterol rises and loss of activity from common mutations like K103N. It also has fewer drug interactions because it neither induces nor inhibits major CYP enzymes. The FDA approved doravirine alone (Pifeltro) and the combination tablet Delstrigo, with lamivudine and tenofovir disoproxil fumarate, together in 2018. Lamivudine dates to BioChem Pharma in Montreal in the 1990s, and tenofovir to the nucleotide work of Antonin Holy and Erik De Clercq.", "timeline": [["1995", "Lamivudine is approved for HIV."], ["2001", "Tenofovir disoproxil fumarate is approved as Viread."], ["2018", "FDA approves doravirine as Pifeltro and in the combination tablet Delstrigo."]], "cell": "All three drugs target HIV-1 reverse transcriptase. Doravirine binds the allosteric NNRTI pocket near the active site and freezes the enzyme in an inactive shape. Its structure lets it keep binding in the presence of K103N, Y181C and G190A, mutations that defeat older NNRTIs. Lamivudine is phosphorylated to lamivudine triphosphate, a cytidine analog, and tenofovir disoproxil is converted to tenofovir and then phosphorylated to tenofovir diphosphate, an adenosine analog. Reverse transcriptase builds these into viral DNA, and the missing 3'-hydroxyl group stops further elongation. Lamivudine and tenofovir also inhibit hepatitis B polymerase.", "body": [["Immune system", "Viral suppression lets CD4 cells recover and lowers infection risk."], ["Brain", "Dizziness and abnormal dreams can occur, but less often than with efavirenz."], ["Blood lipids", "Doravirine has a neutral effect on cholesterol and triglycerides compared with efavirenz."], ["Kidneys", "Tenofovir disoproxil concentrates in proximal tubule cells and can cause kidney injury or Fanconi syndrome."], ["Bones", "Tenofovir disoproxil can lower bone mineral density."], ["Liver", "Stopping the tablet can cause a hepatitis B flare in co-infected people."]], "journey": "The tablet is taken once daily with or without food. Doravirine is absorbed in about two hours, is broken down by CYP3A in the liver and has a half-life of about 15 hours. Strong CYP3A inducers such as rifampin lower doravirine levels sharply and are avoided. Lamivudine and tenofovir are cleared mostly unchanged by the kidneys through filtration and tubular secretion, so kidney function matters.", "sidefx": "Doravirine is generally well tolerated; mild nervous system effects are less frequent than with efavirenz. Tenofovir kidney toxicity happens because organic anion transporters pull tenofovir into proximal tubule cells, where it can damage mitochondria. Phosphate wasting from tubular injury contributes to bone loss. Hepatitis B flare after stopping is a boxed warning. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Delstrigo (doravirine, lamivudine, tenofovir disoproxil fumarate) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "rilpivirine-emtricitabine-tenofovir-alafenamide": {"origin": "Rilpivirine is a second diarylpyrimidine NNRTI from the Janssen and Tibotec program that also produced etravirine. Its flexible structure keeps activity against some resistant strains and allows once-daily dosing. It was approved as Edurant in 2011, and the same year it was combined with emtricitabine and tenofovir disoproxil fumarate in Complera. Gilead later developed tenofovir alafenamide (TAF), a prodrug that delivers tenofovir into lymphocytes with lower blood levels, easing kidney and bone effects. The FDA approved Odefsey, with TAF in place of the older tenofovir, in 2016. Rilpivirine is also used as a long-acting injection with cabotegravir.", "timeline": [["2011", "Rilpivirine is approved as Edurant and in Complera."], ["2016", "FDA approves Odefsey (emtricitabine, rilpivirine, tenofovir alafenamide)."]], "cell": "Rilpivirine binds a hydrophobic allosteric pocket next to the active site of HIV-1 reverse transcriptase, stiffening the enzyme so it cannot make DNA. Emtricitabine is phosphorylated to emtricitabine triphosphate, a cytidine analog. TAF enters lymphocytes and is cleaved by cathepsin A to tenofovir, then phosphorylated to tenofovir diphosphate, an adenosine analog. Both nucleotide analogs compete with natural nucleotides; once added to viral DNA they block the next link because they lack a 3'-hydroxyl group. Together the two mechanisms suppress replication. Emtricitabine and tenofovir also inhibit hepatitis B polymerase.", "body": [["Immune system", "Viral suppression lets CD4 cells rise. This regimen is used when starting viral load is not very high, because rilpivirine failure is more common with high viral loads."], ["Brain and mood", "Headache, insomnia, abnormal dreams and depressed mood can occur."], ["Heart", "At high levels rilpivirine can prolong the QT interval."], ["Kidneys and bones", "TAF gives much lower plasma tenofovir than tenofovir disoproxil, so kidney and bone effects are smaller, though kidney problems can still occur."], ["Liver", "Stopping the tablet can cause a severe hepatitis B flare in co-infected people."]], "journey": "The tablet is taken once daily with a meal, because rilpivirine needs food and stomach acid to dissolve and be absorbed. Proton pump inhibitors lower absorption so much that they are not used with it. Rilpivirine is highly bound to albumin, metabolized by CYP3A and leaves mainly in feces, with a half-life of about two days. Emtricitabine is cleared by the kidneys. TAF is converted inside cells, so its plasma half-life is short while the active form lasts a long time inside lymphocytes.", "sidefx": "Depression and mood effects with rilpivirine are not fully explained but are listed in labeling. QT prolongation is dose related. Absorption depends on stomach acid, so acid-lowering drugs cause treatment failure and resistance. Strong CYP3A inducers lower levels. Severe rash and DRESS are immune reactions. Hepatitis B flare after stopping is a boxed warning. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Odefsey (emtricitabine, rilpivirine, tenofovir alafenamide) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "paromomycin": {"origin": "Paromomycin is an aminoglycoside antibiotic made by soil bacteria. It was isolated in the 1950s from a strain of Streptomyces (Streptomyces rimosus forma paromomycinus) by researchers at Parke-Davis, and it was marketed as Humatin. Like neomycin, it is barely absorbed from the gut, which made it useful for killing organisms that live inside the intestine. It became a standard luminal amebicide for clearing Entamoeba histolytica from the bowel. It is also given by injection in parts of the world to treat visceral leishmaniasis, though that use is not part of US labeling.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Paromomycin is isolated from a Streptomyces strain by Parke-Davis researchers."], ["2006", "An injectable paromomycin is approved in India for visceral leishmaniasis, a non-US use."]], "cell": "Paromomycin binds the A-site of the 16S ribosomal RNA in the small (30S) ribosomal subunit. This site normally checks that each transfer RNA matches the messenger RNA codon. When paromomycin is bound, the ribosome accepts wrong transfer RNAs, so the cell makes faulty proteins and protein synthesis eventually stalls. Misfolded proteins inserted into the membrane make it leaky, which helps kill the organism. Entamoeba histolytica trophozoites and cysts in the bowel lumen are susceptible, as are many gut bacteria that make ammonia. Because so little drug is absorbed, it does not reach amoebas that have invaded the liver or bowel wall, which need a tissue drug such as metronidazole.", "body": [["Bowel lumen", "Amoebic cysts and trophozoites living inside the gut are killed, preventing relapse and spread to others."], ["Gut bacteria", "Many intestinal bacteria are reduced, which lowers ammonia production and is the basis for its add-on use in hepatic encephalopathy."], ["Gut lining", "Diarrhea, cramps and nausea can occur; long courses can cause malabsorption by altering the gut lining and bacteria."], ["Ear and kidney", "If a lot is absorbed, such as with bowel ulceration or kidney failure, it can cause the hearing and kidney toxicity typical of aminoglycosides."]], "journey": "Paromomycin is taken by mouth as capsules. It is highly polar and poorly absorbed, so almost all of it stays in the gut and leaves unchanged in the feces. It is not metabolized. Inflamed or ulcerated bowel can let more through, and absorbed drug is cleared by the kidneys.", "sidefx": "Gut side effects come from direct irritation and from killing normal gut flora, which also allows overgrowth of resistant organisms. Like other aminoglycosides, any absorbed drug can concentrate in inner ear hair cells and proximal tubule cells, causing hearing loss and kidney injury. This is rare with oral use but more likely with damaged bowel or poor kidney function. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Humatin (paromomycin sulfate) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "triclabendazole": {"origin": "Triclabendazole is a benzimidazole developed by Ciba-Geigy, later part of Novartis, originally as a veterinary flukicide for sheep and cattle. It stood out because it killed both immature and adult liver flukes, unlike older drugs. Human use followed in countries where fascioliasis is common, and the World Health Organization added it to its essential medicines list. Novartis donates the drug to WHO for endemic regions. The FDA approved Egaten in 2019, making it the first FDA-approved treatment for fascioliasis in the United States.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Triclabendazole is introduced for veterinary fluke infection."], ["2019", "FDA approves Egaten (triclabendazole) for fascioliasis in patients 6 years and older."]], "cell": "Triclabendazole is taken up by the fluke mainly through its outer skin-like layer, the tegument. In the host, it is oxidized to sulfoxide and sulfone metabolites, which are the main active forms. They are thought to bind fluke beta-tubulin and block assembly of microtubules. Microtubules are needed for cell shape, transport of secretory materials to the tegument and cell division. Without them the tegument swells, blebs and breaks down, the fluke's movement and feeding fail, and it dies. The label notes that the exact mechanism is not fully established. It works on both young flukes migrating through the liver and adults in the bile ducts.", "body": [["Liver", "Young flukes moving through liver tissue are killed, stopping further tissue damage."], ["Bile ducts", "Dying adult flukes in the bile ducts can block bile flow, causing biliary colic, jaundice or abdominal pain after treatment."], ["Immune response", "Release of fluke antigens as worms die can cause sweating, fever, hives and malaise."], ["Heart", "The label notes possible QT prolongation."]], "journey": "Triclabendazole is taken by mouth with food, which greatly increases absorption. It is quickly oxidized, largely by CYP1A2 with other CYPs, into sulfoxide and sulfone metabolites that are highly protein bound and reach the liver and bile, where the flukes live. The parent drug has a half-life of a few hours and the sulfoxide a bit longer. The drug and metabolites leave mostly through bile in the feces. Treatment is completed in a single day.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from the death of the worms rather than from the drug itself. Dead and dying flukes passing through the bile ducts cause biliary colic and sometimes jaundice. Released parasite proteins trigger inflammation, sweating and hives. QT prolongation has been seen and is a label warning. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Humans usually catch Fasciola by eating watercress or other water plants that carry fluke cysts from water where infected sheep or cattle graze.", "src": ["Egaten (triclabendazole) FDA label", "World Health Organization fascioliasis fact sheet"]}, "gepotidacin": {"origin": "Gepotidacin was discovered and developed by GlaxoSmithKline, with funding support from the US Biomedical Advanced Research and Development Authority (BARDA) as part of efforts against drug-resistant infections. It is the first member of a new chemical class, the triazaacenaphthylenes. Fluoroquinolone resistance had made many urinary tract infections and gonorrhea harder to treat, so GSK looked for a molecule that hits the same bacterial enzymes in a different way. The FDA approved Blujepa in 2025 for uncomplicated urinary tract infection, the first new oral antibiotic class for this infection in decades, and later approved it for uncomplicated urogenital gonorrhea.", "timeline": [["2010s", "GSK develops gepotidacin with BARDA support."], ["2025", "FDA approves Blujepa (gepotidacin) for uncomplicated urinary tract infection in females 12 and older."], ["2025", "Approval expands to uncomplicated urogenital gonorrhea."]], "cell": "Bacteria need two type II topoisomerases to manage their DNA: DNA gyrase (GyrA and GyrB) relaxes the supercoils that build up ahead of the replication fork, and topoisomerase IV (ParC and ParE) separates the linked daughter chromosomes after copying. Gepotidacin binds a site on these enzyme-DNA complexes that differs from the fluoroquinolone site, so most fluoroquinolone resistance mutations do not affect it. It inhibits both enzymes in a fairly balanced way. Because a bacterium would need mutations in both targets at once to resist it, resistance is less likely to arise in one step. Blocked enzymes stall DNA replication and the bacteria die. Gepotidacin also weakly inhibits human acetylcholinesterase, which explains some side effects.", "body": [["Bladder and urinary tract", "High drug levels in urine kill E. coli, Staphylococcus saprophyticus and other common uropathogens, easing burning and urgency within days."], ["Genital tract", "It kills Neisseria gonorrhoeae, including many fluoroquinolone-resistant strains."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea and abdominal pain are the most common effects, from direct gut effects, cholinergic action and changes in gut flora."], ["Nervous system and muscles", "Acetylcholinesterase inhibition can cause cholinergic effects such as sweating, salivation or muscle symptoms, and can worsen myasthenia gravis."], ["Heart", "Gepotidacin can prolong the QT interval."]], "journey": "Gepotidacin is taken by mouth as tablets, ideally after food to reduce stomach upset. It is absorbed within a couple of hours and is moderately protein bound. It is broken down mainly by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise its levels and inducers lower them. A good share leaves unchanged in urine, giving high levels in the bladder, and the rest leaves in feces. Its half-life is about nine hours, supporting twice-daily dosing for a short course.", "sidefx": "Gut upset is the most common side effect. Cholinergic effects come from partial blockade of acetylcholinesterase, which lets acetylcholine build up at nerve endings. QT prolongation means it is used carefully with other QT-prolonging drugs. Like other antibiotics, it can disrupt gut flora and allow C. difficile infection. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "src": ["Blujepa (gepotidacin) FDA label", "Wagenlehner F et al. Lancet, 2024 (EAGLE-2 and EAGLE-3 trials)"]}, "sulopenem": {"origin": "Sulopenem is a thiopenem beta-lactam first discovered by Pfizer researchers decades ago, but it did not reach the market at that time. Iterum Therapeutics later took up the compound and paired an oral prodrug, sulopenem etzadroxil, with probenecid, a decades-old gout drug that slows kidney excretion of many antibiotics. The goal was an oral carbapenem-like antibiotic for urinary infections caused by resistant gram-negative bacteria, which often require IV therapy. The FDA approved Orlynvah in 2024 as the first oral penem in the US, for uncomplicated urinary tract infection in adult women with limited or no other oral options.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Sulopenem is studied by Pfizer as an injectable and oral penem."], ["2024", "FDA approves Orlynvah (sulopenem etzadroxil and probenecid) for uncomplicated UTI."]], "cell": "Sulopenem etzadroxil is an ester prodrug. Esterases in the gut wall strip off the ester to release active sulopenem. Sulopenem binds and acylates penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), the transpeptidase enzymes that cross-link peptidoglycan strands in the bacterial cell wall. Without cross-links, the wall weakens, autolysins keep breaking it down, and the bacterium bursts from internal pressure. The penem ring structure is stable to many extended-spectrum beta-lactamases (ESBLs) and AmpC enzymes, so it kills many resistant E. coli and Klebsiella strains. Probenecid blocks organic anion transporters in the kidney tubule, which reduces sulopenem secretion into urine and keeps blood levels higher for longer.", "body": [["Urinary tract", "Sulopenem kills E. coli, Klebsiella pneumoniae and Proteus mirabilis in the bladder, including many ESBL producers."], ["Kidneys", "Probenecid slows tubular secretion of sulopenem. It also blocks uric acid reabsorption, so more uric acid enters the urine, which can form uric acid stones."], ["Joints", "Probenecid shifts uric acid levels and can trigger a gout flare in people with gout."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and nausea are common, partly from disruption of gut flora; C. difficile infection can occur."], ["Genital tract", "Killing normal bacteria can allow vaginal yeast overgrowth."]], "journey": "The tablet is taken by mouth with food, which improves absorption of the prodrug. In the gut wall the ester is cleaved to sulopenem, which reaches the blood in about one to two hours. Sulopenem has a short half-life of about an hour and is cleared by the kidneys and partly in feces. Probenecid slows its tubular secretion to extend exposure. Probenecid also slows excretion of other drugs, which creates interactions.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea and C. difficile infection follow from killing normal gut bacteria. Vaginal yeast infections arise the same way. Probenecid increases uric acid excretion, which can cause stones, and it can precipitate gout flares. Like all beta-lactams, sulopenem can cause allergic reactions including anaphylaxis. Probenecid raises levels of other drugs cleared by renal anion transport, such as methotrexate. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Orlynvah (sulopenem etzadroxil and probenecid) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "mupirocin-nasal": {"origin": "Mupirocin is a natural antibiotic made by the soil bacterium Pseudomonas fluorescens, which produces a family of compounds called pseudomonic acids. Pseudomonic acid A was isolated and characterized in the 1970s by scientists at Beecham Pharmaceuticals in Britain. Because it is broken down quickly in the body, it was developed only for topical use. Bactroban ointment for skin infections reached the US in the late 1980s. A nasal ointment followed for clearing Staphylococcus aureus, including MRSA, from the nose, where carriers harbor the bacteria that often cause their own surgical site and bloodstream infections.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Beecham scientists characterize pseudomonic acid A from Pseudomonas fluorescens."], ["1987", "Mupirocin ointment (Bactroban) is approved in the US for skin infections."], ["1990s", "Bactroban Nasal is approved for eradicating nasal MRSA colonization."]], "cell": "Mupirocin works on a target no other marketed antibiotic class uses: bacterial isoleucyl-tRNA synthetase (IleS). This enzyme attaches the amino acid isoleucine to its transfer RNA so it can be used to build proteins. Mupirocin mimics the isoleucyl-adenylate intermediate and binds the enzyme's active site reversibly. Without charged isoleucine tRNA, protein synthesis stops, and the stalled cell also shuts down RNA synthesis. Mupirocin is bacteriostatic at lower levels and bactericidal at the high levels reached in the nose. Because the target is unique, there is no cross-resistance with other classes. High-level resistance comes from a plasmid gene (mupA) that encodes a different, resistant synthetase.", "body": [["Nose (anterior nares)", "Staph aureus and MRSA living in the front of the nose are cleared within days, lowering the risk that carriers infect their own surgical wounds or spread MRSA."], ["Nasal lining", "The ointment can cause burning, stinging or runny nose from local irritation."], ["Throat and taste", "Ointment spreads to the throat, which can cause sore throat and taste change."], ["Whole body", "Almost none is absorbed, so it has no systemic antibiotic effect."]], "journey": "The ointment is applied inside each nostril, and pressing the sides of the nose together spreads it over the lining. It acts locally on bacteria in the anterior nares. Very little crosses the nasal lining, and any that does is quickly converted to inactive monic acid and cleared in the urine. That rapid inactivation is why mupirocin cannot be used to treat infections inside the body.", "sidefx": "Side effects are local. Burning and runny nose come from the ointment base and drug on sensitive mucosa. Headache and sore throat are reported. Overuse breeds resistant staph that carry the mupA gene, which is why courses are kept short. Eye contact causes irritation. There is no antidote, and none is needed.", "src": ["Bactroban Nasal (mupirocin calcium ointment) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "silver-nitrate": {"origin": "Silver nitrate has been used in medicine for centuries. Alchemists linked silver to the moon, and solid silver nitrate became known as 'lunar caustic'. Surgeons used it to burn away warts and excess tissue and to seal small bleeding points. In 1881 the German obstetrician Carl Crede showed that dropping a dilute silver nitrate solution into newborns' eyes prevented gonococcal blindness, a practice that spread worldwide before being replaced by antibiotic ointments. Today silver nitrate sticks are still used for chemical cautery. They are an older, unapproved marketed drug in the US rather than a product approved through the modern FDA process.", "timeline": [["1881", "Carl Crede introduces silver nitrate eye drops to prevent newborn gonococcal eye infection."], ["20th century", "Silver nitrate applicator sticks become standard for small-area chemical cautery."]], "cell": "Silver nitrate does not act on a receptor. When the moist tissue touches the stick, silver ions are released and bind strongly to proteins, especially sulfur-containing groups and other charged sites. This binding denatures and precipitates the proteins, forming a firm layer called an eschar. In small blood vessels the coagulated protein plugs the vessel and stops bleeding. The same silver binding damages bacterial enzymes, membranes and DNA, giving an antiseptic effect. Nitrate adds oxidizing action. Silver that is reduced to metallic silver in the tissue appears as a gray-black stain.", "body": [["Small bleeding points", "Coagulated proteins seal capillaries, stopping oozing from small cuts, the nasal septum or other surfaces."], ["Granulation tissue", "Excess granulation tissue around wounds, stomas or umbilical stumps is destroyed so healthy skin can grow over it."], ["Warts and skin lesions", "Repeated applications destroy wart tissue."], ["Skin", "Contact leaves a black or gray stain that wears off as skin sheds."], ["Nasal septum", "Overly deep, repeated or two-sided cautery can damage cartilage blood supply and cause septal perforation."]], "journey": "Silver nitrate is applied directly to a small area of moist tissue. It acts within seconds where it touches. Silver ions bind tissue proteins locally and very little is absorbed into the blood. The coagulated layer and silver deposits are shed as the area heals. Large ingestions are a separate toxic situation.", "sidefx": "Its main effect is a controlled chemical burn, so its side effects are burns that go too far: pain, stinging, tissue necrosis and, in the nose, septal perforation. Staining happens because silver is reduced to dark metallic particles. Large amounts on broken skin or wounds can rarely cause methemoglobinemia because nitrate can be reduced to nitrite, which oxidizes hemoglobin; methylene blue treats methemoglobinemia. Chronic heavy silver exposure can cause argyria, a permanent blue-gray skin color.", "fun": "The black stain from silver nitrate is the same chemistry that once made photographic film work: light or reducing agents turn silver salts into dark metallic silver.", "src": ["Silver nitrate applicator product labeling", "Crede CSF. Archiv fur Gynakologie, 1881", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "zonisamide": {"origin": "Zonisamide is a sulfonamide-type molecule discovered at Dainippon Pharmaceutical in Japan in the 1970s. Chemists had been studying benzisoxazole compounds, and zonisamide stood out in animal seizure tests. It was approved in Japan in 1989 and gained wide use there, including in children. The FDA approved it as Zonegran capsules in 2000 as add-on therapy for partial seizures in adults. A liquid form, Zonisade, was later approved. In Japan it is also used for Parkinson's disease, a non-US use discovered by chance when a patient with both conditions improved.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Zonisamide is discovered at Dainippon Pharmaceutical in Japan."], ["1989", "Zonisamide is approved in Japan for epilepsy."], ["2000", "FDA approves Zonegran for partial-onset seizures in adults."]], "cell": "Zonisamide acts at several targets. It blocks voltage-gated sodium channels, which limits the sustained, rapid repetitive firing that lets a seizure build. It also blocks low-threshold T-type calcium channels, which help generate rhythmic burst firing in thalamic and cortical neurons. Together these reduce the spread of seizure activity from a focus. Zonisamide is a weak inhibitor of carbonic anhydrase, the enzyme that makes bicarbonate. That effect is not thought to be the main antiseizure action but explains many side effects. It may also have mild effects on dopamine and serotonin pathways.", "body": [["Brain", "Fewer partial seizures as excessive neuronal firing is dampened. Sleepiness, dizziness, slowed thinking and mood or psychiatric changes can occur."], ["Kidneys", "Carbonic anhydrase inhibition in the proximal tubule causes bicarbonate loss (mild metabolic acidosis) and lower urine citrate, which promotes calcium kidney stones."], ["Sweat glands", "Reduced sweating, especially in children, can lead to overheating and heat stroke in hot weather."], ["Appetite and weight", "Appetite loss and weight loss are common."], ["Eyes", "Rarely it causes acute angle-closure glaucoma with sudden eye pain and blurred vision."], ["Skin and blood", "As a sulfonamide, it can rarely cause severe rashes or blood disorders."]], "journey": "Zonisamide is taken by mouth and is well absorbed, peaking in two to six hours. It binds to red blood cells, where levels are higher than in plasma. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A4 reduction and N-acetylation, and the kidneys clear the drug and its metabolites. Its plasma half-life is very long, about 63 hours, so steady levels take about two weeks and once-daily dosing is possible. Enzyme-inducing seizure drugs shorten its half-life.", "sidefx": "Many side effects trace to carbonic anhydrase blockade: metabolic acidosis, kidney stones and decreased sweating. Its sulfonamide structure explains rare but serious reactions such as SJS, TEN, DRESS and blood dyscrasias. Nervous system effects come from dampened neuronal firing. Stopping suddenly can provoke seizures or status epilepticus. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Zonegran (zonisamide) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "rufinamide": {"origin": "Rufinamide is a triazole derivative first made at Ciba-Geigy, which later became Novartis. Its structure is unlike other seizure medicines. Eisai took over development and focused on Lennox-Gastaut syndrome, a severe childhood epilepsy with many seizure types, including drop attacks that cause sudden falls and injuries. In trials it reduced tonic-atonic drop seizures. Rufinamide was approved in Europe in 2007 and by the FDA as Banzel in 2008 as an orphan drug for Lennox-Gastaut syndrome.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Rufinamide is synthesized at Ciba-Geigy."], ["2008", "FDA approves Banzel (rufinamide) for Lennox-Gastaut syndrome seizures."]], "cell": "Rufinamide acts on voltage-gated sodium channels. After a sodium channel opens and then inactivates, it normally recovers quickly so it can fire again. Rufinamide prolongs the inactive state, slowing recovery. Neurons that fire at very high rates, as during a seizure, have many channels cycling through inactivation, so they are hit hardest, while normal firing is less affected. This limits sustained high-frequency bursts of action potentials and keeps seizure activity from spreading, which seems especially useful for the tonic and atonic seizures that cause drop attacks.", "body": [["Brain", "Fewer seizures, especially tonic and atonic drop seizures in Lennox-Gastaut syndrome; sleepiness, dizziness, headache and fatigue are common."], ["Heart", "Rufinamide shortens the QT interval in a dose-related way, so it is avoided in familial short QT syndrome."], ["Gut", "Vomiting, nausea and decreased appetite are common."], ["Balance", "Ataxia and gait problems can occur at higher levels."], ["Immune system", "A multiorgan hypersensitivity reaction (DRESS) can occur, usually in children in the first weeks."]], "journey": "Rufinamide is taken by mouth with food, which improves absorption. Peak levels come four to six hours later. It is not broken down by CYP enzymes; instead carboxylesterases hydrolyze it to an inactive acid that is cleared by the kidneys. Its half-life is six to ten hours, so it is taken twice daily. Valproate slows its clearance and raises levels, and enzyme inducers such as carbamazepine speed it up.", "sidefx": "Nervous system side effects reflect reduced neuronal firing. QT shortening comes from sodium and possibly other channel effects in heart cells. Hypersensitivity reactions are immune mediated and require stopping the drug. Sudden withdrawal can trigger status epilepticus, and like all antiseizure drugs it carries a warning for suicidal thoughts. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Banzel (rufinamide) FDA label", "Glauser T et al. Neurology, 2008"]}, "perampanel": {"origin": "Perampanel was discovered by Eisai in Japan through screening for compounds that block AMPA receptors, the glutamate receptors that carry most fast excitatory signaling in the brain. Researchers had long known that glutamate drives seizure spread, but earlier glutamate blockers were too toxic. Perampanel was the first selective noncompetitive AMPA antagonist to reach the market. The FDA approved Fycompa in 2012 for partial-onset seizures, and later for primary generalized tonic-clonic seizures. It is a Schedule III controlled substance because high doses caused euphoria in abuse studies.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Eisai discovers and develops perampanel."], ["2012", "FDA approves Fycompa for partial-onset seizures."], ["2015", "Approval expands to primary generalized tonic-clonic seizures."]], "cell": "Glutamate released at excitatory synapses binds AMPA receptors on the postsynaptic neuron. These receptors are ligand-gated ion channels that let sodium in and depolarize the cell within milliseconds. In a seizure, waves of glutamate release recruit more and more neurons through AMPA receptors. Perampanel binds an allosteric site on the AMPA receptor, separate from the glutamate site, and prevents the channel from opening even when glutamate is bound (noncompetitive antagonism). Because extra glutamate cannot overcome this block, it reliably reduces fast excitatory transmission and limits seizure spread. It does not significantly block NMDA or kainate receptors.", "body": [["Brain (seizures)", "Reduced fast excitatory signaling lowers partial-onset and generalized tonic-clonic seizures."], ["Brain (mood and behavior)", "Irritability, anger, aggression and hostility occur, sometimes severe, and are a boxed warning."], ["Balance and coordination", "Dizziness, vertigo, ataxia and falls are common because the cerebellum depends heavily on AMPA signaling."], ["Alertness", "Sleepiness and fatigue are common, which is why it is taken at bedtime."], ["Weight", "Weight gain can occur, possibly from changes in appetite and activity."]], "journey": "Perampanel is taken by mouth at bedtime and is almost completely absorbed, peaking in one to two hours. It is highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A4, followed by glucuronidation, and metabolites leave in feces and urine. Its half-life is very long, about 105 hours, so changes take two to three weeks to reach steady state. Enzyme inducers such as carbamazepine sharply lower its levels.", "sidefx": "Because AMPA receptors are everywhere in the brain, blocking them broadly slows coordination and alertness, causing dizziness, falls and sleepiness. Psychiatric effects such as aggression and, rarely, homicidal or suicidal thoughts are a boxed warning and may relate to altered excitatory balance in emotion circuits. Alcohol and other depressants add to impairment. There is no antidote, and the long half-life means effects persist for days after stopping.", "src": ["Fycompa (perampanel) FDA label", "Rogawski MA, Hanada T. Acta Neurol Scand, 2013"]}, "vigabatrin": {"origin": "Vigabatrin was designed, not discovered by chance. In the 1970s Michel Jung, Brian Metcalf and colleagues at the Merrell Dow research center in Strasbourg, France, set out to build an enzyme-activated irreversible inhibitor of GABA transaminase, the enzyme that breaks down the brain's main calming transmitter. They added a vinyl group to GABA, creating a molecule the enzyme would grab and then be trapped by. It was approved in the UK in 1989. US approval was delayed for years by concerns about brain changes in animals and later permanent visual field loss in people. The FDA approved Sabril in 2009 for infantile spasms and refractory complex partial seizures, with a restricted program requiring vision monitoring.", "timeline": [["1977", "Merrell Dow scientists in Strasbourg report vigabatrin as a GABA transaminase inactivator."], ["1989", "Vigabatrin is approved in the UK."], ["1997", "Permanent visual field defects are reported in patients."], ["2009", "FDA approves Sabril with a vision safety program."]], "cell": "GABA is broken down inside neurons and glia by mitochondrial GABA transaminase (GABA-T). Vigabatrin is a structural analog of GABA. GABA-T binds it and starts its normal reaction, which converts vigabatrin into a reactive form that bonds permanently to the enzyme's active site. This is called suicide inhibition. With GABA-T disabled, GABA builds up in nerve terminals and the synaptic cleft, strengthening inhibitory signaling through GABA-A and GABA-B receptors. The effect lasts until the cell makes new enzyme, which takes days, so effect long outlasts the drug's plasma half-life. In infantile spasms, often linked to tuberous sclerosis, this extra inhibition can stop spasms quickly.", "body": [["Brain", "Higher GABA levels reduce infantile spasms and refractory focal seizures. Sleepiness, fatigue and irritability are common."], ["Eyes (retina)", "GABA also builds up in the retina, and retinal cell damage causes permanent, often unnoticed loss of peripheral vision, a boxed warning."], ["Brain MRI in infants", "Reversible MRI signal changes in deep brain structures can appear in treated infants."], ["Weight and fluid", "Weight gain and swelling from fluid (edema) can occur during treatment."], ["Blood and nerves", "Mild anemia and peripheral neuropathy have been reported."]], "journey": "Vigabatrin is given by mouth as tablets or as a powder or liquid for infants. It is quickly and almost completely absorbed, peaking in about an hour, and it does not bind plasma proteins. It is not significantly metabolized and leaves mostly unchanged in the urine, so kidney function guides dosing. The plasma half-life is about seven hours, but because GABA-T is destroyed, the effect lasts days.", "sidefx": "Vision loss is the defining risk. GABA and vigabatrin accumulate in the retina, and retinal cells, especially in the outer field, are damaged; the loss does not recover after stopping, which is why vision is tested regularly. Sedation follows from increased GABA. Abrupt withdrawal can trigger seizures. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Sabril (vigabatrin) FDA label", "Lippert B, Metcalf BW, Jung MJ, Casara P. Eur J Biochem, 1977", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "tiagabine": {"origin": "Tiagabine came from a rational design program at Novo Nordisk in Denmark. Scientists knew that nipecotic acid blocks GABA reuptake in the lab but cannot cross the blood-brain barrier. They attached a fat-soluble anchor to nipecotic acid so the molecule could enter the brain while still blocking the GABA transporter. The result, tiagabine, became the first approved drug designed to block GABA reuptake. The FDA approved Gabitril in 1997 as add-on therapy for partial seizures.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Novo Nordisk designs lipophilic nipecotic acid derivatives as GABA uptake inhibitors."], ["1997", "FDA approves Gabitril (tiagabine) for partial seizures."], ["2005", "FDA warns about new-onset seizures in people without epilepsy who used tiagabine off label."]], "cell": "After GABA is released into the synapse, it is cleared mainly by GABA transporter 1 (GAT-1, gene SLC6A1) on presynaptic neurons and surrounding glia. Tiagabine selectively blocks GAT-1. GABA then stays in the synapse longer and spills over to nearby receptors, so both postsynaptic GABA-A (chloride channel) and GABA-B (G-protein coupled) receptors are activated more. The neuron becomes more hyperpolarized and less likely to fire, limiting seizure spread. Tiagabine does not itself bind GABA receptors; it only amplifies GABA the brain releases.", "body": [["Brain (seizures)", "Fewer partial seizures as natural GABA signaling is prolonged."], ["Brain (side effects)", "Dizziness, sleepiness, tremor, nervousness and trouble concentrating are common."], ["Brain (paradoxical seizures)", "In some patients, especially people without epilepsy, it can trigger new seizures or nonconvulsive status epilepticus."], ["Muscles", "Generalized weakness (asthenia) can occur, likely from increased GABA tone."], ["Gut", "Abdominal pain and nausea can occur, especially early in treatment."]], "journey": "Tiagabine is taken by mouth with food, which slows absorption and smooths peaks. Absorption is fast and nearly complete, peaking within about an hour on an empty stomach. It is highly protein bound. CYP3A4 in the liver breaks it down, and metabolites leave in feces and urine. Its half-life is seven to nine hours but drops to two to five hours with enzyme-inducing seizure drugs, so dosing differs depending on other medicines.", "sidefx": "Most side effects reflect extra GABA tone: dizziness, sleepiness and difficulty concentrating. Paradoxical seizures are thought to arise because excess GABA can activate GABA-B receptors and disrupt network rhythms, particularly in thalamocortical circuits. Rash and mood changes can occur. There is no antidote; overdose is managed supportively.", "src": ["Gabitril (tiagabine) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "felbamate": {"origin": "Felbamate is a dicarbamate closely related to meprobamate, the 1950s anti-anxiety drug. It was developed by Carter-Wallace (Wallace Laboratories) and approved by the FDA in 1993 as Felbatol, the first new seizure drug in the US in about 15 years. It worked well, even in Lennox-Gastaut syndrome. Within about a year, however, reports of aplastic anemia and acute liver failure emerged, some fatal. In 1994 the FDA and the company issued warnings and use dropped sharply. Felbamate remains available only for severe epilepsy when the benefit justifies these risks, with written informed consent.", "timeline": [["1993", "FDA approves Felbatol (felbamate) for partial seizures and Lennox-Gastaut syndrome."], ["1994", "Reports of aplastic anemia and liver failure lead to strong FDA warnings and restricted use."]], "cell": "Felbamate acts on several targets. It blocks NMDA glutamate receptors by acting at the strychnine-insensitive glycine coagonist site, with some selectivity for receptors containing the NR2B subunit, reducing calcium entry and excitatory signaling. It also enhances GABA-A receptor responses and inhibits voltage-gated sodium channels. Together these raise the seizure threshold and limit seizure spread. Unlike most antiseizure drugs, it tends to be stimulating rather than sedating.", "body": [["Brain", "Raised seizure threshold reduces partial seizures and Lennox-Gastaut seizures. Insomnia, headache and dizziness are common, and it is often alerting."], ["Bone marrow", "Aplastic anemia, a failure of all blood cell lines, occurs at a rate far above the general population and can be fatal."], ["Liver", "Acute liver failure can occur, so liver tests are monitored."], ["Appetite and weight", "Loss of appetite and weight loss are common."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are common, particularly at the start of treatment."]], "journey": "Felbamate is taken by mouth and is well absorbed. It is only modestly protein bound. About half is cleared unchanged by the kidneys, and the rest is metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4 and CYP2E1 through hydroxylation and conjugation. One minor pathway makes a reactive metabolite, atropaldehyde. Its half-life is about 20 hours. Felbamate raises levels of phenytoin and valproate and lowers carbamazepine levels while raising its epoxide metabolite.", "sidefx": "The serious toxicities are thought to come from atropaldehyde, a reactive aldehyde that can bind cell proteins and trigger immune attack on marrow and liver cells. Aplastic anemia and liver failure have a boxed warning. The stimulating side effects such as insomnia and appetite loss likely relate to NMDA and other actions. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped at the first sign of trouble.", "src": ["Felbatol (felbamate) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "stiripentol": {"origin": "Stiripentol is an aromatic allylic alcohol developed in France by the company Biocodex, unrelated in structure to other seizure drugs. It was studied for decades, and a French trial led by Catherine Chiron showed that adding it to clobazam and valproate greatly reduced seizures in Dravet syndrome, a severe genetic epilepsy usually caused by SCN1A mutations. Europe granted approval for Dravet syndrome in 2007. The FDA approved Diacomit in 2018 for Dravet syndrome seizures in patients taking clobazam.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Stiripentol is first described as an anticonvulsant."], ["2000", "A French trial led by C. Chiron shows benefit in Dravet syndrome."], ["2007", "Stiripentol is approved in Europe for Dravet syndrome."], ["2018", "FDA approves Diacomit with clobazam for Dravet syndrome."]], "cell": "Stiripentol acts in two ways. Directly, it is a positive allosteric modulator of GABA-A receptors at a site different from the benzodiazepine site, with greater activity at receptors containing the alpha-3 subunit, which are prominent in the developing brain. It increases how long the chloride channel stays open, making neurons harder to fire. Indirectly, it inhibits liver enzymes CYP3A4, CYP1A2 and CYP2C19. Blocking CYP2C19 slows breakdown of norclobazam, clobazam's active metabolite, so clobazam and norclobazam levels rise substantially, boosting their GABA-A effect. In Dravet syndrome, loss of sodium channel function in inhibitory interneurons weakens GABA signaling, so strengthening GABA-A responses helps rebalance the network.", "body": [["Brain (seizures)", "Fewer generalized tonic-clonic and prolonged seizures in Dravet syndrome."], ["Brain (side effects)", "Sleepiness, agitation, ataxia, tremor and low muscle tone are common, partly from higher clobazam levels."], ["Appetite and weight", "Decreased appetite and weight loss are common and are monitored in children."], ["Blood", "Neutropenia and low platelets can occur, so blood counts are checked."], ["Liver enzymes", "Its CYP inhibition raises levels of many other drugs."]], "journey": "Stiripentol is taken by mouth with a meal, as capsules or powder in water. It is absorbed within a few hours and is very highly protein bound. The liver metabolizes it extensively through CYP1A2, CYP2C19 and CYP3A4, and metabolites leave in urine. Its kinetics are nonlinear: as the dose rises, clearance falls, so levels rise more than expected. Its half-life ranges widely with dose.", "sidefx": "Sedation and ataxia come from combined GABA-A enhancement by stiripentol and the higher clobazam levels it causes; lowering the clobazam dose often helps. Appetite loss and weight loss are common. Neutropenia and thrombocytopenia are less understood. Drug interactions follow from CYP inhibition. Abrupt stopping can trigger seizures. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Diacomit (stiripentol) FDA label", "Chiron C et al. Lancet, 2000"]}, "fenfluramine": {"origin": "Fenfluramine is an amphetamine derivative developed in the 1960s as an appetite suppressant and approved in the US in 1973 as Pondimin. In the 1990s the combination 'fen-phen' with phentermine became a popular weight loss regimen. In 1997 researchers at the Mayo Clinic reported heart valve disease in fen-phen users, and fenfluramine was withdrawn from the US market that year. Meanwhile, Belgian doctors in Leuven had been giving low doses to a small number of children with Dravet syndrome since the 1980s and saw strong seizure control without obvious valve damage. Zogenix ran formal trials, and the FDA approved Fintepla oral solution in 2020 for Dravet syndrome and in 2022 for Lennox-Gastaut syndrome, with mandatory heart ultrasound monitoring.", "timeline": [["1973", "Fenfluramine is approved in the US as an appetite suppressant."], ["1980s", "Belgian clinicians begin low-dose fenfluramine for Dravet syndrome."], ["1997", "Valve disease linked to fen-phen leads to US withdrawal."], ["2020", "FDA approves Fintepla for Dravet syndrome seizures."], ["2022", "Approval extends to Lennox-Gastaut syndrome; fenfluramine is removed from Schedule IV."]], "cell": "Fenfluramine enters serotonin nerve terminals through the serotonin transporter (SERT), disrupts storage in synaptic vesicles and reverses SERT, so serotonin pours out into the synapse. Released serotonin, together with the active metabolite norfenfluramine, activates several serotonin receptors, including 5-HT1D, 5-HT2A and 5-HT2C, which are thought to boost inhibitory GABA interneuron activity and reduce seizures. Fenfluramine also positively modulates sigma-1 receptors, which may add antiseizure effect. Norfenfluramine is a potent agonist at 5-HT2B receptors on heart valve cells; chronic 5-HT2B activation drives fibroblast growth and thickening of the valves, the mechanism of valvulopathy.", "body": [["Brain (seizures)", "Fewer convulsive seizures in Dravet syndrome and drop seizures in Lennox-Gastaut syndrome."], ["Appetite and weight", "Serotonin action in the hypothalamus suppresses appetite, causing weight loss that must be watched in children."], ["Heart valves", "5-HT2B stimulation can thicken mitral and aortic valves, so echocardiograms are done before, during and after treatment."], ["Lungs", "Pulmonary arterial hypertension can develop from serotonin effects on pulmonary vessel smooth muscle."], ["Alertness", "Sleepiness and fatigue are common, partly from serotonin effects in the brain."], ["Eyes and blood pressure", "Pupil dilation can trigger angle-closure glaucoma, and blood pressure can rise."]], "journey": "Fenfluramine is taken by mouth as a liquid and is well absorbed, peaking in a few hours. The liver converts it, mainly through CYP1A2, CYP2B6 and CYP2D6, to active norfenfluramine. Both are cleared mostly in the urine after further metabolism. Its half-life is about 20 hours. Stiripentol and other enzyme inhibitors raise levels, while strong inducers such as rifampin lower them.", "sidefx": "Valvular heart disease and pulmonary arterial hypertension are boxed warnings and come from 5-HT2B activation by norfenfluramine, which makes valve and vessel cells proliferate. This is why the drug is available only through a restricted program with heart ultrasound monitoring. Appetite loss is from hypothalamic serotonin. Combining it with other serotonergic drugs can cause serotonin syndrome. Abrupt stopping can trigger seizures. There is no antidote.", "fun": "A drug pulled from the market for heart valve damage returned two decades later as an orphan epilepsy medicine, thanks in part to careful long-term observation by a few Belgian clinicians.", "src": ["Fintepla (fenfluramine) FDA label", "Connolly HM et al. N Engl J Med, 1997", "Lagae L et al. Lancet, 2019"]}, "ganaxolone": {"origin": "Ganaxolone is a synthetic version of allopregnanolone, a natural neurosteroid the brain makes from progesterone. Researchers in the 1980s and 1990s found that allopregnanolone is a powerful enhancer of GABA-A receptors, but natural neurosteroids are quickly broken down and can be converted back into hormonally active steroids. Adding a 3-beta-methyl group solved both problems. Ganaxolone was studied for many years in different epilepsies and was developed through approval by Marinus Pharmaceuticals. The FDA approved Ztalmy oral suspension in 2022 for seizures in CDKL5 deficiency disorder, a rare genetic epilepsy of early childhood, making it the first drug approved specifically for that condition.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Ganaxolone is developed as a stable synthetic analog of allopregnanolone."], ["2022", "FDA approves Ztalmy (ganaxolone) for seizures in CDKL5 deficiency disorder."]], "cell": "GABA-A receptors are chloride channels built from five subunits. Neurosteroids bind a site in the transmembrane part of the receptor that is separate from both the GABA site and the benzodiazepine site. Ganaxolone binds this neurosteroid site and increases chloride flow when GABA binds, hyperpolarizing the neuron. It acts on synaptic receptors, which carry fast phasic inhibition, and on extrasynaptic receptors containing delta subunits, which produce steady tonic inhibition. Benzodiazepines do not act on these delta-containing receptors, so ganaxolone adds a type of inhibition they cannot. The 3-beta-methyl group blocks conversion back to progesterone-like steroids, so it has no hormonal effects.", "body": [["Brain (seizures)", "Stronger phasic and tonic inhibition reduces the frequency of major motor seizures in CDKL5 deficiency disorder."], ["Brain (alertness)", "Sleepiness and sedation are the most common effects, reflecting widespread GABA-A enhancement."], ["Salivary glands", "Increased saliva production is reported."], ["Airways", "Upper respiratory infections and bronchitis were more common in trials."], ["Mood", "Like all antiseizure drugs it carries a warning for suicidal thoughts and behavior."]], "journey": "Ganaxolone is taken by mouth as a liquid with food, which greatly increases its absorption because it is very fat soluble. It peaks in a few hours and is highly protein bound. It is metabolized mainly by CYP3A4 in the liver, so strong inducers such as rifampin or carbamazepine lower its levels. Metabolites leave mostly in the feces, with some in urine. Its half-life is roughly a day and a half.", "sidefx": "Sedation and gait problems follow directly from enhanced GABA-A inhibition, and they add to the effects of other depressants such as alcohol, opioids and benzodiazepines. It is a Schedule V controlled substance because of its sedative and mood effects. Sudden stopping can provoke seizures or status epilepticus, so it is tapered. There is no specific antidote; flumazenil does not act at the neurosteroid site.", "fun": "The brain's own allopregnanolone rises in late pregnancy and falls after birth, and an IV form of allopregnanolone (brexanolone) was approved for postpartum depression.", "src": ["Ztalmy (ganaxolone) FDA label", "Knight EMP et al. Lancet Neurol, 2022 (Marigold trial)"]}, "diazepam-nasal": {"origin": "Diazepam was created by chemist Leo Sternbach at Hoffmann-La Roche as a follow-up to chlordiazepoxide, the first benzodiazepine. Roche launched it as Valium in 1963, and it became one of the most prescribed drugs in the world. For seizure emergencies at home, families long relied on a rectal diazepam gel. Neurelis developed a nasal spray that dissolves diazepam in vitamin E with an absorption enhancer, dodecyl maltoside (the Intravail technology), so the drug could cross the nasal lining. The FDA approved Valtoco in 2020 for seizure clusters in people with epilepsy, giving caregivers a less invasive option.", "timeline": [["1963", "Roche launches diazepam as Valium, developed by Leo Sternbach."], ["1997", "Rectal diazepam gel is approved for seizure clusters."], ["2020", "FDA approves Valtoco diazepam nasal spray."]], "cell": "GABA-A receptors are chloride channels made of five subunits. Diazepam binds the benzodiazepine site at the interface between an alpha subunit (alpha-1, 2, 3 or 5) and a gamma-2 subunit. It does not open the channel on its own. Instead it increases how often the channel opens when GABA is bound, so more chloride flows in and the neuron becomes hyperpolarized and harder to fire. In a seizure cluster, this extra inhibition stops runaway firing. Alpha-1 receptors mediate sedation and amnesia, alpha-2 and alpha-3 anxiety relief and muscle relaxation, and spinal receptors add muscle relaxation.", "body": [["Brain (seizures)", "Boosted GABA-A inhibition stops a seizure cluster from continuing."], ["Brain (alertness)", "Sleepiness and confusion are common after a dose."], ["Breathing", "Benzodiazepines blunt the brainstem's response to carbon dioxide, especially with opioids or alcohol, which can depress breathing."], ["Muscles", "Muscle relaxation and unsteadiness (ataxia) can occur."], ["Nose and taste", "Nasal discomfort, nosebleed and taste change can follow the spray."]], "journey": "The spray is given into one or both nostrils. Diazepam is very fat soluble, and the vitamin E and dodecyl maltoside help it pass through the nasal lining into the blood, avoiding liver first-pass metabolism. It enters the brain within minutes, though peak blood levels come later. It is highly protein bound. The liver converts it via CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 to the active metabolite desmethyldiazepam, which lasts even longer. Diazepam's half-life is about two days, and conjugated metabolites are cleared in urine.", "sidefx": "Sedation, ataxia and confusion follow from widespread GABA-A enhancement. Respiratory depression is the most serious risk, and the boxed warning stresses combining it with opioids. Dependence and withdrawal can occur with repeated use. Paradoxical agitation sometimes occurs. Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepine effects but can trigger seizures in people with epilepsy, so it is used with great care in this setting.", "src": ["Valtoco (diazepam nasal spray) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "midazolam-nasal": {"origin": "Midazolam was synthesized at Hoffmann-La Roche in the mid-1970s by Armin Walser and Rodney Fryer. Its imidazole ring made it water soluble in acidic solution yet fat soluble at body pH, so it could be injected without painful solvents and still enter the brain quickly. It was approved in the US in the 1980s as Versed for sedation and anesthesia. Researchers later showed that midazolam given into the nose or cheek could stop seizures outside the hospital. UCB brought a ready-to-use nasal spray to market, and the FDA approved Nayzilam in 2019 for seizure clusters, the first nasal benzodiazepine approved for this use.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Walser and Fryer synthesize midazolam at Hoffmann-La Roche."], ["1980s", "Injectable midazolam (Versed) is approved in the US."], ["2019", "FDA approves Nayzilam midazolam nasal spray for seizure clusters."]], "cell": "Midazolam binds the benzodiazepine site at the alpha and gamma-2 subunit interface of GABA-A receptors containing alpha-1, 2, 3 or 5 subunits. When GABA is bound, midazolam makes the chloride channel open more often. More chloride enters, the neuron hyperpolarizes and seizure firing stops. Its effect at alpha-1 receptors gives sedation and strong anterograde amnesia. Midazolam is potent and short acting, and its quick entry into the brain makes it well suited to stopping seizures rapidly.", "body": [["Brain (seizures)", "Seizure activity often stops within about ten minutes as GABA inhibition rises."], ["Brain (alertness and memory)", "Sleepiness and amnesia for the event are common."], ["Breathing", "Brainstem respiratory drive is reduced, especially with opioids or other sedatives."], ["Nose and throat", "Nasal discomfort, runny nose and throat irritation are common after the spray."], ["Heart and vessels", "Mild drops in blood pressure can occur from reduced sympathetic tone and vessel relaxation."]], "journey": "The spray is given into one nostril. Midazolam crosses the nasal lining quickly, reaching peak blood levels in about a quarter of an hour, and bypasses first-pass liver metabolism. It is highly protein bound and enters the brain rapidly. The liver converts it by CYP3A4 to 1-hydroxymidazolam, which is also active, and then to glucuronides cleared by the kidneys. Its half-life is a few hours, much shorter than diazepam. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels and prolong sedation.", "sidefx": "Respiratory depression is the main danger and is greatest with opioids, alcohol or other depressants, which is a boxed warning. Prolonged sedation can occur when CYP3A4 inhibitors slow its clearance. Dependence can develop with frequent use. Flumazenil can reverse midazolam but may provoke seizures in people with epilepsy and is used cautiously.", "src": ["Nayzilam (midazolam nasal spray) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "eletriptan": {"origin": "The triptan story began at Glaxo, where pharmacologist Patrick Humphrey and colleagues set out to make a serotonin-like drug that would constrict the swollen head blood vessels of migraine without broadly squeezing other vessels. Their work produced sumatriptan, approved in the US in the early 1990s. Many companies followed with 'second-generation' triptans that were better absorbed by mouth. Pfizer developed eletriptan, a fat-soluble triptan with high oral bioavailability and strong 5-HT1B/1D binding. The FDA approved Relpax in 2002 for acute migraine in adults.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Glaxo researchers led by P. Humphrey develop sumatriptan."], ["1992", "Sumatriptan becomes the first triptan approved in the US."], ["2002", "FDA approves Relpax (eletriptan) for acute migraine."]], "cell": "During migraine, the trigeminal nerve fibers around meningeal blood vessels release CGRP, substance P and other peptides that dilate vessels and cause neurogenic inflammation, and pain signals travel to the brainstem. Eletriptan is an agonist at 5-HT1B receptors on smooth muscle of the dilated intracranial vessels, constricting them. It also stimulates 5-HT1D receptors on trigeminal nerve terminals, which are Gi-coupled; this lowers cAMP and blocks the release of CGRP and substance P. Action at receptors in the brainstem trigeminal nucleus may reduce pain transmission centrally. Eletriptan also binds 5-HT1F receptors. Its high fat solubility helps it reach these sites.", "body": [["Head blood vessels", "Dilated meningeal vessels constrict, easing throbbing pain."], ["Trigeminal nerves", "Less CGRP and substance P release stops neurogenic inflammation and pain signaling."], ["Heart", "5-HT1B receptors also exist on coronary arteries, so triptans can cause coronary vasospasm; they are avoided in coronary disease."], ["Chest and throat", "Tightness or pressure in the chest, throat or jaw is common and usually not from heart ischemia."], ["Brain", "Weakness, sleepiness, dizziness and tingling can occur."], ["Blood pressure", "A transient rise in blood pressure can occur."]], "journey": "Eletriptan is taken by mouth and is well absorbed, peaking in about an hour and a half; it is more fat soluble than most triptans. It is moderately protein bound. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP3A4, forming a less abundant active N-desmethyl metabolite. Its half-life is about four hours. Because CYP3A4 is the main route, strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ketoconazole or clarithromycin raise levels several fold, and the combination is avoided.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow from 5-HT1B agonism outside the head. Coronary and peripheral vasoconstriction can cause chest symptoms, angina, heart attack or stroke in people with vascular disease. Tingling, warmth and pressure sensations are common. Combining with other serotonergic drugs can rarely cause serotonin syndrome. Overuse can cause medication overuse headache. There is no antidote; vasospasm is treated supportively, for example with nitrates.", "src": ["Relpax (eletriptan) FDA label", "Humphrey PP. Headache, 2008"]}, "naratriptan": {"origin": "After sumatriptan proved that a selective 5-HT1B/1D agonist could stop migraine, Glaxo chemists worked to improve on it. Sumatriptan had low oral bioavailability and a short half-life, so headaches often came back. Naratriptan was designed to be better absorbed and to last longer. The tradeoff was a slower, gentler onset. Glaxo Wellcome brought it to the US as Amerge, approved by the FDA in 1998. It is often described as a slow, well-tolerated triptan with lower headache recurrence.", "timeline": [["1992", "Sumatriptan, the first triptan, is approved in the US."], ["1998", "FDA approves Amerge (naratriptan) for acute migraine."]], "cell": "Naratriptan is a selective agonist at 5-HT1B and 5-HT1D receptors. 5-HT1B receptors on smooth muscle of meningeal and dural blood vessels cause vasoconstriction of the vessels that dilate during migraine. 5-HT1D receptors on trigeminal nerve terminals are Gi-coupled and reduce cAMP, which inhibits release of CGRP, substance P and neurokinin A. That stops neurogenic inflammation around vessels and reduces pain transmission to the trigeminal nucleus caudalis in the brainstem. Together these actions break the cycle of a migraine attack.", "body": [["Head blood vessels", "Dilated intracranial vessels constrict, reducing throbbing pain."], ["Trigeminal nerves", "Blocked neuropeptide release reduces inflammation and pain signaling."], ["Heart", "Coronary 5-HT1B receptors can cause vasospasm, so it is avoided in heart disease."], ["Brain", "Dizziness, drowsiness and malaise can occur, usually less than with faster triptans."], ["Chest, throat and neck", "Pressure or tightness sensations can occur."]], "journey": "Naratriptan is taken by mouth and is well absorbed, with higher oral bioavailability than sumatriptan. It peaks in two to three hours, which explains its slower onset. It has low protein binding. About half is excreted unchanged in the urine, and the rest is metabolized by several CYP enzymes; it is not broken down by MAO-A. Its half-life is about six hours, the second longest among triptans, which helps lower recurrence. Kidney disease raises levels.", "sidefx": "Like other triptans, naratriptan can constrict coronary and peripheral arteries, so it is avoided in coronary disease, stroke history, peripheral vascular disease and uncontrolled hypertension. Tingling and chest tightness are common and usually harmless. Serotonin syndrome is a rare risk with other serotonergic drugs. Frequent use can cause medication overuse headache. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Amerge (naratriptan) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "almotriptan": {"origin": "Almotriptan was discovered and developed by the Spanish company Almirall, one of the second-generation triptans that followed sumatriptan. It was designed to have good oral absorption and a tolerability profile close to placebo while still giving fast relief. The FDA approved it in 2001 for acute migraine in adults under the brand name Axert, and later for adolescents with long-lasting migraine attacks. The Axert brand has since been discontinued in the US, but generic almotriptan remains.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Almirall develops almotriptan in Spain."], ["2001", "FDA approves almotriptan (Axert) for acute migraine in adults."], ["2009", "Approval extends to adolescents 12 to 17 years with long migraine attacks."]], "cell": "Almotriptan is a selective agonist at 5-HT1B and 5-HT1D receptors. On smooth muscle of dilated meningeal vessels, 5-HT1B activation causes vasoconstriction. On trigeminal nerve endings, 5-HT1D activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase through Gi proteins and blocks release of CGRP and substance P, which stops neurogenic inflammation. It also dampens pain transmission in the trigeminal nucleus in the brainstem. These combined actions stop the migraine cascade once it starts.", "body": [["Head blood vessels", "Vasoconstriction of dilated dural vessels eases pain."], ["Trigeminal nerves", "Reduced neuropeptide release limits inflammation and pain transmission."], ["Heart", "Coronary vasospasm is possible, so it is avoided in coronary artery disease."], ["Gut", "Nausea and dry mouth can occur, usually mild and short lived."], ["Brain and nerves", "Sleepiness, dizziness and tingling are reported, generally at low rates."]], "journey": "Almotriptan is taken by mouth and is well absorbed, with about 70 percent bioavailability, peaking in one to three hours. Protein binding is low. It is cleared by several routes: MAO-A and CYP3A4 and CYP2D6 in the liver produce inactive metabolites, and a large share leaves unchanged in the urine. Its half-life is about three to four hours. Having multiple clearance routes means single inhibitors have modest effects, though strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels.", "sidefx": "Side effects reflect 5-HT1B and 5-HT1D activity outside the head. Constriction of coronary or other arteries can cause chest pain, ischemia or stroke in people at risk. Tingling, warmth and dry mouth are usually mild. Combining with serotonergic antidepressants or MAO inhibitors can contribute to serotonin syndrome. Frequent use can cause medication overuse headache. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Axert (almotriptan) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "frovatriptan": {"origin": "Frovatriptan was discovered at SmithKline Beecham and developed by the British company Vanguard Medica, which later became Vernalis. Its developers aimed for a triptan with a very long half-life to reduce headache recurrence, a common complaint with short-acting triptans. Frovatriptan reached that goal with a half-life of about a day, the longest of any triptan. The FDA approved it as Frova in 2001 for acute migraine in adults. Its slow onset and long action have made it a subject of research for menstrually related migraine.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Frovatriptan is discovered and developed by SmithKline Beecham and Vanguard Medica."], ["2001", "FDA approves Frova (frovatriptan) for acute migraine."]], "cell": "Frovatriptan is a high-affinity agonist at 5-HT1B and 5-HT1D receptors. 5-HT1B activation on cranial vascular smooth muscle constricts dilated meningeal and dural vessels. 5-HT1D activation on trigeminal nerve terminals reduces cAMP through Gi signaling and blocks release of CGRP and substance P, preventing neurogenic inflammation. It also reduces transmission of pain signals to the brainstem trigeminal nucleus. Laboratory studies suggest it is somewhat more selective for cerebral than coronary vessels, though it still carries the same cardiovascular warnings as other triptans.", "body": [["Head blood vessels", "Constriction of dilated intracranial vessels eases pain."], ["Trigeminal nerves", "Blocked CGRP and substance P release stops inflammation and pain signaling."], ["Heart", "Coronary vasospasm is possible, so it is avoided in heart disease."], ["Brain and nerves", "Dizziness, fatigue, tingling and headache can occur."], ["Chest and throat", "Tightness or pressure sensations are reported."]], "journey": "Frovatriptan is taken by mouth. Its bioavailability is modest, and it peaks in two to four hours, so its onset is slow. Protein binding is low. CYP1A2 in the liver is the main metabolic enzyme, and the drug and metabolites leave in urine and feces. Its half-life is about 26 hours, the longest of the triptans, which helps keep headache from returning.", "sidefx": "As with all triptans, 5-HT1B activation in coronary and peripheral arteries can cause vasospasm, so it is avoided in people with heart disease, stroke or uncontrolled hypertension. Tingling, dizziness and chest tightness are common and usually harmless. The long half-life means side effects can also last longer. Serotonin syndrome is a rare risk. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Frova (frovatriptan) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "ergotamine": {"origin": "Ergotamine comes from ergot, the fungus Claviceps purpurea that infects rye and other grains. In the Middle Ages, people who ate bread made from infected rye developed ergotism, called St. Anthony's fire, with burning pain and gangrene from severe vasoconstriction, and sometimes hallucinations and convulsions. Midwives used ergot to strengthen labor contractions. In 1918 Arthur Stoll at Sandoz in Basel isolated pure ergotamine, the first chemically pure ergot alkaloid. By the 1920s it was being used for migraine, and in 1938 Graham and Wolff showed that ergotamine relief of migraine pain matched narrowing of the temporal artery pulsations, supporting the vascular theory of migraine. Ergotamine with caffeine (Cafergot) became a standard migraine treatment before triptans.", "timeline": [["9th century", "Outbreaks of ergotism (St. Anthony's fire) from ergot-contaminated rye are recorded in Europe."], ["1918", "Arthur Stoll at Sandoz isolates ergotamine."], ["1920s", "Ergotamine begins to be used for migraine."], ["1938", "Graham and Wolff link ergotamine pain relief to cranial artery constriction."]], "cell": "Ergotamine binds many receptors because its structure resembles serotonin, norepinephrine and dopamine. Its migraine effect comes mainly from partial agonism at 5-HT1B receptors on cranial vessels, causing vasoconstriction, and at 5-HT1D receptors on trigeminal nerve terminals, blocking release of CGRP and substance P. It also acts at 5-HT2 receptors and as a partial agonist at alpha-1 and alpha-2 adrenergic receptors on vascular smooth muscle, producing strong and long-lasting constriction of arteries throughout the body, including the coronary arteries. Dopamine D2 stimulation in the chemoreceptor trigger zone causes nausea and vomiting. Caffeine adds cerebral vasoconstriction and improves ergotamine absorption.", "body": [["Head blood vessels", "Constriction of dilated cranial vessels and blocked trigeminal peptide release stop migraine and cluster headache."], ["Arteries throughout the body", "Strong alpha-adrenergic and 5-HT2 vasoconstriction can reduce blood flow to the fingers, toes, legs and gut, causing cold limbs, numbness, cramps and in severe cases gangrene."], ["Heart", "Coronary vasoconstriction can cause angina or heart attack."], ["Brain (vomiting center)", "Dopamine D2 and serotonin stimulation in the chemoreceptor trigger zone causes nausea and vomiting."], ["Uterus", "Ergot alkaloids make the uterus contract strongly, so ergotamine is contraindicated in pregnancy."], ["Heart valves and tissues", "Long-term use can cause fibrosis of heart valves, lungs or the retroperitoneum through 5-HT2B effects."]], "journey": "Ergotamine is taken by mouth with caffeine or under the tongue. Oral absorption is poor and first-pass metabolism by CYP3A4 in the liver is extensive, so only a small fraction reaches the blood. It is highly protein bound. Although its plasma half-life is about two hours, it binds tightly to receptors in vessel walls, so vasoconstriction can last about a day. Metabolites leave mainly in bile and feces.", "sidefx": "Nearly all serious effects are from excess vasoconstriction in places other than the head. The boxed warning concerns strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as clarithromycin, ritonavir or ketoconazole, which can raise ergotamine levels and cause severe peripheral, cerebral or mesenteric ischemia. Nausea is a central D2 effect. Daily use leads to rebound headache and dependence. Fibrosis follows long-term serotonin receptor stimulation. Severe vasospasm is treated with vasodilators such as nitroprusside or nitroglycerin and anticoagulation as needed; there is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Albert Hofmann was studying ergot alkaloids at Sandoz, the same lab that isolated ergotamine, when he synthesized LSD in 1938.", "src": ["Cafergot (ergotamine tartrate and caffeine) FDA label", "Graham JR, Wolff HG. Arch Neurol Psychiatry, 1938", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "ubrogepant": {"origin": "In 1982, Susan Amara, Ronald Evans, Michael Rosenfeld and colleagues discovered calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP), produced by alternative splicing of the calcitonin gene. In 1990 Peter Goadsby and Lars Edvinsson showed that CGRP rises in the jugular blood during migraine attacks. That set off a hunt for CGRP blockers. Early small-molecule 'gepants' worked, but telcagepant was halted because of liver toxicity. Merck discovered ubrogepant as a new gepant without that liver signal and licensed it to Allergan, which completed development. The FDA approved Ubrelvy in 2019 as the first oral CGRP receptor antagonist for acute migraine, offering an option for people who cannot take triptans because of vascular disease.", "timeline": [["1982", "CGRP is discovered from alternative splicing of the calcitonin gene."], ["1990", "Goadsby and Edvinsson show CGRP release during migraine attacks."], ["2011", "Development of the earlier gepant telcagepant stops over liver concerns."], ["2019", "FDA approves Ubrelvy (ubrogepant) for acute migraine."], ["2021", "The Brain Prize honors Edvinsson, Goadsby, Ashina and Olesen for CGRP and migraine research."]], "cell": "CGRP is a 37-amino-acid peptide released from trigeminal sensory nerves during a migraine. It binds the CGRP receptor, made of calcitonin receptor-like receptor (CLR) and receptor activity-modifying protein 1 (RAMP1). This Gs-coupled receptor raises cAMP, which relaxes vascular smooth muscle and sensitizes pain neurons in the trigeminal ganglion and brainstem. Ubrogepant is a small molecule that binds the CLR/RAMP1 receptor and competitively blocks CGRP from activating it. Pain signaling and vasodilation fall. Unlike triptans, ubrogepant does not actively constrict blood vessels; it only blocks CGRP-driven dilation.", "body": [["Trigeminal pain pathway", "Blocking CGRP receptors stops sensitization of pain neurons, ending migraine pain within about two hours in many patients."], ["Head blood vessels", "CGRP-driven vasodilation is blocked without active vasoconstriction."], ["Heart and vessels", "Because it does not constrict arteries, it can be an option for people who cannot take triptans, though rare hypertension and Raynaud phenomenon have been reported."], ["Gut and alertness", "Nausea, sleepiness and dry mouth are the most common side effects."]], "journey": "Ubrogepant is taken by mouth, peaking in about an hour and a half. It is mostly protein bound. CYP3A4 in the liver is the main route of metabolism, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors are contraindicated and strong inducers make it ineffective. It leaves mainly in bile and feces, with little in urine. Its half-life is about five to seven hours.", "sidefx": "Side effects are generally mild. Nausea and sleepiness may reflect CGRP's role in the brain and gut. Because CGRP also helps keep blood vessels open, blocking it may unmask high blood pressure or Raynaud phenomenon in some people. Hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis, have been reported. Drug interactions through CYP3A4 are the main practical concern. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Ubrelvy (ubrogepant) FDA label", "Amara SG et al. Nature, 1982", "Goadsby PJ, Edvinsson L, Ekman R. Ann Neurol, 1990"]}, "zavegepant": {"origin": "Zavegepant was discovered at Bristol-Myers Squibb and developed by Biohaven Pharmaceuticals, the company behind the oral gepant rimegepant. Biohaven focused on a gepant that could be given as a nasal spray, helpful for people whose migraine causes nausea and vomiting or whose pills absorb slowly during an attack. Pfizer acquired Biohaven's migraine business in 2022. The FDA approved Zavzpret in 2023 as the first nasal spray CGRP receptor antagonist for acute migraine in adults.", "timeline": [["2010s", "Biohaven develops zavegepant, a compound originating at Bristol-Myers Squibb."], ["2022", "Pfizer acquires Biohaven's CGRP migraine portfolio."], ["2023", "FDA approves Zavzpret (zavegepant) nasal spray for acute migraine."]], "cell": "During migraine, trigeminal nerves release CGRP, which binds the CGRP receptor formed by calcitonin receptor-like receptor (CLR) and RAMP1. The receptor is Gs-coupled and raises cAMP, which dilates meningeal vessels and sensitizes pain neurons in the trigeminal system. Zavegepant is a small-molecule competitive antagonist at CLR/RAMP1. By keeping CGRP off its receptor, it reduces pain transmission and vasodilation without causing active vasoconstriction. Its chemistry makes it suited to rapid absorption across the nasal lining rather than by mouth.", "body": [["Trigeminal pain pathway", "Blocking CGRP signaling relieves migraine pain, with some patients reporting relief within about 15 minutes in trials."], ["Head blood vessels", "CGRP-driven dilation is blocked without active vessel narrowing."], ["Taste and nose", "Taste disturbance is the most common side effect because part of the spray drains to the back of the throat; nasal discomfort also occurs."], ["Gut", "Nausea can occur, though the nasal route avoids problems with swallowing pills during vomiting."], ["Heart and vessels", "Rare hypertension or Raynaud phenomenon can occur from loss of CGRP's vessel-opening effect."]], "journey": "Zavegepant is sprayed into one nostril and absorbed across the nasal lining, peaking in about half an hour. Its oral bioavailability would be very low, which is why it is given nasally. It is mostly protein bound. Much of the drug is excreted unchanged through bile in the feces, with a minor role for CYP3A4 metabolism. Drugs that block the liver uptake transporters OATP1B3 or NTCP can raise levels and are avoided. Its half-life is about six and a half hours.", "sidefx": "Bad taste comes from drug drip onto the tongue. Nasal discomfort is local irritation. Like other gepants, blocking CGRP's vessel-relaxing role can raise blood pressure or trigger Raynaud phenomenon in some people. Hypersensitivity reactions can occur. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Zavzpret (zavegepant) FDA label", "Lipton RB et al. Lancet Neurol, 2023"]}, "fremanezumab": {"origin": "Once CGRP was proven to drive migraine pain, researchers asked whether an antibody could trap it for weeks at a time. Fremanezumab began at Rinat Neuroscience, a biotech unit acquired by Pfizer, and was licensed to Labrys Biologics, which Teva bought in 2014. Teva completed development. The FDA approved Ajovy in 2018 as one of the first monoclonal antibodies against the CGRP pathway for preventing migraine, with a monthly or every-three-month injection schedule. Approval was later extended to episodic migraine in children and teens.", "timeline": [["1982", "CGRP is discovered."], ["2014", "Teva acquires Labrys Biologics and the antibody that becomes fremanezumab."], ["2018", "FDA approves Ajovy (fremanezumab) for migraine prevention."]], "cell": "Fremanezumab is a humanized IgG2 antibody engineered to have little ability to activate immune effector functions. It binds both forms of the CGRP peptide, alpha-CGRP and beta-CGRP, in the circulation and tissues. Bound CGRP cannot attach to its receptor (CLR/RAMP1) on trigeminal neurons and vascular smooth muscle. With less CGRP signaling, the trigeminovascular system is less easily sensitized and fewer attacks are triggered. Because it binds the ligand rather than the receptor, it leaves the receptor available to other related peptides. Antibodies are too large to cross the blood-brain barrier well, so the effect is mostly on peripheral trigeminal structures that sit outside it, such as the trigeminal ganglion and meningeal vessels.", "body": [["Trigeminovascular system", "Trapping CGRP lowers the number of migraine and headache days each month."], ["Skin at injection site", "Pain, redness and hardening at the injection site are the most common side effects."], ["Immune system", "Hypersensitivity reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, can occur."], ["Blood vessels", "Reduced CGRP signaling could in theory affect vessel tone, and hypertension and Raynaud phenomenon have been reported after marketing with CGRP antibodies."]], "journey": "Fremanezumab is injected under the skin monthly or every three months. It is absorbed slowly through the lymphatics into the blood, peaking about five to seven days later. Like other IgG antibodies, it is protected from breakdown by the neonatal Fc receptor and is cleared by gradual protein breakdown into amino acids, not by the liver or kidneys. Its half-life is about a month. Benefit appears within the first month.", "sidefx": "Injection site reactions are local inflammation from the injection. Hypersensitivity reactions are immune responses to the protein. Because CGRP normally helps keep vessels open, there is a theoretical concern for vascular effects, and constipation and blood pressure changes have been seen with drugs in this class. Drug interactions are minimal since it does not use CYP enzymes. There is no antidote, and the long half-life means effects persist for months after stopping.", "src": ["Ajovy (fremanezumab-vfrm) FDA label", "Silberstein SD et al. N Engl J Med, 2017"]}, "eptinezumab": {"origin": "Eptinezumab was developed by Alder Biopharmaceuticals, a company in Washington State that produced antibodies in yeast. It was designed as an IV antibody against CGRP so that full levels would be reached immediately at the end of the infusion. Alder was acquired by the Danish company Lundbeck in 2019. The FDA approved Vyepti in 2020 as the first IV treatment for migraine prevention in adults, given every three months.", "timeline": [["1982", "CGRP is discovered."], ["2019", "Lundbeck acquires Alder Biopharmaceuticals."], ["2020", "FDA approves Vyepti (eptinezumab) for preventive treatment of migraine."]], "cell": "Eptinezumab is a humanized IgG1 antibody that binds the alpha and beta forms of CGRP with very high affinity. Bound CGRP cannot activate the CLR/RAMP1 receptor on trigeminal neurons and vascular smooth muscle, so the cAMP signal that drives vasodilation and pain sensitization is reduced. With less CGRP signaling, the trigeminovascular system is less likely to launch an attack. Being an antibody, it acts mainly on structures outside the blood-brain barrier, such as the trigeminal ganglion and meningeal vessels. IV dosing gives complete bioavailability and peak levels at the end of the infusion, which may explain benefit seen as early as the day after treatment.", "body": [["Trigeminovascular system", "Fewer monthly migraine days, with an effect seen as early as the first day after infusion in trials."], ["Nose and throat", "Nasopharyngitis was the most common side effect in trials."], ["Immune system", "Hypersensitivity reactions such as rash, itching and flushing can occur, mostly during the infusion."], ["Blood vessels", "As with other CGRP blockers, reduced vessel-relaxing signaling is a theoretical concern, and hypertension has been reported with drugs in this class."]], "journey": "Eptinezumab is given by IV infusion every three months, so the full dose enters the blood at once and peaks at the end of the infusion. It stays mostly in the blood and extracellular fluid. Like other IgG antibodies it is recycled by the neonatal Fc receptor and broken down gradually by protein catabolism into peptides and amino acids, not by CYP enzymes or the kidneys. Its half-life is about four weeks.", "sidefx": "Side effects are few. Nasopharyngitis may reflect minor immune changes. Hypersensitivity and infusion reactions are immune responses to the protein and are managed by slowing or stopping the infusion and treating symptoms. The vascular effects seen with other CGRP pathway drugs are a theoretical concern. There are no meaningful CYP drug interactions and no antidote.", "fun": "Unlike most therapeutic antibodies, which are made in hamster ovary cells, eptinezumab is produced in the yeast Pichia pastoris.", "src": ["Vyepti (eptinezumab-jjmr) FDA label", "Lipton RB et al. Neurology, 2020 (PROMISE-2)"]}, "safinamide": {"origin": "Safinamide is an alpha-aminoamide first made by researchers at the Italian company Farmitalia Carlo Erba while searching for new seizure drugs. Testing showed it was also a potent, selective and reversible inhibitor of MAO-B, the enzyme that breaks down dopamine in the brain. The Italian biotech Newron Pharmaceuticals, with partner Zambon, developed it for Parkinson's disease. It was approved in Europe in 2015, and the FDA approved Xadago in 2017 as an add-on to carbidopa-levodopa for off episodes. Unlike selegiline and rasagiline, which bind MAO-B permanently, safinamide binds reversibly and also has nondopaminergic actions on sodium channels and glutamate release.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Safinamide is discovered in Italy during a search for antiseizure compounds."], ["2015", "Safinamide is approved in Europe for Parkinson's disease."], ["2017", "FDA approves Xadago as add-on therapy for off episodes."]], "cell": "In the striatum, dopamine released from the remaining nigrostriatal terminals, and dopamine made from levodopa, is broken down largely by monoamine oxidase type B (MAO-B) in glial cells. Safinamide binds MAO-B selectively and reversibly, blocking oxidative deamination of dopamine to DOPAC. Dopamine therefore lasts longer at the synapse, which smooths and extends each levodopa dose. At normal doses it has little effect on MAO-A, which handles dietary tyramine in the gut, so dangerous tyramine reactions are unlikely though still a caution. Safinamide also blocks voltage-gated sodium channels in a use-dependent way and reduces stimulated glutamate release; whether these actions add to its clinical effect is not established.", "body": [["Brain (movement)", "Longer dopamine action increases on time and shortens off time between levodopa doses."], ["Brain (dyskinesia)", "More dopamine stimulation can worsen involuntary movements (dyskinesia), especially when levodopa doses are high."], ["Brain (mind and sleep)", "Hallucinations, insomnia, sleepiness and sudden sleep attacks can occur, as with other dopaminergic drugs."], ["Behavior", "Impulse control problems such as compulsive gambling or shopping can arise from dopamine effects on reward circuits."], ["Blood pressure", "Blood pressure can rise, and high-tyramine foods or sympathomimetic drugs can raise it further."], ["Eyes", "Retinal degeneration was seen in animal studies, so caution is noted in people with retinal disease."]], "journey": "Safinamide is taken by mouth once daily and is almost completely absorbed, peaking in two to three hours. It is mostly protein bound. It is broken down mainly by non-CYP pathways, including amidase hydrolysis and oxidation, and inactive metabolites leave mainly in the urine. Its half-life is about a day, and because it binds MAO-B reversibly, enzyme activity returns within days of stopping, faster than with irreversible MAO-B inhibitors.", "sidefx": "Dyskinesia, hallucinations, sleepiness and impulse control disorders are all consequences of increased dopamine signaling in different brain circuits. Hypertension follows from higher monoamine levels. Combining it with other serotonergic or MAO-inhibiting drugs, such as SSRIs at high doses, meperidine or dextromethorphan, raises the risk of serotonin syndrome. Very high tyramine intake could cause a hypertensive reaction. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Xadago (safinamide) FDA label", "Schapira AHV et al. JAMA Neurol, 2017"]}, "opicapone": {"origin": "Opicapone was discovered by the Portuguese company BIAL, which had earlier developed the antiseizure drug eslicarbazepine. Older COMT inhibitors had problems: entacapone wore off quickly and had to be taken with every levodopa dose, and tolcapone could injure the liver. BIAL chemists designed a nitrocatechol with a pyridine N-oxide group that binds COMT very tightly and lets go only slowly, so a single bedtime dose blocks the enzyme all day. It was approved in Europe in 2016, and the FDA approved Ongentys in 2020 as an add-on to carbidopa-levodopa for off episodes.", "timeline": [["2000s", "BIAL designs opicapone as a long-acting peripheral COMT inhibitor."], ["2016", "Opicapone is approved in Europe."], ["2020", "FDA approves Ongentys for Parkinson's disease off episodes."]], "cell": "When levodopa is taken with carbidopa, dopa decarboxylase in the gut and blood is blocked, so the main remaining route for breaking down levodopa outside the brain is catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT), which methylates it to 3-O-methyldopa. Opicapone binds peripheral COMT with very high affinity and dissociates slowly. With COMT blocked, more levodopa remains in the blood for longer, and a larger share crosses the blood-brain barrier through the large neutral amino acid transporter, where it is converted to dopamine. Less 3-O-methyldopa also means less competition for that transporter. Opicapone does not meaningfully enter the brain, so its effect is peripheral. Its long hold on the enzyme gives COMT inhibition for more than 24 hours despite a short plasma half-life.", "body": [["Brain (movement)", "Steadier levodopa levels increase on time and reduce wearing off between doses."], ["Brain (dyskinesia)", "Higher levodopa exposure can increase involuntary movements, often managed by lowering the levodopa dose."], ["Brain (mind and sleep)", "Hallucinations, sleepiness and impulse control problems can arise from increased dopamine signaling."], ["Blood pressure", "Orthostatic hypotension and fainting can occur because more dopamine is made outside and inside the brain."], ["Gut", "Constipation and dry mouth are reported; diarrhea is less common than with entacapone or tolcapone."], ["Muscle", "Raised creatine kinase levels have been seen."]], "journey": "Opicapone is taken by mouth at bedtime, at least an hour before or after food, because food lowers its absorption. It peaks in one to two and a half hours and is very highly protein bound. It is metabolized mainly by sulfation, with glucuronidation and reduction as other routes and little CYP involvement. It leaves mostly through bile in the feces. Its plasma half-life is only one to two hours, but tight binding to COMT keeps the enzyme inhibited for more than a day.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are the effects of more levodopa reaching the brain: dyskinesia, hallucinations, sleepiness and low blood pressure. Impulse control disorders reflect dopamine effects on reward circuits. Stopping it abruptly can drop levodopa effect quickly and, rarely, trigger a syndrome like neuroleptic malignant syndrome with fever and confusion. Because it acts on catechol metabolism, it is not used with nonselective MAO inhibitors. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Ongentys (opicapone) FDA label", "Ferreira JJ et al. Lancet Neurol, 2016 (BIPARK-I)"]}, "tolcapone": {"origin": "Tolcapone was developed by Hoffmann-La Roche as one of the first nitrocatechol COMT inhibitors designed to extend levodopa. The FDA approved Tasmar in 1998. Within months, reports of fatal acute liver failure appeared. Tolcapone was withdrawn in Europe and Canada later that year, while in the US it stayed available with a boxed warning, strict liver test monitoring and a requirement for informed consent. Europe later reintroduced it with tight restrictions. Today it is used mainly when other add-on drugs have not worked, because it is the most potent COMT inhibitor and the only one that also acts in the brain.", "timeline": [["1998", "FDA approves Tasmar (tolcapone) for Parkinson's disease."], ["1998", "Fatal liver failure reports lead to withdrawal in Europe and Canada and a US boxed warning."], ["2004", "Tolcapone is reintroduced in Europe with strict liver monitoring."]], "cell": "Tolcapone selectively and reversibly inhibits catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT). In the gut, liver and blood, blocking COMT stops methylation of levodopa to 3-O-methyldopa, so plasma levodopa lasts longer and more enters the brain. Unlike entacapone and opicapone, tolcapone crosses the blood-brain barrier and also blocks COMT in the brain, slowing breakdown of dopamine there. Together these effects give more sustained dopamine stimulation of striatal receptors. As a nitrocatechol, tolcapone can uncouple oxidative phosphorylation in mitochondria, which is linked to its liver toxicity.", "body": [["Brain (movement)", "Longer levodopa action increases on time and reduces wearing off."], ["Brain (dyskinesia and mind)", "Dyskinesia, hallucinations and sleep problems increase with more dopamine stimulation."], ["Liver", "Hepatocellular injury, rarely fulminant liver failure, is the defining risk; liver tests are monitored regularly."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, sometimes severe, is common, along with nausea and loss of appetite."], ["Urine", "Tolcapone and its metabolites turn urine bright yellow, which is harmless."], ["Blood pressure", "Orthostatic hypotension can occur as dopamine stimulation lowers sympathetic tone."]], "journey": "Tolcapone is taken by mouth three times daily and is absorbed quickly, peaking in about two hours. It is very highly bound to albumin. It is metabolized mainly by glucuronidation, with COMT methylation and CYP3A4 and CYP2A6 oxidation as minor routes. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. Its half-life is two to three hours, which is why it is taken several times a day.", "sidefx": "Liver injury is thought to come from tolcapone's ability to uncouple mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation in hepatocytes, depleting cell energy, along with reactive metabolites; this is why it carries a boxed warning and is stopped if liver enzymes rise or there is no benefit within weeks. Diarrhea is a class effect of COMT inhibitors, worse with tolcapone. Dyskinesia and hallucinations come from more dopamine. Abrupt stopping can cause a fever and confusion syndrome. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Tasmar (tolcapone) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "istradefylline": {"origin": "Istradefylline is a xanthine, chemically related to caffeine, developed by the Japanese company Kyowa Hakko Kogyo, now Kyowa Kirin. Researchers knew that adenosine A2A receptors sit side by side with dopamine D2 receptors on striatal neurons and push movement circuits in the opposite direction from dopamine. Blocking A2A offered a way to treat Parkinson's disease without acting on dopamine directly. The FDA declined to approve it in 2008 because trial results were mixed. It was approved in Japan in 2013, and after further data the FDA approved Nourianz in 2019 as an add-on to carbidopa-levodopa for off episodes.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Kyowa Hakko researchers develop istradefylline as a selective A2A antagonist."], ["2008", "FDA issues a not-approvable letter."], ["2013", "Istradefylline is approved in Japan."], ["2019", "FDA approves Nourianz for off episodes in Parkinson's disease."]], "cell": "Movement is controlled by two striatal output pathways. The indirect pathway uses GABAergic medium spiny neurons that carry both dopamine D2 and adenosine A2A receptors. Dopamine acting on D2 (Gi) normally quiets this pathway, while adenosine acting on A2A (Gs) raises cAMP and excites it. In Parkinson's disease, dopamine loss leaves A2A signaling unopposed, so the indirect pathway becomes overactive and movement is suppressed. Istradefylline selectively blocks A2A receptors, lowering cAMP signaling in these neurons and reducing indirect pathway overactivity. Movement improves without directly stimulating dopamine receptors, and it enhances the effect of the dopamine that levodopa provides.", "body": [["Brain (movement)", "Reduced indirect pathway overactivity shortens off time when added to levodopa."], ["Brain (dyskinesia)", "Dyskinesia increases, because it amplifies levodopa's effect."], ["Brain (mind and sleep)", "Hallucinations and insomnia can occur, the latter resembling caffeine's alerting effect through adenosine blockade."], ["Gut", "Constipation and nausea are reported, generally mild."], ["Behavior", "Impulse control disorders have been reported."]], "journey": "Istradefylline is taken by mouth once daily, peaking in about four hours. It is highly protein bound. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP1A1 and CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels and strong inducers lower them. Smoking induces CYP1A1 and lowers its levels, so smokers may need a different dose. It leaves mostly in feces. Its half-life is very long, about 83 hours.", "sidefx": "Dyskinesia and hallucinations come from amplified dopaminergic signaling in striatal and cortical circuits. Insomnia reflects adenosine receptor blockade, similar to how caffeine keeps people awake. Impulse control problems are linked to altered reward signaling. Interactions occur through CYP3A4 and smoking. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Caffeine is also an adenosine receptor antagonist, and large population studies have linked coffee drinking with a lower risk of developing Parkinson's disease.", "src": ["Nourianz (istradefylline) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "rotigotine": {"origin": "Rotigotine is a non-ergot dopamine agonist that was developed for skin delivery because it is broken down almost completely by the liver when taken by mouth. The German company Schwarz Pharma designed a silicone-based patch that releases the drug steadily for 24 hours. The FDA approved Neupro in 2007 for early Parkinson's disease, and UCB acquired Schwarz Pharma. In 2008 the patch was recalled in the US because rotigotine formed crystals in the patch, which reduced drug delivery. After reformulation it returned in 2012, when it was also approved for advanced Parkinson's disease and restless legs syndrome.", "timeline": [["2006", "Rotigotine patch is approved in Europe."], ["2007", "FDA approves Neupro for early Parkinson's disease."], ["2008", "Neupro is recalled in the US because of crystal formation in the patch."], ["2012", "Reformulated Neupro returns with approvals for advanced Parkinson's disease and restless legs syndrome."]], "cell": "Rotigotine is a non-ergoline agonist at dopamine receptors, with the highest affinity for D3, then D2 and D1, plus 5-HT1A agonism and alpha-2B antagonism. In Parkinson's disease, striatal neurons lack dopamine input. Rotigotine stimulates postsynaptic D2-like receptors on indirect pathway neurons, which quiets that pathway, and D1 receptors on direct pathway neurons, which activates it. Both changes release the brake on movement. Because the patch keeps blood levels steady, receptors are stimulated continuously rather than in peaks and troughs, which may reduce motor fluctuations. In restless legs syndrome, dopamine agonism in spinal and subcortical circuits relieves the urge to move.", "body": [["Brain (movement)", "Continuous dopamine receptor stimulation improves slowness, stiffness and tremor in Parkinson's disease."], ["Legs and sleep", "Uncomfortable leg sensations of restless legs syndrome ease, though long-term use can cause augmentation, with symptoms starting earlier in the day."], ["Brain (alertness)", "Sleepiness and sudden sleep attacks can occur, even while driving."], ["Brain (mind and behavior)", "Hallucinations and impulse control disorders such as compulsive gambling arise from D3 and D2 stimulation in limbic circuits."], ["Blood pressure", "Orthostatic hypotension occurs because dopamine agonism reduces sympathetic tone."], ["Gut and skin", "Nausea and vomiting come from the chemoreceptor trigger zone; application site reactions are common."]], "journey": "Rotigotine is absorbed through the skin from a patch changed once daily, avoiding the heavy first-pass liver metabolism that makes oral use impractical. Blood levels rise over many hours and reach a steady state within a day or two, staying fairly flat across the 24-hour wear. The drug is extensively broken down by conjugation (sulfation and glucuronidation) and N-dealkylation, and inactive conjugates leave in urine. After the patch is removed, levels fall with a half-life of about five to seven hours.", "sidefx": "Nearly every side effect reflects dopamine receptor stimulation in places other than the motor circuits: nausea in the vomiting center, sleepiness and sleep attacks, hallucinations and impulse control disorders in limbic areas, and low blood pressure from reduced sympathetic outflow. Skin reactions come from the adhesive and the drug. The patch contains sodium metabisulfite, which can cause allergic reactions in sulfite-sensitive people. Stopping suddenly can cause a fever and confusion syndrome, so it is tapered. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Neupro patches contain a thin aluminum layer, so they are removed before MRI scans to avoid skin burns.", "src": ["Neupro (rotigotine transdermal system) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "foscarbidopa-foslevodopa": {"origin": "Levodopa therapy rests on discoveries in the late 1950s and 1960s. Arvid Carlsson in Sweden showed that dopamine is a brain transmitter and that levodopa reversed movement loss in animals, work honored with the 2000 Nobel Prize. In 1960 Oleh Hornykiewicz found that dopamine is depleted in the brains of people with Parkinson's disease, and in 1967 George Cotzias showed that high oral doses of levodopa worked in patients. Carbidopa, a decarboxylase inhibitor, was paired with levodopa in Sinemet in 1975. Over years of treatment, pills give rising and falling levels that cause motor fluctuations. To deliver levodopa continuously under the skin, AbbVie made soluble phosphate prodrugs of both drugs. The FDA approved Vyalev in 2024 as a continuous subcutaneous infusion for motor fluctuations in advanced Parkinson's disease.", "timeline": [["1957", "Arvid Carlsson shows levodopa reverses reserpine-induced movement loss in animals."], ["1960", "Hornykiewicz reports dopamine depletion in Parkinson's disease brains."], ["1967", "Cotzias reports success of high-dose oral levodopa."], ["1975", "Carbidopa-levodopa (Sinemet) is approved in the US."], ["2024", "FDA approves Vyalev (foscarbidopa and foslevodopa) subcutaneous infusion."]], "cell": "Foslevodopa and foscarbidopa are phosphate prodrugs that are highly water soluble, so a concentrated solution can be pumped under the skin. In tissue and blood, alkaline phosphatases remove the phosphate groups to release levodopa and carbidopa. Carbidopa blocks aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (dopa decarboxylase) outside the brain and does not cross the blood-brain barrier itself. That prevents levodopa from being turned into dopamine in the blood, which would cause nausea and low blood pressure. Levodopa crosses into the brain on the large neutral amino acid transporter, where surviving dopaminergic terminals and other cells decarboxylate it to dopamine. Dopamine then stimulates D1 and D2 receptors in the striatum. Continuous delivery keeps levodopa levels steady, so striatal receptors are stimulated more evenly, reducing off time and peak-dose dyskinesia.", "body": [["Brain (movement)", "Steady dopamine production reduces off periods and smooths motor control through the day."], ["Brain (dyskinesia and mind)", "Dyskinesia, hallucinations and sleepiness can occur from dopamine stimulation."], ["Skin at infusion site", "Redness, nodules, swelling and infections such as cellulitis or abscess are common because the cannula stays under the skin."], ["Blood pressure", "Orthostatic hypotension and falls can occur."], ["Gut", "Carbidopa reduces, but does not remove, nausea from peripheral dopamine."]], "journey": "The solution is infused continuously under the skin through a small cannula from a portable pump. The prodrugs are rapidly dephosphorylated, and levodopa levels rise within about an hour and then stay steady during the infusion. Levodopa has a short half-life of about an hour and a half, which is why continuous delivery is useful. It is metabolized by decarboxylation and by COMT and MAO, and metabolites leave in urine. Carbidopa is cleared by the kidneys.", "sidefx": "Infusion site reactions and infections come from the cannula and the solution under the skin. Dyskinesia, hallucinations and sleepiness come from dopamine stimulation of motor and limbic circuits. Low blood pressure reflects dopamine effects on vessels and sympathetic tone. If the pump stops suddenly, the drop in dopamine can trigger a hyperpyrexia and confusion syndrome resembling neuroleptic malignant syndrome. Long-term levodopa can also lower vitamin B6 and raise homocysteine. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Vyalev (foscarbidopa and foslevodopa) FDA label", "Cotzias GC et al. N Engl J Med, 1967", "Carlsson A. Nobel Lecture, 2000"]}, "tofersen": {"origin": "In 1993 a team led by Robert Brown and colleagues (Rosen et al.) discovered that mutations in the SOD1 gene cause some inherited cases of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS). Later work showed that mutant SOD1 protein misfolds and becomes toxic to motor neurons rather than simply losing its function. That suggested lowering the protein might help. Ionis Pharmaceuticals, a leader in antisense drugs, designed tofersen, and Biogen co-developed it. The main trial missed its primary clinical endpoint, but it showed clear drops in SOD1 and in neurofilament light chain, a blood marker of nerve damage. Based on that biomarker, the FDA granted accelerated approval to Qalsody in 2023, the first therapy for a genetic form of ALS.", "timeline": [["1993", "SOD1 mutations are linked to familial ALS."], ["2022", "The VALOR trial reports tofersen results."], ["2023", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Qalsody (tofersen) for SOD1-ALS."]], "cell": "Tofersen is an antisense oligonucleotide: a short synthetic strand of nucleic acid whose sequence is complementary to SOD1 messenger RNA. It has a chemically modified backbone (phosphorothioate links and 2'-O-methoxyethyl sugars at the ends) that resists breakdown, with a central DNA-like stretch. When it pairs with SOD1 mRNA, the resulting RNA-DNA hybrid is recognized by the enzyme RNase H1, which cuts the mRNA. The cut message is degraded, so less SOD1 protein is made. In SOD1-ALS, this reduces the misfolded mutant protein that damages motor neurons through aggregation, mitochondrial stress and other toxic effects. Falling neurofilament light chain indicates less ongoing axon injury.", "body": [["Spinal cord and brain", "SOD1 protein in the spinal fluid falls, and plasma neurofilament light chain drops, suggesting less motor neuron injury; clinical benefit is still being confirmed."], ["Nervous system (inflammation)", "Myelitis, radiculitis, aseptic meningitis and papilledema can occur, reflecting inflammation from the drug in the spinal fluid."], ["Spinal fluid", "White cell counts and protein in the spinal fluid often rise."], ["Muscles and joints", "Pain, fatigue, muscle aches and joint pain are common."], ["Head", "Headache after lumbar puncture is common because the drug is given by spinal injection."]], "journey": "Tofersen cannot cross the blood-brain barrier, so it is injected directly into the spinal fluid by lumbar puncture. It spreads through the spinal fluid and into spinal cord and brain tissue, where nerve cells take it up. A small amount reaches the blood within hours. It is broken down slowly by nucleases into shorter fragments, which leave in the urine. Its half-life in the spinal fluid is about four weeks, which supports monthly maintenance dosing.", "sidefx": "Many side effects come from the procedure: post-lumbar puncture headache and back pain. Inflammatory reactions in the nervous system, including myelitis, radiculitis, aseptic meningitis and raised intracranial pressure, are thought to be immune responses to the oligonucleotide in the spinal fluid. Spinal fluid white cells and protein rise for the same reason. There is no antidote; serious inflammation is treated supportively and sometimes with steroids.", "src": ["Qalsody (tofersen) FDA label", "Rosen DR et al. Nature, 1993", "Miller TM et al. N Engl J Med, 2022 (VALOR)"]}, "ponesimod": {"origin": "The sphingosine 1-phosphate (S1P) receptor drugs trace back to myriocin, a natural compound from the fungus Isaria sinclairii, long used in Chinese medicine. Japanese researchers led by Tetsuro Fujita modified it into fingolimod, the first S1P modulator, approved for MS in 2010. Fingolimod hit several S1P receptor subtypes and caused heart rate effects that lasted a while. The Swiss company Actelion designed ponesimod to be selective for S1P1 and to clear quickly from the body. Johnson & Johnson acquired Actelion in 2017 and completed development. The FDA approved Ponvory in 2021 for relapsing forms of MS after it reduced relapses more than teriflunomide in a head-to-head trial.", "timeline": [["2010", "Fingolimod becomes the first S1P receptor modulator approved for MS."], ["2017", "Johnson & Johnson acquires Actelion, the developer of ponesimod."], ["2021", "FDA approves Ponvory (ponesimod) for relapsing MS."]], "cell": "Lymphocytes leave lymph nodes by following a gradient of sphingosine 1-phosphate (S1P), which is higher in blood and lymph. They sense this signal through the S1P1 receptor. Ponesimod binds S1P1 with high affinity and first activates it, but then causes the receptor to be pulled inside the cell and degraded. Without surface S1P1, lymphocytes, especially naive and central memory T cells and B cells, cannot sense the exit signal and stay trapped in lymph nodes. Fewer autoreactive lymphocytes reach the brain and spinal cord, so fewer MS lesions form. This is called functional antagonism. S1P1 is also on atrial heart cells, where its initial activation opens GIRK potassium channels and slows the heart rate, which is why the dose is slowly raised at the start.", "body": [["Immune system", "Circulating lymphocyte counts drop as cells stay in lymph nodes, lowering MS relapses and new lesions."], ["Heart", "First-dose activation of atrial S1P1 receptors slows heart rate and can cause AV block, so dosing starts low and is increased gradually."], ["Eyes", "Macular edema can occur, likely from S1P effects on retinal blood vessel barriers."], ["Lungs", "Mild declines in lung function tests and shortness of breath can occur."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise, so liver tests are checked before starting."], ["Infection risk", "Lower circulating lymphocytes raise the risk of infections, including herpes viruses and rare PML or cryptococcal infection."]], "journey": "Ponesimod is taken by mouth once daily and is well absorbed, peaking in two to four hours. It is very highly protein bound. It is broken down by several CYP and non-CYP oxidation pathways and by glucuronidation, and leaves mostly in feces. Its half-life is about 33 hours. Because it is cleared relatively quickly, lymphocyte counts return to normal within about a week after stopping, faster than with fingolimod.", "sidefx": "Slow heart rate and AV block at the start come from activation of S1P1 on atrial cells before receptor internalization takes hold. Infections follow from trapping lymphocytes. Macular edema and lung function changes reflect S1P1 roles in blood vessel barriers. Blood pressure can rise. After stopping, MS activity can rebound as lymphocytes flood back. Lymphocytes recover on their own after stopping; there is no antidote.", "src": ["Ponvory (ponesimod) FDA label", "Kappos L et al. JAMA Neurol, 2021 (OPTIMUM trial)"]}, "cladribine-oral": {"origin": "Cladribine was developed as a medicine in the late 1970s and 1980s by Dennis Carson and colleagues at the Scripps Clinic. They were inspired by adenosine deaminase deficiency, a genetic disease in which a buildup of deoxyadenosine destroys lymphocytes. They reasoned that a deoxyadenosine analog that resists adenosine deaminase could selectively kill lymphocytes. The result, 2-chlorodeoxyadenosine, proved remarkably effective against hairy cell leukemia and was approved as IV Leustatin in 1993. Merck KGaA later developed an oral form for multiple sclerosis. US regulators declined it in 2011 over safety concerns, but after longer follow-up it was approved in Europe in 2017 and by the FDA as Mavenclad in 2019 for relapsing forms of MS.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Carson and colleagues at Scripps develop cladribine as a lymphocyte-targeting drug."], ["1993", "IV cladribine (Leustatin) is approved for hairy cell leukemia."], ["2010", "The CLARITY trial shows oral cladribine reduces MS relapses."], ["2017", "Oral cladribine is approved in Europe for MS."], ["2019", "FDA approves Mavenclad for relapsing forms of MS."]], "cell": "Cladribine is a purine nucleoside analog, deoxyadenosine with a chlorine atom that makes it resistant to adenosine deaminase. It is a prodrug. Inside cells, deoxycytidine kinase adds a phosphate, and further phosphorylation produces cladribine triphosphate. Lymphocytes have high deoxycytidine kinase and low 5'-nucleotidase (which would remove the phosphate), so the active drug builds up mainly in them. Cladribine triphosphate is built into DNA, disrupts DNA synthesis and repair, depletes NAD and ATP and triggers apoptosis, even in resting cells. B cells are depleted more deeply than T cells. In MS, this short, targeted depletion is followed by slow repopulation, which may reset the immune system and reduce attacks for years.", "body": [["Immune system", "B and T lymphocytes drop sharply after each yearly course, with B cells recovering over months and T cells more slowly, which reduces MS relapses."], ["Infection risk", "Lymphopenia raises the risk of herpes zoster, tuberculosis reactivation and other opportunistic infections."], ["Cancer risk", "Malignancies were seen more often in trials, a boxed warning."], ["Unborn baby", "As a drug that disrupts DNA synthesis, it can cause birth defects, so pregnancy is avoided during and after treatment (boxed warning)."], ["Liver", "Liver injury has been reported, so liver tests are checked before treatment."]], "journey": "Oral cladribine is taken as short courses of a few days in two treatment years. About 40 percent is absorbed, peaking within about an hour. Protein binding is low and it spreads widely in tissues. Inside cells it is activated to its triphosphate, and the active form stays in lymphocytes much longer than the drug stays in plasma. It is cleared by both the kidneys and nonrenal routes, with a terminal half-life of about one day. The immune effects, however, last for months after each course.", "sidefx": "Lymphopenia is the intended effect and also the main risk, leading to infections like shingles and TB. Because it damages DNA, it raises concern for cancer and is teratogenic, which are boxed warnings. Patients are screened for infections such as TB and hepatitis, and vaccinations are updated before treatment. There is no antidote; lymphocytes recover on their own over time.", "src": ["Mavenclad (cladribine tablets) FDA label", "Carson DA et al. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA, 1980", "Giovannoni G et al. N Engl J Med, 2010 (CLARITY)"]}, "ublituximab": {"origin": "Ublituximab was first developed by the French company LFB, which produced antibodies in a rat cell line (YB2/0) that adds little fucose to the antibody's sugar chain. Low fucose makes antibodies bind immune cell Fc receptors much more strongly. TG Therapeutics licensed ublituximab and developed it for multiple sclerosis, building on evidence that removing B cells with anti-CD20 antibodies such as rituximab and ocrelizumab controls MS. The ULTIMATE I and II trials showed it lowered relapse rates compared with teriflunomide. The FDA approved Briumvi in 2022, notable for an infusion that can be given in about an hour after the first dose.", "timeline": [["2017", "Ocrelizumab, an anti-CD20 antibody, is approved for MS, supporting B-cell depletion."], ["2022", "ULTIMATE I and II results are published."], ["2022", "FDA approves Briumvi (ublituximab-xiiy) for relapsing MS."]], "cell": "CD20 is a protein on the surface of pre-B cells, mature B cells and memory B cells, but not on stem cells or most plasma cells. Ublituximab is a chimeric IgG1 antibody that binds a unique epitope on CD20. Because of its low fucose content, its Fc region binds Fc-gamma receptor IIIa on natural killer cells and macrophages with high affinity. This triggers antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity, in which NK cells kill the antibody-coated B cells, along with some complement-mediated killing. In MS, B cells present antigen to T cells, release inflammatory cytokines and help form brain lesions, so removing them reduces relapses. Since plasma cells lack CD20, existing antibody levels fall only slowly.", "body": [["Immune system", "Circulating B cells fall within a day of infusion and stay low between doses every six months, reducing MS relapses and new lesions."], ["Infusion reactions", "Fever, chills, headache and flushing can occur during infusion as B cells are destroyed and release cytokines; premedication lowers the risk."], ["Antibody levels", "Immunoglobulin levels can fall over time, raising infection risk."], ["Infection risk", "Respiratory infections are more common, and hepatitis B can reactivate."], ["Vaccines", "Responses to vaccines are blunted, so vaccines are given before starting."]], "journey": "Ublituximab is given by IV infusion, with a first slow infusion, a second two weeks later, and then shorter infusions every six months. Levels peak at the end of the infusion. It is cleared by gradual protein breakdown, as well as by binding to CD20 on B cells. Its half-life is about three weeks, but B-cell depletion lasts much longer because new B cells take months to grow back from the bone marrow.", "sidefx": "Infusion reactions are caused by rapid B-cell destruction and cytokine release. Loss of B cells reduces antibody production and raises the risk of infections, including hepatitis B reactivation and rarely PML. Hepatitis B screening is required before starting. Vaccine responses are reduced. There is no antidote; B cells recover on their own over months.", "src": ["Briumvi (ublituximab-xiiy) FDA label", "Steinman L et al. N Engl J Med, 2022 (ULTIMATE I and II)"]}, "ravulizumab": {"origin": "The complement system was described in the 1890s by Jules Bordet, who found that a heat-sensitive part of blood 'complemented' antibodies in killing bacteria. A century later, Alexion Pharmaceuticals developed eculizumab, an antibody that blocks complement protein C5, approved in 2007 for paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH). Eculizumab worked but needed infusions every two weeks. Alexion engineered ravulizumab from eculizumab by changing a few amino acids so the antibody releases C5 inside cells and is recycled back to the blood instead of being destroyed. The FDA approved Ultomiris in 2018 for PNH with dosing every eight weeks, and later for atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome, generalized myasthenia gravis and neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder.", "timeline": [["1890s", "Jules Bordet describes complement activity in blood."], ["2007", "Eculizumab is approved for PNH."], ["2018", "FDA approves Ultomiris (ravulizumab) for PNH."], ["2019", "Approval extends to atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome."], ["2022", "Approval extends to AChR antibody-positive generalized myasthenia gravis."], ["2024", "Approval extends to AQP4 antibody-positive NMOSD."]], "cell": "All three complement pathways meet at C5. C5 convertase cuts C5 into C5a, a strong inflammatory signal, and C5b, which joins C6 through C9 to form the membrane attack complex (C5b-9) that punches holes in cell membranes. Ravulizumab binds C5 with high affinity and blocks its cleavage, so neither C5a nor the membrane attack complex forms. Earlier steps, such as C3b coating of bacteria, still work. In PNH, red cells lack the protective proteins CD55 and CD59, so blocking C5 stops them from being destroyed in the blood. In aHUS it stops complement damage to vessel walls. In myasthenia gravis it protects the neuromuscular junction from complement attack triggered by anti-AChR antibodies, and in NMOSD it protects astrocytes targeted by anti-AQP4 antibodies. Its engineered changes help it let go of C5 in acidic endosomes and bind FcRn more, extending its half-life.", "body": [["Blood (red cells)", "In PNH, red cells stop bursting in the circulation, raising hemoglobin and reducing transfusion needs and clot risk."], ["Kidneys and vessels", "In aHUS, complement damage to small vessel linings stops, protecting kidney function and platelet counts."], ["Neuromuscular junction", "In myasthenia gravis, complement destruction of the muscle endplate is blocked, improving weakness."], ["Brain, spinal cord and optic nerves", "In NMOSD, astrocyte injury from complement is prevented, lowering relapse risk."], ["Infection defense", "Without membrane attack complex, the body cannot easily kill Neisseria bacteria, so meningococcal infections become much more likely."]], "journey": "Ravulizumab is given by IV infusion, with a loading dose followed by maintenance infusions every eight weeks. Complement is blocked immediately after the loading dose. As an IgG antibody, it is recycled by the neonatal Fc receptor and broken down slowly into amino acids. Its half-life is about 50 days, roughly four times longer than eculizumab. Plasma exchange and IV immunoglobulin remove or affect it, so extra doses may be needed after those treatments.", "sidefx": "The key danger follows directly from the mechanism. Neisseria meningitidis is normally killed by the membrane attack complex, so blocking C5 can lead to rapid, life-threatening meningococcal infection. This is a boxed warning, and the drug is given only through a restricted program that requires meningococcal vaccination before treatment. Other infections, especially encapsulated bacteria, are more likely. Stopping treatment in PNH or aHUS can cause sudden hemolysis or microangiopathy. Infusion reactions can occur. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Ultomiris (ravulizumab-cwvz) FDA label", "Lee JW et al. Blood, 2019", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "efgartigimod": {"origin": "In the 1960s the British biologist F. W. Rogers Brambell proposed that a special receptor protects IgG antibodies from breakdown, explaining why IgG lasts weeks in the blood. This receptor, later identified as the neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn), also carries IgG across the placenta. Researchers including E. Sally Ward found that engineering an IgG Fc fragment to bind FcRn more tightly could crowd out normal IgG and speed its destruction. The Belgian-Dutch company argenx built efgartigimod on this concept. The FDA approved Vyvgart in 2021 for generalized myasthenia gravis in adults who are anti-AChR antibody positive, the first FcRn blocker on the market. A subcutaneous version with hyaluronidase, Vyvgart Hytrulo, followed in 2023.", "timeline": [["1960s", "F. W. R. Brambell proposes a receptor that protects IgG from breakdown."], ["2005", "Engineered Fc fragments that enhance IgG degradation are described."], ["2021", "FDA approves Vyvgart (efgartigimod alfa) for generalized myasthenia gravis."], ["2023", "Subcutaneous Vyvgart Hytrulo is approved."]], "cell": "Cells lining blood vessels constantly sip up plasma, including IgG and albumin. In the acidic endosome, IgG binds FcRn, which carries it back to the cell surface and releases it into the blood. Unbound IgG goes to lysosomes and is destroyed. Efgartigimod is a human IgG1 Fc fragment carrying ABDEG mutations that make it bind FcRn much more tightly, at both acidic and neutral pH. It occupies FcRn so normal IgG cannot be rescued, and that IgG is sent to lysosomes. Total IgG falls, including the pathogenic anti-acetylcholine receptor antibodies that block and destroy receptors at the neuromuscular junction. IgA, IgM and IgE are not affected because they do not rely on FcRn, and albumin is spared because it binds FcRn at a different site.", "body": [["Neuromuscular junction", "Lower anti-AChR antibody levels mean less receptor blockade and complement damage, so muscle strength improves, with benefit in daily activities within a few weeks."], ["Blood (IgG)", "Total IgG falls by about 60 to 70 percent at its lowest, then returns toward baseline over weeks after a cycle."], ["Infection defense", "Lower IgG raises the risk of respiratory and urinary tract infections."], ["Head and nerves", "Headache and tingling (paresthesia) are reported, usually mild."], ["Immune system", "Hypersensitivity and infusion reactions can occur."]], "journey": "Efgartigimod is given as IV infusions once a week for four weeks, or as a subcutaneous injection with hyaluronidase, in cycles repeated based on symptoms. It binds FcRn and is broken down by protein catabolism; a small amount appears in urine. Its own half-life is a few days, but IgG lowering lasts longer because IgG takes weeks to rebuild. The lowest IgG level is reached about a week after the fourth infusion.", "sidefx": "Infections are more common because protective IgG is lowered along with harmful IgG. Because it clears IgG, it also lowers levels of IgG-based drugs such as other monoclonal antibodies and IVIG, which affects timing of those treatments. Live vaccines are avoided during treatment. Hypersensitivity reactions are immune responses to the protein. There is no antidote; IgG recovers on its own after cycles end.", "src": ["Vyvgart (efgartigimod alfa-fcab) FDA label", "Howard JF Jr et al. Lancet Neurol, 2021 (ADAPT)", "Vaccaro C et al. Nat Biotechnol, 2005"]}, "rozanolixizumab": {"origin": "Rozanolixizumab was developed by the Belgian company UCB as a full monoclonal antibody against the neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn), following the same idea as efgartigimod: if FcRn cannot recycle IgG, harmful autoantibodies are destroyed faster. Unlike efgartigimod, which is an Fc fragment, rozanolixizumab is a whole humanized IgG4 antibody that binds FcRn directly at the IgG binding site. In the MycarinG trial it improved daily function in generalized myasthenia gravis. The FDA approved Rystiggo in 2023 for adults with anti-AChR or anti-MuSK antibody positive generalized myasthenia gravis, making it the first FcRn blocker approved for both antibody types.", "timeline": [["2021", "Efgartigimod becomes the first FcRn blocker approved for myasthenia gravis."], ["2023", "MycarinG trial results are published."], ["2023", "FDA approves Rystiggo (rozanolixizumab-noli) for AChR and MuSK antibody positive generalized myasthenia gravis."]], "cell": "FcRn in endothelial and other cells rescues IgG from lysosomal breakdown by binding it in acidic endosomes and returning it to the blood. Rozanolixizumab is a humanized IgG4P antibody (the P marks a stabilizing hinge mutation) that binds FcRn at the site where IgG would attach. With FcRn blocked, IgG taken into cells cannot be recycled and is degraded in lysosomes. Total IgG levels fall, including pathogenic anti-acetylcholine receptor antibodies, which damage the receptor and activate complement, and anti-MuSK antibodies, which disrupt receptor clustering at the neuromuscular junction. Albumin also uses FcRn but binds a different site, so albumin is little affected. IgG4 has weak effector function, so the antibody does not kill FcRn-bearing cells.", "body": [["Neuromuscular junction", "Lower anti-AChR or anti-MuSK antibody levels allow better signal transmission, improving weakness, swallowing and breathing symptoms."], ["Blood (IgG)", "Total IgG falls in a dose-dependent way within days and recovers over several weeks after a treatment cycle."], ["Head", "Headache is very common and can be moderate to severe; aseptic meningitis has been reported."], ["Gut and temperature", "Diarrhea and fever are reported during treatment cycles."], ["Infection defense", "Lower IgG levels increase the risk of infections."]], "journey": "Rozanolixizumab is given as a weekly subcutaneous infusion for several weeks, in cycles. It is absorbed from the skin into the blood over days. Because it binds FcRn, its own clearance is target mediated and nonlinear, giving a short half-life of days. It is broken down by protein catabolism. The largest IgG drop comes about a week after the last weekly dose, and levels return toward baseline over about eight weeks.", "sidefx": "Infections rise because protective IgG falls along with harmful antibodies. Headache is the most common side effect; its cause is not fully known, and aseptic meningitis has occurred. Like efgartigimod, it lowers the levels of other IgG-based drugs and IVIG. Live vaccines are avoided during treatment. Hypersensitivity reactions can occur. There is no antidote; IgG recovers after the cycle ends.", "src": ["Rystiggo (rozanolixizumab-noli) FDA label", "Bril V et al. Lancet Neurol, 2023 (MycarinG)"]}, "armodafinil": {"origin": "Armodafinil is the purified right-handed (R) mirror image of modafinil. Modafinil grew out of work at the French company Lafon Laboratories in the 1970s, where chemists made adrafinil and then its active metabolite, modafinil. The French sleep scientist Michel Jouvet studied it in animals and people with narcolepsy, and it reached France in the 1990s. Cephalon brought modafinil to the US as Provigil in 1998. Because the R-enantiomer lasts longer in the body than the S form, Cephalon developed it as a single-isomer product, and the FDA approved armodafinil as Nuvigil in 2007. It offered a wake-promoting effect that holds later into the day than racemic modafinil.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Lafon Laboratories in France develops adrafinil and modafinil."], ["1998", "FDA approves modafinil (Provigil) for narcolepsy."], ["2007", "FDA approves armodafinil (Nuvigil), the longer-lasting R-enantiomer."]], "cell": "Armodafinil binds the dopamine transporter (DAT) on dopamine nerve terminals and blocks reuptake of dopamine from the synapse. Extracellular dopamine rises, mainly in the striatum and cortex. The extra dopamine signaling is thought to switch on downstream wake systems, including orexin (hypocretin) neurons in the lateral hypothalamus and histamine neurons in the tuberomammillary nucleus, and to quiet sleep-promoting GABA neurons in the ventrolateral preoptic area. It is not a releasing agent like amphetamine, so it does not dump stored monoamines from vesicles. The result is steady alertness with less euphoria and less sympathetic surge than classic stimulants.", "body": [["Brain", "Raised dopamine and recruited orexin and histamine pathways keep the cortex awake, reducing sleep attacks and daytime sleepiness in narcolepsy, sleep apnea and shift work disorder."], ["Heart and vessels", "Mild sympathetic activation can raise heart rate and blood pressure modestly, which matters in people with heart disease."], ["Mood and behavior", "Excess dopamine signaling in susceptible people can trigger anxiety, insomnia, mania or psychosis."], ["Liver", "It induces CYP3A4, which speeds the breakdown of hormonal contraceptives and other drugs, and it inhibits CYP2C19, which raises levels of drugs like omeprazole and phenytoin."], ["Skin and immune system", "Rarely, an immune reaction causes severe rashes such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome or DRESS."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth in the morning, armodafinil is well absorbed and peaks in about two hours on an empty stomach; food can delay the peak. It spreads through the body and is moderately bound to plasma proteins. The liver clears it mainly by amide hydrolysis, with a smaller share handled by CYP3A4/5, and the inactive metabolites leave in urine. The half-life is about 15 hours, which is why one morning dose covers most of the waking day. Because it induces CYP3A4, its interaction effects on other drugs can last about a month after stopping.", "sidefx": "Headache, insomnia and anxiety come from too much arousal in the brain. Higher heart rate and blood pressure reflect mild sympathetic activation. Psychiatric effects such as mania or psychosis follow from increased dopamine signaling. Serious rashes are immune reactions, not a dose effect, so any rash leads to stopping the drug. Lower contraceptive levels come from CYP3A4 induction. There is no antidote; overdose care is supportive, with benzodiazepines for agitation. It treats sleepiness in sleep apnea but does not fix the blocked airway.", "fun": "The exact way modafinil and armodafinil keep people awake was debated for decades. Brain imaging studies in the 2000s showed they occupy the dopamine transporter in people, which helped settle the question.", "src": ["Nuvigil (armodafinil) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Volkow ND et al. JAMA 2009 (modafinil and dopamine transporter)"]}, "solriamfetol": {"origin": "Solriamfetol is a small molecule derived from the amino acid phenylalanine. It was discovered by SK Biopharmaceuticals in South Korea and later licensed for development to Aerial Biopharma and then Jazz Pharmaceuticals. Jazz ran the clinical trials in people with narcolepsy and obstructive sleep apnea, and the FDA approved it as Sunosi in 2019 for excessive daytime sleepiness in both conditions. It was the first approved drug that blocks reuptake of both dopamine and norepinephrine for this use without releasing them. In 2022 the US rights moved to Axsome Therapeutics.", "timeline": [["2000s", "SK Biopharmaceuticals discovers the compound in South Korea."], ["2019", "FDA approves solriamfetol (Sunosi) for daytime sleepiness in narcolepsy and sleep apnea."], ["2022", "US rights transfer from Jazz Pharmaceuticals to Axsome Therapeutics."]], "cell": "Solriamfetol binds and blocks the dopamine transporter (DAT) and the norepinephrine transporter (NET). These transporters normally pull dopamine and norepinephrine back into the nerve terminal after release. With them blocked, both transmitters stay longer in the synapse and keep stimulating their receptors in wake-promoting circuits of the cortex, striatum and brainstem. Unlike amphetamines, it does not reverse the transporters or empty vesicles, and it has little effect on serotonin. The increased catecholamine tone raises cortical arousal and reduces sleepiness.", "body": [["Brain", "More dopamine and norepinephrine in arousal circuits improves wakefulness and the ability to stay awake during the day."], ["Heart and vessels", "Norepinephrine acting on the heart and blood vessels raises blood pressure and heart rate in a dose-related way."], ["Mood", "Extra catecholamine signaling can cause anxiety, irritability and trouble sleeping at night."], ["Appetite and gut", "Catecholamine activity suppresses appetite and can cause nausea."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys clear almost all of the drug unchanged, so reduced kidney function raises blood levels."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth in the morning, solriamfetol is almost completely absorbed and peaks in about two hours. It binds very little to plasma proteins and spreads freely. The liver barely touches it: about 1 percent is metabolized, and about 95 percent leaves unchanged in the urine. The half-life is about 7 hours, giving most of a day of effect. Because the kidneys do nearly all the work, kidney disease lengthens its stay in the body.", "sidefx": "Higher blood pressure and heart rate come straight from more norepinephrine at sympathetic targets, which is why blood pressure is checked before and during therapy. Headache, anxiety, insomnia and irritability reflect brain overarousal. Reduced appetite and nausea are catecholamine effects on hunger signals and the gut. There is no specific antidote; overdose care is supportive. In sleep apnea it treats sleepiness only, not the airway obstruction.", "fun": "Although it raises dopamine, solriamfetol does not release monoamines the way amphetamines do, and it is a Schedule IV controlled substance rather than Schedule II.", "src": ["Sunosi (solriamfetol) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "pitolisant": {"origin": "Pitolisant came from decades of histamine research in Paris. In 1983 Jean-Charles Schwartz and colleagues at INSERM discovered the histamine H3 receptor, a brake on histamine release in the brain. Schwartz's group worked with the French company Bioprojet to design a drug that would block this brake, and they created pitolisant. It was approved in the European Union in 2016 for narcolepsy. In the US, Harmony Biosciences brought it to market as Wakix after FDA approval in 2019 for daytime sleepiness in narcolepsy, followed in 2020 by approval for cataplexy. It was the first approved H3 receptor drug and is not a controlled substance.", "timeline": [["1983", "Arrang, Garbarg and Schwartz report the histamine H3 receptor in Nature."], ["2016", "Pitolisant is approved in the European Union for narcolepsy."], ["2019", "FDA approves pitolisant (Wakix) for excessive daytime sleepiness in narcolepsy."], ["2020", "FDA adds cataplexy in adults with narcolepsy."]], "cell": "Histamine neurons in the tuberomammillary nucleus of the hypothalamus project across the brain and keep it awake. These neurons carry H3 autoreceptors, which are Gi-coupled and have high constitutive (built-in) activity; when stimulated they reduce histamine synthesis and release. Pitolisant is an H3 antagonist and inverse agonist: it blocks histamine at the receptor and also turns down the receptor's background activity. The brake comes off, so histamine release rises and acts on postsynaptic H1 receptors in the cortex to promote wakefulness. H3 receptors also sit on other nerve terminals as heteroreceptors, so blocking them increases release of acetylcholine, norepinephrine and dopamine in the cortex, but not dopamine in the nucleus accumbens, which explains its low abuse potential.", "body": [["Brain", "More cortical histamine, acetylcholine and norepinephrine increase alertness and reduce daytime sleepiness and cataplexy attacks."], ["Sleep at night", "Increased arousal signals can cause insomnia."], ["Heart", "It can lengthen the QT interval, especially at high levels, so drugs or conditions that raise its levels or prolong QT add risk."], ["Liver", "It is a mild CYP3A4 inducer, which can lower hormonal contraceptive levels; CYP2D6 does most of its breakdown."], ["Stomach and mood", "Nausea and anxiety reflect increased neurotransmitter activity."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth in the morning, pitolisant is well absorbed and peaks in about three hours. It is highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP2D6, with help from CYP3A4, and the inactive metabolites leave in urine. The half-life is about 20 hours. People who are poor CYP2D6 metabolizers, or who take strong CYP2D6 inhibitors, build up higher levels. The full benefit on sleepiness builds over weeks.", "sidefx": "Insomnia, anxiety and headache follow from increased arousal transmitters. QT prolongation is a direct effect on cardiac repolarization at higher concentrations, which is why CYP2D6 inhibitors and other QT drugs matter. Reduced contraceptive effect comes from CYP3A4 induction, so a nonhormonal method is suggested during and for 21 days after therapy. Because it does not raise dopamine in the reward circuit, misuse is uncommon. There is no antidote; care for overdose is supportive with ECG monitoring.", "fun": "Pitolisant is one of the few wake-promoting drugs for narcolepsy in the US that is not a controlled substance.", "src": ["Wakix (pitolisant) FDA label", "Arrang JM, Garbarg M, Schwartz JC. Nature 1983", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "lemborexant": {"origin": "Lemborexant builds on a 1998 discovery. That year two separate teams found the same pair of brain peptides: Luis de Lecea and colleagues named them hypocretins, and Takeshi Sakurai and Masashi Yanagisawa named them orexins. Loss of orexin neurons was soon linked to narcolepsy, which showed that orexin keeps the brain awake. Drug makers reasoned that blocking orexin receptors could help insomnia without the broad brain depression of GABA drugs. Suvorexant became the first such drug in 2014. The Japanese company Eisai then developed lemborexant, and the FDA approved it as Dayvigo in December 2019 for adults with trouble falling or staying asleep.", "timeline": [["1998", "Two teams independently discover orexin (hypocretin) peptides."], ["1999", "Orexin signaling defects are linked to narcolepsy in dogs and mice."], ["2014", "Suvorexant becomes the first FDA-approved orexin receptor antagonist."], ["2019", "FDA approves lemborexant (Dayvigo) from Eisai for insomnia."]], "cell": "Orexin A and orexin B are released by neurons in the lateral hypothalamus. They act on two G protein-coupled receptors, OX1R and OX2R, found on arousal centers: the locus coeruleus (norepinephrine), raphe nuclei (serotonin), tuberomammillary nucleus (histamine) and basal forebrain (acetylcholine). Activation of these receptors depolarizes those neurons and holds the brain in the wake state. Lemborexant is a competitive antagonist at both OX1R and OX2R, with stronger binding at OX2R. By blocking orexin, it removes the wake signal so that the normal sleep drive can take over. It does not touch GABA-A receptors, so it lets sleep happen rather than forcing sedation.", "body": [["Brain", "Turning off orexin input to arousal nuclei shortens the time to fall asleep and reduces waking during the night."], ["Next-day function", "Its long half-life can leave some drug on board in the morning, causing next-day sleepiness and impaired driving."], ["Sleep-wake boundary", "Partial loss of orexin signaling mimics mild narcolepsy, which explains sleep paralysis, vivid dreams and hallucinations at sleep onset or waking."], ["Breathing", "Because it spares GABA systems, it causes little respiratory depression in studies, though risk rises with other CNS depressants."], ["Mood", "Worsening depression or suicidal thoughts can occur, as with other hypnotics."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth just before bedtime, lemborexant is absorbed quickly and peaks in one to three hours; a high-fat meal delays it. It is about 94 percent protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels and inducers lower them. Most of it leaves in feces, with some in urine. The effective half-life is long, about 17 to 19 hours, which is why at least seven hours in bed is part of label dosing.", "sidefx": "Somnolence and next-day impairment come from orexin blockade lasting into the morning. Sleep paralysis, hypnagogic hallucinations and cataplexy-like weakness happen because orexin normally stabilizes the line between sleep and waking. Complex sleep behaviors are rare. It is contraindicated in narcolepsy, where orexin is already missing. There is no specific antidote; overdose care is supportive.", "fun": "Narcolepsy research in Doberman pinschers helped reveal orexin. Narcoleptic dogs in a Stanford colony had a mutation in the orexin 2 receptor gene, reported in 1999.", "src": ["Dayvigo (lemborexant) FDA label", "Sakurai T et al. Cell 1998", "de Lecea L et al. PNAS 1998", "Lin L et al. Cell 1999"]}, "daridorexant": {"origin": "Daridorexant was developed by Idorsia, a Swiss company spun off from Actelion in 2017. Actelion scientists had worked on orexin receptor blockers for years after orexin was discovered in 1998. Idorsia's chemists aimed for a dual orexin receptor antagonist with a shorter half-life than earlier drugs, hoping to keep people asleep through the night while clearing by morning. The FDA approved daridorexant as Quviviq in January 2022 for adults with insomnia, making it the third drug in this class in the US after suvorexant and lemborexant.", "timeline": [["1998", "Orexin (hypocretin) peptides are discovered."], ["2017", "Idorsia is formed from Actelion's drug discovery group."], ["2022", "FDA approves daridorexant (Quviviq) for insomnia."]], "cell": "Daridorexant competitively blocks both orexin receptors, OX1R and OX2R, with similar strength at each. These Gq-coupled receptors on the locus coeruleus, raphe, tuberomammillary nucleus and basal forebrain normally receive orexin A and B from the lateral hypothalamus and drive those arousal neurons to fire. With orexin blocked, the arousal network quiets and the brain can slip into sleep. Because it does not act on GABA-A receptors, sleep architecture is largely preserved. Its shorter half-life (about 8 hours) means receptor blockade fades by morning in most people.", "body": [["Brain", "Blocking the orexin wake signal helps people fall asleep faster and stay asleep longer."], ["Morning alertness", "The shorter half-life is associated with less next-morning residual effect than longer-acting orexin blockers, though impairment can still occur."], ["Sleep-wake boundary", "Reduced orexin tone can cause sleep paralysis, hallucinations at sleep onset and brief cataplexy-like weakness."], ["Breathing", "Studies in mild to moderate sleep apnea and COPD showed little effect on breathing."], ["Mood", "Rarely, depression or suicidal thinking may worsen, as with other hypnotics."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth within 30 minutes of bedtime, daridorexant peaks in one to two hours; a heavy meal delays it. It is about 99.7 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver metabolizes it mostly by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors are avoided and moderate inhibitors require caution. Metabolites leave mainly in feces, with some in urine. The half-life is about 8 hours.", "sidefx": "Headache, sleepiness and fatigue come from orexin blockade, and next-day impairment can occur if less than a full night remains. Sleep paralysis and hallucinations reflect lost orexin stabilization of sleep-wake transitions. It is contraindicated in narcolepsy. Combining it with alcohol or other sedatives adds CNS depression. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Daridorexant was among the first products of Idorsia, a company founded the same year Johnson & Johnson bought Actelion.", "src": ["Quviviq (daridorexant) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "zaleplon": {"origin": "Zaleplon is a pyrazolopyrimidine, a chemical family unrelated to the benzodiazepines in structure. It was developed at American Cyanamid's Lederle research labs, which later became part of Wyeth. The goal was a sleeping pill that acted only at the GABA-A receptor subtype linked to sedation and that wore off fast. The FDA approved it as Sonata in 1999, joining zolpidem as one of the 'Z-drugs.' Its very short action made it useful for trouble falling asleep or for middle-of-the-night waking with several hours still left before getting up. The Sonata brand has been discontinued, but generic capsules remain.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Zaleplon is developed at American Cyanamid's Lederle laboratories."], ["1999", "FDA approves zaleplon (Sonata) for short-term treatment of insomnia."], ["2019", "FDA adds a boxed warning for complex sleep behaviors to Z-drugs."]], "cell": "GABA-A receptors are chloride channels made of five subunits. Zaleplon binds the benzodiazepine site, which lies between an alpha and a gamma-2 subunit, and it prefers receptors containing the alpha-1 subunit (the BZ1 or omega-1 type). Binding does not open the channel itself; it makes the receptor respond more strongly to GABA by increasing how often the chloride channel opens. More chloride flows in, the neuron becomes hyperpolarized and is harder to fire. Alpha-1 receptors are dense in the cortex and thalamus and drive sedation, so zaleplon causes sleep with little anxiolytic, muscle-relaxant or anticonvulsant effect. Flumazenil can reverse it by competing at the same site.", "body": [["Brain", "Enhanced GABA inhibition in sleep-wake circuits brings on sleep quickly, often within 20 to 30 minutes."], ["Memory", "Inhibition in the hippocampus can cause anterograde amnesia, especially if the person does not go to sleep."], ["Behavior", "Partial arousal while sedated can lead to complex sleep behaviors like sleep-walking or sleep-driving."], ["Breathing", "Alone it has little effect on breathing, but with alcohol or opioids respiratory depression can occur."], ["Muscle and balance", "Dizziness and impaired coordination raise fall risk, especially in older adults."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth, zaleplon is rapidly absorbed and peaks in about an hour, but heavy first-pass metabolism leaves only about 30 percent available. A heavy or high-fat meal slows its absorption. The liver breaks it down mainly by aldehyde oxidase, with a minor role for CYP3A4, into inactive products that leave in urine. The half-life is only about one hour, so the effect lasts a few hours and next-day hangover is uncommon.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness, dizziness and memory gaps all come from extra GABA-A inhibition in the brain. Complex sleep behaviors happen when parts of the brain wake up while judgment and memory stay suppressed; they carry a boxed warning and mean the drug is stopped. Combining it with alcohol, opioids or other sedatives stacks inhibition and can depress breathing. Stopping suddenly after regular use can cause rebound insomnia. Flumazenil reverses its effects at the benzodiazepine site.", "fun": "Zaleplon's one-hour half-life is among the shortest of any prescription sleeping pill.", "src": ["Sonata (zaleplon) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "flurazepam": {"origin": "Flurazepam came from the benzodiazepine work at Hoffmann-La Roche. In 1955 chemist Leo Sternbach made a compound that became chlordiazepoxide (Librium, 1960), and diazepam (Valium) followed in 1963. Roche then developed flurazepam as a benzodiazepine aimed at sleep, and it reached the US as Dalmane in 1970. It was one of the first benzodiazepines marketed specifically for insomnia and was widely used in the 1970s. Its very long-lived active metabolite later made shorter-acting hypnotics more popular. The Dalmane brand has been discontinued; generic capsules remain.", "timeline": [["1955", "Leo Sternbach at Roche makes the first benzodiazepine."], ["1960", "Chlordiazepoxide (Librium) is marketed."], ["1970", "Flurazepam (Dalmane) is introduced in the US for insomnia."]], "cell": "Flurazepam and its metabolites bind the benzodiazepine site at the interface of the alpha and gamma-2 subunits of the GABA-A receptor. This is a positive allosteric site: it changes the receptor shape so GABA binds and works better. The chloride channel opens more often, chloride enters, and the neuron hyperpolarizes. It acts on receptors containing alpha-1, 2, 3 or 5 subunits, giving sedation (alpha-1), anxiety relief (alpha-2 and 3) and memory effects (alpha-5). The parent drug is quickly converted to N1-desalkylflurazepam, an active metabolite with a half-life of two to four days that builds up with nightly use.", "body": [["Brain", "Increased GABA inhibition shortens the time to fall asleep and reduces night waking."], ["Next day", "The long-lived metabolite accumulates, so daytime drowsiness, slowed thinking and impaired driving grow over several nights of use."], ["Muscles and balance", "Ataxia and dizziness raise the risk of falls and hip fractures, especially in older adults."], ["Breathing", "Combined with opioids or alcohol, inhibition of brainstem breathing centers can become dangerous."], ["Memory", "Anterograde amnesia can occur, especially at higher doses."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth at bedtime, flurazepam is absorbed rapidly and the parent drug is converted in the liver to active metabolites, including N1-desalkylflurazepam, within hours. The parent has a half-life of about two hours, but the main metabolite lasts roughly 47 to 100 hours, so levels keep rising for one to two weeks of nightly use. The metabolites are highly protein bound. They are conjugated and leave in the urine. In older adults the metabolite stays even longer.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from too much GABA inhibition: drowsiness, poor coordination and confusion. The accumulating metabolite explains next-day hangover and the higher fall risk in older people. The boxed warning covers profound sedation and breathing suppression with opioids, abuse and misuse, and dependence. Regular use adapts the receptors, so sudden stopping can cause withdrawal with anxiety, insomnia and seizures; a gradual taper is used. Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepine effects but can trigger seizures in dependent patients.", "fun": "Because its metabolite lingers for days, flurazepam has a built-in slow taper, so rebound insomnia after stopping is milder than with short-acting sleeping pills.", "src": ["Flurazepam hydrochloride capsules FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Lopez-Munoz F et al. J Psychopharmacol 2011 (history of benzodiazepines)"]}, "tasimelteon": {"origin": "Tasimelteon was first made at Bristol-Myers Squibb as a melatonin receptor agonist. Vanda Pharmaceuticals licensed it in 2004 and tested it in an unusual group: totally blind people whose body clocks drift because no light reaches the brain's clock. This condition, called non-24-hour sleep-wake disorder, had no approved treatment. The FDA approved tasimelteon as Hetlioz in 2014 for non-24, as an orphan drug. In 2020 it was also approved for nighttime sleep problems in Smith-Magenis syndrome, a genetic disorder with an inverted melatonin rhythm, including a liquid form for children.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Bristol-Myers Squibb discovers the compound."], ["2004", "Vanda Pharmaceuticals licenses tasimelteon."], ["2014", "FDA approves Hetlioz for non-24-hour sleep-wake disorder."], ["2020", "FDA approves it for nighttime sleep disturbances in Smith-Magenis syndrome."]], "cell": "The suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) in the hypothalamus is the body's master clock. The pineal gland releases melatonin at night, and melatonin acts on two Gi-coupled receptors in the SCN. MT1 activation lowers cAMP and suppresses SCN neuron firing, which reduces the clock's wake-promoting output and helps sleep. MT2 activation shifts the timing of the clock (phase shifting). Tasimelteon is an agonist at both, with higher affinity for MT2. Taken at the same time every night, it acts like a strong, well-timed melatonin signal that resets the drifting clock to a 24-hour cycle, a process called entrainment. It has no action at GABA-A receptors.", "body": [["Brain clock", "Nightly MT2 activation pulls the free-running circadian rhythm into line with the 24-hour day, so sleep timing becomes stable."], ["Sleep", "MT1 activation in the SCN reduces the alerting signal and makes it easier to fall asleep at night."], ["Daytime function", "Somnolence can affect activities that require alertness."], ["Liver", "Mild ALT increases have been seen in trials."], ["Dreams", "Abnormal dreams or nightmares are among the common effects."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth at the same time each night without food, tasimelteon peaks in about half an hour to three hours; a high-fat meal lowers and delays the peak. It is about 90 percent protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP1A2 and CYP3A4, so smoking (which induces CYP1A2) lowers levels and fluvoxamine raises them. Metabolites leave mostly in urine. Its half-life is short, about 1.3 hours, but the clock-shifting effect builds over weeks to months.", "sidefx": "Sleepiness is an expected effect of melatonin receptor activation. Headache, nightmares and abnormal dreams are common. Because entrainment depends on each person's own clock, benefit may not appear for weeks or months, and missing doses can let the rhythm drift again. Elevated ALT has been seen. It has no abuse potential. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "People with non-24 who are totally blind cycle in and out of normal sleep timing, feeling like they have jet lag for weeks at a time, then fine, then jet-lagged again.", "src": ["Hetlioz (tasimelteon) FDA label", "Lockley SW et al. Lancet 2015 (tasimelteon in non-24)"]}, "chlorzoxazone": {"origin": "Chlorzoxazone is a benzoxazolone, a small synthetic molecule made in the 1950s. McNeil Laboratories introduced it in the US in the late 1950s as Paraflex, and it was soon combined with acetaminophen in a product called Parafon Forte. It belongs to the group of older central muscle relaxants used for short-term muscle spasm with sprains and strains. Today it is sold as Parafon Forte DSC, Lorzone and generics. It has also become an important research tool, because the liver breaks it down almost entirely through one enzyme.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Chlorzoxazone is synthesized and introduced by McNeil Laboratories."], ["1990s", "Researchers adopt chlorzoxazone as a standard probe for CYP2E1 activity."]], "cell": "Chlorzoxazone acts in the central nervous system rather than on muscle. It depresses multisynaptic reflex arcs in the spinal cord and in subcortical areas of the brain. These reflex loops pass through interneurons that link sensory input to motor neurons and help maintain muscle spasm after injury. Slowing transmission through these chains lowers motor neuron output to the spasming muscle. The exact molecular target is not established. It has no direct effect on muscle fibers, the neuromuscular junction or nerve conduction, and part of its clinical benefit may come from general sedation.", "body": [["Spinal cord", "Dampened polysynaptic reflexes reduce muscle spasm and the pain it causes after acute strains."], ["Brain", "Central depression causes drowsiness, dizziness and lightheadedness."], ["Liver", "Rarely, an idiosyncratic reaction causes serious, even fatal, hepatocellular injury."], ["Kidneys and urine", "A metabolite can turn urine orange or purple-red, which is harmless."], ["Gut", "Stomach upset is common and GI bleeding has been reported rarely."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth, chlorzoxazone is absorbed quickly and peaks in one to two hours. It works within about an hour and lasts three to four hours. In the liver, CYP2E1 adds a hydroxyl group to make 6-hydroxychlorzoxazone, which is then glucuronidated and excreted in urine. The half-life is about one hour. Because CYP2E1 activity changes with alcohol use and other factors, the drug's clearance can vary.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness and dizziness come from general central depression, and they add to the effects of alcohol and other sedatives. The most serious risk is rare idiosyncratic liver injury, which is not related to the dose; signs like jaundice, dark urine, nausea or right upper belly pain lead to stopping the drug. Allergic rashes and rarely anaphylaxis can occur. Urine color change is harmless. There is no antidote; overdose care is supportive.", "fun": "Because CYP2E1 handles nearly all of its metabolism, scientists give chlorzoxazone to volunteers to measure how active their CYP2E1 enzyme is.", "src": ["Chlorzoxazone tablets FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "inebilizumab": {"origin": "Inebilizumab grew out of a turning point in understanding neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder (NMOSD). In 2004 and 2005 Vanda Lennon, Claudia Lucchinetti and colleagues at the Mayo Clinic found an antibody in patients' blood, NMO-IgG, and showed it targets aquaporin-4, a water channel on astrocytes. That proved NMOSD is an antibody-driven disease, separate from multiple sclerosis. Researchers at MedImmune, part of AstraZeneca, designed an antibody against CD19 to remove the B cells and plasmablasts that make such antibodies. Viela Bio, spun out of AstraZeneca in 2018, finished development, and the FDA approved inebilizumab as Uplizna in 2020 for adults with anti-AQP4 positive NMOSD.", "timeline": [["2004", "Mayo Clinic researchers describe the NMO-IgG antibody."], ["2005", "Aquaporin-4 is identified as the target of NMO-IgG."], ["2018", "Viela Bio is formed from AstraZeneca to develop inebilizumab."], ["2020", "FDA approves inebilizumab (Uplizna) for AQP4 antibody positive NMOSD."]], "cell": "Inebilizumab is a humanized IgG1 kappa antibody that binds CD19, a surface protein found on B cells from the pre-B stage through mature B cells, and also on plasmablasts and some plasma cells. CD19 is present on more of the B-cell line than CD20, so it can reach antibody-making plasmablasts that anti-CD20 drugs miss. The antibody's Fc region lacks fucose sugar (afucosylated), which makes it bind much more tightly to FcgammaRIIIa on natural killer cells. Those NK cells then kill the coated B cells by antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity. With fewer plasmablasts, production of anti-AQP4 antibodies falls, so there is less complement attack on astrocytes in the optic nerves and spinal cord.", "body": [["Optic nerve and spinal cord", "Lower anti-AQP4 antibody production reduces attacks of optic neuritis and transverse myelitis, protecting vision and walking."], ["Immune system", "B-cell counts fall within days and stay low for months, which reduces autoantibody production but weakens defense against infection."], ["Antibody levels", "Over time total immunoglobulins can drop (hypogammaglobulinemia), raising infection risk."], ["Liver", "Loss of B cells can let hidden hepatitis B virus reactivate, so HBV screening is done before treatment."], ["Pregnancy", "IgG1 crosses the placenta and can deplete B cells in the fetus."]], "journey": "Inebilizumab is given as an intravenous infusion, so it enters the blood fully at the end of the infusion. Like other large antibodies, it stays mostly in blood and extracellular fluid. It is not metabolized by liver enzymes; cells break it down into amino acids by normal protein catabolism, and the kidneys do not excrete it. Its half-life is about 18 days. B-cell depletion begins within days and lasts for months, which allows dosing every six months after the starting doses.", "sidefx": "Infusion reactions such as headache, nausea and rash come from cytokine release as B cells are destroyed and from the protein infusion itself. Urinary tract and respiratory infections reflect weakened antibody defenses. Hepatitis B reactivation and the rare risk of PML happen because B cells normally help control latent viruses. Falling immunoglobulin levels add to infection risk. Live vaccines are avoided because the body may not control the vaccine virus. There is no reversal agent; effects fade as B cells slowly return.", "fun": "Before the aquaporin-4 antibody was found, many people with NMOSD were diagnosed with multiple sclerosis, and some MS drugs can actually worsen NMOSD.", "src": ["Uplizna (inebilizumab-cdon) FDA label", "Lennon VA et al. Lancet 2004", "Lennon VA et al. J Exp Med 2005", "Cree BAC et al. Lancet 2019 (N-MOmentum trial)"]}, "satralizumab": {"origin": "Satralizumab was created by Chugai Pharmaceutical in Japan, a member of the Roche group. Chugai had already developed tocilizumab, the first antibody against the interleukin-6 receptor. Its scientists then engineered a 'recycling' antibody that lets go of its target inside cells and returns to the bloodstream to bind again, so it lasts much longer. Researchers had found high IL-6 levels in the spinal fluid of people with NMOSD and that IL-6 helps antibody-making plasmablasts survive. The FDA approved satralizumab as Enspryng in 2020 for adults with anti-AQP4 positive NMOSD, as a subcutaneous injection given every four weeks after loading doses.", "timeline": [["2010", "Tocilizumab, Chugai's earlier anti-IL-6 receptor antibody, is approved in the US."], ["2010s", "Chugai develops recycling antibody technology used in satralizumab."], ["2020", "FDA approves satralizumab (Enspryng) for AQP4 antibody positive NMOSD."]], "cell": "Interleukin-6 signals by binding the IL-6 receptor (soluble or on the cell membrane), which then pairs with gp130 and activates JAK kinases and STAT3. In NMOSD, IL-6 helps plasmablasts survive and make anti-AQP4 antibodies, pushes T cells toward the inflammatory Th17 type and makes the blood-brain barrier leakier. Satralizumab, a humanized IgG2, binds both forms of the IL-6 receptor and blocks IL-6 from docking. It was engineered with pH-dependent binding: in the acidic endosome it releases the receptor, which is degraded, while the antibody is rescued by the neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn) and sent back to the blood. One antibody can therefore block receptors many times, prolonging its effect.", "body": [["Optic nerve and spinal cord", "Blocking IL-6 reduces production of anti-AQP4 antibodies and inflammation, lowering the relapse rate."], ["Immune system", "Reduced IL-6 signaling weakens some defenses, so infections are the most common serious effect."], ["Fever and CRP", "IL-6 drives fever and the liver's CRP production, so infections may be masked with little fever or CRP rise."], ["Blood", "Neutrophil counts can fall, since IL-6 helps regulate neutrophil production and release from the bone marrow."], ["Liver and lipids", "Liver enzymes and blood lipids can rise, as seen with other IL-6 blockers."]], "journey": "Satralizumab is injected under the skin. Lymph vessels slowly carry it into the blood, and it peaks about four to five days after a dose. It stays mainly in the bloodstream and tissue fluid. It is broken down like other proteins into amino acids, not by liver enzymes, and is not excreted by the kidneys. Thanks to FcRn recycling, its half-life is about 30 days, which allows dosing every four weeks.", "sidefx": "Infections occur because IL-6 helps coordinate immune defense. The danger is that blocking IL-6 also blunts fever and CRP, so a serious infection can look mild. Neutropenia and liver enzyme rises reflect IL-6 roles in the bone marrow and liver. Injection-site reactions are local responses to the protein. Live vaccines are avoided during treatment. There is no reversal agent; effects wear off as the drug clears over weeks.", "fun": "Satralizumab's recycling design lets a single antibody molecule bind and remove IL-6 receptors several times, instead of being destroyed with the first one.", "src": ["Enspryng (satralizumab-mwge) FDA label", "Yamamura T et al. N Engl J Med 2019 (SAkuraSky)", "Igawa T et al. Nat Biotechnol 2010 (recycling antibodies)"]}, "methsuximide": {"origin": "Methsuximide is one of three succinimide seizure drugs developed by Parke-Davis in the 1950s, when researchers were searching for safer treatments for absence (petit mal) seizures than the toxic oxazolidinediones. Phensuximide came first, then methsuximide, marketed as Celontin in the late 1950s, and then ethosuximide (Zarontin) around 1960. Ethosuximide became the standard succinimide for absence seizures. Methsuximide is kept for absence seizures that do not respond to other drugs, and it has some activity against other seizure types.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Parke-Davis develops the succinimide anticonvulsants."], ["1957", "Methsuximide (Celontin) is introduced for absence seizures."], ["1960", "Ethosuximide (Zarontin) follows and becomes the main succinimide."]], "cell": "Absence seizures arise from rhythmic firing between the thalamus and cortex. Thalamic relay neurons have low-threshold T-type (Cav3) calcium channels that open when the cell is slightly hyperpolarized and produce bursts of firing. These bursts drive the 3 per second spike-and-wave pattern seen on EEG. Methsuximide is converted in the liver to N-desmethylmethsuximide, which blocks these T-type channels. With fewer calcium bursts, the thalamocortical loop cannot sustain the abnormal rhythm, so absence seizures are suppressed and the seizure threshold rises.", "body": [["Brain", "Blocking thalamic T-type channels stops the spike-and-wave rhythm of absence seizures."], ["Alertness and coordination", "Drowsiness, dizziness and ataxia come from central nervous system depression."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting, loss of appetite and hiccups are common."], ["Blood", "Rare but sometimes fatal blood dyscrasias, such as low white cells or platelets, can occur, so blood counts are checked."], ["Immune system", "Rarely it can trigger drug-induced lupus."], ["Mood and behavior", "Behavior changes and, as with all seizure drugs, suicidal thoughts can occur."]], "journey": "Methsuximide is taken by mouth as capsules and absorbed fairly quickly, peaking in one to three hours. The liver quickly removes a methyl group to make N-desmethylmethsuximide, the main active form. The parent drug lasts only two to four hours, but the metabolite has a half-life of about 28 to 80 hours, so it builds up over days and drives most of the effect. Metabolites leave mainly in the urine. Protein binding is low.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness, dizziness and ataxia reflect general brain depression from the drug. GI upset is common early. Blood dyscrasias and lupus-like illness are idiosyncratic immune or marrow reactions, which is why blood counts and liver and kidney tests are checked. Stopping suddenly can trigger absence status, so it is withdrawn slowly. Like other antiepileptic drugs, it carries a warning about suicidal thoughts. There is no antidote; overdose is treated with supportive care.", "fun": "Methsuximide's active metabolite outlives the parent drug by roughly twentyfold, so the capsule a person swallows is really a delivery system for N-desmethylmethsuximide.", "src": ["Celontin (methsuximide) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "zilucoplan": {"origin": "Zilucoplan is a small ring-shaped (macrocyclic) peptide, not an antibody. It was discovered by Ra Pharmaceuticals in Cambridge, Massachusetts, using mRNA display, a method that screens enormous libraries of peptides for ones that grip a target tightly. Ra designed it to block complement C5, the same target as the antibody eculizumab, but in a form small enough for daily self-injection under the skin. UCB acquired Ra Pharmaceuticals in 2020, and the FDA approved zilucoplan as Zilbrysq in October 2023 for adults with generalized myasthenia gravis who have antibodies against the acetylcholine receptor.", "timeline": [["2007", "Eculizumab, the first C5 inhibitor antibody, is approved in the US."], ["2010s", "Ra Pharmaceuticals discovers zilucoplan with mRNA display peptide screening."], ["2020", "UCB acquires Ra Pharmaceuticals."], ["2023", "FDA approves zilucoplan (Zilbrysq) for anti-AChR positive generalized myasthenia gravis."]], "cell": "In myasthenia gravis, antibodies against acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction activate the complement cascade. This leads to cleavage of C5 by C5 convertase into C5a, which draws in inflammatory cells, and C5b, which joins C6 through C9 to form the membrane attack complex (MAC). The MAC punches holes in the muscle endplate and destroys acetylcholine receptors. Zilucoplan is a 15-amino-acid macrocyclic peptide that binds C5 tightly and stops it from being cleaved. It also binds C5b and prevents it from attaching to C6, a second block on MAC assembly. With the MAC blocked, the endplate keeps its receptors and nerve signals pass more reliably, improving strength.", "body": [["Neuromuscular junction", "Preventing MAC damage preserves acetylcholine receptors, so muscle strength and fatigue improve."], ["Muscles", "Better transmission eases eye, swallowing, breathing and limb weakness from myasthenia gravis."], ["Immune defense", "The MAC is the main way the body kills Neisseria bacteria, so blocking it greatly raises the risk of meningococcal infection."], ["Pancreas", "Pancreatitis and pancreatic cysts have been reported, and lipase and amylase can rise."], ["Skin", "Injection-site reactions are common; rare morphea has been seen."]], "journey": "Zilucoplan is injected under the skin once a day from a prefilled syringe. It is absorbed over several hours and peaks about three to six hours after a dose, with complement inhibition starting within hours. It binds very tightly to plasma proteins, more than 99 percent. It is broken down by proteolysis into smaller pieces, some of which still block C5, and very little leaves through the kidneys. The half-life is about seven days, so daily dosing keeps C5 steadily suppressed.", "sidefx": "The main danger comes straight from the mechanism: without terminal complement, the body cannot easily kill encapsulated Neisseria. That is why there is a boxed warning for meningococcal infection, vaccination is required at least two weeks before starting unless treatment cannot wait, and the drug is only available through a REMS program. Pancreatitis and pancreatic cysts have been reported, so lipase and amylase are checked. Injection-site bruising and pain are common. There is no reversal agent; complement function returns as the drug clears.", "fun": "Zilucoplan is the first once-daily, self-injected C5 inhibitor peptide for myasthenia gravis, a small synthetic ring doing the job that previously required an IV antibody infusion.", "src": ["Zilbrysq (zilucoplan) FDA label", "Howard JF Jr et al. Lancet Neurol 2023 (RAISE trial)"]}, "nipocalimab": {"origin": "Nipocalimab works by hijacking a recycling system first proposed in the 1960s by F. W. Rogers Brambell, who suggested a receptor protects IgG antibodies from being broken down and carries them from mother to baby. That receptor, now called the neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn), explains why IgG lasts weeks in the blood. Momenta Pharmaceuticals developed nipocalimab, a fully human antibody that blocks FcRn, under the name M281. Johnson & Johnson acquired Momenta in 2020 and completed trials in myasthenia gravis. The FDA approved nipocalimab as Imaavy in 2025 for generalized myasthenia gravis in adults and adolescents who are anti-AChR or anti-MuSK antibody positive.", "timeline": [["1960s", "F. W. R. Brambell proposes a receptor that protects IgG from breakdown."], ["2020", "Johnson & Johnson acquires Momenta Pharmaceuticals and nipocalimab."], ["2021", "Efgartigimod becomes the first FDA-approved FcRn blocker."], ["2025", "FDA approves nipocalimab (Imaavy) for generalized myasthenia gravis."]], "cell": "Cells lining blood vessels constantly take in plasma proteins by pinocytosis. In the acidic endosome, IgG binds FcRn, which carries it back to the cell surface and releases it into the blood, rescuing it from the lysosome. This recycling gives IgG its long half-life. Nipocalimab is an aglycosylated human IgG1 that binds FcRn tightly at both neutral and acidic pH, filling the site where IgG normally binds. Without FcRn rescue, IgG molecules, including pathogenic anti-AChR and anti-MuSK autoantibodies, are sent to lysosomes and degraded. Total IgG levels fall quickly, so fewer autoantibodies attack the neuromuscular junction. Removing the sugar from its Fc region removes effector functions, so it does not kill cells. IgA, IgM and albumin are largely unaffected.", "body": [["Neuromuscular junction", "Lower levels of autoantibodies mean less attack on acetylcholine receptors and MuSK, improving muscle strength."], ["Immune system", "Total IgG falls substantially, which weakens antibody defense and raises infection risk."], ["Vaccines", "Lower IgG may blunt vaccine responses, so vaccines are usually given before starting; live vaccines are avoided."], ["Muscles and limbs", "Muscle spasms and peripheral edema have been reported."], ["Pregnancy", "FcRn also carries IgG across the placenta, so blocking it reduces antibody transfer to the fetus."]], "journey": "Nipocalimab is given as an intravenous infusion, typically every two weeks after a loading dose. It reaches peak levels at the end of the infusion. Because it binds its own target, FcRn, its clearance is nonlinear and target-mediated: as receptors become saturated, more drug stays in the blood. It is broken down by normal protein catabolism, not liver enzymes, and is not excreted by the kidneys. IgG levels drop within days and rise again as the drug clears.", "sidefx": "Infections are the main risk, because IgG is a key part of immune defense and the drug lowers it across the board. Hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis and angioedema, and infusion reactions are responses to the protein itself. Headache, diarrhea, edema and muscle spasms are also reported. Live vaccines are avoided during treatment. There is no reversal agent; IgG returns as FcRn becomes free again.", "fun": "FcRn got the name 'neonatal' because it was first found transporting antibodies from mother's milk across the gut of newborn rats.", "src": ["Imaavy (nipocalimab-aahu) FDA label", "Brambell FWR. Lancet 1966", "Roopenian DC, Akilesh S. Nat Rev Immunol 2007 (FcRn review)"]}, "dihydroergotamine-nasal": {"origin": "Dihydroergotamine (DHE) comes from ergot, a fungus (Claviceps purpurea) that infects rye and caused outbreaks of poisoning called St. Anthony's fire in the Middle Ages. In 1918 Arthur Stoll at Sandoz in Basel isolated ergotamine, which became a migraine treatment. In 1943 Stoll and Albert Hofmann made DHE by adding hydrogen to ergotamine, producing a gentler drug with less artery constriction. An injectable form, D.H.E. 45, was used for decades. To avoid needles, a nasal spray (Migranal) was approved in the US in 1997, and a device that sprays deeper into the upper nasal cavity (Trudhesa) followed in 2021.", "timeline": [["9th century", "Outbreaks of ergot poisoning from contaminated rye, later called St. Anthony's fire, are recorded in Europe."], ["1918", "Arthur Stoll at Sandoz isolates ergotamine."], ["1943", "Stoll and Albert Hofmann synthesize dihydroergotamine."], ["1997", "FDA approves DHE nasal spray (Migranal) for migraine."], ["2021", "FDA approves Trudhesa, an upper nasal DHE delivery device."]], "cell": "DHE acts on many receptors because its ergoline ring resembles serotonin, norepinephrine and dopamine. For migraine, the key targets are 5-HT1B receptors on smooth muscle of dilated cranial blood vessels, where activation causes vasoconstriction, and 5-HT1D receptors on trigeminal nerve endings, where activation reduces release of CGRP and substance P. That calms neurogenic inflammation and blocks pain signals in the trigeminal system. DHE dissociates slowly from its receptors, so its action outlasts its blood levels and headaches come back less often. It also acts at 5-HT1A, 5-HT2, alpha-adrenergic and dopamine D2 receptors, which explains nausea and narrowing of arteries in the limbs.", "body": [["Cranial blood vessels", "5-HT1B activation narrows painfully widened meningeal vessels."], ["Trigeminal nerves", "5-HT1D activation stops release of CGRP and substance P, interrupting migraine pain and inflammation."], ["Nose", "The spray can irritate the nasal lining and change taste."], ["Stomach and brain", "Dopamine D2 activity in the vomiting center causes nausea."], ["Heart and limbs", "Alpha-adrenergic and 5-HT effects can constrict coronary and peripheral arteries, which is dangerous in people with heart or vascular disease."]], "journey": "DHE is poorly absorbed from the gut, so it is given by nasal spray (or by injection). From the nose it crosses the lining into the blood, starting to work in about 30 minutes; a portion is swallowed and largely destroyed by first-pass metabolism. About 93 percent is protein bound. The liver metabolizes it through CYP3A4 into active 8'-hydroxy-dihydroergotamine. Most of it leaves in bile and feces. The terminal half-life is about 10 hours, and slow receptor release prolongs the effect.", "sidefx": "Nasal irritation and taste changes come from contact with the nasal lining. Nausea reflects dopamine receptor activity. The most serious risk is ischemia: DHE constricts arteries, and when strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as protease inhibitors or macrolide antibiotics block its breakdown, levels rise and can cut off blood flow to the brain or limbs, which carries a boxed warning. It can also trigger coronary vasospasm. Frequent long-term use of ergots can cause fibrosis. There is no specific antidote; vasodilators and supportive care are used for vasospasm.", "fun": "Albert Hofmann, who co-created DHE, also made LSD in 1938 while studying ergot compounds at Sandoz.", "src": ["Migranal (dihydroergotamine mesylate) nasal spray FDA label", "Trudhesa FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "daxibotulinumtoxin": {"origin": "All botulinum toxins come from Clostridium botulinum, a soil bacterium. The disease botulism was described in the 1820s by the German doctor Justinus Kerner, who linked it to spoiled sausages, and in 1895 Emile van Ermengem in Belgium identified the bacterium. Tiny purified amounts became medicines starting in the 1980s. Revance Therapeutics in California developed daxibotulinumtoxinA with a different stabilizer: instead of human albumin, it uses a man-made peptide called RTP004 that carries a positive charge and helps the toxin stick in tissue. The FDA approved it as Daxxify in 2022 for frown (glabellar) lines and in 2023 for cervical dystonia. Its main selling point is a longer duration of effect.", "timeline": [["1820s", "Justinus Kerner describes 'sausage poisoning,' later called botulism."], ["1895", "Emile van Ermengem identifies Clostridium botulinum."], ["2022", "FDA approves daxibotulinumtoxinA (Daxxify) for glabellar lines."], ["2023", "FDA approves Daxxify for cervical dystonia."]], "cell": "Botulinum toxin type A has a heavy chain and a light chain. The heavy chain binds gangliosides and the SV2 protein on cholinergic nerve terminals, and the toxin is taken in by endocytosis. Acid in the vesicle lets the heavy chain form a pore so the light chain escapes into the cytoplasm. The light chain is a zinc-dependent protease that cuts SNAP-25, one of the SNARE proteins that zip vesicles to the cell membrane. Without intact SNAP-25, vesicles cannot fuse and acetylcholine is not released, so the nearby muscle relaxes (chemodenervation). In daxibotulinumtoxinA, the RTP004 peptide is thought to help hold the toxin in place, and it is associated with a longer effect. Nerves recover slowly by sprouting new terminals and replacing SNAP-25.", "body": [["Face", "Relaxed corrugator and procerus muscles smooth frown lines, often for about six months."], ["Neck", "In cervical dystonia, weakened overactive neck muscles reduce abnormal head posture and pain."], ["Eyelids and face", "Spread to nearby muscles can cause eyelid droop (ptosis) or facial weakness."], ["Throat", "Spread to swallowing muscles can cause trouble swallowing, mainly after neck injections."], ["Whole body", "Rarely, toxin spreads far from the injection site, causing generalized weakness, double vision and breathing trouble."]], "journey": "DaxibotulinumtoxinA is injected directly into the target muscle in very small amounts. It is not measurable in the blood at labeled doses, because it binds nearby nerve terminals and stays local. Effects start in one to two days, peak around two weeks and, for frown lines, last a median of about six months. The toxin inside nerves is eventually broken down by proteolysis, and function returns as new nerve sprouts form and SNAP-25 is replaced.", "sidefx": "Ptosis, facial weakness and swallowing trouble happen when the toxin spreads to muscles next to the injection site. The boxed warning describes distant spread hours to weeks later, which can cause botulism-like symptoms such as weakness, double vision, slurred speech and breathing difficulty. People with neuromuscular disease, such as myasthenia gravis, are more sensitive. Units are not interchangeable with other botulinum products. Headache and injection-site reactions are common. Botulism antitoxin exists for systemic botulism but is not routinely used for these local effects.", "fun": "Daxxify was the first botulinum toxin approved without human serum albumin, using a synthetic peptide as its stabilizer instead.", "src": ["Daxxify (daxibotulinumtoxinA-lanm) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Erbguth FJ. Mov Disord 2004 (history of botulinum toxin)"]}, "incobotulinumtoxina": {"origin": "IncobotulinumtoxinA was developed by Merz Pharmaceuticals in Frankfurt, Germany. Botulinum toxin is normally made by Clostridium botulinum bound to a group of 'complexing' proteins that protect it in the gut. Merz purified the toxin away from these proteins, leaving the 150-kilodalton active neurotoxin alone, partly to lower the chance of the body making antibodies that block the drug. It was first approved in Germany in 2005. The FDA approved it as Xeomin in 2010 for cervical dystonia and blepharospasm, then for frown lines, upper limb spasticity and chronic drooling (sialorrhea).", "timeline": [["2005", "Incobotulinumtoxin A is first approved in Germany."], ["2010", "FDA approves Xeomin for cervical dystonia and blepharospasm."], ["2011", "FDA approves Xeomin for glabellar lines."], ["2018", "FDA approves Xeomin for chronic sialorrhea in adults."]], "cell": "The heavy chain of the toxin binds gangliosides and SV2 on cholinergic nerve terminals and is pulled inside by endocytosis. In the acidic vesicle, the heavy chain helps the light chain cross into the cytoplasm. The light chain, a zinc-dependent endopeptidase, cleaves SNAP-25, a SNARE protein needed for synaptic vesicle fusion. Without SNAP-25, acetylcholine cannot be released at the neuromuscular junction, so the muscle weakens. It also blocks cholinergic autonomic nerves, such as those to salivary glands, which is why it reduces drooling. Because no complexing proteins are present, there is less foreign protein to provoke neutralizing antibodies.", "body": [["Muscles", "Local weakness relieves cervical dystonia, blepharospasm and upper limb spasticity for about three months."], ["Salivary glands", "Blocking cholinergic nerves to the parotid and submandibular glands reduces saliva and drooling."], ["Face", "Relaxing frown muscles smooths glabellar lines."], ["Eyes", "In blepharospasm, weaker eyelid closure can cause dry eye and corneal exposure."], ["Throat", "Spread to swallowing muscles can cause dysphagia and, rarely, aspiration."]], "journey": "Incobotulinumtoxin A is injected into the target muscle or gland. It binds local nerve terminals and is not measurable in the blood at labeled doses. Effects appear in about three to seven days, peak at two to six weeks and fade over about three months. The toxin is broken down inside nerve terminals by proteolysis, and function returns as nerves sprout new endings. Unlike some botulinum products, the unopened powder can be stored at room temperature.", "sidefx": "Dysphagia, neck pain and muscle weakness come from toxin acting on or spreading to nearby muscles. Dry mouth reflects blocked salivary nerves. The boxed warning covers distant spread of toxin, which can cause botulism-like weakness, double vision, swallowing and breathing problems hours to weeks after injection. People with neuromuscular junction disorders are at higher risk. Units are not interchangeable with other botulinum toxins. Botulism antitoxin exists for true systemic botulism.", "fun": "Because Xeomin contains only the bare neurotoxin, it is sometimes called a 'naked' botulinum toxin.", "src": ["Xeomin (incobotulinumtoxinA) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "rimabotulinumtoxinb": {"origin": "RimabotulinumtoxinB is the only botulinum toxin type B approved in the US. Clostridium botulinum strains make seven classic toxin types, A through G, and type B attacks a different protein in nerve endings than type A. Elan Pharmaceuticals developed type B as a treatment for cervical dystonia, partly to help patients whose bodies had made antibodies against type A. The FDA approved it as Myobloc in 2000 for cervical dystonia, and in 2019 for chronic drooling (sialorrhea) in adults. It comes as a ready-to-use acidic liquid.", "timeline": [["1895", "Emile van Ermengem identifies Clostridium botulinum."], ["1989", "FDA approves the first botulinum toxin type A product."], ["2000", "FDA approves rimabotulinumtoxinB (Myobloc) for cervical dystonia."], ["2019", "FDA approves Myobloc for chronic sialorrhea in adults."]], "cell": "Like other botulinum toxins, type B has a heavy chain that binds cholinergic nerve terminals (using gangliosides and synaptotagmin as its protein receptor) and carries the light chain inside. The type B light chain is a zinc-dependent protease that cuts synaptobrevin, also called VAMP (vesicle-associated membrane protein). Synaptobrevin sits on the synaptic vesicle and is needed to form the SNARE complex with SNAP-25 and syntaxin. Without it, vesicles cannot fuse, so acetylcholine release stops at the neuromuscular junction and at cholinergic autonomic terminals. Type B has especially strong effects on autonomic nerves, which explains its frequent dry mouth.", "body": [["Neck muscles", "Weakening overactive neck muscles reduces the twisting and pain of cervical dystonia."], ["Salivary glands", "Strong block of cholinergic nerves to salivary glands reduces drooling, and also causes dry mouth."], ["Throat", "Spread to swallowing muscles can cause dysphagia and aspiration."], ["Eyes", "Autonomic effects can blur vision by affecting focusing (accommodation)."], ["Whole body", "Distant spread can rarely cause generalized weakness and breathing problems."]], "journey": "RimabotulinumtoxinB is injected into the target muscle or salivary gland as a liquid. It binds local nerve terminals and is not measurable in the blood at labeled doses. Effects start in about three to seven days, peak at two to four weeks and last about 12 to 16 weeks. The toxin is broken down inside nerves, and function returns as the nerve makes new synaptobrevin and sprouts new terminals. The acidic solution can sting on injection.", "sidefx": "Dry mouth is the most typical side effect, because type B strongly blocks cholinergic autonomic nerves. Dysphagia and neck weakness come from local spread to nearby muscles. The boxed warning covers distant spread of toxin effect, with botulism-like weakness, swallowing and breathing problems. Units are very different from type A products and not interchangeable. Botulism antitoxin exists for systemic botulism.", "fun": "Because it cleaves synaptobrevin instead of SNAP-25, type B toxin can still work in some people whose bodies have made blocking antibodies against type A.", "src": ["Myobloc (rimabotulinumtoxinB) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "abobotulinumtoxina": {"origin": "AbobotulinumtoxinA traces back to the UK government research center at Porton Down, England, where scientists produced and purified botulinum toxin type A. The product was developed through Porton's spinoff companies and later by the French company Ipsen. It was approved in the UK in the early 1990s as Dysport. The FDA approved Dysport in 2009 for cervical dystonia and glabellar lines, and later for spasticity in adults and children. Its units are measured with a different test than other type A toxins, so Dysport units are not equal to Botox units.", "timeline": [["1895", "Emile van Ermengem identifies Clostridium botulinum."], ["1991", "Dysport is approved in the United Kingdom."], ["2009", "FDA approves Dysport for cervical dystonia and glabellar lines."], ["2015", "FDA approves Dysport for upper limb spasticity in adults."]], "cell": "Botulinum toxin type A works as a two-part molecule. Its heavy chain binds gangliosides and the SV2 protein on cholinergic nerve terminals, and the toxin enters by endocytosis. Acidification of the vesicle allows the light chain to escape into the cytoplasm. The light chain, a zinc-dependent endopeptidase, cleaves SNAP-25, one of the SNARE proteins that fuse acetylcholine vesicles with the nerve membrane. Acetylcholine release stops and the muscle becomes weaker and relaxed. The effect lasts until the nerve sprouts new terminals and replaces SNAP-25, usually about three to four months. Dysport is a toxin-protein complex formulated with lactose and human albumin.", "body": [["Muscles", "Local weakness reduces spasticity and abnormal posture in the arms and legs and the twisting of cervical dystonia."], ["Face", "Relaxed frown muscles smooth glabellar lines; spread can cause eyelid droop."], ["Throat", "Spread to swallowing muscles can cause dysphagia, with risk of aspiration."], ["Mouth", "Dry mouth can occur from effects on cholinergic autonomic nerves."], ["Whole body", "Distant spread, especially in children treated for spasticity, can cause generalized weakness and breathing problems."]], "journey": "AbobotulinumtoxinA is injected directly into the target muscles. It binds nearby nerve terminals and is not measurable in the blood at labeled doses. Effects begin in about two to seven days, peak at two to four weeks and last about three to four months. The toxin is broken down inside nerve terminals by proteolysis, and normal signaling returns as nerves sprout and rebuild their release machinery.", "sidefx": "Weakness, dysphagia and ptosis come from local toxin acting on or spreading to nearby muscles. The boxed warning covers distant spread, with botulism-like symptoms such as generalized weakness, double vision and breathing trouble hours to weeks later; children treated for spasticity are at particular risk. Units are not interchangeable with other botulinum toxins. The product may contain trace cow's milk protein, a concern for people with milk allergy. Botulism antitoxin exists for systemic botulism.", "fun": "Porton Down, where Dysport began, is also known as the British government's chemical and biological defense laboratory.", "src": ["Dysport (abobotulinumtoxinA) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "carbidopa-levodopa-entacapone": {"origin": "This three-part tablet stacks several discoveries. In 1957 and 1958 Arvid Carlsson in Sweden showed that dopamine is a brain transmitter and that levodopa reverses reserpine-induced stiffness in animals. In 1960 Oleh Hornykiewicz in Vienna found that the brains of people with Parkinson disease lacked dopamine. In 1967 George Cotzias showed that slowly increasing oral levodopa could transform the disease. Merck added carbidopa to block levodopa breakdown outside the brain, and Sinemet was approved in 1975. The Finnish company Orion Pharma then developed entacapone, a COMT blocker approved in the US in 1999 as Comtan. Orion combined all three, and the FDA approved Stalevo in 2003 to smooth out 'wearing-off' between doses.", "timeline": [["1957", "Arvid Carlsson shows dopamine is a brain transmitter and levodopa reverses reserpine effects."], ["1960", "Oleh Hornykiewicz finds dopamine loss in Parkinson disease brains."], ["1967", "George Cotzias reports high-dose oral levodopa benefit."], ["1975", "Carbidopa-levodopa (Sinemet) is approved in the US."], ["1999", "Entacapone (Comtan) is approved in the US."], ["2003", "FDA approves carbidopa-levodopa-entacapone (Stalevo)."]], "cell": "Parkinson disease destroys dopamine neurons of the substantia nigra that project to the striatum. Dopamine itself cannot cross the blood-brain barrier, but its precursor levodopa can, carried by the large neutral amino acid transporter. Inside the brain, aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (DOPA decarboxylase) turns levodopa into dopamine in surviving nerve terminals and other cells, restoring stimulation of D1 and D2 receptors. Carbidopa blocks DOPA decarboxylase in the gut and blood but cannot enter the brain, so more levodopa reaches the brain and less dopamine forms in the body. Entacapone reversibly blocks catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT) in the periphery, which otherwise turns levodopa into 3-O-methyldopa. Blocking both routes keeps levodopa levels steadier and longer, which extends each dose's 'on' time.", "body": [["Basal ganglia", "Restored dopamine signaling in the striatum improves slowness, stiffness and tremor, and the longer levodopa exposure reduces end-of-dose wearing off."], ["Movement", "Too much dopamine stimulation in a sensitized striatum causes dyskinesias, extra writhing movements."], ["Gut", "Peripheral dopamine and entacapone cause nausea; entacapone can cause diarrhea and rarely colitis."], ["Blood pressure", "Dopamine effects on vessels and the sympathetic system can cause orthostatic hypotension."], ["Mind and behavior", "Dopamine in limbic circuits can cause hallucinations, psychosis, impulse control problems and sudden sleep episodes."], ["Urine", "Entacapone and its metabolites turn urine brownish orange, which is harmless."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth, levodopa is absorbed in the small intestine by an amino acid transporter, so high-protein meals compete with it and iron reduces its absorption. Carbidopa and entacapone protect it from breakdown in the gut wall and blood, raising the amount that reaches the brain. Levodopa peaks in about an hour. With both helpers its half-life is about one and a half hours, longer than with carbidopa alone. Levodopa is converted to dopamine and broken down to products cleared in urine. Entacapone is very short-lived, highly protein bound and leaves mainly in bile after glucuronidation.", "sidefx": "Nausea, low blood pressure and hallucinations come from dopamine acting outside the motor circuit. Dyskinesia reflects peaks of dopamine stimulation in a damaged striatum. Impulse control disorders and sleep attacks are dopamine effects on reward and arousal systems. Entacapone adds diarrhea and urine discoloration. Stopping suddenly can cause high fever, rigidity and confusion resembling neuroleptic malignant syndrome, because dopamine signaling crashes. There is no antidote; doses are adjusted and symptoms treated.", "fun": "Arvid Carlsson shared the 2000 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for discovering that dopamine is a brain transmitter, the insight behind levodopa therapy.", "src": ["Stalevo (carbidopa, levodopa and entacapone) FDA label", "Hornykiewicz O. Wien Klin Wochenschr 1960", "Cotzias GC et al. N Engl J Med 1967", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "levomilnacipran": {"origin": "Levomilnacipran is the active mirror-image form of milnacipran, an antidepressant created by the French company Pierre Fabre and first marketed in France in the late 1990s. Milnacipran is a 50:50 mix of two enantiomers, and research showed the 1S,2R form carries most of the activity, especially at the norepinephrine transporter. Forest Laboratories partnered with Pierre Fabre to develop this single enantiomer for depression in the US, and the FDA approved it as Fetzima in 2013 for major depressive disorder in adults. (Racemic milnacipran is sold in the US as Savella for fibromyalgia.)", "timeline": [["1990s", "Pierre Fabre introduces milnacipran as an antidepressant in France."], ["2009", "FDA approves racemic milnacipran (Savella) for fibromyalgia."], ["2013", "FDA approves levomilnacipran (Fetzima) for major depressive disorder."]], "cell": "Levomilnacipran binds and blocks two transporters on nerve terminals: the serotonin transporter (SERT) and the norepinephrine transporter (NET). It is about twice as potent at NET as at SERT in the lab, which is unusual among SNRIs, most of which favor serotonin. Blocking reuptake leaves more serotonin and norepinephrine in the synapse. Over weeks, the sustained increase leads to adaptive changes, including desensitization of inhibitory autoreceptors (5-HT1A and alpha-2) and changes in gene expression and neuroplasticity, which are thought to underlie the antidepressant effect. It has almost no affinity for muscarinic, histamine or alpha-adrenergic receptors, so it causes little sedation or dry mouth from receptor blockade.", "body": [["Brain", "More serotonin and norepinephrine in mood circuits relieves depression over several weeks."], ["Heart and vessels", "Higher norepinephrine raises heart rate and blood pressure and can cause palpitations."], ["Bladder", "Norepinephrine tightens the bladder neck through alpha-1 receptors, causing urinary hesitancy or retention."], ["Skin", "Sympathetic activation of sweat glands causes heavy sweating."], ["Gut", "Serotonin acting on gut receptors causes nausea and constipation or vomiting."], ["Sexual function", "Raised serotonin commonly causes erectile and ejaculation problems."]], "journey": "Levomilnacipran is taken by mouth as an extended-release capsule, which releases drug slowly and peaks in about six to eight hours. It is well absorbed and only lightly protein bound, about 22 percent. The liver handles part of it through CYP3A4 desethylation and conjugation, but a large share, about 58 percent, leaves unchanged in the urine. The half-life is about 12 hours. Kidney disease raises its levels, and strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise them too.", "sidefx": "Nausea, constipation, sweating, fast heart rate and urinary hesitancy all trace to more serotonin and especially norepinephrine at their targets. Raised blood pressure and heart rate are why these are checked. Like all antidepressants, it carries a boxed warning for suicidal thoughts in young people. Combined with other serotonergic drugs it can cause serotonin syndrome. Effects on platelet serotonin raise bleeding risk, and stopping suddenly can cause a discontinuation syndrome. Hyponatremia, angle-closure glaucoma and mania activation are rarer. There is no antidote; serotonin syndrome may be treated with cyproheptadine and supportive care.", "fun": "Among the SNRIs, levomilnacipran is the one that leans hardest toward norepinephrine reuptake rather than serotonin.", "src": ["Fetzima (levomilnacipran) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "nefazodone": {"origin": "Nefazodone was developed by Bristol-Myers Squibb as a chemical relative of trazodone, aiming for an antidepressant that eased anxiety and insomnia without the sexual side effects common with SSRIs. The FDA approved it as Serzone in 1994. Reports of serious liver injury followed, and in 2001 the FDA added a boxed warning about life-threatening liver failure. Bristol-Myers Squibb withdrew Serzone in Europe and Canada in 2003 and in the US in 2004. A generic version remains available in the US, generally used only when other antidepressants have not worked.", "timeline": [["1994", "FDA approves nefazodone (Serzone) for depression."], ["2001", "FDA adds a boxed warning for life-threatening liver failure."], ["2004", "Bristol-Myers Squibb withdraws the Serzone brand from the US market; generic remains."]], "cell": "Nefazodone is a phenylpiperazine that mainly blocks postsynaptic 5-HT2A receptors. Blocking 5-HT2A reduces anxiety, insomnia and agitation and is thought to limit sexual side effects. It also moderately inhibits the serotonin and norepinephrine transporters, raising synaptic levels of both. Its alpha-1 adrenergic blockade lowers vascular tone and can cause dizziness on standing. The liver converts it to active metabolites, hydroxynefazodone and m-chlorophenylpiperazine (mCPP), a serotonin receptor agonist. Nefazodone is also a strong inhibitor of the CYP3A4 enzyme, which drives many of its drug interactions.", "body": [["Brain", "5-HT2A blockade plus modest reuptake inhibition improves depression and anxiety, while preserving deep sleep."], ["Sexual function", "Sexual dysfunction is less common than with SSRIs because 5-HT2A is blocked."], ["Blood pressure", "Alpha-1 blockade relaxes blood vessels and can cause dizziness or fainting on standing."], ["Liver", "Rarely, it causes severe hepatic injury and failure, which can be fatal or need transplant."], ["Vision", "Some people see visual trails or afterimages."], ["Drug interactions", "Strong CYP3A4 inhibition raises levels of drugs such as some statins, benzodiazepines and pimozide."]], "journey": "Nefazodone is taken by mouth and absorbed quickly, but heavy first-pass metabolism leaves only about 20 percent available. It peaks in about an hour and is more than 99 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it extensively through CYP3A4 into hydroxynefazodone, a triazoledione and mCPP. Its kinetics are nonlinear, so levels rise more than expected as the dose goes up. The parent half-life is short, about two to four hours, which is why it is taken twice a day. Metabolites leave mainly in urine.", "sidefx": "Sleepiness and dizziness reflect 5-HT2A and alpha-1 blockade. The most serious problem is idiosyncratic liver failure, estimated at about one death or transplant per 250,000 to 300,000 patient-years in the US, which is why it carries a boxed warning and symptoms like jaundice or dark urine lead to evaluation. Like other antidepressants it carries a boxed warning for suicidality in young people. CYP3A4 inhibition causes dangerous interactions. Serotonin syndrome can occur with other serotonergic drugs. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Nefazodone's withdrawal as a brand while generics stayed on sale left it in an unusual spot: still legal to prescribe in the US, but rarely chosen.", "src": ["Nefazodone hydrochloride tablets FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "FDA safety communication on Serzone (2002 to 2004)"]}, "desipramine": {"origin": "Desipramine is what imipramine becomes in the body. In 1957 the Swiss psychiatrist Roland Kuhn reported that imipramine, a Geigy compound tested for psychosis, lifted depression. Scientists soon found that the liver removes a methyl group from imipramine to make desipramine, an active metabolite that strongly blocks norepinephrine reuptake. Desipramine was marketed as its own drug in the early 1960s, in the US as Norpramin. Around the same time Julius Axelrod and colleagues showed that tricyclic drugs block the reuptake of norepinephrine into nerve endings, work that contributed to his 1970 Nobel Prize and shaped the monoamine theory of depression.", "timeline": [["1957", "Roland Kuhn reports the antidepressant effect of imipramine."], ["1961", "Axelrod and colleagues show imipramine blocks norepinephrine uptake into nerve endings."], ["1960s", "Desipramine is introduced as a separate antidepressant."], ["1970", "Julius Axelrod shares the Nobel Prize for work on neurotransmitter reuptake."]], "cell": "Desipramine is a secondary amine tricyclic. Its strongest action is blocking the norepinephrine transporter (NET), which keeps norepinephrine in the synapse longer; it blocks the serotonin transporter much more weakly. Over weeks, sustained norepinephrine signaling leads to downregulation of beta-adrenergic receptors and other adaptive changes linked to mood improvement. Like other tricyclics, it also blocks muscarinic, histamine H1 and alpha-1 receptors, but less than tertiary amines such as amitriptyline, so it is less sedating and less anticholinergic. At high levels it blocks fast sodium channels in the heart, slowing conduction.", "body": [["Brain", "More norepinephrine in mood circuits relieves depression; it tends to be more activating than sedating."], ["Heart", "Norepinephrine raises heart rate, and sodium channel block slows conduction, widening the QRS complex in overdose and risking arrhythmias."], ["Autonomic system", "Milder anticholinergic effects still cause dry mouth, constipation, blurred vision and urinary retention."], ["Blood pressure", "Alpha-1 blockade can cause dizziness on standing."], ["Seizure threshold", "It can lower the seizure threshold, especially at high levels."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth, desipramine is well absorbed and peaks in about four to six hours. It is about 90 percent protein bound and spreads widely into tissues. The liver hydroxylates it mainly through CYP2D6, so people who are poor CYP2D6 metabolizers, or who take strong CYP2D6 inhibitors like fluoxetine or paroxetine, can have much higher levels. The half-life is about 12 to 30 hours, longer in poor metabolizers. Metabolites are excreted in urine.", "sidefx": "Fast heart rate, sweating and insomnia come from extra norepinephrine. Dry mouth and constipation are milder anticholinergic effects. The most dangerous problem is cardiac: sodium channel block slows conduction and can cause ventricular arrhythmias, and sudden deaths have been reported in children. In overdose this causes QRS widening, low blood pressure, seizures and coma; sodium bicarbonate is used to reverse the sodium channel block. It carries the antidepressant boxed warning for suicidality in young people.", "fun": "Desipramine is so tightly linked to the norepinephrine transporter that researchers use it in the lab to block NET and test whether an effect depends on norepinephrine reuptake.", "src": ["Norpramin (desipramine) FDA label", "Kuhn R. Am J Psychiatry 1958", "Hertting G, Axelrod J, Whitby LG. J Pharmacol Exp Ther 1961", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "clomipramine": {"origin": "Clomipramine is imipramine with one chlorine atom added. It was made by the Swiss company Geigy in the early 1960s and marketed in Europe as Anafranil from the mid 1960s. Clinicians, including the Spanish psychiatrist Juan Lopez-Ibor, noticed it helped people with obsessive-compulsive symptoms, something other antidepressants did poorly. Controlled trials later confirmed this. The FDA approved clomipramine in 1989 for obsessive-compulsive disorder, making it the first drug approved in the US for OCD.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Geigy develops clomipramine and markets it in Europe."], ["1960s", "Clinicians report benefit in obsessive-compulsive symptoms."], ["1989", "FDA approves clomipramine (Anafranil) for OCD, the first US drug for this disorder."]], "cell": "Clomipramine is a tertiary amine tricyclic and the strongest serotonin transporter (SERT) blocker of all the tricyclics. Blocking SERT raises serotonin in the synapse, and over weeks this leads to desensitized 5-HT1A autoreceptors and greater serotonin transmission in orbitofrontal and striatal circuits linked to obsessions and compulsions. In the liver it is demethylated to desmethylclomipramine, which mainly blocks norepinephrine reuptake, so the combination raises both transmitters. Clomipramine also blocks muscarinic, histamine H1 and alpha-1 receptors, and at high levels it blocks cardiac sodium channels.", "body": [["Brain", "Strong serotonin reuptake inhibition reduces obsessions and compulsions, usually after many weeks."], ["Seizure threshold", "It lowers the seizure threshold in a dose-related way, which is why the maximum dose is capped."], ["Autonomic system", "Anticholinergic effects cause dry mouth, constipation, blurred vision and urinary retention."], ["Sexual function", "High serotonin activity commonly causes ejaculation failure and impotence."], ["Heart and blood pressure", "Alpha-1 blockade causes orthostatic hypotension, and sodium channel block can cause conduction problems and arrhythmias."], ["Weight and energy", "Histamine blockade causes sleepiness and weight gain."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth with food to reduce stomach upset, clomipramine is well absorbed but undergoes first-pass metabolism. It peaks in about two to six hours and is about 97 percent protein bound. The liver converts it through CYP2D6, CYP1A2, CYP3A4 and CYP2C19 to active desmethylclomipramine. The parent half-life is about 32 hours and the metabolite about 69 hours, so steady levels take a couple of weeks. Metabolites leave mostly in urine, with some in feces.", "sidefx": "Dry mouth, constipation and urinary problems come from muscarinic blockade; sleepiness and weight gain from H1 blockade; dizziness on standing from alpha-1 blockade. Sexual dysfunction reflects strong serotonin activity. Seizure risk rises with dose. Serotonin syndrome can occur with other serotonergic drugs, and hyperthermia has been reported, especially with antipsychotics. In overdose, sodium channel block widens the QRS and can cause fatal arrhythmias; sodium bicarbonate is used. It carries the boxed warning for suicidality in young people.", "fun": "For years clomipramine was the only drug approved in the US for OCD, and it remains a benchmark against which newer OCD drugs are compared.", "src": ["Anafranil (clomipramine) FDA label", "Clomipramine Collaborative Study Group. Arch Gen Psychiatry 1991", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "protriptyline": {"origin": "Protriptyline was developed by Merck in the 1960s, during the first wave of tricyclic antidepressants that followed imipramine. It was marketed in the US as Vivactil. Unlike many tricyclics, which make people drowsy, protriptyline is energizing, so it found a place for depressed patients with low energy and sleepiness. Because it suppresses REM sleep and is alerting, it was also used at times for narcolepsy and sleep apnea before better options existed. The Vivactil brand has been discontinued, but generic protriptyline remains available.", "timeline": [["1957", "Imipramine opens the tricyclic antidepressant era."], ["1960s", "Merck introduces protriptyline (Vivactil) in the US."]], "cell": "Protriptyline is a secondary amine tricyclic. It blocks the norepinephrine transporter (NET) more strongly than the serotonin transporter (SERT), increasing synaptic norepinephrine. Sustained norepinephrine signaling over weeks causes adaptive changes, such as downregulation of beta-adrenergic receptors, that are associated with antidepressant effect. It also blocks muscarinic, histamine H1 and alpha-1 receptors. Its norepinephrine effect outweighs its sedating H1 blockade, giving an activating profile. It strongly suppresses REM sleep. At high concentrations it blocks cardiac fast sodium channels.", "body": [["Brain", "More norepinephrine improves mood and energy; it is activating rather than sedating."], ["Sleep", "Activation and REM suppression can cause insomnia and anxiety."], ["Heart", "Norepinephrine raises heart rate, and sodium channel block can cause QRS widening and arrhythmias, especially in overdose or older adults."], ["Autonomic system", "Anticholinergic effects cause dry mouth, constipation, blurred vision and urinary retention."], ["Skin", "Photosensitivity can make sunburn more likely."]], "journey": "Protriptyline is taken by mouth and absorbed slowly, peaking in about 6 to 12 hours. It is about 92 percent protein bound and spreads widely into tissues. The liver metabolizes it, and metabolites leave in urine. Its half-life is very long, about 54 to 92 hours, the longest of the tricyclics, so it takes a week or more to reach steady levels and lingers for days after stopping. Activation may appear before the antidepressant effect, which takes weeks.", "sidefx": "Insomnia, anxiety and fast heart rate come from extra norepinephrine. Dry mouth, constipation and urinary retention reflect muscarinic blockade. The main danger is cardiac: sodium channel block slows conduction, and in overdose causes QRS widening, ventricular arrhythmias, seizures and coma; sodium bicarbonate is used. The long half-life means toxicity can last a long time. It carries the antidepressant boxed warning for suicidality in young people and can trigger mania.", "fun": "Protriptyline's long half-life and REM-suppressing effect once made it one of the few drug options for cataplexy and sleep apnea symptoms.", "src": ["Protriptyline hydrochloride tablets FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "trimipramine": {"origin": "Trimipramine is a tricyclic antidepressant developed by the French company Rhone-Poulenc around 1960, close in structure to imipramine but with a branched side chain. It was marketed in Europe in the 1960s and later sold in the US as Surmontil. It stands out because it barely blocks monoamine reuptake, the very action once thought essential for tricyclics to work, yet it still relieves depression. That made it a puzzle for the monoamine theory. The Surmontil brand has been discontinued in the US, and generic capsules remain.", "timeline": [["c. 1960", "Rhone-Poulenc develops trimipramine in France."], ["1960s", "Trimipramine is marketed in Europe."]], "cell": "Unlike most tricyclics, trimipramine is a weak inhibitor of both the serotonin and norepinephrine transporters. Its effects come mainly from receptor blockade. It is a potent antagonist at histamine H1 receptors (strong sedation and appetite gain), 5-HT2A receptors (better sleep and less anxiety), alpha-1 adrenergic receptors (orthostatic hypotension) and muscarinic M1 receptors (anticholinergic effects), and a moderate antagonist at dopamine D2 receptors, a profile somewhat like a low-potency antipsychotic. How this mix produces an antidepressant effect is not fully understood. Like other tricyclics, it blocks cardiac fast sodium channels at high levels.", "body": [["Brain", "Antidepressant and anxiety-relieving effects arise mainly from receptor blockade rather than reuptake inhibition."], ["Sleep", "Strong H1 and 5-HT2A blockade causes heavy sedation and deeper sleep, which is helpful when insomnia accompanies depression."], ["Appetite", "Histamine blockade increases appetite and weight."], ["Autonomic system", "Muscarinic blockade causes dry mouth, constipation, blurred vision and urinary retention; older adults may become confused."], ["Blood pressure and heart", "Alpha-1 blockade causes dizziness on standing, and sodium channel block can cause conduction delays."]], "journey": "Trimipramine is taken by mouth and absorbed fairly quickly, peaking in about two hours, though first-pass metabolism reduces how much reaches the blood. It is about 95 percent protein bound. The liver breaks it down through CYP2D6, CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 into desmethyltrimipramine and hydroxylated products, which leave in urine. The half-life is about 23 hours (roughly 16 to 40). Sedation starts within hours, while the antidepressant effect builds over about 10 days to 4 weeks.", "sidefx": "Sedation, weight gain and dizziness come from H1 and alpha-1 blockade, and dry mouth, constipation and confusion from muscarinic blockade. In overdose, sodium channel block widens the QRS and can cause ventricular dysrhythmias, seizures and coma; sodium bicarbonate is the standard treatment. It carries the antidepressant boxed warning for suicidality in young people, and it can trigger mania in bipolar disorder. Serotonin syndrome is possible with other serotonergic drugs.", "fun": "Trimipramine works as an antidepressant despite barely blocking monoamine reuptake, which is often cited as a challenge to the simplest version of the monoamine theory of depression.", "src": ["Trimipramine maleate capsules FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "amoxapine": {"origin": "Amoxapine is the N-desmethyl metabolite of the antipsychotic loxapine, and both are dibenzoxazepines. Lederle Laboratories, part of American Cyanamid, developed it as an antidepressant and introduced it in the US around 1980 as Asendin. Researchers noted that some patients improved faster than with older tricyclics. Because one of its metabolites blocks dopamine receptors, it also carries antipsychotic-like effects and risks, including movement disorders. The Asendin brand has been discontinued; generic tablets remain.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Loxapine, the parent compound family, is developed."], ["c. 1980", "Lederle introduces amoxapine (Asendin) in the US as an antidepressant."]], "cell": "Amoxapine blocks the norepinephrine transporter more than the serotonin transporter, raising synaptic norepinephrine, and it blocks 5-HT2A receptors. In the liver it is hydroxylated to 7-hydroxyamoxapine, which blocks dopamine D2 receptors, and 8-hydroxyamoxapine, which adds reuptake inhibition. D2 blockade in the striatum and pituitary causes antipsychotic-like effects, including extrapyramidal symptoms and prolactin rise. It also blocks histamine H1 and muscarinic receptors. In overdose it is notable for causing seizures, with relatively less sodium channel cardiotoxicity than classic tricyclics.", "body": [["Brain", "More norepinephrine and 5-HT2A blockade relieve depression; some people respond within about a week."], ["Movement", "D2 blockade by its metabolite can cause stiffness, restlessness (akathisia), tardive dyskinesia and rarely neuroleptic malignant syndrome."], ["Pituitary", "Blocking dopamine raises prolactin, which can cause breast milk discharge and menstrual changes."], ["Autonomic system", "Anticholinergic effects cause dry mouth, constipation and blurred vision."], ["Seizure threshold", "It lowers the seizure threshold; seizures and status epilepticus are a hallmark of overdose."], ["Kidneys", "Overdose seizures can lead to muscle breakdown and acute kidney injury."]], "journey": "Amoxapine is taken by mouth, absorbed quickly and peaks in about 90 minutes. It is about 90 percent protein bound. The liver, mainly through CYP2D6, converts it to 7-hydroxy and 8-hydroxy metabolites, which are active. The parent half-life is about 8 hours, while 7-hydroxyamoxapine lasts about 30 hours, so the dopamine-blocking effect lingers. Metabolites are conjugated and leave in urine.", "sidefx": "Sedation and anticholinergic effects come from H1 and muscarinic blockade. Movement disorders, high prolactin and neuroleptic malignant syndrome come from dopamine D2 blockade by its metabolite, a risk not usually seen with antidepressants. Seizures are the main overdose danger, and they can be hard to stop; prolonged seizures can lead to rhabdomyolysis and kidney failure. It carries the antidepressant boxed warning for suicidality in young people. There is no antidote; seizures are treated with benzodiazepines and supportive care.", "fun": "Amoxapine is a metabolite of the antipsychotic loxapine, so a drug sold as an antidepressant carries a built-in piece of antipsychotic action.", "src": ["Amoxapine tablets FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "maprotiline": {"origin": "Maprotiline was developed by the Swiss company Ciba in the late 1960s. Chemists built a four-ring (tetracyclic) structure with an ethylene bridge across the middle ring, making a molecule that blocks norepinephrine reuptake very selectively. It was marketed in Europe in the early 1970s as Ludiomil and later in the US. Its tendency to cause seizures, especially at higher doses, limited its use. The Ludiomil brand was discontinued, and US marketing of the generic tablets ended in 2020.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Ciba develops maprotiline, a tetracyclic antidepressant."], ["1970s", "Maprotiline (Ludiomil) is marketed in Europe and later in the US."], ["2020", "US marketing of maprotiline tablets ends."]], "cell": "Maprotiline is a potent, selective blocker of the norepinephrine transporter (NET) with almost no effect on serotonin reuptake. Blocking NET raises norepinephrine in the synapse, and over weeks this leads to adaptive changes such as beta-adrenergic receptor downregulation associated with the antidepressant effect. It is also a strong histamine H1 antagonist, which explains its sedation, and a moderate alpha-1 and muscarinic antagonist. At high concentrations it blocks cardiac sodium channels like the tricyclics. It lowers the seizure threshold in a dose-related way.", "body": [["Brain", "Selective norepinephrine reuptake inhibition relieves depression, usually over two to three weeks."], ["Sleep", "Strong histamine H1 blockade causes drowsiness, especially early in treatment."], ["Seizure threshold", "It lowers the seizure threshold, so seizures can occur even in people with no seizure history, especially with fast dose increases."], ["Autonomic system", "Muscarinic blockade causes dry mouth, constipation and blurred vision."], ["Heart and blood pressure", "Alpha-1 blockade causes dizziness on standing, and sodium channel block can cause conduction delay and arrhythmia."], ["Skin", "Rash is more common than with many tricyclics."]], "journey": "Maprotiline is taken by mouth, absorbed slowly and completely, and peaks around 12 hours after a dose. It is about 88 percent protein bound and spreads widely into tissues. The liver demethylates it, mainly through CYP2D6, to active desmethylmaprotiline. The half-life is about 51 hours, so it takes a week or two to reach steady levels. Metabolites leave about 60 percent in urine and the rest in feces.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness comes from H1 blockade and dry mouth and constipation from muscarinic blockade. Seizure risk is the defining problem and rises with dose and with rapid dose increases. In overdose it behaves like a tricyclic, with QRS widening, arrhythmias and seizures; sodium bicarbonate is used for the cardiac effects. It carries the antidepressant boxed warning for suicidality in young people and can trigger mania.", "fun": "Although often grouped with the tricyclics, maprotiline has four rings, which is where the name 'tetracyclic antidepressant' comes from.", "src": ["Maprotiline hydrochloride tablets FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "isocarboxazid": {"origin": "The MAO inhibitor story began with tuberculosis. In the early 1950s doctors at Sea View Hospital in New York noticed that patients given iproniazid, a TB drug from Roche, became unusually cheerful. In 1957 Nathan Kline and colleagues reported iproniazid helped depression, and it was found to block monoamine oxidase. Iproniazid proved toxic to the liver, so Roche developed related hydrazine compounds. Isocarboxazid was one of them, approved in the US in 1959 as Marplan. It is one of the few older MAOIs still available, used mainly for depression that has not responded to other treatments.", "timeline": [["1952", "Iproniazid, a TB drug, is noticed to lift mood in patients."], ["1957", "Nathan Kline reports iproniazid as an antidepressant."], ["1959", "Roche's isocarboxazid (Marplan) is approved in the US."]], "cell": "Monoamine oxidase is an enzyme on the outer membrane of mitochondria that breaks down monoamines. MAO-A prefers serotonin and norepinephrine; MAO-B prefers phenylethylamine and (in the brain) dopamine; both act on tyramine. Isocarboxazid is a hydrazine that is converted to a reactive form and binds MAO-A and MAO-B irreversibly. With the enzyme disabled, more serotonin, norepinephrine and dopamine build up in nerve terminals and are released into synapses, and over weeks adaptive changes produce the antidepressant effect. Recovery requires making new enzyme, which takes about two weeks. MAO-A blockade in the gut and liver also lets dietary tyramine reach the blood, where it releases norepinephrine from nerve endings and can trigger a dangerous blood pressure surge.", "body": [["Brain", "Higher monoamine levels relieve depression, sometimes in people who did not respond to other antidepressants."], ["Blood pressure", "Orthostatic hypotension is common, partly because false transmitters build up in sympathetic nerves."], ["Gut and liver", "With intestinal MAO-A blocked, tyramine from aged cheese, cured meats and similar foods is absorbed intact and can cause a hypertensive crisis."], ["Liver", "Hepatotoxicity can occur, as with other hydrazine MAOIs."], ["Sleep and sexual function", "Insomnia and sexual dysfunction are common."]], "journey": "Isocarboxazid is taken by mouth, absorbed quickly and peaks in about one to two hours. The liver metabolizes it, and metabolites leave in urine. Its plasma half-life is short, only a few hours, but because enzyme inhibition is irreversible, the effect lasts until the body makes new MAO, about two weeks after stopping. That is why a 14-day washout is needed before starting other serotonergic antidepressants, and why it is stopped at least 10 days before elective surgery.", "sidefx": "Hypertensive crisis happens when tyramine or sympathomimetic drugs release norepinephrine that MAO can no longer clear, causing severe headache, very high blood pressure and risk of stroke. Serotonin syndrome occurs when serotonergic drugs are combined, because serotonin cannot be broken down. Orthostatic hypotension, dizziness and insomnia are common. It carries the antidepressant boxed warning for suicidality in young people. There is no antidote; a hypertensive crisis is treated with fast-acting blood pressure drugs such as phentolamine, and serotonin syndrome with cyproheptadine and supportive care.", "fun": "Widely reprinted news photos from the early 1950s showed tuberculosis patients on iproniazid dancing in the wards, an early clue that led to the MAO inhibitors.", "src": ["Marplan (isocarboxazid) FDA label", "Lopez-Munoz F, Alamo C. Curr Pharm Des 2009 (history of antidepressants)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "selegiline-transdermal": {"origin": "Selegiline, first called deprenyl, was made in 1962 by Zoltan Ecseri at the Chinoin company in Budapest, Hungary. The pharmacologist Jozsef Knoll showed that, unlike older MAO inhibitors, it selectively blocks MAO-B at low levels and does not cause the dangerous 'cheese reaction.' It became a Parkinson disease drug in pill form. Higher oral doses needed for depression lose that selectivity and block gut MAO-A. To get around this, Somerset Pharmaceuticals developed a skin patch that delivers selegiline straight into the blood, skipping the gut and liver. The FDA approved it as Emsam in 2006, the first transdermal antidepressant in the US.", "timeline": [["1962", "Zoltan Ecseri at Chinoin in Budapest synthesizes deprenyl (selegiline)."], ["1960s", "Jozsef Knoll describes its selective MAO-B inhibition."], ["1989", "Oral selegiline is approved in the US for Parkinson disease."], ["2006", "FDA approves the selegiline patch (Emsam) for major depressive disorder."]], "cell": "Selegiline is a propargylamine that binds the FAD cofactor of monoamine oxidase and inactivates it permanently. At low exposure it prefers MAO-B, which in the brain breaks down dopamine. At the levels reached with the antidepressant patch, it also blocks MAO-A in the brain, which breaks down serotonin and norepinephrine. With both enzymes disabled, serotonin, norepinephrine and dopamine accumulate in nerve terminals and synapses, leading over weeks to the antidepressant effect. Because the patch avoids first-pass absorption through the gut wall, intestinal MAO-A is relatively spared at the lowest patch strength, so dietary tyramine is still broken down. At higher strengths, gut MAO-A is inhibited enough that dietary tyramine limits apply. The body converts selegiline to N-desmethylselegiline, L-methamphetamine and L-amphetamine.", "body": [["Brain", "Inhibition of MAO-A and MAO-B raises serotonin, norepinephrine and dopamine, relieving depression over several weeks."], ["Skin", "The patch can cause redness, itching and irritation at the application site."], ["Gut", "At the lowest strength, gut MAO-A is largely spared, so tyramine sensitivity is lower than with oral MAOIs; this protection fades at higher strengths."], ["Blood pressure", "Orthostatic hypotension is common, and tyramine or sympathomimetic drugs can cause a hypertensive crisis."], ["Sleep", "Insomnia is common, partly from amphetamine metabolites and raised monoamines."]], "journey": "The patch is applied to dry, intact skin once a day. Selegiline crosses the skin and enters the bloodstream directly, avoiding the heavy first-pass metabolism that destroys most of an oral dose, so blood levels of the parent drug are much higher and metabolite levels lower. Steady state is reached in about five days. It is about 90 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it through CYP2B6, CYP2C9 and CYP3A4 to N-desmethylselegiline, L-amphetamine and L-methamphetamine, which leave in urine. Its half-life from the patch is about 18 to 25 hours, but MAO inhibition lasts about two weeks after removal while new enzyme is made.", "sidefx": "Application-site reactions are a skin response to the patch. Insomnia, headache and dry mouth reflect raised monoamines. Hypertensive crisis can occur if tyramine-rich food is eaten at higher patch strengths, or with sympathomimetic drugs, because norepinephrine released by tyramine cannot be cleared. Serotonin syndrome can occur with SSRIs, SNRIs, meperidine, tramadol and similar drugs, which is why washout periods are required before and after. It carries the antidepressant boxed warning for suicidality in young people. Hypertensive crisis is treated with fast-acting antihypertensives such as phentolamine, and serotonin syndrome with cyproheptadine and supportive care.", "fun": "Because selegiline is metabolized to L-methamphetamine and L-amphetamine, people using it can test positive for amphetamines on some drug screens.", "src": ["Emsam (selegiline transdermal system) FDA label", "Knoll J, Magyar K. Adv Biochem Psychopharmacol 1972", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "gepirone": {"origin": "Gepirone is an azapirone, a cousin of the anxiety drug buspirone. It was first developed by Bristol-Myers in the 1980s. The rights later passed through Organon and then to Fabre-Kramer Pharmaceuticals, a small Texas company that kept pursuing it. The FDA turned down the application several times over two decades because of mixed trial results. After further review, the FDA approved an extended-release form as Exxua in September 2023 for major depressive disorder in adults. It was notable as an antidepressant that acts mainly at one serotonin receptor rather than blocking reuptake.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Bristol-Myers develops gepirone, an analog of buspirone."], ["2000s", "The FDA declines to approve gepirone several times."], ["2023", "FDA approves extended-release gepirone (Exxua) for major depressive disorder."]], "cell": "Gepirone selectively activates 5-HT1A receptors, which are Gi-coupled. On serotonin neurons in the raphe nuclei, these act as somatodendritic autoreceptors; gepirone is a full agonist here, so at first it slows serotonin neuron firing. With continued use, these autoreceptors desensitize, and serotonin neuron firing and release recover. Meanwhile gepirone acts as a partial agonist at postsynaptic 5-HT1A receptors in the hippocampus and cortex, adding direct serotonin signaling in mood circuits. It has little effect on serotonin reuptake. A metabolite, 1-(2-pyrimidinyl)piperazine, blocks alpha-2 adrenergic receptors, and 3-hydroxygepirone also activates 5-HT1A.", "body": [["Brain", "Postsynaptic 5-HT1A activation and gradual autoreceptor desensitization relieve depression over weeks."], ["Sexual function", "Because it does not flood synapses with serotonin, sexual dysfunction and weight gain are uncommon."], ["Heart", "It can prolong the QTc interval in a concentration-related way, so ECGs are checked."], ["Dizziness and balance", "Dizziness is the most common side effect, likely from 5-HT1A effects in the brainstem."], ["Gut", "Nausea, stomach pain and indigestion occur."]], "journey": "Gepirone is taken by mouth as an extended-release tablet with food, which helps it absorb more steadily. It peaks about six hours after a dose and is about 72 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it extensively through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors are contraindicated because they raise levels and QT risk. Its metabolites, including 3-hydroxygepirone and 1-PP, are active. The half-life is about five hours, and the extended-release form allows once-daily dosing. Metabolites leave mainly in urine.", "sidefx": "Dizziness, nausea and insomnia relate to 5-HT1A activity in the brainstem and gut. QTc prolongation is the key safety concern, which is why ECGs and electrolytes are checked and strong CYP3A4 inhibitors are avoided. It carries the antidepressant boxed warning for suicidality in young people. Serotonin syndrome can occur with other serotonergic drugs, and it can trigger mania. There is no antidote; care is supportive with cardiac monitoring.", "fun": "Gepirone holds a record of sorts: it was turned down by the FDA multiple times before finally being approved about two decades after it was first submitted.", "src": ["Exxua (gepirone) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "iloperidone": {"origin": "Iloperidone was discovered at Hoechst Marion Roussel in the 1990s as a new second-generation antipsychotic. Rights passed to Titan Pharmaceuticals and then Novartis, and finally to Vanda Pharmaceuticals, which completed development. The FDA approved iloperidone as Fanapt in 2009 for schizophrenia in adults, and in 2024 for acute manic or mixed episodes of bipolar I disorder. Its very strong alpha-1 blocking activity requires a slow start, which shaped how it is used.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Hoechst Marion Roussel discovers iloperidone."], ["2009", "FDA approves iloperidone (Fanapt) for schizophrenia."], ["2024", "FDA approves iloperidone for bipolar I mania."]], "cell": "Iloperidone blocks dopamine D2 and D3 receptors and serotonin 5-HT2A receptors. D2 blockade in the mesolimbic pathway reduces positive psychotic symptoms such as hallucinations and delusions. Strong 5-HT2A blockade releases dopamine in the nigrostriatal pathway, which helps keep extrapyramidal side effects low. Its standout feature is very high affinity for alpha-1 adrenergic receptors, which relaxes blood vessels and explains orthostatic hypotension, tachycardia, nasal congestion and priapism. It has low affinity for muscarinic and histamine H1 receptors, so it causes little anticholinergic effect. It also slows cardiac potassium currents enough to prolong the QTc interval.", "body": [["Brain", "D2 and 5-HT2A blockade reduce psychosis and mania with a low rate of movement side effects."], ["Blood pressure", "Strong alpha-1 blockade drops blood pressure on standing and can cause fainting, which is why dosing starts low and rises slowly."], ["Heart", "QTc prolongation can occur, especially with CYP2D6 or CYP3A4 inhibitors."], ["Nose", "Alpha-1 blockade in nasal vessels causes stuffiness."], ["Reproductive system", "Alpha-1 blockade can cause priapism, a prolonged painful erection."], ["Metabolism", "Weight gain and changes in glucose and lipids can occur, as with other atypical antipsychotics."]], "journey": "Iloperidone is taken by mouth and absorbed well, peaking in two to four hours. It is about 95 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it by three routes: carbonyl reduction to the active metabolite P88, CYP2D6 hydroxylation leading to P95, and CYP3A4 O-demethylation. The half-life is about 18 hours in normal CYP2D6 metabolizers and about 33 hours in poor metabolizers, who therefore need lower doses. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. If more than three days are missed, it must be restarted slowly because tolerance to the blood pressure effect is lost.", "sidefx": "Dizziness, fainting, fast heart rate and nasal congestion all trace to alpha-1 blockade. Priapism comes from the same mechanism. QTc prolongation reflects potassium channel effects and rises with interacting drugs. Weight gain and metabolic changes are class effects. Like all antipsychotics, it carries a boxed warning for increased death in elderly people with dementia-related psychosis, and it can cause neuroleptic malignant syndrome and tardive dyskinesia. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Iloperidone passed through four companies, from Hoechst Marion Roussel to Titan to Novartis to Vanda, before reaching patients.", "src": ["Fanapt (iloperidone) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "olanzapine-samidorphan": {"origin": "Olanzapine was discovered at Eli Lilly and approved in 1996. It works well for schizophrenia and bipolar disorder but often causes large weight gain. Animal and human studies suggested that the brain's opioid system helps drive the appetite and reward changes behind that weight gain. Alkermes designed samidorphan, an opioid receptor blocker related to naltrexone, and combined it with olanzapine in one tablet. In trials the combination caused less weight gain than olanzapine alone. The FDA approved it as Lybalvi in 2021 for schizophrenia and bipolar I disorder in adults.", "timeline": [["1996", "Olanzapine (Zyprexa) is approved in the US."], ["2010s", "Alkermes develops samidorphan, an opioid antagonist."], ["2021", "FDA approves olanzapine-samidorphan (Lybalvi)."]], "cell": "Olanzapine blocks dopamine D2 receptors in the mesolimbic pathway, reducing psychosis and mania, and blocks 5-HT2A receptors, which lowers movement side effects. It also blocks histamine H1, muscarinic, alpha-1 and 5-HT2C receptors; H1 and 5-HT2C blockade are strongly tied to increased appetite and weight gain. Samidorphan is a mu-opioid receptor antagonist with partial activity at kappa and delta receptors. By blocking opioid signaling in reward and feeding circuits and possibly in metabolic tissues, it blunts some of olanzapine's effect on appetite and weight. It does not reduce olanzapine's antipsychotic action. Because it blocks mu receptors, it also blocks the effects of opioid drugs.", "body": [["Brain", "D2 and 5-HT2A blockade reduce psychosis and stabilize mood."], ["Weight and appetite", "Samidorphan reduces, but does not eliminate, the weight gain caused by olanzapine."], ["Metabolism", "Olanzapine can still raise blood sugar and lipids, so these are monitored."], ["Opioid system", "Samidorphan blocks opioid pain relief and can cause sudden withdrawal in people who use opioids."], ["Alertness and blood pressure", "H1 and alpha-1 blockade cause sleepiness and dizziness on standing."], ["Pituitary", "D2 blockade in the tuberoinfundibular pathway can raise prolactin, though usually modestly."]], "journey": "The tablet is taken by mouth once a day. Olanzapine peaks in about five hours, is about 93 percent protein bound and is metabolized by CYP1A2 and glucuronidation, with a half-life of about 30 hours; smoking speeds its clearance. Samidorphan peaks in one to two hours, is only lightly protein bound, is metabolized mainly by CYP3A4 and has a half-life of about 7 to 9 hours. Both leave in urine and feces as metabolites.", "sidefx": "Sleepiness, dry mouth and weight gain come from olanzapine's H1, muscarinic and 5-HT2C blockade. High blood sugar and lipids reflect olanzapine's metabolic effects. Samidorphan causes precipitated opioid withdrawal in people with opioids in their system, which is why opioids must be stopped 7 to 14 days before starting. Trying to overcome the blockade with large amounts of opioids can lead to fatal overdose. Like other antipsychotics, it carries the boxed warning for increased death in elderly patients with dementia-related psychosis, and NMS and DRESS can occur. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Samidorphan is structurally related to naltrexone but has a modified ring that makes it longer-lasting when taken by mouth.", "src": ["Lybalvi (olanzapine and samidorphan) FDA label", "Correll CU et al. Am J Psychiatry 2020 (ENLIGHTEN-2)"]}, "thiothixene": {"origin": "Thiothixene belongs to the thioxanthenes, a family made by swapping one nitrogen in the phenothiazine ring for a carbon. The Danish company Lundbeck made the first thioxanthene antipsychotic, chlorprothixene, in the late 1950s. Pfizer then developed thiothixene and introduced it in the US in the late 1960s as Navane. It became a widely used high-potency first-generation antipsychotic for schizophrenia. The Navane brand has been discontinued; generic capsules remain.", "timeline": [["1952", "Chlorpromazine begins the antipsychotic era."], ["1950s", "Lundbeck introduces chlorprothixene, the first thioxanthene."], ["1960s", "Pfizer introduces thiothixene (Navane) in the US."]], "cell": "Thiothixene is a high-potency antagonist at dopamine D2 receptors. In the mesolimbic pathway, blocking D2 reduces positive symptoms such as hallucinations and delusions. In the nigrostriatal pathway, the same blockade disturbs movement control, causing extrapyramidal symptoms. In the tuberoinfundibular pathway, it removes dopamine's brake on prolactin. It blocks D1, alpha-1, H1 and muscarinic receptors less strongly, so it causes less sedation, low blood pressure and anticholinergic effects than low-potency drugs like chlorpromazine.", "body": [["Brain", "D2 blockade in limbic circuits reduces psychotic symptoms over days to weeks."], ["Movement", "Nigrostriatal D2 blockade causes acute dystonia, akathisia, parkinsonism and, with long use, tardive dyskinesia."], ["Pituitary", "Loss of dopamine inhibition raises prolactin, which can cause breast enlargement, milk discharge and menstrual changes."], ["Autonomic system", "Mild anticholinergic and alpha-1 effects cause dry mouth, constipation and dizziness."], ["Temperature control", "Dopamine blockade in the hypothalamus impairs heat regulation."], ["Blood", "Rarely, white blood cell counts fall, including agranulocytosis, so infections with fever need prompt blood counts."]], "journey": "Thiothixene is taken by mouth and absorbed fairly quickly, peaking in one to three hours. It is highly protein bound and spreads widely into the brain and fat. The liver metabolizes it, mainly through CYP1A2, so smoking can lower its levels. The half-life is about 34 hours, which allows once-daily dosing. It leaves mainly in bile and feces, with some in urine.", "sidefx": "Extrapyramidal effects are the main burden, because high-potency D2 blockade strongly disrupts the nigrostriatal system; anticholinergic drugs can treat acute dystonia. Tardive dyskinesia reflects long-term changes in striatal dopamine receptors. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome, with fever, rigidity and confusion, is a rare emergency treated with supportive care, sometimes with dantrolene or bromocriptine. Prolactin rise comes from pituitary D2 blockade. It carries the class boxed warning for increased death in elderly people with dementia-related psychosis. Seizure threshold can fall.", "fun": "The name thiothixene reflects its thioxanthene core, a xanthene ring in which a sulfur atom takes the place of oxygen.", "src": ["Thiothixene capsules FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "trifluoperazine": {"origin": "Trifluoperazine is a phenothiazine, the same family as chlorpromazine. Chlorpromazine was made by Rhone-Poulenc in 1950, and in 1952 the French psychiatrists Jean Delay and Pierre Deniker showed it calmed psychosis, starting the antipsychotic era. Chemists then made stronger versions by adding a piperazine side chain and a trifluoromethyl group. Smith Kline & French introduced trifluoperazine in the US in the late 1950s as Stelazine. It became a standard high-potency antipsychotic. The brand is discontinued; generic tablets remain.", "timeline": [["1950", "Rhone-Poulenc synthesizes chlorpromazine."], ["1952", "Delay and Deniker report chlorpromazine for psychosis."], ["1950s", "Smith Kline & French introduces trifluoperazine (Stelazine)."]], "cell": "Trifluoperazine is a piperazine phenothiazine that blocks dopamine D2 receptors with high potency. In mesolimbic pathways this reduces hallucinations and delusions. In the chemoreceptor trigger zone of the brainstem, D2 blockade reduces nausea and vomiting. In the nigrostriatal pathway it disrupts motor control, causing extrapyramidal symptoms, and in the pituitary it removes dopamine's brake on prolactin. It has modest alpha-1, H1 and muscarinic blocking effects, so sedation and low blood pressure are less prominent than with low-potency phenothiazines.", "body": [["Brain", "D2 blockade reduces psychosis; it is also used short term for nonpsychotic anxiety."], ["Brainstem", "Blocking D2 in the chemoreceptor trigger zone reduces nausea and vomiting."], ["Movement", "High-potency D2 blockade often causes akathisia, parkinsonism and acute dystonia, and long use can cause tardive dyskinesia."], ["Liver", "Phenothiazines can cause cholestatic jaundice, an allergic-type reaction in the bile ducts."], ["Skin", "Phenothiazines increase sensitivity to sunlight."], ["Blood", "Rarely, agranulocytosis occurs, usually in the first months, leaving the body open to serious infection."]], "journey": "Trifluoperazine is taken by mouth and absorbed reasonably well, though first-pass metabolism reduces the amount reaching the blood. It peaks in about 1.5 to 6 hours and is more than 90 percent protein bound. It spreads widely, including into the brain. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP1A2, and metabolites leave in urine and feces. The half-life is about 10 to 20 hours.", "sidefx": "Extrapyramidal effects come from strong D2 blockade in the striatum; acute dystonia responds to anticholinergic drugs like benztropine or diphenhydramine. Tardive dyskinesia reflects long-term changes in dopamine receptors. Prolactin rise comes from pituitary D2 blockade. Cholestatic jaundice, photosensitivity and agranulocytosis are idiosyncratic phenothiazine reactions. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is a rare emergency. It carries the class boxed warning for increased death in elderly people with dementia-related psychosis. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Trifluoperazine is also a classic lab tool: it blocks calmodulin, the calcium-binding protein, and has been used in research for decades for that reason.", "src": ["Trifluoperazine hydrochloride tablets FDA label", "Lopez-Munoz F et al. Ann Clin Psychiatry 2005 (history of chlorpromazine)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "pimozide": {"origin": "Pimozide was created at Janssen Pharmaceutica in Belgium, the company founded by Paul Janssen, who also discovered haloperidol in 1958. In the 1960s Janssen's team made the diphenylbutylpiperidines, a group of long-acting dopamine blockers, and pimozide was one of them. In the US it was approved in 1984 as Orap, specifically for suppressing severe motor and vocal tics of Tourette syndrome that do not respond to standard treatment. It was approved as an orphan drug. The Orap brand is discontinued; generic tablets remain.", "timeline": [["1958", "Paul Janssen's company discovers haloperidol."], ["1960s", "Janssen develops pimozide, a diphenylbutylpiperidine."], ["1984", "FDA approves pimozide (Orap) for Tourette syndrome."]], "cell": "Pimozide is a potent and selective antagonist of central dopamine D2 receptors. Tics in Tourette syndrome are linked to overactive dopamine signaling in the cortico-striato-thalamic circuits, and blocking D2 in the striatum suppresses them. The same blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway can cause extrapyramidal symptoms, and in the pituitary it raises prolactin. Pimozide also blocks the hERG channel, which carries the rapid delayed rectifier potassium current (IKr) in heart cells. Blocking IKr slows repolarization and prolongs the QT interval, creating a risk of torsades de pointes.", "body": [["Brain", "D2 blockade in basal ganglia circuits suppresses motor and vocal tics."], ["Heart", "hERG potassium channel block lengthens the QT interval and can trigger torsades de pointes and sudden death."], ["Movement", "Extrapyramidal reactions such as akathisia and stiffness can occur, and long use can cause tardive dyskinesia."], ["Pituitary", "Blocking dopamine in the pituitary raises prolactin, which can cause breast and menstrual changes."], ["Alertness", "Sedation, dry mouth and constipation are common."]], "journey": "Pimozide is taken by mouth; more than half is absorbed, and levels peak in about six to eight hours. It is highly protein bound and lipophilic. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP3A4, with help from CYP1A2 and CYP2D6. That is why strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (such as macrolide antibiotics and azole antifungals) and strong CYP2D6 inhibitors raise its levels and its QT risk. The half-life is long, about 55 hours in adults, which allows once-daily dosing. Metabolites leave mainly in urine.", "sidefx": "QT prolongation and torsades de pointes come from hERG block, and they become much more likely when other drugs raise pimozide levels or also prolong QT, or when potassium or magnesium is low. ECGs are checked at baseline and during dose changes. Extrapyramidal effects and tardive dyskinesia reflect striatal D2 blockade. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is a rare emergency. There is no antidote; torsades is treated with intravenous magnesium and correction of electrolytes.", "fun": "Pimozide's many contraindicated drug combinations make it one of the most common examples used to teach CYP3A4 interactions and QT risk.", "src": ["Orap (pimozide) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "molindone": {"origin": "Molindone is a dihydroindolone, a chemical family different from phenothiazines and butyrophenones. It was developed by Endo Laboratories in the 1960s and introduced in the US in the 1970s as Moban for schizophrenia. It drew interest because, unlike most older antipsychotics, it tended not to cause weight gain. The Moban brand was discontinued, and generic tablets are available.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Endo Laboratories develops molindone."], ["1970s", "Molindone (Moban) is introduced in the US for schizophrenia."]], "cell": "Molindone blocks dopamine D2 receptors. Blockade in the mesolimbic pathway reduces positive psychotic symptoms. In the nigrostriatal pathway it disrupts motor control, causing extrapyramidal effects, and in the pituitary it raises prolactin. It has only weak anticholinergic and alpha-adrenergic blocking activity, so dry mouth and low blood pressure are less prominent than with low-potency phenothiazines. Its low effect on histamine and 5-HT2C receptors may explain why it causes little weight gain.", "body": [["Brain", "D2 blockade reduces hallucinations and delusions over weeks."], ["Movement", "Akathisia, parkinsonism and acute dystonia are common, and tardive dyskinesia can develop with long use."], ["Weight", "It causes relatively little weight gain compared with most antipsychotics."], ["Pituitary", "Prolactin can rise, causing breast and menstrual changes."], ["Alertness", "Drowsiness occurs, especially early in treatment."]], "journey": "Molindone is taken by mouth and absorbed quickly, peaking in about 1.5 hours. The liver metabolizes it extensively into many metabolites, some active. The parent half-life is short, about 1.5 hours, but its effects last about 24 to 36 hours, probably due to active metabolites. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. The tablets contain calcium sulfate, which can bind phenytoin and tetracyclines in the gut and lower their absorption.", "sidefx": "Extrapyramidal symptoms, including restlessness and stiffness, come from striatal D2 blockade. Tardive dyskinesia is a long-term risk. Prolactin rise is a pituitary effect. Drowsiness and a lowered seizure threshold also occur. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome and agranulocytosis are rare but serious. It carries the class boxed warning for increased death in elderly people with dementia-related psychosis. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Molindone was one of the few older antipsychotics linked to weight loss in some patients, rather than weight gain.", "src": ["Molindone hydrochloride tablets FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "clorazepate": {"origin": "Clorazepate followed the benzodiazepine boom set off by Leo Sternbach's work at Roche in the late 1950s. It was designed as a prodrug: a compound that is inactive itself but turns into the active benzodiazepine desmethyldiazepam (nordiazepam) in the stomach. It was introduced in the early 1970s, and in the US Abbott Laboratories marketed it as Tranxene. It is used for anxiety, alcohol withdrawal and as an add-on for partial seizures.", "timeline": [["1955", "Leo Sternbach makes the first benzodiazepine at Roche."], ["1960s", "Clorazepate is developed as a benzodiazepine prodrug."], ["1970s", "Clorazepate (Tranxene) is marketed in the US."]], "cell": "In the acid of the stomach, clorazepate loses a carboxyl group and becomes nordiazepam. Nordiazepam binds the benzodiazepine site between the alpha and gamma-2 subunits of the GABA-A receptor. This positive allosteric binding makes GABA work better, so the chloride channel opens more often. Chloride flows in, the neuron hyperpolarizes and becomes harder to fire. Actions at alpha-2 and alpha-3 receptors reduce anxiety and relax muscles, alpha-1 actions cause sedation and help stop seizures, and alpha-5 actions affect memory.", "body": [["Brain", "Enhanced GABA inhibition reduces anxiety and helps control seizures and alcohol withdrawal."], ["Alertness and coordination", "Drowsiness, dizziness and ataxia reflect broad CNS inhibition."], ["Muscles", "Spinal GABA effects relax skeletal muscle."], ["Breathing", "Combined with opioids or alcohol, it can dangerously depress breathing."], ["Dependence", "Long-term use adapts the receptors, so stopping suddenly can cause withdrawal and seizures."]], "journey": "Clorazepate is taken by mouth. In the acidic stomach it is quickly converted to nordiazepam, which is absorbed and peaks in about an hour. Antacids that reduce stomach acid can slow this conversion. Nordiazepam is about 97 to 98 percent protein bound and has a very long half-life, roughly 40 to 50 hours or more, so levels build up over days. The liver oxidizes it to oxazepam, which is then glucuronidated and excreted in urine.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness, dizziness and ataxia come from enhanced GABA signaling. The boxed warning covers profound sedation and respiratory depression with opioids, abuse and misuse, and physical dependence. Abrupt stopping after regular use can cause severe withdrawal, including seizures, so it is tapered. The long-lived metabolite makes accumulation and next-day impairment likely, especially in older adults. Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepine effects but can provoke seizures in dependent patients.", "fun": "Clorazepate's active form, nordiazepam, is the same long-lived metabolite that diazepam and chlordiazepoxide produce in the body.", "src": ["Tranxene (clorazepate dipotassium) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "meprobamate": {"origin": "Meprobamate was the first blockbuster 'tranquilizer.' In the 1940s Frank Berger, a Czech-born pharmacologist working in England, found that the muscle relaxant mephenesin calmed animals but wore off too quickly. After moving to the US, Berger and chemist Bernard Ludwig at Carter Products made a longer-acting relative, meprobamate, in 1950. It was launched in 1955 as Miltown (named after Milltown, New Jersey) by Carter's Wallace Laboratories, and as Equanil by Wyeth. It became a cultural sensation in the late 1950s, until the benzodiazepines replaced it in the 1960s. It is now Schedule IV and rarely used.", "timeline": [["1940s", "Frank Berger studies mephenesin's calming effect in animals."], ["1950", "Berger and Bernard Ludwig synthesize meprobamate at Carter Products."], ["1955", "Meprobamate is launched as Miltown and Equanil."], ["1960s", "Benzodiazepines largely replace meprobamate."]], "cell": "Meprobamate is a propanediol carbamate that acts at the GABA-A receptor in a barbiturate-like way. At usual levels it potentiates GABA's effect, increasing chloride flow through the channel. At high concentrations it can open the channel directly, even without GABA, which makes overdose more dangerous than with benzodiazepines. The resulting inhibition depresses activity in the thalamus and limbic system, reducing anxiety, and dampens spinal polysynaptic reflexes, producing muscle relaxation. The muscle relaxant carisoprodol is metabolized to meprobamate.", "body": [["Brain", "Depressed thalamic and limbic activity reduces anxiety and produces sedation."], ["Spinal cord", "Dampened polysynaptic reflexes relax muscles."], ["Coordination", "Drowsiness, dizziness and ataxia impair driving."], ["Breathing", "In overdose, direct GABA-A activation can cause coma and respiratory depression."], ["Dependence", "Regular use causes physical dependence, and abrupt stopping can cause withdrawal with seizures."]], "journey": "Meprobamate is taken by mouth, absorbed well and peaks in one to three hours. It is only about 20 percent protein bound and spreads evenly through body water. The liver metabolizes most of it, and about 10 to 20 percent leaves unchanged in urine. The half-life is about 6 to 17 hours and gets longer with chronic use or overdose. Large overdoses can form pill masses (bezoars) in the stomach that keep releasing drug.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness and ataxia come from GABA-A enhancement. Because it can open the chloride channel directly at high levels, overdose can cause deep coma, low blood pressure and respiratory failure, more like barbiturates than benzodiazepines. Physical dependence develops with regular use, and abrupt stopping can cause withdrawal with anxiety, tremor and seizures, so it is tapered. Allergic rashes and rare blood disorders occur. Flumazenil does not reverse it; care is supportive.", "fun": "Miltown was so popular in the 1950s that the comedian Milton Berle reportedly joked about calling himself 'Miltown Berle.'", "src": ["Meprobamate tablets FDA label", "Tone A. The Age of Anxiety, 2009", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "serdexmethylphenidate": {"origin": "Methylphenidate was first made in 1944 by Leandro Panizzon, a chemist at Ciba in Switzerland. His wife Marguerite, nicknamed Rita, tried it to boost her tennis game, and the brand name Ritalin came from her nickname. Ritalin reached the US in the 1950s. Later research showed that the d-threo enantiomer (dexmethylphenidate, Focalin, approved 2001) carries most of the activity. KemPharm then designed serdexmethylphenidate, a prodrug in which dexmethylphenidate is attached to the amino acid serine, so that it is released slowly in the lower gut. Combined with some immediate dexmethylphenidate, the capsule was approved by the FDA in 2021 as Azstarys for ADHD in people 6 years and older.", "timeline": [["1944", "Leandro Panizzon synthesizes methylphenidate at Ciba."], ["1955", "Methylphenidate (Ritalin) is approved in the US."], ["2001", "Dexmethylphenidate (Focalin) is approved."], ["2021", "FDA approves serdexmethylphenidate with dexmethylphenidate (Azstarys) for ADHD."]], "cell": "Serdexmethylphenidate has no stimulant activity until it is converted to dexmethylphenidate, mostly in the lower gastrointestinal tract. Dexmethylphenidate binds the dopamine transporter (DAT) and norepinephrine transporter (NET) on presynaptic terminals and blocks reuptake, so dopamine and norepinephrine stay longer in the synapse. In the prefrontal cortex, more norepinephrine acting on alpha-2A receptors and more dopamine acting on D1 receptors strengthen signal-to-noise in working memory and attention networks. In the striatum, more dopamine improves motivation and impulse control. Unlike amphetamines, it does not release monoamines from vesicles. The immediate-release part provides an early effect, and the prodrug extends it through the day.", "body": [["Brain", "More dopamine and norepinephrine in the prefrontal cortex and striatum improve attention, focus and impulse control."], ["Heart and vessels", "Norepinephrine raises heart rate and blood pressure; rare serious cardiac events occur, especially with structural heart disease."], ["Appetite and growth", "Catecholamines suppress appetite, and children may grow more slowly in height and weight."], ["Sleep", "Increased catecholamine arousal late in the day can cause trouble falling asleep."], ["Peripheral vessels", "Vasoconstriction can cause Raynaud phenomenon in fingers and toes."], ["Mood and behavior", "Excess dopamine can trigger tics, anxiety, mania or psychosis in susceptible people."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth in the morning, the immediate dexmethylphenidate is absorbed quickly, with onset in about 30 minutes. Serdexmethylphenidate itself is poorly absorbed and is gradually converted to dexmethylphenidate in the lower GI tract, which extends levels for up to about 13 hours. Dexmethylphenidate peaks around two hours. It is then broken down by carboxylesterase 1 (CES1) in the liver to inactive ritalinic acid, which leaves in urine. The effective half-life is prolonged by the slow conversion, roughly 11 hours.", "sidefx": "Loss of appetite, insomnia, headache and irritability come from extra catecholamine signaling. Higher heart rate and blood pressure reflect norepinephrine on the heart and vessels, and stimulants carry warnings for sudden death in structural heart disease, stroke and heart attack. Psychosis, mania and tics reflect excess dopamine. The boxed warning covers abuse, misuse and addiction, because blocked dopamine reuptake in reward circuits can be reinforcing. Growth slowing, priapism and Raynaud phenomenon are also possible. There is no antidote; overdose care is supportive, with benzodiazepines for agitation.", "fun": "Serdexmethylphenidate is the first FDA-approved prodrug of dexmethylphenidate; it stays largely inactive until the body releases the active drug in the lower gut.", "src": ["Azstarys (serdexmethylphenidate and dexmethylphenidate) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "lofexidine": {"origin": "Lofexidine is a close relative of clonidine. It was first studied as a blood pressure drug but found its main use in easing opioid withdrawal. In the United Kingdom it has been used for opioid detoxification since the early 1990s under the name Britlofex. US WorldMeds developed it for the US, and the FDA approved it as Lucemyra in 2018 to lessen withdrawal symptoms during abrupt opioid discontinuation in adults. It was the first non-opioid medicine approved in the US specifically for opioid withdrawal symptoms.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Lofexidine is developed as a clonidine-like alpha-2 agonist."], ["1990s", "Lofexidine is used in the UK for opioid withdrawal."], ["2018", "FDA approves lofexidine (Lucemyra) for opioid withdrawal symptoms."]], "cell": "Opioids normally inhibit neurons of the locus coeruleus, the brain's main norepinephrine center, by acting on mu receptors that lower cAMP. With chronic opioid use, these neurons adapt by increasing cAMP signaling. When opioids are stopped, the brake is suddenly gone and the neurons fire wildly, releasing large amounts of norepinephrine. This drives sweating, fast heart rate, goosebumps, anxiety, cramps and runny nose. Lofexidine activates alpha-2A adrenergic receptors, which are Gi-coupled, on these same neurons. That lowers cAMP and reduces firing and norepinephrine release, calming the sympathetic storm of withdrawal. It does not act on opioid receptors and does not treat craving.", "body": [["Brain", "Calming the locus coeruleus reduces anxiety, restlessness and insomnia of withdrawal."], ["Autonomic system", "Less norepinephrine output reduces sweating, chills, goosebumps, runny nose and stomach cramps."], ["Heart", "Reduced sympathetic tone slows the heart, and it can prolong the QT interval."], ["Blood pressure", "Blood pressure falls and dizziness or fainting on standing can occur; it is less pronounced than with clonidine."], ["Alertness", "Sedation and dry mouth are common, because alpha-2 activation dampens arousal and salivary secretion."]], "journey": "Lofexidine is taken by mouth, absorbed well and starts working within one to two hours, with peak levels in about three to five hours. It is about 55 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP2D6, with help from CYP1A2 and CYP2C19, so CYP2D6 inhibitors such as paroxetine raise its levels. Metabolites and some unchanged drug leave in urine. The half-life is about 11 to 13 hours, and it is dosed four times a day during the withdrawal period, usually up to about two weeks.", "sidefx": "Low blood pressure, slow heart rate and fainting come directly from reduced sympathetic output. QT prolongation means caution with other QT drugs. Sedation adds to other CNS depressants. Stopping suddenly can cause a rebound rise in blood pressure, so it is tapered over two to four days. An important indirect risk: after detox, a person's opioid tolerance falls, so returning to a previous level of opioid use can cause fatal overdose. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Lofexidine was the first medicine in the US approved for opioid withdrawal symptoms that is not itself an opioid.", "src": ["Lucemyra (lofexidine) FDA label", "Gowing L et al. Cochrane Database Syst Rev (alpha-2 agonists for opioid withdrawal)"]}, "naltrexone-extended-release-injection": {"origin": "Naltrexone was first made in the 1960s at Endo Laboratories while chemists were searching for an opioid blocker that lasted longer and worked better by mouth than naloxone. Oral naltrexone was approved in the US in 1984 for opioid dependence and in 1994 for alcohol dependence. Daily pills are easy to skip, so Alkermes packed naltrexone into biodegradable polymer microspheres (polylactide-co-glycolide) that release the drug for about a month after one injection. The FDA approved it as Vivitrol in 2006 for alcohol dependence and in 2010 for preventing relapse to opioid dependence after detoxification.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Naltrexone is synthesized at Endo Laboratories."], ["1984", "Oral naltrexone is approved in the US for opioid dependence."], ["1994", "Oral naltrexone is approved for alcohol dependence."], ["2006", "FDA approves extended-release injectable naltrexone (Vivitrol) for alcohol dependence."], ["2010", "Vivitrol is approved for preventing relapse to opioid dependence."]], "cell": "Naltrexone is a competitive antagonist at mu-opioid receptors, and to a lesser degree at kappa and delta receptors. It binds the receptor tightly but does not activate it. If an opioid is taken, naltrexone occupies the receptor so the opioid cannot produce euphoria, analgesia or respiratory depression unless very large amounts are used. For alcohol, drinking releases endogenous opioids (such as beta-endorphin) that stimulate mu receptors on GABA interneurons in the ventral tegmental area, which disinhibits dopamine release into the nucleus accumbens. Blocking that step reduces alcohol's reward, craving and heavy drinking. The microspheres slowly dissolve and keep receptor occupancy high for about a month.", "body": [["Brain reward system", "Blocking mu receptors reduces the reward from alcohol and blocks opioid euphoria, helping prevent relapse."], ["Opioid response", "Opioid pain medicines are much less effective during treatment, which complicates emergency pain control."], ["Injection site", "The gluteal depot can cause pain, swelling, lumps, abscess and rarely tissue necrosis."], ["Liver", "Naltrexone can raise liver enzymes, and hepatitis has been reported."], ["Lungs", "Rarely, eosinophilic pneumonia occurs, causing cough, shortness of breath and low oxygen."], ["Mood", "Depression and suicidal thoughts have been reported in treated patients."]], "journey": "Vivitrol is given as a deep intramuscular injection into the gluteal muscle by a health care provider. There is a first peak about two hours after injection as surface drug is released, and a second peak two to three days later as the microspheres begin to break down. Levels then slowly decline over about four weeks. It is about 21 percent protein bound. Unlike most drugs, naltrexone is not broken down by CYP enzymes; dihydrodiol dehydrogenase in the liver converts it to 6-beta-naltrexol, a weaker antagonist. Both leave mainly in urine. Because the injection bypasses the gut, there is no first-pass effect.", "sidefx": "If opioids are still in the body, naltrexone displaces them from receptors and causes sudden, intense withdrawal, so people must be opioid free first. Injection-site reactions come from the polymer depot and needle placement. Nausea, headache and fatigue are common. The most serious risk is overdose: after treatment ends or if someone tries to overcome the blockade with large opioid doses, opioid tolerance is low and overdose can be fatal. Naloxone can still be used for overdose but may need higher amounts. Hepatotoxicity and eosinophilic pneumonia are rare.", "fun": "The same polymer used in Vivitrol's microspheres, polylactide-co-glycolide, is also used in dissolvable surgical stitches.", "src": ["Vivitrol (naltrexone for extended-release injectable suspension) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "buprenorphine-extended-release-injection": {"origin": "Buprenorphine was made in 1966 by chemists at Reckitt & Colman in England, led by Kenneth Bentley and John Lewis, who were looking for a strong painkiller derived from thebaine, a poppy alkaloid, with less risk of addiction. It reached the US first as an injectable pain medicine and then, in 2002, as tablets for opioid use disorder. Daily doses are hard to keep up and can be diverted, so Indivior, Reckitt's spinoff, developed a monthly subcutaneous depot. The FDA approved it as Sublocade in 2017. A second long-acting product, Brixadi, using a different liquid-crystal depot from Braeburn and Camurus with weekly and monthly options, was approved in 2023.", "timeline": [["1966", "Buprenorphine is synthesized at Reckitt & Colman from thebaine."], ["1981", "Injectable buprenorphine (Buprenex) is approved in the US for pain."], ["2002", "Sublingual buprenorphine is approved for opioid dependence."], ["2017", "FDA approves extended-release buprenorphine injection (Sublocade)."], ["2023", "FDA approves Brixadi, a weekly or monthly buprenorphine injection."]], "cell": "Buprenorphine is a partial agonist at mu-opioid receptors with very high affinity and slow dissociation. It binds tightly and activates the receptor only partway, enough to relieve withdrawal and craving but with a ceiling on respiratory depression when used alone. Its tight binding also crowds out other opioids, so heroin or fentanyl produce less effect. It is an antagonist at kappa receptors, which may reduce the negative mood linked to withdrawal. In Sublocade, buprenorphine is dissolved in a biodegradable polymer (Atrigel system); after injection under the skin, the liquid meets body fluid and solidifies into a depot that releases drug over a month.", "body": [["Brain", "Steady mu receptor occupancy relieves withdrawal and craving and blocks the effects of other opioids."], ["Breathing", "Respiratory depression has a ceiling alone, but becomes dangerous with benzodiazepines, alcohol or other depressants."], ["Gut", "Mu activation in the gut slows movement, causing constipation and nausea."], ["Injection site", "The depot forms a lump under the skin and can cause pain, itching or redness."], ["Hormones", "Long-term opioid exposure can cause adrenal insufficiency and lower sex hormones."], ["Pregnancy", "Babies exposed in the womb can have neonatal opioid withdrawal syndrome."]], "journey": "Sublocade is injected under the skin of the abdomen by a health care provider. The liquid forms a solid depot, and buprenorphine levels peak about 24 hours later, then settle into steady release over the month. Because the depot bypasses the gut, there is no first-pass loss. Buprenorphine is about 96 percent protein bound and fat soluble. The liver metabolizes it through CYP3A4 to norbuprenorphine, then glucuronidation, and most leaves in feces. The apparent half-life from the depot is about 43 to 60 days, so levels stay up for months after the last injection.", "sidefx": "Constipation, nausea and headache come from mu receptor activity. The boxed warning describes serious harm or death if the depot is injected into a vein, because it can solidify and travel as emboli to the lungs or block vessels; that is why it is only given by a provider under a REMS program. Respiratory depression is the main danger with benzodiazepines or alcohol. Starting while full agonists are on board can cause precipitated withdrawal, since a partial agonist displaces them. Naloxone can reverse overdose, though high doses may be needed because buprenorphine binds so tightly.", "fun": "The Sublocade depot can be felt as a small bump under the skin, and in early weeks a provider can surgically remove it if needed.", "src": ["Sublocade (buprenorphine extended-release injection) FDA label", "Brixadi FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "aripiprazole-long-acting": {"origin": "Aripiprazole was discovered by Yasuo Oshiro and colleagues at Otsuka Pharmaceutical in Japan in the late 1980s. It was the first approved dopamine partial agonist antipsychotic, earning FDA approval in 2002. Because many people with schizophrenia stop taking daily pills, Otsuka and Lundbeck developed a long-acting injectable made of slowly dissolving aripiprazole monohydrate crystals. The FDA approved it as Abilify Maintena in 2013 for monthly injection, and later approved Abilify Asimtufii in 2023 for injection every two months. (Aristada, aripiprazole lauroxil, is a different prodrug product.)", "timeline": [["1988", "Otsuka scientists discover aripiprazole."], ["2002", "FDA approves oral aripiprazole (Abilify)."], ["2013", "FDA approves Abilify Maintena, a monthly long-acting injection."], ["2023", "FDA approves Abilify Asimtufii, given every two months."]], "cell": "Aripiprazole is a partial agonist at dopamine D2 receptors. It activates them about a quarter to a third as strongly as dopamine does. Where dopamine is too high, as in the mesolimbic pathway in psychosis, it acts like a blocker and reduces signaling; where dopamine is low, as in parts of the prefrontal cortex, it adds some stimulation. This 'dopamine stabilizer' effect relieves psychosis with fewer movement side effects and little prolactin rise. It is also a partial agonist at 5-HT1A and an antagonist at 5-HT2A receptors. In the long-acting injection, monohydrate crystals sit in the muscle and dissolve slowly, keeping steady blood levels for weeks.", "body": [["Brain", "D2 partial agonism reduces psychosis and stabilizes mood in bipolar I disorder."], ["Movement", "Akathisia, an inner restlessness, is common; parkinsonism and tardive dyskinesia are less frequent but possible."], ["Pituitary", "Partial agonism keeps prolactin low, unlike full D2 blockers."], ["Metabolism", "Weight gain and changes in glucose and lipids can occur, though often less than with some other atypicals."], ["Reward and impulse", "Dopamine agonist activity can cause compulsive gambling, shopping or eating."], ["Injection site", "Pain at the gluteal or deltoid injection site is common."]], "journey": "Abilify Maintena is injected into the deltoid or gluteal muscle. The crystals dissolve slowly, and blood levels peak about five to seven days after injection. Because the depot takes time to work, oral aripiprazole is continued for two weeks or a two-injection start is used. Aripiprazole is more than 99 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it through CYP2D6 and CYP3A4 to active dehydro-aripiprazole, so strong inhibitors or inducers change the dosing. The apparent half-life from the depot is about 30 to 47 days, and it leaves mostly in feces.", "sidefx": "Akathisia is thought to reflect D2 partial agonism in motor circuits. Impulse-control problems come from its agonist action in reward pathways. Metabolic changes and weight gain are class effects. Orthostatic hypotension can cause falls. As with all antipsychotics, there is a boxed warning for increased death in elderly people with dementia-related psychosis, and NMS and tardive dyskinesia are possible. Because the depot cannot be removed, tolerability is checked with oral aripiprazole first. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Aripiprazole was the first antipsychotic to work as a dopamine partial agonist rather than a pure blocker, a new idea at the time.", "src": ["Abilify Maintena (aripiprazole) FDA label", "Abilify Asimtufii FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "estazolam": {"origin": "Estazolam is a triazolobenzodiazepine, related to triazolam and alprazolam, with a triazole ring fused onto the classic benzodiazepine structure. It was first developed in Japan in the 1970s and was used there before reaching the US. Abbott Laboratories brought it to the US as ProSom, approved by the FDA around 1990 for short-term treatment of insomnia. Its intermediate half-life sits between very short-acting drugs like triazolam and long-acting ones like flurazepam. The ProSom brand has been discontinued; generic tablets remain.", "timeline": [["1955", "Leo Sternbach makes the first benzodiazepine at Roche."], ["1970s", "Estazolam is developed in Japan."], ["1990", "Estazolam (ProSom) is approved in the US for insomnia."]], "cell": "Estazolam binds the benzodiazepine site at the alpha and gamma-2 subunit interface of the GABA-A chloride channel. This positive allosteric modulation makes GABA more effective, increasing the frequency of channel opening. Chloride enters, the neuron hyperpolarizes and is less likely to fire. Unlike barbiturates, it does not lengthen opening time and cannot open the channel without GABA, which gives it a wider safety margin when taken alone. It acts on receptors containing alpha-1 (sedation), alpha-2 and alpha-3 (anxiety relief, muscle relaxation) and alpha-5 (memory) subunits.", "body": [["Brain", "Enhanced GABA inhibition shortens the time to fall asleep and reduces night waking."], ["Memory", "Anterograde amnesia can occur, especially if a person is awakened during the night."], ["Next day", "Residual drowsiness and impaired driving can occur the next morning."], ["Muscles and balance", "Poor coordination raises fall and hip fracture risk in older adults."], ["Breathing", "Combined with opioids or alcohol, it can depress breathing severely."]], "journey": "Estazolam is taken by mouth at bedtime, absorbed well and peaks in about two hours (range 0.5 to 6). It is about 93 percent protein bound. The liver oxidizes it mainly through CYP3A4 to largely inactive metabolites, 4-hydroxy and 1-oxo estazolam, which leave in urine. The half-life is about 10 to 24 hours, so effects last through the night with some risk of next-day carryover. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ketoconazole raise its levels.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness, dizziness and poor coordination come from enhanced GABA inhibition. Complex sleep behaviors, such as sleep-driving, happen when parts of the brain are active while judgment and memory are suppressed. The boxed warning covers profound sedation and breathing suppression with opioids, abuse and misuse, and dependence; sudden stopping after regular use can cause withdrawal and seizures, so it is tapered. Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepine effects but can trigger seizures in dependent people.", "fun": "Estazolam's triazole ring is the same structural feature that defines alprazolam (Xanax) and triazolam (Halcion).", "src": ["Estazolam tablets FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "quazepam": {"origin": "Quazepam is a benzodiazepine with a trifluoroethyl group and a sulfur atom in place of the usual oxygen, which gives it an unusual preference for one GABA-A receptor subtype. It was developed in the United States by Schering in the 1970s and approved by the FDA in the mid 1980s as Doral for insomnia. It is still available as Doral and as generic tablets. It shares a very long-lived metabolite with flurazepam, which affects how it builds up with nightly use.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Schering develops quazepam."], ["1980s", "Quazepam (Doral) is approved in the US for insomnia."]], "cell": "Quazepam is a positive allosteric modulator at the benzodiazepine site of GABA-A receptors, increasing how often the chloride channel opens when GABA binds. The parent drug and its active metabolite 2-oxoquazepam show relative selectivity for receptors containing the alpha-1 subunit (the BZ1 type), which mainly drive sedation. Its other major metabolite, N-desalkyl-2-oxoquazepam (the same as a flurazepam metabolite), is nonselective and acts at alpha-1, 2, 3 and 5 receptors. The combined effect is sleep onset and maintenance, with some anxiety relief and memory effects. Chloride entry hyperpolarizes neurons, reducing activity in arousal networks.", "body": [["Brain", "Enhanced GABA inhibition helps people fall asleep and stay asleep."], ["Next day", "The long-lived metabolite builds up, so next-day drowsiness and impaired alertness are common with repeated use."], ["Older adults", "Accumulation causes confusion, poor coordination and falls."], ["Memory", "Anterograde amnesia and complex sleep behaviors can occur."], ["Breathing", "With opioids or alcohol, breathing can be dangerously suppressed."]], "journey": "Quazepam is taken by mouth at bedtime, absorbed well and peaks in about two hours. It is more than 95 percent protein bound and fat soluble. The liver, partly through CYP3A4, converts it to 2-oxoquazepam and then N-desalkyl-2-oxoquazepam. Quazepam and 2-oxoquazepam have half-lives of about 39 hours, and N-desalkyl-2-oxoquazepam lasts about 73 hours. That means levels keep rising for one to two weeks of nightly use. Metabolites leave in urine and feces.", "sidefx": "Daytime drowsiness and fatigue reflect accumulation of long-lived active metabolites. Confusion and falls are especially common in older adults. The boxed warning covers profound sedation and respiratory depression with opioids, abuse and misuse, and dependence; withdrawal after sudden stopping can include seizures, so it is tapered. Complex sleep behaviors and worsening depression can occur. Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepine effects but can trigger seizures in dependent people.", "fun": "Quazepam is one of the few benzodiazepines that contains a sulfur atom, a thione in place of the usual carbonyl oxygen.", "src": ["Doral (quazepam) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "perphenazine-amitriptyline": {"origin": "This combination pairs two drugs from the first generation of psychiatric medicines. Perphenazine, a piperazine phenothiazine, was introduced by Schering in the late 1950s as Trilafon. Amitriptyline, a tricyclic antidepressant, was approved in the US in 1961 and marketed by Merck as Elavil. In the 1960s both companies sold fixed combinations aimed at depression with anxiety or agitation: Merck as Triavil and Schering as Etrafon. Both brands are discontinued, but generic tablets remain. Perphenazine itself gained renewed attention in 2005, when the large CATIE trial found it performed about as well as several newer antipsychotics.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Schering introduces perphenazine (Trilafon)."], ["1961", "Amitriptyline is approved in the US."], ["1960s", "Fixed combinations Triavil and Etrafon are marketed."], ["2005", "The CATIE trial finds perphenazine comparable to several newer antipsychotics."]], "cell": "Perphenazine blocks dopamine D2 receptors. In the mesolimbic pathway this calms agitation and psychotic symptoms, and in the chemoreceptor trigger zone it reduces nausea. Amitriptyline blocks the serotonin and norepinephrine transporters (SERT and NET), raising both transmitters in the synapse; over weeks, adaptive receptor changes lead to the antidepressant effect. Amitriptyline also blocks histamine H1, muscarinic and alpha-1 receptors, causing sedation, anticholinergic effects and low blood pressure, and at high levels it blocks cardiac fast sodium channels. Together they treat depression with prominent anxiety or agitation, or depression with schizophrenia.", "body": [["Brain", "Amitriptyline relieves depression over weeks while perphenazine reduces agitation, anxiety and psychotic symptoms."], ["Sleep", "Strong H1 blockade from amitriptyline causes sedation."], ["Movement", "Perphenazine's D2 blockade can cause dystonia, akathisia, parkinsonism and tardive dyskinesia."], ["Autonomic system", "Amitriptyline's muscarinic blockade causes dry mouth, constipation, blurred vision and urinary retention."], ["Heart and blood pressure", "Alpha-1 blockade causes orthostatic hypotension, and sodium channel block can cause arrhythmias, especially in overdose."], ["Weight", "Histamine blockade increases appetite and weight."]], "journey": "The tablet is taken by mouth, and both drugs are absorbed, peaking in about two to six hours. Both are more than 90 percent protein bound. Perphenazine is metabolized mainly by CYP2D6 and has a half-life of about 9 to 12 hours. Amitriptyline is metabolized by CYP2C19, CYP3A4 and CYP2D6 to nortriptyline, which is also an active antidepressant; amitriptyline's half-life is about 10 to 50 hours, and nortriptyline's is longer. Metabolites leave in urine. Poor CYP2D6 metabolizers or strong CYP2D6 inhibitors raise levels of both.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness, dry mouth, constipation and dizziness come from amitriptyline's receptor blockade. Movement disorders and NMS come from perphenazine's D2 blockade. Overdose is especially dangerous because of the tricyclic component: sodium channel block causes QRS widening, ventricular arrhythmias, seizures and coma; sodium bicarbonate is the key treatment. The product carries both class boxed warnings: suicidality in young people taking antidepressants and increased death in elderly people with dementia-related psychosis. Blood dyscrasias are rare.", "fun": "Perphenazine was the older drug chosen as a comparison in the CATIE trial, and its strong showing changed how many clinicians viewed first-generation antipsychotics.", "src": ["Perphenazine and amitriptyline hydrochloride tablets FDA label", "Lieberman JA et al. N Engl J Med 2005 (CATIE)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "olanzapine-fluoxetine": {"origin": "Both parts of this combination came from Eli Lilly. Fluoxetine was discovered in the early 1970s by David Wong, Bryan Molloy and Ray Fuller and approved in 1987 as Prozac, the first SSRI in the US. Olanzapine was approved in 1996 as Zyprexa. Lilly researchers found that the two together raised dopamine and norepinephrine in the prefrontal cortex of animals more than either alone, and clinical trials showed benefit in bipolar depression. The FDA approved the combination as Symbyax in 2003, making it the first drug approved for depressive episodes of bipolar I disorder, and in 2009 for treatment-resistant depression.", "timeline": [["1972", "Lilly scientists identify fluoxetine as a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor."], ["1987", "Fluoxetine (Prozac) is approved in the US."], ["1996", "Olanzapine (Zyprexa) is approved in the US."], ["2003", "FDA approves olanzapine-fluoxetine (Symbyax) for bipolar depression."], ["2009", "Symbyax is approved for treatment-resistant depression."]], "cell": "Fluoxetine selectively blocks the serotonin transporter (SERT), raising synaptic serotonin; over weeks this desensitizes 5-HT1A autoreceptors and boosts serotonin transmission. Olanzapine blocks D2 receptors in the mesolimbic pathway (preventing mania and psychosis) and 5-HT2A and 5-HT2C receptors. Blocking 5-HT2C on GABA interneurons in the prefrontal cortex removes a brake on dopamine and norepinephrine neurons, so the combination raises prefrontal dopamine and norepinephrine more than either drug alone. Olanzapine also blocks H1, muscarinic and alpha-1 receptors. Fluoxetine strongly inhibits CYP2D6, which affects many other drugs.", "body": [["Brain", "Combined serotonin, dopamine and norepinephrine effects relieve bipolar and treatment-resistant depression, while D2 blockade helps prevent switching into mania."], ["Weight and appetite", "Olanzapine's H1 and 5-HT2C blockade strongly increase appetite and weight."], ["Metabolism", "Blood sugar and lipids often rise, increasing diabetes risk."], ["Alertness", "Sleepiness and fatigue are common from H1 blockade."], ["Blood pressure", "Alpha-1 blockade can cause dizziness on standing."], ["Pituitary", "D2 blockade in the pituitary can raise prolactin, causing breast or menstrual changes."]], "journey": "The capsule is taken by mouth, usually in the evening. Olanzapine peaks in about four hours and fluoxetine in about six. Olanzapine is metabolized by CYP1A2 and glucuronidation, with a half-life of about 30 hours. Fluoxetine is converted by CYP2D6 to norfluoxetine, also an active SSRI; fluoxetine lasts one to four days and norfluoxetine 7 to 15 days. Because of norfluoxetine, effects and interactions persist for weeks after stopping. Both are highly protein bound and leave as metabolites in urine and feces.", "sidefx": "Weight gain, high blood sugar and high lipids come from olanzapine's receptor profile and its effects on insulin sensitivity. Sleepiness and dizziness reflect H1 and alpha-1 blockade. Serotonin syndrome can occur with other serotonergic drugs. The product carries boxed warnings for suicidality in young people and for increased death in elderly people with dementia-related psychosis. NMS, DRESS, QT prolongation, bleeding and hyponatremia are rarer. Fluoxetine's CYP2D6 inhibition raises levels of many drugs. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Symbyax was the first medicine the FDA approved specifically for the depressive phase of bipolar I disorder.", "src": ["Symbyax (olanzapine and fluoxetine) FDA label", "Wong DT et al. Life Sci 1974", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "aripiprazole-lauroxil": {"origin": "Aripiprazole lauroxil was designed by Alkermes as a prodrug of Otsuka's aripiprazole. Alkermes chemists attached a long fatty chain (lauric acid) through a linker to aripiprazole, making a molecule that is very poorly soluble in water. Injected into muscle, it dissolves only slowly, giving one to two months of drug from one injection. The FDA approved it as Aristada in 2015 for schizophrenia in adults. In 2018 Alkermes added Aristada Initio, a smaller-particle version that dissolves faster and helps start treatment along with one oral dose, so patients do not need weeks of oral overlap.", "timeline": [["2002", "Oral aripiprazole (Abilify) is approved in the US."], ["2015", "FDA approves aripiprazole lauroxil (Aristada) for schizophrenia."], ["2018", "FDA approves Aristada Initio for starting treatment."]], "cell": "After injection, aripiprazole lauroxil slowly dissolves and is cleaved by tissue esterases to N-hydroxymethyl aripiprazole, which breaks down without enzymes to release aripiprazole. Aripiprazole is a partial agonist at dopamine D2 and D3 receptors and serotonin 5-HT1A receptors, and an antagonist at 5-HT2A receptors. Partial agonism at D2 lowers signaling in overactive mesolimbic pathways, reducing psychosis, while providing some stimulation where dopamine is low, which keeps prolactin low and limits movement side effects. The slow dissolution rate, not the active drug, sets how long each injection lasts.", "body": [["Brain", "Steady D2 partial agonism controls positive symptoms of schizophrenia and prevents relapse."], ["Movement", "Akathisia is the most common side effect; parkinsonism and tardive dyskinesia are possible."], ["Pituitary", "Prolactin stays low because D2 receptors are partly activated rather than fully blocked."], ["Metabolism", "Weight gain and changes in glucose and lipids can occur."], ["Reward and impulse", "Dopamine agonist activity can lead to compulsive gambling or other urges."], ["Injection site", "Pain at the deltoid or gluteal injection site is common."]], "journey": "Aristada is injected into the deltoid or gluteal muscle. Without help, aripiprazole does not appear in the blood for five to six days, so treatment starts with Aristada Initio plus one oral aripiprazole dose, or with three weeks of oral aripiprazole. Levels rise over weeks and reach steady state after about four months. Once released, aripiprazole is more than 99 percent protein bound and is metabolized by CYP2D6 and CYP3A4 to active dehydro-aripiprazole. The apparent half-life is about 29 to 35 days, and metabolites leave in feces and urine.", "sidefx": "Akathisia reflects D2 partial agonism in motor circuits. Compulsive behaviors come from agonist action in reward pathways. Metabolic changes are class effects. The class boxed warning covers increased death in elderly people with dementia-related psychosis, and NMS, tardive dyskinesia, orthostasis, falls and seizures can occur. Because an injection cannot be removed, side effects may last weeks, which is why tolerability with oral aripiprazole is checked first in people who have never taken it. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Aripiprazole lauroxil uses a fatty acid tail from lauric acid, the same fatty acid that makes up much of coconut oil.", "src": ["Aristada (aripiprazole lauroxil) FDA label", "Aristada Initio FDA label"]}, "risperidone-long-acting": {"origin": "Risperidone was discovered at Janssen Pharmaceutica in Belgium in the 1980s and approved in the US in 1993 as Risperdal. To help people who struggle to take daily pills, Janssen worked with Alkermes, whose microsphere technology wraps drug inside biodegradable polylactide-co-glycolide spheres. The FDA approved Risperdal Consta in 2003, the first long-acting injectable second-generation antipsychotic, given every two weeks. Other long-acting risperidone products followed with different delivery systems, including Perseris (2018), Uzedy (2023) and Rykindo (2023).", "timeline": [["1980s", "Janssen discovers risperidone."], ["1993", "Oral risperidone (Risperdal) is approved in the US."], ["2003", "FDA approves Risperdal Consta, the first long-acting injectable atypical antipsychotic."], ["2018", "FDA approves Perseris, a monthly subcutaneous risperidone."], ["2023", "FDA approves Uzedy and Rykindo, newer long-acting risperidone products."]], "cell": "Risperidone and its active metabolite 9-hydroxyrisperidone (paliperidone) block dopamine D2 receptors and, even more strongly, serotonin 5-HT2A receptors. D2 blockade in the mesolimbic pathway reduces hallucinations and delusions. 5-HT2A blockade increases dopamine release in the striatum and cortex, which partly offsets movement side effects at lower exposures. At higher exposures D2 blockade dominates, so parkinsonism and prolactin rise become more likely. It also blocks alpha-1, alpha-2 and H1 receptors, causing dizziness and sedation. In Risperdal Consta, drug trapped in microspheres is released as the polymer slowly breaks down.", "body": [["Brain", "D2 and 5-HT2A blockade control psychosis in schizophrenia and help maintain mood stability in bipolar I disorder."], ["Pituitary", "Risperidone raises prolactin more than most atypicals, which can cause breast milk discharge, missed periods and sexual problems."], ["Movement", "Parkinsonism and akathisia are dose-related, and tardive dyskinesia can develop."], ["Blood pressure", "Alpha-1 blockade can cause dizziness on standing."], ["Metabolism", "Weight gain and higher glucose and lipids can occur."], ["Injection site", "Pain at the deltoid or gluteal injection site is common."]], "journey": "Risperdal Consta is injected into the deltoid or gluteal muscle every two weeks. Only a small amount is released at first; the main release begins about three weeks after injection, so oral antipsychotic coverage continues for three weeks. Levels peak at four to six weeks and fall off by about seven weeks after the last injection. The liver converts risperidone through CYP2D6 to active 9-hydroxyrisperidone. Risperidone is about 90 percent protein bound and 9-hydroxyrisperidone about 77 percent. About 70 percent leaves in urine, the rest in feces.", "sidefx": "High prolactin comes from D2 blockade in the pituitary, where risperidone is especially active. Movement disorders come from striatal D2 blockade and rise with exposure. Dizziness and fainting reflect alpha-1 blockade. Weight gain and metabolic changes are class effects. The class boxed warning covers increased death and cerebrovascular events in elderly people with dementia-related psychosis. NMS, low white counts, seizures and priapism are rare. Because the depot lasts weeks, oral tolerability is checked first. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Risperdal Consta is stored in the refrigerator and its microsphere powder is mixed with a liquid diluent just before injection.", "src": ["Risperdal Consta (risperidone long-acting injection) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "paliperidone-palmitate": {"origin": "Paliperidone grew out of risperidone, the atypical antipsychotic that Janssen Pharmaceutica in Belgium brought to the US market in 1993. Researchers knew that the liver turns risperidone into 9-hydroxyrisperidone, an active metabolite that does much of the work. Janssen developed that metabolite as its own drug, paliperidone, approved as oral Invega in 2006. To solve the problem of missed pills, the company attached a long palmitic acid (a fatty acid) tail and milled the ester into nanocrystals that dissolve slowly in muscle. Invega Sustenna, the monthly shot, was approved in 2009. Longer versions followed: Invega Trinza every three months in 2015 and Invega Hafyera every six months in 2021. A newer product, Erzofri, uses the same molecule. These products mattered because relapse in schizophrenia is often driven by stopping medication.", "timeline": [["1993", "Risperidone, the parent drug, is approved in the US."], ["2006", "Oral paliperidone (Invega) is approved."], ["2009", "FDA approves monthly Invega Sustenna."], ["2015", "Three-month Invega Trinza is approved."], ["2021", "Six-month Invega Hafyera is approved."]], "cell": "Once the palmitate ester is cleaved, free paliperidone blocks dopamine D2 receptors. In the mesolimbic pathway, D2 blockade reduces the overactive dopamine signaling linked to hallucinations and delusions. D2 receptors are Gi-coupled, so blocking them removes dopamine's brake on adenylyl cyclase in those neurons. Paliperidone also blocks serotonin 5-HT2A receptors with high affinity. 5-HT2A blockade on cortical and nigrostriatal circuits allows some dopamine release in the striatum, which helps explain why movement side effects are less common than with older drugs at modest exposure. Blockade in the tuberoinfundibular pathway removes dopamine's inhibition of pituitary lactotrophs, so prolactin rises. Alpha-1 blockade relaxes vessels and H1 blockade causes sedation and weight gain. It has almost no muscarinic activity, so dry mouth and constipation are uncommon.", "body": [["Brain (mood and thinking)", "Calmer dopamine signaling in limbic areas reduces psychotic symptoms over weeks of steady exposure."], ["Brain (movement)", "D2 blockade in the striatum can cause restlessness (akathisia), stiffness and tremor, more so at higher exposure."], ["Pituitary", "Loss of dopamine's brake raises prolactin, which can cause breast enlargement, milk discharge and menstrual changes. Paliperidone raises prolactin more than many atypicals."], ["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 blockade widens vessels and can drop blood pressure on standing, with dizziness or fainting."], ["Metabolism", "H1 and 5-HT2C effects raise appetite, and weight, glucose and lipids can climb over time."], ["Muscle (injection site)", "The deltoid or gluteal muscle serves as a slow-release reservoir, and local pain or swelling can occur."]], "journey": "After deep IM injection, the nanocrystals sit in muscle and dissolve slowly because the palmitate ester is poorly water soluble. Dissolved ester is hydrolyzed to paliperidone, which enters the blood. The first two injections go into the deltoid, where blood flow is higher, to reach useful levels within days. Peak levels come about two weeks after a dose. Because release is the slow step, the apparent half-life is about four to seven weeks. Paliperidone itself is barely metabolized, so CYP2D6 differences matter little. About 59% leaves unchanged in urine, which is why kidney impairment raises levels and moderate to severe impairment is not recommended.", "sidefx": "Most side effects trace to receptor blockade. Striatal D2 blockade produces akathisia and parkinsonism, and long exposure can lead to tardive dyskinesia from adaptive changes in dopamine receptors. Pituitary D2 blockade raises prolactin. Alpha-1 blockade causes orthostasis and, rarely, priapism. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is a rare reaction to sudden central dopamine blockade with rigidity, fever and autonomic instability. The QT interval can lengthen from potassium channel effects. Elderly people with dementia have higher death rates, mostly from heart and infection causes, which is a class boxed warning. Because the depot cannot be removed, side effects can last for weeks. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Paliperidone is literally what risperidone becomes in your liver, so taking paliperidone skips the CYP2D6 step that makes risperidone levels vary between people.", "src": ["Invega Sustenna (paliperidone palmitate) FDA label", "Invega Trinza and Invega Hafyera FDA labels", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "olanzapine-pamoate": {"origin": "Olanzapine was designed by chemists at Eli Lilly in the 1980s, working to build a drug with the benefits of clozapine but without its risk of agranulocytosis. They kept clozapine's general three-ring shape and changed parts of it, creating a thienobenzodiazepine. Oral olanzapine (Zyprexa) was approved in 1996 and became one of the most used antipsychotics. To help people who stop pills, Lilly paired olanzapine with pamoic acid, forming a salt that dissolves very slowly in muscle. Zyprexa Relprevv was approved by the FDA in 2009. During trials, a small number of patients became very sleepy or confused within hours of an injection, which led to strict observation rules under a REMS program.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Lilly chemists develop olanzapine as a safer relative of clozapine."], ["1996", "FDA approves oral olanzapine (Zyprexa)."], ["2009", "FDA approves Zyprexa Relprevv, the long-acting pamoate injection."]], "cell": "Olanzapine blocks dopamine D2 receptors in the mesolimbic pathway, damping the excess dopamine signal linked to positive symptoms of schizophrenia. It also blocks 5-HT2A receptors strongly, which favors dopamine release in the striatum and prefrontal cortex and lowers the risk of movement side effects compared with older drugs. Its strong blockade of histamine H1 and serotonin 5-HT2C receptors drives sedation and appetite. It also blocks muscarinic receptors and alpha-1 adrenergic receptors. The pamoate salt itself has no activity; it only controls how fast olanzapine reaches the blood. If a large amount reaches the blood quickly, the combined H1, muscarinic and alpha-1 blockade produces the sedation and delirium seen in post-injection delirium/sedation syndrome.", "body": [["Brain (psychosis)", "Lower limbic dopamine signaling eases hallucinations and delusions with steady release over weeks."], ["Brain (alertness)", "H1 blockade causes sleepiness, and a sudden high level can cause deep sedation, confusion or coma."], ["Appetite and metabolism", "H1 and 5-HT2C blockade increase hunger. Weight gain, higher glucose, diabetic ketoacidosis in rare cases and higher lipids can follow."], ["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 blockade relaxes vessels and can cause dizziness on standing."], ["Autonomic system", "Muscarinic blockade causes dry mouth, constipation and urinary retention, and can add to confusion."], ["Buttock muscle", "The gluteal muscle holds the depot. Injection into a small vessel may let drug escape quickly, which is the leading theory for post-injection syndrome."]], "journey": "The suspension is injected deep into the gluteal muscle. The pamoate salt slowly dissolves and separates into olanzapine and pamoic acid, so release, not elimination, sets the pace. Levels peak a few days after a shot and the apparent half-life is about 30 days. In the liver, olanzapine is mainly glucuronidated and oxidized by CYP1A2, with a minor CYP2D6 role. Smoking induces CYP1A2 and lowers levels. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. It is not for IV or subcutaneous use, and it is not interchangeable with the short-acting olanzapine IM shot.", "sidefx": "Post-injection delirium/sedation syndrome is the hallmark risk: if drug enters the blood too fast, levels spike and the strong H1, muscarinic and alpha-1 blockade causes sleepiness, confusion, slurred speech, unsteady walking or coma. That is why each injection is followed by at least three hours of observation in a registered facility. Weight gain and high glucose come from appetite effects and changes in insulin sensitivity. Movement effects are less common than with typical drugs but can occur from D2 blockade. NMS and DRESS are rare but serious. There is no antidote; care is supportive, and symptoms fade as levels fall.", "fun": "Pamoic acid, the partner in this salt, is also used to make long-acting forms of other drugs, such as hydroxyzine pamoate and the deworming drug pyrantel pamoate.", "src": ["Zyprexa Relprevv (olanzapine pamoate) FDA label and REMS", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "haloperidol-decanoate": {"origin": "Haloperidol was made in 1958 at Janssen Pharmaceutica in Belgium, the company founded by Paul Janssen. His team was modifying pethidine (meperidine)-like molecules in search of better painkillers and noticed that one butyrophenone compound calmed animals in a way that resembled chlorpromazine. That compound was haloperidol. It spread quickly in Europe and was approved in the US in 1967. Because many people with schizophrenia relapse after stopping pills, chemists attached a ten-carbon decanoic acid chain to haloperidol's hydroxyl group and dissolved it in sesame oil. This oily depot releases drug over about a month. Haloperidol decanoate became available in the US in the 1980s and remains a common long-acting injection.", "timeline": [["1958", "Paul Janssen's team synthesizes haloperidol in Belgium."], ["1967", "Haloperidol is approved in the US."], ["1980s", "Haloperidol decanoate becomes available in the US."]], "cell": "After esterases cut off the decanoate chain, haloperidol binds dopamine D2 receptors with high affinity and blocks them. D2 receptors are Gi-coupled, so dopamine normally lowers cAMP in the receiving neuron. In the mesolimbic pathway, blocking this signal reduces hallucinations and delusions. The same blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway upsets the dopamine-acetylcholine balance that controls movement, causing extrapyramidal symptoms. In the tuberoinfundibular pathway, dopamine normally suppresses prolactin release, so blockade raises prolactin. Haloperidol has only weak alpha-1 activity and very little muscarinic or histamine blockade, so it causes less sedation, low blood pressure and dry mouth than low-potency drugs, but more movement problems.", "body": [["Brain (psychosis)", "Strong D2 blockade in limbic areas reduces positive symptoms such as hallucinations and paranoia."], ["Brain (movement)", "Striatal D2 blockade causes dystonia, parkinsonism and akathisia. With years of use, tardive dyskinesia can develop."], ["Pituitary", "Prolactin rises, which can cause breast changes, milk discharge, menstrual changes and sexual dysfunction."], ["Heart", "Haloperidol can lengthen the QT interval by blocking cardiac potassium (hERG) channels, raising the risk of torsades de pointes."], ["Temperature control", "Dopamine blockade in the hypothalamus contributes to the fever and instability of NMS."]], "journey": "Haloperidol decanoate in sesame oil is given by deep IM injection, never IV. The oily depot slowly releases the ester into tissue fluid, where esterases free active haloperidol. Levels peak about six days after an injection, and the apparent half-life is about three weeks, which allows monthly dosing. Steady state takes several months. In the liver, haloperidol is processed by CYP3A4, CYP2D6, glucuronidation and reduction to reduced haloperidol. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. About 90% is bound to plasma proteins.", "sidefx": "Movement side effects are the main issue and come from nigrostriatal D2 blockade. Acute dystonia, including dangerous laryngeal spasm, can occur early. Akathisia and parkinsonism follow, and tardive dyskinesia develops after long exposure as receptors become hypersensitive. Anticholinergic drugs such as benztropine or diphenhydramine treat acute dystonia, but there is no reversal for the drug itself. QT prolongation reflects hERG potassium channel blockade. NMS is a rare, severe reaction with rigidity, fever and rising CK. Since the depot keeps releasing drug, effects can last for weeks, which is why oral tolerance is confirmed first.", "fun": "Haloperidol was discovered while Janssen's team was hunting for painkillers, a reminder that some of medicine's key drugs were found by watching animals closely for unexpected effects.", "src": ["Haloperidol decanoate injection FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "fluphenazine-decanoate": {"origin": "Fluphenazine is a phenothiazine, a family that began with chlorpromazine, made in France in 1950 by Rhone-Poulenc and found in 1952 to calm patients with psychosis. Chemists then tuned the phenothiazine ring with different side chains. Adding a trifluoromethyl group and a piperazine side chain produced fluphenazine, a very potent D2 blocker developed by Squibb in the late 1950s. In the 1960s Squibb attached fatty acid esters to its free hydroxyl group, first enanthate and then decanoate, and dissolved them in sesame oil. Fluphenazine decanoate was among the first long-acting injectable antipsychotics, a major step for keeping people out of the hospital. The Prolixin Decanoate brand is discontinued, but generic fluphenazine decanoate remains available.", "timeline": [["1950", "Chlorpromazine, the first phenothiazine antipsychotic, is synthesized in France."], ["1950s", "Squibb develops fluphenazine, a high-potency phenothiazine."], ["1960s", "Long-acting fluphenazine esters (enanthate, then decanoate) are introduced."]], "cell": "The depot releases fluphenazine decanoate, and esterases cut off the decanoate chain to free fluphenazine. Fluphenazine competitively blocks postsynaptic dopamine D2 receptors. In the mesolimbic and mesocortical pathways, this reduces the excess dopamine signaling linked to hallucinations and delusions. In the nigrostriatal pathway, the same blockade reduces dopamine's control of movement and allows relative cholinergic excess, causing extrapyramidal symptoms. In the tuberoinfundibular pathway it removes dopamine's inhibition of prolactin release. As a high-potency piperazine phenothiazine, it has only weak muscarinic, H1 and alpha-1 blockade compared with chlorpromazine, so it causes less sedation and low blood pressure but more movement effects.", "body": [["Brain (psychosis)", "One injection can keep D2 receptors blocked for weeks, reducing positive symptoms and relapse."], ["Brain (movement)", "Akathisia, parkinsonism and dystonia are common and can persist for weeks because the depot keeps releasing drug."], ["Pituitary", "Prolactin rises, with possible breast changes, menstrual changes and sexual dysfunction."], ["Blood", "Phenothiazines can rarely cause leukopenia or agranulocytosis, raising infection risk."], ["Liver", "Cholestatic jaundice is a known, uncommon phenothiazine reaction."], ["Temperature and autonomic system", "Dopamine blockade in the hypothalamus can impair heat control and plays a role in NMS."]], "journey": "Fluphenazine decanoate is dissolved in sesame oil and injected IM or subcutaneously. The ester leaks out of the oil depot slowly, and esterases release active fluphenazine. Antipsychotic effect can begin within one to three days, with a peak level about a day or two after injection. The half-life is about one to two weeks, so effects last three to four weeks or longer and it takes months to reach steady state. The liver oxidizes fluphenazine, largely by CYP2D6, and conjugates it, with elimination in urine and bile.", "sidefx": "Extrapyramidal symptoms are the signature problem and come from strong nigrostriatal D2 blockade. Acute dystonia can involve the neck, eyes or larynx. Akathisia and parkinsonism are common, and tardive dyskinesia can appear after long use as dopamine receptors become supersensitive. Anticholinergic drugs treat acute dystonia and parkinsonism, but the depot itself cannot be reversed, so symptoms can persist. NMS is a rare, life-threatening reaction. Low blood counts, cholestatic jaundice and seizures are rare phenothiazine effects. Elderly people with dementia have increased mortality, a class boxed warning.", "fun": "Fluphenazine is roughly 50 times more potent per milligram at D2 receptors than chlorpromazine, which is why it is called a high-potency antipsychotic.", "src": ["Fluphenazine decanoate injection FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "dextromethorphan-quinidine": {"origin": "This combination joins two old drugs for a new use. Dextromethorphan was developed in the 1950s as a non-addictive cough suppressant related to the opioid levorphanol, and was approved as a cough medicine in 1958. Quinidine comes from the bark of the cinchona tree, the same South American source as quinine, and was long used for heart rhythm problems. In the 1990s, researchers working with neurologist Richard Smith in San Diego noticed that dextromethorphan seemed to help pseudobulbar affect, a condition of sudden, uncontrollable laughing or crying after brain injury, ALS or multiple sclerosis. The problem was that the liver broke it down too quickly. Adding a small amount of quinidine, which blocks CYP2D6, kept dextromethorphan in the blood. Avanir Pharmaceuticals developed the product as Nuedexta, approved in 2010 as the first FDA-approved treatment for pseudobulbar affect.", "timeline": [["1958", "Dextromethorphan is approved as a cough suppressant."], ["1990s", "Researchers explore dextromethorphan with quinidine for pseudobulbar affect."], ["2010", "FDA approves Nuedexta, the first treatment for pseudobulbar affect."]], "cell": "Dextromethorphan blocks the NMDA glutamate receptor in an uncompetitive way, meaning it binds inside the open channel and reduces excess excitatory signaling. It is also a sigma-1 receptor agonist; sigma-1 is a chaperone protein in neuron membranes that modulates calcium signaling and glutamate release. It weakly inhibits serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake. How these actions calm pseudobulbar affect is not fully known, but the circuits linking the cortex, brainstem and cerebellum that control emotional expression are thought to be involved. Quinidine's role is pharmacokinetic. It competitively inhibits CYP2D6, the liver enzyme that O-demethylates dextromethorphan into dextrorphan. With CYP2D6 blocked, dextromethorphan levels rise many times over and stay high.", "body": [["Brain (emotional expression)", "Fewer and milder bursts of crying or laughing that do not match how the person feels."], ["Brain (balance)", "Dizziness and falls can occur, partly from central effects on balance."], ["Liver enzymes", "Quinidine blocks CYP2D6, which can raise levels of other drugs that rely on it, such as some antidepressants and beta-blockers."], ["Heart", "Quinidine can lengthen the QT interval by blocking potassium channels, so torsades de pointes is a risk with QT-prolonging drugs."], ["Blood", "Quinidine can trigger immune-mediated thrombocytopenia, with bruising or bleeding."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea and vomiting are common."]], "journey": "Both parts are taken by mouth as one capsule. Quinidine peaks in about two hours and dextromethorphan in about three to four hours. Without quinidine, dextromethorphan is cleared so fast by CYP2D6 that little stays in the blood. With quinidine, its half-life stretches to about 13 hours. Dextromethorphan is then handled more by CYP3A4. Quinidine itself is cleared by CYP3A4 with a half-life of about seven hours. The amount of quinidine is far lower than what was once used for arrhythmias. Metabolites leave in urine. Benefit builds over days to weeks.", "sidefx": "Dizziness and falls come from dextromethorphan's central effects. QT prolongation and torsades come from quinidine's potassium channel blockade and grow with CYP3A4 inhibitors or other QT drugs. Quinidine can bind platelets and trigger antibodies that destroy them, causing sudden thrombocytopenia; the drug is stopped if this happens. Blocking CYP2D6 raises other drugs' levels, and with serotonergic drugs, the combination can cause serotonin syndrome. Liver injury and hypersensitivity can occur. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "The quinidine in this capsule is not there to treat anything; it is a 'booster' that disables a liver enzyme, the same idea used when ritonavir boosts other HIV drugs.", "src": ["Nuedexta (dextromethorphan and quinidine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "ciclesonide": {"origin": "Ciclesonide was developed in Germany by Altana Pharma (formerly Byk Gulden) with a clear goal: an inhaled steroid that stays inactive until it reaches the lungs. Chemists built it as a prodrug ester. Enzymes in airway cells cut it into the active form, des-ciclesonide, so less active steroid lands in the mouth and throat. In the US, the nasal spray Omnaris was approved in 2006 and the asthma inhaler Alvesco in 2008, followed by the Zetonna nasal aerosol. Ciclesonide also drew attention in 2020, when lab studies suggested it could block coronavirus replication, though clinical trials did not show a clear benefit.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Altana (Byk Gulden) develops ciclesonide as an on-site activated inhaled steroid."], ["2006", "Omnaris nasal spray is approved in the US."], ["2008", "FDA approves Alvesco inhalation aerosol for asthma."]], "cell": "Ciclesonide itself barely binds the glucocorticoid receptor. Esterases in the airway lining remove its isobutyryl group to form des-ciclesonide, which binds the receptor with about 100 times higher affinity. Inside the cell, the bound receptor moves to the nucleus. There it switches on anti-inflammatory genes and, more importantly, blocks pro-inflammatory transcription factors such as NF-kB and AP-1. Fewer cytokines such as IL-4, IL-5 and IL-13 are made, so eosinophils, mast cells and T cells in the airway decline. Airway swelling and twitchiness fall over days to weeks. Des-ciclesonide also forms fatty acid conjugates inside lung cells that act as a slow-release store.", "body": [["Airways", "Less inflammation and swelling make airways less reactive, with fewer asthma attacks and better lung function over weeks."], ["Mouth and throat", "Less active steroid lands in the mouth than with some inhaled steroids, but thrush and hoarseness can still occur."], ["Nose (nasal forms)", "Reduced congestion, sneezing and runny nose in allergic rhinitis."], ["Adrenal glands", "Steroid that reaches the blood can suppress the body's own cortisol production, especially at high exposure."], ["Bones and eyes", "Long-term steroid exposure can lower bone density and raise the risk of cataracts and glaucoma."], ["Growth", "Children on inhaled steroids may have a small slowing of growth."]], "journey": "Inhaled as a fine aerosol, much of the dose reaches the lungs. Lung esterases convert it to des-ciclesonide, which peaks in blood about an hour after inhalation. The swallowed portion has almost no oral bioavailability because both parent drug and active metabolite are destroyed on first pass through the liver by CYP3A4. Des-ciclesonide is about 99% bound to plasma proteins, which leaves little free drug to act elsewhere. Its half-life is about six to seven hours. Metabolites leave mainly in feces.", "sidefx": "Side effects reflect glucocorticoid action where the drug lands or reaches. Steroid on the throat lining lowers local immunity, so thrush can occur; rinsing the mouth helps. Steroid that reaches the blood can suppress the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis, so switching from oral steroids too fast can unmask adrenal insufficiency. Immune suppression can make chickenpox and measles more severe. Long-term exposure can thin bones, slow growth and affect the eyes. It does not open airways quickly and is not for acute attacks. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Because ciclesonide is switched on by enzymes inside airway cells, it has been described as an 'on-site activated' steroid.", "src": ["Alvesco (ciclesonide) FDA label", "Omnaris (ciclesonide) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "flunisolide": {"origin": "Flunisolide is a synthetic corticosteroid from the era when companies raced to make steroids that worked strongly in the nose and lungs but were broken down quickly once swallowed. It was developed by Syntex, the company in Palo Alto known for its steroid chemistry, and was introduced in the US in the early 1980s as Nasalide nasal spray. An inhaler form for asthma followed. Over time the brands Nasalide, Nasarel and the Aerospan HFA inhaler were discontinued, but generic flunisolide nasal solution remains available.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Synthetic corticosteroids with fluorine substitutions are developed for stronger effect."], ["1980s", "Flunisolide nasal spray (Nasalide) is introduced in the US."], ["2006", "The Aerospan HFA inhaler form is approved."]], "cell": "Flunisolide is a fluorinated corticosteroid that enters cells of the nasal lining and binds the glucocorticoid receptor in the cytoplasm. The receptor-drug complex moves into the nucleus. There it turns up anti-inflammatory genes and blocks pro-inflammatory transcription factors such as NF-kB and AP-1. The result is fewer cytokines and chemokines, so mast cells, eosinophils and T cells gather less in the nasal tissue. Vessels leak less fluid, so swelling, mucus and sneezing decrease. Because these are gene-level effects, benefit takes several days to build.", "body": [["Nose", "Less swelling, runny nose, itching and sneezing in allergic and non-allergic rhinitis, building over days."], ["Nasal lining", "Local steroid can thin the lining, causing nosebleeds and, rarely, septal perforation."], ["Throat and sinuses", "Less post-nasal drip as inflammation calms; sore throat and altered taste or smell can occur."], ["Adrenal glands", "With excessive use, absorbed steroid can suppress the body's cortisol production."], ["Eyes", "Long-term corticosteroid exposure is linked to cataracts and glaucoma."]], "journey": "Sprayed into the nose, flunisolide acts mostly on the nasal lining. Some is absorbed through the lining, and much of the spray is swallowed. The swallowed drug is heavily removed on its first pass through the liver, which converts it to a 6-beta-hydroxy metabolite with little activity. The half-life in blood is short, about one to two hours. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. Benefit starts within days, with full effect over one to three weeks.", "sidefx": "Nasal burning and stinging are partly from the solution itself. Nosebleeds and rare septal perforation reflect steroid thinning of the nasal mucosa. Lower local immunity can allow nasal Candida infection. At high exposure, absorbed steroid can suppress the adrenal axis, and switching from oral steroids can unmask adrenal insufficiency. Immune suppression can worsen chickenpox or measles. Children may show slowed growth with long use. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Flunisolide nasal solution FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "arformoterol": {"origin": "Arformoterol is one mirror-image half of formoterol, a long-acting beta-2 agonist developed in Japan by Yamanouchi in the 1970s. Formoterol is a racemic mix of two enantiomers. The (R,R) form does the bronchodilating work, while the (S,S) form binds the receptor poorly. Sepracor, a US company known for developing single-isomer versions of existing drugs, purified the active (R,R) form and turned it into a nebulizer solution for COPD. Brovana was approved by the FDA in 2006 as the first long-acting beta-2 agonist given by nebulizer, which helped patients who struggle to use handheld inhalers.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Formoterol is developed by Yamanouchi in Japan."], ["2006", "FDA approves Brovana (arformoterol) nebulizer solution for COPD."]], "cell": "Arformoterol binds beta-2 adrenergic receptors on bronchial smooth muscle. These are Gs-coupled receptors, so binding activates adenylyl cyclase and raises cyclic AMP. Cyclic AMP turns on protein kinase A, which lowers calcium in the cell and inhibits myosin light chain kinase. The muscle relaxes and the airway opens. Formoterol-type molecules are moderately lipophilic, so they enter the cell membrane and leak out to the receptor over time, giving fast onset with a 12-hour effect. Beta-2 activation on mast cells also reduces mediator release. At higher exposure, beta-2 receptors in the heart, skeletal muscle and liver cause tachycardia, tremor, low potassium and high glucose.", "body": [["Airways", "Bronchial smooth muscle relaxes within about 20 minutes and stays open for about 12 hours."], ["Heart", "Beta stimulation can raise heart rate and, in some people, cause arrhythmias."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Beta-2 activation causes tremor and leg cramps."], ["Potassium balance", "Beta-2 activation pushes potassium into cells via the Na-K ATPase, lowering blood potassium."], ["Liver and glucose", "Increased glycogen breakdown raises blood glucose."]], "journey": "Arformoterol is breathed in as a mist from a nebulizer. Most of its action happens in the airway, and a small part is absorbed into the blood, peaking in about one to three hours. It is mainly broken down by direct glucuronidation, with some O-demethylation by CYP2D6 and CYP2C19. It leaves mostly in urine. The half-life is about 26 hours in COPD patients, though the dosing is twice daily because bronchodilation lasts about 12 hours.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from beta-2 stimulation outside the lungs. Tachycardia and arrhythmias arise from cardiac beta receptors. Tremor and cramps come from skeletal muscle beta-2 receptors. Low potassium follows from potassium shifting into cells, and high glucose from liver glycogen breakdown. Paradoxical bronchospasm is rare and calls for stopping the drug. It is not a rescue drug and is not indicated for asthma, where LABA use without a steroid was linked to serious events. Beta-blockers can reverse effects but may trigger bronchospasm.", "fun": "Arformoterol is an example of a 'chiral switch': taking an existing racemic drug and developing only its active mirror image.", "src": ["Brovana (arformoterol) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "olodaterol": {"origin": "Olodaterol was developed by Boehringer Ingelheim in Germany as a beta-2 agonist that lasts a full day. Earlier long-acting agonists such as salmeterol and formoterol needed twice-daily dosing. Boehringer designed olodaterol to bind tightly and stay near the receptor for 24 hours, and paired it with its Respimat soft-mist inhaler, which releases a slow-moving cloud. Striverdi Respimat was approved by the FDA in 2014 for COPD. In 2015 olodaterol was combined with tiotropium as Stiolto Respimat.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Boehringer Ingelheim develops olodaterol as a once-daily beta-2 agonist."], ["2014", "FDA approves Striverdi Respimat for COPD."], ["2015", "Stiolto Respimat (tiotropium and olodaterol) is approved."]], "cell": "Olodaterol is a selective beta-2 adrenergic agonist with high intrinsic activity, meaning it nearly fully activates the receptor. Binding to beta-2 receptors on airway smooth muscle activates Gs, which turns on adenylyl cyclase and raises cyclic AMP. Protein kinase A then lowers intracellular calcium and reduces myosin light chain phosphorylation, so the muscle relaxes. Olodaterol stays associated with the receptor and lung tissue for a long time, which gives a 24-hour effect. Its onset is fast, within about five minutes.", "body": [["Airways", "Bronchodilation starts within minutes and lasts about 24 hours, easing breathlessness in COPD."], ["Heart", "Beta stimulation can raise heart rate and cause palpitations or arrhythmias."], ["Muscles", "Beta-2 activation in skeletal muscle can cause tremor."], ["Electrolytes and glucose", "Potassium shifts into cells and glucose can rise at higher exposure."]], "journey": "The Respimat inhaler produces a slow soft mist that is breathed into the lungs. Plasma levels peak within about 10 to 20 minutes. About 60% is bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down by glucuronidation and O-demethylation by CYP2C9 and CYP2C8. It leaves in both feces and urine. The terminal half-life is about 45 hours, while the effective half-life is about 7.5 hours, matching once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow from beta-2 activation outside the lung: fast heart rate and arrhythmias, tremor, low potassium and raised glucose. Paradoxical bronchospasm is rare. Olodaterol is not a rescue inhaler and is not approved for asthma because long-acting beta agonists without an inhaled steroid were linked to serious asthma events. Using it with another LABA raises the risk of overdose effects. There is no specific antidote; a cardioselective beta-blocker can be used with caution in severe cases.", "src": ["Striverdi Respimat (olodaterol) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "aclidinium": {"origin": "Aclidinium is a modern descendant of atropine, the alkaloid from plants such as belladonna and jimsonweed. For centuries people smoked Datura leaves to ease asthma, which worked because of their antimuscarinic action. Chemists later made quaternary ammonium versions that stay in the lungs and do not cross into the brain. Aclidinium was developed by Almirall in Spain to be broken down very quickly in the blood, so it acts in the airway with few effects elsewhere. Partnered with Forest Laboratories in the US, it was approved by the FDA in 2012 as Tudorza Pressair, a dry powder inhaler for COPD.", "timeline": [["1800s", "Smoking Datura (stramonium) for asthma is common in Western medicine."], ["2012", "FDA approves Tudorza Pressair (aclidinium) for COPD."], ["2019", "Aclidinium combined with formoterol (Duaklir Pressair) is approved."]], "cell": "In the airway, acetylcholine released from vagal nerves binds M3 muscarinic receptors on smooth muscle and glands. M3 is Gq-coupled, raising IP3 and calcium to contract the muscle and drive mucus secretion. Aclidinium competitively blocks these receptors. It binds all five muscarinic subtypes but dissociates much more slowly from M3 than from M2. M2 receptors sit on nerve endings and limit acetylcholine release, so letting M2 recover sooner is useful. Blocking M3 relaxes the airway and reduces mucus. Aclidinium is an ester that plasma butyrylcholinesterase breaks down quickly, which is the key to its low systemic anticholinergic effect.", "body": [["Airways", "Airways relax and stay open for about 12 hours, with less mucus."], ["Mouth", "Dry mouth is less common than with some antimuscarinics because little active drug circulates."], ["Eyes", "If the powder reaches the eyes, or in susceptible people, pupil dilation can worsen narrow-angle glaucoma."], ["Bladder", "Antimuscarinic action can make it harder to urinate, especially with an enlarged prostate."]], "journey": "Aclidinium is inhaled as a dry powder with a lactose carrier. It reaches the airways and is absorbed into the blood, peaking within about ten minutes. In plasma it is quickly hydrolyzed, mostly by butyrylcholinesterase, into an inactive alcohol and an inactive acid. So the amount of active drug in blood is very small. Its effective half-life is about five to eight hours, with dosing twice daily. Metabolites leave in urine and feces.", "sidefx": "Side effects reflect muscarinic blockade wherever the drug reaches. Eye exposure can widen the pupil and trigger narrow-angle glaucoma. Bladder muscarinic blockade can cause urinary retention. Rapid breakdown in plasma limits classic effects such as fast heart rate and constipation. Paradoxical bronchospasm can occur. Hypersensitivity is possible, including in people with severe milk protein allergy because of the lactose carrier. It is not a rescue inhaler. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "The quaternary ammonium group in drugs like aclidinium carries a permanent positive charge, which keeps them from crossing into the brain the way atropine does.", "src": ["Tudorza Pressair (aclidinium) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "revefenacin": {"origin": "Revefenacin was discovered by Theravance (later Theravance Biopharma), a California company focused on respiratory drugs, and developed with Mylan. Most long-acting muscarinic antagonists came as handheld inhalers, which can be hard for frail COPD patients to use. Revefenacin was designed to last 24 hours and to be given by nebulizer. Yupelri was approved by the FDA in 2018 as the first once-daily nebulized long-acting antimuscarinic for COPD.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Theravance discovers revefenacin as a long-acting antimuscarinic."], ["2018", "FDA approves Yupelri, a once-daily nebulized LAMA for COPD."]], "cell": "Revefenacin is a competitive antagonist at muscarinic receptors, with special effect at the M3 receptor on airway smooth muscle. Normally vagal acetylcholine activates Gq-coupled M3 receptors, increasing IP3 and calcium and causing contraction and mucus secretion. Revefenacin binds the receptor and leaves it very slowly, so bronchoconstriction is blocked for about 24 hours. It binds all five subtypes but stays longest at M3. In the liver it is hydrolyzed to an active metabolite that is less potent.", "body": [["Airways", "Bronchodilation over hours that lasts a full day, with less mucus."], ["Eyes", "Antimuscarinic action can worsen narrow-angle glaucoma if pressure rises."], ["Bladder", "Muscarinic blockade can worsen urinary retention, especially with prostate enlargement."], ["Mouth and throat", "Cough and upper airway irritation are among the common effects."]], "journey": "Revefenacin is inhaled as a nebulized solution. A small part of the dose reaches the blood, peaking shortly after nebulization. It is about 71% bound to plasma proteins. The liver hydrolyzes it to an active metabolite. The metabolite is taken up by the liver transporters OATP1B1 and OATP1B3, so drugs such as rifampin or cyclosporine that block them raise its exposure. It is not recommended in liver impairment. Elimination is mainly in feces. The terminal half-life ranges widely, about 22 to 70 hours.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow from muscarinic blockade. Eye pain, blurred vision or halos can signal narrow-angle glaucoma. Trouble urinating reflects bladder muscarinic blockade. Paradoxical bronchospasm and hypersensitivity can occur. Because it acts mostly in the lungs, dry mouth and systemic effects are relatively mild. It is not a rescue drug. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Yupelri (revefenacin) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "fluticasone-umeclidinium-vilanterol": {"origin": "Trelegy Ellipta combines three drugs from GSK's respiratory program into one inhaler. Fluticasone furoate is a newer member of the fluticasone family, made to stay in lung tissue for a full day. Umeclidinium is a long-acting antimuscarinic, and vilanterol is a 24-hour beta-2 agonist that GSK developed with Theravance. Before Trelegy, people with severe COPD often juggled two or three devices. In 2017 the FDA approved Trelegy as the first once-daily single-inhaler triple therapy for COPD, and an asthma indication followed in 2020. Trials showed fewer COPD flares with triple therapy than with dual combinations.", "timeline": [["2013", "Breo Ellipta (fluticasone furoate and vilanterol) and Anoro Ellipta (umeclidinium and vilanterol) are approved."], ["2017", "FDA approves Trelegy Ellipta, the first once-daily single-inhaler triple therapy for COPD."], ["2020", "Trelegy is approved for asthma."]], "cell": "Each part targets a different pathway. Fluticasone furoate binds the glucocorticoid receptor with high affinity; the complex moves to the nucleus and blocks NF-kB and AP-1, cutting cytokine production and inflammatory cell influx. Umeclidinium is a competitive antagonist at muscarinic receptors with slow release from M3 on airway smooth muscle, blocking acetylcholine's Gq-driven calcium rise and contraction. Vilanterol is a selective beta-2 agonist that activates Gs and adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP and protein kinase A activity, which relaxes airway smooth muscle. The two bronchodilators work on opposite sides of airway tone, so their effects add, while the steroid lowers inflammation that drives flares.", "body": [["Airways", "Two bronchodilators keep airways open for 24 hours while the steroid calms inflammation, reducing COPD flares."], ["Mouth and throat", "Steroid deposited in the mouth can cause thrush and voice changes."], ["Lungs (infection)", "Inhaled steroids raise the risk of pneumonia in people with COPD."], ["Heart", "Vilanterol can raise heart rate and cause palpitations."], ["Eyes and bladder", "Umeclidinium can worsen narrow-angle glaucoma and urinary retention; steroids add cataract and glaucoma risk."], ["Bones and adrenal glands", "Long-term steroid exposure can lower bone density and suppress cortisol production."]], "journey": "All three drugs are inhaled as a dry powder from one Ellipta device. Vilanterol and umeclidinium peak in blood within minutes, and fluticasone furoate within about an hour. Swallowed drug is poorly available because of first-pass metabolism. Fluticasone furoate and vilanterol are broken down by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise their levels. Umeclidinium is mainly metabolized by CYP2D6. All three are mostly eliminated in feces. Half-lives are about 24 hours for fluticasone furoate and about 11 hours for the other two, supporting once-daily use.", "sidefx": "Each part brings its own side effects. The steroid can cause thrush, pneumonia in COPD, adrenal suppression, bone loss and eye effects. Umeclidinium's muscarinic blockade can worsen glaucoma and urinary retention. Vilanterol's beta-2 action can cause fast heart rate, low potassium and high glucose. Paradoxical bronchospasm is rare. It is not a rescue inhaler and is not combined with other LABA or LAMA products. There is no single antidote.", "src": ["Trelegy Ellipta FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "reslizumab": {"origin": "Reslizumab came from research on interleukin-5, the cytokine that tells the bone marrow to make eosinophils and keeps them alive in tissue. In the 1990s researchers at Schering-Plough developed a humanized antibody against IL-5. Early asthma trials in unselected patients were disappointing. Later work showed these antibodies help mainly people whose asthma is driven by eosinophils. The drug passed through several companies and was finally developed by Teva, which ran trials in patients with high blood eosinophil counts. Cinqair was approved by the FDA in 2016 for severe eosinophilic asthma. It is the only anti-IL-5 antibody given by IV infusion.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Schering-Plough develops a humanized anti-IL-5 antibody."], ["2015", "Mepolizumab, the first anti-IL-5 antibody, is approved."], ["2016", "FDA approves reslizumab (Cinqair) for severe eosinophilic asthma."]], "cell": "IL-5 is made by Th2 cells and group 2 innate lymphoid cells. It binds the IL-5 receptor alpha chain on eosinophils and their precursors, which then pairs with the common beta chain and activates JAK-STAT signaling. That signal drives eosinophil production, maturation, activation and survival. Reslizumab is a humanized IgG4 antibody that binds circulating IL-5 and prevents it from attaching to IL-5 receptor alpha. Without IL-5, fewer eosinophils are made and existing eosinophils die by apoptosis. Fewer eosinophils mean less release of toxic granule proteins and leukotrienes that damage the airway and drive asthma attacks.", "body": [["Blood", "Blood eosinophil counts drop within days."], ["Airways", "Fewer eosinophils in the airway lining reduce inflammation, mucus and flares, with modest improvement in lung function."], ["Immune system", "Eosinophils help fight parasitic worms, so helminth infections are treated before starting."], ["Muscles", "Muscle aches and a rise in CPK have been reported."], ["Whole body", "Anaphylaxis can occur during or soon after infusion."]], "journey": "Reslizumab is given as an IV infusion in a healthcare setting, so it enters the blood completely and peaks at the end of the infusion. As a large protein, it stays mostly in the blood and extracellular fluid. Like other IgG antibodies, it is broken down slowly by protein-digesting enzymes throughout the body, not by the liver or kidneys. Its half-life is about 24 days, so it is given every four weeks.", "sidefx": "The boxed warning is anaphylaxis, an immune reaction to the foreign protein, so patients are watched after each infusion. Lower eosinophils weaken defense against parasitic worms. A small imbalance in cancers was seen in trials, although a cause was not shown. Muscle aches and CPK rise are reported. It does not treat acute attacks, and steroids are not stopped suddenly. There is no antidote; effects fade as the antibody is cleared over weeks.", "src": ["Cinqair (reslizumab) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "tezepelumab": {"origin": "Tezepelumab targets thymic stromal lymphopoietin (TSLP), a cytokine first found in the 1990s in the supernatant of a mouse thymic stromal cell line. Researchers later learned that airway lining cells release TSLP when they meet allergens, viruses or pollution, and that it sits at the top of the inflammatory cascade. Amgen and AstraZeneca developed a human antibody against it. Because TSLP acts upstream of many pathways, the drug reduced asthma attacks in people with and without high eosinophil counts. Tezspire was approved by the FDA in 2021 for severe asthma with no restriction to a specific phenotype or biomarker, a first among asthma biologics. An approval for chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyps followed.", "timeline": [["1990s", "TSLP is identified from a mouse thymic stromal cell line."], ["2021", "FDA approves Tezspire (tezepelumab) for severe asthma."]], "cell": "When the airway epithelium is stressed by allergens, viruses or smoke, its cells release alarmins: TSLP, IL-33 and IL-25. TSLP binds a receptor made of the TSLP receptor chain plus IL-7 receptor alpha on dendritic cells, mast cells, innate lymphoid cells and T cells. This drives Th2 responses with IL-4, IL-5 and IL-13, which in turn raise IgE, recruit eosinophils and increase nitric oxide production. TSLP also supports some non-type 2 inflammation. Tezepelumab is a human IgG2 lambda antibody that binds TSLP and prevents it from reaching its receptor. Blocking this signal at the top of the cascade lowers eosinophils, IgE and FeNO together.", "body": [["Airways", "Fewer asthma attacks and better lung function, regardless of baseline eosinophil level."], ["Blood", "Blood eosinophils and IgE levels fall over weeks."], ["Nose and sinuses", "Reduced inflammation can shrink nasal polyps."], ["Immune system", "Defense against parasitic worms may be weaker, and live vaccines are avoided."], ["Joints and throat", "Joint pain, back pain and sore throat are among reported effects."]], "journey": "Tezepelumab is injected under the skin. It is absorbed slowly through the lymphatic system and peaks about three to ten days later. It is spread mainly in the blood and tissue fluid. Like other IgG antibodies, it is broken down by protein digestion throughout the body, not by liver enzymes or kidneys. The half-life is about 26 days, which supports dosing every four weeks.", "sidefx": "Because TSLP helps drive immune responses, blocking it may weaken defense against parasitic worms, so these are treated first. Hypersensitivity reactions such as rash or allergic conjunctivitis can occur, and anaphylaxis is rare. Sore throat, joint pain and back pain are common. It does not stop acute bronchospasm, and steroids are not stopped abruptly. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Tezepelumab acts on the airway's 'alarm signal' itself, which is why it can help asthma that is not driven by eosinophils.", "src": ["Tezspire (tezepelumab) FDA label", "Menzies-Gow A, et al. NAVIGATOR trial, N Engl J Med 2021"]}, "tobramycin-inhaled": {"origin": "Tobramycin was isolated from the soil bacterium Streptomyces tenebrarius in the 1960s and developed by Eli Lilly, which introduced injectable tobramycin in the 1970s. It was strong against Pseudomonas aeruginosa, the bacterium that chronically infects the lungs of people with cystic fibrosis. IV aminoglycosides reach low levels in thick sputum and can damage the ears and kidneys. Researchers, including Bonnie Ramsey and colleagues in Seattle, showed that breathing tobramycin directly into the lungs gives very high sputum levels with little in the blood. PathoGenesis Corporation developed a preservative-free solution, TOBI, approved by the FDA in 1997. A dry powder form, TOBI Podhaler, followed in 2013.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Tobramycin is isolated from Streptomyces tenebrarius."], ["1970s", "Injectable tobramycin is introduced."], ["1997", "FDA approves TOBI inhalation solution for cystic fibrosis."], ["1999", "A key NEJM trial confirms benefit of inhaled tobramycin in CF."], ["2013", "TOBI Podhaler dry powder is approved."]], "cell": "Tobramycin is a positively charged aminoglycoside. It first binds negatively charged lipopolysaccharide on the outer membrane of gram-negative bacteria, then crosses the inner membrane by an oxygen-dependent transport process, which is why it does not work against anaerobes. Inside, it binds irreversibly to the 16S rRNA of the 30S ribosomal subunit. This blocks formation of the initiation complex and causes misreading of mRNA, so faulty proteins are made. Some of these proteins insert into the membrane and make it leakier, letting in more drug. Killing is concentration-dependent with a post-antibiotic effect, so the very high sputum levels from inhalation kill Pseudomonas well.", "body": [["Lungs", "Less Pseudomonas in sputum, better lung function and fewer hospital stays in cystic fibrosis."], ["Airways", "Cough, voice changes and bronchospasm can occur from local irritation."], ["Ears", "Even small amounts in blood can damage hair cells in the inner ear, causing tinnitus or hearing loss."], ["Kidneys", "Absorbed drug is taken up by proximal tubule cells and can injure them, though this is uncommon with inhalation."], ["Nerves and muscles", "Aminoglycosides can worsen neuromuscular weakness, as in myasthenia gravis."]], "journey": "Inhaled tobramycin lands on airway surfaces and sputum, where levels peak within about ten minutes and stay far above the level needed to kill Pseudomonas. Only a small amount crosses into the blood, peaking in about an hour. Tobramycin is not metabolized. The absorbed fraction leaves unchanged through the kidneys with a serum half-life of about two hours. Much of the unabsorbed drug is coughed up in sputum. Treatment is usually given in alternating on and off months to reduce resistance.", "sidefx": "Local airway irritation explains cough, voice changes and bronchospasm. Ototoxicity happens because aminoglycosides build up in inner ear fluid and damage hair cells through free radical injury; the damage can be permanent. Nephrotoxicity reflects uptake into proximal tubule cells. Aminoglycosides also block presynaptic calcium entry at the neuromuscular junction, worsening weakness. They can cross the placenta and cause congenital deafness. Combining with loop diuretics or other nephrotoxic drugs increases risk. There is no antidote; calcium can partly reverse neuromuscular blockade.", "src": ["TOBI (tobramycin inhalation solution) FDA label", "Ramsey BW, et al. N Engl J Med 1999", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "amikacin-liposome-inhaled": {"origin": "Amikacin was made in 1972 in Japan by Hiroshi Kawaguchi and colleagues at Bristol-Banyu, who chemically added a side chain to kanamycin, a Streptomyces antibiotic. The side chain shields the molecule from many bacterial enzymes that inactivate aminoglycosides. Decades later, Insmed packaged amikacin inside tiny lipid spheres called liposomes so it could be inhaled and taken up by the lung's macrophages, where Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) bacteria hide. Arikayce was approved by the FDA in 2018 under accelerated approval for refractory MAC lung disease, the first drug approved through the Limited Population Pathway for Antibacterial and Antifungal Drugs.", "timeline": [["1972", "Amikacin is synthesized from kanamycin in Japan."], ["2018", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Arikayce for refractory MAC lung disease."]], "cell": "Amikacin enters bacteria by binding the cell surface and then crossing the inner membrane through an energy-dependent process. Inside, it binds irreversibly to the 16S rRNA in the 30S ribosomal subunit. This causes mRNA misreading and stops normal protein synthesis, which kills the bacteria. Its extra side chain blocks access for most aminoglycoside-modifying enzymes, so it often works when other aminoglycosides fail. MAC bacteria live inside alveolar macrophages and in biofilms. The liposome form is taken up by macrophages, which carry the drug to where the bacteria live. Liposomes also help the drug move through mucus and biofilm.", "body": [["Lungs", "Over months of therapy, more patients' sputum cultures turn negative for MAC."], ["Airways", "Voice change, cough, bronchospasm and coughing up blood can occur from local effects."], ["Lung tissue", "Hypersensitivity pneumonitis, an inflammatory reaction in the lung, can occur."], ["Ears", "Absorbed amikacin can damage inner ear hair cells, causing hearing loss, tinnitus or dizziness."], ["Kidneys", "Uptake into proximal tubule cells can cause kidney injury."]], "journey": "The liposome suspension is inhaled using a specific nebulizer system once daily. Liposomes deposit in the airways and alveoli, where macrophages take them up. Amikacin is slowly released inside the lung, so lung levels are high while blood levels stay modest, peaking about one to four hours after inhalation. Amikacin is not metabolized. The absorbed portion leaves unchanged in urine, with a serum half-life of about six hours. Much of the drug stays in the lung or is coughed up.", "sidefx": "The boxed warning covers respiratory reactions such as hypersensitivity pneumonitis, bleeding, bronchospasm and worsening of underlying lung disease. These come from direct delivery of drug and lipid to diseased airways. Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity reflect aminoglycoside build-up in inner ear and kidney cells. Neuromuscular blockade can worsen weakness in myasthenia gravis. Aminoglycosides can harm the fetus's hearing. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Arikayce (amikacin liposome inhalation suspension) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "zileuton": {"origin": "Zileuton grew out of the discovery of leukotrienes. In the 1930s researchers described a 'slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis' that caused long-lasting airway contraction. In 1979 Bengt Samuelsson's group in Sweden showed this substance was a mix of leukotrienes, made from arachidonic acid by the enzyme 5-lipoxygenase. Samuelsson shared the 1982 Nobel Prize for work on these lipid signals. Abbott Laboratories developed zileuton to block 5-lipoxygenase directly. Zyflo was approved by the FDA in 1996 as the first drug to stop leukotriene synthesis. An extended-release form, Zyflo CR, followed in 2007.", "timeline": [["1938", "Slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis is described."], ["1979", "Samuelsson's group identifies leukotrienes."], ["1982", "Samuelsson shares the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for this work."], ["1996", "FDA approves Zyflo (zileuton) for asthma."], ["2007", "Extended-release Zyflo CR is approved."]], "cell": "When inflammatory cells such as mast cells, eosinophils and neutrophils are activated, phospholipase A2 releases arachidonic acid from membranes. The enzyme 5-lipoxygenase, helped by 5-lipoxygenase-activating protein (FLAP), converts arachidonic acid into 5-HPETE and then leukotriene A4. LTA4 becomes LTB4, which attracts neutrophils, or the cysteinyl leukotrienes LTC4, LTD4 and LTE4. Cysteinyl leukotrienes act on CysLT1 receptors to contract airway smooth muscle, increase mucus and make vessels leak. Zileuton inhibits 5-lipoxygenase by chelating its iron, so none of these leukotrienes are made. This differs from montelukast, which only blocks the CysLT1 receptor.", "body": [["Airways", "Less bronchoconstriction, swelling and mucus, with modest improvement in lung function and symptoms."], ["Immune cells", "Less LTB4 means fewer neutrophils drawn in; eosinophil recruitment also falls."], ["Liver", "ALT can rise, usually in the first months, and rare serious liver injury occurs."], ["Brain", "Sleep disturbance and changes in mood or behavior have been reported."], ["Gut", "Dyspepsia, nausea and abdominal pain are common."]], "journey": "Zileuton is taken by mouth and absorbed quickly, peaking in about 1.7 hours. It is about 93% bound to plasma proteins. The liver metabolizes it through CYP1A2, CYP2C9 and CYP3A4 and by glucuronidation. Metabolites leave in urine. The half-life is short, about 2.5 hours, which is why the immediate-release form needs dosing four times daily. It inhibits CYP1A2 and can raise theophylline and warfarin levels.", "sidefx": "Liver injury is the main concern. It is thought to relate to how the liver processes the drug, and ALT is monitored for the first year. Neuropsychiatric events such as sleep problems and behavior changes are reported with leukotriene modifiers. Because zileuton inhibits CYP1A2, levels of theophylline can rise and cause toxicity. It is not a rescue drug for acute attacks. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Zyflo and Zyflo CR (zileuton) FDA label", "Samuelsson B. Science 1983", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "mometasone-formoterol": {"origin": "Dulera pairs two drugs with separate histories. Mometasone furoate is a potent synthetic corticosteroid developed by Schering-Plough, first sold as a skin cream (Elocon) in the 1980s and later as a nasal spray and asthma inhaler. Formoterol is a long-acting beta-2 agonist discovered by Yamanouchi in Japan in the 1970s, notable for working within minutes yet lasting about 12 hours. Combining a steroid with a long-acting bronchodilator in one inhaler had proven useful with earlier products. Dulera was approved by the FDA in 2010 for asthma and is now marketed by Merck, which merged with Schering-Plough in 2009.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Formoterol is developed by Yamanouchi in Japan."], ["1980s", "Mometasone furoate is introduced as a topical steroid."], ["2010", "FDA approves Dulera (mometasone and formoterol) for asthma."]], "cell": "Mometasone furoate binds the glucocorticoid receptor in airway cells with very high affinity. The receptor complex moves to the nucleus, where it blocks the pro-inflammatory transcription factors NF-kB and AP-1 and switches on anti-inflammatory genes. This reduces Th2 cytokines, eosinophils, mast cells and airway swelling. Formoterol binds beta-2 receptors on airway smooth muscle, activates Gs and adenylyl cyclase, and raises cAMP. Protein kinase A lowers calcium and relaxes the muscle quickly. Steroids also increase beta-2 receptor production, and beta agonists may help the glucocorticoid receptor move to the nucleus, so the two parts support each other.", "body": [["Airways", "Less inflammation over one to two weeks plus fast, 12-hour bronchodilation, giving fewer asthma attacks."], ["Mouth and throat", "Steroid deposited in the mouth can cause thrush and hoarseness."], ["Heart", "Formoterol can raise heart rate and cause palpitations."], ["Muscles and electrolytes", "Beta-2 activation can cause tremor, low potassium and higher glucose."], ["Adrenal glands and bones", "Absorbed steroid at high exposure can suppress cortisol production and lower bone density."], ["Eyes", "Long-term steroid exposure is linked to cataracts and glaucoma."]], "journey": "Both drugs are inhaled from a pressurized metered-dose inhaler. Formoterol is absorbed and peaks in blood within about an hour; it is broken down by glucuronidation and O-demethylation, with a half-life of about ten hours. Mometasone is absorbed slowly from the lung; swallowed drug is almost all destroyed by CYP3A4 on first pass through the liver. Mometasone has a long half-life of about 25 hours, is highly protein bound, and leaves mainly in feces.", "sidefx": "The steroid part can cause thrush by lowering local immunity, along with adrenal suppression, bone loss, slowed growth in children and eye changes. The formoterol part causes beta-2 effects outside the lung: fast heart rate, tremor, low potassium and high glucose. Paradoxical bronchospasm is rare. It is not used to relieve acute attacks. The old boxed warning on LABA asthma deaths was removed from ICS/LABA combinations in 2017 after safety trials. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Dulera (mometasone furoate and formoterol) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "fluticasone-furoate-vilanterol": {"origin": "Breo Ellipta was developed by GSK with Theravance as a once-daily successor to Advair, which combines fluticasone propionate and salmeterol twice daily. Fluticasone furoate is a different ester of fluticasone that binds the receptor very strongly and stays in lung tissue for about a day. Vilanterol is a beta-2 agonist designed for 24-hour action. Both were put in the Ellipta dry powder inhaler. Breo was approved by the FDA in 2013 for COPD and in 2015 for asthma. A large trial in Salford, England, tested it in everyday practice and found better asthma control than usual care.", "timeline": [["2000", "Advair, the twice-daily fluticasone propionate and salmeterol inhaler, is approved in the US."], ["2013", "FDA approves Breo Ellipta for COPD."], ["2015", "Breo Ellipta is approved for asthma."]], "cell": "Fluticasone furoate binds the glucocorticoid receptor with very high affinity and is retained in lung tissue. The receptor complex enters the nucleus, blocks NF-kB and AP-1 driven inflammatory genes and turns on anti-inflammatory genes. Fewer cytokines are made, so eosinophils, T cells and mast cells decline and swelling falls. Vilanterol is a selective beta-2 agonist that activates Gs and adenylyl cyclase on airway smooth muscle. Higher cAMP activates protein kinase A, which lowers calcium and relaxes the muscle. Vilanterol has a fast onset and lasts 24 hours.", "body": [["Airways", "Once-daily bronchodilation plus reduced inflammation give fewer flares in asthma and COPD."], ["Lungs (infection)", "In COPD, inhaled steroids raise pneumonia risk."], ["Mouth and throat", "Thrush and hoarseness can occur from steroid deposited in the mouth."], ["Heart", "Vilanterol can increase heart rate and cause palpitations."], ["Bones and adrenal glands", "Long-term steroid exposure can lower bone density, raise fracture risk and suppress cortisol production."]], "journey": "Both drugs are inhaled as dry powder with a lactose carrier. Vilanterol peaks in blood within about ten minutes and fluticasone furoate within about an hour. Swallowed drug has very low availability because CYP3A4 in the gut and liver breaks it down. Both are metabolized by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ketoconazole raise their levels. Fluticasone furoate has a half-life of about 24 hours and leaves mainly in feces; vilanterol leaves in urine and feces as metabolites.", "sidefx": "Steroid effects include thrush, pneumonia in COPD, adrenal suppression, bone loss and eye changes such as cataracts and glaucoma. Beta-2 effects from vilanterol include fast heart rate, tremor, low potassium and high glucose. Paradoxical bronchospasm is rare. The lactose carrier contains milk proteins, which can cause reactions in severe milk allergy. It is not a rescue inhaler. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Breo Ellipta (fluticasone furoate and vilanterol) FDA label", "Woodcock A, et al. Salford Lung Study, Lancet 2017"]}, "budesonide-glycopyrrolate-formoterol": {"origin": "Breztri Aerosphere puts three established drugs into one pressurized inhaler. Budesonide is a corticosteroid developed by Astra (later AstraZeneca) in Sweden in the 1970s. Glycopyrrolate is a quaternary antimuscarinic first marketed in 1961 for peptic ulcers and later used in anesthesia. Formoterol is a long-acting beta-2 agonist from Japan. The Aerosphere technology, developed by Pearl Therapeutics (acquired by AstraZeneca in 2013), uses porous phospholipid particles that keep the drugs evenly suspended. Breztri was approved by the FDA in 2020 for COPD.", "timeline": [["1961", "Glycopyrrolate is first approved, for peptic ulcer disease."], ["1970s", "Budesonide is developed by Astra in Sweden."], ["2016", "Bevespi Aerosphere (glycopyrrolate and formoterol) is approved."], ["2020", "FDA approves Breztri Aerosphere for COPD."]], "cell": "Budesonide binds the glucocorticoid receptor; in the nucleus it blocks NF-kB and AP-1 and lowers inflammatory cytokines, reducing airway inflammation. Glycopyrrolate competitively blocks muscarinic receptors, mainly M3 on airway smooth muscle and glands. M3 is Gq-coupled, so blocking it prevents the IP3 and calcium rise that causes contraction and mucus secretion. Formoterol activates Gs-coupled beta-2 receptors, raising cAMP and protein kinase A activity, which relaxes smooth muscle within minutes. The two bronchodilators work through opposite controls of airway tone, so they add together, while budesonide lowers the inflammation that causes flares.", "body": [["Airways", "Dual bronchodilation plus anti-inflammatory action reduces COPD flares and breathlessness."], ["Lungs (infection)", "Inhaled steroids raise pneumonia risk in COPD."], ["Mouth and throat", "Thrush and voice changes can occur from steroid in the mouth."], ["Eyes and bladder", "Glycopyrrolate can worsen narrow-angle glaucoma and urinary retention."], ["Heart", "Formoterol can raise heart rate; glycopyrrolate can also add to it."], ["Bones and adrenal glands", "Long-term steroid exposure can thin bones and suppress cortisol production."]], "journey": "All three are inhaled from one metered-dose inhaler. They peak in blood within about an hour. Budesonide that is swallowed is mostly destroyed by CYP3A4 on first pass. Budesonide has a half-life of about five hours. Glycopyrrolate is barely metabolized and leaves mainly unchanged in urine, with a half-life of about 12 to 15 hours. Formoterol is cleared by glucuronidation and O-demethylation with a half-life of about ten hours. Dosing is twice daily.", "sidefx": "Each part adds its own risks. Budesonide can cause thrush, pneumonia, adrenal suppression and bone loss. Glycopyrrolate's muscarinic blockade can worsen glaucoma and urinary retention and cause dry mouth. Formoterol can cause fast heart rate, low potassium and high glucose. Paradoxical bronchospasm is rare. It is not a rescue inhaler. There is no single antidote.", "src": ["Breztri Aerosphere FDA label", "Rabe KF, et al. ETHOS trial, N Engl J Med 2020"]}, "glycopyrrolate-formoterol": {"origin": "Glycopyrrolate was first marketed in 1961 by A. H. Robins as Robinul for peptic ulcers, because blocking muscarinic receptors reduces stomach acid. It later became a staple in anesthesia to dry secretions. Formoterol is a long-acting beta-2 agonist from Japan. Pearl Therapeutics, a California company later bought by AstraZeneca, developed a co-suspension inhaler that holds both drugs on light, porous phospholipid particles. Bevespi Aerosphere was approved by the FDA in 2016 for COPD as a dual bronchodilator in a pressurized inhaler.", "timeline": [["1961", "Glycopyrrolate (Robinul) is first approved."], ["1970s", "Formoterol is developed in Japan."], ["2016", "FDA approves Bevespi Aerosphere for COPD."]], "cell": "Glycopyrrolate is a quaternary ammonium antagonist at muscarinic receptors. In the airway it blocks M3 receptors on smooth muscle and glands, so acetylcholine from vagal nerves can no longer trigger the Gq, IP3 and calcium signal that causes contraction and mucus secretion. Formoterol activates beta-2 receptors on the same muscle, turning on Gs, adenylyl cyclase and cAMP, which activates protein kinase A and relaxes the muscle. One drug removes the contracting signal and the other adds a relaxing signal, so bronchodilation is greater than with either alone.", "body": [["Airways", "Faster and greater bronchodilation in COPD, improving FEV1 and breathlessness."], ["Mouth", "Muscarinic blockade can cause dry mouth."], ["Bladder", "Antimuscarinic action can worsen urinary retention."], ["Eyes", "Glycopyrrolate can raise eye pressure in narrow-angle glaucoma."], ["Heart", "Formoterol can raise heart rate and cause palpitations."], ["Electrolytes and glucose", "Beta-2 activation can lower potassium and raise glucose."]], "journey": "Both drugs are inhaled from a metered-dose inhaler and reach the blood within minutes. Glycopyrrolate is permanently charged, so it is poorly absorbed from the gut and does not cross into the brain; it is barely metabolized and leaves mainly unchanged in urine with a half-life of about 12 hours. Formoterol is cleared by glucuronidation and O-demethylation with a half-life of about ten hours. Dosing is twice daily.", "sidefx": "Glycopyrrolate's muscarinic blockade explains dry mouth, urinary retention and glaucoma risk. Formoterol's beta-2 stimulation explains fast heart rate, tremor, low potassium and high glucose. Paradoxical bronchospasm is rare. It is not approved for asthma or for rescue use. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Bevespi Aerosphere FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "albuterol-budesonide": {"origin": "For decades the standard asthma rescue inhaler was albuterol alone. Albuterol (salbutamol) was developed in 1966 by David Jack's team at Allen & Hanburys in England. Budesonide is an inhaled steroid developed by Astra in Sweden in the 1970s. Researchers learned that many asthma attacks happen because inflammation goes untreated, and that people often reach only for their rescue inhaler. Adding a steroid to every rescue puff was studied in the MANDALA trial, which showed fewer severe attacks than albuterol alone. AstraZeneca and Avillion developed Airsupra, approved by the FDA in 2023 as the first albuterol and inhaled steroid rescue inhaler in the US.", "timeline": [["1966", "Albuterol (salbutamol) is developed in England."], ["1970s", "Budesonide is developed by Astra in Sweden."], ["2022", "The MANDALA trial reports fewer severe attacks with albuterol plus budesonide."], ["2023", "FDA approves Airsupra as an as-needed rescue inhaler."]], "cell": "Albuterol is a short-acting selective beta-2 agonist. It binds beta-2 receptors on airway smooth muscle, activates Gs and adenylyl cyclase and raises cAMP. Protein kinase A then lowers calcium and reduces myosin light chain kinase activity, relaxing the muscle within minutes. Budesonide binds the glucocorticoid receptor; the complex moves to the nucleus, blocks NF-kB and AP-1 and switches on anti-inflammatory genes, lowering cytokines and inflammatory cells in the airway. Delivering both at once means that each time a person needs relief, inflammation is also being treated.", "body": [["Airways", "Fast relief of bronchospasm with an added anti-inflammatory dose, reducing severe attacks."], ["Heart", "Albuterol can raise heart rate and cause palpitations."], ["Muscles", "Tremor is a common effect of beta-2 activation in skeletal muscle."], ["Electrolytes and glucose", "Potassium shifts into cells and glucose can rise."], ["Mouth and throat", "Steroid in the mouth can cause thrush and hoarseness."], ["Adrenal glands", "Frequent use adds up steroid exposure, which can suppress cortisol production."]], "journey": "Both drugs are inhaled from a metered-dose inhaler. Albuterol works within about five minutes and lasts four to six hours. Swallowed albuterol is sulfated in the gut wall and liver, and it leaves in urine with a half-life of about four to five hours. Budesonide is absorbed from the lung; swallowed budesonide is largely destroyed by CYP3A4 on first pass. Budesonide's half-life is about two to four hours, with metabolites leaving in urine and feces.", "sidefx": "Albuterol's beta-2 effects cause tremor, fast heart rate, low potassium and high glucose. Budesonide can cause thrush, voice change and, with heavy use, adrenal suppression, bone loss and eye effects. Paradoxical bronchospasm is rare. Needing it more often is a sign asthma is worsening. It is not a daily controller. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Airsupra (albuterol and budesonide) FDA label", "Papi A, et al. MANDALA trial, N Engl J Med 2022"]}, "tiotropium-olodaterol": {"origin": "Stiolto Respimat combines two once-daily bronchodilators from Boehringer Ingelheim. Tiotropium, approved in the US in 2004 as Spiriva, became the best-known long-acting antimuscarinic for COPD. Olodaterol, a 24-hour beta-2 agonist, was approved alone in 2014. Both are delivered by the Respimat soft-mist inhaler, which produces a slow cloud that is easier to inhale than a fast spray. Stiolto was approved by the FDA in 2015 for COPD, giving patients two bronchodilators in one daily inhalation.", "timeline": [["2004", "Tiotropium (Spiriva HandiHaler) is approved in the US."], ["2014", "Olodaterol (Striverdi Respimat) is approved."], ["2015", "FDA approves Stiolto Respimat for COPD."]], "cell": "Tiotropium is a quaternary ammonium muscarinic antagonist. It binds all muscarinic subtypes but dissociates very slowly from M3 and faster from M2, so M3 on airway smooth muscle stays blocked for over 24 hours. This prevents acetylcholine from triggering the Gq, IP3 and calcium signal that contracts the airway and drives mucus secretion. Olodaterol is a selective beta-2 agonist that activates Gs and adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP and protein kinase A to relax the muscle. Blocking contraction and promoting relaxation together gives more bronchodilation than either drug alone.", "body": [["Airways", "Sustained 24-hour bronchodilation with improved FEV1 and breathlessness."], ["Mouth", "Dry mouth is a common antimuscarinic effect."], ["Eyes", "Mist sprayed into the eyes can dilate the pupil and trigger angle-closure glaucoma."], ["Bladder", "Muscarinic blockade can worsen urinary retention."], ["Heart", "Beta-2 stimulation can raise heart rate and cause arrhythmias."]], "journey": "Each daily dose is two puffs of soft mist. Both drugs reach peak blood levels within minutes. Tiotropium is minimally metabolized and is excreted mainly unchanged in urine, so kidney impairment raises levels; its terminal half-life is about five to six days, with an effective half-life near 25 hours. Olodaterol is cleared by glucuronidation and O-demethylation by CYP2C9 and CYP2C8 and leaves in urine and feces, with a terminal half-life of about 45 hours.", "sidefx": "Tiotropium's muscarinic blockade causes dry mouth and can worsen glaucoma and urinary retention. Olodaterol's beta-2 stimulation can cause fast heart rate, low potassium and high glucose. Paradoxical bronchospasm and angioedema are rare. It is not for acute attacks or for asthma. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Stiolto Respimat FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "umeclidinium-vilanterol": {"origin": "Anoro Ellipta was developed by GSK with Theravance and approved by the FDA in 2013 as the first once-daily inhaler in the US combining a long-acting antimuscarinic and a long-acting beta-2 agonist for COPD. Umeclidinium was designed to bind muscarinic receptors for a full day. Vilanterol was designed as a 24-hour beta-2 agonist and is also used in Breo and Trelegy. The Ellipta dry powder device holds each drug in a separate blister strip that opens at the same time.", "timeline": [["2013", "FDA approves Anoro Ellipta, the first once-daily LAMA/LABA inhaler in the US."], ["2014", "Umeclidinium alone (Incruse Ellipta) is approved."]], "cell": "Umeclidinium competitively blocks muscarinic receptors and leaves M3 on airway smooth muscle slowly. This stops acetylcholine from activating the Gq, IP3 and calcium pathway that contracts the airway. Vilanterol binds beta-2 receptors on the same muscle, activating Gs and adenylyl cyclase and raising cAMP. Protein kinase A then lowers calcium and relaxes the muscle. Removing the contracting signal and adding a relaxing one gives more bronchodilation than either drug alone, and both last about 24 hours.", "body": [["Airways", "Once-daily bronchodilation improves FEV1 and breathlessness in COPD."], ["Throat and sinuses", "Sore throat, sinusitis and upper respiratory infections are common."], ["Bladder", "Muscarinic blockade can worsen urinary retention."], ["Eyes", "Antimuscarinic action can raise eye pressure in narrow-angle glaucoma."], ["Heart", "Vilanterol can cause fast heart rate and, rarely, atrial fibrillation."], ["Gut", "Constipation and diarrhea can occur, partly from muscarinic effects on gut motility."]], "journey": "Both drugs are inhaled as a dry powder with a lactose carrier and reach peak blood levels within about 5 to 15 minutes. Umeclidinium is metabolized mainly by CYP2D6 and by glucuronidation and is excreted mostly in feces. Vilanterol is metabolized by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise its level, and leaves in urine and feces. Both have half-lives of about 11 hours, and the effect lasts 24 hours.", "sidefx": "Umeclidinium's antimuscarinic action explains urinary retention and glaucoma risk. Vilanterol's beta-2 action explains fast heart rate, palpitations, low potassium and high glucose. Paradoxical bronchospasm is rare. The lactose carrier contains milk proteins. It is not approved for asthma or rescue use. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Anoro Ellipta (umeclidinium and vilanterol) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "aclidinium-formoterol": {"origin": "Duaklir Pressair combines two bronchodilators in a breath-actuated dry powder inhaler. Aclidinium was developed by Almirall in Spain as an antimuscarinic that is rapidly broken down in blood. Formoterol is a fast-acting long-acting beta-2 agonist developed in Japan in the 1970s. The combination was first approved in Europe and was approved by the FDA in 2019 for COPD. The Pressair device gives a click and a color change to show that a full dose was inhaled.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Formoterol is developed in Japan."], ["2012", "Aclidinium (Tudorza Pressair) is approved in the US."], ["2019", "FDA approves Duaklir Pressair for COPD."]], "cell": "Aclidinium competitively blocks muscarinic receptors, staying longer at M3 than at M2. Blocking M3 on airway smooth muscle prevents acetylcholine's Gq, IP3 and calcium signal, so the airway does not contract and glands secrete less mucus. Formoterol activates beta-2 receptors on the same muscle, turning on Gs, adenylyl cyclase and cAMP, which activates protein kinase A and relaxes the muscle within minutes. The two actions add together. Aclidinium is quickly hydrolyzed in plasma, which keeps its effects mostly in the lungs.", "body": [["Airways", "Twice-daily dual bronchodilation with fast onset, improving FEV1 in COPD."], ["Bladder", "Muscarinic blockade can worsen urinary retention."], ["Eyes", "Antimuscarinic action can worsen narrow-angle glaucoma."], ["Heart", "Formoterol can raise heart rate and cause palpitations."], ["Electrolytes and glucose", "Beta-2 activation can lower potassium and raise glucose."]], "journey": "The powder is inhaled and both drugs reach blood within about five to ten minutes. Aclidinium is rapidly hydrolyzed by plasma esterases into inactive products, so little active drug circulates; its effective half-life is about five to eight hours. Formoterol is broken down by glucuronidation and O-demethylation and has a half-life of about ten hours. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. Dosing is twice daily.", "sidefx": "Aclidinium's antimuscarinic action explains urinary retention and glaucoma risk, though fast breakdown in plasma limits systemic effects. Formoterol's beta-2 effects cause fast heart rate, tremor, low potassium and high glucose. Paradoxical bronchospasm and hypersensitivity are rare; the lactose carrier contains milk proteins. It is not for asthma or rescue use. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Duaklir Pressair (aclidinium and formoterol) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "albuterol-ipratropium": {"origin": "This combination pairs two lines of bronchodilator history. Ipratropium descends from atropine, the alkaloid in belladonna and Datura, plants once smoked to relieve asthma. In the 1970s Boehringer Ingelheim made ipratropium, a quaternary form of atropine that stays in the airways and does not enter the brain. Albuterol (salbutamol) was developed in 1966 by David Jack's group at Allen & Hanburys in England as a selective beta-2 agonist. Studies showed the two together opened airways more than either alone in COPD. Combivent metered-dose inhaler was approved in the US in 1996, the nebulized form (DuoNeb) in 2001 and Combivent Respimat in 2011. The DuoNeb brand is discontinued but generic nebulizer solution remains widely used in EMS and hospitals.", "timeline": [["1966", "Albuterol is developed at Allen & Hanburys."], ["1970s", "Boehringer Ingelheim develops ipratropium from atropine."], ["1996", "Combivent inhaler is approved in the US."], ["2001", "The nebulized combination (DuoNeb) is approved."], ["2011", "Combivent Respimat is approved."]], "cell": "Albuterol binds beta-2 receptors on bronchial smooth muscle and activates Gs and adenylyl cyclase. Higher cAMP activates protein kinase A, which lowers intracellular calcium and inhibits myosin light chain kinase, relaxing the muscle within minutes. Ipratropium is a quaternary muscarinic antagonist that blocks M1, M2 and M3 receptors. Blocking M3 prevents acetylcholine from triggering the Gq, IP3 and calcium signal that contracts the airway and drives mucus secretion. Vagal tone is a large part of airway narrowing in COPD, so this helps. The two drugs act through different pathways and give more bronchodilation together than alone.", "body": [["Airways", "Fast, additive bronchodilation with less mucus, useful in COPD and acute bronchospasm."], ["Heart", "Albuterol raises heart rate and can cause palpitations and ECG changes."], ["Muscles", "Tremor and nervousness come from beta-2 activation."], ["Electrolytes and glucose", "Potassium shifts into cells and glucose rises."], ["Mouth", "Ipratropium blocks muscarinic receptors on salivary glands and causes dry mouth."], ["Eyes", "Mist escaping a mask into the eyes can dilate the pupil and trigger angle-closure glaucoma."]], "journey": "Inhaled as a mist or spray, both drugs act directly on the airways. Albuterol works within about 5 to 15 minutes and lasts four to five hours. The swallowed portion is sulfated in the gut wall and liver, and albuterol leaves in urine with a half-life of about four to six hours. Ipratropium is a charged molecule that is barely absorbed from the lung or gut. Most of it is swallowed and leaves in feces. Its effect starts within about 15 minutes and lasts up to four to six hours.", "sidefx": "Albuterol's beta-2 effects explain tremor, fast heart rate, low potassium and high glucose. Ipratropium's antimuscarinic action explains dry mouth and, if it reaches the eyes, pupil dilation and angle-closure glaucoma; it can also worsen urinary retention. Paradoxical bronchospasm is rare but serious. Excessive use of inhaled sympathomimetics has been linked to deaths. There is no specific antidote; a beta-blocker can reverse severe albuterol effects but may worsen bronchospasm.", "fun": "Asthma cigarettes made with stramonium (Datura) leaves were sold in pharmacies into the 20th century, an early form of inhaled antimuscarinic therapy.", "src": ["Combivent Respimat FDA label", "Ipratropium bromide and albuterol sulfate inhalation solution FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "brensocatib": {"origin": "Brensocatib came from a link between a rare genetic disease and common lung damage. People with Papillon-Lefevre syndrome lack the enzyme dipeptidyl peptidase 1 (DPP1, also called cathepsin C). Their neutrophils cannot activate their protein-cutting enzymes, yet they do not suffer the severe infections that might be expected; they mainly have thick skin on the palms and soles and gum disease. That suggested that blocking DPP1 could calm neutrophil damage without wrecking immunity. AstraZeneca discovered the molecule and licensed it to Insmed in 2016. In the ASPEN trial, it reduced flares of non-cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis. The FDA approved Brinsupri in 2025 as the first DPP1 inhibitor and the first approved treatment for non-cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis.", "timeline": [["1999", "Loss of cathepsin C is identified as the cause of Papillon-Lefevre syndrome."], ["2016", "Insmed licenses the DPP1 inhibitor from AstraZeneca."], ["2025", "FDA approves Brinsupri (brensocatib) for non-cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis."]], "cell": "In the bone marrow, developing neutrophils make serine proteases such as neutrophil elastase, proteinase 3 and cathepsin G as inactive forms. DPP1 cuts two amino acids off each one to turn them on before they are packed into granules. Brensocatib reversibly inhibits DPP1 in these precursor cells. Neutrophils that mature during treatment carry less active protease. In bronchiectasis, neutrophils swarm the airways and release elastase, which damages airway walls, slows mucus clearance and keeps a cycle of infection and inflammation going. Less active elastase breaks that cycle. Because the effect is set during neutrophil development, it builds over weeks.", "body": [["Airways", "Lower sputum elastase activity and fewer bronchiectasis flares."], ["Skin", "Rash, dry skin and thickened skin (hyperkeratosis), similar to features of Papillon-Lefevre syndrome."], ["Gums and teeth", "Gum and periodontal problems can occur, so dental care is part of monitoring."], ["Immune system", "Neutrophil function is altered, so live vaccines are evaluated before and during treatment."], ["Blood pressure", "Hypertension has been reported among the common adverse reactions in trials."]], "journey": "Brensocatib is taken by mouth once daily. It is about 87% bound to plasma proteins. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP3A, with minor CYP2C8 and CYP2D6 roles; thiocyanate is a major circulating metabolite. About half leaves in urine and about a quarter in feces. The half-life is about 25 to 39 hours. Its effect on lung neutrophils lags because only newly made neutrophils carry the change.", "sidefx": "Skin and gum effects mirror what happens in people born without DPP1, because the enzyme also activates proteases in skin and gum tissue. Hyperkeratosis, rash, dry skin and periodontal disease are the main label warnings. Changes in neutrophil protease activity are the reason live vaccines are considered carefully. Headache and upper respiratory infection are common. There is no antidote; effects wane as new neutrophils form after stopping.", "fun": "Brensocatib is an example of 'human knockout' drug design: a rare genetic disease showed what blocking an enzyme would do before any drug existed.", "src": ["Brinsupri (brensocatib) FDA label", "Chalmers JD, et al. ASPEN trial, N Engl J Med 2025"]}, "treprostinil-inhaled": {"origin": "Treprostinil traces back to prostacyclin, a lipid made by blood vessel walls that was discovered in 1976 by Salvador Moncada, John Vane and colleagues. Prostacyclin widens vessels and stops platelets, but natural prostacyclin (epoprostenol) breaks down in minutes and needs a constant IV line. Treprostinil is a more stable synthetic analog developed by United Therapeutics, first approved as subcutaneous Remodulin in 2002. United Therapeutics then developed an inhaled solution, Tyvaso, approved in 2009 for pulmonary arterial hypertension. Inhalation delivers the drug to ventilated parts of the lung. A dry powder form, Tyvaso DPI, followed in 2022, and another dry powder product, Yutrepia, was later approved. Tyvaso is also approved for pulmonary hypertension from interstitial lung disease.", "timeline": [["1976", "Moncada, Vane and colleagues discover prostacyclin."], ["2002", "Subcutaneous treprostinil (Remodulin) is approved."], ["2009", "FDA approves Tyvaso inhalation solution for PAH."], ["2021", "Tyvaso is approved for pulmonary hypertension with interstitial lung disease."], ["2022", "Tyvaso DPI dry powder is approved."]], "cell": "Treprostinil activates the prostacyclin (IP) receptor on pulmonary artery smooth muscle. IP is Gs-coupled, so binding raises adenylyl cyclase activity and cAMP. Protein kinase A lowers calcium and relaxes the muscle, widening pulmonary arteries and lowering pulmonary vascular resistance. Treprostinil also binds EP2 and DP1 receptors, which add to vasodilation. In platelets, higher cAMP blocks activation and aggregation. Prostacyclin signaling also slows smooth muscle growth in vessel walls. Because inhaled drug lands in alveoli that are breathing, it widens vessels next to ventilated lung, which helps match blood flow to air.", "body": [["Lungs (blood vessels)", "Pulmonary arteries widen, lowering resistance and easing the load on the right heart, with better walking distance."], ["Airways", "Cough and throat irritation are common, and bronchospasm can occur in reactive airways."], ["Systemic vessels", "Absorbed drug can widen vessels elsewhere, causing flushing, headache, dizziness and low blood pressure."], ["Platelets", "Platelet inhibition can increase bleeding risk."], ["Gut", "Nausea and diarrhea reflect prostaglandin effects on the gut."]], "journey": "Inhaled treprostinil deposits in the lungs and is absorbed into blood, peaking in about 10 to 15 minutes. It is about 91% bound to plasma proteins. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP2C8, so gemfibrozil raises levels and rifampin lowers them. Metabolites leave mostly in urine. The half-life is about four hours, and the effect lasts about three to four hours, which is why it is taken in several sessions a day.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from prostacyclin receptor activation outside the lung: flushing, headache and low blood pressure from systemic vasodilation, and bleeding from platelet inhibition. Cough and throat irritation come from direct airway contact. Bronchospasm can occur in people with reactive airways. Stopping prostacyclin therapy suddenly can cause rebound worsening of PAH. There is no antidote; effects fade within hours.", "src": ["Tyvaso and Tyvaso DPI (treprostinil) FDA labels", "Moncada S, et al. Nature 1976"]}, "vanzacaftor-tezacaftor-deutivacaftor": {"origin": "Cystic fibrosis is caused by mutations in the CFTR gene, found in 1989 by teams led by Lap-Chee Tsui, John Riordan and Francis Collins. CFTR is a chloride and bicarbonate channel. In the most common mutation, F508del, the protein misfolds and rarely reaches the cell surface. Vertex Pharmaceuticals developed drugs that fix the protein: ivacaftor (2012) opens the channel, and correctors help it fold. Trikafta (elexacaftor, tezacaftor and ivacaftor) in 2019 transformed care. Alyftrek is Vertex's next triple combination, approved by the FDA in December 2024. It swaps elexacaftor for a new corrector, vanzacaftor, and uses deutivacaftor, a version of ivacaftor in which some hydrogen atoms are replaced with deuterium so it lasts longer and can be taken once daily. Vertex obtained deutivacaftor when it acquired the molecule from Concert Pharmaceuticals.", "timeline": [["1989", "The CFTR gene is identified."], ["2012", "Ivacaftor, the first CFTR potentiator, is approved."], ["2019", "Trikafta (elexacaftor, tezacaftor, ivacaftor) is approved."], ["2024", "FDA approves Alyftrek (vanzacaftor, tezacaftor, deutivacaftor)."]], "cell": "In F508del and other processing mutations, CFTR misfolds in the endoplasmic reticulum and is destroyed before reaching the cell surface. Vanzacaftor and tezacaftor are correctors that bind different sites on CFTR and help it fold, process and travel to the apical membrane of epithelial cells. Once there, the channel may still open poorly. Deutivacaftor is a potentiator that binds CFTR and increases the chance the channel is open (gating). Together, more channels reach the surface and each passes more chloride and bicarbonate. Better ion flow draws water onto airway surfaces, thinning mucus and improving clearance. The same effect in sweat glands lowers sweat chloride.", "body": [["Lungs", "Thinner mucus, better FEV1 and fewer pulmonary flares."], ["Sweat glands", "Sweat chloride falls, a direct marker that CFTR is working better."], ["Pancreas and gut", "Better bicarbonate and fluid secretion may improve digestion and nutrition."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise, and serious liver injury is a boxed warning."], ["Eyes", "Cataracts have been seen in children taking CFTR modulators."], ["Skin", "Rash can occur, and is more common in some groups taking CFTR modulators."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth once daily with fat-containing food, which improves absorption. All three drugs are over 99% bound to plasma proteins. The liver metabolizes them mainly by CYP3A, so strong CYP3A inducers lower levels and inhibitors raise them, and grapefruit is avoided. Deuterium makes carbon-deuterium bonds harder for enzymes to break, slowing deutivacaftor metabolism and allowing once-daily dosing. Elimination is mainly in feces. Sweat chloride falls within days to weeks.", "sidefx": "The boxed warning is drug-induced liver injury and liver failure, seen with this class, so liver tests are checked regularly. The exact cause is not fully known. Rash and hypersensitivity can occur. Cataracts in children are a class concern. Because all three rely on CYP3A, interactions with inducers such as rifampin can make the drug ineffective. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Deutivacaftor is one of a small group of 'deuterated' drugs; swapping hydrogen for its heavier twin deuterium can slow metabolism without changing how the drug fits its target.", "src": ["Alyftrek FDA label", "Riordan JR, et al. Science 1989", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "mannitol-inhaled": {"origin": "Mannitol is a sugar alcohol named after manna, the sweet dried sap of the flowering ash tree (Fraxinus ornus) from which it was first obtained in the early 1800s. It has long been used IV to reduce brain swelling. The Australian company Pharmaxis developed a dry powder of inhaled mannitol: first as Aridol, a bronchial challenge test that provokes airway narrowing in asthma, and then as Bronchitol, a treatment to hydrate mucus in cystic fibrosis. Bronchitol was approved by the FDA in 2020 as add-on maintenance therapy for adults with cystic fibrosis and is marketed in the US by Chiesi.", "timeline": [["1800s", "Mannitol is isolated from manna, the dried sap of the flowering ash."], ["2010", "Aridol inhaled mannitol challenge test is approved in the US."], ["2020", "FDA approves Bronchitol for cystic fibrosis in adults."]], "cell": "Inhaled mannitol does not act on a receptor. It dissolves in the thin layer of fluid on the airway surface and makes that fluid hyperosmolar. Water then moves from airway cells into the surface liquid by osmosis. In cystic fibrosis, faulty CFTR channels leave the airway surface dehydrated and mucus thick and sticky. Rehydration lowers mucus viscosity and helps cilia sweep it out. Mannitol also triggers cough, which helps clear mucus. The same hyperosmolar stress can trigger mast cells to release mediators such as histamine and leukotrienes, which is why it can cause bronchospasm and why it works as a challenge test for asthma.", "body": [["Airways", "Wetter, thinner mucus is cleared more easily, with modest lung function benefit in CF."], ["Airway smooth muscle", "Airway narrowing can occur from mast cell mediator release, so a bronchodilator is used before each dose."], ["Throat", "Cough and throat irritation are common from direct contact with the powder."], ["Lungs (bleeding)", "Coughing up blood can occur, especially in damaged CF airways."], ["Kidneys", "Absorbed mannitol is filtered and leaves in urine largely unchanged."]], "journey": "Mannitol is inhaled from capsules as a dry powder. It acts on the airway surface within minutes. Some is absorbed into the blood. A small fraction is metabolized by the liver, and most is filtered by the kidneys and excreted unchanged. The half-life is about 4.7 hours. Protein binding is negligible. Before first use, a supervised test dose checks for airway hyperreactivity.", "sidefx": "Bronchospasm is the main risk. The hyperosmolar airway surface triggers mast cells and airway nerves, causing narrowing; pre-treatment with a short-acting bronchodilator reduces this. Coughing up blood (hemoptysis) can occur because cough and osmotic stress affect fragile CF airways. Cough, sore throat and vomiting are common. There is no antidote; a short-acting bronchodilator treats bronchospasm.", "fun": "The same inhaled mannitol powder is used in a different product to deliberately provoke airway narrowing as a test for asthma.", "src": ["Bronchitol (mannitol) FDA label", "Aridol (mannitol) FDA label"]}, "sarilumab": {"origin": "Sarilumab targets interleukin-6, a cytokine cloned in 1986 by Tadamitsu Kishimoto's group in Osaka, Japan, and others. IL-6 turned out to drive fever, the acute-phase response, anemia of inflammation and joint damage in rheumatoid arthritis. The first IL-6 receptor blocker, tocilizumab, came from Kishimoto's work with Chugai. Regeneron used its VelocImmune mice, which make fully human antibodies, to create sarilumab, and developed it with Sanofi. Kevzara was approved by the FDA in 2017 for rheumatoid arthritis. Approvals followed for polymyalgia rheumatica in 2023 and for polyarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis.", "timeline": [["1986", "The IL-6 gene is cloned."], ["2010", "Tocilizumab, the first IL-6 receptor blocker, is approved in the US."], ["2017", "FDA approves Kevzara (sarilumab) for rheumatoid arthritis."], ["2023", "Sarilumab is approved for polymyalgia rheumatica."]], "cell": "IL-6 signals in two ways. In classic signaling it binds membrane IL-6 receptor alpha on liver cells and some immune cells. In trans-signaling, IL-6 binds soluble IL-6 receptor, and that complex can act on almost any cell. Either way, the complex recruits gp130, which activates JAK1 and JAK2 and then STAT3. STAT3 turns on genes for acute-phase proteins such as CRP and hepcidin, drives Th17 differentiation and B-cell antibody production, and activates synovial fibroblasts and osteoclasts. Sarilumab is a fully human IgG1 antibody that binds both soluble and membrane IL-6 receptor alpha and blocks IL-6 from binding. This lowers synovial inflammation, CRP and joint damage.", "body": [["Joints", "Less synovitis means less pain, swelling and stiffness, and slower joint damage."], ["Liver", "CRP, fibrinogen and hepcidin production fall because the liver's IL-6 signal is blocked. Liver enzymes can rise."], ["Blood", "Neutrophil and platelet counts can fall. Anemia of inflammation may improve as hepcidin drops."], ["Immune system", "Serious infections, including TB reactivation and fungal infections, are more likely."], ["Lipids", "Cholesterol and triglycerides rise, a known class effect of IL-6 blockade."], ["Gut", "IL-6 blockade is linked to GI perforation, especially in people with diverticulitis."]], "journey": "Sarilumab is injected under the skin every two weeks. It is absorbed slowly through lymphatics and peaks about two to four days later. Clearance has two parts: normal antibody breakdown by protein digestion, and target-mediated clearance when it binds receptors. At low levels target binding dominates, so the half-life depends on concentration and reaches up to about ten days at steady state. It is not cleared by the kidneys or liver enzymes, but blocking IL-6 can raise CYP enzyme activity that inflammation had suppressed, changing levels of drugs such as warfarin.", "sidefx": "Serious infections are the boxed warning, because IL-6 helps coordinate defense against bacteria and fungi. CRP can stay low during infection because the liver's IL-6 signal is blocked, which can hide fever and lab clues. Neutropenia reflects IL-6's role in neutrophil production and movement. Lipid rises and liver enzyme rises are class effects. GI perforation is linked to impaired gut wall healing. There is no antidote; the effect fades over weeks.", "src": ["Kevzara (sarilumab) FDA label", "Hirano T, et al. Nature 1986", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "canakinumab": {"origin": "Canakinumab came from research into rare autoinflammatory diseases. In 2001 Hal Hoffman and colleagues found that cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes (CAPS) are caused by mutations in the NLRP3 gene. NLRP3 forms the inflammasome, a protein machine that activates IL-1 beta. People with these mutations make too much IL-1 beta and suffer fevers, rashes and deafness. Novartis developed a fully human antibody against IL-1 beta, and Ilaris was approved by the FDA in 2009 for CAPS. Later approvals include periodic fever syndromes, Still's disease and gout flares. The large CANTOS trial in 2017 showed that blocking IL-1 beta lowered repeat heart attacks, supporting the idea that inflammation drives atherosclerosis.", "timeline": [["2001", "NLRP3 mutations are linked to cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes."], ["2009", "FDA approves Ilaris (canakinumab) for CAPS."], ["2017", "The CANTOS trial links IL-1 beta blockade to fewer cardiovascular events."], ["2023", "Canakinumab is approved for gout flares."]], "cell": "Inside macrophages and other innate immune cells, danger signals such as urate crystals, damaged cell contents or microbial products activate the NLRP3 inflammasome. This activates caspase-1, which cuts pro-IL-1 beta into active IL-1 beta. Active IL-1 beta binds the IL-1 receptor type I and recruits the accessory protein, activating MyD88, IRAK kinases and NF-kB. This drives fever, IL-6 production, the acute-phase response and neutrophil recruitment. Canakinumab binds IL-1 beta with high affinity and prevents it from engaging the receptor. It does not bind IL-1 alpha or the natural IL-1 receptor antagonist, so it is more targeted than rilonacept or anakinra.", "body": [["Whole body (fever)", "Fever and fatigue resolve within days because IL-1 beta is a key fever signal in the hypothalamus."], ["Skin", "The hive-like rashes of CAPS clear as IL-1 beta signaling in the skin falls."], ["Joints", "Gout flares and arthritis calm because urate crystals drive IL-1 beta release."], ["Liver", "CRP and serum amyloid A fall, reducing the risk of AA amyloidosis over time."], ["Immune system", "Infections, especially respiratory infections, are more common, and serious infections can occur."]], "journey": "Canakinumab is injected under the skin. It is absorbed slowly and peaks about seven days later. Like other IgG antibodies, it is broken down by protein digestion throughout the body and recycled by FcRn, giving a long half-life of about 26 days. This allows dosing every four to eight weeks for many conditions. It is not cleared by the kidneys or liver enzymes.", "sidefx": "Blocking IL-1 beta weakens part of the innate immune response, so infections are more likely and TB screening comes first. Neutropenia can occur. In systemic juvenile arthritis and Still's disease, macrophage activation syndrome can occur, related to the underlying disease. Live vaccines are avoided. In CANTOS, fatal infections were slightly more common. There is no antidote; effects fade over weeks.", "fun": "In CANTOS, people on canakinumab also had fewer lung cancer diagnoses and deaths, an unexpected finding that sparked new research into inflammation and cancer.", "src": ["Ilaris (canakinumab) FDA label", "Ridker PM, et al. CANTOS, N Engl J Med 2017", "Hoffman HM, et al. Nat Genet 2001"]}, "rilonacept": {"origin": "Rilonacept was built by Regeneron using its 'trap' technology, which fuses parts of natural receptors to the Fc tail of an antibody to make a soluble decoy. The same approach later produced aflibercept for eye disease. Rilonacept joins the outer parts of the IL-1 receptor type I and the IL-1 receptor accessory protein, so it grabs IL-1 the way the cell surface would. Arcalyst was approved by the FDA in 2008 for cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes. In 2021 it became the first FDA-approved treatment for recurrent pericarditis, developed for that use with Kiniksa Pharmaceuticals.", "timeline": [["2008", "FDA approves Arcalyst (rilonacept) for CAPS."], ["2021", "Rilonacept is approved for recurrent pericarditis."]], "cell": "IL-1 alpha and IL-1 beta both signal by binding IL-1 receptor type I, which then recruits the IL-1 receptor accessory protein. This complex activates MyD88, IRAK kinases and NF-kB, turning on genes for fever, IL-6, acute-phase proteins and neutrophil recruitment. Rilonacept is a dimeric fusion protein that contains both receptor pieces linked to the Fc part of human IgG1. It acts as a soluble decoy: it binds IL-1 alpha and IL-1 beta tightly in the fluid around cells so they cannot reach cell-surface receptors. In recurrent pericarditis, IL-1 released from damaged pericardial cells and inflammasome activation keep the inflammation returning, so trapping IL-1 prevents recurrences.", "body": [["Pericardium", "Recurrent pericarditis flares are prevented, with less chest pain and lower CRP."], ["Whole body (fever)", "Fevers and fatigue of CAPS ease as IL-1 signaling falls."], ["Skin", "The hive-like rashes of CAPS clear as trapped IL-1 can no longer drive skin inflammation."], ["Liver", "CRP and serum amyloid A fall as the liver's inflammatory signal drops."], ["Immune system", "Infections are more likely, and combination with TNF blockers is not recommended."], ["Lipids", "Cholesterol and triglycerides can rise, so lipids are checked periodically."]], "journey": "Rilonacept is injected under the skin once weekly. It is absorbed slowly and distributed mainly in blood and tissue fluid. As a protein with an IgG Fc tail, it is broken down by protein digestion and partly recycled by FcRn. Its half-life is about seven days in recurrent pericarditis. It is not cleared by the kidneys or liver enzymes.", "sidefx": "Because it traps both IL-1 alpha and beta, innate immune defense is weakened, so serious infections can occur. Combining with TNF blockers raised serious infection rates with other IL-1 blockers. Injection site reactions are common. Lipid rises are reported. Live vaccines are avoided. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Rilonacept's 'trap' design, a receptor fused to an antibody tail, is the same idea behind aflibercept for macular degeneration.", "src": ["Arcalyst (rilonacept) FDA label", "Klein AL, et al. RHAPSODY trial, N Engl J Med 2021"]}, "ixekizumab": {"origin": "Ixekizumab came from the discovery of Th17 cells in the mid-2000s, a T cell subset that makes IL-17 and drives psoriasis and other autoimmune diseases. IL-17A was found to be a major signal in psoriatic plaques. Eli Lilly developed ixekizumab, a humanized antibody that binds IL-17A with high affinity. Taltz was approved by the FDA in 2016 for plaque psoriasis, then for psoriatic arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis and non-radiographic axial spondyloarthritis. Secukinumab, another IL-17A antibody, had been approved a year earlier.", "timeline": [["2005", "Th17 cells are described as a distinct T helper lineage."], ["2015", "Secukinumab, the first IL-17A antibody, is approved."], ["2016", "FDA approves Taltz (ixekizumab) for plaque psoriasis."]], "cell": "In psoriasis, dendritic cells in the skin release IL-23, which keeps Th17 cells and other immune cells producing IL-17A. IL-17A binds a receptor made of IL-17RA and IL-17RC on keratinocytes. This activates Act1 and TRAF6, leading to NF-kB and other pathways. Keratinocytes then make chemokines that recruit neutrophils, antimicrobial peptides and more cytokines, and they multiply quickly. Ixekizumab is a humanized IgG4 antibody that binds IL-17A and prevents it from engaging its receptor. Without this signal, the inflammatory loop in the skin breaks, keratinocyte growth slows and plaques clear. IL-17A also drives inflammation at entheses and joints, explaining benefit in spondyloarthritis.", "body": [["Skin", "Psoriatic plaques thin and clear, often by week 12."], ["Joints and entheses", "Less joint swelling and enthesitis in psoriatic arthritis and spondyloarthritis."], ["Mouth and skin (infection)", "IL-17 is key for defense against Candida, so oral thrush and skin fungal infections are more common."], ["Gut", "IL-17 helps protect the gut lining, and blocking it can trigger or worsen inflammatory bowel disease."], ["Injection site", "Redness and pain at the injection site are common."]], "journey": "Ixekizumab is injected under the skin and absorbed slowly, peaking about four days later. As an IgG antibody, it is broken down by protein digestion and recycled by FcRn. Its half-life is about 13 days, which allows dosing every four weeks after starting doses. It is not cleared by the kidneys or liver enzymes.", "sidefx": "Blocking IL-17A weakens mucosal and skin defense against fungi, so Candida infections are more common. Bacterial and serious infections can occur, and TB is screened first. Inflammatory bowel disease can start or worsen because IL-17 helps maintain the gut barrier. Eczema-like rashes and hypersensitivity can occur. Live vaccines are avoided. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Taltz (ixekizumab) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "bimekizumab": {"origin": "Bimekizumab was developed by UCB in Belgium. After IL-17A antibodies proved effective, researchers showed that IL-17F, a closely related cytokine, also drives inflammation in psoriasis and arthritis and is often more abundant in skin. UCB designed a single antibody that binds both IL-17A and IL-17F. In head-to-head trials it cleared psoriasis more completely than some other biologics. Bimzelx was approved by the FDA in 2023 for plaque psoriasis, followed in 2024 by psoriatic arthritis, axial spondyloarthritis and hidradenitis suppurativa.", "timeline": [["2016", "IL-17A antibodies are established for psoriasis."], ["2023", "FDA approves Bimzelx (bimekizumab) for plaque psoriasis."], ["2024", "Bimekizumab is approved for psoriatic arthritis, axial spondyloarthritis and hidradenitis suppurativa."]], "cell": "IL-17A and IL-17F are made by Th17 cells, gamma-delta T cells and innate lymphoid cells under IL-23 stimulation. They exist as IL-17A dimers, IL-17F dimers and IL-17A/F heterodimers, all of which signal through the IL-17RA and IL-17RC receptor complex. Signaling activates Act1 and NF-kB in keratinocytes, fibroblasts and synovial cells, driving chemokines, neutrophil recruitment and tissue inflammation. Bimekizumab is a humanized IgG1 antibody with two binding sites that each recognize both IL-17A and IL-17F. Blocking both forms gives a deeper reduction in IL-17 signaling than blocking IL-17A alone.", "body": [["Skin", "Rapid and often complete clearing of psoriatic plaques."], ["Joints and entheses", "Less joint swelling and enthesitis in psoriatic arthritis and axial spondyloarthritis."], ["Skin folds (HS)", "Fewer painful nodules and draining tunnels in hidradenitis suppurativa."], ["Mouth", "Oral candidiasis is common because IL-17A and IL-17F both defend mucosa against fungi."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes and bilirubin can rise, so they are checked periodically."], ["Mood", "Suicidal ideation and behavior are listed in the label warnings."]], "journey": "Bimekizumab is injected under the skin and peaks in about three to four days. It is broken down like other IgG antibodies by protein digestion and recycled by FcRn. Its half-life is about 23 days, allowing dosing every four or eight weeks after starting doses. It is not cleared by the kidneys or liver enzymes.", "sidefx": "Dual blockade of IL-17A and IL-17F strongly weakens antifungal defense in the mouth and skin, so Candida infections are more common than with IL-17A blockers. Other infections can occur and TB is screened first. Inflammatory bowel disease can start or worsen because IL-17 supports the gut barrier. Liver enzyme rises are monitored. Suicidal thoughts were reported in trials. Live vaccines are avoided. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Bimzelx (bimekizumab) FDA label", "Reich K, et al. BE RADIANT trial, N Engl J Med 2021"]}, "brodalumab": {"origin": "Brodalumab was discovered by Amgen and developed with AstraZeneca. Unlike other IL-17 drugs, it blocks the receptor, IL-17RA, rather than the cytokine. That shuts down several IL-17 family members at once. Trials showed strong clearing of psoriasis, but a small number of suicides were reported in the program. Amgen withdrew in 2015 and Valeant (now Bausch Health) took over. A causal link was not established, but when the FDA approved Siliq in 2017, it carried a boxed warning about suicidal ideation and was limited to a REMS program.", "timeline": [["2015", "Amgen ends its involvement after reports of suicidal behavior in trials."], ["2017", "FDA approves Siliq (brodalumab) with a boxed warning and REMS."]], "cell": "IL-17RA is a shared receptor subunit used by several IL-17 family cytokines. Paired with IL-17RC it carries IL-17A, IL-17F and IL-17A/F signals; paired with IL-17RE it carries IL-17C; and paired with IL-17RB it carries IL-17E (IL-25). Signaling through Act1 and TRAF6 activates NF-kB and drives keratinocyte proliferation, chemokine release and neutrophil recruitment. Brodalumab is a human IgG2 antibody that binds IL-17RA and blocks all of these signals. This broad blockade quiets the IL-23 and Th17 loop that sustains psoriatic plaques.", "body": [["Skin", "Rapid thinning and clearing of psoriatic plaques."], ["Mood", "Suicidal ideation and behavior occurred in trials, which is why a REMS program and mood counseling are required."], ["Mouth and skin (infection)", "Candida and other fungal infections are more common because IL-17 signaling defends mucosa."], ["Gut", "Brodalumab is contraindicated in Crohn's disease, which IL-17 blockade can worsen."], ["Blood", "Neutropenia can occur, as IL-17 helps drive neutrophil production."]], "journey": "Brodalumab is injected under the skin every two weeks after starting doses. It peaks in about three days. Its clearance is nonlinear: at low levels, binding to IL-17RA on cells pulls the antibody out of circulation faster (target-mediated clearance). Otherwise it is broken down by protein digestion like other antibodies. It is not cleared by the kidneys or liver enzymes.", "sidefx": "The boxed warning covers suicidal ideation and behavior; a direct causal role has not been proven, but monitoring under the REMS is required. Blocking IL-17RA weakens antifungal defense, so Candida infections are common. Serious infections and TB reactivation can occur. Crohn's disease can be triggered or worsened, reflecting IL-17's protective role in the gut. Neutropenia reflects reduced neutrophil signaling. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Siliq (brodalumab) FDA label and REMS", "Lebwohl M, et al. AMAGINE trials, N Engl J Med 2015"]}, "tildrakizumab": {"origin": "Tildrakizumab grew out of the discovery that IL-23, not IL-12, is the main driver of Th17 inflammation. IL-23 was described in 2000 as a cytokine sharing the p40 subunit with IL-12 but having its own p19 subunit. Ustekinumab blocks p40 and so blocks both IL-12 and IL-23. Researchers then made antibodies to p19 alone, which spare IL-12. Tildrakizumab was developed at Merck and licensed to Sun Pharma, and Ilumya was approved by the FDA in 2018 for moderate to severe plaque psoriasis. It is given every 12 weeks after starting doses.", "timeline": [["2000", "IL-23 is described as a cytokine with a unique p19 subunit."], ["2009", "Ustekinumab, an IL-12 and IL-23 p40 blocker, is approved."], ["2018", "FDA approves Ilumya (tildrakizumab) for plaque psoriasis."]], "cell": "IL-23 is made by dendritic cells and macrophages. It is a two-part cytokine: p19 plus p40. It binds the IL-23 receptor and IL-12 receptor beta-1 on Th17 cells, gamma-delta T cells and innate lymphoid cells, activating JAK2, TYK2 and STAT3. This keeps Th17 cells alive and making IL-17A, IL-17F and IL-22, which drive keratinocyte growth and skin inflammation. Tildrakizumab is a humanized IgG1 kappa antibody that binds the p19 subunit, so IL-23 cannot reach its receptor. IL-12, which shares p40 but not p19, is not blocked, so Th1 immunity that controls intracellular infections is relatively preserved.", "body": [["Skin", "Psoriatic plaques thin and clear over weeks, with long-lasting effect between doses."], ["Immune system", "Upper respiratory infections are more common, and serious infections and TB can occur."], ["Injection site", "Redness or pain at the injection site may occur."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea has been reported in clinical trials."], ["Whole body", "Hypersensitivity reactions such as angioedema and hives can occur."]], "journey": "Tildrakizumab is injected under the skin by a healthcare provider. It is absorbed slowly and peaks about six days later. As an IgG antibody, it is broken down by protein digestion and recycled by FcRn, giving a half-life of about 23 days. Because IL-23 sits upstream, a single dose has effects that last longer than the drug's level suggests, allowing dosing every 12 weeks.", "sidefx": "Blocking IL-23 lowers Th17 activity, which weakens some defenses against bacteria and fungi, so infections can increase and TB is screened first. Because IL-12 is spared, the effect on intracellular pathogen defense is smaller than with p40 blockers. Hypersensitivity can occur. Live vaccines are avoided. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Ilumya (tildrakizumab) FDA label", "Oppmann B, et al. Immunity 2000"]}, "anifrolumab": {"origin": "Anifrolumab is based on the 'interferon signature' in lupus. Interferon was discovered in 1957 by Alick Isaacs and Jean Lindenmann as a substance that interferes with virus growth. Decades later, researchers found that many people with systemic lupus erythematosus have high activity of genes switched on by type I interferons. Instead of blocking one interferon, MedImmune (part of AstraZeneca) made an antibody against the shared receptor, IFNAR1, to block them all. After the TULIP trials, Saphnelo was approved by the FDA in 2021 for moderate to severe SLE, the first new lupus drug in about a decade after belimumab.", "timeline": [["1957", "Isaacs and Lindenmann discover interferon."], ["2000s", "The type I interferon gene signature is described in lupus."], ["2011", "Belimumab is approved for lupus."], ["2021", "FDA approves Saphnelo (anifrolumab) for SLE."]], "cell": "In lupus, immune complexes containing self DNA and RNA are taken up by plasmacytoid dendritic cells and trigger Toll-like receptors 7 and 9. These cells release large amounts of type I interferons, mainly IFN-alpha. Type I interferons bind IFNAR1 and IFNAR2, activating JAK1 and TYK2 and then STAT1 and STAT2. This switches on hundreds of interferon-stimulated genes, activates B cells and dendritic cells and promotes autoantibody production, feeding a loop. Anifrolumab is a human IgG1 kappa antibody that binds IFNAR1 and blocks signaling by all type I interferons, including IFN-alpha, IFN-beta and IFN-omega. The interferon signature falls and disease activity improves.", "body": [["Skin", "Lupus rashes improve as interferon-driven skin inflammation calms."], ["Joints", "Joint pain and swelling decrease as interferon-driven inflammation calms."], ["Immune system", "Interferon-stimulated gene activity drops, with less B cell and dendritic cell activation."], ["Nerves (zoster)", "Type I interferons control herpes viruses, so shingles is more common, including disseminated cases."], ["Airways", "Upper respiratory infections and bronchitis are more common."], ["Steroid needs", "Many patients can lower their oral steroid use, reducing steroid side effects."]], "journey": "Anifrolumab is given as an IV infusion every four weeks, so it reaches full levels at the end of the infusion. Its clearance is nonlinear: binding to IFNAR1 on cells, which then internalize it, removes it faster at low concentrations. Otherwise it is broken down by protein digestion like other IgG antibodies. It is not cleared by the kidneys or liver enzymes.", "sidefx": "Type I interferons are front-line antiviral defense, so blocking their receptor raises the risk of viral infections, especially herpes zoster and respiratory infections. Serious infections can occur. Infusion reactions and hypersensitivity, including anaphylaxis, are possible. Live vaccines are avoided. It has not been studied in severe active lupus nephritis or severe CNS lupus. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Saphnelo (anifrolumab) FDA label", "Morand EF, et al. TULIP-2, N Engl J Med 2020", "Isaacs A, Lindenmann J. Proc R Soc Lond B 1957"]}, "voclosporin": {"origin": "Voclosporin is a close cousin of cyclosporine, which was found in the early 1970s at Sandoz in Switzerland from the soil fungus Tolypocladium inflatum. Jean-Francois Borel showed cyclosporine's immune-suppressing power, and it changed organ transplantation in the 1980s. Cyclosporine levels vary widely, though, which makes dosing hard. Chemists at the Canadian company Isotechnika modified one amino acid of cyclosporine to make voclosporin, which binds calcineurin more strongly and has more predictable levels. Aurinia Pharmaceuticals developed it for lupus nephritis, and Lupkynis was approved by the FDA in 2021 as the first oral drug for lupus nephritis, used with background immunosuppression.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Cyclosporine is discovered at Sandoz from Tolypocladium inflatum."], ["1983", "Cyclosporine is approved in the US for transplant rejection."], ["2021", "FDA approves Lupkynis (voclosporin) for lupus nephritis."]], "cell": "In T cells, antigen recognition raises calcium, which activates calcineurin, a calcium and calmodulin-dependent phosphatase. Calcineurin removes phosphate groups from NFAT, letting it move into the nucleus and switch on IL-2 and other cytokine genes. Voclosporin binds cyclophilin A inside the cell, and this complex blocks calcineurin. NFAT stays in the cytoplasm, IL-2 is not made and T cell activation stalls. In the kidney, calcineurin also controls the podocyte protein synaptopodin. Inhibiting calcineurin protects synaptopodin from breakdown, stabilizing the podocyte actin skeleton and reducing protein leak. This is why proteinuria drops quickly in lupus nephritis.", "body": [["Kidneys (filter)", "Protein in the urine falls as podocytes stabilize and immune attack calms."], ["Kidneys (blood flow)", "Calcineurin inhibitors constrict the afferent arteriole, which can drop GFR, so eGFR is checked regularly."], ["Blood pressure", "Hypertension is common, related to vasoconstriction and sodium retention."], ["Immune system", "Suppressed T cells raise the risk of serious infections and of lymphoma and skin cancer."], ["Nervous system", "Tremor, headache and rare PRES can occur."], ["Electrolytes", "Potassium can rise because calcineurin inhibitors reduce potassium excretion."]], "journey": "Voclosporin is taken by mouth twice daily. It is absorbed quickly and peaks in about 1.5 hours. It is about 97% bound to plasma proteins and spreads into red blood cells. The liver metabolizes it by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors are avoided and inducers lower its levels. It is also a P-gp substrate. It leaves mainly in bile and feces. The half-life is about 30 hours.", "sidefx": "The boxed warning covers serious infections and cancers such as lymphoma and skin cancer, because suppressing T cells weakens immune surveillance. Kidney function can fall because calcineurin inhibition constricts the afferent arteriole. Hypertension and hyperkalemia reflect effects on vessel tone and kidney tubules. Neurotoxicity, including PRES, can occur. QT prolongation is listed. Live vaccines are avoided. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Cyclosporine, the parent of voclosporin, was found in a soil sample collected in Norway, which is part of why it changed transplantation history.", "src": ["Lupkynis (voclosporin) FDA label", "Rovin BH, et al. AURORA 1, Lancet 2021", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "auranofin": {"origin": "Gold has a long medical story. In 1890 Robert Koch found that gold cyanide inhibited the tuberculosis bacillus in the lab. In 1929 the French physician Jacques Forestier began using injectable gold salts for rheumatoid arthritis, on the idea that RA might be infectious. Gold injections became a standard RA treatment for decades. Smith Kline & French developed auranofin as an oral gold compound, approved by the FDA in 1985 as Ridaura. It was easier to take than weekly injections, but its effect was modest. Use fell after methotrexate and biologics took over. Researchers continue to study auranofin in the lab for cancer and infections, but those uses are not approved.", "timeline": [["1890", "Robert Koch reports gold cyanide inhibits tuberculosis bacteria."], ["1929", "Jacques Forestier begins treating rheumatoid arthritis with gold salts."], ["1985", "FDA approves oral auranofin (Ridaura)."]], "cell": "Auranofin contains gold(I) bound to a thioglucose and a triethylphosphine ligand. In the body, gold is released from these ligands and binds sulfhydryl groups and selenocysteine on proteins. A key target is thioredoxin reductase, an enzyme that keeps cells in a reduced state. Inhibiting it raises oxidative stress inside cells. Gold also builds up in macrophage lysosomes and reduces phagocytosis, lysosomal enzyme release and production of cytokines and prostaglandins. It can suppress NF-kB activity. How these effects add up to slow rheumatoid arthritis is not fully known, but the net result is less synovial inflammation over months.", "body": [["Joints", "Slow reduction in joint swelling and pain over three to six months, with lower rheumatoid factor levels."], ["Gut", "Loose stools or diarrhea are very common, and rare severe enterocolitis can occur."], ["Skin and mouth", "Itchy rashes and mouth sores are common gold reactions."], ["Kidneys", "Gold can cause proteinuria and membranous nephropathy, an immune-complex kidney injury."], ["Blood", "Gold can suppress the bone marrow, causing low platelets, low white cells or aplastic anemia."], ["Lungs", "Rare interstitial pneumonitis can occur."]], "journey": "Auranofin is taken by mouth, and about a quarter of the gold is absorbed. Blood gold peaks in one to two hours. About 60% of blood gold is protein bound, much of it to albumin and red cells. Gold builds up in tissues such as the synovium, kidneys and liver over time. The blood half-life of gold is about 26 days, and total body gold has a half-life of about 80 days. Absorbed gold leaves about 60% in urine and the rest in feces.", "sidefx": "Gold toxicity is the boxed warning. Bone marrow suppression, causing low platelets, low white cells and rarely aplastic anemia, is thought to be immune mediated, so blood counts and urinalysis are checked monthly. Proteinuria and nephrotic syndrome come from immune complexes in the kidney. Rash, itching and mouth sores reflect gold deposits and immune reactions in skin and mucosa. Diarrhea is the most common effect. Dimercaprol has been used historically as a chelator for severe gold toxicity.", "fun": "Gold therapy for arthritis began because doctors thought rheumatoid arthritis might be a form of tuberculosis.", "src": ["Ridaura (auranofin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "subcutaneous-immune-globulin": {"origin": "Immune globulin therapy began with blood plasma. In the 1940s Edwin Cohn at Harvard developed a method to separate plasma into fractions, including gamma globulin, which carries antibodies. In 1952 Ogden Bruton, an army pediatrician, described a boy with no gamma globulin who kept getting infections, the first known case of agammaglobulinemia. Bruton treated him with gamma globulin injected under the skin, and the infections fell. IV forms later became standard, but in the 2000s concentrated subcutaneous products returned to allow home infusion. Vivaglobin was the first US subcutaneous product in 2006, followed by Hizentra in 2010 and others, including HyQvia, which adds hyaluronidase. Hizentra was later approved for CIDP.", "timeline": [["1940s", "Edwin Cohn develops plasma fractionation to isolate gamma globulin."], ["1952", "Ogden Bruton describes agammaglobulinemia and treats it with subcutaneous gamma globulin."], ["2006", "The first US subcutaneous immune globulin (Vivaglobin) is approved."], ["2010", "Hizentra 20% subcutaneous immune globulin is approved."], ["2018", "Hizentra is approved for CIDP maintenance."]], "cell": "Immune globulin is pooled IgG from thousands of healthy donors, so it contains antibodies against a broad range of bacteria and viruses. In primary immunodeficiency it replaces missing antibodies. These IgG molecules bind pathogens, coat them for phagocytes through Fc gamma receptors (opsonization), neutralize toxins and viruses and activate complement. In autoimmune nerve disease such as CIDP, extra effects matter. Large amounts of IgG saturate FcRn, the receptor that normally recycles IgG, so the patient's own harmful autoantibodies are broken down faster. IgG also blocks Fc receptors on macrophages, neutralizes autoantibodies with anti-idiotype antibodies and dampens complement activation.", "body": [["Immune system", "Steady IgG levels lower infection rates in people with primary immunodeficiency."], ["Nerves", "In CIDP, immune attack on myelin is damped, helping maintain strength and function."], ["Skin (infusion sites)", "Swelling, redness and itching where fluid is infused under the skin are very common and fade within days."], ["Blood vessels", "IgG raises blood viscosity, and thrombosis can occur, especially with risk factors."], ["Brain coverings", "Aseptic meningitis with headache and neck stiffness can occur, though it is less common than with IV forms."], ["Red blood cells", "Donor antibodies against blood group antigens can cause hemolysis."]], "journey": "The concentrated solution is infused under the skin, often at home, through one or more sites. IgG is absorbed slowly through lymphatics into the blood, peaking about two to three days later. Because absorption is gradual and dosing is often weekly, IgG levels stay steadier than with monthly IV infusions. IgG is broken down by cells of the reticuloendothelial system, while FcRn recycling protects it and gives a half-life of about three to four weeks. With HyQvia, hyaluronidase temporarily loosens tissue so larger volumes can be infused.", "sidefx": "Local reactions come from a volume of protein solution in the tissue under the skin. Thrombosis, the boxed warning, is linked to higher blood viscosity and procoagulant factors, so hydration is part of care. Aseptic meningitis likely reflects immune effects in the meninges. Hemolysis comes from donor anti-A and anti-B antibodies. Hypersensitivity is more likely in IgA-deficient patients with anti-IgA antibodies. As a human plasma product, it carries a theoretical risk of transmitting infectious agents despite screening and viral inactivation. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Ogden Bruton's first treatment for agammaglobulinemia in 1952 was given under the skin, so modern home subcutaneous therapy is in a sense a return to where the field started.", "src": ["Hizentra (immune globulin subcutaneous, human) FDA label", "Bruton OC. Pediatrics 1952", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "oxaprozin": {"origin": "Oxaprozin is a propionic acid NSAID, a family that includes ibuprofen and naproxen. It was developed by Wyeth Laboratories as part of the search for anti-inflammatory drugs with long action. Its standout feature is a very long half-life, which allows once-daily dosing. It was approved in the US in 1992 as Daypro. It remains available as generic tablets for osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis and juvenile arthritis.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Propionic acid NSAIDs such as ibuprofen are introduced."], ["1992", "Oxaprozin (Daypro) is approved in the US."]], "cell": "Oxaprozin reversibly inhibits both COX-1 and COX-2. These enzymes convert arachidonic acid, released from membranes by phospholipase A2, into prostaglandin H2, the parent of PGE2, prostacyclin and thromboxane A2. Less PGE2 at sites of injury means less vasodilation, swelling and sensitization of pain nerve endings. Less PGE2 in the hypothalamus lowers fever. COX-1 blockade in the stomach lining reduces protective prostaglandins. In platelets, reversible COX-1 blockade lowers thromboxane while the drug is present. COX-2 blockade in vessel walls lowers prostacyclin, tipping balance toward clotting.", "body": [["Joints", "Less swelling and pain in arthritis, with steady effect from once-daily dosing."], ["Stomach", "Loss of protective prostaglandins can cause ulcers and bleeding."], ["Kidneys", "Prostaglandins keep kidney blood flow up when it is stressed; blocking them can cause kidney injury and high potassium."], ["Heart and vessels", "Higher thrombotic risk and fluid retention can raise blood pressure and worsen heart failure."], ["Skin", "Oxaprozin can cause photosensitivity and rash in sun-exposed skin, a reaction noted in its label."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises and rare serious liver injury can occur."]], "journey": "Oxaprozin is taken by mouth and is well absorbed, peaking in three to five hours. It is about 99% bound to albumin, and binding saturates at higher levels, so free drug rises more than expected. The liver breaks it down by oxidation and glucuronidation, with metabolites leaving in urine and feces. Its half-life is very long, about 41 to 55 hours, so it takes several days to reach steady levels and effects last more than a day.", "sidefx": "Side effects come from losing prostaglandins. In the stomach, less mucus and bicarbonate lead to ulcers and bleeding. In the kidney, reduced vasodilating prostaglandins cause kidney injury, salt retention and high potassium. Unbalanced COX-2 blockade raises heart attack and stroke risk, the boxed warning. The long half-life means side effects can linger after stopping. It can cause a false-positive urine benzodiazepine screen. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Oxaprozin can cause false-positive results on urine screening tests for benzodiazepines for several days after the last dose.", "src": ["Daypro (oxaprozin) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "flurbiprofen": {"origin": "Flurbiprofen was developed by the Boots Company in Nottingham, England, the same research group that discovered ibuprofen in the early 1960s under Stewart Adams. Boots chemists kept exploring propionic acid derivatives and found that adding a fluorine atom and a second ring produced a more potent molecule. Flurbiprofen was introduced in the UK in the 1970s. In the US, ophthalmic flurbiprofen (Ocufen) was approved in the 1980s to prevent pupil constriction during eye surgery, and oral tablets (Ansaid) followed in the late 1980s. The oral brand is discontinued, but generic tablets remain. Flurbiprofen lozenges for sore throat are sold in some other countries.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Boots scientists discover ibuprofen and related propionic acids."], ["1970s", "Flurbiprofen is introduced in the UK."], ["1980s", "Ophthalmic and oral flurbiprofen are approved in the US."]], "cell": "Flurbiprofen is a racemic mix, and the S-enantiomer does most of the COX inhibition. It reversibly blocks COX-1 and COX-2, so arachidonic acid is not converted to prostaglandin H2 and downstream PGE2, prostacyclin and thromboxane. Less PGE2 reduces inflammation, pain nerve sensitization and fever. In the eye, prostaglandins released during surgery constrict the pupil through the iris sphincter, so blocking them helps keep the pupil wide. In the stomach, loss of COX-1 prostaglandins weakens mucosal protection. In platelets, reversible COX-1 blockade lowers thromboxane while drug is present.", "body": [["Joints", "Less pain, stiffness and swelling in osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis."], ["Eye (ophthalmic form)", "Prevents pupil constriction during eye surgery by blocking iris prostaglandins."], ["Stomach", "Reduced protective prostaglandins can cause ulcers and bleeding."], ["Kidneys", "Blocking renal prostaglandins can reduce blood flow, causing kidney injury and high potassium."], ["Heart and vessels", "Higher risk of heart attack and stroke, fluid retention and raised blood pressure."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth, flurbiprofen is well absorbed and peaks in about 1.5 hours. It is about 99% bound to plasma proteins. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP2C9 hydroxylation, then glucuronidation, so people who are CYP2C9 poor metabolizers have higher levels. Metabolites leave in urine. The half-life is about five hours. Eye drops act locally with very little reaching the blood.", "sidefx": "Like other NSAIDs, its side effects come from loss of prostaglandins: stomach ulcers and bleeding from reduced mucosal protection, kidney injury and fluid retention from reduced renal blood flow, and increased heart attack and stroke risk from unbalanced COX-2 blockade, which is the boxed warning. Liver injury and serious skin reactions are rare. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "src": ["Flurbiprofen tablets FDA label", "Ocufen (flurbiprofen ophthalmic) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "mefenamic-acid": {"origin": "Mefenamic acid belongs to the fenamates, a group of NSAIDs based on anthranilic acid. Scientists at Parke-Davis in the US discovered the fenamates in the early 1960s while screening compounds for anti-inflammatory effect. Mefenamic acid was marketed in several countries in the 1960s and approved in the US as Ponstel in 1967. It became especially known for treating painful periods, because prostaglandins drive uterine cramping. Ponstel remains available along with generic capsules.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Parke-Davis scientists discover the fenamate NSAIDs."], ["1967", "Mefenamic acid (Ponstel) is approved in the US."]], "cell": "Mefenamic acid reversibly inhibits COX-1 and COX-2, the enzymes that turn arachidonic acid into prostaglandin H2. This lowers production of PGE2 and PGF2-alpha. In the uterus, PGF2-alpha and PGE2 cause strong contractions and constrict blood vessels during menstruation, which produces cramping pain. Lowering them reduces cramping and menstrual pain. Fenamates are also described as blocking prostaglandin receptors, an extra action beyond reducing synthesis. Elsewhere, reduced PGE2 lowers pain nerve sensitivity and fever, while loss of COX-1 prostaglandins weakens stomach protection and reversibly reduces platelet thromboxane.", "body": [["Uterus", "Fewer prostaglandin-driven contractions ease menstrual cramps."], ["Nerves", "Pain nerve endings become less sensitive as PGE2 falls."], ["Stomach", "Loss of protective prostaglandins can cause ulcers and bleeding."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is common and can be severe, a classic reason to stop the drug."], ["Kidneys", "Blocking renal prostaglandins can reduce filtration and raise potassium."], ["Blood", "Rare autoimmune hemolytic anemia has been reported."]], "journey": "Mefenamic acid is taken by mouth and absorbed well, peaking in two to four hours. It is over 90% bound to plasma proteins. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP2C9 to 3-hydroxymethyl mefenamic acid, which is then glucuronidated. About half leaves in urine and the rest in feces. The half-life is about two hours. Use is typically short-term, matching its label for no more than about a week.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow from prostaglandin loss: ulcers and bleeding in the stomach, reduced kidney blood flow and potassium rise, and increased thrombotic risk from unbalanced COX-2 blockade, which is the boxed warning. Diarrhea is more common than with many NSAIDs. Rash and rare hemolytic anemia have immune causes. In late pregnancy, NSAIDs can close the fetal ductus arteriosus early and reduce amniotic fluid. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Ponstel (mefenamic acid) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "diflunisal": {"origin": "Diflunisal was developed by Merck Sharp & Dohme in the 1970s as an improved salicylate. Chemists added a difluorophenyl ring to salicylic acid, making a more potent and longer-acting drug that is not converted to salicylic acid in the body. It was approved in the US in 1982 as Dolobid. Decades later, Jeffery Kelly's lab at Scripps Research showed that diflunisal binds and stabilizes transthyretin, a blood protein that can misfold into amyloid. A clinical trial found that diflunisal slowed nerve damage in hereditary transthyretin amyloidosis, an off-label use that helped pave the way for drugs like tafamidis.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Merck develops diflunisal from salicylic acid."], ["1982", "Diflunisal (Dolobid) is approved in the US."], ["2013", "A randomized trial shows diflunisal slows transthyretin amyloid polyneuropathy."]], "cell": "Diflunisal reversibly inhibits COX-1 and COX-2, lowering prostaglandin production from arachidonic acid. This reduces inflammation and pain nerve sensitization. Unlike aspirin, it does not acetylate COX, so its antiplatelet effect is reversible and depends on dose. It has little fever-lowering effect at usual doses, possibly because it does not enter the brain well. Separately, transthyretin is a four-part protein that carries thyroxine. When the tetramer falls apart, the single units can misfold into amyloid fibrils. Diflunisal binds the thyroxine-binding pockets and holds the tetramer together, slowing amyloid formation.", "body": [["Joints and muscles", "Pain relief and less inflammation in osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis and mild to moderate pain."], ["Stomach", "Loss of protective prostaglandins can cause ulcers and bleeding."], ["Kidneys", "Blocking renal prostaglandins can reduce kidney blood flow and cause kidney injury and high potassium."], ["Heart and vessels", "Higher thrombotic risk, fluid retention and raised blood pressure are NSAID class effects."], ["Nerves (TTR amyloid)", "Stabilizing transthyretin slows amyloid nerve damage, an off-label use."], ["Ears", "Tinnitus can occur, as with other salicylate derivatives."]], "journey": "Diflunisal is taken by mouth and absorbed well, peaking in two to three hours. It is over 99% bound to plasma proteins. The liver conjugates it mainly with glucuronic acid, and about 90% leaves in urine as glucuronides. It is not turned into salicylic acid. The half-life is about 8 to 12 hours and gets longer at higher doses because elimination saturates, so twice-daily dosing works. It can raise acetaminophen levels.", "sidefx": "Side effects reflect prostaglandin loss: GI ulcers and bleeding, kidney injury and fluid retention, and higher cardiovascular risk, the class boxed warning. As a salicylic acid derivative, labeling notes a possible link to Reye syndrome in children with viral illness. Tinnitus and rash can occur. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "An arthritis pill from 1982 turned out to stabilize a misfolding blood protein, an example of an old drug finding a new purpose.", "src": ["Diflunisal tablets FDA label", "Berk JL, et al. JAMA 2013", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "salsalate": {"origin": "Salsalate is part of the salicylate story that began with willow bark, used for pain and fever since ancient times. In 1763 Edward Stone reported willow bark for fevers to the Royal Society. Salicylic acid was later made in the lab, and in 1897 Felix Hoffmann at Bayer made acetylsalicylic acid (aspirin). Salsalate is salicylsalicylic acid: two salicylic acid molecules joined by an ester bond. It was sold as Disalcid and in generic form, but it is marketed in the US without FDA approval, as an older drug that never went through the modern approval process. It drew new interest when trials led by Steven Shoelson at Joslin Diabetes Center showed it can modestly lower blood glucose in type 2 diabetes, though that use is not approved.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Willow bark is used for pain and fever."], ["1763", "Edward Stone reports willow bark for fever to the Royal Society."], ["1897", "Felix Hoffmann at Bayer synthesizes aspirin."], ["2010s", "Trials show salsalate modestly lowers glucose in type 2 diabetes."]], "cell": "Salsalate is a prodrug. It is hydrolyzed to two molecules of salicylic acid in the small intestine and after absorption. Salicylate is a weak direct inhibitor of COX-1 and COX-2 enzyme activity and also lowers COX-2 gene expression. It also inhibits IKK-beta, a kinase that activates NF-kB, the master switch for many inflammatory genes. This NF-kB effect is thought to underlie both its anti-inflammatory action and its effect on insulin sensitivity. Because it is nonacetylated, salsalate does not permanently acetylate platelet COX-1 the way aspirin does, so its antiplatelet effect is small.", "body": [["Joints", "Reduced pain and inflammation in arthritis, with full benefit after a few days of steady dosing."], ["Stomach", "Less stomach injury than aspirin, because salsalate is absorbed largely intact and is nonacetylated, though ulcers can still occur."], ["Platelets", "Minimal antiplatelet effect, which may suit people at bleeding risk compared with aspirin."], ["Ears", "High salicylate levels cause tinnitus and hearing loss, an early sign of toxicity."], ["Metabolism", "In studies, salsalate lowered blood glucose modestly through NF-kB inhibition, an unapproved use."], ["Airways", "People with aspirin-sensitive asthma can still react."]], "journey": "Salsalate is taken by mouth. It is insoluble in stomach acid and dissolves in the small intestine, where it is absorbed and hydrolyzed to salicylic acid. Salicylic acid peaks in about 1.5 to 4 hours and is about 90% bound to albumin, less at high levels. The liver conjugates salicylic acid with glycine to form salicyluric acid and with glucuronic acid. These pathways saturate, so the half-life rises sharply as levels go up, from a few hours to over half a day. Products leave in urine, and alkaline urine speeds salicylate excretion.", "sidefx": "Salicylism, with tinnitus, hearing loss, nausea and confusion, comes from high salicylate levels, which rise unpredictably because metabolism saturates. Severe salicylate toxicity causes respiratory alkalosis and metabolic acidosis from uncoupling of cell metabolism. GI ulcers and kidney effects follow from prostaglandin loss, though they are less marked than with aspirin. Reye syndrome is a risk in children with viral illness. There is no specific antidote; severe toxicity is treated with urine alkalinization and, if needed, hemodialysis.", "src": ["Salsalate tablets product labeling", "Goldfine AB, et al. TINSAL-T2D, Ann Intern Med 2010", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "tretinoin-topical": {"origin": "Tretinoin is all-trans retinoic acid, the active acid form of vitamin A. Vitamin A was identified in the early 20th century, and its role in skin was clear from the rough skin seen with deficiency. In 1969 dermatologists Albert Kligman and James Fulton at the University of Pennsylvania reported that topical tretinoin cleared acne by loosening plugged follicles. Johnson and Johnson's Ortho division brought it to market as Retin-A, approved in 1971. In 1988 a JAMA study by John Voorhees's group at Michigan showed it improved sun-damaged skin, fueling a public boom, and Renova was later approved for photodamage. Kligman's career also includes controversial skin experiments on prisoners at Holmesburg Prison, now a well-known case in research ethics.", "timeline": [["1913", "Vitamin A (fat-soluble factor A) is identified."], ["1969", "Kligman and Fulton report topical tretinoin for acne."], ["1971", "Retin-A is approved in the US."], ["1988", "A JAMA study shows tretinoin improves photoaged skin."], ["1995", "Renova is approved for photodamage."]], "cell": "Tretinoin enters skin cells and binds cellular retinoic acid binding proteins, which carry it to the nucleus. There it binds retinoic acid receptors RAR-alpha, RAR-beta and RAR-gamma; RAR-gamma is the main form in the epidermis. RARs pair with RXRs and bind retinoic acid response elements in DNA, changing gene transcription. In the hair follicle, this normalizes how keratinocytes mature and lowers their stickiness, so plugs loosen and fall out (comedolysis) and new microcomedones form less. Epidermal turnover speeds up and the stratum corneum becomes thinner. In sun-damaged skin, it boosts collagen production and reduces collagen breakdown.", "body": [["Hair follicles", "Comedones loosen and are expelled, and new ones form less, clearing acne over weeks."], ["Epidermis", "Faster cell turnover and a thinner outer layer cause dryness, peeling and redness early on."], ["Dermis", "With long use, increased collagen improves fine wrinkles and texture in sun-damaged skin."], ["Pigment cells", "Uneven pigment and dark spots fade; irritation can sometimes cause light or dark patches."], ["Sun sensitivity", "A thinner outer layer lets more UV through, raising sunburn risk."]], "journey": "Applied to the skin, tretinoin stays mostly in the epidermis and follicles. Only a very small amount is absorbed into the blood, and blood retinoid levels stay close to the body's natural levels. Absorbed tretinoin is metabolized by liver CYP enzymes and leaves in urine and feces. Improvement in acne takes two to three weeks or more, and full benefit often needs over six weeks. The early 'purge' reflects existing microcomedones being pushed to the surface.", "sidefx": "Redness, peeling, burning and dryness come from faster turnover and thinning of the outer skin layer and from retinoid effects on skin barrier lipids. Increased sensitivity to sun, wind and cold follows from the thinner stratum corneum. Harsh soaps and other irritants make this worse. Systemic effects are not expected with topical use, but because oral retinoids cause birth defects, topical tretinoin is often avoided in pregnancy. The gel forms are flammable. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The same molecule taken by mouth, all-trans retinoic acid, is a cancer drug that forces leukemia cells in acute promyelocytic leukemia to mature.", "src": ["Retin-A (tretinoin) FDA label", "Kligman AM, et al. Arch Dermatol 1969", "Weiss JS, et al. JAMA 1988"]}, "tazarotene": {"origin": "Tazarotene was designed at Allergan in the 1990s as a receptor-selective retinoid. Natural retinoic acid binds all retinoic acid receptors, and the goal was a molecule that would hit only the ones that matter most in skin. Allergan's chemists built an acetylenic retinoid prodrug that, once activated, binds RAR-beta and RAR-gamma. Tazorac gel was approved by the FDA in 1997 for plaque psoriasis and acne, making it the first topical retinoid approved for psoriasis. Cream, foam and lotion forms followed, and in 2019 it was combined with halobetasol as Duobrii.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Allergan develops tazarotene as an RAR-beta and RAR-gamma selective retinoid."], ["1997", "FDA approves Tazorac gel for psoriasis and acne."], ["2019", "Duobrii (halobetasol and tazarotene) lotion is approved."]], "cell": "Tazarotene is a prodrug. Esterases in the skin remove its ethyl ester to form tazarotenic acid. Tazarotenic acid binds RAR-beta and RAR-gamma, but not RXR receptors. The bound receptor pairs with RXR in the nucleus and changes gene expression. In psoriasis it normalizes keratinocyte differentiation, slows the overactive cell division and lowers inflammatory markers. It also switches on tazarotene-induced genes, which are thought to restrain cell growth. In acne it loosens plugged follicles, like other retinoids.", "body": [["Skin (psoriasis)", "Plaques become thinner, less scaly and less raised over weeks."], ["Hair follicles", "Comedones loosen and acne lesions decrease."], ["Epidermis", "Burning, redness, peeling and itching are common, especially on healthy skin around lesions."], ["Sun sensitivity", "Changes in the outer skin layer raise sunburn risk."], ["Fetus", "Retinoids can cause birth defects, so pregnancy is avoided during treatment."]], "journey": "Tazarotene is applied to the skin, where it is quickly converted to tazarotenic acid. Only a small amount reaches the blood, more when large areas are treated. In blood, tazarotenic acid is over 99% bound to proteins and has a half-life of about 18 hours. It is further oxidized to sulfoxide and sulfone metabolites and leaves in urine and feces. Psoriasis improves over two to four weeks, with maximum response after two to three months.", "sidefx": "Irritation, burning and peeling come from retinoid effects on keratinocyte turnover and barrier function, and worsen when the product touches healthy skin. Photosensitivity follows from a thinner outer skin layer. The key safety issue is embryofetal toxicity: retinoic acid signaling directs fetal development, so a negative pregnancy test and effective contraception are part of the label. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Tazorac (tazarotene) FDA label", "Duobrii FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "retapamulin": {"origin": "Retapamulin is a pleuromutilin, a class that began with a mushroom. In 1951 researchers in New York, Frederick Kavanagh and colleagues, isolated pleuromutilin from the fungus then called Pleurotus mutilus (now Clitopilus). For decades its derivatives, such as tiamulin, were used only in animals. GlaxoSmithKline developed retapamulin as a semisynthetic ointment for skin infections, and Altabax was approved by the FDA in 2007 for impetigo, the first pleuromutilin approved for human use. A systemic pleuromutilin, lefamulin, followed in 2019. US availability of Altabax is now limited.", "timeline": [["1951", "Pleuromutilin is isolated from a mushroom fungus."], ["1970s", "Pleuromutilin derivatives such as tiamulin enter veterinary use."], ["2007", "FDA approves Altabax (retapamulin) ointment for impetigo."], ["2019", "Lefamulin becomes the first systemic pleuromutilin approved for humans."]], "cell": "Retapamulin binds the 50S subunit of the bacterial ribosome at the peptidyl transferase center, in domain V of 23S rRNA near ribosomal protein L3. This is the site where amino acids are joined into a growing protein chain. Binding blocks peptidyl transfer, interferes with tRNA binding in the P site and prevents normal formation of active 50S subunits. Protein synthesis stops, which is mainly bacteriostatic. Because its binding site differs from those of macrolides, lincosamides and other 50S drugs, cross-resistance with other antibiotic classes is uncommon. It is active against Staphylococcus aureus (methicillin-susceptible) and Streptococcus pyogenes, the main causes of impetigo.", "body": [["Skin", "Impetigo lesions heal as staph and strep stop growing."], ["Application site", "Mild irritation or itching where the ointment is applied."], ["Immune system", "Contact sensitization can cause allergic dermatitis."], ["Nose", "Nosebleeds were reported with nasal use, which is not an approved route."]], "journey": "Retapamulin ointment is applied to the skin lesion, where it acts locally. Very little reaches the blood, even on damaged skin, so systemic levels are low and usually not measurable. The small amount absorbed is about 94% bound to plasma proteins and is metabolized by CYP3A4. Systemic half-life is not clinically relevant. Treatment courses are short, and no response after a few days is a reason to reassess.", "sidefx": "Side effects are mostly local because so little enters the blood. Irritation and itching reflect contact with inflamed skin. Contact dermatitis is a delayed immune reaction to the drug. Superinfection with nonsusceptible organisms can occur if used too long. It is not used in the nose, eyes, mouth or vagina. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Retapamulin's ancestor, pleuromutilin, was isolated from a gilled mushroom, an unusual source for an antibiotic used in people.", "src": ["Altabax (retapamulin) FDA label", "Kavanagh F, et al. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA 1951"]}, "silver-sulfadiazine": {"origin": "Silver has been used against infection for centuries, and silver nitrate solution was used on burns in the 1960s. Sulfonamides date back to 1932, when Gerhard Domagk at Bayer showed that the dye Prontosil cured streptococcal infections. In 1968 Charles Fox Jr. at Columbia University combined silver with the sulfonamide sulfadiazine into a cream that was easier to use than silver nitrate and active against Pseudomonas, a deadly burn pathogen. Silver sulfadiazine (Silvadene) was approved in the US in the 1970s and became the standard topical agent for burns for decades. Newer silver dressings now compete with it, but it remains widely used.", "timeline": [["1932", "Gerhard Domagk discovers the antibacterial effect of Prontosil."], ["1960s", "Silver nitrate solution is used to prevent burn infection."], ["1968", "Charles Fox Jr. introduces silver sulfadiazine for burns."], ["1970s", "Silver sulfadiazine cream (Silvadene) is approved in the US."]], "cell": "The silver sulfadiazine complex slowly releases silver ions and sulfadiazine where it touches the wound. Silver ions bind proteins and enzymes in bacterial cell membranes and cell walls, particularly their sulfhydryl groups, and they bind DNA. This disrupts membrane function, respiration and replication, killing bacteria. Sulfadiazine is a PABA analog that competitively inhibits dihydropteroate synthase, blocking bacterial folate synthesis and DNA building blocks. Together these give broad activity against gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria, including Pseudomonas aeruginosa, and some yeasts such as Candida. The cream works on the surface and does not penetrate deep eschar well.", "body": [["Burn wound", "Fewer bacteria colonize the burn surface, lowering the risk of wound infection and sepsis."], ["Skin around wound", "Burning, itching or rash where the cream is applied."], ["Blood", "Transient leukopenia often appears within a few days, thought by many to reflect white cells moving out of circulation rather than marrow damage, and typically recovers on its own."], ["Red blood cells", "Absorbed sulfadiazine can cause hemolysis in people with G6PD deficiency."], ["Kidneys", "Absorbed sulfonamide can rarely cause interstitial nephritis."], ["Skin color", "Silver deposits can rarely cause gray discoloration (argyria-like)."]], "journey": "Silver sulfadiazine cream is spread over the burn. Very little silver is absorbed. Some sulfadiazine is absorbed, more so when burns are large, and blood levels can approach those from oral sulfa drugs in extensive burns. Absorbed sulfadiazine is partly acetylated in the liver and leaves in urine, with a variable half-life of about ten hours. In very large burns, the propylene glycol in the cream can also be absorbed and cause hyperosmolality.", "sidefx": "Transient leukopenia is common early and is usually mild and self-limited. Sulfonamide hypersensitivity, from rash to Stevens-Johnson syndrome, comes from immune reactions to absorbed sulfadiazine and its metabolites. Hemolysis occurs in G6PD deficiency because sulfonamides cause oxidative stress in red cells. Sulfonamides can displace bilirubin from albumin, so they are avoided in late pregnancy, premature infants and newborns because of kernicterus risk. Fungal overgrowth can follow suppression of bacteria. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Silver sulfadiazine changed burn care so much that for decades it was the default cream on nearly every major burn unit's shelf.", "src": ["Silvadene (silver sulfadiazine) FDA label", "Fox CL Jr. Arch Surg 1968", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "ketoconazole-topical": {"origin": "Ketoconazole was designed by chemists at Janssen Pharmaceutica in Belgium, the company founded by Paul Janssen, as part of a long program building azole antifungals in the 1960s and 1970s. Earlier imidazoles like miconazole worked well on skin but were poorly absorbed. Ketoconazole, reported in the late 1970s, was the first azole that could be swallowed to treat deep fungal infections, and the FDA approved oral Nizoral in 1981. Over time oral ketoconazole proved hard on the liver and the adrenal glands, and in 2013 the FDA restricted the tablets to serious infections with no other option. The creams, foams and shampoos never carried those problems because almost none of the drug reaches the blood. Today topical ketoconazole is a mainstay for tinea versicolor and seborrheic dermatitis, where its activity against Malassezia yeast is the key.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Janssen Pharmaceutica chemists develop ketoconazole as an orally active azole antifungal."], ["1981", "FDA approves oral ketoconazole (Nizoral) tablets."], ["2013", "FDA restricts oral ketoconazole because of liver injury and adrenal effects; topical forms are unaffected."]], "cell": "Fungi build their cell membranes with ergosterol instead of cholesterol. Ketoconazole is an imidazole that binds the heme iron of the fungal cytochrome P450 enzyme lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51, encoded by ERG11). With that enzyme blocked, lanosterol cannot be converted on the path to ergosterol. Two things then go wrong for the fungus: ergosterol runs short, and toxic 14-alpha-methylated sterols pile up in the membrane. The membrane becomes leaky and disordered, membrane-bound enzymes such as chitin synthase work poorly, and growth stops (a mainly fungistatic effect). Dermatophytes, Candida and Malassezia are all sensitive. Less Malassezia on the scalp and face means fewer irritating fatty acids from yeast lipases, which is why seborrheic dermatitis and dandruff improve.", "body": [["Skin", "Dermatophyte and yeast growth in the outer skin layer slows, so scaling, redness and itching of ringworm, jock itch and tinea versicolor fade over one to two weeks."], ["Scalp", "As a shampoo it lowers Malassezia counts on the scalp, reducing flaking and the itchy, greasy scale of seborrheic dermatitis."], ["Rest of the body", "Because measurable blood levels are not seen with topical use, the liver toxicity and hormone effects of oral ketoconazole do not occur."]], "journey": "Ketoconazole cream, foam or shampoo is spread on the skin, where the drug dissolves into the oily stratum corneum and stays mostly in the outer layers and hair follicles. Very little crosses into the blood, and plasma levels after topical use are usually not detectable. Shampoo is left on briefly and rinsed away, but some drug binds to the scalp and hair. Any small amount absorbed would be broken down by the liver. The local effect builds over days with regular use.", "sidefx": "Side effects are local. The drug and its vehicle can irritate skin, causing burning or itching, and some people develop allergic contact dermatitis. Some cream products contain sulfites, which can trigger allergic reactions in sensitive people. Shampoo in the eyes stings. Because so little is absorbed, the oral drug's problems, which come from blocking human P450 enzymes in the liver and adrenal steroid pathways, are not expected. There is no antidote; the product is washed off.", "fun": "Oral ketoconazole blocks human steroid-making enzymes so well that it has been used off-label to lower cortisol in Cushing syndrome, and a related single isomer, levoketoconazole, was later approved for that purpose.", "src": ["Nizoral and ketoconazole cream FDA labels", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "FDA Drug Safety Communication on oral ketoconazole, 2013"]}, "ciclopirox": {"origin": "Ciclopirox is a synthetic hydroxypyridone antifungal developed at the German company Hoechst AG in the early 1970s. It does not belong to the azole or allylamine families, and it was valued because it attacks fungi in a different way, by grabbing metal ions they need. Creams and lotions came first for skin infections. In 1999 the FDA approved ciclopirox nail lacquer (Penlac), the first topical prescription treatment approved in the US for fungal nail infection. It is painted on like clear nail polish, and the labeled program pairs it with regular trimming and professional removal of diseased nail.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Hoechst AG in Germany develops ciclopirox as a new class of topical antifungal."], ["1999", "FDA approves ciclopirox nail lacquer (Penlac), the first topical prescription treatment for onychomycosis in the US."]], "cell": "Ciclopirox is a chelator. It binds trivalent metal ions, especially iron (Fe3+) and aluminum (Al3+), with high affinity. Many fungal enzymes need iron to work, including cytochromes in the mitochondrial electron transport chain and the enzymes catalase and peroxidase that destroy toxic hydrogen peroxide. By pulling iron away from these enzymes, ciclopirox cripples energy production and leaves the cell exposed to oxidative damage. It also disrupts the uptake of nutrients across the fungal cell membrane. The result is fungicidal activity against dermatophytes, Candida and some bacteria. Because the target is a basic need rather than one enzyme, resistance is rare. It also has some anti-inflammatory activity, which helps red, irritated skin.", "body": [["Nails", "The lacquer slowly penetrates the nail plate and kills fungus in the nail bed. Clear nail grows in from the base over many months as infected nail is trimmed away."], ["Skin", "Creams and gels clear tinea and Candida skin infections and calm inflammation in the treated area."], ["Scalp", "The shampoo form reduces Malassezia yeast, easing seborrheic dermatitis."], ["Rest of the body", "Only a small fraction is absorbed, so no systemic effects are expected."]], "journey": "Ciclopirox is applied directly to the nail or skin. The lacquer dries into a film that acts as a drug reservoir, letting drug diffuse into and through the hard keratin of the nail. From the skin, only a small fraction is absorbed. The absorbed part has a short half-life of under two hours, is conjugated with glucuronic acid, and leaves in the urine. Nail clearing takes many months because the nail itself must grow out.", "sidefx": "Most problems are local. Redness and irritation around the nail come from the drug and its solvents touching the surrounding skin. Nail shape or color changes and ingrown nails can occur. The lacquer contains flammable solvents, so it is kept away from flame. Cure rates are modest because the drug must cross thick nail to reach the fungus. There is no antidote; the lacquer can be removed with alcohol.", "fun": "Researchers have studied ciclopirox's iron-grabbing ability in cancer and viral research, because human cells also depend on iron-containing enzymes.", "src": ["Penlac (ciclopirox) nail lacquer FDA label", "Loprox FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "efinaconazole": {"origin": "Efinaconazole was discovered by the Japanese company Kaken Pharmaceutical, which set out to build a triazole that could work through a toenail when painted on. The challenge with nail fungus is that most antifungals bind tightly to keratin and get stuck before reaching the nail bed. Kaken's chemists found a triazole with low keratin binding, and the product was formulated as a low-surface-tension solution that wicks under and through the nail. The FDA approved it as Jublia in June 2014, marketed in the US by Valeant (later Bausch Health). It was the first new topical prescription treatment for toenail fungus in the US in many years.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Kaken Pharmaceutical in Japan develops efinaconazole as a topical triazole for nails."], ["2014", "FDA approves efinaconazole topical solution (Jublia) for toenail onychomycosis."]], "cell": "Efinaconazole is a triazole that blocks fungal lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51), a cytochrome P450 enzyme. Its triazole nitrogen binds the heme iron in the enzyme's active site, stopping the removal of a methyl group from lanosterol. Ergosterol, the main sterol of fungal membranes, runs short while methylated sterol precursors build up. The membrane loses its normal packing and permeability, and membrane enzymes falter. Against Trichophyton rubrum and Trichophyton mentagrophytes, the main causes of toenail fungus, this leads to fungistatic and, at the high local levels reached in nail, fungicidal effects. A key property is weak binding to keratin, so more drug stays free to act on the fungus.", "body": [["Nails", "The solution travels through and under the nail to the nail bed, where Trichophyton lives. As fungus dies, new clear nail grows out over about a year."], ["Skin around nails", "Some solution touches the nail folds, which can cause redness, blistering or pain at the site."], ["Rest of the body", "Systemic exposure is very low, so effects on the liver and other organs are not expected."]], "journey": "The solution is brushed onto the nail with a flow-through applicator. Its low surface tension lets it spread into the space under the nail plate as well as through it. Very little reaches the blood. Plasma levels are low, and the small absorbed amount is metabolized in the liver and leaves in urine and feces. With daily use over months, the drug builds up in the nail. Results take close to a year because the nail grows slowly.", "sidefx": "Side effects are mostly where the liquid touches skin: dermatitis, small blisters and pain around the nail, and ingrown toenails. These come from the drug and the solvent drying and irritating the thin skin of the nail folds. The solution is flammable. Complete cure rates in trials were modest because nail fungus is deep and slow to clear. There is no antidote; the product is simply stopped.", "fun": "Efinaconazole's design focused on keratin binding: drugs that cling to nail protein are trapped, so a weaker grip on keratin let more drug reach the fungus underneath.", "src": ["Jublia (efinaconazole) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "tavaborole": {"origin": "Tavaborole came from Anacor Pharmaceuticals, a California company that built drugs around boron, an element rarely used in medicine. Anacor's scientists found that small boron-containing rings called oxaboroles could lock onto RNA inside an enzyme. Because the molecule is small and water-loving, it passes through the nail plate better than many antifungals. The FDA approved tavaborole as Kerydin in July 2014 for toenail fungus, a few weeks after efinaconazole. Anacor later also produced crisaborole, a boron drug for eczema, and was acquired by Pfizer in 2016.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Anacor Pharmaceuticals develops oxaborole compounds that use boron to target fungal enzymes."], ["2014", "FDA approves tavaborole (Kerydin) for toenail onychomycosis."], ["2016", "Pfizer acquires Anacor."]], "cell": "Tavaborole targets fungal leucyl-tRNA synthetase (LeuRS), the enzyme that loads the amino acid leucine onto its matching transfer RNA. This enzyme has an editing site that checks for mistakes. Tavaborole's boron atom forms a covalent adduct with the two hydroxyl groups on the end adenosine of tRNA-Leu right inside that editing site. This traps the tRNA in the enzyme, so the enzyme cannot charge any more tRNA with leucine. Without charged tRNA-Leu, the ribosome stalls and the fungus cannot make new proteins, so growth stops. This mechanism is different from the azoles, which act on cell membranes.", "body": [["Nails", "Tavaborole moves through the nail plate to reach Trichophyton fungus in the nail and nail bed. Over months, clear nail replaces infected nail."], ["Skin around nails", "Peeling, redness and dermatitis can develop where the liquid touches the surrounding skin."], ["Rest of the body", "Systemic exposure is low, so the drug acts locally."]], "journey": "The solution is dropped onto the nail and under its tip, where it spreads and soaks in. Its small size and solubility help it cross the dense keratin of the nail. Some drug is absorbed into the blood at low levels, where it is extensively metabolized and the products are excreted mainly by the kidneys. Because the nail grows slowly, treatment runs close to a year.", "sidefx": "The main side effects are local: peeling, redness and dermatitis at the application site and ingrown toenails. These come from the solution irritating skin around the nail. The solution is flammable. Cure rates in trials were low because killing fungus deep in a thick nail is difficult. No systemic toxicity is expected, and there is no antidote.", "fun": "Boron is uncommon in drugs; the cancer drug bortezomib and Anacor's tavaborole and crisaborole are among the few approved medicines that contain it.", "src": ["Kerydin (tavaborole) FDA label", "Rock FL et al. Science 2007 (oxaborole tRNA trapping mechanism)"]}, "nystatin-topical": {"origin": "Nystatin was the first effective, safe antifungal antibiotic. It was discovered around 1950 by two women at the New York State Department of Health: microbiologist Elizabeth Lee Hazen and chemist Rachel Fuller Brown. Hazen collected soil samples and found that a bacterium in soil from a farm in Virginia, later named Streptomyces noursei, made a substance that killed fungi. Brown purified it in Albany and mailed samples back and forth with Hazen in New York City. They named it nystatin after New York State. Squibb brought it to market as Mycostatin in the mid-1950s. Hazen and Brown gave their patent royalties to a nonprofit research fund that supported science and women in science.", "timeline": [["1950", "Hazen and Brown report nystatin from Streptomyces noursei."], ["1950s", "Squibb markets nystatin as Mycostatin, the first widely used antifungal antibiotic."], ["1994", "Hazen and Brown are inducted into the National Inventors Hall of Fame."]], "cell": "Nystatin is a polyene macrolide, a large ring with a string of conjugated double bonds on one side and hydroxyl groups on the other. It binds ergosterol, the sterol in fungal cell membranes, much more strongly than cholesterol in human membranes. Several nystatin and ergosterol molecules gather to form ring-shaped pores through the membrane. Potassium, other ions and small molecules leak out through these pores, the cell loses its internal balance and dies. Nystatin is active against Candida yeasts but not against dermatophytes or bacteria, which is why it is used for yeast rashes and not ringworm.", "body": [["Skin", "In diaper rash, skin folds and other moist areas infected with Candida, the yeast dies and redness, satellite spots and itching settle within days."], ["Rest of the body", "Nystatin is not absorbed through intact skin or mucous membranes, so it has no effects beyond where it is applied."]], "journey": "Nystatin stays on the surface. The cream, ointment or powder puts drug in direct contact with yeast on the skin, and the molecule is too large and poorly soluble to cross intact skin. Nothing meaningful enters the blood, so there is no liver metabolism or kidney clearance to consider. The powder is preferred for very moist areas because it also keeps the skin drier. It works only while it is on the skin.", "sidefx": "Nystatin is very well tolerated. Because human cells use cholesterol instead of ergosterol and the drug is not absorbed, there is no systemic toxicity. Irritation or allergy at the site can occur, sometimes from the vehicle. A rash that does not respond may be caused by a dermatophyte or bacteria, which nystatin does not cover. There is no antidote; the product is washed off.", "fun": "Hazen and Brown never met in a lab every day; they worked about 150 miles apart and mailed cultures and samples between New York City and Albany.", "src": ["Nystatin cream and ointment FDA labels", "Hazen EL, Brown R. Proc Soc Exp Biol Med 1950", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "econazole": {"origin": "Econazole is an imidazole antifungal from Janssen Pharmaceutica in Belgium, which created a whole family of azole antifungals in the 1960s and 1970s, including miconazole and later ketoconazole. Econazole is a close relative of miconazole, with a chlorine atom swapped on one ring. It was developed for skin use and became a broad-spectrum cream for athlete's foot, jock itch, ringworm, tinea versicolor and skin yeast. In the US it was sold as Spectazole cream, a brand now discontinued, and a foam form (Ecoza) was approved later. Generic cream remains common.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Janssen Pharmaceutica develops econazole as a topical imidazole antifungal."], ["1980s", "Econazole nitrate cream is marketed in the US as Spectazole."]], "cell": "Econazole blocks fungal lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51). Its imidazole ring binds the heme iron of the enzyme so it can no longer remove the 14-alpha methyl group from lanosterol. Ergosterol production falls and toxic methylated sterols build up in the fungal membrane, which becomes leaky and loses normal function. At the high concentrations reached in skin, the membrane damage can be directly fungicidal. Econazole is active against dermatophytes (Trichophyton, Microsporum, Epidermophyton), Candida and Malassezia, and also has some activity against gram-positive bacteria, which can help when a rash is secondarily infected.", "body": [["Skin", "Fungal growth in the outer skin slows and stops, so scaling, itching and redness of athlete's foot, jock itch and ringworm ease, often within days."], ["Skin folds", "Activity against both Candida and dermatophytes, plus some antibacterial action, makes it useful in warm, moist folds where mixed infections occur."], ["Rest of the body", "Absorption is minimal, so systemic effects are not expected."]], "journey": "Econazole is spread on the rash and a margin of normal skin. It dissolves into the stratum corneum and builds up there, with the highest levels in the outer layers. The label reports that less than one percent of an applied dose is recovered in urine and feces, showing how little enters the body. The small absorbed amount is metabolized in the liver.", "sidefx": "Side effects are local: burning, itching, stinging and redness where it is applied, from irritation by the drug or the cream base. Allergic contact sensitization can develop. Because blood levels are negligible, the drug interactions and liver effects that come with oral azoles are not expected. There is no antidote; the product is stopped and washed off.", "fun": "Miconazole, econazole and ketoconazole all came from the same Janssen azole program, which turned one chemical idea into a long line of antifungal drugs.", "src": ["Econazole nitrate cream FDA label", "Ecoza (econazole) foam FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "selenium-sulfide": {"origin": "Selenium sulfide is a simple inorganic compound of selenium and sulfur. Its use against dandruff dates to the mid-20th century, when it was introduced in medicated suspensions and shampoos sold under the Selsun name in the 1950s. Dermatologists then found it also clears tinea versicolor, a common rash caused by Malassezia yeast. It remains one of the classic over-the-counter dandruff ingredients, and a stronger lotion is available by prescription.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Selenium sulfide suspension is introduced for dandruff under the Selsun name."], ["1950s", "Selenium sulfide becomes a standard treatment for tinea versicolor."]], "cell": "Selenium sulfide works in two ways. First, it is cytostatic to the epidermis: it slows the turnover of keratinocytes in the scalp and hair follicles, so fewer skin cells are shed as visible flakes. The exact molecular target is not fully defined. Second, it is antifungal against Malassezia (Pityrosporum) yeasts. Malassezia lives on oily skin and breaks down sebum into irritating fatty acids that drive the inflammation and scaling of seborrheic dermatitis and the patches of tinea versicolor. Reducing yeast numbers removes that trigger, so scaling and discoloration improve.", "body": [["Scalp", "Less rapid cell turnover and fewer Malassezia yeast lead to less flaking and itching in dandruff and seborrheic dermatitis."], ["Skin of the trunk", "In tinea versicolor the yeast is suppressed, so the scaly light or dark patches stop spreading. Skin color can take weeks to months to even out."], ["Hair", "Hair can become oily or dry, change color, or shed more, which reflects effects on the hair shaft and scalp."]], "journey": "Selenium sulfide is applied as a lotion or shampoo, left on for a short time and rinsed off. It acts at the skin surface and in hair follicles. Absorption through intact skin is minimal, and the drug is removed by rinsing. Use on broken or inflamed skin is avoided because absorption could be higher there. Recurrence is common, so repeated applications are often used.", "sidefx": "The main side effects are irritation and burning, especially if it reaches the eyes or genital skin, because the compound is irritating to thin, sensitive tissue. It can tarnish or damage metal jewelry, which reacts with selenium and sulfur. Changes in hair oiliness, color or shedding can occur. Swallowing is toxic, so it is kept from children. There is no specific antidote; the product is rinsed off.", "fun": "Selenium sulfide can discolor gray, blond or chemically treated hair, which is why careful rinsing after use is part of the instructions.", "src": ["Selenium sulfide lotion 2.5% labeling", "OTC dandruff and seborrheic dermatitis monograph (21 CFR 358)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "betamethasone-dipropionate-topical": {"origin": "The story of strong skin steroids starts in 1952, when dermatologists Marion Sulzberger and Victor Witten showed that hydrocortisone rubbed on the skin could clear eczema. Chemists then raced to make steroids stronger. In 1954 Josef Fried and Emily Sabo at Squibb found that adding a fluorine atom at the 9-alpha position greatly boosted glucocorticoid power. Building on that, Schering Corporation developed betamethasone around 1960, a fluorinated, 16-beta-methyl steroid with strong anti-inflammatory action and little salt-retaining effect. Adding two propionate esters made betamethasone dipropionate much more fat-soluble, so it soaks into skin well. Schering sold it as Diprosone, and an augmented vehicle (Diprolene) later pushed the same molecule into the super-high potency class.", "timeline": [["1952", "Sulzberger and Witten report topical hydrocortisone for skin disease."], ["1954", "Fried and Sabo show 9-alpha fluorination boosts steroid potency."], ["c. 1960", "Schering develops betamethasone, a fluorinated 16-beta-methyl glucocorticoid."], ["1970s", "Betamethasone dipropionate is marketed for skin as Diprosone."]], "cell": "Betamethasone dipropionate is a lipophilic ester that crosses the cell membrane and binds the glucocorticoid receptor in the cytoplasm. Binding releases heat shock proteins, and the receptor-steroid complex moves into the nucleus. There it switches on anti-inflammatory genes such as annexin A1 (lipocortin), which inhibits phospholipase A2 so less arachidonic acid is freed to make prostaglandins and leukotrienes. The complex also ties up the transcription factors NF-kB and AP-1, which lowers production of cytokines like IL-1, IL-2, TNF-alpha and chemokines. Fewer immune cells are drawn into the skin, local blood vessels constrict, and keratinocyte and fibroblast division slows. The fluorine and methyl groups make it bind the receptor tightly and resist breakdown in skin, which is why it is high potency.", "body": [["Skin inflammation", "Redness, swelling and itch in psoriasis and eczema fall quickly as cytokine and prostaglandin production drops."], ["Skin cells", "Slower keratinocyte turnover thins psoriatic plaques, but long use also thins normal skin and weakens collagen, causing atrophy and stretch marks."], ["Blood vessels", "Constriction of small dermal vessels causes visible blanching; with long use the vessel walls weaken, leading to telangiectasia."], ["Adrenal axis", "Enough absorbed steroid can suppress the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis, lowering the body's own cortisol output."], ["Metabolism", "Large absorbed amounts can raise blood sugar and, rarely, cause Cushing syndrome features."]], "journey": "Applied as a thin film, betamethasone dipropionate dissolves into the oily stratum corneum, which acts as a reservoir that keeps releasing drug for hours. Absorption depends on the vehicle (ointment more than cream or lotion), skin thickness, inflammation and area treated. Thin skin, broken skin, large areas, children and occlusion all raise absorption. What reaches the blood is metabolized in the liver and excreted by the kidneys and bile. The local effect starts within days.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow directly from strong glucocorticoid action. Suppressed fibroblasts and keratinocytes produce skin atrophy, striae and easy tearing. Weakened vessel walls cause telangiectasia. Local immune suppression lets untreated fungal or bacterial infections spread. When enough is absorbed, the HPA axis is suppressed, which can cause adrenal insufficiency after stopping, along with hyperglycemia and Cushing features. There is no antidote; the drug is reduced or stopped, and suppressed adrenal function recovers with time.", "fun": "Topical steroid potency is ranked by a skin blanching test developed by McKenzie and Stoughton in 1962: the more a steroid whitens the skin by constricting vessels, the stronger it is.", "src": ["Betamethasone dipropionate cream FDA label", "Sulzberger MB, Witten VH. J Invest Dermatol 1952", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "fluocinonide": {"origin": "Fluocinonide was developed at Syntex, the company that grew up in Mexico City making steroid hormones from wild yams and later moved to Palo Alto, California. In the late 1950s and 1960s Syntex chemists built fluocinolone acetonide, a steroid with two fluorine atoms and an acetonide ring. They then added an acetate ester at carbon 21, which made the molecule even more fat-soluble and potent in skin. This was fluocinonide, sold as Lidex. It became one of the best known high-potency skin steroids in the US. A stronger 0.1% cream (Vanos) came later, and many generic forms remain in use.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Syntex develops fluocinolone acetonide, a doubly fluorinated topical steroid."], ["1960s", "Syntex creates fluocinonide, the 21-acetate ester of fluocinolone acetonide, sold as Lidex."]], "cell": "Fluocinonide crosses into skin cells and binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor. The activated receptor enters the nucleus, where it increases transcription of anti-inflammatory genes, including annexin A1 (lipocortin), which inhibits phospholipase A2. Less arachidonic acid means fewer prostaglandins and leukotrienes. The receptor also blocks NF-kB and AP-1, cutting cytokine and adhesion molecule production, so fewer white cells enter the skin. The two fluorine atoms (6-alpha and 9-alpha), the acetonide and the acetate ester all raise receptor affinity and skin penetration, putting fluocinonide in the high-potency group.", "body": [["Skin inflammation", "Itching, redness and swelling of psoriasis and eczema settle within days as inflammatory mediators fall."], ["Skin structure", "Reduced fibroblast and keratinocyte activity can thin the skin, causing atrophy, striae and fragile skin with long use."], ["Blood vessels", "Local vasoconstriction lightens redness; long-term use can cause small visible vessels."], ["Adrenal axis", "Systemic absorption from large areas or occlusion can suppress cortisol production, and children are especially susceptible."]], "journey": "Fluocinonide is applied in a thin film and enters the stratum corneum, where it forms a depot that keeps feeding drug into deeper layers. Occlusion greatly increases penetration and is used only in specific cases. Absorption is higher in thin, inflamed or broken skin. The absorbed fraction is metabolized in the liver and leaves through the kidneys and bile.", "sidefx": "Because fluocinonide is potent, the classic topical steroid effects appear faster with misuse: thin skin, stretch marks, visible vessels and acne-like or rosacea-like eruptions on the face. Immune suppression in the skin can mask or worsen infection. Absorbed drug can suppress the HPA axis, with Cushing syndrome and hyperglycemia in severe cases, and children have a larger skin area for their weight. There is no antidote; stopping or tapering lets the skin and adrenal axis recover.", "fun": "Syntex made early fortunes turning diosgenin from Mexican wild yams into steroid hormones, the same chemistry that later produced the first oral contraceptive norethindrone.", "src": ["Fluocinonide cream FDA label", "Vanos (fluocinonide) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "triamcinolone-topical": {"origin": "Triamcinolone was created at Lederle Laboratories in the mid-1950s, during the rush to build steroids more powerful than cortisone. Lederle chemists added a 9-alpha fluorine (for potency) and a 16-alpha hydroxyl group (to cut salt retention) to the prednisolone skeleton. Joining the 16 and 17 hydroxyls with acetone formed triamcinolone acetonide, which was much more potent on skin. Squibb later sold it widely as Kenalog. Today triamcinolone acetonide comes as creams, ointments, lotions and sprays for skin, a dental paste for mouth sores, and injectable forms used in joints and skin lesions. It is a workhorse medium-potency steroid.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Lederle Laboratories develops triamcinolone, a fluorinated prednisolone derivative."], ["1950s", "Triamcinolone acetonide, formed with acetone, is found to be far more potent on skin."], ["1960s", "Kenalog brand topical triamcinolone acetonide becomes widely used."]], "cell": "Triamcinolone acetonide binds the glucocorticoid receptor inside skin and immune cells. The ligand-bound receptor sheds its chaperone proteins and moves to the nucleus. There it turns on genes like annexin A1, which blocks phospholipase A2 and lowers prostaglandin and leukotriene synthesis, and it represses NF-kB and AP-1 so cytokines such as IL-1, IL-6 and TNF-alpha fall. Mast cells, T cells and eosinophils become less active in the treated skin, and small vessels constrict. The acetonide ring makes the drug fat-soluble so it penetrates skin, and the 9-alpha fluorine increases receptor binding.", "body": [["Skin", "Inflamed, itchy eczema and dermatitis calm within days as immune cell recruitment and mediator release decline."], ["Mouth", "As a dental paste, it sticks to mouth ulcers and reduces local inflammation and pain."], ["Skin structure", "With long use, reduced collagen production thins skin and can cause striae, especially in folds."], ["Adrenal axis", "Large areas, occlusion or long courses can allow enough absorption to suppress the body's cortisol production."]], "journey": "Topical triamcinolone soaks into the stratum corneum, which holds a reservoir of drug. Potency rises with strength and vehicle, and with occlusion, which the label permits for some conditions. Thin or inflamed skin and children absorb more. The absorbed drug is metabolized in the liver to inactive forms that leave in urine. Relief begins within days.", "sidefx": "Topical triamcinolone causes the usual steroid effects when overused: skin atrophy and stretch marks from reduced collagen, visible vessels, and steroid acne or perioral dermatitis on the face. Local immune suppression can let infections spread, so occlusion is stopped if infection appears. Absorbed steroid can suppress the HPA axis and cause Cushing features, more likely in children. There is no antidote; recovery follows stopping or tapering.", "fun": "The same molecule is injected directly into keloids and alopecia areata patches, where it can cause a dent in the skin from local fat atrophy.", "src": ["Triamcinolone acetonide cream FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "desonide": {"origin": "After topical hydrocortisone was shown to work in 1952, most steroid research aimed for more power, often by adding fluorine. That made strong drugs, but they could thin delicate skin. Desonide went the other way. It is a non-fluorinated steroid, a 16,17-acetonide of 16-alpha-hydroxyprednisolone, built to calm mild inflammation with a lower risk of thinning skin. It found its place on the face, eyelids, skin folds and in children, where potent steroids cause the most harm. Over the years it was sold as creams, ointments and lotions, and newer gel and foam versions were approved in the 2000s.", "timeline": [["1952", "Sulzberger and Witten show hydrocortisone works on the skin, starting topical steroid therapy."], ["2000s", "Newer desonide gel and foam vehicles are approved for atopic dermatitis."]], "cell": "Desonide binds the glucocorticoid receptor in skin cells. The activated receptor moves to the nucleus and increases transcription of anti-inflammatory proteins such as annexin A1, which blocks phospholipase A2 and lowers prostaglandin and leukotriene production. It also represses NF-kB and AP-1, which reduces cytokines that recruit T cells and eosinophils into eczema. Because it has no fluorine, it binds the receptor less strongly and is broken down more easily than fluorinated steroids. That gives a milder anti-inflammatory and vasoconstrictive effect with less suppression of collagen-making fibroblasts.", "body": [["Facial and fold skin", "Mild eczema and dermatitis on the face, eyelids, groin and armpits settle with less risk of atrophy than with stronger steroids."], ["Children's skin", "It is a common choice for children with eczema because low potency means less HPA axis risk, though diapers act like occlusion and raise absorption."], ["Skin structure", "Thinning, stretch marks and visible vessels are less common than with potent steroids but can still occur with long use."], ["Adrenal axis", "HPA axis suppression is uncommon but possible with large areas or prolonged use."]], "journey": "Desonide is spread in a thin layer and absorbed into the stratum corneum. Thin skin on the face and folds absorbs more than thick skin on palms. Diapers and skin folds trap the drug and act like an occlusive dressing. The small absorbed fraction is metabolized by the liver and excreted in urine. Relief usually begins within days, and the label calls for reassessment if there is no improvement within two weeks.", "sidefx": "Side effects are the same type as other steroids but less frequent: mild burning, dryness, atrophy, striae and perioral dermatitis, all from glucocorticoid effects on skin cells and vessels. Local immune suppression can worsen untreated infections. Absorption from large areas can still suppress the HPA axis, particularly in small children. There is no antidote; stopping usually reverses the effects.", "fun": "Topical steroids are ranked into seven US potency classes, and desonide sits near the mildest end, class VI, while halobetasol sits at class I.", "src": ["Desonide cream FDA label", "Desonate and Verdeso FDA labels", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "mometasone-topical": {"origin": "Mometasone furoate was developed by Schering-Plough in the 1980s with a clear goal: a strong topical steroid that worked once daily with less systemic effect. Its chemists put a furoate ester at the 17 position and chlorine atoms at 9 and 21, creating a very lipophilic molecule that binds the glucocorticoid receptor tightly. The FDA approved it as Elocon cream in 1987. The same molecule later became a widely used nasal spray (Nasonex) and inhaler (Asmanex), because it is potent where applied but is largely broken down by the liver after any absorption.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Schering-Plough designs mometasone furoate as a potent once-daily steroid."], ["1987", "FDA approves Elocon (mometasone furoate) for skin."], ["1997", "Mometasone nasal spray (Nasonex) is approved for allergic rhinitis."]], "cell": "Mometasone furoate enters skin cells and binds the cytosolic glucocorticoid receptor with very high affinity. The receptor-drug complex moves to the nucleus, where it induces annexin A1, which inhibits phospholipase A2 and arachidonic acid release. It also represses NF-kB and AP-1 driven genes, lowering IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha and other cytokines. The result is less recruitment of inflammatory cells, less vascular leak and vasoconstriction, plus antiproliferative effects on keratinocytes. The furoate ester helps the drug stay in skin and supports once-daily dosing.", "body": [["Skin inflammation", "Redness, itch, scaling and swelling of eczema and psoriasis decrease with once-daily use."], ["Skin structure", "Prolonged use can thin the skin through reduced collagen synthesis."], ["Eyes", "Use near the eyes can raise eye pressure or contribute to cataracts, like other corticosteroids."], ["Adrenal axis", "Large-area, prolonged or occlusive use can suppress cortisol production, with a higher risk in children."]], "journey": "Mometasone furoate partitions into the lipid-rich stratum corneum and builds a local reservoir. Its high lipophilicity and strong receptor binding give a long local effect, allowing once-daily application. Only a small fraction crosses into the blood, where it is highly protein bound and extensively metabolized by the liver, then excreted in urine and bile.", "sidefx": "Strong receptor activation in skin explains local side effects: atrophy, striae, telangiectasia and steroid acne. Suppressed local immunity can allow infections to spread. Absorbed drug can suppress the HPA axis and cause hyperglycemia or Cushing syndrome, especially in children, who have more skin area for their weight. Near the eyes, steroid effects on the trabecular meshwork and lens can cause glaucoma and cataract. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped or tapered.", "fun": "Mometasone is a rare drug that reached patients as a skin cream, a nasal spray and an asthma inhaler, all built on the same molecule.", "src": ["Elocon (mometasone furoate) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "halobetasol": {"origin": "Halobetasol propionate is one of the strongest topical steroids ever made. It was developed by Ciba-Geigy in Switzerland and introduced in the US as Ultravate around 1990. Its structure stacks every potency-boosting feature known at the time: fluorine atoms at the 6-alpha and 9-alpha positions, a chlorine at carbon 21, a 16-beta methyl group and a 17-propionate ester. This puts it in class I, the super-high potency group, used for short courses on thick psoriasis plaques and severe dermatoses. Later products added a lower-strength lotion and a combination with the retinoid tazarotene (Duobrii, approved in 2019), designed to allow longer use with less steroid exposure.", "timeline": [["c. 1990", "Halobetasol propionate is introduced in the US as Ultravate, a super-high potency steroid."], ["2019", "FDA approves Duobrii, combining halobetasol with tazarotene for plaque psoriasis."]], "cell": "Halobetasol propionate binds the glucocorticoid receptor with very high affinity. The complex moves to the nucleus, where it switches on annexin A1 (which blocks phospholipase A2 and the arachidonic acid cascade) and switches off NF-kB and AP-1 driven cytokines and adhesion molecules. In psoriasis this reduces T-cell activation and the IL-17 and IL-23 driven inflammation, and slows the rapid division of keratinocytes so plaques flatten. Its halogens and ester make it resist breakdown in skin, which keeps the receptor activated for a long time.", "body": [["Psoriatic plaques", "Thick, scaly plaques flatten and lose redness quickly because inflammation and keratinocyte overgrowth are strongly suppressed."], ["Skin structure", "The same strong antiproliferative effect causes atrophy, striae and telangiectasia if used too long or on thin skin."], ["Adrenal axis", "Even short courses over large areas can suppress the HPA axis, which is why treatment length and weekly amount are limited."], ["Eyes", "Steroid reaching the eye area can raise eye pressure and contribute to cataracts."]], "journey": "Halobetasol is applied as a thin layer and penetrates the stratum corneum, forming a reservoir. Its high potency means even modest absorption can matter. Absorption rises sharply with occlusion, large areas and thin or broken skin. The absorbed fraction is metabolized by the liver and excreted by the kidneys. Effects appear within days.", "sidefx": "Because it is super-potent, halobetasol's side effects are the extreme end of topical steroid effects. Strong suppression of fibroblasts and keratinocytes causes skin atrophy and stretch marks. HPA axis suppression and Cushing syndrome can occur, and hyperglycemia may appear with heavy use. Local immune suppression can let infection spread. Use is limited to short courses and avoided on the face and folds for some products. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped and the adrenal axis recovers over time.", "fun": "Pairing halobetasol with tazarotene in one lotion helps offset each drug's weak point: the retinoid helps counter steroid-induced skin thinning, and the steroid calms retinoid irritation.", "src": ["Ultravate (halobetasol propionate) FDA label", "Duobrii FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "desoximetasone": {"origin": "Desoximetasone was developed by the German company Hoechst and introduced as Topicort in the 1970s. It is unusual among potent topical steroids because it lacks the hydroxyl or ester group at carbon 17 that most others carry. The name reflects this: it is a 17-desoxy relative of dexamethasone. Despite missing that group, it is fluorinated and methylated and turned out to be a high-potency steroid with good penetration. It became available in creams, ointments, a gel useful for hairy areas and later a spray.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Hoechst develops desoximetasone and introduces it as Topicort."], ["2010s", "A desoximetasone spray formulation is approved for plaque psoriasis."]], "cell": "Desoximetasone binds the glucocorticoid receptor in skin cells. The receptor complex enters the nucleus and induces annexin A1, which inhibits phospholipase A2 and lowers prostaglandin and leukotriene production. It also suppresses NF-kB and AP-1 dependent genes for cytokines, chemokines and adhesion molecules, so fewer immune cells move into inflamed skin. Local vasoconstriction reduces redness. Its 9-alpha fluorine and 16-alpha methyl groups raise receptor binding and resistance to breakdown.", "body": [["Skin inflammation", "Redness, itch and scaling in eczema and psoriasis improve within days as inflammatory signaling falls."], ["Hairy areas", "The gel form spreads into the scalp and hairy skin without a greasy base, reaching inflamed skin under the hair."], ["Skin structure", "Long use can thin skin and cause stretch marks from reduced collagen and cell turnover."], ["Adrenal axis", "Absorption can suppress cortisol production; children are more susceptible, including to intracranial hypertension from steroid exposure."]], "journey": "Desoximetasone dissolves into the stratum corneum, which stores and slowly releases it. Penetration depends on the base, with ointment strongest, and rises with occlusion, inflamed skin and large areas. The absorbed fraction is metabolized by the liver and excreted in urine. Clinical effects appear within hours to days.", "sidefx": "Side effects reflect strong glucocorticoid action: thinning skin, stretch marks, visible vessels and folliculitis locally. Infections can worsen because skin immunity is suppressed. Enough absorption suppresses the HPA axis, which can cause Cushing syndrome, hyperglycemia and, in children, slowed growth or raised intracranial pressure. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped or tapered.", "fun": "Desoximetasone shows that the 17-position group is not essential for potency: the molecule reaches high potency even without the ester most strong topical steroids rely on.", "src": ["Topicort (desoximetasone) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "fluocinolone-topical": {"origin": "Fluocinolone acetonide was developed at Syntex in the late 1950s and introduced as Synalar. Syntex chemists added two fluorine atoms (at the 6-alpha and 9-alpha positions) and a 16,17 acetonide ring to the steroid core, which made a potent, skin-penetrating anti-inflammatory steroid. It later became the parent of fluocinonide. Fluocinolone itself found many niches: a scalp oil (Derma-Smoothe/FS) that can be used in children with eczema, a shampoo for seborrheic dermatitis, ear drops, and part of Tri-Luma, a melasma cream combining it with hydroquinone and tretinoin that the FDA approved in 2002. It is even used in tiny implants placed in the eye for chronic uveitis and macular edema.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Syntex develops fluocinolone acetonide, sold as Synalar."], ["2002", "FDA approves Tri-Luma, combining fluocinolone with hydroquinone and tretinoin for melasma."], ["2005", "A fluocinolone intravitreal implant (Retisert) is approved for chronic uveitis."]], "cell": "Fluocinolone acetonide binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor. The receptor-drug complex moves to the nucleus, where it increases transcription of annexin A1, which inhibits phospholipase A2, and decreases transcription of cytokine and adhesion molecule genes by blocking NF-kB and AP-1. Less arachidonic acid release means fewer prostaglandins and leukotrienes. Inflammatory cells such as T cells and eosinophils stay out of the skin, mast cells release less, and small vessels constrict. At low strengths in oil or shampoo, it delivers a milder, medium to low potency effect suited to the scalp and to children.", "body": [["Skin", "Eczema, dermatitis and psoriasis become less red and itchy as inflammatory signaling is suppressed."], ["Scalp", "The oil and shampoo reach skin under hair, easing scale and itch of seborrheic dermatitis and scalp eczema."], ["Pigment (in Tri-Luma)", "Combined with hydroquinone and tretinoin, the steroid reduces irritation from the other two and helps fade melasma."], ["Skin structure", "Long use can thin skin and cause folliculitis or stretch marks."]], "journey": "Fluocinolone in cream, oil or shampoo enters the stratum corneum and hair follicles, where it forms a local reservoir. Absorption is greater with occlusion, thin or inflamed skin and large areas. The absorbed fraction is broken down in the liver and excreted in urine. Shampoo is rinsed off, so exposure is brief. Effects begin within hours to days.", "sidefx": "Like other topical steroids, it can cause skin thinning, stretch marks, folliculitis and perioral dermatitis, from suppression of skin cells and immunity. Infections may grow under occlusion, so occlusion is stopped if infection develops. Extensive use can suppress the HPA axis and cause hyperglycemia or Cushing features. The oil contains peanut oil, which matters for people with peanut allergy. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped.", "fun": "The same steroid used in a scalp oil is also built into rice-grain-sized eye implants that release drug for years inside the eye.", "src": ["Synalar (fluocinolone acetonide) FDA label", "Derma-Smoothe/FS FDA label", "Tri-Luma FDA label"]}, "betamethasone-valerate-topical": {"origin": "Betamethasone valerate is the 17-valerate ester of betamethasone, a fluorinated steroid developed around 1960. In the early 1960s the British company Glaxo introduced it as Betnovate, and it quickly became one of the most used skin steroids in the world. Pairing the five-carbon valerate ester with the 17 position made the molecule very fat-soluble, so it penetrated skin far better than hydrocortisone. In the US it was sold as Valisone, and later as a foam (Luxiq) for the scalp. It sits in the medium-potency range and is a common comparison point in steroid potency tables.", "timeline": [["c. 1960", "Betamethasone, a fluorinated 16-beta-methyl glucocorticoid, is developed."], ["1960s", "Glaxo introduces betamethasone valerate as Betnovate in the UK."], ["1962", "McKenzie and Stoughton describe the skin blanching assay used to rank topical steroid potency."]], "cell": "Betamethasone valerate binds the glucocorticoid receptor, a nuclear receptor that waits in the cytoplasm bound to heat shock proteins. Ligand binding frees the receptor, which enters the nucleus and acts on glucocorticoid response elements. It induces annexin A1, which blocks phospholipase A2 and the formation of prostaglandins and leukotrienes, and it represses NF-kB and AP-1, lowering cytokines and adhesion molecules. The result is vasoconstriction, less edema and itch, and reduced proliferation of keratinocytes and fibroblasts. The 17-valerate ester can shift to the 21 position over time, which lowers potency, so formulation matters.", "body": [["Skin", "Redness, swelling and itch of eczema, psoriasis and contact dermatitis lessen as inflammatory mediators fall."], ["Scalp", "The lotion and foam reach the scalp skin through hair, easing scalp psoriasis and dermatitis."], ["Blood vessels", "Dermal vessels constrict, producing visible blanching that is the basis of potency testing."], ["Skin structure", "Reduced keratinocyte and fibroblast growth explains atrophy, striae and visible vessels with long use."], ["Adrenal axis", "Absorbed drug can suppress cortisol production, especially in children, with large areas or with occlusion."]], "journey": "Applied as cream, ointment, lotion or foam, betamethasone valerate enters the stratum corneum, which acts as a reservoir. Ointment gives more penetration than cream or lotion of the same strength. Occlusion, thin or inflamed skin and large areas increase absorption. Absorbed betamethasone has a plasma half-life of several hours but a longer biologic effect, is metabolized by the liver and excreted mainly in urine.", "sidefx": "Side effects come from glucocorticoid receptor activation: skin atrophy and striae from reduced collagen, telangiectasia, steroid acne and perioral dermatitis. Local immune suppression can let infection spread. Allergic contact dermatitis to the steroid itself can occur. Systemic absorption may suppress the HPA axis and raise blood sugar. Near the eyes, cataract and glaucoma are possible. There is no antidote; the product is stopped or tapered.", "fun": "Betamethasone valerate is so widely studied that it is often used as the benchmark comparator in clinical trials of new topical steroids.", "src": ["Betamethasone valerate cream FDA label", "Luxiq (betamethasone valerate) foam FDA label", "McKenzie AW, Stoughton RB. Arch Dermatol 1962"]}, "calcipotriene-betamethasone": {"origin": "This combination joins two separate ideas. The first came in the 1980s, when researchers noticed that vitamin D compounds slowed skin cell growth and that psoriasis improved in some patients taking vitamin D for bone disease. Natural active vitamin D raises blood calcium too much, so LEO Pharma in Denmark designed calcipotriol (called calcipotriene in the US), an analog that acts on skin but is broken down quickly once absorbed. It was approved in the US as Dovonex in the 1990s. The second idea is the potent steroid betamethasone dipropionate. LEO combined them in one ointment, Taclonex, approved by the FDA in 2006. The pair works better than either alone, and the steroid reduces the vitamin D analog's skin irritation. A foam (Enstilar), scalp suspension and cream (Wynzora) followed.", "timeline": [["1980s", "LEO Pharma develops calcipotriol, a vitamin D analog with low calcium effects."], ["1990s", "Calcipotriene (Dovonex) is approved in the US for plaque psoriasis."], ["2006", "FDA approves Taclonex ointment, combining calcipotriene and betamethasone dipropionate."], ["2015", "Enstilar foam version is approved."]], "cell": "Calcipotriene binds the vitamin D receptor (VDR), a nuclear receptor in keratinocytes and immune cells. VDR partners with the retinoid X receptor and binds vitamin D response elements on DNA. In psoriatic skin this slows keratinocyte proliferation, pushes cells toward normal differentiation and shifts T cells away from the IL-17 and IL-23 driven inflammation of psoriasis. Betamethasone dipropionate binds the glucocorticoid receptor, which induces annexin A1 to block phospholipase A2 and represses NF-kB driven cytokines. The two pathways attack psoriasis from different sides: one normalizes skin cell growth, the other shuts down inflammation.", "body": [["Psoriatic plaques", "Plaques flatten and lose scale and redness, often visibly within one to two weeks, as cell overgrowth and inflammation both decline."], ["Skin tolerance", "The steroid calms the burning and irritation that calcipotriene alone often causes."], ["Calcium balance", "Absorbed calcipotriene can raise blood and urine calcium, especially when large amounts are used."], ["Adrenal axis", "Absorbed betamethasone can suppress cortisol production with large areas or heavy use."], ["Skin structure", "Long-term steroid exposure risks atrophy, though calcipotriene does not itself thin skin."]], "journey": "The ointment, foam, cream or suspension is rubbed into plaques once daily. Both drugs enter the skin, and only small amounts reach the blood. Absorbed calcipotriene is rapidly broken down in the liver to inactive metabolites, which is why its effect on calcium is limited. Absorbed betamethasone is metabolized by the liver. Both leave in bile and urine. The weekly amount and body area treated are limited to keep absorption low.", "sidefx": "Calcipotriene can irritate skin and, if too much is absorbed, increase intestinal calcium absorption and bone resorption, causing hypercalcemia and hypercalciuria; the product is stopped until calcium normalizes. Betamethasone can cause skin thinning, striae, HPA axis suppression, hyperglycemia and Cushing features with heavy use. Allergic contact dermatitis is possible. There is no antidote for either; levels fall after stopping.", "fun": "Calcipotriol is cleared from the blood so quickly that its effect on blood calcium is roughly one-hundredth or less that of natural calcitriol, which is what made it practical for skin use.", "src": ["Taclonex FDA label", "Enstilar FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "crisaborole": {"origin": "Crisaborole came from Anacor Pharmaceuticals in Palo Alto, California, a company built around boron chemistry. Anacor's scientists found that benzoxaboroles, small rings containing a boron atom, could fit into enzyme active sites in unusual ways. One of these, crisaborole, inhibited phosphodiesterase 4 and was small enough to penetrate skin. The FDA approved it as Eucrisa ointment in December 2016 for mild to moderate eczema, the first new nonsteroidal prescription eczema treatment in the US in more than a decade. Pfizer bought Anacor that same year, and the approval was later extended to infants as young as 3 months.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Anacor Pharmaceuticals develops boron-based benzoxaborole drugs."], ["2016", "Pfizer acquires Anacor; FDA approves crisaborole (Eucrisa) for mild to moderate atopic dermatitis."], ["2020", "Approval is expanded to children 3 months and older."]], "cell": "Crisaborole inhibits phosphodiesterase 4 (PDE4), the main enzyme that breaks down cyclic AMP inside immune cells such as T cells, monocytes and dendritic cells, and in keratinocytes. Its boron atom binds the bimetal (zinc and magnesium) center in the PDE4 catalytic pocket. With PDE4 blocked, cAMP levels rise and activate protein kinase A. PKA phosphorylates CREB and interferes with NF-kB signaling, which reduces production of inflammatory cytokines including TNF-alpha, IL-4, IL-13, IL-23 and others involved in eczema. Less cytokine release means less inflammation and itch. Crisaborole does not act on the glucocorticoid receptor, so it does not cause skin atrophy.", "body": [["Skin", "Red, itchy eczema patches calm over a few weeks as cytokine production drops."], ["Sensitive areas", "Because it does not thin skin, it can be used on the face, eyelids and folds where steroids cause more harm."], ["Rest of the body", "Absorbed crisaborole is quickly broken down to inactive products, so systemic PDE4 effects like nausea are not prominent."]], "journey": "Crisaborole ointment is spread thinly on eczema. Its low molecular weight lets it penetrate the skin barrier. Some drug reaches the blood, where it is highly protein bound and rapidly hydrolyzed to inactive metabolites. These are further oxidized and conjugated, then excreted mainly by the kidneys. Because the circulating parent drug is short-lived and inactive metabolites dominate, effects stay local. Improvement develops over weeks.", "sidefx": "The most common side effect is burning or stinging where it is applied, which is likely irritation of inflamed skin and usually does not mean allergy. True hypersensitivity, including contact urticaria, can occur and leads to stopping the drug. Skin atrophy does not occur because there is no steroid receptor activity. There is no antidote; the ointment is washed off.", "fun": "Crisaborole and tavaborole, from the same company, both use boron, but one blocks a human immune enzyme and the other traps fungal tRNA.", "src": ["Eucrisa (crisaborole) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "roflumilast-topical": {"origin": "Roflumilast was first developed by the German company Altana (formerly Byk Gulden) as an oral PDE4 inhibitor for chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, approved in the US as Daliresp in 2011. The pill caused nausea, diarrhea and weight loss, which limited its use. Arcutis Biotherapeutics in California reasoned that applying roflumilast to the skin could deliver its anti-inflammatory power with far less body exposure. They developed a cream and foam that the FDA approved as Zoryve: cream for plaque psoriasis in 2022, foam for seborrheic dermatitis in 2023, and a lower strength cream for atopic dermatitis in 2024.", "timeline": [["2011", "Oral roflumilast (Daliresp) is approved for severe COPD."], ["2022", "FDA approves roflumilast cream (Zoryve) for plaque psoriasis."], ["2023", "Roflumilast foam is approved for seborrheic dermatitis."], ["2024", "A lower strength cream is approved for mild to moderate atopic dermatitis."]], "cell": "Roflumilast is a highly selective, potent inhibitor of phosphodiesterase 4 (PDE4), the main enzyme that degrades cyclic AMP in keratinocytes and immune cells. Blocking PDE4 raises intracellular cAMP, which activates protein kinase A and the transcription factor CREB and dampens NF-kB signaling. This reduces production of inflammatory cytokines, including TNF-alpha, IL-17, IL-23 and interferon-gamma, central to psoriasis, and type 2 cytokines involved in eczema. Its main metabolite, roflumilast N-oxide, also inhibits PDE4. Roflumilast is much more potent at PDE4 than crisaborole, which helps it work once daily.", "body": [["Skin plaques", "Psoriasis plaques become less red, thick and scaly, including in skin folds, where steroids are riskier."], ["Scalp and face", "The foam calms the redness and greasy scale of seborrheic dermatitis."], ["Itch", "Itch drops as inflammatory cytokine signaling in the skin falls."], ["Gut and appetite", "At topical exposure, nausea and diarrhea are much less common than with the oral COPD pill, but they can occur."]], "journey": "Roflumilast cream or foam is rubbed into the skin once daily. A small amount is absorbed and reaches a steady blood level. In the liver, CYP3A4 and CYP1A2 convert it to the active N-oxide metabolite. Both are highly protein bound, and the parent drug has a long effective half-life after topical use of several days. Metabolites leave mainly in urine. Strong CYP3A4 or CYP1A2 inhibitors can raise exposure.", "sidefx": "Topical roflumilast is generally well tolerated. Diarrhea, headache, insomnia and nausea are reported because some drug reaches the blood and PDE4 inhibition affects the gut and brain, as with the oral form. Local burning is less common than with crisaborole. Using it with oral roflumilast would double PDE4 inhibition. There is no antidote; it is stopped.", "fun": "Roflumilast cream is a rare case of a pill drug becoming more successful in dermatology than in its original use, by moving it from the gut to the skin.", "src": ["Zoryve (roflumilast) cream and foam FDA labels", "Daliresp (roflumilast) FDA label"]}, "tapinarof": {"origin": "Tapinarof has one of the strangest origins in dermatology. It is a small molecule first found as a natural product of Photorhabdus luminescens, a glowing bacterium that lives inside soil nematodes that invade insects. The bacterium makes the compound to help the worm and itself survive inside the insect. Researchers noticed it had anti-inflammatory activity in skin. The Canadian company Welichem Biotech developed it as benvitimod, approved in China for psoriasis in 2019. Dermavant Sciences developed it in the US, where the FDA approved it as Vtama cream for plaque psoriasis in May 2022 and for atopic dermatitis in December 2024. It is the first approved drug that works by turning on the aryl hydrocarbon receptor.", "timeline": [["2019", "Tapinarof (benvitimod) is approved in China for psoriasis."], ["2022", "FDA approves tapinarof (Vtama) cream for plaque psoriasis in adults."], ["2024", "FDA approves tapinarof for atopic dermatitis in adults and children 2 years and older."]], "cell": "Tapinarof binds and activates the aryl hydrocarbon receptor (AhR), a transcription factor that sits in the cytoplasm of keratinocytes and immune cells. When tapinarof binds, AhR moves into the nucleus and pairs with ARNT. The pair binds xenobiotic response elements on DNA and changes which genes are made. Three effects follow. Th17 cytokines IL-17A and IL-17F decrease, which drives psoriasis plaques. Skin barrier proteins such as filaggrin and loricrin increase, which helps the barrier defect in eczema. And the NRF2 antioxidant pathway turns on, reducing oxidative stress. Tapinarof does not act on the glucocorticoid receptor.", "body": [["Psoriatic skin", "Lower IL-17 signaling reduces redness, thickness and scale of plaques over weeks."], ["Eczema skin", "More barrier proteins and less inflammation reduce dryness, itch and redness in atopic dermatitis."], ["Hair follicles", "Folliculitis is the most typical side effect, likely because AhR activation affects follicle cells."], ["Rest of the body", "Plasma levels are mostly low or undetectable, so systemic immune suppression is not described."]], "journey": "Tapinarof cream is applied once daily. Most drug stays in the skin, and blood levels after use are usually low or undetectable. The small absorbed amount is highly protein bound and metabolized by the liver through several CYP pathways, sulfation and glucuronidation. Effects build over weeks, and in trials some patients stayed clear for a time after stopping, consistent with gene-level changes in skin.", "sidefx": "Folliculitis, small red bumps around hair follicles, is the hallmark side effect, thought to reflect AhR activation in follicles, and is more common under occlusion or on hairy areas. Contact dermatitis and headache are also reported. Skin atrophy is not expected because it is not a steroid. There is no antidote; the cream is stopped.", "fun": "The nematode-bacteria partners that make tapinarof can make dead insects glow, because Photorhabdus luminescens is bioluminescent.", "src": ["Vtama (tapinarof) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "delgocitinib": {"origin": "Delgocitinib was discovered by Japan Tobacco, which runs a pharmaceutical research arm. It is a pan-JAK inhibitor, blocking all four Janus kinases, designed for use on the skin. In Japan it was approved in 2020 as an ointment (Corectim) for atopic dermatitis, making it the first topical JAK inhibitor approved anywhere. LEO Pharma, the Danish dermatology company, licensed it outside Japan and developed a cream for chronic hand eczema, a hard-to-treat condition that can damage people's ability to work. The cream was approved in the European Union in 2024 and by the FDA as Anzupgo in July 2025, the first FDA-approved drug specifically for chronic hand eczema.", "timeline": [["2010s", "Japan Tobacco discovers delgocitinib, a pan-JAK inhibitor."], ["2020", "Delgocitinib ointment is approved in Japan for atopic dermatitis."], ["2024", "Delgocitinib cream is approved in the European Union for chronic hand eczema."], ["2025", "FDA approves delgocitinib cream (Anzupgo) for moderate to severe chronic hand eczema in adults."]], "cell": "Delgocitinib reversibly blocks the ATP binding site of all four Janus kinases: JAK1, JAK2, JAK3 and TYK2. These enzymes sit on the inner tails of cytokine receptors. When a cytokine such as IL-4, IL-13, IL-31, IL-22 or interferon binds its receptor, paired JAKs phosphorylate each other and then recruit and phosphorylate STAT proteins. Phosphorylated STATs travel to the nucleus and turn on inflammatory genes. By blocking the JAKs, delgocitinib stops this relay in keratinocytes and immune cells of the hand skin. Itch signaling (IL-31), type 2 inflammation (IL-4, IL-13) and barrier disruption (IL-22) are all reduced.", "body": [["Hand skin", "Itch, redness, scaling and painful cracks on the hands and wrists decrease over weeks as cytokine signaling is blocked."], ["Itch nerves", "Blocking IL-31 and related signals quiets itch quickly, often within days."], ["Immune system", "Systemic exposure is low but not zero, so the label lists class concerns such as serious infections."], ["Skin cancer risk", "Non-melanoma skin cancers are listed in the label as a potential risk."]], "journey": "Delgocitinib cream is applied to the hands and wrists. Very little reaches the blood; plasma levels are low after repeated use. The absorbed drug is mostly unchanged in circulation, has limited metabolism mainly by CYP3A4/5, and leaves mostly unchanged in urine and feces. The half-life after repeated use is about a day. Itch relief begins within days and skin clearing over weeks.", "sidefx": "Local application site reactions can occur. The label lists serious infections, non-melanoma skin cancers and the JAK inhibitor class risks seen with oral JAK drugs, because JAK enzymes support normal immune defense. With such low blood levels these risks are thought to be much smaller than with pills, but vaccinations such as zoster are usually completed beforehand. Combining it with other JAK inhibitors or strong immunosuppressants is not recommended. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Delgocitinib was the first topical JAK inhibitor approved anywhere in the world, in Japan in 2020.", "src": ["Anzupgo (delgocitinib) cream FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "clascoterone": {"origin": "Clascoterone is cortexolone 17-alpha-propionate, a steroid ester developed by the Italian company Cassiopea, part of Cosmo Pharmaceuticals. Androgens such as dihydrotestosterone drive oil glands and are a root cause of acne, but drugs that block androgens throughout the body, like spironolactone, cause hormonal side effects and are unsuitable for many males. Cassiopea's idea was an antiandrogen that works in the skin and then breaks down quickly into an inactive form. The FDA approved it as Winlevi cream in August 2020 for acne in people 12 and older. It was the first topical acne drug with a new mechanism of action in roughly four decades, and the first topical androgen receptor inhibitor for acne.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Cassiopea develops clascoterone (cortexolone 17-alpha-propionate) as a topical antiandrogen."], ["2020", "FDA approves clascoterone cream (Winlevi) for acne vulgaris."]], "cell": "In sebaceous glands, testosterone is converted by 5-alpha-reductase to dihydrotestosterone (DHT). DHT binds androgen receptors in sebocytes, the receptor moves to the nucleus and turns on genes that make sebocytes grow and produce more oil. Clascoterone competitively binds the androgen receptor in sebocytes and follicle cells, blocking DHT from activating it. Less receptor activation means less sebum production and less release of inflammatory cytokines such as IL-6 and IL-8 from sebocytes. Lower oil also starves Cutibacterium acnes, which feeds on sebum. In skin and blood, clascoterone is hydrolyzed to cortexolone, which has much weaker activity, limiting effects elsewhere.", "body": [["Oil glands", "Sebum output falls because androgen signals to the glands are blocked."], ["Acne lesions", "Both inflammatory pimples and comedones decrease over about 12 weeks."], ["Adrenal axis", "In trials, some users showed HPA axis suppression, likely because cortexolone is related to cortisol precursors; it reversed after stopping."], ["Electrolytes", "Mild rises in potassium were seen in some trial participants."]], "journey": "Clascoterone cream is applied to the skin twice daily. It penetrates to the sebaceous glands and some is absorbed, reaching steady blood levels within about five days. In the skin and blood it is hydrolyzed to cortexolone, an inactive or weak metabolite. Protein binding is moderate to high. Because it is quickly converted, systemic antiandrogen effects are minimal.", "sidefx": "Local redness, scaling, dryness and itching are the most common side effects, from irritation of treated skin. HPA axis suppression was seen in some trial participants, more likely with large areas, long use or occlusion; it is linked to the steroid structure and its metabolite. Hyperkalemia appeared in a few participants. Because systemic antiandrogen effects are minimal, the feminizing effects of oral antiandrogens are not expected. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Clascoterone gives a hormonal acne treatment option that can be used in males, since its antiandrogen action stays mostly in the skin.", "src": ["Winlevi (clascoterone) FDA label", "Hebert A et al. JAMA Dermatology 2020 (phase 3 trials)"]}, "trifarotene": {"origin": "Trifarotene was developed by Galderma, a Swiss-based dermatology company with a long history in retinoids that also created adapalene. Earlier retinoids like tretinoin bind all three retinoic acid receptor types (alpha, beta and gamma). Galderma's researchers aimed for a molecule that hit only RAR-gamma, the form most common in the epidermis, hoping for effective acne treatment with less body exposure. The FDA approved it as Aklief cream in October 2019, the first new retinoid molecule approved for acne in the US in about 20 years. It was also the first to be studied specifically for acne on the chest, shoulders and back as well as the face.", "timeline": [["1996", "Galderma's earlier retinoid adapalene is approved in the US."], ["2019", "FDA approves trifarotene (Aklief) for acne of the face and trunk."]], "cell": "Trifarotene is a selective agonist of retinoic acid receptor gamma (RAR-gamma), the main RAR in the epidermis. It enters keratinocytes and binds RAR-gamma in the nucleus. The RAR-gamma/RXR heterodimer then binds retinoic acid response elements on DNA and changes gene expression. This normalizes how follicle-lining cells divide and shed, so they stop sticking together and plugging the follicle, which prevents microcomedones (the first step of acne). It also reduces inflammatory signaling. Because it is selective and broken down quickly in the liver, effects stay mostly in the skin.", "body": [["Hair follicles", "Follicle linings shed normally, so fewer clogged pores and blackheads form."], ["Acne lesions", "Inflammatory and noninflammatory acne lesions on the face, chest and back decrease over weeks."], ["Skin surface", "Redness, scaling, dryness and stinging are common in the first weeks as cell turnover speeds up and the barrier thins."], ["Sun sensitivity", "The thinner outer layer raises the risk of sunburn."]], "journey": "Trifarotene cream is applied in the evening to clean skin. Very little reaches the blood, and plasma levels are low and short-lived. What is absorbed is very highly protein bound and quickly metabolized in the liver by CYP2C9, CYP3A4, CYP2C8 and CYP2B6, then excreted mainly in the feces. This rapid breakdown is part of why selective topical retinoids have low systemic exposure.", "sidefx": "Retinoid irritation is the main side effect: redness, scaling, dryness, stinging and burning, worst in the first month. Faster turnover thins the stratum corneum and weakens the barrier, which explains the dryness and higher sensitivity to sun, wind and cold. Waxing treated skin can tear it. Moisturizer from the start is part of the labeled regimen. There is no antidote; reducing frequency or stopping allows the skin to recover.", "fun": "Trifarotene is so selective for RAR-gamma that its effect on the other two RAR types is many times weaker, making it the first fourth-generation topical retinoid.", "src": ["Aklief (trifarotene) FDA label", "Tan J et al. J Am Acad Dermatol 2019 (phase 3 trials)"]}, "adapalene-benzoyl-peroxide": {"origin": "This gel combines two drugs with long histories. Benzoyl peroxide is an old organic peroxide used for acne since the mid-20th century because it kills acne bacteria and does not breed resistance. Adapalene was designed by Galderma researchers as a synthetic, chemically stable retinoid; it was approved in the US as Differin in 1996 and became available over the counter in 2016. Most retinoids such as tretinoin break down when mixed with benzoyl peroxide, but adapalene stays stable. That allowed Galderma to put both in one gel, approved by the FDA as Epiduo in 2008. A stronger version, Epiduo Forte, followed in 2015.", "timeline": [["1996", "FDA approves adapalene (Differin) for acne."], ["2008", "FDA approves Epiduo, the adapalene and benzoyl peroxide combination gel."], ["2015", "A higher-strength version, Epiduo Forte, is approved."], ["2016", "Adapalene alone becomes available over the counter."]], "cell": "Adapalene, a naphthoic acid derivative, binds the nuclear retinoic acid receptors beta and gamma. Activated receptors pair with RXR and change gene expression, normalizing the differentiation of keratinocytes lining hair follicles so they shed instead of sticking together. This breaks up existing comedones and prevents new microcomedones. Adapalene also reduces inflammatory signals by inhibiting AP-1 and lowering toll-like receptor 2 expression on immune cells. Benzoyl peroxide penetrates the follicle, breaks down into benzoic acid and releases reactive oxygen species that oxidize bacterial proteins, killing Cutibacterium acnes. Because it acts by oxidation rather than a single target, bacteria do not develop resistance to it.", "body": [["Follicles", "Retinoid action clears blackheads and whiteheads and prevents new plugs."], ["Bacteria", "Benzoyl peroxide lowers C. acnes counts, reducing inflamed pimples."], ["Skin surface", "Both drugs dry and irritate, causing redness, scaling and stinging, especially in the first weeks."], ["Hair and fabric", "Benzoyl peroxide is a bleaching agent and can lighten hair and colored fabric that it touches."]], "journey": "The gel is applied once daily to the face or trunk. Adapalene stays mostly in the follicles and outer skin, and plasma levels are very low. What is absorbed is minimally metabolized and eliminated mainly in bile. Benzoyl peroxide is converted to benzoic acid within the skin; any absorbed benzoic acid is conjugated with glycine to hippuric acid and excreted by the kidneys. Improvement can appear within one to four weeks, with fuller effect by about 12 weeks.", "sidefx": "Irritation is the main problem: redness, dryness, scaling, stinging and burning from faster skin turnover (adapalene) and oxidative damage to the skin surface (benzoyl peroxide). The thinned outer layer makes skin more sensitive to sun and waxing. Rare serious allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis and angioedema, have been linked to benzoyl peroxide. Bleaching of hair and fabrics is common. There is no antidote; the product is reduced or stopped.", "fun": "Mixing tretinoin with benzoyl peroxide can destroy the tretinoin by oxidation, and adapalene's resistance to this was key to making a one-tube combination.", "src": ["Epiduo and Epiduo Forte FDA labels", "Differin (adapalene) FDA label", "American Academy of Dermatology acne guidelines"]}, "clindamycin-benzoyl-peroxide": {"origin": "Clindamycin is a semisynthetic relative of lincomycin, an antibiotic made by Streptomyces lincolnensis, a soil bacterium found in a sample from near Lincoln, Nebraska. Chemists at Upjohn modified lincomycin in the 1960s by replacing a hydroxyl group with chlorine, creating clindamycin, which the FDA approved in 1970. Dermatologists soon used it on the skin for acne. Over time C. acnes became resistant to topical antibiotics used alone. Benzoyl peroxide, an old oxidizing agent that bacteria cannot resist, was added to protect clindamycin. Combination gels such as BenzaClin and Duac reached the US market in the early 2000s, and lower benzoyl peroxide versions (Acanya, Onexton) followed.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Upjohn chemists create clindamycin from lincomycin, made by Streptomyces lincolnensis."], ["1970", "FDA approves clindamycin."], ["2000s", "Clindamycin and benzoyl peroxide combination gels (BenzaClin, Duac) are approved for acne."]], "cell": "Clindamycin binds the 23S ribosomal RNA of the 50S subunit of bacterial ribosomes, near the peptidyl transferase center. This blocks peptide chain elongation, so C. acnes cannot make proteins and stops growing. With fewer bacteria, less sebum is broken down into irritating free fatty acids, and less bacterial material triggers innate immune signaling, so inflamed papules and pustules settle. Benzoyl peroxide penetrates the follicle and releases reactive oxygen species that oxidize bacterial proteins and membranes, killing C. acnes directly. It is also mildly comedolytic. Because benzoyl peroxide kills bacteria in a nonspecific way, it lowers the number of clindamycin-resistant C. acnes that would emerge with clindamycin alone.", "body": [["Follicles", "Bacteria counts in follicles fall, reducing inflamed pimples and pustules within weeks."], ["Skin surface", "Benzoyl peroxide can cause dryness, peeling and redness through oxidative irritation."], ["Gut", "Small amounts of absorbed clindamycin can rarely alter gut flora, allowing C. difficile overgrowth and colitis."], ["Hair and fabric", "Benzoyl peroxide bleaches hair, towels and colored clothing."]], "journey": "The gel is applied to clean skin once or twice daily depending on the product. Some kits are mixed by the pharmacist and have a limited shelf life. Benzoyl peroxide is converted to benzoic acid in the skin, and any absorbed benzoic acid is excreted by the kidneys as hippuric acid. A small fraction of clindamycin is absorbed, metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4 and excreted in urine and bile. Blood levels are minimal.", "sidefx": "Skin dryness, peeling and irritation come from benzoyl peroxide's oxidative action. Clindamycin's main serious risk, even when applied to skin, is C. difficile colitis, because absorbed drug can kill normal gut bacteria and let toxin-producing C. difficile grow. Erythromycin products compete for the same ribosome site and antagonize clindamycin. Rare serious allergic reactions to benzoyl peroxide have been described by the FDA. There is no antidote; the product is stopped, and C. difficile colitis is treated separately.", "fun": "Benzoyl peroxide is also used as a flour bleaching agent and in plastics manufacturing, the same oxidizing power that makes it useful against acne bacteria.", "src": ["BenzaClin and Duac FDA labels", "Cleocin (clindamycin) FDA label", "American Academy of Dermatology acne guidelines"]}, "clindamycin-tretinoin": {"origin": "This gel combines two well established acne drugs. Clindamycin, created at Upjohn in the 1960s from the Streptomyces product lincomycin and approved in 1970, reduces acne bacteria. Tretinoin, all-trans retinoic acid (the acid form of vitamin A), was shown to treat acne by dermatologist Albert Kligman and his trainee James Fulton at the University of Pennsylvania in the late 1960s, and Retin-A was approved by the FDA in 1971. Combining them in one stable gel took formulation work, because tretinoin breaks down easily. The combination was approved by the FDA in 2006 (Ziana), with Veltin and generics following.", "timeline": [["1969", "Kligman and Fulton report topical tretinoin for acne."], ["1970", "FDA approves clindamycin."], ["1971", "FDA approves tretinoin (Retin-A) for acne."], ["2006", "FDA approves a clindamycin and tretinoin combination gel (Ziana)."]], "cell": "Clindamycin binds the 23S rRNA of the bacterial 50S ribosomal subunit and blocks peptide chain elongation, stopping protein synthesis in Cutibacterium acnes. Fewer bacteria means less sebum breakdown into irritating fatty acids and less activation of innate immune receptors. Tretinoin enters keratinocytes and binds the nuclear retinoic acid receptors alpha, beta and gamma. The RAR/RXR dimer changes gene expression to speed turnover of the follicle lining and reduce the stickiness of cells, so existing comedones are pushed out and new microcomedones do not form. The two drugs work on different steps of acne: the plug and the bacteria.", "body": [["Follicles", "Faster cell turnover clears blackheads and whiteheads and prevents new plugs."], ["Bacteria", "Clindamycin lowers C. acnes numbers, reducing inflammatory papules and pustules."], ["Skin surface", "Tretinoin causes dryness, peeling and redness, and thins the outer layer, raising sunburn risk."], ["Gut", "Absorbed clindamycin can, rarely, disrupt gut flora and allow C. difficile colitis."]], "journey": "The gel is applied once at bedtime, partly because tretinoin breaks down in sunlight. Only small amounts of either drug reach the blood. Absorbed clindamycin is metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4 and excreted in urine and bile. Absorbed tretinoin is quickly isomerized and oxidized in the liver and binds tightly to albumin. Improvement appears over four to eight weeks.", "sidefx": "Tretinoin's increase in cell turnover thins the protective stratum corneum, causing dryness, peeling, redness and sun sensitivity. Clindamycin can cause C. difficile colitis even from skin use because small absorbed amounts reach the gut. Erythromycin competes for the same ribosome site and antagonizes clindamycin. Benzoyl peroxide applied at the same time can oxidize tretinoin. There is no antidote; the gel is stopped, and colitis is treated separately.", "fun": "Kligman, who helped introduce tretinoin for acne, later studied it for sun-damaged skin, which led to tretinoin being marketed for fine wrinkles.", "src": ["Ziana (clindamycin and tretinoin) FDA label", "Kligman AM et al. Arch Dermatol 1969", "Retin-A FDA label"]}, "minocycline-topical-foam": {"origin": "Minocycline is a semisynthetic tetracycline developed at Lederle Laboratories in the 1960s and approved as an oral antibiotic in the early 1970s. Oral minocycline became a common acne treatment, but it can cause dizziness, skin discoloration and rare serious reactions. Putting minocycline on the skin was hard because it breaks down in water and light. The Israeli company Foamix Pharmaceuticals developed a hydrophobic, oil-based foam that keeps minocycline stable and delivers it into hair follicles. The FDA approved it as Amzeeq for acne in October 2019, the first topical minocycline. A lower-strength foam, Zilxi, was approved in 2020 for rosacea.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Lederle Laboratories develops minocycline, a semisynthetic tetracycline."], ["1971", "Oral minocycline is approved in the US."], ["2019", "FDA approves minocycline foam (Amzeeq), the first topical minocycline, for acne."], ["2020", "Minocycline foam (Zilxi) is approved for rosacea."]], "cell": "Minocycline binds the 16S rRNA of the bacterial 30S ribosomal subunit and blocks aminoacyl-tRNA from docking at the A site, so new amino acids cannot be added and protein synthesis stops. This is bacteriostatic against Cutibacterium acnes. Minocycline also has anti-inflammatory actions separate from killing bacteria: it inhibits neutrophil chemotaxis, matrix metalloproteinases and some cytokine production. The hydrophobic foam carries the lipophilic drug into the oily pilosebaceous unit, where acne begins.", "body": [["Hair follicles", "Drug concentrates in the oily follicle, lowering C. acnes and inflammation, so inflammatory papules and pustules decrease over weeks."], ["Skin surface", "Local irritation is uncommon, since the vehicle is not drying like many acne products."], ["Rest of the body", "Blood levels are far below those from oral minocycline, so systemic tetracycline effects are much less likely, though the label carries class warnings."]], "journey": "The foam is applied once daily and melts into the skin, carrying minocycline into hair follicles. Only a small amount reaches the blood, far less than with oral dosing. Absorbed minocycline is lipophilic, partly metabolized in the liver, has a long half-life in oral data, and is excreted in urine and feces. Bathing or swimming is avoided for an hour after application so drug is not washed off.", "sidefx": "The propellant is flammable until dry, so fire and smoking are avoided after application. Because some minocycline is absorbed, the label includes tetracycline class warnings: tooth discoloration and slowed bone growth in children and in pregnancy (tetracyclines bind calcium in teeth and bone), photosensitivity, C. difficile colitis, liver injury, intracranial hypertension, autoimmune syndromes and hyperpigmentation. These are much less likely at topical exposure. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Minocycline can stain skin, nails and even bone blue-gray with long oral use, a pigmentation rarely seen with the foam because so little reaches the blood.", "src": ["Amzeeq (minocycline) topical foam FDA label", "Zilxi FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "doxycycline-low-dose-rosacea": {"origin": "Doxycycline was developed by Pfizer in the 1960s as a semisynthetic tetracycline and approved in 1967. In the 1980s and 1990s Lorne Golub and colleagues at Stony Brook University found that tetracyclines can inhibit collagen-destroying enzymes called matrix metalloproteinases, even at doses too low to kill bacteria. This led CollaGenex Pharmaceuticals to develop low-dose doxycycline (Periostat, approved in 1998) for gum disease. CollaGenex then made a capsule with immediate and delayed-release beads for rosacea, which was approved as Oracea in 2006. It was the first oral drug approved in the US for rosacea and was designed to be anti-inflammatory without selecting for resistant bacteria.", "timeline": [["1967", "FDA approves doxycycline, a Pfizer semisynthetic tetracycline."], ["1980s", "Golub and colleagues show tetracyclines inhibit matrix metalloproteinases."], ["1998", "Low-dose doxycycline (Periostat) is approved for periodontitis."], ["2006", "FDA approves Oracea for inflammatory lesions of rosacea."]], "cell": "At antibacterial doses, doxycycline binds the 30S ribosomal subunit and blocks aminoacyl-tRNA binding, stopping bacterial protein synthesis. At the low, subantimicrobial levels used for rosacea, its main effects are anti-inflammatory. It inhibits matrix metalloproteinases (such as MMP-8 and MMP-9), partly by binding the zinc and calcium these enzymes need. Lower MMP activity reduces the activation of kallikrein-5, which normally cuts cathelicidin into the inflammatory peptide LL-37 that drives rosacea redness and pustules. Doxycycline also reduces neutrophil chemotaxis and reactive oxygen species. Blood levels stay below those needed to kill most bacteria.", "body": [["Facial skin", "Inflammatory papules and pustules of rosacea decrease over 4 to 12 weeks as MMP and cathelicidin activity fall."], ["Bacteria", "At this dose it does not significantly kill bacteria or select for resistance in studies, so it is not used for infections."], ["Esophagus", "Capsules lodged in the esophagus can cause irritation and ulcers, so it is taken upright with fluid."], ["Skin and sun", "Tetracyclines can cause photosensitivity and exaggerated sunburn."]], "journey": "The capsule contains immediate-release and delayed-release beads, so blood levels stay low and steady over the day. It is taken on an empty stomach, because calcium, iron, magnesium and antacids bind doxycycline in the gut and lower absorption. It reaches peak levels in a few hours, is highly protein bound, is only minimally metabolized, and leaves largely unchanged in feces and urine. Its half-life is long, close to a day.", "sidefx": "Doxycycline chelates calcium, which explains tooth discoloration and enamel defects in young children and fetal exposure late in pregnancy. Photosensitivity comes from the drug absorbing UV light in skin. Esophageal ulcers occur when a capsule dissolves against the esophageal wall. Like other tetracyclines it can rarely cause intracranial hypertension, C. difficile colitis and drug-induced lupus. Using it with isotretinoin raises the risk of intracranial hypertension. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Low-dose doxycycline was first approved for gum disease, where its ability to block collagen-eating enzymes helped protect the tissue around teeth.", "src": ["Oracea (doxycycline) FDA label", "Golub LM and colleagues, tetracycline and matrix metalloproteinase research, 1980s", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "podofilox": {"origin": "Podofilox is purified podophyllotoxin, a lignan from the roots of the mayapple (Podophyllum peltatum) of eastern North America and its Himalayan relative. Native American healers used mayapple root as a strong purgative, and its crude resin, podophyllin, entered Western medicine in the 1800s. Podophyllotoxin was isolated from the resin in 1880. In 1942 the New Orleans physician I. W. Kaplan reported that podophyllin resin destroyed genital warts. The crude resin varied in strength and had to be applied by clinicians, so a purified, standardized form of the active compound was developed that patients could apply themselves at home. Semisynthetic podophyllotoxin derivatives also became the cancer drugs etoposide and teniposide.", "timeline": [["1880", "Podophyllotoxin is isolated from podophyllin resin."], ["1942", "Kaplan reports podophyllin resin for genital warts."], ["1983", "Etoposide, a semisynthetic podophyllotoxin derivative, is approved for cancer."]], "cell": "Podofilox binds tubulin, the protein building block of microtubules, at a site near the colchicine binding site. This stops tubulin from polymerizing into microtubules. Without microtubules, a dividing cell cannot build its mitotic spindle, so it arrests in metaphase (M phase). Wart tissue caused by human papillomavirus is full of rapidly dividing keratinocytes, so these cells are especially sensitive. Arrested cells die, and the wart tissue becomes necrotic and sloughs off over several days. The local inflammation that follows peaks a few days after application. The drug does not kill the virus itself, which is why warts can come back. Etoposide works differently, by inhibiting topoisomerase II.", "body": [["Wart tissue", "Visible genital and perianal warts become inflamed, turn white or dark, erode and fall off over days as the dividing cells die."], ["Surrounding skin", "Healthy skin touched by the drug can become red, sore and eroded because skin cells there also divide."], ["Rest of the body", "Systemic absorption is limited when applied to small areas as labeled, but large amounts could cause toxicity, which is why the treated area is limited."]], "journey": "Podofilox gel or solution is applied only to visible wart tissue and allowed to dry. Most drug stays in the wart. Some is absorbed, more so from large areas or broken skin. Data on absorbed podofilox are limited, with a short half-life of a few hours. Treatment runs in weekly cycles of a few days on and a few days off, giving inflamed tissue time to slough and heal.", "sidefx": "Because podofilox kills dividing cells, it injures nearby healthy skin, causing burning, pain, inflammation and erosion. These effects peak in the first week. Too much drug or treating too large an area increases absorption, and systemic podophyllin poisoning (seen with the crude resin) can cause nausea, nerve damage and bone marrow suppression, the same pathway behind microtubule toxicity. The diagnosis is confirmed before use because cancerous lesions can look like warts. There is no antidote; it is washed off and care is supportive.", "fun": "Mayapple was used by Native American peoples as a purgative and also as a poison, and its toxin turned out to be the parent of a widely used chemotherapy drug.", "src": ["Condylox (podofilox) FDA label", "Kaplan IW. New Orleans Med Surg J 1942", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "ivermectin-topical": {"origin": "Ivermectin traces back to a soil sample collected in the 1970s near a golf course in Shizuoka Prefecture, Japan, by Satoshi Omura of the Kitasato Institute. Omura cultured a new bacterium, Streptomyces avermitilis, and sent it to William C. Campbell's team at Merck in New Jersey. They found the broth killed parasitic worms in mice and isolated the avermectins. A slightly modified form, ivermectin, was launched for animals in 1981 and approved for people in 1987, when Merck began donating it to fight river blindness. Omura and Campbell shared the 2015 Nobel Prize. Ivermectin skin products came later: a lotion for head lice (Sklice, 2012) and a cream for rosacea (Soolantra, 2014), which targets Demodex mites on the face.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Satoshi Omura isolates Streptomyces avermitilis from Japanese soil; Merck scientists find antiparasitic avermectins."], ["1981", "Ivermectin is introduced for veterinary use."], ["1987", "Merck begins donating ivermectin (Mectizan) for river blindness."], ["2014", "FDA approves ivermectin cream (Soolantra) for inflammatory lesions of rosacea."], ["2015", "Omura and Campbell share the Nobel Prize for avermectin discovery."]], "cell": "Ivermectin binds glutamate-gated chloride channels found only in invertebrate nerve and muscle cells. Binding locks the channels open, so chloride flows in and the cells hyperpolarize. The mite or worm becomes paralyzed and dies. Mammals do not have glutamate-gated chloride channels, and ivermectin is kept out of the human brain by P-glycoprotein at the blood-brain barrier, which protects human GABA-A channels from it. In rosacea, Demodex folliculorum mites are more numerous in affected skin, and their bodies and associated bacteria trigger inflammation. Killing the mites lowers this trigger. Ivermectin also has anti-inflammatory effects of its own, reducing cytokine release and neutrophil activity.", "body": [["Facial skin", "Inflammatory bumps and pimples of rosacea decrease by about four weeks and more by twelve as Demodex counts and inflammation fall."], ["Mites", "Demodex mites in hair follicles are paralyzed and die."], ["Redness", "Background redness may improve less, since the cream targets lesions rather than blood vessels."], ["Rest of the body", "Plasma levels are very low, so systemic effects are not expected with labeled use."]], "journey": "Ivermectin cream is applied once daily to the face. A small amount is absorbed, reaching low steady blood levels after about two weeks. The absorbed drug is very lipophilic and highly protein bound, is metabolized by CYP3A4 in the liver, and leaves mostly in the feces. Its plasma half-life after topical use is about six days. The lotion for lice is applied once to dry hair and rinsed off.", "sidefx": "The main side effects are skin burning and irritation where applied. A temporary flare of rosacea can occur early, possibly as mites die and release inflammatory material. Systemic toxicity, which with large oral overdoses involves the nervous system, is not expected because so little is absorbed and P-glycoprotein limits brain entry. There is no antidote; the cream is stopped.", "fun": "Demodex mites live in the hair follicles of most adults, and people with rosacea tend to carry more of them.", "src": ["Soolantra (ivermectin) cream FDA label", "Sklice (ivermectin) lotion FDA label", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 2015 announcement"]}, "brimonidine-topical": {"origin": "Brimonidine is an alpha-2 adrenergic agonist developed by Allergan for glaucoma. As eye drops (Alphagan), approved in 1996, it lowers eye pressure by reducing fluid production. Its ability to constrict small blood vessels made it a candidate for the persistent redness of rosacea, a problem that had no approved treatment. Galderma developed a facial gel, which the FDA approved as Mirvaso in 2013, the first drug approved specifically for the facial redness (erythema) of rosacea. It reduces redness for several hours but does not cure rosacea.", "timeline": [["1996", "Brimonidine eye drops (Alphagan) are approved for glaucoma."], ["2013", "FDA approves brimonidine gel (Mirvaso) for persistent facial erythema of rosacea."]], "cell": "Brimonidine is a highly selective agonist of alpha-2 adrenergic receptors. In the face, alpha-2 receptors are found on the smooth muscle of small superficial dermal arterioles and veins. Brimonidine binding activates the inhibitory G protein (Gi), which lowers cyclic AMP and favors smooth muscle contraction. The small vessels narrow, and less blood fills the skin, so redness fades within about 30 minutes. Because the drug acts directly on vessels, the effect lasts only as long as it stays on the receptors, about up to 12 hours. In the brain, alpha-2 stimulation lowers sympathetic outflow, which is why absorbed drug can lower blood pressure.", "body": [["Facial vessels", "Small vessels constrict, reducing persistent redness for several hours after application."], ["Redness rebound", "As the effect wears off, some people have redness worse than before, or flushing, from vessels re-dilating."], ["Heart and vessels", "Absorbed drug can lower blood pressure and heart rate by reducing sympathetic outflow from the brainstem."], ["Brain", "Dizziness and sleepiness can occur, more so with accidental swallowing."]], "journey": "The gel is spread in a thin, even layer over the face once daily. Redness begins to fade within about 30 minutes and is lowest at about 3 to 6 hours. A small amount is absorbed into the blood, where it has a half-life of about three hours. The liver extensively metabolizes it, and metabolites are excreted by the kidneys. Accidental ingestion by young children can produce high blood levels.", "sidefx": "Rebound or worsened redness and flushing can happen as vasoconstriction fades and vessels re-open. Absorbed drug can lower blood pressure and heart rate, and the label warns about potentiating vascular insufficiency such as Raynaud phenomenon. Young children who swallowed the gel have had serious slowing of breathing, low heart rate, low blood pressure and coma, because alpha-2 stimulation in the brain is strong. Allergic contact dermatitis is possible. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "The same molecule that whitens rosacea redness also lightens bloodshot eyes as a low-strength eye drop sold over the counter.", "src": ["Mirvaso (brimonidine) gel FDA label", "Alphagan P FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "oxymetazoline-topical": {"origin": "Oxymetazoline is an imidazoline vasoconstrictor developed at the German company E. Merck in the early 1960s and best known as a nasal decongestant spray, sold in the US as Afrin. Like brimonidine, its ability to constrict small blood vessels made it a candidate for the persistent redness of rosacea. Allergan developed a facial cream, approved by the FDA as Rhofade in January 2017. It became the second approved drug for rosacea redness. A related eye drop (Upneeq), approved in 2020, uses oxymetazoline to lift a drooping eyelid by tightening a small eyelid muscle.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Oxymetazoline is developed in Germany as a vasoconstrictor."], ["2017", "FDA approves oxymetazoline cream (Rhofade) for persistent facial erythema of rosacea."], ["2020", "Oxymetazoline eye drops (Upneeq) are approved for acquired eyelid ptosis."]], "cell": "Oxymetazoline is an agonist at alpha-1A adrenergic receptors, with partial alpha-2 activity. Alpha-1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle signal through the Gq protein, which activates phospholipase C, makes IP3 and raises intracellular calcium. Calcium drives smooth muscle contraction, so small dermal arterioles in the face narrow and less blood fills the skin. Redness fades within about an hour and stays reduced for much of the day. Its alpha-2 action adds to vasoconstriction. The same mechanism shrinks swollen nasal blood vessels when used as a spray.", "body": [["Facial vessels", "Constriction of superficial dermal vessels reduces persistent redness, starting within about an hour and lasting up to about 12 hours."], ["Facial skin", "Application site dermatitis and worsening of inflammatory bumps can occur in some people."], ["Heart and vessels", "Absorbed drug could raise blood pressure in people with heart disease and can worsen vascular insufficiency."], ["Eyes", "If it reaches the eye in a person with narrow angles, it can dilate the pupil and trigger angle-closure glaucoma."]], "journey": "The cream is applied once daily in a thin layer over the face, avoiding eyes and lips, and hands are washed immediately afterward. A small amount is absorbed into the blood. Oxymetazoline undergoes little metabolism in the liver and is excreted by the kidneys and feces. The effect begins within about an hour, peaks in 3 to 6 hours and lasts up to about 12 hours.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow from alpha-adrenergic stimulation. Absorbed drug can raise blood pressure and narrow vessels elsewhere, which matters in heart disease and Raynaud phenomenon. Alpha stimulation can dilate the pupil and precipitate angle-closure glaucoma. Local dermatitis and worsening papules can occur. Using it with oxymetazoline nasal sprays or eye drops adds exposure. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Oxymetazoline nasal spray is the classic cause of rebound congestion (rhinitis medicamentosa) when used for more than a few days, a reminder of how blood vessels can adapt to repeated vasoconstriction.", "src": ["Rhofade (oxymetazoline) cream FDA label", "Upneeq FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "hydroquinone": {"origin": "Hydroquinone is a simple phenol long used in photography as a film developer. Its ability to lighten skin was noticed in the mid-20th century, and in 1965 dermatologists Kenneth Arndt and Thomas Fitzpatrick published a study of topical hydroquinone as a depigmenting agent. In 1975 Albert Kligman and Willis described a combination of hydroquinone, tretinoin and a steroid for melasma, which inspired Tri-Luma, an FDA-approved triple combination cream from 2002. Single-ingredient prescription hydroquinone creams, however, have been marketed in the US without FDA approval. Over-the-counter hydroquinone products were removed from legal sale in the US after the 2020 OTC monograph reform under the CARES Act.", "timeline": [["1965", "Arndt and Fitzpatrick report topical hydroquinone as a depigmenting agent."], ["1975", "Kligman and Willis describe a hydroquinone, tretinoin and steroid formula for melasma."], ["2002", "FDA approves Tri-Luma, a hydroquinone, tretinoin and fluocinolone combination."], ["2020", "OTC hydroquinone products lose legal US marketing status under OTC monograph reform."]], "cell": "Melanin is made in melanocytes inside organelles called melanosomes. The key enzyme is tyrosinase, a copper-containing enzyme that oxidizes tyrosine to DOPA and DOPA to dopaquinone, the starting point for melanin. Hydroquinone is structurally similar to tyrosine and acts as an alternative substrate and inhibitor of tyrosinase, so less dopaquinone and less melanin are made. Hydroquinone is also oxidized into reactive quinones and oxygen radicals that damage melanosome membranes and melanocytes, adding to its effect. Existing pigment is shed slowly as skin cells turn over, which is why lightening takes weeks to months.", "body": [["Pigmented skin", "Dark patches of melasma, age spots and post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation gradually lighten as new melanin production falls."], ["Sun-exposed skin", "UV light strongly stimulates melanocytes, so without sunscreen the pigment returns or worsens."], ["Skin (long term)", "Prolonged use, especially at high strength, can cause exogenous ochronosis, a blue-black darkening from pigment deposits in the dermis."], ["Skin surface", "Redness, stinging and contact dermatitis can occur."]], "journey": "Hydroquinone cream is applied only to the dark areas. Some drug is absorbed through the skin and conjugated in the liver, then excreted by the kidneys as sulfate and glucuronide conjugates. Systemic exposure at topical use is not considered clinically relevant. Its effect depends on continued use and sun protection; lightening is usually seen over two to three months.", "sidefx": "Irritation and contact dermatitis come from the reactive, oxidizing nature of hydroquinone and its quinones. Exogenous ochronosis, a paradoxical darkening, is thought to arise when a related enzyme, homogentisic acid oxidase, is inhibited in the skin, leading to deposits of pigment-like polymers in the dermis. Peroxide products on the same area can cause temporary dark staining. Some products contain sulfites. There is no antidote; it is stopped, and ochronosis can be slow to fade.", "fun": "Hydroquinone was a standard ingredient in black-and-white photo developers because it reduces silver salts to metallic silver, the same oxidizing and reducing chemistry it shows in skin.", "src": ["Tri-Luma FDA label", "Arndt KA, Fitzpatrick TB. JAMA 1965", "FDA notice on OTC hydroquinone after the CARES Act"]}, "capsaicin-topical": {"origin": "Capsaicin is the compound that makes chili peppers hot. Peppers of the genus Capsicum were domesticated in the Americas thousands of years ago and spread worldwide after the 1500s. An impure extract was obtained in 1816 by the German pharmacist Christian Friedrich Bucholz, and in 1876 the English chemist John Clough Thresh isolated it in purer form and named it capsaicin. Low-strength creams were used for pain for decades. The molecule's target, the TRPV1 receptor, was cloned in 1997 by David Julius's lab at UCSF, work that shared the 2021 Nobel Prize. A high-strength patch, Qutenza, was approved by the FDA in 2009 for postherpetic neuralgia and in 2020 for painful diabetic neuropathy of the feet.", "timeline": [["1816", "Bucholz obtains an impure pungent extract from peppers."], ["1876", "Thresh isolates and names capsaicin."], ["1997", "Julius's lab clones TRPV1, the capsaicin receptor."], ["2009", "FDA approves the high-strength capsaicin patch (Qutenza) for postherpetic neuralgia."], ["2021", "Julius shares the Nobel Prize for discovering temperature and touch receptors."]], "cell": "Capsaicin binds TRPV1 (transient receptor potential vanilloid 1), a ligand-gated cation channel on the endings of pain-sensing C fibers and some A-delta fibers. TRPV1 normally opens with heat above about 43 C and with acid. Capsaicin opens it, letting sodium and calcium flood in, so the nerve fires and releases substance P and CGRP; this is the burning feeling. With high or repeated exposure, the large calcium influx overwhelms the nerve ending. Mitochondria fail, calcium-dependent enzymes break down the cytoskeleton, and the fine nerve terminals in the epidermis retract. This process, called defunctionalization, leaves the area less able to send pain signals for weeks to months until fibers regrow.", "body": [["Skin nerves", "Pain fiber endings in the treated area first fire intensely, then retract, reducing neuropathic pain for up to about three months after one patch."], ["Skin", "Redness and burning appear during application from substance P and CGRP release, which dilate vessels."], ["Heart and vessels", "Pain during the procedure can transiently raise blood pressure."], ["Airway and eyes", "Airborne capsaicin irritates eyes, nose and throat, causing coughing and sneezing."]], "journey": "The high-strength patch is applied in a clinic for 30 to 60 minutes, then removed and the area cleansed with a special gel. Capsaicin penetrates skin to the nerve endings. Systemic levels are low and short-lived, with a half-life of under two hours, and absorbed drug is metabolized by liver CYP enzymes. Low-strength creams are rubbed in several times daily and work gradually as nerve endings are repeatedly desensitized.", "sidefx": "Burning pain during and after application comes directly from TRPV1 activation. Redness and swelling follow neuropeptide release. Pain can transiently raise blood pressure. Reduced sensation in the treated area follows nerve terminal retraction. Contact with eyes and mucous membranes causes severe burning, and inhaled particles cause coughing. Cooling and the cleansing gel reduce the burn. There is no antidote; capsaicin is lipophilic, so oil-based cleansers remove it better than water.", "fun": "Birds lack a capsaicin-sensitive TRPV1, so they eat chili peppers without feeling the burn and spread the seeds.", "src": ["Qutenza (capsaicin) FDA label", "Caterina MJ, Julius D et al. Nature 1997", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 2021 announcement"]}, "pramoxine": {"origin": "Pramoxine (also called pramocaine) is a synthetic local anesthetic introduced in the 1950s. Most local anesthetics belong to two chemical families, esters such as benzocaine and amides such as lidocaine. Pramoxine is a morpholine ether and belongs to neither, which turned out to be useful: people allergic to ester or amide anesthetics usually do not react to it. It is too irritating for injection and is used only on skin and mucous membranes. Today it is a common over-the-counter ingredient in anti-itch lotions, hemorrhoid creams and combinations with hydrocortisone or calamine.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Pramoxine is introduced as a topical anesthetic distinct from ester and amide types."]], "cell": "Pramoxine reversibly blocks voltage-gated sodium channels in the membranes of sensory nerve endings in the skin. When an itch or pain stimulus arrives, these channels normally open and let sodium rush in to fire an action potential. Pramoxine binds inside the channel and stabilizes it in an inactive state, so the nerve cannot depolarize enough to fire and the signal never reaches the spinal cord. Both itch fibers and pain fibers are small and lightly myelinated or unmyelinated, so they are blocked easily. Its ether link and morpholine ring make it chemically different from ester and amide anesthetics, which explains low cross-allergy.", "body": [["Skin nerves", "Itch and pain from bites, minor burns, rashes and poison ivy are dulled within minutes."], ["Anorectal area", "In hemorrhoid products it numbs the sensitive anal skin, easing itching and burning."], ["Rest of the body", "Absorption is negligible, so systemic effects are not expected."]], "journey": "Pramoxine is applied as a lotion, cream, gel or foam directly on the itchy or painful area. It acts within minutes on nerve endings just under the surface and lasts several hours. Very little is absorbed, and systemic metabolism is not well characterized. Its effect wears off as it diffuses away from the nerve endings and is washed off.", "sidefx": "Side effects are uncommon and local: stinging, dryness or allergic contact dermatitis. Because it does not reach meaningful blood levels, the CNS and heart toxicity seen with large absorbed amounts of lidocaine or benzocaine methemoglobinemia are not expected. Eye contact is avoided because it irritates. There is no antidote; the product is washed off.", "fun": "Pramoxine is often chosen as a numbing agent for people who report allergy to the 'caine' anesthetics, because its chemical family is different.", "src": ["OTC external analgesic monograph (21 CFR 348)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "zinc-oxide": {"origin": "Zinc oxide is one of the oldest skin remedies. Ancient Greek, Roman and Indian medical writers described zinc-containing ores and ash used in ointments for wounds and eye problems. It became a standard ingredient in European pharmacies, appearing in pastes such as Lassar's paste in the 1800s. In the 20th century it became the main ingredient of diaper rash ointments and a mineral sunscreen. In 2019 the FDA proposed that only two sunscreen active ingredients, zinc oxide and titanium dioxide, had enough data to be considered generally recognized as safe and effective.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Zinc-containing preparations are used in ointments for wounds and skin by Greek, Roman and Indian healers."], ["1800s", "Zinc oxide pastes become standard in European dermatology."], ["2019", "FDA proposes zinc oxide and titanium dioxide as the sunscreen actives generally recognized as safe and effective."]], "cell": "Zinc oxide works mostly physically rather than through a receptor. It is an insoluble white powder that, in a paste or ointment, forms a coating over the skin. This barrier keeps urine, stool, enzymes and moisture away from irritated skin, so the outer layer can repair itself. Zinc oxide is mildly astringent, tightening surface proteins and drying weeping areas, and has mild antiseptic action. Zinc ions released in small amounts may support wound healing because many skin enzymes need zinc. As a sunscreen, zinc oxide particles scatter and absorb ultraviolet light across both UVB and UVA ranges, protecting skin cells from DNA damage.", "body": [["Diaper area", "A thick layer shields irritated skin from urine and stool, letting diaper rash heal."], ["Chafed skin", "The barrier reduces friction and moisture, helping raw skin recover."], ["Sun protection", "Particles reflect, scatter and absorb UVA and UVB, giving broad-spectrum protection."], ["Rest of the body", "Zinc oxide is not meaningfully absorbed through intact skin."]], "journey": "Zinc oxide sits on top of the skin. It is not absorbed in meaningful amounts through intact skin, so there is no metabolism or excretion to speak of. It is removed by washing, which is why it is reapplied with diaper changes or after swimming. Its effect begins immediately once the barrier forms.", "sidefx": "Topical zinc oxide is very safe. Side effects are rare, such as irritation or allergy to other ingredients in the base. A thick paste can trap bacteria or yeast if infected skin is not cleaned first. Large amounts swallowed can upset the stomach. Inhaling zinc oxide fumes, as in welding galvanized metal, causes metal fume fever, which is unrelated to skin use. There is no antidote needed.", "fun": "Calamine lotion is mostly zinc oxide with a little iron oxide added, which gives it its pink color.", "src": ["OTC skin protectant monograph (21 CFR 347)", "FDA proposed rule on sunscreen active ingredients, 2019"]}, "lidocaine-topical": {"origin": "Lidocaine was synthesized in 1943 by Swedish chemists Nils Lofgren and Bengt Lundqvist at Stockholm University. They were studying compounds related to gramine, a plant alkaloid that numbed the tongue. Lundqvist tested the new compound on himself. It proved more reliable and less allergenic than older ester anesthetics like procaine. The Swedish company Astra marketed it as Xylocaine in 1948, and it became the most widely used local anesthetic in the world. Topical forms such as ointment, jelly and viscous solution numb skin and mucous membranes, and lidocaine is also given by injection and IV as an antiarrhythmic.", "timeline": [["1943", "Lofgren and Lundqvist synthesize lidocaine in Stockholm."], ["1948", "Astra launches lidocaine as Xylocaine."], ["1960s", "Lidocaine becomes standard IV treatment for ventricular arrhythmias."]], "cell": "Lidocaine is an amide local anesthetic. In tissue it exists as both an uncharged base and a charged form. The uncharged base crosses the nerve membrane. Inside, it picks up a proton and the charged form binds a site inside the pore of voltage-gated sodium channels (such as Nav1.7 and Nav1.8 on pain fibers). It binds best to open and inactivated channels, so nerves that fire often are blocked first (use-dependent block). With sodium channels blocked, the nerve cannot reach threshold and action potentials stop, so pain signals do not travel. Small, unmyelinated pain and temperature fibers block before larger touch and motor fibers. Inflamed, acidic tissue holds more drug in the charged form outside the nerve, which slows onset.", "body": [["Skin", "Minor burns, scrapes and insect bites become numb within minutes."], ["Mouth and throat", "Mucosa numbs in 2 to 5 minutes, easing pain from sores or procedures; swallowing reflexes are also blunted, raising aspiration risk."], ["Airway devices", "Lidocaine jelly lubricates and numbs during tube or catheter placement."], ["Brain", "Too much absorbed lidocaine causes numb lips, ringing ears, agitation and seizures, then CNS depression."], ["Heart", "High levels slow conduction and can cause low blood pressure, slow heart rate and arrest."]], "journey": "Topical lidocaine is absorbed little through intact skin but readily through mucous membranes and broken skin. Absorbed drug binds alpha-1 acid glycoprotein and is metabolized in the liver by CYP1A2 and CYP3A4 to the active metabolites MEGX and GX, which are excreted by the kidneys. The systemic half-life is about one and a half to two hours. Numbness lasts about 30 to 60 minutes on mucosa and longer on skin.", "sidefx": "Local anesthetic systemic toxicity occurs when too much is absorbed, such as with large mucosal areas or several lidocaine products together. Sodium channel block in the brain first removes inhibitory neurons (causing agitation and seizures), then depresses the whole CNS. In the heart, sodium channel block slows conduction and depresses contraction. Intravenous lipid emulsion is used as a rescue therapy for severe toxicity. Methemoglobinemia can occur. Numbing the throat can cause aspiration and bite injuries.", "fun": "Bengt Lundqvist tested lidocaine on himself, including nerve block injections, before it was studied in patients.", "src": ["Xylocaine (lidocaine) ointment FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "diphenhydramine-topical": {"origin": "Diphenhydramine was discovered in 1943 by George Rieveschl, a chemistry professor at the University of Cincinnati, while he was searching for drugs to relax muscle spasms. A student working with him helped make the compound, and it proved to be a strong antihistamine. Parke-Davis developed it as Benadryl, and in 1946 it became the first prescription antihistamine approved by the FDA. Its sedating effect later made it a sleep aid as well. Topical diphenhydramine creams and sprays, often with zinc acetate, are sold over the counter for insect bites and poison ivy itch.", "timeline": [["1943", "George Rieveschl's team at the University of Cincinnati discovers diphenhydramine."], ["1946", "FDA approves Benadryl, the first prescription antihistamine in the US."]], "cell": "Histamine released from mast cells in skin binds H1 receptors on sensory nerve endings and small blood vessels. On nerves this causes itch; on vessels it causes dilation and leak, making wheals. Diphenhydramine is an inverse agonist at H1 receptors: it binds and stabilizes the receptor in its inactive state, so histamine signaling through the Gq pathway is blocked. It also blocks voltage-gated sodium channels, giving a local anesthetic effect that dulls itch and pain directly. In combination products, zinc acetate is an astringent that dries oozing rashes. As a first-generation antihistamine it also blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, which matters if much is absorbed.", "body": [["Skin", "Itch and pain from insect bites, minor burns and plant rashes decrease within minutes as H1 signaling and nerve firing are blocked."], ["Weeping rashes", "Zinc acetate in some products dries oozing blisters."], ["Brain", "If absorbed in large amounts it crosses into the brain, causing drowsiness or, in children, confusion and agitation."], ["Autonomic system", "Absorbed drug blocks muscarinic receptors, causing dry mouth, flushing, fast heart rate and urinary retention."]], "journey": "Applied to intact skin, only a small amount is absorbed. Absorption rises sharply when it is used on large areas, broken skin, or blistering rashes like chickenpox. Absorbed diphenhydramine is metabolized in the liver mainly by CYP2D6 and excreted in urine, with a half-life of several hours. Topical effects start within minutes and last several hours.", "sidefx": "Repeated use can cause allergic contact dermatitis, because the drug can sensitize the skin. When large amounts are absorbed, especially in children or when combined with oral diphenhydramine, anticholinergic toxicity can occur: hot dry skin, flushing, dilated pupils, fast heart rate, urinary retention, confusion and hallucinations. This follows from muscarinic receptor blockade in the brain and body. In selected severe cases physostigmine can reverse anticholinergic delirium; otherwise care is supportive.", "fun": "Rieveschl was originally looking for a muscle relaxant, and diphenhydramine's reliable drowsiness later made it the active ingredient in many over-the-counter sleep aids.", "src": ["Benadryl Itch Stopping Cream Drug Facts label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "abametapir": {"origin": "Abametapir was developed by the Australian company Hatchtech, which focused on a weak point of head lice treatment: most older products kill live lice but not all their eggs, so treatment often had to be repeated and nits combed out. Hatchtech studied metalloproteinase enzymes that louse eggs need to develop and hatch, and found that abametapir, a simple bipyridine metal chelator, blocks them. The FDA approved it as Xeglyze lotion in July 2020 for head lice in people 6 months and older, given as a single application.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Hatchtech in Australia develops abametapir as a louse egg metalloproteinase inhibitor."], ["2020", "FDA approves abametapir lotion (Xeglyze) for head lice."]], "cell": "Abametapir (5,5'-dimethyl-2,2'-bipyridine) is a metal chelator. Louse eggs depend on metalloproteinases, enzymes that need metal ions such as zinc to work, for development of the embryo, hatching and early survival. Abametapir binds these metal ions and blocks the enzymes, so eggs fail to develop or hatch. It is also toxic to live lice through the same enzyme inhibition. Because it acts on both lice and eggs (lousicidal and ovicidal), a single treatment is usually enough, and nit combing or a second application is not required in most cases.", "body": [["Scalp and hair", "Lice and unhatched eggs die after a single application of about ten minutes, then the lotion is rinsed out."], ["Scalp skin", "Redness, irritation and a burning feeling can occur."], ["Liver enzymes", "Absorbed abametapir inhibits CYP3A4, CYP2B6 and CYP1A2 for about two weeks, raising levels of some other drugs."], ["Infants", "The lotion contains benzyl alcohol, which can be dangerous for infants under 6 months."]], "journey": "The lotion is applied to dry hair and scalp, left on for a short time and rinsed out. Some drug is absorbed, peaking in the blood within hours. The liver metabolizes it by CYP1A2 to a carboxyl metabolite, which lasts longer in the blood than the parent drug. Abametapir has a half-life of under a day, and its metabolites are excreted mostly in urine. The enzyme inhibition explains why the label advises avoiding certain other drugs for two weeks.", "sidefx": "Local effects include scalp redness, irritation, rash and eye irritation, because the lotion contacts skin and can drip into eyes. The benzyl alcohol vehicle can cause gasping syndrome in very young infants, where the immature liver cannot clear benzyl alcohol and acid builds up. Swallowing the lotion is dangerous, so children are supervised during use. Drug interactions follow CYP inhibition. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Abametapir was chosen because louse eggs, unlike many lice treatments' targets, rely on metal-dependent enzymes to break out of their shells.", "src": ["Xeglyze (abametapir) lotion FDA label", "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention head lice treatment guidance"]}, "tirbanibulin": {"origin": "Tirbanibulin was discovered by Kinex Pharmaceuticals (later Athenex) in Buffalo, New York, and was first studied as an oral cancer drug. It disrupts microtubules and interferes with Src kinase signaling, two features that slow dividing cells. Researchers later tested it as an ointment for actinic keratoses, the rough precancerous patches caused by sun damage. The Spanish company Almirall licensed it for dermatology. The FDA approved it as Klisyri in December 2020, notable for a short five-day treatment course compared with weeks for older field treatments such as 5-fluorouracil or imiquimod.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Kinex Pharmaceuticals develops tirbanibulin as an anticancer microtubule and Src signaling inhibitor."], ["2020", "FDA approves tirbanibulin ointment (Klisyri) for actinic keratoses of the face and scalp."]], "cell": "Tirbanibulin binds tubulin at the colchicine site and blocks tubulin polymerization, so microtubules break down. Without microtubules, dividing cells cannot form a mitotic spindle and arrest in the G2/M phase of the cell cycle. Arrested cells undergo apoptosis. Atypical keratinocytes in actinic keratoses divide much faster than normal skin cells, so they are hit hardest. Tirbanibulin also interferes with Src tyrosine kinase signaling, which supports cell growth and migration. The result is selective loss of abnormal cells in the treated field, followed by healing.", "body": [["Sun-damaged skin", "Actinic keratoses in the treated field are cleared as abnormal dividing cells die."], ["Skin surface", "Redness, flaking, crusting and swelling appear, peaking around day 8, and usually fade within weeks."], ["Eyes", "Ointment transferred near the eyes can cause irritation."], ["Rest of the body", "Plasma levels are low after use on a limited area."]], "journey": "Tirbanibulin ointment is applied once daily for five days to a defined area on the face or balding scalp. The area is not washed or touched for about eight hours after application. Only low levels reach the blood. The absorbed drug is metabolized mainly by CYP3A4 and, to a lesser degree, CYP2C8, with a short half-life. Clearance of lesions is assessed about eight weeks after the course.", "sidefx": "The local skin reactions, including redness, scaling, crusting, swelling, erosion and blisters, are directly linked to the drug killing dividing cells in the treated area and the inflammation that follows. They are typically milder and shorter than with 5-fluorouracil because the course is so brief. Eye irritation happens if ointment is spread to the eyelids. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Tirbanibulin began life as an oral cancer drug and found its approval instead as a five-day ointment for sun damage.", "src": ["Klisyri (tirbanibulin) ointment FDA label", "Blauvelt A et al. N Engl J Med 2021 (phase 3 trials)"]}, "berdazimer": {"origin": "Berdazimer sodium was developed by Novan, a North Carolina company built on nitric oxide chemistry research at the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill. Nitric oxide is a gas the body makes to fight microbes and control blood vessels; its role as a signal molecule earned the 1998 Nobel Prize. The challenge was delivering a short-lived gas into skin. Novan attached nitric oxide-releasing groups to a large silica-based polymer that releases the gas only when mixed with a hydrogel. The FDA approved it as Zelsuvmi in January 2024 for molluscum contagiosum, a contagious poxvirus skin infection common in children, as the first prescription treatment that can be applied at home.", "timeline": [["1998", "Nobel Prize awarded for the discovery of nitric oxide as a signaling molecule."], ["2000s", "Novan develops nitric oxide-releasing polymers based on university research in North Carolina."], ["2024", "FDA approves berdazimer gel (Zelsuvmi) for molluscum contagiosum."]], "cell": "Berdazimer sodium is a large polysiloxane molecule carrying N-diazeniumdiolate groups. On its own it is stable. When it is mixed with the acidic hydrogel from the second tube, the diazeniumdiolate groups are protonated and release nitric oxide gas directly onto molluscum bumps. Nitric oxide is thought to work against the molluscum contagiosum poxvirus by damaging viral proteins and DNA through reactive nitrogen species, and by stimulating local immune responses that help clear infected keratinocytes. The FDA label states the exact mechanism is not known. Nitric oxide lasts only seconds, so its action stays local.", "body": [["Molluscum bumps", "Lesions become inflamed and then clear over weeks as infected cells are damaged and the immune system responds."], ["Skin surface", "Pain, redness, itching, peeling and swelling at the application site are common."], ["Rest of the body", "Nitric oxide is quickly oxidized to nitrite and nitrate, so systemic exposure is negligible."]], "journey": "Just before each use, equal amounts from two tubes are mixed and applied to each bump, then allowed to dry. Nitric oxide is released at the skin surface and lasts only seconds before being converted to nitrite and nitrate. These are naturally present in the body and are excreted by the kidneys. Washing, bathing and swimming are avoided for an hour so the gel stays in place. Clearance is usually assessed at about 12 weeks.", "sidefx": "Side effects are local: pain, redness, itching, peeling, dermatitis, swelling and erosion at the treated bumps. They reflect nitric oxide's reactive, inflammatory effects on skin and the immune response against infected cells. Allergic contact dermatitis has been reported. Because nitric oxide is so short-lived, systemic effects like low blood pressure are not expected. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Nitric oxide is the same molecule released by nitroglycerin to relax blood vessels, but in berdazimer it is used as a local antiviral weapon.", "src": ["Zelsuvmi (berdazimer) FDA label", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1998 announcement"]}, "methoxsalen": {"origin": "Methoxsalen (8-methoxypsoralen) comes from plants. Healers in Egypt and India used extracts of plants such as Ammi majus (bishop's weed) with sunlight to repigment vitiligo for centuries. In the late 1940s chemists in Egypt isolated 8-methoxypsoralen from Ammi majus, and the Cairo dermatologist Abdel Monem El Mofty used it with sunlight to treat vitiligo. In 1974 John Parrish, Thomas Fitzpatrick and colleagues at Harvard reported in the New England Journal of Medicine that oral methoxsalen plus artificial UVA light (PUVA) cleared severe psoriasis. In 1987 Richard Edelson and colleagues reported photopheresis, in which a patient's blood cells are treated with methoxsalen and UVA outside the body, for cutaneous T-cell lymphoma.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Plant extracts containing psoralens are used with sunlight for vitiligo in Egypt and India."], ["1940s", "8-methoxypsoralen is isolated from Ammi majus in Egypt and used by El Mofty for vitiligo."], ["1974", "Parrish, Fitzpatrick and colleagues report oral methoxsalen plus UVA (PUVA) for psoriasis."], ["1987", "Edelson and colleagues report extracorporeal photopheresis for cutaneous T-cell lymphoma."]], "cell": "Methoxsalen is a flat furocoumarin that slips between DNA base pairs (intercalation). On its own it does little. When UVA light (320 to 400 nm) hits it, the drug becomes reactive and forms covalent bonds with pyrimidine bases, mainly thymine. A first photon makes a monoadduct with one strand; a second photon can link it to the other strand, forming interstrand cross-links. Cross-linked DNA cannot be copied, so DNA synthesis and division of rapidly growing keratinocytes stop. PUVA also triggers apoptosis of skin T lymphocytes, which drive psoriasis and cutaneous lymphoma, and stimulates melanocytes to make more melanin.", "body": [["Skin", "Psoriasis plaques clear as hyperproliferating keratinocytes stop dividing and skin T cells die."], ["Pigment", "Melanocytes make more melanin, causing tanning and repigmentation in vitiligo."], ["Eyes", "The drug binds to the lens when exposed to UV light, which can cause cataracts; UVA-blocking glasses are worn for a day after each dose."], ["Long-term skin", "Repeated PUVA ages skin and raises the risk of skin cancers, including squamous cell carcinoma and melanoma."], ["Gut and liver", "Nausea is common, and liver injury is reported rarely."]], "journey": "Methoxsalen is taken by mouth with low-fat food or milk before a UVA session. Absorption varies between products; the soft gelatin capsule is absorbed faster and more completely, so timing and UVA dosimetry are product-specific. Peak photosensitivity comes about one and a half to two hours after the soft capsule. It is highly bound to albumin, extensively metabolized by the liver and excreted in urine. Skin sensitivity lasts about eight hours, but lens binding can last up to a day. For photopheresis, methoxsalen is added to blood outside the body.", "sidefx": "Severe burns occur when the drug is activated by too much UVA or sunlight, because DNA damage and inflammation spread to normal skin. Lens binding and UV activation can cause cataracts. Cumulative DNA damage explains premature aging and skin cancers, which is why the label carries a boxed warning and restricts use to experienced physicians. Other photosensitizing drugs and foods such as limes, figs and celery add to phototoxicity. There is no antidote; care is supportive with sun avoidance.", "fun": "Lime juice on the skin in the sun can cause blistering burns called phytophotodermatitis, from psoralens similar to methoxsalen.", "src": ["Oxsoralen-Ultra (methoxsalen) FDA label", "Parrish JA et al. N Engl J Med 1974", "Edelson R et al. N Engl J Med 1987"]}, "afamelanotide": {"origin": "Afamelanotide began in the 1980s at the University of Arizona, where researchers Victor Hruby and Mac Hadley modified alpha-melanocyte-stimulating hormone (alpha-MSH), the body's natural tanning signal. Swapping two amino acids produced a peptide that was far more potent and longer lasting. Their idea was a tan without sun that might protect fair skin from skin cancer, and the compound was first called Melanotan-1. The Australian company Clinuvel Pharmaceuticals developed it for erythropoietic protoporphyria (EPP), a rare genetic disease in which light causes severe burning pain. It was approved in Europe in 2014 and by the FDA in 2019 as Scenesse, a small implant under the skin. A different peptide, melanotan II, is sold illegally online as a tanning product and is not FDA approved.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Hruby and Hadley at the University of Arizona develop a potent alpha-MSH analog (Melanotan-1)."], ["2014", "Afamelanotide (Scenesse) is approved in the European Union for EPP."], ["2019", "FDA approves afamelanotide implant for adults with EPP."]], "cell": "Afamelanotide is a 13-amino-acid analog of alpha-MSH ([Nle4, D-Phe7]-alpha-MSH). The changes make it resistant to enzyme breakdown and bind the melanocortin 1 receptor (MC1R) more strongly than the natural hormone. MC1R on melanocytes is a Gs-coupled receptor; activation raises cyclic AMP, activates protein kinase A and the transcription factor MITF, and increases tyrosinase and other melanin-making enzymes. Melanocytes shift toward eumelanin, the brown-black pigment. Eumelanin absorbs and scatters visible light and quenches reactive oxygen species. In EPP, protoporphyrin IX builds up in skin and is excited by visible light, creating damaging oxygen radicals; more eumelanin reduces that phototoxic reaction.", "body": [["Skin", "Skin darkens over days to weeks as melanocytes make more eumelanin, giving light protection without sun exposure."], ["Pain in light", "People with EPP can spend more time in light before the burning pain begins."], ["Moles and freckles", "Existing nevi and freckles darken, so full-body skin exams are done twice yearly."], ["Gut and head", "Nausea and headache are common, likely from melanocortin receptor effects outside the skin."]], "journey": "The rice-grain-sized implant is placed under the skin, usually above the hip, by a trained clinician. It dissolves and releases afamelanotide over several days, with most drug released early. The peptide has a short plasma half-life and is broken down by peptide hydrolysis like other small proteins. Skin pigmentation and protection last about two months per implant, so implants are repeated.", "sidefx": "Darkening of skin, moles and freckles follows directly from MC1R activation in melanocytes, which is why skin checks are part of the label. Nausea, headache and flushing may reflect activation of other melanocortin receptors. Implant site reactions occur. Hypersensitivity reactions are possible, so patients are observed after insertion. Unapproved melanotan products carry unknown risks. There is no antidote; the effect fades as the implant is used up.", "fun": "In EPP the pain is triggered mainly by visible light, not just UV, so ordinary sunscreens help little; eumelanin helps because it absorbs visible light as well.", "src": ["Scenesse (afamelanotide) implant FDA label", "Langendonk JG et al. N Engl J Med 2015 (EPP trials)"]}, "tralokinumab": {"origin": "Tralokinumab is a fully human antibody first developed by Cambridge Antibody Technology in the UK, a company later absorbed into AstraZeneca's MedImmune unit. It was first tested in asthma, where interleukin-13 seemed a promising target, but large asthma trials did not show enough benefit. Meanwhile, research showed IL-13 is the main type 2 cytokine expressed in the skin of people with atopic dermatitis. LEO Pharma licensed the antibody in 2016 and developed it for eczema. It was approved in Europe in 2021 (Adtralza) and by the FDA in December 2021 as Adbry, the first approved biologic that targets IL-13 alone. Use was extended to adolescents in 2023.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Cambridge Antibody Technology and MedImmune develop tralokinumab, first studied for asthma."], ["2016", "LEO Pharma licenses tralokinumab for skin disease."], ["2021", "FDA approves tralokinumab (Adbry) for moderate to severe atopic dermatitis in adults."], ["2023", "Approval is extended to patients 12 years and older."]], "cell": "Atopic dermatitis is driven by type 2 inflammation, in which Th2 cells, ILC2 cells and others release IL-4 and IL-13. In skin, IL-13 is especially prominent. IL-13 normally binds IL-13 receptor alpha-1, which then pairs with IL-4 receptor alpha to form a signaling complex that activates JAK1 and TYK2 and the transcription factor STAT6. STAT6 switches on genes that weaken the skin barrier (lowering filaggrin and loricrin), attract eosinophils and other immune cells via chemokines, increase IgE class switching and sensitize itch nerves. Tralokinumab is an IgG4 antibody that binds IL-13 at the site that contacts IL-13 receptor alpha-1, so IL-13 cannot bind either IL-13 receptor alpha-1 or the decoy receptor IL-13 receptor alpha-2. The STAT6 signal does not start.", "body": [["Skin", "Inflammation, redness and thickening decrease, and the barrier recovers over weeks to months."], ["Itch", "Less IL-13 signaling to sensory nerves and immune cells reduces itch."], ["Eyes", "Conjunctivitis and keratitis can occur, possibly because IL-13 helps maintain goblet cells that make the tear film's mucus layer."], ["Immune defense", "IL-13 helps fight intestinal worms, so existing helminth infections are treated before starting."]], "journey": "Tralokinumab is injected under the skin. As a large antibody, it is absorbed slowly through the lymphatic system, reaching peak levels about five to eight days later. It stays mostly in the blood and extracellular fluid. Like other antibodies, it is not broken down by the liver's CYP enzymes but by the body's general protein catabolism into peptides and amino acids. Its half-life is about three weeks, which allows dosing every two weeks or less often.", "sidefx": "Conjunctivitis is the most distinctive side effect of IL-13 blockade, thought to relate to reduced goblet cell mucus in the eye surface. Injection site reactions occur. Hypersensitivity, including anaphylaxis and angioedema, is possible with any antibody. Because IL-13 helps expel parasitic worms, helminth infections can worsen. Live vaccines are avoided during treatment. Cytokine changes can alter CYP enzyme levels, affecting narrow-margin drugs. There is no antidote; the antibody is cleared over weeks.", "fun": "Tralokinumab failed in asthma before succeeding in eczema, a reminder that the same cytokine can play different roles in different organs.", "src": ["Adbry (tralokinumab) FDA label", "Wollenberg A et al. Br J Dermatol 2021 (ECZTRA trials)"]}, "lebrikizumab": {"origin": "Lebrikizumab was created at Genentech as a humanized antibody against interleukin-13 and was first studied in asthma, where results were mixed and development stopped. Dermatologists then focused on IL-13 as the dominant cytokine in eczema skin. Dermira licensed the antibody for skin disease in 2017, and Eli Lilly acquired Dermira in 2020 to complete development, with Almirall holding European rights. It was approved in Europe in 2023 and by the FDA in September 2024 as Ebglyss for moderate to severe atopic dermatitis in adults and children 12 and older. After an initial period of every-two-week dosing, many people can be maintained on monthly injections.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Genentech develops lebrikizumab, first studied for asthma."], ["2017", "Dermira licenses lebrikizumab for atopic dermatitis."], ["2020", "Eli Lilly acquires Dermira."], ["2024", "FDA approves lebrikizumab (Ebglyss) for moderate to severe atopic dermatitis."]], "cell": "Lebrikizumab binds soluble IL-13 with high affinity. It grabs IL-13 at a spot that overlaps the docking site for IL-4 receptor alpha. IL-13 can still attach to IL-13 receptor alpha-1, but the second chain, IL-4 receptor alpha, cannot join to form the active receptor pair. Without that pair, JAK1, TYK2 and STAT6 are not activated, and the type 2 gene program is not switched on. This differs from tralokinumab, which blocks IL-13 from binding IL-13 receptor alpha-1 at all. Lebrikizumab leaves IL-13 receptor alpha-2 binding intact, so this decoy receptor can still capture and clear IL-13.", "body": [["Skin", "Lesions become less red and thickened, and the barrier improves as type 2 signaling is suppressed."], ["Itch", "Itch decreases within weeks because IL-13 no longer drives itch nerve sensitization and inflammation."], ["Eyes", "Conjunctivitis and keratitis can occur, likely from loss of IL-13 support for tear-film goblet cells."], ["Immune defense", "IL-13 helps fight helminth worms, so worm infections are treated beforehand."]], "journey": "Lebrikizumab is injected under the skin and absorbed slowly through lymphatics, reaching peak blood levels in about a week. It distributes mostly in blood and extracellular fluid. It is cleared by general protein catabolism into peptides and amino acids, not by liver enzymes or kidneys. Its half-life is about 24 days, which supports less frequent injections once the skin is controlled.", "sidefx": "Conjunctivitis and keratitis are the hallmark side effects of IL-13 blockade, along with injection site reactions. Hypersensitivity, including anaphylaxis and angioedema, can occur. Parasitic infections may worsen because IL-13 helps the gut expel worms. Live vaccines are avoided. There is no antidote; the antibody washes out over weeks to months.", "fun": "Lebrikizumab and tralokinumab both block IL-13 but grab it at different spots, so they shut down the same signal by different molecular routes.", "src": ["Ebglyss (lebrikizumab) FDA label", "Silverberg JI et al. N Engl J Med 2023 (ADvocate trials)"]}, "nemolizumab": {"origin": "Interleukin-31 was described in 2004 as a cytokine made mainly by Th2 cells that strongly triggers itch. Researchers at Chugai Pharmaceutical in Japan, part of the Roche group, created nemolizumab, a humanized antibody that blocks the IL-31 receptor. It was first approved in Japan in 2022 for itch in atopic dermatitis. Galderma licensed it for the rest of the world and studied it in prurigo nodularis, a disease of intensely itchy hard nodules. The FDA approved it as Nemluvio in August 2024 for prurigo nodularis and in December 2024 for moderate to severe atopic dermatitis in patients 12 and older, used with topical therapy.", "timeline": [["2004", "Interleukin-31 is identified as an itch-inducing cytokine."], ["2022", "Nemolizumab is approved in Japan for itch in atopic dermatitis."], ["2024", "FDA approves nemolizumab (Nemluvio) for prurigo nodularis and later for atopic dermatitis."]], "cell": "IL-31 is released by Th2 cells and other immune cells in itchy skin. It signals through a receptor made of two chains, IL-31 receptor alpha (IL-31RA) and oncostatin M receptor beta, found on sensory nerve fibers in the skin and dorsal root ganglia, keratinocytes and some immune cells. When IL-31 binds, the receptor activates JAK1 and JAK2, then STAT3 and other pathways, which excite itch neurons and promote inflammation, nerve sprouting and skin thickening. Nemolizumab is an IgG2 antibody that binds IL-31RA itself, so IL-31 cannot engage the receptor. The itch signal is cut at its source, and with less scratching, the itch-scratch cycle that builds nodules and eczema slowly breaks.", "body": [["Itch nerves", "Itch drops quickly, often within the first weeks, because IL-31 can no longer activate sensory neurons."], ["Skin nodules", "In prurigo nodularis, hard nodules flatten and heal over months as scratching stops and IL-31 driven fibrosis is reduced."], ["Eczema skin", "Lesions improve as itch-driven scratching and inflammation decrease."], ["Airways and skin", "New or worsening eczema-like rashes and asthma symptoms were reported in prurigo nodularis trials."]], "journey": "Nemolizumab is given by injection under the skin from a pen that mixes powder and liquid. It is absorbed slowly through lymphatics, peaking about four to six days later, and remains in the blood and tissue fluid. Like other antibodies, it is broken down by general protein catabolism into amino acids. The half-life is about 18 days, supporting dosing every four weeks and, for some people with eczema, every eight weeks.", "sidefx": "Headache, injection site reactions and eczema-like rashes are reported. Some people in prurigo nodularis trials developed new eczema or asthma symptoms, possibly because blocking IL-31 shifts the balance of type 2 signals. Hypersensitivity, including angioedema, can occur. Live vaccines are avoided. Cytokine changes can alter liver CYP enzymes. There is no antidote; the antibody is cleared over weeks.", "fun": "IL-31 is sometimes called the itch cytokine, and dogs with allergic itch are treated with a veterinary antibody against IL-31 itself.", "src": ["Nemluvio (nemolizumab) FDA label", "Dillon SR et al. Nat Immunol 2004", "Kwatra SG et al. N Engl J Med 2023 (prurigo nodularis trial)"]}, "abrocitinib": {"origin": "Janus kinases were discovered around 1990 and nicknamed 'just another kinase' before their central role in cytokine signaling was understood. The first JAK inhibitors approved were ruxolitinib for myelofibrosis in 2011 and tofacitinib for rheumatoid arthritis in 2012. Pfizer then designed abrocitinib to be more selective for JAK1, the kinase used by many eczema and itch cytokines, while sparing JAK2, which supports blood cell production. The FDA approved it as Cibinqo in January 2022 for adults with refractory moderate to severe atopic dermatitis, and in 2023 for adolescents 12 and older. Like other JAK inhibitors it carries a boxed warning added after a large tofacitinib safety study.", "timeline": [["c. 1990", "Janus kinases are discovered as a new family of tyrosine kinases."], ["2012", "Tofacitinib becomes the first JAK inhibitor approved for rheumatoid arthritis."], ["2021", "FDA strengthens JAK inhibitor boxed warnings after the ORAL Surveillance study."], ["2022", "FDA approves abrocitinib (Cibinqo) for refractory moderate to severe atopic dermatitis."], ["2023", "Approval is extended to adolescents 12 and older."]], "cell": "Many cytokines signal through receptors that have no enzyme activity of their own and rely on Janus kinases attached to their inner tails. When a cytokine binds, the paired JAKs phosphorylate each other and the receptor, creating docking sites for STAT proteins, which are then phosphorylated, pair up and move to the nucleus to switch on inflammatory genes. Abrocitinib is a reversible, ATP-competitive inhibitor selective for JAK1. JAK1 is needed for signaling by IL-4, IL-13, IL-31, IL-22 and TSLP, key drivers of eczema inflammation, barrier disruption and itch. Blocking JAK1 shuts down all of these at once, inside the cell, which is why itch can improve within days.", "body": [["Skin", "Eczema lesions clear and the barrier improves as type 2 and IL-22 signaling are blocked."], ["Itch nerves", "Blocking JAK1 downstream of IL-31 and other itch cytokines relieves itch within days."], ["Blood", "Dose-related drops in platelets and lymphocytes occur, so blood counts are checked."], ["Immune defense", "Weaker cytokine signaling raises the risk of serious infections and herpes zoster."], ["Lipids and muscle", "LDL cholesterol and creatine kinase can rise."], ["Heart and vessels", "JAK inhibitors as a class carry boxed warnings for heart attack, stroke and blood clots."]], "journey": "Abrocitinib is taken by mouth and absorbed quickly, peaking in about an hour. It is moderately protein bound. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP2C19, with CYP2C9 and CYP3A4 contributing, into active metabolites M1 and M2 that add to the effect. The parent half-life is about three to five hours, and metabolites leave mainly in urine. Strong CYP2C19 inhibitors raise levels, and strong inducers lower them.", "sidefx": "JAK1 supports immune defense, so serious infections, herpes zoster and reactivated tuberculosis can occur. The boxed warning lists serious infections, death, cancer, major cardiovascular events and blood clots, based on class data. Drops in platelets come from effects on blood cell signaling and explain why antiplatelet drugs are contraindicated early in treatment at higher doses. Nausea, headache and acne are common. Lipid and CK rises reflect cytokine effects on metabolism. There is no antidote; with a short half-life, effects fade within days of stopping.", "fun": "The name Janus comes from the two-faced Roman god, because JAK enzymes have two kinase-like domains, one active and one regulatory.", "src": ["Cibinqo (abrocitinib) FDA label", "FDA Drug Safety Communication on JAK inhibitors, 2021", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "ritlecitinib": {"origin": "Alopecia areata is an autoimmune disease in which CD8 T cells and natural killer cells attack growing hair follicles. Research in the 2010s showed that interferon-gamma and gamma-chain cytokines like IL-15 sustain this attack, and that JAK inhibitors could regrow hair in mice and people. Pfizer designed ritlecitinib as an irreversible inhibitor of JAK3 and the TEC kinase family, to block lymphocyte signals while sparing JAK1 and JAK2. The FDA approved it as Litfulo in June 2023 for severe alopecia areata in people 12 and older, the first treatment for this condition approved for adolescents. Baricitinib had been approved for adults with alopecia areata a year earlier.", "timeline": [["2014", "Researchers report that JAK inhibition reverses alopecia areata in mice and people."], ["2022", "Baricitinib becomes the first FDA-approved systemic treatment for severe alopecia areata in adults."], ["2023", "FDA approves ritlecitinib (Litfulo) for severe alopecia areata in patients 12 and older."]], "cell": "Ritlecitinib forms a covalent bond with cysteine 909 in the ATP pocket of JAK3. JAK1, JAK2 and TYK2 lack this cysteine, which gives ritlecitinib selectivity for JAK3. JAK3 pairs with JAK1 on receptors for common gamma-chain cytokines (IL-2, IL-4, IL-7, IL-15 and IL-21), which control the growth and activity of T cells and NK cells. Ritlecitinib also irreversibly binds a matching cysteine in TEC family kinases (BTK, ITK, TXK, BMX and TEC), which relay signals from T-cell and NK-cell receptors. Blocking both pathways reduces the cytotoxic CD8 T-cell and NK-cell attack on hair follicles in the anagen (growth) phase. Follicles recover their immune privilege and resume growing hair.", "body": [["Hair follicles", "Hair regrows on the scalp, and sometimes eyebrows and eyelashes, over months as the immune attack subsides."], ["Blood", "Lymphocyte and platelet counts can fall, reflecting reduced JAK3 and TEC signaling in blood cells."], ["Immune defense", "Serious infections, herpes zoster and tuberculosis reactivation can occur."], ["Skin", "Acne, rash and urticaria are reported, and nonmelanoma skin cancer risk is listed."], ["Liver and muscle", "Liver enzymes and creatine kinase can rise."]], "journey": "Ritlecitinib is taken by mouth and absorbed quickly, peaking in about an hour. It has low protein binding. Because it binds JAK3 covalently, its effect outlasts its short plasma half-life of about two hours; the enzyme stays blocked until the cell makes new JAK3. It is metabolized by several pathways, including glutathione S-transferase and CYP3A, CYP2C8 and CYP1A2, and leaves mainly in urine. It inhibits CYP3A and CYP1A2, raising levels of some other drugs.", "sidefx": "Weakening T-cell and NK-cell signaling explains the main risks: serious infections, herpes zoster and tuberculosis reactivation, and possibly cancer. The JAK class boxed warning covers infections, death, cancer, cardiovascular events and blood clots. Reduced lymphocyte and platelet counts reflect cytokine blockade in blood cells. Headache, diarrhea, acne and rash are common. Live vaccines are avoided. There is no antidote; new enzyme must be made after stopping.", "fun": "Ritlecitinib's selectivity hinges on a single amino acid: JAK3 has a cysteine in its active site that its JAK cousins lack.", "src": ["Litfulo (ritlecitinib) FDA label", "Xing L et al. Nat Med 2014", "King B et al. Lancet 2023 (ALLEGRO trial)"]}, "deuruxolitinib": {"origin": "Deuruxolitinib is a modified version of ruxolitinib, a JAK1 and JAK2 inhibitor approved in 2011 for myelofibrosis. Concert Pharmaceuticals, a Massachusetts company, specialized in replacing certain hydrogen atoms in known drugs with deuterium, a heavier, stable form of hydrogen. Carbon-deuterium bonds are harder for liver enzymes to break, which can slow metabolism and change how a drug behaves. Concert applied this to ruxolitinib and studied it in severe alopecia areata. Sun Pharma acquired Concert in 2023, and the FDA approved deuruxolitinib as Leqselvi in July 2024 for adults with severe alopecia areata.", "timeline": [["2011", "Ruxolitinib becomes the first JAK inhibitor approved in the US."], ["2017", "Deutetrabenazine becomes the first deuterated drug approved by the FDA."], ["2023", "Sun Pharma acquires Concert Pharmaceuticals."], ["2024", "FDA approves deuruxolitinib (Leqselvi) for severe alopecia areata in adults."]], "cell": "Deuruxolitinib is a reversible, ATP-competitive inhibitor of JAK1 and JAK2. In alopecia areata, CD8 T cells that carry the NKG2D receptor attack hair follicles. Interferon-gamma (which signals through JAK1 and JAK2) and IL-15 (which signals through JAK1 and JAK3) keep this attack going in a feed-forward loop. Deuruxolitinib blocks STAT phosphorylation downstream of these cytokines, so the follicle stops being flagged as a target and T-cell activity falls. Deuterium at specific positions slows its breakdown by CYP enzymes compared with ruxolitinib, changing the drug's exposure without changing how it binds the kinases.", "body": [["Hair follicles", "Scalp hair regrows over months as the immune attack on follicles is suppressed."], ["Blood", "Blocking JAK2, which supports red cell and platelet production, can cause anemia, and neutrophil and lymphocyte counts can fall."], ["Immune defense", "Serious infections, herpes zoster and tuberculosis reactivation can occur."], ["Lipids", "Cholesterol levels can rise, a known effect of JAK inhibition on lipid metabolism."], ["Skin", "Acne is common, possibly from changes in cytokine signaling around hair follicles."]], "journey": "Deuruxolitinib is taken by mouth twice daily and absorbed quickly, peaking in about one and a half hours. It is highly protein bound. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP2C9 with a smaller CYP3A4 contribution, and metabolites leave in urine and feces. The half-life is about four hours. People whose CYP2C9 works poorly, and those taking moderate or strong CYP2C9 inhibitors such as fluconazole, would have much higher levels, which is why genotype and interactions matter.", "sidefx": "JAK1 and JAK2 blockade dampens immune defense and blood cell production. That explains serious infections, herpes zoster, anemia, neutropenia and lymphopenia. The class boxed warning covers serious infections, death, cancer, major cardiovascular events and blood clots. Rises in cholesterol and CK are related to cytokine effects on metabolism. Headache and acne are common. Live vaccines are avoided. There is no antidote; effects fade over days after stopping.", "fun": "Deuterium is chemically almost the same as hydrogen but about twice as heavy, enough to make the bonds it forms tougher for enzymes to break.", "src": ["Leqselvi (deuruxolitinib) FDA label", "Jakafi (ruxolitinib) FDA label", "THRIVE-AA1 and THRIVE-AA2 phase 3 trials"]}, "spesolimab": {"origin": "In 2011 researchers led by Slaheddine Marrakchi reported in the New England Journal of Medicine that some families with generalized pustular psoriasis (GPP) had mutations in IL36RN, the gene for the IL-36 receptor antagonist. Without this natural brake, IL-36 signaling ran out of control, causing sudden flares of widespread sterile pustules, fever and illness. The condition was called DITRA. That discovery made the IL-36 receptor an obvious target. Boehringer Ingelheim developed spesolimab, a humanized antibody against the receptor. The FDA approved it as Spevigo in September 2022 as an IV infusion for GPP flares, the first approved treatment for GPP in the US. An under-the-skin form for preventing flares followed in 2024.", "timeline": [["2011", "IL36RN mutations are linked to generalized pustular psoriasis (DITRA)."], ["2022", "FDA approves IV spesolimab (Spevigo) for GPP flares in adults."], ["2024", "Subcutaneous spesolimab is approved for GPP when not flaring, including patients 12 and older."]], "cell": "IL-36 alpha, beta and gamma are members of the IL-1 family made by keratinocytes. They bind the IL-36 receptor (IL1RL2), which pairs with IL-1 receptor accessory protein and signals through MyD88, IRAK kinases, NF-kB and MAP kinases. This makes keratinocytes release chemokines such as CXCL1 and CXCL8 (IL-8), which pull neutrophils into the epidermis, forming sterile pustules. It also drives the IL-23 and IL-17 axis, feeding the loop. Normally the IL-36 receptor antagonist (IL-36Ra) limits this. Spesolimab is an IgG1 antibody that binds IL-36R and blocks all three IL-36 agonists, shutting down the neutrophil recruitment that defines GPP.", "body": [["Skin", "Widespread pustules often clear within about a week of an infusion as neutrophil recruitment stops."], ["Whole body", "Fever, raised white blood cell count and CRP fall as the inflammatory flare settles."], ["Skin pain", "Burning skin pain lessens as inflammation resolves."], ["Immune defense", "Infections, including urinary and respiratory infections, can occur, and tuberculosis is screened for before starting."]], "journey": "For a flare, spesolimab is given as an IV infusion, so peak blood levels are reached at the end of the infusion. For prevention, it is injected under the skin and absorbed slowly through lymphatics. As an antibody, it remains in blood and tissue fluid and is broken down by general protein catabolism. Its half-life is about 25 days, so one infusion keeps working for weeks.", "sidefx": "Blocking IL-36 signaling dampens one arm of innate defense, so infections can occur, and latent tuberculosis can reactivate. Hypersensitivity and infusion reactions are possible, and DRESS (a severe drug reaction with rash, fever and organ involvement) has been reported. Fatigue, nausea, vomiting, headache and itch occurred in trials. Live vaccines are avoided. There is no antidote; the antibody clears over weeks.", "fun": "GPP flares can be triggered by withdrawing systemic steroids, pregnancy or infection, and before spesolimab there was no FDA-approved therapy for it.", "src": ["Spevigo (spesolimab) FDA label", "Marrakchi S et al. N Engl J Med 2011", "Bachelez H et al. N Engl J Med 2021 (Effisayil 1 trial)"]}, "eflornithine-cream": {"origin": "Eflornithine, also called DFMO (difluoromethylornithine), was designed in the late 1970s by chemists at the Merrell research center in Strasbourg, France, as an enzyme-activated, irreversible inhibitor of ornithine decarboxylase. The goal was cancer treatment, because fast-growing tumor cells need polyamines to divide. It did not succeed as a broad cancer drug, but it proved able to kill the parasite that causes West African sleeping sickness, and the injectable form was approved by the FDA in 1990. Hair follicles in their growth phase also depend on ornithine decarboxylase, so the idea of a skin cream followed. Vaniqa cream, developed with interest from the shaving company Gillette, was approved in 2000 as the first prescription cream to slow unwanted facial hair in women. Brand availability has varied since, and some similar products are sold as unapproved drugs.", "timeline": [["1978", "Merrell researchers report DFMO as an irreversible ornithine decarboxylase inhibitor."], ["1990", "FDA approves injectable eflornithine for West African sleeping sickness."], ["2000", "FDA approves Vaniqa cream for unwanted facial hair in women."], ["2023", "An oral eflornithine product is approved to lower relapse risk in high-risk neuroblastoma."]], "cell": "Ornithine decarboxylase (ODC) is the rate-limiting enzyme of polyamine synthesis. It removes a carboxyl group from ornithine to make putrescine, which is then built into spermidine and spermine. Polyamines bind DNA and RNA and are needed for rapid cell division. Eflornithine looks like ornithine, so ODC accepts it into the active site. During the reaction the drug is turned into a reactive form that bonds covalently to the enzyme, so it is a suicide (mechanism-based) inhibitor and the enzyme is destroyed. In the hair bulb during anagen (the growth phase), matrix cells divide quickly and ODC activity is high. With polyamines depleted, these cells divide more slowly, so the hair shaft grows more slowly and may look finer. It does not destroy the follicle, so hair returns after the cream is stopped.", "body": [["Hair follicles", "Matrix cells in growing follicles divide more slowly, so facial hair grows back more slowly between hair removal sessions. Visible change takes weeks."], ["Skin", "The cream can sting, burn or tingle, and it can cause redness, rash and acne-like bumps, especially on freshly shaved or irritated skin."], ["Hair after stopping", "Because the follicle is not destroyed, hair growth returns to its pretreatment rate about eight weeks after the cream is stopped."], ["Whole body", "Very little drug is absorbed through intact facial skin, so effects outside the skin are not expected."]], "journey": "The cream is rubbed into the skin of the face and under the chin. Only a small fraction, typically under one percent, crosses intact skin into the blood. The absorbed drug is not significantly metabolized and is cleared unchanged by the kidneys, with a half-life of about eight hours. Most of the effect stays in the follicles near where it is applied. The effect builds slowly over four to eight weeks because hair growth itself is slow.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are local. Stinging, burning and redness come from irritation of the skin barrier, which is worse right after hair removal when the skin is broken. Acne and pseudofolliculitis barbae (ingrown hair bumps) relate to changes in how hairs grow and exit the follicle. Contact allergy can occur. Systemic toxicity seen with the injectable form, such as bone marrow suppression and hearing loss, is not expected because so little cream is absorbed. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Injectable eflornithine earned the nickname \"the resurrection drug\" because it could revive people who were near coma from late-stage sleeping sickness.", "src": ["Vaniqa (eflornithine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Metcalf BW et al. J Am Chem Soc 1978"]}, "bimatoprost-eyelash": {"origin": "Bimatoprost was developed by the eye care company Allergan as a glaucoma drop. Allergan scientists, including David Woodward, described it as a prostamide, a synthetic relative of prostaglandin F2-alpha. It was approved as Lumigan for high eye pressure in 2001. Glaucoma patients and their doctors soon noticed a striking side effect: eyelashes grew longer, thicker and darker. Allergan studied that effect on purpose, and in 2008 the FDA approved Latisse, a version applied to the base of the upper eyelashes with a sterile brush, for hypotrichosis (thin or sparse lashes). It is a rare case of a side effect becoming its own approved use.", "timeline": [["2001", "FDA approves bimatoprost (Lumigan) eye drops for glaucoma and ocular hypertension."], ["2000s", "Eyelash growth is recognized as a consistent side effect of prostaglandin glaucoma drops."], ["2008", "FDA approves Latisse for hypotrichosis of the eyelashes."]], "cell": "Bimatoprost binds prostaglandin F (FP) receptors and proposed prostamide receptors, which are G-protein coupled receptors. In the hair follicle of the eyelash, this signaling lengthens anagen, the active growth phase, and pushes more resting follicles into anagen. More follicles growing for longer means more lashes and longer lashes. The bulb also gets larger, which gives thicker hairs. Prostaglandin signaling increases melanin production by follicle melanocytes, so lashes turn darker. The same receptors in the ciliary body and trabecular meshwork increase aqueous humor outflow, which is why the molecule also lowers eye pressure.", "body": [["Eyelashes", "Lashes become longer, fuller and darker over about two to four months as more follicles stay in the growth phase."], ["Eyelid skin", "Melanocytes in the eyelid skin can make more pigment, causing darkening of the lid margin that usually fades after stopping."], ["Iris", "Iris pigmentation can increase, mainly if drug gets into the eye. This change may be permanent, which matters most in light-colored eyes."], ["Conjunctiva", "Prostaglandin-induced vasodilation can make the eye look red and itchy."], ["Eye pressure", "Drug that reaches the eye can lower intraocular pressure, which matters for people already using pressure-lowering drops."], ["Nearby skin", "Hair can grow on skin that the solution touches repeatedly, so the solution is kept to the lid margin."]], "journey": "The solution is brushed along the skin of the upper lid margin at the base of the lashes. A small amount is absorbed into the follicles and lid skin, and some may reach the tear film. Systemic exposure is very low. Any bimatoprost that reaches the blood is cleared quickly, with a half-life of about 45 minutes, through oxidation, N-deethylation and glucuronidation, then leaves in urine and feces. Lashes respond slowly, with full effect at about three to four months, and return to their earlier state over weeks to months after stopping.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow from prostaglandin signaling where the drug lands. Redness and itching reflect vasodilation of conjunctival vessels. Darker eyelid skin and iris come from stimulated melanin production. Unwanted hair growth happens where the solution touches other skin. Rarely, drug inside the eye can contribute to inflammation or macular edema in at-risk eyes. There is no antidote; stopping use lets most cosmetic effects fade, though iris color change may not.", "fun": "Latisse is often cited as one of the best-known examples of a drug side effect, longer eyelashes in glaucoma patients, being turned into a separate FDA-approved product.", "src": ["Latisse (bimatoprost) FDA label", "Lumigan (bimatoprost) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "travoprost": {"origin": "The idea of treating glaucoma with prostaglandins came from Laszlo Bito at Columbia University in the 1970s and 1980s. He found that small amounts of prostaglandin F2-alpha lowered eye pressure in animals instead of raising it as expected. The natural molecule caused too much redness and irritation, so chemists modified it. Latanoprost, from Pharmacia, came first in 1996. Alcon developed travoprost, a more selective FP receptor agonist made as an isopropyl ester prodrug, and the FDA approved it as Travatan in 2001. In 2006 Travatan Z was approved without the common preservative benzalkonium chloride, using a different preservative system that is gentler on the ocular surface. A slow-release travoprost implant was approved separately in 2023.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Laszlo Bito begins research showing prostaglandins can lower eye pressure."], ["1996", "Latanoprost becomes the first prostaglandin analog approved for glaucoma in the US."], ["2001", "FDA approves travoprost (Travatan) for open-angle glaucoma and ocular hypertension."], ["2006", "Travatan Z is approved without benzalkonium chloride preservative."], ["2023", "FDA approves iDose TR, a travoprost intracameral implant."]], "cell": "Travoprost is an isopropyl ester prodrug. Esterases in the cornea split off the ester to make travoprost free acid, the active drug. The free acid is a selective full agonist at the prostaglandin F (FP) receptor, a Gq-coupled receptor on ciliary muscle cells. FP activation turns on signaling that increases production of matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs). These enzymes break down collagen and other extracellular matrix between ciliary muscle bundles. Wider spaces between the muscle bundles let more aqueous humor drain out through the uveoscleral (unconventional) pathway. Production of aqueous humor is not reduced. More outflow with the same production lowers intraocular pressure.", "body": [["Eye pressure", "Intraocular pressure falls over hours and the effect lasts more than a day, which protects the optic nerve in glaucoma."], ["Conjunctiva", "Eye redness (hyperemia) is common because FP stimulation dilates conjunctival vessels."], ["Iris", "Iris melanocytes make more melanin, so hazel or mixed-color irises can slowly turn browner, and this change can be permanent."], ["Eyelashes and lids", "Lashes grow longer and darker and eyelid skin can darken, the same effect used for cosmetic lash products."], ["Orbit fat", "Long-term use can shrink fat around the eye (prostaglandin-associated periorbitopathy), giving a sunken look."], ["Retina", "In eyes with a torn lens capsule or other risk factors, prostaglandins can contribute to cystoid macular edema or uveitis."]], "journey": "A drop placed on the eye soaks into the cornea, where esterases convert the prodrug into the active free acid as it passes through. The free acid reaches the aqueous humor and ciliary body within hours, with peak pressure lowering at about 12 hours. Only a small amount enters the blood through the conjunctiva and nasal lining. In plasma the free acid is cleared quickly, with a half-life of about 45 minutes, by beta-oxidation like natural fatty acids. Inactive metabolites leave in urine.", "sidefx": "The side effects are FP receptor effects in nearby tissues. Vasodilation causes red eyes and itching. Stimulated melanogenesis darkens the iris and lid skin. Effects on hair follicles cause lash growth. Changes in orbital fat cells cause periorbital fat atrophy. Prostaglandin signaling can increase inflammation and vessel leakage, which is why macular edema and uveitis are rare warnings. Systemic effects are minimal because so little reaches the blood. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Travatan Z replaced benzalkonium chloride with a preservative system based on borate, zinc and sorbitol, aimed at being kinder to the eye surface for people using drops for decades.", "src": ["Travatan Z (travoprost) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "tafluprost": {"origin": "Tafluprost was discovered in Japan through work by the eye care company Santen and the chemical company Asahi Glass, which had expertise in fluorine chemistry. The team replaced the hydroxyl group at carbon 15 of the prostaglandin chain with two fluorine atoms, which increased affinity for the FP receptor. Tafluprost was first marketed in Europe and Japan in the late 2000s. In the United States it was approved in 2012 as Zioptan, the first preservative-free prostaglandin analog, supplied in single-dose containers for people whose eyes react to preservatives.", "timeline": [["1996", "Latanoprost opens the era of prostaglandin analogs for glaucoma in the US."], ["2000s", "Santen and Asahi Glass develop tafluprost, and it is launched in Europe and Japan."], ["2012", "FDA approves Zioptan, the first preservative-free prostaglandin analog in the US."]], "cell": "Tafluprost is an isopropyl ester prodrug. Corneal esterases convert it to tafluprost acid, the active form. Tafluprost acid binds the prostaglandin F (FP) receptor with high affinity, helped by the 15,15-difluoro substitution. The FP receptor is a Gq-coupled receptor; activation in the ciliary muscle increases matrix metalloproteinase expression, which remodels the extracellular matrix between muscle bundles. This opens the uveoscleral outflow pathway, so aqueous humor drains more easily. Aqueous production is unchanged, but higher outflow lowers intraocular pressure.", "body": [["Eye pressure", "Intraocular pressure drops within a few hours, peaks around 12 hours and stays lower for at least a day."], ["Ocular surface", "Without preservative, the drops are less likely to damage surface cells, which helps people with dry eye or preservative sensitivity."], ["Conjunctiva", "Prostaglandin-induced vasodilation can make the eyes red and itchy."], ["Iris and lids", "Increased melanin production can darken the iris and eyelid skin over months, and the iris change may be permanent."], ["Eyelashes", "Lashes may become longer, thicker and darker from prolonged hair follicle growth phase."], ["Head", "Headache is reported in some people, likely from vascular effects of absorbed drug."]], "journey": "The drop is absorbed through the cornea, where esterases unmask the active acid. Effect begins in two to four hours and lasts about a day. A small amount reaches the blood through the conjunctiva and nasal mucosa, but plasma tafluprost acid falls below measurable levels within about 30 minutes. The acid is broken down by fatty acid beta-oxidation and glucuronidation, and metabolites leave in urine and feces.", "sidefx": "Side effects reflect FP receptor activity in and around the eye. Redness comes from conjunctival vasodilation. Darkening of iris and lids comes from more melanin made by melanocytes. Eyelash changes come from longer anagen in lash follicles. Rarely, prostaglandin-driven inflammation and vascular leakage can cause uveitis or macular edema in eyes at risk. Systemic effects are minimal. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Tafluprost was the first prostaglandin glaucoma drop in the US sold completely free of preservatives, in small single-use vials.", "src": ["Zioptan (tafluprost) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "latanoprostene-bunod": {"origin": "Latanoprostene bunod was designed by the French company Nicox, which specializes in drugs that release nitric oxide. The chemists attached a nitric oxide-donating group (butanediol mononitrate) to latanoprost acid, the active form of the well-known glaucoma drug latanoprost. The goal was a single molecule that opens two drainage routes in the eye. Bausch + Lomb licensed and developed the drug, and the FDA approved it as Vyzulta in 2017. It was the first nitric oxide-donating drug approved to lower eye pressure.", "timeline": [["1996", "Latanoprost is approved in the US, establishing FP receptor agonists for glaucoma."], ["2000s", "Nicox designs a nitric oxide-donating version of latanoprost acid."], ["2017", "FDA approves Vyzulta (latanoprostene bunod)."]], "cell": "After the drop enters the cornea, esterases split latanoprostene bunod into two active parts. The first, latanoprost acid, activates prostaglandin F (FP) receptors in the ciliary muscle. That induces matrix metalloproteinases, remodels the matrix and increases uveoscleral outflow. The second part, butanediol mononitrate, releases nitric oxide (NO). NO activates soluble guanylate cyclase in trabecular meshwork and Schlemm canal cells, raising cGMP. cGMP activates protein kinase G, which relaxes the contractile cells of the meshwork and widens the conventional (trabecular) drainage route. With both pathways open, aqueous humor leaves the eye faster and intraocular pressure falls.", "body": [["Eye pressure", "Pressure falls through both the uveoscleral and trabecular outflow routes, with peak effect around 11 to 13 hours after a drop."], ["Trabecular meshwork", "Nitric oxide relaxes the meshwork, the drainage tissue that is often stiff in open-angle glaucoma."], ["Conjunctiva", "Both prostaglandins and nitric oxide dilate vessels, so redness and irritation are common."], ["Iris and lids", "As with other prostaglandin drops, iris and eyelid pigmentation can increase over time."], ["Eyelashes", "Lashes can grow longer and darker from the prostaglandin component."]], "journey": "The drop is absorbed through the cornea, and esterases split it into latanoprost acid and butanediol mononitrate in the eye tissues. Pressure lowering starts in one to three hours and lasts at least a day. A small amount of latanoprost acid reaches the blood, where it is cleared quickly, with a plasma half-life of about 20 minutes, by beta-oxidation in the liver. Metabolites are excreted mainly by the kidneys. The nitric oxide part is short lived and is metabolized locally.", "sidefx": "Conjunctival hyperemia and eye irritation come from vasodilation by both the prostaglandin and the nitric oxide parts. Iris and eyelid darkening and lash growth are FP receptor effects on melanocytes and hair follicles. Prostaglandin signaling can promote inflammation and leakage, so macular edema and uveitis are rare warnings in at-risk eyes. Systemic effects are minimal. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "The scientists behind nitric oxide signaling won the 1998 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine, and this drop puts that same signal to work in the eye's drain.", "src": ["Vyzulta (latanoprostene bunod) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "netarsudil": {"origin": "Rho kinase (ROCK) inhibitors grew out of research on how cells control their internal skeleton. In Japan, fasudil was approved in 1995 for brain vessel spasm, and ripasudil was approved in Japan as a glaucoma drop in 2014. In the United States, Aerie Pharmaceuticals designed netarsudil, a ROCK inhibitor that also blocks the norepinephrine transporter. The FDA approved it as Rhopressa in 2017, the first US drug that works directly on the trabecular meshwork, the tissue that is diseased in open-angle glaucoma. A combination with latanoprost, Rocklatan, followed in 2019.", "timeline": [["1995", "Fasudil, a ROCK inhibitor, is approved in Japan for cerebral vasospasm."], ["2014", "Ripasudil is approved in Japan as the first ROCK inhibitor glaucoma drop."], ["2017", "FDA approves netarsudil (Rhopressa)."], ["2019", "FDA approves Rocklatan, netarsudil combined with latanoprost."]], "cell": "Trabecular meshwork cells behave like smooth muscle. Rho GTPase activates Rho-associated coiled-coil kinase (ROCK1 and ROCK2), which phosphorylates and inhibits myosin light chain phosphatase. That keeps myosin light chains phosphorylated, building actin stress fibers and making the cells contract and stiffen, which resists outflow. Netarsudil inhibits ROCK, so myosin light chains are dephosphorylated, stress fibers break down and the cells relax. The meshwork becomes more porous, and aqueous humor drains more easily through the conventional pathway into Schlemm canal. Netarsudil also lowers episcleral venous pressure, the back pressure the drain empties against. It inhibits the norepinephrine transporter too, which may reduce aqueous production. Its corneal metabolite AR-13503 is also active.", "body": [["Eye pressure", "Intraocular pressure falls mainly through better trabecular outflow, the route that is impaired in open-angle glaucoma."], ["Conjunctiva", "ROCK inhibition relaxes vascular smooth muscle, so eye redness is the most common side effect."], ["Cornea", "Drug can deposit in the corneal epithelium in a whorl pattern (corneal verticillata). It usually does not affect vision."], ["Eye surface vessels", "Small bleeds under the conjunctiva can appear, likely linked to vessel dilation."], ["Whole body", "Blood levels are mostly too low to measure, so systemic effects are not expected."]], "journey": "A drop soaks into the cornea, where esterases convert netarsudil into its active metabolite AR-13503. Pressure lowering develops over hours and lasts about a day. Plasma levels of netarsudil and its metabolite are mostly below what tests can detect, so little is known about systemic metabolism and excretion. Nearly all of its action stays inside the eye.", "sidefx": "The main effects come from relaxing smooth muscle in vessel walls, which dilates conjunctival vessels and causes redness and sometimes small hemorrhages. Corneal verticillata are fine drug-related deposits in the corneal epithelium, similar to those seen with amiodarone, and usually fade after stopping. Stinging at instillation is common. Blurred vision and tearing can occur. There is no specific antidote, and systemic toxicity is not expected.", "fun": "Netarsudil was the first new class of glaucoma drop approved in the US in more than 20 years when it arrived in 2017.", "src": ["Rhopressa (netarsudil) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "brinzolamide": {"origin": "Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors started with sulfa drugs. In 1940 researchers noticed that sulfanilamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase. That led Lederle chemists to make acetazolamide in the early 1950s, and in 1954 ophthalmologist Bernard Becker showed that it lowered eye pressure in glaucoma. Pills caused tingling, fatigue and acidosis, so for decades researchers tried to make an eye drop that could reach the ciliary body. Merck succeeded first with dorzolamide in 1994. Alcon developed brinzolamide, a related sulfonamide made as a suspension, and the FDA approved it as Azopt in 1998. A fixed combination with brimonidine, Simbrinza, was approved in 2013.", "timeline": [["1940", "Sulfanilamide is found to inhibit carbonic anhydrase."], ["1954", "Bernard Becker reports that acetazolamide lowers intraocular pressure."], ["1994", "Dorzolamide becomes the first topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitor approved in the US."], ["1998", "FDA approves brinzolamide (Azopt)."], ["2013", "FDA approves Simbrinza (brinzolamide with brimonidine)."]], "cell": "Aqueous humor is made by the ciliary process epithelium. Inside these cells, carbonic anhydrase II rapidly converts carbon dioxide and water into carbonic acid, which splits into bicarbonate and a hydrogen ion. Bicarbonate secretion into the posterior chamber pulls sodium along, and water follows by osmosis. Brinzolamide is a sulfonamide that binds the zinc in the active site of carbonic anhydrase II and blocks it. Less bicarbonate formed means less sodium and water moving into the eye, so aqueous humor production falls and intraocular pressure goes down. Drug absorbed into the blood binds carbonic anhydrase in red blood cells, but at the low levels reached it has little effect on body acid-base balance.", "body": [["Eye pressure", "Aqueous production slows, so intraocular pressure falls, with peak effect around two hours after a drop."], ["Vision", "The drop is a white suspension, so vision is briefly blurred until it spreads and clears."], ["Taste", "Drug that drains down the tear duct reaches the mouth and can cause a bitter or sour taste."], ["Ocular surface", "Eye discomfort, foreign body sensation and lid inflammation can occur from local irritation."], ["Blood", "Absorbed drug accumulates in red blood cells by binding carbonic anhydrase, but systemic acidosis is uncommon."], ["Cornea", "Corneal endothelial cells also use carbonic anhydrase to pump fluid, so eyes with weak endothelium can swell."]], "journey": "The suspension is placed on the eye and enters through the cornea and sclera to reach the ciliary body. Some drug drains through the nasolacrimal duct and is absorbed into the blood. There it binds tightly to carbonic anhydrase in red blood cells, so whole-blood levels build up slowly and the whole-blood half-life is very long, about 111 days. It is partly metabolized to N-desethyl brinzolamide, which is also active, and is excreted mainly unchanged in urine along with metabolites.", "sidefx": "Blurred vision after dosing happens because the drop is a particulate suspension. Bitter taste comes from drug draining into the throat, where carbonic anhydrase in taste cells is affected. Because brinzolamide is a sulfonamide, rare sulfonamide-type hypersensitivity reactions, including severe skin reactions, can occur. Systemic effects of carbonic anhydrase inhibition, such as tingling, fatigue and acidosis, are much less common than with pills. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Brinzolamide can stay in red blood cells for months, because it latches onto the carbonic anhydrase enzyme those cells carry in large amounts.", "src": ["Azopt (brinzolamide) FDA label", "Becker B. Am J Ophthalmol 1954", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "apraclonidine": {"origin": "Apraclonidine is a chemical cousin of clonidine, the alpha-2 agonist blood pressure drug developed in Germany in the 1960s. Clonidine drops lowered eye pressure but also crossed into the brain and dropped blood pressure. Chemists added a polar para-amino group to the clonidine molecule, which made it much less able to cross the blood-brain barrier. Alcon developed this para-aminoclonidine as Iopidine. It was approved by the FDA in the late 1980s to prevent the sudden pressure spikes that can follow laser eye surgery, and a lower strength was later approved for short-term add-on glaucoma treatment.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Clonidine is developed in Germany as an alpha-2 agonist."], ["1980s", "Alcon develops apraclonidine (para-aminoclonidine) eye drops for use around laser eye surgery."], ["1990s", "A lower-strength apraclonidine drop is approved for short-term glaucoma treatment."]], "cell": "Apraclonidine is a relatively selective agonist at alpha-2 adrenergic receptors in the ciliary body. Alpha-2 receptors couple to Gi proteins, which inhibit adenylyl cyclase and lower cAMP in the ciliary epithelium. Less cAMP means less aqueous humor secretion, so intraocular pressure falls. Unlike brimonidine, apraclonidine keeps meaningful alpha-1 agonist activity. Alpha-1 stimulation contracts the Mueller muscle of the upper lid (lid lift), constricts conjunctival vessels (whiter eyes) and contracts the iris dilator (mild pupil widening). The polar amino group keeps most drug out of the brain, so central sedation and hypotension are less common than with clonidine.", "body": [["Eye pressure", "Aqueous humor production falls within about an hour, which blunts pressure spikes after laser procedures."], ["Conjunctiva", "Alpha-1 vasoconstriction blanches the white of the eye, which can also reduce bleeding during laser surgery."], ["Eyelid", "Contraction of the Mueller muscle raises the upper lid, so the eye can look more open."], ["Pupil", "Alpha-1 stimulation of the iris dilator can make the pupil slightly larger."], ["Mouth and nose", "Drug draining down the tear duct can cause dry mouth and dry nose from alpha-2 effects on glands."], ["Heart and brain", "A small amount reaches the circulation and can cause slow heart rate, low blood pressure or tiredness in sensitive people."]], "journey": "The drop is absorbed through the cornea and conjunctiva and reaches the ciliary body quickly, with onset in about an hour and peak effect in three to five hours. Some drug drains through the nasolacrimal duct and is absorbed into the blood, where its half-life is about eight hours. Because of its polar structure, little crosses the blood-brain barrier. It is cleared mainly by the kidneys.", "sidefx": "Lid retraction, conjunctival blanching and mild mydriasis are alpha-1 effects. Dry mouth and fatigue reflect alpha-2 effects from absorbed drug. A common problem with longer use is an allergic-like follicular conjunctivitis with red, itchy eyes and lid swelling, which is one reason the drug is used short term. Slow heart rate and low blood pressure can occur from systemic alpha-2 stimulation. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Apraclonidine drops are used as a diagnostic test for Horner syndrome. The denervated iris is supersensitive to its alpha-1 effect, so the small pupil dilates and the droopy lid lifts.", "src": ["Iopidine (apraclonidine) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "pilocarpine-ophthalmic": {"origin": "Pilocarpine comes from the leaves of jaborandi shrubs (Pilocarpus species) native to Brazil, where people used the leaves to cause sweating and drooling. In 1873 the Brazilian physician Symphronio Coutinho brought jaborandi leaves to Paris. In 1875 the active alkaloid, pilocarpine, was isolated independently by chemists in France and England. Within a few years European ophthalmologists, including Adolf Weber in Germany, found that it shrank the pupil and lowered eye pressure in glaucoma. Pilocarpine became a mainstay of glaucoma care for about a century. Newer drops have largely replaced it for glaucoma, but in 2021 the FDA approved a pilocarpine drop (Vuity) for presbyopia, the age-related loss of near focus, and another (Qlosi) followed in 2023.", "timeline": [["1873", "Symphronio Coutinho brings jaborandi leaves from Brazil to Paris."], ["1875", "Pilocarpine is isolated from jaborandi leaves."], ["1870s", "Pilocarpine is first used as a miotic to treat glaucoma."], ["2021", "FDA approves a pilocarpine drop (Vuity) for presbyopia."]], "cell": "Pilocarpine is a direct-acting agonist at muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, mainly M3 receptors on the iris sphincter and ciliary muscle. M3 receptors couple to Gq, which activates phospholipase C, raises IP3 and releases calcium, causing smooth muscle contraction. Contraction of the iris sphincter makes the pupil small (miosis) and pulls the peripheral iris away from the drainage angle. Contraction of the longitudinal ciliary muscle pulls on the scleral spur, which opens the spaces of the trabecular meshwork and increases conventional aqueous outflow, so intraocular pressure falls. Ciliary muscle contraction also relaxes the zonules and lets the lens become rounder, shifting focus to near (accommodation). A small pupil adds a pinhole effect that improves near vision, which is the basis for its presbyopia use.", "body": [["Pupil", "The iris sphincter contracts and the pupil becomes small within 10 to 30 minutes, which makes dim light look darker."], ["Drainage angle", "Ciliary muscle pull opens the trabecular meshwork, so aqueous humor drains faster and eye pressure falls."], ["Focus", "Accommodative spasm shifts focus toward near, causing blurred distance vision, especially in younger people."], ["Brow and head", "Strong ciliary muscle contraction causes a brow ache or headache, worst in the first days of use."], ["Retina", "Ciliary muscle pull can tug on the peripheral retina, and rare retinal tears or detachment have been reported."], ["Whole body", "Absorbed drug can stimulate muscarinic receptors elsewhere, causing sweating, drooling, nausea, slow heart rate or wheezing with excess use."]], "journey": "The drop crosses the cornea and acts on the iris and ciliary muscle within minutes. Miosis lasts about four to eight hours, which is why glaucoma dosing used to be frequent. Esterases in eye tissue and plasma break pilocarpine down. Some drug drains through the tear duct and is absorbed, but its plasma half-life is short, about an hour, and it is cleared by the kidneys.", "sidefx": "Nearly all effects are muscarinic. Brow ache and blurred vision come from ciliary muscle spasm. Poor night vision comes from the small pupil letting in less light. Traction on the retina explains rare retinal detachment, so a retinal exam is part of standard precautions. Absorbed drug can cause systemic cholinergic effects such as bradycardia, bronchospasm, sweating and salivation. Atropine, a muscarinic antagonist, reverses systemic cholinergic toxicity.", "fun": "Pilocarpine is also used in the sweat test for cystic fibrosis. It is pushed into the skin with a small electric current to make the skin sweat so the sweat chloride can be measured.", "src": ["Vuity (pilocarpine) FDA label", "Isopto Carpine (pilocarpine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "dexamethasone-ophthalmic": {"origin": "Corticosteroids entered eye care soon after cortisone was first used in patients in 1948. By the early 1950s cortisone drops were calming eye inflammation such as iritis. Chemists then tried to make stronger steroids. Dexamethasone, a fluorinated glucocorticoid about 25 times stronger than cortisol as an anti-inflammatory, was made in the late 1950s by chemists at Merck and was approved in 1958. Eye drop suspensions such as Maxidex became standard for treating inflammation after surgery and for steroid-responsive eye disease. Newer delivery forms include an intraocular injection (Dexycu) and a dissolving insert placed in the tear duct (Dextenza), both approved in 2018, and an intravitreal implant (Ozurdex).", "timeline": [["1948", "Cortisone is first given to a patient, opening the steroid era."], ["1950s", "Steroid eye drops come into use for ocular inflammation."], ["1958", "Dexamethasone is approved after being developed at Merck."], ["2018", "FDA approves Dextenza (canalicular insert) and Dexycu (intraocular suspension)."]], "cell": "Dexamethasone is lipophilic and passes through cell membranes. In the cytoplasm it binds the glucocorticoid receptor, which releases heat shock proteins and moves into the nucleus. There it binds glucocorticoid response elements and switches on anti-inflammatory genes, including annexin A1 (lipocortin), which inhibits phospholipase A2 and so cuts the supply of arachidonic acid for prostaglandins and leukotrienes. The receptor also binds and blocks the transcription factors NF-kB and AP-1, turning down cytokines (IL-1, TNF-alpha), adhesion molecules and COX-2. The result is less capillary dilation and leakage, less fibrin, fewer white cells entering eye tissue and less scarring. The same gene effects in the trabecular meshwork can reduce aqueous outflow and raise eye pressure in steroid responders.", "body": [["Anterior chamber", "Fewer inflammatory cells and less protein leakage in the front of the eye after surgery or in iritis, which eases pain and light sensitivity."], ["Conjunctiva and cornea", "Redness, swelling and allergic-type inflammation settle, but wound healing of the cornea is slowed."], ["Trabecular meshwork", "Changes in meshwork cells reduce outflow, so some people, called steroid responders, develop raised eye pressure that can damage the optic nerve."], ["Lens", "Long-term use can cause posterior subcapsular cataract."], ["Immune defense", "Suppressed local immunity makes it easier for herpes simplex, fungi and bacteria to infect the eye."]], "journey": "Shaken before use, the suspension releases drug that soaks into the cornea and conjunctiva and reaches the aqueous humor within hours. Effects last several hours per drop. A small amount drains through the tear duct and is absorbed. In the body dexamethasone is metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4, with a plasma half-life of about three to four hours, and is excreted by the kidneys. Systemic steroid effects are rare with eye drops.", "sidefx": "Raised eye pressure happens because glucocorticoid signaling changes trabecular meshwork cells, adding extracellular material and stiffening the drain. Cataract relates to steroid effects on lens fiber cells. Slower healing and infection risk come from suppressed fibroblast activity and immune responses, which is why steroids can worsen herpes simplex keratitis and fungal infections. Thinning of the cornea or sclera and perforation can occur with prolonged use in eyes that are already thin. There is no antidote; pressure usually falls after the steroid is tapered and stopped.", "fun": "The Dextenza insert placed in the tear drainage canal contains fluorescein, so a clinician can see it glow under blue light and confirm it is still in place.", "src": ["Maxidex (dexamethasone) FDA label", "Dextenza (dexamethasone insert) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "loteprednol": {"origin": "Loteprednol came from the \"soft drug\" idea of chemist Nicholas Bodor at the University of Florida, who worked on what he called retrometabolic drug design. The goal was a drug that does its job at the target and is then quickly broken down into inactive pieces before it can cause harm. Bodor started from cortienic acid, an inactive metabolite of prednisolone, and built an ester steroid that tissue esterases would quickly turn back into inactive acid. The result, loteprednol etabonate, was approved by the FDA in 1998 as Lotemax and Alrex. The design was meant to keep strong anti-inflammatory activity while lowering the chance of raising eye pressure.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Nicholas Bodor designs loteprednol etabonate using the soft drug approach."], ["1998", "FDA approves Lotemax and Alrex (loteprednol) eye drops."], ["2000s", "Combination with tobramycin (Zylet) and gel forms are introduced."], ["2020", "Eysuvis is approved for short-term treatment of dry eye flares."]], "cell": "Loteprednol binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor with high affinity. The receptor complex moves to the nucleus, where it switches on annexin A1 (lipocortin), which inhibits phospholipase A2 and limits prostaglandin and leukotriene synthesis. It also blocks NF-kB and AP-1 driven transcription of cytokines, adhesion molecules and COX-2, reducing redness, swelling and cell infiltration. Structurally it lacks the 20-ketone of classic steroids and instead has a 17-beta chloromethyl ester. Esterases in ocular tissue quickly hydrolyze it to the inactive metabolites delta-1 cortienic acid etabonate and delta-1 cortienic acid. This fast local inactivation is thought to explain why it raises intraocular pressure less often than prednisolone acetate.", "body": [["Eye inflammation", "Postoperative and allergic inflammation settles as fewer inflammatory cells and mediators reach the eye tissues."], ["Conjunctiva", "In allergic conjunctivitis, itching and redness improve because late-phase inflammation is suppressed."], ["Eye pressure", "Pressure rises are less common than with stronger steroids, but they can still happen, so pressure is monitored with longer use."], ["Lens", "Cataract can occur with prolonged steroid use."], ["Cornea and defense", "Healing slows and the risk of herpes, fungal and bacterial infections rises because local immunity is suppressed."]], "journey": "Applied as a suspension, gel or ointment, loteprednol enters the cornea and conjunctiva. As it moves through tissues, esterases convert it into inactive carboxylic acids. Plasma levels after eye use are below the limit of detection, so systemic effects are not expected. Effects last several hours per dose.", "sidefx": "Side effects are glucocorticoid effects in the eye. Higher eye pressure comes from steroid changes in the trabecular meshwork, though the rapid breakdown makes this less frequent than with other steroids. Cataract, delayed wound healing and secondary infection reflect suppressed cell growth and immunity. Blurred vision and burning come from the drop itself. There is no antidote; effects resolve after stopping under supervision.", "fun": "Loteprednol is a textbook example of a \"soft drug\", a medicine designed from the start to self-destruct into harmless pieces after it acts.", "src": ["Lotemax (loteprednol) FDA label", "Bodor N. retrometabolic drug design papers", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "difluprednate": {"origin": "Difluprednate is a difluorinated derivative of prednisolone that was first used in Japan as a strong steroid cream for skin disease. Researchers later reformulated it as an eye drop emulsion, a fine oil-in-water mixture that keeps the drug evenly dispersed without the need to shake. The FDA approved it as Durezol in 2008 for inflammation and pain after eye surgery, and its labeling was later extended to anterior uveitis. It is one of the most potent topical steroids used in the eye.", "timeline": [["2008", "FDA approves Durezol emulsion for postoperative eye inflammation and pain."], ["2010s", "Durezol labeling adds treatment of endogenous anterior uveitis."]], "cell": "Difluprednate is a prodrug. In the eye it is deacetylated to difluoroprednisolone butyrate (DFB), the active form. DFB binds the glucocorticoid receptor with high affinity; fluorine atoms at the 6 and 9 positions increase receptor potency. The activated receptor moves into the nucleus, induces annexin A1, which inhibits phospholipase A2 and the arachidonic acid cascade, and represses NF-kB and AP-1 targets such as IL-1, TNF-alpha, adhesion molecules and COX-2. The anterior chamber gets fewer white cells and less protein flare, and pain and light sensitivity improve. Its strength also makes trabecular meshwork changes, and therefore pressure rises, more frequent.", "body": [["Anterior chamber", "Marked reduction in cells and flare after surgery and in uveitis, often faster than with older steroid drops."], ["Pain", "Less prostaglandin and cytokine production reduces ocular pain and photophobia after surgery."], ["Eye pressure", "Intraocular pressure rises are relatively common, especially in children and steroid responders, because of steroid effects on the drain."], ["Cornea", "Corneal edema and slowed epithelial healing can occur."], ["Lens", "Cataract and posterior capsule opacity are reported with steroid exposure."], ["Infection risk", "Suppressed immunity raises the risk of herpes, fungal and bacterial infection of the eye."]], "journey": "The emulsion spreads over the eye and the drug enters the cornea, where it is converted to the active DFB. Effects last several hours per dose. Systemic levels after eye use are very low and are not well characterized. Any absorbed steroid would be handled by the liver like other glucocorticoids.", "sidefx": "Because it is very potent, difluprednate shows typical steroid eye effects more often. Pressure rises come from glucocorticoid-induced changes in trabecular meshwork cells that reduce outflow. Cataract, delayed healing and infection risk reflect suppressed cell growth and immunity. Corneal edema and redness can occur. There is no antidote; pressure usually returns toward baseline after supervised tapering.", "fun": "Durezol is an emulsion rather than a suspension, so the drug stays evenly mixed and the bottle does not need shaking for a consistent dose.", "src": ["Durezol (difluprednate) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "ketorolac-ophthalmic": {"origin": "Ketorolac was developed at the drug company Syntex in California in the 1980s, in a program led by chemist Joseph Muchowski. It was a strong NSAID with pain relief close to that of opioids in some settings, and the injectable form (Toradol) was approved in 1989. Because prostaglandins drive much of the pain and swelling after eye surgery and much of the itch in allergic eye disease, an eye drop was developed and approved as Acular in the early 1990s. Lower-strength and preservative-free versions followed, and in 2014 ketorolac was combined with phenylephrine in an irrigation solution (Omidria) used during cataract surgery.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Ketorolac is developed at Syntex."], ["1989", "Injectable ketorolac (Toradol) is approved in the US."], ["1990s", "Acular ketorolac eye drops are approved."], ["2014", "FDA approves Omidria, ketorolac with phenylephrine for use during lens surgery."]], "cell": "Ketorolac reversibly inhibits cyclooxygenase-1 and cyclooxygenase-2 in ocular tissues. These enzymes turn arachidonic acid into prostaglandin H2, the precursor of PGE2, PGF2-alpha and other prostanoids. In the eye these prostaglandins break down the blood-aqueous barrier, dilate vessels, sensitize corneal pain nerves and make the iris sphincter contract during surgery. They also promote fluid leakage from perifoveal retinal capillaries, which drives cystoid macular edema after cataract surgery. With COX blocked, less prostaglandin is made, so there is less pain, less inflammation, less pupil constriction during surgery and less itch in allergic conjunctivitis.", "body": [["Cornea and nerves", "Pain after eye surgery and after corneal procedures eases because prostaglandins no longer sensitize corneal nerve endings."], ["Anterior chamber", "Inflammation and protein leakage drop because the blood-aqueous barrier stays more intact."], ["Pupil", "During surgery, blocking prostaglandin release helps keep the pupil from constricting."], ["Conjunctiva", "In seasonal allergic conjunctivitis, itching is reduced."], ["Retina", "Less prostaglandin lowers the chance of fluid buildup in the macula after cataract surgery."], ["Corneal surface", "Prolonged use can slow epithelial healing and, rarely, cause corneal melting."]], "journey": "The drop penetrates the cornea and reaches the aqueous humor within an hour. Effects last about six to eight hours. Only a tiny amount reaches the blood, where ketorolac is highly protein bound, metabolized by the liver and cleared by the kidneys. Systemic NSAID effects are not expected with eye use.", "sidefx": "Stinging and burning on instillation are common. With prolonged or frequent use, loss of prostaglandins that support the corneal epithelium can cause punctate keratitis, epithelial breakdown and, rarely, corneal melting or perforation, especially after complicated surgery or with diabetes or dry eye. NSAIDs can slow healing and, in theory, increase bleeding of eye tissues during surgery. Cross-sensitivity with aspirin and other NSAIDs is possible. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Omidria is added to the irrigation fluid used during cataract surgery so the pupil stays wide and postoperative pain is lower, combining an alpha-1 agonist with an NSAID.", "src": ["Acular (ketorolac) FDA label", "Omidria FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "bromfenac": {"origin": "Bromfenac has an unusual history. It was first approved in the United States in 1997 as an oral painkiller, Duract, by Wyeth-Ayerst. Within a year reports of severe liver failure, some fatal, appeared in people who took it for longer than the recommended short course, and it was withdrawn in 1998. In Japan, Senju Pharmaceutical developed bromfenac as an eye drop, where the tiny amounts used do not cause the same liver risk. The eye drop was approved in the US as Xibrom in 2005, followed by once-daily versions such as Prolensa (2013) and BromSite (2016) for inflammation and pain after cataract surgery.", "timeline": [["1997", "Oral bromfenac (Duract) is approved in the US as a short-term pain reliever."], ["1998", "Oral bromfenac is withdrawn after reports of severe liver injury."], ["2005", "FDA approves bromfenac eye drops (Xibrom)."], ["2013", "Prolensa, a once-daily formulation, is approved."]], "cell": "Bromfenac inhibits cyclooxygenase, with relatively more potency against COX-2 than COX-1. Blocking COX stops arachidonic acid from being converted to prostaglandin H2, so less PGE2 and related prostanoids are made in the cornea, iris, ciliary body and retina. These prostaglandins normally open the blood-aqueous barrier, dilate vessels, sensitize corneal nerves and cause macular capillary leakage. Bromfenac's bromine atom makes the molecule more lipophilic, so it penetrates the cornea well and builds up useful tissue levels, which supports once-daily dosing.", "body": [["Anterior chamber", "Less postoperative inflammation, with fewer cells and less flare in the front of the eye."], ["Pain", "Eye pain and light sensitivity after cataract surgery are reduced because corneal nerves are less sensitized."], ["Retina", "Lower prostaglandin levels help protect against cystoid macular edema after cataract surgery."], ["Corneal surface", "Long use can delay epithelial healing and rarely cause keratitis or corneal melting."], ["Whole body", "Plasma levels after eye use are below detection, so the liver toxicity of the old oral form is not expected."]], "journey": "Bromfenac drops are absorbed through the cornea, reaching aqueous humor levels that persist long enough for one dose a day. Systemic exposure is so low that plasma levels are below the limit of detection. Any absorbed drug would be highly protein bound and metabolized by the liver.", "sidefx": "Ocular side effects come from reduced prostaglandins and the drop itself. Corneal epithelial prostaglandins help healing, so blocking them can lead to keratitis, epithelial breakdown and rarely corneal melting or perforation, especially with extended use or diabetes. Some formulations contain sulfite, which can trigger allergic reactions in sensitive people. NSAIDs may slow healing and can increase tissue bleeding. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Bromfenac went from a failed pill, withdrawn for liver failure, to a successful eye drop, because the eye needs only a tiny fraction of the drug exposure that pills deliver.", "src": ["Prolensa (bromfenac) FDA label", "FDA withdrawal of Duract, 1998", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "nepafenac": {"origin": "Nepafenac was developed by the eye care company Alcon as a prodrug of amfenac, an older NSAID. Amfenac itself crosses the cornea poorly. Converting it into an amide (nepafenac) made a neutral molecule that soaks through the cornea far more easily and is then turned back into amfenac inside the eye. The FDA approved Nevanac in 2005 for pain and inflammation after cataract surgery. A stronger once-daily form, Ilevro, followed in 2012, and labeling later added reduction of the risk of macular edema after cataract surgery in people with diabetes.", "timeline": [["2005", "FDA approves Nevanac (nepafenac) for pain and inflammation after cataract surgery."], ["2012", "FDA approves Ilevro, a once-daily nepafenac formulation."]], "cell": "Nepafenac is an amide prodrug with no strong COX activity of its own. It penetrates the cornea and is hydrolyzed by intraocular hydrolases in the iris, ciliary body, retina and choroid to amfenac. Amfenac inhibits cyclooxygenase-1 and cyclooxygenase-2, blocking conversion of arachidonic acid to prostaglandin H2. Lower prostaglandin levels mean the blood-aqueous and blood-retinal barriers leak less, corneal nerves are less sensitized and macular capillaries leak less fluid. Because conversion happens in tissues that have the hydrolase, including the retina, drug activity reaches the back of the eye.", "body": [["Anterior chamber", "Inflammation after cataract surgery decreases, with fewer cells and less flare."], ["Pain", "Postoperative pain and photophobia ease as prostaglandin-driven nerve sensitization drops."], ["Retina", "Amfenac formed in the retina lowers prostaglandins that cause cystoid macular edema, including in people with diabetes."], ["Cornea", "Epithelial healing can slow and, rarely, keratitis or corneal melting occurs."], ["Lens capsule", "Capsular opacity and a sticky sensation are reported after surgery, partly reflecting the surgery itself."]], "journey": "Drops placed on the eye are absorbed through the cornea, and nepafenac spreads through the eye before hydrolases convert it to amfenac. Amfenac levels in aqueous humor peak in about one to two hours. Very little reaches the blood. Absorbed amfenac is highly bound to albumin, then hydroxylated and glucuronidated and excreted mainly in urine.", "sidefx": "The main risks come from losing prostaglandins in the corneal epithelium, which support healing. This can cause punctate keratitis, epithelial breakdown and, rarely, corneal melting or perforation, especially with use beyond the labeled course or in eyes with diabetes, dry eye or complicated surgery. Increased bleeding of eye tissue is possible, and raised eye pressure and headache are reported. Cross-sensitivity with other NSAIDs is possible. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Nepafenac is one of few eye drops designed so its active drug is made inside the eye, which helps it reach the retina from a surface drop.", "src": ["Ilevro (nepafenac) FDA label", "Nevanac (nepafenac) FDA label"]}, "gatifloxacin-ophthalmic": {"origin": "The quinolone antibiotics began in 1962 when George Lesher and colleagues at Sterling-Winthrop found nalidixic acid as a by-product of making the antimalarial chloroquine. Adding a fluorine atom in the 1970s and 1980s produced the far stronger fluoroquinolones. Gatifloxacin, an 8-methoxy fluoroquinolone, was discovered by the Japanese company Kyorin. An oral form (Tequin) was approved in the US in 1999 but was withdrawn in 2006 because it caused dangerous swings in blood sugar. The eye drop, made by Allergan, never had that problem because so little reaches the blood. Zymar was approved in 2003 for bacterial conjunctivitis, and a stronger version, Zymaxid, replaced it in 2010.", "timeline": [["1962", "Nalidixic acid, the first quinolone, is discovered at Sterling-Winthrop."], ["1999", "Oral gatifloxacin (Tequin) is approved in the US."], ["2003", "FDA approves gatifloxacin eye drops (Zymar)."], ["2006", "Oral gatifloxacin is withdrawn in the US because of blood sugar disturbances."], ["2010", "Zymaxid, a stronger gatifloxacin eye drop, is approved."]], "cell": "Bacteria must constantly cut and reseal their DNA to manage supercoiling as they copy it. DNA gyrase (topoisomerase II, subunits GyrA and GyrB) adds negative supercoils ahead of the replication fork, and topoisomerase IV separates the linked daughter chromosomes after copying. Gatifloxacin binds the enzyme-DNA complex after the DNA has been cut and traps it, so the breaks cannot be resealed. Stalled replication forks and double-strand breaks trigger bacterial death, so the drug is bactericidal and kills faster at higher concentrations. Gyrase is the main target in gram-negative bacteria and topoisomerase IV in gram-positive bacteria. The 8-methoxy group improves activity against gram-positive organisms and means two mutations are usually needed for resistance.", "body": [["Conjunctiva", "Susceptible bacteria such as Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae are killed, so redness and discharge improve in a few days."], ["Tear film", "High drug levels are reached in tears right after each drop, which favors concentration-dependent killing."], ["Taste", "Drug draining down the tear duct can cause an altered or bitter taste."], ["Ocular surface", "Irritation, eye pain and temporary blurring can occur from the drop itself."], ["Normal eye flora", "Use can select resistant bacteria or allow fungal overgrowth."]], "journey": "The drop mixes with the tear film and enters the conjunctiva and cornea, giving high local levels shortly after instillation. Most is washed away by tears through the nasolacrimal duct. The amount absorbed into the blood is very small. Absorbed gatifloxacin has a half-life of roughly 7 to 14 hours, is little metabolized and is excreted mainly unchanged in urine.", "sidefx": "Eye irritation, worsening redness and eye pain come from the drop on an already inflamed surface. Altered taste comes from drainage into the throat. Killing susceptible bacteria can let resistant bacteria or fungi overgrow. Allergic reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, can occur as with any fluoroquinolone. The blood sugar problems and other systemic warnings of oral fluoroquinolones are not expected with eye use because blood levels are so low. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Gatifloxacin is a drug that disappeared from US pharmacies as a pill but stayed available as an eye drop.", "src": ["Zymaxid (gatifloxacin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "ofloxacin-ophthalmic": {"origin": "Ofloxacin was developed by the Japanese company Daiichi Seiyaku in the early 1980s as one of the first broad-spectrum fluoroquinolones. It built on nalidixic acid, discovered in 1962, and on the discovery that a fluorine atom greatly boosted potency. Oral ofloxacin was approved in the US around 1990. Allergan then developed the eye drop, Ocuflox, approved in the early 1990s for bacterial conjunctivitis and corneal ulcers. Ofloxacin is a mixture of two mirror-image forms, and Daiichi later developed the active form alone as levofloxacin.", "timeline": [["1962", "Nalidixic acid, the first quinolone, is discovered."], ["1980s", "Daiichi Seiyaku develops ofloxacin in Japan."], ["1990s", "Ofloxacin eye drops (Ocuflox) are approved in the US."], ["1996", "Levofloxacin, the active isomer of ofloxacin, is approved in the US."]], "cell": "Ofloxacin inhibits two bacterial type II topoisomerases. DNA gyrase introduces negative supercoils to relieve strain as DNA unwinds for copying, and topoisomerase IV separates interlinked daughter chromosomes. The drug binds the enzyme while it is attached to cut DNA and stabilizes this cleavage complex, so the strand cannot be resealed. Replication stalls and double-strand breaks accumulate, which kills the bacterium. Killing is concentration dependent. Ofloxacin has strong gram-negative activity, including Pseudomonas aeruginosa, which matters for contact lens related corneal ulcers.", "body": [["Conjunctiva", "Bacterial conjunctivitis improves as susceptible organisms are killed and discharge decreases."], ["Cornea", "In bacterial keratitis, including Pseudomonas ulcers, frequent drops keep corneal drug levels high enough to stop the infection."], ["Ocular surface", "Burning, stinging, redness and light sensitivity can follow each drop."], ["Normal flora", "Resistant bacteria or fungi can overgrow with prolonged use."], ["Immune system", "Rare hypersensitivity reactions can occur, including severe skin reactions."]], "journey": "The drop enters the tear film and penetrates the cornea, reaching useful levels in corneal tissue and some in the aqueous humor. Much is drained through the tear duct. Systemic absorption is very low. Absorbed ofloxacin has a half-life of about four to eight hours, is barely metabolized and is excreted mostly unchanged in urine.", "sidefx": "Local burning and discomfort are effects of the solution on the ocular surface. Overgrowth of resistant or fungal organisms follows suppression of normal flora. Corneal white precipitates are uncommon with ofloxacin, unlike ciprofloxacin, because ofloxacin stays more soluble at tear pH. Hypersensitivity, including rare anaphylaxis or Stevens-Johnson syndrome, is an immune reaction to the quinolone structure. Tendon and nerve problems of oral quinolones are not expected with eye use. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Ofloxacin is a 50:50 mix of two mirror-image molecules, and nearly all of its antibacterial power comes from one of them, which became the separate drug levofloxacin.", "src": ["Ocuflox (ofloxacin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "polymyxin-b-trimethoprim": {"origin": "This eye drop combines two antibiotics with very different backstories. Polymyxins were discovered in 1947 by several research groups working independently, from the soil bacterium Bacillus polymyxa (now Paenibacillus polymyxa); one of these groups was at Burroughs Wellcome. Trimethoprim came from the rational drug design program of George Hitchings and Gertrude Elion at Burroughs Wellcome, who searched for molecules that block folate enzymes more in microbes than in people. Hitchings and Elion shared the 1988 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. Combining the two in one drop (Polytrim) gave coverage of both gram-positive and gram-negative eye pathogens without using an aminoglycoside.", "timeline": [["1947", "Polymyxins are discovered from Bacillus polymyxa."], ["1950s", "Hitchings and colleagues at Burroughs Wellcome develop trimethoprim."], ["1988", "Hitchings and Elion share the Nobel Prize for principles of drug design."]], "cell": "Polymyxin B is a cationic, ring-shaped polypeptide with a fatty acid tail. Its positive charges bind the negatively charged lipid A of lipopolysaccharide on gram-negative bacteria, pushing away the magnesium and calcium ions that hold the outer membrane together. The fatty tail then inserts into the membrane like a detergent, disrupting both the outer and inner membranes so cell contents leak out and the bacterium dies. Trimethoprim binds bacterial dihydrofolate reductase thousands of times more tightly than the human enzyme. Blocking it stops conversion of dihydrofolate to tetrahydrofolate, which is needed to make thymidine, purines and some amino acids, so bacterial DNA synthesis halts. Trimethoprim covers many gram-positive organisms including Staphylococcus and Streptococcus pneumoniae, while polymyxin B covers gram-negatives including Pseudomonas aeruginosa.", "body": [["Conjunctiva", "Bacterial conjunctivitis and blepharoconjunctivitis improve as both gram-positive and gram-negative organisms are suppressed."], ["Eyelids", "Infections along the lid margin can be treated by the same drop as it bathes the lid area."], ["Ocular surface", "Burning, stinging and redness can occur after each drop."], ["Immune system", "Allergy can cause lid swelling, itching and rash around the eye."]], "journey": "The drops stay on the ocular surface and in the tear film, giving high local concentrations. Polymyxin B is a large charged molecule that is poorly absorbed through tissues, and trimethoprim reaches only tiny amounts in the blood. Most drug drains through the tear duct or is washed away. Systemic effects are not expected.", "sidefx": "Local irritation is the main side effect, from the drop on inflamed tissue. Hypersensitivity can cause lid swelling, itching and redness. The kidney and nerve toxicity of injected polymyxin B, and the folate-related blood effects of oral trimethoprim, are not expected with eye drops because almost no drug reaches the blood. Prolonged use can allow resistant organisms or fungi to overgrow. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Trimethoprim was one of the early triumphs of rational drug design: it was built to fit the bacterial version of an enzyme better than the human one.", "src": ["Polytrim (polymyxin B and trimethoprim) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Nobel Prize 1988 Physiology or Medicine"]}, "lifitegrast": {"origin": "Lifitegrast was designed as a small molecule that mimics the part of ICAM-1 that binds the immune cell integrin LFA-1. Research in the 1990s and 2000s showed that LFA-1 and ICAM-1 binding is a key step in T-cell driven inflammation, including in dry eye disease. The compound was developed by SARcode Bioscience, a small California company, and SARcode was bought by Shire in 2013. The FDA approved Xiidra in 2016 as the first drug approved to treat both the signs and symptoms of dry eye disease. Ownership later moved to Novartis and then to Bausch + Lomb.", "timeline": [["2000s", "SARcode Bioscience develops lifitegrast as a small-molecule LFA-1 antagonist."], ["2013", "Shire acquires SARcode and the lifitegrast program."], ["2016", "FDA approves Xiidra (lifitegrast) for dry eye disease."]], "cell": "In dry eye disease, stress on the ocular surface raises ICAM-1 on corneal and conjunctival cells and on blood vessels. T cells carry the integrin LFA-1 (CD11a/CD18). When LFA-1 binds ICAM-1, it helps T cells stick to vessel walls, migrate into the tissue and form the immune synapse with antigen-presenting cells. That leads to T-cell activation and release of cytokines such as interferon-gamma and IL-1, which damage goblet cells and the epithelium and worsen tear film instability. Lifitegrast binds LFA-1 and blocks its interaction with ICAM-1. Fewer T cells enter and activate, cytokine release falls and the inflammation cycle eases over weeks.", "body": [["Ocular surface", "Corneal staining and surface inflammation decrease as T-cell activity is reduced."], ["Dryness symptoms", "Eye dryness and discomfort can begin to improve within about two weeks of regular use."], ["Taste", "Drug draining to the throat commonly causes a bitter or altered taste."], ["Eye on dosing", "Burning or irritation at instillation and brief blurred vision are common."], ["Nose and head", "Headache and sinusitis are reported, probably reflecting drainage and local irritation."]], "journey": "Each single-use vial is placed in the eye and the drug acts in the conjunctiva and cornea. A small amount reaches the blood, with low plasma levels that peak within minutes to hours and fall with a half-life of hours. It is not extensively metabolized and is eliminated mainly in feces. Effects build over weeks of twice-daily use rather than after a single drop.", "sidefx": "Dysgeusia, an unpleasant taste, occurs because drug drains through the nasolacrimal duct to the mouth. Instillation-site irritation and blurred vision come from the solution on a sensitive surface. Because it acts locally on immune cell adhesion, broad immune suppression is not expected. Rare hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis, have been reported. There is no antidote.", "fun": "An older antibody that blocked LFA-1 throughout the body, efalizumab for psoriasis, was withdrawn in 2009. Lifitegrast uses the same target but stays almost entirely in the eye.", "src": ["Xiidra (lifitegrast) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "perfluorohexyloctane": {"origin": "Perfluorohexyloctane belongs to a family of chemicals called semifluorinated alkanes, which have one part made of carbon and fluorine and one part made of carbon and hydrogen. They are clear, very slippery and do not mix with water. The German company Novaliq built an eye drop technology around these liquids and developed perfluorohexyloctane for dry eye. Bausch + Lomb licensed it, and the FDA approved it as Miebo in 2023. It was the first FDA-approved drug for dry eye disease that directly targets tear evaporation, a central problem in meibomian gland dysfunction.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Novaliq develops eye drop technology based on semifluorinated alkanes."], ["2023", "FDA approves Miebo (perfluorohexyloctane) for the signs and symptoms of dry eye disease."]], "cell": "Perfluorohexyloctane does not bind a receptor or enzyme. Its action is physical. The tear film has an outer oily layer made by the meibomian glands that slows evaporation. In meibomian gland dysfunction this layer is thin or poor, so tears evaporate fast, the film becomes salty (hyperosmolar) and surface cells become inflamed. Perfluorohexyloctane has very low surface tension, so a single small drop spreads rapidly across the eye. It forms a thin layer at the air-tear interface that acts as a barrier against evaporation. Tear evaporation falls, tear osmolarity improves and surface cells are less stressed, which shows as less corneal staining and less dryness over time.", "body": [["Tear film", "Evaporation slows because the drug forms a barrier at the air-liquid interface, keeping the watery layer in place."], ["Cornea", "Corneal staining decreases over weeks as surface cells are less stressed by dryness."], ["Dryness symptoms", "Discomfort and dryness improve within days to weeks, especially in people with meibomian gland dysfunction."], ["Vision", "Blurred vision right after a drop is common as the liquid spreads."], ["Conjunctiva", "Some people notice mild redness of the white of the eye after using the drops."]], "journey": "The drop spreads over the eye surface and acts there. It contains no water and no preservative. A small amount is absorbed. Absorbed drug is not metabolized; instead it is thought to be carried to the lungs and breathed out. Systemic exposure is low.", "sidefx": "Blurred vision occurs because the liquid has a different refractive behavior than tears until it spreads evenly. Mild redness may reflect surface irritation. Because it has no receptor target and no preservative, few other side effects are expected. Hypersensitivity is possible. Contact lenses are removed before use per labeling. There is no antidote, and none is needed.", "fun": "Miebo contains no water at all, so there is nothing in the bottle for bacteria to grow in, which is why it needs no preservative.", "src": ["Miebo (perfluorohexyloctane) FDA label"]}, "varenicline-nasal": {"origin": "Varenicline traces back to cytisine, a plant alkaloid from the laburnum tree that was used in Eastern Europe to help people quit smoking. At Pfizer in the 1990s, a team led by chemist Jotham Coe used cytisine as a starting point to design varenicline, a partial agonist at nicotinic receptors. It was approved as Chantix in 2006 for smoking cessation. Later, the company Oyster Point Pharma found a new use: when sprayed in the nose at a tiny fraction of the smoking dose, varenicline triggers the nerve reflex that makes tears. The FDA approved this nasal spray, Tyrvaya, in 2021 as the first nasal spray for dry eye disease.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Pfizer chemists design varenicline using the plant alkaloid cytisine as a model."], ["2006", "FDA approves oral varenicline (Chantix) for smoking cessation."], ["2021", "FDA approves Tyrvaya varenicline nasal spray for dry eye disease."]], "cell": "The nasal lining is densely supplied by branches of the trigeminal nerve, which carry nicotinic acetylcholine receptors. Varenicline binds these receptors, including the alpha4beta2 subtype, and opens their cation channels, so sodium and calcium flow in and the nerve endings fire. The signal travels to the brainstem and activates the trigeminal parasympathetic pathway, the nasolacrimal reflex. Parasympathetic fibers then release acetylcholine onto the lacrimal glands, conjunctival goblet cells and meibomian glands. More natural tears are produced, including water, mucin and lipid. This is the same reflex that makes the eyes water when something irritates the nose. It works through the nose, not through drops in the eye.", "body": [["Lacrimal glands", "Basal tear production increases within minutes as the nasolacrimal reflex is triggered."], ["Ocular surface", "With more natural tears, the eye surface is better covered and dryness signs can improve."], ["Nose", "Sneezing occurs in most people right after spraying, along with nasal irritation and sometimes nosebleeds."], ["Throat and airway", "Cough and throat irritation are common as spray reaches the back of the nose."], ["Brain", "Systemic levels are far lower than with smoking-cessation tablets, so central effects such as nausea or vivid dreams are less expected."]], "journey": "The spray is absorbed partly through the nasal lining. It acts locally on trigeminal nerve endings within minutes. Some reaches the blood, peaking within about two hours, but exposure is much lower than with oral varenicline. In the body it is barely metabolized and is excreted mostly unchanged in the urine with a half-life of about 19 hours.", "sidefx": "Sneezing, cough and throat and nose irritation come directly from activating nicotinic receptors on sensory nerves in the nose, the very effect that drives the tear reflex. Nasal discomfort and minor nosebleeds come from local irritation. Because blood levels are low, the nausea and sleep effects of oral varenicline are not expected at labeled use. There is no antidote.", "fun": "In clinical trials, about four out of five people sneezed after using the spray, a side effect that shows the nasal nerve reflex is working.", "src": ["Tyrvaya (varenicline nasal spray) FDA label", "Chantix (varenicline) FDA label", "Coe JW et al. J Med Chem 2005"]}, "faricimab": {"origin": "Faricimab grew out of decades of research on how abnormal blood vessels form in the eye. In 1989 Napoleone Ferrara and colleagues at Genentech isolated vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF), and anti-VEGF injections transformed care of wet macular degeneration in the 2000s. Researchers then found that a second signal, angiopoietin-2 (Ang-2), makes vessels unstable and leaky. Roche and Genentech used their CrossMAb antibody engineering to build one antibody with two different arms, one binding VEGF-A and one binding Ang-2. The FDA approved it as Vabysmo in 2022 for wet age-related macular degeneration and diabetic macular edema, making it the first bispecific antibody approved for the eye. Approval for macular edema after retinal vein occlusion followed in 2023.", "timeline": [["1989", "Napoleone Ferrara's team at Genentech isolates VEGF."], ["2006", "Ranibizumab, an anti-VEGF antibody fragment, is approved for wet AMD."], ["2022", "FDA approves faricimab (Vabysmo) for wet AMD and diabetic macular edema."], ["2023", "Faricimab is approved for macular edema after retinal vein occlusion."]], "cell": "In wet AMD, diabetic macular edema and vein occlusion, low oxygen and inflammation drive retinal cells to release VEGF-A. VEGF-A binds VEGFR-1 and VEGFR-2 on endothelial cells, triggering tyrosine kinase signaling that makes cells divide, form new vessels and loosen their junctions, so fluid leaks. Ang-2 competes with angiopoietin-1 at the Tie2 receptor and blocks its stabilizing signal, which leaves vessels leaky and inflamed and makes them more sensitive to VEGF. Faricimab is a humanized bispecific IgG1 antibody. One arm neutralizes VEGF-A so it cannot reach VEGFR-1 or VEGFR-2. The other arm neutralizes Ang-2, so Tie2 signaling from angiopoietin-1 can restore stable vessel walls. The Fc region is engineered to remove immune effector functions and FcRn binding, which helps the antibody clear quickly once it leaves the eye.", "body": [["Retina", "Fluid in and under the retina dries as vessel leakage falls, which can stabilize or improve vision."], ["Choroidal vessels", "Abnormal new vessels in wet AMD stop growing and leaking."], ["Retinal vessels", "Blocking Ang-2 helps vessels stay stable, which may allow longer gaps between injections."], ["Eye after injection", "Conjunctival bleeding, eye pain, floaters and a brief rise in eye pressure are related to the needle injection."], ["Inside the eye", "Rare inflammation, retinal vasculitis or vessel blockage can occur and may threaten vision."], ["Whole body", "Very little reaches the circulation, but arterial clot events are a class warning for anti-VEGF drugs."]], "journey": "Faricimab is injected directly into the vitreous gel by an eye specialist. From there it spreads to the retina and choroid and slowly leaves the eye. Small amounts enter the blood, peaking within about two days, with a systemic half-life of about seven and a half days. As a protein, it is broken down by normal protein catabolism into amino acids. The effect in the eye lasts long enough to space injections weeks to months apart.", "sidefx": "Most serious risks come from the injection itself: endophthalmitis (infection inside the eye) from introducing organisms, retinal tear or detachment from needle and vitreous traction, and pressure spikes from adding volume to the eye. Intraocular inflammation and rare retinal vasculitis are immune reactions to the antibody inside the eye. Arterial thromboembolic events are a theoretical class risk because VEGF helps maintain healthy vessels. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Faricimab's name hides its trick: one antibody that grabs two different targets, a design called bispecific.", "src": ["Vabysmo (faricimab-svoa) FDA label", "Ferrara N, Henzel WJ. Biochem Biophys Res Commun 1989"]}, "brolucizumab": {"origin": "Brolucizumab started as a single-chain antibody fragment developed by ESBATech, a Swiss biotech company spun out of the University of Zurich. Alcon acquired ESBATech in 2009, and the program continued under Novartis. The team stripped an antibody down to its smallest binding part so that much more drug could fit in one tiny injection, with the hope of longer intervals between treatments for wet age-related macular degeneration. The FDA approved it as Beovu in 2019. In 2020 reports of retinal vasculitis and retinal vessel blockage, sometimes with severe vision loss, led to updated warnings, and its use became more cautious. Approval for diabetic macular edema followed in 2022.", "timeline": [["1989", "VEGF is isolated, opening the path to anti-VEGF eye therapy."], ["2009", "Alcon acquires ESBATech, developer of the brolucizumab fragment."], ["2019", "FDA approves brolucizumab (Beovu) for wet AMD."], ["2020", "Labeling is updated with warnings for retinal vasculitis and retinal vascular occlusion."], ["2022", "Brolucizumab is approved for diabetic macular edema."]], "cell": "VEGF-A released by stressed retinal cells binds VEGFR-1 and VEGFR-2 on endothelial cells, activating tyrosine kinase signaling that makes vessel cells divide, sprout and leak. Brolucizumab is a humanized single-chain variable fragment (scFv), the antigen-binding part of an antibody with no constant regions, at about 26 kilodaltons. It binds all major isoforms of VEGF-A and blocks their binding to VEGFR-1 and VEGFR-2. Without VEGF signaling, endothelial proliferation and permeability fall, choroidal neovascular membranes regress and fluid in the retina resorbs. Its small size lets a high molar amount fit into one injection and may help it penetrate retinal tissue, aiming for longer-lasting effect.", "body": [["Retina", "Retinal fluid and swelling decrease, which can stabilize or improve central vision."], ["Choroid", "Abnormal new vessels beneath the retina in wet AMD regress and stop leaking."], ["Retinal vessels", "Rarely, immune-mediated inflammation of retinal vessels (vasculitis) can block them and cause severe vision loss."], ["Eye after injection", "Floaters, conjunctival bleeding, eye pain and pressure rise relate to the injection procedure."], ["Lens", "Cataract is reported in trials, partly linked to repeated injections."], ["Whole body", "Small amounts reach the blood, and arterial clot events are a class warning for anti-VEGF agents."]], "journey": "Brolucizumab is injected into the vitreous by an eye specialist. It diffuses into the retina and choroid and then slowly exits the eye. Small amounts reach the blood, peaking within about a day, with a systemic half-life of about four and a half days. Being a small protein, it is broken down by proteolysis, and some may be filtered by the kidneys. Maintenance injections can be spaced weeks to months apart.", "sidefx": "Intraocular inflammation is more common than with some other anti-VEGF drugs, and in some people it involves the retinal vessels, leading to occlusive vasculitis. This is thought to be an immune response, and anti-drug antibodies are associated with higher risk. Endophthalmitis, retinal detachment and pressure spikes come from the injection. Arterial clot events are a theoretical class effect of VEGF blockade. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Brolucizumab is roughly one-sixth the size of a full antibody, which is why so many more molecules fit into each injection.", "src": ["Beovu (brolucizumab-dbll) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "pegcetacoplan": {"origin": "Pegcetacoplan descends from compstatin, a small ring-shaped peptide found in 1996 by John Lambris and colleagues at the University of Pennsylvania. They screened huge libraries of random peptides displayed on bacteriophages and found one that binds complement component C3, the central hub of the complement system. Apellis Pharmaceuticals built on this work, pairing two compstatin-like peptides with polyethylene glycol (PEG) to make the drug last longer. Under the name Empaveli it was approved in 2021 as an under-the-skin treatment for paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria. In 2023 the FDA approved an eye injection, Syfovre, as the first treatment for geographic atrophy, the advanced dry form of age-related macular degeneration.", "timeline": [["1996", "The Lambris lab at the University of Pennsylvania discovers compstatin, a C3-binding peptide."], ["2021", "FDA approves pegcetacoplan (Empaveli) for paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria."], ["2023", "FDA approves Syfovre as the first treatment for geographic atrophy."]], "cell": "The complement system is a cascade of blood and tissue proteins. All three pathways (classical, lectin and alternative) meet at C3. C3 convertases split C3 into C3a, an inflammatory signal, and C3b, which coats cells (opsonization), amplifies the alternative pathway and builds the C5 convertase. C5 is then split into C5a and C5b, and C5b starts the membrane attack complex (C5b-9) that punches holes in cells. Genetic and pathology studies link overactive complement to retinal pigment epithelium and photoreceptor loss in geographic atrophy. Pegcetacoplan binds C3 and C3b and blocks C3 cleavage and the amplification loop. Less C3b deposition, less C3a and less downstream C5 activation and membrane attack complex means less complement-driven injury, so atrophy lesions grow more slowly. It does not restore cells that are already lost.", "body": [["Retina", "Geographic atrophy lesions enlarge more slowly over months, but vision already lost does not come back."], ["Choroid", "New wet macular degeneration (choroidal neovascularization) develops in some treated eyes, so eyes are monitored for it."], ["Inside the eye", "Inflammation, including rare retinal vasculitis that can block vessels and cause severe vision loss, has been reported."], ["Eye after injection", "Conjunctival bleeding, eye discomfort, floaters and a rise in eye pressure are linked to the injection procedure."], ["Whole body", "Systemic levels after eye injection are low, so body-wide complement suppression is not expected as it is with the subcutaneous product."]], "journey": "Syfovre is injected into the vitreous by an eye specialist, where the PEG chains slow its clearance so each dose lasts weeks. The drug diffuses toward the retina and slowly leaves the eye. Systemic concentrations are low. It is expected to be broken down into peptides and amino acids, while the PEG portion is cleared slowly by the kidneys.", "sidefx": "Endophthalmitis, retinal detachment and pressure rises come from the injection itself. Intraocular inflammation and occlusive retinal vasculitis are thought to be immune reactions, possibly related to the drug or its PEG component. The higher rate of new wet AMD may relate to how complement inhibition changes the local balance of signals in the choroid. The subcutaneous form carries a boxed warning for serious infections with encapsulated bacteria, but that risk relates to body-wide complement blockade. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Compstatin was found by phage display, a method that lets viruses that infect bacteria carry billions of random peptides so scientists can fish out the few that stick to a target.", "src": ["Syfovre (pegcetacoplan) FDA label", "Empaveli (pegcetacoplan) FDA label", "Sahu A, Kay BK, Lambris JD. J Immunol 1996"]}, "avacincaptad-pegol": {"origin": "Avacincaptad pegol is an aptamer, a short strand of synthetic RNA that folds into a shape that grabs a protein target, a little like an antibody. Aptamers became possible in 1990 when two groups, Craig Tuerk and Larry Gold, and Andrew Ellington and Jack Szostak, described methods to evolve RNA strands in a test tube to bind targets (the method called SELEX). The first aptamer drug, pegaptanib, was approved for wet macular degeneration in 2004. Avacincaptad pegol, which binds complement C5, was first developed as ARC1905 by Archemix, then by Ophthotech, later renamed IVERIC bio, which Astellas acquired in 2023. The FDA approved it as Izervay in 2023 for geographic atrophy.", "timeline": [["1990", "Tuerk and Gold, and Ellington and Szostak, describe in vitro selection of RNA aptamers."], ["2004", "Pegaptanib becomes the first FDA-approved aptamer drug."], ["2023", "FDA approves avacincaptad pegol (Izervay) for geographic atrophy."]], "cell": "In the complement cascade, C5 convertase splits C5 into C5a and C5b. C5a is a strong anaphylatoxin that attracts and activates inflammatory cells. C5b joins C6, C7, C8 and several C9 molecules to form the membrane attack complex (C5b-9), a pore that can damage retinal pigment epithelium and photoreceptor cells. Avacincaptad pegol is a pegylated RNA aptamer that binds C5 and blocks its cleavage. Neither C5a nor C5b is formed, so the membrane attack complex does not assemble and C5a-driven inflammation is reduced. Unlike C3 inhibition, upstream C3 activity, including opsonization by C3b, is preserved. Less terminal complement injury slows the growth of geographic atrophy lesions.", "body": [["Retina", "Geographic atrophy lesions grow more slowly over months; lost vision is not restored."], ["Choroid", "Conversion to wet macular degeneration (choroidal neovascularization) can occur, so eyes are watched for new bleeding or fluid."], ["Eye after injection", "Conjunctival bleeding, eye pain, blurred vision and a rise in eye pressure follow the injection procedure."], ["Inside the eye", "Intraocular inflammation and rare endophthalmitis can occur."], ["Whole body", "Very little reaches the blood, so body-wide complement effects are not expected."]], "journey": "The drug is injected into the vitreous by an eye specialist. The PEG chain slows its exit from the eye, giving long residence in the vitreous. It is expected to be broken down by endonucleases and exonucleases, enzymes that cut RNA, and very little reaches the circulation. Effects show only over months of repeated injections.", "sidefx": "Most serious events relate to the injection: endophthalmitis from bacteria introduced by the needle, retinal detachment from traction and pressure spikes from added volume. Conversion to wet AMD may reflect shifts in local signaling when complement is blocked. Inflammation inside the eye can occur. Because the RNA is chemically modified and pegylated, it resists breakdown and does not act like a gene medicine. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Aptamers are sometimes called chemical antibodies because they are built from RNA or DNA instead of protein, yet bind their targets with antibody-like precision.", "src": ["Izervay (avacincaptad pegol) FDA label", "Tuerk C, Gold L. Science 1990", "Ellington AD, Szostak JW. Nature 1990"]}, "verteporfin": {"origin": "Verteporfin is a benzoporphyrin derivative developed in Vancouver, Canada. Chemist David Dolphin at the University of British Columbia and immunologist Julia Levy co-founded Quadra Logic Technologies (later QLT) to develop light-activated drugs. Porphyrins are pigment molecules related to the heme in blood, and they can absorb light and pass the energy to oxygen. QLT and Ciba Vision (Novartis) developed verteporfin for photodynamic therapy of abnormal blood vessels under the retina. The FDA approved it as Visudyne in 2000. It was one of the first treatments that could slow vision loss in wet macular degeneration, before anti-VEGF injections largely replaced it.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Quadra Logic Technologies is founded in Vancouver to develop photodynamic drugs."], ["2000", "FDA approves verteporfin (Visudyne) for photodynamic therapy of wet macular degeneration."], ["2006", "Anti-VEGF injections become first-line therapy and largely replace photodynamic therapy for wet AMD."]], "cell": "Verteporfin is a light-activated drug with no receptor target. In the blood it rides mainly on low-density lipoprotein (LDL) and other lipoproteins. Fast-growing new vessel cells in choroidal neovascularization express many LDL receptors, so they take up more of the drug. About 15 minutes after the infusion starts, a nonthermal laser at 689 nanometers is shone only on the abnormal vessels. Verteporfin absorbs this red light and moves into an excited state, then passes the energy to oxygen to make singlet oxygen and other reactive oxygen species. These damage the endothelial cell membranes, activate platelets and cause local clotting, so the leaking vessels close. Tissue that is not lit is largely spared because the drug only becomes toxic where the light hits.", "body": [["Choroidal vessels", "Abnormal leaking vessels are sealed by clotting, which slows vision loss in certain forms of wet macular degeneration."], ["Retina", "Some people notice temporary blurred vision, visual field changes or, rarely, severe loss of vision within a week."], ["Skin", "Drug circulating in the skin makes it sensitive to sunlight and bright indoor light for about five days, so burns can occur."], ["Eyes", "The eyes are also sensitive to bright light during the precaution period."], ["Infusion site", "Pain, swelling and inflammation can occur, and leakage out of the vein can damage tissue that is then exposed to light."], ["Back", "Back pain during the infusion is a known reaction of uncertain cause."]], "journey": "Verteporfin is given by intravenous infusion and is carried by lipoproteins and albumin. It reaches the neovascular tissue within minutes, which is why the laser is applied at a fixed time after the infusion starts. Its plasma half-life is about five to six hours. Liver and plasma esterases form a minor diacid metabolite, and the drug leaves mainly in the bile and feces, with almost none in the urine.", "sidefx": "Photosensitivity happens because the drug is in skin and eye tissues and will make reactive oxygen anywhere strong light falls, so direct sunlight and bright lights are avoided for five days. Extravasation is dangerous for the same reason: drug in the tissues plus light causes burns. Severe decreased vision within a week can reflect injury to normal choroidal vessels or retinal pigment epithelium. Infusion back pain and rare anaphylaxis can occur. There is no specific antidote; protection from light is the key precaution.", "fun": "Researchers later found that verteporfin also blocks the YAP-TEAD signaling complex in cells, without any light, and it is widely used as a laboratory tool in cancer biology.", "src": ["Visudyne (verteporfin) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "lotilaner-ophthalmic": {"origin": "Lotilaner began as a veterinary medicine. It belongs to the isoxazoline class of parasite killers, and it came out of animal health research and was marketed by Elanco as Credelio, a chewable flea and tick treatment for dogs approved in 2018. Researchers at Tarsus Pharmaceuticals realized that the same chemistry could kill Demodex mites, tiny relatives of spiders that live in human eyelash follicles and can cause Demodex blepharitis, with crusty collarettes at the base of the lashes. They developed a lotilaner eye drop, and in 2023 the FDA approved Xdemvy as the first treatment specifically for Demodex blepharitis.", "timeline": [["2018", "Lotilaner is approved for dogs as the flea and tick product Credelio."], ["2023", "FDA approves Xdemvy lotilaner eye drops for Demodex blepharitis."]], "cell": "Demodex folliculorum and Demodex brevis are mites with nerve and muscle cells controlled by gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA)-gated chloride channels. In mites, GABA opening these channels lets chloride in, which calms nerve and muscle activity. Lotilaner is an isoxazoline that blocks these mite GABA-gated chloride channels. Without this inhibitory signal, mite nerves fire uncontrollably, causing spastic paralysis and death. The drug is lipophilic, so it concentrates in the oily lid margin and follicles where the mites live. It has much lower affinity for mammalian GABA-A receptors, which gives a wide safety margin in people.", "body": [["Eyelash follicles", "Mites in the follicles are killed, so mite counts fall over weeks."], ["Lash base", "Collarettes, the waxy cylindrical crusts around lash bases, clear as mite activity stops."], ["Eyelid margin", "Redness and inflammation of the lid margin improve as the mite load drops."], ["Eye on dosing", "Stinging and burning at instillation occur in about one in ten people."], ["Eyelid glands", "Chalazion or stye is reported uncommonly."]], "journey": "The drops are placed on the eye and spread to the lid margin, where the lipophilic drug concentrates in the oily secretions and follicles. A full course lasts six weeks so that mites hatching from eggs are also killed. Systemic exposure is low, and metabolism and excretion after eye use are not well characterized.", "sidefx": "Stinging and burning come from the solution on the ocular surface. Chalazion, stye and punctate keratitis are uncommon. Because lotilaner binds mammalian GABA-A channels weakly, nervous system effects are not expected at the tiny exposures from eye drops. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The drug in a human eye drop for eyelash mites is the same molecule sold as a chewable that kills fleas and ticks on dogs.", "src": ["Xdemvy (lotilaner ophthalmic solution) FDA label", "Credelio (lotilaner) FDA veterinary label"]}, "ciprofloxacin-ophthalmic": {"origin": "Ciprofloxacin was discovered in 1981 by chemist Klaus Grohe and colleagues at Bayer in Germany. Adding a cyclopropyl group to the fluoroquinolone core gave a drug with far stronger activity, especially against gram-negative bacteria such as Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Oral ciprofloxacin was approved in the US in 1987 and became one of the most used antibiotics in the world. Alcon developed eye forms, Ciloxan drops and later an ointment, which became standard for bacterial conjunctivitis and corneal ulcers, including Pseudomonas ulcers in contact lens wearers.", "timeline": [["1962", "Nalidixic acid, the first quinolone, is discovered."], ["1981", "Klaus Grohe's team at Bayer synthesizes ciprofloxacin."], ["1987", "Oral ciprofloxacin is approved in the US."], ["1990s", "Ciprofloxacin eye drops and ointment (Ciloxan) become available in the US."]], "cell": "Ciprofloxacin enters bacteria through porin channels and binds DNA gyrase (GyrA and GyrB subunits) and topoisomerase IV. These enzymes cut both strands of DNA, pass another segment through and reseal the cut, which relieves supercoiling and separates daughter chromosomes. Ciprofloxacin traps the enzyme on cut DNA, forming a stable cleavage complex. Replication forks crash into these complexes and the DNA breaks, which triggers cell death. Killing is concentration dependent. Gyrase is the main target in gram-negative bacteria and topoisomerase IV in gram-positive bacteria. Human topoisomerases are much less affected.", "body": [["Cornea", "In bacterial corneal ulcers, high drug levels stop bacterial growth, including Pseudomonas aeruginosa, and allow healing."], ["Conjunctiva", "Bacterial conjunctivitis resolves as Haemophilus, Staphylococcus and other organisms are killed."], ["Corneal deposits", "A white crystalline precipitate can form in a corneal ulcer as drug crystallizes at the pH of the eye surface."], ["Ocular surface", "Burning, crusting and foreign body sensation can occur after drops."], ["Taste", "Drug draining down the tear duct can leave a bad taste."]], "journey": "Drops or ointment provide high concentrations in tears and corneal tissue. Most of the dose is washed away by tears and drains through the nasolacrimal duct. Only a small amount reaches the blood, where ciprofloxacin has a half-life of about four hours and is cleared mainly by the kidneys. Systemic effects are not expected.", "sidefx": "The white corneal precipitate happens because ciprofloxacin is less soluble at the near-neutral pH of the eye surface than in the acidic solution in the bottle. It usually clears and does not stop treatment. Burning and discomfort come from the drop. Prolonged use can allow resistant bacteria or fungi to overgrow. Hypersensitivity, including rare anaphylaxis, can occur. The tendon, nerve and heart rhythm warnings of oral fluoroquinolones are not expected with eye use. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Ciprofloxacin became a household name in 2001 when it was widely used to protect people exposed to anthrax spores in the US.", "src": ["Ciloxan (ciprofloxacin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "tobramycin-dexamethasone-ophthalmic": {"origin": "This combination joins two older drugs. Tobramycin is an aminoglycoside from the soil bacterium Streptomyces tenebrarius, part of a group of compounds found by Eli Lilly researchers in the late 1960s and approved in the 1970s. Dexamethasone is a fluorinated corticosteroid made at Merck in the late 1950s. Alcon combined them in TobraDex, a suspension and ointment for eye inflammation where bacterial infection is present or likely, such as after surgery or with blepharitis. A version with a lower steroid strength in a thicker vehicle, TobraDex ST, was approved later.", "timeline": [["1958", "Dexamethasone is approved after development at Merck."], ["1960s", "Eli Lilly researchers find tobramycin in Streptomyces tenebrarius cultures."], ["1970s", "Tobramycin is approved for clinical use in the US."], ["1980s", "TobraDex ophthalmic suspension becomes available."]], "cell": "Tobramycin crosses bacterial membranes by an oxygen-dependent transport step, so it works on aerobic bacteria. Inside, it binds the 30S ribosomal subunit, blocks formation of the initiation complex and makes the ribosome misread mRNA. Faulty proteins insert into the membrane, letting in more drug, and the cell dies (bactericidal). It covers many gram-negative organisms, including Pseudomonas aeruginosa, and Staphylococcus. Dexamethasone enters cells and binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor, which moves to the nucleus. It induces annexin A1, which inhibits phospholipase A2 and prostaglandin and leukotriene production, and represses NF-kB and AP-1 driven cytokines and adhesion molecules. The steroid calms the inflammation, while the antibiotic deals with the bacteria that steroid suppression might otherwise let grow.", "body": [["Eyelids and conjunctiva", "Inflammation such as blepharitis or postoperative swelling improves while susceptible bacteria are controlled."], ["Anterior segment", "Less redness, swelling, fibrin and cellular infiltration as the steroid blocks inflammatory signaling."], ["Eye pressure", "The steroid can raise intraocular pressure in some people by changing the trabecular meshwork, so pressure is monitored with use beyond about ten days."], ["Lens", "Prolonged steroid use can cause posterior subcapsular cataract."], ["Cornea", "Healing can slow and herpes simplex or fungal keratitis can flare because local immunity is suppressed."], ["Immune system", "Tobramycin can cause local allergy with itching and lid swelling."]], "journey": "The suspension is shaken and placed on the eye. Tobramycin acts mainly on the surface and in superficial tissues, while dexamethasone penetrates the cornea into the anterior chamber. Effects last several hours per dose. Systemic absorption is minimal. Any absorbed dexamethasone is metabolized by liver CYP3A4, and any absorbed tobramycin is cleared unchanged by the kidneys.", "sidefx": "The steroid part drives most risks: raised eye pressure from trabecular meshwork changes, cataract, delayed healing, thinning of the cornea or sclera and secondary infection, including fungal and herpes keratitis, because immune defenses are suppressed. Tobramycin can cause local allergy and may let resistant organisms or fungi overgrow. The kidney and ear toxicity of injected aminoglycosides is not expected with eye use. There is no antidote; effects are managed by stopping under supervision.", "src": ["TobraDex (tobramycin and dexamethasone) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "neomycin-polymyxin-dexamethasone": {"origin": "This three-part eye product brings together antibiotics and a steroid from the golden age of antibiotic discovery. Neomycin was isolated in 1949 by Selman Waksman and Hubert Lechevalier at Rutgers University from the soil bacterium Streptomyces fradiae. Polymyxins were found in 1947 in Bacillus polymyxa. Dexamethasone was made at Merck in the late 1950s. Alcon combined the three in Maxitrol suspension and ointment, giving broad antibacterial coverage plus strong anti-inflammatory action for steroid-responsive eye inflammation with bacterial infection or risk of it.", "timeline": [["1947", "Polymyxins are discovered from Bacillus polymyxa."], ["1949", "Waksman and Lechevalier isolate neomycin from Streptomyces fradiae."], ["1958", "Dexamethasone is approved after development at Merck."]], "cell": "Neomycin is an aminoglycoside that binds the 30S ribosomal subunit, causing misreading of mRNA and faulty protein synthesis, which kills many gram-negative bacteria and Staphylococcus aureus. It is weak against Pseudomonas and streptococci. Polymyxin B fills the Pseudomonas gap: it is a cationic peptide that binds lipid A of lipopolysaccharide, displaces stabilizing calcium and magnesium and disrupts gram-negative outer and inner membranes like a detergent. Dexamethasone binds the glucocorticoid receptor, which moves into the nucleus, induces annexin A1 to inhibit phospholipase A2, and represses NF-kB and AP-1 driven inflammatory genes. Together, the antibiotics cover a broad spectrum while the steroid calms inflammation.", "body": [["Eyelids and conjunctiva", "Steroid-responsive inflammation such as blepharitis or conjunctivitis improves while bacteria are controlled."], ["Anterior segment", "Postoperative redness, swelling and cell infiltration decrease."], ["Skin around the eye", "Neomycin is a common contact allergen, so some people develop itchy, red, swollen lids that can look like worsening infection."], ["Eye pressure", "The steroid can raise intraocular pressure in steroid responders."], ["Lens", "Long courses of steroid can cause posterior subcapsular cataract."], ["Cornea", "Herpes simplex and fungal keratitis can worsen because local immunity is suppressed, and healing slows."]], "journey": "The suspension or ointment acts on the ocular surface and lids. Dexamethasone penetrates the cornea into the eye, while the antibiotics stay mostly on the surface. Systemic absorption is minimal. Any absorbed dexamethasone is metabolized by liver CYP3A4, and absorbed neomycin and polymyxin B are cleared by the kidneys.", "sidefx": "Neomycin sensitization is a classic cause of allergic contact dermatitis, with lid itching and swelling caused by a delayed T-cell reaction to the drug. Steroid effects explain raised eye pressure, cataract, slowed healing, thinning of the cornea or sclera and secondary fungal or herpes infection. Antibiotic use can allow resistant organisms to overgrow. Ear and kidney toxicity of systemic aminoglycosides and polymyxins is not expected with eye use. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Neomycin was named Allergen of the Year in 2010 by the American Contact Dermatitis Society because it so often causes skin allergy.", "src": ["Maxitrol (neomycin, polymyxin B and dexamethasone) FDA label", "Waksman SA, Lechevalier HA. Science 1949"]}, "methazolamide": {"origin": "Methazolamide comes from the sulfonamide family. In 1940 researchers found that sulfanilamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase. Chemists at Lederle Laboratories then made acetazolamide in the early 1950s, and in 1954 ophthalmologist Bernard Becker showed that it lowers eye pressure. Lederle chemists modified the molecule further, adding a methyl group to the thiadiazole ring, to make methazolamide. It was introduced in the late 1950s as Neptazane. It is more lipid soluble than acetazolamide, enters the eye better and depends less on the kidneys for clearance. The brand has been discontinued, but generic tablets are available.", "timeline": [["1940", "Sulfanilamide is found to inhibit carbonic anhydrase."], ["1954", "Bernard Becker reports acetazolamide lowers eye pressure in glaucoma."], ["1950s", "Lederle introduces methazolamide (Neptazane)."]], "cell": "Methazolamide noncompetitively inhibits carbonic anhydrase, mainly isoenzyme II, in the ciliary process epithelium. Carbonic anhydrase speeds the reaction of carbon dioxide and water into bicarbonate and a hydrogen ion. Bicarbonate secretion into the posterior chamber carries sodium and water with it to form aqueous humor. With the enzyme blocked, less bicarbonate, sodium and water move into the eye, so aqueous production and intraocular pressure fall. In the proximal tubule of the kidney, carbonic anhydrase inhibition reduces bicarbonate reabsorption, causing a mild bicarbonate diuresis and metabolic acidosis. This renal effect is weaker than with acetazolamide.", "body": [["Eye pressure", "Aqueous humor production falls and intraocular pressure drops within a few hours, with peak effect at six to eight hours."], ["Kidneys", "Bicarbonate is lost in the urine, causing mild metabolic acidosis and more urine; potassium can be lost too."], ["Nerves", "Tingling of fingers, toes and around the mouth is common, likely related to acidosis and changes in nerve ion balance."], ["Taste", "Carbonated drinks taste flat because carbonic anhydrase on sour-sensing taste cells helps detect carbonation."], ["Brain and mood", "Fatigue, malaise, depression and confusion can occur, especially in older adults."], ["Urinary tract", "Kidney stones can form because alkaline urine and less citrate favor calcium phosphate stones."]], "journey": "Methazolamide is taken by mouth and absorbed well from the gut. It is moderately protein bound and lipid soluble, so it enters the eye easily. Effects begin in two to four hours. It is partly metabolized in the liver, with roughly a quarter excreted unchanged in urine. Its half-life is about 14 hours, and pressure lowering lasts 10 to 18 hours.", "sidefx": "Most side effects follow from carbonic anhydrase inhibition throughout the body. Kidney effects cause metabolic acidosis and low potassium. Acidosis and electrolyte shifts lead to tingling, fatigue and loss of appetite. Taste changes come from blocked carbonic anhydrase in taste cells. Kidney stones relate to altered urine chemistry. Because it is a sulfonamide, rare but serious reactions such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome, aplastic anemia, agranulocytosis and liver necrosis can occur. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Research in mice showed that carbonic anhydrase on sour-sensing taste cells is what lets us sense the fizz of carbonation, which explains why these drugs make soda taste flat.", "src": ["Methazolamide tablets FDA label", "Becker B. Am J Ophthalmol 1954", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "ganciclovir-ophthalmic": {"origin": "Ganciclovir is a guanosine analog made in the early 1980s, and researchers at Syntex in California were among those who developed it. It was approved in 1989 as an intravenous drug (Cytovene) for cytomegalovirus retinitis, a blinding infection in people with AIDS. Ganciclovir is also active against herpes simplex virus. A gel for herpes simplex eye infection was first used in Europe, and in 2009 the FDA approved Zirgan, ganciclovir eye gel, for acute herpetic keratitis with dendritic ulcers.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Ganciclovir is synthesized and developed, including work at Syntex."], ["1989", "Intravenous ganciclovir is approved for CMV retinitis."], ["2009", "FDA approves ganciclovir ophthalmic gel (Zirgan) for herpetic keratitis."]], "cell": "Ganciclovir is an inactive prodrug until it gets into a virus-infected cell. In cells infected with herpes simplex virus, the viral thymidine kinase adds the first phosphate to make ganciclovir monophosphate. Normal cells do this poorly, which concentrates activation in infected cells. Cellular kinases then add two more phosphates to form ganciclovir triphosphate. This molecule competes with deoxyguanosine triphosphate at the viral DNA polymerase, and when incorporated it slows and eventually stops chain elongation. Viral replication in the corneal epithelium falls, so the branching dendritic ulcer heals.", "body": [["Cornea", "Viral replication in the corneal epithelium falls, and dendritic ulcers heal over days."], ["Vision", "The gel causes brief blurred vision after each application."], ["Ocular surface", "Irritation, redness and punctate keratitis can occur from the gel and from the drug's effect on surface cells."], ["Whole body", "Very little drug reaches the blood, so the bone marrow toxicity of IV ganciclovir is not expected."]], "journey": "The gel is applied into the lower conjunctival sac, where it spreads over the cornea and stays longer than a drop. The drug enters corneal epithelial cells, and in infected cells it is phosphorylated to the active triphosphate. Systemic absorption is minimal. By comparison, systemic ganciclovir is cleared by the kidneys with a half-life of about two and a half to three and a half hours.", "sidefx": "Blurred vision comes from the gel coating the eye. Irritation and punctate keratitis reflect contact of the gel with surface cells, and some uptake by healthy cells. Systemic ganciclovir causes neutropenia, anemia and thrombocytopenia because it also affects dividing marrow cells, but these effects are not expected with eye gel because blood levels are tiny. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The same molecule used as a gel for a cold sore virus in the cornea was first approved as an IV drug to save the sight of people with AIDS-related CMV retinitis.", "src": ["Zirgan (ganciclovir ophthalmic gel) FDA label", "Cytovene (ganciclovir) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "trifluridine-ophthalmic": {"origin": "Trifluridine, also called trifluorothymidine, was first made in the early 1960s by Charles Heidelberger and colleagues at the University of Wisconsin, the same researcher behind the cancer drug 5-fluorouracil. It was studied first as an anticancer agent. Around the same time, Herbert Kaufman showed in 1962 that idoxuridine, a related nucleoside, could treat herpes infection of the cornea, the first proven antiviral therapy. Trifluridine proved more potent and better tolerated for herpes keratitis, and the eye drop Viroptic was approved by the FDA in 1980. Decades later, trifluridine returned to cancer care when it was combined with tipiracil in an oral tablet approved in 2015 for colorectal cancer.", "timeline": [["1962", "Herbert Kaufman reports idoxuridine for herpes keratitis, the first clinical antiviral therapy."], ["1960s", "Charles Heidelberger's group synthesizes trifluridine."], ["1980", "FDA approves trifluridine eye drops (Viroptic)."], ["2015", "Trifluridine with tipiracil is approved as an oral cancer treatment."]], "cell": "Trifluridine is a thymidine analog with three fluorine atoms on its methyl group. Cellular thymidine kinase phosphorylates it to the monophosphate, which inhibits thymidylate synthase and reduces the supply of normal thymidine. Further phosphorylation produces trifluridine triphosphate, which DNA polymerases incorporate into DNA in place of thymidine. The resulting DNA is defective, and herpes simplex virus cannot produce working copies of its genome, so viral replication in the corneal epithelium falls. Unlike acyclovir and ganciclovir, trifluridine does not need viral thymidine kinase for activation, so it also enters DNA of uninfected cells. That lack of selectivity explains its toxicity to the corneal surface with prolonged use.", "body": [["Cornea", "Herpes simplex replication in the epithelium falls, so dendritic and geographic ulcers heal over days to about two weeks."], ["Corneal epithelium", "Because healthy cells also take up the drug, prolonged use can cause punctate keratopathy and slow healing."], ["Eyelids", "Lid swelling and mild burning or stinging can occur after drops."], ["Whole body", "Systemic absorption is negligible, so the bone marrow effects seen with oral trifluridine are not expected."]], "journey": "Drops are placed on the eye frequently because the drug is cleared quickly. It penetrates the corneal epithelium and is rapidly metabolized in the eye and blood. Systemic absorption is minimal, and trifluridine that does reach the blood has a half-life of only about 12 minutes. The short presence on the eye is why dosing is frequent.", "sidefx": "Burning and stinging come from the solution. Superficial punctate keratopathy and epithelial toxicity happen because trifluridine is incorporated into the DNA of normal dividing corneal cells, not just virus-infected ones. This is why the course is limited in length. Hypersensitivity and stromal edema can occur. There is no antidote; toxicity resolves after stopping.", "fun": "The same molecule that heals herpes ulcers on the eye is also part of an oral pill for advanced colorectal cancer.", "src": ["Viroptic (trifluridine) FDA label", "Kaufman HE. Proc Soc Exp Biol Med 1962", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "natamycin": {"origin": "Natamycin was isolated in 1955 by scientists at the Dutch company Gist-Brocades from a strain of the soil bacterium Streptomyces natalensis, collected in soil near Pietermaritzburg in the Natal province of South Africa, which gave the drug its name. It was first called pimaricin. Natamycin is a polyene like amphotericin B and nystatin, but it binds fungal sterol without making large pores, and it is poorly absorbed, so it is well suited to surface infections. It became the only antifungal eye drop approved by the FDA, marketed as Natacyn, and is a key treatment for fungal keratitis caused by filamentous fungi such as Fusarium. It is also widely used as a food preservative.", "timeline": [["1955", "Natamycin is isolated from Streptomyces natalensis at Gist-Brocades in the Netherlands."]], "cell": "Natamycin binds ergosterol, the main sterol in fungal cell membranes, which human cells replace with cholesterol. Unlike amphotericin B and nystatin, it does not form large ion-leaking pores. Instead, binding ergosterol blocks ergosterol-dependent processes in the membrane, such as amino acid and glucose transport proteins and the fusion of vacuoles. Without these, fungal growth stops, and at higher concentrations the cells die. It is active against filamentous fungi such as Fusarium and Aspergillus and against Candida.", "body": [["Cornea", "Fungal growth in the corneal stroma is halted, which allows corneal ulcers to heal over weeks."], ["Conjunctiva", "Irritation, redness and swelling of the conjunctiva can occur."], ["Vision", "The thick suspension coats the eye and blurs vision temporarily."], ["Whole body", "Natamycin is barely absorbed, so systemic effects are not expected."]], "journey": "The suspension is placed on the eye frequently at first. It adheres to the ulcer and the cornea, and drug penetrates the corneal stroma, best when the epithelium is missing over the ulcer. Penetration into deep stroma and the inside of the eye is limited. Systemic absorption is negligible, and it is not significantly metabolized.", "sidefx": "Eye irritation, chemosis and redness come from the suspension on inflamed tissue. Blurred vision is due to the particles in the suspension. Because natamycin acts on ergosterol rather than cholesterol and is not absorbed, the kidney toxicity of amphotericin B is not seen. Its limited penetration means deep infections may not respond. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Natamycin is approved as a food additive (E235 in Europe) and is used on the surface of many cheeses to stop mold growth.", "src": ["Natacyn (natamycin) FDA label", "Struyk AP et al. Antibiotics Annual 1957-1958"]}, "dexamethasone-intravitreal-implant": {"origin": "Treating swelling at the back of the eye with steroid drops does not work well, because drops cannot deliver enough drug to the retina. The solution was to place a tiny slow-release steroid implant directly inside the eye. Oculex Pharmaceuticals developed an early version, and Allergan acquired the company in 2003. Allergan used a biodegradable polymer made of lactic and glycolic acid (PLGA), the same type of material used in dissolving stitches, and created an injector that places the rod-shaped implant into the vitreous through a fine needle. The FDA approved Ozurdex in 2009 for macular edema after retinal vein occlusion, then for noninfectious posterior uveitis in 2010 and diabetic macular edema in 2014.", "timeline": [["1958", "Dexamethasone is approved after development at Merck."], ["2003", "Allergan acquires Oculex Pharmaceuticals, developer of the implant technology."], ["2009", "FDA approves Ozurdex for macular edema after retinal vein occlusion."], ["2010", "Ozurdex is approved for noninfectious posterior uveitis."], ["2014", "Ozurdex is approved for diabetic macular edema."]], "cell": "Dexamethasone released from the implant diffuses into retinal cells and binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor. The receptor complex moves into the nucleus and changes gene transcription. It induces annexin A1, which inhibits phospholipase A2 and the prostaglandin and leukotriene cascade. It also blocks NF-kB and AP-1, turning down inflammatory cytokines, adhesion molecules and VEGF. In retinal vessels, this tightens endothelial junctions and stabilizes the blood-retinal barrier, so less fluid leaks into the macula. In uveitis, fewer inflammatory cells enter the vitreous. The PLGA polymer slowly breaks down by hydrolysis into lactic acid and glycolic acid, which the body handles normally.", "body": [["Macula", "Swelling in the central retina decreases over weeks, which can improve vision in vein occlusion and diabetic macular edema."], ["Vitreous", "In uveitis, inflammation and haze in the vitreous decrease."], ["Eye pressure", "Intraocular pressure rises in a sizeable share of eyes because steroids reduce outflow through the trabecular meshwork; some need drops or surgery."], ["Lens", "Cataract develops or worsens in many eyes over time with repeated implants."], ["Eye after injection", "Conjunctival bleeding, pain and floaters relate to the injection procedure."], ["Whole body", "Plasma levels are mostly undetectable, so systemic steroid effects are not expected."]], "journey": "The implant is injected into the vitreous and releases dexamethasone gradually as the polymer erodes. Peak effect comes at about two months in vein occlusion studies, and benefit lasts about three to six months. The polymer degrades completely to lactic and glycolic acid. Plasma dexamethasone is mostly undetectable; any absorbed drug would be metabolized by liver CYP3A4.", "sidefx": "Steroid effects inside the eye are the main issue. Raised eye pressure comes from glucocorticoid-induced changes in trabecular meshwork cells that block outflow. Cataract reflects steroid effects on lens fiber cells. Endophthalmitis and retinal detachment are risks of any injection into the eye. The implant can migrate into the front chamber, especially in eyes without an intact lens capsule, where it can damage the cornea. There is no antidote; pressure is managed with drops or surgery.", "fun": "Ozurdex dissolves completely inside the eye, breaking down into the same lactic and glycolic acids that dissolving stitches leave behind.", "src": ["Ozurdex (dexamethasone intravitreal implant) FDA label"]}, "fluocinolone-intravitreal-implant": {"origin": "Fluocinolone acetonide is a potent fluorinated corticosteroid first made around 1960 by chemists at Syntex and used widely in skin creams. For chronic eye inflammation, the idea was to place a small device inside the eye that releases steroid steadily for years. Control Delivery Systems, working with pharmacologist Paul Ashton, developed the first such device, Retisert, which is sutured into the eye during surgery and was approved in 2005 for chronic noninfectious posterior uveitis. A tiny injectable version followed: Iluvien, developed by Alimera Sciences with pSivida, was approved in 2014 for diabetic macular edema, and Yutiq was approved in 2018 for posterior uveitis.", "timeline": [["1960", "Fluocinolone acetonide is developed at Syntex around this time."], ["2005", "Retisert, a surgically placed fluocinolone implant, is approved for posterior uveitis."], ["2014", "FDA approves Iluvien injectable implant for diabetic macular edema."], ["2018", "Yutiq injectable implant is approved for chronic noninfectious posterior uveitis."]], "cell": "Fluocinolone acetonide released from the implant enters retinal and choroidal cells and binds the glucocorticoid receptor. The receptor complex moves into the nucleus, where it induces anti-inflammatory proteins such as annexin A1 (which inhibits phospholipase A2) and represses NF-kB and AP-1 targets including cytokines, adhesion molecules and VEGF. This reduces leukocyte stasis in retinal vessels and stabilizes tight junctions of the blood-retinal barrier, so less fluid leaks into the macula. The implant is a tiny nonbioerodible polyimide tube with a membrane that lets drug out at a low, steady rate, giving continuous steroid effect for about three years.", "body": [["Macula", "Chronic diabetic macular edema decreases with steady low-level steroid exposure, which can improve or stabilize vision."], ["Posterior segment", "In uveitis, recurrences of inflammation in the back of the eye become less frequent."], ["Eye pressure", "Intraocular pressure rises in many eyes because steroids reduce trabecular outflow; some need drops or glaucoma surgery."], ["Lens", "Cataract is very common over the three-year release period in eyes that still have their natural lens."], ["Eye after injection", "Bleeding under the conjunctiva, eye pain and floaters relate to the procedure, and the implant itself may be seen as a floater."], ["Whole body", "Plasma levels are below quantification, so systemic steroid effects are not expected."]], "journey": "The injectable implant is placed into the vitreous through a fine needle. It releases fluocinolone at a low continuous rate for about 36 months. Effects build over weeks to months. Plasma levels stay below the limit of quantification. The implant does not dissolve and remains in the eye after the drug is used up.", "sidefx": "Long, continuous steroid exposure inside the eye explains the high rates of cataract and raised intraocular pressure. Pressure rises come from glucocorticoid effects on trabecular meshwork cells, and some eyes need glaucoma surgery. Endophthalmitis and retinal detachment are risks of injection. The implant can migrate to the front of the eye if the lens capsule is not intact. There is no antidote; the effect cannot be quickly stopped once the implant is in place.", "fun": "The Iluvien implant is smaller than a grain of rice and keeps releasing medicine for about three years, long after people forget it is there.", "src": ["Iluvien (fluocinolone acetonide intravitreal implant) FDA label", "Retisert FDA label", "Yutiq FDA label"]}, "cenegermin": {"origin": "Cenegermin is a recombinant form of nerve growth factor (NGF), the first growth factor ever discovered. In the early 1950s Rita Levi-Montalcini, working with Viktor Hamburger at Washington University in St. Louis, found that a mouse tumor released a substance that made nerve fibers sprout. Biochemist Stanley Cohen helped purify the factor, and Levi-Montalcini and Cohen shared the 1986 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. In 1998 Italian researchers led by Alessandro Lambiase reported that NGF drops helped heal neurotrophic corneal ulcers, which occur when corneal nerves are damaged. The Italian company Dompe produced human NGF in E. coli, and the FDA approved Oxervate in 2018 as the first treatment for neurotrophic keratitis.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Rita Levi-Montalcini and colleagues discover nerve growth factor."], ["1986", "Levi-Montalcini and Stanley Cohen share the Nobel Prize for growth factor discoveries."], ["1998", "Lambiase and colleagues report NGF eye drops heal neurotrophic corneal ulcers."], ["2017", "Cenegermin is approved in Europe for neurotrophic keratitis."], ["2018", "FDA approves Oxervate (cenegermin) for neurotrophic keratitis."]], "cell": "Neurotrophic keratitis happens when the trigeminal nerve supply to the cornea is damaged, for example by herpes infection, surgery or diabetes. Without nerve signals, the corneal epithelium loses trophic support, tear secretion falls and wounds do not heal. Cenegermin is recombinant human NGF made in E. coli and is identical in sequence to the mature human protein. It binds the high-affinity receptor TrkA, a tyrosine kinase, which activates the PI3K-Akt and MAPK pathways to promote cell survival, proliferation and differentiation. It also binds the low-affinity p75 neurotrophin receptor. On corneal epithelial cells this drives healing of persistent defects. On corneal nerve endings it supports regrowth and sensitivity. It may also support tear secretion and the conjunctival goblet cells.", "body": [["Corneal epithelium", "Persistent epithelial defects and ulcers close over weeks as epithelial cells grow and differentiate."], ["Corneal nerves", "Nerve fibers may regrow, and corneal sensitivity can improve in some people."], ["Tear film", "Tear production and surface quality may improve, helping maintain healing."], ["Eye discomfort", "As nerves recover, eye pain can appear, which is the most common side effect."], ["Ocular surface", "Redness, foreign body sensation, increased tearing and corneal deposits can occur."]], "journey": "Cenegermin is placed on the eye as drops many times a day over an eight-week course. It acts locally on the cornea and conjunctiva and is degraded by proteases like other proteins. Systemic absorption is minimal and not clinically meaningful. Bottles are kept cold and used within a day to protect the protein.", "sidefx": "Eye pain is common and is thought to reflect returning corneal nerve function, since NGF also sensitizes pain-sensing nerves. Inflammation, redness and foreign body sensation come from local effects on an already damaged surface. Corneal deposits have been reported. Because it acts locally and is broken down quickly, systemic effects are not expected. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Rita Levi-Montalcini did some of her early nerve research in a home laboratory in her bedroom in Italy during World War II.", "src": ["Oxervate (cenegermin-bkbj) FDA label", "Lambiase A et al. N Engl J Med 1998", "Nobel Prize 1986 Physiology or Medicine"]}, "oxymetazoline-ophthalmic": {"origin": "Oxymetazoline is an imidazoline alpha agonist developed in Germany in the early 1960s by Wolfgang Fruhstorfer at E. Merck. It became famous as a long-acting nasal decongestant spray. Researchers later reasoned that its alpha-1A activity could lift a drooping eyelid by contracting the Mueller muscle, a small smooth muscle in the upper lid that is controlled by sympathetic nerves. The company Osmotica, later RVL Pharmaceuticals, developed an eye drop, and the FDA approved Upneeq in 2020 as the first drug for acquired blepharoptosis (drooping upper eyelid) in adults. A cream form had been approved in 2017 for facial redness from rosacea.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Wolfgang Fruhstorfer at E. Merck develops oxymetazoline."], ["2017", "Oxymetazoline cream is approved for persistent facial redness of rosacea."], ["2020", "FDA approves Upneeq oxymetazoline eye drops for acquired blepharoptosis."]], "cell": "The superior tarsal (Mueller) muscle is a thin smooth muscle that helps hold the upper lid up. It is controlled by sympathetic nerves through alpha-adrenergic receptors. Oxymetazoline is an agonist at alpha-1A receptors, which couple to Gq. Activation increases phospholipase C activity, raises IP3 and releases calcium, causing smooth muscle contraction. Contraction of Mueller muscle raises the upper lid by about one to two millimeters, widening the eye opening. Oxymetazoline also stimulates alpha-2 receptors, and alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction of conjunctival vessels makes the eyes look whiter. It does not strengthen the main lid lifting muscle, the levator, so it helps mild drooping rather than severe ptosis.", "body": [["Upper eyelid", "The lid rises a small amount within minutes as Mueller muscle contracts, with effect lasting several hours."], ["Visual field", "Lifting the lid can improve the upper field of vision in people whose drooping lid blocks sight."], ["Conjunctiva", "Vasoconstriction makes the eyes look whiter, though some people get redness or irritation."], ["Cornea", "Punctate keratitis and dry eye can occur."], ["Drainage angle", "Alpha stimulation can widen the pupil slightly, which could trigger angle-closure glaucoma in eyes with narrow angles."], ["Heart and vessels", "Absorbed drug can affect blood pressure, which matters in people with heart disease or uncontrolled blood pressure."]], "journey": "The drop is absorbed into the eyelid tissues and conjunctiva. Lid lift begins within about 5 to 15 minutes, peaks around one to two hours and lasts about six to eight hours. A small amount reaches the blood, where oxymetazoline has a half-life of several hours based on nasal data. Little is metabolized, and it is excreted in urine and feces.", "sidefx": "Side effects come from alpha-adrenergic stimulation. Vasoconstriction and surface irritation lead to keratitis, redness and dryness. Pupil changes can narrow the drainage angle in predisposed eyes. Absorbed drug can raise or lower blood pressure through alpha-1 and alpha-2 effects. Headache is reported. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "The drug in a common nasal decongestant spray is the same one used in an eye drop that lifts a droopy eyelid.", "src": ["Upneeq (oxymetazoline ophthalmic) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "acoltremon": {"origin": "Acoltremon works on TRPM8, the cold and menthol sensor on nerve endings. TRPM8 was identified in 2002 by the groups of David Julius at the University of California, San Francisco, and Ardem Patapoutian at Scripps Research, work that contributed to their shared 2021 Nobel Prize. Researchers noticed that cool air and cold sensations trigger reflex tearing through corneal nerves. Aerie Pharmaceuticals developed a TRPM8 agonist eye drop, AR-15512, and Alcon acquired the program in 2022. The FDA approved acoltremon as Tryptyr in 2025 for the signs and symptoms of dry eye disease, the first approved drug that targets TRPM8.", "timeline": [["2002", "The TRPM8 cold and menthol receptor is identified."], ["2021", "David Julius and Ardem Patapoutian share the Nobel Prize for discovering temperature and touch receptors."], ["2022", "Alcon acquires Aerie Pharmaceuticals and the AR-15512 program."], ["2025", "FDA approves acoltremon (Tryptyr) for dry eye disease."]], "cell": "The cornea is the most densely innervated surface in the body, supplied by the ophthalmic branch of the trigeminal nerve. Some of its sensory nerve endings carry TRPM8, a cation channel that opens with cooling or menthol. As tears evaporate and the corneal surface cools, TRPM8 channels open and the nerves fire, which reflexly drives basal tear secretion through the lacrimal functional unit (brainstem and parasympathetic nerves to the lacrimal gland). Acoltremon is a TRPM8 agonist. It opens these channels, increasing the firing of cold-sensing nerve endings. This signal travels to the brainstem, and parasympathetic output increases tear production by the lacrimal glands.", "body": [["Tear glands", "Tear production increases within the first days of treatment as the corneal cold-sensing reflex is activated."], ["Ocular surface", "Better tear coverage can ease dryness signs over time."], ["Cornea on dosing", "A cooling or stinging sensation at instillation is very common because the drug activates cold-sensing nerves."], ["Eye", "Redness, irritation and blurred vision can occur."], ["Whole body", "Systemic absorption is expected to be minimal, so effects elsewhere are not expected."]], "journey": "Drops are placed on the eye and act on corneal nerve endings at the surface. Tear production rises soon after use. Pharmacokinetic details in people are not well established, and systemic absorption is expected to be minimal.", "sidefx": "Instillation site pain and a cold, stinging feeling occur in about half of users and come straight from activating TRPM8, the same channel that makes menthol feel cold. Irritation, redness and blurred vision can occur from the drop. Because the action is local on nerve endings, systemic effects are not expected. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The receptor this drop targets is the same one that makes peppermint and menthol feel cool on the skin and in the mouth.", "src": ["Tryptyr (acoltremon) FDA label", "McKemy DD, Neuhausser WM, Julius D. Nature 2002", "Peier AM et al. Cell 2002"]}, "timolol-dorzolamide": {"origin": "This combination joins two Merck glaucoma drugs. Timolol, a nonselective beta blocker, became in 1978 the first beta blocker eye drop approved in the US (Timoptic) and quickly became a mainstay of glaucoma treatment. Dorzolamide was designed at Merck in the 1980s through a long effort to make a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor that could work as an eye drop instead of a pill. Using knowledge of the enzyme's structure, Merck chemists made a water-soluble drug able to cross the cornea. Dorzolamide was approved as Trusopt in 1994, and Merck combined it with timolol as Cosopt, approved in 1998. A preservative-free version, Cosopt PF, followed later.", "timeline": [["1978", "Timolol (Timoptic) becomes the first beta blocker eye drop approved in the US."], ["1994", "FDA approves dorzolamide (Trusopt), the first topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitor in the US."], ["1998", "FDA approves Cosopt, dorzolamide combined with timolol."]], "cell": "Both drugs reduce aqueous humor production by the ciliary epithelium, but in different ways. Dorzolamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase II, which normally converts carbon dioxide and water into bicarbonate and a hydrogen ion. Bicarbonate secretion drags sodium and water into the posterior chamber, so blocking the enzyme cuts fluid formation. Timolol blocks beta-1 and beta-2 adrenergic receptors on the ciliary epithelium. Beta-2 receptors normally couple to Gs, raising cAMP and supporting aqueous secretion, so blocking them lowers cAMP and further cuts production. With less aqueous made and outflow unchanged, intraocular pressure falls more than with either drug alone.", "body": [["Eye pressure", "Aqueous production falls from two mechanisms at once, lowering intraocular pressure within one to two hours."], ["Eye on dosing", "Burning, stinging and redness are common after drops."], ["Taste", "Dorzolamide draining to the throat causes a bitter taste."], ["Heart", "Absorbed timolol can slow the heart and worsen heart block or heart failure because it blocks beta-1 receptors."], ["Lungs", "Timolol blocks beta-2 receptors in airway smooth muscle, which can trigger bronchospasm in asthma or COPD."], ["Cornea", "Carbonic anhydrase inhibition can impair the corneal endothelial pump, so corneas with weak endothelium may swell."]], "journey": "The drops pass through the cornea to the ciliary body. A meaningful amount drains down the nasolacrimal duct and is absorbed through the nasal mucosa directly into the circulation, bypassing liver first-pass metabolism. Timolol is metabolized by liver CYP2D6 and has a systemic half-life of about four hours. Dorzolamide binds carbonic anhydrase in red blood cells and has a very long elimination half-life of over four months, being slowly converted to N-desethyldorzolamide. Both are excreted by the kidneys.", "sidefx": "Timolol's systemic beta blockade explains slow heart rate, heart block, low blood pressure, fatigue and bronchospasm, which can be severe in people with asthma. Because nasal absorption skips first-pass metabolism, even eye drops can produce these effects. Dorzolamide is a sulfonamide, so rare sulfonamide hypersensitivity reactions, including Stevens-Johnson syndrome, can occur. Bitter taste and stinging are local effects. For serious beta blocker toxicity, treatment may include atropine and glucagon.", "fun": "Dorzolamide is often cited as one of the earliest marketed drugs created with the help of structure-based drug design.", "src": ["Cosopt (dorzolamide and timolol) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "brimonidine-timolol": {"origin": "This combination joins brimonidine and timolol. Timolol, from Merck, became the first beta blocker eye drop approved in the US in 1978. Brimonidine is a highly selective alpha-2 adrenergic agonist developed by Allergan and approved in 1996 as Alphagan. Compared with the earlier apraclonidine, it has much less alpha-1 activity and causes fewer allergic reactions. Allergan combined the two into one bottle as Combigan, approved by the FDA in 2007, to lower eye pressure with two mechanisms while reducing the number of drops a person needs.", "timeline": [["1978", "Timolol (Timoptic) becomes the first beta blocker eye drop approved in the US."], ["1996", "FDA approves brimonidine (Alphagan) for glaucoma."], ["2007", "FDA approves Combigan, brimonidine combined with timolol."]], "cell": "Brimonidine binds alpha-2 adrenergic receptors in the ciliary body. These couple to Gi, which inhibits adenylyl cyclase and lowers cAMP, reducing aqueous humor secretion. Brimonidine also increases uveoscleral outflow, probably through prostaglandin-related effects in the ciliary muscle. Timolol blocks beta-1 and beta-2 adrenergic receptors on the ciliary epithelium. Normally beta-2 activation raises cAMP and supports aqueous production, so blocking it lowers production further. The two drugs together lower cAMP from two directions and add an outflow effect, giving a larger drop in intraocular pressure than either alone.", "body": [["Eye pressure", "Lower aqueous production plus more uveoscleral outflow reduces intraocular pressure, starting within about an hour."], ["Conjunctiva", "Allergic conjunctivitis with redness and itching can develop with brimonidine over weeks to months."], ["Brain", "Brimonidine crosses the blood-brain barrier, so drowsiness and fatigue can occur; in infants it can cause severe sedation and breathing pauses."], ["Mouth", "Dry mouth comes from alpha-2 effects of absorbed brimonidine on salivary glands."], ["Heart and vessels", "Timolol slows the heart and both drugs can lower blood pressure."], ["Lungs", "Timolol's beta-2 blockade can cause bronchospasm in asthma or COPD."]], "journey": "Drops enter the eye through the cornea and conjunctiva. Some drains through the nasolacrimal duct and is absorbed through the nasal mucosa straight into the circulation. Brimonidine has a systemic half-life of about three hours and is metabolized in the liver. Timolol has a half-life of about four hours and is metabolized by liver CYP2D6. Metabolites are excreted in the urine. Each dose lasts about 12 hours.", "sidefx": "Brimonidine's alpha-2 action in the brain causes sleepiness and, in infants and small children, can cause apnea, low heart rate and coma, so the drops are not used in young children. Allergic follicular conjunctivitis is a delayed immune reaction to brimonidine. Timolol's beta blockade can cause bradycardia, heart block, fatigue and bronchospasm, because nasal absorption bypasses first-pass metabolism. For serious beta blocker toxicity, atropine and glucagon may be used; care is otherwise supportive.", "fun": "Brimonidine is also used as a skin gel to reduce facial redness in rosacea, because the same alpha-2 effect narrows small skin blood vessels.", "src": ["Combigan (brimonidine and timolol) FDA label", "Alphagan P (brimonidine) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "brinzolamide-brimonidine": {"origin": "Simbrinza combines two glaucoma drugs that reduce eye pressure without a beta blocker. Brinzolamide, a topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitor from Alcon, was approved as Azopt in 1998, building on the discovery in the 1950s that carbonic anhydrase inhibitors lower eye pressure. Brimonidine, a selective alpha-2 agonist from Allergan, was approved as Alphagan in 1996. Many people cannot use timolol-containing combinations because of asthma, COPD, slow heart rate or heart block. Alcon developed this fixed combination for them, and the FDA approved Simbrinza in 2013. It was the first fixed-dose glaucoma combination in the US without a beta blocker.", "timeline": [["1954", "Bernard Becker shows that carbonic anhydrase inhibition lowers eye pressure."], ["1996", "FDA approves brimonidine (Alphagan)."], ["1998", "FDA approves brinzolamide (Azopt)."], ["2013", "FDA approves Simbrinza, the first US fixed glaucoma combination without a beta blocker."]], "cell": "Brinzolamide is a sulfonamide that binds the zinc ion in the active site of carbonic anhydrase II in the ciliary process epithelium. With the enzyme blocked, less bicarbonate is made from carbon dioxide and water, so less sodium and water follow into the posterior chamber and aqueous humor production falls. Brimonidine is a highly selective alpha-2 adrenergic agonist. Alpha-2 receptors couple to Gi proteins, which inhibit adenylyl cyclase and lower cAMP in the ciliary epithelium, further reducing aqueous secretion. Brimonidine also increases uveoscleral outflow. Two separate ways of reducing production plus an outflow effect give a larger pressure drop than either drug alone.", "body": [["Eye pressure", "Intraocular pressure falls within one to two hours, and the drops are used three times daily to keep it down."], ["Vision", "Brinzolamide is a suspension, so vision is briefly blurred after each drop."], ["Taste and mouth", "Bitter taste comes from brinzolamide draining to the throat, and dry mouth from brimonidine's alpha-2 effect on salivary glands."], ["Brain", "Brimonidine crosses into the brain and can cause drowsiness and fatigue; infants are at risk of severe sedation and breathing problems."], ["Heart and vessels", "Brimonidine can modestly lower blood pressure, but there is no beta blocker effect on heart rate or airways."], ["Conjunctiva", "Allergic reactions with red, itchy eyes can develop over time, mainly from brimonidine."]], "journey": "The suspension is shaken and placed on the eye. Both drugs cross the cornea to the ciliary body. Some drains through the nasolacrimal duct and is absorbed. Brinzolamide binds carbonic anhydrase in red blood cells, giving a very long whole-blood half-life of about 111 days, and is metabolized to N-desethylbrinzolamide, partly by CYP3A4. Brimonidine has a plasma half-life of about three hours and is metabolized in the liver. Both are excreted mainly by the kidneys.", "sidefx": "Blurred vision and bitter taste come from brinzolamide's suspension and drainage to the mouth. As a sulfonamide, brinzolamide can rarely cause serious hypersensitivity reactions such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome. Brimonidine's central alpha-2 action explains sleepiness and fatigue, and in infants can cause apnea and coma, so it is not used in very young children. Allergic conjunctivitis is a delayed immune response. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Simbrinza was built for people whose lungs or hearts cannot tolerate timolol, giving them a two-drug combination without any beta blocker.", "src": ["Simbrinza (brinzolamide and brimonidine) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "netarsudil-latanoprost": {"origin": "Rocklatan pairs a new and an established glaucoma drug. Latanoprost, developed by Johan Stjernschantz and colleagues at Pharmacia in Sweden from Laszlo Bito's discovery that prostaglandins lower eye pressure, was approved as Xalatan in 1996 and became the most widely prescribed glaucoma drop. Netarsudil, a Rho kinase inhibitor designed by Aerie Pharmaceuticals, was approved as Rhopressa in 2017. Because one drug improves drainage through the uveoscleral route and the other through the trabecular meshwork, Aerie combined them in a single once-daily drop. The FDA approved Rocklatan in 2019.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Laszlo Bito shows that prostaglandins can lower intraocular pressure."], ["1996", "FDA approves latanoprost (Xalatan)."], ["2017", "FDA approves netarsudil (Rhopressa)."], ["2019", "FDA approves Rocklatan, netarsudil combined with latanoprost."]], "cell": "Aqueous humor leaves the eye through two routes. The conventional route passes through the trabecular meshwork into Schlemm canal and episcleral veins. Netarsudil inhibits Rho-associated protein kinase (ROCK), which normally keeps myosin light chains phosphorylated and the meshwork cells contracted and stiff. Blocking ROCK relaxes these cells and loosens the meshwork, increasing conventional outflow. It also lowers episcleral venous pressure and inhibits the norepinephrine transporter. The second, uveoscleral route passes through the ciliary muscle. Latanoprost is an isopropyl ester prodrug converted by corneal esterases to latanoprost acid, an agonist at the prostaglandin FP receptor. FP activation induces matrix metalloproteinases that remodel the ciliary muscle matrix and widen this route. Opening both routes gives more pressure lowering than either drug alone.", "body": [["Eye pressure", "Intraocular pressure falls through both drainage routes, with peak effect about 8 to 12 hours after a drop."], ["Conjunctiva", "Eye redness is very common, from vasodilation by both components, and small conjunctival hemorrhages can occur."], ["Cornea", "Netarsudil can form whorl-shaped deposits in the corneal epithelium (verticillata), usually without affecting vision."], ["Iris and lids", "Latanoprost can slowly darken the iris and eyelid skin; iris change may be permanent."], ["Eyelashes", "Lashes may become longer, thicker and darker."], ["Retina", "Prostaglandin signaling can contribute to macular edema in eyes at risk."]], "journey": "The single drop is absorbed through the cornea, where esterases convert netarsudil to its active metabolite AR-13503 and latanoprost to latanoprost acid. Pressure lowering begins in a few hours and lasts about a day. Very little reaches the blood. Latanoprost acid in plasma is cleared in minutes, with a half-life of about 17 minutes, by liver beta-oxidation, and metabolites leave in the urine. Netarsudil and its metabolite are mostly undetectable in plasma.", "sidefx": "Redness and hemorrhage reflect vascular smooth muscle relaxation by netarsudil and vasodilation by the prostaglandin. Corneal verticillata are drug deposits in the epithelium. Iris and lid darkening and lash growth come from FP receptor stimulation of melanocytes and hair follicles. Prostaglandin-driven inflammation can contribute to macular edema, uveitis and reactivation of herpes keratitis in susceptible eyes. Instillation pain is common. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Rocklatan is a two-pathway drop: one ingredient works on the eye's main drain and the other on its backup drain.", "src": ["Rocklatan (netarsudil and latanoprost) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "bimatoprost-implant": {"origin": "Glaucoma drops only work if they are used every day, and many people find that hard. Allergan, which had developed bimatoprost drops (Lumigan, approved 2001), set out to deliver the same drug from a tiny biodegradable implant placed inside the front chamber of the eye. The implant is made of a dissolving polymer that releases bimatoprost slowly over months. The FDA approved Durysta in 2020 as the first intracameral (inside the anterior chamber) implant for lowering eye pressure in open-angle glaucoma or ocular hypertension. Allergan is now part of AbbVie.", "timeline": [["2001", "FDA approves bimatoprost (Lumigan) eye drops."], ["2020", "FDA approves Durysta, the first intracameral implant for glaucoma."]], "cell": "Bimatoprost is a synthetic prostamide related to prostaglandin F2-alpha. It acts on prostaglandin FP receptors and proposed prostamide receptors in the ciliary body and trabecular meshwork. FP receptor activation induces matrix metalloproteinases that remodel the extracellular matrix of the ciliary muscle, opening the uveoscleral (pressure-insensitive) outflow route. It also increases outflow through the trabecular pathway. Because the implant sits in the anterior chamber angle, drug is released right next to the tissues it acts on. That allows a very small total amount of drug, which reduces exposure of the conjunctiva and is linked to less redness than with drops.", "body": [["Eye pressure", "Intraocular pressure falls within days and stays lower for months, with duration varying between people."], ["Corneal endothelium", "The implant can contact or affect the inner layer of cells on the cornea, causing endothelial cell loss and corneal swelling, especially in narrow angles."], ["Conjunctiva", "Redness still occurs in some people but is generally milder than with daily drops."], ["Iris", "Iritis (inflammation in the front of the eye) can occur after placement."], ["Eye after injection", "Foreign body sensation, eye pain, light sensitivity and bleeding are related to the procedure."]], "journey": "An eye specialist injects the implant into the anterior chamber, where it settles in the lower drainage angle. The polymer slowly erodes and releases bimatoprost into the aqueous humor over months. Systemic exposure is minimal. Bimatoprost that does reach the blood has a half-life of about 45 minutes and is hydrolyzed and then oxidized, N-deethylated and glucuronidated before excretion in urine and feces.", "sidefx": "Corneal endothelial injury happens because the implant sits close to the inner corneal surface, and endothelial cells do not regenerate well, so loss can cause lasting corneal swelling. Risk is higher in eyes with narrow angles or prior corneal problems. Iritis and pressure spikes can follow placement. Endophthalmitis is a risk of any injection into the eye. Prostaglandin effects such as redness can occur. There is no antidote; an implant can be removed surgically if needed.", "fun": "Durysta delivers in one tiny implant what would otherwise take months of daily drops, aimed at people who struggle to use drops every day.", "src": ["Durysta (bimatoprost implant) FDA label", "Lumigan (bimatoprost) FDA label"]}, "travoprost-implant": {"origin": "Glaukos, a company known for microscopic stents placed in the eye's drainage system during glaucoma surgery, developed the iDose TR implant. It is a tiny titanium device filled with travoprost that is anchored through the trabecular meshwork into the eye wall. A membrane controls how fast the drug leaves the reservoir, so the eye receives a steady supply of travoprost for many months. The FDA approved iDose TR in December 2023 for lowering eye pressure in open-angle glaucoma and ocular hypertension. Travoprost itself had been approved as Travatan drops in 2001.", "timeline": [["2001", "FDA approves travoprost (Travatan) eye drops."], ["2023", "FDA approves iDose TR, a travoprost intracameral implant."]], "cell": "Travoprost is an isopropyl ester prodrug. As it elutes from the implant into the aqueous humor, esterases in ocular tissue hydrolyze it to travoprost free acid, a selective full agonist at the prostaglandin FP receptor. FP activation in the ciliary muscle increases matrix metalloproteinase activity, which remodels the extracellular matrix and widens the uveoscleral outflow pathway. It also increases trabecular outflow. The titanium implant releases drug continuously at low levels directly in the anterior chamber, so the target tissues see a steady signal while the ocular surface sees much less drug than with drops.", "body": [["Eye pressure", "Intraocular pressure falls within days and stays lower for many months as the reservoir releases drug."], ["Drainage angle", "The implant is anchored through the trabecular meshwork into the sclera and stays in place."], ["Corneal endothelium", "Endothelial cell loss can occur, a concern with devices placed in the anterior chamber."], ["Front of the eye", "Iritis, eye pain, redness and dry eye are reported after placement."], ["Vision", "Reduced visual acuity and visual field changes are listed among reactions in the label."]], "journey": "An eye surgeon places the implant through a small incision. Travoprost diffuses out through a membrane into the aqueous humor, where it is converted to the active free acid and acts on nearby ciliary tissue. Systemic exposure is minimal. Any travoprost acid that reaches the blood has a plasma half-life of about 45 minutes and is cleared by beta-oxidation, with metabolites in the urine. When the drug is used up, the titanium implant can be removed and replaced.", "sidefx": "Corneal endothelial cell loss and device dislocation relate to having a device in the anterior chamber. Iritis and pressure spikes can follow the procedure. Endophthalmitis is a risk of any surgery that opens the eye. Prostaglandin effects such as redness and iris changes are possible but less prominent than with drops because surface exposure is lower. There is no antidote; the implant can be removed surgically.", "fun": "The iDose TR is made of titanium and is smaller than a grain of rice, yet it holds months of glaucoma medicine.", "src": ["iDose TR (travoprost intracameral implant) FDA label", "Travatan Z (travoprost) FDA label"]}, "darifenacin": {"origin": "Darifenacin was discovered by scientists at Pfizer's research center in Sandwich, England, during the 1990s, as part of efforts to make muscarinic blockers that favor the M3 receptor, the subtype that drives bladder contraction. The idea was that sparing M1 receptors in the brain would cause fewer memory and thinking problems. Novartis took over development, and the FDA approved darifenacin extended-release tablets as Enablex in 2004 for overactive bladder. It joined a crowded field of antimuscarinics that started with the much older drug oxybutynin.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Darifenacin is discovered at Pfizer in Sandwich, England."], ["2004", "FDA approves darifenacin extended-release tablets (Enablex) for overactive bladder."]], "cell": "The detrusor muscle of the bladder wall contracts when parasympathetic nerves release acetylcholine onto muscarinic receptors. M3 receptors couple to Gq, activating phospholipase C and raising IP3 and calcium, which triggers contraction. M2 receptors are more numerous and help by reducing cAMP-mediated relaxation. In overactive bladder, unwanted contractions cause urgency and leakage. Darifenacin is a competitive antagonist with relative selectivity for M3 receptors. It blocks acetylcholine at the detrusor, so involuntary contractions are fewer and weaker and the bladder holds more urine. Its lower affinity for M1 receptors, which are important in the brain, is linked to fewer cognitive effects, although M3 blockade in glands and the gut still causes typical anticholinergic effects.", "body": [["Bladder", "Fewer involuntary detrusor contractions mean less urgency, fewer leaks and a larger bladder capacity, building over about two weeks."], ["Mouth", "M3 receptors drive salivary secretion, so dry mouth is the most common side effect."], ["Gut", "Reduced muscarinic tone slows gut movement, causing constipation and indigestion; severe constipation can occur."], ["Eyes", "Blurred vision can occur from blocked muscarinic receptors in the ciliary muscle and iris."], ["Urinary outflow", "In people with bladder outlet obstruction, urinary retention can occur."], ["Skin and temperature", "Reduced sweating can cause heat intolerance."]], "journey": "Darifenacin is taken as an extended-release tablet and absorbed in the gut, with substantial first-pass metabolism by the liver. Peak levels come about seven hours after a dose. It is about 98 percent protein bound. It is metabolized in the liver by CYP2D6 and CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels. The half-life is about 13 to 19 hours. Metabolites leave about 60 percent in urine and 40 percent in feces.", "sidefx": "Side effects reflect muscarinic blockade outside the bladder. M3 blockade in salivary glands causes dry mouth, in gut smooth muscle constipation, and in the eye blurred vision. Reduced detrusor contraction can lead to retention when the outlet is narrowed. Sweat glands use muscarinic receptors, so heat illness is possible. Angioedema is a rare allergic reaction. Physostigmine can reverse severe anticholinergic toxicity, and otherwise care is supportive.", "fun": "Darifenacin was one of the first bladder drugs designed specifically to favor the M3 muscarinic receptor subtype rather than block all five.", "src": ["Enablex (darifenacin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "trospium": {"origin": "Trospium is a quaternary ammonium compound related to atropine-like alkaloids. It was developed in Germany and was used in Europe for years to relax smooth muscle of the bladder and gut before reaching the US. Its permanent positive charge keeps it from crossing into the brain easily, which made it attractive for older adults worried about confusion. The FDA approved trospium tablets as Sanctura in 2004 for overactive bladder, followed by an extended-release capsule. The Sanctura brand has been discontinued, but generics remain available.", "timeline": [["2004", "FDA approves trospium tablets (Sanctura) for overactive bladder."], ["2007", "An extended-release trospium capsule is approved in the US."]], "cell": "Trospium is a competitive antagonist of acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors M1 through M5, without strong subtype selectivity. In the bladder, blocking M3 receptors stops Gq-mediated calcium release that drives detrusor contraction, and blocking M2 receptors removes their inhibition of cAMP-mediated relaxation. Involuntary contractions are fewer, urgency decreases and the bladder holds more. Trospium has a permanent positive charge from its quaternary nitrogen. Charged molecules cross lipid membranes poorly, and trospium is also a substrate of P-glycoprotein at the blood-brain barrier, so very little enters the brain. This is why it causes fewer central effects such as confusion than tertiary amines like oxybutynin.", "body": [["Bladder", "Detrusor overactivity decreases, with fewer urge episodes and leaks, often within about a week."], ["Mouth", "Saliva production drops because salivary muscarinic receptors are blocked, causing dry mouth."], ["Gut", "Constipation, indigestion and abdominal pain come from reduced gut smooth muscle activity."], ["Brain", "Because it barely crosses the blood-brain barrier, confusion and memory effects are uncommon compared with other antimuscarinics."], ["Heart", "Blocking cardiac M2 receptors can speed the heart rate."], ["Eyes and skin", "Blurred vision and reduced sweating with heat intolerance can occur."]], "journey": "Trospium is poorly absorbed from the gut, with bioavailability under ten percent, and food lowers absorption further, so it is taken on an empty stomach. Peak levels come in about five to six hours. Little is metabolized by CYP enzymes; some is hydrolyzed and glucuronidated. It is cleared mainly unchanged by the kidneys through active tubular secretion, so kidney impairment raises levels. The half-life is about 20 hours. Unabsorbed drug leaves in feces.", "sidefx": "Dry mouth, constipation, blurred vision and urinary retention all come from muscarinic blockade outside the bladder. Tachycardia reflects cardiac M2 blockade. Heat intolerance follows reduced sweating. Because it depends on renal tubular secretion, it can interact with other drugs secreted by the same transporters and builds up in kidney disease. Angioedema is a rare serious reaction. Severe anticholinergic toxicity is managed supportively, and physostigmine may be used for peripheral and central effects.", "fun": "Trospium's built-in positive charge works like a passport the brain will not stamp, keeping most of the drug out of the central nervous system.", "src": ["Trospium chloride FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "pentosan-polysulfate": {"origin": "Pentosan polysulfate is made from xylan, a sugar chain extracted from beechwood, which is chemically sulfated to resemble heparin and the natural glycosaminoglycans that coat the bladder lining. It was first developed in Germany by Bene-Arzneimittel and used in Europe as an anticoagulant and anti-inflammatory agent. In the 1980s, urologist C. Lowell Parsons at the University of California, San Diego proposed that interstitial cystitis is linked to a leaky glycosaminoglycan layer on the bladder surface and studied pentosan to repair it. The FDA approved Elmiron in 1996 as the only oral drug for bladder pain and discomfort of interstitial cystitis. In 2018 researchers at Emory University described a distinctive pigmentary maculopathy in long-term users, and the label was updated with this warning in 2020.", "timeline": [["1980s", "C. Lowell Parsons studies pentosan for interstitial cystitis."], ["1996", "FDA approves Elmiron for bladder pain of interstitial cystitis."], ["2018", "Emory researchers describe pigmentary maculopathy with long-term pentosan use."], ["2020", "Elmiron labeling adds a warning about retinal pigmentary changes."]], "cell": "The bladder urothelium is covered by a glycosaminoglycan (GAG) layer that keeps urine components such as potassium from leaking into the bladder wall. In interstitial cystitis this layer is thought to be defective, so solutes irritate nerves and mast cells and cause pain. Pentosan polysulfate is a low-molecular-weight sulfated polysaccharide that resembles GAGs. A small fraction of each oral dose is absorbed and excreted in the urine, where it is thought to adhere to the urothelium and patch the deficient GAG layer, limiting permeation of irritating solutes. It may also reduce mast cell activation. Because it is heparin-like, it has weak anticoagulant activity, about one-fifteenth that of heparin, and some fibrinolytic activity.", "body": [["Bladder", "Bladder pain and discomfort may lessen, but relief usually takes three to six months to appear."], ["Blood", "Weak anticoagulant activity can cause easy bruising or rectal and gum bleeding, especially with other blood thinners."], ["Retina", "Long-term use can cause pigmentary maculopathy, with trouble reading, adapting to dim light or seeing details, which may not fully reverse."], ["Hair", "Hair thinning or loss (alopecia) is a common reported side effect."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea, indigestion and abdominal pain occur in some people."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise, and the drug is partly processed by the liver."]], "journey": "Pentosan is taken by mouth, but only a few percent of each dose is absorbed. Absorbed drug peaks in about two hours and is bound in tissues including the urothelium, liver and spleen. It is desulfated in the liver and spleen and broken into smaller pieces in the kidney. A small fraction appears in urine, which is the portion thought to act on the bladder. Most of the dose passes through and leaves unabsorbed in the feces.", "sidefx": "Bleeding risk comes from its heparin-like anticoagulant and fibrinolytic effects, which add to warfarin, heparin, aspirin and NSAIDs. The cause of pigmentary maculopathy is not fully understood but is thought to involve direct toxicity to the retinal pigment epithelium, with risk rising with cumulative exposure; baseline and periodic retinal exams are recommended in the label. Hair loss and gut upset are common. There is no specific antidote, and bleeding is managed by stopping the drug and supportive care.", "fun": "This bladder drug starts out as a sugar chain from beech trees, which chemists then sulfate to make it look like the bladder's own protective coating.", "src": ["Elmiron (pentosan polysulfate sodium) FDA label", "Pearce WA et al. Ophthalmology 2018", "Parsons CL, Mulholland SG. J Urol 1987"]}, "vardenafil": {"origin": "Vardenafil followed the surprising story of sildenafil. In the early 1990s Pfizer tested sildenafil for angina, and volunteers reported erections as a side effect, which led to Viagra in 1998. The science behind it, nitric oxide signaling through cGMP, earned Robert Furchgott, Louis Ignarro and Ferid Murad the 1998 Nobel Prize. Bayer chemists in Germany then designed vardenafil, a PDE5 inhibitor with a structure close to sildenafil but with a different ring system that increased its potency. Bayer and GlaxoSmithKline brought it to market, and the FDA approved it as Levitra in 2003. An orally disintegrating version, Staxyn, followed in 2010. The Levitra brand has since been discontinued in the US, while generics remain.", "timeline": [["1998", "Sildenafil (Viagra) becomes the first approved PDE5 inhibitor; nitric oxide signaling wins the Nobel Prize."], ["2003", "FDA approves vardenafil (Levitra) for erectile dysfunction."], ["2010", "Vardenafil orally disintegrating tablet (Staxyn) is approved."]], "cell": "During sexual stimulation, nerves and endothelial cells in the corpus cavernosum release nitric oxide (NO). NO activates soluble guanylate cyclase in smooth muscle cells, raising cyclic GMP. cGMP activates protein kinase G, which lowers intracellular calcium and relaxes the smooth muscle of the cavernosal arteries and sinusoids. Blood flows in and the erection forms as outflow veins are compressed. Phosphodiesterase type 5 (PDE5) breaks down cGMP and ends the signal. Vardenafil is a potent selective PDE5 inhibitor, so cGMP stays higher for longer, but only when NO is being released, which is why stimulation is still needed. Vardenafil also weakly inhibits PDE6 in retinal rods and cones, which explains color vision changes.", "body": [["Penis", "Cavernosal smooth muscle relaxes more fully with sexual stimulation, allowing a firmer and longer erection."], ["Blood vessels", "Mild vasodilation elsewhere causes small drops in blood pressure, flushing and headache."], ["Nose", "Dilated nasal vessels cause stuffiness or runny nose."], ["Eyes", "Weak PDE6 inhibition in the retina can cause a blue tinge or light sensitivity; sudden vision loss from NAION is a rare warning."], ["Heart", "A small QT prolongation effect has been seen, so it is used with caution in people with long QT or certain antiarrhythmics."], ["Gut and back", "Indigestion and back pain are reported, possibly related to smooth muscle effects."]], "journey": "Vardenafil is absorbed quickly after swallowing, with onset in about 25 to 60 minutes; a high-fat meal can delay absorption. Bioavailability is modest due to first-pass metabolism. It is about 95 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP3A4, with minor roles for CYP3A5 and CYP2C9, forming an active desethyl metabolite. The half-life is about four to five hours. Most is excreted in the feces, with a small amount in urine. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels markedly.", "sidefx": "Headache, flushing and nasal congestion come from vasodilation by cGMP in vessels outside the penis. With nitrates, both drugs push cGMP higher through different steps, which can cause profound, dangerous hypotension, so the combination is contraindicated. Alpha blockers can add to blood pressure drops. PDE6 inhibition explains color vision changes. Priapism is a rare effect of excessive cavernosal relaxation and is an emergency. There is no antidote; hypotension is treated supportively.", "fun": "Vardenafil and sildenafil look almost the same on paper. The key difference is a swapped ring system in the core of the molecule.", "src": ["Levitra (vardenafil) FDA label", "Staxyn (vardenafil ODT) FDA label", "Nobel Prize 1998 Physiology or Medicine"]}, "avanafil": {"origin": "Avanafil was discovered in Japan by Tanabe Seiyaku, now Mitsubishi Tanabe Pharma. The goal was a PDE5 inhibitor that works faster than earlier drugs and is more selective for PDE5 over other phosphodiesterases such as PDE6 in the retina and PDE11. The US company Vivus licensed and developed it, and the FDA approved it as Stendra in 2012. In 2014 labeling was updated to say it could be taken as little as about 15 minutes before sexual activity, the fastest labeled onset in its class.", "timeline": [["1998", "Sildenafil becomes the first approved PDE5 inhibitor."], ["2012", "FDA approves avanafil (Stendra) for erectile dysfunction."], ["2014", "Stendra labeling is updated to reflect onset in about 15 minutes."]], "cell": "Nitric oxide released by nerves and endothelium during sexual stimulation activates soluble guanylate cyclase in cavernosal smooth muscle, raising cGMP. cGMP activates protein kinase G, lowering intracellular calcium and relaxing the smooth muscle so blood can fill the erectile tissue. PDE5 breaks cGMP down and ends the signal. Avanafil competitively inhibits PDE5, so cGMP signaling is prolonged, but only when nitric oxide is being released. It is highly selective for PDE5 over PDE6, found in retinal photoreceptors, and PDE11, found in skeletal muscle and testis. Higher selectivity is linked to fewer color vision changes and less muscle pain than with some older drugs.", "body": [["Penis", "Erections form more easily with sexual stimulation, with effect beginning in about 15 minutes in some people."], ["Blood vessels", "Systemic vasodilation causes headache, flushing and small drops in blood pressure."], ["Nose and throat", "Nasal congestion and nasopharyngitis are reported from vessel dilation in the nasal lining."], ["Eyes", "Visual disturbances are less common due to PDE6 selectivity, but NAION is a rare warning for the class."], ["Back and muscles", "Back pain is reported in a small share of people in clinical trials of the drug."], ["Heart", "Blood pressure drops matter in people with heart disease or on nitrates or alpha blockers."]], "journey": "Avanafil is absorbed quickly from the gut, with peak levels in about 30 to 45 minutes, which explains its fast onset. A high-fat meal slows absorption. It is about 99 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP3A4, with a minor role for CYP2C9, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors are avoided. The half-life is about five hours. Metabolites are excreted in feces (about 62 percent) and urine (about 21 percent).", "sidefx": "Headache, flushing and nasal congestion come from cGMP-driven vasodilation outside the penis. Combined with nitrates, which raise cGMP by another route, avanafil can cause severe hypotension, so the combination is contraindicated. Alpha blockers and alcohol can add to blood pressure drops. Priapism is a rare emergency from excessive cavernosal relaxation. Sudden hearing loss and NAION are rare class warnings. There is no antidote; hypotension is treated supportively.", "fun": "Avanafil has the fastest labeled onset of the US PDE5 inhibitors, at about 15 minutes in some people.", "src": ["Stendra (avanafil) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "testosterone-undecanoate": {"origin": "Testosterone was first isolated in 1935 by Ernst Laqueur's group in Amsterdam from bull testes, and the same year Adolf Butenandt and Leopold Ruzicka each reported ways to make it from cholesterol, work that helped earn them the 1939 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. Plain testosterone works poorly as a pill because the liver destroys most of it on the first pass. Chemists solved this by attaching fatty acid chains (esters). Testosterone undecanoate carries a long 11-carbon chain. The Dutch company Organon launched an oral form (Andriol) outside the US in the 1970s. In the US, the long-acting injection Aveed was approved in 2014, and oral capsules followed: Jatenzo in 2019, then Tlando and Kyzatrex in 2022. These gave men with low testosterone a pill option that does not carry the liver toxicity of the older 17-alpha-methylated oral androgens.", "timeline": [["1935", "Laqueur's group isolates testosterone; Butenandt and Ruzicka report its synthesis."], ["1970s", "Organon introduces oral testosterone undecanoate (Andriol) outside the US."], ["2014", "FDA approves Aveed, the long-acting intramuscular injection."], ["2019", "FDA approves Jatenzo, the first oral testosterone undecanoate capsule in the US."], ["2022", "Tlando and Kyzatrex oral capsules are approved."]], "cell": "Testosterone undecanoate is a prodrug. Esterases cut off the undecanoate chain and release free testosterone. Testosterone crosses the cell membrane and binds the androgen receptor in the cytoplasm. The receptor sheds its heat shock proteins, pairs up (dimerizes), moves into the nucleus and binds androgen response elements on DNA, turning on genes for muscle protein, red cell production and male sex traits. In tissues such as prostate, skin and hair follicles, 5-alpha reductase converts testosterone to dihydrotestosterone (DHT), which binds the same receptor more tightly. Aromatase converts some testosterone to estradiol, which acts on estrogen receptors in bone and brain. Testosterone also feeds back on the hypothalamus and pituitary, lowering GnRH, LH and FSH.", "body": [["Muscle and bone", "Androgen receptor activation increases muscle protein synthesis and lean mass. Estradiol made from testosterone helps keep bone dense."], ["Brain and libido", "Androgen and estrogen signaling in the brain restore sex drive, energy and mood in men with low levels."], ["Blood", "Testosterone stimulates erythropoietin release and iron use, raising hematocrit, which can become too high (polycythemia)."], ["Heart and vessels", "Blood pressure can rise, which is why the oral capsules carry a boxed warning about blood pressure increases."], ["Testes", "Feedback suppression of LH and FSH shrinks the testes and lowers sperm production, so therapy can cause infertility."], ["Skin and prostate", "DHT drives oil glands and prostate growth, causing acne and possible worsening of BPH symptoms."]], "journey": "The oral capsules work only with food that contains fat. The very fatty ester dissolves in dietary fat and is taken up into intestinal lymph vessels with chylomicrons, entering the blood through the thoracic duct and skipping the liver's first pass. Esterases then free testosterone, which peaks a few hours after a dose and is gone within hours, so capsules are taken twice a day. The Aveed injection forms an oily depot in muscle that releases the ester slowly over weeks. Testosterone is tightly bound to SHBG and albumin, is metabolized in the liver to DHT, estradiol and inactive conjugates, and leaves mainly in urine.", "sidefx": "Most effects come from too much androgen action. Extra red cell production raises hematocrit and clot risk. DHT causes acne and prostate growth. Aromatization to estradiol can cause breast enlargement. Feedback suppression of LH and FSH lowers sperm counts. Fluid retention and blood pressure rises add to cardiovascular concerns, and sleep apnea can worsen. The Aveed injection carries a boxed warning for pulmonary oil microembolism (POME), when tiny droplets of the oily depot reach the lungs and cause cough and shortness of breath, and for anaphylaxis, so patients are watched after each injection. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped.", "fun": "Because the oral form rides into the body with dietary fat through the lymph system, a fat-free meal can leave blood testosterone far lower than expected.", "src": ["Jatenzo (testosterone undecanoate) FDA label", "Aveed (testosterone undecanoate) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "flavoxate": {"origin": "Flavoxate is a synthetic molecule built on a flavone ring, the same basic chemical skeleton found in many plant pigments. It was developed in the 1960s by the Italian drug company Recordati as a smooth muscle relaxant for the urinary tract. It was later sold in the US under the brand Urispas for the burning, urgency and frequency that come with bladder and urethral irritation. The brand has since been discontinued, but generic tablets remain. It filled a niche as a symptom reliever used alongside antibiotics or other treatment of the cause.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Recordati in Italy develops flavoxate as a urinary smooth muscle relaxant."]], "cell": "Flavoxate relaxes the smooth muscle of the bladder and urethra mainly by a direct action on the muscle cells rather than through a single receptor. Proposed parts of this action include blocking calcium entry into smooth muscle cells (weak calcium channel antagonism) and inhibiting phosphodiesterase, both of which reduce the calcium available to the contractile machinery. It also has weak antimuscarinic activity, blocking acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors on the detrusor, and a local anesthetic effect that may quiet irritated sensory nerves in the bladder lining. The net result is less spasm and less urge, while the infection or inflammation that caused the irritation is not treated.", "body": [["Bladder and urethra", "Relaxed smooth muscle and calmer sensory nerves ease urgency, frequency, suprapubic pain and painful urination."], ["Mouth and eyes", "Weak antimuscarinic action reduces saliva and can blur vision by relaxing the focusing muscle of the eye."], ["Brain", "Drowsiness, nervousness and confusion can occur, especially in older adults."], ["Heart", "Anticholinergic effects can speed the heart and cause palpitations."], ["Blood", "Rare cases of low white cell counts (leukopenia) have been reported."]], "journey": "Flavoxate is taken by mouth and absorbed from the gut, with effects starting in about an hour. The liver converts much of it to an active metabolite, 3-methylflavone-8-carboxylic acid. The kidneys then excrete this metabolite, with more than half of a dose appearing in the urine within a day. Because the effect lasts only several hours, it is taken several times a day.", "sidefx": "The side effects follow from its weak antimuscarinic action: dry mouth, blurred vision, fast heart rate and, in people with a narrowed bladder outlet, urinary retention. Blocking muscarinic receptors in the brain explains drowsiness and confusion in older adults, and the reduced ability to sweat raises the risk of heat illness, especially when combined with other anticholinergic drugs. It is avoided in bowel or bladder outlet obstruction and narrow-angle glaucoma. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Flavoxate's flavone backbone links it chemically to the yellow pigments that color many flowers and fruits, which is where the name flavone comes from (Latin flavus, yellow).", "src": ["Flavoxate hydrochloride tablets FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "dutasteride-tamsulosin": {"origin": "This capsule joins two drugs that attack an enlarged prostate from two sides. The idea for 5-alpha reductase inhibitors came from a 1974 report by Julianne Imperato-McGinley on a group of people in the Dominican Republic born with an inherited lack of type 2 5-alpha reductase. They had small prostates that never enlarged, showing that DHT drives prostate growth. GlaxoSmithKline then developed dutasteride, which blocks both type 1 and type 2 enzymes, and FDA approved it as Avodart in 2001. Tamsulosin came from the Japanese company Yamanouchi and was approved in the US as Flomax in 1997 as a uroselective alpha-1 blocker. The CombAT trial showed the two together worked better than either alone, and FDA approved the combination capsule Jalyn in 2010.", "timeline": [["1974", "Imperato-McGinley describes 5-alpha reductase deficiency, linking DHT to prostate growth."], ["1997", "FDA approves tamsulosin (Flomax)."], ["2001", "FDA approves dutasteride (Avodart)."], ["2010", "FDA approves the combination capsule Jalyn."]], "cell": "Dutasteride is a 4-azasteroid that looks like testosterone. It binds and inhibits both type 1 and type 2 5-alpha reductase, the enzymes that turn testosterone into dihydrotestosterone (DHT). Serum DHT falls by about 90 percent. With less DHT acting on androgen receptors in prostate cells, growth signals fall, glandular tissue shrinks and PSA drops. Tamsulosin is a competitive antagonist at alpha-1A (and alpha-1D) adrenergic receptors. These Gq-coupled receptors normally raise intracellular calcium in the smooth muscle of the prostate stroma, capsule and bladder neck when norepinephrine binds. Blocking them relaxes that muscle and opens the urethra. The two parts therefore treat the static component (tissue size) and the dynamic component (muscle tone) of obstruction.", "body": [["Prostate", "Less DHT shrinks the gland by about a quarter over months, lowering the risk of urinary retention and surgery."], ["Bladder neck and urethra", "Alpha-1A blockade relaxes smooth muscle within days, improving urine flow and reducing straining."], ["Blood vessels", "Some alpha-1 blockade in vessels can cause dizziness and a drop in blood pressure on standing."], ["Sexual function", "Lower DHT reduces libido and erections, and tamsulosin can reduce or stop ejaculation."], ["Breast tissue", "Shifting the androgen to estrogen balance can cause breast enlargement or tenderness."], ["PSA blood test", "Dutasteride roughly halves PSA, so the value must be interpreted with this in mind."], ["Eyes", "Alpha-1A blockade in the iris dilator muscle can cause floppy iris syndrome during cataract surgery."]], "journey": "Both drugs are taken by mouth in one capsule, usually after the same meal each day. Tamsulosin is absorbed over several hours and cleared by CYP3A4 and CYP2D6 with a half-life of about half a day; its benefit appears within days. Dutasteride is very lipophilic, highly protein bound and broken down slowly by CYP3A4, with a half-life of about five weeks. Because of this it takes months for prostate shrinkage and can stay in the body for months after stopping. Dutasteride metabolites leave mainly in feces.", "sidefx": "Dutasteride side effects come from lowering DHT: less libido, erectile dysfunction and breast changes. The 5-ARI class carries a warning about a higher rate of high-grade prostate cancer in trials. Because it can be absorbed through the skin and could affect a male fetus, women who are or could become pregnant should not handle broken capsules. Tamsulosin's alpha blockade causes dizziness, orthostatic hypotension and fainting, especially with the first doses, plus ejaculation problems and floppy iris syndrome during eye surgery. Priapism is a rare effect. There is no antidote; hypotension is treated with fluids and lying flat.", "fun": "Dutasteride's half-life of about five weeks is one of the longest of any common pill, which is why people who take it are asked not to donate blood for months after their last dose.", "src": ["Jalyn (dutasteride and tamsulosin) FDA label", "Imperato-McGinley J et al. Science 1974", "Roehrborn CG et al. CombAT trial"]}, "finasteride-tadalafil": {"origin": "Finasteride was developed by Merck after the discovery that people born without type 2 5-alpha reductase have small prostates. Merck chemists designed a 4-azasteroid that blocks the enzyme, and FDA approved it as Proscar for enlarged prostate in 1992, then as Propecia for hair loss in 1997. Tadalafil started as a compound called IC351 from a partnership between ICOS and Glaxo, later developed by Lilly ICOS. It was approved for erectile dysfunction as Cialis in 2003 and for BPH symptoms in 2011. Veru combined the two in one capsule, Entadfi, which FDA approved in December 2021 for starting BPH treatment for up to 26 weeks, covering the time before finasteride alone takes full effect.", "timeline": [["1992", "FDA approves finasteride (Proscar) for BPH."], ["2003", "FDA approves tadalafil (Cialis) for erectile dysfunction."], ["2011", "Tadalafil gains approval for BPH symptoms."], ["2021", "FDA approves the finasteride-tadalafil capsule Entadfi."]], "cell": "Finasteride is a competitive, mechanism-based inhibitor of type 2 5-alpha reductase, the main form in the prostate. It blocks conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone (DHT). Less DHT on prostate androgen receptors means less growth signaling, so the gland slowly shrinks and PSA falls. Tadalafil inhibits phosphodiesterase type 5 (PDE5), the enzyme that breaks down cyclic GMP. Nitric oxide from nerves and endothelium activates soluble guanylate cyclase to make cGMP. With PDE5 blocked, cGMP builds up, protein kinase G is activated, intracellular calcium falls and smooth muscle relaxes in the prostate, bladder neck and blood vessels. Better blood flow to the pelvic organs and less sensory nerve activity may also help urinary symptoms.", "body": [["Prostate", "Less DHT shrinks the gland over months, while PDE5 inhibition relaxes its smooth muscle within weeks."], ["Bladder", "Relaxed bladder neck and less afferent nerve signaling reduce urgency, frequency and weak stream."], ["Blood vessels", "cGMP-driven vasodilation causes headache, flushing and a mild blood pressure drop, which becomes dangerous with nitrates."], ["Sexual function", "Tadalafil supports erections, while finasteride can lower libido and cause ejaculation problems."], ["Muscles", "PDE11 inhibition in skeletal muscle is a proposed reason for the back pain and muscle aches seen with tadalafil."], ["Eyes and ears", "Rare cases of sudden vision loss (NAION) and sudden hearing loss have been reported with PDE5 inhibitors."]], "journey": "Both drugs are taken by mouth in one capsule. Tadalafil is absorbed in about two hours, is broken down by CYP3A4 and has a long half-life of about 17 hours, which allows once-daily use. Finasteride is also absorbed within a couple of hours, metabolized by CYP3A4 and has a half-life of about 5 to 6 hours, though its effect on DHT lasts much longer because it binds the enzyme very tightly. Metabolites of both leave mainly in feces. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise tadalafil levels.", "sidefx": "Tadalafil's side effects come from vasodilation and PDE inhibition elsewhere: headache, indigestion from relaxed esophageal sphincter, back pain and muscle aches. Combining it with nitrates or riociguat stacks two cGMP-raising effects and can drop blood pressure severely, so these combinations are contraindicated. Prolonged erections (priapism) are rare. Finasteride's effects come from low DHT: reduced libido, erectile and ejaculation problems, breast tenderness and, in the class, a higher rate of high-grade prostate cancer in trials. Women who may be pregnant should not handle crushed or broken finasteride because it can affect a male fetus. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Tadalafil's long half-life earned Cialis the nickname 'the weekend pill' when it launched.", "src": ["Entadfi (finasteride and tadalafil) FDA label", "Proscar FDA label", "Cialis FDA label"]}, "oxybutynin-transdermal": {"origin": "Oxybutynin is a synthetic antimuscarinic first developed in the 1960s, and oral tablets were approved in the US as Ditropan in 1975. Oral oxybutynin works well for overactive bladder, but many people stop it because of severe dry mouth. Researchers traced much of this to N-desethyloxybutynin, a metabolite made when the gut wall and liver process the swallowed drug. Delivering the drug through the skin avoids that first pass. Watson Pharmaceuticals developed the Oxytrol patch, approved by FDA in 2003. In 2013 Oxytrol for Women became the first overactive bladder treatment sold over the counter in the US.", "timeline": [["1975", "FDA approves oral oxybutynin (Ditropan)."], ["2003", "FDA approves the Oxytrol transdermal patch."], ["2013", "Oxytrol for Women becomes the first OTC overactive bladder product in the US."]], "cell": "Oxybutynin is a tertiary amine that competitively blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors. In the bladder, parasympathetic nerves release acetylcholine onto M3 receptors on detrusor smooth muscle. M3 is Gq-coupled, so binding normally activates phospholipase C, makes IP3 and releases calcium for contraction. Blocking M3 stops these involuntary contractions, so the bladder can fill to a larger volume before urgency strikes. Oxybutynin also blocks M1 receptors and has a weak direct relaxing effect on smooth muscle. Because it is a tertiary amine, it can cross into the brain, which explains its central side effects in older adults.", "body": [["Bladder", "Fewer involuntary detrusor contractions increase capacity and reduce urgency, frequency and leakage."], ["Mouth", "Salivary glands use M3 receptors, so saliva falls, though less with the patch than with tablets."], ["Gut", "Slower gut movement from muscarinic blockade causes constipation."], ["Eyes", "Blocking the ciliary muscle and pupil sphincter can blur near vision."], ["Brain", "Central M1 blockade can cause drowsiness, memory problems and confusion, especially in older adults."], ["Skin", "Itching and redness at the patch site are the most common local effects."]], "journey": "The patch is applied to the abdomen, hip or buttock and releases oxybutynin steadily through the skin into the blood for three to four days. Blood levels rise over the first one to two days and reach steady state after the second patch. Because the drug enters the blood directly rather than through the gut and liver, much less N-desethyloxybutynin is made than with tablets. Oxybutynin that does reach the liver is metabolized by CYP3A4, and metabolites leave in the urine. After the patch is removed levels fall with a half-life of several hours.", "sidefx": "The side effects are classic antimuscarinic effects: dry mouth, constipation, blurred vision and urinary retention in people with a weak bladder or outlet obstruction. Less of the metabolite means less dry mouth than with tablets. Brain muscarinic blockade causes sleepiness and confusion and is a reason anticholinergics are listed as risky for older adults. Reduced sweating can lead to heat illness. The adhesive and drug can irritate the skin, so sites are rotated. Physostigmine can reverse severe central anticholinergic toxicity, though this is rarely needed with a patch.", "fun": "Oxytrol for Women was the first prescription overactive bladder drug to switch to over-the-counter sale in the US, sold only to women because men's urgency symptoms may come from prostate disease that needs a diagnosis.", "src": ["Oxytrol (oxybutynin transdermal system) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "acetohydroxamic-acid": {"origin": "Acetohydroxamic acid targets urease, an enzyme with a famous history. In 1926 James B. Sumner crystallized urease from jack beans, the first enzyme ever crystallized, proving that enzymes are proteins and earning a share of the 1946 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. Later, doctors learned that bacteria such as Proteus use their own urease to split urea in urine, making it alkaline and building large branching struvite (infection) stones. Hydroxamic acids, which resemble urea, were found to block urease. Acetohydroxamic acid was developed as Lithostat by Mission Pharmacal and approved by FDA in 1983 as an add-on to antibiotics and surgery for chronic urease-producing urinary infections.", "timeline": [["1926", "James B. Sumner crystallizes jack bean urease, the first crystallized enzyme."], ["1946", "Sumner shares the Nobel Prize in Chemistry."], ["1983", "FDA approves acetohydroxamic acid (Lithostat)."]], "cell": "Urease is a nickel-containing enzyme made by bacteria such as Proteus, Klebsiella and some Staphylococcus species. It splits urea into ammonia and carbon dioxide. Ammonia raises urine pH, and in alkaline urine magnesium, ammonium and phosphate precipitate as struvite, along with calcium phosphate (carbonate apatite). Acetohydroxamic acid looks like urea and binds the active site, where its hydroxamate group chelates the two nickel ions. This blocks the enzyme. Less ammonia means lower urine pH and less supersaturation, so stones grow more slowly and new ones are less likely. It does not kill bacteria, but lower ammonia and pH can help antibiotics work.", "body": [["Urinary tract", "Lower urinary ammonia and pH slow the growth of struvite stones and reduce new stone formation."], ["Blood", "It can cause a hemolytic anemia that is usually mild but can be serious, so blood counts are watched."], ["Veins", "Phlebitis and deep vein thrombosis have been reported, including pulmonary embolism."], ["Brain and mood", "Headache is common, and anxiety, depression and tremor can occur."], ["Gut and skin", "Nausea, loss of appetite and hair loss occur in some patients."]], "journey": "Acetohydroxamic acid is taken by mouth and is well absorbed, peaking within an hour. Part of it is broken down to acetamide. A large fraction is excreted unchanged by the kidneys, which concentrates it in the urine where the target bacteria live. The half-life is several hours with normal kidney function but longer when kidneys are impaired, and it is less effective and more toxic in poor kidney function.", "sidefx": "Hydroxamic acids chelate metals and interact with other enzymes, which may explain their effects outside bacteria. Hemolytic anemia, sometimes with reticulocytosis, is a key toxicity and is more likely in kidney disease. Clotting problems such as phlebitis and deep vein thrombosis have been reported. Headache, anxiety and nausea are common. The drug is teratogenic in animals and is contraindicated in pregnancy. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Urease from jack beans was the first enzyme ever crystallized, and the same enzyme family in bacteria is what this drug was designed to block.", "src": ["Lithostat (acetohydroxamic acid) FDA label", "Sumner JB. J Biol Chem 1926"]}, "sodium-ferric-gluconate": {"origin": "Intravenous iron began with iron dextran, which works but can cause dangerous allergic reactions, partly due to antibodies against the dextran sugar. Sodium ferric gluconate complex in sucrose, a dextran-free iron-sugar complex, was used in Europe for years before it reached the US. FDA approved it as Ferrlecit in 1999 for iron deficiency in people on hemodialysis receiving epoetin. It mattered because people on dialysis lose iron steadily and often cannot absorb enough from pills, and ESA therapy works only when enough iron is available.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Iron dextran introduces intravenous iron but carries anaphylaxis risk."], ["1999", "FDA approves sodium ferric gluconate complex (Ferrlecit) for dialysis patients."]], "cell": "Each particle has a core of ferric oxyhydroxide wrapped in a shell of gluconate and sucrose that keeps the iron from being released as free, reactive iron. After infusion, macrophages of the reticuloendothelial system in the liver, spleen and bone marrow take up the complex. Inside these cells the iron is freed from the shell and either stored as ferritin or exported through ferroportin to transferrin in the blood. Transferrin carries the iron to developing red cells in the marrow, which take it in through transferrin receptor 1. There it is inserted into protoporphyrin IX to make heme for hemoglobin.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "More iron available to red cell precursors means more hemoglobin, especially when ESA therapy is driving production."], ["Iron stores", "Ferritin and transferrin saturation rise as macrophages and the liver store the new iron."], ["Blood vessels", "Infusion can cause flushing, low blood pressure and chest discomfort from free iron and infusion reactions."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and cramps can occur during or after infusion."]], "journey": "It is given intravenously, often during dialysis. The complex is cleared from plasma within hours as macrophages take it up. Iron is then released gradually to transferrin and incorporated into red cells over days to weeks. Very little iron is lost in urine, and the body has no active way to excrete iron, so the iron stays in body stores. This is why iron levels are checked to avoid overload.", "sidefx": "Hypotension and flushing are thought to come from labile iron released too quickly and from infusion reactions, which is why the infusion is given slowly. Hypersensitivity reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, can occur, though less often than with high molecular weight iron dextran. Repeated dosing without monitoring can cause iron overload, since the body cannot excrete excess iron. Serious reactions are treated with standard anaphylaxis care; deferoxamine chelation is used only for true iron overload.", "fun": "The human body has no regulated way to excrete iron; it controls iron only at the point of absorption, which is why intravenous iron goes straight into storage.", "src": ["Ferrlecit (sodium ferric gluconate complex in sucrose) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "methoxy-polyethylene-glycol-epoetin-beta": {"origin": "In 1977 Eugene Goldwasser at the University of Chicago purified human erythropoietin (EPO) from the urine of people with aplastic anemia. In the early 1980s Fu-Kuei Lin at Amgen cloned the EPO gene, leading to epoetin alfa in 1989. Epoetin beta, a similar recombinant EPO, was developed in Europe. Roche then attached a large methoxy polyethylene glycol chain to epoetin beta, creating a continuous erythropoietin receptor activator (CERA) that lasts weeks. FDA approved it as Mircera in 2007 for anemia of chronic kidney disease, but patent litigation with Amgen kept it off the US market until 2014. It later gained pediatric approval.", "timeline": [["1977", "Eugene Goldwasser purifies human erythropoietin from urine."], ["1989", "FDA approves the first recombinant EPO, epoetin alfa."], ["2007", "FDA approves Mircera."], ["2014", "Mircera launches in the US after patent litigation ends."]], "cell": "Mircera binds the erythropoietin receptor (EpoR) on erythroid progenitor cells (CFU-E and proerythroblasts) in the bone marrow. EpoR is a cytokine receptor without its own enzyme activity. Binding brings two receptor chains together and activates JAK2 kinases attached to them. JAK2 phosphorylates the receptor and STAT5, which moves into the nucleus and turns on survival genes such as Bcl-xL. The main effect is to stop programmed cell death of red cell precursors, so more of them mature into reticulocytes and red cells. Compared with epoetin, it binds the receptor with lower affinity and lets go faster, but the huge PEG chain slows its clearance, so it stimulates the receptor steadily for weeks.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "More red cell precursors survive and mature, raising reticulocytes and then hemoglobin over weeks."], ["Blood", "Higher hemoglobin reduces fatigue and the need for transfusions, but thicker blood raises clot risk."], ["Blood vessels", "Blood pressure often rises, linked to increased red cell mass and vascular effects."], ["Heart and brain", "Aiming for high hemoglobin targets increases heart attack, stroke and death, so the lowest effective target is used."], ["Dialysis access", "Higher red cell mass increases clotting of dialysis access grafts."]], "journey": "It is given by IV or subcutaneous injection, usually once or twice a month. The large pegylated molecule stays mostly in the blood and is cleared very slowly, with a half-life of more than five days by either route, much longer than epoetin. It is not broken down by liver enzymes like a small molecule; it is removed by receptor uptake and protein breakdown. Reticulocytes rise within days and hemoglobin climbs over weeks.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow from making more red cells. Rising red cell mass and blood viscosity raise blood pressure and clot risk, and high hemoglobin targets increase heart attack, stroke and death, which is the subject of the boxed warning. Seizures can occur when blood pressure rises quickly. Rarely, antibodies against EPO cause pure red cell aplasia, in which the marrow stops making red cells. In cancer patients, ESAs carry a warning about tumor progression; Mircera is not indicated for anemia from chemotherapy. There is no antidote; the dose is held.", "fun": "Mircera was approved by FDA in 2007 but could not be sold in the US for seven years because a court found it infringed Amgen's EPO patents.", "src": ["Mircera (methoxy polyethylene glycol-epoetin beta) FDA label", "Miyake T, Kung CK, Goldwasser E. J Biol Chem 1977"]}, "daprodustat": {"origin": "Daprodustat grew out of Nobel-winning science on how cells sense oxygen. In the 1990s Gregg Semenza identified hypoxia-inducible factor (HIF), the switch that turns on the EPO gene, and William Kaelin and Peter Ratcliffe showed how prolyl hydroxylase enzymes and the von Hippel-Lindau protein tag HIF for destruction when oxygen is plentiful. They shared the 2019 Nobel Prize. Drug companies reasoned that blocking prolyl hydroxylase would fool the body into making its own EPO. GlaxoSmithKline developed daprodustat, which was approved in Japan in 2020. FDA approved it as Jesduvroq in February 2023 as the first oral treatment for anemia of CKD in adults on dialysis for at least four months.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Semenza, Kaelin and Ratcliffe uncover the HIF oxygen-sensing pathway."], ["2019", "The three share the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."], ["2020", "Daprodustat is approved in Japan."], ["2023", "FDA approves Jesduvroq for anemia of CKD in dialysis patients."]], "cell": "In normal oxygen, HIF prolyl hydroxylase enzymes (PHD1, PHD2 and PHD3) use oxygen, iron and 2-oxoglutarate to add hydroxyl groups to proline residues on HIF-alpha. The von Hippel-Lindau protein then recognizes hydroxylated HIF-alpha and marks it with ubiquitin for destruction by the proteasome. Daprodustat reversibly blocks these PHD enzymes by competing with 2-oxoglutarate. HIF-alpha (especially HIF-2 alpha) then escapes destruction, pairs with HIF-beta in the nucleus and turns on hypoxia genes. These include EPO in the kidney and liver, and iron handling genes such as DMT1, duodenal cytochrome b and transferrin. Hepcidin also falls, so more iron is absorbed and released from stores.", "body": [["Kidney and liver", "HIF activation raises the body's own EPO production into a near-normal range."], ["Bone marrow", "More EPO drives red cell production and a gradual rise in hemoglobin."], ["Gut and iron stores", "Lower hepcidin and more iron transporters improve iron absorption and release for new red cells."], ["Heart and vessels", "Blood pressure can rise, and thrombotic events and heart failure hospitalizations are concerns."], ["Stomach and gut", "GI erosions and bleeding have been reported."]], "journey": "Daprodustat is a tablet taken once a day. It is absorbed within a few hours and is highly protein bound. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP2C8, so strong CYP2C8 inhibitors such as gemfibrozil are contraindicated because they greatly raise levels. The parent drug has a short half-life of a few hours, but some active metabolites last longer. Metabolites leave in feces and urine. Hemoglobin rises over weeks.", "sidefx": "Like ESAs, raising hemoglobin increases clot risk, and Jesduvroq carries a boxed warning for death, heart attack, stroke, venous thromboembolism and dialysis access thrombosis. Because HIF controls many genes beyond EPO, including some related to blood vessel growth, there are theoretical concerns about tumor growth and retinal vessel disease. Heart failure hospitalizations and GI erosions occurred more often in trials. It is not approved for patients not on dialysis. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped.", "fun": "Daprodustat essentially tricks kidney cells into thinking they are short of oxygen, much like the body's natural response to living at high altitude.", "src": ["Jesduvroq (daprodustat) FDA label", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 2019 press release", "Singh AK et al. ASCEND-D trial, NEJM 2021"]}, "vadadustat": {"origin": "Vadadustat was developed by Akebia Therapeutics in the US, based on the hypoxia-inducible factor pathway discovered by Semenza, Kaelin and Ratcliffe (Nobel Prize 2019). Akebia aimed for an oral pill that would raise the body's own EPO more gently than injected ESAs. It was approved in Japan in 2020 through partner Mitsubishi Tanabe. FDA first declined to approve it in 2022 over safety concerns, including liver injury and thrombotic events in patients not on dialysis. After further review, FDA approved it as Vafseo in March 2024 for anemia of CKD in adults who have been on dialysis for at least three months.", "timeline": [["2019", "The HIF oxygen-sensing discovery wins the Nobel Prize."], ["2020", "Vadadustat is approved in Japan."], ["2022", "FDA issues a complete response letter declining approval."], ["2024", "FDA approves Vafseo for anemia of CKD in dialysis patients."]], "cell": "Vadadustat reversibly inhibits HIF prolyl hydroxylase domain enzymes PHD1, PHD2 and PHD3, competing with their cofactor 2-oxoglutarate. Without hydroxylation, HIF-alpha is not recognized by the von Hippel-Lindau ubiquitin ligase and is not sent to the proteasome. Stabilized HIF-alpha, particularly HIF-2 alpha, moves into the nucleus, pairs with HIF-beta and binds hypoxia response elements. This increases transcription of EPO in kidney interstitial cells and the liver, and of iron handling genes. Hepcidin falls, so ferroportin stays on gut cells and macrophages and more iron reaches transferrin. The combined effect is more red cell production with better iron use.", "body": [["Kidney and liver", "Stabilized HIF raises endogenous EPO production, pulsing up after each dose."], ["Bone marrow", "EPO stimulates red cell precursors, increasing hemoglobin over weeks."], ["Iron handling", "Lower hepcidin improves iron absorption and mobilization from stores."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises and rare serious liver injury have been reported, so liver tests are monitored."], ["Heart and vessels", "Thrombotic events, high blood pressure and heart failure are key risks."], ["Brain", "Seizures have been reported, sometimes linked to rising blood pressure."]], "journey": "Vadadustat is a tablet taken once daily, peaking in a few hours. It is very highly protein bound. Unlike daprodustat, it is metabolized mainly by glucuronidation (UGT enzymes) rather than CYP450. Its half-life is about 4.5 hours in healthy people and roughly twice that in dialysis patients. Oral iron and phosphate binders containing iron, calcium or aluminum can bind it in the gut, so it is taken apart from them.", "sidefx": "Raising hemoglobin and blood viscosity increases thrombosis, and Vafseo carries a boxed warning for death, heart attack, stroke, venous thromboembolism and access thrombosis. Hepatotoxicity, including rare severe cases, is a specific concern for vadadustat. Because HIF also regulates genes for blood vessel growth, malignancy is a theoretical concern. GI erosions and seizures have been seen. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Vadadustat reached patients in Japan four years before FDA approved it in the US.", "src": ["Vafseo (vadadustat) FDA label", "Eckardt KU et al. INNO2VATE trials, NEJM 2021"]}, "lusutrombopag": {"origin": "Thrombopoietin (TPO), the main hormone that controls platelet production, was cloned in 1994 by several groups after decades of searching. Recombinant TPO caused antibody problems, so companies designed small molecules that activate the TPO receptor instead. The Japanese company Shionogi developed lusutrombopag, which was approved in Japan in 2015. FDA approved it as Mulpleta in 2018 to raise platelet counts in adults with chronic liver disease who are scheduled for a procedure, offering an alternative to platelet transfusion.", "timeline": [["1994", "Thrombopoietin is cloned by several research groups."], ["2015", "Lusutrombopag is approved in Japan."], ["2018", "FDA approves Mulpleta for thrombocytopenia before procedures in chronic liver disease."]], "cell": "Lusutrombopag is a small-molecule agonist of the thrombopoietin receptor (c-Mpl). Unlike natural TPO, which binds the outside of the receptor, it binds the transmembrane region, so it does not compete with TPO. Binding activates the receptor-associated JAK2 kinase, which phosphorylates STAT3 and STAT5 and also activates the MAPK and PI3K-AKT pathways. These signals tell megakaryocyte progenitors in the bone marrow to multiply and mature. Mature megakaryocytes then shed more platelets into the blood. In liver disease, the damaged liver makes less TPO and the enlarged spleen traps platelets, so boosting production helps raise the count.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Megakaryocytes multiply and mature, so platelet output increases over about one to two weeks."], ["Blood", "A higher platelet count lowers the need for platelet transfusion before procedures."], ["Portal vein", "More platelets in a slow-flowing portal system can raise the risk of portal vein thrombosis."], ["Head", "Headache is the most common side effect."]], "journey": "It is taken by mouth once daily for a short course of about a week, starting before the planned procedure. It is absorbed well and very highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly by omega-oxidation through CYP4A enzymes, and it leaves mostly in feces. Its half-life is about a day. Platelets peak about two weeks after starting and drift back toward baseline over several weeks.", "sidefx": "The main concern is clotting, especially portal vein thrombosis, because raising platelet counts in people whose liver disease already slows portal blood flow adds to clot risk. Overshooting the platelet count increases this risk. Headache is the most common complaint. There is no reversal agent; the effect fades as platelets turn over.", "fun": "Lusutrombopag binds a different part of the TPO receptor than the body's own hormone, so the two can activate the receptor at the same time.", "src": ["Mulpleta (lusutrombopag) FDA label", "Kuter DJ. Thrombopoietin receptor agonists review"]}, "fostamatinib": {"origin": "Fostamatinib was developed by Rigel Pharmaceuticals in South San Francisco. Rigel discovered R406, a small molecule that blocks spleen tyrosine kinase (SYK), an enzyme that relays signals from antibody receptors on immune cells. Because R406 was poorly absorbed, Rigel made a phosphate prodrug, fostamatinib. It was first studied in rheumatoid arthritis, but a partnership with AstraZeneca ended after mixed results. Rigel then focused on immune thrombocytopenia (ITP), where antibody-coated platelets are eaten by spleen macrophages. FDA approved it as Tavalisse in 2018 as the first SYK inhibitor for chronic ITP.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Rigel discovers the SYK inhibitor R406 and its prodrug fostamatinib."], ["2013", "AstraZeneca ends development for rheumatoid arthritis."], ["2018", "FDA approves Tavalisse for chronic ITP."]], "cell": "In ITP, autoantibodies coat platelets. Macrophages in the spleen and liver recognize the antibody tails with Fc-gamma receptors. When those receptors cluster, they activate spleen tyrosine kinase (SYK) through ITAM motifs. SYK then drives the actin rearrangement and signaling needed to engulf and destroy the platelet. R406, the active form of fostamatinib, competes with ATP at the SYK kinase site and blocks this signal. Macrophages can still see the coated platelets but cannot eat them as well, so platelets survive longer and the count rises. SYK also signals downstream of B cell receptors, which may contribute to its effect.", "body": [["Spleen and liver", "Less Fc receptor driven phagocytosis means fewer platelets destroyed and a higher platelet count."], ["Blood vessels", "Off-target kinase effects, including on VEGF receptor signaling, raise blood pressure."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea and abdominal pain are common."], ["Liver", "ALT and AST can rise, so liver tests are monitored."], ["White blood cells", "Neutrophil counts can fall, raising infection risk, so blood counts are checked."]], "journey": "Fostamatinib is a tablet taken twice daily. In the gut, intestinal alkaline phosphatase removes the phosphate group and releases R406, which is absorbed and peaks within a couple of hours. Almost no fostamatinib reaches the blood intact. R406 is highly protein bound and is broken down by CYP3A4 and UGT1A9. Its half-life is about 15 hours. Most of the dose leaves in feces. Responders usually see platelet rises within a few weeks.", "sidefx": "R406 is not perfectly selective and inhibits other kinases, including VEGF receptor 2, which is thought to explain the high blood pressure. Diarrhea and liver enzyme rises are common and are managed with dose changes. Blocking SYK in neutrophils and other immune cells helps explain neutropenia and infections. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise R406 levels. It can harm a fetus. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Fostamatinib works without stopping the antibodies; it simply makes spleen macrophages worse at eating the antibody-coated platelets.", "src": ["Tavalisse (fostamatinib) FDA label", "Bussel J et al. FIT trials, Am J Hematol 2018"]}, "crizanlizumab": {"origin": "P-selectin is an adhesion molecule stored in platelets and endothelial cells, identified in the 1980s. Researchers learned that in sickle cell disease, P-selectin helps sickle red cells, white cells and platelets stick to vessel walls and to each other, plugging small vessels. The antibody was developed by Selexys Pharmaceuticals as SelG1 and acquired by Novartis in 2016. After the SUSTAIN trial showed fewer pain crises, FDA approved it as Adakveo in 2019 to reduce vaso-occlusive crises in people 16 and older. A later trial, STAND, did not confirm the benefit, and European regulators revoked its authorization in 2023, but it remains FDA approved in the US.", "timeline": [["1980s", "P-selectin is identified on activated platelets and endothelium."], ["2016", "Novartis acquires Selexys and its antibody SelG1."], ["2017", "SUSTAIN trial results show fewer vaso-occlusive crises."], ["2019", "FDA approves Adakveo."], ["2023", "Europe revokes its authorization after the STAND trial."]], "cell": "During inflammation and hypoxia, activated endothelial cells and platelets move P-selectin from storage granules (Weibel-Palade bodies and alpha granules) to their surface. P-selectin binds P-selectin glycoprotein ligand-1 (PSGL-1) on white cells and other ligands on sickle red cells. This tethers cells to the vessel wall and to each other, slowing flow so more cells sickle and form clumps that block small vessels. Crizanlizumab is a humanized IgG2 antibody that binds P-selectin and blocks this interaction. Fewer cells stick, so the chain of adhesion, stasis and vaso-occlusion is less likely to start.", "body": [["Small blood vessels", "Less adhesion of red cells, white cells and platelets reduces vessel blockage and pain crises."], ["Bones and joints", "Fewer vaso-occlusive events means fewer episodes of severe bone pain, though joint and back pain occur as side effects."], ["Immune system", "Infusion reactions with fever, chills and pain can occur."], ["Lab tests", "It can interfere with automated platelet counts in EDTA tubes, causing falsely low readings."]], "journey": "Crizanlizumab is given as an IV infusion, with two loading doses two weeks apart and then every four weeks. As an antibody, it stays mainly in the blood and is cleared slowly by protein breakdown and by binding its target. The half-life is about 11 days. It is not metabolized by liver enzymes.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are infusion-related reactions, including fever, chills, nausea and pain, sometimes resembling a pain crisis. Arthralgia and back pain are common. Because P-selectin also has a role in immune cell trafficking, infection is a theoretical concern. Severe reactions are treated by stopping the infusion and giving supportive care. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Crizanlizumab can make a lab platelet count look falsely low because the antibody causes platelets to clump in the EDTA collection tube.", "src": ["Adakveo (crizanlizumab-tmca) FDA label", "Ataga KI et al. SUSTAIN trial, NEJM 2017"]}, "voxelotor": {"origin": "Sickle cell disease was first described in Western medicine by James Herrick in 1910, and in 1949 Linus Pauling showed it was a 'molecular disease' caused by abnormal hemoglobin. Hemoglobin S polymerizes only when it gives up oxygen, so researchers reasoned that holding more hemoglobin in the oxygenated state could prevent sickling. Global Blood Therapeutics designed voxelotor to do this, and FDA granted it accelerated approval as Oxbryta in 2019. Pfizer acquired the company in 2022. In September 2024 Pfizer withdrew Oxbryta from the US and worldwide markets after newer data showed more vaso-occlusive crises and deaths, so it is no longer available.", "timeline": [["1910", "James Herrick describes sickle-shaped red cells."], ["1949", "Linus Pauling identifies sickle cell disease as a molecular disease of hemoglobin."], ["2019", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Oxbryta."], ["2022", "Pfizer acquires Global Blood Therapeutics."], ["2024", "Oxbryta is withdrawn from the US market in September."]], "cell": "Hemoglobin S has a valine in place of glutamate in the beta chain. When it releases oxygen and shifts to the tense (T, deoxy) state, this valine sticks to a pocket on a neighboring hemoglobin, forming long polymers that bend the red cell into a sickle. Voxelotor binds covalently but reversibly (as a Schiff base) to the N-terminal valine of one alpha chain. This stabilizes the relaxed (R, oxygenated) state and raises hemoglobin's oxygen affinity, shifting the oxygen dissociation curve to the left. Fewer molecules are in the deoxy state at any time, so polymer formation, sickling and hemolysis fall.", "body": [["Red blood cells", "Less sickling means red cells live longer, raising hemoglobin and lowering reticulocytes."], ["Liver and bilirubin", "Less hemolysis lowers indirect bilirubin and LDH."], ["Tissues and oxygen", "Hemoglobin that holds oxygen more tightly may release less oxygen to tissues, a concern raised after withdrawal."], ["Gut and skin", "Diarrhea, nausea, abdominal pain, headache and rash were common side effects."]], "journey": "Voxelotor was a once-daily tablet. After absorption it partitions heavily into red blood cells, where it binds hemoglobin, so levels in whole blood are far higher than in plasma. It is metabolized in the liver mainly by CYP3A4, and its half-life in sickle cell disease is about a day and a half. It leaves in both feces and urine.", "sidefx": "Common side effects were headache, diarrhea and rash, and hypersensitivity reactions occurred. Post-marketing studies found an imbalance in vaso-occlusive crises and deaths, leading to withdrawal in 2024. One proposed explanation is that a left-shifted oxygen curve raises hemoglobin numbers without improving, or even reducing, oxygen delivery to tissues. It also interfered with some lab measurements of hemoglobin subtypes. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Voxelotor binds so heavily to hemoglobin that most of the drug in the body is carried inside red blood cells rather than in plasma.", "src": ["Oxbryta (voxelotor) FDA label (withdrawn)", "FDA Drug Safety Communication, September 2024", "Vichinsky E et al. HOPE trial, NEJM 2019"]}, "mitapivat": {"origin": "Pyruvate kinase deficiency is the most common enzyme defect of glycolysis and causes lifelong hemolytic anemia because red cells, which lack mitochondria, depend on glycolysis for energy. Agios Pharmaceuticals, a company founded on cancer metabolism research, developed mitapivat as the first drug that activates the red cell pyruvate kinase enzyme, including many mutant forms. FDA approved it as Pyrukynd in February 2022 as the first disease-modifying treatment for hemolytic anemia in adults with PK deficiency. In December 2025 FDA also approved it under the name Aqvesme for adults with alpha or beta thalassemia.", "timeline": [["1961", "Pyruvate kinase deficiency is described as a cause of hemolytic anemia."], ["2022", "FDA approves mitapivat (Pyrukynd) for PK deficiency."], ["2025", "FDA approves mitapivat (Aqvesme) for thalassemia."]], "cell": "Pyruvate kinase catalyzes the last step of glycolysis, moving a phosphate from phosphoenolpyruvate to ADP to make ATP and pyruvate. In red cells this enzyme is the PKR isoform. Mitapivat binds an allosteric site at the interface between subunits of the PKR tetramer, stabilizing the active form. This works on normal enzyme and on many mutant enzymes that are unstable or less active. Faster flux through glycolysis raises ATP and lowers upstream intermediates, including 2,3-diphosphoglycerate (2,3-DPG). More ATP helps red cells maintain their membranes and ion pumps, so they survive longer and hemolysis falls. In thalassemia, extra ATP helps red cells cope with damage from excess globin chains.", "body": [["Red blood cells", "More ATP lets red cells keep their shape and survive longer, reducing hemolysis and raising hemoglobin."], ["Spleen and liver", "Fewer damaged cells means less clearance by the spleen and less jaundice."], ["Hormones", "Mitapivat inhibits aromatase, lowering estrone and estradiol in men."], ["Liver", "Hepatocellular injury has been reported in thalassemia patients, so liver tests are monitored."], ["Bones and sleep", "Back pain, joint pain and insomnia are among reported side effects."]], "journey": "Mitapivat is taken by mouth twice daily and absorbed quickly, peaking within an hour. It is highly protein bound and metabolized in the liver mainly by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A inhibitors and inducers change levels. Its effective half-life is a few hours. Metabolites leave in feces and urine. Hemoglobin rises over weeks.", "sidefx": "Stopping mitapivat suddenly can trigger acute hemolysis because red cells lose their energy boost at once, so the dose is tapered. Inhibition of aromatase explains lower estrogen levels in men and may contribute to bone effects. Raised urate and liver injury are reported. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Mitapivat came from a company founded to target cancer metabolism, and its first approval ended up being for an inherited red blood cell disease.", "src": ["Pyrukynd (mitapivat) FDA label", "Al-Samkari H et al. ACTIVATE trial, NEJM 2022"]}, "concizumab": {"origin": "Tissue factor pathway inhibitor (TFPI) was characterized in the 1980s by George Broze and colleagues as the natural brake on the tissue factor clotting pathway. Researchers proposed that releasing this brake could restore clotting in hemophilia without replacing the missing factor. Novo Nordisk developed concizumab, a humanized antibody against TFPI. Its trials were paused in 2020 after thrombotic events, then resumed with a new dosing approach. FDA approved it as Alhemo in December 2024 for prophylaxis in people with hemophilia A or B with inhibitors, and in July 2025 expanded approval to people without inhibitors. It is a daily injection given with a pen.", "timeline": [["1980s", "George Broze and colleagues characterize tissue factor pathway inhibitor."], ["2020", "Concizumab trials pause after thrombotic events, then resume."], ["2024", "FDA approves Alhemo for hemophilia A or B with inhibitors."], ["2025", "Approval expands to hemophilia A or B without inhibitors."]], "cell": "Clotting starts when tissue factor (TF) at a wound binds factor VIIa and activates factors X and IX. TFPI quickly shuts this down: its Kunitz-2 domain binds and inhibits factor Xa, and its Kunitz-1 domain then locks the TF-VIIa complex. Normally the intrinsic tenase (factor VIIIa with IXa) takes over and amplifies thrombin. In hemophilia A or B, that amplification is missing. Concizumab binds the Kunitz-2 domain of TFPI and blocks it from inhibiting factor Xa. TF-VIIa can then keep producing factor Xa, which makes enough thrombin to form a stable clot without factor VIII or IX. This works whether or not the patient has inhibitors against replacement factors.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "More factor Xa and thrombin generation prevents spontaneous bleeds and joint bleeds."], ["Joints", "Fewer joint bleeds protect against hemophilic arthropathy over time."], ["Blood vessels", "Too much thrombin, especially with other clotting products, can cause arterial or venous clots."], ["Skin", "Injection site reactions and hives are common."]], "journey": "Concizumab is injected under the skin once daily with a prefilled pen. As an antibody, it is absorbed slowly from the injection site. Its clearance is nonlinear because binding to TFPI on endothelium removes it (target-mediated disposition), so levels depend on how much TFPI is available. Drug levels are checked early to adjust the dose. It is broken down like other proteins and is not handled by liver enzymes.", "sidefx": "The main risk follows directly from the mechanism: with the natural brake removed, adding extra procoagulants (such as high amounts of factor concentrate or bypassing agents for a breakthrough bleed) can cause thrombosis. Thromboembolic events led to the 2020 trial pause. Hypersensitivity and injection site reactions occur. There is no direct antidote; the drug is held and supportive care given.", "fun": "Concizumab and marstacimab target the same small Kunitz-2 domain of TFPI, but concizumab is given every day while marstacimab is given once a week.", "src": ["Alhemo (concizumab-mtci) FDA label", "Broze GJ Jr. Tissue factor pathway inhibitor review"]}, "marstacimab": {"origin": "Marstacimab is a fully human IgG1 antibody developed by Pfizer against tissue factor pathway inhibitor (TFPI), the natural brake on the tissue factor clotting pathway first characterized in the 1980s. Pfizer aimed for a simpler preventive treatment for hemophilia than repeated IV factor infusions. In the BASIS trial, weekly injections cut bleeding rates compared with on-demand and prophylactic factor therapy. FDA approved it as Hympavzi in October 2024 for routine prophylaxis in people 12 and older with hemophilia A or B without inhibitors, making it the first once-weekly anti-TFPI treatment.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Tissue factor pathway inhibitor is characterized as a natural anticoagulant."], ["2024", "FDA approves Hympavzi for hemophilia A or B without inhibitors."]], "cell": "TFPI uses its Kunitz-2 (K2) domain to bind and inhibit factor Xa, and its Kunitz-1 domain to inhibit the tissue factor-factor VIIa complex. This shuts off the extrinsic start of clotting within seconds, leaving amplification to the intrinsic tenase (factor VIIIa-IXa), which is missing in hemophilia. Marstacimab binds the K2 domain and neutralizes TFPI's ability to block factor Xa. The tissue factor-VIIa complex can then keep generating factor Xa, which, with factor Va, converts prothrombin to thrombin. Thrombin generation rises enough to support clotting without factor VIII or IX. Lab markers such as D-dimer and prothrombin fragment 1.2 rise as expected.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Sustained factor Xa and thrombin generation prevents many spontaneous bleeds."], ["Joints", "Fewer joint bleeds help protect joints from long-term damage."], ["Blood vessels", "Excess thrombin generation can cause clots, especially with other procoagulant treatments or other clot risks."], ["Skin", "Injection site reactions and itching are common."], ["Pregnancy", "It may cause fetal harm, based on animal data and mechanism."]], "journey": "Marstacimab is injected under the skin once weekly with a prefilled pen or syringe. It is absorbed slowly over several days. Binding to TFPI on endothelial cells removes it from the blood (target-mediated clearance), so it has no single fixed half-life. Like other IgG antibodies, it is broken down into peptides and amino acids rather than by liver enzymes or kidneys.", "sidefx": "Because it removes a natural anticoagulant, thromboembolic events are the main safety concern, particularly when other clotting products are used for a breakthrough bleed or when other clot risks exist. Hypersensitivity reactions and injection site reactions occur. Animal data suggest embryofetal risk. There is no antidote; it is held and factor or supportive care given as needed.", "fun": "Marstacimab turns a bleeding problem on its head: instead of adding the missing factor, it takes away a brake on clotting.", "src": ["Hympavzi (marstacimab-hncq) FDA label", "Matino D et al. BASIS trial"]}, "fitusiran": {"origin": "Fitusiran comes from RNA interference, a natural gene-silencing system described in 1998 by Andrew Fire and Craig Mello in worms, work that won the 2006 Nobel Prize. Alnylam Pharmaceuticals turned RNAi into medicines by attaching small interfering RNAs (siRNAs) to a sugar, GalNAc, that liver cells grab. Alnylam reasoned that people with a natural antithrombin deficiency who also had hemophilia bled less, so lowering antithrombin might rebalance clotting. Fitusiran was developed by Alnylam and then Sanofi. FDA approved it as Qfitlia in March 2025 for prophylaxis in people 12 and older with hemophilia A or B with or without inhibitors, the first siRNA for hemophilia.", "timeline": [["1998", "Fire and Mello describe RNA interference."], ["2006", "Fire and Mello receive the Nobel Prize."], ["2025", "FDA approves Qfitlia for hemophilia A or B with or without inhibitors."]], "cell": "Antithrombin, made by the liver from the SERPINC1 gene, is a natural anticoagulant that inactivates thrombin and factor Xa. Fitusiran is a double-stranded siRNA linked to three GalNAc sugars. GalNAc binds the asialoglycoprotein receptor on hepatocytes, which pulls the drug inside. The guide strand loads into the RNA-induced silencing complex (RISC), which finds the matching SERPINC1 mRNA and cuts it. With less mRNA, the liver makes less antithrombin. Lower antithrombin lets more thrombin survive and act, so thrombin generation rises even though factor VIII or IX is missing. Because RISC is reused, one dose silences the gene for weeks to months.", "body": [["Liver", "Hepatocytes make much less antithrombin; liver enzyme rises can occur."], ["Blood clotting", "With antithrombin lowered to a target range, more thrombin forms and bleeding episodes fall."], ["Blood vessels", "If antithrombin falls too low, or other clot risks are present, arterial or venous clots, including in the brain, can occur."], ["Gallbladder", "Gallstones and gallbladder inflammation have been seen, which is part of the boxed warning."]], "journey": "Fitusiran is injected under the skin every one or two months, with timing adjusted by antithrombin levels. It leaves the blood within hours because liver cells take it up so efficiently. Inside the liver it is slowly broken down by nucleases. The drug's plasma half-life is short, but the silencing effect lasts weeks to months because RISC keeps working. Little intact drug is excreted in urine.", "sidefx": "The key risk follows from the mechanism: too little antithrombin tips the balance toward clotting. Qfitlia carries a boxed warning for thrombotic events and for acute and recurrent gallbladder disease. Antithrombin activity is monitored to keep it in a target range. Breakthrough bleeds are treated with reduced amounts of factor or bypassing agents to avoid clots. Because the silencing lasts for months, the effect cannot be switched off quickly after a dose. Liver enzyme rises are also monitored.", "fun": "Fitusiran was inspired by an observation that people with hemophilia who also inherited an antithrombin or protein C deficiency tended to bleed less.", "src": ["Qfitlia (fitusiran) FDA label", "Fire A, Mello CC et al. Nature 1998"]}, "protein-c-concentrate": {"origin": "Protein C was discovered in 1976 by Swedish biochemist Johan Stenflo, who found a new vitamin K-dependent protein in bovine plasma and named it for its position as the third peak, peak C, in his chromatography. Researchers soon learned it is a natural anticoagulant. Babies born with severe protein C deficiency develop purpura fulminans, widespread clotting in skin vessels that can be fatal. Fresh frozen plasma was the only option for years. Baxter developed Ceprotin, a purified concentrate made from human plasma, and FDA approved it in 2007 for severe congenital protein C deficiency.", "timeline": [["1976", "Johan Stenflo discovers protein C."], ["1980s", "Severe congenital protein C deficiency is linked to neonatal purpura fulminans."], ["2007", "FDA approves Ceprotin."]], "cell": "Protein C is a vitamin K-dependent zymogen made by the liver. When thrombin binds thrombomodulin on healthy endothelium, thrombin's job changes: instead of making fibrin, it activates protein C, a step made faster by the endothelial protein C receptor. Activated protein C (APC) is a serine protease. With protein S as its cofactor on phospholipid surfaces, APC cuts and inactivates factors Va and VIIIa. Without these cofactors, the prothrombinase and tenase complexes slow down and thrombin generation falls. APC also inhibits plasminogen activator inhibitor-1, favoring clot breakdown, and has anti-inflammatory signaling effects. Replacing protein C restores this brake on clotting.", "body": [["Small blood vessels", "Restored protein C stops microvascular clots that cause purpura fulminans and skin death."], ["Skin", "It helps prevent warfarin-induced skin necrosis in deficient patients starting warfarin."], ["Blood clotting", "Inactivation of factors Va and VIIIa reduces thrombin generation and new clot formation."], ["Immune system", "Itching, rash and rare allergic reactions can occur, as with other plasma products."]], "journey": "Ceprotin is given as an IV injection, so it acts immediately. It circulates as the inactive zymogen until activated on endothelium. It is consumed by activation and cleared like other plasma proteins. The half-life is about 10 hours on average but can be much shorter during active clotting, when protein C is used up quickly, so dosing and monitoring are frequent during acute episodes.", "sidefx": "Because it boosts a natural anticoagulant, bleeding can increase when it is combined with other anticoagulants or thrombolytics. Being a human plasma product, it carries a small theoretical risk of transmitting infections despite viral inactivation steps, and it can cause allergic reactions. It contains trace heparin, so it is avoided in people with heparin-induced thrombocytopenia. There is no specific reversal agent.", "fun": "Protein C got its simple name because it was the third peak (C) in Stenflo's chromatography column.", "src": ["Ceprotin (protein C concentrate, human) FDA label", "Stenflo J. J Biol Chem 1976"]}, "aminolevulinic-acid": {"origin": "Photodynamic therapy began around 1900 when Oscar Raab, a medical student in Munich working with Hermann von Tappeiner, found that acridine dye plus light killed microbes. Von Tappeiner coined the term photodynamic. Aminolevulinic acid (ALA) is the first committed building block of heme, made naturally in every cell. In 1990 James Kennedy, Roy Pottier and colleagues in Canada showed that ALA applied to skin causes abnormal cells to build up the light-sensitive pigment protoporphyrin IX, which light then activates. DUSA Pharmaceuticals developed the Levulan Kerastick, approved by FDA in 1999 for actinic keratoses with blue light. Ameluz gel was approved in 2016, and oral ALA (Gleolan) was approved in 2017 to make malignant glioma glow pink under blue light during surgery.", "timeline": [["c. 1900", "Raab and von Tappeiner describe the photodynamic effect."], ["1990", "Kennedy and Pottier report topical ALA photodynamic therapy."], ["1999", "FDA approves Levulan Kerastick for actinic keratoses."], ["2016", "FDA approves Ameluz gel."], ["2017", "FDA approves oral ALA (Gleolan) for glioma visualization."]], "cell": "Normally the heme pathway is held back by feedback: heme inhibits ALA synthase, the first enzyme. Supplying ALA directly bypasses this control. Cells take up ALA and convert it through porphobilinogen and other intermediates to protoporphyrin IX (PpIX) in the mitochondria. Ferrochelatase then inserts iron to make heme, but this step is slow, and dysplastic and tumor cells have relatively low ferrochelatase and high uptake, so PpIX accumulates in them. When light near 417 nm (blue) or red light hits PpIX, it absorbs energy and transfers it to oxygen, forming singlet oxygen and other reactive oxygen species. These damage membranes and mitochondria and kill the abnormal cells, while normal skin accumulates less PpIX.", "body": [["Skin lesions", "Actinic keratosis cells that build up PpIX are destroyed by light, and the lesions crust and clear."], ["Treated skin", "Stinging, burning, redness and swelling occur during and after illumination."], ["Whole skin", "For about two days the treated area stays light sensitive, so sunlight and bright light are avoided."], ["Brain tumor (oral form)", "Glioma cells accumulate PpIX and fluoresce pink under blue light, helping surgeons see tumor edges."]], "journey": "For actinic keratoses, the solution is painted onto lesions and left for several hours, sometimes under occlusion, then the skin is exposed to blue light. Very little ALA reaches the blood from the skin, and the small amount absorbed is cleared within hours. Locally, PpIX builds up over hours and is then converted to heme or cleared, so photosensitivity fades over about two days. Oral ALA for glioma is absorbed quickly and taken a few hours before surgery.", "sidefx": "Side effects come directly from light-activated damage: stinging, burning, redness, swelling, scaling and crusting at treated sites. Pain during illumination reflects reactive oxygen species activating nerve endings. Until PpIX clears, sun exposure can cause a severe phototoxic burn. Severe hypersensitivity is rare. There is no antidote; light avoidance and cooling help.", "fun": "With oral ALA, surgeons switch the microscope to blue light and malignant glioma tissue glows a vivid pink-violet against normal brain.", "src": ["Levulan Kerastick (aminolevulinic acid) FDA label", "Gleolan FDA label", "Kennedy JC, Pottier RH, Pross DC. J Photochem Photobiol B 1990"]}, "givosiran": {"origin": "Acute hepatic porphyrias are inherited defects in heme synthesis. During an attack, the liver's ALA synthase 1 (ALAS1) ramps up, and toxic intermediates ALA and porphobilinogen flood the body, causing severe abdominal pain and nerve damage. Treatment was hemin infusion, which works by feedback. Alnylam Pharmaceuticals, a pioneer of RNA interference drugs, designed givosiran to switch off ALAS1 directly in the liver. FDA approved it as Givlaari in November 2019, the first treatment for acute hepatic porphyria to prevent attacks and the first GalNAc-conjugated siRNA to reach patients.", "timeline": [["1998", "Fire and Mello describe RNA interference."], ["2018", "FDA approves the first siRNA drug, patisiran."], ["2019", "FDA approves givosiran (Givlaari) for acute hepatic porphyria."]], "cell": "Givosiran is a double-stranded siRNA linked to a GalNAc sugar cluster. GalNAc binds the asialoglycoprotein receptor on hepatocytes, which draws the drug into liver cells. The antisense strand loads into the RNA-induced silencing complex (RISC), which binds and cleaves ALAS1 mRNA. Less ALAS1 enzyme means less ALA made at the start of the heme pathway. Because the downstream defect in porphyria (such as low porphobilinogen deaminase) is a bottleneck, lowering input prevents the buildup of ALA and porphobilinogen, which are thought to be neurotoxic. Attacks become less frequent and chronic symptoms improve.", "body": [["Liver", "Hepatic ALAS1 falls, cutting production of ALA and porphobilinogen; transaminases can rise."], ["Nerves", "Lower neurotoxic intermediates mean fewer attacks of abdominal pain, weakness and neuropathy."], ["Kidneys", "Rises in creatinine and declines in kidney function have been reported."], ["Blood", "Homocysteine can rise, possibly because heme-dependent enzymes in the liver are affected."], ["Pancreas", "Acute pancreatitis has been reported, sometimes severe."]], "journey": "Givosiran is injected under the skin once a month by a health care professional. It is absorbed and taken up by the liver within hours, with a plasma half-life of about six hours. Nucleases in the liver break it down, forming an active metabolite with one fewer nucleotide. A small fraction leaves unchanged in urine. Because RISC keeps working, ALA and porphobilinogen stay lowered for weeks after each dose.", "sidefx": "Liver enzyme rises reflect the drug's concentration in hepatocytes. Kidney toxicity, raised homocysteine and pancreatitis are reported, and the reasons are not fully understood. Injection site reactions and nausea are common. Anaphylaxis can occur, so patients are observed. There is no reversal agent; the drug is stopped.", "fun": "Some historians have speculated that King George III's bouts of madness were due to porphyria, though this theory is debated.", "src": ["Givlaari (givosiran) FDA label", "Balwani M et al. ENVISION trial, NEJM 2020"]}, "danicopan": {"origin": "Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH) is a rare blood disease in which red cells lack the protective proteins CD55 and CD59, so complement attacks them. The C5 antibodies eculizumab (2007) and ravulizumab (2018) stopped red cells from bursting in the bloodstream, but many patients stayed anemic because C3 fragments still coated their cells and the spleen and liver removed them (extravascular hemolysis). Achillion Pharmaceuticals developed danicopan, an oral factor D inhibitor, and Alexion (now part of AstraZeneca) acquired Achillion in 2019. FDA approved danicopan as Voydeya in March 2024 as an add-on to ravulizumab or eculizumab for extravascular hemolysis in adults with PNH, the first factor D inhibitor.", "timeline": [["2007", "FDA approves eculizumab for PNH."], ["2019", "Alexion acquires Achillion and danicopan."], ["2024", "FDA approves Voydeya as add-on therapy for PNH."]], "cell": "The alternative complement pathway runs continuously at a low level. C3b deposited on a cell binds factor B, and factor D, a serine protease, cleaves bound factor B into Bb. The resulting C3bBb is the alternative pathway C3 convertase, which splits more C3 and creates an amplification loop. Danicopan reversibly binds and blocks factor D, so this convertase cannot form. In PNH, red cells lacking CD55 (which normally breaks up convertases) and CD59 get coated with C3 fragments. C5 inhibitors stop membrane attack complex formation but not C3 coating. By blocking upstream at factor D, danicopan reduces C3 deposition, so macrophages in the spleen and liver remove fewer red cells.", "body": [["Red blood cells", "Less C3 coating means fewer red cells removed by the spleen and liver, raising hemoglobin."], ["Spleen and liver", "Extravascular hemolysis falls, lowering the need for transfusions."], ["Immune system", "Blocking the alternative pathway weakens defense against encapsulated bacteria such as meningococcus."], ["Liver tests and lipids", "Raised liver enzymes and higher cholesterol have been reported."]], "journey": "Danicopan is a tablet taken three times a day with food, alongside a C5 inhibitor. It peaks in a few hours and has a half-life of about eight hours, which is why it is taken several times daily. It is highly protein bound and metabolized by several liver pathways, with CYP3A4 a minor route. It leaves mainly in feces.", "sidefx": "Because complement is key for killing encapsulated bacteria, blocking factor D raises the risk of serious infections, especially Neisseria meningitidis, Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae. Voydeya carries a boxed warning for these and requires vaccination and a REMS program. Stopping it suddenly can let hemolysis rebound. Headache, vomiting and raised liver enzymes are reported. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Factor D circulates at the lowest concentration of any complement protein, making it an efficient bottleneck to target with a pill.", "src": ["Voydeya (danicopan) FDA label", "Lee JW et al. ALPHA trial, Lancet Haematol 2023"]}, "tbo-filgrastim": {"origin": "Granulocyte colony-stimulating factor (G-CSF) was purified in the mid-1980s, with work by Karl Welte and Malcolm Moore at Memorial Sloan Kettering and others, and Amgen cloned the human gene. Amgen's filgrastim (Neupogen) was approved in 1991. Tbo-filgrastim is a non-glycosylated G-CSF made in E. coli, developed by a company later acquired by Teva. It was approved in Europe in 2008 as a biosimilar. In the US the biosimilar pathway did not yet exist, so Teva filed a full biologics license application, and FDA approved it as Granix in 2012. It is therefore a separate biologic, not a legally interchangeable biosimilar of filgrastim.", "timeline": [["1980s", "G-CSF is purified and its gene cloned."], ["1991", "FDA approves filgrastim (Neupogen)."], ["2008", "Tbo-filgrastim is approved in Europe."], ["2012", "FDA approves Granix through a full biologics license application."]], "cell": "Tbo-filgrastim binds the G-CSF receptor (CSF3R) on myeloid progenitor cells and mature neutrophils. Binding causes two receptor chains to pair, activating JAK1 and JAK2 kinases, which phosphorylate STAT3 and STAT5. Other pathways such as PI3K-AKT and MAPK are also turned on. These signals drive neutrophil precursors in the marrow to multiply, mature faster and survive longer. G-CSF also lowers CXCL12 retention signals in the marrow, so mature neutrophils are released into the blood. It can enhance neutrophil function, such as phagocytosis. The result is a faster rise in neutrophil counts after chemotherapy.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Neutrophil precursors multiply and mature faster, and expansion of the marrow causes bone pain."], ["Blood", "Absolute neutrophil count rises within a day or two, shortening the time spent with severe neutropenia."], ["Spleen", "Neutrophil production in the spleen can enlarge it and, rarely, cause rupture."], ["Lungs", "Neutrophil accumulation can cause ARDS in rare cases."], ["Kidneys", "Glomerulonephritis has been reported with G-CSF products."]], "journey": "Tbo-filgrastim is injected under the skin once daily, starting a day after chemotherapy. It peaks within several hours and has a half-life of about three to four hours. It is cleared by binding to neutrophils (receptor-mediated uptake) and by the kidneys. As neutrophil counts rise, more drug is removed, which acts as a natural brake on its levels.", "sidefx": "Bone pain is the most common effect, caused by rapid marrow expansion and is often treated with simple analgesics. Splenic enlargement and rupture, ARDS, capillary leak syndrome and glomerulonephritis are rare but serious effects linked to neutrophil activation and expansion. In sickle cell disease, G-CSF can trigger severe sickle crises. Allergic reactions occur. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped.", "fun": "Tbo-filgrastim was sold as a biosimilar in Europe but had to be approved as a new biologic in the US because the US biosimilar law was not yet in place.", "src": ["Granix (tbo-filgrastim) FDA label", "Welte K et al. PNAS 1985"]}, "factor-ix": {"origin": "In 1952 Rosemary Biggs and colleagues in Oxford reported that some people with hemophilia lacked a different clotting factor than the classic factor VIII. They named it Christmas disease after their first patient, Stephen Christmas, and the paper appeared in the Christmas issue of the BMJ. The missing protein became known as factor IX. Early treatments used plasma and then prothrombin complex concentrates in the 1970s, which carried factors II, VII, IX and X and could cause clots. In the 1980s plasma products transmitted HIV and hepatitis to many people with hemophilia. This led to purified, virus-inactivated products such as AlphaNine SD, whose name reflects a solvent detergent step that destroys enveloped viruses.", "timeline": [["1952", "Biggs and colleagues describe Christmas disease (hemophilia B)."], ["1970s", "Prothrombin complex concentrates are used to treat hemophilia B."], ["1980s", "HIV and hepatitis transmission through plasma products drive viral inactivation methods."], ["1990s", "High-purity plasma-derived factor IX products become available."]], "cell": "Factor IX is a vitamin K-dependent serine protease zymogen made by the liver. At a wound, factor XIa or the tissue factor-factor VIIa complex cleaves factor IX into active factor IXa. On the surface of activated platelets, factor IXa binds its cofactor factor VIIIa with calcium, forming the intrinsic tenase complex. This complex activates factor X far faster than tissue factor-VIIa alone, creating the large burst of thrombin needed for a stable clot. In hemophilia B, low factor IX means this amplification fails. Infused factor IX replaces the missing zymogen, restoring tenase activity and thrombin generation.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Restored factor IX corrects the aPTT and allows normal thrombin burst and stable clots."], ["Joints and muscles", "Treating or preventing bleeds protects joints from blood-induced damage."], ["Immune system", "Some people form neutralizing antibodies (inhibitors), and factor IX inhibitors are linked to anaphylaxis and kidney problems."], ["Blood vessels", "Older, less pure products with other factors raised the risk of clotting and DIC."]], "journey": "Factor IX is given by IV infusion and acts immediately. A large share of infused factor IX moves out of the blood into tissues and binds collagen IV in vessel walls, so recovery in plasma is lower than with factor VIII. The half-life is about 18 to 24 hours. It is cleared like other plasma proteins by catabolism.", "sidefx": "The most serious immune problem is inhibitor development; in hemophilia B, inhibitors often come with allergic reactions or anaphylaxis, and repeated exposure can cause nephrotic syndrome. Clots and DIC were more common with impure complex concentrates containing activated factors. Mild headache, nausea and chills occur. As a plasma product, it carries a small theoretical infection risk despite viral inactivation. There is no reversal agent.", "fun": "Analysis of DNA from the remains of the Russian royal family in 2009 showed that the 'royal disease' passed down from Queen Victoria was hemophilia B, a factor IX deficiency.", "src": ["AlphaNine SD (coagulation factor IX, human) FDA label", "Biggs R et al. BMJ 1952", "Rogaev EI et al. Science 2009"]}, "von-willebrand-factor": {"origin": "In 1926 the Finnish physician Erik von Willebrand described a bleeding disorder in a family from the Aland Islands that affected both males and females, unlike hemophilia. It became known as von Willebrand disease, now the most common inherited bleeding disorder. The missing protein, von Willebrand factor (VWF), was identified decades later. Plasma-derived VWF/factor VIII concentrates such as Humate-P became the standard treatment. Baxalta (later Takeda) developed vonicog alfa, a recombinant VWF made in Chinese hamster ovary cells, and FDA approved it as Vonvendi in 2015, the first recombinant VWF product.", "timeline": [["1926", "Erik von Willebrand describes a bleeding disorder in an Aland Islands family."], ["1970s", "Von Willebrand factor is identified as distinct from factor VIII."], ["2015", "FDA approves Vonvendi, the first recombinant VWF."]], "cell": "VWF is a large multimeric glycoprotein made by endothelial cells and megakaryocytes. At sites of injury under high shear, VWF binds exposed subendothelial collagen through its A3 domain, and the shear force stretches it so its A1 domain grabs platelet glycoprotein Ib. This tethers platelets to the wound so they can activate and aggregate through GPIIb/IIIa. VWF also binds and protects factor VIII in the blood, preventing its rapid breakdown. Larger multimers are more active. Recombinant VWF contains ultra-large multimers because it is not exposed to ADAMTS13 during manufacture; ADAMTS13 in the patient's blood then trims them. Vonvendi contains no factor VIII, but the patient's own factor VIII rises within hours as VWF protects it.", "body": [["Platelets and vessel wall", "Restored VWF lets platelets stick to injured vessels, stopping mucosal and surgical bleeding."], ["Blood clotting", "Endogenous factor VIII rises over 6 to 24 hours because VWF stabilizes it."], ["Immune system", "Itching, allergic reactions and rare neutralizing antibodies can occur."], ["Blood vessels", "Thromboembolic events can occur, especially with high factor VIII levels or other clot risks."]], "journey": "Recombinant VWF is given by IV infusion, often with factor VIII for the first dose in an acute bleed if factor VIII is low. It acts immediately on platelet adhesion. ADAMTS13 in the blood cleaves the ultra-large multimers into the normal size range. Its half-life is about a day. It is cleared by protein breakdown.", "sidefx": "Because it supports platelet adhesion and raises factor VIII, too much can contribute to clotting, so thromboembolic events are a warning. Hypersensitivity and anaphylaxis can occur, and some patients develop neutralizing antibodies that reduce its effect. Mild itching, headache and nausea are reported. There is no reversal agent.", "fun": "The first patient von Willebrand studied was a girl named Hjordis from the Aland Islands, and several of her sisters had died of bleeding.", "src": ["Vonvendi (von Willebrand factor, recombinant) FDA label", "von Willebrand EA. Finska Lakaresallskapets Handlingar 1926"]}, "anagrelide": {"origin": "Anagrelide is an imidazoquinazoline first studied in the 1970s as a drug to stop platelets clumping together. In early human studies, researchers noticed that it also lowered the platelet count itself, at amounts below those needed for the anti-aggregation effect. This surprise turned it into a treatment for essential thrombocythemia and other myeloproliferative disorders with very high platelet counts. FDA approved it as Agrylin in 1997. It gave patients an option that lowers platelets without the broad marrow suppression of hydroxyurea.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Anagrelide is studied as an inhibitor of platelet aggregation."], ["1980s", "Its platelet-lowering effect in humans is recognized and studied in thrombocythemia."], ["1997", "FDA approves Agrylin."]], "cell": "Anagrelide lowers platelet production by blocking the late maturation of megakaryocytes in the bone marrow. It reduces megakaryocyte size, ploidy and cytoplasmic maturation in the postmitotic phase, so fewer platelets bud off. The exact molecular target for this effect is not fully known, and it has little effect on red or white cell lines. Separately, anagrelide and especially its active metabolite 3-hydroxy-anagrelide inhibit phosphodiesterase 3 (PDE3). Higher cAMP in platelets reduces aggregation. In vascular smooth muscle, higher cAMP causes vasodilation, and in heart muscle it increases contractility and heart rate.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Megakaryocyte maturation slows, so platelet counts fall over one to two weeks."], ["Platelets", "PDE3 inhibition reduces aggregation, adding to bleeding risk with aspirin or anticoagulants."], ["Heart", "PDE3 effects cause palpitations, tachycardia, arrhythmias, QT prolongation and possible heart failure."], ["Blood vessels", "Vasodilation causes headache, flushing, dizziness and fluid retention."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea and abdominal pain are common."], ["Lungs", "Rare interstitial lung disease has been reported."]], "journey": "Anagrelide is taken by mouth and absorbed quickly, peaking in about an hour. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP1A2 to the active 3-hydroxy-anagrelide and the inactive RL603. The parent half-life is short, about 1.5 hours, and the active metabolite lasts a bit longer, so it is taken several times a day. Metabolites leave in the urine. Platelets rise back toward baseline within days of stopping. Smoking and CYP1A2 inhibitors such as fluvoxamine change its levels.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from PDE3 inhibition rather than the platelet effect. Raised cAMP in the heart explains palpitations, tachycardia, atrial fibrillation and, in some patients, heart failure; QT prolongation and torsades have been reported. Vasodilation explains headache and edema. Bleeding risk rises when combined with aspirin or other antithrombotics. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped and supportive care given.", "fun": "Anagrelide was meant to be an antiplatelet drug, but its real use came from a side effect: it lowered platelet counts.", "src": ["Agrylin (anagrelide) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "factor-xiii-concentrate": {"origin": "In 1948 Hungarian scientists Koloman Laki and Laszlo Lorand discovered that a plasma factor made fibrin clots insoluble, which became known as the Laki-Lorand factor, later factor XIII. In 1960 Duckert, Jung and Shmerling in Switzerland described the first patient with congenital factor XIII deficiency, a child with severe bleeding including from the umbilical cord. This rare disorder causes delayed bleeding, poor wound healing, miscarriage and a high risk of brain bleeds. CSL Behring developed a plasma-derived concentrate (Fibrogammin in Europe), which FDA approved as Corifact in 2011. A recombinant A-subunit product, Tretten, followed in 2013.", "timeline": [["1948", "Laki and Lorand discover the fibrin-stabilizing factor."], ["1960", "Duckert and colleagues describe congenital factor XIII deficiency."], ["2011", "FDA approves Corifact."], ["2013", "FDA approves recombinant factor XIII A-subunit (Tretten)."]], "cell": "Factor XIII circulates as a tetramer of two A subunits, which carry the enzyme, and two B subunits, which carry and protect them. Thrombin cleaves an activation peptide from the A subunits, and in the presence of calcium the B subunits fall away, leaving active factor XIIIa. Factor XIIIa is a transglutaminase. It forms covalent gamma-glutamyl-epsilon-lysyl bonds between fibrin gamma chains and alpha chains, turning a loose fibrin network into a strong cross-linked mesh. It also attaches alpha2-antiplasmin to fibrin, making the clot resistant to plasmin. Without it, clots form but break down early, causing delayed bleeding.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Cross-linked fibrin makes clots stronger and slower to dissolve, preventing delayed bleeding."], ["Brain", "Regular prophylaxis lowers the high risk of intracranial hemorrhage in deficient patients."], ["Wounds", "Factor XIII helps wound healing by cross-linking matrix proteins."], ["Pregnancy", "It is needed to keep the placenta attached, so deficient women may miscarry without replacement."], ["Immune system", "Rash, itching and rare anaphylaxis can occur."]], "journey": "Corifact is given by IV infusion, usually once a month as prophylaxis. It acts immediately. Factor XIII has a very long half-life of about a week in adults, a bit shorter in children, which allows monthly dosing. It is used up as clots form and broken down like other plasma proteins.", "sidefx": "Because it strengthens clots, too much factor XIII or use in people with other clot risks can contribute to thrombosis. Some patients develop inhibitors against factor XIII. Hypersensitivity reactions, rash, itching, joint pain and headache are reported. As a plasma product, it has a small theoretical infection risk. There is no reversal agent.", "fun": "Umbilical stump bleeding in the first days of life is a classic first sign of severe congenital factor XIII deficiency.", "src": ["Corifact (factor XIII concentrate, human) FDA label", "Laki K, Lorand L. Science 1948"]}, "efanesoctocog-alfa": {"origin": "Factor VIII was cloned in 1984 by teams at Genentech and Genetics Institute, and the first recombinant factor VIII was approved in 1992. Extended half-life versions, such as Fc fusion products, came later but hit a limit: factor VIII in the blood is carried by the patient's own von Willebrand factor, so it cannot outlast VWF's half-life of about 15 hours. Bioverativ (part of Sanofi) and Sobi designed efanesoctocog alfa to break this ceiling by adding a piece of VWF, an Fc region and XTEN polypeptides. FDA approved it as Altuviiio in February 2023 as a once-weekly factor VIII for hemophilia A.", "timeline": [["1984", "The factor VIII gene is cloned."], ["1992", "FDA approves the first recombinant factor VIII."], ["2014", "Fc fusion extended half-life factor VIII is approved."], ["2023", "FDA approves Altuviiio, the first factor VIII to break the VWF half-life ceiling."]], "cell": "Factor VIII is a cofactor. When activated by thrombin, factor VIIIa binds factor IXa on activated platelet membranes, forming the intrinsic tenase complex that activates factor X much faster, producing the thrombin burst needed for a stable clot. In hemophilia A, this complex fails. Efanesoctocog alfa is a single-chain factor VIII fused to the D'D3 domain of VWF, which normally binds factor VIII, so it does not bind the patient's own VWF and is not cleared along with it. An Fc region binds the neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn), which rescues the protein from breakdown in endothelial cells and recycles it into the blood. Two XTEN polypeptides add bulk and slow clearance. When activated, the add-on parts are released and factor VIIIa works normally.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Factor VIII activity stays in the normal to near-normal range for much of the week after a dose."], ["Joints", "High sustained factor levels prevent most joint bleeds and protect joint health."], ["Immune system", "Inhibitor formation and hypersensitivity are possible, as with other factor VIII products."], ["Muscles and joints", "Headache, joint pain and back pain are reported side effects."]], "journey": "Altuviiio is given by IV injection once weekly for prophylaxis. It acts immediately. Because it is decoupled from endogenous VWF and recycled by FcRn, its half-life is about 47 hours in adults, about three to four times that of standard factor VIII. It is eventually broken down by protein catabolism.", "sidefx": "The main immune risk is forming neutralizing antibodies (inhibitors) that block factor VIII, though this has been uncommon in previously treated patients. Hypersensitivity and anaphylaxis can occur. Headache and joint pain are reported. Very high factor VIII levels could theoretically add to clot risk in people with other risk factors. There is no reversal agent.", "fun": "Altuviiio's design pulls factor VIII away from the patient's own VWF, which normally caps how long any factor VIII product can last in the blood.", "src": ["Altuviiio (antihemophilic factor recombinant, Fc-VWF-XTEN fusion protein-ehtl) FDA label", "von Drygalski A et al. XTEND-1 trial, NEJM 2023"]}, "crovalimab": {"origin": "Crovalimab was developed by Chugai Pharmaceutical, a member of the Roche group in Japan, using its SMART-Ig recycling antibody technology. Standard C5 antibodies like eculizumab (2007) and ravulizumab (2018) are given by IV infusion. Chugai engineered crovalimab to release C5 inside cells so the same antibody can be recycled and reused, allowing small subcutaneous injections. It also binds a C5 variant common in some people of East Asian ancestry that eculizumab cannot bind. It was first approved in China in early 2024, and FDA approved it as Piasky in June 2024 for PNH in patients 13 and older.", "timeline": [["2007", "FDA approves eculizumab, the first C5 inhibitor, for PNH."], ["2024", "Crovalimab is approved in China and then by FDA in June as Piasky."]], "cell": "In PNH, red cells lack the GPI-anchored proteins CD55 and CD59, so complement C5 convertases form and the membrane attack complex (C5b-9) punches holes in them. Crovalimab is a humanized IgG1 that binds complement C5 at a site different from eculizumab and blocks its cleavage into C5a and C5b. Without C5b, the membrane attack complex cannot form and intravascular hemolysis stops. Its binding is pH-dependent: it binds C5 tightly in blood but releases it in the acidic endosome. The C5 is degraded in lysosomes, while the free antibody binds FcRn and is recycled back to the blood to bind more C5. It also binds the R885H C5 variant that resists eculizumab.", "body": [["Red blood cells", "Blocking the membrane attack complex stops intravascular hemolysis, lowering LDH and raising hemoglobin."], ["Blood vessels", "Less free hemoglobin preserves nitric oxide and lowers the high clot risk of PNH."], ["Immune system", "Blocking C5 weakens defense against Neisseria species, especially meningococcus."], ["Switching patients", "Drug-target-drug complexes can form when switching from another C5 antibody, causing type III hypersensitivity reactions."]], "journey": "Treatment starts with an IV loading dose, followed by weekly subcutaneous injections for several weeks and then injections every four weeks. Because it is recycled through FcRn and pH-dependent C5 release, its half-life is very long, about 53 days. Like other antibodies, it is broken down by protein catabolism rather than liver enzymes.", "sidefx": "Because the terminal complement pathway is needed to kill Neisseria bacteria, C5 blockade sharply raises the risk of meningococcal infection, the subject of a boxed warning; vaccination and a REMS program are required. Switching from eculizumab or ravulizumab can cause immune complexes of antibody, C5 and the other antibody, leading to type III hypersensitivity reactions such as rash, joint pain and fever. Infusion and injection site reactions and upper respiratory infections occur. There is no antidote.", "fun": "One crovalimab molecule can capture, release and recapture C5 many times, which is why monthly injections of a small volume are enough.", "src": ["Piasky (crovalimab-akkz) FDA label", "Roth A et al. COMMODORE trials"]}, "ferric-maltol": {"origin": "Oral iron salts such as ferrous sulfate have been used since the 19th century but often cause stomach upset, partly because free iron irritates the gut. Maltol is a natural compound found in larch bark, pine needles and roasted malt, also used as a flavor enhancer. Researchers found that ferric iron bound tightly to three maltol molecules forms a stable, neutral complex that can deliver iron with less free iron in the gut. The UK company Shield Therapeutics developed ferric maltol, approved in Europe in 2016 as Feraccru. FDA approved it as Accrufer in 2019 for iron deficiency in adults.", "timeline": [["1830s", "Blaud's pills of ferrous sulfate popularize oral iron therapy."], ["2016", "Ferric maltol (Feraccru) is approved in Europe."], ["2019", "FDA approves Accrufer for iron deficiency in adults."]], "cell": "In ferric maltol, one ferric iron atom is held by three maltol molecules. This neutral, fat-soluble complex keeps iron from being released as free ferric ions in the gut lumen, where it would form insoluble hydroxides or irritate the lining. At the duodenal surface the complex releases its iron, which follows the normal path: duodenal cytochrome b reduces ferric to ferrous iron, DMT1 carries it into the enterocyte, and ferroportin exports it to the blood, where hephaestin oxidizes it back to ferric iron for transferrin. Because the body's normal controls (hepcidin acting on ferroportin) still apply, absorption adjusts to need. Transferrin then delivers iron to the marrow for hemoglobin and to ferritin stores.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "More iron available for red cell production raises hemoglobin within weeks."], ["Iron stores", "Ferritin and transferrin saturation gradually rise as stores refill."], ["Gut", "Gas, diarrhea, constipation, abdominal pain and dark stools can occur, though the complex is designed to be gentler."], ["Bowel disease", "In inflammatory bowel disease, it may be linked to symptom flares in some patients."]], "journey": "Ferric maltol is a capsule taken twice daily on an empty stomach. The complex dissociates at the gut wall; iron is absorbed through the normal duodenal pathway and bound to transferrin, while maltol is absorbed separately and rapidly glucuronidated in the liver and excreted by the kidneys. Iron itself has no regulated route of excretion and is stored. Hemoglobin rises over weeks.", "sidefx": "Gut side effects such as gas, diarrhea, constipation and abdominal pain come from iron and the maltol complex in the bowel. Stools often darken from unabsorbed iron. Long-term use without monitoring could cause iron overload. Accidental ingestion by children can cause iron poisoning, which damages the gut and then the liver; severe poisoning is treated with deferoxamine.", "fun": "Maltol, the iron carrier in this drug, gives freshly baked bread and caramel part of their sweet aroma.", "src": ["Accrufer (ferric maltol) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "ferric-pyrophosphate-citrate": {"origin": "People on hemodialysis lose iron with every treatment through blood left in the circuit and testing. Intravenous iron-sugar complexes help, but they must first be processed by macrophages, which in chronic kidney disease are often blocked by high hepcidin from inflammation. Nephrologist Ajay Gupta developed soluble ferric pyrophosphate, an iron salt that can hand iron straight to transferrin, and Rockwell Medical brought it to market. FDA approved Triferic, added to the dialysate, in 2015, and an IV form, Triferic AVNU, in 2020. It was the first iron product designed to replace ongoing dialysis losses without building up ferritin stores.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Soluble ferric pyrophosphate is developed for dialysis iron replacement."], ["2015", "FDA approves Triferic, added to dialysate."], ["2020", "FDA approves Triferic AVNU for IV use."]], "cell": "Ferric pyrophosphate citrate is a water-soluble complex in which ferric iron is held by pyrophosphate and citrate. When it reaches the blood, it donates iron directly to apotransferrin, the empty form of the iron carrier. This bypasses the reticuloendothelial macrophages that must normally break down iron-carbohydrate complexes. In CKD, inflammation raises hepcidin, which degrades ferroportin and traps iron inside macrophages, a key cause of poor iron use. By loading transferrin directly, the drug avoids this hepcidin block. Transferrin then delivers iron to erythroid precursors through transferrin receptor 1 for heme synthesis.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Directly delivered iron supports steady hemoglobin production, which helps maintain hemoglobin with ESA therapy."], ["Iron stores", "Because iron goes to transferrin rather than macrophages, ferritin stores do not build up."], ["Blood vessels", "Hypotension and peripheral edema are reported during dialysis treatment."], ["Muscles", "Muscle spasms and pain in the arms or legs are reported."], ["Dialysis access", "Bleeding at the arteriovenous fistula site has been reported."]], "journey": "Triferic is added to the bicarbonate concentrate of dialysate so iron crosses the dialyzer membrane into the blood during each session; Triferic AVNU is infused into the dialysis circuit. Iron is transferred to transferrin within minutes to hours, and serum iron returns toward baseline with a half-life of about 1.5 hours. The iron is used for red cells or stored, since the body has no active route to excrete iron.", "sidefx": "Side effects such as headache, low blood pressure, edema and muscle cramps overlap with symptoms common during dialysis. Hypersensitivity reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, can occur. Iron overload is possible without monitoring. There is no specific antidote; true iron overload would be treated with chelation.", "fun": "Triferic is the only iron product that can be given through the dialysis fluid itself, crossing the dialyzer membrane like other dissolved salts.", "src": ["Triferic and Triferic AVNU (ferric pyrophosphate citrate) FDA labels", "Gupta A et al. CRUISE trials"]}, "pentazocine": {"origin": "Pentazocine is a benzomorphan opioid synthesized in the late 1950s by chemists at Sterling-Winthrop as part of an effort to find a strong painkiller with less addiction risk than morphine. FDA approved it as Talwin in 1967. Because it partly blocks mu receptors, it was first thought to have little abuse potential. In the 1970s, however, people began injecting crushed pentazocine tablets mixed with the antihistamine tripelennamine, a combination called 'Ts and Blues'. In response, the manufacturer added naloxone to the tablets, creating Talwin NX in the early 1980s. Naloxone does nothing when swallowed but blocks pentazocine if the tablet is injected. The brand has been discontinued and the injectable form is no longer marketed, but generic pentazocine-naloxone tablets remain.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Sterling-Winthrop chemists synthesize pentazocine."], ["1967", "FDA approves Talwin."], ["1970s", "Injection misuse of pentazocine with tripelennamine spreads."], ["1980s", "Pentazocine-naloxone tablets (Talwin NX) are introduced."]], "cell": "Pentazocine is a mixed agonist-antagonist. It is an agonist at kappa-opioid receptors, which are Gi/o-coupled and, like mu receptors, reduce cAMP, open potassium channels and close calcium channels on pain-transmitting neurons, especially in the spinal cord. At mu-opioid receptors it acts as a weak partial agonist or antagonist. This gives a ceiling effect: past a certain point, more drug does not add much pain relief or respiratory depression. Kappa activation in the brain causes dysphoria and hallucinations. The added naloxone is a mu antagonist with poor oral bioavailability, so it only matters if the tablet is injected.", "body": [["Spinal cord and brain", "Kappa agonism reduces pain transmission, giving moderate pain relief with a ceiling."], ["Mood and perception", "Kappa activation can cause dysphoria, anxiety, confusion and hallucinations."], ["Heart and vessels", "It raises blood pressure, heart rate, pulmonary artery pressure and cardiac workload, unlike morphine."], ["Lungs", "Respiratory depression occurs but tends to plateau at higher amounts."], ["Opioid-dependent patients", "Mu antagonism can precipitate withdrawal in people taking full mu agonists."]], "journey": "Pentazocine is taken by mouth and absorbed well, but extensive first-pass metabolism in the liver lets only a fraction reach the blood. It starts working within about half an hour, and its half-life is about 2 to 3 hours. The liver oxidizes and glucuronidates it, and metabolites leave in the urine. Naloxone in the tablet is almost completely destroyed on the first pass when swallowed.", "sidefx": "Kappa effects explain the psychotomimetic side effects: dysphoria, hallucinations and confusion. Its sympathetic and cardiac effects raise blood pressure and heart work, making it a poor choice after a heart attack. Partial mu blockade can trigger withdrawal in people dependent on other opioids. Sedation, nausea and dizziness are common. Respiratory depression is possible, especially with other CNS depressants, and naloxone reverses it, though higher amounts may be needed than for pure mu agonists.", "fun": "Adding naloxone to pentazocine tablets was one of the first attempts at an abuse-deterrent opioid formulation.", "src": ["Pentazocine and naloxone tablets FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "levorphanol": {"origin": "In 1946 the German chemist Rudolf Grewe synthesized morphinan, the basic four-ring skeleton of morphine without the oxygen bridge. Chemists at Hoffmann-La Roche used this to make racemorphan, a mix of two mirror-image forms. They found that the left-handed (levo) form, levorphanol, was the potent painkiller, while the right-handed form had cough-suppressing effects; its relative dextromethorphan became a common cough medicine. Levorphanol was introduced in the US as Levo-Dromoran in the early 1950s. It later fell out of favor but has regained interest for its long action and NMDA blocking effect in neuropathic pain. The brand is gone but generic tablets remain.", "timeline": [["1946", "Rudolf Grewe synthesizes the morphinan skeleton."], ["1940s", "Hoffmann-La Roche develops racemorphan and separates levorphanol."], ["1950s", "Levorphanol is introduced in the US as Levo-Dromoran."]], "cell": "Levorphanol is a full agonist at mu-opioid receptors. Mu receptors are Gi/o-coupled: activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase, opens potassium channels that hyperpolarize neurons, and closes presynaptic calcium channels, which reduces release of glutamate and substance P from pain fibers. It also activates delta and kappa receptors. Unlike morphine, it blocks NMDA glutamate receptors (noncompetitive antagonism), which may help with neuropathic pain and slow tolerance. It also inhibits reuptake of serotonin and norepinephrine, strengthening descending pain-inhibiting pathways but adding a risk of serotonin syndrome.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Mu, delta and kappa activation plus NMDA blockade give strong, long-lasting pain relief."], ["Lungs", "Mu activation in the brainstem blunts the response to carbon dioxide, causing respiratory depression."], ["Gut", "Mu receptors in the gut slow movement and cause constipation."], ["Eyes", "Pupils constrict (miosis) from mu-driven parasympathetic activity."], ["Mood and sedation", "Sedation, dizziness and euphoria can occur."]], "journey": "Levorphanol is taken by mouth and absorbed well, with onset within an hour. It has a long half-life of about 11 to 16 hours, much longer than its pain-relief duration, so it can build up over several days. The liver metabolizes it by glucuronidation rather than CYP3A4, which means fewer CYP drug interactions. The glucuronide leaves in the urine, so kidney disease can raise levels.", "sidefx": "Mu agonism explains constipation, nausea, sedation and respiratory depression. Because its half-life outlasts its pain relief, repeated doses can accumulate and cause delayed or prolonged overdose. Reuptake inhibition adds risk of serotonin syndrome with other serotonergic drugs. Combining it with benzodiazepines or alcohol greatly increases respiratory depression. Naloxone reverses opioid effects, but repeated doses or an infusion may be needed because levorphanol lasts longer than naloxone.", "fun": "Levorphanol's mirror image, dextrorphan, is the active metabolite of the cough suppressant dextromethorphan.", "src": ["Levorphanol tartrate tablets FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "codeine-acetaminophen": {"origin": "Codeine was isolated from opium in 1832 by French chemist Pierre-Jean Robiquet. Acetaminophen was first made by Harmon Northrop Morse in 1878 and tested in patients by Joseph von Mering in the 1880s, but it was ignored until 1948, when Bernard Brodie and Julius Axelrod showed it was the active product of the older drugs acetanilide and phenacetin. McNeil launched Tylenol in 1955. Combining the two gave a moderate pain reliever, and Tylenol with Codeine became one of the most widely used opioid combinations. After deaths in children who were ultra-rapid CYP2D6 metabolizers, FDA in 2017 contraindicated codeine in children under 12 and for pain after tonsil or adenoid surgery in those under 18.", "timeline": [["1832", "Robiquet isolates codeine from opium."], ["1878", "Harmon Northrop Morse synthesizes acetaminophen."], ["1948", "Brodie and Axelrod identify acetaminophen as the active metabolite of acetanilide."], ["1955", "McNeil launches Tylenol."], ["2017", "FDA contraindicates codeine in children under 12."]], "cell": "Codeine itself binds mu-opioid receptors weakly. It works mainly as a prodrug: CYP2D6 in the liver O-demethylates about 5 to 10 percent of it to morphine, a full mu agonist. Mu receptors are Gi/o-coupled; activation lowers cAMP, opens potassium channels and closes presynaptic calcium channels, so pain signals are dampened in the spinal cord and brain. Acetaminophen inhibits prostaglandin synthesis in the central nervous system by acting on the peroxidase site of COX, which works best where peroxide levels are low, as in the brain, explaining its weak anti-inflammatory effect. It also acts through descending serotonergic pathways and a metabolite (AM404) that affects cannabinoid and TRPV1 signaling. The two parts combine opioid and non-opioid pain relief.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Morphine from codeine and central COX inhibition by acetaminophen combine to relieve pain."], ["Cough center", "Codeine suppresses the cough reflex in the brainstem."], ["Lungs", "Mu activation depresses breathing, dangerously so in ultra-rapid metabolizers who make too much morphine."], ["Gut", "Mu receptors slow gut movement, causing constipation and nausea."], ["Liver", "Acetaminophen overdose forms toxic NAPQI, which can cause severe liver injury."], ["Hypothalamus", "Acetaminophen lowers fever by reducing prostaglandin E2 in the temperature center."]], "journey": "Both parts are well absorbed by mouth, with effects in 30 to 60 minutes. Codeine is converted by CYP2D6 to morphine, by CYP3A4 to norcodeine and by glucuronidation to codeine-6-glucuronide. People with low CYP2D6 activity get little pain relief, while ultra-rapid metabolizers make much more morphine. Acetaminophen is mostly glucuronidated and sulfated; a small part is oxidized by CYP2E1 to NAPQI, which glutathione detoxifies. Both have half-lives of a few hours and leave in the urine.", "sidefx": "Codeine's effects come from morphine at mu receptors: sedation, constipation, nausea and respiratory depression, which is the boxed warning, especially in ultra-rapid metabolizers and with other CNS depressants. Breastfed infants of ultra-rapid metabolizing mothers can be exposed to high morphine levels. Acetaminophen in excess saturates the safe pathways, and NAPQI depletes glutathione and damages liver cells. Naloxone reverses opioid effects, and N-acetylcysteine restores glutathione to treat acetaminophen overdose.", "fun": "Depending on ancestry, roughly 1 to 10 in 100 people are CYP2D6 ultra-rapid metabolizers who turn codeine into morphine unusually fast, with the highest rates reported in some North African, Ethiopian and Arab populations.", "src": ["Tylenol with Codeine (acetaminophen and codeine) FDA label", "FDA Drug Safety Communication 2017", "Brodie BB, Axelrod J. J Pharmacol Exp Ther 1948"]}, "hydrocodone-acetaminophen": {"origin": "Hydrocodone was first synthesized from codeine in 1920 by German chemists Carl Mannich and Helene Lowenheim. It was approved in the US in 1943, first mainly as a cough suppressant. Combined with acetaminophen, which Brodie and Axelrod had shown in 1948 to be the active metabolite of older pain relievers, it became products such as Vicodin, Lortab and Norco. For decades it was the most prescribed drug in the US. Because combination products were in Schedule III, they were easy to refill, and they became central to the prescription opioid crisis. In 2014 the DEA moved hydrocodone combination products to Schedule II. FDA also limited the amount of acetaminophen per tablet in prescription combinations to reduce liver injury.", "timeline": [["1920", "Mannich and Lowenheim synthesize hydrocodone."], ["1943", "Hydrocodone is approved in the US."], ["2011", "FDA announces limits on acetaminophen content in prescription combination products."], ["2014", "DEA reschedules hydrocodone combination products to Schedule II."]], "cell": "Hydrocodone is a semisynthetic phenanthrene opioid and a full agonist at mu-opioid receptors. Mu receptors are Gi/o-coupled. Activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase and lowers cAMP, opens G protein-gated inward rectifier potassium (GIRK) channels that hyperpolarize neurons, and closes presynaptic N-type calcium channels, cutting the release of glutamate and substance P from pain fibers in the spinal cord. In the brain, mu activation also changes the emotional response to pain and releases dopamine in reward pathways, which drives euphoria and addiction. CYP2D6 converts part of hydrocodone to hydromorphone, a more potent mu agonist. Acetaminophen inhibits central prostaglandin synthesis at the COX peroxidase site and acts through descending serotonergic pathways, adding non-opioid pain relief.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Mu activation and central COX inhibition together relieve moderate to severe pain."], ["Brainstem breathing center", "Mu activation reduces the response to carbon dioxide, causing slow, shallow breathing that can be fatal."], ["Reward system", "Dopamine release in reward pathways causes euphoria and can lead to misuse and addiction."], ["Gut", "Mu receptors in the gut wall slow motility, causing constipation and nausea."], ["Liver", "Acetaminophen in excess forms toxic NAPQI and can cause acute liver failure."], ["Ears", "Hearing loss has been reported with long-term high-level misuse."]], "journey": "Both drugs are taken by mouth and absorbed within about an hour. Hydrocodone is metabolized in the liver mainly by CYP3A4 to norhydrocodone and partly by CYP2D6 to hydromorphone, so CYP3A4 inhibitors can raise levels dangerously. Its half-life is about 4 hours. Acetaminophen is mainly glucuronidated and sulfated, with a small part oxidized by CYP2E1 to NAPQI, which glutathione neutralizes. Both leave mainly in the urine.", "sidefx": "Respiratory depression is the most dangerous effect and is worse with benzodiazepines, alcohol and other CNS depressants, as the boxed warning states. Constipation, nausea and drowsiness come from mu activation in the gut and brain. Tolerance and physical dependence develop with regular use, and stopping suddenly causes withdrawal. Acetaminophen toxicity happens when the glucuronide and sulfate pathways are overwhelmed and NAPQI depletes glutathione, damaging liver cells. Naloxone reverses opioid effects, and N-acetylcysteine is the antidote for acetaminophen overdose.", "fun": "Before 2014 rescheduling, hydrocodone-acetaminophen was the most prescribed medication in the United States.", "src": ["Norco (hydrocodone bitartrate and acetaminophen) FDA label", "DEA Final Rule, Federal Register 2014", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "oxycodone-acetaminophen": {"origin": "Oxycodone was synthesized in 1916 by Martin Freund and Edmund Speyer at the University of Frankfurt from thebaine, an opium alkaloid. It was introduced in Germany the next year. In the US it was first combined with aspirin (Percodan), and in 1976 Endo Laboratories introduced Percocet, oxycodone combined with acetaminophen. Percocet became one of the most common opioid prescriptions for acute pain after injuries and surgery. Like other opioid combinations, it is a Schedule II drug with boxed warnings about addiction, respiratory depression and liver injury from acetaminophen.", "timeline": [["1916", "Freund and Speyer synthesize oxycodone from thebaine."], ["1917", "Oxycodone is introduced in Germany."], ["1976", "Percocet (oxycodone and acetaminophen) is introduced in the US."]], "cell": "Oxycodone is a semisynthetic full agonist at mu-opioid receptors. Through Gi/o proteins it inhibits adenylyl cyclase, opens potassium channels that hyperpolarize neurons and closes presynaptic calcium channels, blocking pain signal transmission in the dorsal horn and modifying pain perception in the brain. It has some kappa activity as well. Its metabolite oxymorphone, made by CYP2D6, is more potent, but most of the effect comes from oxycodone itself. Acetaminophen inhibits central prostaglandin synthesis at the COX peroxidase site and works through descending serotonergic pathways. Together they give stronger pain relief than either alone.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Combined opioid and non-opioid action relieves moderate to severe acute pain."], ["Brainstem", "Mu activation depresses breathing, the main cause of death in overdose."], ["Reward system", "Dopamine release produces euphoria, a driver of misuse."], ["Gut", "Slowed motility causes constipation, nausea and vomiting."], ["Liver", "Acetaminophen in excess produces NAPQI and can cause severe liver injury."], ["Hormones", "Long-term opioid use can cause adrenal insufficiency and low sex hormones."]], "journey": "Oxycodone is well absorbed by mouth, with higher bioavailability than morphine, and starts working within 30 minutes. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP3A4 to noroxycodone and partly by CYP2D6 to oxymorphone. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors can raise levels and cause overdose. Its half-life is about 3.5 hours. Acetaminophen is glucuronidated and sulfated, with a small CYP2E1 fraction forming NAPQI. Both leave in the urine.", "sidefx": "Mu agonism explains constipation, nausea, sedation, itching and the boxed warning for respiratory depression, which is greatly worsened by benzodiazepines and alcohol. Repeated use leads to tolerance, dependence and possible addiction. Opioids suppress the hypothalamic-pituitary axes, which can cause adrenal insufficiency. Too much acetaminophen saturates normal pathways and NAPQI injures the liver. Naloxone reverses opioid overdose, and N-acetylcysteine treats acetaminophen poisoning.", "fun": "Oxycodone was first made in Germany in 1916 in the hope of creating an opioid with less addiction potential than heroin.", "src": ["Percocet (oxycodone and acetaminophen) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "ziconotide": {"origin": "Ziconotide comes from the venom of Conus magus, the magician's cone snail, a fish-hunting marine snail that harpoons prey and paralyzes it with a cocktail of small peptides. Starting in the 1970s, Baldomero Olivera and colleagues at the University of Utah studied these conotoxins and found that some precisely block nerve calcium channels. One of them, omega-conotoxin MVIIA, blocks N-type calcium channels used by pain-transmitting neurons. The company Neurex developed a synthetic version, later carried on by Elan, and FDA approved it as Prialt in 2004 for severe chronic pain, given only by intrathecal infusion into the spinal fluid. It was the first drug from cone snail venom and a non-opioid option for patients who cannot tolerate opioids.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Baldomero Olivera's lab begins studying cone snail venom peptides."], ["1980s", "Omega-conotoxins are shown to block N-type calcium channels."], ["2004", "FDA approves ziconotide (Prialt) for intrathecal use."]], "cell": "Ziconotide is a 25-amino acid peptide held in shape by three disulfide bonds. It binds and blocks N-type voltage-gated calcium channels (CaV2.2), which are concentrated on the central terminals of primary afferent pain fibers in the superficial dorsal horn of the spinal cord. When a pain signal arrives, these channels normally open, letting calcium in to trigger release of glutamate, substance P and CGRP onto second-order neurons. By plugging the channel pore, ziconotide stops this release, so the pain signal is not passed on. It does not act on opioid receptors, so it does not cause tolerance, withdrawal or respiratory depression.", "body": [["Spinal cord", "Blocking calcium entry at pain fiber terminals stops pain signals from crossing to spinal neurons."], ["Brain", "Spread into the brain causes dizziness, confusion, memory problems, nystagmus and unsteady gait."], ["Mind and mood", "Hallucinations, paranoia, depression and suicidal thoughts can occur, which is the subject of a boxed warning."], ["Muscle", "Creatine kinase can rise, sometimes with muscle weakness."], ["Breathing", "It does not depress breathing, unlike opioids."]], "journey": "Ziconotide is a peptide that would be destroyed in the gut and does not cross into the spinal fluid well from the blood, so it is given by continuous infusion through an implanted pump directly into the spinal fluid (intrathecal). It spreads within the cerebrospinal fluid, with a half-life of about 4.5 hours there. Small amounts reach the blood, where peptidases quickly break it into amino acids. The dose is increased slowly, and pain relief develops over days. Effects fade within hours when the infusion stops.", "sidefx": "N-type calcium channels are also used by neurons in the brain, so drug that spreads upward in the spinal fluid causes neurological and psychiatric effects: dizziness, confusion, memory loss, nystagmus, hallucinations and severe depression. The boxed warning covers severe psychiatric symptoms and neurological impairment. The implanted catheter can introduce infection and meningitis. Because it does not act on opioid receptors, it can be stopped without withdrawal. There is no antidote; the infusion is stopped.", "fun": "A single cone snail's venom can contain more than a hundred different peptides, each fine-tuned to block a specific ion channel or receptor.", "src": ["Prialt (ziconotide) FDA label", "Olivera BM et al. Science 1985"]}, "suzetrigine": {"origin": "In 1996 John Wood and colleagues at University College London cloned a sodium channel found only in pain-sensing nerves, now called NaV1.8. Because it is absent from the brain and heart, it promised pain relief without opioid side effects. Many companies tried and failed to make selective blockers. Vertex Pharmaceuticals discovered suzetrigine (VX-548), which binds NaV1.8 with very high selectivity. After trials in pain following bunionectomy and abdominoplasty, FDA approved it as Journavx in January 2025 for moderate to severe acute pain in adults. It was the first new class of pain medicine approved in more than 20 years and is not a controlled substance.", "timeline": [["1996", "NaV1.8 (SNS) is cloned from pain-sensing neurons."], ["2010s", "Vertex develops selective NaV1.8 inhibitors."], ["2025", "FDA approves suzetrigine (Journavx) for acute pain."]], "cell": "Pain-sensing neurons (nociceptors) in the dorsal root ganglia express NaV1.8, a tetrodotoxin-resistant voltage-gated sodium channel encoded by SCN10A. It carries much of the sodium current during the upstroke of action potentials in these neurons and supports repetitive firing. Suzetrigine binds the second voltage-sensing domain (VSD2) of NaV1.8 rather than the pore. This stabilizes the channel in its closed state (allosteric inhibition), so it opens less in response to depolarization. Nociceptors fire fewer action potentials, and fewer pain signals reach the spinal cord. Because NaV1.8 is largely limited to peripheral nociceptors, it does not affect brain opioid systems or cause sedation or respiratory depression.", "body": [["Peripheral pain nerves", "Blocking NaV1.8 reduces firing of nociceptors so fewer pain signals are sent to the spinal cord."], ["Brain", "It does not act in the brain's reward or breathing centers, so it does not cause euphoria, sedation or respiratory depression."], ["Muscles", "Muscle spasms and increased creatine kinase have been reported."], ["Skin", "Itching and rash are among the more common side effects."], ["Kidneys", "Decreases in estimated kidney filtration have been reported."]], "journey": "Suzetrigine is taken by mouth, with a larger first dose followed by doses every 12 hours. It is absorbed in a few hours and is highly protein bound. The liver converts it via CYP3A to an active metabolite, M6-SUZ, which is less potent but lasts longer. The parent half-life is about a day. It leaves in feces and urine. Strong CYP3A inhibitors are avoided, grapefruit is avoided, and CYP3A inducers reduce its levels.", "sidefx": "Side effects in trials were mostly mild, including itching, muscle spasms, raised creatine kinase and rash. It can reduce the effectiveness of hormonal contraceptives containing progestins, so additional contraception is advised. Because it targets only peripheral nociceptors, it lacks the opioid risks of addiction and respiratory depression. Reduced kidney function raises exposure. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Some people with rare gain-of-function mutations in sodium channels on pain nerves have painful disorders, which helped researchers see these channels as pain targets.", "src": ["Journavx (suzetrigine) FDA label", "Akopian AN, Sivilotti L, Wood JN. Nature 1996"]}, "butorphanol-nasal": {"origin": "Butorphanol is a morphinan opioid synthesized in the early 1970s at Bristol Laboratories as part of the search for strong pain relievers with less addiction and breathing risk. FDA approved the injection as Stadol in 1978. Because swallowed butorphanol is mostly destroyed by the liver, the company developed a nasal spray, Stadol NS, approved in 1991 as the first opioid nasal spray, popular for migraine. It was originally not controlled, but reports of misuse and dependence led the DEA to place it in Schedule IV in 1997. The brand has been discontinued, but generic nasal spray remains.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Bristol Laboratories develops butorphanol."], ["1978", "FDA approves Stadol injection."], ["1991", "FDA approves Stadol NS, the first opioid nasal spray."], ["1997", "Butorphanol is placed in Schedule IV."]], "cell": "Butorphanol is a mixed agonist-antagonist. It is an agonist at kappa-opioid receptors, which are Gi/o-coupled, so it reduces cAMP, opens potassium channels and closes calcium channels on pain neurons in the spinal cord and brain. At mu-opioid receptors it acts as a partial agonist or antagonist. This gives a ceiling effect: beyond a certain point, more drug adds little pain relief or respiratory depression. In someone dependent on a full mu agonist, its mu antagonism can trigger withdrawal. Kappa activation also causes sedation and dysphoria.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Kappa agonism relieves moderate to severe pain, including migraine headache."], ["Breathing", "Respiratory depression occurs but plateaus, though it remains dangerous with other depressants."], ["Mood and perception", "Kappa activation can cause dysphoria, confusion and hallucinations."], ["Heart and lungs", "It can raise pulmonary artery pressure and cardiac workload."], ["Nose and mouth", "Nasal congestion and an unpleasant taste are common with the spray."]], "journey": "The spray is absorbed across the blood-rich nasal lining directly into the blood, bypassing the liver first pass that makes oral butorphanol ineffective. Pain relief starts within about 15 minutes and peaks within an hour. The liver hydroxylates and dealkylates it, and metabolites leave mainly in urine. The half-life is about 5 hours and longer in older adults.", "sidefx": "Kappa effects explain the drowsiness, dizziness and psychotomimetic effects such as dysphoria and hallucinations. Mu antagonism can precipitate withdrawal in people using other opioids. Although respiratory depression has a ceiling, it can still be severe with benzodiazepines or alcohol. Overuse for migraine can lead to dependence and medication overuse headache. Naloxone reverses its effects, though larger amounts may be needed.", "fun": "Stadol NS was the first opioid approved in the US as a nasal spray.", "src": ["Butorphanol tartrate nasal spray FDA label", "DEA scheduling notice, 1997"]}, "buprenorphine-transdermal": {"origin": "Buprenorphine was created in 1966 by chemists at the British company Reckitt and Colman, led by John Lewis and Kenneth Bentley, who were looking for a strong painkiller with less abuse potential. Made from thebaine, an opium alkaloid, it was found to be a partial mu agonist that binds very tightly. The injection Buprenex was approved in the US in 1981, and sublingual forms for opioid addiction followed in 2002. Because swallowed buprenorphine is largely destroyed by the liver, Purdue Pharma developed a seven-day skin patch, Butrans, approved by FDA in 2010 for chronic pain requiring long-term opioid treatment.", "timeline": [["1966", "Reckitt and Colman chemists synthesize buprenorphine."], ["1981", "FDA approves Buprenex injection."], ["2002", "Sublingual buprenorphine is approved for opioid use disorder."], ["2010", "FDA approves Butrans transdermal patch."]], "cell": "Buprenorphine is a partial agonist at mu-opioid receptors with very high affinity and slow dissociation. It activates mu receptors enough to relieve pain but produces a lower maximum effect than full agonists, especially on breathing (a ceiling effect). Through Gi/o proteins it lowers cAMP, opens potassium channels and closes calcium channels on pain neurons. Its tight binding means it can displace full agonists such as morphine and block their effects, which can precipitate withdrawal. It is also an antagonist at kappa-opioid receptors, which may reduce dysphoria. The patch delivers it slowly and steadily.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Steady partial mu agonism provides continuous relief of chronic pain."], ["Breathing", "Respiratory depression has a ceiling at analgesic levels but is still dangerous with CNS depressants or in children."], ["Heart", "It can prolong the QTc interval at higher patch strengths."], ["Gut", "Constipation and nausea occur but are often milder than with full agonists."], ["Skin", "Itching, redness and rash at the patch site are common."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises and rare hepatitis have been reported."]], "journey": "The patch releases buprenorphine through the skin over seven days. Blood levels rise slowly over the first one to two days and reach steady state by about day three. Heat from heating pads, fever or hot baths increases absorption. The liver metabolizes it by CYP3A4 to norbuprenorphine and by glucuronidation, and most leaves in feces. After removal, levels fall by about half within 12 hours, with an apparent half-life of about a day.", "sidefx": "Mu partial agonism explains nausea, constipation, dizziness and sleepiness. High receptor affinity means it can trigger withdrawal in people on full agonists and makes naloxone reversal harder: larger, repeated amounts of naloxone may be needed. QTc prolongation is a labeled risk. Accidental exposure of a child to a patch can be fatal, which is part of the boxed warning. Heat on the patch can raise absorption and cause overdose.", "fun": "Buprenorphine binds the mu receptor so tightly that it can take hours to dissociate, which is why naloxone has a hard time knocking it off.", "src": ["Butrans (buprenorphine transdermal system) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "morphine-extended-release": {"origin": "Morphine was isolated from opium by the German pharmacist Friedrich Serturner in the early 1800s, the first alkaloid ever isolated from a plant. He named it after Morpheus, the Greek god of dreams. Its structure was worked out by Robert Robinson in 1925, and Marshall Gates synthesized it in 1952. Immediate-release morphine wore off in a few hours, so people with chronic cancer pain needed frequent doses. Napp Pharmaceuticals in the UK, a sister company of Purdue, developed the Continus controlled-release system, and MS Contin was introduced in the US in 1987. It became a standard long-acting opioid for chronic severe pain.", "timeline": [["1804", "Friedrich Serturner begins isolating morphine from opium."], ["1925", "Robert Robinson determines morphine's structure."], ["1952", "Marshall Gates completes the first total synthesis of morphine."], ["1987", "MS Contin is introduced in the US."]], "cell": "Morphine is a full agonist at mu-opioid receptors, with weaker action at kappa and delta receptors. Mu receptors are Gi/o-coupled. Activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase, opens GIRK potassium channels that hyperpolarize neurons and closes presynaptic calcium channels, reducing release of glutamate and substance P from pain fibers. In the brain, it activates descending inhibitory pathways from the periaqueductal gray and rostral ventromedial medulla and changes the emotional experience of pain. Mu activation of reward pathways releases dopamine. Morphine also releases histamine from mast cells. The extended-release tablet uses a matrix that releases drug over 8 to 12 hours.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Mu activation blocks pain transmission and changes pain perception."], ["Brainstem breathing center", "Reduced sensitivity to carbon dioxide causes respiratory depression, the main danger."], ["Gut", "Slowed motility and increased sphincter tone cause constipation and can trigger biliary spasm."], ["Skin and vessels", "Histamine release causes flushing, itching, sweating and orthostatic hypotension."], ["Eyes", "Mu activation constricts the pupils (miosis)."], ["Hormones", "Long-term use can cause adrenal insufficiency and low sex hormones."]], "journey": "The tablet is swallowed whole and releases morphine slowly in the gut. Only about a quarter to a third reaches the blood because of first-pass glucuronidation in the liver. The liver uses UGT2B7 to make morphine-3-glucuronide, which is inactive for pain but can excite neurons, and morphine-6-glucuronide, which is an active analgesic. Both glucuronides leave in the urine and build up in kidney failure. Morphine's own half-life is a few hours, but the slow release keeps levels steady for 8 to 12 hours. Crushing or chewing the tablet releases the full dose at once.", "sidefx": "Mu agonism causes constipation, nausea, sedation and respiratory depression, which is worsened by CNS depressants and by crushing the tablet. Histamine release causes itching and low blood pressure. In kidney failure, buildup of morphine-3-glucuronide can cause myoclonus and seizures, and morphine-6-glucuronide can cause prolonged sedation. Long-term use leads to tolerance, dependence and opioid-induced hyperalgesia. Naloxone reverses toxicity but may need repeated dosing because the tablet keeps releasing drug.", "fun": "Morphine was the first active compound ever isolated from a plant, opening the field of alkaloid chemistry.", "src": ["MS Contin (morphine sulfate extended-release) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "oxycodone-extended-release": {"origin": "Oxycodone was first made in Germany in 1916. In 1995 FDA approved Purdue Pharma's OxyContin, an extended-release oxycodone tablet given every 12 hours. Purdue marketed it aggressively, including claims that its addiction risk was low, and it became one of the drugs most closely linked to the US opioid crisis. People learned to crush the tablets to release the full dose at once. In 2007 Purdue and executives pleaded guilty to federal charges of misbranding. In 2010 FDA approved a reformulated tablet made with a polyethylene oxide matrix that resists crushing and turns to a gel when wet, and in 2020 Purdue pleaded guilty again to federal charges.", "timeline": [["1916", "Freund and Speyer synthesize oxycodone."], ["1995", "FDA approves OxyContin."], ["2007", "Purdue pleads guilty to federal misbranding charges."], ["2010", "FDA approves reformulated, crush-resistant OxyContin."], ["2020", "Purdue pleads guilty to further federal charges."]], "cell": "Oxycodone is a full mu-opioid receptor agonist. Through Gi/o proteins, it inhibits adenylyl cyclase, opens postsynaptic potassium channels that hyperpolarize neurons and blocks presynaptic calcium entry, cutting the release of pain neurotransmitters in the spinal cord dorsal horn. In the brain it activates descending pain control pathways and reward pathways that release dopamine. Its CYP2D6 metabolite oxymorphone is more potent, but most of the effect comes from oxycodone. The extended-release tablet releases drug in two phases over about 12 hours.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Mu activation gives sustained relief of severe chronic pain."], ["Brainstem breathing center", "Mu activation depresses breathing, which can be fatal especially early in treatment or after dose increases."], ["Reward system", "Dopamine release causes euphoria and drives addiction."], ["Gut", "Slowed motility causes constipation and nausea."], ["Throat and gut", "The swollen gel tablet can cause choking or blockage if not swallowed with enough water."], ["Hormones", "Long-term use can suppress adrenal and gonadal hormones."]], "journey": "OxyContin is swallowed whole. It releases oxycodone in two phases, with an early peak at about 1 hour and a later peak at 3 to 5 hours, giving 12 hours of effect. Oral bioavailability is relatively high. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP3A4 to noroxycodone and partly by CYP2D6 to oxymorphone; CYP3A4 inhibitors can raise levels and cause overdose. Metabolites and some unchanged drug leave in the urine. Crushing or chewing defeats the extended release.", "sidefx": "Mu activation explains constipation, nausea, drowsiness, itching and respiratory depression. The boxed warnings cover addiction, life-threatening respiratory depression, accidental ingestion by children, neonatal opioid withdrawal syndrome, CYP3A4 interactions and risks with benzodiazepines. The abuse-deterrent matrix makes crushing harder, but it can still be misused by swallowing. Naloxone reverses toxicity but may need repeated dosing as the tablet keeps releasing drug.", "fun": "The 2010 OxyContin reformulation is studied as a turning point in the opioid crisis, as some people who misused it switched to heroin afterward.", "src": ["OxyContin (oxycodone hydrochloride extended-release) FDA label", "US Department of Justice press releases, 2007 and 2020"]}, "hydrocodone-ibuprofen": {"origin": "Ibuprofen was developed at Boots in Nottingham, England, by Stewart Adams and John Nicholson, who patented it in 1961. It reached the UK market in 1969 and the US in 1974. Adams famously took a dose himself to treat a hangover before a speech. Hydrocodone, synthesized in Germany in 1920, had long been paired with acetaminophen. Combining it instead with an NSAID added anti-inflammatory action. Knoll Pharmaceutical introduced the combination as Vicoprofen, approved by FDA in 1997. The Vicoprofen brand has been discontinued, but generic tablets remain, and the product is Schedule II like other hydrocodone combinations.", "timeline": [["1920", "Hydrocodone is synthesized in Germany."], ["1961", "Stewart Adams and John Nicholson patent ibuprofen at Boots."], ["1974", "Ibuprofen is approved in the US."], ["1997", "FDA approves Vicoprofen (hydrocodone and ibuprofen)."], ["2014", "Hydrocodone combinations are moved to Schedule II."]], "cell": "Hydrocodone is a full mu-opioid receptor agonist. Through Gi/o proteins it lowers cAMP, opens potassium channels and blocks presynaptic calcium channels, reducing pain transmission in the spinal cord and brain. CYP2D6 converts part of it to the more potent hydromorphone. Ibuprofen reversibly inhibits both cyclooxygenase-1 and -2, blocking conversion of arachidonic acid to prostaglandin H2. Less PGE2 and prostacyclin at injured tissue means less sensitization of pain nerve endings, less swelling and less fever. The combination treats pain both centrally (opioid) and at the injury (NSAID).", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Mu activation reduces pain transmission and perception."], ["Injured tissue", "COX inhibition lowers prostaglandins, reducing swelling and nerve sensitization."], ["Breathing", "Mu activation in the brainstem can cause respiratory depression."], ["Stomach", "Loss of protective prostaglandins raises the risk of ulcers and bleeding."], ["Kidneys", "Blocking renal prostaglandins can reduce filtration, cause fluid retention and raise potassium."], ["Heart and vessels", "NSAID effects raise blood pressure and the risk of heart attack and stroke."]], "journey": "Both parts are absorbed by mouth within about an hour or two. Hydrocodone is metabolized by CYP3A4 to norhydrocodone and by CYP2D6 to hydromorphone, with a half-life of about 4 hours. Ibuprofen is highly protein bound and oxidized mainly by CYP2C9, with a half-life of about 2 hours. Metabolites of both leave in the urine. The product is meant for short-term use.", "sidefx": "The opioid part causes sedation, constipation, nausea and respiratory depression, worsened by CNS depressants. The NSAID part causes stomach ulcers and bleeding, acute kidney injury, fluid retention and increased cardiovascular risk, all from blocked prostaglandins. Together they carry boxed warnings for both opioid risks and NSAID cardiovascular and GI risks. Naloxone reverses opioid effects; there is no antidote for ibuprofen, and care is supportive.", "fun": "Stewart Adams, co-inventor of ibuprofen, tested an early dose on his own hangover before giving a speech in Moscow.", "src": ["Hydrocodone bitartrate and ibuprofen tablets FDA label", "Adams SS. The propionic acids: a personal perspective, J Clin Pharmacol 1992"]}, "benzhydrocodone-acetaminophen": {"origin": "Benzhydrocodone was developed by KemPharm, a company in Iowa that designs prodrugs by chemically linking drugs to other molecules. KemPharm attached hydrocodone to benzoic acid, creating a compound that is inactive until enzymes in the gut cut it apart. The aim was to make the drug harder to misuse by snorting or injecting. FDA approved the combination with acetaminophen as Apadaz in February 2018 for short-term management of acute pain. FDA advisory committees did not support an abuse-deterrent label, and it was approved without one. It is a Schedule II drug.", "timeline": [["2010s", "KemPharm develops benzhydrocodone as a hydrocodone prodrug."], ["2018", "FDA approves Apadaz without abuse-deterrent labeling."]], "cell": "Benzhydrocodone itself has little activity. After it is swallowed, enzymes in the intestinal tract cleave the bond between hydrocodone and benzoic acid, releasing hydrocodone. Hydrocodone is a full mu-opioid receptor agonist that acts through Gi/o proteins to lower cAMP, open potassium channels and close presynaptic calcium channels, dampening pain signal transmission in the spinal cord and brain. Acetaminophen acts centrally by inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis at the COX peroxidase site and through serotonergic pathways, with little anti-inflammatory effect. The combination provides opioid and non-opioid pain relief.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Hydrocodone and acetaminophen act together to reduce acute pain."], ["Breathing", "Mu activation can cause respiratory depression, worsened by alcohol and sedatives."], ["Gut", "Slowed motility causes constipation and nausea."], ["Liver", "Acetaminophen in excess forms NAPQI, which can cause acute liver failure."], ["Skin", "Itching occurs, and acetaminophen rarely causes serious skin reactions."]], "journey": "After oral dosing, benzhydrocodone is converted in the gut to hydrocodone, which reaches its peak within about an hour and a half. Hydrocodone is metabolized by CYP3A4 to norhydrocodone and by CYP2D6 to hydromorphone, with a half-life of about 4 hours. Benzoic acid is conjugated with glycine to hippuric acid and excreted in urine. Acetaminophen is glucuronidated and sulfated, with a small CYP2E1 fraction forming NAPQI. Metabolites leave mainly in the urine.", "sidefx": "Side effects are those of hydrocodone: nausea, sleepiness, constipation, itching and respiratory depression, with boxed warnings for addiction, respiratory depression, CYP3A4 interactions and use with benzodiazepines. The prodrug design does not prevent misuse by swallowing. Acetaminophen can cause liver failure in overdose and rare serious skin reactions. Naloxone reverses opioid effects, and N-acetylcysteine treats acetaminophen overdose.", "fun": "The benzoic acid released from benzhydrocodone is the same compound used as a food preservative.", "src": ["Apadaz (benzhydrocodone and acetaminophen) FDA label", "FDA advisory committee briefing, 2016"]}, "tramadol-acetaminophen": {"origin": "Tramadol was synthesized in 1962 by the German company Grunenthal and launched in Germany in 1977. Ortho-McNeil brought it to the US as Ultram, approved in 1995, initially as an uncontrolled drug thought to have low abuse potential. The combination with acetaminophen, Ultracet, was approved in 2001 for short-term acute pain. After reports of misuse, dependence and overdose, the DEA placed tramadol in Schedule IV in 2014. FDA has also restricted its use in children because of the risk of breathing problems in ultra-rapid CYP2D6 metabolizers.", "timeline": [["1962", "Grunenthal synthesizes tramadol."], ["1977", "Tramadol is launched in Germany."], ["1995", "FDA approves tramadol (Ultram)."], ["2001", "FDA approves Ultracet."], ["2014", "DEA places tramadol in Schedule IV."]], "cell": "Tramadol is a racemic mixture with two kinds of action. The parent drug is a weak mu-opioid agonist, but CYP2D6 converts it to O-desmethyltramadol (M1), which binds mu receptors far more strongly and provides most of the opioid effect. Separately, the (+) enantiomer inhibits serotonin reuptake and the (-) enantiomer inhibits norepinephrine reuptake, strengthening descending inhibitory pain pathways from the brainstem to the spinal cord. Acetaminophen adds central COX inhibition and serotonergic effects. The opioid and monoamine actions together give moderate pain relief.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Mu activation by M1 and increased serotonin and norepinephrine reduce pain."], ["Brain excitability", "Tramadol lowers the seizure threshold, so seizures can occur even at usual doses."], ["Serotonin system", "Combining it with SSRIs, SNRIs, MAOIs or triptans can cause serotonin syndrome."], ["Breathing", "Mu activation can depress breathing, especially in ultra-rapid metabolizers."], ["Metabolism", "Low blood sugar and low sodium (hyponatremia) have been reported."], ["Liver", "Acetaminophen in overdose forms toxic NAPQI and can cause liver failure."]], "journey": "Both drugs are absorbed well by mouth, with effects within an hour. Tramadol is converted by CYP2D6 to active M1 and by CYP3A4 and CYP2B6 to inactive N-desmethyltramadol. M1 peaks about 3 hours after a dose. Tramadol and M1 have half-lives of about 6 to 7 hours. CYP2D6 poor metabolizers get less pain relief, and ultra-rapid metabolizers get more M1 and more risk. Acetaminophen is mainly glucuronidated and sulfated. Both leave in the urine.", "sidefx": "Mu activation causes nausea, constipation, dizziness and respiratory depression. Increased serotonin and norepinephrine explain sweating, serotonin syndrome and the lowered seizure threshold. Hypoglycemia and hyponatremia have been reported. Withdrawal can include both opioid symptoms and unusual symptoms such as anxiety and panic. Acetaminophen overdose damages the liver. Naloxone reverses opioid effects but may increase seizure risk in tramadol overdose; benzodiazepines treat seizures, and N-acetylcysteine treats acetaminophen toxicity.", "fun": "In 2013 researchers reported finding tramadol in the roots of an African tree, but later work showed it came from contamination, because farmers had given tramadol to their cattle.", "src": ["Ultracet (tramadol and acetaminophen) FDA label", "Kusari S et al. Angew Chem 2014"]}, "follitropin": {"origin": "Fertility drugs began with gonadotropins extracted from urine. In the 1950s Piero Donini at the Italian company Serono purified gonadotropins from the urine of postmenopausal women, and Bruno Lunenfeld used this product, Pergonal, to achieve a pregnancy in 1962. To get enough urine, Serono collected it from convents of nuns in Italy. Recombinant DNA technology later allowed pure follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) to be made in Chinese hamster ovary cells. Serono's follitropin alfa (Gonal-f) and Organon's follitropin beta (Follistim) were approved in the US in the late 1990s, giving consistent FSH without depending on urine collection.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Piero Donini purifies urinary gonadotropins at Serono."], ["1962", "Bruno Lunenfeld reports a pregnancy after gonadotropin treatment."], ["1990s", "Recombinant FSH (follitropin alfa and beta) is approved in the US."]], "cell": "FSH is a glycoprotein hormone with an alpha subunit shared with LH, hCG and TSH and a hormone-specific beta subunit. Recombinant FSH binds the FSH receptor, a Gs-coupled receptor on ovarian granulosa cells. Gs activates adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP and activating protein kinase A. This drives granulosa cells to multiply, rescues growing follicles from atresia, and induces aromatase, which converts androgens from theca cells into estradiol. Given in higher amounts than the natural cycle, FSH lets several follicles grow at once. In men, FSH acts on Sertoli cells in the testes to support sperm production, usually with hCG.", "body": [["Ovaries", "Multiple follicles grow and mature, producing more estradiol."], ["Uterus", "Rising estradiol thickens the endometrium in preparation for implantation."], ["Testes", "In men with hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, FSH supports Sertoli cells and sperm production."], ["Fluid balance", "Overstimulated ovaries can release vasoactive factors such as VEGF that cause ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome (OHSS)."], ["Abdomen", "Enlarged ovaries cause bloating, pain and a risk of ovarian torsion."]], "journey": "Follitropin is injected under the skin, usually daily with a pen. It is absorbed slowly, peaks after about half a day, and has a half-life of about a day or more, so levels build with repeated doses. It is broken down like the body's own glycoproteins and cleared mainly by the kidneys. Follicles grow over days, guided by ultrasound and estradiol levels, and then a separate hCG or GnRH agonist trigger completes oocyte maturation.", "sidefx": "The main risks come from overstimulation. Many follicles growing at once release VEGF and other factors that make vessels leaky, causing fluid shifts into the abdomen and lungs (OHSS), with risk of blood clots and kidney problems. Enlarged ovaries can twist (torsion). Multiple pregnancy is more likely when several eggs are released. Ectopic pregnancy and injection site reactions also occur. There is no antidote; OHSS is managed by withholding the trigger, fluid management and supportive care.", "fun": "Before recombinant FSH, a major supplier of fertility hormone was the urine of postmenopausal nuns in Italian convents.", "src": ["Gonal-f (follitropin alfa) FDA label", "Lunenfeld B. Historical perspectives in gonadotrophin therapy, Hum Reprod Update 2004"]}, "menotropins": {"origin": "Menotropins, also called human menopausal gonadotropin (hMG), are FSH and LH purified from the urine of postmenopausal women, whose pituitaries make large amounts of gonadotropins. In the 1950s Piero Donini at Serono in Italy developed the purification method, and the product Pergonal led to the first pregnancy in 1962 under Bruno Lunenfeld. Serono collected the urine from Italian convents. Pergonal became a mainstay of ovulation induction and early IVF. Today the main US product is Menopur from Ferring, a highly purified hMG.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Piero Donini develops urinary gonadotropin extraction at Serono."], ["1962", "The first pregnancy after Pergonal treatment is reported."], ["2000s", "Highly purified hMG (Menopur) becomes available in the US."]], "cell": "Menotropins supply both FSH and LH activity, with some LH activity coming from hCG present in the product. FSH binds Gs-coupled FSH receptors on granulosa cells, raising cAMP and driving follicle growth and aromatase expression. LH activity binds LH/hCG receptors on theca cells, stimulating androgen production. Granulosa cell aromatase converts those androgens into estradiol (the two-cell, two-gonadotropin model). Having both activities can support estradiol production, especially in women with low natural LH. Higher than physiologic FSH lets several follicles develop at once.", "body": [["Ovaries", "FSH and LH activity together drive multiple follicles to grow and make estradiol."], ["Uterus", "Rising estradiol thickens the endometrium before implantation."], ["Fluid balance", "Overstimulation can cause OHSS, with fluid shifting into the abdomen and chest."], ["Abdomen", "Ovarian enlargement causes bloating and pain and can lead to torsion."], ["Skin", "Injection site pain and swelling are common."]], "journey": "Menotropins are injected under the skin, usually daily. FSH levels rise within hours and build with repeated doses, with a half-life of roughly a day. The glycoproteins are broken down like natural hormones and cleared partly by the kidneys. Follicle growth is followed by ultrasound and estradiol levels, and a separate hCG trigger completes maturation.", "sidefx": "The side effects come from ovarian overstimulation: OHSS, ovarian torsion and multiple pregnancy. In OHSS, many follicles release VEGF and other factors that make vessels leak, leading to ascites, low blood volume, clots and kidney failure in severe cases. As a urine-derived protein product, it can cause allergic reactions, including rare anaphylaxis. Treatment of OHSS is supportive.", "fun": "Menotropins come from a natural source: after menopause, the pituitary makes very high levels of FSH and LH, which spill into the urine.", "src": ["Menopur (menotropins) FDA label", "Lunenfeld B. Hum Reprod Update 2004"]}, "choriogonadotropin-alfa": {"origin": "Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) was discovered in 1927 by German scientists Selmar Aschheim and Bernhard Zondek, who found that urine from pregnant women made the ovaries of immature mice mature. This became the first pregnancy test. For decades, hCG for fertility treatment was purified from pregnancy urine. Serono developed choriogonadotropin alfa, a recombinant hCG made in Chinese hamster ovary cells, and FDA approved it as Ovidrel in 2000. It is used as the 'trigger shot' that mimics the natural LH surge to complete egg maturation before IVF retrieval or timed intercourse.", "timeline": [["1927", "Aschheim and Zondek discover hCG and create a pregnancy test."], ["1930s", "Urinary hCG is used in medicine."], ["2000", "FDA approves recombinant hCG (Ovidrel)."]], "cell": "hCG shares its alpha subunit with LH, FSH and TSH, and its beta subunit is very similar to LH. It binds the LH/choriogonadotropin receptor (LHCGR), a Gs-coupled receptor on granulosa and theca cells of mature follicles. Rising cAMP mimics the natural midcycle LH surge. This restarts meiosis in the oocyte, which had been paused in prophase I, causes the cumulus cells to expand, and starts enzyme changes that rupture the follicle about 36 hours later. hCG also drives luteinization, turning the follicle into a corpus luteum that makes progesterone to support early pregnancy. Its longer half-life than LH keeps this signal going.", "body": [["Ovaries", "The final maturation of eggs and ovulation occur about 36 hours after the trigger."], ["Corpus luteum", "hCG sustains progesterone production to prepare and support the uterine lining."], ["Fluid balance", "hCG strongly drives VEGF release from many follicles, a key cause of OHSS."], ["Pregnancy test", "It can cause a false-positive pregnancy test for about 10 days after the injection."], ["Skin", "Pain and bruising at the injection site are common."]], "journey": "Choriogonadotropin alfa is given as a single subcutaneous injection. It is absorbed slowly, peaks about 12 to 24 hours later, and has a terminal half-life of about a day or more. It can stay detectable for about 10 days. It is cleared mostly by glycoprotein breakdown, with a small fraction excreted in urine.", "sidefx": "The biggest risk is OHSS, because hCG's long-lasting LH signal makes many follicles release VEGF, increasing vascular permeability and causing fluid shifts, ascites and possible clots. Using a GnRH agonist trigger instead can lower this risk in some IVF cycles. Multiple pregnancy and ectopic pregnancy are possible. Injection site pain is common. Treatment of OHSS is supportive.", "fun": "The first hCG pregnancy test, the Aschheim-Zondek test, involved injecting a woman's urine into immature female mice and checking their ovaries days later.", "src": ["Ovidrel (choriogonadotropin alfa) FDA label", "Aschheim S, Zondek B. Klin Wochenschr 1928"]}, "ganirelix": {"origin": "Gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) was isolated and its structure determined in 1971 by teams led by Andrew Schally and Roger Guillemin, who shared the 1977 Nobel Prize. Scientists then made many modified versions: agonists that suppress the pituitary after an initial flare, and antagonists that block it immediately. Early antagonists caused histamine release and allergic reactions, so later generations were redesigned to avoid this. Ganirelix is one of these third-generation antagonists. Organon brought it to market for IVF, and FDA approved it in 1999 to prevent premature LH surges during ovarian stimulation. Generic versions such as Fyremadel followed.", "timeline": [["1971", "Schally and Guillemin's teams determine the structure of GnRH."], ["1977", "Schally and Guillemin share the Nobel Prize."], ["1999", "FDA approves ganirelix acetate injection."]], "cell": "GnRH is released from the hypothalamus in pulses and binds GnRH receptors, which are Gq-coupled, on pituitary gonadotroph cells. Activation raises IP3 and calcium and triggers release of LH and FSH. Ganirelix is a synthetic decapeptide with several unnatural amino acids that binds the GnRH receptor tightly but does not activate it. As a competitive antagonist, it blocks natural GnRH right away, so LH (and, to a lesser degree, FSH) falls within hours with no initial flare. During ovarian stimulation, rising estradiol could otherwise trigger an early LH surge and premature ovulation. Blocking the receptor prevents this until the planned trigger, and the effect reverses within about two days after stopping.", "body": [["Pituitary", "LH release is quickly suppressed, preventing a premature LH surge."], ["Ovaries", "Follicles keep growing under injected FSH without ovulating too early."], ["Skin", "Redness, swelling and itching at the injection site are common."], ["Immune system", "Hypersensitivity reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, can occur."]], "journey": "Ganirelix is injected under the skin once daily, usually starting mid-way through stimulation. It is absorbed rapidly, peaking within about an hour or two, with high bioavailability. Its half-life is about 13 hours after one injection and a bit longer with repeated doses. Peptidases break it into fragments, which leave in feces and urine. Pituitary function recovers within about 48 hours after the last dose.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are local injection site reactions. Headache and nausea occur. OHSS, abdominal pain and multiple pregnancy reflect the overall stimulation cycle rather than ganirelix itself. Some prefilled syringe covers have contained natural rubber latex, a concern in latex allergy. Rare hypersensitivity reactions occur. There is no antidote; effects reverse quickly after stopping.", "fun": "GnRH antagonists work within hours, while GnRH agonists first cause a surge of LH and FSH (a flare) before suppressing the pituitary.", "src": ["Ganirelix acetate injection FDA label", "Schally AV et al. Science 1971"]}, "cetrorelix": {"origin": "Cetrorelix came out of the GnRH research of Andrew Schally, who shared the 1977 Nobel Prize for determining the structure of hypothalamic hormones. Schally's laboratory at Tulane University in New Orleans worked with the German company Asta Medica to design GnRH antagonists that block the pituitary without causing histamine release. Cetrorelix was one of these third-generation antagonists. Serono brought it to the US as Cetrotide, approved by FDA in 2000, to prevent premature ovulation during controlled ovarian stimulation. Generic versions are now available.", "timeline": [["1971", "Andrew Schally's team determines the structure of GnRH."], ["1977", "Schally shares the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."], ["2000", "FDA approves Cetrotide."]], "cell": "Cetrorelix is a synthetic decapeptide that competitively binds GnRH receptors on anterior pituitary gonadotrophs. These Gq-coupled receptors normally respond to pulsed GnRH by releasing LH and FSH. Because cetrorelix occupies the receptor without activating it, LH and FSH secretion falls within hours, with no initial flare like that seen with GnRH agonists. During ovarian stimulation, this prevents the premature LH surge that could cause early ovulation or luteinization. The suppression is dose-dependent and reverses after the drug is stopped.", "body": [["Pituitary", "LH and FSH release fall quickly, blocking a premature LH surge."], ["Ovaries", "Follicles can be stimulated with injected gonadotropins and timed for retrieval without ovulating early."], ["Skin", "Injection site reactions such as redness, swelling and itching are common."], ["Immune system", "Hypersensitivity reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, can occur."]], "journey": "Cetrorelix is injected under the skin, usually once daily during the later days of stimulation. It is absorbed quickly, peaking within about an hour, with high bioavailability. Its half-life is several hours after one injection and longer with daily dosing. Peptidases break it down, and fragments and some unchanged drug leave in feces and urine. Pituitary function recovers after the last dose.", "sidefx": "Side effects are mainly injection site reactions, headache and nausea. OHSS and multiple pregnancy are risks of the overall stimulation cycle. Rare hypersensitivity reactions occur, though newer antagonists release much less histamine than early GnRH antagonists. It is contraindicated in pregnancy. There is no antidote; effects fade as the drug is cleared.", "fun": "Early GnRH antagonists caused so much histamine release that they produced allergic-type reactions, which is why cetrorelix and ganirelix were redesigned with different amino acids.", "src": ["Cetrotide (cetrorelix acetate) FDA label", "Schally AV. Nobel lecture 1977"]}, "ethinyl-estradiol-norgestimate": {"origin": "This pill joins two lab-made hormones. Ethinyl estradiol was first made in 1938 by Hans Herloff Inhoffen and Walter Hohlweg at Schering AG in Berlin. Adding an ethinyl group to natural estradiol slowed its breakdown in the liver, so it worked well by mouth. The first birth control pill, Enovid, reached the US market in 1960. Early pills used progestins that also acted a bit like testosterone, which could worsen acne and lipids. Researchers at Ortho Pharmaceutical (Johnson & Johnson) developed norgestimate as a more selective progestin with low androgenic activity. The FDA approved Ortho-Cyclen and the triphasic Ortho Tri-Cyclen in 1992, and in 1997 Ortho Tri-Cyclen became one of the first pills labeled to treat acne. Generic versions such as Sprintec and Tri-Sprintec are now among the most used pills in the US.", "timeline": [["1938", "Inhoffen and Hohlweg at Schering AG synthesize ethinyl estradiol."], ["1960", "Enovid becomes the first FDA-approved oral contraceptive."], ["1992", "FDA approves Ortho-Cyclen and Ortho Tri-Cyclen with norgestimate."], ["1997", "Ortho Tri-Cyclen gains an FDA label for moderate acne."]], "cell": "Ethinyl estradiol binds estrogen receptors (ER-alpha and ER-beta) and norgestimate's active metabolites bind progesterone receptors. Both are nuclear receptors that change gene transcription. In the hypothalamus and pituitary, steady progestin plus estrogen exert negative feedback. Progestin slows the pulse frequency of GnRH and blocks the mid-cycle LH surge, so the dominant follicle never ruptures. Estrogen suppresses FSH, so fewer follicles start to grow, and it stabilizes the endometrium to reduce breakthrough bleeding. Progestin also thickens cervical mucus so sperm cannot pass, and makes the endometrium thin and less receptive. Norgestimate itself is a prodrug: it is converted to norelgestromin and norgestrel (levonorgestrel), which carry the progestin activity. Its low androgenic profile, plus the estrogen-driven rise in sex hormone binding globulin (SHBG) that lowers free testosterone, explains the benefit for acne.", "body": [["Hypothalamus and pituitary", "Feedback from both hormones lowers FSH and blocks the LH surge, so ovulation does not happen."], ["Ovaries", "Follicles stay small and quiet, which lowers the risk of ovarian cysts and, over years, ovarian cancer."], ["Uterus and cervix", "The lining stays thin, periods are lighter and more regular, and thick cervical mucus blocks sperm."], ["Skin", "More SHBG and less free testosterone reduce oil production, which helps acne."], ["Liver", "Ethinyl estradiol strongly stimulates the liver to make clotting factors, angiotensinogen and binding proteins, which explains clot and blood pressure risks."], ["Blood vessels", "More clotting factors and less natural anticoagulant activity raise the risk of venous clots, stroke and heart attack, especially in smokers over 35."]], "journey": "Both hormones are swallowed and absorbed well from the gut. Ethinyl estradiol peaks in about one to two hours, is partly broken down in the gut wall and liver by CYP3A4 and sulfation, and recycles through bile and the gut. Norgestimate is quickly deacetylated to norelgestromin and then to norgestrel, which binds SHBG and albumin and lasts one to two days. Ethinyl estradiol lasts about 15 to 20 hours. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. Daily dosing at the same time keeps levels steady. Drugs that induce CYP3A4, such as rifampin and some antiseizure drugs, can lower levels and cause pill failure.", "sidefx": "Nausea, breast tenderness and headache come mostly from estrogen acting on the brain and breast tissue. Breakthrough bleeding happens while the endometrium adjusts to a thin, steady state. Serious risks trace to the liver: ethinyl estradiol increases clotting factors, so deep vein thrombosis, pulmonary embolism, stroke and heart attack become more likely, especially with smoking, migraine with aura or older age. More angiotensinogen can raise blood pressure. Estrogen makes bile more saturated with cholesterol, which favors gallstones, and rarely drives liver adenomas. There is no antidote; the pill is stopped and clots are treated with anticoagulants.", "fun": "Norgestimate's main working metabolite is levonorgestrel, the same progestin found in many other pills and in emergency contraception.", "src": ["Ortho Tri-Cyclen and Tri-Sprintec FDA labels", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "segesterone-ethinyl-estradiol-ring": {"origin": "Annovera came from the Population Council, a nonprofit research group in New York that has worked on contraception since the 1950s. Its scientists developed segesterone acetate, also called Nestorone, a progestin related to 19-norprogesterone. It is strongly active on progesterone receptors but has almost no androgen or estrogen effect, and it is poorly active when swallowed because the liver breaks it down quickly, which makes it suited to delivery through the vaginal wall. The Council paired it with ethinyl estradiol in a soft silicone ring that a person can remove, wash and reuse. After years of development and clinical trials, the FDA approved Annovera in August 2018 as the first contraceptive that provides a full year of protection while controlled by the user without a clinic procedure.", "timeline": [["1950s", "The Population Council begins work on reproductive health research."], ["2018", "FDA approves Annovera, the first one-year reusable contraceptive vaginal ring."]], "cell": "Segesterone acetate binds the progesterone receptor strongly and selectively, with very little binding to androgen receptors and no binding to SHBG. Ethinyl estradiol binds estrogen receptors. Together they exert negative feedback on the hypothalamus and pituitary: GnRH pulses slow, the mid-cycle LH surge is blocked and FSH falls, so follicles do not mature and ovulation does not occur. The progestin thickens cervical mucus so sperm cannot pass and thins the endometrium. The estrogen stabilizes the lining, which limits irregular bleeding. Both hormones diffuse out of the silicone ring at a slow, near-constant rate across the vaginal epithelium, so blood levels stay steady instead of rising and falling like a daily pill.", "body": [["Hypothalamus and pituitary", "Steady hormone levels suppress LH and FSH, so the ovaries do not release an egg."], ["Vagina", "The ring sits against the vaginal wall and the hormones pass directly into nearby blood vessels; some people notice discharge or discomfort."], ["Cervix and uterus", "Thick mucus blocks sperm and the endometrium stays thin; the scheduled 7 days without the ring allow a withdrawal bleed."], ["Liver", "Ethinyl estradiol still reaches the liver through the blood and raises clotting factors and angiotensinogen, even though the drug skipped first-pass metabolism."], ["Blood vessels", "Higher clotting factor levels raise the risk of venous clots, stroke and heart attack, especially in smokers over 35."]], "journey": "The hormones leave the ring by diffusion and are absorbed through the vaginal lining into the blood. This bypasses the gut and the first pass through the liver. Levels rise in the first days of each cycle and then hold fairly steady for the 21 days the ring is in place. Segesterone acetate is cleared quickly from blood, with a half-life of about four and a half hours, but the ring keeps resupplying it. Ethinyl estradiol lasts about 15 hours. The liver breaks both down mainly with CYP3A4, and metabolites leave in urine and feces. One ring is used for 13 cycles, about a year.", "sidefx": "Headache, nausea and mood change reflect estrogen and progestin effects on the brain. Vaginal discharge and discomfort come from a foreign device resting against the mucosa, and the ring can slip out. Irregular bleeding happens as the lining adjusts. The serious risks come from estrogen acting on the liver: more clotting factors mean a higher risk of deep vein thrombosis, pulmonary embolism, stroke and heart attack. Toxic shock syndrome is a rare risk with any device left in the vagina, linked to toxin-producing Staphylococcus aureus. There is no antidote; the ring is removed and complications are treated directly.", "fun": "Segesterone acetate is weak when swallowed because the liver breaks it down so fast, which is one reason it was chosen for a ring.", "src": ["Annovera FDA label", "Population Council", "Goodman & Gilman's"]}, "drospirenone-only": {"origin": "Drospirenone was developed by Schering AG in Germany as a progestin that behaves like the body's own progesterone more closely than older progestins. It is a chemical relative of spironolactone, so it blocks mineralocorticoid (aldosterone) and androgen receptors as well as acting on progesterone receptors. It first reached the US in the combined pill Yasmin, approved in 2001. Older progestin-only pills used norethindrone and had to be taken within a narrow three-hour window each day. The company Exeltis developed Slynd, a drospirenone-only pill with 24 active days, to give an estrogen-free option with a more forgiving schedule. The FDA approved Slynd in 2019.", "timeline": [["2001", "Drospirenone reaches the US in the combined pill Yasmin."], ["2019", "FDA approves Slynd, a drospirenone-only pill without estrogen."]], "cell": "Drospirenone binds the progesterone receptor, a nuclear receptor that changes gene transcription. At the hypothalamus and pituitary this reduces GnRH pulse frequency and blocks the mid-cycle LH surge, so ovulation is suppressed. Unlike older progestin-only pills, which rely mostly on cervical mucus, drospirenone reliably stops ovulation. It also thickens cervical mucus so sperm cannot pass and makes the endometrium thin. Because it is a spironolactone analog, drospirenone also blocks mineralocorticoid receptors in the kidney's collecting duct, which promotes sodium and water loss and potassium retention, and blocks androgen receptors, which can help acne. With no estrogen, it does not drive the liver to make extra clotting factors in the way combined pills do.", "body": [["Hypothalamus and pituitary", "Progestin feedback blocks the LH surge so an egg is not released."], ["Cervix and uterus", "Thick mucus blocks sperm and the lining becomes thin; without estrogen support, bleeding is often irregular."], ["Kidneys", "Antimineralocorticoid action leads to mild sodium loss and potassium retention, so potassium can rise in kidney disease or with other potassium-raising drugs."], ["Skin", "Antiandrogen activity can lessen oily skin and acne, though some users still develop acne."], ["Blood and liver", "No estrogen means less stimulation of liver clotting factor production, which is why it is an option when estrogen must be avoided."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth, drospirenone is well absorbed and peaks in about two to six hours. It binds albumin but not SHBG. Its half-life is about 30 hours, which is longer than norethindrone, so a late pill is less likely to let ovulation return. The liver breaks it down, partly by CYP3A4, into inactive acid and sulfate metabolites that leave in urine and feces. Strong CYP3A4 inducers can lower levels and reduce effectiveness.", "sidefx": "Irregular or unscheduled bleeding is the most common problem, because a progestin alone keeps the endometrium thin and unstable without estrogen support. Headache, breast pain, nausea and mood changes reflect progestin effects on the brain and breast. High potassium can occur because drospirenone blocks aldosterone at the kidney, especially with kidney disease, ACE inhibitors, ARBs or potassium-sparing diuretics. If pregnancy does happen, progestin slowing of tubal movement makes an ectopic pregnancy relatively more likely. Clots are rare. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Drospirenone is derived from 17-alpha-spirolactone, the same chemical family as the diuretic spironolactone.", "src": ["Slynd FDA label", "Yasmin FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's"]}, "estetrol-drospirenone": {"origin": "Estetrol (E4) is a natural estrogen made only by the human fetal liver during pregnancy. It was discovered in 1965 by the group of Egon Diczfalusy at the Karolinska Institute in Sweden, but for decades nobody knew what to do with it. In the 2000s the Belgian company Mithra and its partners studied E4 as a drug and found it acts on estrogen receptors in a more selective way than estradiol or ethinyl estradiol, with less effect on liver proteins. Paired with Schering's progestin drospirenone, it became Nextstellis, marketed in the US by Mayne Pharma. The FDA approved it in April 2021, the first new estrogen in a US birth control pill in decades.", "timeline": [["1965", "Diczfalusy's group at the Karolinska Institute discovers estetrol in pregnancy."], ["2000s", "Mithra and partners begin developing estetrol as a medicine."], ["2021", "FDA approves Nextstellis, estetrol with drospirenone."]], "cell": "Estetrol binds estrogen receptor alpha in the nucleus and turns on gene transcription like other estrogens. It acts weakly on the receptor's membrane-initiated signaling pathway, and in some tissues it can even oppose estradiol, so it is described as a native estrogen with selective tissue action. Drospirenone binds the progesterone receptor and slows GnRH pulses and blocks the LH surge, so ovulation stops. Estetrol adds FSH suppression and stabilizes the endometrium. Drospirenone also thickens cervical mucus and, as a spironolactone analog, blocks mineralocorticoid and androgen receptors. In studies estetrol caused smaller rises in liver-made proteins such as SHBG, angiotensinogen and clotting factors than ethinyl estradiol, which is thought to matter for clot risk, although the label still carries the same clot warnings as other combined pills.", "body": [["Hypothalamus and pituitary", "Combined estrogen and progestin feedback lowers FSH and blocks the LH surge, preventing ovulation."], ["Uterus and cervix", "Thick mucus blocks sperm; estetrol keeps the thin lining stable so bleeding is more predictable."], ["Liver", "Estetrol changes liver protein production less than ethinyl estradiol in studies, but some rise in clotting factors still occurs."], ["Kidneys", "Drospirenone blocks aldosterone, causing mild sodium loss and potassium retention."], ["Blood vessels", "Like all estrogen-containing pills, it carries warnings for venous clots, stroke and heart attack, especially in smokers over 35."]], "journey": "Both parts are well absorbed by mouth and peak within about half an hour to two hours. Estetrol is not bound to SHBG and is broken down mainly by glucuronidation and sulfation rather than CYP enzymes, then leaves in urine. Drospirenone is partly handled by CYP3A4 and leaves in urine and feces. Their half-lives are long, about 27 hours for estetrol and 34 hours for drospirenone, which supports steady daily levels.", "sidefx": "Bleeding irregularities, headache, breast pain and mood changes reflect the shift in hormone signaling. Acne and weight gain are reported. Drospirenone's aldosterone blockade can raise potassium, which matters with kidney disease or potassium-raising drugs. Estrogen exposure carries a boxed warning about cigarette smoking and serious cardiovascular events, because estrogens raise clotting factor production and can cause deep vein thrombosis, pulmonary embolism, stroke and heart attack. Blood pressure can rise. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Estetrol has four hydroxyl groups, which is where the name and the E4 label come from (estradiol is E2, estriol is E3).", "src": ["Nextstellis FDA label", "Diczfalusy group, Karolinska Institute, 1965 estetrol reports", "Goodman & Gilman's"]}, "elagolix-estradiol-norethindrone": {"origin": "Elagolix was discovered by Neurocrine Biosciences in San Diego as a small-molecule blocker of the GnRH receptor that can be swallowed. Earlier ways to shut down ovarian hormones used injected GnRH agonists such as leuprolide, which first cause a flare before suppression. Neurocrine partnered with AbbVie, and elagolix alone was approved as Orilissa in 2018 for endometriosis pain. Fibroids grow under estrogen, so lowering estrogen reduces bleeding, but low estrogen also causes hot flashes and bone loss. AbbVie therefore combined elagolix with low-dose estradiol and norethindrone acetate as add-back therapy. The FDA approved this regimen as Oriahnn in May 2020 for heavy menstrual bleeding from uterine fibroids.", "timeline": [["1971", "Andrew Schally's lab describes the structure of GnRH, later honored with a Nobel Prize."], ["2018", "FDA approves elagolix alone as Orilissa for endometriosis pain."], ["2020", "FDA approves Oriahnn, elagolix with estradiol and norethindrone acetate add-back, for fibroid bleeding."]], "cell": "Elagolix is a nonpeptide competitive antagonist of the GnRH receptor, a Gq-coupled receptor on pituitary gonadotroph cells. Blocking it stops GnRH from triggering phospholipase C, IP3 and calcium signaling, so the pituitary releases less LH and FSH within hours, with no initial flare. Lower gonadotropins mean the ovaries make less estradiol and progesterone. Uterine fibroids (leiomyomas) depend on these hormones, so bleeding drops. The add-back estradiol binds estrogen receptors to keep levels in a range that protects bone and limits hot flashes, while norethindrone acetate, a progestin, protects the endometrium from unopposed estrogen. The goal is a middle zone of estrogen exposure: low enough to calm fibroid bleeding, high enough to limit side effects.", "body": [["Pituitary", "GnRH receptors are blocked, so LH and FSH fall quickly and stay low while the drug is taken."], ["Ovaries", "Less stimulation means less estrogen and progesterone production and usually no ovulation."], ["Uterus", "Fibroid-related bleeding decreases because the endometrium and fibroids get less hormone drive; the progestin keeps the lining from overgrowing."], ["Bones", "Lower estrogen can reduce bone mineral density over time, which is why add-back hormones are included and treatment length is limited."], ["Blood vessels", "The estrogen part brings the usual estrogen warnings for clots, stroke and heart attack."], ["Brain", "Lower estrogen can cause hot flashes, and mood changes including depression have been reported."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth twice daily, elagolix is absorbed quickly, peaks in about an hour and has a short half-life of about four to six hours, so hormone suppression reverses within days to weeks after stopping. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A, with minor CYP2D6, CYP2C8 and UGT pathways, and it is a substrate of OATP1B1 and P-glycoprotein, so many drug interactions exist. It leaves mainly in feces. The morning capsule also delivers estradiol and norethindrone acetate, which are absorbed and processed like other hormone therapy; norethindrone acetate is converted to norethindrone.", "sidefx": "Hot flushes and headaches come from lower estrogen. Irregular bleeding reflects shifting hormone levels in the lining. Bone loss happens because estrogen normally restrains bone breakdown by osteoclasts; this is why treatment duration is limited. The estrogen and progestin add-back carry a boxed warning for blood clots and cardiovascular events, since estrogen increases liver clotting factor production. Depression and suicidal thoughts have been reported. Liver enzyme rises can occur, and blood pressure can climb. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped.", "fun": "Unlike injected GnRH agonists, elagolix lowers hormones without a first-week flare because it blocks the receptor rather than overstimulating it.", "src": ["Oriahnn FDA label", "Orilissa FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "pneumococcal-polysaccharide-vaccine": {"origin": "Streptococcus pneumoniae was one of the first bacteria shown to have many capsule types, and early in the 20th century researchers learned that antibodies against a specific capsule sugar protect against that type. Crude capsule vaccines were tested in the 1940s but were abandoned when penicillin arrived. Robert Austrian later showed that pneumococcal disease still killed many people despite antibiotics, which revived interest. Working with Merck, he helped develop a 14-valent purified capsular polysaccharide vaccine licensed in the US in 1977. In 1983 it was expanded to 23 serotypes as Pneumovax 23, which remains in use for adults and high-risk people, alongside newer conjugate vaccines.", "timeline": [["1977", "A 14-valent pneumococcal polysaccharide vaccine is licensed in the US."], ["1983", "The 23-valent vaccine, Pneumovax 23, is licensed."]], "cell": "PPSV23 contains purified capsular polysaccharides from 23 serotypes. These long, repeating sugar chains are T-cell independent antigens. They cross-link many B-cell receptors at once on mature B cells, especially marginal zone B cells in the spleen, which triggers antibody production without help from CD4 T cells. The result is mostly IgM and IgG2 antibodies against the capsule. When pneumococci later enter the body, these antibodies coat the capsule and activate complement, so neutrophils and macrophages can engulf and kill the bacteria (opsonophagocytosis). Because there is no T-cell help, the response does not build strong memory B cells, repeat doses do not boost well, and children under 2 respond poorly since their marginal zone B cells are immature.", "body": [["Immune system", "B cells in the spleen and lymph nodes make serotype-specific antibodies over 2 to 3 weeks."], ["Lungs and blood", "Capsule antibodies help clear pneumococci before they cause bacteremia, meningitis or invasive pneumonia."], ["Spleen", "The spleen is the main site for clearing coated bacteria, which is why people without a working spleen are a key group for pneumococcal vaccines."], ["Injection site", "Pain, swelling and redness are common because the polysaccharides trigger local inflammation."]], "journey": "The vaccine is injected into muscle or under the skin. Antigen drains to regional lymph nodes and the spleen, where B cells respond. Antibody levels rise over about 2 to 3 weeks and peak in about a month. The polysaccharides are slowly broken down by immune cells. Protection lasts several years but wanes, especially in older adults. There is no drug-style metabolism or excretion.", "sidefx": "Pain, swelling, redness and warmth at the site come from local inflammation as innate immune cells respond to the injected sugars. Headache, tiredness and muscle aches reflect cytokines released as the immune system reacts. Large local reactions that look like cellulitis can occur, especially with repeat doses, likely because existing antibodies form immune complexes at the site. Severe allergic reactions are rare and are treated with epinephrine.", "fun": "There are more than 90 known pneumococcal capsule types, so vaccines target the ones that cause the most serious disease.", "src": ["Pneumovax 23 FDA label", "CDC Pink Book: Pneumococcal Disease", "Plotkin's Vaccines"]}, "typhoid-vaccine": {"origin": "Typhoid fever is caused by Salmonella enterica serovar Typhi. In 1896 Almroth Wright in England, and Richard Pfeiffer and Wilhelm Kolle in Germany, developed killed whole-cell typhoid vaccines, which were widely used in the military but caused strong reactions. In the 1930s researchers described the Vi (virulence) capsular antigen of S. Typhi. Two better tolerated vaccines followed. Ty21a, a live attenuated oral strain made by chemical mutagenesis in Switzerland in the 1970s, was licensed in the US as Vivotif in 1989. The injected Vi capsular polysaccharide vaccine, Typhim Vi, was licensed in the US in 1994. Both are used mainly for travelers to areas where typhoid is common.", "timeline": [["1896", "Almroth Wright and, separately, Pfeiffer and Kolle develop killed typhoid vaccines."], ["1970s", "The live attenuated oral Ty21a strain is developed in Switzerland."], ["1989", "Oral Ty21a vaccine (Vivotif) is licensed in the US."], ["1994", "Vi polysaccharide vaccine (Typhim Vi) is licensed in the US."]], "cell": "Typhim Vi contains purified Vi capsular polysaccharide from the Ty2 strain. Like other unconjugated polysaccharides, it is a T-cell independent antigen. Its repeating sugar units cross-link B-cell receptors and drive antibody production without CD4 T-cell help, producing mostly IgG and IgM against Vi. The Vi capsule normally hides S. Typhi from complement and phagocytes. Anti-Vi antibodies coat the bacterium, activate complement and allow phagocytes to kill it, which blocks invasion and bloodstream spread. Without T-cell help, memory is limited and booster doses are given every couple of years for continued risk. Vivotif works differently: the live weakened Ty21a strain briefly colonizes the gut and triggers mucosal IgA and cell-mediated immunity.", "body": [["Immune system", "B cells make anti-Vi antibodies over about 2 weeks, giving protection for about 2 years."], ["Gut", "Antibodies help stop S. Typhi from invading the intestinal lining and spreading to lymph tissue and blood."], ["Injection site", "Pain, tenderness and firmness at the site come from local inflammation."], ["Whole body", "Headache, malaise and muscle aches are mild and short-lived signs of immune activation."]], "journey": "Typhim Vi is injected into muscle. The antigen travels to lymph nodes and the spleen, where B cells respond. Antibodies appear in about 2 weeks and peak in 2 to 4 weeks. Protection lasts about 2 years. The polysaccharide is processed by immune cells, not metabolized like a drug.", "sidefx": "Pain, tenderness and induration at the injection site come from local innate immune activation. Headache, malaise and muscle aches reflect cytokine release. Fever is uncommon. Severe allergic reactions are rare and are treated with epinephrine. Because the Vi vaccine contains no living bacteria, it cannot cause typhoid.", "fun": "Mary Mallon, known as Typhoid Mary, was an apparently healthy carrier in early 1900s New York who was linked to many typhoid cases.", "src": ["Typhim Vi FDA label", "Vivotif FDA label", "CDC Yellow Book: Typhoid and Paratyphoid Fever"]}, "yellow-fever-vaccine": {"origin": "Yellow fever is a flavivirus spread by Aedes aegypti mosquitoes. In 1900 Walter Reed's US Army commission in Cuba confirmed mosquito transmission, building on Carlos Finlay's idea. In the 1930s Max Theiler and colleagues at the Rockefeller Foundation in New York passed the Asibi strain of the virus many times through mouse and chicken embryo tissues. By 1937 they had a weakened strain, 17D, that caused only mild infection but strong lasting immunity. Theiler received the 1951 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for this work. Substrains of 17D, including 17D-204 used in YF-Vax, are still the basis for yellow fever vaccines worldwide.", "timeline": [["1900", "Walter Reed's commission confirms that mosquitoes spread yellow fever."], ["1937", "Max Theiler and colleagues develop the live attenuated 17D strain."], ["1951", "Theiler receives the Nobel Prize for the yellow fever vaccine."]], "cell": "YF-Vax contains live attenuated yellow fever virus (17D-204). After injection under the skin, the virus infects dendritic cells and other local cells and replicates at a low level. Viral RNA is sensed by innate receptors such as TLR7, TLR8, RIG-I and MDA5, which trigger type I interferon and inflammatory signals. Dendritic cells carry viral antigen to lymph nodes, where they activate CD4 helper T cells, CD8 cytotoxic T cells and B cells. B cells, with T-cell help, make neutralizing antibodies, mostly against the viral envelope (E) protein, which block the virus from entering cells. Because the virus replicates, the immune system sees antigen over days in a natural way, which gives strong and very long-lasting immunity, often lifelong after one dose.", "body": [["Immune system", "A mild controlled infection produces neutralizing antibodies by about 10 days and long-lived memory B and T cells."], ["Whole body", "Headache, muscle aches and low fever 5 to 10 days later reflect interferon and cytokine release as the virus replicates."], ["Liver and organs (rare)", "In rare cases the vaccine virus spreads widely and causes multiorgan illness that resembles wild yellow fever (YEL-AVD), more often in older adults and people with thymus disease."], ["Nervous system (rare)", "Rarely, the virus or the immune response affects the brain or nerves (YEL-AND), causing encephalitis or Guillain-Barre syndrome."]], "journey": "The vaccine is injected under the skin. The live virus multiplies locally and in lymph nodes, and low levels can appear in the blood for a few days. Neutralizing antibodies appear by about 10 days and peak in 2 to 4 weeks. The immune system clears the virus completely. There is no drug-style metabolism or excretion. Immunity lasts decades and is likely lifelong for most people.", "sidefx": "Headache, muscle aches, low fever and soreness, often 5 to 10 days later, match the time the virus is replicating and interferon is high. The serious adverse events come from the virus being live. In YEL-AVD, the vaccine virus spreads to the liver and other organs and can cause organ failure; risk is higher in people over 60 and those with thymus disorders, because their early immune control is weaker. YEL-AND involves the nervous system. Because the virus is grown in chicken embryos, people with severe egg allergy can react. There is no antidote; care is supportive. The vaccine is avoided in severely immunocompromised people.", "fun": "Many countries require proof of yellow fever vaccination for entry, recorded on the International Certificate of Vaccination, often called the yellow card.", "src": ["YF-Vax FDA label", "CDC Yellow Book: Yellow Fever", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1951"]}, "japanese-encephalitis-vaccine": {"origin": "Japanese encephalitis is a mosquito-borne flavivirus first recognized in large outbreaks in Japan. The virus was isolated in Japan in the 1930s. Early vaccines were made from virus grown in mouse brains and inactivated; one of these was used in the US for travelers for years before being discontinued. In China, scientists developed the live attenuated SA14-14-2 strain. The Austrian company Intercell (now Valneva) took this strain, grew it in Vero cells and inactivated it to make a purified vaccine with an aluminum adjuvant. The FDA approved it as Ixiaro in 2009 for adults, later extending it to children.", "timeline": [["1930s", "Japanese encephalitis virus is isolated in Japan."], ["2009", "FDA approves Ixiaro, a Vero cell-derived inactivated JE vaccine."]], "cell": "Ixiaro contains whole Japanese encephalitis virus that has been killed with formaldehyde so it cannot replicate, adsorbed to aluminum hydroxide. The aluminum adjuvant holds antigen at the injection site and activates innate immune signals, including the NLRP3 inflammasome, which draws in antigen-presenting cells. Dendritic cells take up viral proteins and present them on MHC class II to CD4 helper T cells in lymph nodes. Those T cells help B cells make neutralizing IgG antibodies, mainly against the envelope (E) protein, which coats the virus and blocks it from attaching to and fusing with host cells. Because the virus does not replicate, two doses are needed to build adequate antibody levels and memory.", "body": [["Immune system", "After two doses, B cells make neutralizing antibodies that block the virus before it can reach the brain."], ["Brain", "Protection matters because JE infection can cause encephalitis with lasting neurologic damage or death."], ["Injection site", "Pain and tenderness come from local inflammation driven by antigen and the aluminum adjuvant."], ["Whole body", "Headache, muscle aches, fatigue and fever in young children reflect a short burst of immune signaling."]], "journey": "The vaccine is injected into muscle. The aluminum-bound antigen is released slowly and taken up by immune cells that travel to nearby lymph nodes. Antibodies appear mainly after the second dose and peak within a few weeks. Protection lasts at least 1 to 2 years after the primary series, and a booster extends it. The killed virus is broken down by immune cells.", "sidefx": "Injection site pain and tenderness come from the local inflammation that the adjuvant is designed to cause. Headache, muscle aches, fatigue and fever reflect cytokines from immune activation. Because the virus is inactivated, it cannot cause infection. Hypersensitivity reactions are rare, and anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine. The older mouse brain-derived vaccine had more allergic and neurologic reactions, which was one reason it was replaced.", "fun": "Pigs and wading birds are the main hosts that amplify Japanese encephalitis virus; people are dead-end hosts.", "src": ["Ixiaro FDA label", "CDC Yellow Book: Japanese Encephalitis", "Plotkin's Vaccines"]}, "cholera-vaccine": {"origin": "Cholera is caused by Vibrio cholerae, first seen by Filippo Pacini in 1854 and isolated in pure culture by Robert Koch in 1883. Cholera toxin causes massive watery diarrhea. In the 1980s Myron Levine, James Kaper and colleagues at the University of Maryland Center for Vaccine Development engineered a strain, CVD 103-HgR, by deleting the gene for the toxic A subunit of cholera toxin while keeping the harmless B subunit. A Swiss product based on this strain was used abroad. Later, PaxVax developed a US version, and the FDA approved it as Vaxchora in 2016, the only cholera vaccine licensed in the US.", "timeline": [["1854", "Filippo Pacini describes the cholera bacterium."], ["1883", "Robert Koch isolates Vibrio cholerae."], ["1980s", "University of Maryland researchers build the attenuated CVD 103-HgR strain."], ["2016", "FDA approves Vaxchora, a single-dose oral cholera vaccine."]], "cell": "Vaxchora contains live V. cholerae O1 classical Inaba bacteria that lack the gene for the cholera toxin A subunit, so they cannot cause the toxin-driven diarrhea. After being swallowed with a buffer that protects them from stomach acid, the bacteria briefly colonize the small intestine. M cells over Peyer's patches sample them and pass antigens to dendritic cells. These activate T cells and B cells in gut lymphoid tissue. B cells make IgA and serum vibriocidal antibodies against the O1 lipopolysaccharide, and antibodies against the cholera toxin B subunit. Vibriocidal antibodies, with complement, kill V. cholerae, and mucosal antibodies block bacteria from attaching to the gut lining. Serum vibriocidal antibody is the measured marker of protection.", "body": [["Gut", "The weakened bacteria briefly live in the small intestine, which trains local IgA defenses against cholera."], ["Immune system", "Vibriocidal antibodies rise by about 10 days, matching the timing of protection."], ["Stomach and bowel", "Mild abdominal pain, nausea, loss of appetite and loose stools reflect the bacteria colonizing the gut."], ["Whole body", "Tiredness and headache come from a short-lived immune response."]], "journey": "The dose is drunk as a suspension made with a bicarbonate buffer, which neutralizes stomach acid long enough for the bacteria to reach the small intestine. They multiply there briefly and are then cleared by the immune response. Vaccine bacteria are shed in stool for at least 7 days, so hand hygiene matters around close contacts. Antibiotics taken shortly before or after can kill the vaccine strain, and chloroquine may blunt the response, which is why the label sets timing rules.", "sidefx": "Tiredness, headache, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, poor appetite and diarrhea come from live bacteria growing briefly in the gut and the local inflammation that follows. Because the toxin A gene is deleted, severe cholera-type diarrhea does not occur. Shedding in stool means the strain could reach close contacts. Allergic reactions are rare and are treated with epinephrine.", "fun": "John Snow's 1854 removal of the Broad Street pump handle in London is a founding story of epidemiology and involved cholera.", "src": ["Vaxchora FDA label", "CDC Yellow Book: Cholera", "Levine MM, Kaper JB. Lancet 1988"]}, "chikungunya-vaccine": {"origin": "Chikungunya virus is an alphavirus spread by Aedes mosquitoes. It was first identified during an outbreak in what is now Tanzania in 1952; the name comes from a Makonde word that describes the stooped posture caused by severe joint pain. Large outbreaks spread across the Indian Ocean, Asia and the Americas in the 2000s and 2010s. Two US vaccines followed. Ixchiq, a live attenuated vaccine from Valneva, was approved in 2023, but the FDA suspended its US license in August 2025 over serious adverse events. Vimkunya, from Bavarian Nordic, was approved by the FDA in February 2025. It uses virus-like particles first developed at the US National Institutes of Health, which look like the virus but contain no genetic material.", "timeline": [["1952", "Chikungunya virus is identified during an outbreak in present-day Tanzania."], ["2013", "Chikungunya spreads in the Caribbean and the Americas."], ["2023", "FDA approves Ixchiq, a live attenuated chikungunya vaccine."], ["2025", "FDA approves the virus-like particle vaccine Vimkunya and suspends the Ixchiq license."]], "cell": "Vimkunya contains virus-like particles (VLPs) built from the chikungunya capsid protein and the E1 and E2 envelope glycoproteins. These proteins self-assemble into particles with the same outer shape as the virus but no RNA, so they cannot replicate. The VLPs are adsorbed to aluminum hydroxide adjuvant. The repeating, organized pattern of E2 and E1 on the particle surface strongly cross-links B-cell receptors, and dendritic cells take up the particles and present them to CD4 helper T cells. With T-cell help, B cells make neutralizing IgG, largely against E2, which blocks the virus from attaching to and entering host cells such as fibroblasts and joint tissue cells.", "body": [["Immune system", "Neutralizing antibodies appear within about 1 to 3 weeks after a single injection."], ["Joints", "Protection matters because chikungunya infection can cause severe joint pain that sometimes lasts months."], ["Injection site", "Pain at the site comes from local inflammation driven by antigen and adjuvant."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue, headache and muscle aches are short-lived signs of immune activation."]], "journey": "Vimkunya is injected into the deltoid muscle. The aluminum-bound particles are taken up by immune cells and carried to nearby lymph nodes, where the antibody response begins. Antibodies rise over about 3 weeks. The particles are broken down by immune cells. How long protection lasts is still being studied.", "sidefx": "Pain at the site, fatigue, headache and muscle aches come from normal innate immune activation and cytokine release. Because the VLPs contain no viral genetic material, they cannot cause infection, which is a key difference from the suspended live vaccine Ixchiq. Severe allergic reactions are rare and are treated with epinephrine.", "fun": "Chikungunya is usually translated as 'that which bends up', a description of people stooped over by joint pain.", "src": ["Vimkunya FDA label", "FDA safety communication on Ixchiq, 2025", "CDC Yellow Book: Chikungunya"]}, "bcg-vaccine": {"origin": "BCG stands for Bacillus Calmette-Guerin, named for Albert Calmette and Camille Guerin at the Pasteur Institute in Lille, France. Starting in 1908 they grew a strain of Mycobacterium bovis, the cattle tuberculosis germ, on a medium of potato, glycerin and ox bile, and passed it 230 times over about 13 years. The strain lost its ability to cause disease but still triggered immunity. It was first given to a human infant in 1921. Over time labs around the world kept different daughter strains. The TICE substrain used in the US came from work at the University of Illinois. BCG remains one of the most widely used vaccines in the world, but in the US it is used only for selected people with high, unavoidable TB exposure.", "timeline": [["1908", "Calmette and Guerin begin serial culture of Mycobacterium bovis in Lille."], ["1921", "BCG is first given to a human infant."], ["1930", "A contamination disaster in Lubeck, Germany, kills many infants, later traced to virulent TB in a vaccine batch, not BCG itself."]], "cell": "BCG is live attenuated Mycobacterium bovis. It lacks a genomic region called RD1, which encodes the ESX-1 secretion system that virulent M. tuberculosis uses to escape the phagosome and damage host cells. After percutaneous delivery, BCG is taken up by macrophages and dendritic cells. Mycobacterial cell wall components activate TLR2, TLR4 and other innate receptors. Dendritic cells present antigens to CD4 T cells, driving a Th1 response with interferon-gamma, which activates macrophages to kill intracellular mycobacteria. CD8 T cells and memory T cells also form. This cell-mediated immunity is the main defense against TB. BCG protects young children against severe forms such as TB meningitis and miliary TB better than it protects adults against lung TB.", "body": [["Skin", "A papule forms, becomes a pustule and then a small ulcer that heals with a scar over weeks."], ["Lymph nodes", "Nearby nodes may swell as immune cells respond; rarely they form pus (suppurative lymphadenitis)."], ["Immune system", "T cells learn to recognize mycobacteria, and the tuberculin skin test usually turns positive within 2 to 3 months."], ["Bones (rare)", "Rarely the bacteria seed bone and cause osteitis."], ["Whole body (rare)", "In people with weak cell-mediated immunity, BCG can spread and cause disseminated infection."]], "journey": "The reconstituted vaccine is placed on the skin and pushed in with a multiple puncture device. The bacteria live and multiply briefly in the skin and nearby lymph nodes, then the immune system controls and clears them over weeks to months. Tuberculin conversion appears in about 2 to 3 months. Protection varies and wanes over 10 to 20 years. There is no drug-style metabolism or excretion.", "sidefx": "The local papule, pustule, ulcer and scar are the expected result of live bacteria triggering inflammation in the skin. Swollen lymph nodes reflect bacteria draining to the nodes. Osteitis and disseminated BCG infection happen when the immune system cannot contain the live organism, which is why the vaccine is avoided in immunocompromised people. BCG is sensitive to standard TB drugs other than pyrazinamide, because M. bovis is naturally resistant to pyrazinamide, which matters if treatment is needed. BCG can also turn a tuberculin skin test positive, complicating TB screening, while interferon-gamma release assays are less affected.", "fun": "A separate form of TICE BCG is placed into the bladder to treat early bladder cancer by provoking a local immune attack.", "src": ["BCG Vaccine (TICE) FDA label", "CDC: BCG Vaccine Fact Sheet", "Plotkin's Vaccines"]}, "anthrax-vaccine": {"origin": "Anthrax is caused by Bacillus anthracis. In 1876 Robert Koch used anthrax to show that a specific microbe causes a specific disease. In 1881 Louis Pasteur publicly tested an attenuated anthrax vaccine on sheep at Pouilly-le-Fort, France. Modern human vaccines came from the US in the 1950s and 1960s, when researchers found that a cell-free filtrate from an avirulent strain could protect against the toxin. The current anthrax vaccine adsorbed (AVA) was licensed in 1970 and later sold as BioThrax by Emergent BioSolutions. In 2023 the FDA approved Cyfendus, an adjuvanted version for use after exposure together with antibiotics.", "timeline": [["1876", "Robert Koch links Bacillus anthracis to anthrax."], ["1881", "Pasteur demonstrates an anthrax vaccine in sheep at Pouilly-le-Fort."], ["1970", "Anthrax vaccine adsorbed is licensed in the US."], ["2001", "Anthrax letters in the US raise interest in post-exposure vaccination."], ["2023", "FDA approves Cyfendus for post-exposure prophylaxis with antibiotics."]], "cell": "Anthrax toxin has three parts: protective antigen (PA), lethal factor and edema factor. PA binds receptors on host cells (such as ANTXR2), is cut by furin, and forms a pore that carries lethal factor and edema factor into the cell, where they disrupt signaling and kill immune cells. The vaccine is a cell-free filtrate rich in PA, adsorbed to aluminum hydroxide. Antigen-presenting cells process PA and activate CD4 helper T cells, which help B cells make IgG against PA. These antibodies block PA from binding its receptor and forming the pore, so the toxins cannot get into cells. Cyfendus adds an extra adjuvant (a CpG oligonucleotide that activates TLR9) to speed and strengthen the antibody response for post-exposure use.", "body": [["Immune system", "After 2 to 3 doses, B cells make anti-PA antibodies that neutralize anthrax toxin; boosters keep levels up."], ["Blood and tissues", "Neutralizing antibodies stop toxin from entering immune and vascular cells, which prevents the shock seen in inhalation anthrax."], ["Injection site", "Tenderness, pain, redness, swelling and limited arm motion are common because of the adjuvant and local inflammation."], ["Whole body", "Muscle aches, headache and fatigue reflect cytokine release after vaccination."]], "journey": "The vaccine is injected into muscle for pre-exposure use or under the skin for post-exposure use. Aluminum-bound antigen stays at the site and is slowly taken up by immune cells that travel to nearby lymph nodes. Antibodies build over the series. After exposure, antibiotics clear germinating spores while the vaccine builds antibodies against toxin from any spores that germinate later. The antigen is processed by the immune system, not metabolized like a drug.", "sidefx": "Pain, tenderness, redness, swelling and limited arm movement come from strong local inflammation caused by the aluminum adjuvant and protein antigen. Larger local reactions can occur, sometimes more with later doses as antibodies build up. Muscle aches, headache and fatigue reflect immune activation. Severe allergic reactions are rare and are treated with epinephrine. The vaccine contains no live bacteria and cannot cause anthrax.", "fun": "The name anthrax comes from the Greek word for coal, because of the black scab seen in skin anthrax.", "src": ["BioThrax FDA label", "Cyfendus FDA label", "CDC: Anthrax Vaccination"]}, "influenza-vaccine-high-dose": {"origin": "Influenza virus was first isolated from humans in 1933 in London. In the 1940s Thomas Francis Jr. and Jonas Salk at the University of Michigan helped develop inactivated influenza vaccines grown in fertilized chicken eggs, and the first was licensed in the US in 1945, initially for the military. Older adults respond weakly to standard flu shots because the immune system ages, a change called immunosenescence. Sanofi Pasteur reasoned that more antigen could overcome this. Fluzone High-Dose, with four times the hemagglutinin of a standard dose, was licensed by the FDA in 2009 for people 65 and older. A large randomized trial later showed it prevented more influenza than standard-dose vaccine in this age group. A quadrivalent version followed, and the product returned to a trivalent form after B/Yamagata viruses stopped circulating.", "timeline": [["1933", "Influenza virus is first isolated from humans."], ["1945", "The first inactivated influenza vaccine is licensed in the US."], ["2009", "FDA licenses Fluzone High-Dose for adults 65 and older."], ["2014", "A large trial shows better protection than standard-dose vaccine in older adults."]], "cell": "The vaccine contains split, formaldehyde-inactivated influenza viruses, standardized by their hemagglutinin (HA) content, for the season's A/H1N1, A/H3N2 and B strains. Dendritic cells and macrophages take up HA and neuraminidase and present peptides on MHC class II to CD4 helper T cells in lymph nodes. With T-cell help, B cells, including memory B cells from earlier flu exposures, make IgG against the HA head. These antibodies block HA from binding sialic acid receptors on airway cells, which prevents viral entry (measured as hemagglutination inhibition). In older adults fewer naive cells and weaker T-cell help blunt this response. Giving more antigen recruits more B cells and produces higher antibody titers. Because HA changes from year to year (antigenic drift), the strains are updated and the vaccine is repeated each season.", "body": [["Immune system", "Higher antigen content produces higher antibody levels in older adults within about 2 weeks."], ["Lungs and airways", "Antibodies block flu virus from infecting airway cells, which lowers the risk of influenza, pneumonia and hospitalization."], ["Injection site", "Pain, redness and swelling are more common than with standard-dose vaccine because more antigen causes more local inflammation."], ["Whole body", "Muscle aches, headache and malaise reflect cytokine release and fade within days."]], "journey": "The vaccine is injected into the deltoid muscle. Antigen is taken up by immune cells and carried to nearby lymph nodes. Antibodies rise in about 2 weeks and peak by 2 to 4 weeks. Protection wanes over the season, and the circulating strains change, so the shot is given every year. The viral proteins are processed by immune cells, not metabolized like a drug. The virus is inactivated, so it cannot cause flu.", "sidefx": "Pain, redness and swelling at the site, along with muscle aches, headache and malaise, come from innate immune activation and are somewhat more frequent than with standard-dose vaccine because of the larger antigen amount. Fainting can happen around any injection. Guillain-Barre syndrome has been linked very rarely to influenza vaccines, likely through an immune cross-reaction with nerve components. Anaphylaxis is rare and is treated with epinephrine.", "fun": "Each year experts choose the vaccine strains months ahead, based on global surveillance, so egg-based production can be ready by fall.", "src": ["Fluzone High-Dose FDA label", "DiazGranados CA et al. N Engl J Med 2014", "CDC Pink Book: Influenza"]}, "tick-borne-encephalitis-vaccine": {"origin": "Tick-borne encephalitis (TBE) is a flavivirus infection spread mainly by Ixodes ticks, and sometimes by unpasteurized milk, in forested parts of Europe and Asia. The virus was first isolated in the Soviet Far East in the 1930s. In Austria, where TBE was common, Christian Kunz and colleagues developed an inactivated whole-virus vaccine with the company Immuno AG, introduced in the 1970s and later known as FSME-Immun. Mass vaccination in Austria sharply reduced cases. Pfizer acquired the product, and the FDA approved it for US travelers as TicoVac in August 2021, the first TBE vaccine licensed in the US.", "timeline": [["1930s", "Tick-borne encephalitis virus is first isolated in the Soviet Far East."], ["1970s", "An inactivated TBE vaccine developed in Austria comes into use."], ["2021", "FDA approves TicoVac for people 1 year and older."]], "cell": "TicoVac contains whole TBE virus (Neudorfl strain) grown in chick embryo cells and killed with formaldehyde, adsorbed to aluminum hydroxide. The adjuvant holds the antigen at the site and activates innate immune signals. Dendritic cells take up viral proteins, especially the envelope (E) protein, and present them to CD4 helper T cells in lymph nodes. Those T cells help B cells switch to IgG and form memory. Neutralizing antibodies against the E protein bind the virus and block attachment and membrane fusion, so the virus cannot infect cells or reach the brain. Because it does not replicate, a series of doses is needed to reach protective levels.", "body": [["Immune system", "Antibodies rise after the second dose, and a third dose gives longer protection."], ["Brain and meninges", "Protection matters because TBE infection can cause meningitis, encephalitis and lasting neurologic problems."], ["Injection site", "Pain and tenderness are common because of local inflammation from the adjuvant."], ["Whole body", "Headache, fatigue, muscle aches and fever in young children reflect a brief immune response."]], "journey": "The vaccine is injected into muscle. The aluminum-bound antigen is released slowly and carried by immune cells to lymph nodes. Antibodies appear after the second dose and peak within weeks after the second or third dose. Protection lasts at least 3 years after the primary series, and boosters are used for continued risk. The killed virus is broken down by immune cells.", "sidefx": "Pain and tenderness at the site, headache, fatigue and muscle aches come from normal innate immune activation and cytokine release. Fever is more frequent in young children, whose immune systems react strongly to new antigens. Because the virus is killed, it cannot cause infection. Allergic reactions are rare and are treated with epinephrine. The virus is grown in chick embryo cells, so severe egg allergy is a consideration.", "fun": "In parts of Europe TBE has been linked to raw milk or cheese from infected goats, not just tick bites.", "src": ["TicoVac FDA label", "CDC Yellow Book: Tick-Borne Encephalitis"]}, "pneumococcal-21-valent-conjugate-vaccine": {"origin": "Plain polysaccharide pneumococcal vaccines do not work well in young children and give limited memory. In the 1980s John Robbins and Rachel Schneerson at the US National Institutes of Health showed that linking a capsule sugar to a carrier protein made it a T-cell dependent antigen, first for Haemophilus influenzae type b. This conjugate idea led to pneumococcal conjugate vaccines, starting with a 7-valent vaccine in 2000. Those vaccines were designed around serotypes that cause disease in children. Merck designed Capvaxive (V116) for adults instead, choosing 21 serotypes, including several not found in earlier vaccines, that cause most invasive disease in adults. The FDA approved it in June 2024.", "timeline": [["1980s", "NIH researchers Robbins and Schneerson develop the conjugate vaccine concept."], ["2000", "The first pneumococcal conjugate vaccine, PCV7, is licensed in the US."], ["2024", "FDA approves Capvaxive, a 21-valent conjugate vaccine designed for adults."]], "cell": "Each of the 21 capsular polysaccharides is chemically linked to CRM197, a nontoxic mutant of diphtheria toxin. A B cell whose receptor recognizes a capsule sugar takes up the whole conjugate, digests the protein part and presents carrier peptides on MHC class II. CD4 helper T cells that recognize those peptides then give the B cell help through CD40 ligand and cytokines. This drives germinal center reactions, class switching to IgG, affinity maturation and memory B cells, which a plain polysaccharide cannot do. The resulting IgG coats the pneumococcal capsule, fixes complement and allows neutrophils and macrophages to engulf and kill the bacteria (opsonophagocytic activity).", "body": [["Immune system", "T-cell help produces high-affinity IgG and memory B cells against each of the 21 capsule types."], ["Lungs and blood", "Antibodies help clear pneumococci early, which lowers the risk of pneumonia, bacteremia and meningitis."], ["Spleen", "Opsonized bacteria are cleared by phagocytes in the spleen and liver."], ["Injection site", "Pain, swelling and redness come from local inflammation."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue, headache and muscle aches reflect brief cytokine release."]], "journey": "The vaccine is injected into the deltoid muscle. Antigen-presenting cells carry the conjugates to nearby lymph nodes, where germinal centers form. Antibodies rise within about 2 weeks and peak around a month. Memory B cells persist for years. The protein and sugar parts are broken down by immune cells, not metabolized like a drug.", "sidefx": "Injection site pain, swelling and redness, fatigue, headache and muscle aches come from innate immune activation and cytokines. Fainting can happen around any injection. Fever is uncommon. Severe allergic reactions, including to the diphtheria-derived carrier, are rare and are treated with epinephrine. The vaccine contains no live bacteria.", "fun": "Capvaxive includes serotypes such as 15A, 23B and 35B that were not covered by earlier pneumococcal vaccines.", "src": ["Capvaxive FDA label", "CDC: Pneumococcal Vaccine Recommendations", "Plotkin's Vaccines"]}, "meningococcal-abcwy-vaccine": {"origin": "Neisseria meningitidis was identified by Anton Weichselbaum in 1887. Serogroups A, C, W and Y have polysaccharide capsules that work well in conjugate vaccines. Serogroup B was harder, because its capsule sugar resembles a molecule on human nerve cells and does not trigger a useful response. Researchers turned to surface proteins instead, using a genome-based approach called reverse vaccinology, pioneered by Rino Rappuoli and colleagues. This led to the MenB vaccines Trumenba (Pfizer, based on factor H binding protein) and Bexsero (GSK, 4CMenB). Combining a MenACWY conjugate with a MenB component cut the number of shots. The FDA approved Pfizer's Penbraya in October 2023 and GSK's Penmenvy in February 2025.", "timeline": [["1887", "Anton Weichselbaum identifies Neisseria meningitidis."], ["2000", "Reverse vaccinology work begins to identify MenB protein antigens from the genome."], ["2014", "FDA approves the first MenB vaccine, Trumenba."], ["2023", "FDA approves Penbraya, a pentavalent MenABCWY vaccine."], ["2025", "FDA approves Penmenvy, a second MenABCWY vaccine."]], "cell": "The vaccine has two parts. The MenACWY part contains capsular polysaccharides from groups A, C, W and Y, each linked to a carrier protein (tetanus toxoid in Penbraya, CRM197 in Penmenvy). The carrier lets CD4 T cells help capsule-specific B cells, which produces IgG and memory. The MenB part contains protein antigens instead of sugar. In Penbraya these are two lipidated variants of factor H binding protein (fHbp). Meningococci use fHbp to grab human factor H and shut down complement on their surface. Antibodies against fHbp and against the capsules let complement assemble the membrane attack complex and lyse the bacteria. This serum bactericidal activity, measured with human complement, is the marker of protection.", "body": [["Immune system", "B cells make bactericidal antibodies against all five serogroups within 1 to 2 weeks."], ["Blood and meninges", "Antibodies with complement kill meningococci before they cause meningitis or septicemia, which can kill within hours."], ["Injection site", "Pain at the site is common, especially from the MenB protein component."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue, headache, muscle aches and chills reflect cytokine release."]], "journey": "The lyophilized MenACWY part is reconstituted with the liquid MenB part and injected into the deltoid. Immune cells carry the antigens to nearby lymph nodes. Antibodies rise within 1 to 2 weeks and peak in about a month. Protection against groups A, C, W and Y lasts several years; MenB antibodies wane faster. The antigens are processed by immune cells.", "sidefx": "Injection site pain, fatigue, headache, muscle aches and chills come from innate immune activation, and the lipidated MenB proteins are especially reactogenic. Fever and fainting can occur. Because the vaccine has no live bacteria, it cannot cause meningococcal disease. Severe allergic reactions are rare and are treated with epinephrine.", "fun": "The MenB capsule is avoided as a vaccine target because it chemically resembles polysialic acid on human nerve cell adhesion molecules.", "src": ["Penbraya FDA label", "Penmenvy FDA label", "CDC: Meningococcal Vaccine Recommendations"]}, "rsv-monoclonal-clesrovimab": {"origin": "Respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) is the leading cause of bronchiolitis and hospitalization in infants. A failed formalin-inactivated RSV vaccine in the 1960s made some infants sicker, which slowed vaccine work for decades. Passive protection came first: palivizumab, an antibody given monthly to high-risk infants, was approved in 1998. Later, structural work showed that the RSV fusion (F) protein has different shapes before and after fusion. This guided new antibodies. Merck developed clesrovimab, a human IgG1 antibody with a long half-life, so one shot could cover a whole RSV season. The FDA approved it as Enflonsia in June 2025 for infants entering their first RSV season.", "timeline": [["1956", "RSV is first isolated, from chimpanzees, and soon after from children."], ["1960s", "A formalin-inactivated RSV vaccine trial leads to enhanced disease in infants."], ["1998", "FDA approves palivizumab for RSV prevention in high-risk infants."], ["2025", "FDA approves clesrovimab (Enflonsia) as a single-dose infant RSV antibody."]], "cell": "RSV enters airway epithelial cells using its F glycoprotein, which changes shape from a prefusion to a postfusion form to fuse the viral and cell membranes. Clesrovimab binds a conserved site on F (antigenic site IV), which is present on both forms. Binding blocks the shape change needed for fusion, so the virus cannot enter cells or spread by fusing neighboring cells into syncytia. This is passive immunization: the infant receives ready-made antibody rather than making its own. Changes in the Fc region increase binding to the neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn) at acidic pH, so more antibody is rescued from breakdown inside cells and recycled to the blood, which extends its half-life to about 44 days and lets one dose last about 5 months.", "body": [["Lungs and airways", "Antibody in airway fluid and blood blocks RSV from infecting the small airways, lowering the risk of bronchiolitis and pneumonia."], ["Immune system", "Protection begins within days, without needing the infant's immature immune system to respond."], ["Injection site", "Redness and swelling at the thigh are the most common reactions."], ["Skin", "Rash can occur, likely as a mild immune reaction to the antibody."]], "journey": "Clesrovimab is injected into the anterolateral thigh. It is absorbed slowly from muscle into the lymph and blood, reaching peak levels in about a week, with protective levels within days. It spreads into tissue fluid, including the lungs. Like other antibodies, it is broken down by proteolysis in cells throughout the body, not by the liver or kidneys. FcRn recycling gives it a half-life of about 44 days, so one dose covers about 5 months.", "sidefx": "Redness and swelling at the injection site come from local reaction to a protein injection. Rash may reflect a mild immune response to the antibody. Because clesrovimab is a human protein, serious hypersensitivity is rare but possible, and anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine. It is used with caution in infants with bleeding disorders because any intramuscular injection can cause bleeding into muscle.", "fun": "An antibody shot like this gives immunity in days, but it fades over months because the infant's body is not making the antibody itself.", "src": ["Enflonsia FDA label", "CDC: RSV Immunization for Infants", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "smallpox-vaccine-acam2000": {"origin": "In 1796 Edward Jenner in England took material from a cowpox sore on milkmaid Sarah Nelmes and scratched it into the arm of a boy, James Phipps, who then resisted smallpox. The word vaccine comes from vacca, Latin for cow. Over time the virus used became vaccinia, a related orthopoxvirus whose exact origin is uncertain. A worldwide vaccination campaign led WHO to declare smallpox eradicated in 1980. The older US vaccine, Dryvax, was grown on calf skin. After 2001, concern about bioterrorism led the US to stockpile a modern version. Acambis cloned virus from Dryvax and grew it in Vero cell culture to make ACAM2000, which the FDA approved in 2007. In 2024 its label was expanded to include mpox.", "timeline": [["1796", "Edward Jenner inoculates James Phipps with cowpox material."], ["1980", "WHO declares smallpox eradicated."], ["2007", "FDA approves ACAM2000, replacing Dryvax."], ["2024", "FDA expands the ACAM2000 label to include mpox prevention."]], "cell": "ACAM2000 is live, replicating vaccinia virus. It is placed into the skin with a bifurcated needle, where it infects keratinocytes and replicates, forming a local lesion. Innate sensors detect the viral DNA and proteins, triggering interferon and inflammation. Dendritic cells carry viral antigens to lymph nodes and activate CD4 T cells, CD8 cytotoxic T cells and B cells. Neutralizing antibodies target vaccinia surface proteins, and T cells kill infected cells. Orthopoxviruses share many proteins, so immunity to vaccinia cross-protects against variola (smallpox) and mpox. A successful 'take', with a papule, vesicle and pustule at the site, shows the virus replicated and the immune system responded.", "body": [["Skin", "A papule appears at 3 to 4 days, turns into a pustule, then scabs and leaves a scar over 2 to 3 weeks."], ["Lymph nodes", "Nearby nodes swell as immune cells respond to the replicating virus."], ["Immune system", "Antibodies and T cells develop that protect against smallpox and mpox for years."], ["Heart", "Myocarditis and pericarditis can occur, mostly in young men, likely from immune-mediated inflammation."], ["Eyes", "Touching the site and then the eye can spread live virus and cause eye infection."]], "journey": "The vaccine is delivered by pricking the skin many times with a bifurcated needle. The live virus replicates in the skin, and the lesion contains infectious virus until the scab falls off, so the site is covered and contaminated dressings are handled as infectious waste. The virus usually stays local and is cleared by the immune system within weeks. There is no drug-style metabolism or excretion.", "sidefx": "Itching, local reaction, swollen nodes, fever and muscle aches come from live virus replicating and the immune response. Because the virus is live and shed from the site, it can spread to other body areas (autoinoculation), especially the eye, or to close contacts. Myocarditis and pericarditis carry a boxed warning. In people with weak cell-mediated immunity the virus can keep growing (progressive vaccinia), and in eczema it can spread widely on the skin (eczema vaccinatum); encephalitis and fetal vaccinia also occur. Vaccinia immune globulin intravenous (VIGIV) is used for certain serious complications, and the antiviral tecovirimat may be used under special access.", "fun": "Smallpox is the only human disease ever eradicated worldwide.", "src": ["ACAM2000 FDA label", "CDC: Smallpox Vaccine", "Jenner E. An Inquiry into the Causes and Effects of the Variolae Vaccinae, 1798"]}, "cysteamine-ophthalmic": {"origin": "Cystinosis is a rare inherited disease in which the amino acid cystine builds up inside lysosomes and forms crystals in the kidneys, eyes and other organs. In the 1970s researchers, including Jess Thoene and Jerry Schneider, showed that cysteamine, a simple aminothiol, could deplete cystine from cystinotic cells. Oral cysteamine (Cystagon) was approved in 1994 and changed the course of kidney disease. But oral cysteamine does not reach the cornea, which has no blood vessels, so corneal crystals kept growing and caused painful light sensitivity. Eye drops were developed, first through the US National Institutes of Health. The FDA approved Cystaran in 2012 and the more viscous Cystadrops in 2020.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Researchers show that cysteamine depletes cystine from cystinotic cells."], ["1994", "FDA approves oral cysteamine (Cystagon) for cystinosis."], ["2012", "FDA approves Cystaran cysteamine eye drops."], ["2020", "FDA approves Cystadrops, a more viscous cysteamine eye drop."]], "cell": "In cystinosis, mutations in the CTNS gene disable cystinosin, the transporter that normally moves cystine out of the lysosome. Cystine is poorly soluble, so it piles up and crystallizes, including in the cornea. Cysteamine enters cells and lysosomes and reacts with cystine in a thiol-disulfide exchange. This splits cystine into cysteine and a cysteine-cysteamine mixed disulfide. The mixed disulfide resembles lysine and leaves the lysosome through a cationic amino acid transporter (PQLC2), and free cysteine leaves through its own transporter. Over months, this lowers crystal load in the corneal cells, which reduces light sensitivity and pain.", "body": [["Cornea", "Crystal density falls slowly over months, which eases photophobia, pain and the risk of corneal erosions."], ["Eye surface", "The drops can sting and cause redness or irritation because cysteamine is a reactive thiol."], ["Vision", "Less scattered light from crystals can improve comfort and glare."], ["Rest of body", "Very little reaches the blood, so the drops do not treat kidney or other organ disease; oral cysteamine is still needed."]], "journey": "The drops are placed on the eye and act locally in the cornea. Cysteamine is quickly oxidized and loses activity, which is why drops are needed frequently through the day and why unopened bottles are kept frozen or cold. Only tiny amounts are absorbed into the blood. What is not absorbed drains through the tear ducts. There is no meaningful systemic metabolism or excretion to track.", "sidefx": "Light sensitivity, redness, eye pain and irritation come from the chemically reactive thiol drug touching the corneal surface, and from the disease itself. Headache may follow frequent dosing and eye strain. Visual field changes have been reported. There is no antidote; the drops are rinsed out or stopped if irritation is severe.", "fun": "Cysteamine has a strong sulfur smell, and people taking it by mouth can develop bad breath and body odor; the eye drops avoid that.", "src": ["Cystaran FDA label", "Cystadrops FDA label", "Gahl WA et al. N Engl J Med 2002 (cystinosis review)"]}, "olezarsen": {"origin": "Olezarsen came from Ionis Pharmaceuticals in California, a company built around antisense drugs, short strands of modified DNA that bind a specific messenger RNA. Apolipoprotein C-III (apoC-III) was known to slow the clearance of triglyceride-rich particles, and people born with loss-of-function APOC3 variants have low triglycerides. Ionis first made volanesorsen, an apoC-III antisense drug that worked but caused low platelets; it was not approved in the US but was approved in Europe. Olezarsen adds a GalNAc sugar cluster that steers the drug to liver cells, allowing much smaller doses. The FDA approved it as Tryngolza in December 2024 for familial chylomicronemia syndrome, the first approval of the drug.", "timeline": [["2008", "Studies of an Amish population link APOC3 loss-of-function variants to low triglycerides."], ["2019", "Volanesorsen, an earlier apoC-III antisense drug, is approved in Europe but not in the US."], ["2024", "FDA approves olezarsen (Tryngolza) for familial chylomicronemia syndrome."]], "cell": "Olezarsen is a gapmer antisense oligonucleotide with 2'-O-methoxyethyl (2'-MOE) wings and a central DNA gap. A GalNAc cluster on one end binds the asialoglycoprotein receptor (ASGPR) on hepatocytes, so the drug is taken up mainly by liver cells. Inside, it pairs with APOC3 messenger RNA. The DNA-RNA hybrid is recognized by RNase H1, which cuts the mRNA so apoC-III protein is not made. ApoC-III normally inhibits lipoprotein lipase and blocks the liver from clearing triglyceride-rich lipoproteins through receptors. With less apoC-III, lipoprotein lipase works better and the liver clears chylomicron and VLDL remnants faster, even in people who lack normal lipase pathways. Triglyceride levels fall, and so does the risk of pancreatitis.", "body": [["Liver", "Hepatocytes make much less apoC-III, which speeds up removal of triglyceride-rich particles."], ["Blood", "Triglycerides drop substantially over weeks of monthly dosing."], ["Pancreas", "Lower chylomicron levels reduce the risk of acute pancreatitis, a major danger of very high triglycerides."], ["Injection site", "Redness, swelling or pain are common reactions to the injected oligonucleotide."], ["Platelets", "Mild drops in platelet count can occur, but much less than with the earlier non-GalNAc drug."]], "journey": "Olezarsen is injected under the skin monthly. It is absorbed into the blood within hours, binds plasma proteins strongly and is quickly taken up by the liver through ASGPR. It is not broken down by CYP enzymes. Instead, nucleases slowly cut it into shorter pieces in tissues, and those pieces leave in the urine. Its tissue half-life is weeks, which allows once-monthly dosing.", "sidefx": "Injection site reactions come from local immune and inflammatory responses to the oligonucleotide. Joint pain has been reported. Platelet counts can fall mildly, a known class effect of 2'-MOE antisense drugs; GalNAc targeting keeps systemic exposure low, which helps limit this. Liver enzyme rises reflect drug concentrated in hepatocytes. Hypersensitivity reactions are rare. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped.", "fun": "The GalNAc sugar works like a postal code: the asialoglycoprotein receptor that reads it is found almost only on liver cells.", "src": ["Tryngolza FDA label", "Pollin TI et al. Science 2008", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "sutimlimab": {"origin": "Cold agglutinin disease is a rare autoimmune anemia in which IgM antibodies stick to red blood cells in cool parts of the body and turn on complement. For years there was no approved drug. Sutimlimab was developed from a humanized antibody against complement C1s, first by True North Therapeutics, which was acquired by Bioverativ; Sanofi then acquired Bioverativ in 2018 and completed development. The FDA approved sutimlimab as Enjaymo in February 2022, the first approved treatment for cold agglutinin disease. It was a proof that selectively blocking the classical complement pathway could stop a disease while leaving the other pathways working.", "timeline": [["2018", "Sanofi acquires Bioverativ, the developer of sutimlimab."], ["2022", "FDA approves sutimlimab (Enjaymo) for hemolysis in cold agglutinin disease."]], "cell": "In cold agglutinin disease, IgM autoantibodies bind the I antigen on red cells at cool temperatures in the fingers, toes and nose. IgM strongly binds C1q, which activates the C1 complex. C1s, a serine protease, then cleaves C4 and C2 to form the C3 convertase. C3b coats the red cells, and as they warm, IgM falls off but C3b stays. Liver macrophages recognize C3b and remove the cells (extravascular hemolysis); a smaller part proceeds to the membrane attack complex and intravascular hemolysis. Sutimlimab is a humanized IgG4 antibody that binds C1s and blocks it from cleaving C4. This stops the classical pathway at its start, so red cells are no longer coated with C3b. The lectin and alternative complement pathways keep working.", "body": [["Blood", "Hemolysis slows, so hemoglobin rises and transfusion needs fall within weeks."], ["Liver", "Fewer C3b-coated red cells are cleared by Kupffer cells, which is the main way red cells are destroyed in this disease."], ["Energy", "Fatigue and shortness of breath improve as hemoglobin rises and anemia corrects."], ["Immune system", "Classical pathway blockade can make some infections, especially with encapsulated bacteria, harder to clear."], ["Skin and fingers", "Raynaud phenomenon and acrocyanosis can still occur, because the drug does not stop red cells from clumping in the cold."]], "journey": "Sutimlimab is given by IV infusion, with loading doses followed by doses every 2 weeks. It stays mostly in the blood and tissue fluid. Like other antibodies, it is broken down into peptides and amino acids by cells throughout the body and is not cleared by the kidneys or liver enzymes. Part of its clearance comes from binding its target, so its half-life is concentration dependent and lasts weeks. Hemolysis returns after the drug is stopped.", "sidefx": "Infections, including urinary and respiratory infections, happen because the classical complement pathway helps fight bacteria; vaccination against encapsulated bacteria such as meningococcus and pneumococcus is recommended. Headache, high blood pressure and infusion reactions can occur. Raynaud phenomenon and acrocyanosis persist because agglutination in the cold is a direct IgM effect, not complement. Because the classical pathway helps clear immune complexes, lupus-like autoimmunity is a theoretical and reported concern. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped.", "fun": "People with cold agglutinin disease can notice symptoms worsen with cold drinks or winter weather, because the antibodies bind best in the cold.", "src": ["Enjaymo FDA label", "Roth A et al. N Engl J Med 2021", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "lumacaftor-ivacaftor": {"origin": "In 1989 teams led by Lap-Chee Tsui, John Riordan and Francis Collins identified the CFTR gene, whose defects cause cystic fibrosis. The most common mutation, F508del, makes a misfolded protein that the cell destroys before it reaches the surface. The Cystic Fibrosis Foundation funded drug screening at Aurora Biosciences, later part of Vertex Pharmaceuticals, in a model called venture philanthropy. Screening found ivacaftor, a potentiator approved alone as Kalydeco in 2012 for gating mutations, and lumacaftor, a corrector that helps F508del protein fold. Neither worked well alone for F508del, but together they gave a modest benefit. The FDA approved the combination as Orkambi in July 2015, the first treatment aimed at the basic defect in people with two copies of F508del.", "timeline": [["1989", "Tsui, Riordan and Collins identify the CFTR gene."], ["2012", "FDA approves ivacaftor (Kalydeco) for CF with the G551D mutation."], ["2015", "FDA approves lumacaftor-ivacaftor (Orkambi) for people homozygous for F508del."]], "cell": "CFTR is an ATP-gated chloride and bicarbonate channel in the apical membrane of epithelial cells in the airways, pancreas, gut, sweat glands and reproductive tract. The F508del mutation causes the protein to misfold in the endoplasmic reticulum, where quality control sends most of it to the proteasome. The little that reaches the surface opens poorly. Lumacaftor is a corrector: it binds the first membrane-spanning domain and stabilizes folding so more CFTR moves through the Golgi to the cell surface. Ivacaftor is a potentiator: it binds CFTR at the membrane and increases the time the channel spends open. More channels that open more often move more chloride and bicarbonate out of the cell. Water follows, so airway surface liquid and mucus become thinner and easier to clear, and sweat chloride falls.", "body": [["Lungs", "Thinner mucus and better clearance give a modest improvement in lung function and fewer pulmonary exacerbations."], ["Airways (early)", "Chest tightness and shortness of breath can occur when starting, especially with low lung function."], ["Sweat glands", "Sweat chloride drops within about 2 weeks, a marker that CFTR is working better."], ["Pancreas and gut", "Better chloride and bicarbonate secretion can help gut function, though most patients still need enzyme replacement."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise, and advanced liver disease can worsen."], ["Eyes", "Cataracts have been reported in children, so eye exams are done."]], "journey": "Both drugs are taken by mouth with fat-containing food, which greatly increases absorption. They peak in about 4 hours and are about 99 percent bound to plasma proteins. Lumacaftor is barely metabolized and lasts about 26 hours. Ivacaftor is broken down extensively by CYP3A and lasts about 9 hours. Most leaves in the feces. Lumacaftor is a strong CYP3A inducer, so it lowers ivacaftor levels and those of many other drugs, including hormonal contraceptives, which may become unreliable.", "sidefx": "Chest tightness and shortness of breath at the start are thought to reflect changes in airway surface and mucus as CFTR function shifts. Nausea, diarrhea and flatulence relate to effects on gut fluid secretion. Liver enzyme rises and, in people with cirrhosis, liver decompensation reflect drug handling by the liver. Blood pressure can rise. Cataracts in children have been seen with CFTR modulators. Many problems arise from lumacaftor's strong CYP3A induction, which lowers the levels of other drugs. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The Cystic Fibrosis Foundation's investment in Vertex was later sold for royalty rights worth more than 3 billion US dollars, which it put back into research.", "src": ["Orkambi FDA label", "Riordan JR et al. Science 1989", "Wainwright CE et al. N Engl J Med 2015"]}, "tezacaftor-ivacaftor": {"origin": "Tezacaftor was developed by Vertex Pharmaceuticals as a second-generation CFTR corrector after lumacaftor. Lumacaftor helped F508del protein fold, but it caused chest tightness and was a strong CYP3A inducer that weakened ivacaftor and many other drugs. Tezacaftor works at a similar site on CFTR but causes fewer respiratory side effects and fewer drug interactions. In trials, tezacaftor plus ivacaftor improved lung function in people with two copies of F508del and in people with one F508del copy plus a residual function mutation. The FDA approved it as Symdeko in February 2018. Tezacaftor later became part of the triple combination with elexacaftor (Trikafta), which helps many more people.", "timeline": [["1989", "The CFTR gene is identified."], ["2018", "FDA approves tezacaftor-ivacaftor (Symdeko)."], ["2019", "Tezacaftor is included in the triple combination elexacaftor-tezacaftor-ivacaftor."]], "cell": "CFTR is an apical chloride and bicarbonate channel in epithelial cells. F508del CFTR misfolds in the endoplasmic reticulum and is degraded, and what reaches the surface opens poorly. Tezacaftor is a type I corrector that binds the first membrane-spanning domain and stabilizes the protein during folding, so more CFTR is trafficked to the cell membrane. Ivacaftor is a potentiator that binds CFTR at the surface and increases its open probability. The extra channels move more chloride and bicarbonate out of the cell. Water follows by osmosis, so airway surface liquid rehydrates, mucus thins and cilia clear it better. For residual function mutations, which already reach the surface but conduct poorly, ivacaftor's potentiation plus extra protein from tezacaftor adds to the benefit.", "body": [["Lungs", "Better airway hydration improves lung function and lowers the rate of pulmonary exacerbations."], ["Sweat glands", "Sweat chloride falls within about 2 weeks as more CFTR works in the sweat duct."], ["Sinuses", "Sinus congestion is a reported side effect, even as mucus becomes thinner."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise, so they are checked during treatment."], ["Eyes", "Cataracts have been reported in children taking CFTR modulators."], ["Brain", "Headache and dizziness are among the more common reported side effects."]], "journey": "Both drugs are taken by mouth with fat-containing food, which raises absorption. Tezacaftor peaks in about 2 to 4 hours and ivacaftor in about 4 to 6 hours. Both are about 99 percent protein bound. The liver breaks them down mainly with CYP3A4 and CYP3A5, and they leave mostly in the feces. Tezacaftor's half-life is about 15 hours, with a long terminal phase from metabolites. Unlike lumacaftor, tezacaftor is not a strong enzyme inducer, but strong CYP3A inhibitors raise levels a lot, so the schedule is changed when they are used.", "sidefx": "Headache, nausea, sinus congestion and dizziness are the most common effects and are generally mild. Liver enzyme rises reflect metabolism in the liver, and rare severe liver injury has been reported. Cataracts have been seen in children taking CFTR modulators. Compared with lumacaftor, chest tightness and drug interactions from enzyme induction are much less of a problem. CYP3A inducers such as rifampin can drop levels so far that the drug no longer works. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Symdeko is taken as two different tablets: a combination tablet in the morning and plain ivacaftor in the evening.", "src": ["Symdeko FDA label", "Taylor-Cousar JL et al. N Engl J Med 2017", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "fluticasone-nasal": {"origin": "Fluticasone propionate was developed by Glaxo in the UK in the early 1980s as a topical corticosteroid designed to be very potent where it is applied but almost completely inactivated when swallowed. Its fluorine atoms and 17-carbothioate ester give it strong glucocorticoid receptor binding, and nearly all swallowed drug is destroyed on the first pass through the liver. In the US, Flonase nasal spray was approved in 1994 for allergic rhinitis. It became one of the most prescribed nasal steroids. In 2014 the FDA approved fluticasone propionate nasal spray for over-the-counter sale, and the related fluticasone furoate (Flonase Sensimist) also became available without a prescription.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Glaxo develops fluticasone propionate as a topical corticosteroid."], ["1994", "FDA approves Flonase nasal spray."], ["2014", "FDA approves fluticasone propionate nasal spray for over-the-counter use."]], "cell": "Fluticasone is a lipophilic glucocorticoid that crosses cell membranes in the nasal lining and binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor with very high affinity. The drug-receptor complex moves to the nucleus. There it represses the transcription factors NF-kappa B and AP-1 (transrepression), turning down genes for cytokines such as IL-4, IL-5 and IL-13, chemokines and adhesion molecules. It also turns on anti-inflammatory genes such as annexin-1 and MAP kinase phosphatase-1 (transactivation). Over days, the nose has fewer eosinophils, mast cells and T cells, less vascular leak and less mucus secretion. This explains why it controls congestion, sneezing, itching and runny nose, and why the full effect takes days to build rather than minutes.", "body": [["Nasal lining", "Fewer inflammatory cells and less swelling reduce congestion, sneezing, itching and runny nose."], ["Eyes", "Less nasal inflammation also lessens allergic eye symptoms through nasal-ocular reflexes."], ["Sinuses and nose", "Nosebleeds, dryness and burning occur because steroids thin the nasal mucosa a little and the spray irritates it."], ["Throat", "Some spray drips back and may cause throat irritation or local yeast infection."], ["Eyes (long term)", "Glaucoma and cataracts are listed warnings with long-term corticosteroid use."], ["Growth", "Children may grow slightly more slowly with long-term use, so the lowest effective dose is used."]], "journey": "Sprayed into the nose, most of the drug stays on the nasal lining. A small part is absorbed directly, and much is swallowed. The swallowed part is almost all destroyed by CYP3A4 in the gut wall and liver on its first pass, so oral bioavailability is under about 1 percent and blood levels are mostly undetectable. Absorbed drug is converted by CYP3A4 to an inactive 17-beta-carboxylic acid metabolite and leaves mainly in the feces. Effects start within 12 hours to several days and build over 1 to 2 weeks.", "sidefx": "Nosebleeds, nasal burning and dryness come from the spray's direct contact and from steroids reducing the turnover of nasal tissue; aiming away from the septum helps prevent septal injury. Local immune suppression explains nasal or throat yeast infection. Long-term steroid exposure carries warnings for glaucoma, cataracts and reduced growth in children. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors, especially ritonavir, block fluticasone breakdown so much that blood levels rise and can cause Cushing syndrome and adrenal suppression. There is no antidote; the drug is tapered or changed.", "fun": "Fluticasone is so thoroughly destroyed on first pass that swallowed drug adds almost nothing to blood levels.", "src": ["Flonase FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "budesonide-nasal": {"origin": "Budesonide was developed in the 1970s by the Swedish company Draco, part of Astra (now AstraZeneca). Chemists designed it to bind the glucocorticoid receptor strongly while being cleared quickly by the liver, so that drug absorbed into the blood would have less effect on the rest of the body. Unlike fluticasone and beclomethasone, budesonide has no halogen atoms. It was introduced first for asthma and allergic rhinitis in Europe, then as Rhinocort in the US in the 1990s. In 2015 the FDA approved budesonide nasal spray for over-the-counter sale as Rhinocort Allergy. Large pregnancy registries have studied budesonide more than other nasal steroids, which is why it is often chosen in pregnancy.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Draco (Astra) develops budesonide in Sweden."], ["1990s", "Budesonide nasal spray is marketed in the US as Rhinocort."], ["2015", "FDA approves budesonide nasal spray for over-the-counter use."]], "cell": "Budesonide enters nasal epithelial and immune cells and binds the glucocorticoid receptor. The complex moves into the nucleus, where it represses pro-inflammatory transcription factors such as NF-kappa B and AP-1, reducing production of cytokines, chemokines and adhesion molecules. It also increases transcription of anti-inflammatory proteins. Inside cells, budesonide forms reversible fatty acid esters, which act as a local reservoir that slowly releases active drug and supports once-daily dosing. Over days, fewer eosinophils, mast cells and lymphocytes enter the nasal lining, blood vessels leak less, and mucus glands calm down, so congestion, sneezing and runny nose improve.", "body": [["Nasal lining", "Allergic inflammation settles over days, easing congestion, sneezing, itching and runny nose."], ["Nose and throat", "Nosebleeds, irritation and sore throat come from direct contact and mild thinning of the mucosa."], ["Pregnancy", "Registry data on budesonide are the most reassuring among nasal steroids, which shapes its common use in pregnancy."], ["Eyes (long term)", "Glaucoma and cataracts are listed warnings with long-term corticosteroid exposure."], ["Growth", "Long-term use in children may slightly slow growth."]], "journey": "After spraying, most of the drug stays on the nasal lining, and some is swallowed. Budesonide that is swallowed or absorbed is broken down extensively by CYP3A4 in the liver on its first pass, to metabolites with weak activity. Its systemic half-life is about 2 to 3 hours. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. Symptom relief may start within about 10 hours to a day, with full effect in about 2 weeks.", "sidefx": "Nosebleeds, nasal irritation and cough come from the spray hitting the nasal lining and from steroids thinning it slightly over time. Local immune suppression allows Candida infection. Septal perforation is rare and linked to spraying toward the septum. Glaucoma, cataracts and slowed growth in children are class warnings for corticosteroids. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors can raise budesonide levels and add systemic steroid effects. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Budesonide's intracellular fatty acid esters act like a slow-release depot inside the very cells it treats.", "src": ["Rhinocort Allergy label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "beclomethasone-nasal": {"origin": "Beclomethasone was first made in the 1960s, and its dipropionate ester was one of the first potent corticosteroids that could be given topically to the airways with limited effect on the rest of the body. Glaxo in the UK introduced it as an asthma inhaler in the early 1970s and as the Beconase nasal spray soon after. This showed that steroids could treat airway inflammation locally, changing care for asthma and allergic rhinitis. In the US, Beconase AQ became a long-used aqueous spray. In 2012 the FDA approved Qnasl, a beclomethasone nasal aerosol from Teva that delivers a dry mist instead of a liquid spray.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Beclomethasone dipropionate is developed as a topical corticosteroid."], ["1972", "Glaxo introduces beclomethasone as an asthma inhaler in the UK."], ["2012", "FDA approves Qnasl, a dry beclomethasone nasal aerosol."]], "cell": "Beclomethasone dipropionate is a prodrug. Esterases in the nasal lining and lungs remove one propionate group to form beclomethasone 17-monopropionate, which binds the glucocorticoid receptor far more strongly than the parent. The drug-receptor complex enters the nucleus, where it suppresses NF-kappa B and AP-1 driven transcription of inflammatory cytokines, chemokines and adhesion molecules, and switches on anti-inflammatory genes. Over days this lowers the number of eosinophils, mast cells and T cells in the nasal mucosa and reduces vascular leak and mucus production, which relieves congestion, sneezing, itching and runny nose.", "body": [["Nasal lining", "Allergic inflammation decreases, easing congestion, sneezing, itching and runny nose."], ["Nasal polyps", "Beconase AQ is labeled to help prevent nasal polyps from coming back after surgery."], ["Nose", "Nasal discomfort and nosebleeds reflect local irritation and slight thinning of the lining."], ["Eyes (long term)", "Glaucoma and cataracts are listed warnings with long-term corticosteroid use."], ["Growth", "Long-term use in children can slightly slow growth."]], "journey": "Beclomethasone is sprayed or puffed onto the nasal lining, where esterases convert it to the active 17-monopropionate. Some of the dose is swallowed. The active form is cleared by CYP3A4 in the liver, with a systemic half-life of about 2.7 hours, and leaves mainly in the feces. Effects start in about 1 to 3 days and reach their peak in 1 to 2 weeks. The dry aerosol form leaves less liquid dripping down the throat or out of the nose.", "sidefx": "Nasal discomfort, nosebleeds and headache come from direct contact with the nasal mucosa. Ulceration and septal perforation are rare, linked to steroid-related thinning and spraying toward the septum. Local immune suppression allows Candida infection. Glaucoma, cataracts and slowed growth are steroid class warnings. Hypersensitivity reactions are rare. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Beclomethasone helped prove that inhaled and nasal steroids could replace many courses of oral steroids for airway disease.", "src": ["Qnasl FDA label", "Beconase AQ FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's"]}, "ciclesonide-nasal": {"origin": "Ciclesonide was discovered by the German company Byk Gulden (later Altana Pharma) as a corticosteroid prodrug that is activated mainly where it is needed. The idea was to send in an inactive molecule that local esterases in the airway convert to the active drug, des-ciclesonide, while the parent compound does little elsewhere. Development partners brought it to the US, where the FDA approved Omnaris nasal spray in 2006 for allergic rhinitis. Zetonna, a dry nasal aerosol form, followed in 2012. Ciclesonide is also sold as the asthma inhaler Alvesco.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Byk Gulden in Germany develops ciclesonide as a corticosteroid prodrug."], ["2006", "FDA approves Omnaris ciclesonide nasal spray."], ["2012", "FDA approves Zetonna, a ciclesonide nasal aerosol."]], "cell": "Ciclesonide itself binds the glucocorticoid receptor weakly. Esterases in the nasal epithelium remove its isobutyryl group to form des-ciclesonide, which binds the receptor about 100 times more strongly. Des-ciclesonide also forms reversible fatty acid conjugates inside cells, which hold it in the tissue and support once-daily dosing. Bound to the glucocorticoid receptor, it enters the nucleus and suppresses NF-kappa B and AP-1 driven inflammatory genes while turning on anti-inflammatory genes. This reduces eosinophils, mast cells and cytokines in the nasal lining, which calms congestion, sneezing, itching and runny nose over days. Very high plasma protein binding and rapid liver clearance limit effects on the rest of the body.", "body": [["Nasal lining", "Local activation calms allergic inflammation, easing congestion, sneezing and runny nose."], ["Nose", "Nosebleeds and nasal irritation can occur, though the hypotonic spray is designed to feel gentle."], ["Throat", "Nasopharyngitis is a reported side effect."], ["Eyes (long term)", "Glaucoma and cataracts are listed warnings for corticosteroids."], ["Growth", "As with other nasal steroids, long-term use in children can slightly slow growth."]], "journey": "After spraying, ciclesonide is converted in the nasal lining by esterases to des-ciclesonide. Very little reaches the blood, and what does is 99 percent or more bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks des-ciclesonide down with CYP3A4, and metabolites leave mainly in the feces. Data from the inhaled form suggest a systemic half-life of about 6 to 7 hours. Effects begin in 24 to 48 hours and peak over 1 to 2 weeks.", "sidefx": "Headache, nosebleeds and nasopharyngitis come from local irritation and steroid effects on the nasal lining. Ulceration and septal perforation are rare. Candida infection reflects local immune suppression. Glaucoma, cataracts and growth effects are corticosteroid class warnings, though very little ciclesonide reaches the blood. Hypersensitivity reactions are rare. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Ciclesonide is a prodrug that is mostly switched on in the airway itself, a design meant to keep its action local.", "src": ["Omnaris FDA label", "Zetonna FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's"]}, "ipratropium-nasal": {"origin": "Ipratropium descends from plant medicines. Atropine comes from deadly nightshade (Atropa belladonna) and related plants, and smoking Datura leaves was an old remedy for asthma. Atropine dries secretions but reaches the brain and causes many side effects. In the 1970s Boehringer Ingelheim in Germany made ipratropium by adding an isopropyl group to the nitrogen of an atropine-like molecule. This gave it a permanent positive charge, so it is poorly absorbed through membranes and stays where it is applied. It was first used as the Atrovent inhaler for lung disease, and the nasal spray was approved in the US in the 1990s for runny nose.", "timeline": [["1831", "Atropine is isolated from belladonna plants."], ["1970s", "Boehringer Ingelheim develops ipratropium bromide."], ["1990s", "FDA approves Atrovent nasal spray for rhinorrhea."]], "cell": "Glands in the nasal lining make watery mucus mainly in response to parasympathetic nerves that release acetylcholine onto M3 muscarinic receptors. M3 receptors are Gq coupled: they activate phospholipase C, raise IP3 and intracellular calcium, and drive gland secretion. Ipratropium is a competitive antagonist at muscarinic receptors (M1, M2 and M3). Blocking M3 on seromucous glands stops this signal, so the glands secrete less fluid. Because it is a quaternary ammonium compound with a permanent charge, it crosses membranes poorly, stays mostly on the nasal surface and barely enters the brain. It reduces runny nose but does little for congestion, sneezing or itching, which are driven by histamine and other mediators.", "body": [["Nasal glands", "Less acetylcholine signaling means less watery discharge within about 15 minutes."], ["Nasal lining", "Dryness, irritation and nosebleeds occur because the lining loses some of its normal moisture."], ["Mouth and throat", "Some drug reaches the throat and causes dry mouth or taste change."], ["Eyes", "If sprayed into the eyes, it blocks the pupil and focusing muscles, causing dilated pupils, blurred vision and, rarely, acute angle-closure glaucoma."], ["Bladder", "Rarely, systemic anticholinergic effects can worsen urinary retention, especially with prostate enlargement."]], "journey": "Sprayed into the nose, ipratropium acts on the surface. Because it carries a permanent charge, less than 20 percent of a nasal dose is absorbed into the blood. What is absorbed is partly broken down by ester hydrolysis in the liver and leaves in urine and feces, with a systemic half-life of about 1.6 hours. Its local effect starts in about 15 minutes and lasts about 4 to 8 hours.", "sidefx": "Nasal dryness, irritation and nosebleeds come from reduced gland secretion in the nose. Dry mouth and taste changes happen when spray drains to the throat. Eye exposure blocks muscarinic receptors in the iris sphincter and ciliary muscle, causing pupil dilation and blurred vision, and in people with narrow angles this can trigger acute angle-closure glaucoma. Urinary retention is rare because little drug is absorbed. Hypersensitivity can rarely cause angioedema or bronchospasm. There is no specific antidote for the mild effects; physostigmine is reserved for serious anticholinergic toxicity, which is very unlikely from nasal use.", "fun": "The name belladonna means beautiful lady; atropine-containing drops were once used to widen pupils for cosmetic effect.", "src": ["Atrovent Nasal Spray FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "olopatadine-nasal": {"origin": "Olopatadine was discovered by Kyowa Hakko Kogyo in Japan as an antihistamine related to doxepin's tricyclic structure. Alcon licensed it and first developed it as an eye drop, Patanol, approved in the US in 1996, which became one of the most used allergy eye drops. Alcon then developed a nasal spray form, Patanase, which the FDA approved in 2008 for seasonal allergic rhinitis. Olopatadine was later combined with the steroid mometasone in the nasal spray Ryaltris.", "timeline": [["1996", "FDA approves olopatadine eye drops (Patanol)."], ["2008", "FDA approves Patanase olopatadine nasal spray."]], "cell": "Histamine released from mast cells activates H1 receptors on nasal nerves, blood vessels and glands. H1 is Gq coupled: it raises intracellular calcium and turns on NF-kappa B. On sensory nerves this triggers itching and sneezing; on blood vessels it increases permeability and dilation, causing swelling and congestion; on glands it adds to secretion. Olopatadine is a selective H1 receptor inverse agonist that stabilizes the receptor in its inactive state, so these signals are blocked. It also stabilizes mast cells in lab studies, reducing release of histamine and other mediators. Applied in the nose, it acts within about 30 minutes because it does not need to change gene expression like a steroid.", "body": [["Nasal nerves", "Blocking H1 receptors on sensory nerves quickly reduces itching and sneezing."], ["Nasal blood vessels", "Less histamine-driven leak and dilation eases runny nose and some congestion."], ["Mouth", "A bitter taste is common because some spray drains to the back of the throat and tongue."], ["Nose", "Nosebleeds and, rarely, ulceration or septal perforation can occur from local irritation."], ["Brain", "Some people feel drowsy, though less than with older oral antihistamines."]], "journey": "Sprayed into the nose, olopatadine acts locally, but a fair amount is absorbed and swallowed, with blood levels peaking in 30 minutes to an hour. It is about 55 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes very little of it, and most leaves unchanged in the urine. Its systemic half-life is about 8 to 12 hours, which supports twice-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Bitter taste comes from the drug draining onto taste buds. Headache, nosebleeds and throat pain reflect local effects on the nasal and throat lining. Somnolence happens because some drug reaches the blood and a little crosses into the brain, where H1 receptors maintain wakefulness. Nasal ulceration and septal perforation are rare. Hypersensitivity is rare. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Olopatadine first became popular as an eye drop before it was turned into a nasal spray.", "src": ["Patanase FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's"]}, "phenylephrine-oral": {"origin": "Phenylephrine is a synthetic relative of epinephrine, made and patented in the 1930s. It became widely used as a nasal spray, eye drop and IV vasopressor. As an oral decongestant, it was reviewed and accepted in the FDA's OTC drug review of cold products that began in the 1970s. Its big moment came after the Combat Methamphetamine Epidemic Act of 2005 moved pseudoephedrine behind the pharmacy counter, because pseudoephedrine can be used to make methamphetamine. Many companies reformulated products with oral phenylephrine. However, studies found that oral phenylephrine works poorly because the gut and liver destroy most of it. In 2023 an FDA advisory committee concluded that oral phenylephrine is not effective as a decongestant, and in 2024 the FDA proposed removing it from the OTC monograph.", "timeline": [["1930s", "Phenylephrine is synthesized and patented."], ["1970s", "Oral phenylephrine is accepted in the FDA OTC review of cold products."], ["2006", "Pseudoephedrine moves behind the counter, and many products switch to oral phenylephrine."], ["2023", "An FDA advisory committee concludes oral phenylephrine is not effective as a decongestant."], ["2024", "FDA proposes removing oral phenylephrine from the OTC monograph."]], "cell": "Phenylephrine is a direct-acting, selective alpha-1 adrenergic agonist. Alpha-1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle are Gq coupled: binding activates phospholipase C, raises IP3 and intracellular calcium, and causes the muscle to contract. In the nose, constricting the large venous sinusoids in the turbinates reduces blood pooling and swelling, which opens the airway. Phenylephrine has little action on beta receptors and does not release norepinephrine from nerves. When given by mouth, most of the drug is inactivated by MAO-A and sulfation in the gut wall before it reaches the blood, which is the main reason its decongestant effect is weak in clinical studies.", "body": [["Nasal blood vessels", "Alpha-1 activation can shrink swollen nasal tissue, though by mouth this effect is small in studies."], ["Blood vessels", "Constriction of vessels elsewhere can raise blood pressure, which matters in hypertension."], ["Heart", "Higher blood pressure can trigger a reflex slowing of the heart rate; palpitations can still occur."], ["Brain", "Nervousness, restlessness, insomnia and headache are reported."], ["Bladder", "Alpha-1 receptors in the bladder neck and prostate contract, which can cause urinary retention."]], "journey": "Swallowed phenylephrine is absorbed but then heavily broken down in the gut wall and liver by MAO-A and sulfate conjugation. Only a small fraction, often cited as under about 40 percent and possibly far less, reaches the circulation as active drug. Its effect, if any, starts in 15 to 30 minutes and lasts about 4 hours, with a half-life of about 2 to 3 hours. Inactive metabolites leave in the urine.", "sidefx": "Nervousness, restlessness and insomnia reflect sympathetic stimulation. Raised blood pressure and palpitations come from alpha-1 constriction of blood vessels throughout the body. Urinary retention happens because alpha-1 receptors tighten the bladder outlet. With MAO inhibitors, phenylephrine is not broken down normally, which can cause a hypertensive crisis. A dangerous blood pressure rise is treated with an alpha blocker such as phentolamine.", "fun": "Phenylephrine works well as a nasal spray, where it acts directly on the nasal lining; the debate is about the pill.", "src": ["FDA Nonprescription Drugs Advisory Committee, September 2023", "FDA proposed order on oral phenylephrine, 2024", "Goodman & Gilman's"]}, "epinephrine-auto-injector": {"origin": "Epinephrine (adrenaline) is the hormone of the adrenal medulla. In 1901 Jokichi Takamine and his assistant Keizo Uenaka isolated it in pure form, and it was sold as Adrenalin by Parke-Davis. It soon became a treatment for asthma attacks and allergic reactions. The auto-injector came from military technology: Sheldon Kaplan developed a spring-loaded injector to deliver nerve agent antidotes to soldiers. That design was adapted for epinephrine, and the EpiPen was approved by the FDA in 1987, letting patients and families treat anaphylaxis in the moments before help arrives. Later devices include Auvi-Q, which talks users through the steps, and generic auto-injectors.", "timeline": [["1901", "Jokichi Takamine isolates epinephrine, sold as Adrenalin."], ["1970s", "Sheldon Kaplan develops a spring-loaded auto-injector for the military."], ["1987", "FDA approves the EpiPen."], ["2012", "FDA approves Auvi-Q, a voice-guided epinephrine auto-injector."]], "cell": "Epinephrine activates alpha-1, alpha-2, beta-1 and beta-2 adrenergic receptors. Alpha-1 receptors (Gq) on blood vessels raise intracellular calcium and constrict vessels, which raises blood pressure and reduces swelling of the airway and skin. Beta-1 receptors (Gs) on the heart increase cAMP, which raises heart rate and contraction force. Beta-2 receptors (Gs) on bronchial smooth muscle raise cAMP and activate protein kinase A, which relaxes the airways. Beta-2 signaling on mast cells and basophils also raises cAMP and reduces further release of histamine, tryptase and leukotrienes. This combination reverses the core problems of anaphylaxis: vasodilation and leaky vessels, airway swelling and bronchospasm, and continued mediator release.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 constriction raises blood pressure and reduces fluid leak, swelling of the lips, tongue and throat, and hives."], ["Airways", "Beta-2 relaxation opens narrowed bronchi and eases wheeze."], ["Heart", "Beta-1 stimulation increases heart rate and output, which supports blood pressure but can cause palpitations."], ["Mast cells", "Higher cAMP in mast cells slows further release of allergy chemicals."], ["Metabolism", "Epinephrine raises blood sugar and can lower potassium as it drives potassium into cells."], ["Brain and muscles", "Anxiety, tremor and headache come from widespread adrenergic stimulation."]], "journey": "The auto-injector pushes epinephrine into the thigh muscle, which has rich blood flow, so it is absorbed within minutes and peaks in about 5 to 10 minutes. Injecting into the thigh gives faster peaks than under the skin of the arm. In the blood, epinephrine is cleared in minutes by uptake into nerves and by the enzymes COMT and MAO in the liver and other tissues. Metabolites such as metanephrine and vanillylmandelic acid leave in the urine. Because the effect lasts only about 10 to 20 minutes, symptoms can return, which is why emergency care follows use.", "sidefx": "Anxiety, tremor, palpitations, pallor and headache come from broad adrenergic stimulation. Strong beta-1 effects raise heart oxygen demand, so arrhythmias and angina can occur, especially in people with heart disease. Very high levels, mostly from accidental IV injection or overdose, can cause severe hypertension, pulmonary edema, ventricular arrhythmias or brain bleeding. Injecting into a finger can cause intense local vessel constriction; this is sometimes treated with phentolamine. In anaphylaxis, no condition is considered an absolute reason to withhold epinephrine, per anaphylaxis guidelines.", "fun": "The British name adrenaline and the American name epinephrine both mean 'on the kidney', from Latin and Greek.", "src": ["EpiPen FDA label", "Takamine J. Adrenalin, the active principle of the suprarenal glands, 1901", "Goodman & Gilman's"]}, "sublingual-grass-pollen-extract": {"origin": "In 1873 the English physician Charles Blackley showed that grass pollen causes hay fever, partly by testing pollen on himself. In 1911 Leonard Noon and John Freeman at St Mary's Hospital in London reported that injecting small, rising amounts of grass pollen extract could protect against hay fever, the start of allergen immunotherapy. For a century this meant regular allergy shots. Researchers in Europe then found that holding allergen under the tongue could build tolerance with less risk. The French company Stallergenes developed Oralair, a tablet with standardized pollen from five grasses (sweet vernal, orchard, perennial rye, Timothy and Kentucky blue). The FDA approved Oralair in April 2014, the same month it approved ALK's Timothy grass tablet Grastek, bringing sublingual tablet immunotherapy to the US.", "timeline": [["1873", "Charles Blackley shows that grass pollen causes hay fever."], ["1911", "Noon and Freeman report the first pollen immunotherapy injections."], ["1980s", "Sublingual immunotherapy is studied in Europe as an alternative to shots."], ["2014", "FDA approves Oralair and Grastek sublingual grass pollen tablets."]], "cell": "In grass allergy, Th2 cells drive B cells to make IgE against grass pollen proteins, such as group 1 and group 5 allergens. IgE sits on mast cells and basophils, so pollen exposure cross-links it and releases histamine and other mediators. With daily sublingual exposure, allergen is taken up by oral dendritic cells (including Langerhans-type cells) that tend to promote tolerance. They carry it to nearby lymph nodes and favor regulatory T cells that make IL-10 and TGF-beta. Over months the response shifts away from Th2: specific IgG4 and IgA antibodies rise and act as blocking antibodies that catch pollen before it reaches IgE on mast cells, and mast cell and basophil reactivity falls. Clinical benefit grows over the first season and can persist after several years of treatment.", "body": [["Mouth and throat", "Itching, irritation and swelling under the tongue are common because the allergen meets mast cells right where the tablet dissolves."], ["Nose", "Sneezing, runny nose and congestion during grass season decrease as tolerance builds."], ["Eyes", "Allergic eye symptoms during grass season also improve."], ["Immune system", "Regulatory T cells and blocking IgG4 increase, which turns down the allergic response over months to years."], ["Esophagus", "Rarely, ongoing allergen exposure triggers eosinophilic esophagitis, with trouble swallowing or chest pain."], ["Airways", "Throat swelling and asthma flares can occur, so severe or unstable asthma is a contraindication."]], "journey": "The tablet dissolves under the tongue, and the allergen proteins contact the oral mucosa, where immune cells sample them. The rest is swallowed and digested like food protein. The proteins are not absorbed and metabolized like a drug; their effect comes from training the immune system. Treatment begins about 4 months before grass season and continues through it, and benefit builds over weeks to months.", "sidefx": "Oral itching, throat irritation, mouth and tongue swelling and ear itching come from the allergen activating IgE-coated mast cells in the mouth. These usually fade over the first weeks. More serious reactions, including laryngopharyngeal swelling, asthma flares and anaphylaxis, happen when the allergic response is stronger or the allergen spreads. The label carries a boxed warning for severe allergic reactions, and an epinephrine auto-injector is prescribed for use if one occurs. The first dose is observed in a clinic. Eosinophilic esophagitis reflects a chronic allergic response in the esophagus.", "fun": "Noon and Freeman's 1911 paper was published in The Lancet, and their method remains the basis for allergy shots.", "src": ["Oralair FDA label", "Noon L. Lancet 1911", "Middleton's Allergy"]}, "dust-mite-allergen-extract": {"origin": "People long knew that house dust triggered asthma and rhinitis, but not why. In 1964 Dutch researchers Reindert Voorhorst and Frits Spieksma showed that tiny mites living in house dust, Dermatophagoides pteronyssinus and related species, were the main source of the allergen. Mite proteins come mostly from their fecal pellets. Standardized mite extracts were then used for allergy shots. The Danish company ALK-Abello developed a sublingual tablet with extract of both D. farinae and D. pteronyssinus, standardized in SQ-HDM units. The FDA approved it as Odactra in March 2017, the first sublingual tablet for house dust mite allergy in the US.", "timeline": [["1964", "Voorhorst and Spieksma identify house dust mites as the main house dust allergen."], ["2017", "FDA approves Odactra, a house dust mite sublingual tablet."]], "cell": "House dust mite allergens include group 1 proteins such as Der p 1 and Der f 1, which are cysteine proteases, and group 2 proteins such as Der p 2. The proteases can loosen tight junctions between airway cells, and Der p 2 resembles a partner of TLR4, which helps explain why mites are such strong allergens. In mite allergy, Th2 cells drive IgE that arms mast cells. Daily sublingual dosing exposes oral dendritic cells, which favor tolerance, to these proteins. This promotes regulatory T cells that make IL-10 and TGF-beta, shifts the T-cell balance away from Th2, raises blocking IgG4 antibodies and lowers mast cell and basophil reactivity. Over months, mite exposure at home causes less nasal and eye inflammation.", "body": [["Mouth and throat", "Itching, irritation and swelling of the mouth and tongue occur because the tablet's allergen activates local mast cells."], ["Nose", "Year-round congestion, sneezing and runny nose from mites decrease over months of treatment."], ["Eyes", "Allergic conjunctivitis from mites can also improve."], ["Immune system", "Regulatory T cells and blocking antibodies grow, damping the allergic response."], ["Esophagus", "Rarely, eosinophilic esophagitis develops."], ["Airways", "Asthma flares or throat swelling can occur, so severe or unstable asthma is a contraindication."]], "journey": "The tablet dissolves under the tongue within seconds. The allergen proteins contact the oral mucosa, where immune cells take them up; the rest is swallowed and digested as protein. Nothing is metabolized like a conventional drug. Benefit builds over several months of daily dosing and continues with ongoing treatment, which is usually planned over years.", "sidefx": "Throat irritation, oral and ear itching, swelling of the mouth and tongue, and stomach upset come from allergen meeting IgE-armed mast cells in the mouth and gut. These are most common early. Serious reactions such as laryngopharyngeal edema, asthma exacerbation and anaphylaxis are why the label has a boxed warning, the first dose is observed in a clinic and an epinephrine auto-injector is prescribed. Eosinophilic esophagitis reflects a chronic allergic process in the esophagus.", "fun": "Dust mites do not drink water; they absorb moisture from the air, which is why they thrive in humid bedrooms.", "src": ["Odactra FDA label", "Voorhorst R, Spieksma FTM et al. 1964", "Middleton's Allergy"]}, "grass-pollen-allergen-extract": {"origin": "Timothy grass (Phleum pratense) is one of the most common causes of hay fever in North America and Europe, and its allergens cross-react with those of many other northern grasses. Building on a century of pollen immunotherapy that began with Leonard Noon and John Freeman's 1911 injection work in London, the Danish company ALK-Abello developed a sublingual tablet with standardized Timothy grass pollen extract. It was approved in Europe as Grazax in 2006. In the US it was approved by the FDA in April 2014 as Grastek, initially marketed with Merck, for grass pollen allergic rhinitis in children and adults.", "timeline": [["1911", "Noon and Freeman report grass pollen immunotherapy injections in London."], ["2006", "ALK's Timothy grass tablet is approved in Europe as Grazax."], ["2014", "FDA approves Grastek, the Timothy grass sublingual tablet."]], "cell": "The tablet delivers Timothy grass allergens, especially Phl p 1 and Phl p 5. Because grasses of the Pooideae subfamily share similar proteins, tolerance to Timothy grass carries over to related grasses. In the mouth, dendritic cells take up the allergen in a tolerance-favoring setting and present it to T cells in nearby lymph nodes. Over months this builds regulatory T cells that release IL-10 and TGF-beta, reduces the Th2 cytokines IL-4, IL-5 and IL-13, and drives B cells to make allergen-specific IgG4. IgG4 binds pollen proteins before they reach IgE on mast cells and also blocks IgE-facilitated allergen presentation. The net result is fewer symptoms when grass pollen is inhaled.", "body": [["Mouth", "Mouth and lip itching or swelling are common as the allergen meets IgE-armed mast cells under the tongue."], ["Ears and throat", "Ear itching and throat irritation reflect shared sensory nerves and nearby mast cells."], ["Nose and eyes", "Seasonal sneezing, runny nose, congestion and itchy eyes decrease as tolerance builds."], ["Immune system", "Regulatory T cells and IgG4 rise, shifting the response away from allergy."], ["Esophagus", "Eosinophilic esophagitis is a rare complication of daily allergen exposure."]], "journey": "The tablet dissolves under the tongue, and the proteins stay in contact with the oral mucosa for about a minute before swallowing. The swallowed portion is digested as protein. There is no drug-style metabolism or excretion. Treatment starts at least 12 weeks before grass season, and multi-year courses give benefit that can last after stopping.", "sidefx": "Mouth itching, throat irritation, ear itching and tongue or lip swelling happen because the allergen is placed directly where mast cells sit. Systemic allergic reactions and anaphylaxis are possible because the patient is allergic to the very material in the tablet. The label has a boxed warning, the first dose is given under observation, and an epinephrine auto-injector is prescribed. Eosinophilic esophagitis reflects ongoing allergic inflammation in the esophagus.", "fun": "Timothy grass is reportedly named after Timothy Hanson, who promoted it as a hay crop in the American colonies in the 1700s.", "src": ["Grastek FDA label", "Noon L. Lancet 1911", "Middleton's Allergy"]}, "ragweed-allergen-extract": {"origin": "Short ragweed (Ambrosia artemisiifolia) is the main cause of late summer and fall hay fever in North America. A single plant can release enormous amounts of light pollen that travels far on the wind. In the 1960s researchers at Johns Hopkins, including Te Piao King and Philip Norman, purified the major ragweed allergen, then called antigen E and now known as Amb a 1, and used it to standardize extracts and study immunotherapy. Standardized ragweed extracts were used for allergy shots for decades. ALK-Abello developed a sublingual tablet standardized to Amb a 1 content, which the FDA approved as Ragwitek in April 2014.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Johns Hopkins researchers purify the major ragweed allergen Amb a 1 (antigen E)."], ["2014", "FDA approves Ragwitek, a short ragweed sublingual tablet."]], "cell": "Amb a 1 is a pectate lyase protein in ragweed pollen. In ragweed allergy, Th2 cells drive IgE against it, which arms mast cells in the nose and eyes. Each daily tablet places Amb a 1 under the tongue, where oral dendritic cells take it up in a setting that favors tolerance. They present it in nearby lymph nodes to T cells, promoting regulatory T cells that secrete IL-10 and TGF-beta. Over time Th2 activity falls, B cells make allergen-specific IgG4 that blocks pollen from cross-linking IgE, and mast cells and basophils become less reactive. This reduces sneezing, congestion and itchy eyes during ragweed season.", "body": [["Mouth and throat", "Throat irritation and mouth itching or swelling are common as the allergen meets local mast cells."], ["Ears", "Ear itching is common and likely relates to shared sensory nerves in the throat."], ["Nose and eyes", "Fall hay fever symptoms decrease once tolerance builds."], ["Immune system", "Blocking IgG4 and regulatory T cells increase over the treatment period."], ["Esophagus", "Rarely, eosinophilic esophagitis develops from daily allergen exposure."]], "journey": "The tablet dissolves under the tongue and the allergen contacts the oral mucosa for about a minute before swallowing. The swallowed part is digested as protein. There is no drug-style metabolism or excretion. Treatment starts at least 12 weeks before ragweed season and continues through it.", "sidefx": "Throat irritation, mouth and ear itching and oral swelling are local allergic reactions to the tablet's allergen. Systemic reactions and anaphylaxis can occur because the person is allergic to the material being given. The label has a boxed warning for severe allergic reactions, the first dose is observed in a clinic and an epinephrine auto-injector is prescribed. Eosinophilic esophagitis reflects chronic allergic inflammation in the esophagus.", "fun": "The genus name Ambrosia, the food of the Greek gods, is an odd fit for a plant that causes so much misery.", "src": ["Ragwitek FDA label", "King TP, Norman PS. Biochemistry 1962", "Middleton's Allergy"]}, "peanut-allergen-powder": {"origin": "Peanut allergy rose sharply in Western countries in the late 20th century and rarely goes away. Researchers studied oral immunotherapy, giving tiny, gradually increasing amounts of peanut protein to build tolerance, but home-made doses were hard to standardize. Aimmune Therapeutics, founded in 2011, developed a pharmaceutical-grade peanut powder with tightly controlled amounts of key peanut proteins. In January 2020 the FDA approved it as Palforzia, the first FDA-approved treatment for any food allergy, for children 4 through 17. The approval was later extended to children as young as 1. A separate line of research, the 2015 LEAP study, showed that early peanut introduction in infancy can prevent peanut allergy from developing.", "timeline": [["2015", "The LEAP study shows early peanut introduction prevents many cases of peanut allergy."], ["2020", "FDA approves Palforzia, the first FDA-approved treatment for a food allergy."], ["2024", "FDA extends Palforzia to children starting at age 1."]], "cell": "In peanut allergy, B cells make IgE against peanut proteins, mainly Ara h 1, Ara h 2 and Ara h 3, and that IgE sits on mast cells and basophils. Even small exposures cross-link IgE and trigger histamine, tryptase and leukotriene release, which can cause anaphylaxis. Oral immunotherapy starts below the reaction threshold and slowly increases. Repeated exposure is thought to make mast cells and basophils less responsive (desensitization), and over longer periods to promote regulatory T cells, lower Th2 signals and raise peanut-specific IgG4, which binds peanut proteins before they reach IgE. The goal is to raise the amount of peanut that causes a reaction, so accidental exposure is less dangerous. It is not a cure, and protection fades if daily dosing stops.", "body": [["Mouth and throat", "Itching and throat irritation are common because peanut protein contacts mast cells as it is eaten."], ["Stomach and gut", "Abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting are common, especially during up-dosing, as gut mast cells react."], ["Skin", "Hives and itching are signs of a mild allergic reaction to the dose."], ["Airways", "Cough and wheezing can occur, and asthma must be well controlled."], ["Esophagus", "Eosinophilic esophagitis can develop with daily allergen exposure."], ["Immune system", "Tolerance to peanut builds over months, raising the amount needed to cause a reaction."]], "journey": "The powder is mixed into soft food and eaten. Peanut proteins contact the mouth and gut lining, where immune cells sample them, and are then digested like any food protein. Nothing is metabolized like a drug. Desensitization builds over about 6 months of careful up-dosing, and daily maintenance is needed to keep it; missed days lower tolerance.", "sidefx": "Because the product is peanut protein given to people with peanut allergy, allergic reactions are expected. Mouth and throat itching, abdominal pain, vomiting, hives and cough come from IgE-armed mast cells reacting to the dose. Anaphylaxis can occur, which is why the product is available only through a REMS program, dose increases happen in a clinic and an epinephrine auto-injector is prescribed. Exercise, hot showers, illness and missed doses can lower the reaction threshold. Eosinophilic esophagitis reflects chronic allergic inflammation in the esophagus.", "fun": "Palforzia's powder is measured in peanut protein, and the full maintenance amount is roughly equal to one peanut.", "src": ["Palforzia FDA label", "Du Toit G et al. N Engl J Med 2015 (LEAP)", "PALISADE Group. N Engl J Med 2018"]}, "dexchlorpheniramine": {"origin": "Chlorpheniramine is an alkylamine antihistamine developed by Schering Corporation in the late 1940s, during the first wave of antihistamines that followed Daniel Bovet's antihistamine research in France in the 1930s and 1940s. Chlorpheniramine is a racemic mixture of two mirror-image forms. Researchers found that nearly all the H1-blocking activity comes from the dextrorotatory (S) form. Separating this form gave dexchlorpheniramine, which is about twice as potent as racemic chlorpheniramine, so less drug is needed for the same effect. It was marketed as Polaramine and is now available as Ryclora oral solution and in some prescription cough and cold liquids.", "timeline": [["1937", "Daniel Bovet and colleagues describe the first antihistamine compounds in France."], ["1940s", "Schering develops chlorpheniramine."], ["1957", "Bovet receives the Nobel Prize, partly for antihistamine discovery."]], "cell": "Histamine from mast cells activates H1 receptors, which are Gq coupled. This activates phospholipase C, raises IP3 and calcium and turns on NF-kappa B. The result is itching and sneezing through sensory nerves, leaky and dilated vessels causing hives and nasal swelling, and gland secretion. Dexchlorpheniramine is an H1 inverse agonist that binds the receptor and holds it in its inactive state, blocking these effects. As a first-generation antihistamine, it is lipophilic and crosses the blood-brain barrier, where it blocks H1 receptors that maintain wakefulness, causing drowsiness. It also blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors to some degree, which dries secretions and explains dry mouth and urinary retention.", "body": [["Nose and eyes", "Blocking H1 receptors reduces sneezing, runny nose and itchy, watery eyes."], ["Skin", "Hives and itching decrease because histamine can no longer drive vessel leak and nerve firing."], ["Brain", "Drowsiness and dizziness are common because it enters the brain; children and some others may become agitated instead."], ["Mouth and airways", "Anticholinergic effects cause dry mouth and thicker secretions."], ["Bladder", "Muscarinic blockade can cause urinary retention, especially with prostate enlargement."], ["Anaphylaxis", "It is an add-on after epinephrine, treating itching and hives but not airway or blood pressure problems."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth, dexchlorpheniramine is well absorbed and starts working within 15 to 60 minutes, peaking in about 2 to 3 hours. It is about 70 percent protein bound and spreads widely, including into the brain. The liver breaks it down by N-demethylation, partly via CYP2D6, and metabolites leave in the urine. Its half-life is long, about 20 to 30 hours, even though doses are given every 4 to 6 hours for symptom control.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness, dizziness and impaired thinking come from blocking H1 receptors in the brain that normally keep people alert. Dry mouth, thick secretions, constipation and urinary retention come from muscarinic blockade. Older adults are more likely to become confused because of these anticholinergic effects. Young children can show paradoxical excitation. In overdose, anticholinergic toxicity and sodium channel effects can cause seizures and arrhythmias. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive, and physostigmine is sometimes used for severe anticholinergic delirium.", "fun": "Dexchlorpheniramine is an example of a chiral switch: selling only the active mirror-image form of an older drug.", "src": ["Ryclora prescribing information", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1957"]}, "cetirizine-pseudoephedrine": {"origin": "This tablet combines two drugs with very different histories. Pseudoephedrine comes from the Ephedra plant (ma huang), used in Chinese medicine for thousands of years. Japanese chemist Nagai Nagayoshi isolated ephedrine from it in 1885, and pseudoephedrine, a stereoisomer, was described soon after. Cetirizine was developed by UCB in Belgium as the active carboxylic acid metabolite of the older antihistamine hydroxyzine, giving strong H1 blockade with less drowsiness. Zyrtec was approved in the US in 1995 and marketed by Pfizer. The combination Zyrtec-D was approved in 2001 and moved to over-the-counter sale in the late 2000s, behind the pharmacy counter because of 2006 federal limits on pseudoephedrine sales.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Ephedra (ma huang) is used in traditional Chinese medicine."], ["1885", "Nagai Nagayoshi isolates ephedrine from Ephedra."], ["1995", "FDA approves cetirizine (Zyrtec)."], ["2001", "FDA approves Zyrtec-D, cetirizine with pseudoephedrine."], ["2006", "The Combat Methamphetamine Epidemic Act moves pseudoephedrine behind the counter."]], "cell": "Cetirizine is a selective peripheral H1 receptor inverse agonist. It holds the Gq-coupled H1 receptor in its inactive state, so histamine cannot trigger itching, sneezing, vessel leak or gland secretion. Its carboxyl group makes it zwitterionic and a P-glycoprotein substrate, which limits brain entry, though it is somewhat more sedating than loratadine or fexofenadine. Pseudoephedrine is a sympathomimetic with mixed action: it mainly enters nerve terminals and causes release of norepinephrine, and it also has some direct agonist activity. Norepinephrine acts on alpha-1 receptors on the venous sinusoids of the nasal turbinates, contracting them so swollen tissue shrinks and the nasal airway opens. Cetirizine handles histamine symptoms and pseudoephedrine handles congestion, which antihistamines alone treat poorly.", "body": [["Nose", "Cetirizine reduces sneezing and runny nose, and pseudoephedrine shrinks swollen nasal vessels to relieve congestion."], ["Eyes and skin", "H1 blockade reduces itchy, watery eyes and itching."], ["Brain", "Pseudoephedrine stimulates the brain, causing insomnia or nervousness, while cetirizine causes mild drowsiness in some people."], ["Heart and blood vessels", "Released norepinephrine can raise blood pressure and heart rate and cause palpitations."], ["Bladder", "Alpha-1 stimulation tightens the bladder outlet, which can cause urinary retention with prostate enlargement."]], "journey": "The extended-release tablet releases cetirizine quickly, peaking in about an hour, while pseudoephedrine is released slowly and peaks in about 4 to 6 hours. Both are minimally metabolized and leave mostly unchanged in the urine. Cetirizine is about 93 percent protein bound with a half-life of about 8 hours. Pseudoephedrine's half-life is about 5 to 8 hours and changes with urine pH: acidic urine speeds its elimination and alkaline urine slows it. Kidney impairment raises levels of both.", "sidefx": "Insomnia, nervousness and headache come from pseudoephedrine stimulating the central nervous system. High blood pressure and palpitations come from norepinephrine release acting on alpha and beta receptors. Urinary retention reflects alpha-1 tightening of the bladder neck. Drowsiness and dry mouth come from cetirizine's H1 blockade. With MAO inhibitors, released norepinephrine is not broken down, which can cause a hypertensive crisis. Overdose can cause arrhythmias. There is no specific antidote; severe hypertension is treated with drugs such as phentolamine.", "fun": "Pseudoephedrine sales are limited by law because it can be chemically converted to methamphetamine.", "src": ["Zyrtec-D label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Combat Methamphetamine Epidemic Act of 2005"]}, "loratadine-pseudoephedrine": {"origin": "Loratadine was developed by Schering-Plough as a second-generation antihistamine that does not readily enter the brain, so it causes little drowsiness. The FDA approved Claritin in 1993, and it became one of the best-selling allergy drugs. A combination with the decongestant pseudoephedrine, Claritin-D, followed in 12-hour and 24-hour forms. In 2002 loratadine and Claritin-D became available over the counter. Pseudoephedrine itself traces back to the Ephedra plant used in Chinese medicine; after the 2006 Combat Methamphetamine Epidemic Act, Claritin-D moved behind the pharmacy counter with ID and purchase limits.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Ephedra (ma huang), the source of ephedrine alkaloids, is used in Chinese medicine."], ["1993", "FDA approves loratadine (Claritin)."], ["2002", "Loratadine and Claritin-D become available over the counter."], ["2006", "Federal law moves pseudoephedrine products behind the counter."]], "cell": "Loratadine is a peripheral H1 receptor inverse agonist and a prodrug in part: the liver converts it to desloratadine, which is even more potent and lasts longer. Holding the Gq-coupled H1 receptor in its inactive state prevents histamine from causing itching, sneezing, vessel leak and secretions. Loratadine and desloratadine are P-glycoprotein substrates that are pumped out of the brain, so drowsiness is uncommon at labeled doses. Pseudoephedrine enters sympathetic nerve terminals and releases norepinephrine, with some direct receptor action. Norepinephrine activates alpha-1 receptors on nasal venous sinusoids, which constrict and shrink the swollen nasal lining. Together the two drugs treat both histamine symptoms and nasal congestion.", "body": [["Nose", "Loratadine reduces sneezing and runny nose; pseudoephedrine relieves congestion by shrinking nasal vessels."], ["Eyes and skin", "H1 blockade eases itchy, watery eyes and itching."], ["Brain", "Pseudoephedrine can cause insomnia and nervousness; loratadine rarely causes drowsiness."], ["Heart and blood vessels", "Sympathetic stimulation can raise blood pressure and heart rate."], ["Bladder", "Alpha-1 stimulation of the bladder outlet can cause urinary retention."]], "journey": "Loratadine is absorbed quickly, peaking in about 1 to 1.5 hours, and is extensively metabolized by CYP3A4 and CYP2D6 to desloratadine, which has a half-life of about 27 hours. It is highly protein bound, and metabolites leave in urine and feces. Pseudoephedrine is released from the extended-release tablet over hours, peaking around 4 to 6 hours. It is barely metabolized and leaves in the urine, with a half-life of about 5 to 8 hours that depends on urine pH.", "sidefx": "Insomnia, nervousness and headache come from pseudoephedrine's stimulation of the central nervous system. Raised blood pressure and palpitations come from norepinephrine release. Urinary retention reflects alpha-1 effects on the bladder neck. Dry mouth may come from both drugs. With MAO inhibitors, norepinephrine accumulates and can cause a hypertensive crisis. Overdose can cause arrhythmias. There is no antidote; severe hypertension is treated with drugs such as phentolamine.", "fun": "Claritin's switch to over-the-counter status in 2002 was unusual because an insurance company had petitioned the FDA to make it nonprescription.", "src": ["Claritin-D label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "fexofenadine-pseudoephedrine": {"origin": "Fexofenadine came from a safety problem. Terfenadine (Seldane), one of the first nonsedating antihistamines, could cause the dangerous heart rhythm torsades de pointes when its breakdown by CYP3A4 was blocked by drugs such as ketoconazole or erythromycin. Researchers found that terfenadine's active metabolite, fexofenadine, gave the antihistamine benefit without blocking cardiac hERG potassium channels. Hoechst Marion Roussel developed fexofenadine, approved in the US in 1996 as Allegra, and terfenadine was withdrawn from the US market in the late 1990s. The combination with pseudoephedrine, Allegra-D, followed. Fexofenadine went over the counter in 2011, and Allegra-D is sold behind the pharmacy counter because of federal pseudoephedrine limits.", "timeline": [["1996", "FDA approves fexofenadine (Allegra)."], ["1998", "Terfenadine is removed from the US market after fexofenadine becomes available."], ["2011", "Fexofenadine and Allegra-D become available over the counter."]], "cell": "Fexofenadine is a peripheral H1 receptor inverse agonist. By holding the Gq-coupled H1 receptor inactive, it blocks histamine-driven itching, sneezing, vascular leak and secretion. It is a zwitterion and a P-glycoprotein substrate, so it is pumped out of the brain and causes essentially no sedation. Unlike its parent terfenadine, it does not block hERG potassium channels in the heart. Pseudoephedrine mainly releases norepinephrine from sympathetic nerve endings and has some direct agonist action. Norepinephrine contracts alpha-1 receptors on the venous sinusoids of the nasal turbinates, shrinking the swollen lining and opening the airway. The pairing covers histamine symptoms plus congestion.", "body": [["Nose", "Fexofenadine reduces sneezing and runny nose; pseudoephedrine relieves stuffiness by shrinking nasal blood vessels."], ["Eyes and skin", "H1 blockade eases itchy eyes and itching."], ["Brain", "Fexofenadine does not cause drowsiness, but pseudoephedrine can cause insomnia and nervousness."], ["Heart and blood vessels", "Pseudoephedrine can raise blood pressure and heart rate; fexofenadine itself does not affect the QT interval."], ["Bladder", "Alpha-1 stimulation can cause urinary retention, especially with prostate enlargement."]], "journey": "Fexofenadine is absorbed by transporters in the gut, including OATP1A2, and peaks in about 2 hours. Fruit juices such as grapefruit, orange and apple inhibit these transporters and can cut absorption, which is why it is taken with water. Only about 5 percent is metabolized; most leaves through bile into the feces, with some in urine, and its half-life is about 14 hours. Pseudoephedrine is released slowly from the extended-release layer, peaks around 6 hours, is barely metabolized and leaves in urine with a half-life of about 5 to 8 hours that depends on urine pH.", "sidefx": "Insomnia, nervousness and headache come from pseudoephedrine stimulating the brain. Raised blood pressure and palpitations reflect norepinephrine release. Urinary retention comes from alpha-1 effects on the bladder neck. Nausea and dry mouth are reported. With MAO inhibitors, a hypertensive crisis can occur because norepinephrine is not broken down. Overdose can cause arrhythmias. There is no antidote; severe hypertension is treated with drugs such as phentolamine.", "fun": "Fexofenadine is a case where a drug's own metabolite turned out to be the safer medicine.", "src": ["Allegra-D label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "sodium-picosulfate": {"origin": "Sodium picosulfate is a stimulant laxative developed in Germany in the 1960s. It belongs to the same family as bisacodyl, and both are converted in the gut to the same active compound. On its own it was sold in Europe as a laxative. Combined with magnesium oxide and citric acid, which react in water to form magnesium citrate, it became a bowel cleansing preparation used in the UK and elsewhere for decades. Ferring Pharmaceuticals brought this combination to the US as Prepopik powder, approved by the FDA in 2012, followed by the ready-to-drink Clenpiq in 2017. Its appeal was a much smaller volume to drink than traditional polyethylene glycol preps. Prepopik is now discontinued in the US.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Sodium picosulfate is developed in Germany as a stimulant laxative."], ["2012", "FDA approves Prepopik, picosulfate with magnesium citrate, for colonoscopy prep."], ["2017", "FDA approves Clenpiq, a ready-to-drink version."]], "cell": "Sodium picosulfate is a prodrug. It passes through the stomach and small intestine mostly unchanged. In the colon, bacterial sulfatase enzymes remove its sulfate groups to form BHPM (bis-(p-hydroxyphenyl)-pyridyl-2-methane), the same active metabolite as bisacodyl. BHPM acts on the colon lining and its enteric nerves to increase propulsive contractions, and it reduces water absorption while increasing water and electrolyte secretion into the lumen, partly through prostaglandin signaling. Magnesium oxide and citric acid form magnesium citrate. Magnesium ions are poorly absorbed, so they hold water in the bowel by osmosis and may also release cholecystokinin, which adds to secretion and motility. The stimulant and osmotic actions together produce large-volume watery stools that clear the colon.", "body": [["Colon", "Strong contractions and fluid in the lumen produce watery diarrhea that empties the bowel for colonoscopy."], ["Gut", "Cramping, bloating, nausea and vomiting come from rapid stretching and contraction of the bowel."], ["Fluid and electrolytes", "Large fluid losses can cause dehydration, low sodium and low potassium, which is why clear liquids are taken with it."], ["Kidneys", "Absorbed magnesium is cleared by the kidneys, so kidney disease can lead to high magnesium."], ["Brain", "Low sodium can cause headache, confusion and, rarely, seizures."], ["Heart", "Electrolyte shifts can trigger arrhythmias in people at risk."]], "journey": "The solution is swallowed in two portions, each followed by clear liquids. Picosulfate is barely absorbed and travels to the colon, where bacteria activate it to BHPM, which is also poorly absorbed. Bowel movements usually begin within about 2 to 3 hours of the first dose. Some magnesium is absorbed into the blood and cleared by the kidneys; the rest, along with picosulfate and BHPM, leaves in the stool. Antibiotics that alter gut bacteria could in theory reduce activation.", "sidefx": "Nausea, cramping, bloating and vomiting reflect intense bowel stimulation and distension. Dehydration, low sodium and low potassium come from large fluid and salt losses in the stool, and low sodium can lead to seizures. High magnesium occurs when the kidneys cannot clear absorbed magnesium. Fainting can follow fluid loss. Arrhythmias relate to electrolyte changes. Ischemic colitis is rare and may come from reduced blood flow during intense bowel activity. Aspiration is a risk in people who cannot swallow safely. There is no antidote; fluids and electrolytes are replaced.", "fun": "Picosulfate needs gut bacteria to work: it is switched on only after colonic bacteria cut off its sulfate groups.", "src": ["Clenpiq FDA label", "Prepopik FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's"]}, "lidocaine-patch": {"origin": "Lidocaine was synthesized in 1943 by Swedish chemists Nils Lofgren and Bengt Lundqvist at Stockholm University while they studied compounds related to gramine, a barley alkaloid. It was more stable than ester anesthetics such as procaine and caused fewer allergic reactions. Astra marketed it as Xylocaine in 1948, and it became the most widely used local anesthetic in the world. Decades later, researchers found that applying lidocaine to the skin could calm the damaged nerves of postherpetic neuralgia, the long-lasting pain after shingles. The Lidoderm 5 percent patch was approved by the FDA in 1999 for this condition. Later products include ZTlido and lower-strength over-the-counter patches.", "timeline": [["1943", "Lofgren and Lundqvist synthesize lidocaine in Stockholm."], ["1948", "Astra launches lidocaine as Xylocaine."], ["1999", "FDA approves the Lidoderm 5 percent patch for postherpetic neuralgia."], ["2018", "FDA approves ZTlido, a lower-load lidocaine topical system."]], "cell": "Lidocaine blocks voltage-gated sodium channels in nerve membranes. The uncharged form crosses the membrane, then the charged form binds a site inside the channel pore from the cytoplasmic side. It binds best to channels that are open or inactivated, so nerves that fire often are blocked more (use-dependent block). After shingles, damaged pain fibers in the skin develop extra sodium channels, including Nav1.7 and Nav1.8, and fire spontaneously, which drives burning pain and allodynia. Lidocaine from the patch reaches these superficial fibers in low concentrations that quiet the abnormal firing without fully numbing the skin. The patch itself also acts as a barrier that protects sensitive skin from clothing touch.", "body": [["Skin nerves", "Abnormal firing in damaged pain fibers drops, which reduces burning pain and touch-evoked pain."], ["Skin", "Redness, swelling, itching or burning at the site come from the adhesive and local drug effect."], ["Touch sensation", "The patch reduces pain but usually does not make the skin fully numb."], ["Heart and brain", "Very little reaches the blood at labeled use, but excess patches, broken skin or heat can raise levels enough to affect heart rhythm and the brain."]], "journey": "Lidocaine diffuses from the patch into the skin, where it acts on local nerve endings. Only a small fraction, around 3 percent of the drug in the patch with labeled use, enters the blood, and peak blood levels are low, near the end of the wear period. Absorbed lidocaine is broken down in the liver by CYP1A2 and CYP3A4 to MEGX and glycinexylidide, and metabolites leave in the urine. Its systemic half-life is about 1.5 to 2 hours. Heating pads, broken skin, more patches or longer wear raise absorption. A used patch still contains most of its lidocaine.", "sidefx": "Redness, swelling, itching and burning at the site come from the adhesive and drug on the skin. Allergic reactions, blisters and skin discoloration are uncommon. Systemic toxicity happens only if enough lidocaine reaches the blood, such as from many patches, heat, broken skin or a child chewing a used patch. Then sodium channel block in the brain causes numb lips, ringing ears, confusion and seizures, and in the heart causes slow rhythms and arrhythmias. Lidocaine can oxidize hemoglobin, causing methemoglobinemia, which is treated with methylene blue. Severe local anesthetic toxicity is treated with lipid emulsion therapy and supportive care.", "fun": "A used Lidoderm patch still holds most of its lidocaine, which is why used patches are folded sticky sides together before disposal.", "src": ["Lidoderm FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Lofgren N. Studies on Local Anesthetics: Xylocaine, 1948"]}, "dyclonine": {"origin": "Dyclonine is a local anesthetic from the mid-20th century with an unusual structure. Most local anesthetics are either esters (like benzocaine and procaine) or amides (like lidocaine), but dyclonine is a ketone. This made it useful for people allergic to both classes. It was once sold as Dyclone solution for numbing the mouth and airway before procedures, but that product was discontinued. Today dyclonine is mainly found in over-the-counter sore throat lozenges such as Sucrets, where it numbs the throat for short periods.", "timeline": [["20th century", "Dyclonine is introduced as a topical anesthetic that is neither an ester nor an amide."]], "cell": "Like other local anesthetics, dyclonine blocks voltage-gated sodium channels in the membranes of sensory nerve endings. It enters the nerve and binds a site in the channel pore, stabilizing the channel in its inactivated state. Sodium cannot rush in, so the nerve cannot generate the action potentials that carry pain signals to the brain. Small, unmyelinated pain fibers near the surface of the mouth and throat lining are blocked easily, which is why the throat feels numb within minutes. Because dyclonine is a ketone, it is not broken down by plasma esterases into para-aminobenzoic acid, the trigger for ester anesthetic allergies, and it does not share the amide structure.", "body": [["Mouth and throat", "Sensory nerves in the mucosa are blocked, so sore throat and mouth pain ease within minutes."], ["Swallowing", "Numbness of the throat can blunt the gag and swallowing reflexes, which matters in people at risk of choking."], ["Mouth lining", "Mild local irritation or a stinging feeling can occur where the lozenge dissolves."], ["Immune system", "Allergic reactions are rare, and the ketone structure avoids cross-allergy with esters and amides."]], "journey": "The lozenge dissolves slowly, and dyclonine acts directly on the surface of the mouth and throat. Its effect starts within minutes and lasts up to about an hour. Some drug is swallowed, but the amount that reaches the blood from a lozenge is small. Its systemic metabolism and excretion are not well characterized.", "sidefx": "Oral numbness is the expected effect of sodium channel block in the mucosa. Hot foods and drinks can cause burns because the numb area does not sense heat. Throat numbness can blunt swallowing reflexes and raise aspiration risk in people with impaired swallowing. Mild local irritation and rare allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, can occur. There is no specific antidote; very large exposures would be treated like other local anesthetic toxicity.", "fun": "Dyclonine is often listed as the only common local anesthetic that is neither an ester nor an amide.", "src": ["Sucrets Sore Throat lozenge label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Miller's Anesthesia"]}, "chlorhexidine-oral-rinse": {"origin": "Chlorhexidine was discovered in the early 1950s at Imperial Chemical Industries (ICI) in Manchester, England, where chemists were making bisbiguanide compounds while searching for antimalarial drugs. The compound turned out to be a powerful antiseptic, and it was introduced in the UK in 1954. It became standard for skin antisepsis, wound care and hand scrubs. Around 1970 researchers in Denmark, including Harald Loe and colleagues, showed that chlorhexidine mouth rinses could prevent dental plaque and gingivitis. In the US, chlorhexidine gluconate oral rinse was approved as Peridex in the 1980s.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Chlorhexidine is discovered at ICI in England during antimalarial research."], ["1954", "Chlorhexidine is introduced as an antiseptic in the UK."], ["1970", "Loe and Schiott report that chlorhexidine rinses prevent plaque."], ["1980s", "Chlorhexidine oral rinse (Peridex) is approved in the US."]], "cell": "Chlorhexidine is a cationic bisbiguanide. At the pH of the mouth it carries strong positive charges that bind to the negatively charged bacterial cell surface, including phosphate groups in the cell wall and membrane. At lower concentrations it disrupts membrane integrity, so potassium and other small molecules leak out and the bacteria stop growing (bacteriostatic). At higher concentrations it causes the cytoplasm to coagulate and the cell dies (bactericidal). Chlorhexidine also binds to tooth enamel, the pellicle, plaque and the oral mucosa and is released slowly over hours. This property, called substantivity, gives about 12 hours of antibacterial action and prevents bacteria from rebuilding plaque.", "body": [["Teeth and gums", "Less plaque means less gum inflammation, so gums bleed and swell less over weeks."], ["Teeth and fillings", "Brown staining of teeth, tongue and fillings is common because chlorhexidine binds surfaces and reacts with dietary pigments."], ["Taste", "Taste can be altered for a few hours because chlorhexidine binds to taste receptor areas."], ["Tartar", "Calculus can increase on teeth during use."], ["Mouth lining", "Superficial peeling and soreness of the mucosa can occur."]], "journey": "The rinse is swished for 30 seconds and spit out. About a third of the dose stays bound in the mouth and is released slowly over hours. Little is swallowed, and what is swallowed is poorly absorbed from the gut and leaves in the feces. Toothpaste, eating or rinsing right after use can inactivate it, because ingredients such as sodium lauryl sulfate are anionic and neutralize the positively charged drug.", "sidefx": "Staining of teeth, tongue and restorations happens because chlorhexidine binds tooth surfaces and the pellicle and traps pigments from coffee, tea and wine. Altered taste reflects binding near taste cells. More tartar forms, possibly because chlorhexidine changes how salivary proteins and minerals deposit. Mucosal peeling and soreness reflect direct irritation. Severe allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, are rare but recognized with chlorhexidine products and are treated with epinephrine.", "fun": "Chlorhexidine was found by chemists hunting for malaria drugs, not antiseptics.", "src": ["Peridex FDA label", "Davies GE et al. Br J Pharmacol 1954", "Loe H, Schiott CR. J Periodontal Res 1970"]}, "triamcinolone-dental-paste": {"origin": "Triamcinolone was made at Lederle Laboratories in the 1950s as part of the wave of synthetic corticosteroids that followed the discovery of cortisone's effects. Adding a 9-alpha fluorine and a 16-alpha hydroxyl group gave strong anti-inflammatory action with little salt retention. Making the acetonide derivative increased potency on the skin and mucous membranes. To keep a steroid on wet oral tissues, it was combined with Orabase, an adhesive base of gelatin, pectin and carboxymethylcellulose that sticks to the mouth lining. Kenalog in Orabase was used for decades and has been discontinued, and generic triamcinolone acetonide dental pastes and brands such as Oralone continue its use for mouth ulcers.", "timeline": [["1948", "Philip Hench and colleagues first treat a patient with cortisone at the Mayo Clinic."], ["1950", "Hench, Kendall and Reichstein receive the Nobel Prize for work on adrenal hormones."], ["1950s", "Lederle Laboratories develops triamcinolone."]], "cell": "Triamcinolone acetonide diffuses into cells of the oral mucosa and binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor. The complex moves to the nucleus, where it represses the transcription factors NF-kappa B and AP-1, lowering production of cytokines such as IL-1, TNF-alpha and IL-6, chemokines and adhesion molecules. It also induces anti-inflammatory proteins such as annexin-1, which reduces phospholipase A2 activity and the release of arachidonic acid, so fewer prostaglandins and leukotrienes are made. Fewer white cells enter the ulcer, vessels leak less, and pain and swelling ease. The adhesive paste base forms a film over the sore, protecting it from food, teeth and saliva while holding the steroid in place.", "body": [["Mouth ulcers", "Less inflammation and a protective film reduce pain and may help ulcers heal faster."], ["Mouth lining", "Local burning, irritation or a gritty feel come from the paste and its contact with a raw ulcer."], ["Taste", "Taste can be altered while the paste is in place."], ["Local immunity", "Steroid action can allow oral thrush or worsen herpes sores, because local immune defenses are dampened."], ["Rest of body", "Very little is absorbed, so effects on the adrenal glands are rare with short courses."]], "journey": "The paste is pressed onto a dried ulcer and forms a slippery film that adheres for a few hours. Triamcinolone acts in the tissue directly under it. Only a small amount is absorbed through the mucosa or swallowed. Absorbed drug is broken down in the liver by CYP3A4 and leaves in urine and bile, with a systemic half-life of about 2 to 5 hours. Bedtime use lengthens contact because saliva flow and movement are lower during sleep.", "sidefx": "Local burning, irritation and gritty texture come from the paste on a sensitive, open sore. Oral candidiasis and worsened herpetic lesions happen because the steroid suppresses local immune cells that normally control yeast and viruses; this is why it is not used on viral or fungal sores. Allergic contact reactions to the base can occur. Suppression of the body's own cortisol production is rare with short courses because very little drug reaches the blood. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Pressing, not rubbing, matters: rubbing makes the paste crumble instead of forming its protective film.", "src": ["Triamcinolone acetonide dental paste labeling", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1950"]}, "tedizolid": {"origin": "The oxazolidinone antibiotics were discovered at DuPont in the 1980s, and Pharmacia and Upjohn developed linezolid, approved in 2000 as the first of the class. Linezolid works well against MRSA and other resistant gram-positive bacteria, but it can cause low blood counts and nerve damage with longer use. Tedizolid was discovered by Dong-A Pharmaceutical in South Korea and developed by Trius Therapeutics, which was later acquired by Cubist and then Merck. Changes to the side chain gave it stronger ribosome binding, allowing once-daily dosing and a short course. The FDA approved tedizolid as Sivextro in June 2014 for acute bacterial skin and skin structure infections.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Oxazolidinone antibiotics are discovered at DuPont."], ["2000", "FDA approves linezolid, the first oxazolidinone."], ["2014", "FDA approves tedizolid (Sivextro) for skin and skin structure infections."]], "cell": "Tedizolid phosphate is a prodrug that phosphatases quickly convert to tedizolid. Tedizolid binds the 23S ribosomal RNA of the bacterial 50S subunit at the peptidyl transferase center. This interferes with correct positioning of the first aminoacyl-tRNA and blocks formation of a functional 70S initiation complex, so protein synthesis cannot begin. Without new proteins, bacteria stop growing; the effect is mainly bacteriostatic against staphylococci and enterococci. Its D-ring and hydroxymethyl side chain add extra binding contacts, which makes it more potent than linezolid and keeps activity against many strains carrying the cfr gene, which methylates 23S rRNA and causes linezolid resistance. It covers MRSA, streptococci and enterococci but not most gram-negative bacteria.", "body": [["Skin and soft tissue", "Bacterial growth stops in infected skin, which lets immune cells clear cellulitis, abscesses and wound infections."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are common; changes to gut bacteria can allow C. difficile colitis."], ["Brain", "Headache and dizziness are among the more common side effects."], ["Bone marrow", "Drops in hemoglobin, platelets or neutrophils can occur, as with other oxazolidinones."], ["Nerves and eyes", "Peripheral and optic nerve damage are class concerns with longer use, likely from effects on mitochondrial ribosomes."]], "journey": "Tedizolid phosphate is given by mouth or by IV infusion. Oral bioavailability is very high, around 90 percent, so the two routes are nearly interchangeable. Phosphatases in the blood and tissues convert it to active tedizolid. It is about 70 to 90 percent protein bound and spreads into skin and soft tissue. The liver converts it to an inactive sulfate conjugate without using CYP enzymes, and about 82 percent leaves in the feces with the rest in urine. Its half-life is about 12 hours, supporting once-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Nausea, diarrhea, vomiting and headache come from effects on the gut and its bacteria. Clostridioides difficile colitis can follow, as with most antibiotics. Bacterial ribosomes resemble mitochondrial ribosomes, so oxazolidinones can slow mitochondrial protein synthesis in human cells, which is thought to explain bone marrow suppression and peripheral and optic neuropathy with longer use. Tedizolid is a weak, reversible MAO inhibitor, but serotonin syndrome has been less of a concern than with linezolid. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Tedizolid's standard course for skin infections is only 6 days.", "src": ["Sivextro FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "sulfadiazine": {"origin": "The sulfa drug era began when Gerhard Domagk at Bayer found in 1932 that the red dye Prontosil protected mice from streptococcal infection. Researchers at the Pasteur Institute then showed that Prontosil was broken down in the body to sulfanilamide, the true active drug. Domagk received the 1939 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. Chemists made thousands of related sulfonamides. In 1940 Richard Roblin and colleagues at American Cyanamid made sulfadiazine, which was more potent and better tolerated than earlier sulfas and was widely used in World War II. Today it is mainly used with pyrimethamine for toxoplasmosis and for rheumatic fever prevention in people allergic to penicillin.", "timeline": [["1932", "Gerhard Domagk discovers the antibacterial effect of Prontosil."], ["1935", "Researchers show Prontosil is converted to sulfanilamide."], ["1939", "Domagk is awarded the Nobel Prize for sulfa drug discovery."], ["1940", "Sulfadiazine is synthesized at American Cyanamid."]], "cell": "Bacteria and protozoa such as Toxoplasma gondii must make their own folate, while humans take folate from food. Sulfadiazine is a structural analog of para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA). It competitively inhibits dihydropteroate synthase, the enzyme that joins PABA to a pteridine to make dihydropteroic acid, an early step toward dihydrofolate. Without folate, the organism cannot make enough thymidine, purines and some amino acids, so DNA synthesis and growth stop; the effect is bacteriostatic. Pyrimethamine blocks a later step, dihydrofolate reductase, so the pair causes a sequential blockade that is much stronger against Toxoplasma than either drug alone. Leucovorin (folinic acid) is given with pyrimethamine to protect human bone marrow, because the parasite cannot use it well.", "body": [["Brain and eyes", "With pyrimethamine, it stops Toxoplasma multiplying in brain and retinal tissue, which is key in toxoplasmic encephalitis and chorioretinitis."], ["Heart (prevention)", "Long-term use helps prevent streptococcal infections that could trigger rheumatic fever."], ["Kidneys", "The drug and its acetyl metabolite can crystallize in urine, causing crystalluria, blood in the urine and kidney injury, especially with low fluid intake."], ["Skin", "Rash and photosensitivity are common; rarely, severe reactions such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome occur."], ["Blood", "Hemolytic anemia in G6PD deficiency, low white counts and, rarely, agranulocytosis or aplastic anemia can occur."], ["Liver", "Hepatitis is uncommon but can be severe."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth, sulfadiazine is well absorbed and peaks in about 3 to 6 hours. It is about 38 to 48 percent protein bound and spreads well into tissues, including the cerebrospinal fluid, which helps in brain infections. The liver acetylates it to an N4-acetyl metabolite that is less soluble. Both the parent drug and the metabolite leave through the kidneys, with a half-life of about 10 to 12 hours that lengthens in kidney disease. Generous fluid intake keeps urine dilute to reduce crystal formation.", "sidefx": "Crystalluria and kidney injury happen because sulfadiazine and its acetyl metabolite are poorly soluble in acidic urine; fluids and sometimes urine alkalinization help prevent this. Rashes, fever and severe skin reactions such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis reflect immune reactions to sulfonamide metabolites. Hemolytic anemia in G6PD deficiency is caused by oxidative stress on red cells. In newborns, sulfonamides push bilirubin off albumin, which can cause kernicterus. Bone marrow suppression is worsened when combined with pyrimethamine, which is why leucovorin is given. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Prontosil was a dye, and patients taking it sometimes turned reddish; the active part turned out to be colorless sulfanilamide.", "src": ["Sulfadiazine tablets prescribing information", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1939"]}, "tinidazole": {"origin": "The nitroimidazole story began with azomycin, a natural 2-nitroimidazole from Streptomyces bacteria found in the 1950s. Chemists at Rhone-Poulenc in France made metronidazole from this lead, and it was introduced around 1960 for trichomoniasis. Tinidazole, a close relative with a longer half-life, was developed by Pfizer in the late 1960s and used for decades in Europe, Asia and other regions. It was not sold in the US until 2004, when the FDA approved Tindamax for trichomoniasis, giardiasis and amebiasis. A bacterial vaginosis indication followed in 2007.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Azomycin, a natural nitroimidazole from Streptomyces, is identified."], ["1960", "Metronidazole is introduced for trichomoniasis."], ["1960s", "Pfizer develops tinidazole."], ["2004", "FDA approves tinidazole (Tindamax) in the US."], ["2007", "Tinidazole is approved in the US for bacterial vaginosis."]], "cell": "Tinidazole is a prodrug that enters cells by passive diffusion. In anaerobic bacteria and protozoa such as Trichomonas, Giardia and Entamoeba, low-redox electron carriers such as ferredoxin (reduced by pyruvate-ferredoxin oxidoreductase) donate electrons to its nitro group. This forms short-lived nitro radical anions and other reactive intermediates. These damage DNA by causing strand breaks and loss of helical structure, which kills the organism. Human cells and aerobic bacteria lack these low-redox systems and oxygen quickly reoxidizes any radical formed, so the drug is selectively toxic to anaerobes. Constant reduction inside anaerobes also keeps a concentration gradient that draws more drug into the cell.", "body": [["Vagina and genital tract", "Killing Trichomonas and anaerobic bacteria clears trichomoniasis and bacterial vaginosis."], ["Intestine", "It kills Giardia and Entamoeba in the gut, and it reaches the liver for amebic abscess."], ["Mouth", "A metallic or bitter taste is common, partly because the drug is secreted into saliva."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral neuropathy and, rarely, seizures are linked to nitroimidazole effects on nerve tissue."], ["Vaginal flora", "Killing normal bacteria can allow vaginal yeast overgrowth."]], "journey": "Tinidazole is almost completely absorbed by mouth and peaks in about 1.5 to 2 hours. Taking it with food lessens stomach upset. It is only about 12 percent protein bound and spreads widely, including into the cerebrospinal fluid, saliva and breast milk, and it crosses the placenta. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP3A4. About 20 to 25 percent leaves unchanged in the urine, with the rest as metabolites in urine and feces. Its half-life is about 12 to 14 hours, longer than metronidazole, which allows single-dose or short regimens.", "sidefx": "Metallic or bitter taste, nausea, loss of appetite and fatigue are common and relate to the drug's presence in saliva and the gut. Peripheral neuropathy and seizures reflect toxicity to nerve tissue, possibly from reactive metabolites. Vaginal yeast infection follows disruption of normal flora. Alcohol during and for several days after treatment can cause a disulfiram-like reaction with flushing, vomiting and palpitations. The label carries a boxed warning based on carcinogenicity seen in rodents with metronidazole. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Metronidazole was originally developed only for trichomoniasis; its activity against anaerobic bacteria was found by chance when a patient's gum infection cleared.", "src": ["Tindamax FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "secnidazole": {"origin": "Secnidazole is a 5-nitroimidazole, a relative of metronidazole and tinidazole, that has been used outside the US for decades, particularly in Europe, Latin America and Africa, for intestinal and genital protozoal infections. Its standout feature is a long half-life, which allows a single dose to work. US companies developed a granule form to give a single-dose oral treatment for bacterial vaginosis. The FDA approved it as Solosec in September 2017. In 2021 the FDA added trichomoniasis to its label, making it a single-dose option for both conditions.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Metronidazole's success leads to development of related nitroimidazoles."], ["2017", "FDA approves secnidazole (Solosec) for bacterial vaginosis."], ["2021", "FDA adds trichomoniasis to the Solosec label."]], "cell": "Secnidazole is a prodrug that diffuses into microbial cells. Inside anaerobic bacteria such as Gardnerella and Prevotella and protozoa such as Trichomonas vaginalis, low-redox electron carriers like ferredoxin reduce its nitro group. This produces nitro radical anions and other reactive intermediates that break DNA strands and destabilize the helix, so the organism dies. Aerobic human cells cannot reduce the drug efficiently, and any radical formed is quickly reoxidized by oxygen, so human cells are largely spared. Its long half-life keeps drug levels above the minimum inhibitory concentration for days after one dose.", "body": [["Vagina", "Killing the overgrown anaerobic bacteria clears bacterial vaginosis and lets normal lactobacilli recover."], ["Genital tract", "It kills Trichomonas vaginalis to treat trichomoniasis, and sexual partners are treated as well."], ["Vaginal flora", "Disruption of normal bacteria can allow yeast overgrowth, causing vulvovaginal candidiasis and itching."], ["Gut", "Nausea, diarrhea, abdominal pain and vomiting reflect effects on the gut and its flora."], ["Taste and head", "Altered taste and headache are reported, as with other nitroimidazoles."]], "journey": "The granules are sprinkled on soft food and eaten. Secnidazole is well absorbed and peaks in about 4 hours. It is less than 5 percent protein bound and spreads widely through body fluids. The liver metabolizes only a small amount through CYP-mediated oxidation. About 15 percent leaves unchanged in the urine. Its half-life is about 17 hours, the longest of the common nitroimidazoles, so one dose covers several days.", "sidefx": "Vulvovaginal candidiasis and vaginal itching happen because killing normal flora gives yeast room to grow. Headache, nausea, diarrhea, abdominal pain and taste change are typical nitroimidazole effects on the gut and nervous system. Like related drugs, it carries cautions about alcohol and potential for neurologic effects, and carcinogenicity seen in rodents with other nitroimidazoles has shaped labeling. Hypersensitivity reactions can occur. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Secnidazole's half-life is long enough that a single dose is the whole treatment course.", "src": ["Solosec FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's"]}, "lefamulin": {"origin": "Lefamulin belongs to the pleuromutilins, a family that began with a mushroom. In 1951 researchers described pleuromutilin, an antibacterial compound made by the gilled fungus then called Pleurotus mutilus (now Clitopilus). For decades the family was used only in animals: semisynthetic versions such as tiamulin and valnemulin treated respiratory and gut infections in pigs and poultry. A topical version, retapamulin, reached human skin in 2007. Chemists at the Austrian company Nabriva Therapeutics, which grew out of Sandoz research in Vienna, modified the core so it could be given by vein and by mouth with good blood levels. The FDA approved lefamulin as Xenleta in 2019, making it the first systemic pleuromutilin for people and the first new antibiotic class for community-acquired pneumonia in many years.", "timeline": [["1951", "Pleuromutilin is described from the fungus Pleurotus (Clitopilus) mutilus."], ["1970s", "Semisynthetic pleuromutilins such as tiamulin enter veterinary use."], ["2007", "Retapamulin ointment becomes the first pleuromutilin approved for human skin infections."], ["2019", "FDA approves lefamulin (Xenleta) IV and tablets for community-acquired bacterial pneumonia."]], "cell": "Lefamulin binds the peptidyl transferase center (PTC) of the bacterial 50S ribosomal subunit, on domain V of the 23S rRNA. Its tricyclic core sits in a pocket that overlaps both the A site and the P site, and its side chain reaches into the P site. The pocket closes tightly around the drug (an induced fit), so transfer RNAs carrying amino acids cannot line up correctly and peptide bonds cannot form. Protein synthesis stops at the earliest steps. Because this binding site and mode differ from macrolides, lincosamides, tetracyclines and oxazolidinones, cross-resistance with those classes is uncommon. The effect is mainly bacteriostatic against many organisms, and it covers typical pneumonia pathogens (Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Staphylococcus aureus) and atypical ones (Mycoplasma, Chlamydophila, Legionella).", "body": [["Lungs", "Lefamulin concentrates in the lining fluid of the airways, where it stops both typical and atypical pneumonia bacteria from making proteins, so the infection is controlled while immune cells clear it."], ["Heart", "It can prolong the QT interval on the ECG by slowing repolarization, which adds to the effect of other QT-prolonging drugs."], ["Gut", "The oral tablets often cause nausea, vomiting and diarrhea, and like any antibiotic it can disturb gut flora and allow C. difficile to grow."], ["Veins", "The IV form can irritate the vein at the infusion site, which is why it comes with a citrate-buffered diluent."], ["Liver", "The liver breaks the drug down through CYP3A4, so strong inhibitors or inducers of that enzyme change blood levels."]], "journey": "Lefamulin can be given by vein or as a tablet. Oral absorption is modest and food lowers it, so tablets are taken on an empty stomach. In the blood it is highly protein bound, yet it reaches good levels in the airway lining fluid. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP3A4, and it is also a P-glycoprotein substrate. Most of the drug and its metabolites leave in the feces, with only a small amount in urine. The half-life is about eight hours, which supports twice-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea come largely from direct irritation of the gut and from changes in gut bacteria; disruption of normal flora also explains the risk of C. difficile colitis. QT prolongation reflects an effect on cardiac potassium currents during repolarization, so combining it with other QT-prolonging drugs or giving it with low potassium raises the risk of torsades de pointes. Infusion-site reactions are local vein irritation. The drug harmed fetal development in animal studies, so the label warns about use in pregnancy. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "The first pleuromutilin drugs were built for farm animals, so this antibiotic class spent nearly half a century treating pigs before it was given to people by mouth or vein.", "src": ["Xenleta (lefamulin) FDA label", "Kavanagh F et al. Antibiotic substances from basidiomycetes, 1951", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "cycloserine": {"origin": "Cycloserine is a natural antibiotic made by soil bacteria. In the mid-1950s several groups, including researchers at Merck and at Eli Lilly, independently isolated it from Streptomyces species such as Streptomyces orchidaceus and Streptomyces garyphalus. It turned out to be a simple molecule, a ring-shaped copy of the amino acid D-alanine, and it was soon made synthetically. It was active against Mycobacterium tuberculosis, and Lilly marketed it as Seromycin. Its psychiatric and seizure side effects kept it from first-line use, but it remained an important reserve drug for drug-resistant tuberculosis.", "timeline": [["1954", "Cycloserine is isolated from Streptomyces cultures by several research groups."], ["1955", "Clinical studies begin using cycloserine against tuberculosis."], ["1990s", "Rising multidrug-resistant TB renews interest in cycloserine as a second-line drug."]], "cell": "Cycloserine is a structural analog of D-alanine. Bacteria need D-alanyl-D-alanine as the end of the peptide stem that cross-links peptidoglycan. Cycloserine competitively inhibits two enzymes in that pathway: alanine racemase, which converts L-alanine into D-alanine, and D-alanine:D-alanine ligase, which joins two D-alanines together. Without D-alanyl-D-alanine, the cytoplasmic building block (UDP-muramyl pentapeptide) cannot be completed and the wall cannot be built or repaired. This acts earlier in cell wall synthesis than beta-lactams or vancomycin. In the human brain, cycloserine also acts at the glycine co-agonist site of the NMDA glutamate receptor (as a partial agonist), which helps explain its neuropsychiatric effects.", "body": [["Lungs and TB lesions", "In drug-resistant tuberculosis it blocks wall building in the bacilli, adding to other drugs in the regimen so resistance is less likely to emerge."], ["Brain", "It crosses into the brain well and changes NMDA receptor signaling, causing headache, drowsiness, irritability, depression, psychosis and seizures."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral neuropathy can occur, and pyridoxine is commonly given alongside to lower neurologic toxicity."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys remove most of the drug unchanged, so poor kidney function raises levels and toxicity."], ["Urinary tract", "High urine concentrations explain its use for some urinary tract infections."]], "journey": "Cycloserine is taken by mouth and absorbed well, reaching peak levels within a few hours. It spreads widely through body fluids and tissues, including the cerebrospinal fluid, where levels approach those in blood. Very little is metabolized. Most of the drug leaves unchanged in the urine by glomerular filtration, so the half-life of about ten hours stretches out when the kidneys are impaired. Blood levels are often measured to keep exposure in a safer range.", "sidefx": "The main problems are in the brain. Because cycloserine reaches high levels in the central nervous system and acts on NMDA receptors, it can cause anxiety, confusion, depression, psychosis, suicidal thinking and seizures, and the risk rises with higher blood levels, kidney disease and alcohol use. Combining it with ethionamide or isoniazid adds central nervous system toxicity. Pyridoxine is often given to reduce neurotoxicity. There is no specific antidote; seizures are treated with standard anticonvulsant care.", "fun": "Researchers later tested the NMDA effect of cycloserine on purpose, studying it as an add-on to exposure therapy for anxiety disorders, a rare case of an antibiotic side effect becoming a research tool in psychiatry.", "src": ["Seromycin (cycloserine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "WHO consolidated guidelines on drug-resistant tuberculosis"]}, "aminosalicylic-acid": {"origin": "Para-aminosalicylic acid (PAS) came from clever reasoning by the Swedish chemist Jorgen Lehmann. In 1940 Frederick Bernheim reported that salicylate increased the oxygen use of tuberculosis bacteria, which suggested they consumed it. Lehmann guessed that a slightly changed salicylate might jam that process instead. He had PAS made in 1944, found it blocked TB growth and published in 1946. In the late 1940s British Medical Research Council trials showed that giving PAS with streptomycin sharply reduced streptomycin resistance. This was one of the first clear proofs that combining TB drugs prevents resistance, a principle still central to TB care. Today PAS is a second-line drug, supplied in the US as delayed-release granules (Paser).", "timeline": [["1940", "Bernheim reports that salicylate stimulates oxygen uptake by TB bacteria."], ["1944", "Jorgen Lehmann has PAS synthesized and finds it inhibits TB."], ["1946", "Lehmann publishes the first clinical results with PAS."], ["1950", "MRC trial shows PAS plus streptomycin limits drug resistance."]], "cell": "PAS is a structural analog of para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA), the starting material for folate in bacteria. Older teaching described it simply as a competitive inhibitor of dihydropteroate synthase. Newer work shows it is actually a prodrug inside Mycobacterium tuberculosis: dihydropteroate synthase accepts PAS in place of PABA, and dihydrofolate synthase then converts the product into a hydroxyl-dihydrofolate analog. That false folate inhibits dihydrofolate reductase, so the bacterium runs short of reduced folates needed to make thymidine, purines and some amino acids. Growth slows (bacteriostatic). Human cells take in preformed folate from the diet and do not use PABA, which gives selectivity.", "body": [["TB bacteria", "It slows the growth of tuberculosis bacilli and helps protect partner drugs from resistance in multidrug-resistant TB regimens."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain and diarrhea are common and are the main reason people struggle to take it."], ["Thyroid", "Long-term use can block thyroid hormone production and cause goiter or hypothyroidism, worse when combined with ethionamide."], ["Liver", "Hepatitis can occur, often as part of a hypersensitivity reaction in the first weeks."], ["Nutrition", "It can reduce absorption of vitamin B12, folate and fat from the gut."], ["Blood", "Rarely it causes agranulocytosis or hemolytic anemia, especially in people with G6PD deficiency."]], "journey": "The granules are coated with an acid-resistant layer, so they pass the stomach intact and release drug in the more neutral small intestine; they are sprinkled on acidic food to keep the coat working. Peak levels arrive about six hours after a dose. PAS spreads into most tissues and fluids but enters the cerebrospinal fluid poorly unless the meninges are inflamed. The liver and gut wall inactivate it by N-acetylation. More than 80 percent leaves in the urine as parent drug and acetylated metabolite. The parent half-life is only about an hour, so it is taken several times a day.", "sidefx": "Gut upset comes from direct irritation and high drug loads. Hypothyroidism happens because PAS interferes with iodine use in the thyroid, an effect added to by ethionamide. Hepatitis and fever with rash are largely hypersensitivity reactions. Reduced B12 and folate absorption reflects effects on the intestinal lining. Hemolysis in G6PD deficiency reflects oxidant stress on red cells. PAS competes with isoniazid for acetylation, so isoniazid levels rise. There is no antidote; reactions are managed by stopping the drug.", "fun": "When PAS was introduced it was taken in large amounts every day, and patients often called it one of the hardest parts of TB treatment to swallow.", "src": ["Paser (aminosalicylic acid) FDA label", "Lehmann J. Para-aminosalicylic acid in the treatment of tuberculosis. Lancet, 1946", "Medical Research Council trial of streptomycin and PAS, BMJ 1950"]}, "clofazimine": {"origin": "Clofazimine began as a dye. In 1957 a team led by Vincent Barry at the Medical Research Council laboratories in Dublin, Ireland, made a series of red riminophenazine compounds while searching for TB drugs. One of them, coded B663, became clofazimine. It was disappointing in tuberculosis at first but proved very active against Mycobacterium leprae, the cause of leprosy. It also calmed the painful inflammatory leprosy reaction called erythema nodosum leprosum. Clofazimine, sold as Lamprene, became one of the three core drugs in the World Health Organization multidrug therapy for leprosy, and the FDA approved it in 1986. US commercial supply later ended, and today it is available in the US through the National Hansen's Disease Program and FDA expanded access. It has also returned as a key drug for drug-resistant tuberculosis.", "timeline": [["1957", "Vincent Barry's team in Dublin synthesizes clofazimine (B663)."], ["1960s", "Clofazimine is shown to be effective against leprosy."], ["1981", "A WHO study group recommends multidrug therapy for leprosy, which includes clofazimine for multibacillary disease."], ["1986", "FDA approves Lamprene for leprosy."], ["2010s", "Clofazimine is added to many drug-resistant TB regimens."]], "cell": "Clofazimine is very fat-soluble and collects in mycobacterial membranes and inside macrophages, where mycobacteria live. Its exact mechanism is not fully settled. Leading ideas are that it binds guanine-rich mycobacterial DNA and interferes with replication, and that it is reduced by the bacterial respiratory enzyme NDH-2 and then reoxidized, a redox cycle that releases reactive oxygen species which damage the bacterium. It also disrupts membrane function and potassium transport. The overall effect is slow killing or growth inhibition. Separately, clofazimine has anti-inflammatory and immune-modulating effects, including reduced neutrophil and lymphocyte activity, which is why it helps erythema nodosum leprosum.", "body": [["Skin", "The red drug builds up in skin and fat, giving a pink to reddish-brown or almost black discoloration that fades slowly over months to years after stopping. Dry, scaly skin is common."], ["Body fluids", "Urine, sweat, tears, sputum and feces can turn pink, red or brown because the dye is excreted in them."], ["Gut", "Crystals can deposit in the bowel wall and lymph nodes, causing pain and, rarely, obstruction or bleeding."], ["Heart", "It can prolong the QT interval, especially combined with bedaquiline, fluoroquinolones or macrolides."], ["Eyes", "Crystal deposits in the conjunctiva and cornea can cause dry eye or discoloration."], ["Mood", "Depression has been reported, partly linked to the visible skin changes."]], "journey": "Clofazimine is taken by mouth with food, which improves its uneven absorption. Because it dissolves in fat, it leaves the blood and is stored in fat, skin, macrophages of the liver, spleen and lymph nodes, and the bowel wall, where it can form crystals. Release from these stores is very slow. The liver metabolizes only a little. Most leaves in the feces through bile or as unabsorbed drug, with small amounts in urine, sweat and skin oils. With repeated dosing the half-life is about 70 days, so it stays in the body for months.", "sidefx": "Nearly every side effect comes from the drug piling up as a colored, fat-loving compound. Skin and fluid discoloration happen because the dye stains the tissues and fluids it reaches. Crystal-storing macrophages in the bowel wall and spleen explain abdominal pain, rare obstruction and splenic infarction. QT prolongation reflects effects on cardiac repolarization and adds to other QT drugs. There is no antidote, and effects fade slowly because of the long tissue half-life.", "fun": "Clofazimine is a true dye; its color is strong enough that it visibly tints skin, and the change can last for months after the last dose.", "src": ["Lamprene (clofazimine) FDA label", "Barry VC et al. A new series of phenazines with high antituberculosis activity. Nature, 1957", "National Hansen's Disease Program guidance"]}, "cefditoren": {"origin": "Cefditoren was developed by the Japanese company Meiji Seika as an oral third-generation cephalosporin. The active molecule is not absorbed well from the gut, so chemists attached a pivoxil ester (a pivalate group) to make cefditoren pivoxil, a prodrug that crosses the intestinal wall and then releases the active drug. It was first used in Japan in the 1990s, and the FDA approved it in the US as Spectracef in 2001 for mild to moderate respiratory and skin infections.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Meiji Seika develops cefditoren pivoxil and launches it in Japan."], ["2001", "FDA approves Spectracef (cefditoren pivoxil) tablets."]], "cell": "Cefditoren is a beta-lactam. It binds penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), the transpeptidase enzymes that cross-link peptidoglycan strands in the bacterial cell wall. Its beta-lactam ring mimics the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of the peptide stem, so the enzyme forms a stable acyl bond with the drug and is inactivated. Without cross-linking, the wall weakens while the bacterium keeps growing, and autolysins break it down further, so the cell bursts. Cefditoren has high affinity for PBPs of Streptococcus pneumoniae, including some penicillin-intermediate strains, and is stable against many common beta-lactamases of Haemophilus influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis. It does not cover MRSA or Pseudomonas.", "body": [["Airways and lungs", "It kills common pneumonia and bronchitis bacteria such as pneumococcus, H. influenzae and Moraxella."], ["Throat and skin", "It treats strep throat and mild uncomplicated skin infections caused by streptococci and methicillin-susceptible staphylococci."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and nausea are common, and changes in gut flora can allow C. difficile overgrowth."], ["Muscles and metabolism", "The released pivalate binds carnitine and is excreted in urine, so carnitine stores can drop with longer use."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys clear the active drug, so kidney impairment raises levels."]], "journey": "Cefditoren pivoxil is swallowed as a tablet and is absorbed better with a moderate or high-fat meal. Antacids and acid blockers lower absorption. In the intestinal wall, esterases split the ester, releasing active cefditoren and pivalate. Cefditoren is highly bound to albumin, peaks in about two to three hours and is not appreciably metabolized. The kidneys remove it unchanged by filtration and tubular secretion, with a short half-life of under two hours, which is why it is taken twice daily.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea and C. difficile colitis come from disruption of normal gut bacteria. Allergic reactions, from rash to rare anaphylaxis, reflect the shared beta-lactam structure. Carnitine depletion is specific to pivoxil prodrugs: the pivalate released must be joined to carnitine to be excreted, so carnitine is lost in the urine, and the drug is avoided in people with carnitine deficiency. The tablets contain sodium caseinate, a milk protein, so true milk-protein allergy is a contraindication (lactose intolerance is not). There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Spectracef (cefditoren pivoxil) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "ceftibuten": {"origin": "Ceftibuten is an oral third-generation cephalosporin discovered in Japan in the 1980s and developed for the US market by Schering-Plough, which sold it as Cedax. It was approved by the FDA in the mid-1990s as a once-daily capsule and a fruit-flavored suspension for children. Its structure is unusual for a cephalosporin because it lacks the typical side group at the 3 position, which helps make it stable to many beta-lactamases and well absorbed by mouth without a prodrug ester.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Ceftibuten is discovered and developed in Japan."], ["1990s", "FDA approves Cedax capsules and oral suspension."]], "cell": "Ceftibuten binds penicillin-binding proteins in the bacterial cell membrane, with particularly strong binding to PBP3 of gram-negative bacteria, which is needed for septum formation during division. Binding inactivates the transpeptidase, so new peptidoglycan cannot be cross-linked. Bacteria form long filaments, the wall weakens and the cells lyse. It is stable against many plasmid-mediated beta-lactamases, giving it good activity against Haemophilus influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis (including beta-lactamase producers) and Streptococcus pyogenes. Activity against Streptococcus pneumoniae and Staphylococcus aureus is weaker than many other cephalosporins, which limits its uses.", "body": [["Middle ear", "It reaches the middle ear fluid and treats otitis media caused by H. influenzae, Moraxella and streptococci."], ["Throat", "It kills group A streptococci in strep throat and tonsillitis."], ["Airways", "It treats bacterial flares of chronic bronchitis caused by susceptible gram-negative organisms."], ["Gut", "Nausea, diarrhea and abdominal pain can occur, and C. difficile colitis is possible after any antibiotic."], ["Kidneys", "Most of the drug leaves unchanged in the urine, so kidney function affects levels."]], "journey": "Ceftibuten is absorbed well from the gut without needing a prodrug. Food lowers absorption of the suspension, which is taken on an empty stomach. It peaks in two to three hours and is moderately protein bound. A small portion converts to a less active trans isomer. Most of the dose leaves unchanged in the urine, with a half-life of about two to two and a half hours. Even so, it is effective once daily for the infections it is used for.", "sidefx": "Gut upset and C. difficile colitis come from changes in the normal gut bacteria. Rash and rare anaphylaxis reflect beta-lactam hypersensitivity. Mild rises in liver enzymes can occur. Because the kidneys clear it, reduced kidney function raises levels and side effect risk. There is no antidote; serious reactions are managed by stopping the drug.", "src": ["Cedax (ceftibuten) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "butenafine": {"origin": "Butenafine was developed in Japan by Kaken Pharmaceutical. It is a benzylamine, a close chemical cousin of the allylamine antifungals naftifine and terbinafine. The FDA approved it as a prescription cream (Mentax) in the late 1990s, and in 2001 it became available over the counter as Lotrimin Ultra for athlete's foot, jock itch and ringworm.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Kaken Pharmaceutical develops butenafine in Japan."], ["1996", "FDA approves butenafine cream (Mentax)."], ["2001", "Butenafine becomes available over the counter as Lotrimin Ultra."]], "cell": "Butenafine inhibits squalene epoxidase, an enzyme early in the fungal pathway that builds ergosterol, the main sterol of fungal cell membranes. Blocking this step has two effects. Ergosterol runs short, so the membrane becomes leaky and its enzymes work poorly. Squalene, the substrate, piles up inside the cell to toxic levels and disrupts membranes. The second effect makes butenafine fungicidal against dermatophytes such as Trichophyton, Microsporum and Epidermophyton. The human version of squalene epoxidase is much less sensitive, which gives selectivity.", "body": [["Skin", "It kills dermatophyte fungi in the outer skin layer, clearing athlete's foot, jock itch and ringworm."], ["Stratum corneum", "It binds to the outer dead skin layer and stays there after the last application, so fungi keep being killed for some time after a short course."], ["Application site", "Burning, stinging, itching or redness can occur where it is applied."]], "journey": "Butenafine is applied to the skin and stays mostly in the stratum corneum, the outer dead layer, where it forms a reservoir. Only a small amount passes into the bloodstream, and any that does is metabolized by the liver. Because so little reaches the blood, it has no meaningful effects elsewhere in the body.", "sidefx": "Side effects are local. Burning, stinging and redness are direct irritation of inflamed skin. Contact dermatitis is an allergic response to the drug or cream ingredients. Systemic effects are not expected because blood levels are very low. There is no antidote, and none is needed for topical use.", "src": ["Lotrimin Ultra (butenafine) Drug Facts label", "Mentax (butenafine) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "naftifine": {"origin": "Naftifine was the first allylamine antifungal, and it was found by chance. In the 1970s chemists at the Sandoz Research Institute in Vienna, including Anton Stutz, were working on compounds for the nervous system when one of them showed strong antifungal activity. That compound became naftifine. Studying how it worked revealed a new antifungal target, squalene epoxidase. The same team then refined the structure and created terbinafine, which became one of the most widely used antifungals in the world. Naftifine itself was approved in the US as Naftin cream in 1988.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Sandoz researchers in Vienna discover the antifungal activity of naftifine."], ["1988", "FDA approves Naftin (naftifine) cream."], ["1990s", "The allylamine work leads to terbinafine, a close relative of naftifine."]], "cell": "Naftifine blocks squalene epoxidase, the fungal enzyme that turns squalene into squalene 2,3-epoxide on the way to ergosterol. Ergosterol is the sterol that keeps fungal membranes stable. With the enzyme blocked, ergosterol falls and squalene builds up inside the cell. The lack of ergosterol makes the membrane leaky, and the excess squalene is toxic and disrupts membranes, so dermatophytes are killed (fungicidal). Naftifine also has some anti-inflammatory activity, which may help itch and redness in tinea.", "body": [["Skin", "It kills dermatophytes that cause athlete's foot between the toes, jock itch and ringworm of the body."], ["Inflamed skin", "Mild anti-inflammatory activity may ease redness and itching as the infection clears."], ["Application site", "Burning, stinging, dryness or redness can occur where it is applied."]], "journey": "Naftifine is spread on the skin as a cream or gel and penetrates the outer layers, where it lingers as a reservoir. A small fraction is absorbed into the blood. That absorbed drug is partly metabolized by the liver and leaves in the urine and feces, with a half-life of about two to three days. The amounts in blood are far too low to cause body-wide effects.", "sidefx": "The side effects are mostly local irritation, because the cream or gel sits on skin that is already inflamed. Allergic contact dermatitis is uncommon. Systemic side effects are not expected because so little reaches the blood. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Naftifine was discovered while researchers were looking for drugs that act on the nervous system, and its accidental antifungal activity opened up a whole new class of medicines.", "src": ["Naftin (naftifine) FDA label", "Stutz A, Petranyi G. Naftifine-related allylamine antimycotics. J Med Chem, 1984"]}, "oxiconazole": {"origin": "Oxiconazole is an imidazole antifungal, part of the azole family that grew from work in the late 1960s showing that imidazole rings could block fungal sterol production. It was developed in the late 1970s and early 1980s and was approved in the US in the late 1980s as Oxistat cream, followed by a lotion. It was designed as a topical drug for dermatophyte infections and tinea versicolor.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Early imidazole antifungals such as clotrimazole and miconazole are developed."], ["1980s", "Oxiconazole cream is approved in the US as Oxistat."]], "cell": "Oxiconazole inhibits lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase, a fungal cytochrome P450 enzyme (CYP51). The nitrogen atom on the imidazole ring binds the heme iron of the enzyme, so it cannot remove a methyl group from lanosterol. Ergosterol production falls and toxic 14-alpha-methylated sterols build up in the membrane. The membrane becomes disordered and leaky, and membrane-bound enzymes fail. This is mainly fungistatic, though high skin concentrations can kill fungi. Oxiconazole covers dermatophytes (Trichophyton, Epidermophyton, Microsporum) and Malassezia, the yeast behind tinea versicolor.", "body": [["Skin", "It stops dermatophytes and Malassezia from building their membranes, clearing athlete's foot, jock itch, ringworm and (with the cream) tinea versicolor."], ["Application site", "Itching, burning and irritation can occur, and maceration can develop if the skin stays wet."]], "journey": "Oxiconazole is applied once or twice a day and moves into the outer layers of the skin, where it reaches high concentrations. Less than 0.3 percent of the applied amount is absorbed into the body, so blood levels are negligible.", "sidefx": "Because so little enters the blood, side effects are local. Itching and burning reflect irritation of inflamed skin, and contact dermatitis is an allergic response to the drug or base. The liver enzyme interactions seen with oral azoles are not a concern at these tiny systemic levels. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Oxistat (oxiconazole) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "sertaconazole": {"origin": "Sertaconazole was developed by the Spanish company Ferrer Internacional in Barcelona in the late 1980s and early 1990s. Its designers added a benzothiophene ring to the classic imidazole antifungal structure, which makes it very fat-soluble and gives it a second way to damage fungal membranes. It was used in Europe first, and the FDA approved it in the US as Ertaczo cream in 2003 for athlete's foot between the toes.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Ferrer Internacional develops sertaconazole in Spain."], ["2003", "FDA approves Ertaczo (sertaconazole) cream for interdigital tinea pedis."]], "cell": "Like other imidazoles, sertaconazole binds the heme iron of lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51) and blocks ergosterol production, so methylated sterols accumulate and the fungal membrane becomes disordered (a fungistatic effect). Its benzothiophene ring is thought to resemble the amino acid tryptophan and to insert directly into the fungal membrane, forming pores that let cell contents such as ATP leak out. This direct membrane damage can kill fungi at higher concentrations. Laboratory studies also suggest anti-inflammatory and anti-itch activity, including reduced release of inflammatory mediators.", "body": [["Skin between the toes", "It clears dermatophyte infection in interdigital athlete's foot by blocking membrane building and damaging the membrane directly."], ["Inflamed skin", "Proposed anti-inflammatory effects may ease itching as the infection clears."], ["Application site", "Contact dermatitis, dry skin and burning can occur where it is applied."]], "journey": "Sertaconazole is applied to dry skin twice daily and stays in the outer layers of the skin, helped by its high fat solubility. Studies after repeated application found no detectable drug in the blood, so it acts only where it is applied.", "sidefx": "Side effects are local. Burning and dryness are irritation of skin that is already inflamed. Contact dermatitis and sensitization are allergic responses. Systemic effects do not occur because the drug is not measurably absorbed. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Ertaczo (sertaconazole) FDA label", "Croxtall JD, Plosker GL. Sertaconazole: a review. Drugs, 2009"]}, "ethionamide": {"origin": "Ethionamide grew out of the search for drugs related to isoniazid, the breakthrough TB drug of 1952. Isoniazid is a derivative of isonicotinic acid, and chemists in France made a series of thioamide versions of that core. In 1956 a group working with the French company Theraplix reported that ethionamide (2-ethylthioisonicotinamide) was strongly active against tuberculosis. It was too hard on the stomach to become a first-line drug, but it became an important second-line agent and is sold in the US as Trecator for drug-resistant TB.", "timeline": [["1952", "Isoniazid is introduced, prompting a search for related compounds."], ["1956", "Ethionamide is reported as an antituberculosis thioamide in France."], ["1990s", "Ethionamide becomes a standard part of many multidrug-resistant TB regimens."]], "cell": "Ethionamide is a prodrug. Inside Mycobacterium tuberculosis, the flavin monooxygenase EthA (whose production is controlled by the repressor EthR) oxidizes it to a reactive intermediate. That intermediate joins with NAD to form an ethionamide-NAD adduct, which tightly inhibits InhA, the enoyl-acyl carrier protein reductase of the type II fatty acid synthase. InhA is needed to lengthen the long fatty acid chains that become mycolic acids, the waxy core of the mycobacterial cell wall. Without mycolic acids the wall cannot be built and growth stops (mostly bacteriostatic). Isoniazid hits the same enzyme but is activated by a different enzyme, KatG. So bacteria with katG mutations often stay sensitive to ethionamide, while mutations in the inhA promoter cause resistance to both drugs.", "body": [["TB bacteria", "It blocks mycolic acid production so the bacilli cannot build their protective cell wall, which helps treat TB that resists isoniazid or rifampin."], ["Stomach and gut", "Nausea, vomiting, metallic taste, drooling and loss of appetite are very common and often limit use."], ["Liver", "It can inflame the liver, from mild enzyme rises to serious hepatitis."], ["Thyroid", "It resembles the antithyroid drug methimazole and can cause hypothyroidism and goiter, more so with PAS."], ["Nerves and eyes", "Peripheral neuropathy and, rarely, optic neuritis can occur; pyridoxine is given alongside."], ["Brain and mood", "Depression, drowsiness and, rarely, psychotic reactions can occur, adding to the effects of cycloserine."], ["Blood sugar", "It can lower blood glucose, which matters in people with diabetes."]], "journey": "Ethionamide is taken by mouth, often with food or at bedtime to ease nausea. It is absorbed well, peaks in about an hour and spreads widely through the body, including into the cerebrospinal fluid, which makes it useful in TB meningitis. The liver extensively metabolizes it, including to ethionamide sulfoxide, which is also active. Less than 1 percent leaves unchanged; the rest is excreted in the urine as metabolites. The half-life is about two hours.", "sidefx": "The gut effects are thought to come partly from direct action on the brain's vomiting centers and partly from local irritation. Hepatitis reflects liver injury from reactive metabolites. Hypothyroidism happens because its thioamide structure, like methimazole, interferes with thyroid hormone synthesis. Neuropathy may relate to interference with pyridoxine (vitamin B6) use, so B6 is given. Combining it with cycloserine or isoniazid adds nerve and brain toxicity. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Trecator (ethionamide) FDA label", "Vannelli TA et al. The antituberculosis drug ethionamide is activated by a flavoprotein monooxygenase. J Biol Chem, 2002", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "indacaterol": {"origin": "Indacaterol was designed by Novartis in the 2000s to be a truly once-daily beta2 agonist for COPD. Older long-acting agents such as salmeterol and formoterol last about twelve hours. Novartis chemists built a molecule that stays in the lipid layers of airway smooth muscle cell membranes and keeps stimulating receptors for about a day. It was approved in Europe in 2009 and by the FDA in 2011 as Arcapta Neohaler, the first ultra-long-acting beta agonist in the US. It was also combined with the anticholinergic glycopyrrolate as Utibron Neohaler. US availability of these brands has since become limited.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Novartis develops indacaterol as a once-daily beta2 agonist."], ["2009", "Indacaterol is approved in the European Union for COPD."], ["2011", "FDA approves Arcapta Neohaler for COPD maintenance."], ["2015", "FDA approves Utibron Neohaler (indacaterol and glycopyrrolate)."]], "cell": "Indacaterol is a selective agonist at beta2-adrenergic receptors on airway smooth muscle. Binding activates the stimulatory G protein Gs, which turns on adenylyl cyclase and raises cyclic AMP. Cyclic AMP activates protein kinase A, which phosphorylates targets that lower intracellular calcium and inactivate myosin light chain kinase, while opening calcium-activated potassium channels. The muscle relaxes and the airway widens. Indacaterol is a high-efficacy agonist with a fast onset like formoterol. Its long action is thought to come from its affinity for lipid raft regions of the cell membrane, which act as a local depot that keeps feeding drug to the receptor for about 24 hours.", "body": [["Airways", "Relaxed smooth muscle keeps the bronchi open for about a day, easing breathlessness and improving exercise ability in COPD."], ["Heart", "Some drug reaches beta receptors in the heart, which can raise heart rate and, rarely, trigger arrhythmias."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Beta2 stimulation can cause tremor and muscle cramps."], ["Potassium and glucose", "Beta2 stimulation drives potassium into cells and releases glucose, so blood potassium can fall and blood sugar can rise."], ["Throat", "A brief cough right after inhaling the powder is common."]], "journey": "The dry powder is inhaled from a pierced capsule, and much of it deposits in the airways while the swallowed part is absorbed from the gut. Plasma levels peak about fifteen minutes after a dose, and bronchodilation starts within about five minutes. It is highly protein bound and spreads widely. The liver clears it through UGT1A1 glucuronidation and CYP3A4 hydroxylation, and it is a P-glycoprotein substrate. Most leaves in the feces, with less than 2 percent in urine. The effective half-life is about two days, and steady state comes after about two weeks of daily use.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from beta2 stimulation outside the lungs: tremor, fast heart rate, low potassium and high blood sugar. Cough after inhalation is local airway irritation. Paradoxical bronchospasm is a rare reaction to the inhaled powder. Long-acting beta agonists used alone in asthma were linked to asthma-related deaths, so indacaterol is approved only for COPD and is not a rescue inhaler. Nonselective beta blockers block its effect. Overdose is treated with supportive care; a cardioselective beta blocker may be used with great caution.", "src": ["Arcapta Neohaler (indacaterol) FDA label", "GOLD Report"]}, "glycopyrronium-topical": {"origin": "Glycopyrronium (glycopyrrolate) is a synthetic anticholinergic that was first approved in the US in 1961 as Robinul tablets for peptic ulcer disease, and it later became a standard drug in anesthesia to dry secretions and protect against slow heart rates. Because it is a quaternary ammonium compound, it carries a permanent positive charge and does not easily cross membranes such as the blood-brain barrier. The company Dermira used that property to create a topical treatment for underarm sweating. A pre-moistened cloth containing glycopyrronium tosylate was approved by the FDA in 2018 as Qbrexza, the first topical anticholinergic for primary axillary hyperhidrosis.", "timeline": [["1961", "FDA approves glycopyrrolate tablets (Robinul) for peptic ulcer."], ["1970s", "Injectable glycopyrrolate becomes common in anesthesia."], ["2018", "FDA approves Qbrexza cloths for primary axillary hyperhidrosis."]], "cell": "Eccrine sweat glands are unusual: they are driven by sympathetic nerves, but those nerves release acetylcholine, which acts on M3 muscarinic receptors on the gland cells. M3 receptors signal through Gq, activating phospholipase C, raising IP3 and releasing intracellular calcium, which drives the secretion of sweat. Glycopyrronium is a competitive antagonist at muscarinic receptors. Applied to the underarm skin, it diffuses to the glands, occupies the M3 receptors and blocks acetylcholine, so the gland cells are not signaled to secrete and sweat output falls.", "body": [["Underarm sweat glands", "Blocking muscarinic receptors on eccrine glands reduces underarm sweating within the first week of daily use."], ["Mouth", "Some absorbed drug blocks salivary gland receptors and causes dry mouth, the most common side effect."], ["Eyes", "Drug left on the fingers can reach the eye and dilate the pupil, causing blurred vision, sometimes in one eye only."], ["Bladder", "Absorbed drug can relax the bladder muscle and cause urinary hesitation or retention."], ["Temperature control", "Less sweating means less cooling, so heat illness is possible in hot environments."], ["Skin", "Pain, redness and itching can occur where it is applied."]], "journey": "The cloth is wiped across each underarm once daily, and the drug moves into the skin around the sweat glands. Only a small amount is absorbed into the blood. Because the molecule is permanently charged, very little crosses into the brain. Any glycopyrronium that reaches the blood is metabolized little and is cleared mainly by the kidneys, largely unchanged. The local effect lasts about a day per application.", "sidefx": "The side effects are classic anticholinergic effects from the small amount absorbed: dry mouth, blurred vision, constipation and urinary hesitation. Pupil dilation and blurred vision usually mean drug on the hands touched the eye, which is why hands are washed after use. Reduced sweating lowers heat loss. Effects add to other anticholinergic drugs. Physostigmine reverses anticholinergic toxicity in general, but it does little for the peripheral effects of a quaternary drug and is rarely needed with topical use.", "src": ["Qbrexza (glycopyrronium) FDA label", "Robinul (glycopyrrolate) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "penciclovir": {"origin": "Penciclovir was developed in the 1980s by researchers at Beecham Pharmaceuticals in the UK (later SmithKline Beecham) as an acyclovir-like guanosine analog with a longer-lasting active form. It is absorbed poorly by mouth, so the company also made famciclovir, an oral prodrug that the body converts into penciclovir. For skin use, penciclovir itself was formulated as a cream. The FDA approved penciclovir cream as Denavir in 1996 for recurrent cold sores on the lips and face.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Beecham researchers develop penciclovir and its oral prodrug famciclovir."], ["1994", "FDA approves famciclovir, the oral prodrug of penciclovir."], ["1996", "FDA approves Denavir (penciclovir) cream for cold sores."]], "cell": "Penciclovir is a guanosine analog. In herpes simplex-infected skin cells, the viral thymidine kinase adds the first phosphate, a step that uninfected cells do poorly, so the drug concentrates where the virus is. Cellular kinases then add two more phosphates to make penciclovir triphosphate. The triphosphate competes with deoxyguanosine triphosphate for the viral DNA polymerase and slows viral DNA synthesis. Unlike acyclovir, penciclovir keeps a hydroxyl group that allows limited chain extension, so it is not an obligate chain terminator. Its triphosphate lasts much longer inside infected cells (about 7 to 20 hours) than acyclovir triphosphate, which supports its use in a short, frequent course.", "body": [["Lips and facial skin", "It slows viral copying in infected skin cells, so cold sores heal somewhat faster and pain and viral shedding end sooner, especially if started at the first tingle."], ["Application site", "Mild redness or irritation can occur where the cream is applied."], ["Rest of the body", "With topical use, so little enters the blood that effects elsewhere are not expected."]], "journey": "Penciclovir cream is applied to the lesion every two hours while awake, starting at the earliest sign. The drug moves into the outer skin cells, where virus-infected cells trap it by phosphorylation. Blood levels after topical use are too low to measure. When penciclovir does reach the blood (as from oral famciclovir), it is cleared mainly by the kidneys.", "sidefx": "Side effects are minor and local, such as redness or a mild reaction at the site, because the drug needs viral thymidine kinase for activation and barely reaches the blood. Headache and altered taste have been reported in trials but were similar to placebo cream. There is no antidote and none is needed.", "src": ["Denavir (penciclovir) FDA label", "Famvir (famciclovir) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "sinecatechins": {"origin": "Sinecatechins comes from green tea, the leaves of Camellia sinensis. Green tea polyphenols called catechins, especially epigallocatechin gallate (EGCG), had long been studied for antioxidant and antitumor effects in the lab. The German company MediGene developed a partially purified extract of green tea leaves as an ointment for genital warts. In 2006 the FDA approved it as Veregen, the first botanical drug approved under the FDA's botanical drug guidance. A botanical drug is a mixture from a plant rather than a single purified chemical, so its quality is controlled by a standardized manufacturing process and chemical profile.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Green tea from Camellia sinensis is used as a drink in China."], ["1990s", "Green tea catechins are studied for antiviral, antioxidant and antitumor effects."], ["2006", "FDA approves Veregen, the first botanical drug under its botanical guidance, for external genital warts."]], "cell": "The exact way sinecatechins clears warts is not known. The extract is mostly catechins, with EGCG the largest part. Laboratory studies suggest several actions. Catechins are antioxidants and may change how keratinocytes infected with human papillomavirus grow, slowing proliferation and encouraging apoptosis (programmed cell death). They may inhibit transcription factors such as NF-kappa B and AP-1 that support infected cell growth. They may also affect local immune signaling, including activation of immune cells that recognize infected tissue. Clinical trials show the ointment clears warts better than its vehicle, but how much each action contributes is not established.", "body": [["Genital and perianal skin", "Over weeks, warts shrink and clear in many people, likely through effects on infected skin cells and local immunity."], ["Application site", "Redness, itching, burning, pain, swelling and erosions are very common and reflect local inflammation as warts are attacked."], ["Foreskin and urethra", "In uncircumcised males, inflammation under the foreskin can cause phimosis, and lesions near the urethral opening can lead to meatal narrowing."], ["Barrier contraceptives", "The ointment can weaken latex condoms and diaphragms."]], "journey": "The ointment is dabbed on the warts several times a day for up to about four months and is not washed off between applications. It acts in the skin. Studies found negligible levels of catechins in the blood, similar to what is seen after drinking green tea, so body-wide effects are not expected.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are local inflammation, which may be part of how the treatment works: redness, itching, burning, swelling and sometimes ulceration or blisters. Swollen lymph nodes in the groin can reflect this local immune activity. Phimosis and urethral narrowing happen when inflammation scars delicate tissue. The ointment's oily base weakens latex. It does not cure HPV infection, so new warts can appear. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Veregen was the first prescription drug approved by the FDA under its botanical drug pathway, which allows a complex plant mixture to be approved as a drug rather than requiring a single purified molecule.", "src": ["Veregen (sinecatechins) FDA label", "FDA Guidance for Industry: Botanical Drug Development", "Tatti S et al. Sinecatechins in the treatment of external genital warts. Br J Dermatol, 2010"]}, "cantharidin": {"origin": "Cantharidin is a natural poison made by blister beetles, especially the emerald beetle Lytta vesicatoria, known as Spanish fly. The beetles release it as a defense, and it blisters skin on contact. Dried, powdered beetles were used in medicine since ancient times to raise blisters, a common treatment in older medicine, and they were also infamous as a dangerous supposed aphrodisiac that caused deaths. In 1810 the French chemist Pierre Robiquet isolated cantharidin as the active compound. For decades dermatologists used compounded cantharidin to treat warts and molluscum without an FDA-approved product. In 2023 the FDA approved Ycanth, a standardized cantharidin solution from Verrica Pharmaceuticals, as the first approved treatment for molluscum contagiosum.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Powdered blister beetles are used to raise blisters on the skin."], ["1810", "Pierre Robiquet isolates cantharidin from blister beetles."], ["1950s", "Dermatologists begin using cantharidin to treat warts and molluscum."], ["2023", "FDA approves Ycanth, the first approved treatment for molluscum contagiosum."]], "cell": "Cantharidin inhibits protein phosphatase 2A and, to a lesser degree, protein phosphatase 1, enzymes that remove phosphate groups from many cell proteins. In the epidermis, keratinocytes are joined by desmosomes. When phosphatases are blocked, desmosomal proteins stay abnormally phosphorylated, the desmosomes break down and the cells separate from each other. This process, called acantholysis, opens a fluid-filled blister inside the epidermis, just above the basal layer. The blister lifts off the molluscum lesion, which contains the poxvirus-filled cells, and the area heals without scarring in most cases because the deeper dermis is not damaged. Cantharidin does not directly act on the virus.", "body": [["Skin at treated bumps", "A blister forms within about a day, lifting the molluscum lesion away; it crusts and heals over about a week."], ["Surrounding skin", "Pain, itching, redness and temporary dark or light spots can occur, especially if the liquid spreads beyond the lesion."], ["Eyes and mucous membranes", "Accidental contact causes severe irritation and blistering, so it is kept away from the eyes, lips and mucosa."], ["Gut and kidneys if swallowed", "Ingestion causes severe burns of the mouth and gut, bleeding and kidney failure, and can be fatal."]], "journey": "A health professional paints the solution onto each lesion with a single-use applicator, and the liquid dries into a film. A purple dye marks treated spots. The drug is washed off with soap and water after about 24 hours, or sooner if blistering is severe. Very little is absorbed through intact skin. When cantharidin is swallowed, it is absorbed from the gut and excreted by the kidneys, where it damages the tubules and bladder lining.", "sidefx": "Blistering is the intended effect, but the same phosphatase block can blister too much skin if the solution spreads or is left on too long, causing pain, infection and pigment changes. It is extremely toxic if swallowed: it blisters the mouth, esophagus and stomach, causes gut bleeding and injures the kidneys and urinary tract, which is why historic Spanish fly poisonings were deadly. The product is flammable. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Cantharidin is often nicknamed beetle juice by dermatologists, and the beetles themselves were once a feared poison despite their reputation as a love potion.", "src": ["Ycanth (cantharidin) FDA label", "Moed L et al. Cantharidin revisited. Arch Dermatol, 2001", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "tioconazole": {"origin": "Tioconazole is an imidazole antifungal developed by Pfizer researchers in the UK in the 1970s, part of the wave of azole antifungals that followed clotrimazole and miconazole. It was used topically for skin and nail infections and was formulated as a single-dose vaginal ointment. In the US it became an over-the-counter one-day treatment for vaginal yeast infections, sold as Vagistat-1 and Monistat 1.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Pfizer researchers develop tioconazole."], ["1990s", "Single-dose tioconazole vaginal ointment becomes available over the counter in the US."]], "cell": "Tioconazole blocks lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51), the fungal cytochrome P450 enzyme that removes a methyl group from lanosterol on the way to ergosterol. The imidazole nitrogen binds the heme iron in the enzyme. Ergosterol levels fall and toxic methylated sterols build up, so the Candida membrane becomes disordered and leaky. At the high concentrations reached in the vagina, azoles can also directly damage the membrane and kill the yeast. The ointment acts on Candida albicans and many other Candida species.", "body": [["Vagina", "The ointment stays in contact with the vaginal lining for several days and kills Candida, so itching, discharge and burning improve over one to three days."], ["Vulva", "Local burning, itching or irritation can occur, especially when the skin is already inflamed."], ["Barrier contraceptives", "The ointment base can weaken latex condoms and diaphragms."]], "journey": "A single applicator of ointment is inserted into the vagina at bedtime. The drug stays in the vaginal fluid and lining for days, which is why one dose is enough. Only a small amount is absorbed into the blood, and any absorbed drug is handled by the liver. Systemic levels are very low.", "sidefx": "Burning, itching and irritation are local effects on inflamed tissue. Allergic reactions are uncommon. Although systemic absorption is small, case reports have linked vaginal azoles with increased INR and bleeding in people taking warfarin, likely because the small absorbed amount still inhibits CYP2C9. The oily base can weaken latex. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Monistat 1 (tioconazole) Drug Facts label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "mecamylamine": {"origin": "Mecamylamine came from the era when high blood pressure was treated by blocking the autonomic ganglia. Early ganglion blockers such as hexamethonium were charged molecules that were poorly absorbed by mouth. In the mid-1950s Merck researchers introduced mecamylamine, a secondary amine that is well absorbed orally, as Inversine in 1956. It lowered blood pressure strongly, but because it blocks all autonomic ganglia it caused many side effects, and it was largely replaced by newer drugs in the 1960s and later. It remains available in the US as Vecamyl. Because it crosses into the brain and blocks nicotinic receptors, it has also been studied in smoking cessation and Tourette syndrome.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Hexamethonium and other ganglion blockers are used for severe hypertension."], ["1956", "Merck introduces mecamylamine as Inversine."], ["1960s", "Newer antihypertensives begin replacing ganglion blockers."], ["2013", "Mecamylamine is relaunched in the US as Vecamyl."]], "cell": "Mecamylamine is a noncompetitive antagonist of neuronal nicotinic acetylcholine receptors (the Nn type). These receptors are ligand-gated cation channels on the postganglionic neurons of both sympathetic and parasympathetic ganglia. Normally, acetylcholine from preganglionic fibers opens these channels and fires the postganglionic neuron. Mecamylamine blocks the open channel, so the signal is not passed on. Losing sympathetic tone dilates arterioles and veins, which lowers blood pressure, especially when standing, when reflex vasoconstriction is needed most. The effect on any organ depends on which branch normally dominates there: in blood vessels the sympathetic system dominates, while in the gut, bladder, eye and salivary glands the parasympathetic system dominates, so those organs show anticholinergic-like effects.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Loss of sympathetic tone widens arteries and veins, so blood pressure falls sharply, most noticeably on standing."], ["Heart", "Heart rate often rises modestly because vagal tone to the SA node is also blocked."], ["Gut", "Loss of parasympathetic drive slows the bowel, causing constipation and, at worst, paralytic ileus."], ["Bladder", "The bladder contracts poorly, causing hesitancy or retention."], ["Eyes", "The pupils dilate and focusing for near vision is impaired, causing blurred vision."], ["Mouth and sexual function", "Saliva production falls, and impotence and reduced libido are common."], ["Brain", "It crosses into the brain, where it can cause tremor, confusion or mental changes."]], "journey": "Mecamylamine is taken by mouth and absorbed almost completely from the gut. It acts within about half an hour to two hours, with peak effect in about three to five hours. Because it is not permanently charged, it crosses into the brain and placenta. It is metabolized very little and is excreted unchanged by the kidneys. Acidic urine speeds its removal, and alkaline urine slows it, so drugs such as sodium bicarbonate or acetazolamide raise levels. Its action lasts many hours.", "sidefx": "Nearly every side effect follows from blocking both branches of the autonomic nervous system at once. Orthostatic hypotension and fainting come from loss of sympathetic reflexes. Dry mouth, constipation, ileus, urinary retention and blurred vision come from loss of parasympathetic tone. Impotence reflects blocked parasympathetic erection and sympathetic ejaculation. Brain effects such as tremor and confusion come from central nicotinic blockade. There is no specific antidote; severe low blood pressure is treated with fluids, positioning and vasopressors.", "fun": "Mecamylamine is one of the few drugs that blocks both the sympathetic and parasympathetic systems at the same time, which is why it is a favorite example in autonomic pharmacology teaching.", "src": ["Vecamyl (mecamylamine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "ketorolac-nasal": {"origin": "Ketorolac was developed by the Syntex company in California in the 1980s as a strong NSAID that could be given by injection as an alternative to opioids for short-term pain. The injection was approved in the US in 1989 as Toradol, and tablets followed. To offer opioid-level relief without needles, the drug was later formulated as a nasal spray. The FDA approved intranasal ketorolac as Sprix in 2010 for short-term management of moderate to moderately severe pain in adults, with the same five-day total limit as other ketorolac forms.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Syntex develops ketorolac as a potent NSAID analgesic."], ["1989", "FDA approves ketorolac injection (Toradol)."], ["2010", "FDA approves Sprix ketorolac nasal spray."]], "cell": "Ketorolac is a nonselective inhibitor of cyclooxygenase, blocking both COX-1 and COX-2, with somewhat more potency at COX-1. These enzymes convert arachidonic acid, released from cell membranes by phospholipase A2, into prostaglandin H2, the precursor of PGE2, prostacyclin and thromboxane A2. At sites of injury, PGE2 sensitizes nociceptors so they fire more easily; in the spinal cord, prostaglandins enhance pain transmission. Blocking their production reduces this peripheral and central sensitization, giving strong pain relief. Less thromboxane in platelets reduces aggregation, and less PGE2 in the hypothalamus lowers fever.", "body": [["Pain pathways", "Lower prostaglandin levels make pain nerve endings and spinal pain pathways less sensitive, giving relief comparable to moderate opioid doses for short-term pain."], ["Nose", "The spray can cause nasal discomfort, burning and watery eyes as it contacts the nasal lining."], ["Stomach", "Loss of COX-1 prostaglandins weakens the stomach's protective mucus and blood flow, so ulcers and bleeding can occur, even with nasal use."], ["Kidneys", "Prostaglandins help keep kidney blood flow up when it is stressed; blocking them can cause acute kidney injury, low urine output and high potassium."], ["Platelets", "Less thromboxane means platelets clump less, raising bleeding risk."], ["Heart and vessels", "Like other NSAIDs, it raises the risk of heart attack and stroke and can raise blood pressure and cause fluid retention."]], "journey": "The spray is absorbed through the rich blood supply of the nasal lining, bypassing first-pass liver metabolism and reaching the blood quickly, with pain relief in about twenty to thirty minutes. Blood levels peak in under an hour. Ketorolac is more than 99 percent bound to albumin. The liver hydroxylates and glucuronidates it, and about 90 percent leaves in the urine. The half-life is about five to six hours, longer in older adults and kidney disease.", "sidefx": "Ketorolac is one of the most potent COX-1 blockers, which explains its high rate of stomach bleeding and its effects on platelets. Kidney injury happens because prostaglandins are needed to keep the afferent arterioles open when blood volume is low. High potassium follows from reduced renin release. Bronchospasm can occur in aspirin-sensitive asthma, where blocking COX shunts arachidonic acid toward leukotrienes. Cardiovascular risk relates to less prostacyclin in vessel walls. Treatment is limited to five days total for these reasons. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "src": ["Sprix (ketorolac tromethamine) nasal spray FDA label", "Toradol (ketorolac) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "fenoprofen": {"origin": "Fenoprofen is a propionic acid NSAID, in the same family as ibuprofen and naproxen. It was developed by Eli Lilly in the late 1960s and early 1970s, and the FDA approved it as Nalfon in 1976 for arthritis and pain. It never became as common as ibuprofen, but it remains available as generic capsules and tablets. It is noted in pharmacology texts for a relatively high association with kidney problems such as interstitial nephritis and nephrotic syndrome.", "timeline": [["1960s", "British researchers introduce ibuprofen, starting the propionic acid NSAID family."], ["1976", "FDA approves Nalfon (fenoprofen) for arthritis and pain."]], "cell": "Fenoprofen reversibly inhibits both cyclooxygenase isoforms, COX-1 and COX-2. These enzymes turn arachidonic acid into prostaglandin H2, which becomes PGE2, prostacyclin and thromboxane A2. In inflamed joints, less PGE2 means less vasodilation, swelling and nociceptor sensitization, so pain and stiffness ease. Less PGE2 in the hypothalamus lowers fever. Reduced platelet thromboxane mildly impairs aggregation, but because the binding is reversible this effect fades as the drug leaves, unlike aspirin.", "body": [["Joints", "Lower prostaglandin levels reduce pain, swelling and stiffness in rheumatoid arthritis and osteoarthritis."], ["Pain nerves", "Less PGE2 makes pain nerve endings less sensitive, giving relief for mild to moderate pain."], ["Stomach", "Loss of protective prostaglandins can cause indigestion, ulcers and bleeding."], ["Kidneys", "It can reduce kidney blood flow and is linked with interstitial nephritis and nephrotic syndrome, sometimes with heavy protein loss."], ["Ears", "Tinnitus and reduced hearing can occur, as with other NSAIDs at higher levels."], ["Heart and vessels", "Like other NSAIDs it raises the risk of heart attack and stroke and can cause fluid retention."]], "journey": "Fenoprofen is taken by mouth and absorbed well; food slows absorption a little. It peaks in about one to two hours and is about 99 percent bound to albumin. The liver converts it to glucuronide conjugates and a 4-hydroxy metabolite, and about 90 percent leaves in the urine as conjugates. The half-life is about three hours, so it is taken several times a day.", "sidefx": "Stomach ulcers and bleeding come from loss of COX-1 prostaglandins that protect the stomach lining. Kidney injury has two causes: reduced prostaglandin support of kidney blood flow, and an immune reaction in the kidney tissue (interstitial nephritis), which can come with nephrotic-range protein loss. High potassium follows from reduced renin. Heart attack and stroke risk relates to the imbalance between prostacyclin and thromboxane. Asthma attacks can occur in aspirin-sensitive people. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Nalfon (fenoprofen) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "meclofenamate": {"origin": "Meclofenamate belongs to the fenamates, NSAIDs built on an anthranilic acid (N-phenylanthranilic acid) core. Researchers at Parke-Davis in Michigan developed the fenamate family in the 1960s, including mefenamic acid and flufenamic acid, and then meclofenamic acid. Meclofenamate sodium was approved in the US around 1980 as Meclomen for arthritis and was later also labeled for menstrual cramps and heavy menstrual bleeding. The Meclomen brand has been discontinued, but generic capsules remain.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Parke-Davis researchers develop the fenamate NSAIDs."], ["1980", "Meclofenamate sodium (Meclomen) is approved in the US for arthritis."]], "cell": "Meclofenamate inhibits both cyclooxygenase isoforms, COX-1 and COX-2, so arachidonic acid is not converted to prostaglandin H2 and downstream PGE2, PGF2-alpha, prostacyclin and thromboxane fall. Fenamates are unusual because they also appear to block prostaglandin receptors directly, so they reduce both the making and the action of prostaglandins. Lab studies also show some inhibition of 5-lipoxygenase. In the uterus, PGF2-alpha and PGE2 drive strong contractions and pain in dysmenorrhea, and prostaglandins in the endometrium promote heavy bleeding; reducing them eases cramps and reduces menstrual blood loss. In joints, less PGE2 means less swelling and pain.", "body": [["Uterus", "Fewer prostaglandins mean weaker, less painful uterine contractions and less menstrual blood loss."], ["Joints", "Less inflammation and pain in rheumatoid arthritis and osteoarthritis."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is notably common with fenamates, along with nausea, indigestion, ulcers and bleeding."], ["Kidneys", "Reduced prostaglandin support of kidney blood flow can cause acute kidney injury and high potassium."], ["Blood", "Platelet function is reduced, and it enhances the effect of warfarin; rare hemolytic anemia has been reported."], ["Heart and vessels", "Like other NSAIDs it raises the risk of heart attack, stroke and fluid retention."]], "journey": "Meclofenamate is taken by mouth and absorbed quickly, peaking within about half an hour to two hours. More than 99 percent is bound to albumin. The liver oxidizes it to an active 3-hydroxymethyl metabolite that lasts longer than the parent and then glucuronidates it. About two thirds leaves in the urine and the rest in the feces. The parent half-life is about one to three hours.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea is a hallmark of fenamates and may reflect effects on intestinal fluid and prostaglandin balance; it can be severe enough to stop the drug. Ulcers and bleeding come from loss of protective COX-1 prostaglandins in the stomach. Kidney injury and high potassium follow from blocked kidney prostaglandins. Bleeding risk rises with warfarin because of both platelet effects and protein-binding interactions. Cardiovascular risk relates to less vascular prostacyclin. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Meclofenamate sodium capsules FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "mometasone-sinus-implant": {"origin": "The mometasone sinus implant joined two older ideas: a potent topical steroid and a dissolvable stent. Mometasone furoate was developed by Schering-Plough in the 1980s and became widely used in nasal sprays and skin products. Intersect ENT, a California company, designed bioabsorbable steroid-releasing sinus implants. Its first product, a small stent placed during sinus surgery, was cleared in 2011. Sinuva, a larger spring-like implant that an ENT physician places in the office for nasal polyps that return after ethmoid surgery, was approved by the FDA as a drug in 2017. It offered an option between daily sprays and repeat surgery.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Schering-Plough develops mometasone furoate."], ["2011", "Intersect ENT's first steroid-releasing sinus stent is cleared for use after sinus surgery."], ["2017", "FDA approves Sinuva sinus implant for recurrent nasal polyps after ethmoid surgery."]], "cell": "Mometasone furoate is a lipophilic glucocorticoid with very high affinity for the glucocorticoid receptor in the cytoplasm of nasal and sinus lining cells and immune cells. Binding releases the receptor from heat shock proteins, and the drug-receptor complex moves into the nucleus. There it switches on anti-inflammatory genes (transactivation, such as lipocortin-1 and MAP kinase phosphatase-1) and represses pro-inflammatory transcription factors such as NF-kappa B and AP-1 (transrepression). The result is fewer cytokines (IL-4, IL-5, IL-13), less eosinophil survival and recruitment, fewer mast cells and less tissue swelling. Nasal polyps in chronic rhinosinusitis are driven largely by this type 2 eosinophilic inflammation, so they shrink.", "body": [["Ethmoid sinus and polyps", "Steady local steroid release over about 90 days shrinks polyps and swelling, opening blocked sinus passages."], ["Nose", "Patients often notice less congestion and obstruction and may avoid repeat surgery."], ["Nasal lining", "Nosebleeds and local irritation can occur, and the implant can move or become infected."], ["Rest of the body", "Plasma levels are very low and mostly unmeasurable after the first weeks, so body-wide steroid effects are unlikely."]], "journey": "The implant is collapsed in a delivery tool, pushed through the nostril into the ethmoid sinus under endoscopic guidance and allowed to spring open against the sinus walls. Its polymer slowly dissolves and releases mometasone directly onto the polyp tissue for up to 90 days, with the highest tissue levels in the early weeks. Only a small amount reaches the blood. What does enter the blood is extensively metabolized by liver CYP3A4 and excreted mainly in bile and feces.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are local: nosebleeds, irritation, and implant migration or infection, which may require removal. Because steroids suppress local immunity, infections can occur. Systemic steroid effects such as adrenal suppression are unlikely because blood levels are very low, but strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ritonavir could raise exposure. Very rarely, intranasal steroids are linked to glaucoma or cataracts. There is no antidote; the implant can be removed.", "src": ["Sinuva (mometasone furoate) sinus implant FDA label", "Kern RC et al. Bioabsorbable steroid-eluting implants for recurrent nasal polyps. Int Forum Allergy Rhinol, 2018"]}, "fluticasone-exhalation-nasal": {"origin": "Fluticasone propionate was developed by Glaxo in the 1980s and became famous as Flonase nasal spray, approved in the US in 1994. Standard sprays deposit much of the drug at the front of the nose, away from the sinus openings. The company OptiNose built a breath-powered exhalation delivery system: the user blows into a mouthpiece, which closes the soft palate and pushes the spray high and deep into the nasal passages. The FDA approved this product as Xhance in 2017 for nasal polyps and later added chronic rhinosinusitis without polyps.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Glaxo develops fluticasone propionate."], ["1994", "FDA approves Flonase fluticasone nasal spray."], ["2017", "FDA approves Xhance exhalation delivery system for nasal polyps."], ["2024", "FDA expands Xhance approval to chronic rhinosinusitis without nasal polyps."]], "cell": "Fluticasone propionate is a highly lipophilic glucocorticoid with strong affinity for the glucocorticoid receptor. In cells of the nasal and sinus lining and in immune cells, binding sends the receptor into the nucleus, where it turns on anti-inflammatory genes and shuts down NF-kappa B and AP-1 driven inflammatory genes. Cytokines such as IL-4, IL-5 and IL-13 fall, eosinophils and mast cells decline, and blood vessel leakiness and mucus production drop. The device matters because chronic sinus disease sits in the upper and back parts of the nose around the sinus drainage pathways, and the exhalation system delivers more drug to those regions than a standard spray.", "body": [["Sinus drainage pathways", "More steroid reaches the middle meatus and upper nasal areas, reducing swelling so sinuses can drain and ventilate."], ["Nasal polyps", "Eosinophilic inflammation is suppressed, so polyps shrink and congestion improves."], ["Nasal lining", "Nosebleeds, irritation and, rarely, septal ulcers or perforation can occur from local steroid and device contact."], ["Eyes", "Long-term intranasal steroid use is associated with a small risk of glaucoma and cataracts."], ["Adrenal glands", "Usually little effect, but with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ritonavir, fluticasone levels can rise enough to cause Cushing syndrome and adrenal suppression."]], "journey": "The user places the sealing nosepiece in one nostril and blows into the mouthpiece, which closes the soft palate and carries the spray along the nasal passage; little reaches the throat or lungs. The drug acts on the nasal lining. Any swallowed fluticasone is almost entirely removed by first-pass metabolism in the liver, and absorbed drug is converted by CYP3A4 to an inactive carboxylic acid metabolite. It leaves mainly in the feces through bile. Symptom relief builds over days to weeks.", "sidefx": "Local steroid effects explain nosebleeds, irritation, nasal Candida infection and septal ulcers or perforation, as steroids thin the lining and slow healing. Systemic effects are uncommon because of low absorption and high first-pass metabolism, but blocking CYP3A4 with ritonavir or cobicistat prevents breakdown and can cause Cushing syndrome and adrenal suppression. Long-term use may slightly raise eye pressure or speed cataracts. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Xhance (fluticasone propionate) FDA label", "Flonase (fluticasone propionate) FDA label"]}, "prothrombin-complex-concentrate": {"origin": "Prothrombin complex concentrates were first made in the 1960s and 1970s by fractionating donated human plasma. The early products were three-factor concentrates used mostly for hemophilia B, before purified factor IX existed. In Europe, four-factor concentrates containing factors II, VII, IX and X were used for decades to reverse vitamin K antagonists such as warfarin. In the US, CSL Behring's Kcentra became the first FDA-approved four-factor PCC in 2013 for urgent warfarin reversal with major bleeding, and a second product, Octapharma's Balfaxar, was approved in 2023. Viral inactivation steps and added anticoagulant proteins have made modern products safer than early concentrates. Four-factor PCC is also widely used, outside its labeled indication, to help manage bleeding with factor Xa inhibitors.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Plasma-derived concentrates of vitamin K-dependent factors are developed."], ["1990s", "Four-factor PCCs are used in Europe for warfarin reversal."], ["2013", "FDA approves Kcentra, the first four-factor PCC in the US."], ["2023", "FDA approves Balfaxar, a second four-factor PCC."]], "cell": "Warfarin blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase in the liver, so the liver makes factors II, VII, IX and X (and proteins C and S) without their gamma-carboxyglutamate groups. Without those groups, the factors cannot bind calcium and phospholipid on activated platelets, and clotting fails. Four-factor PCC delivers fully carboxylated, functional factors II, VII, IX and X purified from donor plasma, along with the natural anticoagulant proteins C and S, and in some products heparin and antithrombin to limit unwanted activation. Once infused, these factors join the coagulation cascade at once: factor VIIa with tissue factor activates IX and X, the tenase and prothrombinase complexes form, and prothrombin is converted to thrombin, which turns fibrinogen into fibrin. The INR falls within minutes. Because the factors are replaced directly, it works much faster than vitamin K, which needs the liver to make new factors over many hours.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Replacing missing vitamin K-dependent factors restores thrombin generation and brings the INR down within about half an hour."], ["Bleeding sites", "Stopping bleeding in the brain, gut or after trauma depends on restoring clot formation quickly, which PCC does with a small volume."], ["Circulation", "The small infusion volume avoids the fluid overload that large amounts of plasma can cause."], ["Veins and arteries", "Pushing the system toward clotting can cause unwanted clots such as DVT, PE, stroke or heart attack."], ["Immune system", "As a plasma-derived product, allergic reactions are possible, and there is a small theoretical risk of transmitting infections."]], "journey": "PCC is reconstituted and given by vein, so the factors are in the blood immediately and the INR corrects within about thirty minutes. Each factor then behaves like the body's own: factor VII fades in about four hours, factor IX in about a day, factor X in about a day and a half, and factor II over two to three days. They are used up in clotting and broken down like other plasma proteins. Because factor VII wears off first while warfarin is still present, vitamin K is given at the same time so the liver can keep making functional factors.", "sidefx": "The main risk is thrombosis. Restoring clotting factors quickly in patients who were anticoagulated for a reason, such as atrial fibrillation or a mechanical valve, can lead to stroke, heart attack, DVT or PE, and combining PCC with factor VIIa or antifibrinolytics adds to this. Disseminated intravascular coagulation is a rare, serious form. Hypersensitivity and, for heparin-containing products, heparin-induced thrombocytopenia are possible. Headache and nausea are common. There is no reversal agent for PCC itself.", "src": ["Kcentra (prothrombin complex concentrate, human) FDA label", "Balfaxar FDA label", "Sarode R et al. Efficacy and safety of a 4-factor PCC in patients on vitamin K antagonists. Circulation, 2013"]}, "hypertonic-saline-3-percent": {"origin": "Saltwater infusions date back to 1832, when the Scottish physician Thomas Latta injected salt solution into the veins of cholera patients. The idea of using concentrated salt to pull water out of the brain came later. In 1919 Lewis Weed and Paul McKibben at Johns Hopkins showed that injecting concentrated salt solution into animals reduced brain volume and cerebrospinal fluid pressure, while dilute solutions raised it. Hypertonic saline was used for severe hyponatremia for decades, and from the 1980s and 1990s it gained wide use, alongside mannitol, to lower intracranial pressure after brain injury. Today 3 percent sodium chloride is a standard premixed solution, about 3.3 times the salt of normal saline.", "timeline": [["1832", "Thomas Latta gives intravenous salt solution to cholera patients."], ["1919", "Weed and McKibben show concentrated salt solutions shrink the brain and lower CSF pressure in animals."], ["1980s", "Hypertonic saline is studied for raised intracranial pressure and trauma resuscitation."]], "cell": "Water moves freely across most cell membranes, but sodium is largely kept outside cells by the sodium-potassium pump. When 3 percent saline raises plasma sodium, the fluid outside cells becomes more concentrated (higher osmolality). Water then moves by osmosis from inside cells into the blood. In the brain, the intact blood-brain barrier is nearly impermeable to sodium, so the osmotic gradient pulls water out of brain tissue into the capillaries, shrinking swollen brain and lowering intracranial pressure. In hyponatremia, the same shift reverses the cell swelling that causes confusion and seizures. Hypertonic saline also expands blood volume and may improve blood flow in small vessels and reduce inflammation, effects that are still being studied.", "body": [["Brain", "Water is drawn out of swollen brain tissue, lowering intracranial pressure within minutes and reversing swelling from hyponatremia."], ["Blood volume", "Water pulled from cells into the bloodstream expands circulating volume and can raise blood pressure."], ["Electrolytes", "Sodium and chloride rise; the extra chloride can cause hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis, and potassium can fall."], ["Lungs and heart", "Rapid volume expansion can cause fluid overload and pulmonary edema in people with heart failure."], ["Veins", "The concentrated solution can irritate veins and injure tissue if it leaks out."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys gradually excrete the extra sodium and chloride."]], "journey": "Three percent saline is given by vein and sometimes by intraosseous line in emergencies. It acts almost immediately: sodium rises and water shifts within minutes, and intracranial pressure falls within about fifteen to thirty minutes. It is not metabolized. Sodium and chloride spread through the extracellular fluid and are removed by the kidneys. The intracranial pressure effect lasts a few hours, while the sodium change persists until the kidneys excrete it. Brain cells adapt to new sodium levels over a day or two, which is why the speed of change matters.", "sidefx": "Osmotic demyelination syndrome is the most feared risk. In chronic hyponatremia, brain cells have shed solutes to avoid swelling. If sodium is then raised too quickly, water leaves those cells faster than they can rebuild solutes, and myelin in the pons and elsewhere is damaged, causing trouble speaking and swallowing, paralysis or a locked-in state. Hypernatremia and fluid overload come from giving more salt than needed. Hyperchloremic acidosis comes from the high chloride load. Rebound intracranial pressure can occur as the effect wears off. Vein irritation reflects the concentrated solution. If sodium rises too fast, it can be lowered again with free water and desmopressin under expert care.", "src": ["3% Sodium Chloride Injection USP FDA label", "Weed LH, McKibben PS. Pressure changes in the cerebro-spinal fluid following intravenous injection of solutions of various concentrations. Am J Physiol, 1919", "Brain Trauma Foundation Guidelines"]}, "ifosfamide": {"origin": "Ifosfamide is a close chemical relative of cyclophosphamide. Both were created at the German company Asta-Werke, where Norbert Brock and colleagues designed nitrogen mustard prodrugs that would be inactive until converted in the body. Cyclophosphamide came first in the late 1950s; ifosfamide, which moves one chloroethyl group to a different nitrogen, followed in the 1960s. Ifosfamide caused severe bleeding from the bladder, which limited its use until mesna, a drug that neutralizes the toxic metabolite in urine, was developed by the same company. The FDA approved ifosfamide (Ifex) in 1988, together with mesna, for testicular cancer.", "timeline": [["1958", "Cyclophosphamide is introduced by Asta-Werke."], ["1960s", "Ifosfamide is synthesized as a cyclophosphamide analog."], ["1970s", "Mesna is developed to protect the bladder from acrolein."], ["1988", "FDA approves ifosfamide (Ifex) and mesna."]], "cell": "Ifosfamide is a prodrug. In the liver, CYP3A4 and CYP2B6 hydroxylate it to 4-hydroxyifosfamide, which breaks down into ifosforamide mustard and acrolein. Ifosforamide mustard forms reactive aziridinium ions that alkylate DNA, mainly at the N7 position of guanine. Because it has two reactive arms, it can bond two guanines together, forming DNA interstrand and intrastrand cross-links. Cross-linked DNA cannot be unzipped for replication or transcription, so the cell triggers p53 and apoptosis. It acts in all phases of the cell cycle (cell cycle nonspecific). A larger share of ifosfamide than of cyclophosphamide is diverted down a side pathway (N-dechloroethylation), which produces chloroacetaldehyde, linked to brain and kidney toxicity.", "body": [["Tumors", "Cross-linked DNA kills rapidly dividing cancer cells in testicular cancer, sarcomas and some lymphomas."], ["Bone marrow", "White cell, platelet and red cell production fall, with the low point about one to two weeks after treatment."], ["Bladder", "Acrolein in urine damages the bladder lining and causes hemorrhagic cystitis unless mesna and fluids are given."], ["Brain", "Encephalopathy with confusion, sleepiness, hallucinations or coma can occur, likely from chloroacetaldehyde."], ["Kidneys", "Damage to the proximal tubules causes Fanconi syndrome, with loss of phosphate, potassium, glucose and bicarbonate, and can cause rickets in children."], ["Gut and hair", "Nausea, vomiting and hair loss are common."]], "journey": "Ifosfamide is given by vein and must be activated by the liver over hours. Activation speeds up with repeated doses because ifosfamide induces its own metabolism. Ifosforamide mustard enters tumor cells throughout the body. The half-life is about seven to fifteen hours depending on dose. The parent drug and metabolites, including acrolein and chloroacetaldehyde, leave in the urine, which is why bladder toxicity occurs and why kidney function matters.", "sidefx": "Marrow suppression, hair loss and gut upset come from killing dividing normal cells. Hemorrhagic cystitis is caused by acrolein concentrating in the bladder; mesna is a thiol that binds acrolein in the urine and inactivates it. Encephalopathy and kidney tubule damage are linked to chloroacetaldehyde. CYP inducers can shift metabolism and raise toxic metabolites. Methylene blue is used, outside its labeled indications, to treat or prevent ifosfamide encephalopathy. Secondary cancers can follow DNA damage.", "fun": "Mesna was designed to act only in the bladder: in the blood it pairs with itself into an inactive form, and the kidneys split it back into its active thiol right where acrolein collects.", "src": ["Ifex (ifosfamide) FDA label", "Mesnex (mesna) FDA label", "Brock N. Oxazaphosphorine cytostatics: past, present, future. Cancer Res, 1989"]}, "melphalan": {"origin": "Melphalan was designed in the early 1950s at the Chester Beatty Research Institute in London by Frank Bergel and John Stock. Their idea was to attach a nitrogen mustard to phenylalanine, the amino acid that pigment cells use to make melanin, hoping cells such as melanoma cells would take it up eagerly. The L form of this molecule became melphalan (L-phenylalanine mustard). It turned out to be most useful in multiple myeloma, where it became a mainstay of treatment, and the FDA approved Alkeran in 1964. High-dose melphalan with autologous stem cell rescue later became the standard transplant regimen for myeloma, and a propylene glycol-free IV form, Evomela, was approved in 2016.", "timeline": [["1953", "Bergel and Stock synthesize melphalan at the Chester Beatty Research Institute."], ["1964", "FDA approves Alkeran (melphalan) tablets."], ["1980s", "High-dose melphalan with stem cell rescue is developed for myeloma."], ["2016", "FDA approves Evomela, a captisol-based IV melphalan."]], "cell": "Melphalan is a bifunctional nitrogen mustard. Its chloroethyl arms spontaneously form reactive aziridinium ions, which attack DNA bases, mainly the N7 position of guanine. With two arms, one molecule can link two guanines on opposite strands (interstrand cross-links), which blocks the DNA from separating for replication and transcription. The cell recognizes the damage, stalls and dies through apoptosis. Because melphalan looks like phenylalanine, it enters cells through amino acid transporters such as LAT1. It works in all phases of the cell cycle, and plasma cells and bone marrow stem cells are especially sensitive, which is why it is used both against myeloma and to clear the marrow before transplant.", "body": [["Myeloma and plasma cells", "DNA cross-linking kills malignant plasma cells, lowering abnormal proteins and bone damage in multiple myeloma."], ["Bone marrow", "At high doses it wipes out normal marrow, so stem cells are reinfused to rebuild blood counts."], ["Mouth and gut", "Damage to dividing lining cells causes mucositis, nausea, vomiting and diarrhea."], ["Immune system", "Low white cell counts cause serious infection risk until counts recover."], ["Hair and fertility", "Hair loss and damage to the ovaries or testes can occur."], ["Long term", "DNA damage can lead to secondary leukemia or myelodysplasia years later."]], "journey": "Oral melphalan is absorbed unevenly, and food lowers absorption. IV melphalan reaches the blood immediately. It enters cells through amino acid transporters. Melphalan does not need liver activation; it breaks down by spontaneous chemical hydrolysis into inactive products, so its half-life is short, about one to two hours. Breakdown products leave in the urine and feces. Kidney impairment raises exposure and toxicity.", "sidefx": "Marrow suppression, mucositis, hair loss and infection come from killing dividing normal cells, and with high-dose transplant regimens they are expected until the infused stem cells engraft. Nausea reflects both gut damage and effects on the brain's vomiting center. Hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis, can occur with IV use. Secondary leukemia reflects permanent DNA damage to marrow stem cells. There is no antidote; supportive care includes growth factors, transfusions and infection prevention.", "src": ["Alkeran (melphalan) FDA label", "Evomela (melphalan) FDA label", "Bergel F, Stock JA. Cyto-active amino-acid and peptide derivatives. J Chem Soc, 1954"]}, "bendamustine": {"origin": "Bendamustine was created in 1963 in Jena, East Germany, by Werner Ozegowski and Dieter Krebs at the Institute for Microbiology and Experimental Therapy. They combined a nitrogen mustard with a benzimidazole ring, hoping it might also act like a purine analog. It was used in East Germany for decades under the name Cytostasan, but was largely unknown to the rest of the world until after German reunification, when it was studied more widely. In 2008 the FDA approved it as Treanda for chronic lymphocytic leukemia and indolent B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma. Faster-infusing formulations such as Bendeka followed.", "timeline": [["1963", "Ozegowski and Krebs synthesize bendamustine in Jena, East Germany."], ["1970s", "Bendamustine is used clinically in East Germany as Cytostasan."], ["1990s", "After reunification, bendamustine is studied widely in Western trials."], ["2008", "FDA approves Treanda for CLL and indolent B-cell lymphoma."]], "cell": "Bendamustine has a nitrogen mustard group with two chloroethyl arms, which form aziridinium ions that alkylate DNA at guanine and create interstrand and intrastrand cross-links. Its benzimidazole ring resembles a purine. Compared with other alkylators, bendamustine causes more durable DNA double-strand breaks, activates a DNA damage response that relies more on base excision repair than on alkyltransferase repair, and induces apoptosis through p53. It can also cause mitotic catastrophe, a form of cell death when cells try to divide with damaged DNA. This distinct pattern may explain why it works in some lymphomas that resist other alkylating agents.", "body": [["Lymphoma and CLL cells", "DNA damage kills malignant B cells in CLL and indolent lymphomas, often in combination with rituximab."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils and platelets drop, with low points around the third week."], ["Immune system", "Lymphocyte counts fall deeply and for a long time, raising the risk of opportunistic infections such as herpes zoster and, rarely, PML."], ["Skin", "Rashes are common, and severe reactions such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome can occur, especially with allopurinol."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are common."], ["Metabolism", "Rapid tumor cell death can cause tumor lysis syndrome."]], "journey": "Bendamustine is given by vein and is more than 95 percent protein bound. It is broken down mainly by hydrolysis into inactive products, with a smaller part oxidized by CYP1A2 into active metabolites (gamma-hydroxybendamustine and M4). The parent drug has a short half-life of about forty minutes. Most of the drug and its products leave in the feces. CYP1A2 inhibitors such as ciprofloxacin and fluvoxamine can raise levels, and smoking can lower them.", "sidefx": "Marrow suppression, nausea and infections come from killing dividing cells and the lymphocytes it targets. Long-lasting lymphopenia explains opportunistic infections. Infusion reactions and rash reflect immune reactions to the drug; combining it with allopurinol has been linked to more severe skin reactions. Tumor lysis syndrome follows rapid breakdown of a large tumor load. Leaking out of the vein can injure tissue. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Bendamustine spent about four decades as a little-known East German drug before Western trials turned it into a widely used lymphoma treatment.", "src": ["Treanda (bendamustine) FDA label", "Cheson BD, Rummel MJ. Bendamustine: rebirth of an old drug. J Clin Oncol, 2009"]}, "busulfan": {"origin": "Busulfan was developed in the early 1950s at the Chester Beatty Research Institute in London by Alexander Haddow and Geoffrey Timmis, who studied alkyl sulfonates as a new kind of alkylating agent. In 1953 David Galton reported that it controlled chronic myeloid leukemia, and the oral tablet Myleran was approved in the US in 1954. For decades it was the main drug for CML until targeted drugs such as imatinib replaced it. Because it is so toxic to marrow stem cells, busulfan became a key part of conditioning before stem cell transplant. An IV form, Busulfex, was approved in 1999, giving more predictable blood levels than tablets.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Haddow and Timmis develop alkyl sulfonates including busulfan."], ["1953", "Galton reports busulfan controls chronic myeloid leukemia."], ["1954", "FDA approves Myleran (busulfan) tablets."], ["1999", "FDA approves Busulfex (IV busulfan) for transplant conditioning."]], "cell": "Busulfan is a bifunctional alkyl sulfonate. Its two methanesulfonate groups are good leaving groups, so the four-carbon chain between them reacts with DNA, mainly at the N7 position of guanine. One molecule can bond to two guanines, forming intrastrand cross-links (and some interstrand cross-links), and it also forms DNA-protein cross-links. Busulfan is especially toxic to early hematopoietic stem cells, which divide slowly and are less able to repair this damage, while it spares lymphocytes relatively. This makes it strongly myeloablative but only modestly immunosuppressive, so it is combined with drugs such as cyclophosphamide or fludarabine before transplant.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "It destroys marrow stem cells, emptying the marrow so donor or stored stem cells can engraft."], ["Brain", "It crosses into the brain and can cause seizures, so seizure prevention is given beforehand."], ["Liver", "Damage to small liver veins can cause sinusoidal obstruction syndrome (veno-occlusive disease), with painful liver swelling, jaundice and fluid gain."], ["Lungs", "Long-term use can cause pulmonary fibrosis, known as busulfan lung, sometimes years later."], ["Skin", "It can darken the skin (hyperpigmentation)."], ["Mouth and gut", "Mucositis, nausea and vomiting are common."], ["Fertility", "Ovarian and testicular failure are common after high doses."]], "journey": "IV busulfan is infused through a central line and reaches predictable blood levels; oral busulfan is absorbed well but more variably. It crosses into the cerebrospinal fluid at levels close to those in plasma. The liver conjugates it with glutathione, mainly through glutathione S-transferase A1, and the products leave in the urine. The half-life is about two to three hours. Blood levels are often measured during conditioning to adjust exposure, and drugs that deplete glutathione or inhibit metabolism, such as acetaminophen, itraconazole or metronidazole, can raise levels.", "sidefx": "Myeloablation is the intended effect, so infection and bleeding risks are high until engraftment. Seizures happen because busulfan reaches the brain easily. Veno-occlusive disease reflects injury to the endothelium of liver sinusoids, and the risk is higher with higher exposure and with cyclophosphamide. Busulfan lung and skin darkening are classic long-term effects of the oral drug. Glutathione depletion by acetaminophen slows clearance. There is no antidote; defibrotide treats severe veno-occlusive disease.", "src": ["Busulfex (busulfan) injection FDA label", "Myleran (busulfan) FDA label", "Galton DAG. Myleran in chronic myeloid leukaemia. Lancet, 1953"]}, "carmustine": {"origin": "Carmustine, also called BCNU, came out of the US National Cancer Institute screening program. In the 1960s chemists at the Southern Research Institute in Birmingham, Alabama, led by John Montgomery and Thomas Johnston, studied nitrosoureas after one showed activity against leukemia in mice. BCNU was notable because it was lipid soluble and crossed the blood-brain barrier, making it useful for brain tumors. The FDA approved IV carmustine as BiCNU in 1977. In 1996 the FDA approved Gliadel, a dissolvable wafer containing carmustine that surgeons place in the brain cavity after removing a glioma, developed by Henry Brem at Johns Hopkins and Robert Langer at MIT.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Southern Research Institute chemists develop carmustine (BCNU) in the NCI program."], ["1977", "FDA approves BiCNU (IV carmustine)."], ["1996", "FDA approves Gliadel carmustine wafer for glioblastoma after surgery."]], "cell": "Carmustine does not need enzymes to activate it. In the body it breaks down spontaneously into two reactive pieces. A chloroethyl diazonium ion alkylates DNA, especially the O6 position of guanine; over the next hours, this chloroethyl adduct reacts with the cytosine on the opposite strand, forming an interstrand cross-link that blocks replication and kills the cell. An isocyanate piece carbamoylates lysine residues on proteins, which can inactivate DNA repair enzymes and glutathione reductase. Tumor cells that make the repair enzyme O6-methylguanine-DNA methyltransferase (MGMT) can remove the chloroethyl group before the cross-link forms, which is a key resistance mechanism. Carmustine is cell cycle nonspecific.", "body": [["Brain tumors", "Its fat solubility lets it cross the blood-brain barrier and kill glioma cells; the wafer delivers high local doses after surgery."], ["Bone marrow", "Marrow suppression is delayed, with platelets lowest at about four to five weeks and white cells at five to six weeks."], ["Lungs", "Pulmonary infiltrates and fibrosis can develop, sometimes many years later, and the risk rises with total exposure."], ["Liver and kidneys", "Liver enzyme rises and kidney damage can occur."], ["Eyes", "Optic neuroretinitis has been reported, especially with intra-arterial use."], ["Infusion site", "The alcohol diluent and the drug can cause flushing, burning and low blood pressure with fast infusion."]], "journey": "IV carmustine is distributed quickly and, being lipid soluble, enters the brain and cerebrospinal fluid well. The parent drug lasts only about fifteen to thirty minutes because it breaks down spontaneously and is also metabolized by the liver; its active pieces work within that time. Metabolites leave mostly in the urine, and some is exhaled as carbon dioxide. Gliadel wafers release carmustine into the surrounding brain over a few weeks as the polymer dissolves, with little reaching the blood.", "sidefx": "Delayed marrow suppression reflects damage to marrow stem cells, which shows up only as mature cells age out. Pulmonary fibrosis is thought to come from carbamoylation and oxidative injury of lung cells, and it can appear years later, especially after chest radiation. Nausea is from effects on the brain's vomiting center. Flushing and low blood pressure come from fast infusion and the alcohol diluent. Gliadel wafers can cause seizures, brain swelling and healing problems near the cavity. There is no antidote.", "src": ["BiCNU (carmustine) FDA label", "Gliadel (carmustine wafer) FDA label", "Brem H et al. Placebo-controlled trial of safety and efficacy of intraoperative controlled delivery of carmustine. Lancet, 1995"]}, "thiotepa": {"origin": "Thiotepa is one of the oldest chemotherapy drugs still in use. It came from 1950s research at American Cyanamid's Lederle Laboratories, which studied ethylenimine compounds related to the nitrogen mustards. Chemists found that molecules with several three-membered aziridine rings could alkylate DNA directly. Thiotepa (triethylenethiophosphoramide) was approved in the US in 1959 and was used for breast, ovarian and bladder cancers and for fluid buildup around the lungs caused by cancer. Later it became valued in high-dose transplant conditioning because it crosses into the brain. In 2017 the FDA approved Tepadina for conditioning before stem cell transplant in children with beta-thalassemia.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Lederle Laboratories develops ethylenimine alkylating agents including thiotepa."], ["1959", "FDA approves thiotepa."], ["1980s", "High-dose thiotepa is studied in stem cell transplant regimens."], ["2017", "FDA approves Tepadina for conditioning in pediatric beta-thalassemia transplant."]], "cell": "Thiotepa carries three aziridine (ethylenimine) rings. Each ring can open and attach an ethyl group to DNA, mainly at the N7 position of guanine. With several reactive rings, a single molecule can link two DNA bases, forming interstrand and intrastrand cross-links that block DNA strand separation, stall replication and trigger apoptosis. Liver CYP3A4 and CYP2B6 convert thiotepa to TEPA, its oxygen analog, which also alkylates DNA and lasts longer. Some TEPA releases aziridine, which may add to activity. Thiotepa is cell cycle nonspecific. It is also a strong inhibitor of CYP2B6, which matters when it is combined with cyclophosphamide.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "It is strongly myeloablative, so it is used to empty the marrow before transplant; counts fall to their lowest at about two to three weeks."], ["Brain", "It crosses the blood-brain barrier, which is useful for brain lymphoma regimens but can cause confusion or other central nervous system effects at high doses."], ["Skin", "It is excreted in sweat, so it can darken, redden, burn or peel the skin, especially in skin folds and under dressings."], ["Bladder", "Placed directly in the bladder, it treats superficial bladder cancer with little absorption."], ["Liver", "High doses can cause sinusoidal obstruction syndrome (veno-occlusive disease)."], ["Mouth and gut", "Mucositis, nausea and vomiting are common."]], "journey": "Thiotepa can be given by vein, into the bladder or into body cavities such as the pleural space. IV thiotepa spreads widely, including into the cerebrospinal fluid. The liver converts it through CYP3A4 and CYP2B6 to TEPA, an active metabolite that lasts longer than the parent drug. The half-life of thiotepa is about one and a half to four hours. Metabolites leave in the urine. Part of the drug is also excreted through the skin in sweat. Some drug is absorbed from the bladder, especially if the lining is damaged.", "sidefx": "Marrow suppression, mucositis and infection come from killing dividing cells, and at transplant doses they are expected until engraftment. Skin darkening and peeling occur because the drug comes out in sweat and damages skin cells directly, which is why frequent bathing and changing of dressings is advised during high-dose treatment. Central nervous system toxicity reflects its good brain penetration. Veno-occlusive disease reflects injury to liver sinusoid lining cells. Secondary cancers can occur after DNA damage. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Tepadina (thiotepa) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "dacarbazine": {"origin": "Dacarbazine (DTIC) was created at the Southern Research Institute in Birmingham, Alabama, around 1959, as part of the National Cancer Institute program. Chemists including Y. Fulmer Shealy and John Montgomery were making imidazole compounds that resembled 5-aminoimidazole-4-carboxamide, an intermediate in purine synthesis, hoping to make an antimetabolite. Instead, the triazene group turned out to release a methylating agent, so dacarbazine acts as an alkylating drug. The FDA approved it in 1975 for metastatic melanoma, and it became the D in the ABVD regimen for Hodgkin lymphoma. Its chemistry later inspired temozolomide, an oral drug that produces the same active compound without liver activation.", "timeline": [["1959", "Dacarbazine is synthesized at the Southern Research Institute."], ["1975", "FDA approves dacarbazine for metastatic melanoma."], ["1975", "The ABVD regimen including dacarbazine is reported for Hodgkin lymphoma."], ["1999", "Temozolomide, a related drug that forms the same active compound, is approved in the US."]], "cell": "Dacarbazine is a prodrug. In the liver, CYP1A2 and CYP2E1 (and CYP1A1) remove a methyl group (N-demethylation), producing MTIC (monomethyl triazenoimidazole carboxamide). MTIC breaks down into a methyldiazonium ion, a highly reactive methylating agent, and an inactive imidazole carboxamide (AIC). The methyldiazonium ion adds methyl groups to DNA, mainly at N7 and O6 of guanine. O6-methylguanine is the key lesion: during replication it pairs with thymine instead of cytosine, and the mismatch repair system repeatedly tries and fails to fix it, causing DNA breaks and apoptosis. Tumors with high levels of the repair enzyme MGMT, which removes the methyl group, are more resistant.", "body": [["Melanoma and lymphoma", "DNA methylation kills melanoma cells and, combined with other drugs in ABVD, Hodgkin lymphoma cells."], ["Stomach and brain", "Severe nausea and vomiting are common because it is highly emetogenic."], ["Bone marrow", "White cell and platelet counts fall, with the low point about two to four weeks after treatment."], ["Whole body", "A flu-like illness with fever, muscle aches and tiredness can follow a dose."], ["Liver", "A rare but serious reaction causes clots in the hepatic veins and liver cell death."], ["Skin", "Sun sensitivity, facial flushing and hair loss can occur."], ["Veins", "It irritates veins, causing pain and burning, especially if infused quickly or leaked."]], "journey": "Dacarbazine is given by vein and must be activated in the liver. It is weakly protein bound and does not enter the brain well. The terminal half-life is about five hours. About 40 percent leaves unchanged in the urine, and the inactive breakdown product AIC is also excreted in urine. Because CYP1A2 activates it, smoking and enzyme inducers can increase activation while CYP1A2 inhibitors can reduce it. The drug is light sensitive and is protected from light.", "sidefx": "Nausea and vomiting are among the worst of any chemotherapy drug, likely from direct effects on the chemoreceptor trigger zone. Marrow suppression comes from DNA damage in dividing blood cell precursors. The flu-like syndrome may reflect cytokine release. Hepatic vein thrombosis with liver necrosis is rare and thought to involve damage to small liver veins. Photosensitivity relates to light-sensitive breakdown products. Vein irritation is direct chemical injury. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Dacarbazine for injection FDA label", "Bonadonna G et al. ABVD regimen in Hodgkin disease. Cancer, 1975"]}, "streptozocin": {"origin": "Streptozocin is a natural product from the soil bacterium Streptomyces achromogenes. Scientists at the Upjohn Company isolated it around 1959 while searching for new antibiotics. In the early 1960s researchers found that it destroyed the insulin-making beta cells of the pancreas in animals, causing diabetes. That property made it a classic laboratory tool for creating diabetic animals, and it also suggested a cancer use. Because it selectively targets beta cells, it was tested in pancreatic islet cell tumors and approved by the FDA in 1982 as Zanosar for metastatic islet cell carcinoma.", "timeline": [["1959", "Streptozocin is isolated from Streptomyces achromogenes at Upjohn."], ["1963", "Its diabetes-causing effect on pancreatic beta cells is reported in animals."], ["1982", "FDA approves Zanosar for metastatic pancreatic islet cell carcinoma."]], "cell": "Streptozocin is a methylnitrosourea attached to glucosamine, a sugar. The sugar part lets it enter cells through the glucose transporter GLUT2, which is abundant on pancreatic beta cells, liver cells and kidney tubule cells. Inside the cell, the nitrosourea breaks down into a methyldiazonium ion, which methylates DNA, especially at the N7 and O6 positions of guanine. The DNA damage activates poly(ADP-ribose) polymerase (PARP), which uses up NAD and ATP; the beta cell runs out of energy and dies. Streptozocin also releases nitric oxide and creates free radicals that add to the damage. Unlike most nitrosoureas, it causes relatively little cross-linking and little marrow toxicity.", "body": [["Pancreatic islet tumors", "It kills neuroendocrine tumor cells that, like beta cells, take it in through glucose transporters."], ["Kidneys", "Kidney tubule cells also take it up, so proteinuria, rising creatinine, low phosphate and renal tubular damage are common and cumulative."], ["Blood sugar", "Destroying insulin-producing cells can first release insulin and cause hypoglycemia, then later lead to high blood sugar."], ["Stomach and brain", "Severe nausea and vomiting are common, from effects on the brain vomiting center."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise, as liver cells also carry GLUT2."], ["Bone marrow", "Blood counts usually fall only mildly, unlike most other nitrosoureas."]], "journey": "Streptozocin is given by vein and spreads quickly to the liver, kidneys and pancreas, the organs with high GLUT2 levels. The parent drug disappears from plasma quickly, with a half-life of about thirty-five minutes, while its metabolites last longer. It is metabolized in the liver and kidneys. Most of the drug leaves in the urine as metabolites, which is part of why kidney injury is its main toxicity.", "sidefx": "Kidney damage is the dose-limiting toxicity because tubule cells absorb streptozocin through GLUT2 and are injured by the same DNA methylation and energy depletion that kill beta cells; the damage builds with each course, so kidney function is followed closely and other nephrotoxins add to it. Sudden insulin release from dying beta cells can cause hypoglycemia, and loss of beta cells can cause diabetes. Nausea is from effects on the vomiting center. Liver enzyme rises reflect liver cell uptake. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Streptozocin is one of the most widely used chemicals in diabetes research, because a single injection in rodents reliably destroys beta cells and creates a model of type 1 diabetes.", "src": ["Zanosar (streptozocin) FDA label", "Rakieten N et al. Studies on the diabetogenic action of streptozotocin. Cancer Chemother Rep, 1963"]}, "trabectedin": {"origin": "Trabectedin comes from the sea. In 1969 US National Cancer Institute screening found that extracts of the Caribbean mangrove tunicate (sea squirt) Ecteinascidia turbinata had antitumor activity. The active compound was present in tiny amounts, and its structure was finally solved in 1990 by the group of Kenneth Rinehart at the University of Illinois, and independently by researchers at Harbor Branch Oceanographic Institution. The Spanish company PharmaMar developed it as ET-743. Harvesting tons of sea squirts gave only grams of drug, so chemists built it in the lab; E. J. Corey published a full synthesis in 1996, and PharmaMar later made it from a bacterial starting material. It was approved in Europe in 2007 and by the FDA in 2015 as Yondelis for liposarcoma and leiomyosarcoma.", "timeline": [["1969", "NCI screening finds antitumor activity in Ecteinascidia turbinata extracts."], ["1990", "The structure of ecteinascidin 743 is determined."], ["1996", "E. J. Corey's group reports a total synthesis."], ["2007", "Trabectedin is approved in the European Union for soft tissue sarcoma."], ["2015", "FDA approves Yondelis for liposarcoma and leiomyosarcoma."]], "cell": "Trabectedin binds the minor groove of DNA and forms a covalent bond with the N2 amino group of guanine, preferring certain sequences. This bends the DNA toward the major groove. The distorted DNA traps the transcription-coupled nucleotide excision repair machinery, which tries to cut the lesion but creates toxic DNA breaks, and it stalls RNA polymerase II, which is then degraded, so transcription of many genes stops. Cells with defective homologous recombination are especially sensitive. In myxoid liposarcoma, trabectedin displaces the abnormal FUS-CHOP fusion transcription factor from its target genes, which helps tumor cells mature. It also kills tumor-associated macrophages and monocytes, reducing the supportive inflammatory environment around the tumor.", "body": [["Sarcoma", "Disrupting transcription and DNA repair slows or shrinks liposarcoma and leiomyosarcoma, especially myxoid liposarcoma."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes rise early in each cycle; dexamethasone premedication lowers this risk."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils fall, with the low point about ten to fifteen days after a dose."], ["Muscle", "It can damage muscle, raising CPK and rarely causing rhabdomyolysis."], ["Heart", "Cardiomyopathy and heart failure can occur."], ["Blood vessels", "Capillary leak syndrome, with swelling and low blood pressure, is a rare serious effect."], ["Infusion site", "Leakage outside a vein causes tissue necrosis, so a central line is used."]], "journey": "Trabectedin is given as a 24-hour infusion through a central line. It is about 97 percent protein bound and spreads widely into tissues, giving a very large volume of distribution. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A inhibitors raise levels and inducers lower them. Most leaves in the feces, with little in urine. The terminal half-life is very long, about a week.", "sidefx": "Marrow suppression reflects DNA damage in blood cell precursors. Liver injury reflects hepatocyte exposure during metabolism and is reduced by dexamethasone, which also helps prevent nausea. Muscle injury and cardiomyopathy may reflect effects on high-energy, transcription-dependent cells. Capillary leak relates to damage to the vessel lining. Extravasation necrosis is direct tissue toxicity. CYP3A inhibitors raise all of these risks. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Early supplies of trabectedin required harvesting about a ton of sea squirts to obtain roughly a gram of drug, which pushed chemists to find ways to make it in the lab.", "src": ["Yondelis (trabectedin) FDA label", "Rinehart KL et al. Ecteinascidins 729, 743, 745. J Org Chem, 1990", "Corey EJ et al. Enantioselective total synthesis of ecteinascidin 743. J Am Chem Soc, 1996"]}, "lurbinectedin": {"origin": "Lurbinectedin is a fully synthetic relative of trabectedin, the sea squirt compound. Scientists at the Spanish company PharmaMar modified one part of the trabectedin structure (replacing a tetrahydroisoquinoline unit with a tetrahydro-beta-carboline) to change how the drug distributes in the body and acts on transcription. Small cell lung cancer relies heavily on certain transcription programs, and lurbinectedin showed activity in patients whose disease had come back after platinum chemotherapy. In 2020 the FDA gave it accelerated approval as Zepzelca, marketed in the US by Jazz Pharmaceuticals, for metastatic small cell lung cancer after platinum therapy. It was later approved for maintenance treatment with atezolizumab in extensive-stage disease.", "timeline": [["2000s", "PharmaMar designs lurbinectedin as a synthetic trabectedin analog."], ["2020", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Zepzelca for metastatic small cell lung cancer after platinum."]], "cell": "Lurbinectedin binds guanine residues in the minor groove of DNA, especially in GC-rich regions near gene promoters, forming covalent adducts that bend the helix. RNA polymerase II stalls on these adducts, is tagged and degraded by the proteasome, and transcription of many genes stops, including oncogenic transcription programs that small cell lung cancer depends on, such as those driven by the transcription factor ASCL1. The adducts also trigger DNA double-strand breaks and cell death, particularly in cells that rely on homologous recombination for repair. Like trabectedin, lurbinectedin reduces tumor-associated macrophages and the inflammatory signals they release.", "body": [["Small cell lung cancer", "Shutting down transcription programs that tumor cells depend on, and breaking their DNA, shrinks tumors in some patients after platinum therapy."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia is common, with the low point about one to two weeks after a dose, and febrile neutropenia and sepsis can occur."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise as the liver handles the drug."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting, constipation and diarrhea are common."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue and decreased appetite are frequent."], ["Infusion site", "Leakage outside the vein can cause tissue necrosis, so a central line is preferred."]], "journey": "Lurbinectedin is given as a one-hour infusion. It is about 99 percent protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP3A4, so strong or moderate CYP3A inhibitors (including grapefruit) raise levels and inducers lower them. About 89 percent leaves in the feces and a small fraction in urine. The terminal half-life is about two days.", "sidefx": "Neutropenia and other cytopenias come from DNA damage in dividing blood cell precursors, and they set the main safety limits. Liver enzyme rises reflect liver exposure. Nausea and fatigue are common with DNA-damaging chemotherapy. Extravasation necrosis is direct tissue toxicity. Rhabdomyolysis has been reported. CYP3A inhibitors raise all of these risks. There is no antidote; growth factor support is used for neutropenia.", "src": ["Zepzelca (lurbinectedin) FDA label", "Trigo J et al. Lurbinectedin as second-line treatment for SCLC. Lancet Oncol, 2020"]}, "treosulfan": {"origin": "Treosulfan is a sulfonate alkylating agent related to busulfan. It was developed in Germany and was used there for decades, first against ovarian cancer, before researchers recognized that it could empty the bone marrow with less liver and lung toxicity than busulfan. European transplant centers adopted treosulfan with fludarabine as a reduced-toxicity conditioning regimen, and it was approved for that use in the European Union. The FDA approved treosulfan as Grafapex in 2025, in combination with fludarabine, as conditioning before allogeneic stem cell transplant for AML and MDS.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Treosulfan is used in Germany for ovarian cancer."], ["2000s", "Treosulfan plus fludarabine is studied as transplant conditioning in Europe."], ["2025", "FDA approves Grafapex (treosulfan) with fludarabine for transplant conditioning in AML and MDS."]], "cell": "Treosulfan is a prodrug. Under the normal pH and temperature of the body, without enzymes, it converts step by step into a monoepoxide and then L-diepoxybutane. These epoxides are reactive alkylating agents that attack DNA, mainly at guanine, and form interstrand and intrastrand cross-links. The cross-links block replication and transcription and trigger apoptosis. Treosulfan is strongly toxic to hematopoietic stem cells and also kills lymphocytes, which supports both clearing the recipient's marrow and suppressing the immune system so donor cells can engraft. Combining it with fludarabine adds further immune suppression.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "It destroys the patient's marrow so donor stem cells can take its place, leaving profound low counts until engraftment."], ["Immune system", "Killing lymphocytes helps prevent rejection of the donor graft but raises infection risk."], ["Skin", "Darkening, redness and peeling of the skin can occur, especially in skin folds and the diaper area of young children."], ["Mouth and gut", "Stomatitis, nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are common."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises can occur, but serious liver vessel injury appears less common than with busulfan."], ["Brain", "Seizures have been reported, particularly in infants."], ["Muscles and joints", "Musculoskeletal pain is common during and after conditioning."]], "journey": "Treosulfan is given by vein over about two hours on several days. It converts nonenzymatically into its active epoxides in the blood and tissues, so it does not depend on liver enzymes. It has a half-life of about two hours. A portion leaves unchanged in the urine, and the rest is excreted as breakdown products.", "sidefx": "Profound cytopenias, infection and graft failure risk are part of the intended marrow destruction. Mucositis and gut upset come from killing dividing lining cells. Skin toxicity reflects drug reaching the skin and damaging dividing cells there, which is why skin hygiene in the diaper area matters for young children. Seizures may reflect central nervous system exposure. Secondary cancers can follow DNA damage. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Grafapex (treosulfan) FDA label", "Beelen DW et al. Treosulfan or busulfan plus fludarabine as conditioning in AML or MDS. Lancet Haematol, 2020"]}, "carboplatin": {"origin": "Carboplatin was created to make cisplatin easier to tolerate. Cisplatin's anticancer effect was discovered by Barnett Rosenberg at Michigan State University in 1965, when platinum electrodes in a bacterial culture stopped the bacteria from dividing. Cisplatin was powerful but very hard on the kidneys and nerves and caused severe vomiting. In the 1970s researchers at the Institute of Cancer Research in London, working with the platinum company Johnson Matthey and with Bristol-Myers, searched for gentler analogs. They found carboplatin, which replaces cisplatin's chloride groups with a more stable dicarboxylate ring. Hilary Calvert and colleagues later created the Calvert formula, which uses kidney function to plan carboplatin exposure. The FDA approved carboplatin (Paraplatin) in 1989.", "timeline": [["1965", "Barnett Rosenberg discovers that platinum compounds stop cell division."], ["1978", "FDA approves cisplatin."], ["1970s", "Carboplatin is developed in London as a less toxic cisplatin analog."], ["1989", "FDA approves carboplatin (Paraplatin)."], ["1989", "Calvert and colleagues publish a kidney function-based dosing formula."]], "cell": "Carboplatin is a platinum compound whose cyclobutanedicarboxylate ring comes off slowly in the body (aquation). The activated platinum then binds DNA, mainly at the N7 position of guanine. Most adducts are intrastrand cross-links between neighboring guanines, with fewer interstrand cross-links. These bend the DNA, block replication and transcription, and are recognized by damage-sensing proteins, leading to cell cycle arrest and apoptosis. It is cell cycle nonspecific. Carboplatin forms the same DNA adducts as cisplatin, but because its ring leaves more slowly, fewer reactive species form in the kidney and nerves, so it is less nephrotoxic, neurotoxic and emetogenic, while marrow suppression becomes the main limit. Cells resist through better DNA repair, reduced uptake and higher glutathione.", "body": [["Tumors", "DNA cross-links kill ovarian, lung, head and neck and other cancer cells."], ["Bone marrow", "Platelets and neutrophils fall, with the low point around three weeks, and thrombocytopenia is the dose-limiting effect."], ["Stomach and brain", "Nausea and vomiting occur, though less than with cisplatin."], ["Kidneys", "It is less toxic to the kidneys than cisplatin, but it still causes loss of magnesium, potassium, calcium and sodium."], ["Nerves and ears", "Peripheral neuropathy and hearing loss are possible but less common than with cisplatin."], ["Immune system", "Allergic reactions become more common after many cycles, sometimes severe."]], "journey": "Carboplatin is given by vein. It is not metabolized by liver enzymes; instead it slowly releases its ring through aquation. About 70 percent leaves in the urine within a day, mainly by glomerular filtration, so total exposure depends directly on kidney function, which is the basis of the Calvert formula. The half-life of free carboplatin is a few hours with normal kidneys, while platinum bound irreversibly to proteins lingers for days. Aluminum reacts with carboplatin, so aluminum needles and sets are avoided.", "sidefx": "Marrow suppression, especially of platelets, reflects DNA damage in dividing marrow cells and is predictable from kidney function and exposure. Electrolyte loss comes from kidney tubule effects. Nausea is less than with cisplatin because fewer reactive species reach the gut and brain quickly. Hypersensitivity reactions rise after six or more courses as the immune system becomes sensitized to platinum. Nerve and ear damage reflect platinum adducts in those cells. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Platinum's anticancer effect was discovered by accident when an electric current through platinum electrodes made E. coli grow into long filaments instead of dividing.", "src": ["Carboplatin injection FDA label", "Rosenberg B et al. Inhibition of cell division in Escherichia coli by electrolysis products from a platinum electrode. Nature, 1965", "Calvert AH et al. Carboplatin dosage: prospective evaluation of a simple formula. J Clin Oncol, 1989"]}, "oxaliplatin": {"origin": "Oxaliplatin was first synthesized in 1976 by Yoshinori Kidani at Nagoya City University in Japan, who was exploring platinum compounds that carry a diaminocyclohexane (DACH) group. The bulky DACH ring made oxaliplatin active against tumors that resisted cisplatin. The Swiss company Debiopharm developed it, and it was first approved in France in 1996. The FDA approved oxaliplatin (Eloxatin) in 2002 for colorectal cancer in combination with fluorouracil and leucovorin, the FOLFOX regimen, and it later became standard after surgery for stage III colon cancer.", "timeline": [["1976", "Yoshinori Kidani synthesizes oxaliplatin in Japan."], ["1996", "Oxaliplatin is first approved in France."], ["2002", "FDA approves oxaliplatin (Eloxatin) for colorectal cancer."], ["2004", "FDA approves oxaliplatin with fluorouracil and leucovorin after surgery for stage III colon cancer."]], "cell": "Oxaliplatin is a third-generation platinum compound. In the body its oxalate group is replaced by water and chloride, forming reactive platinum species that bind DNA at the N7 position of guanine and create mainly intrastrand cross-links. Because the platinum carries the bulky DACH ring, the adducts are larger and distort DNA differently from cisplatin and carboplatin adducts. Mismatch repair proteins do not recognize these adducts well, so tumors with defective mismatch repair, which resist cisplatin, can still respond. The adducts block DNA replication and transcription and trigger apoptosis. Oxaliplatin also induces immunogenic cell death and inhibits ribosome production, which may add to its effect. The released oxalate chelates calcium and affects voltage-gated sodium channels in nerves, which drives its acute neurotoxicity.", "body": [["Colon and rectal cancer", "DNA adducts kill colorectal cancer cells, especially combined with fluorouracil and leucovorin."], ["Peripheral nerves", "Acute cold-triggered tingling of the hands, feet and mouth occurs within hours; repeated cycles cause a cumulative, longer-lasting sensory neuropathy."], ["Throat", "Cold air or drinks can trigger a frightening sense of throat tightness (pharyngolaryngeal dysesthesia) without true airway blockage."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils and platelets fall moderately."], ["Liver and spleen", "Injury to liver sinusoids can enlarge the spleen and lower platelets over time."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are common."], ["Immune system", "Allergic reactions, sometimes severe, can occur after multiple cycles."]], "journey": "Oxaliplatin is given by vein, mixed only in dextrose because chloride solutions make it unstable. It transforms quickly and nonenzymatically into reactive platinum species, and much of the platinum binds irreversibly to plasma proteins and red blood cells. No CYP enzymes are involved. About half of the platinum leaves in the urine. Free platinum lingers with a very long terminal half-life of more than two weeks, reflecting slow release from tissues.", "sidefx": "Acute neuropathy is thought to come from oxalate binding calcium and altering voltage-gated sodium channels, making nerves overexcitable, especially with cold; this is why cold drinks and cold air trigger it. Chronic neuropathy reflects platinum building up in dorsal root ganglion neurons. Marrow suppression and gut effects reflect damage to dividing cells. Sinusoidal liver injury may relate to damage to the cells lining liver vessels. Hypersensitivity becomes more common with repeated exposure. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Eloxatin (oxaliplatin) FDA label", "Andre T et al. Oxaliplatin, fluorouracil and leucovorin as adjuvant treatment for colon cancer (MOSAIC). N Engl J Med, 2004"]}, "pemetrexed": {"origin": "Pemetrexed was designed in the 1990s through a collaboration between Edward C. Taylor, an organic chemist at Princeton University, and Eli Lilly. Taylor had been working on folate-like molecules built on a pyrrolo-pyrimidine core. The goal was an antifolate that would block several folate enzymes at once and be retained inside cancer cells. Early trials showed severe toxicity in some patients, and researchers found that people with low folate and vitamin B12 status were most at risk. Giving folic acid and B12 alongside made the drug much safer. The FDA approved pemetrexed (Alimta) in 2004 for mesothelioma with cisplatin, and later for nonsquamous non-small cell lung cancer.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Edward C. Taylor and Eli Lilly develop pemetrexed."], ["1999", "Vitamin supplementation is introduced to reduce pemetrexed toxicity."], ["2004", "FDA approves Alimta for malignant pleural mesothelioma with cisplatin."], ["2008", "FDA approves pemetrexed with cisplatin as first-line treatment for nonsquamous NSCLC."]], "cell": "Pemetrexed enters cells mainly through the reduced folate carrier. Inside, folylpolyglutamate synthetase adds several glutamate groups, which traps the drug in the cell and makes it a more potent inhibitor. The polyglutamated drug blocks several folate-dependent enzymes: thymidylate synthase (TS), its main target, which makes thymidine for DNA; dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR), which regenerates reduced folate; and glycinamide ribonucleotide formyltransferase (GARFT), needed for purine synthesis. Cells run short of thymidine and purines, DNA synthesis stalls in S phase and cells die. Nonsquamous lung cancers tend to express less thymidylate synthase than squamous tumors, which helps explain why they respond better.", "body": [["Lung and pleura", "Blocking DNA building blocks kills nonsquamous lung cancer and mesothelioma cells."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils, platelets and red cells fall, with the low point about eight to ten days after a dose; vitamin supplements reduce this."], ["Mouth and gut", "Mucositis, nausea and loss of appetite reflect damage to dividing lining cells."], ["Skin", "Rash is common and is reduced by dexamethasone premedication."], ["Kidneys", "It is cleared by the kidneys, so poor kidney function raises exposure; kidney injury can occur."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis is an uncommon but serious effect."]], "journey": "Pemetrexed is given by vein over a short infusion. About 81 percent is protein bound. It is not significantly metabolized. The kidneys clear 70 to 90 percent unchanged in the urine within a day, by filtration and tubular secretion. The half-life is about three and a half hours with normal kidney function. NSAIDs such as ibuprofen reduce its clearance in people with reduced kidney function.", "sidefx": "Marrow suppression, mucositis and diarrhea come from starving dividing normal cells of DNA building blocks. Low folate and B12 make this much worse, because the cells have less reserve to recover, so folic acid and B12 are given before and during treatment. Rash appears to be an inflammatory skin reaction, which dexamethasone prevents. Kidney injury raises levels and toxicity. Leucovorin can be used to rescue normal cells in severe toxicity or overdose.", "src": ["Alimta (pemetrexed) FDA label", "Vogelzang NJ et al. Pemetrexed plus cisplatin in mesothelioma. J Clin Oncol, 2003"]}, "pralatrexate": {"origin": "Pralatrexate was designed by researchers at Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center and SRI International, including Francis Sirotnak and Joseph DeGraw, as an improved version of methotrexate. Their goal was an antifolate that cancer cells would take up and trap far better than methotrexate. Allos Therapeutics developed it, and in 2009 the FDA approved it as Folotyn for relapsed or refractory peripheral T-cell lymphoma, making it the first drug approved for that disease.", "timeline": [["1948", "Aminopterin, an early antifolate, produces remissions in childhood leukemia."], ["1990s", "Pralatrexate is designed at Memorial Sloan Kettering and SRI International."], ["2009", "FDA approves Folotyn, the first drug for relapsed peripheral T-cell lymphoma."]], "cell": "Pralatrexate is a folate analog. It has very high affinity for reduced folate carrier type 1 (RFC-1), the main transporter that brings folates into cells, and many cancer cells overexpress this carrier. Once inside, folylpolyglutamate synthetase adds glutamate groups, which traps the drug in the cell. Pralatrexate is both a better substrate for RFC-1 and for this enzyme than methotrexate, so it builds up to higher levels in tumor cells. The polyglutamated drug competitively inhibits dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR), so the cell cannot regenerate tetrahydrofolate. Without reduced folates, thymidylate and purine synthesis stop, DNA synthesis fails in S phase and the lymphoma cells die.", "body": [["Lymphoma cells", "High uptake and trapping of the drug in malignant T cells starves them of DNA building blocks, shrinking some peripheral T-cell lymphomas."], ["Mouth", "Mucositis is the main dose-limiting side effect, often appearing in the first cycle; folic acid and B12 reduce it."], ["Bone marrow", "Platelets especially fall, along with neutrophils and red cells."], ["Skin", "Rash is common, and severe skin reactions such as toxic epidermal necrolysis can occur."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme levels can rise during treatment and are checked regularly."], ["Metabolism", "Tumor lysis syndrome can occur with rapid tumor breakdown."]], "journey": "Pralatrexate is given as a short IV push. It is about two thirds protein bound. It is not significantly metabolized by CYP enzymes. About a third leaves unchanged in the urine and some in the feces. The plasma half-life is about twelve to eighteen hours, while polyglutamated drug persists longer inside cells. Probenecid, NSAIDs and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole can slow its renal clearance.", "sidefx": "Mucositis, low platelets and other cytopenias come from starving rapidly dividing normal cells of folate-dependent building blocks. Folic acid and vitamin B12 are given before and during treatment because a folate-poor state makes normal cells more vulnerable. Skin reactions reflect damage to dividing skin cells and immune mechanisms. Reduced kidney clearance raises exposure. Leucovorin can be used to bypass the DHFR block in normal cells in severe toxicity.", "src": ["Folotyn (pralatrexate) FDA label", "O'Connor OA et al. Pralatrexate in relapsed or refractory peripheral T-cell lymphoma (PROPEL). J Clin Oncol, 2011"]}, "gemcitabine": {"origin": "Gemcitabine was first made in the early 1980s by Larry Hertel, a chemist at Eli Lilly, who was building fluorinated sugars and attached two fluorine atoms to deoxycytidine. The compound was first explored as an antiviral, but lab testing showed strong activity against cancer cells, including solid tumors that resisted cytarabine, its closest relative. A landmark trial showed that gemcitabine eased symptoms and improved survival in advanced pancreatic cancer compared with fluorouracil, and the FDA approved it as Gemzar in 1996. It later became part of standard regimens for lung, breast, ovarian, bladder and biliary cancers.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Larry Hertel at Eli Lilly synthesizes gemcitabine."], ["1996", "FDA approves Gemzar for advanced pancreatic cancer."], ["1998", "FDA approves gemcitabine with cisplatin for non-small cell lung cancer."], ["2013", "Gemcitabine with nab-paclitaxel is approved for metastatic pancreatic cancer."]], "cell": "Gemcitabine (2',2'-difluorodeoxycytidine) enters cells through nucleoside transporters, mainly hENT1. Deoxycytidine kinase adds the first phosphate, which is the rate-limiting step, and other kinases make the diphosphate and triphosphate. The diphosphate (dFdCDP) inhibits ribonucleotide reductase, lowering the cell's supply of normal deoxynucleotides, especially dCTP. The triphosphate (dFdCTP) competes with dCTP and is built into DNA. After it is added, DNA polymerase adds one more normal nucleotide and then stops, a process called masked chain termination, which hides the drug from proofreading repair. Lower dCTP levels also mean less competition for dFdCTP and less breakdown of the drug, a self-potentiation loop. DNA synthesis stalls and cells die, mainly in S phase and at the G1/S boundary.", "body": [["Tumors", "Blocking DNA synthesis kills pancreatic, lung, breast, ovarian and bladder cancer cells."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils, platelets and red cells fall, with the low point about one week to ten days after a dose."], ["Whole body", "A flu-like illness with fever, chills and muscle aches is common, especially after the first doses."], ["Lungs", "Rarely it causes pneumonitis, capillary leak or acute respiratory distress."], ["Kidneys and blood", "Hemolytic uremic syndrome, a rare but serious form of small vessel damage, can cause kidney failure."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes often rise mildly and are usually monitored during treatment."], ["Skin and swelling", "Rash and swelling of the legs (edema) are common."]], "journey": "Gemcitabine is given by vein, usually over thirty minutes. Infusion time matters, because longer infusions increase marrow toxicity. In the blood and liver, cytidine deaminase quickly converts it to an inactive metabolite, dFdU, so the plasma half-life is short, about forty to ninety minutes. Inside tumor cells, the active triphosphate persists much longer. More than 90 percent of the dose leaves in the urine, mostly as dFdU.", "sidefx": "Marrow suppression reflects stalled DNA synthesis in dividing blood precursors. The flu-like syndrome likely relates to cytokine release. Rare lung injury, capillary leak and hemolytic uremic syndrome appear to involve damage to the endothelium, the lining of small blood vessels. Gemcitabine is a strong radiosensitizer, so radiation given at the same time can cause severe tissue injury. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "src": ["Gemzar (gemcitabine) FDA label", "Burris HA et al. Gemcitabine versus fluorouracil in advanced pancreatic cancer. J Clin Oncol, 1997"]}, "azacitidine": {"origin": "Azacitidine was synthesized in 1964 by the Czech chemists Alois Piskala and Frantisek Sorm in Prague, who made cytidine analogs in which a nitrogen replaces carbon 5 of the base. It was first tested as a standard cytotoxic chemotherapy drug for leukemia. In the late 1970s and early 1980s, researchers including Peter Jones and Shirley Taylor discovered that it blocks DNA methylation and can switch silenced genes back on. Low-dose schedules were then studied in myelodysplastic syndromes. In 2004 the FDA approved azacitidine (Vidaza) as the first drug for MDS, and a later trial showed it improved overall survival. An oral form, Onureg, is a separate product for maintenance in AML.", "timeline": [["1964", "Piskala and Sorm synthesize azacitidine in Czechoslovakia."], ["1980", "Jones and Taylor show azacitidine inhibits DNA methylation and changes gene expression."], ["2004", "FDA approves Vidaza, the first drug for myelodysplastic syndromes."], ["2009", "A phase 3 trial shows azacitidine improves survival in higher-risk MDS."], ["2020", "FDA approves oral azacitidine (Onureg) for AML maintenance."]], "cell": "Azacitidine is a cytidine analog with a nitrogen at position 5 of the ring. It enters cells through nucleoside transporters and is phosphorylated by uridine-cytidine kinase. Most of it (about 80 to 90 percent) is built into RNA, where it disrupts protein synthesis and kills cells. A smaller part is converted by ribonucleotide reductase into the deoxy form and built into DNA. In DNA, when DNA methyltransferase 1 (DNMT1) tries to methylate the abnormal base, the enzyme becomes trapped in a covalent bond and is then degraded. As cells divide without DNMT1, methylation marks are lost, so tumor suppressor and differentiation genes that were silenced by promoter methylation can switch back on. At higher exposures, the trapped DNMT adducts and RNA damage cause direct cytotoxicity.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Abnormal MDS cells are killed or pushed to mature, and over months many patients make more normal blood cells and need fewer transfusions."], ["Blood counts", "Neutrophils, platelets and red cells often fall first, especially in the early cycles, before any improvement."], ["Injection sites", "Under the skin it causes redness, bruising and pain at injection sites."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting, constipation and diarrhea are common."], ["Kidneys", "Rarely it causes renal tubular acidosis, rising creatinine and low potassium."], ["Liver", "In people with widespread liver tumors, it can worsen liver function."]], "journey": "Azacitidine is given under the skin or by vein over several days each cycle. Under the skin it is absorbed quickly and peaks in about half an hour. It is unstable in solution and breaks down by spontaneous hydrolysis and by cytidine deaminase, so its half-life is short, under an hour. Its effects on methylation, though, accumulate as cells divide, and responses take several cycles, often months. Most of the drug and its breakdown products leave in the urine.", "sidefx": "Low blood counts happen because azacitidine kills dividing marrow cells, normal and abnormal, before normal blood production recovers; infections and bleeding follow from this. Nausea reflects effects on the gut and vomiting center. Injection site reactions are local irritation. Kidney effects reflect toxicity to tubule cells. Liver problems are more likely when the liver is already heavily involved by tumor. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Vidaza (azacitidine) FDA label", "Jones PA, Taylor SM. Cellular differentiation, cytidine analogs and DNA methylation. Cell, 1980", "Fenaux P et al. Azacitidine versus conventional care in higher-risk MDS. Lancet Oncol, 2009"]}, "decitabine": {"origin": "Decitabine (5-aza-2'-deoxycytidine) was synthesized in 1964 by Alois Piskala and Frantisek Sorm in Prague, alongside its relative azacitidine. Unlike azacitidine, decitabine is a deoxyribose nucleoside, so it is built only into DNA, not RNA. It was first tested at high cytotoxic doses for leukemia. Work in the 1980s showed it is a powerful inhibitor of DNA methylation, and lower-dose schedules were developed to reactivate silenced genes in abnormal marrow cells. The FDA approved decitabine (Dacogen) in 2006 for myelodysplastic syndromes. In 2020 an oral tablet, Inqovi, combined decitabine with cedazuridine, which blocks the gut and liver enzyme that would otherwise destroy decitabine.", "timeline": [["1964", "Piskala and Sorm synthesize decitabine in Czechoslovakia."], ["1980s", "Decitabine is shown to be a potent inhibitor of DNA methylation."], ["2006", "FDA approves Dacogen for myelodysplastic syndromes."], ["2020", "FDA approves Inqovi, oral decitabine with cedazuridine."]], "cell": "Decitabine enters cells through nucleoside transporters and is phosphorylated by deoxycytidine kinase to its monophosphate and then to decitabine triphosphate. DNA polymerase builds it into newly copied DNA in place of cytosine during S phase. When DNA methyltransferase 1 (DNMT1) tries to methylate the abnormal base, it forms a covalent bond and is trapped, and the cell then degrades the enzyme. With DNMT1 depleted, methylation patterns are not copied to the new strands as cells divide, so promoters of silenced tumor suppressor and differentiation genes become unmethylated and active again. Abnormal cells mature or die. At higher exposures, the trapped DNMT-DNA adducts themselves cause DNA damage and cytotoxicity. Because it acts only during DNA synthesis, it affects dividing cells.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Over several cycles, abnormal MDS clones shrink or mature, and normal blood production can improve."], ["Blood counts", "Neutropenia, thrombocytopenia and anemia are common, especially early, before counts recover."], ["Immune system", "Low white cell counts raise the risk of fever, pneumonia and other infections."], ["Bleeding", "Low platelets cause bruising, petechiae and bleeding."], ["Gut and whole body", "Nausea, constipation, diarrhea, fatigue and fever are common."], ["Metabolism", "High blood sugar has been reported during treatment."]], "journey": "IV decitabine is infused over one or three hours depending on the schedule. It is barely protein bound. It is quickly broken down by cytidine deaminase in the liver and other tissues, giving a plasma half-life of only about half an hour, while its effect on methylation builds across cycles. Only a small fraction leaves unchanged in the urine. Taken by mouth alone, it would be destroyed by cytidine deaminase in the gut and liver, which is why the oral product pairs it with the deaminase inhibitor cedazuridine.", "sidefx": "Cytopenias reflect damage to dividing marrow cells, both normal and abnormal, and they lead to infections and bleeding until counts recover. Gut effects and fatigue are common with chemotherapy. Decitabine does not have the RNA effects of azacitidine, but its marrow effects are similar. It can harm a developing fetus because it disrupts DNA methylation, which is vital in early development. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Dacogen (decitabine) FDA label", "Inqovi (decitabine and cedazuridine) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "fludarabine": {"origin": "Fludarabine came from the work of John Montgomery and colleagues at the Southern Research Institute in Birmingham, Alabama. The antiviral and anticancer nucleoside vidarabine (ara-A) was quickly destroyed by the enzyme adenosine deaminase. In the 1960s Montgomery's team added a fluorine atom at the 2 position of the adenine base, which made the molecule resistant to adenosine deaminase. A phosphate was added to make it soluble for IV use, creating fludarabine phosphate. The FDA approved it as Fludara in 1991 for B-cell chronic lymphocytic leukemia. It became widely used with cyclophosphamide and rituximab for CLL, and today it is a central part of lymphodepletion before CAR-T cell therapy and of conditioning before stem cell transplant.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Montgomery's team at Southern Research Institute synthesizes 2-fluoro-ara-A."], ["1991", "FDA approves Fludara for B-cell CLL."], ["2010s", "Fludarabine with cyclophosphamide becomes standard lymphodepletion before CAR-T therapy."]], "cell": "Fludarabine phosphate is a prodrug. In the blood it is quickly dephosphorylated to 2-fluoro-ara-A, which enters cells through nucleoside transporters. Inside, deoxycytidine kinase phosphorylates it, and other kinases make the active triphosphate, F-ara-ATP. F-ara-ATP inhibits several enzymes: ribonucleotide reductase (reducing deoxynucleotide supplies), DNA polymerases and DNA primase, and DNA ligase. It is built into DNA and halts chain elongation, and it is also built into RNA and impairs RNA synthesis. Importantly, it interferes with DNA repair, so it kills resting (non-dividing) lymphocytes as well as dividing cells by triggering apoptosis. Lymphocytes have high deoxycytidine kinase activity, which concentrates the active drug in them.", "body": [["Leukemia and lymphoma cells", "Active drug accumulates in malignant B lymphocytes and kills both dividing and resting cells in CLL and indolent lymphomas."], ["Immune system", "It causes deep, long-lasting loss of CD4 T cells and other lymphocytes, lasting months to over a year, which is useful before CAR-T or transplant but raises infection risk."], ["Infections", "Opportunistic infections such as Pneumocystis pneumonia, herpes zoster and cytomegalovirus are more common, so prevention is often used."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils, platelets and red cells fall, with low points about two weeks after a dose."], ["Red blood cells", "Autoimmune hemolytic anemia can develop or worsen."], ["Brain and nerves", "At high doses it can cause severe neurotoxicity, including blindness, coma and death; lower doses can cause milder confusion or neuropathy."], ["Blood transfusions", "Because of severe T-cell suppression, irradiated blood products are used to prevent transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease."]], "journey": "Fludarabine phosphate is given by vein and is converted almost immediately in the blood to 2-fluoro-ara-A. That form spreads to tissues and enters cells, where it is phosphorylated to the active triphosphate, which stays inside lymphocytes for many hours. About 60 percent leaves in the urine, so kidney impairment raises exposure and toxicity. The half-life of 2-fluoro-ara-A is about twenty hours.", "sidefx": "Profound lymphocyte loss is the main effect and also the main risk: it explains opportunistic infections and the need for irradiated blood. Marrow suppression reflects damage to dividing precursors. Autoimmune hemolytic anemia may come from disrupted immune regulation as T cells are depleted. Neurotoxicity is dose-related and reflects nucleoside analog toxicity in neurons. Combining it with pentostatin caused fatal lung toxicity in trials, so they are not used together. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Fludarabine phosphate injection FDA label", "Montgomery JA, Hewson K. Nucleosides of 2-fluoroadenine. J Med Chem, 1969", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "cladribine": {"origin": "Cladribine (2-chlorodeoxyadenosine, 2-CdA) was developed into a drug by Dennis Carson and colleagues at the Scripps Clinic in California in the early 1980s. They were inspired by a rare disease: children with inherited adenosine deaminase (ADA) deficiency lose their lymphocytes because toxic deoxyadenosine products build up in them. Carson's team reasoned that a deoxyadenosine analog that ADA could not break down would selectively kill lymphocytes. Adding a chlorine to deoxyadenosine did exactly that. A single seven-day infusion produced long-lasting complete remissions in most patients with hairy cell leukemia, and the FDA approved cladribine (Leustatin) in 1993. An oral form, Mavenclad, was approved in 2019 for relapsing multiple sclerosis.", "timeline": [["1972", "Inherited adenosine deaminase deficiency is linked to severe combined immunodeficiency."], ["1980s", "Dennis Carson's group at Scripps develops cladribine as a lymphocyte-targeting drug."], ["1990", "A single seven-day course is reported to produce durable remissions in hairy cell leukemia."], ["1993", "FDA approves Leustatin for hairy cell leukemia."], ["2019", "FDA approves oral cladribine (Mavenclad) for relapsing multiple sclerosis."]], "cell": "Cladribine is a deoxyadenosine analog with a chlorine at position 2, which makes it resistant to adenosine deaminase. It enters cells through nucleoside transporters and is phosphorylated by deoxycytidine kinase to the monophosphate, then to the active triphosphate (Cd-ATP). Lymphocytes have high deoxycytidine kinase activity and low activity of 5'-nucleotidase, the enzyme that removes the phosphate, so the drug accumulates selectively in them. Cd-ATP inhibits ribonucleotide reductase, is built into DNA and causes DNA strand breaks. In resting cells, repair of these breaks uses up NAD and ATP through PARP activation, and the cell dies of energy depletion and apoptosis. This lets cladribine kill both dividing and resting lymphocytes, including the slow-growing cells of hairy cell leukemia.", "body": [["Hairy cell leukemia", "Malignant B cells trap the active drug and die, so a single course often produces long remissions."], ["Immune system", "CD4 T cells and B cells fall sharply and stay low for months, which in multiple sclerosis reduces the immune attack on the nervous system."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutrophil counts fall, with the low point about one to two weeks after treatment, often causing fever."], ["Infections", "Low lymphocytes raise the risk of opportunistic infections such as herpes zoster."], ["Nerves and kidneys", "Very high doses, beyond those used today, caused severe nerve damage and kidney failure in early studies."], ["Skin", "Rash and injection site reactions can occur."]], "journey": "IV cladribine is given as a continuous infusion for seven days. Oral cladribine, used in multiple sclerosis, is absorbed moderately. About 20 percent is protein bound. Cladribine enters cells and is trapped as its phosphorylated forms, especially in lymphocytes, where it persists well beyond the plasma half-life of about five hours. The kidneys clear much of the drug, so kidney impairment raises exposure.", "sidefx": "Lymphocyte depletion explains both the benefit and the infection risk, and irradiated blood products are commonly used to prevent transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease. Neutropenic fever is common in the first weeks of treatment. Neurotoxicity and kidney injury were seen with much higher doses and reflect nucleoside analog toxicity in those tissues. Tumor lysis can occur with a high tumor burden. Because it damages DNA, it may increase cancer risk, which is noted for the oral MS product. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Cladribine injection FDA label", "Mavenclad (cladribine) FDA label", "Piro LD, Carrera CJ, Carson DA et al. Lasting remissions in hairy-cell leukemia induced by a single infusion of 2-chlorodeoxyadenosine. N Engl J Med, 1990"]}, "clofarabine": {"origin": "Clofarabine was designed at the Southern Research Institute in Birmingham, Alabama, by John Montgomery and John Secrist and colleagues, building on their work with fludarabine and with cladribine. It combines features of both: a chlorine at position 2 of the adenine (like cladribine) and a fluorine on the sugar (which makes it stable in stomach acid and resistant to phosphorylase enzymes). It was developed by Ilex Oncology and Genzyme, and in 2004 the FDA approved it as Clolar for children and young adults with relapsed or refractory acute lymphoblastic leukemia after at least two prior regimens. It was the first new leukemia drug approved specifically for children in more than a decade.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Southern Research Institute chemists design clofarabine as a next-generation purine analog."], ["2004", "FDA approves Clolar for relapsed or refractory pediatric acute lymphoblastic leukemia."]], "cell": "Clofarabine is a deoxyadenosine analog resistant to adenosine deaminase and to purine nucleoside phosphorylase. It enters cells through nucleoside transporters and is phosphorylated by deoxycytidine kinase and then other kinases to clofarabine triphosphate. The triphosphate strongly inhibits ribonucleotide reductase, depleting deoxynucleotide pools, and competes with dATP for DNA polymerases, so it is built into DNA and stops chain elongation. It also damages mitochondria, causing release of cytochrome c and apoptosis-inducing factor, which triggers apoptosis directly. This combination kills both dividing and some non-dividing leukemia cells.", "body": [["Leukemia cells", "Active drug accumulates in leukemic lymphoblasts and kills them, clearing the marrow in some children with relapsed ALL."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils, platelets and red cells fall sharply, with serious infection risk."], ["Blood vessels", "Systemic inflammatory response syndrome and capillary leak, with fever, low blood pressure, fluid shifts and breathing trouble, can occur, probably from cytokine release as leukemia cells die."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes and bilirubin often rise, and veno-occlusive disease can occur, especially after transplant."], ["Kidneys", "Acute kidney injury can occur, and kidney function affects clearance."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea and enterocolitis are common."], ["Skin", "Rash, itching and, rarely, severe skin reactions can occur."]], "journey": "Clofarabine is given by vein over two hours on several days in a row. About 47 percent is protein bound. It is not heavily metabolized by the liver; instead it is phosphorylated inside cells, where the triphosphate persists. About half of the dose leaves unchanged in the urine, so kidney impairment raises exposure. The plasma half-life is about five hours.", "sidefx": "Marrow suppression and infections reflect killing of dividing marrow cells. Capillary leak and the inflammatory syndrome are thought to come from cytokines released as large numbers of leukemia cells die; tumor lysis syndrome follows the same rapid cell death, so fluids are given during dosing. Liver and kidney toxicity reflect direct nucleoside analog injury and are made worse by other liver- or kidney-toxic drugs. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Clolar (clofarabine) FDA label", "Jeha S et al. Phase II study of clofarabine in pediatric patients with refractory or relapsed ALL. J Clin Oncol, 2006"]}, "nelarabine": {"origin": "Nelarabine grew out of purine research at Burroughs Wellcome, the company where Gertrude Elion and George Hitchings pioneered purine-based drugs. Children born without the enzyme purine nucleoside phosphorylase (PNP) lose their T cells, because deoxyguanosine builds up and is converted to dGTP, which is toxic to T cells. Researchers reasoned that a guanine analog resistant to PNP could selectively kill T cells. Ara-G (9-beta-D-arabinofuranosylguanine) fit that idea but was very hard to dissolve. The Burroughs Wellcome team made nelarabine, a soluble prodrug that the body converts to ara-G. Glaxo Wellcome and later GlaxoSmithKline developed it, and the FDA approved it as Arranon in 2005 for T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia and T-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma that relapsed after at least two regimens.", "timeline": [["1975", "Purine nucleoside phosphorylase deficiency is linked to selective T-cell immunodeficiency."], ["1990s", "Nelarabine is developed as a soluble prodrug of ara-G."], ["2005", "FDA approves Arranon for relapsed T-cell ALL and lymphoblastic lymphoma."]], "cell": "Nelarabine is a prodrug. In the blood, adenosine deaminase removes its methoxy group, converting it to ara-G. Ara-G enters cells through nucleoside transporters and is phosphorylated by deoxyguanosine kinase and deoxycytidine kinase, then by other kinases, to ara-GTP. T cells, especially malignant T lymphoblasts, build up much more ara-GTP than B cells, probably because of higher kinase activity and slower breakdown. Ara-GTP competes with dGTP for DNA polymerase and is built into DNA, which halts DNA synthesis and triggers apoptosis. This T-cell selectivity mirrors the T-cell loss seen in inherited PNP deficiency.", "body": [["Leukemia and lymphoma cells", "Malignant T lymphoblasts trap ara-GTP and die, producing responses in relapsed T-cell ALL and lymphoblastic lymphoma."], ["Brain", "Drowsiness, confusion, seizures and ataxia can occur, and the risk is higher after prior intrathecal chemotherapy or craniospinal radiation."], ["Peripheral nerves", "Numbness, tingling, weakness and, rarely, ascending paralysis resembling Guillain-Barre syndrome can occur and may not fully reverse."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils, platelets and red cells fall, with the low point about one to two weeks after treatment."], ["Gut and whole body", "Nausea, diarrhea, fatigue and fever are common."], ["Metabolism", "Rapid tumor breakdown can cause tumor lysis syndrome."]], "journey": "Nelarabine is given by vein over one to two hours. It is converted quickly in the blood by adenosine deaminase to ara-G, so nelarabine itself lasts only about thirty minutes, while ara-G has a half-life of about three hours. Ara-G spreads into tissues, including the central nervous system, and is phosphorylated inside cells, where ara-GTP persists. Ara-G is also broken down to guanine. Part of the drug and its products leave in the urine. Pentostatin, an adenosine deaminase inhibitor, blocks conversion to ara-G, so the two are not combined.", "sidefx": "Neurotoxicity is the dose-limiting and most serious effect. It appears to come from ara-G reaching the brain and nerves and damaging neurons and myelin, and it can be severe or permanent, which is why neurologic checks are done before each dose. Marrow suppression reflects DNA synthesis inhibition in dividing precursors. Tumor lysis follows rapid leukemia cell death. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped for serious neurologic effects.", "src": ["Arranon (nelarabine) FDA label", "Giblett ER et al. Nucleoside-phosphorylase deficiency in a child with severely defective T-cell immunity. Lancet, 1975", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "pentostatin": {"origin": "Pentostatin (2'-deoxycoformycin) is a natural product of the bacterium Streptomyces antibioticus. Scientists at Parke-Davis isolated it from fermentation broth in the early 1970s while searching for compounds that would protect the antiviral drug vidarabine from being destroyed by adenosine deaminase. It turned out to be one of the most potent adenosine deaminase inhibitors known. Researchers then realized that blocking this enzyme could mimic inherited adenosine deaminase deficiency, a condition in which lymphocytes die. Pentostatin proved highly effective in hairy cell leukemia, and the FDA approved it as Nipent in 1991.", "timeline": [["1972", "Inherited adenosine deaminase deficiency is linked to severe combined immunodeficiency."], ["1974", "Pentostatin is reported from Streptomyces antibioticus fermentation."], ["1980s", "Pentostatin produces high response rates in hairy cell leukemia."], ["1991", "FDA approves Nipent for hairy cell leukemia."]], "cell": "Pentostatin is a transition-state analog that binds adenosine deaminase (ADA) so tightly that inhibition is nearly irreversible. ADA normally breaks down adenosine and deoxyadenosine. With ADA blocked, deoxyadenosine accumulates and lymphocytes, which have high deoxycytidine kinase and low 5'-nucleotidase activity, convert it to dATP. High dATP inhibits ribonucleotide reductase, starving the cell of other deoxynucleotides needed for DNA synthesis and repair. Deoxyadenosine also inactivates S-adenosylhomocysteine hydrolase, disrupting cellular methylation reactions. DNA strand breaks accumulate, PARP activation depletes NAD and ATP, and the cells die by apoptosis. Because this works in resting cells, it is effective against slow-growing hairy cell leukemia.", "body": [["Hairy cell leukemia", "Toxic deoxyadenosine products build up in malignant B cells and kill them, producing long remissions."], ["Immune system", "Lymphocyte counts, especially CD4 T cells, fall and stay low for months, raising infection risk."], ["Kidneys", "It is cleared by the kidneys and can cause kidney toxicity, so hydration is given and kidney function guides use."], ["Brain", "At high doses, lethargy, confusion and coma have occurred."], ["Eyes", "Keratoconjunctivitis and other eye irritation can occur."], ["Lungs", "Combined with fludarabine it caused severe or fatal lung toxicity, so the two are not given together."], ["Gut and whole body", "Nausea, vomiting, fever, rash and fatigue are common."]], "journey": "Pentostatin is given by vein as a bolus or short infusion, with IV fluids before and after. It is barely protein bound and spreads through body water, including some entry into the cerebrospinal fluid. It is metabolized very little. The kidneys excrete most of it unchanged, with a half-life of about six hours that lengthens with kidney impairment. ADA inhibition lasts for days, much longer than the drug stays in plasma, because binding is so tight.", "sidefx": "Lymphocyte depletion explains both its benefit and the infections that follow. Kidney and nervous system toxicity are dose related and were severe at the high doses used in early studies. Combining it with fludarabine caused fatal pulmonary toxicity, and ADA inhibition also blocks the activation of nelarabine. It also strengthens the toxicity of vidarabine, the drug it was first meant to protect. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Pentostatin was found while scientists were looking for a helper drug to protect an antiviral, and it became a leukemia treatment by mimicking a rare inherited immune disease.", "src": ["Nipent (pentostatin) FDA label", "Woo PWK et al. A new adenosine deaminase inhibitor from Streptomyces antibioticus. J Heterocycl Chem, 1974", "Giblett ER et al. Adenosine-deaminase deficiency in two patients with severely impaired cellular immunity. Lancet, 1972"]}, "floxuridine": {"origin": "Floxuridine (5-fluoro-2'-deoxyuridine, FUDR) was created alongside fluorouracil in the late 1950s. Charles Heidelberger at the University of Wisconsin noticed that tumor cells used the base uracil more than normal cells did and reasoned that a fluorinated uracil could block DNA synthesis. Working with Robert Duschinsky and colleagues at Hoffmann-La Roche, he made fluorouracil in 1957 and then floxuridine, its deoxyribose nucleoside. Because the liver removes most floxuridine on its first pass, it became ideal for delivering very high doses directly into the hepatic artery to treat colorectal cancer that had spread to the liver, while sparing the rest of the body. It is used through implanted pumps and catheters in specialized centers.", "timeline": [["1957", "Heidelberger and Duschinsky report fluorouracil as an anticancer agent."], ["1958", "Floxuridine, the deoxyribonucleoside of fluorouracil, is described."], ["1980s", "Implanted pumps allow continuous hepatic arterial floxuridine for liver metastases."]], "cell": "Floxuridine is a prodrug of the active nucleotide FdUMP. Thymidine kinase phosphorylates it directly to FdUMP, a shorter path than fluorouracil takes. FdUMP binds thymidylate synthase together with the folate cofactor 5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate, forming a stable three-part complex that locks the enzyme. Thymidylate synthase normally makes thymidine monophosphate from deoxyuridine monophosphate, so the cell runs short of thymidine for DNA (thymineless death), and uracil is mistakenly added to DNA, causing breaks during repair. Floxuridine is also partly converted to fluorouracil, whose metabolites are incorporated into RNA and add to toxicity. The effect is greatest in S phase. Leucovorin raises the folate cofactor and stabilizes the complex, increasing the effect.", "body": [["Liver metastases", "Delivered into the hepatic artery, which feeds liver tumors, it reaches very high concentrations in tumor tissue and kills colorectal cancer cells there."], ["Liver and bile ducts", "High local drug levels can inflame the liver and scar the bile ducts (sclerosing cholangitis), sometimes permanently."], ["Stomach and duodenum", "If drug leaks into arteries that feed the stomach, ulcers and gastritis can develop."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, cramps and mouth sores can occur."], ["Bone marrow", "Blood counts fall less than with systemic fluoropyrimidines because the liver captures most of the drug."], ["Catheter and pump", "The catheter can clot, move or become infected."]], "journey": "Floxuridine is infused continuously through a catheter placed in the hepatic artery, usually by an implanted pump. The liver extracts most of the drug on its first pass, often more than 90 percent, so liver tumor levels are very high while blood levels stay low. The liver breaks it down to fluorouracil and inactive metabolites through the DPD pathway. Metabolites leave in the urine, and some carbon is breathed out as carbon dioxide. It clears from plasma quickly.", "sidefx": "Because the liver captures the drug, the main toxicities are in the liver and bile ducts: chemical hepatitis and biliary sclerosis come from high local concentrations damaging bile duct cells, which depend heavily on the hepatic artery for blood. Peptic ulcers come from drug reaching stomach and duodenal branches, which is why catheter position is confirmed before infusion. Gut and marrow toxicity reflect thymidylate synthase inhibition in dividing cells. Uridine triacetate is an FDA-approved antidote for fluorouracil or capecitabine overdose and severe early toxicity.", "src": ["Floxuridine for injection FDA label", "Heidelberger C et al. Fluorinated pyrimidines, a new class of tumour-inhibitory compounds. Nature, 1957"]}, "daunorubicin-cytarabine-liposome": {"origin": "For decades, newly diagnosed AML was treated with the 7+3 regimen, cytarabine for seven days and daunorubicin for three days, developed in the early 1970s. Researchers at Celator Pharmaceuticals in Vancouver, Canada, studied many ratios of the two drugs in the lab and found that a 1:5 molar ratio of daunorubicin to cytarabine was especially synergistic. The challenge was keeping that ratio after injection, because free drugs leave the blood at different rates. Their CombiPlex technology packed both drugs together inside liposomes that keep the ratio fixed and deliver it to leukemia cells. Jazz Pharmaceuticals acquired Celator in 2016, and in 2017 the FDA approved the product, CPX-351, as Vyxeos for therapy-related AML and AML with myelodysplasia-related changes, the first approval to show a survival advantage over 7+3 in these high-risk groups.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Daunorubicin is isolated from Streptomyces bacteria, and cytarabine enters leukemia treatment."], ["1973", "The 7+3 combination of cytarabine and daunorubicin is reported for AML."], ["2000s", "Celator Pharmaceuticals develops CPX-351 using a fixed 1:5 drug ratio in liposomes."], ["2017", "FDA approves Vyxeos for therapy-related AML and AML with myelodysplasia-related changes."]], "cell": "Vyxeos is a bilamellar liposome that holds daunorubicin and cytarabine at a fixed 1:5 molar ratio. The liposomes circulate intact, collect in the bone marrow and are taken up more by leukemia cells than by normal marrow cells. Inside the cell, the liposomes break down and release both drugs. Daunorubicin, an anthracycline, intercalates between DNA base pairs and traps topoisomerase II after it cuts DNA, leaving double-strand breaks; it also generates free radicals. Cytarabine is phosphorylated to ara-CTP, which competes with dCTP and is built into DNA, blocking DNA polymerase and chain elongation in S phase. Together at this ratio, the two drugs are synergistic, meaning they kill more leukemia cells than either one would at the same doses.", "body": [["Leukemia cells", "Liposomes concentrate both drugs in AML cells in the marrow, killing them and improving survival in high-risk AML compared with 7+3."], ["Bone marrow", "All blood counts fall deeply and recover more slowly than after 7+3, with high risk of infection and bleeding."], ["Bleeding", "Low platelets can cause serious bleeding, including in the brain."], ["Heart", "Daunorubicin can damage heart muscle, causing heart failure or arrhythmias, and the risk adds to any previous anthracycline exposure."], ["Mouth, gut and skin", "Mucositis, nausea, diarrhea, rash and swelling are common."], ["Immune system", "Allergic reactions to the liposomal product can occur."]], "journey": "Vyxeos is given by vein over ninety minutes on a few days of each cycle. The liposomes keep both drugs in the bloodstream far longer than free drugs would, so the total-drug half-lives are about thirty-one hours for daunorubicin and forty hours for cytarabine. Most of each drug stays inside liposomes in the plasma. Once released, daunorubicin is reduced to daunorubicinol by carbonyl reductase and leaves in urine and bile, and cytarabine is deaminated to inactive ara-U by cytidine deaminase and leaves in urine.", "sidefx": "Deep and prolonged cytopenias come from killing both leukemic and normal marrow cells, so fevers, infections and bleeding are common. Heart damage comes from daunorubicin: anthracyclines produce free radicals and affect topoisomerase II-beta in heart muscle cells, and the risk rises with the total lifetime anthracycline dose. Mucositis and diarrhea reflect damage to dividing gut lining cells. Because the doses and liposomal form differ from free drugs, the product is not interchangeable with other daunorubicin or cytarabine products. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "src": ["Vyxeos (daunorubicin and cytarabine) liposome FDA label", "Lancet JE et al. CPX-351 versus 7+3 in older adults with newly diagnosed secondary AML. J Clin Oncol, 2018"]}, "doxorubicin-liposomal": {"origin": "Doxorubicin itself came from soil. In the late 1960s scientists at the Italian company Farmitalia, led by Federico Arcamone, grew a mutant strain of Streptomyces peucetius and found it made a red pigment, doxorubicin, that worked against far more cancers than its parent drug daunorubicin. Its big problem was heart damage. In the 1980s and early 1990s researchers including Yechezkel Barenholz and Alberto Gabizon, working with the company Liposome Technology (later Sequus), packed doxorubicin inside tiny fat bubbles (liposomes) coated with polyethylene glycol. The PEG coat hides the bubbles from the immune cells that would normally clear them, so they circulate for days and leak into tumors through their leaky blood vessels. The FDA approved the product as Doxil in 1995 for AIDS-related Kaposi sarcoma. It later gained approval for ovarian cancer and, with bortezomib, multiple myeloma.", "timeline": [["1969", "Farmitalia reports doxorubicin from a mutant Streptomyces peucetius strain."], ["1980s", "Long-circulating PEG-coated (stealth) liposomes are developed."], ["1995", "FDA approves Doxil for AIDS-related Kaposi sarcoma, the first FDA-approved nanodrug."], ["1999", "Approval expands to ovarian cancer after platinum therapy."], ["2007", "Approval for multiple myeloma in combination with bortezomib."]], "cell": "The drug inside the liposome is ordinary doxorubicin. The PEG-coated liposome is too large to leave normal capillaries but slips out through the gaps in tumor vessels, which is called the enhanced permeability and retention effect. Once in the tumor, doxorubicin is slowly released. Its flat ring system slides between DNA base pairs (intercalation). It traps topoisomerase II on DNA after the enzyme has cut both strands, so the breaks cannot be resealed. Double-strand breaks pile up and trigger p53-driven apoptosis. The quinone part of the molecule also cycles electrons with iron and oxygen to make free radicals that damage membranes. The drug acts in any phase but hits S phase hardest. Because the liposome keeps free drug levels low and steady, heart-damaging peaks are blunted.", "body": [["Tumors", "Liposomes collect in tumor tissue through leaky vessels, giving a longer, more focused exposure to doxorubicin than the free drug."], ["Skin of hands and feet", "Long-circulating liposomes leak into small capillaries of the palms and soles, especially where pressure and friction occur, causing redness, peeling and pain (hand-foot syndrome)."], ["Mouth and gut", "Rapidly dividing lining cells are injured, producing stomatitis and nausea."], ["Bone marrow", "Dividing blood precursors are killed, lowering neutrophils, red cells and platelets."], ["Heart", "Free-radical injury to heart muscle cells can still cause cardiomyopathy, with risk rising with the total lifetime anthracycline amount, though peak-related damage is less than with conventional doxorubicin."], ["Immune system", "Liposomes can activate complement during the first infusion, causing flushing, chest tightness and back pain."]], "journey": "Given only by IV infusion. Most of the drug stays inside the liposomes in the bloodstream, so the volume of distribution is small, close to blood volume. The PEG coat lets the liposomes avoid the liver and spleen macrophages for a long time, with a half-life of about two to three days. Liposomes slowly deposit in tumors and skin, where doxorubicin leaks out. Released drug is metabolized like regular doxorubicin, mainly to doxorubicinol, and leaves mostly through bile. It cannot be swapped for conventional doxorubicin on an equal basis because the two behave so differently.", "sidefx": "Hand-foot syndrome is the signature effect and comes from long-lived liposomes leaking drug into the skin of the palms and soles. Mucositis comes from the same slow exposure of mouth lining cells. Marrow suppression reflects killing of dividing blood precursors. Cardiomyopathy comes from iron-driven free radicals and topoisomerase II-beta injury in heart cells; total lifetime anthracycline exposure is tracked for that reason. Infusion reactions are thought to come from complement activation by the liposomes (CARPA), which is why the first infusion starts slowly. There is no specific antidote; dexrazoxane is used to protect the heart with conventional doxorubicin in some settings.", "fun": "During a severe Doxil shortage in 2011 and 2012, the FDA allowed temporary import of a similar liposomal doxorubicin from India called Lipodox.", "src": ["Doxil (doxorubicin HCl liposome injection) FDA label", "Barenholz Y. Doxil: the first FDA-approved nano-drug. J Control Release 2012", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "daunorubicin": {"origin": "Daunorubicin was the first anthracycline. In the late 1950s and early 1960s, scientists at the Italian company Farmitalia screened soil microbes for cancer-fighting compounds. A Streptomyces peucetius strain from soil near Castel del Monte in the Apulia region made a bright red substance. The name joins the Dauni, an ancient people of Apulia, with the French word rubis, meaning ruby. At about the same time a French team at Rhone-Poulenc found the same molecule and called it rubidomycin. It proved strongly active against acute leukemias. Combined with cytarabine in the 7+3 regimen, it became the backbone of AML induction for decades. A later liposome product combining daunorubicin and cytarabine (Vyxeos) was approved in 2017.", "timeline": [["1963", "Italian and French teams report the red Streptomyces antibiotic daunorubicin (rubidomycin)."], ["1970s", "The 7+3 regimen of cytarabine plus daunorubicin becomes standard AML induction."], ["1979", "FDA approves daunorubicin (Cerubidine)."], ["2017", "FDA approves a liposomal daunorubicin and cytarabine combination for certain AML."]], "cell": "Daunorubicin has a flat four-ring anthraquinone core attached to an amino sugar. The flat rings slide between DNA base pairs (intercalation), and the sugar sits in the minor groove. This locks topoisomerase II onto DNA right after it has made a double-strand cut, stabilizing the cleavage complex so the break cannot be resealed. When replication or transcription runs into these complexes, permanent double-strand breaks form and the cell triggers apoptosis. The quinone ring can also accept an electron and pass it to oxygen, especially with iron, forming superoxide and hydroxyl radicals that damage lipids and DNA. It works in all phases but most in S phase. Leukemia blasts, which divide rapidly and rely heavily on topoisomerase II, are especially sensitive.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Leukemic blasts and normal blood precursors are both killed, which is the goal of induction but leaves a period of very low counts with a nadir around 10 to 14 days."], ["Heart", "Free radicals and topoisomerase II-beta injury damage heart muscle cells, causing cardiomyopathy that becomes more likely as the lifetime total rises, especially after chest radiation."], ["Mouth and gut", "Dividing lining cells are injured, causing mucositis, nausea and vomiting."], ["Hair follicles", "Rapidly dividing follicle cells stop growing, so hair falls out."], ["Urine", "The red drug and its metabolites color the urine red for a day or two; this is harmless."], ["Kidneys and metabolism", "Rapid killing of leukemia cells releases uric acid, potassium and phosphate, which can cause tumor lysis syndrome."]], "journey": "Given only into a running IV line, because it badly damages tissue if it leaks. It spreads rapidly into tissues and binds tightly to DNA, so tissue levels are much higher than blood levels. Aldo-keto reductase enzymes in the liver and other tissues turn it into daunorubicinol, which is also active. Both are cleared through bile and urine. The parent drug's half-life is about 18 hours, and daunorubicinol lasts longer. Liver or kidney impairment raises exposure and calls for dose reduction per the label.", "sidefx": "Marrow suppression, mucositis and hair loss come from killing any rapidly dividing cell. Heart damage comes from two effects in heart muscle: iron-catalyzed free radicals that injure mitochondria and membranes, and blocking of topoisomerase II-beta, which disrupts mitochondrial gene expression. Heart cells cannot regenerate, so the damage adds up over a lifetime. Leakage outside the vein binds DNA in local tissue and causes spreading necrosis; dexrazoxane (Totect) is an FDA-approved treatment for anthracycline extravasation. Red urine is just drug color. Tumor lysis is handled with hydration and uric acid lowering.", "fun": "Daunorubicin's name is a little geography lesson: the Dauni were a pre-Roman people of the same Apulian region where the drug-making microbe was found.", "src": ["Daunorubicin hydrochloride injection FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Weiss RB. The anthracyclines: will we ever find a better doxorubicin. Semin Oncol 1992"]}, "epirubicin": {"origin": "Epirubicin was made by chemists at Farmitalia in Milan in the 1970s, in the same group led by Federico Arcamone that developed doxorubicin. They wanted an anthracycline that killed tumors as well as doxorubicin but was gentler on the heart. They flipped the orientation of a single hydroxyl group on the sugar (the 4-prime position), making a mirror-image form called an epimer. That small change lets the liver attach glucuronic acid to the drug, so it is cleared faster. Epirubicin was used in Europe for years before the FDA approved it as Ellence in 1999 for adjuvant treatment of node-positive breast cancer after surgery.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Farmitalia chemists create epirubicin by changing the sugar of doxorubicin."], ["1980s", "Epirubicin enters clinical use in Europe."], ["1999", "FDA approves Ellence for adjuvant treatment of node-positive breast cancer."]], "cell": "Like other anthracyclines, epirubicin intercalates between DNA base pairs and stabilizes the topoisomerase II-DNA cleavage complex. Topoisomerase II normally cuts both DNA strands to untangle them and then reseals the cut. With epirubicin bound, the enzyme stays stuck on the cut ends, leaving double-strand breaks that trigger apoptosis when the cell tries to replicate or divide. The quinone ring also forms free radicals with iron and oxygen. It is cell-cycle nonspecific, with the most effect in S and G2. The 4-prime epimer sugar changes its handling by the body more than its action on DNA.", "body": [["Breast tumor cells", "Rapidly dividing cancer cells accumulate DNA breaks and die, lowering the chance of relapse after surgery."], ["Bone marrow", "White cells drop with a nadir around 10 to 14 days, raising infection risk."], ["Heart", "Cumulative exposure can damage heart muscle, though at equal anticancer effect it is somewhat less cardiotoxic than doxorubicin."], ["Mouth, gut and hair", "Dividing lining and follicle cells are harmed, causing mucositis, nausea and hair loss."], ["Ovaries", "Damage to ovarian follicles can cause amenorrhea and hot flashes in premenopausal women."], ["Bone marrow, late", "DNA damage in stem cells can rarely lead to secondary AML or MDS years later."]], "journey": "Given by IV infusion. It spreads widely into tissues and binds DNA, giving a large volume of distribution. The liver converts it to epirubicinol and, uniquely among anthracyclines, to glucuronide conjugates, which are inactive and cleared quickly. Most is eliminated through bile, with a smaller part in urine. The half-life is about 33 hours. Liver impairment raises exposure because the liver does most of the work.", "sidefx": "Marrow suppression, hair loss and mucositis come from attacking dividing cells. Heart damage comes from iron-driven free radicals and topoisomerase II-beta blockade in heart muscle, building up with total lifetime dose. Secondary AML and MDS arise because topoisomerase II poisons can create chromosome breaks and translocations in marrow stem cells. Extravasation causes severe tissue injury because the drug binds local DNA; dexrazoxane (Totect) is an FDA-approved treatment for anthracycline extravasation. Red urine is just the color of the drug.", "fun": "The only structural difference between epirubicin and doxorubicin is the direction one hydroxyl group points on the sugar ring.", "src": ["Ellence (epirubicin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "idarubicin": {"origin": "Idarubicin was made at Farmitalia in Italy in the 1970s by the Arcamone group, the same chemists behind doxorubicin. They removed the methoxy group from one ring of daunorubicin, creating 4-demethoxydaunorubicin. Losing that group made the molecule more fat-soluble, so it enters cells faster and in greater amounts. That made it more potent and, unusually for an anthracycline, it can even be absorbed when taken by mouth (an oral form was used outside the US). The FDA approved IV idarubicin as Idamycin in 1990 for adult AML, where it is used in place of daunorubicin with cytarabine.", "timeline": [["1976", "Farmitalia chemists report 4-demethoxydaunorubicin (idarubicin)."], ["1990", "FDA approves Idamycin for acute myeloid leukemia in adults."]], "cell": "Idarubicin intercalates between DNA base pairs and stabilizes the topoisomerase II-DNA cleavage complex, leaving double-strand breaks that trigger apoptosis. Because it is more lipophilic than daunorubicin, it crosses cell membranes more readily and reaches higher levels inside leukemic cells. It is also less easily pumped out by P-glycoprotein, which helps in some resistant leukemias. Its main metabolite, idarubicinol, is nearly as active and lasts much longer, extending the cytotoxic effect. It is cell-cycle nonspecific.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Leukemic and normal blood cells are killed, giving deep cytopenias with a nadir around 10 to 14 days."], ["Mouth and gut", "Mucositis, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea and abdominal pain come from injury to dividing lining cells."], ["Heart", "Like other anthracyclines it can damage heart muscle, causing heart failure or arrhythmia."], ["Liver and kidneys", "These clear the drug; impairment raises exposure and toxicity, so the label calls for dose changes."], ["Hair and urine", "Hair loss is common and urine turns red from the drug color."]], "journey": "Given IV over a few minutes into a running line. It spreads widely and rapidly into tissues because it is so fat-soluble. Liver and other tissue enzymes reduce it to idarubicinol, an active metabolite with a half-life of about two days or longer, compared with about 22 hours for the parent. Both are cleared mainly in bile, with some in urine. Idarubicinol also crosses into the cerebrospinal fluid.", "sidefx": "Marrow suppression, mucositis and hair loss reflect killing of dividing cells. Heart toxicity comes from free radicals and topoisomerase II-beta injury in heart muscle. Secondary AML and MDS come from DNA breaks in stem cells. If it leaks out of the vein it binds tissue DNA and causes severe necrosis; dexrazoxane (Totect) is an FDA-approved treatment for anthracycline extravasation. Tumor lysis from rapid blast killing is managed with hydration and uric acid lowering.", "fun": "Removing just one small methoxy group made idarubicin several times more potent than its parent, daunorubicin.", "src": ["Idamycin PFS (idarubicin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "mitoxantrone": {"origin": "Mitoxantrone is fully synthetic. In the 1970s chemists at Lederle Laboratories (American Cyanamid) and other groups were studying anthraquinone compounds, a chemical family related to textile dyes, looking for drugs that act like anthracyclines without their heart toxicity. They made the anthracenedione mitoxantrone, which keeps the flat DNA-binding rings but lacks the sugar and the quinone structure that drives much of anthracycline free-radical formation. The FDA approved it as Novantrone in 1987 for AML, later for pain in advanced prostate cancer, and in 2000 for worsening forms of multiple sclerosis. The Novantrone brand has since been discontinued, but generic mitoxantrone remains available.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Lederle chemists synthesize mitoxantrone from the anthraquinone family."], ["1987", "FDA approves Novantrone for acute nonlymphocytic leukemia."], ["1996", "Approval for pain from advanced hormone-refractory prostate cancer."], ["2000", "FDA approves mitoxantrone for secondary progressive and worsening relapsing MS."]], "cell": "Mitoxantrone's flat three-ring core intercalates into DNA, and its side chains bind the phosphate backbone, crosslinking strands electrostatically. It inhibits topoisomerase II, stabilizing the enzyme-DNA cleavage complex and causing strand breaks that lead to apoptosis. It forms fewer free radicals than anthracyclines because it resists one-electron reduction. In multiple sclerosis its effect is immune suppression: it kills or inhibits dividing B cells, T cells and macrophages, reduces antigen presentation and lowers the release of inflammatory cytokines, so fewer immune cells attack myelin.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils fall with a nadir around 10 to 14 days, raising infection risk."], ["Immune system", "Fewer active lymphocytes and macrophages enter the brain and spinal cord, which slows MS relapses and disability progression."], ["Heart", "Despite fewer free radicals, it still causes cumulative cardiomyopathy, sometimes years after treatment, so MS use has a lifetime limit and LVEF checks."], ["Urine and eyes", "The dark blue drug turns urine blue-green and can tint the whites of the eyes bluish for a short time."], ["Reproductive system", "Menstrual changes and amenorrhea are common in women treated for MS."], ["Bone marrow, late", "Topoisomerase II damage in stem cells can lead to treatment-related AML."]], "journey": "Given IV only, never intrathecally. It distributes very widely and binds tissue DNA, giving a huge volume of distribution and slow release back into the blood. The liver oxidizes and conjugates it, and most leaves through bile and feces, with only a small amount in urine. The terminal half-life is long, a median of about 75 hours. Liver impairment raises levels.", "sidefx": "Cardiotoxicity likely reflects topoisomerase II-beta injury in heart muscle and some residual oxidative stress, and it adds up with total lifetime exposure, so MS treatment is capped. Secondary AML arises because topoisomerase II poisons cause chromosome translocations in stem cells. Marrow suppression and infections reflect killing of dividing blood cells. Blue-green urine and bluish sclera are just drug color. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Mitoxantrone is a deep blue dye-like molecule, a family resemblance to the anthraquinone dyes chemists started from.", "src": ["Mitoxantrone injection FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "valrubicin": {"origin": "Valrubicin is a semisynthetic relative of doxorubicin first studied in the 1970s under the code name AD-32 by researchers in the United States. Chemists added a trifluoroacetyl group to the sugar and a valerate ester to the side chain, making the molecule much more fat-soluble. That lets it soak quickly into the cells lining the bladder when instilled through a catheter, while very little reaches the blood. The FDA approved it as Valstar in 1998 for carcinoma in situ of the bladder that had not responded to BCG, in patients for whom immediate bladder removal was not an option. It was taken off the market in 2002 because of a manufacturing problem and returned in 2009.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Valrubicin (AD-32) is developed as a lipophilic doxorubicin derivative."], ["1998", "FDA approves Valstar for BCG-refractory carcinoma in situ of the bladder."], ["2002", "Valstar is withdrawn because of a manufacturing issue."], ["2009", "Valstar returns to the US market."]], "cell": "Valrubicin enters urothelial cells quickly because it is very lipophilic. Inside, it interferes with incorporation of nucleosides into DNA and RNA and disrupts the topoisomerase II-DNA cleavage complex, causing chromosomal damage. Unlike doxorubicin, it intercalates into DNA only weakly; its main effects come from topoisomerase II interference and metabolic disruption. Damaged cells arrest in G2 and die. Because it stays in the bladder, it acts mainly on the bladder lining, including the flat carcinoma in situ cells.", "body": [["Bladder lining", "Cancerous and normal urothelial cells are exposed directly during the dwell, killing carcinoma in situ cells in some patients."], ["Bladder function", "Irritation of the lining causes frequency, urgency, painful urination, spasms and sometimes blood in the urine for a few days."], ["Urine", "The red drug colors urine red for about a day after treatment."], ["Bone marrow", "Normally unaffected because little drug is absorbed, but a bladder perforation can allow absorption and marrow suppression."]], "journey": "Instilled into the bladder through a urinary catheter and held for about two hours before the patient voids. It enters the bladder wall but very little crosses into the blood when the bladder is intact. Almost all the drug leaves in the voided urine, largely unchanged. That is why systemic effects like hair loss or marrow suppression are rare. Treatment is delayed after a bladder resection so the drug does not leak through raw tissue.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are local: the drug irritates and kills bladder lining cells, causing cystitis-like symptoms. If the bladder wall is perforated or compromised, the drug can reach the bloodstream and cause anthracycline effects such as marrow suppression. A key risk is not the drug itself but delay: if the cancer does not respond, putting off cystectomy can allow it to spread, so response is checked about three months after therapy.", "fun": "Valrubicin is used inside the bladder rather than in the bloodstream because its fat-soluble design lets it soak into the bladder lining quickly while very little is absorbed.", "src": ["Valstar (valrubicin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "dactinomycin": {"origin": "Dactinomycin, also called actinomycin D, comes from soil bacteria. In 1940 Selman Waksman and H. Boyd Woodruff at Rutgers University isolated actinomycin from Streptomyces (Actinomyces) antibioticus. It was the first antibiotic isolated from an actinomycete, but it was far too toxic to use against infections. In the 1950s Sidney Farber and colleagues in Boston showed it was active against Wilms tumor, a childhood kidney cancer, making it one of the first antibiotics used as a cancer drug. Merck marketed it as Cosmegen, approved by the FDA in 1964. It remains part of standard regimens for Wilms tumor, rhabdomyosarcoma, Ewing sarcoma and gestational trophoblastic disease.", "timeline": [["1940", "Selman Waksman and H. Boyd Woodruff isolate actinomycin from Streptomyces."], ["1950s", "Sidney Farber's group shows activity in Wilms tumor."], ["1964", "FDA approves Cosmegen (dactinomycin)."]], "cell": "Dactinomycin has a flat phenoxazone ring with two cyclic peptide loops. The ring intercalates between guanine-cytosine base pairs, and the peptide loops sit in the minor groove, anchoring it firmly. This blocks DNA-dependent RNA polymerase from moving along the template, so transcription stops, especially of ribosomal RNA. Without new RNA and protein, cells cannot grow or divide and they die. It also causes single-strand DNA breaks, possibly through topoisomerase interference. It is largely cell-cycle nonspecific. It also makes cells more sensitive to radiation.", "body": [["Tumor cells", "Blocking transcription starves fast-growing pediatric tumors of new proteins, killing them."], ["Bone marrow", "White cells and platelets fall, with a nadir around one to three weeks."], ["Mouth and gut", "Severe mucositis, diarrhea, nausea and vomiting reflect injury to rapidly renewing lining cells."], ["Liver", "Damage to the small hepatic veins can cause sinusoidal obstruction syndrome (veno-occlusive disease), with liver enlargement, fluid buildup and jaundice."], ["Skin", "Hair loss and acne-like rashes occur, and previously irradiated skin can flare (radiation recall)."]], "journey": "Given IV. It leaves the blood very quickly and concentrates in tissues, where it binds tightly to DNA. Very little is metabolized. It is slowly excreted unchanged in bile and urine, with a terminal half-life of about 36 hours. Doses are measured in micrograms because it is so potent. It does not cross into the brain well.", "sidefx": "Because it stops RNA synthesis in all fast-renewing cells, the main toxicities are marrow suppression, mucositis, diarrhea and hair loss. Liver veno-occlusive disease comes from injury to sinusoidal endothelial cells. It is a strong vesicant, so leakage outside a vein causes tissue necrosis. Because it sensitizes cells to radiation, it can worsen radiation injury and cause radiation recall. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Actinomycin D is also a standard lab tool: biologists add it to cultured cells to shut off transcription and measure how long RNA molecules last.", "src": ["Cosmegen (dactinomycin) FDA label", "Waksman SA, Woodruff HB. Proc Soc Exp Biol Med 1940", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "mitomycin": {"origin": "Mitomycin C was discovered in Japan. In the mid-1950s scientists at Kyowa Hakko, including Toju Hata and Shigetoshi Wakaki, isolated a family of mitomycins from the soil bacterium Streptomyces caespitosus. Mitomycin C proved the most useful against tumors. The FDA approved it in 1974, and it was sold as Mutamycin. Over the years it found new roles outside the bloodstream: it is applied to the eye during glaucoma surgery (Mitosol, approved 2012) to stop scarring, and gel forms for urothelial cancer were approved as Jelmyto in 2020 for upper tract tumors and Zusduri in 2025 for certain bladder tumors.", "timeline": [["1956", "Kyowa Hakko scientists report mitomycins from Streptomyces caespitosus."], ["1974", "FDA approves mitomycin for gastric and pancreatic cancer."], ["2012", "FDA approves Mitosol for use during glaucoma surgery."], ["2020", "FDA approves Jelmyto, a gel for low-grade upper tract urothelial cancer."], ["2025", "FDA approves Zusduri for certain low-grade bladder tumors."]], "cell": "Mitomycin is a prodrug. Inside cells, enzymes such as NAD(P)H quinone oxidoreductase (DT-diaphorase) and cytochrome P450 reductase reduce its quinone ring. This unmasks two reactive sites that alkylate DNA, mainly at the N2 position of guanine in CpG sequences. Because it has two reactive sites, it forms crosslinks between the two DNA strands. Crosslinked strands cannot separate for replication or transcription, so the cell dies. Reduction is favored in low-oxygen conditions, so mitomycin is somewhat more active in hypoxic tumor cells. It is cell-cycle nonspecific, with most effect in late G1 and S. In the eye, it stops fibroblasts from dividing so scar tissue does not close the surgical drainage channel.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Platelets and white cells fall late, around four to six weeks, and the effect builds up with repeated cycles."], ["Kidneys and blood vessels", "Endothelial injury can cause hemolytic uremic syndrome with broken red cells, low platelets and kidney failure."], ["Lungs", "Rarely it causes interstitial pneumonitis and fibrosis."], ["Gut and skin", "Nausea, mouth sores and hair loss occur; leaking from a vein causes deep tissue necrosis."], ["Bladder and urinary tract", "Local gels or instillations kill tumor cells on the lining and cause local irritation, with little systemic exposure."], ["Eye", "Applied during surgery, it prevents fibroblast scarring but can thin the tissue around the surgical site."]], "journey": "For systemic use it is given IV and cleared from the blood very quickly, with a plasma half-life of under an hour. It is activated and broken down mainly in the liver and other tissues, and only a small part leaves unchanged in urine. Even though it leaves the blood quickly, its DNA crosslinks persist, which explains delayed marrow effects. When put into the bladder, the upper urinary tract or on the eye, only small amounts are absorbed.", "sidefx": "Delayed, cumulative marrow suppression reflects DNA crosslinks in slowly cycling stem cells. Hemolytic uremic syndrome and lung injury are thought to come from damage to endothelial cells, possibly through reactive oxygen species formed during redox cycling of the quinone. It is a vesicant, so extravasation causes severe necrosis. With urothelial gels, ureteric narrowing can occur from local scarring. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "The same drug used for stomach cancer is dabbed on the eye for a few minutes during glaucoma surgery to keep the new drainage channel from scarring shut.", "src": ["Mitomycin for injection FDA label", "Jelmyto (mitomycin) FDA label", "Mitosol (mitomycin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "irinotecan": {"origin": "Irinotecan traces back to a Chinese tree, Camptotheca acuminata, sometimes called the happy tree. In 1966 Monroe Wall and Mansukh Wani at the Research Triangle Institute in North Carolina isolated camptothecin from its bark as part of a National Cancer Institute plant screening program. Camptothecin was active against tumors but too toxic and insoluble, and early trials were stopped. In 1985 Leroy Liu and colleagues showed its target was topoisomerase I, which revived interest. Japanese scientists at Yakult Honsha and Daiichi made a water-soluble prodrug, irinotecan, in the 1980s. The FDA approved it as Camptosar in 1996 for colorectal cancer, where it became a core part of FOLFIRI.", "timeline": [["1966", "Monroe Wall and Mansukh Wani isolate camptothecin from Camptotheca acuminata."], ["1980s", "Japanese researchers synthesize irinotecan, a soluble camptothecin prodrug."], ["1985", "Topoisomerase I is identified as the target of camptothecin."], ["1996", "FDA approves Camptosar for metastatic colorectal cancer."]], "cell": "Irinotecan itself is a weak drug. Carboxylesterase enzymes, mainly in the liver, cut off its bulky side chain to release SN-38, which is about 100 to 1,000 times more potent. Topoisomerase I relieves DNA twisting by cutting one strand, letting it rotate and then resealing it. SN-38 wedges into the enzyme-DNA complex and blocks the resealing step. When a replication fork runs into this trapped complex, the single-strand nick becomes a lethal double-strand break, so the drug is S-phase specific. The liver then inactivates SN-38 by attaching glucuronic acid using UGT1A1.", "body": [["Tumor cells", "Dividing colorectal and other cancer cells collect replication-linked DNA breaks and die."], ["Gut, early", "Within hours of infusion, irinotecan inhibits acetylcholinesterase, producing a cholinergic syndrome of cramping, sweating, watery eyes, salivation and diarrhea."], ["Gut, late", "Days later, SN-38 released in the gut injures the lining and causes secretory diarrhea that can be severe and dehydrating."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils drop, with a nadir around one to three weeks."], ["Hair and energy", "Hair loss and fatigue are common, reflecting injury to follicle cells and the general toll of treatment."], ["Lungs", "Rarely, interstitial lung disease occurs."]], "journey": "Given IV. Carboxylesterase in the liver converts some irinotecan into active SN-38, which peaks about an hour after the infusion. CYP3A4 turns some irinotecan into inactive products, so strong CYP3A4 inducers or inhibitors change exposure. UGT1A1 glucuronidates SN-38 into inactive SN-38 glucuronide, which goes into bile. In the intestine, bacterial beta-glucuronidase can cut off the glucuronide and release active SN-38 again, right next to the gut lining. Half-lives are roughly 6 to 12 hours for irinotecan and 10 to 20 hours for SN-38.", "sidefx": "Early diarrhea is cholinergic, from acetylcholinesterase inhibition, and is treated with atropine. Late diarrhea comes from SN-38 being reactivated by gut bacteria and damaging crypt cells, and it calls for prompt loperamide and fluids. Neutropenia comes from killing dividing marrow cells. People with low UGT1A1 activity, such as those homozygous for UGT1A1*28 (the variant behind Gilbert syndrome), clear SN-38 slowly and have more neutropenia, so the label discusses lower starting doses. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "People with Gilbert syndrome, which only causes mild harmless jaundice, can get severe toxicity from irinotecan because the same enzyme handles both bilirubin and SN-38.", "src": ["Camptosar (irinotecan) FDA label", "Wall ME, Wani MC et al. J Am Chem Soc 1966", "Hsiang YH, Liu LF et al. J Biol Chem 1985", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "irinotecan-liposome": {"origin": "Liposomal irinotecan builds on the camptothecin story that began with the happy tree, Camptotheca acuminata, and the 1966 isolation of camptothecin by Monroe Wall and Mansukh Wani. The aim was to keep irinotecan in the bloodstream longer and deliver more of it to tumors. Researchers at Hermes Biosciences in California developed a way to load irinotecan densely inside small liposomes, and Merrimack Pharmaceuticals carried the product forward. The FDA approved it as Onivyde in 2015 for metastatic pancreatic cancer after gemcitabine-based therapy, given with fluorouracil and leucovorin. In 2024 it was approved for first-line metastatic pancreatic cancer in the NALIRIFOX regimen. It is now marketed by Ipsen.", "timeline": [["1966", "Camptothecin is isolated from Camptotheca acuminata."], ["2000s", "Hermes Biosciences develops liposome-encapsulated irinotecan."], ["2015", "FDA approves Onivyde for pancreatic cancer after gemcitabine."], ["2024", "FDA approves Onivyde as first-line therapy in NALIRIFOX."]], "cell": "Inside the bloodstream, irinotecan stays trapped in the liposomes, protected from early conversion and clearance. The liposomes collect in tumor tissue through leaky vessels, where tumor-associated macrophages are thought to take them up and release irinotecan. Carboxylesterase then converts irinotecan to SN-38. SN-38 traps topoisomerase I on DNA after it nicks one strand, so the nick cannot be resealed. When a replication fork collides with this complex, a double-strand break forms and the cell dies. The drug is S-phase specific. The liposome changes where and how long the drug acts, not the target.", "body": [["Pancreatic tumor", "Longer exposure inside the tumor increases DNA damage in dividing cancer cells."], ["Gut", "SN-38 injures intestinal lining cells, causing diarrhea that can be severe, sometimes with early cholinergic cramping."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils fall and can lead to serious infections and sepsis."], ["Mouth and appetite", "Stomatitis, nausea, vomiting and poor appetite are common."], ["Lungs", "Rarely, interstitial lung disease develops."]], "journey": "Given by IV infusion. Most irinotecan circulates inside liposomes, giving much longer exposure than conventional irinotecan. Liposomes slowly release drug, especially in tumors and in tissues with many macrophages. Released irinotecan is converted to SN-38 by carboxylesterase and broken down by CYP3A4, and SN-38 is cleared by UGT1A1 glucuronidation and biliary excretion. It is not interchangeable with conventional irinotecan. People homozygous for UGT1A1*28 start at a lower dose per the label.", "sidefx": "Neutropenia comes from SN-38 killing dividing marrow cells, and the boxed warning highlights fatal neutropenic sepsis. Diarrhea comes from SN-38 damage to intestinal crypt cells, with early diarrhea partly from cholinergic effects that respond to atropine and late diarrhea treated with loperamide. Slow UGT1A1 clearance raises SN-38 levels and toxicity. Premedication with a corticosteroid and antiemetic is used before each dose. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Onivyde (irinotecan liposome) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Wang-Gillam A et al. NAPOLI-1, Lancet 2016"]}, "topotecan": {"origin": "Topotecan is a descendant of camptothecin, the alkaloid that Monroe Wall and Mansukh Wani isolated in 1966 from the bark of the Chinese happy tree, Camptotheca acuminata. Camptothecin itself failed early trials because it was insoluble and toxic, and the water-soluble salt form used was much less active. Once topoisomerase I was identified as its target in 1985, chemists at SmithKline Beecham designed topotecan, a semisynthetic camptothecin with a side chain that makes it water-soluble while keeping the active lactone ring. The FDA approved it as Hycamtin in 1996 for ovarian cancer after initial chemotherapy. It later gained approvals for small cell lung cancer, including an oral capsule, and for cervical cancer with cisplatin.", "timeline": [["1966", "Camptothecin is isolated from Camptotheca acuminata."], ["1985", "Topoisomerase I is identified as camptothecin's target."], ["1996", "FDA approves Hycamtin for ovarian cancer."], ["2006", "Approval for cervical cancer in combination with cisplatin."], ["2007", "Oral topotecan capsules are approved for relapsed small cell lung cancer."]], "cell": "Topoisomerase I relaxes supercoiled DNA by nicking one strand, letting it swivel, then resealing it. Topotecan binds the enzyme-DNA complex at the nick and blocks resealing, trapping a covalent topoisomerase I-DNA cleavable complex. When a replication fork reaches this trapped complex, the single-strand nick turns into a double-strand break, which the cell cannot repair well, so it undergoes apoptosis. This makes the drug S-phase specific. Its active form has a closed lactone ring; at body pH the ring opens reversibly into a less active carboxylate form.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils, platelets and red cells fall, with a nadir around 7 to 11 days; this is the dose-limiting toxicity."], ["Tumor cells", "Dividing ovarian, lung and cervical cancer cells accumulate replication-linked DNA breaks and die."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea occur, and rarely neutropenic colitis with fever and abdominal pain."], ["Hair", "Hair loss is common because follicle cells divide rapidly."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease is a rare but serious effect."]], "journey": "Given IV, or by mouth as capsules with about 40 percent bioavailability. In the blood, the lactone ring opens and closes depending on pH. Unlike irinotecan, topotecan needs no activation and is not mainly handled by the liver. A small amount is N-demethylated by CYP enzymes, but most is cleared by the kidneys, so kidney impairment raises exposure and calls for dose reduction per the label. The half-life is short, about two to three hours. It crosses into the cerebrospinal fluid fairly well.", "sidefx": "Marrow suppression is the main problem and comes directly from killing dividing blood precursors; the label limits starting treatment to adequate baseline neutrophil and platelet counts. Diarrhea and mucositis reflect injury to gut lining cells. Neutropenic colitis happens when a damaged gut wall meets very low neutrophils. Kidney impairment increases toxicity because the kidneys clear the drug. There is no antidote; growth factors and transfusions support recovery.", "src": ["Hycamtin (topotecan) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "vinblastine": {"origin": "Vinblastine comes from the Madagascar periwinkle, Catharanthus roseus, a plant used in folk medicine in several countries to treat diabetes. In the 1950s Robert Noble and Charles Beer at the University of Western Ontario tested periwinkle extracts for blood sugar effects. Instead, the extracts made rats' white cell counts plunge. They isolated the responsible alkaloid, vincaleukoblastine, later called vinblastine, and reported it in 1958. At nearly the same time, Gordon Svoboda's group at Eli Lilly was isolating periwinkle alkaloids, including vincristine. Vinblastine was approved in the early 1960s as Velban and became a key drug for Hodgkin lymphoma (in ABVD) and testicular cancer.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Noble and Beer find that periwinkle extract lowers white cell counts in rats."], ["1958", "Vinblastine (vincaleukoblastine) is reported."], ["1960s", "Eli Lilly markets vinblastine as Velban."], ["1970s", "ABVD, which includes vinblastine, is developed for Hodgkin lymphoma."]], "cell": "Microtubules are hollow tubes made of alpha- and beta-tubulin that form the mitotic spindle. Vinblastine binds beta-tubulin at the vinca site, near the growing plus end. This blocks addition of new tubulin dimers, so microtubules cannot polymerize, and at higher levels it causes tubulin to clump into paracrystals. Without a working spindle, chromosomes cannot line up and separate, so cells stall in metaphase (M phase) and undergo apoptosis. Neurons also depend on microtubules for axonal transport, but vinblastine affects them less than vincristine does.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "White cell counts drop sharply with a nadir around 5 to 10 days; this limits dosing."], ["Nerves", "Interference with axonal microtubules can cause numbness, tingling and loss of reflexes, though less than with vincristine."], ["Gut", "Autonomic nerve effects slow the bowel, causing constipation and sometimes ileus."], ["Jaw and bones", "Jaw pain and bone pain occur, likely from nerve effects."], ["Kidneys and sodium", "Inappropriate release of antidiuretic hormone (SIADH) can make the kidneys hold water and lower blood sodium."], ["Hair", "Hair loss is common because hair follicle cells divide rapidly."]], "journey": "Given only IV, often as a short push into a running line. It leaves the blood quickly and binds tightly to tissues and platelets. The liver breaks it down through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as azole antifungals raise toxicity. Most leaves in bile and feces. The terminal half-life is about a day. It does not enter the brain well. Liver impairment raises exposure.", "sidefx": "Marrow suppression comes from blocking mitosis in dividing blood precursors. Neuropathy, constipation and jaw pain come from disrupted axonal microtubules. The deadliest risk is giving it into the spinal fluid by mistake: the drug destroys nerve tissue and causes ascending paralysis and death, so syringes carry a 'for IV use only, fatal if given by other routes' label and it is often dispensed in a small IV bag. It is a vesicant; leakage causes tissue damage, often managed with hyaluronidase and warm compresses. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Vinblastine was found by accident: researchers were testing periwinkle as a diabetes remedy and noticed it wiped out white blood cells instead.", "src": ["Vinblastine sulfate injection FDA label", "Noble RL, Beer CT, Cutts JH. Ann N Y Acad Sci 1958", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "vinorelbine": {"origin": "Vinorelbine is a semisynthetic vinca alkaloid designed in France. In the late 1970s Pierre Potier and colleagues at the Institut de Chimie des Substances Naturelles, part of the French national research center CNRS, worked on modifying the periwinkle alkaloids that gave vinblastine and vincristine. By changing the catharanthine part of the molecule, they made vinorelbine, which binds mitotic spindle microtubules more than the microtubules in nerve axons. Developed with Pierre Fabre, it was launched in France in 1989. The FDA approved it as Navelbine in 1994 for non-small cell lung cancer, and it is widely used for breast cancer as well.", "timeline": [["1979", "Pierre Potier's group at CNRS in France prepares vinorelbine."], ["1989", "Vinorelbine is launched in France."], ["1994", "FDA approves Navelbine for non-small cell lung cancer."]], "cell": "Vinorelbine binds beta-tubulin at the vinca binding site near the microtubule plus end and blocks addition of new tubulin, preventing microtubule polymerization. The mitotic spindle cannot form properly, so cells arrest in metaphase (M phase) and die by apoptosis. Compared with vincristine, it has relatively more effect on mitotic microtubules than on the axonal microtubules that carry cargo along nerves, which explains its lower nerve toxicity. Its main dose-limiting effect is on dividing granulocyte precursors.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Granulocytes fall sharply with a nadir around 7 to 10 days, raising infection risk."], ["Nerves", "Mild numbness and tingling can occur, but much less than with vincristine."], ["Gut", "Constipation is common and, rarely, paralytic ileus develops from autonomic nerve effects."], ["Veins", "It irritates veins, causing phlebitis, so the vein is flushed after each dose."], ["Lungs", "Rare acute shortness of breath, interstitial pneumonitis and ARDS can occur."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme elevations occur, and liver impairment raises drug levels."]], "journey": "Given IV as a short infusion (an oral form exists outside the US). It spreads widely into tissues, including lung tissue, giving a large volume of distribution. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors can increase toxicity. Most is excreted in bile and feces, with a smaller share in urine. The terminal half-life is roughly 28 to 44 hours.", "sidefx": "Severe neutropenia, the boxed warning, comes from mitotic arrest of granulocyte precursors and can lead to septic shock. Constipation and neuropathy reflect microtubule disruption in autonomic and sensory nerves. Phlebitis and extravasation injury arise because the drug is a vesicant that damages vein walls. Like the other vincas, it is fatal if given by any route other than IV. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "src": ["Navelbine (vinorelbine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "paclitaxel-protein-bound": {"origin": "Paclitaxel came from the bark of the Pacific yew, Taxus brevifolia. A US Department of Agriculture botanist, Arthur Barclay, collected the bark in 1962 for a National Cancer Institute plant screening program, and Monroe Wall and Mansukh Wani reported paclitaxel's structure in 1971. In 1979 Susan Horwitz at Albert Einstein College of Medicine showed it worked in a new way, by freezing microtubules. Conventional paclitaxel is almost insoluble and must be dissolved in castor-oil-based Cremophor, which causes allergic reactions. American BioScience (later Abraxis BioScience), founded by Patrick Soon-Shiong, bound paclitaxel to human albumin to form nanoparticles that need no solvent. The FDA approved this nab-paclitaxel as Abraxane in 2005 for metastatic breast cancer, followed by non-small cell lung cancer in 2012 and pancreatic cancer in 2013.", "timeline": [["1962", "Pacific yew bark is collected for the National Cancer Institute screening program."], ["1971", "Wall and Wani report the structure of paclitaxel."], ["1979", "Susan Horwitz shows paclitaxel stabilizes microtubules."], ["2005", "FDA approves Abraxane for metastatic breast cancer."], ["2012", "Approval for non-small cell lung cancer."], ["2013", "Approval for metastatic pancreatic cancer with gemcitabine."]], "cell": "Paclitaxel binds the beta-tubulin subunit on the inside of microtubules. Instead of blocking assembly like the vinca alkaloids, it does the opposite: it promotes assembly and stops microtubules from coming apart. Normal mitosis needs microtubules to grow and shrink rapidly. Frozen, overly stable microtubules cannot form a working spindle, so the spindle checkpoint holds cells in G2/M, and they die by apoptosis. The albumin carrier may help delivery to tumors through albumin transport pathways such as gp60 and SPARC, though how much this matters in people is still debated.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils drop with a nadir around days 8 to 15, which is the main dose-limiting effect."], ["Nerves", "Stabilized microtubules disrupt transport along long sensory axons, causing numbness, tingling and pain in the hands and feet."], ["Muscles and joints", "Aches in muscles and joints are common in the days after treatment."], ["Hair", "Nearly complete hair loss is typical because follicle cells are blocked in mitosis."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis can occur, especially with gemcitabine."], ["Immune system", "Without Cremophor, severe allergic infusion reactions are much less common than with conventional paclitaxel."]], "journey": "Given IV as a short infusion. The albumin nanoparticles break apart quickly in the blood, releasing paclitaxel bound to albumin, which spreads widely into tissues. The liver breaks paclitaxel down mainly through CYP2C8 and CYP3A4, so drugs that strongly block or induce these enzymes change its levels. Most leaves through bile in the feces. The terminal half-life is about a day. It is not interchangeable with conventional paclitaxel.", "sidefx": "Neutropenia comes from mitotic arrest in dividing marrow cells, so the label requires adequate neutrophil counts before each dose. Sensory neuropathy comes from rigid microtubules blocking axonal transport, and it tends to improve after the drug is held. Infections and sepsis, notably in pancreatic cancer, follow neutropenia. Because it contains human albumin, there is a remote theoretical risk of transmitting viral disease. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "One early-1990s estimate held that treating a single patient with paclitaxel took the bark of several mature yew trees, which pushed chemists to make it from renewable yew needles and later by plant cell culture.", "src": ["Abraxane (paclitaxel protein-bound) FDA label", "Schiff PB, Fant J, Horwitz SB. Nature 1979", "Wani MC et al. J Am Chem Soc 1971"]}, "docetaxel": {"origin": "Docetaxel came from a search for a sustainable taxane. Paclitaxel needed bark from the slow-growing Pacific yew, which was scarce. In the 1980s Pierre Potier's team at the CNRS Institut de Chimie des Substances Naturelles in Gif-sur-Yvette, France, worked with 10-deacetylbaccatin III, a taxane core that can be extracted from the needles of the common European yew, Taxus baccata. Needles regrow, so the source was renewable. By adding a new side chain, they made docetaxel, which turned out to be about twice as potent as paclitaxel at binding microtubules in lab studies. Rhone-Poulenc Rorer developed it, and the FDA approved it as Taxotere in 1996. It is now used for breast, lung, prostate, gastric, and head and neck cancers.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Pierre Potier's CNRS team synthesizes docetaxel from European yew needle extract."], ["1996", "FDA approves Taxotere for advanced breast cancer."], ["2004", "Approval with prednisone for metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer."]], "cell": "Docetaxel binds the taxane site on beta-tubulin inside the microtubule. It pushes tubulin to assemble and blocks disassembly, producing rigid, nonfunctional microtubule bundles. The mitotic spindle cannot reorganize, so the spindle assembly checkpoint holds cells in M phase (G2/M), and they undergo apoptosis. It also promotes phosphorylation of the anti-apoptotic protein bcl-2, removing a survival signal. In prostate cancer, microtubule stabilization also hinders transport of the androgen receptor into the nucleus.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils drop, with a nadir at about one week, and febrile neutropenia is common."], ["Fluid balance", "Capillaries become leaky over repeated cycles, causing swelling, weight gain, pleural effusions and ascites; dexamethasone premedication reduces this."], ["Skin and nails", "Nail discoloration and loss, rash and hand-foot changes occur."], ["Nerves", "Sensory neuropathy develops as with other taxanes."], ["Eyes", "Excess tearing from tear duct narrowing and rarely cystoid macular edema occur."], ["Hair", "Hair loss is common, and some patients have persistent alopecia."]], "journey": "Given IV as a one-hour infusion. It binds tightly to plasma proteins and spreads widely into tissues. The liver breaks it down through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors increase exposure, and liver impairment sharply raises the risk of fatal toxicity. About three quarters leaves in the feces through bile and only a small amount in urine. The terminal half-life is about 11 hours. The formulation contains polysorbate 80 and alcohol, and the alcohol content can cause intoxication-like effects.", "sidefx": "Neutropenia and infections come from mitotic arrest of marrow precursors. Fluid retention is a cumulative capillary leak that corticosteroid premedication reduces. Hypersensitivity reactions are partly linked to the polysorbate 80 solvent. Neuropathy comes from rigid axonal microtubules. Toxic deaths are much more likely with abnormal liver tests because the liver clears the drug, which is why the boxed warning centers on liver function. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Docetaxel was made from needles instead of bark, so yew trees did not have to be cut down to produce it.", "src": ["Taxotere (docetaxel) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Gueritte-Voegelein F et al. J Med Chem 1991"]}, "cabazitaxel": {"origin": "Cabazitaxel was developed by Sanofi as a next-generation taxane for tumors that had stopped responding to docetaxel. A common way tumors resist taxanes is by pumping them out with P-glycoprotein (MDR1). Sanofi chemists modified the docetaxel structure, adding two methoxy groups, which made the molecule a poor substrate for this pump. Like docetaxel, it is made semisynthetically from 10-deacetylbaccatin III extracted from yew needles. In the TROPIC trial, cabazitaxel with prednisone improved survival in men with metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer that had progressed after docetaxel, and the FDA approved it as Jevtana in 2010.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Sanofi develops cabazitaxel to overcome P-glycoprotein-mediated taxane resistance."], ["2010", "The TROPIC trial shows a survival benefit after docetaxel."], ["2010", "FDA approves Jevtana for metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer."]], "cell": "Cabazitaxel binds beta-tubulin at the taxane site and stabilizes microtubules by promoting assembly and blocking disassembly. The rigid microtubules prevent the mitotic spindle from working, so cells arrest at G2/M and die by apoptosis. Its key difference from docetaxel is low affinity for P-glycoprotein, the ATP-driven efflux pump that many resistant cancer cells overexpress, so it stays inside those cells. The same property lets it cross the blood-brain barrier better than other taxanes.", "body": [["Prostate cancer cells", "Docetaxel-resistant tumor cells that pump out other taxanes are still killed during mitosis."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia is common and can be fatal; neutrophils bottom out in the second week of the cycle."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea and vomiting come from injury to dividing gut lining cells and can cause dehydration."], ["Kidneys and bladder", "Dehydration, sepsis or urinary obstruction can cause kidney failure, and cystitis with blood in the urine can occur, including radiation recall."], ["Immune system", "Severe hypersensitivity reactions can occur, partly linked to the polysorbate 80 solvent."]], "journey": "Given IV as a one-hour infusion, with antihistamine, corticosteroid and H2 blocker premedication. It spreads widely into tissues and binds plasma proteins. The liver metabolizes most of it through CYP3A4 and CYP3A5, with a little CYP2C8, so strong CYP3A inhibitors and inducers alter its levels. About three quarters is excreted in feces and very little in urine. Its terminal half-life is long, about four days.", "sidefx": "Neutropenic deaths, in the boxed warning, come from mitotic arrest of granulocyte precursors; prophylactic growth factor support is often used. Severe hypersensitivity is linked to polysorbate 80. Diarrhea comes from killing dividing intestinal cells. Kidney failure often follows dehydration or sepsis. Liver impairment raises exposure because the liver clears the drug. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Jevtana (cabazitaxel) FDA label", "de Bono JS et al. TROPIC trial, Lancet 2010", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "eribulin": {"origin": "Eribulin comes from the sea. In 1986 Japanese chemists Daisuke Uemura and Yoshimasa Hirata isolated halichondrin B from the marine sponge Halichondria okadai. It was extremely potent against cancer cells, but sponges made only tiny amounts. In 1992 Yoshito Kishi's lab at Harvard completed a total synthesis of halichondrin B. Working with Kishi, scientists at the Japanese company Eisai found that the right half of the molecule carried most of the activity and designed a simpler, fully synthetic version, eribulin. Even simplified, it takes dozens of chemical steps to make, making it one of the most complex drugs produced by total synthesis. The FDA approved it as Halaven in 2010 for metastatic breast cancer and in 2016 for liposarcoma.", "timeline": [["1986", "Uemura and Hirata isolate halichondrin B from the sponge Halichondria okadai."], ["1992", "Yoshito Kishi's group at Harvard completes the total synthesis of halichondrin B."], ["2010", "FDA approves Halaven for metastatic breast cancer."], ["2016", "FDA approves eribulin for unresectable or metastatic liposarcoma."]], "cell": "Eribulin works differently from both taxanes and vinca alkaloids. It binds to the plus ends of microtubules with high affinity and suppresses the growth phase of dynamic instability, while leaving the shortening phase largely untouched. It also traps free tubulin in nonproductive aggregates. Without normal growth at the plus ends, the mitotic spindle cannot form, and cells arrest at G2/M. The block is irreversible, so cells die by apoptosis even after the drug is gone. In lab and animal studies it also remodels tumor blood vessels and reverses epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition, which may limit spread.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia is the most common serious effect, with a nadir around day 13."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral neuropathy develops from disrupted axonal microtubules, though often milder than with taxanes."], ["Heart rhythm", "It can prolong the QT interval, especially with low potassium or magnesium."], ["Hair and energy", "Hair loss and fatigue are common, along with nausea and constipation."], ["Tumor tissue", "Breast cancer and liposarcoma cells stall in mitosis and die."]], "journey": "Given IV over two to five minutes. It spreads widely into tissues and has a long half-life of about 40 hours. Unlike taxanes, it is barely metabolized and CYP3A4 plays little role, so drug interactions are few. Most of it is excreted unchanged in the feces, with a small amount in urine. Liver and moderate or severe kidney impairment raise exposure and call for lower doses per the label.", "sidefx": "Neutropenia comes from mitotic arrest of marrow precursors, so blood counts are checked before each dose. Neuropathy comes from impaired microtubule growth in long nerve axons. QT prolongation was seen in studies and is more likely with low potassium or magnesium, heart failure or other QT drugs. It is not a vesicant like the vincas. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Early isolation work processed about 600 kilograms of sponge to obtain roughly a third of a gram of halichondrin B, which is why a fully synthetic version was the only practical route.", "src": ["Halaven (eribulin) FDA label", "Hirata Y, Uemura D. Pure Appl Chem 1986", "Aicher TD, Kishi Y et al. J Am Chem Soc 1992"]}, "ixabepilone": {"origin": "Ixabepilone is based on epothilones, natural products made by a soil myxobacterium, Sorangium cellulosum. In 1987 Gerhard Hofle and Hans Reichenbach at the German Research Centre for Biotechnology (GBF) in Braunschweig isolated epothilones, first noting antifungal activity. In 1995 researchers at Merck showed epothilones stabilize microtubules like paclitaxel, even in taxane-resistant cells. Natural epothilone B is unstable in the body because enzymes break its lactone ring. Bristol-Myers Squibb chemists replaced the ring oxygen with nitrogen, making a lactam that resists breakdown. This ixabepilone was approved by the FDA as Ixempra in 2007 for metastatic or locally advanced breast cancer resistant to other drugs.", "timeline": [["1987", "Hofle and Reichenbach isolate epothilones from Sorangium cellulosum."], ["1995", "Epothilones are shown to stabilize microtubules like paclitaxel."], ["2007", "FDA approves Ixempra for resistant metastatic or locally advanced breast cancer."]], "cell": "Ixabepilone binds beta-tubulin at or near the taxane site, promoting microtubule assembly and preventing disassembly. Hyperstable microtubules cannot form a working mitotic spindle, so cells arrest at G2/M and undergo apoptosis. It is less affected than taxanes by common resistance mechanisms: it is a poor substrate for P-glycoprotein and MRP-1 efflux pumps and retains activity in cells that overexpress beta-III tubulin, an isotype linked to taxane resistance.", "body": [["Breast cancer cells", "Tumors resistant to anthracyclines and taxanes can still be arrested in mitosis and killed."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral sensory neuropathy is common and is the main dose-limiting effect."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia with a nadir in the second week raises infection risk."], ["Muscles and joints", "Muscle and joint aches, fatigue and hair loss are common."], ["Heart", "Rarely it is linked to myocardial ischemia and ventricular dysfunction."]], "journey": "Given IV as a three-hour infusion after premedication with H1 and H2 blockers, because the solvent contains polyoxyl 35 castor oil (Cremophor). It spreads widely into tissues. The liver breaks it down extensively through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors and inducers alter exposure. Most leaves in the feces, with some in urine. The half-life is about 52 hours.", "sidefx": "Neuropathy comes from rigid microtubules disrupting transport along long sensory nerves. Neutropenia reflects mitotic arrest of marrow precursors. Hypersensitivity reactions are linked to the castor oil solvent, so premedication is required. The boxed warning against combining it with capecitabine in liver impairment exists because reduced CYP3A4 clearance raises drug levels and caused neutropenia-related deaths. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Ixempra (ixabepilone) FDA label", "Gerth K, Bedorf N, Hofle G, Reichenbach H. J Antibiot 1996", "Bollag DM et al. Cancer Res 1995"]}, "arsenic-trioxide": {"origin": "Arsenic has a long and grim history as a poison, but also as a medicine. Arsenic compounds appear in ancient Greek and Chinese medicine. In 1786 the English physician Thomas Fowler introduced Fowler's solution, a potassium arsenite tonic, and in the late 1800s doctors noticed it lowered white cell counts in leukemia. The modern story began in China. In the 1970s Zhang Tingdong and colleagues at Harbin Medical University found that an arsenic trioxide preparation could put patients with acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL) into remission. In the 1990s Shanghai researchers, including Chen Zhu and Wang Zhen-yi, showed how it works on the PML-RARA protein. A 1998 New England Journal of Medicine study from Memorial Sloan Kettering confirmed the results, and the FDA approved Trisenox in 2000. In 2018 it was approved with tretinoin as first-line therapy for low-risk APL, a chemotherapy-free regimen that cures most patients.", "timeline": [["1786", "Thomas Fowler introduces Fowler's solution, an arsenic tonic."], ["1970s", "Zhang Tingdong's group in Harbin reports arsenic remissions in APL."], ["1998", "Memorial Sloan Kettering study of arsenic trioxide in relapsed APL is published."], ["2000", "FDA approves Trisenox for relapsed or refractory APL."], ["2018", "FDA approves arsenic trioxide with tretinoin as first-line therapy for low-risk APL."]], "cell": "APL is driven by the t(15;17) translocation, which fuses the PML gene to the retinoic acid receptor alpha gene (RARA). The PML-RARA fusion protein blocks myeloid differentiation, so cells pile up as promyelocytes. Arsenic binds directly to cysteine residues in the PML part of the fusion protein. This causes the protein to clump and become tagged with SUMO and then ubiquitin, which marks it for destruction by the proteasome. With PML-RARA gone, the blasts partially mature into neutrophil-like cells. Arsenic also triggers apoptosis by damaging mitochondria, increasing reactive oxygen species and activating caspases. Tretinoin attacks the RARA side of the same fusion protein, so the two drugs work together.", "body": [["Bone marrow and blood", "Leukemic promyelocytes mature and die, and white counts often rise first (leukocytosis) as cells differentiate."], ["Lungs and whole body", "Differentiating cells release cytokines and stick to vessel walls, causing differentiation syndrome with fever, shortness of breath, lung infiltrates, fluid gain and low blood pressure."], ["Heart", "It prolongs the QT interval, which can lead to torsade de pointes or AV block."], ["Blood clotting", "Clearing APL cells resolves the dangerous bleeding and clotting disorder (DIC) typical of APL."], ["Nervous system", "It can cause peripheral neuropathy and encephalopathy, including Wernicke encephalopathy."], ["Liver and metabolism", "Liver enzyme rises, high blood sugar and low potassium and magnesium are common."]], "journey": "Given IV. In the blood, arsenic trioxide dissolves into arsenious acid, the active trivalent form. It distributes widely into tissues, including the liver, kidneys, heart, lungs, hair and nails. The liver reduces and methylates it into monomethylarsonic acid and dimethylarsinic acid, which are less toxic. CYP enzymes are not involved. Most is excreted in urine, so kidney impairment can raise exposure. The half-life of trivalent arsenic is about 10 to 14 hours, but arsenic lingers in tissues much longer.", "sidefx": "Differentiation syndrome comes from the rapid maturation of many leukemic cells that release cytokines and migrate into the lungs and other tissues; it is treated with high-dose corticosteroids. QT prolongation reflects arsenic effects on cardiac potassium and calcium channels and is worse with low potassium or magnesium, so electrolytes and ECGs are monitored. Encephalopathy, including thiamine-related Wernicke encephalopathy, has been reported. For acute arsenic poisoning, chelating agents such as dimercaprol or succimer are used, but they are not used to reverse therapeutic effects.", "fun": "Fowler's solution was so widely used in the 1800s that Charles Darwin is thought to have taken it for skin problems.", "src": ["Trisenox (arsenic trioxide) FDA label", "Soignet SL et al. N Engl J Med 1998", "Lo-Coco F et al. N Engl J Med 2013", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "pegaspargase": {"origin": "Asparaginase therapy started with an odd observation. In 1953 John Kidd at Cornell found that guinea pig serum made lymphomas melt away in mice, but the serum of horses or rabbits did not. In 1961 J. D. Broome showed the active factor was the enzyme L-asparaginase. Scientists then found that the bacterium Escherichia coli makes a similar enzyme in large amounts, and E. coli asparaginase (Elspar) was approved by the FDA in 1978 for acute lymphoblastic leukemia. Because the bacterial protein triggered allergic reactions and was cleared quickly, Enzon attached polyethylene glycol chains to it. This PEG-asparaginase, Oncaspar, was approved in 1994 for patients allergic to native asparaginase, and in 2006 for first-line ALL. It is now the standard asparaginase in US ALL regimens.", "timeline": [["1953", "John Kidd finds guinea pig serum makes lymphomas regress in mice."], ["1961", "J. D. Broome identifies L-asparaginase as the active factor."], ["1978", "FDA approves native E. coli asparaginase (Elspar)."], ["1994", "FDA approves Oncaspar for patients allergic to native asparaginase."], ["2006", "Oncaspar is approved for first-line treatment of ALL."]], "cell": "Pegaspargase is E. coli L-asparaginase with many PEG chains attached. The enzyme stays in the bloodstream and breaks down the amino acid L-asparagine into aspartic acid and ammonia. Most normal cells can make their own asparagine using asparagine synthetase. Leukemic lymphoblasts have very low asparagine synthetase activity, so they depend on asparagine from the blood. When it is gone, they cannot make proteins, stop in G1 and die by apoptosis. The enzyme also breaks down some glutamine. The PEG chains shield the protein from antibodies and slow its clearance, so one dose works for about two weeks or more.", "body": [["Leukemic cells", "Starved of asparagine, ALL lymphoblasts stop making proteins and die."], ["Liver", "The liver's own protein production also falls, lowering albumin, clotting factors and anticoagulant proteins, and causing fatty liver and enzyme rises."], ["Blood clotting", "Lower antithrombin, protein C and protein S alongside lower fibrinogen raise the risk of both clots, including cerebral venous sinus thrombosis, and bleeding."], ["Pancreas", "It can cause pancreatitis, sometimes severe or necrotizing."], ["Blood sugar", "Less insulin production and possibly insulin resistance cause high blood sugar, especially with steroids."], ["Immune system", "The bacterial protein can still trigger allergic reactions or silent inactivating antibodies."]], "journey": "Given IV or IM. It is a large protein that stays mainly in the blood and lymph, with little entry into tissues. It is not cleared by the kidneys or liver enzymes; it is slowly broken down by proteases and taken up by the reticuloendothelial system. The PEG coat stretches the half-life to about five to six days, so asparaginase activity lasts about two to three weeks. Serum asparaginase activity can be measured to confirm the drug is working.", "sidefx": "Most toxicities come from asparagine and glutamine depletion in normal organs that make a lot of protein. The liver makes less albumin, fibrinogen and natural anticoagulants, which explains clots, bleeding and hepatotoxicity. Reduced insulin synthesis causes high blood sugar. Pancreatitis is a direct toxic effect on the pancreas. Allergic reactions come from antibodies against the bacterial protein; these can also neutralize the drug silently. Patients who react are usually switched to Erwinia-derived asparaginase. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The first clue to asparaginase came from guinea pigs: only their serum, among many animals tested, made lymphomas shrink in mice.", "src": ["Oncaspar (pegaspargase) FDA label", "Kidd JG. J Exp Med 1953", "Broome JD. Nature 1961", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "calaspargase-pegol": {"origin": "Calaspargase pegol is the newest step in the asparaginase story that began in 1953, when John Kidd found that guinea pig serum shrank lymphomas in mice, and in 1961, when J. D. Broome traced that effect to the enzyme L-asparaginase. E. coli asparaginase and then pegaspargase (PEG-linked E. coli asparaginase) became pillars of ALL treatment. Pegaspargase attaches its PEG chains through a succinimidyl succinate linker, which can break down over time. Calaspargase pegol uses the same E. coli enzyme but a more stable succinimidyl carbonate linker, so the PEG coat stays on longer and enzyme activity lasts longer. The FDA approved it as Asparlas, marketed by Servier, in 2018 as part of multi-agent therapy for ALL in patients from 1 month to 21 years old.", "timeline": [["1953", "John Kidd observes lymphoma regression from guinea pig serum."], ["1961", "J. D. Broome identifies L-asparaginase as the active factor."], ["1994", "Pegaspargase, an earlier PEGylated E. coli asparaginase, is FDA approved."], ["2018", "FDA approves Asparlas (calaspargase pegol-mknl) for ALL in patients 1 month to 21 years."]], "cell": "Calaspargase pegol is E. coli L-asparaginase with PEG chains linked by succinimidyl carbonate bonds. In the blood, the enzyme splits L-asparagine into aspartic acid and ammonia, and it also breaks down some glutamine. Normal cells can make asparagine with asparagine synthetase, but ALL lymphoblasts have very little of this enzyme. Without asparagine from the blood, they cannot build proteins, arrest in G1 and die by apoptosis. The more stable linker keeps the PEG shield attached, slowing clearance and reducing exposure of the enzyme to antibodies, so asparagine stays depleted for longer between doses.", "body": [["Leukemic cells", "Lymphoblasts starved of asparagine stop making proteins and die."], ["Liver", "Reduced protein synthesis lowers albumin, fibrinogen and natural anticoagulants and can cause fatty liver, liver enzyme rises and high bilirubin."], ["Blood clotting", "Imbalanced clotting and anticoagulant proteins increase the risk of both thrombosis and bleeding."], ["Pancreas", "Pancreatitis can occur and may be severe."], ["Blood sugar", "Lower insulin production can cause high blood sugar, especially with steroids."], ["Immune system", "Antibodies to the bacterial protein can cause allergic reactions or silently inactivate the drug."]], "journey": "Given by IV infusion. Like other asparaginases, it is a large protein that stays mostly in the bloodstream. It is not cleared by the kidneys or by liver CYP enzymes; instead it is slowly broken down by proteases and removed by the reticuloendothelial system. The stable linker gives it a long half-life of about 16 days, so asparaginase activity stays high for about three weeks or longer, allowing less frequent dosing than pegaspargase. Patients are watched for one hour after each dose for allergic reactions.", "sidefx": "Side effects mirror its mechanism. Depleting asparagine and glutamine slows protein synthesis in the liver, which explains clotting problems, bleeding and liver toxicity. Reduced insulin synthesis raises blood sugar. Pancreatitis is a known direct toxicity. Hypersensitivity comes from antibodies to the E. coli protein; patients who react are generally switched to an Erwinia-derived asparaginase, which the antibodies do not recognize. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Asparlas (calaspargase pegol-mknl) FDA label", "Broome JD. Nature 1961", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "asparaginase-erwinia-recombinant": {"origin": "Asparaginase from the plant-disease bacterium Erwinia chrysanthemi (now often called Dickeya chrysanthemi) has been used for decades as a backup for patients allergic to E. coli asparaginase, because the two enzymes look different to the immune system. A native Erwinia product, Erwinaze, was FDA approved in 2011, but it was made in limited supply and shortages were common. To fix that, Jazz Pharmaceuticals developed a recombinant version, produced by inserting the Erwinia enzyme gene into the bacterium Pseudomonas fluorescens. The FDA approved it as Rylaze in 2021 for ALL and lymphoblastic lymphoma in patients who developed hypersensitivity to E. coli-derived asparaginase. It was reviewed under the FDA's Real-Time Oncology Review program.", "timeline": [["1961", "L-asparaginase is identified as the antileukemic factor in guinea pig serum."], ["2011", "FDA approves native Erwinia asparaginase (Erwinaze)."], ["2021", "FDA approves Rylaze, a recombinant Erwinia asparaginase made in Pseudomonas fluorescens."]], "cell": "Rylaze is an L-asparaginase with the amino acid sequence of the Erwinia chrysanthemi enzyme. In the blood it breaks L-asparagine into aspartic acid and ammonia, and it breaks down glutamine somewhat more than the E. coli enzyme does. ALL lymphoblasts lack enough asparagine synthetase to make their own asparagine, so when blood levels drop they cannot build proteins, arrest in G1 and undergo apoptosis. Because its structure differs from E. coli asparaginase, antibodies a patient made against the E. coli enzyme generally do not bind or neutralize it, so it keeps working after an allergy.", "body": [["Leukemic cells", "Asparagine starvation kills lymphoblasts that cannot make their own asparagine."], ["Liver", "Lower protein production can raise liver enzymes and bilirubin and reduce clotting proteins."], ["Blood clotting", "Changes in fibrinogen and natural anticoagulants raise the risk of clots and bleeding."], ["Pancreas", "Pancreatitis can occur, likely from direct toxicity to pancreatic cells that make large amounts of protein."], ["Blood sugar", "Reduced insulin synthesis can raise blood sugar."], ["Injection site and immune system", "IM injection can cause local pain, and the protein can still cause allergic reactions."]], "journey": "Given as an IM injection. It peaks in the blood in about one to two days. Without a PEG coat, it has a short half-life of about 18 hours, so it is given frequently, such as every 48 hours or on a Monday-Wednesday-Friday schedule, to keep asparaginase activity high. Like other protein drugs, it is broken down by proteases and the reticuloendothelial system, not by the kidneys or liver enzymes.", "sidefx": "Like all asparaginases, its toxicities come from depleting asparagine and glutamine, which slows protein synthesis in the liver and pancreas. That explains liver toxicity, low clotting proteins with clots or bleeding, high blood sugar and pancreatitis. Hypersensitivity can still occur because it is a bacterial protein, though cross-reactivity with E. coli asparaginase antibodies is low. Nausea and fatigue are common. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The Erwinia bacterium that supplies this enzyme's gene is best known as a cause of soft rot in plants such as potatoes and chrysanthemums.", "src": ["Rylaze (asparaginase erwinia chrysanthemi (recombinant)-rywn) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "romidepsin": {"origin": "Romidepsin is a natural product from a soil bacterium. In the early 1990s scientists at the Japanese company Fujisawa Pharmaceutical isolated it, then called FR901228 or FK228, from Chromobacterium violaceum, a bacterium known for its violet pigment. It showed striking activity against tumor cells, and its mechanism was later found to be inhibition of histone deacetylases. The US National Cancer Institute helped carry it through early trials, and Gloucester Pharmaceuticals took it to approval. The FDA approved it as Istodax in 2009 for cutaneous T-cell lymphoma (CTCL) after at least one prior systemic therapy. A peripheral T-cell lymphoma indication granted in 2011 was withdrawn in 2021 after a confirmatory trial did not show benefit.", "timeline": [["1994", "Fujisawa scientists report FR901228 (romidepsin) from Chromobacterium violaceum."], ["1998", "Romidepsin is shown to inhibit histone deacetylases."], ["2009", "FDA approves Istodax for cutaneous T-cell lymphoma."], ["2021", "The peripheral T-cell lymphoma indication is withdrawn in the US."]], "cell": "DNA in the nucleus is wrapped around histone proteins. Histone deacetylases (HDACs) remove acetyl groups from histone tails, which tightens chromatin and silences genes. Romidepsin is a prodrug with an internal disulfide bond. Inside the cell, glutathione reduces the disulfide, freeing a thiol group that binds the zinc ion at the active site of class I HDACs, mainly HDAC1 and HDAC2. With these enzymes blocked, acetylated histones build up, chromatin opens and silenced genes, including the cell-cycle brake p21, are switched back on. Malignant T cells then arrest in G1 and G2/M and undergo apoptosis. Acetylation of non-histone proteins, such as transcription factors, also contributes.", "body": [["Malignant T cells", "Lymphoma cells in the skin and blood stop dividing and die, which can shrink skin plaques and tumors."], ["Bone marrow", "Platelets, neutrophils and red cells fall, raising bleeding and infection risk."], ["Gut and taste", "Nausea, vomiting, poor appetite and altered taste are very common."], ["Heart", "It causes T-wave and ST-segment changes on ECG and can prolong the QT interval, especially with low potassium or magnesium."], ["Immune system", "Serious infections occur, including reactivation of Epstein-Barr virus and hepatitis B."], ["Energy", "Fatigue is one of the most frequent complaints."]], "journey": "Given by IV infusion over four hours. It binds strongly to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels and inducers lower them. It is also a P-glycoprotein substrate. The half-life is short, about three hours, but its effects on gene expression outlast its presence in the blood. Elimination is mostly by liver metabolism.", "sidefx": "HDAC inhibition affects many normal cells, not just cancer cells. Cytopenias come from effects on dividing blood precursors. Nausea and fatigue are common class effects. ECG changes and QT prolongation are typical of HDAC inhibitors, so potassium and magnesium are kept in range before each dose. Infections and viral reactivation reflect immune suppression and depletion of normal T cells. Tumor lysis syndrome can occur in heavily burdened disease. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Romidepsin's producer, Chromobacterium violaceum, makes a striking purple pigment called violacein that makes its colonies look violet.", "src": ["Istodax (romidepsin) FDA label", "Ueda H et al. J Antibiot 1994", "Nakajima H et al. Exp Cell Res 1998"]}, "belinostat": {"origin": "Belinostat is a synthetic histone deacetylase (HDAC) inhibitor. It was first developed in the early 2000s as PXD101 by the British company Prolifix, later part of the Danish company TopoTarget. Its design draws on the hydroxamic acid chemistry of earlier HDAC inhibitors such as vorinostat, which grip the zinc ion inside the enzyme. Spectrum Pharmaceuticals later took it through registration. Based on response rates in the BELIEF trial, the FDA granted accelerated approval to Beleodaq in 2014 for relapsed or refractory peripheral T-cell lymphoma (PTCL), a group of lymphomas with few treatment options.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Belinostat (PXD101) is developed by Prolifix and TopoTarget."], ["2014", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Beleodaq for relapsed or refractory PTCL."]], "cell": "Belinostat is a hydroxamic acid. Its hydroxamate group binds the zinc ion in the catalytic pocket of histone deacetylases, blocking class I, II and IV HDACs, so it is called a pan-HDAC inhibitor. With HDACs blocked, acetyl groups build up on histones and on many other proteins, including p53, tubulin and the chaperone HSP90. Open chromatin allows silenced tumor suppressor genes to be expressed again. The combined effects cause cell-cycle arrest, failure of DNA repair and apoptosis, which malignant T cells seem especially sensitive to.", "body": [["Malignant T cells", "Lymphoma cells arrest and die, shrinking tumors in a subset of patients with PTCL."], ["Bone marrow", "Thrombocytopenia, neutropenia and anemia occur, raising bleeding and infection risk."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea and constipation are common."], ["Liver", "Liver toxicity can occur, so liver tests are checked before each cycle."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue and fever are frequent, reflecting broad effects on many cell types and immune signaling."]], "journey": "Given IV over 30 minutes. It binds strongly to plasma proteins. The liver clears it mainly by glucuronidation through UGT1A1, with minor CYP2A6, CYP2C9 and CYP3A4 involvement. People who are homozygous for the UGT1A1*28 variant clear it more slowly and start at a reduced dose per the label. The half-life is short, about one hour. Metabolites leave mostly in urine.", "sidefx": "Broad HDAC inhibition affects normal cells, especially blood precursors, which explains cytopenias. Nausea, vomiting and fatigue are common class effects. Serious infections, including fatal sepsis and pneumonia, follow low blood counts and immune suppression. Liver toxicity may relate to hepatic metabolism of the drug. Tumor lysis syndrome can occur with large tumor burden. Slow UGT1A1 clearance raises exposure and toxicity. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Beleodaq (belinostat) FDA label", "O'Connor OA et al. BELIEF trial, J Clin Oncol 2015"]}, "bortezomib": {"origin": "Bortezomib grew out of research on the ubiquitin-proteasome system, the cell's protein disposal machinery, whose discovery earned Aaron Ciechanover, Avram Hershko and Irwin Rose the 2004 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. In the mid-1990s chemist Julian Adams and colleagues at a small Massachusetts company, MyoGenics (renamed ProScript), designed boronic acid peptides that block the proteasome. Many experts doubted that blocking such a basic cell process could be safe. ProScript was bought by Millennium Pharmaceuticals, which developed the compound, PS-341. Myeloma cells turned out to be unusually sensitive. The FDA granted accelerated approval as Velcade in 2003 for relapsed multiple myeloma, making it the first proteasome inhibitor. It was approved for first-line myeloma in 2008 and for mantle cell lymphoma in 2006.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Ciechanover, Hershko and Rose work out ubiquitin-mediated protein degradation."], ["1995", "Julian Adams's team at ProScript designs PS-341 (bortezomib)."], ["2003", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Velcade for relapsed multiple myeloma."], ["2006", "Approval for relapsed mantle cell lymphoma."], ["2008", "Approval for previously untreated multiple myeloma."]], "cell": "Cells tag unwanted proteins with chains of ubiquitin and feed them into the 26S proteasome, a barrel-shaped complex whose 20S core contains protease sites. Bortezomib's boron atom binds reversibly to the threonine in the beta5 subunit, which has chymotrypsin-like activity. With the proteasome blocked, misfolded and regulatory proteins pile up. IkappaB, which normally holds NF-kappaB inactive, is not degraded, so NF-kappaB survival signaling drops. Pro-apoptotic proteins such as Bax, Noxa and p53 accumulate. Myeloma plasma cells make huge amounts of immunoglobulin, so misfolded protein builds up fast in their endoplasmic reticulum, triggering the unfolded protein response, ER stress and apoptosis.", "body": [["Myeloma cells", "Overwhelmed by unfolded protein, plasma cells die and M-protein levels fall."], ["Peripheral nerves", "Painful sensory neuropathy is common, especially with IV use; subcutaneous dosing reduces it."], ["Blood pressure", "Autonomic nerve injury and other effects cause orthostatic hypotension."], ["Platelets", "Platelet counts fall in a cyclic pattern because proteasome inhibition reversibly slows platelet release from megakaryocytes."], ["Immune system", "Reduced T-cell function increases herpes zoster reactivation, so antiviral prophylaxis is common."], ["Gut and heart", "Diarrhea, constipation and nausea are common; heart failure and lung toxicity are less common but serious."]], "journey": "Given IV or subcutaneously. Subcutaneous dosing gives lower peak levels with similar total exposure, which lowers neuropathy. It distributes widely into tissues but does not enter the brain much. The liver breaks it down by oxidative deboronation through CYP3A4, CYP2C19 and CYP1A2, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors and inducers alter exposure. The half-life lengthens with repeated doses, ranging from about 40 to 193 hours. Proteasome activity recovers within about 72 hours, which sets the twice-weekly or weekly schedules.", "sidefx": "Neuropathy is thought to come from proteasome inhibition in sensory neurons and possibly off-target inhibition of other proteases such as HtrA2/Omi; it is length dependent and often painful. Thrombocytopenia is cyclic and reversible because megakaryocytes need proteasome activity to release platelets. Hypotension reflects autonomic neuropathy. Herpes zoster reactivation reflects impaired cellular immunity. Rapid myeloma cell death can cause tumor lysis syndrome. Giving it intrathecally is fatal. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Bortezomib was the first drug approved that works by blocking the proteasome, a target many scientists once thought was too essential to touch safely.", "src": ["Velcade (bortezomib) FDA label", "Adams J et al. Cancer Res 1999", "Richardson PG et al. N Engl J Med 2003", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "carfilzomib": {"origin": "Carfilzomib traces back to a natural product. In the early 1990s, researchers in Japan isolated epoxomicin, an antitumor compound from an actinomycete soil bacterium. In 1999 Craig Crews and colleagues at Yale University showed that epoxomicin binds the proteasome very selectively and permanently, through its epoxyketone group. Crews's lab made improved analogs, including YU-101, and cofounded the company Proteolix to develop them. Proteolix optimized the molecule into carfilzomib. Onyx Pharmaceuticals acquired Proteolix and took it to approval, and the FDA granted accelerated approval to Kyprolis in 2012 for relapsed and refractory multiple myeloma. Amgen later acquired Onyx.", "timeline": [["1992", "Epoxomicin is reported from an actinomycete in Japan."], ["1999", "Craig Crews's lab at Yale shows epoxomicin selectively and irreversibly inhibits the proteasome."], ["2000s", "Proteolix is founded to develop epoxyketone proteasome inhibitors."], ["2012", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Kyprolis for relapsed and refractory multiple myeloma."]], "cell": "Carfilzomib is a tetrapeptide epoxyketone. Its epoxyketone binds the N-terminal threonine of the beta5 (chymotrypsin-like) subunit of the 20S proteasome and the immunoproteasome, forming a stable covalent ring. This bond is irreversible, so proteasome activity returns only when cells make new proteasomes. Polyubiquitinated proteins pile up, the unfolded protein response is overwhelmed, and NF-kappaB signaling falls because IkappaB is not degraded. Myeloma plasma cells, which make huge amounts of antibody protein, undergo ER stress and apoptosis. Carfilzomib is more selective than bortezomib and hits fewer non-proteasome proteases, which is thought to explain its much lower rate of neuropathy.", "body": [["Myeloma cells", "Plasma cells die from proteasome blockade, and M-protein falls, including in some bortezomib-resistant disease."], ["Heart", "New or worsening heart failure, ischemia and arrhythmias can occur."], ["Blood vessels and pressure", "Hypertension, including crisis, and pulmonary hypertension occur, possibly from endothelial and nitric oxide effects."], ["Kidneys", "Acute kidney injury can result from tumor lysis, thrombotic microangiopathy or direct effects."], ["Lungs", "Shortness of breath is common, and ARDS and lung toxicity can occur."], ["Peripheral nerves", "Neuropathy is much less common than with bortezomib."]], "journey": "Given by IV infusion, with hydration and dexamethasone premedication early in treatment. It binds strongly to plasma proteins. It is cleared very quickly, with a half-life of an hour or less, mostly by peptidases and epoxide hydrolysis outside the liver; CYP enzymes play only a minor role, so CYP drug interactions are few. Even though it leaves the blood quickly, proteasome inhibition persists because the binding is permanent. Metabolites leave mainly in urine.", "sidefx": "Cardiac and vascular effects are the major concern. Proposed mechanisms include proteasome inhibition in heart muscle cells, which depend on the proteasome to clear damaged proteins, and reduced nitric oxide signaling in blood vessels, which raises blood pressure. Kidney injury comes from tumor lysis, thrombotic microangiopathy and fluid shifts. Infusion reactions involve cytokine release, which dexamethasone premedication blunts. Neuropathy is rare because carfilzomib spares the off-target proteases bortezomib blocks. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Kyprolis (carfilzomib) FDA label", "Meng L, Crews CM et al. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA 1999", "Siegel DS et al. Blood 2012"]}, "imetelstat": {"origin": "Imetelstat goes after telomerase, the enzyme that rebuilds the protective caps (telomeres) at the ends of chromosomes. Elizabeth Blackburn and Carol Greider discovered telomerase in 1984 using the pond organism Tetrahymena, and they shared the 2009 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine with Jack Szostak. Most normal adult cells have little telomerase, but many cancer cells switch it on to keep dividing. Scientists at Geron Corporation in California designed imetelstat, a short lipid-linked oligonucleotide that blocks the enzyme's RNA template. After decades of trials, the FDA approved it as Rytelo in 2024 for adults with lower-risk myelodysplastic syndromes (MDS) and transfusion-dependent anemia who did not respond to or could not receive erythropoiesis-stimulating agents. It was the first telomerase inhibitor approved.", "timeline": [["1984", "Elizabeth Blackburn and Carol Greider discover telomerase activity."], ["2000s", "Geron develops imetelstat, a lipid-conjugated telomerase template antagonist."], ["2009", "Blackburn, Greider and Szostak share the Nobel Prize for telomere and telomerase discoveries."], ["2024", "FDA approves Rytelo for lower-risk MDS with transfusion-dependent anemia."]], "cell": "Telomerase is made of a reverse transcriptase protein (hTERT) and an RNA component (hTR) whose template sequence is copied to add TTAGGG repeats onto chromosome ends. Imetelstat is a 13-base thio-phosphoramidate oligonucleotide that pairs exactly with the hTR template region, blocking it so the enzyme cannot extend telomeres. A lipid tail helps it enter cells. Malignant myeloid stem and progenitor cells in MDS have high telomerase activity and short telomeres, so they are more dependent on the enzyme. Blocking it drives these malignant clones into apoptosis, which lets normal red cell production recover and reduces the need for transfusions.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Malignant MDS clones shrink, and healthier progenitors restore red cell production, so many patients stop needing regular transfusions."], ["Platelets", "Thrombocytopenia is common and often severe early in treatment because the drug also affects megakaryocyte precursors and the malignant clone that is still making platelets."], ["White blood cells", "Neutropenia is common in the first cycles, raising infection risk."], ["Liver", "Mild liver enzyme rises can occur because the liver takes up a large share of the drug."], ["Clotting tests", "It can prolong the aPTT lab test, an effect seen with this type of oligonucleotide."]], "journey": "Given by IV infusion every four weeks, after premedication with an antihistamine and a corticosteroid. It binds strongly to plasma proteins and is taken up into tissues, including the bone marrow, liver, kidneys and spleen. It is not broken down by CYP enzymes; instead nucleases cut it into shorter pieces. Very little intact drug appears in urine. The half-life is several days. Responses build over weeks as red cell production recovers.", "sidefx": "Thrombocytopenia and neutropenia are on-target effects: the drug kills malignant progenitors that make many of the patient's blood cells and also affects normal dividing progenitors, so counts drop early, usually in the first cycles, and recover. Counts are checked weekly at first. Infusion reactions are reduced with premedication. aPTT prolongation is a lab effect rather than a true bleeding disorder. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Telomerase was discovered in Tetrahymena, a single-celled pond organism with thousands of tiny chromosomes, which made telomere biology easier to study.", "src": ["Rytelo (imetelstat) FDA label", "Greider CW, Blackburn EH. Cell 1985", "Platzbecker U et al. IMerge trial, Lancet 2024"]}, "temsirolimus": {"origin": "Temsirolimus is built from rapamycin, a molecule discovered in soil from Easter Island, which islanders call Rapa Nui. A 1964 Canadian scientific expedition collected the soil, and in 1972 Suren Sehgal at Ayerst Research Laboratories in Montreal isolated rapamycin (sirolimus) from the bacterium Streptomyces hygroscopicus. It turned out to be a powerful immunosuppressant and to slow tumor growth. Its target was later named mTOR, the mechanistic target of rapamycin. Wyeth chemists made temsirolimus (CCI-779), an ester prodrug of sirolimus that is soluble enough for IV use. The FDA approved it as Torisel in 2007 for advanced renal cell carcinoma, making it the first mTOR inhibitor approved for cancer.", "timeline": [["1964", "A Canadian expedition collects soil samples on Easter Island (Rapa Nui)."], ["1972", "Suren Sehgal at Ayerst isolates rapamycin from Streptomyces hygroscopicus."], ["1990s", "mTOR is identified as the cellular target of rapamycin."], ["2007", "FDA approves Torisel for advanced renal cell carcinoma."]], "cell": "Temsirolimus and its active metabolite sirolimus bind the intracellular protein FKBP-12. The drug-FKBP-12 complex attaches to mTOR and inhibits mTOR complex 1 (mTORC1). mTORC1 normally senses growth signals and nutrients and turns on protein synthesis by phosphorylating p70 S6 kinase and 4E-BP1. With mTORC1 blocked, 4E-BP1 stays bound to the translation factor eIF4E, and making of key proteins falls, including cyclin D1 and hypoxia-inducible factors HIF-1 alpha and HIF-2 alpha. Less cyclin D causes G1 arrest. Less HIF means less VEGF, which reduces blood vessel growth in kidney cancers, where VHL loss makes HIF signaling especially important.", "body": [["Kidney tumor", "Tumor cells grow more slowly and fewer new blood vessels form, which helps control advanced renal cell carcinoma."], ["Metabolism", "mTOR blockade disrupts insulin signaling and lipid handling, raising blood sugar, cholesterol and triglycerides."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease (noninfectious pneumonitis) can develop."], ["Mouth and skin", "Mouth sores, rash and nail changes come from slowed renewal of epithelial cells."], ["Immune system", "Immune suppression raises infection risk, including Pneumocystis pneumonia."], ["Wound healing", "Reduced cell growth and angiogenesis slow healing of wounds and surgical sites."]], "journey": "Given IV once weekly, after an antihistamine premedication. In the liver, CYP3A4 converts it largely to sirolimus, which is active and has a longer half-life, about 55 hours versus about 17 hours for temsirolimus. Both bind extensively to red blood cells and plasma proteins. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise and inducers lower sirolimus levels. Most is excreted in the feces, with very little in urine.", "sidefx": "Most side effects follow from mTOR's role in normal growth and metabolism. Blocking insulin and nutrient signaling causes high blood sugar and lipids. Slowed epithelial renewal causes mouth sores and rash. Immune suppression, the same effect that makes sirolimus a transplant drug, increases infections. Pneumonitis is a class effect of mTOR inhibitors. Reduced angiogenesis and cell growth impair wound healing and may contribute to bowel perforation. Infusion reactions are reduced by antihistamine premedication. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Rapamycin is named after Rapa Nui, the native name for Easter Island, where the soil sample that produced it was collected.", "src": ["Torisel (temsirolimus) FDA label", "Vezina C, Kudelski A, Sehgal SN. J Antibiot 1975", "Hudes G et al. N Engl J Med 2007"]}, "pertuzumab": {"origin": "Pertuzumab came from Genentech, the company that also made trastuzumab (Herceptin). Researchers there studied how HER2, a receptor overexpressed in about one in five breast cancers, signals. HER2 has no known ligand of its own; it works mainly by pairing (dimerizing) with partner receptors such as HER3. Genentech scientists developed a mouse antibody called 2C4 that blocked the pairing arm of HER2, then humanized it to make pertuzumab. Because it binds a different part of HER2 than trastuzumab, the two work together. The FDA approved Perjeta in 2012 for metastatic HER2-positive breast cancer, based on the CLEOPATRA trial. In 2013 it became the first drug to receive accelerated approval for neoadjuvant (before surgery) breast cancer treatment. A subcutaneous combination with trastuzumab and hyaluronidase, Phesgo, was approved in 2020.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Genentech develops the 2C4 antibody against the HER2 dimerization domain."], ["2012", "FDA approves Perjeta for metastatic HER2-positive breast cancer."], ["2013", "Accelerated approval for neoadjuvant treatment, the first based on pathologic complete response."], ["2017", "Approval for adjuvant treatment of high-risk early HER2-positive breast cancer."], ["2020", "FDA approves Phesgo, a subcutaneous pertuzumab, trastuzumab and hyaluronidase combination."]], "cell": "HER2 belongs to the EGFR family of receptor tyrosine kinases. It signals by forming pairs (dimers) with EGFR, HER3 or HER4, and the HER2-HER3 pair is an especially strong driver of PI3K/AKT survival signaling. Pertuzumab binds extracellular subdomain II of HER2, the dimerization arm, and physically blocks ligand-driven pairing. Without dimerization, the tyrosine kinases do not cross-phosphorylate, so MAPK and PI3K/AKT pathways stay quiet, and cells stop growing and die. As an IgG1 it also recruits NK cells for antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity. Trastuzumab binds subdomain IV and blocks ligand-independent signaling, so together they shut down HER2 more completely.", "body": [["HER2-positive tumors", "Breast cancer cells lose a major growth signal, and in combination with trastuzumab and a taxane tumors shrink and survival improves."], ["Heart", "HER2 signaling helps heart muscle cells cope with stress, so blocking it can lower ejection fraction and cause heart failure, usually reversible."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is common because EGFR family signaling helps regulate gut lining fluid handling."], ["Skin and hair", "Rash and hair loss (largely from the chemotherapy partner) are common."], ["Pregnancy", "HER2 is needed for fetal kidney development, so exposure can cause low amniotic fluid and fetal harm."]], "journey": "Given as an IV infusion every three weeks, or subcutaneously as part of Phesgo, in which hyaluronidase temporarily breaks down tissue barriers so a large volume can be absorbed. Like other IgG antibodies, it is too large to be filtered by the kidneys and is not metabolized by liver enzymes. It is slowly broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids, and the neonatal Fc receptor recycles it, giving a half-life of about 18 days.", "sidefx": "Heart dysfunction comes from blocking HER2 signals that help cardiomyocytes survive stress; it is monitored with regular LVEF checks and usually reverses when treatment is held. Diarrhea is an EGFR-family class effect on the gut. Fetal toxicity reflects HER2's role in fetal kidney development, so pregnancy is checked before starting. Infusion reactions are generally mild. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Perjeta (pertuzumab) FDA label", "Phesgo FDA label", "Baselga J et al. CLEOPATRA, N Engl J Med 2012"]}, "margetuximab": {"origin": "Margetuximab was developed by the Maryland company MacroGenics. It comes from the same mouse antibody, 4D5, that Genentech humanized to make trastuzumab, so it binds the same spot on HER2. MacroGenics kept the antibody in a chimeric form and engineered its Fc tail, the part that talks to immune cells, to bind more strongly to the activating receptor CD16A on natural killer cells and less to the inhibitory receptor CD32B. The idea was to strengthen immune killing, including in people whose CD16A gene variant normally binds antibodies weakly. Based on the SOPHIA trial, the FDA approved Margenza in 2020, with chemotherapy, for metastatic HER2-positive breast cancer after two or more prior anti-HER2 regimens.", "timeline": [["1980s", "The mouse anti-HER2 antibody 4D5 is developed at Genentech."], ["2010s", "MacroGenics engineers the Fc region of a chimeric 4D5 antibody to enhance immune cell binding."], ["2020", "FDA approves Margenza for pretreated metastatic HER2-positive breast cancer."]], "cell": "Margetuximab binds HER2 extracellular subdomain IV, the same epitope as trastuzumab. This blocks ligand-independent HER2 signaling, reduces cleavage of HER2's outer part (shedding), and lowers MAPK and PI3K/AKT growth signals. Its main design feature is the Fc region. Five amino acid changes increase binding to CD16A (FcgammaRIIIa), including its lower-affinity 158F form, and reduce binding to CD32B (FcgammaRIIb), an inhibitory receptor. Natural killer cells and macrophages are therefore more strongly activated, enhancing antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity against HER2-positive cancer cells.", "body": [["HER2-positive tumors", "Cancer cells lose HER2 growth signals and are targeted more strongly by NK cells."], ["Heart", "Like other HER2 drugs, it can lower ejection fraction and cause heart failure because HER2 signals help heart cells handle stress."], ["Immune system", "Infusion reactions such as fever, chills and rash are common, especially with the first infusion."], ["Gut and energy", "Fatigue, nausea, diarrhea and vomiting are common, partly from the chemotherapy partner."], ["Pregnancy", "Blocking HER2 can harm the fetus, causing low amniotic fluid and kidney problems."]], "journey": "Given IV every three weeks. As a large IgG antibody it stays mostly in the blood and extracellular fluid. It is not cleared by the kidneys or by CYP enzymes; it is broken down slowly into peptides and amino acids. The neonatal Fc receptor protects it from breakdown, giving a half-life of about 19 days. Drug interactions through liver enzymes are not expected.", "sidefx": "Left ventricular dysfunction is a HER2 class effect, so LVEF is checked before and every three months during therapy. Infusion reactions come from immune activation and cytokine release, likely increased by the enhanced Fc design. Fetal harm reflects HER2's role in fetal kidney development. Gut effects and fatigue are largely shared with the chemotherapy partner. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Margenza (margetuximab-cmkb) FDA label", "Rugo HS et al. SOPHIA trial, JAMA Oncol 2021"]}, "obinutuzumab": {"origin": "Obinutuzumab came from a Swiss idea about sugar. Antibodies carry a sugar chain on their Fc tail, and researchers found that removing one sugar, fucose, makes the tail grip the CD16A receptor on natural killer cells much more strongly. Pablo Umana and colleagues at GlycArt Biotechnology in Zurich developed a way to make antibodies with less fucose by engineering the cells that produce them. Roche acquired GlycArt in 2005 and used the method to build GA101, a humanized type II anti-CD20 antibody. Named obinutuzumab, it was approved by the FDA as Gazyva in 2013, with chlorambucil, for previously untreated chronic lymphocytic leukemia. It was the first drug approved after receiving the FDA's new Breakthrough Therapy designation. It later gained approvals for follicular lymphoma and, in 2025, for lupus nephritis.", "timeline": [["2000s", "GlycArt Biotechnology develops glycoengineering to reduce antibody fucose."], ["2013", "FDA approves Gazyva with chlorambucil for untreated CLL, the first approval for a Breakthrough Therapy-designated drug."], ["2016", "Approval for relapsed or refractory follicular lymphoma with bendamustine."], ["2017", "Approval for previously untreated follicular lymphoma."], ["2025", "FDA approves obinutuzumab for active lupus nephritis."]], "cell": "CD20 is a membrane protein on pre-B and mature B cells, including most B-cell lymphoma and CLL cells, but not on stem cells or plasma cells. Obinutuzumab binds CD20 in a different orientation from rituximab, which makes it a type II antibody. Type II antibodies do not cluster CD20 into lipid rafts, so they activate complement less but cause more direct, non-apoptotic cell death. Its Fc region has reduced fucose, which raises its binding to FcgammaRIIIa (CD16A) on NK cells and macrophages and strongly boosts antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity and phagocytosis. The result is deeper B-cell depletion than with rituximab in CLL.", "body": [["Malignant B cells", "CLL and lymphoma cells are killed by NK cells, macrophages and direct cell death, often lowering blood counts of leukemia cells within days."], ["Normal B cells", "Healthy B cells are depleted for months, lowering antibody production and raising infection risk."], ["Immune system on first infusion", "Rapid killing of many cells releases cytokines, causing fever, chills, low blood pressure and breathing trouble during the first infusion."], ["Kidneys and metabolism", "Rapid tumor breakdown can cause tumor lysis syndrome, with high potassium, phosphate and uric acid."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia and thrombocytopenia occur, sometimes late after treatment ends."], ["Liver", "Hepatitis B can reactivate when B cells and antibodies are depleted, leading to severe hepatitis."]], "journey": "Given IV, starting with a split first dose to limit infusion reactions. As an IgG antibody it stays mainly in the blood and is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids rather than by the liver or kidneys. Early on, clearance is faster because many CD20-positive cells bind and remove it (target-mediated clearance). As B cells disappear, clearance slows and the terminal half-life is about 26 to 36 days. B-cell depletion lasts many months after the last dose.", "sidefx": "Infusion reactions come from cytokine release as large numbers of B cells are killed quickly; premedication with acetaminophen, an antihistamine and a corticosteroid reduces them. Tumor lysis syndrome reflects rapid cell death in high-burden CLL. Hepatitis B reactivation and progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML), the boxed warnings, happen because B-cell depletion removes immune control of latent viruses, so HBV screening is done first. Neutropenia may involve immune effects on the marrow. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Gazyva (obinutuzumab) FDA label", "Goede V et al. CLL11, N Engl J Med 2014", "Mossner E et al. Blood 2010"]}, "ramucirumab": {"origin": "Ramucirumab came from the New York biotech company ImClone Systems, which developed a fully human antibody (code name IMC-1121B) against VEGF receptor 2, the main receptor that drives blood vessel growth in tumors. The idea that tumors need new blood vessels to grow was championed by Judah Folkman in the 1970s. Unlike bevacizumab, which mops up the VEGF-A protein, ramucirumab blocks the receptor itself. Eli Lilly acquired ImClone in 2008 and completed development. The FDA approved Cyramza in 2014 for advanced gastric or gastroesophageal junction cancer after prior chemotherapy. It later gained approvals for non-small cell lung cancer, colorectal cancer and hepatocellular carcinoma with high alpha-fetoprotein.", "timeline": [["1971", "Judah Folkman proposes that tumors depend on angiogenesis."], ["2000s", "ImClone develops IMC-1121B, a human antibody against VEGFR2."], ["2008", "Eli Lilly acquires ImClone."], ["2014", "FDA approves Cyramza for advanced gastric cancer."], ["2019", "Approval for hepatocellular carcinoma with high alpha-fetoprotein levels."]], "cell": "Vascular endothelial growth factor receptor 2 (VEGFR2, also called KDR) is a receptor tyrosine kinase on endothelial cells. When VEGF-A, VEGF-C or VEGF-D binds, the receptor pairs up, phosphorylates itself and turns on signals (PLC-gamma, MAPK and PI3K/AKT) that make endothelial cells divide, migrate, survive and become leaky. Ramucirumab is a fully human IgG1 that binds the extracellular domain of VEGFR2 and blocks all three ligands from attaching. Without this signal, new tumor vessels do not form, existing abnormal vessels regress, and tumors lose blood supply. It is usually combined with chemotherapy, and vessel normalization may also help chemotherapy reach the tumor.", "body": [["Tumor blood vessels", "Fewer new vessels grow, which slows tumor growth, especially with chemotherapy."], ["Blood pressure", "Less VEGF signaling lowers nitric oxide production in vessel walls, so blood pressure rises."], ["Kidneys", "VEGF supports the filtering cells of the glomerulus, so blocking it causes protein in the urine and, rarely, nephrotic syndrome."], ["Bleeding and healing", "Weakened vessel repair raises the risk of bleeding, nosebleeds, poor wound healing and bowel perforation."], ["Arteries", "Arterial clots such as heart attack and stroke are uncommon but serious."], ["Liver in cirrhosis", "In patients with advanced cirrhosis, worsening liver function and encephalopathy have been reported."]], "journey": "Given by IV infusion, usually every two or three weeks, after an H1 antihistamine premedication. As a large IgG antibody, it stays in the blood and extracellular fluid. It is not metabolized by liver enzymes or cleared by the kidneys; cells break it down into peptides and amino acids. The neonatal Fc receptor recycles it, giving a half-life of about two weeks. No CYP drug interactions are expected.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow from blocking VEGF signals that normal blood vessels need. Lower nitric oxide and reduced capillary density raise blood pressure. Glomerular injury causes proteinuria. Impaired vessel maintenance and repair causes bleeding, poor wound healing and GI perforation, so it is held around surgery. Endothelial dysfunction raises arterial clot risk. Reversible posterior encephalopathy syndrome (PRES) can follow severe blood pressure changes. The boxed warning for bleeding, perforation and wound healing was removed in 2020, but these remain warnings. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Cyramza (ramucirumab) FDA label", "Fuchs CS et al. REGARD trial, Lancet 2014", "Folkman J. N Engl J Med 1971"]}, "cetuximab": {"origin": "Cetuximab began in the early 1980s in San Diego, where John Mendelsohn, Gordon Sato and colleagues at the University of California made mouse antibodies against the epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR), including one called 225. They showed that blocking EGFR could stop cancer cells from growing, a new idea at the time. The antibody was turned into a mouse-human chimera (C225) and developed by ImClone Systems. In 2001 the FDA refused to review ImClone's first application, and a related insider trading case became a national news story. After more trials, the FDA approved Erbitux in 2004 for metastatic colorectal cancer and in 2006 for head and neck cancer with radiation. In 2009 its use in colorectal cancer was limited to tumors without KRAS mutations, an early example of a genetic test guiding a cancer drug.", "timeline": [["1983", "Mendelsohn and Sato's group at UC San Diego develops anti-EGFR mouse antibody 225."], ["2001", "The FDA declines to file ImClone's first cetuximab application."], ["2004", "FDA approves Erbitux for metastatic colorectal cancer."], ["2006", "Approval for head and neck squamous cell cancer with radiation."], ["2009", "Labeling limits colorectal use to KRAS wild-type tumors."]], "cell": "EGFR (HER1, ERBB1) is a receptor tyrosine kinase on many epithelial cells and cancers. When EGF or TGF-alpha binds, EGFR pairs with another receptor, phosphorylates itself and switches on RAS-RAF-MEK-ERK and PI3K-AKT pathways that drive growth and survival. Cetuximab binds domain III of the EGFR extracellular region and blocks the ligand from binding. Without signaling, cells arrest in G1, make less VEGF and are more easily killed by radiation. The antibody-receptor complex is pulled inside and degraded, lowering EGFR on the surface. As an IgG1 it also recruits NK cells for antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity. If RAS is mutated, the pathway is switched on below the receptor, so blocking EGFR does not help.", "body": [["Tumor cells", "Colorectal and head and neck cancer cells lose EGFR-driven growth signals and become more sensitive to radiation and chemotherapy."], ["Skin", "EGFR keeps hair follicles and skin cells maturing normally, so blocking it causes an acne-like rash, dry skin, itching and nail fold infections."], ["Kidneys and electrolytes", "EGFR in the kidney tubule drives magnesium reabsorption, so blocking it causes magnesium wasting and low magnesium, which can lower calcium and potassium."], ["Immune system", "Severe infusion reactions can occur, sometimes on the first dose."], ["Heart and lungs", "Cardiopulmonary arrest has been reported with radiation or platinum-based chemotherapy, and interstitial lung disease can occur."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and mucositis, especially with radiation, are common."]], "journey": "Given by IV infusion, weekly or every two weeks. As a large IgG antibody, it stays mostly in the blood and extracellular fluid. It is cleared in two ways: general protein breakdown and binding to EGFR on cells, which pulls it inside to be degraded. That target-mediated clearance means levels rise more than proportionally at higher exposure. At steady state the half-life is about 112 hours (roughly five days). It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes.", "sidefx": "Skin toxicity is an on-target effect, because EGFR is essential for normal skin and follicle biology; the acneiform rash is common enough that it is used as a sign the drug is active. Low magnesium comes from blocked EGFR in the kidney's thick ascending limb and distal tubule. Severe infusion reactions are often anaphylactic and are linked to preexisting IgE antibodies against galactose-alpha-1,3-galactose (alpha-gal), a sugar on cetuximab made in mouse cells. Cardiopulmonary arrest may relate to electrolyte changes. There is no antidote; premedication with an H1 antagonist is used.", "fun": "Anaphylaxis to cetuximab was most common in the southeastern US, and the cause was traced to alpha-gal antibodies, which can follow lone star tick bites and also cause red meat allergy.", "src": ["Erbitux (cetuximab) FDA label", "Chung CH et al. N Engl J Med 2008 (alpha-gal)", "Mendelsohn J. Clin Cancer Res 1997"]}, "panitumumab": {"origin": "Panitumumab was one of the first fully human antibodies made using transgenic mice. Scientists at the California company Abgenix engineered the XenoMouse, whose own antibody genes were switched off and replaced with human antibody genes. When these mice were immunized with EGFR, they made fully human anti-EGFR antibodies, avoiding the mouse protein that can trigger immune reactions. Amgen, which later acquired Abgenix, developed panitumumab. The FDA approved Vectibix in 2006 for EGFR-expressing metastatic colorectal cancer that progressed after chemotherapy. In 2009 the label was limited to tumors without KRAS mutations, and later RAS (KRAS and NRAS) testing became required.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Abgenix develops the XenoMouse, a mouse that makes human antibodies."], ["2006", "FDA approves Vectibix for metastatic colorectal cancer after chemotherapy."], ["2009", "Use is limited to KRAS wild-type colorectal cancer."], ["2014", "Approval for first-line RAS wild-type colorectal cancer with FOLFOX."]], "cell": "Panitumumab binds domain III of the EGFR extracellular region and competitively blocks EGF and TGF-alpha. Without ligand, EGFR cannot pair up and autophosphorylate, so the RAS-RAF-MEK-ERK and PI3K-AKT pathways stay off. Tumor cells arrest in G1, make less VEGF and undergo apoptosis, and the receptor is internalized and degraded. Unlike cetuximab, panitumumab is an IgG2, which binds Fc receptors on NK cells poorly, so its effect relies mainly on signal blockade rather than immune killing. KRAS or NRAS mutations activate the pathway downstream of EGFR, so the drug does not work in those tumors.", "body": [["Colorectal tumors", "RAS wild-type colon cancer cells lose a key growth signal and stop growing or shrink."], ["Skin", "Acne-like rash, dry skin, cracks, itching and nail fold infections occur in about 90 percent of patients because EGFR is needed for normal skin and follicle renewal."], ["Kidneys and electrolytes", "Blocking EGFR in the kidney tubule causes magnesium wasting, with low magnesium, calcium and potassium."], ["Eyes", "Dry eye, keratitis and eyelash changes come from EGFR's role in the cornea and lid."], ["Sun sensitivity", "Skin reactions worsen with sunlight, so sun protection helps."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease and pulmonary fibrosis are rare but serious."]], "journey": "Given by IV infusion every two weeks. As an IgG2 antibody it stays mainly in the blood and extracellular fluid. It is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids. At low levels, binding to EGFR and being pulled inside cells adds to clearance (target-mediated clearance). The half-life is about seven and a half days. It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes, so liver drug interactions are not expected.", "sidefx": "Skin toxicity, the boxed warning, is on target: EGFR controls normal skin cell maturation and hair follicle function, so blocking it causes inflammation, rash and cracking that can become infected or, rarely, lead to necrotizing fasciitis. Low magnesium comes from blocked tubular reabsorption. As a fully human IgG2, it causes fewer infusion reactions than cetuximab, though severe ones can occur. Adding it to bevacizumab plus chemotherapy increased toxicity and deaths, so that combination is avoided. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Vectibix (panitumumab) FDA label", "Jakobovits A et al. Nat Biotechnol 2007", "Amado RG et al. J Clin Oncol 2008"]}, "necitumumab": {"origin": "Necitumumab was developed by ImClone Systems, the company behind cetuximab, as a fully human IgG1 antibody against EGFR (code name IMC-11F8). After Eli Lilly acquired ImClone in 2008, Lilly tested it in lung cancer. The SQUIRE trial added necitumumab to gemcitabine and cisplatin for previously untreated metastatic squamous non-small cell lung cancer and showed a modest improvement in survival. A separate trial in non-squamous lung cancer was stopped early because of more clots and deaths. The FDA approved Portrazza in 2015 for squamous non-small cell lung cancer only, in combination with gemcitabine and cisplatin.", "timeline": [["2000s", "ImClone develops IMC-11F8, a fully human anti-EGFR IgG1."], ["2008", "Eli Lilly acquires ImClone."], ["2015", "SQUIRE trial results are published, and the FDA approves Portrazza for squamous NSCLC."]], "cell": "Necitumumab binds the ligand-binding domain III of EGFR, blocking EGF and TGF-alpha. The receptor cannot pair, autophosphorylate or turn on RAS-RAF-MEK-ERK and PI3K-AKT signaling, so tumor cells grow and divide less and make less VEGF. The antibody also causes the receptor to be internalized and degraded. As an IgG1 it can recruit NK cells to kill EGFR-expressing cells through antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity. Squamous lung cancers often express high levels of EGFR, which made them the target population.", "body": [["Squamous lung tumors", "EGFR-dependent cancer cells grow more slowly, producing a modest survival gain with chemotherapy."], ["Electrolytes", "Blocking EGFR in the kidney tubules causes magnesium wasting, often with low calcium and potassium."], ["Heart", "Cardiopulmonary arrest and sudden death occurred in about 3 percent of patients, possibly linked to electrolyte losses."], ["Blood vessels", "Venous clots, including pulmonary embolism, and arterial events such as stroke are more common."], ["Skin", "Acne-like rash, dry skin and nail fold infections come from EGFR's role in normal skin."]], "journey": "Given by IV infusion on days 1 and 8 of each three-week cycle, before gemcitabine and cisplatin. As an IgG1 antibody it stays in the blood and extracellular fluid. Clearance is through protein breakdown and binding to EGFR (target-mediated clearance). The half-life is about two weeks. It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes.", "sidefx": "Low magnesium is on target, from blocked EGFR-driven magnesium reabsorption in the kidney, and cisplatin adds to the loss. The boxed warning for cardiopulmonary arrest is thought to relate partly to these electrolyte disturbances, so electrolytes are checked and replaced. Clots may reflect effects on endothelium and the combination with cisplatin; it is not used in non-squamous lung cancer because of more clots and deaths there. Skin toxicity is an EGFR class effect. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Portrazza (necitumumab) FDA label", "Thatcher N et al. SQUIRE trial, Lancet Oncol 2015"]}, "amivantamab": {"origin": "Amivantamab is a bispecific antibody, meaning each molecule has two different arms. It was created with Genmab's DuoBody platform, which lets two half-antibodies with different targets swap pieces and join, and was developed by Janssen (Johnson & Johnson). One arm targets EGFR and the other targets MET, the receptor for hepatocyte growth factor. Some lung cancers have unusual EGFR mutations called exon 20 insertions that resist standard EGFR pills, and MET activation is a common way tumors escape EGFR drugs. The FDA granted accelerated approval to Rybrevant in 2021 for non-small cell lung cancer with EGFR exon 20 insertions after platinum chemotherapy, the first therapy approved for this group. In 2024 it was approved with the EGFR pill lazertinib as first-line therapy for common EGFR mutations.", "timeline": [["2010s", "Janssen and Genmab develop an EGFR-MET bispecific antibody with DuoBody technology."], ["2021", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Rybrevant for EGFR exon 20 insertion NSCLC."], ["2024", "Approvals for first-line exon 20 disease with chemotherapy and, with lazertinib, for common EGFR mutations."]], "cell": "One arm of amivantamab binds EGFR and the other binds MET. By blocking EGF from EGFR and hepatocyte growth factor from MET, it shuts down signaling through both receptors, which share downstream MAPK and PI3K/AKT pathways. Because it binds the outside of the receptor, it works regardless of which mutation is in the kinase domain inside, including exon 20 insertions. The bound receptors are pulled into the cell and degraded. Its Fc region has low fucose, which strengthens binding to Fc receptors on NK cells and macrophages and drives antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity and trogocytosis, in which immune cells nibble away receptor-bearing membrane from tumor cells.", "body": [["Lung tumors", "EGFR-mutant lung cancer cells lose both EGFR and MET survival signals and are attacked by immune cells."], ["Skin and nails", "Acne-like rash, dry skin and painful nail fold infections are very common because EGFR is needed for normal skin renewal."], ["Immune system", "Infusion reactions are very common with the first dose, so the first dose is split over two days."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease or pneumonitis can occur and may be fatal."], ["Veins", "With lazertinib, deep vein clots and pulmonary emboli are common, so preventive anticoagulation is recommended early in treatment."], ["Eyes, mouth and fluid", "Keratitis, mouth sores, swelling and low albumin occur, the last partly reflecting MET's role in the liver and blood vessels."]], "journey": "Given by IV infusion, starting weekly and then every two or three weeks. As an IgG1 antibody, it stays mainly in the blood and extracellular fluid. Clearance is through protein breakdown and through binding to EGFR and MET on cells (target-mediated clearance). The half-life is about 11 days. It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes, so drug interactions through liver enzymes are not expected.", "sidefx": "Skin and nail problems are on-target EGFR effects. Infusion reactions, mostly with the first dose, come from cytokine release and are reduced by premedication with an antihistamine, antipyretic and glucocorticoid and by splitting the first dose. Interstitial lung disease is an EGFR-class effect. The clot risk with lazertinib is not fully explained, which is why prophylactic anticoagulation is recommended for the first four months. Low albumin and swelling are linked to MET blockade. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Rybrevant (amivantamab-vmjw) FDA label", "Park K et al. CHRYSALIS, J Clin Oncol 2021", "Cho BC et al. MARIPOSA, N Engl J Med 2024"]}, "daratumumab": {"origin": "Daratumumab was made by the Danish biotech company Genmab using transgenic mice that produce fully human antibodies. Genmab chose CD38, a protein found at very high levels on myeloma plasma cells. In 2012 Genmab licensed it to Janssen, which ran the trials. Even as a single drug it shrank myeloma that had resisted proteasome inhibitors and immunomodulatory drugs, which was striking for an antibody. The FDA approved Darzalex in 2015 for heavily pretreated multiple myeloma, the first anti-CD38 antibody. It moved into combinations and first-line care, and in 2020 a subcutaneous form with hyaluronidase, Darzalex Faspro, shortened administration from hours to minutes.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Genmab develops daratumumab, a human anti-CD38 antibody."], ["2012", "Genmab licenses daratumumab to Janssen."], ["2015", "FDA approves Darzalex for multiple myeloma after at least three prior lines."], ["2020", "FDA approves Darzalex Faspro for subcutaneous use."]], "cell": "CD38 is a transmembrane glycoprotein that works as an enzyme on the cell surface, turning NAD into signaling molecules such as cyclic ADP-ribose, and also as an adhesion receptor. It is expressed at very high levels on myeloma plasma cells. Daratumumab binds CD38 and kills the cell in several ways: it activates complement (complement-dependent cytotoxicity), recruits NK cells (antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity), triggers macrophages to engulf the cell (phagocytosis) and, when cross-linked by Fc receptors, triggers apoptosis. It also removes CD38-positive immune suppressor cells, such as regulatory T cells, regulatory B cells and myeloid-derived suppressor cells, which allows T cells that recognize the tumor to expand.", "body": [["Myeloma cells", "Plasma cells are killed, so M-protein in the blood and urine falls over weeks."], ["Immune system", "Removing suppressor cells boosts T-cell activity, while NK cells, which also express CD38, are reduced."], ["Airways", "Infusion reactions often affect the airways, with nasal congestion, throat irritation, cough and bronchospasm, especially with the first dose."], ["Blood bank testing", "It binds CD38 on reagent red cells, making antibody screens and crossmatches falsely positive for months."], ["Lab tests for myeloma", "As an IgG kappa antibody, it can show up on protein electrophoresis and immunofixation and mimic residual myeloma."], ["Bone marrow and infections", "Neutropenia, thrombocytopenia and infections such as pneumonia occur, especially with other myeloma drugs."]], "journey": "Given IV or subcutaneously with hyaluronidase, which loosens tissue so a larger volume can be absorbed under the skin. Schedules start weekly and then spread out to every two to four weeks. As an IgG antibody, it is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids, not by the liver or kidneys. Clearance is faster when many CD38-positive cells are present and slows as the tumor shrinks. With weekly dosing the half-life is about 18 days.", "sidefx": "Infusion reactions come from cytokine release as CD38-bearing cells are attacked, and they are reduced by premedication with a corticosteroid, antihistamine and acetaminophen; patients with COPD may get bronchodilators afterward. Blood bank interference occurs because red cells carry small amounts of CD38; labs can treat test cells with dithiothreitol (DTT) to remove it, so a type and screen is done before the first dose. Neutropenia, infections and hepatitis B reactivation reflect immune effects. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Because daratumumab makes red cell antibody screens falsely positive, blood banks are told before treatment starts so they can treat test cells to work around it.", "src": ["Darzalex (daratumumab) FDA label", "Darzalex Faspro FDA label", "Lokhorst HM et al. N Engl J Med 2015"]}, "isatuximab": {"origin": "Isatuximab was discovered by the US company ImmunoGen and developed by Sanofi as a second anti-CD38 antibody for multiple myeloma. It binds a different spot on CD38 than daratumumab, one that also blocks CD38's enzyme activity and lets it trigger cell death directly. Sanofi tested it with pomalidomide and dexamethasone in the ICARIA-MM trial, which improved progression-free survival in relapsed myeloma. The FDA approved Sarclisa in 2020 for that combination. Later approvals added combination with carfilzomib and dexamethasone (2021) and with bortezomib, lenalidomide and dexamethasone for newly diagnosed patients not eligible for transplant (2024).", "timeline": [["2000s", "ImmunoGen discovers the anti-CD38 antibody later named isatuximab, developed by Sanofi."], ["2020", "FDA approves Sarclisa with pomalidomide and dexamethasone for relapsed myeloma."], ["2021", "Approval with carfilzomib and dexamethasone."], ["2024", "Approval with VRd for newly diagnosed transplant-ineligible myeloma."]], "cell": "Isatuximab is a chimeric IgG1 that binds a specific epitope on CD38, a surface enzyme highly expressed on myeloma plasma cells. Binding at this site inhibits CD38's ADP-ribosyl cyclase activity. It kills myeloma cells through antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity by NK cells, antibody-dependent phagocytosis by macrophages and complement-dependent cytotoxicity, and unlike daratumumab it can trigger apoptosis directly without needing cross-linking by Fc receptors. It also activates NK cells and removes CD38-positive regulatory T cells, which helps the immune system attack the tumor. Pomalidomide further stimulates NK and T cells, so the two work together.", "body": [["Myeloma cells", "Plasma cells are killed and M-protein declines over weeks."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia is common, especially with pomalidomide, raising infection risk."], ["Immune system", "Infusion reactions, mostly with the first dose, cause shortness of breath, cough and chills."], ["Lungs and airways", "Upper respiratory infections and pneumonia are common in treated patients."], ["Lab tests", "It interferes with red cell antibody screening and can mimic M-protein on electrophoresis and immunofixation."], ["Second cancers", "Skin cancers and other second primary malignancies were seen more often in trials."]], "journey": "Given by IV infusion, weekly at first and then every two weeks. As an IgG antibody, it stays in the blood and is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids. Clearance includes binding to CD38 on tumor cells, so it falls as the tumor shrinks, and the half-life lengthens over time, with a median terminal half-life of about four weeks reported. It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes.", "sidefx": "Infusion reactions reflect cytokine release as CD38-bearing cells are attacked and are reduced by premedication with dexamethasone, acetaminophen, an H2 blocker and diphenhydramine. Neutropenia and infections come from immune and marrow effects. Interference with blood bank testing comes from CD38 on red cells, so a type and screen is done before the first dose. The excess of second cancers in trials is not fully explained. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Sarclisa (isatuximab-irfc) FDA label", "Attal M et al. ICARIA-MM, Lancet 2019"]}, "elotuzumab": {"origin": "Elotuzumab targets SLAMF7 (also called CS1), a surface protein that researchers found on nearly all myeloma cells and on natural killer cells. The antibody (originally HuLuc63) was developed by PDL BioPharma, then by Bristol-Myers Squibb in partnership with AbbVie. On its own it did little against myeloma, but with lenalidomide, which boosts NK cells, it worked well. The ELOQUENT-2 trial showed better progression-free survival when elotuzumab was added to lenalidomide and dexamethasone, and the FDA approved Empliciti in 2015. It was the first immunostimulatory antibody approved for myeloma. A combination with pomalidomide and dexamethasone was approved in 2018.", "timeline": [["2000s", "PDL BioPharma develops HuLuc63, an anti-CS1 (SLAMF7) antibody."], ["2015", "FDA approves Empliciti with lenalidomide and dexamethasone for relapsed myeloma."], ["2018", "Approval with pomalidomide and dexamethasone."]], "cell": "SLAMF7 is a signaling receptor on myeloma cells and NK cells. Elotuzumab works in two ways. First, it binds SLAMF7 on NK cells and directly activates them through the adaptor protein EAT-2. Second, it coats myeloma cells, and its Fc tail engages CD16 on NK cells, triggering antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity. On myeloma cells, which lack EAT-2, SLAMF7 binding does not activate them. Lenalidomide and pomalidomide increase NK cell numbers and activity, which is why elotuzumab works with them but has little effect alone.", "body": [["Myeloma cells", "Activated NK cells kill plasma cells, and M-protein falls over weeks."], ["Immune system", "NK cell activation and immune changes increase infections such as pneumonia and herpes zoster."], ["Infusion reactions", "Fever, chills and blood pressure changes can occur, mostly early, and are reduced by premedication."], ["Lab tests", "As an IgG kappa antibody, it can appear on serum protein electrophoresis and immunofixation and mimic myeloma protein."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue, diarrhea, cough and constipation are common, partly from the combined drugs."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme elevations can occur, so liver tests are checked periodically."]], "journey": "Given by IV infusion, weekly at first and then every two to four weeks. As an IgG antibody, it stays in the blood and is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids. Clearance is partly through binding to SLAMF7 on cells, so it is nonlinear: clearance falls at higher exposure. The half-life is long at labeled doses. It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes.", "sidefx": "Infusion reactions come from immune activation and are reduced by premedication with dexamethasone, an H1 and H2 blocker and acetaminophen. Infections reflect immune effects plus the combined myeloma drugs and steroids. Second primary malignancies were reported more often in trials. Lab interference comes from the antibody itself showing up as an IgG kappa band. Embryo-fetal toxicity warnings come from its partners lenalidomide and pomalidomide. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Empliciti (elotuzumab) FDA label", "Lonial S et al. ELOQUENT-2, N Engl J Med 2015"]}, "alemtuzumab": {"origin": "Alemtuzumab was born in the pathology department at the University of Cambridge, which gave it its original name, Campath (Cambridge Pathology). In the early 1980s Herman Waldmann and colleagues made rat antibodies against lymphocytes, aiming to remove T cells from donor marrow before transplants. One, Campath-1, bound a target later named CD52. In 1988 Lutz Riechmann, Greg Winter and Waldmann humanized it by grafting the rat binding loops onto a human antibody, making Campath-1H one of the first humanized antibodies. The FDA approved Campath in 2001 for B-cell chronic lymphocytic leukemia. In 2012 it was withdrawn from the commercial market for CLL (it remains available through a distribution program) and repurposed at a different schedule as Lemtrada, approved in 2014 for relapsing multiple sclerosis.", "timeline": [["1983", "Herman Waldmann's Cambridge group develops the Campath-1 rat antibodies."], ["1988", "Riechmann, Winter and Waldmann report humanized Campath-1H."], ["2001", "FDA approves Campath for B-cell CLL."], ["2012", "Campath is withdrawn from commercial sale and offered through a distribution program."], ["2014", "FDA approves Lemtrada for relapsing multiple sclerosis."]], "cell": "CD52 is a small protein anchored to the cell surface by a GPI anchor. It is found on mature B and T lymphocytes, NK cells, monocytes and macrophages, but not on blood stem cells. Alemtuzumab binds CD52 and destroys these cells by recruiting NK cells and macrophages (antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity) and by activating complement (complement-dependent cytotoxicity). This causes rapid, profound lymphocyte depletion. In CLL it clears malignant B cells. In MS, the immune system rebuilds over months from stem cells, and the new repertoire seems less prone to attack myelin. B cells return within months, but CD4 T cells take a year or more, which shapes both benefit and risk.", "body": [["Lymphocytes", "B and T cells fall to very low levels within days and recover slowly, CD4 cells taking more than a year."], ["Immune defense", "Severe immune suppression leads to opportunistic infections such as CMV, Pneumocystis pneumonia, herpes viruses and Listeria."], ["Infusion reactions", "Massive cytokine release during early infusions causes fever, rigors, rash, low blood pressure and breathing trouble."], ["Blood cells", "Pancytopenia, autoimmune hemolytic anemia and immune thrombocytopenia can occur."], ["Thyroid and kidneys (MS use)", "As the immune system rebuilds, autoimmune thyroid disease is common and anti-GBM kidney disease can occur."], ["Brain and vessels (MS use)", "Stroke and arterial dissection have been reported within days of infusion."]], "journey": "Given by IV infusion. It binds quickly to CD52-bearing cells, and early on many target cells clear it fast, so the half-life after the first dose is only about 11 hours. As lymphocytes disappear, clearance slows and the half-life rises to about six days with repeated dosing. It is broken down like other antibodies into peptides and amino acids, not by liver enzymes or the kidneys. Its effect on lymphocytes lasts months to years, far longer than the drug itself.", "sidefx": "Infusion reactions come from cytokine release as large numbers of lymphocytes are destroyed at once; premedication with steroids, antihistamines and acetaminophen reduces them. Opportunistic infections follow profound T-cell depletion, so antiviral and Pneumocystis prophylaxis and CMV monitoring are used. Autoimmune diseases after MS treatment are thought to come from unbalanced immune reconstitution, in which B cells return well before regulatory T cells. Cytopenias include both marrow effects and autoimmunity. Lemtrada is available only through a REMS program. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The name Campath comes from Cambridge Pathology, the university department where the antibody was first made.", "src": ["Campath (alemtuzumab) FDA label", "Lemtrada (alemtuzumab) FDA label", "Riechmann L, Clark M, Waldmann H, Winter G. Nature 1988"]}, "dinutuximab": {"origin": "Dinutuximab targets GD2, a sugar-containing fat (ganglioside) that coats neuroblastoma cells, a childhood cancer of the developing nervous system. In the 1980s Ralph Reisfeld and colleagues at the Scripps Research Institute in La Jolla made a mouse antibody against GD2, then a mouse-human chimeric version called ch14.18. For years the National Cancer Institute made the antibody itself to supply trials. In a Children's Oncology Group trial published in 2010, adding ch14.18 with GM-CSF and IL-2 to isotretinoin after intensive therapy improved survival in high-risk neuroblastoma. United Therapeutics took over manufacturing, and the FDA approved Unituxin in 2015, the first antibody approved for neuroblastoma.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Ralph Reisfeld's Scripps group develops anti-GD2 antibodies, leading to chimeric ch14.18."], ["2010", "Children's Oncology Group trial shows improved survival with ch14.18 immunotherapy."], ["2015", "FDA approves Unituxin for high-risk neuroblastoma."]], "cell": "GD2 is a disialoganglioside that sits in the outer membrane of neuroblastoma cells and other neuroectodermal tumors, at much higher levels than on most normal tissues. Dinutuximab binds GD2 and marks the cell for destruction. Its IgG1 Fc tail recruits neutrophils, macrophages and NK cells to kill the cell by antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity, and it activates complement (complement-dependent cytotoxicity). GM-CSF boosts neutrophils and macrophages, and IL-2 activates NK cells, which is why it is given with these cytokines. Normal peripheral pain nerve fibers also carry GD2, so the antibody activates complement on these nerves and causes severe pain.", "body": [["Neuroblastoma cells", "Remaining tumor cells after intensive therapy are cleared by immune cells, lowering the chance of relapse."], ["Pain nerves", "Binding to GD2 on sensory nerve fibers triggers complement and causes severe neuropathic pain in the abdomen, limbs and back during infusions."], ["Blood vessels", "Cytokine release causes capillary leak, with swelling, low blood pressure and fluid shifts."], ["Eyes and nerves", "Blurred vision, light sensitivity, dilated pupils and, rarely, transverse myelitis can occur."], ["Electrolytes", "Low potassium, sodium and calcium are common, partly from capillary leak and fluid shifts."], ["Immune system", "Serious infusion reactions, including anaphylaxis, can occur."]], "journey": "Given by IV infusion over several hours on consecutive days in each cycle, with GM-CSF or IL-2 and followed by oral isotretinoin. As an IgG antibody, it stays mainly in the blood and extracellular fluid. It is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids and is not cleared by the liver or kidneys. The half-life is about 10 days. Immune killing happens over days as effector cells are recruited.", "sidefx": "Neuropathic pain, part of the boxed warning, is on target: GD2 sits on pain fibers, and antibody plus complement activates them. Strong opioids and other pain medicines are given before and during infusions. Infusion reactions and capillary leak come from immune activation and cytokines, made stronger by IL-2. Neurologic eye effects and rare transverse myelitis or posterior encephalopathy (RPLS) reflect nerve and vessel effects. Electrolyte losses follow fluid shifts. There is no antidote.", "fun": "For years before approval, the National Cancer Institute itself manufactured the ch14.18 antibody so children in trials could receive it.", "src": ["Unituxin (dinutuximab) FDA label", "Yu AL et al. N Engl J Med 2010"]}, "naxitamab": {"origin": "Naxitamab grew from decades of work by Nai-Kong Cheung at Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center in New York. In the 1980s his group made a mouse antibody against GD2 called 3F8 and treated children with neuroblastoma with it. Later they humanized the antibody, making hu3F8, to lower immune reactions against mouse protein and allow repeated courses. Y-mAbs Therapeutics licensed it and developed it as naxitamab. The FDA granted accelerated approval to Danyelza in 2020, with GM-CSF, for children 1 year and older and adults with relapsed or refractory high-risk neuroblastoma in the bone or bone marrow that has partly responded or stabilized with earlier therapy.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Nai-Kong Cheung's group at Memorial Sloan Kettering develops the anti-GD2 mouse antibody 3F8."], ["2010s", "The antibody is humanized as hu3F8 and licensed to Y-mAbs Therapeutics."], ["2020", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Danyelza for relapsed or refractory high-risk neuroblastoma in bone or marrow."]], "cell": "Naxitamab is a humanized IgG1 that binds GD2, a disialoganglioside highly expressed on neuroblastoma and other neuroectodermal tumors. Once bound, its Fc tail recruits granulocytes, macrophages and NK cells to kill the tumor cell through antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity and activates complement (complement-dependent cytotoxicity). GM-CSF, given alongside, increases the number and activity of granulocytes and macrophages, strengthening this killing. GD2 is also on peripheral pain fibers, so the antibody causes intense neuropathic pain during infusions. It is not cell-cycle specific.", "body": [["Neuroblastoma in bone and marrow", "Tumor cells coated with antibody are destroyed by immune cells, producing responses in disease that resisted other treatments."], ["Pain nerves", "Binding to GD2 on sensory fibers activates complement and causes severe pain during infusions, often in the abdomen, bones and limbs."], ["Airways and immune system", "Serious infusion reactions such as bronchospasm, stridor, hives and low blood pressure are common."], ["Blood pressure", "High blood pressure and fast heart rate can occur during and soon after infusions."], ["Nervous system", "Rare transverse myelitis, posterior encephalopathy (RPLS), urinary retention and pupil changes can occur."]], "journey": "Given as an IV infusion over about half an hour to an hour on days 1, 3 and 5 of each cycle, with GM-CSF injections under the skin before and during the cycle. It is usually given in an outpatient setting with premedication. As an IgG antibody, it is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids, not by the liver or kidneys. The half-life is about 8 days.", "sidefx": "Severe neuropathic pain is on target, from GD2 on sensory nerve fibers, so patients receive opioids and other pain medicines around each infusion. Serious infusion reactions come from immune activation and cytokine release, and premedication with antihistamines, corticosteroids and acetaminophen reduces them. Hypertension is common and is checked during and after infusions. Transverse myelitis and RPLS reflect nerve and vessel effects. Both neurotoxicity and infusion reactions are in the boxed warning. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Danyelza (naxitamab-gqgk) FDA label", "Cheung NK et al. J Clin Oncol 1987"]}, "mogamulizumab": {"origin": "Mogamulizumab came from the Japanese company Kyowa Kirin (formerly Kyowa Hakko Kirin). It targets CCR4, a chemokine receptor found on malignant T cells in adult T-cell leukemia-lymphoma and cutaneous T-cell lymphomas. Kyowa's POTELLIGENT technology produces antibodies in cells that lack the enzyme that adds fucose to the antibody's sugar chain, which makes immune cells kill antibody-coated targets much more strongly. Mogamulizumab was first approved in Japan in 2012 for adult T-cell leukemia-lymphoma. Based on the MAVORIC trial, which compared it with vorinostat, the FDA approved Poteligeo in 2018 for adults with relapsed or refractory mycosis fungoides or Sezary syndrome after at least one prior systemic therapy.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Kyowa Hakko Kirin develops POTELLIGENT defucosylated antibody technology."], ["2012", "Mogamulizumab is approved in Japan for adult T-cell leukemia-lymphoma."], ["2018", "FDA approves Poteligeo for mycosis fungoides and Sezary syndrome."]], "cell": "CCR4 is a receptor for the chemokines CCL17 and CCL22 that guides T cells into the skin. Malignant T cells in mycosis fungoides and Sezary syndrome express high levels of it. Mogamulizumab binds CCR4 and marks these cells for destruction. Because its Fc region lacks fucose, it binds FcgammaRIIIa (CD16A) on NK cells far more tightly, strongly enhancing antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity. Regulatory T cells also carry CCR4, so they are depleted too. This loss of immune brakes can boost anti-tumor immunity but also drives many of its autoimmune side effects.", "body": [["Malignant T cells in blood and skin", "Cancerous T cells are killed by NK cells, often clearing blood involvement in Sezary syndrome before skin lesions improve."], ["Skin", "Drug eruptions are common and can look like disease progression; severe reactions such as SJS and TEN can occur."], ["Immune balance", "Depleting regulatory T cells can cause autoimmune problems such as myositis, myocarditis, hepatitis, pneumonitis and thyroiditis."], ["Infusion reactions", "Fever, chills and nausea occur mostly with the first infusion."], ["Transplant", "Patients who later receive an allogeneic stem cell transplant have more severe graft-versus-host disease."]], "journey": "Given by IV infusion, weekly in the first cycle and then every two weeks. As an IgG antibody, it stays mainly in the blood and extracellular fluid and is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids rather than by liver enzymes or the kidneys. The half-life is about 17 days. Because regulatory T cells take time to recover, its immune effects outlast the drug.", "sidefx": "Most serious effects trace back to loss of CCR4-positive regulatory T cells, which normally keep the immune system from attacking the body. That explains the drug rashes, autoimmune organ inflammation and worse graft-versus-host disease after transplant. Because a mogamulizumab rash can mimic lymphoma, a skin biopsy is often used to tell them apart. Infusion reactions are reduced by premedication with diphenhydramine and acetaminophen. Infections follow immune changes. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Poteligeo (mogamulizumab-kpkc) FDA label", "Kim YH et al. MAVORIC, Lancet Oncol 2018"]}, "tafasitamab": {"origin": "Tafasitamab began at Xencor, a California company that engineers antibody Fc regions. Xencor designed an anti-CD19 antibody (XmAb5574) with two amino acid changes in its Fc tail that make it bind immune cell Fc receptors much more strongly. MorphoSys, a German company, licensed it in 2010 and developed it as MOR208, later partnering with Incyte. In the single-arm L-MIND trial, tafasitamab plus lenalidomide produced durable responses in relapsed or refractory diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) in people who could not have a stem cell transplant. The FDA granted accelerated approval to Monjuvi in 2020 for that use. It later gained approval for relapsed or refractory follicular lymphoma with lenalidomide and rituximab.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Xencor engineers XmAb5574, an Fc-enhanced anti-CD19 antibody."], ["2010", "MorphoSys licenses the antibody and develops it as MOR208."], ["2020", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Monjuvi with lenalidomide for relapsed or refractory DLBCL."], ["2025", "Approval for relapsed or refractory follicular lymphoma with lenalidomide and rituximab."]], "cell": "CD19 is a B-cell surface protein present from early B-cell development through mature B cells, and it remains on most B-cell lymphomas, including some that have lost CD20. Tafasitamab binds CD19. Its Fc region carries two amino acid substitutions that increase binding to Fc gamma receptors, especially FcgammaRIIIa on NK cells and receptors on macrophages. This strengthens antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity and antibody-dependent cellular phagocytosis, and binding can also trigger some direct apoptosis. Lenalidomide boosts NK cell numbers and activity and has its own anti-lymphoma effects, so the two combine well.", "body": [["Lymphoma cells", "CD19-positive DLBCL and follicular lymphoma cells are killed by NK cells and macrophages, shrinking tumors."], ["Normal B cells", "Healthy B cells are depleted too, lowering antibody production and raising infection risk."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia, thrombocytopenia and anemia are common, largely with lenalidomide."], ["Infusion reactions", "Fever, chills and rash can occur, mostly with the first infusions."], ["Lungs and infections", "Respiratory infections and pneumonia are common serious effects."]], "journey": "Given by IV infusion, more often in the first cycles and then every two weeks. As an IgG antibody, it stays in the blood and extracellular fluid and is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids, not by liver enzymes or the kidneys. The half-life is about 17 days. CYP drug interactions are not expected; lenalidomide is cleared separately by the kidneys.", "sidefx": "Neutropenia and thrombocytopenia reflect combined effects of B-cell depletion, immune activation and lenalidomide's marrow suppression. Infections follow low neutrophils and loss of normal B cells. Infusion reactions come from cytokine release and are reduced with premedication for the first infusions. Lenalidomide adds its own boxed warnings for fetal harm, blood count drops and clots. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Monjuvi (tafasitamab-cxix) FDA label", "Salles G et al. L-MIND, Lancet Oncol 2020", "Horton HM et al. Cancer Res 2008"]}, "zolbetuximab": {"origin": "Zolbetuximab was created at Ganymed Pharmaceuticals in Mainz, Germany, a company cofounded by Ugur Sahin and Ozlem Tureci, who later founded BioNTech. Their team searched for proteins that are normally hidden in healthy tissue but exposed on cancer cells. They found claudin 18.2, a tight-junction protein in the stomach lining that becomes accessible on many gastric cancer cells. Ganymed developed the antibody IMAB362, and Astellas acquired the company in 2016. Two phase 3 trials, SPOTLIGHT and GLOW, showed longer survival when zolbetuximab was added to chemotherapy. The FDA approved Vyloy in 2024 for first-line treatment of HER2-negative, CLDN18.2-positive gastric or gastroesophageal junction adenocarcinoma, the first claudin-targeted drug.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Ganymed Pharmaceuticals identifies claudin 18.2 as a cancer target and develops IMAB362."], ["2016", "Astellas acquires Ganymed."], ["2023", "SPOTLIGHT and GLOW phase 3 results are published."], ["2024", "FDA approves Vyloy, the first claudin 18.2-directed therapy."]], "cell": "Claudins are proteins that seal the tight junctions between epithelial cells. In the normal stomach, claudin 18.2 is tucked into these junctions and is hard for antibodies to reach. When stomach cells become cancerous, cell polarity and junctions are disrupted, so claudin 18.2 becomes exposed on the cell surface. Zolbetuximab, a chimeric IgG1, binds it and triggers antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity by NK cells and complement-dependent cytotoxicity. Chemotherapy appears to increase claudin 18.2 exposure and immune cell recruitment, adding to the effect. Tumors are tested with an FDA-approved immunohistochemistry test to confirm claudin 18.2 positivity.", "body": [["Gastric and GEJ tumors", "Claudin 18.2-positive cancer cells are attacked by immune cells and complement, improving survival with chemotherapy."], ["Stomach lining", "Binding to claudin 18.2 in normal gastric mucosa causes severe nausea and vomiting, often during the first infusion."], ["Fluid and electrolytes", "Repeated vomiting can cause dehydration and low potassium."], ["Appetite and weight", "Poor appetite and low albumin are common."], ["Immune system", "Hypersensitivity and infusion reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, can occur."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia occurs, largely from the chemotherapy partner."]], "journey": "Given by IV infusion every two or three weeks, matched to the chemotherapy schedule. As an IgG antibody, it stays mainly in the blood and extracellular fluid and is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids, not by the liver or kidneys. The half-life is long, measured in weeks. Nausea is worst at the start of the first infusion, so infusion rates can be slowed.", "sidefx": "Nausea and vomiting are on target: the antibody reaches claudin 18.2 in the normal stomach lining and causes local injury and inflammation, especially early in treatment. Antiemetic premedication with combinations such as an NK1 and a 5-HT3 antagonist is used, and the infusion rate is slowed or interrupted when vomiting occurs. Dehydration and low potassium follow vomiting. Hypersensitivity comes from immune reactions to the antibody. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Zolbetuximab's inventors, Ugur Sahin and Ozlem Tureci, later led BioNTech, the company that developed one of the first mRNA COVID-19 vaccines.", "src": ["Vyloy (zolbetuximab-clzb) FDA label", "Shitara K et al. SPOTLIGHT, Lancet 2023", "Shah MA et al. GLOW, Nat Med 2023"]}, "nivolumab": {"origin": "Nivolumab rests on a discovery made in Kyoto. In 1992 Tasuku Honjo's lab at Kyoto University identified PD-1 (programmed death-1), a receptor on T cells, and later showed it acts as a brake on immune responses. Tumors exploit this brake by displaying its ligand, PD-L1. The Japanese company Ono Pharmaceutical and the US company Medarex developed a fully human anti-PD-1 antibody, and Bristol-Myers Squibb acquired Medarex in 2009. Nivolumab was approved in Japan in July 2014 and by the FDA as Opdivo in December 2014 for advanced melanoma. It went on to approvals in many cancers, including lung, kidney, head and neck, Hodgkin lymphoma and others. Honjo shared the 2018 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine with James Allison for checkpoint discoveries.", "timeline": [["1992", "Tasuku Honjo's lab identifies PD-1."], ["2009", "Bristol-Myers Squibb acquires Medarex, co-developer of nivolumab with Ono."], ["2014", "Nivolumab is approved in Japan in July and by the FDA in December for melanoma."], ["2018", "Honjo and James Allison share the Nobel Prize for immune checkpoint discoveries."], ["2024", "FDA approves a subcutaneous nivolumab with hyaluronidase (Opdivo Qvantig)."]], "cell": "PD-1 is an inhibitory receptor that appears on T cells after they are activated, especially during long-lasting stimulation such as chronic infection or cancer. When PD-1 binds PD-L1 or PD-L2 on tumor or immune cells, it recruits the phosphatase SHP-2, which removes phosphate groups from T-cell receptor and CD28 signaling molecules. T cells then stop dividing, make less cytokine and become exhausted. Nivolumab, a fully human IgG4 antibody, binds PD-1 and blocks both ligands. This releases the brake mainly in the effector phase, inside tumors and peripheral tissues, so tumor-specific T cells recover function and kill cancer cells. The IgG4 format has little ability to recruit Fc-driven killing, so it does not deplete the T cells it binds.", "body": [["Tumors", "Reactivated T cells attack cancer cells, which can produce durable responses, sometimes after initial swelling of tumors from immune cell infiltration."], ["Lungs", "T-cell attack on lung tissue can cause pneumonitis with cough and shortness of breath."], ["Gut and liver", "Immune-mediated colitis causes diarrhea, and hepatitis causes liver enzyme rises."], ["Endocrine glands", "The thyroid, pituitary, adrenal glands and pancreas can be attacked, causing hypothyroidism, hypophysitis, adrenal insufficiency or type 1 diabetes that may be permanent."], ["Skin", "Rash, itching and vitiligo are common; severe reactions such as SJS and TEN are rare."], ["Heart, kidneys and nerves", "Myocarditis, nephritis and encephalitis are uncommon but can be serious."]], "journey": "Given by IV infusion every two to four weeks, or subcutaneously with hyaluronidase. As an IgG4 antibody, it stays mainly in the blood and extracellular fluid and is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids, not by liver enzymes or the kidneys. The neonatal Fc receptor recycles it, giving a half-life of about 25 days. Its blockade of PD-1 and its immune effects can last months after the last dose.", "sidefx": "Side effects are immune-mediated: releasing the PD-1 brake lets T cells attack normal tissues as well as tumors. Any organ can be affected, most often skin, gut, liver, lungs and endocrine glands. Most inflammatory effects are treated by holding the drug and giving corticosteroids, with other immune suppressants such as infliximab for resistant colitis. Endocrine damage often needs lifelong hormone replacement rather than steroids. Myocarditis is rare but can be fatal, especially in combination with CTLA-4 blockade. There is no reversal agent.", "fun": "Honjo's lab originally named PD-1 for programmed death because they thought it was involved in cell death; its real job as an immune brake was worked out later.", "src": ["Opdivo (nivolumab) FDA label", "Ishida Y, Agata Y, Shibahara K, Honjo T. EMBO J 1992", "Robert C et al. N Engl J Med 2015"]}, "nivolumab-relatlimab": {"origin": "This product joins two immune checkpoint blockers in one vial. Nivolumab blocks PD-1, discovered by Tasuku Honjo's Kyoto lab in 1992. Relatlimab blocks LAG-3 (lymphocyte activation gene 3), a second T-cell brake identified in 1990 by Frederic Triebel and colleagues in France. Bristol-Myers Squibb developed relatlimab and tested the fixed combination in the RELATIVITY-047 trial in untreated advanced melanoma, where it roughly doubled progression-free survival compared with nivolumab alone. The FDA approved Opdualag in 2022 for unresectable or metastatic melanoma in adults and children 12 years and older. It was the first LAG-3 blocking drug and the third distinct checkpoint pathway (after CTLA-4 and PD-1/PD-L1) to be targeted.", "timeline": [["1990", "Frederic Triebel's group identifies LAG-3."], ["1992", "Tasuku Honjo's lab identifies PD-1."], ["2014", "Nivolumab is first approved for melanoma."], ["2022", "FDA approves Opdualag, the first LAG-3 blocking antibody combination."]], "cell": "Exhausted T cells inside tumors often carry several inhibitory receptors at once. PD-1, when bound by PD-L1 or PD-L2, recruits SHP-2 and shuts down T-cell receptor and CD28 signaling. LAG-3 is a CD4-like receptor that binds MHC class II, and also ligands such as FGL1, and dampens T-cell proliferation and cytokine production through its own intracellular motif. The two pathways act in complementary ways. Nivolumab (human IgG4) blocks PD-1, and relatlimab (human IgG4) blocks LAG-3 from binding its ligands. Releasing both brakes restores the function of exhausted CD8 and CD4 T cells more than PD-1 blockade alone, increasing tumor killing.", "body": [["Melanoma", "Reinvigorated T cells attack melanoma, doubling the time before progression in trials compared with nivolumab alone."], ["Heart", "Myocarditis is more frequent than with nivolumab alone, so troponin is often checked."], ["Endocrine glands", "Hypothyroidism, adrenal insufficiency and hypophysitis can occur."], ["Gut and liver", "Colitis causes diarrhea, and hepatitis raises liver enzymes."], ["Lungs and kidneys", "Pneumonitis and nephritis can develop when T cells infiltrate lung and kidney tissue."], ["Skin and joints", "Rash, itching and musculoskeletal pain are common."]], "journey": "Given as a single IV infusion every four weeks containing both antibodies in a fixed ratio. Both are IgG4 antibodies that stay mainly in the blood and extracellular fluid. They are broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids, not by the liver or kidneys. Half-lives are similar, about 26 days for nivolumab and 27 days for relatlimab. Immune effects persist for months.", "sidefx": "All side effects are immune-mediated: releasing two T-cell brakes lets T cells attack normal tissue. Rates of serious immune effects were higher than with nivolumab alone but lower than with nivolumab plus ipilimumab in trials. Myocarditis is a key concern. Treatment involves holding the drug and giving corticosteroids, with other immune suppressants for steroid-resistant cases. Endocrine gland damage usually needs lifelong hormone replacement. There is no reversal agent.", "src": ["Opdualag (nivolumab and relatlimab-rmbw) FDA label", "Tawbi HA et al. RELATIVITY-047, N Engl J Med 2022", "Triebel F et al. J Exp Med 1990"]}, "ipilimumab": {"origin": "Ipilimumab was the first immune checkpoint inhibitor. CTLA-4 was identified in 1987 by Pierre Golstein's group in Marseille, France, and was later shown to be a brake on T-cell activation. In 1996 James Allison's lab at the University of California, Berkeley showed that an antibody blocking CTLA-4 made tumors in mice shrink, introducing the idea of releasing immune brakes to fight cancer. Medarex developed a fully human anti-CTLA-4 antibody (MDX-010), and Bristol-Myers Squibb took it through phase 3 trials. In 2010 it became the first drug ever shown to improve overall survival in metastatic melanoma, and the FDA approved Yervoy in 2011. Allison shared the 2018 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine with Tasuku Honjo.", "timeline": [["1987", "CTLA-4 is identified by Pierre Golstein's group."], ["1996", "James Allison's lab shows CTLA-4 blockade causes tumor rejection in mice."], ["2010", "Phase 3 trial shows improved survival in metastatic melanoma."], ["2011", "FDA approves Yervoy, the first immune checkpoint inhibitor."], ["2015", "FDA approves the nivolumab plus ipilimumab combination for melanoma."], ["2018", "Allison and Honjo share the Nobel Prize for checkpoint discoveries."]], "cell": "T cells need two signals to activate: the antigen signal through the T-cell receptor and a co-stimulatory signal when CD28 binds B7 molecules (CD80 and CD86) on antigen-presenting cells. Soon after activation, T cells put CTLA-4 on their surface. CTLA-4 binds CD80 and CD86 much more tightly than CD28 does, outcompeting it and pulling B7 off antigen-presenting cells, which shuts activation down. Regulatory T cells also use CTLA-4 constantly. Ipilimumab, a fully human IgG1, binds CTLA-4 and blocks it. This happens mainly in lymph nodes during the priming phase, so more T cells are activated, more diverse T-cell clones expand and travel to tumors. Its IgG1 tail may also deplete CTLA-4-rich regulatory T cells inside tumors.", "body": [["Tumors", "A larger and broader army of T cells attacks melanoma and other cancers, with some patients having very long-lasting responses."], ["Colon", "Enterocolitis with diarrhea is the classic early serious effect and can lead to perforation."], ["Pituitary", "Hypophysitis is more common than with PD-1 drugs, causing headache, fatigue and low levels of pituitary hormones."], ["Liver", "Immune hepatitis from T cells attacking liver cells raises liver enzymes."], ["Skin", "Rash, itching and vitiligo are common as T cells react to skin and pigment cells."], ["Other organs", "Thyroid, adrenal, nerve, eye, kidney, lung and heart inflammation can occur."]], "journey": "Given by IV infusion, usually every three weeks for a limited number of doses, alone or with nivolumab. As an IgG1 antibody, it stays mainly in the blood and extracellular fluid and is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids, not by liver enzymes or the kidneys. The half-life is about 15 days. Immune effects begin within weeks and can continue for months after the last dose.", "sidefx": "Side effects are immune-mediated. Because CTLA-4 blockade broadens T-cell priming in lymph nodes, it unleashes more widespread autoimmunity than PD-1 blockade, and serious effects are more frequent and rise with dose. Colitis, hepatitis, skin reactions and hypophysitis are most typical. Treatment is to hold the drug and give corticosteroids, adding drugs such as infliximab for steroid-resistant colitis. Hormone deficits often need lifelong replacement. There is no reversal agent.", "fun": "James Allison played harmonica in a blues band made up of immunologists called the Checkpoints.", "src": ["Yervoy (ipilimumab) FDA label", "Leach DR, Krummel MF, Allison JP. Science 1996", "Hodi FS et al. N Engl J Med 2010"]}, "tremelimumab": {"origin": "Tremelimumab is a fully human anti-CTLA-4 antibody originally developed by Pfizer (code name CP-675,206), building on James Allison's 1996 finding that blocking CTLA-4 made mouse tumors regress. In a phase 3 melanoma trial it did not improve survival compared with chemotherapy, and its development stalled while ipilimumab went on to approval. MedImmune, part of AstraZeneca, later acquired the rights and tested it with its anti-PD-L1 antibody durvalumab. In the HIMALAYA trial for unresectable liver cancer, a single priming dose of tremelimumab plus regular durvalumab (the STRIDE regimen) improved survival over sorafenib. The FDA approved Imjudo in 2022 for unresectable hepatocellular carcinoma with durvalumab, and later that year for metastatic non-small cell lung cancer with durvalumab and chemotherapy.", "timeline": [["1996", "Allison's lab shows CTLA-4 blockade causes tumor rejection in mice."], ["2000s", "Pfizer develops tremelimumab; a phase 3 melanoma trial does not show a survival benefit."], ["2010s", "AstraZeneca's MedImmune develops tremelimumab with durvalumab."], ["2022", "FDA approves Imjudo with durvalumab for unresectable hepatocellular carcinoma and for metastatic NSCLC."]], "cell": "CTLA-4 is an inhibitory receptor that T cells raise after activation. It binds the co-stimulatory molecules CD80 and CD86 on antigen-presenting cells more tightly than CD28 does, cutting off the second signal T cells need to fully activate. Tremelimumab, a human IgG2, binds CTLA-4 and blocks it from binding CD80 and CD86, so more CD28 co-stimulation occurs during T-cell priming in lymph nodes and more tumor-specific T cells expand. Durvalumab blocks PD-L1 in the tumor, releasing the brake on T cells in the effector phase. In the STRIDE regimen, one priming dose of tremelimumab is meant to expand T cells while limiting the cumulative toxicity that repeated CTLA-4 blockade causes.", "body": [["Liver and lung tumors", "Expanded and reactivated T cells attack cancer cells, improving survival with durvalumab."], ["Liver tissue", "Immune hepatitis can be hard to separate from underlying liver disease in patients with hepatocellular carcinoma."], ["Gut", "Colitis causes diarrhea and, rarely, perforation."], ["Endocrine glands", "Thyroid disease, adrenal insufficiency and hypophysitis can occur."], ["Skin", "Rash and itching are common as activated T cells infiltrate the skin."], ["Lungs, kidneys and heart", "Pneumonitis, nephritis and rare myocarditis can develop."]], "journey": "Given by IV infusion: a single dose with the first durvalumab dose in liver cancer, or several doses with chemotherapy in lung cancer. As an IgG2 antibody, it stays mainly in the blood and extracellular fluid and is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids, not by the liver or kidneys. The half-life is about 16 to 18 days. Immune effects begin within weeks and last well beyond the drug's presence.", "sidefx": "Side effects are immune-mediated, from T cells released by CTLA-4 and PD-L1 blockade attacking normal tissues. A single priming dose was chosen partly to limit the higher rates of colitis and other immune effects seen with repeated CTLA-4 blockade. Management is to hold therapy and give corticosteroids, with additional immune suppressants if needed. Endocrine damage often needs lifelong hormone replacement. There is no reversal agent.", "src": ["Imjudo (tremelimumab-actl) FDA label", "Abou-Alfa GK et al. HIMALAYA, NEJM Evid 2022", "Ribas A et al. J Clin Oncol 2013"]}, "atezolizumab": {"origin": "Atezolizumab blocks PD-L1, the partner that tumors use to switch off PD-1 on T cells. PD-L1 was described in 1999 by Lieping Chen's lab at the Mayo Clinic (as B7-H1), and in 2000 Gordon Freeman, Tasuku Honjo and colleagues showed it binds PD-1. Genentech, part of Roche, engineered a humanized anti-PD-L1 IgG1 with an altered Fc region so it would not kill the PD-L1-positive immune cells it binds. The FDA granted accelerated approval to Tecentriq in 2016 for advanced urothelial (bladder) cancer, making it the first PD-L1 inhibitor approved. Its bladder and breast cancer indications were later withdrawn after confirmatory trials fell short, but it remains approved for lung cancer, small cell lung cancer, liver cancer, melanoma and alveolar soft part sarcoma. A separate subcutaneous product with hyaluronidase, Tecentriq Hybreza, was approved in 2024.", "timeline": [["1999", "Lieping Chen's lab describes B7-H1, later named PD-L1."], ["2000", "PD-L1 is shown to bind PD-1 and inhibit T cells."], ["2016", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Tecentriq for urothelial cancer, the first PD-L1 inhibitor."], ["2019", "Approval with carboplatin and etoposide for extensive-stage small cell lung cancer."], ["2020", "Approval with bevacizumab for unresectable hepatocellular carcinoma."], ["2024", "FDA approves subcutaneous Tecentriq Hybreza."]], "cell": "Many tumor cells and tumor-infiltrating immune cells display PD-L1 (CD274), often in response to interferon-gamma released by attacking T cells. PD-L1 binds PD-1 on T cells, shutting them down, and also binds B7.1 (CD80), another inhibitory interaction. Atezolizumab binds PD-L1 and blocks both interactions, restoring cytotoxic T-cell activity against the tumor. It leaves PD-L2 free to bind PD-1, which some think may help preserve immune balance in the lung. An N298A substitution removes the sugar on its Fc region, so it does not trigger antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity and does not deplete PD-L1-expressing activated T cells.", "body": [["Tumors", "T cells in the tumor recover the ability to kill cancer cells, producing durable responses in some patients."], ["Lungs", "Immune pneumonitis can cause cough, shortness of breath and low oxygen."], ["Gut and liver", "Colitis causes diarrhea, and hepatitis raises liver enzymes."], ["Endocrine glands", "Thyroiditis, hypophysitis, adrenal insufficiency and type 1 diabetes can occur and may be permanent."], ["Skin", "Rash and itching are common; severe reactions are rare."], ["Kidneys, heart and nerves", "Nephritis, myocarditis and neurologic inflammation are uncommon but serious."]], "journey": "Given by IV infusion every two, three or four weeks, depending on the schedule. As an IgG1 antibody, it stays mainly in the blood and extracellular fluid. It is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids, with no meaningful liver or kidney clearance, so CYP drug interactions are not expected. The half-life is about 27 days. PD-L1 blockade lasts weeks, and immune side effects can appear months after the last dose.", "sidefx": "Side effects are immune-mediated: blocking PD-L1 releases T cells that can attack normal tissues. The most common affected organs are skin, thyroid, lungs, gut and liver. Inflammatory effects are managed by holding the drug and giving corticosteroids, adding other immune suppressants for resistant cases. Endocrine gland damage often needs lifelong hormone replacement. Infusion reactions are usually mild. There is no reversal agent.", "src": ["Tecentriq (atezolizumab) FDA label", "Dong H, Zhu G, Tamada K, Chen L. Nat Med 1999", "Freeman GJ et al. J Exp Med 2000"]}, "durvalumab": {"origin": "Durvalumab grew out of the discovery of the PD-1 'brake' on T cells. Tasuku Honjo's lab in Kyoto described PD-1 in 1992, and its partner PD-L1 was identified around 1999 to 2000 by Lieping Chen's group and by Gordon Freeman and colleagues. Tumors were found to coat themselves in PD-L1 to switch off attacking T cells. MedImmune, the biologics arm of AstraZeneca, developed durvalumab as a fully human antibody against PD-L1 and engineered its Fc tail so it would not kill the PD-L1-carrying immune cells it binds. The FDA first approved it as Imfinzi in 2017. Its landmark came in 2018, when the PACIFIC trial showed that giving it after chemoradiation for unresectable Stage III lung cancer improved survival, which changed standard care for that stage of disease.", "timeline": [["1992", "Tasuku Honjo's lab in Kyoto describes the PD-1 gene."], ["2000", "PD-L1 is shown to bind PD-1 and turn T cells off."], ["2017", "FDA grants first approval of durvalumab (Imfinzi)."], ["2018", "FDA approves durvalumab after chemoradiation for Stage III NSCLC based on the PACIFIC trial."], ["2018", "Honjo shares the Nobel Prize for checkpoint discoveries with James Allison."]], "cell": "Activated T cells display PD-1. Tumor cells and immune cells in the tumor display PD-L1 (CD274), often in response to interferon-gamma from the T cells themselves. When PD-L1 engages PD-1, the PD-1 tail recruits the phosphatase SHP-2, which strips phosphates from the T-cell receptor and CD28 signaling molecules. The T cell stops dividing, makes less cytokine and becomes 'exhausted'. Durvalumab is a human IgG1 kappa antibody that binds PD-L1 and blocks it from engaging both PD-1 and CD80 (B7.1). With the brake released, tumor-specific T cells regain proliferation, cytokine release and killing. A triple Fc mutation (L234F, L235E, P331S) reduces Fc-gamma receptor and complement binding, so the antibody does not destroy PD-L1-positive immune cells by ADCC or CDC. It acts on the immune system, not on the cancer cell cycle.", "body": [["Tumor", "Reawakened cytotoxic T cells infiltrate and kill cancer cells. Responses can take weeks to months and may follow early swelling of tumors."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis can occur from T cells attacking lung tissue, and the risk is a special concern after chest radiation, which already inflames the lung."], ["Gut and liver", "Loss of self-tolerance can cause colitis with diarrhea and immune hepatitis with rising liver enzymes."], ["Endocrine glands", "The thyroid, pituitary, adrenal glands and pancreatic islet cells can be inflamed, causing hypothyroidism, adrenal insufficiency or new type 1 diabetes, which are often permanent."], ["Skin", "Rash and itching are common. Rare severe reactions such as SJS/TEN can occur."], ["Heart and kidneys", "Rare myocarditis and nephritis reflect the same immune attack on normal organs."]], "journey": "Durvalumab is given by IV infusion, so it enters the blood completely. As a large IgG protein it stays mostly in the blood and the fluid between cells. It is not handled by liver CYP enzymes or cleared by the kidneys. Instead, cells throughout the body take it up and break it down into peptides and amino acids, while the FcRn receptor recycles some of it and extends its life. Its half-life at steady state is about 18 days, and the blockade of PD-L1 lasts for weeks. Immune side effects can appear months after the last dose.", "sidefx": "The main toxicities are immune-mediated. Removing the PD-L1 brake lets T cells attack normal tissues that also rely on PD-1 signaling for tolerance, which explains pneumonitis, colitis, hepatitis, thyroiditis, adrenal insufficiency, type 1 diabetes, nephritis, skin reactions and rare myocarditis. These are treated by stopping the drug and giving corticosteroids or other immunosuppressants. Lost hormone gland function is replaced with hormones. Infusion reactions come from the protein itself. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "The PACIFIC trial was one of the first to show that an immunotherapy given after curative-intent chemoradiation could improve overall survival in Stage III lung cancer.", "src": ["Imfinzi (durvalumab) FDA label", "Antonia SJ et al. PACIFIC trial, N Engl J Med 2017", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "avelumab": {"origin": "Avelumab was developed by the German company Merck KGaA (known as EMD Serono in the US) in alliance with Pfizer. It is a fully human anti-PD-L1 antibody that, unlike several of its relatives, keeps a normal IgG1 Fc tail. In March 2017 it became the first drug approved by the FDA for metastatic Merkel cell carcinoma, a rare and aggressive skin cancer often linked to Merkel cell polyomavirus. In 2020 the JAVELIN Bladder 100 trial showed that starting avelumab as maintenance right after successful platinum chemotherapy for advanced bladder cancer helped people live longer, which created a new maintenance approach for urothelial cancer.", "timeline": [["2000", "PD-L1 is shown to switch off T cells through PD-1."], ["2017", "FDA approves avelumab (Bavencio) for metastatic Merkel cell carcinoma, the first drug for this cancer."], ["2020", "FDA approves avelumab as first-line maintenance for advanced urothelial carcinoma after JAVELIN Bladder 100."]], "cell": "Avelumab is a human IgG1 lambda antibody that binds PD-L1 (CD274) on tumor cells and immune cells. It blocks PD-L1 from engaging PD-1 and B7.1 (CD80) on T cells. Normally PD-1 engagement recruits the phosphatase SHP-2 and dampens T-cell receptor and CD28 signals, so T cells stop dividing and killing. Blocking the ligand releases that brake, and tumor-specific cytotoxic T cells resume proliferation, interferon-gamma release and perforin and granzyme killing. Because avelumab keeps a native Fc region, it can also recruit natural killer cells to kill PD-L1-positive tumor cells by antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity, which has been shown in laboratory studies. The effect is immune, not cell-cycle specific.", "body": [["Tumor", "Released T cells and possibly NK cells attack cancer cells. Merkel cell carcinoma, which often carries viral antigens, responds well."], ["Immune system", "The native Fc tail and the protein itself make infusion reactions with fever, chills and flushing relatively common, especially early."], ["Gut and liver", "Immune colitis can cause diarrhea, and immune hepatitis can raise liver enzymes."], ["Endocrine glands", "Thyroiditis, adrenal insufficiency, hypophysitis and type 1 diabetes can result from T cells attacking hormone-making cells."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis causes cough and shortness of breath from immune inflammation of lung tissue."], ["Kidneys", "Immune nephritis is uncommon but can reduce kidney function."]], "journey": "Avelumab is given by IV infusion over about an hour, so all of it reaches the blood. It distributes mainly in plasma and tissue fluid. Like other IgG antibodies it is broken down by protein catabolism in cells throughout the body, not by liver enzymes or kidney filtration. Its half-life is shorter than many checkpoint antibodies, about 6 days, which is why it is given every 2 weeks. Premedication is used before early infusions because reactions are common.", "sidefx": "Infusion reactions with fever, chills, flushing, low blood pressure and breathing trouble come from the immune response to an infused protein and are more frequent with avelumab than with some related antibodies. The immune-mediated side effects, including pneumonitis, colitis, hepatitis, endocrine gland damage, nephritis and rare myocarditis, occur because PD-L1 blockade also releases T cells against normal tissue. These are managed with corticosteroids and holding the drug. Hormone loss is replaced. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Merkel cell carcinoma is linked to a polyomavirus in most cases, so the tumor shows foreign viral proteins that make it a natural target for a reawakened immune system.", "src": ["Bavencio (avelumab) FDA label", "Powles T et al. JAVELIN Bladder 100, N Engl J Med 2020", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "cemiplimab": {"origin": "Cemiplimab was made by Regeneron Pharmaceuticals, developed together with Sanofi. Regeneron used its VelocImmune mice, which have human antibody genes in place of their own, so the antibodies these mice make are fully human. The target was PD-1, the T-cell brake first described by Tasuku Honjo's lab in 1992. In September 2018 the FDA approved cemiplimab (Libtayo) as the first drug for advanced cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma, a sun-related skin cancer that can be disfiguring when surgery or radiation are not options. Later approvals added basal cell carcinoma after hedgehog inhibitors and non-small cell lung cancer.", "timeline": [["1992", "PD-1 is described by Tasuku Honjo's lab."], ["2018", "FDA approves cemiplimab (Libtayo) for advanced cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma."], ["2021", "FDA approvals extend cemiplimab to advanced basal cell carcinoma and PD-L1-high NSCLC."]], "cell": "Cemiplimab is a human IgG4 antibody with a stabilizing hinge mutation (S228P) that binds the PD-1 receptor (CD279) on T cells. It blocks PD-1 from engaging both of its ligands, PD-L1 and PD-L2. Normally ligand binding recruits SHP-2 to the PD-1 tail, which dephosphorylates T-cell receptor and CD28 signaling proteins, slowing T-cell division, cytokine release and killing. With PD-1 blocked, tumor-specific T cells recover these functions. Skin squamous cell cancers carry very high numbers of UV-induced mutations, so they display many abnormal proteins (neoantigens) that T cells can recognize once the brake is released. The IgG4 backbone limits Fc effector functions so the antibody does not deplete the T cells it binds.", "body": [["Skin tumors", "Reactivated T cells attack heavily mutated skin cancers, and lesions can shrink over weeks to months."], ["Lungs", "Immune pneumonitis can cause cough and breathlessness."], ["Gut and liver", "Colitis causes diarrhea, and immune hepatitis causes liver enzyme rises."], ["Endocrine glands", "Thyroid inflammation, adrenal insufficiency, hypophysitis and type 1 diabetes can develop and may be permanent."], ["Transplanted organs", "Releasing T cells can trigger rejection of a solid organ transplant, a real concern because many people with skin cancer are transplant recipients."], ["Skin", "Rash and itching are common immune effects on normal skin."]], "journey": "Cemiplimab is given by IV infusion, so it is fully available in the blood. It stays mainly in plasma and tissue fluid and is broken down slowly by protein catabolism in cells throughout the body, with FcRn recycling extending its life. It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes and is not cleared by the kidneys. The half-life is about 20 days, and receptor blockade lasts weeks. Immune side effects can start long after the last dose.", "sidefx": "The key side effects are immune-mediated inflammation of normal organs: pneumonitis, colitis, hepatitis, thyroiditis, adrenal insufficiency, type 1 diabetes, nephritis, severe skin reactions and rare myocarditis. They happen because PD-1 also keeps T cells tolerant of healthy tissue. Treatment is holding the drug and giving corticosteroids, with hormone replacement when a gland is destroyed. Organ transplant rejection can occur for the same reason. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma has one of the highest mutation counts of any human cancer because of lifelong sun exposure, which helps explain why it responds so well to PD-1 blockade.", "src": ["Libtayo (cemiplimab-rwlc) FDA label", "Migden MR et al. N Engl J Med 2018", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "dostarlimab": {"origin": "Dostarlimab was discovered by AnaptysBio and developed by Tesaro, which was bought by GlaxoSmithKline (GSK) in 2019. It is a humanized anti-PD-1 antibody. The FDA first approved it as Jemperli in April 2021 for mismatch repair-deficient (dMMR) endometrial cancer that had come back after platinum chemotherapy, and later for dMMR solid tumors of any origin and, with chemotherapy, for primary advanced endometrial cancer. It drew wide attention in 2022 when a small trial at Memorial Sloan Kettering reported that every patient with dMMR locally advanced rectal cancer had a complete clinical response.", "timeline": [["1992", "PD-1 is described by Tasuku Honjo's lab."], ["2019", "GSK acquires Tesaro, the developer of dostarlimab."], ["2021", "FDA grants accelerated approval for dMMR recurrent or advanced endometrial cancer."], ["2022", "Cercek and colleagues report complete clinical responses in all patients in a small dMMR rectal cancer trial."], ["2023", "FDA approves dostarlimab with carboplatin and paclitaxel for primary advanced endometrial cancer."]], "cell": "Dostarlimab is a humanized IgG4 antibody that binds PD-1 (CD279) on T cells and blocks its interaction with PD-L1 and PD-L2. PD-1 ligation normally recruits the phosphatase SHP-2, which turns down T-cell receptor and CD28 signaling and leaves T cells exhausted. Blocking PD-1 restores T-cell proliferation, cytokine release and cytotoxic killing. Mismatch repair-deficient tumors cannot fix small DNA copying errors, so they build up thousands of mutations and make many abnormal proteins called neoantigens. These tumors are full of T cells already primed to see them, which is why releasing the PD-1 brake works especially well in dMMR and MSI-high cancers. It is not cell-cycle specific.", "body": [["Tumor", "T cells recognize the many neoantigens in dMMR tumors and destroy cancer cells, sometimes completely."], ["Endocrine glands", "Hypothyroidism is common, and adrenal insufficiency, hypophysitis and type 1 diabetes can occur from immune attack on hormone-making cells."], ["Gut and liver", "Immune colitis causes diarrhea, and immune hepatitis raises liver enzymes."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis is an uncommon but serious immune inflammation of the lung."], ["Joints and skin", "Joint pain and rash reflect immune activity in normal tissues."], ["Blood (with chemotherapy)", "When combined with carboplatin and paclitaxel, low blood counts, nerve damage and hair loss come mostly from the chemotherapy."]], "journey": "Dostarlimab is given by IV infusion, so it enters the blood fully. It stays largely in plasma and tissue fluid and is degraded slowly by protein catabolism in cells, protected in part by FcRn recycling. Liver enzymes and the kidneys play no meaningful role. The half-life is about 23 days, long enough to allow dosing every 3 weeks at first and then every 6 weeks. PD-1 blockade lasts for weeks after a dose.", "sidefx": "Side effects come from the immune system turned loose on normal tissue: pneumonitis, colitis, hepatitis, thyroid and other gland damage, nephritis, skin reactions and rare myocarditis. PD-1 normally helps keep self-reactive T cells quiet, so blocking it lowers that barrier. Management is holding the drug and giving corticosteroids or other immunosuppressants. Glands that stop working need lifelong hormone replacement. There is no antidote.", "fun": "In the 2022 rectal cancer study, patients were able to avoid planned surgery, radiation and chemotherapy because their tumors disappeared on dostarlimab alone.", "src": ["Jemperli (dostarlimab-gxly) FDA label", "Cercek A et al. N Engl J Med 2022", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "retifanlimab": {"origin": "Retifanlimab was originally developed by MacroGenics and licensed to Incyte, which carried it through approval. It is a humanized IgG4 anti-PD-1 antibody. In March 2023 the FDA granted it accelerated approval as Zynyz for metastatic or recurrent locally advanced Merkel cell carcinoma, a rare skin cancer. In 2025 it was approved for squamous cell carcinoma of the anal canal, both with carboplatin and paclitaxel and as a single agent after platinum chemotherapy, giving that uncommon cancer one of its first approved immunotherapies.", "timeline": [["1992", "PD-1 is described by Tasuku Honjo's lab."], ["2017", "Incyte licenses retifanlimab from MacroGenics."], ["2023", "FDA grants accelerated approval for Merkel cell carcinoma."], ["2025", "FDA approves retifanlimab for squamous cell carcinoma of the anal canal."]], "cell": "Retifanlimab is a humanized IgG4 antibody that binds PD-1 (CD279) on activated T cells and blocks its binding to PD-L1 and PD-L2. When PD-1 is engaged, it recruits SHP-2, which dephosphorylates key molecules downstream of the T-cell receptor and CD28 and shuts down T-cell activation. Blocking PD-1 restores T-cell proliferation, interferon-gamma release and killing of tumor cells. Merkel cell carcinoma and anal squamous cell carcinoma are often driven by viruses (Merkel cell polyomavirus and human papillomavirus), so they display viral proteins that T cells can recognize once the brake is lifted. The IgG4 format limits Fc effector activity against the T cells it binds.", "body": [["Tumor", "Reactivated T cells kill tumor cells that display viral or mutated proteins."], ["Endocrine glands", "Thyroiditis, adrenal insufficiency, hypophysitis and type 1 diabetes can follow immune attack on hormone-making tissue."], ["Gut and liver", "Colitis causes diarrhea, and immune hepatitis raises liver enzymes."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis can develop from immune inflammation of lung tissue."], ["Skin", "Itching and rash are common, reflecting immune activity in normal skin."], ["Blood (with chemotherapy)", "With carboplatin and paclitaxel, low blood counts and nerve damage come mostly from the chemotherapy."]], "journey": "Retifanlimab is given by IV infusion, so it reaches the blood completely. It stays mostly in plasma and tissue fluid and is broken down slowly by protein catabolism in cells throughout the body. CYP enzymes and kidney filtration do not play a meaningful role. Like other IgG4 antibodies, its half-life is measured in weeks, and PD-1 blockade lasts weeks after a dose. Immune reactions can appear months later.", "sidefx": "Its serious side effects are immune-mediated: pneumonitis, colitis, hepatitis, thyroid and adrenal damage, type 1 diabetes, nephritis, severe skin reactions and rare myocarditis. These arise because PD-1 also restrains T cells that react against healthy tissue. They are managed by holding the drug and giving corticosteroids, with hormone replacement for failed glands. Infusion reactions can occur. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Zynyz (retifanlimab-dlwr) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "toripalimab": {"origin": "Toripalimab was developed by Shanghai Junshi Biosciences in China and first approved there in 2018. It is a humanized IgG4 anti-PD-1 antibody. Junshi partnered with Coherus BioSciences for the US market. In October 2023 the FDA approved it as Loqtorzi for nasopharyngeal carcinoma, a cancer of the upper throat behind the nose that is strongly linked to Epstein-Barr virus and is much more common in Southern China and Southeast Asia. It was the first drug FDA approved specifically for this cancer and one of the first PD-1 antibodies developed in China to reach the US market.", "timeline": [["1992", "PD-1 is described by Tasuku Honjo's lab."], ["2018", "Toripalimab is approved in China."], ["2023", "FDA approves toripalimab (Loqtorzi) for nasopharyngeal carcinoma."]], "cell": "Toripalimab is a humanized IgG4 kappa antibody that binds PD-1 (CD279) on T cells and blocks its interaction with PD-L1 and PD-L2. PD-1 engagement normally recruits SHP-2, which dephosphorylates T-cell receptor and CD28 signaling proteins and shuts down T-cell activity. Blocking it restores proliferation, cytokine release and tumor cell killing. Nasopharyngeal carcinoma usually carries Epstein-Barr virus and is heavily infiltrated by immune cells, and it often expresses PD-L1, so releasing T cells against virus-infected tumor cells is effective. In combination with cisplatin and gemcitabine, chemotherapy kills tumor cells and releases antigens that T cells can then target.", "body": [["Tumor", "Reactivated T cells attack EBV-infected nasopharyngeal cancer cells."], ["Thyroid", "Hypothyroidism is common, partly from immune thyroiditis and partly from prior neck radiation."], ["Gut and liver", "Immune colitis and hepatitis can cause diarrhea and liver enzyme rises."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis can cause cough and shortness of breath."], ["Skin", "Rash and itching reflect immune activity in normal skin."], ["Blood and gut (with chemotherapy)", "Nausea, vomiting and low blood counts come mostly from cisplatin and gemcitabine."]], "journey": "Toripalimab is given by IV infusion, so all of it enters the bloodstream. It distributes mostly in plasma and tissue fluid. Like other IgG antibodies, it is broken down by protein catabolism in cells throughout the body and is not handled by liver CYP enzymes or the kidneys. Its half-life is measured in weeks, and PD-1 blockade persists for weeks between doses.", "sidefx": "The serious side effects are immune-mediated inflammation of normal organs, including pneumonitis, colitis, hepatitis, endocrine gland damage, nephritis, skin reactions and rare myocarditis. These follow from PD-1 blockade, which removes a brake that keeps self-reactive T cells quiet. Corticosteroids and holding the drug are the main treatments, with hormone replacement when a gland fails. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is so closely tied to Epstein-Barr virus that viral DNA in the blood is used to track the disease.", "src": ["Loqtorzi (toripalimab-tpzi) FDA label", "Mai HQ et al. JUPITER-02 trial, Nat Med 2021"]}, "tislelizumab": {"origin": "Tislelizumab was developed by BeiGene, a company founded in Beijing in 2010 (later renamed BeOne Medicines). It is a humanized IgG4 anti-PD-1 antibody whose Fc region was specifically engineered to avoid binding Fc-gamma receptors on macrophages, a design meant to stop macrophages from eating the very T cells the antibody is trying to help. It was first approved in China in 2019. The FDA approved it as Tevimbra in March 2024 for esophageal squamous cell carcinoma after prior chemotherapy, and later for first-line esophageal and gastric cancers with chemotherapy.", "timeline": [["2010", "BeiGene is founded in Beijing."], ["2019", "Tislelizumab receives its first approval in China."], ["2024", "FDA approves tislelizumab (Tevimbra) for previously treated esophageal squamous cell carcinoma."]], "cell": "Tislelizumab binds PD-1 (CD279) on T cells with high affinity and blocks its binding to PD-L1 and PD-L2. That prevents SHP-2 from being recruited to the PD-1 tail, so T-cell receptor and CD28 signals stay on and T cells keep dividing, making cytokines and killing tumor cells. Studies of other anti-PD-1 antibodies suggested that macrophages carrying Fc-gamma receptors could grab antibody-coated T cells and remove them, which might blunt the effect. Tislelizumab's Fc was engineered to minimize this binding, limiting antibody-dependent phagocytosis of PD-1-positive T cells. The effect is immune and not cell-cycle specific.", "body": [["Tumor", "Released T cells attack esophageal and gastric cancer cells, especially when PD-L1 is present."], ["Thyroid and other glands", "Hypothyroidism is common, and adrenal insufficiency, hypophysitis and type 1 diabetes can occur."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises can reflect immune hepatitis."], ["Gut", "Immune colitis causes diarrhea and abdominal pain."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis can cause cough and breathlessness."], ["Blood and nerves (with chemotherapy)", "Low blood counts, nerve damage and diarrhea with combination regimens mostly come from the chemotherapy."]], "journey": "Tislelizumab is given by IV infusion, so it is fully available in the blood. It distributes mainly in the plasma and tissue fluid and is cleared slowly by protein catabolism in cells throughout the body. It is not a CYP substrate and is not filtered by the kidneys. The half-life is measured in weeks, so PD-1 blockade lasts across the dosing interval and beyond.", "sidefx": "The main dangers are immune-mediated: pneumonitis, colitis, hepatitis, thyroid and adrenal damage, type 1 diabetes, nephritis, severe skin reactions and myocarditis. They occur because the same PD-1 brake protects normal tissues from self-reactive T cells. Treatment is holding the drug and giving corticosteroids or other immunosuppressants, plus hormone replacement as needed. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Tevimbra (tislelizumab-jsgr) FDA label", "Shen L et al. RATIONALE-302 trial, J Clin Oncol 2022"]}, "penpulimab": {"origin": "Penpulimab was developed by Akeso, a biotechnology company in Zhongshan, China. It is a humanized IgG1 anti-PD-1 antibody whose Fc region was engineered to remove Fc-gamma receptor binding, so it blocks PD-1 without triggering immune cells to attack the T cells it coats. It was first approved in China. In 2025 the FDA approved penpulimab-kcqx for recurrent or metastatic non-keratinizing nasopharyngeal carcinoma, both with platinum and gemcitabine and alone after prior therapy. Its US label uses its nonproprietary name.", "timeline": [["1992", "PD-1 is described by Tasuku Honjo's lab."], ["2021", "Penpulimab is first approved in China."], ["2025", "FDA approves penpulimab-kcqx for nasopharyngeal carcinoma."]], "cell": "Penpulimab binds PD-1 (CD279) on activated T cells and blocks it from engaging PD-L1 and PD-L2. When PD-1 is engaged, SHP-2 phosphatase is recruited and turns off T-cell receptor and CD28 signals, so the T cell stops dividing, makes less cytokine and kills less. Blocking PD-1 restores these functions against tumor cells. Although penpulimab is an IgG1, its Fc region was engineered so it does not bind Fc-gamma receptors, which prevents ADCC and antibody-dependent phagocytosis of the PD-1-positive T cells. Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is usually Epstein-Barr virus positive and rich in immune cells, so it responds to checkpoint blockade. It is not cell-cycle specific.", "body": [["Tumor", "Reactivated T cells target EBV-associated nasopharyngeal cancer cells."], ["Thyroid", "Hypothyroidism is common, from immune thyroiditis often added to damage from prior neck radiation."], ["Gut and liver", "Immune colitis and hepatitis can cause diarrhea and liver enzyme rises."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis can be serious and sometimes fatal."], ["Pancreas and adrenals", "Immune attack can cause type 1 diabetes with ketoacidosis or adrenal insufficiency."], ["Nerves and muscle", "Rare myasthenia gravis and myocarditis can occur and may occur together."]], "journey": "Penpulimab is given by IV infusion, so it enters the blood completely. It stays mostly in plasma and tissue fluid and is broken down into peptides and amino acids by protein catabolism throughout the body. It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes and is not cleared by the kidneys. Its half-life is measured in weeks, and PD-1 blockade persists for weeks after each dose.", "sidefx": "Like other PD-1 blockers, penpulimab releases T cells against normal organs, causing pneumonitis, colitis, hepatitis, nephritis, hypophysitis, adrenal insufficiency, type 1 diabetes and rare myocarditis or myasthenia gravis. Severe skin reactions can occur. These are managed by holding the drug and giving corticosteroids, with hormone replacement for failed glands. Nausea and low blood counts in combination regimens come mostly from the chemotherapy. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Penpulimab-kcqx FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "cosibelimab": {"origin": "Cosibelimab is a fully human anti-PD-L1 antibody developed by Checkpoint Therapeutics, a US company that licensed it from the Dana-Farber Cancer Institute. Unlike several PD-L1 antibodies, it keeps a working IgG1 Fc region. The FDA approved it as Unloxcyt in December 2024 for metastatic or locally advanced cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma when surgery or radiation cannot cure the disease. It was the first PD-L1 blocker approved for this sun-related skin cancer, which had previously been treated with PD-1 blockers.", "timeline": [["2000", "PD-L1 is shown to switch off T cells through PD-1."], ["2024", "FDA approves cosibelimab (Unloxcyt) for advanced cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma."]], "cell": "Cosibelimab is a human IgG1 antibody that binds PD-L1 (CD274) on tumor and immune cells. It blocks PD-L1 from engaging PD-1 and B7.1 (CD80). PD-L1 to PD-1 binding normally recruits SHP-2 to the T-cell side and turns down T-cell receptor and CD28 signals, so T cells stop attacking. Removing that signal reactivates tumor-specific cytotoxic T cells. Because cosibelimab keeps a functional Fc region, it can also flag PD-L1-positive tumor cells for antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity by NK cells. Skin squamous cell carcinomas carry very many UV-induced mutations, giving T cells many targets once they are released.", "body": [["Skin tumors", "Reactivated T cells, possibly helped by NK cells, shrink sun-damaged skin cancers."], ["Thyroid and other glands", "Hypothyroidism, adrenal insufficiency and hypophysitis can follow immune attack on hormone-making cells."], ["Gut and liver", "Diarrhea from colitis and liver enzyme rises from hepatitis can occur."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis from immune inflammation of lung tissue can be serious or fatal."], ["Skin", "Rash reflects immune activity in normal skin."], ["Muscles and joints", "Musculoskeletal pain and fatigue are common."]], "journey": "Cosibelimab is given by IV infusion, so it enters the bloodstream fully. It distributes in plasma and tissue fluid and binds PD-L1 on cells, which can speed its removal when target levels are high. It is broken down by protein catabolism rather than by liver enzymes or the kidneys. Its half-life is measured in weeks, and ligand blockade persists between doses.", "sidefx": "Its serious side effects are immune-mediated: pneumonitis, colitis, hepatitis, nephritis, endocrine gland damage, severe skin reactions, rare myocarditis and type 1 diabetes with ketoacidosis. Blocking PD-L1 lifts a brake that also protects healthy tissue from self-reactive T cells. Holding the drug and giving corticosteroids is the main treatment, with hormone replacement for lost gland function. Infusion reactions can occur. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Unloxcyt (cosibelimab-ipdl) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "blinatumomab": {"origin": "Blinatumomab is the first approved bispecific T-cell engager, or BiTE. The idea came from German researchers, including Gert Riethmuller and Peter Kufer in Munich, who designed small proteins made of two antibody binding pieces joined by a short linker: one piece grabs a cancer cell and the other grabs a T cell. The Munich company Micromet developed the CD19-targeted version, and Amgen bought Micromet in 2012. In December 2014 the FDA granted accelerated approval to blinatumomab (Blincyto) for relapsed or refractory Philadelphia chromosome-negative B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia. It later gained approvals for measurable residual disease and for consolidation, and it opened the door to a whole family of T-cell engagers.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Researchers in Munich develop single-chain bispecific antibodies that link T cells to tumor cells."], ["2008", "Early trial of blinatumomab in lymphoma reports tumor regressions at very low concentrations."], ["2012", "Amgen acquires Micromet, the developer of blinatumomab."], ["2014", "FDA grants accelerated approval for relapsed or refractory B-cell ALL."], ["2018", "FDA approves blinatumomab for ALL in remission with measurable residual disease."]], "cell": "Blinatumomab is a fusion of two single-chain variable fragments (scFv) joined by a flexible linker, about 54 kDa, with no Fc region. One arm binds CD19, present on almost all normal and malignant B cells. The other binds CD3 epsilon, part of the T-cell receptor complex. By bridging the two cells it forms an artificial immune synapse. This activates the T cell without needing antigen presentation on MHC or a matching T-cell receptor, so any nearby cytotoxic T cell can be recruited. The activated T cell releases perforin and granzymes into the B cell, triggering apoptosis, and then can move on to kill more targets (serial killing). T cells also release cytokines such as IL-6, IFN-gamma and TNF-alpha and multiply, which drives both the antileukemic effect and cytokine release syndrome.", "body": [["Bone marrow and blood", "Leukemic B blasts are killed, and minimal residual disease can be cleared. Normal B cells are also destroyed."], ["Immune system", "Loss of normal B cells lowers antibody levels (hypogammaglobulinemia), raising infection risk."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release from activated T cells causes fever, low blood pressure and low oxygen, called cytokine release syndrome."], ["Brain", "Neurotoxicity can cause tremor, confusion, trouble speaking, encephalopathy and seizures, thought to involve T-cell and cytokine effects on the brain and its blood vessels."], ["Pancreas", "Pancreatitis has been reported, sometimes severe."], ["Metabolism", "Rapid killing of many leukemia cells can cause tumor lysis syndrome."]], "journey": "Because it is small and has no Fc region to be recycled by FcRn, blinatumomab is cleared quickly, with a half-life of about 2 hours. It is therefore given as a continuous IV infusion through a portable pump for weeks at a time in each cycle. Steady state is reached within about a day. It is broken down by protein catabolism into small peptides and amino acids, with a small part filtered by the kidneys. Effects fade within hours after the infusion stops, which helps when toxicity needs to be controlled.", "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome happens because many T cells are activated at once and flood the blood with IL-6, IFN-gamma and other cytokines. Stepping up the dose and giving corticosteroid premedication lowers this risk. Severe CRS is treated by stopping the infusion, giving tocilizumab (an IL-6 receptor blocker) and corticosteroids. Neurologic toxicity is managed by stopping the drug and giving corticosteroids. B-cell loss causes low antibody levels and infections. Tumor lysis can follow rapid leukemia cell death.", "fun": "Blinatumomab works at extraordinarily low concentrations because each recruited T cell can kill target after target, which is why it is dosed in micrograms rather than the much larger amounts typical of antibodies.", "src": ["Blincyto (blinatumomab) FDA label", "Bargou R et al. Science 2008", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "teclistamab": {"origin": "Teclistamab was developed by Janssen, the pharmaceutical company of Johnson & Johnson. It targets B-cell maturation antigen (BCMA), a protein found almost only on plasma cells and myeloma cells, which made it an ideal myeloma target. Unlike the small, short-lived BiTE format of blinatumomab, teclistamab is a full-size IgG4 bispecific antibody, so it lasts long enough to be given as an injection under the skin. In October 2022 the FDA granted it accelerated approval as Tecvayli, making it the first BCMA-directed bispecific T-cell engager approved for relapsed or refractory multiple myeloma. Later it was approved with daratumumab for earlier relapse.", "timeline": [["1990s", "BCMA is identified as a receptor on mature B cells and plasma cells."], ["2022", "FDA grants accelerated approval for teclistamab (Tecvayli), the first BCMA bispecific for myeloma."]], "cell": "Teclistamab is a humanized IgG4 bispecific antibody with one arm that binds BCMA (TNFRSF17) on plasma cells and myeloma cells and one arm that binds CD3 on T cells. Bridging the two forms an immune synapse that activates the T cell without needing MHC presentation. The T cell releases perforin and granzymes, killing the myeloma cell by apoptosis, and secretes cytokines such as IL-6, IFN-gamma and TNF-alpha while multiplying. Because normal plasma cells also carry BCMA, they are destroyed too, which lowers immunoglobulin levels. The IgG4 backbone and Fc changes reduce unwanted Fc effector activity, and the full-length format gives it a long half-life.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Myeloma cells are killed by redirected T cells, lowering M-protein and marrow plasma cells."], ["Immune system", "Normal plasma cells are depleted, so antibody levels fall and serious bacterial, viral and opportunistic infections become common."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome with fever, low blood pressure and low oxygen is most common after early step-up doses."], ["Brain", "ICANS (immune effector cell-associated neurotoxicity) can cause confusion, word-finding problems and rarely seizures."], ["Blood counts", "Neutropenia, anemia and low platelets are common, partly from marrow inflammation and disease."], ["Skin", "Injection-site redness and swelling come from local immune activation."]], "journey": "Teclistamab is injected under the skin. It is absorbed slowly through the lymph into the blood, reaching peak levels several days after a dose. As a full IgG antibody it is protected by FcRn recycling, giving a half-life of days to weeks. It is broken down by protein catabolism into peptides and amino acids, not by liver enzymes or the kidneys. Treatment starts with step-up doses to lower the cytokine surge, and later dosing can be spaced out once a response is established.", "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome comes from sudden mass activation of T cells, which release IL-6 and other cytokines. It is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids, and premedication and step-up dosing reduce it. Neurotoxicity (ICANS) is managed with corticosteroids. Because BCMA is also on healthy plasma cells, antibody levels fall and infections, including pneumonia and PML, are a leading cause of harm, so immunoglobulin replacement and infection prevention are used. Low blood counts add to infection risk.", "src": ["Tecvayli (teclistamab-cqyv) FDA label", "Moreau P et al. MajesTEC-1, N Engl J Med 2022"]}, "talquetamab": {"origin": "Talquetamab was developed by Janssen (Johnson & Johnson) to give myeloma a second T-cell target besides BCMA. It is aimed at GPRC5D, an orphan G protein-coupled receptor that is highly expressed on myeloma cells but, outside of them, mainly in hard keratinized tissues such as hair follicles, nails and the tongue. In August 2023 the FDA granted accelerated approval to talquetamab (Talvey), the first GPRC5D-directed therapy, for multiple myeloma after at least four prior lines of therapy. Because its target differs, it can work in people whose myeloma has escaped BCMA-directed treatment.", "timeline": [["2010s", "GPRC5D is identified as a protein highly expressed on myeloma cells."], ["2023", "FDA grants accelerated approval for talquetamab (Talvey), the first GPRC5D-directed therapy."]], "cell": "Talquetamab is a humanized IgG4 bispecific antibody. One arm binds GPRC5D on myeloma cells and the other binds CD3 on T cells. Linking the two builds an immune synapse that activates the T cell independent of MHC, leading to perforin and granzyme release and apoptosis of the myeloma cell, plus cytokine release and T-cell expansion. GPRC5D is also present in keratinized tissues, including the papillae of the tongue, nail beds and the skin, so T cells are also directed at these normal cells. That on-target, off-tumor effect explains its unusual taste, nail and skin side effects.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Myeloma cells carrying GPRC5D are killed, including in disease resistant to BCMA therapy."], ["Mouth and tongue", "Taste changes, dry mouth and trouble swallowing are very common because GPRC5D is present in tongue papillae."], ["Nails and skin", "Nail thinning or loss, skin peeling and rash come from T-cell attack on keratinized tissue."], ["Weight and nutrition", "Taste loss and swallowing problems can lead to marked weight loss."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome with fever and low blood pressure follows early step-up doses."], ["Brain", "ICANS can cause confusion and, rarely, seizures."]], "journey": "Talquetamab is injected under the skin and absorbed slowly through the lymph into the blood over several days. As a full IgG antibody it is recycled by FcRn and lasts days to weeks. It is broken down by protein catabolism rather than by CYP enzymes or the kidneys. Treatment begins with step-up doses before full dosing to limit cytokine release, and dosing can be weekly or every 2 weeks.", "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome results from mass T-cell activation and IL-6 release and is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids. ICANS is managed with corticosteroids. The taste, mouth, nail and skin problems are on-target effects: T cells are drawn to GPRC5D in normal keratinized cells. Infections arise from immune disruption, low blood counts and loss of normal plasma cells, though infection rates have been somewhat lower than with BCMA-directed bispecifics.", "fun": "Talquetamab's taste and nail side effects are a textbook example of 'on-target, off-tumor' toxicity, where the drug hits the right protein in the wrong tissue.", "src": ["Talvey (talquetamab-tgvs) FDA label", "Chari A et al. MonumenTAL-1, N Engl J Med 2022"]}, "elranatamab": {"origin": "Elranatamab was developed by Pfizer as a BCMA-directed bispecific T-cell engager for multiple myeloma. It is built on a humanized IgG2 backbone and is given as a fixed dose under the skin. In August 2023 the FDA granted it accelerated approval as Elrexfio for multiple myeloma that had relapsed after at least four prior lines of therapy, including a proteasome inhibitor, an immunomodulatory drug and an anti-CD38 antibody. It became the second BCMA bispecific available in the US, after teclistamab.", "timeline": [["1990s", "BCMA is identified on mature B cells and plasma cells."], ["2023", "FDA grants accelerated approval for elranatamab (Elrexfio)."]], "cell": "Elranatamab is a humanized IgG2 bispecific antibody. One arm binds BCMA (TNFRSF17), which is expressed on plasma cells and myeloma cells and supports their survival. The other arm binds CD3 on T cells. Linking them creates an immune synapse that activates the T cell regardless of its natural specificity and without MHC involvement. The T cell releases perforin and granzymes to kill the myeloma cell, then secretes cytokines and multiplies. Normal plasma cells carrying BCMA are destroyed too, so immunoglobulin production falls. The IgG2 format with Fc changes limits Fc-mediated effects, while the full-size structure gives it a long half-life.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Myeloma cells are destroyed, lowering paraprotein levels."], ["Immune system", "Loss of normal plasma cells causes low antibody levels and a high rate of serious infections such as pneumonia and sepsis."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome with fever, low blood pressure and low oxygen is most common after the first step-up doses."], ["Brain", "ICANS can cause confusion, sleepiness and trouble speaking."], ["Blood counts", "Neutropenia, anemia and low platelets are common."], ["Skin", "Injection-site reactions reflect local immune activation."]], "journey": "Elranatamab is injected under the skin and reaches the blood slowly, with peak levels several days after a dose. FcRn recycling gives it a half-life of weeks. It is broken down by protein catabolism, not CYP metabolism or kidney filtration. Step-up dosing in the first week lowers the cytokine surge, after which it is given on a regular schedule that can be spaced out in responders.", "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome follows mass activation of T cells and IL-6 release, and is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids. Neurotoxicity is treated with corticosteroids. Because BCMA is on healthy plasma cells, antibody levels drop and infections, including opportunistic ones and PML, are common and can be fatal. Immunoglobulin replacement and infection prevention are part of care. Low blood counts add to the risk.", "src": ["Elrexfio (elranatamab-bcmm) FDA label", "Lesokhin AM et al. MagnetisMM-3, Nat Med 2023"]}, "linvoseltamab": {"origin": "Linvoseltamab was developed by Regeneron Pharmaceuticals using its VelocImmune mice, which make fully human antibodies, and its platform for building bispecific antibodies. It targets BCMA on myeloma cells and CD3 on T cells. Unlike the other BCMA bispecifics in the US, it is given by IV infusion. Its first FDA review in 2024 was delayed because of issues at a third-party manufacturing site, not because of its clinical data. The FDA granted it accelerated approval as Lynozyfic in 2025 for relapsed or refractory multiple myeloma after at least four prior lines of therapy.", "timeline": [["1990s", "BCMA is identified on mature B cells and plasma cells."], ["2024", "Initial FDA review is delayed by findings at a third-party manufacturing site."], ["2025", "FDA grants accelerated approval for linvoseltamab (Lynozyfic)."]], "cell": "Linvoseltamab is a fully human IgG4 bispecific antibody. One arm binds BCMA (TNFRSF17) on plasma cells and myeloma cells, and the other binds CD3 on T cells. By holding the two cells together it forms an immune synapse that activates T cells without MHC involvement. Activated T cells release perforin and granzymes, killing the myeloma cell, and secrete cytokines while expanding. Normal plasma cells are also killed because they express BCMA, so immunoglobulin production falls. The IgG4 format with Fc changes limits Fc effector functions.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Myeloma cells are killed by redirected T cells."], ["Immune system", "Normal plasma cell loss lowers antibody levels, and infections such as pneumonia are a major risk."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome usually appears within about a day of a step-up dose."], ["Brain", "ICANS can cause confusion, sleepiness and trouble speaking."], ["Blood counts", "Neutropenia, anemia and low platelets are common."], ["Muscles and lungs", "Musculoskeletal pain, cough and upper respiratory infections are common."]], "journey": "Linvoseltamab is given by IV infusion, so it is fully in the blood at the end of the infusion. As a full IgG antibody it is recycled by FcRn and has a half-life of weeks. It is broken down by protein catabolism, not by CYP enzymes or the kidneys. Step-up doses are given first, often with hospital monitoring, and then dosing becomes less frequent once a good response is reached.", "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome is caused by rapid activation of many T cells and IL-6 release and is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids. ICANS is treated with corticosteroids. Loss of normal plasma cells and low blood counts make infections common and sometimes fatal, so immunoglobulin replacement and preventive antimicrobials are often used. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Lynozyfic (linvoseltamab-gcpt) FDA label", "Bumma N et al. LINKER-MM1, J Clin Oncol 2024"]}, "epcoritamab": {"origin": "Epcoritamab was created by the Danish biotechnology company Genmab using its DuoBody platform, which pairs two different antibody halves into one stable bispecific IgG1. It was co-developed with AbbVie. In May 2023 the FDA granted it accelerated approval as Epkinly for relapsed or refractory diffuse large B-cell lymphoma after two or more lines of therapy. It was the first bispecific T-cell engager for lymphoma given as an injection under the skin. Approvals for follicular lymphoma followed, alone and with lenalidomide and rituximab.", "timeline": [["1999", "Genmab is founded in Copenhagen."], ["2023", "FDA grants accelerated approval for epcoritamab (Epkinly) in DLBCL."], ["2024", "FDA approves epcoritamab for relapsed or refractory follicular lymphoma."]], "cell": "Epcoritamab is a humanized IgG1 bispecific antibody with a silenced Fc region. One arm binds CD20 (MS4A1) on normal and malignant B cells. The other binds CD3 on T cells. The bridge forms a cytolytic synapse that activates T cells independent of MHC. The T cells release perforin and granzymes, killing CD20-positive lymphoma cells, and secrete cytokines while multiplying. Its CD20 binding site differs from that of rituximab, so it can be combined with rituximab. Silencing the Fc prevents the antibody from triggering ADCC or complement against the T cells it binds.", "body": [["Lymph nodes and tumor", "Lymphoma cells are killed, and responses can be deep and lasting in some patients."], ["Immune system", "Normal B cells are depleted, lowering antibodies and raising infection risk."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome most often follows the first full dose in cycle 1."], ["Brain", "ICANS can cause confusion and aphasia, and rarely seizures."], ["Skin", "Injection-site reactions come from local immune activity."], ["Metabolism", "Rapid killing of bulky disease can cause tumor lysis syndrome."]], "journey": "Epcoritamab is injected under the skin. It is absorbed slowly through the lymphatic system, which brings it straight to the lymph nodes where much of the lymphoma lives, and peaks in the blood several days later. FcRn recycling gives it a half-life of weeks. It is broken down by protein catabolism, not by liver enzymes or the kidneys. Step-up dosing in the first cycle lowers the cytokine surge.", "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome comes from mass T-cell activation and IL-6 release, is lowered by step-up dosing and corticosteroid premedication, and is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids. ICANS is treated with corticosteroids. B-cell depletion and low blood counts lead to infections, including PML. Tumor lysis can follow rapid lymphoma cell death. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Epkinly (epcoritamab-bysp) FDA label", "Thieblemont C et al. EPCORE NHL-1, J Clin Oncol 2023"]}, "glofitamab": {"origin": "Glofitamab was developed by Roche and its US company Genentech. Its design is unusual: a 2:1 format with two arms that bind CD20 and one arm that binds CD3, which gives it strong grip on lymphoma cells. To lower the risk of a dangerous cytokine surge, each course begins with one dose of obinutuzumab, an anti-CD20 antibody, to clear B cells from the blood before glofitamab is given. In June 2023 the FDA granted it accelerated approval as Columvi for relapsed or refractory diffuse large B-cell lymphoma after two or more lines of therapy. It is given for a fixed number of cycles rather than indefinitely.", "timeline": [["2010s", "Roche develops a 2:1 CD20 by CD3 bispecific antibody format."], ["2023", "FDA grants accelerated approval for glofitamab (Columvi) in large B-cell lymphoma."]], "cell": "Glofitamab is a humanized IgG1 bispecific antibody with two CD20-binding regions and one CD3 epsilon-binding region, plus a silenced Fc. Binding CD20 with two arms gives high avidity, so it holds onto B cells tightly even when CD20 levels are low. The CD3 arm then engages a T cell, forming an immune synapse that activates the T cell independent of MHC. The T cell releases perforin and granzymes and kills the CD20-positive cell, while releasing cytokines and expanding. Obinutuzumab pretreatment depletes circulating and some tissue B cells first, which lowers the number of targets that trigger T cells all at once and reduces cytokine release syndrome.", "body": [["Lymph nodes and tumor", "Lymphoma cells are killed, and responses can be complete and lasting after a fixed course."], ["Immune system", "Normal B cells are depleted for months, lowering antibodies and raising infection risk."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome usually begins within the first day after the first step-up dose."], ["Tumor sites", "Tumor flare can cause pain and swelling where lymphoma sits as T cells pour in."], ["Brain", "ICANS can cause confusion, and rarely seizures."], ["Blood counts and minerals", "Lymphopenia, neutropenia and low phosphate are common."]], "journey": "Glofitamab is given by IV infusion, starting with step-up doses after a single obinutuzumab pretreatment. It is fully available in the blood and spreads into lymphoid tissue. As an IgG it is recycled by FcRn, with a terminal half-life of about a week. It is broken down by protein catabolism, not CYP enzymes or the kidneys. The course is fixed at 12 cycles, but B-cell depletion persists for months after.", "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome is caused by mass T-cell activation and is reduced by obinutuzumab pretreatment, step-up dosing and corticosteroid premedication. It is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids. ICANS is managed with corticosteroids. Tumor flare reflects immune cells flooding tumor sites. B-cell loss and neutropenia lead to serious infections. Low phosphate likely reflects cell shifts during immune activation.", "fun": "The 2:1 design means each glofitamab molecule can grip two CD20 proteins at once, which boosts its hold on lymphoma cells.", "src": ["Columvi (glofitamab-gxbm) FDA label", "Dickinson MJ et al. N Engl J Med 2022"]}, "mosunetuzumab": {"origin": "Mosunetuzumab was developed by Genentech, part of Roche. It is a full-length humanized IgG1 bispecific antibody that binds CD20 on B cells and CD3 on T cells. In December 2022 the FDA granted it accelerated approval as Lunsumio for relapsed or refractory follicular lymphoma after two or more lines of therapy, making it the first CD20 by CD3 bispecific T-cell engager approved in the US. It is given for a fixed number of cycles, a time-limited approach that allows treatment-free intervals. A subcutaneous form, Lunsumio Velo, was later approved.", "timeline": [["2010s", "Genentech develops a full-length CD20 by CD3 bispecific antibody."], ["2022", "FDA grants accelerated approval for mosunetuzumab (Lunsumio), the first CD20 by CD3 bispecific in the US."]], "cell": "Mosunetuzumab binds CD20 (MS4A1) on normal and malignant B cells with one arm and CD3 epsilon on T cells with the other. This pulls the cells together into a cytolytic synapse that activates T cells independent of MHC. The T cells release perforin and granzymes, killing the CD20-positive B cells, and release cytokines as they expand. The Fc region is modified to reduce Fc-gamma receptor binding, which limits nonspecific immune activation and protects T cells from being killed. Follicular lymphoma cells reliably carry CD20, making them a good target.", "body": [["Lymph nodes and tumor", "Follicular lymphoma cells are killed, and many patients reach complete remission after a fixed course."], ["Immune system", "Normal B cells are depleted for months, lowering antibodies and raising infection risk."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome is most common after the larger doses early in cycle 1."], ["Tumor sites", "Tumor flare can cause pain and swelling at lymphoma sites."], ["Skin", "Rash and itching can occur from immune activation."], ["Blood counts and minerals", "Lymphopenia, neutropenia and low phosphate are common."]], "journey": "Mosunetuzumab is given by IV infusion with step-up doses in the first cycle, or by injection under the skin in its newer form. Once in the blood it distributes into lymphoid tissue. As a full IgG it is recycled by FcRn, giving a terminal half-life of about 16 days. It is broken down by protein catabolism rather than CYP metabolism or kidney clearance. The course lasts 8 cycles for complete responders or up to 17 cycles otherwise.", "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome is caused by T-cell activation releasing IL-6 and other cytokines. Step-up dosing and premedication reduce it, and it is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids. ICANS is treated with corticosteroids. B-cell depletion and neutropenia cause serious infections. Tumor flare comes from immune cells entering tumor sites. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Lunsumio (mosunetuzumab-axgb) FDA label", "Budde LE et al. Lancet Oncol 2022"]}, "tarlatamab": {"origin": "Tarlatamab was developed by Amgen using its BiTE platform, the same technology behind blinatumomab, but with an added Fc piece that extends its half-life. It targets DLL3 (delta-like ligand 3), a protein that is rarely on the surface of normal cells but is found on most small cell lung cancers. An earlier DLL3-directed antibody-drug conjugate from another company had failed in trials, so DLL3 had looked like a hard target. In May 2024 the FDA granted accelerated approval to tarlatamab (Imdelltra) for extensive-stage small cell lung cancer that progressed after platinum chemotherapy. It was the first T-cell engager approved for a common solid tumor.", "timeline": [["2010s", "DLL3 is identified as a surface target on small cell lung cancer."], ["2024", "FDA grants accelerated approval for tarlatamab (Imdelltra), the first DLL3-directed T-cell engager."]], "cell": "Tarlatamab is a half-life extended BiTE. It contains two single-chain variable fragments, one binding DLL3 and one binding CD3 epsilon, fused to a single-chain Fc that slows its clearance. DLL3 normally inhibits Notch signaling inside cells during development and is mostly kept inside the cell, but small cell lung cancer and other neuroendocrine tumors display it on their surface. When tarlatamab links a DLL3-positive tumor cell to a T cell, the T cell is activated without MHC involvement and releases perforin and granzymes, killing the tumor cell. Activated T cells also release cytokines and multiply.", "body": [["Lungs and tumor sites", "Small cell lung cancer cells displaying DLL3 are killed by redirected T cells."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome with fever, low blood pressure and low oxygen occurs mostly after the first two doses."], ["Brain", "ICANS can cause confusion, tremor, trouble speaking and rarely seizures."], ["Mouth and appetite", "Taste changes and loss of appetite are common."], ["Blood and salts", "Anemia, lymphopenia and low sodium are common."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises can occur during treatment and are monitored."]], "journey": "Tarlatamab is given by IV infusion. The Fc extension lets it last days rather than hours, so it can be given every 2 weeks instead of by continuous infusion like blinatumomab. It is broken down by protein catabolism and is not cleared by CYP enzymes or the kidneys. Because CRS and neurotoxicity are most likely early, the first doses are given with a period of monitoring and IV fluids.", "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome happens when many T cells are activated at once and release IL-6 and other cytokines. It is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids, and early doses are given with monitoring. ICANS is treated with corticosteroids. Serious infections and neutropenia can occur. Low sodium and taste changes are common. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "DLL3 is mostly hidden inside normal cells, which is why a T-cell engager aimed at it can spare most healthy tissue.", "src": ["Imdelltra (tarlatamab-dlle) FDA label", "Ahn MJ et al. DeLLphi-301, N Engl J Med 2023"]}, "zanidatamab": {"origin": "Zanidatamab was created by Zymeworks, a Canadian biotechnology company, using its Azymetric platform for building bispecific antibodies. Instead of linking a cancer cell to a T cell, it binds two different sites on the same target, HER2. One arm binds the site used by trastuzumab and the other binds the site used by pertuzumab. Zymeworks licensed it to Jazz Pharmaceuticals. In November 2024 the FDA granted it accelerated approval as Ziihera for previously treated HER2-positive biliary tract cancer. It was later approved with chemotherapy for HER2-positive gastroesophageal adenocarcinoma.", "timeline": [["1998", "Trastuzumab is approved as the first HER2-targeted antibody."], ["2022", "Jazz Pharmaceuticals licenses zanidatamab from Zymeworks."], ["2024", "FDA grants accelerated approval for zanidatamab (Ziihera) in HER2-positive biliary tract cancer."]], "cell": "HER2 (ERBB2) is a receptor tyrosine kinase. When it is overexpressed, it pairs with itself or with HER3 and other family members, driving PI3K/AKT and MAPK growth signals. Zanidatamab is a humanized IgG1 biparatopic antibody: one arm binds extracellular domain 4 (the trastuzumab epitope) and the other binds domain 2 (the pertuzumab epitope, the dimerization arm). Because the two arms can bind different HER2 molecules at once, the antibodies cross-link receptors into large clusters on the cell surface. These clusters are pulled into the cell and degraded, lowering surface HER2 and shutting down its signaling. The Fc region also triggers complement-dependent cytotoxicity and ADCC.", "body": [["Tumor", "HER2-driven biliary and gastroesophageal cancer cells lose their growth signal and are tagged for immune killing."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is the most common side effect and can be severe, likely from HER-family blockade in the intestinal lining."], ["Heart", "Heart muscle uses HER2 signaling to cope with stress, so blocking it can lower ejection fraction."], ["Immune system", "Infusion reactions with fever and chills can occur during or after infusions."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises are common during treatment and are monitored."], ["Pregnancy", "HER2 blockade can harm fetal kidney development and lower amniotic fluid."]], "journey": "Zanidatamab is given by IV infusion every 2 or 3 weeks. It stays mainly in the blood and tissue fluid, binds HER2 on tumor cells and is taken into cells. Like other IgG antibodies it is recycled by FcRn and broken down by protein catabolism into peptides and amino acids. CYP enzymes and the kidneys do not clear it. Its half-life is long, measured in days to weeks.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea comes from HER-family signaling loss in the gut lining and can lead to dehydration and kidney injury, so it is treated early with fluids and antidiarrheals. Lower heart function reflects loss of HER2 survival signals in heart muscle cells, so heart function is checked before and during treatment. Infusion reactions come from the protein and its Fc activity. Embryo-fetal toxicity occurs because HER2 is needed for fetal development. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Ziihera (zanidatamab-hrii) FDA label", "Harding JJ et al. HERIZON-BTC-01, Lancet Oncol 2023"]}, "zenocutuzumab": {"origin": "Zenocutuzumab was developed by Merus, a Dutch company whose Biclonics platform makes full-length bispecific antibodies. It was built to treat cancers driven by NRG1 gene fusions, rare changes where part of the neuregulin 1 gene fuses to another gene and produces a protein that constantly switches on HER3. These fusions occur in a small fraction of lung, pancreatic and other cancers and had no targeted treatment. In December 2024 the FDA granted accelerated approval to zenocutuzumab (Bizengri) for NRG1 fusion-positive non-small cell lung cancer and pancreatic adenocarcinoma, the first therapy approved for this genetic driver.", "timeline": [["2014", "NRG1 gene fusions are described as cancer drivers in lung adenocarcinoma."], ["2024", "FDA grants accelerated approval for zenocutuzumab (Bizengri), the first therapy for NRG1 fusion cancers."]], "cell": "NRG1 fusion proteins carry the EGF-like domain of neuregulin 1, the natural ligand of HER3 (ERBB3). When the fusion ligand binds HER3, HER3 pairs with HER2, and the HER2-HER3 dimer is one of the strongest activators of PI3K/AKT growth signaling. Zenocutuzumab is a humanized IgG1 bispecific antibody. Its HER2 arm docks on HER2, which positions its HER3 arm to block the ligand-binding site on HER3. This 'dock and block' mechanism stops NRG1 fusion proteins from activating HER3 and prevents HER2-HER3 pairing, shutting down PI3K/AKT and MAPK signals. The low-fucose Fc also enhances ADCC by NK cells.", "body": [["Tumor", "NRG1 fusion-driven lung and pancreatic cancer cells lose their main growth signal."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and nausea reflect HER-family blockade in the gut lining."], ["Immune system", "Infusion reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, can occur, especially with the first dose."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease or pneumonitis can occur and can be fatal."], ["Heart", "Blocking HER2 signals can reduce heart muscle function."], ["Minerals", "Low magnesium is common. HER-family signaling helps kidney tubules reabsorb magnesium, which may explain this effect."]], "journey": "Zenocutuzumab is given by IV infusion every 2 weeks, so all of it reaches the blood. It distributes into plasma and tissue fluid and binds HER2 and HER3 on cells. It is broken down by protein catabolism into peptides and amino acids, not by liver enzymes or kidney filtration. Its half-life is measured in days.", "sidefx": "Infusion reactions come from the protein and its enhanced Fc activity and are reduced with premedication. Diarrhea and low magnesium reflect HER-family signaling loss in the gut lining and kidney. Reduced heart function reflects HER2 blockade in heart muscle. Pneumonitis is a serious immune-related lung injury. Embryo-fetal harm is expected from HER2 and HER3 blockade. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Bizengri (zenocutuzumab-zbco) FDA label", "Schram AM et al. N Engl J Med 2025"]}, "tebentafusp": {"origin": "Tebentafusp was developed by Immunocore, a company spun out of the University of Oxford, using its ImmTAC platform (immune-mobilizing monoclonal T-cell receptors against cancer). Instead of an antibody, its targeting end is an engineered soluble T-cell receptor, which can see protein fragments from inside cells as they are displayed on HLA molecules. In January 2022 the FDA approved tebentafusp (Kimmtrak) for HLA-A*02:01-positive adults with unresectable or metastatic uveal melanoma, an eye cancer with few options. It was the first approved T-cell receptor therapeutic, the first drug approved for this cancer, and the first bispecific T-cell engager to improve overall survival in a solid tumor.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Immunocore develops soluble T-cell receptor drugs from Oxford research."], ["2021", "A randomized trial shows tebentafusp improves overall survival in metastatic uveal melanoma."], ["2022", "FDA approves tebentafusp (Kimmtrak), the first T-cell receptor therapeutic."]], "cell": "Cells chop internal proteins into peptides and display them on HLA class I molecules. Melanocytes and melanoma cells display a peptide from gp100, a melanosome protein. Tebentafusp has two parts. One is a soluble T-cell receptor engineered to bind the gp100 peptide-HLA-A*02:01 complex with very high affinity, far tighter than a natural T-cell receptor. The other is an anti-CD3 single-chain variable fragment. When the TCR end binds a melanoma cell, the anti-CD3 end recruits any nearby T cell, activating it to release cytokines and perforin and granzymes that kill the cell. Because recognition depends on HLA-A*02:01, it only works in people with that tissue type.", "body": [["Liver (main metastatic site)", "Uveal melanoma spreads mainly to the liver, where redirected T cells attack tumor cells."], ["Skin", "Rash, itching and dry skin are very common because normal skin melanocytes also show gp100, and T cells attack them."], ["Hair and skin color", "Loss of pigment in skin and hair can occur from melanocyte destruction."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome with fever, chills and low blood pressure is common within hours of the first three infusions."], ["Liver enzymes", "Liver enzyme rises can occur, especially with liver metastases."], ["Eyes", "Melanocytes in the eye can be affected, though serious eye problems are uncommon."]], "journey": "Tebentafusp is given by IV infusion once a week. It is a relatively small protein without a full antibody Fc, so it is cleared quickly, with a half-life of roughly 6 to 8 hours. It is broken down by protein catabolism into peptides and amino acids. Because cytokine release syndrome is most likely with the first three doses, these are given in a hospital or with overnight monitoring.", "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome occurs because the drug activates many T cells at once, releasing IL-6 and other cytokines, and is treated with fluids, corticosteroids and, if needed, tocilizumab. Skin rash, itching and depigmentation happen because normal melanocytes also display the gp100 peptide, an on-target effect. Liver enzyme rises reflect inflammation around liver metastases. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Tebentafusp can only be used if a blood test shows the HLA-A*02:01 tissue type, which is present in roughly half of people of European descent.", "src": ["Kimmtrak (tebentafusp-tebn) FDA label", "Nathan P et al. N Engl J Med 2021"]}, "ado-trastuzumab-emtansine": {"origin": "T-DM1 joins two stories. The first is trastuzumab, the HER2 antibody developed at Genentech and approved in 1998. The second is maytansine, a very potent cell poison isolated in the early 1970s by S. Morris Kupchan and colleagues from an East African shrub of the genus Maytenus. Maytansine was too toxic to use as ordinary chemotherapy. ImmunoGen, a Massachusetts company, developed derivatives called maytansinoids, including DM1, and a way to attach them to antibodies. Genentech linked DM1 to trastuzumab through a stable linker, so the poison would be released mainly inside HER2-positive cancer cells. The FDA approved it as Kadcyla in February 2013 for HER2-positive metastatic breast cancer, the first ADC approved for a solid tumor, and in 2019 for early breast cancer with disease left after presurgical therapy.", "timeline": [["1972", "Kupchan and colleagues report maytansine from a Maytenus shrub."], ["1998", "FDA approves trastuzumab for HER2-positive breast cancer."], ["2013", "FDA approves ado-trastuzumab emtansine (Kadcyla) for metastatic HER2-positive breast cancer."], ["2019", "FDA approves T-DM1 for residual early breast cancer after the KATHERINE trial."]], "cell": "The trastuzumab part, a humanized IgG1, binds subdomain IV of the HER2 receptor on tumor cells. The whole complex is then pulled into the cell by endocytosis and sent to lysosomes. Because the MCC linker is a stable thioether that cannot be cut by enzymes, the payload is freed only when the antibody itself is digested, releasing lysine-MCC-DM1. DM1 binds tubulin at the vinca region and stops microtubules from assembling. Without a working mitotic spindle, the cell arrests in G2/M and dies by apoptosis. The charged lysine-MCC-DM1 does not easily cross membranes, so there is little bystander killing of nearby cells. The antibody also keeps its own actions: blocking HER2 signaling and recruiting NK cells for ADCC. On average about 3.5 DM1 molecules are attached to each antibody.", "body": [["Breast tumor", "HER2-positive cancer cells take up the conjugate and die during cell division."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises are common, and rare severe liver injury and nodular regenerative hyperplasia can occur, likely from DM1 effects on liver cells."], ["Platelets", "Thrombocytopenia is a hallmark effect, thought to come from DM1 damaging megakaryocytes, the platelet-making cells."], ["Heart", "Blocking HER2 can reduce left ventricular function, because heart cells use HER2 for stress survival."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral neuropathy can occur because microtubules are needed for transport along long nerve fibers."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease or pneumonitis is uncommon but can be fatal."]], "journey": "T-DM1 is given by IV infusion every 3 weeks. The conjugate stays mostly in the blood and tissue fluid and has a half-life of about 4 days. It is taken up by HER2-positive cells and digested in lysosomes, releasing DM1-containing catabolites. Any free DM1 is metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4 and CYP3A5 and leaves the body mainly in bile and feces. Free DM1 in the blood is highly protein bound. The antibody portion is broken down to peptides and amino acids like other proteins.", "sidefx": "Most side effects follow from the payload. DM1 stops microtubules, which explains low platelets, nerve damage and liver injury in cells that are exposed to the drug. Bleeding risk rises when platelets are low. Heart dysfunction comes from the trastuzumab part blocking HER2 in heart muscle. Embryo-fetal harm comes from both parts. T-DM1 must not be substituted for trastuzumab or for trastuzumab deruxtecan because the names are similar and the drugs are different. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Maytansine was once tested as a stand-alone cancer drug but was too toxic. Attaching it to an antibody turned a failed chemotherapy into a targeted one.", "src": ["Kadcyla (ado-trastuzumab emtansine) FDA label", "Kupchan SM et al. J Am Chem Soc 1972", "Verma S et al. EMILIA trial, N Engl J Med 2012"]}, "trastuzumab-deruxtecan": {"origin": "Trastuzumab deruxtecan was developed by the Japanese company Daiichi Sankyo, later in partnership with AstraZeneca. Daiichi Sankyo designed a new payload, DXd, from exatecan, a derivative of camptothecin, the topoisomerase I poison first isolated in 1966 by Monroe Wall and Mansukh Wani from the Chinese 'happy tree', Camptotheca acuminata. They attached about eight DXd molecules to each trastuzumab antibody with a cleavable linker. The FDA first approved it as Enhertu in December 2019 for HER2-positive metastatic breast cancer. In 2022 the DESTINY-Breast04 trial showed it worked in 'HER2-low' breast cancer, creating a new treatment category for tumors once thought to be HER2-negative. Approvals followed in HER2-mutant lung cancer, gastric cancer, HER2-ultralow breast cancer and HER2-positive solid tumors of any origin.", "timeline": [["1966", "Wall and Wani isolate camptothecin from Camptotheca acuminata."], ["2019", "FDA approves trastuzumab deruxtecan (Enhertu) for HER2-positive metastatic breast cancer."], ["2022", "FDA approves T-DXd for HER2-low breast cancer and HER2-mutant NSCLC."], ["2024", "FDA grants a tumor-agnostic approval for HER2-positive (IHC 3+) solid tumors."]], "cell": "The trastuzumab IgG1 binds HER2 on tumor cells and the conjugate is taken into the cell and moved to lysosomes. There, cathepsin enzymes cut the tetrapeptide (GGFG) linker, which is designed to be stable in blood, releasing DXd. DXd blocks topoisomerase I, the enzyme that nicks one DNA strand to relieve twisting during replication. DXd traps the enzyme on DNA, so when a replication fork runs into the trapped complex during S phase, it becomes a double-strand break and the cell dies by apoptosis. DXd crosses membranes easily, so after release it can diffuse into neighboring cancer cells and kill them even if they have little or no HER2. This bystander effect, together with a high drug-to-antibody ratio of about 8, helps explain its activity in HER2-low tumors.", "body": [["Tumor", "HER2-positive and HER2-low cancer cells and their neighbors are killed through DNA damage."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease or pneumonitis is the most important serious toxicity and can be fatal; the cause is not fully understood."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia and anemia come from DXd damaging rapidly dividing blood-forming cells."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are common, and the drug is considered moderately to highly emetogenic, along with diarrhea or constipation."], ["Hair", "Hair loss reflects damage to dividing hair follicle cells."], ["Heart", "Reduced left ventricular function can occur from HER2 blockade."]], "journey": "T-DXd is given by IV infusion every 3 weeks. The intact conjugate circulates with a half-life of about 6 to 7 days. After uptake into HER2-expressing cells, DXd is released in lysosomes. Free DXd in the blood is short-lived and highly protein bound, about 97 percent. DXd is metabolized mainly by CYP3A4 in the liver and leaves mostly through bile into feces. The antibody portion is broken down into peptides and amino acids.", "sidefx": "Topoisomerase I poisoning by DXd damages DNA in any dividing cell it reaches, explaining neutropenia, anemia, nausea, hair loss and gut effects. Interstitial lung disease is a boxed warning; the exact cause is unclear, but it may involve uptake of the conjugate by lung immune cells. Suspected ILD is treated by stopping the drug and giving corticosteroids. Heart dysfunction comes from HER2 blockade. It must not be substituted for trastuzumab or ado-trastuzumab emtansine. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The DESTINY-Breast04 results drew a standing ovation at the 2022 ASCO meeting because they changed the care of about half of all breast cancers, those with low HER2 levels.", "src": ["Enhertu (fam-trastuzumab deruxtecan-nxki) FDA label", "Modi S et al. DESTINY-Breast04, N Engl J Med 2022", "Wall ME, Wani MC et al. J Am Chem Soc 1966"]}, "sacituzumab-govitecan": {"origin": "Sacituzumab govitecan was developed by Immunomedics, a New Jersey company founded by David Goldenberg, a pioneer of antibody-targeted cancer therapy. Its antibody targets Trop-2, a surface protein found on many epithelial cancers, and its payload is SN-38, the active form of the chemotherapy irinotecan. SN-38 traces back to camptothecin, isolated in 1966 from the Chinese 'happy tree', Camptotheca acuminata. SN-38 is too poorly soluble and too toxic to give alone, but attached to an antibody it could be delivered to tumors. The FDA granted accelerated approval to Trodelvy in April 2020 for metastatic triple-negative breast cancer, a hard-to-treat subtype, and Gilead Sciences bought Immunomedics later that year. It was later approved for HR-positive, HER2-negative breast cancer.", "timeline": [["1966", "Camptothecin is isolated from Camptotheca acuminata."], ["1996", "FDA approves irinotecan, whose active metabolite is SN-38."], ["2020", "FDA grants accelerated approval for sacituzumab govitecan (Trodelvy) in triple-negative breast cancer."], ["2020", "Gilead Sciences acquires Immunomedics."], ["2023", "FDA approves it for pretreated HR-positive, HER2-negative breast cancer."]], "cell": "The humanized IgG1 antibody (hRS7) binds Trop-2 (TACSTD2), a transmembrane protein overexpressed on many carcinomas. About 7 to 8 SN-38 molecules are attached through the CL2A linker, which is sensitive to acid and hydrolysis. Some SN-38 is released inside the tumor cell after the conjugate is internalized, and some is released in the acidic tumor environment, where it can enter nearby cells (bystander effect). SN-38 binds the topoisomerase I-DNA complex and stops the enzyme from resealing the DNA nick. When a replication fork collides with this trapped complex during S phase, a double-strand break forms and the cell undergoes apoptosis.", "body": [["Tumor", "Trop-2-expressing breast cancer cells and nearby cancer cells are killed by DNA damage."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia is common and can be severe, because SN-38 damages dividing white cell precursors."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is common and can be severe, as SN-38 injures the rapidly dividing lining of the intestine, the same toxicity seen with irinotecan."], ["Stomach and appetite", "Nausea, vomiting and loss of appetite are common."], ["Hair", "Hair loss comes from injury to dividing follicle cells."], ["Immune system", "Hypersensitivity and infusion reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, can occur."]], "journey": "Sacituzumab govitecan is given by IV infusion on days 1 and 8 of a 3-week cycle. Unlike many ADCs, it has a short half-life, about 16 hours for the conjugate, because the linker releases SN-38 steadily. Free SN-38 is very highly bound to albumin. The liver inactivates SN-38 by glucuronidation through UGT1A1 to SN-38 glucuronide, which leaves mainly through bile into the gut. People with reduced UGT1A1 activity, such as those homozygous for the UGT1A1*28 allele, clear SN-38 more slowly and have more neutropenia.", "sidefx": "Both boxed warnings, neutropenia and diarrhea, come from SN-38 damaging rapidly dividing cells in the marrow and gut lining. In the gut, bacteria can also cut the glucuronide back into active SN-38, which adds to intestinal injury. Late diarrhea is treated with loperamide and fluids, and early cholinergic-type diarrhea may be treated with atropine. Growth factors and dose delays are used for neutropenia. People with reduced UGT1A1 function are at higher risk. Infusion reactions come from the protein. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Trop-2 got its name as a 'trophoblast antigen' because it was first found on trophoblast cells of the placenta, which, like cancer cells, invade surrounding tissue.", "src": ["Trodelvy (sacituzumab govitecan-hziy) FDA label", "Bardia A et al. ASCENT trial, N Engl J Med 2021"]}, "datopotamab-deruxtecan": {"origin": "Datopotamab deruxtecan comes from Daiichi Sankyo, developed with AstraZeneca. It uses the same DXd payload and cleavable linker technology as trastuzumab deruxtecan, but its antibody targets Trop-2, the same surface protein targeted by sacituzumab govitecan. Daiichi Sankyo chose a lower drug-to-antibody ratio of about 4 to balance potency and tolerability. The FDA approved it as Datroway in January 2025 for previously treated HR-positive, HER2-negative breast cancer, and in 2025 it received accelerated approval for EGFR-mutated non-small cell lung cancer after EGFR-targeted therapy and platinum chemotherapy.", "timeline": [["1966", "Camptothecin, ancestor of the DXd payload, is isolated from Camptotheca acuminata."], ["2019", "Trastuzumab deruxtecan proves the DXd ADC platform."], ["2025", "FDA approves datopotamab deruxtecan (Datroway) for HR-positive, HER2-negative breast cancer."], ["2025", "FDA grants accelerated approval for EGFR-mutated NSCLC."]], "cell": "The humanized IgG1 antibody binds Trop-2 (TACSTD2) on tumor cells and the conjugate is internalized and moved to lysosomes. Lysosomal enzymes cleave the GGFG tetrapeptide linker and release DXd, an exatecan-derived topoisomerase I inhibitor. DXd traps topoisomerase I on DNA, so when replication forks hit the trapped complexes during S phase they become double-strand breaks, triggering apoptosis. DXd is membrane permeable and can diffuse into nearby cancer cells, killing them even if they express little Trop-2. The drug-to-antibody ratio is about 4, half that of trastuzumab deruxtecan.", "body": [["Tumor", "Trop-2-expressing breast and lung cancer cells and their neighbors are killed through DNA damage."], ["Mouth", "Stomatitis (mouth sores) is the most common side effect, likely because Trop-2 is present in the lining of the mouth and the payload damages those cells."], ["Eyes", "Dry eye and keratitis can occur, as the corneal surface also expresses Trop-2."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease or pneumonitis can occur and can be fatal."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and constipation are common."], ["Hair", "Hair loss reflects injury to dividing follicle cells."]], "journey": "Datopotamab deruxtecan is given by IV infusion every 3 weeks. The conjugate has a half-life of about 4 to 5 days. After uptake into Trop-2-positive cells, DXd is released in lysosomes. Free DXd in blood is short-lived and about 97 percent protein bound. DXd is metabolized mainly by CYP3A4 and leaves through bile into feces. The antibody portion is broken down to peptides and amino acids.", "sidefx": "Stomatitis and eye surface problems reflect Trop-2 on normal mucosal and corneal cells, where the conjugate delivers its payload. Steroid mouthwash and regular eye care are used to reduce these. Interstitial lung disease is the most serious risk and is treated by stopping the drug and giving corticosteroids. Nausea, hair loss and low blood counts come from DXd damaging dividing cells. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Datroway (datopotamab deruxtecan-dlnk) FDA label", "TROPION-Breast01 trial (Bardia A et al.)"]}, "enfortumab-vedotin": {"origin": "Enfortumab vedotin was developed by Astellas and Seagen (formerly Seattle Genetics, now part of Pfizer). Its antibody targets Nectin-4, a cell adhesion protein found on almost all urothelial (bladder) cancers. Its payload, monomethyl auristatin E (MMAE), is a synthetic relative of dolastatin 10, a compound isolated in 1987 by George R. Pettit of Arizona State University from the sea hare Dolabella auricularia, and later traced to cyanobacteria the sea hare eats. The FDA granted accelerated approval to Padcev in December 2019 for advanced urothelial cancer after prior therapy. In 2023 the EV-302 trial showed that enfortumab vedotin with pembrolizumab nearly doubled overall survival compared with platinum chemotherapy, replacing chemotherapy as first-line treatment after decades.", "timeline": [["1987", "George R. Pettit reports dolastatin 10 from the sea hare Dolabella auricularia."], ["2019", "FDA grants accelerated approval for enfortumab vedotin (Padcev)."], ["2023", "FDA approves enfortumab vedotin with pembrolizumab as first-line therapy for advanced urothelial cancer."]], "cell": "The fully human IgG1 antibody binds Nectin-4 on urothelial cancer cells. The conjugate is internalized and moved to lysosomes, where proteases cut the valine-citrulline linker and release MMAE. MMAE binds tubulin and blocks microtubule polymerization, so the mitotic spindle cannot form, the cell arrests in G2/M and dies by apoptosis. MMAE can cross membranes, so it also kills neighboring tumor cells (bystander effect). Dying cells release danger signals that can prime an immune response (immunogenic cell death), which may help explain why it works so well with pembrolizumab.", "body": [["Urothelial tumor", "Nectin-4-rich bladder and urinary tract cancer cells die during division."], ["Skin", "Rash is very common and can progress to SJS/TEN, because Nectin-4 is present in normal skin keratinocytes and sweat glands."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral sensory neuropathy is common because microtubules are needed to transport material along long nerve fibers."], ["Blood sugar", "Hyperglycemia and diabetic ketoacidosis can occur, even in people without diabetes; the exact cause is unclear."], ["Eyes", "Dry eye, blurred vision and keratitis can occur."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis is uncommon but can be severe or fatal."]], "journey": "Enfortumab vedotin is given by IV infusion on a weekly schedule within each cycle. The conjugate has a half-life of about 3.4 days, and free MMAE about 2.4 days. After uptake, MMAE is released in lysosomes. Free MMAE is metabolized mainly by CYP3A4 and is excreted mostly in feces. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors can raise MMAE levels and side effects. The antibody portion is broken down into peptides and amino acids.", "sidefx": "Skin reactions arise because Nectin-4 is expressed in normal skin, so the conjugate delivers MMAE to skin cells; severe reactions such as SJS/TEN carry a boxed warning. Nerve damage comes from MMAE disrupting microtubules in nerve axons. High blood sugar and DKA are recognized risks, so glucose is checked. Eye surface toxicity and pneumonitis occur less often. Management is holding or stopping the drug and treating the specific problem, such as corticosteroids for severe skin reactions. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The sea hare that supplied dolastatin 10 is a soft-bodied marine snail. The compound was present in such tiny amounts that huge numbers of animals had to be collected to isolate enough to solve its structure.", "src": ["Padcev (enfortumab vedotin-ejfv) FDA label", "Powles T et al. EV-302, N Engl J Med 2024", "Pettit GR et al. J Am Chem Soc 1987"]}, "tisotumab-vedotin": {"origin": "Tisotumab vedotin was developed by the Danish company Genmab together with Seagen (now part of Pfizer). Its antibody targets tissue factor, the protein that starts the extrinsic clotting cascade, which many cancers, including cervical cancer, display in large amounts. It carries the MMAE payload derived from dolastatin 10, a compound first found in a sea hare. The FDA granted it accelerated approval as Tivdak in September 2021 for recurrent or metastatic cervical cancer that progressed on or after chemotherapy, and later converted it to full approval after a confirmatory trial showed improved survival.", "timeline": [["1987", "Dolastatin 10, the parent of MMAE, is reported from a sea hare."], ["2021", "FDA grants accelerated approval for tisotumab vedotin (Tivdak) in cervical cancer."], ["2024", "FDA grants full approval after the innovaTV 301 trial."]], "cell": "The fully human IgG1 antibody binds tissue factor (TF, CD142) on tumor cells. The conjugate is internalized, and lysosomal proteases cleave the valine-citrulline linker to release MMAE. MMAE binds tubulin and blocks microtubule assembly, causing G2/M arrest and apoptosis. Freed MMAE can enter nearby cells and kill them too (bystander effect). The antibody also recruits immune cells by ADCC and blocks TF-factor VIIa signaling that tumors use for growth, while being designed to have little effect on normal clotting.", "body": [["Cervical tumor", "TF-rich cancer cells are killed during division."], ["Eyes", "Conjunctivitis, dry eye and keratitis are common and can lead to corneal ulcers, because TF and the conjugate affect the eye surface."], ["Nose and bleeding", "Nosebleeds are common, and serious bleeding can occur, possibly related to TF targeting and vessel effects."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral neuropathy comes from MMAE disrupting microtubule transport in nerves."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis is uncommon but can be severe."], ["Hair and skin", "Hair loss and rash can occur, reflecting payload effects on dividing cells."]], "journey": "Tisotumab vedotin is given by IV infusion every 3 weeks. The conjugate has a half-life of about 4 days and free MMAE about 2.6 days. MMAE released in cells or in blood is metabolized mainly by CYP3A4 and excreted mostly in feces. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors can raise MMAE exposure. The antibody portion is broken down by protein catabolism.", "sidefx": "Eye toxicity carries a boxed warning. It is thought to come from the conjugate reaching the conjunctiva and cornea, so eye exams, lubricating drops, steroid drops and cooling eye pads during infusion are used to lower risk. Bleeding, mostly nosebleeds, may relate to TF targeting in vessels. Neuropathy reflects MMAE damage to nerve microtubules. Pneumonitis can occur. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Tivdak (tisotumab vedotin-tftv) FDA label", "Coleman RL et al. innovaTV 204, Lancet Oncol 2021"]}, "mirvetuximab-soravtansine": {"origin": "Mirvetuximab soravtansine was developed by ImmunoGen, the Massachusetts company that pioneered maytansinoid payloads, a family that traces back to maytansine, isolated in the early 1970s from an African Maytenus shrub. Its antibody targets folate receptor alpha (FRalpha), a protein that helps cells take in folate and is overexpressed on many ovarian cancers but scarce on most normal tissues. The FDA granted accelerated approval to Elahere in November 2022 for FRalpha-positive, platinum-resistant ovarian cancer, a setting with few effective options, and full approval in 2024 after the MIRASOL trial showed longer survival. AbbVie acquired ImmunoGen in 2024.", "timeline": [["1972", "Maytansine is reported from a Maytenus shrub."], ["2022", "FDA grants accelerated approval for mirvetuximab soravtansine (Elahere)."], ["2024", "FDA grants full approval after the MIRASOL trial."], ["2024", "AbbVie acquires ImmunoGen."]], "cell": "The humanized IgG1 antibody binds folate receptor alpha (FOLR1) on ovarian cancer cells. The conjugate is internalized and processed in lysosomes, and the cleavable disulfide (sulfo-SPDB) linker releases DM4, a maytansinoid. DM4 is converted to S-methyl-DM4, and both bind tubulin and block microtubule assembly. The mitotic spindle fails, the cell arrests in G2/M and dies by apoptosis. These payload forms can cross membranes and kill nearby tumor cells (bystander effect). Patients are selected with an FDA-approved test showing high FRalpha on the tumor.", "body": [["Ovarian tumor", "FRalpha-rich cancer cells die during division."], ["Eyes", "Blurred vision and keratopathy (changes in the corneal surface) are very common and are thought to come from off-target uptake of the payload by corneal epithelial cells."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral neuropathy reflects payload damage to nerve microtubules."], ["Gut", "Nausea, abdominal pain and diarrhea are common."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises can occur and are checked during treatment."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis can occur and can be severe."]], "journey": "Mirvetuximab soravtansine is given by IV infusion every 3 weeks, with the amount based on adjusted ideal body weight. The conjugate has a half-life of about 4.8 days. DM4 is released in cells and is metabolized by CYP3A4 to S-methyl-DM4. The payload is highly protein bound and is eliminated mainly in feces. The antibody portion is broken down into peptides and amino acids.", "sidefx": "Eye toxicity carries a boxed warning. Off-target uptake of the maytansinoid by rapidly renewing corneal epithelial cells causes blurred vision and keratopathy, so eye exams, lubricating drops and steroid eye drops are used. Symptoms usually improve after holding the drug. Neuropathy comes from microtubule damage in nerves. Pneumonitis is treated by stopping the drug and giving corticosteroids. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Elahere (mirvetuximab soravtansine-gynx) FDA label", "Moore KN et al. MIRASOL, N Engl J Med 2023"]}, "brentuximab-vedotin": {"origin": "Brentuximab vedotin was developed by Seattle Genetics (later Seagen, now part of Pfizer), with Takeda as partner outside the US. Its target, CD30, was first identified in the early 1980s by German researchers studying the Reed-Sternberg cells of Hodgkin lymphoma, then named the Ki-1 antigen. An earlier naked CD30 antibody had shown little effect, so Seattle Genetics attached MMAE, a potent synthetic relative of dolastatin 10, a compound first isolated from a sea hare. The FDA granted accelerated approval to Adcetris in August 2011 for relapsed Hodgkin lymphoma and systemic anaplastic large cell lymphoma. It was the first new Hodgkin lymphoma drug approved in the US in decades, and it later moved into first-line treatment.", "timeline": [["1982", "German researchers describe the Ki-1 (CD30) antigen on Reed-Sternberg cells."], ["1987", "Dolastatin 10, parent of MMAE, is reported from a sea hare."], ["2011", "FDA grants accelerated approval for brentuximab vedotin (Adcetris)."], ["2018", "FDA approves brentuximab with AVD for previously untreated Stage III or IV Hodgkin lymphoma."]], "cell": "The chimeric IgG1 antibody (cAC10) binds CD30 (TNFRSF8), which is highly expressed on Reed-Sternberg cells of classical Hodgkin lymphoma and on anaplastic large cell lymphoma and some other T-cell lymphomas. The conjugate is internalized and moved to lysosomes, where proteases cut the valine-citrulline linker to release MMAE. MMAE binds tubulin and blocks microtubule polymerization, so the cell arrests in G2/M and undergoes apoptosis. Free MMAE can diffuse into neighboring cells, including the many supporting immune cells around Reed-Sternberg cells (bystander effect).", "body": [["Lymph nodes", "CD30-positive Hodgkin and T-cell lymphoma cells are killed during division."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral sensory neuropathy is common and builds with each dose, because MMAE disrupts microtubule transport in long nerves; motor neuropathy can also occur."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia is common, especially with chemotherapy, raising infection risk."], ["Pancreas", "Acute pancreatitis is rare but can be fatal."], ["Brain", "Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML), a JC virus brain infection, is a rare boxed warning risk."], ["Liver and lungs", "Hepatotoxicity and pulmonary toxicity can occur, and the lung risk is greatly raised with bleomycin."]], "journey": "Brentuximab vedotin is given by IV infusion every 2 to 3 weeks. The conjugate has a half-life of about 4 to 6 days and free MMAE about 3 to 4 days. MMAE is metabolized by CYP3A4 and is excreted mostly in feces, with some in urine. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise MMAE levels. Severe kidney or liver impairment raises MMAE exposure. The antibody part is broken down by protein catabolism.", "sidefx": "Neuropathy, the most common dose-limiting problem, comes from MMAE disrupting microtubules that carry material along nerve fibers; it usually improves after the drug is stopped or reduced. Neutropenia reflects payload effects on marrow. Use with bleomycin is contraindicated because of severe lung toxicity. PML occurs from JC virus reactivation in an immunosuppressed person. Pancreatitis and severe skin reactions are rare. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Reed-Sternberg cells are often a small minority of the cells in a Hodgkin lymph node; most of the mass is a crowd of immune cells drawn in by the cancer cells.", "src": ["Adcetris (brentuximab vedotin) FDA label", "Younes A et al. N Engl J Med 2010", "Schwab U, Stein H et al. Nature 1982"]}, "polatuzumab-vedotin": {"origin": "Polatuzumab vedotin was developed by Genentech, part of Roche, using ADC technology licensed from Seattle Genetics. Its antibody targets CD79b, part of the B-cell receptor complex found on almost all mature B-cell lymphomas. Like brentuximab and enfortumab, it carries MMAE, a synthetic relative of the sea hare compound dolastatin 10. The FDA granted accelerated approval to Polivy in June 2019 with bendamustine and rituximab for relapsed or refractory diffuse large B-cell lymphoma. In 2023, based on the POLARIX trial, it was approved as part of Pola-R-CHP for previously untreated DLBCL, the first change to standard first-line DLBCL treatment in about 20 years.", "timeline": [["1987", "Dolastatin 10, parent of MMAE, is reported from a sea hare."], ["2019", "FDA grants accelerated approval for polatuzumab vedotin (Polivy) in relapsed DLBCL."], ["2023", "FDA approves Pola-R-CHP for previously untreated DLBCL."]], "cell": "The humanized IgG1 antibody binds CD79b, a signaling component of the B-cell receptor complex. CD79b is naturally internalized, so the conjugate is efficiently carried into the B cell and sent to lysosomes. Proteases there cut the valine-citrulline linker and release MMAE. MMAE binds tubulin and blocks microtubule polymerization, causing G2/M arrest and apoptosis. Freed MMAE can also kill nearby cells (bystander effect). In Pola-R-CHP, polatuzumab replaces vincristine, another microtubule poison, which helps avoid stacking two drugs with similar nerve toxicity.", "body": [["Lymph nodes and tumor", "CD79b-positive lymphoma cells are killed during division."], ["Immune system", "Normal B cells also carry CD79b and are depleted, adding to infection risk."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral neuropathy comes from MMAE disrupting microtubule transport in nerves."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia, anemia and low platelets are common, especially with chemotherapy."], ["Infections", "Pneumonia, sepsis and rarely PML can occur."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises and hepatotoxicity can occur."]], "journey": "Polatuzumab vedotin is given by IV infusion once every 21-day cycle for a fixed number of cycles. Antibody-conjugated MMAE has a half-life of about 12 days. Released MMAE is metabolized by CYP3A4 and leaves mostly through bile into feces, with a small amount in urine. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors can raise MMAE exposure. The antibody is broken down by protein catabolism.", "sidefx": "Neuropathy and low blood counts reflect the MMAE payload: microtubule disruption in nerve axons and in dividing marrow cells. Infections arise from neutropenia, B-cell depletion and chemotherapy, so preventive antimicrobials and growth factors are used. Infusion reactions come from the protein. Tumor lysis can follow rapid lymphoma cell death. PML is rare. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Polivy (polatuzumab vedotin-piiq) FDA label", "Tilly H et al. POLARIX, N Engl J Med 2022"]}, "loncastuximab-tesirine": {"origin": "Loncastuximab tesirine was developed by ADC Therapeutics, a company based in Lausanne, Switzerland. Its payload belongs to the pyrrolobenzodiazepines (PBDs), a family whose first member, anthramycin, was discovered in the 1960s from the soil bacterium Streptomyces refuineus. PBDs bind DNA with great precision, and chemists later joined two PBD units into dimers that cross-link both DNA strands. ADC Therapeutics linked such a dimer to an antibody against CD19, a protein on nearly all B-cell lymphomas. The FDA granted accelerated approval to Zynlonta in April 2021 for relapsed or refractory large B-cell lymphoma after two or more lines of therapy.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Anthramycin, the first pyrrolobenzodiazepine, is discovered from Streptomyces bacteria."], ["2000s", "PBD dimers are developed as ultra-potent DNA cross-linking payloads."], ["2021", "FDA grants accelerated approval for loncastuximab tesirine (Zynlonta)."]], "cell": "The humanized IgG1 antibody binds CD19 on B cells, and the conjugate is internalized and moved to lysosomes. Proteases there cut the valine-alanine linker of tesirine and release SG3199, a PBD dimer. SG3199 slides into the minor groove of DNA and covalently binds guanine bases on opposite strands, forming interstrand cross-links. Because these cross-links cause little distortion of the DNA helix, they are hard for repair enzymes to detect. The cross-links block DNA replication and transcription, leading to cell death. This action does not depend on a specific phase of the cell cycle.", "body": [["Lymph nodes and tumor", "CD19-positive lymphoma cells die from unrepaired DNA cross-links."], ["Fluid balance", "Edema, pleural and pericardial effusions and ascites can develop, a recognized PBD payload effect thought to involve vascular leak."], ["Skin", "Photosensitivity and rash can occur, so sun exposure is limited."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia, low platelets and anemia come from payload effects on marrow cells."], ["Liver", "Increased GGT and other liver enzymes are common."], ["Infections", "Serious infections can occur with low counts and B-cell depletion."]], "journey": "Loncastuximab tesirine is given by IV infusion every 3 weeks. The conjugate has a half-life of about 2 to 3 weeks. After uptake into CD19-positive cells, the PBD payload is released. Free SG3199 is highly protein bound and is partly metabolized by CYP3A4. The antibody part is broken down by protein catabolism, and minor amounts are eliminated in bile and urine.", "sidefx": "Fluid build-up, skin reactions and photosensitivity are linked to the PBD payload. Dexamethasone premedication is used to reduce edema and effusions. Low blood counts come from DNA cross-linking in dividing marrow cells. Infections follow low counts and loss of normal B cells. Embryo-fetal harm is expected because the payload damages DNA. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Zynlonta (loncastuximab tesirine-lpyl) FDA label", "Caimi PF et al. LOTIS-2, Lancet Oncol 2021"]}, "inotuzumab-ozogamicin": {"origin": "Inotuzumab ozogamicin carries calicheamicin, one of the most potent natural cell poisons known. Calicheamicin was discovered in the 1980s by scientists at Lederle Laboratories from the soil bacterium Micromonospora echinospora, found in a chalky soil in Texas called caliche, which gave the compound its name. It was so toxic that it could only be used when attached to an antibody. Wyeth (which absorbed Lederle) developed antibody-calicheamicin conjugates, and Pfizer completed inotuzumab after acquiring Wyeth. It targets CD22, found on most B-cell leukemia blasts. The FDA approved Besponsa in August 2017 for relapsed or refractory B-cell precursor acute lymphoblastic leukemia in adults, later extended to children 1 year and older.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Lederle scientists discover calicheamicin from Micromonospora echinospora in Texas soil."], ["2009", "Pfizer acquires Wyeth, developer of calicheamicin conjugates."], ["2017", "FDA approves inotuzumab ozogamicin (Besponsa) for relapsed or refractory B-cell ALL."]], "cell": "The humanized IgG4 antibody binds CD22 on B-lineage leukemia cells, and the conjugate is rapidly internalized. In the acidic lysosome, the acid-labile hydrazone linker breaks and releases N-acetyl-gamma-calicheamicin. Inside the cell, reduction of its trisulfide group (by glutathione) triggers a chemical rearrangement called Bergman cyclization of its enediyne core, forming a highly reactive diradical. Calicheamicin sits in the DNA minor groove, and this diradical tears hydrogen atoms from the sugar backbone of both strands, causing double-strand DNA breaks. The cell arrests and dies by apoptosis. This does not depend on a specific cell-cycle phase.", "body": [["Bone marrow and blood", "CD22-positive leukemic blasts are killed, often leading to remission and measurable residual disease negativity."], ["Liver", "Hepatic veno-occlusive disease (sinusoidal obstruction syndrome) is a hallmark risk, as calicheamicin injures the cells lining liver sinusoids, blocking blood flow and causing liver swelling, fluid gain and jaundice."], ["Platelets and white cells", "Thrombocytopenia and neutropenia are common, raising bleeding and infection risk."], ["Heart", "QT prolongation can occur, so ECGs and electrolytes are monitored."], ["After transplant", "Non-relapse deaths after stem cell transplant are increased, largely from VOD."], ["Immune system", "Infusion reactions can occur, and premedication is used."]], "journey": "Inotuzumab ozogamicin is given by IV infusion on a weekly schedule within each cycle. It binds CD22 and is cleared partly by uptake into target cells, so its clearance changes as leukemia cells are removed. Its half-life at steady state is about 12 days. Calicheamicin is broken down mainly by nonenzymatic reduction, and the antibody by protein catabolism. Very little is cleared by the kidneys.", "sidefx": "VOD/SOS carries a boxed warning. Calicheamicin damages sinusoidal endothelial cells in the liver, which swell and block blood flow, causing liver enlargement, fluid retention and rising bilirubin. Risk is highest when stem cell transplant follows, especially with conditioning regimens that contain two alkylating agents. Defibrotide is a treatment used for severe VOD. Low counts reflect marrow toxicity and leukemia. QT prolongation is monitored with ECGs. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Calicheamicin is named after caliche, the hard calcium-rich soil of Texas where the bacterium that makes it was found.", "src": ["Besponsa (inotuzumab ozogamicin) FDA label", "Kantarjian HM et al. N Engl J Med 2016", "Lee MD et al. J Am Chem Soc 1987"]}, "gemtuzumab-ozogamicin": {"origin": "Gemtuzumab ozogamicin was the first antibody-drug conjugate ever approved. It was developed by Wyeth, combining an antibody against CD33, a protein on most acute myeloid leukemia blasts, with calicheamicin, a powerful DNA-breaking toxin discovered by Lederle scientists in the 1980s in a soil bacterium from Texas. The FDA granted accelerated approval to Mylotarg in May 2000 for older adults with relapsed AML. A later confirmatory trial did not show benefit and found more early deaths, so Pfizer voluntarily withdrew it in 2010. Further studies showed that smaller, split (fractionated) doses were safer and effective, and the FDA approved it again in September 2017 for newly diagnosed and relapsed CD33-positive AML.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Calicheamicin is discovered in a Texas soil bacterium."], ["2000", "FDA approves gemtuzumab ozogamicin (Mylotarg), the first antibody-drug conjugate."], ["2010", "Mylotarg is voluntarily withdrawn from the US market after a confirmatory trial fails."], ["2017", "FDA re-approves gemtuzumab ozogamicin with a lower fractionated dosing schedule."]], "cell": "The humanized IgG4 antibody binds CD33 (Siglec-3), a surface protein on myeloid cells and on blasts in most AML. CD33 is internalized after binding, carrying the conjugate into the cell. In the acidic lysosome, the hydrazone linker breaks and releases N-acetyl-gamma-calicheamicin. Reduction of calicheamicin's trisulfide by glutathione triggers Bergman cyclization of its enediyne core, producing a diradical that sits in the DNA minor groove and abstracts hydrogens from the sugar backbone. This causes double-strand DNA breaks, cell-cycle arrest and apoptosis. Normal myeloid precursors that carry CD33 are also killed, which explains prolonged low blood counts.", "body": [["Bone marrow and blood", "CD33-positive leukemic blasts are destroyed, increasing remission rates when combined with chemotherapy."], ["Platelets and white cells", "Normal CD33-positive myeloid cells are also killed, so neutropenia and thrombocytopenia can be prolonged."], ["Liver", "Hepatic veno-occlusive disease (sinusoidal obstruction syndrome) can occur from calicheamicin injury to the cells lining liver sinusoids."], ["Bleeding", "Low platelets can lead to serious hemorrhage, including in the brain."], ["Immune system", "Infusion reactions, including anaphylaxis, can occur, so premedication is used."], ["Mouth and gut", "Mucositis, nausea and fever are common as dividing lining cells are injured."]], "journey": "Gemtuzumab ozogamicin is given by IV infusion, usually as split doses on specific days. It is cleared partly by binding to CD33 on blasts, so levels and half-life are higher after the second dose when fewer blasts remain. Calicheamicin is inactivated mainly by nonenzymatic reduction, and the antibody is broken down by protein catabolism. Very little leaves through the kidneys.", "sidefx": "VOD/SOS carries a boxed warning: calicheamicin damages sinusoidal endothelial cells in the liver, causing blocked blood flow, liver enlargement, fluid gain and jaundice. Defibrotide is a treatment used for severe VOD. Prolonged low counts reflect killing of normal CD33-positive myeloid cells. Bleeding comes from low platelets. Infusion reactions come from the protein and from cytokine release as blasts die. The lower, fractionated dosing used today was chosen because it lowered these early toxicities. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Mylotarg's withdrawal and return is a classic story of how changing the dose schedule, not the molecule, can turn a failed drug into a useful one.", "src": ["Mylotarg (gemtuzumab ozogamicin) FDA label", "Castaigne S et al. ALFA-0701, Lancet 2012"]}, "belantamab-mafodotin": {"origin": "Belantamab mafodotin was developed by GlaxoSmithKline (GSK). It targets BCMA, a protein on myeloma cells, and carries MMAF, an auristatin related to the sea hare compound dolastatin 10. The FDA granted it accelerated approval as Blenrep in August 2020 for heavily pretreated myeloma, the first anti-BCMA therapy approved. In November 2022 GSK withdrew it from the US market after the DREAMM-3 confirmatory trial did not meet its main goal. Later trials combining it with standard myeloma drugs, including DREAMM-7 with bortezomib and dexamethasone, showed benefit, and it returned to the US market in 2025 in combination with bortezomib and dexamethasone, with a safety program for its eye effects.", "timeline": [["1987", "Dolastatin 10, parent of the auristatins, is reported from a sea hare."], ["2020", "FDA grants accelerated approval for belantamab mafodotin (Blenrep), the first BCMA-directed therapy."], ["2022", "Blenrep is withdrawn from the US market after DREAMM-3."], ["2025", "Blenrep returns to the US market with bortezomib and dexamethasone."]], "cell": "The humanized IgG1 antibody binds BCMA (TNFRSF17) on plasma cells and myeloma cells. The conjugate is internalized and fully digested in lysosomes, because its maleimidocaproyl linker cannot be cut, releasing cysteine-mcMMAF. MMAF inhibits tubulin polymerization, so the cell arrests in G2/M and undergoes apoptosis. MMAF carries a charged group that makes it poorly membrane permeable, so it stays trapped in the target cell, with little bystander killing. The antibody's Fc is afucosylated, which strengthens binding to Fc-gamma receptor IIIa on NK cells and enhances ADCC, and it also blocks BCMA survival signals.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "BCMA-positive myeloma cells are killed by the payload and by immune effector cells."], ["Eyes", "Keratopathy (changes in the corneal epithelium), blurred vision, dry eye and light sensitivity are very common, because corneal cells take up the conjugate nonspecifically."], ["Platelets", "Thrombocytopenia is common and can lead to bleeding."], ["Immune system", "Infections such as pneumonia and upper respiratory infections are common in treated myeloma."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and nausea can occur during treatment cycles."], ["Liver", "Liver toxicity with enzyme rises can occur and is monitored."]], "journey": "Belantamab mafodotin is given by IV infusion once every 3-week cycle, or less often depending on eye findings. The conjugate has a half-life of about 2 weeks. The antibody is broken down by protein catabolism, while released MMAF is barely metabolized and is eliminated mainly in feces. Because MMAF is not a major CYP substrate, drug interactions are few.", "sidefx": "Eye toxicity carries a boxed warning. Corneal epithelial cells take up the conjugate by a target-independent route, and the MMAF payload injures them, producing microcyst-like changes, blurred vision and, rarely, severe vision loss or corneal ulcers. Regular eye exams before each dose are required, and doses are held or spaced out when changes appear; most eye changes recover. Low platelets reflect marrow effects and disease. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Blenrep (belantamab mafodotin-blmf) FDA label", "Hungria V et al. DREAMM-7, N Engl J Med 2024"]}, "telisotuzumab-vedotin": {"origin": "Telisotuzumab vedotin was developed by AbbVie. Its antibody targets c-Met, the receptor for hepatocyte growth factor, which is overexpressed in a subset of non-small cell lung cancers. Earlier attempts to block c-Met with antibodies had not succeeded, so AbbVie used the antibody instead as a delivery vehicle for MMAE, the auristatin payload derived from the sea hare compound dolastatin 10. In 2025 the FDA granted accelerated approval to telisotuzumab vedotin (Emrelis) for previously treated advanced nonsquamous non-small cell lung cancer with high c-Met protein overexpression, the first c-Met-directed antibody-drug conjugate.", "timeline": [["1980s", "The MET gene and its receptor, c-Met, are discovered."], ["1987", "Dolastatin 10, parent of MMAE, is reported from a sea hare."], ["2025", "FDA grants accelerated approval for telisotuzumab vedotin (Emrelis)."]], "cell": "The humanized IgG1 antibody binds c-Met (the MET receptor tyrosine kinase) on tumor cells. The conjugate is internalized and moved to lysosomes, where proteases cut the valine-citrulline linker and release MMAE. MMAE binds tubulin and blocks microtubule polymerization, so the cell arrests in G2/M and dies by apoptosis. Freed MMAE can enter neighboring tumor cells (bystander effect). Patients are selected using an FDA-approved test showing high c-Met protein, meaning strong staining in at least half of tumor cells. Protein overexpression differs from MET exon 14 skipping mutations, which are treated with MET kinase inhibitors.", "body": [["Lung tumor", "c-Met-rich cancer cells are killed during division."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral neuropathy is the most common side effect, from MMAE disrupting microtubule transport in nerves."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease or pneumonitis can occur and can be fatal."], ["Eyes", "Keratitis and blurred vision can occur from payload effects on the eye surface."], ["Fluid balance", "Peripheral edema (swelling of the legs and feet) is common."], ["General", "Fatigue and decreased appetite are common."]], "journey": "Telisotuzumab vedotin is given by IV infusion every 2 weeks. The conjugate has a half-life of several days. Released MMAE is metabolized mainly by CYP3A4 and leaves mostly through bile into feces. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors can raise MMAE exposure. The antibody part is broken down by protein catabolism.", "sidefx": "Neuropathy reflects MMAE damage to the microtubules that move material along nerve fibers, and it is managed by holding or reducing doses. Pneumonitis is the most serious risk and is treated by stopping the drug and giving corticosteroids. Eye surface problems and infusion reactions can occur. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Emrelis (telisotuzumab vedotin-tllv) FDA label", "LUMINOSITY trial (Camidge DR et al.)"]}, "tagraxofusp": {"origin": "Tagraxofusp borrows its killing power from diphtheria toxin, the poison made by Corynebacterium diphtheriae that Emile Roux and Alexandre Yersin showed in 1888 could cause disease on its own. Researchers later learned to cut off the part of the toxin that binds normal cells and replace it with a human hormone, so the toxin would enter only cells carrying that hormone's receptor. Academic work led by Arthur Frankel and colleagues joined truncated diphtheria toxin to interleukin-3, aiming at cells with the IL-3 receptor (CD123). Stemline Therapeutics developed it, and in December 2018 the FDA approved tagraxofusp (Elzonris) for blastic plasmacytoid dendritic cell neoplasm (BPDCN), a rare, aggressive blood cancer whose cells carry very high levels of CD123. It was the first drug approved for BPDCN and the first CD123-directed therapy.", "timeline": [["1888", "Roux and Yersin show that diphtheria bacteria release a toxin."], ["1990s", "Researchers build fusion toxins that replace diphtheria toxin's binding domain with human cytokines."], ["2018", "FDA approves tagraxofusp (Elzonris), the first treatment for BPDCN."]], "cell": "Tagraxofusp is a recombinant fusion protein of human IL-3 and a truncated diphtheria toxin that keeps the catalytic (A) and translocation domains but lacks the native receptor-binding domain. The IL-3 portion binds CD123, the IL-3 receptor alpha chain, which is strongly expressed on BPDCN cells. The complex is taken into the cell by endocytosis. In the acidic endosome, the translocation domain inserts into the membrane and moves the catalytic fragment into the cytoplasm. There the catalytic domain uses NAD+ to ADP-ribosylate diphthamide, a modified histidine on elongation factor 2 (EF-2). Inactivated EF-2 can no longer move ribosomes along messenger RNA, so protein synthesis stops and the cell dies by apoptosis. A single toxin molecule can inactivate many EF-2 molecules. It works regardless of cell-cycle phase.", "body": [["Blood and skin cancer sites", "CD123-rich BPDCN cells in skin, marrow and lymph nodes are killed."], ["Blood vessels", "Capillary leak syndrome can occur because the toxin injures endothelial cells, letting fluid and albumin seep into tissues."], ["Fluid balance", "Low albumin, edema and rapid weight gain are early signs of capillary leak."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises are common and may reflect injury to liver cells."], ["Platelets", "Low platelets can occur, raising bleeding risk."], ["Immune system", "Hypersensitivity reactions and fever can occur."]], "journey": "Tagraxofusp is given by IV infusion once daily for 5 days in each cycle. It is a small protein that is cleared very quickly, with a half-life of under an hour, which is why it is given on consecutive days. It is broken down by protein catabolism and not by CYP enzymes, and little is cleared unchanged by the kidneys. The first cycle is given in a hospital so that early capillary leak can be detected.", "sidefx": "Capillary leak syndrome carries a boxed warning. Diphtheria toxin damages endothelial cells, so plasma fluid and albumin leak out of vessels, causing edema, weight gain, low albumin and low blood pressure, and it can be fatal. Albumin, weight and blood pressure are monitored, and doses are held with albumin replacement and diuretics when warning signs appear. Liver enzyme rises reflect liver injury. Hypersensitivity comes from the foreign protein. There is no antidote.", "fun": "BPDCN was long confused with leukemia or lymphoma. Its cells are now known to come from plasmacytoid dendritic cells, the immune cells that make large amounts of interferon during viral infections.", "src": ["Elzonris (tagraxofusp-erzs) FDA label", "Pemmaraju N et al. N Engl J Med 2019"]}, "denileukin-diftitox": {"origin": "Denileukin diftitox combines interleukin-2 with fragments of diphtheria toxin, an idea pioneered by John R. Murphy and colleagues at Boston University, who showed that replacing the toxin's own binding domain with IL-2 would steer it to activated T cells carrying IL-2 receptors. The original product, Ontak, was developed by Seragen and approved by the FDA in 1999 for cutaneous T-cell lymphoma. It was the first fusion protein toxin approved for cancer. Ontak was taken off the market in 2014 because of manufacturing problems. A new, more purified version made by Citius Pharmaceuticals was approved in August 2024 as Lymphir for relapsed or refractory cutaneous T-cell lymphoma.", "timeline": [["1888", "Roux and Yersin show that diphtheria bacteria release a toxin."], ["1980s", "Murphy and colleagues create IL-2 to diphtheria toxin fusion proteins."], ["1999", "FDA approves denileukin diftitox (Ontak) for cutaneous T-cell lymphoma."], ["2014", "Ontak is discontinued because of manufacturing problems."], ["2024", "FDA approves a new formulation, denileukin diftitox-cxdl (Lymphir)."]], "cell": "Denileukin diftitox is a recombinant fusion of human IL-2 and the catalytic (A) and translocation (B) fragments of diphtheria toxin. The IL-2 portion binds the IL-2 receptor, especially the high-affinity complex containing CD25 (IL-2R alpha), which is present on malignant T cells in many cutaneous T-cell lymphomas and on regulatory T cells. After binding, the complex is taken up by endocytosis. In the acidic endosome the translocation fragment moves the catalytic fragment into the cytoplasm, where it ADP-ribosylates diphthamide on elongation factor 2 using NAD+. This stops protein synthesis and the cell dies. Depleting regulatory T cells may also lift some immune suppression against the tumor.", "body": [["Skin", "Malignant T cells in skin patches, plaques and tumors are killed, so lesions improve."], ["Blood vessels", "Capillary leak syndrome occurs because the toxin damages endothelial cells, letting fluid escape into tissues."], ["Fluid balance", "Edema, low albumin and weight gain are common signs of vascular leak."], ["Immune system", "Infusion reactions with fever, chills and low blood pressure are common, especially with the first doses."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises are common and usually appear in the first cycles."], ["Eyes", "Vision changes, including loss of color vision, have been reported."]], "journey": "Denileukin diftitox is given by IV infusion once daily for 5 days in each cycle. Like other small fusion proteins it is cleared quickly, with a half-life of about an hour or less. It is broken down by protein catabolism, not by liver enzymes, and little is removed unchanged by the kidneys. Antibodies against the drug commonly develop over repeated cycles.", "sidefx": "Capillary leak syndrome, which carries a boxed warning, comes from diphtheria toxin injury to endothelial cells, so fluid and albumin leak out and cause edema, low blood pressure and weight gain. Albumin and weight are monitored and doses are held when leak develops. Infusion reactions come from the foreign protein and cytokine release, and premedication is used. Liver enzyme rises and vision changes can occur. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Lymphir (denileukin diftitox-cxdl) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "fulvestrant": {"origin": "Fulvestrant was discovered at Imperial Chemical Industries (ICI) in England, the same company that developed tamoxifen. A team led by Alan Wakeling searched for a 'pure' antiestrogen that, unlike tamoxifen, would have no estrogen-like effects at all. They found that adding a long side chain to the 7-alpha position of estradiol produced a molecule that blocked the receptor completely and caused it to be destroyed. The compound, first called ICI 182,780, was reported in 1991. ICI's pharmaceutical business became Zeneca and then AstraZeneca, which brought fulvestrant (Faslodex) to FDA approval in 2002 for hormone receptor-positive advanced breast cancer. It was the first selective estrogen receptor degrader (SERD).", "timeline": [["1960s", "ICI develops tamoxifen, the first major antiestrogen."], ["1991", "Wakeling and colleagues report ICI 182,780, a pure antiestrogen."], ["2002", "FDA approves fulvestrant (Faslodex) for advanced breast cancer."], ["2017", "FDA approves fulvestrant as first-line therapy for endocrine-naive advanced breast cancer."]], "cell": "In hormone receptor-positive breast cancer, estradiol binds estrogen receptor alpha (ER-alpha), which pairs up (dimerizes), moves into the nucleus and switches on genes for cell growth, such as cyclin D1. Fulvestrant is a steroidal estradiol analog with a long 7-alpha alkylsulfinyl side chain. It binds ER-alpha competitively with high affinity. The bulky side chain disrupts the receptor's shape, blocking dimerization, nuclear localization and the activation functions AF-1 and AF-2. The misfolded receptor is then tagged and destroyed by the proteasome, so the cell has far less ER to respond to any estrogen. Unlike tamoxifen, it has no partial agonist activity, including in the endometrium.", "body": [["Breast cancer", "Tumor cells lose estrogen receptor signaling and stop growing or shrink."], ["Endometrium", "Because it is a pure antagonist, it does not stimulate the uterine lining as tamoxifen can."], ["Blood vessels and brain", "Hot flashes reflect loss of estrogen signaling in temperature-regulating centers."], ["Injection sites", "Injection-site pain is common, and large-volume gluteal injections can rarely irritate the sciatic nerve."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises can occur because the liver metabolizes the drug."], ["Muscles and joints", "Musculoskeletal pain and fatigue are common."]], "journey": "Fulvestrant is given as an intramuscular injection into the buttock, as two injections at each dosing visit. It is not effective by mouth. The oil-based depot releases drug slowly, with peak levels about 7 days after injection and a long half-life of about 40 days. Steady state comes within about a month because of a loading schedule. It is about 99 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down, including by CYP3A4 and conjugation, and it leaves mostly in feces through bile, with very little in urine.", "sidefx": "Hot flashes, fatigue and joint pain come from loss of estrogen signaling. Injection-site pain and rare sciatic nerve irritation come from the large intramuscular depot. Because it is given into muscle, it can cause bleeding or bruising in people with low platelets or on anticoagulants. Liver enzyme rises reflect its hepatic metabolism. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Fulvestrant's discovery helped inspire a new generation of oral SERDs designed to destroy the estrogen receptor without the need for large injections.", "src": ["Faslodex (fulvestrant) FDA label", "Wakeling AE et al. Cancer Res 1991", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "goserelin": {"origin": "Goserelin grew out of the discovery of gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH). In 1971 Andrew Schally's lab determined the structure of this 10-amino-acid hypothalamic hormone, and Roger Guillemin's lab worked on hypothalamic hormones in parallel; they shared the 1977 Nobel Prize. Researchers then found that giving GnRH continuously, rather than in natural pulses, paradoxically shut the pituitary down. Chemists at ICI in England made goserelin, a more stable and potent GnRH analog, and designed a biodegradable implant that releases it steadily. The FDA approved Zoladex in 1989 for advanced prostate cancer. It was later approved for advanced breast cancer, endometriosis and endometrial thinning.", "timeline": [["1971", "Andrew Schally's lab determines the structure of GnRH."], ["1977", "Schally and Guillemin share the Nobel Prize for hypothalamic hormones."], ["1989", "FDA approves the goserelin implant (Zoladex) for advanced prostate cancer."]], "cell": "Native GnRH is released from the hypothalamus in pulses and binds Gq-coupled GnRH receptors on pituitary gonadotroph cells, causing LH and FSH release. Goserelin is a synthetic decapeptide GnRH agonist with amino acid changes that resist breakdown and bind the receptor more strongly. At first it acts like a strong burst of GnRH, so LH, FSH and testosterone or estradiol rise for about 1 to 2 weeks (the flare). With continuous rather than pulsatile stimulation, gonadotroph cells internalize and downregulate their receptors and uncouple them from signaling. LH and FSH fall, and testosterone drops to castrate levels in men and estradiol to postmenopausal levels in women within about 2 to 4 weeks. This is called medical castration.", "body": [["Prostate cancer", "Hormone-sensitive prostate cancer cells lose testosterone and stop growing or shrink."], ["Breast and uterus", "Low estradiol slows hormone-sensitive breast cancer and shrinks endometriosis and the uterine lining."], ["Brain and skin", "Hot flashes and sweating come from low sex hormones affecting the temperature-regulating center."], ["Bones", "Low sex hormones speed bone loss, raising fracture risk over time."], ["Metabolism and heart", "Androgen deprivation can raise blood sugar and weight and is linked to diabetes and cardiovascular risk."], ["Sexual function", "Low libido and erectile dysfunction in men, and vaginal dryness in women, follow hormone loss."]], "journey": "Goserelin is placed as a small solid implant under the skin of the abdominal wall with a special syringe. The implant is made of a biodegradable polymer that slowly releases the peptide over 28 days or 3 months, depending on the product. Released goserelin itself has a short half-life of a few hours, but the steady release maintains suppression. The peptide is broken down by hydrolysis and leaves mainly in urine.", "sidefx": "Hot flashes, sexual dysfunction, bone loss and metabolic changes come directly from medical castration. The early flare in testosterone can temporarily worsen prostate cancer, causing bone pain, urinary blockage or spinal cord compression in men with spine metastases, which is why an antiandrogen is often started first. Injection of the implant can rarely injure abdominal wall vessels and cause serious bleeding, especially in thin people or those on anticoagulants. There is no antidote; effects reverse slowly as the implant is used up.", "fun": "The paradox that a hormone 'gas pedal' can act as a brake when pressed constantly was one of the key insights of reproductive endocrinology.", "src": ["Zoladex (goserelin implant) FDA label", "Schally AV et al. Science 1971", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "triptorelin": {"origin": "Triptorelin is one of the GnRH agonists that followed the 1971 discovery of the structure of gonadotropin-releasing hormone in Andrew Schally's lab. Researchers found that swapping the glycine at position 6 of GnRH for a D-amino acid made the peptide resist breakdown and bind its receptor more tightly. Triptorelin carries D-tryptophan at that position. It was developed in Europe and brought to market in long-acting depot forms by the Swiss company Debiopharm and its partners. In the US it was approved as Trelstar in 2000 for palliative treatment of advanced prostate cancer, and a separate product, Triptodur, was later approved for central precocious puberty in children.", "timeline": [["1971", "Schally's lab determines the structure of GnRH."], ["1970s", "D-amino acid substitutions at position 6 produce long-acting GnRH agonists such as triptorelin."], ["2000", "FDA approves triptorelin (Trelstar) for advanced prostate cancer."], ["2017", "FDA approves Triptodur for central precocious puberty."]], "cell": "Triptorelin is a synthetic GnRH decapeptide with D-tryptophan at position 6, which slows its breakdown and increases its affinity for the pituitary GnRH receptor, a Gq-coupled receptor on gonadotroph cells. At first it acts as a strong agonist, causing a surge of LH and FSH and a rise in testosterone during the first week (the flare). Continuous, non-pulsatile exposure then causes the gonadotrophs to internalize and downregulate their GnRH receptors and uncouple them from signaling. LH and FSH secretion falls, and testosterone drops to castrate levels in about 2 to 4 weeks. Prostate cancer cells that depend on testosterone stop growing.", "body": [["Prostate cancer", "Testosterone falls to castrate levels, so hormone-sensitive prostate cancer stops growing or shrinks."], ["Brain and skin", "Hot flashes and sweating follow loss of testosterone."], ["Bones", "Long-term low testosterone causes bone loss and raises fracture risk."], ["Metabolism", "Weight gain and higher blood sugar can occur with androgen deprivation."], ["Heart", "Androgen deprivation is linked to cardiovascular events and can prolong the QT interval."], ["Mood and sexual function", "Depression, low libido and erectile dysfunction can occur."]], "journey": "Triptorelin is given as an intramuscular injection of a slow-release depot. Peak blood levels occur within hours, and then the depot keeps releasing drug for 4, 12 or 24 weeks depending on the product strength. Triptorelin itself has a short terminal half-life of a few hours after IV dosing, so the depot design is what keeps suppression steady. It is broken down by peptidases, not by CYP enzymes, and is eliminated by both the kidneys and the liver.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from low testosterone: hot flashes, sexual dysfunction, bone loss, weight gain and higher blood sugar. The early testosterone flare can briefly worsen prostate cancer, causing bone pain, urinary blockage or spinal cord compression in men with spinal metastases, so close monitoring or an antiandrogen is used early. QT prolongation and cardiovascular risks are linked to androgen deprivation. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Triptorelin is also used in other formulations outside prostate cancer, including as a trigger for egg maturation in IVF, because a single burst of GnRH agonist causes a natural LH surge.", "src": ["Trelstar (triptorelin pamoate) FDA label", "Schally AV et al. Science 1971", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "degarelix": {"origin": "GnRH agonists such as leuprolide lower testosterone only after a temporary surge, which can be dangerous in men with advanced prostate cancer. Researchers therefore worked for decades on GnRH antagonists that would block the receptor directly. Early antagonists caused allergic reactions by releasing histamine. Degarelix was designed as a peptide antagonist with much less histamine release, and it forms a slow-release gel depot under the skin. An earlier antagonist, abarelix, reached the US market but was withdrawn, largely because of allergic reactions. Degarelix was developed by the Danish company Ferring Pharmaceuticals and approved by the FDA in December 2008 as Firmagon for advanced prostate cancer.", "timeline": [["1971", "The structure of GnRH is determined in Andrew Schally's lab."], ["1990s", "Researchers develop GnRH antagonists with less histamine release."], ["2008", "FDA approves degarelix (Firmagon) for advanced prostate cancer."]], "cell": "Degarelix is a synthetic decapeptide that binds GnRH receptors on pituitary gonadotroph cells competitively and reversibly. Unlike an agonist, it does not activate the receptor at all, so there is no initial burst of LH and FSH release. LH and FSH fall immediately, and with less LH reaching the testes, Leydig cells make far less testosterone. Testosterone reaches castrate levels within about 3 days, much faster than with GnRH agonists. Because there is no flare, there is no early worsening of prostate cancer. After injection the drug forms a gel depot under the skin that slowly releases it.", "body": [["Prostate cancer", "Rapid testosterone suppression stops hormone-sensitive prostate cancer growth, which is useful when quick control is needed."], ["Injection site", "Pain, redness and swelling at the injection site are very common because the drug forms a depot under the skin."], ["Brain and skin", "Hot flashes come from loss of testosterone."], ["Bones", "Long-term androgen deprivation causes bone loss and fracture risk."], ["Heart", "Androgen deprivation can prolong the QT interval and is linked to cardiovascular risk."], ["Metabolism and liver", "Weight gain is common, and liver enzyme rises can occur."]], "journey": "Degarelix is injected under the skin of the abdomen. Once injected, it forms a gel depot from which it is released slowly. Peak blood levels occur within about 2 days, and maintenance doses last about 28 days. The apparent half-life after the starting dose is about 53 days, reflecting slow release from the depot. It is about 90 percent protein bound. It is broken down by peptide hydrolysis in the hepatobiliary system, not by CYP enzymes, and leaves mostly in feces with some in urine.", "sidefx": "Injection-site reactions come from the depot forming under the skin. Hot flashes, weight gain, bone loss and sexual side effects reflect testosterone suppression. QT prolongation is a risk of androgen deprivation itself, so caution is used with other QT-prolonging drugs and with electrolyte problems. Hypersensitivity can occur, though histamine release is lower than with older antagonists. There is no antidote; effects fade as the depot is used up.", "fun": "Because there is no flare, degarelix and other antagonists do not need an antiandrogen started beforehand to prevent early tumor growth.", "src": ["Firmagon (degarelix) FDA label", "Klotz L et al. BJU Int 2008", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "tisagenlecleucel": {"origin": "Tisagenlecleucel was the first CAR-T cell therapy approved in the US. The idea of giving T cells an artificial receptor came from Zelig Eshhar in Israel, who described 'T-bodies' in 1989. Later researchers added costimulatory domains, including the 4-1BB domain used in this product, from work by Dario Campana and colleagues at St. Jude Children's Research Hospital. At the University of Pennsylvania, Carl June, David Porter, Bruce Levine and colleagues treated adults with CLL in 2010, and in 2012 Stephan Grupp's team at the Children's Hospital of Philadelphia treated Emily Whitehead, a 6-year-old girl with relapsed ALL, who went into a remission that has lasted for years. Novartis developed the therapy, and the FDA approved Kymriah in August 2017 for children and young adults with relapsed ALL. It was also the first gene therapy approved in the US.", "timeline": [["1989", "Zelig Eshhar describes T cells with chimeric antigen receptors."], ["2004", "Campana's lab reports a CD19 CAR with a 4-1BB costimulatory domain."], ["2011", "Penn researchers report durable remissions in CLL with CD19 CAR-T cells."], ["2012", "Emily Whitehead becomes the first child with ALL treated with this CAR-T therapy."], ["2017", "FDA approves tisagenlecleucel (Kymriah), the first CAR-T and first US gene therapy."]], "cell": "T cells collected from the patient are transduced with a lentiviral vector carrying a chimeric antigen receptor gene. The CAR has an anti-CD19 single-chain variable fragment on the outside, a CD8 hinge and transmembrane region, and inside the cell a 4-1BB (CD137) costimulatory domain and the CD3-zeta signaling domain. When the CAR binds CD19 on a B cell, CD3-zeta delivers the activation signal and 4-1BB delivers the second signal that supports survival and memory. This works without MHC antigen presentation. Activated CAR-T cells kill CD19-positive cells with perforin and granzymes, release cytokines such as IL-6 and IFN-gamma and multiply many times over. The 4-1BB domain is linked to slower expansion and longer persistence than CD28-based products.", "body": [["Bone marrow and blood", "Leukemic and lymphoma B cells are destroyed, often leading to deep remission."], ["Immune system", "Normal B cells are also destroyed (B-cell aplasia), which can last as long as the CAR-T cells persist, causing low antibody levels and infections."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome with fever, low blood pressure and low oxygen occurs as CAR-T cells expand."], ["Brain", "ICANS can cause confusion, trouble speaking, tremor, seizures and rarely cerebral edema."], ["Bone marrow (normal)", "Low blood counts can persist for weeks after infusion, partly from lymphodepleting chemotherapy."], ["Immune activation", "Rarely, HLH/MAS, an extreme inflammatory state, can develop."]], "journey": "The patient's T cells are collected by leukapheresis, shipped to a manufacturing site, engineered and expanded over weeks, then frozen and sent back. Before infusion, lymphodepleting chemotherapy (usually fludarabine and cyclophosphamide) clears space for the new cells. After a single IV infusion, the CAR-T cells travel through blood to the marrow, lymph nodes and even the cerebrospinal fluid. They expand rapidly when they meet CD19, peaking around 10 days, and then contract, but some can persist for months to years. There is no metabolism in the usual sense.", "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome is caused by massive CAR-T activation and release of IL-6 and other cytokines. It is treated with tocilizumab, an IL-6 receptor antibody, and corticosteroids. Neurotoxicity (ICANS) is thought to involve cytokines and endothelial activation in the brain and is treated with corticosteroids. B-cell aplasia causes low antibody levels, treated with immunoglobulin replacement. Prolonged low counts raise infection risk. A boxed warning also notes rare T-cell cancers after CAR-T therapy.", "fun": "The lentiviral vector used to make these CAR-T cells is derived from HIV, with the genes needed to cause disease removed, so a disabled virus is used to rewire T cells against cancer.", "src": ["Kymriah (tisagenlecleucel) FDA label", "Maude SL et al. N Engl J Med 2014", "Eshhar Z et al. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA 1993"]}, "axicabtagene-ciloleucel": {"origin": "Axicabtagene ciloleucel grew out of work at the National Cancer Institute, where Steven Rosenberg and James Kochenderfer built an anti-CD19 CAR with a CD28 costimulatory domain. In a 2010 report, Kochenderfer and colleagues described a patient with advanced follicular lymphoma whose disease regressed after treatment, one of the first CD19 CAR-T successes. Kite Pharma licensed the technology and developed it for large B-cell lymphoma. The FDA approved Yescarta in October 2017, the second CAR-T therapy and the first for adults with non-Hodgkin lymphoma. Gilead Sciences bought Kite the same year. In 2022 the ZUMA-7 trial led to its approval as second-line therapy for large B-cell lymphoma that relapses early.", "timeline": [["1989", "Zelig Eshhar describes the chimeric antigen receptor concept."], ["2010", "Kochenderfer and Rosenberg at NCI report regression of lymphoma with anti-CD19 CAR-T cells."], ["2017", "FDA approves axicabtagene ciloleucel (Yescarta) for large B-cell lymphoma."], ["2017", "Gilead Sciences acquires Kite Pharma."], ["2022", "FDA approves Yescarta as second-line therapy for early relapsing LBCL."]], "cell": "The patient's T cells are transduced with a gammaretroviral vector carrying a CAR gene. The CAR contains a murine anti-CD19 single-chain variable fragment (FMC63), linked to a CD28 hinge, transmembrane and costimulatory domain and a CD3-zeta signaling domain. Binding CD19 on B cells triggers CD3-zeta signaling, while CD28 provides strong costimulation. This drives rapid T-cell activation and proliferation, release of cytokines including IL-6, IFN-gamma and GM-CSF, and MHC-independent killing of CD19-positive cells with perforin and granzymes. CD28-based CARs tend to expand faster and to higher peaks than 4-1BB-based CARs, which is linked to faster responses but more early toxicity.", "body": [["Lymph nodes and tumor", "CD19-positive lymphoma cells are destroyed, and about 4 in 10 patients in key trials had long-lasting remissions."], ["Immune system", "Normal B cells are destroyed, causing low antibody levels and infections."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome with fever, fast heart rate and low blood pressure is very common, usually starting within 2 to 3 days."], ["Brain", "Neurotoxicity (ICANS), including confusion, trouble speaking, tremor and seizures, is more common than with 4-1BB products."], ["Bone marrow", "Low blood counts can last for weeks after treatment."], ["Infections", "Febrile neutropenia and bacterial, viral and fungal infections can occur."]], "journey": "T cells are collected by leukapheresis, engineered and expanded at a central facility, then frozen and shipped back, a process taking a few weeks. The patient receives lymphodepleting chemotherapy with fludarabine and cyclophosphamide, then a single IV infusion of the cells. The CAR-T cells travel to lymph nodes, marrow and other tissues, expand rapidly with a peak between 7 and 14 days, and then contract. Some persist for months. They are not metabolized like drugs.", "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome comes from rapid activation and expansion of CAR-T cells releasing IL-6 and other cytokines. It is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids. Neurotoxicity is treated mainly with corticosteroids and supportive care, and seizure prevention may be used. B-cell aplasia lowers antibody levels, so immunoglobulin replacement may be needed. Prolonged low blood counts raise infection risk. A boxed warning notes rare T-cell cancers after CAR-T therapy.", "src": ["Yescarta (axicabtagene ciloleucel) FDA label", "Kochenderfer JN et al. Blood 2010", "Neelapu SS et al. ZUMA-1, N Engl J Med 2017"]}, "brexucabtagene-autoleucel": {"origin": "Brexucabtagene autoleucel was developed by Kite Pharma (part of Gilead) using the same CD28-based anti-CD19 CAR as axicabtagene ciloleucel, which came from National Cancer Institute research. The difference lies in manufacturing: an extra T-cell enrichment step removes circulating cancerous B cells from the collected blood, which matters in diseases like mantle cell lymphoma and ALL where tumor cells often circulate. In July 2020 the FDA approved Tecartus for relapsed or refractory mantle cell lymphoma, the first CAR-T therapy for that disease. In October 2021 it became the first CAR-T approved for adults with relapsed or refractory B-cell ALL.", "timeline": [["2010", "NCI researchers report early success with CD28-based anti-CD19 CAR-T cells."], ["2020", "FDA approves brexucabtagene autoleucel (Tecartus) for mantle cell lymphoma."], ["2021", "FDA approves Tecartus for adults with relapsed or refractory B-cell ALL."]], "cell": "The patient's T cells are enriched, then transduced with a gammaretroviral vector carrying the same CAR as axi-cel: a murine anti-CD19 single-chain variable fragment (FMC63), a CD28 costimulatory domain and the CD3-zeta signaling domain. Enrichment removes CD19-positive tumor cells from the starting product, so they do not interfere with manufacturing by activating the CAR-T cells too early. After infusion, CAR binding to CD19 drives strong T-cell activation and expansion, release of cytokines and MHC-independent killing of CD19-positive B cells by perforin and granzymes.", "body": [["Lymph nodes, blood and marrow", "Mantle cell lymphoma and ALL cells are destroyed, often leading to deep remissions."], ["Immune system", "Normal B cells are lost, lowering antibody levels and raising infection risk."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome with fever and low blood pressure is very common."], ["Brain", "Neurotoxicity is frequent and often severe, especially in ALL, with confusion, trouble speaking, tremor and seizures."], ["Bone marrow", "Low blood counts can last for weeks or longer."], ["Infections", "Bacterial, viral and fungal infections are common during recovery."]], "journey": "T cells are collected by leukapheresis, enriched, engineered and expanded over a few weeks, then frozen and shipped back. After lymphodepleting chemotherapy with fludarabine and cyclophosphamide, the cells are given as a single IV infusion. CAR-T cells home to marrow, lymph nodes and blood, peak in about 1 to 2 weeks, then contract while some persist for months. They are not metabolized like small-molecule drugs.", "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome results from rapid CAR-T activation and cytokine release and is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids. Neurotoxicity is treated with corticosteroids and supportive care and is especially common in adult ALL. B-cell aplasia causes low antibody levels, sometimes needing immunoglobulin replacement. Prolonged low counts raise infection risk. A boxed warning notes rare T-cell cancers after CAR-T therapy.", "src": ["Tecartus (brexucabtagene autoleucel) FDA label", "Wang M et al. ZUMA-2, N Engl J Med 2020"]}, "lisocabtagene-maraleucel": {"origin": "Lisocabtagene maraleucel came from research at the Fred Hutchinson Cancer Research Center, Memorial Sloan Kettering and Seattle Children's Research Institute, which founded Juno Therapeutics in 2013. A key idea was to give CD8 killer T cells and CD4 helper T cells in a defined 1:1 ratio, aiming for more consistent results from patient to patient. Celgene bought Juno in 2018 and was itself acquired by Bristol Myers Squibb in 2019. The FDA approved Breyanzi in February 2021 for relapsed or refractory large B-cell lymphoma. Later approvals made it the first CAR-T approved for chronic lymphocytic leukemia in 2024, along with follicular, mantle cell and marginal zone lymphoma.", "timeline": [["2013", "Juno Therapeutics is founded by Seattle and New York cancer centers."], ["2018", "Celgene acquires Juno."], ["2019", "Bristol Myers Squibb acquires Celgene."], ["2021", "FDA approves lisocabtagene maraleucel (Breyanzi) for large B-cell lymphoma."], ["2024", "Breyanzi becomes the first CAR-T approved for CLL/SLL."]], "cell": "CD8 and CD4 T cells are separated from the patient's blood and each is transduced with a lentiviral vector encoding a CAR: an anti-CD19 single-chain variable fragment (FMC63), an IgG4 hinge, a CD28 transmembrane region, a 4-1BB costimulatory domain and the CD3-zeta signaling domain. A truncated EGFR tag is also expressed as a marker. The two cell types are given in equal numbers. CD19 binding activates the CAR-T cells to expand, release cytokines and kill CD19-positive cells without MHC presentation. CD4 cells help CD8 cells expand and persist. The 4-1BB domain is linked to steadier expansion and lower rates of severe CRS than CD28-based CARs.", "body": [["Lymph nodes, blood and marrow", "CD19-positive lymphoma and CLL cells are destroyed."], ["Immune system", "Normal B cells are also destroyed, lowering antibody levels and raising infection risk."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome is common but usually lower grade, starting about 5 days after infusion on average."], ["Brain", "Neurotoxicity, including confusion and tremor, can occur and occasionally is severe."], ["Bone marrow", "Low blood counts can last for weeks after infusion."], ["Infections", "Serious infections can occur during recovery."]], "journey": "T cells are collected by leukapheresis, separated into CD8 and CD4 cells, engineered and expanded over a few weeks, then frozen and returned as two components. After lymphodepleting chemotherapy with fludarabine and cyclophosphamide, the CD8 and CD4 components are infused IV one after the other. The cells home to lymph nodes, marrow and blood, peak in about 10 to 14 days, then contract while some persist for months. Its lower rate of severe early toxicity has allowed treatment in outpatient settings at experienced centers.", "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome follows CAR-T activation and IL-6 release and is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids. Neurotoxicity is treated with corticosteroids. B-cell aplasia causes low antibody levels. Prolonged low counts raise infection risk. A boxed warning notes rare T-cell cancers after CAR-T therapy. Severe CRS is less common than with CD28-based products, consistent with the slower 4-1BB signaling.", "src": ["Breyanzi (lisocabtagene maraleucel) FDA label", "Abramson JS et al. TRANSCEND NHL 001, Lancet 2020"]}, "idecabtagene-vicleucel": {"origin": "Idecabtagene vicleucel was developed by bluebird bio in partnership with Celgene, later Bristol Myers Squibb. It applied the CAR-T approach to multiple myeloma by targeting BCMA (B-cell maturation antigen), a protein found on plasma cells and myeloma cells but not on most other tissues. In March 2021 the FDA approved Abecma, the first CAR-T cell therapy for multiple myeloma, for patients whose disease had relapsed after several prior lines of therapy. It was the first cell-based gene therapy approved for this disease.", "timeline": [["1990s", "BCMA is identified on mature B cells and plasma cells."], ["2013", "Researchers identify BCMA as a promising CAR-T target in myeloma."], ["2021", "FDA approves idecabtagene vicleucel (Abecma), the first CAR-T for multiple myeloma."]], "cell": "The patient's T cells are transduced with a lentiviral vector encoding a CAR made of a murine anti-BCMA single-chain variable fragment, a CD8 hinge and transmembrane domain, a 4-1BB costimulatory domain and the CD3-zeta signaling domain. When the CAR binds BCMA (TNFRSF17) on plasma cells, the T cell is activated without MHC presentation. It releases perforin and granzymes, killing the myeloma cell, secretes cytokines and multiplies. Because normal plasma cells carry BCMA, they are also eliminated, which lowers antibody production.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Myeloma cells are destroyed, often lowering paraprotein to very low levels."], ["Immune system", "Normal plasma cells are lost, causing low antibody levels and infections."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome is very common and often starts within a day of infusion."], ["Bone marrow (normal)", "Low neutrophils, platelets and red cells can last for weeks to months, partly from lymphodepleting chemotherapy and inflammation."], ["Brain", "Neurotoxicity can occur, including confusion and tremor."], ["Immune activation", "HLH/MAS, an extreme inflammatory state, can occur and be fatal."]], "journey": "T cells are collected by leukapheresis, engineered and expanded over a few weeks, then frozen and shipped back. After lymphodepleting chemotherapy with fludarabine and cyclophosphamide, the cells are given as one IV infusion. They home to the marrow and lymphoid tissues, expand to a peak around 11 days and then contract, with some persisting for months. They are not metabolized like drugs.", "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome is caused by CAR-T activation and cytokine release and is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids. Neurotoxicity is treated with corticosteroids. Prolonged low blood counts are a major risk and can lead to bleeding and infection. Loss of normal plasma cells causes low antibody levels, treated with immunoglobulin replacement. HLH/MAS reflects runaway immune activation. A boxed warning notes rare T-cell cancers after CAR-T therapy.", "src": ["Abecma (idecabtagene vicleucel) FDA label", "Munshi NC et al. KarMMa, N Engl J Med 2021"]}, "ciltacabtagene-autoleucel": {"origin": "Ciltacabtagene autoleucel was developed by Legend Biotech, a company based in Nanjing, China, and later in partnership with Janssen (Johnson & Johnson). Its CAR is unusual: instead of a standard antibody fragment, it uses two single-domain antibodies (VHH fragments) of the kind made naturally by camels and llamas, each binding a different part of BCMA, which gives a very strong grip on myeloma cells. Early results from China were striking. The FDA approved Carvykti in February 2022 for heavily pretreated multiple myeloma, and in 2024 for earlier use in lenalidomide-refractory disease after at least one prior line.", "timeline": [["1993", "Belgian researchers report that camels make antibodies with only heavy chains."], ["2017", "Legend Biotech reports early cilta-cel results from China."], ["2022", "FDA approves ciltacabtagene autoleucel (Carvykti) for relapsed multiple myeloma."], ["2024", "FDA approves Carvykti after at least one prior line in lenalidomide-refractory myeloma."]], "cell": "The patient's T cells are transduced with a lentiviral vector encoding a CAR with two BCMA-binding single-domain antibodies (camelid VHH) arranged in tandem, a CD8 hinge and transmembrane domain, a 4-1BB costimulatory domain and the CD3-zeta signaling domain. The two binders recognize different epitopes on BCMA, increasing avidity. When the CAR binds BCMA on plasma cells, the T cell is activated without MHC, expands strongly, releases cytokines and kills BCMA-positive cells with perforin and granzymes. Normal plasma cells are also eliminated, lowering antibody production.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Myeloma cells are destroyed, and many patients reach deep, lasting remission."], ["Immune system", "Loss of normal plasma cells lowers antibody levels and raises infection risk."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome is very common, with a median onset around 7 days, later than with many CAR-T products."], ["Brain and nerves", "Delayed neurotoxicity can appear weeks to months later, including parkinsonism, cranial nerve palsies (often facial) and Guillain-Barre syndrome."], ["Bone marrow (normal)", "Low blood counts can be prolonged for weeks after infusion."], ["Immune activation", "HLH/MAS, a runaway inflammatory state, can occur."]], "journey": "T cells are collected by leukapheresis, engineered and expanded over several weeks, then frozen and sent back. Bridging therapy may be given while waiting. After lymphodepleting chemotherapy with fludarabine and cyclophosphamide, the cells are infused IV once. They home to the marrow and expand to a peak around 13 days before contracting, with some persisting for months. They are not metabolized like drugs.", "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome follows CAR-T activation and is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids. Early neurotoxicity is treated with corticosteroids. Delayed movement and nerve disorders, such as parkinsonism, are a distinctive boxed warning risk; they may be linked to high CAR-T expansion and possibly low-level BCMA in parts of the brain, and they may not fully recover. Prolonged low counts and low antibody levels raise infection risk. A boxed warning also notes rare T-cell cancers after CAR-T therapy.", "fun": "The two binding domains in cilta-cel's CAR are modeled on single-domain antibodies first discovered in camels, which make antibodies built from heavy chains alone.", "src": ["Carvykti (ciltacabtagene autoleucel) FDA label", "Berdeja JG et al. CARTITUDE-1, Lancet 2021", "Hamers-Casterman C et al. Nature 1993"]}, "obecabtagene-autoleucel": {"origin": "Obecabtagene autoleucel was developed by Autolus Therapeutics, a company spun out of University College London, based on work by Martin Pule and colleagues. They designed a new anti-CD19 binder, CAT19, that lets go of its target quickly (a fast off-rate). The idea was that a gentler, more natural-like T-cell engagement would reduce overactivation and toxicity while helping the CAR-T cells persist longer. In November 2024 the FDA approved Aucatzyl for adults with relapsed or refractory B-cell precursor acute lymphoblastic leukemia, a group that often tolerates other CAR-T products poorly.", "timeline": [["2014", "Autolus Therapeutics is founded from University College London research."], ["2024", "FDA approves obecabtagene autoleucel (Aucatzyl) for adult relapsed or refractory B-cell ALL."]], "cell": "The patient's T cells are transduced with a lentiviral vector encoding a second-generation CAR: the CAT19 anti-CD19 single-chain variable fragment, a 4-1BB costimulatory domain and the CD3-zeta signaling domain. CAT19 binds CD19 strongly enough to trigger killing but releases it quickly. This fast off-rate is designed to mimic the brief contacts of natural T-cell receptors, so each CAR-T cell is not overstimulated. When engaged, the cells kill CD19-positive cells without MHC, release cytokines and expand. The aim is less cytokine release and less exhaustion, which could support persistence.", "body": [["Bone marrow and blood", "Leukemic B blasts are destroyed, and many adults reach remission."], ["Immune system", "Normal B cells are also destroyed, lowering antibody levels and raising infection risk."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome is common, though severe CRS has been less frequent than with some other CD19 CAR-T products in adult ALL."], ["Brain", "Neurotoxicity (ICANS), including confusion and seizures, can occur."], ["Bone marrow (normal)", "Low blood counts can be prolonged for weeks after infusion."], ["Infections", "Serious infections are a leading risk during recovery."]], "journey": "T cells are collected by leukapheresis, engineered and expanded, then frozen and returned. After lymphodepleting chemotherapy with fludarabine and cyclophosphamide, the dose is split: part is infused first and the rest about 10 days later, with the size of the first part adjusted to the amount of leukemia in the marrow. This tailoring aims to lower toxicity in people with high disease burden. CAR-T cells expand over the first weeks and can persist for months.", "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome comes from CAR-T activation and release of IL-6 and other cytokines and is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids. Neurotoxicity is treated with corticosteroids. B-cell loss and prolonged low counts cause infections, which can be fatal. HLH/MAS can occur. A boxed warning notes rare secondary T-cell cancers after CAR-T therapy.", "src": ["Aucatzyl (obecabtagene autoleucel) FDA label", "Roddie C et al. FELIX trial, N Engl J Med 2024"]}, "afamitresgene-autoleucel": {"origin": "Afamitresgene autoleucel was developed by Adaptimmune, a company formed from research at the University of Oxford on engineering T-cell receptors with enhanced binding. Unlike CAR-T products, it gives T cells a modified natural T-cell receptor, which lets them see protein fragments from inside cancer cells. Its target is MAGE-A4, a 'cancer-testis antigen' that is made by many tumors, including most synovial sarcomas, but is silent in normal adult tissues except the testis. In August 2024 the FDA granted accelerated approval to Tecelra for synovial sarcoma, the first engineered T-cell receptor therapy approved in the US and the first new treatment for this rare soft tissue cancer in more than a decade.", "timeline": [["1991", "The first MAGE cancer-testis antigen is discovered in Belgium."], ["2008", "Adaptimmune is founded to develop engineered T-cell receptors."], ["2024", "FDA grants accelerated approval for afamitresgene autoleucel (Tecelra), the first engineered TCR therapy."]], "cell": "Cells chop up internal proteins and display the fragments on HLA class I molecules. MAGE-A4 is an intracellular protein, so an antibody or CAR cannot reach it, but a T-cell receptor can see its peptide when it is displayed on HLA-A*02. The patient's T cells are transduced with a lentiviral vector encoding a T-cell receptor engineered for high affinity to a MAGE-A4 peptide bound to certain HLA-A*02 types. When these T cells meet a tumor cell displaying the complex, the TCR signals through the natural CD3 machinery, activating the T cell to release cytokines and kill with perforin and granzymes. Because recognition is HLA-restricted, patients must have a matching HLA-A*02 type and a MAGE-A4-positive tumor.", "body": [["Tumor", "Synovial sarcoma cells displaying MAGE-A4 are killed, and tumors can shrink."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome with fever, fast heart rate and low blood pressure is common."], ["Bone marrow", "Prolonged low lymphocytes, neutrophils and platelets occur, largely from lymphodepleting chemotherapy."], ["Brain", "Neurotoxicity (ICANS) can occur, though usually less than with CD19 CAR-T."], ["Infections", "Low blood counts raise the risk of serious infections."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and constipation are common."]], "journey": "T cells are collected by leukapheresis, engineered and expanded, then frozen and returned. After lymphodepleting chemotherapy with fludarabine and cyclophosphamide, the cells are given as one IV infusion. They expand in the first 1 to 2 weeks and travel through the blood into tumors. Some persist for months. They are not metabolized like drugs.", "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome results from mass T-cell activation and is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids. ICANS is treated with corticosteroids. Prolonged low blood counts come from the lymphodepleting regimen and immune activation, raising infection and bleeding risk. Because the cells are HLA-restricted and MAGE-A4 is absent from most normal tissues, off-tumor injury has been limited. A boxed warning notes the risk of severe or fatal CRS.", "fun": "MAGE stands for melanoma-associated antigen, and the first member of the family was discovered by Thierry Boon's team in Brussels as a protein that patients' own T cells could recognize.", "src": ["Tecelra (afamitresgene autoleucel) FDA label", "D'Angelo SP et al. SPEARHEAD-1, Lancet 2024", "van der Bruggen P et al. Science 1991"]}, "lifileucel": {"origin": "Lifileucel is built on decades of work by Steven Rosenberg at the National Cancer Institute. In the 1980s his team found that lymphocytes taken from inside melanoma tumors, called tumor-infiltrating lymphocytes (TILs), could be grown in large numbers with interleukin-2 and returned to patients. A 1988 report showed tumor regressions in some people with metastatic melanoma. For many years TIL therapy was offered only at a few academic centers. Iovance Biotherapeutics developed a centralized manufacturing process to make it widely available. In February 2024 the FDA granted accelerated approval to Amtagvi for advanced melanoma after PD-1 therapy, the first cellular therapy approved for a solid tumor.", "timeline": [["1986", "Rosenberg's team describes the potency of tumor-infiltrating lymphocytes in animals."], ["1988", "Rosenberg and colleagues report melanoma regressions with TIL therapy in patients."], ["1992", "FDA approves high-dose IL-2 (aldesleukin) for metastatic kidney cancer."], ["2024", "FDA grants accelerated approval for lifileucel (Amtagvi), the first cell therapy for a solid tumor."]], "cell": "Melanomas are often infiltrated by T cells that already recognize the tumor, but inside the tumor they are outnumbered and suppressed. A piece of tumor is removed surgically, and these TILs, mostly CD8 and CD4 T cells, are expanded in the lab with IL-2 into billions of cells. The cells are not genetically modified. Their natural, polyclonal T-cell receptors recognize many targets, including neoantigens created by the many UV-induced mutations in melanoma, presented on MHC class I and II. Before infusion, lymphodepleting chemotherapy removes competing lymphocytes and regulatory T cells and raises homeostatic cytokines such as IL-7 and IL-15. After infusion, high-dose IL-2 (aldesleukin) supports their expansion, and the TILs then kill tumor cells with perforin, granzymes and cytokines.", "body": [["Melanoma tumors", "The returned T cells attack tumor cells at many sites, and some responses last years."], ["Whole body (IL-2 and cytokines)", "Fever, chills and fast heart rate are very common, mostly from IL-2 and the immune response."], ["Blood vessels", "Capillary leak from IL-2 can cause low blood pressure, edema, low oxygen and kidney injury."], ["Bone marrow", "Lymphodepleting chemotherapy causes severe, sometimes prolonged low blood counts with febrile neutropenia."], ["Heart", "Arrhythmias can occur during the IL-2 period."], ["Skin and hair", "Rash, hair loss and sometimes vitiligo-like pigment loss can occur as T cells also target normal melanocytes."]], "journey": "A tumor is surgically removed and shipped to a central facility, where TILs are grown over a few weeks, then frozen and returned. The patient receives a week of nonmyeloablative lymphodepleting chemotherapy with cyclophosphamide and fludarabine, then a single IV infusion of TILs, followed by a short course of high-dose IL-2 to support expansion. The cells spread through the blood and home to tumor sites. IL-2 itself has a short half-life of about 85 minutes. Responding TILs can persist for months.", "sidefx": "Many acute side effects come from high-dose IL-2, which causes fever, chills and capillary leak with low blood pressure, edema, low oxygen and kidney injury, and from the lymphodepleting chemotherapy, which causes prolonged low blood counts and infection risk. Treatment is given in hospitals with intensive care support. A boxed warning covers treatment-related deaths, prolonged severe cytopenia, severe infection, cardiopulmonary problems and kidney impairment. Corticosteroids are avoided where possible because they can blunt the T cells.", "fun": "Each lifileucel product is unique to one person, grown from the T cells that were already fighting inside that person's own tumor.", "src": ["Amtagvi (lifileucel) FDA label", "Rosenberg SA et al. N Engl J Med 1988", "Chesney J et al. J Immunother Cancer 2022"]}, "sipuleucel-t": {"origin": "Sipuleucel-T grew out of a simple idea: if the immune system could be taught to see a protein that prostate cancer cells carry, it might attack the tumor on its own. Researchers at the Seattle-area company Dendreon chose prostatic acid phosphatase (PAP), an enzyme made by most prostate cancers, and fused it to GM-CSF, an immune growth signal, to make the lab protein PA2024. Each patient's own white blood cells are collected, mixed with PA2024 so the antigen-presenting cells load up on PAP, and then given back. In 2010 the IMPACT trial showed a modest survival gain in men with metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer, and the FDA approved Provenge that year. It was the first autologous cellular immunotherapy approved for cancer in the US, and it opened the door for later personalized cell therapies.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Dendreon develops the PAP-GM-CSF fusion antigen approach for prostate cancer."], ["2010", "The IMPACT trial reports a survival benefit in metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer."], ["2010", "FDA approves Provenge, the first autologous cellular immunotherapy for cancer."]], "cell": "About three days before each infusion, the patient's peripheral blood mononuclear cells are collected by leukapheresis. In the lab they are cultured with PA2024, a fusion of prostatic acid phosphatase and GM-CSF. The GM-CSF part binds GM-CSF receptors on antigen-presenting cells such as dendritic cells, which activates them and helps them take up the attached PAP. The cells break PAP into peptides and display them on MHC class I and II molecules, along with co-stimulatory signals. Upregulation of CD54 (ICAM-1) is used as the potency marker. After infusion, these activated antigen-presenting cells prime helper and cytotoxic T cells that recognize PAP. The primed T cells can then find and kill PAP-expressing prostate cancer cells. The effect is active immunity, so it builds over weeks and may extend survival without shrinking tumors on scans.", "body": [["Immune system", "Primes T cells and antibody responses against prostatic acid phosphatase, a protein on most prostate cancer cells, creating antigen-specific antitumor immunity."], ["Prostate cancer", "Survival benefit is seen without consistent tumor shrinkage or PSA drop, because the immune effect works slowly and indirectly."], ["Whole body (infusion)", "Cytokines released by the activated cells cause chills, fever, fatigue and body aches, usually within a day of each infusion."], ["Veins and catheters", "Repeated leukapheresis may need a central line, which carries a risk of catheter infection and clots."], ["Heart and vessels", "Thromboembolic events, stroke and heart attack have been reported in trials, though a direct cause is not fully defined."]], "journey": "Sipuleucel-T is a living cell product, so it has no classic pharmacokinetics. The patient's own cells are collected, activated in a central lab for a few days, shipped back in an insulated container and infused through a vein. The activated cells circulate and travel to lymphoid tissue, where they present PAP to T cells. The cells themselves are not metabolized by liver enzymes. Their job is done once T cells are primed, and the resulting immune response develops over weeks after the full course.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are infusion reactions. The activated cells and the GM-CSF in the antigen release cytokines such as IL-6 and TNF, which reset the body's temperature set point and cause chills, fever, fatigue, back pain and joint aches. Rarely the reaction includes high blood pressure or low oxygen. Catheter infections come from the venous access needed for repeated collection. Because the product is made only for one person, matching the patient to the bag before infusion is critical. There is no antidote; reactions are managed with supportive care.", "fun": "Each Provenge dose is made for only one person, using that person's own blood cells, so the whole supply chain from collection to infusion runs on a tight clock of a few days.", "src": ["Provenge (sipuleucel-T) FDA label", "Kantoff PW et al. N Engl J Med 2010 (IMPACT trial)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "talimogene-laherparepvec": {"origin": "Talimogene laherparepvec, or T-VEC, began as a herpes simplex virus type 1 strain called JS1, taken from a cold sore. Scientists at the British biotech company BioVex, founded by Robert Coffin, engineered the virus to grow in cancer cells but not in healthy cells and added the gene for human GM-CSF to rally the immune system. Amgen bought BioVex in 2011. After the OPTiM trial showed durable responses in injected melanoma lesions, the FDA approved Imlygic in October 2015. It was the first oncolytic virus therapy approved in the United States, turning a decades-old idea, that viruses can kill tumors, into a licensed treatment.", "timeline": [["1990s", "BioVex scientists begin engineering herpes simplex virus as a cancer-killing agent."], ["2011", "Amgen acquires BioVex and its oncolytic virus program."], ["2015", "FDA approves Imlygic, the first oncolytic viral therapy in the US."]], "cell": "T-VEC is a live, attenuated HSV-1 with two key deletions and one insertion. Deleting ICP34.5 removes the gene the virus normally uses to overcome the cell's protein kinase R (PKR) antiviral defense. Healthy cells with working PKR and interferon pathways shut down viral protein synthesis, but many tumor cells have defective pathways, so the virus replicates selectively in them. Deleting ICP47 keeps the infected cell able to load viral and tumor antigens onto MHC class I, so cytotoxic T cells can see it. The inserted human GM-CSF gene is expressed as the virus multiplies. Viral replication ends in lysis, which spills tumor antigens and GM-CSF into the area. GM-CSF recruits and matures dendritic cells, which carry tumor antigens to lymph nodes and prime T cells. This can shrink both injected lesions and, sometimes, lesions that were never injected.", "body": [["Injected tumors", "Direct viral lysis and local inflammation destroy injected melanoma nodules in the skin, under the skin and in lymph nodes."], ["Immune system", "Released tumor antigens and GM-CSF prime T cells, which can cause regression of some uninjected lesions."], ["Whole body", "Viral replication and cytokines cause flu-like illness, fever, chills and fatigue, mostly after the first injections."], ["Skin", "Cellulitis, ulceration or necrosis can occur at injection sites; vitiligo may appear as T cells attack normal melanocytes that share antigens."], ["Herpes risk", "As a live HSV-1, it can cause herpetic lesions in patients or close contacts and is dangerous in people with weak immune systems."]], "journey": "T-VEC is injected straight into skin, subcutaneous and nodal lesions, using a fanning pattern to spread it through the tumor. Most of the virus stays in and around the injected tissue, where it multiplies in tumor cells. Small amounts of viral DNA can be detected for a short time in blood and urine, and live virus can be on the injection site surface, which is why the site is covered. The virus is not broken down by liver enzymes; the immune system clears it. Responses take months to appear and can last for years.", "sidefx": "Flu-like symptoms come from the body's antiviral response to a replicating virus and from GM-CSF. Injection site pain, cellulitis and necrosis reflect local tumor lysis and inflammation. Because it is a live herpes virus, it can spread to others through contact with the site and can cause disseminated herpes in immunocompromised people, which is why pregnant or immunocompromised staff do not handle it. The virus remains sensitive to acyclovir, which can be used to treat herpetic complications, though that also blocks the therapy. Vitiligo reflects immune attack on normal pigment cells.", "fun": "The parent virus strain was isolated from a cold sore, so a common nuisance virus became the first approved cancer-killing virus in the US.", "src": ["Imlygic (talimogene laherparepvec) FDA label", "Andtbacka RH et al. J Clin Oncol 2015 (OPTiM trial)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "nadofaragene-firadenovec": {"origin": "Interferon alfa has long been known to slow bladder cancer, but bladder lining cells lose it quickly when it is simply poured in. Researchers set out to make the bladder produce its own interferon. They packed the human interferon alfa-2b gene into a non-replicating adenovirus vector and paired it with Syn3, a surfactant that helps the virus enter bladder cells. The program passed through several companies before Ferring Pharmaceuticals completed development. In December 2022 the FDA approved Adstiladrin for BCG-unresponsive non-muscle invasive bladder cancer with carcinoma in situ. It was the first gene therapy approved for bladder cancer and gave patients who had failed BCG an option other than removing the bladder.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Early studies test an adenovirus carrying the interferon alfa-2b gene with the Syn3 enhancer in the bladder."], ["2022", "FDA approves Adstiladrin, the first gene therapy for bladder cancer."]], "cell": "Nadofaragene firadenovec is a recombinant adenovirus serotype 5 with the genes needed for replication removed, so it cannot copy itself. The Syn3 surfactant in the formulation loosens the glycosaminoglycan layer and helps the vector enter urothelial cells. Once inside, the vector delivers the human interferon alfa-2b gene to the nucleus, where it stays as an episome without integrating into chromosomes. The cells then transcribe and secrete interferon alfa-2b. The interferon binds type I interferon receptors (IFNAR1 and IFNAR2) on tumor and immune cells, activating JAK1 and TYK2 and STAT signaling. This switches on genes that slow cell division, promote apoptosis, block new vessel growth and increase MHC expression. It also activates NK cells and T cells in the bladder wall. Because transduced cells keep making interferon for weeks, one instillation lasts a long time.", "body": [["Bladder lining", "Transduced urothelial cells secrete interferon alfa-2b that slows growth and triggers death of carcinoma in situ and papillary tumor cells."], ["Local immune system", "Interferon recruits and activates NK cells, T cells and dendritic cells in the bladder wall and boosts antigen display on tumor cells."], ["Urinary tract", "Instillation and local inflammation cause bladder spasm, urgency, painful urination, blood in urine and discharge."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue, chills and fever reflect small amounts of interferon effect and the local immune reaction."]], "journey": "The suspension is instilled through a urinary catheter and held in the bladder for about an hour while the patient changes position so the whole lining is coated. The vector enters lining cells, and almost none reaches the bloodstream. Interferon levels in the bladder rise within days and stay up for weeks as the transduced cells keep working, which allows dosing every three months. The vector does not replicate and is cleared as the lining cells turn over. Vector DNA is shed in urine for several days, so urine is handled with care.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are local. The catheter and the irritant effect of the instillation cause discharge, spasm, urgency and dysuria, and inflammation from interferon causes some blood in the urine. Anticholinergic medication beforehand helps with spasm. Chills, fever and fatigue reflect interferon's flu-like effects. Urinary infections relate to catheterization. The main serious risk is not a toxicity but delay: if the cancer does not respond, waiting too long to remove the bladder can allow it to become muscle-invasive. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Adstiladrin (nadofaragene firadenovec-vncg) FDA label", "Boorjian SA et al. Lancet Oncol 2021", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "bcg-live-intravesical": {"origin": "BCG stands for Bacillus Calmette-Guerin, named for Albert Calmette and Camille Guerin of the Pasteur Institute in Lille, France. Starting in 1908 they grew a strain of Mycobacterium bovis, the cattle tuberculosis germ, on a potato, bile and glycerin medium and transferred it 230 times over 13 years until it lost its ability to cause disease. It was first given as a tuberculosis vaccine in 1921. In 1929 Raymond Pearl noted less cancer at autopsy in people who had tuberculosis, which sparked interest in BCG against tumors. In 1976 Alvaro Morales and colleagues in Canada reported that BCG placed into the bladder prevented bladder cancer from coming back. Around 1990 the FDA approved intravesical BCG, and it remains the standard immunotherapy for high-risk non-muscle invasive bladder cancer.", "timeline": [["1908", "Calmette and Guerin begin serial passage of Mycobacterium bovis in Lille."], ["1921", "BCG is first given as a tuberculosis vaccine."], ["1929", "Raymond Pearl reports less cancer at autopsy in people with tuberculosis."], ["1976", "Morales and colleagues report intravesical BCG for superficial bladder cancer."], ["c. 1990", "FDA approves intravesical BCG for bladder carcinoma in situ."]], "cell": "Live attenuated M. bovis is instilled into the bladder and sticks to the urothelium by binding fibronectin, exposed where the lining is damaged. Urothelial and tumor cells take up the bacteria. Pattern-recognition receptors such as Toll-like receptors 2 and 4 sense mycobacterial cell wall components, and infected cells release IL-6, IL-8 and other chemokines. Neutrophils, macrophages, dendritic cells, NK cells and T cells pour into the bladder wall. A Th1-type response develops, with IL-2, IFN-gamma and TNF-alpha, forming granulomas. Cytotoxic T cells, NK cells and activated macrophages kill tumor cells through perforin, granzymes, TRAIL and other pathways. Repeated instillations amplify this local immune response, which explains both its antitumor effect and the cystitis it causes.", "body": [["Bladder", "Intense local inflammation and immune cell influx destroy carcinoma in situ and lower the chance that papillary tumors return after surgery."], ["Urinary tract", "Cystitis causes burning, frequency, urgency and blood in urine, usually peaking a day or two after each treatment."], ["Whole body", "Fever, malaise and flu-like symptoms reflect cytokines released by the immune response."], ["Prostate and testes", "BCG can spread locally and cause granulomatous prostatitis or epididymitis."], ["Lungs, liver, joints", "Rarely BCG enters the blood and causes pneumonitis, hepatitis, reactive arthritis or a life-threatening disseminated infection."]], "journey": "BCG is instilled through a catheter by gravity and held in the bladder for about two hours before it is voided. It acts on the bladder surface and wall, and normally very little enters the bloodstream. Trauma to the lining, such as from a difficult catheterization, can let organisms into the blood, which is why treatment is delayed after bleeding or recent surgery. The bacteria are not metabolized; the immune system kills and clears them. Live organisms are shed in urine for hours to days, so voided urine is disinfected.", "sidefx": "The irritative bladder symptoms are the intended immune reaction showing itself as cystitis. Fever and malaise come from cytokines entering the circulation. BCG is a live mycobacterium, so if it escapes the bladder it can infect the prostate, epididymis, lungs, liver or bloodstream, and disseminated BCG sepsis can be fatal. BCG is naturally resistant to pyrazinamide, so systemic infections are treated with other antituberculosis drugs such as isoniazid, rifampin and ethambutol, sometimes with steroids. Handling precautions protect staff because it is infectious.", "fun": "The same bacterium used in the bladder is the BCG tuberculosis vaccine, which has been given to billions of people worldwide, making it one of the most widely used vaccines in history.", "src": ["TICE BCG FDA label", "Morales A et al. J Urol 1976", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "nogapendekin-alfa-inbakicept": {"origin": "Interleukin-15 is a natural cytokine that makes natural killer cells and killer T cells grow, but on its own it lasts only minutes in the body. Scientists at Altor BioScience, led by Hing Wong, created a souped-up complex: a mutant IL-15 (the N72D superagonist) bound to the sushi domain of its partner receptor IL-15R alpha, fused to an antibody Fc tail for stability. The compound, called ALT-803 and later N-803, moved to ImmunityBio, the company founded by Patrick Soon-Shiong. Given into the bladder together with BCG, it produced lasting complete responses in BCG-unresponsive carcinoma in situ. The FDA approved it as Anktiva in April 2024.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Altor BioScience develops the IL-15 superagonist complex ALT-803."], ["2010s", "The program moves to ImmunityBio as N-803."], ["2024", "FDA approves Anktiva with BCG for BCG-unresponsive bladder carcinoma in situ."]], "cell": "Nogapendekin alfa is an IL-15 variant with an asparagine to aspartate change at position 72 that increases its activity. Inbakicept is a dimer of the IL-15 receptor alpha sushi domain fused to a human IgG1 Fc. Together they mimic trans-presentation, the natural way IL-15 is held out to immune cells by IL-15R alpha on dendritic cells and macrophages. The complex binds the shared IL-2/IL-15 receptor beta (CD122) and common gamma chain (CD132) on NK cells and CD8 T cells. This activates JAK1 and JAK3, which phosphorylate STAT5, along with PI3K-AKT and MAPK pathways. The result is proliferation, survival and greater cytotoxic activity of NK and memory CD8 T cells, with little expansion of regulatory T cells because it does not use CD25. Combined with BCG, which brings immune cells into the bladder, it strengthens their killing of tumor cells.", "body": [["Bladder", "Activated NK cells and CD8 T cells in the bladder wall attack carcinoma in situ and papillary tumor cells alongside the BCG response."], ["Urinary tract", "Instillation with BCG causes dysuria, frequency, blood in urine and urinary infections."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue, chills, fever and muscle aches reflect immune activation, but blood levels stay low because little is absorbed."]], "journey": "The protein is mixed with BCG and instilled through a urinary catheter, then held in the bladder before voiding. It acts mainly on immune cells in and under the bladder lining. Very little crosses into the bloodstream, so systemic exposure is minimal and its blood kinetics are not well characterized. Like other proteins, any that is absorbed is broken down into amino acids. The immune effect builds over induction and maintenance courses.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from the combined bladder instillation: catheter trauma and BCG cystitis cause burning, frequency and hematuria, and catheterization brings a risk of urinary infection. Systemic immune activation is small because little reaches the blood. Because it is given with live BCG, BCG infection risks and handling precautions apply. As with other bladder-sparing options, waiting too long to remove the bladder if the cancer does not respond can allow progression. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Anktiva (nogapendekin alfa inbakicept-pmln) FDA label", "Chamie K et al. NEJM Evidence 2023 (QUILT 3.032)"]}, "aldesleukin": {"origin": "In 1976 Doris Morgan, Francis Ruscetti and Robert Gallo at the US National Cancer Institute found that a factor made by stimulated lymphocytes kept T cells growing in culture. They called it T cell growth factor; it was later named interleukin-2. Steven Rosenberg at the NCI saw a way to use it against cancer. Once Cetus Corporation produced large amounts of recombinant IL-2 in E. coli, Rosenberg's team gave high doses to patients, and in 1984 a woman with metastatic melanoma had a complete, lasting remission. The FDA approved aldesleukin (Proleukin) for metastatic kidney cancer in 1992 and for metastatic melanoma in 1998. It was one of the first proofs that the immune system alone could cure some advanced cancers.", "timeline": [["1976", "Morgan, Ruscetti and Gallo describe T cell growth factor, later called IL-2."], ["1983", "The IL-2 gene is cloned, allowing recombinant production."], ["1984", "Rosenberg's team at the NCI sees a durable complete response with high-dose IL-2."], ["1992", "FDA approves aldesleukin for metastatic renal cell carcinoma."], ["1998", "FDA approves aldesleukin for metastatic melanoma."]], "cell": "Aldesleukin is a recombinant IL-2 that lacks the first alanine and has serine instead of cysteine at position 125, which prevents misfolding. It binds the IL-2 receptor on T cells and NK cells. The high-affinity receptor has three chains: CD25 (alpha), CD122 (beta) and CD132 (common gamma). The intermediate-affinity form has only CD122 and CD132 and is found on NK cells and resting memory T cells, which high doses reach. Binding brings JAK1 (on CD122) and JAK3 (on CD132) together, and they phosphorylate STAT5, along with PI3K-AKT and MAPK signaling. This drives clonal expansion of activated cytotoxic T cells and NK cells and creates lymphokine-activated killer cells. The activated cells release a second wave of cytokines, including IFN-gamma, TNF-alpha and IL-1. These kill tumor cells in some patients but also damage blood vessel lining, which leads to capillary leak.", "body": [["Immune system", "T cells and NK cells multiply and become more cytotoxic, producing durable responses in a minority of patients with kidney cancer or melanoma."], ["Blood vessels", "Cytokines and activated lymphocytes injure the endothelium, so fluid and protein leak into tissues: capillary leak syndrome with low blood pressure, edema and weight gain."], ["Heart", "Low blood pressure and cytokines cause fast heart rate, atrial arrhythmias and, rarely, myocardial ischemia."], ["Lungs", "Leaked fluid causes pulmonary edema and pleural effusions, leading to shortness of breath."], ["Kidneys", "Low blood pressure and reduced renal blood flow cause oliguria and rising creatinine, usually reversible."], ["Brain", "Lethargy, confusion and somnolence can progress to coma, so mental status is watched closely."], ["Neutrophils", "Neutrophil chemotaxis is impaired, raising the risk of bacterial infections, especially of central lines."]], "journey": "Aldesleukin is given as a short IV infusion in the hospital. It spreads quickly into the blood and extracellular fluid, with a distribution half-life of about 13 minutes and an elimination half-life of about 85 minutes. The kidneys filter it, and proximal tubule cells break it down into amino acids. Because it is cleared so fast, it is given repeatedly in scheduled hospital courses. Fever and chills start within hours, and most toxicities fade within days after the last dose.", "sidefx": "The side effects are the immune cascade spilling over. Cytokines such as TNF-alpha and IL-1 reset the brain's temperature set point, causing fever and rigors. Vessel injury causes capillary leak, which explains hypotension, edema, effusions, kidney injury and organ hypoperfusion. Impaired neutrophil function raises infection risk. IL-2 can unmask autoimmune disease, especially thyroiditis. There is no specific antidote. Care is supportive with fluids and vasopressors, and stopping doses reverses most effects. Dexamethasone can blunt toxicity but also blunts the antitumor effect.", "fun": "Steven Rosenberg treated 66 patients with IL-2 related therapies before seeing his first major response, and that first patient remained cancer-free for decades.", "src": ["Proleukin (aldesleukin) FDA label", "Morgan DA, Ruscetti FW, Gallo R. Science 1976", "Rosenberg SA. J Immunol 2014 (IL-2: the first effective immunotherapy)"]}, "lutetium-lu-177-dotatate": {"origin": "Lutetium Lu 177 dotatate is a guided missile built on the hormone somatostatin, which Roger Guillemin's group described in 1973. Many neuroendocrine tumors are covered with somatostatin receptors, especially subtype 2. In the 1980s Sandoz made octreotide, a stable somatostatin analog, and radiolabeled versions were soon used to image these tumors. Researchers led by Eric Krenning at Erasmus Medical Center in Rotterdam then attached therapeutic radioisotopes. Their lutetium-177 version of the analog dotatate, studied from around 2000, shrank tumors and extended control. The French company Advanced Accelerator Applications ran the NETTER-1 trial, and the FDA approved Lutathera in January 2018 for somatostatin receptor-positive gastroenteropancreatic neuroendocrine tumors. Novartis acquired the company that year.", "timeline": [["1973", "Somatostatin is described by Roger Guillemin's laboratory."], ["1980s", "Octreotide is developed, and radiolabeled analogs are used to image neuroendocrine tumors."], ["2000s", "Erasmus MC in Rotterdam treats patients with lutetium-177 dotatate."], ["2018", "FDA approves Lutathera after the NETTER-1 trial."]], "cell": "Dotatate (DOTA-Tyr3-octreotate) is a somatostatin analog that binds somatostatin receptor subtype 2 (SSTR2) with high affinity. The DOTA cage tightly holds lutetium-177. After binding, the receptor-ligand complex is internalized, so the radioactive atom is trapped inside the tumor cell. Lutetium-177 decays by emitting beta particles (electrons) that travel up to about 2 millimeters in tissue. These electrons ionize water and DNA, creating free radicals and single and double-strand DNA breaks. Cells that cannot repair the damage die, regardless of where they are in the cell cycle. Because the range spans many cell widths, neighboring tumor cells that lack the receptor are also hit (the crossfire effect). Lutetium-177 also gives off gamma photons, which allow imaging of where the drug went.", "body": [["Neuroendocrine tumors", "Concentrated radiation in SSTR2-positive tumors slows growth and extends progression-free survival in gut and pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors."], ["Bone marrow", "Circulating drug irradiates the marrow, lowering lymphocytes, platelets, red cells and neutrophils; a small risk of MDS or leukemia follows."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys filter the peptide and partly reabsorb it in the proximal tubule, so a co-infused amino acid solution of lysine and arginine is given to block this uptake."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting come mostly from the amino acid infusion rather than the radiation."], ["Hormones", "Damaged tumor cells can release stored hormones and cause a neuroendocrine hormonal crisis with flushing, diarrhea or blood pressure swings."], ["Liver", "Liver metastases receive radiation, and liver enzymes can rise."]], "journey": "It is infused into a vein, with amino acids before and during to protect the kidneys. It leaves the blood quickly, with an effective blood half-life of a few hours, and binds receptor-rich tumors within hours. The drug is not metabolized; lutetium-177 simply decays, with a physical half-life of about 6.65 days, so the tumor receives radiation over weeks. Unbound drug is filtered by the kidneys, and most of it leaves in the urine within two days, which is why urine is handled with radiation precautions.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow where the radioactivity goes. Marrow exposure causes low blood counts and, rarely, myelodysplastic syndrome or acute leukemia years later. The kidneys handle the peptide, so radiation nephropathy is possible, which the amino acid infusion reduces. The concentrated amino acids cause nausea and can cause high potassium. Hormone release from dying tumors causes crises. Long-acting somatostatin analogs are paused before treatment because they compete for the same receptor. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "The same tumor-seeking molecule can carry an imaging isotope like gallium-68 for a PET scan or lutetium-177 for treatment, a pairing called theranostics.", "src": ["Lutathera (lutetium Lu 177 dotatate) FDA label", "Strosberg J et al. N Engl J Med 2017 (NETTER-1)", "Kwekkeboom DJ, Krenning EP et al. J Clin Oncol 2008"]}, "lutetium-lu-177-vipivotide-tetraxetan": {"origin": "Prostate-specific membrane antigen (PSMA) is an enzyme, glutamate carboxypeptidase II, packed onto the surface of most prostate cancer cells. Researchers at the German Cancer Research Center (DKFZ) and Heidelberg University designed small molecules that grab PSMA tightly, and around 2015 they described PSMA-617, a ligand with a chelator that can hold lutetium-177. Early compassionate use in Germany produced striking drops in PSA. Endocyte licensed the compound, Novartis acquired Endocyte in 2018, and the VISION trial showed longer survival in men with PSMA-positive metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer. The FDA approved Pluvicto in March 2022.", "timeline": [["1990s", "PSMA is characterized as a prostate cancer surface enzyme."], ["2015", "Heidelberg and DKFZ researchers describe the ligand PSMA-617."], ["2018", "Novartis acquires Endocyte, which held rights to PSMA-617."], ["2022", "FDA approves Pluvicto for PSMA-positive metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer."]], "cell": "Vipivotide tetraxetan (PSMA-617) has a glutamate-urea-lysine motif that fits the active site of PSMA (glutamate carboxypeptidase II, the FOLH1 gene product), a linker, and a DOTA chelator holding lutetium-177. After binding, PSMA is internalized, carrying the radionuclide into the cancer cell. Lutetium-177 emits beta particles that travel up to about 2 millimeters, ionizing water and DNA and causing single and double-strand breaks. Cells that cannot repair the damage die by apoptosis or mitotic catastrophe, independent of cell-cycle phase. The crossfire effect also irradiates nearby tumor cells with lower PSMA levels. Gamma photons allow imaging. Normal tissues that express PSMA, such as salivary and lacrimal glands and kidney tubules, also take up the drug.", "body": [["Prostate cancer", "Radiation concentrated in PSMA-positive metastases in bone, lymph nodes and organs lowers PSA and extends survival."], ["Salivary and tear glands", "These glands express PSMA, so radiation causes dry mouth and dry eyes, sometimes long-lasting."], ["Bone marrow", "Radiation to the marrow, often already crowded with bone metastases, causes anemia, low platelets and low white counts."], ["Kidneys", "Tubular uptake and urinary excretion expose the kidneys to radiation and can cause kidney injury."], ["Gut", "Nausea, loss of appetite and constipation are common during treatment."]], "journey": "Pluvicto is given by IV injection. It clears from the blood quickly, with a terminal blood half-life of about 41 hours, and binds PSMA-positive tissue within hours. It is not metabolized; lutetium-177 decays with a physical half-life of about 6.65 days, delivering radiation over days to weeks. The unbound drug is filtered by the kidneys and leaves mainly in urine. Patients follow radiation safety steps for a few days to limit exposure to others.", "sidefx": "Each main side effect tracks a tissue that holds the drug. PSMA in salivary and lacrimal glands explains xerostomia and dry eye. Marrow exposure explains the cytopenias, which are worse when bone metastases already crowd out normal blood cells. Renal handling explains kidney toxicity, so hydration is encouraged. Radiation exposure can harm sperm, so contraception is discussed. There is no antidote; care is supportive, with transfusions or growth factors for low counts.", "fun": "Before treatment, a PSMA PET scan using a gallium-68 or fluorine-18 version of a similar ligand shows which tumors will light up, so doctors can see the target before they hit it.", "src": ["Pluvicto (lutetium Lu 177 vipivotide tetraxetan) FDA label", "Sartor O et al. N Engl J Med 2021 (VISION)", "Benesova M et al. J Nucl Med 2015"]}, "radium-ra-223-dichloride": {"origin": "Marie and Pierre Curie discovered the element radium in 1898. Radium is chemically similar to calcium, so the body treats it like calcium and deposits it in bone. Radium-223 is a short-lived isotope that gives off alpha particles, which are powerful but travel less than a tenth of a millimeter. Norwegian researchers Roy Larsen and Oyvind Bruland saw that this could hit bone metastases while sparing most of the marrow, and they founded the company Algeta to develop it, later partnering with Bayer. The ALSYMPCA trial showed longer survival in men with prostate cancer that had spread to bone, and the FDA approved Xofigo in May 2013. It was the first alpha-emitting radiopharmaceutical approved in the US.", "timeline": [["1898", "Marie and Pierre Curie discover radium."], ["1990s", "Norwegian researchers propose radium-223 as a bone-seeking alpha emitter."], ["2013", "FDA approves Xofigo, the first alpha-emitting therapy, for prostate cancer bone metastases."]], "cell": "Radium-223 is a calcium mimetic. It forms complexes with hydroxyapatite in bone, concentrating where bone is being rapidly built and broken down, such as osteoblastic metastases. As it and its daughter isotopes decay, they release four alpha particles. Alpha particles are helium nuclei with high linear energy transfer: they deposit a lot of energy over a very short path of less than 100 micrometers, only a few cell widths. This creates clustered double-strand DNA breaks that cells cannot easily repair, killing tumor cells, osteoblasts and osteoclasts right next to the deposit. The damage does not depend on oxygen or cell-cycle phase. The short range limits exposure of the nearby marrow compared with beta-emitting bone agents.", "body": [["Bone metastases", "Alpha particles kill tumor cells and abnormal bone-forming cells in osteoblastic lesions, slowing disease and extending survival."], ["Bone marrow", "Some marrow lies close to the deposits, so anemia, low platelets and low neutrophils occur, especially with widespread bone disease."], ["Gut", "The small intestine excretes radium, so nausea, diarrhea and vomiting are common."], ["Skeleton", "Fractures are more common when combined with abiraterone and prednisone, so that combination is avoided."]], "journey": "Radium-223 is given by slow IV injection. It leaves the blood within hours, going mostly to bone and to the intestine. It is not metabolized; it simply decays with a physical half-life of about 11.4 days, so bone deposits receive radiation over weeks. Unlike most radiopharmaceuticals, it leaves the body mainly in the feces through the small intestine, with only a small amount in urine. This is why stool and body fluids are handled carefully for a time after each injection.", "sidefx": "The cytopenias come from alpha radiation reaching marrow cells next to bone deposits. Because the intestine excretes the drug, the gut lining is irradiated, causing nausea and diarrhea. Peripheral edema has also been reported. The fracture and death risk seen with abiraterone is not fully explained, but it led to a warning against that combination. There is no antidote; supportive care includes transfusions and holding treatment until counts recover.", "fun": "In the 1920s, workers who painted watch dials with radium paint, the Radium Girls, developed bone damage because radium settles in bone like calcium, the same property Xofigo uses on purpose.", "src": ["Xofigo (radium Ra 223 dichloride) FDA label", "Parker C et al. N Engl J Med 2013 (ALSYMPCA)"]}, "sodium-iodide-i-131": {"origin": "The thyroid is the only tissue that greedily pulls iodine from the blood to make thyroid hormone. In 1938 Glenn Seaborg and John Livingood produced iodine-131. Saul Hertz, a physician at Massachusetts General Hospital, worked with MIT physicist Arthur Roberts to study radioactive iodine uptake, and in 1941 he gave the first therapeutic radioiodine to a patient with hyperthyroidism. In the 1940s Samuel Seidlin used it to treat metastatic thyroid cancer. After World War II, reactors made I-131 widely available, and it became one of the first targeted radiation treatments and one of the founding drugs of nuclear medicine. It is still a standard treatment for Graves disease, toxic nodules and differentiated thyroid cancer.", "timeline": [["1938", "Seaborg and Livingood produce iodine-131."], ["1941", "Saul Hertz gives the first therapeutic radioiodine for hyperthyroidism."], ["1946", "Seidlin reports radioiodine treatment of metastatic thyroid cancer."], ["1951", "FDA approves sodium iodide I-131 for thyroid conditions."]], "cell": "Iodide-131 behaves chemically like ordinary iodide. The sodium-iodide symporter (NIS) on the basolateral membrane of thyroid follicular cells uses the sodium gradient made by the Na-K ATPase to pump iodide into the cell. TSH increases NIS expression. Inside, the iodide moves to the follicle lumen, where thyroid peroxidase oxidizes it and attaches it to tyrosines on thyroglobulin (organification), trapping it in the gland. As I-131 decays, it emits beta particles with a mean range of about half a millimeter that break DNA and kill follicular cells over the next weeks, and gamma rays that allow imaging. Well-differentiated thyroid cancers often keep NIS, so they also take up the isotope, which allows ablation of remnant tissue and treatment of iodine-avid metastases.", "body": [["Thyroid", "Radiation destroys overactive thyroid tissue in hyperthyroidism and ablates remaining tissue after thyroid cancer surgery, often leading to permanent hypothyroidism."], ["Thyroid cancer metastases", "Iodine-avid spread to lungs, lymph nodes or bone takes up the isotope and is irradiated."], ["Salivary glands", "These glands also express NIS, so they concentrate the iodine and can become swollen and painful, with dry mouth and taste change."], ["Bone marrow", "Circulating radiation can lower blood counts, especially after large cumulative treatment for cancer."], ["Neck", "Radiation thyroiditis can cause neck tenderness and a short burst of released thyroid hormone."]], "journey": "Sodium iodide I-131 is swallowed as a capsule or solution and is almost completely absorbed from the gut. It travels in the blood as free iodide. The thyroid traps it within hours, with peak uptake around 24 hours. Iodide that the thyroid does not take is filtered by the kidneys and leaves in the urine within days, with smaller amounts in saliva, sweat and breast milk. The trapped iodine is not otherwise broken down; it decays with a physical half-life of about eight days. Patients follow radiation precautions for a few days, and breastfeeding must stop.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow the tissues that collect iodide. The thyroid takes the most, so thyroid destruction and hypothyroidism are expected, and early thyroiditis can briefly release hormone and worsen symptoms. Salivary and tear glands have NIS, causing sialadenitis and dry mouth. High cumulative activity can suppress marrow, cause radiation pneumonitis in diffuse lung metastases, and slightly raise the risk of later leukemia. Pregnancy is a contraindication because the fetal thyroid takes up iodine after about 10 to 12 weeks. There is no reversal agent once taken; hydration and frequent voiding speed clearance.", "fun": "Saul Hertz's 1941 treatment is often called the birth of nuclear medicine and of the idea of a targeted cancer theranostic.", "src": ["Sodium iodide I-131 FDA label", "Hertz S, Roberts A. JAMA 1946", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "samarium-sm-153-lexidronam": {"origin": "Bone metastases often cause severe pain, and in the 1980s researchers looked for radioactive atoms that would settle in bone and calm it. A team at the University of Missouri, home to a powerful research reactor, worked with Dow Chemical to attach samarium-153 to EDTMP, a phosphonate that sticks to growing bone like the bisphosphonates. The product was licensed to Cytogen and approved by the FDA as Quadramet in 1997 for pain from osteoblastic bone metastases. It was used for years, especially in prostate and breast cancer. It has since been reported discontinued by its US manufacturer, and no current US label was found on DailyMed, so it is largely of historical and educational interest in the US.", "timeline": [["1980s", "University of Missouri and Dow Chemical researchers develop samarium-153 EDTMP."], ["1997", "FDA approves Quadramet for bone metastasis pain."], ["2010s", "The US product is reported discontinued and no current label is listed."]], "cell": "Lexidronam is EDTMP (ethylenediamine tetramethylene phosphonate), a chelator with four phosphonate groups. Phosphonates bind calcium in hydroxyapatite, especially where osteoblasts are actively laying down new mineral, as in osteoblastic metastases. The EDTMP carries samarium-153 into these areas. Samarium-153 emits beta particles with a mean soft tissue range of about 0.6 millimeter that cause DNA strand breaks in nearby tumor cells and bone cells. This damage, along with effects on cells that release pain mediators, reduces bone pain. It is a bone-seeking radionuclide rather than a receptor drug, and its effect does not depend on cell-cycle phase. It also gives off gamma photons that allow imaging.", "body": [["Bone metastases", "Beta radiation to osteoblastic lesions relieves bone pain for weeks to months in many patients."], ["Bone marrow", "Marrow near bone deposits is irradiated, so platelets and white cells fall, with the lowest point about three to five weeks later."], ["Pain", "A short flare of bone pain can occur in the first days before relief begins."]], "journey": "It was given as a slow IV injection. The bone took up the drug within hours, in proportion to how active each area was, matching the pattern seen on a bone scan. The part that did not bind bone was filtered by the kidneys and left in the urine within hours. The bound samarium was not metabolized; it decayed with a physical half-life of about 46 hours, so most of its radiation was delivered within about a week. Pain relief usually began within about a week.", "sidefx": "Bone marrow suppression is the main toxicity because the marrow sits next to the bone surface where the drug lands. The short pain flare is thought to reflect local inflammation as the radiation acts. Radiation exposure means pregnancy must be avoided. Pre-existing low counts or extensive prior chemotherapy raised the risk of severe pancytopenia. There is no antidote; supportive care with transfusions was used for marrow suppression.", "src": ["Quadramet (samarium Sm 153 lexidronam) historical FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "dexrazoxane": {"origin": "Dexrazoxane was first made as a possible cancer drug. In the late 1960s Andrew Creighton and colleagues at the Imperial Cancer Research Fund in London synthesized a family of bisdioxopiperazines, ring-closed relatives of the chelator EDTA designed to slip into cells. Racemic razoxane (ICRF-159) had modest antitumor activity, and its d-isomer, ICRF-187, became dexrazoxane. In the 1970s and 1980s Eugene Herman and others found that it protected animal hearts from doxorubicin damage. Clinical trials confirmed less cardiomyopathy, and the FDA approved Zinecard in 1995 to protect the heart in women receiving doxorubicin for metastatic breast cancer. In 2007 a second product, Totect, was approved to treat anthracycline extravasation.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Creighton and colleagues at the Imperial Cancer Research Fund make bisdioxopiperazines including ICRF-187."], ["1970s", "Animal studies show protection against anthracycline heart damage."], ["1995", "FDA approves Zinecard as a cardioprotectant with doxorubicin."], ["2007", "FDA approves Totect for anthracycline extravasation."]], "cell": "Dexrazoxane is a cyclic, uncharged EDTA analog that crosses cell membranes easily. Inside heart cells it is hydrolyzed to the open-ring form ADR-925, a strong metal chelator. Anthracyclines such as doxorubicin bind iron, and the iron-drug complex cycles electrons to oxygen, making superoxide and hydroxyl radicals that damage mitochondria and membranes in cardiomyocytes, which have weak antioxidant defenses. ADR-925 strips iron from this complex and binds free iron, cutting radical production. Dexrazoxane is also a catalytic inhibitor of topoisomerase II. It locks the enzyme in a closed clamp before it cuts DNA, and it causes degradation of topoisomerase II beta, the form in quiescent heart cells. Because doxorubicin poisons topoisomerase II beta to create DNA double-strand breaks in the heart, removing the enzyme blocks that injury.", "body": [["Heart", "Less iron-driven oxidative injury and topoisomerase II beta damage means less anthracycline cardiomyopathy and a slower fall in ejection fraction."], ["Tissue at an extravasation site", "Given IV after an anthracycline leaks into tissue, it limits the spreading tissue death that would otherwise need surgery."], ["Bone marrow", "Topoisomerase II inhibition in dividing marrow cells adds to chemotherapy myelosuppression, lowering neutrophils and platelets."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise during treatment."]], "journey": "Dexrazoxane is given as a short IV infusion shortly before the anthracycline dose. It distributes into body water, including heart cells, and is not significantly bound to plasma proteins. It is hydrolyzed inside cells and in plasma to the open-ring chelating form ADR-925. A large share is excreted unchanged in the urine, and the elimination half-life is about two to two and a half hours. Kidney impairment raises levels.", "sidefx": "Most side effects add to those of the chemotherapy it is given with. Its topoisomerase II inhibition in dividing marrow cells deepens neutropenia and thrombocytopenia. Because topoisomerase II inhibitors can cause chromosome breaks, secondary AML and MDS have been reported. Injection-site pain and liver enzyme rises also occur. Early concern that it might reduce tumor response has not been confirmed in most analyses. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "src": ["Zinecard and Totect (dexrazoxane) FDA labels", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Hasinoff BB. Cardiovasc Toxicol 2007"]}, "rasburicase": {"origin": "Most mammals carry urate oxidase, or uricase, an enzyme that turns uric acid into soluble allantoin. Humans and other great apes lost working uricase through gene mutations millions of years ago, which is why we can get gout and kidney stones from uric acid. Scientists borrowed the enzyme from the mold Aspergillus flavus. A purified, non-recombinant mold uricase was used in France and Italy from the 1970s. Sanofi then produced a recombinant version in yeast, called rasburicase. The FDA approved Elitek in 2002 for children with cancer at risk of tumor lysis syndrome and extended it to adults in 2009. It gave doctors a way to destroy uric acid already in the blood, rather than just stop new production.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Non-recombinant Aspergillus uricase is used in Europe for hyperuricemia."], ["2002", "FDA approves Elitek for pediatric tumor lysis prevention."], ["2009", "FDA extends approval to adults."]], "cell": "Rasburicase is a recombinant Aspergillus flavus urate oxidase expressed in Saccharomyces cerevisiae. It is a tetramer that oxidizes uric acid in the blood, using molecular oxygen, to 5-hydroxyisourate, which breaks down to allantoin. Allantoin is five to ten times more water soluble than uric acid, so it is filtered and excreted without crystallizing in renal tubules. Each reaction makes hydrogen peroxide as a by-product. Red cells normally neutralize hydrogen peroxide with glutathione, which is kept in reduced form by NADPH from glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD). In G6PD deficiency this defense fails, so peroxide oxidizes hemoglobin and membranes, causing hemolysis and methemoglobinemia. Unlike allopurinol, which blocks xanthine oxidase and prevents new uric acid, rasburicase removes uric acid that already exists.", "body": [["Blood", "Plasma uric acid falls sharply within hours as the enzyme converts it to allantoin."], ["Kidneys", "Less uric acid reaching the tubules means fewer urate crystals and less acute kidney injury during tumor lysis."], ["Red cells", "Hydrogen peroxide made by the reaction can cause hemolysis and methemoglobinemia, especially with G6PD deficiency."], ["Immune system", "As a foreign fungal protein it can cause allergic reactions and anaphylaxis, and antibodies form with repeated use."]], "journey": "Rasburicase is given by IV infusion. It works in the bloodstream, staying mostly in the plasma volume. Uric acid falls within about four hours of the first dose. As a protein it is broken down by normal protein catabolism into peptides and amino acids rather than by liver enzymes, and its clearance does not depend on the kidneys. The half-life is about 16 to 21 hours. The enzyme keeps working in a blood sample at room temperature, so samples for uric acid are collected in prechilled tubes and kept on ice, or the result will be falsely low.", "sidefx": "The boxed warnings come straight from the chemistry and the source of the protein. The hydrogen peroxide made with each reaction overwhelms red cell defenses in G6PD deficiency, causing severe hemolysis and methemoglobinemia, so patients at risk are screened first. Methylene blue does not work well, and can be harmful, in G6PD deficiency. Being a fungal protein, it can trigger anaphylaxis. Nausea, vomiting and fever are common. There is no antidote; the infusion is stopped and reactions are treated supportively.", "fun": "Losing uricase may have helped our ancestors: one theory is that higher uric acid helped great apes keep blood pressure up on a low-salt diet or acted as an antioxidant.", "src": ["Elitek (rasburicase) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "palifermin": {"origin": "In 1989 Jeffrey Rubin and Stuart Aaronson at the US National Cancer Institute discovered keratinocyte growth factor (KGF, later called FGF-7), a protein made by connective tissue cells that tells nearby epithelial cells to grow. Because it acts on the linings of the mouth and gut, Amgen developed a truncated recombinant version, palifermin, to protect these linings from the damage of high-dose chemotherapy and radiation. In a trial in patients with blood cancers receiving total body irradiation and chemotherapy before stem cell transplant, it reduced the incidence and length of severe oral mucositis. The FDA approved Kepivance in December 2004, the first drug approved to prevent oral mucositis.", "timeline": [["1989", "Rubin and Aaronson at the NCI discover keratinocyte growth factor."], ["2004", "FDA approves Kepivance to reduce severe oral mucositis in stem cell transplant patients."]], "cell": "Palifermin is human KGF with the first 23 amino acids removed, made in E. coli, which makes it more stable. It binds the KGF receptor, the FGFR2 IIIb isoform, a receptor tyrosine kinase found mainly on epithelial cells. Binding causes receptor dimerization and autophosphorylation, which activates RAS-MAPK, PI3K-AKT and PLC-gamma pathways. These signals drive epithelial proliferation and differentiation and turn on protective programs, including antioxidant enzymes and anti-apoptotic proteins. The result is a thicker, more resilient lining of the tongue, cheeks and gut. When chemotherapy and radiation later kill dividing cells, the thicker lining is less likely to break down into painful ulcers, and it heals faster.", "body": [["Mouth", "A thicker, more resistant lining lowers the incidence and length of severe oral mucositis after high-dose therapy."], ["Tongue and taste", "Epithelial growth thickens or discolors the tongue and changes taste or sensation in the mouth."], ["Skin", "KGF receptors in the skin cause rash, redness, itching and swelling."], ["Pancreas", "Amylase and lipase can rise, usually without symptoms."], ["Tumors", "Because some tumor cells carry the KGF receptor, there is a theoretical concern about stimulating growth of epithelial cancers."]], "journey": "Palifermin is given by IV bolus for three days before and three days after the conditioning regimen, but not on the same day as chemotherapy. Levels drop quickly at first and then more slowly, with a half-life of about four and a half hours. It is a protein, so it is broken down by proteolysis, and the kidneys are not the main route of clearance. Its effect on the epithelium lasts several days after each block of doses. It is not given within 24 hours of chemotherapy because freshly stimulated, dividing epithelial cells would be more sensitive to the chemotherapy.", "sidefx": "Side effects reflect growth of epithelium where the receptor is found. Thickening of the tongue and mouth lining causes dysesthesia and taste change. Skin effects such as rash, redness and edema come from receptor activation in skin. Rising amylase and lipase reflect effects on the pancreas and salivary glands. Giving it too close to chemotherapy can worsen mucositis because stimulated cells are more vulnerable. Tumor growth stimulation remains a theoretical concern. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Kepivance (palifermin) FDA label", "Rubin JS et al. PNAS 1989", "Spielberger R et al. N Engl J Med 2004"]}, "amifostine": {"origin": "Amifostine was born from Cold War fears of nuclear war. In 1959 the US Army began an antiradiation drug program at the Walter Reed Army Institute of Research, and chemists synthesized and screened thousands of sulfur-containing compounds in mice for protection against radiation. One compound, WR-2721, stood out. It turned out to protect normal tissues better than tumors, which made it interesting for cancer treatment. The FDA approved it as Ethyol in 1995 to reduce cumulative kidney toxicity from cisplatin in advanced ovarian cancer, and in 1999 to reduce dry mouth from radiation for head and neck cancer.", "timeline": [["1959", "The US Army antiradiation drug program begins at Walter Reed."], ["1960s", "WR-2721 is identified as a strong radioprotector in animals."], ["1995", "FDA approves Ethyol to reduce cisplatin kidney toxicity."], ["1999", "FDA approves Ethyol to reduce radiation-induced xerostomia."]], "cell": "Amifostine is an inactive organic thiophosphate prodrug. Alkaline phosphatase on cell membranes removes the phosphate to make the free thiol WR-1065, which enters cells. Normal tissues have more of this enzyme activity, better blood flow and a more neutral pH than many tumors, which are often hypoxic and acidic, so normal cells make and take up more WR-1065. Inside, the thiol scavenges reactive oxygen species made by radiation and certain chemotherapy, donates hydrogen to repair damaged DNA, and binds the reactive species of cisplatin and alkylating agents before they attack DNA. WR-1065 is further oxidized to disulfides that may also be protective. The net effect is selective protection of normal cells, such as kidney tubules and salivary glands.", "body": [["Kidneys", "WR-1065 binds reactive cisplatin species in tubular cells, reducing cumulative kidney injury."], ["Salivary glands", "Scavenging radiation-generated free radicals in gland tissue reduces acute and late dry mouth."], ["Blood vessels", "It relaxes vascular smooth muscle, so blood pressure can drop during the infusion."], ["Gut and brain", "Nausea and vomiting are common, and sleepiness can occur."], ["Parathyroid and calcium", "It can lower parathyroid hormone release, causing low calcium."], ["Skin", "Rash and rarely Stevens-Johnson syndrome or toxic epidermal necrolysis occur."]], "journey": "Amifostine is given IV over a short time shortly before chemotherapy or radiation. It leaves the plasma very fast, with a half-life of about eight minutes, as membrane alkaline phosphatase converts it to WR-1065 and tissues take it up. The active thiol is then oxidized to disulfides inside cells. Very little of the parent drug is excreted in urine. Its protective window is short, which is why timing relative to treatment matters.", "sidefx": "Low blood pressure, the main limiting effect, comes from relaxation of blood vessel smooth muscle, so patients lie flat and are hydrated with blood pressure checked often. Nausea and vomiting are frequent and are prevented with antiemetics. Sulfur compounds cause a metallic taste, flushing and sneezing. Low calcium reflects reduced parathyroid hormone release. Serious skin reactions and anaphylaxis are rare but require stopping the drug. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Of the thousands of compounds screened in the Walter Reed program, WR-2721 was the one that made it to the pharmacy shelf decades later as a cancer supportive drug.", "src": ["Ethyol (amifostine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "levoleucovorin": {"origin": "In 1948 researchers found a factor in liver that the bacterium Leuconostoc citrovorum needed to grow. They called it citrovorum factor, and it proved to be folinic acid (5-formyltetrahydrofolate), later called leucovorin. Doctors soon learned that it could rescue normal cells from methotrexate and, from the 1980s, that it boosted fluorouracil against colon cancer. Leucovorin is a 50-50 mix of two mirror-image forms, and only the l-form is active. Spectrum Pharmaceuticals developed the pure active isomer, levoleucovorin, and the FDA approved Fusilev in 2008 for methotrexate rescue after high-dose therapy in osteosarcoma, with colorectal cancer use with fluorouracil added in 2011.", "timeline": [["1948", "Citrovorum factor, later known as leucovorin, is described as a bacterial growth factor."], ["1950s", "Leucovorin is used to rescue normal cells from methotrexate."], ["2008", "FDA approves levoleucovorin (Fusilev) for methotrexate rescue."], ["2011", "Approval is extended to use with fluorouracil in colorectal cancer."]], "cell": "Levoleucovorin is the active (6S) isomer of 5-formyltetrahydrofolate, already in reduced form. Methotrexate blocks dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR), so cells cannot regenerate tetrahydrofolate, and they run out of the folate cofactors needed for purine synthesis and for thymidylate synthase to make dTMP. DNA synthesis stops in S phase. Levoleucovorin enters cells through the reduced folate carrier and is converted to 5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate and other active folates without needing DHFR, so normal cells can make DNA again. This is rescue. With fluorouracil, the active metabolite FdUMP binds thymidylate synthase. Extra 5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate from levoleucovorin forms a tight ternary complex with FdUMP and the enzyme, prolonging inhibition and making fluorouracil more effective, and more toxic.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Restoring folate cofactors lets marrow cells resume DNA synthesis after high-dose methotrexate, preventing severe cytopenias."], ["Gut and mouth", "Mucosa is protected from methotrexate, but with fluorouracil the stronger enzyme block causes more stomatitis and diarrhea."], ["Kidneys", "Rescue lowers methotrexate injury to cells, though high methotrexate levels still require hydration and urine alkalinization."], ["Tumor", "Combined with fluorouracil it increases killing of colorectal cancer cells."]], "journey": "Levoleucovorin is given IV, either on a timed schedule after methotrexate or just before fluorouracil. It is quickly converted in the body to 5-methyltetrahydrofolate and other reduced folates, which circulate and enter cells. Its half-life is about five to six hours, and the active 5-methyl metabolite lasts a little longer. Folate metabolites leave mainly in the urine. Because it is already the active isomer, the amount needed is half that of racemic leucovorin.", "sidefx": "With methotrexate rescue, levoleucovorin has few side effects of its own. With fluorouracil, the main problems are fluorouracil toxicities made stronger by a tighter thymidylate synthase block: stomatitis, diarrhea, dehydration and myelosuppression, which can be severe in older adults. Hypersensitivity and rare seizures have been reported. It is not given intrathecally. It cannot rescue cells if given too late after methotrexate, and it competes with methotrexate for cell entry, so timing matters. For methotrexate toxicity with poor clearance, glucarpidase can also be used to break down methotrexate.", "src": ["Fusilev and Khapzory (levoleucovorin) FDA labels", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "eflapegrastim": {"origin": "The story of eflapegrastim starts with the colony-stimulating factors, which Donald Metcalf and colleagues in Melbourne began describing in the 1960s, and with Amgen's recombinant G-CSF filgrastim, approved in 1991. Filgrastim lasts only hours, so pegfilgrastim later added a polyethylene glycol tail for once-per-cycle use. The Korean company Hanmi Pharmaceutical took a different approach with its LAPSCOVERY platform, linking G-CSF to a human antibody Fc fragment through a short PEG linker to slow clearance. Spectrum Pharmaceuticals licensed it, and the FDA approved Rolvedon in September 2022 to reduce febrile neutropenia in patients receiving myelosuppressive chemotherapy.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Metcalf and colleagues describe colony-stimulating factors in Melbourne."], ["1991", "FDA approves filgrastim, the first recombinant G-CSF."], ["2022", "FDA approves eflapegrastim (Rolvedon) for chemotherapy-induced neutropenia."]], "cell": "Eflapegrastim is a recombinant human G-CSF analog joined by a short PEG linker to a human IgG4 Fc fragment. The G-CSF part binds the G-CSF receptor (CSF3R) on myeloid progenitor cells and neutrophils. Receptor dimerization activates JAK1 and JAK2, which phosphorylate STAT3 and STAT5, along with PI3K-AKT and MAPK pathways. These signals increase proliferation of neutrophil precursors, speed their maturation, and prevent apoptosis. G-CSF also cleaves CXCR4 retention signals in the marrow and pushes mature neutrophils into the blood. The Fc and linker make the molecule large, so it escapes kidney filtration, and Fc binding to the neonatal Fc receptor may also help it persist. It is cleared mostly by binding to G-CSF receptors on neutrophils, so as neutrophil counts recover, clearance speeds up.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Neutrophil precursors multiply and mature faster, so the neutrophil low point after chemotherapy is shallower and shorter."], ["Blood", "Absolute neutrophil count rises within about a day; very high white counts can occur."], ["Bones", "Marrow expansion stretches the bone lining and causes bone pain, especially in the pelvis, back and long bones."], ["Spleen", "Myeloid cells can expand in the spleen, rarely causing splenic enlargement or rupture."], ["Lungs", "Neutrophils activated in the lung can rarely cause ARDS."]], "journey": "Eflapegrastim is injected under the skin once per chemotherapy cycle, at least a day after chemotherapy. It is absorbed slowly from the injection site. Its large size keeps it out of kidney filtration. It is cleared mainly by neutrophils that bind and internalize it, plus general protein breakdown, so its half-life is long when neutrophils are low and shortens as counts rise. This self-regulating clearance lets one dose cover a cycle.", "sidefx": "Bone pain comes from rapid marrow expansion. Leukocytosis is an overshoot of the intended effect. Splenic rupture can occur because myeloid cells crowd the spleen; left shoulder or upper abdominal pain is a warning sign. ARDS reflects activated neutrophils collecting in the lung. People with sickle cell disease can have crises because more white cells promote vessel blockage. Capillary leak and glomerulonephritis are rare class effects. It is not given within the 14 days before or 24 hours after chemotherapy because it would make dividing marrow cells more vulnerable. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Rolvedon (eflapegrastim-xnst) FDA label", "Metcalf D. Blood 2008 (hematopoietic cytokines review)"]}, "sargramostim": {"origin": "In 1966 Ray Bradley and Donald Metcalf in Melbourne, and separately Dov Pluznik and Leo Sachs in Israel, showed that blood cell precursors could form colonies in soft agar if a growth factor was present. One of these colony-stimulating factors, granulocyte-macrophage CSF (GM-CSF), was purified and its gene was cloned in the 1980s. The Seattle company Immunex produced a recombinant human GM-CSF in yeast, called sargramostim. The FDA approved Leukine in 1991 to speed recovery of white cells after bone marrow transplant. In 2018 it was also approved to improve survival after a dangerous whole-body radiation exposure, the hematopoietic syndrome of acute radiation syndrome.", "timeline": [["1966", "Bradley and Metcalf, and Pluznik and Sachs, develop the colony assay that reveals colony-stimulating factors."], ["1980s", "The GM-CSF gene is cloned and recombinant protein is produced."], ["1991", "FDA approves Leukine (sargramostim) for marrow transplant recovery."], ["2018", "FDA approves sargramostim for acute radiation syndrome."]], "cell": "Sargramostim is a glycosylated recombinant human GM-CSF made in Saccharomyces cerevisiae, with leucine in place of proline at position 23. It binds the GM-CSF receptor, which has a ligand-specific alpha chain (CSF2RA) and a common beta chain shared with IL-3 and IL-5 receptors. Binding brings JAK2 molecules together, which phosphorylate STAT5 and activate MAPK and PI3K pathways. This stimulates proliferation and differentiation of granulocyte, macrophage and eosinophil progenitors, and it enhances the functions of mature cells, including neutrophil and macrophage killing, antigen presentation by dendritic cells, and cytokine release. That broader action, compared with G-CSF, explains both its uses and its fluid-retaining side effects.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "More neutrophil, monocyte and eosinophil precursors are made, speeding white cell recovery after transplant or chemotherapy."], ["Immune function", "Mature neutrophils, macrophages and dendritic cells become more active at fighting infection and presenting antigens."], ["Blood vessels", "Cytokine effects make vessels leaky, causing fluid retention, edema, effusions and rarely capillary leak syndrome."], ["Lungs", "Shortness of breath can occur as activated white cells collect in the lungs, especially during infusion."], ["First dose", "A first-dose reaction with flushing, low blood pressure, rapid heart rate and low oxygen can occur."], ["Whole body", "Fever, bone pain, fatigue and diarrhea reflect cytokine release and marrow expansion."]], "journey": "Sargramostim can be given by IV infusion or by injection under the skin. Subcutaneous doses peak in about one to four hours. The half-life is about one hour IV and two and a half to three hours subcutaneously, so it is given daily. It is broken down by proteolysis and taken up by cells carrying the receptor, and the kidneys are not the main route. White counts rise within days.", "sidefx": "GM-CSF acts on many myeloid cells and activates them, so side effects are broader than with G-CSF. Cytokines released by activated macrophages cause fever and malaise. Vessel leak causes fluid retention, pleural and pericardial effusions and weight gain, so caution is needed with heart failure or fluid overload. The first-dose reaction reflects sudden neutrophil activation and pooling in the lungs. Supraventricular arrhythmias can occur. Like other CSFs, it is not given within 24 hours of chemotherapy. There is no antidote; stopping it reverses most effects.", "fun": "Sargramostim is stockpiled by the US government for radiation emergencies because it can help the marrow recover after a large radiation exposure.", "src": ["Leukine (sargramostim) FDA label", "Metcalf D. Blood 2008", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "plerixafor": {"origin": "Plerixafor began as an HIV drug. In the late 1980s Erik De Clercq and colleagues at the Rega Institute in Belgium noticed that a commercial sample of cyclam, a ring-shaped metal binding compound, blocked HIV, and the activity came from a contaminant: two cyclam rings joined together, a bicyclam. The optimized bicyclam, AMD3100, was later found to block CXCR4, the co-receptor some HIV strains use to enter cells. In HIV trials it caused a surprising rise in white blood cells. Researchers realized it was releasing stem cells from the bone marrow. The Canadian company AnorMED redirected it to stem cell mobilization, Genzyme acquired AnorMED in 2006, and the FDA approved Mozobil in December 2008 for use with G-CSF in non-Hodgkin lymphoma and multiple myeloma.", "timeline": [["1980s", "De Clercq's group finds anti-HIV activity in a bicyclam contaminant of cyclam."], ["1997", "AMD3100 is shown to block the CXCR4 chemokine receptor."], ["2000s", "HIV studies reveal leukocytosis, leading to stem cell mobilization trials."], ["2008", "FDA approves Mozobil for stem cell mobilization."]], "cell": "Hematopoietic stem and progenitor cells are anchored in the bone marrow niche by CXCR4, a G protein-coupled chemokine receptor on their surface, which binds stromal cell-derived factor-1 alpha (SDF-1 alpha, also called CXCL12) made by marrow stromal cells and osteoblasts. CXCL12 binding activates Gi signaling, adhesion molecules such as VLA-4, and chemotaxis that keep the cells in place. Plerixafor is a positively charged bicyclam that binds acidic residues in the CXCR4 pocket and reversibly blocks CXCL12 binding. Without this retention signal, stem cells detach and move into the blood. CD34-positive cell counts peak about 10 to 14 hours after a dose. G-CSF works in a complementary way, partly by lowering marrow CXCL12 and cleaving adhesion molecules, so the combination yields more cells for apheresis. Plerixafor is not cytotoxic.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Stem and progenitor cells are released from the marrow niche into the circulation."], ["Blood", "CD34-positive cells and total white cells rise for about a day, providing enough stem cells for collection and later transplant."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and nausea are common, possibly from CXCR4 blockade on gut cells."], ["Spleen", "Mobilized cells can pool in the spleen, causing enlargement and, rarely, rupture."], ["Circulation", "Vasovagal reactions and orthostatic hypotension can occur, often soon after injection."]], "journey": "Plerixafor is injected under the skin in the evening, so that the CD34 peak lines up with apheresis the next morning. It is absorbed quickly, with plasma levels peaking in 30 to 60 minutes. Up to about half is bound to plasma proteins. It is not metabolized and is not a CYP substrate. About 70 percent leaves unchanged in the urine within a day, with a half-life of about three to five hours in normal kidney function, so the dose is reduced in kidney impairment.", "sidefx": "Gastrointestinal effects such as diarrhea and nausea are the most common, and injection-site redness reflects local irritation. Leukocytosis is the expected effect of releasing white cells. Splenic enlargement and rare rupture come from mobilized cells pooling in the spleen. In leukemia, it can also mobilize leukemic cells, so it is not used for that purpose. Vasovagal reactions and anaphylaxis can occur shortly after a dose. It causes fetal harm in animals. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Plerixafor's discovery started with a contaminant: the anti-HIV activity in a bottle of cyclam came from a tiny amount of a joined double-ring impurity.", "src": ["Mozobil (plerixafor) FDA label", "De Clercq E. Nat Rev Drug Discov 2003"]}, "motixafortide": {"origin": "Motixafortide belongs to a family of CXCR4-blocking peptides with an unusual ancestor: polyphemusin II, a defense peptide from the blood cells of the horseshoe crab. In the 1990s Japanese chemists, including the groups of Nobutaka Fujii and Hirokazu Tamamura in Kyoto, shortened and stabilized these peptides into potent CXCR4 inhibitors called the T-140 series. A refined cyclic peptide from this line, BKT140, was developed by the Israeli company Biokine Therapeutics and then BioLineRx as BL-8040. In the GENESIS trial, a single dose with filgrastim let most patients with multiple myeloma collect enough stem cells in one or two apheresis sessions. The FDA approved Aphexda in September 2023.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Japanese chemists develop T-140 peptides from horseshoe crab polyphemusin as CXCR4 blockers."], ["2010s", "BioLineRx develops the cyclic peptide BL-8040, later named motixafortide."], ["2023", "FDA approves Aphexda with filgrastim for stem cell mobilization in multiple myeloma."]], "cell": "Motixafortide is a synthetic cyclic peptide of 14 amino acids that binds CXCR4, the chemokine receptor that holds hematopoietic stem and progenitor cells in the marrow niche. It binds with high affinity and dissociates slowly, so it occupies the receptor much longer than plerixafor. By blocking binding of the ligand CXCL12 (SDF-1 alpha), it stops Gi-coupled signaling, integrin activation and the chemotactic pull that keep these cells anchored. The stem cells leave the marrow and enter the blood, where CD34-positive counts rise within hours and stay high through the apheresis window. Combined with filgrastim, which increases the number of progenitors and loosens other retention signals, it produces large stem cell yields from a single dose. It is not cytotoxic.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Stem and progenitor cells are released in large numbers because the CXCR4 retention signal is blocked for many hours."], ["Blood", "CD34-positive cells and white cells rise markedly, allowing enough stem cells to be collected in fewer sessions."], ["Skin and mast cells", "Injection-site pain, redness, itching and flushing are common, likely from mast cell histamine release by the cationic peptide."], ["Immune system", "Systemic hypersensitivity with hives, flushing or low blood pressure can occur, so premedication is used."]], "journey": "Motixafortide is given as a subcutaneous injection, after several days of filgrastim and about 10 to 14 hours before the first apheresis. It is absorbed from under the skin and has a plasma half-life of about two hours, but because it stays bound to CXCR4 for a long time, its effect lasts much longer than its blood levels. As a peptide it is broken down by proteases into smaller peptides and amino acids.", "sidefx": "Injection-site reactions, itching and flushing are thought to reflect mast cell degranulation and histamine release triggered by the positively charged peptide, which is why antihistamines, an H2 blocker and a leukotriene blocker are given beforehand. Systemic hypersensitivity and rare anaphylactic shock can follow. Leukocytosis is part of the intended effect. Like plerixafor, it can mobilize tumor cells, so it is not used in leukemia. There is no antidote; reactions are treated supportively.", "fun": "Horseshoe crabs, whose blue blood is also used to test drugs for bacterial toxins, supplied the starting peptide for this drug family.", "src": ["Aphexda (motixafortide) FDA label", "Crees ZD et al. Nat Med 2023 (GENESIS)", "Tamamura H et al. Biochem Biophys Res Commun 1998"]}, "trilaciclib": {"origin": "Trilaciclib grew from basic research on how blood stem cells depend on the enzymes CDK4 and CDK6 to leave the resting G1 phase. Norman Sharpless and colleagues at the University of North Carolina showed in mice that briefly pausing these stem cells with a CDK4/6 inhibitor protected them from chemotherapy. The company G1 Therapeutics, founded in North Carolina in 2008 based on this work, designed trilaciclib as a short-acting IV CDK4/6 inhibitor. Because small cell lung cancer cells usually lack the Rb protein, the drug pauses the marrow but not the tumor. The FDA approved Cosela in February 2021 to reduce chemotherapy-induced myelosuppression in extensive-stage small cell lung cancer, the first approved drug of its kind.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Sharpless and colleagues show that CDK4/6 inhibition protects mouse marrow from chemotherapy."], ["2008", "G1 Therapeutics is founded to develop the approach."], ["2021", "FDA approves Cosela, the first myeloprotective CDK4/6 inhibitor."]], "cell": "To move from G1 into S phase, a cell uses cyclin D bound to cyclin-dependent kinases 4 and 6. These kinases phosphorylate the retinoblastoma protein (Rb), which then releases the E2F transcription factors that switch on S-phase genes. Trilaciclib reversibly inhibits CDK4 and CDK6, keeping Rb unphosphorylated and E2F bound, so hematopoietic stem and progenitor cells pause in G1. Most cytotoxic chemotherapy, such as platinum, etoposide and topotecan, kills cells in S phase or mitosis, so paused marrow cells are spared. The pause lasts only hours, so cells resume dividing once the chemotherapy is gone. Small cell lung cancer cells usually have lost Rb, so they do not depend on CDK4/6 and are not arrested, keeping them sensitive to chemotherapy.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "A brief G1 pause protects stem and progenitor cells, lowering severe neutropenia and the need for red cell and platelet transfusions."], ["Blood cells", "Fewer episodes of severe neutropenia mean less need for G-CSF and fewer chemotherapy delays."], ["Veins", "The IV infusion can cause phlebitis and thrombophlebitis at the infusion site."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease or pneumonitis can occur, as with other CDK4/6 inhibitors."], ["Electrolytes", "Low calcium, potassium and phosphate are common lab findings."]], "journey": "Trilaciclib is given as a short IV infusion within four hours before chemotherapy on each treatment day. It spreads widely into tissues and is about 70 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down, mainly by CYP3A4, and the products leave mostly in the feces with little in the urine. The half-life is about 14 hours, but the G1 arrest in marrow cells is transient and wears off in hours.", "sidefx": "Fatigue and electrolyte changes are common. The infusion is irritating to veins, causing phlebitis. Interstitial lung disease is a class effect of CDK4/6 inhibitors and may reflect immune or direct effects on lung cells. Hypersensitivity reactions can occur. Because it inhibits drug transporters such as OCT2 and MATE, it can raise levels of drugs like dofetilide and cisplatin cleared by those transporters. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Cosela (trilaciclib) FDA label", "Johnson SM et al. J Clin Invest 2010", "Weiss JM et al. Ann Oncol 2019"]}, "fosaprepitant": {"origin": "In 1931 Ulf von Euler and John Gaddum found a substance in horse brain and gut extracts that contracted intestine and lowered blood pressure. They called it substance P. Decades later it was shown to be a neuropeptide that acts on neurokinin-1 (NK1) receptors, including in the brainstem centers that control vomiting. Merck chemists developed aprepitant, an oral NK1 antagonist approved in 2003 that especially helped delayed vomiting after chemotherapy. Aprepitant does not dissolve well in water, so Merck made fosaprepitant, a water-soluble phosphate prodrug for IV use. The FDA approved Emend for injection in January 2008, allowing a single IV dose on the day of chemotherapy.", "timeline": [["1931", "Von Euler and Gaddum discover substance P."], ["1980s", "Substance P is shown to act on NK1 receptors."], ["2003", "FDA approves oral aprepitant."], ["2008", "FDA approves IV fosaprepitant (Emend for injection)."]], "cell": "Fosaprepitant is a phosphoryl prodrug. Phosphatases in the blood and tissues remove the phosphate within about 30 minutes, releasing aprepitant. Aprepitant crosses the blood-brain barrier and binds the human NK1 receptor, a Gq-coupled receptor for substance P, with high affinity and selectivity. Chemotherapy injures gut cells, which release substance P and serotonin, and substance P is also released centrally in the nucleus tractus solitarius and area postrema. Blocking NK1 receptors there prevents substance P from activating phospholipase C and the calcium signals that drive the vomiting reflex. The NK1 pathway dominates the delayed phase of chemotherapy-induced emesis, days two to five, which is why NK1 antagonists add so much to 5-HT3 antagonists and dexamethasone. It also augments their effect in the acute phase.", "body": [["Brainstem", "Blocking NK1 receptors in the nucleus tractus solitarius and area postrema prevents acute and especially delayed nausea and vomiting after chemotherapy."], ["Gut", "Fewer vomiting episodes, with occasional diarrhea or constipation."], ["Infusion site", "The infusion can irritate the vein, causing pain, redness or thrombophlebitis, and rarely necrosis with extravasation."], ["Liver enzymes", "Aprepitant moderately inhibits and then induces CYP3A4 and induces CYP2C9, changing levels of drugs like dexamethasone and warfarin."]], "journey": "Fosaprepitant is infused IV before chemotherapy and is converted to aprepitant within about 30 minutes. Aprepitant is over 95 percent bound to plasma proteins and crosses into the brain. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP3A4, with smaller roles for CYP1A2 and CYP2C19, and the metabolites leave in urine and feces. Its half-life is about 9 to 13 hours, and a single dose covers the delayed phase.", "sidefx": "The drug itself is usually well tolerated; many reported effects such as fatigue and neutropenia come from the chemotherapy. Infusion-site reactions come from the formulation irritating the vein, and hypersensitivity can include anaphylaxis. The most important issues are interactions. Inhibiting CYP3A4 raises levels of dexamethasone (so its dose is lowered) and some chemotherapy drugs. Inducing CYP2C9 lowers warfarin levels and INR for a couple of weeks, and lowers hormonal contraceptive effect. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The P in substance P simply stood for the powder the extract was dried into, not for pain.", "src": ["Emend for injection (fosaprepitant) FDA label", "von Euler US, Gaddum JH. J Physiol 1931", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "palonosetron": {"origin": "In the late 1980s and early 1990s the first 5-HT3 antagonists, ondansetron and granisetron, transformed chemotherapy nausea, but they mostly helped the first day. Chemists at Syntex in California made a new 5-HT3 blocker with a very different structure and much higher receptor affinity, coded RS-25259. The Swiss company Helsinn Healthcare developed it as palonosetron. With a half-life of about 40 hours, it protected into the delayed phase. The FDA approved Aloxi in 2003 for chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting, and later for postoperative nausea and vomiting.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Syntex researchers make RS-25259, later named palonosetron."], ["2003", "FDA approves Aloxi for chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting."], ["2008", "FDA approves palonosetron for postoperative nausea and vomiting."]], "cell": "The 5-HT3 receptor is a ligand-gated cation channel, not a G protein-coupled receptor. Chemotherapy damages enterochromaffin cells in the gut lining, which release serotonin. Serotonin binds 5-HT3 receptors on vagal afferent nerve endings, opening the channel and sending signals to the nucleus tractus solitarius and the chemoreceptor trigger zone in the area postrema, which also has 5-HT3 receptors. Palonosetron competitively blocks these receptors with very high affinity. It also binds an allosteric site with positive cooperativity, triggers receptor internalization, and inhibits crosstalk between 5-HT3 and NK1 receptor signaling. Together with its long half-life, this extends protection beyond the first day into the delayed phase, unlike earlier drugs in the class.", "body": [["Gut and vagus nerve", "Blocking 5-HT3 receptors on vagal afferents prevents serotonin released by damaged gut cells from triggering the vomiting reflex."], ["Brainstem", "Blocking receptors in the area postrema and nucleus tractus solitarius reduces nausea from chemotherapy and after surgery."], ["Bowel", "Reduced serotonin signaling in the gut slows motility, causing constipation."], ["Head", "Headache is the most common side effect, as with other drugs in the class."], ["Heart", "Unlike older 5-HT3 blockers, it has little clinically meaningful effect on the QT interval at approved doses."]], "journey": "Palonosetron is given as a single IV injection about 30 minutes before chemotherapy or before anesthesia. It works within about 30 minutes and has a long half-life of about 40 hours. About 62 percent is bound to plasma proteins. Roughly half is metabolized in the liver, mainly by CYP2D6 with help from CYP3A4 and CYP1A2, into nearly inactive metabolites. About 40 percent leaves unchanged in the urine and the rest as metabolites.", "sidefx": "Headache and constipation come from 5-HT3 blockade: serotonin normally helps drive gut motility, and changes in cranial vessel or nerve signaling may explain headache. Dizziness and fatigue occur. Serotonin syndrome is possible when combined with other serotonergic drugs, because blocking one receptor type may shunt serotonin to others. Hypersensitivity and rare anaphylaxis occur, including in people allergic to other 5-HT3 antagonists. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Aloxi (palonosetron) FDA label", "Rojas C et al. Anesth Analg 2008", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "luspatercept": {"origin": "Luspatercept came from work at Acceleron Pharma in Cambridge, Massachusetts, on the activin receptor family, which controls muscle, bone and blood cell growth. Acceleron fused the outer part of a modified activin receptor type IIB to an antibody Fc and found that, in animals, this ligand trap sharply raised red blood cells by helping late red cell precursors mature, a different step from erythropoietin. Acceleron developed it with Celgene, later part of Bristol Myers Squibb. The FDA approved Reblozyl in November 2019 for anemia in beta thalassemia requiring regular transfusions, and in April 2020 for anemia in lower-risk myelodysplastic syndromes.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Acceleron develops activin receptor ligand traps and notes their effects on red cells."], ["2019", "FDA approves Reblozyl for transfusion-dependent beta thalassemia."], ["2020", "FDA approves Reblozyl for anemia in lower-risk myelodysplastic syndromes."]], "cell": "Luspatercept is a fusion protein made of a modified extracellular domain of human activin receptor type IIB linked to human IgG1 Fc. It acts as a ligand trap, binding members of the TGF-beta superfamily such as GDF11, activin B and GDF8 before they reach their receptors. In ineffective erythropoiesis, as in beta thalassemia and MDS, these ligands signal through activin receptors to phosphorylate Smad2 and Smad3, which pile up and block late-stage red cell precursors from maturing. Trapping the ligands lowers Smad2/3 signaling, so late erythroblasts can mature into red cells. This is why it is called an erythroid maturation agent: erythropoiesis-stimulating agents act mainly on early progenitors through the EPO receptor, while luspatercept acts later in the pathway.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Late red cell precursors mature more effectively, so more red cells reach the blood and fewer transfusions are needed."], ["Blood", "Hemoglobin rises gradually over several weeks as the newly matured red cells accumulate."], ["Blood vessels", "Blood pressure can rise, and a higher red cell mass may contribute to clotting events such as DVT, pulmonary embolism and stroke."], ["Bones and muscles", "Bone pain, joint pain and muscle aches are common, possibly reflecting activity of TGF-beta family ligands in these tissues."], ["Extramedullary masses", "In beta thalassemia, stimulated blood-forming tissue outside the marrow can grow and press on the spinal cord."]], "journey": "Luspatercept is injected under the skin every three weeks. It is absorbed slowly, with blood levels peaking about a week after injection. Like antibodies, it is a large protein, so it is not filtered by the kidneys or metabolized by liver enzymes; it is broken down into peptides and amino acids by general protein catabolism. Its half-life is about 11 days. Hemoglobin response develops over several weeks.", "sidefx": "Higher red cell mass and changes in vascular signaling explain rises in blood pressure and an increase in thromboembolic events, especially in splenectomized thalassemia patients or those with other clot risks. Extramedullary hematopoietic masses can expand because the drug stimulates red cell production wherever it happens. Musculoskeletal pain, headache and fatigue are common. It caused fetal harm in animals, consistent with the role of these ligands in development. There is no antidote.", "fun": "A close relative built on the same activin receptor idea, sotatercept, is used for pulmonary arterial hypertension, showing how one signaling family touches both blood and blood vessels.", "src": ["Reblozyl (luspatercept-aamt) FDA label", "Cappellini MD et al. N Engl J Med 2020 (BELIEVE)", "Fenaux P et al. N Engl J Med 2020 (MEDALIST)"]}, "zoledronic-acid": {"origin": "Bisphosphonates started as industrial chemicals. In the 19th century chemists made them, and in the 20th century they were used to keep calcium scale out of pipes and textile equipment. In the 1960s Herbert Fleisch and colleagues, working in Davos and Bern, showed that pyrophosphate, a natural inhibitor of calcium crystal formation, controlled bone mineral, and that stable bisphosphonate look-alikes could block bone resorption. Over the next decades, adding nitrogen to the side chain made the drugs far more potent. Zoledronic acid, with a nitrogen-containing imidazole ring, was developed by Ciba-Geigy, which became Novartis. The FDA approved Zometa in 2001 for hypercalcemia of malignancy, later for bone metastases and myeloma, and approved Reclast in 2007 as a once-yearly infusion for osteoporosis.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Fleisch and colleagues show that bisphosphonates inhibit bone resorption."], ["1990s", "Nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates are found to inhibit farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase."], ["2001", "FDA approves Zometa for hypercalcemia of malignancy."], ["2007", "FDA approves Reclast as a once-yearly osteoporosis treatment."]], "cell": "Zoledronic acid has a P-C-P backbone that binds calcium in hydroxyapatite, so it concentrates on bone surfaces where resorption is active. When osteoclasts dissolve bone, they take up the drug by endocytosis. Inside, the nitrogen-containing imidazole side chain inhibits farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase, an enzyme of the mevalonate (cholesterol synthesis) pathway. Without farnesyl and geranylgeranyl pyrophosphate, small GTPases such as Ras, Rho, Rac and Rab cannot be prenylated and attached to membranes. These proteins are needed for the osteoclast's ruffled border, cytoskeleton and vesicle traffic. The osteoclasts stop resorbing bone and undergo apoptosis. Less bone breakdown means less calcium released into the blood and fewer fractures and skeletal events. In circulating gamma delta T cells, the buildup of upstream isoprenoids triggers activation, which explains the acute phase reaction.", "body": [["Bone", "Osteoclast activity drops, so bone breakdown slows, bone density rises and fractures and skeletal-related events from metastases decrease."], ["Blood calcium", "Less calcium released from bone lowers serum calcium in hypercalcemia of malignancy over several days."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys excrete the drug unchanged; high peak levels can injure tubules, so infusion time and kidney function matter."], ["Immune system", "The first infusion often causes an acute phase reaction with fever, muscle aches and flu-like symptoms."], ["Jaw", "Long-term suppression of bone turnover can lead to osteonecrosis of the jaw, especially after dental extractions."], ["Eyes", "Rarely causes eye inflammation such as uveitis or scleritis, which needs prompt eye evaluation."]], "journey": "Zoledronic acid is given by IV infusion over at least 15 minutes. Plasma levels fall quickly as the drug binds bone or is filtered by the kidneys. It is not metabolized. About 40 percent is excreted unchanged in the urine within a day, and the rest is bound to bone, where it can remain for years and is released very slowly, giving a terminal half-life of about 146 hours from plasma. Calcium falls over several days, and the effect on bone lasts a year or more.", "sidefx": "The acute phase reaction comes from isoprenoid buildup that activates gamma delta T cells and releases cytokines. Kidney toxicity reflects high drug concentration in tubular cells, so dosing depends on kidney function and infusion is not rushed. Hypocalcemia follows from blocking bone resorption, especially with low vitamin D. Osteonecrosis of the jaw and atypical femur fractures are thought to come from long-term suppression of normal bone remodeling, so old microdamage is not repaired. Ocular inflammation is rare. There is no antidote; calcium and vitamin D are used for hypocalcemia.", "fun": "Bisphosphonates bind bone so tightly that a single dose of zoledronic acid can still be detected in the skeleton years later.", "src": ["Zometa and Reclast (zoledronic acid) FDA labels", "Fleisch H. Bisphosphonates in Bone Disease", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "pamidronate": {"origin": "Pamidronate, also known as APD, was one of the first nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates. It was synthesized in the 1970s and studied closely by researchers at Leiden University in the Netherlands, who used it to treat Paget disease and cancer-related high calcium. Adding an amino group to the side chain made it far more potent than etidronate, the first clinical bisphosphonate. Ciba-Geigy marketed it as Aredia, and the FDA approved it in 1991 for hypercalcemia of malignancy, later adding Paget disease, osteolytic bone metastases from breast cancer and myeloma bone lesions. The Aredia brand has been discontinued, but generic pamidronate remains available.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Pamidronate (APD) is synthesized and studied, notably in Leiden."], ["1991", "FDA approves Aredia for hypercalcemia of malignancy."], ["1990s", "Approvals expand to Paget disease, breast cancer bone metastases and myeloma."]], "cell": "Pamidronate's P-C-P backbone binds hydroxyapatite on bone surfaces, especially where resorption is active. Osteoclasts take it up as they dissolve bone. Its aminopropyl side chain inhibits farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase in the mevalonate pathway, though less potently than zoledronic acid. Without farnesyl and geranylgeranyl groups, small GTPases such as Ras, Rho and Rab cannot be prenylated and anchored to membranes. The osteoclast loses its ruffled border, cytoskeletal organization and vesicle transport, stops resorbing bone and undergoes apoptosis. The result is less calcium released from bone, lower serum calcium, and fewer skeletal complications in metastatic disease. Isoprenoid buildup also activates gamma delta T cells, causing a fever reaction.", "body": [["Bone", "Slowed osteoclast resorption reduces bone pain, fractures and other skeletal complications from metastases and myeloma, and calms Paget disease."], ["Blood calcium", "Serum calcium falls within one to two days and reaches its lowest point about a week later."], ["Kidneys", "The drug is excreted unchanged by the kidneys and can damage them, including a form of collapsing focal segmental glomerulosclerosis."], ["Immune system", "Fever and flu-like symptoms after the first infusion reflect an acute phase reaction."], ["Electrolytes", "Low phosphate, magnesium and potassium are common."], ["Jaw and eyes", "Osteonecrosis of the jaw and eye inflammation such as uveitis or scleritis are rare but recognized."]], "journey": "Pamidronate is given by slow IV infusion over several hours. It quickly leaves the blood to bind bone or be filtered by the kidneys. It is not metabolized. The portion not taken up by bone is excreted unchanged in urine, with a plasma elimination half-life of about 28 hours, while the bone-bound drug stays for a long time. Serum calcium usually returns to normal within days and stays normal for about two weeks or longer.", "sidefx": "Most side effects follow from its two homes, bone and kidney. Blocking resorption lowers calcium, phosphate and magnesium. High concentrations in the kidney injure tubules and glomeruli, so it is infused slowly and renal function is checked before each dose. The acute phase reaction comes from gamma delta T cell activation. Osteonecrosis of the jaw and atypical fractures reflect long-term suppression of bone remodeling. Infusion site reactions occur. There is no antidote; calcium and vitamin D correct hypocalcemia.", "src": ["Pamidronate disodium injection FDA label", "Fleisch H. Bisphosphonates in Bone Disease", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "crotalidae-polyvalent-immune-fab": {"origin": "Antivenom therapy began in the 1890s, when Albert Calmette at the Pasteur Institute and others showed that serum from animals immunized with venom could protect against snakebite, building on Emil von Behring and Shibasaburo Kitasato's 1890 work on antitoxins. For decades US rattlesnake bites were treated with a whole-serum horse antivenom made by Wyeth from the 1950s, which caused frequent allergic reactions and serum sickness. Researchers then developed a purer product: sheep were immunized with venoms from four North American pit vipers, and their antibodies were cut with the enzyme papain into small Fab fragments, removing the Fc portion that drives many reactions. The FDA approved CroFab in October 2000, and it became the standard US treatment for rattlesnake, copperhead and cottonmouth envenomation.", "timeline": [["1890", "Von Behring and Kitasato describe antitoxin serum therapy."], ["1890s", "Calmette develops one of the first snake antivenoms."], ["1950s", "Wyeth's equine whole-IgG crotalid antivenom comes into use in the US."], ["2000", "FDA approves CroFab, an ovine Fab antivenom."]], "cell": "Sheep are immunized with venom from the western diamondback (Crotalus atrox), eastern diamondback (Crotalus adamanteus), Mojave rattlesnake (Crotalus scutulatus) and cottonmouth (Agkistrodon piscivorus). Their IgG is purified and digested with papain into Fab fragments of about 50 kDa, each with one antigen-binding site and no Fc. The fragments bind venom components including snake venom metalloproteinases that break down capillary basement membranes and cause swelling and bleeding, phospholipase A2 enzymes that damage cell membranes and muscle, and thrombin-like enzymes that consume fibrinogen. Binding blocks their active sites or traps them in complexes, and the small size helps Fab reach venom in the tissues and redistribute it into the blood, where the complexes are filtered by the kidneys. Fab does not repair damage that has already occurred.", "body": [["Bite site and limb", "Neutralizing metalloproteinases and phospholipases stops progression of swelling, pain and tissue injury."], ["Blood clotting", "Binding thrombin-like enzymes reverses low fibrinogen and prolonged clotting times."], ["Platelets", "Low platelet counts from venom recover as toxins are neutralized."], ["Circulation", "Systemic effects such as low blood pressure and vomiting resolve."], ["Immune system", "Sheep proteins can cause hives, itching or anaphylaxis, and delayed serum sickness one to three weeks later."]], "journey": "CroFab is reconstituted and given by IV infusion, with repeat doses until the envenomation is controlled, then maintenance doses. The Fab fragments spread from the blood into tissue fluid and bind venom. Fab is small enough for the kidneys to filter, and both free Fab and Fab-venom complexes are cleared renally, with an elimination half-life of about 12 to 23 hours. Venom from the bite site can keep entering the blood longer than Fab persists, which is why coagulopathy can recur days later.", "sidefx": "Allergic reactions arise because the body sees sheep protein as foreign; removing the Fc portion and the immunogenic parts lowers but does not remove this risk. People allergic to papain or papaya may react. Serum sickness is a type III immune complex reaction days to weeks later. Recurrent coagulopathy and low platelets happen because Fab is cleared faster than venom depots in the tissue, so follow-up labs are checked. Anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine and stopping the infusion.", "fun": "The sheep used to make CroFab are raised in Australia, which is free of many livestock diseases.", "src": ["CroFab (crotalidae polyvalent immune fab) FDA label", "Dart RC et al. Arch Intern Med 2001", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]}, "crotalidae-immune-fab2-equine": {"origin": "Anavip was developed by Instituto Bioclon in Mexico, a company with a long history of making antivenoms for Latin American snakes and scorpions. Horses are immunized with venom from two Latin American pit vipers, the fer-de-lance Bothrops asper and the South American rattlesnake Crotalus durissus, and their antibodies are digested with pepsin to make F(ab')2 fragments. Because pit viper venoms share many toxin families, these antibodies cross-neutralize North American rattlesnake venom. The product was used in Latin America before US trials showed it worked against rattlesnake bites and had less late return of coagulopathy than CroFab. The FDA approved Anavip in May 2015 for North American rattlesnake envenomation, and its label later broadened to North American pit vipers.", "timeline": [["1890s", "Calmette develops early snake antivenoms using immunized animals."], ["2000s", "Instituto Bioclon's equine F(ab')2 antivenom is studied in US rattlesnake bites."], ["2015", "FDA approves Anavip for North American rattlesnake envenomation."]], "cell": "Horses are immunized with Bothrops asper and Crotalus durissus venoms, and their IgG is digested with pepsin. Pepsin removes the Fc portion but leaves the two antigen-binding arms joined, making F(ab')2 fragments of about 110 kDa with two binding sites each. These fragments bind and neutralize conserved pit viper toxins: metalloproteinases that break down capillary walls and cause swelling and bleeding, phospholipase A2 that damages membranes and muscle, and thrombin-like enzymes that consume fibrinogen. Two binding sites allow them to cross-link toxins into larger complexes that are removed by the reticuloendothelial system. Because the fragment is too big for fast kidney filtration, it stays in the blood for days, matching the slow release of venom from the bite site and lowering late recurrence of coagulopathy.", "body": [["Bite site and limb", "Neutralizing local toxins stops progression of swelling, pain and tissue damage."], ["Blood clotting", "Binding thrombin-like enzymes reverses venom-induced low fibrinogen and prolonged clotting, with less late recurrence."], ["Platelets", "Venom-induced thrombocytopenia improves as toxins are neutralized."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause itching, rash, hives, fever, anaphylaxis or delayed serum sickness."]], "journey": "Anavip is reconstituted and given as an IV infusion, with additional doses if signs of envenomation continue. The large F(ab')2 fragments mostly stay in the blood and nearby tissue fluid, where they bind venom as it leaks from the bite. They are too large for rapid kidney filtration and are broken down slowly by protein catabolism, with a half-life of about 133 hours, about five to six days. This long presence covers the time venom keeps entering the blood.", "sidefx": "Allergic reactions occur because horse proteins are foreign; removing Fc and purifying the product reduces but does not remove the risk. People with horse allergy or prior equine antivenom are at higher risk. Serum sickness, a delayed immune complex reaction, can cause rash, fever and joint pain. Because the fragments last longer, late coagulopathy is less common than with Fab products, but labs are still followed. Anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine.", "src": ["Anavip (crotalidae immune F(ab')2 equine) FDA label", "Bush SP et al. Ann Emerg Med 2015", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]}, "north-american-coral-snake-antivenin": {"origin": "The eastern coral snake, Micrurus fulvius, is an elapid, a relative of cobras, and its venom causes paralysis rather than swelling. Wyeth Laboratories produced North American Coral Snake Antivenin by immunizing horses with Micrurus fulvius venom and purifying their antibodies, and it was licensed in the US in 1967. Because coral snake bites are rare and the product was hard to make profitably, Wyeth stopped routine production in the early 2000s. To avoid running out, the FDA repeatedly extended the expiration dates of existing lots after testing. Pfizer, which acquired Wyeth, later made new lots. It remains the only FDA-approved US coral snake antivenom, and supply has often been limited.", "timeline": [["1890s", "Early antivenoms are made from the serum of immunized animals."], ["1967", "Wyeth's North American Coral Snake Antivenin is licensed in the US."], ["2000s", "Production stops and the FDA extends expiration dates to preserve supply."], ["2010s", "Pfizer produces new lots of the antivenin."]], "cell": "Coral snake venom contains alpha-neurotoxins, small three-finger toxin proteins that bind the alpha subunits of nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction. This blocks acetylcholine from opening the receptor channel, so muscles cannot depolarize, causing descending flaccid paralysis similar to a nondepolarizing paralytic drug. The antivenin is whole equine IgG raised against Micrurus fulvius venom. Its antibodies bind free toxin in the blood and tissues, preventing new binding to receptors. Toxin that has already bound tightly to receptors is not easily removed, so the antivenin stops paralysis from progressing but does not quickly reverse it. Recovery depends on the body making new receptors. This is why treatment is given early, often before symptoms appear, when a bite is confirmed.", "body": [["Nerves and muscles", "Neutralizing alpha-neurotoxins prevents further blockade of nicotinic receptors and stops paralysis from progressing."], ["Eyes and face", "Ptosis, double vision and slurred speech are early signs of venom action that treatment aims to halt."], ["Breathing", "By preventing diaphragm and respiratory muscle paralysis, it can avoid the need for mechanical ventilation."], ["Immune system", "Whole horse IgG commonly causes allergic reactions, anaphylaxis and delayed serum sickness."]], "journey": "The antivenin is reconstituted and given by slow IV infusion. Whole IgG is large and stays mainly in the blood and extracellular fluid. It is not filtered by the kidneys; it is removed slowly by the reticuloendothelial system and normal antibody catabolism, so it lasts several days. It binds toxin soon after infusion. Because coral snake symptoms can be delayed for many hours, early treatment of a confirmed bite is common.", "sidefx": "Because it is whole horse immunoglobulin with its Fc portion, allergic reactions are more frequent than with fragment antivenoms. Immediate reactions range from hives to anaphylaxis, so epinephrine and airway equipment are kept ready. Serum sickness, an immune complex reaction to the foreign protein, causes rash, fever and joint pain one to three weeks later and is treated with steroids and antihistamines. If antivenin is not available, care is supportive with airway protection and ventilation until new receptors form.", "fun": "The rhyme often used to tell coral snakes from harmless mimics only works for North American species and is not reliable elsewhere.", "src": ["North American Coral Snake Antivenin FDA label", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]}, "black-widow-spider-antivenin": {"origin": "Black widow spiders, Latrodectus mactans and related species, produce alpha-latrotoxin, one of the most potent nerve toxins known for its size. In the early 20th century researchers learned to make antivenom by injecting horses with small amounts of spider venom and collecting their serum. Merck's Antivenin (Latrodectus mactans) has been licensed in the US since the 1930s and is still made by immunizing horses with venom extracted from spiders. It is whole equine serum protein, so it carries a real allergy risk, and it is usually reserved for severe envenomation with pain and muscle spasm not controlled by other treatment.", "timeline": [["1900s", "Horse serum antivenoms are developed for venomous animal bites."], ["1930s", "Merck's black widow antivenin is licensed in the US."], ["1990s", "Neurexins and latrophilins are identified as receptors for alpha-latrotoxin."]], "cell": "Alpha-latrotoxin is a large protein that binds receptors on presynaptic nerve terminals, mainly neurexins and latrophilins (adhesion G protein-coupled receptors). After binding, it forms tetramers that insert into the membrane and create cation-permeable pores. Calcium floods in, and the toxin also directly triggers vesicle fusion, causing massive release of acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction and norepinephrine and other transmitters at autonomic synapses. The result is painful muscle cramping and rigidity, sweating, high blood pressure and fast heart rate. The antivenin is equine IgG against Latrodectus mactans venom. It binds circulating toxin and blocks it from reaching nerve terminals, and it can relieve symptoms quickly when toxin is still active.", "body": [["Muscles", "Neutralizing toxin relieves severe cramps, abdominal rigidity and back and chest pain caused by excess acetylcholine release."], ["Autonomic system", "Sweating, high blood pressure and fast heart rate from norepinephrine release settle as toxin is bound."], ["Pain", "Symptom relief often begins within one to three hours, sooner than with opioids and benzodiazepines alone."], ["Immune system", "Horse serum protein can cause allergic reactions, anaphylaxis and delayed serum sickness."]], "journey": "The antivenin is reconstituted and given IV, often diluted and infused slowly, or by IM injection. Whole IgG stays in the blood and tissue fluid and is cleared slowly by the reticuloendothelial system over several days. It binds toxin that has not yet reached nerve terminals. A skin test may be considered by some clinicians, but it does not reliably predict reactions.", "sidefx": "Allergic reactions come from the foreign horse proteins in a whole serum product, and anaphylaxis, including deaths, has been reported. People with horse allergy, asthma or prior equine products are at higher risk, so epinephrine is kept ready. Serum sickness one to three weeks later causes rash, fever and joint pain. Because black widow bites are rarely fatal, its risks are weighed against severity. Care without antivenin relies on opioids and benzodiazepines.", "fun": "Only the larger female black widow can deliver a dangerous bite; males are small and rarely bite people.", "src": ["Antivenin (Latrodectus mactans) FDA label", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]}, "centruroides-scorpion-immune-fab2": {"origin": "In the southwestern United States, especially Arizona, the bark scorpion Centruroides sculpturatus causes thousands of stings each year, and young children can develop severe neurotoxic symptoms. A goat-derived antivenom produced in Arizona was used locally for years but was never FDA approved, and production ended. Instituto Bioclon in Mexico, where Centruroides stings are a major public health problem, made an equine F(ab')2 antivenom against several Mexican Centruroides species. Clinical work led by researchers at the University of Arizona, including Leslie Boyer, showed that it quickly reversed severe scorpion envenomation in children. The FDA approved Anascorp in August 2011, the first FDA-approved scorpion antivenom.", "timeline": [["2009", "A trial shows rapid symptom resolution in children with severe scorpion stings."], ["2011", "FDA approves Anascorp, the first US scorpion antivenom."]], "cell": "Centruroides venom contains small neurotoxins that bind voltage-gated sodium channels on nerve cells. Beta toxins shift channel activation to more negative voltages, and other toxins delay inactivation, so channels open too easily and stay open longer. Neurons fire repeatedly, releasing acetylcholine and catecholamines. This causes roving eye movements, muscle fasciculations, involuntary jerking, excess saliva, agitation and breathing trouble. Anascorp is made by immunizing horses with venom from C. noxius, C. limpidus limpidus, C. limpidus tecomanus and C. suffusus suffusus, then digesting the IgG with pepsin to remove Fc and make F(ab')2 fragments. These fragments cross-neutralize the closely related toxins of the Arizona bark scorpion, binding them so they cannot reach the sodium channels.", "body": [["Nervous system", "Neutralizing sodium channel toxins stops repetitive nerve firing, so roving eye movements, jerking and fasciculations resolve."], ["Airway and breathing", "Less hypersalivation and muscle incoordination reduce airway risk and the need for sedation or intubation."], ["Behavior", "Agitation and restlessness in children settle, usually within hours."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause rash, itching, fever, vomiting, anaphylaxis or delayed serum sickness."]], "journey": "Anascorp is reconstituted and given as an IV infusion, sometimes followed by more vials if symptoms persist. The F(ab')2 fragments distribute into the blood and tissue fluid and bind venom. Symptoms often resolve within about one to four hours. The fragments are too large for rapid kidney filtration and are broken down slowly, with a half-life of about 159 hours, so they remain for days.", "sidefx": "Side effects mainly reflect reaction to foreign horse protein, even though the Fc portion is removed. Vomiting, fever, rash, nausea and itching are the most common. Serum sickness, an immune complex reaction days to weeks later, causes rash, fever and joint pain. Anaphylaxis is possible, particularly in people with horse allergy or prior equine products, so epinephrine is kept ready. Without antivenom, severe cases are managed with sedation and airway support.", "src": ["Anascorp (Centruroides scorpion immune F(ab')2 equine) FDA label", "Boyer LV et al. N Engl J Med 2009", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]}, "nimodipine": {"origin": "Nimodipine was developed by the German company Bayer in the 1970s as part of its work on dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers, a family that also includes nifedipine. Chemists made it more lipid soluble so it could cross the blood-brain barrier and act on brain vessels. Trials in the 1980s, including a landmark British trial, showed that it reduced death and disability from delayed brain ischemia after aneurysmal subarachnoid hemorrhage. The FDA approved Nimotop capsules in 1988 for this use. In 2013 an oral solution, Nymalize, was approved, partly to reduce the danger of nurses drawing capsule liquid into syringes and giving it by vein. The Nimotop brand has since been discontinued, with generic capsules and Nymalize still available.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Bayer develops nimodipine as a cerebrovascular-selective dihydropyridine."], ["1988", "FDA approves Nimotop for subarachnoid hemorrhage."], ["1989", "The British aneurysm nimodipine trial reports improved outcomes."], ["2013", "FDA approves Nymalize oral solution."]], "cell": "Nimodipine blocks L-type voltage-gated calcium channels (Cav1.2) by binding the dihydropyridine site on the alpha-1 subunit, favoring the inactivated state of the channel. In vascular smooth muscle, calcium entering through these channels binds calmodulin, activating myosin light chain kinase and contraction. Blocking entry relaxes arterial smooth muscle. Its high lipophilicity lets it cross the blood-brain barrier, and it acts preferentially on cerebral arterioles. After subarachnoid hemorrhage, it improves outcomes even though large-vessel angiographic vasospasm is not clearly reduced. Proposed explanations include dilation of small vessels and collateral flow, and neuroprotection by limiting calcium overload in ischemic neurons, which otherwise activates proteases, phospholipases and cell death pathways.", "body": [["Brain", "Fewer delayed ischemic neurologic deficits after aneurysmal subarachnoid hemorrhage, improving survival and recovery."], ["Systemic blood vessels", "Arterial dilation lowers blood pressure, which can reduce brain perfusion if it falls too far."], ["Heart", "Reflex tachycardia or sometimes bradycardia can occur; little direct depression of the heart at usual doses."], ["Head", "Headache and flushing come from dilation of cranial and skin vessels."], ["Gut", "Nausea and other stomach upset are common."]], "journey": "Nimodipine is given by mouth or through a feeding tube every few hours, starting within 96 hours of the bleed. It is absorbed quickly, but extensive first-pass CYP3A4 metabolism in the gut wall and liver leaves bioavailability around 13 percent. It is over 95 percent protein bound and crosses into the brain. Its half-life is short, one to two hours, with a terminal phase of about eight to nine hours. Inactive metabolites leave in urine and bile. CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels and inducers lower them.", "sidefx": "Hypotension, the main dose-limiting effect, comes from relaxing systemic arteries, and it matters because the injured brain needs adequate pressure for blood flow. Headache and flushing reflect vessel dilation. The boxed warning is about route: capsule contents injected IV have caused cardiovascular collapse and cardiac arrest, because a large dose acts instantly on heart and vessels. Oral syringes that cannot connect to IV lines are used. There is no specific antidote; calcium and vasopressors are used for severe hypotension.", "src": ["Nymalize (nimodipine) FDA label", "Pickard JD et al. BMJ 1989", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "tirofiban": {"origin": "In the 1980s researchers learned that the final step in platelet clumping is fibrinogen bridging glycoprotein IIb/IIIa receptors on neighboring platelets, and that fibrinogen grips the receptor through a short sequence, arginine-glycine-aspartate (RGD). Snake venom proteins called disintegrins, such as echistatin, also use RGD to block the receptor. Chemists at Merck set out to build a small non-peptide molecule that mimicked this sequence. Starting from the amino acid tyrosine, they designed tirofiban, a compact RGD mimic. The FDA approved Aggrastat in 1998 for acute coronary syndromes, and it later passed to Medicure. It joined abciximab and eptifibatide as the GP IIb/IIIa inhibitors.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Fibrinogen binding to GP IIb/IIIa through the RGD sequence is described."], ["1990s", "Merck designs tirofiban as a non-peptide RGD mimic."], ["1998", "FDA approves Aggrastat for acute coronary syndromes."]], "cell": "When platelets are activated by any stimulus, whether thrombin, ADP, thromboxane A2 or collagen, inside-out signaling changes the shape of glycoprotein IIb/IIIa (integrin alphaIIb-beta3) so it can bind fibrinogen and von Willebrand factor. Each fibrinogen molecule can bridge two platelets, building the platelet plug. Tirofiban is a tyrosine-derived non-peptide that mimics the RGD sequence and binds the receptor's ligand pocket reversibly. Blocking the receptor stops fibrinogen and von Willebrand factor binding, so platelets cannot cross-link, regardless of what activated them. That makes it a final common pathway inhibitor. Because binding is reversible and the drug is cleared fairly fast, platelet function returns within about four to eight hours after the infusion stops.", "body": [["Platelets", "Aggregation is strongly inhibited, preventing new platelet-rich clots in narrowed or stented coronary arteries."], ["Heart", "Fewer heart attacks and ischemic events in acute coronary syndromes and during PCI."], ["Bleeding sites", "Access sites, gut, urinary tract and brain can bleed because platelets cannot form plugs."], ["Platelet count", "Rarely platelets fall suddenly and profoundly within hours, likely from antibodies that recognize the drug-bound receptor."]], "journey": "Tirofiban is given IV as a bolus followed by an infusion, and platelet inhibition begins within minutes. About 65 percent is bound to plasma proteins. It is minimally metabolized and leaves the body largely unchanged in urine and bile. The half-life is about two hours, and kidney impairment slows clearance, so the infusion is reduced when kidney function is low. Platelet function recovers toward baseline within about four to eight hours after stopping.", "sidefx": "Bleeding is the main risk because platelets cannot form plugs, and it is greater with heparin, aspirin and P2Y12 inhibitors given together. Acute thrombocytopenia can occur within hours, thought to be immune-mediated as antibodies bind receptors altered by drug binding, so counts are checked early. There is no specific antidote. Because binding is reversible and clearance is fast, stopping the infusion restores platelet function in hours; platelet transfusion is less effective than with abciximab because free drug can bind new platelets.", "fun": "Tirofiban is a small molecule that comes off its receptor quickly, so unlike the antibody abciximab, platelet function returns within hours of stopping it.", "src": ["Aggrastat (tirofiban) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "cosyntropin": {"origin": "ACTH, the pituitary hormone that drives the adrenal glands, was identified in the 1930s, and its full structure of 39 amino acids was worked out in the 1950s. Researchers found that the first 24 amino acids carry all of its biological activity, while the rest varies between species and triggers allergy. In the early 1960s Robert Schwyzer and colleagues at Ciba in Basel synthesized this 24-amino-acid peptide, tetracosactide, one of the early landmarks of peptide synthesis. In the US it is sold as cosyntropin (Cortrosyn). It replaced animal-derived ACTH for the ACTH stimulation test, the standard test for adrenal insufficiency.", "timeline": [["1930s", "ACTH is identified as a pituitary hormone that stimulates the adrenal cortex."], ["1950s", "The amino acid sequence of ACTH is determined."], ["1960s", "Schwyzer's group at Ciba synthesizes ACTH 1-24 (tetracosactide)."]], "cell": "Cosyntropin is identical to amino acids 1 to 24 of human ACTH. It binds the melanocortin 2 receptor (MC2R), a Gs-coupled receptor on cells of the adrenal cortex, which needs the accessory protein MRAP to reach the cell surface. Gs activates adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP and activating protein kinase A. Within minutes, PKA increases cholesterol delivery to mitochondria through the StAR protein and activates side-chain cleavage of cholesterol to pregnenolone, the rate-limiting step of steroid synthesis. Cortisol output from the zona fasciculata rises within 30 to 60 minutes. A healthy adrenal gland responds with a clear cortisol rise. In primary adrenal insufficiency, the gland is damaged and cannot respond. In long-standing secondary insufficiency, the gland has atrophied without ACTH and also responds poorly.", "body": [["Adrenal cortex", "Rapid stimulation of cortisol synthesis and release, used to test whether the adrenal glands work."], ["Blood cortisol", "Cortisol rises and peaks 30 to 60 minutes after the dose in a normal gland."], ["Aldosterone", "A smaller, transient rise in aldosterone may occur, since ACTH also has a modest effect on the zona glomerulosa."], ["Skin and vessels", "Flushing and mild injection site reactions can occur."]], "journey": "Cosyntropin is given by IV or IM injection, and blood cortisol is measured before and 30 and 60 minutes after. The peptide is cleared from plasma quickly, with a half-life of about 15 minutes, and is broken down by proteolysis into fragments that leave mainly in urine. The cortisol rise lasts a few hours. Because it is a short peptide used once for testing, interactions mostly involve drugs that change cortisol measurement, such as glucocorticoids or estrogen that raises cortisol-binding globulin.", "sidefx": "Side effects are few because it is used once and acts briefly. Flushing and nausea can occur. Because the allergenic C-terminal portion of animal ACTH is absent, allergic reactions are uncommon, but hypersensitivity and rare anaphylaxis have been reported, especially in people with prior reactions to ACTH. Results can be misleading after recent glucocorticoid use or with conditions that change cortisol-binding globulin. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Cortrosyn (cosyntropin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "oxacillin": {"origin": "Oxacillin descends from Alexander Fleming's 1928 discovery that a Penicillium mold killed staphylococci. By the 1950s many hospital staph strains made penicillinase, an enzyme that cuts penicillin's beta-lactam ring. In 1957 scientists at Beecham Research Laboratories in England isolated 6-aminopenicillanic acid, the bare penicillin core, which let chemists attach new side chains. They produced methicillin in 1959, and in the early 1960s a series of isoxazolyl penicillins, including oxacillin, cloxacillin and dicloxacillin, whose bulky side chains shield the ring from penicillinase. Oxacillin became a standard IV drug for methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus, and its name is still used in lab testing, where oxacillin resistance defines MRSA.", "timeline": [["1928", "Alexander Fleming observes penicillin killing staphylococci."], ["1957", "Beecham scientists isolate 6-aminopenicillanic acid."], ["1959", "Methicillin, the first penicillinase-resistant penicillin, is introduced."], ["1960s", "Isoxazolyl penicillins including oxacillin enter clinical use."]], "cell": "Oxacillin is a beta-lactam whose strained four-membered ring mimics the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of peptidoglycan precursors. Penicillin-binding proteins (transpeptidases) attack the ring and become covalently acylated, so they cannot form the peptide cross-links that give the cell wall strength. Without cross-linking, autolysins break down the wall, and the cell bursts from internal pressure. The killing is time-dependent, so effect depends on how long levels stay above the MIC. The bulky phenyl isoxazolyl side chain blocks staphylococcal penicillinase from reaching the ring. MRSA resists oxacillin because the mecA gene encodes PBP2a, a transpeptidase with low affinity for beta-lactams that keeps building the wall when other PBPs are blocked. Its gram-negative activity is poor because it does not pass well through porins.", "body": [["Skin and soft tissue", "Kills methicillin-susceptible S. aureus and streptococci causing cellulitis, abscesses and wound infections."], ["Blood and heart valves", "Used for MSSA bacteremia and endocarditis, where it often works better than vancomycin for susceptible strains."], ["Bones and joints", "Treats MSSA osteomyelitis and septic arthritis."], ["Liver", "Can raise liver enzymes or cause hepatitis, more often than nafcillin."], ["Kidneys and blood", "Rarely causes interstitial nephritis or neutropenia, usually after prolonged use."]], "journey": "Oxacillin is given IV or IM because oral absorption is poor. It spreads into most tissues, including bone and inflamed joints, and crosses inflamed meninges to a limited degree. It is about 94 percent protein bound. Part is metabolized in the liver, and the drug and metabolites leave through both kidneys and bile, so dose changes are less often needed in kidney disease. The half-life is short, about 20 to 30 minutes, so it is dosed every four to six hours or by continuous infusion.", "sidefx": "Allergic reactions, from rash to anaphylaxis, occur because the beta-lactam ring opens and binds body proteins, forming antigens; cross-reactivity applies to other penicillins. Liver enzyme rises reflect hepatic handling. Interstitial nephritis is an immune reaction in the kidney. Neutropenia occurs with long courses. Hypokalemia can result from the large sodium load delivered with the drug. Disrupting gut flora can allow C. difficile to overgrow. Phlebitis comes from irritation of the vein. There is no antidote; anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine.", "src": ["Oxacillin for injection FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases"]}, "piperacillin": {"origin": "Piperacillin was created in the 1970s by Toyama Chemical in Japan as part of a push to extend penicillins against Pseudomonas aeruginosa and other gram-negative rods. It is a ureidopenicillin, built on the ampicillin skeleton with a polar piperazine side chain that helps it pass through gram-negative porins and bind their penicillin-binding proteins. It was sold as a single agent in the US (Pipracil) from the early 1980s. Because many bacteria destroy it with beta-lactamases, it was later paired with the inhibitor tazobactam, approved as Zosyn in 1993, which became one of the most widely used hospital antibiotics. No single-agent piperacillin product is currently marketed in the US; it is available only as piperacillin-tazobactam.", "timeline": [["1928", "Alexander Fleming observes penicillin's antibacterial effect."], ["1970s", "Toyama Chemical develops piperacillin."], ["1980s", "Single-agent piperacillin enters US use."], ["1993", "FDA approves piperacillin-tazobactam (Zosyn)."]], "cell": "Piperacillin is a beta-lactam that acylates penicillin-binding proteins, especially PBP3 of gram-negative rods, which builds the septum during division. Acylated PBPs cannot cross-link peptidoglycan. Blocking PBP3 causes long filaments before the cells lyse. The killing is time-dependent, so effect depends on the time levels stay above the MIC, which is why extended infusions are sometimes used. The polar ureido-piperazine side chain improves passage through porins in the outer membrane and increases affinity for gram-negative PBPs, giving activity against Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Enterobacterales, streptococci, some enterococci and anaerobes. However, it is hydrolyzed by many beta-lactamases, including staphylococcal penicillinase, ESBLs and AmpC, which is why it is paired with tazobactam, an irreversible inhibitor of many class A enzymes.", "body": [["Lungs", "Treats hospital-acquired pneumonia caused by Pseudomonas and other gram-negative rods."], ["Abdomen", "With its anaerobic and gram-negative coverage it treats intra-abdominal infections."], ["Blood", "Can interfere with platelet function and cause bleeding, especially in kidney failure."], ["Electrolytes", "Acts as a non-absorbable anion in the kidney tubule, causing potassium loss and hypokalemia."], ["Kidneys and marrow", "Rarely causes interstitial nephritis and, with long courses, neutropenia."]], "journey": "Piperacillin is given IV. It distributes into most tissues and fluids, including lung, bile and abdominal tissue, and into inflamed meninges to some extent. It is about 30 percent protein bound and is minimally metabolized. Most is excreted unchanged by the kidneys through filtration and tubular secretion, and some is excreted in bile. The half-life is about one hour, so dosing is frequent, and doses are adjusted in kidney impairment.", "sidefx": "As with all penicillins, the opened beta-lactam binds body proteins and can trigger allergy, from rash to anaphylaxis. Bleeding comes from interference with platelet aggregation. Hypokalemia occurs because the drug acts as an unreabsorbed anion in the distal tubule, pulling potassium out. Interstitial nephritis is an immune reaction. Long courses can cause neutropenia. Gut flora disruption allows C. difficile. With vancomycin, piperacillin-tazobactam has been linked to higher rates of acute kidney injury. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Zosyn (piperacillin and tazobactam) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases"]}, "cefoxitin": {"origin": "The cephalosporin story began in 1945, when Giuseppe Brotzu in Sardinia found that a fungus, later called Acremonium (Cephalosporium), from sea water near a sewage outlet produced antibiotic substances. In the early 1970s researchers at Merck and at Eli Lilly found a related group, the cephamycins, made by Streptomyces bacteria. Merck chemically modified cephamycin C from Streptomyces lactamdurans to create cefoxitin. Its methoxy group gave stability against many beta-lactamases and strong activity against anaerobes such as Bacteroides fragilis. Marketed as Mefoxin around 1978, it became widely used for surgical prophylaxis and mixed abdominal and pelvic infections. It is now available as a generic.", "timeline": [["1945", "Giuseppe Brotzu discovers cephalosporin-producing fungus in Sardinia."], ["1971", "Cephamycins are reported from Streptomyces species."], ["1978", "Cefoxitin is introduced in the US as Mefoxin."]], "cell": "Cefoxitin is a beta-lactam that binds and acylates penicillin-binding proteins, blocking the transpeptidation that cross-links peptidoglycan. Weakened walls and autolysin activity cause cell lysis, so it is bactericidal and time-dependent. What sets it apart is the 7-alpha-methoxy group of the cephamycins, which sterically blocks many beta-lactamases from attacking the ring. This gives it activity against anaerobes such as Bacteroides fragilis and some beta-lactamase-producing gram-negatives. It also strongly induces chromosomal AmpC beta-lactamase in organisms such as Enterobacter, which can lead to resistance to other cephalosporins. It is inactive against enterococci (their PBPs bind it poorly), MRSA (PBP2a) and Pseudomonas. Because it induces mecA expression, the cefoxitin disk is used in labs to detect MRSA.", "body": [["Abdomen and pelvis", "Covers gut gram-negatives and anaerobes, so it is used for intra-abdominal and gynecologic infections and surgical prophylaxis."], ["Skin and soft tissue", "Treats mixed aerobic and anaerobic infections such as diabetic foot or bite wounds."], ["Gut", "Disrupts normal flora, allowing diarrhea and C. difficile infection."], ["Blood", "Can cause a positive Coombs test, eosinophilia and rarely hemolytic anemia."], ["Kidneys", "Rarely causes allergic interstitial nephritis with fever, rash and rising creatinine."]], "journey": "Cefoxitin is given IV. It distributes into most tissues and fluids, including the peritoneal fluid and pelvic tissues, but it does not reach the cerebrospinal fluid well enough for meningitis. It is about 65 to 80 percent protein bound and is minimally metabolized. Most is excreted unchanged in the urine through filtration and tubular secretion, and probenecid slows this. The half-life is about 45 to 60 minutes, so doses are repeated every six to eight hours, and during long surgery it is re-dosed.", "sidefx": "Allergic reactions occur because the opened beta-lactam binds proteins; cross-reactivity with penicillins is low but possible, largely determined by side-chain similarity. Phlebitis comes from vein irritation. C. difficile diarrhea follows disruption of gut anaerobes. Positive Coombs tests and rare hemolysis come from drug binding to red cell membranes. Interstitial nephritis is immune-mediated. Cefoxitin can falsely raise creatinine on some lab methods. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Cefoxitin for injection FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases"]}, "ceftazidime": {"origin": "Ceftazidime is a third-generation cephalosporin, part of the family that began with Giuseppe Brotzu's 1945 discovery of an antibiotic-making fungus off the coast of Sardinia. In the late 1970s chemists at Glaxo in Britain added an aminothiazolyl oxime and a carboxypropyl group to the cephalosporin core, which gave strong activity against Pseudomonas aeruginosa, a major cause of hospital and cystic fibrosis infections. It was introduced in the early 1980s and approved in the US in the mid-1980s as Fortaz, with Tazicef as another brand. For years it was a mainstay antipseudomonal drug. Its weakness against newer beta-lactamases later led to its pairing with avibactam.", "timeline": [["1945", "Giuseppe Brotzu discovers cephalosporin-producing fungus in Sardinia."], ["1970s", "Glaxo chemists develop ceftazidime."], ["1980s", "Ceftazidime enters clinical use as an antipseudomonal cephalosporin."], ["2015", "Ceftazidime is paired with avibactam as Avycaz."]], "cell": "Ceftazidime binds penicillin-binding protein 3 (PBP3) of gram-negative bacilli with high affinity. PBP3 builds the septum when bacteria divide, so blocking it causes long filaments before lysis. Like other beta-lactams, the ring acylates the PBP active site, stopping peptidoglycan cross-linking, and the weakened wall bursts. Killing is time-dependent. The aminothiazolyl oxime group adds stability against some beta-lactamases and gram-negative potency, and the carboxypropyl group adds activity against Pseudomonas and helps passage through porins. It is hydrolyzed by ESBLs, AmpC and carbapenemases. It binds staphylococcal PBPs weakly, so its gram-positive activity is poor, and it has no useful anaerobic activity.", "body": [["Lungs", "Treats Pseudomonas and other gram-negative pneumonias, including in cystic fibrosis."], ["Brain and meninges", "Crosses inflamed meninges well enough to treat gram-negative meningitis, such as after neurosurgery."], ["Urinary tract", "High urine levels help treat complicated urinary infections."], ["Fever with neutropenia", "Its Pseudomonas coverage made it a traditional choice for febrile neutropenia."], ["Brain toxicity", "In kidney failure, high levels can cause confusion, myoclonus, encephalopathy and seizures."]], "journey": "Ceftazidime is given IV or IM. It distributes widely into tissues and fluids, including bone, lung and inflamed meninges. It is less than 10 percent protein bound and is not metabolized. The kidneys excrete about 80 to 90 percent unchanged by glomerular filtration. The half-life is about two hours in normal kidney function and much longer in kidney failure, so doses are adjusted to kidney function.", "sidefx": "Allergic reactions occur because the opened beta-lactam binds body proteins. Ceftazidime shares its side chain with aztreonam, so cross-reactivity is possible between them. Neurotoxicity occurs when kidney failure lets levels build up, as beta-lactams in high concentrations block GABA-A receptors in the brain. Phlebitis comes from vein irritation, and C. difficile follows disruption of gut flora. A positive Coombs test reflects drug binding to red cells. Dialysis removes it. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Fortaz (ceftazidime) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases"]}, "ceftolozane-tazobactam": {"origin": "Ceftolozane was discovered by the Japanese company Astellas, which was searching for a cephalosporin that could beat Pseudomonas aeruginosa even when the bacteria pumped drugs out or closed their porins. Its structure is related to ceftazidime but with a bulkier side chain that makes it stable against the AmpC enzyme of Pseudomonas. The US company Calixa licensed it and paired it with tazobactam, a beta-lactamase inhibitor discovered at Taiho Pharmaceutical in Japan and used since the 1990s with piperacillin. Cubist Pharmaceuticals acquired Calixa, and the FDA approved Zerbaxa in December 2014 for complicated abdominal and urinary infections, later adding hospital-acquired pneumonia. Merck acquired Cubist in 2015.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Taiho Pharmaceutical discovers tazobactam."], ["2000s", "Astellas discovers ceftolozane, later developed by Calixa and Cubist."], ["2014", "FDA approves Zerbaxa for complicated intra-abdominal and urinary infections."], ["2019", "FDA approves Zerbaxa for hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia."]], "cell": "Ceftolozane is a beta-lactam that binds penicillin-binding proteins, with high affinity for P. aeruginosa PBP1b, PBP1c and PBP3. Acylating these enzymes stops peptidoglycan cross-linking, and the cell lyses. Its side chain makes it a poor substrate for Pseudomonas AmpC, and its potency is little affected by loss of the OprD porin or by upregulated efflux pumps, the main ways Pseudomonas resists other drugs. That explains its activity against many multidrug-resistant Pseudomonas strains. Tazobactam is a penicillanic acid sulfone that acts as a suicide inhibitor: it acylates the active-site serine of many class A beta-lactamases, including most ESBLs, and some class C enzymes, permanently disabling them. Tazobactam extends ceftolozane's coverage to ESBL-producing Enterobacterales. Neither part inhibits carbapenemases such as KPC or metallo-beta-lactamases.", "body": [["Lungs", "Treats hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia, especially with multidrug-resistant Pseudomonas."], ["Abdomen", "With metronidazole, treats complicated intra-abdominal infections."], ["Urinary tract", "Treats complicated urinary infections and pyelonephritis, including ESBL producers."], ["Gut", "Nausea, diarrhea and C. difficile infection can follow disruption of gut flora."], ["Kidneys", "Both parts are cleared by the kidneys, and lower effectiveness was seen with reduced kidney function in trials."]], "journey": "Zerbaxa is given IV over one hour every eight hours. Both components are minimally protein bound and distribute into body tissues, including the lung lining fluid. Ceftolozane is not metabolized and leaves unchanged by glomerular filtration, with a half-life of about two and a half to three hours. Tazobactam is partly hydrolyzed to an inactive metabolite and is cleared by both filtration and tubular secretion, with a half-life of about one hour. Doses are adjusted for kidney function.", "sidefx": "Like other beta-lactams, the drug can cause allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, through protein binding by the opened ring. Broad gram-negative activity disrupts gut flora, causing diarrhea and C. difficile colitis. Hypokalemia can occur. In kidney impairment, accumulating beta-lactams can affect the brain. Lower cure rates seen with moderate kidney impairment in trials are thought to relate partly to dosing as kidney function changes. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Zerbaxa (ceftolozane and tazobactam) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "ceftazidime-avibactam": {"origin": "By the 2000s ceftazidime was losing ground to ESBLs and carbapenemases. Avibactam came from a new chemical class, the diazabicyclooctanes, discovered by scientists at the French company Hoechst Marion Roussel and developed by its spin-off Novexel. Unlike older beta-lactamase inhibitors, it is not itself a beta-lactam and it can inhibit KPC carbapenemases. AstraZeneca and Forest Laboratories, later Actavis and Allergan, developed it with ceftazidime, and the FDA approved Avycaz in February 2015 under the Qualified Infectious Disease Product program for complicated abdominal and urinary infections. Hospital-acquired pneumonia was added in 2018. It became a key drug for carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales that make KPC.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Ceftazidime enters clinical use."], ["1990s", "Diazabicyclooctane inhibitors, including avibactam, are discovered in France."], ["2015", "FDA approves Avycaz for complicated intra-abdominal and urinary infections."], ["2018", "FDA approves Avycaz for hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia."]], "cell": "Ceftazidime binds PBP3 of gram-negative bacilli, blocking septal peptidoglycan cross-linking and causing filamentation and lysis. It is time-dependent and bactericidal. Avibactam protects it. As a diazabicyclooctane, it covalently acylates the active-site serine of beta-lactamases, but unlike tazobactam, the reaction is reversible: the ring re-closes and avibactam is released intact to inhibit another enzyme. It inhibits Ambler class A enzymes, including ESBLs and KPC carbapenemases, class C AmpC enzymes, and some class D enzymes such as OXA-48. It does not inhibit class B metallo-beta-lactamases such as NDM, VIM and IMP, which use zinc rather than serine, so it is combined with aztreonam in some cases. Resistance can arise from KPC mutations such as D179Y that reduce avibactam binding.", "body": [["Abdomen", "With metronidazole, treats complicated intra-abdominal infections including resistant gram-negatives."], ["Urinary tract", "Treats complicated urinary infections and pyelonephritis caused by ESBL and KPC producers."], ["Lungs", "Treats hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia from susceptible gram-negative bacteria."], ["Brain", "In kidney impairment, accumulation can cause confusion, myoclonus, seizures or encephalopathy."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea and C. difficile infection can follow disruption of gut flora."]], "journey": "Avycaz is given IV over two hours every eight hours. Both drugs have low protein binding and similar distribution into tissues, including lung lining fluid. Neither is significantly metabolized. Both leave mainly unchanged in the urine by glomerular filtration, with half-lives of about two to three hours, so they stay matched. Doses are reduced when kidney function falls.", "sidefx": "Allergic reactions occur because opened beta-lactams bind body proteins; avibactam is not a beta-lactam, but ceftazidime can still cross-react, especially with aztreonam, which shares a side chain. Neurotoxicity, including seizures and status epilepticus, occurs mainly with kidney impairment or dosing errors, because high beta-lactam levels block brain GABA-A receptors. Broad spectrum explains C. difficile colitis. A positive Coombs test may occur. There is no antidote, though dialysis removes both components.", "src": ["Avycaz (ceftazidime and avibactam) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Ehmann DE et al. PNAS 2012"]}, "cefiderocol": {"origin": "Bacteria need iron, and to get it they release siderophores, small molecules that grab iron and are then pulled back into the cell by dedicated transporters. Scientists at the Japanese company Shionogi used this as a Trojan horse. They attached a catechol iron-binding group to a cephalosporin, so bacteria would actively pump the antibiotic in along with iron. The result, cefiderocol, gets past outer membrane barriers that keep other drugs out and is stable against many carbapenemases, including metallo-beta-lactamases. The FDA approved Fetroja in November 2019 for complicated urinary infections in patients with limited options, and in 2020 for hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia. It was the first siderophore antibiotic approved in the US.", "timeline": [["1945", "Giuseppe Brotzu discovers cephalosporin-producing fungus in Sardinia."], ["2000s", "Shionogi designs cefiderocol, a siderophore cephalosporin."], ["2019", "FDA approves Fetroja for complicated urinary tract infections."], ["2020", "FDA approves Fetroja for hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia."]], "cell": "Cefiderocol has a catechol group on its C-3 side chain that binds ferric iron (Fe3+) like a natural siderophore. Gram-negative bacteria recognize this iron complex and actively transport it across the outer membrane through TonB-dependent iron transporters, such as CirA and Fiu in E. coli and PiuA in Pseudomonas. This raises drug levels in the periplasm, in addition to passive entry through porins, so loss of porins or overactive efflux pumps matter less. In the periplasm it binds penicillin-binding proteins, mainly PBP3, blocking peptidoglycan cross-linking and causing lysis. Its side chains, similar to those of ceftazidime and cefepime, make it stable against many serine beta-lactamases, including KPC and OXA-48, and against metallo-beta-lactamases such as NDM and VIM. It is time-dependent and bactericidal against many carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales, Pseudomonas, Acinetobacter and Stenotrophomonas.", "body": [["Urinary tract", "Treats complicated urinary infections and pyelonephritis caused by carbapenem-resistant gram-negative bacteria."], ["Lungs", "Treats hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia from resistant gram-negatives."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea and C. difficile infection follow disruption of gut flora."], ["Blood and electrolytes", "Low potassium, raised liver tests and low platelets can occur."], ["Brain", "Seizures and other neurotoxicity can occur, especially with kidney impairment."]], "journey": "Cefiderocol is given IV as a three-hour infusion every eight hours, which keeps levels above the MIC for longer. It is about 40 to 60 percent bound to albumin and distributes into lung lining fluid. It is minimally metabolized and leaves mostly unchanged in the urine, with a half-life of about two to three hours. Doses are lowered in kidney impairment and raised in augmented renal clearance, which is common in young critically ill patients.", "sidefx": "Allergy and C. difficile colitis follow the same patterns as other cephalosporins. Seizures reflect beta-lactam blockade of brain GABA-A receptors when levels are high. In a trial of patients with carbapenem-resistant infections, all-cause death was higher with cefiderocol than with best available therapy, especially in Acinetobacter infections, and the reason is not fully explained; this is a labeled warning. It can interfere with some urine dipstick tests. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Cefiderocol's iron-grabbing design turns the bacteria's own iron-scavenging system into an entry gate, the same Trojan horse idea nature already uses in some antibiotic-producing microbes.", "src": ["Fetroja (cefiderocol) FDA label", "Bassetti M et al. Lancet Infect Dis 2021 (CREDIBLE-CR)"]}, "ertapenem": {"origin": "Carbapenems trace back to thienamycin, which Merck scientists isolated in 1976 from the soil bacterium Streptomyces cattleya. Thienamycin was powerful but unstable, so Merck made imipenem, approved in 1985 with cilastatin, and later meropenem appeared from another company. Merck then designed ertapenem to last longer in the body. A benzoic acid side chain makes it highly bound to albumin, which slows its clearance and allows once-daily dosing, though it also lost activity against Pseudomonas and Acinetobacter. The FDA approved Invanz in November 2001 for community-acquired abdominal, pelvic, skin, lung and urinary infections, and later for prevention of infection after colorectal surgery.", "timeline": [["1976", "Merck scientists discover thienamycin from Streptomyces cattleya."], ["1985", "FDA approves imipenem-cilastatin, the first carbapenem."], ["2001", "FDA approves ertapenem (Invanz) as a once-daily carbapenem."]], "cell": "Ertapenem is a carbapenem, a beta-lactam with a carbon in place of sulfur and a double bond in the five-membered ring. It binds penicillin-binding proteins, especially PBP2 and PBP3 in E. coli, blocking peptidoglycan cross-linking and causing lysis. The trans-hydroxyethyl side chain makes it stable against most serine beta-lactamases, including ESBLs and AmpC, which is why it is commonly used for ESBL-producing Enterobacterales. Its negatively charged benzoic acid side chain increases protein binding and slows elimination, but it reduces passage through Pseudomonas porins and affinity for some PBPs. As a result it has poor activity against Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Acinetobacter and Enterococcus. Carbapenemases such as KPC and NDM destroy it.", "body": [["Abdomen and pelvis", "Treats community-acquired intra-abdominal and pelvic infections, covering gram-negatives and anaerobes such as Bacteroides."], ["Urinary tract", "Treats complicated urinary infections, including those from ESBL producers."], ["Skin and soft tissue", "Treats complicated skin infections, including diabetic foot infections without Pseudomonas."], ["Brain", "Can lower the seizure threshold, especially in kidney impairment or existing brain disease."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and C. difficile infection follow disruption of normal flora."]], "journey": "Ertapenem is given once daily IV, or IM where it is about 90 percent absorbed. It is highly and concentration-dependently bound to albumin, about 85 to 95 percent, which keeps it in the blood and gives a half-life of about four hours. Unlike imipenem, it is fairly stable to kidney dehydropeptidase-I, though some is hydrolyzed to an inactive open-ring metabolite. About 80 percent leaves in the urine as unchanged drug and metabolite, and about 10 percent in feces. Doses are reduced in severe kidney impairment.", "sidefx": "Seizures come from carbapenem blockade of brain GABA-A receptors, which is more likely when kidney failure raises levels. Carbapenems also sharply lower valproic acid levels, which can trigger seizures, so the combination is avoided. Allergic reactions occur, though cross-reactivity with penicillin allergy is low. Broad spectrum explains C. difficile and yeast infections such as vaginitis. Liver enzymes can rise. There is no antidote; it is removed by hemodialysis.", "src": ["Invanz (ertapenem) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Kahan JS et al. J Antibiot 1979"]}, "tobramycin": {"origin": "Tobramycin comes from the soil bacterium Streptomyces tenebrarius, which makes a group of related antibiotics called the nebramycin complex. Scientists at Eli Lilly described the complex in the 1960s, and tobramycin was isolated as one of its components. It proved especially strong against Pseudomonas aeruginosa and was introduced in the US in the 1970s as an injection. In 1997 an inhaled form, TOBI, was approved for people with cystic fibrosis, whose lungs are chronically infected with Pseudomonas, delivering high drug levels to the airways with little reaching the blood. Ophthalmic forms are also used for eye infections.", "timeline": [["1943", "Streptomycin, the first aminoglycoside, is discovered."], ["1960s", "Eli Lilly scientists describe the nebramycin complex from Streptomyces tenebrarius."], ["1970s", "Tobramycin injection enters US clinical use."], ["1997", "FDA approves inhaled tobramycin (TOBI) for cystic fibrosis."]], "cell": "Tobramycin is a polycationic aminoglycoside. It first binds negatively charged lipopolysaccharide and displaces magnesium and calcium bridges in the gram-negative outer membrane, then crosses the inner membrane by an oxygen-dependent active transport driven by the electron transport chain. This is why anaerobes are resistant and why beta-lactams, which damage the wall, increase uptake (synergy). Inside, it binds irreversibly to the A site of 16S rRNA in the 30S ribosomal subunit. It blocks formation of the initiation complex and causes misreading of mRNA, so faulty proteins are made. Misfolded proteins inserted into the membrane create pores that let in more drug, speeding killing. Killing is concentration-dependent with a post-antibiotic effect, which supports once-daily extended-interval dosing. Resistance comes mainly from aminoglycoside-modifying enzymes.", "body": [["Lungs", "Inhaled tobramycin suppresses chronic Pseudomonas infection in cystic fibrosis, and IV forms treat serious gram-negative pneumonia."], ["Blood and organs", "Treats serious gram-negative infections, often with a beta-lactam for synergy."], ["Kidneys", "Accumulates in proximal tubule cells and causes acute tubular injury, rising creatinine and loss of magnesium, potassium and calcium."], ["Inner ear", "Damages cochlear and vestibular hair cells, causing tinnitus, high-frequency hearing loss and balance problems, which can be permanent."], ["Neuromuscular junction", "Blocks presynaptic calcium entry, reducing acetylcholine release and rarely causing respiratory paralysis."], ["Eyes", "Topical drops treat bacterial conjunctivitis with little drug reaching the blood."]], "journey": "Tobramycin is poorly absorbed from the gut, so it is given IV, IM, by inhalation or as eye drops. After IV dosing it stays mostly in extracellular fluid, with low protein binding, and penetrates poorly into the brain and cerebrospinal fluid. It is not metabolized and is filtered unchanged by the kidneys, with a half-life of about two hours that grows much longer in kidney failure. Blood levels guide dosing. Inhaled tobramycin gives high airway levels, and only a small fraction reaches the blood.", "sidefx": "Nephrotoxicity happens because tubular cells take up the drug through megalin receptors and it builds up in lysosomes, injuring the cells; this is usually reversible. Ototoxicity comes from accumulation in inner ear fluid and free radical damage to hair cells, which do not regrow, so it is often permanent; certain mitochondrial rRNA gene variants raise this risk. Neuromuscular blockade comes from blocking presynaptic calcium channels and can be reversed with IV calcium. Once-daily dosing and level monitoring lower toxicity. Inhaled forms can cause bronchospasm.", "src": ["Tobramycin injection and TOBI FDA labels", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "amikacin": {"origin": "Amikacin was made in 1972 by Hiroshi Kawaguchi and colleagues at Bristol-Banyu Research Institute in Japan. They started from kanamycin, an aminoglycoside discovered by Hamao Umezawa in 1957 from Streptomyces kanamyceticus. By then, many bacteria resisted kanamycin and gentamicin by making enzymes that add chemical groups to the drug. Kawaguchi's team attached an L-hydroxyaminobutyric acid side chain that blocks most of these enzymes. The resulting semisynthetic drug, amikacin, became the aminoglycoside held in reserve for resistant gram-negative infections and an important drug for mycobacteria. In 2018 the FDA approved Arikayce, a liposome inhaled form, for refractory Mycobacterium avium complex lung disease.", "timeline": [["1957", "Hamao Umezawa discovers kanamycin."], ["1972", "Kawaguchi and colleagues synthesize amikacin from kanamycin."], ["1970s", "Amikacin injection enters clinical use."], ["2018", "FDA approves Arikayce for refractory MAC lung disease."]], "cell": "Amikacin is a polycationic aminoglycoside. It binds the gram-negative outer membrane and then crosses the inner membrane by oxygen-dependent active transport, so anaerobes are resistant, and cell wall agents increase its uptake. Inside, it binds irreversibly to the A site of 16S rRNA in the 30S ribosomal subunit, blocking the initiation complex and causing mRNA misreading. Faulty proteins damage the membrane and increase drug uptake, causing rapid, concentration-dependent killing with a post-antibiotic effect. Its L-hydroxyaminobutyric acid (AHBA) side chain on the 1-amino group sterically blocks most aminoglycoside-modifying enzymes, including acetyltransferases, adenylyltransferases and phosphotransferases, so it often remains active against strains resistant to gentamicin and tobramycin. It is also active against Mycobacterium tuberculosis and nontuberculous mycobacteria.", "body": [["Blood and organs", "Treats serious gram-negative infections resistant to other aminoglycosides, often with a beta-lactam."], ["Lungs", "Inhaled liposomal amikacin treats refractory MAC lung disease, and IV forms are part of some regimens for drug-resistant tuberculosis and nontuberculous mycobacteria."], ["Kidneys", "Builds up in proximal tubule cells, causing usually reversible tubular injury and rising creatinine."], ["Inner ear", "Damages cochlear hair cells more than vestibular cells, causing high-frequency hearing loss that can be permanent."], ["Neuromuscular junction", "Can reduce acetylcholine release and rarely cause respiratory paralysis."], ["Airways (inhaled)", "Inhaled form can cause hoarseness, cough and bronchospasm and a rare hypersensitivity pneumonitis."]], "journey": "Amikacin is not absorbed from the gut, so it is given IV, IM or by inhalation. After injection it stays mostly in extracellular fluid, with low protein binding. It is not metabolized and is filtered unchanged by the kidneys, with a half-life of about two to three hours that lengthens a lot in kidney failure, so levels guide dosing. The inhaled liposomal form keeps most drug in the lungs, where liposomes are taken up by macrophages that harbor mycobacteria, and blood levels stay low.", "sidefx": "Like other aminoglycosides, amikacin is taken up by kidney tubule cells through megalin and accumulates, causing tubular injury. Inner ear hair cells are damaged by drug buildup and free radicals and do not regrow, so hearing loss is often permanent, especially with long courses and in people with certain mitochondrial rRNA variants. Neuromuscular blockade comes from blocking presynaptic calcium entry and is reversed with calcium. Inhaled use irritates airways. Toxicity rises with duration, high trough levels and other nephrotoxic drugs.", "src": ["Amikacin injection and Arikayce FDA labels", "Kawaguchi H et al. J Antibiot 1972", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "streptomycin": {"origin": "Streptomycin was the first effective drug against tuberculosis. In 1943 Albert Schatz, a graduate student in Selman Waksman's lab at Rutgers University, isolated it from Streptomyces griseus, a soil bacterium, while screening microbes for antibiotics. At the Mayo Clinic, William Feldman and Corwin Hinshaw showed it cured tuberculosis in guinea pigs and then treated the first human patients in 1944 and 1945. In 1948 the British Medical Research Council published a trial of streptomycin for pulmonary tuberculosis, one of the first randomized controlled trials in medicine. Waksman received the 1952 Nobel Prize, and a later dispute over Schatz's credit became a famous story about recognition in science. Streptomycin is still used for some tuberculosis regimens, plague and tularemia.", "timeline": [["1943", "Albert Schatz in Selman Waksman's lab isolates streptomycin from Streptomyces griseus."], ["1944", "Feldman and Hinshaw at the Mayo Clinic begin treating tuberculosis with streptomycin."], ["1948", "The British MRC publishes one of the first randomized controlled trials, testing streptomycin for tuberculosis."], ["1952", "Selman Waksman receives the Nobel Prize for the discovery."]], "cell": "Streptomycin enters bacteria by oxygen-dependent active transport, so anaerobes are resistant. Inside, it binds irreversibly to the 30S ribosomal subunit at a site formed by 16S rRNA and the ribosomal protein S12. This distorts the decoding site, interferes with the initiation complex and causes misreading of mRNA, so faulty proteins are made. Some inserted into the membrane worsen damage and drug uptake, and killing is bactericidal and concentration-dependent. Resistance arises from mutations in rpsL, the gene for S12, or in rrs, the 16S rRNA gene, or from modifying enzymes. It is active against Mycobacterium tuberculosis in extracellular, actively dividing populations, as well as Yersinia pestis, Francisella tularensis and Brucella. It does not reach bacteria inside cells well.", "body": [["Lungs", "Kills actively dividing M. tuberculosis in cavities as part of multidrug regimens, and treats pneumonic plague."], ["Lymph nodes and blood", "Treats plague, tularemia and, with other drugs, brucellosis."], ["Vestibular system", "Damages vestibular hair cells more than cochlear cells, causing vertigo and ataxia that can be permanent."], ["Hearing", "Can cause tinnitus and high-frequency hearing loss."], ["Kidneys", "Less nephrotoxic than other aminoglycosides, but can still raise creatinine."], ["Skin and nerves", "Rash, eosinophilia and tingling around the mouth can occur."]], "journey": "Streptomycin is not absorbed from the gut, so it is given by deep IM injection, and occasionally IV. Peak levels occur one to two hours after injection. It stays mainly in extracellular fluid, with about 35 percent protein binding, and enters cerebrospinal fluid poorly. It is not metabolized and is filtered unchanged by the kidneys, with a half-life of about two and a half hours that lengthens in kidney failure and older adults, so doses are adjusted.", "sidefx": "Vestibular toxicity is the classic problem: streptomycin concentrates in inner ear fluid and generates free radicals that kill vestibular hair cells, which do not regrow, leading to permanent dizziness and imbalance. Hearing loss occurs by the same process in the cochlea. Neuromuscular blockade comes from blocking presynaptic calcium entry and can cause respiratory paralysis, especially with anesthesia or myasthenia; calcium can reverse it. Kidney injury reflects tubular uptake. It crosses the placenta and can cause congenital deafness. Hypersensitivity and painful injections are common.", "fun": "Waksman coined the word antibiotic in its modern sense to describe substances like streptomycin made by microbes to kill other microbes.", "src": ["Streptomycin for injection FDA label", "Schatz A, Bugie E, Waksman SA. Proc Soc Exp Biol Med 1944", "Medical Research Council. BMJ 1948"]}, "tigecycline": {"origin": "Tetracyclines date to 1948, when chlortetracycline was found in Streptomyces aureofaciens at Lederle Laboratories. Over decades, bacteria learned to resist them by pumping them out with Tet efflux pumps or by making ribosomal protection proteins that knock the drug off the ribosome. In the 1990s researchers at Lederle, later part of Wyeth, added a bulky glycylamido group to minocycline, creating a new subclass called glycylcyclines. The leading compound, tigecycline, escaped both resistance mechanisms. The FDA approved Tygacil in June 2005 for complicated skin and intra-abdominal infections, later adding community-acquired pneumonia. In 2010 and 2013 the FDA added warnings, including a boxed warning, after analyses found higher all-cause mortality than with comparator drugs.", "timeline": [["1948", "Chlortetracycline, the first tetracycline, is discovered at Lederle Laboratories."], ["1990s", "Lederle and Wyeth scientists develop glycylcyclines from minocycline."], ["2005", "FDA approves Tygacil (tigecycline)."], ["2013", "FDA adds a boxed warning for increased all-cause mortality."]], "cell": "Tigecycline binds the 30S ribosomal subunit at the A site, formed by 16S rRNA. This blocks aminoacyl-tRNA from docking, so new amino acids cannot be added and peptide elongation stops. The effect is mostly bacteriostatic. It binds the ribosome about five times more tightly than older tetracyclines. Its 9-tert-butylglycylamido side chain on the minocycline core adds steric bulk that keeps it from being pumped out by Tet efflux pumps and makes it resistant to ribosomal protection proteins such as TetM. This gives activity against MRSA, VRE, ESBL-producing Enterobacterales, Acinetobacter and anaerobes. It is pumped out by multidrug efflux systems such as MexXY-OprM in Pseudomonas aeruginosa and is weak against Proteus, Providencia and Morganella.", "body": [["Skin and soft tissue", "Treats complicated skin infections, including MRSA, as a broad-spectrum option."], ["Abdomen", "Covers aerobes and anaerobes in complicated intra-abdominal infections."], ["Gut and stomach", "Nausea and vomiting are very common, more than with older tetracyclines."], ["Liver and pancreas", "Can raise liver tests, cause hepatotoxicity and, rarely, acute pancreatitis."], ["Clotting", "Can lower fibrinogen and prolong PT and aPTT."], ["Teeth and skin", "Like other tetracyclines, it can discolor developing teeth and cause photosensitivity."]], "journey": "Tigecycline is given IV only, every 12 hours. It has a very large volume of distribution, moving into tissues such as bone, lung, gallbladder and colon, which leaves blood levels low. That limits its use in bloodstream infections. It is 71 to 89 percent protein bound. It is minimally metabolized and leaves mostly unchanged, about 59 percent through bile and feces and about 33 percent in urine. The half-life is long, about 27 hours after one dose and 42 hours after multiple doses. Dose is reduced in severe liver impairment.", "sidefx": "Nausea and vomiting likely come from direct effects on the gut and central receptors. Higher all-cause mortality in trials, the basis of its boxed warning, is thought to reflect poorer cure rates, especially in ventilator-associated pneumonia, perhaps because blood levels are low. Liver injury and pancreatitis are recognized class effects. Low fibrinogen and coagulopathy are reported. As a tetracycline, it binds calcium in growing teeth and bones and can cause tooth discoloration in children and fetal harm. Disrupting gut flora allows C. difficile. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Tygacil (tigecycline) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "FDA Drug Safety Communication 2013"]}, "dalbavancin": {"origin": "Dalbavancin is a modern cousin of vancomycin, which was found in a soil sample from Borneo in the 1950s. Its parent compound, A40926, is a teicoplanin-like glycopeptide made by the actinomycete Nonomuraea. In the 1990s chemists at the Italian company Biosearch Italia modified A40926, adding a lipid tail and an amide group that let the drug anchor to bacterial membranes and stay in the body far longer. The program passed to Vicuron, Pfizer and then Durata Therapeutics, which completed trials showing that one or two infusions could treat serious skin infections. The FDA approved Dalvance in May 2014, one of the first products approved under the GAIN Act's Qualified Infectious Disease Product designation, for acute bacterial skin and skin structure infections.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Vancomycin is discovered from a Borneo soil organism."], ["1990s", "Biosearch Italia develops dalbavancin from the glycopeptide A40926."], ["2014", "FDA approves Dalvance for acute bacterial skin and skin structure infections."]], "cell": "Dalbavancin binds the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of lipid II, the peptidoglycan building block, at the outer surface of the cell membrane. Bound this way, the precursor cannot be used by transglycosylases to extend glycan chains or by transpeptidases to cross-link peptides, so the cell wall cannot be built and the bacterium dies. Its lipophilic side chain inserts into the bacterial membrane, holding the drug close to its target, and the drug also forms dimers that bind more tightly. This raises potency against Staphylococcus aureus, including MRSA, and streptococci. It is bactericidal and concentration-dependent. It cannot cross the outer membrane of gram-negative bacteria. VanA enterococci replace D-Ala-D-Ala with D-Ala-D-lactate, which it binds poorly, so they are resistant.", "body": [["Skin and soft tissue", "One or two infusions treat cellulitis, abscesses and wound infections caused by MRSA, MSSA and streptococci."], ["Bones and blood (off-label)", "Because levels last weeks, it is studied for osteomyelitis and some bloodstream infections."], ["Liver", "ALT can rise, sometimes several times above normal."], ["Infusion", "Rapid infusion can cause flushing, hives and itching, similar to vancomycin flushing syndrome."], ["Gut", "Nausea, diarrhea and C. difficile infection can occur."]], "journey": "Dalbavancin is given IV, either as one infusion or as two doses a week apart. It is about 93 percent bound to albumin, which, with its membrane anchoring, gives an extremely long half-life of about 346 hours, about two weeks. It reaches skin, bone and blister fluid and stays there for weeks. It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes; a small hydroxy metabolite forms. About a third leaves unchanged in the urine and about a fifth in feces. Doses are lowered in severe kidney impairment without dialysis.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are mild: nausea, headache and diarrhea. Infusion reactions with flushing and urticaria resemble vancomycin flushing syndrome from histamine release and are reduced by infusing over 30 minutes. ALT elevations occur more often than with comparators. Hypersensitivity can cross-react with other glycopeptides. Because the drug lasts so long, any adverse reaction cannot be quickly reversed by stopping it. There is no antidote.", "fun": "With a half-life of about two weeks, a single infusion of dalbavancin can replace roughly two weeks of daily antibiotic treatment.", "src": ["Dalvance (dalbavancin) FDA label", "Boucher HW et al. N Engl J Med 2014 (DISCOVER trials)"]}, "amphotericin-b-liposomal": {"origin": "Amphotericin B was discovered in 1955 by scientists at the Squibb Institute, including Gold, Stout, Pagano and Donovick, from Streptomyces nodosus, a bacterium in a soil sample from the Orinoco River region of Venezuela. It became the first broad and reliable treatment for life-threatening fungal infections, but the original deoxycholate form often damaged the kidneys and caused severe fevers and chills, earning it the nickname amphoterrible. In the 1980s and 1990s, researchers packed the drug into small, single-layer liposomes, which carry it to infected tissues while sparing human cells. Vestar, later NeXstar Pharmaceuticals, developed AmBisome, and the FDA approved it in 1997. It also became a key drug for visceral leishmaniasis.", "timeline": [["1955", "Squibb scientists discover amphotericin B from Streptomyces nodosus from Venezuelan soil."], ["1950s", "Conventional amphotericin B deoxycholate enters clinical use."], ["1997", "FDA approves AmBisome, liposomal amphotericin B."]], "cell": "Amphotericin B is a polyene macrolide with a rigid row of double bonds on one side and hydroxyl groups on the other. It binds ergosterol, the main sterol in fungal cell membranes, much more tightly than cholesterol, the human sterol. Several drug molecules arrange with ergosterol to form pores across the membrane, and the drug may also pull ergosterol out of the membrane as a sterol sponge. Potassium, sodium and other small molecules leak out, and the fungal cell dies, so it is fungicidal. It also causes oxidative damage. In AmBisome, the drug is held in small unilamellar liposomes with cholesterol. The liposomes circulate longer, are taken up by macrophages and reach infected tissue, where fungal cells pull the drug out. Less free drug binds human kidney tubule membranes, lowering kidney toxicity and infusion reactions. Leishmania parasites also contain ergosterol-like sterols, which explains its antiprotozoal use.", "body": [["Fungal infections", "Fungicidal against Candida, Aspergillus, Cryptococcus, Mucorales and endemic fungi such as Histoplasma, used for invasive infections and fever with neutropenia."], ["Leishmania", "Kills Leishmania parasites in macrophages in visceral leishmaniasis."], ["Kidneys", "Less nephrotoxic than conventional amphotericin, but it can still constrict renal arterioles and injure tubules, raising creatinine."], ["Electrolytes", "Tubular pore formation leaks potassium and magnesium into urine, causing low potassium and magnesium."], ["Infusion reactions", "Fever, chills and back or chest pain can occur during infusion from cytokine release and liposome-related reactions."], ["Liver and blood", "Liver enzymes can rise, and anemia can develop from reduced erythropoietin."]], "journey": "Liposomal amphotericin is given only IV. The liposomes stay in the blood longer than conventional drug, and are taken up by the liver, spleen and macrophages, then release drug at sites of infection. It is highly bound in the blood. It is not significantly metabolized and is cleared slowly, as drug redistributes from tissues, with an initial half-life of about 7 to 10 hours and a terminal half-life of about 100 to 150 hours. Little reaches the urine or cerebrospinal fluid, but it is still used for cryptococcal meningitis because it reaches brain tissue.", "sidefx": "Because amphotericin also binds cholesterol in human membranes, though weakly, kidney tubule cells become leaky, losing potassium and magnesium, and renal vessels constrict, lowering filtration. Saline loading and replacing electrolytes reduce this. Fever and chills come from cytokine release by monocytes. Back or chest pain during infusion is a liposome-related reaction. Anemia reflects less erythropoietin. A key danger is confusing products: conventional amphotericin given at liposomal doses has caused fatal cardiac arrest. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Amphotericin B got its name from its amphoteric chemistry, meaning it can act as both an acid and a base.", "src": ["AmBisome (amphotericin B liposome) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "artesunate": {"origin": "Artesunate comes from sweet wormwood, Artemisia annua, known in China as qinghao. A 4th-century text by Ge Hong described soaking the herb in cold water and drinking the juice for fevers. During the Vietnam War, China launched Project 523 to find new malaria drugs. In 1971 and 1972 Tu Youyou and her team tested a low-temperature extract, inspired by Ge Hong's cold-water method, and isolated artemisinin, which cleared malaria in mice, monkeys and people. Chinese chemists then made artesunate in the late 1970s, a water-soluble derivative that can be injected. Large trials showed it lowered death from severe malaria compared with quinine. The CDC supplied it in the US under an investigational program, and the FDA approved Artesunate for Injection (Amivas) in May 2020. Tu Youyou shared the 2015 Nobel Prize.", "timeline": [["4th century", "Ge Hong describes qinghao for fevers in a Chinese medical text."], ["1972", "Tu Youyou's team isolates artemisinin under Project 523."], ["1970s", "Chinese chemists synthesize artesunate."], ["2015", "Tu Youyou shares the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."], ["2020", "FDA approves Artesunate for Injection for severe malaria."]], "cell": "Artesunate is a prodrug that plasma esterases quickly hydrolyze to dihydroartemisinin (DHA). The key part of the molecule is an endoperoxide bridge. Inside red cells, the malaria parasite digests hemoglobin in its food vacuole, freeing heme with ferrous iron (Fe2+). Heme iron cleaves the endoperoxide bridge, producing reactive oxygen and carbon-centered free radicals. These radicals alkylate many parasite proteins and heme, damage membranes, and disrupt targets proposed to include the calcium pump PfATP6. Because it acts on many stages, from young ring forms to mature schizonts, it clears parasites faster than any other antimalarial and prevents rings from maturing into sticky forms that block small vessels. Resistance in Southeast Asia is linked to kelch13 mutations that let ring stages survive.", "body": [["Blood (parasites)", "Rapidly kills Plasmodium in red cells, clearing parasitemia faster than quinine and lowering death in severe malaria."], ["Brain and organs", "Stopping parasites before they mature reduces blockage of small vessels, which drives cerebral malaria and organ failure."], ["Red cells", "Delayed hemolysis can occur one to several weeks later as once-infected red cells, pitted by the spleen, are cleared."], ["Kidneys and liver", "Hemoglobinuria, kidney injury and jaundice are seen, often as part of severe malaria itself."]], "journey": "Artesunate is given IV, usually at set intervals, followed by a full oral course of another antimalarial. It is converted within minutes by plasma esterases to dihydroartemisinin, which is about 93 percent protein bound. DHA peaks within 15 to 25 minutes. Artesunate has a half-life under 15 minutes, and DHA about half an hour to an hour and a half. DHA is glucuronidated by UGT1A9 and UGT2B7 and leaves in the urine. Because it is cleared so fast, a partner drug is needed to finish off the remaining parasites.", "sidefx": "Post-artesunate delayed hemolysis happens because the drug kills parasites inside red cells, and the spleen removes the parasite but returns the damaged red cells to the blood. These once-infected cells die about one to three weeks later, causing anemia, so hemoglobin is checked for weeks afterward. Hemoglobinuria, kidney injury and jaundice often reflect severe malaria. Allergic reactions and anaphylaxis are rare. In animals, artemisinins can harm early embryos, though they are used in pregnancy for severe malaria. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Tu Youyou volunteered to be one of the first people to take the artemisinin extract to test its safety before it was given to patients.", "src": ["Artesunate for Injection FDA label", "Tu Y. Nat Med 2011 (The discovery of artemisinin)", "Dondorp AM et al. Lancet 2010 (AQUAMAT)"]}, "rabies-immune-globulin": {"origin": "In 1885 Louis Pasteur gave the first rabies vaccine to Joseph Meister, a boy bitten by a rabid dog. Vaccines take one to two weeks to produce antibodies, which can be too slow after a severe bite. In 1954 researchers at the Pasteur Institute of Iran, working with the World Health Organization, treated people bitten by a rabid wolf and showed that adding antirabies serum to vaccine saved more lives than vaccine alone. Early serum came from horses and caused serum sickness, so human rabies immune globulin, purified from donors with high antibody levels, was developed and licensed in the US in the 1970s. Today HyperRAB, Imogam Rabies-HT and Kedrab supply this passive immunity, given once at the start of post-exposure prophylaxis together with vaccine.", "timeline": [["1885", "Louis Pasteur gives the first rabies vaccine to Joseph Meister."], ["1954", "A study in Iran after rabid wolf bites shows serum plus vaccine works better than vaccine alone."], ["1970s", "Human rabies immune globulin is licensed in the US."], ["2017", "FDA approves Kedrab, another human rabies immune globulin."]], "cell": "Rabies immune globulin is pooled human IgG from donors hyperimmunized with rabies vaccine. Its antibodies bind the rabies virus glycoprotein, the spike that lets the virus attach to nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction and other receptors before entering nerve endings. Once inside a nerve, the virus travels by retrograde axonal transport to the brain, where antibodies cannot reach it. By neutralizing virus at the wound before it enters nerves, RIG blocks infection. Antibody-coated virus is also cleared through Fc-mediated uptake by phagocytes and complement. This passive protection bridges the gap of about 7 to 14 days until the vaccine causes the body to make its own antibodies. Larger or repeated doses can bind vaccine antigen and suppress the active response, which is why it is given only once and only up to day 7 after the first vaccine dose.", "body": [["Wound site", "Infiltrating the wound with antibody neutralizes virus before it can enter peripheral nerves."], ["Blood", "Any remaining antibody given intramuscularly provides circulating neutralizing antibody for weeks."], ["Immune system", "Provides passive protection while the vaccine builds active immunity; too much can blunt that vaccine response."], ["Injection site", "Pain, tenderness and soreness at the wound and injection sites are common."]], "journey": "As much of the dose as anatomically feasible is infiltrated into and around the wound, and any remainder is injected IM at a site away from the vaccine. Wound infiltration gives immediate local neutralization. From the IM site, antibody is absorbed slowly, and serum levels peak in about two to seven days. It is broken down like the body's own IgG by catabolism, with a half-life of about three weeks.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are local pain and tenderness from injecting volume into tissue, plus headache or low-grade fever. Because it is human IgG rather than horse serum, serum sickness is rare. Hypersensitivity and anaphylaxis are possible, especially in people with IgA deficiency who have anti-IgA antibodies. Rarely, immune globulins can cause thrombosis. As a blood product, it carries a theoretical risk of transmitting infectious agents, minimized by donor screening and viral inactivation. Giving vaccine and RIG in the same syringe or site would neutralize the vaccine, so they are kept apart.", "fun": "Joseph Meister later worked as a caretaker at the Pasteur Institute in Paris for many years.", "src": ["HyperRAB, Imogam Rabies-HT and Kedrab FDA labels", "CDC ACIP rabies post-exposure prophylaxis recommendations", "Baltazard M et al. Bull World Health Organ 1955"]}, "tetanus-immune-globulin": {"origin": "The idea behind tetanus immune globulin goes back to 1890, when Emil von Behring and Shibasaburo Kitasato, working in Robert Koch's institute in Berlin, showed that serum from animals immunized against tetanus toxin could protect other animals. This was the birth of antitoxin therapy, and von Behring received the first Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 1901 for serum therapy. For decades the antitoxin came from horses, which often caused serum sickness and allergic reactions. Starting in the 1960s, antitoxin made from the plasma of human donors with high anti-tetanus antibody levels replaced horse serum in the United States. Today's product, sold as HyperTET, is pooled human IgG with a high titer of antibody to tetanospasmin.", "timeline": [["1884", "Arthur Nicolaier produces tetanus in animals with soil samples, pointing to a soil microbe."], ["1890", "Von Behring and Kitasato show that antitoxin serum protects animals against tetanus toxin."], ["1901", "Von Behring receives the first Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for serum therapy."], ["1960s", "Human tetanus immune globulin replaces horse antitoxin in the US."]], "cell": "Clostridium tetani grows in dirty, low-oxygen wounds and releases tetanospasmin. The toxin binds to motor nerve endings, is taken up and carried backward along the axon to the spinal cord. There it crosses into inhibitory interneurons and cleaves synaptobrevin, a SNARE protein needed to release GABA and glycine. Without this inhibition, motor neurons fire without control, causing rigidity and spasms. Tetanus immune globulin is human IgG that binds the toxin while it is still free in the blood and tissue fluid. The antibody-toxin complex can no longer attach to nerve endings and is cleared by phagocytes. It cannot reach toxin already inside neurons, which is why it prevents worsening but does not reverse spasms that have started.", "body": [["Wound and tissue fluid", "Antibodies given near the time of injury catch toxin as it leaks out of the wound, before it can bind to nerve terminals."], ["Nervous system", "By blocking new toxin uptake, it limits how many inhibitory synapses are lost, which can reduce the severity and length of spasms."], ["Immune system", "It provides borrowed (passive) immunity for about four weeks while the tetanus toxoid vaccine teaches the person's own immune system to make antibody."], ["Injection site", "Soreness and tenderness come from the volume of protein injected into muscle."]], "journey": "Tetanus immune globulin is injected into muscle. The IgG is absorbed slowly from the muscle into the blood, reaching peak levels in about two days. It spreads through the blood and tissue fluid, where it meets the toxin. It is not metabolized by the liver or cleared by the kidneys. Instead it is broken down like the person's own IgG, with a half-life of about three to four weeks, giving roughly a month of protection.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are local pain and low-grade fever from injecting a large amount of foreign protein into muscle. Because it is a human product rather than horse serum, serum sickness is uncommon. Rare allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, can happen with any plasma protein. Like other immune globulins, it carries a small risk of blood clots. Because it is made from pooled human plasma, donor screening and viral inactivation steps reduce the risk of passing on infections.", "fun": "Before human antitoxin, horse antitoxin was so common that serum sickness, a delayed reaction to horse proteins, was first described in detail in 1905 by Clemens von Pirquet and Bela Schick in children treated with horse serum.", "src": ["HyperTET S/D (tetanus immune globulin) FDA label", "CDC Pink Book: Tetanus", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "hepatitis-b-immune-globulin": {"origin": "Hepatitis B immune globulin grew out of the discovery of the hepatitis B virus itself. In the mid-1960s Baruch Blumberg found an unusual protein in the blood of an Australian Aboriginal man, which he called the Australia antigen. Later work showed it was the surface coat of the hepatitis B virus (HBsAg), and Blumberg shared the 1976 Nobel Prize for this work. Once scientists could measure antibody to that antigen (anti-HBs), they could collect plasma from donors with very high levels and purify their IgG. Hyperimmune globulins of this kind were developed in the 1970s. Today HBIG (HyperHEP B, Nabi-HB, HepaGam B) is given with hepatitis B vaccine to newborns of infected mothers and after needlestick or sexual exposures, and to liver transplant recipients with hepatitis B.", "timeline": [["1965", "Baruch Blumberg reports the Australia antigen, later shown to be hepatitis B surface antigen."], ["1970s", "Hyperimmune globulin rich in anti-HBs is developed for post-exposure protection."], ["1976", "Blumberg shares the Nobel Prize for discoveries about hepatitis B."], ["1981", "The first hepatitis B vaccine, made from plasma, is licensed in the US."]], "cell": "The hepatitis B virus enters liver cells when its surface protein (HBsAg) binds to a bile acid transporter on hepatocytes called NTCP. HBIG contains large amounts of anti-HBs IgG. These antibodies coat circulating virions and free HBsAg particles, blocking the part of the surface protein the virus needs to bind and enter liver cells. Coated virus is also tagged for removal by phagocytes in the liver and spleen. This is passive immunity: the antibody works at once but fades over weeks. Given together with the vaccine, it covers the gap while the person's own B cells learn to make anti-HBs. It does not clear virus already living inside infected hepatocytes.", "body": [["Blood", "Antibodies bind free virus and surface antigen in the blood right after exposure, lowering the amount of virus that reaches the liver."], ["Liver", "With fewer virions reaching hepatocytes, infection is less likely to take hold. In newborns, HBIG plus vaccine prevents most mother-to-child transmission."], ["Immune system", "It supplies borrowed antibody for several weeks and does not block the vaccine from working when given at a different site."], ["Transplanted liver", "In liver transplant for hepatitis B, ongoing HBIG helps keep the virus from infecting the new liver."]], "journey": "HBIG is usually injected into muscle, where it is absorbed slowly into the blood over several days. Some products can be given by vein, as in liver transplant, which gives immediate high levels. The antibody spreads through blood and tissue fluid. It is not metabolized by liver enzymes or filtered by the kidneys. It is broken down by cells like the body's own IgG, with a half-life of about three weeks.", "sidefx": "Pain at the injection site, headache and muscle aches come from the injected protein and the immune response to it. Allergic reactions are rare but possible with any plasma product, and people with IgA deficiency who have antibodies to IgA are at higher risk. Like other immune globulins, it carries a small risk of blood clots. Donor screening and viral inactivation steps lower the risk of passing on infections.", "fun": "Blumberg was not looking for a virus at all. He was studying inherited differences in blood proteins among people around the world when he stumbled on the Australia antigen.", "src": ["HyperHEP B (hepatitis B immune globulin) FDA label", "Nabi-HB FDA label", "CDC Pink Book: Hepatitis B"]}, "varicella-zoster-immune-globulin": {"origin": "In the late 1960s researchers including Philip Brunell showed that plasma from people recovering from shingles (zoster) had high levels of antibody against the varicella zoster virus and could protect exposed high-risk children from severe chickenpox. This zoster immune globulin was later replaced by varicella zoster immune globulin (VZIG), made from donor plasma with high antibody titers, which was licensed in the US in 1981. When the US maker stopped producing VZIG in the mid-2000s, a Canadian product, VARIZIG, became available under an investigational protocol and was then FDA approved in 2012. It protects people who cannot safely receive the live varicella vaccine, such as some immunocompromised patients, pregnant women and certain newborns.", "timeline": [["1954", "Thomas Weller grows varicella zoster virus in cell culture."], ["1969", "Zoster immune globulin from convalescent plasma is shown to protect exposed children."], ["1981", "Varicella zoster immune globulin (VZIG) is licensed in the US."], ["2012", "FDA approves VARIZIG after US supply of the older product ended."]], "cell": "Varicella zoster virus spreads by breathing in virus particles. It first infects cells in the airway and nearby lymph nodes, then spreads through the blood, carried by infected T cells, to the skin and organs during an incubation period of about two weeks. VARIZIG is purified human IgG rich in antibodies to the virus envelope glycoproteins. These antibodies bind virus particles outside cells, blocking attachment and fusion, and mark infected cells for destruction by complement and natural killer cells. Given during the incubation period, it lowers the amount of virus that reaches the skin and organs. It provides passive protection only and does not treat chickenpox once the rash has appeared.", "body": [["Blood", "Antibodies neutralize virus during the early spread through the blood, which lowers the chance of widespread infection."], ["Lungs", "Less virus spread means a lower risk of varicella pneumonia, a dangerous complication in pregnant women and adults."], ["Brain", "Reducing viral spread lowers the risk of encephalitis in high-risk patients."], ["Skin", "Infection, if it still happens, tends to be milder with fewer lesions."], ["Immune system", "It provides borrowed protection for a few weeks without training the person's own immunity."]], "journey": "VARIZIG is injected into muscle and absorbed slowly into the blood, with peak levels in about five days. It works best when given as soon as possible after exposure, within the time window in the label. The antibody spreads through blood and tissue fluid and is broken down by cells like the body's own IgG, with a half-life of about three and a half weeks.", "sidefx": "Pain at the injection site and headache come from injecting a concentrated protein solution. Allergic reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, can occur with any plasma-derived product, and the risk is higher in people with IgA deficiency who have anti-IgA antibodies. Immune globulins carry a small risk of blood clots, more so in older people and those with clotting risk factors. Antibody from VARIZIG can interfere with live vaccines given soon after, so those vaccines are timed later.", "fun": "Shingles patients were the original donors. Their reactivated virus boosts their antibody levels so high that their plasma made the first protective globulin.", "src": ["VARIZIG (varicella zoster immune globulin) FDA label", "CDC Pink Book: Varicella", "Brunell PA et al. NEJM 1969"]}, "botulism-antitoxin-heptavalent": {"origin": "Botulism was first described in detail in the 1820s by the German physician Justinus Kerner, who studied deaths from spoiled sausages (botulus is Latin for sausage). In 1895, after a funeral meal in Belgium sickened many guests, Emile van Ermengem isolated the bacterium now called Clostridium botulinum. Horse antitoxins against some toxin types were used for decades. Because botulinum toxin is a potential bioterrorism agent, the US government sponsored development of an antitoxin covering all seven classic serotypes, A through G. The horse IgG is cut with enzymes to remove most of the Fc portion, making it 'despeciated' and less likely to cause reactions. This heptavalent botulism antitoxin (BAT) was approved by the FDA in 2013 and is supplied through the CDC and the Strategic National Stockpile.", "timeline": [["1820s", "Justinus Kerner describes sausage poisoning, later called botulism."], ["1895", "Emile van Ermengem isolates Clostridium botulinum after a Belgian outbreak."], ["1940s", "Horse antitoxins against some toxin types come into use."], ["2013", "FDA approves heptavalent botulism antitoxin (BAT)."]], "cell": "Botulinum toxin has a heavy chain and a light chain. The heavy chain binds receptors on cholinergic nerve terminals and the toxin is taken in by endocytosis. The light chain, a zinc protease, then escapes into the cytoplasm and cuts SNARE proteins: SNAP-25 for types A and E, and synaptobrevin for types B, D, F and G. Without intact SNAREs, vesicles of acetylcholine cannot fuse with the membrane, so nerves cannot signal muscles. BAT is a mix of horse antibody fragments (Fab and F(ab')2) against all seven types. The fragments bind toxin still circulating in the blood, block it from attaching to nerve terminals, and allow it to be cleared. Toxin already inside nerve endings is not reached. Recovery needs new nerve sprouts and SNARE proteins, which takes weeks to months.", "body": [["Nerve terminals", "By stopping more toxin from entering nerve endings, BAT halts the spread of paralysis but cannot undo the paralysis already present."], ["Muscles", "Weakness typically starts in the cranial nerves (double vision, drooping eyelids, trouble swallowing) and descends. Early antitoxin limits how far it goes."], ["Breathing", "Limiting paralysis of the diaphragm and chest muscles can reduce how long a patient needs a ventilator."], ["Autonomic nerves", "Botulism also blocks autonomic cholinergic nerves, causing dry mouth, constipation and urinary retention. Antitoxin stops further loss."], ["Immune system", "Horse proteins can trigger allergic reactions or delayed serum sickness, even after the Fc portion is removed."]], "journey": "BAT is given by slow infusion into a vein so that antibody reaches circulating toxin right away. The smaller Fab pieces clear faster, partly through the kidneys, while the larger F(ab')2 pieces last longer. Depending on the serotype, half-lives range from under a day to more than a day. The fragments are broken down as proteins. Because toxin binds nerve endings quickly, giving it early matters more than its exact half-life.", "sidefx": "The main risks come from the horse source. Immediate allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, can occur because the immune system sees horse protein as foreign. Delayed serum sickness, with fever, rash and joint pain one to three weeks later, happens when antibodies against horse protein form immune complexes. Infusion reactions such as fever and chills are common. Slow heart rate has been seen during infusion. Staff are prepared to treat anaphylaxis with epinephrine during the infusion.", "fun": "Botulinum toxin is one of the most potent toxins known, yet the same molecule, in tiny medical amounts, is used as Botox to relax wrinkles and treat muscle spasms.", "src": ["BAT (botulism antitoxin heptavalent, equine) FDA label", "CDC Botulism clinical guidance", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "esketamine": {"origin": "Esketamine is the mirror-image S form of ketamine. Ketamine was made in 1962 by chemist C. L. Stevens at Parke-Davis as a safer replacement for phencyclidine (PCP), and it was approved as an anesthetic in 1970. In 2000 a small study at Yale led by Robert Berman, Dennis Charney and John Krystal found that a single ketamine infusion lifted depression within hours, a striking result because standard antidepressants take weeks. Janssen developed the S-enantiomer, which binds NMDA receptors more strongly, as a nasal spray. The FDA approved it as Spravato in 2019 for treatment-resistant depression, the first new mechanism for depression in decades, and later for depressive symptoms in adults with major depression and suicidal thoughts. It is given only in certified clinics under a REMS program.", "timeline": [["1962", "Ketamine is synthesized at Parke-Davis."], ["1970", "FDA approves ketamine as an anesthetic."], ["2000", "Yale researchers report a rapid antidepressant effect of ketamine."], ["2019", "FDA approves esketamine nasal spray (Spravato) for treatment-resistant depression."], ["2020", "Label expands to depressive symptoms in adults with major depression and suicidal ideation or behavior."]], "cell": "Esketamine blocks the NMDA receptor, an ion channel opened by glutamate. It enters the open channel and binds at the PCP site inside the pore, so it is a non-competitive blocker. A leading theory is that it blocks NMDA receptors on GABA interneurons more than on pyramidal neurons. With the brakes off, pyramidal neurons release a burst of glutamate, which activates AMPA receptors. AMPA signaling triggers release of BDNF, which activates TrkB receptors and the mTOR pathway. This drives new protein synthesis and the growth of new dendritic spines and synapses in the prefrontal cortex, reversing synapse loss seen with chronic stress. The same NMDA block causes the short-lived dissociation and perceptual changes.", "body": [["Brain: mood circuits", "Rapid growth of new synapses in the prefrontal cortex is thought to underlie mood improvement that can start within a day."], ["Brain: perception", "NMDA block disconnects normal sensory processing, causing dissociation, a dreamlike or detached feeling, that peaks soon after a dose and fades within about two hours."], ["Brain: alertness", "Sedation and dizziness are common, which is why patients are observed and do not drive until the next day."], ["Heart and blood vessels", "Blood pressure rises temporarily, peaking around 40 minutes, from increased sympathetic activity."], ["Bladder", "Ketamine-related drugs can irritate the bladder lining, and cystitis is a listed risk."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are common, which is why patients avoid food before a session."]], "journey": "Esketamine is sprayed into the nose, where it is absorbed through the nasal lining into the blood, skipping first-pass liver breakdown. Bioavailability is under half. It is fat-soluble and enters the brain within minutes. The liver breaks it down, mainly by CYP2B6 and CYP3A4, to noresketamine and other metabolites, which leave in urine. Dissociation and sedation fade in about two hours, and the terminal half-life is about 7 to 12 hours.", "sidefx": "Dissociation, dizziness and altered perception come directly from NMDA block in sensory and cortical circuits. Sedation and, rarely, breathing suppression are more likely when combined with benzodiazepines, opioids or alcohol. Blood pressure rises because ketamine-like drugs boost sympathetic outflow and block norepinephrine reuptake, so it is avoided in people with aneurysms or bleeding in the brain. The euphoric and dissociative effects create abuse potential, which is why it is Schedule III and given only under supervision. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Spravato is the only antidepressant patients take in a clinic and then must sit and be watched for at least two hours before going home.", "src": ["Spravato (esketamine) FDA label", "Berman RM et al. Biol Psychiatry 2000", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "brexanolone": {"origin": "Brexanolone is a medical version of allopregnanolone, a steroid the body makes from progesterone. In the 1980s researchers discovered that certain progesterone breakdown products act on brain GABA-A receptors, calming nerve activity. Allopregnanolone levels rise sharply in pregnancy and fall after birth, and that drop was proposed as one trigger for postpartum depression. Sage Therapeutics developed a stable IV form, dissolving the steroid in a cyclodextrin carrier. In 2019 the FDA approved it as Zulresso, the first drug approved specifically for postpartum depression. It required a continuous infusion over about 60 hours in a monitored facility because of sudden sedation. After the oral neurosteroid zuranolone was approved in 2023, Zulresso was discontinued and has no current US label.", "timeline": [["1986", "Researchers show steroid metabolites can act on GABA-A receptors."], ["2019", "FDA approves brexanolone (Zulresso) for postpartum depression."], ["2023", "Oral zuranolone is approved for postpartum depression."], ["2020s", "Zulresso is discontinued in the US."]], "cell": "GABA-A receptors are chloride channels that quiet neurons. There are two main kinds: synaptic receptors, which respond to bursts of GABA, and extrasynaptic receptors (often containing delta subunits), which create a steady background inhibition called tonic inhibition. Brexanolone binds a neurosteroid site in the transmembrane part of the receptor, separate from the benzodiazepine site. It is a positive allosteric modulator, so it makes GABA open the channel more effectively, letting more chloride in and hyperpolarizing the neuron. Because it acts on extrasynaptic receptors that benzodiazepines do not affect, it boosts tonic inhibition. The theory is that this rapidly resets overactive stress and mood networks that were destabilized by the fall in allopregnanolone after childbirth.", "body": [["Brain: mood", "Depressive symptoms improved within a day or two in trials, much faster than standard antidepressants, and the benefit lasted to 30 days."], ["Brain: alertness", "Enhanced GABA signaling causes sleepiness and, in some patients, sudden loss of consciousness, which is why oxygen levels were monitored throughout."], ["Breathing", "Heavy sedation can lead to low oxygen levels, especially with other sedatives."], ["Skin and blood vessels", "Flushing and hot flashes were reported during the infusion, along with dry mouth."], ["Breast milk", "Small amounts pass into milk, but the oral bioavailability in an infant is low."]], "journey": "Brexanolone was given only by continuous IV infusion over about 60 hours, with the rate stepped up and then down. It is very fat-soluble and more than 99 percent bound to plasma proteins, and it spreads widely, including into the brain. It is not broken down by CYP enzymes. Instead it undergoes keto-reduction, glucuronidation and sulfation into inactive metabolites. These leave in both feces and urine. The half-life is about nine hours.", "sidefx": "Nearly all key side effects come from boosting GABA-A inhibition throughout the brain. Sleepiness, dizziness and sudden loss of consciousness follow from strong neuronal quieting, and low oxygen can happen if breathing slows. Because of this, the label carried a boxed warning and the drug was given only in certified settings with continuous pulse oximetry. Other sedatives, such as alcohol, opioids and benzodiazepines, add to these effects. There is no specific reversal agent; stopping the infusion lets symptoms resolve within about 15 to 60 minutes.", "fun": "Brexanolone is chemically the same as a hormone every pregnant woman makes, which is why it was described as replacing what the brain lost rather than adding something new.", "src": ["Zulresso (brexanolone) FDA label", "Meltzer-Brody S et al. Lancet 2018", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "surfactant-beractant": {"origin": "In 1959 Mary Ellen Avery and Jere Mead at Harvard showed that the lungs of premature babies who died of respiratory distress syndrome lacked surfactant, a soapy film that keeps the tiny air sacs open. That discovery made replacing surfactant a goal. In 1980 Tetsuro Fujiwara and colleagues in Japan reported the first successful treatment of premature babies with a surfactant extracted from cow lungs and enriched with lipids. That preparation became the basis of beractant, developed in the US by Abbott (Ross Laboratories) as Survanta. The FDA approved Survanta in 1991. Surfactant therapy, along with steroids before birth, sharply lowered deaths from respiratory distress in premature infants.", "timeline": [["1959", "Avery and Mead link respiratory distress in premature babies to surfactant deficiency."], ["1980", "Fujiwara reports successful treatment of premature babies with bovine-derived surfactant."], ["1991", "FDA approves beractant (Survanta)."]], "cell": "Alveoli are lined by a thin layer of water. Water molecules pull on each other, creating surface tension that tends to collapse small air sacs, especially at the end of a breath. By the law of Laplace, the pressure needed to keep a bubble open rises as the radius shrinks, so small alveoli empty into larger ones. Type II pneumocytes normally secrete surfactant, mostly dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (DPPC) with surfactant proteins SP-B and SP-C. These proteins help the lipids spread and form a film at the air-water surface. Premature lungs have too few mature type II cells. Beractant, a cow lung extract with SP-B and SP-C plus added DPPC, palmitic acid and tripalmitin, spreads across the alveolar surface and lowers surface tension. Alveoli stay open, lung compliance rises and gas exchange improves.", "body": [["Lungs", "Air sacs stay open at the end of each breath, so the lungs become less stiff and the baby needs less ventilator pressure."], ["Blood oxygen", "Oxygen levels often improve within minutes, allowing the inspired oxygen to be lowered."], ["Airway", "The liquid is placed through the breathing tube, so it can briefly block the tube or cause reflux into it during dosing."], ["Heart", "Brief slow heart rate and oxygen drops can occur during the dose as the airway is temporarily filled with liquid."], ["Lung blood vessels", "As lung pressures change quickly, rare bleeding into the lungs and air leaks such as pneumothorax can occur."]], "journey": "Beractant is dripped directly into the trachea through an endotracheal tube, often in portions with the baby positioned to spread it to all parts of the lungs. It stays in the lungs and joins the baby's own surfactant pool. Type II cells take up and recycle the lipids, and alveolar macrophages break down the rest. Almost none reaches the general circulation. Effects on oxygen appear within minutes and last for hours, and repeat doses can be given if needed.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from the way it is given. Filling the airway with liquid can briefly cause a slow heart rate and drop in oxygen, and the tube can become blocked. Improved lung compliance happens fast, so ventilator settings may suddenly be too high, which can overinflate the lungs and cause air leaks or pneumothorax. Pulmonary hemorrhage may relate to rapid changes in lung blood flow as pressures fall. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Mary Ellen Avery's discovery started with a simple observation: foam from healthy lung tissue was stable, but foam from the lungs of babies who died of respiratory distress was not.", "src": ["Survanta (beractant) FDA label", "Avery ME, Mead J. Am J Dis Child 1959", "Fujiwara T et al. Lancet 1980"]}, "nitric-oxide-inhaled": {"origin": "In 1980 Robert Furchgott showed that blood vessels relax only when their inner lining (endothelium) is intact, and he called the unknown signal endothelium-derived relaxing factor. In 1987 groups led by Louis Ignarro and Salvador Moncada identified it as nitric oxide, a simple gas. Ferid Murad had already shown that nitric oxide activates guanylate cyclase. Furchgott, Ignarro and Murad shared the 1998 Nobel Prize. In 1991 Warren Zapol's group at Massachusetts General Hospital showed that breathing nitric oxide relaxed lung blood vessels without dropping systemic blood pressure. The FDA approved inhaled nitric oxide (INOmax) in 1999 for term and near-term newborns with hypoxic respiratory failure and pulmonary hypertension, reducing the need for heart-lung bypass (ECMO).", "timeline": [["1980", "Furchgott describes endothelium-derived relaxing factor."], ["1987", "Ignarro and Moncada identify it as nitric oxide."], ["1991", "Zapol's group shows inhaled nitric oxide is a selective lung vasodilator."], ["1998", "Furchgott, Ignarro and Murad share the Nobel Prize for nitric oxide signaling."], ["1999", "FDA approves inhaled nitric oxide (INOmax) for newborns."]], "cell": "Nitric oxide is a small gas that passes straight through cell membranes. Breathed in, it reaches only alveoli that are ventilated and diffuses into the smooth muscle of nearby small arteries. There it binds the heme group of soluble guanylate cyclase, which converts GTP to cyclic GMP. Cyclic GMP activates protein kinase G, which lowers calcium inside the cell and activates myosin light chain phosphatase. The muscle relaxes and the vessel widens. Because only vessels next to air-filled alveoli widen, blood is steered toward lung units that can pick up oxygen, which improves ventilation-perfusion matching. When nitric oxide reaches the blood, hemoglobin binds and inactivates it within seconds, so systemic vessels are barely affected.", "body": [["Lung blood vessels", "Pulmonary arteries relax, lowering pulmonary artery pressure and reducing the strain on the right heart."], ["Gas exchange", "Blood is redirected to ventilated alveoli, so oxygen levels rise and less blood shunts past the lungs."], ["Heart: shunts in newborns", "Lower lung pressure reduces right-to-left flow through the ductus arteriosus and foramen ovale in newborns with pulmonary hypertension."], ["Blood", "Nitric oxide reacts with hemoglobin to form methemoglobin, which cannot carry oxygen, so levels are monitored."], ["Systemic circulation", "Because hemoglobin inactivates it so fast, blood pressure usually changes little."], ["Left heart", "In left heart failure, more blood returning to the left heart can raise pressures and cause pulmonary edema."]], "journey": "Nitric oxide is delivered as a gas mixed into the ventilator circuit, from a cylinder or a bedside generator. It is absorbed across the alveoli in seconds and acts on nearby vessels. In the blood it combines with oxyhemoglobin to form methemoglobin and nitrate. Red cell enzymes convert methemoglobin back to hemoglobin, and nitrate is excreted in the urine. Its effect starts within minutes and stops within minutes after it is turned off.", "sidefx": "Methemoglobinemia happens because nitric oxide oxidizes hemoglobin iron. Nitric oxide also reacts with oxygen to form nitrogen dioxide, which irritates the airways, so its level is monitored in the circuit. Stopping it suddenly can cause rebound pulmonary hypertension and a sharp fall in oxygen, because the body's own nitric oxide production is suppressed during treatment, so it is weaned slowly. In patients with left ventricular failure, more lung blood flow can overload the left heart and cause pulmonary edema. Methemoglobinemia can be treated with methylene blue.", "fun": "Nitric oxide was named Molecule of the Year by the journal Science in 1992, a remarkable honor for a gas once known mainly as an air pollutant.", "src": ["INOmax (nitric oxide) FDA label", "Furchgott RF, Zawadzki JV. Nature 1980", "Frostell C, Zapol WM et al. Circulation 1991"]}, "basiliximab": {"origin": "Basiliximab came from decades of work on how T cells drive organ rejection. In the mid-1970s researchers discovered interleukin-2 (IL-2), a growth signal T cells use to multiply. The high-affinity IL-2 receptor includes an alpha chain, CD25 (once called the Tac antigen), that appears mainly on activated T cells. Blocking it promised to stop T cells that were reacting to a transplant while leaving resting T cells alone. Novartis built basiliximab as a chimeric antibody, with mouse binding regions joined to a human IgG1 frame to reduce immune reactions against it. The FDA approved it as Simulect in 1998 for preventing acute rejection in kidney transplants, given as induction around the time of surgery.", "timeline": [["1976", "A T-cell growth factor, later named interleukin-2, is described."], ["1981", "The Tac antigen, later known as CD25, is identified on activated T cells."], ["1998", "FDA approves basiliximab (Simulect) for kidney transplant induction."]], "cell": "When a T cell recognizes donor antigens, it becomes activated and makes both IL-2 and the IL-2 receptor alpha chain, CD25. CD25 joins the beta and gamma chains to form the high-affinity IL-2 receptor. IL-2 binding activates JAK1 and JAK3 and STAT5 signaling, along with mTOR, pushing the T cell from the G1 phase into the S phase of the cell cycle so it divides into a clone. Basiliximab binds CD25 with high affinity and blocks IL-2 from binding. Without this signal, activated T cells cannot expand. The antibody does not kill T cells, so it does not deplete lymphocytes or cause cytokine release. CD25 remains saturated for about four to six weeks, covering the early period when rejection risk is highest.", "body": [["Immune system", "Only T cells that have been activated by the new organ are held back, so overall immune defense is less affected than with depleting antibodies."], ["Transplanted kidney", "Fewer activated T cells reach the graft, which lowers the rate of acute rejection in the first weeks after transplant."], ["Infection risk", "Infections are similar to placebo in trials, but they remain a concern because it is always used with other immunosuppressants."], ["Gut and general", "Constipation, nausea, swelling and high blood pressure were common in trials but occurred at similar rates with placebo, reflecting transplant care in general."]], "journey": "Basiliximab is given by IV infusion or injection, usually once before transplant surgery and once a few days later. It stays mostly in the blood and lymph fluid, where it binds CD25 on T cells. Like other IgG antibodies, it is not metabolized by the liver or cleared by the kidneys. It is broken down by cells into amino acids. Its half-life is about a week in adults, and CD25 stays blocked for about a month or more.", "sidefx": "Because it blocks only one receptor and does not destroy cells, basiliximab causes far fewer reactions than older depleting antibodies. Its chimeric design lowers but does not eliminate the chance of allergic reactions, and rare severe hypersensitivity with anaphylaxis, capillary leak or pulmonary edema has been reported, especially on re-exposure. Long-term suppression of T cells together with other drugs raises the risk of infection and post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder, a lymphoma linked to Epstein-Barr virus. There is no reversal agent.", "fun": "The -ximab ending signals a chimeric antibody: part mouse, part human. Fully human antibodies end in -umab.", "src": ["Simulect (basiliximab) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "antithymocyte-globulin": {"origin": "Anti-thymocyte globulin is an old idea: inject human immune cells into an animal, collect the antibodies it makes, and give them back to people to wipe out their T cells. Antilymphocyte serums were studied in the 1960s, including in early kidney transplant programs. Upjohn developed an equine product, Atgam, made by immunizing horses with human thymus cells; the FDA approved it in 1981 for kidney transplant rejection, and it is also used for aplastic anemia. A rabbit product, Thymoglobulin, made by immunizing rabbits with human thymocytes, was approved in the US in 1998 for treating acute kidney transplant rejection. It is widely used for induction at the time of transplant, a use backed by guidelines.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Antilymphocyte serums are studied to prevent transplant rejection."], ["1981", "FDA approves equine anti-thymocyte globulin (Atgam)."], ["1998", "FDA approves rabbit anti-thymocyte globulin (Thymoglobulin)."], ["2017", "Thymoglobulin label expands to prophylaxis of rejection in kidney transplant."]], "cell": "Rabbit ATG is polyclonal: it contains many different antibodies that recognize many molecules on T cells, including CD2, CD3, CD4, CD8, CD11a, CD18, CD25, CD45 and HLA class I and II. Once they coat T cells, several things happen. In the blood, the Fc part of the antibodies activates complement, punching holes in the cell membrane and lysing the cell. In the spleen and lymph nodes, macrophages with Fc receptors engulf coated cells (opsonization). Some antibodies also trigger apoptosis. The result is deep and long-lasting T-cell depletion. Binding to adhesion molecules and receptors also blocks T-cell activation and trafficking. With fewer T cells, there are fewer to attack the graft.", "body": [["Blood", "Lymphocyte counts fall within hours and T-cell numbers can stay low for months. White cell and platelet counts can also drop because of cross-reacting antibodies."], ["Immune system", "Broad T-cell depletion reduces acute rejection but raises the risk of infections such as cytomegalovirus and fungal infections."], ["Whole body", "Fever, chills and low blood pressure with the first doses come from cytokines released as T cells are destroyed."], ["Transplanted kidney", "Fewer T cells means less attack on the new organ, both at induction and during a rejection episode."], ["Joints and skin", "Serum sickness, with fever, rash and joint pain days later, can follow because the body makes antibodies against the rabbit protein."]], "journey": "ATG is given by slow IV infusion through a large vein, usually over several days. It binds quickly to lymphocytes in blood and lymphoid tissue. The free rabbit IgG falls with a half-life of about two to three days at first, but it can be detected for weeks, with a terminal half-life of about a month. It is broken down as protein, not by the liver or kidneys. Patients are often given steroids, acetaminophen and antihistamines first to blunt infusion reactions.", "sidefx": "The first-dose reaction is cytokine release: as T cells are destroyed they release TNF, IL-6 and other cytokines, causing fever, chills, rigors and sometimes low blood pressure or breathing problems. Low white cell and platelet counts happen because some antibodies bind those cells too. Foreign animal protein can cause anaphylaxis or delayed serum sickness. Profound, long T-cell depletion raises the risk of serious infection and Epstein-Barr virus driven lymphoma (PTLD). There is no antidote; reactions are treated supportively.", "fun": "Each lot of ATG is a little different because it comes from a group of animals' immune responses rather than from a single cloned cell, which is why it is called polyclonal.", "src": ["Thymoglobulin (anti-thymocyte globulin, rabbit) FDA label", "Atgam (lymphocyte immune globulin, equine) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "intravenous-immune-globulin": {"origin": "IVIG rests on two milestones. During World War II, Edwin Cohn at Harvard developed cold ethanol fractionation, a way to split donated plasma into albumin, gamma globulin and other parts. In 1952 Ogden Bruton, an Army pediatrician, described a boy with repeated infections and no gamma globulin in his blood, the first recognized primary immunodeficiency, and treated him with injections of gamma globulin. Early products could only be given into muscle because they clumped and caused severe reactions in a vein. Processing changes in the late 1970s and early 1980s made IgG safe for IV use, and IV immune globulin was licensed in the US in the early 1980s. In 1981 Paul Imbach reported that IVIG raised platelets in children with immune thrombocytopenia, opening its use as an immune modulator.", "timeline": [["1940s", "Edwin Cohn develops cold ethanol fractionation of plasma."], ["1952", "Ogden Bruton describes agammaglobulinemia and treats it with gamma globulin."], ["1981", "Imbach reports IVIG raises platelet counts in childhood ITP."], ["1980s", "IV immune globulin products are licensed in the US."]], "cell": "IVIG is IgG pooled from thousands of donors, so it holds antibodies against a huge range of germs. In antibody deficiency it simply replaces missing IgG: the antibodies neutralize toxins and viruses and coat bacteria (opsonization) so phagocytes can eat them. At high levels it also changes immune activity in several ways. It saturates Fc gamma receptors on spleen macrophages, so they clear fewer antibody-coated platelets in ITP. It saturates FcRn, the receptor that normally rescues IgG from breakdown, which speeds removal of harmful autoantibodies. Anti-idiotype antibodies in the pool can bind and neutralize autoantibodies. It also mops up activated complement fragments, increases expression of the inhibitory receptor Fc gamma RIIB, and shifts cytokine production toward calmer patterns.", "body": [["Immune defense", "In people who cannot make antibodies, regular infusions restore IgG levels and greatly reduce pneumonia, sinus and other bacterial infections."], ["Platelets", "In ITP, blocking spleen Fc receptors slows the destruction of antibody-coated platelets, so counts rise within days."], ["Nerves", "In Guillain-Barre syndrome and CIDP, it lowers the activity of autoantibodies and complement that damage peripheral nerve myelin."], ["Heart and vessels", "In Kawasaki disease, it reduces vessel inflammation and the risk of coronary artery aneurysms."], ["Blood thickness", "A large protein load raises blood viscosity, which contributes to the risk of clots."], ["Kidneys", "Products stabilized with sucrose have been linked to kidney injury from osmotic damage to tubule cells."]], "journey": "IVIG is infused into a vein, so levels peak at the end of the infusion. The IgG first stays in the blood, then over a few days moves into tissue fluid until about half is outside the bloodstream. It is not metabolized by the liver or cleared by the kidneys. It is recycled by FcRn and slowly broken down by cells of the reticuloendothelial system, with a half-life of about three to five weeks, which is why replacement is given every three to four weeks.", "sidefx": "Headache, chills, flushing and muscle aches during or after infusion come partly from immune complexes and cytokine release, and often relate to infusion speed. Aseptic meningitis, a severe headache with neck stiffness, may come from antibodies and inflammation in the meninges. Hemolysis happens because donor anti-A and anti-B antibodies coat the patient's red cells. Clots, including stroke and heart attack, relate to higher blood viscosity and procoagulant factors, leading to a boxed warning for thrombosis and kidney injury. People with IgA deficiency and anti-IgA antibodies can have anaphylaxis. TRALI, a lung injury, is rare.", "fun": "A single lot of IVIG can contain plasma from more than 10,000 donors, so it carries a sampling of the immune memories of a whole population.", "src": ["Gammagard Liquid (immune globulin) FDA label", "Bruton OC. Pediatrics 1952", "Imbach P et al. Lancet 1981"]}, "rho-d-immune-globulin": {"origin": "In 1939 and 1940 Philip Levine, Karl Landsteiner and Alexander Wiener described the Rh blood group and linked it to hemolytic disease of the newborn, in which an Rh-negative mother makes antibodies that destroy her Rh-positive baby's red cells. In the early 1960s two teams worked on prevention. In New York, Vincent Freda, John Gorman and William Pollack (at Ortho) tested anti-D antibody given to Rh-negative men and then mothers. In Liverpool, Cyril Clarke and Ronald Finn did similar work. Giving anti-D just after delivery cleared fetal red cells before the mother's immune system could learn to react. The FDA licensed RhoGAM in 1968. Its use, later extended to during pregnancy, made Rh disease rare in countries with routine prophylaxis.", "timeline": [["1940", "Landsteiner and Wiener describe the Rh factor; Levine links it to newborn hemolytic disease."], ["1960s", "Teams in New York and Liverpool show anti-D can prevent Rh sensitization."], ["1968", "FDA licenses RhoGAM."], ["1990s", "IV anti-D is approved to treat immune thrombocytopenia in Rh-positive patients."]], "cell": "During pregnancy and especially at delivery, a small amount of fetal blood can enter the mother's circulation. If the mother is RhD-negative and the baby is RhD-positive, her immune system can see the D antigen as foreign and form memory B cells. In a later pregnancy, IgG anti-D crosses the placenta and destroys fetal red cells. Rho(D) immune globulin is IgG anti-D from donors. It binds the D antigen on fetal red cells in the mother's blood. Coated cells are removed by spleen macrophages through Fc receptors, and the antibody also suppresses the mother's primary B-cell response, so no anti-D memory forms. In ITP, IV anti-D coats the patient's own Rh-positive red cells, which keep spleen Fc receptors busy and spare antibody-coated platelets.", "body": [["Mother's immune system", "It prevents the mother from forming her own long-lasting anti-D antibodies, which protects future pregnancies."], ["Fetus and newborn", "Without maternal anti-D, later Rh-positive babies are spared hemolysis, anemia, jaundice and hydrops fetalis."], ["Spleen", "Antibody-coated red cells are cleared in the spleen. In ITP, this busy spleen clears fewer platelets."], ["Blood (ITP use)", "IV anti-D causes mild breakdown of the patient's own red cells, which lowers hemoglobin a little and, rarely, causes severe intravascular hemolysis."], ["Injection site", "Soreness and swelling come from the volume of protein injected into muscle."]], "journey": "Rho(D) immune globulin is given by injection into muscle or, for some products, into a vein. After an IM injection, levels peak in about two to seven days. It spreads through the blood and binds fetal red cells there. It is not metabolized by liver enzymes or cleared by the kidneys. It is broken down like the body's own IgG, with a half-life of about three to four weeks, which gives protection for about 12 weeks. That is why a dose at 28 weeks covers the rest of pregnancy.", "sidefx": "Local pain and mild fever come from injecting a protein solution. Because the drug works by coating red cells, a mild drop in hemoglobin can occur. When IV anti-D is used for ITP in Rh-positive patients, many red cells are coated at once, and rarely this causes intravascular hemolysis with kidney failure and disseminated intravascular coagulation, which led to a boxed warning. Allergic reactions are rare, with higher risk in IgA-deficient people with anti-IgA. It must not be given to the newborn.", "fun": "Some of the first anti-D donors were mothers who had already been sensitized, and a few men with very high anti-D levels donated plasma for decades, helping protect millions of babies.", "src": ["RhoGAM (Rho(D) immune globulin) FDA label", "WinRho SDF FDA label", "Freda VJ, Gorman JG, Pollack W. Transfusion 1964"]}, "tropicamide": {"origin": "Tropicamide belongs to a family that began with the deadly nightshade plant, Atropa belladonna. In Renaissance Italy women reportedly used belladonna drops to widen their pupils, which gave the plant its name, meaning beautiful woman. Atropine, the plant's main alkaloid, was isolated in the 1830s and became the classic pupil-dilating drug, but its effect can last a week or more. Eye doctors wanted something that dilated the pupil for an exam and wore off the same day. Tropicamide, a synthetic antimuscarinic, was developed in the 1950s and became the standard short-acting drop for eye exams. It is sold as Mydriacyl and in generics, and in Paremyd with hydroxyamphetamine.", "timeline": [["c. 1500s", "Belladonna is reportedly used to dilate pupils for beauty."], ["1830s", "Atropine is isolated from Atropa belladonna."], ["1950s", "Tropicamide is developed as a short-acting synthetic mydriatic."]], "cell": "Two muscles in the eye respond to acetylcholine from parasympathetic nerves. The sphincter pupillae in the iris narrows the pupil, and the ciliary muscle contracts to focus on near objects (accommodation). Both use M3 muscarinic receptors, which are Gq-coupled: acetylcholine binding activates phospholipase C, makes IP3, releases calcium and contracts the muscle. Tropicamide competitively blocks these muscarinic receptors. The sphincter relaxes, and the opposing dilator muscle, driven by sympathetic alpha-1 receptors, widens the pupil (mydriasis). The ciliary muscle also relaxes, so the eye cannot focus up close (cycloplegia). Tropicamide binds and leaves the receptor more quickly than atropine, so its effect lasts hours rather than days.", "body": [["Iris", "The pupil widens within about 20 to 40 minutes, letting the clinician see the retina and optic nerve."], ["Ciliary muscle", "Near focusing is temporarily lost, causing blurred near vision for a few hours."], ["Light sensitivity", "A wide pupil lets in too much light, so bright light feels uncomfortable until it wears off."], ["Eye drainage angle", "In people with narrow angles, the bunched-up iris can block fluid drainage and trigger acute angle-closure glaucoma."], ["Whole body", "Drops that drain through the tear duct into the nose can be absorbed; in small children this can rarely cause behavior changes or cardiorespiratory effects."]], "journey": "Tropicamide is put on the eye as a drop. It passes through the cornea into the front of the eye and reaches the iris and ciliary body. Dilation starts in 15 to 20 minutes and peaks in 20 to 40 minutes. The pupil usually returns to normal in about 4 to 8 hours, sometimes up to a day. Some of the drop drains through the tear duct into the nose and throat, where a little is absorbed, but blood levels are very low. Pressing on the inner corner of the eye reduces this.", "sidefx": "Stinging is from the drop itself. Blurred vision and light sensitivity follow directly from blocked pupil and ciliary muscles. In eyes with a narrow drainage angle, a dilated pupil can push the iris against the angle and suddenly raise eye pressure, causing angle-closure glaucoma. When drops are absorbed through the nose, especially in infants and small children, blocked muscarinic receptors in the brain and heart can cause agitation, psychotic reactions, fast heart rate or, rarely, collapse. Severe anticholinergic toxicity can be reversed with physostigmine in a monitored setting.", "fun": "Tropicamide's quick offset is why many eye clinics can dilate your eyes in the morning and let you drive home by evening.", "src": ["Mydriacyl (tropicamide) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "proparacaine": {"origin": "Eye anesthesia began in 1884, when Karl Koller, a young doctor in Vienna, showed that cocaine drops numbed the cornea so surgery could be done on an awake patient. Cocaine was addictive and damaged the corneal surface, so chemists made safer ester anesthetics, starting with procaine in 1905. Proparacaine was introduced in the 1950s as a topical eye anesthetic that stung less than earlier drugs. It is sold as Alcaine and in generics, and combined with fluorescein as Fluoracaine for measuring eye pressure. Today it is one of the most common drops in eye clinics and emergency departments for exams, foreign body removal and tonometry.", "timeline": [["1884", "Karl Koller shows cocaine drops numb the eye for surgery."], ["1905", "Alfred Einhorn's procaine is introduced as a safer ester local anesthetic."], ["1950s", "Proparacaine is introduced as a topical eye anesthetic."]], "cell": "Pain from the cornea travels along very fine nerve endings from the trigeminal nerve. To send a signal, these nerves open voltage-gated sodium channels, letting sodium rush in and carry an action potential to the brain. Proparacaine is a weak base. Its uncharged form passes through the nerve membrane. Inside the nerve it picks up a proton and the charged form binds a site inside the sodium channel pore, mostly when the channel is open or inactivated. Blocked channels cannot open, so the nerve cannot fire and pain signals stop. The cornea's nerve endings sit very close to the surface, which is why a single drop works within seconds.", "body": [["Cornea", "The cornea becomes numb within about 30 seconds, allowing tonometry, removal of foreign bodies and other procedures."], ["Conjunctiva", "The lining of the eye also numbs, with brief redness and stinging when the drop goes in."], ["Blink reflex", "With the cornea numb, the blink reflex is lost, so the eye is unprotected from dust and rubbing for about 15 to 20 minutes."], ["Corneal surface", "Repeated use slows the healing of the corneal surface cells and can break down the epithelium."]], "journey": "Proparacaine is dropped onto the eye and acts right at the surface. Numbness starts in about 20 to 30 seconds and lasts about 15 to 20 minutes. Very little reaches the blood. What is absorbed is an ester and is quickly broken down by plasma pseudocholinesterase, and the products leave in the urine. Because so little enters the body, systemic effects are very rare.", "sidefx": "Stinging and redness come from the drop itself. Loss of the blink reflex is a direct result of numbing the cornea, which is why the eye is protected afterward. Local anesthetics are toxic to corneal epithelial cells and slow their movement and repair. Used over and over, as when patients are sent home with drops, this can cause keratitis, ulcers, scarring and even corneal perforation, which is why it is meant for in-office use only. Allergic reactions, including contact dermatitis of the eyelids, are uncommon.", "fun": "Proparacaine is one of the few eye medicines that is often kept in the refrigerator, because it can darken and lose strength at room temperature over time.", "src": ["Alcaine (proparacaine) FDA label", "Koller K. 1884 report on cocaine anesthesia of the eye", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "tetracaine": {"origin": "Tetracaine is an ester local anesthetic built on the same para-aminobenzoic acid core as procaine, the first synthetic local anesthetic, introduced in 1905. Procaine was safe but weak and short-acting. In the late 1920s chemists in Germany added a butyl group to the molecule, making it more fat-soluble, more potent and longer acting. The new drug, also called amethocaine, was introduced around 1930 and was used for spinal anesthesia, topical anesthesia of the airway and the eye. Today the eye drop form is used to numb the cornea for exams and minor procedures, and other topical and spinal forms exist.", "timeline": [["1884", "Karl Koller shows cocaine numbs the eye."], ["1905", "Procaine is introduced as the first widely used synthetic local anesthetic."], ["c. 1930", "Tetracaine is introduced as a more potent ester local anesthetic."]], "cell": "Tetracaine blocks voltage-gated sodium channels in the sensory nerve endings of the cornea and conjunctiva. The uncharged drug crosses the nerve membrane, becomes charged inside the cell, and binds a site inside the channel pore when the channel is open or inactivated. With sodium unable to enter, the nerve cannot produce an action potential, so pain signals do not reach the brain. Its butyl side chain makes it much more fat-soluble than procaine, so it enters the nerve membrane more easily and binds more strongly, which explains its greater potency. As an ester of para-aminobenzoic acid, any drug that reaches the blood is broken down by plasma pseudocholinesterase.", "body": [["Cornea", "The cornea numbs within seconds, allowing tonometry, foreign body removal and other quick procedures."], ["Conjunctiva", "The lining of the eye also numbs, but tetracaine usually stings more on instillation than proparacaine."], ["Blink reflex", "Because the cornea is numb, the blink reflex is lost for about 15 to 20 minutes, so the eye can be injured without the person noticing."], ["Corneal surface", "Like all topical anesthetics, it slows healing of the corneal surface, especially with repeated use."]], "journey": "Tetracaine eye drops act right on the surface of the eye, with onset in about 10 to 20 seconds and effect for about 15 to 20 minutes. Only tiny amounts reach the blood. The absorbed drug is broken down by plasma pseudocholinesterase to para-aminobutylbenzoic acid and other products, which leave in the urine. When tetracaine is used on larger mucous surfaces such as the airway, more can be absorbed, and it is broken down more slowly than other esters.", "sidefx": "Stinging and burning are from the drug and its acidity. Loss of the blink reflex follows from corneal numbness. Repeated use is toxic to corneal epithelial cells and can lead to keratitis, scarring and opacification, which is why the drops are not sent home with patients. Allergic reactions are possible because ester anesthetics form para-aminobenzoic acid compounds that some people react to. When large amounts are absorbed from mucous membranes, it can cause local anesthetic systemic toxicity, which is treated with supportive care and lipid emulsion.", "fun": "Tetracaine is far more potent than procaine. Adding a single four-carbon chain to the molecule made the difference.", "src": ["Tetracaine hydrochloride ophthalmic solution FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "factor-viia-recombinant": {"origin": "Recombinant factor VIIa was developed to solve a hard problem in hemophilia. Some patients with hemophilia A or B form inhibitors, antibodies that destroy replacement factor VIII or IX. In the late 1970s and 1980s, the Swedish physician Ulla Hedner, working with Walter Kisiel, proposed that high levels of activated factor VII could bypass factors VIII and IX entirely. Plasma-derived factor VIIa was first used in a patient in 1983. Novo Nordisk then produced it with recombinant DNA in hamster kidney cells, and it was first given in 1988. The FDA approved NovoSeven in 1999 for bleeding in hemophilia with inhibitors. A second product, eptacog beta (Sevenfact), made in transgenic rabbits, was approved in 2020.", "timeline": [["1983", "Plasma-derived factor VIIa is first used to treat a hemophilia patient with inhibitors."], ["1988", "Recombinant factor VIIa is first given to a patient."], ["1999", "FDA approves NovoSeven for hemophilia with inhibitors."], ["2020", "FDA approves eptacog beta (Sevenfact)."]], "cell": "Clotting starts when blood vessel injury exposes tissue factor. Factor VIIa bound to tissue factor activates factors X and IX. Normally, factors VIIIa and IXa then form the tenase complex on activated platelets, which makes most of the factor Xa needed for a large thrombin burst. In hemophilia this step fails. At the high levels reached with therapy, recombinant factor VIIa does two things. It binds tissue factor at the injury. It also binds directly to the phospholipid surface of activated platelets, where it activates factor X without needing tissue factor or factors VIII and IX. Factor Xa with factor Va converts prothrombin to thrombin, and the thrombin burst turns fibrinogen into fibrin. Because activated platelets gather only where there is injury, the effect is mostly local.", "body": [["Blood at the injury site", "A local thrombin burst forms a fibrin clot that controls bleeding even when factors VIII or IX are blocked by inhibitors."], ["Platelets", "Thrombin also activates more platelets, strengthening the platelet plug."], ["Laboratory tests", "PT and INR shorten quickly, but this lab change does not prove that bleeding is controlled."], ["Arteries", "Excess thrombin generation can form clots in arteries, causing stroke or heart attack, especially in patients with vascular disease."], ["Veins and lungs", "Venous clots and pulmonary emboli can occur, particularly with use outside approved indications."]], "journey": "Recombinant factor VIIa is given as an IV bolus, so it acts within minutes. It stays mostly in the blood. It is inactivated by natural inhibitors, antithrombin and tissue factor pathway inhibitor, and cleared mainly by the liver. Its half-life is short, about two to three hours in adults and shorter in children, which is why doses are often repeated every few hours until bleeding stops.", "sidefx": "The main risk is clotting in the wrong place. Because high levels of factor VIIa generate thrombin on any activated platelet or exposed tissue factor, patients with atherosclerosis, crush injury or sepsis can form arterial or venous clots, and there is a boxed warning for thrombosis with use outside approved indications. Disseminated intravascular coagulation has been reported. Fever, headache and blood pressure changes are common. Some patients form antibodies that reduce response. There is no specific reversal agent.", "fun": "Factor VII has the shortest half-life of all the clotting factors in the body, only a few hours, which is why warfarin first lowers factor VII and raises the INR within a day or two.", "src": ["NovoSeven RT (coagulation factor VIIa, recombinant) FDA label", "Sevenfact FDA label", "Hedner U. Semin Hematol 2006"]}, "fibrinogen-concentrate": {"origin": "Fibrinogen, also called factor I, was the first clotting protein ever described; 19th century scientists noticed that blood clotted because a soluble plasma protein turned into insoluble fibrin threads. For decades the only way to replace it was cryoprecipitate or fresh frozen plasma, which vary in strength and carry transfusion risks. Fibrinogen concentrates, purified from pooled human plasma and treated to inactivate viruses, were used in Europe for years before reaching the US. The FDA approved CSL Behring's RiaSTAP in 2009 for bleeding in people with congenital fibrinogen deficiency, and Octapharma's Fibryga in 2017. It allows a known amount of fibrinogen to be given quickly without thawing plasma.", "timeline": [["1800s", "Scientists identify fibrinogen as the protein that becomes fibrin in clots."], ["1960s", "Cryoprecipitate becomes a standard source of fibrinogen."], ["2009", "FDA approves RiaSTAP for congenital fibrinogen deficiency."], ["2017", "FDA approves Fibryga."]], "cell": "Fibrinogen is a large protein made of three pairs of chains. At the final step of clotting, thrombin cuts off small pieces called fibrinopeptides A and B. The remaining fibrin monomers stick to each other end to end and side to side, forming long strands. Factor XIIIa, also activated by thrombin, then cross-links these strands into a strong, stable mesh that holds the clot together. Fibrinogen also has a second role: it bridges activated platelets by binding their glycoprotein IIb/IIIa receptors, linking platelets into a plug. Fibrinogen concentrate simply adds back this substrate, so when thrombin is made at a wound there is enough raw material to form a firm clot.", "body": [["Blood", "Plasma fibrinogen rises right away, and clot strength improves on viscoelastic tests such as TEG or ROTEM."], ["Bleeding sites", "With enough fibrinogen, clots form faster and are firmer, which helps stop bleeding in people with low fibrinogen."], ["Platelets", "More fibrinogen links platelets together more effectively through IIb/IIIa receptors."], ["Veins and arteries", "Too much fibrinogen can favor unwanted clots, such as deep vein thrombosis, pulmonary embolism and heart attack."]], "journey": "Fibrinogen concentrate is a powder mixed with sterile water and given into a vein, so the plasma level rises immediately. It stays mostly in the blood. It is used up when clots form and the rest is broken down slowly like the body's own fibrinogen. The half-life is about three days in adults, so one dose can keep levels up for several days.", "sidefx": "Clot-related problems are the main serious risk because fibrinogen is the building block of clots. Raising it too high, especially in people with other clotting risks, can contribute to venous or arterial thrombosis. Allergic reactions and anaphylaxis can occur with any plasma protein. Fever and headache are common. Because it is made from human plasma, there is a very small theoretical risk of transmitting infections, which donor screening and viral inactivation lower.", "fun": "Fibrinogen is one of the most plentiful proteins in plasma, and its level rises with inflammation, which is part of why the erythrocyte sedimentation rate goes up: fibrinogen makes red cells stack and fall faster.", "src": ["RiaSTAP (fibrinogen concentrate) FDA label", "Fibryga FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "antithrombin": {"origin": "In 1939 researchers including Kenneth Brinkhous found that heparin needed a helper protein in plasma to work, which they called heparin cofactor. That protein is antithrombin. In 1965 the Norwegian doctor Olav Egeberg described a family with frequent blood clots and low antithrombin, the first known inherited thrombophilia. This showed antithrombin is one of the body's main natural anticoagulants. A purified human plasma product, Thrombate III, was approved in the US in 1991 for people with hereditary antithrombin deficiency, to prevent or treat clots around surgery, childbirth or other high-risk times.", "timeline": [["1939", "Brinkhous and colleagues show heparin needs a plasma cofactor."], ["1965", "Egeberg describes inherited antithrombin deficiency in a Norwegian family."], ["1991", "Thrombate III is approved in the US for hereditary antithrombin deficiency."]], "cell": "Antithrombin is a serpin, a serine protease inhibitor made by the liver. It acts like a molecular trap. Its reactive loop looks like the normal target of thrombin and factor Xa. When one of these enzymes cuts the loop, antithrombin snaps into a new shape and locks the enzyme in a tight, permanent one-to-one complex. It also inhibits factors IXa, XIa and XIIa to a lesser degree. On its own the reaction is slow. Heparin and the heparan sulfate on blood vessel walls carry a special pentasaccharide sequence that binds antithrombin and changes its shape, speeding inhibition about a thousandfold. People with antithrombin deficiency make too much thrombin and form clots, and they may not respond well to heparin.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Restoring antithrombin lowers thrombin and factor Xa activity, reducing the risk of venous clots in deficient patients."], ["Response to heparin", "In people with low antithrombin, adding it back restores the effect of heparin."], ["Bleeding", "Combined with heparin, extra antithrombin can increase bleeding, so heparin may be adjusted."], ["Liver and spleen", "Antithrombin-enzyme complexes are removed by the liver and reticuloendothelial system."]], "journey": "Antithrombin is given into a vein, so the level rises at once. It stays mostly in the blood and the lining of vessels. It is used up as it forms complexes with clotting enzymes, which are then cleared by the liver. Its half-life is about two and a half to almost four days, but it is shorter during surgery, active clotting or heparin use, when more is consumed.", "sidefx": "Because antithrombin adds to the body's anticoagulant power, bleeding is a risk, especially with heparin, which speeds its action greatly. Dizziness, chest discomfort, nausea and a foul taste have been reported during infusion. Allergic reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, can occur with plasma proteins. As a plasma product, it carries a very small risk of transmitting infections. There is no specific reversal agent, although stopping heparin removes the accelerating effect.", "fun": "Heparin is not really an anticoagulant on its own. It works almost entirely by supercharging antithrombin, so heparin barely works in someone with very low antithrombin.", "src": ["Thrombate III (antithrombin III, human) FDA label", "Egeberg O. Thromb Diath Haemorrh 1965", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "hemin": {"origin": "The porphyrias are inherited disorders of heme production. In acute intermittent porphyria, a partial lack of the enzyme porphobilinogen deaminase lets toxic precursors build up during attacks, causing severe abdominal pain, nerve damage and mental changes. Researchers reasoned that if heme normally switches off its own production, giving heme might calm an attack. In 1971 Herbert Bonkowsky and colleagues reported that IV hematin lowered the precursors in patients with acute porphyria. Abbott developed the product as Panhematin, and in 1983 it became the first drug approved under the new US Orphan Drug Act. It is now made by Recordati Rare Diseases.", "timeline": [["1971", "Bonkowsky and colleagues report that IV hematin lowers porphyrin precursors in acute porphyria."], ["1983", "Panhematin becomes the first drug approved under the US Orphan Drug Act."], ["2019", "Givosiran, an RNA-based drug, is approved for acute hepatic porphyria."]], "cell": "Heme is built in eight enzyme steps. The first and rate-limiting step in the liver is made by delta-aminolevulinic acid synthase 1 (ALAS1). Normally, heme acts as a brake on ALAS1: it reduces the gene's expression, destabilizes its messenger RNA, and blocks the enzyme's transport into mitochondria. In acute intermittent porphyria, a downstream enzyme is weak. Drugs, fasting, hormones or stress raise the demand for heme, which turns up ALAS1, and the precursors delta-aminolevulinic acid (ALA) and porphobilinogen (PBG) pile up. These are thought to be toxic to nerves. Hemin is a heme analog. Taken up by liver cells, it refills the free heme pool and switches ALAS1 back down, so ALA and PBG production falls.", "body": [["Liver", "The liver makes fewer heme precursors, so urinary ALA and PBG fall within a few days."], ["Gut and abdomen", "Severe abdominal pain, caused by effects on autonomic nerves, usually eases as precursor levels fall."], ["Nerves", "Early treatment can limit the progression of motor neuropathy and weakness."], ["Brain and autonomic system", "Confusion, anxiety, fast heart rate and high blood pressure tend to settle as the attack resolves."], ["Veins", "The infusion is irritating, and phlebitis at the IV site is common."], ["Blood clotting", "Hemin can briefly prolong clotting times and lower platelets."]], "journey": "Hemin is a powder mixed with sterile water, or more often with human albumin to make it more stable, and infused into a vein. In the blood it binds hemopexin and albumin, which carry it to the liver. Liver cells take it up, where it represses ALAS1. It is broken down by heme oxygenase into biliverdin, iron and carbon monoxide. Bilirubin from biliverdin leaves in bile, and the iron joins body stores. The half-life is about 11 hours but varies.", "sidefx": "Phlebitis happens because hemin and its breakdown products irritate the vein lining, which is one reason it is mixed with albumin and given through a large vein. Hemin breakdown products can interfere with clotting factors and platelets, briefly prolonging PT and aPTT. In overdose it can cause acute kidney injury. Repeated courses can add to iron stores. Allergic reactions are uncommon. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Some historians have suggested that King George III's episodes of madness were caused by porphyria, though this idea is still debated.", "src": ["Panhematin (hemin for injection) FDA label", "Bonkowsky HL et al. PNAS 1971", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "atropine-pralidoxime-auto-injector": {"origin": "This kit pairs two antidotes with separate histories. Atropine comes from the deadly nightshade plant and was isolated in the 1830s; it blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors. Organophosphate nerve agents were discovered in Germany in 1936, when Gerhard Schrader, searching for insecticides, made tabun. These agents block acetylcholinesterase. In 1955 Irwin Wilson and Sara Ginsburg at Columbia University designed pralidoxime, an oxime that could pull the organophosphate off the enzyme and restore it. The US military used the two as separate auto-injectors in the Mark I kit. DuoDote, a single auto-injector holding both drugs, was approved by the FDA in 2006 for civilian first responders and EMS treating nerve agent or organophosphate insecticide poisoning.", "timeline": [["1830s", "Atropine is isolated from Atropa belladonna."], ["1936", "Gerhard Schrader synthesizes tabun, the first nerve agent."], ["1955", "Wilson and Ginsburg develop pralidoxime to reactivate acetylcholinesterase."], ["1980s", "Mark I kits with separate atropine and pralidoxime injectors are issued to US troops."], ["2006", "FDA approves DuoDote, a combined auto-injector."]], "cell": "Organophosphates phosphorylate the serine at the active site of acetylcholinesterase, so the enzyme can no longer break down acetylcholine. Acetylcholine builds up at muscarinic receptors (glands, smooth muscle, heart), nicotinic receptors (skeletal muscle and autonomic ganglia) and in the brain. Atropine is a competitive antagonist at muscarinic M1 to M5 receptors. It does not touch the enzyme or nicotinic receptors, but it blocks the excess acetylcholine at muscarinic sites, drying secretions, opening the airways and raising the heart rate. Pralidoxime is a quaternary oxime. It binds near the blocked active site and attacks the phosphorus, pulling the phosphoryl group off the serine and freeing the enzyme. This only works before 'aging', when the bound agent loses a chemical group and becomes permanent. Restored enzyme helps most at nicotinic neuromuscular junctions, easing weakness.", "body": [["Airways and lungs", "Atropine dries the flood of airway secretions and relaxes bronchospasm, which are the main causes of death in nerve agent poisoning."], ["Heart", "Atropine reverses the slow heart rate caused by excess vagal acetylcholine and usually causes a faster rate."], ["Glands", "Sweating, drooling, tearing and runny nose dry up as muscarinic receptors are blocked."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Pralidoxime restores the enzyme at nerve-muscle junctions, reducing twitching, weakness and paralysis of breathing muscles."], ["Gut and bladder", "Atropine reduces cramps, vomiting, diarrhea and involuntary urination."], ["Brain", "Atropine enters the brain and can help some central effects, but pralidoxime is charged and enters poorly, so seizures are treated with benzodiazepines."]], "journey": "DuoDote is injected into the thigh muscle through clothing. Both drugs are absorbed from muscle within minutes. Atropine spreads widely, crosses into the brain and is partly broken down by the liver, with much excreted unchanged in urine; its half-life is about two to four hours. Pralidoxime stays mostly outside the brain because it carries a permanent positive charge. It is barely metabolized and leaves through the kidneys mostly unchanged, with a half-life of about one to two and a half hours.", "sidefx": "Side effects come from blocking muscarinic receptors beyond what is needed: dry mouth, blurred vision, flushing, fast heart rate, urinary retention and, with excess, confusion, delirium and high body temperature because the person cannot sweat. In someone who has not been poisoned, atropine toxicity can be dangerous, especially in heat. Pralidoxime can cause dizziness, blurred vision and, given quickly, muscle weakness or high blood pressure. Severe anticholinergic toxicity is managed supportively.", "fun": "The endpoint used for atropine in organophosphate poisoning is drying of lung secretions, not the size of the pupils, because pupils can stay pinpoint even after enough atropine.", "src": ["DuoDote (atropine and pralidoxime chloride) FDA label", "Wilson IB, Ginsburg S. Biochim Biophys Acta 1955", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]}, "sodium-nitrite": {"origin": "Sodium nitrite as a cyanide antidote came out of animal studies in the early 1930s. In 1933 K. K. Chen, Charles Rose and George Clowes at Eli Lilly reported that dogs given lethal cyanide survived when treated with nitrites followed by sodium thiosulfate. Their idea was to create methemoglobin on purpose, because methemoglobin grabs cyanide more tightly than the cell's own enzymes do. The combination of amyl nitrite, sodium nitrite and sodium thiosulfate became the classic cyanide antidote kit for decades. For many years it was sold without a modern FDA label. Formal FDA approval came in the early 2010s, both as Nithiodote (sodium nitrite with sodium thiosulfate) and as separate sodium nitrite injection. Hydroxocobalamin (Cyanokit), approved in 2006, is now often preferred, especially for smoke inhalation.", "timeline": [["1933", "Chen, Rose and Clowes at Eli Lilly show nitrite plus thiosulfate protects dogs from cyanide."], ["1930s", "The nitrite-thiosulfate cyanide antidote kit comes into clinical use."], ["2006", "Hydroxocobalamin (Cyanokit) is approved as an alternative cyanide antidote."], ["2010s", "Sodium nitrite receives formal FDA approval, alone and in Nithiodote."]], "cell": "Cyanide kills by binding the ferric iron in cytochrome c oxidase (complex IV), the last enzyme of the mitochondrial electron transport chain. Cells can no longer use oxygen to make ATP, even though plenty of oxygen is in the blood. They switch to anaerobic metabolism, and lactic acid builds up. Sodium nitrite oxidizes the iron in hemoglobin from the ferrous (Fe2+) to the ferric (Fe3+) state, forming methemoglobin. Methemoglobin's ferric iron has a higher affinity for cyanide than cytochrome oxidase does, so it pulls cyanide out of the mitochondria, forming cyanmethemoglobin. Freed cytochrome oxidase restarts oxidative phosphorylation. Nitrite also produces nitric oxide, which may help through other effects. The cyanide in cyanmethemoglobin is then removed with sodium thiosulfate.", "body": [["Blood", "A portion of hemoglobin becomes methemoglobin, which cannot carry oxygen and gives the skin a bluish color."], ["Mitochondria", "Cytochrome oxidase is freed from cyanide, so cells can use oxygen again and lactic acidosis starts to improve."], ["Blood vessels", "Nitrite and nitric oxide relax vascular smooth muscle, which lowers blood pressure and can cause flushing and headache."], ["Heart", "Low blood pressure can cause a fast heart rate, dizziness or fainting."], ["Red cells in G6PD deficiency", "Oxidant stress can break down red cells in people with G6PD deficiency."]], "journey": "Sodium nitrite is given slowly into a vein, so it reaches red cells within minutes. Methemoglobin rises over the next 30 to 60 minutes. Nitrite has a half-life of about 45 minutes. Part is converted to other nitrogen compounds and a large share is excreted unchanged in the urine. Red cell enzymes (methemoglobin reductase) slowly convert methemoglobin back to normal hemoglobin over hours.", "sidefx": "The main dangers come from the same actions that make it work. Methemoglobin cannot carry oxygen, so too much worsens tissue hypoxia, which is a major concern in smoke inhalation victims who already have carbon monoxide poisoning. That is why hydroxocobalamin is preferred in fires. Vasodilation causes low blood pressure, headache and fainting. In G6PD deficiency, the oxidant stress can cause hemolysis. Excess methemoglobinemia can be treated with methylene blue, though this also releases cyanide, so it is used with care.", "fun": "The nitrite strategy turns a poisoning into a treatment: doctors deliberately cause one condition, methemoglobinemia, to cure another, cyanide poisoning.", "src": ["Sodium Nitrite Injection FDA label", "Nithiodote FDA label", "Chen KK, Rose CL, Clowes GHA. 1933 cyanide antidote studies"]}, "sodium-thiosulfate": {"origin": "Sodium thiosulfate has been known for centuries as a simple sulfur salt, used in photography as 'hypo' to fix images. In the 1890s and early 1900s scientists noticed that the body changes cyanide into thiocyanate, and in the early 1930s the liver enzyme that does this was described and named rhodanese. In 1933 K. K. Chen and colleagues at Eli Lilly showed that adding sodium thiosulfate to nitrite treatment rescued dogs from lethal cyanide, creating the classic cyanide antidote kit. It received formal FDA approval in the early 2010s, alone and as part of Nithiodote. In 2022 a separate product, Pedmark, was approved to lower the risk of hearing loss from cisplatin chemotherapy in children with certain solid tumors.", "timeline": [["1930s", "The enzyme rhodanese, which turns cyanide into thiocyanate, is described."], ["1933", "Chen and colleagues show thiosulfate plus nitrite protects dogs from cyanide."], ["2010s", "Sodium thiosulfate receives formal FDA approval for cyanide poisoning."], ["2022", "FDA approves Pedmark to reduce cisplatin-related hearing loss in children."]], "cell": "The body has a natural cyanide disposal system. Rhodanese (thiosulfate sulfurtransferase), an enzyme found mainly in mitochondria of the liver and kidney, moves a sulfur atom from a donor onto cyanide, making thiocyanate. Thiocyanate is much less toxic and leaves in the urine. Normally the supply of sulfur donors is small, so the pathway is quickly overwhelmed. Sodium thiosulfate supplies large amounts of sulfur for rhodanese, speeding cyanide removal many times over. The reaction is fairly slow to start, which is why it is paired with faster agents such as nitrite or hydroxocobalamin. In cisplatin protection, thiosulfate is thought to bind and inactivate platinum in the inner ear, where it reaches the hair cells.", "body": [["Mitochondria", "As cyanide is converted to thiocyanate, cytochrome oxidase is freed and cells can use oxygen again."], ["Blood chemistry", "Lactic acidosis gradually improves as aerobic metabolism resumes."], ["Kidneys", "Thiosulfate and thiocyanate are cleared by the kidneys; in kidney failure thiocyanate can build up."], ["Blood pressure", "Thiosulfate itself can lower blood pressure, and the drop is more marked when given with nitrite."], ["Inner ear", "In children receiving cisplatin, it reduces damage to cochlear hair cells and lowers the rate of hearing loss."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and a salty taste are common during infusion."]], "journey": "Sodium thiosulfate is given into a vein. It spreads through the fluid outside cells but enters cells poorly, which limits how fast it works. The kidneys remove it quickly, and its half-life is under an hour. The thiocyanate it forms with cyanide lasts much longer, especially if the kidneys are weak. Some thiosulfate is oxidized to sulfate. For cisplatin protection, timing after the cisplatin dose is important so it does not reduce the anticancer effect.", "sidefx": "Nausea, vomiting and a salty taste come from the large salt load. Low blood pressure can happen, mostly when given with sodium nitrite. Thiocyanate, the end product, can build up in kidney failure and cause confusion, weakness and psychosis. The sodium load can matter in people with heart failure. With Pedmark, high sodium and low potassium can occur. There is no specific antidote for thiosulfate itself; thiocyanate can be removed by dialysis.", "fun": "The same chemical photographers used to fix images, called hypo, also fixes cyanide into a harmless form in the body.", "src": ["Sodium Thiosulfate Injection FDA label", "Pedmark (sodium thiosulfate) FDA label", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]}, "amyl-nitrite": {"origin": "Amyl nitrite was first made in 1844 by the French chemist Antoine Jerome Balard. Later researchers noticed that breathing its vapor caused flushing and a racing pulse. In 1867 the Scottish physician Thomas Lauder Brunton used inhaled amyl nitrite to relieve angina, reasoning that it lowered pressure in the vessels. This was one of the first drug treatments for angina, before nitroglycerin. In 1933 it became part of the cyanide antidote kit developed at Eli Lilly, used first as a quick bridge before IV sodium nitrite. It came in fragile glass capsules, called pearls, that were crushed and inhaled. Its use in cyanide kits has largely ended, and it remains available as a prescription inhalant. Related alkyl nitrites are misused as 'poppers'.", "timeline": [["1844", "Antoine Jerome Balard first synthesizes amyl nitrite."], ["1867", "Thomas Lauder Brunton uses inhaled amyl nitrite to relieve angina."], ["1933", "Amyl nitrite becomes part of the nitrite-thiosulfate cyanide antidote kit."], ["1970s", "Recreational misuse of alkyl nitrites as poppers becomes widespread."]], "cell": "Amyl nitrite is an organic nitrite that releases nitric oxide in vascular smooth muscle. Nitric oxide activates soluble guanylate cyclase, which converts GTP to cyclic GMP. Cyclic GMP activates protein kinase G, which lowers calcium inside the cell and activates myosin light chain phosphatase. The muscle relaxes. Veins dilate more than arteries, so blood pools in the veins and less returns to the heart (lower preload), which lowers the heart's oxygen demand and relieves angina. Nitrite also oxidizes hemoglobin iron to the ferric state, forming methemoglobin, which binds cyanide as cyanmethemoglobin. Inhaled amyl nitrite produces only modest methemoglobin, which is why it was used only as a bridge in cyanide poisoning.", "body": [["Veins", "Veins widen and blood pools, reducing the work of the heart, which explains the relief of angina."], ["Arteries", "Arteries in the head and skin widen, causing flushing, a throbbing headache and a fall in blood pressure."], ["Heart", "The drop in blood pressure triggers a reflex fast heart rate and pounding pulse."], ["Brain", "Low blood pressure can cause dizziness or fainting, especially when standing."], ["Blood", "Some hemoglobin becomes methemoglobin, which cannot carry oxygen."]], "journey": "Amyl nitrite is a volatile liquid. When the capsule is crushed, the vapor is breathed in and absorbed through the lungs within seconds. The effect starts within about 30 seconds and lasts only about three to five minutes. It is rapidly broken down by hydrolysis into isoamyl alcohol and nitrite, and the products leave in the urine. Swallowing it is much more dangerous than inhaling it.", "sidefx": "Headache, flushing, dizziness and fast heart rate all come from sudden vasodilation and the body's reflex response. Low blood pressure can cause fainting. Combined with PDE-5 inhibitors such as sildenafil, which also raise cyclic GMP, it can cause a severe and dangerous drop in blood pressure. Large amounts, especially if swallowed, can cause life-threatening methemoglobinemia, which is treated with methylene blue. The vapor is highly flammable.", "fun": "The name poppers comes from the popping sound the glass ampules made when they were crushed between the fingers.", "src": ["Amyl Nitrite Inhalant FDA label", "Brunton TL. Lancet 1867", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "prussian-blue": {"origin": "Prussian blue was one of the first synthetic pigments. It was made by accident around 1706 in Berlin by the color maker Johann Jacob Diesbach, and painters soon used its deep blue. Much later, scientists found that its crystal lattice could trap cesium and thallium ions. Beginning in the 1960s, it was studied as a treatment for thallium poisoning and radioactive cesium. Its best known use came in 1987 in Goiania, Brazil, when a stolen radiotherapy source spread cesium-137 and Prussian blue was given to many contaminated people. After the 2001 terrorist attacks, the FDA reviewed the evidence and in 2003 approved Radiogardase (insoluble Prussian blue capsules) for internal contamination with radioactive cesium or with thallium. It is kept in the Strategic National Stockpile.", "timeline": [["c. 1706", "Johann Jacob Diesbach makes Prussian blue pigment in Berlin."], ["1960s", "Prussian blue is studied for removing thallium and cesium from the body."], ["1987", "Prussian blue is used for many people after the cesium-137 accident in Goiania, Brazil."], ["2003", "FDA approves Radiogardase for cesium and thallium contamination."]], "cell": "Prussian blue is insoluble ferric hexacyanoferrate. It is not absorbed. Its crystal is a cube-shaped lattice with spaces that fit monovalent ions of the right size. Cesium and thallium ions (similar in size to potassium) fit into these spaces and are held tightly, while other ions are released in exchange. Normally, cesium and thallium in the body are secreted into the gut in bile and intestinal fluid and then reabsorbed, a recycling loop called enterohepatic and enteroenteric circulation. Prussian blue sits in the gut lumen and grabs these ions as they enter, breaking the loop. The bound metal leaves in the stool. This shortens the time cesium-137 stays in the body from about 110 days to about 30 days, and thallium from about eight days to three.", "body": [["Gut", "It acts entirely inside the intestine, binding cesium and thallium before they can be reabsorbed."], ["Whole body radiation", "Faster removal of cesium-137 lowers the total internal radiation dose to tissues and bone marrow."], ["Nerves and hair (thallium)", "Lowering thallium levels limits nerve damage, hair loss and other toxicity."], ["Stool", "Stools turn blue, which is harmless and expected."], ["Bowel habits", "Constipation is common and slows the drug's work, so a fiber-rich diet or laxatives may be used."], ["Electrolytes", "It can bind some potassium, and low potassium has been reported."]], "journey": "Prussian blue is taken by mouth as capsules. It is not absorbed through the gut wall, is not metabolized and passes through the intestines unchanged. It works for as long as it is in the gut, binding cesium and thallium secreted into the bowel, and leaves in the stool with the bound metal. Treatment lasts at least 30 days and is guided by measuring radioactivity in the body or urine and stool.", "sidefx": "Because it stays in the gut, side effects are mostly local. Constipation and stomach upset are common, and blue stool is expected. Because the lattice can also exchange potassium, low potassium and other electrolyte shifts can occur, especially with long use. Patients who handle their stool, including caregivers of contaminated people, may be exposed to radioactive material in it. There is no antidote, and none is needed.", "fun": "The same pigment is the blue in many 18th and 19th century paintings, including Hokusai's famous print The Great Wave off Kanagawa.", "src": ["Radiogardase (Prussian blue insoluble capsules) FDA label", "REAC/TS radiation emergency guidance", "IAEA report on the Goiania radiological accident"]}, "calcium-disodium-edta": {"origin": "EDTA (ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid) was first made in Germany in the 1930s by the chemist Ferdinand Munz, who wanted a substitute for citric acid to soften water for the textile industry. Its claw-like grip on metal ions caught the interest of doctors. In the early 1950s, physicians treating lead poisoning, including workers and children, found that EDTA could pull lead into the urine. Giving plain disodium EDTA also stripped calcium from the blood and could cause fatal hypocalcemia, so the calcium salt, calcium disodium EDTA, was developed. It became a standard treatment for lead poisoning, sold as Calcium Disodium Versenate. The brand has been discontinued, and generic edetate calcium disodium injection remains available.", "timeline": [["1930s", "Ferdinand Munz first synthesizes EDTA in Germany."], ["1950s", "Calcium disodium EDTA comes into use for lead poisoning."], ["2000s", "Deaths from mix-ups with edetate disodium lead to FDA safety warnings."]], "cell": "EDTA has four acid groups and two nitrogen atoms that wrap around a metal ion like a cage. Each metal binds with a different strength. Calcium disodium EDTA comes already loaded with calcium. Lead binds EDTA much more tightly than calcium, so when the drug meets lead, the lead takes calcium's place. The result is a stable, water-soluble lead chelate that stays in the fluid outside cells. The kidneys filter it out into the urine. Because the drug already carries calcium, it does not pull calcium from the blood. EDTA mostly stays outside cells, so it removes lead from the blood and soft tissues and from bone surfaces, rather than from deep inside cells. It also binds zinc, iron, manganese and copper, which explains some side effects.", "body": [["Blood", "Blood lead falls during treatment, but it often rebounds days later as lead shifts out of bone, so repeat courses may be needed."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys carry the chelated lead out in urine. High lead loads and the drug can injure the tubules, causing protein and blood in the urine."], ["Bone", "Lead stored in bone is slowly mobilized into the blood, which is why blood levels rebound."], ["Brain", "In lead encephalopathy, moving lead can briefly worsen brain swelling, so it is usually given after dimercaprol, which is started first."], ["Trace minerals", "Zinc and other trace metals are also removed, and zinc levels can fall."], ["Injection site", "IM injection is painful, so a local anesthetic is often mixed in."]], "journey": "Calcium disodium EDTA is given into a vein or into muscle. It is poorly absorbed by mouth, so oral use is not effective. It stays mainly in the fluid outside cells. It is not metabolized. The kidneys filter it out quickly, with a half-life of about 20 to 60 minutes after IV dosing and almost all gone within a day. Lead excretion in urine rises within an hour of starting treatment.", "sidefx": "Kidney damage is the main dose-limiting toxicity. The kidneys handle a large load of chelated lead and drug, and tubular cells can be injured, so kidney function and urine are checked. Loss of zinc and other trace metals comes from the drug's broad binding. In lead encephalopathy, shifting lead can raise brain pressure, which is why dimercaprol is given first. The most dangerous error is confusing it with edetate disodium, a different salt that binds calcium and caused deaths from sudden hypocalcemia and cardiac arrest. Edetate disodium is no longer marketed in the US.", "fun": "EDTA is also in many everyday products, from mayonnaise and shampoo to the purple-top blood tubes in labs, where it keeps blood from clotting by binding calcium.", "src": ["Edetate calcium disodium injection FDA label", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "pentetate-calcium-trisodium": {"origin": "Pentetate, or DTPA (diethylenetriaminepentaacetic acid), is a larger cousin of EDTA with a stronger grip on heavy metals. It was developed as a chelating agent in the mid-20th century. In the 1950s and 1960s, scientists in the nuclear field studied it for removing plutonium and other transuranic elements from workers who had been internally contaminated. For decades it was used in the US under an investigational protocol run through the government. After the 2001 terrorist attacks raised concern about radiological 'dirty bombs', the FDA approved Ca-DTPA and Zn-DTPA (made by Hameln Pharma) in 2004 for internal contamination with plutonium, americium or curium. Both are stocked in the Strategic National Stockpile.", "timeline": [["1950s", "DTPA is developed as a chelating agent."], ["1960s", "DTPA is used to remove plutonium from contaminated nuclear workers under investigational use."], ["2004", "FDA approves Ca-DTPA for plutonium, americium and curium contamination."]], "cell": "DTPA has five acid groups and three nitrogen atoms that wrap tightly around a metal ion. Ca-DTPA comes loaded with calcium. Transuranic elements such as plutonium, americium and curium bind DTPA far more strongly than calcium does, so they replace it. The result is a stable, water-soluble chelate that the kidneys filter out into the urine. DTPA stays mostly in the blood and fluid outside cells. It works best on metal that is still circulating, before it settles into bone and liver, where it is held tightly and is hard to reach. It does not bind uranium or neptunium well. In the first 24 hours, Ca-DTPA removes about ten times more plutonium than Zn-DTPA, which is why it is preferred as the first dose when available.", "body": [["Blood and tissue fluid", "Circulating plutonium, americium and curium are captured and carried out in urine, lowering the long-term radiation dose."], ["Bone and liver", "Removing metal before it deposits protects bone surfaces and liver cells from years of radiation exposure."], ["Trace minerals", "Ca-DTPA also strips zinc, magnesium and manganese from the body, which is why later doses switch to Zn-DTPA."], ["Lungs", "When given by nebulizer for inhaled contamination, it can trigger cough and wheezing, especially in people with asthma."], ["Pregnancy", "Zinc depletion from Ca-DTPA can harm a developing fetus, so Zn-DTPA is preferred in pregnancy."]], "journey": "Ca-DTPA is given into a vein, or breathed in from a nebulizer if contamination was by inhalation. It spreads through the fluid outside cells and does not enter cells much. It is not metabolized. The kidneys filter it out quickly, with a half-life of about 90 minutes, and almost all of a dose is gone within 24 hours, carrying the bound metal with it. Urine and stool are radioactive during treatment.", "sidefx": "The main problem is loss of essential trace metals. Because DTPA binds zinc, magnesium and manganese, repeated Ca-DTPA can cause deficiencies, so it is used for the first dose and then replaced by Zn-DTPA. Zinc depletion can harm a fetus, explaining the teratogenicity warning. Nebulized use can cause bronchospasm. Nausea, headache, a metallic taste and lightheadedness are reported. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The patient's urine after DTPA treatment becomes the main way doctors measure how much radioactive metal was removed, and caregivers must handle it as radioactive waste.", "src": ["Pentetate calcium trisodium injection (Ca-DTPA) FDA label", "REAC/TS radiation emergency guidance", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]}, "pentetate-zinc-trisodium": {"origin": "Zn-DTPA is the zinc form of pentetate, the same chelator as Ca-DTPA but loaded with zinc instead of calcium. DTPA was studied in the 1950s and 1960s for removing plutonium and similar elements from contaminated nuclear workers. Researchers found that the calcium form worked faster in the first day, but repeated doses stripped the body of zinc and other trace metals. Loading the molecule with zinc solved that problem for longer treatment. Both forms were used under an investigational protocol for decades. In 2004 the FDA approved Zn-DTPA and Ca-DTPA, made by Hameln Pharma, for internal contamination with plutonium, americium or curium, as part of preparations for radiological emergencies.", "timeline": [["1950s", "DTPA is developed as a chelating agent."], ["1960s", "DTPA is used under investigational protocols for plutonium contamination."], ["2004", "FDA approves Zn-DTPA for plutonium, americium and curium contamination."]], "cell": "Like Ca-DTPA, Zn-DTPA wraps tightly around metal ions with its five acid groups and three nitrogens. The zinc in Zn-DTPA is bound less tightly than plutonium, americium or curium would be, so these transuranic elements displace the zinc and form stable, water-soluble chelates. The kidneys filter out the chelates in urine. Because the molecule already carries zinc, it does not pull much zinc or manganese out of the body. Zinc is held more tightly than calcium, so the exchange is a bit slower, and Zn-DTPA removes less plutonium than Ca-DTPA in the first 24 hours. After the first day, the two work about equally well, so Zn-DTPA is used for continued treatment and as the first choice in pregnancy.", "body": [["Blood and tissue fluid", "It captures circulating transuranic elements and moves them into urine, lowering long-term radiation dose."], ["Bone and liver", "Removing metal before it settles in bone and liver protects these tissues from long-term radiation."], ["Trace minerals", "Because it carries its own zinc, it causes little net loss of zinc and manganese, making it safer for longer courses."], ["Lungs", "Nebulized use for inhaled contamination can cause cough and wheezing, especially with asthma."], ["Pregnancy", "It is preferred over Ca-DTPA in pregnancy because it does not deplete the zinc a fetus needs."]], "journey": "Zn-DTPA is given into a vein, or by nebulizer for inhaled contamination. It stays in the fluid outside cells. It is not metabolized. The kidneys filter it out quickly, with a half-life of about 90 minutes, and nearly all of a dose is cleared in a day, taking the bound metal with it. Because effects depend on catching circulating metal, the earlier it is started the better.", "sidefx": "Side effects are generally mild: nausea, headache, lightheadedness and a metallic taste. Because the zinc salt does not strip trace metals, mineral deficiency is much less of a problem than with Ca-DTPA. Nebulized use can cause bronchospasm in people with asthma. Urine and stool become radioactive and need careful handling. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Swapping a single metal atom, calcium for zinc, turns the same drug from a fast first-day treatment into a safer long-term one.", "src": ["Pentetate zinc trisodium injection (Zn-DTPA) FDA label", "REAC/TS radiation emergency guidance", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]}, "lipid-emulsion": {"origin": "Giving fat into a vein was dangerous for decades because early emulsions caused fever, clotting problems and organ damage. In the early 1960s the Swedish physician and nutrition researcher Arvid Wretlind developed Intralipid, a stable emulsion of soybean oil with egg phospholipid and glycerin. It allowed patients who could not eat to receive essential fatty acids and calories by vein. It reached the US market in the 1970s. A new use came from anesthesia. In 1998 Guy Weinberg and colleagues showed that lipid emulsion rescued rats from bupivacaine overdose. In 2006 the first human case reports described patients in cardiac arrest from local anesthetic toxicity who recovered after lipid infusion. Its use for this purpose is off-label but is part of anesthesia society guidelines.", "timeline": [["1961", "Arvid Wretlind develops Intralipid in Sweden."], ["1970s", "Intralipid becomes available in the US for parenteral nutrition."], ["1998", "Weinberg and colleagues show lipid emulsion reverses bupivacaine toxicity in rats."], ["2006", "First human case reports of lipid rescue in local anesthetic cardiac arrest are published."]], "cell": "As nutrition, the emulsion's tiny fat droplets behave like natural chylomicrons. Lipoprotein lipase on blood vessel walls breaks the triglycerides into free fatty acids and glycerol. Cells burn the fatty acids for energy or store them, and the emulsion supplies essential fatty acids such as linoleic and alpha-linolenic acid. In poisoning, several mechanisms are proposed. The lipid sink idea is that a large fat phase in the blood soaks up fat-soluble drugs like bupivacaine, pulling them out of heart and brain tissue. The lipid shuttle idea is that it carries the drug to the liver and muscle for storage or breakdown. Fatty acids may also directly fuel heart muscle, whose energy use is blocked by bupivacaine, and may influence calcium handling, improving contractility.", "body": [["Heart", "In local anesthetic toxicity, it can improve contractions and restore circulation, often within minutes."], ["Blood fats", "Plasma triglycerides rise sharply, which can interfere with some lab tests."], ["Nutrition", "As part of parenteral nutrition, it provides calories and essential fatty acids, preventing fatty acid deficiency."], ["Liver", "Long-term use, especially in infants, can cause fatty liver and cholestasis."], ["Pancreas", "Very high triglycerides can trigger pancreatitis."], ["Lungs", "Fat overload can affect lung gas exchange and, rarely, cause an ARDS-like picture."]], "journey": "Lipid emulsion is given into a vein, as a bolus and infusion for toxicity or as a slower infusion for nutrition. The droplets circulate like chylomicrons. Lipoprotein lipase breaks them down into fatty acids and glycerol, which are burned for energy in many tissues or stored in fat. Some droplets are taken up by the liver and spleen. Triglycerides clear from the blood with a half-time of about 30 to 60 minutes when the body's clearance is not overwhelmed.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from too much fat in the blood. Very high triglycerides can cause pancreatitis, interfere with lab tests and, with large doses, cause fat overload syndrome with fever, liver enlargement, low platelets and lung problems. Premature infants clear fat slowly, and deaths have been reported from fat buildup in their lungs. Allergic reactions can occur in people allergic to egg or soy. Because it is a rich nutrient, infected IV lines are a concern. There is no antidote; the infusion is stopped.", "fun": "Intralipid looks like milk for the same reason milk is white: billions of tiny fat droplets scatter light.", "src": ["Intralipid (lipid injectable emulsion) FDA label", "Weinberg GL et al. Anesthesiology 1998", "ASRA Practice Advisory on Local Anesthetic Systemic Toxicity"]}, "remifentanil": {"origin": "Remifentanil was designed on purpose to wear off fast. In the late 1980s and early 1990s chemists at Glaxo set out to build an opioid as potent as fentanyl but with an ester side chain that blood enzymes would cut quickly. The idea, called soft drug design, was that the drug would be broken down so fast that it would not build up no matter how long it was infused. The result was remifentanil, approved by the FDA in 1996 as Ultiva. It changed anesthesia practice by letting anesthesiologists give deep opioid analgesia during surgery and have patients wake up within minutes after the infusion stopped.", "timeline": [["1960", "Paul Janssen's team synthesizes fentanyl, the parent of the anilidopiperidine opioids."], ["1990s", "Glaxo chemists design remifentanil with an ester linkage for rapid breakdown."], ["1996", "FDA approves remifentanil (Ultiva)."]], "cell": "Remifentanil is a selective full agonist at the mu-opioid receptor, a Gi/o-coupled receptor. When it binds, the G protein inhibits adenylyl cyclase, lowering cyclic AMP. The G protein beta-gamma subunits open potassium channels (GIRK), which hyperpolarize neurons, and close voltage-gated calcium channels at nerve terminals, which reduces release of pain transmitters like glutamate and substance P. In the spinal cord dorsal horn and in brain areas such as the periaqueductal gray, this blocks pain signals. In the brainstem it lowers the drive to breathe. Its key feature is the methyl ester side chain, which nonspecific esterases in blood and tissues (not pseudocholinesterase) quickly hydrolyze to a nearly inactive carboxylic acid.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Strong pain relief during surgery, which lets less of other anesthetics be used."], ["Breathing", "Mu receptors in the brainstem reduce the response to carbon dioxide, so breathing slows or stops at higher levels."], ["Heart", "Increased vagal tone causes slow heart rate, and blood pressure often falls."], ["Chest wall muscles", "Fast boluses can cause muscle rigidity, making ventilation difficult."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are common after anesthesia."], ["Pain after surgery", "Because it wears off in minutes, patients need a longer-acting pain plan before it stops. Some develop heightened pain sensitivity after high-dose use."]], "journey": "Remifentanil is given only by IV infusion or bolus. It reaches the brain in about a minute and acts quickly. Esterases in blood and tissues break it down so fast that its context-sensitive half-time is about three to four minutes, whether it has been running for one hour or ten. This organ-independent breakdown means kidney or liver failure barely changes its offset. The inactive acid metabolite is excreted by the kidneys. Effects fade within about five to ten minutes after stopping.", "sidefx": "Respiratory depression and apnea come straight from mu-receptor action on brainstem breathing centers. Slow heart rate and low blood pressure come from vagal stimulation and reduced sympathetic tone. Chest wall rigidity occurs with rapid boluses. Because it wears off so fast, patients can wake suddenly in severe pain if other analgesics have not been given. Naloxone reverses its effects, but its short duration often makes that unnecessary once the infusion stops. It contains glycine, so it is not given into the spine.", "fun": "Remifentanil is one of the clearest examples of soft drug design: a drug built from the start to be inactivated quickly and predictably by the body.", "src": ["Ultiva (remifentanil) FDA label", "Miller's Anesthesia", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "sufentanil": {"origin": "Sufentanil came from the lab of Paul Janssen at Janssen Pharmaceutica in Belgium, which created fentanyl in 1960 and then a series of related opioids. Sufentanil was synthesized in the 1970s as a thienyl analog of fentanyl and proved to be several times more potent, with very stable effects on the heart. The FDA approved it for IV and epidural use as Sufenta in 1984. It became popular in heart surgery and in epidural pain relief during labor. In 2018 the FDA approved a small sublingual tablet, Dsuvia, for acute pain in medically supervised settings, a decision that drew debate because of the potency of the drug.", "timeline": [["1960", "Paul Janssen's team synthesizes fentanyl."], ["1970s", "Sufentanil is synthesized at Janssen Pharmaceutica."], ["1984", "FDA approves sufentanil (Sufenta) injection."], ["2018", "FDA approves sublingual sufentanil tablet (Dsuvia) for supervised settings."]], "cell": "Sufentanil is a highly selective full agonist at mu-opioid receptors. Binding activates Gi/o proteins, which inhibit adenylyl cyclase and lower cyclic AMP. The beta-gamma subunits open GIRK potassium channels, hyperpolarizing neurons, and close voltage-gated calcium channels at presynaptic terminals, reducing release of glutamate and substance P from pain fibers. In the spinal cord, brainstem and higher brain centers, this blocks the transmission and perception of pain. Sufentanil binds mu receptors more tightly than fentanyl and is very fat-soluble, so it reaches the brain and spinal cord quickly. It causes very little histamine release, which helps keep blood pressure stable.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Profound pain relief and sedation, used in anesthesia and in epidurals during labor."], ["Breathing", "Mu receptors in the brainstem reduce the drive to breathe, causing slow breathing or apnea at higher levels."], ["Heart", "Increased vagal tone slows the heart rate. Because it releases little histamine, blood pressure is generally stable."], ["Chest wall muscles", "Fast IV doses can cause muscle rigidity that makes ventilation difficult."], ["Skin", "Itching is common, especially with epidural use, from mu receptors in the spinal cord."], ["Newborns", "When used in labor, it can cross the placenta and depress the baby's breathing."]], "journey": "Sufentanil is given into a vein, into the epidural space or under the tongue. Because it is very fat-soluble, it enters the brain within minutes and then redistributes into fat and muscle, which ends the effect of a single dose in about 30 to 60 minutes. It is about 92 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down through CYP3A4, by N-dealkylation and O-demethylation, and metabolites leave in urine and stool. The elimination half-life is about two and a half to three hours.", "sidefx": "Respiratory depression is the main danger, caused by mu-receptor action on the brainstem, and it is worse with other sedatives. Slow heart rate comes from vagal stimulation. Chest wall rigidity occurs with rapid boluses. Itching and urinary retention are especially common with epidural use. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors can raise its levels and prolong breathing suppression. Naloxone reverses its effects, though repeated naloxone may be needed because sufentanil can outlast it.", "fun": "Sufentanil is so potent that the Dsuvia tablet is only about 3 millimeters across.", "src": ["Sufenta (sufentanil citrate) FDA label", "Dsuvia (sufentanil sublingual tablet) FDA label", "Miller's Anesthesia"]}, "nalbuphine": {"origin": "Nalbuphine came from a long search for a strong painkiller with less addiction and less breathing suppression than morphine. Chemists built it from the morphine-like phenanthrene skeleton, related to oxymorphone and naloxone, at Endo Laboratories in the 1960s. The goal was a mixed agonist-antagonist: a drug that activates kappa-opioid receptors for pain relief while blocking mu receptors, which drive euphoria and respiratory depression. Nalbuphine was approved in the US in 1979 as Nubain. It is not a federally controlled substance. The Nubain brand has been discontinued, and generic injection remains, used for moderate to severe pain, in labor, and to reverse itching or breathing suppression from mu opioids.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Nalbuphine is developed at Endo Laboratories."], ["1979", "FDA approves nalbuphine (Nubain) injection."]], "cell": "Nalbuphine acts on two opioid receptors in opposite ways. At kappa-opioid receptors, it is an agonist. Kappa receptors are Gi/o-coupled; activation lowers cyclic AMP, opens potassium channels and closes calcium channels in pain neurons of the spinal cord and brain, producing pain relief and sedation. At mu-opioid receptors it is an antagonist or very weak partial agonist. It binds mu receptors but barely activates them. This creates a ceiling: above a certain level, more drug does not further slow breathing, because the mu receptors that control breathing are not fully activated. In a person taking a full mu agonist such as morphine or fentanyl, nalbuphine displaces it from mu receptors, which can reverse breathing suppression and itching or cause withdrawal in someone dependent on opioids.", "body": [["Spinal cord and brain", "Kappa activation relieves moderate to severe pain, though it may be less effective for very severe pain than full mu agonists."], ["Breathing", "Breathing slows but shows a ceiling effect, so it is less likely than full mu agonists to cause severe respiratory depression when used alone."], ["Sedation", "Sleepiness is the most common side effect, from kappa action in the brain."], ["Mood and perception", "Kappa activation can cause dysphoria, anxiety or hallucinations, especially at higher levels."], ["Heart and blood pressure", "It has little effect on heart rate or blood pressure, unlike some other mixed agonist-antagonists."], ["Skin", "Its mu-blocking effect can relieve itching caused by spinal or epidural morphine."]], "journey": "Nalbuphine is given into a vein, muscle or under the skin. By vein it starts working in two to three minutes; by muscle or skin in under 15 minutes. It is not used by mouth because the liver destroys most of it on first pass. It is about half bound to plasma proteins and crosses the placenta. The liver inactivates it mainly by glucuronidation, and metabolites leave in feces and urine. The half-life is about five hours, with pain relief lasting three to six hours.", "sidefx": "Sedation, dizziness and sweating come from kappa activity in the brain. Dysphoria and hallucinations also come from kappa receptors, which are linked to aversive feelings. Breathing suppression is limited by the ceiling effect, but it can still be serious when combined with alcohol, benzodiazepines or other sedatives. In people dependent on mu opioids, blocking mu receptors can trigger acute withdrawal. In labor, it can slow the fetal heart rate. Naloxone reverses its effects, although higher amounts may be needed.", "fun": "Nalbuphine can be used to treat the itching that morphine causes, by blocking mu receptors just enough while still providing its own kappa-based pain relief.", "src": ["Nalbuphine hydrochloride injection FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "methohexital": {"origin": "Barbiturates were first made in the late 1800s and early 1900s, and in 1934 John Lundy at the Mayo Clinic introduced thiopental, the first widely used IV anesthetic, which put patients to sleep in seconds. Chemists then looked for an even shorter-acting barbiturate. Methohexital, an oxybarbiturate (it has an oxygen where thiopental has a sulfur), was developed by Eli Lilly and introduced as Brevital in the late 1950s. It wakes patients faster than thiopental. Unlike most barbiturates, it can lower the seizure threshold, which made it the classic anesthetic for electroconvulsive therapy (ECT). After thiopental left the US market around 2011, methohexital became the main barbiturate anesthetic still available.", "timeline": [["1934", "John Lundy introduces thiopental at the Mayo Clinic."], ["1950s", "Eli Lilly introduces methohexital (Brevital)."], ["2011", "Thiopental is no longer available in the US, leaving methohexital as the main barbiturate anesthetic."]], "cell": "Methohexital binds a barbiturate site on the GABA-A receptor, a chloride channel made of five subunits. At low concentrations it is a positive allosteric modulator: it makes the channel stay open longer each time GABA binds (benzodiazepines instead increase how often it opens). More chloride flows in and the neuron is hyperpolarized and harder to fire. At higher concentrations, methohexital opens the channel directly even without GABA, which is why barbiturates can cause much deeper depression than benzodiazepines. It also blocks AMPA glutamate receptors, cutting excitatory signaling. The result is quick loss of consciousness. It wakes the patient fast because it redistributes from the brain to muscle and fat within minutes.", "body": [["Brain", "Consciousness is lost within about a minute, and the patient wakes in five to seven minutes after a single dose as the drug leaves the brain."], ["Seizure threshold", "Unlike thiopental, it can lower the seizure threshold and cause twitching or seizures, which makes it useful for ECT."], ["Breathing", "Breathing slows or stops briefly because GABA signaling suppresses the brainstem breathing centers."], ["Heart and vessels", "Veins dilate and the heart pumps less forcefully, so blood pressure falls and the heart rate rises as a reflex."], ["Airway", "It can trigger hiccups, coughing, laryngospasm or bronchospasm."], ["Blood vessels at injection", "Injection can be painful, and accidental injection into an artery can cause severe tissue damage."]], "journey": "Methohexital is usually given into a vein, though it can be given into muscle or rectally, mostly in children. It is very fat-soluble and passes into the brain within seconds. It then quickly moves from the brain to muscle and fat, which ends the effect of a single dose in minutes. The liver slowly breaks it down by demethylation and oxidation, and metabolites leave in the urine. Its elimination half-life is about four hours, much shorter than thiopental's, so repeated doses build up less.", "sidefx": "Respiratory depression and apnea come from GABA-A enhancement in the brainstem. Low blood pressure comes from vein dilation and reduced heart contraction. Excitatory effects such as muscle twitching, hiccups and seizures are linked to its effects on the cortex and are unusual for a barbiturate. Barbiturates induce the liver enzyme ALA synthase and can trigger attacks of acute porphyria, so they are avoided in porphyria. Injection into an artery can cause severe vasospasm and gangrene. There is no reversal agent; care is supportive.", "fun": "Methohexital is one of the few anesthetics that can make seizures more likely, the opposite of most barbiturates, which is why it became the gold standard for ECT.", "src": ["Brevital (methohexital) FDA label", "Miller's Anesthesia", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "chloroprocaine": {"origin": "Chloroprocaine is a close cousin of procaine, the first widely used synthetic local anesthetic, which Alfred Einhorn created in 1904 and which was introduced in 1905. Adding a chlorine atom to procaine's ring made a drug that is broken down in the blood even faster. Chloroprocaine was introduced in 1952 as Nesacaine. It is the shortest-acting ester local anesthetic, which makes it useful for epidural anesthesia in labor and for short procedures. Early epidural formulations with preservatives were linked to nerve injury and back pain, which led to preservative-free versions. In 2017 the FDA approved a preservative-free spinal form, Clorotekal, for short surgeries.", "timeline": [["1904", "Alfred Einhorn synthesizes procaine."], ["1952", "Chloroprocaine is introduced as Nesacaine."], ["1980s", "Nerve injury reports with older formulations lead to preservative-free products."], ["2017", "FDA approves a spinal form of chloroprocaine (Clorotekal)."]], "cell": "Chloroprocaine blocks voltage-gated sodium channels in nerve fibers. The uncharged base crosses the nerve membrane; inside the cell, it becomes charged and binds a site within the channel pore. It binds most strongly when the channel is open or inactivated, so it blocks busy nerves best. When sodium cannot rush in, the nerve cannot fire an action potential. Small fibers, such as sympathetic fibers and pain and temperature fibers (C and A-delta), are blocked first, followed by touch and then motor fibers. The ester bond is quickly hydrolyzed by plasma pseudocholinesterase into para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA) and 2-chloro-4-aminobenzoic acid, which is why its effects end fast and its systemic toxicity is low.", "body": [["Nerves in the blocked area", "Pain, temperature, touch and then movement are lost, allowing surgery or pain relief in labor."], ["Sympathetic nerves", "Neuraxial block relaxes blood vessels in the lower body, so blood pressure can drop."], ["Fetus", "Because it is broken down within seconds in the blood, very little reaches the baby during labor."], ["Heart and brain", "If enough reaches the blood, it can cause local anesthetic systemic toxicity, but its quick breakdown makes this less likely than with amide drugs."], ["Blood", "Very rarely it can cause methemoglobinemia."]], "journey": "Chloroprocaine is injected near nerves, into the epidural space or into the spinal fluid. It works within about 6 to 12 minutes and lasts about 30 to 60 minutes. Whatever reaches the blood is broken down by pseudocholinesterase extremely quickly, with a plasma half-life of only about 20 seconds in adults. The breakdown products leave in the urine. People with pseudocholinesterase deficiency break it down more slowly.", "sidefx": "Low blood pressure in neuraxial use comes from blocking sympathetic nerves. Back pain was linked to older epidural formulations containing EDTA. If injected into a vessel, it can cause local anesthetic systemic toxicity, with seizures and heart rhythm problems, though rapid breakdown limits this. Allergic reactions are more common with esters than amides because they form PABA, a known allergen. Methemoglobinemia is rare. LAST is treated with lipid emulsion and supportive care, and methemoglobinemia with methylene blue.", "fun": "Chloroprocaine's half-life in blood is measured in seconds, which makes it one of the safest local anesthetics for a mother and baby during labor.", "src": ["Nesacaine (chloroprocaine) FDA label", "Clorotekal FDA label", "Miller's Anesthesia"]}, "mepivacaine": {"origin": "After lidocaine was introduced in Sweden in the late 1940s, Swedish chemists kept exploring the amide family of local anesthetics. In 1956 Bo af Ekenstam and colleagues at the Swedish company AB Bofors made a series of pipecolyl xylidide compounds. Mepivacaine was the first to reach patients, introduced in 1957. The same series later produced bupivacaine and ropivacaine. Mepivacaine causes less vasodilation than lidocaine, so it can be used without epinephrine, which made it popular in dentistry for patients who should avoid epinephrine. It is sold as Carbocaine, Polocaine and Scandonest.", "timeline": [["1948", "Lidocaine is introduced in Sweden as the first amide local anesthetic."], ["1956", "Bo af Ekenstam's group synthesizes mepivacaine and related compounds."], ["1957", "Mepivacaine is introduced into clinical use."]], "cell": "Mepivacaine is an amide local anesthetic that blocks voltage-gated sodium channels. The uncharged base crosses the nerve membrane. Inside the nerve, it picks up a proton, and the charged form binds a site inside the channel pore from the inner side, mostly in the open and inactivated states. Blocked channels stop sodium from entering, so the nerve cannot depolarize and no action potential is sent. Small pain and temperature fibers are blocked first, then touch and motor fibers. Mepivacaine has less direct effect on the smooth muscle of blood vessels than lidocaine, so it causes less vasodilation. That keeps it at the site longer, which is why it works reasonably well even without epinephrine.", "body": [["Nerves in the blocked area", "Pain and touch are lost in the area, allowing dental or minor surgery."], ["Mouth and lips (dental use)", "Soft tissue numbness can last two to three hours, so children may bite their lips or cheeks without noticing."], ["Blood vessels", "Less vessel widening than lidocaine means it stays near the nerve longer without epinephrine."], ["Brain", "If blood levels get too high, it can cause dizziness, ringing in the ears and seizures."], ["Heart", "Very high levels can slow conduction and depress the heart."]], "journey": "Mepivacaine is injected near nerves, such as in the mouth for dental work. It works within about three to five minutes in dental use. Some is absorbed into the blood, where it binds proteins. The liver breaks it down by N-demethylation and hydroxylation, and metabolites, plus a small unchanged amount, leave in the urine. The half-life is about two to three hours in adults, but around nine hours in newborns, whose livers are immature.", "sidefx": "Numbness that outlasts the procedure is a direct result of nerve block, and lip biting is a concern in children. If too much reaches the blood, sodium channel block in the brain causes local anesthetic systemic toxicity: early signs like dizziness and a metallic taste, then seizures, and at higher levels heart rhythm problems and cardiac arrest. Allergy to amide anesthetics is rare. Methemoglobinemia can occur rarely. LAST is treated with lipid emulsion and supportive care.", "fun": "Mepivacaine is often called the dentist's choice for patients who should avoid epinephrine, because it numbs well enough on its own.", "src": ["Carbocaine (mepivacaine) FDA label", "Scandonest FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "ropivacaine": {"origin": "Ropivacaine was designed in response to a safety problem. Bupivacaine, introduced in the 1960s, gives long-lasting nerve block but is very toxic to the heart, and in 1979 an editorial by George Albright described cardiac arrests that were hard to resuscitate after bupivacaine. Researchers at the Swedish company Astra turned to a close relative from the same pipecolyl xylidide series made by Bo af Ekenstam in the 1950s. They developed it as a pure S-enantiomer, because the S form of these drugs is less toxic to the heart, and with a propyl side chain instead of bupivacaine's butyl. The result was ropivacaine, approved by the FDA in 1996 as Naropin. It is widely used for epidurals and nerve blocks.", "timeline": [["1957", "Bo af Ekenstam's group synthesizes the pipecolyl xylidide family that includes ropivacaine."], ["1979", "Albright warns of cardiac arrest after bupivacaine, spurring safer alternatives."], ["1996", "FDA approves ropivacaine (Naropin)."]], "cell": "Ropivacaine blocks voltage-gated sodium channels from the inside of the nerve. The uncharged drug crosses the membrane, becomes charged and binds within the channel pore when it is open or inactivated. Sodium entry stops and the nerve cannot fire. Ropivacaine is less fat-soluble than bupivacaine because of its shorter side chain. It enters thin, unmyelinated pain fibers well but penetrates the thick myelinated motor fibers less, so at low concentrations it blocks pain more than movement (sensory-motor separation). In the heart, the pure S-enantiomer binds cardiac sodium channels less tightly and leaves them faster than the R form in bupivacaine, which lowers its cardiotoxicity.", "body": [["Nerves in the blocked area", "Long-lasting pain relief lets it be used for surgery and for hours of pain control after surgery."], ["Motor nerves", "At low concentrations, patients can often still move their legs, helpful for labor epidurals."], ["Sympathetic nerves", "Neuraxial block relaxes blood vessels and can lower blood pressure."], ["Heart", "It is less cardiotoxic than bupivacaine, but high blood levels can still cause rhythm problems and cardiac arrest."], ["Brain", "High blood levels can cause dizziness, tinnitus, a metallic taste and seizures."], ["Blood vessels at the site", "It causes mild vasoconstriction at low concentrations, which helps it stay near the nerve."]], "journey": "Ropivacaine is injected near nerves, into the epidural space or into tissue. Onset is about 10 to 30 minutes, and effect lasts hours, up to about 10 hours for major nerve blocks. Absorbed drug is about 94 percent bound to plasma proteins, mainly alpha-1 acid glycoprotein. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP1A2 to 3-hydroxy-ropivacaine, with a minor CYP3A4 role, and metabolites leave in the urine. The half-life is about two hours by vein and about four hours after epidural use, because absorption from the site is slow.", "sidefx": "Low blood pressure and slow heart rate with neuraxial use come from sympathetic block. If too much reaches the blood, sodium channel block in the brain and heart causes local anesthetic systemic toxicity: early agitation and tinnitus, then seizures, and then arrhythmias and cardiac arrest, though the risk is lower than with bupivacaine. Strong CYP1A2 inhibitors such as fluvoxamine can raise levels. LAST is treated with lipid emulsion and supportive care.", "fun": "Ropivacaine is one of the first local anesthetics developed as a single mirror-image form specifically to make it safer for the heart.", "src": ["Naropin (ropivacaine) FDA label", "Albright GA. Anesthesiology 1979", "Miller's Anesthesia"]}, "articaine": {"origin": "Articaine was made in 1969 by the chemist H. Rusching and colleagues at the German company Hoechst, under the name carticaine. It is unusual among amide local anesthetics because it has a thiophene ring instead of a benzene ring and an extra ester group. It entered dental practice in Germany in 1976 and became the most used dental anesthetic in much of Europe. In the US, the FDA approved articaine with epinephrine as Septocaine in 2000. It is now one of the most common dental anesthetics in the US as well, valued for fast onset and good spread through bone.", "timeline": [["1969", "Articaine (carticaine) is synthesized at Hoechst in Germany."], ["1976", "Articaine enters dental use in Germany."], ["2000", "FDA approves articaine with epinephrine (Septocaine)."]], "cell": "Articaine blocks voltage-gated sodium channels from the inside of the nerve, the same way other amide anesthetics do. Its thiophene ring makes it more fat-soluble, helping it spread through bone and soft tissue to reach dental nerves. Once inside the nerve, the charged form binds the channel pore, stopping sodium entry and preventing action potentials, so pain signals from the tooth cannot reach the brain. Articaine also has an ester side chain. Plasma carboxylesterases cut this chain quickly, turning about 90 percent of the drug into inactive articainic acid. The epinephrine in the cartridge stimulates alpha-1 receptors on nearby blood vessels, narrowing them and slowing removal of the anesthetic, which prolongs numbness and reduces bleeding.", "body": [["Teeth and jaw", "Fast, deep numbness of the teeth, allowing fillings, extractions and root canals."], ["Gums and soft tissue", "Soft tissue numbness lasts several hours, longer than the numbness of the tooth itself."], ["Blood vessels", "Epinephrine narrows local vessels, reducing bleeding in the surgical area."], ["Heart", "Some epinephrine reaches the blood, which can raise heart rate and blood pressure."], ["Nerves", "Rarely, numbness or tingling can persist for weeks after a lower jaw nerve block."], ["Blood", "Very rarely, it can cause methemoglobinemia."]], "journey": "Articaine is injected into the mouth near the target nerve. Onset is about one to six minutes. Pulpal (tooth) anesthesia lasts about an hour, and soft tissue numbness lasts longer. Absorbed articaine is broken down quickly in the blood by carboxylesterases, mainly to articainic acid, with only a small amount handled by the liver. Metabolites leave in the urine. Its half-life is short, about 20 to 45 minutes, so systemic levels fall quickly.", "sidefx": "Pain and swelling at the injection site come from the injection itself. Prolonged numbness or tingling may reflect local nerve irritation; some studies suggest it is more common with higher-concentration anesthetics in lower jaw blocks, though this is debated. If injected into a vessel or given in excess, it can cause local anesthetic systemic toxicity with seizures and arrhythmias. Epinephrine can cause fast heart rate, high blood pressure and rhythm problems in sensitive patients. The product contains sulfite as a preservative for epinephrine, which can trigger reactions in sulfite-sensitive people.", "fun": "Because blood enzymes break it down so fast, articaine's half-life is much shorter than that of lidocaine.", "src": ["Septocaine (articaine and epinephrine) FDA label", "Malamed SF. Handbook of Local Anesthesia", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "atracurium": {"origin": "Muscle relaxants for surgery trace back to curare, the arrow poison used by Indigenous peoples of South America. Its active part, tubocurarine, was first used in anesthesia in 1942. Early relaxants depended on the liver and kidneys for removal, which caused problems in very sick patients. In the 1970s and early 1980s John Stenlake and colleagues at the University of Strathclyde in Glasgow designed atracurium to break down on its own in the body, using a chemical reaction called Hofmann elimination that happens at normal body temperature and pH. Developed with Burroughs Wellcome, it was approved in the US in 1983 as Tracrium. Its success led to cisatracurium, a purified isomer. The Tracrium brand has been discontinued, and generic atracurium remains.", "timeline": [["1942", "Harold Griffith and Enid Johnson first use curare in anesthesia in Montreal."], ["1970s", "John Stenlake's group designs atracurium to undergo Hofmann elimination."], ["1983", "FDA approves atracurium (Tracrium)."], ["1995", "Cisatracurium, a single isomer of atracurium, is approved."]], "cell": "At the neuromuscular junction, acetylcholine released from the nerve binds nicotinic receptors on the muscle end plate. These receptors are ion channels made of two alpha, one beta, one delta and one epsilon subunit; acetylcholine must bind both alpha subunits to open them. Sodium rushes in and the muscle contracts. Atracurium is a bulky benzylisoquinolinium compound that competes with acetylcholine for the alpha sites but does not open the channel, so the end plate cannot depolarize and the muscle relaxes (nondepolarizing block). Atracurium breaks down by Hofmann elimination, a spontaneous chemical reaction at body pH and temperature, and by ester hydrolysis in plasma. Both produce laudanosine and a quaternary acrylate. Cholinesterase inhibitors such as neostigmine raise acetylcholine to outcompete it.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Muscles relax and become limp, allowing intubation and surgery. Small muscles such as the eyes are affected first, then limbs, then the diaphragm."], ["Breathing", "The diaphragm and chest muscles are paralyzed, so breathing must be supported."], ["Skin and blood vessels", "Atracurium releases histamine from mast cells, causing flushing and a drop in blood pressure, especially with rapid or large doses."], ["Airways", "Histamine can cause bronchospasm, a concern in people with asthma."], ["Brain", "It does not affect awareness or pain, so patients must be fully sedated before paralysis."], ["Liver and kidneys", "Because its breakdown does not depend on these organs, it can be used in liver or kidney failure."]], "journey": "Atracurium is given only into a vein. Paralysis begins in about two minutes and peaks in three to five minutes. Its breakdown happens in the plasma by Hofmann elimination and ester hydrolysis, with a half-life of about 20 minutes, regardless of liver or kidney function. Effects last about 20 to 35 minutes. The metabolite laudanosine is cleared by the liver and kidneys, and it can build up with long infusions.", "sidefx": "Flushing, low blood pressure and fast heart rate come from histamine released from mast cells, which is why it is given slowly. Bronchospasm is also histamine-related. Prolonged weakness can follow long use in critical illness. Laudanosine can cross into the brain and, at high levels in animals, cause seizures, though this is rarely a problem in people. Because it does not affect consciousness, awareness under paralysis can happen if sedation is inadequate. Anaphylaxis is a known risk with neuromuscular blockers. Neostigmine with an antimuscarinic can reverse the block once some recovery has begun.", "fun": "Atracurium's breakdown is pure chemistry. Like a timer, it falls apart on its own at body temperature, which is why the vials are kept in the refrigerator.", "src": ["Atracurium besylate injection FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Miller's Anesthesia"]}, "remimazolam": {"origin": "Remimazolam was built to do for benzodiazepines what remifentanil did for opioids. In the 1990s scientists at Glaxo Wellcome added an ester side chain to a midazolam-like structure so that tissue esterases would quickly turn it into an inactive compound. The idea was a sedative that wears off fast and predictably. Glaxo did not develop it further, and the program was later picked up by other companies, including the German company PAION. After trials in procedural sedation, the FDA approved it as Byfavo in 2020 for sedation during procedures lasting 30 minutes or less, such as colonoscopy and bronchoscopy. It is a Schedule IV controlled substance.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Glaxo Wellcome scientists design remimazolam as an ester-based soft benzodiazepine."], ["2000s", "Development moves to other companies, including PAION."], ["2020", "FDA approves remimazolam (Byfavo) for procedural sedation."]], "cell": "Remimazolam binds the benzodiazepine site on the GABA-A receptor, at the interface between the alpha and gamma subunits. It is a positive allosteric modulator: it does not open the channel by itself, but it increases how often the channel opens when GABA binds. More chloride enters the neuron, hyperpolarizing it and making it less likely to fire. This produces sedation, anxiety relief and memory loss for the procedure, but no pain relief. Its key feature is a carboxylic ester side chain. Carboxylesterase-1, mainly in the liver, hydrolyzes it to CNS7054, an inactive metabolite with much weaker binding. Because this breakdown is fast and does not depend on CYP enzymes, its effect ends quickly.", "body": [["Brain", "Rapid sedation and anterograde amnesia let patients tolerate procedures and not remember them."], ["Breathing", "GABA effects on breathing centers can slow breathing or cause low oxygen, especially with opioids."], ["Heart and blood pressure", "Blood pressure can fall, though in trials this was often less marked than with propofol."], ["Recovery", "Most patients are alert again within about 10 to 20 minutes after the last dose."], ["Pain", "It has no pain-relieving action, so opioids such as fentanyl are usually given with it."]], "journey": "Remimazolam is given only into a vein. It begins working in one to three minutes, and peak sedation comes about three minutes after a dose. It is more than 91 percent bound to albumin. Carboxylesterase-1 in the liver and other tissues breaks it down quickly to inactive CNS7054, which leaves mainly in the urine. The half-life is about 37 to 53 minutes. Severe liver disease can slow recovery because less carboxylesterase is available.", "sidefx": "Low blood pressure, low oxygen and slow breathing come from GABA-A enhancement in the brainstem, and these effects add up with opioids and other sedatives. Prolonged sedation can occur in severe liver impairment. Because the formulation contains dextran 40, people allergic to dextran should avoid it. Flumazenil, a benzodiazepine antagonist, can reverse sedation, though its own short action may require repeat doses.", "fun": "Remimazolam's design shows how adding a single ester group can turn a familiar drug into one with a whole new pharmacokinetic profile.", "src": ["Byfavo (remimazolam) FDA label", "Kilpatrick GJ et al. Anesthesiology 2007", "Miller's Anesthesia"]}, "ephedrine": {"origin": "Ephedrine comes from Ephedra plants, known in Chinese medicine as ma huang, which have been used for thousands of years for cough, asthma and colds. In 1885 the Japanese chemist Nagai Nagayoshi isolated ephedrine from the plant. Its medical value was recognized in 1924 when K. K. Chen and Carl Schmidt at Peking Union Medical College showed that it acted much like adrenaline but lasted longer and worked by mouth. It became a major asthma drug before modern inhalers. Ephedra supplements were banned in the US in 2004 after reports of heart attacks and strokes, and oral ephedrine is tightly regulated because it can be used to make methamphetamine. Injectable ephedrine, including FDA-approved Akovaz since 2016, is mainly used to treat low blood pressure during anesthesia.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Ephedra (ma huang) is used in Chinese medicine for breathing problems."], ["1885", "Nagai Nagayoshi isolates ephedrine from Ephedra."], ["1924", "Chen and Schmidt describe ephedrine's adrenaline-like effects."], ["2004", "FDA bans dietary supplements containing ephedra alkaloids."], ["2016", "FDA approves ephedrine sulfate injection (Akovaz)."]], "cell": "Ephedrine acts in two ways. Directly, it is an agonist at alpha-1, beta-1 and beta-2 adrenergic receptors. Alpha-1 activation (Gq) narrows blood vessels. Beta-1 activation (Gs) raises cyclic AMP in heart muscle, increasing heart rate and force. Beta-2 activation relaxes bronchial smooth muscle. Indirectly, ephedrine is taken up into sympathetic nerve endings by the norepinephrine transporter and into vesicles by VMAT, where it displaces stored norepinephrine, which leaks out and stimulates receptors. This indirect action means repeated doses work less and less (tachyphylaxis) as norepinephrine stores run low. It also works poorly in people whose stores are depleted, such as those taking reserpine.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 stimulation narrows vessels, raising blood pressure."], ["Heart", "Beta-1 stimulation increases heart rate and contractility, raising cardiac output."], ["Lungs", "Beta-2 stimulation relaxes airway muscle, which is why it was once a common asthma drug."], ["Brain", "It crosses into the brain and causes alertness, restlessness, anxiety and insomnia."], ["Uterus and placenta", "In obstetric anesthesia, it raises blood pressure while generally preserving blood flow to the uterus."], ["Bladder", "Alpha effects on the bladder outlet can cause urinary retention."]], "journey": "Injectable ephedrine is given into a vein and acts within about a minute. It is not broken down by MAO or COMT, unlike adrenaline, which is why it lasts longer and works by mouth. A small amount is demethylated by the liver, but most is excreted unchanged by the kidneys. Acidic urine speeds its removal. The half-life is about three to six hours, and the pressor effect of a single IV dose lasts about 10 to 60 minutes.", "sidefx": "Fast heart rate, palpitations and high blood pressure come from beta-1 and alpha-1 stimulation. Arrhythmias and heart ischemia can follow when the heart works harder than its blood supply allows. CNS stimulation causes restlessness, anxiety and insomnia. Tachyphylaxis means repeated doses become less effective. With MAO inhibitors, released norepinephrine is not broken down, which can cause a hypertensive crisis. There is no specific antidote; treatment is supportive, with short-acting vasodilators or beta blockers for severe effects.", "fun": "Ephedrine's structure is so close to methamphetamine that ephedrine and pseudoephedrine products are regulated as List I chemicals to prevent illicit drug production.", "src": ["Akovaz (ephedrine sulfate) FDA label", "Chen KK, Schmidt CF. J Pharmacol Exp Ther 1924", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "albumin-human": {"origin": "Albumin therapy was born during World War II. The US military needed a stable blood product that could be shipped to battlefields without refrigeration. At Harvard, Edwin Cohn developed cold ethanol fractionation, a method to separate plasma into its protein parts, including albumin, by carefully changing temperature, alcohol and acidity. Albumin was heated to kill viruses, which made it one of the safest plasma products. It was used to treat burned and wounded sailors after the attack on Pearl Harbor in 1941. Today many brands, including Albutein, Plasbumin, AlbuRx and Flexbumin, are used for volume replacement, low albumin states, burns, liver disease and large-volume paracentesis.", "timeline": [["1940", "Edwin Cohn begins large-scale plasma fractionation work at Harvard."], ["1941", "Albumin is used to treat casualties after the Pearl Harbor attack."], ["1940s", "Heat treatment of albumin is introduced to inactivate viruses."]], "cell": "Albumin is a single protein chain of about 66.5 kDa, made in the liver. It is too large to pass easily through normal capillary walls. It creates most of the colloid oncotic pressure in plasma, the force that pulls water back into vessels and balances the hydrostatic pressure pushing fluid out (Starling forces). Giving 5 percent albumin, which has an oncotic pressure similar to plasma, adds volume roughly equal to the amount given. Giving 25 percent albumin draws extra water from the tissues into the blood, about three to four times its own volume. Albumin also binds and carries many substances, including bilirubin, fatty acids, calcium, hormones and drugs. In septic or burned patients with leaky capillaries, albumin escapes into the tissues and the effect is shorter.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "More fluid stays in the vessels, raising blood volume, blood pressure and cardiac output."], ["Tissues", "In low-albumin states, 25 percent albumin can pull fluid out of swollen tissues."], ["Liver disease", "After large-volume paracentesis, albumin helps prevent circulatory problems and kidney injury in cirrhosis."], ["Kidneys", "Better blood volume improves blood flow to the kidneys in some patients."], ["Lungs", "Too much volume can overload the circulation and cause pulmonary edema, especially in heart failure."], ["Drug binding", "Albumin carries many drugs, so its level affects how much free drug is active in the blood."]], "journey": "Albumin is given into a vein. It stays mainly in the blood at first and then slowly moves into the tissue fluid, with about 40 percent of the body's albumin inside vessels at any time. It returns to the blood through the lymphatic system. It is not cleared by the kidneys under normal conditions. Cells throughout the body take it up and break it down into amino acids. Its half-life is about 15 to 20 days.", "sidefx": "Fever, chills and hives come from rare reactions to the protein or trace contaminants. Circulatory overload and pulmonary edema happen because albumin pulls fluid into vessels, especially with 25 percent albumin in people with weak hearts. Because it is made from heat-treated plasma, the risk of transmitting viruses is extremely low. Allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, are rare. There is no antidote; overload is treated with diuretics and supportive care.", "fun": "Albumin is the most abundant protein in human plasma. Its name comes from the Latin albus, meaning white, the same root as albumen, the white of an egg.", "src": ["Albutein (albumin human) FDA label", "Plasbumin FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "hetastarch": {"origin": "Hetastarch is a man-made colloid built from amylopectin, the branched starch in waxy corn. Plain starch is broken down in minutes by amylase in the blood, so chemists attached hydroxyethyl groups to the glucose units to slow that breakdown and keep the molecules in the vessels longer. Hydroxyethyl starch was studied as a plasma substitute in the 1960s, and hetastarch (Hespan) reached the US market in the early 1970s as a cheaper, shelf-stable alternative to albumin. Hextend, hetastarch in a balanced electrolyte solution, was approved in 1999. Large trials later linked hydroxyethyl starches to kidney injury, bleeding and higher death rates in critically ill and septic patients. The FDA added a boxed warning in 2013 and in 2021 strengthened it to say these products should not be used unless adequate alternatives are unavailable.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Hydroxyethyl starch is studied as a synthetic plasma volume expander."], ["1970s", "Hetastarch (Hespan) becomes available in the US."], ["1999", "Hextend, hetastarch in a lactated electrolyte solution, is approved."], ["2013", "FDA adds a boxed warning on mortality, kidney injury and bleeding."], ["2021", "FDA strengthens warnings to limit use to when alternatives are unavailable."]], "cell": "Hetastarch molecules are large, highly branched starch chains with an average size of about 600 kDa. Hydroxyethyl groups on the glucose units block amylase from cutting the chains quickly. Like albumin, these large molecules stay in the blood and create colloid oncotic pressure that holds water inside vessels, expanding blood volume. Amylase slowly trims large molecules into smaller ones, which the kidneys filter out. The largest pieces are taken up by macrophages and other cells and stored in tissues such as the liver, skin and kidney tubules for a long time. Large starch molecules also lower factor VIII and von Willebrand factor levels and coat platelets, which impairs clotting. Uptake into kidney tubule cells causes osmotic swelling and injury.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Blood volume increases and lasts about a day or more, raising blood pressure and cardiac output in hypovolemia."], ["Blood clotting", "Lower factor VIII and von Willebrand factor and impaired platelets prolong bleeding, especially after large volumes."], ["Kidneys", "Starch taken up by tubule cells causes swelling (osmotic nephrosis) and acute kidney injury, especially in sepsis."], ["Skin", "Starch stored in skin nerves can cause severe itching that starts weeks later and lasts months."], ["Pancreas lab tests", "Serum amylase rises because amylase binds to starch, which can falsely suggest pancreatitis."], ["Whole body", "In septic and critically ill patients, trials showed a higher risk of death."]], "journey": "Hetastarch is infused into a vein, where it acts at once. Its volume effect lasts about a day or more. Amylase in the blood breaks large molecules into smaller ones. Smaller pieces are filtered by the kidneys into the urine within days, while the largest molecules are taken up by tissues and released very slowly, so traces can remain for weeks or longer. That long tissue storage explains delayed itching.", "sidefx": "Bleeding comes from dilution of clotting factors, lower factor VIII and von Willebrand factor, and coated platelets. Kidney injury comes from starch uptake into tubule cells, causing osmotic swelling. Itching comes from starch stored in skin nerves. These effects explain the boxed warning against use in critically ill adults, including sepsis, unless no adequate alternative exists. Anaphylactoid reactions can occur. There is no antidote; it is stopped and the patient is supported.", "fun": "Hetastarch is made from the starch of waxy corn, the same kind of starch used to thicken foods.", "src": ["Hespan (hetastarch) FDA label", "FDA Safety Communication on hydroxyethyl starch products, 2013 and 2021", "Myburgh JA et al. NEJM 2012 (CHEST trial)"]}, "dextran-40": {"origin": "Dextran is a sugar polymer made by bacteria. In the 1800s sugar refiners were plagued by slimy, gummy masses in their syrups, which turned out to be dextran made by the bacterium Leuconostoc mesenteroides. In the 1940s Swedish scientists Anders Gronwall and Bjorn Ingelman at Uppsala University showed that partly broken-down dextran could be used as a plasma substitute, and a larger dextran product (Macrodex) became available in the late 1940s. A smaller version with an average size of about 40 kDa, low molecular weight dextran, was introduced later to improve blood flow in small vessels. Dextran 40 (LMD) is used for plasma volume expansion and as an adjunct to prevent clots in some surgeries.", "timeline": [["1800s", "Sugar refiners notice slimy dextran masses made by bacteria."], ["1940s", "Gronwall and Ingelman develop dextran as a plasma substitute in Sweden."], ["1960s", "Low molecular weight dextran 40 comes into use for improving microcirculation."]], "cell": "Dextran 40 is a branched chain of glucose units linked mainly by alpha-1,6 bonds, made by bacterial fermentation of sugar. The chains are large enough to stay in the blood at first and create colloid oncotic pressure, which holds water in the vessels and expands blood volume. Because many molecules are small enough to be filtered by the kidneys, the effect is shorter than with larger colloids. Dextran also coats red cells, platelets and the vessel wall. This makes red cells less likely to stack together, lowers blood viscosity, reduces platelet sticking and lowers factor VIII activity. Together these effects improve flow in small vessels and make clots less likely.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Blood volume increases quickly, with the effect lasting several hours."], ["Microcirculation", "Lower viscosity and less red cell clumping improve blood flow through small vessels."], ["Blood clotting", "Coated platelets and lower factor VIII reduce clotting and prolong bleeding time."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys filter most of it. In dehydration, dextran can concentrate in the tubules and cause kidney failure."], ["Lab tests", "Dextran can interfere with blood typing and crossmatching, so samples are drawn before it is given."]], "journey": "Dextran 40 is given into a vein and acts at once. Its half-life is about two to three hours. About 70 percent of a dose is filtered by the kidneys within a day. Larger molecules are taken up by tissues and slowly broken down by dextranase enzymes into glucose, which the body then uses. The volume effect lasts about six hours, shorter than albumin.", "sidefx": "Severe anaphylactoid reactions happen because many people have antibodies against bacterial dextrans from earlier exposure; these antibodies form immune complexes when dextran is infused. Bleeding risk comes from its antiplatelet and anti-factor VIII effects. Kidney failure can occur when highly concentrated dextran in the tubules raises urine viscosity and injures tubule cells, especially in dehydration. Fluid overload is possible in heart failure. There is no specific reversal agent.", "fun": "Dextran molecules form the same gooey film that bacteria use to stick to teeth, contributing to dental plaque.", "src": ["LMD (dextran 40) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Gronwall A, Ingelman B. Acta Physiol Scand 1944"]}, "lactated-ringers": {"origin": "In the early 1880s Sydney Ringer, a physician in London, was studying how frog hearts beat outside the body in a salt solution. His hearts kept beating well, until he realized his lab assistant had been using London tap water, which contained calcium and potassium. When distilled water was used, the hearts stopped. This accident led Ringer to show that calcium and potassium in the right balance are essential for heart contraction, and Ringer's solution was born. In 1932 the American pediatrician Alexis Hartmann added sodium lactate to treat acidosis in sick children, creating lactated Ringer's, also called Hartmann's solution. Today it is one of the most used IV fluids in trauma, surgery and burns.", "timeline": [["1882", "Sydney Ringer begins publishing studies of salt solutions on frog hearts."], ["1883", "Ringer reports that calcium and potassium are needed for heart contraction."], ["1932", "Alexis Hartmann adds lactate to Ringer's solution to treat acidosis."]], "cell": "Lactated Ringer's is an isotonic crystalloid with sodium, potassium, calcium, chloride and lactate in amounts close to plasma. Because it has a similar effective osmolality to plasma, it does not shift water into or out of cells. It distributes throughout the fluid outside cells: the small ions pass freely through capillary walls, so within about 30 to 60 minutes only about a quarter of an infused volume stays in the blood vessels and the rest moves into the tissue fluid. The lactate is taken up mainly by the liver and converted through pyruvate to glucose or carbon dioxide, consuming a hydrogen ion and generating bicarbonate. Because it has less chloride than normal saline, it causes less hyperchloremic acidosis.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Volume increases at first, raising preload, cardiac output and blood pressure in dehydration or blood loss."], ["Tissue fluid", "Most of the fluid moves into the tissues within an hour, so large volumes cause edema."], ["Acid-base balance", "Lactate becomes bicarbonate in the liver, so it causes less acidosis than normal saline."], ["Kidneys", "Better blood volume improves kidney blood flow and urine output."], ["Blood transfusions", "Its calcium can bind citrate in stored blood and cause clots, so it is not mixed directly with some blood products."], ["Lab lactate", "Lactate from the fluid is quickly cleared with normal liver function and does not usually cause lactic acidosis."]], "journey": "Lactated Ringer's is given into a vein. It reaches all of the fluid outside cells within about 30 to 60 minutes, with about a quarter staying in the vessels. The liver converts lactate to bicarbonate within about 20 minutes in healthy people. The kidneys excrete the extra water and electrolytes over hours.", "sidefx": "Fluid overload, swelling and pulmonary edema happen because most of the infused fluid moves into the tissues and lungs, especially in heart or kidney failure. Its sodium is a little lower than plasma, so large volumes can lower sodium. Its potassium can add to high potassium in severe kidney failure. Calcium can react with citrate in blood products and with some drugs, such as ceftriaxone in newborns. Severe liver failure slows lactate conversion. Allergic reactions are very rare.", "fun": "One of medicine's most common IV fluids exists because a lab assistant used tap water instead of distilled water.", "src": ["Lactated Ringer's Injection USP FDA label", "Ringer S. J Physiol 1883", "Hartmann AF, Senn MJE. J Clin Invest 1932"]}, "plasma-lyte": {"origin": "Plasma-Lyte belongs to the family of balanced crystalloids that began with Sydney Ringer's salt solutions in the 1880s and Alexis Hartmann's lactated Ringer's in 1932. Later, Baxter developed Multiple Electrolytes Injection Type 1, sold as Plasma-Lyte A and Plasma-Lyte 148, with a mix of electrolytes designed to resemble plasma more closely. It contains magnesium instead of calcium and uses acetate and gluconate instead of lactate as buffers. Because it has no calcium, it can run in the same IV line as blood products. Recent large trials comparing balanced fluids with saline in critically ill adults have increased interest in fluids like Plasma-Lyte.", "timeline": [["1880s", "Sydney Ringer shows calcium and potassium are needed in salt solutions for heart function."], ["1932", "Alexis Hartmann creates lactated Ringer's, the first buffered balanced crystalloid."], ["2018", "The SMART trial reports fewer kidney complications with balanced crystalloids than saline in critically ill adults."]], "cell": "Plasma-Lyte is an isotonic crystalloid with sodium, potassium, magnesium, chloride, acetate and gluconate, and a pH near 7.4. Its osmolality is close to plasma, so it does not shift water into or out of cells. Small ions cross capillaries freely, so it spreads through all the fluid outside cells, with only about a quarter staying in the blood vessels after redistribution. Its chloride is close to plasma levels, much lower than saline, so it does not cause hyperchloremic acidosis. Acetate is taken up by muscle and other tissues and converted to bicarbonate. Gluconate is only partly metabolized and some is excreted unchanged. The bicarbonate made helps prevent acidosis.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Volume increases at first, raising preload, cardiac output and blood pressure in hypovolemia."], ["Tissue fluid", "Most of the fluid moves into the tissues within an hour, which can cause swelling with large volumes."], ["Acid-base balance", "Acetate and gluconate produce bicarbonate, and its lower chloride avoids the acidosis seen with saline."], ["Kidneys", "Lower chloride may reduce kidney vasoconstriction compared with saline, which is one theory for fewer kidney complications in trials."], ["Blood transfusions", "Because it contains no calcium, it does not cause clotting when run with blood products."], ["Heart", "Acetate can relax vessels, and rapid large infusions have been linked to lower blood pressure in some settings."]], "journey": "Plasma-Lyte is given into a vein. It spreads through all of the fluid outside cells within about 30 to 60 minutes. Acetate is converted to bicarbonate in muscle and liver within minutes, so it does not depend on the liver as much as lactate does. Gluconate is metabolized more slowly and partly excreted. The kidneys clear the extra water and electrolytes over hours.", "sidefx": "Fluid overload, swelling and pulmonary edema come from large volumes of fluid spreading into tissues, especially in heart or kidney failure. Its potassium and magnesium can build up in severe kidney failure. Gluconate can cause falsely positive results on some fungal galactomannan tests, a lab problem reported with older products. There is no specific antidote; overload is treated with fluid restriction and diuretics.", "fun": "Plasma-Lyte's makers tried to make an IV fluid that looks as much like plasma as possible, including a pH near the body's own.", "src": ["Plasma-Lyte A Injection pH 7.4 FDA label", "Semler MW et al. NEJM 2018 (SMART trial)", "Miller's Anesthesia"]}, "normal-saline": {"origin": "Saline as a medical treatment began with cholera. In 1832, during a cholera epidemic in Britain, the Scottish doctor Thomas Latta injected salt water into the veins of dying patients and saw some recover, one of the first uses of IV fluid. Later in the 1800s, physiologists, including the Dutch scientist Hartog Hamburger, studied red cells in salt solutions and found that about 0.9 percent sodium chloride did not make them swell or shrink. This concentration became known as 'normal' saline, although it is not truly normal: it has more chloride than plasma. In the 20th century, sterile, sealed bags made it the most used IV fluid in the world. Recent trials show balanced fluids may cause fewer kidney complications in critically ill adults.", "timeline": [["1832", "Thomas Latta gives IV salt solutions to cholera patients."], ["1880s", "Hamburger's red cell studies help define 0.9 percent saline as isotonic."], ["1970s", "Flexible plastic IV bags become widely used."], ["2018", "The SMART trial compares saline with balanced crystalloids in critically ill adults."]], "cell": "Normal saline contains 154 mEq per liter each of sodium and chloride, with an osmolarity of about 308 mOsm per liter, close to plasma. Because it does not change the osmolality of the fluid outside cells much, it does not shift water into or out of cells. Sodium and chloride cross capillaries freely, so the fluid spreads through all the extracellular space, leaving about a quarter in the blood vessels after 30 to 60 minutes. Its chloride content is much higher than plasma. Large volumes raise blood chloride. By the strong ion difference concept, this lowers bicarbonate and causes a non-anion gap (hyperchloremic) metabolic acidosis. High chloride in the kidney's macula densa may also narrow kidney arterioles and lower filtration.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Volume rises at first, increasing preload, cardiac output and blood pressure in dehydration or blood loss."], ["Tissue fluid", "Most of the fluid moves into the tissues, so large volumes cause swelling."], ["Acid-base balance", "High chloride lowers bicarbonate and can cause hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis."], ["Kidneys", "High chloride may reduce kidney blood flow and filtration, a possible reason for more kidney complications in some trials."], ["Sodium levels", "Large volumes can raise sodium in people who lose free water; overly fast correction of chronic low sodium can damage the brain."], ["Blood transfusions", "Because it has no calcium, it is the standard fluid to run with blood products."]], "journey": "Normal saline is given into a vein. It distributes through all the extracellular fluid within about 30 to 60 minutes, so only about a quarter stays in the blood. It is not metabolized. The kidneys gradually excrete the extra sodium, chloride and water, which can take longer than with balanced fluids because high chloride may reduce kidney blood flow.", "sidefx": "Fluid overload and pulmonary edema come from large volumes spreading into tissues and lungs. Hyperchloremic acidosis follows from its high chloride content. Raising sodium too fast in someone with chronic low sodium can cause osmotic demyelination, as brain cells that adapted to low sodium shrink too quickly. Infiltration of the IV can cause swelling in tissues. Air embolism is a risk with pressurized bags. There is no antidote; care involves adjusting fluids and using diuretics.", "fun": "Normal saline is not normal at all: its chloride level is about 50 percent higher than that of human plasma.", "src": ["0.9% Sodium Chloride Injection USP FDA label", "Latta T. Lancet 1832", "Semler MW et al. NEJM 2018 (SMART trial)"]}, "sodium-chloride-23-4-percent": {"origin": "Concentrated sodium chloride was first made as a pharmacy additive: a small vial added to IV bags or parenteral nutrition to supply sodium. Its second use comes from brain research. In 1919 Lewis Weed and Paul McKibben at Johns Hopkins showed that injecting concentrated salt solutions into the blood shrank the brain and lowered cerebrospinal fluid pressure, while dilute solutions made it swell. This was the start of osmotherapy. Mannitol became the standard osmotic agent in the 1960s, but hypertonic saline gained new interest in neurocritical care. Undiluted 23.4 percent saline given through a central line to lower dangerous brain pressure is an off-label use. Because accidental undiluted injection has killed patients, it is treated as a high-alert medication.", "timeline": [["1919", "Weed and McKibben show hypertonic solutions shrink the brain and lower CSF pressure."], ["1960s", "Mannitol becomes the standard osmotic therapy for raised intracranial pressure."], ["1990s", "Concentrated hypertonic saline is increasingly studied for brain swelling."]], "cell": "23.4 percent saline holds about 4 mEq per mL each of sodium and chloride, with an osmolarity around 8,000 mOsm per liter, more than 25 times that of plasma. When diluted, it simply replaces sodium and chloride. Given in small volumes through a central line, it causes an abrupt rise in plasma sodium and osmolality. The blood-brain barrier is mostly impermeable to sodium, so the brain cannot quickly match the change. Water moves out of brain cells and tissue into the hypertonic blood by osmosis, shrinking brain volume and lowering intracranial pressure. The rise in plasma osmolality also draws water from cells and tissues throughout the body into the vessels, expanding plasma volume and improving blood pressure.", "body": [["Brain", "Water moves out of the brain, lowering intracranial pressure within minutes. Where the blood-brain barrier is damaged, the effect is weaker."], ["Blood volume", "Fluid shifts from cells and tissues into vessels, raising blood volume and blood pressure."], ["Sodium levels", "Serum sodium and osmolality rise quickly and must be monitored."], ["Kidneys", "The sodium load is excreted by the kidneys, which may cause large urine volumes and potassium loss."], ["Veins", "The extreme concentration damages vein walls, so it must go through a central line."], ["Acid-base balance", "The chloride load can cause hyperchloremic acidosis."]], "journey": "For most uses, 23.4 percent saline is diluted into larger IV fluids or parenteral nutrition before it is given. For brain swelling, a small undiluted amount is pushed through a central line, and the rise in sodium is immediate. The sodium and chloride spread throughout the fluid outside cells. They are not metabolized. The kidneys excrete them over hours to days. The effect on brain pressure from a bolus can last hours.", "sidefx": "The extreme concentration is the danger. Accidental undiluted injection into a peripheral vein or the wrong patient can cause sudden severe hypernatremia, brain shrinkage, bleeding in the brain and death, which is why it is a high-alert drug stored separately. Leakage into tissue can cause tissue death. Very fast rises in sodium can damage the brain through osmotic demyelination, especially in people with chronic low sodium. Hemolysis can occur near the infusion site. Fluid overload and pulmonary edema are possible. Treatment is supportive.", "fun": "23.4 percent saline is so concentrated that it is several times saltier than seawater.", "src": ["23.4% Sodium Chloride Injection USP FDA label", "Weed LH, McKibben PS. Am J Physiol 1919", "ISMP list of high-alert medications"]}, "potassium-phosphate": {"origin": "Phosphorus was discovered in 1669 by the German alchemist Hennig Brand, who boiled down urine looking for gold and found a substance that glowed in the dark. Much later, scientists learned how important phosphate is in the body. In 1929 Karl Lohmann, and separately Cyrus Fiske and Yellapragada Subbarow, discovered ATP, the cell's energy molecule, built around phosphate. Low phosphate became a recognized problem when parenteral nutrition and refeeding of starved people showed that sudden feeding can drive phosphate into cells and cause dangerous drops. Potassium phosphates injection was long used in the US without a formal approval, and FDA-approved products became available in the late 2010s. It replaces phosphate when patients cannot take it by mouth.", "timeline": [["1669", "Hennig Brand discovers phosphorus by distilling urine."], ["1929", "Lohmann, and separately Fiske and Subbarow, discover ATP."], ["1970s", "Low phosphate is recognized as a complication of parenteral nutrition and refeeding."], ["2010s", "FDA-approved potassium phosphates injection products become available."]], "cell": "Inorganic phosphate is a building block of ATP and creatine phosphate, the cell's energy currency. It is also part of DNA, RNA, cell membrane phospholipids and 2,3-diphosphoglycerate (2,3-DPG) in red cells, which helps hemoglobin release oxygen to tissues. Phosphate also buffers acid in the urine. When phosphate is low, cells cannot make enough ATP. Muscle, including the diaphragm and heart, becomes weak, and red cells release oxygen poorly. Potassium phosphates injection supplies both phosphate and potassium. Phosphate enters cells through sodium-phosphate cotransporters and refills cell stores, restoring ATP and 2,3-DPG. The added potassium refills the cell's main positive ion, which is often low at the same time.", "body": [["Muscles", "Restored ATP improves muscle strength, including the diaphragm, which can help patients wean from a ventilator."], ["Heart", "Better energy supply improves heart contraction. The potassium also affects heart rhythm."], ["Red blood cells", "More 2,3-DPG helps hemoglobin release oxygen to tissues."], ["Calcium balance", "Phosphate binds calcium. Rapid infusion can lower blood calcium and cause tetany."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys filter phosphate and reabsorb part of it in the proximal tubule. Calcium phosphate can deposit in the kidneys at high levels."], ["Nerves", "Severe low phosphate can cause confusion and seizures, which improve as levels are restored."]], "journey": "Potassium phosphates injection is diluted and infused into a vein. Phosphate and potassium move into cells, so blood phosphate can drift back down over hours after the infusion ends. The phosphate joins the body's pool inside cells and bone. The kidneys filter phosphate and potassium and excrete what is not reabsorbed. In kidney failure, both can build up.", "sidefx": "High potassium is the most dangerous risk, because rapid infusion raises blood potassium faster than cells can absorb it, which can cause arrhythmias and cardiac arrest. Phosphate binds calcium, so too much or too fast lowers blood calcium, causing tetany, and can deposit calcium phosphate in tissues, kidneys and vessels. Kidney injury can result from these deposits. Phlebitis comes from the irritating solution. There is no antidote; calcium is given for hypocalcemia or hyperkalemia effects on the heart.", "fun": "Phosphorus means light bearer in Greek, named for the eerie glow Hennig Brand saw from the substance he extracted from urine.", "src": ["Potassium Phosphates Injection FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "ASPEN Parenteral Nutrition Handbook"]}, "sodium-phosphate": {"origin": "Sodium phosphates injection supplies the same phosphate as potassium phosphates but with sodium instead of potassium, which matters when a patient has high potassium or kidney problems. Interest in phosphate replacement grew with discoveries about red cells and energy. In 1967 Reinhold and Ruth Benesch, and separately Chanutin and Curnish, showed that 2,3-diphosphoglycerate controls how readily hemoglobin releases oxygen. Doctors also learned that phosphate falls sharply during treatment of diabetic ketoacidosis and during refeeding, as insulin drives phosphate into cells. Sodium phosphates injection has been used in US hospitals for decades to correct low phosphate in patients who cannot take it by mouth.", "timeline": [["1929", "Scientists discover ATP, the cell's phosphate-based energy molecule."], ["1967", "Benesch and Benesch show 2,3-DPG controls oxygen release from hemoglobin."], ["1970s", "Low phosphate is recognized as a complication of IV feeding and diabetic ketoacidosis treatment."]], "cell": "Sodium phosphates injection provides inorganic phosphate in a mix of monobasic and dibasic salts. Inside cells, phosphate is needed to make ATP from ADP, so it powers muscles, nerves and the heart. Red cells use it to make 2,3-diphosphoglycerate, which binds hemoglobin and shifts the oxygen curve to the right, so oxygen is released more easily to tissues. Phosphate is also part of membrane phospholipids and nucleic acids and helps the kidney excrete acid. Phosphate enters cells through sodium-phosphate cotransporters. Insulin and glucose drive phosphate into cells, which is why levels drop during feeding and diabetic ketoacidosis treatment. The sodium in the product adds to the body's sodium load.", "body": [["Muscles", "ATP restoration improves weakness, including breathing muscle weakness and rhabdomyolysis risk."], ["Red blood cells", "More 2,3-DPG improves oxygen delivery and red cells become less fragile."], ["Calcium balance", "Phosphate binds calcium, so too much can lower blood calcium and cause tetany and arrhythmias."], ["Fluid and sodium", "The sodium load can cause fluid retention and raise sodium, a concern in heart failure."], ["Kidneys", "High levels can cause calcium phosphate deposits in the kidney and acute kidney injury."], ["Nerves and brain", "Correcting severe low phosphate can relieve confusion, numbness and seizures."]], "journey": "Sodium phosphates injection is diluted and given into a vein. Phosphate moves into cells and bone, so blood levels fall again within hours. Insulin, glucose and alkalosis speed this shift. The kidneys filter phosphate and reabsorb part of it, excreting the rest in urine along with the sodium. In kidney failure, phosphate builds up and dosing must be very cautious.", "sidefx": "High phosphate binds calcium in the blood, which lowers calcium and can cause tetany, seizures and heart rhythm problems. The calcium-phosphate product can rise enough to deposit crystals in kidneys, vessels and soft tissues, leading to kidney injury. The sodium load can cause high sodium and fluid overload. Phlebitis occurs from the irritating solution. There is no antidote; calcium is given for symptomatic hypocalcemia.", "fun": "Each red cell contains about as many molecules of 2,3-DPG as hemoglobin, making phosphate a key player in getting oxygen to tissues.", "src": ["Sodium Phosphates Injection FDA label", "Benesch R, Benesch RE. Biochem Biophys Res Commun 1967", "ASPEN Parenteral Nutrition Handbook"]}, "amino-acids-parenteral": {"origin": "Feeding protein by vein began in 1937, when Robert Elman at Washington University in St. Louis gave patients a protein hydrolysate (broken-down casein) by IV. Full IV nutrition seemed impossible for decades because the concentrated solutions needed destroyed small veins. In the 1960s Stanley Dudrick and Jonathan Rhoads at the University of Pennsylvania solved this by placing catheters in large central veins, where blood flow dilutes concentrated fluids quickly. In 1968 they reported that beagle puppies grew normally on IV nutrition alone, followed by a human infant. Crystalline amino acid solutions later replaced hydrolysates. Today products like Travasol, Clinisol, Trophamine and Clinimix provide the protein part of parenteral nutrition.", "timeline": [["1937", "Robert Elman gives IV protein hydrolysates to patients."], ["1968", "Dudrick and Rhoads report growth on total parenteral nutrition in puppies and an infant."], ["1970s", "Crystalline amino acid solutions replace protein hydrolysates."], ["1980s", "Pediatric amino acid formulas such as Trophamine are developed for infants."]], "cell": "Amino acid injections supply all the essential amino acids the body cannot make, plus many nonessential ones, in their L form. Cells use these to build new proteins through ribosomal synthesis, including muscle, enzymes, immune proteins and wound repair proteins. To build protein rather than burn it, the body also needs enough nonprotein energy from dextrose and lipid. When energy is adequate, nitrogen balance turns positive, meaning more protein is built than broken down. Amino acids also serve as building blocks for neurotransmitters, nucleotides, glutathione and other molecules, and can be used for gluconeogenesis. Excess amino acids are deaminated in the liver, and their nitrogen is converted to urea through the urea cycle.", "body": [["Muscles", "Muscle breakdown slows and lean body mass is better preserved in patients who cannot eat."], ["Wounds and skin", "Supplying protein supports collagen synthesis and wound healing."], ["Liver", "Long-term parenteral nutrition can cause fatty liver and cholestasis, especially in infants."], ["Kidneys and urea", "Breakdown of extra amino acids raises blood urea nitrogen, which can be a problem in kidney failure."], ["Blood sugar", "When given with dextrose, blood sugar can rise, requiring insulin."], ["Metabolism", "Refeeding a starved patient can drive phosphate, potassium and magnesium into cells, causing dangerous drops."]], "journey": "Amino acid solutions are usually mixed with dextrose, lipids, electrolytes and vitamins and infused into a vein. Concentrated mixes go through a central line, while dilute ones may use a peripheral vein. The amino acids enter cells through transporters and are used for protein synthesis or energy. The liver removes excess amino groups and makes urea, which the kidneys excrete. Effects continue only while the infusion runs.", "sidefx": "Many complications relate to the route and the mixture rather than the amino acids themselves. Catheter-related bloodstream infection happens because the nutrient-rich fluid and central line give bacteria and fungi a place to grow. Refeeding syndrome happens when nutrition drives electrolytes into cells. Raised BUN and ammonia occur when amino acid breakdown outpaces the liver and kidneys. Liver problems relate to long-term use. Precipitates of calcium and phosphate in the mixture have caused deadly lung emboli, which is why in-line filters are used.", "fun": "Stanley Dudrick's beagle puppies grew so well on IV nutrition alone that they matched their littermates who ate normally.", "src": ["Travasol (amino acids) FDA label", "Dudrick SJ, Wilmore DW, Vars HM, Rhoads JE. Surgery 1968", "ASPEN Parenteral Nutrition Handbook"]}, "chlorothiazide": {"origin": "Chlorothiazide was the first thiazide diuretic. In the 1950s a team at Merck Sharp & Dohme, including pharmacologist Karl Beyer and chemists James Sprague, Frederick Novello and John Baer, was building on sulfonamide drugs that inhibited carbonic anhydrase and caused weak diuresis. They wanted a drug that made the kidneys excrete chloride along with sodium. In 1957 they made chlorothiazide, which caused strong sodium and chloride loss. It was approved in 1958 as Diuril and quickly became one of the first well-tolerated oral drugs for high blood pressure and swelling. The team received a Lasker Award in 1975. Today the IV form, Sodium Diuril, is used in hospitals, often with loop diuretics.", "timeline": [["1957", "Merck scientists synthesize chlorothiazide."], ["1958", "FDA approves chlorothiazide (Diuril)."], ["1975", "Beyer, Sprague, Novello and Baer receive a Lasker Award for thiazide diuretics."]], "cell": "Chlorothiazide is secreted into the proximal tubule by organic anion transporters and travels with the urine to the distal convoluted tubule. There it binds and blocks the sodium-chloride cotransporter (NCC, SLC12A3) on the inner (luminal) membrane. Normally NCC reabsorbs about 5 to 10 percent of filtered sodium. With it blocked, more sodium, chloride and water stay in the urine. More sodium then reaches the collecting duct, where ENaC reabsorbs it in exchange for potassium and hydrogen ions, so potassium and acid are lost (hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis). Lower sodium inside the distal cells boosts calcium reabsorption through the basolateral sodium-calcium exchanger, reducing urine calcium. It also mildly inhibits carbonic anhydrase.", "body": [["Kidneys", "More sodium, chloride and water are lost in urine, which reduces fluid volume and swelling."], ["Blood pressure", "Lower blood volume first, and later reduced vascular resistance, bring blood pressure down."], ["Potassium and acid", "More sodium reaching the collecting duct drives potassium and hydrogen loss, causing low potassium and metabolic alkalosis."], ["Calcium", "Calcium reabsorption increases, lowering urine calcium and sometimes raising blood calcium."], ["Uric acid", "It competes with uric acid for secretion and increases reabsorption, which can trigger gout."], ["Blood sugar and lipids", "Low potassium reduces insulin release, raising blood sugar. Lipids may also rise."]], "journey": "IV chlorothiazide acts within about 15 minutes and peaks in about 30 minutes, with effects lasting about two hours. By mouth it is absorbed incompletely. It is not metabolized. It is secreted into the tubule by organic anion transporters and leaves unchanged in the urine. Its half-life is about 45 to 120 minutes. It must reach the tubule lumen to work, so it is less effective in severe kidney failure.", "sidefx": "Most side effects follow from its site of action. Extra sodium delivery to the collecting duct causes potassium and magnesium loss and metabolic alkalosis. Low sodium occurs because it blocks dilution of urine in the distal tubule while thirst and ADH continue. Higher calcium, uric acid and glucose follow from changes in tubular transport and insulin release. Fluid loss can cause dizziness and low blood pressure. Low potassium can trigger arrhythmias, especially with digoxin. As a sulfonamide, it can cause allergic reactions. There is no antidote; electrolytes are replaced.", "fun": "Chlorothiazide was the parent of the thiazides. Hydrochlorothiazide, one of the most prescribed drugs in the world, was introduced shortly after as a more potent relative.", "src": ["Sodium Diuril (chlorothiazide sodium) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "alvimopan": {"origin": "Alvimopan was built to solve a frustrating problem after bowel surgery. Opioids given for pain, plus the stress of surgery itself, make the gut stop moving for days, a state called postoperative ileus. Chemists at Eli Lilly first made the molecule in the 1990s as a mu-opioid blocker that was too large and too charged to cross into the brain. It was later licensed to Adolor Corporation, which developed it with GlaxoSmithKline. The FDA approved it as Entereg in 2008 for short-term hospital use after partial bowel resection. Because a long-term study in patients with chronic opioid constipation showed more heart attacks, it was approved only for short courses and only through hospitals enrolled in a restricted program.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Eli Lilly chemists create alvimopan as a gut-restricted opioid antagonist."], ["2008", "FDA approves Entereg for postoperative ileus after bowel resection, with a restricted hospital program."]], "cell": "Alvimopan is a competitive antagonist at mu-opioid receptors. These receptors sit on neurons of the enteric nervous system, especially the myenteric plexus. When an opioid activates them, Gi/o signaling lowers cAMP, opens potassium channels and closes calcium channels, so the neurons release less acetylcholine. Less acetylcholine means weaker coordinated contractions and more fluid absorption, which stalls transit. Alvimopan binds the same receptors with high affinity and keeps opioids from activating them. Its zwitterionic structure and size keep it from crossing the blood-brain barrier, so receptors in the brain and spinal cord stay available to the opioid and pain relief is kept. Gut bacteria convert some of it to an active amide metabolite that also blocks the receptor.", "body": [["Stomach and small bowel", "With enteric mu receptors blocked, normal propulsive waves come back sooner, so patients tolerate solid food earlier after surgery."], ["Colon", "Restored motility shortens the time to the first bowel movement, which is a main marker of recovered gut function."], ["Brain and spinal cord", "Very little drug reaches the central nervous system, so opioid pain relief and its central effects are not reversed."], ["Heart", "A higher rate of heart attacks was seen with long-term use in a chronic opioid study, which is why use is limited to a short hospital course."], ["Gut in opioid-tolerant patients", "People already used to opioids can get cramping and diarrhea because suddenly blocking gut receptors acts like a local withdrawal."]], "journey": "Alvimopan is a capsule taken by mouth before surgery and continued for a short time after. Only a small portion is absorbed, which suits a drug meant to work on the gut wall. It reaches its peak in about two hours. In the intestine, gut bacteria hydrolyze it to an active amide metabolite, so antibiotics or bowel preparation can change how much metabolite forms. Most of the drug leaves in bile and feces, with a small part in the urine. The parent drug has a terminal half-life of about 10 to 17 hours.", "sidefx": "The main concern is myocardial infarction, seen in a long-term trial in people taking opioids for chronic pain. The reason is not fully understood, but it led to a boxed warning and a restricted hospital-only program limited to a short course. Dyspepsia, back pain, low potassium and urinary retention are common reported effects. In people already tolerant to opioids, blocking gut receptors can trigger cramping and diarrhea, a local withdrawal-like effect. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Alvimopan was among the first drugs designed on purpose to block opioid effects in the gut while leaving pain relief in the brain untouched, a class now called PAMORAs.", "src": ["Entereg (alvimopan) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "cefotetan": {"origin": "Cefotetan belongs to the cephamycins, a branch of the cephalosporin family that came from soil bacteria. In the early 1970s researchers at Merck found cephamycin C, a beta-lactam made by Streptomyces bacteria that carried an extra methoxy group which protected it from many beta-lactamase enzymes. That idea led to cefoxitin and later to cefotetan, which was developed by the Japanese company Yamanouchi and introduced in the US in the 1980s as Cefotan. It became popular for surgical prophylaxis and pelvic infections because it covers gut anaerobes such as Bacteroides fragilis and lasts long enough for less frequent dosing.", "timeline": [["1945", "Giuseppe Brotzu isolates the cephalosporin-producing mold in Sardinia."], ["1970s", "Merck researchers describe cephamycins from Streptomyces with a protective methoxy group."], ["1980s", "Cefotetan is introduced in the US as Cefotan."]], "cell": "Cefotetan is a beta-lactam that binds penicillin-binding proteins, the transpeptidases that cross-link peptidoglycan strands in the bacterial cell wall. The beta-lactam ring mimics the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of the wall precursor, so the enzyme acylates itself on the drug and is inactivated. Without cross-links, the wall weakens while autolysins keep cutting it, and the growing cell bursts. The 7-alpha-methoxy group shields the ring from many beta-lactamases, including those of Bacteroides fragilis. Its N-methylthiotetrazole (NMTT) side chain is the chemical reason for two off-target effects: it inhibits vitamin K epoxide reductase in the liver and blocks aldehyde dehydrogenase.", "body": [["Bacteria", "Gram-negative rods, streptococci and gut anaerobes are killed as their cell walls fail; MRSA, enterococci and Pseudomonas are not covered."], ["Blood clotting", "The NMTT side chain lowers recycling of vitamin K, so clotting factors II, VII, IX and X fall and the INR can rise, especially in malnourished patients."], ["Red blood cells", "Cefotetan can coat red cells and trigger antibody-mediated hemolysis, which is more common with this drug than with most cephalosporins and can be fatal."], ["Liver and alcohol metabolism", "Blocking aldehyde dehydrogenase lets acetaldehyde build up after alcohol, causing flushing, nausea and racing heart (a disulfiram-like reaction)."], ["Gut flora", "Broad killing of normal colon bacteria makes room for Clostridioides difficile."]], "journey": "Cefotetan is given IV or by deep IM injection because it is not absorbed by mouth. It is highly protein bound and spreads into most tissues, including pelvic and abdominal sites. It is barely metabolized, though part of it changes into a tautomer form in the body. The kidneys clear most of it unchanged, with some in bile. Its half-life of about 3 to 4.6 hours is longer than many cephalosporins, which allows twice-daily dosing, and kidney disease slows its removal.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea and C. difficile infection come from wiping out normal gut flora. The NMTT side chain explains low prothrombin and bleeding, because it blocks vitamin K recycling, and vitamin K can correct it. The same side chain causes the disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol. Immune hemolytic anemia happens when the drug binds red cells and antibodies attack them; it can be severe and is a classic reason to stop the drug. Allergy is shared with other beta-lactams. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Cefotetan, cefoxitin and cefazolin are a well-known look-alike, sound-alike trio, even though they cover quite different bacteria.", "src": ["Cefotetan for injection FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "cefotaxime": {"origin": "Cefotaxime traces back to 1945, when the Italian scientist Giuseppe Brotzu found that a mold growing near a sewage outlet in Sardinia, later called Cephalosporium (now Acremonium), made a substance that killed bacteria. Oxford chemists then isolated cephalosporin C, whose core could be modified. In the 1970s chemists at Hoechst in Germany, working with Roussel Uclaf, added an aminothiazole ring and a methoxyimino group to that core. The result, cefotaxime, was the first third-generation cephalosporin, stable against many beta-lactamases and able to reach the cerebrospinal fluid. It was approved in the US as Claforan in 1981 and became a standard treatment for meningitis and newborn infections. The Claforan brand has since been discontinued, but generic cefotaxime remains.", "timeline": [["1945", "Giuseppe Brotzu discovers a cephalosporin-producing mold in Sardinia."], ["1950s", "Oxford researchers isolate cephalosporin C."], ["1970s", "Hoechst and Roussel Uclaf develop cefotaxime."], ["1981", "FDA approves Claforan, the first third-generation cephalosporin in the US."]], "cell": "Cefotaxime acylates penicillin-binding proteins, mainly PBP1a, PBP1b and PBP3. These enzymes perform the last cross-linking step in peptidoglycan synthesis. The drug's beta-lactam ring looks like the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of the wall building block, so the enzyme binds it and becomes stuck. Without cross-links the wall cannot hold the cell's internal pressure, autolysins keep breaking bonds, and the bacterium lyses. Killing depends on the time drug levels stay above the MIC. The methoxyimino side chain blocks many common beta-lactamases, but extended-spectrum beta-lactamases (ESBLs) and AmpC enzymes can still destroy it.", "body": [["Bacteria", "Kills Streptococcus pneumoniae, Neisseria, Haemophilus and many Enterobacterales; it has no reliable activity against Pseudomonas, enterococci, MRSA or Listeria."], ["Brain and meninges", "Inflamed meninges let enough drug into the cerebrospinal fluid to treat bacterial meningitis."], ["Newborns", "Unlike ceftriaxone, it does not push bilirubin off albumin and is not linked to calcium precipitates, which made it a classic choice in neonates."], ["Gut flora", "Killing normal bowel bacteria can allow C. difficile overgrowth and diarrhea."], ["Blood and kidneys", "Rarely it causes low white cells, eosinophilia or allergic interstitial nephritis, which are immune reactions."], ["Heart", "Very rapid bolus through a central line has been linked to arrhythmias."]], "journey": "Cefotaxime is given IV or IM because the gut does not absorb it. It spreads into body fluids and crosses inflamed meninges. The liver removes an acetyl group to make desacetylcefotaxime, which is also active and works together with the parent drug. Both are cleared by the kidneys. The parent drug has a short half-life of about one hour, so it is given several times a day, and the half-life is longer in newborns and in kidney failure.", "sidefx": "Pain or phlebitis at the injection site comes from local irritation. Rash and anaphylaxis are immune reactions to the beta-lactam structure. Diarrhea and C. difficile colitis come from disrupting normal gut bacteria. Neutropenia, eosinophilia, interstitial nephritis and a positive Coombs test are immune-mediated and usually reverse when the drug is stopped. Arrhythmia has been reported after very fast central injection. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Brotzu found his mold near a sewage outlet because he reasoned that seawater there seemed to clean itself of germs faster than expected.", "src": ["Cefotaxime for injection FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "meropenem-vaborbactam": {"origin": "This combination pairs an older antibiotic with a new protector. The carbapenem family began with thienamycin, which Merck scientists found in 1976 in the soil bacterium Streptomyces cattleya. Meropenem, a more stable carbapenem developed by Sumitomo in Japan, was approved in the US in 1996. By the 2000s, Klebsiella strains carrying the KPC carbapenemase were spreading and destroying carbapenems. Scientists at Rempex Pharmaceuticals, part of The Medicines Company, designed vaborbactam, a cyclic boronic acid that traps serine beta-lactamases such as KPC. The FDA approved the pair as Vabomere in 2017 for complicated urinary tract infections.", "timeline": [["1976", "Merck scientists discover thienamycin, the first carbapenem, from Streptomyces cattleya."], ["1996", "FDA approves meropenem in the US."], ["2017", "FDA approves Vabomere (meropenem-vaborbactam)."]], "cell": "Meropenem binds penicillin-binding proteins, especially PBP2 and PBP3, and blocks the transpeptidation that cross-links peptidoglycan. The weakened wall bursts as autolysins act, so the bacterium dies. KPC enzymes are serine beta-lactamases that attack the beta-lactam ring with an active-site serine. Vaborbactam is not a beta-lactam. Its boron atom forms a reversible covalent bond with that serine, mimicking the transition state, and holds the enzyme in an inactive state. This protects meropenem from Ambler class A (including KPC) and class C enzymes. Vaborbactam does nothing against metallo-beta-lactamases (NDM, VIM, IMP), which use zinc instead of serine, or against OXA-48.", "body": [["Bacteria", "KPC-producing Klebsiella and other Enterobacterales become susceptible again because their carbapenemase is blocked."], ["Kidneys and urinary tract", "Both drugs are cleared by the kidneys and reach high levels in urine, which suits kidney and bladder infections."], ["Brain", "Carbapenems can lower the seizure threshold, probably by interfering with GABA-A signaling, though meropenem does this less than imipenem."], ["Gut flora", "Broad killing of gut bacteria can lead to diarrhea and C. difficile colitis."], ["Veins", "The long infusion can irritate the vein and cause phlebitis."]], "journey": "Both parts are given together as an IV infusion over about three hours, which keeps meropenem above the MIC for longer. Meropenem is barely protein bound, while vaborbactam is about one-third bound. Meropenem is partly broken to an inactive open-ring form; vaborbactam is not metabolized. Both leave mainly through the kidneys, each with a half-life of about one to two hours, so doses are adjusted in kidney disease.", "sidefx": "Headache, phlebitis, nausea and diarrhea are the most common effects. Diarrhea and C. difficile reflect disruption of normal flora. Seizures can occur, especially in kidney failure or with brain disease, because carbapenems interfere with inhibitory GABA signaling. Allergy is possible in people with beta-lactam allergy. Meropenem also sharply lowers valproic acid levels, which can lead to breakthrough seizures. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Vaborbactam was the first boron-based beta-lactamase inhibitor approved, using a chemical trick that resembles how some cancer drugs trap enzymes.", "src": ["Vabomere (meropenem and vaborbactam) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "imipenem-relebactam": {"origin": "Imipenem was the first carbapenem used in patients. Merck scientists found thienamycin in 1976 in Streptomyces cattleya, but it was too unstable to make into a drug. They created imipenem, a stable derivative, and found that a kidney enzyme called dehydropeptidase-I broke it down. Their answer was to add cilastatin, which blocks that enzyme, and Primaxin (imipenem-cilastatin) was approved in 1985. Decades later, carbapenem-resistant bacteria made imipenem less reliable. Merck then added relebactam, a diazabicyclooctane beta-lactamase inhibitor, and the three-drug combination was approved as Recarbrio in 2019.", "timeline": [["1976", "Merck scientists discover thienamycin from Streptomyces cattleya."], ["1985", "FDA approves Primaxin (imipenem-cilastatin), the first carbapenem."], ["2019", "FDA approves Recarbrio (imipenem, cilastatin and relebactam)."], ["2020", "Recarbrio gains approval for hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia."]], "cell": "Each of the three parts has a separate job. Imipenem binds penicillin-binding proteins, especially PBP2, and stops peptidoglycan cross-linking, so the bacterial cell wall fails and the cell bursts. Cilastatin has no antibacterial effect; it blocks renal dehydropeptidase-I on the brush border of proximal tubule cells, which would otherwise break down imipenem and leave little active drug in the urine. Relebactam is a non-beta-lactam inhibitor that forms a reversible covalent bond with the active serine of Ambler class A enzymes (including KPC) and class C AmpC enzymes. It also helps against Pseudomonas that combines AmpC with porin loss. It does not block metallo-beta-lactamases or OXA-48.", "body": [["Bacteria", "Many imipenem-resistant Enterobacterales and Pseudomonas aeruginosa become treatable because their serine beta-lactamases are inhibited."], ["Kidneys", "Cilastatin protects imipenem from tubular breakdown, which keeps urine levels up and avoids tubular toxicity from breakdown products."], ["Brain", "Imipenem has the highest seizure risk among carbapenems, linked to blocking GABA-A receptors, especially at high levels or in kidney failure."], ["Gut flora", "Broad activity disturbs normal bowel bacteria, so diarrhea and C. difficile can follow."], ["Blood", "Anemia and low potassium have been reported in treated patients."]], "journey": "The three drugs are given together by IV infusion. Imipenem and relebactam each have a half-life of about one hour and are mostly cleared by the kidneys. Without cilastatin, kidney dehydropeptidase-I would hydrolyze much of the imipenem. Cilastatin is more protein bound than the other two. Because all three depend on the kidneys, doses are adjusted when kidney function falls.", "sidefx": "Seizures are the classic concern with imipenem and come from interference with inhibitory GABA-A signaling in the brain; the risk rises with kidney failure and brain lesions. Diarrhea and C. difficile colitis reflect loss of normal flora. Phlebitis is local irritation. Cross-allergy with other beta-lactams is possible. Like other carbapenems it lowers valproic acid levels and can cause breakthrough seizures. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Cilastatin is one of the few drugs added to a product only to protect another drug from the patient's own kidney enzyme.", "src": ["Recarbrio (imipenem, cilastatin and relebactam) FDA label", "Primaxin FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "sulbactam-durlobactam": {"origin": "Sulbactam was made by Pfizer in the late 1970s as a beta-lactamase inhibitor and was paired with ampicillin as Unasyn, approved in the US in 1986. Doctors later noticed that sulbactam itself kills Acinetobacter baumannii, a hospital germ that often resists nearly everything. But Acinetobacter makes OXA-type enzymes that destroy sulbactam. Scientists at Entasis Therapeutics, a company spun out of AstraZeneca, designed durlobactam, a diazabicyclooctane inhibitor that blocks those enzymes. After a trial in patients with carbapenem-resistant Acinetobacter infections, the FDA approved the combination as Xacduro in 2023 for hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia caused by this organism.", "timeline": [["1986", "FDA approves ampicillin-sulbactam (Unasyn)."], ["2023", "FDA approves Xacduro (sulbactam-durlobactam) for Acinetobacter pneumonia."]], "cell": "In most bacteria sulbactam acts only as a decoy for beta-lactamases. In Acinetobacter it binds PBP1 and PBP3, the transpeptidases that build and divide the cell wall, so it is a true antibiotic against this organism and kills it. Acinetobacter fights back with beta-lactamases, especially class D OXA carbapenemases plus class A and C enzymes. Durlobactam forms a reversible covalent bond with the active serine of these class A, C and D enzymes, keeping sulbactam intact. Durlobactam also binds PBP2 in Acinetobacter, adding to the effect. Neither part works against metallo-beta-lactamases, which use zinc instead of serine.", "body": [["Lungs", "Treats pneumonia caused by carbapenem-resistant Acinetobacter baumannii-calcoaceticus complex, which often leaves few other options."], ["Bacteria", "The activity is narrow; it is designed for Acinetobacter, so it is typically given with another drug such as imipenem-cilastatin to cover other germs."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises are among the most common lab changes."], ["Blood and electrolytes", "Anemia and low potassium were seen in treated critically ill patients."], ["Gut flora", "Diarrhea and C. difficile can follow broad antibiotic use."]], "journey": "Both drugs are given together by IV infusion. They are lightly metabolized and are cleared mainly by the kidneys, each with a half-life of about two hours. Kidney function changes doses, and patients with augmented renal clearance can need more frequent dosing. Both reach the lining fluid of the lungs, where the infection lives.", "sidefx": "Liver enzyme rises, diarrhea, anemia and low potassium were the most common findings in trials. C. difficile reflects disruption of normal gut bacteria. As beta-lactam-type molecules, both can trigger allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, in people allergic to beta-lactams. Arrhythmias were reported in very sick trial patients. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Sulbactam spent decades known only as a helper drug before clinicians realized it was one of the few beta-lactams that kills Acinetobacter on its own.", "src": ["Xacduro (sulbactam and durlobactam) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "aztreonam-avibactam": {"origin": "Aztreonam came from a 1981 Squibb discovery of monobactams, single-ring beta-lactams made by soil bacteria such as Chromobacterium violaceum. Squibb made a synthetic version, aztreonam, approved as Azactam in 1986. Aztreonam has an unusual strength: metallo-beta-lactamases such as NDM cannot destroy it. The problem was that the same bacteria usually also carry other enzymes that do. Avibactam, a diazabicyclooctane inhibitor, was already in use with ceftazidime (Avycaz, 2015). Pfizer and AbbVie paired it with aztreonam so that avibactam blocks the other enzymes while aztreonam escapes the metallo-enzyme. Approved in Europe in 2024, it was approved by the FDA as Emblaveo in 2025 for complicated intra-abdominal infections, used with metronidazole.", "timeline": [["1981", "Squibb scientists report monobactams from soil bacteria."], ["1986", "FDA approves aztreonam (Azactam)."], ["2015", "Avibactam is first approved, paired with ceftazidime."], ["2025", "FDA approves Emblaveo (aztreonam-avibactam)."]], "cell": "Aztreonam binds penicillin-binding protein 3 of gram-negative rods and blocks cell wall cross-linking at the division site, so cells form long filaments and then lyse. Metallo-beta-lactamases (class B: NDM, VIM, IMP) use zinc to break beta-lactam rings, but they do not hydrolyze aztreonam well. Bacteria that carry metallo-enzymes usually also make serine enzymes such as ESBLs, AmpC, KPC or OXA-48, which do destroy aztreonam. Avibactam forms a reversible covalent bond with the active serine of those class A, class C and some class D enzymes. The combination lets aztreonam reach its PBP3 target intact in strains that defeat almost every other beta-lactam.", "body": [["Bacteria", "Active against Enterobacterales that make metallo-beta-lactamases plus serine enzymes; it has no useful activity against gram-positive bacteria or anaerobes."], ["Abdomen", "Used for complicated intra-abdominal infection, with metronidazole added to cover anaerobes."], ["Liver", "Raised liver enzymes and liver injury have been reported, so liver tests are watched."], ["Blood", "Anemia and changes in platelet counts were reported in trials."], ["Gut flora", "Diarrhea and C. difficile can follow disruption of normal bowel bacteria."]], "journey": "Both drugs are given together by IV infusion. Neither is much metabolized. Both are cleared by the kidneys, each with a half-life of about two hours, so doses depend on kidney function. Aztreonam is about half protein bound, avibactam very little.", "sidefx": "Anemia, diarrhea, nausea, raised liver enzymes and infusion site reactions are the common effects. Liver injury is a labeled concern and is watched with blood tests. C. difficile comes from loss of normal gut bacteria. Aztreonam rarely cross-reacts with penicillin allergy, but it shares a side chain with ceftazidime, so people allergic to ceftazidime can react. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Before this product existed, clinicians treating metallo-beta-lactamase infections would sometimes give aztreonam and ceftazidime-avibactam side by side just to combine aztreonam with avibactam.", "src": ["Emblaveo (aztreonam and avibactam) FDA label", "Azactam (aztreonam) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "plazomicin": {"origin": "Plazomicin is a modern answer to an old drug class. Aminoglycosides began with streptomycin, isolated in 1943 by Albert Schatz in Selman Waksman's lab at Rutgers from Streptomyces griseus. Over time bacteria learned to disable aminoglycosides with modifying enzymes that add acetyl, phosphate or nucleotide groups. Scientists at Achaogen, a California company, started from sisomicin, a natural aminoglycoside from Micromonospora bacteria, and changed its structure so most of those enzymes could no longer attach. The FDA approved it as Zemdri in 2018 for complicated urinary tract infections. Achaogen went bankrupt in 2019, and the drug's rights were later bought by Cipla.", "timeline": [["1943", "Albert Schatz and Selman Waksman isolate streptomycin."], ["2018", "FDA approves Zemdri (plazomicin) for complicated urinary tract infections."], ["2019", "Achaogen files for bankruptcy; plazomicin rights later go to Cipla."]], "cell": "Plazomicin enters gram-negative bacteria in steps. Its positive charges let it cross the outer membrane, and then an oxygen-dependent transport process pulls it across the inner membrane, which is why aminoglycosides do not work against anaerobes. Inside, it binds the A site of 16S rRNA in the 30S ribosomal subunit. This causes misreading of mRNA, so faulty proteins are made and inserted into the membrane, making it leakier and pulling in more drug. Killing is fast and concentration dependent, with a post-antibiotic effect. Its modified structure blocks most aminoglycoside-modifying enzymes, but bacteria with 16S rRNA methyltransferases, which change the binding site, remain resistant.", "body": [["Bacteria", "Kills many ESBL-producing and carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales that resist older aminoglycosides."], ["Kidneys", "Proximal tubule cells take up the drug through megalin receptors and are injured, so kidney function can fall."], ["Inner ear", "Hair cells in the cochlea and vestibular system take up aminoglycosides and can be permanently damaged, causing hearing loss, ringing or imbalance."], ["Neuromuscular junction", "It reduces presynaptic acetylcholine release, which can cause weakness and, rarely, breathing paralysis."], ["Fetus", "Aminoglycosides cross the placenta and can cause deafness in the baby."]], "journey": "Plazomicin is given once daily by IV infusion because it is not absorbed from the gut. It stays mostly in the blood and extracellular fluid. It is not metabolized and is cleared almost entirely by the kidneys unchanged, with a half-life of about 3.5 hours. Blood level monitoring is used in some patients, and dosing depends on kidney function.", "sidefx": "Kidney injury happens because tubule cells actively take up the drug, which damages their lysosomes and mitochondria. Ototoxicity comes from drug building up in inner ear hair cells and creating free radicals; it can be permanent. Neuromuscular blockade occurs because aminoglycosides compete with calcium at nerve endings; calcium can help reverse it. Plazomicin has a boxed warning for nephrotoxicity, ototoxicity, neuromuscular blockade and fetal harm. There is no specific antidote; hemodialysis can remove it.", "fun": "Once-daily aminoglycoside dosing works because of the post-antibiotic effect: bacteria stay suppressed for hours after levels fall.", "src": ["Zemdri (plazomicin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Schatz A, Bugie E, Waksman SA. Proc Soc Exp Biol Med, 1944"]}, "eravacycline": {"origin": "Tetracyclines began in 1948, when Benjamin Duggar at Lederle Laboratories described chlortetracycline from the golden soil mold Streptomyces aureofaciens. For decades every tetracycline was made by tweaking natural fermentation products. Bacteria fought back with efflux pumps and ribosome-protection proteins. In the 2000s chemist Andrew Myers and his group at Harvard worked out a way to build tetracyclines completely from scratch, which allowed changes that fermentation chemistry could not make. Tetraphase Pharmaceuticals used this platform to create eravacycline, a fluorocycline with a fluorine at C-7 and a pyrrolidinoacetamido group at C-9. The FDA approved it as Xerava in 2018 for complicated intra-abdominal infections.", "timeline": [["1948", "Benjamin Duggar reports chlortetracycline from Streptomyces aureofaciens."], ["2000s", "Andrew Myers' lab at Harvard develops a fully synthetic route to tetracyclines."], ["2018", "FDA approves Xerava (eravacycline) for complicated intra-abdominal infections."]], "cell": "Eravacycline enters bacteria and binds the 30S ribosomal subunit. It sits where aminoacyl-tRNA must dock at the A site, so new amino acids cannot be added to the growing protein chain. Protein synthesis stalls and the bacterium stops growing, which is mainly a bacteriostatic effect. Older tetracyclines fail when bacteria pump them out with Tet efflux proteins or knock them off the ribosome with ribosomal protection proteins such as TetM. Eravacycline's C-7 fluorine and C-9 side chain help it bind more tightly and escape both mechanisms. Because it targets ribosomes rather than cell walls, beta-lactamases, including carbapenemases, do not affect it.", "body": [["Bacteria", "Covers ESBL and carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales, Acinetobacter, anaerobes, MRSA and VRE, but not Pseudomonas aeruginosa."], ["Abdomen", "Good tissue penetration supports its use for mixed infections inside the abdomen."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are common, similar to other tetracycline-class drugs."], ["Teeth and bones", "Like other tetracyclines it binds calcium and can stain developing teeth and slow bone growth in fetuses and young children."], ["Pancreas and liver", "Pancreatitis and liver enzyme rises have been reported with the class."], ["Veins", "Infusion site reactions and phlebitis are among the most common effects."]], "journey": "Eravacycline is given only by IV infusion. It is highly protein bound and spreads widely into tissues. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP3A4 and flavin monooxygenase, so strong CYP3A4 inducers lower its levels. About half leaves in feces and about one-third in urine. Its long half-life of about 20 hours supports twice-daily dosing.", "sidefx": "Nausea and vomiting reflect local and central gut effects common to tetracyclines. Diarrhea and C. difficile come from changes in gut flora. Tooth discoloration and slowed bone growth happen because the drug binds calcium in mineralizing tissue, which is why it is avoided in late pregnancy and young children. Photosensitivity, pancreatitis and liver injury are class effects. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Eravacycline was among the first antibiotics in its class made by total chemical synthesis rather than by modifying a fermentation product.", "src": ["Xerava (eravacycline) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "anidulafungin": {"origin": "Anidulafungin comes from a fungus that fights other fungi. In the 1970s researchers found echinocandin B, a lipopeptide made by Aspergillus species, including Aspergillus nidulans. It killed Candida but also broke open red blood cells because of its natural fatty tail. Chemists at Eli Lilly replaced that tail with a different side chain, which removed most of the hemolysis. The compound passed through Versicor and Vicuron Pharmaceuticals and then to Pfizer, which brought it to market. The FDA approved it as Eraxis in 2006, making it the third echinocandin after caspofungin and micafungin.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Echinocandin B is discovered in Aspergillus fermentation."], ["2006", "FDA approves Eraxis (anidulafungin) for candidemia and esophageal candidiasis."]], "cell": "Fungal cell walls are held together by beta-(1,3)-D-glucan, a sugar polymer that human cells do not make. Anidulafungin noncompetitively inhibits the enzyme that builds it, beta-(1,3)-D-glucan synthase, by binding its FKS1 subunit in the fungal membrane. Without new glucan, the wall weakens, especially at growing tips and budding sites. The fungus can no longer resist its own internal pressure and swells and bursts. This is fungicidal against Candida. Against Aspergillus, which grows only at hyphal tips, it stops growth (fungistatic) instead. Mutations in FKS1 hot spots cause echinocandin resistance.", "body": [["Candida", "Candida cells lyse because their walls fail, which is why it is used for candidemia and invasive candidiasis."], ["Esophagus", "Treats Candida esophagitis when oral options are not suitable."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise and, rarely, hepatic dysfunction occurs, so liver tests are followed."], ["Infusion-related", "Rapid infusion can release histamine, causing flushing, rash, itching or low blood pressure."], ["Electrolytes", "Low potassium is among the reported effects."]], "journey": "Anidulafungin is given by IV infusion because it is not absorbed by mouth. It is more than 99 percent protein bound and spreads into tissues. Unusually, it is not broken down by liver enzymes. Instead it slowly degrades chemically in the blood to an inactive open-ring peptide, which is then cleared in feces. Very little appears in urine. Its long half-life of about 40 to 50 hours allows once-daily dosing, and neither kidney nor liver disease requires dose changes.", "sidefx": "Because humans lack glucan synthase, side effects are mild compared with older antifungals. Histamine release during fast infusion explains flushing, rash and low blood pressure, so slower infusion helps. Liver enzyme elevations and rare hepatic dysfunction can occur. Diarrhea, nausea and low potassium are common reports. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "The echinocandins have been called a penicillin for fungi, because they attack a cell wall that human cells simply do not have.", "src": ["Eraxis (anidulafungin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "rezafungin": {"origin": "Rezafungin was designed to make echinocandins last longer. Scientists at Cidara Therapeutics in San Diego started from anidulafungin and added a choline group to its side chain. This change made the molecule far more chemically stable, so it no longer broke down quickly in the blood. The result was a drug with a half-life of several days that can be given once a week. The FDA approved it as Rezzayo in 2023 for candidemia and invasive candidiasis in adults with limited or no other options.", "timeline": [["2006", "Anidulafungin, the parent molecule, is approved in the US."], ["2023", "FDA approves Rezzayo (rezafungin), a once-weekly echinocandin."]], "cell": "Like other echinocandins, rezafungin inhibits beta-(1,3)-D-glucan synthase by binding its FKS subunit in the fungal cell membrane. This stops production of beta-(1,3)-D-glucan, a key structural polymer of the fungal wall that human cells lack. With the wall weakened, Candida cells cannot hold their internal pressure and burst. The choline-modified side chain does not change the target but resists the chemical ring-opening that degrades anidulafungin. This keeps levels high, and killing is concentration dependent, so large peaks early in treatment are thought to help.", "body": [["Candida", "High, sustained levels kill Candida in the bloodstream and deep tissues."], ["Skin", "Photosensitivity was seen with rezafungin, so sun exposure can cause skin reactions."], ["Infusion-related", "Flushing, warmth, low blood pressure and chest tightness can occur during infusion, likely from histamine release."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises have been reported, as with other echinocandins, so liver tests are followed."], ["Electrolytes and blood", "Low potassium, low magnesium and anemia were common in trials of very sick patients."], ["Nerves", "Tremor appeared in animal studies at high exposure and is noted as a potential risk."]], "journey": "Rezafungin is given by IV infusion once a week. It is more than 97 percent protein bound. It is chemically stable and is only minimally metabolized. Most of it leaves slowly in feces. Its half-life of about 130 to 150 hours is what allows weekly dosing, and it has few drug interactions because it is not a CYP substrate.", "sidefx": "Because the target is absent in human cells, most toxicity is mild. Infusion reactions likely reflect histamine release. Photosensitivity is a labeled warning. Liver test changes can occur, as with other echinocandins. Tremor was seen in animal studies at high levels. Low potassium, fever and gut upset are common in hospitalized patients receiving it. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "A single weekly rezafungin infusion can replace seven daily infusions of an older echinocandin.", "src": ["Rezzayo (rezafungin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "cidofovir": {"origin": "Cidofovir came from a famous partnership between two chemists. Antonin Holy at the Institute of Organic Chemistry and Biochemistry in Prague designed acyclic nucleoside phosphonates, molecules that look like nucleotides but already carry a stable phosphate-like group. Erik De Clercq at the Rega Institute in Belgium tested them against viruses and found strong activity against herpesviruses. Gilead Sciences developed cidofovir, and the FDA approved it as Vistide in 1996 for cytomegalovirus (CMV) retinitis in people with AIDS. The same chemical family later produced tenofovir, a backbone of HIV treatment.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Antonin Holy and Erik De Clercq develop acyclic nucleoside phosphonates."], ["1996", "FDA approves Vistide (cidofovir) for CMV retinitis in AIDS."]], "cell": "Many antivirals, such as ganciclovir and acyclovir, need a viral kinase to add the first phosphate. Cidofovir already carries a phosphonate group, so it skips that step. Host cell kinases add two more phosphates, making cidofovir diphosphate. This active form competes with deoxycytidine triphosphate for the viral DNA polymerase (UL54 in CMV) and can be built into the growing viral DNA, slowing further chain elongation. Viral polymerase prefers it over human polymerases, which gives selectivity. Because it does not need viral UL97 or thymidine kinase, it stays active against ganciclovir-resistant CMV and acyclovir-resistant herpes simplex and varicella-zoster. The diphosphate lasts for days inside cells.", "body": [["Eyes", "Stops CMV from destroying the retina in people with AIDS; it can also lower eye pressure and cause inflammation of the iris and uvea."], ["Kidneys", "Proximal tubule cells take up cidofovir through the OAT1 transporter and are damaged, causing protein in urine, rising creatinine and sometimes Fanconi syndrome."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia can occur, so blood counts are checked."], ["Blood chemistry", "Tubular injury can cause metabolic acidosis from bicarbonate loss."], ["Whole body", "Nausea, fever and rash are common, and many are linked to probenecid given with it."]], "journey": "Cidofovir is given by IV infusion because it is poorly absorbed by mouth. It is barely protein bound and is not metabolized by the liver. Cells convert it to the active diphosphate. The kidneys clear the parent drug unchanged through filtration and secretion by OAT1, with a plasma half-life of about 2.6 hours, but the active diphosphate lasts for days inside cells, which allows maintenance doses every two weeks. Probenecid is given with each dose to block OAT1 and reduce kidney uptake, and IV saline is given to help protect the kidneys.", "sidefx": "Nephrotoxicity is the main danger and carries a boxed warning. Proximal tubule cells actively take up cidofovir through OAT1 and the drug builds up and injures them, which explains protein in the urine, Fanconi syndrome and acute kidney failure. Probenecid and IV fluids reduce this. Neutropenia reflects effects on dividing marrow cells. Ocular hypotony and uveitis reflect injury to the eye's fluid-producing tissues. Animal studies showed carcinogenic and fertility effects. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Holy and De Clercq's acyclic nucleoside phosphonates led to cidofovir, adefovir and tenofovir, one of the most productive chemistry partnerships in antiviral history.", "src": ["Vistide (cidofovir) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "De Clercq E, Holy A. Nat Rev Drug Discov, 2005"]}, "peramivir": {"origin": "Peramivir came from structure-based drug design. In 1983 Peter Colman and colleagues in Australia solved the 3D structure of influenza neuraminidase, the enzyme that lets new virus particles escape infected cells. That map allowed chemists to design molecules that fit the enzyme's active site. Scientists at BioCryst Pharmaceuticals in Alabama designed peramivir in the 1990s, building it on a cyclopentane ring. It was first used widely under an emergency authorization during the 2009 H1N1 pandemic and was approved in Japan in 2010. The FDA approved it as Rapivab in 2014, the first IV neuraminidase inhibitor in the US.", "timeline": [["1983", "Peter Colman's group solves the structure of influenza neuraminidase."], ["1990s", "BioCryst designs peramivir by structure-based methods."], ["2009", "Peramivir is used under emergency authorization during the H1N1 pandemic."], ["2014", "FDA approves Rapivab (peramivir)."]], "cell": "Influenza viruses bind sialic acid on cell surfaces to get in. When new virions bud out, they would stick to the same sialic acid and clump on the cell surface. Neuraminidase cuts sialic acid off so they can be released and spread. Peramivir is a transition-state analog of sialic acid. It binds the neuraminidase active site of influenza A and B tightly and competitively, so the enzyme cannot clip sialic acid. New virions stay stuck to the infected cell and to each other and cannot spread through the airway. Peramivir comes off the enzyme slowly, which supports single-dose treatment.", "body": [["Airways", "Less virus spreads from cell to cell in the respiratory tract, so symptoms resolve somewhat sooner when started within two days."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is the most common side effect."], ["Blood", "Neutropenia has been seen, especially in children."], ["Brain and behavior", "Confusion, abnormal behavior and delirium have been reported in patients with influenza on neuraminidase inhibitors, though influenza itself can cause these."], ["Skin", "Rare serious skin reactions, including Stevens-Johnson syndrome, have been reported."]], "journey": "Peramivir is given as a single IV infusion, which helps people who cannot take oral medicine. It is not strongly protein bound and is not significantly metabolized. About 90 percent leaves unchanged in the urine. Its half-life is about 20 hours, and doses are lowered in kidney disease.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea is the most common effect. Because the drug acts on a viral enzyme with no direct human counterpart, serious side effects are uncommon. Allergic and skin reactions, including Stevens-Johnson syndrome, are immune-mediated. Neuropsychiatric events have been reported with this class, mostly in children, though it is unclear how much comes from the drug versus influenza. Live influenza nasal vaccine may not work if given close to treatment, because the drug blocks the vaccine virus too. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Structure-based design of neuraminidase inhibitors was one of the early triumphs of designing drugs from a protein's 3D shape.", "src": ["Rapivab (peramivir) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Varghese JN, Laver WG, Colman PM. Nature, 1983"]}, "anthrax-immune-globulin": {"origin": "Anthrax was the first disease proven to be caused by a specific microbe, when Robert Koch traced it to Bacillus anthracis in 1876. In inhalation anthrax the bacteria release toxins that keep killing even after antibiotics clear the germs. After the 2001 anthrax letter attacks in the US, the government funded new antitoxins. Cangene Corporation, later part of Emergent BioSolutions, collected plasma from people vaccinated with anthrax vaccine and purified their antibodies. Because human trials of anthrax are not possible, the FDA approved Anthrasil in 2015 under the Animal Rule, using animal studies to show it works.", "timeline": [["1876", "Robert Koch shows that Bacillus anthracis causes anthrax."], ["2001", "Anthrax letter attacks in the US prompt new antitoxin development."], ["2015", "FDA approves Anthrasil under the Animal Rule."]], "cell": "Anthrax toxin has three parts. Protective antigen (PA) binds receptors on host cells (ANTXR1 and ANTXR2), is cut by a host protease, and forms a ring that acts as a delivery pore. Lethal factor and edema factor then ride through this pore into the cell. Lethal factor cuts MAP kinase kinases and disrupts immune cells and blood vessels; edema factor is an adenylyl cyclase that floods cells with cAMP and causes swelling. Anthrasil is polyclonal human IgG from vaccinated donors. Its antibodies bind PA and block it from reaching receptors or forming the pore, so the other two toxins cannot get in. This is passive immunity: it neutralizes toxin already circulating but does not kill bacteria.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Neutralizing toxin helps prevent the vascular leak, shock and fluid around the lungs seen in severe anthrax."], ["Immune cells", "Blocking lethal factor entry protects macrophages and other immune cells so the body can keep fighting."], ["Bacteria", "It does not kill Bacillus anthracis, so antibiotics are always given with it."], ["Blood clotting", "Like other IV immune globulins, it can raise the risk of blood clots."], ["Kidneys and blood", "IV immune globulins can cause kidney injury and hemolysis in some patients."]], "journey": "Anthrasil is given by IV infusion. It binds free toxin almost immediately. As human IgG, it stays in the blood and tissue fluid for weeks, and the body breaks it down like its own antibodies through normal protein catabolism. It is not cleared by the kidneys or liver in the usual drug sense.", "sidefx": "Headache, back pain, nausea and infusion site reactions are common with immune globulin products. Thrombosis carries a boxed warning; IgG can increase blood thickness and activate clotting. The product contains maltose, which some blood glucose meters read as glucose, giving falsely high readings that can hide true hypoglycemia; this is a boxed warning too. Hemolysis comes from donor antibodies against blood group antigens. Aseptic meningitis, kidney injury, TRALI and anaphylaxis are rare immune globulin risks.", "fun": "The FDA Animal Rule, used to approve Anthrasil, lets drugs for deadly threats be approved on animal evidence when human trials would be unethical.", "src": ["Anthrasil (anthrax immune globulin intravenous) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Koch R. 1876 anthrax etiology paper"]}, "obiltoxaximab": {"origin": "Obiltoxaximab was built for biodefense. Robert Koch showed in 1876 that Bacillus anthracis causes anthrax, and in the 20th century researchers learned that its toxins, not just the bacteria, kill in inhalation anthrax. After the 2001 anthrax attacks, US agencies funded antitoxins. The first, raxibacumab, was approved in 2012. Elusys Therapeutics, a small New Jersey company, developed obiltoxaximab, a chimeric monoclonal antibody against anthrax protective antigen. It was approved as Anthim in 2016 under the FDA Animal Rule, since human anthrax trials are not possible.", "timeline": [["1876", "Robert Koch shows that Bacillus anthracis causes anthrax."], ["2012", "Raxibacumab becomes the first approved anthrax monoclonal antibody."], ["2016", "FDA approves Anthim (obiltoxaximab) under the Animal Rule."]], "cell": "Anthrax toxin needs protective antigen (PA) to get its harmful parts, lethal factor and edema factor, into cells. PA normally binds the host receptors ANTXR1 and ANTXR2, is cut by furin, forms a heptamer pore and carries lethal and edema factor inside. Obiltoxaximab is a chimeric IgG1 kappa antibody that binds free PA with high affinity and blocks it from binding its receptors. Without PA attached to cells, the toxin cannot enter. Lethal factor would otherwise cleave MAP kinase kinases and cripple immune cells; edema factor would raise cAMP and cause tissue swelling. Like other antitoxins, it neutralizes toxin but does not kill the bacteria.", "body": [["Blood vessels and lungs", "Blocking toxin entry helps prevent the shock, vascular leak and pleural effusions that make inhalation anthrax deadly."], ["Immune system", "Immune cells are spared from lethal factor damage, so the body and antibiotics can clear the infection."], ["Brain", "It does not cross the blood-brain barrier well enough to treat anthrax meningitis."], ["Skin and airways", "Itching, hives, cough and upper respiratory symptoms are among the reported effects."]], "journey": "Obiltoxaximab is given as a single IV infusion, with premedication with diphenhydramine recommended. It binds circulating PA quickly. As an IgG antibody it is broken down slowly by normal protein catabolism and has a long half-life of about 15 to 23 days, so one dose covers the danger period while antibiotics work.", "sidefx": "Hypersensitivity and anaphylaxis are the key risks and carry a boxed warning, because the antibody is a foreign protein with mouse-derived parts that the immune system can react to. This is why it is given where reactions can be treated and with antihistamine premedication. Headache, itching, hives, cough and infusion site swelling are common. It has no antibacterial effect, so it is used alongside antibiotics.", "fun": "Anthim is stockpiled in the US Strategic National Stockpile for use after a possible anthrax attack.", "src": ["Anthim (obiltoxaximab) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "penicillin-g-benzathine": {"origin": "Penicillin G was the first penicillin used in patients, after Alexander Fleming noticed in 1928 that Penicillium mold killed staphylococci and Howard Florey and Ernst Chain's Oxford team purified it around 1940. Penicillin G leaves the body in about half an hour, so early treatment meant many shots. To make one injection last, researchers looked for poorly soluble salts that would dissolve slowly from muscle. In the early 1950s Wyeth introduced benzathine penicillin G, which keeps low levels in the blood for weeks. It became the standard treatment for syphilis and the mainstay of preventing repeat rheumatic fever. Its sole US maker has faced repeated supply shortages.", "timeline": [["1928", "Alexander Fleming observes penicillin's effect on staphylococci."], ["1940", "Florey and Chain's Oxford team purifies penicillin and shows it protects mice."], ["1950s", "Wyeth introduces long-acting benzathine penicillin G (Bicillin)."], ["2020s", "Repeated shortages of benzathine penicillin affect syphilis treatment in the US."]], "cell": "After injection, the benzathine salt dissolves slowly and releases penicillin G. Penicillin G acylates penicillin-binding proteins, the transpeptidases that cross-link peptidoglycan in the bacterial wall, by mimicking the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of the wall precursor. Without cross-links the wall weakens, autolysins keep cutting it, and the dividing bacterium lyses. Killing depends on time above the MIC, not peak height. That is why a low but steady level works for organisms that are extremely sensitive, such as Treponema pallidum and Streptococcus pyogenes. Beta-lactamase enzymes destroy penicillin G, so it does not treat beta-lactamase producers.", "body": [["Syphilis bacteria", "Treponema pallidum divides slowly and is very sensitive, so weeks of low penicillin levels cure it; it remains the only recommended treatment for syphilis in pregnancy."], ["Throat", "A single shot treats strep throat and, given monthly, prevents repeat infections that would trigger rheumatic fever."], ["Heart", "Preventing repeat strep infection protects heart valves from rheumatic heart disease."], ["Injection site", "The thick depot causes pain and firm swelling in the muscle."], ["Whole body", "During syphilis treatment, dying spirochetes release inflammatory products that can cause fever and chills within hours (Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction)."]], "journey": "Benzathine penicillin is given only by deep IM injection. The salt sits in the muscle as a depot and slowly dissolves, releasing penicillin G that peaks in about 13 to 24 hours. Low levels can be found for about two to four weeks. Once released, penicillin G itself has a half-life of only about 30 minutes and is cleared by kidney tubular secretion, so the slow release from the muscle, not slow elimination, explains the long effect. It is never given IV.", "sidefx": "Injection site pain comes from the thick suspension in muscle. The Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction is caused by cytokines released as spirochetes die. Accidental injection into a vessel can block small arteries with crystals, causing Nicolau syndrome (tissue death) or cardiorespiratory arrest, which is why it carries a boxed warning against IV use. Allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, come from immune responses to penicillin breakdown products; epinephrine is the treatment for anaphylaxis.", "fun": "To prevent rheumatic fever from coming back, some patients receive a benzathine penicillin shot every three to four weeks for ten years or longer.", "src": ["Bicillin L-A (penicillin G benzathine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "CDC Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines"]}, "amphotericin-b-deoxycholate": {"origin": "Amphotericin B came from a soil sample collected near the Orinoco River in Venezuela. In 1955 researchers at the Squibb Institute for Medical Research isolated two antifungal substances from the bacterium Streptomyces nodosus in that soil and named them amphotericin A and B. Amphotericin B was more active but did not dissolve in water, so it was combined with the bile salt deoxycholate to make an IV form. Introduced in the late 1950s as Fungizone, it was for decades the only reliable treatment for deadly fungal infections. Its toxicity earned it the nickname amphoterrible and later drove development of lipid forms, but the conventional deoxycholate form remains in use.", "timeline": [["1955", "Squibb researchers isolate amphotericin B from Streptomyces nodosus from Venezuelan soil."], ["1950s", "Conventional amphotericin B deoxycholate (Fungizone) enters clinical use."], ["1990s", "Lipid formulations are introduced to reduce kidney toxicity."]], "cell": "Amphotericin B is a polyene with one water-loving side and one fat-loving side. It binds ergosterol, the main sterol in fungal cell membranes. Several molecules gather around ergosterol and form pores through the membrane. Potassium, magnesium and other small molecules leak out, and the fungal cell dies. It may also pull ergosterol out of the membrane and cause oxidative damage. It binds cholesterol in human membranes much less, but not zero, which explains much of its toxicity, especially in kidney tubule cells. It also triggers cytokine release from immune cells and constricts the afferent arteriole of the kidney.", "body": [["Fungi", "Kills Candida, Cryptococcus, Aspergillus, Histoplasma, Mucor and many other fungi by punching holes in their membranes."], ["Kidneys", "Afferent arteriole narrowing lowers filtration, and tubule injury wastes potassium and magnesium and causes distal renal tubular acidosis."], ["Immune system", "Release of TNF and IL-1 from immune cells causes fever, shaking chills and headache during the infusion."], ["Blood", "Reduced erythropoietin from injured kidneys causes a slowly developing anemia."], ["Heart", "Very fast infusion or overdose can cause arrhythmias and cardiac arrest, partly from potassium shifts."], ["Veins", "The infusion irritates veins and causes phlebitis."]], "journey": "Amphotericin B is given by slow IV infusion because the gut does not absorb it. It is more than 90 percent bound to proteins and lipoproteins and moves into tissues such as liver, spleen and kidney, though little enters cerebrospinal fluid. It is not much metabolized. It leaves the tissues very slowly, with an initial half-life of about a day and a terminal half-life of about 15 days, and traces appear in urine for weeks. Kidney failure does not greatly change its levels, and dialysis does not remove it.", "sidefx": "Fever and rigors come from cytokine release by immune cells; premedication with acetaminophen, antihistamines or hydrocortisone may be used. Nephrotoxicity is the main dose-limiting problem: arteriole narrowing lowers filtration, and pores in tubule cells waste potassium and magnesium. Saline loading reduces this. Anemia follows lower erythropoietin. Overdose can cause cardiac arrest, so the conventional and lipid forms must not be confused. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Its old nickname, amphoterrible, reflects how hard the infusion was on patients, yet it is still the reference drug that newer antifungals are compared against.", "src": ["Amphotericin B for Injection FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "ceftobiprole": {"origin": "Ceftobiprole was developed to bring a cephalosporin that kills MRSA. MRSA resists nearly all beta-lactams because it carries PBP2a, a cell wall enzyme that ignores them. Scientists at Basilea Pharmaceutica, a Swiss company spun out of Roche, developed ceftobiprole with a side chain that fits into PBP2a. It was approved in Canada and several European countries around 2008 but stalled in the US for years. After new trials, the FDA approved it as Zevtera in 2024 for Staphylococcus aureus bloodstream infections, acute bacterial skin infections and community-acquired pneumonia.", "timeline": [["1945", "Giuseppe Brotzu discovers the cephalosporin-producing mold in Sardinia."], ["2008", "Ceftobiprole is first approved outside the US, starting with Canada."], ["2024", "FDA approves Zevtera (ceftobiprole medocaril)."]], "cell": "Ceftobiprole is given as ceftobiprole medocaril, a water-soluble prodrug that plasma esterases quickly convert to active ceftobiprole. The active drug binds penicillin-binding proteins and blocks transpeptidation, the cross-linking of peptidoglycan. Its large side chain fits into PBP2a of MRSA, whose active site is normally closed to beta-lactams. It also binds PBP2x of penicillin-resistant Streptococcus pneumoniae and PBP3 of gram-negative rods. Without cross-links, the cell wall fails and the bacteria lyse. It remains vulnerable to ESBLs and carbapenemases.", "body": [["Bacteria", "Kills MSSA, MRSA, streptococci, many Enterobacterales and some Pseudomonas aeruginosa."], ["Bloodstream", "Approved for Staphylococcus aureus bacteremia, including right-sided endocarditis."], ["Brain", "Like other cephalosporins it can cause seizures, especially when kidney function is poor and levels build up."], ["Mouth and gut", "Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea and an altered sense of taste are common."], ["Electrolytes", "Low sodium has been reported in treated patients, so sodium levels are watched."], ["Gut flora", "Disruption of normal bacteria can lead to C. difficile."]], "journey": "Ceftobiprole medocaril is given by IV infusion, and esterases in the blood convert it to active ceftobiprole almost right away. Only about 16 percent is protein bound. Little more metabolism happens. The kidneys clear it, mainly by filtration, largely unchanged, with a half-life of about three hours. Doses are adjusted for kidney function.", "sidefx": "Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea and taste changes are the most common effects. Seizures reflect the beta-lactam effect on GABA-A signaling and are more likely when kidney failure raises levels. Hypersensitivity and anaphylaxis are shared with other beta-lactams. C. difficile results from loss of normal gut flora. There is no antidote; hemodialysis can remove some drug.", "fun": "Ceftobiprole waited about 16 years between its first approval abroad and its US approval.", "src": ["Zevtera (ceftobiprole medocaril) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "onasemnogene-abeparvovec": {"origin": "Spinal muscular atrophy (SMA) was once the leading genetic cause of death in infants. In 1995 a team in Paris led by Judith Melki identified the cause: loss of the SMN1 gene, which makes survival motor neuron protein. Researchers then found that AAV9, a harmless adeno-associated virus, could cross into the spinal cord after IV injection. At Nationwide Children's Hospital in Ohio, Jerry Mendell and Brian Kaspar used it to carry a working SMN1 gene into motor neurons, and an early trial showed infants surviving and sitting who otherwise would not. The company AveXis, bought by Novartis in 2018, developed it as Zolgensma, approved by the FDA in 2019 as a one-time IV gene therapy. An intrathecal version, Itvisma, was later approved.", "timeline": [["1995", "Judith Melki's team identifies SMN1 as the SMA gene."], ["2017", "Mendell and colleagues publish striking results of gene therapy in infants with SMA type 1."], ["2018", "Novartis acquires AveXis."], ["2019", "FDA approves Zolgensma, a one-time IV gene therapy for SMA."]], "cell": "In SMA, both copies of SMN1 are deleted or mutated. The nearby SMN2 gene makes mostly an unstable, shortened protein, so motor neurons in the anterior horn of the spinal cord lack enough SMN protein, which is needed to assemble the small nuclear ribonucleoproteins that run mRNA splicing. These neurons slowly die. Onasemnogene abeparvovec is a non-replicating AAV9 capsid holding a full-length human SMN1 gene with a strong, continuous promoter. After infusion the capsid crosses the blood-brain barrier, enters motor neurons and delivers its DNA to the nucleus. The DNA stays there as an episome, mostly without integrating into chromosomes, and makes SMN protein steadily. Because neurons do not divide, the episome is not diluted, so one dose can last.", "body": [["Motor neurons", "Steady SMN protein stops further loss of anterior horn cells, so muscles keep their nerve supply."], ["Muscles and breathing", "Treated infants reach milestones like head control and sitting and are much more likely to survive without permanent breathing support, especially when treated before symptoms."], ["Liver", "The liver takes up much of the vector, and the immune response to infected liver cells can cause enzyme rises and even acute liver failure; steroids are given around the infusion."], ["Blood and kidneys", "Thrombocytopenia and thrombotic microangiopathy, with clots in small vessels and kidney injury, have been reported in the weeks after infusion."], ["Heart", "Troponin rises have been seen, so heart markers are checked."]], "journey": "Zolgensma is a single IV infusion given over about an hour. The vector spreads through the blood to the liver, heart, muscle and, importantly, the brain and spinal cord. It is not metabolized like a drug; instead its DNA persists inside cells as an episome and makes SMN protein for the long term. Vector DNA is shed in stool, saliva and urine for weeks, so caregivers use careful hand hygiene with diapers. Patients are tested for antibodies to AAV9 beforehand, because existing antibodies would neutralize the vector.", "sidefx": "Most serious side effects come from the immune system reacting to the viral capsid and the cells that take it up. Infected liver cells are attacked, causing aminotransferase rises and, rarely, acute liver failure, which carries a boxed warning; corticosteroids are given before and after the infusion to dampen this. Complement activation by the capsid is thought to drive thrombotic microangiopathy with low platelets and kidney injury. Vomiting is common. There is no way to remove the vector once given.", "fun": "When approved, Zolgensma was one of the most expensive drugs ever sold, priced at about two million US dollars for a single infusion.", "src": ["Zolgensma (onasemnogene abeparvovec-xioi) FDA label", "Lefebvre S, et al. Cell, 1995", "Mendell JR, et al. N Engl J Med, 2017"]}, "loxapine-inhaled": {"origin": "Loxapine is an older antipsychotic of the dibenzoxazepine family, developed in the 1960s and approved in the US as an oral capsule (Loxitane) in the 1970s. Decades later, Alexza Pharmaceuticals in California built the Staccato system, a single-use device that heats a thin film of drug in a fraction of a second to make an aerosol a person breathes in. Inhaled drug reaches the blood almost as fast as an IV injection without needles. The FDA approved inhaled loxapine as Adasuve in 2012 for acute agitation in schizophrenia or bipolar I disorder, under a restricted program because it can cause bronchospasm.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Oral loxapine is approved in the US as Loxitane."], ["2012", "FDA approves Adasuve, inhaled loxapine, with a restricted program for bronchospasm risk."]], "cell": "Loxapine blocks dopamine D2 receptors in the mesolimbic pathway, which reduces overactive dopamine signaling linked to agitation and psychosis. Blocking these Gi-coupled receptors removes dopamine's brake on adenylyl cyclase in target neurons. It also blocks serotonin 5-HT2A receptors fairly strongly for a typical antipsychotic. Blocking H1 histamine receptors causes sedation, alpha-1 blockade lowers blood pressure, and muscarinic blockade causes dry mouth. D2 blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway can cause extrapyramidal symptoms, and in the pituitary it raises prolactin. Part of loxapine is converted to amoxapine, a tricyclic antidepressant.", "body": [["Brain", "Rapid D2 and 5-HT2A blockade calms agitation within minutes, and H1 blockade adds sedation."], ["Airways", "The aerosol can irritate the airways and trigger bronchospasm, especially in asthma or COPD, which is why a rescue bronchodilator must be on hand."], ["Blood pressure", "Alpha-1 blockade can cause low blood pressure and dizziness on standing."], ["Movement", "D2 blockade in the basal ganglia can cause dystonia, stiffness or restlessness."], ["Mouth", "A bad or bitter taste is the most common side effect, from drug deposited in the mouth."]], "journey": "Inhaled loxapine is breathed in through the device in one deep breath. The aerosol deposits deep in the lungs, and the drug crosses the huge alveolar surface, reaching peak blood levels in about two minutes. Calming begins within about ten minutes. It is highly protein bound. The liver metabolizes it through CYP1A2, CYP3A4 and CYP2D6 to hydroxylated metabolites and amoxapine, which leave in urine and feces. Its half-life is about six to eight hours.", "sidefx": "Bronchospasm is the defining danger and carries a boxed warning: the aerosol irritates sensitive airways, so it is given only in enrolled settings that can treat respiratory distress. A boxed warning also covers higher death rates in older people with dementia-related psychosis, as with all antipsychotics. D2 blockade explains extrapyramidal symptoms and rarely neuroleptic malignant syndrome. Alpha-1 blockade causes hypotension, and the drug lowers the seizure threshold. Bronchospasm is treated with an inhaled beta-agonist.", "fun": "The Staccato device heats the drug film for less than a second, turning it into vapor that condenses into particles small enough to reach the alveoli.", "src": ["Adasuve (loxapine) inhalation powder FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "doxapram": {"origin": "Doxapram is an analeptic, a drug that stimulates breathing. Its story builds on the Belgian physiologist Corneille Heymans, who won the 1938 Nobel Prize for showing that the carotid and aortic bodies sense blood chemistry and drive breathing. In the early 1960s chemists at A. H. Robins in Richmond, Virginia, developed doxapram, and it was marketed as Dopram. It was used for breathing depression after anesthesia and drug overdose. Its role has shrunk because ventilation and specific reversal agents such as naloxone are safer and more reliable.", "timeline": [["1938", "Corneille Heymans wins the Nobel Prize for work on carotid and aortic body reflexes."], ["1960s", "A. H. Robins develops doxapram, marketed as Dopram."]], "cell": "At lower levels doxapram acts mainly on the peripheral chemoreceptors in the carotid bodies, and to a lesser degree the aortic bodies. In carotid glomus cells it is thought to block oxygen-sensitive potassium channels, especially TASK-type channels. With fewer potassium channels open, the glomus cells depolarize, calcium enters, and they release transmitters that fire the carotid sinus nerve. The brainstem reads this as low oxygen and increases breathing, mostly by raising tidal volume. At higher levels doxapram directly stimulates the medullary respiratory center and other parts of the brain and spinal cord. It also causes catecholamine release, which raises blood pressure and heart rate.", "body": [["Lungs and breathing", "Tidal volume and minute ventilation rise within seconds, which can improve breathing after anesthesia."], ["Heart and vessels", "Catecholamine release raises blood pressure and heart rate and can trigger arrhythmias."], ["Brain", "Broader central stimulation causes arousal, agitation and, at high levels, seizures."], ["Airways", "Cough, laryngospasm and bronchospasm can occur."], ["Skin and body temperature", "Flushing, sweating and sometimes hyperthermia come from sympathetic activation."], ["Bladder", "Urinary retention has been reported, likely from sympathetic stimulation of the bladder outlet."]], "journey": "Doxapram is given IV as an injection or infusion. Breathing rises within 20 to 40 seconds and peaks in one to two minutes, but a single injection wears off in about 5 to 12 minutes as the drug redistributes. The liver breaks it down extensively, including to ketodoxapram, an active metabolite, and the products leave in urine. Its elimination half-life is about 3.4 hours.", "sidefx": "Nearly all side effects come from stimulating the nervous system beyond the breathing centers. Catecholamine release causes hypertension, tachycardia and arrhythmias. Wider brain stimulation causes agitation, tremor and seizures. It is risky in people with coronary disease, uncontrolled hypertension or seizure disorders, and with MAO inhibitors or sympathomimetics. It does not reverse opioid or sedative effects and is not a substitute for ventilation. There is no antidote; seizures are treated with short-acting anticonvulsants.", "fun": "Heymans showed the carotid body's role using dogs whose heads were perfused by the blood of another dog, separating the sensor from the brain.", "src": ["Dopram (doxapram hydrochloride) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1938 record"]}, "emapalumab": {"origin": "Hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (HLH) was first described in 1952 by the Scottish doctors James Farquhar and Albert Claireaux as a familial disease of infants. In HLH, killer T cells and NK cells cannot clear infected cells, so they keep pumping out interferon gamma, which drives macrophages into a destructive frenzy. Researchers at the Swiss company Novimmune developed emapalumab, a fully human antibody against interferon gamma, and the program was later taken over by Sobi. The FDA approved it as Gamifant in 2018, the first drug approved specifically for primary HLH. Its label was later expanded to macrophage activation syndrome in Still disease.", "timeline": [["1952", "Farquhar and Claireaux describe familial hemophagocytic reticulosis (HLH)."], ["2018", "FDA approves Gamifant (emapalumab), the first drug for primary HLH."], ["2025", "Gamifant label expands to macrophage activation syndrome in Still disease."]], "cell": "In primary HLH, gene defects such as PRF1 (perforin) mutations prevent cytotoxic T cells and NK cells from killing infected or activated cells. The immune response never shuts off, and these cells keep releasing interferon gamma. Interferon gamma binds the IFNGR1/IFNGR2 receptor on macrophages, activates JAK1/JAK2 and STAT1, and turns on genes that make macrophages eat blood cells (hemophagocytosis) and release more cytokines and the chemokine CXCL9. Emapalumab is a human IgG1 antibody that binds interferon gamma, both free and receptor-bound, and neutralizes it. This breaks the loop: macrophage activation falls, CXCL9 and ferritin drop, and fever and cytopenias improve, bridging patients to stem cell transplant.", "body": [["Immune system", "Neutralizing interferon gamma calms the cytokine storm, lowering fever, ferritin, soluble CD25 and CXCL9."], ["Bone marrow and blood", "With less hemophagocytosis, red cells, white cells and platelets recover."], ["Liver and spleen", "Less macrophage infiltration lets the enlarged liver and spleen shrink and liver tests improve."], ["Infection defense", "Interferon gamma is key against intracellular germs, so blocking it raises the risk of tuberculosis, histoplasmosis, CMV, adenovirus and herpes zoster."], ["Blood pressure", "High blood pressure is a common reported effect."]], "journey": "Emapalumab is given by IV infusion several times a week at first. As an IgG antibody it is broken down by normal protein catabolism and by binding to its target. In healthy people its half-life is about 22 days, but in active HLH the large amount of interferon gamma speeds its clearance, shortening the half-life to as little as a few days, so doses are adjusted to response.", "sidefx": "The main risk is infection, because interferon gamma activates macrophages to kill intracellular microbes. Without it, latent tuberculosis can reactivate and fungal and viral infections can spread; patients are screened for TB and given prophylaxis against herpes zoster, Pneumocystis and fungi. Live vaccines are avoided. Infusion reactions are immune responses to the antibody. Hypertension, fever and low potassium are common. There is no reversal agent.", "fun": "Interferon gamma was originally named immune interferon because it was made by stimulated immune cells rather than by virus-infected cells.", "src": ["Gamifant (emapalumab-lzsg) FDA label", "Farquhar JW, Claireaux AE. Arch Dis Child, 1952", "Locatelli F, et al. N Engl J Med, 2020"]}, "cyclopentolate": {"origin": "Cyclopentolate is a synthetic cousin of atropine. Atropine comes from deadly nightshade, Atropa belladonna, whose name means beautiful lady: women in Renaissance Italy were said to use its juice to widen their pupils. Atropine was isolated from the plant in the 1830s, but it dilates the pupil and blocks focusing for a week or more. In the 1950s chemists made cyclopentolate, a shorter-acting antimuscarinic. Sold as Cyclogyl, it became a standard eye drop for dilating pupils and relaxing the focusing muscle during eye exams, especially to measure refraction in children.", "timeline": [["1830s", "Atropine is isolated from Atropa belladonna."], ["1950s", "Cyclopentolate is developed as a shorter-acting synthetic antimuscarinic eye drop."]], "cell": "The iris has two muscles: the sphincter pupillae, which constricts the pupil, and the dilator pupillae, which widens it. The sphincter and the ciliary muscle, which changes lens shape for near focus, are run by parasympathetic nerves releasing acetylcholine onto M3 muscarinic receptors. M3 activates Gq, phospholipase C and IP3, raising calcium and causing contraction. Cyclopentolate competitively blocks these M3 receptors. The sphincter relaxes and the sympathetic dilator acts unopposed, so the pupil widens (mydriasis). The ciliary muscle relaxes and cannot focus up close (cycloplegia), which reveals the eye's true refractive error.", "body": [["Iris", "The pupil dilates within 30 to 60 minutes, which lets the examiner see the retina."], ["Ciliary muscle", "Focusing is paralyzed, which gives an accurate glasses measurement in children and relieves painful ciliary spasm in uveitis."], ["Vision", "Blurred near vision and light sensitivity last until the drug wears off, often within a day."], ["Eye pressure", "A widened pupil can block the drainage angle in narrow-angle eyes and trigger angle-closure glaucoma."], ["Brain", "Absorbed drug can cross into the brain and cause restlessness, confusion, ataxia or hallucinations, especially in young children."], ["Whole body", "Absorbed drug can cause fast heart rate, dry mouth, flushing and fever."]], "journey": "Cyclopentolate is applied as eye drops. It passes through the cornea into the iris and ciliary body. Some drains through the tear duct into the nose, where it can be absorbed into the blood, which explains whole-body effects. Pressing on the inner corner of the eye reduces this. Recovery from cycloplegia usually happens within about 24 hours, though dilation can last longer. Its systemic metabolism is not well characterized.", "sidefx": "Stinging on instillation is local irritation. Blurred vision and light sensitivity are the direct, expected effects of blocking M3 in the eye. Angle-closure glaucoma can occur in eyes with narrow angles because the dilated iris crowds the drainage angle. Systemic antimuscarinic effects, including tachycardia, flushing, fever, dry mouth and CNS effects such as hallucinations and seizures, come from absorption through the nasal mucosa and are most likely in infants and small children. Physostigmine reverses central anticholinergic toxicity, but care is usually supportive.", "fun": "Antimuscarinic toxicity is remembered as hot as a hare, dry as a bone, red as a beet, blind as a bat and mad as a hatter, and cyclopentolate drops can cause a mild version in small children.", "src": ["Cyclogyl (cyclopentolate) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "fluorescein": {"origin": "Fluorescein was born in a chemistry lab. In 1871 the German chemist Adolf von Baeyer heated phthalic anhydride with resorcinol and made a dye that glows bright yellow-green under light. In 1882 Paul Ehrlich used it to trace fluid in the eye. Its big clinical moment came in 1961, when Harold Novotny and David Alvis, then medical students in Indiana, published the first method of fluorescein angiography, photographing the dye as it flowed through retinal vessels. Fluorescein strips and drops also became the standard way to find corneal scratches. Today IV forms such as AK-Fluor and Fluorescite are used for angiography.", "timeline": [["1871", "Adolf von Baeyer synthesizes fluorescein."], ["1882", "Paul Ehrlich uses fluorescein to study fluid in the eye."], ["1961", "Novotny and Alvis describe fluorescein angiography of the retina."]], "cell": "Fluorescein is not a receptor drug; it works through light. The molecule absorbs blue light at about 465 to 490 nm and emits yellow-green light at about 520 to 530 nm. In angiography, IV fluorescein travels through the blood, and a camera with filters records it filling the choroid, then retinal arteries and veins. Healthy retinal vessels have tight junctions, the blood-retinal barrier, that keep fluorescein inside. Where vessels are leaky, such as in diabetic retinopathy or macular degeneration, the dye seeps out and glows. On the eye surface, intact epithelium repels it, but where cells are missing, such as an abrasion or ulcer, the dye pools and glows under cobalt-blue light.", "body": [["Eyes", "Shows retinal vessel leaks, blockages and abnormal new vessels in angiography, and outlines corneal scratches and ulcers on the surface."], ["Skin", "IV dye turns the skin yellow for several hours."], ["Kidneys and urine", "The kidneys clear the dye, turning urine bright yellow-orange for a day or more."], ["Stomach and brain", "Nausea and vomiting are common shortly after IV injection."], ["Immune system", "Hives, itching and rarely anaphylaxis or bronchospasm can occur after IV use."], ["Veins and tissues", "If IV dye leaks out of the vein, it can irritate and injure tissue."]], "journey": "For angiography fluorescein is injected into an arm vein and reaches the retina within about 7 to 14 seconds. It is moderately bound to plasma proteins. The liver and kidneys convert it to fluorescein glucuronide, which also fluoresces. Both are cleared by the kidneys. Free fluorescein has a half-life of under an hour, the glucuronide several hours. Topical strips and drops stay on the eye surface, and only tiny amounts reach the blood.", "sidefx": "Nausea and vomiting after IV injection are the most common effects and usually pass quickly. Yellow skin and urine are simply the dye being carried and excreted. Hives and itching reflect mild hypersensitivity, and rare anaphylaxis, bronchospasm and cardiac arrest are why emergency equipment is kept nearby. Extravasation can cause painful tissue injury because the solution is alkaline. Allergic reactions are treated with the usual emergency measures, including epinephrine for anaphylaxis.", "fun": "Fluorescein was used to turn the Chicago River green for Saint Patrick's Day in the 1960s, and it is still used to trace underground water flow.", "src": ["AK-Fluor and Fluorescite (fluorescein injection) FDA labels", "Novotny HR, Alvis DL. Circulation, 1961", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "lidocaine-ophthalmic-gel": {"origin": "Eye anesthesia started with cocaine: in 1884 the Viennese ophthalmologist Karl Koller showed that cocaine drops numbed the eye, which opened the field of local anesthesia. Cocaine was toxic and addictive, so chemists looked for safer options. In 1943 Nils Lofgren and Bengt Lundqvist in Stockholm synthesized lidocaine, the first amide local anesthetic, and the Swedish company Astra marketed it as Xylocaine in 1948. Much later, a thick gel form for the eye was developed. Akten, lidocaine hydrochloride ophthalmic gel 3.5 percent, was approved by the FDA in 2008 to numb the eye surface during eye procedures.", "timeline": [["1884", "Karl Koller shows cocaine numbs the eye."], ["1943", "Lofgren and Lundqvist synthesize lidocaine."], ["1948", "Astra launches lidocaine as Xylocaine."], ["2008", "FDA approves Akten lidocaine ophthalmic gel."]], "cell": "Lidocaine blocks voltage-gated sodium channels in the sensory nerve endings of the cornea and conjunctiva, which belong to the ophthalmic branch of the trigeminal nerve. The uncharged form crosses the nerve membrane, then picks up a proton inside the cell. The charged form binds a site inside the channel pore, reaching it from the cytoplasmic side, and binds best to open and inactivated channels. With sodium unable to rush in, the nerve cannot fire an action potential, so pain signals do not reach the brain. The corneal nerves are small and close to the surface, so they are blocked quickly. The gel holds lidocaine against the eye longer than drops would.", "body": [["Cornea", "The cornea, one of the most densely innervated tissues in the body, becomes numb within about a minute, allowing injections and procedures without pain."], ["Conjunctiva", "The conjunctiva is also numbed, and some redness can appear from the gel and the procedure."], ["Blink reflex and protection", "Loss of corneal sensation also removes the warning pain of scratches or foreign bodies until it wears off."], ["Corneal surface", "Repeated anesthetic exposure can damage and slow healing of the corneal epithelium."], ["Whole body", "Very little reaches the blood, so systemic toxicity is unlikely with ocular use."]], "journey": "The gel is placed on the eye surface and lidocaine soaks into the corneal and conjunctival nerve endings. Numbness starts within about a minute and is renewed by reapplying. Only a tiny fraction reaches the blood through conjunctival vessels and the tear duct. Any lidocaine that does is broken down by the liver through CYP1A2 and CYP3A4 to MEGX and glycinexylidide and cleared by the kidneys, with a systemic half-life of about 1.5 to 2 hours.", "sidefx": "Redness, burning and headache are the most common reported effects. Loss of corneal sensation means the patient will not feel a scratch or foreign body, so the eye is protected until feeling returns. Anesthetics can damage the corneal epithelium, which is why they are used for procedures, not for ongoing home pain relief. Hypersensitivity is rare. Because so little enters the blood, local anesthetic systemic toxicity is unlikely; if it occurred, lipid emulsion is the standard rescue for local anesthetic toxicity.", "fun": "Koller's 1884 cocaine discovery was presented at a Heidelberg meeting by a colleague because Koller could not afford the trip.", "src": ["Akten (lidocaine hydrochloride ophthalmic gel) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "phenylephrine-ketorolac-intraocular": {"origin": "During cataract and lens replacement surgery, the pupil tends to shrink as the operation goes on, partly from surgical trauma that releases prostaglandins. A small pupil makes surgery harder and riskier. Omeros Corporation in Seattle combined two well-known drugs, the alpha-1 agonist phenylephrine and the NSAID ketorolac, into a solution added to the irrigation fluid that flows through the eye during surgery. The FDA approved it as Omidria in 2014 to keep the pupil dilated during surgery and to reduce early pain after surgery. Each of its parts had a long history: phenylephrine as a decongestant and pressor, and ketorolac as an injectable NSAID first approved in the US in the late 1980s.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Ketorolac is first approved in the US."], ["2014", "FDA approves Omidria for use in ocular irrigating solution during lens surgery."]], "cell": "The two parts act on different pupil muscles and pathways. Phenylephrine stimulates alpha-1 adrenergic receptors on the iris dilator (radial) muscle. Alpha-1 is Gq-coupled, activating phospholipase C and raising calcium, so the dilator contracts and the pupil widens. Ketorolac inhibits cyclooxygenase-1 and -2. Surgical trauma releases arachidonic acid, which COX turns into prostaglandins such as PGE2. These prostaglandins cause the iris sphincter to constrict independently of cholinergic nerves, and they promote inflammation and pain. Blocking their synthesis keeps the pupil from shrinking and lowers early postoperative pain.", "body": [["Iris", "The pupil stays wide during surgery because the dilator is stimulated and prostaglandin-driven constriction is blocked."], ["Eye tissues", "Less prostaglandin production lowers early inflammation and pain after surgery."], ["Eye pressure", "Increased eye pressure and inflammation in the anterior chamber can occur after surgery."], ["Lens capsule", "Posterior capsule opacification has been reported after the operation."], ["Blood pressure", "Any phenylephrine that is absorbed can raise blood pressure by activating vascular alpha-1 receptors."]], "journey": "Omidria is diluted into the irrigating solution and flows through the front of the eye during surgery, so it works right where it is needed. Only small amounts reach the bloodstream. Absorbed phenylephrine is broken down by monoamine oxidase and sulfation, with a systemic half-life of about two to three hours. Absorbed ketorolac is metabolized by the liver through glucuronidation and hydroxylation, with a half-life of about five hours. Both are cleared by the kidneys.", "sidefx": "Eye irritation, inflammation, increased eye pressure and capsule opacification are the most common reported effects and may reflect the surgery as well as the drug. Absorbed phenylephrine can raise blood pressure through vascular alpha-1 receptors, which matters in people with heart disease. Ketorolac carries the NSAID cross-sensitivity risk in people allergic to aspirin or other NSAIDs. Serious hypersensitivity is rare. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Omidria is not dropped on the eye or injected directly; it is mixed into the fluid that washes through the eye during the entire operation.", "src": ["Omidria (phenylephrine and ketorolac intraocular solution) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "exagamglogene-autotemcel": {"origin": "Exagamglogene autotemcel, or exa-cel, is the first approved medicine made with CRISPR-Cas9 gene editing. In 2012 Jennifer Doudna and Emmanuelle Charpentier showed that the bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 system could be programmed to cut DNA at chosen sites, work that won the 2020 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. Separately, Stuart Orkin's lab at Boston Children's Hospital found that the gene BCL11A shuts off fetal hemoglobin after birth, and later that a red cell-specific enhancer controls it. CRISPR Therapeutics and Vertex Pharmaceuticals used these discoveries to edit patients' own stem cells. In 2019 Victoria Gray of Mississippi became the first US sickle cell patient treated. The FDA approved it as Casgevy for sickle cell disease in December 2023 and for transfusion-dependent beta-thalassemia in January 2024.", "timeline": [["2008", "Orkin's lab identifies BCL11A as a repressor of fetal hemoglobin."], ["2012", "Doudna and Charpentier describe programmable CRISPR-Cas9 DNA cutting."], ["2019", "Victoria Gray becomes the first US sickle cell patient treated with exa-cel."], ["2023", "FDA approves Casgevy for sickle cell disease."], ["2024", "FDA approves Casgevy for transfusion-dependent beta-thalassemia."]], "cell": "Before birth, red cells make fetal hemoglobin (HbF, two alpha and two gamma chains). After birth, the transcription factor BCL11A shuts off gamma-globin and switches cells to adult beta-globin. In sickle cell disease the beta-globin is mutated, so deoxygenated HbS polymerizes and sickles red cells; in beta-thalassemia too little beta-globin is made. For exa-cel, a patient's own CD34+ stem cells are collected and edited outside the body with Cas9 and a guide RNA targeting the erythroid-specific enhancer of BCL11A. The cut disrupts the enhancer, so BCL11A falls only in red cell precursors. Gamma-globin turns back on, HbF rises, HbF blocks HbS polymerization and stands in for missing beta-globin.", "body": [["Red blood cells", "Many red cells now contain high fetal hemoglobin, so they resist sickling and live longer."], ["Blood vessels and pain", "Most sickle cell patients in trials stopped having severe vaso-occlusive pain crises."], ["Transfusion needs", "Most thalassemia patients in trials stopped needing regular transfusions."], ["Bone marrow", "Busulfan conditioning wipes out the marrow to make room for edited cells, causing weeks of low blood counts until engraftment."], ["Mouth and gut", "Mucositis, nausea and appetite loss mainly come from busulfan."], ["Fertility", "Busulfan conditioning can cause infertility, so fertility preservation is discussed beforehand."]], "journey": "The process takes months. Stem cells are mobilized and collected from the blood, edited in a manufacturing facility, and frozen. The patient then gets high-dose busulfan to clear the marrow, and the edited cells are infused IV. They home to the bone marrow and begin making new blood cells. Neutrophils recover around four weeks, and fetal hemoglobin rises over months. Because the edit is permanent in the stem cells, the effect is expected to last for life.", "sidefx": "Most short-term side effects come from busulfan conditioning, not the edited cells. Busulfan damages fast-dividing cells, causing mucositis, nausea, low blood counts and infections during the neutropenic period. Platelet recovery can be slow, raising bleeding risk. Busulfan can also cause infertility. Engraftment failure, while rare, would leave the patient without blood cell production. Long-term risks of off-target edits are being studied in follow-up.", "fun": "Exa-cel mimics a natural condition, hereditary persistence of fetal hemoglobin, in which people with sickle cell genes have mild disease because they keep making fetal hemoglobin.", "src": ["Casgevy (exagamglogene autotemcel) FDA label", "Frangoul H, et al. N Engl J Med, 2021", "Jinek M, et al. Science, 2012"]}, "lovotibeglogene-autotemcel": {"origin": "Lovotibeglogene autotemcel, or lovo-cel, grew out of decades of work on gene addition using lentiviruses, viral vectors derived from HIV and stripped of their ability to cause disease. Researchers led by Philippe Leboulch developed a lentiviral vector carrying a modified beta-globin gene with an anti-sickling change. The company bluebird bio developed it under the earlier name LentiGlobin. The FDA approved it as Lyfgenia on the same day in December 2023 as exa-cel, giving sickle cell disease its first two gene therapies. It carries a boxed warning for blood cancers.", "timeline": [["2010", "A thalassemia patient in France becomes transfusion independent after lentiviral beta-globin gene therapy."], ["2023", "FDA approves Lyfgenia (lovotibeglogene autotemcel) for sickle cell disease."]], "cell": "In sickle cell disease, a single mutation in beta-globin (glutamic acid to valine at position 6) makes HbS polymerize when it loses oxygen, which deforms red cells. For lovo-cel, a patient's own CD34+ stem cells are collected and exposed to the BB305 lentiviral vector. The vector inserts, permanently and at varying sites in the genome, a gene for beta-A-T87Q-globin. This is an adult beta-globin with a threonine changed to glutamine at position 87, the same spot that makes gamma-globin resist polymer formation. Red cells made from these stem cells contain HbA-T87Q, which does not join sickle polymers and dilutes HbS, so fewer cells sickle and hemolysis falls.", "body": [["Red blood cells", "New red cells carry anti-sickling HbA-T87Q, so they sickle less and survive longer."], ["Blood vessels and pain", "Most treated patients had far fewer vaso-occlusive events in trials."], ["Bone marrow", "Busulfan conditioning clears the marrow so modified cells can engraft, leaving weeks of low counts."], ["Mouth and gut", "Stomatitis and gut effects mainly come from busulfan."], ["Blood cancer risk", "Some patients developed AML or MDS, which led to a boxed warning and lifelong monitoring."]], "journey": "Stem cells are collected from the blood after mobilization, transduced with the vector in a central facility, and frozen. The patient receives busulfan conditioning, then the modified cells are infused IV. They home to the marrow and begin making blood. Neutrophils return in about three weeks, and HbA-T87Q rises over months. Because the gene is integrated into stem cell DNA, its effect is expected to be lifelong.", "sidefx": "Short-term side effects mostly come from busulfan, which harms fast-dividing cells and causes stomatitis, low blood counts, infections and possible infertility. Platelet recovery can be delayed. The boxed warning for hematologic malignancy reflects AML and MDS cases in trial patients; possible causes include busulfan, the stress of sickle cell disease on stem cells, and insertion of the vector near cancer-related genes. Patients are followed with blood counts for at least 15 years.", "fun": "The T87Q change borrows a trick from fetal gamma-globin, whose amino acid at that position helps it resist joining sickle hemoglobin polymers.", "src": ["Lyfgenia (lovotibeglogene autotemcel) FDA label", "Kanter J, et al. N Engl J Med, 2022", "Cavazzana-Calvo M, et al. Nature, 2010"]}, "etranacogene-dezaparvovec": {"origin": "Hemophilia B, also called Christmas disease after Stephen Christmas, the first patient described with it in 1952, comes from too little clotting factor IX. For decades patients needed frequent factor infusions. In 2009 Italian researchers led by Paolo Simioni in Padua described a family with blood clots caused by a factor IX variant, R338L, that is about eight times more active than normal. This Padua variant made gene therapy far more efficient. The Dutch company uniQure built an AAV5 vector carrying the Padua factor IX gene and licensed it to CSL Behring. The FDA approved it as Hemgenix in 2022, the first gene therapy for hemophilia B, at a launch price of about 3.5 million US dollars.", "timeline": [["1952", "Christmas disease (hemophilia B) is described, named after patient Stephen Christmas."], ["2009", "Simioni and colleagues describe the hyperactive factor IX Padua variant."], ["2022", "FDA approves Hemgenix, the first gene therapy for hemophilia B."]], "cell": "Factor IX is a vitamin K-dependent protein made in the liver. When activated, factor IXa joins factor VIIIa on platelet surfaces to activate factor X, a key step in making thrombin. In hemophilia B this step is weak, so clots form poorly. Etranacogene dezaparvovec is a non-replicating AAV5 capsid carrying a codon-optimized human factor IX gene with the Padua R338L change, driven by a liver-specific promoter. After IV infusion the capsid enters hepatocytes and the DNA travels to the nucleus, where it stays mostly as an episome rather than joining chromosomes. Hepatocytes then make and secrete Padua factor IX, which is several times more active per molecule, so even modest protein levels restore useful clotting.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Steady factor IX activity improves thrombin generation, so spontaneous bleeds fall and most patients stop routine factor infusions."], ["Joints", "Fewer bleeds into joints help prevent the joint damage that defines severe hemophilia."], ["Liver", "Immune responses to transduced liver cells can raise ALT, and corticosteroids may be needed to protect expression."], ["Immune system", "Infusion reactions and flu-like symptoms can occur in the hours to days after infusion."], ["Cancer risk", "Rare vector integration into DNA raises a theoretical risk of liver cancer, so liver imaging is followed in people with risk factors."]], "journey": "Hemgenix is given once by IV infusion. The AAV5 capsid is taken up mainly by the liver. Its DNA persists in hepatocytes and factor IX activity rises over weeks to months, then holds steady for years in trials. Vector DNA is shed in blood, semen and other fluids for a time. Because adult hepatocytes divide slowly, the episomes are not quickly lost, but liver injury or regrowth could reduce expression. The therapy cannot be repeated with the same capsid, because the body makes lasting antibodies against AAV5.", "sidefx": "ALT rises are the most common lab effect and reflect immune T cells attacking liver cells that display capsid pieces; steroids are used if enzymes climb. Headache, fatigue, nausea and flu-like symptoms reflect immune activation. Infusion reactions are immune responses to the capsid. Liver cancer risk is theoretical, tied to rare vector integration. Patients are monitored for factor IX levels and for any new inhibitor antibodies.", "fun": "The Padua variant was found because a young Italian man had unexplained blood clots, and his family turned out to carry a supercharged factor IX gene.", "src": ["Hemgenix (etranacogene dezaparvovec-drlb) FDA label", "Simioni P, et al. N Engl J Med, 2009", "Pipe SW, et al. N Engl J Med, 2023"]}, "valoctocogene-roxaparvovec": {"origin": "Hemophilia A, the most common form of hemophilia, results from too little clotting factor VIII. It famously ran in the royal families of Europe through descendants of Queen Victoria. The factor VIII gene is very large, too big to fit inside an AAV capsid, so researchers used a B-domain-deleted version, a shortened factor VIII that still works. BioMarin Pharmaceutical developed an AAV5 vector carrying this hFVIII-SQ gene with a liver-specific promoter. After approval in Europe in 2022, the FDA approved it as Roctavian in 2023 for adults with severe hemophilia A who do not have antibodies to AAV5. It was the first gene therapy for hemophilia A.", "timeline": [["1984", "The human factor VIII gene is cloned."], ["2022", "Roctavian receives conditional approval in Europe."], ["2023", "FDA approves Roctavian, the first gene therapy for severe hemophilia A."]], "cell": "Factor VIII is a cofactor that, once activated by thrombin, joins activated factor IX on platelet surfaces to activate factor X strongly. Without it, thrombin forms too slowly, and bleeding into joints and muscles follows. Valoctocogene roxaparvovec is a non-replicating AAV5 capsid holding a B-domain-deleted human factor VIII gene with a liver-specific promoter. After IV infusion it enters hepatocytes, and the DNA forms episomes in the nucleus. Hepatocytes, which do not normally make much factor VIII (that is mostly endothelial cells), are pushed to make and secrete it. Making a large secreted protein can stress the endoplasmic reticulum of liver cells, which may help explain falling expression over time.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Factor VIII activity rises and annual bleeds fall sharply for many patients, often allowing them to stop routine factor VIII prophylaxis."], ["Joints and muscles", "Fewer bleeds help protect joints from hemophilic arthropathy."], ["Liver", "ALT rises are common and may reflect immune attack on transduced cells or cell stress; corticosteroids are often needed."], ["Over time", "Factor VIII levels peak in the first year and then decline gradually over years, so the effect may not be lifelong."], ["Blood vessels", "Thromboembolic events have been reported, particularly in people with clot risk factors."]], "journey": "Roctavian is a single IV infusion. Most of the vector goes to the liver, and factor VIII activity rises over weeks to months, peaks within about a year, then slowly declines. Vector DNA is shed in semen and other fluids for weeks to months, so barrier contraception is advised for a time. It cannot be repeated, because patients make lasting antibodies against AAV5. People with prior AAV5 antibodies are screened out because those antibodies would block the vector.", "sidefx": "ALT elevation is the most common problem and can lower factor VIII expression if not controlled, so liver tests are checked often and steroids are given when needed; steroids themselves add side effects. Nausea, fatigue and headache follow infusion and immune activation. Infusion reactions are immune responses to the capsid. High factor VIII levels can contribute to blood clots. Long-term liver cancer risk is theoretical, from rare vector integration.", "fun": "Factor VIII is so large that its full gene would not fit inside an AAV capsid, so the therapy uses a trimmed version without the B domain, which turns out not to be needed for clotting.", "src": ["Roctavian (valoctocogene roxaparvovec-rvox) FDA label", "Ozelo MC, et al. N Engl J Med, 2022"]}, "alfentanil": {"origin": "Alfentanil comes from the family of synthetic opioids designed by Paul Janssen and his company, Janssen Pharmaceutica, in Belgium. After fentanyl in 1960, the Janssen team made a series of related molecules to fine-tune speed and duration. Alfentanil was made in the 1970s as a fentanyl analog that acts faster and wears off sooner, useful for short, painful procedures and anesthesia. It was approved in the US as Alfenta in the 1980s. Its unique chemistry, a low pKa that leaves most of the drug un-ionized in the blood, became a classic textbook example of how ionization controls how fast a drug enters the brain.", "timeline": [["1960", "Paul Janssen's team synthesizes fentanyl."], ["1970s", "Janssen Pharmaceutica develops alfentanil."], ["1980s", "FDA approves alfentanil (Alfenta) for anesthesia."]], "cell": "Alfentanil is a full agonist at mu-opioid receptors, which are Gi/o-coupled. Activation lowers cAMP by inhibiting adenylyl cyclase, opens G protein-coupled inward-rectifying potassium channels that hyperpolarize neurons, and closes presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels so less glutamate and substance P are released. This damps pain signals in the dorsal horn, brainstem and higher centers. In the brainstem it also reduces the respiratory center's response to carbon dioxide. Alfentanil's pKa is about 6.5, so about 90 percent is un-ionized at body pH. Even though it is less lipid soluble than fentanyl, this large un-ionized fraction lets it cross the blood-brain barrier fast, giving onset in about a minute.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Strong, fast analgesia and sedation for intubation, short procedures or as part of anesthesia."], ["Breathing", "The brainstem becomes less sensitive to carbon dioxide, so breathing slows or stops, which is why it is used where airway support is available."], ["Chest wall muscles", "Rapid large doses can cause chest wall rigidity, which makes ventilation hard and may need a neuromuscular blocker."], ["Heart", "Increased vagal tone slows the heart rate, and blood pressure can drop."], ["Pupils and gut", "Pupils constrict, and nausea, vomiting and slowed gut motility can occur."]], "journey": "Alfentanil is given IV. It reaches the brain in about one to one and a half minutes, and a single bolus lasts only about ten minutes as it redistributes. It has a small volume of distribution because it is highly bound to alpha-1 acid glycoprotein and less fat soluble than fentanyl, which keeps it from piling up in fat. The liver metabolizes it through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as erythromycin can prolong it. Its elimination half-life is about 1.5 to 1.8 hours, and metabolites leave in urine.", "sidefx": "Respiratory depression and apnea are direct results of mu receptor activation in the brainstem breathing centers. Chest wall rigidity is thought to come from opioid effects on brainstem and spinal motor pathways. Bradycardia comes from vagal stimulation. Nausea comes from the chemoreceptor trigger zone. Naloxone, a competitive mu antagonist, reverses opioid effects, though large doses and repeat dosing may be needed and pain returns as the opioid is blocked.", "fun": "Alfentanil reaches its peak brain effect faster than fentanyl even though it is less fat soluble, a classic lesson that the un-ionized fraction, not just lipid solubility, controls speed.", "src": ["Alfenta (alfentanil) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Miller's Anesthesia"]}, "prilocaine": {"origin": "Prilocaine is one of the amide local anesthetics that followed lidocaine, which Nils Lofgren and Bengt Lundqvist made in Stockholm in 1943. Lofgren continued this work, and in the 1950s he and colleagues at the Swedish company Astra synthesized prilocaine, a toluidine derivative. It entered clinical use around 1960. In the US it is best known as Citanest for dental anesthesia, plain or with epinephrine. It is also one half of EMLA cream, a mix of lidocaine and prilocaine that numbs intact skin. Prilocaine is famous among students for one unusual toxicity: it can turn hemoglobin into methemoglobin.", "timeline": [["1943", "Lofgren and Lundqvist synthesize lidocaine."], ["1950s", "Lofgren and colleagues at Astra synthesize prilocaine."], ["1960", "Prilocaine enters clinical use."], ["1980s", "EMLA cream (lidocaine and prilocaine) is introduced."]], "cell": "Prilocaine blocks voltage-gated sodium channels from inside the nerve cell. The uncharged base form crosses the nerve membrane; inside, it gains a proton, and the charged form binds a site within the channel pore. It binds best to open and inactivated channels, so actively firing nerves are blocked more readily. Sodium cannot enter, the membrane cannot depolarize, and the action potential cannot travel. Small unmyelinated C fibers and thin A-delta pain fibers are blocked first, then temperature, touch and motor fibers. Amidases in the liver, kidney and lungs break prilocaine into o-toluidine, and o-toluidine metabolites oxidize the iron in hemoglobin from ferrous to ferric, making methemoglobin.", "body": [["Nerves", "Sensory nerves stop sending pain signals, numbing the injected tooth or tissue."], ["Mouth and lips", "Soft tissue numbness lasts longer than the dental anesthesia, which can lead to lip or tongue biting, especially in children."], ["Blood", "Methemoglobin cannot carry oxygen, so high levels cause cyanosis, low pulse oximetry readings and tissue hypoxia."], ["Blood vessels", "Prilocaine causes less vasodilation than lidocaine, so it can work without epinephrine; the epinephrine form further narrows vessels to slow absorption."], ["Brain and heart", "If too much reaches the blood, sodium channel blockade can cause seizures and cardiac depression."]], "journey": "Prilocaine is injected near the nerve or into the tissue in dental work. Onset is two to four minutes. It is absorbed into the blood from the injection site, and epinephrine slows this. It is less protein bound than many amides and has a large volume of distribution. Unlike most amides, which are broken down only in the liver, prilocaine is also hydrolyzed in the kidneys and lungs, so it is cleared quickly. The products, including o-toluidine and N-propylalanine, leave in urine, and its half-life is about 1.6 hours.", "sidefx": "Methemoglobinemia is prilocaine's signature risk. Its o-toluidine metabolites oxidize hemoglobin iron, so the blood turns chocolate-brown and carries less oxygen. Infants, people with G6PD deficiency or congenital methemoglobinemia, and those taking other oxidizing drugs are more vulnerable. Methylene blue reverses it by helping reduce methemoglobin back to hemoglobin, though it is avoided in G6PD deficiency. Local anesthetic systemic toxicity causes seizures and cardiac arrest from widespread sodium channel blockade and is treated with lipid emulsion and resuscitation.", "fun": "Pulse oximeters tend to read around 85 percent in significant methemoglobinemia no matter how much oxygen is given, a classic clue on exams and in practice.", "src": ["Citanest Plain Dental (prilocaine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "bupivacaine-liposomal": {"origin": "Bupivacaine was synthesized in 1957 by Bo af Ekenstam in Sweden and entered clinical use in the 1960s as a long-acting amide local anesthetic. Even so, a single injection lasts only hours, and surgical pain lasts days. In the 1990s the San Diego company DepoTech developed DepoFoam, tiny multivesicular lipid particles that hold drug in many small chambers and release it slowly. Pacira Pharmaceuticals used this technology to load bupivacaine, and the FDA approved it as Exparel in 2011 for infiltration at the surgical site. Approval was later expanded to certain nerve blocks, as part of efforts to reduce opioid use after surgery.", "timeline": [["1957", "Bo af Ekenstam synthesizes bupivacaine."], ["1990s", "DepoTech develops DepoFoam multivesicular liposome technology."], ["2011", "FDA approves Exparel (liposomal bupivacaine)."], ["2018", "Exparel is approved for interscalene brachial plexus nerve block."]], "cell": "Bupivacaine blocks voltage-gated sodium channels on nerve membranes. The uncharged form enters the nerve, gains a proton inside, and the charged form binds within the channel pore, favoring open and inactivated states. Without sodium entry, action potentials cannot start or travel, so pain signals stop at the site. Bupivacaine binds the channel strongly and comes off slowly, which gives a long block. In the heart it also binds cardiac sodium channels tightly, which is why its toxicity is especially dangerous. The liposomes do not change the mechanism. They hold the drug in many tiny water-filled chambers inside lipid walls that break down over days, releasing bupivacaine gradually.", "body": [["Surgical site", "Slow release keeps nerves near the wound numb for up to about 72 hours, which can lower opioid needs."], ["Peripheral nerves", "When used as a nerve block, the area supplied by that nerve becomes numb and may be weak."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and constipation are common after surgery and may also reflect other drugs and the operation."], ["Heart", "Bupivacaine that reaches the blood can slow conduction and cause arrhythmias or cardiac arrest at toxic levels."], ["Brain", "High blood levels cause dizziness, metallic taste, ringing in the ears and then seizures."]], "journey": "Exparel is injected into the surgical site or around a nerve and is not given IV. A small free fraction acts quickly, then the liposomes release bupivacaine over several days. Blood levels show two peaks: an early one from free drug and a later one around 12 to 36 hours from liposome release. Absorbed bupivacaine is about 95 percent protein bound and is metabolized by liver CYP3A4 to pipecolylxylidine, which leaves in urine. Other local anesthetics, especially lidocaine, can cause an immediate release of bupivacaine from the liposomes, so timing between them matters.", "sidefx": "Local anesthetic systemic toxicity is the main danger: bupivacaine that reaches the blood blocks sodium channels in the brain and heart, causing seizures, arrhythmias and cardiac arrest. Bupivacaine binds cardiac sodium channels more tightly than lidocaine, making resuscitation harder. Lipid emulsion therapy is the specific rescue, thought to soak up the drug. Nerve block use can cause motor weakness and falls. Hypotension and bradycardia reflect autonomic and cardiac effects.", "fun": "A single DepoFoam particle looks like a tiny honeycomb under a microscope, with many separate chambers that open one after another.", "src": ["Exparel (bupivacaine liposome injectable suspension) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Miller's Anesthesia"]}, "edrophonium": {"origin": "In 1934 the Scottish physician Mary Walker showed that physostigmine, a cholinesterase inhibitor from the Calabar bean, quickly but briefly restored strength in myasthenia gravis. Physostigmine and neostigmine act for hours, so a faster, shorter agent was sought for testing. Edrophonium, a quaternary ammonium compound, was introduced by Hoffmann-La Roche around 1950 as Tensilon. In the early 1950s doctors began using the Tensilon test: a short IV dose that, in myasthenia, briefly improved a drooping eyelid or weak muscles. Antibody tests, nerve studies and the ice pack test have largely replaced it, and US supply has been very limited.", "timeline": [["1934", "Mary Walker shows physostigmine improves myasthenia gravis."], ["1950s", "Edrophonium (Tensilon) is introduced, and the Tensilon test enters practice."]], "cell": "In myasthenia gravis, antibodies attack nicotinic acetylcholine receptors on skeletal muscle, so fewer receptors are left to respond. Acetylcholinesterase normally breaks down acetylcholine in the synaptic cleft within milliseconds. Edrophonium binds acetylcholinesterase reversibly through an ionic bond at the anionic site, but unlike neostigmine it does not carbamylate the enzyme. It simply occupies the enzyme briefly. Acetylcholine lasts longer in the cleft and can bind the remaining receptors more times, which raises muscle strength for a few minutes. It also increases acetylcholine at muscarinic sites, causing parasympathetic effects. Its quaternary charge keeps it out of the brain.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "In myasthenia, strength improves within a minute and fades in five to ten minutes, which is the basis of the Tensilon test."], ["Heart", "Extra acetylcholine at muscarinic M2 receptors slows the heart and AV conduction, sometimes severely; atropine is kept at hand."], ["Airways", "Muscarinic activation narrows airways and increases secretions, so bronchospasm can occur, especially in asthma."], ["Glands", "Salivation, sweating and tearing increase."], ["Gut", "Cramping, nausea and diarrhea come from increased muscarinic gut activity."], ["Muscle fibers", "Fasciculations can appear as nicotinic receptors are overstimulated."]], "journey": "Edrophonium is given IV and starts working within 30 to 60 seconds. It peaks in about a minute and lasts only five to ten minutes, because it binds the enzyme loosely and comes off quickly. It does not cross the blood-brain barrier. It is barely metabolized and is cleared mainly by the kidneys unchanged, with a plasma half-life of about one to two hours.", "sidefx": "Side effects are cholinergic: extra acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors causes bradycardia, bronchospasm, salivation, sweating and cramps, and at nicotinic receptors causes fasciculations. In a patient already overdosed on cholinesterase inhibitors (cholinergic crisis), more acetylcholine can worsen weakness and breathing failure. Atropine blocks the muscarinic effects but not the nicotinic weakness. Because effects are short, most reactions pass quickly, but cardiac arrest has been reported.", "fun": "Mary Walker's 1934 finding with physostigmine became known as the miracle at St Alfege's, after the London hospital where she worked.", "src": ["Enlon (edrophonium chloride) FDA label", "Walker MB. Lancet, 1934", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "amisulpride": {"origin": "Amisulpride is a substituted benzamide, a relative of sulpiride and metoclopramide. It was developed in France by Synthelabo (later part of Sanofi) and has been used for decades in Europe and elsewhere as an oral antipsychotic, but it was never approved in the US for psychosis. Researchers at the British company Acacia Pharma noticed that very low doses blocked the dopamine receptors that trigger nausea without the usual sedation or movement effects. They developed an IV form for postoperative nausea and vomiting, and the FDA approved it as Barhemsys in 2020. It was the first antiemetic approved in the US for treating nausea and vomiting after surgery in patients whose earlier prophylaxis had failed.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Amisulpride is developed in France as an antipsychotic."], ["2020", "FDA approves IV amisulpride (Barhemsys) for postoperative nausea and vomiting."]], "cell": "Nausea and vomiting after surgery are driven partly by the chemoreceptor trigger zone in the area postrema, a brain region outside the blood-brain barrier that samples the blood for toxins and drugs. Dopamine D2 and D3 receptors there send signals to the vomiting center in the medulla. Amisulpride selectively and competitively blocks D2 and D3 receptors, so these signals are damped. It has little affinity for serotonin, histamine, alpha-adrenergic or muscarinic receptors, which is why it causes less sedation, dry mouth and low blood pressure than older dopamine antagonists such as droperidol or prochlorperazine. Blocking D2 in the pituitary raises prolactin.", "body": [["Brain (area postrema)", "Blocking dopamine signals in the trigger zone reduces nausea and vomiting after surgery."], ["Heart", "It can prolong the QT interval in a dose-dependent way, so it is avoided in people with long QT and used with care with other QT-prolonging drugs."], ["Pituitary", "Prolactin rises because dopamine normally suppresses prolactin release."], ["Movement", "Extrapyramidal effects are uncommon at antiemetic doses but possible as with other D2 blockers."], ["Injection site", "Infusion site pain is among the common effects."]], "journey": "Barhemsys is given IV as a short injection and works within minutes. It is only about 25 to 30 percent protein bound and is barely metabolized. The kidneys clear most of it unchanged, with a half-life of about four to five hours. Severe kidney disease raises levels.", "sidefx": "QT prolongation happens because amisulpride, like many D2 antagonists, can block cardiac hERG potassium channels at higher levels, delaying repolarization; torsades de pointes is rare. Prolactin rise reflects pituitary D2 blockade. Infusion site pain, chills, low potassium and low blood pressure around surgery have been reported. Extrapyramidal symptoms are possible but uncommon. There is no antidote; acute dystonia from D2 blockade is typically treated with diphenhydramine or benztropine.", "fun": "The antiemetic dose of amisulpride is a small fraction of the doses used to treat schizophrenia in Europe, which is why it causes so little sedation.", "src": ["Barhemsys (amisulpride) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "oliceridine": {"origin": "Oliceridine came from the idea of biased agonism. Opioid receptors signal through G proteins and through a second pathway involving beta-arrestin-2. In 1999 Laura Bohn and colleagues in Robert Lefkowitz and Marc Caron's group at Duke reported that mice lacking beta-arrestin-2 had stronger morphine pain relief, and later work suggested less respiratory depression and constipation. That led Trevena, a company co-founded by Lefkowitz, to design oliceridine, a mu-opioid agonist that favors G protein signaling. After an initial FDA rejection in 2018, it was approved as Olinvyk in 2020 for severe acute pain in hospitals. Later research has questioned how much the beta-arrestin idea explains respiratory depression, and the label carries full opioid warnings.", "timeline": [["1999", "Bohn and colleagues report enhanced morphine analgesia in beta-arrestin-2 knockout mice."], ["2018", "FDA issues a complete response letter for oliceridine."], ["2020", "FDA approves Olinvyk (oliceridine) for severe acute pain."]], "cell": "Oliceridine binds the mu-opioid receptor and activates its Gi/o protein. This lowers cAMP, opens GIRK potassium channels, which hyperpolarize neurons, and closes presynaptic calcium channels, which reduces release of pain transmitters in the dorsal horn and brain. Compared with morphine, it recruits less beta-arrestin-2, the protein that ends G protein signaling, triggers receptor internalization and starts other signaling. In theory this keeps analgesia while lowering respiratory depression and gut slowing. In practice, oliceridine still causes respiratory depression in a dose-related way. Low intrinsic efficacy may explain part of its profile.", "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Mu receptor activation reduces pain signaling and the emotional response to pain."], ["Breathing", "Respiratory depression still occurs, especially with benzodiazepines or other sedatives."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and constipation occur, though trial rates were somewhat lower than with morphine at similar pain relief."], ["Heart", "QT prolongation was seen at higher cumulative exposure, which limits the total daily amount."], ["Skin", "Itching can occur, as with other opioids."]], "journey": "Oliceridine is given IV in the hospital, as a bolus or patient-controlled dose. It works within two to five minutes and lasts one to three hours. It is about 77 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it through CYP3A4 and CYP2D6 to inactive metabolites, so CYP2D6 poor metabolizers and people on strong CYP3A4 or CYP2D6 inhibitors have higher levels. The half-life is about 1.3 to 3 hours, and metabolites leave in urine and feces.", "sidefx": "Like all mu agonists, it can cause life-threatening respiratory depression, abuse and addiction, and dangerous sedation with benzodiazepines, all boxed warnings. These come from mu receptor effects in the brainstem and reward pathways. Nausea comes from the chemoreceptor trigger zone. QT prolongation limits total exposure. Naloxone reverses its effects as with other opioids.", "fun": "Oliceridine was the first drug approved that was designed around the concept of a biased agonist at a G protein-coupled receptor.", "src": ["Olinvyk (oliceridine) FDA label", "Bohn LM, et al. Science, 1999", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "dengue-vaccine": {"origin": "Dengvaxia was built on the back of another famous vaccine. In the 1930s Max Theiler at the Rockefeller Foundation created the yellow fever 17D strain, a live weakened virus, and won the 1951 Nobel Prize. Decades later the company Acambis developed ChimeriVax, a method of swapping 17D's outer protein genes for those of related viruses. Sanofi Pasteur used this to make a vaccine with the outer genes of all four dengue serotypes. It was first approved in Mexico and the Philippines in 2015. A 2017 analysis showed people never infected before had a higher risk of severe dengue after vaccination, leading to controversy in the Philippines. The FDA approved it in 2019 only for children 9 to 16 with lab-confirmed prior dengue living in endemic US areas. Sanofi later announced it would stop making it because of low demand.", "timeline": [["1930s", "Max Theiler develops the yellow fever 17D vaccine strain."], ["2015", "Dengvaxia is first approved, in Mexico and the Philippines."], ["2017", "Data show higher severe dengue risk in vaccinated people with no prior infection."], ["2019", "FDA approves Dengvaxia for children 9 to 16 with confirmed prior dengue in endemic areas."]], "cell": "Dengvaxia is a live attenuated chimeric virus: the yellow fever 17D backbone provides replication machinery, while the prM and envelope (E) genes come from dengue serotypes 1 to 4. After injection, the vaccine viruses replicate a little, and dendritic cells take up the dengue proteins and present them to T cells, which help B cells make neutralizing antibodies against each serotype. In someone who has had dengue before, the vaccine acts like a second exposure and gives broad, strong protection. In someone who has never had dengue, it acts like a first infection. Then a later natural infection can behave like a second infection, where weakly binding antibodies help the virus enter Fc receptor-bearing cells (antibody-dependent enhancement) and severe dengue becomes more likely.", "body": [["Immune system", "Neutralizing antibodies to all four dengue serotypes form after the three-dose series."], ["Previously infected children", "In those with prior dengue, it lowers the risk of dengue illness, hospitalization and severe disease."], ["Never-infected recipients", "Without prior infection, a later natural infection is more likely to be severe, which is why lab confirmation of prior infection is required."], ["Injection site and whole body", "Pain at the site, headache, malaise, weakness and muscle aches are common for a few days."]], "journey": "Dengvaxia is given as three injections six months apart. The live vaccine viruses replicate at low levels near the injection site and in lymph tissue, then the immune system clears them. Antibodies rise after each dose, and protection is strongest after the third, though it wanes over several years. As a live vaccine, it is avoided in people with weakened immune systems and in pregnancy.", "sidefx": "Common reactions such as soreness, headache and aches are the expected signs of immune activation. The serious risk is severe dengue after later natural infection in people who were dengue-naive when vaccinated, explained by antibody-dependent enhancement. Allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, can occur, as with any vaccine. As a live vaccine it can cause problems in immunocompromised people. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Dengvaxia's backbone is the same yellow fever 17D strain that Max Theiler made in the 1930s, one of the most successful vaccines ever created.", "src": ["Dengvaxia (dengue tetravalent vaccine, live) FDA label", "CDC dengue vaccine recommendations", "Sridhar S, et al. N Engl J Med, 2018"]}, "sterile-water": {"origin": "Sterile Water for Injection is not a drug but the foundation of many. Early IV infusions in the 1900s were often followed by sudden fevers and chills, and no one knew why. In the 1920s the American biochemist Florence Seibert showed that the culprit was pyrogens, heat-stable substances from bacteria that survived ordinary sterilization in distilled water. She designed a distillation apparatus that kept spray from carrying pyrogens into the product. Her work led to pyrogen-free water standards that made modern IV therapy safe. Today Sterile Water for Injection, USP, is distilled or purified, sterile, nonpyrogenic and free of any solute, and is used mainly to dissolve and dilute other drugs.", "timeline": [["1920s", "Florence Seibert shows that bacterial pyrogens cause fevers after IV injections and develops a way to remove them."], ["1940s", "Pyrogen testing becomes part of US Pharmacopeia standards for injections."]], "cell": "Sterile water has no pharmacologic target. It works as a solvent. Its importance comes from osmosis. Plain water has an osmolality of zero, while blood is about 285 to 295 mOsm/kg. If it enters the blood without enough solute, water moves across cell membranes from the hypotonic plasma into red blood cells, which have higher osmolality. Red cells swell and burst (hemolysis), releasing potassium and hemoglobin. Free water also lowers plasma sodium and shifts into brain cells, which can cause cerebral edema. That is why sterile water is used only after drugs or solutes are added to make the final solution suitable.", "body": [["Drug preparation", "Dissolves powdered drugs and dilutes concentrated ones so they can be given safely."], ["Red blood cells", "If given IV alone in volume, water rushes into red cells and makes them burst."], ["Kidneys", "Released hemoglobin can injure the kidneys, causing acute kidney injury."], ["Blood potassium", "Burst red cells spill potassium into the blood, which can cause dangerous heart rhythms."], ["Brain", "Large amounts of free water lower plasma sodium and swell brain cells, causing headache, confusion, seizures or coma."]], "journey": "Sterile water is not given by itself in large IV amounts; it serves as a vehicle. Once a diluted drug enters the blood, the water simply joins total body water and distributes across cells. It is not metabolized. The kidneys excrete excess free water, which they can do efficiently when they work normally.", "sidefx": "When used correctly as a diluent, it has no side effects of its own. The harm comes from mistakes: infusing plain sterile water, especially in large volumes, causes hemolysis from osmotic swelling of red cells, hyperkalemia from released potassium, kidney injury from hemoglobin, and hypotonic hyponatremia with brain swelling. There is no antidote; treatment is supportive, with care of potassium, sodium and kidney function. Mix-ups with similar-looking bags of IV fluid are a known cause.", "fun": "Florence Seibert, who made pyrogen-free IV water possible, is also known for purifying the tuberculin protein used in the TB skin test.", "src": ["Sterile Water for Injection, USP FDA labeling", "Seibert FB. Am J Physiol, 1923", "ISMP safety alerts on sterile water"]}, "tromethamine": {"origin": "Tromethamine, also called THAM or Tris, began as a laboratory buffer. Biochemists used tris(hydroxymethyl)aminomethane to hold the pH of solutions steady, and it is still in many lab buffers today. In the late 1950s researchers, notably Gabriel Nahas, studied it as a way to treat acidosis in patients without adding sodium or making carbon dioxide, problems seen with sodium bicarbonate. It became available in the US as THAM Solution for treating metabolic acidosis, especially during cardiac bypass surgery and cardiac arrest. Its use today is limited and specialized.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Tris buffer is studied as a clinical treatment for acidosis, notably by Gabriel Nahas."], ["1960s", "THAM solution enters clinical use in the US."]], "cell": "Tromethamine is not a receptor drug. It is a weak base with a pKa of about 7.8, close to body pH, which makes it a good buffer. It accepts hydrogen ions: THAM plus H+ becomes THAM-H+. It can buffer both metabolic acids and carbonic acid. When it takes a proton from carbonic acid, bicarbonate is left behind, and carbon dioxide falls rather than rises, unlike sodium bicarbonate, which generates CO2. About 30 percent of THAM is un-ionized at body pH and can enter cells, so it also buffers inside cells. The protonated form is excreted by the kidneys, carrying acid out and causing an osmotic diuresis.", "body": [["Blood chemistry", "Blood pH rises and PaCO2 falls because hydrogen ions are bound without making carbon dioxide."], ["Breathing", "Lower CO2 reduces the drive to breathe, which can depress ventilation, especially in patients not on a ventilator."], ["Kidneys", "Protonated THAM is excreted in urine, carrying acid out and producing an osmotic diuresis."], ["Blood sugar", "THAM can lower blood glucose, possibly by increasing insulin release and cell glucose uptake."], ["Potassium", "It can raise blood potassium, particularly in kidney failure."], ["Veins and tissues", "The alkaline solution irritates veins and can cause tissue death if it leaks."]], "journey": "THAM is given by IV infusion, ideally into a large vein. It works within minutes. It is not metabolized. It distributes into extracellular fluid and partly into cells. The kidneys excrete the protonated form, with a half-life of about five to six hours in normal kidney function; in kidney failure it builds up, so it is used with great caution or avoided.", "sidefx": "Respiratory depression comes from lowering CO2 and raising pH, which both reduce the drive to breathe, which is why ventilation support should be available. Hypoglycemia, which can last hours, and hyperkalemia are known metabolic effects. The alkaline solution causes venospasm and phlebitis and, if extravasated, tissue necrosis. Use through the umbilical vein in newborns has been linked to liver necrosis. Accumulation in kidney failure increases toxicity. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Tris is one of the most common buffers in biology labs worldwide, used in everything from DNA gels to protein experiments.", "src": ["THAM Solution (tromethamine injection) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Nahas GG. Pharmacol Rev, 1962"]}, "multivitamin-infusion": {"origin": "The story of IV multivitamins joins two histories. The first is the discovery of vitamins: James Lind's 1747 citrus trial for scurvy, Christiaan Eijkman's 1890s work on beriberi in chickens fed polished rice, and Casimir Funk's 1912 coining of the word vitamine. The second is IV nutrition. In the late 1960s Stanley Dudrick and colleagues at the University of Pennsylvania showed that beagle puppies, then infants, could grow on IV nutrition alone. Patients fed only by vein soon showed vitamin deficiencies, so standardized IV multivitamin products followed. Around 2000 adult formulas such as Infuvite Adult and M.V.I. Adult were updated to add vitamin K and increase several B vitamins, giving 13 vitamins in one product.", "timeline": [["1747", "James Lind's trial shows citrus fruit cures scurvy."], ["1912", "Casimir Funk coins the term vitamine."], ["1960s", "Stanley Dudrick and colleagues develop total parenteral nutrition."], ["2000", "Adult IV multivitamin formulas are updated to include vitamin K."]], "cell": "Each vitamin feeds a different set of enzymes. Thiamine becomes thiamine pyrophosphate, a cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase and transketolase, so without it cells cannot burn glucose well, which harms neurons first. Riboflavin forms FAD and FMN, and niacinamide forms NAD and NADP, the electron carriers of metabolism. Pyridoxine becomes pyridoxal phosphate for transaminases and neurotransmitter synthesis. Folic acid and B12 run one-carbon transfers needed for DNA synthesis. Biotin and pantothenic acid support carboxylases and coenzyme A. Vitamin K enables gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors II, VII, IX and X. Vitamin A acts in the retina and on nuclear receptors, vitamin D on the vitamin D receptor, vitamin E as a membrane antioxidant, and vitamin C for collagen hydroxylation.", "body": [["Brain and nerves", "Thiamine prevents Wernicke encephalopathy, and B6 and B12 protect peripheral nerves."], ["Blood", "Folic acid and B12 prevent megaloblastic anemia, and vitamin K keeps clotting factors working."], ["Skin, gums and wounds", "Vitamin C maintains collagen, preventing scurvy, while niacin prevents pellagra's rash."], ["Eyes", "Vitamin A supports night vision and the surface of the eye."], ["Bones", "Vitamin D supports calcium absorption and bone mineralization."], ["Kidneys and urine", "Excess water-soluble vitamins are cleared in urine; riboflavin turns it bright yellow."]], "journey": "The vitamins are added to an IV nutrition bag or an IV fluid and enter the blood directly, bypassing the gut. Water-soluble vitamins (B group and C) circulate and are taken up by cells, and any excess is cleared quickly in urine. Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E and K) bind carrier proteins and are stored in the liver and fat, so they can build up with long use. Some vitamins break down with light, so bags may be protected from light.", "sidefx": "Bright yellow urine is harmless and comes from riboflavin. Allergic reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, have been linked especially to thiamine. Vitamin K in the product can counteract warfarin, so INR is watched. With long-term use, fat-soluble vitamins, especially vitamin A, can accumulate, particularly in kidney failure. There is no antidote; reactions are managed by stopping the infusion and supportive care.", "fun": "The bright yellow banana bag used in emergency departments for alcohol-related malnutrition gets its color from riboflavin in the multivitamin.", "src": ["Infuvite Adult and M.V.I. Adult FDA labels", "ASPEN parenteral nutrition guidelines", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "trace-elements": {"origin": "As IV nutrition spread after Stanley Dudrick's work in the late 1960s, patients fed only by vein began showing deficiencies of trace metals such as zinc (rash and poor healing), copper (anemia and low white counts) and selenium (heart muscle disease). For years hospitals used older trace element mixes that were marketed without full FDA approval. In the late 2010s the FDA pushed for approved, updated products with amounts that matched modern recommendations. Tralement, a four-element adult product with zinc, copper, manganese and selenium from American Regent, was approved around 2020, followed by Multrys for neonates and children. These products lowered the manganese content of older mixes, which had been linked to brain accumulation.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Total parenteral nutrition is developed, revealing trace element deficiencies."], ["2020", "FDA approves Tralement, an updated four-element trace element product."]], "cell": "Each element is a cofactor for specific enzymes. Zinc sits in more than 300 metalloenzymes, including carbonic anhydrase, alkaline phosphatase and DNA and RNA polymerases, and in zinc-finger proteins that control gene transcription, which matters for cell division and healing. Copper is needed for ceruloplasmin, which oxidizes iron so it can load onto transferrin, for cytochrome c oxidase in the mitochondria, for superoxide dismutase and for lysyl oxidase, which cross-links collagen and elastin. Manganese supports manganese superoxide dismutase, arginase and pyruvate carboxylase. Selenium is built into selenocysteine in glutathione peroxidase, thioredoxin reductase and the deiodinases that turn T4 into T3.", "body": [["Skin and wound healing", "Zinc supports rapid cell division, preventing the scaly rash, hair loss and poor wound healing of zinc deficiency."], ["Blood", "Copper lets iron be used to make red cells, preventing anemia and neutropenia."], ["Heart and muscles", "Selenium protects cells from oxidative damage, preventing cardiomyopathy and muscle weakness."], ["Thyroid", "Selenium-dependent deiodinases activate thyroid hormone."], ["Liver and brain", "Copper and manganese are cleared through bile, so liver disease can let them build up, and manganese can deposit in the brain's basal ganglia."]], "journey": "The elements are added to the IV nutrition bag and enter the blood directly. Copper binds ceruloplasmin and zinc binds albumin in the blood. They are taken up by tissues and built into enzymes. Zinc and selenium are cleared mainly in urine, while copper and manganese are cleared mainly through bile into the stool, which is why blocked bile flow or liver disease can cause buildup.", "sidefx": "At labeled amounts side effects are uncommon. Copper and manganese can build up in cholestatic liver disease because bile is their exit route; manganese excess can cause parkinsonism-like movement problems. Precipitates in nutrition mixtures, if they form, can lodge in the lung vessels, which is why compatibility and filters matter. Hypersensitivity is rare. There is no antidote; excess is managed by reducing or stopping the elements.", "fun": "Zinc deficiency in IV nutrition can cause a rash around the mouth and buttocks that looks like the inherited disorder acrodermatitis enteropathica.", "src": ["Tralement (trace elements injection 4) FDA label", "ASPEN parenteral nutrition guidelines"]}, "selenium": {"origin": "Selenium was discovered in 1817 by the Swedish chemist Jons Jacob Berzelius, who found it in sludge from a sulfuric acid plant and named it after Selene, the Greek goddess of the moon. For a long time it was known mainly as a toxin, because livestock grazing on selenium-rich plants became sick. In 1957 Klaus Schwarz and Calvin Foltz showed it was an essential nutrient. In China, a deadly heart muscle disease called Keshan disease was linked to low soil selenium and prevented with supplements in the 1970s. In the US, patients on long-term IV nutrition developed selenium deficiency, and Selenious Acid Injection from American Regent later received FDA approval as an IV nutrition additive.", "timeline": [["1817", "Jons Jacob Berzelius discovers selenium."], ["1957", "Schwarz and Foltz show selenium is an essential nutrient."], ["1970s", "Selenium supplementation is shown to prevent Keshan disease in China."]], "cell": "Selenium is built into the amino acid selenocysteine, sometimes called the 21st amino acid, which is inserted into proteins at a special UGA codon. These selenoproteins include glutathione peroxidases, which use glutathione to break down hydrogen peroxide and lipid peroxides before they damage membranes; thioredoxin reductases, which keep cellular redox balance; and the iodothyronine deiodinases, which convert T4 to the active thyroid hormone T3. Selenoprotein P carries selenium in the blood to tissues. Without selenium, cells, especially heart and skeletal muscle cells, are more open to oxidative injury.", "body": [["Heart", "Adequate selenium protects heart muscle from oxidative damage, preventing the cardiomyopathy seen in deficiency."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Deficiency causes muscle pain and weakness, which correct with replacement."], ["Thyroid", "Deiodinases need selenium to activate thyroid hormone."], ["Immune system", "Selenoproteins support immune cell function and antioxidant defense."], ["Hair and nails", "Deficiency can cause whitening of the nail beds, while excess causes brittle nails and hair loss."]], "journey": "Selenious acid is added to IV nutrition and enters the blood directly. It is carried mainly by selenoprotein P and albumin and is built into selenoproteins in the liver, muscle and other tissues, which act as long-term stores. Excess selenium is methylated and leaves mainly in urine. With larger excess, some is breathed out as dimethyl selenide, which smells like garlic.", "sidefx": "At labeled amounts side effects are rare. Selenium has a narrow margin between need and toxicity. Too much over time causes selenosis: garlic breath, hair loss, brittle nails, skin rash, fatigue and nerve problems, because excess selenium generates reactive oxygen species and interferes with sulfur-containing proteins. Acute poisoning can cause heart and gut toxicity. Precipitates in nutrition mixtures can cause lung emboli. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Berzelius named selenium for the moon because it closely resembled tellurium, an element already named for the Earth.", "src": ["Selenious Acid Injection FDA label", "Schwarz K, Foltz CM. J Am Chem Soc, 1957", "ASPEN parenteral nutrition guidelines"]}, "copper": {"origin": "Copper was one of the first metals humans used, worked into tools more than ten thousand years ago. Its role in the body came into view much later. In the 1920s and 1930s researchers at the University of Wisconsin showed that rats fed only milk needed copper as well as iron to make hemoglobin. Two genetic diseases revealed its balance: Wilson disease, described by S. A. Kinnier Wilson in 1912, in which copper builds up, and Menkes disease, described by John Menkes in 1962, in which copper cannot be absorbed. Copper deficiency also appeared in patients on long-term IV nutrition and after gastric bypass surgery. Cupric chloride injection is used as a copper additive in IV nutrition.", "timeline": [["1912", "S. A. Kinnier Wilson describes the copper overload disease that bears his name."], ["1928", "University of Wisconsin researchers show copper is needed with iron to make hemoglobin."], ["1962", "John Menkes describes the copper deficiency disorder later named for him."]], "cell": "Copper is a cofactor that moves electrons for several key enzymes. Ceruloplasmin, made in the liver, is a ferroxidase that converts iron from ferrous to ferric so it can bind transferrin and reach the bone marrow. Cytochrome c oxidase, complex IV of the mitochondrial respiratory chain, uses copper to pass electrons to oxygen and make ATP. Copper-zinc superoxide dismutase removes superoxide radicals. Lysyl oxidase cross-links collagen and elastin. Dopamine beta-hydroxylase turns dopamine into norepinephrine, and tyrosinase starts melanin production. Low copper slows all of these.", "body": [["Blood", "Copper lets iron be moved and used, so deficiency causes anemia that does not respond to iron and low white cell counts."], ["Nerves and spinal cord", "Deficiency can damage the spinal cord and nerves, causing a gait problem similar to B12 deficiency."], ["Connective tissue and vessels", "Lysyl oxidase needs copper to cross-link collagen and elastin, which keeps vessels and bones strong."], ["Skin and hair", "Tyrosinase needs copper to make melanin, so deficiency can lighten skin and hair."], ["Liver", "The liver stores copper and excretes extra in bile, so liver disease can lead to buildup and injury."]], "journey": "Cupric chloride is added to IV nutrition and goes straight into the blood. The liver takes up copper and builds it into ceruloplasmin, which carries about 95 percent of plasma copper. The liver is the main store. Copper leaves mainly through bile into stool, with only a small amount in urine, so blocked bile flow raises body copper. Stores last a long time, so deficiency develops slowly.", "sidefx": "At labeled amounts side effects are uncommon. Excess copper builds up in the liver, especially with cholestasis, and causes liver injury because free copper generates reactive oxygen species. Large excess can cause hemolysis because free copper damages red cell membranes. Hypersensitivity is rare. Chelating agents such as penicillamine are used for chronic copper overload in Wilson disease, but for IV nutrition excess the usual step is reducing or stopping copper.", "fun": "Some sea creatures, such as octopuses and horseshoe crabs, use a copper protein called hemocyanin instead of hemoglobin, which makes their blood blue.", "src": ["Cupric Chloride Injection, USP FDA labeling", "ASPEN parenteral nutrition guidelines", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "activated-charcoal-sorbitol": {"origin": "Charcoal has long been used for poisoning, and two French scientists made the point dramatically. In 1813 the chemist Michel Bertrand swallowed arsenic mixed with charcoal and survived. In 1831 the pharmacist Pierre-Fleury Touery swallowed a large dose of strychnine mixed with charcoal in front of the French Academy of Medicine and suffered no ill effects. In the early 1900s, activating charcoal with steam or chemicals greatly increased its surface area. Sorbitol, a sugar alcohol found in fruits such as rowan berries, was added to some charcoal products as a cathartic to speed the charcoal and poison through the gut. Products such as Actidose with Sorbitol followed. Toxicology groups now say cathartics have not been shown to improve outcomes, and charcoal is used selectively.", "timeline": [["1813", "Michel Bertrand survives swallowing arsenic mixed with charcoal."], ["1831", "Pierre-Fleury Touery swallows strychnine with charcoal before the French Academy of Medicine."], ["1900s", "Activation processes greatly increase charcoal's adsorptive surface area."], ["2000s", "Toxicology position statements advise against routine cathartic use."]], "cell": "Activated charcoal is not absorbed and has no receptor target. It is carbon heated with steam or gases so it is riddled with tiny pores, giving a huge surface area. Many drug and toxin molecules stick to this surface through weak physical forces (adsorption), so they stay in the gut instead of crossing into the blood. It works best on large, uncharged organic molecules. It binds alcohols, iron, lithium, strong acids and alkalis, and other metals poorly. Sorbitol is a poorly absorbed polyol. It stays in the bowel and draws water in by osmosis, which loosens stool and speeds transit. Gut bacteria ferment some of it, producing gas.", "body": [["Stomach and intestines", "Charcoal holds many toxins in the gut lumen so less is absorbed, with the greatest benefit if given within about an hour of ingestion."], ["Bowel", "Sorbitol pulls water into the bowel, causing loose stools and cramping."], ["Fluids and electrolytes", "Repeated sorbitol can cause dehydration and electrolyte problems, including high sodium, especially in children."], ["Lungs", "If vomited and inhaled, charcoal can cause severe aspiration pneumonitis or ARDS, which is why airway protection matters."], ["Stool", "Stools turn black as the unabsorbed charcoal passes through, which is expected and harmless."]], "journey": "The product is swallowed or given through a gastric tube. Charcoal begins binding toxin as soon as it touches it, and it is not absorbed or metabolized. It travels through the gut and leaves in the stool, carrying bound toxin. Sorbitol is mostly not absorbed. It pulls water into the bowel and is partly fermented by colon bacteria, producing a bowel movement within hours.", "sidefx": "Black stool, vomiting, cramping and diarrhea are expected. Vomiting is a key danger because aspirated charcoal injures the lungs, so it is avoided in patients who cannot protect their airway. Sorbitol's osmotic pull can cause dehydration, hypernatremia and electrolyte shifts, especially with repeated doses or in small children, which is why the sorbitol form is generally used for one dose at most. Charcoal can cause bowel obstruction when gut motility is poor. It is not used for caustics or hydrocarbons.", "fun": "Repeated doses of plain charcoal can pull some drugs, such as phenobarbital, back out of the blood across the gut wall, a process nicknamed gut dialysis.", "src": ["Actidose with Sorbitol product labeling", "AACT/EAPCCT position papers on single-dose activated charcoal and cathartics", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]}, "alteplase-catheter-clearance": {"origin": "Tissue plasminogen activator (tPA) is a natural human enzyme that dissolves clots. In the late 1970s and early 1980s Desire Collen and colleagues in Leuven, Belgium, purified it from a human melanoma cell line that made large amounts. In 1983 Genentech scientists cloned the gene, and recombinant alteplase was approved as Activase in 1987 for heart attacks. Central venous catheters often become blocked by small clots at the tip or in the lumen. Genentech developed a small-volume form designed to be instilled into the catheter itself, approved as Cathflo Activase in 2001 to restore function to blocked central venous access devices.", "timeline": [["1981", "Rijken and Collen report purifying tPA from a human melanoma cell line."], ["1983", "Genentech scientists clone the tPA gene."], ["1987", "FDA approves Activase (alteplase) for heart attacks."], ["2001", "FDA approves Cathflo Activase for occluded central venous catheters."]], "cell": "Alteplase is a glycosylated serine protease. It has finger and kringle domains that bind fibrin, so it works best on plasminogen that is already sitting on a fibrin clot. Once bound, it cleaves plasminogen at the Arg561-Val562 bond, turning it into plasmin. Plasmin then breaks fibrin strands into soluble fibrin degradation products, and the clot falls apart. In catheter clearance the drug is placed inside the blocked lumen and left to dwell. It acts on the fibrin clot or fibrin sheath inside or at the tip of the catheter, restoring the ability to draw blood and infuse.", "body": [["Catheter lumen", "Fibrin plugs dissolve, and many catheters can be used again within 30 to 120 minutes."], ["Bloodstream", "Only a tiny amount reaches the circulation, so whole-body clot breakdown is minimal."], ["Bleeding risk", "Small risk of bleeding exists, especially in patients with conditions that already raise bleeding risk."], ["Infection", "Clearing an infected clot can release bacteria into the blood, so catheter-related sepsis is a known risk."], ["Catheter itself", "Forceful instillation into a fully blocked catheter can rupture it."]], "journey": "Cathflo is reconstituted and gently instilled into the blocked catheter, then left to dwell. Most of it stays in the catheter. After the dwell, it is withdrawn rather than flushed into the patient. Any alteplase that does reach the blood is cleared very quickly by the liver, with a half-life of about five minutes.", "sidefx": "Because so little drug reaches the circulation, side effects are uncommon. Bleeding is possible from plasmin's fibrin breakdown. If the catheter is infected, dissolving the clot can release bacteria and cause sepsis. Forcing fluid into a fully blocked line can tear the catheter. Rarely, a dislodged clot can travel as an embolus. Hypersensitivity is rare. Systemic bleeding from alteplase can be countered with antifibrinolytics such as aminocaproic acid or tranexamic acid and with cryoprecipitate.", "fun": "The melanoma cell line used to purify tPA, called Bowes, made the enzyme in unusually large amounts, which made the first studies possible.", "src": ["Cathflo Activase (alteplase) FDA label", "Pennica D, et al. Nature, 1983", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "polymyxin-b": {"origin": "Polymyxin B came from a soil bacterium. In 1947 three research groups in the US and Britain independently reported antibiotics from Bacillus polymyxa (now Paenibacillus polymyxa) that killed gram-negative bacteria. One of these, polymyxin B, became a clinical drug. By the 1970s it fell out of favor because of kidney and nerve toxicity and the arrival of safer antibiotics. In the 2000s, as Acinetobacter, Pseudomonas and Klebsiella strains became resistant to almost everything, polymyxin B came back as a last-resort IV antibiotic.", "timeline": [["1947", "Three groups independently report polymyxins from Bacillus polymyxa."], ["1970s", "Use declines because of kidney and nerve toxicity."], ["2000s", "Polymyxins return as last-line agents for multidrug-resistant gram-negatives."]], "cell": "Polymyxin B is a cationic cyclic lipopeptide with a fatty acid tail. Gram-negative bacteria have an outer membrane whose lipopolysaccharide (LPS) carries negatively charged phosphate groups on lipid A, held together by calcium and magnesium bridges. Polymyxin B's positive amino groups bind these phosphates and push out the calcium and magnesium. Its fatty tail then inserts into the membrane, acting like a detergent. The outer membrane becomes leaky, the drug reaches and disrupts the inner membrane, and cell contents spill out, killing the bacteria quickly in a concentration-dependent way. It also binds and neutralizes endotoxin. Resistance arises when bacteria add positive groups to lipid A, such as through the plasmid gene mcr-1, so the drug cannot bind.", "body": [["Bacteria", "Rapidly kills Pseudomonas, Acinetobacter, Klebsiella and E. coli; Proteus, Serratia, Providencia, Burkholderia and gram-positives are naturally resistant."], ["Kidneys", "Tubule cells take up the drug and are damaged, raising creatinine and causing protein and casts in urine."], ["Nerves", "Numbness around the mouth and in the limbs, dizziness and confusion reflect effects on nerve membranes."], ["Neuromuscular junction", "It can block acetylcholine release, causing muscle weakness and, rarely, breathing paralysis."], ["Skin", "Skin darkening (hyperpigmentation) has been reported with polymyxin B."]], "journey": "Polymyxin B is given by IV infusion; it is not absorbed by mouth. It is about half protein bound. Unlike colistimethate, it is given in active form and is cleared mostly by nonrenal routes, with only a small fraction in urine, so it reaches lower urine levels and is less favored for urinary infections. Its half-life is variable, often about 6 to 12 hours in critically ill patients.", "sidefx": "Nephrotoxicity comes from the drug's detergent action on kidney tubule cell membranes after uptake, so kidney function is followed closely. Neurotoxicity, such as tingling and dizziness, reflects similar effects on nerve membranes. Neuromuscular blockade, more likely with anesthetics or other neuromuscular blockers, can stop breathing and carries a boxed warning along with kidney and nerve toxicity. Calcium or neostigmine may not fully reverse it, so ventilation support may be needed. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Polymyxin B is also bound to fibers in cartridges used outside the US to filter endotoxin out of the blood in septic shock.", "src": ["Polymyxin B for Injection FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "International consensus guidelines for polymyxin use, 2019"]}, "colistimethate": {"origin": "Colistin, also called polymyxin E, was discovered in 1949 by Japanese researcher Y. Koyama and colleagues from a soil bacterium, Bacillus polymyxa var. colistinus (now Paenibacillus). Colistin itself was very irritating when injected, so drug makers created colistimethate sodium, a less toxic inactive form that the body converts to colistin. It was marketed in the US as Coly-Mycin M. Like polymyxin B, it fell out of favor in the 1970s due to kidney and nerve toxicity. In the 2000s it returned as a last-line drug for multidrug-resistant gram-negative infections. In 2015 researchers in China reported mcr-1, a plasmid gene that spreads colistin resistance between bacteria, linked to heavy colistin use in farm animals.", "timeline": [["1949", "Koyama and colleagues in Japan discover colistin."], ["1970s", "Use declines because of kidney and nerve toxicity."], ["2000s", "Colistin returns as a last-line drug against resistant gram-negatives."], ["2015", "Researchers in China report the transferable colistin resistance gene mcr-1."]], "cell": "Colistimethate is an inactive prodrug. In solution and in the body, it is slowly hydrolyzed to colistin, the active form. Colistin is a cationic cyclic lipopeptide. Its positive charges bind the negatively charged phosphate groups on lipid A of lipopolysaccharide in the gram-negative outer membrane, displacing the calcium and magnesium that stabilize it. The fatty acid tail inserts into the membrane, which becomes leaky; colistin then disrupts the inner membrane, cell contents escape and the bacterium dies. Killing is concentration dependent. It also binds endotoxin. Resistance happens when bacteria modify lipid A with positive groups, including through chromosomal changes or the plasmid gene mcr-1.", "body": [["Bacteria", "Kills Pseudomonas, Acinetobacter, Klebsiella, Enterobacter and E. coli; Proteus, Serratia, Providencia, Burkholderia and gram-positives are naturally resistant."], ["Urinary tract", "Because the prodrug is cleared by the kidneys and converts to colistin in urine, it reaches higher urine levels than polymyxin B."], ["Kidneys", "Colistin is reabsorbed by tubule cells and damages them, causing rising creatinine and low urine output."], ["Nerves", "Tingling, numbness, dizziness and confusion reflect effects on nerve membranes."], ["Neuromuscular junction", "It can block nerve-to-muscle signaling, leading to weakness, apnea and respiratory arrest."]], "journey": "Colistimethate is given IV or IM, and it is also inhaled in some settings. In the blood it is partly cleared by the kidneys as prodrug and partly converts to colistin, which takes time to reach steady levels, so loading doses are used. Colistimethate has a short half-life of about two hours. Colistin itself is cleared mostly by nonrenal routes because the kidney tubules reabsorb it, and its half-life is longer, often 9 to 14 hours in critical illness. Doses are confusing because products are labeled in colistin base activity or international units, which is a known source of errors.", "sidefx": "Nephrotoxicity is the main limitation: colistin builds up in proximal tubule cells and damages their membranes and mitochondria. Neurotoxicity, such as tingling and dizziness, reflects similar membrane effects on nerves. Neuromuscular blockade can cause apnea, especially with anesthetics or other neuromuscular blockers. Premixed solutions left standing can convert to more colistin and become more toxic. There is no specific antidote; kidney function and breathing are watched closely.", "fun": "The mcr-1 gene was discovered in bacteria from pigs in China, a reminder that antibiotic use in agriculture can affect human medicine.", "src": ["Coly-Mycin M Parenteral (colistimethate) FDA label", "Liu YY, et al. Lancet Infect Dis, 2016", "International consensus guidelines for polymyxin use, 2019"]}, "penicillin-g-procaine": {"origin": "After Alexander Fleming's 1928 discovery of penicillin and Florey and Chain's purification around 1940, doctors quickly found that penicillin G left the body within hours, so it had to be injected many times a day. Researchers looked for ways to make a depot in the muscle. Procaine, the local anesthetic synthesized by Alfred Einhorn in 1905, formed a poorly soluble salt with penicillin G. Procaine penicillin, introduced in the late 1940s, released penicillin over about a day and numbed the injection site too. It was widely used for syphilis, gonorrhea and pneumonia. It is also combined with benzathine penicillin in Bicillin C-R, a separate product.", "timeline": [["1905", "Alfred Einhorn synthesizes procaine."], ["1928", "Alexander Fleming observes penicillin."], ["1940s", "Procaine penicillin G is introduced as a repository IM penicillin."]], "cell": "Penicillin G procaine is a salt of penicillin G and procaine that dissolves slowly from muscle, releasing penicillin G over about 24 hours. Penicillin G binds penicillin-binding proteins, the transpeptidases that cross-link peptidoglycan, because its beta-lactam ring resembles the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of the wall building block. The enzyme is acylated and stops working, the wall weakens, autolysins break it further and the bacterium lyses. Killing is time dependent, so steady moderate levels work well. Beta-lactamases destroy penicillin G. The procaine part blocks sodium channels in local nerves, reducing injection pain, and is hydrolyzed by plasma pseudocholinesterase to PABA.", "body": [["Bacteria", "Kills streptococci, pneumococci, Treponema pallidum and other penicillin-susceptible organisms with moderate levels lasting about a day."], ["Injection site", "Procaine numbs the muscle, but the thick suspension still causes some pain and swelling."], ["Brain", "Procaine reaching the blood can cause a frightening reaction with anxiety, hallucinations, a sense of doom and sometimes seizures (Hoigne syndrome)."], ["Whole body", "In syphilis, dying spirochetes cause fever, chills and aches within hours (Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction)."], ["Kidneys and immune system", "Rarely it causes interstitial nephritis or serum sickness, which are immune reactions."]], "journey": "Penicillin G procaine is given only by deep IM injection. The depot releases penicillin G slowly, with peak levels in about one to four hours and useful levels for about 24 hours. Once absorbed, penicillin G has a half-life of about 30 minutes and is cleared mostly unchanged by kidney tubular secretion, which probenecid can slow. Procaine is hydrolyzed by plasma pseudocholinesterase to PABA and diethylaminoethanol. It must never be given IV.", "sidefx": "Allergy, including anaphylaxis, is an immune reaction to penicillin breakdown products and is treated with epinephrine. The procaine reaction (Hoigne syndrome) happens when some suspension enters a vessel and procaine reaches the brain, causing sudden fear, hallucinations and sometimes seizures; it is distinct from allergy and usually passes. People allergic to procaine or with pseudocholinesterase deficiency are at higher risk. The Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction is caused by cytokines released as spirochetes die. Accidental IV injection can cause cardiorespiratory arrest.", "fun": "Before benzathine penicillin, procaine penicillin was a workhorse of mass syphilis treatment campaigns in the late 1940s and 1950s.", "src": ["Penicillin G Procaine Injectable Suspension FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "quinupristin-dalfopristin": {"origin": "Streptogramins come from Streptomyces bacteria. Pristinamycin, an oral streptogramin from Streptomyces pristinaespiralis, was used in France for decades, and virginiamycin was used in animal feed. These natural products were mixtures of two parts, A and B, that worked much better together. Chemists at Rhone-Poulenc Rorer made water-soluble derivatives, quinupristin (from pristinamycin IA, a B component) and dalfopristin (from pristinamycin IIA, an A component), so the pair could be given IV. The FDA approved the combination as Synercid in 1999, at a time of rising vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus faecium, for which it was one of the very few options.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Pristinamycin from Streptomyces pristinaespiralis is used in France."], ["1999", "FDA approves Synercid (quinupristin-dalfopristin)."]], "cell": "Both drugs bind the 50S ribosomal subunit at neighboring sites. Dalfopristin, the streptogramin A part, binds the peptidyl transferase center of 23S rRNA and blocks early chain elongation. Its binding changes the ribosome's shape so quinupristin, the streptogramin B part, binds about a hundred times more tightly nearby in the exit tunnel where new protein emerges. Quinupristin blocks later elongation and causes release of incomplete peptides. Each alone is bacteriostatic, but together they are synergistic and bactericidal against most staphylococci and streptococci. Against E. faecium they are bacteriostatic. E. faecalis is naturally resistant because of an efflux pump (Lsa). Resistance can also come from erm methylation, which blocks the B part.", "body": [["Bacteria", "Treats vancomycin-resistant E. faecium and some staphylococcal and streptococcal skin infections; it does not cover E. faecalis."], ["Veins", "The infusion strongly irritates veins, causing pain, inflammation and thrombophlebitis, so a central line is often used."], ["Joints and muscles", "Arthralgia and myalgia, sometimes severe, are common, especially with longer courses."], ["Liver", "Bilirubin and liver enzymes can rise, partly because the drugs compete with bilirubin for excretion."], ["Drug interactions", "It inhibits CYP3A4, raising levels of drugs such as cyclosporine, midazolam and some calcium channel blockers."]], "journey": "Synercid is given by IV infusion, mixed only in dextrose, because saline makes it incompatible. Both parts are converted by nonenzymatic reactions to active metabolites: quinupristin conjugates with glutathione and cysteine, and dalfopristin is hydrolyzed to pristinamycin IIA. The parent drugs have half-lives under an hour, but a post-antibiotic effect extends their action. Most of the drug and metabolites leave in bile and feces, with a smaller part in urine.", "sidefx": "Infusion site pain and thrombophlebitis are the most common problems and come from direct irritation of the vein wall; flushing the line with dextrose rather than saline helps. Arthralgia and myalgia are distinctive, with an uncertain mechanism; they can be severe enough to stop therapy. Hyperbilirubinemia reflects liver handling and competition for excretion. CYP3A4 inhibition explains drug interactions. C. difficile results from loss of normal gut flora. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The name Synercid reflects synergy: neither part alone is very strong, but together they kill bacteria.", "src": ["Synercid (quinupristin and dalfopristin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "pentamidine": {"origin": "Pentamidine was born from a search for diabetes drugs. In the 1930s researchers noticed that the guanidine compound synthalin lowered blood sugar and also killed trypanosomes, which need a lot of glucose. In the late 1930s British researchers, including chemists at the drug company May and Baker, made a series of aromatic diamidines, and pentamidine stood out against African sleeping sickness. It was used in Africa from the 1940s. It later proved effective against Pneumocystis pneumonia. In 1981, unusual requests to the CDC for pentamidine, then available only from the CDC, helped reveal the start of the AIDS epidemic. An inhaled form, NebuPent, was approved in 1989 to prevent Pneumocystis pneumonia.", "timeline": [["1930s", "British researchers, including May and Baker chemists, develop aromatic diamidines such as pentamidine."], ["1940s", "Pentamidine is used against African sleeping sickness."], ["1981", "Requests for pentamidine at the CDC help reveal the emerging AIDS epidemic."], ["1989", "Inhaled pentamidine (NebuPent) is approved to prevent Pneumocystis pneumonia."]], "cell": "Pentamidine is an aromatic diamidine with two positively charged ends. Its exact mechanism is not fully known, and it likely acts in several ways. It binds AT-rich regions of the DNA minor groove, especially the kinetoplast DNA in the mitochondria of trypanosomes and Leishmania. It also inhibits topoisomerases and interferes with DNA, RNA, phospholipid and protein synthesis and with mitochondrial function. Susceptible organisms concentrate it through specific transporters, which helps explain selective killing. In people, pentamidine is toxic to pancreatic beta cells: it first causes them to release insulin, which drops blood sugar, and then can destroy them, leading to high blood sugar or diabetes.", "body": [["Lungs", "Kills Pneumocystis jirovecii, an alternative treatment for Pneumocystis pneumonia when trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole cannot be used."], ["Pancreas", "Beta cell injury causes early hypoglycemia and later hyperglycemia; pancreatitis can also occur."], ["Heart and blood pressure", "Fast IV infusion can drop blood pressure, and QT prolongation can cause torsades de pointes."], ["Kidneys", "Nephrotoxicity raises creatinine and can raise potassium and lower calcium and magnesium."], ["Bone marrow", "White cell and platelet counts can fall."], ["Injection site", "IM injection often causes sterile abscesses and pain."]], "journey": "For treatment pentamidine is given by slow IV infusion or, less often, IM; it is not absorbed by mouth. The inhaled form is used for prevention and reaches the lungs with little entering the blood. After IV dosing it binds tissues heavily and builds up in the liver, kidneys, spleen and lungs. Little is metabolized. The initial half-life is about six hours, but it is released from tissues slowly over weeks. A small part leaves in urine unchanged.", "sidefx": "Hypoglycemia, which can be sudden and severe, comes from injured beta cells dumping insulin; later beta cell destruction causes hyperglycemia, so blood glucose is checked often. Hypotension during fast infusion reflects vasodilation, so it is given slowly with the patient lying down. QT prolongation and torsades come from blocking cardiac potassium currents. Kidney damage, pancreatitis, low blood counts and electrolyte changes are direct toxic effects. Hypoglycemia is treated with dextrose. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "In 1981 a CDC drug technician, Sandra Ford, noticed a cluster of unusual pentamidine requests for young men with Pneumocystis pneumonia, one of the first clues to AIDS.", "src": ["Pentam 300 (pentamidine isethionate) FDA label", "CDC MMWR, June 5, 1981", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "telavancin": {"origin": "Telavancin is a semisynthetic cousin of vancomycin. Vancomycin itself came from a soil microbe, now called Amycolatopsis orientalis, found in a soil sample from Borneo and developed by Eli Lilly in the 1950s. As MRSA and vancomycin-resistant strains spread, chemists looked for ways to make the glycopeptide scaffold hit harder. Scientists at Theravance in South San Francisco added a fat-loving (lipophilic) side chain and a polar phosphonate group to the vancomycin core. The fatty tail let the molecule anchor in bacterial membranes, giving it a second way to kill. The FDA approved telavancin as Vibativ in 2009 for complicated skin infections and in 2013 for hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia caused by Staphylococcus aureus.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Vancomycin is isolated from a soil actinomycete and developed by Eli Lilly."], ["2000s", "Theravance designs telavancin by adding a lipophilic side chain to the vancomycin core."], ["2009", "FDA approves telavancin (Vibativ) for complicated skin and skin structure infections."], ["2013", "Approval expands to hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated bacterial pneumonia."]], "cell": "Telavancin is a lipoglycopeptide with two actions. First, like vancomycin, its glycopeptide core forms hydrogen bonds with the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of lipid II, the building block that carries new peptidoglycan to the cell wall. Capping that end blocks transglycosylation (linking sugars into chains) and transpeptidation (cross-linking the chains), so the wall cannot be built. Second, its lipophilic decylaminoethyl tail inserts into the bacterial cell membrane near lipid II. This depolarizes the membrane and makes it leaky, so ions escape and the cell loses its energy gradient. Together these effects give rapid, concentration-dependent killing of gram-positive bacteria, including MRSA. Gram-negative bacteria are not affected because their outer membrane keeps the large molecule out.", "body": [["Skin and soft tissue", "Kills Staphylococcus aureus, including MRSA, and streptococci in deep skin infections, abscesses and wound infections."], ["Lungs", "Treats hospital and ventilator pneumonia caused by susceptible Staphylococcus aureus, used when other options are not suitable."], ["Kidneys", "Most of the drug leaves through the kidneys, and it can injure them. Kidney injury and worse outcomes were seen in patients who already had moderate or severe kidney impairment."], ["Heart", "It can lengthen the QTc interval on the ECG, which matters when combined with other QT-prolonging drugs."], ["Taste and gut", "A metallic or soapy taste, nausea and vomiting are common and reflect drug effects on taste and the gut."], ["Lab tests", "It binds the phospholipid reagents used in some clotting tests, falsely prolonging PT/INR and aPTT without truly thinning the blood."]], "journey": "Telavancin is given only by IV infusion because it is not absorbed from the gut. It stays mostly in the bloodstream and tissues, tightly bound to albumin. It is not significantly broken down by liver CYP enzymes. The kidneys clear most of it unchanged in the urine, with a half-life of about eight hours in normal kidney function, which supports once-daily dosing. Reduced kidney function raises levels and lengthens its stay.", "sidefx": "Kidney injury likely comes from drug concentrating in kidney tubules, the same organ that clears it, so pre-existing kidney disease raises risk. Taste disturbance and nausea are common. QTc prolongation reflects an effect on cardiac repolarization. Rapid infusion can release histamine and cause flushing and itching (an infusion reaction similar to vancomycin's), which is why it is infused slowly. Animal studies showed fetal harm, so the label carries a warning about pregnancy. Foamy urine has been reported. Like other antibiotics, it can allow C. difficile overgrowth. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Telavancin can make clotting tests look abnormal for hours after a dose. The label notes that blood for these tests is drawn just before the next dose, when drug levels are lowest.", "src": ["Vibativ (telavancin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "landiolol": {"origin": "Landiolol was designed in Japan by Ono Pharmaceutical as an ultra-short-acting beta blocker, building on the idea behind esmolol: put an ester bond in the molecule so blood enzymes break it down within minutes. The goal was a beta blocker that could be turned on and off quickly in the ICU and operating room, and that was even more selective for heart beta1 receptors than esmolol so it would lower blood pressure less. It was approved in Japan in the early 2000s and was later used in Europe. The FDA approved it as Rapiblyk in 2024 for short-term control of a fast ventricular rate in supraventricular tachycardia, including atrial fibrillation and flutter.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Esmolol establishes the concept of an esterase-cleared, ultra-short-acting beta blocker."], ["2000s", "Landiolol is approved in Japan for perioperative tachyarrhythmias."], ["2024", "FDA approves landiolol (Rapiblyk) for rate control in supraventricular tachycardia."]], "cell": "Landiolol competitively blocks beta1-adrenergic receptors, which are Gs protein-coupled receptors found mainly on heart cells. Normally norepinephrine binding activates Gs, which turns on adenylyl cyclase, raises cAMP and activates protein kinase A. In the SA node this speeds phase 4 depolarization through funny (If) channels and L-type calcium channels; in the AV node it speeds conduction. By blocking the receptor, landiolol lowers cAMP, slowing the SA node's firing rate and lengthening AV nodal conduction and refractoriness. That slows the ventricular rate in atrial fibrillation or flutter. Its beta1 selectivity is very high, roughly 255 to 1 over beta2, so it has less effect on bronchial and vascular beta2 receptors. It has no intrinsic sympathomimetic activity.", "body": [["Heart rate", "Slows the SA node and the AV node within minutes, lowering a fast heart rate in atrial fibrillation, flutter and other supraventricular tachycardias."], ["Heart muscle", "Reduces contractility somewhat, but less than many beta blockers, which helps in patients with borderline heart function."], ["Blood pressure", "Lower heart rate and cardiac output can drop blood pressure, though its high beta1 selectivity limits this."], ["Lungs", "High selectivity means less beta2 blockade in the airways, but bronchospasm remains possible in reactive airway disease."], ["Whole body", "Because blood esterases break it down so fast, its effects wear off within minutes after the infusion stops."]], "journey": "Landiolol is given only as a continuous IV infusion. It acts within minutes and reaches a steady effect within about 10 to 15 minutes of a rate change. Esterases in the blood and liver (pseudocholinesterase and carboxylesterases) cut its ester bond, producing inactive metabolites. Its half-life is only about 3 to 4 minutes, so the effect fades within minutes after stopping. The inactive metabolites leave through the kidneys. Because it does not rely on the liver or kidneys for its own clearance, its short action is fairly predictable.", "sidefx": "Side effects come straight from beta1 blockade. Slowing the SA and AV nodes too much causes bradycardia or heart block. Lower cardiac output causes hypotension and can worsen heart failure. Some residual beta2 blockade can trigger bronchospasm. In overdose, severe bradycardia, cardiogenic shock and asystole can occur. The main reversal is stopping the infusion, since the drug clears in minutes. For severe beta blocker toxicity, references describe glucagon, which raises cAMP through its own receptor, plus atropine, vasopressors and pacing.", "fun": "Landiolol and esmolol both carry a built-in weak ester link that blood enzymes cut, so the drug switches off almost as fast as it is turned on. Landiolol's half-life of a few minutes is even shorter than esmolol's.", "src": ["Rapiblyk (landiolol) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "bupivacaine-meloxicam": {"origin": "This product joins two older drugs in a new delivery system. Bupivacaine was synthesized in Sweden in 1957 by Bo af Ekenstam and became one of the most widely used long-acting local anesthetics. Meloxicam is an oxicam NSAID developed by Boehringer Ingelheim. Heron Therapeutics combined them in a thick, bioerodible polymer that is dripped into the open surgical wound and slowly releases both drugs for about three days. Heron's reasoning was that inflamed, acidic wound tissue makes local anesthetics work poorly, and the NSAID helps by calming that inflammation. The FDA approved it as Zynrelef in 2021 for postsurgical pain after certain operations, with the goal of reducing opioid use.", "timeline": [["1957", "Bo af Ekenstam synthesizes bupivacaine in Sweden."], ["1990s", "Meloxicam, an oxicam NSAID from Boehringer Ingelheim, enters wide use."], ["2021", "FDA approves bupivacaine and meloxicam extended-release solution (Zynrelef) for postsurgical pain."]], "cell": "Bupivacaine is a lipid-soluble amide local anesthetic. The uncharged form crosses the nerve membrane, then picks up a proton inside the cell. The charged form binds a site inside the pore of voltage-gated sodium channels, mostly when the channels are open or inactivated. Blocked channels cannot carry the sodium current needed for an action potential, so pain fibers stop sending signals from the incision. Meloxicam inhibits cyclooxygenase, with a preference for COX-2, lowering prostaglandin production in the wound. Less prostaglandin means less inflammation and less local acidity. Since acidic tissue traps local anesthetic in its charged form outside the nerve, reducing inflammation may help more bupivacaine reach its target. The polymer vehicle releases both drugs slowly over about 72 hours.", "body": [["Surgical site", "The gel stays in the wound and keeps nerves near the incision numb for up to about three days while reducing local inflammation."], ["Pain nerves", "Sodium channel blockade stops small pain fibers from firing, so fewer pain signals reach the spinal cord."], ["Brain", "Too much bupivacaine in the blood can cause ringing ears, a metallic taste, confusion and seizures, which are early signs of local anesthetic systemic toxicity."], ["Heart", "High blood levels of bupivacaine can block cardiac sodium channels, causing slow rhythms, arrhythmias and cardiac arrest."], ["Kidneys", "Meloxicam can lower kidney blood flow when the body depends on prostaglandins, such as with dehydration."], ["Stomach and vessels", "Like other NSAIDs, meloxicam carries boxed warnings for GI bleeding and for heart attack and stroke."]], "journey": "The product is applied once into the wound at the end of surgery, not injected with a needle and not given IV. The polymer slowly erodes and releases both drugs into nearby tissue, and some is absorbed into the blood. Bupivacaine levels peak within the first day, while meloxicam peaks later. Both are highly protein bound. The liver breaks down bupivacaine mainly by CYP3A4 and meloxicam mainly by CYP2C9, and their metabolites leave in urine and, for meloxicam, also feces. Slow release makes their apparent half-lives long.", "sidefx": "If enough bupivacaine reaches the blood, it blocks sodium channels in the brain and heart, causing local anesthetic systemic toxicity: seizures, arrhythmias and cardiac arrest. Giving other local anesthetics in the following days adds to that risk. The rescue for this toxicity is lipid emulsion therapy plus airway and seizure care. Local anesthetics can also cause methemoglobinemia, which is reversed with methylene blue. Meloxicam blocks protective prostaglandins in the kidney and stomach and tilts vessels toward clotting, explaining kidney injury, GI bleeding and cardiovascular warnings. Constipation, nausea and vomiting are common after surgery.", "fun": "The applicator is designed to drip the drug onto tissues inside the incision, so the product reaches the wound without a single needle stick.", "src": ["Zynrelef (bupivacaine and meloxicam) FDA label", "Miller's Anesthesia", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "agalsidase-beta": {"origin": "Fabry disease was described in 1898 by two dermatologists working separately, Johannes Fabry in Germany and William Anderson in England, who both noticed the dark red skin spots (angiokeratomas) it causes. In 1967 Roscoe Brady and colleagues at the US National Institutes of Health showed the cause: missing activity of the lysosomal enzyme alpha-galactosidase A, which lets a fatty substance called globotriaosylceramide (Gb3) build up. Genzyme made the human enzyme in Chinese hamster ovary cells using recombinant DNA. In 2003 the FDA approved agalsidase beta as Fabrazyme, the first enzyme replacement therapy for Fabry disease in the US.", "timeline": [["1898", "Johannes Fabry and William Anderson separately describe the disease."], ["1967", "Roscoe Brady's NIH group links Fabry disease to alpha-galactosidase A deficiency."], ["2003", "FDA approves agalsidase beta (Fabrazyme)."]], "cell": "Fabry disease comes from variants in the X-linked GLA gene that reduce alpha-galactosidase A activity. Without the enzyme, Gb3 (GL-3) and related lipids pile up in lysosomes, especially in blood vessel endothelium, kidney podocytes, heart muscle and small nerves. Agalsidase beta is recombinant human alpha-galactosidase A carrying mannose-6-phosphate tags on its sugar chains. Cells take it up through mannose-6-phosphate receptors on their surface, and the receptor carries it in endosomes to the lysosome. There, in the acidic environment, the enzyme cuts the terminal alpha-galactose off Gb3, letting the lipid be broken down further. Over time this clears GL-3 deposits from capillary endothelium in the kidney, heart and skin.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Clearing Gb3 from vessel lining cells helps protect small vessels in many organs, which is the root of much of the damage in Fabry disease."], ["Kidneys", "Gb3 builds up in podocytes and tubules, leading to protein in the urine and kidney failure; therapy reduces these deposits."], ["Heart", "Lipid storage in heart muscle causes thickening and arrhythmias. Infusion reactions can stress the heart in advanced cardiomyopathy."], ["Nerves", "Small nerve fiber damage causes burning pain in hands and feet (acroparesthesia); reducing storage may lessen this over time."], ["Skin", "Treatment clears Gb3 from skin capillaries, which was one of the measures used for its approval."], ["Immune system", "Many patients form IgG antibodies against the foreign-looking protein, and some form IgE, which can cause allergic reactions."]], "journey": "Agalsidase beta is a large protein, so it is given by IV infusion. It leaves the blood quickly, with a plasma half-life of roughly 45 to 100 minutes, because cells pull it in through mannose-6-phosphate receptors. Inside lysosomes it keeps working long after it is gone from the blood, so infusions every two weeks are enough. It is eventually broken down like any other protein into amino acids. It is not cleared by the liver CYP system or excreted intact by the kidneys.", "sidefx": "The most common problems are infusion reactions such as fever, chills, rigors, flushing and headache. These happen because the immune system sees the recombinant enzyme as foreign and makes antibodies, which can activate complement and release cytokines. True IgE-mediated anaphylaxis is possible, so infusions are given where emergency treatment is available. Slowing the infusion and premedication reduce reactions. Patients with advanced Fabry heart disease may not tolerate the fluid and blood pressure changes of an infusion reaction well. There is no antidote; reactions are managed by stopping or slowing the infusion and treating symptoms.", "fun": "Fabry disease is X-linked, but women who carry one faulty GLA copy are not simply carriers. Because of random X inactivation, many have real symptoms.", "src": ["Fabrazyme (agalsidase beta) FDA label", "Brady RO et al. N Engl J Med 1967", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "imiglucerase": {"origin": "Gaucher disease was first described in 1882 by French medical student Philippe Gaucher, who saw a patient with a huge spleen. In the 1960s Roscoe Brady at the NIH found the cause: a missing lysosomal enzyme, glucocerebrosidase. Brady's team then learned that the enzyme only reached macrophages well if its sugar chains were trimmed to expose mannose, which macrophage mannose receptors recognize. Genzyme first made the enzyme from human placentas as alglucerase (Ceredase), approved in 1991, which took thousands of placentas per patient each year. Imiglucerase (Cerezyme) is the recombinant version made in hamster ovary cells, approved in 1994. It replaced the placental product and became one of the first big successes of enzyme replacement therapy.", "timeline": [["1882", "Philippe Gaucher describes the disease in his doctoral thesis."], ["1965", "Roscoe Brady identifies glucocerebrosidase deficiency as the cause."], ["1991", "FDA approves placenta-derived alglucerase (Ceredase)."], ["1994", "FDA approves recombinant imiglucerase (Cerezyme)."]], "cell": "In Gaucher disease, variants in the GBA1 gene reduce glucocerebrosidase (acid beta-glucosidase). Glucocerebroside (glucosylceramide), released when old blood cells are broken down, builds up inside macrophage lysosomes. These swollen cells, called Gaucher cells, crowd the spleen, liver and bone marrow. Imiglucerase is recombinant glucocerebrosidase whose sugar chains are trimmed down to expose mannose. Mannose receptors on macrophages bind it and carry it into endosomes and then lysosomes. There it splits glucocerebroside into glucose and ceramide, emptying the storage. It differs from the natural enzyme by one amino acid (arginine to histidine at position 495). It does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so it does not treat the brain disease of type 2 Gaucher disease.", "body": [["Spleen", "Gaucher cells enlarge the spleen greatly. Clearing stored lipid shrinks it over months."], ["Liver", "The liver also shrinks as macrophage storage clears."], ["Blood", "An enlarged spleen traps and destroys blood cells, and marrow gets crowded. Therapy raises hemoglobin and platelet counts."], ["Bones", "Gaucher cells in marrow cause bone pain, bone crises and fractures. Long-term therapy reduces bone crises."], ["Brain", "The enzyme does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so it does not help the neurologic disease of type 2 and only partly addresses type 3."], ["Immune system", "About 15% of patients form IgG antibodies, and some have allergic-type infusion reactions."]], "journey": "Imiglucerase is given by IV infusion over one to two hours. Macrophages in the liver, spleen and bone marrow take it up so quickly that its plasma half-life is only minutes. Once inside lysosomes it keeps working, so infusions range from three times weekly to every two weeks. It is broken down like other proteins inside cells and is not cleared through CYP enzymes or excreted intact in urine.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from the immune system reacting to a foreign protein. Headache, itching, rash, fever and discomfort at the infusion site are common. About 15% of patients form IgG antibodies, and a few develop hypersensitivity or anaphylaxis, so infusions are given with emergency care available. Patients with antibodies are watched more closely for reactions. There is no antidote; reactions are handled by slowing or stopping the infusion and treating symptoms.", "fun": "The earlier placental version, alglucerase, needed enzyme from tens of thousands of placentas each year for a single adult patient, which is why the recombinant version mattered so much.", "src": ["Cerezyme (imiglucerase) FDA label", "Barton NW et al. N Engl J Med 1991", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "asfotase-alfa": {"origin": "Hypophosphatasia was named in 1948 by Canadian pediatrician John Rathbun, who described an infant with soft, poorly mineralized bones and very low alkaline phosphatase in the blood. Later work showed the cause was variants in the ALPL gene, which codes for tissue-nonspecific alkaline phosphatase (TNSALP). Researchers at the Montreal company Enobia Pharma engineered a fusion protein that carries the enzyme to bone using a chain of ten aspartic acids, which sticks to bone mineral. Alexion acquired Enobia in 2011. Clinical trials, many led by Michael Whyte in St. Louis, showed that infants who would likely have died survived and grew. The FDA approved it as Strensiq in 2015, the first treatment for the disease.", "timeline": [["1948", "John Rathbun names and describes hypophosphatasia."], ["2000s", "Enobia Pharma develops a bone-targeted alkaline phosphatase fusion protein."], ["2012", "Trial results in infants with life-threatening disease are published in the New England Journal of Medicine."], ["2015", "FDA approves asfotase alfa (Strensiq)."]], "cell": "TNSALP sits on the surface of bone-forming cells and cleaves inorganic pyrophosphate (PPi), a natural inhibitor of crystal growth. With too little enzyme, PPi builds up and blocks hydroxyapatite (calcium phosphate) crystals from forming, so bones and teeth stay soft. TNSALP also removes phosphate from pyridoxal 5-prime-phosphate (PLP, the active form of vitamin B6) so it can cross into cells, including brain cells. Asfotase alfa is a fusion protein made of the TNSALP catalytic domain, the Fc part of human IgG1 (which lengthens its life in blood) and a deca-aspartate tail that binds bone mineral. It concentrates in bone, breaks down PPi and lets mineral deposit normally. It also lowers PLP and phosphoethanolamine, the other built-up substrates.", "body": [["Bones", "Breaking down pyrophosphate lets calcium phosphate crystals form, so the skeleton mineralizes, rickets-like changes heal and fractures become less common."], ["Lungs and chest", "In infants, a soft rib cage causes breathing failure. Better rib mineralization improves breathing and survival."], ["Growth", "Children grow better as their growth plates mineralize normally."], ["Teeth", "Early loss of baby teeth is a classic sign because tooth roots cannot anchor without normal mineral."], ["Brain", "Infants can have vitamin B6-dependent seizures because PLP cannot be dephosphorylated to enter the brain; restoring enzyme activity addresses this cause."], ["Skin and fat at injection site", "Repeated injections can cause redness, discoloration, lumps and lipodystrophy, so sites are rotated."]], "journey": "Asfotase alfa is injected under the skin several times a week. It is absorbed slowly, peaking about one to two days after a dose. The bone-targeting tail binds hydroxyapatite, so much of the enzyme collects at mineralizing bone. The Fc portion helps protect it from breakdown and gives it a half-life of about five days. Like other proteins, it is eventually broken down into amino acids rather than cleared by the liver or kidneys intact.", "sidefx": "Injection site reactions such as redness, pain, swelling and color change are very common, and fat loss or buildup (lipodystrophy) can follow repeated injections in one area. Because the drug promotes mineral deposition, calcium phosphate can form in the wrong places, such as the eyes and kidneys (ectopic calcification and nephrocalcinosis). Craniosynostosis, early fusion of skull sutures, is a known feature of the disease and is watched for during treatment. Antibodies can form against the protein, and hypersensitivity including anaphylaxis can occur. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The bone-targeting tail is ten aspartic acid residues in a row. Their negative charges grab calcium in bone mineral, a little like a magnet for the skeleton.", "src": ["Strensiq (asfotase alfa) FDA label", "Whyte MP et al. N Engl J Med 2012", "Rathbun JC. Am J Dis Child 1948"]}, "patisiran": {"origin": "Patisiran rests on RNA interference, a natural gene-silencing process. In 1998 Andrew Fire and Craig Mello showed that double-stranded RNA can switch off a matching gene in worms, work that won the 2006 Nobel Prize. Alnylam Pharmaceuticals, founded in 2002, spent years solving the hardest problem: getting fragile RNA into human cells. Their answer for patisiran was a lipid nanoparticle that coats itself with the body's apolipoprotein E and is carried into liver cells. Their target was transthyretin (TTR), a liver-made protein that misfolds into amyloid in hereditary TTR amyloidosis. In 2018 the FDA approved patisiran as Onpattro, the first siRNA medicine ever approved.", "timeline": [["1998", "Fire and Mello describe RNA interference in Caenorhabditis elegans."], ["2002", "Alnylam Pharmaceuticals is founded to develop RNAi medicines."], ["2006", "Fire and Mello receive the Nobel Prize."], ["2018", "FDA approves patisiran (Onpattro), the first siRNA drug, for hATTR polyneuropathy."]], "cell": "Transthyretin is a four-part (tetramer) protein made mainly in the liver. It carries thyroxine and retinol-binding protein in the blood. In hereditary ATTR amyloidosis, a TTR gene variant makes the tetramer unstable, so it falls apart into monomers that misfold and stack into amyloid fibrils in nerves, heart and other tissues. Patisiran is a double-stranded siRNA packed in a lipid nanoparticle. In the blood the particle picks up apolipoprotein E and enters liver cells through LDL receptors. Inside, the guide strand loads into the RNA-induced silencing complex (RISC). RISC finds TTR mRNA by base pairing in its 3-prime untranslated region and cuts it. Because it targets a region shared by normal and variant TTR, both are lowered, by about 80%. Less TTR means less raw material for amyloid.", "body": [["Peripheral nerves", "Less amyloid forms in nerves, slowing the numbness, weakness and pain of hATTR polyneuropathy, and in trials many patients improved."], ["Autonomic nerves", "Amyloid damages nerves that control blood pressure, bowels and bladder; lowering TTR may slow this damage."], ["Heart", "Amyloid in heart muscle stiffens it; studies showed effects on heart function and markers, though its US label is for polyneuropathy."], ["Liver", "The liver is the site of action, where it silences TTR production in hepatocytes."], ["Eyes and vitamin A", "TTR helps carry vitamin A in the blood, so serum vitamin A falls; supplementation is used to avoid night blindness."], ["Immune system", "The lipid particle can trigger infusion reactions, so premedication is given beforehand."]], "journey": "Patisiran is given by IV infusion every three weeks after premedication with a steroid, acetaminophen and antihistamines. The lipid nanoparticle travels in the blood, picks up apolipoprotein E and is taken into liver cells. The siRNA itself has a half-life of about three days in plasma, but its silencing effect in the liver lasts much longer, with maximal TTR lowering about 10 to 14 days after a dose. Nucleases break down the RNA, and esterases clear the lipid parts. Less than 1% leaves unchanged in urine.", "sidefx": "Infusion reactions such as flushing, back pain, nausea, shortness of breath and headache happen because the lipid nanoparticles activate parts of the innate immune system. Premedication with a steroid, antihistamines and acetaminophen reduces this. Lowering TTR also lowers its partner, retinol-binding protein, so blood vitamin A falls and eye symptoms such as night blindness are possible without supplements. Upper respiratory infections were more common in trials. There is no antidote; infusion reactions are managed by slowing or stopping the infusion.", "fun": "The drug's name is a hint at how it works: patisiran ends in -siran, the stem used for small interfering RNA drugs.", "src": ["Onpattro (patisiran) FDA label", "Fire A, Mello CC et al. Nature 1998", "Adams D et al. N Engl J Med 2018 (APOLLO)"]}, "vutrisiran": {"origin": "Vutrisiran is Alnylam's second-generation TTR silencer. After patisiran showed that RNA interference could treat hereditary transthyretin amyloidosis, Alnylam worked to drop the lipid particle and the IV infusion. Their solution was GalNAc conjugation: attaching three N-acetylgalactosamine sugars that bind a receptor found almost only on liver cells. Vutrisiran also uses an enhanced chemical stabilization pattern that protects it from breakdown, so it can be given as a small injection under the skin every three months. The FDA approved it as Amvuttra in 2022 for hATTR polyneuropathy, and in 2025 for ATTR cardiomyopathy, both wild-type and hereditary, based on the HELIOS-B trial.", "timeline": [["2018", "Patisiran becomes the first approved siRNA drug for hATTR."], ["2022", "FDA approves vutrisiran (Amvuttra) for hATTR polyneuropathy."], ["2024", "HELIOS-B trial reports fewer deaths and cardiovascular events in ATTR cardiomyopathy."], ["2025", "FDA expands approval to ATTR cardiomyopathy."]], "cell": "Vutrisiran is a chemically modified double-stranded siRNA attached to a triantennary GalNAc ligand. After injection, GalNAc binds the asialoglycoprotein receptor (ASGPR) on hepatocytes, and the drug is pulled into the cell by endocytosis. A small fraction escapes the endosome and the guide strand loads into RISC. RISC uses base pairing to find TTR mRNA and cleaves it, so ribosomes cannot make TTR protein. The target sequence is shared by normal and variant TTR, so both drop, by more than 80%. With less TTR circulating, fewer unstable tetramers break into monomers that misfold into amyloid fibrils in nerves and heart.", "body": [["Peripheral nerves", "Lower TTR slows amyloid buildup in nerves, slowing and in some patients improving the neuropathy of hATTR."], ["Heart", "In ATTR cardiomyopathy it reduced cardiovascular death and heart failure events in HELIOS-B, reflecting less amyloid in heart muscle over time."], ["Liver", "The liver is where it works, silencing TTR production in hepatocytes taken up through ASGPR."], ["Eyes and vitamin A", "Lower TTR means lower retinol-binding protein and vitamin A in blood, so vitamin A supplementation is recommended to protect night vision."], ["Joints and limbs", "Joint pain and pain in the arms or legs were reported more often in trials."]], "journey": "Vutrisiran is injected under the skin by a health care professional every three months. GalNAc targeting sends it rapidly to the liver, so it leaves the blood within hours (plasma half-life about five hours). Inside liver cells it is very stable and keeps RISC loaded for months, which is why maximum TTR lowering takes about three to four months. Nucleases slowly break it into shorter pieces. About a fifth of a dose leaves unchanged in urine. It is not a CYP substrate.", "sidefx": "Vutrisiran is generally well tolerated. Its main expected effect is low vitamin A, because TTR carries retinol-binding protein; without supplements, night blindness and other eye symptoms can occur. Injection site reactions are usually mild. Joint pain, breathlessness and limb pain were reported in trials. Because it uses GalNAc delivery rather than lipid nanoparticles, it does not need premedication and causes few infusion-type reactions. There is no antidote, and its effect wears off slowly over months.", "fun": "A single injection silences TTR for about three months, so the drug can keep working in the liver long after it has disappeared from the blood.", "src": ["Amvuttra (vutrisiran) FDA label", "Fontana M et al. N Engl J Med 2025 (HELIOS-B)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "tafamidis": {"origin": "Tafamidis came from basic chemistry research by Jeffery Kelly at the Scripps Research Institute. Kelly's lab showed that transthyretin amyloid forms when the four-part TTR protein first falls apart, and that this breakup is the slow, rate-limiting step. They also noticed that some people carry a protective TTR variant that holds the tetramer together. That suggested a drug that binds the tetramer and stabilizes it could prevent disease. Kelly's team designed small molecules that fit into TTR's empty thyroxine-binding pockets. The biotech FoldRx developed tafamidis, and Pfizer acquired FoldRx in 2010. Europe approved it in 2011 for polyneuropathy. In 2019 the FDA approved Vyndaqel and Vyndamax for ATTR cardiomyopathy after the ATTR-ACT trial showed fewer deaths and hospital stays.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Jeffery Kelly's lab shows TTR tetramer dissociation is the rate-limiting step in amyloid formation."], ["2000s", "Kelly's group designs small-molecule kinetic stabilizers, leading to tafamidis."], ["2011", "Tafamidis is approved in the European Union for hATTR polyneuropathy."], ["2018", "The ATTR-ACT trial reports lower mortality and cardiovascular hospitalizations."], ["2019", "FDA approves tafamidis (Vyndaqel, Vyndamax) for ATTR cardiomyopathy."]], "cell": "Transthyretin circulates as a tetramer of four identical subunits. It has two binding channels for thyroxine at the dimer-dimer interface, but in the blood these sites are almost always empty because other proteins carry most thyroxine. For TTR to form amyloid, the tetramer must first dissociate into monomers, which then partly unfold and assemble into fibrils. Tafamidis binds selectively into the thyroxine-binding sites and bridges the two dimers. This raises the energy barrier for dissociation, a process called kinetic stabilization. With the tetramer held together, fewer monomers are available to misfold. It works on both wild-type and many variant forms of TTR. It does not lower TTR production, so serum TTR levels stay the same or rise.", "body": [["Heart", "Less amyloid builds up in heart muscle over time, which slows stiffening and loss of function. In ATTR-ACT, the benefit on death and hospitalizations appeared after about 18 months."], ["Peripheral nerves", "In other countries tafamidis is used to slow polyneuropathy from hereditary TTR amyloidosis."], ["Thyroid hormone", "Although it occupies thyroxine-binding sites on TTR, it does not cause clinical thyroid problems because other proteins carry most thyroxine."], ["Gut and urinary tract", "Diarrhea, upper abdominal pain and urinary tract infections were reported in trials, but overall the drug is well tolerated."], ["Pregnancy", "Animal studies raised concern for fetal harm, so the label advises against pregnancy during treatment."]], "journey": "Tafamidis is taken by mouth once daily and is well absorbed. Vyndamax (tafamidis free acid) peaks about four hours after a dose. In the blood it binds almost entirely to proteins, mostly to transthyretin itself, its own target. It has a long half-life of about two days. The liver conjugates it with glucuronic acid, and it leaves in feces and urine, mostly as the glucuronide. Vyndaqel and Vyndamax are different salt forms and are not interchangeable capsule for capsule.", "sidefx": "Tafamidis has few side effects because it simply sits in a protein pocket and stabilizes it, without blocking an enzyme or receptor. Diarrhea, urinary tract infection and upper abdominal pain were more common in trials. Animal studies showed embryo-fetal harm, so pregnancy is avoided during treatment. It does not reverse amyloid already deposited, so disease progression can continue. There is no antidote, and none is generally needed.", "fun": "The idea for tafamidis came partly from nature: a TTR variant called T119M, found in some Portuguese families, protects carriers from amyloid disease by making the tetramer extra stable.", "src": ["Vyndaqel and Vyndamax (tafamidis) FDA label", "Maurer MS et al. N Engl J Med 2018 (ATTR-ACT)", "Bulawa CE et al. PNAS 2012"]}, "nitisinone": {"origin": "Nitisinone began as herbicide research. Scientists at a British agrochemical company (later part of Zeneca) studied leptospermone, a natural chemical from the bottlebrush plant Callistemon that kept weeds from growing near it. Their synthetic triketone, nitisinone (NTBC), blocked the plant enzyme 4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase (HPPD), which plants need to make pigments. When toxicology tests in rats raised blood tyrosine and caused eye lesions, researchers realized it blocked the same enzyme in animals. Swedish physician Sven Lindstedt and colleagues in Gothenburg saw that blocking HPPD could stop the toxic buildup in hereditary tyrosinemia type 1 and treated the first children in 1991, reporting results in The Lancet in 1992. It turned a disease that often killed children through liver failure into a manageable condition. The FDA approved it as Orfadin in 2002.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Agrochemical scientists study leptospermone, a natural herbicide from Callistemon plants."], ["1980s", "Synthetic triketone nitisinone is found to inhibit HPPD."], ["1992", "Lindstedt and colleagues report treating tyrosinemia type 1 with nitisinone in The Lancet."], ["2002", "FDA approves nitisinone (Orfadin)."]], "cell": "Tyrosine is broken down in the liver in a series of steps. The second step is 4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase (HPPD), which turns 4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate into homogentisate. The final step is fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase (FAH). In tyrosinemia type 1, FAH is missing, so maleylacetoacetate and fumarylacetoacetate build up and are converted to succinylacetone. These compounds damage DNA and proteins in liver cells and kidney tubules, cause liver failure and liver cancer, and block heme synthesis, which causes porphyria-like nerve crises. Nitisinone is a reversible competitive inhibitor of HPPD. Blocking this upstream step prevents formation of the toxic downstream products. The trade-off is that tyrosine itself rises, so a diet low in tyrosine and phenylalanine is required.", "body": [["Liver", "Stopping toxic metabolite buildup prevents acute liver failure, reverses liver damage and greatly lowers the risk of liver cancer, especially when started early."], ["Kidneys", "Protecting tubule cells reverses Fanconi-type losses of phosphate, glucose and amino acids, helping rickets heal."], ["Nerves", "Succinylacetone blocks an enzyme in heme synthesis; lowering it prevents painful porphyria-like neurologic crises."], ["Eyes", "Because tyrosine rises, tyrosine crystals can form in the cornea, causing irritation, light sensitivity and corneal clouding."], ["Skin", "High tyrosine can cause painful thickened plaques on palms and soles."], ["Blood", "Low platelets and low white cells can occur and are monitored."]], "journey": "Nitisinone is taken by mouth and is well absorbed, peaking about three hours after a dose. It is highly protein bound and has a long half-life of about two days in healthy volunteers. It is largely stable in the body, with only minor CYP3A4 metabolism. It is eliminated in urine and feces, though data are limited. Succinylacetone in the urine falls within days, while liver recovery takes weeks.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from the predictable rise in tyrosine caused by blocking HPPD. Tyrosine is poorly soluble, so when levels get high it can crystallize in the cornea and cause conjunctivitis, keratitis, photophobia and corneal opacities, and it can cause skin plaques on palms and soles. A strict low-tyrosine, low-phenylalanine diet keeps levels down. Thrombocytopenia and leukopenia can occur. If therapy lapses, toxic metabolites return and liver failure or cancer risk rises again. There is no antidote; diet control is the main way to manage tyrosine.", "fun": "The same chemistry is still used on farms: mesotrione, a close relative of nitisinone, is a common corn herbicide that blocks HPPD in weeds.", "src": ["Orfadin (nitisinone) FDA label", "Lindstedt S et al. Lancet 1992", "Lock EA et al. J Inherit Metab Dis 1998"]}, "sapropterin": {"origin": "Phenylketonuria was discovered in 1934 by Norwegian physician Asbjorn Folling, who found phenylpyruvic acid in the urine of children with intellectual disability. Later research showed that the enzyme phenylalanine hydroxylase (PAH) needs a helper molecule, tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4), to work. In 1999, Japanese researcher Shigeo Kure and colleagues reported that some children with classic PAH-deficient PKU lowered their blood phenylalanine when given BH4. This meant a cofactor could boost a weak but present enzyme. BioMarin developed sapropterin dihydrochloride, a synthetic form of the natural 6R-BH4. The FDA approved it as Kuvan in 2007, the first drug treatment for PKU beyond diet.", "timeline": [["1934", "Asbjorn Folling describes phenylketonuria."], ["1950s", "Low-phenylalanine diet therapy is introduced."], ["1999", "Kure and colleagues report BH4-responsive PAH deficiency."], ["2007", "FDA approves sapropterin (Kuvan) for BH4-responsive PKU."]], "cell": "Phenylalanine hydroxylase in the liver converts phenylalanine to tyrosine. It needs BH4 as a cofactor and uses oxygen and iron. In PKU, PAH variants reduce enzyme activity, so phenylalanine builds up in the blood and brain, where it interferes with myelin and neurotransmitter production. Sapropterin is synthetic 6R-BH4. In patients who still have some PAH activity, extra BH4 works two ways: it saturates the cofactor site, and it acts as a pharmacologic chaperone, helping misfolded PAH fold correctly and resist breakdown. The result is more conversion of phenylalanine to tyrosine. People with null PAH variants (no enzyme at all) usually do not respond. BH4 is also the cofactor for tyrosine hydroxylase, tryptophan hydroxylase and nitric oxide synthase.", "body": [["Blood and brain", "In responders, blood phenylalanine falls, which protects brain development and function and reduces problems with attention, mood and thinking."], ["Diet", "Lower phenylalanine lets many responders eat more natural protein, though most still follow a modified diet."], ["Liver", "The liver is where PAH lives, so this is where sapropterin does its work."], ["Gut", "Headache, diarrhea, abdominal pain and vomiting are common; gastritis and esophagitis have been reported."], ["Nervous system", "Because BH4 is also a cofactor for dopamine and serotonin synthesis, hyperactivity has been reported."]], "journey": "Sapropterin is taken by mouth once daily, dissolved in water or juice or mixed with soft food. Taking it with a meal increases absorption. It peaks about three to four hours later and has a half-life of about seven hours. The body handles it like natural BH4: it is oxidized to dihydrobiopterin and biopterin, recycled and excreted. A trial period with blood phenylalanine checks shows whether a person responds.", "sidefx": "Side effects are generally mild. Headache, runny nose, diarrhea, abdominal pain and vomiting are common. Inflammation of the stomach or esophagus has occurred. Phenylalanine can drop too low (hypophenylalaninemia) in some patients, especially young children, because phenylalanine is still an essential amino acid. Since BH4 supports nitric oxide synthase, caution is used with drugs that affect nitric oxide, such as PDE5 inhibitors. Because BH4 helps make dopamine, hyperactivity can occur. Allergic reactions including anaphylaxis are rare. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Newborn screening for PKU started with Robert Guthrie's blood spot test in the early 1960s, the same heel-prick card used for newborn screening today.", "src": ["Kuvan (sapropterin) FDA label", "Kure S et al. J Pediatr 1999", "Folling A. Hoppe-Seyler's Z Physiol Chem 1934"]}, "metreleptin": {"origin": "In 1994 Jeffrey Friedman and his team at Rockefeller University cloned the obese (ob) gene in mice and found it codes for a hormone made by fat cells. They named it leptin, from the Greek word for thin. Leptin tells the brain how much fat the body has stored. Hopes that leptin would treat common obesity faded because most people with obesity already have high leptin and are resistant to it. But people who lack fat tissue, as in generalized lipodystrophy, have very low leptin, which leads to huge appetite, severe diabetes and fatty liver. Researchers at the NIH, including Elif Oral and Phillip Gorden, reported in 2002 that leptin replacement dramatically improved these patients. Amgen originally developed the recombinant protein. The FDA approved metreleptin as Myalept in 2014.", "timeline": [["1994", "Jeffrey Friedman's lab clones the ob gene and discovers leptin."], ["2002", "NIH researchers report leptin replacement benefits in lipodystrophy in the New England Journal of Medicine."], ["2014", "FDA approves metreleptin (Myalept) for generalized lipodystrophy."]], "cell": "Leptin is a hormone secreted by fat cells in proportion to fat mass. Metreleptin is recombinant human leptin with an extra methionine at the N-terminus. It binds the leptin receptor (LepR), a class I cytokine receptor found in the hypothalamus and peripheral tissues. Binding activates JAK2, which phosphorylates the receptor and the transcription factor STAT3. In the arcuate nucleus, STAT3 signaling increases POMC neurons (which reduce appetite) and decreases NPY and AgRP neurons (which drive hunger). Leptin also acts on the liver and muscle, directly and through the brain, to increase fat oxidation and improve insulin sensitivity. In lipodystrophy, where there is no fat to store excess lipid, replacing the missing leptin cuts food intake and moves lipid out of liver and muscle.", "body": [["Brain (hypothalamus)", "Restores the fullness signal, reducing the constant hunger seen in people with very low leptin."], ["Liver", "Reduces fat in the liver and liver size, lowering liver enzymes over time."], ["Blood sugar", "Improves insulin sensitivity, lowering HbA1c and the need for insulin; hypoglycemia can occur if diabetes drugs are not adjusted."], ["Blood fats", "Triglycerides fall, which lowers the risk of pancreatitis in these patients."], ["Reproductive system", "Leptin helps signal energy sufficiency to the reproductive axis, and menstrual cycles can return."], ["Immune system", "Neutralizing antibodies can form, and T-cell lymphoma has been reported in patients with acquired lipodystrophy."]], "journey": "Metreleptin is injected under the skin once daily. It peaks about four hours after a dose and has a half-life of about four hours. Like natural leptin, it is cleared mainly through the kidneys, where it is filtered and broken down. Metabolic effects such as lower triglycerides and HbA1c build over weeks.", "sidefx": "Hypoglycemia occurs when improved insulin sensitivity meets unchanged insulin or sulfonylurea doses. Weight loss reflects reduced appetite. Neutralizing antibodies can develop and may block both the drug and any leftover natural leptin, leading to severe infections or loss of metabolic control. T-cell lymphoma has been seen in patients with acquired generalized lipodystrophy, many of whom had immune abnormalities before treatment; whether the drug plays a role is unclear. Because of these risks it is available only through a REMS program. Leptin can stimulate immune cells, which may worsen autoimmune diseases. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The original ob/ob mice were so obese they weighed about three times as much as normal mice, and leptin injections made them lose weight quickly.", "src": ["Myalept (metreleptin) FDA label", "Zhang Y, Friedman JM et al. Nature 1994", "Oral EA et al. N Engl J Med 2002"]}, "lanadelumab": {"origin": "Hereditary angioedema (HAE) causes sudden, severe swelling because the body makes too much bradykinin. Researchers at Dyax Corporation in Massachusetts used phage display, a method of screening huge libraries of antibodies on the surface of bacteriophages, to find a fully human antibody that blocks active plasma kallikrein, the enzyme that releases bradykinin. Dyax had already developed ecallantide, a small kallikrein inhibitor protein. Shire acquired Dyax in 2016, and lanadelumab was tested in the HELP trial for long-term prevention. In 2018 the FDA approved it as Takhzyro, the first monoclonal antibody for preventing HAE attacks. Shire was then acquired by Takeda.", "timeline": [["1888", "William Osler describes the hereditary nature of angioedema."], ["2009", "Ecallantide, an earlier Dyax kallikrein inhibitor, is approved for acute attacks."], ["2018", "HELP trial results and FDA approval of lanadelumab (Takhzyro) for HAE prevention."]], "cell": "In HAE, C1 esterase inhibitor (C1-INH) is missing or does not work. C1-INH normally controls plasma kallikrein and factor XIIa in the contact (kallikrein-kinin) system. Without that control, active plasma kallikrein keeps cutting high-molecular-weight kininogen to release bradykinin. Bradykinin binds B2 receptors on blood vessel endothelium, which loosens the junctions between cells and dilates vessels, so fluid leaks into tissue and causes swelling. Lanadelumab is a fully human IgG1 kappa monoclonal antibody that binds the active site of plasma kallikrein and blocks it. It does not bind the inactive form, prekallikrein. Less kallikrein activity means less bradykinin, less vascular leak and fewer attacks. It does not treat histamine-driven allergic angioedema.", "body": [["Skin and soft tissue", "Fewer episodes of swelling in the face, hands, feet and genitals."], ["Airway", "Preventing attacks lowers the chance of laryngeal swelling, the most dangerous HAE symptom."], ["Gut", "Bowel wall swelling causes severe abdominal pain and vomiting; prevention reduces these attacks."], ["Blood vessels", "Blocking kallikrein lowers bradykinin-driven leaks in small vessels."], ["Clotting tests", "Because kallikrein takes part in contact activation, the antibody can prolong the aPTT test without causing bleeding."]], "journey": "Lanadelumab is injected under the skin, often by the patient, every two to four weeks. It is absorbed slowly, peaking about five days after injection. Like other IgG antibodies, it is protected from breakdown by the neonatal Fc receptor, giving it a half-life of about two weeks, and steady state takes about 70 days. It is broken down by normal protein catabolism, not by the liver CYP system or the kidneys.", "sidefx": "The main side effects are injection site pain, redness and bruising. Upper respiratory infections, headache, rash, muscle pain and dizziness were reported. Hypersensitivity reactions can occur because it is a protein. Bleeding is not a major problem, even though aPTT can be prolonged, because kallikrein is not essential for normal clotting in the body. It is not used for acute attacks; those need fast-acting drugs such as icatibant or C1 inhibitor. There is no specific antidote.", "fun": "Phage display, the method used to find lanadelumab's antibody, earned George Smith and Gregory Winter a share of the 2018 Nobel Prize in Chemistry, the same year the drug was approved.", "src": ["Takhzyro (lanadelumab) FDA label", "Banerji A et al. JAMA 2018 (HELP)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "berotralstat": {"origin": "Berotralstat came from structure-based drug design at BioCryst Pharmaceuticals in North Carolina. BioCryst chemists used X-ray crystal structures of plasma kallikrein to build a small molecule that fits tightly into the enzyme's active site and is still absorbed when swallowed. Earlier HAE prevention options all needed injections or infusions, so a daily pill was a real change for patients. In December 2020 the FDA approved berotralstat as Orladeyo, the first oral drug to prevent HAE attacks. Approval was later extended to younger children with oral pellets.", "timeline": [["2010s", "BioCryst designs berotralstat using crystal structures of plasma kallikrein."], ["2020", "FDA approves berotralstat (Orladeyo), the first oral HAE prevention drug."], ["2020s", "Approval is extended to younger children with an oral pellet form."]], "cell": "In HAE, missing or faulty C1 esterase inhibitor leaves plasma kallikrein poorly controlled. Kallikrein cuts high-molecular-weight kininogen to release bradykinin, which acts on B2 receptors on blood vessel endothelium. This opens gaps between endothelial cells and causes swelling. Berotralstat is a small-molecule, reversible, competitive inhibitor of plasma kallikrein. It sits in the enzyme's active site so kininogen cannot be cut. Lower bradykinin means less B2 receptor signaling, less vascular leak and fewer swelling attacks. It is selective for plasma kallikrein over related serine proteases.", "body": [["Skin and soft tissue", "Reduces how often swelling attacks happen in the face, limbs and genitals."], ["Airway", "Fewer attacks overall means fewer chances of dangerous throat swelling, but acute attacks still need on-demand therapy."], ["Gut", "Abdominal pain, vomiting, diarrhea and reflux are the most common side effects, and taking it with food helps."], ["Heart", "At higher than approved doses it can prolong the QT interval."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises have been seen, and doses are adjusted in liver impairment."]], "journey": "Berotralstat is taken by mouth once daily with food. It is absorbed over several hours and is about 99% protein bound. It has a long half-life of about 93 hours, which allows once-daily dosing and steady blood levels. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP2D6 and CYP3A4, and it leaves mostly in feces. It is a moderate inhibitor of CYP2D6 and CYP3A4, so it raises levels of some other drugs.", "sidefx": "GI side effects such as abdominal pain, vomiting and diarrhea are common and are thought to be local gut irritation; they often ease over time and are reduced by taking the drug with food. At doses higher than approved, berotralstat prolongs the QT interval, so higher doses are avoided and QT-prolonging combinations are watched. It also inhibits CYP2D6, CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein, so it can raise levels of some other drugs, such as digoxin. It does not treat an attack that has already started. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Orladeyo (berotralstat) FDA label", "Zuraw B et al. J Allergy Clin Immunol 2021 (APeX-2)"]}, "c1-esterase-inhibitor": {"origin": "In 1882 Heinrich Quincke described episodes of sudden swelling, and in 1888 William Osler described families in whom it ran across generations, now known as hereditary angioedema. In 1963 Virginia Donaldson and Richard Evans showed that patients lacked a plasma protein that inhibits the first component of complement, C1 esterase inhibitor. Purified C1-INH from donated human plasma was used in Europe for decades. In the US, Cinryze was approved in 2008 for prevention, Berinert in 2009 for acute attacks, Ruconest in 2014 as a recombinant version made in the milk of transgenic rabbits, and Haegarda in 2017 as a subcutaneous preventive form.", "timeline": [["1888", "William Osler describes hereditary angioedema."], ["1963", "Donaldson and Evans show HAE patients lack C1 esterase inhibitor."], ["2008", "FDA approves plasma-derived C1-INH (Cinryze) for prevention."], ["2009", "Berinert is approved for acute attacks."], ["2014", "Ruconest, a recombinant C1-INH from transgenic rabbit milk, is approved."], ["2017", "Haegarda, a subcutaneous C1-INH, is approved for prevention."]], "cell": "C1 esterase inhibitor is a serpin, a serine protease inhibitor. It acts like a mousetrap: a target protease cuts a loop on the serpin, and the serpin then snaps shut, trapping the enzyme in a covalent complex that is cleared from the blood. Its targets are C1r and C1s of the classical complement pathway, plasma kallikrein and factor XIIa of the contact system, and to some degree factor XIa. In HAE, low or nonfunctional C1-INH lets factor XIIa and kallikrein run unchecked, cutting high-molecular-weight kininogen into bradykinin. Bradykinin acts on B2 receptors to make blood vessels leaky. Giving C1-INH restores functional levels, controls kallikrein and factor XIIa, lowers bradykinin and stops or prevents swelling. It also limits complement consumption, so C4 levels can rise.", "body": [["Airway", "Stopping bradykinin-driven swelling can prevent or reverse life-threatening throat swelling."], ["Skin and soft tissue", "Reduces swelling attacks in the face, hands, feet and genitals."], ["Gut", "Bowel swelling during attacks causes severe abdominal pain; replacement shortens or prevents these attacks."], ["Complement system", "Controlling C1r and C1s slows complement consumption, so complement levels such as C4 can rise."], ["Clotting system", "Thrombotic events have been reported, especially with high doses or with indwelling catheters, likely related to effects on contact and clotting pathways."]], "journey": "Most products are given by slow IV injection; Haegarda is injected under the skin. After IV use, functional C1-INH rises right away. Plasma-derived Cinryze has a half-life of about two to three days. Berinert's is somewhat shorter, and recombinant Ruconest's is only a few hours because its different sugar chains lead to faster clearance. C1-INH is used up as it traps target enzymes, and the complexes are removed by the liver. It is not cleared through CYP enzymes.", "sidefx": "Headache, nausea, rash and infusion site reactions are common. Hypersensitivity can occur, and Ruconest is avoided in people allergic to rabbits because trace rabbit proteins may be present. Thromboembolic events have been reported, particularly at high doses or in people with catheters or other clot risks. Plasma-derived products carry a very small theoretical risk of transmitting infectious agents, reduced by donor screening and viral inactivation steps. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Ruconest is one of the few medicines made in animal milk: rabbits are engineered to secrete human C1-INH, which is then purified.", "src": ["Cinryze, Berinert, Haegarda and Ruconest FDA labels", "Donaldson VH, Evans RR. Am J Med 1963", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "icatibant": {"origin": "Bradykinin was discovered in 1949 by Brazilian scientists Mauricio Rocha e Silva, Wilson Beraldo and Gastao Rosenfeld, who found that snake venom released a substance from blood proteins that slowly contracted gut muscle. Later work showed bradykinin drives vascular leak and pain through B2 receptors. In the 1980s and 1990s chemists, including teams at Hoechst in Germany, built peptide antagonists of the B2 receptor by swapping natural amino acids for unnatural ones that resist breakdown. One of these, coded HOE 140, became icatibant. It was approved in Europe in 2008 and by the FDA in 2011 as Firazyr for acute attacks of hereditary angioedema in adults, giving patients a self-injected treatment.", "timeline": [["1949", "Rocha e Silva and colleagues discover bradykinin."], ["1990s", "Hoechst researchers develop the B2 receptor antagonist HOE 140 (icatibant)."], ["2008", "Icatibant is approved in the European Union."], ["2011", "FDA approves icatibant (Firazyr) for acute HAE attacks."]], "cell": "In HAE, missing or faulty C1 esterase inhibitor leaves plasma kallikrein uncontrolled, so it cleaves high-molecular-weight kininogen into large amounts of bradykinin. Bradykinin binds the B2 receptor, a Gq protein-coupled receptor on blood vessel endothelium. Gq activation raises intracellular calcium and leads to nitric oxide and prostacyclin release, causing vasodilation and the opening of gaps between endothelial cells, so fluid leaks into tissues. Icatibant is a synthetic 10-amino-acid peptide that competitively blocks the B2 receptor. It contains nonproteinogenic amino acids that resist the peptidases that normally break down bradykinin quickly. With the receptor blocked, vascular leak stops and swelling resolves. It has no effect on histamine-mediated allergic angioedema.", "body": [["Airway", "Blocking bradykinin can stop laryngeal swelling from progressing, though airway attacks still need emergency care."], ["Skin and soft tissue", "Swelling of the face, hands and feet begins to go down, with meaningful relief in about two hours in trials."], ["Gut", "Bowel wall swelling resolves, easing severe abdominal pain."], ["Injection site", "Bradykinin-like local reactions such as redness, swelling, burning and itching occur at the injection site in most patients."], ["Liver", "Transient rises in liver enzymes were reported."]], "journey": "Icatibant is injected under the skin of the abdomen and is almost fully absorbed. It peaks in the blood in less than an hour. Proteolytic enzymes break it into inactive fragments, not CYP enzymes. Its half-life is about one to two hours, so symptoms can return and a repeat injection is sometimes given. Inactive pieces leave in the urine.", "sidefx": "Injection site reactions such as redness, swelling, burning and itching are nearly universal and come from the injection itself and local peptide effects. Fever, dizziness, nausea, headache, rash and raised liver enzymes have been reported. Because it blocks bradykinin, which can protect the heart during ischemia, caution is noted in acute coronary disease. Laryngeal attacks can still progress despite treatment, so patients seek emergency care afterward. There is no antidote, and its short half-life means effects fade within hours.", "fun": "Bradykinin got its name from the Greek words for slow and movement, because it made gut muscle contract slowly compared with histamine.", "src": ["Firazyr (icatibant) FDA label", "Rocha e Silva M et al. Am J Physiol 1949", "Cicardi M et al. N Engl J Med 2010"]}, "cholic-acid": {"origin": "Cholic acid is one of the two primary bile acids the human liver makes from cholesterol. It was first isolated from ox bile in the 1800s. In the 1980s and 1990s, researchers including Kenneth Setchell at Cincinnati Children's Hospital used mass spectrometry to identify rare inherited defects in bile acid synthesis, where a broken enzyme leaves infants with toxic, abnormal bile acids and severe liver disease. Treating these children with oral cholic acid replaced the missing normal bile acid and switched off production of the toxic ones. After decades of use under research protocols, the FDA approved cholic acid as Cholbam in 2015 for bile acid synthesis disorders and as add-on therapy for peroxisomal disorders.", "timeline": [["1800s", "Cholic acid is isolated from animal bile."], ["1980s", "Setchell and colleagues identify inborn errors of bile acid synthesis using mass spectrometry."], ["2015", "FDA approves cholic acid (Cholbam)."]], "cell": "Normally the liver converts cholesterol into bile acids, starting with the rate-limiting enzyme CYP7A1 (cholesterol 7-alpha-hydroxylase). Bile acids returning to the liver activate the farnesoid X receptor (FXR), a nuclear receptor. FXR switches on SHP and, in the gut, FGF19, both of which turn down CYP7A1. When a downstream enzyme is missing, the liver makes few normal bile acids, so this brake is off. CYP7A1 keeps running and abnormal intermediates pile up, damaging liver cells and causing cholestasis. Oral cholic acid joins the bile acid pool, activates FXR and restores negative feedback, so production of toxic intermediates drops. It also restores bile flow and micelle formation in the gut, allowing fat and fat-soluble vitamins to be absorbed.", "body": [["Liver", "Turning off toxic intermediate production lessens cholestatic liver injury and jaundice, and liver tests can improve."], ["Gut", "Restored bile acid micelles improve fat digestion, reducing steatorrhea; diarrhea is a common side effect."], ["Fat-soluble vitamins", "Better micelles improve absorption of vitamins A, D, E and K, preventing deficiencies such as bleeding and rickets."], ["Growth", "Better nutrition helps children grow and gain weight."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral neuropathy has been reported, possibly related to underlying disease or vitamin E status."]], "journey": "Cholic acid is taken by mouth with food. It is absorbed in the small intestine and enters the enterohepatic circulation like the body's own bile acids. The liver takes it up and conjugates it with glycine or taurine, then secretes it into bile through the bile salt export pump (BSEP). In the gut, much is reabsorbed in the ileum and recycled; some reaches the colon, where bacteria deconjugate it and turn it into deoxycholic acid. A portion leaves in the feces each cycle. Bile acid resins and aluminum antacids bind it and are separated in time.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea happens because bile acids reaching the colon draw water and stimulate secretion. Reflux, nausea and abdominal pain also occur. If too much is given, or in patients with advanced liver disease, extra bile acid can itself be toxic to liver cells, so liver tests are watched and therapy is stopped if they worsen. Worsening liver function is a particular concern in cirrhosis. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Deoxycholic acid, a gut bacterial product of cholic acid, is also an FDA-approved injection (Kybella) used to dissolve fat under the chin.", "src": ["Cholbam (cholic acid) FDA label", "Setchell KDR, Heubi JE. J Pediatr Gastroenterol Nutr 2006"]}, "tiopronin": {"origin": "Cystinuria is one of the oldest known inherited metabolic diseases; William Hyde Wollaston described cystine stones in 1810, and Archibald Garrod listed it among his inborn errors of metabolism in 1908. Patients lose large amounts of cystine in urine, where it crystallizes into stones. In the 1960s doctors found that penicillamine, a thiol drug, broke cystine into more soluble pieces, but its side effects limited its use. Tiopronin (alpha-mercaptopropionylglycine) is a related thiol compound that was used in Japan before it reached the US. It does the same chemical job and was often better tolerated. The FDA approved it as Thiola in 1988, and an enteric-coated version, Thiola EC, was approved in 2019.", "timeline": [["1810", "William Hyde Wollaston describes cystine bladder stones."], ["1908", "Archibald Garrod includes cystinuria among his inborn errors of metabolism."], ["1988", "FDA approves tiopronin (Thiola) for cystinuria."], ["2019", "FDA approves enteric-coated Thiola EC."]], "cell": "In cystinuria, variants in SLC3A1 or SLC7A9 break a kidney tubule transporter that reabsorbs cystine and the dibasic amino acids. Cystine is two cysteine molecules joined by a disulfide bond, and it is poorly soluble in acidic urine, so it forms crystals and stones. Tiopronin has a free thiol (sulfhydryl) group. In urine it attacks cystine's disulfide bond in a thiol-disulfide exchange, splitting it and forming a tiopronin-cysteine mixed disulfide. This mixed disulfide is much more water soluble than cystine. As a result, free cystine falls below its solubility limit, new stones form less often and some existing stones may dissolve. There is no receptor involved; the drug works by direct chemistry.", "body": [["Kidneys and urinary tract", "Lower free cystine in urine means fewer new stones, fewer procedures and protection of kidney function. High fluid intake and urinary alkalinization are part of the plan."], ["Kidney filters", "Some patients develop protein in the urine or nephrotic syndrome, likely from immune complex damage to glomeruli."], ["Skin", "Rash and itching are common reasons people stop the drug."], ["Gut and taste", "Nausea, diarrhea and taste changes are common."], ["Blood and immune system", "Fever, swollen lymph nodes and anemia can occur; agranulocytosis is a rare thiol-class risk."]], "journey": "Tiopronin is taken by mouth in divided doses through the day. It is absorbed from the gut, and the delayed-release form peaks about three to six hours later. Part of it is metabolized to 2-mercaptopropionic acid. The kidneys excrete most of it in the urine, both unchanged and as mixed disulfides with cysteine. This is useful because the urine is where it needs to act. Each dose works for several hours, so it is spread across the day.", "sidefx": "Many side effects resemble penicillamine's because both are reactive thiols. Thiols can bind proteins and act as haptens, which can trigger immune reactions: rash, fever, swollen lymph nodes, joint pain and proteinuria from immune complex glomerular disease. Blood counts and urine protein are monitored. Taste disturbance may relate to binding of trace metals like zinc. Nausea and diarrhea are common. There is no antidote; stopping the drug usually reverses immune effects.", "fun": "Cystine stones are often visible on plain x-rays because the sulfur atoms make them dense, giving them a smooth, ground-glass look.", "src": ["Thiola and Thiola EC (tiopronin) FDA label", "Garrod AE. Inborn Errors of Metabolism, 1909", "Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology"]}, "cysteamine": {"origin": "Cystinosis was first described in 1903 by Swiss biochemist Emil Abderhalden, who found cystine crystals in the organs of children from one family. Later researchers showed the defect: a lysosomal transporter, now called cystinosin, that normally moves cystine out of lysosomes. In 1976 Jerry Schneider and Jess Thoene at the University of California, San Diego showed that cysteamine, a simple aminothiol made in the body from coenzyme A breakdown, could empty cystine from patients' cells. Cysteamine became the standard therapy. The FDA approved immediate-release Cystagon in 1994 and delayed-release Procysbi in 2013, which allows dosing every 12 hours. Cysteamine eye drops (Cystaran, Cystadrops) treat corneal crystals separately.", "timeline": [["1903", "Emil Abderhalden describes cystinosis."], ["1976", "Thoene and Schneider report that cysteamine depletes cystine from cystinotic cells."], ["1994", "FDA approves cysteamine bitartrate (Cystagon)."], ["2013", "FDA approves delayed-release cysteamine (Procysbi)."]], "cell": "In nephropathic cystinosis, variants in the CTNS gene break cystinosin, the transporter that carries cystine out of lysosomes. Cystine builds up and forms crystals inside cells throughout the body, starting with kidney tubule cells. Cysteamine is a small aminothiol that enters lysosomes. Inside, it reacts with cystine in a thiol-disulfide exchange, producing free cysteine and a cysteine-cysteamine mixed disulfide. This mixed disulfide resembles lysine closely enough to exit through a different lysosomal transporter for cationic amino acids, PQLC2. Free cysteine leaves through its own transporter. In this way cysteamine bypasses the broken cystinosin and empties lysosomal cystine. White blood cell cystine levels are used to track the effect.", "body": [["Kidneys", "Lowering cystine in kidney cells slows loss of kidney function and delays kidney failure, though tubular losses (Fanconi syndrome) often persist."], ["Growth", "Children who start treatment early grow better, since stored cystine harms many tissues."], ["Thyroid and other organs", "Long-term therapy delays later problems such as hypothyroidism, diabetes, muscle weakness and swallowing problems."], ["Eyes", "Oral cysteamine does not reach the cornea well, so separate eye drops are used for corneal crystals."], ["Gut", "Cysteamine raises stomach acid production, causing nausea, vomiting, pain and sometimes ulcers."], ["Breath and skin", "Its sulfur metabolites are released in breath and sweat, causing a strong odor."]], "journey": "Cysteamine is taken by mouth. The delayed-release form passes the stomach and is absorbed in the small intestine, peaking about three hours after a dose, and allows dosing every 12 hours. It reaches cells throughout the body, where it enters lysosomes. Its half-life is about four hours. It is metabolized to hypotaurine and taurine, and sulfur-containing products such as dimethylsulfide leave through the breath and sweat. White cell cystine falls within hours of a dose.", "sidefx": "Cysteamine stimulates gastrin and stomach acid, which explains nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain and GI ulcers or bleeding. Sulfur compounds exhaled and sweated out cause the characteristic breath and body odor. High doses have been linked to skin and bone lesions resembling Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, likely from effects on collagen cross-linking, as well as raised intracranial pressure, seizures, lethargy and low white counts. Doses are adjusted using white cell cystine levels. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Cysteamine is a natural molecule: the body makes it when it breaks down coenzyme A, and it is the very tail end of that vitamin B5-containing molecule.", "src": ["Procysbi and Cystagon (cysteamine) FDA labels", "Thoene JG et al. J Clin Invest 1976", "Gahl WA et al. N Engl J Med 2002"]}, "collagenase-clostridium-histolyticum": {"origin": "Clostridium histolyticum is a soil bacterium named for its ability to dissolve tissue; it was found in war wounds during World War I. Its collagenases break down the tough collagen fibers of connective tissue. At Stony Brook University in New York, hand surgeon Lawrence Hurst and researcher Marie Badalamente spent years testing purified collagenase as an alternative to surgery for Dupuytren contracture, in which collagen cords pull fingers into the palm. The technology was licensed to BioSpecifics and developed with Auxilium Pharmaceuticals. The FDA approved it as Xiaflex in 2010 for Dupuytren contracture with a palpable cord and in 2013 for Peyronie disease, making it the first FDA-approved drug for both conditions. Endo later acquired Auxilium.", "timeline": [["1910s", "Clostridium histolyticum is identified in infected war wounds."], ["1990s", "Hurst and Badalamente at Stony Brook test collagenase injections for Dupuytren contracture."], ["2009", "CORD I trial results are published in the New England Journal of Medicine."], ["2010", "FDA approves Xiaflex for Dupuytren contracture."], ["2013", "FDA approves Xiaflex for Peyronie disease."]], "cell": "Xiaflex contains two collagenases from Clostridium histolyticum: class I (AUX-I) and class II (AUX-II). Both are zinc-dependent enzymes that hydrolyze the triple helix of interstitial collagen, mainly types I and III, the main collagens in Dupuytren cords and Peyronie plaques. AUX-I cuts near the ends of the collagen molecule, and AUX-II cuts at sites in the interior, so together they break collagen into smaller pieces faster than either alone. When injected into a cord, they weaken it so a manipulation the next day can rupture it and straighten the finger. In Peyronie disease, they soften the plaque in the tunica albuginea so modeling can reduce curvature. The enzymes do not attack collagen type IV in basement membranes as strongly, but nearby normal structures such as tendons and ligaments can still be affected.", "body": [["Hand (Dupuytren cords)", "The enzyme weakens the collagen cord so a gentle extension procedure can snap it and straighten the contracted finger."], ["Penis (Peyronie plaques)", "Plaque softening plus modeling reduces curvature. Because of corporal rupture risk, Peyronie use is restricted to a REMS program."], ["Tendons and ligaments", "If the enzyme reaches nearby tendons or ligaments, it can weaken and rupture them."], ["Skin and soft tissue", "Swelling, bruising, pain and skin tears during finger extension are common local effects of collagen breakdown."], ["Immune system", "Most patients develop antibodies after repeated injections, and allergic reactions can occur."]], "journey": "The drug is injected directly into the cord or plaque. It acts locally and is quickly broken down by tissue proteases. Blood levels are not detectable after a standard injection into a hand cord, so very little reaches the circulation. Cord weakening happens within a day or so, and the manipulation or modeling step follows.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow directly from collagen breakdown where it is not wanted. Bruising, swelling, pain and bleeding come from local tissue and small vessel damage. Tendon or ligament rupture can happen if enzyme reaches these structures, which is why injection technique matters. Skin can tear during finger extension. In Peyronie disease, weakening the tunica can lead to corporal rupture (penile fracture) or serious hematoma, the basis of its boxed warning. Antibodies and hypersensitivity are possible. People on anticoagulants may bleed more locally. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Dupuytren contracture is sometimes called Viking disease because it is common in people of Northern European descent.", "src": ["Xiaflex (collagenase clostridium histolyticum) FDA label", "Hurst LC et al. N Engl J Med 2009 (CORD I)"]}, "collagenase-peyronie": {"origin": "Peyronie disease is named for Francois Gigot de la Peyronie, surgeon to King Louis XV of France, who described it in 1743. Fibrous, collagen-rich scar tissue (a plaque) forms in the tunica albuginea, the tough sheath around the erectile tissue, causing the penis to curve. For centuries options were limited to observation or surgery. Collagenase clostridium histolyticum, first developed for Dupuytren contracture by Lawrence Hurst and Marie Badalamente at Stony Brook University, was tested for Peyronie plaques in the IMPRESS trials. In 2013 the FDA approved Xiaflex for Peyronie disease with a palpable plaque and curvature of at least 30 degrees, the first FDA-approved drug treatment for the condition.", "timeline": [["1743", "Francois de la Peyronie describes the disease."], ["2010", "Xiaflex is first approved for Dupuytren contracture."], ["2013", "IMPRESS I and II trial results are published, and FDA approves Xiaflex for Peyronie disease."]], "cell": "The plaque in Peyronie disease is made mostly of disorganized type I and III collagen deposited after small injuries to the tunica albuginea. The product contains two Clostridium histolyticum collagenases, AUX-I and AUX-II, which hydrolyze the triple-helical region of these collagens at different sites. Working together, they break the plaque's collagen framework into smaller fragments. This softens and partly dissolves the plaque. A penile modeling procedure after injection then gently stretches the softened area, which helps lengthen the shortened side and reduce curvature. The enzymes act only locally and are broken down quickly by tissue proteases.", "body": [["Penile plaque", "Breaking down plaque collagen and modeling the tissue reduces curvature over several treatment cycles."], ["Tunica albuginea", "Weakening of this sheath can lead to corporal rupture (penile fracture), a reason for the boxed warning and REMS program."], ["Blood vessels in the penis", "Local damage to small vessels causes bruising, swelling and hematoma, which are very common."], ["Urethra", "Urethral injury is a rare serious complication if the enzyme reaches nearby tissue."], ["Immune system", "Antibodies form in most patients after repeated injections, and allergic reactions can occur."]], "journey": "The enzyme is injected into the plaque in the office. It works locally, and systemic blood levels are not measurable after injection. Tissue proteases break it down within hours to days. A modeling procedure follows, and patients do home stretching between cycles. The effect builds over several treatment cycles.", "sidefx": "Because the enzyme breaks down collagen, surrounding tissues can also be weakened. Most men have bruising, swelling or pain, and penile hematoma is common. The most serious risk is corporal rupture, where the weakened tunica tears during an erection or sexual activity, which is why sexual activity is avoided for a period after each cycle and the product is available only through a REMS program. Urethral injury is rare. Anticoagulants increase local bleeding. Hypersensitivity can occur. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The same bottle of enzyme is used for two quite different jobs: snapping finger cords in the hand and softening plaques in the penis, both made of the same tough collagens.", "src": ["Xiaflex (collagenase clostridium histolyticum) FDA label", "Gelbard M et al. J Urol 2013 (IMPRESS I and II)"]}, "pozelimab": {"origin": "CHAPLE disease (CD55 deficiency with hyperactivation of complement, angiopathic thrombosis and protein-losing enteropathy) was described in 2017 by Ahmet Ozen, Michael Lenardo and colleagues at the US National Institutes of Health, who studied families with severe gut protein loss and blood clots. They showed that loss of CD55, a protein that normally brakes complement on cell surfaces, lets complement attack the gut's lymphatic vessels and blood vessels, and that blocking complement C5 helped. Regeneron developed pozelimab, a fully human monoclonal antibody against C5, using its antibody mouse platform. In 2023 the FDA approved it as Veopoz, the first treatment approved for CHAPLE disease.", "timeline": [["2017", "Ozen and colleagues describe CD55 deficiency (CHAPLE disease) in the New England Journal of Medicine."], ["2023", "FDA approves pozelimab (Veopoz) for CHAPLE disease in patients 1 year and older."]], "cell": "The complement system is a cascade of blood proteins that tags and destroys microbes. Several pathways converge on C3 convertases and then C5 convertase, which cuts C5 into C5a and C5b. C5a is a strong inflammatory signal that draws in and activates white cells. C5b starts assembly of the membrane attack complex (C5b-9), a pore that punches holes in cell membranes. CD55, also called decay-accelerating factor, normally sits on our own cells and breaks apart convertases so complement does not attack them. In CHAPLE disease, CD55 is missing, so complement deposits on intestinal lymphatics and vessel lining, damaging them so protein leaks into the gut and clots form. Pozelimab binds C5 and blocks its cleavage, so neither C5a nor the membrane attack complex is made.", "body": [["Gut", "Less complement damage to intestinal lymphatics stops protein leaking into the bowel, easing abdominal pain, diarrhea and vomiting."], ["Blood proteins", "Serum albumin and total protein return toward normal as gut protein loss stops."], ["Fluid balance", "With albumin restored, swelling of the face and limbs (edema) decreases."], ["Blood vessels", "Blocking terminal complement may reduce the clotting tendency seen in the disease."], ["Immune defense", "Without C5 function the body cannot kill Neisseria bacteria well, so meningococcal infections become a serious risk."], ["Growth", "Children gain weight and grow better as nutrition improves."]], "journey": "Pozelimab starts with an IV loading dose and then continues as weekly injections under the skin, which can be given at home after training. It stays in the blood for a long time like other IgG antibodies, protected from breakdown by the neonatal Fc receptor. Its clearance also depends on how much C5 it binds, so its kinetics are partly target driven. It is broken down by normal protein catabolism, not by the liver CYP system or the kidneys.", "sidefx": "Blocking C5 removes the membrane attack complex, which is the body's main way of killing Neisseria bacteria. That is why the drug carries a boxed warning for life-threatening meningococcal infections, requires meningococcal vaccination and is available only through a REMS program. Other serious infections can also occur. Upper respiratory infections, hives, hair loss and fractures were reported in the trial. As a protein it can cause hypersensitivity reactions. There is no rapid reversal; stopping the drug lets C5 activity return over time.", "fun": "CD55's other name, decay-accelerating factor, describes its job: it speeds the falling apart (decay) of complement convertases on the body's own cells.", "src": ["Veopoz (pozelimab) FDA label", "Ozen A et al. N Engl J Med 2017"]}, "alglucosidase-alfa": {"origin": "In 1932 Dutch pathologist Joannes Cassianus Pompe described an infant who died with a massively enlarged heart full of glycogen. In 1963 Belgian biochemist Henri-Gery Hers showed the cause was a missing lysosomal enzyme, acid alpha-glucosidase. This made Pompe disease the first known lysosomal storage disorder. At Duke University, Yuan-Tsong Chen and colleagues produced the human enzyme in Chinese hamster ovary cells and tested it in infants. Genzyme developed the product, and the FDA approved it as Myozyme in 2006. A version made at larger production scale was approved as Lumizyme in 2010. In infants, who often died before age one, it greatly improved survival.", "timeline": [["1932", "J. C. Pompe describes glycogen storage in the heart of an infant."], ["1963", "Henri-Gery Hers links Pompe disease to acid alpha-glucosidase deficiency."], ["1990s", "Duke researchers develop recombinant acid alpha-glucosidase."], ["2006", "FDA approves alglucosidase alfa (Myozyme)."], ["2010", "FDA approves larger-scale alglucosidase alfa as Lumizyme."]], "cell": "Cells constantly bring a little glycogen into lysosomes, where acid alpha-glucosidase (acid maltase) breaks the alpha-1,4 and alpha-1,6 bonds and frees glucose. In Pompe disease, variants in GAA leave the enzyme low or absent. Glycogen piles up in lysosomes, especially in heart, skeletal and breathing muscle. The swollen lysosomes disrupt muscle fibers and autophagy, causing weakness. Alglucosidase alfa is recombinant human acid alpha-glucosidase carrying mannose-6-phosphate sugars. These bind the cation-independent mannose-6-phosphate receptor on muscle cells, which carries the enzyme into endosomes and on to lysosomes. There it hydrolyzes stored glycogen to glucose. Uptake into skeletal muscle is limited by how much mannose-6-phosphate the enzyme carries.", "body": [["Heart", "In infants, the thick, glycogen-filled heart muscle shrinks and works better, which is a major reason survival improves."], ["Breathing muscles", "Glycogen storage in the diaphragm causes breathing failure; therapy improves ventilator-free survival in infants and stabilizes breathing in adults."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Walking distance and strength stabilize or improve in late-onset disease, though skeletal muscle responds more slowly than heart."], ["Immune system", "Many patients form antibodies. Infants who make no natural enzyme (CRIM-negative) can form high antibody levels that blunt the effect."], ["Kidneys and skin", "Immune complex reactions such as nephrotic syndrome and necrotizing skin lesions have been reported with high antibody levels."]], "journey": "Alglucosidase alfa is given by IV infusion every two weeks, with the rate increased in steps over several hours. It leaves the blood within hours as muscle and other cells take it up through mannose-6-phosphate receptors. Inside lysosomes it keeps working for days. It is broken down like other proteins and is not cleared intact by the kidneys or through CYP enzymes.", "sidefx": "Infusion reactions such as fever, rash, flushing, fast heart rate and blood pressure changes are common because the immune system reacts to the recombinant protein. Anaphylaxis can occur, which is part of its boxed warning. Infants with weak hearts and breathing muscles may not tolerate the fluid volume of an infusion, risking acute heart and lung failure. Heart rhythm problems have been seen during anesthesia in infants with cardiomyopathy. High antibody levels can cause immune complex disease such as nephrotic syndrome and skin lesions. Reactions are handled by slowing or stopping the infusion. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Pompe disease was the first disease ever linked to a missing lysosomal enzyme, which helped launch the whole field of lysosomal storage disorders.", "src": ["Lumizyme (alglucosidase alfa) FDA label", "Hers HG. Biochem J 1963", "Kishnani PS et al. Neurology 2007"]}, "avalglucosidase-alfa": {"origin": "Alglucosidase alfa helped Pompe disease, but skeletal muscle took up the enzyme poorly because few of its sugar chains carried mannose-6-phosphate. Scientists at Sanofi Genzyme designed a next-generation enzyme by chemically attaching synthetic bis-mannose-6-phosphate glycans to the same human acid alpha-glucosidase protein. This gave roughly 15 times more mannose-6-phosphate and much stronger binding to the receptor that pulls the enzyme into muscle. In the COMET trial in late-onset Pompe disease, it was compared head to head with alglucosidase alfa. The FDA approved it as Nexviazyme in 2021 for late-onset Pompe disease in people 1 year and older.", "timeline": [["2006", "First enzyme replacement for Pompe disease, alglucosidase alfa, is approved."], ["2010s", "Sanofi Genzyme develops a glycoengineered enzyme with added bis-mannose-6-phosphate."], ["2021", "FDA approves avalglucosidase alfa (Nexviazyme) for late-onset Pompe disease."]], "cell": "In Pompe disease, low acid alpha-glucosidase leaves glycogen stuck in lysosomes of heart, skeletal and breathing muscle. To work, a replacement enzyme must reach those lysosomes. The cation-independent mannose-6-phosphate receptor on muscle cells binds mannose-6-phosphate sugars and carries the cargo through endosomes to lysosomes. Avalglucosidase alfa is recombinant human acid alpha-glucosidase with synthetic bis-mannose-6-phosphate glycans chemically conjugated to it. Each bis-M6P glycan binds the receptor with high affinity, so more enzyme enters muscle cells. In the acidic lysosome it hydrolyzes alpha-1,4 and alpha-1,6 bonds of glycogen, releasing glucose and clearing storage.", "body": [["Breathing muscles", "In COMET, forced vital capacity improved at least as much as with alglucosidase alfa, reflecting better diaphragm function."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Walking distance improved, with numerically greater gains than the older enzyme, consistent with better muscle uptake."], ["Heart", "Late-onset Pompe disease affects the heart less than the infant form, but enzyme also reaches heart muscle."], ["Immune system", "Antibodies can form, and infusion reactions such as headache, rash, itching and chills occur."]], "journey": "Avalglucosidase alfa is given by IV infusion every two weeks over several hours. Cells take it up from the blood quickly, so its plasma half-life is under two hours. Once in lysosomes it continues to clear glycogen. It is broken down like other proteins and is not cleared intact by the kidneys or through CYP enzymes.", "sidefx": "As with other enzyme replacements, the main risks are immune reactions to a foreign-looking protein. Infusion reactions such as headache, rash, itching, nausea and chills are common, and anaphylaxis can occur, which is part of its boxed warning. People with weak heart or breathing muscles may not tolerate the fluid load of an infusion and can develop acute heart and lung failure. Antibodies can reduce effect in some patients. Reactions are managed by slowing or stopping the infusion. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The protein backbone is the same as the older enzyme. The improvement comes entirely from the sugar tags added to it, a strategy called glycoengineering.", "src": ["Nexviazyme (avalglucosidase alfa) FDA label", "Diaz-Manera J et al. Lancet Neurol 2021 (COMET)"]}, "migalastat": {"origin": "Migalastat is 1-deoxygalactonojirimycin, an iminosugar that mimics galactose. Iminosugars were first studied as enzyme inhibitors, but in 1999 Jian-Qiang Fan and colleagues reported a surprising finding in Nature Medicine: at low concentrations, this galactose mimic actually increased the activity of some mutant forms of alpha-galactosidase A in Fabry cells. It worked as an active-site-specific chaperone, helping the misfolded enzyme pass quality control. Amicus Therapeutics developed the idea into a pill. Migalastat was approved in Europe in 2016, and the FDA approved it as Galafold in 2018 for adults with Fabry disease who have an amenable GLA variant, the first oral Fabry treatment.", "timeline": [["1999", "Fan and colleagues show that a galactose analog can rescue mutant alpha-galactosidase A as a chaperone."], ["2000s", "Amicus Therapeutics develops migalastat as an oral pharmacologic chaperone."], ["2016", "Migalastat is approved in the European Union."], ["2018", "FDA approves migalastat (Galafold) for amenable Fabry disease."]], "cell": "Many Fabry disease variants change a single amino acid in alpha-galactosidase A. The enzyme could still work but misfolds, so the endoplasmic reticulum's quality control system sends it for destruction instead of to the lysosome. Migalastat is a galactose look-alike that binds reversibly in the enzyme's active site in the endoplasmic reticulum, where the pH is neutral and substrate is absent. This binding stabilizes the correct fold, so the enzyme passes quality control and travels through the Golgi to the lysosome. In the lysosome, the acidic pH and high concentration of the natural substrate Gb3 (GL-3) push migalastat off the active site, freeing the enzyme to break down Gb3. It only works for amenable variants; it cannot help when the enzyme is absent or its active site is broken.", "body": [["Kidneys", "More working enzyme reduces Gb3 deposits in kidney capillaries, and in trials kidney function stayed relatively stable."], ["Heart", "Left ventricular mass decreased in some studies, consistent with less storage in heart muscle."], ["Blood vessels", "Gb3 clearance from vessel lining cells is thought to slow small vessel damage throughout the body."], ["Nervous system", "Headache is one of the most common side effects reported."], ["Urinary and respiratory tract", "Urinary tract infections, nasal symptoms and fever were reported more often in trials."]], "journey": "Migalastat is taken by mouth every other day, on an empty stomach because food lowers absorption. It peaks about three hours after a dose and is not bound to blood proteins. It reaches tissues throughout the body because it is a small molecule. It is barely metabolized and is cleared mostly unchanged by the kidneys, with a half-life of about four hours. Every-other-day dosing leaves time between doses for the drug to clear from lysosomes so the enzyme is free to work.", "sidefx": "Migalastat is generally well tolerated because it binds only to its target enzyme and is not a foreign protein, so infusion reactions do not occur. Headache, nasopharyngitis, urinary tract infection, nausea and fever were reported. Because it is cleared by the kidneys, it is not used in severe kidney impairment. Taking it more often than every other day could keep the enzyme's active site occupied and reduce its activity. There is no antidote.", "fun": "A drug that blocks an enzyme can also save it: at the right concentration and timing, migalastat helps its own target enzyme survive and then lets go once it reaches the lysosome.", "src": ["Galafold (migalastat) FDA label", "Fan JQ et al. Nat Med 1999", "Germain DP et al. N Engl J Med 2016"]}, "eliglustat": {"origin": "Eliglustat came from decades of work at the University of Michigan. In the 1980s biochemist Norman Radin built ceramide-like molecules that blocked glucosylceramide synthase, the enzyme that makes the lipid stored in Gaucher disease. Later, James Shayman and colleagues refined these into more potent and selective compounds. Genzyme licensed the work and developed eliglustat as a pill for substrate reduction therapy, an alternative to IV enzyme replacement. The FDA approved it as Cerdelga in 2014 for adults with type 1 Gaucher disease. Its label requires CYP2D6 metabolizer status to be known before use, an early example of pharmacogenomics built into prescribing.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Norman Radin develops ceramide analog inhibitors of glucosylceramide synthase."], ["2000s", "James Shayman's group refines the compounds, leading to eliglustat."], ["2014", "FDA approves eliglustat (Cerdelga) for type 1 Gaucher disease."]], "cell": "In Gaucher disease, low glucocerebrosidase lets glucosylceramide build up in macrophage lysosomes. Enzyme replacement adds more enzyme; substrate reduction takes the opposite approach and makes less lipid. Glucosylceramide synthase (UDP-glucose ceramide glucosyltransferase) on the Golgi adds glucose to ceramide, the first committed step in making most glycosphingolipids. Eliglustat is a ceramide analog that potently and specifically inhibits this enzyme. With less glucosylceramide being made, the patient's leftover glucocerebrosidase activity can keep up with breakdown, so storage in Gaucher cells decreases. It does not meaningfully cross the blood-brain barrier, so it is not used for neurologic forms of Gaucher disease.", "body": [["Spleen", "Less storage in Gaucher cells shrinks the enlarged spleen over months."], ["Liver", "Liver volume falls as macrophage storage decreases."], ["Blood", "Hemoglobin and platelet counts rise as the spleen shrinks and marrow is less crowded."], ["Bones", "Bone marrow infiltration and bone disease improve over the long term."], ["Heart", "At high blood levels it can prolong PR, QRS and QTc intervals by blocking cardiac ion channels, which is why genotype and drug interactions matter."], ["Gut", "Indigestion, nausea, diarrhea and upper abdominal pain are common."]], "journey": "Eliglustat is taken by mouth as a capsule, once or twice daily depending on CYP2D6 status. It is absorbed quickly, peaking in about two hours in extensive metabolizers, though first-pass metabolism keeps bioavailability low. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP2D6, with CYP3A4 as a backup path. Poor metabolizers have much higher levels and a longer half-life. Ultrarapid metabolizers may not reach effective levels, so the drug is not used in them. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. Grapefruit and strong CYP2D6 or CYP3A inhibitors raise its levels.", "sidefx": "The main safety concern is the heart. High concentrations block cardiac sodium and potassium channels, prolonging PR, QRS and QTc intervals and raising arrhythmia risk. That is why CYP2D6 genotyping is required, interacting drugs are reviewed, and patients with certain heart conditions or long QT avoid it. Fatigue, headache, nausea, diarrhea, back pain and indigestion are common. There is no antidote; management of toxicity is supportive with heart monitoring.", "fun": "Eliglustat is one of the few drugs whose dosing schedule depends directly on a genetic test result: CYP2D6 metabolizer status decides whether it is taken once or twice a day, or not at all.", "src": ["Cerdelga (eliglustat) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Mistry PK et al. JAMA 2015 (ENGAGE)"]}, "miglustat": {"origin": "Miglustat is N-butyl-deoxynojirimycin, a synthetic relative of deoxynojirimycin, a glucose-like iminosugar found in mulberry leaves and some bacteria. It was first developed in the late 1980s as a possible HIV drug because it blocks enzymes that trim sugars on viral proteins, but it did not work well enough for that use. In 1994 Frances Platt, Terry Butters and Raymond Dwek at the Oxford Glycobiology Institute showed it also blocks glucosylceramide synthase, and they proposed using it to make less of the lipid stored in Gaucher disease, an idea called substrate reduction therapy. Actelion brought it to market, and the FDA approved it as Zavesca in 2003 for adults with mild to moderate type 1 Gaucher disease who cannot use enzyme replacement.", "timeline": [["1980s", "N-butyl-deoxynojirimycin is studied as an HIV drug."], ["1994", "Platt, Butters and Dwek show it inhibits glucosylceramide synthase."], ["2003", "FDA approves miglustat (Zavesca) for type 1 Gaucher disease."], ["2023", "A separate miglustat product (Opfolda) is approved for use with cipaglucosidase alfa in late-onset Pompe disease."]], "cell": "Miglustat is an N-alkylated iminosugar, a glucose mimic. It competitively and reversibly inhibits glucosylceramide synthase, the Golgi enzyme that attaches glucose to ceramide in the first step of making most glycosphingolipids. Less glucosylceramide production lowers the load on deficient glucocerebrosidase in Gaucher disease, so storage in macrophages slowly falls. It is less potent and less specific than eliglustat. Because it resembles glucose, it also inhibits intestinal disaccharidases such as sucrase-isomaltase, which normally split dietary sugars. Undigested sugars stay in the gut, causing osmotic diarrhea and gas. Unlike eliglustat, it crosses the blood-brain barrier.", "body": [["Spleen and liver", "Less glucosylceramide storage gives modest reductions in spleen and liver size."], ["Blood", "Hemoglobin and platelet counts improve somewhat."], ["Gut", "Blocking intestinal sugar-splitting enzymes causes diarrhea, gas, bloating and abdominal pain, especially with carbohydrate-rich meals."], ["Weight", "Weight loss is common, largely from carbohydrate malabsorption and GI symptoms."], ["Nerves and brain", "It crosses into the brain; tremor and peripheral neuropathy have been reported and are monitored."]], "journey": "Miglustat is taken by mouth as a capsule three times a day. It is well absorbed, peaking in about two to two and a half hours, and does not bind plasma proteins. It distributes widely, including into the brain. It is barely metabolized and leaves mostly unchanged in the urine, with a half-life of about six to seven hours, so kidney function affects levels.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from its sugar-like structure. Inhibiting intestinal disaccharidases leaves sugars undigested, so bacteria ferment them and they draw water into the bowel, causing diarrhea, gas and pain. A low-carbohydrate diet and antidiarrheals can help. Weight loss often follows. Tremor and peripheral neuropathy are reported and may relate to its effects in nerve tissue. Low platelets and visual disturbances can occur. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Miglustat began as an HIV drug candidate, failed in that role, and then found a new life treating a lipid storage disease.", "src": ["Zavesca (miglustat) FDA label", "Platt FM et al. J Biol Chem 1994", "Cox T et al. Lancet 2000"]}, "laronidase": {"origin": "Hurler syndrome, the severe form of mucopolysaccharidosis type I (MPS I), was described in 1919 by German pediatrician Gertrud Hurler. Milder forms are named for Harold Scheie. In 1968 Elizabeth Neufeld and colleagues at the NIH showed that cells from patients with different MPS disorders could correct each other's storage when grown together, because cells release and take up lysosomal enzymes. This cross-correction idea became the basis for enzyme replacement. Emil Kakkis, working at Harbor-UCLA, produced recombinant human alpha-L-iduronidase and tested it in dogs and then patients. BioMarin and Genzyme developed it together, and the FDA approved it as Aldurazyme in 2003, the first treatment for MPS I other than stem cell transplant.", "timeline": [["1919", "Gertrud Hurler describes the severe form of MPS I."], ["1968", "Neufeld and colleagues demonstrate cross-correction of MPS cells."], ["2001", "Kakkis and colleagues report the first enzyme replacement trial for MPS I in the New England Journal of Medicine."], ["2003", "FDA approves laronidase (Aldurazyme)."]], "cell": "MPS I comes from variants in IDUA, which encodes alpha-L-iduronidase. This lysosomal enzyme removes terminal alpha-L-iduronic acid from two glycosaminoglycans (GAGs), dermatan sulfate and heparan sulfate. Without it, partly broken-down GAGs pile up in lysosomes in nearly every tissue, thickening connective tissue, heart valves, airways and the cornea, and damaging the brain in severe forms. Laronidase is recombinant human alpha-L-iduronidase bearing mannose-6-phosphate. Cells take it up through mannose-6-phosphate receptors and route it to lysosomes, where it removes iduronic acid residues so the GAG chains can be fully broken down. It does not cross the blood-brain barrier in meaningful amounts, so it does not treat the brain disease of severe Hurler syndrome.", "body": [["Liver and spleen", "GAG storage in the liver and spleen decreases, so these organs shrink."], ["Lungs and airway", "Forced vital capacity improves as GAG storage in airway and chest tissues declines; airway disease also raises infusion reaction risk."], ["Joints and muscles", "Walking distance improves, though stiff joints and bone changes respond only partly."], ["Urine", "Urinary GAG levels fall within weeks, used as a measure of biochemical response."], ["Brain", "Because the enzyme does not cross the blood-brain barrier, severe Hurler syndrome usually needs stem cell transplant early in life to protect the brain."]], "journey": "Laronidase is given by IV infusion once a week over several hours, with the rate increased in steps. Cells pull it from the blood quickly, so its plasma half-life is only a few hours. Inside lysosomes it keeps working through the week. It is broken down by normal protein catabolism and is not cleared intact by the kidneys or through CYP enzymes.", "sidefx": "Most patients form antibodies against the enzyme. Infusion reactions such as rash, flushing, fever, chills and headache are common. Patients with MPS I often have narrowed airways and reduced lung function, which makes reactions such as bronchospasm and low oxygen more dangerous. Anaphylaxis can occur and is part of the boxed warning, so infusions are given where emergency care is available. Reactions are managed by slowing or stopping the infusion and treating symptoms. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The first proof that enzyme replacement could work for MPS came from mixing cells from two different patients in a dish: each one supplied the enzyme the other was missing.", "src": ["Aldurazyme (laronidase) FDA label", "Fratantoni JC, Hall CW, Neufeld EF. Science 1968", "Kakkis ED et al. N Engl J Med 2001"]}, "idursulfase": {"origin": "Hunter syndrome, mucopolysaccharidosis type II, was described in 1917 by Charles Hunter, a Canadian physician, who reported two brothers with coarse features and stiff joints. It is X-linked, so it affects mostly boys. The missing enzyme, iduronate-2-sulfatase, was identified in the 1970s. Transkaryotic Therapies developed idursulfase using a human cell line, switching on the body's own enzyme gene so the cells made human enzyme with human sugar patterns. Shire acquired the company, and the FDA approved idursulfase as Elaprase in 2006, the first treatment for Hunter syndrome. It improves walking but does not treat the brain disease many patients have.", "timeline": [["1917", "Charles Hunter describes the syndrome in two brothers."], ["1970s", "Iduronate-2-sulfatase deficiency is identified as the cause."], ["2006", "FDA approves idursulfase (Elaprase)."]], "cell": "In Hunter syndrome, variants in the X-linked IDS gene reduce iduronate-2-sulfatase. This lysosomal enzyme removes a sulfate group from the 2-position of iduronic acid at the end of dermatan sulfate and heparan sulfate chains. Without it, the next enzymes in the breakdown pathway cannot act, so partly digested GAGs build up in lysosomes. Idursulfase is recombinant human iduronate-2-sulfatase carrying mannose-6-phosphate. It binds mannose-6-phosphate receptors on cells, is taken into lysosomes and removes the 2-O-sulfate, allowing GAG breakdown to continue. It does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so it does not prevent the neurocognitive decline seen in the severe form.", "body": [["Liver and spleen", "Reduced GAG storage shrinks the enlarged liver and spleen."], ["Muscles and joints", "Six-minute walk distance improved in trials, reflecting better mobility."], ["Lungs and airway", "Airway narrowing from GAG storage increases the risk of breathing problems during infusion reactions."], ["Urine", "Urinary GAG levels fall within weeks of starting therapy."], ["Brain", "The enzyme does not reach the brain, so cognitive decline in severe Hunter syndrome is not prevented."]], "journey": "Idursulfase is given by IV infusion once a week, usually over about three hours. Cells take it from the blood quickly, so its plasma half-life is under an hour. Inside lysosomes it keeps working through the week. It is broken down like other proteins and is not cleared intact by the kidneys or through CYP enzymes.", "sidefx": "Infusion reactions such as headache, fever, rash, itching, hives and blood pressure rise are common, reflecting immune responses to the protein. Anaphylaxis can happen and is part of the boxed warning, and some reactions are biphasic, returning up to 24 hours after the infusion, so patients are watched after treatment. Airway disease and reduced lung function in Hunter syndrome make bronchospasm and low oxygen more dangerous. Antibodies can form and may reduce effect. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Idursulfase is made in human cells rather than hamster cells, so its sugar chains look more like those on the body's own enzyme.", "src": ["Elaprase (idursulfase) FDA label", "Muenzer J et al. Genet Med 2006"]}, "elosulfase-alfa": {"origin": "Morquio syndrome was described in 1929 independently by Luis Morquio, a pediatrician in Uruguay, and James Brailsford, a radiologist in England. It causes severe short stature, skeletal deformities and loose joints, but intelligence is usually normal. The type A form, MPS IVA, results from missing N-acetylgalactosamine-6-sulfatase (GALNS). BioMarin Pharmaceutical developed recombinant human GALNS made in Chinese hamster ovary cells. The FDA approved it as Vimizim in 2014, the first treatment for Morquio A syndrome.", "timeline": [["1929", "Luis Morquio and James Brailsford separately describe the syndrome."], ["1970s", "GALNS deficiency is identified as the cause of MPS IVA."], ["2014", "FDA approves elosulfase alfa (Vimizim)."]], "cell": "MPS IVA comes from variants in GALNS. This lysosomal enzyme removes 6-sulfate groups from N-acetylgalactosamine-6-sulfate and galactose-6-sulfate residues in two GAGs: keratan sulfate and chondroitin-6-sulfate. Without it, these GAGs build up, especially in cartilage, bone and growth plates, where keratan sulfate is abundant. Elosulfase alfa is recombinant human GALNS with mannose-6-phosphate sugars. Cells take it up through mannose-6-phosphate receptors and deliver it to lysosomes, where it removes the 6-sulfates so the GAG chains can be broken down. Cartilage has few blood vessels, which limits how much enzyme reaches growth plate cells, so established skeletal changes improve little.", "body": [["Bones and cartilage", "Keratan sulfate storage in cartilage drives skeletal dysplasia; therapy lowers storage but changes established bone shape little."], ["Muscles and endurance", "Six-minute walk distance improved modestly in the pivotal trial."], ["Urine", "Urinary keratan sulfate falls within weeks, a marker of enzyme activity."], ["Airway and lungs", "Narrowed airways and a small chest make breathing problems during infusion reactions more dangerous."], ["Spine", "Instability of the upper neck vertebrae is a serious feature of the disease and is monitored separately."]], "journey": "Elosulfase alfa is given by IV infusion once a week over about four hours or longer. It leaves the blood quickly as cells take it up, with a plasma half-life well under an hour after a single dose. In lysosomes it continues to work. It is broken down like other proteins and is not cleared through CYP enzymes or intact by the kidneys.", "sidefx": "Infusion reactions such as fever, vomiting, headache, nausea, abdominal pain, chills and fatigue are common. Anaphylaxis can occur and is part of the boxed warning. Patients with Morquio A often have small airways and restrictive lung disease, so bronchospasm or low oxygen during a reaction is especially risky, and patients with acute illness may have infusions delayed. Antibodies form in nearly all patients. Reactions are handled by slowing or stopping the infusion. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Unlike many other MPS disorders, Morquio A usually leaves intelligence unaffected, because keratan sulfate is stored mainly in cartilage rather than the brain.", "src": ["Vimizim (elosulfase alfa) FDA label", "Hendriksz CJ et al. J Inherit Metab Dis 2014"]}, "galsulfase": {"origin": "Maroteaux-Lamy syndrome, mucopolysaccharidosis type VI, was described in 1963 by French physicians Pierre Maroteaux and Maurice Lamy. Children have short stature, stiff joints, heart valve disease and clouded corneas, but usually normal intelligence. The missing enzyme is N-acetylgalactosamine-4-sulfatase, also called arylsulfatase B. BioMarin Pharmaceutical developed recombinant human arylsulfatase B in Chinese hamster ovary cells. The FDA approved it as Naglazyme in 2005, the first treatment for MPS VI.", "timeline": [["1963", "Pierre Maroteaux and Maurice Lamy describe MPS VI."], ["1970s", "Arylsulfatase B deficiency is identified as the cause."], ["2005", "FDA approves galsulfase (Naglazyme)."]], "cell": "In MPS VI, variants in ARSB reduce N-acetylgalactosamine-4-sulfatase (arylsulfatase B). This lysosomal enzyme removes the 4-sulfate from N-acetylgalactosamine residues at the end of dermatan sulfate and chondroitin-4-sulfate. Without it, dermatan sulfate builds up in connective tissue, heart valves, cornea, bones and joints. Galsulfase is recombinant human arylsulfatase B with mannose-6-phosphate sugars. Cells take it up through mannose-6-phosphate receptors and send it to lysosomes, where it removes the 4-sulfate so the GAG can be broken down further. Brain involvement is limited in MPS VI, so the blood-brain barrier is less of a problem than in some other MPS types.", "body": [["Muscles and endurance", "Walking distance and stair-climbing capacity improved in trials."], ["Urine", "Urinary GAG levels fall within weeks of starting therapy."], ["Heart", "Dermatan sulfate thickens heart valves; enzyme replacement does not easily reverse established valve disease."], ["Airway and lungs", "Narrow airways and sleep apnea raise the risk of breathing problems during infusion reactions."], ["Eyes and joints", "Corneal clouding and joint stiffness are common disease features and respond slowly, if at all."]], "journey": "Galsulfase is given by IV infusion once a week over at least four hours. Cells take it up from the blood very quickly, so its plasma half-life is minutes. In lysosomes it keeps working through the week. It is broken down like other proteins and is not cleared intact by the kidneys or through CYP enzymes.", "sidefx": "Infusion reactions such as rash, itching, hives, fever, chills, headache, nausea and abdominal pain are common because the immune system reacts to the recombinant protein. Anaphylaxis is possible. Patients with MPS VI may have airway narrowing and sleep apnea, so apnea and breathing difficulty during an infusion are a concern, and infusions are given where emergency care is available. Fluid volume can stress weakened hearts and lungs. Reactions are handled by slowing or stopping the infusion. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Pierre Maroteaux, one of the physicians who described MPS VI, went on to describe many other skeletal dysplasias and is widely regarded as a pioneer of that field.", "src": ["Naglazyme (galsulfase) FDA label", "Harmatz P et al. J Pediatr 2006"]}, "sebelipase-alfa": {"origin": "Lysosomal acid lipase deficiency includes Wolman disease, described in 1956 by Israeli neuropathologist Moshe Wolman, in which infants develop huge livers and spleens, calcified adrenal glands and fatal malabsorption, usually within months. A milder later-onset form is called cholesteryl ester storage disease. Synageva BioPharma developed recombinant human lysosomal acid lipase in an unusual way: it was produced in the egg whites of genetically engineered chickens. Alexion acquired Synageva in 2015, and the FDA approved sebelipase alfa as Kanuma that same year, the first treatment for this disease. The FDA approved the engineered chicken as well, a first for an animal used to make a human drug in eggs.", "timeline": [["1956", "Moshe Wolman describes the infant form of LAL deficiency."], ["2015", "FDA approves sebelipase alfa (Kanuma), produced in transgenic chicken egg whites."]], "cell": "Lysosomal acid lipase (LAL) breaks down cholesteryl esters and triglycerides that enter cells through LDL receptor uptake. It splits them into free cholesterol, glycerol and free fatty acids in the lysosome. Free cholesterol then leaves the lysosome and signals the cell to make less cholesterol and fewer LDL receptors. In LAL deficiency, variants in LIPA block this step. Lipids pile up in liver cells and macrophages, the liver becomes fatty and scarred, and because cells sense too little free cholesterol, they keep making more and take up more LDL, raising blood LDL. Sebelipase alfa is recombinant human LAL. It is taken into cells through mannose-6-phosphate and mannose receptors and delivered to lysosomes, where it hydrolyzes the stored lipids and restores feedback control.", "body": [["Liver", "Lysosomal lipid storage decreases, liver enzymes such as ALT fall and liver fat declines, which may slow fibrosis."], ["Blood lipids", "LDL cholesterol and triglycerides fall and HDL rises as normal cholesterol feedback returns."], ["Spleen", "Splenic enlargement from lipid-filled macrophages decreases."], ["Gut and growth", "In infants, lipid-laden macrophages in the gut wall cause malabsorption; therapy improves weight gain and growth."], ["Survival", "Infants with rapidly progressive disease survived longer with therapy, a disease previously fatal in the first year."]], "journey": "Sebelipase alfa is given by IV infusion, every other week for later-onset disease and more often in infants. It disappears from the blood within minutes because liver cells and macrophages take it up through mannose and mannose-6-phosphate receptors. Inside lysosomes it continues to break down lipid. It is broken down like other proteins and is not cleared through CYP enzymes or intact by the kidneys.", "sidefx": "Infusion reactions such as fever, chills, rash, itching and breathing symptoms are common, and anaphylaxis can occur. Because it is produced in chicken egg whites, people with egg allergies may be at higher risk of hypersensitivity. Antibodies, including neutralizing antibodies, can form. Diarrhea, vomiting, fever and cough were common in infant trials, partly reflecting the illness. Reactions are handled by slowing or stopping the infusion and treating symptoms. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Kanuma's enzyme comes from hens whose DNA was modified so their eggs contain the human enzyme, which is then purified from the egg white.", "src": ["Kanuma (sebelipase alfa) FDA label", "Burton BK et al. N Engl J Med 2015", "Abramov A, Schorr S, Wolman M. AMA J Dis Child 1956"]}, "vosoritide": {"origin": "Achondroplasia is the most common form of short-limbed dwarfism. In 1994 two research groups found the cause: a gain-of-function variant (G380R) in the FGFR3 gene, which overactivates a brake on growth plate cells. Researchers in Japan and elsewhere then showed that C-type natriuretic peptide (CNP), a natural growth plate signal, could counter that brake, and mice that overmade CNP grew longer bones. Natural CNP lasts only minutes in the blood, so BioMarin Pharmaceutical designed a 39-amino-acid analog that resists breakdown by neutral endopeptidase. The FDA approved it as Voxzogo in 2021 to increase linear growth in children with achondroplasia age 5 and older with open growth plates, and in 2023 extended it to children of all ages with open growth plates.", "timeline": [["1994", "FGFR3 variants are identified as the cause of achondroplasia."], ["2000s", "Studies show CNP signaling can counteract FGFR3 overactivity in growth plates."], ["2021", "FDA approves vosoritide (Voxzogo) for achondroplasia."], ["2023", "Approval is extended to younger children with open growth plates."]], "cell": "In growth plates, chondrocytes multiply in columns and then enlarge before being replaced by bone, which lengthens the long bones. FGFR3 is a receptor tyrosine kinase that slows this process. In achondroplasia, the G380R variant keeps FGFR3 overly active, so its downstream MAPK pathway (RAF-1, MEK, ERK1/2) restrains chondrocyte proliferation and differentiation. Vosoritide is a modified C-type natriuretic peptide that binds natriuretic peptide receptor B (NPR-B), a guanylyl cyclase receptor on chondrocytes. Binding raises intracellular cGMP, which activates protein kinase G. PKG inhibits RAF-1, damping the overactive MAPK signal from FGFR3. Released from that brake, chondrocytes multiply and mature, promoting endochondral bone growth. It only works while growth plates are open.", "body": [["Growth plates", "Faster chondrocyte proliferation increases annual growth rate in children, measured as added height per year."], ["Blood vessels", "CNP signaling relaxes vascular smooth muscle, so blood pressure can drop briefly after injection, causing dizziness or fainting."], ["Injection site", "Redness, swelling and hives at the injection site are very common."], ["Gut", "Vomiting is among the reported side effects."], ["Body proportions", "Studies are examining whether longer-term use changes body proportions and other complications of achondroplasia."]], "journey": "Vosoritide is injected under the skin once daily. It is absorbed quickly, peaking about 15 minutes after injection, and is cleared within about half an hour. Neutral endopeptidase and other proteases break it down, though its design slows this compared with natural CNP. Despite the short half-life, a daily pulse of signaling is enough to increase growth. Taking it with food and fluids reduces the drop in blood pressure.", "sidefx": "Because CNP signaling through cGMP also relaxes blood vessels, a temporary drop in blood pressure can occur after injection, causing dizziness, fatigue or fainting. Eating and drinking beforehand lessens this. Injection site redness, swelling and hives are common. Vomiting can occur. It has no effect once growth plates close, so it is stopped then. There is no antidote; low blood pressure is treated by lying down and fluids.", "fun": "Achondroplasia is usually not inherited from a parent: about 80% of cases come from a new FGFR3 variant, often linked to older paternal age.", "src": ["Voxzogo (vosoritide) FDA label", "Savarirayan R et al. Lancet 2020", "Shiang R et al. Cell 1994"]}, "inotersen": {"origin": "Inotersen comes from antisense technology developed over decades at Ionis Pharmaceuticals (formerly Isis Pharmaceuticals) in California. Antisense oligonucleotides are short strands of modified DNA-like chemistry designed to bind a single messenger RNA and mark it for destruction. Ionis used 2-prime-O-methoxyethyl (2-prime-MOE) chemistry to make the strands stable and strong binders. They targeted transthyretin (TTR) mRNA, since liver-made TTR misfolds into amyloid in hereditary TTR amyloidosis. Its affiliate Akcea Therapeutics took it through the NEURO-TTR trial. The FDA approved it as Tegsedi in October 2018, just weeks after patisiran, making TTR amyloidosis the first disease with two approved gene-silencing drugs.", "timeline": [["1989", "Isis Pharmaceuticals (later Ionis) is founded to develop antisense drugs."], ["2018", "NEURO-TTR trial results are published in the New England Journal of Medicine."], ["2018", "FDA approves inotersen (Tegsedi) for hATTR polyneuropathy."]], "cell": "Inotersen is a 20-nucleotide antisense oligonucleotide built as a gapmer: a central stretch of DNA-like units flanked by 2-prime-MOE-modified wings. It has a phosphorothioate backbone that resists nucleases and binds plasma proteins. After injection it reaches the liver and enters hepatocytes. There it binds by Watson-Crick base pairing to the 3-prime untranslated region of TTR mRNA. The DNA-RNA hybrid in the central gap is recognized by RNase H1, which cuts the RNA strand. Cut mRNA is degraded, so less TTR protein is made. The target sequence is shared by normal and variant TTR, so both are lowered. Serum TTR falls by roughly 70 to 80%, reducing the supply of protein that forms amyloid in nerves.", "body": [["Peripheral nerves", "Lower TTR slows amyloid buildup in nerves, slowing the progression of numbness, weakness and pain."], ["Blood platelets", "Platelet counts can drop suddenly and severely, raising the risk of serious bleeding including in the brain; frequent counts are required."], ["Kidneys", "Glomerulonephritis can develop, sometimes needing immunosuppressive treatment, so kidney function and urine protein are checked."], ["Eyes and vitamin A", "Lower TTR lowers retinol-binding protein and vitamin A, so supplements are given to protect night vision."], ["Brain and vessels", "Stroke and cervicocephalic arterial dissection have been reported."]], "journey": "Inotersen is injected under the skin once a week. It is absorbed within hours and is highly bound to plasma proteins, which slows kidney filtration. It distributes mainly to the liver and kidneys. Endonucleases and exonucleases slowly break it into shorter pieces; it is not a CYP substrate. Pieces leave in the urine. Its tissue half-life is about a month, so TTR lowering builds over weeks.", "sidefx": "Severe thrombocytopenia and glomerulonephritis are the main risks and carry boxed warnings; the drug is available only through a REMS program with regular monitoring of platelets and kidney function. These effects are thought to involve immune mechanisms and the phosphorothioate backbone's tendency to bind proteins, including on platelets. Low vitamin A follows from lower TTR. Injection site reactions, nausea, headache, fatigue and fever are common. There is no antidote; affected patients stop the drug and receive supportive care.", "fun": "Inotersen and patisiran silence the same gene in different ways: inotersen uses a single strand and RNase H1, while patisiran uses a double strand and the RISC machinery.", "src": ["Tegsedi (inotersen) FDA label", "Benson MD et al. N Engl J Med 2018 (NEURO-TTR)"]}, "eplontersen": {"origin": "Eplontersen is the next step after inotersen at Ionis Pharmaceuticals. Ionis kept the antisense approach against transthyretin mRNA but attached a cluster of three N-acetylgalactosamine (GalNAc) sugars. GalNAc binds a receptor found almost only on liver cells, so much more drug reaches its target and far less is needed. This allowed monthly self-injection and avoided the platelet and kidney problems that required close monitoring with inotersen. Ionis developed it with AstraZeneca. Based on the NEURO-TTRansform trial, the FDA approved it as Wainua in December 2023 for the polyneuropathy of hereditary TTR amyloidosis.", "timeline": [["2018", "Inotersen, the first antisense TTR drug, is approved."], ["2010s", "Ionis develops GalNAc-conjugated antisense chemistry for targeted liver delivery."], ["2023", "FDA approves eplontersen (Wainua) for hATTR polyneuropathy."]], "cell": "Eplontersen is a 2-prime-MOE gapmer antisense oligonucleotide joined to a triantennary GalNAc ligand. After injection, GalNAc binds the asialoglycoprotein receptor (ASGPR) on hepatocytes, and the drug is carried into the cell by endocytosis. Once inside, the ligand is cleaved off and the oligonucleotide pairs with the 3-prime untranslated region of TTR mRNA. The DNA-RNA hybrid formed in the gapmer's central stretch is a substrate for RNase H1, which cuts the mRNA so it is degraded. Both variant and wild-type TTR production fall, by about 80% in serum. With less TTR in the blood, fewer unstable tetramers break into monomers that misfold into amyloid in nerves.", "body": [["Peripheral nerves", "Lower TTR slows amyloid deposition in nerves, and in trials the neuropathy stabilized or progressed more slowly."], ["Autonomic nerves", "Reducing amyloid may slow damage to nerves controlling blood pressure, gut and bladder."], ["Liver", "The liver is where it works, through GalNAc-targeted uptake into hepatocytes."], ["Eyes and vitamin A", "TTR carries retinol-binding protein, so serum vitamin A falls; supplements protect against night blindness and dry eye."], ["Gut", "Vomiting was reported more often than with placebo."]], "journey": "Eplontersen is self-injected under the skin once a month with an autoinjector. GalNAc targeting delivers it rapidly to the liver; plasma levels peak within hours and then fall as liver cells take it up. It is more than 98% protein bound in plasma. Nucleases break it into shorter oligonucleotides, and it is not a CYP substrate. Fragments leave mainly in the urine. Its terminal half-life is about three weeks, which supports monthly dosing.", "sidefx": "Eplontersen's main expected effect is low vitamin A, because lowering TTR also lowers its partner, retinol-binding protein. Without supplements, night blindness and dry eyes can result. Vomiting and injection site reactions occur. Because GalNAc delivery lets much less drug reach other tissues, the severe platelet drops and glomerulonephritis seen with inotersen were not seen in trials, and the label does not carry those boxed warnings. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Eplontersen and inotersen work the same way inside the liver cell. The key difference is the GalNAc sugar tag that steers eplontersen straight to the liver, so far less drug reaches other organs.", "src": ["Wainua (eplontersen) FDA label", "Coelho T et al. JAMA 2023 (NEURO-TTRansform)"]}, "acoramidis": {"origin": "Acoramidis was designed at Stanford University in the 2010s by Isabella Graef and Mamoun Alhamadsheh, who wanted a drug that would copy a protective gene variant found in nature. Some people with a dangerous transthyretin (TTR) variant are protected from amyloid disease because they also carry a second variant, T119M, which locks the TTR tetramer together. The Stanford team built a small molecule, first called AG10, that binds TTR's thyroxine pockets and forms hydrogen bonds like those that stabilize T119M. Eidos Therapeutics, later part of BridgeBio Pharma, developed it. After the ATTRibute-CM trial, the FDA approved it as Attruby in November 2024 for ATTR cardiomyopathy, the second TTR stabilizer approved for the heart after tafamidis.", "timeline": [["2010s", "Graef and Alhamadsheh at Stanford design AG10 to mimic the protective T119M TTR variant."], ["2023", "ATTRibute-CM trial reports benefit in ATTR cardiomyopathy."], ["2024", "FDA approves acoramidis (Attruby) for ATTR cardiomyopathy."]], "cell": "Transthyretin circulates as a tetramer of four identical subunits with two thyroxine-binding pockets at the interface between its two dimers. Amyloid forms when the tetramer first dissociates into monomers, which then misfold and assemble into fibrils that deposit in heart muscle. Acoramidis binds selectively and with high affinity in the thyroxine-binding pockets. Inside the pocket it forms hydrogen bonds with serine 117 residues on neighboring subunits, mimicking the extra stability of the T119M variant. This kinetic stabilization raises the energy barrier for dissociation, so the tetramer stays intact. At the levels reached in patients, it stabilizes TTR almost completely. Because stabilized TTR is cleared more slowly, serum TTR levels rise, a sign the drug is working. It does not reduce TTR production.", "body": [["Heart", "Less amyloid formation slows heart muscle stiffening and was linked to fewer cardiovascular deaths and hospitalizations in ATTRibute-CM."], ["Blood TTR", "Serum TTR levels rise as stabilized tetramers persist longer in the blood, which is a marker of target engagement."], ["Kidneys", "An early small rise in serum creatinine and fall in eGFR can occur, then levels stabilize; this appears related to kidney handling rather than injury."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and upper abdominal pain were reported more often than with placebo."], ["Thyroid hormone", "Binding the thyroxine pockets of TTR does not cause clinical thyroid problems, because other proteins carry most thyroxine."]], "journey": "Acoramidis is taken by mouth twice daily. It is absorbed quickly, peaking about one hour after a dose, and is about 96% protein bound, largely to TTR itself. It has a half-life of about 27 hours. The liver and other tissues conjugate it with glucuronic acid (UGT enzymes), forming an acyl glucuronide, and it leaves mostly in the urine as that metabolite.", "sidefx": "Acoramidis is generally well tolerated because it simply stabilizes a carrier protein without blocking an enzyme or receptor. Diarrhea and upper abdominal pain occur. An early rise in serum creatinine with a small drop in eGFR is seen after starting, and it usually stabilizes; it is thought to reflect a change in how the kidneys handle creatinine and filtration rather than true kidney damage. There is no antidote, and none is generally needed.", "fun": "Acoramidis is a rare example of a drug designed to copy a protective human gene variant, letting patients borrow the stability that T119M carriers are born with.", "src": ["Attruby (acoramidis) FDA label", "Gillmore JD et al. N Engl J Med 2024 (ATTRibute-CM)", "Penchala SC et al. PNAS 2013"]}, "pegvaliase": {"origin": "Pegvaliase uses an enzyme borrowed from bacteria. Phenylalanine ammonia lyase (PAL) is found in plants, fungi and some bacteria, where it breaks down phenylalanine without needing a cofactor. Researchers had long proposed PAL as a way to treat phenylketonuria (PKU) independent of the patient's own broken enzyme. The challenge was that the body sees PAL as foreign and destroys it. BioMarin Pharmaceutical used PAL from the cyanobacterium Anabaena variabilis and coated it with polyethylene glycol (PEG) to hide it from the immune system and keep it in the blood longer. The FDA approved it as Palynziq in 2018 for adults with PKU whose blood phenylalanine stays too high on existing treatment, with a boxed warning for anaphylaxis.", "timeline": [["1934", "Asbjorn Folling describes phenylketonuria."], ["1980s", "Researchers propose phenylalanine ammonia lyase as an enzyme substitution therapy for PKU."], ["2018", "FDA approves pegvaliase (Palynziq) for adults with PKU."]], "cell": "In PKU, phenylalanine hydroxylase (PAH) in the liver does not work well, so phenylalanine builds up in blood and brain. Pegvaliase supplies a different enzyme that does not need PAH or its cofactor BH4. Phenylalanine ammonia lyase removes the amino group from phenylalanine, producing trans-cinnamic acid and a small amount of ammonia. Trans-cinnamic acid is converted in the liver to hippuric acid, which is excreted in the urine. Because it bypasses PAH entirely, pegvaliase works regardless of the patient's PAH genotype. PEGylation, the attachment of many polyethylene glycol chains, shields the bacterial protein from immune recognition and kidney filtration, extending how long it circulates and reducing, though not eliminating, antibody formation.", "body": [["Brain", "Lower blood phenylalanine reduces its entry into the brain, which may improve attention, mood and thinking that are affected by high levels."], ["Diet", "Many responders can eat more natural protein because the enzyme breaks down phenylalanine from food."], ["Immune system", "Antibodies to PAL and PEG form in nearly everyone, causing hypersensitivity reactions and anaphylaxis, especially early on."], ["Joints and skin", "Joint pain, itching, rashes and injection site reactions are common, largely from immune complex responses."], ["Blood phenylalanine", "Levels can fall too low (hypophenylalaninemia), so diet and dose are adjusted."]], "journey": "Pegvaliase is self-injected under the skin, starting at a low level and slowly increasing over weeks to months to let the immune system adapt. It is absorbed slowly, peaking several hours to a day after injection. PEGylation keeps it circulating for a long time, with a half-life averaging about two days at maintenance but varying widely between people, partly because antibodies affect clearance. It is broken down like other proteins rather than through the liver CYP system or the kidneys.", "sidefx": "The body treats PAL as a foreign bacterial protein. Antibodies against PAL and PEG lead to injection site reactions, joint pain, rash and itching, and the most serious risk is anaphylaxis, which carries a boxed warning. For this reason the first dose is given under medical supervision, the drug is available only through a REMS program, and patients carry auto-injectable epinephrine. Phenylalanine can drop too low when the drug works well, so dietary protein may need to increase. Epinephrine treats anaphylaxis; there is no specific antidote to the enzyme.", "fun": "Phenylalanine ammonia lyase is the same type of enzyme plants use to start making lignin, flavonoids and other compounds, which is how it was first studied.", "src": ["Palynziq (pegvaliase) FDA label", "Thomas J et al. Mol Genet Metab 2018 (PRISM-2)"]}, "carglumic-acid": {"origin": "N-acetylglutamate synthase (NAGS) deficiency, the rarest urea cycle disorder, was first described in 1981 by C. Bachmann and colleagues. Without NAGS, the liver cannot make N-acetylglutamate, the switch that turns on the first urea cycle enzyme, so ammonia builds up to dangerous levels. Because natural N-acetylglutamate is quickly broken down, researchers turned to its stable synthetic analog, carglumic acid (N-carbamylglutamate). Orphan Europe (later part of Recordati) developed it as Carbaglu, approved in Europe in 2003. The FDA approved it in 2010 for hyperammonemia from NAGS deficiency, and in 2021 for acute hyperammonemia in propionic and methylmalonic acidemia.", "timeline": [["1981", "Bachmann and colleagues describe NAGS deficiency."], ["2003", "Carglumic acid (Carbaglu) is approved in the European Union."], ["2010", "FDA approves carglumic acid for NAGS deficiency."], ["2021", "FDA approves carglumic acid for acute hyperammonemia in propionic and methylmalonic acidemia."]], "cell": "The urea cycle in liver mitochondria turns toxic ammonia into urea, which the kidneys excrete. The first step is carbamoyl phosphate synthetase 1 (CPS1), which joins ammonia and bicarbonate. CPS1 is essentially inactive unless N-acetylglutamate (NAG) binds to it as an obligatory allosteric activator. NAG is made by NAGS from glutamate and acetyl-CoA. In NAGS deficiency, NAG is missing, so CPS1 stays off and ammonia builds up. Carglumic acid is a structural analog of NAG that binds CPS1 and activates it, restoring the urea cycle. In propionic and methylmalonic acidemia, propionyl-CoA and methylmalonyl-CoA inhibit NAGS and reduce NAG production; carglumic acid bypasses that inhibition. It is more resistant to breakdown than natural NAG.", "body": [["Liver", "Turning CPS1 back on restores urea production from ammonia, often normalizing ammonia within about a day in NAGS deficiency."], ["Brain", "Lowering ammonia protects against brain swelling, seizures, coma and long-term brain injury caused by hyperammonemia."], ["Diet", "In NAGS deficiency, many patients can relax protein restriction once ammonia is controlled."], ["Gut", "Vomiting, abdominal pain and diarrhea were reported."], ["Immune and blood", "Infections, fever and anemia were reported in clinical use, though some reflect the underlying disease."]], "journey": "Carglumic acid is given by mouth or nasogastric tube as tablets dispersed in water. Absorption is incomplete, and it peaks about three hours after a dose. It enters the liver, where it acts in mitochondria. Gut bacteria break down part of the dose, much of an oral dose leaves in feces, and absorbed drug is excreted largely unchanged in urine. Its median half-life is about five to six hours.", "sidefx": "Carglumic acid simply replaces a missing activator, so serious side effects are uncommon. Vomiting, abdominal pain, fever, tonsillitis, anemia, diarrhea and headache were reported, many of which may reflect the underlying illness in sick patients. Hypersensitivity is rare. In organic acidemias it is an add-on to other ammonia-lowering treatments, not a replacement for them. There is no antidote.", "fun": "In NAGS deficiency, carglumic acid can work so well that it acts almost like a cure for the urea cycle problem, as long as patients keep taking it.", "src": ["Carbaglu (carglumic acid) FDA label", "Bachmann C et al. N Engl J Med 1981", "Haberle J et al. Orphanet J Rare Dis 2019"]}, "sodium-phenylbutyrate": {"origin": "Sodium phenylbutyrate came from the work of Saul Brusilow and colleagues at Johns Hopkins University, who in the late 1970s and 1980s pioneered alternative pathway therapy for urea cycle disorders. Their insight was that if the urea cycle cannot dispose of nitrogen, other compounds can bind nitrogen-carrying amino acids so they leave in the urine instead. Sodium benzoate traps glycine, and phenylacetate traps glutamine. Phenylacetate had a strong, unpleasant smell, so they used phenylbutyrate, an odorless prodrug the body converts to phenylacetate. The FDA approved sodium phenylbutyrate as Buphenyl in 1996 for chronic management of urea cycle disorders.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Saul Brusilow at Johns Hopkins develops alternative pathway nitrogen scavenging."], ["1996", "FDA approves sodium phenylbutyrate (Buphenyl) for urea cycle disorders."], ["2013", "Glycerol phenylbutyrate is approved as a lower-sodium alternative."], ["2022", "A combination with taurursodiol is approved for ALS, then withdrawn in 2024 after a confirmatory trial failed."]], "cell": "Normally the urea cycle packs waste nitrogen into urea. In urea cycle disorders, an enzyme defect blocks this, so ammonia and glutamine (a major nitrogen carrier) build up. Sodium phenylbutyrate is a prodrug. In the liver and other tissues it undergoes beta-oxidation to phenylacetate. Phenylacetate is activated to phenylacetyl-CoA and then joined with glutamine by acetylation to form phenylacetylglutamine. Each phenylacetylglutamine molecule carries two nitrogen atoms, the same as urea, and the kidneys excrete it readily. This removes nitrogen through a route that bypasses the defective urea cycle, lowering glutamine and ammonia. It works best as ongoing therapy alongside protein restriction and essential amino acid supplements.", "body": [["Brain", "Keeping ammonia low protects against brain swelling, confusion, seizures and coma from hyperammonemia."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys excrete phenylacetylglutamine, the nitrogen-carrying waste product."], ["Fluid and electrolytes", "Each dose carries a large amount of sodium, which can cause swelling and worsen heart failure or high blood pressure; low potassium can occur."], ["Reproductive system", "Menstrual changes and amenorrhea are common in women."], ["Taste and smell", "Bad taste, body odor and decreased appetite are common, partly from phenylacetate."], ["Nervous system", "Very high phenylacetate levels cause sleepiness, confusion, headache and vomiting."]], "journey": "Sodium phenylbutyrate is taken by mouth with each meal as tablets or powder. It is absorbed quickly, peaking in about an hour. It is converted by beta-oxidation to phenylacetate, which is conjugated with glutamine in the liver and kidneys. Phenylbutyrate and phenylacetate both have short half-lives, roughly one hour each. Phenylacetylglutamine is excreted in urine. Because the drug is cleared quickly, it is taken in divided doses with meals and feedings.", "sidefx": "The sodium load explains fluid retention, edema and worsening of high blood pressure or heart failure. Losing phenylacetylglutamine in urine can cause potassium loss. Accumulated phenylacetate is itself neurotoxic, causing sleepiness, confusion and vomiting, which can be hard to tell from hyperammonemia. Menstrual dysfunction, body odor and bad taste are common. Too much nitrogen removal can lower essential amino acids such as branched-chain amino acids. There is no antidote; levels are managed by adjusting dose and monitoring.", "fun": "Phenylacetate itself smells so strongly that patients and caregivers found its strong, unpleasant odor hard to live with, which is why the odorless prodrug phenylbutyrate replaced it for daily use.", "src": ["Buphenyl (sodium phenylbutyrate) FDA label", "Brusilow SW et al. N Engl J Med 1984", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "glycerol-phenylbutyrate": {"origin": "Glycerol phenylbutyrate was developed to improve on sodium phenylbutyrate, which had a bad taste, a high sodium load and many tablets or scoops of powder. Chemists joined three phenylbutyrate molecules to a glycerol backbone, making a triglyceride-like liquid that is nearly tasteless and contains no sodium. Pancreatic lipases in the gut release the phenylbutyrate slowly, giving steadier levels through the day. Hyperion Therapeutics developed it, and the FDA approved it as Ravicti in 2013 for chronic management of urea cycle disorders that are not controlled by diet and amino acid supplements alone. It was later extended to younger children.", "timeline": [["1996", "Sodium phenylbutyrate (Buphenyl) is approved for urea cycle disorders."], ["2013", "FDA approves glycerol phenylbutyrate (Ravicti)."]], "cell": "Glycerol phenylbutyrate is a triglyceride made of three phenylbutyrate chains on a glycerol backbone. In the small intestine, pancreatic lipases cut the chains loose, releasing phenylbutyrate, which is absorbed. Phenylbutyrate then undergoes beta-oxidation to phenylacetate. Phenylacetate is conjugated with glutamine to form phenylacetylglutamine, which carries two nitrogen atoms per molecule and is excreted in urine. This alternative route removes nitrogen without the urea cycle, lowering glutamine and ammonia in patients whose urea cycle enzymes are defective. Because lipase hydrolysis is gradual, phenylbutyrate is released slowly, smoothing ammonia control over 24 hours.", "body": [["Brain", "Steady ammonia control protects against hyperammonemic crises that cause confusion, vomiting, seizures and coma."], ["Gut", "Release depends on pancreatic lipase, so it may work less well in people with pancreatic insufficiency; diarrhea and gas are common."], ["Fluid and electrolytes", "It contains no sodium, which avoids the sodium load of sodium phenylbutyrate."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys excrete phenylacetylglutamine, which carries waste nitrogen out of the body."], ["Nervous system", "High phenylacetate levels can cause headache, sleepiness, confusion and numbness."]], "journey": "Glycerol phenylbutyrate is taken by mouth with food, using an oral syringe, or through a feeding tube. In the gut, pancreatic lipases release phenylbutyrate gradually, so levels rise more slowly and are steadier than with the sodium salt. Phenylbutyrate is beta-oxidized to phenylacetate, which is joined with glutamine, and the resulting phenylacetylglutamine leaves in the urine. Divided doses with meals keep ammonia controlled over the day.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea, gas, nausea and decreased appetite come from the oily liquid and gut effects. Headache and fatigue are common. The most serious concern is phenylacetate neurotoxicity: if phenylacetate builds up, it can cause sleepiness, confusion, dizziness, headache and vomiting, similar to signs of high ammonia, so levels may be checked. Hyperammonemia can still occur during illness or high protein intake, which is the underlying disease rather than the drug. Because it needs lipase to activate, pancreatic insufficiency can reduce its effect. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Turning a salt into a triglyceride removed both the sodium and almost all of the taste, which is part of why many families found it easier to use.", "src": ["Ravicti (glycerol phenylbutyrate) FDA label", "Diaz GA et al. Hepatology 2013"]}, "betaine": {"origin": "Betaine (trimethylglycine) was first identified in the 1800s in sugar beets, Beta vulgaris, which gave it its name. It is found in many foods and is made in the body from choline. In homocystinuria, very high homocysteine damages blood vessels, eyes, bones and the brain. Researchers in the 1980s found that betaine could lower homocysteine by giving it a methyl group through an alternative pathway. The FDA approved anhydrous betaine powder as Cystadane in 1996 for homocystinuria from CBS deficiency, MTHFR deficiency and cobalamin cofactor defects.", "timeline": [["1800s", "Betaine is identified in sugar beets."], ["1962", "Homocystinuria is first described in children in Northern Ireland."], ["1980s", "Betaine is shown to lower homocysteine in homocystinuria."], ["1996", "FDA approves betaine (Cystadane) for homocystinuria."]], "cell": "Homocysteine sits at a crossroads in methionine metabolism. It can be turned back into methionine by remethylation or sent down the transsulfuration path by cystathionine beta-synthase (CBS) to make cystathionine and cysteine. The main remethylation route uses methionine synthase, which needs vitamin B12 and methylfolate from MTHFR. In homocystinuria, one of these steps is defective, so homocysteine builds up. Betaine provides a second remethylation route: in the liver and kidney, the enzyme betaine-homocysteine methyltransferase (BHMT) transfers one of betaine's methyl groups to homocysteine, forming methionine and dimethylglycine. This bypasses the folate and B12-dependent pathway and lowers plasma total homocysteine. In CBS deficiency, methionine can rise because the downstream exit through CBS stays blocked.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Lowering homocysteine reduces its damage to vessel walls and lowers the risk of blood clots and strokes."], ["Eyes", "Homocystinuria causes lens dislocation; better control may help protect the eyes over time."], ["Bones", "High homocysteine interferes with collagen cross-linking; lowering it may help bone health."], ["Brain", "In CBS deficiency, very high methionine on betaine can lead to brain swelling (cerebral edema)."], ["Gut", "Nausea, stomach discomfort and diarrhea are reported."], ["Body odor", "An unpleasant body odor can occur, likely from betaine and its metabolites."]], "journey": "Betaine powder is dissolved in a drink and taken by mouth, usually twice daily. It is absorbed quickly and peaks in one to two hours. It is mostly metabolized by BHMT in the liver and kidney to dimethylglycine, which is further broken down. Little leaves unchanged in urine. Its half-life is about 14 hours. Homocysteine levels fall within days.", "sidefx": "Betaine lowers homocysteine by converting it into methionine. In CBS deficiency, where methionine cannot be cleared downstream, this can push methionine very high. Severe hypermethioninemia has been linked to cerebral edema, which is why methionine levels are monitored. GI upset, nausea and diarrhea are common. Body odor may come from betaine metabolites. There is no antidote; if cerebral edema occurs, betaine is stopped and methionine intake is reduced.", "fun": "Betaine is also a natural osmolyte: kidney cells and many sea creatures use it to protect themselves from very salty surroundings.", "src": ["Cystadane (betaine anhydrous) FDA label", "Wilcken DE et al. N Engl J Med 1983", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "lumasiran": {"origin": "Primary hyperoxaluria type 1 (PH1) is a rare inherited disease in which the liver makes too much oxalate, which forms calcium oxalate stones and crystals that destroy the kidneys. For decades, the only definitive fix was a combined liver and kidney transplant. Alnylam Pharmaceuticals, a pioneer in RNA interference, chose a new target: glycolate oxidase, the liver enzyme that makes glyoxylate, the immediate precursor of oxalate. People who naturally lack glycolate oxidase are generally healthy, suggesting that silencing it would be safe. Using its GalNAc-conjugated siRNA platform, Alnylam developed lumasiran. In November 2020 the FDA approved it as Oxlumo, the first treatment for PH1.", "timeline": [["1998", "Fire and Mello describe RNA interference."], ["2010s", "Alnylam develops GalNAc-conjugated siRNA for targeted liver delivery."], ["2020", "FDA approves lumasiran (Oxlumo), the first drug for PH1."]], "cell": "In PH1, the liver enzyme alanine-glyoxylate aminotransferase (AGT, from the AGXT gene) is missing or misdirected. Normally AGT converts glyoxylate to glycine in peroxisomes. Without it, glyoxylate is converted by lactate dehydrogenase to oxalate, which the body cannot break down. Lumasiran is a double-stranded siRNA conjugated to GalNAc. GalNAc binds the asialoglycoprotein receptor (ASGPR) on hepatocytes, so the drug is taken up into liver cells. The guide strand loads into the RNA-induced silencing complex (RISC), which finds and cuts HAO1 mRNA, the message for glycolate oxidase. With less glycolate oxidase, less glycolate is turned into glyoxylate, so less glyoxylate is available to become oxalate. Glycolate builds up instead, but it is soluble and harmlessly excreted in urine.", "body": [["Kidneys", "Lower oxalate production reduces urinary oxalate, which should mean fewer kidney stones, less nephrocalcinosis and slower loss of kidney function."], ["Blood oxalate", "Plasma oxalate falls, which matters especially in patients with reduced kidney function, where oxalate deposits in bone, heart and other tissues."], ["Liver", "The drug acts in the liver, silencing glycolate oxidase in hepatocytes."], ["Urine glycolate", "Glycolate rises in blood and urine as an expected, harmless result of the upstream block."], ["Injection site", "Injection site reactions such as redness, pain and itching are the most common side effects."]], "journey": "Lumasiran is injected under the skin, monthly at first and then every three months. GalNAc delivers it to the liver within hours, so plasma levels peak about four hours after injection and then fall quickly, with a plasma half-life of about five hours. Inside liver cells it lasts for months, sustaining silencing between doses. Nucleases break it into shorter pieces; it is not a CYP substrate. A small amount leaves intact in the urine.", "sidefx": "Because GalNAc delivery sends nearly all the drug to the liver and glycolate oxidase loss is well tolerated, side effects are few. Injection site reactions are the most common, and abdominal pain has been reported. The rise in glycolate is expected and not toxic, because glycolate is soluble. There is no antidote; the silencing effect fades over months after stopping.", "fun": "The idea of targeting glycolate oxidase came partly from people born without it: they have high glycolate levels but no apparent disease, which made it look like a safe enzyme to switch off.", "src": ["Oxlumo (lumasiran) FDA label", "Garrelfs SF et al. N Engl J Med 2021 (ILLUMINATE-A)"]}, "nedosiran": {"origin": "Nedosiran takes a different route to the same goal as lumasiran in primary hyperoxaluria. Instead of blocking an early step, it targets lactate dehydrogenase A (LDHA) in the liver, which catalyzes the final step that converts glyoxylate to oxalate. Because this step is shared by all three types of primary hyperoxaluria, the target looked broadly useful. Dicerna Pharmaceuticals developed nedosiran using its GalXC RNAi platform, which attaches GalNAc sugars to an RNAi molecule for liver delivery. Novo Nordisk acquired Dicerna in 2021. After the PHYOX2 trial, the FDA approved it as Rivfloza in 2023 for PH1 in children 9 years and older and adults with relatively preserved kidney function, and later extended it to younger children.", "timeline": [["2020", "Lumasiran is approved as the first RNAi drug for PH1."], ["2021", "Novo Nordisk acquires Dicerna Pharmaceuticals."], ["2023", "FDA approves nedosiran (Rivfloza) for PH1."]], "cell": "In primary hyperoxaluria, glyoxylate accumulates in liver cells. Lactate dehydrogenase, especially the LDHA isoform in liver, converts glyoxylate to oxalate, which cannot be broken down and crystallizes with calcium in the kidneys. Nedosiran is a GalNAc-conjugated double-stranded RNAi oligonucleotide. GalNAc binds the asialoglycoprotein receptor (ASGPR) on hepatocytes, carrying it into liver cells. The guide strand loads into RISC, which cleaves LDHA mRNA, so less hepatic LDHA protein is made. With less LDHA, less glyoxylate is converted to oxalate. Silencing is limited mostly to the liver, so lactate dehydrogenase in muscle and other tissues continues to work.", "body": [["Kidneys", "Lower urinary oxalate should reduce calcium oxalate stones and kidney damage."], ["Liver", "LDHA is silenced in hepatocytes, the main source of oxalate in primary hyperoxaluria."], ["Muscle and other tissues", "Because GalNAc delivery is liver-specific, lactate dehydrogenase in muscle and red blood cells is largely unaffected."], ["Injection site", "Injection site reactions are the most common side effect."]], "journey": "Nedosiran is injected under the skin once a month. GalNAc targeting brings it to the liver within hours, so plasma levels peak quickly and fall within hours. Inside hepatocytes it stays active for weeks, sustaining LDHA silencing over the monthly interval. Nucleases break it into shorter pieces, and a small amount leaves intact in urine. It is not a CYP substrate.", "sidefx": "Side effects are few because nearly all the drug goes to the liver. Injection site reactions such as redness, pain and bruising are the most common. Silencing liver LDHA has not caused problems with lactate handling in trials, likely because other tissues and isoforms carry on. There is no antidote; the effect fades gradually after stopping.", "fun": "Nedosiran blocks the last step to oxalate, while lumasiran blocks an earlier step, so two RNAi drugs attack the same disease from different points on the same pathway.", "src": ["Rivfloza (nedosiran) FDA label", "Baum MA et al. Kidney Int 2023 (PHYOX2)"]}, "lonafarnib": {"origin": "Lonafarnib started as a cancer drug. Schering-Plough developed it in the 1990s as a farnesyltransferase inhibitor, hoping to block RAS proteins, which need a farnesyl lipid tag to work. It did not succeed in cancer. In 2003 a team led by Francis Collins at the US National Human Genome Research Institute, working with the Progeria Research Foundation founded by Leslie Gordon and Scott Berns, found the cause of Hutchinson-Gilford progeria syndrome: a variant in LMNA that makes an abnormal, permanently farnesylated protein called progerin. Lab studies showed farnesyltransferase inhibitors could reverse nuclear damage in progeria cells. Clinical trials at Boston Children's Hospital began in 2007. Eiger BioPharmaceuticals brought lonafarnib to approval, and the FDA approved it as Zokinvy in 2020, the first treatment for progeria.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Schering-Plough develops lonafarnib as an anticancer farnesyltransferase inhibitor."], ["2003", "LMNA variant causing progeria is identified by Collins and colleagues."], ["2007", "The first clinical trial of lonafarnib in progeria begins at Boston Children's Hospital."], ["2020", "FDA approves lonafarnib (Zokinvy), the first treatment for progeria."]], "cell": "Lamin A is a structural protein of the nuclear envelope. It is made as prelamin A, which ends in a CAAX box that is farnesylated, then trimmed, and finally cleaved by the enzyme ZMPSTE24 to remove the farnesylated tail. In progeria, a single-base change (c.1824C>T) activates a hidden splice site, deleting 50 amino acids that include the ZMPSTE24 cleavage site. The resulting protein, progerin, stays permanently farnesylated and anchored to the inner nuclear membrane. It distorts the nucleus (blebbing), disrupts DNA repair and gene expression and causes cells to age early, especially in blood vessel walls. Lonafarnib inhibits farnesyltransferase, blocking addition of the farnesyl group. Unfarnesylated progerin binds the nuclear membrane less, so nuclear shape improves and cellular damage is reduced.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Progeria causes rapid arterial stiffening and atherosclerosis; lonafarnib slows vascular stiffening, which is thought to explain improved survival."], ["Survival", "Treated children lived longer on average than untreated children in comparison studies."], ["Gut", "Vomiting, diarrhea, nausea and abdominal pain are very common and can limit treatment."], ["Liver and kidneys", "Liver enzyme rises and kidney toxicity have been reported."], ["Eyes", "Retinal toxicity was seen in animal studies and is monitored."], ["Blood", "Myelosuppression and electrolyte changes can occur."]], "journey": "Lonafarnib is taken by mouth twice daily with meals. It peaks about two to four hours after a dose and is more than 99% protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A4. Lonafarnib is also a strong CYP3A inhibitor, so it raises levels of many other drugs and is affected by CYP3A inhibitors and inducers; grapefruit and Seville oranges are avoided. Its half-life is about four to six hours, and it leaves mostly in feces.", "sidefx": "Farnesyltransferase acts on many proteins besides progerin, which helps explain broad side effects. GI effects such as vomiting, diarrhea and nausea are the most common and can cause dehydration and electrolyte changes. Liver enzyme rises, kidney toxicity, bone marrow suppression and retinal toxicity can occur. QTc prolongation is possible. As a strong CYP3A inhibitor it causes many drug interactions, including dangerous rises in levels of certain statins and sedatives. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The Progeria Research Foundation was started by the parents of a boy with progeria, and his mother, physician Leslie Gordon, became a leader of the research that led to the drug's approval.", "src": ["Zokinvy (lonafarnib) FDA label", "Eriksson M et al. Nature 2003", "Gordon LB et al. JAMA 2018"]}, "golodirsen": {"origin": "Duchenne muscular dystrophy (DMD) is caused by variants in the dystrophin gene, often deletions that throw the genetic reading frame out of register so no dystrophin is made. In the milder Becker muscular dystrophy, deletions keep the frame intact, and a shorter but partly working dystrophin is produced. In the 1990s and 2000s researchers proposed antisense exon skipping: using short synthetic strands to hide a chosen exon from the splicing machinery, turning a Duchenne-type message into a Becker-type message. Sarepta Therapeutics, building on phosphorodiamidate morpholino (PMO) chemistry developed at its predecessor AVI BioPharma, first won accelerated approval for eteplirsen (exon 51) in 2016. Golodirsen, which targets exon 53, received FDA accelerated approval as Vyondys 53 in December 2019.", "timeline": [["1986", "The dystrophin gene is identified as the cause of DMD."], ["2016", "Eteplirsen becomes the first exon-skipping drug approved for DMD."], ["2019", "FDA grants accelerated approval to golodirsen (Vyondys 53) for exon 53-amenable DMD."]], "cell": "The dystrophin gene has 79 exons, and its mRNA must be spliced so exons join in frame. Some deletions, for example of exon 52, leave exons that do not fit together in frame, so a premature stop codon ends translation and no functional dystrophin forms. Golodirsen is a phosphorodiamidate morpholino oligomer, an uncharged antisense strand with morpholine rings instead of ribose. It binds by base pairing to a sequence in exon 53 of dystrophin pre-mRNA. This blocks splicing factors from recognizing exon 53, so the spliceosome skips it and joins the flanking exons. For amenable deletions, skipping exon 53 restores the reading frame, and muscle cells make an internally shortened, partly functional dystrophin, similar to that in Becker muscular dystrophy. Because morpholinos are uncharged, they are not cleaved by RNase H and resist nucleases.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Small increases in dystrophin were seen in muscle biopsies, the basis of accelerated approval; clinical benefit has not yet been established in confirmatory trials."], ["Heart muscle", "Dystrophin loss also causes cardiomyopathy in DMD, but heart effects of golodirsen are not established."], ["Kidneys", "Kidney toxicity, including proteinuria and glomerulonephritis, was seen in animal studies and with related antisense drugs, so urine protein and kidney function are monitored."], ["Immune system", "Hypersensitivity reactions including rash, fever and bronchospasm have been reported."], ["General", "Headache, fever, falls, abdominal pain, cough and vomiting were common in trials."]], "journey": "Golodirsen is given by IV infusion once a week. It distributes into muscle and other tissues, though only a small fraction reaches muscle nuclei. Because morpholinos are uncharged and resist nucleases, it is barely metabolized and is not a CYP substrate. The kidneys clear it largely unchanged, with a plasma half-life of about three to four hours. Dystrophin production builds over months of weekly dosing.", "sidefx": "Golodirsen concentrates in the kidney as it is filtered and excreted, which is thought to explain proteinuria and kidney injury, so kidney function and urine protein are checked before and during treatment. Hypersensitivity reactions can occur because the infused oligonucleotide may trigger immune responses. Headache, fever, cough, vomiting and falls were common in trials. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Exon skipping turns a severe disease into a milder one by cutting out even more genetic material, which seems backward until you see that keeping the reading frame matters more than keeping every exon.", "src": ["Vyondys 53 (golodirsen) FDA label", "Frank DE et al. Neurology 2020", "Hoffman EP, Brown RH, Kunkel LM. Cell 1987"]}, "viltolarsen": {"origin": "Viltolarsen was developed in Japan by Nippon Shinyaku together with the National Center of Neurology and Psychiatry in Tokyo. It uses the same exon-skipping idea as Sarepta's drugs and the same phosphorodiamidate morpholino oligomer (PMO) chemistry, but with its own sequence targeting exon 53 of the dystrophin pre-mRNA. Japanese researchers designed it to skip exon 53 efficiently. It was approved in Japan in March 2020, and the FDA granted accelerated approval to Nippon Shinyaku's US subsidiary, NS Pharma, as Viltepso in August 2020, for Duchenne muscular dystrophy amenable to exon 53 skipping. It was the second exon 53-skipping drug approved in the US after golodirsen.", "timeline": [["2016", "Eteplirsen is the first exon-skipping drug approved in the US."], ["2019", "Golodirsen is approved as the first US exon 53-skipping drug."], ["2020", "Viltolarsen is approved in Japan in March."], ["2020", "FDA grants accelerated approval to viltolarsen (Viltepso) in August."]], "cell": "In Duchenne muscular dystrophy, deletions in the dystrophin gene disrupt the reading frame, so muscle cells make no functional dystrophin. Viltolarsen is a phosphorodiamidate morpholino antisense oligomer. Inside the nucleus it binds a target sequence within exon 53 of the dystrophin pre-mRNA. This blocks the splicing machinery from including exon 53, so the spliceosome joins the neighboring exons instead. For patients with deletions amenable to exon 53 skipping, this restores the reading frame and allows production of an internally truncated but partly functional dystrophin, similar to that in Becker muscular dystrophy. The neutral morpholino backbone resists nucleases and does not trigger RNase H cleavage.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Dystrophin levels in muscle biopsies increased in trials, the basis of accelerated approval; clinical benefit is still being confirmed."], ["Kidneys", "Proteinuria and kidney toxicity are possible, so urine protein and kidney function are monitored."], ["Heart", "Decreased ejection fraction has been reported, though DMD itself causes cardiomyopathy."], ["Respiratory tract", "Upper respiratory infections and cough were among the most common side effects in trials."], ["General", "Fever, bruising, joint pain, diarrhea and vomiting were reported."]], "journey": "Viltolarsen is given by IV infusion once a week over about an hour. It distributes into muscle and other tissues, though only a small fraction reaches muscle nuclei. Its uncharged morpholino chemistry makes it resistant to breakdown, and it is not a CYP substrate. It is excreted mostly unchanged in urine, with a plasma half-life of about two to three hours.", "sidefx": "Like other PMO drugs, viltolarsen is filtered and concentrated by the kidney, which explains the warning for kidney toxicity, including glomerulonephritis seen with related antisense drugs. Kidney function and urine protein are monitored. Upper respiratory infections, injection site reactions, cough, fever and bruising were common. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Viltolarsen was the first exon-skipping drug developed and approved in Japan, a country with a long history of exon-skipping research for DMD.", "src": ["Viltepso (viltolarsen) FDA label", "Clemens PR et al. JAMA Neurol 2020"]}, "casimersen": {"origin": "Casimersen is Sarepta Therapeutics' third exon-skipping drug for Duchenne muscular dystrophy. After eteplirsen (exon 51) in 2016 and golodirsen (exon 53) in 2019, Sarepta designed a phosphorodiamidate morpholino oligomer that targets exon 45, which is amenable in roughly 8% of people with DMD. The FDA granted it accelerated approval as Amondys 45 in February 2021, based on an increase in dystrophin in muscle biopsies. Continued approval depends on confirmatory trial evidence of clinical benefit.", "timeline": [["2016", "Eteplirsen (exon 51) is approved."], ["2019", "Golodirsen (exon 53) is approved."], ["2021", "FDA grants accelerated approval to casimersen (Amondys 45) for exon 45-amenable DMD."]], "cell": "In DMD, deletions in the dystrophin gene shift the reading frame so translation stops early and no functional dystrophin is made. Casimersen is a phosphorodiamidate morpholino antisense oligomer that binds a sequence within exon 45 of dystrophin pre-mRNA. By covering that sequence, it blocks the splicing machinery from including exon 45, so the spliceosome joins the neighboring exons. For patients whose deletions are amenable to exon 45 skipping, this restores an in-frame message. Muscle cells then make an internally truncated, partly functional dystrophin similar to the protein in Becker muscular dystrophy. The neutral morpholino backbone resists nucleases and does not recruit RNase H, so the target RNA is redirected rather than destroyed.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Dystrophin in muscle increased in trials, which was the basis for its accelerated approval; clinical benefit is being studied."], ["Kidneys", "Kidney toxicity has been seen with related antisense drugs and in animals, so urine protein and kidney function are monitored."], ["Immune system", "Hypersensitivity reactions such as rash, fever and bronchospasm have been reported."], ["Respiratory tract", "Upper respiratory infection, cough and throat pain were among the more common effects in trials."], ["General", "Fever, headache and joint pain were reported."]], "journey": "Casimersen is given by IV infusion once a week. It distributes into muscle and other tissues, with a small fraction reaching muscle nuclei. It is barely metabolized and is not a CYP substrate. It is excreted largely unchanged by the kidneys, with a plasma half-life of about three to four hours.", "sidefx": "Like other PMO oligomers, casimersen is filtered and concentrated by the kidney, which is thought to underlie the risk of kidney toxicity; kidney function and urine protein are checked before and during treatment. Hypersensitivity reactions can occur during or after infusion. Upper respiratory infections, cough, fever, headache and joint pain were common. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Each exon-skipping drug only fits patients with particular deletions, so genetic testing is needed to know which exon, if any, a drug can skip for a given patient.", "src": ["Amondys 45 (casimersen) FDA label", "Aartsma-Rus A et al. Hum Mutat 2009"]}, "vamorolone": {"origin": "Corticosteroids such as prednisone and deflazacort slow muscle loss in Duchenne muscular dystrophy, but long-term use causes growth delay, weight gain, bone thinning and other harms. Researchers including Eric Hoffman and Kanneboyina Nagaraju, who founded ReveraGen BioPharma, set out to design a steroid that kept the anti-inflammatory benefit with fewer side effects. They modified the steroid structure by adding a delta-9,11 double bond and removing the 11-beta hydroxyl group, which changed how it signals through the glucocorticoid receptor and turned it into a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist. Santhera Pharmaceuticals partnered on development. After the VISION-DMD trial, the FDA approved vamorolone as Agamree in October 2023 for DMD in patients 2 years and older.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Corticosteroids are shown to slow muscle decline in DMD."], ["2010s", "ReveraGen develops vamorolone as a dissociative steroid."], ["2023", "FDA approves vamorolone (Agamree) for Duchenne muscular dystrophy."]], "cell": "Classic glucocorticoids bind the glucocorticoid receptor (GR) and act in two main ways. In transrepression, the GR tethers to and blocks NF-kB and other inflammatory transcription factors, reducing inflammation. In transactivation, GR dimers bind DNA response elements and switch on genes, many of which drive side effects such as bone loss, muscle wasting, high blood sugar and growth suppression. Vamorolone binds GR and favors transrepression, inhibiting NF-kB-driven inflammation, with less transactivation. Lacking the 11-beta hydroxyl group changes how it interacts with steroid receptors, and it acts as an antagonist at the mineralocorticoid receptor rather than an agonist, which may reduce fluid retention and blood pressure effects. It also has membrane-stabilizing effects in muscle in lab studies. In dystrophic muscle, less inflammation slows muscle damage.", "body": [["Muscle", "Reduced inflammation in dystrophin-deficient muscle helps preserve function, with improvements in timed motor tests similar to prednisone in trials."], ["Growth", "Children's height growth was less affected than with prednisone in clinical trials."], ["Bones", "Bone formation markers were less suppressed than with prednisone, though long-term fracture data are limited."], ["Adrenal glands", "It still suppresses the HPA axis, so stopping suddenly can trigger adrenal crisis and stress-dose steroids may be needed during illness."], ["Metabolism and weight", "Cushingoid features, weight gain and increased appetite still occur."], ["Brain and behavior", "Behavior problems and mood changes are reported, as with other steroids."]], "journey": "Vamorolone is taken by mouth once daily with a meal as a liquid suspension. It is absorbed quickly, peaking in about two hours, and is about 88% protein bound. The liver metabolizes it by glucuronidation and CYP3A4. Its half-life is about two hours, though its effects on gene expression last longer. Metabolites leave in feces and urine. When stopping after more than a week, the dose is tapered.", "sidefx": "Because vamorolone still activates the glucocorticoid receptor, it suppresses the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis, so abrupt stopping can cause adrenal insufficiency or crisis. Immune suppression raises infection risk, and live vaccines are generally avoided. Cushingoid features, weight gain, vomiting, behavior changes and vitamin D deficiency are reported. Blood sugar can rise. Its main promise is fewer growth and bone effects than prednisone, not the absence of steroid side effects. Adrenal crisis is treated with stress-dose hydrocortisone.", "fun": "In trials, children taking vamorolone kept growing in height at a more typical rate, while children taking prednisone tended to fall behind.", "src": ["Agamree (vamorolone) FDA label", "Guglieri M et al. JAMA Neurol 2022 (VISION-DMD)", "Heier CR et al. EMBO Mol Med 2013"]}, "givinostat": {"origin": "Givinostat was discovered by Italfarmaco, an Italian pharmaceutical company in Milan, as a histone deacetylase (HDAC) inhibitor, originally studied for blood cancers and inflammatory diseases. In the 2000s researchers including Pier Lorenzo Puri showed that HDAC inhibitors improved muscle regeneration and reduced scarring in mdx mice, a model of Duchenne muscular dystrophy. Italfarmaco then developed givinostat for DMD and tested it in the EPIDYS trial, which showed slower decline in stair climbing and less fat replacement of muscle. In March 2024 the FDA approved it as Duvyzat for DMD in patients 6 years and older, the first nonsteroidal drug approved for DMD regardless of genetic variant.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Italfarmaco develops givinostat as an HDAC inhibitor."], ["2006", "HDAC inhibitors are reported to improve muscle in mdx mice."], ["2024", "FDA approves givinostat (Duvyzat), the first nonsteroidal drug for all genetic forms of DMD."]], "cell": "Histone deacetylases remove acetyl groups from histones, the proteins that package DNA. Deacetylated histones pack DNA tightly and turn genes off. In dystrophin-deficient muscle, the loss of dystrophin disrupts nitric oxide signaling, and HDAC2 becomes constitutively active. This represses genes that muscle stem cells (satellite cells) and fibro-adipogenic progenitors need for regeneration, and pushes those progenitors toward fibrosis and fat. Givinostat inhibits class I and II HDACs. Increased histone acetylation reopens chromatin at regeneration genes, promoting muscle repair, reducing inflammation and limiting replacement of muscle by fat and scar tissue. It does not restore dystrophin, so it works across all DMD variants.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Slower loss of muscle function and less fat infiltration on MRI were seen in trials, reflecting improved regeneration and less fibrosis."], ["Blood platelets", "Thrombocytopenia is common and dose dependent, increasing bleeding risk; platelet counts are monitored."], ["Blood fats", "Triglycerides can rise, so they are checked during treatment."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting are common."], ["Heart", "QTc prolongation is possible, so ECG checks are done in people at risk."]], "journey": "Givinostat is taken by mouth twice daily with food as a liquid suspension. It peaks in about two to three hours and is highly protein bound. It is metabolized in the liver by several non-CYP pathways and has a half-life of about six hours. Metabolites leave in feces and urine. Muscle effects build over months.", "sidefx": "HDACs regulate genes in many cell types, not just muscle. In the bone marrow, HDAC inhibition reduces platelet production by interfering with megakaryocyte maturation, explaining thrombocytopenia, the most important side effect, along with possible anemia and neutropenia. Changes in lipid gene regulation can raise triglycerides. GI effects like diarrhea and vomiting are common. QTc prolongation can occur. Doses are reduced in steps for low platelets, diarrhea or high triglycerides. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Givinostat was first tested in blood disorders like polycythemia vera before finding a role in muscle disease, showing how one mechanism can apply to very different conditions.", "src": ["Duvyzat (givinostat) FDA label", "Mercuri E et al. Lancet Neurol 2024 (EPIDYS)", "Minetti GC et al. Nat Med 2006"]}, "leniolisib": {"origin": "Activated PI3K-delta syndrome (APDS) was described in 2013 by two research groups, one in the United Kingdom and one at the US National Institutes of Health. They found that gain-of-function variants in PIK3CD (APDS1), and later loss-of-function variants in the regulatory subunit gene PIK3R1 (APDS2), make PI3K-delta overactive in immune cells. Patients have recurrent infections, enlarged lymph nodes and spleen, and a high risk of lymphoma. Leniolisib was discovered by Novartis as a selective PI3K-delta inhibitor, originally for autoimmune disease. NIH researchers tested it in APDS patients, and Pharming Group licensed and developed it. The FDA approved it as Joenja in March 2023, the first treatment aimed at the cause of APDS.", "timeline": [["2013", "APDS is described by researchers in the UK and at the NIH."], ["2010s", "Novartis develops leniolisib as a selective PI3K-delta inhibitor."], ["2017", "NIH study shows leniolisib reduces lymphoproliferation in APDS patients."], ["2023", "FDA approves leniolisib (Joenja) for APDS."]], "cell": "Phosphoinositide 3-kinase delta (PI3K-delta) is found mostly in white blood cells. It has a catalytic subunit, p110-delta (from PIK3CD), and a regulatory subunit, p85-alpha (from PIK3R1). When B and T cell receptors are activated, PI3K-delta converts PIP2 to PIP3 in the cell membrane. PIP3 recruits and activates AKT, which turns on mTOR and other signals that drive cell growth, survival and differentiation. In APDS, variants keep PI3K-delta switched on. Too much signaling pushes T cells to become exhausted senescent cells and drives B cells into abnormal differentiation, so few naive B cells and poor antibody responses result, along with lymphoproliferation. Leniolisib selectively inhibits p110-delta, lowering PIP3 and dampening AKT and mTOR signaling toward normal, which rebalances lymphocyte populations.", "body": [["Lymph nodes", "Overactive lymphocytes enlarge lymph nodes; inhibiting PI3K-delta shrinks them."], ["Spleen", "Spleen size decreases as lymphoproliferation calms."], ["B cells", "Naive B cells increase as signaling normalizes, which may improve antibody responses."], ["T cells", "Fewer senescent and exhausted T cells form."], ["Blood counts", "Neutropenia can occur, so white blood cell counts are monitored during treatment."], ["Skin and gut", "Headache, sinusitis, atopic dermatitis and diarrhea were reported in trials."]], "journey": "Leniolisib is taken by mouth twice daily, about 12 hours apart. It is absorbed quickly, peaking about one hour after a dose, and is about 94% protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors and inducers are avoided. Its half-life is about seven hours. It leaves in feces and urine.", "sidefx": "Leniolisib is generally well tolerated in APDS, where it corrects overactive signaling rather than suppressing normal immunity. Headache, sinusitis and atopic dermatitis were common. Neutropenia has been reported. Hypersensitivity reactions can occur. Older, less selective PI3K-delta inhibitors used in cancer caused severe colitis, liver injury and infections, but these were not prominent in the APDS trial, likely because of the different patient group and targeted use. Animal studies showed fetal harm, so pregnancy is avoided. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Leniolisib is a rare case of a targeted drug designed to correct a specific inherited signaling defect, essentially turning down an immune pathway that a gene variant left stuck in the on position.", "src": ["Joenja (leniolisib) FDA label", "Rao VK et al. Blood 2017", "Angulo I et al. Science 2013"]}, "pegunigalsidase-alfa": {"origin": "Fabry disease was described in 1898, separately by the dermatologist Johannes Fabry in Germany and William Anderson in England, who both saw children with dark red skin spots called angiokeratomas. Decades later the cause was traced to a missing lysosomal enzyme, alpha-galactosidase A, coded on the X chromosome. The first replacement enzymes, made in animal or human cell lines, arrived in the early 2000s. Pegunigalsidase alfa took a different route. The Israeli company Protalix BioTherapeutics grew the human enzyme in plant cells (tobacco cell culture) in steel bioreactors, then chemically linked the two halves of the enzyme together with polyethylene glycol (PEG). The goal was an enzyme that is more stable and stays in the blood longer. Developed with Chiesi, it was approved by the FDA in 2023 as Elfabrio for adults with Fabry disease.", "timeline": [["1898", "Johannes Fabry and William Anderson independently describe the disease."], ["1967", "The missing enzyme activity in Fabry disease is identified as a lysosomal alpha-galactosidase defect."], ["2003", "The first enzyme replacement therapy for Fabry disease is approved in the US."], ["2023", "FDA approves pegunigalsidase alfa (Elfabrio), a plant cell-made, PEGylated enzyme."]], "cell": "In Fabry disease, cells lack working alpha-galactosidase A, the lysosomal enzyme that clips the last galactose sugar off globotriaosylceramide (Gb3) and related lipids. Gb3 and its breakdown product lyso-Gb3 pile up inside lysosomes of blood vessel lining cells, kidney podocytes, heart muscle cells and nerves. Pegunigalsidase alfa is a replacement copy of the enzyme. Two enzyme units are cross-linked by PEG into a stable dimer, which slows clearance from blood. Cells take up the enzyme by endocytosis and route it to lysosomes, where the acidic pH suits its activity. There it hydrolyzes the terminal alpha-galactosyl bond, so Gb3 is broken down and storage slowly falls. Less storage means less damage to small vessels and kidney cells over time.", "body": [["Kidneys", "Clearing Gb3 from glomerular cells and capillary lining is meant to slow the loss of kidney filtration (eGFR) that drives Fabry kidney failure."], ["Blood vessels", "Endothelial cells hold less lipid, which may improve small vessel function in skin, kidney, heart and brain."], ["Heart", "Gb3 stored in heart muscle contributes to thickening and rhythm problems; lowering storage aims to slow that process."], ["Nerves", "Small fiber nerves damaged by storage cause burning pain in the hands and feet; enzyme therapy may lessen this over time."], ["Immune system", "As a foreign protein given by vein, it can trigger infusion reactions and anti-drug antibodies."]], "journey": "Pegunigalsidase alfa is given as an intravenous infusion because a protein would be digested if swallowed. The PEG coat and cross-linking help it resist breakdown, so it circulates far longer than older Fabry enzymes, with a half-life of roughly 80 hours. This lets it reach tissues over several days between infusions. It is taken into cells and lysosomes, and in the end it is broken down by normal protein-digesting pathways into peptides and amino acids. It is not excreted by the kidneys as active enzyme.", "sidefx": "Because the body sees the enzyme as foreign, the immune system can react. Infusion-associated reactions (fever, chills, rash, flushing) come from immune activation during or soon after the drip. True allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, can occur, which is why the label carries a boxed warning and resuscitation support is kept ready. Some patients form anti-drug antibodies that may affect response. A form of immune-complex kidney inflammation (membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis) was reported in trials. There is no antidote; reactions are handled by slowing or stopping the infusion and giving standard allergy treatment.", "fun": "Elfabrio is one of very few human medicines made in plant cells. Protalix's earlier plant-made enzyme, taliglucerase alfa for Gaucher disease, was the first plant cell-produced protein drug approved by the FDA, in 2012.", "src": ["Elfabrio (pegunigalsidase alfa-iwxj) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "olipudase-alfa": {"origin": "In 1914 the German pediatrician Albert Niemann described an infant with a huge liver and spleen who died young. The pathologist Ludwig Pick later studied the tissue and showed a stored lipid, and the condition became known as Niemann-Pick disease. In 1966 Roscoe Brady and colleagues at the US National Institutes of Health showed that the missing piece in types A and B was the enzyme acid sphingomyelinase. Today these forms are called acid sphingomyelinase deficiency (ASMD). Researchers, with Genzyme and later Sanofi, made a recombinant copy of the human enzyme in cultured cells. Animal studies showed a key danger: breaking down large stores of sphingomyelin too fast creates ceramide, which is toxic. That led to a slow dose-escalation plan. Olipudase alfa (Xenpozyme) was approved in Japan in early 2022 and by the FDA in August 2022 as the first treatment for the non-brain signs of ASMD.", "timeline": [["1914", "Albert Niemann describes the infantile disease."], ["1966", "Roscoe Brady's NIH group links Niemann-Pick disease to acid sphingomyelinase deficiency."], ["2022", "FDA approves olipudase alfa (Xenpozyme), the first therapy for ASMD."]], "cell": "Acid sphingomyelinase lives in lysosomes, where it splits sphingomyelin, a membrane lipid, into ceramide and phosphocholine. When the SMPD1 gene is faulty, sphingomyelin builds up in macrophages and other cells, forming foamy lipid-laden cells in the spleen, liver, lungs and bone marrow. Olipudase alfa carries mannose-6-phosphate tags on its sugar chains. These bind mannose-6-phosphate receptors on cell surfaces, so cells pull the enzyme in and deliver it to lysosomes. Inside, it breaks down the stored sphingomyelin. As storage clears, the spleen and liver shrink, lung gas exchange improves and blood lipids move toward normal. The enzyme does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so it does not treat the brain disease of type A.", "body": [["Spleen and liver", "Clearing stored lipid from macrophages shrinks the enlarged spleen and liver, which can ease abdominal fullness and low platelet counts from splenic trapping."], ["Lungs", "Less lipid in lung macrophages and tissue improves the lungs' ability to move oxygen into blood, measured as diffusing capacity (DLco)."], ["Blood lipids", "Patients often have high LDL and triglycerides with low HDL; treatment tends to improve this pattern."], ["Liver tests", "Rapid breakdown of stored lipid can release ceramide and cause short-lived rises in liver enzymes after infusions."], ["Brain", "Because the enzyme does not cross into the brain, neurologic disease is not treated."]], "journey": "Olipudase alfa is given by intravenous infusion, since a protein would be digested in the gut. From the blood it is taken up mainly by the liver, spleen and other tissues rich in macrophages, through mannose-6-phosphate receptors. Like other proteins it is eventually broken down into peptides and amino acids rather than cleared intact by the kidneys. Dosing starts low and rises in steps over weeks to let stored lipid be broken down gradually.", "sidefx": "The most important risks come from two sources. First, the immune system may react to a foreign protein, giving infusion reactions (headache, fever, hives, nausea) and rarely anaphylaxis, which is the reason for the boxed warning. Second, clearing a large store of sphingomyelin produces ceramide and other breakdown products that can stress cells, which explains liver enzyme rises within a day or two after infusions and why doses are escalated slowly with liver tests checked. Animal studies showed harm to the fetus. There is no antidote; reactions are managed by stopping the infusion and supportive care.", "fun": "The dangers of fast lipid breakdown were seen first in ASMD mice given high enzyme doses, which is why Xenpozyme has one of the slowest step-up schedules of any enzyme therapy.", "src": ["Xenpozyme (olipudase alfa-rpcp) FDA label", "Brady RO et al. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA 1966"]}, "velmanase-alfa": {"origin": "Alpha-mannosidosis was first described in 1967 by the Swedish physician Per-Arne Ockerman, who studied a boy with coarse facial features, hearing loss and learning problems and found mannose-rich sugars stored in his tissues. The cause is a missing lysosomal enzyme, alpha-mannosidase, coded by the MAN2B1 gene. Researchers in Europe made a recombinant copy of the human enzyme and showed it lowered stored sugars in a mouse model. The program was developed by the Italian company Chiesi. Velmanase alfa (Lamzede) was approved in the European Union in 2018 and by the FDA in February 2023 for the non-brain signs of alpha-mannosidosis, making it the first approved treatment for this very rare disease.", "timeline": [["1967", "Per-Arne Ockerman describes alpha-mannosidosis."], ["2018", "Velmanase alfa is approved in the European Union."], ["2023", "FDA approves velmanase alfa (Lamzede) for non-CNS signs of alpha-mannosidosis."]], "cell": "Lysosomal alpha-mannosidase trims mannose sugars from the ends of glycoproteins being recycled inside lysosomes. Without it, mannose-rich oligosaccharides build up in lysosomes in many tissues, swelling cells and disturbing their function. Velmanase alfa is a recombinant human enzyme that carries mannose-6-phosphate on its sugar chains. Cells take it up through mannose-6-phosphate receptors and send it to lysosomes. There it hydrolyzes alpha-linked mannose bonds, so the stored oligosaccharides can be broken down and cleared. Blood oligosaccharide levels fall, which is the main lab marker of response. The enzyme does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so it is not expected to treat the brain features.", "body": [["Muscles and endurance", "Less storage in tissues was linked in trials with modest gains in walking and stair-climbing endurance."], ["Lungs", "Some patients showed better lung function measures, though gains were modest."], ["Immune system", "Alpha-mannosidosis causes frequent infections from weak immune function; reducing storage may help, and as a foreign protein the drug can also trigger antibodies."], ["Blood markers", "Serum oligosaccharides drop as the stored sugars are broken down."], ["Brain and hearing", "Because the enzyme does not reach the brain, learning problems and hearing loss are not expected to improve."]], "journey": "Velmanase alfa is given as a weekly intravenous infusion because a protein cannot survive digestion. It travels in the blood and is pulled into cells of the liver, spleen and other tissues by mannose-6-phosphate receptors. Inside lysosomes it works until it is broken down by normal protein-recycling pathways into peptides and amino acids. It is not removed by the kidneys as active enzyme.", "sidefx": "The main safety issue is the immune system reacting to a foreign protein. Infusion-associated reactions and true hypersensitivity, including anaphylaxis in about 5 percent of trial patients, explain the boxed warning and the readiness to stop the infusion and treat. Premedication with antihistamines, fever reducers or steroids may be considered. Anti-drug antibodies can form. Animal data raise concern for harm to a fetus. There is no specific antidote; reactions are treated with standard allergy care.", "src": ["Lamzede (velmanase alfa-tycv) FDA label", "Ockerman PA. Lancet 1967"]}, "palovarotene": {"origin": "Fibrodysplasia ossificans progressiva (FOP) is a rare genetic disease in which muscle, tendon and ligament slowly turn into bone, often after small injuries or flare-ups. In 2006 Eileen Shore, Frederick Kaplan and colleagues at the University of Pennsylvania found the cause: a single mutation (R206H) in the ACVR1 gene, a bone morphogenetic protein (BMP) receptor. Separate work by Maurizio Pacifici, Masahiro Iwamoto and colleagues showed that drugs activating the retinoic acid receptor gamma could block cartilage formation and prevent extra bone in mice. Palovarotene had originally been developed by Roche for emphysema. Clementia Pharmaceuticals licensed it for FOP, and Ipsen acquired Clementia in 2019. The FDA approved palovarotene (Sohonos) in August 2023 as the first treatment for FOP, to reduce new heterotopic bone in adults and children of certain ages.", "timeline": [["2006", "Shore, Kaplan and colleagues identify the ACVR1 R206H mutation in FOP."], ["2011", "Mouse studies show RAR-gamma agonists can prevent heterotopic ossification."], ["2019", "Ipsen acquires Clementia and the palovarotene program."], ["2023", "FDA approves palovarotene (Sohonos), the first FOP therapy."]], "cell": "In FOP the ACVR1 (ALK2) receptor carries a gain-of-function mutation, so it overreacts to signals, including activin A, and sends too much signal through the SMAD1/5/8 pathway. This tells soft tissue progenitor cells to become cartilage, which then turns into bone in the same way as normal bone growth (endochondral ossification). Palovarotene is a synthetic retinoid that selectively activates retinoic acid receptor gamma (RAR-gamma), a nuclear receptor. Activated RAR-gamma changes gene transcription and dampens BMP-SMAD signaling. It blocks the early step where progenitor cells become cartilage. Without that cartilage template, less new heterotopic bone forms during and after flare-ups.", "body": [["Muscle and soft tissue", "By blocking the cartilage stage, it reduces the amount of new extra bone that forms in muscles, tendons and ligaments."], ["Growth plates", "Retinoid signaling also acts on normal growth cartilage, which can close growth plates early in children who are still growing."], ["Skin and lips", "Like other retinoids, it causes dry skin, chapped lips, peeling, itching and hair loss from effects on skin cell turnover."], ["Spine and bone", "Lower bone density and vertebral fractures were seen, reflecting retinoid effects on normal bone."], ["Eyes", "Night blindness can occur because retinoids interfere with vitamin A use in the retina."], ["Mood", "Depression and other psychiatric effects have been reported with retinoids."]], "journey": "Palovarotene is taken by mouth, usually with food, and reaches peak levels in about 4 hours. It is highly bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down, mainly by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors or inducers change its levels. Its half-life is about 9 hours, and it leaves the body mainly in feces. Its effect on new bone formation appears over weeks to months.", "sidefx": "Most side effects are classic retinoid effects. Activating retinoic acid receptors in skin and mucous membranes causes dryness, peeling and hair loss. The same pathway that blocks abnormal cartilage also acts on normal growth plate cartilage, which explains early growth plate closure in children and the boxed warning. Retinoids are powerful teratogens because retinoic acid controls embryo patterning, so pregnancy must be prevented. Retinoid effects on bone explain lower bone density and vertebral fractures, and vitamin A pathway interference explains night blindness. There is no antidote; side effects lessen after stopping.", "fun": "The FOP gene was found by studying families, but the field was driven for decades by Frederick Kaplan, who kept a registry of patients around the world. The disease is sometimes called the stone man syndrome.", "src": ["Sohonos (palovarotene) FDA label", "Shore EM et al. Nature Genetics 2006"]}, "mavorixafor": {"origin": "WHIM syndrome is named for its four features: warts, hypogammaglobulinemia, infections and myelokathexis (white cells trapped in the bone marrow). In 2003 researchers found that it is caused by gain-of-function mutations in the chemokine receptor CXCR4. CXCR4 drugs had first been studied for HIV, because some HIV strains use CXCR4 to enter cells. Mavorixafor began as AMD070, an oral CXCR4 blocker made at the Canadian company AnorMED, which also created the injectable CXCR4 blocker plerixafor. X4 Pharmaceuticals later developed mavorixafor for WHIM syndrome. The FDA approved it in April 2024 as Xolremdi, the first treatment approved for WHIM syndrome, to raise circulating neutrophils and lymphocytes.", "timeline": [["2003", "Gain-of-function CXCR4 mutations are identified as the cause of WHIM syndrome."], ["2000s", "AnorMED develops oral CXCR4 antagonists, including AMD070 (later mavorixafor)."], ["2024", "FDA approves mavorixafor (Xolremdi) for WHIM syndrome."]], "cell": "CXCR4 is a G protein-coupled chemokine receptor on white blood cells. Its ligand, CXCL12 (also called SDF-1), is made in large amounts by bone marrow stromal cells and holds cells in the marrow. Normally, after activation, CXCR4 is switched off and pulled into the cell, which lets mature neutrophils leave the marrow. In WHIM syndrome, mutations cut off the receptor's tail, so it cannot be shut off and keeps signaling. Mature neutrophils and lymphocytes stay trapped in the marrow. Mavorixafor binds CXCR4 and blocks CXCL12 binding. With the retention signal reduced, neutrophils and lymphocytes are released into the bloodstream, where they can fight infection.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Blocking the CXCR4 hold signal frees mature white cells that were trapped, correcting myelokathexis."], ["Blood", "Absolute neutrophil and lymphocyte counts rise in the circulation."], ["Immune defense", "More circulating white cells lowered infection rates in the pivotal trial."], ["Heart", "Higher blood levels can prolong the QT interval, so heart rhythm effects are monitored."], ["Skin", "Rash and skin conditions were among reported side effects; warts from HPV are part of the disease itself."]], "journey": "Mavorixafor is a capsule taken by mouth once daily on an empty stomach. It reaches peak levels in about 3 hours and is highly bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down, mainly through CYP3A4, and it also inhibits CYP2D6, which can raise levels of drugs cleared by that enzyme. Its half-life is long, about 3 days, and it leaves the body mostly in feces. Neutrophil counts rise within weeks.", "sidefx": "QT prolongation is concentration-dependent, so drugs that raise mavorixafor levels (strong CYP3A4 inhibitors) and low potassium increase the risk of a dangerous rhythm. Animal studies showed harm to the fetus, likely because CXCR4 and CXCL12 guide cell movement during development, so contraception is used. Low platelets, rash, nosebleeds, runny nose and dizziness were reported. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "WHIM syndrome is one of the few diseases in which a single patient's cure was traced to chance: researchers at NIH described a woman whose WHIM syndrome disappeared after one blood stem cell lost the mutant CXCR4 gene through chromothripsis, a shattering of a chromosome.", "src": ["Xolremdi (mavorixafor) FDA label", "Hernandez PA et al. Nature Genetics 2003"]}, "sebetralstat": {"origin": "Hereditary angioedema (HAE) is a genetic disease in which a lack of working C1 esterase inhibitor lets plasma kallikrein run unchecked, releasing bradykinin and causing sudden swelling of the skin, gut and airway. For years, on-demand treatments had to be injected or infused during an attack. The British-American company KalVista Pharmaceuticals set out to make a small-molecule plasma kallikrein inhibitor that could be swallowed. Sebetralstat came from that program. In the KONFIDENT trial, it sped symptom relief compared with placebo. The FDA approved sebetralstat (Ekterly) in 2025 as the first oral on-demand treatment for HAE attacks in adults and adolescents.", "timeline": [["1888", "William Osler describes a hereditary form of angioedema in families."], ["1963", "Virginia Donaldson links HAE to a lack of C1 esterase inhibitor."], ["2025", "FDA approves sebetralstat (Ekterly), the first oral on-demand HAE therapy."]], "cell": "In HAE, low or faulty C1 esterase inhibitor fails to restrain the contact (kallikrein-kinin) system. Factor XII becomes activated and turns prekallikrein into plasma kallikrein. Kallikrein then cuts high-molecular-weight kininogen to release bradykinin, and it also activates more factor XII in a feedback loop. Bradykinin binds B2 receptors on blood vessel lining cells, raising nitric oxide and prostaglandins and opening gaps between endothelial cells, so fluid leaks into tissues. Sebetralstat is a small molecule that fits into the active site of plasma kallikrein and blocks it competitively and reversibly. Bradykinin release stops, the feedback loop is broken, and swelling stops progressing and begins to resolve.", "body": [["Skin and soft tissue", "Stopping bradykinin release halts the spread of swelling in the face, hands, feet and genitals."], ["Gut", "Abdominal attacks from bowel wall swelling cause severe pain and vomiting; blocking kallikrein shortens these attacks."], ["Airway", "Laryngeal swelling can block breathing; a dose does not replace emergency care because the airway can close before the drug takes effect."], ["Blood vessels", "Less B2 receptor signaling reduces vessel leakiness."], ["Nervous system", "Headache was the most common side effect in trials."]], "journey": "Sebetralstat is a tablet taken by mouth at the first sign of an attack. It is absorbed quickly and reaches peak levels in about an hour, which supports rapid action. The liver breaks it down, mainly by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels and inducers lower them. Its half-life is short, a matter of hours, which fits an on-demand drug used only during attacks.", "sidefx": "Sebetralstat was well tolerated in trials, with headache the most common side effect. At exposures well above normal it can cause a small increase in the QT interval. The biggest danger is not the drug but the disease: a throat attack can block the airway, so emergency care is still needed after a dose. Drug interactions through CYP3A4 can change exposure. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Ekterly (sebetralstat) FDA label", "Riedl MA et al. KONFIDENT trial, NEJM 2024"]}, "ecallantide": {"origin": "Ecallantide was found by phage display, a lab method in which billions of protein variants are displayed on the surface of viruses that infect bacteria, then fished out by how tightly they bind a target. Scientists at Dyax Corporation in Massachusetts started with a Kunitz domain, a small protease-blocking module from a human protein, and screened variants for binding to plasma kallikrein. The winner was a 60-amino-acid protein that blocks kallikrein very tightly. It is produced in the yeast Pichia pastoris. In December 2009 the FDA approved ecallantide (Kalbitor) for acute attacks of hereditary angioedema, one of the first targeted on-demand HAE drugs in the US. Because of anaphylaxis risk, it must be given by a health professional.", "timeline": [["1985", "George Smith describes phage display, later recognized with a Nobel Prize in 2018."], ["1990s", "Dyax uses phage display to engineer a Kunitz-domain kallikrein inhibitor."], ["2009", "FDA approves ecallantide (Kalbitor) for acute HAE attacks."]], "cell": "In hereditary angioedema, C1 esterase inhibitor is missing or faulty, so plasma kallikrein is not held in check. Kallikrein cleaves high-molecular-weight kininogen to release bradykinin, the peptide that causes swelling. Bradykinin binds B2 receptors on endothelial cells, triggering nitric oxide and prostaglandin release and loosening cell junctions, so plasma leaks into tissue. Ecallantide is a Kunitz-domain protein that slots into the active site of plasma kallikrein and blocks it potently and reversibly. With kallikrein inhibited, bradykinin production falls, vessel leak slows and the attack stops growing.", "body": [["Skin and soft tissue", "Blocking bradykinin release stops swelling of the face, limbs and genitals from spreading."], ["Gut", "Bowel wall swelling causes intense abdominal pain; ecallantide shortens these attacks."], ["Airway", "Throat swelling is life-threatening; drug effect takes time, so airway support may still be needed."], ["Blood clotting tests", "Kallikrein takes part in the contact pathway of clotting tests, so ecallantide can prolong the aPTT in the lab without causing bleeding."], ["Immune system", "As a foreign protein made in yeast, it can trigger antibodies and allergic reactions."]], "journey": "Ecallantide is injected under the skin, usually as several injections during an attack. It reaches peak blood levels in about 2 to 3 hours. As a small protein it is broken down by proteases and partly filtered by the kidneys. Its half-life is about 2 hours, which fits its on-demand use.", "sidefx": "The key risk is anaphylaxis, seen in about 4 percent of patients in trials, which led to a boxed warning and the rule that it is given only by a health professional ready to treat allergic reactions. The immune system recognizes ecallantide as foreign, and people who form antibodies after repeated doses may have higher risk. Anaphylaxis can look like an HAE attack, which makes it hard to recognize. Headache, nausea, fatigue, fever and injection site reactions are common. A prolonged aPTT is a lab effect, not true bleeding. There is no antidote; anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine and supportive care.", "src": ["Kalbitor (ecallantide) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "garadacimab": {"origin": "Factor XII was discovered in 1955 when the hematologist Oscar Ratnoff studied a railroad worker named John Hageman. His blood clotted very slowly in a test tube, yet he had no bleeding problem. The missing protein was named Hageman factor, or factor XII. Later research showed that activated factor XII starts the contact system that leads to bradykinin release, the cause of swelling in hereditary angioedema. Because people born without factor XII do not bleed, it looked like a safe target. Researchers working with CSL Behring developed fully human antibodies against activated factor XII using phage display. Garadacimab (Andembry) was approved by the FDA in 2025 for preventing HAE attacks, the first therapy to target factor XIIa.", "timeline": [["1955", "Oscar Ratnoff describes Hageman factor (factor XII) in patient John Hageman."], ["2010s", "Fully human anti-factor XIIa antibodies are developed with CSL Behring."], ["2025", "FDA approves garadacimab (Andembry) for HAE prophylaxis."]], "cell": "In hereditary angioedema, low C1 esterase inhibitor lets the contact system run unchecked. It starts when factor XII contacts negatively charged surfaces and becomes activated factor XII (FXIIa). FXIIa converts prekallikrein to plasma kallikrein, which cleaves high-molecular-weight kininogen to release bradykinin. Kallikrein also activates more factor XII, creating a loop. Bradykinin acts on B2 receptors to make blood vessels leaky, causing swelling. Garadacimab is a fully human IgG4 monoclonal antibody that binds the catalytic (protease) domain of FXIIa and blocks its activity. This shuts the cascade at its first step, so less kallikrein and bradykinin are made and attacks become less frequent.", "body": [["Skin, gut and airway", "Blocking the very top of the bradykinin cascade lowers how often swelling attacks occur in all affected areas."], ["Blood clotting tests", "The aPTT test depends on factor XII, so results are prolonged by the drug even though bleeding does not increase."], ["Clotting in the body", "People who lack factor XII do not bleed abnormally, which is why blocking it does not cause bleeding in normal life."], ["Injection site", "Monthly injections can cause local redness or pain."]], "journey": "Garadacimab is injected under the skin once a month, often with a prefilled pen. As a large antibody it is absorbed slowly through the lymph into the blood. It is recycled by the neonatal Fc receptor, which protects it from breakdown and gives a long half-life of about 17 days. Like other antibodies, it is eventually broken down into peptides and amino acids, not cleared by the kidneys or liver enzymes.", "sidefx": "Garadacimab is generally well tolerated. Injection site reactions, sore throat and cold symptoms and abdominal pain were the most common effects. Because the aPTT test relies on factor XII, the drug makes this lab result look abnormal, which could be misread as a bleeding risk. Hypersensitivity is possible with any protein drug. There is no antidote.", "fun": "John Hageman, the man whose blood led to the discovery of factor XII, died in 1968 of a pulmonary embolism, a blood clot, despite lacking the clotting factor named after him.", "src": ["Andembry (garadacimab-gxii) FDA label", "Ratnoff OD, Colopy JE. J Clin Invest 1955"]}, "donidalorsen": {"origin": "Donidalorsen comes from Ionis Pharmaceuticals in California, a pioneer of antisense drugs. Antisense oligonucleotides are short, chemically modified strands of DNA-like material designed to stick to a single messenger RNA and get it destroyed. Ionis chose prekallikrein, the protein made in the liver that becomes plasma kallikrein, the enzyme that drives bradykinin release in hereditary angioedema. To steer the drug to the liver, they attached a GalNAc sugar cluster that binds a receptor found almost only on liver cells. This makes the drug more potent, so it can be given less often. In trials, injections every 4 or 8 weeks greatly reduced attacks. The FDA approved donidalorsen (Dawnzera) in 2025 for preventing HAE attacks.", "timeline": [["1978", "Paul Zamecnik and Mary Stephenson show a short synthetic DNA strand can block viral RNA, the founding antisense idea."], ["2010s", "GalNAc conjugation is developed to deliver oligonucleotides to liver cells."], ["2025", "FDA approves donidalorsen (Dawnzera) for HAE prophylaxis."]], "cell": "Prekallikrein is made by liver cells from the KLKB1 gene and released into blood. In HAE, low C1 esterase inhibitor allows prekallikrein to be converted freely to kallikrein, which releases bradykinin and causes swelling. Donidalorsen is a single-stranded antisense oligonucleotide joined to a GalNAc cluster. The GalNAc binds the asialoglycoprotein receptor on hepatocytes, so the drug is pulled into liver cells. Inside, it pairs with the KLKB1 messenger RNA by base pairing. The DNA-RNA hybrid is recognized by RNase H1, an enzyme that cuts the RNA strand. With the message destroyed, liver cells make far less prekallikrein. Lower prekallikrein in blood means less kallikrein and bradykinin can form, so attacks occur less often.", "body": [["Liver", "Liver cells take up the drug and reduce their production of prekallikrein."], ["Blood", "Plasma prekallikrein levels fall over weeks, lowering the supply for bradykinin generation."], ["Skin, gut and airway", "With less bradykinin, swelling attacks in all these sites become much less frequent."], ["Platelets and liver tests", "Small drops in platelet counts and mild liver enzyme rises were seen in trials, a pattern known from antisense drugs."], ["Injection site", "Redness or discomfort can occur where the drug is injected."]], "journey": "Donidalorsen is injected under the skin. It is absorbed within hours and quickly leaves the blood for the liver, helped by its GalNAc tag, so plasma levels fall fast in the first hours. It then stays in liver tissue for a long time, with a terminal half-life of about a month, which supports dosing every 4 or 8 weeks. It is broken down by nucleases into shorter fragments, which leave in the urine. It is not processed by CYP enzymes.", "sidefx": "Injection site reactions come from local inflammation as the oligonucleotide is absorbed. Hypersensitivity, including anaphylaxis, can occur. Small platelet declines and mild liver enzyme increases have been linked to oligonucleotide drugs in general, reflecting their uptake by liver and interaction with proteins in blood. Because it works by lowering protein production, its effect builds and fades slowly over weeks. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Dawnzera (donidalorsen) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "cipaglucosidase-alfa": {"origin": "In 1932 the Dutch pathologist J. C. Pompe described a baby girl who died with a massively enlarged heart packed with glycogen. In 1963 the Belgian biochemist Henri-Gery Hers showed the cause: a missing lysosomal enzyme, acid alpha-glucosidase (GAA). Pompe disease thus became the first known lysosomal storage disease. The first enzyme replacement, alglucosidase alfa, was approved in 2006. Amicus Therapeutics designed cipaglucosidase alfa to carry more bis-mannose-6-phosphate sugar tags, so muscle cells could pull in more enzyme. They paired it with miglustat, a small molecule that binds and stabilizes the enzyme in the blood before it reaches muscle. The FDA approved cipaglucosidase alfa (Pombiliti) with miglustat (Opfolda) in September 2023 for adults with late-onset Pompe disease.", "timeline": [["1932", "J. C. Pompe describes glycogen storage in the heart of an infant."], ["1963", "Henri-Gery Hers identifies acid alpha-glucosidase deficiency, the first lysosomal storage disease defect."], ["2006", "The first Pompe enzyme therapy, alglucosidase alfa, is approved."], ["2023", "FDA approves cipaglucosidase alfa (Pombiliti) with miglustat (Opfolda)."]], "cell": "Acid alpha-glucosidase breaks the alpha-1,4 and alpha-1,6 bonds of glycogen inside lysosomes, releasing glucose. In Pompe disease, glycogen builds up in lysosomes of skeletal, heart and breathing muscles. Swollen lysosomes and blocked autophagy damage the muscle fibers. Cipaglucosidase alfa is a recombinant human GAA made with many bis-mannose-6-phosphate glycans. These bind tightly to the cation-independent mannose-6-phosphate receptor on muscle cells, which carries the enzyme into lysosomes. There it is activated and digests glycogen. Miglustat, taken by mouth before the infusion, binds the enzyme in blood and helps keep it folded and active at neutral pH until it is taken up. Less glycogen means less muscle damage and better strength and breathing.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Clearing glycogen from limb muscles helped stabilize or improve walking distance in late-onset disease."], ["Breathing muscles", "The diaphragm is often weak in Pompe disease; enzyme therapy aims to protect lung function, measured as forced vital capacity."], ["Heart", "Heart muscle storage is mainly a problem in infant-onset disease; this product is approved for late-onset disease."], ["Immune system", "As a foreign protein, it can trigger infusion reactions and anti-drug antibodies."], ["Pregnancy", "Miglustat can harm a fetus, so contraception is needed during treatment."]], "journey": "Miglustat capsules are swallowed about an hour before the infusion, timed so their peak meets the enzyme in the blood. Cipaglucosidase alfa is then given by intravenous infusion every two weeks. The enzyme has a short plasma half-life of a few hours because muscle and other cells take it up through mannose-6-phosphate receptors. Inside cells it is eventually broken down into peptides and amino acids. Miglustat, a small molecule, is mostly excreted unchanged by the kidneys.", "sidefx": "Infusion reactions and anaphylaxis come from the immune system reacting to the foreign enzyme, which is why the label has a boxed warning and medical support is kept ready. People with weak heart or lung function can be harmed by the infusion volume and the stress of reactions, leading to acute cardiorespiratory failure. Anti-drug antibodies can form. Miglustat is linked to fetal harm in animals and can cause diarrhea, since it also inhibits gut sugar-digesting enzymes. There is no antidote; reactions are managed by stopping the infusion and supportive care.", "src": ["Pombiliti (cipaglucosidase alfa-atga) FDA label", "Opfolda (miglustat) FDA label", "Hers HG. Biochem J 1963"]}, "vestronidase-alfa": {"origin": "Mucopolysaccharidosis type VII is often called Sly syndrome after William Sly, a pediatrician and biochemist at Saint Louis University, who described the first patient in 1973 and showed the missing enzyme was beta-glucuronidase. Sly's lab went on to help explain how cells capture lysosomal enzymes through the mannose-6-phosphate receptor, work that underpins all enzyme replacement therapy. A mouse model of MPS VII became a key test system for ERT and gene therapy. Ultragenyx Pharmaceutical developed a recombinant human beta-glucuronidase, vestronidase alfa. The FDA approved it as Mepsevii in November 2017, the first treatment for MPS VII, an ultra-rare disease with only a few hundred known patients worldwide.", "timeline": [["1973", "William Sly and colleagues describe beta-glucuronidase deficiency (MPS VII)."], ["1980s", "Sly's group and others define mannose-6-phosphate receptor uptake of lysosomal enzymes."], ["2017", "FDA approves vestronidase alfa (Mepsevii), the first MPS VII therapy."]], "cell": "Glycosaminoglycans (GAGs) such as dermatan sulfate, heparan sulfate and chondroitin sulfate are long sugar chains that cells recycle in lysosomes. Beta-glucuronidase removes glucuronic acid residues from the ends of these chains, one of the steps needed to take them apart. In MPS VII the GUSB gene is faulty, so partly digested GAGs pile up in lysosomes in bones, joints, liver, spleen, heart valves and other tissues. Vestronidase alfa is a recombinant human beta-glucuronidase with mannose-6-phosphate tags. It binds mannose-6-phosphate receptors on cell surfaces and is carried to lysosomes, where it restores the missing step. GAG storage falls, and urine GAG levels drop, the main marker of response. It does not cross the blood-brain barrier.", "body": [["Liver and spleen", "Clearing stored GAGs reduces the enlargement of these organs seen in MPS VII."], ["Bones and joints", "GAG storage stiffens joints and deforms bone; enzyme therapy aims to slow this, though existing bone changes improve little."], ["Lungs and walking", "Some patients showed gains in walking distance and breathing measures in the trial."], ["Kidneys and urine", "Urinary GAG excretion falls as tissue storage clears."], ["Brain", "Because the enzyme does not cross into the brain, effects on learning and behavior are not established."]], "journey": "Vestronidase alfa is given as an intravenous infusion every two weeks. It leaves the blood quickly, with a plasma half-life of a few hours, because tissues such as the liver and spleen take it up through mannose-6-phosphate receptors. Inside lysosomes it works until normal protein turnover breaks it into peptides and amino acids. It is not removed by the kidneys as intact enzyme.", "sidefx": "The main danger is anaphylaxis, which can happen even with the first dose, so the label has a boxed warning and patients are watched during and for an hour after infusion. The immune system recognizes the enzyme as foreign, which also explains infusion reactions such as rash, itching and diarrhea and the formation of anti-drug antibodies. Premedication with an antihistamine is recommended. Leakage of the infusion into tissue can cause local reactions. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The beta-glucuronidase-deficient mouse, a natural model of Sly syndrome, was one of the first animals used to prove that enzyme replacement and gene therapy could correct a lysosomal disease.", "src": ["Mepsevii (vestronidase alfa-vjbk) FDA label", "Sly WS et al. J Pediatr 1973"]}, "arimoclomol": {"origin": "Arimoclomol traces back to Hungarian research on hydroxylamine derivatives. The company Biorex developed bimoclomol and then arimoclomol in the 1990s as drugs that boost the heat shock response, the cell's built-in system for refolding damaged proteins. It was first studied for amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS), but it did not prove effective there. The Danish company Orphazyme then focused on Niemann-Pick disease type C (NPC), a rare inherited disease in which cholesterol and lipids are trapped in lysosomes and the brain degenerates. The program later passed to Zevra Therapeutics. In September 2024 the FDA approved arimoclomol (Miplyffa), used with miglustat, to treat the neurologic signs of NPC. It was the first drug approved in the US for NPC.", "timeline": [["1914", "Albert Niemann describes the first form of Niemann-Pick disease."], ["1997", "The NPC1 gene is identified as the cause of most NPC cases."], ["2024", "FDA approves arimoclomol (Miplyffa), with miglustat, the first US NPC therapy."]], "cell": "Most NPC cases are caused by mutations in NPC1, a membrane protein that moves cholesterol out of late endosomes and lysosomes. Many mutant NPC1 proteins misfold, are caught by quality control in the endoplasmic reticulum, and are destroyed before reaching the lysosome. The exact mechanism of arimoclomol is not fully understood. It is thought to prolong the activation of heat shock factor 1 (HSF1), a transcription factor switched on by cellular stress. Prolonged HSF1 activity raises production of heat shock proteins such as HSP70. These chaperones may help misfolded NPC1 fold properly and reach the lysosome, and HSP70 may also support lysosomal membrane health. In lab studies this reduced lysosomal unesterified cholesterol. It acts as an amplifier: it boosts the stress response in stressed cells rather than turning it on in healthy ones.", "body": [["Brain and nerves", "In the trial, the neurologic decline of NPC (walking, swallowing, speech, fine motor skills) progressed more slowly with arimoclomol."], ["Kidneys", "It blocks the OCT2 transporter that secretes creatinine, so blood creatinine rises without a real loss of kidney function."], ["Skin and airway", "Hives and angioedema can occur as hypersensitivity reactions."], ["Gut and weight", "Diarrhea and weight loss were among the reported side effects."], ["Pregnancy", "Animal studies showed harm to the fetus."]], "journey": "Arimoclomol is taken by mouth as capsules three times a day, which can be opened and mixed with food or given through a feeding tube. It is absorbed quickly, reaching peak levels in about half an hour to 2 hours. It has low binding to plasma proteins. A small part is broken down by glucuronidation and minor oxidation, and most of the drug leaves unchanged in the urine. The half-life is about 4 hours, which explains the three-times-daily schedule.", "sidefx": "Hypersensitivity reactions such as hives and angioedema reflect immune reactions to the drug. Arimoclomol inhibits OCT2, the kidney transporter that secretes creatinine and also handles some drugs, so creatinine rises 10 to 20 percent early in therapy without a fall in true filtration, and levels of OCT2-handled drugs can rise. Animal data showed fetal toxicity. Respiratory infections, diarrhea and weight loss were also reported. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Miplyffa (arimoclomol) FDA label", "Carstea ED et al. Science 1997"]}, "levacetylleucine": {"origin": "Levacetylleucine is the L form of N-acetyl-leucine, a slightly modified version of the amino acid leucine. A mixture of both forms, acetyl-DL-leucine, has been sold in France since the 1950s under the name Tanganil as a treatment for vertigo. In the 2010s doctors noticed that patients with cerebellar ataxias, including Niemann-Pick disease type C (NPC), seemed to improve on it. Researchers at Oxford University and the company IntraBio studied the compound and found that the L form carried the benefit. IntraBio ran trials of the pure L form in NPC. The FDA approved levacetylleucine (Aqneursa) in September 2024 for the neurologic signs of NPC in adults and children, and its label also covers ataxia in ataxia-telangiectasia.", "timeline": [["1957", "Acetyl-DL-leucine (Tanganil) is introduced in France for vertigo."], ["2010s", "Case series report improved ataxia in patients taking acetyl-DL-leucine."], ["2024", "FDA approves levacetylleucine (Aqneursa) for Niemann-Pick disease type C."]], "cell": "The mechanism is not fully understood. Plain leucine enters cells through the LAT1 amino acid transporter. Adding an acetyl group changes the molecule's charge so it is carried instead by monocarboxylate transporters such as MCT1, which lets it enter neurons more readily. Inside cells, it is deacetylated back to L-leucine. It is thought to improve glucose and energy (ATP) metabolism in affected neurons, support autophagy and lysosomal function, and reduce inflammation in the brain. In NPC, where cerebellar neurons slowly die from lysosomal lipid storage, these effects are proposed to help neurons work better, which shows up as better coordination and neurologic scores.", "body": [["Cerebellum and coordination", "In the trial, patients had better scores for ataxia and neurologic function, reflecting improved cerebellar output."], ["Brain energy", "It is proposed to normalize neuron energy metabolism that is disrupted in lysosomal disease."], ["Gut", "Abdominal pain, swallowing difficulty and vomiting were among reported side effects."], ["Airways", "Upper respiratory infections were reported in trials."], ["Pregnancy", "Animal studies suggest fetal harm, so contraception is used during treatment and briefly afterward."]], "journey": "Levacetylleucine is a powder (granules) taken by mouth after being mixed with water, juice or food, or given through a feeding tube. It is absorbed quickly, reaching peak levels in about an hour. It is carried into cells by monocarboxylate transporters and is deacetylated to L-leucine, which then joins normal amino acid metabolism. Its half-life is short, about an hour, so it is taken in divided doses through the day. It is a P-glycoprotein substrate, which can cause drug interactions.", "sidefx": "Side effects are generally mild, with abdominal pain, vomiting and swallowing difficulty reflecting local effects in the gut. The main label warning is embryo-fetal toxicity seen in animals, so pregnancy is avoided. The D form of acetyl-leucine competes with the L form, so the racemic over-the-counter product and N-acetyl-D-leucine should not be combined with it. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Aqneursa (levacetylleucine) FDA label", "Bremova-Ertl T et al. NEJM 2024"]}, "cevimeline": {"origin": "Cevimeline began in Israel as a candidate drug for Alzheimer's disease. In the 1980s the chemist Abraham Fisher and colleagues at the Israel Institute for Biological Research designed a rigid quinuclidine molecule, coded AF102B, to activate M1 muscarinic receptors in the brain and mimic acetylcholine lost in dementia. It did not become a dementia drug, but it was a strong stimulator of salivary glands. Developed further in Japan, it was approved by the FDA in 2000 as Evoxac to treat dry mouth in people with Sjogren syndrome, an autoimmune disease that attacks the salivary and tear glands.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Abraham Fisher's group designs AF102B as an M1 muscarinic agonist for Alzheimer's disease."], ["2000", "FDA approves cevimeline (Evoxac) for dry mouth in Sjogren syndrome."]], "cell": "Cevimeline is a direct agonist at muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, with high affinity for the M1 and M3 subtypes. Salivary and tear gland acinar cells carry M3 receptors. When cevimeline binds, M3 activates the Gq protein, which turns on phospholipase C. This splits PIP2 into IP3 and diacylglycerol. IP3 releases calcium from internal stores, and the calcium rise opens chloride and potassium channels and moves water through aquaporins into the gland ducts. The result is more watery saliva and tears. In Sjogren syndrome, some glandular tissue survives, so stimulating the remaining cells increases secretion. Muscarinic receptors in sweat glands, gut, airways, eyes and heart are also stimulated, which explains its side effects.", "body": [["Salivary glands", "M3 stimulation increases saliva flow, easing dry mouth, difficulty swallowing and the dental decay that dry mouth causes."], ["Sweat glands", "Sympathetic cholinergic sweat glands use muscarinic receptors, so heavy sweating is the most common side effect."], ["Eyes", "Pupil constriction and changes in focusing muscle can blur vision, especially at night, and reduce depth perception."], ["Airways", "Muscarinic stimulation tightens bronchial smooth muscle and increases secretions, a concern in asthma or COPD."], ["Heart", "M2-like effects on the sinus and AV nodes can slow the heart rate and alter conduction."], ["Gut", "Increased gut motility and secretions cause nausea and diarrhea."]], "journey": "Cevimeline is a capsule taken by mouth, usually three times a day. It is absorbed well, with peak levels in about 1.5 to 2 hours, and its effect on saliva starts within about an hour. It binds little to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down by CYP2D6 and CYP3A4, mainly to sulfoxide products, and by glucuronidation. The metabolites leave mainly in the urine. Its half-life is about 5 hours. People who are poor CYP2D6 metabolizers, or who take CYP2D6 or CYP3A4 inhibitors, can have higher levels.", "sidefx": "Nearly every side effect is the muscarinic effect showing up in the wrong place. Sweat glands cause excess sweating and can lead to dehydration. The eye effects cause blurred night vision. Airway receptors cause bronchospasm in people with lung disease. Heart effects cause slow rate or conduction changes. Gut effects cause nausea and diarrhea. Smooth muscle stimulation can cause biliary or kidney stone colic. Severe cholinergic toxicity can be reversed with atropine, a muscarinic antagonist.", "src": ["Evoxac (cevimeline) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "dronedarone": {"origin": "Amiodarone, developed in Belgium in the 1960s, is one of the most effective drugs for keeping the heart in normal rhythm, but its iodine content and fat-loving nature cause thyroid, lung, liver and skin damage. Chemists at Sanofi set out to build a safer cousin. They removed the iodine atoms and added a methanesulfonamide group that makes the molecule less fat-soluble, giving it a much shorter half-life. The result was dronedarone. Early trials were mixed: the ANDROMEDA study in patients with severe heart failure was stopped early because more patients died on the drug. The ATHENA trial then showed fewer cardiovascular hospitalizations in atrial fibrillation patients. The FDA approved dronedarone (Multaq) in July 2009. The PALLAS trial in 2011 found harm in permanent atrial fibrillation, which led to a stronger boxed warning.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Amiodarone, the parent compound, is developed in Belgium."], ["2003", "The ANDROMEDA trial in severe heart failure is stopped early for excess deaths."], ["2009", "ATHENA results support FDA approval of dronedarone (Multaq)."], ["2011", "The PALLAS trial shows harm in permanent atrial fibrillation."]], "cell": "Dronedarone blocks many channels at once, like amiodarone. It blocks several potassium currents (IKr, IKs, IK1 and the acetylcholine-activated IKACh), which slows repolarization in phase 3 and prolongs the action potential and refractory period. It blocks the fast sodium current, which slows conduction, and the L-type calcium current, which slows the sinus and AV nodes. It also blocks alpha and beta adrenergic receptors in a noncompetitive way, reducing the effect of adrenaline on heart rate. Longer refractory periods make it harder for the chaotic reentry circuits of atrial fibrillation to sustain themselves, helping keep the heart in sinus rhythm, while AV nodal slowing controls rate if AF recurs.", "body": [["Atria", "Longer refractory periods in atrial muscle make it harder for atrial fibrillation to start or persist."], ["AV node", "Calcium channel and beta blockade slow AV conduction, keeping the ventricular rate lower during AF."], ["Ventricles", "Longer repolarization shows as a longer QT interval, with a small risk of torsades."], ["Heart pumping", "In decompensated or advanced heart failure, the drug's negative effects on the heart can worsen failure and increase death."], ["Kidneys", "It blocks tubular secretion of creatinine, causing a small creatinine rise without a true drop in kidney function."], ["Liver and lungs", "Rare but serious liver injury and lung inflammation have been reported."]], "journey": "Dronedarone is a tablet taken twice daily with meals; food raises absorption a few times over. Even so, much of the dose is removed on its first pass through the liver. It is more than 98 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A4, including to an active N-debutyl metabolite, so grapefruit and CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels sharply. It leaves mostly in feces. Its half-life is about 13 to 19 hours, far shorter than amiodarone's weeks, and full effect builds over days.", "sidefx": "In heart failure that is decompensated, or in permanent AF, the drug's blocking effects on the heart do more harm than good, doubling the risk of death, stroke and HF hospitalization, which is the boxed warning. Blocking IKr prolongs QT and can rarely trigger torsades. Nodal effects cause slow heart rates. Dronedarone inhibits CYP3A, CYP2D6 and P-glycoprotein, so digoxin, dabigatran and some statins rise. The creatinine bump comes from blocked tubular secretion. Liver injury and lung toxicity are rarer than with amiodarone because there is no iodine and less tissue build-up, but they still occur. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Multaq (dronedarone) FDA label", "Hohnloser SH et al. ATHENA trial, NEJM 2009", "Connolly SJ et al. PALLAS trial, NEJM 2011"]}, "disopyramide": {"origin": "Disopyramide was developed at the US drug company G. D. Searle in the 1960s, when doctors were looking for alternatives to quinidine and procainamide. It was introduced in the US as Norpace in the late 1970s for ventricular arrhythmias. Doctors soon noticed its strong negative effect on heart contraction. That property, a problem in heart failure, became useful in hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy, where a hypercontractile heart blocks its own outflow. Disopyramide became a long-standing off-label option for that condition. The CAST trial in 1989 changed its use: other class I drugs increased deaths after heart attack, and class I agents now carry a boxed warning limiting them to life-threatening ventricular arrhythmias.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Disopyramide is developed at G. D. Searle."], ["1970s", "Disopyramide (Norpace) is introduced in the US."], ["1989", "The CAST trial shows excess deaths with class I antiarrhythmics after heart attack."]], "cell": "Disopyramide is a class Ia antiarrhythmic. It binds open fast voltage-gated sodium channels and blocks the inward sodium current of phase 0, with intermediate on-off kinetics. This slows how fast the electrical impulse spreads, which widens the QRS complex and slows conduction in atria, ventricles and Purkinje fibers. It also blocks the rapid delayed rectifier potassium current (IKr), which slows phase 3 repolarization and lengthens the action potential and QT interval. Longer refractory periods help stop reentry circuits. It also blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors strongly, and it lowers the strength of heart muscle contraction more than most antiarrhythmics.", "body": [["Ventricles and conduction", "Sodium channel blockade slows conduction and widens QRS; potassium blockade prolongs QT, suppressing reentrant arrhythmias."], ["Heart muscle strength", "Strong negative inotropy can worsen heart failure but reduces the outflow obstruction in obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy."], ["Sinus and AV nodes", "Its anticholinergic effect can speed sinus rate and AV conduction, offsetting some direct slowing."], ["Bladder", "Muscarinic blockade relaxes the bladder detrusor, causing hesitancy and retention, especially in older men with large prostates."], ["Eyes and mouth", "Anticholinergic effects cause dry mouth, blurred vision and can provoke angle-closure glaucoma."], ["Blood sugar", "It can cause hypoglycemia, likely by stimulating insulin release from beta cells."]], "journey": "Disopyramide is taken by mouth as immediate- or controlled-release capsules and is absorbed well. It peaks in about 2 hours for the immediate form. Its binding to plasma proteins changes with concentration, so free drug rises more than expected at higher levels. The liver converts part of it by CYP3A4 to a mono-N-dealkylated metabolite with more anticholinergic effect. About half leaves unchanged in the urine. The half-life is about 7 hours and longer in kidney disease.", "sidefx": "Anticholinergic effects (dry mouth, urinary retention, constipation, blurred vision, glaucoma) come from muscarinic blockade. Its strong negative inotropy explains new or worse heart failure and low blood pressure. Potassium channel block prolongs QT and can cause torsades, and sodium channel block can widen QRS and cause ventricular arrhythmias, part of the class I proarrhythmia seen in CAST. Low blood sugar is a known effect. There is no specific antidote; sodium bicarbonate is used for sodium channel blocker toxicity with wide QRS, and magnesium for torsades.", "src": ["Norpace (disopyramide) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "CAST Investigators. NEJM 1989"]}, "mavacamten": {"origin": "Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) is the most common inherited heart disease, often caused by mutations in sarcomere proteins such as beta-myosin. Many of these mutations make the heart contract too hard. In the 2010s scientists at the San Francisco company MyoKardia, founded on work by heart muscle researchers including James Spudich, screened for small molecules that could gently turn down cardiac myosin. Their lead, MYK-461, prevented heart thickening in mice carrying HCM mutations, as reported in Science in 2016. That compound became mavacamten. The EXPLORER-HCM trial showed better exercise capacity and symptoms. Bristol Myers Squibb bought MyoKardia in 2020, and the FDA approved mavacamten (Camzyos) in April 2022 as the first cardiac myosin inhibitor, for symptomatic obstructive HCM.", "timeline": [["1990", "The first HCM-causing mutation, in the beta-myosin heavy chain gene, is reported."], ["2016", "MYK-461 is shown to suppress HCM development in mice."], ["2020", "EXPLORER-HCM trial results are published; BMS acquires MyoKardia."], ["2022", "FDA approves mavacamten (Camzyos), the first cardiac myosin inhibitor."]], "cell": "Heart muscle contracts when myosin heads bind actin, use ATP and pull the filaments past each other. Myosin heads can rest in a super-relaxed state, folded back on the thick filament, using little energy and not available to bind actin. HCM mutations tend to free too many heads, so too many cross-bridges form and the heart contracts too hard and relaxes poorly. Mavacamten binds cardiac beta-myosin and allosterically and reversibly inhibits its ATPase activity. It slows phosphate release and stabilizes the super-relaxed state, so fewer heads join cross-bridges. The heart contracts with less force, the thickened septum pulls less into the outflow tract, the left ventricular outflow tract (LVOT) gradient falls, and filling pressures drop.", "body": [["Left ventricle", "Less hypercontractility lowers the LVOT obstruction gradient and lets the ventricle relax and fill better."], ["Heart pumping", "Ejection fraction falls; if it falls too far, systolic heart failure develops, which is why LVEF is checked by echo."], ["Exercise capacity", "Lower obstruction and filling pressures improved peak oxygen use and symptoms in trials."], ["Lungs", "Lower left heart filling pressures can reduce breathlessness from lung congestion."], ["Brain", "Dizziness and fainting can occur, sometimes from obstruction itself or excessive drop in output."]], "journey": "Mavacamten is a capsule taken by mouth once a day. It is absorbed quickly, peaking in about an hour, and is highly bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP2C19, with help from CYP3A4 and CYP2C9. Its half-life is long, about 6 to 9 days, and about 23 days in people who are CYP2C19 poor metabolizers, so it takes weeks to reach steady state and its effect lasts weeks after stopping. Metabolites leave in urine and feces.", "sidefx": "The main risk follows directly from the mechanism: turning down myosin too far weakens contraction and causes systolic heart failure, which is the boxed warning. Illness such as infection or new atrial fibrillation can further reduce heart function. Because CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels and inducers lower them, drug interactions are dangerous, so it is available only through a REMS program. It can harm a fetus and may reduce the effect of hormonal contraceptives. There is no antidote; the drug is held and the long half-life means recovery takes time.", "src": ["Camzyos (mavacamten) FDA label", "Green EM et al. Science 2016", "Olivotto I et al. EXPLORER-HCM, Lancet 2020"]}, "vericiguat": {"origin": "In the 1980s Robert Furchgott, Louis Ignarro and Ferid Murad showed that nitric oxide (NO) is the signal that relaxes blood vessels, by activating the enzyme soluble guanylate cyclase (sGC). They shared the 1998 Nobel Prize. In heart failure, NO is often in short supply and sGC is less responsive. Bayer chemists, building on an earlier compound called YC-1, developed sGC stimulators that work even with little NO. The first, riociguat, was approved for pulmonary hypertension in 2013. Vericiguat was designed for once-daily use in heart failure. Developed by Bayer and Merck, it reduced the combined risk of cardiovascular death or HF hospitalization in the VICTORIA trial, and the FDA approved it as Verquvo in January 2021 for adults with HFrEF after a recent worsening event.", "timeline": [["1998", "Furchgott, Ignarro and Murad receive the Nobel Prize for nitric oxide signaling."], ["2013", "Riociguat, the first sGC stimulator, is approved."], ["2020", "VICTORIA trial results are published."], ["2021", "FDA approves vericiguat (Verquvo) for heart failure."]], "cell": "Soluble guanylate cyclase is an enzyme inside vascular smooth muscle and heart muscle cells. When NO binds its heme group, sGC turns GTP into cyclic GMP (cGMP). cGMP activates protein kinase G, which lowers intracellular calcium and desensitizes the contractile machinery, so smooth muscle relaxes. In heart failure, oxidative stress and endothelial dysfunction reduce NO and the cGMP signal falls, which contributes to stiff vessels and heart muscle, fibrosis and remodeling. Vericiguat binds sGC at a site separate from the NO site. It directly stimulates the enzyme even without NO, and it stabilizes NO binding so the enzyme responds better to the NO that remains. More cGMP means vasodilation and may improve heart muscle relaxation and remodeling.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "More cGMP relaxes arteries and veins, lowering afterload and preload."], ["Heart", "Restoring cGMP signaling may reduce stiffness, hypertrophy and fibrosis in the failing heart, lowering HF hospitalizations."], ["Blood pressure", "Vasodilation causes a modest fall in blood pressure and sometimes symptomatic hypotension."], ["Blood", "Anemia was more common on vericiguat in trials, for reasons not fully explained."], ["Pregnancy", "Animal studies showed fetal harm, so it is not given in pregnancy."]], "journey": "Vericiguat is a tablet taken once daily with food, which improves absorption. It peaks in about 4 hours and is about 98 percent bound to plasma proteins. Unlike many heart drugs, it is not broken down by CYP enzymes. Instead it is glucuronidated by UGT1A9 and UGT1A1 to an inactive product. Elimination is split between urine and feces. Its half-life is about 30 hours in HF patients, supporting once-daily dosing, and the dose is stepped up over weeks.", "sidefx": "Low blood pressure is the expected result of vasodilation from more cGMP. Combining it with other sGC stimulators or with PDE5 inhibitors, which also raise cGMP, can drop blood pressure too far, so these combinations are avoided. Long-acting nitrates add NO and also raise this risk. Anemia was seen in trials. The boxed warning is for embryo-fetal toxicity seen in animals, so pregnancy is excluded and contraception used. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Verquvo (vericiguat) FDA label", "Armstrong PW et al. VICTORIA trial, NEJM 2020"]}, "lanreotide": {"origin": "Somatostatin was discovered in 1973 by Paul Brazeau, Roger Guillemin and colleagues at the Salk Institute, who found a hypothalamic peptide that blocked the release of growth hormone. Guillemin later shared the 1977 Nobel Prize for his work on brain peptide hormones. Natural somatostatin lasts only a few minutes in blood, so chemists built shorter, stable analogs. Octreotide came first. Lanreotide, a cyclic octapeptide, was developed in the late 1980s in collaboration between Tulane University researchers and the French company Ipsen. Ipsen created a long-acting gel depot that releases the drug slowly for about four weeks. The FDA approved Somatuline Depot in 2007 for acromegaly, and later for gastroenteropancreatic neuroendocrine tumors (2014) and carcinoid syndrome (2017).", "timeline": [["1973", "Brazeau, Guillemin and colleagues discover somatostatin."], ["1980s", "Lanreotide is developed as a stable somatostatin analog."], ["2007", "FDA approves Somatuline Depot for acromegaly."], ["2014", "Lanreotide is approved for GEP neuroendocrine tumors after the CLARINET trial."], ["2017", "FDA approves lanreotide for carcinoid syndrome diarrhea."]], "cell": "Lanreotide mimics somatostatin and binds somatostatin receptors, mainly SSTR2 and SSTR5. These are Gi-coupled receptors. Activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase, lowering cAMP, opens potassium channels and reduces calcium entry. Lower calcium and cAMP reduce hormone release by exocytosis. In the pituitary, this lowers growth hormone release, so the liver makes less IGF-1, which treats acromegaly. In neuroendocrine tumor cells, which often carry many SSTR2 receptors, it lowers release of serotonin and other peptides that cause flushing and diarrhea. Receptor signaling also slows cell division and may trigger cell death, which slows tumor growth. It also suppresses insulin, glucagon, gastrin and gallbladder contraction.", "body": [["Pituitary", "Lower growth hormone and IGF-1 control the overgrowth of acromegaly."], ["Neuroendocrine tumors", "Reduced hormone secretion eases flushing and diarrhea, and antiproliferative effects slow tumor growth."], ["Gallbladder", "Reduced cholecystokinin release and gallbladder contraction cause bile to stagnate, leading to sludge and gallstones."], ["Pancreas", "Suppression of both insulin and glucagon can cause either high or low blood sugar."], ["Gut", "Less pancreatic enzyme secretion and slowed gut motility cause loose stools, fat malabsorption and abdominal pain."], ["Heart and thyroid", "It can slow the heart rate and reduce TSH, causing hypothyroidism."]], "journey": "Lanreotide is given as a deep subcutaneous injection every 4 weeks. The drug forms a gel depot under the skin and is released slowly by passive diffusion, so levels rise within the first day and then stay steady. The apparent half-life of the depot is about 23 to 30 days, which reflects slow release rather than slow breakdown. Once released, it is broken down by peptidases, mostly in the gut and liver, and eliminated mainly through bile and feces.", "sidefx": "Most side effects follow from suppressing gut and pancreatic hormones. Less cholecystokinin and gallbladder emptying cause gallstones and sludge. Less pancreatic enzyme secretion causes fatty stools and diarrhea. Suppressed insulin and glucagon cause high or low blood sugar. Suppressed TSH can cause hypothyroidism. Effects on the sinus node can cause bradycardia, especially with other rate-slowing drugs. Injection site lumps and pain come from the depot. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Somatuline Depot (lanreotide) FDA label", "Brazeau P et al. Science 1973", "Caplin ME et al. CLARINET trial, NEJM 2014"]}, "diazoxide": {"origin": "Diazoxide was made in the late 1950s at the Schering Corporation as part of the thiazide diuretic boom. Chemists created a benzothiadiazine closely related to chlorothiazide but without the sulfonamide group needed for diuresis. To their surprise it lowered blood pressure strongly without causing urine output, and it raised blood sugar. In the 1960s the IV form became a treatment for hypertensive emergencies, and the oral form was found to block insulin release. The oral suspension (Proglycem) became a standard treatment for hypoglycemia caused by too much insulin, such as from insulinomas and congenital hyperinsulinism. Only later did scientists learn that diazoxide works by opening ATP-sensitive potassium channels, the same channels that sulfonylureas close.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Diazoxide is synthesized at Schering as a thiazide relative."], ["1960s", "Its blood pressure-lowering and blood sugar-raising effects are described."], ["1980s", "ATP-sensitive potassium channels are identified as its target in beta cells."]], "cell": "Pancreatic beta cells sense glucose through ATP-sensitive potassium (K-ATP) channels, made of Kir6.2 pore subunits and SUR1 regulatory subunits. When glucose rises, ATP rises and closes the channels. The cell depolarizes, voltage-gated calcium channels open, calcium floods in and insulin granules are released. Diazoxide binds SUR1 and holds K-ATP channels open even when ATP is high. Potassium flows out, the cell stays hyperpolarized, calcium channels stay closed and insulin release is blocked. Blood sugar rises. This is the reverse of how sulfonylureas work. Diazoxide also opens K-ATP channels (SUR2 type) in arteriolar smooth muscle, relaxing those vessels, and it promotes kidney sodium and water retention.", "body": [["Pancreas", "Opening beta cell K-ATP channels blocks insulin release and treats hyperinsulinemic hypoglycemia."], ["Blood sugar", "Less insulin raises glucose and can lead to ketoacidosis or hyperosmolar coma if excessive."], ["Arterioles", "Smooth muscle relaxation dilates arterioles and lowers blood pressure, with reflex tachycardia."], ["Kidneys and fluid", "Sodium and water retention cause edema and can tip people into heart failure; a thiazide is often added to counter it."], ["Hair", "Excess hair growth (hypertrichosis) is common, especially in children, likely from K-ATP opening in hair follicles."], ["Lungs in infants", "Pulmonary hypertension has been reported in infants and newborns."]], "journey": "Oral diazoxide suspension is absorbed well, with effects starting within an hour and lasting up to about 8 hours. It is more than 90 percent bound to plasma proteins. Part is broken down in the liver by oxidation and sulfate conjugation, and the drug and metabolites leave through the kidneys. The half-life is long, about 24 to 36 hours in adults and shorter in children, and longer in kidney disease.", "sidefx": "Fluid retention is the classic problem: kidney sodium retention expands volume, causing edema and sometimes heart failure, which is why a thiazide is often added. Blocking insulin can overshoot, causing high sugar, ketoacidosis or hyperosmolar coma. Vasodilation can cause low blood pressure and fast heart rate. Hair growth, nausea and taste changes are common. Infants can develop pulmonary hypertension. Low platelets and neutrophils can occur. High uric acid comes from reduced kidney urate excretion. There is no antidote; excess hyperglycemia is treated with insulin.", "src": ["Proglycem (diazoxide) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "levocarnitine": {"origin": "Carnitine was discovered in 1905 by the Russian scientists Vladimir Gulevich and Robert Krimberg, who isolated it from meat extract. They named it from the Latin carnis, meaning flesh. For decades its job was unknown. In the 1950s Gottfried Fraenkel found that mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) needed it as a growth factor, so it was briefly called vitamin BT. Around the same time, Irving Fritz and others showed that carnitine is needed for the body to burn long-chain fatty acids in mitochondria. In the 1970s doctors described children with inherited carnitine deficiency and muscle weakness, low blood sugar and heart failure. Pure levocarnitine, the natural L form, was then approved in the US in the 1980s as Carnitor for primary and secondary carnitine deficiency, later including people on dialysis.", "timeline": [["1905", "Gulevich and Krimberg isolate carnitine from meat extract."], ["1950s", "Carnitine is found to be needed by mealworms and named vitamin BT."], ["1955", "Irving Fritz shows carnitine stimulates fatty acid oxidation in liver tissue."], ["1973", "The first human carnitine deficiency cases are reported."]], "cell": "Long-chain fatty acids are activated to acyl-CoA in the cytoplasm, but acyl-CoA cannot cross the inner mitochondrial membrane where beta-oxidation happens. Carnitine is the carrier. Carnitine palmitoyltransferase I (CPT I) on the outer membrane moves the fatty acid from CoA to carnitine, forming acylcarnitine. The carnitine-acylcarnitine translocase carries it across the inner membrane, and CPT II inside the matrix moves the fatty acid back to CoA. The fatty acyl-CoA then enters beta-oxidation, producing acetyl-CoA for energy and, in the liver, ketone bodies. Free carnitine shuttles back out. Carnitine also accepts excess or toxic acyl groups that build up in organic acid disorders, forming acylcarnitines that the kidneys excrete, which frees up CoA inside cells.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Restoring fat burning gives muscle more fuel during exercise and fasting, easing weakness and fatigue."], ["Heart", "Heart muscle relies heavily on fatty acids; replacing carnitine can improve cardiomyopathy in primary carnitine deficiency."], ["Liver", "Fat oxidation and ketone production return, which helps prevent low blood sugar during fasting."], ["Kidneys and dialysis", "Dialysis removes carnitine; replacement can correct low levels in patients on dialysis."], ["Metabolic disorders", "Binding toxic acyl groups helps remove them in urine in organic acidemias and valproate toxicity."], ["Body odor", "Gut bacteria turn unabsorbed oral carnitine into trimethylamine, which can give a fishy odor."]], "journey": "Oral levocarnitine is absorbed partly through the small intestine, with only a modest fraction of a dose taken in. The unabsorbed part is broken down by gut bacteria. Peak blood levels come about 3 hours after an oral dose. IV levocarnitine bypasses the gut and is used in dialysis patients and acute illness. Carnitine is not bound to plasma proteins. It is taken up into muscle and liver, where most of the body's carnitine is stored. The kidneys filter it and normally reabsorb most of it, and excess and acylcarnitines leave in the urine. The IV half-life is about 17 hours.", "sidefx": "Gut upset (nausea, cramps, diarrhea) comes from the large oral amounts in the intestine. Bacteria in the gut metabolize unabsorbed carnitine to trimethylamine, causing a fishy body odor; in severe kidney disease, trimethylamine and its oxide can build up, which is why oral use is avoided there. Seizures have been reported, with or without a prior history, though the mechanism is unclear. Hypersensitivity reactions can occur with the IV form. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Carnitor (levocarnitine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "andexanet-alfa": {"origin": "Direct factor Xa inhibitors such as rivaroxaban and apixaban arrived around 2011 and 2012, but there was no specific way to reverse them in a major bleed. Scientists at Portola Pharmaceuticals in California designed a decoy: a version of human factor Xa that still binds the drugs but cannot clot. They swapped the key serine in the active site for alanine and removed the Gla domain that anchors factor Xa to cell membranes. The ANNEXA-4 study showed it quickly lowered anti-factor Xa activity in bleeding patients. The FDA granted accelerated approval to andexanet alfa (Andexxa) in May 2018. Portola was later acquired by Alexion, part of AstraZeneca. Concerns about blood clots and its confirmatory trial results followed, and andexanet alfa was withdrawn from the US market in 2025. For factor Xa inhibitor reversal in the US, four-factor prothrombin complex concentrate (4F-PCC) is now the usual option.", "timeline": [["2011", "Rivaroxaban, a direct factor Xa inhibitor, is approved in the US."], ["2013", "The design of andexanet as a factor Xa decoy is published."], ["2018", "FDA grants accelerated approval to andexanet alfa (Andexxa)."], ["2025", "Andexanet alfa is withdrawn from the US market."]], "cell": "Factor Xa is the enzyme at the meeting point of the clotting pathways. It joins factor Va on activated platelet membranes to form prothrombinase, which turns prothrombin into thrombin. Apixaban and rivaroxaban bind factor Xa's active site and block it. Andexanet alfa is a modified human factor Xa. Its active site serine is replaced by alanine, so it has no enzyme activity, and its Gla domain is removed, so it cannot join the prothrombinase complex. It still binds factor Xa inhibitors with high affinity, so it soaks them up from the blood like a sponge. Free drug falls, and the patient's own factor Xa is released to work again. It also binds tissue factor pathway inhibitor (TFPI), a natural brake on clotting, which raises tissue factor-driven thrombin generation and adds a procoagulant effect.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Anti-factor Xa activity drops by about 90 percent within minutes of the bolus, and thrombin generation rises."], ["Bleeding sites", "Restored clotting is meant to help stop major bleeding, such as intracranial or gastrointestinal hemorrhage."], ["Arteries and veins", "The procoagulant effect, including TFPI inhibition, raises the risk of heart attack, stroke, DVT and pulmonary embolism."], ["Heparin response", "It can bind heparin-antithrombin complexes and blunt heparin, a problem before cardiac surgery."], ["Lab tests", "Anti-factor Xa activity rises again about 2 hours after the infusion ends as the decoy is cleared."]], "journey": "Andexanet alfa was given as an intravenous bolus followed by a 2-hour infusion. Its effect starts within minutes because it binds circulating drug directly. When the infusion stops, the decoy is cleared, and factor Xa inhibitor still in tissues moves back into blood, so anti-factor Xa activity rises again. As a protein it is broken down by normal protein catabolism, not by the kidneys or liver enzymes. Its terminal half-life is about 5 to 7 hours.", "sidefx": "The boxed warning covered thromboembolism, ischemic events, cardiac arrest and sudden death. These follow from reversing anticoagulation in patients who were anticoagulated because of high clot risk, plus the drug's own procoagulant TFPI binding. Rebound of anti-factor Xa activity after the infusion explains why bleeding could return. Heparin interference made it unsuitable just before cardiopulmonary bypass. Infusion reactions also occurred. With its withdrawal from the US, 4F-PCC is used instead for factor Xa inhibitor reversal.", "src": ["Andexxa (andexanet alfa) FDA label", "Lu G et al. Nature Medicine 2013", "Connolly SJ et al. ANNEXA-4, NEJM 2019"]}, "aminocaproic-acid": {"origin": "Aminocaproic acid came from the work of a Japanese husband-and-wife team, Shosuke Okamoto and Utako Okamoto, who in the 1950s searched for compounds that could stop excessive breakdown of blood clots (fibrinolysis). They found that epsilon-aminocaproic acid, a simple molecule shaped like the amino acid lysine, blocked plasmin formation. Utako Okamoto later discovered tranexamic acid in 1962, about ten times more potent. Aminocaproic acid was approved in the US in the 1960s as Amicar. It became a standard drug for bleeding driven by fibrinolysis, such as after heart surgery, prostate surgery and in some bleeding disorders.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Shosuke and Utako Okamoto identify epsilon-aminocaproic acid as an antifibrinolytic."], ["1962", "Utako Okamoto discovers the more potent tranexamic acid."], ["1960s", "Aminocaproic acid (Amicar) is approved in the US."]], "cell": "Fibrinolysis is the system that dissolves clots. Plasminogen binds to fibrin through lysine-binding sites on its kringle domains. Once bound, tissue plasminogen activator (tPA) or urokinase converts it to plasmin, which cuts fibrin into fragments and dissolves the clot. Aminocaproic acid is a lysine analog. It occupies the lysine-binding sites on plasminogen, so plasminogen cannot attach to fibrin and is activated much less. At high concentrations it also weakly blocks plasmin directly. The result is that formed clots are not broken down as quickly. It does not make new clots; it only protects the ones that form.", "body": [["Clots and bleeding", "Stabilizing fibrin clots reduces bleeding when fibrinolysis is excessive, such as in surgery or certain bleeding disorders."], ["Urinary tract", "The urinary tract is rich in urokinase; blocking it can leave clots in the upper urinary tract, causing obstruction."], ["Muscle", "Long-term use can cause muscle weakness, muscle necrosis and rhabdomyolysis, with high CK."], ["Heart and blood pressure", "Rapid IV injection can cause low blood pressure, slow heart rate and arrhythmias."], ["Vessels", "By stopping clot breakdown it can tip the balance toward thrombosis in at-risk patients."]], "journey": "Aminocaproic acid can be given by mouth or IV and is absorbed well from the gut, peaking in about 1 to 2 hours. It is not significantly bound to plasma proteins and spreads through body water. Very little is metabolized. Most leaves unchanged in the urine, so levels rise in kidney disease. The half-life is about 2 hours, which is why it is given often or by continuous infusion.", "sidefx": "Because it blocks clot breakdown everywhere, clots that form can persist, leading to thrombosis such as heart attack, stroke or pulmonary embolism, especially combined with factor IX complex or anti-inhibitor products. High concentrations in urine can cause clots to stay in the kidney or ureter. Muscle damage with long use is thought to come from a direct toxic effect on muscle. Rapid IV pushes drop blood pressure and slow the heart. Nausea, diarrhea and dizziness are common. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Utako Okamoto had trouble getting doctors in Japan to use tranexamic acid. Decades later, the large CRASH-2 and WOMAN trials showed it saves lives in trauma and childbirth bleeding.", "src": ["Amicar (aminocaproic acid) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "emicizumab": {"origin": "Hemophilia A is caused by a lack of factor VIII, a cofactor that helps factor IXa activate factor X. Replacing factor VIII requires frequent IV infusions, and about a third of severe patients form inhibitor antibodies that neutralize it. Scientists at Chugai Pharmaceutical in Japan, part of the Roche group, had an idea: build an antibody with two different arms, one grabbing factor IXa and the other factor X, so it holds them together the way factor VIII does. After screening many bispecific antibodies and fine-tuning them, they reported the concept in Nature Medicine in 2012. The result, emicizumab, could be injected under the skin weekly or less often. The FDA approved emicizumab (Hemlibra) in November 2017 for hemophilia A with inhibitors and in 2018 for patients without inhibitors.", "timeline": [["2012", "Chugai scientists report a bispecific antibody that mimics factor VIII."], ["2017", "FDA approves emicizumab (Hemlibra) for hemophilia A with inhibitors."], ["2018", "Approval expands to hemophilia A without inhibitors."]], "cell": "In normal clotting, activated factor VIII (FVIIIa) acts as a bridge on the platelet surface, holding activated factor IX (FIXa) and factor X together in the tenase complex. This speeds the activation of factor X to factor Xa by many thousand times, driving a burst of thrombin. Emicizumab is a humanized bispecific IgG4 antibody. One arm binds factor IXa and the other binds factor X, placing them close together so FIXa can activate factor X. Thrombin generation is restored and clots can form. Because emicizumab is not factor VIII and has a different structure, factor VIII inhibitor antibodies do not recognize it. Unlike factor VIII, it does not need activation and is not regulated, so it works continuously.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Restored tenase activity raises thrombin generation, providing steady protection against bleeding."], ["Joints", "Fewer spontaneous joint bleeds protect against the joint damage (hemophilic arthropathy) that drives disability."], ["Lab tests", "Emicizumab shortens the aPTT strongly and makes one-stage factor VIII assays and Bethesda inhibitor tests unreliable."], ["Small vessels", "Combined with large amounts of aPCC, it can cause thrombotic microangiopathy and clots."], ["Injection site", "Local reactions are common with subcutaneous injections."]], "journey": "Emicizumab is injected under the skin, first with weekly loading doses and then weekly, every two weeks or every four weeks. It is absorbed slowly through the lymph, peaking 1 to 2 weeks after a dose. Like other IgG antibodies it is protected by the neonatal Fc receptor, which gives a long half-life of about 4 weeks. It is cleared by protein catabolism rather than by the kidneys or liver enzymes. Steady protection is reached after several weeks.", "sidefx": "Emicizumab is not designed to treat acute bleeding; breakthrough bleeds still need bypassing agents or factor VIII. When patients also received activated prothrombin complex concentrate (aPCC) at high cumulative amounts, the combined procoagulant push caused thrombotic microangiopathy and thrombosis, which is the boxed warning. aPCC contains factor IXa and factor X, the very substrates emicizumab binds. Lab interference occurs because the aPTT reaction is driven by emicizumab instead of factor VIII. Injection site reactions, headache and joint pain are common. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Hemlibra (emicizumab-kxwh) FDA label", "Kitazawa T et al. Nature Medicine 2012"]}, "treprostinil": {"origin": "Prostacyclin was discovered in 1976 by Salvador Moncada, John Vane and colleagues at the Wellcome Research Laboratories in England. It is a natural substance made by blood vessel lining cells that relaxes vessels and stops platelets from sticking. As a drug (epoprostenol), it transformed pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) care in the 1990s, but it breaks down in minutes and needs constant IV infusion through a central line. Treprostinil is a more stable synthetic analog first studied at Burroughs Wellcome. United Therapeutics, founded in 1996 by Martine Rothblatt after her daughter was diagnosed with PAH, licensed and developed it. The FDA approved subcutaneous treprostinil (Remodulin) in 2002, inhaled treprostinil (Tyvaso) in 2009 and the oral tablet (Orenitram) in 2013.", "timeline": [["1976", "Moncada, Vane and colleagues discover prostacyclin."], ["1996", "Martine Rothblatt founds United Therapeutics."], ["2002", "FDA approves subcutaneous treprostinil (Remodulin)."], ["2009", "Inhaled treprostinil (Tyvaso) is approved."], ["2013", "Oral treprostinil (Orenitram) is approved."]], "cell": "In PAH, the small lung arteries narrow and thicken, partly from too little prostacyclin and nitric oxide and too much endothelin and thromboxane. Treprostinil binds the prostacyclin (IP) receptor, a Gs-coupled receptor on vascular smooth muscle and platelets. Gs activates adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP. In smooth muscle, cAMP activates protein kinase A, which lowers intracellular calcium and inhibits myosin light chain kinase, so the vessel relaxes. In platelets, higher cAMP blocks activation and aggregation. Prostacyclin signaling also slows smooth muscle proliferation. The net effect is lower pulmonary vascular resistance, less strain on the right ventricle and better cardiac output. Treprostinil also binds some other prostaglandin receptors, such as DP1 and EP2.", "body": [["Lung arteries", "Vasodilation and less smooth muscle growth lower pulmonary vascular resistance and pressure."], ["Right heart", "Lower resistance reduces right ventricle workload and raises cardiac output, improving exercise capacity."], ["Systemic vessels", "Systemic vasodilation causes flushing, headache and sometimes low blood pressure."], ["Platelets", "Inhibited aggregation raises bleeding risk, especially with anticoagulants."], ["Jaw and muscles", "Jaw pain, a typical prostacyclin side effect, is common, as are leg and muscle pain."], ["Gut", "Prostaglandin-like effects on the gut cause diarrhea and nausea."]], "journey": "Treprostinil can be given by continuous subcutaneous or IV infusion, inhaled several times a day, or swallowed as extended-release tablets. Subcutaneous absorption is nearly complete. It is about 91 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP2C8, so gemfibrozil raises levels and rifampin lowers them. Metabolites leave mainly in urine. The half-life is about 4 hours, much longer than epoprostenol's minutes, but effects still wane after stopping, so infusions must not be interrupted.", "sidefx": "Headache, flushing, jaw pain, diarrhea and nausea are all prostacyclin receptor effects on vessels and the gut. Subcutaneous infusion often causes severe pain at the infusion site from local prostaglandin effects. Platelet inhibition raises bleeding risk. Systemic vasodilation can drop blood pressure. IV central lines can lead to bloodstream infections and sepsis. Stopping suddenly removes vasodilation and can cause rebound PAH, a crisis for the right heart. Inhaled forms cause cough and throat irritation. There is no antidote; the infusion rate is adjusted for side effects.", "src": ["Remodulin, Tyvaso and Orenitram (treprostinil) FDA labels", "Moncada S et al. Nature 1976"]}, "riociguat": {"origin": "Nitric oxide relaxes blood vessels by activating soluble guanylate cyclase (sGC), a discovery recognized by the 1998 Nobel Prize to Furchgott, Ignarro and Murad. In pulmonary hypertension, NO signaling is weak. In 1994 researchers found that a compound called YC-1 could stimulate sGC directly. Building on that, Bayer scientists, including Johannes-Peter Stasch, created more potent sGC stimulators. Riociguat was the first to reach patients. In the CHEST-1 trial in chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension (CTEPH) and PATENT-1 in pulmonary arterial hypertension, it improved walking distance. The FDA approved riociguat (Adempas) in October 2013, and it became the first drug approved for inoperable or persistent CTEPH.", "timeline": [["1994", "YC-1 is found to stimulate soluble guanylate cyclase directly."], ["1998", "Nobel Prize awarded for nitric oxide as a signaling molecule."], ["2013", "FDA approves riociguat (Adempas), the first sGC stimulator, for CTEPH and PAH."]], "cell": "Soluble guanylate cyclase is the receptor for nitric oxide inside smooth muscle cells. When NO binds its heme group, sGC converts GTP to cyclic GMP (cGMP). cGMP activates protein kinase G, which lowers calcium, relaxes smooth muscle and limits cell growth and scarring. Riociguat acts on sGC in two ways. It sensitizes sGC to the body's own NO by stabilizing the NO-sGC bond, and it directly stimulates sGC at a separate site, even when NO is low. More cGMP relaxes the pulmonary arteries and slows the abnormal growth of vessel wall cells. This lowers pulmonary vascular resistance and the load on the right ventricle. Its effect does not depend on cGMP breakdown being blocked, which is how PDE5 inhibitors work.", "body": [["Lung arteries", "More cGMP relaxes pulmonary arteries and lowers vascular resistance in PAH and CTEPH."], ["Right heart", "Less resistance lowers right ventricle strain and improves output and walking distance."], ["Systemic vessels", "Systemic vasodilation lowers blood pressure, causing dizziness and sometimes symptomatic hypotension."], ["Lungs", "Serious bleeding, including coughing up blood, has been reported; pulmonary edema can occur in pulmonary veno-occlusive disease."], ["Gut", "Dyspepsia, nausea and reflux come from relaxation of gut smooth muscle, including the lower esophageal sphincter."], ["Pregnancy", "It caused fetal harm in animals, which is the boxed warning."]], "journey": "Riociguat is a tablet taken three times a day, with nearly complete absorption and peak levels in about 1.5 hours. It is about 95 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down by several enzymes, including CYP1A1, CYP3A4, CYP2C8 and CYP2J2. CYP1A1 is induced by tobacco smoke, so smokers have lower levels. Strong CYP and P-gp/BCRP inhibitors raise levels. It leaves in urine and feces, with a half-life of about 12 hours in patients. The dose is stepped up over weeks.", "sidefx": "Low blood pressure follows from raising cGMP in systemic vessels. Combining riociguat with PDE5 inhibitors or nitrates, which also raise cGMP, can cause dangerous hypotension, so these combinations are contraindicated. Headache, dizziness and gut symptoms reflect smooth muscle relaxation. Bleeding, including hemoptysis, has occurred. The boxed warning is for embryo-fetal toxicity seen in animals, so pregnancy is excluded and contraception used. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Adempas (riociguat) FDA label", "Ghofrani HA et al. CHEST-1 and PATENT-1, NEJM 2013"]}, "ambrisentan": {"origin": "Endothelin-1 was discovered in 1988 by Masashi Yanagisawa, Tomoh Masaki and colleagues at the University of Tsukuba in Japan. It proved to be one of the most powerful blood vessel constrictors known. People with pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) have high endothelin levels in their lungs. Drug companies quickly built endothelin receptor antagonists. Ambrisentan was discovered at the German company Knoll (later part of Abbott) and was developed by Myogen, a Colorado company that Gilead Sciences bought in 2006. It was designed to block the ETA receptor selectively. After the ARIES trials, the FDA approved ambrisentan (Letairis) in June 2007 for PAH. Later, the AMBITION trial showed that starting it together with tadalafil worked better than either alone.", "timeline": [["1988", "Yanagisawa, Masaki and colleagues discover endothelin-1."], ["2001", "Bosentan becomes the first endothelin receptor antagonist approved for PAH."], ["2007", "FDA approves ambrisentan (Letairis) for PAH."], ["2015", "AMBITION trial supports initial combination with tadalafil."], ["2025", "FDA removes the endothelin receptor antagonist pregnancy REMS."]], "cell": "Endothelin-1 is made by blood vessel lining cells. It acts on two receptor types. ETA receptors on vascular smooth muscle are Gq-coupled: they raise intracellular calcium, causing strong constriction, and they drive smooth muscle growth and scarring. ETB receptors on endothelial cells release nitric oxide and prostacyclin and help clear endothelin from blood, although ETB on smooth muscle can also constrict. Ambrisentan is a selective, competitive ETA antagonist. By blocking ETA it stops endothelin-induced constriction and growth of pulmonary artery smooth muscle, while leaving ETB-driven vasodilation and endothelin clearance largely intact. Pulmonary vascular resistance falls, and over time the vessels may remodel less.", "body": [["Lung arteries", "ETA blockade relaxes and slows thickening of pulmonary arteries, lowering pulmonary vascular resistance."], ["Right heart", "Lower resistance reduces strain on the right ventricle and improves walking distance and time to worsening."], ["Fluid balance", "Peripheral edema and fluid retention are common, possibly from endothelin effects on kidney sodium handling and vessel leak."], ["Blood", "Hemoglobin often falls, partly from hemodilution."], ["Nose and sinuses", "Vasodilation in nasal tissue causes congestion and sinusitis."], ["Reproduction", "Endothelin signaling is vital to fetal development, so it causes birth defects; sperm counts can also fall."]], "journey": "Ambrisentan is a once-daily tablet that is absorbed quickly, peaking in about 2 hours. It is about 99 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver handles it mainly by glucuronidation (UGTs) and partly by oxidation through CYP3A4 and CYP2C19. It leaves mainly through bile and feces, not the kidneys. The effective half-life is about 9 hours. Cyclosporine raises its levels. Clinical benefit appears over days to weeks.", "sidefx": "Endothelin is essential for normal formation of the face, heart and great vessels in the embryo, so blocking it causes severe birth defects. This is the boxed warning, and pregnancy must be excluded with effective contraception used. Fluid retention and edema are common and can worsen heart failure. Lower hemoglobin reflects hemodilution and perhaps effects on red cell production. Vasodilation causes headache, flushing and nasal congestion. In pulmonary veno-occlusive disease, dilating arteries upstream of blocked veins can cause pulmonary edema. Liver injury is a class concern but less common than with bosentan. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Letairis (ambrisentan) FDA label", "Yanagisawa M et al. Nature 1988", "Galie N et al. AMBITION, NEJM 2015"]}, "macitentan": {"origin": "Macitentan was created at Actelion, a Swiss company founded in 1997 by Jean-Paul Clozel, Martine Clozel and colleagues who had worked on endothelin at Roche. Actelion's first drug, bosentan, was the first endothelin receptor antagonist approved for pulmonary arterial hypertension in 2001, but it caused liver injury. Actelion chemists set out to make a successor with better tissue penetration, longer receptor binding and less liver toxicity. The result was macitentan. The SERAPHIN trial, one of the first PAH trials built around long-term outcomes rather than walking distance, showed fewer serious events or deaths. The FDA approved macitentan (Opsumit) in October 2013. Johnson & Johnson acquired Actelion in 2017, and a single tablet with tadalafil (Opsynvi) was approved in 2024.", "timeline": [["1988", "Endothelin-1 is discovered in Japan."], ["2001", "Actelion's bosentan becomes the first ERA approved for PAH."], ["2013", "SERAPHIN results lead to FDA approval of macitentan (Opsumit)."], ["2017", "Johnson & Johnson acquires Actelion."], ["2024", "Macitentan with tadalafil (Opsynvi) is approved as a single tablet."]], "cell": "Endothelin-1 is a peptide made by endothelial cells in excess in PAH. It binds ETA and ETB receptors on pulmonary artery smooth muscle. Both are G protein-coupled receptors that raise intracellular calcium, causing constriction, and activate growth pathways that thicken the vessel wall and promote fibrosis. Macitentan is a dual ETA and ETB antagonist. It competes with endothelin for both receptors. Compared with older antagonists, it binds the receptor for longer (slow dissociation) and penetrates tissue well. It is also converted by CYP3A4 to an active metabolite, aprocitentan, which adds to its effect. Blocking endothelin signaling relaxes the pulmonary arteries and slows the remodeling that drives PAH progression.", "body": [["Lung arteries", "Blocking both endothelin receptors reduces constriction and vessel wall thickening, lowering pulmonary vascular resistance."], ["Right heart and outcomes", "Less resistance unloads the right ventricle; in SERAPHIN this meant fewer hospitalizations, disease worsening events and deaths combined."], ["Blood", "Hemoglobin falls, sometimes to anemia, likely from hemodilution and effects on red cell production."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises and rare liver failure can occur, though less often than with bosentan."], ["Fluid balance", "Edema and fluid retention can occur, a class effect of endothelin blockade."], ["Reproduction", "Endothelin is needed for fetal development, so it causes birth defects, and it may lower sperm counts."]], "journey": "Macitentan is a once-daily tablet. It is absorbed slowly, peaking in about 8 hours, and is more than 99 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver converts it mainly by CYP3A4 to aprocitentan, an active metabolite with a long half-life of about 48 hours. The parent drug's half-life is about 16 hours. Strong CYP3A4 inducers such as rifampin lower exposure, and strong inhibitors raise it. Metabolites leave in urine and feces.", "sidefx": "The boxed warning is embryo-fetal toxicity, since endothelin signaling is needed to form the face, heart and great vessels; pregnancy is excluded and contraception used. Anemia reflects hemoglobin decline from hemodilution and perhaps marrow effects. Liver injury is a class effect of endothelin antagonists, so liver tests are checked. Fluid retention and edema can occur. Vasodilation causes headache and nasal congestion. In pulmonary veno-occlusive disease, it can cause pulmonary edema. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Aprocitentan, the active metabolite of macitentan, was later developed as a medicine of its own and approved in 2024 as Tryvio for hard-to-control high blood pressure.", "src": ["Opsumit (macitentan) FDA label", "Pulido T et al. SERAPHIN trial, NEJM 2013"]}, "selexipag": {"origin": "Prostacyclin-based drugs are among the most effective treatments for pulmonary arterial hypertension, but natural prostacyclin and its analogs are unstable or need infusions or many inhaled doses. Scientists at Nippon Shinyaku in Kyoto, Japan, designed a different kind of molecule: one that is not built on the prostaglandin skeleton at all but still activates the prostacyclin (IP) receptor selectively. The result was selexipag, a prodrug converted in the body to a more potent active form. Developed with Actelion, it was tested in GRIPHON, at the time the largest PAH outcome trial, which showed fewer disease progression events. The FDA approved selexipag (Uptravi) as an oral tablet in December 2015, and an IV form followed in 2021 for patients temporarily unable to take tablets.", "timeline": [["1976", "Prostacyclin is discovered by Moncada, Vane and colleagues."], ["2000s", "Nippon Shinyaku designs selexipag, a nonprostanoid IP receptor agonist."], ["2015", "GRIPHON results are published, and FDA approves oral selexipag (Uptravi)."], ["2021", "An IV form of selexipag is approved."]], "cell": "Selexipag is hydrolyzed by carboxylesterase 1 in the liver to its active metabolite (ACT-333679), which is about 37 times more potent at the prostacyclin receptor. Both bind the IP receptor on pulmonary artery smooth muscle and platelets. The IP receptor is Gs-coupled: it activates adenylyl cyclase and raises cAMP. cAMP activates protein kinase A, which lowers intracellular calcium and inhibits myosin light chain kinase, relaxing the muscle. In platelets cAMP blocks aggregation, and in smooth muscle it slows proliferation. Because selexipag is not a prostanoid, it is selective for the IP receptor and does not strongly activate other prostaglandin receptors such as EP3, which can constrict vessels and upset the gut.", "body": [["Lung arteries", "Raising cAMP relaxes pulmonary arteries and slows vessel wall thickening."], ["Disease course", "In GRIPHON it lowered the risk of a combined endpoint of death or PAH disease progression."], ["Head and face", "Systemic vasodilation causes headache and flushing, especially during dose titration."], ["Jaw and muscles", "Jaw pain, muscle aches and limb pain are classic prostacyclin-receptor side effects."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea and vomiting are common and often limit the dose."], ["Thyroid and blood", "Hyperthyroidism and anemia have been reported."]], "journey": "Selexipag is taken by mouth twice daily, and the dose is raised step by step to the highest tolerated level. It is absorbed well, with the parent drug peaking in 1 to 3 hours and the active metabolite in 3 to 4 hours. Carboxylesterase 1 converts it to the active form, which is then broken down by CYP2C8 and CYP3A4 and by glucuronidation. Strong CYP2C8 inhibitors such as gemfibrozil sharply raise active metabolite levels. The parent half-life is about 1 to 2.5 hours and the active metabolite's about 6 to 13 hours. Both are highly protein bound and leave mainly in feces.", "sidefx": "Headache, diarrhea, jaw pain, nausea, muscle pain and flushing all come from IP receptor activation in vessels, gut and muscle; they are most common while doses are being raised. Systemic vasodilation can lower blood pressure. In pulmonary veno-occlusive disease, dilating arteries upstream of blocked veins can cause pulmonary edema. Hyperthyroidism and anemia have been reported. There is no antidote; doses are reduced if side effects are not tolerated.", "src": ["Uptravi (selexipag) FDA label", "Sitbon O et al. GRIPHON trial, NEJM 2015"]}, "inclisiran": {"origin": "Inclisiran brings together two discoveries. In 1998 Andrew Fire and Craig Mello showed that double-stranded RNA can silence a matching gene, a process called RNA interference (RNAi), and they shared the 2006 Nobel Prize. In 2003 French researchers led by Catherine Boileau and Marianne Abifadel linked mutations in the PCSK9 gene to high LDL cholesterol, and in 2005 and 2006 Jonathan Cohen and Helen Hobbs in Dallas showed that people with loss-of-function PCSK9 variants have low LDL and fewer heart attacks. Alnylam Pharmaceuticals designed a small interfering RNA against PCSK9 with a GalNAc sugar tag that targets the liver. The Medicines Company licensed and developed it, and Novartis bought that company in 2020. The FDA approved inclisiran (Leqvio) in December 2021, with injections twice a year after the first doses.", "timeline": [["1998", "Fire and Mello describe RNA interference."], ["2003", "PCSK9 mutations are linked to familial hypercholesterolemia."], ["2006", "Cohen and Hobbs report that low PCSK9 function protects against heart disease."], ["2021", "FDA approves inclisiran (Leqvio)."]], "cell": "PCSK9 is a protein made by liver cells. It binds LDL receptors on the hepatocyte surface, and when the receptor is pulled into the cell with LDL, PCSK9 sends it to the lysosome to be destroyed instead of recycled. Less receptor means less LDL cleared from blood. Inclisiran is a double-stranded siRNA linked to a GalNAc cluster. GalNAc binds the asialoglycoprotein receptor on hepatocytes, so the drug enters liver cells. There the guide strand loads into the RNA-induced silencing complex (RISC). RISC uses the guide to find PCSK9 messenger RNA and cuts it. Each RISC complex can cut many mRNA copies, so PCSK9 production falls for months. With less PCSK9, more LDL receptors recycle to the surface and LDL cholesterol falls by about half.", "body": [["Liver", "Hepatocytes make less PCSK9 and keep more LDL receptors on their surface, clearing LDL from blood."], ["Blood lipids", "LDL cholesterol falls by about 50 percent, with effects lasting about 6 months between doses."], ["Arteries", "Lower LDL is expected to slow atherosclerosis; outcome trial results were not available at first approval."], ["Injection site", "Mild pain, redness or rash at the injection site are the most common effects."], ["Joints and airways", "Joint pain, bronchitis and urinary tract infections were slightly more common in trials."]], "journey": "Inclisiran is injected under the skin by a health professional, then again at 3 months and every 6 months after that. It peaks in plasma about 4 hours after injection and is mostly gone from blood within a day or two, with a plasma half-life of about 9 hours, because the liver takes it up fast. Inside liver cells RISC-bound siRNA lasts for months, which explains the long effect. It is broken down by nucleases, not CYP enzymes, so drug interactions are minimal. About 16 percent leaves unchanged in urine.", "sidefx": "Inclisiran has few side effects because its action is confined to the liver and to one gene. Injection site reactions come from local inflammation. Because it acts through RNA silencing rather than CYP pathways, it has few drug interactions, and it does not cause the muscle symptoms linked to statins. Joint pain, urinary infections and bronchitis were slightly more frequent than with placebo. There is no antidote, and its effect fades only as new PCSK9 production recovers over months.", "src": ["Leqvio (inclisiran) FDA label", "Fire A, Mello CC et al. Nature 1998", "Ray KK et al. ORION-10 and ORION-11, NEJM 2020"]}, "bempedoic-acid": {"origin": "Bempedoic acid comes from Esperion Therapeutics in Michigan, founded by Roger Newton, a scientist who had helped discover atorvastatin (Lipitor) at Parke-Davis. Many patients stop statins because of muscle aches, so Esperion looked for a way to lower cholesterol production without acting in muscle. Their compound, first called ETC-1002, turned out to block ATP-citrate lyase, an enzyme a few steps before the statin target, and to be activated only in the liver. The FDA approved bempedoic acid (Nexletol) and its combination with ezetimibe (Nexlizet) in February 2020 for lowering LDL. In 2023 the CLEAR Outcomes trial in statin-intolerant patients showed fewer heart attacks and other major cardiovascular events, and the label was updated in 2024 to include cardiovascular risk reduction.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Esperion develops ETC-1002, later named bempedoic acid."], ["2020", "FDA approves bempedoic acid (Nexletol) and bempedoic acid with ezetimibe (Nexlizet)."], ["2023", "CLEAR Outcomes trial shows fewer cardiovascular events in statin-intolerant patients."], ["2024", "The label is updated to include cardiovascular risk reduction."]], "cell": "Cholesterol is made in liver cells from acetyl-CoA. ATP-citrate lyase (ACL) is the enzyme that makes cytoplasmic acetyl-CoA from citrate exported out of mitochondria, supplying the pathway that later passes through HMG-CoA reductase, the statin target. Bempedoic acid is a prodrug. In the liver, very-long-chain acyl-CoA synthetase 1 (ACSVL1) attaches CoA to it, forming bempedoyl-CoA, the active form, which inhibits ACL. Less acetyl-CoA means less cholesterol synthesis in hepatocytes. Low intracellular cholesterol activates SREBP-2, which increases LDL receptor production. More LDL receptors pull more LDL from blood, so LDL cholesterol falls. ACSVL1 is essentially absent in skeletal muscle, so the drug is not activated there, which helps explain why muscle side effects are uncommon.", "body": [["Liver", "Reduced cholesterol synthesis increases LDL receptor levels, clearing LDL from blood."], ["Blood lipids", "LDL falls by about 17 to 25 percent alone, more with ezetimibe, and further when added to a statin."], ["Arteries and heart", "In statin-intolerant patients, lower LDL translated into fewer heart attacks and coronary procedures."], ["Kidneys and uric acid", "It competes with uric acid for the kidney transporter OAT2, raising uric acid and gout risk."], ["Tendons", "Tendon rupture, a rare effect, was more common in trials, especially in older adults or with steroids or fluoroquinolones."], ["Muscle", "Because it is not activated in muscle, muscle aches are not more common than placebo."]], "journey": "Bempedoic acid is a once-daily tablet. It is absorbed by mouth and peaks in about 3.5 hours. It is about 99 percent bound to plasma proteins. In the liver ACSVL1 activates it, and it is also converted to an active metabolite (ESU-1) and glucuronidated. Most of a dose leaves in urine as metabolites, with some in feces. The half-life is about 21 hours. LDL lowering is seen within 2 to 4 weeks. It modestly raises simvastatin and pravastatin levels.", "sidefx": "Raised uric acid and gout happen because bempedoic acid and its metabolite inhibit OAT2, a kidney transporter involved in uric acid handling. Small rises in creatinine and BUN have a similar transporter-related cause. Tendon rupture is rare, with an unclear mechanism, and is more likely with steroids, fluoroquinolones, age over 60 or kidney failure. Small drops in hemoglobin and rises in liver enzymes have been seen. Muscle symptoms are uncommon because the drug is not activated in muscle. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Nexletol (bempedoic acid) FDA label", "Nissen SE et al. CLEAR Outcomes, NEJM 2023"]}, "icosapent-ethyl": {"origin": "In the 1970s the Danish doctors Hans Olaf Bang and Jorn Dyerberg studied the Inuit of Greenland, who ate a diet rich in fish and seal fat yet seemed to have little heart disease. They found high levels of omega-3 fatty acids, especially eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA), in their blood. Interest in fish oil for the heart followed, but many trials of mixed EPA and DHA supplements showed little benefit. Amarin, an Irish-American company, developed icosapent ethyl, a highly purified ethyl ester of EPA alone, without DHA. The FDA approved it as Vascepa in July 2012 for very high triglycerides. In 2018 the REDUCE-IT trial showed a 25 percent lower risk of major cardiovascular events in high-risk patients on statins, and in December 2019 the FDA approved it to reduce cardiovascular risk.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Bang and Dyerberg study omega-3 fatty acids in Greenland Inuit."], ["2012", "FDA approves icosapent ethyl (Vascepa) for severe hypertriglyceridemia."], ["2018", "REDUCE-IT shows a 25 percent reduction in major cardiovascular events."], ["2019", "FDA approves icosapent ethyl for cardiovascular risk reduction."]], "cell": "The exact mechanism is not fully defined. EPA lowers triglycerides mainly by reducing how much triglyceride-rich VLDL the liver makes and releases. Proposed steps include inhibiting acyl-CoA:1,2-diacylglycerol acyltransferase (DGAT), the enzyme that finishes triglyceride assembly; increasing burning of fatty acids in mitochondria and peroxisomes; reducing new fat synthesis; and increasing lipoprotein lipase activity, which clears triglycerides from circulating particles. The cardiovascular benefit in REDUCE-IT seemed larger than the triglyceride drop alone would predict. EPA built into cell membranes and plaques may stabilize them, reduce inflammation and oxidation, and change platelet function, since EPA-derived eicosanoids are less pro-clotting than those from arachidonic acid. Because it contains no DHA, LDL does not rise significantly.", "body": [["Liver", "Less VLDL production and more fatty acid oxidation lower blood triglycerides."], ["Arteries", "EPA in plaques and membranes may reduce inflammation and oxidation, contributing to fewer heart attacks and strokes."], ["Platelets", "EPA shifts eicosanoid production away from thromboxane A2, slightly reducing platelet aggregation and raising bleeding risk."], ["Heart rhythm", "Atrial fibrillation and flutter were more common in REDUCE-IT, especially in people with prior AF."], ["Muscles and joints", "Musculoskeletal pain and joint pain were reported in trials."], ["Fluid balance", "Peripheral edema was reported more often than with placebo."]], "journey": "Icosapent ethyl comes as capsules taken with meals. In the intestine, the ethyl ester is cleaved off and free EPA is absorbed through the lymph into the blood. EPA is built into plasma lipids, red cell membranes and tissues, which is why levels build over weeks. It is more than 99 percent bound to plasma proteins. EPA is broken down mainly in the liver by beta-oxidation, like other fats, to carbon dioxide and water. It is not removed by the kidneys. The half-life of EPA in plasma is long, about 89 hours.", "sidefx": "Bleeding risk follows from EPA's effects on platelets, and is higher with anticoagulants or antiplatelet drugs. The higher rate of atrial fibrillation is not fully explained, but omega-3 fatty acids can alter ion channel function in atrial cells. Joint and muscle pain, edema, constipation and gout were also reported. People with fish or shellfish allergy could theoretically react, though the oil is highly purified. There is no antidote.", "fun": "In REDUCE-IT the placebo was mineral oil, which raised LDL and inflammatory markers a little; some experts argued this made the benefit look bigger, a debate that continues.", "src": ["Vascepa (icosapent ethyl) FDA label", "Bhatt DL et al. REDUCE-IT, NEJM 2019"]}, "indomethacin": {"origin": "Indomethacin was discovered at Merck in the early 1960s by a team led by the chemist Tsung-Ying Shen. Inflammation research at the time pointed to serotonin, and the team built indole compounds, related to serotonin, testing hundreds of them in animal models of inflammation. Indomethacin stood out as very potent. It was approved in the US in 1965 as Indocin. In 1971 John Vane showed that aspirin-like drugs work by blocking prostaglandin synthesis, which explained how indomethacin worked. Because prostaglandin E2 keeps the fetal ductus arteriosus open, doctors reasoned that blocking it could close a patent ductus in premature babies. In 1976 two US groups, Heymann, Rudolph and Silverman and Friedman and colleagues, reported success, and IV indomethacin became a standard NICU treatment for PDA.", "timeline": [["1963", "Tsung-Ying Shen's group at Merck discovers indomethacin."], ["1965", "Indomethacin (Indocin) is approved in the US."], ["1971", "John Vane shows NSAIDs block prostaglandin synthesis."], ["1976", "Reports show indomethacin can close a patent ductus arteriosus in preterm infants."]], "cell": "Indomethacin reversibly blocks both cyclooxygenase enzymes, COX-1 and COX-2, which turn arachidonic acid into prostaglandin H2, the parent of prostaglandins and thromboxane. In the fetus, the ductus arteriosus is held open by PGE2 and prostacyclin acting on EP4 receptors on ductal smooth muscle, which raise cAMP and relax the muscle. After birth, oxygen and falling prostaglandin levels close it, but in preterm babies it often stays open. By cutting PGE2, indomethacin lets ductal smooth muscle constrict and close. In adults, less PGE2 at injured tissue reduces swelling and pain, less PGE2 in the hypothalamus lowers fever, and less platelet thromboxane A2 weakens aggregation.", "body": [["Ductus arteriosus", "Loss of PGE2 lets the ductus constrict and close in preterm infants."], ["Kidneys", "Prostaglandins keep kidney blood flow up when it is stressed; blocking them lowers GFR, causing oliguria, sodium and water retention and high potassium."], ["Gut", "Lower mucosal prostaglandins and mesenteric blood flow raise the risk of bleeding, perforation and necrotizing enterocolitis in neonates and ulcers in adults."], ["Brain", "It reduces cerebral blood flow; in adults it can cause headache and dizziness more often than other NSAIDs."], ["Platelets", "Reduced thromboxane impairs platelet function and raises bleeding risk."], ["Joints and soft tissue", "Strong anti-inflammatory action helps gout flares, ankylosing spondylitis and other inflammatory pain."]], "journey": "Indomethacin is absorbed well by mouth, peaking in about 2 hours, and can also be given rectally or IV. For neonates it is given IV. It is about 99 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down by O-demethylation, N-deacylation and glucuronidation, and part of it is recycled through bile into the gut and reabsorbed (enterohepatic recirculation). Metabolites leave in urine and feces. The adult half-life is about 4.5 hours. In preterm infants it is much longer and highly variable, about 12 to 20 hours depending on age.", "sidefx": "All the main toxicities come from losing prostaglandins. In the kidney, blocked vasodilating prostaglandins lower filtration, causing oliguria, rising creatinine, low sodium and high potassium, which is why urine output is checked before each neonatal dose. In the gut, less mucosal protection and lower mesenteric flow cause bleeding, perforation and NEC. Reduced platelet thromboxane causes bleeding. Adult oral forms carry the NSAID boxed warnings for heart attack and stroke and for GI bleeding. Central nervous system effects such as headache are prominent. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "src": ["Indocin (indomethacin) FDA label", "Indomethacin for injection FDA label", "Heymann MA, Rudolph AM, Silverman NH. NEJM 1976"]}, "desflurane": {"origin": "Desflurane was one of hundreds of fluorinated compounds made in the 1960s and 1970s by Ross Terrell and colleagues at Airco (Ohio Medical Products), the same program that produced enflurane and isoflurane. It sat on the shelf for years because it was hard to make (it requires elemental fluorine) and boils near room temperature, so a normal vaporizer could not handle it. Interest returned in the 1980s as outpatient surgery grew and anesthesiologists wanted quicker wake-ups. Desflurane, with very low blood solubility, gave just that. A special heated, pressurized vaporizer was built for it, and the FDA approved it as Suprane in 1992. In recent years its very high global warming potential has led many hospitals and some countries to phase it out.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Ross Terrell's group synthesizes fluorinated ethers, including desflurane."], ["1980s", "Desflurane is revisited for fast-recovery anesthesia."], ["1992", "FDA approves desflurane (Suprane)."], ["2020s", "Health systems begin phasing out desflurane for climate reasons."]], "cell": "Like other volatile anesthetics, desflurane acts on several ion channels rather than one receptor. It enhances inhibitory GABA-A receptors, increasing chloride flow and hyperpolarizing neurons. It also enhances glycine receptors, especially in the spinal cord. It activates two-pore-domain potassium channels such as TREK-1, which also hyperpolarize neurons. It inhibits excitatory NMDA glutamate receptors and neuronal nicotinic receptors. The combined effect dampens neural activity in the brain to produce unconsciousness and amnesia, while effects in the spinal cord produce immobility to pain. Its very low blood:gas partition coefficient (about 0.42) means only a little needs to dissolve in blood before brain levels rise, so it acts and wears off fast.", "body": [["Brain", "Rapid onset of unconsciousness and amnesia, with fast wake-up and return of thinking after it is stopped."], ["Spinal cord", "Glycine and GABA-A effects in the cord prevent movement in response to surgical pain."], ["Airway", "It is pungent and irritates the airway, causing cough, breath holding and laryngospasm, so it is not used for gas induction."], ["Heart and vessels", "It lowers blood pressure by vasodilation; rapid increases in concentration can trigger a sympathetic surge with fast heart rate and higher pressure."], ["Breathing", "It depresses breathing and blunts the response to carbon dioxide."], ["Brain blood flow", "It dilates cerebral vessels, raising cerebral blood flow and possibly intracranial pressure."]], "journey": "Desflurane is breathed in and moves quickly from the lungs into the blood and then the brain. Its low solubility in blood and tissues means brain levels track inhaled levels closely, with onset in 1 to 2 minutes. It is almost entirely removed by breathing it out unchanged; only about 0.02 percent is metabolized in the liver, the least of any modern volatile agent. Emergence often happens within about 5 to 10 minutes of stopping. Because it boils at about 23 degrees C, it needs a heated, pressurized vaporizer.", "sidefx": "Malignant hyperthermia can occur in genetically susceptible people because volatile agents trigger uncontrolled calcium release through mutant ryanodine receptors in muscle; dantrolene is the antidote. In children with some muscle diseases, it can cause severe hyperkalemia and cardiac arrest. Its pungency explains cough and laryngospasm. Vasodilation lowers blood pressure, and fast concentration increases cause sympathetic surges. It can prolong QT. Dry CO2 absorbents can break it down to carbon monoxide. Postoperative nausea and vomiting are common.", "fun": "Desflurane has the highest global warming potential of the common anesthetic gases, and an hour of anesthesia with it has been compared to driving a car hundreds of miles.", "src": ["Suprane (desflurane) FDA label", "Miller's Anesthesia", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "isoflurane": {"origin": "Isoflurane was synthesized in 1965 by Ross Terrell at Airco as part of a large search for safer fluorinated ether anesthetics. It is a structural isomer of enflurane, which was introduced first. Isoflurane's release was delayed by a mid-1970s study suggesting it caused liver cancer in mice. That finding could not be confirmed when the work was repeated, and the FDA approved isoflurane as Forane in 1979. It became one of the most widely used inhaled anesthetics in the world because it is stable, cheap, and causes little heart rhythm sensitization or liver toxicity compared with halothane. It is still common in human and veterinary anesthesia and in ICU sedation in some countries.", "timeline": [["1965", "Ross Terrell synthesizes isoflurane at Airco."], ["1970s", "A mouse cancer concern delays approval and is later not confirmed."], ["1979", "FDA approves isoflurane (Forane)."]], "cell": "Isoflurane acts on several targets at once. It enhances GABA-A receptors, increasing chloride currents that quiet neurons, and enhances glycine receptors in the spinal cord. It opens two-pore-domain potassium channels, hyperpolarizing neurons. It inhibits excitatory NMDA glutamate receptors and neuronal nicotinic acetylcholine receptors. Together these effects reduce neural activity and communication in the cortex, thalamus and brainstem, producing unconsciousness and amnesia, while spinal cord actions produce immobility. Its blood:gas partition coefficient of about 1.4 is higher than desflurane or sevoflurane, so it takes a bit longer to build up and wash out. Its MAC is about 1.15 percent in middle-aged adults.", "body": [["Brain", "Produces unconsciousness and amnesia; above about 1 MAC it dilates cerebral vessels and raises cerebral blood flow."], ["Spinal cord", "Prevents movement in response to surgical stimulation through glycine and GABA-A actions."], ["Heart and vessels", "Lowers blood pressure mainly by reducing systemic vascular resistance, with relatively preserved cardiac output and sometimes a faster heart rate."], ["Airways", "It is a bronchodilator, but its pungent smell irritates the airway during inhaled induction."], ["Breathing", "Depresses ventilation and the response to carbon dioxide and low oxygen."], ["Muscle and uterus", "Relaxes skeletal muscle and the uterus, which can increase bleeding after delivery."]], "journey": "Isoflurane is inhaled and taken up from the lungs into the blood, with surgical anesthesia in about 7 to 10 minutes. Its moderate solubility means a bit more must dissolve in blood and tissues before brain levels rise. Nearly all of it is breathed out unchanged. About 0.2 percent is metabolized in the liver by CYP2E1 to trifluoroacetic acid and small amounts of fluoride, far less than halothane. Wake-up typically takes 10 to 20 minutes after stopping, longer after long cases as fat stores release it.", "sidefx": "Malignant hyperthermia can be triggered in susceptible people with ryanodine receptor mutations, causing runaway muscle calcium release, rigidity and hyperthermia; dantrolene is the antidote. In some muscle disorders, it can cause hyperkalemic cardiac arrest. Vasodilation lowers blood pressure. Raised cerebral blood flow can increase intracranial pressure. Rare liver injury may relate to immune reactions to trifluoroacetylated liver proteins, the same mechanism as halothane hepatitis but far less common. Dry CO2 absorbents can produce carbon monoxide. Nausea, shivering and cough are common.", "src": ["Forane (isoflurane) FDA label", "Miller's Anesthesia", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "iloprost": {"origin": "Iloprost was developed by the German company Schering AG in the late 1970s and 1980s as a stable synthetic version of prostacyclin, the vessel-relaxing molecule discovered by Moncada and Vane in 1976. Natural prostacyclin lasts only minutes, while iloprost was chemically stabilized. It was first used in Europe as an IV drug for severe peripheral vascular disease and Raynaud phenomenon. An inhaled form for pulmonary arterial hypertension, Ventavis, was approved by the FDA in 2004. Doctors in Europe also used IV iloprost for severe frostbite, and studies suggested it reduced amputations. In February 2024 the FDA approved IV iloprost (Aurlumyn) to reduce the risk of finger or toe amputation in adults with severe frostbite, the first FDA-approved frostbite treatment.", "timeline": [["1976", "Prostacyclin is discovered."], ["1980s", "Schering AG develops iloprost, a stable prostacyclin analog."], ["2004", "FDA approves inhaled iloprost (Ventavis) for PAH."], ["2024", "FDA approves IV iloprost (Aurlumyn) for severe frostbite."]], "cell": "Iloprost binds the prostacyclin (IP) receptor, a Gs-coupled receptor on vascular smooth muscle and platelets. Gs activates adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP. In smooth muscle, cAMP activates protein kinase A, which lowers calcium and inhibits myosin light chain kinase, so arteries and arterioles relax. In platelets, higher cAMP blocks activation, shape change and aggregation. In frostbite, cold injury damages endothelium and leads to vasospasm, platelet clumping and microvascular clots that cut off blood flow after rewarming. Iloprost reopens small vessels, keeps platelets from plugging them, and may protect endothelial cells and reduce inflammation. Better microcirculation preserves tissue that would otherwise die.", "body": [["Small vessels", "Vasodilation and platelet inhibition restore blood flow in frostbitten fingers and toes, lowering the chance of amputation."], ["Lung arteries", "Inhaled iloprost dilates pulmonary arteries, lowering pulmonary vascular resistance in PAH."], ["Blood pressure", "Systemic vasodilation can lower blood pressure, sometimes causing dizziness or fainting."], ["Head and face", "Headache and flushing come from dilation of head and neck vessels."], ["Heart", "Palpitations and fast heart rate can occur as a reflex to lower blood pressure."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are common prostanoid effects."]], "journey": "For frostbite, iloprost is given as an IV infusion over several hours daily for several days, adjusted to tolerability. It reaches steady state within minutes of a rate change, and effects fade within about 30 minutes after stopping because its half-life is short, about 20 to 30 minutes. It is about 60 percent bound to plasma proteins. It is broken down mainly by beta-oxidation of its carboxyl side chain, not by CYP enzymes, and metabolites leave mostly in urine. Moderate or severe liver disease slows its breakdown.", "sidefx": "Nearly all side effects come from IP receptor activation in the wrong place. Systemic vasodilation causes headache, flushing, dizziness and low blood pressure. Reflex sympathetic activation causes palpitations and fast heart rate. Prostanoid effects on the gut cause nausea and vomiting. Platelet inhibition raises bleeding risk, especially with anticoagulants or antiplatelet drugs. These effects are the reason the infusion is started low and adjusted. There is no antidote, but the short half-life means effects fade quickly when the infusion is slowed or stopped.", "src": ["Aurlumyn (iloprost) FDA label", "Ventavis (iloprost) FDA label", "Cauchy E et al. NEJM 2011"]}, "cerliponase-alfa": {"origin": "Batten disease, a group of inherited brain disorders also called neuronal ceroid lipofuscinoses, is named after the British neurologist Frederick Batten, who described affected children in 1903. CLN2 disease is the late-infantile form, in which toddlers lose speech, walking and vision and develop seizures. In 1997 Peter Lobel, David Sleat and colleagues at Rutgers identified the missing enzyme as tripeptidyl peptidase 1 (TPP1). Because a replacement enzyme cannot cross the blood-brain barrier, BioMarin Pharmaceutical developed a recombinant TPP1 to be delivered directly into the fluid-filled spaces of the brain through a surgically placed reservoir. The FDA approved cerliponase alfa (Brineura) in April 2017, the first treatment for CLN2 and the first enzyme therapy given into the brain's ventricles.", "timeline": [["1903", "Frederick Batten describes the disease later named after him."], ["1997", "Tripeptidyl peptidase 1 is identified as the enzyme missing in CLN2 disease."], ["2017", "FDA approves cerliponase alfa (Brineura), the first CLN2 treatment."], ["2024", "Approval expands to younger children, including those before symptoms."]], "cell": "Tripeptidyl peptidase 1 (TPP1) is a lysosomal enzyme that clips three-amino-acid pieces off the ends of proteins being recycled. Without it, waxy storage material called ceroid lipopigment builds up in the lysosomes of neurons, and neurons, especially in the cortex and cerebellum, die. Cerliponase alfa is a recombinant human TPP1 proenzyme, an inactive precursor. Delivered into the cerebrospinal fluid, it is taken up by neurons and glia through mannose-6-phosphate receptors and carried to lysosomes. The acidic environment there cuts off the pro-segment and activates the enzyme, which breaks down the stored proteins. Less storage slows neuron death, so loss of motor and language skills slows.", "body": [["Brain", "Restoring TPP1 activity in neurons slowed the decline in walking and language compared with natural history data."], ["Heart rhythm", "Slow heart rate and low blood pressure can occur during infusions, possibly from effects on brainstem autonomic centers."], ["Brain fluid", "CSF protein and white cell counts can rise, reflecting irritation from the infusions."], ["Nervous system", "Seizures, irritability and headache were reported, though seizures are also part of CLN2."], ["Device site", "The implanted reservoir and catheter can become infected or fail, leading to meningitis."]], "journey": "Cerliponase alfa is given every other week as an infusion into the lateral ventricle of the brain through an implanted reservoir and catheter, followed by a flush. This bypasses the blood-brain barrier completely. It spreads through the cerebrospinal fluid and into brain tissue, with CSF levels peaking at the end of infusion. Only small amounts reach the blood. Like other proteins it is eventually broken down in cells. Its effect is measured over months.", "sidefx": "Many risks relate to the delivery route. The implanted device is a foreign body, so infection, meningitis, leaks and blockage can occur, requiring strict sterile technique. Infusing fluid into the ventricles may affect autonomic centers, explaining slow heart rate and low blood pressure, which is why heart monitoring is used in some patients. As a foreign protein, it can trigger hypersensitivity including anaphylaxis, which is the boxed warning. Fever, vomiting and CSF changes reflect irritation of the brain lining. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Brineura (cerliponase alfa) FDA label", "Sleat DE et al. Science 1997", "Schulz A et al. NEJM 2018"]}, "delandistrogene-moxeparvovec": {"origin": "Duchenne muscular dystrophy is named after the French neurologist Guillaume Duchenne, who described it in the 1860s. In 1986 and 1987 Louis Kunkel's lab at Boston Children's Hospital identified the gene and its protein, dystrophin, a giant rod-shaped protein that anchors muscle fibers. The dystrophin gene is too large to fit in an adeno-associated virus (AAV) vector, so researchers built shortened micro-dystrophin genes that keep the most important parts. At Nationwide Children's Hospital in Ohio, Jerry Mendell and Louise Rodino-Klapac developed a micro-dystrophin gene in an AAVrh74 vector with a muscle-specific promoter. Sarepta Therapeutics licensed and developed it. The FDA granted accelerated approval to delandistrogene moxeparvovec (Elevidys) in June 2023 for young ambulatory boys and expanded it in 2024. In 2025, reports of deaths from acute liver failure led to stronger safety warnings and limits on its use.", "timeline": [["1860s", "Guillaume Duchenne describes the muscular dystrophy named after him."], ["1987", "Louis Kunkel's group identifies dystrophin."], ["2023", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Elevidys for ambulatory boys aged 4 to 5."], ["2024", "FDA expands the Elevidys indication."], ["2025", "Fatal acute liver failure cases lead to stronger safety warnings."]], "cell": "In Duchenne muscular dystrophy, mutations in the DMD gene prevent production of dystrophin. Dystrophin links the actin skeleton inside muscle fibers to the dystrophin-associated glycoprotein complex in the cell membrane, which connects to the matrix outside. Without it, the membrane tears with each contraction, calcium floods in, and fibers die and are replaced by fat and scar. Elevidys is a non-replicating AAVrh74 vector carrying a micro-dystrophin gene controlled by the MHCK7 promoter, which is active in skeletal and heart muscle. After one IV infusion, the vector enters muscle cells and its DNA stays in the nucleus mostly as an episome, not integrated into chromosomes. Muscle cells then make micro-dystrophin, a shortened protein that keeps the actin-binding and membrane-anchoring regions, aiming to stabilize the membrane and slow muscle damage.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Micro-dystrophin is expressed in muscle fibers, intended to protect the membrane and slow functional decline."], ["Heart", "The promoter is active in heart muscle; myocarditis has been reported, possibly from immune reactions to vector or transgene."], ["Liver", "AAV vectors are taken up heavily by the liver, and immune responses can cause acute liver injury, including fatal liver failure."], ["Immune system", "Corticosteroids are given around the infusion to blunt immune reactions; preexisting antibodies to AAVrh74 can block treatment."], ["Muscle inflammation", "Immune-mediated myositis has occurred in patients with certain DMD gene deletions."], ["Gut", "Vomiting, nausea and poor appetite are common in the weeks after infusion."]], "journey": "Elevidys is given once as an IV infusion, with the amount based on body weight. The vector spreads through the blood, is taken up by muscle cells throughout the body and also by the liver. Micro-dystrophin expression builds over weeks. The vector DNA persists as episomes in long-lived muscle cells and is meant to last, though it can be lost as damaged fibers die. Vector DNA is shed in urine, saliva and especially stool for weeks. It is not metabolized like a small molecule.", "sidefx": "The liver takes up much of the vector, and the immune system can attack transduced liver cells, causing acute liver injury and, in some cases, fatal liver failure, so steroids are given and liver tests followed weekly. Immune responses to the capsid or transgene can also inflame heart muscle (myocarditis) and skeletal muscle (myositis). Vomiting and nausea are common early. Complement activation and thrombocytopenia can occur. Steroids raise infection risk. Because it is a one-time gene transfer, its effects cannot be reversed.", "src": ["Elevidys (delandistrogene moxeparvovec-rokl) FDA label", "Hoffman EP, Brown RH, Kunkel LM. Cell 1987"]}, "fosdenopterin": {"origin": "Molybdenum cofactor deficiency (MoCD) is an ultra-rare inherited disease in which newborns develop intractable seizures and severe brain damage within days. In type A, the first step of cofactor synthesis, run by the MOCS1 gene, fails, so the intermediate called cyclic pyranopterin monophosphate (cPMP) is never made. The German biochemist Guenter Schwarz and colleagues, studying this pathway, showed that cPMP given back could rescue mice lacking MOCS1. In 2008 an infant in Australia with MoCD type A received cPMP purified from bacteria, and doctors reported a dramatic biochemical response. A synthetic version, fosdenopterin, was developed and later brought forward by Origin Biosciences (part of BridgeBio). The FDA approved fosdenopterin (Nulibry) in February 2021, the first therapy for MoCD type A, to reduce the risk of death.", "timeline": [["2004", "cPMP is shown to rescue MOCS1-deficient mice."], ["2008", "An infant with MoCD type A is first treated with cPMP."], ["2021", "FDA approves fosdenopterin (Nulibry), the first therapy for MoCD type A."]], "cell": "Molybdenum cofactor is built in steps. MOCS1 turns GTP into cyclic pyranopterin monophosphate (cPMP). Later steps add sulfur to make molybdopterin, then load molybdenum to make the finished cofactor. The cofactor is needed by sulfite oxidase, xanthine dehydrogenase and aldehyde oxidase. Sulfite oxidase is the critical one: it converts toxic sulfite to sulfate. Without it, sulfite and S-sulfocysteine build up. S-sulfocysteine resembles glutamate and overstimulates NMDA receptors, causing excitotoxic neuron death and seizures. Fosdenopterin is synthetic cPMP. Given by vein, it enters cells and supplies the missing intermediate, so the remaining steps can make cofactor. Sulfite oxidase activity returns, sulfite and S-sulfocysteine fall, and further brain injury is limited.", "body": [["Brain", "Lower S-sulfocysteine and sulfite reduce excitotoxic injury, decreasing seizures and slowing encephalopathy when started early."], ["Survival", "Treated patients survived longer than untreated natural history controls."], ["Urine markers", "Urinary S-sulfocysteine and xanthine levels move toward normal, showing the cofactor is working."], ["Skin", "Animal studies suggested phototoxicity, so sun and UV exposure are limited."], ["Central line", "Daily infusions need long-term IV access, which brings a risk of catheter infections and bacteremia."]], "journey": "Fosdenopterin is given as a daily IV infusion, ideally starting soon after diagnosis in newborns. It peaks at the end of the infusion and has low binding to plasma proteins. It is taken up by cells and incorporated into molybdenum cofactor, with the rest broken down nonenzymatically. Its half-life is short, about 1.2 to 1.7 hours, and it is mainly cleared by the kidneys. Biochemical improvement appears within days.", "sidefx": "Most serious problems relate to the long-term central line needed for daily infusions: catheter infections, bacteremia and line complications. Fever, respiratory infections, vomiting and gastroenteritis were common in this very ill group. Animal studies showed a potential for phototoxicity, so the label calls for limiting sun and artificial UV exposure. The drug cannot reverse brain damage that has already happened, which is why early treatment matters. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Nulibry (fosdenopterin) FDA label", "Veldman A et al. Pediatrics 2010"]}, "atidarsagene-autotemcel": {"origin": "Metachromatic leukodystrophy (MLD) is an inherited disease in which a missing enzyme, arylsulfatase A (ARSA), lets sulfatide lipids build up and destroy myelin, the insulating layer on nerves. Children with early-onset MLD lose the ability to walk, talk and swallow and often die in childhood. Bone marrow transplants helped only some patients. At the San Raffaele Telethon Institute for Gene Therapy (SR-Tiget) in Milan, Italy, Alessandra Biffi, Luigi Naldini and colleagues developed a way to add a working ARSA gene to a child's own blood stem cells with a lentiviral vector. Their 2013 report in Science showed protection in children treated before symptoms. Orchard Therapeutics developed the therapy, approved in the EU as Libmeldy in 2020. The FDA approved atidarsagene autotemcel (Lenmeldy) in March 2024, the first FDA-approved treatment for MLD.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Arylsulfatase A deficiency is identified as the cause of MLD."], ["2013", "Biffi and colleagues report lentiviral stem cell gene therapy for MLD in Science."], ["2020", "The therapy is approved in the EU as Libmeldy."], ["2024", "FDA approves atidarsagene autotemcel (Lenmeldy)."]], "cell": "ARSA is a lysosomal enzyme that removes a sulfate group from sulfatides, a lipid of myelin. Without it, sulfatides build up in oligodendrocytes, Schwann cells and macrophages, killing myelin-making cells and causing progressive demyelination. To make Lenmeldy, a child's own CD34+ blood stem and progenitor cells are collected and, in a lab, a lentiviral vector inserts a working ARSA gene into their DNA. The child receives busulfan chemotherapy to clear space in the bone marrow, then gets the modified cells back by IV. The cells engraft and make ARSA at high levels. Some of their progeny become microglia-like cells in the brain. These secrete ARSA, which neighboring brain cells take up through mannose-6-phosphate receptors (cross-correction), so sulfatides can be broken down and myelin is protected.", "body": [["Brain and nerves", "Supplying ARSA from engrafted cells and brain microglia slows or prevents demyelination when given before major symptoms."], ["Motor and cognitive function", "Many children treated early kept the ability to walk and develop language, unlike untreated siblings."], ["Bone marrow", "Busulfan conditioning wipes out existing marrow, causing a period of very low blood counts and infection risk."], ["Liver", "Veno-occlusive disease can occur, mainly linked to busulfan conditioning."], ["Blood vessels", "Thrombosis and thromboembolic events, including stroke, have been reported."], ["Long-term", "Because the vector integrates into DNA, there is a theoretical risk of cancer from insertional mutagenesis, so monitoring continues for years."]], "journey": "Lenmeldy is a one-time IV infusion of the child's own gene-modified stem cells, given after busulfan conditioning. The cells home to the bone marrow and engraft over weeks, then make blood cells that carry the ARSA gene for life. ARSA activity in blood rises over months, and gene-modified cells reach the brain as microglia. Because the cells divide and persist, there is no metabolism or elimination in the usual sense.", "sidefx": "Many early side effects, such as febrile neutropenia, mouth sores and infections, come from busulfan conditioning, which destroys the bone marrow before new cells engraft. Veno-occlusive disease of the liver is also a conditioning effect. Delayed platelet recovery causes bleeding risk, and engraftment failure is possible. Thrombosis and encephalitis have been reported. Insertional oncogenesis is a theoretical risk because the lentiviral vector integrates into chromosomes, so blood cancer monitoring continues for at least 15 years. Hypersensitivity to the DMSO preservative can occur.", "fun": "At its US launch, Lenmeldy carried a list price of about 4.25 million dollars, making it one of the most expensive medicines in the world.", "src": ["Lenmeldy (atidarsagene autotemcel) FDA label", "Biffi A et al. Science 2013"]}, "alirocumab": {"origin": "The PCSK9 story began in 2003, when Marianne Abifadel, Catherine Boileau and colleagues in Paris found that gain-of-function PCSK9 mutations cause familial high cholesterol. In 2005 and 2006, Jonathan Cohen and Helen Hobbs in Dallas showed that people with loss-of-function PCSK9 variants have low LDL cholesterol for life and far fewer heart attacks, and that some healthy people have no working PCSK9 at all. That made PCSK9 an attractive drug target. Regeneron used its VelocImmune mice, engineered to make fully human antibodies, to create alirocumab, developed with Sanofi. The FDA approved alirocumab (Praluent) in July 2015, about a month before evolocumab, making it the first PCSK9 inhibitor approved in the US. In 2018 the ODYSSEY OUTCOMES trial showed fewer cardiovascular events after acute coronary syndrome.", "timeline": [["2003", "PCSK9 mutations are linked to familial hypercholesterolemia."], ["2006", "Cohen and Hobbs show low PCSK9 function protects against coronary disease."], ["2015", "FDA approves alirocumab (Praluent), the first US PCSK9 inhibitor."], ["2018", "ODYSSEY OUTCOMES shows fewer cardiovascular events after ACS."]], "cell": "LDL receptors on liver cells bind LDL particles from blood and carry them inside. Normally the receptor then releases LDL in the endosome and recycles to the surface to catch more, many times over. PCSK9 is a protein released by the liver that binds the LDL receptor on the cell surface. When PCSK9 is attached, the receptor is sent to the lysosome and destroyed instead of recycling. Alirocumab is a fully human IgG1 monoclonal antibody that binds free PCSK9 in blood and blocks its binding to LDL receptors. More receptors survive and recycle, so the liver clears more LDL from blood. LDL cholesterol falls by roughly half or more, and lipoprotein(a) falls modestly too. Statins raise PCSK9 levels, which is part of why the two work well together.", "body": [["Liver", "Hepatocytes keep more LDL receptors on their surface and take up more LDL from blood."], ["Blood lipids", "LDL cholesterol falls by about 45 to 60 percent, and lipoprotein(a) also falls."], ["Arteries and heart", "Lower LDL slows atherosclerosis; after ACS it reduced heart attacks, strokes and related events."], ["Nose and throat", "Nasopharyngitis and flu-like symptoms were slightly more common in trials."], ["Skin and immune system", "Injection site reactions and, rarely, hypersensitivity reactions such as angioedema and vasculitis can occur."]], "journey": "Alirocumab is injected under the skin every 2 or 4 weeks, often by the patient with a pen. It is absorbed slowly through the lymph and peaks in about 3 to 7 days. At lower levels it is cleared mainly by binding PCSK9 (target-mediated clearance); at higher levels, by the usual slow breakdown of antibodies into peptides and amino acids. Its half-life is about 17 to 20 days. LDL falls within 1 to 2 weeks. It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes, so drug interactions are minimal.", "sidefx": "Because its action is confined to PCSK9 in blood, side effects are few. Injection site redness, itching and swelling come from local reactions to the injected protein. Allergic reactions, including rare angioedema and hypersensitivity vasculitis, reflect the immune system reacting to the antibody. Cold-like symptoms were slightly more common. Very low LDL levels have not been linked to clear harm in trials, consistent with people born without working PCSK9. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Some of the evidence for PCSK9 inhibition came from a healthy woman in Texas, an aerobics instructor and mother, who had two broken copies of PCSK9 and an LDL cholesterol level far lower than normal.", "src": ["Praluent (alirocumab) FDA label", "Abifadel M et al. Nature Genetics 2003", "Schwartz GG et al. ODYSSEY OUTCOMES, NEJM 2018"]}, "prademagene-zamikeracel": {"origin": "Recessive dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa (RDEB) is a severe inherited skin disease in which the skin blisters and tears with slight friction, leaving chronic wounds and a high risk of skin cancer. It is caused by mutations in COL7A1, the gene for type VII collagen, which forms anchoring fibrils that attach the outer skin to the layer below. At Stanford University, a team including Peter Marinkovich and Jean Tang took patients' own skin cells, added a working COL7A1 gene with a retroviral vector, and grew the cells into sheets that could be grafted onto chronic wounds. Early results showed durable healing in many grafted areas. Abeona Therapeutics developed the therapy, and the FDA approved prademagene zamikeracel (Zevaskyn) in April 2025 for wounds in adults and children with RDEB.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Mutations in COL7A1 are identified as the cause of dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa."], ["2016", "Stanford researchers report early results of gene-corrected skin grafts in RDEB."], ["2025", "FDA approves prademagene zamikeracel (Zevaskyn)."]], "cell": "Type VII collagen is made by keratinocytes and fibroblasts and assembles into anchoring fibrils just below the basement membrane. These fibrils loop into the dermis and hold the epidermis in place. In RDEB, faulty or absent type VII collagen means no working anchoring fibrils, so the layers separate and blisters form with minor friction. To make Zevaskyn, a small skin biopsy is taken from the patient. Keratinocytes are grown and transduced with a replication-incompetent gamma-retroviral vector carrying the full-length COL7A1 gene, which integrates into the cells' DNA. The cells are grown into sheets and surgically placed on chronic wounds. The grafted cells make functional type VII collagen, which forms anchoring fibrils and attaches the new skin firmly, so the wounds can close and resist blistering.", "body": [["Skin wounds", "Grafted gene-corrected sheets cover chronic wounds and produce type VII collagen, leading to wound closure in many treated areas."], ["Skin structure", "New anchoring fibrils hold the epidermis to the dermis, so treated skin blisters less."], ["Pain and itch", "Healed wounds caused less pain; procedural pain and itching were the most common side effects."], ["Immune system", "Antibiotics and reagents used in manufacture can cause hypersensitivity in sensitive patients."], ["Long-term", "Because the vector integrates into DNA, there is a theoretical risk of cancer from insertional mutagenesis."]], "journey": "Zevaskyn is not swallowed or injected. Sheets of the patient's own gene-corrected keratinocytes are placed onto prepared wounds in a surgical procedure under anesthesia. The cells attach and grow as part of the patient's skin over weeks. Because it is a living graft rather than a drug in blood, there is essentially no systemic absorption, distribution or elimination. Wound closure is judged months later, and many grafts remain durable.", "sidefx": "Procedural pain and itching come from the surgery and wound healing. Grafts may fail to take. Hypersensitivity can occur to vancomycin, amikacin or other reagents used in making the sheets. Because the gamma-retroviral vector integrates into the cells' DNA, it could in theory activate a cancer gene, so long-term monitoring is needed. Human- and bovine-derived reagents carry a very small theoretical risk of passing on infections. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Zevaskyn (prademagene zamikeracel) FDA label", "Siprashvili Z et al. JAMA 2016"]}, "ibutilide": {"origin": "Ibutilide was developed at the Upjohn Company in Kalamazoo, Michigan, in the late 1980s and early 1990s. Researchers were seeking a class III antiarrhythmic that could be given IV to quickly convert recent-onset atrial fibrillation or atrial flutter back to normal rhythm without the side effects of older drugs. Its chemistry is related to sotalol, but it has an unusual dual action, blocking potassium current and enhancing a late sodium current. The FDA approved ibutilide (Corvert) in 1995 for rapid conversion of AF or flutter of recent onset. It remains one of the few drugs approved specifically for chemical cardioversion, though its risk of torsades means it is given only with continuous ECG monitoring.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Upjohn researchers develop ibutilide."], ["1995", "FDA approves ibutilide (Corvert) for rapid conversion of atrial fibrillation or flutter."]], "cell": "Ibutilide prolongs repolarization by two mechanisms. It blocks the rapid component of the delayed rectifier potassium current (IKr, carried by hERG channels), which normally returns the cell to rest in phase 3. It also activates a slow inward sodium current during the plateau (phase 2). Both actions keep the cell depolarized longer, lengthening the action potential and effective refractory period in atrial and ventricular muscle. In atrial flutter and fibrillation, reentry circuits need tissue that has recovered and can be excited again. When refractory periods lengthen, the circuits run into tissue that is still refractory and die out, and sinus rhythm returns. It has little effect on heart rate or blood pressure.", "body": [["Atria", "Longer refractory periods break reentry circuits, converting recent-onset AF or flutter to sinus rhythm, flutter more often than AF."], ["Ventricles", "Prolonged repolarization lengthens the QT interval and can trigger torsades de pointes."], ["Heart rate and blood pressure", "Rate and blood pressure change little, unlike with many other antiarrhythmics."], ["Conduction system", "Bundle branch block and AV block can occasionally occur."], ["Head", "Headache is a common, mild side effect, likely from changes in vessel tone."]], "journey": "Ibutilide is given IV over about 10 minutes, and a second dose can be given if the rhythm has not converted. It is not used orally because of very high first-pass metabolism. It spreads widely into tissues and is about 40 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down by oxidation, not through CYP3A4 or CYP2D6. About 82 percent leaves in urine, mostly as metabolites. The half-life averages about 6 hours with a wide range. Conversion usually happens within about 30 minutes.", "sidefx": "The key danger is polymorphic ventricular tachycardia (torsades de pointes), usually within an hour of the infusion. It happens because prolonged repolarization allows early afterdepolarizations, extra electrical activity during the plateau, which can trigger torsades. Risk is higher in women, in heart failure, and with low potassium or magnesium. This is the reason for the boxed warning, correction of electrolytes before use and ECG monitoring for at least 4 hours. Magnesium sulfate and electrical defibrillation are used for torsades. Headache and low blood pressure can occur.", "src": ["Corvert (ibutilide) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "nesiritide": {"origin": "B-type natriuretic peptide (BNP) was discovered in 1988 by Tetsuji Sudoh, Kenji Kangawa, Hisayuki Matsuo and colleagues in Japan, who isolated it from pig brain, which is why it was first called brain natriuretic peptide. It was soon found to come mostly from the ventricles of the heart, released when the heart muscle is stretched. The California company Scios made recombinant human BNP using E. coli and called it nesiritide. The FDA approved nesiritide (Natrecor) in 2001 for acute decompensated heart failure with breathlessness at rest. Concerns about kidney function and survival then arose. The large ASCEND-HF trial, published in 2011, found no benefit on death or rehospitalization and more hypotension, so it is not a first-line therapy today.", "timeline": [["1988", "BNP is discovered in pig brain by Sudoh, Kangawa, Matsuo and colleagues."], ["2001", "FDA approves nesiritide (Natrecor) for acute decompensated heart failure."], ["2011", "ASCEND-HF shows no mortality or rehospitalization benefit."]], "cell": "Natriuretic peptides are the heart's own way of unloading itself. Nesiritide is identical to human BNP. It binds natriuretic peptide receptor-A (NPR-A), a receptor whose inside portion is a guanylate cyclase enzyme. Binding activates the enzyme to make cyclic GMP (cGMP). In vascular smooth muscle, cGMP activates protein kinase G, which lowers intracellular calcium and relaxes the muscle, dilating veins and arteries. This lowers preload and afterload and reduces pulmonary capillary wedge pressure. In the kidney, cGMP signaling increases GFR and reduces sodium reabsorption, producing modest natriuresis. It also suppresses renin, aldosterone and sympathetic activity.", "body": [["Veins", "Venodilation lowers preload and pulmonary capillary wedge pressure, easing lung congestion and breathlessness."], ["Arteries", "Arterial dilation lowers afterload and blood pressure."], ["Kidneys", "It causes modest sodium and water loss, but falling blood pressure can worsen kidney function in some patients."], ["Hormones", "It suppresses renin, aldosterone and norepinephrine, opposing the neurohormonal activation of heart failure."], ["Heart", "Lower filling pressures reduce wall stress; it does not directly stimulate contraction."]], "journey": "Nesiritide is given IV, usually as a bolus followed by a continuous infusion. Most of its effect appears within about 15 minutes. It is removed from blood in three ways: binding to the clearance receptor NPR-C, which pulls it into cells for breakdown; breakdown by the enzyme neprilysin; and a small amount of kidney filtration. Its half-life is about 18 minutes, but its effect on blood pressure can last hours after the infusion stops.", "sidefx": "Low blood pressure is the main side effect and follows directly from cGMP-mediated vasodilation; it can last longer than expected because the hemodynamic effect outlasts plasma levels. Drops in blood pressure can reduce kidney perfusion, explaining rises in creatinine. Headache, nausea and back pain are common. Hypersensitivity can occur with a recombinant protein. Because nesiritide is identical to BNP, it raises BNP measurements, so NT-proBNP is used to track heart failure during therapy. There is no antidote; the infusion is stopped and blood pressure supported.", "src": ["Natrecor (nesiritide) FDA label", "Sudoh T et al. Nature 1988", "O'Connor CM et al. ASCEND-HF, NEJM 2011"]}, "fenoldopam": {"origin": "Fenoldopam was developed at SmithKline Beckman (later SmithKline Beecham) in the late 1970s and 1980s, during research on dopamine receptors. Dopamine itself dilates kidney blood vessels at low levels but activates beta and alpha receptors at higher levels. Researchers wanted a drug that selectively hit the dopamine D1 receptor, which dilates blood vessels, without these other effects. Fenoldopam, a benzazepine derivative, was the result. An oral form was tested but did not succeed because of poor and variable absorption. The IV form was approved by the FDA in 1997 as Corlopam for short-term, in-hospital management of severe hypertension, including hypertensive emergencies.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Fenoldopam is developed at SmithKline as a selective D1 agonist."], ["1997", "FDA approves IV fenoldopam (Corlopam) for severe hypertension."]], "cell": "Fenoldopam is a selective agonist at peripheral dopamine D1 receptors. D1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle are Gs-coupled. Activation turns on adenylyl cyclase and raises cAMP, which activates protein kinase A, lowers calcium and relaxes the smooth muscle. This dilates arterioles, especially in the renal, mesenteric and coronary beds, so systemic vascular resistance and blood pressure fall. In the kidney, D1 activation increases renal blood flow and also acts on tubule cells to reduce sodium reabsorption, causing natriuresis and diuresis. It does not act on D2 or beta receptors and does not cross the blood-brain barrier. It has some alpha-2 adrenergic binding.", "body": [["Arterioles", "D1-mediated vasodilation lowers systemic vascular resistance and blood pressure."], ["Kidneys", "Renal arterioles dilate, raising renal blood flow, and tubular D1 effects cause natriuresis and diuresis."], ["Heart", "Falling blood pressure triggers reflex tachycardia; ST and T wave changes can occur."], ["Eyes", "It can raise intraocular pressure, a concern in glaucoma."], ["Electrolytes", "Natriuresis and diuresis can lower potassium within hours."], ["Head and skin", "Vasodilation causes headache and flushing."]], "journey": "Fenoldopam is given only as a continuous IV infusion. It acts within about 5 minutes and reaches steady state in about 20 minutes. Its half-life is very short, about 5 minutes, so effects fade within about an hour after the infusion stops. The liver breaks it down by conjugation (methylation, glucuronidation and sulfation) rather than CYP enzymes. About 90 percent leaves in urine as metabolites.", "sidefx": "Low blood pressure follows directly from vasodilation. Reflex sympathetic activation causes fast heart rate; combining it with beta blockers can remove that reflex and cause unexpected hypotension. Increased sodium and water loss lowers potassium. Raised intraocular pressure occurs through mechanisms that are not fully clear. Headache and flushing reflect vasodilation of head and skin vessels. The product contains sodium metabisulfite, which can cause reactions in sulfite-sensitive people. There is no antidote; because of the short half-life, effects resolve quickly after stopping.", "src": ["Corlopam (fenoldopam) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "terlipressin": {"origin": "Vasopressin, the body's antidiuretic hormone, was synthesized in the 1950s by Vincent du Vigneaud, who won the 1955 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for his work on peptide hormones. Natural vasopressin is short-acting and has strong effects on the heart. In the 1960s, chemists in Czechoslovakia created terlipressin (triglycyl-lysine vasopressin), a modified form that releases lysine vasopressin slowly and is more selective for V1 receptors. It was used in Europe and elsewhere for decades for bleeding from esophageal varices and later for hepatorenal syndrome, a type of kidney failure in advanced liver disease. In the US it was not available until the CONFIRM trial showed better reversal of hepatorenal syndrome. The FDA approved terlipressin (Terlivaz) in September 2022, the first US-approved treatment for hepatorenal syndrome with rapid reduction in kidney function.", "timeline": [["1954", "Vincent du Vigneaud's group synthesizes vasopressin."], ["1960s", "Terlipressin is developed in Czechoslovakia."], ["1980s", "Terlipressin enters clinical use in Europe for variceal bleeding."], ["2021", "CONFIRM trial results are published."], ["2022", "FDA approves terlipressin (Terlivaz) for hepatorenal syndrome."]], "cell": "Terlipressin is both an active drug and a prodrug. Tissue peptidases slowly cut off its three glycine residues, releasing lysine vasopressin, which also acts on vasopressin receptors. It is about six times more selective for V1 (V1a) than V2 receptors. V1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle are Gq-coupled: activation stimulates phospholipase C, IP3 and calcium release, causing constriction. In cirrhosis, the splanchnic (gut) circulation is massively dilated by nitric oxide and other mediators, which lowers effective blood volume and activates renin and sympathetic systems that clamp down on the kidneys. Terlipressin constricts the splanchnic vessels, shifting blood into the systemic circulation. Mean arterial pressure rises, renal perfusion improves, and kidney vasoconstriction eases, so GFR improves. Splanchnic constriction also lowers portal pressure.", "body": [["Splanchnic circulation", "V1-mediated constriction of gut vessels reduces portal inflow and pressure."], ["Systemic circulation", "Shifting blood into the central circulation raises mean arterial pressure."], ["Kidneys", "Better renal perfusion and reduced renin and sympathetic drive improve GFR and lower creatinine."], ["Lungs", "Higher central volume, especially with albumin, can cause fluid overload and serious or fatal respiratory failure."], ["Heart and limbs", "Vasoconstriction can cause cardiac, intestinal, peripheral and skin ischemia, and bradycardia."], ["Gut", "Abdominal pain, nausea and diarrhea reflect intestinal vasoconstriction and increased motility."]], "journey": "Terlipressin is given IV as a bolus or infusion. It acts within minutes. Tissue peptidases convert it to lysine vasopressin, which peaks about 1 to 2 hours later. The terlipressin half-life is about an hour and lysine vasopressin's about 3 hours, so effects last several hours. Peptidases break both down, and less than 1 percent is excreted unchanged in urine.", "sidefx": "The boxed warning is for serious or fatal respiratory failure. Raising central blood volume while patients also get albumin can flood the lungs, especially in people already overloaded or with severe acute-on-chronic liver failure, so oxygen levels are checked before each dose. Strong V1 vasoconstriction explains ischemia of the heart, gut, limbs and skin, and reflex bradycardia. Abdominal pain and diarrhea reflect gut vasoconstriction and motility. Lowering creatinine can change transplant priority scores. It can harm a fetus. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped and fluids reduced.", "src": ["Terlivaz (terlipressin) FDA label", "Wong F et al. CONFIRM trial, NEJM 2021"]}, "conivaptan": {"origin": "Vasopressin receptor antagonists, called vaptans, grew out of the understanding of how vasopressin controls water. In the 1990s researchers at Yamanouchi Pharmaceutical in Japan (later Astellas) developed conivaptan, a nonpeptide molecule that blocks both V1a and V2 vasopressin receptors. Its main use is treating hyponatremia (low blood sodium), which is often driven by too much vasopressin, such as in SIADH and heart failure. The FDA approved IV conivaptan (Vaprisol) in December 2005 for euvolemic hyponatremia in hospitalized patients, making it the first vaptan approved in the US. In 2007 approval expanded to hypervolemic hyponatremia. Oral tolvaptan, a selective V2 blocker, followed in 2009.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Yamanouchi researchers develop conivaptan, a dual V1a/V2 antagonist."], ["2005", "FDA approves conivaptan (Vaprisol), the first US vaptan."], ["2007", "Approval expands to hypervolemic hyponatremia."]], "cell": "Vasopressin (antidiuretic hormone) acts on V2 receptors on the collecting duct cells of the kidney. V2 is Gs-coupled: it raises cAMP, activating protein kinase A, which moves aquaporin-2 water channels into the cell's luminal membrane. Water then flows from the urine back into the blood, concentrating the urine. When vasopressin is too high, as in SIADH, too much water is kept and sodium is diluted. Conivaptan competitively blocks V2 receptors, so aquaporin-2 stays inside the cell and water is lost in the urine without much sodium or potassium (aquaresis). Serum sodium rises. It also blocks V1a receptors on vascular smooth muscle, where vasopressin causes constriction, so it can lower blood pressure.", "body": [["Kidneys", "V2 blockade causes free water excretion, producing dilute urine and raising serum sodium."], ["Blood sodium", "Serum sodium and osmolality rise, which can improve symptoms of hyponatremia such as confusion."], ["Blood vessels", "V1a blockade can relax vessels and cause hypotension."], ["Brain", "Correcting sodium too fast shrinks brain cells too quickly and can cause osmotic demyelination syndrome."], ["Infusion site", "Irritation and phlebitis are very common at the IV site."], ["Fluid balance", "Thirst, frequent urination and hypovolemia can occur from rapid water loss."]], "journey": "Conivaptan is given IV as a loading dose followed by a continuous infusion for a few days, through a large vein that is changed often. Serum sodium rises over the first day. It is about 99 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down by CYP3A4, and it is also a strong CYP3A4 inhibitor, so it raises levels of many other drugs and is contraindicated with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors. It leaves mostly in feces, with some in urine. Its half-life is about 5 hours.", "sidefx": "The most serious risk is overly rapid correction of chronic hyponatremia. Brain cells adapt to low sodium by losing solutes, and a quick rise in sodium pulls water out of them, which can damage myelin in the pons and elsewhere (osmotic demyelination syndrome). This is why sodium is checked frequently. Rapid water loss can cause hypovolemia and low blood pressure, worsened by V1a blockade. Infusion site reactions are common because the drug irritates veins. Its CYP3A4 inhibition causes drug interactions. There is no antidote; the infusion is stopped and, if sodium has risen too fast, free water may be given.", "src": ["Vaprisol (conivaptan) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "glucarpidase": {"origin": "Glucarpidase is an enzyme from bacteria. In the 1970s British scientists, including Roger Sherwood, at Porton Down in England isolated an enzyme from a Pseudomonas bacterium that could break down folates and methotrexate. They called it carboxypeptidase G2. Doctors realized it could serve as a rescue for patients whose kidneys failed while receiving high-dose methotrexate, leaving toxic levels in the blood. For years it was available only through special programs. The British company BTG made a recombinant version produced in E. coli, and the FDA approved glucarpidase (Voraxaze) in January 2012 for toxic methotrexate levels in patients with delayed clearance due to impaired kidney function.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Carboxypeptidase G2 is isolated from a Pseudomonas strain in the UK."], ["1990s", "The enzyme is used under compassionate programs for methotrexate toxicity."], ["2012", "FDA approves glucarpidase (Voraxaze)."]], "cell": "Methotrexate blocks dihydrofolate reductase, starving cells of reduced folates needed to make DNA. It is mostly cleared by the kidneys, and high doses can damage the kidney tubules, which then slows clearance further and causes severe bone marrow, gut and liver toxicity. Glucarpidase is a recombinant carboxypeptidase G2. It cuts the terminal glutamate off methotrexate, producing glutamate and DAMPA (4-deoxy-4-amino-N10-methylpteroic acid). DAMPA is inactive and is cleared by the liver, so it does not depend on the failing kidneys. Within about 15 minutes, plasma methotrexate falls by 97 percent or more. Because glucarpidase is a large protein, it stays outside cells and does not remove methotrexate already inside cells, so leucovorin rescue is still needed.", "body": [["Blood", "Extracellular methotrexate levels fall within minutes, limiting further exposure of tissues."], ["Bone marrow", "Reducing circulating methotrexate lowers the risk of severe neutropenia and other low counts."], ["Gut and mouth", "Lower exposure reduces mucositis and diarrhea from methotrexate damage to dividing lining cells."], ["Kidneys", "Lowering methotrexate gives the injured kidneys time to recover."], ["Leucovorin", "Glucarpidase also breaks down leucovorin, so the two are given at least 2 hours apart."], ["Lab tests", "DAMPA cross-reacts with methotrexate immunoassays, which overstate levels for about 48 hours."]], "journey": "Glucarpidase is given as a single IV injection. It stays mainly in the blood, since it is a large protein, and acts within 15 minutes. Its half-life is about 5.6 hours, longer in kidney impairment. It is eventually broken down by proteolysis. Because it cannot enter cells, methotrexate stored in tissues can leak back into blood afterward, causing a rebound in levels.", "sidefx": "Glucarpidase is generally well tolerated. Tingling, flushing, nausea, vomiting and headache can occur. Hypersensitivity can happen because it is a bacterial protein, and antibodies can form. It also destroys leucovorin, the folate given to rescue cells, so timing must be separated. The DAMPA it produces interferes with methotrexate immunoassays, so a chromatographic method is used for 48 hours. It does not reverse injury inside cells, so methotrexate toxicity can still occur.", "src": ["Voraxaze (glucarpidase) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "uridine-triacetate": {"origin": "Doctors have long known that uridine, a natural building block of RNA, can protect normal cells from the chemotherapy drug 5-fluorouracil (5-FU). The problem was that plain uridine is poorly absorbed by mouth and given IV causes fever. Researchers at Wellstat Therapeutics in Maryland, including Reid von Borstel, developed uridine triacetate, a prodrug with acetyl groups that make it far better absorbed. It is then converted to uridine in the body. In December 2015 the FDA approved uridine triacetate (Vistogard) as the first antidote for overdoses of 5-FU or capecitabine and for early-onset severe or life-threatening toxicity. The same compound is also approved, as Xuriden, for hereditary orotic aciduria, a rare disorder of pyrimidine synthesis.", "timeline": [["1957", "5-fluorouracil is described by Charles Heidelberger and colleagues."], ["1990s", "Wellstat researchers develop uridine triacetate as an oral uridine prodrug."], ["2015", "FDA approves uridine triacetate (Vistogard) as an antidote for 5-FU and capecitabine toxicity."]], "cell": "5-FU is converted inside cells into several toxic metabolites. FdUMP blocks thymidylate synthase and DNA synthesis, while fluorouridine triphosphate (FUTP) is built into RNA in place of uridine triphosphate (UTP), disrupting RNA processing and function. In overdose or severe toxicity, RNA damage in rapidly dividing normal tissue, such as gut lining and bone marrow, is a major cause of injury. Uridine triacetate is deacetylated by esterases to uridine, which floods the blood. Cells take up uridine and convert it, through the pyrimidine salvage pathway, to UTP. The large pool of UTP competes with FUTP for incorporation into RNA, so much less fluorinated RNA is made. This limits cell damage in normal tissues, reducing toxicity to the gut, bone marrow, heart and nervous system.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Less RNA damage in blood-forming cells reduces severe neutropenia and other low blood counts."], ["Gut", "Protecting the gut lining reduces mucositis, diarrhea and the risk of perforation."], ["Heart", "It may reduce 5-FU cardiac toxicity, such as chest pain and arrhythmias."], ["Brain", "It helps reduce 5-FU neurotoxicity such as encephalopathy and confusion."], ["Survival", "In the pivotal studies, most treated overdose patients survived, compared with low survival in historical reports."]], "journey": "Uridine triacetate comes as granules taken by mouth, mixed with soft food, every 6 hours over several days. The acetyl groups make it fat-soluble so it is absorbed well. Esterases in the gut and blood remove the acetyl groups, releasing uridine, which peaks about 2 to 3 hours after a dose. Uridine joins normal pyrimidine metabolism, with some cleared in urine. Its half-life is about 2 to 2.6 hours. It works best when started early, within 96 hours of the end of 5-FU.", "sidefx": "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are the main side effects; they likely come from the large oral load in the gut, and also overlap with 5-FU toxicity itself. If vomiting occurs within 2 hours of a dose, the dose is repeated. It does not reverse damage already done, which is why timing is critical. It is not meant for routine, nonsevere 5-FU side effects, since it could reduce anticancer effect. Supportive care such as antiemetics and growth factors continues alongside it.", "src": ["Vistogard (uridine triacetate) FDA label", "Ma WW et al. Cancer 2017"]}, "cangrelor": {"origin": "Cangrelor was first developed at AstraZeneca in the 1990s, where chemists modified ATP, a natural blocker of the P2Y12 receptor, to make a stable, potent, IV antiplatelet drug. It was coded AR-C69931MX. The Medicines Company licensed it and ran the CHAMPION trial program. Early trials failed to show benefit, but CHAMPION PHOENIX, published in 2013, showed fewer heart attacks, stent clots and other ischemic events during percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) compared with clopidogrel. The FDA approved cangrelor (Kengreal) in June 2015 as an add-on to PCI for patients not already given an oral P2Y12 blocker. It remains the only IV P2Y12 inhibitor, valued for its fast on and off action.", "timeline": [["1990s", "AstraZeneca chemists develop cangrelor as an ATP analog P2Y12 blocker."], ["2013", "CHAMPION PHOENIX shows fewer ischemic events during PCI."], ["2015", "FDA approves cangrelor (Kengreal)."]], "cell": "When platelets are activated, they release ADP from dense granules. ADP binds two receptors: P2Y1, which starts shape change, and P2Y12, a Gi-coupled receptor that keeps the response going. P2Y12 inhibits adenylyl cyclase, lowering cAMP, and activates PI3-kinase, which together switch GP IIb/IIIa receptors into their active, fibrinogen-binding form and amplify aggregation. Cangrelor is an ATP analog that binds P2Y12 directly and reversibly. It does not need metabolic activation, unlike clopidogrel and prasugrel. With P2Y12 blocked, cAMP stays high, GP IIb/IIIa stays inactive and platelets do not aggregate well. Inhibition is near-complete within 2 minutes and wears off within about an hour of stopping, as the drug is rapidly broken down.", "body": [["Platelets", "ADP-driven aggregation is almost fully blocked within minutes, protecting coronary stents during PCI."], ["Coronary arteries", "Lower platelet activation reduces periprocedural heart attacks and stent thrombosis."], ["Bleeding", "Bleeding risk rises, mostly minor, and increases with heparin or bivalirudin."], ["Lungs", "Dyspnea occurs in some patients, likely from P2Y12 or adenosine-related effects on sensory nerves."], ["Transition to oral drugs", "Clopidogrel and prasugrel metabolites cannot bind P2Y12 while cangrelor occupies it, so they are given after the infusion ends."]], "journey": "Cangrelor is given only IV, as a bolus followed by an infusion. It acts within 2 minutes. It is about 97 to 98 percent bound to plasma proteins. It is rapidly dephosphorylated in the blood to an inactive nucleoside, so it does not depend on the liver or kidneys. Its half-life is only 3 to 6 minutes, and platelet function returns to normal within about an hour after stopping. Metabolites leave in urine and feces.", "sidefx": "Bleeding is the main side effect because platelets cannot aggregate well; it is usually minor but can be serious, especially with other antithrombotics. Its fast offset means bleeding risk drops quickly after stopping, with no reversal agent needed. Dyspnea occurs in some patients, similar to ticagrelor. Hypersensitivity reactions can occur. Because cangrelor blocks the receptor, clopidogrel or prasugrel must be given after the infusion stops, while ticagrelor, which binds a different site, can be given during it.", "src": ["Kengreal (cangrelor) FDA label", "Bhatt DL et al. CHAMPION PHOENIX, NEJM 2013"]}, "angiotensin-ii": {"origin": "The story starts in 1898, when Robert Tigerstedt and Per Bergman in Stockholm found that kidney extracts raised blood pressure and named the active substance renin. Around 1939 and 1940 two teams working apart found that renin acts on a blood protein to make a powerful pressor peptide. Eduardo Braun-Menendez and colleagues in Buenos Aires called it hypertensin, while Irvine Page and his group in Indianapolis called it angiotonin. In 1958 the two groups agreed on the blended name angiotensin. The peptide was synthesized in the late 1950s. For decades it was a research tool, and medicine focused on blocking it with ACE inhibitors and ARBs. La Jolla Pharmaceutical then developed synthetic human angiotensin II as a vasopressor, and after the ATHOS-3 trial the FDA approved it as Giapreza in 2017 for adults with septic or other distributive shock.", "timeline": [["1898", "Tigerstedt and Bergman describe renin from kidney extracts."], ["1940", "Teams in Buenos Aires and Indianapolis independently describe the pressor peptide."], ["1958", "The names hypertensin and angiotonin are merged into angiotensin."], ["2017", "ATHOS-3 trial results are published and the FDA approves Giapreza for distributive shock."]], "cell": "Angiotensin II is an eight-amino-acid peptide identical to the human hormone. It binds the angiotensin II type 1 (AT1) receptor, a Gq-coupled receptor on vascular smooth muscle. Gq activates phospholipase C, which splits PIP2 into IP3 and diacylglycerol. IP3 releases calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, and the calcium-calmodulin complex activates myosin light-chain kinase, so the arteriole contracts. Diacylglycerol activates protein kinase C, which sustains the contraction. AT1 activation in the adrenal zona glomerulosa increases aldosterone release, and central effects raise vasopressin release. The net clinical effect is a rise in systemic vascular resistance and mean arterial pressure. Because it works on a different receptor than catecholamines or vasopressin, it adds a third pathway in shock that does not respond well to the others.", "body": [["Arterioles", "Strong AT1-driven constriction raises systemic vascular resistance and mean arterial pressure, which can lower the needed amount of other vasopressors."], ["Kidneys", "Angiotensin II constricts the efferent arteriole more than the afferent, which helps hold glomerular filtration pressure when blood pressure is low."], ["Adrenal cortex", "Stimulates aldosterone release, which makes the kidney hold sodium and water and lose potassium."], ["Brain and pituitary", "Increases vasopressin release and thirst, adding to fluid retention and vessel tone."], ["Blood and clotting", "Patients receiving it in trials had more venous and arterial clots, so clot prevention is part of care."], ["Metabolism", "Hyperglycemia and a low platelet count were reported in clinical trial patients."]], "journey": "Giapreza is given as a continuous IV infusion because the peptide is broken down almost at once. Peptidases in plasma, red blood cells and tissues, such as aminopeptidase A and ACE2, cut it into smaller fragments like angiotensin (1-7) and angiotensin III. Its plasma half-life is under a minute. Blood pressure begins to rise within about five minutes of starting the infusion, and the effect fades within minutes after it stops. The peptides are broken down to amino acids, so there is no liver enzyme or kidney clearance pathway to adjust for.", "sidefx": "The most serious risk is clotting. Angiotensin II activates platelets and increases plasminogen activator inhibitor-1, which favors clot formation, and deep vein thrombosis was more common in the treatment arm of ATHOS-3. Strong arteriolar constriction can cut blood flow to fingers, toes and the gut, causing ischemia. Tachycardia, delirium, hyperglycemia, thrombocytopenia and fungal infections were also reported in trial patients. There is no specific reversal agent, but the short half-life means effects fade quickly once the infusion is lowered or stopped.", "fun": "Before the synthetic drug existed, the original pressor substance was made by mixing renin from kidney extracts with blood plasma, letting the enzyme cut its protein substrate in a test tube.", "src": ["Giapreza (angiotensin II) FDA label", "Khanna A et al. ATHOS-3, N Engl J Med 2017", "Basso N, Terragno NA. History about the discovery of the renin-angiotensin system, Hypertension 2001"]}, "iohexol": {"origin": "Early iodinated contrast agents were ionic salts. They split into charged particles in solution, so they were several times saltier (more osmotic) than blood and caused pain, heat and fluid shifts. In 1969 the Swedish radiologist Torsten Almen proposed a new idea: build contrast molecules that do not ionize, so each molecule carries its iodine without extra charged particles. The Norwegian company Nyegaard & Co. (later Nycomed, now part of GE HealthCare) took up the idea. Its first nonionic agent, metrizamide, was useful but unstable and costly. Chemists there then developed iohexol, a stable nonionic monomer, which entered clinical use in the early 1980s and was approved in the US as Omnipaque in the mid-1980s. Iohexol helped make low-osmolar contrast the standard for CT and angiography.", "timeline": [["1969", "Torsten Almen proposes nonionic, low-osmolar contrast molecules."], ["1970s", "Nyegaard & Co. introduces metrizamide, the first nonionic agent."], ["1980s", "Iohexol enters clinical use and is approved in the US as Omnipaque."]], "cell": "Iohexol has no receptor target. Each molecule has a benzene ring carrying three iodine atoms. Iodine has a high atomic number, and its K-edge energy sits close to the energy of diagnostic x-rays, so it absorbs x-ray photons strongly by the photoelectric effect. Any space the drug fills blocks more x-rays and looks bright on CT or fluoroscopy. Hydrophilic side chains with many hydroxyl groups make the molecule water soluble and shield the iodine core, which reduces binding to proteins and cell membranes. Because it does not split into ions, a given iodine concentration needs about half as many particles as an ionic monomer, so osmolality is lower. After injection it moves out of the capillaries into the extracellular space but does not enter cells or cross an intact blood-brain barrier.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Fills the vessels within seconds of a bolus, making arteries and veins bright for CT angiography; a warm feeling comes from mild vasodilation."], ["Organs and tumors", "Leaks into the extracellular space of tissues, so areas with more blood flow or leaky vessels, such as many tumors and inflamed tissue, enhance more."], ["Kidneys", "Filtered freely at the glomerulus and concentrated in the urine, which outlines the collecting system on urography; in at-risk patients it can contribute to kidney injury."], ["Thyroid", "Free iodide in the product and from small breakdown can briefly change thyroid hormone production, mainly in infants and people with thyroid disease."], ["Spinal canal", "Specific concentrations are labeled for intrathecal myelography, where it outlines the spinal cord and nerve roots."], ["Gut", "Swallowed or given rectally, it outlines the bowel and is very poorly absorbed through intact mucosa."]], "journey": "Iohexol is injected into a vein or artery, or given into the spinal canal, joints, body cavities or the gut. In the blood it mixes with plasma within seconds and spreads into the extracellular fluid within minutes. It binds very little to proteins and is not metabolized. The kidneys clear it unchanged by glomerular filtration alone, with no tubular secretion or reabsorption, which is why measured iohexol clearance is used as a research gold standard for GFR. The half-life is about two hours with normal kidneys and much longer in kidney failure, and hemodialysis removes it.", "sidefx": "Most reactions are physiologic or allergic-like. Warmth, metallic taste and nausea come from the osmotic load and mild vessel dilation. Allergic-like reactions, from hives to anaphylaxis, happen through direct mast cell activation and complement effects rather than classic IgE allergy in most cases. In people with poor kidney function, contrast can reduce medullary blood flow and stress tubule cells, which explains contrast-associated kidney injury. The iodine load can trigger hyperthyroidism or hypothyroidism. Wrong concentrations given intrathecally can cause seizures or death, which is the reason for the boxed warning. There is no antidote; severe reactions are treated with epinephrine and supportive care, and hemodialysis can remove the drug.", "fun": "Because iohexol is filtered by the kidney and nothing else, researchers use its clearance from blood to measure kidney function more precisely than creatinine-based estimates.", "src": ["Omnipaque (iohexol) FDA label", "ACR Manual on Contrast Media", "Almen T. Contrast agent design, J Theor Biol 1969"]}, "iopamidol": {"origin": "Iopamidol came out of the same push for nonionic contrast that followed Torsten Almen's 1969 proposal. It was first made by chemists in Switzerland in the 1970s and developed by the Italian company Bracco, in Milan. Like iohexol, it is a nonionic monomer with three iodine atoms on a benzene ring and water-loving side chains. It entered clinical use in Europe in the early 1980s and reached the US in the 1980s under the brand name Isovue. A separate low-concentration form, Isovue-M, was made for myelography. Iopamidol became one of the most widely used CT and angiography agents in the world.", "timeline": [["1969", "Almen proposes nonionic, low-osmolar contrast agents."], ["1970s", "Bracco develops iopamidol."], ["1980s", "Iopamidol enters clinical use and reaches the US market as Isovue."]], "cell": "Iopamidol acts by physics rather than pharmacology. Its three iodine atoms absorb x-ray photons by the photoelectric effect, so the blood or tissue it fills blocks more x-rays and appears bright. The molecule does not dissociate into ions, so it delivers the same iodine with fewer particles than older ionic agents, giving lower osmolality and fewer fluid shifts. Its hydroxyl-rich side chains surround the iodinated ring, which makes it very water soluble and lowers interaction with proteins and cell membranes. After injection it distributes in plasma and then into the extracellular space. It does not enter cells or cross an intact blood-brain barrier, so enhancement in the brain means a barrier is broken.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Opacifies arteries and veins during the first pass, which allows CT angiography and catheter angiography."], ["Solid organs", "Spreads into the extracellular space of organs, showing differences in blood supply between normal and diseased tissue."], ["Brain", "Normally stays out of the brain; tumors, abscesses and other lesions that break the blood-brain barrier enhance."], ["Kidneys", "Filtered unchanged and concentrated in urine, producing images of the kidneys and ureters; it causes a mild osmotic diuresis."], ["Heart", "During coronary angiography, contrast briefly reaches the coronary arteries and can cause transient changes in rhythm and contractility."], ["Thyroid", "The iodine load can temporarily alter thyroid hormone production in susceptible people."]], "journey": "Iopamidol is given into a vein or artery, or intrathecally as Isovue-M. Within minutes it moves from plasma into the extracellular space. It is not metabolized and binds little to protein. The kidneys filter it unchanged, and most is in the urine within a day when kidney function is normal. The elimination half-life is about two hours and is prolonged in kidney disease. Dialysis removes it.", "sidefx": "Heat, flushing and metallic taste come from osmotic and vasodilator effects as the bolus passes. Allergic-like reactions, from hives to bronchospasm and anaphylaxis, mostly arise from direct release of histamine and other mediators from mast cells and basophils. Contrast-associated kidney injury in at-risk patients is linked to reduced medullary blood flow and direct tubule toxicity. Leakage outside the vein can injure skin and soft tissue. Only Isovue-M is meant for the spinal canal; the regular strengths can cause fatal neurotoxicity if given intrathecally. There is no antidote, and reactions are treated supportively, with epinephrine for anaphylaxis.", "src": ["Isovue (iopamidol) FDA label", "ACR Manual on Contrast Media", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "iodixanol": {"origin": "After nonionic monomers like iohexol lowered osmolality, chemists asked if they could make contrast that matched blood exactly. The answer was a dimer: two triiodinated rings joined together, so each molecule carries six iodine atoms. Fewer particles are needed for the same iodine dose. Researchers at Nycomed in Norway developed iodixanol along these lines, and it entered clinical use in the early 1990s. Sodium and calcium salts are added so the final solution is iso-osmolar with blood. It was marketed as Visipaque and approved in the US in the 1990s. It became especially popular for cardiac catheterization and for patients at higher risk of kidney injury.", "timeline": [["1969", "Almen proposes nonionic contrast media."], ["1980s", "Nycomed develops nonionic dimeric agents."], ["1990s", "Iodixanol is introduced as Visipaque and approved in the US."]], "cell": "Iodixanol has no receptor. Each molecule is a nonionic dimer of two benzene rings, each carrying three iodine atoms. Iodine absorbs x-rays by the photoelectric effect, so vessels and tissues that hold the drug appear bright. Because one particle carries six iodine atoms instead of three, iodixanol reaches the same iodine concentration with far fewer dissolved particles. Its natural osmolality is actually below blood, so electrolytes are added to bring it to about the same osmolality as plasma. The large molecule is more viscous than monomers. It distributes into the extracellular fluid and does not enter cells or an intact brain.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "Fills arteries with less heat and pain than monomers, which is useful in sensitive procedures like peripheral and cerebral angiography."], ["Heart", "Matching blood osmolality causes less fluid shift and less change in heart rhythm and contractility during coronary injection."], ["Kidneys", "Filtered unchanged; it is often chosen for at-risk patients, although large studies show only small or uncertain differences in kidney injury compared with low-osmolar agents."], ["Organs", "Leaks into the extracellular space of tissues, showing areas of abnormal blood supply on CT."], ["Skin", "Delayed rashes, appearing hours to a week later, seem to be more common than with monomers."]], "journey": "Iodixanol is injected into an artery or vein. It mixes with blood and then spreads into the extracellular space over minutes. It is not metabolized and binds little to protein. The kidneys filter it unchanged, and the half-life is about two hours with normal kidney function. The higher viscosity slows how fast it can be injected and may slow its passage through kidney tubules. Dialysis removes it.", "sidefx": "Because it is iso-osmolar, iodixanol causes less warmth and pain than other agents. Allergic-like reactions still happen through direct mediator release from mast cells and basophils. Delayed skin reactions, thought to be T-cell mediated, appear more often than with monomers and can occasionally be severe. In people with kidney disease, contrast can reduce medullary oxygen delivery and injure tubule cells; the higher viscosity may contribute. It must never be given intrathecally. There is no antidote; anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine and supportive care.", "src": ["Visipaque (iodixanol) FDA label", "ACR Manual on Contrast Media", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "diatrizoate": {"origin": "Diatrizoate was one of the first iodinated contrast agents built on a fully triiodinated benzene ring. It was introduced in the early 1950s, with Sterling-Winthrop in the US and Schering in Germany among the first companies to market it, and it quickly replaced older, more toxic agents for urography and angiography. For decades its sodium and meglumine salts were standard intravascular contrast. Because these salts split into ions, the solutions are very hypertonic. Once safer nonionic agents arrived in the 1980s, diatrizoate moved out of the blood vessels and into the gut and bladder. Today it is used mainly as Gastrografin and similar oral or rectal solutions, and as Cystografin for bladder studies.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Diatrizoate salts are developed and introduced for urography and angiography."], ["1980s", "Nonionic agents begin replacing it for intravascular use."], ["1990s", "Diatrizoate is used mainly for enteric and bladder imaging."]], "cell": "Diatrizoate is an ionic triiodinated benzoic acid. In solution it splits into a negatively charged iodinated anion and a sodium or meglumine cation. The iodine absorbs x-rays by the photoelectric effect, so the bowel or bladder it fills appears bright. Because each molecule makes two particles, the solution is several times more concentrated than plasma. In the gut it is barely absorbed, so it stays in the lumen and pulls water in by osmosis. That extra fluid dilutes the contrast as it moves along, stimulates peristalsis and can soften impacted stool or help move contents past an adhesive small bowel obstruction.", "body": [["Stomach and small bowel", "Outlines the bowel and shows leaks after surgery or perforation; a water-soluble agent is safer than barium if it escapes into the abdomen."], ["Colon", "Pulls water into the colon, which helps in studies of obstruction and can loosen meconium or stool."], ["Fluid balance", "Water drawn into the gut can cause dehydration and low blood volume, especially in infants and frail adults."], ["Lungs", "If aspirated, the hypertonic solution pulls fluid into the airways and can cause chemical pneumonitis and pulmonary edema."], ["Bladder", "Given through a catheter, it outlines the bladder and urethra for cystourethrography."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth or given rectally, diatrizoate reaches the stomach and bowel within minutes and the colon in about one to four hours. Very little passes through healthy intestinal lining, so most leaves unchanged in the stool over one to two days. If the bowel wall is inflamed or perforated, more is absorbed, and that portion is filtered unchanged by the kidneys, which can make the urinary tract faintly visible. It is not metabolized.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from its high osmolality. Drawing water into the gut causes diarrhea, cramping and nausea and can lead to dehydration and electrolyte shifts, which is why infants and dehydrated patients need care. If inhaled into the lungs, it pulls fluid into the alveoli and can cause severe pneumonitis or pulmonary edema. Absorbed contrast can rarely cause allergic-like reactions. There is no antidote; treatment is fluids, oxygen and supportive care.", "src": ["Gastrografin (diatrizoate meglumine and sodium) FDA label", "ACR Manual on Contrast Media"]}, "barium-sulfate": {"origin": "Soon after Wilhelm Roentgen discovered x-rays in 1895, doctors looked for safe ways to see the gut. Early studies used bismuth salts, which were costly and sometimes toxic. In the early 1900s physicians began using barium sulfate, a heavy, chalk-like white powder that is almost totally insoluble in water. Its insolubility is the key: free barium ions are poisonous, but barium sulfate passes through the gut without dissolving. By the 1910s barium meals and enemas were standard. Double-contrast techniques, which coat the mucosa and then add air, were refined later in the century. Barium products were sold for decades before modern drug approval rules, and in the 2010s the FDA required and granted formal approvals for many of them.", "timeline": [["1895", "Wilhelm Roentgen discovers x-rays."], ["1910s", "Barium sulfate replaces bismuth salts for gut imaging."], ["1960s", "Double-contrast barium studies with air are refined."], ["2010s", "The FDA formally approves several long-used barium sulfate products."]], "cell": "Barium sulfate has no receptor or enzyme target. Barium has a high atomic number (56), so it absorbs x-rays strongly and anything it coats or fills appears white. The sulfate salt has extremely low solubility in water and in stomach acid, so essentially no free barium ions are released or absorbed. Products are made as suspensions with suspending agents so the particles coat the mucosa evenly. Thick, dense suspensions coat the lining for double-contrast studies, while dilute suspensions fill the bowel for CT. Different textures, from thin liquid to pudding, are used in swallowing studies to test how a person handles each consistency.", "body": [["Esophagus and swallowing", "Thin to thick textures show how a person swallows and whether material enters the airway during a modified barium swallow."], ["Stomach and small bowel", "Coats the mucosa and fills the lumen, showing ulcers, masses, narrowing and transit time."], ["Colon", "Given as an enema, it outlines the colon and rectum; air added afterward gives fine mucosal detail."], ["Bowel habits", "Barium is not absorbed and can dry out in the colon, causing constipation, white stools and rarely impaction."], ["Peritoneum", "If it leaks through a perforation, barium is not cleared and causes severe inflammation and adhesions, which is why water-soluble contrast is used when a leak is suspected."]], "journey": "Barium sulfate is swallowed or given rectally. It coats the gut lining immediately and moves through the bowel at the normal pace of transit, reaching the colon in about one to four hours after a swallow. Essentially none is absorbed into the blood, and it is not metabolized. It leaves the body unchanged in stool over one to three days. Drinking fluids helps it pass.", "sidefx": "Because barium is not absorbed, its harms are mechanical. Thickened barium in the colon can cause constipation or impaction, especially in people with slow bowels. If it leaks into the abdomen, it causes intense peritonitis and scarring. If aspirated, the particles irritate the lungs and can cause pneumonitis. Very rarely it enters veins through damaged mucosa and causes embolism. Allergic reactions are uncommon and are usually linked to additives or latex, not barium itself. Barium sulfate must never be confused with soluble barium salts, which are poisons. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Barium sulfate is so insoluble that the same compound is used as a white pigment in paints and paper.", "src": ["E-Z-Paque (barium sulfate) FDA label", "ACR Manual on Contrast Media"]}, "gadobutrol": {"origin": "MRI arrived in hospitals in the early 1980s. Researchers soon learned that gadolinium, a rare earth metal with seven unpaired electrons, brightens nearby tissue on T1-weighted images, but free gadolinium is toxic. The fix was to lock it inside a tightly binding organic cage called a chelate. The first approved agent, gadopentetate (Magnevist, 1988), used an open, linear cage. Chemists at Schering AG in Berlin (now Bayer) then designed gadobutrol with a closed, ring-shaped macrocyclic cage for higher stability and made it at twice the usual concentration. It was approved in Europe in the late 1990s and by the FDA as Gadavist in 2011.", "timeline": [["1988", "The first gadolinium contrast agent, gadopentetate, is approved in the US."], ["1990s", "Schering develops gadobutrol and launches it in Europe."], ["2011", "The FDA approves gadobutrol as Gadavist."], ["2017", "The FDA adds warnings about gadolinium retention in the body."]], "cell": "Gadobutrol acts through physics. The Gd3+ ion has seven unpaired electrons, which makes it strongly paramagnetic. When water molecules briefly bind near the gadolinium, its magnetic field speeds up the relaxation of their hydrogen protons after each radiofrequency pulse. Shorter T1 relaxation makes those areas appear bright on T1-weighted images. The macrocyclic butrol ligand wraps gadolinium in a rigid ring, so the ion is released very slowly, which gives high kinetic stability. The higher concentration delivers a compact bolus, useful for MR angiography and perfusion imaging. The agent stays outside cells and crosses only a broken blood-brain barrier.", "body": [["Blood vessels", "During the first pass, the concentrated bolus brightens arteries for MR angiography."], ["Brain and spine", "Leaks only where the blood-brain barrier is broken, so tumors, active multiple sclerosis plaques and infections stand out."], ["Breast and heart", "Shows abnormal blood supply in breast tumors and scarred heart muscle on delayed images."], ["Kidneys", "Filtered unchanged at the glomerulus; in severe kidney failure it stays in the body much longer."], ["Skin and connective tissue", "In severe kidney failure, retained gadolinium agents have been linked to nephrogenic systemic fibrosis, though macrocyclic agents carry the lowest risk."]], "journey": "Gadobutrol is injected into a vein. It circulates in plasma, then moves into the extracellular space within minutes. It does not bind proteins and is not metabolized. The kidneys filter it unchanged, with a half-life of about two hours in people with normal kidney function. Most of it leaves the body in the urine within a day. In kidney failure the half-life is much longer, and dialysis can remove it. Tiny amounts remain in bone, brain and skin for long periods, though less than with linear agents.", "sidefx": "Common effects such as headache, nausea and altered taste are mild and brief. Allergic-like reactions are rare and can range from hives to anaphylaxis. The most feared toxicity is nephrogenic systemic fibrosis, a thickening of skin and organs seen when free gadolinium is released in people with severe kidney failure; stable macrocyclic cages make this very rare. Small amounts of gadolinium are retained in the brain and bone, with no proven harm so far. Intrathecal use can cause severe neurotoxicity. There is no antidote; dialysis removes the agent, and anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine.", "src": ["Gadavist (gadobutrol) FDA label", "ACR Manual on Contrast Media", "FDA Drug Safety Communication on gadolinium retention, 2017"]}, "gadoterate": {"origin": "Gadoterate pairs gadolinium with DOTA, a ring-shaped chelating molecule first made by chemists in the 1970s. The French company Guerbet developed gadoterate meglumine, and it was approved in France as Dotarem in 1989, making it one of the earliest macrocyclic MRI agents. It was widely used in Europe for more than two decades before the FDA approved it for the US in 2013. Its tight DOTA cage made it attractive after nephrogenic systemic fibrosis was linked to less stable gadolinium agents in 2006. Generic versions such as Clariscan followed.", "timeline": [["1989", "Guerbet launches gadoterate as Dotarem in France."], ["2006", "Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis is linked to gadolinium agents."], ["2013", "The FDA approves Dotarem for use in the US."]], "cell": "Gadoterate contains a Gd3+ ion held inside the DOTA macrocycle, a rigid 12-membered ring with four arms that grip the metal. The complex carries a negative charge, so it is balanced by meglumine, which makes it an ionic macrocyclic agent. Gadolinium's seven unpaired electrons create a strong local magnetic field that shortens the T1 relaxation time of nearby water protons, so tissues with more agent look brighter on T1-weighted MRI. The DOTA cage releases gadolinium extremely slowly, giving one of the highest kinetic stabilities of any gadolinium agent. It stays in the extracellular space and only crosses a disrupted blood-brain barrier.", "body": [["Brain and spine", "Brightens lesions where the blood-brain barrier is disrupted, such as tumors, abscesses and active inflammation."], ["Body organs", "Distributes in the extracellular space of organs and highlights areas with abnormal blood supply."], ["Blood vessels", "Gives vessel enhancement during the first pass after injection."], ["Kidneys", "Filtered unchanged into the urine; kidney failure slows its exit from the body."], ["Skin", "Very stable chelation makes nephrogenic systemic fibrosis extremely rare, though caution is still used in severe kidney disease."]], "journey": "Gadoterate is injected into a vein. It spreads through plasma and into the extracellular space within minutes. It does not bind to proteins and is not metabolized. The kidneys clear it unchanged by filtration, with a half-life of about one and a half hours in healthy people. Nearly all is gone in urine within a day. It is cleared by dialysis. Trace retention in tissues is lower than with linear agents.", "sidefx": "Nausea, headache, altered taste and a cold or painful feeling at the injection site are common and brief. Allergic-like reactions are uncommon; mast cell and basophil activation can cause hives, bronchospasm or rarely anaphylaxis. Free gadolinium, if released, can deposit in skin and organs and trigger fibrosis in people with severe kidney failure, but the strong DOTA cage keeps release very low. Intrathecal injection can cause serious neurotoxicity. There is no antidote; dialysis removes the drug and anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine.", "src": ["Dotarem (gadoterate meglumine) FDA label", "ACR Manual on Contrast Media"]}, "gadoxetate": {"origin": "Most gadolinium agents stay outside cells. Chemists at Schering AG (now Bayer) wanted an agent that liver cells would take up, so they could see which tissue contained working hepatocytes. They added a fat-loving ethoxybenzyl group to a linear gadolinium chelate, creating gadoxetate (Gd-EOB-DTPA). It was approved in Europe as Primovist in 2004 and by the FDA as Eovist in 2008. It became a key tool for finding and characterizing liver lesions, including small cancers.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Schering researchers develop Gd-EOB-DTPA as a liver-specific agent."], ["2004", "Gadoxetate is approved in Europe as Primovist."], ["2008", "The FDA approves gadoxetate as Eovist."]], "cell": "Gadoxetate is a linear gadolinium chelate with an ethoxybenzyl side group. Right after injection it acts like other agents, shortening T1 relaxation of water in the blood and extracellular space, so arterial and portal venous phases can be imaged. Then working hepatocytes take it up through organic anion transporting polypeptides OATP1B1 and OATP1B3 on the sinusoidal membrane. Liver cells pump it into bile through MRP2 on the canalicular membrane. About 20 minutes after injection, normal liver is bright on T1 images (the hepatobiliary phase). Lesions without working hepatocytes or transporters, such as metastases and most hepatocellular carcinomas, stay dark against the bright liver.", "body": [["Liver", "Healthy liver tissue takes up the agent and brightens on delayed images, making lesions that lack hepatocytes stand out."], ["Bile ducts", "Excretion into bile outlines the ducts and can show bile leaks after surgery or injury."], ["Blood vessels", "Early phases show arterial and portal venous blood supply to lesions."], ["Kidneys", "About half the dose is filtered into the urine; the rest leaves through bile."], ["Lungs", "Some patients have brief shortness of breath during the arterial phase, which can blur early images."]], "journey": "Gadoxetate is injected into a vein. It first spreads into the extracellular space, then is taken up by liver cells over about 20 minutes. Liver cells secrete it unchanged into bile, and the kidneys filter the rest. About half leaves in urine and half in stool. It binds weakly to plasma proteins and is not metabolized. The half-life is about one hour in healthy adults. Because it has two routes out, severe disease in both the liver and kidneys slows clearance.", "sidefx": "Nausea, headache, altered taste and a feeling of warmth are common and short-lived. Transient breathlessness during the arterial phase is reported and is not fully explained. Allergic-like reactions from mediator release are rare. Its linear cage is less stable than macrocyclic ones, but the very low dose used and dual elimination keep the risk of nephrogenic systemic fibrosis low; it is still a concern in severe kidney failure. There is no antidote; dialysis can remove it and anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine.", "src": ["Eovist (gadoxetate disodium) FDA label", "ACR Manual on Contrast Media"]}, "gadobenate": {"origin": "Gadobenate was developed by the Italian company Bracco in the late 1980s and 1990s. Its chelate, BOPTA, adds a benzyl group to a linear gadolinium cage. That small change makes the molecule stick loosely to albumin, which slows its tumbling in water and boosts how strongly it brightens tissue. A small part is also taken up by liver cells. Gadobenate was approved in Europe in the 1990s and by the FDA as MultiHance in 2004. It was later approved for MR angiography of the kidney and leg arteries and for use in children.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Bracco develops gadobenate and launches it in Europe."], ["2004", "The FDA approves gadobenate as MultiHance for CNS MRI."], ["2010s", "US approval is extended to MR angiography and to young children."]], "cell": "Gadobenate holds Gd3+ in a linear BOPTA chelate. The gadolinium shortens the T1 relaxation time of nearby water protons, so tissues containing the agent look brighter. The benzyloxymethyl group causes weak, temporary binding to serum albumin. When the complex is attached to the large albumin molecule, it rotates more slowly, which increases its relaxivity (the strength of T1 shortening per unit of gadolinium). That gives stronger enhancement than other standard-concentration agents. A few percent of the dose is taken up by hepatocytes and secreted into bile, causing mild delayed liver enhancement.", "body": [["Brain and spine", "Brightens lesions with a disrupted blood-brain barrier, with somewhat stronger enhancement than conventional agents."], ["Blood vessels", "Higher relaxivity and protein binding help MR angiography of the kidney and peripheral arteries."], ["Liver", "Small uptake by hepatocytes gives a mild delayed liver phase one to two hours after injection."], ["Kidneys", "Most of the dose is filtered into the urine unchanged."], ["Skin", "As a linear agent, it carries a higher theoretical risk of nephrogenic systemic fibrosis in severe kidney failure than macrocyclic agents."]], "journey": "Gadobenate is injected into a vein and spreads through plasma and extracellular fluid. It binds weakly and briefly to albumin. It is not metabolized. Most leaves unchanged in urine, and a small part goes into bile and out in stool. The half-life is about one to two hours in people with normal kidneys. Dialysis removes it.", "sidefx": "Nausea, headache, injection site reactions and altered taste are the most common. Allergic-like reactions arise from direct mast cell and basophil activation and range from hives to anaphylaxis. Because the linear cage releases gadolinium more easily than ring-shaped cages, retention in brain and bone is greater, and nephrogenic systemic fibrosis is a concern in severe kidney failure. There is no antidote; dialysis removes it and anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine.", "src": ["MultiHance (gadobenate dimeglumine) FDA label", "ACR Manual on Contrast Media"]}, "gadopiclenol": {"origin": "After gadolinium retention in the brain was reported in 2014, there was a push to get the same image quality with less gadolinium. Guerbet in France designed gadopiclenol around a pyclen-based macrocyclic cage. The cage leaves room for two water molecules to bind directly to gadolinium instead of one, which roughly doubles to triples its brightening power. That lets radiologists use about half the usual gadolinium dose for similar images. The FDA approved gadopiclenol in 2022. Guerbet markets it as Elucirem, and Bracco markets it under a partnership as Vueway.", "timeline": [["2014", "Gadolinium deposits in the brain after repeated MRI are reported."], ["2010s", "Guerbet develops the high-relaxivity agent gadopiclenol."], ["2022", "The FDA approves gadopiclenol as Elucirem and Vueway."]], "cell": "Gadopiclenol holds Gd3+ in a macrocyclic chelate built on a pyclen ring. Most gadolinium agents let only one water molecule sit directly on the metal. Gadopiclenol has two such inner-sphere water sites. Each bound water molecule swaps rapidly with surrounding water, spreading gadolinium's T1-shortening effect to many more protons. Its larger size also slows rotation, which raises relaxivity further. The result is about two to three times the T1 relaxivity of conventional agents. The macrocyclic design keeps kinetic stability high. Like other extracellular agents, it stays outside cells and crosses only a broken blood-brain barrier.", "body": [["Brain and spine", "Highlights lesions where the blood-brain barrier is disrupted, with diagnostic quality similar to standard agents at half the gadolinium dose."], ["Body organs", "Shows abnormal blood supply in the head and neck, chest, abdomen, pelvis and musculoskeletal system."], ["Kidneys", "Filtered unchanged into the urine; kidney failure slows clearance."], ["Long-term gadolinium load", "Less gadolinium per scan is meant to reduce total retention over many scans, though long-term data are still building."]], "journey": "Gadopiclenol is injected into a vein and spreads into the extracellular space within minutes. It does not bind proteins and is not metabolized. The kidneys filter it unchanged, with a half-life of about one and a half hours in adults with normal kidney function. Most is gone in urine within a day. Dialysis can remove it.", "sidefx": "Injection site pain, headache, nausea and altered taste are the most common effects. Allergic-like reactions from mediator release can occur, rarely as anaphylaxis. As with all gadolinium agents, there is a warning about nephrogenic systemic fibrosis in severe kidney failure and about gadolinium retention in tissues, though its stable cage and lower dose are designed to lower these risks. Intrathecal use can cause serious harm. There is no antidote; dialysis removes it.", "src": ["Elucirem (gadopiclenol) FDA label", "ACR Manual on Contrast Media", "Kanda T et al. Radiology 2014"]}, "ferumoxytol": {"origin": "Ferumoxytol grew out of research on tiny iron oxide particles for MRI in the 1980s and 1990s. Scientists found that iron oxide nanoparticles coated with sugar polymers are taken up by macrophages and strongly change MRI signal. Advanced Magnetics, a Massachusetts company later renamed AMAG Pharmaceuticals, developed a carbohydrate-coated particle that could deliver a large amount of iron quickly and safely into a vein. The FDA approved it as Feraheme in 2009 to treat iron deficiency anemia in adults with chronic kidney disease. Serious allergic reactions led to a boxed warning in 2015, and in 2018 its use was widened to adults with iron deficiency anemia who could not take or did not respond to oral iron. Its MRI properties are also used off label as a blood-pool contrast agent.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Researchers develop coated iron oxide nanoparticles for MRI."], ["2009", "The FDA approves Feraheme for iron deficiency anemia in chronic kidney disease."], ["2015", "A boxed warning for serious hypersensitivity reactions is added."], ["2018", "Approval is expanded to iron deficiency anemia beyond kidney disease."]], "cell": "Ferumoxytol is a superparamagnetic iron oxide core wrapped in a polyglucose sorbitol carboxymethylether coat. The coat keeps the iron from being released as free, reactive iron in the blood. Macrophages of the reticuloendothelial system in the liver, spleen and bone marrow engulf the particles. Inside lysosomes, the coat is removed and the iron oxide is broken down. The released iron is either stored as ferritin or exported through ferroportin to bind transferrin in plasma. Transferrin carries iron to red cell precursors in the bone marrow, which use transferrin receptors to take it up and build hemoglobin. In MRI, the magnetic iron core strongly shortens T1 and T2-star relaxation, so it can brighten blood vessels or darken macrophage-rich tissue.", "body": [["Bone marrow", "Iron delivered by transferrin lets red cell precursors make hemoglobin, so hemoglobin rises over one to two weeks."], ["Liver and spleen", "Macrophages here take up most of the particles and act as the iron store and processing site."], ["Iron stores", "Ferritin and transferrin saturation rise as iron is released from macrophages."], ["Blood vessels on MRI", "The particles stay in the blood for hours, so they can brighten vessels as a blood-pool contrast agent."], ["Immune system", "Rare immediate reactions with low blood pressure can occur, likely from complement activation by the nanoparticles."], ["MRI after treatment", "Iron in tissues can change MRI signal for up to about three months, which radiologists need to know."]], "journey": "Ferumoxytol is given only into a vein. The particles circulate for a long time, with a plasma half-life of about 15 hours, before macrophages in the liver, spleen and bone marrow take them up. The iron is then processed and joins the body's normal iron pool, either stored in ferritin or carried by transferrin to the bone marrow. The body has no active way to excrete iron, so it is not meaningfully eliminated in urine or stool, and hemodialysis does not remove it. Red cell production responds over days to weeks.", "sidefx": "The most serious risk is a hypersensitivity reaction, including anaphylaxis and severe low blood pressure, which led to the boxed warning. These reactions are thought to involve complement activation and mediator release triggered by the nanoparticles, sometimes called complement activation-related pseudoallergy. That is why the drug is infused over time with monitoring afterward. Nausea, dizziness, diarrhea and headache are common. Excess iron can build up in tissues if given when not needed. Because the iron changes magnetic properties of tissue, MRI interpretation can be affected for weeks. There is no specific reversal agent; reactions are treated with epinephrine and supportive care.", "src": ["Feraheme (ferumoxytol) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "perflutren": {"origin": "Ultrasound contrast began with a lucky observation in 1968, when Raymond Gramiak and Pravin Shah at the University of Rochester saw a cloud of echoes in the heart after injecting saline during a heart catheterization. Tiny bubbles in the fluid caused the bright signal. Plain air bubbles dissolved too fast to get through the lungs, so researchers spent the next decades making smaller, longer-lasting bubbles. The first FDA-approved agent, Albunex, used air in albumin shells. Later agents used heavy, poorly soluble gases. Perflutren lipid microspheres, made with octafluoropropane gas in a phospholipid shell, were approved as Definity in 2001 and are now made by Lantheus. A boxed warning about serious heart and lung reactions was added in 2007 and later narrowed as more safety data came in.", "timeline": [["1968", "Gramiak and Shah describe echo contrast from injected saline bubbles."], ["1990s", "The first commercial ultrasound contrast agents are approved."], ["2001", "The FDA approves Definity, perflutren lipid microspheres."], ["2007", "A boxed warning about cardiopulmonary reactions is added."]], "cell": "Perflutren has no receptor target. Each microsphere is a tiny gas bubble, about the size of a red blood cell or smaller, filled with octafluoropropane and wrapped in a phospholipid shell. Octafluoropropane dissolves poorly in blood, so the bubbles last long enough to pass through the lung capillaries and reach the left heart. Ultrasound waves hitting the bubbles make them shrink and expand. Gas is very compressible, so the bubbles vibrate strongly and unevenly, sending back a powerful echo with harmonic frequencies. The huge difference in acoustic impedance between gas and blood makes the blood pool very bright. This outlines the left ventricle's inner wall so that wall motion and heart size can be judged.", "body": [["Heart chambers", "Brightens the left ventricle cavity so the inner wall border is clearly seen, improving assessment of wall motion and ejection fraction."], ["Heart muscle", "Some approaches use it to show blood flow within the heart muscle, though that use is not a labeled indication."], ["Lungs", "Bubbles pass through the lung capillaries; the gas eventually diffuses out and is exhaled."], ["Blood vessels", "Stays within the blood vessels as a true blood-pool agent."], ["Circulation", "Rare reactions with low blood pressure, low oxygen or arrhythmia occur, usually within 30 minutes of injection."]], "journey": "Perflutren is given into a vein as a bolus or slow infusion. The bubbles reach the right heart within seconds, pass through the lungs, and fill the left heart. Each bolus gives several minutes of useful contrast. The octafluoropropane gas dissolves out of the bubbles and is breathed out through the lungs; its half-life in blood is only about one to two minutes. The lipid shell joins the body's normal fat metabolism. Nothing needs to be cleared by the liver or kidneys.", "sidefx": "Headache, flushing, nausea and back or chest pain are the most common effects. Rare but serious reactions include low blood pressure, hypoxia, arrhythmia, anaphylaxis and cardiopulmonary arrest, which led to the boxed warning and a period of observation after injection. Some reactions appear to be complement-mediated responses to the lipid microspheres, sometimes called complement activation-related pseudoallergy, and some may relate to the patient's underlying heart disease. Polyethylene glycol (PEG) in the shell can trigger allergy in people sensitive to PEG. There is no antidote; reactions are treated with standard resuscitation.", "fun": "The first echo contrast ever seen came from ordinary saline: the bright cloud was tiny air bubbles stirred up as the fluid was injected.", "src": ["Definity (perflutren lipid microsphere) FDA label", "Gramiak R, Shah PM. Echocardiography of the aortic root, Invest Radiol 1968"]}, "sulfur-hexafluoride": {"origin": "Sulfur hexafluoride lipid microspheres were developed by Bracco Research in Geneva, Switzerland, in the 1990s. The heavy, inert gas sulfur hexafluoride dissolves poorly in blood, so bubbles last long enough to cross the lungs. The product was approved in Europe as SonoVue in 2001 and became widely used in Europe and Asia for heart and liver imaging. The FDA approved it as Lumason in 2014 for echocardiography. In 2016 the FDA approved it for liver lesion imaging in adults and children and for ultrasound of the urinary tract in children, making it the first ultrasound contrast agent approved in the US for liver and pediatric bladder imaging.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Bracco develops sulfur hexafluoride microspheres in Geneva."], ["2001", "The agent is approved in Europe as SonoVue."], ["2014", "The FDA approves Lumason for echocardiography."], ["2016", "FDA approval is expanded to liver imaging and pediatric urinary tract ultrasound."]], "cell": "Sulfur hexafluoride microspheres have no receptor target. Each bubble is about the size of a red blood cell and is wrapped in a flexible phospholipid shell stabilized by polyethylene glycol. Under ultrasound the bubbles compress and expand far more than surrounding tissue, producing strong echoes and harmonic signals that contrast-specific software can pick out. Because the bubbles stay inside blood vessels, they map blood flow in real time. In the liver, a lesion's pattern over the arterial, portal venous and late phases helps tell benign masses from cancers. Placed in the bladder, they show if urine flows back toward the kidneys (vesicoureteral reflux) without radiation.", "body": [["Heart", "Fills the left ventricle so the wall border can be traced clearly for function and size."], ["Liver", "Real-time enhancement patterns help characterize focal liver lesions, such as hemangiomas, focal nodular hyperplasia or cancers."], ["Urinary tract in children", "Given into the bladder through a catheter, it shows reflux of urine into the ureters without x-ray radiation."], ["Lungs", "Bubbles cross the lung capillaries and the gas is exhaled through the lungs."], ["Circulation", "Rare serious reactions, such as low blood pressure or arrhythmia, can happen soon after injection."]], "journey": "Lumason is injected into a vein or, for children's bladder imaging, given into the bladder through a catheter. After IV injection the bubbles reach the heart within seconds and pass through the lungs into the arterial circulation. They remain in the blood vessels, giving several minutes of enhancement. The sulfur hexafluoride gas diffuses out and is breathed out, with nearly all gone within about 15 minutes and a terminal half-life of about 10 minutes. The phospholipid shell enters normal fat pathways. When given into the bladder, very little reaches the blood.", "sidefx": "Headache, nausea, altered taste, chest discomfort and a feeling of warmth are the most common. Rare but serious cardiopulmonary reactions, including low blood pressure, arrhythmia and cardiac arrest, can happen, mostly within 30 minutes; they seem to involve complement activation by the microspheres in some patients. Polyethylene glycol in the shell can cause allergy in PEG-sensitive people. After bladder use, mild painful urination can occur from the catheter. There is no antidote; reactions are managed with resuscitation and supportive care.", "src": ["Lumason (sulfur hexafluoride lipid-type A microspheres) FDA label", "ACR Manual on Contrast Media"]}, "fdg-f18": {"origin": "In the 1920s Otto Warburg noticed that cancer cells use glucose much faster than normal cells, even when oxygen is available. Decades later, Louis Sokoloff at the US National Institutes of Health developed a method using radioactive carbon-labeled deoxyglucose to map glucose use in the brains of animals. To do this in people, a team at Brookhaven National Laboratory, including Tatsuo Ido, Alfred Wolf and Joanna Fowler, labeled deoxyglucose with fluorine-18 in the mid-1970s. In 1976 researchers at the University of Pennsylvania, including Abass Alavi and Martin Reivich, gave it to human volunteers and imaged the brain. FDG became the workhorse of PET imaging, gaining FDA approval in the 1990s and broader approval for cancer, heart and brain imaging in 2000.", "timeline": [["1920s", "Otto Warburg describes high glucose use by cancer cells."], ["1976", "Brookhaven chemists make FDG and Penn researchers image the human brain with it."], ["1977", "Sokoloff publishes the deoxyglucose method for brain glucose use."], ["2000", "The FDA approves FDG for cancer, heart and seizure focus imaging."]], "cell": "FDG is glucose with the hydroxyl group on carbon 2 replaced by radioactive fluorine-18. Cells take it in through GLUT transporters, mainly GLUT1 and GLUT3, just as they take in glucose. Hexokinase then adds a phosphate, making FDG-6-phosphate. Normally the next enzyme, phosphoglucose isomerase, needs the missing 2-hydroxyl group, so FDG-6-phosphate cannot move further into glycolysis. Most tissues have little glucose-6-phosphatase to remove the phosphate, and the charged molecule cannot leave the cell, so it is trapped. The amount trapped reflects how fast the cell uses glucose. Fluorine-18 decays by emitting a positron, which meets an electron and produces two 511 keV photons going in opposite directions. The PET scanner detects these pairs and builds a map of glucose use.", "body": [["Cancers", "Many tumors use glucose heavily (the Warburg effect) and trap more FDG, so they light up on PET for diagnosis, staging and checking treatment response."], ["Brain", "The brain normally uses a lot of glucose; seizure foci between seizures and areas affected by dementia show reduced uptake."], ["Heart", "Working but poorly perfused heart muscle can switch to glucose and take up FDG, which helps show viable tissue after a heart attack."], ["Inflammation", "Activated white cells also take up FDG, so infection and inflammation can light up and mimic cancer."], ["Kidneys and bladder", "Unlike glucose, FDG is not fully reabsorbed by the kidney, so it collects in the urine and the bladder receives the highest radiation dose."]], "journey": "FDG is injected into a vein. It spreads quickly through the blood and is taken up by cells over the next hour, so imaging usually starts about 60 minutes after injection while the patient rests quietly. Fasting beforehand keeps blood glucose low so it does not compete with FDG. Trapped FDG is not broken down further. The fluorine-18 decays with a physical half-life of about 110 minutes. The portion not trapped is filtered by the kidneys into the urine, which is why patients are asked to empty the bladder and drink fluids.", "sidefx": "At the tiny tracer amounts used, FDG has no measurable drug effects, and side effects are very rare. The main risk is radiation exposure, which is kept low by the short half-life of fluorine-18 and by encouraging urination. High blood glucose or recent insulin can reduce tumor uptake or shift FDG into muscle and fat, lowering image quality. Inflammation, recent surgery and brown fat can cause false positive uptake. There is no antidote, and none is needed.", "fun": "Because FDG has a half-life under two hours, it must be made in a cyclotron and rushed to the scanner, often within the same city or region.", "src": ["Fludeoxyglucose F 18 Injection FDA label", "Sokoloff L et al. J Neurochem 1977", "Fowler JS, Ido T. Initial and subsequent approach for the synthesis of 18FDG, Semin Nucl Med 2002"]}, "tc99m-sestamibi": {"origin": "Technetium was the first element made artificially, discovered in 1937 by Carlo Perrier and Emilio Segre in Italy. In the late 1950s, scientists at Brookhaven National Laboratory built a generator that produces technetium-99m from molybdenum-99, which made Tc-99m cheap and available in any hospital. Its 6-hour half-life and clean gamma ray made it ideal for imaging. In the 1980s chemist Alan Davison at MIT and colleagues, working with Alun Jones at Harvard, developed a family of technetium isonitrile complexes. One of them, sestamibi, gave good images of heart muscle blood flow. DuPont marketed it as Cardiolite after FDA approval in 1990. It was later also approved for breast imaging (Miraluma) and is widely used to find overactive parathyroid glands.", "timeline": [["1937", "Perrier and Segre discover technetium."], ["1950s", "Brookhaven scientists develop the molybdenum-99/technetium-99m generator."], ["1980s", "Davison and Jones develop technetium isonitrile complexes including sestamibi."], ["1990", "The FDA approves sestamibi as Cardiolite for heart perfusion imaging."]], "cell": "Tc-99m sestamibi is a positively charged, fat-soluble complex of one technetium atom surrounded by six methoxyisobutyl isonitrile groups. Because it is lipophilic, it diffuses passively across cell membranes. The negative charge inside the cell, and the even stronger negative charge across the inner mitochondrial membrane, pull the cation into mitochondria, where it is held. Uptake is roughly proportional to blood flow and needs living cells with working mitochondria, so dead or scarred tissue takes up little. Sestamibi barely redistributes after uptake, so images taken later still show blood flow at the time of injection. Tc-99m emits a 140 keV gamma ray detected by SPECT. Sestamibi is also a substrate of P-glycoprotein, so tumors that pump it out may show less uptake.", "body": [["Heart muscle", "Areas with normal blood flow take up tracer; areas with reduced flow during stress show less uptake, revealing coronary artery disease."], ["Scarred heart tissue", "Dead muscle after a heart attack takes up little tracer at rest or stress, separating scar from ischemia."], ["Parathyroid glands", "Overactive parathyroid tissue, rich in mitochondria, holds the tracer longer than the thyroid, which helps surgeons find adenomas."], ["Breast", "Some breast cancers take up more tracer than normal breast tissue, which is used in molecular breast imaging."], ["Liver and gut", "The tracer is cleared through bile into the bowel, which can interfere with images of the bottom of the heart."]], "journey": "Sestamibi is injected into a vein at rest and during exercise or drug-induced stress. Heart muscle takes it up within minutes, and imaging is done about 15 to 60 minutes later; a fatty meal or milk may be given to speed clearance from the gallbladder. The complex is not metabolized. It leaves the body mainly through the liver into bile and stool, with a smaller amount in urine. Tc-99m decays with a physical half-life of about 6 hours.", "sidefx": "At tracer doses, sestamibi has no meaningful drug effect. Many people notice a brief metallic or bitter taste or an odd smell at injection, likely from small amounts reaching taste and smell receptors. Headache and flushing can occur, and allergic reactions are rare. Chest pain, arrhythmias or heart attack during the test come from the stress itself, not the tracer. The main risk is a small radiation dose. There is no antidote, and none is needed.", "fun": "Technetium's name comes from the Greek word technetos, meaning artificial, because it was the first element made by people rather than found in nature.", "src": ["Cardiolite (technetium Tc 99m sestamibi) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "tc99m-medronate": {"origin": "Technetium-99m became widely available in the 1960s thanks to the molybdenum generator developed at Brookhaven National Laboratory. Bone imaging needed a carrier that would stick to bone mineral. In the early 1970s Gopal Subramanian and John McAfee at Upstate Medical Center in Syracuse, New York, showed that technetium-labeled phosphate compounds collect in bone. Phosphates with a carbon backbone (diphosphonates), related to bisphosphonate drugs, proved more stable and cleared faster from soft tissue. Methylene diphosphonate (MDP, medronate) became the standard bone scan agent by the late 1970s and remains one of the most common nuclear medicine studies in the world.", "timeline": [["1937", "Technetium is discovered by Perrier and Segre."], ["1960s", "Tc-99m generators make the isotope widely available."], ["1971", "Subramanian and McAfee introduce technetium-labeled phosphates for bone imaging."], ["1970s", "Tc-99m medronate becomes the standard bone scan agent."]], "cell": "Medronate is a diphosphonate, with two phosphonate groups joined by a carbon (a P-C-P backbone). That shape resists breakdown and binds tightly to calcium in hydroxyapatite, the mineral of bone. Labeled with Tc-99m, it attaches to the surface of bone crystals by chemisorption. Uptake depends on two things: how much blood reaches the bone and how active the osteoblasts are in laying down new mineral. Areas with fresh bone formation, such as healing fractures, infection, and many bone metastases, take up more tracer and appear as hot spots. Purely lytic lesions with little new bone formation, such as multiple myeloma, may be missed. Tc-99m emits a 140 keV gamma ray detected by a gamma camera.", "body": [["Bone", "Collects where bone turnover and blood flow are high, showing metastases, stress fractures, infection and Paget disease as hot spots."], ["Kidneys and bladder", "About half the tracer that does not bind bone is filtered into the urine, so the kidneys and bladder appear on images."], ["Soft tissue", "Background soft tissue activity fades over a few hours, which is why images are taken two to four hours after injection."], ["Abnormal calcification", "Can collect in soft tissue calcification, damaged heart muscle or some tumors, which may show up as unexpected uptake."]], "journey": "Tc-99m medronate is injected into a vein. It spreads through the blood and binds bone within minutes. Over the next few hours, the part not bound to bone is filtered by the kidneys and excreted in the urine, which clears soft tissue background. Patients are encouraged to drink fluids and urinate often to reduce bladder radiation. Images are usually taken two to four hours after injection, sometimes with early flow images right after. The compound is not metabolized, and Tc-99m decays with a half-life of about 6 hours.", "sidefx": "At the tiny tracer amounts used, medronate has no measurable drug effect. Rare reactions such as rash, itching or nausea have been reported, and anaphylaxis is very rare. The main risk is a small radiation dose, mostly to the bone surfaces and bladder wall, reduced by hydration and frequent urination. There is no antidote, and none is needed.", "src": ["Technetium Tc 99m Medronate (MDP) kit FDA label", "Subramanian G, McAfee JG. A new complex of 99mTc for skeletal imaging, Radiology 1971"]}, "regadenoson": {"origin": "Adenosine and dipyridamole were long used to dilate coronary arteries for heart stress tests in people who could not exercise. Adenosine works well but acts on all four adenosine receptor subtypes, causing AV block, bronchospasm and chest discomfort, and it must be infused continuously. CV Therapeutics, a California company, set out to design a selective agonist of the A2A receptor, the subtype that dilates coronary arterioles. The result, regadenoson, could be given as a single quick injection. It was developed with Astellas and approved by the FDA as Lexiscan in 2008. It quickly became the most common drug used for pharmacologic stress testing in the US.", "timeline": [["1929", "Drury and Szent-Gyorgyi describe heart effects of adenosine."], ["1990s", "Adenosine becomes a pharmacologic stress agent for perfusion imaging."], ["2008", "The FDA approves regadenoson as Lexiscan."]], "cell": "Regadenoson is a selective, low-affinity agonist at the adenosine A2A receptor. A2A receptors on coronary arteriolar smooth muscle are Gs-coupled. Binding activates adenylyl cyclase and raises cAMP, which activates protein kinase A and opens potassium channels, relaxing the smooth muscle. Coronary blood flow rises two to three times. Normal arteries dilate fully, but arteries narrowed by plaque are already dilated downstream and cannot increase flow further. This creates a difference in flow between healthy and diseased areas, which perfusion tracers like sestamibi reveal. Regadenoson has much weaker action at A1 receptors (AV node), A2B and A3 receptors (airways, mast cells) than adenosine, so AV block and bronchospasm are less common but can still occur.", "body": [["Coronary arteries", "Dilates coronary arterioles and raises blood flow so narrowed arteries can be found on perfusion imaging."], ["Heart rate and rhythm", "Heart rate rises from a reflex to vasodilation and sympathetic activation; AV block is less common than with adenosine but can occur."], ["Blood pressure", "Causes a mild drop in blood pressure from systemic vasodilation; rarely it rises."], ["Lungs", "Shortness of breath is common, and bronchospasm can occur, especially in people with asthma or COPD."], ["Brain", "Headache and dizziness are common; seizures have been reported, especially with a history of seizures."], ["Gut", "Nausea and stomach discomfort can occur during the test."]], "journey": "Regadenoson is given as a rapid IV injection followed by a saline flush, and the tracer is injected shortly after. Coronary flow rises within a minute and peaks within one to four minutes. Its levels fall in three phases: an early phase of a few minutes, a middle phase of about 30 minutes when the drug effect wears off, and a slower final phase of about two hours. It is barely metabolized and is not handled by CYP enzymes. More than half is excreted unchanged in the urine.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from adenosine receptor activation. Flushing, headache and dizziness come from vasodilation. Chest discomfort and shortness of breath are common and are thought to involve A2B receptors and sensory nerve activation. Activity at A1 receptors in the AV node can cause heart block. Bronchospasm is linked to A2B and A3 receptors in the airways. Seizures may relate to adenosine receptor effects in the brain. Myocardial ischemia can occur when flow is diverted from narrowed arteries. Aminophylline, an adenosine receptor antagonist, is used to reverse severe or persistent effects. Caffeine before the test blocks the drug's effect, which is why it is avoided.", "src": ["Lexiscan (regadenoson) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "aminophylline": {"origin": "Aminophylline is theophylline paired with ethylenediamine to make it more water soluble. Theophylline is a methylxanthine found in small amounts in tea, which is where its name comes from (Greek theos, god, and phyllon, leaf, from Thea, the old name for the tea plant). German scientist Albrecht Kossel isolated it from tea leaves in 1888, and it was soon made synthetically. Theophylline was first used as a diuretic in the early 1900s. In the 1920s and 1930s doctors found it relaxed the airways, and IV aminophylline became a standard treatment for severe asthma for decades. Today it is mostly a second-line drug, but it remains useful to reverse the effects of adenosine-receptor stress agents like regadenoson.", "timeline": [["1888", "Albrecht Kossel isolates theophylline from tea leaves."], ["1900s", "Theophylline is used as a diuretic."], ["1930s", "IV aminophylline is used to treat severe asthma attacks."], ["2000s", "Aminophylline is used to reverse pharmacologic stress agents."]], "cell": "Aminophylline releases theophylline in the body. Theophylline competitively blocks adenosine A1 and A2 receptors. That reverses adenosine-driven effects such as coronary vasodilation from regadenoson, AV node slowing and airway narrowing. It also nonselectively inhibits phosphodiesterases, especially PDE3 and PDE4, which normally break down cAMP. Higher cAMP in airway smooth muscle activates protein kinase A and relaxes the muscle, opening the airways. In inflammatory cells, PDE4 inhibition reduces mediator release. Theophylline also strengthens diaphragm contraction and stimulates the brainstem breathing center. At higher levels it may activate histone deacetylase, reducing inflammatory gene expression. Its therapeutic window is narrow.", "body": [["Airways", "Relaxes bronchial smooth muscle through PDE inhibition and adenosine blockade, easing bronchospasm."], ["Heart", "Speeds the heart rate and increases contractility by raising cAMP and blocking adenosine; reverses adenosine-related AV block."], ["Brain", "Stimulates breathing drive, alertness and at high levels can cause seizures by blocking calming adenosine signals."], ["Diaphragm", "Improves diaphragm strength and lowers fatigue."], ["Kidneys", "Causes a mild diuresis through adenosine A1 blockade in the kidney."], ["Gut and metabolism", "Causes nausea, vomiting and acid secretion; at toxic levels it causes low potassium and high blood glucose through catecholamine release."]], "journey": "Aminophylline is usually given IV, where it quickly frees theophylline. Theophylline spreads widely, about 40 percent bound to protein, and crosses into the brain and placenta. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP1A2, with help from CYP2E1 and CYP3A4. Its half-life is about 8 hours in healthy nonsmoking adults, shorter in smokers and children, and much longer in newborns, older adults and people with liver disease or heart failure. Many drugs that inhibit CYP1A2, like ciprofloxacin and fluvoxamine, raise levels. In newborns, part of it is turned into caffeine. Metabolites leave in the urine.", "sidefx": "Theophylline toxicity reflects its mechanisms at too-high levels. PDE inhibition and catecholamine release cause nausea, vomiting, tremor, fast heart rate and arrhythmias such as multifocal atrial tachycardia and ventricular ectopy. Beta-adrenergic stimulation shifts potassium into cells, causing hypokalemia, and raises glucose. Blocking adenosine in the brain removes a natural seizure brake, so seizures can be severe and hard to stop. There is no specific antidote; activated charcoal (including repeat doses), benzodiazepines, beta blockers for some arrhythmias, and hemodialysis for severe cases are used.", "src": ["Aminophylline injection FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "potassium-iodide": {"origin": "Iodine was discovered in 1811 by French chemist Bernard Courtois, who found violet vapor rising from seaweed ash while making gunpowder ingredients. In 1820 Swiss doctor Jean-Francois Coindet used iodine to shrink goiters. In 1923 Henry Plummer at the Mayo Clinic showed that iodine given before surgery made Graves disease safer to operate on. In 1948 Jan Wolff and Israel Chaikoff described how large amounts of iodide briefly shut down thyroid hormone production, now called the Wolff-Chaikoff effect. During the Cold War, scientists recognized that stable iodide could block radioactive iodine from entering the thyroid. The FDA approved potassium iodide for thyroid protection in radiation emergencies in 1982. After the 1986 Chernobyl disaster, Poland gave KI to millions of children, a response often cited when planning for nuclear emergencies.", "timeline": [["1811", "Bernard Courtois discovers iodine in seaweed ash."], ["1923", "Henry Plummer uses iodine to prepare Graves disease patients for surgery."], ["1948", "Wolff and Chaikoff describe iodide's blocking effect on hormone synthesis."], ["1982", "The FDA approves KI for thyroid blocking in radiation emergencies."], ["1986", "Poland gives KI widely to children after the Chernobyl accident."]], "cell": "Thyroid follicular cells pull iodide from the blood through the sodium-iodide symporter (NIS) on their basolateral membrane. A flood of stable iodide from KI competes with radioactive iodide for this transporter, diluting it so far less radioiodine is trapped in the gland. At high iodide levels inside the cell, thyroid peroxidase organification of tyrosine residues on thyroglobulin is inhibited, which is the Wolff-Chaikoff effect, so less T4 and T3 are made. Iodide also blocks proteolysis of thyroglobulin, so stored hormone is released more slowly. In Graves disease, high iodide reduces blood flow to the gland, making surgery less bloody. Normal glands escape the Wolff-Chaikoff effect within days to weeks by lowering NIS expression.", "body": [["Thyroid in radiation emergencies", "Fills the thyroid with stable iodide so radioactive iodine is not absorbed, lowering future thyroid cancer risk, especially in children."], ["Thyroid in hyperthyroidism", "Rapidly blocks hormone release and synthesis, helping control thyroid storm and preparing the gland for surgery."], ["Airways", "Saturated solution (SSKI) has been used as an expectorant because iodide is secreted into respiratory mucus."], ["Salivary glands", "Iodide is concentrated in saliva, which can cause gland swelling and a metallic taste."], ["Skin", "Rash and acne-like eruptions can occur from iodide sensitivity."], ["Newborns", "Repeated exposure can cause neonatal hypothyroidism because the infant thyroid escapes the block less effectively."]], "journey": "Potassium iodide is taken by mouth as a tablet or solution and is absorbed quickly from the gut. Iodide spreads through extracellular fluid and is concentrated by the thyroid, salivary glands, stomach lining and breast tissue. Thyroid protection begins within about 30 minutes and lasts about a day. It is not metabolized. The kidneys excrete most of it in urine within hours to days, and small amounts appear in breast milk, saliva and sweat.", "sidefx": "Stomach upset and metallic taste come from local irritation and secretion into saliva. Iodism, with a burning mouth, runny nose, swollen salivary glands and acne-like rash, reflects iodide building up in glands and skin. In people with nodular goiter or Graves disease, extra iodide can trigger hyperthyroidism (the Jod-Basedow effect). In others, especially with autoimmune thyroiditis, the gland may fail to escape the Wolff-Chaikoff effect, leading to hypothyroidism. Rare allergic reactions, including angioedema, occur. There is no specific antidote; stopping the drug and supportive care are used.", "fun": "Iodine's name comes from the Greek iodes, meaning violet, after the purple vapor Courtois saw.", "src": ["iOSAT and ThyroSafe (potassium iodide) FDA labels", "FDA Guidance: Potassium Iodide as a Thyroid Blocking Agent in Radiation Emergencies", "Wolff J, Chaikoff IL. J Biol Chem 1948"]}, "sincalide": {"origin": "In 1928 Andrew Ivy and Eric Oldberg at Northwestern University found that a substance from the upper small intestine made the gallbladder contract and named it cholecystokinin (CCK), Greek for 'moves the gallbladder.' In 1943 Alan Harper and Henry Raper found a gut hormone that stimulated pancreatic enzymes, which they called pancreozymin. In the 1960s Swedish chemists Erik Jorpes and Viktor Mutt purified the hormone and showed both actions came from the same peptide. Researchers then found that the last eight amino acids carry its full activity. That synthetic C-terminal octapeptide, sincalide, was developed by Squibb and introduced as Kinevac in the 1970s for gallbladder and pancreatic function testing.", "timeline": [["1928", "Ivy and Oldberg describe cholecystokinin."], ["1943", "Harper and Raper describe pancreozymin."], ["1960s", "Jorpes and Mutt show CCK and pancreozymin are the same peptide."], ["1970s", "Synthetic CCK-8 (sincalide) is introduced as Kinevac."]], "cell": "Sincalide is the C-terminal eight-amino-acid fragment of cholecystokinin, with a sulfated tyrosine needed for full activity. It binds the CCK type A receptor (CCK1), a Gq-coupled receptor on gallbladder smooth muscle. Gq activates phospholipase C, producing IP3 and diacylglycerol, which raise intracellular calcium and cause the gallbladder to contract. At the same time, CCK relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, partly through inhibitory nerves that release nitric oxide and VIP, so bile flows into the duodenum. On pancreatic acinar cells, CCK receptors trigger release of digestive enzymes. It also speeds gut motility. These actions mimic the hormone's normal response to fatty food in the duodenum.", "body": [["Gallbladder", "Contracts and empties within minutes; measuring how much empties on a HIDA scan (ejection fraction) helps diagnose gallbladder dysfunction."], ["Sphincter of Oddi", "Relaxes so bile and pancreatic juice flow into the duodenum."], ["Pancreas", "Stimulates enzyme release, which is used with secretin in pancreatic function testing."], ["Intestines", "Speeds small bowel transit and can cause cramping and an urge to have a bowel movement."], ["Circulation", "Flushing and dizziness can occur, and rarely low blood pressure."]], "journey": "Sincalide is given into a vein, either as a slow injection or as an infusion during imaging. The gallbladder begins to contract within minutes, with maximum emptying in about 5 to 15 minutes. Peptidases in blood and tissues break it down quickly, so its effect is short and ends soon after the infusion stops. Its elimination has not been well studied.", "sidefx": "Most side effects reflect CCK's normal actions taken to an extreme. Strong gallbladder and intestinal contraction cause abdominal cramping, nausea and an urge to defecate, especially with fast injection, which is why slow infusion is preferred. Flushing and dizziness come from mild vasodilation. In people with gallstones, strong contraction may push a stone into the duct. Hypersensitivity and anaphylaxis are rare. There is no antidote; effects fade quickly once it is stopped.", "src": ["Kinevac (sincalide) FDA label", "Ivy AC, Oldberg E. Am J Physiol 1928"]}, "secretin": {"origin": "Secretin was the first hormone ever discovered. In 1902 William Bayliss and Ernest Starling at University College London showed that acid in the small intestine caused the pancreas to secrete fluid even when the nerves were cut. They concluded a chemical messenger traveled through the blood and named it secretin. In 1905 Starling coined the word hormone, from the Greek for 'to excite' or 'set in motion.' Erik Jorpes and Viktor Mutt in Stockholm purified secretin in the 1960s and determined its 27-amino-acid sequence. For years, natural porcine secretin was used in testing. A synthetic human secretin, ChiRhoStim, was approved by the FDA in the 2000s for pancreatic function and gastrinoma testing, and later for helping see pancreatic ducts during MRCP.", "timeline": [["1902", "Bayliss and Starling discover secretin."], ["1905", "Starling introduces the word hormone."], ["1960s", "Jorpes and Mutt purify and sequence secretin."], ["2000s", "Synthetic human secretin is approved for diagnostic use in the US."]], "cell": "Secretin is a 27-amino-acid peptide. It binds the secretin receptor, a class B G protein-coupled receptor, on pancreatic duct cells. The receptor activates Gs, which raises cAMP and activates protein kinase A. PKA opens the CFTR chloride channel on the duct lining. Chloride flows into the duct and is swapped for bicarbonate, and water follows, producing a large volume of bicarbonate-rich fluid that neutralizes stomach acid in the duodenum. In normal stomach G cells, secretin lowers gastrin release. Gastrinoma cells in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome have secretin receptors, and secretin makes them release gastrin, so a rise in gastrin after secretin points to a gastrinoma.", "body": [["Pancreas", "Duct cells pour out bicarbonate-rich fluid; measuring the output tests how well the pancreas works in chronic pancreatitis."], ["Pancreatic ducts on MRI", "Extra fluid fills and widens the ducts, improving MRCP images of duct problems."], ["Stomach", "Normally lowers gastrin and acid secretion; gastrinomas react paradoxically by releasing gastrin, which is the basis of the secretin stimulation test."], ["Duodenum", "Fluid fills the duodenum within minutes, which can cause a feeling of fullness or nausea."], ["Liver and bile ducts", "Also increases bicarbonate-rich bile flow from bile duct cells."]], "journey": "Synthetic human secretin is given as a quick IV injection. Pancreatic secretion peaks within about 5 to 10 minutes, and in gastrinoma testing gastrin levels rise within about 10 minutes. The peptide is broken down by peptidases, and its half-life is about 45 minutes. The response lasts about 30 to 60 minutes. Its elimination route has not been fully described.", "sidefx": "Secretin is usually well tolerated. Nausea, flushing, abdominal discomfort and diarrhea come from sudden fluid release into the gut and mild vasodilation. Allergic reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, can happen with any peptide. Drugs like anticholinergics can blunt the response, and proton pump inhibitors can falsely raise gastrin, which affects testing. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Bayliss and Starling's 1902 secretin discovery launched the field of endocrinology, the study of hormones.", "src": ["ChiRhoStim (human secretin) FDA label", "Bayliss WM, Starling EH. J Physiol 1902"]}, "caffeine-citrate": {"origin": "Caffeine was first isolated from coffee beans in 1819 by German chemist Friedlieb Ferdinand Runge, reportedly after Johann Wolfgang von Goethe encouraged him to study coffee. In the 1970s doctors studying preterm babies found that methylxanthines such as theophylline and caffeine reduced apnea of prematurity, pauses in breathing caused by an immature brainstem. Caffeine was preferred because it has a wider safety margin and a long half-life in newborns, allowing once-daily dosing. The FDA approved caffeine citrate as Cafcit in 1999 for short-term treatment of apnea of prematurity. The Caffeine for Apnea of Prematurity (CAP) trial, published in 2006 and 2007, showed it also reduced bronchopulmonary dysplasia and improved survival without neurodevelopmental disability at 18 to 21 months.", "timeline": [["1819", "Friedlieb Runge isolates caffeine from coffee."], ["1970s", "Methylxanthines are shown to reduce apnea in preterm infants."], ["1999", "The FDA approves Cafcit for apnea of prematurity."], ["2006", "The CAP trial shows lower rates of bronchopulmonary dysplasia with caffeine."]], "cell": "Caffeine is a methylxanthine that competitively blocks adenosine receptors. Adenosine normally acts on A1 and A2A receptors in the brain to slow neuron firing and depress breathing, and in preterm infants this braking effect is strong. By blocking these receptors in the medullary respiratory centers, caffeine increases breathing drive, raises sensitivity to carbon dioxide and makes breathing more regular. It also increases diaphragm contractility and minute ventilation. Only at much higher concentrations does caffeine inhibit phosphodiesterases and raise intracellular cAMP, or affect calcium release from intracellular stores. The citrate form is simply a salt that dissolves easily and contains half its weight as caffeine base.", "body": [["Brainstem", "Blocks adenosine in the breathing centers, which reduces apneic pauses and the need for mechanical ventilation."], ["Lungs and diaphragm", "Increases diaphragm strength and minute ventilation, helping infants come off breathing support sooner."], ["Heart", "Mildly increases heart rate; tachycardia is a sign of excess."], ["Brain development", "In the CAP trial, caffeine-treated infants had better neurodevelopmental outcomes at 18 to 21 months."], ["Kidneys", "Blocks adenosine A1 receptors in the kidney, causing a mild diuresis."], ["Gut", "Can cause feeding intolerance and reflux; necrotizing enterocolitis has been reported, though a cause is not proven."]], "journey": "Caffeine citrate is given IV or by mouth to infants and is almost fully absorbed when given orally. It spreads through total body water and easily enters the brain. In preterm newborns, the liver enzyme CYP1A2 is immature, so caffeine is barely metabolized and is cleared mostly unchanged by the kidneys. Its half-life is very long, about 3 to 4 days, which allows once-daily dosing. As the liver matures over the first months of life, demethylation increases and the half-life drops to about 5 hours, similar to adults.", "sidefx": "Side effects come from too much adenosine blockade and stimulation. Tachycardia, jitteriness and irritability reflect central and cardiac stimulation. Feeding intolerance and regurgitation reflect increased gut acid and motility. The mild diuresis can affect fluid balance. At toxic levels, seizures, arrhythmias, high or low blood glucose and metabolic acidosis can occur. Because the half-life is long in newborns, levels build up and stay high for days after stopping. There is no antidote; care is supportive, and in severe cases exchange transfusion or dialysis has been used.", "fun": "Preterm infants given caffeine can take 3 to 4 days to clear half of it, compared with about 5 hours for most adults.", "src": ["Cafcit (caffeine citrate) FDA label", "Schmidt B et al. Caffeine for Apnea of Prematurity trial, N Engl J Med 2006 and 2007"]}, "poractant-alfa": {"origin": "In 1959 Mary Ellen Avery and Jere Mead at Harvard showed that babies who died of respiratory distress syndrome lacked surfactant, the soapy substance that keeps air sacs from collapsing. Treatment took decades to develop. In 1980 Tetsuro Fujiwara in Japan reported the first successful treatment of preterm babies with a surfactant made from cow lungs. In Sweden, Bengt Robertson and Tore Curstedt at the Karolinska Institute developed a surfactant extract from minced pig lungs. The Italian company Chiesi brought it to market as Curosurf, and multicenter European trials in the late 1980s showed it saved lives. The FDA approved poractant alfa in 1999 for treatment of respiratory distress syndrome in preterm infants.", "timeline": [["1959", "Avery and Mead link infant respiratory distress syndrome to surfactant deficiency."], ["1980", "Fujiwara reports the first successful surfactant treatment in preterm infants."], ["1980s", "Robertson and Curstedt develop porcine surfactant at Karolinska."], ["1999", "The FDA approves Curosurf in the US."]], "cell": "Poractant alfa is a natural extract of pig lung surfactant. It contains phospholipids, mainly dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (DPPC), plus the small hydrophobic proteins SP-B and SP-C. Surfactant is normally made by alveolar type II cells. When given into the trachea, the phospholipids spread quickly across the air-liquid surface of the alveoli. SP-B and SP-C help them adsorb and form a stable film. As the alveolus shrinks during exhalation, the DPPC film packs tightly and lowers surface tension to near zero. By the law of Laplace, lower surface tension prevents small alveoli from collapsing at end-expiration. The lungs become easier to inflate, functional residual capacity rises and gas exchange improves.", "body": [["Alveoli", "Keeps air sacs open at the end of each breath, preventing the widespread collapse of respiratory distress syndrome."], ["Lung mechanics", "Compliance improves within minutes to hours, so lower ventilator pressures and oxygen levels are needed."], ["Oxygenation", "Better-open alveoli improve oxygen and carbon dioxide exchange, often quickly after dosing."], ["Heart rate", "Brief bradycardia and drops in oxygen saturation can happen during dosing from airway obstruction or vagal response."], ["Lung injury", "Sudden improvement in compliance can cause overdistension and air leaks such as pneumothorax if ventilator settings are not lowered."], ["Pulmonary blood vessels", "Pulmonary hemorrhage is a rare serious complication, possibly related to shifts in pulmonary blood flow."]], "journey": "Poractant alfa is given directly into the lungs through an endotracheal tube or a thin catheter. It spreads through the airways into the alveoli within minutes. Very little enters the blood. The surfactant lipids are recycled by alveolar type II cells, which take them up and reuse them, and are also broken down by alveolar macrophages. Repeat doses may be needed if surfactant is inactivated by plasma proteins leaking into the alveoli.", "sidefx": "Most problems occur during dosing. The liquid can block the airway or tube briefly, causing bradycardia and oxygen desaturation, and some may reflux up the tube. When the lungs suddenly become more compliant, the same ventilator pressure can overinflate them and cause pneumothorax, so settings are adjusted quickly. Pulmonary hemorrhage is a rare but serious event. Because poractant comes from pig lungs, it contains animal proteins, but immune reactions are not a major issue. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Curosurf (poractant alfa) FDA label", "Avery ME, Mead J. Am J Dis Child 1959", "Fujiwara T et al. Lancet 1980"]}, "alprostadil": {"origin": "Alprostadil is prostaglandin E1. In the 1930s Ulf von Euler in Sweden studied substances in semen that lowered blood pressure and contracted smooth muscle, and he named them prostaglandins because he thought they came from the prostate. In the late 1950s and early 1960s Sune Bergstrom and colleagues at the Karolinska Institute isolated PGE1 and other prostaglandins and worked out their structures. Bergstrom, Bengt Samuelsson and John Vane shared the 1982 Nobel Prize for prostaglandin research. In the mid-1970s pediatric cardiologists, including Peter Olley and Flavio Coceani in Toronto, showed that PGE1 could keep the ductus arteriosus open in newborns with duct-dependent heart defects. Upjohn's Prostin VR Pediatric was approved in the US in the early 1980s. Later, alprostadil was approved for erectile dysfunction as Caverject injection and MUSE urethral pellets in the 1990s.", "timeline": [["1930s", "Ulf von Euler describes and names prostaglandins."], ["1960s", "Bergstrom's group isolates PGE1 and determines its structure."], ["1970s", "PGE1 is shown to keep the ductus arteriosus open in newborns."], ["1982", "Bergstrom, Samuelsson and Vane share the Nobel Prize for prostaglandin work."], ["1990s", "Alprostadil is approved for erectile dysfunction."]], "cell": "Alprostadil binds prostaglandin E (EP) receptors, mainly the Gs-coupled EP2 and EP4 subtypes, on smooth muscle of the ductus arteriosus and other blood vessels. Gs activates adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP, which activates protein kinase A. PKA lowers intracellular calcium and reduces myosin light-chain kinase activity, so the muscle relaxes. Before birth, low oxygen and high PGE2 from the placenta keep the ductus open. After birth, oxygen rises and PGE2 falls, and the ductus constricts. Giving PGE1 restores the relaxing signal and reopens or keeps open the ductus, supplying blood to the lungs or body in duct-dependent heart defects. In the penis, the same cAMP pathway relaxes corpus cavernosum smooth muscle, letting blood fill the erectile tissue. It also inhibits platelet aggregation.", "body": [["Ductus arteriosus", "Keeps the ductus open, allowing blood to reach the lungs or body in newborns with critical congenital heart defects until surgery."], ["Systemic vessels", "Dilates blood vessels, which can cause flushing and low blood pressure."], ["Brainstem", "Apnea is a common serious effect in newborns, especially those under 2 kg, so breathing must be closely watched."], ["Temperature", "Fever is common, reflecting prostaglandin action on the hypothalamus."], ["Bones and gut", "Long-term infusion can cause thickening of the bone surface (cortical hyperostosis) and gastric outlet obstruction from overgrowth of the stomach lining."], ["Penis", "Relaxes cavernosal smooth muscle to produce an erection; overstimulation can cause priapism."]], "journey": "In newborns, alprostadil is given as a continuous IV infusion, or sometimes through an umbilical artery, because it is broken down very fast. Up to about 80 percent is destroyed in a single pass through the lungs by oxidation, and its half-life is about 5 to 10 minutes. The ductus responds within about 15 minutes to a few hours. The inactive metabolites are excreted by the kidneys. If the infusion stops, the ductus can begin to close within hours. For erectile dysfunction it is injected into the penis or placed in the urethra, and little reaches the blood.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from prostaglandin effects in other tissues. Apnea in newborns is thought to come from prostaglandin effects on the breathing centers, and it often appears within the first hour. Vasodilation causes flushing, low blood pressure and sometimes bradycardia or tachycardia. Prostaglandins act on the hypothalamus to cause fever. Long infusions stimulate bone and stomach lining growth, causing cortical hyperostosis and gastric outlet obstruction. In adults, injection into the penis can cause pain, prolonged erection and fibrosis. There is no specific antidote; stopping or lowering the infusion and supportive care, including intubation for apnea, are used.", "src": ["Prostin VR Pediatric (alprostadil) FDA label", "Caverject (alprostadil) FDA label", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1982 materials"]}, "bpc-157": {"origin": "BPC-157, short for Body Protection Compound-157, is a synthetic 15-amino-acid peptide. It was developed and studied mainly by Predrag Sikiric and colleagues at the University of Zagreb in Croatia starting in the early 1990s. They described it as a fragment of a protein found in human gastric juice and reported that it protected the stomach and helped tissue healing in rats. Most published research comes from this one group and is in animals. It is not FDA approved for any use, and human evidence is very limited. It is sold online as an injectable vial, capsule or nasal spray for injury healing. In 2023 the FDA listed BPC-157 among substances that raise significant safety concerns for compounding, and it is banned in sport by the World Anti-Doping Agency.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Sikiric and colleagues in Zagreb begin publishing animal studies of BPC-157."], ["2000s", "A small development program (PL 14736) tests it for inflammatory bowel disease."], ["2023", "The FDA lists BPC-157 among bulk substances with safety concerns for compounding."]], "cell": "How BPC-157 works in humans is not established. In animal and cell studies, it has been proposed to increase signaling through the vascular endothelial growth factor receptor 2 (VEGFR2) pathway, which promotes new blood vessel growth. Studies also suggest it interacts with the nitric oxide system and increases growth hormone receptor expression on tendon cells. Some work points to activation of the FAK-paxillin pathway, which helps cells move and attach during wound healing. These findings come mostly from rodent models of gut, tendon, muscle and nerve injury. Because angiogenesis also supports tumor growth, scientists have raised the theoretical concern that it could promote cancer blood supply.", "body": [["Stomach and gut", "Animal studies report protection against ulcers and faster healing of bowel injury; human benefit is not proven."], ["Tendons and muscle", "Rodent studies report faster healing of tendon and muscle injuries, the main reason people seek it."], ["Blood vessels", "Proposed to stimulate angiogenesis, which could aid healing but raises concern about tumor growth."], ["Injection sites", "Injection of unregulated products can cause pain, redness, abscess or cellulitis."], ["Immune system", "Contaminated or impure peptides can cause allergic reactions or infection."]], "journey": "How BPC-157 moves through the human body is unknown. Animal data suggest it is stable in gastric juice, which is why it is sold in oral forms, but its absorption, distribution, breakdown and elimination have not been well studied in humans. In animals the plasma half-life appears short. Gray-market products vary in purity and content, so what a user actually receives is uncertain.", "sidefx": "Because BPC-157 is not FDA approved, its side effect profile is not established. Reported problems include injection-site pain and redness, nausea, dizziness and headache. The main known risks come from product quality: gray-market vials can be contaminated with bacteria or endotoxin, leading to abscesses or sepsis, or contain the wrong substance or amount. Its proposed angiogenic effect raises a theoretical risk of promoting tumors. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "src": ["FDA: Certain Bulk Drug Substances for Use in Compounding that May Present Significant Safety Risks (2023)", "World Anti-Doping Agency Prohibited List", "Sikiric P et al. review articles on BPC 157"]}, "tb-500": {"origin": "TB-500 is a synthetic peptide based on thymosin beta-4, a natural 43-amino-acid protein. Thymosin beta-4 was first isolated in 1981 from calf thymus extracts by Teresa Low, Allan Goldstein and colleagues at George Washington University while they studied thymic hormones. Researchers later found it is not mainly a thymic hormone; it is found in nearly all cells and binds actin. The company RegeneRx studied full-length thymosin beta-4 for wound and eye healing. TB-500 is a short fragment sold on the gray market, used in horse racing and among athletes and bodybuilders for injury recovery. It is not FDA approved, human evidence is very limited, and it is banned by the World Anti-Doping Agency. In 2023 the FDA listed thymosin beta-4 fragment among substances with safety concerns for compounding.", "timeline": [["1981", "Thymosin beta-4 is isolated from calf thymus by Goldstein's group."], ["1990s", "Thymosin beta-4 is identified as a major actin-sequestering protein."], ["2000s", "Full-length thymosin beta-4 is tested in early human wound and eye studies."], ["2023", "The FDA lists thymosin beta-4 fragment among bulk substances with safety concerns."]], "cell": "The mechanism of the TB-500 fragment itself is not established. Full-length thymosin beta-4 binds monomeric G-actin and keeps it from polymerizing into filaments, acting as a buffer that the cell can draw on when it needs to move. In preclinical studies, thymosin beta-4 promoted cell migration, new blood vessel growth, wound closure and reduced inflammation, and it activated repair pathways in injured heart tissue in mice. The fragment sold as TB-500 contains the actin-binding region, and sellers claim it shares these effects, but direct evidence is limited. Because it supports cell movement and angiogenesis, there is a theoretical concern it could promote tumor growth or spread.", "body": [["Skin and wounds", "Full-length thymosin beta-4 sped wound healing in animal models; human proof is limited."], ["Muscles and tendons", "Users take it hoping for faster recovery from injuries, a use not supported by solid human trials."], ["Blood vessels", "Proposed to promote angiogenesis, which raises concern about effects on tumors."], ["Heart", "Mouse studies suggested heart repair effects after injury; these have not been confirmed in people."], ["Injection sites", "Unregulated injections can cause abscesses, infection and allergic reactions."]], "journey": "How TB-500 is absorbed, distributed, broken down and eliminated in humans is unknown. It is usually injected under the skin or into muscle. Full-length thymosin beta-4 is broken down by peptidases, and some fragments are detectable in urine, which is how anti-doping labs test for it. Product content and purity vary widely.", "sidefx": "Because TB-500 is not FDA approved, its side effect profile is not established. Reported effects include injection-site reactions, headache, fatigue and lightheadedness. The biggest known risks come from poor product quality: contamination can cause abscesses, sepsis or allergic reactions, and vials may contain the wrong amount or a different substance. Its proposed effects on cell migration and angiogenesis raise a theoretical tumor-promoting risk. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "src": ["Low TL, Hu SK, Goldstein AL. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA 1981", "FDA: Certain Bulk Drug Substances for Use in Compounding that May Present Significant Safety Risks (2023)", "World Anti-Doping Agency Prohibited List"]}, "ipamorelin": {"origin": "Ipamorelin is a synthetic five-amino-acid peptide developed at Novo Nordisk in Denmark in the 1990s. It came from a line of research on growth hormone secretagogues, compounds first made in the 1970s and 1980s that release growth hormone through a then-unknown receptor. That receptor was cloned in 1996, and its natural ligand, ghrelin, was discovered in 1999 by Masayasu Kojima and Kenji Kangawa in Japan. A 1998 report described ipamorelin as the first growth hormone secretagogue selective enough to release GH without raising cortisol or prolactin much. It was later tested by other companies for postoperative ileus, but it was not approved. It is not FDA approved, and it is sold on the gray market, often blended with CJC-1295.", "timeline": [["1996", "The growth hormone secretagogue receptor is cloned."], ["1998", "Novo Nordisk researchers describe ipamorelin as a selective GH secretagogue."], ["1999", "Kojima and Kangawa discover ghrelin, the receptor's natural ligand."], ["2000s", "Ipamorelin is tested for postoperative ileus but not approved."]], "cell": "Ipamorelin is an agonist at the growth hormone secretagogue receptor type 1a (GHS-R1a), also called the ghrelin receptor. This is a Gq-coupled receptor on somatotroph cells of the anterior pituitary and on hypothalamic neurons. Activation raises intracellular calcium and triggers release of a pulse of growth hormone. It also boosts GHRH signaling and reduces somatostatin tone in the hypothalamus. Growth hormone then acts on the liver to raise IGF-1. Early studies showed ipamorelin had less effect on ACTH, cortisol and prolactin than older secretagogues. Because the ghrelin receptor also affects appetite and gut motility, it can increase hunger and was studied to speed bowel recovery after surgery.", "body": [["Pituitary", "Releases pulses of growth hormone, which raise IGF-1 levels."], ["Appetite", "Ghrelin receptor activation can increase hunger."], ["Gut", "Increases gut motility, the reason it was studied for postoperative ileus."], ["Metabolism", "GH raises blood glucose and lowers insulin sensitivity, so blood sugar can rise."], ["Fluid balance", "GH and IGF-1 cause salt and water retention, leading to swelling and tingling in the hands."]], "journey": "In early studies, ipamorelin was given by IV or subcutaneous injection. Growth hormone peaked about 30 to 60 minutes after dosing, and ipamorelin had a half-life of about two hours. It was mostly cleared in urine. Its breakdown and full human pharmacokinetics have not been fully described, and gray-market products vary in content.", "sidefx": "Because ipamorelin is not FDA approved, its long-term safety is unknown. Its known effects follow from raising GH: water retention, swelling, joint aches and numbness or tingling in the hands from fluid around nerves. GH counteracts insulin, so blood glucose may rise. Increased hunger reflects ghrelin receptor activation. Injection-site reactions, headache and flushing are reported. Contaminated products can cause infection or allergic reactions. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "src": ["Raun K et al. Ipamorelin, the first selective growth hormone secretagogue, Eur J Endocrinol 1998", "Kojima M et al. Ghrelin, Nature 1999"]}, "cjc-1295": {"origin": "CJC-1295 is a modified version of growth hormone-releasing hormone. The first 29 amino acids of human GHRH carry its full activity, and that fragment was the basis of the drug sermorelin. GHRH(1-29) breaks down within minutes, so the Canadian company ConjuChem, in Montreal, changed several amino acids to resist breakdown and added a chemical linker called drug affinity complex (DAC). DAC bonds the peptide to albumin in the blood, greatly extending how long it lasts. Early human studies published in 2006 showed it raised GH and IGF-1 for days after one injection. Development did not lead to approval. It is not FDA approved, and products are sold on the gray market with or without DAC.", "timeline": [["1982", "Human GHRH is isolated and sequenced."], ["2000s", "ConjuChem develops CJC-1295 with its albumin-binding DAC technology."], ["2006", "Early human studies show prolonged rises in GH and IGF-1."]], "cell": "CJC-1295 is an analog of GHRH(1-29) that binds the GHRH receptor on somatotroph cells of the anterior pituitary. This receptor is Gs-coupled: binding raises cAMP, activates protein kinase A and increases both GH gene transcription and release of stored GH. The amino acid substitutions protect it from dipeptidyl peptidase-4 and other enzymes. In the DAC version, a reactive maleimide group forms a covalent bond with albumin after injection, so the peptide circulates for days instead of minutes. GH release remains pulsatile because somatostatin still controls the pituitary, but baseline GH and IGF-1 stay higher. The version without DAC, often called modified GRF(1-29), has a short half-life.", "body": [["Pituitary", "Stimulates GH production and release, raising GH levels for days with the DAC version."], ["Liver", "Higher GH causes the liver to make more IGF-1, which mediates many growth effects."], ["Muscle and fat", "GH and IGF-1 may increase lean mass and fat breakdown, the reasons people seek it."], ["Metabolism", "GH lowers insulin sensitivity, which can raise blood glucose."], ["Fluid balance", "Water and salt retention can cause swelling and carpal tunnel symptoms."]], "journey": "CJC-1295 is injected under the skin. The DAC version binds albumin and has a half-life of about 6 to 8 days in early human studies, so GH and IGF-1 stay raised for days. The version without DAC lasts only about 30 minutes. It is broken down by proteolysis. Full human elimination data are not available, and gray-market products vary in content and purity.", "sidefx": "Because CJC-1295 is not FDA approved, long-term safety is unknown. Side effects come from prolonged GH and IGF-1 elevation: water retention, swelling, joint pain, tingling and carpal tunnel symptoms. GH blocks insulin action, raising blood glucose. Flushing, headache and injection-site reactions are reported. Prolonged high IGF-1 is a theoretical concern for tumor growth. Contaminated products can cause infection or allergic reactions. There is no antidote; the long half-life of the DAC version means effects last days.", "src": ["Teichman SL et al. J Clin Endocrinol Metab 2006", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "sermorelin": {"origin": "In 1982 two teams found human growth hormone-releasing hormone in pancreatic tumors that were causing acromegaly. Roger Guillemin's group at the Salk Institute and Wylie Vale's group, also at the Salk Institute, each isolated and sequenced the peptide. Researchers then showed the first 29 amino acids carried full activity. That fragment, GHRH(1-29), became sermorelin. Serono marketed it as Geref, which the FDA approved in the 1990s for testing and treating growth hormone deficiency in children. Geref was discontinued in 2008. There is no currently marketed FDA-approved sermorelin product, though compounded versions are sold.", "timeline": [["1982", "Guillemin's and Vale's groups isolate human GHRH from tumors causing acromegaly."], ["1990s", "The FDA approves sermorelin as Geref."], ["2008", "Geref is discontinued in the US."]], "cell": "Sermorelin is the 1-29 amino acid fragment of human GHRH. It binds the GHRH receptor, a Gs-coupled receptor on somatotroph cells in the anterior pituitary. This raises cAMP, activates protein kinase A and the transcription factor CREB, and increases GH synthesis and release. Because sermorelin works upstream of GH, the pituitary still responds to somatostatin and IGF-1 negative feedback, which keeps GH release pulsatile and limits extreme levels. GH acts on the liver and other tissues to raise IGF-1, which drives growth in children. Its response depends on a working pituitary, so it was also used as a diagnostic test of pituitary GH reserve.", "body": [["Pituitary", "Triggers pulsatile GH release, which keeps natural feedback controls in place."], ["Liver", "GH raises IGF-1 production, the main signal for growth."], ["Growth plates", "In children with GH deficiency, raised GH and IGF-1 increase bone length and height."], ["Metabolism", "GH can reduce insulin sensitivity and raise blood glucose."], ["Injection site", "Pain, redness and swelling at the injection site are the most common effects."]], "journey": "Sermorelin is injected under the skin or into a vein. GH rises within minutes and peaks within about 30 to 60 minutes. Sermorelin itself is broken down quickly by peptidases, with a half-life of about 10 to 20 minutes. Its elimination has not been fully described. Compounded products vary in quality and content.", "sidefx": "Most side effects reflect injection or GH effects. Pain, redness and swelling at the injection site are common. Flushing, headache and dizziness can follow injection. Raised GH can lower insulin sensitivity and raise blood glucose. Antibodies against sermorelin have been reported. Rare allergic reactions occur. Compounded products can be contaminated and cause infection. There is no antidote; effects fade quickly because of the short half-life.", "src": ["Geref (sermorelin acetate) FDA label (discontinued)", "Guillemin R et al. Science 1982", "Rivier J, Vale W et al. Nature 1982"]}, "ghk-cu": {"origin": "GHK-Cu is a copper complex of a tiny three-amino-acid peptide: glycine, histidine and lysine. It was discovered in 1973 by Loren Pickart, who found that a factor from the plasma of young people made old liver tissue in the lab behave more like young tissue. He identified it as GHK, a peptide that binds copper tightly. Levels of GHK in plasma fall with age. Since the 1980s it has been widely used in cosmetic skin serums and creams. It is not FDA approved as a drug. It is also sold as an injectable research peptide, a use with no established human safety data.", "timeline": [["1973", "Loren Pickart identifies GHK as a plasma factor that binds copper."], ["1980s", "GHK-Cu is studied in wound healing and begins appearing in skin care products."], ["2010s", "Lab studies describe broad effects of GHK on gene expression."]], "cell": "How GHK-Cu acts in humans is not established. In lab studies, GHK binds copper(II) with high affinity and can deliver it to cells. Copper is a cofactor for lysyl oxidase, which cross-links collagen and elastin, and for superoxide dismutase, an antioxidant enzyme. In cell culture and animal studies, GHK-Cu increases collagen, elastin and glycosaminoglycan production by fibroblasts, attracts immune and endothelial cells to wounds, and promotes tissue remodeling. Gene expression studies suggest GHK changes the activity of many genes involved in repair and inflammation. These are proposed mechanisms from lab work; clinical evidence is mainly small cosmetic studies.", "body": [["Skin", "Cosmetic studies suggest improved skin firmness and reduced fine lines with topical use; evidence is limited."], ["Wounds", "Animal studies showed faster wound healing; clinical proof is lacking."], ["Hair", "Some cosmetic products claim hair growth effects; human evidence is weak."], ["Injection sites", "Injected products can cause pain, redness, bruising and a blue-green tint at the site, reportedly from copper."]], "journey": "On the skin, GHK-Cu acts mostly in the outer layers, and little reaches the blood. GHK occurs naturally in plasma, where it binds albumin and helps carry copper. It is broken down quickly by peptidases. How injected GHK-Cu is distributed and eliminated in humans is not known.", "sidefx": "Topical GHK-Cu is generally well tolerated, though skin irritation can occur. Injected products are not FDA approved, and their safety is unknown. Reported effects include injection-site pain, redness, bruising and a blue-green skin discoloration from copper. Unregulated products can be contaminated, causing infection or sepsis. Theoretically, large amounts of copper could cause toxicity, especially in people with Wilson disease. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Pickart L, Thaler MM. Nature New Biology 1973", "Pickart L, Margolina A. Regenerative and protective actions of the GHK-Cu peptide, Int J Mol Sci 2018"]}, "retatrutide": {"origin": "Retatrutide is a laboratory-designed peptide from Eli Lilly, built on the success of GLP-1 drugs like semaglutide and the dual GIP and GLP-1 agonist tirzepatide. Lilly scientists engineered a single molecule that activates three receptors: GIP, GLP-1 and glucagon. Adding glucagon activity was meant to raise energy use and burn liver fat. A phase 2 trial published in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2023 reported large weight loss over 48 weeks. Phase 3 trials (the TRIUMPH program) followed. It is not FDA approved, and it is an investigational drug. Gray-market products labeled as retatrutide are sold illegally, and their content and safety are unknown.", "timeline": [["2010s", "Eli Lilly develops retatrutide (LY3437943) as a triple hormone receptor agonist."], ["2023", "Phase 2 results in obesity are published in the New England Journal of Medicine."], ["2020s", "Phase 3 trials test retatrutide in obesity and related conditions."]], "cell": "Retatrutide is a single peptide that acts as an agonist at three Gs-coupled receptors. At the GLP-1 receptor in the pancreas, it increases glucose-dependent insulin release and lowers glucagon; in the brain, it reduces appetite; and in the stomach, it slows emptying. At the GIP receptor, it adds insulin release and may reduce nausea and affect fat tissue. At the glucagon receptor in the liver, it increases fat oxidation, reduces liver fat and is thought to raise energy expenditure. Normally glucagon raises blood sugar, but the strong GLP-1 and GIP insulin effects counter this. A fatty acid side chain binds albumin, giving a long half-life.", "body": [["Brain", "Reduces appetite and increases fullness, lowering food intake."], ["Pancreas", "Increases glucose-dependent insulin release, lowering blood sugar."], ["Liver", "Glucagon receptor action promotes fat burning and reduces liver fat."], ["Stomach and gut", "Slows stomach emptying and causes nausea, vomiting, diarrhea or constipation, especially early."], ["Heart", "Heart rate increases in a dose-related way, as with other incretin drugs."], ["Gallbladder and pancreas", "Rapid weight loss and incretin effects may raise risks of gallbladder disease and pancreatitis."]], "journey": "In trials, retatrutide was injected under the skin once a week. A fatty acid chain binds it to albumin, protecting it from breakdown and kidney filtration, giving a half-life of about six days. It is expected to be broken down by proteolysis like other peptides. Full human elimination data are not yet published.", "sidefx": "In trials, most side effects came from incretin effects on the gut: nausea, vomiting, diarrhea and constipation, mostly during dose increases. Vomiting and diarrhea can lead to dehydration and acute kidney injury. Heart rate rose, likely from GLP-1 and glucagon receptor effects. Pancreatitis and gallbladder disease are concerns shared with other incretin drugs. Hypoglycemia is mainly a risk with insulin or sulfonylureas. Because it is investigational, its full safety profile is not known, and gray-market products add risks of contamination. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Jastreboff AM et al. Triple-hormone-receptor agonist retatrutide for obesity, N Engl J Med 2023", "ClinicalTrials.gov: TRIUMPH program"]}, "mots-c": {"origin": "MOTS-c is a small peptide encoded not in the cell nucleus but in mitochondrial DNA. In 2015 Changhan Lee, Pinchas Cohen and colleagues at the University of Southern California reported in Cell Metabolism that a short open reading frame within the mitochondrial 12S rRNA gene codes a 16-amino-acid peptide. In mice, MOTS-c improved insulin sensitivity and prevented diet-induced obesity. It belongs to a growing family of mitochondrial-derived peptides that includes humanin. It is not FDA approved, human evidence is very limited, and it is sold on the gray market as a performance or metabolic product.", "timeline": [["2001", "Humanin, the first mitochondrial-derived peptide, is described."], ["2015", "Lee, Cohen and colleagues describe MOTS-c in Cell Metabolism."], ["2020s", "Animal studies explore MOTS-c in exercise and aging."]], "cell": "How MOTS-c acts in humans is not established. In mouse and cell studies, MOTS-c inhibits the folate cycle and de novo purine synthesis, leading to buildup of AICAR, an intermediate that activates AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK). AMPK is the cell's energy sensor: it increases glucose uptake and fat oxidation in muscle. Under metabolic stress, MOTS-c has been shown to move into the nucleus and regulate genes related to stress response. These effects improved insulin sensitivity and exercise capacity in mice. Because the evidence is mostly from animals, these mechanisms are proposed rather than proven in people.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "In mice, it increased glucose uptake and exercise capacity through AMPK activation."], ["Metabolism", "Mouse studies showed protection from diet-induced obesity and insulin resistance."], ["Blood sugar", "Theoretical risk of low blood sugar when combined with diabetes drugs."], ["Injection sites", "Injected gray-market products can cause site reactions and infection."]], "journey": "How MOTS-c is absorbed, distributed, broken down and eliminated in humans is unknown. It is naturally present at low levels in blood and tissues, where levels appear to rise with exercise and fall with age. Gray-market products are usually injected under the skin, and their content and purity vary.", "sidefx": "Because MOTS-c is not FDA approved, its side effect profile is not established. Reported effects include injection-site reactions, fatigue and palpitations. Its proposed effect on glucose uptake raises a theoretical risk of low blood sugar with diabetes medicines. Contaminated products can cause abscesses, sepsis or allergic reactions. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "src": ["Lee C et al. The mitochondrial-derived peptide MOTS-c promotes metabolic homeostasis, Cell Metab 2015"]}, "aod-9604": {"origin": "AOD-9604 stands for Advanced Obesity Drug 9604. It was developed in the 1990s by Frank Ng and colleagues at Monash University in Melbourne, Australia. They found that the tail end of human growth hormone (amino acids 176 to 191) seemed to drive fat breakdown in animals without GH's effects on growth or blood sugar. AOD-9604 is a modified version of that fragment with an extra tyrosine. The Australian company Metabolic Pharmaceuticals tested it in human obesity trials in the 2000s, but it did not produce meaningful weight loss and development for obesity was stopped. It is not FDA approved, and it is sold on the gray market as a fat-loss peptide.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Frank Ng and colleagues at Monash University develop AOD-9604 from a GH fragment."], ["2000s", "Human obesity trials fail to show meaningful weight loss."], ["2010s", "AOD-9604 is sold on the gray market as a fat-loss peptide."]], "cell": "AOD-9604 is a 16-amino-acid peptide based on the C-terminal region of human growth hormone. Unlike full GH, it does not bind the GH receptor in a way that raises IGF-1. In early animal and fat cell studies, it was proposed to stimulate lipolysis (breakdown of stored fat) and inhibit lipogenesis (new fat formation), possibly through beta-3 adrenergic receptor pathways in fat cells. The exact receptor and pathway are not known. Human trials did not confirm a meaningful fat-loss effect.", "body": [["Fat tissue", "Proposed to increase fat breakdown and reduce fat storage based on animal studies; human trials did not show meaningful weight loss."], ["Growth and IGF-1", "Does not raise IGF-1 in studies, unlike full growth hormone."], ["Blood sugar", "Early studies did not show the insulin resistance seen with full GH."], ["Injection sites", "Unregulated injected products can cause site reactions and infection."]], "journey": "Little is known about how AOD-9604 behaves in the human body. Studies suggest it is broken down by proteolysis and has a short half-life. Human trials used oral and injected forms. Gray-market products vary in content and purity.", "sidefx": "In human trials, AOD-9604 was generally well tolerated, with headache and mild injection-site reactions reported. Because it is not FDA approved, long-term safety is unknown. The main known risks come from unregulated products: contamination can cause infection, sepsis or allergic reactions. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "src": ["Heffernan MA et al. Endocrinology 2001", "FDA: Certain Bulk Drug Substances for Use in Compounding that May Present Significant Safety Risks"]}, "selank": {"origin": "Selank is a synthetic seven-amino-acid peptide developed in Russia. It is based on tuftsin, a natural four-amino-acid peptide from immunoglobulin G, discovered in 1970 by Victor Najjar and Kenji Nishioka at Tufts University and named after it. Scientists at the Institute of Molecular Genetics of the Russian Academy of Sciences, with the Zakusov Institute of Pharmacology in Moscow, added a short Pro-Gly-Pro tail to tuftsin to make it more stable. Selank is registered as a drug in Russia for anxiety. It is not FDA approved, most research comes from Russian groups, and independent human evidence is limited. It is sold online as a nasal spray.", "timeline": [["1970", "Najjar and Nishioka discover tuftsin at Tufts University."], ["1990s", "Russian researchers develop Selank from tuftsin."], ["2000s", "Selank is registered as a drug in Russia."]], "cell": "How Selank works is not fully established. Studies, mostly from Russian groups, propose several actions. It may modulate GABA-A receptor signaling, giving calming effects similar to benzodiazepines but without strong sedation. It is reported to inhibit enzymes that break down enkephalins, so these natural opioid peptides last longer. It is also reported to change expression of genes related to monoamines like serotonin and to immune cytokines, reflecting tuftsin's immune origin. Its Pro-Gly-Pro tail protects it from rapid breakdown. These mechanisms are proposed and have not been confirmed by independent research.", "body": [["Brain", "Claimed to reduce anxiety without sedation; independent human evidence is limited."], ["Immune system", "Studies propose effects on cytokine levels, reflecting its origin from tuftsin."], ["Memory and attention", "Some Russian studies claim improved memory; these claims are not well confirmed."], ["Nose", "Nasal spray use can cause nasal irritation."]], "journey": "Selank is given as a nasal spray in Russia. Studies suggest it is absorbed through the nasal lining and may reach the brain, though how much is unclear. Peptidases break it down quickly, and its half-life is reported to be minutes. Full human pharmacokinetics are not established.", "sidefx": "Because Selank is not FDA approved, its side effect profile is not well characterized in the US. Reported effects include nasal irritation, fatigue and headache. Allergic reactions are possible. Products bought online are unregulated and may be impure or mislabeled. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Najjar VA, Nishioka K. Nature 1970", "Russian state drug register (Selank)"]}, "semax": {"origin": "Semax is a synthetic seven-amino-acid peptide developed in the Soviet Union and Russia in the 1980s and 1990s. Scientists at the Institute of Molecular Genetics of the Russian Academy of Sciences, working with Moscow State University, started from ACTH(4-10), a fragment of adrenocorticotropic hormone known to affect attention and memory in animals without stimulating the adrenal glands. They added a Pro-Gly-Pro tail to slow its breakdown. Semax is registered as a drug in Russia, used as a nasal spray for stroke recovery and cognitive problems. It is not FDA approved, most research comes from Russian groups, and independent human evidence is limited.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Russian researchers develop Semax from the ACTH(4-10) fragment."], ["1990s", "Semax is registered as a drug in Russia."], ["2000s", "Russian studies describe effects on BDNF and gene expression in animals."]], "cell": "How Semax works is not fully established. It is derived from the ACTH(4-10) sequence, which contains the core melanocortin motif, but it lacks the part of ACTH that stimulates cortisol release. Studies, mostly from Russian groups, report that it raises levels of brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF) and its receptor TrkB in rat brain, which could support neuron survival and plasticity. It has also been reported to affect dopamine and serotonin systems and to change expression of genes related to inflammation and blood vessels after experimental stroke. Possible action at melanocortin receptors has been proposed. The Pro-Gly-Pro tail resists peptidases. These mechanisms are proposed and largely unconfirmed outside Russia.", "body": [["Brain after stroke", "Russian studies claim improved recovery after stroke; independent trials are lacking."], ["Attention and memory", "Claimed to improve focus and memory, mainly based on animal and small human studies."], ["Adrenal glands", "Does not raise cortisol, because it lacks the ACTH region that activates adrenal receptors."], ["Nose", "Nasal spray use can cause nasal irritation."], ["Sleep and mood", "Irritability and insomnia are reported, possibly from stimulating effects."]], "journey": "Semax is given as a nasal spray. Studies suggest it is absorbed through the nasal lining and may reach the brain. Peptidases break it down quickly, with a plasma half-life of minutes, though its effects are claimed to last hours, possibly through active fragments such as Pro-Gly-Pro. Full human pharmacokinetics are not established.", "sidefx": "Because Semax is not FDA approved, its side effect profile is not well described in the US. Reported effects include nasal irritation, headache, irritability and trouble sleeping. Allergic reactions are possible. Products bought online are unregulated and may be impure or mislabeled. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Russian state drug register (Semax)", "Dolotov OV et al. Brain Res 2006"]}, "kisspeptin-10": {"origin": "The kisspeptin story began in 1996, when researchers led by Danny Welch at Penn State College of Medicine in Hershey, Pennsylvania, found a gene that suppressed the spread of melanoma. They named it KISS1, a nod to Hershey's famous chocolate Kisses. In 2001 scientists identified its protein products, kisspeptins, as ligands of an orphan receptor called GPR54, and one product was named metastin. In 2003 two groups showed that people with mutations in GPR54 fail to go through puberty, revealing kisspeptin as a master switch for the reproductive axis. Kisspeptin-10 is the shortest active fragment, the last ten amino acids. It is used in research on fertility and puberty. It is not FDA approved and is sold on the gray market.", "timeline": [["1996", "The KISS1 gene is discovered in Hershey, Pennsylvania, as a melanoma metastasis suppressor."], ["2001", "Kisspeptins are identified as ligands for the GPR54 receptor."], ["2003", "GPR54 mutations are shown to block puberty in humans."], ["2010s", "Kisspeptin is studied to trigger egg maturation in IVF research."]], "cell": "Kisspeptin-10 is an agonist at KISS1R (GPR54), a Gq-coupled receptor on gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) neurons in the hypothalamus. Activation turns on phospholipase C, raising intracellular calcium and depolarizing the neurons, which fire and release GnRH into the pituitary portal blood. GnRH then stimulates pituitary gonadotroph cells to release luteinizing hormone (LH) and follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH). LH drives testosterone production by the testes and ovulation in women, and FSH supports sperm and follicle development. Kisspeptin neurons are a key link between sex steroid feedback, energy status and GnRH release. Continuous exposure can desensitize the receptor and suppress the axis.", "body": [["Hypothalamus", "Triggers GnRH neurons to release GnRH, the top signal of the reproductive axis."], ["Pituitary", "GnRH drives a short rise in LH and FSH within minutes."], ["Testes", "LH stimulates testosterone production, so testosterone rises briefly."], ["Ovaries", "In research settings, kisspeptin has been used to trigger egg maturation before IVF."], ["Long-term exposure", "Prolonged use may desensitize the receptor and suppress reproductive hormones, the opposite of the intended effect."]], "journey": "Kisspeptin-10 has been given IV or under the skin in research. LH rises within minutes and peaks around 30 to 60 minutes later. The peptide is broken down quickly by proteolysis, with a half-life of minutes. Its elimination in humans is not well characterized. Gray-market products vary in content and purity.", "sidefx": "Because kisspeptin-10 is not FDA approved, its side effect profile is not established. Reported effects in studies and use include flushing, headache and injection-site reactions. Repeated or continuous use may disrupt the reproductive hormone axis through receptor desensitization. Allergic reactions and infection from contaminated products are possible. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The KISS1 gene was named after Hershey's Kisses because it was discovered in Hershey, Pennsylvania.", "src": ["Lee JH, Welch DR et al. J Natl Cancer Inst 1996", "de Roux N et al. PNAS 2003", "Seminara SB et al. N Engl J Med 2003"]}, "melanotan-ii": {"origin": "Melanotan II was developed at the University of Arizona in the 1980s and 1990s by a team that included chemist Victor Hruby and biologist Mac Hadley. They wanted a synthetic version of alpha-melanocyte-stimulating hormone (alpha-MSH) that could tan the skin without sunlight and possibly lower skin cancer risk. Melanotan II, a small cyclic peptide, tanned the skin but also caused nausea, appetite loss and spontaneous erections in early studies. The sexual effects led to bremelanotide, which the FDA approved as Vyleesi in 2019 for low sexual desire in premenopausal women. A related compound, afamelanotide (Melanotan I), was approved as Scenesse in 2019 for a rare light-sensitivity disorder. Melanotan II itself is not FDA approved and is sold illegally as tanning injections and nasal sprays.", "timeline": [["1980s", "University of Arizona researchers develop melanotan peptides for sunless tanning."], ["1990s", "Melanotan II is found to cause spontaneous erections in early studies."], ["2019", "The FDA approves bremelanotide (Vyleesi) and afamelanotide (Scenesse), related melanocortin drugs."]], "cell": "Melanotan II is a cyclic analog of alpha-MSH that acts as a nonselective agonist at melanocortin receptors. At MC1R on melanocytes, it activates Gs, raises cAMP and increases tyrosinase activity, shifting melanin production toward dark eumelanin and darkening skin, freckles and moles. At MC3R and MC4R in the hypothalamus and spinal cord, it reduces appetite and activates pathways involved in sexual arousal, which explains spontaneous erections. Melanocortin receptors are also involved in autonomic and blood pressure control. Its broad action at multiple receptor subtypes explains its many side effects.", "body": [["Skin", "Darkens skin through increased eumelanin, even without sun exposure."], ["Moles and freckles", "Existing moles can darken and new ones can appear; case reports describe melanoma in users."], ["Appetite and gut", "Reduces appetite and often causes nausea and vomiting, especially after early doses."], ["Sexual function", "Causes spontaneous erections and increased arousal; priapism can occur."], ["Blood vessels and blood pressure", "Causes facial flushing and can raise blood pressure."], ["Brain and kidneys", "Rare case reports describe PRES with seizures, rhabdomyolysis and kidney infarction."]], "journey": "Melanotan II is usually injected under the skin or used as a nasal spray. Its half-life in plasma is about one hour in limited study data, but skin darkening builds up over days and lasts weeks. It is broken down by peptidases. Full human pharmacokinetics are not established, and illegal products vary widely in content and purity.", "sidefx": "Melanotan II's side effects follow from activating many melanocortin receptors at once. MC1R activation darkens skin and moles, which can hide or mimic melanoma, and melanoma has been reported in users. MC3R and MC4R activation causes nausea, appetite loss, yawning and stretching, flushing and spontaneous erections, sometimes prolonged into priapism. Effects on blood pressure and blood vessels may underlie rare serious events such as PRES, kidney infarction and rhabdomyolysis. Unregulated products add risks of contamination and infection. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "The peptide that gave the world bremelanotide started as a tanning project meant to help prevent skin cancer.", "src": ["Hadley ME, Dorr RT. Melanocortin peptide therapeutics, Peptides 2006", "Vyleesi (bremelanotide) FDA label", "FDA warning on melanotan products"]}, "epitalon": {"origin": "Epitalon is a synthetic four-amino-acid peptide (Ala-Glu-Asp-Gly). It was developed in Russia by Vladimir Khavinson and colleagues at the St. Petersburg Institute of Bioregulation and Gerontology. They first worked with epithalamin, a peptide extract from cow pineal glands, in the 1980s, and later designed epitalon as a short synthetic version based on its amino acid makeup. Their studies, done mostly by one research group, claimed effects on aging, melatonin and telomeres. It is not FDA approved, independent human evidence is lacking, and it is sold online as an anti-aging peptide.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Khavinson's group studies epithalamin, a pineal gland extract."], ["1990s", "The synthetic tetrapeptide epitalon is developed."], ["2003", "The group reports telomerase activation in human cell cultures."]], "cell": "How epitalon works is not established. Lab studies, mostly from the Khavinson group, report that it activates telomerase in human cell cultures, lengthening telomeres, the protective caps on chromosomes that shorten with each cell division. Animal studies from the same group report it restores nighttime melatonin release in older animals and affects gene expression. These are proposed mechanisms. Independent confirmation is lacking, and telomerase activation also raises a theoretical concern about cancer, because many cancer cells depend on telomerase to keep dividing.", "body": [["Pineal gland", "Animal studies from one group claim it normalizes melatonin release in older animals."], ["Cells and aging", "Claimed to lengthen telomeres in cell cultures; no proof of anti-aging effects in people."], ["Lifespan", "Rodent studies from the same group claimed longer lifespan; these have not been independently confirmed."], ["Injection sites", "Unregulated injected products can cause site reactions and infection."]], "journey": "How epitalon is absorbed, distributed, broken down and eliminated in humans is unknown. As a small peptide, it is expected to be broken down by peptidases. It is sold as an injectable and in other forms, and product content and purity vary.", "sidefx": "Because epitalon is not FDA approved, its side effect profile is not established. Reported effects include injection-site reactions, headache and drowsiness. Allergic reactions and infections from contaminated products are possible. Its proposed telomerase activation raises a theoretical cancer risk. There is no antidote; care is supportive.", "src": ["Khavinson VK et al. Bull Exp Biol Med 2003", "Anisimov VN, Khavinson VK. Biogerontology 2010"]}, "ethanol": {"origin": "Ethanol is made when yeasts ferment sugars, and people have made alcoholic drinks for thousands of years. Chemical traces in pottery from Jiahu in China show a fermented drink of rice, honey and fruit around 7000 BCE. Wine and beer were central to ancient Mesopotamia and Egypt. Distillation, which concentrates alcohol into spirits, spread in medieval Europe and the Islamic world. Doctors once used alcohol widely as a medicine, and it was given IV as an antidote for methanol and ethylene glycol poisoning before fomepizole became preferred. Today beverage alcohol is legal for adults 21 and older in the US and regulated by the Alcohol and Tobacco Tax and Trade Bureau, not by the FDA as a drug. Alcohol use is a leading cause of preventable death.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Around 7000 BCE, people at Jiahu in China make a fermented drink of rice, honey and fruit."], ["Ancient", "Beer and wine are part of daily life in Mesopotamia and Egypt."], ["1920", "US Prohibition begins under the 18th Amendment."], ["1933", "Prohibition ends with the 21st Amendment."], ["1984", "The National Minimum Drinking Age Act sets age 21 nationwide."]], "cell": "Ethanol is a small molecule that acts on several brain targets at once. It is a positive allosteric modulator of GABA-A receptors, especially those with delta subunits, so it increases chloride flow into neurons and makes them less excitable. It also blocks NMDA-type glutamate receptors, reducing excitatory signaling, which contributes to memory blackouts. It releases dopamine in the reward pathway of the nucleus accumbens and releases endorphins, which drive its rewarding effects. With chronic use, the brain adapts by reducing GABA-A function and increasing NMDA receptors. When alcohol is suddenly stopped, the brain is left overexcited, causing tremor, seizures and delirium tremens.", "body": [["Brain", "Low levels cause disinhibition and relaxation; higher levels cause slurred speech, poor coordination, memory loss, coma and depressed breathing."], ["Liver", "Metabolism makes NADH and acetaldehyde, leading to fatty liver, alcoholic hepatitis and cirrhosis over time; it also blocks gluconeogenesis, causing low blood sugar."], ["Heart", "Binge drinking can trigger atrial fibrillation (holiday heart); chronic heavy use can cause cardiomyopathy."], ["Blood vessels and skin", "Dilates skin vessels, causing flushing and heat loss that leads to hypothermia in the cold."], ["Kidneys", "Suppresses vasopressin release, causing increased urination and dehydration."], ["Stomach and pancreas", "Irritates the stomach lining and can cause gastritis, bleeding and pancreatitis."]], "journey": "Ethanol is absorbed quickly from the stomach and especially the small intestine; food slows absorption by delaying stomach emptying. Peak blood levels come about 30 to 90 minutes after drinking. It spreads through total body water, so people with less body water reach higher levels from the same amount. The liver breaks down over 90 percent: alcohol dehydrogenase converts it to acetaldehyde, and aldehyde dehydrogenase converts that to acetate. In chronic drinkers CYP2E1 also helps. Because these enzymes are saturated at low levels, elimination is zero-order, a fixed amount per hour. Small amounts leave in breath, which is the basis of breathalyzers, and in urine.", "sidefx": "Ethanol's harms come from its actions on many organs. Too much GABA-A enhancement and NMDA blockade depress the brainstem, causing coma and respiratory arrest. Vomiting while sedated can lead to aspiration. Acetaldehyde and oxidative stress damage the liver and other tissues, and acetaldehyde is a known carcinogen. Thiamine deficiency in chronic drinkers can cause Wernicke encephalopathy. Withdrawal after chronic use is dangerous because the brain has adapted to its presence. There is no specific antidote for alcohol intoxication; care focuses on airway, breathing, glucose, thiamine and warmth, and benzodiazepines are used for withdrawal.", "fun": "The flushing some people get after drinking often comes from a common ALDH2 gene variant that slows acetaldehyde breakdown, the same effect produced by the drug disulfiram.", "src": ["McGovern PE et al. Fermented beverages of pre- and proto-historic China, PNAS 2004", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "NIAAA: Alcohol's Effects on the Body"]}, "nicotine": {"origin": "Nicotine comes from the tobacco plant, Nicotiana tabacum, native to the Americas, where Indigenous peoples used tobacco for centuries in ceremonies and medicine. European explorers brought it back in the 1500s. In 1560 Jean Nicot, the French ambassador to Portugal, sent tobacco to the French court, and the plant and its alkaloid were later named after him. In 1828 German chemists Wilhelm Posselt and Karl Reimann at Heidelberg isolated pure nicotine. The 1964 US Surgeon General's report linked smoking to lung cancer. To help smokers quit, Swedish scientists at Leo developed nicotine gum, which the FDA approved as Nicorette in 1984. Patches followed in the early 1990s, and many replacement products became available over the counter in 1996. Tobacco and e-cigarette products are regulated by the FDA as tobacco products, not drugs.", "timeline": [["1560", "Jean Nicot sends tobacco to the French court."], ["1828", "Posselt and Reimann isolate nicotine."], ["1964", "The US Surgeon General's report links smoking to lung cancer."], ["1984", "The FDA approves Nicorette nicotine gum."], ["1996", "Nicotine gum and patches become available over the counter."]], "cell": "Nicotine is an agonist at nicotinic acetylcholine receptors, ligand-gated ion channels made of five subunits. In the brain, the alpha4-beta2 receptor on dopamine neurons of the ventral tegmental area is key: activation lets sodium and calcium in, depolarizes the neuron and releases dopamine in the nucleus accumbens, which reinforces use. Nicotine also activates receptors in autonomic ganglia (alpha3-beta4), stimulating both sympathetic and parasympathetic nerves, and in the adrenal medulla, releasing epinephrine. With repeated exposure, receptors desensitize and the brain makes more of them, which underlies tolerance, dependence and withdrawal. At very high levels, nicotine causes depolarizing blockade, like succinylcholine, at ganglia and the neuromuscular junction.", "body": [["Brain", "Releases dopamine and other neurotransmitters, causing alertness, reward and, with repeated use, dependence and withdrawal symptoms."], ["Heart and vessels", "Sympathetic stimulation and adrenal epinephrine raise heart rate and blood pressure and narrow blood vessels."], ["Gut", "Ganglion stimulation causes nausea, vomiting and increased bowel activity, especially with first exposure or poisoning."], ["Glands", "Increases salivation and sweating through ganglionic stimulation."], ["Muscles", "In poisoning, stimulation then blockade at the neuromuscular junction causes twitching, then weakness and breathing failure."], ["Children", "Small children who swallow e-liquid or gum can be quickly poisoned because the dose is large for their body size."]], "journey": "Inhaled nicotine reaches the brain within seconds, which makes smoking and vaping highly addictive. Gum, lozenges and nasal spray are absorbed through the lining of the mouth or nose over minutes, and patches deliver it steadily through the skin over hours. Swallowed nicotine is largely broken down by the liver on first pass. The liver enzyme CYP2A6 converts most nicotine to cotinine, which is often measured to check exposure. Nicotine's half-life is about two hours, while cotinine's is about 16 to 20 hours. Metabolites leave in the urine.", "sidefx": "Nicotine's side effects follow from stimulating nicotinic receptors throughout the body. Ganglionic and adrenal stimulation cause nausea, a fast heart rate and high blood pressure. Local irritation explains mouth soreness with gum and skin redness under patches. In poisoning, there is first stimulation (vomiting, salivation, tremor, seizures, fast heart rate) and then depolarizing blockade (bradycardia, low blood pressure, muscle weakness and respiratory failure). Most of tobacco's long-term harms, such as cancer and lung disease, come from other chemicals in smoke rather than nicotine itself. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive, with atropine for bradycardia and benzodiazepines for seizures.", "fun": "Nicotine is also a natural insecticide made by the tobacco plant to protect itself from being eaten.", "src": ["Nicorette and NicoDerm CQ FDA labels", "US Surgeon General's Report on Smoking and Health, 1964", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "anabolic-steroid-misuse": {"origin": "In 1889 French physiologist Charles-Edouard Brown-Sequard injected himself with extracts from animal testicles and claimed renewed strength, sparking interest in male hormones. In 1935 Ernst Laqueur's group in Amsterdam isolated testosterone from bull testes, and in the same year Adolf Butenandt and Leopold Ruzicka synthesized it; they shared the 1939 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. Chemists then made synthetic anabolic-androgenic steroids such as nandrolone and stanozolol to maximize muscle growth. By the 1950s and 1960s athletes were using them, and the International Olympic Committee banned them in the 1970s. Sprinter Ben Johnson's positive test at the 1988 Olympics drew worldwide attention. The US Anabolic Steroid Control Act of 1990 made them Schedule III controlled substances. FDA-approved testosterone products exist for medical use, but non-medical use is illegal and harmful.", "timeline": [["1889", "Brown-Sequard reports self-injection of testicular extracts."], ["1935", "Testosterone is isolated and synthesized."], ["1939", "Butenandt and Ruzicka share the Nobel Prize in Chemistry."], ["1988", "Ben Johnson tests positive for stanozolol at the Seoul Olympics."], ["1990", "The Anabolic Steroid Control Act places anabolic steroids in Schedule III."]], "cell": "Anabolic-androgenic steroids are lipid-soluble and diffuse into cells, where they bind the androgen receptor, a nuclear hormone receptor. The bound receptor moves to the nucleus, binds androgen response elements on DNA and turns on genes that increase muscle protein synthesis and muscle fiber growth. In some tissues, such as skin and prostate, testosterone is converted by 5-alpha-reductase to the stronger androgen dihydrotestosterone. Some steroids are converted by aromatase to estrogens, which can cause breast tissue growth. High androgen levels suppress hypothalamic GnRH and pituitary LH and FSH through negative feedback, shutting down the body's own testosterone and sperm production. Androgens also stimulate the kidney to make erythropoietin, raising red blood cell counts.", "body": [["Muscle", "Increases muscle size and strength, the main reason for misuse; tendons do not strengthen as fast, raising injury risk."], ["Heart and vessels", "Raises blood pressure, lowers HDL cholesterol, raises LDL and can thicken the heart muscle, increasing risk of heart attack, stroke and sudden death."], ["Blood", "Raises red blood cell count (erythrocytosis), thickening blood and increasing clot risk."], ["Reproductive system", "In men, suppresses the body's own testosterone, causing testicular shrinkage and infertility; aromatization can cause breast growth. In women, causes deepening voice, facial hair and menstrual changes."], ["Liver", "Oral 17-alpha-alkylated steroids can cause liver injury, cholestasis, and rarely liver tumors or blood-filled cysts (peliosis hepatis)."], ["Brain and mood", "Can cause irritability, aggression and mood swings; depression and suicidal thoughts can occur during withdrawal."], ["Skin and hair", "Acne and male-pattern hair loss come from androgen action on skin and hair follicles."]], "journey": "Misused steroids are taken by mouth or injected into muscle. Injectable esters such as testosterone cypionate or enanthate dissolve in oil and are released slowly from the muscle over days to weeks; the ester is then cut off to free the active steroid. Oral forms are chemically modified (17-alpha-alkylated) so the liver does not destroy them on first pass, which also makes them harder on the liver. In blood, testosterone binds sex hormone-binding globulin and albumin. The liver breaks steroids down, and some are aromatized to estrogens. Metabolites are conjugated and excreted in urine, where anti-doping tests detect them.", "sidefx": "Side effects follow from androgen receptor activation in many tissues and from shutting down the body's own hormone system. Negative feedback on the pituitary causes testicular shrinkage, low sperm counts and, after stopping, a period of low testosterone with low mood. Aromatization to estrogen causes gynecomastia. Androgens raise red blood cell production and alter cholesterol, which, with higher blood pressure and heart muscle thickening, increases heart attack, stroke and clot risk. Oral alkylated steroids stress the liver. Injecting with shared or non-sterile equipment spreads infections. There is no antidote; care involves stopping use and treating complications, sometimes with medical help to restart natural hormone production.", "src": ["Anabolic Steroid Control Act of 1990", "NIDA: Anabolic Steroids DrugFacts", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Nobel Prize in Chemistry 1939 materials"]}, "vitamin-a": {"origin": "Night blindness was treated with liver in ancient Egypt, long before anyone knew why it worked. In 1913 Elmer McCollum and Marguerite Davis at the University of Wisconsin found that rats needed a fat-soluble factor from butter and egg yolk to grow, which they called fat-soluble A. Swiss chemist Paul Karrer worked out the structure of retinol and beta-carotene in the early 1930s and shared the 1937 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. George Wald at Harvard showed how vitamin A forms rhodopsin in the eye and shared the 1967 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. Today, vitamin A injection (Aquasol A) is an FDA-approved prescription product, while oral supplements are sold as dietary supplements and are not FDA approved.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Egyptian texts describe liver as a remedy for night blindness."], ["1913", "McCollum and Davis describe fat-soluble A."], ["1931", "Paul Karrer determines the structure of vitamin A."], ["1937", "Karrer shares the Nobel Prize in Chemistry."], ["1967", "George Wald shares the Nobel Prize for the chemistry of vision."]], "cell": "Vitamin A works through two main forms. In the retina, retinol is converted to 11-cis-retinal, which binds the protein opsin to form rhodopsin in rod cells. When light hits rhodopsin, 11-cis-retinal flips to all-trans-retinal, changing the protein's shape and triggering a signaling cascade through transducin and phosphodiesterase that closes cGMP-gated sodium channels and sends a signal to the brain. The retinal must be recycled back, so a lack of vitamin A first causes night blindness. In other tissues, retinol is converted to retinoic acid, which binds nuclear retinoic acid receptors (RAR) paired with retinoid X receptors (RXR). These complexes bind DNA and control genes for epithelial cell growth and differentiation, immune function and embryonic development.", "body": [["Eyes", "Needed for rhodopsin in rod cells; deficiency causes night blindness, dry eyes (xerophthalmia) and, if severe, corneal damage and blindness."], ["Skin and mucous membranes", "Keeps epithelial cells healthy and mucus-producing; deficiency causes dry, rough skin and keratinized linings."], ["Immune system", "Supports immune cell function; vitamin A supplementation lowers death rates from measles in deficient children."], ["Embryo", "Retinoic acid guides development of organs, but too much causes serious birth defects."], ["Liver", "The liver stores most of the body's vitamin A; long-term excess causes liver injury and fibrosis."], ["Brain and bones", "Excess raises pressure inside the skull (pseudotumor cerebri) and can cause bone pain and fractures."]], "journey": "Vitamin A from food or supplements is absorbed with dietary fat in the small intestine, packed into chylomicrons and carried through the lymph to the liver. The liver stores it as retinyl esters in stellate cells and releases it bound to retinol-binding protein. Stores can last months. Beta-carotene from plants is converted to retinol in the gut. The injection is given into muscle for people who cannot absorb vitamin A. Metabolites leave through bile and urine.", "sidefx": "Because vitamin A is stored in the liver, too much builds up over time. Acute excess causes headache, nausea, vomiting and dizziness, partly from raised pressure in the skull. Chronic excess causes dry skin, hair loss, bone pain, liver injury and pseudotumor cerebri. Retinoic acid controls embryonic gene expression, so excess during pregnancy can cause birth defects of the face, heart and brain. IV vitamin A has caused anaphylaxis, which is why the injection is given into muscle. There is no specific antidote; stopping intake and supportive care are used.", "fun": "Polar bear liver contains so much vitamin A that Arctic explorers who ate it developed severe vitamin A poisoning.", "src": ["Aquasol A (vitamin A palmitate) FDA label", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Vitamin A fact sheet", "Nobel Prize materials 1937 and 1967"]}, "riboflavin": {"origin": "Riboflavin was first seen in 1879, when English chemist Alexander Wynter Blyth extracted a yellow-green fluorescent pigment from milk whey, which he called lactochrome. Its role in nutrition became clear in the 1920s and 1930s, when researchers separated the B vitamin complex into parts. Richard Kuhn in Germany and Paul Karrer in Switzerland determined its structure and synthesized it in the mid-1930s. Its name comes from ribose, a sugar in its structure, and flavus, Latin for yellow. In 2016 the FDA approved Photrexa, a riboflavin eye drop used with UVA light to treat progressive keratoconus, a thinning of the cornea. Oral riboflavin supplements are not FDA approved.", "timeline": [["1879", "Alexander Wynter Blyth isolates a yellow pigment from whey."], ["1930s", "Kuhn and Karrer determine the structure and synthesize riboflavin."], ["1938", "Richard Kuhn is named the 1938 Nobel laureate in Chemistry for carotenoid and vitamin work."], ["2016", "The FDA approves Photrexa for corneal cross-linking."]], "cell": "Riboflavin is converted inside cells to two coenzymes. Flavokinase adds a phosphate to make flavin mononucleotide (FMN), and FAD synthetase adds an adenosine to make flavin adenine dinucleotide (FAD). These carry electrons in many oxidation-reduction reactions. FMN is part of complex I and FAD is part of complex II (succinate dehydrogenase) of the mitochondrial electron transport chain. FAD is also needed for fatty acid oxidation, the citric acid cycle, glutathione reductase and the activation of vitamin B6 and folate. In corneal cross-linking, riboflavin soaked into the cornea absorbs UVA light and makes reactive oxygen species that form new chemical bonds between collagen fibers, stiffening the cornea.", "body": [["Energy metabolism", "FMN and FAD support the electron transport chain and fatty acid breakdown, so all cells depend on them."], ["Skin and mouth", "Deficiency causes cracks at the corners of the mouth (angular cheilitis), a sore red tongue and skin inflammation."], ["Eyes", "Deficiency can cause eye irritation; in keratoconus, Photrexa with UVA light stiffens the cornea and slows its thinning."], ["Blood", "Riboflavin helps activate other B vitamins and supports red blood cell production."], ["Urine", "Excess is excreted unchanged and turns urine bright yellow."]], "journey": "Oral riboflavin is absorbed in the small intestine by a carrier that saturates, so absorption is limited at high amounts. Absorption peaks one to two hours after taking it. In cells it is converted to FMN and FAD. The body stores little, and excess is filtered by the kidneys into the urine within hours, with a half-life of about one hour. In the eye, Photrexa is applied to the cornea, and very little reaches the blood.", "sidefx": "Oral riboflavin is very safe because absorption is limited and excess leaves in the urine. The most noticeable effect is bright yellow-orange urine, which is harmless. With Photrexa, UVA light and reactive oxygen species can damage the corneal surface, causing pain, haze or slow healing, and rarely infection. There is no antidote, and none is needed for oral use.", "src": ["Photrexa (riboflavin 5'-phosphate ophthalmic) FDA label", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Riboflavin fact sheet"]}, "ascorbic-acid": {"origin": "Scurvy killed huge numbers of sailors on long voyages. In 1747 Scottish naval surgeon James Lind ran one of the first controlled clinical trials and found that citrus fruit cured it. In 1907 Axel Holst and Theodor Frolich in Norway created scurvy in guinea pigs, which, like humans, cannot make vitamin C. In 1928 Hungarian scientist Albert Szent-Gyorgyi isolated hexuronic acid, and in 1932 he and Joseph Svirbely, and separately Charles Glen King in the US, showed it was vitamin C. In 1933 Norman Haworth determined its structure and Tadeus Reichstein synthesized it. Szent-Gyorgyi received the 1937 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. Today, Ascor injection is an FDA-approved prescription product for scurvy, while oral supplements are not FDA approved.", "timeline": [["1747", "James Lind shows citrus cures scurvy in a controlled trial."], ["1907", "Holst and Frolich produce scurvy in guinea pigs."], ["1932", "Szent-Gyorgyi and King identify vitamin C."], ["1933", "Haworth determines its structure and Reichstein synthesizes it."], ["1937", "Szent-Gyorgyi and Haworth receive Nobel Prizes."]], "cell": "Ascorbic acid is a reducing agent that keeps metal ions in enzymes in their active state. Its best-known role is in collagen formation: prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases need ferrous iron, and vitamin C keeps that iron reduced. These enzymes add hydroxyl groups to proline and lysine in procollagen, which lets the chains form a stable triple helix. Without vitamin C, collagen is weak, so blood vessels, gums, skin and bone break down. Vitamin C is also a cofactor for dopamine beta-hydroxylase (dopamine to norepinephrine), for carnitine synthesis and for some hormone-processing enzymes. In the gut, it reduces ferric iron to ferrous iron, increasing absorption of iron from plants. It also acts as a water-soluble antioxidant.", "body": [["Connective tissue", "Needed to make strong collagen; deficiency causes scurvy, with weak blood vessels, bleeding gums and poor wound healing."], ["Skin", "Scurvy causes bleeding around hair follicles, corkscrew hairs and easy bruising."], ["Bones and teeth", "Collagen loss causes bone pain, loose teeth and, in children, bleeding under the bone lining."], ["Gut and iron", "Increases absorption of non-heme iron from plant foods."], ["Nervous system", "Supports norepinephrine synthesis; fatigue and mood changes are early signs of deficiency."], ["Kidneys", "Excess is excreted in urine and partly converted to oxalate, which can form kidney stones."]], "journey": "Oral vitamin C is absorbed in the small intestine by sodium-dependent transporters (SVCT1), which become saturated at high intake, so absorption falls as the amount rises. It spreads widely and is concentrated in white blood cells, the adrenal glands and the eye. It is oxidized to dehydroascorbic acid, which can be recycled, and partly broken down to oxalate. The kidneys reabsorb it until plasma levels are saturated, and excess is excreted in urine. IV vitamin C bypasses gut limits, giving much higher blood levels, and has a half-life of about two hours at high levels.", "sidefx": "Large oral amounts pull water into the gut because unabsorbed vitamin C is osmotic, causing diarrhea and cramps. Excess is partly converted to oxalate, which can form kidney stones or, with high IV doses or kidney disease, crystal damage to the kidneys. In people with G6PD deficiency, high IV doses can trigger hemolysis because their red cells cannot handle the oxidative stress. High levels can also interfere with some glucose meters and lab tests. There is no antidote; stopping and hydration are used.", "fun": "Humans, guinea pigs and some primates and bats cannot make their own vitamin C because they lack a working gene for the enzyme L-gulonolactone oxidase.", "src": ["Ascor (ascorbic acid injection) FDA label", "Lind J. A Treatise of the Scurvy, 1753", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Vitamin C fact sheet"]}, "vitamin-e": {"origin": "In 1922 Herbert Evans and Katharine Scott Bishop at the University of California, Berkeley, found that rats fed a purified diet could become pregnant but lost their fetuses. A factor in lettuce and wheat germ prevented this, and they called it factor X, later named vitamin E. In 1936 Evans and colleagues isolated it from wheat germ oil and named it tocopherol, from the Greek words for 'to bear offspring.' Its structure was determined in 1938, and it was later identified as the main fat-soluble antioxidant in cell membranes. In the US, vitamin E is sold as a dietary supplement, which is not FDA approved. Large trials have not shown that high-dose vitamin E prevents heart disease or cancer, and some suggested harms.", "timeline": [["1922", "Evans and Bishop discover a dietary factor needed for rat fertility."], ["1936", "Vitamin E is isolated from wheat germ oil and named tocopherol."], ["1938", "The structure of alpha-tocopherol is determined and it is synthesized."], ["2000s", "Large trials do not show heart or cancer benefits from high-dose vitamin E."]], "cell": "Alpha-tocopherol is a fat-soluble molecule that sits in cell membranes and lipoproteins. Its chromanol ring has a hydroxyl group that donates a hydrogen atom to lipid peroxyl radicals, stopping the chain reaction of lipid peroxidation that would otherwise damage membranes. This turns vitamin E into a tocopheroxyl radical, which vitamin C and other antioxidants can recycle back to its active form. The liver's alpha-tocopherol transfer protein selects alpha-tocopherol and loads it into lipoproteins, which is why this form dominates in blood. At high levels, vitamin E inhibits protein kinase C and platelet aggregation and can interfere with vitamin K-dependent clotting, which raises bleeding risk.", "body": [["Cell membranes", "Protects membrane fats from oxidation, especially in nerves, muscle and red blood cells."], ["Nerves", "Deficiency, usually from fat malabsorption or genetic defects, causes ataxia and loss of sensation from nerve damage."], ["Red blood cells", "Deficiency makes red cells fragile and can cause hemolytic anemia, especially in preterm infants."], ["Blood clotting", "High intake inhibits platelets and antagonizes vitamin K, increasing bleeding, especially with warfarin."], ["Brain", "A large meta-analysis suggested higher hemorrhagic stroke risk with high-dose supplements."]], "journey": "Vitamin E is absorbed with dietary fat in the small intestine, which requires bile and pancreatic enzymes. It is packed into chylomicrons and carried through the lymph to the liver. The liver's alpha-tocopherol transfer protein loads alpha-tocopherol into VLDL for delivery to tissues, while other forms are broken down. It is stored in fat, liver and muscle. Its plasma half-life is about two to three days. CYP4F2 starts its breakdown, and metabolites leave mainly through bile in stool, with some in urine.", "sidefx": "At normal intake vitamin E is safe. High doses can cause nausea, diarrhea and fatigue. Because vitamin E inhibits platelets and interferes with vitamin K-dependent clotting factors, high intake increases bleeding risk, especially with warfarin or antiplatelet drugs, and has been linked to hemorrhagic stroke. The SELECT trial found more prostate cancer in men taking high-dose vitamin E. There is no specific antidote; vitamin K can help reverse bleeding effects.", "src": ["NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Vitamin E fact sheet", "Evans HM, Bishop KS. Science 1922", "Klein EA et al. SELECT, JAMA 2011"]}, "biotin": {"origin": "In 1927 British scientist Margaret Averil Boas found that rats fed large amounts of raw egg white developed hair loss, skin inflammation and paralysis, a condition called egg white injury. A factor in liver protected them. In 1936 German chemists Fritz Kogl and Benno Tonnis isolated a growth factor from egg yolk that they named biotin. It was later shown to be the same as the protective factor and as vitamin H. In 1942 Vincent du Vigneaud at Cornell determined its structure. Avidin, a protein in raw egg white, binds biotin so tightly that it blocks absorption, explaining egg white injury. Biotin is sold as a dietary supplement, which is not FDA approved. In 2017 the FDA warned that high-dose biotin can interfere with lab tests, including troponin.", "timeline": [["1927", "Margaret Boas describes egg white injury in rats."], ["1936", "Kogl and Tonnis isolate biotin from egg yolk."], ["1942", "Vincent du Vigneaud determines biotin's structure."], ["2017", "The FDA warns that biotin can interfere with lab tests."]], "cell": "Biotin is a coenzyme for five carboxylase enzymes in humans. Holocarboxylase synthetase attaches biotin covalently to a lysine residue in each enzyme. The biotin then carries carbon dioxide (as a carboxyl group) to the substrate. Acetyl-CoA carboxylase makes malonyl-CoA, the first step in fatty acid synthesis. Pyruvate carboxylase makes oxaloacetate, needed for gluconeogenesis and the citric acid cycle. Propionyl-CoA carboxylase and methylcrotonyl-CoA carboxylase break down certain amino acids and odd-chain fats. When the enzymes are broken down, biotinidase frees biotin for reuse. Separately, many lab tests use a biotin and streptavidin binding system, so high blood biotin can block the test and give false results.", "body": [["Metabolism", "Supports fatty acid synthesis, glucose production and amino acid breakdown through its carboxylase enzymes."], ["Skin and hair", "Deficiency causes hair loss and a scaly red rash around the eyes, nose and mouth; benefits for hair and nails in healthy people are not proven."], ["Nervous system", "Deficiency can cause depression, lethargy and tingling."], ["Lab tests", "High biotin levels can cause falsely high or falsely low results in thyroid, hormone and troponin tests, which can lead to missed heart attacks."]], "journey": "Biotin from food is freed from proteins by biotinidase and absorbed in the small intestine by the sodium-dependent multivitamin transporter. Gut bacteria also make some biotin. It circulates in plasma, mostly unbound, and is taken up by cells. Some is broken down, and the rest is excreted in the urine, with a half-life of about two hours. High-dose supplements can raise blood levels for many hours, enough to affect lab tests.", "sidefx": "Biotin itself is very safe because excess is excreted in the urine. The main risk is lab test interference. Many immunoassays use biotin and streptavidin to capture their target, and extra biotin in the blood competes in this system, giving falsely high or low results. A falsely low troponin can cause a heart attack to be missed, and falsely abnormal thyroid tests can lead to wrong diagnoses. There is no antidote; telling the lab and stopping supplements before testing are standard approaches.", "fun": "Avidin from raw egg white binds biotin so tightly that the pair is used throughout biotechnology as a molecular glue.", "src": ["FDA Safety Communication: Biotin interference with lab tests (2017, updated 2019)", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Biotin fact sheet"]}, "zinc": {"origin": "Zinc has been used in ointments since ancient times, and zinc oxide is still used on skin today. In 1869 French scientist Jules Raulin showed that zinc was needed for the growth of the mold Aspergillus niger, the first evidence it was essential to living things. Its importance in humans was shown in the early 1960s by Ananda Prasad, who studied young men in Iran and Egypt with dwarfism, delayed puberty and anemia and showed that zinc deficiency was the cause. In 1973 acrodermatitis enteropathica, a rare inherited zinc absorption disorder, was found to respond to zinc. Zinc sulfate injection is FDA approved for parenteral nutrition, while oral zinc supplements and lozenges are not FDA approved.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Zinc compounds are used in ointments for wounds and skin."], ["1869", "Jules Raulin shows zinc is needed for Aspergillus growth."], ["1961", "Ananda Prasad describes human zinc deficiency in Iran."], ["1973", "Acrodermatitis enteropathica is found to respond to zinc."]], "cell": "Zinc is a trace element with structural, catalytic and regulatory roles. It is the catalytic metal in more than 300 enzymes, including carbonic anhydrase, alkaline phosphatase, alcohol dehydrogenase and superoxide dismutase, where it activates water or stabilizes reaction intermediates. It also holds the shape of zinc-finger proteins, a large family of transcription factors that bind DNA and control gene expression. Zinc is needed for DNA and RNA polymerases, cell division and immune cell development, especially T cells. Cells control zinc levels with ZIP and ZnT transporters and metallothionein. Large zinc intake makes gut cells produce more metallothionein, which traps copper and blocks its absorption.", "body": [["Immune system", "Needed for T-cell development and function; deficiency increases infections, and zinc lozenges may shorten colds slightly."], ["Skin and wounds", "Supports skin cell growth and wound healing; deficiency causes rash around the mouth and limbs and poor healing."], ["Growth and puberty", "Needed for cell division and growth; deficiency causes short stature and delayed puberty."], ["Taste and smell", "Deficiency reduces taste and smell; intranasal zinc products have been linked to loss of smell."], ["Gut", "Zinc helps treat diarrhea in children in low-income countries; supplements can cause nausea."], ["Copper balance", "Long-term high zinc intake causes copper deficiency, leading to anemia, low white cells and nerve damage."]], "journey": "Oral zinc is absorbed in the small intestine through ZIP4 transporters, with about 20 to 40 percent absorbed from food depending on diet. Phytates in grains and legumes bind zinc and reduce absorption. In blood it is mostly bound to albumin. It is found in all tissues, with the most in muscle and bone. The body controls zinc by adjusting gut absorption and excretion into stool, which is the main route out, with a small amount in urine. IV zinc in parenteral nutrition bypasses the gut.", "sidefx": "Zinc salts irritate the stomach, causing nausea and a metallic taste. Long-term high intake induces metallothionein in gut cells, which binds copper and keeps it from being absorbed, causing copper deficiency with anemia, low white blood cell counts and a spinal cord disorder (myelopathy) that can resemble vitamin B12 deficiency. Excess zinc in parenteral nutrition can cause vessel precipitates if not properly mixed. There is no specific antidote; stopping zinc and replacing copper are the main treatments.", "fun": "Zinc deficiency causing dwarfism was discovered partly because diets in some regions were high in unleavened bread, whose phytates block zinc absorption.", "src": ["Zinc sulfate injection FDA label", "Prasad AS et al. Am J Med 1961", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Zinc fact sheet"]}, "electrolyte-oral-rehydration": {"origin": "Oral rehydration solution grew out of a physiology discovery. In the early 1960s Robert Crane and colleagues described sodium-glucose cotransport in the small intestine: glucose and sodium cross the gut wall together. Researchers then found this pathway keeps working even during cholera, when the gut is pouring out fluid. In the 1960s teams at the Cholera Research Laboratory in Dhaka and in Calcutta showed that a simple drink of glucose and salts could replace much of the IV fluid patients needed. During the 1971 Bangladesh war, Dilip Mahalanabis used ORS in refugee camps when IV fluid ran out, and deaths fell sharply. In 1978 The Lancet called it potentially the most important medical advance of the century. WHO and UNICEF promoted a standard formula, and in 2002 they switched to a lower osmolarity version that causes less stool output. In the US, ORS products are sold as medical foods or supplements, not FDA approved drugs.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Robert Crane and colleagues describe sodium-glucose cotransport in the intestine."], ["1960s", "Trials in Dhaka and Calcutta show oral glucose-salt solution can treat cholera."], ["1971", "Dilip Mahalanabis uses ORS in Bangladesh war refugee camps with low death rates."], ["1978", "The Lancet calls ORS potentially the most important medical advance of the century."], ["2002", "WHO and UNICEF recommend a reduced osmolarity ORS formula."]], "cell": "The key protein is SGLT1, the sodium-glucose cotransporter on the brush border of small intestine cells. SGLT1 moves two sodium ions and one glucose molecule into the cell together, using the sodium gradient set up by the Na-K ATPase on the basolateral side. Glucose then leaves the cell through GLUT2 into the blood, and sodium is pumped out by the Na-K ATPase. Water follows the absorbed solute by osmosis, both through the cells and between them. Cholera toxin and many diarrheal toxins raise cAMP and open CFTR chloride channels to drive secretion, but they leave SGLT1 intact. That is why a drink with the right ratio of sodium to glucose still gets absorbed when plain water or salty water alone would not. Potassium and a base (citrate) replace losses and help correct acidosis.", "body": [["Small intestine", "Glucose-coupled sodium uptake pulls water into the body even while toxins drive secretion into the gut, so net fluid balance improves."], ["Blood volume and circulation", "Restored plasma volume raises blood pressure and improves perfusion, which reverses the fast heart rate and poor capillary refill of dehydration."], ["Kidneys", "Better kidney blood flow restores urine output and lets the kidneys correct acid-base and electrolyte problems."], ["Acid-base balance", "Citrate is converted to bicarbonate in the liver, which helps correct the metabolic acidosis caused by stool bicarbonate loss."], ["Potassium balance", "Added potassium replaces what is lost in diarrheal stool, lowering the risk of weakness and rhythm problems from hypokalemia."]], "journey": "ORS is swallowed or given by nasogastric tube. Its sodium and glucose are absorbed in the small intestine within minutes to hours, mainly by SGLT1. The absorbed water expands the blood volume and spreads into the tissues. Glucose is used for energy like any dietary sugar. Excess sodium, potassium and water are handled by the kidneys, which adjust urine output as hydration returns. There is no drug half-life in the usual sense; the effect depends on matching intake to ongoing losses.", "sidefx": "Vomiting can happen if large amounts are taken quickly, because a stretched, irritated stomach triggers the vomiting reflex; that is why small frequent sips are used. Homemade mixtures with too much salt cause hypernatremia, which pulls water out of brain cells and can cause seizures; correcting sodium too fast in the other direction can also cause brain swelling. Too much sugar draws water into the gut and worsens diarrhea, which is why sports drinks and juices do not work as ORS. There is no antidote; problems are managed by correcting fluid and sodium carefully.", "fun": "ORS is credited with saving tens of millions of lives worldwide since the 1970s, mostly young children with diarrhea.", "src": ["WHO Oral Rehydration Salts guidance", "CDC and AAP guidance on gastroenteritis", "Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology"]}, "lactobacillus-rhamnosus-gg": {"origin": "LGG was found by two scientists at Tufts University in Boston, Sherwood Gorbach and Barry Goldin. In the 1980s they searched for a lactobacillus that could survive stomach acid and bile, stick to the gut lining and come from a human source. They isolated a strain from the stool of a healthy adult, and the name GG comes from their initials. The strain was patented and licensed widely, first to Valio in Finland for fermented milk and later sold in the US in Culturelle. It became one of the most studied probiotic strains. In 2020 the genus Lactobacillus was split, and the species is now formally called Lacticaseibacillus rhamnosus. It is sold as a dietary supplement and is not an FDA approved drug.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Gorbach and Goldin isolate the strain at Tufts University."], ["1990s", "LGG products reach the market in Finland and then other countries."], ["2018", "A large NEJM trial finds no benefit for LGG in children with acute gastroenteritis."], ["2019", "A genomic study links LGG bacteremia in ICU patients to the probiotic capsules they received."], ["2020", "Lactobacillus rhamnosus is renamed Lacticaseibacillus rhamnosus."]], "cell": "LGG is a live bacterium, not a receptor drug, and its proposed actions come from strain studies. First, it tolerates acid and bile, so many cells reach the intestine alive. Second, it carries SpaCBA pili, hair-like surface proteins that bind intestinal mucus and help it stay near the lining for a while. Third, it secretes two proteins, p40 and p75, that in lab studies activate EGF receptor signaling in epithelial cells, reduce cell death and support tight junctions. Fourth, it ferments sugars to lactic acid and competes with pathogens for space and nutrients. Fifth, it interacts with dendritic cells and other mucosal immune cells through pattern recognition receptors, which may shift cytokine signals. The evidence is strongest for antibiotic-associated diarrhea in children and weak or negative for many other uses.", "body": [["Intestinal lining", "Pili-mediated adhesion and the p40 and p75 proteins are proposed to support the epithelial barrier and help it recover from injury."], ["Gut microbiota", "While taken, LGG adds to the bacterial population and may limit overgrowth of harmful bacteria during antibiotic courses."], ["Mucosal immune system", "Contact with gut immune cells may shift cytokine signals, the basis for studies in allergy prevention and daycare infections, with mixed results."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "In critically ill, central-line or severely immunosuppressed patients, the organism can cross into the blood and cause bacteremia or endocarditis."]], "journey": "LGG is swallowed as capsules, powders or in dairy foods. Many cells survive stomach acid and bile and reach the small and large intestine alive. It is not absorbed or metabolized like a drug. Instead it lives in the gut for a short time, attaching to mucus and multiplying. It shows up in stool within days of starting. After the product is stopped, it usually disappears from stool within about one to two weeks, showing that colonization is temporary. Antibiotics can kill it, so doses are commonly separated by a few hours.", "sidefx": "Gas and bloating come from bacterial fermentation of sugars in the gut, which produces gas and short-chain fatty acids. The serious risk is invasive infection. In people with a damaged gut barrier, central lines or weak immune systems, live bacteria can enter the blood, and a 2019 study matched LGG in ICU patients' blood to their probiotic capsules. FDA warned in 2023 about invasive infection in preterm infants given probiotics. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics the strain is sensitive to.", "fun": "The letters GG honor the two discoverers, Gorbach and Goldin.", "src": ["Culturelle product information", "Freedman SB et al. NEJM 2018", "Yelin I et al. Nature Medicine 2019", "FDA 2023 safety communication on probiotics in preterm infants"]}, "saccharomyces-boulardii": {"origin": "The yeast was found by French microbiologist Henri Boulard in the 1920s while he was in Indochina (now Vietnam and its neighbors) during a cholera outbreak. He noticed that some local people chewed the skins of lychee and mangosteen fruit to control diarrhea, and he isolated a yeast from the fruit. The French company Biocodex developed it into a medicine, launched in France as Ultra-Levure in 1962. It is sold in the US as Florastor. Genetic studies later showed it is a variety of baker's yeast, Saccharomyces cerevisiae, rather than its own species. In the US it is a dietary supplement, not an FDA approved drug.", "timeline": [["1920s", "Henri Boulard isolates the yeast from tropical fruit in Indochina."], ["1962", "Biocodex launches it in France as Ultra-Levure."], ["2000s", "Genome studies show it is a variety of Saccharomyces cerevisiae."], ["2020", "AGA gives a conditional, low certainty suggestion for C. difficile prevention during antibiotics."]], "cell": "S. boulardii is a live yeast, so antibacterial antibiotics do not kill it. Proposed mechanisms come from strain studies. It secretes a 54 kDa serine protease that cleaves C. difficile toxins A and B and the brush-border receptor that toxin A binds, which reduces toxin-driven fluid secretion and inflammation in animal models. It makes a phosphatase that dephosphorylates bacterial endotoxin (LPS), lowering its inflammatory activity. It also releases polyamines and boosts brush-border enzymes such as lactase and sucrase, a trophic effect on the lining. It raises secretory IgA and dampens NF-kB driven inflammatory signaling in lab studies. Human evidence is moderate for antibiotic-associated diarrhea and low certainty for C. difficile prevention.", "body": [["Colon lining", "Breaking down C. difficile toxins protects colon cells from the damage that causes watery diarrhea and colitis in models."], ["Small intestine", "Higher brush-border enzyme activity may help digestion and recovery of the lining after diarrhea."], ["Gut immune system", "More secretory IgA and less inflammatory signaling may calm the gut response to pathogens."], ["Gut microbiota during antibiotics", "Because it is a yeast, it survives antibiotic courses and keeps working while bacteria are being killed."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "In ICU or central-line patients, yeast can enter the blood and cause Saccharomyces fungemia, even by spread from opened capsules nearby."]], "journey": "The yeast is swallowed as capsules or powder. It survives the stomach and passes through the gut alive but does not attach permanently. It is not absorbed into the blood in healthy people and is not metabolized like a drug. Stool levels reach a steady state within about three days of regular intake. After stopping, it is cleared from stool within about two to five days. Antifungal drugs such as fluconazole and nystatin can kill it.", "sidefx": "Gas, bloating and sometimes constipation reflect fermentation and shifts in gut contents. The serious risk is fungemia. A leaky gut lining, central lines or weak immunity let yeast cells reach the blood. Opening capsules near patients spreads yeast on hands and in the air, which explains cases in patients who never took it. There is no antidote; fungemia is treated with antifungal drugs and line removal.", "fun": "It was the first yeast widely sold as a probiotic medicine, and it is often given alongside antibiotics precisely because antibiotics cannot kill it.", "src": ["Florastor product information", "McFarland LV. World J Gastroenterol 2010 review", "AGA 2020 Clinical Practice Guidelines on probiotics"]}, "lactobacillus-reuteri-dsm-17938": {"origin": "The species is named after German microbiologist Gerhard Reuter, who studied gut lactobacilli in the 1960s; it was formally named Lactobacillus reuteri in 1980. The Swedish company BioGaia developed a strain called ATCC 55730, isolated from the breast milk of a mother in Peru, and sold it for years. Researchers then found that it carried plasmids with genes for tetracycline and lincomycin resistance. To remove that risk, scientists cured the strain of those two plasmids and created the daughter strain DSM 17938, described in 2008. It replaced the parent strain in BioGaia Protectis drops. It is now formally Limosilactobacillus reuteri and is sold as a dietary supplement, not an FDA approved drug.", "timeline": [["1980", "Lactobacillus reuteri is formally named, honoring Gerhard Reuter."], ["1980s", "Researchers describe reuterin, the antimicrobial made by L. reuteri."], ["2008", "DSM 17938 is described after removal of two resistance plasmids from ATCC 55730."], ["2018", "An individual participant data meta-analysis finds less crying in breastfed colicky infants."], ["2020", "The species moves to the new genus Limosilactobacillus."]], "cell": "DSM 17938 is a live bacterium, not a receptor drug. Its best known product is reuterin, a mix of compounds centered on 3-hydroxypropionaldehyde, made when the bacterium ferments glycerol. Reuterin is a broad antimicrobial that is thought to work by reacting with thiol groups and causing oxidative stress in other microbes. In animal models the strain reduced the firing of gut sensory nerves after distension and changed intestinal motility, which may explain fewer crying episodes in colicky infants. It also interacts with mucosal immune cells and may shift cytokine signals toward a less inflammatory pattern. These mechanisms are proposed from lab and animal work, and human benefit is limited mainly to breastfed infants with colic.", "body": [["Infant gut nerves", "Lower visceral pain signaling in animal models is proposed to reduce colic-related crying, seen in breastfed but not formula-fed infants."], ["Gut motility", "Animal data show changes in intestinal contractions, the basis for studies in constipation and functional abdominal pain with mixed results."], ["Gut microbiota", "Reuterin production may inhibit competing microbes while the strain is present."], ["Mucosal immune system", "Immune cell contact may shift cytokine balance, studied in acute diarrhea and preterm infants."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Invasive infection is rare but possible in preterm, critically ill or immunocompromised infants and patients."]], "journey": "The drops are an oil suspension placed on a spoon or nipple, and chewable tablets are also sold. The bacteria pass through the stomach and live in the gut for a short time. They are not absorbed into the blood or metabolized like a drug. The strain is detectable in stool within days and usually disappears within weeks after stopping, so colonization is temporary. Antibiotics reduce its survival.", "sidefx": "Gas and spit-up in trials occurred about as often as with placebo. The main serious concern is that any live probiotic can cross a weak gut barrier and cause bacteremia in fragile patients. FDA warned in 2023 that probiotics given to preterm infants have caused invasive infection, including a death. Removing the resistance plasmids from the parent strain was meant to avoid spreading antibiotic resistance genes to other gut bacteria. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.", "fun": "DSM 17938 was made by taking resistance genes out of a natural strain, an early example of cleaning up a probiotic for safety without genetic engineering of new traits.", "src": ["BioGaia Protectis product information", "Rosander A et al. Appl Environ Microbiol 2008", "Sung V et al. Pediatrics 2018", "FDA 2023 safety communication on probiotics in preterm infants"]}, "lactobacillus-reuteri-atcc-pta-5289": {"origin": "ATCC PTA 5289 is an L. reuteri strain from the human mouth that the Swedish company BioGaia selected for oral health in the 2000s. It is combined with DSM 17938 in Prodentis lozenges and gum, and GUM PerioBalance is a licensed version. Earlier gum studies used the parent strain ATCC 55730. Almost all trial evidence is for the two-strain product, so effects cannot be pinned on this strain alone. The details of exactly who isolated the strain and when are not widely published. It is sold as a dietary supplement, not an FDA approved drug, and since 2020 the species has been called Limosilactobacillus reuteri.", "timeline": [["1980", "Lactobacillus reuteri is formally named, honoring Gerhard Reuter."], ["2000s", "BioGaia launches Prodentis combining ATCC PTA 5289 with DSM 17938 for oral health."], ["2020", "The species moves to the new genus Limosilactobacillus."]], "cell": "Proposed mechanisms come from small studies. In the mouth, the lozenge releases live bacteria that contact the plaque biofilm on teeth and gums. Like other L. reuteri strains, it can produce reuterin (3-hydroxypropionaldehyde) from glycerol, a broad antimicrobial that stresses other microbes. It competes with periodontal pathogens such as Porphyromonas gingivalis, Aggregatibacter actinomycetemcomitans and Prevotella intermedia for space in the biofilm. Some trials measured lower inflammatory cytokines (such as IL-1 beta and TNF alpha) in gingival crevicular fluid, which may explain less gum bleeding. These effects are local, short-lived and supported mainly by small trials with conflicting results.", "body": [["Gums", "Less inflammation and bleeding were seen in some small trials, proposed to come from lower inflammatory cytokines in gum fluid."], ["Dental plaque", "Competition with pathogens and reuterin may shift the plaque community, with lower plaque scores in some studies."], ["Periodontal pockets", "As an add-on to scaling and root planing, some trials showed small extra gains in pocket depth, while others did not."], ["Gut", "Swallowed bacteria pass into the gut and are cleared in stool without lasting effect."]], "journey": "Lozenges are dissolved slowly in the mouth, which keeps the bacteria in contact with teeth and gums. The bacteria are detectable in saliva while the product is used. They are not absorbed into the blood or metabolized like a drug. Most are swallowed and pass through the gut into stool. Levels in saliva fall within weeks after stopping. Antiseptic rinses such as chlorhexidine can kill them.", "sidefx": "Side effects are uncommon. Rare allergy reactions come from product ingredients. Like any live bacteria, oral lactobacilli can in theory enter the blood through inflamed gums in severely immunosuppressed or critically ill patients, but this is rare. There is no antidote; infections would be treated with antibiotics.", "fun": "The lozenge form is the point of the product: slow dissolving keeps live bacteria in the mouth long enough to reach the gum line.", "src": ["BioGaia Prodentis product information", "Twetman S et al. Acta Odontol Scand 2009", "Katzung Basic and Clinical Pharmacology (probiotics overview)"]}, "bifidobacterium-lactis-bb-12": {"origin": "BB-12 comes from the culture collection of Chr. Hansen, a Danish company that has supplied dairy cultures since the 1870s. The strain has been in the collection since the early 1980s and in yogurts and fermented milks since the mid 1980s, making it one of the oldest commercial probiotic bifidobacteria. It was chosen because it tolerates acid, bile and oxygen better than many bifidobacteria, which helps it survive in food and in the gut. Its species was originally Bifidobacterium lactis and was made a subspecies of Bifidobacterium animalis in 2004. Chr. Hansen merged into Novonesis in 2024. It is a food and supplement ingredient, not an FDA approved drug.", "timeline": [["1980s", "BB-12 enters the Chr. Hansen collection and dairy products."], ["2004", "B. lactis is reclassified as Bifidobacterium animalis subsp. lactis."], ["2013", "The ProPrems trial of a three-strain mix including BB-12 reports less NEC in preterm infants."], ["2015", "A large trial reports more frequent stools in adults with low stool frequency."]], "cell": "BB-12 is a live bacterium with proposed, strain-level mechanisms. It tolerates gastric acid and bile, so many cells reach the colon alive. It adheres to intestinal mucus, which lengthens its stay near the lining. It ferments carbohydrates through the bifid shunt, a pathway using fructose-6-phosphate phosphoketolase that yields acetate and lactate. These acids lower colonic pH and feed other bacteria that make butyrate. The lower pH and competition for nutrients and binding sites limit pathogens. Short-chain fatty acids may soften stool and speed transit. It also interacts with mucosal immune cells, which may shift cytokine signals. Clinical evidence is strongest for a three-strain combination in preterm infants and for stool frequency in adults.", "body": [["Colon", "Acetate and lactate lower the pH and support butyrate producers, which may soften stools and increase their frequency."], ["Gut microbiota", "While taken, it raises fecal bifidobacteria and competes with potential pathogens."], ["Preterm infant gut", "As part of a three-strain mix, it lowered NEC in the ProPrems trial, though it did not reduce sepsis or death."], ["Mucosal immune system", "Immune cell contact may shift cytokine balance, studied in respiratory infections with conflicting results."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Invasive Bifidobacterium infection is rare but reported mainly in preterm and other high-risk patients."]], "journey": "BB-12 is swallowed in yogurt, fermented milk, capsules, powders or drops. Many cells survive the stomach and small intestine and reach the colon. It is not absorbed or metabolized like a drug. It is detectable in stool within days and usually disappears within weeks after stopping, so its presence is temporary. Heat, moisture and time reduce the number of live cells in a product, and antibiotics reduce its survival in the gut.", "sidefx": "Gas and bloating come from bacterial fermentation in the colon. Dairy forms contain milk protein and lactose, which matter for milk allergy and lactose intolerance. The rare serious risk is bacteremia when a weak gut barrier lets live bacteria into the blood, mainly in preterm infants and immunocompromised patients; FDA warned in 2023 after a preterm infant died from a Bifidobacterium probiotic. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.", "fun": "Chr. Hansen began as a supplier of rennet for cheesemaking in the 1870s, long before anyone sold bacteria as health products.", "src": ["Jungersen M et al. Microorganisms 2014 (BB-12 review)", "Jacobs SE et al. Pediatrics 2013 (ProPrems)", "FDA 2023 safety communication on probiotics in preterm infants"]}, "bifidobacterium-lactis-hn019": {"origin": "HN019 was developed in New Zealand in the 1990s from dairy research linked to the New Zealand Dairy Research Institute, later part of Fonterra. It was selected along with L. rhamnosus HN001 for acid and bile tolerance and immune effects in lab tests. The strain is also known as DR10 and is supplied under the HOWARU Bifido name, which passed from Danisco to DuPont and then to IFF. It was later studied for gut transit, immune markers in older adults and infant eczema. In a 2008 New Zealand infant trial, HN019 did not prevent eczema, while HN001 did. It is a supplement and food ingredient, not an FDA approved drug.", "timeline": [["1990s", "HN019 is selected from New Zealand dairy research."], ["2008", "A New Zealand infant trial finds no eczema prevention with HN019."], ["2011", "A trial reports faster whole gut transit time in adults."], ["2018", "A larger constipation trial finds benefit only in people with three or fewer bowel movements a week."]], "cell": "HN019 is a live bacterium whose mechanisms are proposed from strain studies. It ferments carbohydrates through the bifid shunt to acetate and lactate. These short-chain fatty acids lower colonic pH, provide fuel to colon cells and may stimulate colonic motility through effects on enteric nerves and serotonin-producing enterochromaffin cells, which could explain faster transit. In older adults, small studies found higher natural killer cell activity and phagocytosis, suggesting innate immune signaling through pattern recognition receptors on gut immune cells. None of these are receptor drug actions, and the clinical effects are modest and inconsistent.", "body": [["Colon motility", "Short-chain fatty acids may speed colonic transit, seen as faster whole gut transit in a 2011 trial."], ["Bowel habits", "A 2018 trial found more bowel movements only in people starting with three or fewer per week."], ["Innate immune system", "Small studies in older adults found higher NK cell activity and phagocytosis while taking it."], ["Skin (infant eczema)", "No eczema prevention was seen with HN019 in the 2008 New Zealand trial."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Invasive infection is rare but reported with bifidobacteria in preterm and other high-risk patients."]], "journey": "HN019 is swallowed in capsules, powders or fermented milks, often mixed with other strains or fiber. Many cells survive to the colon. It is not absorbed or metabolized like a drug. It appears in stool within days and usually disappears within weeks after stopping. Heat and time reduce live counts in products, and antibiotics reduce its survival.", "sidefx": "Gas and bloating come from fermentation in the colon. The rare serious risk with any live bifidobacterium is bacteremia in people with a weak gut barrier or immune system, especially preterm infants, as highlighted by the FDA 2023 warning. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.", "fun": "HN019 and its sister strain HN001 were tested side by side in the same infant trial, and only one of them reduced eczema, a clear example that probiotic effects are strain specific.", "src": ["Wickens K et al. J Allergy Clin Immunol 2008", "Waller PA et al. Scand J Gastroenterol 2011", "FDA 2023 safety communication on probiotics in preterm infants"]}, "bifidobacterium-longum-35624": {"origin": "Strain 35624 was isolated at University College Cork in Ireland, from tissue of the healthy human intestine, by researchers linked to what became the Alimentary Pharmabiotic Centre. It was first named Bifidobacterium infantis 35624, and genome sequencing later showed it belongs to B. longum subsp. longum. After positive irritable bowel syndrome trials in 2005 and 2006, Procter and Gamble launched it in the US as Align in the 2000s. It is sold as a dietary supplement, not an FDA approved drug, and current ACG and AGA guidelines do not endorse probiotics for IBS.", "timeline": [["1990s", "The strain is isolated at University College Cork."], ["2005", "A trial of 75 adults with IBS reports symptom benefit."], ["2006", "A trial of 362 women with IBS shows benefit at the middle of three doses."], ["2000s", "Procter and Gamble markets the strain as Align."], ["2010s", "Genome sequencing reclassifies it as B. longum."]], "cell": "Strain 35624 is thought to act mainly as an immune signal rather than by settling in the gut. In an early IBS trial, people taking it had a more normal ratio of IL-10 (an anti-inflammatory cytokine) to IL-12 (a pro-inflammatory cytokine) in blood immune cells. In mouse and cell models it induces regulatory T cells (Foxp3 positive) through dendritic cell signaling, including Toll-like receptor 2 and retinoic acid pathways, which tones down inflammatory responses. Small trials in ulcerative colitis, chronic fatigue syndrome and psoriasis showed lower CRP and some cytokines over six to eight weeks. The link between these immune changes and IBS pain and bloating is proposed, not proven.", "body": [["Gut symptoms (IBS)", "Two early trials reported less abdominal pain and bloating, though the larger trial showed benefit only at one of three doses."], ["Mucosal immune system", "Regulatory T cell induction and higher IL-10 are proposed to calm low-grade gut inflammation."], ["Systemic inflammation", "Small trials found lower CRP and inflammatory cytokines in blood while taking it."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Invasive Bifidobacterium infection is rare but possible in high-risk patients."]], "journey": "Align capsules are swallowed and the bacteria pass through the stomach to the intestine. The strain is recovered from stool during use but does not permanently colonize, and stool levels return to baseline after stopping. It is not absorbed or metabolized like a drug. Heat and time reduce live counts, and antibiotics can kill it.", "sidefx": "Gas and bloating come from bacterial fermentation. The rare serious risk with live bifidobacteria is bacteremia in people with a weak gut barrier or immune system. New IBS-like symptoms after age 50, weight loss or bleeding point to causes a probiotic does not address. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.", "fun": "The strain was renamed after its genome was read, so older papers call it B. infantis 35624 while newer ones call it B. longum 35624.", "src": ["O'Mahony L et al. Gastroenterology 2005", "Whorwell PJ et al. Am J Gastroenterol 2006", "ACG 2021 IBS Guideline", "AGA 2020 Clinical Practice Guidelines on probiotics"]}, "bifidobacterium-infantis-evc001": {"origin": "EVC001 grew out of research on breast milk at the University of California, Davis. Scientists there studied human milk oligosaccharides (HMOs), complex sugars in breast milk that babies cannot digest. They found that B. longum subsp. infantis carries a full set of genes to import and break down HMOs, so breast milk acts as its food. The company Evolve BioSystems, later renamed Infinant Health, was founded in the 2010s to develop a B. infantis strain, EVC001, as Evivo. A 2017 trial (Frese and colleagues) found it colonized breastfed infants and persisted after a short course. In 2023 FDA reported a preterm infant death from B. longum sepsis matched to Evivo with MCT Oil. It is a supplement, not an FDA approved drug.", "timeline": [["2000s", "UC Davis researchers show B. infantis can digest human milk oligosaccharides."], ["2010s", "Evolve BioSystems develops EVC001 as Evivo."], ["2017", "A trial shows lasting colonization in breastfed term infants."], ["2023", "FDA reports a preterm infant death from sepsis linked to Evivo with MCT Oil."]], "cell": "EVC001 has the HMO utilization gene cluster, including ABC transporters that import intact milk oligosaccharides and enzymes such as fucosidases, sialidases and beta-galactosidases that take them apart inside the cell. It ferments the sugars through the bifid shunt to acetate and lactate. These acids lower stool pH, which disfavors Proteobacteria such as Enterobacteriaceae. In study infants, colonization was linked to lower fecal HMOs, lower stool pH, fewer Gram-negative bacteria, lower fecal endotoxin and lower markers of gut inflammation. Because it depends on HMOs, it is studied in breastfed infants. Clinical outcome data such as fewer infections are very limited.", "body": [["Infant colon", "HMO fermentation to acetate and lactate lowers stool pH and makes the gut less friendly to Proteobacteria."], ["Gut microbiota", "The strain can become dominant in breastfed infants and persisted more than 30 days after a three-week course in one study."], ["Gut inflammation", "Lower fecal endotoxin and inflammatory markers were seen in mechanistic studies."], ["Bloodstream (preterm infants)", "In fragile preterm infants, the organism can enter the blood and cause sepsis, including a reported death."]], "journey": "The powder is mixed with a small amount of breast milk and fed to the infant. The bacteria pass to the colon, where they feed on HMOs and multiply. Colonization occurs within days in breastfed infants. Nothing is absorbed into the blood as a drug, and it is not metabolized; the bacteria and their products leave in stool. Unlike most probiotics, it can persist for weeks after stopping when breast milk keeps feeding it. Formula provides little HMO, so it does not thrive.", "sidefx": "Gas or fussiness is uncommon. The serious risk is that an immature gut barrier in preterm or medically fragile infants lets live bacteria into the blood, causing bacteremia and sepsis. FDA states no probiotic is approved to prevent disease in preterm infants. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.", "fun": "Human milk contains large amounts of sugars that the baby cannot digest, which researchers think exist mainly to feed the right gut bacteria.", "src": ["Frese SA et al. mSphere 2017", "Sela DA et al. PNAS 2008", "FDA 2023 safety communication on probiotics in preterm infants"]}, "lactobacillus-acidophilus-ncfm": {"origin": "NCFM stands for North Carolina Food Microbiology. The strain was isolated from a human source at North Carolina State University in the 1970s by researchers including Stanley Gilliland and Marvin Speck. It was used in Sweet Acidophilus milk, an unfermented milk with added bacteria, and later became an ingredient strain supplied by Rhodia, Danisco, DuPont and now IFF. In 2005 its full genome was sequenced, one of the first for a probiotic strain. The species name Lactobacillus acidophilus stayed the same in the 2020 reclassification. It is a supplement ingredient, not an FDA approved drug.", "timeline": [["1970s", "NCFM is isolated at North Carolina State University."], ["1970s", "The strain is used in Sweet Acidophilus milk."], ["2005", "The NCFM genome is published."], ["2009", "A trial in children reports fewer cold symptoms with NCFM alone or with B. lactis."]], "cell": "NCFM is a live lactic acid bacterium, not a receptor drug. It ferments sugars to lactic acid, which lowers local pH and inhibits some competitors. It binds intestinal epithelial cells and mucus, partly through surface layer proteins (SlpA). In lab studies SlpA binds DC-SIGN on dendritic cells, which shifts cytokine output and T cell responses. It also has bile salt hydrolase activity, which helps it survive bile. Proposed clinical effects on IBS pain, bloating and colds come from these local immune and microbial interactions. A 12-week trial of 391 adults with IBS found no overall difference from placebo.", "body": [["Gut lumen", "Lactic acid production and competition for nutrients may limit some harmful microbes while it is present."], ["Gut immune system", "Surface layer protein binding to dendritic cells may shift cytokine responses, studied for colds in children."], ["Bowel symptoms", "Studied for IBS and bloating with small or no overall benefit."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Lactobacillus bacteremia and endocarditis are rare but reported in immunocompromised or critically ill patients."]], "journey": "NCFM is swallowed in capsules, tablets, powders or dairy foods. Many cells survive acid and bile and reach the intestine. It is not absorbed or metabolized like a drug. It is present in the gut within days and usually cleared from stool within days to weeks after stopping. Antibiotics can kill it, and viability depends on storage.", "sidefx": "Gas and bloating come from sugar fermentation by the bacteria. Dairy forms contain milk protein and lactose. The rare serious risk is bacteremia or endocarditis when live bacteria cross a damaged gut barrier or reach damaged heart valves in high-risk patients. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.", "fun": "Sweet Acidophilus milk let people get acidophilus bacteria without the sour taste of fermented milk, because the bacteria were added cold and not allowed to ferment.", "src": ["Sanders ME, Klaenhammer TR. J Dairy Sci 2001 (NCFM review)", "Altermann E et al. PNAS 2005", "Leyer GJ et al. Pediatrics 2009"]}, "lactobacillus-casei-shirota": {"origin": "Minoru Shirota, a medical microbiologist at Kyoto Imperial University in Japan, wanted to prevent infections that were common in Japan at the time. In 1930 he selected and cultured a lactobacillus strain that could survive stomach acid and bile and reach the intestine alive. In 1935 he began selling it in a fermented milk drink called Yakult, which became a global brand. The strain was long called Lactobacillus casei Shirota and is now classified as Lacticaseibacillus paracasei. Yakult is a food, and the strain is not an FDA approved drug.", "timeline": [["1930", "Minoru Shirota cultures the strain in Kyoto."], ["1935", "Yakult fermented milk drink goes on sale in Japan."], ["2003", "A trial of 70 adults reports improvement in constipation."], ["2020", "The strain is reclassified as Lacticaseibacillus paracasei Shirota."]], "cell": "The Shirota strain is acid- and bile-tolerant, so it reaches the colon alive in large numbers. There it ferments sugars to lactic acid, lowering pH and competing with other microbes. Lactic acid and other products may soften stool and support transit. The strain interacts with gut immune cells; its cell wall components are sensed by pattern recognition receptors on macrophages and dendritic cells, which can raise IL-12 and natural killer cell activity in lab and small human studies. These effects are proposed and modest, and pooled IBS trials showed no benefit.", "body": [["Colon", "Lactic acid and fermentation products may soften stools, supported by small constipation trials."], ["Gut microbiota", "While taken, it adds large numbers of lactobacilli and may lower some harmful bacteria."], ["Immune system", "Small trials reported changes in NK cell activity, with mixed results for colds."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Lactobacillus bacteremia is rare but reported in immunocompromised or critically ill patients."]], "journey": "The bacteria are swallowed in the refrigerated milk drink. Many survive the stomach and small intestine and are recovered alive in stool within days. They are not absorbed or metabolized like a drug. After stopping, the strain is cleared from stool within days to weeks. Antibiotics can kill it.", "sidefx": "Gas and bloating come from bacterial fermentation. The drink contains milk protein and added sugar, which matter for milk allergy and diabetes. The rare serious risk is bacteremia in people with a weak gut barrier or immune system. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.", "fun": "Yakult's name comes from 'jahurto', a word for yogurt in Esperanto.", "src": ["Yakult Honsha company history", "Koebnick C et al. Can J Gastroenterol 2003", "Katzung Basic and Clinical Pharmacology (probiotics overview)"]}, "lactobacillus-paracasei-cncm-i-1518": {"origin": "This strain was developed by the French food company Danone and deposited at the Pasteur Institute's national collection (CNCM) under the number I-1518. It was long known as Lactobacillus casei DN-114 001, or L. casei Defensis. Danone launched it in the fermented milk drink Actimel in Europe in the 1990s, and later sold it in the US as DanActive. The drink also contains the two standard yogurt cultures. Its health claims drew regulatory scrutiny; EFSA did not accept a proposed claim on antibiotic-associated diarrhea in 2010. It is a food, not an FDA approved drug.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Danone launches Actimel with the strain in Europe."], ["2007", "A small BMJ trial reports less antibiotic-associated diarrhea in hospital patients over 50."], ["2010", "Trials in daycare children and adults over 70 report effects on common infections."], ["2010", "EFSA rejects a proposed EU health claim on antibiotic-associated diarrhea."]], "cell": "CNCM I-1518 is a live lactic acid bacterium, not a receptor drug. It survives transit and ferments sugars to lactic acid in the gut, lowering pH and competing with other microbes. In lab studies it interacts with intestinal epithelial cells and dendritic cells, dampening NF-kB driven inflammatory signaling in response to pathogens and influencing cytokine release. It is proposed to strengthen innate defenses against common infections. The drink also contains Streptococcus thermophilus and Lactobacillus bulgaricus, which add lactase activity. Clinical evidence is limited and mixed.", "body": [["Gut immune system", "Contact with epithelial and dendritic cells is proposed to tune inflammatory signaling, the basis for infection trials."], ["Respiratory and GI infections", "Trials in daycare children and older adults reported some effects on common infections, with modest evidence."], ["Gut during antibiotics", "A small hospital trial reported less antibiotic-associated diarrhea, but the claim was not accepted by EFSA."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Lactobacillus bacteremia is rare but reported in immunocompromised or critically ill patients."]], "journey": "The bacteria are swallowed in a fermented milk drink. Many survive to the intestine and are recovered alive in stool within days. They are not absorbed or metabolized like a drug. After stopping, the strain is cleared from stool within days to weeks. Antibiotics can kill it.", "sidefx": "Gas and bloating come from fermentation. The drink contains milk protein and added sugar. The rare serious risk is bacteremia when live bacteria cross a weak gut barrier in high-risk patients. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.", "fun": "CNCM is the culture collection of the Pasteur Institute in Paris, where many commercial strains are deposited so that they can be patented and identified.", "src": ["Hickson M et al. BMJ 2007", "Merenstein D et al. Eur J Clin Nutr 2010", "EFSA NDA Panel opinion 2010"]}, "lactobacillus-plantarum-299v": {"origin": "Lp299v was isolated from healthy human intestinal mucosa by researchers at Lund University in Sweden, including microbiologist Goran Molin and surgeon Bengt Jeppsson, in the early 1990s. They chose it for its ability to stick to the gut lining. The Swedish company Probi was founded in 1991 to commercialize Lund strains, and Lp299v first reached consumers in ProViva fruit drinks in Sweden in 1994. It is now sold in supplements such as Jarrow Ideal Bowel Support. Since 2020 its formal name is Lactiplantibacillus plantarum. It is a supplement, not an FDA approved drug.", "timeline": [["1991", "Probi is founded in Sweden to develop Lund University strains."], ["1994", "Lp299v is launched in ProViva fruit drinks in Sweden."], ["2010s", "Isotope studies report higher nonheme iron absorption with Lp299v."], ["2020", "The species is renamed Lactiplantibacillus plantarum."]], "cell": "Lp299v carries a mannose-specific adhesin that binds mannose-containing receptors on intestinal epithelial cells, letting it attach to the mucosa and compete with pathogens such as some E. coli that use the same receptors. It ferments sugars to lactic acid, lowering pH. In lab studies it increased expression of the mucins MUC2 and MUC3, thickening the protective mucus layer. Lower pH and reducing compounds in the gut may help convert ferric iron (Fe3+) to ferrous iron (Fe2+), the form taken up by DMT1 on enterocytes, which explains higher nonheme iron absorption in isotope studies. Effects on IBS symptoms are proposed to come from local barrier and microbial changes; ACG recommends against probiotics for global IBS symptoms.", "body": [["Intestinal lining", "Mannose-specific adhesion and higher mucin production are proposed to strengthen the mucus barrier."], ["Iron absorption", "Isotope studies found more nonheme iron absorbed from meals, likely by favoring the ferrous form."], ["Bowel symptoms", "Studied for IBS pain and bloating with mixed trial results."], ["Gut microbiota", "Temporary colonization and lactic acid may crowd out some competing microbes."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Lactobacillus bacteremia is rare but reported in immunocompromised or critically ill patients."]], "journey": "Lp299v is swallowed in capsules or fruit drinks. It survives the stomach and attaches temporarily to the intestinal lining. It is recovered in stool within days. It is not absorbed or metabolized like a drug, and it is cleared from stool within days to weeks after stopping. Antibiotics can kill it.", "sidefx": "Gas and bloating come from bacterial fermentation. The rare serious risk is bacteremia when live bacteria cross a weak gut barrier in high-risk patients, and FDA warned in 2023 about probiotics in preterm infants. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.", "fun": "Lactiplantibacillus plantarum is one of the bacteria in sauerkraut, sourdough and many other plant ferments.", "src": ["Probi company information", "Ducrotte P et al. World J Gastroenterol 2012", "ACG 2021 IBS Guideline"]}, "lactobacillus-rhamnosus-hn001": {"origin": "HN001, also called DR20, came from New Zealand dairy research in the 1990s at what became Fonterra. It was selected with its sister strain B. lactis HN019 for survival and immune effects in lab studies, and it is now supplied by IFF. HN001 became known for a 2008 New Zealand trial led by Kristin Wickens and Julian Crane, in which mothers took it in late pregnancy and while breastfeeding and infants took it for two years; eczema rates in the infants fell. It has also been studied for gestational diabetes and postpartum mood. It is a supplement, not an FDA approved drug.", "timeline": [["1990s", "HN001 is selected from New Zealand dairy research."], ["2008", "A New Zealand trial reports less infant eczema with HN001."], ["2017", "A trial reports lower gestational diabetes rates in some subgroups with HN001."], ["2020", "The species is renamed Lacticaseibacillus rhamnosus."]], "cell": "HN001 is a live lactic acid bacterium whose effects are thought to come from training the developing immune system. In early life the gut-associated lymphoid tissue learns tolerance from microbial signals. Bacterial cell wall components such as lipoteichoic acid and peptidoglycan bind Toll-like receptor 2 and NOD2 on dendritic cells, which may promote regulatory T cells and tilt the Th1 and Th2 balance away from allergy. This is proposed, not established. The strain was not found in the breast milk of supplemented mothers, and clinical effects were seen when the infants themselves received it.", "body": [["Infant immune system", "Early microbial signals may promote tolerance, proposed to explain less eczema in the 2008 trial."], ["Skin", "Lower eczema rates were reported in infants who took HN001 directly."], ["Metabolism in pregnancy", "One trial reported lower gestational diabetes in some subgroups, a finding that needs confirmation."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Lactobacillus bacteremia is rare but reported in immunocompromised, critically ill or preterm patients."]], "journey": "HN001 is swallowed in capsules or powders. It passes through the stomach and lives in the gut for a short time, appearing in stool within days. It is not absorbed or metabolized like a drug and is cleared from stool after stopping. It was not detected in breast milk of mothers taking it. Antibiotics can kill it.", "sidefx": "Gas and bloating come from fermentation. The rare serious risk is bacteremia in people with a weak gut barrier or immune system, including preterm infants, as noted in the FDA 2023 warning. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.", "src": ["Wickens K et al. J Allergy Clin Immunol 2008", "Wickens KL et al. Br J Nutr 2017", "FDA 2023 safety communication on probiotics in preterm infants"]}, "lactobacillus-rhamnosus-gr-1-and-reuteri-rc-14": {"origin": "These two strains were developed by Gregor Reid and colleagues at the University of Western Ontario (now Western University) in London, Ontario, Canada, with urologist Andrew Bruce, starting in the 1980s. Reid was looking for lactobacilli that could protect women from urinary and vaginal infections. GR-1 was isolated from the urethra of a healthy woman, and RC-14 from a healthy vagina. RC-14 was first named L. fermentum RC-14 and later identified as L. reuteri. The pair is taken by mouth and sold in products such as Jarrow Fem-Dophilus. It is a supplement, not an FDA approved drug.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Gregor Reid and Andrew Bruce begin work on GR-1 and RC-14 in Ontario."], ["2000s", "Trials report shifts in vaginal flora with oral intake."], ["2012", "The combination does not meet noninferiority to TMP-SMX for UTI prevention."], ["2020", "The species are renamed Lacticaseibacillus rhamnosus and Limosilactobacillus reuteri."]], "cell": "After oral intake the strains pass through the gut, and some reach the vagina from the rectal and perineal skin. There they are proposed to act locally. They produce lactic acid, which keeps vaginal pH low and is toxic to many BV-associated anaerobes. RC-14 produces hydrogen peroxide and biosurfactants, surface-active proteins that reduce the ability of uropathogens such as E. coli and Enterococcus to stick to epithelial cells. Both compete with pathogens for binding sites (competitive exclusion). These actions come mainly from lab and small human studies.", "body": [["Vagina", "More lactobacillus-dominated flora and lower pH were reported in some trials, which disfavors BV-associated bacteria."], ["Urinary tract", "Biosurfactants may reduce uropathogen adhesion, but the combination did not match TMP-SMX for UTI prevention."], ["Gut", "The strains pass through the gut temporarily before reaching the vaginal area."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Lactobacillus bacteremia is rare but reported in immunocompromised or critically ill patients."]], "journey": "The capsules are swallowed. The bacteria pass through the gut and are shed in stool. Over weeks of daily intake, some move from the perineal skin to the vagina, where changes in flora have been measured. They are not absorbed into the blood or metabolized like a drug. The effect fades after stopping. Antibiotics can kill them.", "sidefx": "Gas and bloating come from gut fermentation. The rare serious risk is bacteremia in high-risk patients. Because the product did not match standard antibiotic prophylaxis for UTI, relying on it in place of proven therapy could leave infections untreated. There is no antidote; infections are treated with antibiotics.", "fun": "The research program began with the idea that the bacteria of healthy women could be used to protect other women from infection.", "src": ["Reid G et al. FEMS Immunol Med Microbiol 2003", "Beerepoot MA et al. Arch Intern Med 2012", "Jarrow Fem-Dophilus product information"]}, "escherichia-coli-nissle-1917": {"origin": "In 1917, during World War I, German physician and bacteriologist Alfred Nissle in Freiburg looked for an E. coli that could fight intestinal pathogens. He isolated a strain from the stool of a soldier who stayed healthy during a severe outbreak of shigella dysentery while his comrades fell ill. Nissle showed in culture that the strain inhibited other bacteria, a property he called antagonism. He began selling it the same year as Mutaflor, which is still made by Ardeypharm in Germany. In 2006 researchers found that it carries the pks genomic island and makes colibactin, a toxin that damages DNA in cell and mouse studies. It is licensed as a medicine in Germany but is not an FDA approved drug in the US.", "timeline": [["1917", "Alfred Nissle isolates the strain from a healthy WWI soldier and launches Mutaflor."], ["1990s", "Trials compare EcN with mesalamine for keeping ulcerative colitis in remission."], ["2006", "Researchers show EcN carries the pks island that makes colibactin."]], "cell": "EcN is a live, nonpathogenic Gram-negative bacterium of serotype O6:K5:H1. It colonizes the colon and outcompetes other Enterobacteriaceae, mainly for iron. It has several siderophore systems (enterobactin, salmochelin, aerobactin and yersiniabactin) that grab scarce iron. It also makes microcins M and H47, small antibacterial peptides that target related bacteria. Its flagellin binds Toll-like receptor 5 on epithelial cells and induces human beta-defensin 2, an antimicrobial peptide. It up-regulates tight junction proteins such as ZO-1 in lab studies, tightening the barrier. In ulcerative colitis trials it was similar to low-dose mesalamine for maintaining remission. It carries the pks island and makes colibactin, a DNA-alkylating genotoxin whose clinical meaning is unknown.", "body": [["Colon microbiota", "Iron competition and microcins crowd out harmful E. coli, Salmonella and other relatives."], ["Colon lining", "Beta-defensin induction and tighter junctions are proposed to strengthen the barrier."], ["Colonic inflammation", "Studied for keeping ulcerative colitis in remission, with results similar to mesalamine in trials."], ["Colon cells (DNA)", "Colibactin can damage DNA in lab and mouse studies, a theoretical long-term concern."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "As a live Gram-negative organism, it can cause bacteremia in immunocompromised or critically ill patients."]], "journey": "EcN is swallowed in enteric capsules. It survives to the colon, where it multiplies and colonizes within days. It is not absorbed into the blood or metabolized like a drug. It is shed in stool, and fecal colonization usually fades after intake ends. Antibiotics active against E. coli kill it.", "sidefx": "Bloating, flatulence and mild abdominal discomfort come from added bacterial activity in the colon. Because it is an E. coli, it can cause bacteremia or sepsis if it crosses a damaged barrier in vulnerable patients. Colibactin-related DNA damage is a theoretical risk under study. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics active against E. coli.", "fun": "The strain has been sold continuously since 1917, which makes it one of the oldest commercial probiotics in the world.", "src": ["Mutaflor product information (Ardeypharm)", "Kruis W et al. Gut 2004", "Nougayrede JP et al. Science 2006"]}, "bacillus-coagulans-gbi-30": {"origin": "Bacillus coagulans was first described in 1915 by B. W. Hammer, who found it curdling canned evaporated milk, which is where the name 'coagulans' comes from. It forms tough spores and makes lactic acid, so it was later sold as a probiotic. The strain GBI-30, 6086 was developed by Ganeden Biotech, a company based in Ohio, and marketed as GanedenBC30 in supplements and in foods such as baked goods and drinks. Kerry acquired Ganeden in 2017. The species name has since changed to Heyndrickxia coagulans. It has an FDA GRAS notice for food use, but it is not an FDA approved drug.", "timeline": [["1915", "B. W. Hammer describes Bacillus coagulans from coagulated canned milk."], ["2000s", "Ganeden Biotech develops and markets the GBI-30, 6086 strain."], ["2017", "Kerry acquires Ganeden."], ["2020s", "The species is renamed, now Heyndrickxia coagulans."]], "cell": "GBI-30 is sold as spores, a dormant form with a thick protective coat. Spores resist heat, stomach acid and bile, so they survive food processing and the stomach. In the small intestine, moisture and nutrients trigger germination into active cells. The active cells ferment sugars to L-lactic acid, lowering pH and competing with other microbes. Small studies found changes in cytokine release from immune cells exposed to the strain, which is the basis for proposed immune effects. Proposed benefits for gas, bloating and IBS symptoms come from small trials. It is not a receptor drug.", "body": [["Small intestine and colon", "Germinated cells make lactic acid and may shift the local microbial community for a short time."], ["Gut symptoms", "Small trials reported less gas and bloating, with limited evidence."], ["Immune system", "Lab and small human studies report changes in cytokine signaling."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Bacteremia is a recognized risk of live bacterial products in immunocompromised or critically ill patients."]], "journey": "Spores are swallowed in capsules or foods. They pass through the stomach unharmed, germinate in the intestine, and grow for a time. The organism is not absorbed or metabolized like a drug and leaves in stool. It does not permanently colonize, and its presence fades after stopping. Its timing of action is not well defined.", "sidefx": "Gas and bloating come from fermentation by the germinated bacteria. The rare serious risk is bacteremia when live bacteria cross a weak gut barrier in high-risk patients. The GRAS notice addresses safety as a food ingredient, not benefit. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.", "fun": "Because its spores survive baking, the strain is added to products like muffins and pasta that would kill most probiotics.", "src": ["FDA GRAS Notice for Bacillus coagulans GBI-30, 6086", "Hammer BW. Iowa Agricultural Experiment Station Research Bulletin 1915", "Katzung Basic and Clinical Pharmacology (probiotics overview)"]}, "bacillus-clausii": {"origin": "Bacillus clausii spores have been sold in Italy as Enterogermina since the late 1950s, developed by an Italian company whose product line later passed to Sanofi and its consumer health spin-off Opella. The product contains four strains, O/C, N/R, SIN and T, which were selected for resistance to different antibiotics so the bacteria could survive alongside antibiotic treatment. The strains were first labeled Bacillus subtilis and were reclassified as B. clausii in the 2000s; the species is now called Shouchella clausii. It is widely used for childhood diarrhea in Europe, Asia and Latin America but is not marketed in the US and is not an FDA approved drug.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Enterogermina spores go on sale in Italy."], ["2000s", "The strains, first labeled B. subtilis, are identified as Bacillus clausii."], ["2020s", "The species is renamed, now Shouchella clausii."]], "cell": "The product contains spores that resist gastric acid and bile. In the intestine they germinate into active cells. Proposed actions come from lab and small human studies. The active cells produce antimicrobial substances, including a lantibiotic, that inhibit some Gram-positive bacteria such as C. difficile and S. aureus in vitro. They compete with pathogens for nutrients and space. They may stimulate mucosal immunity, including IgA and interferon signals. The strains carry chromosomal resistance genes to macrolides, a beta-lactamase, aminoglycosides and chloramphenicol, which let them persist during antibiotic courses; transfer to other bacteria was not shown in studies.", "body": [["Intestine", "Germinated cells make antimicrobial compounds and compete with pathogens, proposed to shorten diarrhea."], ["Gut during antibiotics", "Because the strains resist many antibiotics, they survive treatment, and two trials reported fewer antibiotic-related GI side effects."], ["Mucosal immune system", "Lab and small human studies suggest stimulation of local immune defenses."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Case reports describe B. clausii bacteremia, mainly in infants and immunocompromised patients."]], "journey": "Spores are swallowed from small oral vials or capsules. They pass through the stomach intact, germinate in the intestine and grow for a time. They are not absorbed or metabolized like a drug and leave in stool. They do not permanently colonize, and the organism fades after stopping. Its timing of action is not well defined.", "sidefx": "It is generally well tolerated, with mild stomach upset and rare rash or allergy. The rare serious risk is bacteremia when live bacteria cross a weak gut barrier, reported in infants and immunocompromised patients. Its built-in antibiotic resistance, the same trait that lets it survive antibiotic courses, narrows treatment choices if bacteremia occurs. There is no antidote; infection is treated with an antibiotic the strain is sensitive to.", "fun": "Its multiple antibiotic resistances are deliberate: the strains were chosen so they would not be wiped out by the antibiotics a patient was taking.", "src": ["Enterogermina product information (Sanofi)", "Senesi S et al. Appl Environ Microbiol 2001", "Ianiro G et al. Curr Pharm Des 2018 review"]}, "bacillus-subtilis-de111": {"origin": "Bacillus subtilis is one of the best known soil bacteria. It was named in 1872 by German botanist Ferdinand Cohn, who studied its heat-resistant spores. It also ferments soybeans into natto, a traditional Japanese food. The strain DE111 was developed as a probiotic ingredient by Deerland Probiotics and Enzymes, a US company now part of ADM, in the 2010s. It is used in capsules, gummies and foods because its spores survive manufacturing and storage. It is a supplement ingredient, not an FDA approved drug.", "timeline": [["1872", "Ferdinand Cohn names Bacillus subtilis and studies its spores."], ["2010s", "Deerland develops and markets the DE111 strain."], ["2010s", "Small trials study DE111 for stool form and immune markers."]], "cell": "DE111 is sold as spores that resist stomach acid and bile. In the intestine they germinate into active cells. Active B. subtilis secretes enzymes (proteases, amylases) and antimicrobial compounds such as lipopeptides (surfactin and related molecules) that can inhibit some competing microbes. Proposed immune effects come from small human studies, including one trial reporting anti-inflammatory changes in immune cell responses. Effects on stool form and blood lipids come from small trials. It is not a receptor drug.", "body": [["Intestine", "Germinated cells release enzymes and antimicrobial compounds and may briefly shift the microbial community."], ["Bowel habits", "Small trials reported changes in stool form with limited evidence."], ["Immune system", "One trial reported anti-inflammatory changes in immune cell responses."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Rare B. subtilis bacteremia has been reported with probiotic use in immunocompromised patients."]], "journey": "Spores are swallowed in capsules, gummies or foods. They pass through the stomach intact, germinate in the intestine and grow for a time. They are not absorbed or metabolized like a drug and leave in stool. They do not permanently colonize. Timing of action is not well defined.", "sidefx": "Gas and bloating are uncommon and come from added microbial activity in the gut. The rare serious risk is bacteremia when bacteria cross a weak gut barrier in immunocompromised patients. Evidence for benefit comes mainly from small trials. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.", "fun": "Bacillus subtilis is a favorite model organism of biologists, used for decades to study how bacteria make spores.", "src": ["Deerland DE111 product information", "Cohn F. Beitrage zur Biologie der Pflanzen 1872", "Katzung Basic and Clinical Pharmacology (probiotics overview)"]}, "streptococcus-salivarius-k12": {"origin": "K12 was isolated by microbiologist John Tagg and colleagues at the University of Otago in Dunedin, New Zealand. Tagg studied why some children rarely got strep throat and found that certain mouth bacteria made bacteriocins, natural antibacterial proteins, that inhibit Streptococcus pyogenes. K12 came from the mouth of a healthy child and produced strong inhibitory activity. BLIS Technologies, named for 'bacteriocin-like inhibitory substances', was formed around 2000 to commercialize it in lozenges. It is sold as a supplement, not an FDA approved drug, and most studies are small and linked to the manufacturer.", "timeline": [["1990s", "John Tagg's group at the University of Otago isolates K12."], ["2000s", "BLIS Technologies brings K12 lozenges to market."], ["2010s", "Small trials study K12 for strep throat, ear infections and bad breath."]], "cell": "K12 is a live oral commensal bacterium. It carries a large plasmid with genes for two lantibiotic bacteriocins, salivaricin A2 and salivaricin B. Lantibiotics are small peptides with unusual lanthionine rings. They bind lipid II or disrupt the membranes of susceptible Gram-positive bacteria, inhibiting cell wall building or causing leakage. In the lab they inhibit Streptococcus pyogenes and some bacteria linked to bad breath. K12 also colonizes the tongue and throat, competing for space with other microbes. Clinical benefit is supported by small, mostly manufacturer-linked studies.", "body": [["Mouth and tongue", "Colonization and bacteriocins may reduce odor-producing bacteria, the basis for bad breath claims."], ["Throat", "Inhibition of Streptococcus pyogenes in the lab is the basis for studies on recurrent strep throat."], ["Middle ear", "Studied for ear infections in children, with limited evidence."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Bacteremia is a recognized risk of live bacterial products in immunocompromised or critically ill patients."]], "journey": "Lozenges dissolve slowly in the mouth so the bacteria can settle on the tongue and throat. Studies found oral colonization within days. The bacteria are not absorbed or metabolized like a drug. Most are swallowed with saliva and pass into stool. Colonization usually fades within weeks after stopping.", "sidefx": "It is generally well tolerated. The rare serious risk is bloodstream infection in immunocompromised or critically ill patients, a concern with any live bacterial product; viridans-type streptococci from the mouth can also seed damaged heart valves. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.", "fun": "The search for K12 started from a simple question: why do some children almost never get strep throat.", "src": ["BLIS K12 product information", "Wescombe PA et al. Future Microbiol 2009", "Tagg JR, Dierksen KP. Trends Biotechnol 2003"]}, "de-simone-formulation": {"origin": "The De Simone Formulation is an eight-strain, high-concentration probiotic mixture created by Italian physician and researcher Claudio De Simone, a professor at the University of L'Aquila. It was sold in the 1990s and 2000s as VSL#3, and most trials published as VSL#3 before 2016 used this formulation, including pouchitis trials led by Paolo Gionchetti in Bologna around 2000. After De Simone's partnership with the VSL#3 brand owner ended in 2016, he licensed the original recipe under new names: Visbiome in the US and Vivomixx in Europe. In 2018 a US federal jury found that VSL#3 made after the split was falsely advertised as the same product. It is not an FDA approved drug.", "timeline": [["1990s", "The eight-strain mix is developed by Claudio De Simone and sold as VSL#3."], ["2000", "A Bologna trial reports fewer pouchitis relapses with the mix."], ["2016", "The partnership ends and the original formulation becomes Visbiome and Vivomixx."], ["2018", "A US jury finds later VSL#3 was falsely advertised as the original."]], "cell": "The product contains eight strains of lactobacilli, bifidobacteria and Streptococcus thermophilus in very high numbers. Proposed mechanisms come from lab and small human studies. The strains compete with pathogens and shift the gut community. In pouchitis studies, mucosal IL-10 (an anti-inflammatory cytokine) rose while inflammatory cytokines such as TNF alpha, IL-1 and interferon gamma fell. Lab work showed support of tight junction proteins and less epithelial cell death. In hepatic encephalopathy studies, changes in gut flora were proposed to lower ammonia production. The mix is not a receptor drug, and trial results depend on which formulation was used.", "body": [["Ileal pouch", "Maintenance trials showed fewer pouchitis relapses, linked to higher mucosal IL-10 and lower inflammatory cytokines."], ["Colon (ulcerative colitis)", "Studied as an add-on for mild to moderate ulcerative colitis, with some positive trials."], ["Gut barrier", "Lab studies show support of tight junctions and less epithelial injury."], ["Liver and brain", "Studied in hepatic encephalopathy, where changes in gut flora may lower ammonia."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Bacteremia is a recognized risk of live bacterial products in immunocompromised or critically ill patients."]], "journey": "The powder or capsules are swallowed, delivering very large numbers of live bacteria. Many survive to the intestine, where the strains can be recovered from stool during use. They are not absorbed or metabolized like a drug. Fecal recovery fades after stopping, so ongoing intake is needed for any effect. Antibiotics reduce survival.", "sidefx": "Bloating, gas and abdominal discomfort are common early, from fermentation by the large bacterial load. The rare serious risk is bacteremia in immunocompromised or critically ill patients. Because products sold under the same brand changed, older trial results do not automatically apply to every product with that name. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.", "fun": "The 2018 court case is a rare example of a probiotic recipe being treated in court as the key to whether clinical trial results apply to a product.", "src": ["Visbiome product information", "Gionchetti P et al. Gastroenterology 2000", "AGA 2020 Clinical Practice Guidelines on probiotics"]}, "bifidobacterium-breve-m-16v": {"origin": "M-16V comes from Morinaga Milk Industry, a Japanese dairy company that has studied infant bifidobacteria for decades. The strain was isolated from the gut of an infant in Japan. It has been given to preterm infants in many Japanese neonatal units since the 1990s, and later in some Australian units. It is also added to some specialty infant formulas, such as Nutricia's Neocate Syneo, as part of synbiotic mixes with prebiotic fibers. It is not an FDA approved drug, and AAP (2021) did not support routine probiotic use in preterm infants.", "timeline": [["1990s", "M-16V use in Japanese neonatal units becomes common."], ["2010s", "M-16V is added to some synbiotic specialty infant formulas."], ["2021", "AAP does not support routine probiotic use in preterm infants."], ["2023", "FDA warns about probiotics in preterm infants after a death from a different product."]], "cell": "M-16V is an infant-type bifidobacterium. It colonizes the infant colon, which in healthy breastfed babies is normally dominated by bifidobacteria. It ferments oligosaccharides through the bifid shunt to acetate and lactate, which lower colonic pH. Lower pH and competition for nutrients are proposed to limit overgrowth of pathogens such as Enterobacteriaceae, which are linked to NEC. Lab and small human studies suggest immune effects, such as shifting the Th1 and Th2 balance and supporting regulatory signals, which is the basis for allergy studies. It is not a receptor drug.", "body": [["Preterm infant gut", "Raises fecal bifidobacteria in preterm infants, proposed to reduce pathogen overgrowth linked to NEC."], ["Colon chemistry", "Acetate and lactate lower stool pH and may protect the epithelium."], ["Infant immune system", "Studied in synbiotic formulas for allergic infants, with proposed effects on immune balance."], ["Bloodstream (preterm infants)", "Bifidobacterium bacteremia and sepsis have been reported with probiotic products in preterm infants."]], "journey": "The bacteria are given in drops or formula. They pass to the infant colon and colonize over weeks in preterm infants. They are not absorbed or metabolized like a drug and leave in stool. A small study found the strain still detectable in stool weeks after hospital discharge.", "sidefx": "It is generally well tolerated in studies. The serious concern is that an immature gut barrier in preterm or immunocompromised infants can let live bacteria into the blood, causing bacteremia or sepsis. FDA warned in 2023 after a preterm infant died from a Bifidobacterium probiotic product (a different product). There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.", "fun": "Bifidobacteria were first seen in the stool of breastfed infants in 1899 by Henry Tissier at the Pasteur Institute.", "src": ["Morinaga M-16V product information", "AAP Clinical Report on probiotics in preterm infants 2021", "FDA 2023 safety communication on probiotics in preterm infants"]}, "lactobacillus-helveticus-r0052-and-bifidobacterium-longum-r0175": {"origin": "Both strains come from the culture collection of Institut Rosell, a Montreal probiotics company founded in the 1930s and later acquired by the Canadian yeast company Lallemand. The blend was marketed in the 2010s as Cerebiome and Probio'Stick for stress and mood, as part of the 'psychobiotic' idea that gut bacteria can affect the brain. Key work included a 2011 French trial by Messaoudi and colleagues in healthy volunteers and a later depression trial. Human results are mixed. It is a supplement, not an FDA approved drug, and not an established treatment for depression.", "timeline": [["1930s", "Institut Rosell is founded in Montreal."], ["2011", "A trial in healthy volunteers reports less psychological distress with the blend."], ["2017", "A depression trial reports benefit as an add-on, with higher BDNF in a secondary analysis."]], "cell": "The mechanism in humans is not established. Animal studies suggest gut-brain axis effects. In rats under stress, the blend reduced intestinal inflammation and permeability, which could lower inflammatory signals reaching the brain. It also blunted stress-induced activation of the HPA axis (lower corticosterone) and changed signaling in brain areas involved in mood. Vagal nerve signaling from the gut to the brainstem is another proposed route. A depression trial found higher serum BDNF than placebo in a secondary analysis. None of these are receptor drug actions, and the human evidence is limited and mixed.", "body": [["Gut lining", "Animal studies show less stress-induced inflammation and leakiness of the intestinal barrier."], ["Stress hormone system", "In animals the blend dampened HPA axis activation; human cortisol effects are inconsistent."], ["Brain and mood", "Some trials reported less distress or depressive symptoms; others found no difference from placebo."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Lactobacillus and Bifidobacterium bacteremia is rare but reported in severely ill or immunocompromised patients."]], "journey": "The bacteria are swallowed as capsules or sachets. They pass through the gut and are detected in stool mainly during intake, so presence is temporary. They are not absorbed or metabolized like a drug. Trials measured outcomes after about one to two months of intake.", "sidefx": "Mild bloating or gas comes from fermentation in the gut. The rare serious risk is bacteremia in severely ill or immunocompromised patients, a class effect of live probiotics. Supplement contents can differ from labels because products are not FDA reviewed before sale. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.", "src": ["Messaoudi M et al. Br J Nutr 2011", "Kazemi A et al. Clin Nutr 2019", "Lallemand Health Solutions product information"]}, "akkermansia-muciniphila": {"origin": "Akkermansia muciniphila was discovered in 2004 by Muriel Derrien and colleagues in Willem de Vos's laboratory at Wageningen University in the Netherlands. They were looking for gut bacteria that feed on mucin, the main protein of intestinal mucus, and named the new genus after Dutch microbiologist Antoon Akkermans. Later work, especially by Patrice Cani's group at UCLouvain in Belgium, linked lower Akkermansia levels to obesity and type 2 diabetes in mice and people. A 2019 human pilot study found that a pasteurized (killed) form improved insulin sensitivity. The EU approved pasteurized Akkermansia as a novel food in 2022, and the US company Pendulum sells live versions. It is a supplement, not an FDA approved drug.", "timeline": [["2004", "Derrien and de Vos describe Akkermansia muciniphila at Wageningen University."], ["2013", "Mouse studies link Akkermansia to protection from obesity and metabolic disease."], ["2019", "A human pilot finds improved insulin sensitivity with pasteurized Akkermansia."], ["2022", "The EU authorizes pasteurized Akkermansia as a novel food."]], "cell": "Akkermansia lives in the mucus layer of the colon and uses mucin as its food. The proposed mechanism comes mainly from mice. An outer membrane protein, Amuc_1100, binds Toll-like receptor 2 on intestinal epithelial cells. This signaling strengthens tight junctions and the gut barrier, which lowers leakage of bacterial lipopolysaccharide (LPS) into the blood, a process called metabolic endotoxemia. Less LPS means less low-grade inflammation that drives insulin resistance. Akkermansia also stimulates mucus production and may influence GLP-1 secretion. Pasteurization keeps Amuc_1100 active, which may be why the killed form worked in mice and in a small human pilot.", "body": [["Gut barrier", "Amuc_1100 signaling through TLR2 tightens the barrier in mice and lowers LPS leakage."], ["Mucus layer", "By feeding on mucin, it stimulates turnover and renewal of the mucus layer."], ["Metabolism", "A small human pilot found better insulin sensitivity and lower insulin and cholesterol with the pasteurized form."], ["Inflammation", "Less LPS in the blood is proposed to lower the low-grade inflammation linked to insulin resistance."]], "journey": "Products are swallowed as capsules containing live or pasteurized bacteria. The bacteria pass to the colon. They are not absorbed or metabolized like a drug and leave in stool. Live strains are detected in stool mainly during intake. Trials measured effects after about three months of daily use.", "sidefx": "Mild bloating or gas can occur. Long-term human safety data are limited. As a mucus-degrading bacterium, there is a theoretical concern about thinning mucus in some disease states, and a theoretical risk of bacteremia with live forms in high-risk patients. Effects of the pasteurized form do not automatically apply to live products. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Akkermansia can make up a few percent of all gut bacteria in healthy adults, even though it was unknown before 2004.", "src": ["Derrien M et al. Int J Syst Evol Microbiol 2004", "Depommier C et al. Nature Medicine 2019", "EFSA NDA Panel opinion on pasteurized Akkermansia muciniphila 2021"]}, "clostridium-butyricum-miyairi-588": {"origin": "Clostridium butyricum MIYAIRI 588 comes from Japan, where research on butyrate-producing Clostridium strains was led by the Miyairi laboratory starting in the 1930s. The 588 strain was selected for its spore-forming hardiness and lack of toxin genes, and the company Miyarisan Pharmaceutical developed it as a medicine for diarrhea and gut flora disturbance. It has been used in Japan for decades. In the 2020s, researchers at City of Hope in California tested it with immunotherapy in kidney cancer, and a 2022 Nature Medicine trial reported better response rates in a small group. It is licensed as a medicine in Japan but is not an FDA approved drug.", "timeline": [["1930s", "The Miyairi laboratory in Japan studies butyrate-producing Clostridium strains."], ["2022", "A small Nature Medicine trial combines CBM588 with nivolumab and ipilimumab in kidney cancer."]], "cell": "CBM588 is sold as spores, which survive stomach acid and germinate in the gut. The active cells ferment carbohydrates to butyrate and other short-chain fatty acids. Butyrate is the main fuel for colonocytes, supports tight junctions and mucus production, and inhibits histone deacetylases in immune cells. That HDAC inhibition promotes Foxp3 regulatory T cells, which calm inflammation. The strain also raises levels of Bifidobacterium in some studies, which was the planned endpoint in the kidney cancer trials because gut bacteria may influence response to checkpoint inhibitors. CBM588 is non-toxigenic; some other C. butyricum strains carry botulinum neurotoxin type E genes.", "body": [["Colon lining", "Butyrate fuels colon cells and supports the barrier and mucus layer."], ["Gut microbiota", "Changes in gut flora include higher Bifidobacterium in some studies."], ["Immune system", "Butyrate-driven regulatory T cells may calm gut inflammation; effects on cancer immunotherapy are under study."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Strain-related bacteremia has been confirmed in hospitalized, mostly immunocompromised or central-line patients."]], "journey": "Spores are swallowed as tablets or powders. They pass through the stomach intact and germinate in the intestine within hours to days. The organism is not absorbed or metabolized like a drug. It produces short-chain fatty acids in the colon and leaves in stool. Its presence is temporary and fades after stopping.", "sidefx": "Mild bloating or gas comes from fermentation. The rare serious risk is bacteremia and sepsis when live bacteria cross a damaged barrier, confirmed by genome sequencing in some Japanese hospital patients. Because some C. butyricum strains can make botulinum toxin type E, strain identity matters. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.", "src": ["Dizman N et al. Nature Medicine 2022", "Miyarisan product information", "Katzung Basic and Clinical Pharmacology (probiotics overview)"]}, "enterococcus-faecium-sf68": {"origin": "SF68 is a strain of Enterococcus faecium developed in Switzerland by Cerbios-Pharma and sold as Bioflorin, licensed as a medicine in Italy, Austria and Switzerland. It was studied mainly in older European, largely Italian, trials for acute diarrhea and antibiotic-associated diarrhea. A version called FortiFlora is sold for dogs and cats. Because E. faecium is also a major hospital pathogen that can carry vancomycin resistance, ESPGHAN does not recommend SF68 for childhood gastroenteritis. It is not an FDA approved drug.", "timeline": [["20th century", "SF68 is developed in Switzerland and studied in older European trials for diarrhea."], ["2000s", "ESPGHAN advises against SF68 for childhood gastroenteritis over resistance gene concerns."]], "cell": "SF68 is a live lactic acid bacterium. Proposed mechanisms include competitive exclusion of pathogens for binding sites and nutrients, production of lactic acid that lowers pH, and possible bacteriocin production. Animal studies suggest immune modulation, including effects on IgA and cytokine responses. It has no single receptor target. The main concern is not its mechanism but its genus: enterococci readily swap mobile genetic elements, and vancomycin resistance genes (vanA, vanB) spread among E. faecium in hospitals. SF68 itself has not been shown to carry vancomycin resistance, but the theoretical risk of acquiring and transferring it limits its use.", "body": [["Gut microbiota", "Competition and lactic acid may limit pathogens, proposed to shorten diarrhea in older trials."], ["Gut immune system", "Animal studies suggest effects on IgA and cytokine responses."], ["Antibiotic resistance ecology", "Enterococci can trade resistance genes, a theoretical risk for spreading vancomycin resistance."], ["Bloodstream and heart valves (rare)", "Enterococci can cause bacteremia and endocarditis, a genus concern in high-risk patients."]], "journey": "SF68 is swallowed in capsules or powders. It passes to the intestine and is present there for a short time. It is not absorbed or metabolized like a drug and leaves in stool. Its presence is temporary. Diarrhea trials measured days to resolution.", "sidefx": "Mild bloating or gas comes from fermentation. The serious concerns are tied to the genus: E. faecium causes hospital infections, including bacteremia and endocarditis, and it can pick up vancomycin resistance genes. These risks are theoretical for SF68 itself. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics chosen by susceptibility testing.", "fun": "The same species includes both this probiotic strain and vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus (VRE), a major hospital superbug.", "src": ["Bioflorin product information", "ESPGHAN position paper on probiotics for acute gastroenteritis", "Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases"]}, "lactobacillus-crispatus-ctv-05": {"origin": "Lactobacillus crispatus is one of the main bacteria in a healthy vagina, and its presence is linked to low pH and a lower risk of bacterial vaginosis. CTV-05 is a hydrogen peroxide-producing strain isolated from a healthy woman and developed by Osel Inc., a California biotech company, as Lactin-V, a vaginal applicator product. It is studied under FDA oversight as an investigational live biotherapeutic. A 2011 trial (Stapleton and colleagues) studied it for recurrent bladder infections, and a 2020 NEJM phase 2b trial (Cohen and colleagues) found fewer BV recurrences after metronidazole. It is not FDA approved and is not sold in stores.", "timeline": [["2000s", "Osel develops L. crispatus CTV-05 as Lactin-V."], ["2011", "A trial reports fewer recurrent UTIs with high-level colonization."], ["2020", "A phase 2b NEJM trial finds lower BV recurrence after metronidazole."]], "cell": "CTV-05 recolonizes the vagina after antibiotics clear BV. L. crispatus ferments glycogen breakdown products from the vaginal lining to lactic acid, especially the D-lactic acid isomer, keeping pH low (about 4). The acid, in its protonated form, is toxic to Gardnerella vaginalis and other BV-associated anaerobes. The strain also makes hydrogen peroxide and competes for binding sites on vaginal epithelial cells. Lactic acid may also have anti-inflammatory effects on the epithelium. In the UTI trial, high-level vaginal colonization was linked to fewer recurrences, likely by reducing the vaginal reservoir of E. coli.", "body": [["Vagina", "Restores a lactobacillus-dominated flora with low pH, which lowered BV recurrence in trials."], ["Bladder", "By reducing vaginal E. coli reservoirs, high-level colonization was linked to fewer recurrent UTIs."], ["Vaginal lining", "Lactic acid may reduce local inflammation and support the epithelium."], ["Local tissue", "Discharge and irritation occurred at rates similar to placebo in trials."]], "journey": "Lactin-V is placed in the vagina with an applicator, usually after an antibiotic course. The bacteria colonize within days. The product acts locally and is not absorbed into the blood or metabolized like a drug. In some users colonization persisted after dosing ended.", "sidefx": "Vaginal discharge, itching and irritation in trials occurred at rates similar to placebo, likely reflecting the vehicle and the vaginal application itself. Serious infections were not described. It is investigational and not FDA approved. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Researchers group vaginal microbiomes into 'community state types', and the one dominated by L. crispatus is generally considered the most protective.", "src": ["Cohen CR et al. NEJM 2020", "Stapleton AE et al. Clin Infect Dis 2011", "Ravel J et al. PNAS 2011"]}, "lactobacillus-gasseri": {"origin": "Lactobacillus gasseri was formally named in 1980 by Lauer and Kandler, who separated it from L. acidophilus, and it is named after French microbiologist Francis Gasser. It lives naturally in the human gut, mouth and vagina. Several commercial strains were later developed, mostly in Japan: SBT2055 (Snow Brand and Megmilk) for belly fat, OLL2716 (Meiji, LG21 yogurt) for H. pylori, and heat-killed CP2305 (Asahi) for stress and sleep, plus BNR17 from Korea. A bottle labeled only L. gasseri may contain a strain with no research behind it. It is a supplement, not an FDA approved drug.", "timeline": [["1980", "Lactobacillus gasseri is named as a separate species."], ["2000s", "Japanese companies develop strains such as OLL2716 and SBT2055."], ["2010", "A trial reports lower abdominal fat with SBT2055 fermented milk."]], "cell": "Mechanisms are strain specific and not established. For SBT2055, studies suggest it may bind bile acids and lower fat absorption, or change lipid metabolism, linked to smaller fat cells in animals. For OLL2716, lactic acid and other secreted factors suppress H. pylori growth and urease activity in the stomach, lowering its density without eradication. For heat-killed CP2305, gut-brain signaling through the vagus nerve is proposed to lower stress responses and improve sleep measures. None of these are receptor drug actions, and each has its own small research base.", "body": [["Abdominal fat", "SBT2055 trials reported small reductions in abdominal fat, which faded after stopping."], ["Stomach", "OLL2716 may lower H. pylori activity and gastric inflammation but does not cure the infection."], ["Brain and sleep", "Heat-killed CP2305 was studied for stress and sleep measures, with small trials."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Lactobacillus bacteremia is rare but reported in severely ill or immunocompromised patients."]], "journey": "Products are swallowed in fermented milks, tablets or capsules. Live strains pass through the gut and are present temporarily; heat-killed forms act as non-living cells. None are absorbed or metabolized like a drug, and they leave in stool. Trials measured outcomes after about three to six months, and effects faded after intake stopped in the SBT2055 trial.", "sidefx": "Mild bloating or gas comes from fermentation. The rare serious risk with live strains is bacteremia in severely ill or immunocompromised patients. Results belong to the specific strain tested. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.", "src": ["Kadooka Y et al. Eur J Clin Nutr 2010", "Lauer E, Kandler O. Zentralbl Bakteriol 1980", "Katzung Basic and Clinical Pharmacology (probiotics overview)"]}, "bio-k-plus-three-strain-blend": {"origin": "Bio-K+ was developed in Quebec, Canada, and launched in the 1990s as a fermented milk containing L. acidophilus CL1285 and L. casei LBC80R. It was studied in Canadian hospitals as a way to prevent antibiotic-associated diarrhea and C. difficile. A 2010 trial by Gao and colleagues in the American Journal of Gastroenterology found a dose-related reduction in both. A third strain, L. rhamnosus CLR2, was later added to the product. It is a licensed natural health product in Canada but is not an FDA approved drug in the US. AGA 2020 guidance named the original two-strain combination.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Bio-K+ fermented milk is launched in Quebec."], ["2010", "A trial reports dose-related reductions in antibiotic-associated diarrhea and C. difficile."], ["2020", "AGA gives a conditional suggestion for the two-strain combination in C. difficile prevention."]], "cell": "Proposed mechanisms come from lab data. The strains produce lactic acid, which lowers pH and inhibits some pathogens. They compete with C. difficile for nutrients and space. In lab studies, products from the strains suppressed expression of C. difficile toxin genes (tcdA and tcdB), which would reduce toxin-driven colitis. They also interact with gut immune cells. There is no single receptor target. Clinical evidence for preventing antibiotic-associated diarrhea in hospital patients is moderate, and the current product adds a strain not used in older trials.", "body": [["Colon during antibiotics", "Fewer cases of antibiotic-associated diarrhea were seen in several hospital trials."], ["C. difficile", "Lab data show suppression of toxin gene expression, proposed to lower infection risk."], ["Gut microbiota", "Lactic acid and competition may limit pathogen overgrowth while antibiotics disrupt normal flora."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Lactobacillus bacteremia is rare but reported in severely ill or immunocompromised patients."]], "journey": "The product is swallowed as a fermented milk, plant-based drink or capsules. The bacteria pass to the intestine and are present temporarily. They are not absorbed or metabolized like a drug and leave in stool. Trials started it within days of beginning antibiotics.", "sidefx": "Mild bloating or gas comes from fermentation. Dairy versions contain milk protein. The rare serious risk is bacteremia in severely ill or immunocompromised patients, a class effect of live probiotics. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.", "src": ["Gao XW et al. Am J Gastroenterol 2010", "AGA 2020 Clinical Practice Guidelines on probiotics", "Bio-K+ product information"]}, "bifidobacterium-bifidum-mimbb75": {"origin": "MIMBb75 was characterized by microbiologist Simone Guglielmetti and colleagues at the University of Milan, who selected it in lab tests for its unusually strong binding to intestinal cells. A 2011 trial in Alimentary Pharmacology and Therapeutics with live MIMBb75 reported less IBS symptoms. The German company Synformulas then developed a heat-inactivated version, sold as Kijimea Irritable Bowel PRO, which is regulated as a medical device in the EU. A large 2020 trial in The Lancet Gastroenterology and Hepatology by Andresen and colleagues found the non-living cells improved IBS symptoms. It is not a common US product and is not an FDA approved drug.", "timeline": [["2010", "University of Milan researchers describe the strain's strong adhesion and the BopA protein."], ["2011", "A trial with live MIMBb75 reports improvement in IBS symptoms."], ["2020", "A large trial finds heat-inactivated MIMBb75 improves IBS symptoms."]], "cell": "The proposed mechanism centers on adhesion rather than colonization. MIMBb75 binds tightly to intestinal epithelial cells, and lab studies identified a surface lipoprotein, BopA, involved in this binding. A layer of bound bacteria or bacterial cell surfaces may physically shield the mucosa from irritants and pathogens. Contact with epithelial and immune cells may dampen mucosal immune activation, including mast cell and cytokine signals, which in turn may reduce visceral hypersensitivity, the heightened gut pain sensation of IBS. Because the 2020 trial used heat-killed cells, the authors proposed that live growth is not essential, which fits a surface-protein mechanism.", "body": [["Intestinal lining", "Strong adhesion may form a protective layer on the mucosa, proposed from lab studies."], ["Gut pain signaling", "Less abdominal pain in trials is proposed to reflect calmer mucosal immune activity and less visceral hypersensitivity."], ["Bowel habits", "Global IBS symptoms, including bloating and irregular stools, improved more than with placebo in trials."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "The live form carries a theoretical bacteremia risk in high-risk patients; the heat-inactivated form contains no live bacteria."]], "journey": "Capsules are swallowed and pass through the stomach to the intestine, where the bacteria or bacterial cells bind the lining for a time. Nothing is absorbed into the blood as a drug. The cells are digested or pass out in stool. Trials judged symptoms over four to eight weeks, and in the 2011 trial the benefit faded within two weeks after stopping.", "sidefx": "Mild gas or bloating can occur from fermentation and shifts in gut contents. Live bacterial probiotics carry a rare bacteremia risk in immunocompromised or critically ill patients and those with central lines; this does not apply to the heat-inactivated product. There is no antidote.", "fun": "This is one of the best known examples of a 'postbiotic', a product of killed bacteria that still showed a clinical effect in a large trial.", "src": ["Guglielmetti S et al. Aliment Pharmacol Ther 2011", "Andresen V et al. Lancet Gastroenterol Hepatol 2020", "Kijimea product information"]}, "bifidobacterium-longum-bb536": {"origin": "BB536 was isolated in 1969 from the gut of a healthy infant by researchers at Morinaga Milk Industry in Japan. Morinaga chose it for its ability to survive in dairy products and in the gut, and it was added to Japanese yogurts and fermented milks from the 1970s. It became one of the most studied bifidobacteria in Japan, tested in pollen allergy, bowel habits, ulcerative colitis and infections in older adults. It is now sold as an ingredient in supplements in many countries. It is not an FDA approved drug.", "timeline": [["1969", "Morinaga researchers isolate BB536 from a healthy infant."], ["1970s", "BB536 is added to Japanese dairy products."], ["2000s", "Japanese trials study BB536 in cedar pollen allergy."], ["2000s", "BB536 receives an FDA GRAS notice for food use."]], "cell": "BB536 is a live bifidobacterium with proposed mechanisms. In the colon it ferments carbohydrates through the bifid shunt to acetate and lactate. These acids lower pH, provide fuel and signals to epithelial cells, and support barrier function; acetate from bifidobacteria protected mice from a lethal E. coli O157 infection in one well known study. It also interacts with mucosal immune cells. In Japanese pollen allergy trials it shifted cytokines away from a Th2 allergy pattern, and in an elderly trial NK cell activity changed. Effects are inconsistent across trials, and it is not a receptor drug.", "body": [["Colon", "Acetate and lactate lower pH and support the epithelial barrier."], ["Bowel habits", "Small trials report changes in stool frequency, with inconsistent results."], ["Immune system and allergy", "Pollen allergy trials reported some relief of eye symptoms and a shift away from Th2 cytokines."], ["Colonic inflammation", "Small ulcerative colitis trials reported partial improvement in scores."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Live bifidobacteria carry a theoretical bacteremia risk in high-risk patients."]], "journey": "BB536 is swallowed in yogurts, fermented milks or capsules. Many cells survive to the colon. It is not absorbed or metabolized like a drug and leaves in stool. It does not permanently colonize most adults, so its presence is transient. Trials ran from two to fourteen weeks.", "sidefx": "Mild gas or bloating comes from fermentation. Live bacterial probiotics carry a rare bacteremia risk in immunocompromised or critically ill patients and those with central lines, and FDA warned in 2023 about probiotics in preterm infants. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.", "src": ["Morinaga BB536 product information", "Fukuda S et al. Nature 2011", "Xiao JZ et al. Clin Exp Allergy 2006"]}, "lactobacillus-johnsonii-la1": {"origin": "La1 was selected by scientists at the Nestle Research Center near Lausanne, Switzerland, from Nestle's culture collection. It was first called Lactobacillus acidophilus La1 and later identified as L. johnsonii, a species named in 1992 after microbiologist Jeffrey Johnson. Nestle launched it in LC1 fermented milks in Europe in the 1990s. Trials studied it mainly against Helicobacter pylori, where it lowered bacterial density and gastritis activity but rarely cured the infection, and in Crohn disease after surgery, where it did not prevent relapse. Its genome was published in 2004. It is not an FDA approved drug.", "timeline": [["1990s", "Nestle launches LC1 fermented milks with La1."], ["1992", "Lactobacillus johnsonii is named as a species."], ["2004", "The La1 (NCC 533) genome is published."]], "cell": "Proposed mechanisms come mainly from lab studies. La1 produces lactic acid and other secreted factors that inhibit H. pylori growth and its ability to attach to gastric epithelial cells. In the stomach this may lower H. pylori density and reduce gastritis activity. La1 also adheres to intestinal epithelial cells and interacts with mucosal immune cells, which may shift cytokine signals. It is not a receptor drug, and in trials it suppressed H. pylori rather than eradicating it.", "body": [["Stomach", "Lower H. pylori density and gastritis activity were seen in trials, without true eradication in most people."], ["Small intestine", "Adhesion to epithelial cells may support the barrier while the strain is present."], ["Gut immune system", "Lab studies show effects on mucosal cytokine signaling."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Live lactobacilli carry a theoretical bacteremia risk in high-risk patients."]], "journey": "La1 is swallowed in fermented milks. It passes through the stomach, where it can act on H. pylori, and into the intestine. It is not absorbed or metabolized like a drug and leaves in stool. H. pylori trials ran about three weeks, and in a child trial H. pylori usually returned to prior levels after stopping.", "sidefx": "Mild gas or bloating comes from fermentation. Fermented milks contain milk protein and lactose. Live bacterial probiotics carry a rare bacteremia risk in immunocompromised or critically ill patients and those with central lines. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.", "src": ["Pridmore RD et al. PNAS 2004", "Felley CP et al. Eur J Gastroenterol Hepatol 2001", "Marteau P et al. Gut 2006"]}, "yogurt-cultures": {"origin": "Yogurt has been made for thousands of years in the Middle East, Central Asia and the Balkans, where warm milk soured into a thick, tart food. In 1905 Bulgarian medical student Stamen Grigorov, working in Geneva, identified the rod-shaped bacterium now called Lactobacillus delbrueckii subsp. bulgaricus as the key yogurt microbe. Soon after, Elie Metchnikoff of the Pasteur Institute popularized the idea that Bulgarian peasants lived long lives because they ate soured milk, in his 1907 book The Prolongation of Life. In 1919 Isaac Carasso began selling yogurt in Barcelona, the start of Danone. US standards of identity require yogurt to be made with both L. bulgaricus and Streptococcus thermophilus. Yogurt is a food, not an FDA approved drug.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Yogurt making develops in the Middle East, Central Asia and the Balkans."], ["1905", "Stamen Grigorov identifies the yogurt bacterium later named L. bulgaricus."], ["1907", "Elie Metchnikoff links soured milk to long life in The Prolongation of Life."], ["1919", "Isaac Carasso starts selling yogurt in Barcelona, founding Danone."]], "cell": "The two cultures work as partners. S. thermophilus grows first and makes formic acid and carbon dioxide that stimulate L. bulgaricus, which in turn breaks milk proteins into peptides and amino acids that feed S. thermophilus. Together they ferment lactose to lactic acid, which drops pH and makes casein proteins gel into yogurt. Medically, the best supported effect is on lactose digestion. The bacteria contain beta-galactosidase (lactase). In the small intestine, bile breaks open the bacterial cells and releases this enzyme, which splits lactose into glucose and galactose in people who make too little of their own lactase. Fermentation also lowers the lactose content, and the semisolid texture slows stomach emptying, giving more time for digestion.", "body": [["Small intestine", "Bacterial lactase released by bile digests lactose, so less undigested sugar reaches the colon."], ["Colon", "Less undigested lactose means less fermentation by colon bacteria, so less gas, bloating and diarrhea in lactose maldigesters."], ["Stomach", "The thick texture slows gastric emptying, giving lactase more time to work."], ["Immune system", "People with milk allergy react to milk proteins in yogurt, which can cause hives or anaphylaxis."]], "journey": "Yogurt is eaten as food. Many cultures die in stomach acid, and survivors are broken open by bile in the small intestine, releasing their lactase. They do not colonize the gut. Nothing is absorbed as a drug; the bacteria are digested like other food proteins, and any remaining cells leave in stool within hours to a day. Yogurts that are heat-treated after culturing contain few live bacteria.", "sidefx": "Most people have no side effects or mild gas. Milk allergy reactions come from milk proteins, not the bacteria, and can include anaphylaxis. Yogurt is not lactose free, so people with severe lactose intolerance may still have symptoms. Infections from these food cultures are very rare. There is no antidote; allergic reactions are treated in the usual way, with epinephrine for anaphylaxis.", "fun": "The 'self-digesting' trick of yogurt, where bacteria carry their own lactase, is why many lactose-intolerant people tolerate yogurt better than milk.", "src": ["FDA standard of identity for yogurt (21 CFR 131.200)", "EFSA NDA Panel opinion on live yogurt cultures and lactose digestion 2010", "Savaiano DA. Am J Clin Nutr 2014"]}, "kefir-cultures": {"origin": "Kefir comes from the Caucasus Mountains, where people fermented milk in skin bags or containers using kefir grains, small cauliflower-like clumps of bacteria and yeasts held together by a sugar matrix. The grains grow and can be passed on, so the culture has been kept alive for generations, and its exact origin is unknown. Kefir spread through Russia in the early 1900s and later worldwide. Scientists have since found that the grains contain Lactobacillus kefiranofaciens, Lentilactobacillus kefiri, Lactococcus, Leuconostoc, acetic acid bacteria and yeasts. Store-bought kefir is often made with defined starter cultures instead of grains. Kefir is a food, not an FDA approved drug.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Kefir is made in the Caucasus Mountains with kefir grains."], ["1900s", "Kefir production spreads across Russia."], ["20th century", "Scientists identify the bacteria, yeasts and the kefiran polysaccharide in kefir grains."]], "cell": "Kefir grains ferment lactose with several microbes at once. Lactic acid bacteria make lactic acid, yeasts make carbon dioxide and a little ethanol, and acetic acid bacteria add acetic acid. L. kefiranofaciens produces kefiran, a branched glucose and galactose polysaccharide that forms much of the grain. Medically, kefir aids lactose digestion like yogurt: bacterial lactase and lower lactose content mean less undigested sugar reaches the colon. Lactic acid, bacteriocins and kefiran may inhibit some pathogens and influence gut immunity in lab and animal studies. Human evidence beyond lactose digestion is limited.", "body": [["Small intestine", "Bacterial lactase and lower lactose content help digestion, with less gas after a lactose load in a small trial."], ["Gut microbiota", "Live bacteria and yeasts pass through the gut for a short time and may shift the community."], ["Gut immune system", "Kefiran and bacterial products are proposed to modulate immunity, mostly from animal data."], ["Immune system (allergy)", "Milk proteins in kefir can cause allergic reactions in people with milk allergy."]], "journey": "Kefir is drunk as food. Some bacteria and yeasts survive the stomach and pass through the gut for a short time. Nothing is absorbed as a drug. The microbes are digested or leave in stool within hours to days. Composition differs widely between grain and commercial kefirs and from batch to batch.", "sidefx": "Bloating and gas come from carbon dioxide and fermentation. Milk allergy reactions come from milk proteins. Kefir contains a small amount of alcohol from yeast fermentation. Live bacteria and yeasts carry a rare infection risk in severely immunocompromised patients, and antifungal drugs can kill the yeast part. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Kefir grains are not cereal grains at all; they are living clumps of microbes that keep growing and can be shared.", "src": ["Rosa DD et al. Nutr Res Rev 2017 (kefir review)", "Hertzler SR, Clancy SM. J Am Diet Assoc 2003", "Katzung Basic and Clinical Pharmacology (probiotics overview)"]}, "garden-of-life-critical-care": {"origin": "Garden of Life is a US supplement company founded in Florida in 2000, and it has been owned by Nestle Health Science since 2017. Its Dr. Formulated Probiotics line was launched in the 2010s with neurologist David Perlmutter. Critical Care is a high-count capsule with 15 Lactobacillus and Bifidobacterium strains plus prebiotic fiber from potato resistant starch and acacia. The name Critical Care is a brand name, not a hospital or ICU product. This exact blend has no published clinical trials. It is a dietary supplement, not an FDA approved drug.", "timeline": [["2000", "Garden of Life is founded in Florida."], ["2017", "Nestle Health Science acquires Garden of Life."], ["2010s", "The Dr. Formulated Probiotics line, including Critical Care, is launched."]], "cell": "No mechanism is established for this blend, and effects of probiotics are strain specific. In general, Lactobacillus and Bifidobacterium strains are proposed to ferment sugars to lactic acid and acetate, lower gut pH, compete with harmful microbes for nutrients and binding sites, and interact with immune cells through pattern recognition receptors such as TLR2. The prebiotic fibers (resistant starch and acacia) are not digested in the small intestine and are fermented in the colon by resident bacteria into short-chain fatty acids like butyrate, which fuel colon cells. Whether these strains together produce any clinical benefit has not been tested.", "body": [["Gut microbiota", "Many strains add to the gut community for a short time while taken."], ["Colon", "Prebiotic fibers are fermented to short-chain fatty acids, which feed colon cells and may cause gas."], ["Gut immune system", "Strains may interact with immune cells, but effects of this blend are unknown."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Lactobacillus and Bifidobacterium bacteremia is rare but reported in immunocompromised, critically ill or preterm patients."]], "journey": "The capsules are swallowed. Bacteria that survive stomach acid and bile pass to the intestine and are present temporarily, showing up in stool within days. They are not absorbed or metabolized like a drug. The fibers reach the colon and are fermented there. The strains are cleared from stool within days to weeks after stopping.", "sidefx": "Gas and bloating are common early, from both bacterial fermentation and the prebiotic fiber. The rare serious risk is bacteremia when live bacteria cross a weak gut barrier, mainly in immunocompromised, critically ill or preterm patients. Despite the product name, probiotics in critically ill patients are linked to rare bloodstream infections. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.", "src": ["Garden of Life product information", "AGA 2020 Clinical Practice Guidelines on probiotics", "FDA 2023 safety communication on probiotics in preterm infants"]}, "magnesium-glycinate": {"origin": "Magnesium was recognized as an element by Scottish chemist Joseph Black in 1755 and first isolated by Humphry Davy in 1808. Its salts have long been used in medicine, starting with Epsom salt (magnesium sulfate) from a spring in Epsom, England, in the 1600s. Magnesium glycinate, also called bisglycinate, binds one magnesium ion to two molecules of the amino acid glycine. Amino acid chelates like this were developed for supplements in the late 20th century, notably by Albion Laboratories in Utah, with the goal of better absorption and less diarrhea than magnesium oxide. It is sold only as a dietary supplement, with no FDA approved drug product.", "timeline": [["1618", "Epsom salt is discovered at a spring in Epsom, England, according to tradition."], ["1755", "Joseph Black recognizes magnesium as an element."], ["1808", "Humphry Davy isolates magnesium metal."], ["20th century", "Amino acid chelates such as magnesium bisglycinate are developed for supplements."]], "cell": "Magnesium is a cofactor for more than 300 enzymes. It binds ATP, and the Mg-ATP complex is the real substrate for kinases, the Na-K ATPase and DNA and protein synthesis. It acts as a physiologic calcium channel blocker, competing with calcium at voltage-gated channels and on contractile proteins, which relaxes smooth muscle. It blocks the NMDA receptor channel at resting membrane potential, limiting calcium entry and neuronal excitation. In the heart it stabilizes membranes and supports potassium handling. The glycinate chelate is proposed to be absorbed partly intact through dipeptide transporters, according to manufacturer and small studies, leaving less unabsorbed magnesium in the bowel to draw in water.", "body": [["Nerves and brain", "NMDA channel block and calcium competition reduce neuronal excitability, the basis for claims about sleep and stress."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Magnesium balances calcium in muscle contraction, the basis for claims about cramps."], ["Heart", "It supports normal rhythm by stabilizing membranes and potassium handling; very high levels slow conduction."], ["Gut", "Less unabsorbed magnesium means less osmotic laxative effect than magnesium oxide or citrate, according to limited studies."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys control magnesium balance, so kidney impairment can lead to dangerous buildup."], ["Bones", "About half of body magnesium is stored in bone, which acts as a reservoir."]], "journey": "Taken by mouth, magnesium is absorbed in the small intestine over several hours by passive paracellular movement and active TRPM6 and TRPM7 channels. The chelated form may also use dipeptide pathways. Only part of a dose is absorbed. In the blood about 30 percent is bound to protein. It is stored in bone and inside cells. It is not metabolized; the kidneys filter it and adjust excretion to keep levels normal. Unabsorbed magnesium leaves in stool, and glycine joins the body's amino acid pool.", "sidefx": "Diarrhea, nausea and cramping happen when unabsorbed magnesium pulls water into the bowel by osmosis, reported less often with glycinate than with oxide or citrate. In kidney impairment magnesium can build up. High magnesium blocks calcium channels and acetylcholine release at nerve-muscle junctions, causing loss of reflexes, weakness, low blood pressure, respiratory depression and heart block. IV calcium gluconate or chloride counteracts the effects of severe hypermagnesemia, and dialysis can remove it.", "fun": "Magnesium sits at the center of every chlorophyll molecule, so green vegetables are a major dietary source.", "src": ["NIH Office of Dietary Supplements Magnesium Fact Sheet", "Goodman and Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology"]}, "cocaine": {"origin": "Cocaine comes from the leaves of the coca shrub, Erythroxylum coca, which people in the Andes of South America have chewed for thousands of years to fight fatigue, hunger and altitude sickness. In 1860 Albert Niemann, a graduate student in Gottingen, Germany, isolated the pure alkaloid and named it cocaine. In 1884 Viennese eye doctor Carl Koller showed that a few drops numbed the eye for surgery, making cocaine the first local anesthetic; the same year Sigmund Freud published his essay Uber Coca. Cocaine was soon in tonics and drinks, and its addictive harms led to the US Harrison Narcotics Act of 1914. Today it is Schedule II. FDA approved cocaine hydrochloride nasal solutions, Goprelto in 2017 and Numbrino in 2020, for numbing the nose during procedures. Street use is illegal.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Andean peoples chew coca leaves."], ["1860", "Albert Niemann isolates cocaine from coca leaves."], ["1884", "Carl Koller introduces cocaine as a local anesthetic for eye surgery."], ["1914", "The Harrison Narcotics Act restricts cocaine in the US."], ["1970", "The Controlled Substances Act places cocaine in Schedule II."], ["2017", "FDA approves Goprelto cocaine nasal solution for nasal anesthesia."]], "cell": "Cocaine has two main actions. First, it binds and blocks the presynaptic reuptake transporters for norepinephrine (NET), dopamine (DAT) and serotonin (SERT). These monoamines stay longer in the synapse. Excess norepinephrine at alpha-1 and beta-1 receptors causes vasoconstriction, high blood pressure, fast heart rate and dilated pupils. Excess dopamine in the nucleus accumbens drives euphoria and the strong reinforcement that underlies addiction. Second, cocaine blocks voltage-gated sodium channels in nerve and heart cells, preventing action potentials. In nerves this produces local anesthesia. In the heart it slows phase 0 depolarization, widening the QRS like a class I antiarrhythmic. The nasal solution uses both effects: numbing plus vasoconstriction that shrinks the mucosa and reduces bleeding.", "body": [["Brain", "Dopamine buildup in reward circuits causes euphoria and craving; high levels cause agitation, psychosis, hyperthermia and seizures."], ["Heart", "Catecholamine excess raises heart rate and oxygen demand while sodium channel block widens the QRS and promotes dangerous rhythms."], ["Coronary arteries", "Alpha-1 driven vasospasm and faster clotting can cause heart attack even in young people."], ["Blood vessels", "Sudden spikes in blood pressure can tear the aorta or cause stroke and brain bleeding."], ["Nose", "Repeated vasoconstriction starves the nasal septum of blood, which can lead to ulcers and perforation."], ["Muscles and temperature", "Agitation and heat production cause hyperthermia and rhabdomyolysis, which can injure the kidneys."]], "journey": "Cocaine is absorbed through mucous membranes, lungs or veins. Smoked or injected, it reaches the brain within seconds and peaks in minutes; through the nose it takes several minutes and lasts longer because vasoconstriction slows its own absorption. It is an ester, so plasma cholinesterase and liver esterases quickly break it into benzoylecgonine and ecgonine methyl ester. Its half-life is about an hour. Urine tests detect benzoylecgonine for about two to four days. With alcohol, the liver makes cocaethylene, which lasts longer and is more toxic to the heart.", "sidefx": "Nearly all serious harm comes from too much catecholamine and too much sodium channel block. Vasospasm and high blood pressure cause heart attack, aortic dissection and stroke. Sodium channel block causes wide QRS rhythms. Brain stimulation causes seizures, psychosis and hyperthermia, which leads to muscle breakdown. Street cocaine is often contaminated with fentanyl and levamisole. There is no specific antidote; benzodiazepines are commonly used for agitation, and sodium bicarbonate is used for sodium channel toxicity with wide QRS.", "fun": "Coca-Cola contained coca leaf extract when it was invented in 1886; the cocaine was removed in the early 1900s.", "src": ["Goprelto (cocaine hydrochloride) nasal solution FDA label", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Katzung Basic and Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "methamphetamine": {"origin": "Methamphetamine was first made in 1893 by Japanese chemist Nagai Nagayoshi from ephedrine, a stimulant he had earlier isolated from the ephedra plant. In 1919 Akira Ogata found a simpler route that produced the crystal form. In 1938 the German company Temmler sold it as Pervitin, and it was widely given to soldiers in World War II to fight fatigue. After the war, misuse spread in Japan and elsewhere. In the US, Abbott's Desoxyn tablets were approved in 1943, and today the tablets are approved for ADHD in children 6 and older. Methamphetamine is Schedule II. Illicit crystal methamphetamine is made in clandestine labs, and its use is illegal and causes addiction, psychosis and organ damage.", "timeline": [["1893", "Nagai Nagayoshi synthesizes methamphetamine from ephedrine."], ["1919", "Akira Ogata produces crystalline methamphetamine."], ["1938", "Temmler markets methamphetamine as Pervitin in Germany."], ["1943", "FDA approves Desoxyn."], ["1971", "Methamphetamine is placed in Schedule II."]], "cell": "Methamphetamine looks enough like dopamine to be carried into nerve terminals by DAT and NET, and it also diffuses in. Inside, it acts on VMAT2, the vesicular transporter, causing monoamines to leak from storage vesicles into the cytoplasm. It also activates TAAR1, an intracellular receptor, which triggers kinase signaling that phosphorylates DAT and makes it run in reverse. The result is a flood of dopamine, norepinephrine and, to a lesser degree, serotonin pushed out into the synapse, independent of nerve firing. It weakly inhibits MAO, which further raises monoamine levels. Dopamine in reward pathways produces intense euphoria and addiction. Norepinephrine drives tachycardia, hypertension, vasoconstriction and hyperthermia. At therapeutic levels in ADHD, the same mechanism improves attention.", "body": [["Brain", "A dopamine surge causes euphoria, alertness and addiction; heavy use causes paranoia, psychosis and long-lasting changes in dopamine terminals."], ["Heart", "Sustained catecholamine stimulation raises heart rate and blood pressure and can cause heart attack and cardiomyopathy."], ["Blood vessels", "Severe hypertension and vasoconstriction can cause stroke, brain bleeding and aortic dissection."], ["Temperature and muscles", "Hyperactivity and heat generation cause hyperthermia and rhabdomyolysis, with risk of kidney failure."], ["Mouth and teeth", "Dry mouth, grinding and poor hygiene cause severe dental decay, often called meth mouth."], ["Skin", "Sensations of bugs under the skin lead to picking and sores."]], "journey": "Methamphetamine is well absorbed by any route. Smoked or injected, it reaches the brain within seconds; by mouth it takes 15 to 30 minutes. It is lipid soluble and crosses into the brain easily. The liver metabolizes it by CYP2D6 to amphetamine and p-hydroxymethamphetamine, but much leaves unchanged in urine, more when urine is acidic. Its half-life is about 10 to 12 hours, so effects last much longer than cocaine. Urine tests detect it for about two to four days.", "sidefx": "The main toxicity is an overdose of monoamine release. Norepinephrine causes dangerous hypertension, arrhythmias, heart attack and stroke. Dopamine excess causes agitation, psychosis and violent behavior. Heat production from agitation and muscle activity causes hyperthermia, rhabdomyolysis and multiorgan failure. Long use damages dopamine nerve terminals. Illicit supply is often mixed with fentanyl. The tablet label has a boxed warning for abuse and dependence. There is no specific antidote; benzodiazepines and cooling are standard supportive measures.", "fun": "Nagai Nagayoshi is also known for isolating ephedrine from the ephedra plant in 1885, the starting point for both decongestants and methamphetamine.", "src": ["Desoxyn (methamphetamine hydrochloride) FDA label", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Katzung Basic and Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "mdma": {"origin": "MDMA was first made by the German company Merck in 1912 as a chemical intermediate, and Merck patented the process in 1914, but it was never developed as a medicine. In the 1970s chemist Alexander Shulgin resynthesized it and, with David Nichols, described its effects in humans in 1978. Some therapists then used it in psychotherapy. As recreational use as 'ecstasy' grew in the 1980s, the DEA placed it in Schedule I in 1985. Later clinical trials studied MDMA-assisted therapy for PTSD, but in 2024 FDA did not approve the application, issuing a complete response letter. It has no approved medical use.", "timeline": [["1912", "Merck chemists synthesize MDMA as an intermediate."], ["1978", "Shulgin and Nichols describe its effects in humans."], ["1985", "The DEA places MDMA in Schedule I."], ["2024", "FDA declines to approve MDMA-assisted therapy for PTSD."]], "cell": "MDMA is an amphetamine with a methylenedioxy ring. It is taken up by monoamine transporters, with the highest affinity for the serotonin transporter (SERT), then NET and DAT. Inside the neuron it disrupts VMAT2 storage and reverses the transporters, so large amounts of serotonin, plus norepinephrine and dopamine, are pushed into the synapse. The serotonin surge acts on many receptors, including 5-HT2A indirectly, producing mood elevation, emotional closeness and altered perception. MDMA also triggers release of oxytocin and vasopressin (ADH). Vasopressin makes the kidneys hold water, which can drop blood sodium. After the surge, serotonin stores are depleted for days, which explains low mood after use. Norepinephrine release drives tachycardia, hypertension and heat production.", "body": [["Brain", "A serotonin surge causes euphoria and empathy; depletion afterward causes low mood; too much serotonin can cause serotonin syndrome."], ["Kidneys and sodium balance", "Vasopressin release makes the kidneys retain water, and with heavy water drinking, sodium falls and the brain swells."], ["Temperature control", "Muscle activity, serotonin and norepinephrine raise body heat, and crowded hot settings make hyperthermia worse."], ["Heart and vessels", "Catecholamine release raises heart rate and blood pressure."], ["Jaw and muscles", "Jaw clenching and teeth grinding are common; severe hyperthermia can cause rhabdomyolysis."]], "journey": "MDMA is swallowed and absorbed from the gut, with effects starting in 30 to 60 minutes and peaking in about one and a half to three hours. It crosses into the brain easily. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP2D6, and MDMA inhibits CYP2D6 itself, so its metabolism is nonlinear and extra doses raise levels more than expected. A main metabolite is MDA, which is also active. The half-life is about eight to nine hours. It is excreted by the kidneys and detected in urine for about one to three days.", "sidefx": "Hyponatremia comes from vasopressin release plus drinking lots of water, and it can cause brain swelling, seizures and death, especially in women. Hyperthermia comes from serotonin and norepinephrine effects on heat production plus exertion, and it can cause DIC and rhabdomyolysis. Serotonin syndrome occurs with other serotonergic drugs. Tablets sold as ecstasy or molly often contain cathinones, methamphetamine or PMA, which can be more toxic. There is no specific antidote; cooling, benzodiazepines and careful sodium correction are standard supportive measures.", "fun": "Merck's 1912 work on MDMA was aimed at making a drug to stop bleeding, and MDMA itself was just a step along the way.", "src": ["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Shulgin AT, Nichols DE. 1978", "FDA complete response letter announcement for midomafetamine 2024"]}, "heroin": {"origin": "Heroin is made by chemically changing morphine, the main alkaloid of the opium poppy, Papaver somniferum. In 1874 English chemist C. R. Alder Wright at St Mary's Hospital in London first made diacetylmorphine by boiling morphine with acetic anhydride. In 1898 the German company Bayer, through chemists including Felix Hoffmann and pharmacologist Heinrich Dreser, marketed it as Heroin, a cough remedy believed to be safer than morphine. It soon proved highly addictive. The US banned its manufacture in 1924, and it is now Schedule I with no approved US medical use, though diamorphine is still used medically in the UK and some other countries. Most US supply has now been replaced or mixed with fentanyl.", "timeline": [["1874", "C. R. Alder Wright synthesizes diacetylmorphine in London."], ["1898", "Bayer markets diacetylmorphine as Heroin."], ["1924", "The US bans heroin manufacture."], ["1970", "The Controlled Substances Act places heroin in Schedule I."], ["2010s", "Fentanyl increasingly replaces or contaminates heroin in the US."]], "cell": "Heroin itself binds opioid receptors weakly. Its two acetyl groups make it more lipid soluble than morphine, so it crosses the blood-brain barrier quickly. In blood and brain, esterases remove one acetyl group to form 6-monoacetylmorphine (6-MAM), then the second to form morphine. 6-MAM and morphine are full agonists at the mu-opioid receptor, a Gi/o protein-coupled receptor. Activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase, lowers cAMP, opens GIRK potassium channels to hyperpolarize neurons and closes voltage-gated calcium channels to cut neurotransmitter release. In the brainstem this blunts the response to carbon dioxide and slows breathing. In the ventral tegmental area it disinhibits dopamine neurons, producing euphoria and reinforcing use. Kappa and delta receptors are also activated to a lesser degree.", "body": [["Brainstem breathing centers", "Mu receptor activation blunts the response to rising carbon dioxide, causing slow, shallow breathing and apnea in overdose."], ["Brain reward and alertness", "Dopamine release in reward circuits causes euphoria and addiction; sedation progresses to coma at high levels."], ["Eyes", "Activation of the Edinger-Westphal nucleus causes pinpoint pupils."], ["Gut", "Reduced motility and secretion cause constipation and nausea."], ["Lungs", "Overdose can cause noncardiogenic pulmonary edema; vomiting while sedated can lead to aspiration."], ["Skin and veins", "Injection introduces bacteria and viruses, causing abscesses, endocarditis, wound botulism, HIV and hepatitis C."]], "journey": "Injected or smoked, heroin reaches the brain within seconds; snorted, within minutes. Because it is lipid soluble, it enters the brain faster than morphine, which is why the rush is stronger. In the blood it is deacetylated within minutes to 6-MAM, which has a half-life of about 30 minutes, then to morphine, which lasts two to three hours. The liver converts morphine to glucuronides. The kidneys excrete them. 6-MAM in urine is specific for heroin but lasts only hours; morphine is detected for one to three days.", "sidefx": "The deadly effect is respiratory depression from mu receptor activation in the brainstem, leading to hypoxia, brain injury and cardiac arrest. Prolonged immobility while unconscious causes compartment syndrome and rhabdomyolysis. Tolerance drops quickly after abstinence, so overdose risk rises after jail, detox or a hospital stay. Fentanyl contamination greatly increases overdose risk. Naloxone, a mu receptor antagonist, reverses the effects and may need repeating, because its effect can wear off before the opioid's.", "fun": "The name Heroin is often said to come from the German word 'heroisch', meaning heroic, because early testers said it made them feel heroic.", "src": ["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Goodman and Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic and Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "carfentanil": {"origin": "Carfentanil was synthesized in 1974 by a team at Janssen Pharmaceutica in Belgium, the company founded by Paul Janssen, whose chemists had made fentanyl in 1960. They were exploring fentanyl analogs and found carfentanil to be among the most potent opioids ever made, roughly 10,000 times morphine and 100 times fentanyl. It was later marketed for veterinary use as Wildnil to immobilize very large animals such as elephants, and it was never approved for humans. In the 2010s it began appearing in the illicit drug supply, causing waves of overdose deaths, including in Ohio in 2016. It is DEA Schedule II.", "timeline": [["1974", "Janssen Pharmaceutica chemists synthesize carfentanil."], ["1980s", "Carfentanil is used as a veterinary immobilizing agent for large animals."], ["2016", "Carfentanil causes clusters of overdose deaths in the US, including Ohio."]], "cell": "Carfentanil is a full agonist at the mu-opioid receptor with extremely high binding affinity and lipid solubility. It enters the brain rapidly and activates mu receptors at tiny concentrations. Like other mu agonists, it couples to Gi/o proteins, lowers cAMP, opens GIRK potassium channels and closes voltage-gated calcium channels, quieting neurons. In the brainstem pre-Botzinger complex and related centers, this stops the breathing rhythm and blunts the carbon dioxide response. In the cortex and arousal centers it causes coma. High potency means a microgram-level amount can activate enough receptors to cause apnea. Rapid high-level mu stimulation can also cause chest wall rigidity, which makes ventilation harder.", "body": [["Brainstem breathing centers", "Massive mu receptor activation causes rapid apnea, often within minutes."], ["Brain and arousal", "Deep sedation progresses quickly to coma."], ["Chest wall", "High-potency opioids can cause muscle rigidity of the chest wall, making breathing and ventilation difficult."], ["Eyes", "Mu activation in the Edinger-Westphal nucleus causes pinpoint pupils."], ["Brain (after hypoxia)", "Prolonged lack of oxygen during apnea causes hypoxic brain injury."]], "journey": "Carfentanil is very lipid soluble and is absorbed quickly through lungs, mucous membranes or veins, reaching the brain within seconds to minutes. It is metabolized in the liver mainly by CYP3A4. Its reported half-life is longer than fentanyl, about seven to eight hours, and its effects can outlast a dose of naloxone. It is excreted in urine and feces. Many standard urine drug screens and fentanyl immunoassays do not detect it.", "sidefx": "The main toxicity is respiratory arrest from overwhelming mu receptor activation, leading to hypoxic brain injury and cardiac arrest. Because its receptor binding is so strong, reversal often requires repeated naloxone doses, and its long duration means breathing can stop again after naloxone wears off. According to ACMT and AACT, incidental skin contact is unlikely to cause toxicity for responders. Naloxone is the antidote, alongside ventilation support.", "fun": "Analysis by UK scientists reported that the aerosol used by Russian forces to end the 2002 Moscow theater siege contained carfentanil and remifentanil.", "src": ["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "ACMT and AACT position statement on fentanyl and analog exposure for emergency responders", "DEA carfentanil warning 2016"]}, "nitazenes": {"origin": "Nitazenes are 2-benzylbenzimidazole opioids first made in the late 1950s by chemists at the Swiss company CIBA, who were searching for new pain relievers. Compounds such as etonitazene and isotonitazene proved extremely potent, but they were never approved for medical use, partly because of severe respiratory depression. Etonitazene was placed in Schedule I in the US in the 1970s. Starting around 2019, isotonitazene appeared in the illicit drug supply in North America and Europe, followed by metonitazene, protonitazene and other analogs. They are chemically unrelated to fentanyl. Several are now Schedule I, with others under temporary scheduling.", "timeline": [["1950s", "CIBA chemists synthesize the benzimidazole opioids now called nitazenes."], ["1970s", "Etonitazene is placed in Schedule I in the US."], ["2019", "Isotonitazene appears in the illicit drug supply."], ["2020", "DEA temporarily schedules isotonitazene."]], "cell": "Nitazenes are potent full agonists at the mu-opioid receptor. Although chemically unrelated to fentanyl or morphine, they fit the mu receptor binding pocket and activate it strongly. Activation couples to Gi/o proteins, lowering cAMP, opening GIRK potassium channels and closing voltage-gated calcium channels. In brainstem respiratory centers this blunts the carbon dioxide response and slows breathing; in arousal centers it causes coma. Some analogs, such as etonitazene and N-pyrrolidino etonitazene, are many times more potent than fentanyl in lab tests. Because they are opioid receptor agonists, naloxone can reverse them, though repeated doses may be needed.", "body": [["Brainstem breathing centers", "Strong mu activation slows and stops breathing, the main cause of death."], ["Brain and arousal", "Mu activation in arousal centers causes sedation that progresses to coma."], ["Eyes", "Mu activation in the Edinger-Westphal nucleus causes pinpoint pupils."], ["Lungs", "Vomiting while sedated can lead to aspiration."]], "journey": "Nitazenes act within minutes when smoked, injected or swallowed, often hidden in fake pills or mixed with other opioids. They are lipid soluble and enter the brain quickly. They are metabolized in the liver, but human pharmacokinetics are not well studied, and duration varies and may be prolonged. They are not detected by fentanyl test strips or standard opioid urine screens and require specialized mass spectrometry testing.", "sidefx": "The main danger is respiratory depression and arrest from mu receptor activation, causing hypoxic brain injury and death. Because people often do not know they have taken a nitazene, and because some analogs are extremely potent, overdoses can be sudden and severe. Naloxone reverses the effect but may need repeated doses and prolonged observation.", "src": ["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "DEA temporary scheduling order for isotonitazene 2020", "Ujvary I et al. ACS Chem Neurosci 2021 (nitazene review)"]}, "xylazine": {"origin": "Xylazine was synthesized in 1962 by the German company Bayer as a possible blood pressure medicine for humans. Early human testing showed too much sedation and low blood pressure, so it was developed for animals instead. It was approved for veterinary use as Rompun and is widely used to sedate horses, cattle and other animals. It is not approved for humans. In the early 2000s it appeared in the illicit drug supply in Puerto Rico, and in the 2010s it spread in Philadelphia and other US cities, mixed into fentanyl as 'tranq'. In 2023 the White House Office of National Drug Control Policy declared fentanyl adulterated with xylazine an emerging threat.", "timeline": [["1962", "Bayer synthesizes xylazine as a possible antihypertensive."], ["1970s", "Xylazine comes into use as a veterinary sedative."], ["2000s", "Xylazine appears in illicit drug use in Puerto Rico."], ["2023", "ONDCP declares fentanyl adulterated with xylazine an emerging threat."]], "cell": "Xylazine is an agonist at alpha-2 adrenergic receptors, similar to clonidine and dexmedetomidine. Alpha-2 receptors are Gi-coupled. On presynaptic noradrenergic neurons, especially in the locus coeruleus of the brainstem, activation lowers cAMP and reduces norepinephrine release. Less norepinephrine means sedation, reduced sympathetic outflow, slow heart rate and low blood pressure. Alpha-2 activation on pancreatic beta cells suppresses insulin release, causing high blood sugar. Xylazine is not an opioid, so naloxone does not reverse its sedation. When combined with fentanyl, the sedative and breathing-suppressing effects add together.", "body": [["Brain", "Reduced norepinephrine release from the locus coeruleus causes deep, prolonged sedation."], ["Heart and blood pressure", "Lower sympathetic tone causes slow heart rate and low blood pressure."], ["Breathing", "Xylazine alone mildly suppresses breathing, but with opioids the combined depression is dangerous."], ["Pancreas", "Alpha-2 activation on beta cells suppresses insulin and raises blood sugar."], ["Skin", "Repeated exposure causes severe necrotic wounds that can appear away from injection sites, possibly from local vasoconstriction and poor tissue blood flow."]], "journey": "Xylazine is usually injected, smoked or snorted with fentanyl. It acts within minutes and causes sedation lasting hours. It is metabolized by the liver, and human pharmacokinetics are not well defined. It is excreted by the kidneys. Standard drug screens do not detect it, though specific tests and test strips exist.", "sidefx": "Sedation, slow heart rate and low blood pressure all come from reduced norepinephrine signaling. The combination with fentanyl causes deeper, longer sedation and greater risk of respiratory arrest. Chronic use causes necrotic skin ulcers, abscesses and bone infections, thought to involve vasoconstriction and impaired wound healing. Withdrawal can cause anxiety, high blood pressure and restlessness from rebound sympathetic activity. There is no approved antidote in humans. Naloxone does not reverse xylazine but is still given because fentanyl is almost always present.", "fun": "Veterinarians reverse xylazine in animals with alpha-2 antagonists such as yohimbine or atipamezole, which are not approved for this use in humans.", "src": ["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "ONDCP emerging threat designation 2023", "FDA alert on xylazine in illicit drugs 2022"]}, "lsd": {"origin": "LSD was first made in 1938 by Swiss chemist Albert Hofmann at the Sandoz laboratories in Basel. He was making derivatives of lysergic acid, a compound from ergot, a fungus (Claviceps purpurea) that infects rye, hoping to find a circulation or breathing stimulant. LSD-25 seemed uninteresting and was set aside. In April 1943 Hofmann made it again, accidentally absorbed a tiny amount and felt strange effects. On April 19, 1943, he deliberately took a small amount and experienced intense hallucinations while riding his bicycle home, a day now called Bicycle Day. Sandoz distributed LSD as Delysid for research in psychiatry from 1947. Recreational use grew in the 1960s, and it became Schedule I in 1970. It has no approved medical use, though it is studied in clinical trials.", "timeline": [["1938", "Albert Hofmann synthesizes LSD at Sandoz."], ["1943", "Hofmann discovers its effects, including on Bicycle Day, April 19."], ["1947", "Sandoz begins supplying LSD as Delysid for research."], ["1970", "LSD is placed in Schedule I."]], "cell": "LSD is an ergoline that binds many receptors, but its hallucinogenic effect comes mainly from partial agonism at the serotonin 5-HT2A receptor. 5-HT2A is a Gq-coupled receptor; activation turns on phospholipase C, producing IP3 and DAG, raising calcium and increasing glutamate release in layer V pyramidal neurons of the cortex. This disrupts normal filtering of sensory input through cortical and thalamic circuits, producing visual distortions, synesthesia and altered sense of self. Crystal structures of LSD bound to the closely related 5-HT2B receptor show it becomes trapped under a 'lid' of protein, which helps explain its long duration and potency at microgram amounts. It also acts at 5-HT1A and dopamine D2 receptors and causes mild sympathetic stimulation.", "body": [["Brain (perception)", "5-HT2A activation in the cortex alters perception, causing visual changes, synesthesia and time distortion."], ["Brain (mood and thinking)", "Emotional intensity can swing to panic or paranoia, and it may unmask psychosis in vulnerable people."], ["Eyes", "Sympathetic stimulation causes dilated pupils."], ["Heart and vessels", "Mild increases in heart rate and blood pressure occur."], ["Body temperature", "Mild temperature rise is typical; severe hyperthermia is rare with LSD itself."]], "journey": "LSD is usually taken by mouth on blotter paper or as a liquid and is absorbed from the gut. Effects begin within 30 to 90 minutes, peak in two to four hours and last 8 to 12 hours. It is metabolized in the liver to inactive products such as 2-oxo-3-hydroxy-LSD. The half-life is about three to four hours, but effects last longer than that because of its tight receptor binding. It is excreted by the kidneys and is not detected on standard drug screens.", "sidefx": "Physical toxicity is low at common exposure. The main risks are behavioral: panic, paranoia and risky actions that cause injury. LSD can unmask psychosis in people with a predisposition. Some people have persistent perception changes (HPPD). Massive ingestions have caused hyperthermia, coma and bleeding problems. Blotter sold as LSD may contain NBOMe compounds, which are much more dangerous and can cause seizures and death. There is no antidote; a calm environment and benzodiazepines for severe agitation are standard supportive measures.", "fun": "LSD is active in such tiny amounts that a typical blotter dose weighs far less than a grain of salt.", "src": ["Hofmann A. LSD: My Problem Child 1980", "Wacker D et al. Cell 2017 (LSD-bound 5-HT2B structure)", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]}, "psilocybin": {"origin": "Psilocybin is the active compound in Psilocybe and related mushrooms, used for centuries in religious ceremonies by Indigenous peoples of Mexico, such as the Mazatec. In 1955 American banker and amateur mycologist R. Gordon Wasson took part in a ceremony led by Mazatec healer Maria Sabina, and his 1957 Life magazine article brought the mushrooms to wide attention. In 1958 Albert Hofmann at Sandoz, the discoverer of LSD, isolated and identified psilocybin and psilocin from mushrooms grown from Wasson's samples. It became Schedule I in 1970. FDA gave breakthrough therapy designations in 2018 and 2019 for depression trials. It is not FDA approved. Oregon and Colorado now have state-regulated psilocybin programs.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Indigenous peoples in Mexico use psilocybin mushrooms in ceremonies."], ["1957", "R. Gordon Wasson's Life magazine article describes Mazatec mushroom ceremonies."], ["1958", "Albert Hofmann isolates psilocybin and psilocin."], ["1970", "Psilocybin is placed in Schedule I."], ["2018", "FDA grants breakthrough therapy designation for psilocybin in treatment-resistant depression."]], "cell": "Psilocybin is a prodrug. Alkaline phosphatase in the gut and liver removes its phosphate group to form psilocin, which is lipid soluble and crosses the blood-brain barrier. Psilocin is a structural cousin of serotonin and acts as an agonist at the 5-HT2A receptor, a Gq-coupled receptor on cortical layer V pyramidal neurons. Activation raises intracellular calcium and glutamate release, disrupting cortical and thalamic filtering of sensory information. Brain imaging shows reduced activity and connectivity in the default mode network, linked to changes in sense of self. Psilocin also binds 5-HT1A and 5-HT2C receptors. Proposed antidepressant effects may involve increased neuroplasticity, but this is under study.", "body": [["Brain (perception and self)", "5-HT2A activation alters perception, emotions and sense of self for several hours."], ["Brain (mood)", "Studied for depression, with proposed increases in neuroplasticity; panic and anxiety can occur during effects."], ["Gut", "Nausea is common early, possibly from serotonin receptors in the gut and brainstem."], ["Eyes", "Pupils dilate from sympathetic stimulation."], ["Heart and vessels", "Mild increases in heart rate and blood pressure occur."]], "journey": "Psilocybin is swallowed in dried mushrooms or edibles. It is absorbed from the gut and quickly converted to psilocin. Effects start within 20 to 40 minutes, peak in one to two hours and last four to six hours. Psilocin is mainly glucuronidated in the liver, with a half-life of about two to three hours. It is excreted by the kidneys and is not detected on standard drug screens.", "sidefx": "Physical toxicity is low. The main acute risks are panic, paranoia and dangerous behavior during altered perception. Wild-picked mushrooms may be misidentified toxic species, such as amatoxin mushrooms, which cause delayed liver failure. Some edible products sold as psilocybin have contained other substances; FDA warned in 2024 about Diamond Shruumz products. There is no antidote; a calm environment and benzodiazepines for severe agitation are standard supportive measures.", "fun": "Psilocybin mushrooms often bruise blue when handled, because psilocin breaks down into blue-colored compounds when exposed to air.", "src": ["Hofmann A et al. Experientia 1958", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "FDA warning on Diamond Shruumz products 2024"]}, "pcp": {"origin": "Phencyclidine was synthesized in 1956 by chemist Victor Maddox at Parke-Davis in Detroit while searching for new pain relievers. Pharmacologists found it produced anesthesia without slowing breathing much, and it was tested in humans as Sernyl in the late 1950s. Many patients woke up with severe agitation, hallucinations and delirium, so human use was dropped in the 1960s, though it was used in veterinary medicine for a time as Sernylan. The search for a safer relative led to ketamine in 1962. PCP became a street drug in the late 1960s and is now Schedule II with no approved use.", "timeline": [["1956", "Victor Maddox synthesizes phencyclidine at Parke-Davis."], ["1950s", "PCP is tested in humans as the anesthetic Sernyl."], ["1960s", "Human use stops because of severe emergence delirium."], ["1962", "Ketamine is synthesized as a safer relative."], ["1978", "PCP is moved to Schedule II."]], "cell": "PCP is a noncompetitive antagonist of the NMDA glutamate receptor. It enters the open ion channel and binds the 'PCP site' inside the pore, blocking calcium and sodium flow even when glutamate is bound. Blocking NMDA receptors in thalamocortical and limbic circuits disconnects sensory input from conscious processing, producing dissociation, analgesia and a trance-like state. PCP also inhibits dopamine reuptake and acts at sigma receptors, which add stimulation, agitation and psychosis. It increases sympathetic output, raising heart rate and blood pressure. Nystagmus, especially vertical, comes from effects on brainstem eye movement control.", "body": [["Brain", "NMDA blockade causes dissociation, hallucinations and psychosis, with agitation and violence that can swing to catatonia or coma."], ["Pain perception", "Strong analgesia means people may not notice serious injuries."], ["Eyes", "Horizontal and vertical nystagmus are classic signs."], ["Heart and vessels", "Sympathetic stimulation raises heart rate and blood pressure, with risk of brain bleeding."], ["Muscles and temperature", "Agitation and rigidity cause hyperthermia and rhabdomyolysis, which can injure the kidneys."]], "journey": "PCP is often smoked on cigarettes or marijuana dipped in liquid PCP, or swallowed. Smoked, it acts in two to five minutes; swallowed, in 30 to 60 minutes. It is very lipid soluble and stores in fat and the brain, which explains fluctuating effects that can last for days. The liver metabolizes it by CYP oxidation. Its half-life varies widely, from about 7 to 46 hours. It is excreted by the kidneys, more in acidic urine because of ion trapping, and is detected in urine for about a week.", "sidefx": "Violent, unpredictable behavior comes from NMDA blockade combined with dopamine effects, and insensitivity to pain makes injuries more likely. Rhabdomyolysis and hyperthermia come from agitation and muscle rigidity. Sympathetic stimulation can cause hypertensive emergencies. Patients may appear calm and then become violent. There is no antidote; benzodiazepines, cooling and protection from injury are standard supportive measures.", "fun": "Ketamine, now an essential anesthetic and depression treatment, was created as a shorter-acting cousin of PCP.", "src": ["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Domino EF. Anesthesiology 2010 (history of PCP and ketamine)", "Katzung Basic and Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "ghb": {"origin": "Gamma-hydroxybutyrate occurs naturally in small amounts in the brain. French physician and researcher Henri Laborit synthesized it in 1960 while studying GABA, aiming to make a GABA-like molecule that could cross into the brain. It was used in Europe as an anesthetic and later for alcohol withdrawal. In the US it was sold in the 1980s as a supplement for bodybuilding and sleep, until FDA banned these sales in 1990 after poisonings. Its link to overdoses and drug-facilitated sexual assault led to the Hillory J. Farias and Samantha Reid Date-Rape Drug Prohibition Act of 2000, which put illicit GHB in Schedule I. In 2002 FDA approved sodium oxybate (Xyrem) for narcolepsy, a Schedule III drug with a restricted distribution program. Xywav followed in 2020 and Lumryz in 2023.", "timeline": [["1960", "Henri Laborit synthesizes GHB."], ["1990", "FDA bans over-the-counter GHB sales after poisonings."], ["2000", "Federal law places illicit GHB in Schedule I."], ["2002", "FDA approves sodium oxybate (Xyrem) for narcolepsy."], ["2020", "FDA approves Xywav, a lower-sodium oxybate."]], "cell": "GHB acts mainly as a weak agonist at the GABA-B receptor, a Gi/o-coupled receptor. Activation opens GIRK potassium channels and closes calcium channels, hyperpolarizing neurons and reducing neurotransmitter release. This causes sedation, amnesia and, at higher levels, coma and slowed breathing. GHB also binds specific high-affinity GHB receptors, whose role is still being worked out. Because GABA-B activation depends steeply on concentration, the gap between sedation and coma is narrow, giving a steep dose-response curve. In narcolepsy, oxybate consolidates nighttime sleep and increases slow-wave sleep, which reduces daytime sleepiness and cataplexy. GBL and 1,4-butanediol are converted to GHB in the body by lactonases and alcohol dehydrogenase.", "body": [["Brain (arousal)", "GABA-B activation causes sedation and amnesia, progressing suddenly to coma at high levels."], ["Breathing", "Respiratory depression occurs, especially when combined with alcohol or other depressants."], ["Heart", "Slow heart rate is common in toxicity, reflecting central depression and increased vagal tone."], ["Body temperature", "Hypothermia can occur as central temperature regulation is depressed."], ["Muscles", "Myoclonic jerks can occur and may be mistaken for seizures."], ["Sleep (oxybate)", "In narcolepsy, more slow-wave sleep at night reduces daytime sleepiness and cataplexy."]], "journey": "GHB is absorbed quickly from the gut, with effects in 15 to 30 minutes and a peak in 30 to 60 minutes. It crosses into the brain easily. It is metabolized mainly by GHB dehydrogenase to succinic semialdehyde, which enters the Krebs cycle and is broken down to carbon dioxide and water. Less than 1 percent is protein bound. The half-life is short, about 30 to 60 minutes, so people often wake suddenly after two to four hours. It is detectable in blood for about six to eight hours and urine for about 12 hours.", "sidefx": "Coma and respiratory depression come from GABA-B activation, and the steep dose-response means a small extra amount can cause sudden unconsciousness. Alcohol greatly increases the risk. Vomiting while unconscious can lead to aspiration. Regular use causes dependence, and withdrawal can resemble severe alcohol withdrawal with delirium. The Xyrem label carries a boxed warning for CNS depression and abuse. According to the label, naloxone and flumazenil are not expected to reverse its effects, so care is supportive, with airway protection.", "src": ["Xyrem (sodium oxybate) FDA label", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Katzung Basic and Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "cannabis": {"origin": "Cannabis sativa has been used for fiber, food and medicine for thousands of years, with early records from China and Central Asia. It entered Western medicine in the 1840s through Irish physician William O'Shaughnessy, who had studied it in India. In the US, the Marihuana Tax Act of 1937 restricted it, and in 1970 marijuana became Schedule I. In 1964 Raphael Mechoulam and Yehiel Gaoni at the Weizmann Institute in Israel isolated and determined the structure of THC, the main psychoactive compound. The CB1 receptor was cloned in 1990, and Mechoulam's group identified anandamide, the body's own cannabinoid, in 1992. FDA approved dronabinol (Marinol) in 1985, nabilone (Cesamet), and cannabidiol (Epidiolex) in 2018. Plant cannabis itself is not FDA approved, though many states allow its use.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Cannabis is used in China and Central Asia for fiber and medicine."], ["1937", "The Marihuana Tax Act restricts cannabis in the US."], ["1964", "Mechoulam and Gaoni isolate and identify THC."], ["1985", "FDA approves dronabinol (Marinol)."], ["1990", "The CB1 cannabinoid receptor is cloned."], ["2018", "FDA approves Epidiolex (cannabidiol) for seizures."]], "cell": "THC is a partial agonist at CB1 and CB2 cannabinoid receptors, which are Gi/o-coupled receptors. CB1 is found on presynaptic nerve terminals throughout the brain, especially in the hippocampus, basal ganglia, cerebellum and cortex. When THC activates CB1, it lowers cAMP, closes calcium channels and opens potassium channels, reducing release of both GABA and glutamate. This retrograde signaling system normally fine-tunes synapses using endocannabinoids like anandamide and 2-AG. THC overrides it, causing euphoria, altered perception, impaired short-term memory (hippocampus) and poor coordination (cerebellum). CB1 in the hypothalamus increases appetite, and CB1 in the brainstem reduces nausea. CB2 on immune cells modulates inflammation. CBD does not activate CB1 the same way and has non-intoxicating mechanisms.", "body": [["Brain (mood and perception)", "CB1 activation causes euphoria and altered perception; high-potency products can trigger anxiety and psychosis."], ["Brain (memory and coordination)", "Hippocampal and cerebellar CB1 effects impair short-term memory, reaction time and coordination, making driving dangerous."], ["Appetite and nausea", "Hypothalamic and brainstem CB1 effects increase appetite and reduce nausea, the basis for dronabinol and nabilone."], ["Heart and vessels", "Heart rate rises and blood vessels dilate, causing red eyes and sometimes fainting on standing."], ["Gut", "Long heavy use can cause cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome with cycles of severe vomiting."], ["Lungs", "Smoking irritates airways, causing cough and bronchitis."]], "journey": "Inhaled THC is absorbed through the lungs and reaches the brain within minutes, with effects lasting two to four hours. Swallowed THC is absorbed slowly and heavily processed by the liver on first pass, with effects starting in 30 minutes to two hours and lasting six to eight hours or longer. The liver converts it by CYP2C9 and CYP3A4 to 11-hydroxy-THC, which is active, then to inactive THC-COOH. THC is about 97 percent protein bound and stores in fat, giving a long terminal half-life. Metabolites leave in feces and urine and can be detected for days to weeks in heavy users.", "sidefx": "Most side effects come from CB1 activation in the brain. Anxiety, panic and psychosis are more likely with high-potency products and in adolescents. Impaired coordination and reaction time explain driving accidents. Children who eat edibles can become deeply sedated or comatose because of their small size. Cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome is linked to long heavy use. Delta-8 THC and other hemp-derived products are not FDA approved, and FDA has warned about poisonings. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "The discovery of THC led scientists to find a whole endocannabinoid system in the body, named after the plant that revealed it.", "src": ["Marinol (dronabinol) FDA label", "Epidiolex (cannabidiol) FDA label", "Gaoni Y, Mechoulam R. J Am Chem Soc 1964", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]}, "synthetic-cannabinoids": {"origin": "Many synthetic cannabinoids were first made by researchers studying the cannabinoid system. The best known, JWH-018, was created in 1995 by organic chemist John W. Huffman at Clemson University, whose lab made hundreds of compounds (the JWH series) to study CB1 and CB2 receptors. They were never intended for human use. Around 2008, forensic chemists in Europe found JWH-018 sprayed on herbal products sold as 'Spice'. Products called K2 and Spice spread in the US, and the DEA used emergency scheduling for several compounds in 2011, followed by the Synthetic Drug Abuse Prevention Act of 2012. Manufacturers keep changing structures to stay ahead of laws. A 2018 outbreak in Illinois involved products contaminated with brodifacoum, a rat poison, causing severe bleeding. They have no approved medical use.", "timeline": [["1995", "John W. Huffman's lab at Clemson synthesizes JWH-018."], ["2008", "JWH-018 is found in Spice herbal products in Europe."], ["2011", "The DEA emergency schedules several synthetic cannabinoids."], ["2012", "The Synthetic Drug Abuse Prevention Act places many in Schedule I."], ["2018", "An Illinois outbreak links synthetic cannabinoids to brodifacoum poisoning."]], "cell": "Unlike THC, which is a partial agonist, most synthetic cannabinoids are full agonists at the CB1 receptor, often with much higher potency. Full activation of Gi/o-coupled CB1 strongly lowers cAMP, closes calcium channels and suppresses release of GABA and glutamate across many brain circuits. Because a partial agonist has a ceiling, THC rarely causes severe toxicity, but full agonists can drive receptor signaling far beyond what THC can reach. Excess suppression of GABA release in some circuits may cause seizures. Some metabolites stay active at CB1, which prolongs effects. Many compounds also hit other targets, and new analogs have unknown pharmacology.", "body": [["Brain", "Full CB1 activation causes severe agitation, psychosis and seizures, much more often than THC."], ["Heart", "Fast heart rate, high blood pressure, chest pain and heart attack have been reported."], ["Kidneys", "Acute kidney injury has occurred in outbreaks, through uncertain mechanisms."], ["Gut", "Severe vomiting is common and can lead to dehydration and electrolyte problems."], ["Blood clotting", "Brodifacoum contamination in 2018 blocked vitamin K recycling and caused severe bleeding."]], "journey": "Synthetic cannabinoids are usually smoked from plant material or paper, so they reach the brain within minutes. Effects last hours, and duration varies by compound. They are metabolized in the liver, and some metabolites remain active at CB1. They are not detected by standard THC drug screens, and testing requires specialized methods.", "sidefx": "Severe toxicity comes from full CB1 activation without the ceiling seen with THC. This explains seizures, psychosis and dangerous agitation. Cardiac effects include tachycardia, heart attack and stroke. Acute kidney injury and mass outbreaks occur because products vary and contain unknown compounds. In the 2018 brodifacoum outbreak, vitamin K was used to reverse the bleeding. Otherwise there is no antidote; benzodiazepines for agitation and seizures and supportive care are standard.", "fun": "John Huffman publicly warned that his research compounds were never meant for people and that their effects in humans were unknown.", "src": ["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "DEA emergency scheduling order 2011", "Kelkar AH et al. NEJM 2018 (brodifacoum outbreak)"]}, "synthetic-cathinones": {"origin": "Synthetic cathinones are lab-made relatives of cathinone, the stimulant in khat (Catha edulis), a shrub whose leaves are chewed in East Africa and the Arabian Peninsula. Some were first made by pharmaceutical researchers: mephedrone was described in 1929, and MDPV was developed by Boehringer Ingelheim in the 1960s, but neither became a medicine. Around 2010 and 2011, products sold as 'bath salts' or 'plant food' and labeled not for human consumption spread in the US, causing severe poisonings. The DEA used emergency scheduling for mephedrone, MDPV and methylone in 2011, and the Synthetic Drug Abuse Prevention Act of 2012 made many Schedule I. New analogs such as alpha-PVP ('flakka') and eutylone followed. They have no approved medical use.", "timeline": [["1929", "Mephedrone is first synthesized."], ["1960s", "Boehringer Ingelheim develops MDPV."], ["2011", "The DEA emergency schedules mephedrone, MDPV and methylone."], ["2012", "The Synthetic Drug Abuse Prevention Act places many in Schedule I."]], "cell": "Synthetic cathinones are beta-keto amphetamines, and they act on monoamine transporters in two different ways. Pyrovalerones such as MDPV and alpha-PVP are potent blockers of the dopamine transporter (DAT) and norepinephrine transporter (NET), like cocaine but stronger and longer lasting, with little serotonin effect. Ring-substituted cathinones such as mephedrone and methylone are transporter substrates that are carried into the neuron and cause release of dopamine, norepinephrine and serotonin, similar to MDMA. Either way, synaptic monoamines rise sharply. Dopamine excess drives intense stimulation, paranoia and compulsive redosing. Norepinephrine drives tachycardia, hypertension and hyperthermia. Serotonin effects with releasers can cause hyponatremia and serotonin toxicity.", "body": [["Brain", "Dopamine surges cause intense stimulation, paranoia, hallucinations and agitated delirium, and psychosis can last for days."], ["Heart and vessels", "Norepinephrine excess causes tachycardia, hypertension, arrhythmias and cardiac arrest."], ["Temperature and muscles", "Extreme agitation and heat generation cause hyperthermia and rhabdomyolysis."], ["Kidneys", "Muscle breakdown products and low blood flow cause acute kidney injury."], ["Sodium balance", "Releaser-type cathinones, like MDMA, can cause hyponatremia."]], "journey": "Synthetic cathinones are snorted, swallowed, smoked or injected, and act within minutes. Effects peak within about 30 to 90 minutes and last several hours, though psychosis may persist for days. They are metabolized in the liver, and pharmacokinetics vary by compound. They are often not detected on routine drug screens and require specialized testing.", "sidefx": "Toxicity comes from excess dopamine and norepinephrine. Agitated delirium with hyperthermia can lead to rhabdomyolysis, kidney failure and multiorgan failure. Sudden death can occur, especially during struggle or restraint, likely from catecholamine-driven arrhythmias and acidosis. Long psychosis reflects prolonged dopamine effects. There is no antidote; benzodiazepines, cooling and supportive care are standard measures.", "fun": "The 'bath salts' name was a marketing trick: the products had nothing to do with bathing and were labeled that way to avoid drug laws.", "src": ["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "DEA emergency scheduling order 2011", "Baumann MH et al. Neuropsychopharmacology 2013"]}, "kratom": {"origin": "Kratom is the leaf of Mitragyna speciosa, a tree in the coffee family that grows in Thailand, Malaysia, Indonesia and Papua New Guinea. For generations farm and dock workers in Southeast Asia chewed the fresh leaves or brewed them as tea to fight fatigue in the heat. The Dutch botanist Pieter Korthals named the genus in the 1800s because the flower parts reminded him of a bishop's mitre. The main alkaloid, mitragynine, was isolated in the early 20th century. Thailand outlawed kratom in 1943 and only relaxed that law in 2021. Kratom spread to the US through online and gas station sales in the 2000s and 2010s. FDA states it is not a lawful dietary supplement ingredient and it has no FDA-approved use. Later research showed that a minor alkaloid, 7-hydroxymitragynine (7-OH), is far stronger at opioid receptors, and concentrated 7-OH products led FDA to recommend scheduling 7-OH in 2025.", "timeline": [["1943", "Thailand passes a law banning kratom."], ["2018", "A multistate Salmonella outbreak is linked to kratom products in the US."], ["2021", "Thailand removes kratom from its narcotics list."], ["2025", "FDA recommends federal scheduling of concentrated 7-OH products."]], "cell": "Mitragynine is an indole alkaloid that acts as a partial agonist at mu-opioid receptors. These are Gi/o-coupled receptors. Binding lowers cyclic AMP, opens GIRK potassium channels and closes voltage-gated calcium channels, so neurons fire less and release less transmitter. In pain pathways that dampens pain signals, and in the brainstem it slows breathing. Mitragynine is described as G protein biased, recruiting beta-arrestin less than morphine in laboratory studies, but human safety has not been proven. In the liver CYP3A4 converts some mitragynine to 7-hydroxymitragynine, a much more potent mu agonist that likely drives much of the opioid effect. Mitragynine also acts on alpha-adrenergic and serotonin receptors, which helps explain the stimulant feel at lower exposure and the sedating, opioid-like effects at higher exposure.", "body": [["Brain and mood", "Lower exposure can feel energizing from adrenergic effects. Higher exposure brings opioid-like calm, euphoria and sedation through mu receptors in reward and arousal circuits."], ["Breathing", "Mu-receptor activity in brainstem breathing centers can slow respiration. Most reported deaths involved kratom taken with other opioids, sedatives or alcohol."], ["Pupils", "Opioid activity on the Edinger-Westphal nucleus produces small pupils."], ["Gut", "Mu receptors in the gut wall slow movement, causing constipation, nausea and appetite loss with weight loss."], ["Liver", "Cholestatic liver injury has been reported, likely an idiosyncratic reaction to the alkaloids or contaminants."], ["Nervous system", "Seizures are reported, especially with high exposure or co-ingestants, and regular use leads to dependence with opioid-like withdrawal."]], "journey": "Kratom is swallowed as leaf powder, capsules, tea, extracts or tablets. Effects begin within about half an hour and peak in one to two hours. The alkaloids are fat soluble and spread widely into the brain and tissues. The liver breaks mitragynine down mainly through CYP3A4, which also makes the more potent 7-OH. Kratom can slow the metabolism of other drugs that use CYP3A4 and CYP2D6. Metabolites leave through the urine. Mitragynine has a long and variable half-life, roughly a day in some studies. Standard drug screens do not detect it, so specific testing is needed. Product strength varies widely because these are unregulated plant products.", "sidefx": "Constipation, nausea, small pupils, sedation and slowed breathing all follow from mu-opioid activation, and repeated exposure causes receptor adaptation, so stopping brings opioid-like withdrawal such as aches, runny nose, irritability and cravings. Stimulant effects from adrenergic receptors explain insomnia and fast heart rate. Liver injury and seizures are less well understood. Contamination is a separate danger: products have carried Salmonella and heavy metals. Babies born to people who used kratom can have neonatal withdrawal. Naloxone can reverse opioid-type breathing depression in case reports, but care is mostly supportive.", "fun": "Kratom is a cousin of the coffee plant: both belong to the family Rubiaceae, yet their main effects come from very different chemicals.", "src": ["FDA statements on kratom and 7-hydroxymitragynine", "NIDA Kratom DrugFacts", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]}, "dmt": {"origin": "N,N-dimethyltryptamine (DMT) occurs naturally in many plants and in small amounts in animals. Indigenous peoples of the Amazon have long brewed ayahuasca from the Banisteriopsis caapi vine and DMT-containing leaves such as Psychotria viridis. The vine contains harmala alkaloids that block the enzyme MAO-A, which is what lets swallowed DMT reach the brain. The Canadian chemist Richard Manske first synthesized DMT in 1931, without knowing it was psychoactive. In 1956 the Hungarian chemist and psychiatrist Stephen Szara injected himself with DMT and described its intense, brief hallucinations. DMT was placed in Schedule I under the 1970 Controlled Substances Act. In the 1990s Rick Strassman at the University of New Mexico ran the first new US human DMT studies in decades. In 2006 the US Supreme Court ruled that a church could use ayahuasca tea in ceremonies. DMT has no FDA-approved use, though it is under study for depression.", "timeline": [["1931", "Richard Manske first synthesizes DMT."], ["1956", "Stephen Szara reports the hallucinogenic effects of DMT in himself."], ["1970", "DMT becomes a Schedule I drug under the Controlled Substances Act."], ["1990s", "Rick Strassman conducts DMT studies in volunteers at the University of New Mexico."], ["2006", "The US Supreme Court allows ceremonial ayahuasca use by the UDV church."]], "cell": "DMT is a tryptamine shaped much like serotonin. Its key action is agonism at serotonin 5-HT2A receptors, which are Gq-coupled receptors dense on layer V pyramidal neurons of the cerebral cortex. Activation turns on phospholipase C, which makes IP3 and DAG, raising calcium and activating protein kinase C. This boosts glutamate release and cortical excitability and disrupts normal filtering of sensory information through thalamus and cortex loops, producing vivid visual and dissociative experiences. DMT also acts at 5-HT1A and 5-HT2C receptors, sigma-1 receptors and trace amine-associated receptors. MAO-A in the gut, liver and brain breaks DMT down within minutes. In ayahuasca, harmine and harmaline reversibly inhibit MAO-A, so DMT survives first-pass metabolism, and those MAO inhibitors raise serotonin and norepinephrine too.", "body": [["Brain", "5-HT2A activation in the cortex causes intense visual hallucinations, altered sense of self and time, and sometimes panic or lasting psychological distress."], ["Heart and vessels", "Sympathetic activation raises heart rate and blood pressure during the experience."], ["Eyes", "Pupils dilate from increased sympathetic tone."], ["Gut (ayahuasca)", "Vomiting and diarrhea are common with ayahuasca, partly from serotonin effects on the gut and the bitter plant brew."], ["Serotonin system", "With ayahuasca the MAO-inhibiting harmala alkaloids raise serotonin, so adding SSRIs, SNRIs, tramadol or linezolid can cause serotonin syndrome."], ["Breathing (5-MeO-DMT)", "The related 5-MeO-DMT has caused apnea and seizures in reports."]], "journey": "Smoked or vaped DMT reaches the brain in seconds, peaks in minutes and fades within about half an hour, because MAO-A destroys it so quickly. Swallowed alone, DMT is almost entirely broken down by MAO-A in the gut and liver before it reaches the blood. In ayahuasca the harmala alkaloids block that enzyme, so effects start in 30 to 60 minutes and last several hours. The main breakdown product is indole-3-acetic acid, which leaves in the urine. DMT does not show up on standard drug screens.", "sidefx": "Fast heart rate, high blood pressure and dilated pupils reflect serotonin and sympathetic activation. Panic and psychosis-like reactions come from intense 5-HT2A-driven changes in perception, and people can be injured while disoriented. The most dangerous drug interactions come from the MAO inhibitors in ayahuasca: they block the breakdown of serotonin and of tyramine and other amines, so serotonergic medicines can trigger serotonin syndrome and stimulants or tyramine-rich foods can trigger hypertensive reactions. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive, with benzodiazepines for agitation and standard serotonin syndrome management.", "fun": "Swallowed DMT alone does almost nothing because gut MAO-A destroys it. Ayahuasca works only because the vine in the brew happens to contain natural MAO inhibitors.", "src": ["Szara S. 1956 report on DMT effects", "Strassman RJ et al. Arch Gen Psychiatry 1994", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "DEA Drug Fact Sheet: DMT"]}, "mescaline": {"origin": "Mescaline is the main psychoactive alkaloid of peyote (Lophophora williamsii), a small spineless cactus of the Chihuahuan Desert in southern Texas and northern Mexico. It is also found in San Pedro and Peruvian torch cacti of the Andes. Peyote has been used in Indigenous ceremonies for thousands of years; dried peyote buttons from caves in the lower Pecos region of Texas have been dated to more than 5,000 years old. In 1897 the German chemist Arthur Heffter isolated mescaline from peyote and tested it on himself to show it was the active compound. Ernst Spath synthesized it in 1919, making it one of the first hallucinogens made in a lab. Aldous Huxley's 1954 book The Doors of Perception described his mescaline experience and made it famous. It became Schedule I in 1970, with a federal religious exemption for peyote in Native American Church ceremonies. It has no FDA-approved use.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Peyote is used in Indigenous ceremonies in what is now Texas and Mexico."], ["1897", "Arthur Heffter isolates mescaline and confirms its effects in himself."], ["1919", "Ernst Spath achieves the first chemical synthesis of mescaline."], ["1954", "Aldous Huxley publishes The Doors of Perception."], ["1970", "Mescaline and peyote are placed in Schedule I."], ["1994", "Federal law protects ceremonial peyote use by members of Native American tribes."]], "cell": "Mescaline is 3,4,5-trimethoxyphenethylamine, a phenethylamine related in shape to dopamine, norepinephrine and amphetamine. Its hallucinogenic action comes from agonism at serotonin 5-HT2A receptors, with additional 5-HT2C activity. 5-HT2A receptors are Gq-coupled: activation stimulates phospholipase C, producing IP3 and DAG, which raise intracellular calcium and activate protein kinase C in cortical pyramidal neurons. This increases glutamate signaling in the prefrontal cortex and loosens normal sensory gating, producing visual changes, altered thinking and emotional shifts. Mescaline is a low-potency agonist compared with LSD, so much more drug is needed to fill the receptors. Its amphetamine-like structure contributes mild sympathetic stimulation.", "body": [["Brain", "5-HT2A activation brings vivid color and pattern changes, altered time sense and strong emotions for many hours. Panic or psychosis-like reactions can occur."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are very common early on, from serotonin receptor effects and the bitter plant material."], ["Heart and vessels", "Mild sympathetic stimulation raises heart rate and blood pressure."], ["Eyes and skin", "Pupils dilate and people may sweat or flush from sympathetic effects."], ["Body as a whole", "Physical toxicity is generally low, but the long duration raises the chance of accidents, trauma and dehydration."]], "journey": "Mescaline is swallowed as dried cactus, tea or powder. It is water soluble and absorbed fairly slowly, so effects begin in one to two hours and peak a few hours later. Because it is polar, it crosses into the brain less easily than many drugs, which partly explains why so much is needed. It lasts a long time, often eight to twelve hours. Some is broken down by oxidative deamination to 3,4,5-trimethoxyphenylacetic acid, but much leaves unchanged in the urine. The half-life is about six hours. Standard drug screens do not detect it.", "sidefx": "Nausea and vomiting come from serotonin receptor stimulation in the gut and brainstem. Fast heart rate, high blood pressure, sweating and wide pupils come from its amphetamine-like sympathetic effects. Psychological harms such as panic, paranoia and risky behavior stem from strong 5-HT2A-driven changes in perception that last many hours. Combining it with stimulants adds to the sympathetic load, and combining it with serotonergic drugs may raise serotonin toxicity risk. Peyote can be confused with other cacti or plants. There is no antidote; care is supportive, with a calm setting and benzodiazepines for severe agitation.", "fun": "Mescaline was named after the Mescalero Apache people, and it was one of the first hallucinogens ever made in a laboratory.", "src": ["Heffter A. 1898 report on peyote alkaloids", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "DEA Drug Fact Sheet: Peyote and Mescaline"]}, "inhalants": {"origin": "Inhalants are not one drug but many household and industrial chemicals breathed in for a quick high. Some have long medical histories. Joseph Priestley made nitrous oxide in 1772, and Humphry Davy described its euphoric 'laughing gas' effect in 1800. The dentist Horace Wells tried it for tooth extraction in 1844, and medical nitrous oxide is still an approved anesthetic gas. Amyl nitrite was first made in the 1840s, and in 1867 Thomas Lauder Brunton used it to relieve angina; its glass ampules made a popping sound when crushed, the source of the name 'poppers'. Solvent sniffing of glue, gasoline and paint thinner drew public attention in the US in the late 1950s and 1960s. In 1970 Millard Bass described 'sudden sniffing death,' cardiac death in young people who inhaled volatile hydrocarbons. Today the most common products include aerosol dusters, butane, nitrous oxide 'whippets' and alkyl nitrites. None is FDA approved for nonmedical use.", "timeline": [["1772", "Joseph Priestley prepares nitrous oxide."], ["1800", "Humphry Davy publishes his studies of laughing gas."], ["1867", "Thomas Lauder Brunton uses amyl nitrite to relieve angina."], ["1960s", "Glue and solvent sniffing becomes a recognized public health problem in the US."], ["1970", "Millard Bass describes sudden sniffing death in JAMA."]], "cell": "Volatile solvents (toluene, gasoline) and propellant gases (butane, propane, difluoroethane) are small fat-soluble molecules that dissolve into nerve cell membranes and act like general anesthetics. They enhance GABA-A and glycine receptor chloride currents, which hyperpolarizes neurons, and they block NMDA glutamate receptors, reducing excitation. The result is quick euphoria, then dizziness, slurred speech and loss of coordination. Halogenated hydrocarbons also sensitize the heart muscle to catecholamines, so a surge of adrenaline can set off ventricular fibrillation. Nitrous oxide is an NMDA antagonist and also irreversibly oxidizes the cobalt atom in vitamin B12, which inactivates methionine synthase and blocks myelin upkeep. Alkyl nitrites release nitric oxide, which activates guanylate cyclase, raises cyclic GMP and relaxes vascular smooth muscle; they also oxidize hemoglobin iron to the ferric state, forming methemoglobin.", "body": [["Brain", "Anesthetic-like depression brings brief euphoria, then confusion, slurred speech and unsteady walking. Long-term toluene use damages white matter (leukoencephalopathy) with memory loss and ataxia."], ["Heart", "Solvents and gases make the heart overly sensitive to adrenaline. A startle, chase or exertion can trigger fatal ventricular fibrillation, called sudden sniffing death."], ["Lungs and airway", "Rebreathing from a bag lowers oxygen and can cause asphyxia. Spraying cold propellant can freeze the throat, and vomit can be aspirated."], ["Kidneys and electrolytes", "Toluene causes distal renal tubular acidosis, which can drop potassium to dangerously low levels and cause severe weakness."], ["Nerves and spinal cord", "Repeated nitrous oxide inactivates vitamin B12, causing numbness, weakness and a spinal cord disorder called subacute combined degeneration."], ["Blood", "Nitrites turn hemoglobin into methemoglobin, which cannot carry oxygen, so the skin looks gray-blue and people become short of breath."], ["Blood vessels", "Nitrites widen vessels, causing flushing, headache and low blood pressure, which can be severe with PDE5 inhibitors such as sildenafil."]], "journey": "Inhalants enter through the lungs, so they reach the brain within seconds. Because they are very fat soluble they pass easily into the brain and then redistribute to body fat, so a single high fades within minutes, which leads to repeated inhaling. Most gases, such as butane and nitrous oxide, leave mostly unchanged in exhaled breath. Toluene is partly exhaled and partly broken down in the liver to hippuric acid, which leaves in the urine. Nitrites are quickly broken down in the blood. Standard drug screens do not detect inhalants.", "sidefx": "The anesthetic action on brain membranes explains intoxication and, with heavy exposure, coma. Cardiac sensitization to catecholamines explains sudden sniffing death, which is why a person who is intoxicated should not be startled or chased. Hypoxia from bags and frostbite from cold propellants are physical injuries. Toluene's damage to kidney tubule acid handling explains low potassium and acidosis. Nitrous oxide's oxidation of B12 explains nerve and spinal cord damage, treated with B12 replacement. Nitrite-induced methemoglobinemia can be reversed with methylene blue, which helps convert methemoglobin back to normal hemoglobin.", "fun": "The name 'poppers' comes from the original glass ampules of amyl nitrite, which made a pop when crushed in a cloth to be sniffed for angina.", "src": ["Bass M. Sudden sniffing death. JAMA 1970", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "NIDA Inhalants Research Report", "FDA warnings on nitrous oxide and nitrite products"]}, "designer-benzodiazepines": {"origin": "The benzodiazepine story began at Hoffmann-La Roche, where chemist Leo Sternbach found chlordiazepoxide in 1955; it was marketed as Librium in 1960, followed by diazepam (Valium) in 1963. Drug companies went on to make thousands of related molecules, and many were described in patents and papers but never sold. Designer benzodiazepines are mostly these shelved or slightly altered compounds. Etizolam, a thienodiazepine, was developed in Japan and approved there in the 1980s, but never in the US. Starting in the early 2010s, compounds such as flubromazolam, clonazolam and flualprazolam appeared online as 'research chemicals.' Bromazolam, first described decades earlier but never marketed, became one of the most common in the early 2020s. These drugs are now often pressed into counterfeit pills or mixed with fentanyl ('benzo dope'). In 2023 DEA temporarily placed several of them in Schedule I. None is FDA approved.", "timeline": [["1955", "Leo Sternbach discovers chlordiazepoxide at Hoffmann-La Roche."], ["1963", "Diazepam is marketed as Valium."], ["1980s", "Etizolam is approved in Japan; it is never approved in the US."], ["2010s", "Unapproved benzodiazepines are sold online as research chemicals."], ["2023", "DEA temporarily schedules etizolam, flualprazolam, clonazolam, flubromazolam and diclazepam."]], "cell": "Designer benzodiazepines bind the benzodiazepine site of the GABA-A receptor, a pocket at the interface between an alpha subunit (alpha1, 2, 3 or 5) and the gamma2 subunit. They are positive allosteric modulators: they do not open the channel themselves but make GABA more effective, increasing how often the chloride channel opens. More chloride flows into the neuron, hyperpolarizing it and making it harder to fire. Alpha1-containing receptors mediate sedation and amnesia, alpha2 and alpha3 anxiety relief and muscle relaxation, and alpha5 memory effects. Many designer compounds carry a triazole ring and halogen atoms (for example bromazolam is a bromine version of alprazolam), which raise binding strength, so tiny amounts are potent. Because they only boost GABA, alone they have a ceiling, but with opioids, alcohol or GHB the depression of brainstem breathing becomes additive.", "body": [["Brain", "Enhanced GABA inhibition causes sedation, slurred speech, poor coordination and memory blackouts. Disinhibition can lead to risky behavior."], ["Breathing", "With opioids, alcohol or GHB the combined depression of the brainstem can stop breathing. Benzodiazepines can also keep people sedated after naloxone reverses the opioid part."], ["Muscles", "Spinal GABA effects relax muscles and impair balance, raising the risk of falls and aspiration."], ["Nervous system with regular use", "GABA-A receptors adapt to constant stimulation. Stopping suddenly unmasks overexcitement, with anxiety, tremor, insomnia, hallucinations and seizures."], ["Memory", "Alpha5 and alpha1 receptor effects in the hippocampus block forming new memories, so people may not recall events."]], "journey": "These drugs are swallowed as pressed pills, powders or blotters, sometimes snorted, and often taken unknowingly in fentanyl. They are fat soluble, are absorbed within about an hour and spread readily into the brain. Most are broken down in the liver, often by CYP3A4, and some have active metabolites. Half-lives vary widely, from a few hours to longer than a day, so sedation can last much longer than the person expects. Metabolites leave in the urine. Standard benzodiazepine immunoassays often miss them, so a negative screen does not rule them out.", "sidefx": "Sedation, amnesia and loss of coordination come directly from boosted GABA-A inhibition. The real danger is synergy: opioids reduce the drive to breathe while benzodiazepines blunt the brain's response, so together they cause apnea and death. Long-acting compounds explain prolonged sedation in emergency departments. Tolerance and receptor downregulation explain severe withdrawal, which can include seizures. Counterfeit pills may contain fentanyl, so naloxone is still given for possible opioid exposure, though it does not reverse the benzodiazepine. Flumazenil blocks the benzodiazepine site but can provoke seizures in dependent people, so care is mostly supportive.", "fun": "Many designer benzodiazepines were first described decades ago in drug company patents and papers, then sat unused until online sellers revived them.", "src": ["DEA temporary scheduling order for designer benzodiazepines, 2023", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "tianeptine": {"origin": "Tianeptine was developed by the French drug company Servier and introduced in France in the late 1980s as an antidepressant under the brand name Stablon. It is a tricyclic compound in shape, but it acts quite differently from classic tricyclic antidepressants. It was approved in several European, Asian and Latin American countries but never in the US. For years it was thought to work by speeding up serotonin reuptake, the opposite of SSRIs. In 2014 researchers at Columbia University reported that tianeptine is actually a full agonist at mu-opioid receptors, which explained reports of misuse and dependence. In the US it began to be sold online and in gas stations as a 'supplement,' earning the nickname 'gas station heroin.' FDA has warned repeatedly since 2018 that it is not lawful in dietary supplements and has caused serious harm, and several states have banned it.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Servier introduces tianeptine in France as the antidepressant Stablon."], ["2014", "Columbia University researchers show tianeptine is a mu-opioid receptor agonist."], ["2018", "FDA issues its first warnings about tianeptine sold as a supplement."], ["2024", "FDA warns that Neptune's Fix products contain synthetic cannabinoids and other substances."]], "cell": "At the high exposures seen with misuse, tianeptine acts as a full agonist at mu-opioid receptors and a weaker agonist at delta-opioid receptors. Mu receptors are Gi/o-coupled: activation lowers cyclic AMP, opens GIRK potassium channels and closes presynaptic calcium channels, reducing neuron firing and transmitter release. In the ventral tegmental area this disinhibits dopamine neurons, producing euphoria and reinforcing repeated use. In the brainstem it depresses the breathing centers. Tianeptine and its active metabolite MC5 also modulate glutamate signaling through AMPA and NMDA receptors, which may underlie its proposed antidepressant effects. The short half-life means effects wear off quickly, driving frequent redosing and rapid dependence.", "body": [["Brain and reward", "Mu-opioid activation produces euphoria and sedation, and repeated exposure leads to tolerance, craving and dependence."], ["Breathing", "Brainstem mu activation slows breathing. Overdose can cause coma and respiratory arrest, especially with other depressants."], ["Pupils", "Opioid activity constricts the pupils, part of the classic opioid toxidrome."], ["Gut", "Mu receptors in the gut slow movement, causing nausea and constipation."], ["Heart and vessels", "Agitation, fast heart rate and high blood pressure are also reported, especially during withdrawal or with adulterants."]], "journey": "Tianeptine is swallowed as pills, powders or liquid shots. It is absorbed quickly, with effects in under an hour and peak levels in about an hour. It is highly bound to plasma proteins. Unlike most drugs, it is broken down mainly by beta-oxidation of its side chain, not by CYP enzymes, forming the active metabolite MC5, which lasts longer than the parent drug. The parent half-life is short, a few hours, and longer in older adults and people with kidney disease. Metabolites leave mainly in the urine. Standard opioid drug screens do not detect it.", "sidefx": "Sedation, small pupils, constipation and slowed breathing all come from mu-opioid activation. Tolerance and dependence develop quickly because of short-acting, frequent exposure, and stopping causes opioid-like withdrawal with anxiety, sweating, diarrhea and cravings. Naloxone, the mu-opioid antagonist, has reversed tianeptine overdose in case reports. Some products have been adulterated with other drugs, including synthetic cannabinoids, which can cause agitation, seizures or unexpected toxicity. Combining it with opioids, alcohol or benzodiazepines adds to respiratory depression.", "fun": "For decades tianeptine was taught as a drug that sped up serotonin reuptake, and only in 2014 did researchers show its strongest action is at the same receptor morphine uses.", "src": ["Gassaway MM et al. Transl Psychiatry 2014", "FDA warnings on tianeptine products 2018-2024", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]}, "phenibut": {"origin": "Phenibut (beta-phenyl-gamma-aminobutyric acid) was made in the Soviet Union in the 1960s by chemists in Leningrad (now St. Petersburg). The idea was simple: GABA itself does not cross well from blood into the brain, so adding a phenyl ring might help it get in. The result became a prescription drug in Russia and nearby countries for anxiety, insomnia and other conditions, sold under names such as Noofen and Anvifen. It is a close relative of baclofen, which has an extra chlorine atom on the ring. Phenibut is often said to have been included in Soviet cosmonaut medical kits, though that story is hard to verify. In the 2010s it began to be sold online in the US as a nootropic or supplement, and poison centers saw rising calls for sedation and withdrawal. FDA states it is not a lawful dietary ingredient, and it has never been FDA approved.", "timeline": [["1960s", "Phenibut is synthesized in the Soviet Union as a brain-penetrating GABA analog."], ["2010s", "Online sales grow in the US and poison center reports of phenibut toxicity rise."], ["2019", "FDA issues warning letters stating phenibut is not a lawful dietary ingredient."]], "cell": "Phenibut has two main targets. First, it is an agonist at GABA-B receptors, the same receptors baclofen activates; mainly the R-enantiomer is active here. GABA-B receptors are Gi/o-coupled metabotropic receptors. Presynaptically they close voltage-gated calcium channels and reduce transmitter release; postsynaptically they open GIRK potassium channels, causing slow, lasting hyperpolarization. Second, both enantiomers bind the alpha-2-delta subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels, like gabapentin and pregabalin, which reduces calcium entry at excitatory nerve terminals and lowers glutamate release. Together these effects calm neural activity, causing relaxation and sedation. With repeated use, GABA-B and calcium channel systems adapt, so stopping leaves the brain in an overexcited state.", "body": [["Brain", "GABA-B and alpha-2-delta effects calm neural firing, causing relaxation and drowsiness. In overdose, consciousness can swing between deep sedation and agitation or delirium."], ["Breathing", "Alone it rarely stops breathing, but with alcohol, benzodiazepines or opioids the combined depression can be dangerous."], ["Muscles", "GABA-B activity in the spinal cord relaxes muscle tone, similar to baclofen."], ["Nervous system with regular use", "Tolerance develops quickly. Stopping can cause severe anxiety, insomnia, tremor, hallucinations, psychosis and seizures."], ["Gut", "Nausea is common, and GABA-B receptors in the gut and brainstem may contribute to it."]], "journey": "Phenibut is swallowed as powder or capsules. Its effects start slowly, often over a few hours, which can lead people to take more before the first amount takes hold. The phenyl ring helps it cross into the brain better than GABA. It undergoes little metabolism and leaves the body mostly unchanged in the urine, so kidney function affects how long it lasts. The half-life is about five hours, but effects can last many hours. Standard drug screens do not detect it.", "sidefx": "Drowsiness, dizziness and confusion come from enhanced GABA-B inhibition and reduced excitatory transmitter release. The alternating agitation and lethargy seen in overdose likely reflect complex GABA-B effects on different brain circuits, similar to baclofen toxicity. Tolerance results from receptor and channel adaptation, which is why withdrawal causes rebound overexcitement with anxiety, psychosis and seizures. There is no specific antidote. Withdrawal has been managed in case reports with baclofen, which acts on the same receptor, and with benzodiazepines.", "fun": "Phenibut and baclofen differ by just one chlorine atom on the phenyl ring, and both act on the same GABA-B receptor.", "src": ["FDA warning letters on phenibut, 2019", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Lapin I. CNS Drug Reviews 2001"]}, "csl-polyvalent-snake-antivenom": {"origin": "Antivenom began in the 1890s, when Albert Calmette of the Pasteur Institute showed that animals immunized with small, increasing amounts of cobra venom made serum that protected others. In Australia, the Commonwealth Serum Laboratories (CSL), founded in Melbourne in 1916, released its first snake antivenom, against tiger snake venom, in 1930. Over the next three decades CSL added separate antivenoms for taipans, brown snakes, death adders and black snakes. Because doctors often could not tell which snake had bitten, CSL combined all five into a single polyvalent product in the early 1960s. It is made by immunizing horses, collecting their plasma and digesting the antibodies with pepsin into F(ab')2 fragments. Today CSL Seqirus makes it for Australia and Papua New Guinea. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.", "timeline": [["1890s", "Albert Calmette develops the first snake antivenom against cobra venom."], ["1916", "Commonwealth Serum Laboratories is founded in Melbourne."], ["1930", "CSL releases its first antivenom, against tiger snake venom."], ["1960s", "CSL combines five Australian snake antivenoms into one polyvalent product."]], "cell": "The vial holds a mix of horse antibody fragments raised against five venoms: brown snake, tiger snake, death adder, taipan and black (mulga) snake. Each F(ab')2 fragment has two antigen-binding arms and no Fc tail. The fragments bind many different surface sites on venom toxins, including prothrombin activators that drive consumption coagulopathy, presynaptic phospholipase A2 neurotoxins that destroy motor nerve terminals, postsynaptic three-finger alpha-neurotoxins that block muscle nicotinic receptors, and myotoxins. Binding blocks the toxin's active site or prevents it reaching its target, and the antibody-toxin complexes are cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Antivenom works only on free, circulating venom. It cannot rebuild nerve terminals that a presynaptic toxin has already damaged, and it cannot replace clotting factors already consumed; the liver must make new ones.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Neutralizing prothrombin activators stops further consumption of fibrinogen and factors V and VIII. Clotting tests then recover over hours as the liver makes new factors."], ["Nerves and muscles", "Binding neurotoxins halts progression of paralysis. Postsynaptic weakness can reverse, but presynaptic nerve terminal damage recovers slowly over days."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Neutralizing myotoxins limits further muscle breakdown, which reduces the risk of kidney injury from myoglobin."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein is foreign to humans, so early reactions such as hives, low blood pressure or wheezing can occur, and serum sickness can follow up to about two weeks later."], ["Volume and protein load", "Because it covers five venoms, a vial carries more foreign protein than a single-snake antivenom, which is why monovalent products are preferred when the snake is known."]], "journey": "The antivenom is given only by slow intravenous infusion after dilution, in a monitored hospital. It enters the bloodstream at once and starts binding venom during the infusion. F(ab')2 fragments are about 100 kDa, too large to pass the kidney filter, so they mostly stay in blood and extracellular fluid and are broken down by proteolysis in the reticuloendothelial system. They persist for days, longer than smaller Fab fragments, which helps cover venom slowly leaking from the bite site.", "sidefx": "Side effects come from the human immune system meeting horse protein. Early reactions, from mild hives to anaphylaxis, arise through complement activation and mast cell release, and through IgE in people already sensitized to horse proteins. Asthma, atopy and prior equine antivenom exposure raise the risk. Adrenaline (epinephrine) is the treatment for anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness is a type III reaction: the body makes antibodies against the horse fragments, immune complexes deposit in joints, skin and vessels, and fever, rash and joint pain appear days later, usually treated with corticosteroids.", "fun": "Australia uses a snake venom detection kit on swabs from the bite site to identify the snake group, so the smaller-volume monovalent antivenom can be chosen instead of polyvalent.", "src": ["CSL Seqirus Polyvalent Snake Antivenom product information (Australia)", "Therapeutic Guidelines: Toxicology and Toxinology (Australia)", "WHO Guidelines for the Production, Control and Regulation of Snake Antivenom Immunoglobulins"]}, "csl-brown-snake-antivenom": {"origin": "Australian brown snakes (Pseudonaja), especially the eastern brown snake, cause most snakebite deaths in Australia. They live in farmland and on the edges of cities, where people meet them often. After releasing tiger snake antivenom in 1930, Commonwealth Serum Laboratories (CSL) in Melbourne worked through the 1950s to make antivenoms for other dangerous Australian snakes, and brown snake antivenom was introduced in that decade. Horses are immunized with eastern brown snake (Pseudonaja textilis) venom, and their antibodies are digested with pepsin into F(ab')2 fragments. Brown snake venom is among the most toxic in mouse tests, but bites usually deliver only a small amount. CSL Seqirus makes it today. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.", "timeline": [["1916", "Commonwealth Serum Laboratories is founded in Melbourne."], ["1950s", "CSL introduces brown snake antivenom."], ["2000s", "Australian studies clarify that venom-induced consumption coagulopathy drives most brown snake deaths."]], "cell": "The main danger in brown snake venom is pseutarin C, a prothrombin activator that works like the human factor Xa plus factor Va complex. It converts prothrombin to thrombin in the bloodstream, and the thrombin uses up fibrinogen, factor V and factor VIII. This venom-induced consumption coagulopathy leaves the blood unable to clot. Some patients also develop thrombotic microangiopathy, with damaged red cells, low platelets and kidney injury. Brown snake F(ab')2 fragments bind the prothrombin activator and other venom components, blocking their active sites and forming complexes that the reticuloendothelial system removes. Binding stops further factor consumption, but the antivenom cannot replace factors already used up. Clotting recovers only as the liver makes new fibrinogen and factors, which takes many hours.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Stopping the prothrombin activator halts consumption of fibrinogen. INR and fibrinogen recover over hours as the liver resupplies clotting factors."], ["Heart and circulation", "Brown snake bites can cause early collapse or cardiac arrest within the first hour, likely from venom effects on circulation. Antivenom stops further venom action but supportive care treats the collapse."], ["Brain", "Unclotting blood raises the risk of intracranial bleeding, the most feared complication of brown snake envenomation."], ["Kidneys", "Thrombotic microangiopathy can injure the kidneys. Antivenom does not clearly reverse this once it has started."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and serum sickness days to two weeks later."]], "journey": "The antivenom is diluted and given by slow IV infusion in a monitored hospital. It enters the bloodstream immediately and binds free venom during the infusion. F(ab')2 fragments, about 100 kDa, are too large for the kidney to filter, so they stay mostly in the blood and tissue fluid and are cleared over days by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system. That long presence helps capture venom still leaking from the bite site.", "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein, not from the venom. Early reactions such as hives, low blood pressure and wheezing arise through complement activation, mast cell release and, in people sensitized to horse proteins, IgE. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness is a type III immune complex reaction in which the body forms antibodies against the horse fragments, causing fever, rash, joint pain and swollen glands days later; corticosteroids are commonly used for it.", "fun": "Brown snake venom's prothrombin activator works so much like human clotting factors that a related brown snake protein has been studied as a possible surgical blood-clotting agent.", "src": ["CSL Seqirus Brown Snake Antivenom product information (Australia)", "Therapeutic Guidelines: Toxicology and Toxinology (Australia)", "Isbister GK et al. Australian Snakebite Project publications"]}, "csl-tiger-snake-antivenom": {"origin": "Tiger snake antivenom was Australia's first commercial snake antivenom. Tiger snakes (Notechis scutatus) live in the cooler south of the country, including Victoria and Tasmania, and were a major cause of snakebite deaths in the early 20th century. Research on their venom in Melbourne in the 1920s, including work by the scientist Charles Kellaway at the Walter and Eliza Hall Institute, helped make an antivenom possible. Commonwealth Serum Laboratories (CSL) released tiger snake antivenom in 1930, raised in horses. Later it was refined into pepsin-digested F(ab')2 fragments. Because related snakes have similar venoms, it is also used for copperheads, rough-scaled snakes, broad-headed snakes and the red-bellied black snake. CSL Seqirus makes it today. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.", "timeline": [["1920s", "Researchers in Melbourne study tiger snake venom and immunization."], ["1930", "CSL releases tiger snake antivenom, Australia's first commercial snake antivenom."], ["1950s", "Further CSL antivenoms follow for other Australian snakes."]], "cell": "Tiger snake venom has three main harmful parts. A prothrombin activator (notecarin) converts prothrombin to thrombin and drives consumption coagulopathy. Presynaptic phospholipase A2 neurotoxins such as notexin enter motor nerve terminals, damage them and stop acetylcholine release, causing descending flaccid paralysis. Myotoxins break down skeletal muscle. The horse F(ab')2 fragments bind these toxins at many sites, block their active regions and form complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Neutralizing free toxin stops further damage. However, once a presynaptic toxin has destroyed a nerve terminal, the antibody cannot fix it; the terminal must regrow over days. Likewise, clotting factors already consumed must be remade by the liver.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Neutralizing the prothrombin activator halts fibrinogen consumption. Clotting recovers over hours as the liver makes new factors."], ["Nerves and muscles", "Binding notexin-like neurotoxins stops paralysis from getting worse. Paralysis that has already developed from nerve terminal damage recovers slowly."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Neutralizing myotoxins limits muscle breakdown (rhabdomyolysis), which lowers the risk of myoglobin-related kidney injury."], ["Kidneys", "Less muscle breakdown and less clotting damage help protect the kidneys from acute injury."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can trigger early allergic-type reactions and later serum sickness."]], "journey": "The antivenom is diluted and given by slow IV infusion in hospital. It reaches the blood at once and binds circulating venom during the infusion. Its F(ab')2 fragments are too large for kidney filtration, so they stay in the blood and tissue fluid for days and are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system. This long presence helps catch venom that keeps seeping from the bite site.", "sidefx": "The side effects come from the immune system reacting to horse protein. Early reactions, from hives to anaphylaxis, arise through complement activation and mast cell release, and through IgE in sensitized people. Asthma, atopy and prior equine antivenom raise the risk. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can cause fever, rash, joint pain and swollen glands up to about two weeks later and is usually treated with corticosteroids.", "fun": "Tiger snake antivenom is the usual choice for red-bellied black snake bites because it works well and comes in a smaller volume than black snake antivenom.", "src": ["CSL Seqirus Tiger Snake Antivenom product information (Australia)", "Therapeutic Guidelines: Toxicology and Toxinology (Australia)", "Sutherland SK. Australian Animal Toxins"]}, "csl-taipan-antivenom": {"origin": "Taipans (Oxyuranus) of northern Australia and Papua New Guinea were once one of the deadliest snakes in the region, and survival after a serious bite was rare. In 1950 Kevin Budden, a 20-year-old amateur snake collector, caught a coastal taipan near Cairns so it could be used for antivenom research. He was bitten and died the next day, but the snake was sent to Commonwealth Serum Laboratories (CSL) in Melbourne. Its venom was used to immunize horses, and CSL released taipan antivenom in the mid-1950s. The inland taipan, which has the most toxic venom of any land snake in mouse tests, is also covered. CSL Seqirus makes the antivenom today as horse F(ab')2 fragments. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.", "timeline": [["1950", "Kevin Budden captures a coastal taipan for research and dies from its bite."], ["1950s", "CSL releases taipan antivenom raised in horses."]], "cell": "Taipan venom attacks blood, nerves and muscle. A prothrombin activator (oscutarin) converts prothrombin to thrombin and causes consumption coagulopathy. Taipoxin, a three-part presynaptic phospholipase A2 neurotoxin, enters motor nerve terminals, destroys them and blocks acetylcholine release, causing descending flaccid paralysis. Postsynaptic three-finger neurotoxins block muscle nicotinic receptors, and myotoxins damage muscle. Horse F(ab')2 fragments bind these toxins at many sites, block their activity and form complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Early antivenom can prevent paralysis, but it cannot undo nerve terminals taipoxin has already destroyed. Consumed clotting factors must be made again by the liver.", "body": [["Nerves and muscles", "Neutralizing taipoxin halts progressive paralysis of eyelids, swallowing and breathing muscles. Damage already done recovers slowly as nerve terminals regrow."], ["Blood clotting", "Stopping the prothrombin activator halts fibrinogen consumption, and clotting returns as the liver makes new factors."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Neutralizing myotoxins limits muscle breakdown and the myoglobin load on the kidneys."], ["Kidneys", "Taipan envenomation can cause thrombotic microangiopathy and kidney injury, which antivenom helps limit but may not reverse."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and later serum sickness."]], "journey": "The antivenom is diluted and infused slowly into a vein in a monitored hospital. It starts binding venom in the blood during the infusion. Its F(ab')2 fragments, about 100 kDa, are too large for kidney filtration and stay in the blood and tissue fluid for days. They are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system. Early use matters most, because neurotoxins move quickly from blood into nerve terminals, where antibodies cannot reach.", "sidefx": "Adverse effects come from the horse protein. Early reactions such as hives, low blood pressure and wheezing arise through complement activation and mast cell release, and in sensitized people through IgE. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can cause fever, rash and joint pain up to about two weeks later, usually treated with corticosteroids.", "fun": "The snake that killed Kevin Budden was used to make Australia's first taipan antivenom, so his capture eventually saved many lives.", "src": ["CSL Seqirus Taipan Antivenom product information (Australia)", "Therapeutic Guidelines: Toxicology and Toxinology (Australia)", "Sutherland SK. Australian Animal Toxins"]}, "csl-death-adder-antivenom": {"origin": "Death adders (Acanthophis) look like vipers, with thick bodies and wide heads, but they are elapids, related to cobras. They lie hidden in leaf litter and wiggle the tip of their tail like a worm to lure prey, which is why people often step on them. Before antivenom, death adder bites were frequently fatal. Commonwealth Serum Laboratories (CSL) in Melbourne introduced death adder antivenom in the 1950s, raised in horses against the common death adder (Acanthophis antarcticus). It is now made by CSL Seqirus as pepsin-digested F(ab')2 fragments and is also used in Papua New Guinea. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.", "timeline": [["1930", "CSL releases its first snake antivenom, against tiger snake venom."], ["1950s", "CSL introduces death adder antivenom."]], "cell": "Death adder venom acts mainly on the neuromuscular junction. Its key toxins are postsynaptic three-finger alpha-neurotoxins, which bind the muscle-type nicotinic acetylcholine receptor on the motor end plate and block acetylcholine from opening it. Without that signal, muscles cannot contract, producing descending flaccid paralysis: drooping eyelids, double vision, trouble swallowing and, in severe cases, breathing failure. Horse F(ab')2 fragments bind the free neurotoxins and can also pull toxin off receptors because that binding is reversible. That makes death adder paralysis different from taipan or tiger snake paralysis: antivenom can actually reverse established weakness rather than just stop it. Unlike most other dangerous Australian snakes, death adder venom does not usually cause coagulopathy.", "body": [["Nerves and muscles", "Removing postsynaptic toxins frees nicotinic receptors, so ptosis, bulbar weakness and limb weakness can improve after antivenom."], ["Breathing", "Reversing neuromuscular block helps the diaphragm and chest muscles recover, which can shorten or prevent mechanical ventilation."], ["Neuromuscular junction", "Because the block is postsynaptic, neostigmine, which raises acetylcholine, can sometimes help alongside antivenom."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and later serum sickness."]], "journey": "The antivenom is diluted and infused slowly into a vein in hospital. It binds free neurotoxin in the blood right away and shifts the balance so toxin comes off receptors. The F(ab')2 fragments are too large for kidney filtration, so they remain in the blood and tissue fluid for days and are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system. Strength usually improves over hours.", "sidefx": "Reactions are caused by horse protein. Early reactions, from hives to anaphylaxis, arise through complement activation and mast cell release, and through IgE in sensitized people. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis. Serum sickness is a type III immune complex reaction days later, with fever, rash and joint pain, usually treated with corticosteroids. Some Papua New Guinean death adder bites respond less completely, likely because of differences in venom.", "fun": "Death adders hunt by 'caudal luring,' twitching a pale tail tip like a grub to attract lizards and birds close enough to strike.", "src": ["CSL Seqirus Death Adder Antivenom product information (Australia)", "Therapeutic Guidelines: Toxicology and Toxinology (Australia)", "Johnston CI et al. Australian Snakebite Project reports on death adder envenoming"]}, "csl-black-snake-antivenom": {"origin": "Black snakes (Pseudechis) include the mulga snake, often called the king brown even though it belongs to the black snake genus. The mulga snake lives across most of mainland Australia and can deliver very large amounts of venom in one bite. Commonwealth Serum Laboratories (CSL) in Melbourne introduced black snake antivenom in the 1950s, raised in horses mainly against mulga snake (Pseudechis australis) venom. The product is made as pepsin-digested F(ab')2 fragments and comes in a larger volume than most other CSL snake antivenoms. CSL Seqirus makes it today. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.", "timeline": [["1930", "CSL releases its first snake antivenom, against tiger snake venom."], ["1950s", "CSL introduces black snake antivenom raised against mulga snake venom."]], "cell": "Black snake venom differs from that of brown, tiger and taipan snakes. Its main toxins are phospholipase A2 myotoxins, which damage the membranes of skeletal muscle fibers and cause rhabdomyolysis, and anticoagulant toxins, which interfere with clotting without the massive factor consumption caused by prothrombin activators. The horse F(ab')2 fragments bind these toxins at many sites, block their enzyme activity and form complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Neutralizing the myotoxins stops new muscle injury, but fibers already damaged must heal or be replaced. Neutralizing the anticoagulant toxins lets clotting tests return toward normal.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Neutralizing myotoxins halts muscle breakdown, with less pain, weakness and rise in creatine kinase."], ["Kidneys", "Less muscle breakdown means less myoglobin reaching the kidneys, lowering the risk of acute kidney injury."], ["Blood clotting", "Binding anticoagulant toxins lets clotting tests improve. This is a milder anticoagulant effect, not the consumptive coagulopathy of brown or tiger snakes."], ["Gut and general", "Black snake envenomation often causes nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain and headache, which settle as venom is neutralized."], ["Immune system", "The large volume of horse protein raises the chance of early allergic-type reactions and later serum sickness."]], "journey": "The antivenom is diluted and given by slow IV infusion in a monitored hospital. It binds venom in the blood during the infusion. The F(ab')2 fragments, about 100 kDa, are too large for kidney filtration, so they stay in the blood and tissue fluid for days and are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system. Because the vial volume is large, the infusion carries a sizable protein load.", "sidefx": "Side effects come from horse protein. Early reactions such as hives, low blood pressure and wheezing arise from complement activation and mast cell release, plus IgE in sensitized people. The larger protein volume of this product increases the chance of reactions. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear up to about two weeks later with fever, rash and joint pain, usually treated with corticosteroids.", "fun": "The mulga snake is called the king brown, but it is a black snake (Pseudechis), not a brown snake, which is why it needs black snake antivenom.", "src": ["CSL Seqirus Black Snake Antivenom product information (Australia)", "Therapeutic Guidelines: Toxicology and Toxinology (Australia)", "Sutherland SK. Australian Animal Toxins"]}, "csl-sea-snake-antivenom": {"origin": "Sea snakes live in warm Indo-Pacific waters, including around northern Australia and Southeast Asia, and most bites happen to fishers sorting nets. Many bites are nearly painless, so serious symptoms can come on hours later. Commonwealth Serum Laboratories (CSL) in Melbourne developed sea snake antivenom in the early 1960s, raising it in horses with venom of the beaked sea snake (Enhydrina schistosa, now often called Hydrophis schistosus), a species responsible for many sea snake deaths in Asia. It is made as pepsin-digested F(ab')2 fragments, and laboratory studies show it neutralizes venoms of several other sea snakes. CSL Seqirus makes it today. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.", "timeline": [["1930", "CSL releases its first snake antivenom, against tiger snake venom."], ["1960s", "CSL develops sea snake antivenom using beaked sea snake venom."]], "cell": "Sea snake venom has two main toxin groups. Phospholipase A2 myotoxins break down the membranes of skeletal muscle fibers, releasing myoglobin, potassium and creatine kinase into the blood. Postsynaptic three-finger alpha-neurotoxins bind the muscle-type nicotinic acetylcholine receptor and block neuromuscular transmission, causing weakness and paralysis. The horse F(ab')2 fragments bind both groups at many sites, blocking their active regions and forming complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Neutralizing myotoxins stops further muscle injury, and neutralizing neurotoxins allows receptors to work again. Muscle already damaged recovers only as fibers regenerate.", "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Stopping myotoxins halts muscle breakdown, which causes pain, stiffness and weakness hours after the bite."], ["Kidneys", "Less myoglobin in the blood reduces the risk of kidney injury and dark urine."], ["Heart", "Muscle breakdown releases potassium. Halting it lowers the risk of dangerous hyperkalemia and heart rhythm problems."], ["Nerves and muscles", "Neutralizing postsynaptic neurotoxins helps reverse weakness, drooping eyelids and trouble swallowing or breathing."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and later serum sickness."]], "journey": "The antivenom is diluted and infused slowly into a vein in hospital. It binds venom in the blood during the infusion. F(ab')2 fragments are too large to pass the kidney filter, so they stay in the blood and tissue fluid for days and are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system.", "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein. Early hives, low blood pressure or wheezing arise through complement activation and mast cell release, and IgE in sensitized people. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear days later with fever, rash and joint pain and is usually treated with corticosteroids.", "fun": "Sea snakes breathe air but can stay underwater for long periods, and some species can take in a little oxygen through their skin.", "src": ["CSL Seqirus Sea Snake Antivenom product information (Australia)", "Therapeutic Guidelines: Toxicology and Toxinology (Australia)", "Sutherland SK. Australian Animal Toxins"]}, "csl-box-jellyfish-antivenom": {"origin": "The box jellyfish Chironex fleckeri lives in the tropical waters of northern Australia and nearby seas and is one of the most dangerous animals in the ocean. Its long tentacles carry millions of stinging cells. The species was named in 1956 in honor of Hugo Flecker, a Queensland doctor who investigated deaths from jellyfish stings. Commonwealth Serum Laboratories (CSL) in Melbourne released box jellyfish antivenom around 1970. Unlike most CSL products it is raised in sheep, using venom collected from the jellyfish's stinging cells. Its benefit is debated: it binds venom strongly in the test tube, but animal studies question how well it prevents cardiovascular collapse. It is carried by the Queensland Ambulance Service and made by CSL Seqirus. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.", "timeline": [["1956", "Chironex fleckeri is named in honor of Hugo Flecker."], ["1970s", "CSL introduces box jellyfish antivenom raised in sheep."]], "cell": "Each tentacle carries nematocysts, tiny capsules that fire a harpoon-like thread into skin and inject venom. The most harmful venom proteins (such as CfTX-1 and CfTX-2) form pores in cell membranes. Pores in red cells and heart and vascular cells let potassium and other ions leak out, which can cause sudden hyperkalemia, cardiac rhythm problems, low blood pressure and cardiovascular collapse within minutes. Pores in skin cells and nerves cause severe pain and skin damage. Sheep immunoglobulin binds these toxins and is intended to block them before they reach their targets, after which complexes are cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Because the venom acts so fast, much of the damage can happen before antivenom arrives, which may explain why clinical benefit is uncertain.", "body": [["Heart and circulation", "The venom can cause collapse and cardiac arrest within minutes. Antivenom is intended to reduce this, but its real-world benefit is debated."], ["Skin", "Venom causes intense pain and whip-like welts that can scar. Antivenom is used when pain is severe and not controlled by other measures."], ["Nerves", "Pore-forming toxins strongly activate pain nerves, causing severe burning pain."], ["Immune system", "Sheep protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and serum sickness days later."]], "journey": "The antivenom is given by IV injection or infusion after dilution, and Queensland ambulance protocols also describe an intraosseous route. It enters the bloodstream immediately. Because it is a whole or large immunoglobulin product it is expected to stay in the blood for days and be cleared by protein breakdown, but its pharmacokinetics in people are not well characterized.", "sidefx": "Reactions come from sheep protein. Early hives, low blood pressure or wheezing arise from complement activation and mast cell release, and IgE in people sensitized to sheep proteins. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear about five to ten days later with fever, rash and joint pain. A strong stinging sensation during injection is also described. The antivenom does not treat Irukandji syndrome from small box jellyfish.", "fun": "Vinegar poured on a Chironex sting does not neutralize injected venom, but it stops the remaining unfired stinging cells on the skin from discharging.", "src": ["CSL Seqirus Box Jellyfish Antivenom product information (Australia)", "Queensland Ambulance Service Drug Therapy Protocols", "Therapeutic Guidelines: Toxicology and Toxinology (Australia)"]}, "csl-redback-spider-antivenom": {"origin": "The redback spider (Latrodectus hasselti) is Australia's widow spider, a close relative of the American black widow. Bites cause latrodectism: severe local and spreading pain, sweating, nausea and high blood pressure that can last for days. Commonwealth Serum Laboratories (CSL) in Melbourne introduced redback antivenom in 1956, raised in horses against venom milked from the spiders. For decades it was one of the most often used antivenoms in Australia. Its benefit was later questioned: the RAVE-II randomized trial, published in 2014, found it gave no more pain relief than placebo, and many clinicians now rely mainly on pain medicines. CSL Seqirus makes it today as horse F(ab')2 fragments. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.", "timeline": [["1956", "CSL introduces redback spider antivenom."], ["2014", "The RAVE-II randomized trial finds no added pain relief over placebo."]], "cell": "The key toxin in redback venom is alpha-latrotoxin, a large protein that binds receptors on nerve terminals called neurexins and latrophilins. After binding it inserts into the membrane and forms a pore that lets calcium and other cations flood in. The surge of calcium triggers massive release of neurotransmitters from vesicles, including acetylcholine at muscles and norepinephrine at sympathetic nerves. This explains the pain, muscle cramps, sweating, high blood pressure and fast heart rate of latrodectism. The horse F(ab')2 fragments bind alpha-latrotoxin in tissue fluid and blood, blocking it from reaching receptors, and the complexes are cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Toxin already fixed to nerve terminals may be less reachable, which may explain inconsistent clinical results.", "body": [["Pain pathways", "Neurotransmitter release at nerve endings causes severe pain that spreads from the bite. Antivenom was intended to relieve it, but trials show little benefit over placebo."], ["Sympathetic nerves", "Norepinephrine release causes sweating (often only near the bite), fast heart rate and high blood pressure."], ["Muscles", "Acetylcholine release at neuromuscular junctions can cause cramps and muscle stiffness."], ["Gut and general", "Nausea, vomiting, headache and feeling unwell are common with systemic envenoming."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and later serum sickness."]], "journey": "Australian product information describes intramuscular injection for most cases, with IV use after dilution reserved for life-threatening envenoming. Given IM, the antibody fragments are absorbed slowly into the lymph and blood over hours; given IV, they enter the blood at once. The F(ab')2 fragments are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system. Detailed pharmacokinetics in people are not well characterized.", "sidefx": "Reactions are caused by horse protein. Early reactions, from hives to anaphylaxis, come from complement activation and mast cell release, and IgE in sensitized people; the IV route is described as more likely to provoke them. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear days to weeks later with fever, rash, joint pain and swollen glands.", "fun": "Redback and American black widow venoms are so similar that the Australian antivenom has been used for widow spider bites in other countries.", "src": ["CSL Seqirus Redback Spider Antivenom product information (Australia)", "Isbister GK et al. RAVE-II trial, Ann Emerg Med 2014", "Therapeutic Guidelines: Toxicology and Toxinology (Australia)"]}, "funnel-web-spider-antivenom": {"origin": "The Sydney funnel-web spider (Atrax robustus) and its relatives in the genus Hadronyche live in eastern Australia. A bite, especially from a male, can cause life-threatening toxicity within an hour, and before antivenom there were recorded deaths, including children. The project to make an antivenom was led by Struan Sutherland at Commonwealth Serum Laboratories (CSL) in Melbourne. A key problem was that primates are very sensitive to the venom while many other mammals are not, so animals could be immunized safely. The team immunized rabbits and purified their IgG. The antivenom was released in 1980, and no deaths from funnel-web bites have been recorded in Australia since it came into use. Venom for production is collected by milking spiders, much of it at the Australian Reptile Park. CSL Seqirus makes it today. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.", "timeline": [["1980", "CSL releases funnel-web spider antivenom developed by Struan Sutherland's team."], ["1981", "Funnel-web antivenom is first used successfully in a patient."]], "cell": "Funnel-web venom contains delta-hexatoxins (also called delta-atracotoxins, such as robustoxin). These toxins bind voltage-gated sodium channels on nerves and slow the channel's inactivation, so each nerve impulse lasts longer and nerves fire repeatedly. That causes massive release of acetylcholine and catecholamines from both somatic and autonomic nerves. The result is an autonomic storm: tingling around the mouth, muscle twitching, drooling, tearing, heavy sweating, very high blood pressure, fast heart rate and fluid in the lungs. Rabbit IgG binds the delta-hexatoxins in blood and tissue fluid, preventing them from reaching sodium channels, and the complexes are cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Because the toxin acts on channels rather than destroying nerves, symptoms can reverse quickly once it is neutralized.", "body": [["Autonomic nervous system", "Neutralizing delta-hexatoxins stops the flood of acetylcholine and adrenaline, so sweating, drooling, tearing and blood pressure swings settle."], ["Muscles", "Muscle twitching and spasms stop as repetitive nerve firing ends."], ["Lungs", "Severe envenoming can cause pulmonary edema. Reversing the autonomic storm helps the lungs clear."], ["Heart and vessels", "Dangerous hypertension and fast heart rate can be followed by low blood pressure in late severe cases; antivenom aims to prevent this progression."], ["Immune system", "Rabbit protein can cause allergic-type reactions, though these are reported as uncommon with this product."]], "journey": "The freeze-dried powder is reconstituted with sterile water and given by slow IV injection in hospital. It enters the blood at once and binds circulating toxin. Clinical improvement often begins within minutes to hours. Whole rabbit IgG is a large molecule, so it is not filtered by the kidneys and persists in the blood for days, being cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system. Pressure immobilization bandaging before arrival slows venom movement through the lymph.", "sidefx": "Reactions come from the immune system meeting rabbit protein. Early reactions such as hives or anaphylaxis can arise through complement activation and mast cell release, and IgE in people sensitized to rabbit products, but they are reported as uncommon. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, is possible up to about two weeks later with fever, rash and joint pain.", "fun": "Funnel-web venom is deadly to people and other primates, yet dogs, cats and rabbits are relatively resistant, which is why rabbits could be used to make the antivenom.", "src": ["CSL Seqirus Funnel Web Spider Antivenom product information (Australia)", "Sutherland SK. Australian Animal Toxins", "Isbister GK et al. Funnel-web spider bite: a systematic review. Med J Aust 2005"]}, "stonefish-antivenom": {"origin": "Stonefish (Synanceia) live on reefs and shallow seabeds in the Indo-Pacific, including northern Australia. They look exactly like rocks, and people get stung when they step on them. Spines along the back inject venom, causing some of the worst pain known from any animal sting. Commonwealth Serum Laboratories (CSL) in Melbourne developed stonefish antivenom in the late 1950s, raised in horses against venom from the spines. It is one of very few antivenoms ever made against a fish. Hot water immersion remains the standard first aid, and the antivenom is reserved for severe envenoming. CSL Seqirus makes it today as horse F(ab')2 fragments. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.", "timeline": [["1930", "CSL releases its first antivenom, against tiger snake venom."], ["1950s", "CSL develops stonefish antivenom raised in horses."]], "cell": "Stonefish venom contains large pore-forming proteins, stonustoxin (from Synanceia horrida) and verrucotoxin (from Synanceia verrucosa). These insert into cell membranes and form channels that let ions leak, damaging and killing cells near the sting. They strongly activate pain nerves, cause local swelling and tissue injury, and in severe cases cause low blood pressure, heart rhythm changes and breathing difficulty, partly through effects on vessel and heart cells. Horse F(ab')2 fragments bind these toxins, block their pore-forming activity and form complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Neutralizing free toxin relieves pain and systemic effects, but tissue already damaged must heal on its own.", "body": [["Pain nerves", "Venom causes intense, spreading pain within minutes. Antivenom often relieves severe pain within an hour when other measures fail."], ["Skin and soft tissue", "Pore-forming toxins damage cells around the sting, causing swelling and sometimes tissue death. Antivenom limits further damage."], ["Heart and circulation", "Severe envenoming can lower blood pressure and disturb heart rhythm. Neutralizing circulating toxin helps reverse these effects."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and later serum sickness, especially with larger volumes."]], "journey": "Australian product information describes intramuscular injection as the preferred route, with IV infusion after dilution reserved for extreme cases. Given IM, the antibody fragments are absorbed into lymph and blood over hours; given IV, they enter the blood at once. The F(ab')2 fragments are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system. Detailed pharmacokinetics in people are not well characterized.", "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein. Early reactions, from hives to anaphylaxis, arise through complement activation and mast cell release, and IgE in sensitized people; the IV route is described as more likely to provoke them. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear days later with fever, rash and joint pain, and is more likely with larger volumes.", "fun": "Stonefish venom proteins break down with heat, which is one reason soaking the stung foot in hot water is the standard first aid.", "src": ["CSL Seqirus Stonefish Antivenom product information (Australia)", "Therapeutic Guidelines: Toxicology and Toxinology (Australia)", "Sutherland SK. Australian Animal Toxins"]}, "saimr-polyvalent-snake-antivenom": {"origin": "The South African Institute for Medical Research (SAIMR) was founded in Johannesburg in 1912 and became the main maker of antivenom for southern Africa during the 20th century. Its polyvalent snake antivenom is raised in horses immunized with the venoms of ten dangerous local snakes: black, green and Jameson's mambas, Cape, forest, snouted and Mozambique spitting cobras, the rinkhals, the puff adder and the Gaboon adder. The horse antibodies are digested into F(ab')2 fragments. Production later moved to South African Vaccine Producers, a subsidiary of the National Health Laboratory Service, which keeps the SAIMR name on the product. It is supplied across southern Africa and to zoos and antivenom banks abroad. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.", "timeline": [["1890s", "Albert Calmette develops the first snake antivenom against cobra venom."], ["1912", "The South African Institute for Medical Research is founded in Johannesburg."], ["2017", "WHO lists snakebite envenoming as a neglected tropical disease."]], "cell": "This product carries antibody fragments against three very different kinds of venom. Mamba venoms contain dendrotoxins, which block voltage-gated potassium channels on motor nerve terminals and boost acetylcholine release, and fasciculins, which inhibit acetylcholinesterase; together they cause twitching and then paralysis. Cobra and rinkhals venoms contain postsynaptic three-finger alpha-neurotoxins that block muscle nicotinic acetylcholine receptors, plus cytotoxins that kill cells around the bite. Puff adder and Gaboon adder venoms are rich in metalloproteinases and other enzymes that destroy vessel walls and tissue, causing massive swelling, bleeding and blistering. Horse F(ab')2 fragments bind these toxins at many sites, block their activity and form complexes removed by the reticuloendothelial system. They stop further progression but cannot rebuild tissue that has already died.", "body": [["Nerves and muscles", "Neutralizing mamba and cobra neurotoxins halts progressive paralysis and twitching, and postsynaptic weakness can improve over hours."], ["Breathing", "Stopping neurotoxins protects breathing muscles, though ventilation may still be needed in severe bites."], ["Skin and limbs", "Neutralizing cytotoxins and adder metalloproteinases halts rapidly spreading painful swelling, but tissue already dead does not recover."], ["Blood and vessels", "Adder venom damages small vessels, causing bleeding and fluid loss into the limb. Antivenom limits further damage."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein causes early reactions in a large share of patients, and serum sickness can follow."]], "journey": "The antivenom is given intravenously, often diluted as an infusion, in a hospital; intraosseous use is described when veins cannot be reached, and intramuscular use is not effective. It enters the blood at once and binds circulating venom. The F(ab')2 fragments are too large for kidney filtration, so they stay in the blood and tissue fluid for days and are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system. Detailed human pharmacokinetics are not well characterized.", "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein and are common with this product. Early reactions such as hives, vomiting, wheezing and low blood pressure arise through complement activation and mast cell release, plus IgE in sensitized people. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and some South African protocols describe giving it in advance. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear days later with fever, rash and joint pain. The product has no value for snakes not covered, such as the boomslang, berg adder and night adders.", "fun": "Most snakebites in southern Africa never need antivenom, because many bites are dry or cause only local symptoms.", "src": ["SAIMR Polyvalent Snake Antivenom product information (South African Vaccine Producers)", "African Snakebite Institute clinical resources", "WHO Guidelines for the Management of Snakebites"]}, "saimr-boomslang-antivenom": {"origin": "The boomslang (Dispholidus typus) is a slender, rear-fanged tree snake found across much of sub-Saharan Africa. Because its fangs sit at the back of the mouth and it is shy, it was long thought harmless to people. That changed in 1957, when the American herpetologist Karl Patterson Schmidt of the Field Museum in Chicago was bitten while identifying a young boomslang. He wrote careful notes about his symptoms and died the next day from internal bleeding. The South African Institute for Medical Research (SAIMR) in Johannesburg made a dedicated monovalent boomslang antivenom raised in horses, now produced by South African Vaccine Producers. It is scarce and expensive and is reserved for bites with laboratory evidence of coagulopathy. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.", "timeline": [["1912", "The South African Institute for Medical Research is founded in Johannesburg."], ["1957", "Herpetologist Karl P. Schmidt dies after a boomslang bite and records his symptoms."]], "cell": "Boomslang venom is rich in metalloproteinases that activate prothrombin (and also factor X). Thrombin forms throughout the bloodstream and uses up fibrinogen and other clotting factors faster than the liver can make them. This venom-induced consumption coagulopathy leaves the blood unable to clot, along with low platelets, so bleeding starts from gums, wounds, the gut and the brain. Effects can be delayed for 24 to 48 hours. The horse F(ab')2 fragments are made specifically against boomslang venom; they bind the prothrombin activators, block them and form complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Once the venom is neutralized, the liver can rebuild clotting factors and bleeding stops. The polyvalent SAIMR product does not neutralize boomslang venom.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Stopping the prothrombin activators lets fibrinogen and other factors recover, so clotting tests normalize over hours."], ["Bleeding sites", "Gum, wound, gut and urinary bleeding stops as clotting returns, and the risk of brain bleeding falls."], ["Kidneys", "Severe envenoming can damage the kidneys, partly from bleeding and microangiopathy. Neutralizing venom helps limit this."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and later serum sickness."]], "journey": "The antivenom is given intravenously in hospital. It binds venom in the blood at once. The F(ab')2 fragments stay in the blood and tissue fluid for days because they are too large for kidney filtration, and they are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system. Clotting tests begin to improve over hours. Blood products alone generally fail while venom is still active, because the venom keeps consuming new factors.", "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein. Early reactions, from hives to anaphylaxis, arise through complement activation and mast cell release, and IgE in sensitized people. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear days later with fever, rash and joint pain. Because supply is very limited, the antivenom is kept for confirmed boomslang bites with coagulopathy.", "fun": "Karl P. Schmidt's diary of his own fatal boomslang bite is one of the most famous documents in herpetology, recording each symptom until shortly before he died.", "src": ["SAIMR Boomslang Antivenom product information (South African Vaccine Producers)", "African Snakebite Institute clinical resources", "Warrell DA. Snake bite. Lancet 2010"]}, "echitab-plus-icp": {"origin": "EchiTAb-Plus-ICP was born from a partnership to fight snakebite in West Africa, where the carpet viper (Echis ocellatus) causes huge numbers of bites in the savanna belt of Nigeria. In the early 2000s the EchiTAb Study Group brought together the Nigerian Federal Ministry of Health, the Liverpool School of Tropical Medicine and the Instituto Clodomiro Picado (ICP) at the University of Costa Rica. ICP, founded in 1970 and named after the Costa Rican scientist Clodomiro Picado Twight, had long experience making antivenom for Central American snakes. It developed a horse antivenom against carpet viper, puff adder and black-necked spitting cobra venoms, purified by caprylic acid precipitation as whole IgG. A randomized trial in Nigeria published in 2010 compared it with a sheep-derived antivenom. It is licensed in Nigeria and other African countries but is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.", "timeline": [["1970", "The Instituto Clodomiro Picado is founded at the University of Costa Rica."], ["2000s", "The EchiTAb Study Group develops antivenoms for Nigerian snakebite."], ["2010", "Abubakar and colleagues publish a randomized trial of EchiTAb-Plus-ICP in Nigeria."], ["2017", "WHO lists snakebite envenoming as a neglected tropical disease."]], "cell": "Carpet viper venom contains metalloproteinases that activate prothrombin, similar to the reference enzyme ecarin. They generate thrombin throughout the bloodstream, which consumes fibrinogen and leaves the blood unable to clot, and hemorrhagins damage small vessel walls so blood leaks out. Puff adder venom adds metalloproteinases that cause massive swelling and bleeding. Black-necked spitting cobra venom contains three-finger cytotoxins and phospholipase A2 that destroy cells and cause local necrosis. EchiTAb-Plus-ICP is whole horse IgG, with both antigen-binding arms and the Fc tail kept. It binds these toxins at many sites, blocks their activity and forms immune complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Neutralizing the procoagulant enzymes lets the liver restore clotting factors.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Neutralizing carpet viper prothrombin activators restores clotting, usually followed with the 20-minute whole blood clotting test."], ["Blood vessels", "Blocking hemorrhagins stops further leaking from damaged vessels, which reduces bleeding from gums, wounds and internal organs."], ["Skin and limbs", "Neutralizing adder and spitting cobra toxins limits further swelling and tissue death, but dead tissue does not recover."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early reactions and later serum sickness."]], "journey": "The antivenom is given by slow IV injection or infusion in a health facility. It binds venom in the blood at once. Whole IgG is about 150 kDa, so it is not filtered by the kidneys and has a long half-life, persisting for days and staying around longer than Fab or F(ab')2 fragments. That long presence helps cover venom still leaking from the bite site and lowers the chance of coagulopathy returning. It is cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system.", "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein. Early reactions such as hives, vomiting, wheezing and low blood pressure arise through complement activation, which aggregates and the retained Fc portion can trigger, and mast cell release, plus IgE in sensitized people. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear days later with fever, rash and joint pain.", "fun": "An antivenom for West African snakes is made in Costa Rica, an example of how venom science from one continent was shared to help another.", "src": ["Instituto Clodomiro Picado product information", "Abubakar IS et al. PLoS Negl Trop Dis 2010", "WHO Guidelines for the Production, Control and Regulation of Snake Antivenom Immunoglobulins"]}, "indian-polyvalent-anti-snake-venom": {"origin": "India has more snakebite deaths than any other country, with tens of thousands each year according to the Million Death Study and later estimates. Most deaths come from the 'big four': the Indian cobra, the common krait, Russell's viper and the saw-scaled viper. Antivenom production in India grew out of public health institutes such as the Haffkine Institute in Mumbai, founded in 1899 by Waldemar Haffkine. Today several manufacturers, including Bharat Serums and Vaccines, VINS Bioproducts, Haffkine Bio-Pharmaceutical and Premium Serums, make a freeze-dried polyvalent antivenom raised in horses against the big four venoms. Much of the venom comes from the Irula snake catchers of Tamil Nadu, who formed a cooperative in 1978. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.", "timeline": [["1899", "Waldemar Haffkine founds the laboratory that becomes the Haffkine Institute in Mumbai."], ["1978", "The Irula Snake Catchers Industrial Cooperative Society is founded in Tamil Nadu."], ["2011", "The Million Death Study estimates about 46,000 snakebite deaths in India in 2005."], ["2017", "WHO lists snakebite envenoming as a neglected tropical disease."]], "cell": "The four venoms work in different ways. Cobra venom carries postsynaptic three-finger alpha-neurotoxins that block muscle nicotinic acetylcholine receptors, plus cytotoxins that cause local necrosis. Krait venom carries beta-bungarotoxin, a presynaptic phospholipase A2 that enters motor nerve terminals, destroys them and stops acetylcholine release, causing paralysis that is slow to recover. Russell's viper venom contains activators of factor X and factor V, and saw-scaled viper venom contains prothrombin activators; both drive consumption coagulopathy, and Russell's viper venom also injures the kidneys. Horse F(ab')2 fragments bind these toxins at many sites, block their activity and form complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. They stop further damage but cannot regrow destroyed nerve terminals or replace consumed clotting factors.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Neutralizing viper procoagulants stops factor consumption. Clotting often returns within hours as the liver makes new factors."], ["Nerves and muscles", "Neutralizing cobra toxins can improve weakness, but krait paralysis from destroyed nerve terminals recovers slowly over days."], ["Breathing", "Neurotoxic bites can stop breathing. Antivenom halts progression, but ventilation is often still needed."], ["Kidneys", "Russell's viper bites often cause acute kidney injury. Early antivenom helps limit it."], ["Skin and limbs", "Neutralizing cobra and viper toxins limits spreading swelling and necrosis."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein frequently causes early reactions, and serum sickness can follow."]], "journey": "The freeze-dried powder is reconstituted with sterile water and given by slow IV injection or infusion; intramuscular use is not recommended because absorption is poor. It enters the blood at once and binds circulating venom. The F(ab')2 fragments are too large for kidney filtration, so they stay in the blood and tissue fluid for days and are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system.", "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein and are common. Early reactions such as hives, fever, rigors, vomiting and wheezing arise through complement activation and mast cell release, plus IgE in sensitized people. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis; skin or eye sensitivity tests do not predict reactions and are not recommended. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear days later. Venom differs between regions of India, which can reduce effectiveness, and the product does not cover snakes such as hump-nosed pit vipers or king cobras.", "fun": "The Irula people of Tamil Nadu, traditional snake catchers, now supply much of the venom used to make India's antivenom through their own cooperative.", "src": ["Indian polyvalent antivenom product information (Indian manufacturers)", "Mohapatra B et al. PLoS Negl Trop Dis 2011 (Million Death Study)", "WHO Guidelines for the Management of Snakebites"]}, "qsmi-king-cobra-antivenin": {"origin": "The king cobra (Ophiophagus hannah) is the longest venomous snake in the world, and its name means snake eater because it feeds mostly on other snakes. A bite delivers a very large amount of venom, and paralysis can develop within about an hour. The Queen Saovabha Memorial Institute (QSMI) in Bangkok, run by the Thai Red Cross Society, was founded in the 1920s and became famous for its snake farm and antivenom production. It makes a freeze-dried monovalent king cobra antivenin raised in horses, digested into F(ab')2 fragments. Because king cobras are kept in zoos worldwide, it is also stocked by some international zoo and poison center antivenom banks. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.", "timeline": [["1890s", "Albert Calmette develops the first cobra antivenom."], ["1920s", "The Thai Red Cross founds the institute in Bangkok that becomes QSMI, with a snake farm for antivenom."]], "cell": "King cobra venom is dominated by three-finger alpha-neurotoxins, both short-chain and long-chain types. These bind the muscle-type nicotinic acetylcholine receptor on the motor end plate and block acetylcholine from opening it, so nerve signals cannot make muscles contract. The result is descending flaccid paralysis, starting with drooping eyelids and spreading to swallowing and breathing muscles. The venom also contains metalloproteinases and L-amino acid oxidase that damage tissue near the bite. Horse F(ab')2 fragments bind these toxins, block their active sites and form complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Because alpha-neurotoxin binding is postsynaptic and partly reversible, neutralizing free toxin can help receptors recover.", "body": [["Nerves and muscles", "Neutralizing alpha-neurotoxins halts descending paralysis of eyelids, face, throat and limbs."], ["Breathing", "Severe bites can cause respiratory failure within an hour. Antivenom limits progression, but airway support and ventilation are central to survival."], ["Skin and limbs", "Neutralizing tissue-damaging enzymes limits local swelling."], ["Neuromuscular junction", "Because the block is postsynaptic, neostigmine with atropine can transiently improve strength alongside antivenom."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and later serum sickness."]], "journey": "The freeze-dried powder is reconstituted with the supplied diluent and given by slow IV injection or infusion in a hospital with ventilator support. It binds venom in the blood at once. The F(ab')2 fragments are too large for kidney filtration, so they stay in the blood and tissue fluid for days and are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system. Strength improves over hours.", "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein. Early reactions, from hives to anaphylaxis, arise through complement activation and mast cell release, plus IgE in sensitized people. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear days later with fever, rash and joint pain.", "fun": "King cobras are the only snakes known to build nests, piling leaves into a mound where the female guards her eggs.", "src": ["QSMI King Cobra Antivenin product information (Thai Red Cross)", "WHO Guidelines for the Management of Snakebites in South-East Asia", "Warrell DA. Snake bite. Lancet 2010"]}, "qsmi-neuro-polyvalent-antivenin": {"origin": "Thailand's most dangerous neurotoxic snakes are the monocled cobra, the king cobra and two kraits, the Malayan krait and the banded krait. In real cases the snake is often not seen, especially with kraits, which bite at night while people sleep on the floor. The Queen Saovabha Memorial Institute (QSMI) in Bangkok, run by the Thai Red Cross Society, already made monovalent antivenins for single species. To cover bites where the snake is unknown, it developed a neuro polyvalent antivenin raised in horses against all four venoms, freeze-dried as F(ab')2 fragments. It is used in Thailand and other Southeast Asian countries. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.", "timeline": [["1920s", "The Thai Red Cross founds the Bangkok institute that becomes QSMI."]], "cell": "The four venoms cause paralysis in two different ways. Cobra and king cobra venoms carry postsynaptic three-finger alpha-neurotoxins that block muscle nicotinic acetylcholine receptors. That block is partly reversible, so neutralizing free toxin can let receptors recover. Krait venoms carry beta-bungarotoxin, a presynaptic phospholipase A2 toxin that enters motor nerve terminals, damages them and stops acetylcholine release. Once a terminal is destroyed, antibodies cannot fix it, and strength returns only as nerve terminals regrow over days. Horse F(ab')2 fragments bind all these toxins in blood and tissue fluid, block their activity and form complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Early treatment matters most for krait bites, before toxin reaches the nerve terminals.", "body": [["Nerves and muscles", "Neutralizing neurotoxins halts descending paralysis. Cobra paralysis can improve over hours; krait paralysis recovers slowly."], ["Breathing", "Neurotoxic bites can cause respiratory failure, and airway protection and mechanical ventilation are central to survival."], ["Neuromuscular junction", "Neostigmine with atropine can transiently help cobra weakness but is less helpful for krait bites because the problem is presynaptic."], ["Skin and limbs", "Cobra cytotoxins can cause local swelling and necrosis. Krait bites are often nearly painless with little local change."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and later serum sickness."]], "journey": "The freeze-dried powder is reconstituted with the supplied diluent and given by slow IV injection or infusion in a hospital with ventilator support. It binds venom in the blood right away. The F(ab')2 fragments are too large for kidney filtration, so they stay in the blood and tissue fluid for days and are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system.", "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein. Early reactions, from hives to anaphylaxis, arise through complement activation and mast cell release, plus IgE in sensitized people. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear days later with fever, rash and joint pain.", "fun": "Beta-bungarotoxin and alpha-bungarotoxin from kraits became classic research tools; alpha-bungarotoxin was used to identify and count nicotinic receptors at the neuromuscular junction.", "src": ["QSMI Neuro Polyvalent Snake Antivenin product information (Thai Red Cross)", "WHO Guidelines for the Management of Snakebites in South-East Asia", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "qsmi-hemato-polyvalent-antivenin": {"origin": "In Thailand the main snakes that cause bleeding and clotting failure are the Malayan pit viper, the Eastern Russell's viper and the white-lipped green pit viper. The Queen Saovabha Memorial Institute (QSMI) in Bangkok, run by the Thai Red Cross Society, made separate antivenins for these species and later combined them into a hemato polyvalent antivenin raised in horses and freeze-dried as F(ab')2 fragments. It is used when one of these vipers is suspected but cannot be identified. It is used in Thailand and other Southeast Asian countries. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.", "timeline": [["1920s", "The Thai Red Cross founds the Bangkok institute that becomes QSMI."]], "cell": "Each viper disrupts clotting in a different way. Russell's viper venom contains enzymes that activate factor X and factor V, generating thrombin throughout the bloodstream. Malayan pit viper and green pit viper venoms contain thrombin-like serine proteases that clip fibrinogen directly into weak fibrin that is quickly broken down. Both routes use up fibrinogen and leave the blood unable to clot. Snake venom metalloproteinases damage small vessel walls, causing bleeding and swelling, and phospholipase A2 adds tissue injury. Horse F(ab')2 fragments bind these toxins at many sites, block their activity and form complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Clotting then recovers over hours as the liver makes new fibrinogen and factors.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Neutralizing procoagulant and thrombin-like enzymes stops fibrinogen consumption, and clotting usually improves over 6 to 24 hours."], ["Blood vessels", "Blocking metalloproteinases reduces bleeding from gums, wounds and internal sites."], ["Kidneys", "Russell's viper bites often cause acute kidney injury. Neutralizing venom early helps limit it."], ["Skin and limbs", "Neutralizing local toxins limits spreading swelling and blistering."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and later serum sickness."]], "journey": "The freeze-dried powder is reconstituted with the supplied diluent and given by slow IV injection or infusion in hospital. It binds venom in the blood at once. The F(ab')2 fragments are too large for kidney filtration, so they stay in the blood and tissue fluid for days and are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system. Coagulopathy can return after initial correction as venom keeps leaking from the bite, so clotting is rechecked.", "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein. Early reactions, from hives to anaphylaxis, arise through complement activation and mast cell release, plus IgE in sensitized people. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear days later with fever, rash and joint pain.", "fun": "A thrombin-like enzyme from Malayan pit viper venom, called ancrod, was studied for decades as a drug to thin fibrinogen in stroke.", "src": ["QSMI Hemato Polyvalent Snake Antivenin product information (Thai Red Cross)", "WHO Guidelines for the Management of Snakebites in South-East Asia", "Warrell DA. Snake bite. Lancet 2010"]}, "viperatab": {"origin": "The European adder (Vipera berus) is the only venomous snake native to the United Kingdom and is found across much of northern Europe and Asia. Bites are rarely fatal but can cause marked swelling, vomiting, low blood pressure and angioedema. For many years UK hospitals used an older horse antivenom made in Zagreb. ViperaTAb was developed in the 1990s using the same sheep Fab approach later used in the US product CroFab: sheep are immunized with adder venom, the antibodies are purified by affinity binding to venom, and papain cuts them into small Fab fragments. It was used in Scandinavia and became the standard adder antivenom in the UK in the 2010s. MicroPharm in Wales makes it. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.", "timeline": [["1890s", "Albert Calmette develops the first snake antivenom."], ["1990s", "Ovine Fab adder antivenom is developed and studied in Europe."], ["2010s", "ViperaTAb becomes the standard adder antivenom in UK practice."]], "cell": "Adder venom is a mix of phospholipase A2, snake venom metalloproteinases, serine proteases and other proteins. These damage small vessel walls and raise their permeability, so fluid leaks into tissues, causing spreading limb swelling and, if widespread, low blood pressure. Some components trigger release of inflammatory mediators, which can cause vomiting, diarrhea and angioedema, and mild coagulopathy can occur. ViperaTAb is affinity-purified sheep Fab: single antigen-binding arms of about 50 kDa without the Fc tail. They bind venom proteins at many sites, block their activity and form small complexes. Because the product is monospecific for Vipera berus, its effect on other European vipers is less certain.", "body": [["Limbs and soft tissue", "Neutralizing venom limits progression of limb swelling. Because Fab clears quickly, swelling can progress again and extra treatment may be needed."], ["Heart and circulation", "Neutralizing vessel-damaging toxins reverses hypotension and shock in severe bites."], ["Gut", "Vomiting, abdominal pain and diarrhea from venom-driven mediator release settle as venom is neutralized."], ["Airway and skin", "Angioedema of the face and lips from the venom can improve after antivenom."], ["Immune system", "Reactions to sheep Fab are reported as uncommon, but allergy to sheep protein or papain raises the risk."]], "journey": "ViperaTAb is diluted and given by IV infusion in hospital. It enters the blood at once and spreads widely into tissues because Fab fragments are small. At about 50 kDa they are filtered by the kidneys and cleared much faster than F(ab')2 or whole IgG. That short half-life means venom still leaking from the bite site can cause swelling to return after the antivenom has gone.", "sidefx": "Reactions come from sheep protein. Early hives or anaphylaxis can occur through mast cell release and IgE in sensitized people, but removing the Fc portion and affinity purification lower the foreign protein load, so reactions are reported as uncommon. Papain used in manufacturing may matter for people with papain allergy. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness is uncommon. Recurrence of swelling reflects fast Fab clearance, not a reaction.", "fun": "The European adder ranges farther north than any other snake, reaching beyond the Arctic Circle in Scandinavia.", "src": ["ViperaTAb product information (MicroPharm)", "UK TOXBASE guidance on adder bites", "Karlson-Stiber C et al. J Intern Med 1997"]}, "antivipmyn": {"origin": "In Mexico and Central America, pit vipers such as rattlesnakes (Crotalus) and the terciopelo or fer-de-lance (Bothrops asper) cause most serious snakebites. Instituto Bioclon in Mexico City, part of the Silanes group, developed Antivipmyn, a horse antivenom made by immunizing horses with Crotalus and Bothrops venoms and digesting the antibodies with pepsin into F(ab')2 fragments. Bioclon called this approach fabotherapy. It works with researchers at the National Autonomous University of Mexico (UNAM). Bioclon's experience led to Anavip, a related equine F(ab')2 rattlesnake antivenom that FDA approved in 2015. Antivipmyn itself is licensed in Mexico and other Latin American countries but is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.", "timeline": [["1890s", "Albert Calmette develops the first snake antivenom."], ["2015", "FDA approves Anavip, a related equine F(ab')2 pit viper antivenom made by Bioclon."]], "cell": "Pit viper venoms are rich in snake venom metalloproteinases, which break down the basement membrane of small vessels, causing bleeding, swelling and blistering. Thrombin-like serine proteases cut fibrinogen into unstable fibrin, using it up and causing clotting failure. Phospholipase A2 enzymes damage muscle and, in some Crotalus venoms such as Mojave and South American rattlesnakes, form crotoxin-like neurotoxins that block acetylcholine release. Antivipmyn's horse F(ab')2 fragments bind these toxins at many sites, block their activity and form complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Neutralizing free venom stops further swelling and lets the liver restore fibrinogen, but tissue already destroyed must heal on its own.", "body": [["Limbs and soft tissue", "Neutralizing metalloproteinases halts progressive swelling, bruising and blistering from the bite."], ["Blood clotting", "Blocking thrombin-like enzymes stops fibrinogen consumption, and clotting improves over hours."], ["Muscles and nerves", "Neutralizing phospholipase A2 limits muscle injury and, for neurotoxic rattlesnakes, weakness."], ["Kidneys", "Less muscle breakdown and bleeding reduce the risk of kidney injury."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and serum sickness 5 to 15 days later."]], "journey": "The lyophilized powder is reconstituted with the supplied diluent, further diluted and given by IV infusion in hospital. It binds venom in the blood immediately. F(ab')2 fragments, about 100 kDa, are too large for kidney filtration and spread through the blood and tissue fluid more widely than whole IgG. They persist longer than Fab fragments, which helps prevent late return of coagulopathy as venom keeps seeping from the bite site. They are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system.", "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein. Early type I and anaphylactoid reactions, from rash to bronchospasm, arise through mast cell release, complement activation and IgE in sensitized people; pepsin digestion removes the Fc portion, which lowers this risk compared with whole IgG. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear 5 to 15 days later with hives and joint pain.", "fun": "Bioclon's approach to antivenom in Mexico led to two US-approved products: Anascorp for scorpion stings and Anavip for rattlesnake bites.", "src": ["Antivipmyn product information (Instituto Bioclon, Mexico)", "Anavip (crotalidae immune F(ab')2 equine) FDA label", "WHO Guidelines for the Production, Control and Regulation of Snake Antivenom Immunoglobulins"]}, "alacramyn": {"origin": "Mexico has one of the highest rates of scorpion stings in the world, with hundreds of thousands of stings reported each year, mostly from bark scorpions of the genus Centruroides. Severe stings can be deadly in small children. Instituto Bioclon in Mexico City, working with scientists at the National Autonomous University of Mexico (UNAM) developed Alacramyn, a horse antivenom digested with pepsin into F(ab')2 fragments. Its wide use in Mexico was followed by a large fall in scorpion sting deaths. A closely related Bioclon product, Anascorp, was tested in Arizona and approved by FDA in 2011 for Centruroides scorpion envenomation. Alacramyn itself is licensed in Mexico, but it is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.", "timeline": [["2011", "FDA approves Anascorp, a related Bioclon scorpion antivenom, for US use."]], "cell": "Centruroides venom contains small peptide neurotoxins. Beta-toxins bind voltage-gated sodium channels on nerves and shift their activation to more negative voltages, so the channels open too easily and nerves fire over and over. Other toxins block potassium channels, further prolonging nerve activity. The result is overactivity of both somatic and autonomic nerves: muscle jerking, roving eye movements, drooling, sweating, fast heart rate and, in severe cases, breathing trouble. Alacramyn's horse F(ab')2 fragments bind these toxins in blood and tissue fluid, block them from reaching channels and form complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Because the toxins act on channels rather than destroying nerves, symptoms can resolve within hours once neutralized.", "body": [["Nerves and muscles", "Neutralizing sodium channel toxins stops muscle jerking, agitation and uncoordinated movements."], ["Eyes", "Roving or jerky eye movements settle as nerve overactivity ends."], ["Autonomic nervous system", "Drooling, sweating, fast heart rate and high blood pressure resolve as excess transmitter release stops."], ["Breathing", "Severe stings can cause airway secretions and breathing failure, especially in small children. Antivenom reverses these effects."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and serum sickness 5 to 15 days later."]], "journey": "The lyophilized powder is reconstituted with the supplied diluent and given intravenously; product information describes IV as ideal and IM as less effective. It enters the blood at once and binds circulating toxin. Symptoms often improve within one to four hours. F(ab')2 fragments are too large for kidney filtration and stay in the blood and tissue fluid longer than Fab fragments. They are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system.", "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein. Early type I and anaphylactoid reactions, from rash to bronchospasm, come from mast cell release, complement activation and IgE in sensitized people. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear 5 to 15 days later with hives and joint pain. Sedatives used for agitation before antivenom can add to breathing depression.", "fun": "Scorpions glow blue-green under ultraviolet light because of compounds in their outer shell, which helps people spot them at night.", "src": ["Alacramyn product information (Instituto Bioclon, Mexico)", "Anascorp (centruroides immune F(ab')2 equine) FDA label", "Boyer LV et al. N Engl J Med 2009"]}, "inoserp-pan-africa": {"origin": "Sub-Saharan Africa has long suffered from a shortage of affordable, effective antivenom, and WHO named snakebite envenoming a neglected tropical disease in 2017. Inoserp Pan-Africa was developed by Inosan Biopharma as a single product for many of Africa's most dangerous snakes. Horses are immunized with venoms of carpet vipers, the puff adder, the Gaboon viper, several cobras and spitting cobras and four mambas, and the antibodies are digested into freeze-dried F(ab')2 fragments. It was introduced in several African countries in the 2010s. Independent laboratory studies have reported variable potency against some African venoms, and WHO has stressed independent testing of African antivenoms. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.", "timeline": [["1890s", "Albert Calmette develops the first snake antivenom."], ["2010s", "Inoserp Pan-Africa is introduced in several African countries."], ["2017", "WHO lists snakebite envenoming as a neglected tropical disease."], ["2019", "WHO launches a strategy aiming to halve snakebite deaths by 2030."]], "cell": "The product targets both viper and elapid venoms. Carpet viper (Echis) venoms contain prothrombin-activating metalloproteinases that consume fibrinogen and make the blood unable to clot, plus hemorrhagins that damage vessel walls. Puff adder and Gaboon viper venoms contain metalloproteinases and other enzymes that cause massive swelling and bleeding. Spitting cobra venoms carry three-finger cytotoxins that kill cells around the bite. Cobra venoms carry postsynaptic alpha-neurotoxins that block muscle nicotinic receptors, and mamba venoms carry dendrotoxins, which block potassium channels to increase acetylcholine release, and fasciculins, which inhibit acetylcholinesterase. Horse F(ab')2 fragments bind these toxins at many sites, block their activity and form complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Neutralizing carpet viper procoagulants lets clotting recover, typically over 6 to 24 hours."], ["Blood vessels", "Blocking hemorrhagins reduces bleeding from gums, wounds and internal sites."], ["Skin and limbs", "Neutralizing cytotoxins and adder enzymes limits spreading swelling and tissue death, but dead tissue does not recover."], ["Nerves and muscles", "Neutralizing cobra and mamba neurotoxins halts progressive paralysis, though ventilation may still be needed."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and later serum sickness."]], "journey": "The freeze-dried powder is reconstituted and given by slow IV injection or infusion in hospital. It binds venom in the blood at once. The F(ab')2 fragments are too large for kidney filtration and spread through the blood and tissue fluid, persisting longer than Fab fragments. They are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system.", "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein. Early reactions, from hives to anaphylaxis, arise through complement activation and mast cell release, plus IgE in sensitized people. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear days later with fever, rash and joint pain. Weak potency against a particular venom would mean more vials are needed or the venom is not fully neutralized.", "fun": "WHO estimates that snakebites kill tens of thousands of people each year worldwide, many of them farmers and children in rural Africa and Asia.", "src": ["Inoserp Pan-Africa product information (Inosan Biopharma)", "WHO Snakebite Envenoming: A Strategy for Prevention and Control, 2019", "WHO Guidelines for the Production, Control and Regulation of Snake Antivenom Immunoglobulins"]}, "brazilian-antibothropic-serum": {"origin": "Brazilian antivenom began with the physician Vital Brazil, who started making serums at the Instituto Butantan in Sao Paulo, founded in 1901. Albert Calmette in France had made a cobra antivenom and hoped one serum might work for all snakes. Vital Brazil showed that antivenoms are specific: serum against lancehead (Bothrops) venom protected against lanceheads but not rattlesnakes, and the reverse. He developed separate antibothropic and anticrotalic serums, which shaped antivenom science worldwide. Lanceheads, especially the jararaca, cause most snakebites in Brazil. Today Butantan makes a pentavalent antibothropic serum raised in horses against five Bothrops venoms and processed into F(ab')2 fragments. Brazil's public health system supplies it free. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.", "timeline": [["1890s", "Albert Calmette develops the first snake antivenom against cobra venom."], ["1901", "Vital Brazil begins serum production at the Instituto Butantan in Sao Paulo."], ["1965", "Sergio Ferreira describes bradykinin-potentiating peptides in jararaca venom."], ["1981", "Captopril, designed from jararaca venom research, is approved by FDA."]], "cell": "Bothrops venoms combine several harmful enzyme groups. Snake venom metalloproteinases (hemorrhagins) digest the basement membrane of small vessels, causing bleeding, swelling and blistering. Thrombin-like serine proteases cut fibrinogen directly, and prothrombin and factor X activators generate thrombin in the bloodstream; both routes consume fibrinogen and leave the blood unable to clot. Phospholipase A2 myotoxins damage muscle near the bite and can lead to necrosis. The horse F(ab')2 fragments, made by pepsin digestion that removes the Fc portion, bind these toxins at many sites, block their activity and form complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Coagulation typically normalizes within about 24 hours, but local tissue damage already done is not reversed.", "body": [["Blood clotting", "Neutralizing procoagulant enzymes stops fibrinogen consumption, and clotting typically normalizes within about a day."], ["Blood vessels", "Blocking metalloproteinases reduces bleeding from gums, wounds and internal organs."], ["Limbs and soft tissue", "Neutralizing venom limits further swelling, blistering and muscle death, but existing necrosis does not reverse."], ["Kidneys", "Bothrops envenoming can cause acute kidney injury. Early neutralization helps limit it."], ["Immune system", "Mild early reactions such as itching and hives are very common, and serum sickness can follow 5 to 24 days later."]], "journey": "The liquid antivenom is diluted in saline or glucose solution and given by IV infusion in hospital. It binds venom in the blood at once. F(ab')2 fragments are too large for kidney filtration and spread through blood and tissue fluid, persisting longer than Fab fragments. They are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system. Coagulation usually improves over the first day.", "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein. Mild early reactions such as itching, hives and flushing are very common, from mast cell release and complement activation; severe reactions such as airway swelling and shock are rare. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, is common and causes fever, rash, joint swelling and swollen glands 5 to 24 days later. Skin testing is not recommended because it does not predict reactions.", "fun": "Research on jararaca venom peptides that strengthen the effect of bradykinin led directly to captopril, the first ACE inhibitor.", "src": ["Instituto Butantan Soro Antibotropico product information", "Ferreira SH. Br J Pharmacol 1965", "WHO Guidelines for the Production, Control and Regulation of Snake Antivenom Immunoglobulins"]}});